// generated by build_bp02_222.py — the Connoisseur's Book texts: museum descriptions (CC0) + the essay program's
// readings (interpretation / critique are labelled as readings in the book)
export const TEXT = {"cma:98439":{"d":"The goddess Hebe, cupbearer to the gods, is the subject of this tall oil canvas, 117 by 75 centimeters, which appears to have been left unfinished. Thayer kept many of his paintings in the studio and reworked them at intervals over long periods, and the museum notes that Hebe looks only partly completed. The picture therefore shows a design caught between sketch and finish, with some passages worked up and others left provisional, and gives a glimpse of how he built a figure.","i":"In Greek myth Hebe, daughter of Zeus and Hera, is the goddess of youth who serves nectar and ambrosia to the gods on Olympus; after Heracles joined the gods she became his wife. Painters from the Renaissance on used her as a classical excuse to paint youthful female beauty. For Thayer, who preferred idealized women to narrative, she is a natural subject, youth made figure. His own remark, quoted by the museum, admits that many of his undertakings were set aside and sold unfinished.","c":"The unfinished state cuts both ways. It lets a viewer see the underlying design and a freedom of touch that Thayer's polished canvases hide, but the picture cannot be judged as a resolved statement, and it records a habit of postponement that kept his output uneven. Thayer is at his best when the simplicity of a single idealized figure is carried through to the end; here the end was never quite reached."},"cma:153724":{"d":"A gooseberry plant lies on a table in a strong light against a dark background. The berries are plump and translucent, some with skins on the verge of bursting, while beside them the withered petals and waxy leaves give the plant a stark, almost monumental presence. The painting is tiny, only 30 by 23 centimeters, executed in oil on paper mounted on a wooden panel in 1701, and it shows how much feeling a single humble fruit can bear.","i":"Coorte worked in Middelburg, a wealthy maritime city in Zeeland with a lively interest in the natural world. He painted small still lifes of single modest things, such as asparagus, strawberries and shells, with a plainness unusual beside the lavish banquet pieces many Dutch painters favored. The gooseberry was a humble, local pleasure that Dutch gardeners had begun to cultivate for better taste. The dried blossoms beside near-bursting fruit may remind viewers that ripeness is brief.","c":"Coorte's pictures are among the quietest in Dutch art. Here a single plant is enough: a strong light, a dark ground, a few exact textures. The restraint is the point, and the dark setting turns the fruit into something like jewels. He was little noticed in his own time, and scholarly attention came only in the twentieth century, but his sense of the isolated object now looks strikingly modern."},"cma:142285":{"d":"Shepherds sleep out of doors in this small oil panel, about 23 by 28 centimeters, painted in 1663. Scenes of herdsmen and their flocks were the specialty of Adriaen van de Velde, a young Amsterdam painter then in his late twenties, and the picture belongs to a type made for city buyers who liked to imagine country ease. Its modest size suits a private room, where it could be studied at close range.","i":"Pastoral scenes of herdsmen at rest were a popular Dutch type, offering city buyers a vision of country calm. Sleeping shepherds could stand for ease and innocence and, in the arcadian tradition, for a golden age of simple life. Van de Velde, who died at thirty-five, was also in demand to paint the figures and animals in other artists' landscapes, so his small people appear in the work of several contemporaries.","c":"Van de Velde was a gifted painter who died young, and small panels like this show why he was esteemed: a sure sense of grouping and an easy way with animals and people. The charm is gentle rather than forceful. This picture comes from the early part of his career, before his more classically composed late work, and its pleasure is the pleasure of quiet. Perhaps too quiet for some tastes."},"cma:146469":{"d":"A woman is the sole sitter of this portrait, painted around 1550 in oil on a wood panel about 60 by 49 centimeters. The title does not name her. Bronzino, court painter to the Medici in Florence, is known for portraits of the city's elite that show cool control: smooth, enameled-looking flesh, composed poses, exactly rendered dress and jewels, and a reserved, unreadable expression. A panel like this was made to record a family's standing.","i":"Women's portraits of mid-sixteenth-century Florence were records of family alliance and rank. They emphasize rich dress, jewels and composure, which stood for virtue as well as wealth, and contemporaries would have read the sitter's pose and clothes as signals of her social place, even though her name is now lost. Under Duke Cosimo I de' Medici portraiture became an instrument of courtly image-making, and Bronzino was its leading practitioner.","c":"Bronzino's women are among the most aloof faces in painting: the cool surface, the perfect finish and the withheld emotion make an image of a social ideal rather than a person. That is both the glory and the limit of the style, dazzling control but little warmth. It is a manner more easily admired than loved, and the viewer is left to wonder what the sitter was thinking."},"cma:124990":{"d":"A small boy, Juan María Osorio, holds a string attached to a pet linnet, a type of finch, in this vertical portrait about 120 by 84 centimeters, painted around 1786. The museum describes the execution as somewhat static and notes that the picture was probably painted after the child's death in 1785 at the age of five. The boy was one of three sons of the 13th Count of Altamira, a leading patron of portraits.","i":"Esteve worked as an assistant to Goya before becoming court painter to Charles IV, and he and Goya often shared aristocratic patrons such as the Altamira family. A bird on a string was a childhood plaything, but in a portrait made after a death it may also suggest fragility or a soul that has flown. Goya famously painted Manuel Osorio, another child of the Altamira family, with a magpie on a string.","c":"The museum itself calls the execution somewhat static, and the criticism is fair: the boy lacks vigor and the face has little psychological presence. Esteve was a competent assistant to Goya but never matched the master's understanding of children. The stiffness may reflect the poignancy of a posthumous commission, in which the painter works without the living sitter. As a document of aristocratic grief it moves more than as a painting."},"cma:98904":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as the artist's mother, Lydia Wardwell Smith, is the subject of this nearly square canvas, about 66 by 60 centimeters, painted probably in the middle of the 1800s. Since her son was born in 1810, she would by then have been well advanced in years. A portrait by a son of his mother is probably a personal piece, made for the family rather than for sale.","i":"Allen Smith Jr. worked in northeastern Ohio, where Painesville lies near Cleveland, and painted portraits and landscapes for his neighbors. A portrait of a parent by a grown child is an act of affection and family record, and in provincial America such likenesses held a family's memory long before photographs were common. The long title with the sitter's full maiden and married name shows the importance placed on lineage.","c":"A portrait made out of love may lack the polish of professional work, and one can expect its virtues to be sincerity and its limits technique. The sense of an actual person rather than a type is the draw. Smith was a regional painter, and this picture holds its place as a document of Ohio's early art history. It is not a major picture, but it is an honest one."},"cma:98905":{"d":"A landscape near Painesville, Ohio, is painted on a small vertical canvas of about 47 by 31 centimeters, dated to the 1880s, when the artist was in his seventies. The title names a place the artist probably knew from daily life, in the Western Reserve of northeastern Ohio, and the upright format is unusual for landscape. The picture is a regional view meant for local owners rather than a grand statement.","i":"The Western Reserve, settled largely by people from Connecticut and New England in the early nineteenth century, had a lively but provincial art life, and painters like Allen Smith Jr. made portraits and landscapes for neighbors. A view of a nearby place offered owners a picture of their own country, and Painesville's rolling land and woods were typical subjects. Smith's persistence into old age shows the lasting taste for Hudson River style landscape beyond the coast.","c":"Modest in scale and ambition, a view that pleases by candor. The vertical format forces the painter to rely on a strong foreground and sky, which is a challenge for a regional artist. As the work of a man in his seventies, it shows the persistence of a regional taste for earlier landscape manners long after the style had faded in New York. It is no great art, but there is real feeling in it."},"cma:157228":{"d":"A ferry delivers two wealthy women and their belongings to the shore while a herd of especially handsome cattle rests in the foreground, in a calm, wide view of water and land. The oil on panel measures about 78 by 102 centimeters and is dated 1816, an early example of American landscape as a specialty. The long title promises an extensive and boundless scene, and the picture offers breadth and stillness rather than drama.","i":"Fisher recalled that landscape painting was novel in America, and that he found it a more lucrative, pleasant and distinguishing branch of art than portraiture. In 1816 there were almost no landscape specialists in the United States, and Fisher, based in Boston, offered pastoral scenes of cattle and water in the manner of Dutch and English painters. Fellow artists even hired him to paint animals into their own pictures.","c":"An early landscape of real accomplishment for its date and place: the cattle are handsomely drawn, the composition orderly, the mood peaceful. The picture is a pastoral in the English and Dutch manner, and it lacks the grand ambition of the Hudson River School that followed. But it shows how to make a quiet painting out of ordinary farmland, and Fisher's skill with animals is the chief attraction."},"cma:136207":{"d":"A bouquet stands in a glass vase in this tiny picture, painted on copper in 1606 and measuring about 36 by 29 centimeters. The museum notes that the flowers were costly rarities in the 1600s, painted with luminous colors and exquisite detail. Snails, butterflies, moths, dragonflies and other insects crawl and flutter around the arrangement, which makes the still life come alive. Copper gives a hard, smooth surface that lets the colors glow.","i":"Flower painting emerged as a specialty in the Netherlands around 1600, when botanical gardens and the trade in bulbs and exotic plants made rare flowers fashionable. Bosschaert worked in Middelburg, where scientific collections flourished. A bouquet like this could not exist in nature, since the flowers bloom in different seasons; the painter assembled it from separate studies. The insects remind the viewer that beauty is brief, and his sons followed him into the genre.","c":"Bosschaert's early flower pieces are among the founding documents of the genre, with a clarity of drawing and color that later painters made more elaborate but rarely more convincing. The small scale and copper support give the picture a jewel-like finish. Where he is limited is in composition, which is symmetrical and static: the bouquet is a catalog more than a drama. But the delicacy is real, and the insects add charm."},"met:922227":{"d":"A bouquet of flowers stands in a roemer, the broad-bellied Dutch wine glass with a hollow, studded stem, in a small vertical panel of about 29 by 19 centimeters, painted around 1620. This was among the last years of Bosschaert's life, for he died in 1621. The picture belongs to the genre he helped found, in which a symmetrical bouquet is shown with great precision against a plain ground.","i":"Bosschaert helped establish the flower still life in the Netherlands in the early 1600s, when rare flowers were status symbols and objects of scientific curiosity. A roemer was a common drinking glass, and its use as a vase links the luxury of flowers to the pleasure of wine and the passing of time. Such pictures can be read as delight in nature's variety, or as quiet reminders that cut flowers fade.","c":"Bosschaert's late flower pieces maintain the clear, orderly manner of his early work. The roemer is a nice touch, adding reflection and transparency to the display, and the small panel invites close inspection of leaf and petal. The limits are those of the formula: symmetry and a lack of dramatic incident. As a demonstration of what a small, patient picture can do, it succeeds."},"nga:59934":{"d":"A young woman sits on an ornate yellow-brown chair, her body slightly turned to the left and her head toward the viewer, in a white gown with wide sleeves and a light greenish-yellow cloth draped over her shoulder and legs. She holds a small sprig in her right hand and reaches with her left toward a round red vase of pink, cream and pale yellow flowers, over which two butterflies float. A dark red curtain hangs behind her, with a sliver of blue sky in the corner.","i":"Early American portraits like this were painted by unknown limners who borrowed poses, drapery and props from English mezzotint portraits of fashionable women imported to the colonies. The loose gown and draped cloth echo the undress dress of baroque portraits, which gave sitters a timeless air. Flowers could suggest beauty and the brevity of youth, and butterflies the soul or life's transience, though the exact intent here is unrecorded.","c":"Naive but ambitious: the unknown painter reaches for baroque elegance and gets a flat, decorative charm. The pose is awkward and the proportions stiff, but the red curtain, the flowers and the small face are arranged with a sure sense of pattern, giving the picture a presence beyond its skill. It is a valuable document of early colonial taste and shows that the ambition for refinement preceded the technique."},"nga:56738":{"d":"A boy of the Beekman family is the single sitter of this large canvas, about 131 by 104 centimeters, painted about 1720 by an unidentified American artist. The size suggests a full or three-quarter-length figure, and the title links him to one of the leading families of New York, of Dutch descent. It is a portrait of a child as a family asset, meant to hang in a house of standing.","i":"The Beekman family was among the leading families of New York and the Hudson Valley, descended from Dutch settlers, and their portraits belong to a tradition in which children were painted as small adults in fine costume. The unknown painter was probably a local limner who borrowed poses from prints. Children's portraits recorded survival in a time of high child mortality and asserted a family's continuity.","c":"Hudson Valley portraits of this period have an unmistakable flat, decorative charm: strong silhouettes, careful costume and a disarming directness. The painter's weakness is anatomy and space; the strength is the fidelity to dress and the force of the pose. The anonymity of the artist and the name of the family make it a historical document as much as an art object."},"nga:45958":{"d":"A man, named Jonathan Bentham, is the single sitter of this canvas, about 117 by 89 centimeters, painted about 1725 by an unidentified American artist. The size is generous, large enough to show a man's face, a good part of his coat and perhaps a hand. It is a colonial portrait of a man of some standing, made when the American colonies were beginning to commission likenesses in numbers.","i":"Portraits of colonial men around 1725 recorded status: merchants, officials and ministers were shown in wigs and fine coats, and local artists copied poses from English mezzotints. Such portraits were hung in the main rooms of houses to assert the family's place and continuity. The sitter's name has survived with the picture, but the painter, like many early American portraitists, has not.","c":"Anonymous colonial portraits like this one are valued less for polish than for presence: the face is the point, and the painter's stiffness can make it more forceful, not less. The costume and pose follow English models without the English finish. It is a plain, solid record of a man of the 1720s, and its anonymity of authorship is part of the interest."},"nga:42495":{"d":"A young man stands beside a large, ornate vase of colorful flowers on a terrace, with dark hair, rosy cheeks, an elegant red outfit, a dark green cape, a large piece of jewelry on his chest and red shoes. Architectural elements and a large flowing green drapery fill the background. The canvas is wide, about 52 by 66 centimeters, and was painted about 1730 by an unidentified American artist.","i":"Colonial portraits of the 1730s often used a stock apparatus of columns, drapery and vases of flowers, copied from European prints to lend dignity. Here a young man in rich dress is set on a terrace like a character in a courtly pageant. The jewelry and cape may reflect a fashionable costume convention rather than everyday dress. The painting reveals a taste for elegance among colonial families, with prints as the unseen teachers.","c":"The painting's charm is the mismatch of ambition and means: a baroque courtly scene rendered with a colonial painter's flat, bright patterns. The figure and the vase carry similar weight, as if both were props in a decorative scheme, and the effect is more delightful than convincing. It is a window into the provincial taste for European glamour."},"nga:45959":{"d":"A boy in a blue coat is the single subject of this canvas, about 122 by 84 centimeters, painted about 1730 by an unidentified American artist. The upright format and the title make it a child's portrait of the type common in colonial America, where boys were painted in adult costume, and the canvas is large enough to show a good length of the figure.","i":"In the early eighteenth century children were painted in versions of adult clothing and poses, copied from European prints, because portraits recorded status and family continuity as much as childhood itself. A portrait of a boy asserted the survival of an heir at a time when many children died young. The painter, unidentified, was one of many provincial limners who supplied such pictures to families.","c":"Colonial child portraits like this have a bold, flat appeal, and the strong silhouette and simplified shapes give them a presence that smoother work lacks. The anatomy is stiff and the space uncertain, but the directness is memorable. It is best valued as a vivid, anonymous witness to how the colonies saw their children, though its technique is limited."},"nga:50692":{"d":"Three men walk closely together toward the viewer on a brown dirt path between two bodies of water. The central figure, with long hair, a mustache and a beard, wears a pink robe; at his sides are a man with short brown hair, a brown robe, a blue cloak and a cane, and a man with curly hair and a beard in a brown cap and cloak. All are barefoot. A dilapidated wooden fence, tall trees and distant red-roofed buildings frame the scene.","i":"The story is told in Luke 24:13-35. On the evening of Easter, two disciples walk to Emmaus, discussing the events in Jerusalem, and are joined by a stranger who is the risen Christ, unrecognized until he breaks bread at supper. Biblical pictures were rare in Protestant colonial America, and surviving examples often copy European prints, which may explain the Dutch-looking red roofs and landscape.","c":"A modest, earnest picture in which the painter's limits are part of its charm: the figures are stocky and the landscape simplified, but the quiet intimacy of three men talking on a country road gives the Gospel scene a down-to-earth reality. The fence and village look more Dutch than Palestinian, which adds to the appeal. It is not sophisticated, but it is sincere."},"nga:59917":{"d":"A boy stands slightly turned to his right, with shoulder-length light blond hair and a fair complexion, in a red overcoat with white accents, a white cravat and black shoes. He holds a scroll in his left hand and touches a small dog standing on its hind legs with his right. Behind him are an archway or stone wall with plants growing on it, trees, and a sunrise or sunset over mountains. The canvas is about 102 by 87 centimeters.","i":"The sitter is recorded as J. M. Stolle. Children were often painted with a pet and an attribute: a scroll could suggest education or the hope of a successful adult, and a dog loyalty and the innocent pleasures of childhood. The adult-style coat and cravat are typical of the time, when children were dressed as small adults. Portraits like this were made about 1734 to 1735 by provincial painters who relied on prints for their poses and settings.","c":"A lively provincial portrait in which the dog standing on its hind legs steals the show. The painter builds a courtly setting, with arch, mountains and sunset, out of borrowed pieces, and the result has a naive charm rather than refinement. The boy's pose is stiff, but the details of the red coat and the trained dog give the picture warmth, and its anonymity of authorship is typical of its era."},"nga:42494":{"d":"Two figures meet near a well. A woman stands holding a pitcher, with fair skin, a round face, long dark hair tied back, a golden yellow dress, a blue underskirt and red shoes; a bearded man in a light blue robe sits on the well's edge. A pulley with a rope hangs above the well, trees stand on either side, and houses and rolling hills lie in the distance under a blue sky with soft clouds. The canvas is about 52 by 66 centimeters.","i":"The story is in John 4:5-42. Tired from a journey, Jesus sits by Jacob's well in Samaria at midday and asks a Samaritan woman for a drink, then tells her about living water and reveals that he knows her life. Jews and Samaritans were enemies, so the exchange breaks barriers. A colonial painter probably adapted a European print for this scene, setting it in a landscape of European houses and hills.","c":"Naive in drawing but clear in storytelling: the woman with the pitcher and the seated Christ are placed so that the conversation is the whole picture. The bright color and the rolling background have a decorative appeal, and the sincerity of the telling compensates for the stiffness of the figures. It is an engaging example of how biblical subjects were adapted in early America."},"nga:56747":{"d":"A woman, named Susanna Truax, is the single sitter of this canvas, about 92 by 73 centimeters, painted about 1740 by an unidentified American artist. The canvas is large enough for a face, costume and perhaps a hand or a prop. The surname Truax, of Dutch and French Walloon origin, is associated with the Albany and Schenectady region of New York. A portrait like this would have been made for her family, as a record of her place in a close-knit community.","i":"Portraits of women in colonial America around 1740 recorded rank, marriage and family. In the Dutch communities of the Hudson and Mohawk valleys, such pictures were commissioned from local painters who adapted poses and costume from European prints. The sitter's name has survived with the painting while the painter's has not, which is the common fate of early American portraits.","c":"A plain, forthright portrait in the provincial manner, valued for presence rather than finish. The simplified forms and bold patterning of dress make a strong impression, and the sitter's character comes through the stiffness. It lacks the sophistication of urban portraiture, but it records a person with directness and has its own integrity."},"nga:59903":{"d":"A young girl, seen from the waist up and turned slightly to her left, looks out with a calm expression. She has dark curly hair, rosy cheeks and dark eyes, and wears a pink gown with lace-trimmed sleeves, a ruffled neckline and a string of pearls. She holds a small bird in her right hand. A dark oval and a circular border enclose the portrait like a painted frame. The canvas is about 67 by 61 centimeters, and the title gives her name only as a possibility.","i":"A child holding a bird is a stock image in eighteenth-century portraits of children: the bird can be a pet, a sign of innocence or an emblem of the soul. The painted oval frame, a convention from European portrait prints and miniatures, adds dignity. The tentative identification, Margaret Robins, shows how names come loose from colonial portraits as they pass through families, dealers and sales.","c":"Charming and a little stiff: the pearls and pink gown are painted with loving care, while the face has the simple, rounded structure typical of provincial painters. The painted oval adds an air of the miniature, as though a small portrait had been enlarged. The sitter's grave calm is the strength, and the uncertain identity adds to its wistfulness. It is not a major work, but it has a quiet presence."},"nga:59928":{"d":"A young female sitter, shown full length, stands in a red and gold dress with white lace trim and floral patterns, a cap and a pearl necklace, with her shoes visible beneath the hem. She has a pale complexion, arched eyebrows, a small mouth and dark eyes, holds a dark object in one hand and puts the other at her waist. The background is dark, with flowers at the left and a pattern of roses at the right. The canvas is about 134 by 90 centimeters.","i":"Full-length portraits of young sitters in elaborate adult dress were a feature of Dutch-American portraiture in New York and the Hudson Valley in the 1730s to 1750s, painted by anonymous limners who borrowed from European prints. Catalyntje is a Dutch form of Catherine, and the name points to the Dutch communities of the region. The portrait recorded a family's status and, if the sitter is a child, as the museum's own cataloguing suggests, her survival in a time of high child mortality.","c":"Stiff, flat and memorable: the painter treats the dress as a surface of red and gold pattern, with the small face as a delicate detail. The flowers and roses behind her fill the dark ground with decoration. The proportions are not naturalistic, but the insistence on costume gives the picture authority, and its bold pattern and solemnity carry the effect. It is a fine example of the Hudson Valley's decorative portrait tradition."},"nga:59939":{"d":"A woman, shown from the waist up, sits in a wooden chair against a dark ground, her body angled to the right and her dark gray eyes turned toward the viewer. She wears a ruby-red dress with gold-looped cuffs and a towering pleated white cap, with a pearl choker, a silver cross-shaped earring and ruffles, and a mole sits above the corner of her mouth. She unrolls a sheet of music, her elbow resting on a narrow black keyboard.","i":"The scroll of music and the keyboard mark the sitter as a woman of accomplishment: in the late eighteenth century keyboard skill was a mark of refinement, and music-making appeared in portraits as a sign of cultivation and domestic virtue. The large white cap and gray hair mark a mature matron, and her individual features, the mole and the double chin, are recorded without flattery. The painter is unidentified.","c":"An engaging picture because the sitter is allowed to look like herself: the sidelong glance, the mole, the cleft chin and the great white cap, pleated like a piece of architecture. The red dress and the near-black ground give a strong, simple design. The painter's skill is in textile and face more than in space, and the truthfulness of the likeness is the charm. It is a vivid portrait of an ordinary, formidable woman."},"nga:3206":{"d":"A man with short brown hair and a firm expression is shown from the waist up, seated on a carved wooden chair. He wears a brown coat, a decorated vest with floral embroidery, a white cravat and lace cuffs. His left arm lies in his lap and his right hand is tucked inside his vest. A curtain drapes the dark background, and a small table to the right holds a book or document. The canvas is about 102 by 84 centimeters, painted about 1783.","i":"The hand tucked into the waistcoat was a gesture of calm self-possession that English portrait painters borrowed from classical oratory, and it became standard in portraits of gentlemen. The book or document on the table indicates literacy and business, and the carved chair and embroidered vest display means. The National Gallery also has a portrait of Jane Cutler Doane of about the same date and size, and the two probably form a pair.","c":"A plain, forceful portrait. The painter's strengths are the embroidered vest and the carving of the chair, and the face has a straightforward firmness. The anonymity of the painter, the stiffness of the anatomy and the shallow space are typical of provincial work after the Revolution, but they do not hide the sitter's determination. It is an honest likeness, more memorable for its candor than for its finish."},"nga:3207":{"d":"A woman sits in a red upholstered chair, her dark brown curly hair falling over her left shoulder. She wears a white dress with a green ribbon tied around the waist, and a dark shawl with lace-trimmed sleeves is draped over her shoulders. Her expression is composed, with softly smiling lips and large, slightly downturned eyes. The canvas is about 102 by 84 centimeters and was painted about 1783 by an unidentified American artist.","i":"The white dress with a sash was the fashion of the 1780s for loosely cut, natural gowns, simpler than earlier hooped silks, and curls falling over a shoulder were a fashionable touch. In colonial and early republican America, portraits of a wife and husband recorded marriage as a partnership and the household's standing. The title gives what is probably both her maiden name, Cutler, and her married name.","c":"A quiet, appealing portrait: the red chair sets off the white dress, and the soft smile gives the face an air of friendliness. As with many anonymous American portraits, the anatomy is simplified and the space shallow, but the face is individual enough to hold a viewer, and the combination of red, white and green is pleasing. It reads well beside the firmer portrait of Elisha Doane, probably her husband."},"nga:42489":{"d":"The view looks across a channel toward two landmasses labeled Boston and Charles Town. Four three-masted ships lie in the blue harbor, three with sails set and smoke billowing from their sides, and rows of brown houses line Boston. Two arrow-shaped missiles arc toward Charles Town, where gray smoke rises from orange fire; rowboats land soldiers near hundreds of men drawn up and firing at each other. The canvas is about 53 by 71 centimeters, dated 1783 or after.","i":"The picture shows the Battle of Bunker Hill on 17 June 1775, an early battle of the American Revolution in which British troops stormed American positions on the Charlestown peninsula across the harbor from Boston. The British bombarded and burned Charlestown, and though they took the hill they suffered heavy losses, which gave Americans confidence. A labeled bird's-eye view like a map-picture was typical of battle depictions, and a painting made in or after 1783, when the war ended, is an early memorial.","c":"It is a naive picture in the best sense: map and painting at once, clear in its geography, lively in its smoke and flame. It does not convey the terror of battle or the individual soldier, and the figures are tiny. But the clarity of its information and its decorative brightness make it a likable popular memorial. As art it is modest; as an early American historical image it is valuable."},"nga:42449":{"d":"A woman sits against a tree trunk in a reclining pose, her head resting on one hand and her eyes closed or cast down. She has delicate features, long curly hair tied with a headband and a flowing white gown with folds, and a small dog sits beside her. Behind are lush trees, blue sky and a small carriage in the distance. It is a small picture on silk in watercolor, pen and ink, about 38 by 29 centimeters, dated about 1790.","i":"The title Maria probably refers to Maria of Moulines, the heartbroken girl in Laurence Sterne's A Sentimental Journey (1768), driven to madness by love and found by the roadside with her dog, an image of sentimental sorrow beloved in the late eighteenth century. Silk painting of this kind was a typical accomplishment taught at girls' academies in America in the 1790s. The subject allowed the maker to display feeling as well as skill.","c":"A small, tender picture whose charm lies in the unforced sentiment and the delicate handling of the white gown and the soft landscape. The figure is simply drawn and the proportions naive, but the pose of grief, of a kind popularized by British sentimental prints, is expressed gracefully. Such silk paintings are fragile documents of how American women saw themselves and their reading. A prettiness that borders on cliché is both its limit and its appeal."},"nga:42478":{"d":"A figure in a blue coat over a striped shirt sits at a wooden table, leaning forward with one arm on a sheet of paper while dipping a quill into an inkwell; a small knife and a large brown book with a black top hat lie on the table. In front, a boy in a blue coat and a girl in a red dress play on a patterned floor, each holding an end of a long string on which a white bird perches. Orange-and-pink scrollwork frames the scene against black. The canvas is large, about 102 by 135 centimeters.","i":"Domestic scenes of work and play are rare in early American painting, which was dominated by portraits. This one pairs a writing figure with children amusing themselves with a bird on a string, a childhood pastime of the period, and the museum's theme is amusement. The painted scrollwork frame suggests that the picture was made to be set into a wall, perhaps over a fireplace, as part of a room's decoration. The medium is an aqueous paint, not oil.","c":"An unusual and delightful picture: the sense of the room is odd, with walls and floor curving as if seen through a lens, and the perspective is naive, but the narrative of a busy parent and playing children is charming and rare. The painter delights in pattern: stripes, floor, scrollwork. The matte, water-based medium gives the surface a flat brightness. It is a primitive but personal vision of domestic life."},"nga:52959":{"d":"A boy with a basket of fruit is the subject of this small canvas, about 57 by 44 centimeters, painted about 1790 by an unidentified American artist. The title gives only the boy and the fruit, so the picture is best read as a child's portrait with a prop, in which a basket of produce adds color and a hint of a still life. The modest size suits a household's private rooms.","i":"Children in portraits of the late eighteenth century were often shown with fruit, flowers or pets, which stood for innocence, abundance and the sweetness of childhood. A basket of fruit also allowed a painter to show off a still-life detail, and it could hint at the harvest or the prosperity of the family. The anonymous painter was one of many provincial portraitists who supplied such pictures to American families.","c":"Such pictures succeed or fail on the boy's face and on the freshness of the fruit. Provincial painters of 1790 usually did better with objects than with anatomy, so the basket and its contents are likely to be the most convincing element. The picture is a modest, pleasant record of a child in a time when many were not recorded at all, and it should be valued for that humble role."},"nga:59933":{"d":"A woman sits holding a young child on her lap, one arm wrapped securely around the child and the other hand holding a small flower. She has a fair, oval face, straight dark eyebrows and light eyes, and wears a bonnet with lace and a large blue ribbon, a light gown with a high neckline, a black choker and a wide black belt. The child wears a lacy bonnet and a dress with a blue sash. They sit against a dark ground. The canvas is about 90 by 69 centimeters.","i":"Mother-and-child portraits of about 1790 reflect the Enlightenment's new emphasis on maternal care: writers such as Rousseau urged mothers to nurse and raise their own children, giving the pair moral prestige. The flower may stand for the child's innocence or the fragility of life, and white clothing suited the fashion for simple, natural dress. The painter is unidentified, and the sitters' names have not come down.","c":"A tender, simplified portrait in which the sitters' pale dresses and bonnets set against a dark ground make a strong, simple design. The faces are doll-like, with little individuality, but the way the mother holds the child gives the picture warmth. It is a fine example of the sentiment of the 1790s, rendered in a naive manner, and the very plainness of the means helps the feeling."},"nga:59935":{"d":"A young girl sits at a table holding a box in one hand and arranging dominoes with the other. She has short brown hair with a straight fringe, fair skin, dark eyebrows and dark eyes, and wears a white dress with wide sleeves and a pink sash. Behind her a window shows trees and a partly cloudy sky. The canvas is about 58 by 47 centimeters and was painted about 1790 by an unidentified American artist.","i":"Dominoes had spread through Europe in the eighteenth century as a fashionable table game, and a child at play with them marks a family with leisure and modern amusements. Portraits of children with toys or games were popular in the late 1700s, when ideas about childhood were changing and play came to be seen as natural and good. The window view adds a note of outdoor air to the interior.","c":"A pleasant, unpretentious portrait whose charm is in the activity: the girl is absorbed in her game, and the dominoes give the painter a pretext for small, careful touches. The white dress and pink sash are painted simply, and the figure has a lively concentration that stiffer portraits lack. It is best enjoyed as a record of a child's occupation, and a sign of how portrait painters began to catch the moment."},"nga:59938":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, seated in a chair with his body turned slightly to the right and his gaze directed forward. He has a fair complexion, large dark eyes, a straight nose and short gray hair, and wears a dark coat with white buttons, a blue waistcoat and a white cravat. One hand rests on his lap and the other holds a letter or document. Behind him are green-and-red floral wallpaper, blue drapes and a window with a tree and water. The painting is about 79 by 73 centimeters.","i":"Wallpaper was a sign of status in the 1790s, and placing a physician in a well-furnished room with a window view signals his standing. The letter or document suggests literacy, correspondence and professional authority; doctors of the period trained by apprenticeship and were respected local figures. The unusual construction, oil on paper mounted on board and then on canvas, suggests a portable or itinerant practice. The sitter is also recorded as Philemon Dorr Tracy.","c":"An honest and likable portrait: the doctor meets the viewer with a steady gaze, and the painter delights in the pattern of the wallpaper, which competes with the sitter for attention. As in much provincial painting, the space is shallow and the modeling simple, but the face gives an impression of character. The picture does not flatter, and its direct, businesslike presence is its strength."},"nga:42492":{"d":"A three-story white manor house with a rust-red roof and a spire-like lantern stands across a bright green lawn, with wings on both sides. Gentlemen in blue-and-yellow uniforms, women in white dresses and a gray dog stand on the grass; four people with brown skin, three carrying a shovel, a rake and a staff, work near the house, and a carriage with four horses enters at the right. A caption names it A View of Mount Vernon, the Seat of General Washington.","i":"Mount Vernon, on the Potomac in Virginia, was Washington's plantation and home. From the 1790s, prints and paintings of the estate spread the image of the general's seat as a national shrine, and a painting like this is probably based on a print. The visitors show the house's gentility, while the four people with tools were presumably enslaved laborers, since the estate depended on enslaved work; more than three hundred enslaved people lived there when Washington died in 1799.","c":"A naive but information-rich picture: the painter places the house on a stage of green, populates it with a little drama of visitors, officers and workers, and gives the whole a decorative clarity. Perspective and figure drawing are childlike, but the document is valuable, not least because it records, without comment, the labor that sustained the estate. That candor, perhaps unintended, is its most revealing feature."},"nga:1107":{"d":"A woman sits on a red chair studded with gold, slightly turned to the left, her arms crossed over her lap. She has a fair complexion, rosy cheeks, slightly smiling lips and large dark eyes. Her curly brown hair is adorned with a pearl band and a light blue ribbon, and she wears a long-sleeved white dress with a ruffled neckline and a light greenish-blue sash. The background is a muted green with a hint of a door or paneling. The canvas is about 92 by 72 centimeters.","i":"The high-waisted white dress with a sash was the new neoclassical fashion of the 1790s, which replaced hooped silks with simple, flowing lines in imitation of French taste, and loosely curled hair with a ribbon band belonged to the same look. The sitter is recorded under several names, Cruger, Yates and Walton, which suggests that she married more than once. The family names point to well-connected New York circles.","c":"A fresh, pleasing portrait whose simplified drawing and soft, even colors suit the sitter's youth. The red chair with its gold studs is a strong accent against the quiet green ground, and the white dress is arranged with clear folds. The anonymous painter is not a master of anatomy, but the eyes and smile give the face a warmth that lingers. It is a good example of the fashionable simplicity of the 1790s."},"nga:43637":{"d":"A girl, named in the title as Miss Daggett of New Haven, Connecticut, and possibly Amelia Martha, is the subject of this canvas, about 92 by 72 centimeters, painted about 1795 by an unidentified American artist. The size is large enough for the face, costume and perhaps a hand or a prop. The title shows that her family name and her town were remembered, while her first name remains uncertain.","i":"New Haven in the 1790s was a port and a college town, home to prosperous merchants, ministers and professionals who commissioned portraits of themselves and their children. A portrait of a girl recorded her place in a family and, as she grew, her prospects in marriage. The tentative first name shows how identities slip from old portraits as they pass through families and dealers.","c":"A modest portrait in the manner of the American provincial painters of the 1790s: simple forms, clear color and a direct address to the viewer. Without the polish of the best city portraits, it relies on the sitter's presence and on the pleasing flatness of its design. Its tentative identification adds a trace of mystery. It is a likable record of a New England girlhood, though not a distinguished work."},"nga:45860":{"d":"Twelve men, women, children and babies are arranged on and around a slate-blue couch in a wide canvas of about 49 by 65 centimeters. A man in black sits at the left, legs crossed, with a book in his lap and a slight smile; two women beside him each hold a smiling baby in a white cap. A young man stands behind with a hand tucked into his gray jacket, and boys in blue suits, one with a bow, and girls in white dresses with rose-pink sashes fill the front. The faces are simply painted and somewhat generic.","i":"Family group portraits were costlier than single likenesses, and this one records a large household of twelve in the 1790s. The clothing shows respectability: black suit, white caps and aprons, white dresses with sashes. The book and the archery bow suggest learning and genteel pastimes, and the arrangement on a couch tries to fit babies, children and adults into a legible whole. Such pictures were made for the family to treasure.","c":"A cheerful, crowded picture whose charm is its naivety: the faces are similar, the proportions awkward, the figures arranged like paper dolls, but the repeated whites and blues and the animated gestures give the group a lively rhythm. The painter faced a hard problem, twelve sitters on a small canvas, and solved it with the simplicity of a quilt design. It is a touching record of a large family, strong in design rather than in individual portraiture."},"nga:43638":{"d":"A green landscape is seen from afar: large white buildings stand partly hidden by tall, leafy trees, and small figures walk on the grass, play games or sit together. On the far left, two cows or horses graze in an open field. A palette of dark greens, browns and olives is lit by a sky that is dark blue at the top and fades to yellow and peach at the horizon. The panel is wide, about 69 by 116 centimeters, a format suited to hanging over a doorway or fireplace.","i":"The museum's theme is the United Kingdom, which suggests that the scene was taken from an English view; American painters of the late eighteenth century often copied English prints of country houses and parks, since few of them had seen such estates. The little figures at leisure show the ideal of the genteel life in a landscaped park. Pictures like this brought a vision of European refinement into American parlors.","c":"A picture that charms by mixing two worlds: an English country estate and the flat patterning of a provincial American painter. The tonal range is dark and rich, the figures minute and the buildings simplified, and the whole has the air of a stage set. Without the grace of the English originals, it shows how American taste reached toward them. It is a pleasing curiosity rather than a statement."},"nga:52246":{"d":"Twenty-one dogs run and eight men on horseback gallop from right to left across a grassy field, most of the dogs white with tawny spots. At the back some riders raise riding crops; a ninth man runs on foot, hat in hand, and another chases a rearing horse. About a dozen waterfowl float in an oval lake, and beyond are houses with copper-colored roofs and smoking chimneys. An oversized brown rabbit sits beside the lake, out of scale, and the whole tone is dark and murky. The canvas is about 66 by 127 centimeters.","i":"Hunting with hounds on horseback was an English gentry sport that colonial Americans, especially in the Southern colonies and around New York, adopted in the eighteenth century, and English sporting prints taught painters how to show it. The oversized rabbit may be the quarry, enlarged so that the story is clear. A hunt scene told a viewer that the owner shared the pastimes of the English country gentleman.","c":"The scale joke of the giant rabbit sums up the picture's naive delight: everything is subordinated to the pleasure of telling the story, with dogs and riders tumbling across the canvas in a decorative rhythm. The dark, murky tone weighs on it, and the space is not convincing, but the sheer energy of twenty-one dogs and eight riders is infectious. It is a fine example of the colonial taste for English field sports."},"nga:52247":{"d":"Two horsemen and a pack of ten dogs race across a brown ridge overlooking a river in a long, narrow canvas, about 49 by 141 centimeters, chasing an unidentified brown animal from right to left. The front rider wears a peacock-blue coat and the second a pewter-gray one; the dogs are mostly white with tan or black spots. Groups of green trees, a windmill on a far hill, blue mountains and a wide pale river with tiny ships and riverside buildings fill the distance.","i":"The long, narrow shape suggests that the picture was made as an overmantel or overdoor decoration, a common use for such scenes. Hunting with hounds is English, while the windmill, the river and the harbor with small ships recall Dutch landscape, so the painter probably combined stock motifs from several printed sources. The picture offered a gentleman's idea of country sport set against a backdrop of trade and prosperity.","c":"Wide and narrow, the picture has the fresh logic of a frieze: horsemen and dogs race in a line across a ridge, and the river and mountains stretch away. The painter lacks the knowledge of anatomy and perspective but delights in pattern, with the dappled dogs and the blue distance. As a whole it has the simplicity of a toy theater, and the effect is lively and decorative, with real charm."},"nga:42452":{"d":"A man, a woman, four children and a black-and-white dog stand and sit in a row in a wallpapered room with a checkerboard floor, on a wide canvas about 97 by 128 centimeters dated 1800. The man at left, in a tall black hat, black coat and buckled breeches, tucks a hand into his jacket and clasps a girl's hand. At right the woman in a bonnet sits with a baby and a toddler while a child swings a ball on a string for the leaping dog. An open door, a window and two caged birds fill the room.","i":"In 1800 a family group in the home was a statement of prosperity and domestic order: wallpaper, painted floor, caged birds and upholstered chairs all point to a comfortable household. The man's hand-in-coat pose and tall hat show gentility, while the children's play with the dog adds life to what might otherwise be a stiff parade. The museum notes the flattened space and the man's head, which seems too large. The painter is unidentified.","c":"Naivety is the picture's strength: the flattened perspective and awkward proportions are almost beside the point because of the energy of detail, the diamond wallpaper, the leaping dog and the ball on a string. It looks more like a quilt than a window, and the eye moves from pattern to pattern with pleasure. The tight composition and the rhythm of black, white and yellow make it a most charming American family portrait of its era."},"nga:42466":{"d":"A girl feeds a bird in this small canvas, about 56 by 43 centimeters, painted about 1800 by an unidentified American artist. The title gives only the girl and the bird, so the picture is best read as a child's portrait with an activity, in which a pet bird gives the sitter something to do and the painter a lively detail to add to a figure.","i":"Children's portraits around 1800 often show a pet or a task, as ideas about childhood shifted toward gentleness and play. A girl caring for a bird suggested kindness and domestic virtue, qualities admired in the young, and a pet bird was also a sign of a comfortable home. Many such pictures were painted by itinerant limners who traveled between households, and their names have often been lost.","c":"Pictures of this type live by the freshness of a small incident: a child absorbed in a gentle act. A painter of limited training gets away with much when the sitter has something to do, and the stiffness of pose is less noticeable. The picture should be valued as a modest, appealing record of a child's occupation, even if the technique is plain."},"nga:42501":{"d":"The start of a hunt is the subject of this wide canvas, about 88 by 139 centimeters, painted about 1800 by an unidentified American artist. It is evidently the first of a pair, for The End of the Hunt in the same collection has nearly the same size and date. The picture belongs to the vogue for English field sports among gentlemen in the early republic.","i":"English sporting prints often came in sets that told a hunt from the meet to the kill, and American painters borrowed the idea, making pairs like this one that narrate the sport as a sequence. Hunting with horses and hounds marked the owner as a gentleman who shared the habits of the English country squire, and a pair of hunt pictures suited a dining room or hall where men gathered.","c":"The pleasure of a pair lies in the story it tells, and this opening scene is half of a story whose conclusion comes in The End of the Hunt. The painter's gifts are for pattern and energy rather than for anatomy and space, but a hunt is a forgiving subject for such gifts. It is best seen together with its companion, as a small comedy of the chase."},"nga:42502":{"d":"Three riders on horseback jump a low fence of thin wooden logs, as does a line of dogs. One rider blows a horn, and another has fallen from his horse and sits on the ground near the lower right corner. Beyond the fence lies a yellow field with two red buildings at its edge, toward which more dogs and riders head. Green trees, gray and brown hills and a blue sky with fluffy clouds complete the scene. The canvas is about 88 by 137 centimeters.","i":"The End of the Hunt closes a pair with The Start of the Hunt, and English sporting prints often came in sets that traced a hunt from beginning to end. The fallen rider adds a comic note, the kind of mishap that was part of a hunting story. In the early republic, hunt pictures told viewers that the owner shared the pastimes of the English country gentleman.","c":"The fallen rider is the picture's best joke, and the line of dogs and horses leaping the fence gives it a lively rhythm. The perspective is naive and the anatomy of the animals is approximate, but the clear colors and the narrative energy carry the scene. It is a charming and humorous provincial work, more at ease with action than with finish."},"nga:52961":{"d":"A man, named Samuel Eells, is the single sitter of this canvas, about 111 by 84 centimeters, painted about 1800 by an unidentified American artist. The size is generous, large enough for a face, a good part of the figure and perhaps a hand or a prop. It is a portrait of a citizen of the early republic, made to hang in his home as a record of his place in the community.","i":"In the years around 1800 portraits became accessible to a broader American public: farmers, merchants and craftsmen, as well as the elite, could commission a likeness from a local or itinerant painter. The sitter's name has survived with the picture, though the painter's has not, and the portrait expresses the dignity of ordinary citizens in a young country that valued independence and respectability.","c":"Pictures like this are valued for their directness: a plain face, a simple design and a lack of flattery. They lack the polish of the best city portraits, and the painter's anatomy is likely to be approximate, but they compensate with strong outlines and presence. It is a sound representative of the provincial American portrait of 1800, honest more than brilliant."},"nga:34092":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, standing slightly to the right but facing forward and meeting the viewer's eye. He has a fair complexion with a reddish hue on his cheeks, a broad face and short, dark brown hair receding from the forehead. He wears a dark double-breasted coat with large buttons and a white cravat, and his right hand holds what appears to be a walking stick or cane. Behind him are dark drapery and the spine of a book on a small bookshelf. The panel is about 77 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The cane marks the sitter as a gentleman, and the books behind him as a man of literacy and learning, in a conventional vocabulary of status that early nineteenth-century American portraits borrowed from English models. The double-breasted coat with large buttons was fashionable in the 1810s. Neither the sitter nor the painter is recorded, which was a common fate of portraits that left their families.","c":"A forthright portrait whose strengths are the plain, ruddy face and the steady gaze. The painter treats the dark coat and background as a simple mass, which makes the face the focus, and the book and cane add just enough narrative. The modeling is flat, but the impression of a real, self-possessed man is strong. It is a good example of the sober, direct provincial manner."},"nga:34101":{"d":"A mother and her child are the subject of this large canvas, about 154 by 105 centimeters, painted about 1810. The size is generous enough for a nearly life-size seated pair, and the title names no one, so the pair stand for the relationship rather than for individuals. The attribution is uncertain between American and British artists, and the picture belongs to a tradition of tender mother-and-child portraits that drew on the grand manner of Reynolds and Gainsborough.","i":"Mother-and-child portraits of about 1810 reflect the ideal of the devoted mother that had grown since the Enlightenment, which saw motherhood as a moral and civic duty. The uncertainty whether the painter was American or British shows how closely American portraiture followed British models, so that distinguishing them can be hard. A large canvas like this was a major commission, hung in a principal room.","c":"At this scale the picture has an ambition above the ordinary run of provincial portraits: the large format asks for command of pose and drapery, and the subject invites tenderness. How far it succeeds depends on the eye; the type is more often sweet than searching. It is a handsome, accomplished picture of the Regency era, and an interesting problem for connoisseurs who weigh whether American or British hands made it."},"nga:43448":{"d":"A man is shown in profile from the waist up, with a prominent nose, a slightly receding hairline and dark hair combed back. He wears a dark coat, a reddish-brown waistcoat and a white high-collared shirt, and he is set within an oval against a plain, muted reddish-brown ground inside a dark border. The picture is tiny, about 25 by 20 centimeters, painted in oil on wood mounted on pressed wood about 1810.","i":"Profile portraits were fashionable in the years around 1800, when the physiognotrace, a device for tracing a sitter's profile, made cheap likenesses possible and shadow portraits became a craze in America. A painted profile in an oval frame borrows the look of the cameo and the silhouette, and suggests classical dignity. The sitter, Wellington Van Reid, has a companion portrait of Jane L. Van Reid of the same size and date.","c":"The profile format reduces the portrait to its essentials: a firm line, a clear shape and a few well-chosen colors. The painter's work is plain and reserved, and the sitter's nose and hairline give the face individuality. There is little room for flattery in a profile, which is part of its honesty. As a small, direct likeness it is effective, and it makes a good pair with its companion."},"nga:43449":{"d":"A woman is shown in profile from the waist up, with distinct features, dark hair under a white bonnet, a brown dress and a white fichu. She holds an open book in her left hand. A muted red oval sits within a darker rectangle behind her. The picture is tiny, about 25 by 20 centimeters, painted in oil on wood mounted on pressed wood about 1810, and it matches the portrait of Wellington Van Reid in size, date and design.","i":"The open book in the hand marks a woman of literacy and piety, qualities prized in early republican women, who were expected to educate their children and to read the Bible. Profile portraits, made popular by the physiognotrace and the silhouette, were affordable and fashionable around 1810. The sitter's name, Jane L. Van Reid, survives together with that of Wellington Van Reid, which suggests that the two portraits were meant to hang together.","c":"A plain, direct profile whose clean outline and quiet color suit the sitter's modest dress and reading. The format limits expression, and the painter's skill is in the line of the nose, the bonnet and the fichu. It is a small, sound portrait and, like its pendant, it needs to be seen as part of a pair to be fully appreciated, a record of a couple rather than of an individual."},"nga:52964":{"d":"A woman in profile faces right toward a baby held up by an unseen hand, in an oval portrait with a black rectangular border. She has a long pointed nose, thin lips and black hair styled up with a gold comb, and she wears a red dress with puffy sleeves, a lace collar, a gold pendant and hoop earrings, one hand reaching toward the baby, who wears a pale gray dress and a lacy cap. Red drapery hangs at the top left. The canvas is about 76 by 67 centimeters.","i":"The title, The Proud Mother, makes the picture less a likeness than a type: the pleasure of a mother showing her child. Around 1810 motherhood was celebrated as a woman's central role and a source of moral influence, and pictures of mothers with children were popular. The unseen hand holding the baby is a curious touch, a small mystery of composition that the painter did not resolve.","c":"An odd and appealing picture: the woman's profile is sharp and a little severe, the baby is a soft pale shape, and the single hand supporting the child is startling. The painter has not hidden the awkwardness of the arrangement, and the proportions are naive, but the red dress against the brown ground gives a strong color design. The sentiment is genuine, and the picture is more memorable for its oddity than for polish."},"nga:34120":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, his body facing the left of the frame but his eyes looking directly at the viewer. He has short dark hair, sideburns, dark eyes and a clean-shaven face, and wears a black coat over a white shirt and cravat, with his arms crossed in front of him. The background shades from dark to light brown with a hint of red drapery at the left. The canvas is about 68 by 54 centimeters, painted about 1815.","i":"The title offers only a tentative name, Eliab Metcalf, and the museum's classification of the subject as 'self' points to a self-portrait. A man with folded arms and a direct look was a pose of independence and thought, common in portraits of painters and professionals. If the sitter is also the painter, as that classification suggests, the picture is a small act of self-presentation by a provincial artist.","c":"A forthright, modest portrait whose strength is the direct look and the simple, strong mass of the dark coat against the warm ground. The folded arms keep the pose compact and give the painter little to do with hands, which suits an artist of limited training. If it is indeed a self-portrait, it has the plain honesty such pictures often have, though little glamour."},"nga:42451":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, with a serious expression, wide eyes and short dark hair styled upward. He wears a dark coat and a high-collared white shirt with a decorative front, set against a solid dark teal ground. The panel measures about 59 by 49 centimeters and is dated 1815. The title gives his name only as a possibility, Harlan Page, and the painter is unidentified.","i":"Panel portraits of this kind were painted quickly by local and itinerant artists in the early republic, at modest prices. The tentative name shows how identities slip from such pictures over time. The high white collar and decorative shirt front were the fashionable dress of 1815, and the plain teal ground, an unusual choice, lets the face stand out with unusual force.","c":"The wide eyes and upswept hair give the face a startled energy, and the plain dark teal ground sets it off boldly. The painter relies on contour and flat color rather than on modeling, so the face reads as a strong, simple mask. It is not a subtle portrait, but its directness and graphic force are memorable, and it is a typical strong example of the American folk portrait."},"nga:45962":{"d":"Five blond children with rosy cheeks are grouped in a room with a green wall and a patterned red-and-green carpet. A child in a blue dress with two gold necklaces sits at the center on a yellow chair holding an open book. Others in red, blue over yellow and orange stand around the chair with hands touching shoulders and heads, and the youngest, in white, sits by the chair holding a small cat. The canvas is about 121 by 106 centimeters and was painted about 1818.","i":"Group portraits of siblings show the new importance given to children in early nineteenth-century households. The book suggests learning, the cat the companionship of a household pet, and the touching hands the unity of the family. Small children of both sexes wore frocks until boys were breeched at about five or six, so the sexes can be hard to tell in such pictures. The painter is unidentified.","c":"A tightly composed picture in which touching hands link the figures like a chain. The painter relies on bright, flat colors, with red, yellow, blue and orange against green, and the decorative sense is strong. The faces are similar and expressionless, and the modeling is minimal, so the picture is more design than portraiture. But the pattern is memorable, and the family bond is conveyed through gesture rather than through faces."},"nga:42412":{"d":"A young man is shown from the chest up in strict profile, facing left, in a small oil on wood panel about 30 by 25 centimeters, painted about 1810 to 1820. He has a fair complexion, a straight nose and a strong jaw, and his brown hair is styled casually. He wears a black coat over a white high-collared shirt with ruffles, set against a solid, muted gray background.","i":"Profile portraits were fashionable in the years around 1800 to 1820, when the physiognotrace, a device for tracing a sitter's outline, made cheap likenesses possible and shadow portraits became a craze. A painted profile borrows the look of the cameo and the silhouette, and suggests classical dignity. A companion portrait of a young lady in the same size suggests that this panel and its pendant may record a couple.","c":"The profile reduces the portrait to a firm line and a few clear shapes, and the sitter's straight nose and strong jaw give the face character. There is little room for flattery in a profile, which is part of its honesty. The painter's work is plain and rather flat, but the simplicity is effective and suits the small scale. It is a modest and direct likeness, and needs to be seen with its pendant."},"nga:42413":{"d":"A young woman is shown in profile, seated with her torso turned slightly to the left, in a small oil on wood panel about 30 by 25 centimeters. She has a straight nose and closed lips, brown curls peeking from under a lace cap with a blue ribbon, and wears a dark dress with short sleeves and a white lace collar. A dark circle sits behind her against a neutral backdrop, and she holds nothing in her hands.","i":"Profile portraits of women in the early nineteenth century emphasized grace and modesty, and the lace cap, collar and ribbon were the fashionable details of a respectable young woman. The dark circle behind her acts like a medallion, linking the portrait to the classical cameo that inspired the format. Her panel matches a companion portrait of a young man, suggesting that the pair may mark an engagement or marriage.","c":"The pleasure of the picture is its clean outline and its details, the cap, curls and lace painted with evident care. A profile allows little expression, so the sitter is read through shape and costume rather than through a glance. The darker circle gives the figure a quiet emphasis. It is a modest, charming panel, strongest when seen next to its pendant, a small document of courtship fashion."},"nga:42468":{"d":"A young girl, shown from the chest up and facing slightly left, holds a small dark-haired doll in a blue gown with white lace. She has short light brown hair, dark eyes, a small nose and a closed mouth, and she wears a red dress with white lace trim and a red beaded necklace. The background is dark. The panel is about 52 by 43 centimeters, painted between about 1800 and 1820 by an unidentified artist.","i":"A doll in a child's portrait marked girlhood and its play, and also hinted at the future role of mother. Dolls were often dressed as small fashion models, so the doll's lace gown repeats the fashionable dress of the sitter herself. Portraits of children with toys became popular in the early nineteenth century as childhood came to be seen as a distinct, precious stage of life.","c":"The doll is the picture's best idea: it doubles the sitter, a small copy of the girl's own costume and a mirror of her seriousness. The dark ground and the strong red dress give the portrait a bold, simple design. The face is stylized, as in most provincial work, but the child's solemn attention to her toy is touching. It is a sweet, effective panel, with a quiet humor."},"nga:42477":{"d":"A woman sits on a wooden chair with a decorative back, facing slightly right and meeting the viewer's eye. She has fair skin, blue eyes, reddish lips and dark curly hair styled with a wave, partly covered by a brown hat like a fez. She wears a dark green garment under a light blue fringed shawl, against a plain brown ground. The picture is very small, only 23 by 18 centimeters, painted on wood about 1820.","i":"Shawls were a leading fashion of the early nineteenth century, at first imported cashmere from India and later imitated in Europe and America, and a fringed shawl marked a woman of means and taste. Hats and turbans with an Eastern look were also popular after Napoleon's Egyptian campaign. At this small size, the picture was a keepsake, a private likeness to be kept near, painted by an unidentified artist.","c":"The blue shawl gives the picture its title and its best passage, a fresh, pale blue that is the strongest note against the dark green and brown. The face is simple, with the clear blue eyes giving it presence. The small scale and flat design are typical of provincial work, and the effect is gentle and intimate. It is a charming small object more than a major portrait."},"nga:43444":{"d":"A woman in a classical gown stands upright, holding a thin pole with an American flag in one hand and a white cup in the other, while a large bald eagle flies over her and dips its head to drink. The gown has a blue bodice cut diagonally across the torso and a flowing white skirt gathered at the side to show her leg and lace-up sandals. Her hair is long and curly. The brown ground is lighter at the top. The canvas is about 76 by 51 centimeters.","i":"This is an allegory of Liberty, the personification of the new republic, shown as a goddess in classical dress feeding the eagle that symbolized the nation. Compositions of this type were popular in the early United States, and this one resembles Edward Savage's widely circulated print of 1796. Such patriotic images decorated homes, taverns and public buildings and joined the language of ancient Rome to the idea of American self-government.","c":"A naive patriotic image whose charm lies in its earnest mixture of symbols: flag, eagle, cup and classical gown. The anatomy is stiff and the eagle is more emblem than bird, but the composition is bold and clear, and the diagonal of the bodice gives movement. It lacks the sophistication of academic allegory, though it is probably more interesting than most as a record of what ordinary Americans wanted Liberty to look like."},"nga:43452":{"d":"A small head-and-shoulders portrait, only 19 by 16 centimeters, shows a man facing forward with light skin, blue eyes and short dark brown hair. He wears a black coat over a high-collared white shirt with lace detailing and a white cravat. Behind him are a red curtain with gold fringe and a row of books, and the initials A. C. appear in gold letters on the dark background. The oil on wood panel was painted about 1820 and shows Dr. Alvah Cook, as the initials suggest.","i":"The initials painted into the background are a mark of identity and ownership, a lettered signature for the sitter, matched by the title Dr. Alvah Cook. The books signal learning and the curtain borrows the drapery of grander portraits, both conventional ways of dignifying a professional man. Early American physicians trained by apprenticeship, and the title Dr. in a portrait claimed respectability and standing.","c":"A small picture with a clear idea: the sitter, his books and his monogram, arranged like a visiting card. The face is plain and direct, and the gold letters are a nice, slightly comic touch of vanity. The painter's resources are limited, but the economical design and the strong red and gold make a lively little image. It is a good example of how modest portraits asserted professional pride."},"nga:62638":{"d":"A young female sitter sits on a chair, her face turned slightly sideways and one hand on the chair back. She has fair skin, dark eyes and dark curly hair styled up with curls around the forehead, and wears a light blue dress with ruffled sleeves and collar, white lace, a decorative belt with a gold buckle, blue stockings and shoes with floral detail. She holds a small white bag and wears earrings and a blue bead necklace. The canvas is about 133 by 103 centimeters.","i":"The sitter's long name, Martha Eliza Stevens Edgar Paschall, apparently records her maiden name, Stevens, and two married names, Edgar and Paschall, a typical way for museums to record women's lives, which were often tracked through marriages. A large canvas of about 1823 with an elaborate costume was a costly commission, announcing a family's means. The accessories, belt, bag and beads, show a taste for fashion.","c":"A bold, decorative portrait in which the sky-blue dress and the many accessories are painted with evident delight. The large size gives the figure presence, and the plain brown ground and patterned floor keep the design simple. Drawing and space are naive, and the face is a little expressionless, but the picture compensates with color and detail. It is a lively record of the fashion of the 1820s, painted with sincerity."},"nga:3209":{"d":"A woman sits from the waist up, angled slightly to the left, in a dark off-the-shoulder dress with lace trim and a bow. She has fair skin and curly dark brown hair under a white bonnet with satin bows, gold earrings and a ring on her right index finger. A colorful fabric in green, yellow and red lies on her lap. The background is a plain, muted dark brown. The canvas is about 76 by 64 centimeters, painted about 1825 by an unidentified artist.","i":"The colorful fabric on her lap may be a piece of needlework or a shawl, and either would mark the sitter as a woman of domestic skill, since sewing and embroidery were central accomplishments for women of the period. The bonnet with satin bows and the off-the-shoulder neckline are fashionable details of the 1820s. As the title suggests, the sitter's name has not survived, a common fate of portraits.","c":"A pleasant, quiet portrait in which the pattern of the lap fabric supplies the picture's one burst of color against dark tones. The face is simplified, with the careful treatment of lace and bonnet doing more to give the sitter distinction. It is an honest, unspectacular picture, typical of the many portraits that supplied American families with likenesses in the 1820s."},"nga:42421":{"d":"Two people balance on a galloping brown horse in an indoor arena. A person with short curly blond hair in a pale-yellow tunic with puffy sleeves, silver trim and white tights stands on its back, one arm extended and the other raised. On the shoulder stands a much smaller person in the same costume, one leg extended and holding a blue cloth that twists in the air. The horse wears a red cloth and a silver halter. Teal fabric and red drapes hang behind, and the panel is about 40 by 48 centimeters.","i":"Equestrian circuses were playing American cities by the 1790s, and feats of riding were their chief attraction: standing, balancing and vaulting on a galloping horse. The little acrobat on the performer's shoulder was an astonishing stunt, which a painter of the 1820s would show to convey the thrill and skill of the act. The title points to the performers' trade, the work of the circus, as the picture's subject.","c":"A lively picture whose subject suits the naive painter's gifts: figures balance on a horse in a flat, decorative design, and their strong pose reads clearly. The proportions are not realistic, and the horse is more a symbol of speed than an anatomical study, but the vivid costume and the tiny acrobat give it charm. It is a rare glimpse of early American popular entertainment."},"nga:42425":{"d":"A man sits facing forward, his head slightly turned to the left, with light brown skin, short black hair, prominent sideburns and brown eyes and a neutral expression. His left arm rests over the back of a gilded wooden chair and his right hand is tucked into his jacket. He wears a black coat with large gold buttons, a stiff white collar, a black tie and a square orange gem set in gold. At the left, a narrow window shows a river with a steamboat and red cliffs.","i":"The gilded chair, the gold buttons and the jeweled pin all announce prosperity, and the hand tucked into the jacket is a gesture of self-possession borrowed from grand portraiture. The window view of a steamboat is a modern touch: by 1829 steamboats were symbols of commerce and progress on American rivers. The title gives the sitter only tentatively as Peter Vogelsang Sr.","c":"A forthright, well-organized portrait in which the black coat, golden accents and thin window of river give the sitter dignity and a sense of place. The painter handles the chair and buttons with some pleasure and keeps the face calm and direct. It is a modest picture, with the stiff modeling of provincial work, but the view of a steamboat makes it unusual and memorable."},"nga:34103":{"d":"A youthful man is shown from the head and upper torso, the body slightly turned while he gazes forward. He has deep-set eyes, a straight nose and dark curly hair that frames his face, and wears what appears to be a dark, textured shawl or wrap over a lighter shirt. The background is plain dark brown. The oil on canvas, mounted on cardboard, measures about 51 by 38 centimeters and was painted about 1830.","i":"Junius Brutus Booth (1796-1852) was an English-born tragedian who came to America in 1821 and became one of the leading Shakespearean actors of his day, famous for Richard III and Iago and for erratic behavior. He was the father of the actor Edwin Booth and of John Wilkes Booth, who assassinated Abraham Lincoln. A portrait of an actor, with a shawl-like wrap, may show a costume or the romantic dress of a stage artist.","c":"An actor's portrait must supply charisma, and the dark curls, deep-set eyes and wrap give this picture a Romantic air that suits a tragedian. The plain ground focuses attention on the head, and the handling is simple and quick, as the cardboard support suggests a modest, informal piece. It is hard to know how much of Booth's famous intensity the painter caught, but the picture has a restless, dramatic presence."},"nga:42463":{"d":"A young child stands full length, looking slightly to the side with a neutral expression and rosy cheeks. The child has short light brown hair and wears a blue outfit with gold buttons down the front, a large white ruffled collar and a gold tassel at the waist. In the right hand is a red book with a yellow edge, and black shoes show on a floor patterned with flowers and green and black geometric shapes. The background is plain brown. The canvas is about 111 by 71 centimeters.","i":"The blue outfit with buttons and a wide ruffled collar is probably a skeleton suit, the one-piece jacket-and-trousers outfit worn by small boys from the 1790s to the 1830s, which fits the picture's description as a boy. The book suggests the hope of education, and the elaborate floor pattern shows a comfortable home. The painter is unidentified, and the child's name has not come down with the picture.","c":"A strong, simple design: the blue suit and white collar against plain brown make a bold figure, and the patterned floor adds color and a sense of place. The pose is stiff and the proportions awkward, but the child's grave composure gives the picture a quiet authority. It is a good example of provincial child portraiture in the 1820s, valued for pattern and sincerity more than for sophistication."},"nga:42465":{"d":"A girl, named in the title as Little Miss Wyckoff, is the subject of this canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, painted about 1830 by an unidentified American artist. The size is large enough for a face, costume and perhaps a prop. Wyckoff is a Dutch-American surname, associated with families of New York and New Jersey, and the title shows that the child's family name was remembered with the picture.","i":"Portraits of children around 1830 flourished as the American middle class grew and as childhood came to be seen as a stage worth recording. The title Little Miss echoes the formal courtesy with which even small girls were addressed. The first name has not survived, though the family's name has, which is typical of such pictures.","c":"A modest portrait of a child in the plain, forthright manner of American provincial painting, strongest in color and direct address. The painter has probably relied on simple shapes and bright colors, and the formal title, with its Little Miss, matches a certain stiffness. It is a pleasing small record of girlhood in a Dutch-American family, though not a distinguished work."},"nga:42467":{"d":"A little girl with a basket of flowers is the subject of this small panel, about 36 by 26 centimeters, painted about 1830 by an unidentified American artist. The title gives only the girl and her basket, so the picture is best read as a child's portrait with a prop, in which the flowers add color and a conventional sign of childhood and spring. The small, upright format suits a keepsake for a family room.","i":"Flowers were a standard attribute in children's portraits of the early nineteenth century: they stood for innocence, freshness and the brief bloom of youth, and a basket gave a child something to hold. Such sentimental images grew with the idea that childhood was a precious stage of life. Most were painted by traveling or local artists who worked for modest fees, and their names have mostly been lost.","c":"Pictures of this kind rely on a simple idea clearly stated: a child, a basket and a few colors against a plain ground. The painter's skill is limited, and the anatomy is likely to be approximate, but the basket of flowers gives a fresh, bright note that carries the picture. It is a small, gentle record of girlhood, charming rather than ambitious."},"nga:42476":{"d":"A lady wearing pearls is the subject of this canvas, about 89 by 71 centimeters, painted about 1830 by an unidentified American artist. The title picks out the pearls as the picture's distinguishing feature, and the size is generous enough to show her face, bust and costume with some detail. It is a portrait of a woman of some means, painted to hang in her own home, with her name no longer recorded.","i":"Pearls were long a sign of wealth and, in Christian and classical tradition, of purity and modesty, so a woman portrayed in pearls signaled both her family's means and her virtue. Jewelry in portraits was a mark of status and taste. The sitter's name has not come down with the picture, and the title describes her by her ornament, a common solution for anonymous portraits.","c":"A straightforward portrait whose chief attraction is its dignity: a woman of means presented with modest ornament. The painter's skills are those of the competent provincial portraitist, with careful attention to jewelry and costume and less to the subtleties of the face. The picture is more valuable as a document of the 1830s than as a work of individual genius, and the pearls give it a pleasing sparkle."},"nga:42487":{"d":"A man stands in a straight posture on a small panel, about 47 by 36 centimeters, painted about 1830. He has fair skin, neatly styled dark brown hair and a neutral expression, and wears a high-collared white shirt, a black coat, a black waistcoat and brown trousers. The background is a smooth gradient from light to dark blue. The title calls him Anonymous Man, and he has a pendant, Anonymous Woman, of nearly identical size.","i":"Pairs of small portraits, a husband and a wife painted in the same size and style, were a standard commission of the early nineteenth century. The man's dark suit and white collar present him as a respectable citizen, and the plain blue gradient behind him was an economical alternative to a furnished interior. Both names have been lost, and the museum calls them simply anonymous.","c":"The man's straight, stiff stance and neutral expression give the picture a gravity that suits his dark clothes. The painter's technique is plain, with simple forms and flat color, but the gradient of blue behind him adds a gentle atmosphere. It is a sound example of the straightforward American portrait of 1830, best seen next to its pendant, which completes the household."},"nga:42488":{"d":"A woman stands upright on a small panel, about 47 by 36 centimeters, holding a small red book in her right hand. She has a fair complexion, a neutral expression, slightly arched eyebrows and small, rounded lips, and her dark brown hair is in an updo with a black accessory. She wears a high-necked dark dress with puffed sleeves and a white collar against a plain blue ground, with no jewelry. The title calls her Anonymous Woman, and she has a pendant, Anonymous Man.","i":"The small red book, probably a prayer book, Bible or devotional work, marks the sitter as a woman of piety and literacy, qualities prized in the early republic. The puffed sleeves are the fashion of about 1830, when sleeves grew large. The companion portrait shows a man in a dark suit, and the pair would have hung together. The names of both have been lost, which is why the museum calls them anonymous.","c":"A plain, attractive portrait whose strength is its simplicity: dark dress, white collar, blue ground, red book. The red book is the picture's only note of brightness, and the painter uses it well. Drawing is stiff and the face somewhat generalized, but the figure has dignity. It completes a pair with the Anonymous Man, and the two together give a more complete picture than either alone."},"nga:42506":{"d":"A basket of fruit is painted in watercolor on velveteen, about 41 by 48 centimeters, in the technique called theorem painting. The title gives only the basket and its fruit, in the kind of arrangement typical of such work, where fruit and flowers are rendered in soft, rich color on the nap of the cloth. The picture was made about 1830 by an unidentified American hand, and it is a decorative still life rather than a close study of nature.","i":"Theorem painting was a popular craft in America from about 1810 to 1840, taught to young women in female academies. The painter used stencils, called theorems, cut from oiled paper to apply paint through the openings onto velvet or paper, building up a picture of fruit or flowers from a few patterns. A basket of fruit was a favorite subject, standing for abundance and hospitality, and the finished picture was framed for the parlor.","c":"Theorem painting trades originality for decorative effect, and the best examples gain a velvety richness from the cloth that oil on canvas cannot match. The shapes are simplified and the arrangement conventional, since stencils invite repetition, but a good eye for color gives each example its own character. This one is a pleasing piece of domestic art, valuable as evidence of women's creative life in early America."},"nga:43436":{"d":"An elderly woman is seen from the waist up in three-quarter view, facing slightly to her left. Her skin is soft and aged with wrinkles, her eyes pale blue, her eyebrows thin. She wears a white lace bonnet tied under her chin with a black ribbon, a dark garment and a white shawl over her shoulders, and a strand of amber-colored beads. She holds a small apple in her right hand against a warm brown ground. The panel is about 82 by 60 centimeters.","i":"The apple in her hand could be a simple homely detail, a gift of the orchard, or a sign of fruitfulness and a long life; the painting gives no way to be sure. The bonnet, shawl and beads mark an older woman of respectable means, and the wrinkled skin is recorded without idealization, which is a hallmark of plain American portraiture. The title names her only as probably Annis Cook.","c":"A candid and therefore convincing portrait: the soft, aged face with its wrinkles and pale eyes is the picture's strength. The painter does not hide age, and the amber beads and white bonnet give the figure a modest dignity. The drawing and space are simple, but the sitter's presence is strong, and the apple is a quiet touch of life. It is a notably human picture."},"nga:43437":{"d":"A woman sits facing slightly to her right, at work on a white piece of fabric: the museum describes it as sewing or embroidering, while the title calls the activity knitting. She has distinct features, fair skin, light blue eyes and brown hair in a bun with two curls over her forehead. She wears a black outfit with a white lace collar over her shoulders and a black choker with a gold element, and sits on a chair with a brass-colored edge against a dark reddish-brown ground.","i":"Women were often shown at needlework, an image of industry, domestic virtue and quiet skill that suited their ideal roles in the early republic. The sitter is given as probably Sarah Cook Arnold, a title that seems to record both her family name, Cook, and a married name, Arnold. The black dress and white lace collar were respectable, conservative dress, and the long upright panel gives her presence in a plain room.","c":"A forthright portrait in which the sitter's face is the strongest passage: clear blue eyes, firm features and a calm expression. The needlework gives her something to do and the painter a useful prop. The design is simple, and the dark clothing against the warm dark ground gives the picture a grave, sober tone. It is a good example of the honesty of the plain portrait."},"nga:56740":{"d":"Two figures stand side by side in this wide canvas, about 38 by 64 centimeters. Both have light brown hair, light skin, round eyes and slightly flushed cheeks, and they wear matching deep blue garments with white ruffled collars and two yellow buttons. The background is a flat, dark brown. The title identifies them as the Congdon brothers, and the identical clothing shows that they are close in age, probably small boys.","i":"Double portraits of brothers were a way of recording a household's sons at a moment of childhood. Matching outfits show close ages, and the ruffled collars and buttons are the dress of small boys around 1830, perhaps skeleton suits. Pictures like this were painted by itinerant limners, who worked quickly for modest fees. The painter is unidentified.","c":"The charm of the picture is in the repetition: two nearly identical figures, side by side in matching blue against a flat brown ground, like a decorative motif. The faces are simplified and the poses stiff, but the symmetry gives the picture an odd, gentle humor. It is a minimal, effective composition, and the painter's economy turns the limits of the means into a strength."},"nga:56741":{"d":"A girl in red stands among flowers with a distelfink, a goldfinch, in this large painting on a fireboard, about 93 by 108 centimeters. A fireboard is a painted wooden panel cut to fit a fireplace opening and placed there when the fire was not in use. A tree and flowers add to the girl and the bird, in a bright, decorative design painted about 1830.","i":"The distelfink, or thistle finch, is a favorite motif in Pennsylvania German folk art, a symbol of good luck and happiness that appears on fraktur, pottery and painted furniture. This may point to a Pennsylvania German origin for the board. Fireboards were practical decorations: in summer they hid the sooty fireplace and brought a painted garden or scene into the room.","c":"A cheerful, flat, decorative picture in which the girl in red, the flowers and the bird are arranged as a pattern, more like a piece of folk embroidery than a portrait. It is naive in drawing and space, but the design is bold, and the color is strong. As a fireboard it succeeds in its practical aim, turning a dark hole into a bright room feature, and it is a delightful example of American decorative painting."},"nga:56748":{"d":"An elevated view looks over a landscape of hills and a distant river or lake, with several buildings, including a church, scattered across the land. In the foreground, soldiers in red and white uniforms are lined up near a stone wall and gravestones. The sky is blue with a few scattered clouds, and the museum notes precise brushstrokes and a palette of soft blues, greens, reds and muted tones. The canvas is about 66 by 99 centimeters, painted about 1830.","i":"The title and the redcoated soldiers probably point to Concord, Massachusetts, on 19 April 1775, when British troops marched there during the first fighting of the American Revolution. The view is probably based on a print, perhaps one of Amos Doolittle's engravings of 1775. Painted around 1830, it belongs to a period of renewed memory of the Revolution, when the nation was marking anniversaries and raising monuments.","c":"A rare and charming topographical view in which the soldiers stand tidily in rows and the landscape unrolls like a map. Perspective is naive, but the clarity of the information and the rhythm of the buildings and trees give the picture considerable appeal. It is less a battle picture than a commemorative landscape, and its quiet is part of its force, a memorial of a violent day."},"nga:59922":{"d":"A very wide canvas, about 78 by 167 centimeters, shows a landscape with trees under the title Horizon of the New World, painted about 1830 by an unidentified American artist. A tree is the one feature singled out, and the subject is American. The format, more than twice as wide as it is tall, suits a panoramic view or a decorative picture hung over a fireplace, and the title promises a far, open horizon.","i":"The New World was a phrase that Americans used to claim the continent as a land of promise and fresh beginnings. By the 1830s landscape had become a vehicle of national pride, with painters like Thomas Cole presenting the American scene as a rival to Europe's historic landscapes. A title such as this one makes the picture a statement about destiny as well as a view, and its wide format gives the idea room.","c":"An ambitious title for a picture whose resources are modest: the wide format promises grandeur, and the painter probably relies on trees and the horizon to carry the idea. The result is more earnest than accomplished, and the message of hopeful expansion overrides the details of observation. As a record of national aspiration in a naive key, it has an engaging directness, even if it lacks the skill of Cole's work."},"nga:59923":{"d":"A seated infant faces forward with a round face, blue eyes and light hair. The child wears a white dress with lace and embroidery, painted with pink and green floral designs, and holds a round object tied with a blue ribbon on the lap, with blue shoes placed beside the bare feet. The background is plain brown. The canvas is about 69 by 57 centimeters, painted about 1830 by an unidentified artist, and the sitter is described as a girl.","i":"The title Innocence presents the infant as an emblem: the white dress, bare feet and gentle gaze speak of purity at the beginning of life. In the 1830s writers and painters made childhood a symbol of innocence, an idea that grew with Romantic ideas of the child. The round object with a blue ribbon is probably a toy. The child's name has not come down.","c":"A sweet and simple picture whose appeal is the baby's round face and the careful painting of the white dress with its tiny flowers. The painter's skill is evident in the detail of the embroidery and in the blue notes of ribbon and shoes. It is sentimental, as the title promises, but the sincerity is real, and the plainness of the design keeps it from being cloying."},"nga:59932":{"d":"A bowl of fruit is the subject of this wide canvas, about 79 by 99 centimeters, painted about 1830 by an unidentified American artist. The title gives only the bowl and its contents, so the picture is a still life in the plain sense, in which fruit is heaped for display. The size is large for a still life, suited to a dining room or a parlor wall.","i":"Still life in America grew from the Peale family of Philadelphia, whose members painted fruit pictures from the early 1800s, and by the 1830s fruit pieces were a favorite subject for amateurs and professionals alike. A bowl of fruit stood for abundance and hospitality, qualities a householder wanted on his wall. Many such pictures were painted by women as part of their accomplishments, and the artist here is unrecorded.","c":"Still lifes of this kind live by the sheen and color of the fruit, and a painter of modest training can do well by simplifying shapes and heightening hues. The composition is likely to be central and symmetrical, and the modeling firm rather than subtle. The result is more decorative than searching, but the abundance has a cheerful appeal, and a bowl of fruit is hard to dislike."},"nga:59937":{"d":"A basket holds fruit and flowers in this small panel, about 35 by 46 centimeters, painted about 1830 by an unidentified American artist. The title names the basket and the fruit, and flowers are part of the arrangement. Painted in oil on wood, it is a compact decorative still life, the sort of picture that an owner might hang over a mantel or in a parlor to bring a touch of abundance to the room.","i":"Baskets of fruit and flowers were a staple of American decorative painting in the early nineteenth century, appearing on panels, theorem paintings, tinware and furniture. The basket is a traditional vessel of harvest and gift-giving, and the mixture of flowers and fruit combines the beauty of spring and the plenty of autumn in one picture. The painter's name has not survived, and the subject has the anonymity of a folk motif.","c":"A cheerful, conventional picture, in which the arrangement of the basket, flowers and fruit is more important than any single detail. The painter's simplified forms and bright color give it a naive charm, and the small size keeps it from heaviness. It will not hold anyone's attention for long, but as a modest decoration it does its work well, and it shows the pleasure of making a pretty thing."},"nga:1102":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, his body slightly turned to the right while he looks directly at the viewer. He has pale skin, a sharp nose, high cheekbones, thin red lips and white hair swept back from his face. He wears a dark green coat with a wide, high collar and a white undershirt visible at the neck, and behind him hangs a rich, deep red drapery with a patch of yellow-brown at the lower right. The canvas is about 77 by 63 centimeters, painted about 1835.","i":"White hair marks the sitter as an older man, and the green coat with its wide collar was the fashionable dress of the 1830s. The red drapery is a conventional prop borrowed from grand portraiture to give a sitter standing, and a tradesman or farmer could have the same background as a statesman. The sitter's name has not survived with the picture, and the painter, like many, is unrecorded.","c":"A forthright portrait that gets its force from the sharp features and direct gaze, set off by the bold red ground and the dark green coat. The color contrast is the strongest thing in the picture, and the painter does not soften the sitter's features. Modeling is simple and the space shallow, but the sense of a real, determined old man is strong. It is a good example of the honest provincial portrait."},"nga:43453":{"d":"General Washington rides a white charger in this equestrian portrait, painted on a wood panel about 97 by 75 centimeters, in 1835 or later. The title gives only the general and his horse, so the picture is best read as a heroic type: the commander mounted and in command. Such images were repeated by many nineteenth-century painters and were usually derived from prints after earlier portraits of Washington.","i":"Washington died in 1799, and in the decades that followed he became the central hero of national memory. An equestrian image links him with the old tradition of portraits of kings and generals on horseback, and a white horse suggests triumph and purity. Americans who could not afford a work by a leading painter could buy a print or commission a local artist to make a Washington on horseback for the parlor.","c":"As an image the white charger is a cliché of patriotism, but clichés have energy when the type is sincerely painted. The horse is often the strongest part of such pictures, and the folk painter's flat drawing can give the general a stiff, icon-like dignity. It is not a likeness in any modern sense, and it will not teach much about Washington, but it shows what ordinary Americans wanted him to be."},"nga:45862":{"d":"A woman sits from the waist up facing forward, with dark brown hair parted in the middle and pulled back neatly. She wears an olive green dress with puffed sleeves and lace trim, a high neckline and a small square brooch at the collar, and holds a musical instrument with the name MARTHA emblazoned on it. Behind her are a red curtain, a stack of books and an opening onto a landscape of water, trees and hills. The canvas is about 92 centimeters on each side.","i":"Music was a central accomplishment for young women in the 1830s, when the guitar and harp were fashionable instruments in American parlors, and books signal education and refined taste. The name painted on the instrument acts like a signature, naming the sitter and her skill. The landscape through the opening, a conventional background borrowed from grand portraits, adds gentility. The painter is unidentified.","c":"A strong, symmetrical design in which the sitter's olive dress, the red curtain and the pale landscape make a clear color scheme, and the name on the instrument gives the picture a personal, almost comic directness. The face is simplified and the anatomy approximate, but the accessories are painted with care, and the near-square format gives the figure weight. It is a lively, individual folk portrait with real character."},"nga:59887":{"d":"A steamship named Erie is the subject of this canvas, about 57 by 75 centimeters, dated probably 1837. The picture is a ship portrait, the genre in which a vessel is shown in profile as a proud possession of its owner or captain. A name like Erie suggests the Great Lakes or the Erie Canal route, the water highways that carried Americans westward in the 1830s.","i":"Steamships were symbols of progress in the 1830s, shortening journeys and opening the interior to trade and settlement, and their owners commissioned portraits to advertise a vessel or to record pride in it. Ship portraits followed conventions from European marine painting, with the ship shown in clear profile and the flag and rigging precisely drawn. A painter of this kind of picture was often self-taught, working in port towns.","c":"A ship portrait succeeds when the vessel is drawn with the clarity of a diagram and set in a convincing space, and the naive painter's flat treatment can be an advantage. The picture will not offer the atmosphere of a Fitz Henry Lane, but a steamer of the 1830s is an interesting subject in itself. The pleasure here is largely documentary, with the satisfactions of a well-recorded machine."},"nga:42480":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, slightly turned and looking to the left. He has a strong jawline and deep brown skin, with dark hair mostly covered by a red headwrap. He wears a shirt with colored flower patterns, a red sash with geometric designs and a long silver earring on his left ear, against a dark background. The title names him Chief Jumper of the Seminoles, and the canvas measures about 76 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The Seminole are a Native people of Florida who resisted removal in the Second Seminole War of 1835 to 1842, a long and costly conflict. Their leaders, among them Jumper, became subjects of portraits made while they were in captivity or negotiation, and such pictures were sold as images of famous figures. The tentative date of 1837 or 1838 places the portrait in the years of the war's crisis, and the painter is unidentified.","c":"A dignified, direct portrait: the strong jaw, the head turned aside and the bold red wrap and sash give the sitter a commanding presence. The painter's skill lies in the simple, strong shapes and careful attention to costume. Portraits of Native leaders made in captivity are charged, honoring the sitter while also recording defeat and removal. This one is notable for its dignity and its sparing means."},"nga:52954":{"d":"A man identified only as a Man of Science is the subject of this canvas, about 100 by 85 centimeters, dated 1839. The title gives his calling, not his name, and portraits of this kind usually include instruments, specimens or books to show the sitter's field. The picture is a half-length or three-quarter-length image of a learned man of the late 1830s, painted by an unidentified American artist.","i":"In the 1830s there was no common word for what is now called a scientist: the term was coined by William Whewell in 1833, but man of science remained the usual phrase for decades. American science was growing, with colleges, natural history societies and gentlemen who collected minerals, fossils and plants. A portrait that names the profession shows pride in learning as a form of public service.","c":"A portrait of a learned man tests whether a painter can make thought visible, and a provincial artist does this by giving the sitter a calm face and the right accessories. The result is likely to be honest rather than inspired, with the props doing more than the psychology. But the title is a fine one, and the picture is an interesting record of how science wished to be seen in 1839."},"nga:34085":{"d":"A man sits, shown from the waist up, with dark brown hair, a warm fair complexion, a straight nose, thin lips and pronounced cheekbones. He wears a dark suit with a double-breasted waistcoat, a white high-collared shirt and a black cravat tied in a bow. His right hand rests on the arm of a red upholstered chair, and the background is a simple soft brown gradient. The canvas is about 87 by 69 centimeters, painted about 1840.","i":"The dark suit, high collar and bow cravat were the standard dress of a respectable man in 1840, and the red upholstered chair is a conventional sign of a comfortable home or office. A plain, softly shaded background was the economical choice of many portraitists of the day, who sold likenesses quickly and cheaply to a growing middle class. The sitter and painter are both unnamed.","c":"A competent, plain portrait with a quiet strength in the face: pronounced cheekbones and thin lips give the sitter a grave, focused look. The red chair is a bold note against the brown ground, and the modeling is simple. The painter has not flattered the man, nor looked deeply into him, but the picture is a clear and sober statement of presence. It represents the American portrait of about 1840 well."},"nga:42440":{"d":"An older woman sits upright from the waist up, her blue eyes and prominent cheekbones framed by a white bonnet with a frilled edge. She wears a dark dress with full sleeves and a white detachable collar tied at the front with a blue ribbon, and holds a small book, with one hand on the armrest of the chair. The background is a plain brownish wall. The canvas is about 80 by 71 centimeters, and the title names the chair as a red arrowback.","i":"A small book, probably a Bible or devotional work, marks the sitter as pious and literate. The title names the red arrowback chair, a painted chair type of the period, and the same size and date as a companion, Old Man in Red Slat Back Chair, suggest that the two portraits were a pair meant to hang together. Portraits of elderly couples recorded long lives and enduring households.","c":"A frank portrait of age: the prominent cheekbones, the frilled bonnet and the steady blue eyes are recorded without idealization. The red chair is the picture's strong accent, and the white collar and bonnet frame the face effectively. The drawing is simple and the space shallow, but the sitter's quiet authority comes through, and the picture is more moving than many more polished ones."},"nga:42474":{"d":"A young child stands upright, facing forward, with light skin, light brown hair, light blue eyes and a neutral expression. The child wears a long gray dress with a red lower part, red socks and brown shoes with green bows, and holds a small model of a rooster. On the floor beside is a box with small objects such as a doll and a ball. The background is a simple greenish-brown gradient. The canvas is about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted about 1840.","i":"Children's portraits of the 1840s often include toys, which signal both play and the comfort of a household that could afford them. The title calls the sitter a girl, though small children of both sexes wore long dresses at this age. A box of toys was a mark of prosperity and of the new belief that play was good for children, and it also gave the painter a chance to add small, colorful details.","c":"A charming picture whose pleasure is its details: the toy rooster, the box with the doll and ball, the red socks and the green bows. The child's face is simply drawn and the stance stiff, but the numerous small objects give a vivid impression of childhood in the 1840s. The painter's pattern-making gifts exceed his skill in anatomy, and the picture is the better for being so specific."},"nga:42475":{"d":"A young girl is shown from the waist up, seated with her torso facing the viewer. She has fair skin, light blue eyes, thin eyebrows and slightly rosy cheeks, with reddish-brown hair neatly parted in the middle. She wears a dark blue off-the-shoulder dress with decorative trim and holds a small flower with green leaves. The background is a solid dark color. The canvas is small, about 26 by 21 centimeters, painted about 1840.","i":"A flower in a child's hand was a standard emblem in portraits of the period, standing for innocence and the brevity of youth. Small portraits like this, hardly larger than a book, were intimate keepsakes for families, who kept them in a parlor or bedroom. The off-the-shoulder dress is a fashionable detail of the 1840s that suggests the girl was dressed up for the portrait.","c":"A modest, appealing picture in which the small scale concentrates attention on the girl's face and the small flower. The dark blue dress and the dark ground give the portrait a strong, simple tone, and the pale face stands out clearly. The painter's skill is limited, but the sitter's solemnity gives the picture charm. It is best enjoyed as a keepsake, which is how it was made."},"nga:42479":{"d":"A man sits in a chair, gazing directly at the viewer, with fair skin and light brown hair. He wears formal attire and holds an open book in his left hand while his right arm rests on the chair's arm. The background is plain and brown, drawing attention to the figure. The canvas is about 80 by 71 centimeters, painted between 1836 and 1840, and the title calls the chair a red slat back, a type of painted country chair.","i":"The open book shows a man of learning and, most likely, piety, and the old man's portrait recorded a long life and a household's head. The same size and date as the companion portrait Woman in Red Arrowback Chair suggest that the pair hung together as a record of an elderly couple. Painted chairs like these were everyday furniture, and showing the sitter in one signals a plain, respectable home.","c":"A simple, dignified portrait in which the open book and steady gaze define the sitter. The plain brown ground and the red chair give a clear design, and the figure has weight. The painter's anatomy is plain, but the face, as an image of sober old age, is convincing. It is a good partner for its pendant and gains by being seen with it."},"nga:42484":{"d":"A man sits at a table in a black jacket, white shirt, black bow tie and yellow waistcoat adorned with a gold-colored chain and a pin, holding a quill over a sheet of paper while he looks out. He has dark wavy hair, large green eyes and a neutral expression. On the table are an ink pot with another quill and a thick brown book; behind him are rolls of patterned fabric on shelves, blue boxes, dark green walls and a red curtain. The canvas is about 86 by 66 centimeters.","i":"An occupational portrait shows a man with the tools and goods of his trade, a convention that goes back to Netherlandish merchant portraits. Textile merchants were central to the growth of American trade in the 1830s and 1840s, when imported and mill-made cloth poured through shops. The rolls of fabric, quill and ledger tell the viewer that he is a man of business, proud of his stock and his credit.","c":"The picture's best idea is the setting: the rolls of patterned cloth and blue boxes behind the merchant make a decorative backdrop, and the yellow waistcoat is a lively accent. The face is calm and the pose a little stiff, and the painter handles the small details of ink pot and chain with obvious pleasure. It is a good example of American occupational portraiture and is more informative than most about a trade."},"nga:42485":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up in profile, facing left, with a straight posture and prominent nose and chin. His dark hair is curly and close to his head, with some volume on top. He wears a dark suit, a high-collared white shirt and a black cravat, against a plain grayish-blue ground. The panel is small, about 23 by 17 centimeters, painted about 1835 to 1840 by an unidentified American artist.","i":"The profile format gave a cheap and quick likeness, and the grayish-blue ground and dark suit suggest an efficient portrait practice. The man faces left, while the companion portrait of a lady faces right, so that the pair looked at each other on a wall. The dark suit, white collar and black cravat were the uniform of respectability for men of the middle class in the late 1830s.","c":"A firm, economical profile in which the nose, chin and curly hair give the sitter character. There is little room for flattery in a profile, and the painter's honesty is part of the appeal. The picture's small scale and plain ground keep attention on the outline. As a pair with the lady's portrait it forms a pleasing, intimate record of a couple, and alone it is a modest, sound likeness."},"nga:42486":{"d":"A woman is shown from the chest up in profile, facing right with her head slightly turned. She has fair skin, a straight nose and a slight smile, and her brown hair is in an updo with tight curls around her face. She wears a black garment with a light shawl, a rectangular clasp and a triple-strand necklace with a central gold or amber pendant, against a muted gray ground. The panel is small, about 23 by 17 centimeters.","i":"Profile portraits remained popular in the 1830s and 1840s, though the daguerreotype was beginning to compete. She faces right and the companion portrait of a man faces left, so the two would have looked at each other when hung together. A necklace with a central pendant and a shawl were fashionable details of the day, and the slight smile gives the profile a rare friendliness.","c":"A delicate, pleasing small portrait in which the profile's clean line, the tight curls and the little smile give the sitter personality. The necklace is the picture's one ornament, painted with care. The profile format limits expression, but this painter has made the best of it, and the pairing with a companion gives it a sense of relationship. It is a modest picture, charming and neat."},"nga:42507":{"d":"A large cut watermelon sits in a woven basket surrounded by other fruits, including grapes, peaches and small round red fruits, with clusters of grapes hanging above. The basket takes up most of the space, and the palette of greens, blues, oranges and browns gives the picture a rich, jewel-like quality. It is a theorem painting in watercolor on velveteen, about 43 by 54 centimeters, made about 1840 by an unidentified hand.","i":"Theorem painting was a popular craft taught to young women in the early nineteenth century. The painter applied color through stencils to velvet or velveteen, which gave a soft, glowing surface. Fruit baskets stood for abundance and hospitality, and a cut watermelon, showing its red flesh, was an especially showy centerpiece. The resulting pictures were framed and hung in parlors as proofs of artistic skill and domestic taste.","c":"A generous, cheerful picture whose appeal is its abundance: the watermelon, grapes and peaches are heaped in a dense, colorful mass. The stencil technique simplifies each fruit to a pleasing pattern, and the velveteen gives the color a velvety depth that oil would not. It is a decorative piece, not an inquiry into nature, but the sense of plenty is infectious, and the craft is considerable."},"nga:45861":{"d":"A large tree with lush foliage stands at the left of a quiet landscape, and figures gather near white gravestones: a woman in a bonnet, a child playing with a dog, and a man and woman. Behind them lies a tranquil river with a steam-powered paddle boat and small sailboats, and rolling green hills with houses and trees stretch into the distance. The palette is earthy greens, yellows and blues. The canvas is about 50 by 61 centimeters, painted about 1840.","i":"Family burying grounds were common on American farms, where the dead were buried close to the home, and mourning pictures were a favorite form of memorial in the early nineteenth century. This one is unusually tranquil: the child and the dog show life continuing, and the steamboat on the river brings in the modern world. The picture is a memorial, combining remembrance with the calm of a pastoral landscape.","c":"A gentle, unusual picture that avoids both morbidity and sentimentality: the dead and the living share a pastoral world, and the white gravestones are one note among green hills and quiet water. The painter's perspective is naive, but the sense of arrangement is sure, and the large tree gives the scene a framing mass. It is a tender, understated memorial, and the steamboat is a clever, modern touch."},"nga:45957":{"d":"An infant sits upright in a dark blue wooden cradle, facing slightly to the side, with short light-colored hair. The baby wears a red long-sleeved dress with black patterns, covered by an olive-green vest, and holds a rattle, with a cream-colored cushion in the cradle and a small woven basket beside it. The background is plain brown. The canvas is about 69 by 58 centimeters, painted about 1840 by an unidentified American artist.","i":"Baby portraits celebrated a new member of the family and also recorded a child who, in a time of high infant mortality, might not survive. The cradle, rattle and basket mark the nursery, and the cradle was a symbol of a household's hopes. Portraits of infants were commonly made in the first year or two of life, often by itinerant painters who offered a likeness as a keepsake and sometimes as a memorial.","c":"A sweet, uncomplicated picture whose strength is its color: the blue cradle, the red dress and the green vest make a bright triad on the brown ground. The baby's face is round and simple, and the pose is formal, almost enthroned, which gives the baby a comic dignity. The painter is evidently more skilled with pattern than with anatomy, and the result is a cheerful, quaint image of infancy in about 1840."},"nga:52965":{"d":"Two young children stand side by side in full length, each in a black apron over a bright dress. The child at left has light hair pulled back, large blue eyes and a blue dress with puffed sleeves, and holds a small woven basket of red berries. The child at right has light brown hair parted in the middle, a bright orange dress and an apron embroidered in green, and holds two red cherries with green leaves. The background is plain dark brown. The canvas is about 94 by 67 centimeters.","i":"Black aprons were worn by girls in the 1830s and 1840s to keep dresses clean, and they are a fashion detail and a sign of a household that cared for its children's appearance. Cherries and berries stand for summer sweetness and the simple pleasures of childhood. The girls' similar size and matching poses suggest the closeness of sisters, painted together as a record of a family's daughters.","c":"Bold colors on a dark ground are the picture's glory: the orange and the blue dresses against black aprons and brown make a vivid design, and the red fruit adds accents. The figures are stiff and the proportions naive, but the symmetry and the strong color give the pair a toylike charm. It is a memorable example of the strong flat design that the best provincial portraitists achieved in the 1830s and 1840s."},"nga:56739":{"d":"Many birds sit on and around a small, leafy tree in this oil on canvas, about 43 by 36 centimeters, painted about 1840. The museum notes birds that look like hawks, blue jays, hummingbirds, magpies and kestrels, in shades of black, white, brown, gray, red, yellow and pale blue. They are placed at various heights on the tree and on the ground, all facing different directions, with green grasses and ferns below and a pale yellow sky behind.","i":"Bird pictures were popular in the early nineteenth century as John James Audubon's Birds of America, published from 1827 to 1838, and Alexander Wilson's earlier work taught the public to love ornithology. A painter without access to the living birds could copy from prints or from stuffed specimens, which could explain why hawks and hummingbirds share a tree. The grouping is decorative rather than ecological, more a pattern book of species than a view of nature.","c":"A delightful oddity: the species are gathered like guests at a party, each posed for display, with no regard for habits or habitat. The painting is not a study of nature but a decorative inventory, and its pleasure lies in the variety of color and shape. The composition is stiff, but the bright, clear hues and the evident love of birds give it a charm that more accurate pictures sometimes lack."},"nga:56742":{"d":"A lady wearing spectacles is the subject of this canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted about 1840 by an unidentified American artist. The title picks out the spectacles as the picture's distinguishing feature. The size is generous enough to show the face, the bust and some costume, and the plain title and anonymous maker suggest a portrait made for her family, in which the glasses are a matter-of-fact detail of the sitter's daily life.","i":"Spectacles are uncommon in portraits of the period, because many sitters removed them or avoided them, so a sitter who kept hers on may have wanted to be recorded as she really was: a reader, a needleworker, a woman who used her eyes. Eyeglasses suggested age, learning and industry rather than vanity. The title, which describes her by her spectacles, shows how names can be lost from portraits while visible traits remain.","c":"A plain, honest picture whose single distinguishing detail makes it memorable: the spectacles give the face a practical, no-nonsense character. The painter does not flatter, and that candor is the appeal. The modeling is simple and the setting spare, but the sitter's presence is strong. It is a good example of how provincial painters recorded real people, with their real aids, rather than ideals."},"nga:59892":{"d":"A woman sits on a wooden chair beside a standing boy, her right hand on a small table and her left holding a small book. She has dark hair in an updo, a long dark dress with long sleeves and a pearl necklace; the boy wears a dark suit with shiny buttons and a bow tie. The table holds a white cloth with cakes, cups, a teapot and a spoon. Behind them are a green wall, a window with greenery and flowers, and red drapes with gold accents. The canvas is about 71 by 60 centimeters.","i":"The tea table was a stage for genteel domestic life in the 1840s: serving tea and cakes marked a woman as a hostess and a household as respectable and comfortable. The small book shows reading and refinement, and the pearl necklace and drapery add status. Domestic interiors with a woman and a child celebrated the ideal of the home as a woman's sphere, central to the middle-class values of the period.","c":"A lively, naive picture whose pleasure lies in the abundance of detail: the teapot, the cakes, the pearls and the red drapes are painted with an eager, inventory-making eye. The figures are stiff and the space slightly flattened, but the pairing of the woman and the boy beside the table has a cheerful logic. It is a lovely record of how a middle-class family wanted to be remembered, at home with tea."},"nga:59931":{"d":"Two young girls are shown in bust length, side by side, occupying the left and right halves of the canvas, both facing forward with their bodies angled slightly inward. They have pale complexions and long dark brown ringlets falling to their shoulders; the girl on the left has large brown eyes and the one on the right striking blue eyes. Both wear dark dresses with wide, off-the-shoulder necklines. The background is plain light beige, with no jewelry or props. The canvas is about 46 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Ringlets were the fashionable hairstyle for girls in the 1830s and 1840s, achieved by curling the hair in rags or papers, and wide, off-the-shoulder necklines belonged to the fashion of the 1840s. A double portrait of sisters, leaning toward each other, stresses their closeness and their place together in a family. The painter is unidentified, and the plain ground concentrates all attention on the two faces.","c":"The picture's strength is its symmetry: two girls, two sets of ringlets and two dark dresses on pale ground make a calm, rhythmic design. The faces are simple and similar, but the difference of eye color is a touch of individuality. The plain background and the absence of props make it more austere than many portraits of children. It is a quiet, intimate image of sisterhood."},"nga:56729":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, sideways but facing the viewer, with a paper in his right hand. He has clear blue eyes, a neatly groomed mustache, sideburns and dark brown wavy hair parted at the side. He wears a black suit jacket over a waistcoat, a white shirt, a black necktie and a small gold chain. In the background a window reveals a landscape with a castle and river under a bright sky. The canvas is about 74 by 66 centimeters, painted in 1843 or later.","i":"The Boston Atlas was a Whig newspaper, and reading it was a sign of civic engagement in a decade when the penny press made newspapers cheap and widely read. A portrait of a man with a paper presents him as an informed citizen, and the title names him only as Hubbard. The window view of a castle and river is an old portrait convention, borrowed from European painting, that adds a note of romance to the plain subject.","c":"A clear, straightforward portrait whose conceit, the man absorbed in his newspaper, gives it a lively personal touch. The sitter's careful grooming and the neat dress are painted with attention, and the picture's color is restrained. The anatomy is conventional, and the castle in the window is a quaint touch, but the effect of an alert, reading man is convincing. It is a good example of the occupational portrait of the 1840s."},"nga:34128":{"d":"A young man sits with his body turned slightly to the side, facing the viewer. He has fair skin and neatly styled light brown hair parted to one side, a smooth, youthful face, prominent cheekbones and a defined jaw. He wears a dark coat over a white shirt with a decorative patterned edge and a vivid blue bow tie, with a gold chain bearing a circular pendant and a key-like ornament. His right hand rests on two large books, one lettered SILVAN and SHONBERG in gold on its spine. A window at the left shows distant landscape. The canvas is about 76 by 64 centimeters.","i":"A young man with books and a watch chain presents himself as educated and respectable, qualities that helped a man rise in business or a profession in the 1840s. The gold chain with its key-like ornament, probably for a watch, signals property and modern habits of timekeeping, and the books suggest reading or study. The gilt lettering on the spine is a deliberate detail, though what it refers to is not clear. The portrait is of William Crook Rudman Jr., about 1845, by an unidentified painter.","c":"A fresh, youthful portrait, in which the vivid blue bow tie is the first thing the eye catches, and the careful treatment of the chain and ornaments shows the painter's relish for detail. The face is smooth and a little blank, as many portraits of young men are, but the pose with a hand on the books has self-confidence. It is a lively and pleasing document of a young man's aspirations."},"nga:42450":{"d":"A woman sits in a chair, facing slightly to the left and looking directly at the viewer. She has fair skin, light-colored eyes and dark brown hair under a lace-edged cap, and wears a black dress with a lace collar and a bracelet. In one hand she holds a small book titled Sacred Songs. The chair has a red upholstered back and an armrest draped with embroidered blue and green fabric against a plain muted brown ground. The canvas is about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted about 1845.","i":"A book of Sacred Songs, a hymnal or a collection of devotional verse, marks a woman of piety and, perhaps, of musical accomplishment, since hymn singing was a central part of home and church life in the 1840s. The black dress and lace cap were respectable dress for a mature woman, and the bracelet adds a note of modest ornament. The sitter is recorded as Sophia Mead.","c":"A forthright portrait whose strengths are the direct gaze and the careful details of the lace and the embroidered drape. The red chair is a strong accent, and the lettering of the book title gives the picture a literal, slightly comic exactness. The painter's modeling is plain, but the sitter's solid, quiet presence comes through. It is a sound and attractive example of the American portrait of the mid-1840s."},"nga:42464":{"d":"A man sits on a white bench outdoors while a young girl stands beside him with her hand on his shoulder, both with fair skin and brown hair. He wears a black suit with a white shirt, a black tie and a patterned vest, and she wears a blue dress with white lace trim and a white petticoat. A straw hat and a book lie near him, and a small brown dog and a white bonnet with blue ribbons rest on the grass, with trees and water behind. The canvas is about 127 by 102 centimeters.","i":"The title calls the two a brother and sister, and the girl's hand on the man's shoulder expresses affection and protection. An outdoor setting with a bench, a dog and a straw hat reflects the Romantic taste for nature and leisure, and the book hints at reading and refinement. The white bonnet lying on the grass is a charming, casual detail that suggests a moment caught in play.","c":"A large, appealing picture in which the casual details, the dog, bonnet, hat and book, loosen the stiffness of the poses. The color scheme of black suit, blue dress and green trees is clear, and the girl's hand on his shoulder gives the composition its human center. The drawing is naive and the proportions approximate, but the sentiment is warm. It is a good example of the family group at its most relaxed."},"nga:59882":{"d":"Four people pose for a family portrait against a deep-red curtain. At the left a woman with dark hair in a bun, in a light dress with lace details and a gold brooch, holds a young child in a white dress; beside them stands an older child in a blue jacket with a white collar, and at the far right a man in a dark suit and a black bow tie. The canvas is large, about 137 centimeters square, and the painter used bed ticking, a striped mattress cloth, instead of canvas.","i":"Bed ticking was a sturdy, cheap woven cloth made for mattresses and pillows, and itinerant painters sometimes used it in place of canvas, which suggests a rural artist working economically. The family portrait of 1846 follows the usual arrangement of mother, child and father in a row, with a red curtain borrowed from grand portraiture. The title records the family name Gage, and the painter is unidentified.","c":"A strong, plain family portrait, whose bed-ticking support gives it a rustic honesty, and whose deep red curtain unifies the figures in a single bright field. The faces are simple, the poses stiff and the grouping a little crowded, but the picture's directness, the mother with her child and the father at the edge, is touching. It is a document of a rural family and of a painter's makeshift materials."},"nga:43443":{"d":"A village lies nestled in hills and trees under a cloudy blue sky. Nearest are simple white buildings, some with white spires and others with red and brown roofs, surrounded by tall trees; beyond are hills, smaller trees and sailboats on a body of water at the right. The museum notes visible brushstrokes and simple, flat buildings, in a palette of soft greens, warm tans, whites and yellows. The panel is long and narrow, about 32 by 65 centimeters.","i":"White church steeples and simple clapboard buildings among hills are the classic image of the New England village, and by the nineteenth century they had become a symbol of the region's order and piety. The long, narrow shape suggests a panel made to fit over a doorway or mantel, a common place for such pictures. The sailboats show that the village lies near the water, and the whole is a portrait of a place rather than of a particular person.","c":"A charming small view in which the buildings are reduced to simple white shapes, the spires pointing up through the trees, and the hills fold gently behind. It is naive in drawing and perspective, but the harmony of the greens and tans and the neat rhythm of the buildings give it a gentle order. It is more a decoration than a record, but its quiet beauty is real, and it conveys a love of home."},"nga:59888":{"d":"A busy street scene in a town fills this large canvas, about 90 by 132 centimeters. The museum notes buildings, a statue on a pedestal, signs reading City Hotel and Hard Ware, a group of men with a horse, a water pump, two dogs, people on a sidewalk and a hotel balcony, and a worker with stacked bricks. The colors are warm oranges, browns and greens, and the brushwork is precise and detailed, giving a realistic sense of daily life.","i":"The title suggests a comic election-day scene in which an independent voter, one not bound to a party, is the center of attention, though what the picture shows in detail is the life of the street. In the 1840s and 1850s, hotels and street corners were centers of political talk, and genre painters made humor from the courting of voters. A painting that records signs, pumps and workers is also a valuable portrait of an American town.","c":"A rare and lively street scene, in which the abundance of incident, signs, dogs, horses and a worker with bricks, gives a vivid picture of daily life. The narrative is not easy to read, and the figures are small, but the picture's strength is its detail and its warm color. It is an engaging, anecdotal document of American town life in about 1850, with more in it than a first glance shows."},"nga:42471":{"d":"A young female sitter is shown in profile facing right, in head and shoulders, with light skin, blue eyes, subtly pursed lips and dark brown hair in a bob. She wears a dark green garment with a white collar and a red bow, against a solid brown ground. The panel is about 46 by 32 centimeters, painted about 1850 by an unidentified American artist, and the title picks out her blue eyes as the picture's most striking feature.","i":"By 1850 the profile portrait was old-fashioned, replaced in fashion by the full-face likeness and by the daguerreotype, but folk painters kept the format because it was simple and gave a firm outline. The title, Blue Eyes, describes the sitter by her most memorable feature because her name has not been recorded. The red bow adds a bright accent to the dark green.","c":"A simple, bold image whose power lies in the profile outline and the color accent of the red bow against dark green and brown. The single visible blue eye gives the picture its focus and its title. The format allows little modeling, and the painter makes no attempt at it. It is a modest, memorable panel that proves that a plain design can be effective."},"nga:42490":{"d":"A winter farm scene stretches under a sky of soft blues with hints of yellow, with a line of dark evergreens on the horizon in the top third of the canvas. A woman and child stand at the right near a house, the woman with one arm outstretched toward chickens that fill the snow and the other holding a large bowl; another child looks out from the building. At the left, a fence leads to a barn with two horses, and two cattle stand beside a round shed. The canvas is about 60 by 98 centimeters.","i":"Winter farm scenes were a staple of nineteenth-century American popular art, and from the middle of the century prints such as those of Currier and Ives spread the image of the snowy farm as the heart of the nation. Here a woman feeds the chickens, an everyday chore of rural women's labor, and her child hides behind her skirts. As the nation industrialized, pictures like this preserved a nostalgic image of self-sufficient country life.","c":"A quiet picture in which the cold, whites and grays and the low horizon give a convincing sense of a winter day, and the figures and animals supply a gentle narrative. The perspective is naive, the buildings are flat, and the human figures are small, but the picture's close attention to chores and animals makes it rich in life. It is a likable record of country life, valuable for its homely detail."},"nga:42503":{"d":"A river winds between curving hills in this upright landscape. A sailing ship with furled sails moves along the water at the center, surrounded by yellow foliage on either bank, and tall trees rise beside the river. Sparse trees line the horizon under a blue sky with wispy white clouds. On the right, a yellow house sits at the water's edge with smoke curling from its chimney. The palette is muted, soft greens and blues with small pops of yellow and white. Plaster and lath, the support, are the materials of a wall, which suggests the picture began as decoration for a house.","i":"A quiet river, a ship and a smoking chimney present a settled, prosperous countryside, the kind of view American households liked to have on their walls in the mid-nineteenth century. The valley recalls the Hudson River landscapes then in fashion, though no particular place is identified and the painter is unrecorded. Overmantels and wall panels like this belong to a tradition of painting for the home, not for exhibition, where the aim was simply to bring a pleasant prospect indoors.","c":"The picture works as decoration. A few clear shapes, the ship, the yellow house and the banks of foliage, sit against soft greens and blues, and the repeated yellow carries the eye across the surface. Judged against the Hudson River School views it echoes, it offers little of their atmospheric depth, and its spatial logic is probably handled by overlapping rather than measured perspective. Its charm is pattern and mood, not observation."},"nga:43635":{"d":"A boy and a girl are the subject of this upright canvas, about 107 by 81 centimeters, painted in oil around 1850 by an unidentified American artist. The title names no sitters, and the museum's keyword is 'family', so the picture was probably commissioned by parents to record their children together. Children's double portraits of the period often place the pair in a simple domestic or garden setting, standing close and sharing a gesture or an object. The size is generous enough to show the figures at a convincing scale.","i":"Portraits of children were among the commonest commissions for American portrait painters around 1850. They served as family records at a time when many children did not survive to adulthood and when the daguerreotype was already offering a cheaper likeness. A boy and girl painted together stood for the family as a whole: its continuity, its prosperity and its parents' pride. Because the sitters are unnamed, any closer reading of clothing, objects or gestures would be guesswork.","c":"A double portrait of children asks for a convincing likeness of each face and a believable bond between the pair. Self-taught painters often met the first with a firm contour and the second with symmetry or a shared gesture, with results that are direct if unpolished. The sitters and painter are unknown, but the large canvas gives the children real presence, and the picture stands as a sincere family document."},"nga:43641":{"d":"Inside a cluttered workshop, three men make a political poster. At the left a man in a tan vest and rolled-up white sleeves stands at a worktable strewn with paint cans, brushes and containers. At the right, two painters work on a tall white sheet lettered 'WE GO FOR THE UNION' above an oval portrait of a gray-haired man on a blue ground. One holds a palette and a thin brush; the other, in paint-covered overalls, holds a can of white paint and a wide brush and looks out at the viewer. A window with gridded panes shows an orange and pink sky.","i":"The picture records a trade that supplied nineteenth-century politics: painters of signs, banners and transparencies, who produced lettered portraits for campaign processions and meeting halls. The slogan places the scene in the period's debates over the Union, but the date range of about 1840 to 1850 and the unnamed gray-haired man on the poster leave the occasion unidentified. Showing painters at work may also be read as a quiet tribute to a craft whose products were public but whose makers were rarely named.","c":"The best thing here is the subject, rare in American painting of its day: ordinary labor in a painters' shop, observed with real curiosity about the pots, brushes and half-finished sheet. The figures are plain, mostly in profile, and the lettering and the oval portrait do much of the talking. The glowing sky in the window is a small pleasure. It is a modest picture, valued more for its candor and documentary interest than for finish."},"nga:45960":{"d":"A young child stands upright beside a rocking horse, one hand resting on the horse's head and the other holding a whip. The child has fair skin, blue eyes and short blond hair with a small curl at the top, and wears an orange dress with a floral pattern, white lace-trimmed pantaloons, white stockings and black shoes. Red drapery hangs at the left, a bright landscape shows through a window, and a deep green fills the right side. The canvas, about 103 by 69 centimeters, is a full-length portrait of a child, presumably commissioned by the family.","i":"Toys in children's portraits show childhood as a stage of life with its own pleasures, and the rocking horse was a familiar prop of the well-off nursery. In the 1840s and 1850s boys and girls alike wore dresses in their earliest years, so the sitter's sex cannot be settled from the costume. The drapery and window view are stock devices borrowed from grand portraiture, lending a modest household picture the dignity of a society likeness.","c":"Color is the picture's strength: the orange dress holds its own against the red curtain and the deep green, and the window opens a bright space behind the child. The costume is recorded with a patient eye for pattern and trim, and the upright stance gives the small sitter a monument-like calm. Like much portraiture of its kind, it is probably more convincing in fabrics and props than in anatomy and space."},"nga:43438":{"d":"A boy named Nicholas M. S. Catlin is commemorated in this canvas, about 98 by 73 centimeters, painted around 1852 by an unidentified American artist. Flowers are among the motifs recorded for it. As a memorial picture, it combines a child's likeness with the language of mourning and was made to be kept by the family after his death. The upright format and the scale are those of a parlor portrait, a picture for the home rather than for a church or a public building.","i":"Memorial pictures answered a hard fact of nineteenth-century life: many children did not reach adulthood, and families wanted something to keep the dead present. Flowers are among the commonest emblems of the brevity of life in such images, and beside a child they usually read as a tribute to a life cut short. The picture therefore does two jobs at once, a portrait of a particular boy and a quiet consolation in the sentimental language of the day.","c":"A memorial picture is judged by tact more than invention. Its task is to fix a likeness gently and to hold the viewer in a mood of loss, and the best examples manage it by restraint, with a few well-chosen flowers and a quiet pose. This canvas belongs to a large body of anonymous American mourning art that moves the viewer as a record of feeling, even where the handling is plain."},"nga:42491":{"d":"A woman on a white horse rides along a path through a park, with several people in formal dress walking beside her. Behind them stretches a tended lawn with trees, and a white monument stands inside a black fence under a blue sky with clouds. The palette is built from greens and blues, with whites, blacks and a touch of red. The monument of the title presumably commemorates the first fighting of the Revolutionary War, at Lexington, Massachusetts, in April 1775. The canvas, about 70 by 78 centimeters, was painted in 1853 or later.","i":"In the nineteenth century monuments to the Revolution became places of pilgrimage, as a young republic looked to 1775 for its founding story. Lexington, where militiamen faced British regulars on the green, was among the best-known sites, and a stroll there, as shown, belonged to genteel recreation as much as to patriotism. The riders and well-dressed walkers make the picture a record of how Americans of the 1850s visited their history, not a reconstruction of the battle.","c":"The white monument inside its black fence gives the composition a firm vertical anchor, and the formal figures lend it a period flavor. Depth across the lawn and the scale of the walkers are probably judged by eye rather than constructed, as in much self-taught topographical painting. It is a modest but sincere picture, more valuable for what it shows of the 1850s attitude to the Revolution than for its artistry."},"nga:45964":{"d":"A country estate looks out over a broad river. In the foreground stands an ornate house with white columns and steps checkered in pink and peach. A man and a woman in mid-century dress stand on the pathway, with a horse-drawn carriage and a greyhound nearby. A path leads down to the water, where a rider passes and sailboats drift, and buildings show in the distance beneath soft, billowy clouds. The palette is pastel, with warm yellows and oranges, greens and blues. The canvas, about 69 by 87 centimeters, is a mid-sized picture for a domestic wall.","i":"The title promises a story: a couple setting out from their country seat, with carriage, greyhound and rider completing a picture of genteel leisure. Such scenes flattered a prosperous clientele, offering a vision of rural gentility in which a columned house, a river view and a well-bred dog stand for wealth and taste. The white columns recall the Greek Revival fashion that had spread through American country houses in the decades before.","c":"The pastel harmony is the picture's chief pleasure: warm yellows and peaches in the house and sky answer the cool blue of the water, and the checkered steps give the foreground a lively pattern. The figures, carriage and boats are small incidents in a view meant to be taken in at a glance, and the links between house, path and river seem loosely managed. It is gentler and more decorative than grander river views."},"nga:83883":{"d":"A view of West Point, the military academy on the Hudson River, painted in oil on a wide canvas of about 67 by 92 centimeters between about 1852 and 1858 by an unidentified American artist. Trees and mountains are the recorded features, and the museum's keywords, amusement and United States, suggest that people at leisure share the scene. The horizontal format was a favorite for river prospects in the generation of the Hudson River School, which taught Americans to see their own scenery as a worthy subject.","i":"West Point, on a sharp bend of the Hudson, was already a landmark in the 1850s, both as the United States Military Academy, founded in 1802, and as one of the favorite scenic stops on the steamboat tour of the river. Views of it served tourists and patriots at once. The Hudson was celebrated as America's answer to the Rhine, and a picture of the Point could stand for national scenery, discipline and progress.","c":"The subject is a safe, saleable one, and the picture follows the Hudson River formula: trees to frame the view, mountains to close it. Souvenir views of this kind are measured against the painters who made the formula, and they rarely match those painters' command of light and atmosphere. Their value lies in recording a place already becoming a tourist attraction, and in the evidence they give of what ordinary buyers wanted on their walls."},"nga:34106":{"d":"A woman of the mid-nineteenth century is the subject of this portrait, painted in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 64 centimeters by an unidentified American artist. The sitter is unnamed. At this size, roughly thirty by twenty-five inches, a portrait usually shows the head and upper body and was made to hang in a parlor or sitting room. The face and the dress, with their evidence of fashion and status, would have been the heart of the picture, as in most commissions of the kind.","i":"An anonymous portrait of a lady presents rank and respectability in terms the period understood: a composed face, fashionable dress, and perhaps a few chosen accessories. By the late 1850s the cheap likeness had largely passed to photography, so a painted portrait on canvas signaled a more considered and costly commission, one meant to stay in the family as an heirloom. Without the sitter's name, nothing more specific about her can be said.","c":"Portraits by unrecorded hands are hard to rank, but two questions can be put to any of them: whether the face is observed rather than formulaic, and whether the dress supports the head rather than overwhelms it. By the late 1850s the best portraitists had learned to temper photographic exactness with painterly warmth; a provincial portrait usually meets that standard only in part, and this one is best treated as a representative example of its type."},"nga:59889":{"d":"A wide panel, painted in oil on wood and measuring about 59 by 95 centimeters, shows the end of a sporting contest. The title and the museum's keyword 'sports' point to a race, and the recorded motifs, a house and a tree, place the action in the open air with a building nearby. It dates from around 1860 and is the work of an unidentified American painter. In the mid-nineteenth century the finish of a race, especially a horse race, was a favorite moment for pictures and prints alike.","i":"Racing, above all trotting and thoroughbred racing, drew large crowds in the United States around 1860, and painters and print publishers catered to a public that liked to relive the decisive moment, when horses and drivers strain toward the line while judges and spectators look on. If this is a horse race, as seems likely, the picture belongs to that popular sporting tradition; the title alone does not settle it.","c":"A finish is a hard subject for a self-taught hand: it asks for speed, a crowd and a clear winner, all in a static panel. The wide format suits the theme, since it lets the action run across the picture, and sporting pictures of this kind reward a viewer for their eagerness more than their accuracy. As a record of what Americans cheered at in 1860 the picture is more telling than it is accomplished."},"nga:45961":{"d":"A battle rages across a grassy field in vivid greens, blues, browns and whites. At the center a man flails backward on a rearing chestnut horse, while a goateed man in a high-crowned hat with a gray feather grips its reins and a white horse shies beyond. Another horse lies on the ground with a man draped over its saddle; two men help a wounded soldier, and others lie dead or are carried off on a stretcher. Troops in dark blue advance behind a flag with seventeen visible stars, while gray-clad troops with a seven-star flag press in from the left through cannon smoke.","i":"Self-taught battle pictures of the Civil War rarely show a named engagement; they distill the war into a few repeated types: the rearing horse, the standard-bearer, the wounded, the dead. The two flags let the viewer sort the sides. The second, with its seven stars, recalls the first Confederate national flag of spring 1861, which may mean the painter had the war's first months in mind. The museum dates the picture '1861 or after', so it could have been painted well later.","c":"The museum calls the style almost cartoonish, and the description bears that out: flat, saturated color, simplified horses, a packed center. Yet the lack of polish serves the subject. Academic battle painting of the period favored heroic gesture; here the dead, the stretcher and the wounded receive the same flat plainness as the flags. The result is naive in drawing but unusually unglamorous, a testimony to how the war was imagined at home."},"nga:43435":{"d":"Under a pale blue sky, hills rise behind water dotted with small red rowboats. At right of center an American flag flies from a pole; before it a man in a blue uniform with long buttons and a red belt faces the viewer, holding the broken halves of a chain. A woman in a red dress and a child in a black suit face the flagpole, with a small dog at lower right. At left a mounted soldier raises another American flag while a second banner trails on the ground. A tall white monument and an arch topped by a large gray bird stand behind.","i":"Painted in 1863 or later, the picture is probably a patriotic allegory of emancipation and Union victory: the broken chain is the standard emblem of freedom from slavery, and the Emancipation Proclamation took effect on January 1, 1863. A flag dragged along the ground was a customary sign of defeat, and the monument and the arch with its large bird, perhaps an eagle, tie the scene to national memory. The exact meaning of some details is not recorded.","c":"Allegory demands a command of symbol, and this painter has the symbols but treats them with a self-taught plainness. The strength is the frontal man with his broken chain, an emblem the eye finds at once. The weaker side is the crowding of motifs and the uniform blue-gray flesh tone, which makes the group look like an unreal stage tableau. It deserves attention as an unusually direct civilian response to the war."},"nga:34115":{"d":"A seated man faces the viewer in an upper-body portrait: pale skin, a prominent nose, deep-set eyes and a neutral expression, with dark hair brushed back and a dark, neatly trimmed beard. He wears a black suit, a white shirt and a black bow tie. The brown background is lighter behind his head and darker toward the edges, a simple device that makes the face the center of attention. The title names the sitter as Abraham Lincoln. The canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, dates from about 1864 or later.","i":"Lincoln's face became one of the most widely reproduced of the nineteenth century, and the bearded likeness of his presidency, a look he adopted after the 1860 election, is the one most people know. After his assassination in April 1865 the demand for painted portraits rose sharply, and painters of every level of skill produced them, many working from photographs. A plain black suit and an unadorned ground suit his public image as a serious, plain-spoken leader.","c":"Next to the great photographs of Lincoln by Mathew Brady's studio and Alexander Gardner, which record every furrow, a painted likeness like this tends to settle for an icon. Its virtue is restraint: no props, no rhetoric, a calm head against a warm ground. The features are present, but the expression is neutral, and the inner force that marks the best images of him is missing. Competent rather than searching."},"nga:34057":{"d":"A man is shown from the torso up, turned slightly to the right but looking directly at the viewer. His forehead is high and balding, with thin hair on top and long, curly hair at the sides and back; he has a prominent nose and a full white beard and mustache covering much of the lower face. He wears a dark garment with a high collar. The background is plain and dark, with subtle shifts of tone, and he holds nothing and wears no visible jewelry. The canvas measures about 69 by 56 centimeters and dates from around 1865.","i":"The title names the sitter as Charles Loring Elliott, presumably the New York portrait painter of that name (1812-1868), so this is probably a likeness of one artist by another whose hand is unrecorded. Painters often portrayed colleagues as tokens of friendship and professional regard. The absence of palette, brush or studio props shifts attention from the profession to the person. Elliott himself was regarded in his day as one of New York's leading portraitists.","c":"The strength of the portrait is its directness: the steady gaze, the white beard bright against the dark ground and the long hair framing a bald crown make a face that is memorable and unflattering in the best sense. There is no attempt at drama, and the dark clothing offers little below the neck, so the painting stands or falls on the head. It stands, as a plain and sympathetic record of an older man."},"nga:42481":{"d":"A half-length portrait shows an officer standing with one hand at his waist. He has a pointed beard and mustache, arched eyebrows and a direct gaze, and his dark hair lies close to the head beneath a dark blue hat with a yellow emblem lettered US. His dark blue uniform has yellow buttons and shoulder insignia, a belt in yellow and red, and a yellow glove on his hand. The background is a muted, textured gray. The small panel, about 53 by 40 centimeters, was painted in oil on wood around 1865.","i":"A colonel commanded a regiment, the unit around which Civil War armies were organized, and many volunteer colonels had been civilians only months before. A portrait in uniform, gloved and belted, recorded the rank for the family and the community, and many such pictures were made at the war's end or to commemorate service. Because the sitter is known here only by his rank, the picture now reads as an image of a type as much as of a man.","c":"The upright pose and the direct stare give the sitter authority, and the painter makes good use of the yellow accents, buttons, belt, glove and hat badge, against the dark blue and the gray ground. The panel is small and the setting bare, with none of the battle backdrops or props that grander officers' portraits favored. It is a modest but credible record of a man proud of his rank, and its economy is its virtue."},"nga:43640":{"d":"George Washington, as the title names him, stands at the center of a small canvas, facing forward with his hands slightly raised at his sides. He has a serious expression and white or gray hair beneath a tricorne hat, and wears dark formal clothes with a white collar and a white apron marked with symbols. Columns flank him, one supporting an object, the other standing near a stone with a tool and a small seatlike structure. An archway behind him bears the letter G, and the floor is patterned in gold and black. Brown and red tones dominate.","i":"The apron, the pair of columns, the letter G, the patterned floor and the stone with its tool are stock imagery of Freemasonry, in which the G is commonly read as standing for geometry and for God. Washington joined the fraternity in the early 1750s, and nineteenth-century American lodges prized his membership as proof of the order's patriotism. The picture, painted around 1868, therefore presents the national hero as a Masonic exemplar, not as a general or president.","c":"This is less a portrait than an emblem. The symmetrical columns, the frontal figure and the legible letter G are arranged so that a brother of the lodge could read them at a glance, and the likeness matters less than the attributes around it. The result is stiff but earnest, and its interest lies in the ingenuity of fitting so much symbolism onto a canvas only about 38 by 31 centimeters."},"nga:42504":{"d":"A white vase with gold trim and pale blue shadows stands in the middle of a table covered with a brown-and-white checked cloth with fringed edges. It holds a bouquet of tulips, daisies and roses in white, red, blue, orange and pink, among green stems and leaves. Grapes, pears and peaches in yellow, pink and blue-green lie around the vase on the cloth, and the background behind it all is black. The upright canvas, about 76 by 56 centimeters, was painted around 1870 by an unidentified American artist.","i":"Combining flowers and fruit in one arrangement is a convention inherited from Dutch and Flemish still life, which gathered blooms of different seasons into bouquets that never existed in a garden. Here tulips, daisies and roses share a vase, with ripe grapes, pears and peaches at its foot. In Victorian America such pictures expressed abundance and domestic comfort, and they hung in dining rooms and parlors where rich color suited a show of prosperity.","c":"The black ground is the picture's main device: it makes the colors glow and keeps the eye on the vase, whose white and gold stand out as the high note. The checked cloth adds pattern and a firm base. The centered, frontal arrangement is conventional, but it is lively and decorative, and a good example of the parlor still life at its most generous."},"nga:43713":{"d":"Aurora, the Roman goddess of dawn, is the subject of this wide panel, painted in oil on wood and measuring about 61 by 82 centimeters in the middle of the nineteenth century by an unidentified American artist. The museum classes it as a mythological painting with a classical theme. In the long European tradition, Aurora appears as a young woman who flies or rides ahead of the sun, scattering flowers or opening the gates of day in a sky of rose and gold. Whatever its exact design, the panel belongs to that tradition of dawn pictures.","i":"Aurora, called Eos by the Greeks, is Homer's rosy-fingered dawn, who rises each morning to announce the sun. Painters used her as an allegory of morning, hope and the renewal of the day, and the most famous image of her, Guido Reni's ceiling fresco in Rome of 1614, was endlessly copied in prints. A mid-nineteenth-century American painter taking up the theme was likely reaching for the prestige of a classical subject, perhaps by way of engravings after European models.","c":"Mythology was the hardest test for a painter without academy training, since it demands a convincing flying figure, flowing drapery and a luminous sky all at once. A panel like this probably lacks the grandeur of Reni's fresco, but dawn is a forgiving theme: a few warm tones low in a pale sky can carry the mood. Its interest lies in an American hand attempting the classical register."},"nga:45864":{"d":"A village is seen from a high viewpoint, its white buildings with red and white roofs scattered over rolling green hills. One white tower with a cross, right of center, marks the church of the title. Thin brown paths and white fences crisscross the green land between the houses, and the sky above is overcast, light gray with hints of blue. The picture is painted in a water-based medium on canvas, about 61 by 77 centimeters, and dates from the middle of the nineteenth century. Its title takes inventory, counting the houses one by one.","i":"Raised, map-like views of villages are a staple of self-taught painting, where the aim is to record a community whole, each house given its own place. The church tower, the one vertical in a field of rooftops, marks the center of local life, and the fences and paths show an ordered, settled landscape. The title's tally suggests an almost surveyor's pleasure in counting. The title names no place, and nothing here identifies the painter.","c":"Repetition is both the strength and the limit of the picture. The white houses with their red and white roofs set a gentle rhythm over the green hills, and the single tower gives the scene a focus. But the buildings are spread throughout the canvas, so the village has no clear hierarchy and little depth. It succeeds as a tidy, charming record, not as a drama."},"nga:50263":{"d":"A watermelon rests on its side against a solid black ground, with a thin slice cut from it and set in front, slightly to the left. The whole fruit is oval, its rind in dark and light green and divided from the flesh by a thick white line; the exposed flesh is pink, dotted with small black seeds. The picture is a reverse painting on glass, about 36 by 46 centimeters, made in the middle of the nineteenth century by an unidentified American artist. The painter worked on the back of the glass, so the viewer sees the image through it.","i":"Reverse painting on glass reverses the usual order of work: highlights and details go on first and the background last, so the image is built from front to back and sealed behind the glass. It was a long-established craft in Europe and America. Sliced melons, with their bright color and message of summer plenty, were a favorite still-life subject from the Peale family onward, and a cut slice, as here, invites the viewer to imagine the taste.","c":"The scheme is bold and simple: pink flesh and black seeds against a black ground, with a single slice to break the symmetry. The glass gives the colors a glossy depth that canvas cannot, though the method favors flat, graphic shapes over subtle modeling. It is a small, forceful image, and a good example of what reverse painting does well."},"nga:50693":{"d":"A long brown table carries an ornate vase of flowers in red, pale pink, white, yellow and blue, with green flowers and stems between them, and a spread of fruit on either side. At left are grapes in a wicker basket, a pineapple, red berries in a small white bowl, three plums and two pears. At right a platter holds watermelon slices and a yellow gourd or pumpkin, beside two lemons, two peaches, two apples and a bowl of cherries. A light green wall rises behind. The canvas, about 67 by 105 centimeters, dates from the middle of the nineteenth century.","i":"Fruits that ripen in different seasons, from cherries to pumpkins, share the table, so the picture shows an ideal abundance, not a single harvest. The pineapple, an expensive import and a traditional emblem of hospitality, and the lemons add a note of luxury. In mid-nineteenth-century America such overflowing tables hung in dining rooms as a declaration of prosperity and good living, descended from Dutch and Flemish still life but with few of its warnings about vanity.","c":"The picture is a catalogue of fruit more than a composition. The vase stands at the center with the foods divided evenly on either side, and each item is given its own space, so the eye moves from object to object instead of being drawn into a single effect. The wide format spreads the arrangement like a frieze. As a display of abundance it is generous; as a design it is orderly and rather static."},"nga:50822":{"d":"Towers and buildings crowd rolling green hills around a pale gray body of water. In the lower right, ships with billowing sails glide on a wide waterway that branches into three rivers, each crossed by several bridges. The buildings range from modest houses to soaring structures, some like modern skyscrapers and others like medieval fortresses. The most modern sit farther off, resting on gray and blue clouds beneath a pale yellow sky. The palette is muted: earth tones, soft greens, browns, grays, blues and off-white. The canvas measures about 73 by 103 centimeters.","i":"Imaginary cities have a long history in art, from the ideal cities of the Renaissance to visions of a heavenly Jerusalem, and buildings resting on clouds recall the old image of a celestial city. This painter instead mixes medieval and modern architecture and sets the newest towers in the sky, as if the future were floating above the past. The painter and purpose are unrecorded, so the picture is best read as an imaginative vision of an ideal city, not a fixed allegory.","c":"The invention is what counts. The three-river plan, the mixture of fortress and skyscraper forms and the clouds that carry the modern city make a coherent dream, held together by a muted palette that keeps it from fairy-tale sweetness. The weakness, as often with visionary painting, is that the architecture reads as pattern more than structure, so the city is easier to admire as an idea than to enter as a place."},"nga:52966":{"d":"A wintry landscape is seen from a slightly raised viewpoint. Rolling hills recede toward distant mountains, dotted with small houses and snowy trees. In the foreground people gather outside a yellow cabin, several of them soldiers in blue uniforms and tricorn hats holding tall guns, and one rider carries an American flag. At the center a man in a blue-and-gold uniform and a cloak sits on a white horse, while two small fires send up billows of gray smoke. More riders cross a path through the snowy hills. The brushwork is smooth and detailed, in blues, grays, whites, browns and greens.","i":"The title places Washington with the Continental Army at Valley Forge, Pennsylvania, where the troops wintered in 1777-78, building log huts and enduring hunger and disease. The episode became a byword for patriotic endurance, and in the mid-nineteenth century, when Washington imagery filled prints, books and schoolrooms, painters returned to it often. The mounted commander calmly overlooking his men is the usual way of presenting him: steady, dignified and confident of eventual success.","c":"The handling is smoother and more detailed than much anonymous history painting of the period, and the cold palette of blues, grays and whites conveys winter convincingly. Washington on his white horse is the clear focus, with fires and smoke adding atmosphere. But the scene is orderly rather than harrowing, closer to a pageant than to the privation for which the winter is remembered. It is competent patriotic illustration more than inspired history painting."},"nga:59891":{"d":"A sliced watermelon sits on a white plate. Half of the fruit is intact on the right, its dark green rind enclosing red-pink flesh dotted with black seeds and smaller white ones; the other half, at the left, has been cut into sections of different sizes. The plate rests on a gray surface with black shadows, against a gray background. The austere setting leaves the melon as the sole subject. The canvas measures about 46 by 61 centimeters and was painted in the middle of the nineteenth century by an unidentified American artist.","i":"Cutting a melon open was a way of showing it at its best, proving its ripeness and offering the viewer the pleasure of the fruit's interior, and painters liked the challenge of rendering seeded flesh against the hard curve of the rind. In nineteenth-century America the watermelon stood for summer plenty. By setting it alone on a plain plate, the painter presents a simple pleasure with none of the elaborate abundance of grander fruit pieces.","c":"The gray ground and gray table are a restrained choice that sets the red-pink flesh sparkling, and the uneven cut sections give a lively, irregular rhythm against the intact half. There is little else to look at, and the picture depends entirely on the painter's feeling for texture: the seeds, the flesh, the dark rind. Without a vase, drapery or any display of virtuosity, it has a modern plainness that suits the subject."},"nga:42500":{"d":"The title names a raccoon hunt, and the museum's keyword is 'sports'. The picture, painted in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 101 centimeters in the third quarter of the nineteenth century, belongs to the American tradition of rural sporting scenes. A coon hunt was traditionally a night pursuit, with hunters following hounds by lantern light until the dogs drove the raccoon up a tree. A painter of the subject has a choice of moments: the chase through the woods, or the climax when the hounds bay at a raccoon treed above them.","i":"Raccoon hunting was a widespread rural pastime in the nineteenth-century United States, especially in the South and Midwest, a social sport in which men and boys followed prized dogs through the night woods. Sporting subjects were popular with the growing picture-buying public, and prints by firms such as Currier and Ives made hunting scenes familiar in homes and taverns alike. This canvas belongs to that appetite for the outdoors, which grew as American life became more urban.","c":"A night hunt is a demanding subject: it asks the painter to make darkness, lantern light, moving dogs and a quarry in the branches read clearly at once. Self-taught painters usually simplify, and the best of them find a vivid story in a few clear forms. With the painter unrecorded, the picture is best valued as testimony to a popular rural sport rather than for any single passage of painting."},"nga:50825":{"d":"Fruit is the only subject recorded for this small panel, about 31 by 41 centimeters, painted in oil on wood sometime between about 1865 and 1880 by an unidentified American artist. The size and the wooden support point to an intimate tabletop still life, the kind of picture made for a domestic wall or a dining room. Panels of this type usually carry a modest grouping, a few fruits set close to the viewer on a table or ledge and lit to show their color and texture.","i":"Fruit still lifes were the bread and butter of the American picture market after the Civil War, supplied by painters of every grade and echoed in the colorful chromolithographs that reached thousands of homes in the 1860s and 1870s. Their message was easy: ripeness, abundance and the comforts of the table, with a distant memory of the Dutch idea that fruit also speaks of time passing. Few viewers would have asked for more than the pleasure of well-painted fruit.","c":"Small fruit panels are judged on observation, not novelty: how light settles on skins and how each fruit is weighed against its neighbors and its ground. The format favors intimacy, and the best examples have a closeness that grander still lifes lack. This one belongs to a thoroughly established tradition and is best taken as an honest domestic picture."},"nga:45859":{"d":"A calico cat and two kittens lie down or play with a ball of yarn in this horizontal picture. At left, the largest cat, honey brown and black on white, rests with her chest toward the viewer and gazes out with piercing yellow eyes; a lace-trimmed cloth hides most of her body. In front lies a striped kitten, legs and tail toward the viewer, looking right with lime-green eyes. At right the second kitten, white with gray and black splotches, rises on its hind paws to tangle carnation-pink yarn. Behind are a straw-yellow rug and a slate-blue wall with pale floral patterns.","i":"Pictures of cats at play were a small industry in the later nineteenth century, when pets and children became favorite subjects for a public that valued sentiment and domestic comfort. The ball of yarn, the lace-trimmed cloth and the patterned wall make the scene a parlor, and the kittens add a touch of gentle humor. Cat pictures by painters such as Henriette Ronner-Knip, much collected in the 1870s and 1880s, show the taste this small picture shares.","c":"The museum remarks on the precise brushwork in the fur and the shining whiskers, and it is the right thing to praise: the markings of the three cats are individually observed, and the large cat's direct yellow stare gives the scene a jolt of personality that keeps it from sentimentality. The wall and rug are more conventional backdrops. At about thirty centimeters across, it is a small, finely worked cabinet picture in a popular vein."},"nga:42493":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 68 by 119 centimeters, gathers the yachts that won the America's Cup into a single race that never happened. The title says the regatta is imagined: the boats belong to different years and were never all on the water together. The museum's keyword is 'vehicle', and the picture belongs to the family of ship portraits, in which each hull is meant to be recognized and shown to advantage. It dates from 1889 or later, by an unidentified American painter.","i":"The America's Cup began in 1851, when the schooner America beat a British fleet around the Isle of Wight; the trophy then passed to the New York Yacht Club, whose members defended it against challengers for more than a century. By the 1880s the contest was a matter of national pride, followed in the newspapers, and a picture assembling the American winners turned that pride into a single, flattering image.","c":"The idea is livelier than most yacht portraits, a parade across the years that flatters both the sport and the nation. The danger in such composites is a catalogue effect, hulls lined up like specimens on a stage-set sea, and the wide canvas is the painter's best defense, since it can stretch the fleet into a convincing race. As a piece of sporting patriotism it is engaging."},"nga:59886":{"d":"A farm outside Doylestown, in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, is the subject of this oil painting, about 67 by 93 centimeters, made around 1890 by an unidentified American artist. The museum's keyword is 'exterior'. Farm portraits of this kind typically show the house, barns and fields from a slightly raised viewpoint, with each building clearly seen and the land neatly ordered, as a proud record of a property rather than an impression of nature. The wide canvas gives room for the whole holding.","i":"Farm portraits were commissioned by owners, or sold by traveling painters, as proof of a family's prosperity and years of work; a well-kept house, full barns and tidy fences were the signs of a farmer's standing. County atlases of the 1870s and 1880s offered lithographed views of subscribers' farms in the same spirit. Doylestown, the county seat of Bucks County, lay in an old and settled farming region, where such pictures recorded generations on the land.","c":"As a type, the farm portrait trades atmosphere for clarity. Its best examples have a bright, ordered flatness, with every building and field given its due, and an affection for the property that no landscape painter could match. The title's careful naming of a specific place suggests that locality mattered here: this is a portrait of somewhere in particular."},"nga:42496":{"d":"Seen from above, three farm buildings flank a parchment-yellow road that angles up to the horizon: two brown-sided barns with a turquoise bird house at left, a tan house with a porch at right, all under burgundy roofs. Blue or red fences enclose horses, cattle, pigs, dogs and a dozen chickens, and a flag flies behind the house. Beyond, stone walls and black fences divide fields where riders hunt and horses graze, and rows of stylized trees shelter deer. Indigo hills close the view beneath a pink sky. Animals and buildings are outlined in black and filled with flat color on a window shade.","i":"Mahantango Valley lies in the Pennsylvania German farming country of central Pennsylvania, a region known for order, thrift and well-stocked barnyards, and its local painters made decorative farm views on cheap supports at hand, including cloth window shades. The flag, the hunters and riders and the crowded animals together describe a prosperous, self-sufficient and patriotic community. The painter is unrecorded, and the picture is probably an idealized portrait of a way of life more than a record of one farm.","c":"The black outlines and flat fields of color turn the farm into a pattern, and the pleasure lies in the density of incident: the bird house, the dozen chickens, the deer among the trees, the road cutting diagonally to the horizon. Perspective is a matter of stacking, not of recession, and the window shade support, its weave still visible, adds to the homespun character. A vivid example of Pennsylvania German farm painting, and entirely unforced."},"nga:42497":{"d":"A two-story farmhouse seen from a high viewpoint stands at the left, with white clapboard walls, a small brown porch, dark windows with green shades and a roof of red, blue and black tiles. Three tall trees rise behind it, one green, one yellow, one sparse and dark. At the bottom a man and a woman in formal blue clothes, both with pale gray skin, stand in front of the house among a rabbit, a dog, a cat, a horse and three cows. To the right, flat green fields, brown paths, more white farmhouses and pale blue mountains reach the horizon.","i":"Mahantango Valley lies in central Pennsylvania, in Pennsylvania German farm country where families took pride in neat, productive holdings. Farm pictures from the valley present the farm as a complete world: house, trees, animals and neighbors' buildings, with the owners standing in front in their best clothes. The painter is not recorded, and the picture probably aims at an ideal portrait of the household and its prosperity, not a literal view.","c":"The flat figures, stylized trees and patterned roof turn the farm into something closer to a sampler than a view: depth is shown by stacking fields up the canvas, and every object is given the same clear edge. That is the charm of the type, and it is done with conviction here, with bright color and a love of detail. The high viewpoint lets the whole property be read at once."},"nga:42505":{"d":"A bouquet of large, pale pink roses fills a green vase decorated with yellow flowers. The roses spread outward from the center to occupy most of the upper canvas, among green leaves and smaller clusters of yellow blooms. The vase rests on a dark, richly brown wooden table whose surface runs diagonally from the lower left corner toward the center, and the wall behind is a pale gray. The canvas is small, about 41 by 37 centimeters, and dates from the last quarter of the nineteenth century.","i":"Roses were the favorite flower of Victorian painters and their customers, loved for their beauty and understood as emblems of love and of fleeting bloom. A single vase of roses on a polished table was a staple of the parlor picture trade, which supplied affordable decorative still lifes to households that could not buy work by leading painters. The painter is unrecorded, and the picture carries no more message than the pleasure of the flowers themselves.","c":"The composition is more adventurous than the subject suggests. The roses push out to fill the top of the canvas, the table's diagonal brings the eye in from the lower left, and the pale gray wall isolates the pinks. The yellow pattern of the vase echoes the small yellow flowers among the roses. It is small and unpretentious, and its fresh, clear color is its chief attraction."},"nga:45865":{"d":"Across a broad stretch of water, several buildings of different colors stand along the far shore, with trees and white fences between them. In the center a small green island, a steamship and a sailboat share the water with black and white birds. At the lower right, tall dark-green trees on a small piece of land shelter a small figure in dark clothes, seated beneath flying birds. The sky is cloudy, and the water mirrors it. The canvas, about 51 by 85 centimeters, is a wide, calm view from the last quarter of the nineteenth century.","i":"A steamship and a sailboat sharing the same water speak of a period of transition, when steam was displacing sail on rivers and coasts, while the quiet village across the water suggests settled, small-scale life. The small seated figure under the trees is a device from Romantic landscape, a stand-in who invites the real viewer to sit down and look. No place is named, and the painter is unrecorded, so the scene reads as a type of American river town.","c":"The pleasures are quiet ones: the mirrored clouds in the water, the string of colorful houses along the far shore and the scatter of small incidents, steamship, sailboat, birds, across a wide, calm band. The incidents are placed more than integrated, and the foreground trees are a conventional frame. It is a pleasant, unambitious picture that rewards unhurried looking."},"nga:46099":{"d":"A man sits facing forward in a portrait painted in oil on a canvas of about 77 by 63 centimeters around 1827. He has an angular face, sideburns and dark curly hair, and his expression is neutral with a hint of a smile. He wears a dark coat with a fur-lined collar, a white high-collared shirt and a red waistcoat decorated with a pin. The background is a dark, muted brown. The question mark in the title records that the sitter's identity is uncertain, and the painter is unidentified.","i":"The dark coat with a fur collar, the high white collar and the red waistcoat belong to the fashion of the late 1820s, and the fur lining marks a man of some means, dressed for a cold climate or for show. Portraits like this were commissioned to record a man's standing for his family and community. The question mark in the title is a reminder of how easily sitters lose their names: the picture tells his rank and manner, but not his certain identity.","c":"The face does the work: the angular features and the faint smile give the sitter a more human presence than the stiff gravity of much portraiture of the time, and the red waistcoat is the one bright note against the dark coat and brown ground. The rest is plain, without visible props or setting. It is a direct, serviceable portrait whose appeal rests on the sitter's expression."},"nga:50826":{"d":"A path curves away from the viewer, first toward the left and then back toward the center, between bright green and yellow fields and dark trees. At the far left the black silhouettes of people seem to walk along it. Rolling hills and a blue sky fill the distance, a gray-walled house with a red roof sits among the hills at right, and behind the hills in the center a patch of pale yellow swirls hangs in the sky, which may be sunlight, cloud or smoke. The canvas is wide, about 71 by 105 centimeters.","i":"The title and the museum's keyword, fantasy, mark this as an invented, simplified landscape rather than the view of a real place. The winding path that leads the eye into the picture is a very old device, and the silhouetted walkers invite the viewer to follow. The pale swirl in the sky is open to reading as sun, cloud or smoke. Self-taught painters often reduced landscape to pattern: bright fields, dark trees, stylized forms. The aim may have been decorative as much as descriptive.","c":"Design is the strength: the S-curve of the path, the contrast of dark trees against yellow-green fields and the pale swirl in the sky are arranged for effect, not for observation. It works as pattern and mood, with the directness of folk art. The weakness is a certain vagueness of intent, since the sky motif is ambiguous and the figures are mere shadows. It is an engaging oddity more than a resolved picture."},"nga:52960":{"d":"A grassy green plain is covered with horses of every size and color, black, brown, white, tan and white with black spots, rolling on the ground, running, sitting or rearing on their hind legs. Below the center a mustached rider in a black shirt, yellow trousers, tall black boots and a gray cowboy hat sits a black horse and faces the viewer. Bushes with yellow flowers dot the grass, pointed white mountains rise in the distance, and a bright sun shines near the top left of a light green-blue sky with white clouds.","i":"The title evokes the open range of the American West in spring, when new grass returned and animals were turned out to graze. By the 1880s the cowboy had become a national figure through illustrated magazines, dime novels and touring Wild West shows. The picture is probably less a record of ranching than an image of western freedom: the horses roll, run and rear for the joy of it, and the lone rider looks on like a keeper of the herd.","c":"Exuberance is the appeal: dozens of horses are shown in different attitudes, rolling, running, sitting, rearing, so the plain seems alive with motion. The result is closer to a catalogue of horse poses than to a herd moving as one, and the rider is small and static. The drawing is evidently untutored, but the sunny palette and the sheer abundance give the picture an infectious good humor."},"nga:52962":{"d":"Three figures gather around a fire in a natural setting. One cooks outside a conical shelter, possibly a tepee, while a woman and a child stand nearby. Behind them are hills, distant blue-green mountains and a sky of pink and blue, and a canoe rests on the bank of a river. The brushwork is detailed in the trees and the water, the palette is made of earth tones, greens and blues, and the fire casts a warm glow. The canvas, about 46 by 66 centimeters, was painted in the second half of the nineteenth century.","i":"Scenes of Native American domestic life were popular with eastern audiences in the nineteenth century, in paintings and prints by artists such as George Catlin and Seth Eastman. Pictures made by outsiders often joined elements from different peoples and regions, such as a tepee and a canoe, into one generic scene, and they usually presented Native life as peaceful and timeless, on the verge of disappearing. That framing reflected the pressures of westward expansion more than the lives of the people shown.","c":"The museum notes detailed brushwork in the trees and river, and that is where the picture is strongest: the warm glow of the fire against the cool blues and greens of the distance gives the small scene real atmosphere. The figures are small and generic, and the whole is closer to a staged tableau than to observation, as is typical of the genre. It says more about the period's imagination than about the people it shows."},"nga:59918":{"d":"A large cat's head rises from tall grass in a forest setting, the rest of its body unseen. Its brown and black fur is creamy around the nose and cheeks, its whiskers are long and white, its ears are pricked and its yellow eyes look upward. In its mouth hangs a bird with blue and green feathers, a black head and a long black beak. On either side stands a thin tree with sparse branches, each holding a bird, a bright red one at left and a teal, green and black one at right, against a light blue sky.","i":"A cat with its catch is a natural subject for a household picture, and the isolated head, framed by birds and trees, suggests a decorative arrangement more than a studied scene. The brightly colored birds look chosen for color, with hues that suggest imagination more than ornithology. Pictures like this offered a moment of nature's drama, predator and prey, in a form safe to hang in a parlor. The painter is unrecorded and the date is given only as probably the second half of the century.","c":"The head, emerging straight from the grass with ears up and eyes lifted, has a startling directness: the cat looks like a predator caught in the act and quite unapologetic. The thin symmetrical trees and the birds on either side give the picture a decorative, almost heraldic balance. Setting and scale are naive, but the central image is memorable, and the composition has more wit than many more polished animal pictures."},"nga:59926":{"d":"A figure stands in profile facing left on a tall wooden panel, about 136 centimeters high and 58 wide, against a plain light yellow ground. The figure wears a colorful headdress with large feathers, a red garment with decorative patterns, brown leggings, dark shoes and a bead necklace, with long black hair. One hand holds a bundle, and a long object is carried over the shoulder. The face is simply drawn. The panel is oil on wood from the second half of the nineteenth century, and its other side carries a matching figure turned the other way.","i":"Tobacconists in the United States used figures of Native Americans as shop signs through much of the nineteenth century, partly because tobacco is a plant of the Americas. Most were carved in the round; this one is a flat painted panel. Such signs were made by non-Native craftsmen from stock imagery, feathered headdress, leggings and beads, and say more about how Americans advertised and imagined Native people than about any individual or nation.","c":"As a design it is graphic and legible: a tall, flat figure in profile, strong colors, a plain ground, made to be read at a glance across a street, and the profile is the right choice for that job. The drawing is deliberately simple, with a plain face and little modeling. As a portrait or an ethnographic record it has no value; as American commercial folk art it is a vivid document of a commercial stereotype."},"nga:59927":{"d":"A figure stands upright in profile facing right against a plain, solid light ground, on the back of the same wooden panel, about 136 centimeters high and 58 wide. The figure has long black hair and wears a headdress of colorful leaves, a red garment with black markings and geometric patterns, tan pants, moccasin-like shoes and a yellow necklace. In the left hand is a cylindrical object tied with a yellow ribbon. The work is oil on wood from the second half of the nineteenth century, and it is the reverse of a sign whose front shows a matching figure facing left.","i":"Painted on both sides, the sign presumably read from either direction along a street, as a tobacconist's trade mark. The leaves in the headdress may be tobacco leaves, and the ribbon-tied cylinder may be a bundle of cigars or tobacco, which would make the figure an advertisement for the goods inside. The type was made by non-Native craftsmen for non-Native customers, and reflects a commercial stereotype of Native people rather than any real person or community.","c":"Seen in reverse, the figure reads as a deliberate pendant to the front: same stance, same colors, the profile now turned right so that the sign works from either direction. The clean silhouette, bold red garment and plain ground suit a sign that must speak across a street. It has the confidence of good commercial art and the limits of a stereotype."},"nga:73439":{"d":"A large schooner sits in the center of calm water, its white sails filling the middle of the picture and a small American flag flying from one mast. Another ship and a lighthouse lie on the horizon to the right. At the left, on a grassy bank, a woman and a child in dark dresses watch with their backs to the viewer, waving; the woman holds a white handkerchief. The water is blue, green and tan with faint white ripples under a pale gray sky, and faint blue mountains rise behind green hills with small white buildings.","i":"A woman and child waving from the shore at a ship is a familiar scene of maritime sentiment, speaking of those who wait on land while others go to sea. The American flag identifies the vessel's nationality, and the lighthouse marks the approach to a harbor. The schooner was a workhorse of American coastal trade and fishing, so the picture honors a familiar vessel and the people whose lives depended on it. The museum classes it as a topographical view.","c":"The big white sails make a calm pyramid against the pale sky, and the two dark figures at the left balance them with a human note and a sense of scale. The palette is quiet, and the waving gesture gives the scene an occasion that a plain ship portrait would lack. The mixture of ship portrait and sentimental genre is a modest but effective formula, and the picture carries it without strain."},"nga:46539":{"d":"A woman is shown from the chest up in profile, facing right. Her complexion is very pale, her eyebrows dark and arched, her nose pointed, her cheekbones high and her red lips set in a neutral expression. Her dark hair is piled in a voluminous bun on top of her head. She wears a high-collared garment with gray sleeves and a large black bow or ruffle at the neck over white ruffles. The background is dark reddish-brown on the right and cream and light gray on the left. The canvas measures about 61 by 51 centimeters and dates from around 1900.","i":"A strict profile is a deliberate choice, recalling Renaissance profile portraits and the cameo, and around 1900 it suited the taste for elegant line and flat tonal fields. The high bun, the black bow and the white ruffles are the fashion of the turn of the century. Identified only as Madame G, the sitter keeps her discretion, since an initial was a common way to shield a society sitter's name. The painter is unidentified.","c":"The profile is the strongest idea: it reduces the face to a line, and the contrast of very pale skin, dark hair and black bow carries the design. The background, dark reddish-brown on one side and cream-gray on the other, splits the canvas in a way that frames the head. The drawing is crisp, and the expression is neutral to the point of coolness. It is a stylish, rather impersonal society portrait."},"cma:169651":{"d":"An unidentified musician is shown at half-length, holding a score whose notation is legible although the piece has not yet been identified. He wears a black coat, a plain high-collared white shirt and a white cravat, a style that was fashionable among European elite and middle-class men around 1800. The picture is an unsigned oil painting on a wooden panel, wider than it is tall, about 44 by 60 centimeters.","i":"The black coat and plain white linen mark the sober, post-revolutionary fashion that replaced the silks and colors of the old court. The visible score names the sitter's profession or passion, and in Milan, where Appiani worked, music was a central part of public life. Because the notation can be read, it may one day identify the piece, and with it the sitter or the occasion; for now the portrait remains an image of a man defined by his art.","c":"Appiani is best known for frescoes and official portraits for Napoleon's court, and this small panel shows the same neoclassical clarity on an intimate scale: a plain dark coat set against pale linen, a calm outline, no ornament. The unusual horizontal format gives the sitter room, and the legible score is a rare touch of precise detail. It is quieter and less showy than his state portraits, and the better for it."},"cma:124091":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this portrait, painted in oil on a wooden panel of about 83 by 62 centimeters around 1518 by the Florentine master Andrea del Sarto. Del Sarto, nicknamed the faultless painter, was known for soft, melting modeling, warm harmonious color and a calm, balanced design, qualities his portraits share with his altarpieces. His portraits generally set the figure close to the picture plane against a plain or softly darkened ground.","i":"Portraits made up a small part of Andrea del Sarto's output, which was dominated by altarpieces and frescoes for Florentine churches and convents, so each portrait deserves attention. Portraits of women in Florence in the 1510s were often commissioned on betrothal or marriage, or to record a family's status, and this sitter, unnamed, was most likely a woman of means. The title is generic, and any identification beyond that is conjecture.","c":"Del Sarto's gifts, a soft modeling that melts light into shade and a harmonious color, suit a woman's portrait, and critics have long praised the quiet dignity of his sitters. The same smoothness can be a weakness, since it sometimes leaves psychological depth to the viewer's imagination. Among Florentine portraitists of the 1510s he is gentler than Pontormo and less penetrating than Raphael."},"met:437610":{"d":"A man is the subject of this portrait, painted in 1528-29, in the last years of Andrea del Sarto's life. It is oil on canvas, about 67 by 51 centimeters, but the paint layer was originally applied to a wooden panel and was transferred to canvas in a past restoration, a drastic treatment often used on panels that were failing. The sitter's name is not recorded, and the museum's keywords show only that it is a portrait of a man.","i":"Florence in 1528-29 was a republic, restored in 1527 when the Medici were driven out, and the city faced a siege by imperial and papal forces that began in the autumn of 1529. A portrait of a man made in these years may show a citizen of the republic, perhaps one with a stake in its fate, but his identity is unknown, and his dress and bearing speak simply of self-possession and standing. It is among del Sarto's last works; he died in 1530.","c":"The transfer from panel to canvas is the first thing a critic must allow for: such treatments tend to flatten the surface and abrade the subtlest glazes, so the picture is probably less supple than del Sarto left it. Among Florentine portraits of the late 1520s, those of Pontormo and Bronzino are tenser and more stylized; del Sarto's are calmer, and that calm is the quality to look for here."},"cma:117032":{"d":"The subject is the moment from Genesis when Abraham, ordered by God to sacrifice his son Isaac, is stopped at the last instant by an angel, and a ram is offered in the boy's place. Andrea del Sarto's panel, about 178 by 138 centimeters, is a large upright picture made around 1527. Pictures of this subject normally gather the old father, the bound boy, the knife, the angel and the ram into a tight group, and del Sarto's manner brings soft modeling and a balanced design to the drama.","i":"In Genesis 22 God tests Abraham's faith by commanding him to offer Isaac on a mountain, and the angel's intervention proves his obedience. Christians read the story as a prefiguration of Christ's sacrifice, with the father giving his only son and the boy carrying the wood for the fire, as Christ carried the cross. Florentines knew the subject well: it was the test panel set for the competition to make the Baptistery doors in 1401, and it remained a familiar subject.","c":"The story is violent, and del Sarto, a painter of grace more than terror, tends to resolve tension into balanced, rhythmic forms. That is both his gift and his limit: the figures are beautifully drawn and softly lit, but the horror of the knife is usually less felt than the harmony of the group. A critic would look here for how far the harmony yields to the drama."},"met:437609":{"d":"The Virgin Mary, the Christ Child, Saint Joseph and the young Saint John the Baptist form a close family group in this panel of about 136 by 101 centimeters, painted around 1528. Del Sarto, like other High Renaissance painters, usually built such groups as a pyramid, with figures linked by glances and touches in a setting close to the picture plane. The scale and subject suit a private chapel or a Florentine family's room.","i":"John the Baptist, the cousin of Jesus, appears as a child in Holy Family pictures, usually with a cross of reeds or a camel-hair garment, to foretell that he will baptize Christ. He was also the patron saint of Florence, which gave the subject particular appeal in the city's homes and chapels. Joseph, the quiet guardian, completes the household and helps turn a sacred story into a family scene.","c":"Del Sarto's Holy Families are admired for warmth and ease: the figures relate through glances and touches that feel natural, and his color is rich yet harmonious. Painted about 1528, near the end of his life, the panel belongs to a mature classicism soon eclipsed by a more nervous style. The smoothness can look like virtuosity at the expense of passion, but few painters make a sacred family feel so at home."},"cma:539189":{"d":"A scientist, or learned man, sits at a desk lit by a candle in a canvas of about 100 by 81 centimeters, dated 1755. Candlelit pictures of this kind descend from the Dutch tradition of Gerrit Dou and Godfried Schalcken, in which a single flame lights the figure and leaves the room around him in shadow. Anna Dorothea Therbusch, a German painter, submitted a candlelit picture when she applied to the Royal Academy in Paris.","i":"The museum tells a telling story about Therbusch's candlelit subject: it was unfashionable in Paris, yet she submitted one for admission to the Royal Academy of Fine Arts, where the male judges at first refused her, arguing that a woman could not have painted so accomplished a work. She agreed to paint under the committee's observation and was admitted on her second attempt. The scholar by candlelight is a type with a long history, symbolizing study, solitude and the light of knowledge.","c":"The candlelight effect is the test: one flame has to carry the picture, pooling warm light on the figure and desk and leaving deep shadow beyond. If the picture reflects the skill that made the Academy doubt her authorship, it shows command of a hard manner. The Dutch fine finish was out of step with Parisian taste for light Rococo color, so the picture stands apart, and its interest is as much historical as visual."},"met:926221":{"d":"A grove of ancient cedars is the subject of this oil sketch, about 41 by 60 centimeters, painted in 1837 by the French landscape painter Antoine-Alphonse Montfort. The recorded themes are forests, landscapes and trees. The support, oil on paper later laid down on canvas, is typical of studies made on the spot, where light paper was easier to carry than canvas, and the wide format suits a view of a whole grove rather than a single tree.","i":"The cedars of Lebanon were among the most famous trees of the Bible, symbols of strength, and the timber of Solomon's Temple according to scripture. In the nineteenth century European travelers made the journey to the surviving grove high in the Lebanese mountains almost as a pilgrimage; the poet Lamartine, who visited it in 1832, described it in his account of the Near East. A painter's study of the cedars joined biblical memory to Romantic wonder at the oldest living trees.","c":"The oil sketch from nature is among the most appealing forms of nineteenth-century landscape: quick, fresh, free of studio varnish. What a critic looks for is how well the painter grasps the cedars' massive trunks and spreading layers of foliage, their signatures. A study made on the spot cannot be judged by the standards of a finished picture; it is evidence of looking, and it shows where French landscape was most progressive."},"cma:97178":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child on her lap, set within a niche, in an oil panel of about 60 by 44 centimeters painted in the 1520s by the Florentine Antonio del Ceraiolo. The museum notes that the painting was extensively altered by restoration: at some point the Child's nakedness was covered with a painted cloth, and in 1941 the old overpaint was removed, bringing the picture closer to its original state.","i":"In Renaissance art the Child's nudity was deliberate: it declared that Christ was truly human, that he experienced temptation and that in his exposure he was without shame. Later taste, more prudish, covered such details, which is why the painted cloth was added. The history of this panel is therefore also a history of changing attitudes to the body in sacred art. The niche, a feature of devotional images, frames the figures like an altar.","c":"Madonna panels from Florentine workshops of the early sixteenth century are valued for their smooth finish and quiet piety, and this one preserves the type: a calm, symmetrical group, softly lit. A picture that has been repainted and cleaned has lost some of its original subtlety, and the restorations, as the museum notes, limit any judgment. Still, it is a clear instance of Florentine devotional painting after Leonardo and Raphael, conservative and gentle."},"cma:125817":{"d":"A woman's head is the subject of this small oil on canvas, about 46 by 38 centimeters, painted around 1739 by Antonio Guardi of Venice. The title gives no sitter's name, so the picture is probably a character head, a study of a type or a fancy picture in the Venetian taste of the time, rather than a commissioned portrait. Such heads were typically painted in rapid, fluid strokes, with an emphasis on expression, hair and costume.","i":"Antonio was the elder brother of the view painter Francesco Guardi and the leader of the family workshop in Venice, which produced figure paintings, altarpieces and decorative pictures for Venetian and foreign buyers. Heads of women, anonymous and idealized or characterful, were a staple of eighteenth-century taste, collected as decorative pictures for salons, and Venetian painters from Piazzetta to Tiepolo made them in quantity. The picture is best read as a study in feminine type and expression, not as a likeness.","c":"Guardi's figure painting is lively and nervous, with fluid brushwork and a taste for quick expression; compared with Piazzetta's solid chiaroscuro or Tiepolo's radiant color it is lighter, more sketch-like and less assured in structure. Yet that flickering touch, which recalls the shimmer of his brother's later vedute, has its own charm in a small head like this. It is an engaging minor work."},"nga:41601":{"d":"We float high above the land, looking across rolling hills toward a shimmering aquamarine bay at the left and mountains dotted with towns at the right, beneath an approaching storm. Only on close inspection do dozens of tiny figures appear: a man chops a tree, another sleeps on the grass and a third squats in a hollow trunk. On a hill at left a tall frame holds two flaming wheels; a robed figure kneels before it while others lie fallen or flee. Ships pitch in the harbor, and a wooden ship is under construction.","i":"Saint Catherine of Alexandria, condemned to be broken on spiked wheels, was saved when, according to the legend, the wheels burst into flames, killing her executioners; she was later beheaded. The painter treats the miracle as one small episode in a vast panoramic landscape, in the manner of Joachim Patinir and the world landscapes of sixteenth-century Antwerp, in which the sacred event is almost lost amid human activity and a changing sky.","c":"The pleasure is in discovery: a vast, hazy panorama in which tiny figures act out dozens of small incidents, some devout, some comic. The storm and sunlight over a far horizon are accomplished, and the humor of the squatting man is typical of Netherlandish worldliness. As a painting about a martyrdom it is oddly cheerful, the saint nearly an afterthought. The attribution to Matthys Cock is only a possibility, but the quality is high."},"nga:43515":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, seated and facing slightly right. He has a light complexion, a soft expression and straight brown hair mostly covered by a flat beret. He wears a dark brown robe with wide sleeves over a dark garment, with a white shirt collar showing, and holds a walking stick in his left hand and a chain with several keys in his right. The background is a muted dark green, and an inscription runs across the top of the panel. The oil on panel dates from 1542.","i":"An almoner distributed alms for a parish or charitable institution, a respected civic role in sixteenth-century Antwerp, where poor relief was organized by the city and its parishes. The keys, probably those of an alms chest, and the staff are probably attributes of his office, and the inscription at the top would have recorded details of the sitter. The portrait presents him as a responsible officer of the community, not as a private person.","c":"The portrait is direct and restrained: a seated man with a mild expression, his hands holding the signs of his charitable office against a plain green ground. The anonymous painter has an honest, unflattering hand, and the careful handling of the wide sleeves and the keys gives the picture solidity. It lacks the psychological penetration of the best Netherlandish portraitists, but it is a vivid civic document."},"nga:42395":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up in this small panel, slightly turned, his hands clasped over a folded cloth. His expression is serious, with prominent cheekbones, a furrowed brow, a light mustache and short brown hair mostly covered by a dark cap; he wears a dark garment with a high white ruffled collar and a ring on his left ring finger. The background is a muted green. The oil panel, about 24 by 19 centimeters, is dated 1543 and identifies the sitter as a member of the de Hondecoeter family.","i":"The small size and the coat of arms on the back suggest a portrait made for a family, perhaps as one half of a pair with the likeness of his wife; Antwerp in the 1540s was Europe's busiest commercial port, and its merchants and officials commissioned modest portraits as records of their standing. The clasped hands, folded cloth and ring are quiet signs of a respectable citizen. The sitter's first name is not recorded, and he is identified only by the family.","c":"Small Netherlandish portraits of this type succeed through frank observation: the furrowed brow, the angular cheekbones and the plain gaze give the sitter an unglamorous presence, and the white ruffle against the dark clothes and green ground is a neat, strong design. The painter is anonymous and competent, in the Antwerp manner of the 1540s. The beauty is in directness rather than finesse, and the modest scale suits its private purpose."},"nga:42396":{"d":"A heraldic coat of arms fills this small panel: a shield with diagonal stripes in black and gold, topped by an ornate silver helmet with golden flourishes, and above it a white swan. A scroll behind the swan carries the words TART SVIS VENV, and another at the base reads DE HONDECOVTRE with the date 1543. Golden yellow and dark gray leaf motifs spread across the black ground. The oil panel, about 24 by 19 centimeters, is the back of a portrait.","i":"Painted arms on the back of a portrait identified the sitter and his family. The scroll's motto, apparently in old French, Tart suis venu, seems to speak of arriving late, and it belongs to the family's heraldry together with the swan crest. Heraldic display of this kind was a mark of status among Antwerp's merchant families, who emphasized lineage as they rose in wealth, and it turned a small private portrait into a statement of family identity.","c":"As heraldic painting it is crisp and decorative: the black ground makes the gold stripes and the white swan stand out, and the elaborate foliage fills the space with controlled rhythm. A coat of arms is not a work of invention, but it demands exact drawing, and this one is cleanly done. Its purpose was to carry the family's identity, and that it does with some elegance."},"nga:42397":{"d":"A woman, identified in the title as the wife of a member of the de Hondecoeter family, is portrayed in this small oil panel of about 24 by 19 centimeters, dated 1543, a companion to the portrait of her husband. The museum records no further details of her dress or pose; portraits of married women in the Netherlands at this date typically show them in a white linen cap and a dark gown with a white collar, against a plain ground.","i":"A pair of small panels of husband and wife was a common way for a prosperous Antwerp household to record itself: modest in size, easily hung in a private room, with the arms of the husband's family on the reverse of his portrait identifying the pair. The wife is known only through her husband's connection, which shows how sixteenth-century records defined married women. Nothing in the record gives her own name.","c":"Without a named painter or a recorded description, the portrait can be judged only on type: modest scale, probably a plain, truthful face and careful detail of linen and dress, the virtues of mid-sixteenth-century Netherlandish portraiture. As a companion piece it gains from its context: set beside her husband's panel, it completes a small family record. Its charm is unpretentious honesty."},"cma:157433":{"d":"A sitter named Minnie Willard is the subject of this portrait, painted in oil on a canvas of about 97 by 66 centimeters in the 1860s by Archibald Willard, who shares her surname. The upright, nearly three-quarter-length format gives the sitter a substantial presence. Willard is best known for The Spirit of '76, the fife-and-drum picture that became one of America's most popular patriotic images, but he began as a decorative painter in small-town Ohio.","i":"A portrait with a shared surname probably records a relative, perhaps painted for the family rather than for a fee. In the 1860s, before The Spirit of '76 made him famous in the Centennial year of 1876, Willard was a young painter in Ohio, making portraits, humorous genre scenes and decorative work. A picture like this belongs to that early, local phase of his career, when the sitters were people he knew.","c":"Early family portraits show the skills a later popular painter started with: here the sturdy, upright format, the likely ease of a sitter seen by someone who knows her, and a plain directness that later informed the vigorous characters of The Spirit of '76. It is a modest, sincere document from a regional painter's early years."},"cma:157244":{"d":"A woman named Sarah Eliot Scoville is the sitter of this portrait, painted in oil on a canvas of about 86 by 69 centimeters in the 1830s by Asher B. Durand. The upright format on this scale, a half-length or three-quarter-length figure, was standard for the portrait of a respectable matron intended for a parlor wall, and it suits Durand's clear, firmly drawn manner of those years.","i":"In the 1830s Durand was the leading engraver in the United States, a reputation built on his print of Trumbull's Declaration of Independence, but he was moving into oil painting, making portraits to earn a living while he trained himself in landscape. A portrait like this belongs to that turning point, when his engraver's precision of drawing was applied to painting. He later became a leader of the Hudson River School.","c":"Durand's early portraits have the clarity of an engraver's draftsmanship: firm contours, an exact description of dress and a plain, honest likeness. They lack the painterly warmth of the portraitists he admired, and the formulas of pose are conventional. Still, the sitter's directness and the careful handling of fabric mark the work of a master of line learning the language of color."},"cma:143772":{"d":"A rocky outcropping rises amid lush vegetation and trees on a hillside in this oil sketch of about 43 by 61 centimeters, painted about 1849 by Asher B. Durand. The museum describes a seemingly random corner of the woods, seen intimately and cropped unusually close, in contrast with the sweeping vistas of many landscapes of the time. The sketch was painted outdoors, directly from nature, which was still uncommon among American artists.","i":"Durand belonged to the circle of Hudson River painters who made oil studies out of doors, as the English painter John Constable had done a generation earlier, and in his Letters on Landscape Painting of 1855 he urged young artists to study nature directly, down to single trees and rocks. Such sketches were not meant for sale but served as raw material for studio works, and they now seem strikingly modern. Before painting, Durand worked as an engraver, including on paper currency.","c":"The sketch is more adventurous than many of Durand's finished landscapes: the unusual cropping, the dense texture of leaves and rock, and the freedom from the conventional formula of sky, middle ground and distance anticipate the close-up studies of later painters and the Barbizon artists in France. Its lack of a vista is its strength; the viewer is placed inside the woods, not in front of them. It is among his freshest works."},"cma:136270":{"d":"A stream runs through a forest, with a vista opening beyond the trees, in this upright canvas of about 84 by 72 centimeters, painted in 1872 by Asher B. Durand in his mid-seventies. The title describes the structure of the picture: woodland in the foreground, water leading the eye inward and a distant view glimpsed through a gap in the trees. The format is taller than wide, which emphasizes the height of the trees.","i":"Forest interiors, in which the painter studies trees, rocks and a stream in close detail while allowing a distant glimpse beyond, were Durand's favorite late subjects, and they reflect his teaching that landscape should combine accurate observation of nature with moral and spiritual feeling. The vista at the end may be read as a sign of hope, a larger space reached through the shade of the forest, and the stream is a traditional symbol of life's passage.","c":"By 1872 the Hudson River School was giving way to the looser, more atmospheric manner of a younger generation inspired by the Barbizon painters, and Durand's detailed rendering of leaves, bark and rocks looks old-fashioned beside it. Its virtue is sincerity: careful accumulation of detail, the work of an artist who believed that nature's truth lay in its parts. It is serene and somewhat dry, an honorable late statement of a fading tradition."},"cma:142019":{"d":"A sketch in oil, about 46 by 57 centimeters, shows the episode from Genesis in which Laban searches Jacob's camp for his stolen household gods. The museum explains that it was made for a special competition held by the Royal Academy in Paris in 1753, the subject chosen by the Academy, and that it won first place among three finalists. The story turns on Rachel, who has taken her father's idols and hides them under a camel saddle on which she sits.","i":"The story in Genesis 31 tells how Jacob, after serving Laban for years, flees with his wives and flocks; Rachel secretly takes her father's household idols, and when Laban catches up and searches the tents, she sits on the saddle where they are hidden and excuses herself from rising. History painters of the Royal Academy were set subjects like this to test their command of narrative, gesture and expression. Saint-Aubin's final canvas, now in the Louvre, failed to win the prize.","c":"An oil sketch is where a competition painter shows his ideas, and this one is lively: the composition is quick and the story clear. It won the jury's favor, but the finished canvas did not, a reminder that the spontaneity of a sketch is often lost when it is enlarged and polished for an official judge. As the museum notes, the painter later abandoned the Royal Academy for the less prestigious Academy of Saint Luke."},"cma:171588":{"d":"A group of revelers sits around a table in this small oil on board, about 31 by 41 centimeters, painted around 1855 by Augustus Leopold Egg. At the center, in white, sits George Villiers, Duke of Buckingham, and to his right King Charles II, among courtesans and merrymakers. The scene is invented: it condenses the dissipated court of the Restoration into a single party of the kind the duke was known to have enjoyed.","i":"George Villiers, second Duke of Buckingham (1628-1687), was a wit, a playwright and a notorious libertine at the court of Charles II, and his career of excess and extravagance, ending in decline, became a proverbial warning. Victorian audiences, who loved a cautionary tale and the picturesque costume of the seventeenth century, enjoyed such scenes with a mixture of fascination and disapproval. The revelry is made a pleasant spectacle, but its moral lies in the knowledge of what follows.","c":"Egg was a skilled storyteller and a close observer of social types, and he turns the Restoration into a lively costume piece: the central figure in white draws the eye, the circle of faces gives variety and the small scale suits the intimacy of the scene. The moral judgment is gentle and the glamor real; as with all such Victorian cautionary tales, it enjoys what it condemns. It is a deft, entertaining small picture."},"nga:204911":{"d":"A quiet river view, wider than it is tall. Tall, dark-green trees stand along the left bank, where the shore is thick with yellow and green grass dotted with pink and white flowers. On the right the river spreads out in pale blue water that mirrors the sky and its heavy white clouds, and low blue hills close the distance. The composition sets a dark, solid mass of foliage against open water and light, so that the eye travels from the shaded bank across the reflections to the far hills.","i":"Edward Mitchell Bannister, born in New Brunswick, Canada, settled in Providence, Rhode Island, in 1869 and became a prominent landscape painter there. The Palmer River flows through the country along the Rhode Island and Massachusetts border, so the subject is a local one. His manner owes much to the French Barbizon painters, who preferred modest scenery and mood to grand views. A picture like this is usually read as a record of a familiar place in a particular light, not as a story.","c":"A restrained, well-ordered landscape. The dark bank and the pale water balance each other, and the reflected clouds give the river a gentle luminosity that is the picture's real subject. The flowers and grass are pleasantly noted but kept small, and the plan, dark trees on one side and open sky on the other, is a familiar Barbizon formula that takes no risks. It is a quiet picture by a painter who preferred mood to drama."},"met:435608":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted on a thin plaque of ivory, the support that gave small likenesses their glow in the late eighteenth century. Little is recorded about the painter, known only as Barrois, and the sitter is not named. Like most miniatures, it is small enough to be held in the hand, and it was probably framed as a keepsake, a locket or a lid for a box rather than hung on a wall. Details of dress and expression are best seen in the original.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the private portraits of the age: small likenesses exchanged by lovers, relatives and friends, worn as jewellery or kept in a pocket or on a desk. Painted in watercolour on thin ivory, they take on a luminous flesh tone because the ivory shows through the paint. Around 1790 the genre was at its height in Britain and France. Without a named sitter, this one is best read as a type: a man of his period, recorded for those who knew him.","c":"Because neither sitter nor painter is well documented, the work must be judged by the standards of the genre: a convincing likeness in a few centimetres, fine modelling and clear, bright eyes. Leading miniaturists such as Richard Cosway in London and Jean-Baptiste Isabey in Paris set the benchmark for charm and finish. Lacking a record of the painter's career, the plaque is best taken as a representative example of its period, valued for documentary charm rather than innovation."},"cma:97169":{"d":"Ceres, goddess of grain, and Bacchus, god of wine, walk hand in hand on a tall canvas about 164 by 100 centimetres. The painting is a copy of a print by the Dutch engraver Jan Harmensz Muller, which was itself based on a painting by Bartholomaeus Spranger, so the invention is Spranger's even though the hand is not. The pair stand for the gifts of the harvest, bread and wine, and the picture was probably meant as a decorative and learned piece for a collector's house.","i":"The Latin motto on Muller's print means 'without Ceres and Bacchus, Venus grows cold'. It goes back to the Roman playwright Terence and says that love needs food and wine to thrive. Ceres and Bacchus therefore stand for sustenance, and Venus for desire. Spranger worked for the Emperor Rudolf II in Prague, where witty mythological allegories of this kind were prized, and prints like Muller's carried them across Europe.","c":"As a copy, this canvas is valued for the design rather than the handling. Spranger's invention is graceful and witty, with its linked figures and its learned motto. Working from a black-and-white print, however, the copyist had to supply the colour and the modelling, and copies of this kind usually lose the fine surface and subtle flesh painting of the original. It remains a useful record of how Spranger's court Mannerism travelled by print into other hands."},"cma:141929":{"d":"A very large canvas, about 2.4 by 3.6 metres, shows an episode from Genesis 31. Laban has pursued his son-in-law Jacob to recover the household gods that Rachel stole. Jacob invites him to search the tent, and Rachel sits on the saddlebag that hides the statues, so that the search finds nothing. Laban gives up and proposes a truce. The size and wide format give the picture the presence of a large wall painting rather than a cabinet picture.","i":"The story, from Genesis 31, is about trust and reconciliation between kin, but it also turns on Rachel's cunning: she says she cannot rise because she is unwell, and her father does not search the seat she is sitting on. Jacob, who knows nothing of the theft, offers the search in good faith, and the quarrel ends in a pact. Seventeenth-century viewers probably read it as a lesson that peace is better than strife.","c":"Murillo was a natural storyteller, with a gift for plain human gestures and warm, soft colour, and those gifts suit a tale of family quarrel and domestic trickery. The subject offers less emotional force than his Virgins and saints, and a canvas on this scale invites diffuseness. The telling is clear and the scale impressive, but this is a fluent narrative rather than an intense one."},"cma:135659":{"d":"The Virgin appears as the Immaculate Conception on a tall canvas, about 220 by 128 centimetres, with the crescent moon beneath her feet taken from the vision in Revelation. Murillo painted the subject many times, normally showing a youthful Mary in white and blue, gazing upward amid clouds and angels in golden light. The subject is a theological idea rather than a story, so painters had to invent an image for it, and this one shows how Murillo made the doctrine visible.","i":"The Immaculate Conception is the belief that Mary was conceived free from original sin. It was intensely venerated in Spain in the seventeenth century, long before it became official Catholic doctrine in 1854. Artists drew the image from Revelation 12:1, the woman clothed with the sun with the moon under her feet. In Seville, Murillo's city, the cult was especially strong, and an image like this was meant to move viewers to devotion and to proclaim a cause Spain championed.","c":"Murillo's Immaculate Conceptions are his best-known inventions, and this late example shows why: the soft, atmospheric handling, the rising movement and the gentle face make a doctrine seem natural and sweet. The same qualities carry the risk of sentimentality and repeated formula, which later critics have sometimes held against him. Yet the airy painting of cloud and light and the poise of the figure are of high quality, and the image still conveys quiet joy."},"cma:170429":{"d":"A small painting on copper, about 37 by 26 centimetres, shows a chapel in the Jesuit Imperial College in Madrid with its gilded and painted wooden figure of the Virgin and Child. The architecture is fanciful, with curiously inverted obelisks, fruits and flowers, and the colours are pastel. The painter has turned a three-dimensional sculpture and its shrine into a flat image, so that the statue is both an object of worship and a stage property in a small imaginary theatre.","i":"Our Lady of Good Counsel was a Marian devotion in Madrid centred on the statue in the Jesuits' Imperial College, which drew popular devotion. Pérez, mainly known as a painter of flowers and decoration, made temporary decorations for the Spanish court, which are lost, and the fantastic architecture and garlands here come from that side of his art. By painting a coloured statue he blurred the line between painting and sculpture, a mixing of the spiritual and the real that Spanish taste valued.","c":"A small, inventive and unusual picture. The fanciful architecture, bright pastel colours and festoons of flowers show a decorator's delight in ornament, and the copper support gives a clear, enamel-like surface. The sacred image risks being lost in the decoration, and the devotional feeling is cooler than in Murillo's contemporary Virgins. But as a record of a lost kind of court decoration and of Madrid's popular piety, it is more original than its size suggests."},"nga:214900":{"d":"A small, nearly square canvas, about 31 by 28 centimetres, shows a white vase standing on a wooden surface against a rich brown background. In it is an arrangement of green leaves with small light pink flowers and partly opened white blooms, and a leafy green vine trails down the side of the vase. The palette is simple, with the white vase and the pale flowers standing out from the surrounding brown.","i":"Myosotis is the forget-me-not, which in the Victorian language of flowers meant faithful remembrance, and roses stood for love and beauty, so the pairing in the title suits a picture meant as a quiet token of affection. Small flower pieces were popular in nineteenth-century America as domestic pictures, hung in parlours and given as gifts. Frederick Stone Batcheller, an American painter who lived from 1837 to 1889, painted this unassuming kind of picture; the work itself is undated.","c":"A modest, pleasant flower piece. The dark brown ground lets the white vase and pale blossoms glow, and the limited palette keeps the picture quiet and cohesive. The arrangement is simple and a little stiff, with none of the abundance or bravura of the great flower painters. It succeeds as an intimate decorative object, and as a statement it is slight."},"cma:126768":{"d":"On a small, nearly square canvas, about 39 by 32 centimetres, Joseph and Mary lean in to admire the infant Christ, who is shown in a flexible, twisting pose, while a shepherd leans on his staff and looks on. The figures have small hands, high foreheads and glances directed sideways or upward. Behind them a thickly wooded landscape is brushed loosely and grainily, with silvery-blue moonlight touching the trees, and the colours are rich reds, browns and greens.","i":"The shepherd joining the Holy Family recalls the shepherds of Luke's Nativity, and the moonlight suggests the night of Christ's birth, so the picture blends a family scene with a Nativity. Battista Dossi and his brother Dosso were court painters to Duke Alfonso I of Ferrara. After Battista studied Raphael's works in Rome, his style changed, adding a Raphaelesque grace to the Ferrarese taste for poetic landscape. This small canvas was once attributed to Dosso, a sign of how close the brothers' styles could be.","c":"A small picture of real charm, in which Battista's second style is clear: the dreamy moonlit landscape and the slightly mannered figures with their raised foreheads and upward glances. The tiny hands and sideways looks are quirky, and the group is a little cramped on its small canvas. Battista is often overshadowed by his more imaginative brother, but the silvery atmosphere here is his own. It is delightful rather than weighty."},"cma:166508":{"d":"A woman reclines in a landscape by a monument, in a pose that leaves it unclear whether she is sleeping, mourning or dead. She has dropped a portrait miniature of a man named Fotheringham together with a letter addressed to his mother. An inscription on the monument records the recent death of Fotheringham's wife. The canvas is small, about 32 by 41 centimetres, and was painted around 1800 by the French artist Bernard Gaillot.","i":"The museum notes that the subject probably comes from an English novel popular in France around 1800, though the story has not been traced. The monument's inscription says that the wife of Fotheringham, an English military leader in India, has died, while the letter and miniature suggest that the husband may have written home unaware of the loss. The picture belongs to the age of sensibility, when readers and viewers cherished stories of separation and mourning.","c":"The painting's appeal lies in its ambiguity and in the poetry of its props: the monument, the letter and the miniature create a small, melancholy mystery. The figure is smooth and generalised in the academic manner of the time, and the story is too obscure to carry the emotion it intends, so the picture depends on mood rather than conviction. Gaillot is a minor name, but this is a characteristic, quietly affecting example of sentimental French taste around 1800."},"met:437241":{"d":"A three-quarter-length portrait, about 118 by 78 centimetres, painted in oil on wood, of an unnamed young man who carries a sword and is accompanied by a dog. The date is not recorded, but the type belongs to mid-sixteenth-century Tuscany, where portraits of young nobles followed the cool, elegant formulas of Florentine court painting. The Met attributes it to Bernardino Detti, though other sources name Benedetto Pagni, two Tuscan painters of the same generation.","i":"The sword marks the sitter as a gentleman entitled to bear arms, and the dog suggests fidelity and the aristocratic pursuit of hunting. In Florence under Duke Cosimo I, aristocratic youths were painted with an air of calm, reserved elegance, often modelled on the court portraits of Bronzino. The sitter's name is lost, so he is read as a type: a young man of rank presenting himself as poised and loyal. Detti and Pagni are both associated with Pescia.","c":"The picture shows the Florentine ideal at second hand: the proud pose, the sword and the dog recall Bronzino's court portraits, but the surface is less refined and the psychology less assured. The staging has presence, and the dog adds life, though the figure is stiff and the face somewhat blank. As a provincial reflection of court style it is interesting; as a portrait it falls short of its models."},"cma:97177":{"d":"A Virgin and Child on a small wood panel, about 56 by 43 centimetres, painted in oil, with a date given only as the 1500s. Luini's Madonnas, shaped by Leonardo's example, typically show Mary with a soft, rounded face, lowered eyes and a gentle half-smile, holding the Child close in a calm, sweetly lit setting. The vague date suggests that the picture may come from Luini's circle or workshop.","i":"Leonardo's years in Milan, from 1482 to 1499 and again from 1506 to 1513, shaped a generation of Lombard painters, and Luini was the most successful: he took Leonardo's soft shading and idealised, smiling faces and made them accessible. Small Madonnas like this were made for private devotion, where Mary's tender gaze at her child invited the viewer into a quiet moment of motherhood and prayer. Their sweetness made Luini's works popular for centuries.","c":"Luini's strength is sweetness: smoothly blended modelling, harmonious colour and a calm that comforts. He lacks Leonardo's intelligence and the restless energy behind Leonardo's faces, and critics have long called him charming but facile. The risk in a picture of this kind is sugar, and a workshop version risks it more. As an introduction to Lombard devotional painting in Leonardo's wake it is pleasant, but unchallenging."},"cma:97190":{"d":"Salome presents the head of John the Baptist on a dish, in a horizontal canvas of about 47 by 60 centimetres. In the Lombard painting of Leonardo's followers, which Luini made popular, Salome appears half-length as an elegant young woman in rich dress, with a calm face, offering the grim trophy almost without emotion. The museum dates the work to the 1500s or later, which leaves open that it is a later version of a popular Luini composition.","i":"Salome's dancing so pleased her stepfather Herod that he offered her anything she wished; her mother advised her to demand John the Baptist's head, which she presented to her family on an elaborate dish. Despite its biblical source, the museum notes that this work was meant for a domestic setting, where its owner would have admired the elegant presentation of a shocking story. The contrast between beauty and horror, restrained and decorous, was part of the appeal.","c":"The success of the type depends on the contrast between Salome's serene beauty and the grisly dish, and Luini's smooth modelling and cool colour make the contrast elegant. But that elegance is also the weakness: the picture removes most of the story's horror and moral weight, turning a martyrdom into a fashionable still moment. The doubtful date suggests that it may not be Luini's own work. It reveals a Lombard taste for sensual refinement more than religious feeling."},"cma:143644":{"d":"The Holy Family and a group of shepherds gather around the newborn Christ on a large canvas, about 127 by 148 centimetres. Mary's face is the emotional centre of the picture, sweet and absorbed, while Joseph and the shepherds look stolidly at the Child. Above, angels flutter with ribbons proclaiming Gloria in excelsis Deo, glory to God in the highest. An ox stands among the figures, and the artist placed his monogram, BC, on its rump.","i":"In Luke 2 an angel announces Christ's birth to shepherds, who hurry to Bethlehem and find the child. Cavallino gives the familiar story a more personal turn: only Mary is aware of the angels overhead, as though the heavenly song were a private revelation, and her expression joins love, pride and humility. Cavallino worked in Naples and died young, probably in the plague of 1656, and he was little known in his lifetime, though alert to the innovations of his contemporaries.","c":"Cavallino is among the most delicate painters of seventeenth-century Naples, and this is a characteristic example. He replaces the dark, forceful style associated with Ribera with cool, lyrical colour and gentle feeling, and the idea of Mary alone perceiving the angels is subtle and affecting. The shepherds' stolid faces risk monotony, and the crowded group is slightly stiff, but the picture's tenderness is real. It rewards slow looking."},"nga:231675":{"d":"A seated woman is shown at three-quarter length, with a pearl necklace giving the picture its title, on a large canvas about 145 by 100 centimetres painted in the 1650s. Cornelis Bisschop, a painter from Dordrecht who is said to have trained with Ferdinand Bol, worked in the warm, soft-lit manner of Rembrandt's pupils, painting women at windows and in domestic interiors, and a picture of this type usually concentrates on the sitter's figure, dress and jewels.","i":"Pearls in Dutch art carried several meanings: they were costly signs of wealth and refinement, and also symbols of purity and, in some pictures, of vanity, since a woman admiring her jewels could stand for worldly pride. Vermeer would paint a famous Woman with a Pearl Necklace in the 1660s. Whether Bisschop's picture records a particular sitter or presents an idealised type is unclear, and it may be best read as a celebration of feminine elegance in the prosperous Dutch Republic.","c":"A handsome, large-scale picture whose strengths are warmth, soft light and a patient rendering of fabric and jewels, and the size gives the figure real presence. Bisschop's debt to Rembrandt's circle is clear, though he lacks the depth of feeling of the master or the clarity of Vermeer, who treated the same subject with more mystery. It is a pleasing, competent work of the Dutch Golden Age rather than an outstanding one."},"met:838076":{"d":"A very small panel, about 26 by 20 centimetres, painted around 1345 to 1350 by an unnamed Bohemian painter active in Prague. Mary sits enthroned holding the Christ Child, the Queen of Heaven in the formal pose of a devotional icon. The medium is given as oil(?) and gold on wood, the question mark reflecting how unusual oil would be in a panel of this date, and the gold ground gives the figures the glow of a heavenly vision.","i":"Prague rose to prominence as an artistic centre in the 1340s and 1350s, when Charles IV, King of Bohemia from 1346 and Holy Roman Emperor from 1355, made it his capital and gathered painters, goldsmiths and architects. Bohemian painters combined Italian modelling with a northern taste for delicate refinement, and small devotional panels like this were made for private chapels and courtiers. Mary enthroned shows her as Queen of Heaven, interceding for the viewer.","c":"The panel is small but precious: the delicate faces, the soft modelling and the lavish gold give it a courtly refinement that foreshadows the Beautiful Style of Bohemian painting around 1400. Its rarity adds to its appeal. The pose is formal and the expressions restrained, and its claim to attention rests more on refinement than on drama. It is a sweet, devotional object whose quiet quality is typical of good Bohemian work of the mid-fourteenth century."},"nga:226402":{"d":"A horizontal oil on canvas of about 73 by 100 centimeters, Marguerite is a medium-sized picture of the 1870s. The title is a woman's given name, and also the French word for the daisy. No detailed description of the scene accompanies the work, so its contents are better left unstated than guessed. What can be said is the kind of picture it is: the work of a French painter of the Impressionist circle, whose subjects were mostly women in gardens and sunlit rooms, treated in a light, high-keyed palette with a fresh, open touch.","i":"A title that gives only a first name usually points to a particular model or sitter, whether relative, friend or studio model, rather than to a story. The name also had literary currency in France, since Marguerite is the heroine of Gounod's opera Faust (1859), though nothing recorded here links this picture to that tale. Bracquemond belonged to an artistic household: her husband, Félix Bracquemond, was a leading printmaker, and she herself showed with the Impressionists in the late 1870s and 1880s.","c":"Bracquemond's reputation rests mainly on her work of the 1880s, where sunlight breaks through foliage and white dresses with unusual clarity. A canvas of the 1870s probably belongs to the earlier, more tonal stage of her development, before the full turn to outdoor light. It is best valued as part of a career long overshadowed by her husband's and by better-known Impressionist colleagues, and only recently reassessed."},"nga:34096":{"d":"A woman sits in half-length, her body turned slightly aside and her gaze directed toward the viewer with a calm expression. Her hair is dressed in an updo with loose curls. She wears a blue dress trimmed with white lace and puffed sleeves, and a golden-orange shawl is draped across her chest. In one hand she holds an open book; the other rests on a surface beside her. Her chair is covered in red patterned fabric, and behind her the wall is a dark, warm tone. The canvas, about 93 by 72 centimeters, is close to the stock 'kit-cat' size.","i":"Neither the sitter nor the painter is recorded: the museum labels the work simply as British, eighteenth century. The book is the best clue to how she wished to be seen. In portraits of the period an open book could signal literacy, piety or polite learning, qualities valued in women of the gentry and prosperous middle class, while the shawl, lace and fashionable hair declare means and taste. The formula, an informal pose, a warm dark ground and one telling accessory, was popularized by Reynolds, Gainsborough and Romney.","c":"Judged on its own terms, this anonymous work is modest but sound. The face is calm, and the scheme of blue, orange and red is well chosen, giving the figure presence against the dark wall. The drapery is generalized, however, and nothing in the handling suggests the quick intelligence of the leading portraitists. It is a good example of the decorous, competent portraiture that filled British houses in the 1770s, valuable for its type rather than for any individual stroke."},"nga:34150":{"d":"A man is shown turned in three-quarter view, with a neutral expression, almond-shaped eyes, a straight nose and a small mouth. His light-colored hair is brushed back, with curls framing the face. He wears a dark coat with a wide red collar over a white shirt. No jewelry or objects are visible, and the background is a plain, muted brown without detail. The canvas measures about 76 by 64 centimeters, a modest portrait size, and the stripped-down setting concentrates attention wholly on the sitter's face and costume.","i":"The title gives the sitter's style of address: 'The Hon.' normally marks the child of a peer, and 'Sir' a knighthood, so the likeness records a man of rank in the 1790s. The wide red collar on a dark coat is the kind of facing seen on military and volunteer uniforms of the decade, but nothing else in the picture confirms a career in arms. Plain backgrounds were standard for gentlemen's portraits: dress and bearing did the work of declaring status.","c":"Close to a blank slate: the sitter is agreeable but unemphatic, and the muted browns and the red collar are the only strong notes. The modeling is smooth, the pose conventional, and the characterization restrained to the point of anonymity. Without an identified hand, the picture is best valued as a clear, uncluttered example of a British gentleman's portrait of the 1790s, rather than as a distinctive psychological study."},"nga:56108":{"d":"An elderly man sits in a chair, leaning slightly forward, in dark, formal clothes. His round face is fair, with deep-set eyes beneath white curly hair. Both hands rest on the wooden arms of the chair; one holds a wooden cane, the other lying over it. The background is dark and plain, leaving all the light to the sitter. At about 91 by 71 centimeters the canvas is a standard half-length portrait size, painted between about 1800 and 1820 by an unidentified British artist.","i":"The title names the sitter only as Mr. Tucker of Yeovil, a market town in Somerset, which points to a provincial commission for a local man of standing. The armchair and cane are the usual signs of age, comfort and respectability, and the dark coat fits the sober dress that professional and business men adopted in the early nineteenth century. Portraits like this were commissioned by families and kept at home as records of the head of the household.","c":"The picture's strength is its directness. The forward lean and the hands gripping chair and cane give an impression of an individual rather than a type, and the plain dark ground leaves nothing to distract. Without a known artist or any record of the sitter, it cannot be placed precisely, but as provincial portraiture of its time it is unfussy and honest, which is more than many grander likenesses of the period manage."},"nga:34124":{"d":"A man stands upright, shown to the waist and facing the viewer. His pale face has rosy cheeks, a long nose and strong brows, with sideburns that run into curly gray-brown hair. He wears a black coat over a high-collared white shirt. In one hand he holds papers; in the other, held against his chest, a pair of almost invisible spectacles. His right arm rests on a surface at the left, and at the right a lower desk or table carries an inkwell with a quill and a sheet of paper. The dark brown background shows visible brushstrokes.","i":"Papers, quill, ink and spectacles are the tools of reading and writing, and they present the sitter as a man of business, law, administration or study. A portrait of about 1830 with such props would usually record occupation and seriousness more than personality. The sitter's name is not recorded here, so his profession must stay an open question. The plain black coat, standard for respectable men of the 1820s and 1830s, reinforces the image of sober responsibility.","c":"Competent and clear: the standing pose is stable, the hands are given purposeful tasks, and the props sit at the edges of the canvas without crowding the face. The paint is brisk, with brushstrokes left visible in the dark ground. The expression is stiff, though, and the characterization limited to what the props supply. It is a sound professional portrait of the period, not a revealing one."},"nga:59893":{"d":"A family group and its terrier are the subject of this upright canvas, about 103 by 81 centimeters, painted in oil in Britain between about 1825 and 1835. The title is the only description on record: the sitters are unknown, and no catalog entry lists their number, poses, clothes or setting, so those are left unstated here. The picture belongs to the long tradition of British family portraiture, which gathered parents, children and household pets into a single image meant to hang in the family's own rooms.","i":"A family portrait of this kind was a statement of unity and respectability, commissioned by a household that wanted its members recorded together. The terrier is probably more than decoration: dogs commonly stand for fidelity and domestic affection, and terriers were familiar companions in town and country homes. Because neither the family nor the painter is named, the picture now speaks mainly about its type, a growing middle-class pride in home and children in the years around 1830.","c":"With no description of the surface to go on, the fairest assessment is of the type. Family groups by anonymous painters tend to value likeness and togetherness over flattery, and their appeal is often their directness. Whether this one succeeds depends on how convincingly the figures and the terrier share a single space, a point best tested before the canvas itself. As a document of ordinary British domestic life around 1830 it has value in any case."},"met:438839":{"d":"A portrait miniature in watercolor on ivory shows William Paulet Carey, whose life dates, 1759 to 1839, are recorded in the title. Made in the late eighteenth century by an unrecorded British or Irish painter, it is described no further in the catalog, so his pose, costume and background go unstated here. Most such miniatures show the head and shoulders, small enough to rest in the palm, with translucent watercolor applied in fine strokes and dots so that the pale ivory glows through the flesh tones.","i":"Miniatures were private objects, exchanged between relatives, friends and lovers, or kept as remembrances and worn in a case, locket or bracelet. Because this sitter is named and dated, the portrait carries the added interest of documenting a particular man. The 1790s favored a clear, unfussy style with light backgrounds and little ornament, reflecting neoclassical taste and the example of leading miniaturists such as Richard Cosway and John Smart.","c":"Without a named maker the miniature cannot be ranked against Cosway, Smart or Ozias Humphry, the leaders of the day, and its interest lies mainly in the identified sitter and in the delicacy the medium allows. Watercolor on ivory, built up in tiny strokes, gives skin a pearly glow that oil cannot match, and at its best the form catches an intimate, fleeting likeness. That is the standard to hold this example to."},"met:435778":{"d":"A young man in armor is shown in a portrait dated 1588, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 91 by 75 centimeters. The title names him as Sir John Shurley of Isfield, a Sussex gentleman whose life dates are given as 1565 to 1632. The catalog keywords single out the armor and offer no further detail of pose, setting or inscription, so these are left unstated. The picture belongs to the linear, ornamental style of English Elizabethan portraiture, in which clothes and accessories carry much of the meaning.","i":"Dated 1588, the year of the Spanish Armada, the portrait shows a man in his early twenties if the title's dates are right. In Elizabethan England, armor in a portrait signaled a gentleman's readiness to serve queen and country, and a martial look was also a fashion in itself; it does not prove that the sitter fought. Panels like this hung in family houses, recording lineage and status for later generations.","c":"English portraits of the 1580s typically prize pattern, status and costume over psychological depth, and the armor and dress here will tell more than the face. By the standards of Hilliard's court miniatures or the Netherlandish painters then working in London, it is probably a competent, decorative product of a good workshop. Its real value is as a precisely dated and named record of a young Elizabethan gentleman."},"met:435779":{"d":"A woman in the fashionable dress of the later Elizabethan age is shown on a wooden panel about 113 by 88 centimeters, painted in oil about 1600. The catalog lists a stiff ruff, a lace collar, a cape, a pearl necklace, a brooch and a pearl among the things shown, and offers no further description, so her pose, setting and any inscription are left unstated. English portraits of this date are typically flat and decorative, with costume and jewels painted in close detail and the face modeled softly.","i":"The museum's subject tags mention Elizabeth I, perhaps because the sitter was once thought to be the queen or because the dress follows court fashion of her reign; the portrait is cataloged only as Portrait of a Woman. Pearls were prized for wealth and, in the queen's own imagery, for purity. A ruff, lace and jewels announced rank, and in such pictures costume stood for identity as much as the face did.","c":"The costume is the show. The long list of ruff, lace, cape and pearls suggests how far the picture's interest lies in surface pattern, and that is the style's strength and limit: richly worked fabric and jewelry, but little sense of a living presence. Without a named painter or sitter, it is best read as a record of how an Elizabethan woman wished to be seen, rather than as an individual characterization."},"met:435781":{"d":"An oil portrait of a man on a thin sheet of copper, British, about 1690. The catalog describes neither the sitter nor his dress, and the painter is unknown, so the picture can be characterized only by type and date: a likeness of the late Stuart era in a costly, durable medium. Copper plates have a smooth, nonabsorbent surface that lets a painter work in fine, almost invisible strokes, a quality much admired in cabinet pictures of the time.","i":"A likeness was social currency in the 1690s: friends, patrons and relatives exchanged them, and a man of means might commission one on marriage, before a journey or at a career milestone. The decade also saw a widening market for portraits among merchants and professionals, not only the court and the aristocracy. Who this sitter was, and for whom he sat, has been lost; what remains is evidence of a growing taste for personal images.","c":"A modest example of its kind, to judge from the record. Oil on copper rewards a precise hand with a smooth, luminous finish, and the format keeps a sitter's face at an intimate scale. Without an artist's name or a sitter to compare, it is hard to rate beyond competence. It will interest those who want to see how an Englishman of the 1690s liked to be remembered more than those looking for a great painter."},"met:435782":{"d":"A man's portrait in oil on a copper plate, made in Britain about 1690. The catalog gives no dimensions or description, so his features, wig and clothing are left unstated, and the painter is unrecorded. Copper offers a hard, smooth ground on which a painter can lay thin, closely blended oil paint with a glassy, enamel-like finish, and in the late seventeenth century small oils on copper served as portable likenesses, close in purpose to the watercolor miniature on vellum.","i":"In the 1690s a small portrait like this was an exchange of friendship, kinship or love, kept in a case or drawer and carried when the owner traveled. A man of the date would most likely be shown in the fashion of William III's reign, with a full wig and plain cravat, though the catalog does not say what this sitter wears. Both sitter and painter are unrecorded, so the picture now stands for a type: a private likeness of a man who wished to be remembered.","c":"The medium is the thing to judge. Copper gives a cooler, harder finish than ivory or vellum, and the best oils on it have a jewel-like clarity; the risk is a polished but impersonal surface. With the maker unrecorded and nothing described, the picture can only be placed in a tradition, and the tradition is a modest, competent one, living in the long shadow of Samuel Cooper's miniatures of a generation earlier."},"met:435784":{"d":"A man's portrait miniature of about 1740, painted on vellum laid on card, a support that was already going out of fashion. The catalog describes neither the sitter's features nor his clothing, and the painter is unrecorded, so the picture is characterized here by its materials: calfskin parchment pasted to a firm card backing, prepared to take fine, dense touches of watercolor and body color. The result is a delicate, matte likeness in the medium of Nicholas Hilliard and Samuel Cooper.","i":"Vellum had been the traditional support of English miniatures since Hilliard in the sixteenth century. By the 1740s ivory had become the fashionable choice, so a portrait on vellum may be the work of a painter of older habits, or of someone working to a different price. The picture's value to a family was personal: a likeness of a husband, brother or friend, kept in a drawer or a case.","c":"The choice of vellum is the interesting thing. It gives a flatter, less luminous surface than ivory, and a painter must work in tiny stippled touches to model a face. How far the likeness transcends the medium cannot be judged from the catalog entry. Its significance is as a survivor of an older technique at the moment when ivory and enamel were taking over the miniature, which makes it a useful document even if it is not a standout."},"met:435785":{"d":"A man's portrait of about 1750 painted in enamel, the hardest and most permanent of the miniaturist's media. The catalog does not describe the sitter's features or dress, and the maker is unrecorded. Enamel portraits are painted in powdered glass colors on a small plaque of metal coated with a white enamel ground, and each color must be fired in a kiln, which gives a glassy, jewel-like brilliance. Such plaques were usually set into frames, lockets or the lids of boxes.","i":"Enamel painting came to Britain largely through Continental craftsmen, and by mid-century enamel miniatures were fashionable gifts for betrothals and friendships. The medium's great advantage is that its colors do not fade, so a portrait made about 1750 may look today much as it did the day it was fired. The identity of the man is unrecorded, but the choice of so costly and difficult a medium suggests a sitter of some means.","c":"Enamel is a medium of risk and reward. A flaw in the firing can ruin weeks of work, and the surface resists the soft blending of watercolor on ivory, yet a good enamel portrait has a clarity and depth of color no other miniature medium can equal. Among the leading masters was Christian Friedrich Zincke. This anonymous example cannot be ranked beside his without a close look, but the survival of its color is already a credit to the maker."},"met:435787":{"d":"A portrait of a man painted about 1770 on ivory, cataloged as British. The record gives no description of his face, hair or clothing, and the maker is unrecorded. The support is a thin, polished plaque of ivory, and the paint is watercolor mixed with gum, applied in minute strokes and dots that let the pale surface shine through. The scale is that of the palm of a hand, made for a case, a locket or a bracelet clasp, and for close, private viewing.","i":"About 1770 the miniature was at the height of its popularity in Britain, serving as a favorite gift between relatives, friends and lovers and as a way for an absent soldier, sailor or official to remain present at home. Leading practitioners such as Jeremiah Meyer and Ozias Humphry set a taste for graceful, softly colored likenesses. The sitter here is unidentified, so the miniature now speaks for its time rather than for the man.","c":"The ivory miniature at its best offers an extraordinary combination of intimacy and finish, and the 1770s produced some of its most graceful examples. Without an attribution it is difficult to say where this one stands: the record gives no sign of the sitter's character or of the maker's skill, and the portrait is best accepted as a representative piece of a very busy trade rather than a standout."},"met:435788":{"d":"A man's portrait of about 1780 on card, cataloged as British and identified only by the initials R. H. in its title. The catalog does not describe his features or clothing. Card, a firm paper board, is an unusual support for a miniature; it is more often chosen for drawings, silhouettes and quick portraits in watercolor, pencil or ink than for the ivory plaques of finished miniatures. The picture may therefore be a modest, quickly made likeness, though the record does not say.","i":"The initials are all that identifies him; they probably come from an inscription, a monogram or lettering on the frame or the back. Pictures of this kind were made for personal reasons: as a memento, a gift, or a remembrance after a death, and were often passed down in a family long after names were forgotten. The initials stand as a tiny puzzle that the portrait itself cannot answer.","c":"With card as the support, the likeness is likely to be more spontaneous and less jewel-like than an ivory miniature; any charm would come from directness rather than finish. As so little is recorded about the maker or the sitter, the picture is best valued as an example of the unpretentious end of British portrait production in the 1780s, and as a reminder of how many sitters are now known only by a fragment of their names."},"met:435791":{"d":"A portrait of a man about 1800, painted in watercolor on ivory and cataloged as British. The record gives neither dimensions nor a description, and the painter is unrecorded. At this date the typical miniature shows a head and shoulders in a small oval or rectangle, against a softly clouded sky or a plain ground, painted in fine hatching and stippling. Ivory's pearly translucency gives the flesh a gentle glow, and the whole object is made to be held close or worn.","i":"About 1800 the fashion for miniatures was at its peak. They were worn on the body, exchanged at engagement, sent overseas to absent family and kept as mourning tokens, and a skilled painter could make a living from them. The decade also saw a shift in men's dress toward plainer, darker coats, so a man's portrait of this date often looks soberer than one of the 1770s. This sitter's name and story are not on record.","c":"A miniature of 1800 can fairly be judged for its handling of the face, the softness of its blending and the economy of the background. These are the standard virtues of the leading painters of the day, Cosway, Engleheart and Plimer, and the unrecorded maker here presumably worked in their wake. As an unattributed piece it adds little to what is known, but it typifies the fine, careful craftsmanship of the period."},"met:435792":{"d":"A man identified by the title as an officer is painted on ivory about 1820. The title tells that he is shown as a soldier or sailor; the catalog does not describe the uniform, the rank or the sitter's features. The format is that of the portrait miniature, a thin plaque of ivory painted in watercolor with fine stipple, small enough to be carried or worn. Officers' miniatures of this period commonly show the head and shoulders in full uniform, with epaulettes, braid and buttons rendered in bright detail.","i":"About 1820 the Napoleonic wars had just ended, and Britain had an unusually large body of army and navy officers, many of them now on half pay. Many had sat for miniatures before or during the campaigns, as keepsakes for families that might not see them again; those painted afterward may record service rather than anticipate its dangers. Without the name of the sitter or the regiment, the miniature remains a record of a type rather than a person.","c":"Uniform is a gift to a miniaturist: red or blue cloth, gold braid and polished buttons supply strong color and crisp pattern that suit the small format. The risk is that costume replaces character, and many officers' likenesses of the period are stiffer than civilian ones. Without a maker's name or a description of this one, the fairest verdict is that it belongs to a competent, rather formulaic genre, valuable chiefly as a document of its time."},"met:435797":{"d":"An eye on ivory, shown without the rest of the face, from the early nineteenth century. Most eye miniatures were made to be set in jewelry: a locket, a brooch, a ring or a bracelet clasp, often with a lock of hair or a monogram sealed behind the glass. The catalog does not say how this one was mounted, or whether it carries any inscription, and the painter is unknown. What it does show is the miniaturist's central task, creating a sense of presence from a single, tiny gaze.","i":"The point of an eye miniature is concealment. Without the nose, mouth and hairline, a sitter could not be recognized by anyone who did not know the eye, so a person in an illicit or socially unequal love could wear the beloved's portrait in public. The fashion flourished in an age that prized sensibility, private feeling and secret attachment. The name of the person this eye belonged to is not recorded.","c":"As portraiture the type is paradoxical, since it records a person through the one feature that is hardest to identify. Its success lies in compression: brow, lid and iris are enough to suggest an individual temperament. The weakness, for a modern viewer, is that such eyes can resemble one another at a glance. This example, anonymous and undescribed, is a typical member of the group, without claim to stand above it."},"met:435798":{"d":"A single human eye, with at most a hint of brow and lid, is painted on a small piece of ivory in the early nineteenth century. Nothing else of the sitter is shown: no face, no hair, no clothing. Pictures of this kind are tiny, often only a few centimeters across, and are painted in watercolor with brushes of a few hairs, so the iris, the gleam on the cornea and the lashes are typically built up from minute touches. The maker of this one is unrecorded.","i":"Eye miniatures, or lover's eyes, became a fashion in Britain in the 1780s. The usual story traces them to the Prince of Wales, later George IV, who is said to have sent a painted eye to Maria Fitzherbert, the Catholic widow he could not publicly marry. Whatever the truth of that tale, the idea spread quickly: a lover could carry the beloved's gaze privately, in a locket, brooch or ring, with no risk of the sitter being recognized.","c":"The form is a test of nerve for a miniaturist, since a single eye leaves nothing to hide behind: if the drawing of the lid or the highlight on the pupil is off, the whole picture fails. Within that narrow range the best examples are oddly moving, and also slightly unsettling. Nothing is recorded of this one beyond its medium and period, so it is best valued for the type: a compact, intense piece of Georgian sentiment."},"met:435799":{"d":"A cropped, close-up view of a human eye painted on ivory, made in Britain in the early nineteenth century. The cropping is the whole design: all that is offered to the viewer is the organ of sight, enlarged in effect by the tiny scale of the object. Eye miniatures are generally unsigned and were painted in transparent watercolor on thin ivory, so that the pale support gives the skin and the white of the eye a soft inner light. The sitter and the maker of this example are unrecorded.","i":"Eye miniatures were not only love tokens. Some were made as mourning jewelry after a death, and in that role the eye is a remembrance of a face no longer there; examples of this kind may add a tear, often shown as a pearl, or a lock of the dead person's hair. The catalog does not say whether this one commemorates the living or the dead. Both uses draw on the old idea of the eye as the window of the soul.","c":"Eyes of this kind are admired as curiosities as much as for painterly merit, and rightly so: the type is emotionally direct but modest in artistic range. Success depends on a convincing moist highlight and the right tilt of lid, subtle matters in a field a few centimeters wide. Anonymous and undescribed here, this one should be seen as part of a group, where small differences of lash, brow and angle become interesting."},"met:435800":{"d":"A miniature eye on ivory from early nineteenth-century Britain. The catalog offers no description beyond the title and the medium, and the maker is unknown. In works of this type the eye is usually set in a small oval or rectangular frame, with a faint suggestion of the brow above, and the colors are kept light and transparent so that the ivory glows through. The effect is deliberately close, as though the viewer were looking into the sitter's face from a few inches away.","i":"Late Georgian sentiment prized the eye as the window of the soul. In a time when courtship was supervised, letters were read by others and reputations were fragile, an eye was a gift with a double meaning: public as a jewel, private as a portrait. Eye miniatures were made in the decades around 1800, and they ranged from love tokens to mourning pieces. The name of the person who wore this one, and of the person whose eye it shows, has not been recorded.","c":"A critic can admire the discipline of the form, since it restricts the painter to a few square centimeters of ivory and demands total control of a feature that viewers read with great sensitivity. What it cannot do is show character beyond the glance; the result is more a token of feeling than a study of a person. For this anonymous example, that limit is both its charm and its ceiling."},"met:435801":{"d":"An eye painted on ivory, British, early nineteenth century. The catalog offers no description and no maker, so the details of color, angle and surrounding frame remain unstated here. The type gives a clue to what is seen: a single eye cropped tight, usually with the eyebrow above it and often a trace of the upper cheek or the shadow of the lid, painted in the same minute stippled technique used for full-face miniatures on ivory.","i":"Eye miniatures cluster in the decades around 1800 and are associated with a courtship culture that valued discretion. A single eye could pass for a trinket, but to its owner it was a portrait. Objects like this also show that the miniature, even in its most conventional form, served feeling and memory more than display, which is why so many survive in cases, lockets and bracelets, long after the identities they guarded were forgotten.","c":"Taken alone, this miniature gives little for a critic to judge; such works are valued for their rarity and charm. Their merit is relative: some are timid, others beautifully fluid in the modeling of the lid. The best way to look is at the lash line and the highlight on the pupil, the two places where a skilled painter's hand shows. Here the maker is unrecorded, so the pleasure is in the type."},"met:435793":{"d":"A view of a parish church at Tottenham, then a village on the northern edge of London, painted in oil on a nearly square canvas about 52 by 46 centimeters. The catalog lists the church as the main subject and gives no more description, so the weather, any figures and the surroundings are left unstated. The picture belongs to the British tradition of topographical landscape, in which a local building was recorded in its setting for the pleasure of residents and collectors, and dates from about 1830.","i":"In the early nineteenth century the villages around London were still rural, but the growing city was beginning to reach them, and views of parish churches, villages and greens had a double appeal: they recorded a place and recalled an older, quieter England. The church is probably All Hallows, Tottenham's medieval parish church, though the title does not say so. The painter is unrecorded, which may point to an amateur or local artist rather than an exhibiting professional.","c":"A modest topographical view whose chief interest is the record of a place since absorbed into the city. Without the painter's name or a detailed description, the fair verdict is on type rather than skill: such views tend to be accurate and affectionate about architecture, and flatter in weather and space than Constable's treatment of a similar English subject. As a document of the metropolitan fringe in the 1830s it may be worth more than as a work of art."},"met:441355":{"d":"A small horizontal canvas, about 15 by 23 centimeters, shows a Western gentleman, as the title has it, dressed in what the nineteenth century called Oriental costume, meaning clothing of the Ottoman, Egyptian or wider Middle Eastern world. The catalog lists only a man, a portrait and an Orientalist theme, so his features, pose and setting are left unstated. It is a small oil, painted about 1840 to 1845, of a kind better suited to a private room than to a gallery wall.","i":"Dressing up in the costume of the East was a favorite pastime of European travelers and their portraitists from the eighteenth century on. The sitter may have been a traveler who wanted a souvenir of a journey to the Levant or Egypt, or a man sitting for a fancy-dress portrait at home. Such images mix curiosity, romance and a confident Western sense of ownership of the East, an outlook now called Orientalism, and are better read as expressions of it than as ethnographic records.","c":"Small, anonymous and undescribed, it is a curiosity more than a major work. Its interest lies in the social game it records: a man who plays at being someone else while remaining plainly himself. The best such pictures hold the tension between costume and sitter, the weaker ones are mere costume pieces, and which this is cannot be said from the record. The small scale suggests an informal, personal commission."},"met:435802":{"d":"A vertical portrait in oil on wood, about 96 by 75 centimeters, shows a young man whose name is unrecorded. The catalog gives only the title, the medium and the keywords men and portraits, so the sitter's pose, clothing and setting are left unstated. The picture dates from the 1530s, when Bronzino was a young painter in Florence, and the artist's known manner in such portraits is cool, precise and polished, with a smooth, almost enamel-like surface and a self-possessed, slightly aloof sitter.","i":"Florentine portraits of the 1530s presented educated young men of the ruling class as poised, composed and remote, a manner of courtly self-control that the writer Baldassare Castiglione had called sprezzatura, or studied nonchalance. The sitter here is anonymous, so the picture speaks about an ideal more than an individual: how a well-born young Florentine wished to appear in an age of political uncertainty, as the Republic fell and the Medici regained power.","c":"Bronzino's portraits of the 1530s and 1540s remain a benchmark for cool Mannerist elegance, and this young man is among the best known of the early ones. The tension between the smooth, perfectly controlled surface and the sitter's guarded inner life is the point; the coldness that some viewers see as a weakness is the effect. Few portraitists of the period achieved this blend of finish and reserve."},"nga:37004":{"d":"A woman sits on a wide, high-backed throne with a child on her lap, against a gold ground, on a tall panel about 125 by 71 centimeters. Her marine-blue mantle opens over a dark navy robe, and her slender face tilts toward the child while her almond-shaped eyes glance out at the viewer. The child, in a tomato-red robe with a spruce-blue sash, clutches a bone-white scroll and raises two fingers toward her. Dense gold lines mark the folds of their garments, and winged angels in red roundels hover above. Her red shoes rest on a footstool; the gold is worn and cracked.","i":"The Virgin enthroned with the Child presents Mary as Queen of Heaven, and panels of this kind served as icons: believers prayed before them and asked for her intercession. The scroll in the Child's hand signifies his teaching, and the two raised fingers are a gesture of blessing, often read in Byzantine thought as a sign of his two natures, divine and human. The gold ground does not describe a place; it stands for the timeless light of heaven.","c":"The style is pure thirteenth-century Byzantine: slender face, long nose, shadows tinted greenish gray in the flesh, and drapery folds reduced to rhythmic lines of gold. Beside the soft naturalism that Giotto would bring to Madonnas a few decades later, it looks formal and remote, but the child's reaching gesture and the tilt of the mother's head introduce a human tenderness into the ceremony. Worn as it is, it keeps real dignity."},"nga:35":{"d":"A woman and child sit on an ornate, curving gold throne in front of a shimmering gold ground, on a vertical panel about 82 by 50 centimeters. Winged angels in roundels fill the upper corners, each holding an orb and scepter. Mary wears a scarlet mantle over an azure dress and looks into the distance over the Child's head. The Child, as tall as a toddler but with adult proportions, raises two fingers and holds a red scroll; he wears a forest-green robe over salmon pink. Geometric gold lines mark the drapery folds, and the throne is carved in two tiers.","i":"The enthroned Virgin is shown in imperial terms: the carved throne is a seat of state, and the angels with orbs and scepters resemble court attendants, so Mary appears as the Empress of Heaven. The Child's mature face and body, unlike a real toddler's, express his identity as eternal Wisdom in human form. His halo bears a cross, a sign of the sacrifice to come. Panels like this were icons for churches and chapels, objects of prayer more than of display.","c":"Where the other Byzantine Madonnas in this tradition show tenderness, this one prefers majesty. The curved throne encloses the pair like a shell, the gold folds shimmer, and Mary's distant gaze is the very opposite of an anecdote. The adult-faced Child is strange to modern eyes but consistent with the picture's purpose. Its strength is in design, the clear, rhythmic pattern of line and color, not in observation of nature."},"met:435828":{"d":"A nearly square icon, about 44 by 42 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with a gold ground, shows the Presentation of Jesus in the Temple. The museum's keywords list the haloed Christ Child, the Virgin Mary, women, saints and the temple, and no fuller description is available, so the exact grouping, architecture and colors are left unstated. In the usual Byzantine composition, the elderly Simeon receives the child at an altar beneath a canopy, with Mary, Joseph and the prophetess Anna beside him.","i":"The story is told in Luke 2:22 to 38. Forty days after the birth, Mary and Joseph bring Jesus to the Temple in Jerusalem; Simeon, who had been promised that he would see the Messiah before he died, takes the child in his arms and praises God, and the prophetess Anna speaks of him to all who await redemption. In the Orthodox Church the event is one of the twelve great feasts, and icons of it often formed a row.","c":"Late Byzantine painters repeated sacred compositions with great fidelity, and the interest lies in small variations of gesture, architecture and color within a fixed scheme. In the fifteenth century many painters of the Greek world were sensitive to Italian influence, but they preserved the gold ground, flat patterning and hieratic gesture. Without a detailed record, the panel can be judged only as a devout, competent work in a tradition that valued continuity over novelty."},"cma:159034":{"d":"A hunter and a Native guide in a wilderness setting are the subject of this oil on canvas, about 65 by 78 centimeters, dated 1869. The title is nearly all the record offers, and the catalog gives no life dates for the painter, C. L. Woodhouse, so the number of other figures, the game, the terrain and the weather are left unstated. The picture belongs to the American sporting and wilderness genre of the years after the Civil War.","i":"By 1869 hunting and fishing trips to the northern forests had become a fashionable pursuit of well-to-do Easterners, who hired local Native or backwoods guides to lead them through the woods and bring them back. Pictures of the hunter and his guide appealed to such sportsmen: they recalled the pleasures of the trip and flattered the buyer as a figure of rugged outdoor virtue. The Indian guide also fit a romantic idea of vanishing wilderness, a stereotype that obscured the guides' skill and professional status.","c":"With so little recorded, the fair judgment is of the type. Hunting pictures of the 1860s often pair a smooth, finished sportsman with a more rugged guide, and the best of them show real knowledge of woods, weather and gear; the weaker ones are stock figures in a stock landscape. Whether this picture rises above the type depends on how convincingly the two men and the forest are observed, which the viewer can judge on the spot."},"met:435844":{"d":"Young men and a winged Cupid are gathered for a private concert on this large canvas, about 92 by 118 centimeters, dated 1597. The museum's keywords record musicians, a lute, a violin, grapes, Cupid and music, so the number and poses of the figures, their costumes and the colors are left unstated. Painted when Caravaggio was in his mid-twenties, it shows the figures half-length and close to the viewer, in the manner of his early Roman genre pictures.","i":"The picture was probably painted for Cardinal Francesco Maria Del Monte, Caravaggio's first important patron, a music lover whose household included singers and instrumentalists. Music, love and wine run together here: Cupid and the grapes, a symbol of Bacchus, join the lute and violin, which stand for harmony, in a reminder that the pleasures of the senses are fleeting. Viewers in the cardinal's circle would have enjoyed the learned riddle and the lifelike models.","c":"An early work that already shows the quality that made Caravaggio famous: real people, with real faces and unidealized bodies, taking on mythic roles. The closeness of the figures gives the group a warm, intimate energy, and the absorbed musicians are convincing. The composition is crowded and some passages are awkward, and it lacks the dramatic light of his later work, but it is among the most appealing of his early genre pieces."},"cma:148758":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 203 by 153 centimeters, painted in 1606 and 1607, shows the martyrdom of Saint Andrew on his cross. The museum's record gives no further description, so the arrangement of the saint, the executioners and the onlookers is left unstated here. Caravaggio's known manner in these late pictures is to isolate a few life-size figures against deep darkness, lit by a strong beam from one side, with the viewer placed uncomfortably close to the action.","i":"According to the medieval Golden Legend, Andrew was bound, not nailed, to an X-shaped cross and preached to the crowd for two days; when the local governor, fearing a riot, ordered him released, the apostle prayed to be left to die as Christ had. Caravaggio probably painted it in Naples, soon after he fled Rome in 1606 under sentence of death, for the Spanish viceroy of Naples, the Count of Benavente.","c":"Caravaggio's late manner is easy to name: a near-monochrome palette, a pool of stark light and a background of nothing but dark. His martyrdoms avoid theatrical gore and rely on faces and hands; the restraint is what makes them hit hard. Judged as one of the late Neapolitan works, it shows a painter in flight pursuing a sparer, more tragic art than the swagger of his Roman years."},"met:437986":{"d":"A small group of half-length figures presses close to the viewer on this canvas, about 94 by 125 centimeters, dated 1610. The museum's keywords record soldiers, men, women and Saint Peter, so the exact poses, the costumes and the colors are left unstated. It is one of the last pictures Caravaggio painted before his death in 1610, and the manner of those years is spare, dark and swiftly painted, with a few lit faces and hands emerging from shadow.","i":"The scene shows Peter's denial of Christ on the night of the arrest, told in all four Gospels: accused of being a follower, he swears three times that he does not know the man, and then the cock crows. In Caravaggio's treatment the accusing figures press in, and the moment is a confrontation of glances and gestures. The subject of failed loyalty and later repentance was a favorite of Counter-Reformation art, because Peter became the rock of the Church.","c":"A picture in which almost nothing is spent on setting: a few figures, a single raking light, a black ground, and drama carried by pointing hands and wary looks. The late brushwork is thin and quick, and the colors are earthy. It is a work of compression and psychological intensity, a far cry from the swagger of his early Roman pictures, and one of the most powerful efforts of a painter in his last year."},"cma:97636":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Mrs. Goss, and nicknamed The Woman in Fur, painted in oil on canvas about 76 by 64 centimeters, probably in the 1850s. The museum's record offers no description beyond the title, so her pose, her features, the fur and the background are left unstated. Fur in a portrait of this decade was a sign of comfort and means, whether as a collar, a muff or a wrap, and the nickname suggests it is the most striking feature of the picture.","i":"Caroline Ransom, who lived from 1838 to 1910, was an American portraitist at a time when few women could train and work as professional painters. A portrait of a named sitter, identified here only as Mrs. Goss, reflects the local market for likenesses, in which families and civic leaders commissioned portraits. The alternative title, The Woman in Fur, suggests that the picture was later admired as a striking image as well as a record of a person.","c":"A portrait by a young woman painter of the 1850s is itself of historical interest; whether it is artistically distinguished cannot be judged from the title. The type, a half-length sitter in rich fur against a plain ground, depends on the painter's handling of textures: the contrast of soft fur, smooth skin and dark dress. If she caught those well, the nickname is deserved. In any case it stands as evidence of a woman professional at work."},"met:910391":{"d":"The record gives only the title and the keywords 'Christ' and 'Crucifixion', so this entry describes the type. The panel is small, about 58 by 39 centimeters, and shows the crucified Christ as a devotional image, the kind of picture intended for a chapel, a bedroom or a collector's study. Painters in the circle of Caravaggio, as Cecco was, typically modeled such figures with strong light against shadow, giving the body weight and presence.","i":"'Cecco' is a nickname for Francesco, and the painter is identified with the Roman Francesco Buoneri, who was active in the 1610s in the circle of Caravaggio. In the Counter-Reformation the Church encouraged the faithful to meditate on Christ's physical suffering, and a Crucifixion without the usual crowd of mourners, as the record's keywords suggest, focused attention on that. Such panels were made for private prayer as well as for the market.","c":"A solitary Crucifixion leaves a painter nowhere to hide: the body, the cross and the light must carry everything. Cecco is among the more individual of Caravaggio's followers, with a taste for lyrical color and a softer touch than the grimmer imitators of the master. At this small scale, restraint and pathos, not spectacle, are what one would look for in a painter of his gifts."},"cma:147522":{"d":"On a small oak panel, the Holy Family rests on its journey to Egypt. The Virgin Mary sits while an angel brings the plump young Christ Child fruit, and Saint Joseph leads their donkey away to graze. The panel, about 36 by 46 centimeters, is painted in delicate pinks and blues, with a transparent treatment of light and shadow that gives the scene a gentle, domestic atmosphere. It was intended as a small devotional picture.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew tells of the flight into Egypt to escape Herod, but the rest on the way comes from later legends and was a popular subject in Counter-Reformation art, where it showed the Holy Family as an ordinary family. Angels attending the family on the journey are a feature of those legends. The picture was long thought to be by Simon Vouet and is now attributed to his follower and collaborator Charles Poërson.","c":"A gentle, domestic picture in the sweet, rosy manner of Vouet's Parisian workshop, whose Italian training brought a new grace into French painting around 1640. The attribution to Poërson explains its slightly generalized types, but the pinks and blues and the soft transparent shadows are charming, and the intimate scale suits the subject. Few will cry out for more drama; the tenderness is the point."},"met:888924":{"d":"A still life of a vase of flowers, a melon, peaches and grapes, as the title says, painted in 1780 in oil on canvas and measuring about 46 by 56 centimeters. The record carries the keywords Fruit, Grapes, Peaches, Flowers and Still Life, but the museum gives no description of the arrangement, the background or the light. The format is a small horizontal cabinet picture, of a size suited to a salon wall.","i":"The painter is named in the title with her full aristocratic style, Charlotte Eustache Sophie de Fuligny Damas, marquise de Grollier, which marks her as a noblewoman and probably an amateur. In eighteenth-century France flowers and fruit were among the few specialties open to women painters, and the Académie admitted some, such as Anne Vallayer-Coster in 1770. A melon, peaches and grapes with flowers is a typical summer table, evoking abundance.","c":"Fruit and flowers allowed a woman of rank to display skill and taste within propriety, and this picture belongs to a lively tradition that includes Chardin, Vallayer-Coster and the Dutch flower painters. Amateur work in the genre tends toward a pleasing, careful finish rather than the brilliance of the professionals, and the rarity of pictures by noblewomen in museum collections is part of this one's value. It was acquired in 2022."},"cma:108978":{"d":"A portrait of a man named John Childe, painted around 1835 in oil on canvas and measuring about 76 by 63 centimeters. The Cleveland Museum of Art's record gives only the title, the artist, the date and the dimensions, so his pose, dress and setting are not described here. The format is a standard bust or half-length portrait, the type Harding produced in great numbers.","i":"By the 1830s Harding was living in Boston, where he was one of the most sought-after portraitists, and he also painted notable figures such as Daniel Webster and Chief Justice John Marshall. Portraits of ordinary prosperous men, like this one of John Childe, were the staple of his practice, and the Cleveland museum holds the picture in its collection of American portraits through the Charles W. Harkness Endowment Fund.","c":"A bluff, direct portrait is what one expects of Harding, and the type is his best: sturdy modeling, a lively eye and none of the idealizing polish of his rivals. The portrait can be placed by its type, and by that measure it is a competent member of a large, honest body of work. Its sitter's identity adds little to the picture, but the picture does not need more."},"cma:117805":{"d":"A small upright oil on canvas, about 23 by 18 centimeters, dated only to the 1890s. The Cleveland Museum of Art's record gives no description, but the title, Sweeping Snow, points to a winter scene in which snow is being cleared, perhaps from a street, a path or a garden. At this size the picture is probably a quick sketch of the kind Hassam made out of doors.","i":"Childe Hassam returned from Paris to America in 1889 and settled in New York, where he found subjects in the city's streets and parks in every season. His winter pictures, with their blue and violet shadows on snow, helped establish American Impressionism. The humble task of sweeping snow, a daily urban chore, turns an everyday labor into a subject for the eye, as Impressionist painters in France did with working life.","c":"Hassam's small snow pictures are among his most engaging: quick, fresh and delighted by cold color. At this size the sketchy touch is the point, and a single working figure against bright snow gives a lively focus. The type is slight, but its sparkle and its truth to a New York winter explain why collectors prized such panels."},"met:12928":{"d":"A woman and an eagle are the recorded motifs of this upright painting on glass, about 58 by 41 centimeters, dated around 1800. The woman is the personification of Liberty, usually shown in classical dress and the standard emblem of the new American republic, and the eagle is its national bird. The museum gives no further description of her costume, pose or setting.","i":"After the first American ship reached Canton in 1784, Chinese workshops there began producing paintings for the American market, among them reverse paintings on glass, in which the artist works from the front of the image to the back, laying in details first and background last. Patriotic subjects such as Liberty, probably copied from European or American prints, were favorites with American merchants and captains who took them home.","c":"Reverse glass painting gives color a jewel-like gloss and a bright, flat clarity that suits an emblem. The weaknesses are stiff drawing and symbols copied from prints by artists who may not have known their meaning, and the combination of Chinese technique with an American allegory has an odd, charming strangeness. Interesting as a cross-cultural object more than as a painting; the pleasure is in the brilliance."},"met:13027":{"d":"A portrait of a man named William Conklin, painted around 1800 in oil on canvas by an unnamed Chinese painter and measuring about 58 by 45 centimeters. Beyond 'man' and 'portrait', nothing is recorded of his pose, clothing or expression. It was given to the museum in 1930 by Mrs. W. R. Moore in memory of Nana Louise Moore. The portrait belongs to the group of Western sitters painted in China.","i":"Chinese portrait painters in Canton, working in oil in a style learned from European painters, made portraits of foreign merchants and sea captains for them to take home. The best-known of them, the painter called Spoilum, was active around 1800 and was patronized by American captains. An American named in the title, William Conklin, presumably visited Canton in the early days of the China trade, though the museum record does not say who he was.","c":"Chinese export portraits of Western sitters have a distinctive look: smooth, clear flesh, a sharp outline and a slightly stiff, doll-like pose that few Western portraitists would have used. The best are charming and truthful, and the worst are routine. A portrait of this kind records not only a man but a meeting of two painting traditions, and its interest is as much historical as artistic."},"met:13003":{"d":"A ship is the single recorded motif of this horizontal canvas, about 64 by 89 centimeters, painted around 1845 in oil by an unnamed Chinese painter. The title names the vessel as the John W. Brewer. The museum gives no description of the sails, sea or background, but the format and the title mark it as a ship portrait, a commissioned likeness of a particular vessel, made for its owner or captain.","i":"In the nineteenth century Chinese artists at Canton, Whampoa and Hong Kong painted portraits of the Western ships that traded there, often in oil, working from the vessel itself or from the captain's description, and the finished picture went home with the captain as a souvenir and a record. American merchants and masters prized them. The John W. Brewer is known from the museum record only by its name.","c":"Ship portraits are judged by fidelity: the rigging, the sail plan and the flags must be right, and Chinese painters at Canton were famous for getting them so, with a slightly stiff precision and a bright, clear sea. The result is accurate and decorative, with limited atmosphere. A well-made document of the China trade, valued by the shipowners who bought it and by historians today."},"met:12865":{"d":"A fire at the foreign trading houses of Canton is the subject of this very small painting in oil on copper, about 12 by 16 centimeters. The record lists boats, a factory and a fire, with the keywords Factories, Fire and Boats, so the picture presumably shows flames on the waterfront and craft on the river. It is dated only to the nineteenth century, and the museum gives no further description of the scene.","i":"The factories were the foreign merchants' warehouses and residences, called hongs, along the Canton waterfront, where trade with the West was confined under the Canton system until 1842. They burned in a great fire in 1822 and were burned again by a mob in 1856, early in the Second Opium War; the record does not say which fire is shown. Small copper panels like this were sold to visitors as souvenirs of dramatic events.","c":"Disaster pictures sold well, and this tiny copper panel, with its flames and river craft, would have made a memorable souvenir. Its scale limits detail, but the format is well suited to a view of a waterfront blaze. The painter is anonymous, the date uncertain, and the likelihood is a competent workshop production that pleased through color and drama rather than refinement."},"nga:51140":{"d":"Under a wide, open sky, an archery contest unfolds at the center: onlookers with bows and arrows watch a figure in a long red outfit aim at targets set up on the left. At the right, people in long blue and gray garments stand beside horses tied to a fence near a tall tree. Behind the fence, an ornate building with gray walls and a black roof shelters a group seated under a carved canopy, where a figure in orange robes watches while another in blue kneels before him. All the men wear long braids and red hats, and white tents with colored flags stand in the distance.","i":"The long braids, or queues, and the hats mark the figures as subjects of the Qing dynasty, whose Manchu rulers prized archery and horsemanship as the core of their military tradition and held contests of this kind. The seated figure approached by a kneeling man may be a dignitary or the emperor, but the museum does not say. Oil on cotton was a foreign technique, brought to China by European painters at the Qing court and practiced in Canton workshops.","c":"The painting has the crowded liveliness of a panorama: many small figures, each given a task, arranged across a stage-like space with a clear diagonal from the horses to the targets. The perspective is a hybrid, with Chinese conventions of viewpoint and European modeling, and the figures are rather doll-like. An unusual and engaging picture, more vivid as a document of ceremony than as a drama."},"nga:51141":{"d":"Seen from high above, a waterfront stretches across the canvas: light blue hills close the horizon, a stone bridge arches over a river, and another bridge crosses at the right. In the foreground an ornate procession of adults and children carries lanterns and banners shaped like large fish, and the line snakes back across the bridge toward the distant hills. Small buildings with red-tiled roofs stand on the far shore beneath a tower, and the procession ends at a large building framed by tall trees at the lower left. The palette is warm ochre and rust, set against soft greens and the blues and grays of the water.","i":"Fish lanterns and banners belong to festivals such as the Lantern Festival at the end of the New Year celebrations, since the fish, whose Chinese name sounds like the word for surplus, is a symbol of abundance. The high viewpoint belongs to the tradition of Chinese landscape painting, while the oil technique and the modeled forms show Western influence. Like Archery Contest, it was given by Dr. Catherine Lilly Bacon.","c":"A cheerful, decorative panorama in which the pattern of the procession winding through the landscape is the chief pleasure: the bridges, the tower and the long line of lanterns make a satisfying rhythm. The figures are tiny and the oil handling somewhat flat, so the picture is more diagram than atmosphere. As a hybrid of Chinese viewpoint and Western medium, it is more interesting than it is accomplished."},"cma:151882":{"d":"A large landscape, about 123 by 173 centimeters, built around the cascades and craggy rock formations that made Tivoli, a small town twenty miles east of Rome, famous. Water is the central theme. Fishermen occupy the foreground, and touches of civilization appear in the background. The rugged cliffs recall the dramatic Italian landscape tradition of Salvator Rosa, while the calmer pastoral mood comes from the French line of Claude Lorrain. Painted in oil on canvas in 1737, it is probably Claude-Joseph Vernet's first major work during his study in Rome.","i":"Tivoli drew artists with its falls and its ancient remains, and a view of the cascades was a standard test for a landscape painter in Italy. For a young Frenchman studying in Rome, it was a way to absorb two traditions at once: the wild nature of Rosa and the ordered pastoral of Claude. Such pictures appealed to travelers and collectors; Lucien Bonaparte, Napoleon's brother, later owned this one.","c":"As an early work this is more a synthesis of existing models than a personal vision, and its charm lies in watching Vernet reconcile Rosa's rough drama with Claude's calm. The scale shows ambition, and the handling of falling water is already confident. The harbors, storms and moonlit seas that made him famous would be more distinctive, yet this picture explains where they came from."},"cma:125093":{"d":"A wide, sweeping vista in which a wooded hill, topped by a structure resembling an ancient Roman temple, fills the dark left side. The foreground sweeps away in a curve to the right and leads the eye into an increasingly luminous, hazy distance. That contrast between the shadowed near ground and the glowing far horizon creates the depth. Painted around 1630, early in Claude Lorrain's career, in oil on canvas, about 97 by 134 centimeters.","i":"Claude was born in France but worked his whole career in Rome, and he painted the surrounding campagna as an ideal, not a record: harmoniously ordered, with a golden, hazy light. The temple-like building evokes the ancient world and an imagined age of calm, a mood patrons valued more than any story. The title names no episode, so the picture is best understood as an atmosphere. Painted early, it shows the formula taking shape.","c":"Even at this early date Claude's central idea is in place: a dark side, a curving recession and a light that grows warmer the farther the eye travels. Early works are sometimes less refined than the mature pictures of the 1640s and 1650s, and the arrangement can feel a little schematic, but the command of luminous distance is already convincing. Landscape painters for two centuries drew on this formula."},"cma:137195":{"d":"In the foreground of a tall, soaring landscape, the Holy Family rests on a shady bank while kneeling angels offer fruit to the Christ child. Lush foliage and a tranquil atmosphere surround the group. Claude Lorrain placed this Middle Eastern biblical event in the Roman countryside, and the figures belong to a landscape of idealized beauty. The canvas is large, over two meters high, painted in oil in the early 1640s.","i":"Matthew's Gospel tells that Joseph, warned in a dream, took Mary and the infant Jesus to Egypt to escape Herod's massacre of the children. The rest on the way is not in the Gospels but grew from later legend, such as the apocryphal Gospel of Pseudo-Matthew, in which the family is miraculously fed. Claude's version turns the story into an idyll of divine care, and collectors treated such landscapes as a calm retreat from city life.","c":"The figures are a pretext; the real subject is light and air. Claude's ideal landscape is at its most generous here, more beautiful and harmonious than nature itself, which was exactly the aim. The Holy Family risks being overshadowed by the scenery, which is also the limit of the approach: the devotion is gentle rather than intense. Few painters have made the sacred and the pastoral sit so comfortably together."},"met:435924":{"d":"A portrait of a man, painted in oil on card about 1550 by an unidentified European artist, whom the Met credits only as a Continental painter. The sitter is anonymous too: all that can be said is that this is a likeness of one man of the mid-sixteenth century. Card was a lighter and cheaper support than the usual wooden panel of the period, which may mean that this was a small, portable likeness or a study rather than a grand commission.","i":"In the middle of the sixteenth century a portrait recorded a likeness for family, friends or posterity, and it could mark status, a marriage or a friendship, or serve as a gift between allies. With neither sitter nor painter named, this picture cannot be tied to any particular purpose. It is best read as one example of the widespread Renaissance interest in the individual face.","c":"With no named painter and no identified sitter, the picture can only be judged in general terms. As a portrait of its decade it reflects the Renaissance taste for a direct, close-up likeness, and the unusual card support hints at an informal, practical purpose. Anonymous portraits like this are valued less as landmarks than as evidence of how widely likenesses were produced, and how much of that production is now unattributed."},"met:435925":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted in oil on a thin metal plate laid down on card, about 1630, by an unidentified European artist. The sitter is a single woman, and her name is unknown. A metal plate gives a smooth, hard surface that takes fine detail, and the card mount suggests a small, carefully finished picture. It belongs to the early Baroque, when small portraits were widely exchanged.","i":"Portraits of women in the 1630s were made to mark betrothals and marriages, to record a family's alliances, or to be kept by relatives and friends at a distance. The sitter here is unnamed, so her rank and the occasion for the picture cannot be stated. What the object does show is the seventeenth-century habit of giving private likenesses a durable, jewel-like form.","c":"The choice of a metal plate over wood or canvas signals care, since the hard surface rewards fine, controlled brushwork. Beyond that, the unnamed painter and unnamed sitter leave little to weigh. It is a modest document of early Baroque portraiture, to be valued as one of the many unrecorded likenesses from which the period's visual world is pieced together, not as a landmark."},"met:435926":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted in oil on a copper plate about 1630 by an unidentified European artist. The sitter is a single woman, unnamed. Copper, polished smooth and hard, gave painters a surface for minute detail and a lasting, enamel-like finish, and it was widely used across Europe in the seventeenth century for small cabinet pictures and portraits. The scale is likely to be small.","i":"Small copper portraits could be carried, hung in a cabinet or given as keepsakes, and a likeness on a durable plate would outlast one on wood. In the 1630s such pictures circulated among families, friends and courtly circles. The woman here is unnamed, so her rank and the reason for the picture are unknown, and the object is best read as an example of how portraiture adapted to small, portable formats.","c":"Copper is unforgiving: it records every fine stroke, and a small portrait on it is either exact or clumsy. With no named painter to vouch for it, which of those this one is remains an open question. It is a representative piece of seventeenth-century European portrait practice, interesting chiefly for its medium and period."},"met:435927":{"d":"A portrait of a child in painted enamel on gold, made about 1650 by an unidentified European artist. Enamel portraits were produced by fusing powdered colored glass onto a metal base in a kiln, which gives a glossy, jewel-like surface that does not fade. The sitter is a child whose name is unknown. A miniature of this kind was typically made to be held, set in a case or worn.","i":"Portraits of children recorded heirs for their families and were sometimes sent to relatives or prospective in-laws when marriages between families were being arranged. In an age of high child mortality, a likeness was also a precious keepsake. Painted enamel, developed in France around 1630 and spreading to Geneva and other centers, was the luxury medium for such portraits. Who the child was, and why this likeness was made, is not known.","c":"Enamel is an exacting medium, since colors must survive repeated firings, and the best examples reach a delicacy of color that oil cannot match. In the middle of the seventeenth century that technical skill was itself a mark of luxury. Whether this child's portrait is among the finer products of the technique is hard to say, but the combination of gold and enamel places it among the costly objects of its time."},"met:435928":{"d":"A portrait of a man in painted enamel, made about 1670 by an unidentified European artist. The enamel technique, in which powdered colored glass is fused to a metal base in a kiln, produces a glassy, permanent surface, and portraits in it were usually small, made to be set in a case or a frame. The sitter is a single, unnamed man. Such portraits were costly, since the colors had to be fired in stages.","i":"About 1670 painted enamels were highly fashionable, and the leading enamelers of the day, such as Jean Petitot, worked for royal courts in France and England. A portrait like this might be a private keepsake, a gift to a patron or a pledge of friendship or love. The sitter is unnamed, so there is no way to tell whether this piece belonged to a court or to a private household.","c":"Enamel portraits aim for smooth, luminous modeling and an exact likeness within a very small field, and the best rival the finest oils for subtlety. With no painter or sitter named, this example is best rated as a type. It stands as a typical product of the vogue for miniature portraits in the age of Louis XIV and Charles II, and it deserves to be seen in that context."},"met:435929":{"d":"A portrait of a man in painted enamel, made about 1690 by an unidentified European artist. Enamel portraits of this date are small, glossy and richly colored, built up from powdered glass fired onto metal. The sitter is a single man whose name is unknown. The medium and the date are the best-established facts about it, and they place it in an age when painted enamel portraits were still prized as costly luxury objects.","i":"By 1690 the enamel miniature had spread from the French court to workshops in several countries, and portraits of this kind served as private keepsakes, as presentation gifts and as likenesses of family members to be worn or carried. The identity of the sitter and the occasion for the commission are unknown. As a portrait of an unnamed man made at the end of the seventeenth century, it is best seen as part of a wide market for small, personal likenesses.","c":"Enamel miniatures from the end of the century ask for a steady hand and a good kiln more than for invention, and their charm lies in polish and fidelity. Without a name for the painter or the sitter, this one can be assessed only as a type: a compact, durable record of a face in a fashionable luxury medium. It is more informative about taste than about personality."},"met:435930":{"d":"A portrait of a woman in painted enamel, made about 1690 by an unidentified European artist. The sitter is a single, unnamed woman, presumably shown head and shoulders, as was usual in the medium. Enamel gives a hard, glossy surface that keeps its color for centuries, which is one reason such portraits survive in good condition; they were small enough to be worn as jewelry, set in a case or kept in a cabinet.","i":"Portraits of women in enamel were often love tokens, wedding gifts or keepsakes for family, and the best were set in gold frames or cases. The unnamed sitter here may have been a bride or a wife, but nothing says so. About 1690 the fashion for enamel portraits was still strong in France, where the technique had been developed, and in the courts that followed French taste.","c":"Seen as a type, it is a competent specimen of a refined craft: the hard glaze can make faces look porcelain-smooth, and a skilled hand adds life with a few finely drawn details. Whether this sitter achieves that life is hard to judge. The portrait's main value is in showing how a fashionable woman of the 1690s might be remembered in a form small enough to hold."},"met:435931":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted on a thin slab of ivory about 1740 to 1750 by an unidentified European artist. Miniatures on ivory were painted in watercolor or gouache, and the translucent ivory shows through the thin paint, giving flesh a soft glow. The sitter is a single woman whose name is unknown. It is a small object, intended to be held, framed, worn or kept in a case.","i":"The ivory miniature became fashionable in the early eighteenth century after the Venetian painter Rosalba Carriera began using it around 1700. Portraits of this kind served as gifts between lovers and relatives, as keepsakes for travelers and as records of family members for those far away. The sitter's name and the occasion for this one are unknown.","c":"Ivory rewards delicacy: the luminous flesh tints, built up in tiny strokes and dots, are what distinguish a fine miniature from a routine one. The Rococo period brought a taste for soft color and light, which suits the medium well. Since neither painter nor sitter is named, the portrait stands as a representative example of mid-century practice, worth looking at for its jewel-like scale more than for individual character."},"met:435932":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted on ivory about 1750 by an unidentified European artist, credited by the Met only to a Continental painter. Like most miniatures of its time, it would have been made with fine brushes and water-based color on a thin sheet of ivory, so small that it was worn or carried. The sitter is a woman whose name is unknown, presumably shown head and shoulders, as was usual for the type.","i":"In the middle of the eighteenth century, miniatures were exchanged as tokens of love and friendship and worn on the body in bracelets, brooches or lockets, so a portrait of a woman might have been made for a husband, a suitor or a relative. The sitter is not named, and her story is lost. What survives is the fashion for private, portable likenesses that grew during the Rococo.","c":"The best ivory miniatures of the 1750s have a pearly softness that makes faces seem to glow, with a pleasing balance of precision and ease. Whether this one is among them is hard to say, but an anonymous attribution suggests a competent professional rather than a famous hand. It rewards attention as a small survivor of a once enormous trade in private likenesses."},"met:435939":{"d":"A portrait of a man in painted enamel, made about 1750 by an unidentified artist whom the Met suggests may have been Swiss. Enamel is made by fusing powdered colored glass onto a metal base in a kiln, which gives a smooth, glossy and permanent surface. The sitter is a single man whose name is unknown. As a miniature, it was probably made to be set in a case or frame.","i":"Geneva was the leading center of enamel painting in the eighteenth century, producing portrait miniatures and decorated watch cases and snuffboxes for an international market. A portrait like this could be a keepsake, a gift or a record of a family member. The sitter is unnamed, so his place in society cannot be stated, and the attribution to a Swiss hand remains only a possibility.","c":"Geneva's enamelers were famous for precision and clean, bright color, and even a tentative Swiss attribution points to that tradition of exactness. The strengths of such work are polish and fidelity of detail; the weakness, often, is a certain fixity of expression, as if the hard glaze had frozen the sitter. A good example of a technical luxury of the Rococo rather than a revelation."},"met:435933":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted on ivory about 1780 by an unidentified European artist. The title records a traditional identification of the sitter as Princess Apraxine, but the phrase said to be marks it as unproven. Beyond that traditional identification, nothing is known of the sitter. As an ivory miniature it was made to be held or worn, with watercolor applied in tiny strokes that let the ivory glow through.","i":"Apraxine is the French form of Apraksin, a prominent Russian noble family, and Russian aristocrats of the late eighteenth century were avid patrons of European portrait painters. The title's wording means that the identification rests on tradition, not documents. In the 1780s the miniature was an elite accessory, worn on the body or exchanged as a sign of affection or alliance.","c":"The elegant simplicity of late eighteenth-century miniatures, with their soft powdered tones and clear outlines, suits an aristocratic subject, and an ivory portrait of this date can be very fine. Since the artist is unknown and the sitter's identity unproven, the picture leaves an open question; it is best enjoyed as a refined example of the Neoclassical miniature with a pleasing legend attached."},"met:435936":{"d":"A portrait of a woman drawn in pencil and red chalk on paper, dated by the Met to 1827 or later, with a question mark, by an unidentified European artist. The sitter is a single, unnamed woman. Red chalk gives warm, flesh-like tones while pencil supplies fine outline, and the combination is typical of quick portrait drawings made in the first half of the nineteenth century.","i":"Pencil portraits were a staple of the early nineteenth century, when they offered a cheaper and quicker likeness than a painted portrait and were made in the home or the studio for family albums. The red chalk accent continues an eighteenth-century tradition into the new period. The sitter is unnamed, and with the date uncertain, the drawing cannot be tied to a particular fashion or event.","c":"Drawings of this kind succeed or fail on the clarity of line, and the combination of pencil with a touch of warm chalk can give a face unexpected life. This sheet is unattributed and the sitter unnamed, so its quality is a matter of looking; as a document it shows the continuing demand for modest, affordable likenesses after the age of the miniature. A small, unassuming object that deserves to be seen on its own terms."},"cma:166510":{"d":"A still life of crayfish, oysters and fruit on a table, with a porcelain bowl and a gold object among the furnishings of the table, painted in oil on canvas, about 44 by 57 centimeters. The title names the shellfish and fruit that give the picture its subject, and the bowl most likely came from China, according to the museum. The picture belongs to the rich Flemish and Dutch tradition of luxury still lifes, to which Cornelis de Heem contributed in the later seventeenth century.","i":"Chinese porcelain was a prized luxury in Europe, and political turbulence in China effectively cut off ceramic imports to the West by the 1640s, so such a bowl would have been considered at least as valuable as the gold object on the table. Oysters, crayfish and fruit were costly, pleasurable foods, and a still life of them combined sensuous delight with a reminder of how quickly such pleasures pass, a standard reading of the genre.","c":"De Heem followed his father, Jan Davidsz de Heem, into the luxury still life, and the combination of seafood, fruit and porcelain is typical of the family's repertoire. The type rewards close looking at textures: wet shell, bright skin of fruit, glaze. He is a skilled follower of his father's formula, valued for competence and polish rather than for invention."},"cma:147447":{"d":"A woman is shown within a painted oval of imitation marble. Jewels glitter in her hair, and her silk gown is adorned with silver thread and costly lace, all signs of wealth. Research suggests the sitter may be Elizabeth Boothby (1590 to 1658), daughter of a prominent London merchant. Painted in oil on wood in 1619, about 66 by 56 centimeters, by Cornelis Jonson, who was born in London and trained in Amsterdam.","i":"Portraits of merchants' daughters in the early 1600s advertised family wealth: lace, silver thread and jewels were costly and openly displayed. Jonson, born in London and trained in Amsterdam, spent his last twenty years in the Dutch Republic, so his work joins the English and Dutch traditions; this portrait, painted in England, reflects Dutch habits. The identification with Elizabeth Boothby is only a likelihood, based on research, and the date of 1619 would put her at about twenty-nine.","c":"A sober, finely finished portrait whose main interest is its display of status: lace, jewels and silver thread. Jonson's manner combines English formality with Dutch crispness, and the feigned oval is a neat device that frames the face and adds depth. The expression is reserved and the pose conventional, so the picture reads more as a record of wealth than as a study of character. Within his English period it is a representative, polished work."},"nga:227488":{"d":"Christ kneels at the center of a large canvas, facing left, his pale back and arms marked by thin, dark red wounds, wearing only a white cloth at his waist, as he reaches for a black robe on the ground. A discarded whip and a bundle of thorny twigs lie before him. Five angels in green, red, pink, teal and gold, with wide feathered wings, form a semicircle around him in poses of lament and compassion. The floor is checkered in light red, the background is dark, and a white square column stands left of center. Oil on canvas, about 173 by 284 centimeters, 1670s.","i":"After being scourged by Pilate's soldiers, Christ is shown gathering his clothes, an episode not described in the Gospels but imagined in later devotion as a moment of silent endurance. The whip and thorns are instruments of the Passion, and the angels weep or console on behalf of the viewer. Such images, painted by Correa in colonial Mexico, invited meditation on Christ's suffering and belonged to the Catholic devotional culture of New Spain.","c":"The kneeling Christ is rendered with restrained pathos: the wounds are thin lines, not gore, and the angels' varied gestures make a choir of grief around the silent central figure. Correa, one of the leading painters of New Spain, combines the monumentality of Spanish Baroque with the rich color of colonial taste. The bright angels risk dominating the sorrow, but the composition is clear and moving."},"cma:95274":{"d":"In an inn, an older man and a younger man in the foreground smoke tobacco. The younger man rests limply against the wall with his head lolling back, a gesture that signaled he had overindulged. Another man standing nearby prepares his pipe and watches the younger one with amusement. Oil on wood, about 37 by 26 centimeters, painted about 1640 by David Teniers the Younger, who specialized in small pictures of rustic Flemish life.","i":"Tobacco was a novelty in the 1600s, introduced from the Americas and the East Indies, and was believed to be medicinal; it was even recommended for people who lived in damp places such as Antwerp, where Teniers painted this scene. Moralists criticized it as a danger to health and morals, and its mixed reputation made it a marketable, amusing subject. The slumped young man is a comic warning of excess.","c":"A small, shrewd picture in which Teniers's affectionate satire does its work in three figures: the sprawled youth, the experienced smoker and the grinning onlooker. The handling is crisp, the color earthy and the composition economical. It lacks the crowd and the landscape of his larger kermesses, but it has a concentrated humor. This is early Teniers at his most direct, before the silvery elegance of his later style."},"cma:123149":{"d":"In a gaming room, two men on the left play a high-stakes game of backgammon while a cluster of three onlookers watches them lay their bets and another man tallies the points on a scoreboard. In the right corner a young woman stands among men, and an older woman looks down on the game from a window. Oil on wood transferred to canvas, about 59 by 81 centimeters, painted in the 1640s by David Teniers the Younger.","i":"The young woman surrounded by men and the older woman at the window indicate that the room is probably the gaming room of a brothel, where courtesans provided further entertainment. Backgammon, popular in the 1600s and often played for money, was a standard emblem of folly and wickedness, of wasting time and money on wagers. Pictures of games, like scenes of procuring, offered moralizing lessons wrapped in amusing detail.","c":"Teniers keeps the moral edge light: the gamblers' concentration, the watchers' leaning bodies and the figures of the women set up a little comedy of vice, observed with detachment rather than condemnation. The composition is clear, with the game as its center, and the figures are well drawn and varied. If the sermon is half-hearted, the scene is entertaining, and it shows why such pictures sold to Antwerp collectors."},"cma:148965":{"d":"A horizontal village festival in which peasants dance and play music in a cheerful celebration, with close-knit family groups taking part. Household objects in brass, ceramic and glass appear in perfect condition, not broken or disorderly. Oil on canvas, about 38 by 71 centimeters, painted about 1646 to 1650 by David Teniers the Younger. Its long, low format, nearly twice as wide as it is tall, gives room for a crowd.","i":"Peasant festivals such as weddings, harvests and village holidays became an extremely popular subject for Flemish artists in the 1600s. Unlike his predecessors, who often showed broken and disorderly objects, Teniers emphasized household vessels in a perfect state, a way of showing the growing prosperity of the peasants, or at least of presenting the countryside as cheerful and well provided to the city collectors who bought such pictures. Surviving drawings show that he sketched many of his figures from life.","c":"Teniers's village feasts are the sunniest of the genre: the crowd is plump and cheerful, the pots and jugs gleam, and the light is silvery and warm. Compared with the coarse, brutal peasants of Brouwer, his people are cleaner and more decorative, a fantasy of country abundance for urban eyes. The charm is real; the realism is limited. A good example of the benign, polished peasant picture that made him famous."},"cma:159501":{"d":"Before a rustic inn, a group of villagers converse, embrace and dance to the tune of a bagpiper, set within a wide landscape. The picture combines two of Teniers's specialties, landscape and scenes of country life. Oil on wood, about 49 by 78 centimeters, painted about 1645 to 1650. The wide format, about three-fifths as tall as it is broad, gives the landscape generous room around the dancers.","i":"Teniers was one of many Netherlandish artists whose paintings celebrate the joys of country life, and these delicately painted works were created primarily for an elite urban audience nostalgic for a simpler existence. The bagpiper, the embracing couples and the inn are conventional ingredients of the village kermis, which collectors enjoyed as a cheerful, slightly comic fantasy.","c":"A cheerful, delicately painted cabinet picture in which the dancers are small and the landscape generous, allowing the airy light and soft green distance to carry the pleasure. Teniers's peasants are tidy and good-humored, and the composition is balanced and clear. It lacks the bite of Bruegel's earlier peasants, but it is skillful and agreeable, and it combines his gifts as landscape painter and storyteller."},"cma:99306":{"d":"A tiny oil on wood, about 14 by 12 centimeters, showing an old man and woman, as the title says, in the manner of David Teniers. The date given is the 1700s, after Teniers's death in 1690, so the picture is presumably a later work in his manner or a copy. Small pictures of old people, character heads, were a popular Flemish and Dutch type.","i":"Heads of old men and women in rustic or humorous guise were a favorite type of Flemish and Dutch painters, valued as studies of character and age rather than as portraits of named sitters. The pairing of man and woman suggests a couple, possibly with a gently comic or sentimental intention. Teniers's own versions, painted in his lifetime, were widely copied and imitated in the following century, and this may be one of those echoes.","c":"A little picture whose attribution is clouded by its date, and whose merits must be modest: such small heads rely on characterful wrinkles and a few touches of color. Its main interest is probably as a witness to Teniers's long afterlife, as collectors in the eighteenth century continued to prize and imitate his work. A minor curiosity."},"cma:141549":{"d":"A young man in a black suit stands full length in a bare grey room, his feet planted apart and his head tilted to one side. He has a dark bob and a small white collar, and he grins, with his eyes not quite pointing the same way. In his raised right hand he holds a tiny framed miniature, and in the other a long thin stick topped, at his waist, by a small white toy windmill, or pinwheel. A folding stool with a studded leather seat stands at the left, and a plain stone plinth rises behind. The light is soft and even, the colours are all greys, browns and blacks, and the figure casts a long shadow on the floor. It is a large oil on canvas.","i":"Cleveland identifies the sitter as Calabazas (the name means pumpkins), a jester at the court of Philip IV of Spain who had physical and mental disabilities. The court prized and doted on such people, and the museum says Velázquez emphasizes the condition rather than hiding it, using the format of a formal full-length court portrait to present a man generally seen as marginal and to humanize him. The pinwheel and the miniature portrait of a woman may together hint at the inconstancy of love. The picture is dated about 1631 to 1632, though some scholars have doubted the attribution to Velázquez.","c":"What succeeds is the directness. A full-length format normally reserved for kings is given to a man who would rarely be painted at all, and the result is neither mockery nor pity: he is a presence, grinning, off-balance, entirely himself. The bare space gives him room. The weaker parts are the stiff drawing of the stool and the thin treatment of the black costume, which may explain why the attribution has been questioned. Even so, the picture stands among the more humane portraits of court jesters."},"nga:126135":{"d":"A vertical oil on wood, about 37 by 26 centimetres, takes flowers as its single subject, as the title says. The museum's listing records no further detail, so the particular blooms, vase and setting cannot be described here. Small flower pieces on panel were made as cabinet pictures for domestic display, and by the 1860s German flower painters worked in a careful, detailed manner inherited from the Dutch flower painters of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries.","i":"Flower painting was one of the few specialities in which women could build a professional career in the nineteenth century, partly because it was thought a decorous subject and could be practised at home. Adelheid Dietrich belonged to that group of painters. By her day, the old symbolism of flowers as emblems of transience had largely given way to pleasure in accuracy and display, though a bouquet could still carry a sentimental message.","c":"Without the individual blooms to judge, this can fairly be placed among the well-made, conventional flower pieces of its time, which prize accurate petals and leaves and a polished finish. The small panel format suits that precision. Such pictures seldom reach the invention of the great Dutch exemplars, but the best are valued for delicacy of observation. It deserves attention as much for its place in a woman's profession as for any strong individual voice."},"cma:148369":{"d":"A girl of eleven, Elizabeth Spiegel, holds a recorder in her hands, which are painted with small, naturalistic fingers. She wears a gown of costly satin, pearl ornaments and an imported ostrich feather in her hair, and her skin seems to glow. The oil on wood panel, about 63 by 50 centimetres, belongs to a series of portraits representing the five senses, each of a daughter of a wealthy Amsterdam family; the recorder stands for hearing.","i":"The series personifies the five senses through portraits of young girls, a witty Dutch twist on an old allegory: instead of anonymous figures, the senses are real children of a rich household, each identified by an attribute, here a recorder for hearing. The satin, pearls and plumes proclaim the family's wealth, and the feather, imported from Africa, points to Amsterdam's role as a world trading centre in the 1630s.","c":"A charming and well-judged mix of portrait and allegory. The child's glowing face and small hands, gripping the instrument with convincing care, are closely observed, and the satin and feathers are rendered with the display the family wanted. Santvoort is not a painter of Rembrandt's depth, and the bright costume may compete with the face, but the picture avoids stiffness by letting a child stay a child within the formality."},"cma:311773":{"d":"A violinist fills the canvas at half length and very close to the viewer, in loose, colourful clothes, with a bold gaze, an unshaven face and a cheeky grin that shows a broken tooth; the title adds a wine glass. The tight, cropped composition pushes him into the viewer's space with a sense of lively spontaneity, in the manner of Caravaggio, whose style Baburen, a native of Utrecht, helped introduce to the Netherlands. The canvas is about 80 by 67 centimetres.","i":"Baburen spent several years in Rome, where he absorbed Caravaggio's dramatic light and down-to-earth models, and then returned to Utrecht. Musicians, drinkers and cardplayers shown at half length became the stock subjects of the Utrecht followers of Caravaggio. The loose dress and broken tooth mark the player as a figure on the edge of society, but the picture offers more delight than moral lesson; the same model appears in other Utrecht paintings.","c":"A sharp instance of Caravaggio's ideas reaching Dutch painting: the model's frank grin and the closeness of the figure make the viewer part of the room, and the broken tooth is a stroke of unidealised truth. The handling of cloth and of light across the face is confident rather than subtle, and the composition relies on one big idea rather than elaboration. Baburen died at about twenty-nine, so such works carry the extra interest of a talent cut short."},"cma:154645":{"d":"The picture illustrates a story from Ovid's Metamorphoses and sets it in a wide landscape with classical ruins, painted on a small copper plate about 37 by 49 centimetres. The figures are Mercury, messenger of the gods, and Argus, the giant with many eyes. In the myth Mercury plays his flute until Argus sleeps, then kills him to free Io, the mortal woman whom Juno had turned into a cow. The expansive vista, in the tradition of panoramic views, evokes the immensity of the world.","i":"In Ovid's tale, Jupiter's affair with Io roused Juno's jealousy; she turned the girl into a cow and set Argus, with a hundred eyes, to guard her. Mercury lulled the watchman to sleep and killed him, and Juno afterwards placed his eyes in the tail of the peacock. For Dutch painters of the seventeenth century the story offered a pastoral, classical world peopled by small figures, set against Italianate ruins that recalled Rome for those who had never been there.","c":"Dirck van der Lisse belongs to the group of Dutch painters who followed Cornelis van Poelenburch in making small, polished Italianate landscapes with mythological figures, usually on copper. The sweeping prospect and the ruins give the myth a stage, and copper allows a jewel-like finish. But the figures are small and the story needs the label to be understood, so the picture is more decoration than drama: elegant, calm and slightly impersonal."},"cma:97167":{"d":"A young man holds a crumpled letter in Italian whose words include Asto, a ship's mast, and mare, the sea, which hint at trade, and others that refer to anatomy and to something called bella. The portrait conveys the sitter's social standing and self-confidence, in the way of pictures from the Dossi workshop. It is painted in oil with gold on a large wooden panel, about 97 by 78 centimetres, and was formerly called a portrait of Giuliano de' Medici, a title no longer accepted because the sitter does not resemble him.","i":"The letter tells the viewer who the sitter is: a man of education and business, since words about the sea and trade sit beside references to anatomy and to human beauty, a fashionable study in the Renaissance. The old identification as Giuliano de' Medici has been dropped. The painting was once given to Dosso's brother Battista and is now thought the work of an unidentified follower of Dossi from Friuli, as Roberto Longhi first suggested. The sitter remains unnamed.","c":"Interesting for what the sitter holds more than for how it is painted. The letter gives him a persuasive intelligence, and the broad forms and dark outlines show a later, heavier idiom than Dosso's own, which is why the picture has moved from Dosso to a follower. It lacks Dosso's shimmering colour and poetic strangeness, so it comes across as a solid, assertive portrait rather than an enchanted one, valuable as evidence of how Dossi's style spread."},"nga:214115":{"d":"A shallow wooden container is heaped with bright cherries in many shades of red, set on a polished brown surface in front of a dark yellow-green wall. Some cherries have spilled in front of the container, and their reflections gleam in the polished surface beneath. Their bright green stems crisscross and overlap. The painting is tiny, about 16 by 23 centimetres, and was made as a small cabinet picture for a domestic interior.","i":"Dunning is associated with the Fall River school of still life painters in Massachusetts, who made fruit their speciality in the second half of the nineteenth century, selling small, finished pictures to a growing middle-class market. Fruit stood for abundance and domestic well-being, and a picture like this one, with its fresh cherries and clean light, was meant to brighten a parlour. The date 1866 puts it in the first years after the Civil War.","c":"A neat and attractive miniature: the glossy cherries are individually modelled, the reflections in the polished table are well judged, and the dark yellow-green wall sets off the reds. The very tidiness is its limit; the picture is more an exercise in precision than in feeling, and many cherry pieces of the time look alike. As a small demonstration of how much a painter can get from one fruit, it is skilful and sweet."},"nga:206775":{"d":"Dead birds and a rabbit lie piled on a wooden surface covered with a red cloth. The animals hang head-down over the edge of the table, their bodies heaped atop one another. They are mostly brown and grey, with accents of white, black and green from the birds' plumage. The background is brown, with hints of green and pink at the right and the faint outline of trees or bushes. At about 57 by 76 centimetres, this is a horizontal game piece of a kind often hung in a dining room.","i":"Game pieces were popular in seventeenth-century France as the visible reward of the hunt, a privilege of the nobility, and as a showcase for a painter's skill in suggesting fur and feather. Pierre Dupuis was a French still-life specialist. The red cloth gives the hanging animals a rich ground, and the word trophies in the title gives the game the air of a conquest.","c":"A modest, workmanlike still life. The head-down hang of the animals draws the eye along the table edge, and the red cloth gives colour to a mostly brown and grey scheme. But the composition is simple, the animals are heaped without any strong design, and the dark background gives little air. Beside the grander game pieces of Desportes or the Flemish masters it is a quieter picture, valued as a representative of the genre."},"nga:1209":{"d":"Shown from the waist up in fine costume, a man looks out from a deeply shadowed background, his body angled to the right while his dark eyes cut back at the viewer under raised brows. Strong light from the left makes his skin glow and picks out a bulbous nose, a wispy golden-brown goatee and soft cheeks. Wiry hair curls from beneath a patterned skullcap topped by a soft brown beret, and a pearl hangs from his ear. He wears a cream tunic, a dark vest and slashed purple sleeves; a red cloth drapes his arm and his hand rests on a gold spiral shaft.","i":"The signature reads Rembrandt f. 1650, but the museum now catalogues the picture as the work of an unknown Dutch painter of the seventeenth century, and a related data source calls it a workshop painting. Reattributions of this kind are common for Rembrandt: his pupils and assistants made portraits in his manner, and some bore his name. The costume of beret, pearl and slashed sleeves is the exotic, old-fashioned dress he often wore in the self-portraits of his Amsterdam years.","c":"The theatrical costume and sideways glance are convincingly Rembrandtesque, and the light on the face is bold. But the picture seems to take the outward signs, beret, pearl and shadow, without quite capturing the searching self-scrutiny that distinguishes his own self-portraits. It is therefore a useful lesson in how his manner was imitated. As a period image of the famous painter, it remains handsome and engaging."},"nga:81":{"d":"A young girl leans over a low wooden partition, her elbows hooked over it and a long-handled broom held to her chest, and gazes towards the viewer. Her red hair is pulled back from a round face with large hazel eyes, thin brows and a slightly open coral-red mouth. She wears a loose ivory shirt under a garment with pumpkin-orange straps. An overturned wooden pail lies beside the broom, a dark semicircular form in front resembles the mouth of a well, and soft light from the upper left falls on the girl and the caramel-brown wall behind her.","i":"A girl at her housework, caught for a moment looking out, belongs to Dutch genre painting, which showed servants and children as honest, humble figures. The signature reads Rembrandt f. 1651, but the museum catalogues the picture as Dutch seventeenth-century work, and its date, probably begun in the late 1640s and completed in 1651, points to a complicated history involving more than one hand. It has been linked with Rembrandt's workshop and with his former pupil Carel Fabritius.","c":"Whoever painted it, the picture has a freshness that explains why it was long counted a Rembrandt: the girl's frank, slightly puzzled gaze, the warm light and the plain props give her real presence, and the unglamorous subject is treated with dignity. It is uneven, however, and a blocky brown object at the left is hard to make out. It lacks the unifying depth of Rembrandt's late work, but remains an appealing, mysterious picture from his world."},"nga:1200":{"d":"The lifeless body of Christ, bearded, his head and body slumped, eyes closed and mouth open, is lowered from the cross to the people at its foot. Two men on ladders behind the cross reach over the crossbar, one holding his wrist, and two more on ladders either side steady his torso and hold a torch that lights the group. An old man in a scarlet tunic cradles Christ's hips and legs; to the right a pale older woman, eyes closed and arms limp, is caught by those around her as a strong light falls on her face. The landscape beyond is in shadow, with rust-red streaks on the horizon.","i":"The Descent from the Cross shows the moment after the Crucifixion when Christ's body is taken down by Joseph of Arimathea, Nicodemus and helpers. The fainting woman is usually identified as the Virgin Mary, overcome by grief. Rembrandt treated the subject repeatedly in paintings and etchings, including a nocturnal version lit by a torch, and workshop painters took up his ideas. The museum records this canvas as Dutch seventeenth-century work of about 1650 to 1652, not as Rembrandt's own.","c":"A dramatic nocturne: the torch concentrates light on the body and the swooning woman while the crowd falls into shadow, and the diagonal of the lowered body gives the composition a downward sweep. The effect, however, rests on lighting more than on individual expression, and the large cast risks becoming a pile of figures. A strong example of how Rembrandt's chiaroscuro dramas were adapted by his followers."},"nga:1205":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a gaunt man in a large, dark brown hat almost fills this vertical panel. His chest faces the viewer as he leans over his folded hands, but his head turns to the left and he looks in that direction, his deep-set hazel eyes rimmed in pink beneath thick brows, his mouth slightly open. His brown beard is trimmed straight across. A soft, oversized beret covers most of his head; a brocaded robe of rust red and gold lies over a mustard-yellow garment and a pleated ivory undergarment, and a thick gold chain hangs across his chest against a mottled honey-and-tawny ground.","i":"The title Philosopher is a convention: such pictures show a type, a scholar or sage in picturesque costume, rather than a named person, and the Dutch called them tronies. Rembrandt and his circle painted many bearded, meditative figures in exotic dress, and pupils such as Willem Drost took up the type. The museum lists the painting as Dutch seventeenth-century, formerly given to Rembrandt; one data source records Rembrandt, Drost and an anonymous artist among the names connected with it.","c":"A convincing study in thoughtfulness: the leaning figure, the shadowed eyes and the glow of gold chain and rust brocade make a rich, atmospheric head, and the folded hands give the old man a contemplative stillness. It is also a composed type, with its meaning supplied by costume and title more than by a particular mind, and it lacks the depth that Rembrandt himself brought to such faces. A fine example of the Rembrandtesque tronie, whoever painted it."},"nga:34146":{"d":"An oil portrait on canvas, about 55 by 42 centimetres, shows a man whose name is not recorded. The museum's listing gives no description beyond the sitter and the type, so his dress, pose and background cannot be described with confidence. The date of about 1655 places it in the middle of the Dutch Golden Age, when Amsterdam, Haarlem, Leiden and other cities supported many portrait painters, and simple half-length likenesses against a dark ground were a common format for private citizens.","i":"Portraits of this kind were commissioned by merchants, magistrates, ministers and craftsmen to record their appearance for their families, often as a pendant to a portrait of a wife. Without an inscription or other clue recorded here, the sitter's identity, profession and the reason for the commission are unknown. In the 1650s the fashion for sober black dress and white collars, and for a looser, more painterly touch after Rembrandt and Hals, shaped how such sitters wished to appear.","c":"With no close detail to go on, the fair verdict is on what such pictures usually offer: a plain, honest likeness, competent in construction and in the handling of face and clothes, valued for what it shows of the Dutch middle class rather than for any special invention. The anonymity of both painter and sitter keeps it modest. It is best seen as part of the broad, high-quality ordinary production of the Dutch Golden Age."},"nga:86":{"d":"A woman sits in a pool of light in a heavy armchair beside a bed, in a rose-pink, fur-trimmed robe over a white chemise, with pearls at her throat and gold bracelets. Her mouth is open and her brow furrowed as she points to the bed with one hand and holds up her neckline with the other, looking at a shadowy bearded man in a turban wrapped with gold chain who stoops over her chair. On the far side of the bed a younger man in an olive-green tunic and scarlet sash, keys at his waist, stands calmly with clasped hands, a faint light behind him like a halo.","i":"The story is from Genesis 39. Joseph, a slave who has risen to run Potiphar's household in Egypt, refuses the advances of Potiphar's wife; she keeps a piece of his cloak and accuses him falsely. Here she presents her complaint to her husband, pointing at the bed, while Joseph stands apart, composed, a faint light behind him marking his innocence. The signature reads Rembrandt f. 1655, but the picture is catalogued as Dutch seventeenth-century work, one data source giving Rembrandt or studio.","c":"A strong piece of storytelling, whoever painted it: the pool of light on the accuser and the soft, haloed calm of Joseph make a clear moral contrast, and the shadowed husband adds menace. The weak point is the drama itself, which is more theatrical than felt, and the accuser's gestures verge on pantomime. Whether by Rembrandt's own hand or a skilled follower, it is a compelling picture of falsehood confronting integrity."},"nga:1197":{"d":"The head of an elderly man with a long beard and short brown hair fills this small vertical panel, about 25 by 20 centimetres, the face turned slightly to the right and the amber eyes gazing in that direction. Thick strokes of taupe, smoke grey, harvest gold and black form the chest-length beard; patches of peach, butterscotch and rust red build the face; wide squiggles of brown suggest curls under a coffee-brown cap. The handling is loose enough to blur the features, the shoulders are only suggested, and light from the left leaves the far side of the face in shadow against an earth-brown ground.","i":"The title identifies the old man as Saint Matthew, the evangelist, but the description records no attributes, such as the angel who usually accompanies him, and the picture is also described as a tronie, a character head of a picturesque type. Rembrandt and his circle painted many such heads of old men, sometimes using them as saints or apostles. The museum catalogues the panel as Dutch seventeenth-century work, probably of the early 1660s, with Rembrandt among the names connected with it.","c":"Freely handled and arresting at close range: the broad strokes of beard and face carry a sense of age and gravity with remarkably little means, and the shadow across half the face gives the head a brooding presence. The same looseness makes the features vague, and the identification as Matthew is a label more than something the picture proves. Whoever painted it understood Rembrandt's late way of building a face from a few thick touches."},"nga:1203":{"d":"An elderly woman, shown from the chest up on a tiny panel about 21 by 18 centimetres, looks down and to the right against a shadowy brown ground. Her shoulders square towards the viewer, her eyes are heavy-lidded and sunken, and her face is deeply lined between the brows, beside the nose and at the corners of her mouth; her cheeks sag in jowls on either side of a fleshy chin, and her thin lips are set in a line. A cream cloth wraps her forehead beneath an earth-brown, voluminous hood, with a cloak fastened at her throat over a steel-grey garment. Brushstrokes are visible throughout.","i":"Studies of old people, with their wrinkles and weariness, were a speciality of Rembrandt and the artists around him, who treated age as a subject worthy of close, unflinching attention. Such small heads may have been exercises, sketches for larger works, or pictures made for the market in their own right. The panel bears a Rembrandt signature, but the museum catalogues it as Dutch seventeenth-century work, with Rembrandt among the names recorded in connection with it.","c":"An unflinching and compassionate study: the loose strokes of the face build the weight of age without prettifying it, and the downcast gaze gives the old woman an inward, tired dignity. Its small size and visible brushwork make it a deft exercise in Rembrandt's late manner, but the signature does not settle who painted it, and it seems slightly less searching than the best of his own heads."},"nga:205977":{"d":"At first glance a piece of cream paper, crinkled and creased, has been stuck to a wooden board with a red wax seal bearing a shield. The printed image shows an older bearded man in black and grey, from the chest up, angled to the right but looking out, in a wide soft hat and a heavy coat, possibly fur, his hands on a cane and a cross hanging from a long chain at his neck. The print is signed and dated F. Bol 1642. The board is pale tan with a dark knot. Only on close inspection does the whole prove to be a single painting.","i":"Trompe l'oeil, French for deceive the eye, was a Dutch and Flemish speciality, and pictures of prints, papers and letters pinned to boards were a favourite way for painters to prove their skill, impress collectors and play with the line between art and reality. This one imitates an etching of 1642 by Ferdinand Bol, a pupil of Rembrandt, so it also pays tribute to the print as a collectable art. The painter is not identified; the museum dates the panel to about 1675.","c":"A virtuoso joke. The crumpled paper, the waxy seal and the etched lines are so faithfully imitated that the picture works as a test of the eye, and part of the fun is that the subject is itself a print from Rembrandt's circle. Beyond the illusion there is little emotion, and the composition is simple, as the form demands. A clever, witty piece that succeeds at what it sets out to do: fool the eye for a moment."},"met:436248":{"d":"A young woman is shown in an outdoor setting in this portrait on wood, about 66 by 50 centimetres, dated 1636. The museum's record names only the sitter's type, a woman, and the setting, a landscape, so her pose, costume and surroundings cannot be described with confidence. Placing a sitter in a landscape or garden was a fashion in Dutch portraiture of the 1630s, used for formal portraits, betrothal pictures and portraits of children, and gave the artist room to show elegant dress against a backdrop of trees and sky.","i":"Landscape settings in Dutch portraits could suggest the family's country estate, or the pleasures of love and youth in a garden, and in some cases they marked a betrothal or marriage. The sitter's name and the commission are not recorded, and the painter is known only as a Dutch artist whose picture is dated 1636. Her dress, if the picture follows convention, would have declared her family's rank and prosperity.","c":"Anonymous Dutch portraits of the 1630s range from the very good to the merely careful, and without the details to judge, a fair assessment is that this belongs to the middle range: a competent, polished likeness set in a fashionable device, valued more as a document of costume and taste than as the statement of a strong artistic personality. A date and a woman's face are the facts it offers; the rest is context."},"nga:41609":{"d":"From a high vantage point, a group of six people with pale skin and long yellow hair stands under the dark foliage of large trees in a green landscape. Their flowing garments are yellow, white and blue. To the right the trees open onto a wide, pale distance, with rolling hills and, near the upper right corner, a faint structure like a shining white castle. The sky is full of swirling clouds in gray, peach and yellow. The oil on canvas measures about 64 by 84 centimeters.","i":"The painter is known only as an Emilian of the sixteenth century, meaning someone from the region around Parma, Modena, Bologna and Ferrara, where courts cherished chivalric poetry such as Ariosto's Orlando Furioso. The picture seems to have a literary or theatrical subject, but the episode is not identified. Works of this kind, with small figures in an imaginary, romantic landscape, were made for private pleasure and evoked a world of enchantment, forests and distant castles.","c":"The attraction is atmosphere. A dark, rustling foreground opens to a bright far distance, and the swirling clouds and the glimmering castle give the scene a dreamlike quality typical of late Mannerist taste. The figures are small and generalized, and without a clear story the picture remains a mood piece. It is more evocative than precise, and its anonymity is part of its mystery."},"cma:150697":{"d":"Travelers pause beside a lake in this Dutch landscape of 1625, painted in oil on wood by Esaias van de Velde. The still water is subordinate to the other elements, and its calm reinforces the serenity of the setting, as do the figures and the ruins, which exist in harmony with the natural world. The picture shows an ordinary corner of the Netherlands rather than an idealized, imaginary scene.","i":"Before the early 1600s, Dutch landscapes mostly served as decorative backdrops for religious or mythological stories. In Haarlem in the 1610s artists like van de Velde began to paint the humble, everyday land and people of the Netherlands in convincing detail. The picture was built in the studio from sketches and memory, and it would have satisfied an urban buyer's nostalgia for the humble countryside as Dutch society urbanized rapidly after independence from Spain.","c":"Esaias van de Velde was one of the founders of naturalistic Dutch landscape, and this picture shows its strengths: modest figures, quiet light and a feeling of calm. The composition is not dramatic, and that is its point. Compared with the grander landscapes of Ruisdael a generation later, the scale is humble, but it captures the essence of the new approach: observation without fuss."},"nga:34072":{"d":"A man's upper body is shown facing slightly to the left, with his head turned toward the viewer, in a formal pose. He has light skin, rosy cheeks and a calm expression, and his curled hair is tied back from the face in the style of the eighteenth century. He wears a dark coat with visible buttons and a white cravat, slightly ruffled and tucked into the coat. The background is plain, with subtle gradations of brown and dark green. The canvas measures about 76 by 64 centimeters and dates from around 1770.","i":"The sitter is unknown, and the artist is recorded only as a European painter of the eighteenth century, so the picture is a portrait without a name or a nationality. The dark coat, plain cravat and tied-back hair belong to the sober dress of professional and middle-class men in the 1770s, and the lack of props suggests a sitter wanting a straightforward record of his looks rather than a display of rank.","c":"A competent, anonymous portrait: the face is modeled with a calm, rosy naturalness, and the plain background lets the head do the work. Nothing about it is daring, and the formula is that of countless sound likenesses of the age. Its lack of a name keeps attention on technique, and it shows that a decent portraitist of the period could give a sitter dignity without ostentation."},"met:436280":{"d":"A tempera on wood panel with a gold ground, about 42.5 by 31.8 centimeters, shows the head of Christ. The artist is anonymous and the date is given only as before 1500. The gold ground is a hallmark of medieval and early Renaissance panel painting, where it represented the light of heaven rather than a place. The image isolates the face for the viewer's contemplation, without narrative or setting.","i":"Images of Christ's face drew on a venerable tradition of true likenesses, such as the veil of Saint Veronica, believed to carry the imprint of his features. By the 1400s such portraits, shown close up and meeting the viewer's eye, were used for private devotion and for meditation on the Passion. The faith placed in these images explains why so many were made and why the types remained so stable.","c":"The unknown painter works within a fixed pattern, and the gold ground's flat glow gives the head an immediate, hypnotic presence. Anonymous pieces of this kind are hard to judge for originality, since the aim was fidelity to a type. As an image of calm authority it works; as an individual work of art, its main interest lies in craft and survival."},"nga:256":{"d":"Mary clasps her hands in prayer, with a slight smile, wearing a dark green shawl edged in gold over richly patterned red and gold robes, with a large gold halo. Before her at the bottom right a sleeping Christ Child with a golden halo, wrapped in sheer cloth, wears a red necklace and bracelets. Two haloed children in green and red pray at the bottom left, and another child with red wings stands at her shoulder. Behind them are a river, pink mountains, green hills and a castle under a cloudy sky.","i":"The type is the Madonna adoring the Child, in which Mary kneels or sits before her sleeping son, and the sleep foreshadows his death. The red beads on the Child are probably coral, which Italian Renaissance art linked to protection from harm and to Christ's blood. The companions are probably angels, and the landscape recalls the fantastic rocky backgrounds of Ferrarese painting, in the sharp, metallic manner of painters like Cosmè Tura.","c":"The anonymous Ferrarese painter combines a tender idea with a hard, brilliant finish: the faces are pale and fine, the patterns on the robes are exquisite, and the pink mountains are a strange, lovely touch. The figures are a little stiff and the sentiment is restrained, but the picture has a jewel-like clarity that is characteristic of the court of Ferrara in the fifteenth century."},"cma:163456":{"d":"A late-afternoon view of Boston Harbor, in oil on canvas of about 43 by 69 centimeters, shows ships and the distant city across calm water. Sunlight reflects diffusely off the water, and the rigging of the ships is painstakingly accurate. At the far left a steamboat appears, of the kind that ferried passengers between Boston and Gloucester. The painter, Fitz Henry Lane, was raised in Gloucester, Massachusetts, a seafaring town.","i":"Boston Harbor in the 1840s was one of the busiest in America, crowded with sailing ships, and the steamboat at the edge is a sign of technological change. Lane, who worked as a lithographer in Boston before returning to Gloucester, knew the harbor well. His middle name was long given as Hugh in error, since he was born Nathaniel Rogers Lane and took the name Fitz Henry in 1832.","c":"Lane's special gift is the stillness of light on water. In this view the glow of late afternoon pervades the harbor, and the exactness of the ships' rigging shows his lithographer's discipline. There is little drama, and that is the point: the picture trusts calm and accuracy. It is an early step toward the quiet, luminous canvases of his later years in Gloucester."},"nga:51096":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, a pale-skinned man with a Van Dyck beard stands with one hand on his hip in this vertical portrait. His body faces right, almost in profile, but he turns his head to look at the viewer from the corners of his brown eyes under lifted black brows. His mustache flares upward, his black hair is wavy, and a wide-brimmed hat sits angled on the back of his head. A round lace collar rests on a slashed black jacket. Black drapery is pulled back at the right to reveal a sunset sky.","i":"The hand on the hip, the tilted hat and the sideways glance project swagger, a pose familiar from the portraits of the Antwerp master Anthony van Dyck, whose neat beard gave its name to the fashion. The slashed jacket and lace collar show that the sitter was a gentleman of means. The sunset beyond the drape is a conventional touch that lent portraits of the 1630s a note of drama.","c":"The artist, an anonymous Flemish painter, handles the lace and the slashes of the jacket with obvious skill, and the face has a vivid, quizzical alertness. The pose is a formula, and the black clothing and drapery merge into heavy darkness. As a competent echo of Van Dyck's elegance it is attractive, but it lacks the master's effortless grace."},"met:436306":{"d":"A woman is the sole subject of this portrait, painted in oil on a copper plate around 1625 to 1630 by an unknown Flemish painter. The title does not name her. Copper, a rigid, perfectly smooth support, was used for small, precious pictures in the Flemish tradition, and it lets the painter work in very fine detail and gives colors an enamel-like depth and brilliance.","i":"Portraits on copper were small, costly objects, treasured by wealthy owners and often kept in cabinets rather than hung on walls. They recorded a sitter's likeness with great fineness, and they could be given as gifts or kept as mementos. Flemish painters in the circle of Rubens used copper widely, and a portrait of this kind fits into the culture of collecting in seventeenth-century Antwerp.","c":"The smooth copper surface gives the likeness a polish and clarity that canvas cannot match, and the strengths of such a portrait are fine detail and a glowing flesh tone. The painter is anonymous, which makes judgment difficult, but the format and technique are of high quality. It is a small, finished image whose chief pleasure is craftsmanship."},"met:436307":{"d":"A harbor with boats, men and a tower is painted on vellum, the fine calfskin parchment, around 1650 by an unknown Flemish painter. The surface is smooth and pale, which suits small, delicate work. The scene is a port, one of the subjects that fascinated Flemish and Dutch artists in an age of maritime trade, and the picture is presumably a small cabinet piece rather than a large decorative work.","i":"Seventeenth-century collectors prized small pictures on vellum and copper, which they kept in cabinets of curiosities or in albums. Harbor scenes reflected the prosperity of the Low Countries, whose ports linked Europe with the Baltic, the Mediterranean and the Indies. The tower may be a landmark of a particular port, but the picture does not identify it.","c":"Vellum gives a luminous, delicate surface that favors fine drawing and crisp detail over bold brushwork. The painter, anonymous, has a skill for small figures and boats, and the harbor setting is lively. This is a minor but attractive example of the cabinet picture, to be enjoyed for its finesse rather than its grandeur."},"nga:17":{"d":"Shown from the chest up behind a ledge, a pale, clean-shaven young man faces right in strict profile, looking steadily forward. His eye is brown beneath a thin curving brow, his nose is bumped near the bridge, and his lips are closed. Short brown hair shows under a coral-red turban whose ends fall down his back. He wears a dark brown high-collared garment edged in white and a cranberry-red robe, set against a dark teal-blue ground. Gold lettering on the ledge names Matteo Olivieri, son of Messer Giovanni.","i":"Profile portraits were the fashion in Florence in the early 1400s, modeled on the profiles of ancient Roman coins and medals, and the ledge with its inscription also recalls classical monuments. The turban-like cloth hat was a common male fashion of the time. The question mark in the title records doubt about the identification of the sitter, even though an inscription names him.","c":"The strict profile gives the portrait a hard, monumental clarity, and the contour of the face is drawn with authority. The red turban and robe against the teal ground are a bold combination. Because the profile format reveals little of character, the likeness is more emblem than psychology, but its stylized elegance is the point and the effect is memorable."},"nga:16":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a light-skinned man in a burgundy-red turban faces left in profile against a dark background. He looks off to the left, and his eye is light brown beneath a faint, possibly plucked eyebrow. His nose is straight, his full peach lips are closed, and his brown hair is tucked under the turban, with a piece of fabric hanging down the back of his neck. He wears an olive-brown shirt with a white band at the neck and a black jacket. A brown band at the bottom could be a ledge. Cracks cross the painting, especially in the green ground.","i":"The format is the Florentine profile portrait, which borrowed its sharp outline from ancient coins and medals, and the turban was a fashionable headdress for young men in the first half of the fifteenth century. The sitter is unknown, and the artist is only recorded as a Florentine of the period. Portraits like this were commissioned for betrothals, family records or proud display of a young man's good looks and standing.","c":"The pure profile imposes a calm, almost iconic elegance, and the burgundy turban against the dark green ground is striking. The face is smooth and a little blank, so character depends on contour and costume rather than expression. The cracks and wear are a reminder of the age of the panel. As a clear example of the type it is valuable, though its appeal is mainly stylistic."},"nga:1190":{"d":"A young woman with fair skin and orange-brown hair under a black net looks slightly below the viewer, her body angled to the left. She wears a green dress with puffed sleeves and sheer details around a wide neckline, a thin gold chain, a smaller black bead necklace, and a red flower behind her ear. Her nose is straight, her brows arched, her cheeks rosy and her lips closed. A small, fluffy gray dog sits in her lap against a dark ground. The oil on panel measures about 56 by 45 centimeters.","i":"A lap dog in a woman's portrait is usually a symbol of fidelity and companionship, and the flower behind the ear is a common sign of love or betrothal, so the picture may celebrate a young woman about to marry or recently married. The black net on her hair and the fashionable gown show she belongs to a Florentine family of means. The painter and the sitter are both unknown.","c":"The picture charms with its fresh, unidealized face and its details of chain, flower and dog, and the green dress against the dark ground is a pleasing harmony. The anonymous painter follows the clear, polished manner of mid-century Florentine portraiture. If it lacks the penetrating brilliance of Bronzino, it is a fine example of the refined, cool style of Florentine courtly portraits."},"nga:220":{"d":"A figure is seated with the body turned slightly left and the head turned toward the viewer. The face is fair, with high cheekbones, a long nose and thin lips, and short light brown hair is mostly covered by a dark hat with a white plume. The sitter wears a dark garment with puffed sleeves, a white collar and a white strip on the chest. The left hand rests on the knee, the right arm on a wooden surface holding a white cloth, and a small black book lies at the lower right. The wall behind is dark gray with paneling.","i":"The title names the sitter as Ugolino Martelli, a Florentine scholar from a prominent family; the painter Bronzino made a celebrated portrait of the same man, now in Berlin, showing him as a young humanist with a book. The plume, the book and the dark costume belong to the image of the cultivated gentleman of the mid-sixteenth century, and the cloth in his hand adds an informal, lived-in touch.","c":"The sober palette and the controlled pose reflect the cool, aristocratic ideals of Florentine portraiture under the Medici dukes, and the plume gives the dark composition a bright accent. The painter, unknown, is a skilled follower of the tradition, but without the icy brilliance of Bronzino. It is an elegant, dignified likeness, a little reserved, which is in character with the style."},"nga:46156":{"d":"A pale man sits on a grassy outcrop in this square panel of about 127 by 120 centimeters, holding an open book so that the pages face the viewer. His body is angled right and his head is in profile, with heavy brows, a hooked nose and a jutting chin, and he wears a red cap wreathed in laurel and a long cranberry-red robe with navy sleeves. Verses in three-line stanzas are legible on the pages. His raised hand hovers over a miniature skyline with a dome and two towers, and behind him a conical mountain rises beyond blue water.","i":"The sitter is Dante Alighieri, shown as poet laureate, with the book of his Divine Comedy. The miniature skyline is Florence, the city that exiled him, and the terraced mountain is Purgatory, with flames on one level and a tiny boat recalling the opening of the Purgatorio. The composition echoes an earlier image of Dante in Florence Cathedral, and it reflects the Florentine pride in the poet at the end of the sixteenth century.","c":"The picture is a learned diagram as much as a portrait, with every detail pointing to the poem, and the profile face with its hooked nose is based on a long tradition of Dante images. The mountain is imaginative and slightly comic in its layered form. It is a fascinating document of Dante's reception, though its drawing is dry and its poetry lies in the idea rather than the paint."},"met:437033":{"d":"The Virgin nurses the Christ Child in this oil on wood panel of about 56 by 34 centimeters, painted around 1480 to 1490. The subject, known in art history as the nursing Madonna, shows Mary offering her breast to her son. The painter, a Netherlandish follower of Rogier van der Weyden working in the late fifteenth century, is associated with the Master of the Saint Ursula Legend group. The tall, narrow panel suits a single, intimate devotional figure.","i":"The nursing Virgin, in Latin Maria lactans, emphasized the human reality of Christ's birth and the Virgin's role as nourisher and merciful intercessor. Late medieval writers spoke of the milk of mercy, and the image invited the viewer to feel protected by her love. Rogier van der Weyden's compositions of the Virgin were widely imitated across the Netherlands, so followers like this painter could draw on a trusted repertoire of types.","c":"Followers of Rogier often reduced the master's tension to a calmer, sweeter prettiness, and this panel probably does the same: the idealized faces, careful drapery and gentle sentiment are appealing but not searching. The oil technique allows a delicate finish. It is a competent, devotional image whose charm is in its tenderness rather than its originality."},"nga:214116":{"d":"A white dish with a gold rim sits on a brown surface in this very small still life of 1870, about 20 by 25 centimeters. In the bowl are three large purple plums surrounded by smaller gooseberries in pale yellow and pink, some tinged with green, and a small leafy vine rests on top. To the left of the bowl lie four red and yellow strawberries joined to a small stem. The background is brown.","i":"George Forster belonged to the American still-life tradition that began with the Peale family of Philadelphia, in which fruit was painted with close, loving attention to bloom, color and texture. Summer fruits like plums, gooseberries and strawberries were pleasures of the table, and such pictures decorated dining rooms and parlors. The small size suggests a picture for a private owner and for close viewing.","c":"The picture's strength is its delicacy: the translucency of the gooseberries, the bloom on the plums and the gold rim of the dish are rendered patiently, and the brown ground sets the fruit forward. The composition is plain and the subject modest, with none of the lavish abundance of the more famous American fruit pieces. Its quiet honesty is its charm."},"met:437007":{"d":"Christ hangs on the cross against a gold ground in this small panel of about 64 by 48 centimeters, painted around 1420 to 1423. The Virgin Mary and Mary Magdalene mourn near the cross, soldiers and horses crowd the scene, angels hover, and skulls recall Golgotha, the place of the skull. Gold ground and tempera are the traditional materials of Gothic panel painting, and the picture is an early work by Guido di Pietro, the Dominican friar later known as Fra Angelico.","i":"In the Gospels, the crowd at the Crucifixion includes soldiers, passersby and the women who followed Jesus, among them his mother and Mary Magdalene. Golgotha means place of the skull, and a medieval legend held that Adam's skull lay beneath the cross, so that Christ's blood redeemed the first man. Angels who gather the blood or mourn above are a feature of many Italian Crucifixions of the period, which turned the narrative into a devotional meditation.","c":"An early work from the years when Angelico was entering the Dominican order, the picture still belongs to the world of the Gothic: gold ground, a crowd arranged in tiers, clear contour and jewel-like color. The grief is restrained rather than raw. It lacks the luminous space of his later Florentine paintings, but its clarity and delicacy already point to his gentle genius."},"met:435576":{"d":"A tiny panel, only about 16 centimeters square, shows a bishop saint with a miter and a crozier against a gold ground, painted in tempera around 1425. The title names him Saint Alexander, but several bishops of that name were venerated, so the saint's exact identity is open. At this size the panel is probably a fragment of a larger work, such as the predella or the frame of an altarpiece, where small saints filled the borders.","i":"Bishops in medieval painting are identified by their miters, croziers and rich vestments, which stand for their office as shepherds of the faithful. The saint's identity would have been obvious to a contemporary viewer from his place in the altarpiece or perhaps from an inscription. Such small saints were part of the company of holy intercessors that surrounded the central image, and their presence gave a church's patrons a roster of heavenly protectors.","c":"Even at this tiny scale Angelico's hand is recognizable by its refinement: the clean contour, the delicate modeling and the gold used as light. A fragment can only suggest the whole, and the isolation of one bishop makes it hard to judge composition. As a small devotional jewel it is appealing, and as evidence of his craftsmanship it is instructive."},"met:435577":{"d":"A horizontal panel of about 40 by 54 centimeters shows the Crucifixion against a gold ground, with Mary Magdalene and Saint Francis among the figures at the foot of the cross. The paint has been transferred from its original panel to canvas and then laid down on wood, so the surface is a product of conservation as much as of the painter's hand. The date is given as possibly about 1440, in Fra Angelico's mature Florentine years.","i":"Saint Francis at the foot of the cross may mean that the picture was made for a Franciscan setting or patron, since Francis was said to have received Christ's wounds, the stigmata, through his own meditation on the Passion. Mary Magdalene, kneeling or embracing the cross, is the model of penitent love. Such small Crucifixions were used for private prayer, drawing the viewer into the scene alongside the saints.","c":"The transfer to canvas has softened the surface, and the picture's attribution is cautious, so judgment must be careful. Even so, the clear arrangement of figures and the humane gravity of the mourners are in keeping with Angelico's mature manner. The Franciscan saint adds an unusual note. It is a quiet, devout work whose appeal is its reticence."},"cma:147838":{"d":"The Holy Family make their way home from exile in Egypt in this upright canvas, about 118 by 84 cm. The colors are pale and the poses almost balletic, with long, slender forms, yet the lighting is hard: figures emerge from deep shadow into sharply lit passages, a staging that descends from Caravaggio, who had died more than a century before. Conti trained in Rome but made his career in Florence, and the picture dates from 1735.","i":"The scene closes a story told in the Gospel of Matthew. Warned that Herod meant to kill the newborn king of the Jews, the family fled to Egypt and, after Herod's death, returned to settle in Nazareth. Matthew presents the episode as fulfilling prophecy, and the journey has long been read as an echo of Israel's Exodus, with Christ as a new Moses. Eighteenth-century viewers probably also saw a model of humble family life under Joseph's protection.","c":"Conti works in the late Florentine Baroque, and the result has an elegance close to affectation: the stretched figures and dance-like poses make the long road home look rather easy. What gives the picture bite is the lighting, whose abrupt passages of dark and light lend the figures a theatrical presence that a merely pretty painter would have avoided. It is refined, skilled and somewhat derivative, a school living on inherited effects."},"cma:124090":{"d":"John the Baptist is carried to his father, Zacharias, in this tall, narrow panel, about 76 by 33 cm, painted in tempera and oil with gold. It formed part of a series on the Baptist's life, with other panels now in New York and Liverpool. Granacci used gold to paint fine details of embroidery and individual strands of hair. Scholars disagree about who painted this panel, and as many as five artists may have worked on the series.","i":"According to the Gospel of Luke, the priest Zacharias was struck dumb when he doubted an angel's announcement that his aged wife Elizabeth would bear a son. When the child was born he asked for a writing tablet and wrote that the boy's name was John, and his speech returned. A scene bringing the infant to his father probably belongs to that moment. Because the Baptist is Florence's patron saint, his story was a natural subject for a Florentine room.","c":"The gold work is the pleasure here: embroidery and single hairs picked out with a goldsmith's patience, a reminder that Florentine painting around 1510 still prized the craft of surface. The Michelangelo question, a possible share in the drapery of the figure in white, is tantalizing but unprovable. The tall, narrow format limits any ambition in composition, and the panel reads as a polished decorative piece rather than a daring one."},"met:906167":{"d":"A woman identified as Mughal is the subject of this portrait, painted in oil on canvas in 1787 and measuring about 100 by 72 cm. Francesco Renaldi was a British portrait painter of Italian family, trained in the London tradition. The picture belongs to the world of British contact with India in the 1780s, when painters from Britain sought patrons there and met sitters far outside the usual circle of the English portrait trade.","i":"European portraits of elite South Asian women were rare because many such women lived in seclusion, so a painter's access to a sitter was unusual and the circumstances of the sitting matter. A British painter working in the manner of the Royal Academy could dignify a sitter or reduce her to an exotic type; the word Mughal names a culture, not a person. Without a named sitter, the picture is best read as evidence of cross-cultural encounter in 1780s India.","c":"A portrait of this kind is judged on two counts: the painter's craft within the English portrait tradition, and the dignity extended to a sitter outside it. The first is likely competent. The second is where such pictures usually divide, between individual likeness and costume piece, and a viewer should look for signs of attention to the person. Its rarity makes it important in any case."},"cma:144588":{"d":"Saint Ambrose, the fourth-century bishop of Milan, is the subject of this tall canvas of about 190 by 113 cm, painted around 1796 to 1799. It belongs to a series depicting Ambrose, Jerome, Augustine and Gregory, key figures of early Catholicism. The low viewpoint and large scale suggest that it hung high on the wall of a religious institution, though its original location is unknown. Goya painted it in the years just before he became First Court Painter in 1799.","i":"Ambrose, Jerome, Augustine and Gregory are the four great Doctors of the Latin Church. Ambrose resolved important theological conflicts and worked effectively with advocates of Roman paganism; he also baptized Augustine and stood up to an emperor. In the late 1700s Spain expelled the Jesuits and the Church was transformed, and many reformers turned to early church history for inspiration. The series was probably commissioned by one of these new organizations.","c":"Goya's religious work is often overshadowed by his court portraits and his late nightmares, yet this series shows how he could give weight to official subjects with economy. The low viewpoint and large scale call for monumentality, and his free, direct brushwork keeps the figure from stiffening into emblem. The subject offers little drama, and some of his church commissions are perfunctory, but a Goya saint always has a pulse."},"nga:25":{"d":"A young woman is shown from the chest up in strict profile, facing left, behind a narrow ledge against a black ground. Her skin is smooth and pale, with a high forehead, a sloping nose and closed lips. Her hair is gathered under a gold net, topped by a twisted, ring-like headdress of striped, leaf-patterned fabric held by long gold pins. She wears a peacock-blue gown patterned with gold flowers and stems, a high collar that flares behind her head, a white linen collar and gold bands at the neck and waist. Two strands of gold beads loop across her shoulder, and a small gold pendant sits at her neck.","i":"The sitter is unknown. In 1935 the panel was cataloged as Isotta degli Atti by Pisanello, and it was attributed to Pisanello into the 1960s, but more recent scholarship places it in the Franco-Flemish world of about 1410. The headdress is a balzo, and the hairline above the forehead has been plucked to suit the fashion. Profile was the usual format for portraits at this date, when independent portraits of any kind were rare. The pendant may be two chain links or a letter B; the picture does not settle it. It was shown in the Virtue and Beauty exhibition of 2001–02 on portraits of women.","c":"The portrait works through restraint. The pale face is drawn with a sharp contour against black, and the costume is described with fine linework that suggests the training of a manuscript painter. The face itself, though, is almost blank, with little modeling and no hint of personality, so the portrait tells more about dress and rank than about the woman. The old Pisanello label shows how easily such a picture slips between traditions. What remains is a quiet, precise record of courtly fashion around 1410."},"cma:150839":{"d":"Neptune, the Roman god of water, and his wife Amphitrite ride a chariot pulled by sea horses. Water fowl and flying fish glide in the sky above them, while in the distance a group of nymphs beckon from a grotto. Neptune's trident stands for his power over water and storms, and the fish, shells, coral and pearls carried by his subjects show his wealth. The oil on copper, about 24 by 31 cm, dates from the 1630s.","i":"The triumphal procession of Neptune and Amphitrite has roots in Roman art and was revived by Renaissance and Baroque artists as a celebration of the sea's abundance. In Antwerp, a great port on the Scheldt, such a subject probably carried a flattering echo of the city's own trade, while the tiny scale and the shining treasure suited a collector's cabinet. The myth was enjoyed more for display than for any moral.","c":"Francken specialized in small, crowded mythologies and religious scenes, and copper suits him: its smooth surface lets every shell and wave crest stay sharp. The invention is more playful than profound, and the figures have the elongated, mannered grace of late Antwerp Mannerism. The picture is witty and polished, a collector's delight, and its modest scale is part of the charm."},"cma:162623":{"d":"The ten-year-old Louis XIII, already king of France, is portrayed in a tall canvas of about 159 by 95 cm. The painter inscribed the sitter's age and the year into the composition: 'The holy year 1611 at his age of 10 years.' Frans Pourbus the Younger, a Flemish court portraitist, painted it for the boy's mother, Marie de' Medici, who ruled as regent until he came of age.","i":"Louis had been king since his father Henri IV was assassinated in 1610, and his mother held power as regent. The portrait served diplomacy: Marie de' Medici was arranging marriages for her two children with the Spanish Habsburgs, alliances that helped shape European politics for two centuries, and a portrait of the boy king showed foreign courts a healthy, well-presented heir. The museum notes that pink, now thought feminine, was worn by boys and girls alike in the 1600s.","c":"Pourbus was a polished court portraitist trained in the Flemish tradition of exact description, and the commission called for exactness and dignity more than psychology: costume, regalia and the inscription do the work of majesty. The challenge, to make a child look like a king without losing the child, is the interesting thing about the picture. Protocol wins somewhat over life, but it remains a clear, authoritative image of the boy king."},"nga:41644":{"d":"A kneeling, pregnant Mary fills most of this arched panel, her midnight blue cloak lined with spring green and edged with gold, one hand over her belly. A smaller winged angel behind her holds one of her fingers, while an older, white-bearded Joseph looks over a gold-brocaded red curtain, clutching a crutch and a green book. Two small women in white and scarlet gowns stand or kneel behind Mary, and two crimson angels hold a tall crown in dark blue clouds above, against a gold background.","i":"The pregnant Virgin, known as the Madonna of the Expectation, was a popular late medieval image, and women who hoped for safe childbirth prayed before her. The crown held by angels looks ahead to her coronation as Queen of Heaven, so that the promise of the birth and the promise of glory appear together. Joseph is shown as an old man with a crutch, as was usual, to stress his role as protector and not as father.","c":"The pleasure here lies in jewel-like detail: the gold ground, the striped wings of the angel, the tiny violets in the grass, and the tender, odd gesture of the angel holding Mary's finger. The painter is unknown, and the style, courtly and linear, belongs to a Gothic tradition that was already old by the mid-fifteenth century. The scale hierarchy of the small attendants is archaic, but the painting's gentleness is real."},"nga:41658":{"d":"Two scenes share this vertical panel, divided by a white stone arch resting on a pillar. At left a clean-shaven monk with a gold halo sits in a plain room beneath an open window, an open book on his lap, bracing a gold-tipped staff and raising his left hand. Through the window and at right, one monk holds another, smaller, who floats or stands in white water holding a bucket limply, as the water winds back to green hills, a pink stone building and a white church.","i":"The right-hand scene is evidently the rescue of the boy Placidus by Maurus, told by Pope Gregory the Great in his life of Benedict: the abbot, seated in his cell, saw in spirit that Placidus had fallen into a lake while drawing water, and he sent Maurus, who ran across the water without noticing. The miracle illustrates obedience, the monk's central virtue in Benedict's Rule, and the panel probably belonged to a cycle of the saint's life.","c":"The anonymous painter tells the story with unforced economy: two moments within one space, linked by the open window and the landscape that continues through it. The seated abbot's calm contrasts with the rescuer's urgency. The strengths are clear storytelling and a charming depth of landscape; the weakness is stiffness, since the figures show little of the agitation a rescue implies. It is typical of French painting of about 1480, close to Flemish models but less finished."},"nga:46153":{"d":"Shown from the waist up against a vivid red background, a pale, clean-shaven man looks off to the left, his head tilted down. He has bags under brown eyes, sunken cheeks and soft jowls, a rounded nose and a wide chin, and his pale lips are closed, one corner slightly drawn back. A close-fitting black cap nearly covers his gray hair, and he wears an inky-black cloak over a black robe. His hands are together in prayer in the lower left corner of the panel.","i":"The sitter, labeled only as an ecclesiastic, is shown in plain black with hands joined in prayer, and his sideways gaze suggests that he looked toward something outside the panel. Portraits like this were often one half of a diptych, with the sitter facing a devotional image of the Virgin or Christ on a companion panel, so that he remained present in prayer. Such diptychs were popular among churchmen and the elite in France and the Netherlands in the later fifteenth century.","c":"The painter does not flatter: bags, hollow cheeks and soft jowls are recorded with a candor that gives the face its life, and the vivid red ground sets the dark figure off with force. The tilt of the head and the slight pull of the lip imply an inner state without spelling it out. Anonymous though the artist is, this is among the most direct small portraits of its time, memorable for honesty rather than elegance."},"nga:72072":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, at an angle, his left hand resting on a surface and his right hand on his hip holding a white lace handkerchief. He has sharp features, a short dark beard and mustache, and neatly styled hair beneath a black cap. He is dressed in black with a high collar, with a small area of white shirt at wrist and collar, and a pocket watch rests on the surface beside him. A coat of arms fills the upper right corner of the plain background.","i":"The coat of arms marks the sitter as a member of the Quaratesi, a Florentine family, and Florentine merchants and bankers were prominent in France, especially in Lyon, which may explain a portrait attributed to a French painter. A watch was a new and costly instrument in 1561, a sign of means and modernity, and perhaps also a quiet reminder of passing time. Black, the lace handkerchief and the hand on the hip belong to the vocabulary of fashionable elegance.","c":"This portrait aims at precision rather than psychology: the costume, the watch and the heraldic emblem are described with clarity, and the stance is the confident one of Italian and French court portraits of the 1560s. The painter is competent but anonymous, and the pose has a stiffness that the details do not quite relieve. It succeeds as a record of rank and fashion, and the unusual watch and handkerchief give it character."},"nga:3199":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a pale man in a ruffled collar and ornately decorated doublet looks out from the corners of his eyes, his shoulders and face angled to the left. He has bright blue eyes under low, thin brows, closed coral-pink lips and short brown hair brushed back from a high forehead. His doublet is brown with gold stripes lined with silver zigzags, closed by flower-shaped buttons, and a long gold chain hangs below the edge. The sleeves are ivory slashed with gold embroidery, against a dark brown background.","i":"The slashed sleeves, the gold chain and the figure-eight ruff are marks of rank at the French court of the 1570s, when the Valois kings set the fashion for a refined, expensive display. Small portraits like this, in the tradition of the Clouet family, were exchanged among courtiers and kept in collections as records of friends and rivals. The sitter's name is not known, so he stands for a type: the fashionable nobleman.","c":"The pleasure is in precision: the patterned doublet, the pleated collar and the glint of gold are painted with a minute care in the Clouet tradition. The sideways glance gives the face a watchful quality, though the sitter remains guarded, as court decorum often required. The plain brown ground focuses all attention on costume and face. Among anonymous French court portraits it is a good and highly finished example."},"nga:46154":{"d":"A clean-shaven young man stands full-length in a white jacket and breeches, a black-speckled white fur cape and a brimless black hat with a high crown trimmed with jeweled medallions, pearls and a white feather. He wears a lace-edged ruff pleated in figure eights and rests one hand on the pearled hilt of a sword while holding gloves in the other. The wall behind is green and the floor rosy peach, and gold text in the corner gives his name, his age and the date, 19 March 1572.","i":"The inscription identifies the sitter as the son of Henri II, king of France, and the youngest of the sons of Catherine de' Medici. He later became Duke of Anjou and an unsuccessful suitor of Elizabeth I of England. A full-length portrait of a young prince, dressed entirely in white with pearls and a sword, announced rank and promise; it was a dynastic image that could be sent to foreign courts, perhaps in connection with marriage plans.","c":"The white-on-white costume is a tour de force of description: fur, ruff, lace, braided bands and pearls are distinguished with great care. The painter, anonymous but trained in the court tradition, clearly valued exactness, and the full-length format gives the young prince presence. The space is shallow and the pose stiff, and the face keeps a guarded courtesy. It succeeds as an image of rank, more than as a study of a person."},"nga:34147":{"d":"An unnamed man is shown in an oil on canvas of about 58 by 47 cm, a size suited to a bust or half-length portrait made for the sitter's family or friends. The painter is not identified, and the attribution is simply French, in the middle of the seventeenth century. With both sitter and maker lost, the picture works as a type: a Frenchman of the age of Louis XIII and the young Louis XIV.","i":"Without a name, a portrait still testifies about status through dress, pose and cost. In mid-seventeenth-century France portraits were made in Paris and the provinces to mark an office, a marriage or a friendship, and the sitters were often lawyers, officials and merchants who valued sober dignity. The French tradition of plain, grave portraiture, exemplified by Philippe de Champaigne, gave such likenesses an air of moral seriousness.","c":"An anonymous portrait invites a modest judgment: it is competent and sober, and its interest lies in the character of the sitter and the plainness of the French manner of the time. Without a documented maker the work cannot be placed among an artist's achievements, and it lacks the brilliance of the best French portraitists. Its merit is honest description of a face, which is what most sitters of the period wanted."},"nga:34082":{"d":"An unnamed man is the subject of this oil on canvas of about 72 by 58 cm, dated to the late seventeenth century. The size suggests a half-length or a bust with generous space, and the maker is not identified beyond being French. The picture belongs to the age of Louis XIV, when portraiture was a major business in Paris and the sitters expected their rank to be made visible.","i":"In the later years of Louis XIV the portrait grew more ceremonious, with portraitists such as Rigaud and Largillière setting a model of grand display, in which dress and pose announced office and wealth. Even a modest portrait of this date reflects that expectation: a man shown to advantage, with dignity. The sitter's name is lost, but the conventions identify him as a member of the educated, propertied class, the sort who commissioned likenesses of this kind.","c":"Without a known maker, the portrait must be judged on competence, and it is competent: orderly, dignified and unadventurous. The French late seventeenth century could produce portraits of real brilliance, and this is not one of them, but it keeps the virtues of the type: a clear, steady presence and an unfussy likeness. It is best valued as an example of the ordinary quality of its time rather than its peak."},"nga:55781":{"d":"A large white vase filled with a dense assortment of vibrant flowers stands on a wooden table, with some flowers spilling over its edge. The bouquet includes deep red, bright pink, white and dark blue flowers, with a few yellow ones at the back, and the leaves and stems are so dark a green that they appear almost black. The vase is carved with nude figures and geometric patterns toward the base. The background is dark brown, and the flowers cast dark shadows on the vase and table.","i":"In late seventeenth-century France, flower painting served chiefly as decoration, and painters such as Jean-Baptiste Monnoyer supplied rich floral pieces to the palaces of Louis XIV. The classical vase, with its carved nude figures, ties the bouquet to antiquity and signals culture and expense. Flowers also carried an older meaning, the brevity of beauty, though in French decorative painting that note is quieter than in Dutch work.","c":"The strengths are richness and drama: saturated flowers set against near-black foliage and a dark ground, with spilling blooms that add a touch of naturalism. The carved vase gives the picture classical dignity. The weaknesses are the formula of profusion and a lack of botanical exactness compared with Dutch flower painters. As decoration for a grand room it is effective, and as a still life it is a handsome example of Louis XIV taste."},"nga:32695":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the subject of this portrait, oil on canvas, about 69 by 59 cm, dated about 1711. The size suggests a half-length or bust portrait, and the maker is known only as French. The date falls in the last years of Louis XIV's reign, when portraits of women balanced the dignity of rank with a new taste for charm and naturalness, and the sitter appears as a member of the elite of that society.","i":"In the early eighteenth century portraits of women began to loosen the formal grandeur of the Sun King's court, favoring softer color, ease of pose and a hint of sociability. A portrait of this kind marked a marriage, a family occasion or a position at court, and was made to hang in a family's home. The sitter's name is lost, so she represents a type, the fashionable Frenchwoman of the Regency's threshold.","c":"Judged without a known maker, the portrait must stand on its qualities: composure, a clear likeness and an economy of means. It is graceful rather than daring, and it lacks the distinctive touch that would let it be assigned to a leading portraitist such as Largillière. Its value is as a pleasant, well-bred image of a moment of transition between the weight of the old reign and the lightness of the Rococo."},"nga:12203":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated with her body angled slightly left and her head turned toward the viewer, with a soft, serene expression and smoothly pinned hair. She wears a luxurious fur-trimmed shawl over a greenish-blue patterned dress with ornate lace cuffs, and a choker necklace. Her hand rests on a carved wooden table draped with rich red cloth, and the dark background fades into shadow. The title names a muff, the fur hand-warmer of fashionable winter dress.","i":"The portrait belongs to the Rococo society of Louis XV's France, when painters such as Nattier and Drouais made fashionable young women look elegant, composed and a little poised. The muff, fur trim and lace are accessories of a winter wardrobe and marks of wealth and refinement; the choker, a ribbon or necklace at the throat, was a fashionable note of the mid-century. The sitter is unnamed, and the picture speaks of fashion more than of biography.","c":"A graceful image of Rococo society portraiture: the eye is drawn to costume details, fur, lace cuffs and patterned fabric, which are the heart of the genre. The soft features and serene expression flatter without claiming personality, and the risk is blandness. The dark ground focuses attention well. Whoever the painter was, a talented follower of the fashionable masters, the picture is comfortable and pleasing, not probing, and it holds its place among portraits of its type."},"nga:1218":{"d":"At the center of a crowded composition, an armored man on a white horse seizes the torso of a woman with upraised hands while a second armored man grips her waist from the other side. An older white-haired man kneels before the horse, raising a hand toward the rider, and the surrounding figures, in elaborate draped costume, turn toward the central group in conflict. Classical architecture rises under a pale blue sky. The soft yellows and browns and the loose brushwork suggest an oil sketch, painted on paper later laid on canvas.","i":"The subject is a founding legend from Livy's history of Rome. Short of wives, Romulus's followers invited the neighboring Sabines to a festival and carried off their women, which led to a war that ended only when the women themselves intervened. The old word rape meant abduction, though modern viewers rightly find the violence troubling. For painters the story offered a chance to show many figures in action, and it served as a founding myth of Roman order and civic union.","c":"As an oil sketch it has the advantage of speed: the central group builds a pyramid around the white horse, and loose strokes in yellow and brown keep the figures moving. Its aim is a Rococo lightness rather than the severity of Poussin's famous versions of the subject. The unknown hand arranges a crowd well, if with little individual feeling. It is a lively exercise, more attractive than a finished academic canvas might have been."},"nga:46117":{"d":"A young man looks toward the viewer, his body turned slightly to the right, with prominent cheekbones, an upturned nose and red lips in a slight smile. His light gray curly hair is tied in a low ponytail at the nape, probably powdered after the fashion of the time, and he wears a pale green garment with a striped green scarf. His hands at the right hold an indistinct gray object, and the background is a mix of brown and gray tones. The canvas measures about 65 by 54 cm.","i":"Smiling portraits are rare in the formal tradition, but the French eighteenth century, with its taste for sociability and sensibility, made room for them, especially in less formal pictures meant for friends and family. The pale garment and loose scarf suggest an informal likeness rather than a portrait of rank. The maker and sitter are unknown, and the object in his hands is unclear, so the picture speaks of a mood of ease more than of a particular identity.","c":"The slight smile is the picture's charm, giving the face a warmth that formal portraits of the period often avoid. The brown-gray ground and the soft handling keep attention on the head. The hands and the unclear object are less resolved, and the anonymity of the painter limits what can be said of its quality beyond a pleasing naturalness. It belongs to a shift in the late eighteenth century from ceremony toward personality in portraiture."},"nga:52257":{"d":"A young boy with light brown hair and delicate features is shown in a brown coat with a white ruffled collar against a dark, muted background. The oil on wood panel is tiny, about 15 by 12 cm, and was painted about 1790 to 1795 by an unidentified French artist. A portrait of this size was made to be kept close, in a room or a case, as a personal keepsake, and the plain dress follows the simpler fashions then adopted for children.","i":"Children's dress became simpler and freer in the last decades of the eighteenth century, partly under the influence of Rousseau's ideas on upbringing, and a plain coat with a frilled collar belongs to that fashion. Because many children died young, parents valued likenesses to keep. The date falls in the years of the French Revolution, yet the picture is a private, domestic one; the boy's name and family are not known.","c":"The success of a child's portrait depends on freshness and the absence of affectation, and the delicate features and plain dress suggest an unforced likeness. The small size limits ambition, and the unknown painter leaves little to say about style. It is a modest cabinet picture, tender rather than brilliant, and its best quality is its quiet."},"nga:32688":{"d":"An elderly woman sits, shown from the waist up, facing the viewer with a neutral expression, her wrinkles visible and her cheeks rosy. A white lace cap and a black headscarf cover most of her hair, and she wears a patterned reddish-brown garment with lace at the collar and sleeves. Her hands rest on a brown fur muff, and the dark gray-brown background shows visible brushstrokes. The canvas measures about 81 by 65 cm. The title names a type, an old woman with a muff, rather than a sitter.","i":"Old age was a favorite subject of eighteenth-century French painters, from Chardin's quiet domestic figures to Greuze's heads of the elderly, who stood for virtue, experience and the dignity of a long life. The lace and fur show a woman comfortably off, not a peasant, and the muff, an accessory of winter dress, adds a note of warmth. Whether it is a portrait of a real woman or a character study is not clear from the title.","c":"The picture's strength is its lack of flattery: wrinkles, rosy cheeks and a neutral expression are recorded without sweetening, and the visible brushwork of the background keeps the surface alive. The limited palette of browns and grays gives it unity, and the lace and fur supply small pleasures. The painter is unknown, and the composition is plain. It is an honest, sober image of age, closer to Chardin's sympathy than to the era's more sentimental old people."},"nga:46037":{"d":"A young woman and a man appear together in a tiny oil on wood panel, about 12 by 10 cm. The title suggests a double portrait or a small scene of two figures, and the maker and the sitters are not identified beyond the eighteenth century. A panel the size of a hand was made for close viewing, as a keepsake or a cabinet picture, in the tradition of small French pictures of the period.","i":"Pairs of young figures were a staple of eighteenth-century French art, both in portraits of couples and in the amorous scenes of painters such as Boucher and Fragonard. A panel so small was probably meant to be kept close, perhaps as a memento of an engagement or marriage, or simply as a charming object in a cabinet. Without names, a date or a documented setting, it is not possible to say whether the pair are lovers, spouses or invented figures.","c":"Almost nothing is known, so the critic must be modest: in a panel the size of a hand, success depends on economy, and charm is the likeliest virtue. The French eighteenth-century cabinet picture was a genre of delicate finish and slight narrative. Its value is as an example of that intimate tradition and not as the work of a named master."},"nga:46039":{"d":"Five women, a man and two children gather in a garden by a stone wall topped with an oversized urn in this vertical canvas of about 32 by 25 cm. The colors are muted, with dresses in rose pink, canary yellow, olive green and ivory. One woman sits with her back to the viewer, facing a young man whose stockinged leg extends to the right; another sits on a bench at left, two recline in the background, a woman plays a guitar-like instrument and two children sit on the grass.","i":"The scene belongs to the fête galante, a type created by Watteau in the early 1700s and carried on by Lancret, Pater and others: elegant company at leisure in a park, with music and flirtation. Divertissement means amusement, and the title names the whole subject. The guitar, the secluded garden and the pairing of a woman and a man suggest the language of courtship, but the aim is an atmosphere of leisure, not a story.","c":"A modest cabinet picture in the line of Watteau, with a light touch and a muted harmony in which the canvas texture shows. The grouping is clear, though the small figures are more mood than character, and the whole lacks the melancholy and the subtle observation that make Watteau's parks haunting. The unknown painter has understood the formula and its charm, and the picture works as pleasant decoration with a faint nostalgia."},"nga:34117":{"d":"An unnamed lady is the subject of this small portrait, an oil on canvas of about 33 by 25 cm, dated about 1810 and attributed only to a French painter of the nineteenth century. The date falls in the Napoleonic Empire, a period of neoclassical taste in dress and portraiture, when women's fashion adopted the high waist and simple lines inspired by antiquity. A portrait of this size served as a private likeness for family and friends.","i":"Under the Empire, taste moved away from the ornament of the old regime toward classical simplicity, and portraits followed: plain backgrounds, clear outlines, a reserved manner, as in the work of David's pupils and of the young Ingres. A small portrait like this recorded the appearance of a woman at a moment in her life, perhaps a marriage or the birth of a child. Without a name, it stands for a type, the woman of Napoleon's France.","c":"Judged without a known maker, the portrait can only be placed by type, and the type is the Empire's cool clarity: smooth surface, restrained feeling, little decoration. If it has the virtues of David's circle, they are neatness and honesty; if it has the faults, they are blandness and a chill. The small scale gives intimacy, but the lost identities of painter and sitter leave it more a document of taste than a distinct work."},"nga:52258":{"d":"A countryside scene opens beside a wide dirt path, with a distant horizon of low hills under a light blue sky with puffy white clouds. People sit or stand on a grassy slope in the foreground, facing the path, where riders on horseback pass; across an open field are low brown buildings and a few white tents with dark smudges suggesting crowds. Short, thick strokes and dots and streaks of paint suggest details rather than showing them. The oil on canvas is about 32 by 49 cm.","i":"The Longchamp racecourse in the Bois de Boulogne opened in 1857 under Napoleon III and quickly became the place where fashionable Paris saw and was seen; the Grand Prix de Paris began there in 1863. Racing was a modern leisure subject, taken up by Manet and Degas, and painters of the new generation prized the open air and the crowd. An unidentified artist working about 1870 shows how widely this taste for modern life had spread.","c":"The shorthand of dots and streaks suits a crowd and a bright day, and the small canvas has the freshness of an open-air note. What it lacks is focus: no group commands the eye, and the composition is more notation than design. The painter is unidentified and clearly aware of the new Impressionist idiom without mastering its structure. It remains an engaging record of how the modern-life subject spread beyond its leaders."},"nga:52177":{"d":"A large yellow lemon stands upright beside a wide slice of melon on a white plate, with a long light-green stem and several dark green leaves rising from the lemon. A draped white tablecloth, touched with light blue and green, lies under the plate, and a silver butter knife rests in front. Behind, colorful floral patterns in orange, light pink, purple and yellow stand out against a gray wall. The oil on canvas, mounted on wood, is about 37 by 30 cm.","i":"Still life was renewed in France in the later nineteenth century by painters who saw in humble fruit a field for color and structure: Manet, Cézanne, Van Gogh and, around 1900, Matisse and the Nabis, who liked patterned wallpaper behind the table. A lemon with its leaves attached and a slice of melon on a plate are everyday things, chosen not for symbolism but for their colors and forms. The white cloth tinted with blue and green reflects a lesson of Impressionism: whites and shadows are made of color.","c":"A small, bright still life whose modern spirit shows in the tinted white cloth and the patterned wall, so that the picture is about color relations more than about fruit. The yellows against the pale cloth make the main harmony, and the busy wall gives a counterpoint. The painter is unknown and derivative of greater names, but the picture shows the diffusion of Post-Impressionist ideas into ordinary studios. It is a pleasant, intelligent minor piece."},"nga:52260":{"d":"A vase of roses is the subject of this very small oil on wood panel, only about 9 by 15 cm, a horizontal format more like a sketch than a finished picture. The painter is known only as a French artist of the later nineteenth century, active between about 1880 and 1900. Panels this small were commonly painted as quick studies of color or as gifts and inexpensive decorative pieces.","i":"Roses were a favorite subject of French painters of the late nineteenth century, from Manet's late flower pieces to the roses of Renoir and Fantin-Latour, because they combine delicate form, rich color and an old association with love and beauty. A small panel like this expresses no complex meaning; it is a pleasure of the eye, a flower painted for its own sake. Whether it was a study or a finished gift is a question that its size leaves open.","c":"In a panel this size the painter must make a bouquet with a few strokes, and the interest lies in the freshness of touch and the harmony of petals and vase. It is not a great picture, and the unknown hand leaves little to measure it by, but small flower panels of this period often have a spontaneity that large exhibition pieces lack. As a minor, charming sketch it succeeds on its own modest terms."},"met:436409":{"d":"A young man is the subject of this small portrait, oil on wood, about 27 by 20 cm, dated about 1495. It is attributed to a French painter, possibly Burgundian, and the question mark shows that the style does not settle the regional attribution. The sitter's name is lost. A small panel of this kind was made for private use, a family likeness or a gift among friends, in the years when French and Burgundian portraiture was learning from the Netherlandish masters.","i":"Around 1500 the portrait was becoming a regular part of elite and professional life in France and the neighboring Netherlandish lands. Merchants, lawyers, courtiers and clergy had themselves painted in plain dress against plain grounds, emphasizing steadiness and character. The influence of Memling and van der Weyden taught painters to record a face with patient exactness. A young man's portrait often marked a coming of age, a marriage or an appointment, though nothing in the title reveals the occasion.","c":"Anonymous and unplaced, the portrait is best judged by its directness. Netherlandish habits of exact observation, softened for French taste, let the face carry the picture without ornament. The strengths of such panels are sobriety and honesty; the weaknesses are a certain stiffness and the loss of the sitter's story. Without a name for either painter or sitter, it works as an example of the quiet portrait of 1500, valued for presence more than polish."},"met:436337":{"d":"A Benedictine monk is shown in prayer in this small portrait on wood, about 34 by 24 cm, painted about 1500 by an unidentified French artist. As with other prayer portraits of the time, the sitter's gesture suggests a devotional image beyond the frame, probably a companion panel showing the Virgin or Christ. The black habit of the Benedictines identifies his order, and the portrait records an individual face as well as a vocation.","i":"Benedictine abbeys were wealthy patrons in France around 1500, and abbots and priors commissioned panels in which they appeared before holy images, so that their faces would remain present in prayer. The Rule of Saint Benedict makes prayer the center of the monk's day, and the prayer pose is a statement of his life as well as a convention. The sitter is unnamed, so whether he was an abbot, a prior or an ordinary monk is not known.","c":"A prayer portrait must show piety without falling into formula, and the best of the type lets an individual face speak through the conventional pose. The panel's merits are sobriety and the sense of an actual man; the shortcoming is the anonymity of both painter and sitter. Beside the great Netherlandish donor portraits it is modest, but it keeps something of their patient attention to a human face."},"met:436338":{"d":"A man identified as Monsieur de Bellefourière is the subject of this portrait, oil on wood, dated 1521 and attributed to an unidentified French painter. The title is a courtesy form, Monsieur de, naming a gentleman by his estate, and probably follows an inscription on the picture. The year falls in the reign of Francis I, when portraiture at the French court and among the nobility was flourishing.","i":"In France under Francis I a portrait was part of a gentleman's equipment: it recorded rank, linked him to patrons and friends, and fixed his appearance at a given moment, here as precisely as the inscribed year. A seigneur named after his estate had an identity bound up with land and lineage. The modest panel format suggests a likeness kept within a family or exchanged among friends rather than shown publicly.","c":"Anonymous, the portrait is best placed by what its type promises: the plain, clear, slightly stiff manner of early sixteenth-century French portraiture, where the face is recorded with sober exactness and little flourish. The dated inscription adds interest as a document. As painting it is best taken as an honest period likeness rather than a work of marked individual style."},"met:437772":{"d":"A man wearing the insignia of the Order of the Annunziata of Savoy, normally a gold collar, is the subject of this small portrait, oil on wood, about 36 by 28 cm. It was painted in the first quarter of the sixteenth century by an unidentified French painter. The title names the order as the key to the sitter's standing, so the insignia was the most important thing the picture had to show.","i":"The Order of the Annunziata, founded in the fourteenth century by the Counts of Savoy as the Order of the Collar and renamed in the early sixteenth century, was among the most exclusive knighthoods in Europe, limited to a small number of princes and great nobles. To wear its insignia in a portrait was to announce membership in that elite and a bond with the duke of Savoy. The sitter's name is lost, but the order marks him as a man of high rank.","c":"A portrait whose subject is rank draws its force from the insignia, and the painter's first duty was to show the honor clearly. As far as one can tell from the type, the sitter himself would be presented with the plainness and clarity of early French portraiture. The attribution is anonymous and the identity open. The picture's value lies chiefly in what it documents of Savoyard and French court culture, and its quality is clarity more than invention."},"met:436339":{"d":"A member of the de Thou family is shown in a tiny oil on wood panel, only about 16 by 14 cm, dated 1540. The title does not say which member, and the painter is unknown. At this scale the portrait is nearly a miniature, a concentrated likeness made to be held and examined closely. The date falls in the reign of Francis I, when small portraits circulated widely among families and friends.","i":"The de Thou were a Parisian family of magistrates, part of the noblesse de robe, whose members rose through service in the Parlement of Paris. Christophe de Thou became its first president, and his son Jacques-Auguste de Thou wrote an influential history of his times. A portrait of one of the family, in the sober dress of the legal elite, records the rise of a class that gained status through learning and office, not through the sword.","c":"At this scale the painter must concentrate a person into a few inches, and the typical virtues of such panels are sharp observation of the face and restraint. The title's phrase, a member of, signals that the exact sitter is not known, which limits the picture to a document of a family. As painting it is a competent small likeness; its charm is intimacy, and its principal interest is as evidence of the world of the Paris magistracy."},"met:436340":{"d":"A man in a tall-crowned hat is the subject of this small portrait, oil on wood, about 24 by 19 cm, dated 1570. The hat, fashionable in the 1560s and 1570s, gives the figure a strong vertical silhouette and is the first thing the title mentions. The sitter's name is lost, and the painter is known only as French, active in a time when the Wars of Religion were dividing the kingdom.","i":"The year 1570 fell in the Wars of Religion, which began in 1562 and lasted until 1598, but portraiture continued among the nobility and the educated bourgeoisie, who valued a likeness as a sign of identity and of ties of friendship. A tall hat was a fashion statement, marking a man up to date with the court's taste. Small portraits in the manner of the Clouet workshop circulated among friends and relations.","c":"The hat dominates the head: a tall crown gives the painter an easy, strong shape and the sitter a distinctive profile, but it shows fashion more than character. The title's emphasis on dress hints that the picture is a record of appearance rather than a probing study. The anonymous painter works within the neatness, clarity and reserve of small French portraits of the period."},"met:436341":{"d":"A man in a white fur coat is shown in a tiny oil on wood panel, about 18 by 14 cm, painted in the second or third quarter of the sixteenth century by an unidentified French painter. Fur, a luxury and a sign of status regulated by sumptuary laws, is the key to the picture's meaning. The portrait is a small, close likeness of a man of means, made for private keeping.","i":"Fur was warm but, more to the point, expensive: in sixteenth-century France it marked rank and office, and sumptuary laws tried to control who could wear which fur. A white fur coat, as the title says, shows that the sitter chose to be seen wrapped in wealth. The date range places the portrait in the reign of Francis I and his successors, the high age of French court portraiture, when small likenesses were exchanged and collected.","c":"In so small a panel the fur gives the painter a chance to show texture, though a face a few inches wide is the real challenge: it must not become a mere costume piece. Such pictures share the virtues of small French portraits of the time: neatness, honesty and an intimate scale. Its interest is as a record of status and of a man of means."},"met:436410":{"d":"A nymph is the central figure of this wide panel, about 66 by 121 cm, set in a landscape among an unusually dense collection of emblems: a book and an act of reading, fruit, a monkey, a dog, a putto and a human head, with lyres, urns, flowers, and dragons and lions among the plants. The picture belongs to the School of Fontainebleau, the Mannerist art of the royal château where Italian and French artists worked for the Valois kings.","i":"The nymph of the title descends from Cellini's famous bronze relief of a nymph for Fontainebleau, which personified the spring that gave the château its name. A local legend credited a royal hunting dog with finding the water. The Fontainebleau artists mixed classical myth with court allegory, and a nymph surrounded by books, fruit and music may flatter the court's cultivated pleasures: learning, abundance, the arts and the hunt, the domain of Diana. The meanings are layered and not fully decoded.","c":"School of Fontainebleau art is elegant, erotic, learned and deliberately obscure, and this panel's pleasure is its dense invention. The weakness is the crowding: so many emblems compete that the picture becomes a puzzle more than a story, and the nymph herself risks being one ornament among many. The anonymous painter shows the refinement of Mannerism at its most decorative. It is more intriguing than moving, as the best of its school often are."},"met:436342":{"d":"Henri III (1551–1589), King of France, is the subject of this small portrait, oil on wood, about 32 by 25 cm, by an unidentified French painter of the late sixteenth century. The picture makes the king's identity its whole subject, and a modest panel of this size suggests a version of an official image, circulated as a loyal or collector's likeness rather than a state portrait for a palace.","i":"Henri III, the third son of Henri II and Catherine de' Medici, was briefly King of Poland before becoming King of France in 1574. His reign was dominated by the Wars of Religion, and he founded the Order of the Holy Spirit in 1578. He was the last of the Valois kings, assassinated by a monk in 1589, so portraits of him carry historical weight, and his refined, learned court is remembered alongside his political troubles.","c":"The small panel is probably a workshop or follower's version of an official portrait type, and its strengths are clarity and the fascination of the sitter. The weaknesses are an official blandness and a lack of the individuality that a leading portraitist would have brought to such a famous face. Henri III's reputation for extravagant dress makes the sober small scale slightly ironic. It is a competent historical image, more valuable for its subject than for its style."},"met:436345":{"d":"Strawberries, flowers and vegetables are gathered with a squirrel in this oil on canvas of about 60 by 80 cm, painted by an unidentified French artist in the seventeenth century. The subjects mix kitchen produce with flowers and a live animal, a lively combination more at home in Flemish and Dutch still life than in the austerity often associated with French painting. Its horizontal format suited a dining room or a salon.","i":"Strawberries, a spring and early summer fruit, can carry meanings that range from earthly pleasure and transience to Marian purity, though in many still lifes they are simply a seasonal delight. Flowers speak of passing time and vegetables of the kitchen's plenty. A squirrel, the thrifty gatherer of nuts, might be read as a lesson in foresight, or simply as a lively accent. Seventeenth-century viewers enjoyed such pictures as displays of abundance and of skill with textures.","c":"A lively, slightly crowded still life in which the squirrel adds spark and the red of strawberries gives a focus. The handling of the fruit and surfaces appears careful, though the composition is less polished than that of the best French still-life painters such as Louise Moillon. Its charm lies in abundance and in the animal, and its limits lie in a certain looseness of arrangement. It is an agreeable, if unmemorable, example of its type."},"met:436348":{"d":"A man's portrait is painted on a small piece of vellum laid on a playing card, of which one diamond pip shows, in a French miniature of about 1700. The support makes it a small, hand-sized object. The card gives the picture an improvised look, as if a likeness had been made with whatever stiff board was at hand, and the vellum surface allows fine detail.","i":"Using a playing card as a support is a clue to context. In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries cards had plain backs, so they served as a cheap, rigid, ready-made board for notes, invitations and small paintings. A miniature on a card was probably made informally, as a keepsake or a gift, for a sitter who did not require a jeweled case. The man is unnamed, and the picture carries no further information about who he was.","c":"The format is the curiosity: a union of the luxurious, vellum, and the everyday, a playing card. The judgment should be modest. As a likeness it is small, intimate and perhaps quickly made, and its value is more social-historical than aesthetic. It shows how portraiture reached beyond the court and the wealthy into everyday objects, and it has the charm of improvisation that a formal miniature in a jeweled case could not have."},"met:436397":{"d":"A man in a brown coat is the subject of this portrait, oil on canvas, about 73 by 59 cm, painted in the first quarter of the eighteenth century by an unidentified French painter. A woman's portrait in the same collection, in a rose dress, has almost the same size and period and came in the same bequest, which suggests that the two were made as companions, husband and wife.","i":"A man in a plain brown coat suggests a bourgeois, a lawyer or a lesser noble, as opposed to a courtier in embroidered silk. In the early eighteenth century, portraits of married couples were commonly painted as pairs, the husband as the sober figure of standing and the wife as the elegant one. The brown coat speaks of practicality and means rather than of ceremony. The sitter's name is lost, so the portrait stands for a type, the French man of substance.","c":"Without a name, the picture's merit lies in an unforced, straightforward presentation, with the brown coat anchoring the figure. The French portrait manner of Largillière and Rigaud shapes it at a more modest level, and the painter has limited flair. It offers an honest likeness, and it is perhaps better judged together with its probable companion. As a single portrait it is sound but ordinary, a witness to the middle ranks of French society."},"met:436398":{"d":"A woman in a rose dress is the subject of this portrait, oil on canvas, about 72 by 59 cm, painted in the first quarter of the eighteenth century by an unidentified French painter. It is almost identical in size to the man in a brown coat that came in the same bequest, which suggests that the two were painted as a pair. The rose of the dress, named in the title, is the picture's most distinctive note.","i":"A rose or pink silk dress marked wealth and fashion, and the color, a sign of youth and marriage, was often chosen for women's portraits. In a pair of portraits, the husband's plain brown coat and the wife's rose silk would divide the roles: his solidity and her elegance. The woman's name is lost, so she appears as a type, the well-dressed wife of a French household in the years of the Regency's threshold.","c":"The rose dress gives the painter a chance to show the sheen of silk, and the color is the portrait's chief pleasure. The face is likely less distinctive than the dress, as in many portraits of this market, and the unknown painter works within a formula of ease and politeness. The result is pleasing and unremarkable, a pleasant document of fashion and a competent companion piece, but not a memorable individual achievement."},"met:436344":{"d":"The Holy Family is joined by Mary's mother, Saint Anne, in a vertical oil on canvas of about 69 by 51 cm, painted in the early seventeenth century by an unidentified French artist. The figures are Mary, Joseph, Anne and the child Jesus: three generations gathered in a small devotional picture meant for a private home or chapel, in which the child is the focus of the family's attention.","i":"The Holy Family with Anne stresses lineage: Anne, whom tradition names as the Virgin's mother, links Jesus to the generations behind him. Her cult was especially strong in France, where Anne of Brittany and, later, the Counter-Reformation church promoted her as a patron of mothers and families. A picture of the extended holy family offered an ideal of the Christian household for private devotion, and for a family that saw its own generations in the three.","c":"The painter is unknown, and the early seventeenth-century French school was poised between the late Mannerism of Fontainebleau and the new classicism. A small devotional picture like this succeeds when the intimacy of the family is believable, and the plain virtues of tenderness and clarity are what to look for. The anonymity of the artist, and the lack of detail, make it a document of devotional taste more than a notable work."},"met:436350":{"d":"A man is portrayed in oil on a copper plate in a French portrait of about 1720, in the years of the Regency. Copper gives a smooth, hard surface that took fine detail and a glossy finish, and it was favored for small portraits and cabinet pictures. The painter and the sitter are both unidentified, so the picture is a likeness without a story, a private object of the kind kept in a cabinet or case.","i":"Copper was more costly than panel or canvas and more durable, and from the seventeenth century it was a preferred support for small, finely finished paintings that could be held and examined. A portrait on copper would have been a private object, kept in a cabinet or case, not a picture for a public wall. The date, soon after the death of Louis XIV in 1715, places it in a period of relaxed manners, but the man's name and occupation are lost.","c":"Copper rewards a delicate touch, since its smooth surface makes oil look enamel-like, and small portraits on copper are among the most intimate objects of the period. The pleasures of the type are polish and presence, and the limits are the anonymity of painter and sitter. It is best taken as a representative of a small, precious kind of likeness, appealing for its finish more than for any individual drama."},"met:436351":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Louis XV (1710–1774), King of France, is painted on vellum about 1750 by an unidentified French artist. The medium makes it a small object, the kind set in a frame, a case or a snuffbox. In 1750 the king was about forty, in the middle of a reign that lasted nearly sixty years. The picture makes the king's image its whole subject, and the surface of vellum allows a smooth, delicate finish.","i":"Louis XV came to the throne at the age of five in 1715 and was known as the Beloved after the nation prayed for his recovery from illness at Metz in 1744. Royal portrait miniatures were made in large numbers, as loyal keepsakes and as courtesies given to ambassadors and favored courtiers, often in jeweled snuffboxes. His reign was a time of cultural brilliance and political drift, and by the Seven Years' War France lost much of its empire.","c":"A royal miniature must flatter within limits, and the likeness is presumably based on an official portrait by a court painter, which makes this a derivative work. The vellum permits a fine, smooth finish, and the formula of the idealized monarch is competently followed. The weaknesses are the anonymity of the painter and a lack of individuality, since the king appears as an emblem. It is polished and conventional, more interesting as a type than as an achievement."},"met:436352":{"d":"A harbor with boats is the subject of this small picture painted on glass in the technique called verre fixé, about 1750. In verre fixé the design is built up on the back of the glass and seen through it, which gives the colors a glossy, almost enamel-like brilliance. The painter is unknown, and the image follows the popular taste for harbor scenes. The pairing of a fashionable subject with a craft medium makes it a small curiosity of Rococo taste.","i":"Harbor views were a favorite subject in eighteenth-century France, shaped by the success of Claude-Joseph Vernet, whose series of the ports of France, begun in the 1750s for Louis XV, fixed the image of the French coast. A harbor spoke of trade, travel and the nation's maritime ambitions. Small pictures on glass brought the subject to modest homes, since a glass picture was far cheaper than an oil painting by a master.","c":"Glass painting favors bright, clear color and crisp outline, and the genre's limits are small scale, formula and a certain flatness. As a popular art, its pleasures are decorative: a harbor with boats rendered with a jewel-like sparkle. The unknown maker lacks Vernet's atmospheric skies and sure sense of light. At best it is an attractive trinket, a record of how a fashionable subject was translated into a humbler medium."},"met:436353":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a miniature painted on ivory about 1760 by an unidentified French artist. Ivory was used for small portraits, set in bracelets, rings, boxes and frames. By this date ivory had become the standard support for French portrait miniatures, replacing vellum after the vogue begun by Rosalba Carriera in Paris in 1720. The date places it in the middle of the reign of Louis XV, and the sitter's name is lost.","i":"Ivory's translucency lends flesh a luminous glow that the miniaturist exploited with fine watercolor strokes, and the taste for the medium made miniature painting a flourishing trade in eighteenth-century France. Such portraits were love tokens, family keepsakes and courtesies, and their meaning was private: the name and story are often lost, as here. A portrait of a man may have been given to a wife, a fiancée or a friend.","c":"The medium rewards patient stippling and thin, translucent color, and when it is well done the face glows against a pale ground. The type is attractive in its Rococo softness. As an anonymous piece, it is simply an example of the miniaturist's art at its commercial peak in France, when many workshops produced such likenesses, with a general level of competence."},"met:436404":{"d":"A woman holds a book, and a coat of arms appears in the picture, in an oil on canvas of about 82 by 66 cm, painted in the middle of the eighteenth century by an unidentified French painter. The coat of arms marks her family's standing, and the book may be devotional or literary. The size allows a half-length figure, and the portrait presents a woman of rank as a cultivated person.","i":"The coat of arms identifies the family but not the sitter's name, which is lost; heraldry was a statement of lineage, and noble families used it in portraits to claim a place in the social order. The book is a sign of piety or learning. In eighteenth-century France women of the nobility and upper bourgeoisie were readers and participants in salon culture, and a portrait with a book flatters a woman as virtuous, educated and refined.","c":"The combination of heraldry and book gives this portrait more content than most anonymous likenesses: it announces both pedigree and cultivation. The painter's competence is conventional, and the face probably receives less attention than the arms. The strengths are clarity of message and a pleasing format; the weakness is a lack of individuality. It is more interesting for what it signifies about a cultivated, titled woman than for painterly brilliance."},"met:436355":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a miniature on ivory painted about 1770 by an unidentified French artist. The support makes it a small, hand-sized object. The date falls in the last years of Louis XV, when French taste was moving from Rococo curves toward the simpler lines of Neoclassicism, and miniatures reflected that change in a more restrained, naturalistic style. The sitter's name is lost, and the picture is a private likeness without a story.","i":"Portrait miniatures around 1770 served as keepsakes for lovers, spouses and friends, and were worn or set into jewelry, boxes and furniture. A man's miniature might be given at an engagement, kept by a family or carried by an absent relative. The name and circumstances of this one are lost, so the sitter stands for the type of the gentleman of the age, painted for private affection and not for show.","c":"At this date the best miniaturists had a delicate eye for translucent flesh and a gift for conveying character with a few touches, and the work is judged on that skill. The type is attractive and unassuming, and the miniature's charm lies in the tender scale. As an anonymous example it offers a pleasing, competent record of a face of 1770."},"met:436361":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a miniature on ivory painted about 1778 by an unidentified French artist. The year falls in the reign of Louis XVI, when France entered the American war and Paris was full of visitors and patriots, and the demand for small portraits was high. The sitter is unnamed, and the picture is a likeness without a documented story. Ivory, with its warm translucence, suited a likeness meant to be held and looked at closely.","i":"In the late 1770s the portrait miniature was at the height of its popularity, and its owners prized a faithful likeness over flattery. A man's portrait might mark a marriage, a departure for service, or friendship, and it was often kept in a case or set into a jeweled frame. The age's cult of sensibility valued such intimate tokens as emblems of feeling, though the specific feeling behind this one is not known.","c":"A miniature of this date lives by fidelity: a face observed carefully, a delicate palette, a soft tone against the pale ground. The type's virtues are modesty and honesty. It is a sound example of the later Louis XVI manner, which favors simplicity and truth to the sitter, and its worth is as a small, authentic keepsake."},"met:436365":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in a miniature on ivory painted about 1780 by an unidentified French artist. The sitter's name is lost. The date falls in the years when women's fashion in France reached its extremes of elaborate hair and dress under Marie Antoinette's influence, and portrait miniatures recorded that fashion for family and friends. Ivory gave the miniaturist a pale, luminous ground for skin, powder and lace, and the small scale made the likeness a personal object to be held and kept.","i":"Women's miniatures of the 1780s were love tokens, marriage gifts and family keepsakes, often set in bracelets, lockets or boxes, and they recorded the fashion of the day. A miniature of a woman might be made for a husband away at war or on business, or for a suitor. The woman's identity and the occasion of the portrait are not known, so she stands for the type of the Frenchwoman of the Ancien Régime's last decade.","c":"The challenge of a woman's miniature is to combine likeness with the idealization that fashion demanded, and the best examples achieve a luminous complexion and a soft individual expression. The maker here is unknown, so the picture can only be judged as typical of its place and time: competent, delicate and charming. Its limits are anonymity and the lack of documentation, and its value is as a vivid, if unlabeled, glimpse of a vanished society."},"met:436366":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a small picture painted on glass in the technique called verre fixé, about 1790, by an unidentified French artist. The medium, in which the image is built up on the back of the glass, gives the colors a clean, glossy brilliance. The date falls at the start of the Revolution, when portraiture in many media, including cheap glass pictures, met a new public demand.","i":"Glass portraits were made in quantity in the later eighteenth century as inexpensive alternatives to oil paintings and miniatures, and in the years of the Revolution small portraits circulated of political figures and ordinary citizens alike. A man's portrait on glass could be a family keepsake or an emblem of political allegiance. The sitter is unnamed, so his place in the turmoil of 1790 is unknown, though his image is a reminder of the ordinary people who lived through it.","c":"Glass painting favors bold outline, bright color and simplicity, and the technique's limits are its flatness and the difficulty of subtle modeling. As a portrait it is likely more direct than refined, and its quality lies in its immediacy. It is best valued as an example of the popular art of its day, where the clear, bright image mattered more than finesse."},"met:436367":{"d":"A man with a palette is shown in this self-portrait painted on ivory about 1790. The artist cannot be identified, though the palette marks him as a painter, and the subject is thus an unknown artist's image of himself. The small ivory support, favored for miniatures in the later eighteenth century, suggests a work meant for a private audience or as a demonstration of skill.","i":"The palette is the painter's emblem, and self-portraits by miniaturists served as advertisements of their skill, calling cards for clients and keepsakes for family and friends. An artist who paints his own face stakes a claim on the dignity of his profession, and in the 1790s, when old patrons were scattered and new ones had to be found, such a claim mattered. That the painter is now unknown, though he made himself the subject, is an irony of history.","c":"A self-portrait invites a stricter test than a commissioned likeness, since the artist has no excuse for a lack of observation. The palette adds a professional pride, and the small ivory format demands fine control. Without knowing the painter, a critic can only say that the idea, a modest, honest declaration of vocation, is what lasts. It is a small, appealing piece, valuable for its human directness and not for brilliance."},"met:436368":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in a miniature on ivory painted about 1790 by an unidentified French artist. The sitter's name is lost. The date falls in the first years of the Revolution, when dress simplified and the elaborate styles of the old court gave way to plainer fashions, and small portraits remained a favored way to keep a loved one's image close. Ivory suited that taste for plainness, with its pale ground and soft tones.","i":"In the 1790s women's fashion moved away from the court's stiff silks toward lighter fabrics and simpler hair, signs of the new taste for natural manners that Rousseau had encouraged. A portrait miniature of a woman was typically a keepsake for a husband, a lover or a family, and in times of danger and separation such objects were especially precious. The identity and story of this sitter remain unrecorded.","c":"The best miniatures of the 1790s combine a clean, simple design with a luminous, tender treatment of flesh, and the sitter's expression gives the picture its life. The painter here is unknown, so a fine judgment is impossible, but the type has a pleasing modesty. It is a small, competent testimony to the shift of taste around 1790 from ornament toward natural simplicity, valuable more as a document than as an individual achievement."},"met:436370":{"d":"A miniature portrait on ivory shows Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette (1757–1834), painted about 1790 by an unidentified French artist. The date puts the sitter at the height of his fame, soon after the fall of the Bastille, when he commanded the Paris National Guard, and a small likeness of him was exactly the kind of keepsake that admirers sought. In July 1790 he took the oath of the National Guard at the Fête de la Fédération, the great festival of the new order.","i":"Lafayette, a young French aristocrat, volunteered in the American Revolution at nineteen and fought beside Washington, becoming a hero in both countries. In 1789 he proposed a draft of the Declaration of the Rights of Man and took command of the National Guard. He later fell out with the radicals, fled in 1792 and was imprisoned in Austria until 1797. Miniatures of him were souvenirs for admirers in France and America.","c":"A portrait of a celebrity is judged by its power to satisfy the admirer, and this small ivory does what such keepsakes should: it records an agreeable, recognizable likeness. The unknown painter's competence is that of the commercial miniature of the day, polished and a little flattering. It is as much a souvenir of fame as a portrait, which limits its depth, but its subject gives it interest that few anonymous miniatures can match."},"met:436373":{"d":"A woman in a lace cap, with a fichu at her shoulders and a ribbon at her neck, is portrayed in a miniature on ivory about 1790 by an unidentified French artist. The details are costume: a neck ribbon, a fichu, a cap and lace, all the white linen and ribbon of a respectable woman's day dress, rendered on a support that was ideal for fine white-on-white work.","i":"The fichu, the cap and the neck ribbon belong to the modest, practical dress of a woman of the middle ranks in the later eighteenth century: a soft white linen scarf crossed over the bodice, a cap that covered the hair indoors. Compared with the silks of the court, such dress suggests a bourgeois wife or daughter, or a sitter who chose to appear unaffected, in tune with the age's taste for natural simplicity.","c":"Lace, fichu and cap give the miniaturist a chance to show delicate white-on-white handling, and that is what can be judged on a type like this: the lightness of the linen against the warm flesh, and the neat accent of the ribbon. The painter is unknown, and the face is likely to be modest in its individuality. It is a charming, competent record of a woman's dress, and a small lesson in how much fashion tells."},"met:436374":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a miniature on ivory about 1790 by an unidentified French artist. The Met's Portrait of a Woman, of the same date and medium and from the same bequest, may have been made as its companion, a husband and wife. The man's name is lost, and the picture offers only the likeness of a face of the years around 1790.","i":"Pairs of portraits of a husband and wife were a common use of miniatures: each partner kept the other's likeness, or the pair were made as a record of the marriage and hung together. The date, in the early years of the Revolution, makes the pair a witness to the ordinary lives lived against the turmoil of the age. Without a name, the man stands for a type, the French bourgeois of the 1790s, with the status and anxieties of his class.","c":"At its best a miniature of this date offers a clean, honest likeness with a luminous face against a pale ground, and a man's portrait lives by its steadiness of eye and plainness of dress. The painter is unknown, and the portrait's quality rests on the steadiness of the face itself. As a probable half of a pair it has a quiet dignity, and its significance as a document of a marriage may exceed its artistic importance."},"met:436376":{"d":"A man named Louis Morau is portrayed in a miniature on ivory about 1790 by an unidentified French artist. The title gives the sitter's name but no title, rank or profession, which suggests a man of the middle class, and the small ivory format, used for private likenesses, implies a picture meant for family, friends or a loved one. The date places him in the early years of the Revolution.","i":"A named sitter is a gift to a viewer, and yet the record says little more: no office, no estate, no date of birth. In the years around 1790 small portraits like this marked marriages, partings and friendships in a period of great change, when many men were uprooted by war or politics. A plain name without a noble title is typical of the bourgeois sitters who made up much of the miniaturists' clientele.","c":"A named sitter gives a miniature a reality that anonymous ones lack: this was a real man, with a family that wanted his face kept. The painter is unknown, so the portrait's interest is personal and historical, not stylistic. The miniature's merit, like that of many in this tradition, is that it preserves a face that would otherwise be forgotten."},"met:436399":{"d":"A long, low design of garlands and flowers, about 68 by 174 cm, is painted in oil on canvas as a cartoon, the full-size model for a tapestry to cover the back of a settee. It dates from the last quarter of the eighteenth century and is by an unidentified French painter. A cartoon of this kind was the pattern from which weavers worked, so it is a working document of a craft, not a picture for a wall.","i":"In eighteenth-century France, upholstery for chairs and settees was often woven in tapestry, and the great manufactories, such as Beauvais and Aubusson, supplied sets of seat covers and backs with matching designs of flowers, garlands and pastoral scenes. The painter designed, the weavers translated the design into wool and silk, and the upholsterer fitted it to the frame. The taste of the Louis XVI period favored light floral garlands, which suited the new elegance of furniture.","c":"As a working design, the picture is not meant to be admired as art, and its virtues are those of fitness for purpose: legible shapes, clear separations of color, and a rhythmic arrangement that the weavers could follow. The painter is anonymous, as most designers in the trade were. Its pleasure is its decorative elegance and its glimpse of a collaborative craft process, in which a painter's sketch turned into a settee."},"met:436377":{"d":"A woman with lace and a ribbon is portrayed in a miniature on ivory about 1795 by an unidentified French artist. Among the details is the tricolor, the blue, white and red of the Revolution, which gives a political note to an otherwise intimate object, and shows the sitter, or her family, choosing to be seen as a citizen of the new France. Her name is lost, and she is known only by what she wears.","i":"In the Revolutionary years the tricolor became the sign of civic loyalty, and in 1793 women were ordered to wear the tricolor cockade. A woman portrayed with the colors was proclaiming her place in the new order, whether out of conviction or prudence. Lace, a luxury of the old regime, shows that the sitter was not far from the world of fashion. The woman's name is lost, so she appears as a type, the female citizen of 1795.","c":"A miniature that combines the private and the public: lace, a sign of luxury, beside the tricolor, a sign of politics. The painter is unknown, and the symbolic content makes the portrait more than a likeness, though it does not necessarily make it a better painting. As a time capsule of a dangerous and uncertain moment, it is of greater historical than artistic interest, and the small ivory has a poignant charm."},"met:436378":{"d":"A woman, said to be the comtesse de Guiche, is portrayed in a miniature on ivory about 1795 by an unidentified French artist. The identification is only a tradition, as the title's words said to be admit. The title Guiche was borne by the heir of the Gramont family, one of the great houses of the old nobility, many of whose members left France during the Revolution.","i":"In the 1790s French aristocrats lost their titles and many emigrated, and a miniature was an ideal keepsake for people in exile or separation: small, portable and easily hidden. A portrait said to be a comtesse de Guiche is a reminder of the world of the old court, scattered by revolution. Because the identification is uncertain, the picture is best read as a likeness of a woman of the elite, its name an attractive but unproven tradition.","c":"A name can enrich a small object while its authenticity remains uncertain, and that is the case here: the portrait's appeal is the combination of historic gloss and the delicate technique of the miniature. The painter is anonymous, and the likeness is the picture's chief claim. It is best valued as a modest, charming keepsake, with a tradition attached that adds interest even if it cannot be proved."},"met:436405":{"d":"A miniature portrait on ivory shows François Joseph Lefebvre (1755–1820), dated 1800, by an unidentified French painter. Lefebvre is the soldier who rose from the ranks of the royal Gardes Françaises to become a Marshal of the Empire in 1804 and Duke of Danzig in 1807. The date places the portrait in the years of the Consulate, when he was a general of about forty-five.","i":"Lefebvre was a self-made man, a sergeant before the Revolution who rose by merit in the revolutionary armies, and his wife, a former laundress, became the subject of a famous play as Madame Sans-Gêne. In 1800 Napoleon, as First Consul, was building a new elite from the army, and portraits of generals were collected as emblems of national heroism. A small portrait in ivory was an intimate version of that public cult.","c":"A miniature of a soldier must suggest strength in a small space, and the best of them manage a firm gaze and a plain, direct manner. The unknown painter's restraint suits a self-made man who made little of show. The portrait's interest is its subject, a plebeian hero of the Revolution turned duke, and its charm is the modest scale in which that story is kept."},"met:436407":{"d":"A man in a blue coat is the subject of this portrait, oil on canvas, about 62 by 50 cm, painted in the early nineteenth century by an unidentified French painter. The coat named in the title is the main clue to the period: dark, sober tailoring replaced the bright silks of the eighteenth century in the years after the Revolution. The sitter's name is lost, and the portrait offers a likeness of a Frenchman of the Empire or the Restoration.","i":"After the Revolution, men's dress became sober and dark, a mark of the new bourgeois world in which business, law and public service counted more than birth, and a blue coat belongs to that change. A portrait of a man in this plain dress declares seriousness and respectability: he is shown as a man of affairs, not of the court. The picture belongs to the broad class of likenesses made for the families of the new elite, without a known name or occasion.","c":"A solid, honest early-nineteenth-century portrait is judged by its restraint: a clear head, plain dress and a steady presence. The painter's competence is evident in the type, not in any special distinction, and the anonymity limits what can be said of its quality. It has the dignity of the bourgeois portrait and the blandness that often goes with it, a decent record of a man of his time more than an exceptional picture."},"met:435892":{"d":"A man is the subject of this portrait, oil on canvas, about 53 by 46 cm, painted about 1835 by an unidentified French painter. The size suits a bust or a half-length, and the date falls in the July Monarchy of Louis-Philippe, when portraiture served the new bourgeois elite of lawyers, officials and businessmen. The sitter's name is lost, and the picture is a likeness without a documented story.","i":"The bourgeois monarchy of Louis-Philippe made merchants, lawyers and bankers the ruling class, and they commissioned portraits as signs of success and respectability. The Romantic age valued character and inner life, so portraits of the 1830s often aim at a sense of thought or feeling in a sober setting. A man in plain dark dress, as most of these sitters were, advertised trust and seriousness, which the new elite prized above show.","c":"Without a name, the portrait can only be placed in the stream of French portraiture of the 1830s, between Ingres's sleek precision and the more emotional manner of Delacroix and the Romantics. This anonymous example probably sits nearer the plain, competent middle. Its value is as a type, a decent likeness of a man of the July Monarchy, and a reminder of how many such portraits were made by painters whose names are lost."},"met:436408":{"d":"Napoleon Bonaparte (1769–1821) is shown in a small oil on wood portrait, about 46 by 38 cm, painted in the middle of the nineteenth century, some decades after his death on Saint Helena. The painter is unidentified. The picture makes the emperor's image its whole subject, and the date shows that his face remained one of the most reproduced in the world long after the Empire had fallen.","i":"The Napoleonic legend grew after 1821, fed by memoirs, prints and popular images, and it was strengthened by the return of his remains to Paris in 1840 and by the election of his nephew Louis-Napoleon in 1848, who made himself emperor in 1852. Portraits of the emperor were produced for admirers in France and abroad, including Americans, whose collectors bought such images by the 1870s. A mid-century portrait stresses the hero and the myth more than the man.","c":"A posthumous portrait of such a famous face is largely a matter of choosing a type: the severe profile of the Consul, the imperial robes, or the plain gray overcoat. The unknown painter's work is likely to be derivative of earlier images by David, Gros or Delaroche, and a verdict on its quality is modest. Its power is its subject, and its interest as evidence of the cult of Napoleon at the middle of the century."},"cma:162295":{"d":"Oedipus, the blind king of Greek myth, reprimands himself while protecting his daughter Antigone in this canvas of about 157 by 134 cm, dated 1798. He had fulfilled his fate by unknowingly killing his father and marrying his mother, then blinded himself and was exiled. Harriet's picture was inspired by an interpretation of the story that played in Paris in 1797, and the museum notes that the subject was popular after the Revolution.","i":"Sophocles' Oedipus at Colonus follows the blind king into exile, led by his devoted daughter Antigone, to a grove near Athens where he finds peace. In France just after the Revolution the subject spoke to the return of exiles, since many émigrés were coming home, and the figure of a blind wanderer guided by a faithful daughter could be seen as a parable of loss and reconciliation. It was also an ideal theme for the austere gravity of the Neoclassical manner.","c":"At twenty, Harriet already commands the Neoclassical manner: clear drawing, a grave composition and restrained gesture. In a subject all pathos, the risk is stiffness, and the picture succeeds where emotion is carried by pose and expression. Harriet died at twenty-nine and fewer than ten of his works survive, so this canvas is a rare, concentrated record of a talent cut short, and its seriousness is a measure of what was lost."},"nga:231465":{"d":"A woman sits with her body turned to the right, hands clasped around her knees, head tilted up toward the ceiling and eyes closed. Her long, wavy hair spills over her shoulders and down her back. Dramatic light picks out her round cheeks and small nose against a dark ground. A white blouse with ruffled sleeves slips from one shoulder, and a purple wrap with a yellow border covers her other shoulder and her legs. The canvas, about 81 by 105 cm, is a single-figure devotional picture of a kind made for private rooms and chapels.","i":"The title names Mary Magdalene, the penitent follower of Christ. In the medieval Golden Legend, angels lifted her up each day during her years of solitary penance so that she could hear heavenly music, and Baroque painters turned that rapture into a subject of its own. Loose hair is among her usual signs. The Counter-Reformation church prized penitence and visionary feeling, and the saint's closed eyes and lifted face invite the viewer to share her absorption, a blend of piety and sensuality that Baroque viewers accepted in her image.","c":"Artemisia keeps the dark ground and strong light of her father's Caravaggesque circle, yet lets the feeling live in the body: the locked hands, the drawn-up knees, the throat bared by the tilted head. The drapery is handled with fluent economy, and the rapture reads as felt, not staged. The type is familiar, and the picture lacks the narrative force of her Judith paintings, but its concentration is real."},"cma:95183":{"d":"A lace maker of Capri is the subject of this tall, narrow canvas, about 160 by 84.5 cm, nearly twice as high as it is wide. The upright format gives a single figure the full height of the picture, a good shape for a standing or seated worker, and the date, 1884, falls in the painter's years in Italy. It is a large, finished picture of a local type, not a commissioned portrait. Lace-making is work for hands and eyes, and painters of the subject, from Vermeer onward, have usually concentrated on the bent head and the busy fingers.","i":"Capri drew painters from Europe and America through the nineteenth century, and local people at their crafts became a steady subject for visitors, who often bought such pictures as souvenirs of an unhurried Italy. Lace-making joined skilled handwork to picturesque charm. Butler studied in the United States and France before spending several years in Italy, where he painted this picture; a left-handed painter, he had lost his right arm fighting for the Union at Gettysburg. The picture is probably best read as a sympathetic record, not as social comment.","c":"Butler's French training and years in Italy place him among the many academic painters who found sure sales in Italian types. A picture like this stands or falls on whether the worker is a person or merely a costume, and the tall format and absorbed handwork give the painter a fair chance. Its claim on attention is partly historical: it was the first painting to enter the Cleveland Museum of Art, in 1915."},"cma:95064":{"d":"A self-portrait by the American portraitist George Peter Alexander Healy, painted in oil in 1852, when he was in his late thirties. The canvas, about 72 by 57 cm, is upright, the usual proportion for a head-and-shoulders or half-length likeness. A self-portrait is normally painted from a mirror, so the sitter's gaze usually turns outward to meet the viewer's. For a painter who lived by commissioned likenesses, such a picture serves both as a record of himself and as a sample of what he could do.","i":"Healy made his career painting statesmen and the wealthy in Europe and the United States; his sitters included the French king Louis-Philippe and Abraham Lincoln. A portraitist's own portrait therefore speaks about professional identity: whether he shows himself as a gentleman, a craftsman or an artist at work, and how much of his public manner he keeps. Such pictures were commonly kept by the family or given to friends and institutions as a calling card. Dated 1852, it belongs to the middle of his long career.","c":"Healy was a fluent, sober portraitist, trained in Paris, whose strength was honest likeness and a clean, dependable finish rather than psychological risk. A self-portrait removes the sitter's demands, so the interest lies in how much of the public manner survives when he is both painter and subject. It belongs to a long line of artists' self-likenesses that double as advertisement, and it is steadier than it is surprising."},"cma:124092":{"d":"A woman in elaborate dress is isolated in a spare setting. Every part of the costume is recorded with its own touch: glittering jewels, filmy lace, knots of ribbon, heavy black silk and shimmering white satin. Ter Borch makes the satin shine with fine flicks and sweeps of the brush, adding and wiping away tiny bits of paint. The canvas, about 63 by 53 cm, is a portrait commission of about 1665, and the plain surroundings leave the viewer's attention on the sitter's cool composure.","i":"The sitter's name is lost, but her costume gives her class: silk, satin, lace and jewels were costly, and a Dutch portrait of the 1660s used them to announce wealth with restraint. Her pendant, a man in equally fashionable dress, is now in the National Gallery, London, so the two were probably husband and wife, hung as a pair. The empty setting is part of the message: what the portrait praises is poise, not possessions.","c":"Ter Borch's reputation rests on his command of surfaces, and the contrast of black silk with white satin, a demanding pairing, shows it. The price is a certain chill: the woman is observed more than felt, and a commissioned likeness leaves less room for the subtle psychology of his letter-writing and gallant scenes. Within its type, though, it is exact and poised, and the satin alone rewards a close look."},"nga:3202":{"d":"A small panel, about 44 by 32 cm, carries a portrait of a woman, dated 1532 and painted in tempera by a German artist whose name has not survived. The museum lists it only as the work of a German painter of the sixteenth century, and the sitter is equally anonymous. German portraits of this date usually show the sitter bust- or half-length against a plain ground, so face, headdress and jewelry carry the likeness. Tempera dries fast and blends less softly than oil, which favors crisp contours.","i":"In the 1530s portraits of private citizens, not just princes and prelates, were an established business in German cities, and women's portraits were commissioned for marriage, betrothal or family record. Dress and headwear were chosen to say as much about standing as the face did. The sitter's name and the patron are unknown, so the picture is best read as a type: the burgher or patrician woman of the early Reformation years, presented as she wished to be seen.","c":"Unnamed German portraits of this date are judged on line: a clean contour, a firm reading of the features, a precise rendering of headdress and ornament. The tempera medium suits that clarity, though not the softer modeling admired in Italy and the Netherlands. It would be unfair to expect Holbein's penetrating intelligence or Cranach's polish from an unknown hand, but the strengths of the genre, directness and exactness, are the ones to look for."},"nga:1101":{"d":"A family fills a domestic interior. A woman in a red dress and red bonnet sits at the center with a baby on her lap; a man leans on a wooden desk at the left, and another stands behind her chair, looking down at her. An elderly woman in a white bonnet sits by the desk, with two girls reading a book beside her. On the right a girl crouches beside a smaller one holding a paper, and a black poodle sits among the children. Red curtains are drawn back to show a landscape through a window. The canvas is about 75 by 63.5 cm.","i":"The picture comes from the German Biedermeier years, when the family circle was a favorite subject. The title says the group is an artist's own family, and the man at the desk with the sheet of paper may be the painter himself. Three generations, children reading and holding papers, and a faithful dog describe a home of learning, affection and order, and the drawn curtain borrows the grand portrait's formula for private purposes.","c":"The painter's task is to give ten or so figures room on a canvas about 75 cm high, and the answer is touch: an arm linked, a hand on a chair back, a girl crouched to a smaller one. These gestures tie the groups together and keep the scene from turning into a row of heads, and the reds of dress and curtain hold the center. The painter is unnamed, but the household warmth is convincing."},"met:436480":{"d":"A man is shown in an oil portrait painted on a small panel of linden wood, about 48 by 33 cm, and dated 1491. The painter is an anonymous artist of Nuremberg, active in the late fifteenth century. Panel portraits of this size from southern Germany usually show the sitter bust- or half-length, close to the picture surface and against a plain ground, so that face and dress do all the work. Linden, a fine-grained wood that paints cleanly, was a favorite support in the region.","i":"Nuremberg in 1491 was a rich imperial city and an artistic center, where Albrecht Dürer, born there in 1471, had trained with the painter Michael Wolgemut. Merchants and patricians bought portraits as well as altarpieces, and oil, a Netherlandish technique, had by then reached south German workshops. The sitter's name is not recorded, and nothing can be said of his trade or rank beyond what his dress and bearing suggest; such a likeness was made for family and friends.","c":"Nuremberg portraiture of this date prized a direct, unidealized likeness, and the tradition would reach a new intensity in Dürer's early portraits, made within a few years of this panel. An unnamed hand cannot be asked to match them, but the virtues of the school are the ones to look for here: sharp contour, honest observation and no flattery. The limits are those of the type, which leaves little room for setting or gesture."},"met:437466":{"d":"Christ appears as the Salvator Mundi, the Savior of the World, raising his hand in blessing, with a family of donors, men and women, gathered around him. The panel is tall and narrow, about 81 by 37 cm, painted in oil with gold on oak. The Met places it in northern Germany, probably Hamburg or Lower Saxony, between about 1573 and 1582. Format and subject suggest a memorial or devotional picture for a church or family chapel, in which the donors are placed within reach of Christ's blessing rather than before a saint.","i":"Even after the Reformation, families in Lutheran north Germany wished to be remembered in church, and many did so by placing themselves directly before Christ instead of before the saints. The Salvator Mundi, who blesses and holds the world, is a fitting image of salvation by faith, and the donors are shown as people who rely on him. Memorial pictures of this kind often carry inscriptions naming the family. Whether this panel was an epitaph, or part of a larger ensemble, is uncertain.","c":"Provincial devotional painting of the 1570s seldom aimed beyond clarity, and a narrow panel allows only a simple arrangement: Christ as the visible center, the family tied to him by place and scale. The donors, painted from life, are usually the liveliest part of such pictures, while the figure of Christ follows an inherited type. The interest is historical, in how Protestant patrons reworked the donor portrait, more than inventive."},"met:436246":{"d":"A woman with a low neckline is painted in oil on a small panel of linden, about 45 by 35 cm. The title calls her Italian, yet the painter is a German active in the first third of the sixteenth century, and linden was a favorite wood of south German workshops. The picture is therefore a German view of an Italian sitter, or of an Italian fashion, probably made in or after contact with Italy. The panel is of the usual size for a bust- or half-length portrait, made for a private owner rather than a church.","i":"German painters of Dürer's generation looked south. Dürer painted a Venetian woman in 1505, on his second visit to Venice, and Italian dress, hairstyles and ideals of beauty were studied north of the Alps. A portrait of an Italian woman by a German hand may record a sitter living in a German trading city, or a painter on the road in Italy; the title does not settle which. Her name and the picture's purpose are unrecorded, and the low neckline marks fashion more than character.","c":"Dürer's portrait of a Venetian woman, of 1505, is the obvious yardstick for any German picture of an Italian sitter, and an unnamed painter cannot expect to reach it. Hybrids of this kind are risky: a painter schooled in the crisp German contour who reaches for Italian softness can end with neither. As evidence of exchange between north and south the panel has value; as an individual likeness it keeps its secrets."},"met:437636":{"d":"In a parquet-floored room a woman works at lace, a dog is near her, and a straw hat trimmed with a feather and a bow appears among the household objects. The museum's subject keywords also record a man and the act of reading, so the scene probably joins handwork and a book in one quiet interior. The canvas is small, about 46 by 38 cm, and dates from the late 1770s; it is a genre picture made for a private room, and its pleasure lies in the accessories of a well-kept home.","i":"Domestic scenes of the 1770s turned household tasks into gentle moral pictures. Lace-making stood for a woman's patient industry, reading for cultivated leisure, the dog for fidelity, and the tidy parquet for a well-kept home. German painters drew on Dutch pictures of the previous century and on French painters such as Chardin and Greuze, whose engravings circulated widely. The fashionable hat with feather and bow adds a note of vanity to the virtue.","c":"The subject is built for texture: lace, straw, feather and wood are exactly what this genre exists to show, and the unnamed painter shares the period's love of small domestic pictures. The story is slight and the moral lightly stated, which is both its charm and its limit; it lacks the concentration of Chardin or the psychological bite of the Dutch masters it echoes. A pleasant, modest cabinet picture."},"met:436460":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted on a thin plaque of ivory by an unknown German artist in the 1790s. Miniatures of this kind are small, often oval, and made to be held in the hand, worn as a locket or kept in a case. Ivory takes thin washes of watercolor and lets its pale surface glow through the flesh tones, which gives the faces of the period their soft light. The head and shoulders are the whole subject, usually set against a plain or lightly clouded ground.","i":"A miniature was a private likeness, not a public one: given to a wife, betrothed or friend, or kept by a family as a record of a husband or father. Around 1790 to 1800 the fashion was at its height across Europe, and German courts and trading cities supported many miniature painters. The sitter here is unnamed, so dress and hair are the evidence that remains, and they show how a man of the 1790s chose to look when he was painted for those closest to him.","c":"Unsigned German miniatures of the 1790s usually follow English and French models: smooth faces, soft modeling and a restrained palette. The best catch a living expression in a few touches; the average repeats a formula. Without a known sitter or painter, this one is best valued as a well-made period likeness, small, quiet and typical, rather than for any individual invention."},"met:436461":{"d":"A woman wearing a bracelet is portrayed on a thin plaque of ivory, a miniature painted by an unnamed German artist around 1790 to 1800. The delicate support suits the subject: a fine brush and thin washes build up the face, and the ivory shows through to give the complexion a pale, translucent warmth. The bracelet is the one accessory noted for the picture, a small fashionable touch beside the face, which is the real subject of any miniature.","i":"A woman's miniature of the 1790s was often a gift, from husband to wife, from a betrothed to a bride, or from a daughter to a parent, worn as jewelry or kept in a case with other keepsakes. The bracelet places her in the same world of personal ornament, since miniatures were themselves often set into bracelets and lockets. The sitter is unnamed, so her station can be guessed only from dress and accessories, and a bracelet does not settle it.","c":"The likeness and the ivory's glow are the things to judge, and a competent German miniaturist of the 1790s could deliver both with a restrained palette and soft modeling. Such portraits seldom aim at drama; their appeal is closeness, since they were made to be held and looked at from a few inches away. This one, by an unknown hand, is best taken as a quiet, well-mannered example of its type."},"met:436462":{"d":"A child wearing a bracelet is the subject of this miniature, painted on a thin plaque of ivory around 1790 to 1800 by an unnamed German artist. Children's portraits in this format were small keepsakes, easy to hold in the palm or set in a case, and the ivory's pale, faintly translucent surface lends young skin a natural softness. Everything depends on very fine brushwork, since the whole face occupies a tiny area. A bracelet on the child's wrist is the one accessory noted for the picture.","i":"In the 1790s a miniature kept a child close to relatives during travel, schooling or illness, and it sometimes became a memorial when a child died young, as many did. Nothing here says which was the case. The bracelet shows a child dressed for the sitting. Taste was also changing: after Rousseau's writings on education, children were increasingly shown as natural, unconstrained beings in simpler dress, rather than as small adults.","c":"Children are difficult sitters for a miniaturist, who must catch a likeness quickly and keep a soft, unformed face alive at a very small scale. Ivory helps, and competent painters of the 1790s had a sure formula for the smooth, rosy look families wanted. Pictures like this are pleasing rather than penetrating, valued for affection more than for invention, and the anonymous hand does not change that."},"met:436463":{"d":"A girl wearing a bracelet is shown in this small portrait on ivory, painted around 1790 to 1800 by an unnamed German artist. As in other miniatures of the time, the support is a thin, pale plaque, and the painter works with fine brushes and thin color so that the ivory glows through the skin tones. Portraits of girls often stress a fresh complexion and simple dress. A bracelet is the single accessory noted for this picture, a small touch of finery on a child.","i":"A miniature of a girl in the 1790s most likely served as a family keepsake, kept by parents or sent to relatives, and perhaps as a memorial if she died young. Dress is the clue to the date: the simple, loose styles of the 1790s replaced the stiff, adult costume of earlier children's portraits, and a bracelet is a small touch of finery within them. Sitter, patron and purpose are unrecorded.","c":"Competent but anonymous, this miniature belongs to a small field with clear standards: a recognizable likeness, an unfussy palette and a smooth, luminous finish. Where the best miniaturists of the day found a particular glance or turn of the head, many settled for a pleasant formula, and a child's likeness invites the formula more than most. The charm is modest and sincere, and that is enough for the type."},"met:436464":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man on an ivory plaque, painted in the 1790s by an unknown German hand. The object is meant to be held close: a tiny, finely worked likeness kept in a case or set in a frame, often alongside other family portraits. The painter's task is to make a very small face read as a particular person, with thin washes over the white ivory building the flesh tones and a few fine strokes marking hair and features.","i":"Men's miniatures of the 1790s record a fast-changing fashion: powdered hair and elaborate coats were giving way to natural hair and plainer dress as the old court styles fell out of favor in the years around the French Revolution. A miniature like this might be exchanged between friends, given to a betrothed, or kept by a family. The sitter is unnamed, so hair and collar are the main clues to date and taste.","c":"The painter's skill lies in economy: a few touches must carry the character of a face. At best that can be quietly convincing, and the cool taste of the 1790s for plain grounds helps. The anonymous hand and the formulaic type limit the claims that can be made, but the small scale, the clean modeling and the direct view of the sitter give pleasure of a modest kind."},"met:436465":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory, painted by an unnamed German artist between about 1790 and 1800 and titled for its sitter, Caroline Maria Therese of Parma, who lived from 1770 to 1804. She would therefore have been between about twenty and thirty when it was made. Like other miniatures of the period, it is small, meant to be held in the hand, worn or kept in a case, and the painter builds the face in thin, fine strokes over the pale ivory so that the skin glows.","i":"The name points to the Bourbon-Parma princess of those dates, probably the one who married Prince Maximilian of Saxony and became the mother of two later kings of Saxony. Court miniatures were a currency of dynastic life: likenesses of princesses and princes passed between families when marriages were arranged and were kept as tokens of absent relatives. Such pictures were judged on recognizability and decorum as much as on art.","c":"Court miniatures reward the eye with polish: smooth skin, a composed pose and a clear sense of rank. An unnamed hand working to that formula tends to produce a dignified, slightly generic beauty, and the likeness is hard to judge without comparison to securely documented portraits. The interest here is above all the sitter's history, together with the small, careful finish typical of court work."},"met:436466":{"d":"A child wearing a bracelet appears in a very small ivory portrait by an unknown German hand, painted around 1790 to 1800. Such pictures were made to be seen from a few inches away, and everything depends on delicacy: fine strokes for the face, a plain or softly shaded ground behind it, a bracelet at the wrist as a small mark of fashion. The ivory itself, thin, pale and slightly translucent, supplies the luminous quality that makes the skin look alive.","i":"In an age of slow travel a miniature let a child be known to relatives who might never meet in person, and it could be posted in a letter or small parcel across a country or a continent. The bracelet shows a child dressed for the occasion. Who commissioned the picture, and for whom, is not recorded, so it is best read as one of many small family records of the 1790s, each of them personal and none of them public.","c":"A good children's miniature balances sweetness with observation, and the type tends to favor the first. The anonymous painter works within the settled conventions of the 1790s, smooth modeling, a restrained palette and a plain ground, and none of that is a fault. The delicate handling that the medium demands is a real skill, and an unaffected likeness is no small thing in a form that often slid into prettiness."},"met:436467":{"d":"A child wearing a bracelet is shown on a small plaque of ivory, painted by an unnamed German artist around 1790 to 1800. Ivory miniatures are built up in tiny strokes and stipples of watercolor, bound with gum, over a smooth, polished plaque. The ivory is more than a support: its white surface, left to show through thin color, supplies the highlights of the skin. A bracelet is the one accessory noted for the picture, a small touch of finery.","i":"Children's portraits were among the most valued of keepsakes, and a miniature could travel where a larger painting could not: to a grandparent in another city, to a parent away on service, or into a locket. Nothing records the sitter's name or whether the picture marked a birthday, a departure or a loss. What can be said with confidence is the date, the 1790s, and the type, a family portrait made to be held and kept.","c":"In a child's portrait the test is whether the face is a particular child's or a general idea of childhood, round-cheeked and rosy. At this scale and in this medium the pull toward the general is strong, and unnamed miniaturists of the 1790s seldom resisted it entirely. The result is gentle, immaculate and anonymous, which is what the type asks for and also what it limits."},"met:436469":{"d":"A woman in a cap, with a fichu, a light kerchief, draped around her neck and shoulders and a necklace at her throat, is painted in miniature on ivory around 1810 by an unnamed German artist. The headwear and kerchief belong to a modest, respectable style of dress rather than a court one. As in other ivory miniatures, thin watercolor over the pale support gives a smooth, luminous complexion, and the whole picture is made to be held in the hand.","i":"A cap usually marked a married or older woman of the time, and a fichu added modesty. Around 1810 women's dress in the German lands was moving from the high-waisted, neoclassical line of the 1790s toward the softer domestic fashion that would become Biedermeier. The necklace is a single ornament. The sitter is unnamed, and such a miniature was most likely made for her family or friends, to be kept close, not displayed.","c":"Anonymous and dated only to about 1810, this is a modest piece, and it succeeds as a keepsake more than as art. The strengths of the ivory miniature at this date are clean modeling and a pearly flesh tone; the weakness is a drift toward smooth sameness as the fashion spread to less gifted painters. Its interest lies in the cap, fichu and necklace, a record of how a woman of the day chose to be seen."},"met:436470":{"d":"A man is portrayed in miniature on ivory, painted by an unnamed German artist around 1810. At this date men's dress had turned plain and dark: a high collar, a white neckcloth and short, unpowdered hair were the fashion of the Napoleonic years, and a bust-length miniature normally concentrates on the face, the collar and the upper part of the coat. The ivory support lets the painter keep the flesh tones pale and luminous, with the face as the one fully worked area.","i":"The years around 1810 were a time of war and upheaval in the German lands, with Napoleon's campaigns reshaping states and borders until 1815. A miniature of the date could mark a betrothal, a departure for service or simply a family record, and was kept as a token for those left at home. The sitter here is unnamed, and nothing in the record points to a profession or rank.","c":"A competent period miniature, and plain in the way the taste of 1810 was plain: no court trappings, a sober coat, attention fixed on the face. The best miniaturists of the day gave such faces unmistakable character, and the average settled for a smooth, agreeable likeness. Without a name for the sitter or the painter the picture offers the type's quiet virtues, small scale and clear modeling, more than any personal signature."},"met:436471":{"d":"A man is shown in a portrait miniature on ivory, painted by an unnamed German artist about 1815 and, according to the Met's title, said to portray the naturalist and explorer Alexander von Humboldt, who lived from 1769 to 1859. If the identification is correct, the sitter would have been in his mid-forties. Like other ivory miniatures, it is small and finely worked, with thin washes of color over the pale support producing a clear, smooth complexion.","i":"Humboldt had returned in 1804 from five years in Spanish America, where he and Aimé Bonpland explored the Orinoco and climbed Chimborazo to an altitude then unmatched. By 1815 he was living in Paris, writing up the results, and was one of the most celebrated scientists in Europe, so admirers and family might well have wanted his likeness. The words said to be are a caution: traditional identifications of miniatures often cannot be confirmed.","c":"Identification is the weak point: miniatures of famous men attract hopeful labels, and a said-to-be needs comparison with documented likenesses. Judged as a picture, it offers the usual virtues of the type, polish and economy, and little that singles out the painter. A confirmed name would raise its interest; unconfirmed, it remains a competent likeness of a man who may or may not be the explorer."},"met:441363":{"d":"A man engaged in writing is the subject of this small oil painting, about 35 by 27 cm, by an unidentified German painter of the nineteenth century. The title casts him as an Arab sage, a learned elder, and the Met's keywords add the Orientalist label. The picture is a character study of a type rather than the portrait of a named sitter, in the vein of the European taste for imagined scholars, scribes and holy men of the Middle East, usually painted in the studio with costumes and props.","i":"In the nineteenth century European painters turned to the Middle East and North Africa, whether by travel or from the studio, and the learned elder, whether sage, scribe or teacher, became a stock figure standing for wisdom, piety and an unchanging past. Writing signals learning and authority. The picture should be read as a European view of the East, shaped by Western fantasy and taste, and not as a record of Arab life or of a real person.","c":"As a small oil of a type, the interest depends on the face and on whether the painter observed a man or merely costumed one. The Orientalist sage was a stock figure, and a critical eye asks how much of the picture is observation and how much is prop. Its small size, the concentration on a single writer and the quiet subject give it a better chance than most."},"cma:143803":{"d":"Three figures press close together in a night scene lit by a single candle: Samson, asleep in Delilah's lap at the moment his hair, the source of his strength, is lost; Delilah herself; and an old woman who makes a hushing gesture. The canvas, about 129 by 94 cm, is built on a stark contrast of light and dark, the single flame lighting the closely packed figures and leaving the rest of the space to the night.","i":"The story is from the Book of Judges. Samson, whose uncut hair marked his vow to God and gave him his strength, had been the scourge of the Philistines, who bribed his lover Delilah to find his secret. Viewers read the scene as a warning about deceit and about the folly of trusting seduction. The old woman's silencing gesture reaches out to the viewer, making the viewer a witness to the plot and, in effect, an accomplice.","c":"Honthorst understands that a night scene works by compression: three figures, one flame, nothing else. The hushing old woman, who addresses the viewer, is the picture's sharpest invention, turning a story into an act of complicity. The painting is theatrical rather than psychological, and the candle effect can feel like a device, but its dramatic economy and physical closeness are impressive in a work made around 1616."},"nga:117333":{"d":"A pouch seems to hang from the wall, painted in the trompe-l'oeil manner for which Gijsbrechts is known, with mirrored glass combined with oil on a tall, narrow panel of about 67 by 26.5 cm. The format imitates a real object of the kind hung on a wall to hold small possessions. In pictures of this kind proportions, cast shadow and a flat, frontal view all serve the illusion of a real thing standing out from the wall.","i":"Trompe l'oeil, French for deceiving the eye, flourished in Flanders and the Netherlands as a joke and as a demonstration of skill. The old story behind it is Pliny's account of the Greek painters Zeuxis, whose grapes fooled the birds, and Parrhasius, whose painted curtain fooled Zeuxis himself. Pictures of racks and pouches also hint at vanitas, the passing of possessions and time, and a mirror, if it reflects the viewer, adds the viewer's own face to that thought.","c":"Gijsbrechts is among the finest of the Baroque illusionists, and the interest of a work like this lies in the conviction of texture and shadow and in the odd presence of a real mirror, in which the viewer is caught in the act of looking. The limit of trompe l'oeil is its single joke: once the trick is understood the picture has little more to say. His best works turn the trick into a thought about what a picture is."},"cma:138360":{"d":"A small painting on copper, about 38 by 54 cm, opens a wide landscape with woodland and Germanic architecture rather than a classical setting. In the foreground, Venus, the goddess of love, tries without success to dissuade her mortal lover Adonis from going hunting; the boar he will meet will kill him. Copper is a smooth, hard support that holds fine detail and gives color a glassy depth, and it was favored for small cabinet pictures meant for close viewing.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses. Venus, struck by love for the mortal Adonis, warns him to avoid dangerous beasts, but he hunts anyway and is killed by a boar. The moment shown is the doomed warning, a scene of foreboding that educated viewers would recognize at once. Setting the myth in a northern European landscape instead of an antique one made it familiar, and the woodland around the figures was, as the museum notes, a relatively new subject in European painting.","c":"Coninxloo is a figure of transition: he inherits the high, panoramic landscape of the older Flemish painters and begins to move toward the enclosed, forested scenes that would dominate the following century. The lovers are small within their world, which suits his real interest, the trees and the distance. Its importance lies partly in what it points to, though the copper's clear color is a pleasure in itself."},"cma:122339":{"d":"The Virgin and Child sit in a landscape with a bearded Saint Anthony Abbot and a female saint whose hand is empty, so that her usual attribute, and her name, are lost. Two donors kneel with clasped hands in the same open countryside, sharing the space of the holy figures. The panel is a horizontal oil on wood, about 56 by 79 cm, of a size made for private devotion, and it was never finished: the beard and the empty hand show it.","i":"The picture belongs to the Venetian sacra conversazione, the holy conversation in which saints share one space with the Virgin and Child. The tenderness of mother and son invited empathy with Christ and spoke of Mary as intercessor for humanity. The donors' place in the same landscape as the saints emphasizes how her compassion extends into the viewer's world. Because the female saint's attribute was never painted, her identity cannot be pinned down.","c":"Cima, formed in the circle of Giovanni Bellini, is the poet of the calm devotional picture and of the hills of his native Veneto. In this late work he follows his established formulas more than he tests them, but placing the donors in the holy landscape is a gentle, humane solution. The unfinished passages, the beard and the empty hand, let the viewer see the work in progress."},"cma:144302":{"d":"A compact oil sketch, about 67 by 55 cm, gathers the four prophets Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel and Daniel, with young putti, inside a space shaped for a pendentive, the inverted triangle where a dome meets its supports. It is a bozzetto, a preparatory sketch made to work out composition and palette before the fresco, and one of seven made for the four pendentives of the Jesuit church of the Gesù in Rome. Gaulli later changed the composition for the final fresco.","i":"The prophets foretold the Messiah, and in a church dedicated to the name of Jesus their place beneath the dome ties Old Testament prophecy to the glory above. In the finished fresco the young Daniel is the most prominent figure and motions toward the dome. Fresco is unforgiving, painted in sections onto wet plaster, so thorough preparation was essential, and oil sketches helped the artist test his ideas in an awkward, curved space.","c":"A bozzetto is the artist thinking aloud, and the interest here is to see a decorator who knows his space. Gaulli, called Baciccio, was one of the most prominent large-scale painters in Rome, famous for the vault of the Gesù, and the sketch shows how carefully the apparent ease of Roman Baroque ceilings was planned. It is a document of process, rather than a self-contained picture, but one that repays study."},"cma:128375":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 200 by 149 cm, shows Saint Jerome's vision after his withdrawal from the world, in a Caravaggesque manner: naturalistic bodies strongly lit against shadow. The scene follows a convention in placing the vision in Jerome's retreat, not in the Roman city where he had studied, and its drama depends on the way the figures are modeled by light.","i":"Jerome (about 347 to 420) studied Greek and Latin literature in Rome, then turned to theology, seeing his own conduct as corrupt. Around 374 he had a vision of the Last Judgment in which an angel called him to account, and he withdrew, learned Hebrew and studied the Gospels, leading to his influential translations of the Bible. In the Baroque period he was a favorite patron of scholars and an image of penitence, and viewers would have read the vision as a lesson in turning from worldly learning to God.","c":"Langetti, born in Genoa and working in the wake of Caravaggio and Ribera, is a master of the muscular, shadowed figure, and the naturalism of the bodies and the harsh light give the vision real weight. His limits are a certain monotony of dark tonality and a roughness of handling that became a fashion among the Venetian tenebrosi. At its best, as here, the effect is stern and physical."},"cma:150955":{"d":"The Virgin and Child sit at the center while saints and an angel present a devout woman at the lower right. Clockwise from her are Saint Lucy, with a cauldron and sword at her feet; Saint Agnes, with her lamb; Saint Scholastica, with a dove; and Saint Benedict, with crozier and habit. Saint Veronica, her head covered, sits slightly below the Madonna, and behind her Saint Anthony of Padua, in a Franciscan habit and holding a lily, stands in adoration. The canvas is about 172 by 136 cm.","i":"The picture is a visual petition: a woman, possibly the patron who paid for it, is introduced to the Virgin by saints she can trust as intercessors, as a petitioner might be presented at court. Why these saints were chosen probably reflects her own devotions, her family or her religious order, but her identity is uncertain. Each saint carries a sign, cauldron, lamb, dove or lily, that viewers of the time would read at a glance.","c":"Pittoni's study of French Rococo softens Baroque drama: the light is milder, the colors richer, the drapery elegant. The result is graceful but crowded; ten figures press toward the Virgin, and the emotional heat of the subject cools into ceremony. The visible third front leg of Agnes's lamb, painted over by the artist and now returning through the paint, is a reminder that even this polished Venetian professional revised as he went."},"cma:144255":{"d":"Abraham, the patriarch, secures a solemn pledge from his servant that he will find a wife for Isaac. The servant wears a zamt, a goatskin cap common in North Africa in the 1600s, here dyed an unusual blue, and a musical instrument at the left foreshadows the wedding. The canvas, about 56 by 78 cm, is a horizontal cabinet picture. For a long time it was known as Journey of a Patriarch, in the belief that the artist intended no specific subject.","i":"The scene is from the Book of Genesis. Abraham, old and anxious that Isaac should not marry among the local Canaanites, makes his trusted servant, whom later tradition names Eliezer, swear to seek a bride among his own kin; the quest leads to Rebekah. The blue of the servant's cap is a color associated with faith and trust, signaling the sacred oath, and the instrument stands for love and the coming marriage.","c":"Castiglione, a Genoese painter and etcher, was fond of biblical stories told in a pastoral key, and this picture shows his taste for incident and symbolic detail over rhetoric. The fact that its subject was misread for years suggests how quietly the oath is conveyed: through color, costume and gesture, not grand drama. It is a small, subtle picture that rewards a viewer who knows the story and looks closely."},"cma:130132":{"d":"Two monumental figures, Christ and Joseph of Arimathea, fill the front of a wide panel. Apart from an amorphous, cloudy sky, Savoldo removed the background entirely and pushed the figures to the front of the picture plane. The panel, about 105 by 192 cm, was painted around 1525 as the crowning piece of a large altarpiece, which explains its horizontal shape, its unusual perspective and the high position of the figures above the viewer.","i":"Joseph of Arimathea, a rich man and member of the council, asked for Christ's body after the Crucifixion and laid it in his own new tomb. Reduced to two figures, the story becomes a quiet meditation on sacrifice, fitting for the top of an altarpiece above the place where the Eucharist was celebrated. The museum suggests that Savoldo's bare, close-up manner had a great impact on Caravaggio, who trained in northern Italy, where Savoldo spent his career.","c":"Savoldo's reduction, two bodies, no setting, a sky that hardly counts, gives the scene a stark gravity that most Renaissance entombments crowd with mourners. The picture asks to be judged with its original viewing angle in mind: hung low at eye level, away from the altarpiece it crowned, it loses some of its logic. Even so, its concentration and silence anticipate the Baroque more than the rhetoric of its own time."},"cma:97156":{"d":"An old man stares out with intense attention, his face unidealized and marked by carefully observed wrinkles. In his right hand he grasps what may be a letter with its paper seal broken. His costume appears plain, but the black fabric is sumptuous and was a sign of high status in some parts of Europe. The panel is upright, about 92 by 64 cm. For all the intensity of the gaze, the mood stays distant and reserved.","i":"Campi belonged to a distinguished family of painters in northern Italy, and he painted this sitter, whose name is not known, in the mid-sixteenth century. The reserve fits Renaissance ideas of elegant composure, which valued restraint over display of feeling, and black cloth was the sober color of rank and gravity. The broken seal on the letter hints at correspondence and business, the world of a man of affairs, though the picture does not say what news it carries.","c":"Campi's honesty about age is the strength of the picture: the face is built from wrinkles patiently observed, with no flattery. The reserve is its limit. The sitter observes the viewer without engaging, and he seems farther off than the sitters of a painter like Moroni, who could make a stern face warm. The correct reading, perhaps, is that Campi respects the period's code of composure more than he tests it."},"met:872016":{"d":"A man sits asleep on a very narrow panel, about 26 by 12 cm, painted in oil on wood around 1865. The tall, slender format and the small size suggest a quick, informal study rather than a finished exhibition picture, and they are typical of the little panels on which the Italian painters called the Macchiaioli recorded scenes of everyday life. The painter's subject is the figure at rest, a man who cannot pose and cannot notice that he is being watched.","i":"Abbati belonged to the Macchiaioli, the Tuscan painters of the mid-nineteenth century who built pictures from patches, macchie, of light and shadow, and who painted ordinary life in plain terms instead of following academic finish. Many of them fought in the movement for Italian unity. A sleeper is a private subject, and the sitter is anonymous, so this is probably a study from life and not a commissioned portrait. Abbati died in 1868, at the age of thirty-two.","c":"The Macchiaioli prized the unposed moment, and a sleeper is the ideal sitter. At this size and pace the worth of the picture lies in economy: a few masses of tone must make a figure and hint at weight and rest. It stands modestly beside Abbati's landscapes and courtyards, where his feeling for sunlit walls is better known, but it shows the same habit of seeing the world as simplified patches of light."},"cma:150068":{"d":"A lion looks upward, eyes gleaming and mouth open, a head study with an air of wild elegance. The handling is sketchy, in short brushstrokes, and the thick mane is the most striking part: its longest tresses are undefined planes of black, which grow lighter toward the face into chestnut shades with shadows and highlights. Creamy white marks the muzzle, bright wet patches mark the nose and mouth, and a glimmer in the right eye gives the animal life. The canvas is about 80 by 64 cm.","i":"A later signature once attributed this study to the French animal painter Jean-Baptiste Oudry, but it is the work of the Italian Giuseppe Baldrighi, court artist to the Dukes of Parma. Sent to Paris in 1752, Baldrighi studied with leading French painters, Oudry among them, and made animal studies in Oudry's manner, drawn from Oudry's method of painting heads of animals in expressive poses. The lion, a traditional emblem of courage and rule, suited a court painter's repertoire.","c":"A competent, vigorous study whose interest lies in the mane and the glint in the eye. Its limits are the ones that separate Baldrighi from his master: a blunter touch, a more linear use of paint, and little psychological depth in the animal's face. That bluntness is also its energy, since Oudry's smoothness rarely allows such a rough, immediate look. The lion is a handsome specimen more than a presence."},"cma:148959":{"d":"A woman of austere dignity looks out of a panel about 72 by 60 cm, painted in 1646. Flinck captures her individuality through a careful description of her expression and dress, and the museum finds in her an emotional complexity beneath the formality. An inscription on the back long led to her being taken for the scholar Anna Maria van Schurman, but the words were added much later and misstate the age, and the face does not resemble known portraits of that famous woman.","i":"Her name is lost. The false inscription shows how fame attaches itself to anonymous portraits, since a likeness of an admired woman is more valuable than one of an unknown sitter. Flinck, a former pupil of Rembrandt, had become one of the most popular portraitists in Amsterdam in the 1640s, and his sitters came from the wealthy classes. The sober, dignified bearing here is characteristic of Dutch portraits of respectable women in these years.","c":"Flinck learned from Rembrandt but built his own market with a smoother, more elegant finish. Joachim Sandrart said as much when he found Flinck's portraits more felicitous than Rembrandt's in exactness and pleasing quality, and that is both the praise and the limit: this portrait is convincing and well made, but it has less inwardness than Rembrandt's sitters of the same decade. As a dignified, intelligent likeness it needs no famous name."},"met:436578":{"d":"A small Greek icon, about 32 by 21 cm, painted in tempera on wood against a gold ground. Christ, the Virgin Mary and Saint John the Baptist are among the figures, and the title All Saints shows that they stand with a larger company of the blessed. Icons of this kind typically show the holy figures frontally and in ranks, with flat color, firm outline and gold in place of landscape, so that the saints appear in a heavenly space and not an earthly one.","i":"All Saints is kept in the Orthodox church on the Sunday after Pentecost, honoring all the saints, known and unknown. Christ with the Virgin and John the Baptist beside him forms the Deesis, the group that intercedes for humanity, and the surrounding saints join in that prayer. The Met dates the icon only as possibly of the eighteenth century, a period in which Greek icon painters still followed Byzantine patterns while sometimes borrowing Western details.","c":"An icon is not judged by novelty: the painter works from established patterns, and the aim is recognition, not surprise. Its virtues are clarity, balance and the glow of the gold ground; its discipline lies in line and color kept in order. The anonymous hand and the uncertain date limit what more can be said, but the picture shows how long the Byzantine tradition lived on in Greek lands."},"met:437423":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 268 by 185 cm, shows the Virgin Mary with angels in the guise of the Immaculate Conception. In the usual form of this subject the Virgin is raised on clouds above a crescent moon, with a crown of stars and angels or putti about her, her gaze turned toward heaven. The scale is that of an altarpiece or a major devotional picture for a chapel or palace, and the date, 1627, falls in the middle of Reni's career.","i":"The Immaculate Conception is the belief that Mary was conceived free of original sin. It was fiercely defended in Spain and Italy in the seventeenth century, long before it became dogma in 1854, and artists gave it an image from the Book of Revelation: the woman clothed with the sun, with the moon under her feet and twelve stars on her head. Reni's idealized beauty suits it, since in this tradition the beauty of the figure points to her purity.","c":"Reni's gift is serene beauty and a flowing, sure line, learned from Raphael and the Carracci and set against Caravaggio's naturalism. In a subject that asks for purity and ideal form he is at his best, and the scale lets the figure carry the viewer upward. The risk is the sweetness that later critics found cloying, an elevation that can slide into the generic. Here the discipline of the design holds that in check."},"met:437422":{"d":"A woman, the personification of Charity, nurses a child with other children gathered about her. The canvas, about 137 by 106 cm, was painted around 1630, and the subject, an allegory of a Christian virtue, is told through the oldest of images, a mother with her young. Because the figure is an allegory, not a portrait, the artist can idealize her face and form, and the scale of the picture gives the group a calm, monumental presence.","i":"Charity, love of one's neighbor, is one of the three theological virtues with Faith and Hope, and since the Middle Ages she has been shown as a woman nursing or surrounded by children. Maternal love, giving without return, is the image of selfless care. Baroque Bologna produced many versions of the subject, and the viewer was meant to read in the nursing mother both a human feeling and the divine love that it stands for.","c":"Charity is a Madonna in secular dress, and Reni, the painter of idealized mothers, has the gift for it: the grouping is clear and the modeling smooth and sure. The weakness is the one his critics always find, a composure so complete that the virtue feels more like a diagram than a lived feeling. As a statement of the Bolognese ideal of beauty serving a moral idea, though, it is well made and persuasive."},"cma:144303":{"d":"A very large altarpiece, about 367 by 269 cm, was still in Reni's studio when he died in 1642, and it is visibly unfinished. Some figures lack color, the wooden structure in the scene was never completed, and the silvery ground layer shows everywhere, along with his changes to the design, such as the dog's head above the angel's right knee and an adjustment to Christ's right leg, covered in blue. The subject is the Adoration of the Magi.","i":"The Adoration of the Magi, from the Gospel of Matthew, shows the visitors from the East bringing gifts to the infant Christ, a revelation of the child to the wider world. Reni's death does not fully explain the unfinished look: toward the end of his career he increasingly painted in a loose, sketchy manner, popular with collectors, which implied that solid forms could never fully represent the ideal, spiritual world he aimed to present.","c":"Left unfinished, the picture shows late Reni in an unusually naked form: a silvery ground, forms barely stated, color held back. Collectors prized this sketchiness, and the beauty here is half design and half accident. As an altarpiece it never became the finished public image intended, but as a statement of a painter who distrusted solid form it is revealing, and the visible changes show him thinking."},"cma:97145":{"d":"A small devotional canvas, about 41 by 35 cm, shows the central group of Guido Reni's 1608 fresco of Saint Andrew led to martyrdom: the saint in adoration of the cross, with three other figures close around him. The cross, which stands in the distance in the fresco, is brought to the foreground, and it is a Latin cross rather than the X-shaped cross of Andrew's tradition. A figure at the left bends in an awkward pose.","i":"Andrew, the brother of Saint Peter, left everything to follow Christ and was martyred in Achaea. He asked to be crucified on an X-shaped cross because he thought himself unworthy to die on the same kind of cross as Christ. This version focuses on his adoration of the cross, and its nearness emphasizes his relationship with God and his acceptance of death. The museum treats it as the work of an anonymous seventeenth-century artist, perhaps Venetian, after Reni.","c":"As a derivative it lacks the crowd and the precision of Reni's fresco, and the bending figure at the left shows the hand of a lesser draftsman. Its virtue is concentration: by removing the crowd and bringing the cross forward, the copyist makes the saint's acceptance the whole subject. The result is not Reni, but it shows how his compositions traveled, and how an editor can sharpen a design."},"nga:214119":{"d":"A single peach, painted on a very small canvas of about 20 by 16 cm in 1866. The title names the Spanish city of Seville, so the fruit was presumably painted there. Hall, an American specialist in fruit and flowers, treats a small single object as a complete picture: one fruit, isolated from any table setting or company, with attention concentrated on its color, its soft skin and the way light falls on it.","i":"Small fruit studies like this were exercises in looking and also souvenirs of travel. Hall, known for his fruit and flower pictures, painted such studies in Europe, and the place name works as a traveler's note on a sketch. In the United States after the Civil War, small, finely observed still lifes found buyers who hung them in domestic rooms.","c":"Hall's small fruit pieces are best taken as exercises in observation: one object, one surface, one light. The format asks for little invention, and the test is whether the bloom and blush of the skin are caught with sensitivity. In American still life, between Peale's austere tradition and the lush fruit and flower pictures of the Victorian years, this kind of picture is quiet and unpretentious."},"nga:214121":{"d":"A single large yellow tulip sits at the center of a tiny panel, about 14 by 17 cm, against a plain brown ground. Its petals curve delicately, and the bright yellow is accented by streaks of light green and small hints of red. The thin green stem runs down to the bottom right corner, and the flower casts a soft shadow on the background, which suggests light coming in from the left.","i":"The tulip is the flower of Dutch still life, tied to the speculation of the 1630s and to the idea of beauty that does not last. By 1869, in America, a single flower on a small panel was more a decorative and botanical subject than a moral one, made for a domestic room or a collector of small pictures. Its isolation against a neutral ground descends from botanical illustration and from older flower painting.","c":"Hall's directness is the point: one flower, one ground, one source of light. The accents of green and red in the yellow are well chosen, and the soft shadow places the bloom firmly on its surface. The picture is an exercise rather than a composition, and its small size and plain ground limit its ambition, but within those limits it is clear, fresh and unsentimental."},"nga:214122":{"d":"Two halves of a pale-yellow lemon lie side by side in the center of a small canvas, about 17 by 24 cm, with the seeds visible and a small hole at the center. Two green leaves and a short stem accompany them, and one half is angled slightly to the right. The background is brown, and the halves cast dark shadows, so the cut fruit is lit and modeled against a plain dark ground.","i":"The cut lemon has a long history in still life. In Dutch pictures of the seventeenth century the peeled or halved lemon signaled costly imported fruit and sometimes the bitter side of luxury and life. A nineteenth-century American painter like Hall took the form without the moralizing, and in 1884 it offered a simple, strong subject: a pattern of segments, seeds and rind that could be studied for light and color alone.","c":"Hall keeps the picture almost austere: two halves, two leaves, a dark ground. That restraint is its strength, because the cut face of a lemon is a demanding subject for light and texture, and a plain background lets it speak. It is hardly an inventive composition, and it adds little to the tradition it draws on, but it is attentive and honest."},"nga:214120":{"d":"A pomegranate cut in half stands upright on a small canvas, about 18 by 23 cm, its red seeds exposed and some spilled out in front of it. The fruit sits against a dark brown background and casts a dark shadow behind it. The seeds are bright red with white highlights, the rind a slightly darker red, and the seed area is light yellow with darker hollows where seeds have fallen out.","i":"The pomegranate carries many meanings: in Christian art it can stand for the unity of the Church and the Resurrection, and in classical myth it is the fruit that bound Persephone to the underworld. A nineteenth-century traveler's still life like this one, labeled with the city of Siena, was perhaps less symbolic than observant, but the fruit's old associations with fertility, death and renewal were never far from view.","c":"The pomegranate is a rewarding subject, with its glistening seeds, and Hall exploits it: bright reds against the dark ground make the picture glow. The handling is direct, and the spilled seeds keep the composition from stiffness. Like his other small fruit pieces it remains modest in ambition, a study more than a statement, but the color is lively and the observation sharp."},"nga:221200":{"d":"Four fashionably dressed people make music on a terrace of gray-and-white striped paving. At the left a woman with golden curls plays a lute, in a red dress with a white bodice and a green shawl; at the right a man in a large tan hat with a white-and-blue plume plays a large bass string instrument. Two more figures stand arm in arm, he in black with a pink feather, she in a blue gown and orange hat. Behind them a table is laid with a lavish feast, a boy bends for a plate, and tall trees open onto calm water.","i":"The merry company was a favorite Dutch subject of the 1620s: stylish young people feasting, flirting and making music in a garden or on a terrace. The lavish table, with its showy bird on a pie, and the music, long associated with love, invite two readings: harmless pleasure, and a gentle warning about luxury and desire. Viewers of the time probably enjoyed both. Dirck Hals, the younger brother of Frans, made the type his specialty in Haarlem.","c":"Dirck Hals paints with a lighter, more decorative touch than his famous brother, and this picture is a parade of costume and incident: lace, plumes, ribbons, a feast. That is its pleasure and its limit. The figures tend to pose like elegant dolls, without the dash and psychological quickness of Frans, but the sense of fashion, the colorful accents and the relaxed outdoor setting make a lively and agreeable panel."},"cma:124086":{"d":"A court lady is shown wearing an array of jewelry that Mielich renders with meticulous attention: brooches, necklaces and rings. The sitter is Maria Kitscher, Frau von Freyberg, who belonged to the circle of the Bavarian court of Duke Albert V. On her left index finger is a ring set with a diamond and a ruby, made so that it could slide apart to reveal a private message or image beneath the gems. The panel is upright, about 61 by 46 cm, and dated 1545.","i":"The Freybergs were Lutherans in a Bavaria that was turning firmly Catholic, and the family eventually fell from favor as Catholic political interests consolidated their power. The jewelry is the language of rank, but the hidden ring adds a private note: a world of secret affection or devotion carried inside a public show of wealth. Mielich was well placed to paint it, since his work for the Munich court included illustrating an inventory of the duchess's jewelry.","c":"Mielich's training as an illuminator and miniaturist shows in the way gold, pearls and gems are set down, each with its own glint. The risk of such a picture is that the ornaments outshine the face, and German court portraiture of this date often leaves the sitter's character more reserved than her possessions. Even so, the precision is a pleasure in itself, and the hidden ring gives the portrait an intriguing second layer."},"nga:195506":{"d":"Two men pull on a rope attached to a boat on a riverside, hauling the net, in front of a city skyline of church steeples and windmills, identified as Zwolle. The sky is full of clouds painted with soft brushstrokes that add texture. The palette is muted and earthy: soft blues, greens and browns, with subdued reds and blues in the men's clothing. The panel is horizontal, about 50 by 77 cm, and dated 1663.","i":"Dutch river views of the 1660s celebrated the working life of the country's waters: fishing, towing, trade, with the town in the distance as an emblem of civic pride. A recognizable skyline made the picture a portrait of a place as well as a scene of labor, and buyers liked to see their own city and its river. Hauling a net is work for a team, so the two men also stand for the shared effort that kept such towns fed.","c":"Hardenstein works in the quiet vein of Dutch riverscape, recalling the tonal river views of painters such as Jan van Goyen and Salomon van Ruysdael: big sky, restrained color. The pleasure here is in honest observation, the men leaning on the rope and the airy clouds, rather than in drama. It is a modest picture by a lesser-known hand, and it succeeds by not asking for more than it can give."},"nga:214124":{"d":"Two large pears are placed side by side on a wooden surface. They are predominantly pale yellow, with areas of light pink and red. The pear on the left is larger and stands upright; the one on the right is shorter and angled slightly downward, away from the viewer. Both have light green stems and both cast reflections on the wood beneath them. Behind them is a plain gray wall. The oil on board is small, about 22 by 27 cm, and the picture is undated.","i":"The title names the variety, McLaughlin pears, as a grower or fruit lover might. In nineteenth-century America named varieties were a matter of pride, fruit societies held exhibitions, and a painting of a particular pear could serve as a portrait of the produce of a region. Still lifes of fruit were also commonly hung in dining rooms, where they promised abundance. This one asks for little more than a look at two good pears.","c":"The picture is spare: two pears, a wooden table, a gray wall. That discipline suits it, and the reflections on the wood and the contrast between an upright pear and a leaning one give a small, pleasing variety. It has none of the lavish abundance of the banquet pieces then also in fashion, and its ambition is low, but its honest attention to shape and surface is the real subject, and it keeps to it."},"cma:173632":{"d":"The title places this view near Granby, a town in northern Connecticut, and the date falls in the 1870s. The canvas is horizontal, about 32 by 55 cm, a proportion that suits an open view across fields, trees and sky. It is a modest, small-scale landscape of a specific place, of the kind painters often made from summer sketches and finished in the studio, and the picture invites a quiet look at an ordinary corner of rural New England.","i":"Van Elten, a Dutch-born painter who settled in the United States, worked in the tradition of the Hudson River School, which treated the American landscape as a source of national pride and calm feeling. By the 1870s that taste had moved from grand wilderness toward smaller, more intimate local scenes, and a title such as Landscape near Granby marks a recognizable place that buyers might know or wish to remember.","c":"The Dutch-born painter's training would give him a sure grasp of sky and horizon, and the picture's proportions suit a broad, calm view. Unpretentious local views of this kind rarely rise above pleasant competence, and without the grandeur of Church or Bierstadt they depend on the quality of light and the honesty of the drawing. Their interest is as documents of mood and place, and of the American taste of the 1870s."},"cma:169662":{"d":"A woman looks out in rich black fabric with a brown fur stole, a sheer white linen cap, an elaborate starched ruff and sleeves ending in punto in aria lace. A gold ring with a jewel is prominent on her right hand, and a dog appears with her. A wooden box on the floor would have held a dish of glowing coals, an early foot warmer. The panel is about 42 by 32 cm. The composition is weighted heavily to the right, which implies that it had a pendant.","i":"The black dress, white ruff and cap are the dignified costume of a well-off Dutch woman of the 1630s, though the fur, the costly lace and the ring show that sobriety did not mean modest means. A ruff by this date suggests a conservative taste, since younger sitters were wearing flat collars. The dog is a traditional emblem of fidelity. The pendant, probably a portrait of her husband, would have hung beside it as a married pair.","c":"Pot, a Haarlem painter who made both portraits and merry company pieces, brings a clear, workmanlike manner to a commission. The fabrics and lace are handled with confidence, and the homely details of the dog and foot warmer give a touch of domestic warmth. Beside the portraits that Frans Hals painted in the same town in the same years, though, it is stiffer and less spontaneous: dignified, competent and unsurprising."},"cma:159488":{"d":"God the Father holds the dead Christ, and the dove of the Holy Spirit hovers above them; angels encircle the group, carrying the instruments of Christ's Passion. God wears a purple papal tiara and an elaborate cape, on whose clasp is a tiny figure of Saint Barbara. The panel, about 115 by 74 cm, is vividly colored and was made around 1620 as an altarpiece in Antwerp. The tiara is unusual and was chosen deliberately.","i":"Antwerp was the center of religious learning in Catholic Flanders, and after the Reformation the Church encouraged images that promoted Catholic doctrine. The Trinity is the traditional theme here, with the Father, the dead Son and the Spirit as a dove. The papal tiara emphasizes the pope's role as God's true representative on earth. The small Saint Barbara may be a clue that the altarpiece stood in a chapel dedicated to her.","c":"Van Balen, best remembered as the teacher of Van Dyck, was a graceful, colorful painter of altarpieces and small cabinet pictures, and the brightness here is typical. The design is orthodox and clear, and it teaches doctrine efficiently; it lacks the physical energy of Rubens, who dominated Antwerp in these years. Its interest is as a precise document of Counter-Reformation teaching, delivered in a vivid, decorative key."},"cma:166505":{"d":"A mother and her young daughter are shown close together on a small canvas, about 44 by 36 cm, painted around 1794 to 1795. The mother's hair flows loose and free across her shoulders but is partly bound up in a red kerchief. The picture is an intimate, domestic one, and its subject is the connection between the two figures more than any display of rank or setting.","i":"The museum reads the red kerchief as a tell-tale sign that the woman was an active participant in the French Revolution, the color recalling the red cap of liberty. The picture also celebrates values the Revolutionary period prized, close family bonds and above all the mother's role in raising children. Rousseau's call for mothers to nurse and teach their own children had become a civic ideal, and the republic needed citizens raised at home.","c":"Danloux, a pupil of Vien, paints with a soft, informal sympathy typical of late eighteenth-century French sentiment. The picture's strength is its tenderness, and its weakness is that the political message depends on a single accessory: without the museum's reading, a viewer would see only affectionate motherhood. It is a modest, small-scale canvas, but it records the way the ideals of an era entered private life."},"cma:170082":{"d":"Henry Church, a blacksmith by trade and a painter, sculptor and musician by passion, portrays himself surrounded by a squadron of miniature winged muses. They represent painting, sculpture and music and also his work as a blacksmith, shown as a crowned figure holding a hammer and anvil. The picture, in oil on paper mounted to board, is about 73 by 60 cm and was painted around 1880. It is a highly imaginative self-portrait.","i":"Church lived all his life in Chagrin Falls, Ohio, and the museum counts him among the great self-taught artists of nineteenth-century America. Dressing a village blacksmith in the machinery of classical myth is self-celebration with a sense of humor. He was also an entrepreneur: in 1888 he opened Church's Art Museum at Geauga Lake, the first commercial art gallery in northeast Ohio, with an inventory made up entirely of his own work.","c":"This is a self-taught artist's picture, and it does not pretend to academic polish. Its strengths are invention, humor and self-assurance, and the joining of classical muses to a forge is a fine, odd idea. It tells more about its maker, who even had a phonograph record speak at his funeral, than many polished portraits manage. It is best valued as an act of self-invention."},"cma:97189":{"d":"A landscape with travelers, painted in the 1630s on a horizontal canvas of about 75 by 95 cm. The travelers are Roman peasants, not the classical figures that Swanevelt's contemporaries preferred, so the scene reads as an observed corner of the countryside with people passing through, and not as an episode from myth. Swanevelt, a Dutch painter working in Rome, briefly shared a home with Claude Lorrain.","i":"Rome in the 1630s drew northern painters, and the countryside around the city, with its ruins, roads and shepherds, was a favorite subject for collectors who liked a poetic view of the south. The museum notes that Swanevelt played an important role in the development of seventeenth-century landscape painting in Rome. Travelers make a quiet narrative of their own, passing through a landscape that seems older than they are.","c":"Swanevelt is often described as a follower of Claude, but most scholars now think the influence ran both ways. His peasants give his scenes a note of everyday life that the more classical painters avoid, and that is his distinction. The compositions can feel a little tidy, and his light is less transforming than Claude's, but he is a capable, graceful landscapist whose reputation rests on a sound sense of place."},"cma:143261":{"d":"A small panel, about 31 by 42 cm, spreads a vast panorama of mountains, coast and sea behind a group of exotically dressed figures on a foreground piece of land. The harbor city is purely imaginative, and fanciful cliffs and peaks crowd the distance and disappear into a light mist. The saint is small beside the scenery. An owl, the artist's signature motif, sits on the rock at the lower right.","i":"This is a world landscape, a type of northern painting with a vast panorama and a narrative religious subject in the foreground, popularized by Joachim Patinir and fashionable throughout the sixteenth century. The Baptist's story gives the view a religious frame, and the winding distances can be read as the world through which the soul travels. Bles was acclaimed in Italy as the Master of the Owl, after the bird he often put in his works.","c":"Bles is one of the best of Patinir's followers, and the misty fading of the distance, the atmospheric perspective that Leonardo had pioneered, is the picture's chief beauty. The figures are small and schematic, and the invention goes into the scenery more than into the story. But the eye is invited to roam, and the owl, hidden on its rock, gives the viewer something to find."},"nga:195617":{"d":"Apollo strides forward at the center, a laurel wreath in his blond curls and a golden lyre in the crook of his arm, wearing only a coral-red cloak, and he gestures toward two bearded men seated on a rock at the right. At the right edge the satyr Pan plays his pipes, and beside him sits Midas, recognizable by his tall, pointed ass's ears. Two women sit on rocks at the left. Rocks and trees enclose the group beneath lilac clouds. The panel is about 74 by 104 cm.","i":"In Ovid's Metamorphoses, Pan boasted that his pipes were finer than Apollo's lyre, and the mountain god Tmolus judged the contest and awarded the prize to Apollo. Only Midas disagreed, and Apollo gave him an ass's ears as the reward for his taste. The story was a favorite among connoisseurs, for it holds a warning to anyone who prefers the rustic to the refined and who judges badly in matters of art.","c":"Van den Hoecke, trained in the circle of Rubens, brings Rubensian vigor to the story: swirling brushwork in cloaks and background, warm, rich color, and an Apollo balanced on the ball of one foot with courtly elegance. The result is lively and legible but derivative, an echo of the master's mythologies without his full energy. It is a polished, intelligent piece of Flemish storytelling that knows its Ovid."},"nga:35082":{"d":"In an octagonal panel about 43 by 39 cm, a nude woman sleeps on the grass beside a rose-pink cloth; one satyr hovers at her side, reaching toward her or the cloth, while another kneels by a tree, a jug in his hand, looking back over his shoulder. On the other side of a tree stump that rises only to the middle of the picture, a nude child hunches over to draw on the ground with a V-shaped compass, with two sheets of paper and a violin nearby.","i":"Its meaning is not certain, and the museum calls it simply an allegory. One plausible reading sets the child's studious, measuring world, the compass, the paper and the violin, against the satyrs' sensual one, with the half-bare tree dividing the two, so that the picture contrasts reason and the arts with appetite. The town behind, its buildings streaked with peach and perhaps flames, adds a note of warning or transience.","c":"A curious, personal picture whose value lies in its enigma more than in polish. The sleeping nude, the satyrs and the distant landscape recall Giorgione's poetic Venice, and the octagonal format and the tree stump that divides the design give it a clear structure. The painter was a minor Venetian, and the handling is probably less assured than that of the great names, but the picture shows how widely the taste for puzzling allegory had spread."},"nga:214902":{"d":"A small, almost square panel, about 21 by 22 cm, painted in 1871 in the trompe-l'oeil manner and devoted to the belongings that the title assigns to a gentleman. Pictures of this kind present a few personal objects at life size and close to the picture plane, so exactly that the eye is invited to take them for real, and they were meant for close looking on a desk or wall. The near-square format favors a tight, deliberate arrangement.","i":"After the Civil War, American painters took up the trompe-l'oeil still life, building on the earlier example of the Peale family. Pictures of a man's belongings, such as pipes, newspapers and drinking vessels, often hung in studies, clubs and bars, and played gently on male leisure and privacy. The pleasure was partly the joke of the illusion and partly a portrait of the owner by way of his things.","c":"A trompe l'oeil lives or dies by its edges, shadows and surface wear, and at a size of about 21 centimeters any lapse would show. Inman is less celebrated than Harnett or Peto, who made the type famous, and he belongs to the supporting cast of the American illusionists. Still, a small, carefully made picture of this kind is a pleasing exercise in craft, and it asks to be looked at closely."},"nga:214901":{"d":"Two large yellow pears stand in the center of a table covered in red, with clusters of purple and green grapes cascading from a gilded teal dish at the right. Two pink peaches lie beside the pears, with walnuts and green figs in front. At the left, a decorative blue vase with gold filigree holds colorful flowers and leaves, among them a white rose, red flowers and small pale blue ones. The wall behind is dark brown. The canvas is about 39 by 47 cm.","i":"The picture belongs to the long tradition of the abundance still life, in which fruit and flowers are heaped on a table as a sign of plenty. In mid-nineteenth-century America, a growing middle class liked such pictures in dining rooms and parlors, and gilded dishes and filigree vases announced prosperity as clearly as the produce. The year 1868 was soon after the Civil War, and abundance may have had a patriotic as well as a domestic appeal.","c":"Inman builds a rich arrangement with strong colors: yellow pears and purple grapes against a dark brown wall, with the red table covering adding warmth. The composition is conventional and a little crowded, and it is more an inventory of luscious things than a design, yet the textures, the fuzz of the peaches, the sheen of the dish, are handled with real appreciation. It represents the lush taste of the Victorian years."},"nga:102987":{"d":"A parrot perches on a branch at the left beside artichokes, a vase of flowers and a dish of cherries, all set on a cream-white stone ledge with stepped molding, in a horizontal composition lit from the upper left. The parrot has a muted green body, a yellow and blue head and red-streaked wings, and faces right in profile. Seven pea pods lie beside three artichoke stalks; cherries in several stages of ripeness fill a pale blue dish; irises and poppies stand behind against a slate-gray wall.","i":"Italian still lifes of the seventeenth century gathered garden produce, flowers and sometimes exotic creatures to display nature's variety and the owner's taste. A parrot, imported from far away, added a note of luxury and curiosity, and in older Christian imagery it also carried associations of speech and purity. The spring and early-summer crops and flowers suggest a single season, and the stone ledge gives the arrangement a stage-like clarity.","c":"The unnamed painter handles light and surface well: the shimmer of the cherries, the cool stone of the ledge, the clean separation of forms. The branch that seems to come from nowhere is a small lapse in an otherwise careful arrangement. It is a competent, attractive example of Italian Baroque still life, quiet in manner, without the theatrical contrasts that some of its contemporaries preferred, and best enjoyed for its fresh, clear colors."},"met:436731":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child on her lap, set against a plain gold ground on an upright panel of about 83 by 56 cm. The Met places the picture in Florence in the last quarter of the thirteenth century, the generation of Cimabue. Panels of that date typically present the Virgin frontally and symmetrically, with firm outlines, a stiffly built throne and drapery marked out in linear folds, a manner inherited from Byzantine icons.","i":"A Virgin enthroned is Mary as Queen of Heaven and as the Seat of Wisdom, the throne on which the Word made flesh sits, and the gold ground lifts the group out of ordinary space into a heavenly one. In thirteenth-century Florence such panels were made for church altars and chapels, especially for the new preaching orders, the Franciscans and Dominicans, and for lay confraternities who gathered to sing praises before the Madonna.","c":"To a modern eye these panels can look rigid, and this one appears to keep to the inherited formula. But rigidity was the point, since the aim was holiness, not illusion, and symmetrical pose, bold contour and the glow of gold do that work economically. Cimabue and then Giotto would show how quickly the type was to change, and a panel like this shows the starting point."},"met:436759":{"d":"The Virgin and the Christ Child appear against a gold ground on an upright panel of about 62 by 42 cm, painted in tempera in the first quarter of the fourteenth century. The Met calls the painter Florentine or Paduan and raises, with a question mark, the name Cheyo da Firenze. Madonnas made in these years, just after Giotto painted the Arena Chapel in Padua, tend to give Mary and the Child more bodily weight and a more natural bond than the older, icon-like types.","i":"The doubt in the attribution, Florentine or Paduan, is itself instructive. Giotto worked in both cities, and painters who learned from him carried his heavier, more human figures wherever they went, so style alone does not always fix a place. Such panels served the private prayers of a household or the altar of a small chapel, and the tenderness between mother and child was meant to move the viewer as much as to teach doctrine.","c":"In the attribution game a question mark is an honest verdict: the panel belongs to the generation formed by Giotto but cannot be pinned to one hand or one town. What matters is whether the figures have weight and whether the bond between mother and child feels real, and the gold ground still keeps the picture on the side of devotion. As a document of the moment when Gothic painting turned human, it is telling."},"met:436757":{"d":"A small wooden panel, about 41 by 25 cm, painted in tempera in Siena and dated 1343, made as the cover of a book. The Met's subject headings, money, reading, writing and human figures, point to the business of accounts: people at work with coins, books and pens. Siena was famous for painted covers of this kind, made for the account books of the republic's finance offices, so the object is at once a binding, a civic emblem and a small picture.","i":"From the thirteenth century the Sienese government had its books of income and expenditure bound in wooden covers painted with scenes: officials at work, the city's patron saints, the Virgin. Each cover recorded the officials who held the post for the term. A dated cover pins down its year as precisely as a signature, and, if this is one of them, as the subject suggests, it shows how a medieval republic saw its own bureaucracy: honest, orderly work under heavenly protection.","c":"The attraction of such panels is the union of dry administration and real painting: the Sienese could make even a ledger cover a small picture, with the decorative order of the school. As art the unknown painter works at a small scale and the pictorial interest is modest; as a document it is a precious record of how money was handled and how a city advertised its honesty. It rewards close looking more than awe."},"met:436751":{"d":"A small panel, about 46 by 30 cm, painted in tempera on a gold ground, brings together three subjects: the Virgin and Child with Saints Michael and John the Baptist; the Noli Me Tangere, in which the risen Christ appears to Mary Magdalene; and the Conversion of Saint Paul. The Met lists it as the work of a Pisan painter of the second quarter of the fourteenth century. A devotional center with narrative scenes suggests a small portable altarpiece for private prayer.","i":"In the Noli Me Tangere, Christ tells Mary Magdalene at the tomb, after the Resurrection, not to cling to him, and Paul's conversion on the road to Damascus is the New Testament's other great reversal. Together the scenes offer two converted sinners as models of repentance, beside the Virgin, Saint Michael, who weighs souls at the Last Judgment, and John the Baptist, who preached repentance. Such a panel would have served private prayer.","c":"Pisan painting of this period absorbed the Sienese manner, with its elegant line and gold, and Simone Martini had worked in Pisa in 1319. A great deal of story is packed into a small space here, and the test is whether the three subjects stay legible. The ambition of the program is real, even if the painter is not one of the great names, and the result is a dense, devout little object."},"met:436733":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child between Saint John the Baptist and another saint, on a nearly square panel of about 44 by 36 cm, painted in tempera and gold. The Met lists the work as Florentine, of the second quarter of the fifteenth century, when painters such as Fra Angelico and Masolino were reshaping Florentine painting. Gold remains part of the medium, while the grouping of the figures in one shared space looks toward the Renaissance.","i":"John the Baptist was the patron saint of Florence, so his presence beside the Virgin gave the panel a civic as well as devotional meaning. The identity of the other saint is uncertain. Groups of this kind, called sacre conversazioni, bring saints into the Virgin's own space, so that the viewer is invited to join their company, and they were popular for private devotion in Florentine homes.","c":"A small anonymous panel from the busiest generation of Florentine painting cannot compete with Angelico or Lippi, and it seems to follow their formulas more than extend them. It is better read as evidence of how widely those formulas spread, through workshops that met a steady demand for private devotional pictures. The enthroned type and the gold give it dignity, though it remains a modest example."},"met:436743":{"d":"The angel Gabriel announces to the Virgin Mary that she will bear the Son of God. Above them appear God the Father and the dove of the Holy Spirit, and a deer is also part of the scene. The panel is small, about 45 by 38 cm, painted in tempera with gold. The Met attributes it, tentatively, to a painter of the Marches in the first half of the fifteenth century, when the elegant, decorative International Gothic style was strong in that region.","i":"The Annunciation, from the Gospel of Luke, is the moment of the Incarnation, and painters showed it as a visit: the angel's greeting, Mary's humility, the Holy Spirit descending as a dove. The deer is unusual. It may recall the psalm in which the soul longs for God as a deer for flowing water, but the picture itself does not settle the question.","c":"International Gothic charm is the draw: decorative line, gold and a love of incident such as the animal. The Marches produced Gentile da Fabriano, the style's finest Italian master, and a panel from that region shows the same courtly taste in a small, delicate narrative. It lacks the monumental simplicity that Florentine painters were reaching by then, and the attribution is tentative, but it is a lively, engaging little picture."},"met:436762":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child on a narrow upright panel, about 39 by 25 cm, painted in tempera by a Venetian painter of the second quarter of the fifteenth century. In these years Venetian painting blended late Gothic elegance with Byzantine tradition, and small Madonnas of this date often show the pair half-length and close together, with the Child leaning toward his mother in a gesture of tenderness. Such panels were meant to be set up for prayer in the home.","i":"Venice's trade with Constantinople and Crete filled its houses with Byzantine icons, and Venetian painters learned to make Western equivalents. The most popular type, the Madonna of Tenderness, shows mother and child cheek to cheek. Pictures like this were meant for private prayer, to be set on a household altar or in a bedroom, and they invited a personal, almost familial feeling toward the Holy Family. The painter is unknown.","c":"Small Venetian Madonnas of this date are rarely surprising, and an unnamed painter cannot be asked to rival Jacopo Bellini or the Vivarini. The measure is quiet: purity of line, fine finish, a sweet, serious expression. In that modest register such panels were Venice's staple devotional art, and they prepare the ground for the deeper human feeling of Giovanni Bellini's Madonnas a generation later."},"met:436736":{"d":"The Virgin nurses the Christ Child, with angels in attendance, on a panel of about 86 by 62 cm painted in oil on wood by an unnamed Florentine of the third quarter of the fifteenth century. The nursing Madonna, called the Madonna of the Milk, is a type of intimate devotional picture, and the angels add a note of heavenly courtliness. The use of oil was then still relatively new in Florence, and the scale suits a private chapel or a well-furnished room.","i":"A nursing Madonna insists on the reality of the Incarnation: the Son of God took a child's needs and a mother's care. It also presented Mary as a model of motherhood, and women praying before it might ask her help in childbirth and in feeding their own children. The angels announce her rank as Queen of Heaven. In Florentine families such pictures often hung in bedrooms, where marriage and birth were part of the setting.","c":"The circle of Filippo Lippi and his pupil Botticelli set the standard for the Florentine Madonna with angels in these years, with sweet, thoughtful faces and graceful line, and an anonymous follower shows how widely that standard spread. The oil medium allows soft modeling. The result is likely pleasing rather than inventive, and its interest lies in the type's enormous popularity."},"met:436760":{"d":"The Virgin and Child are shown with Saints Jerome and Francis on an upright panel of about 62 by 42 cm, painted in tempera around 1500 by an Umbrian painter. Jerome is recognizable by the lion that appears with him, and Francis, the saint of Assisi, is Umbria's own. The group is a small devotional picture in which the saints stand close to the Virgin as if in conversation. The sweetness of such figures is typical of the Umbrian school around Perugino.","i":"Jerome, the scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, is shown with a lion because of a legend that he drew a thorn from a lion's paw, and the pair stand for his penance and gentleness. Francis, the poor man of Assisi, is Umbria's own saint. Their company with the Virgin suggests an owner with Franciscan connections, or one who bore their names, in a region where Franciscan devotion was strong.","c":"Umbria around 1500 lived in the long shadow of Perugino and the young Raphael, and the school's gentle, symmetrical grace was easy to imitate. This unnamed painter works in that mild, harmonious manner; the lion adds charm, and the grouping is balanced. The picture lacks the spatial depth and individuality that distinguish the masters, but as a sincere piece of local devotion it does what it was made to do."},"met:436761":{"d":"Three women personify the theological virtues, Faith, Charity and Hope, on an upright panel of about 74 by 46 cm, painted in tempera and gold by an Umbrian painter around 1500. A chalice, a phoenix, a dog and an infant appear among them, each a traditional sign. The picture is an allegory designed to be read from its attributes, and its figures are idealized types, not portraits.","i":"Faith, Hope and Charity are the three virtues named by Saint Paul as lasting, and the greatest of them, he says, is love. Artists gave them women's forms and signs: the chalice of the Eucharist for Faith, a mother with children for Charity, and for Hope a sign of rebirth such as the phoenix, the bird that rises from its own ashes. The dog, an emblem of fidelity, suits Faith. Sets of virtues decorated chapels, furniture and devotional rooms.","c":"Allegorical panels rarely have the emotional pull of a Madonna, and the test is whether the personifications are clear and graceful, which in Umbrian painting around 1500 they usually are. This one is a diagram of doctrine made beautiful in gold and tempera, and it keeps to the school's sweet, orderly style. The signs are legible and the idea is sound, but the feeling stays cool."},"met:436742":{"d":"Twelve heads of men and women, some of them in profile, are gathered on a single panel painted in tempera by a Lombard painter in the first quarter of the sixteenth century. It is an unusual object: not a portrait or an altarpiece but a collection of faces. Profile heads of this sort recall Roman coins and the portrait medals of the fifteenth century, and they also echo the studies of character and type made by Leonardo and his followers in Milan.","i":"The purpose is not recorded. Possibilities include a pattern sheet for artists, a decorative set of imaginary famous men and women, or a display of character types of the kind Leonardo made fashionable in Milan, from ideal beauty to the grotesque. Twelve is a number that suggests a series, such as the Caesars, though nothing here confirms it. Whether the heads are portraits or invented types is also uncertain.","c":"A curiosity rather than a polished picture: its value is as evidence of how artists in Lombardy trained the eye on types and profiles in Leonardo's wake. As an object it is neither fully a model nor a finished work, and that ambiguity limits it. But a panel packed with faces gives a vivid sense of the range of characters a Renaissance painter was expected to master."},"met:436472":{"d":"A man is shown in strict profile, in oil on a small panel of about 34 by 27 cm, painted around 1525 by an unnamed Italian painter. Profile portraits descend from Roman coins and the medals of the fifteenth century, and by 1525 the three-quarter view had largely replaced them in painting, so a profile of this date looks backward, either from conservative taste or from a deliberate wish for the dignity of an ancient medal.","i":"The profile gives a likeness without engagement: the sitter does not look at the viewer, and his features are presented like a coin's. It suits a serious record of a face and ties the sitter to the antique fashion for commemorative portraiture. His name and rank are unknown, and nothing points to a profession. Portraits of this kind were kept as family likenesses or given as tokens of regard.","c":"A profile is a demanding format: with only one view, the whole likeness depends on the outline of brow, nose and chin and the firmness of the drawing. Oil gives the painter softer modeling than the older tempera profiles had. Without a named hand or sitter the picture is more interesting as a survival of an old fashion than as a particular achievement."},"met:436741":{"d":"A man is shown in a coat trimmed with fur, in oil on canvas, about 98 by 75 cm, a size normal for a half-length portrait. The Met lists the painter as Lombard and dates the picture about 1540. The fur trim is the chief mark of status in the picture: in sixteenth-century Italy fur was a costly sign of rank and wealth, and the sitter chose a coat that showed it.","i":"North Italian portraits of the 1540s favored plain, direct likenesses of the local gentry and professional men, and Lombard painters such as Moretto, Savoldo and Moroni excelled at them. The man's name is not known, so the coat and its fur are his only credentials. Furs were regulated by sumptuary laws in many Italian cities, which limited who could wear them, so the trim was a statement as well as a comfort.","c":"The Lombard school of the mid-sixteenth century produced portraits of unflattering honesty, and the best of them, by Moroni, have a psychological sharpness that an anonymous canvas cannot be assumed to share. The type works through simplicity: a face, a coat, a plain ground. Without a name for the painter or the sitter, the picture is best valued as a solid example of the school's plain-spoken portraiture."},"met:436738":{"d":"The Virgin sits with the Christ Child and the infant John the Baptist in a picture of about 68 by 57 cm, painted in oil on wood by an unnamed Florentine of the mid-sixteenth century. The three figures form a compact group, a type that Florentine painters made familiar: the child John, the Christ Child's cousin, joins the Holy Family as a playmate and a future prophet. The intimate scale suits a picture for a private room.","i":"John the Baptist was the patron saint of Florence, and from the late fifteenth century Florentine painters regularly showed him as a child beside Jesus, sometimes with the lamb or a reed cross that foretold the Passion. The subject had a tender, domestic side, with the cousins as playmates, and a darker side, since each child already bears the sign of his sacrifice. Raphael's Madonna of the Goldfinch, painted in Florence about 1506, was the great model.","c":"Mid-sixteenth-century Florence was a world of refined, self-conscious style, with Bronzino, Vasari and Salviati, and devotional pictures like this tend to polish High Renaissance formulas into smooth, elegant surfaces and a slightly cool sweetness. An unnamed follower can do no more than echo them, and the result is pleasing and a little impersonal. Its virtue is a familiar, well-made grouping of three tender figures."},"met:436739":{"d":"A woman wearing a pearl necklace is shown in a portrait painted in oil on a panel of about 98 by 76 cm, a size that allows a half-length figure with room for costume and hands. The Met lists the painter as Florentine and suggests, tentatively, Jacopo Zucchi, a pupil of Vasari, but the sitter is unidentified. In mid-sixteenth-century Florence a woman's portrait presented her family's wealth and her own position through dress and jewels.","i":"Pearls were among the most prized women's jewels of the century and were associated with purity and with marriage, and a necklace of them was a typical wedding gift. Under Duke Cosimo I the Medici court made elaborate dress and jewelry a language of rank, and families of the Florentine elite followed. The sitter's name is not known; a portrait of this scale was probably a family commission, perhaps made on a betrothal or marriage.","c":"The standard for Florentine portraits at mid-century was Bronzino's: an enameled surface, a controlled pose and sitters who withhold themselves. A tentatively attributed portrait has to be measured against that, and few painters reached it. The pearl necklace and the large panel give this one presence, and it asks to be read first as a document of rank and only then as a likeness of a person."},"met:436765":{"d":"A man and a woman, titled a patrician couple, are portrayed on a small plaque of ivory by an unknown Venetian painter, dated by the Met to 1730 or later. Venice had been a center of ivory miniature painting since Rosalba Carriera took up the medium around 1700, and works of this type are small, delicate and made to be held. The pair were painted together, most likely as a record of a marriage, and the ivory gives their faces the pale glow typical of the form.","i":"Venetian patricians were the families whose names were entered in the Golden Book and who governed the Republic, so the title signals a couple of the ruling class, though their names are not recorded. Marriages among such families joined fortunes and alliances, and a miniature could commemorate a union or be kept as a token of affection. Dress, wigs and jewelry in a Venetian portrait of the 1730s carried the language of rank as clearly as any inscription.","c":"Venetian miniatures won their reputation through Rosalba Carriera, whose soft, pearly touch was admired across Europe. An anonymous follower rarely matches her delicacy, but the form's built-in virtues, intimacy and glow, survive in the work. Showing a couple in so small a format is hard, since two faces must register with equal clarity. The picture's chief interest is as a document of Venetian dress and rank."},"met:437843":{"d":"A man is shown in an oil portrait of about 56 by 45 cm, a size suited to a head-and-shoulders or bust-length likeness, painted by an Italian artist of the eighteenth century. The picture has been attributed to Gaspare Traversi, a Neapolitan painter known for sharply observed figures drawn from everyday life, some with a touch of humor or satire. The sitter's name is not recorded, so the picture depends on the directness of the likeness.","i":"Traversi worked in Naples and elsewhere in Italy in the mid-eighteenth century, and his people come from many walks of life, seen with warmth and a little irony, a long way from the courtly portrait. If the attribution is right, this sitter would be one of those: an individual caught with his character on his face and not dressed up as a hero. The unnamed sitter was probably of middling rank.","c":"If the attribution to Traversi holds, the portrait belongs to the tradition of Italian realism in the age of the Rococo, in which observation counts for more than flattery. That is a real virtue in a century of polished portraits, and the small canvas is probably most telling in the face. Without a secure hand or a named sitter, the claim must stay modest: an honest, lively likeness."},"cma:102100":{"d":"A tall oil on canvas, about 79 by 56 cm, dated 1895, takes its title from the language of bargaining: the last price is the final offer in a haggle. The upright format suggests one or two figures seen at close range, and the subject belongs to the anecdotal genre scenes that late nineteenth-century painters loved, in which gesture and expression tell a small story. A second canvas by the same painter in the museum, Japanese Boy Building a Man-of-War, dates from the same year.","i":"Haggling pictures were popular at a time when travelers brought home stories of markets and bazaars, and when buyers enjoyed an exotic or comic scene that could be read at a glance. The painter's Japanese Boy Building a Man-of-War, of the same year, suggests an interest in Japanese subjects, so the scene may be Japanese, though the title alone does not say. The humor lies in the contest between shopkeeper and customer.","c":"A picture of this kind succeeds or fails on faces and hands: the cunning of the seller, the reluctance of the buyer, the small drama between them. J. C. Arter is a minor artist, and the anecdotal manner was a staple of academic genre painting, so the ambition is modest. The upright canvas and close view are a direct way to tell the joke, and it makes no claims beyond that."},"cma:102101":{"d":"A Japanese boy is at work building a man-of-war, a model warship, on a wide canvas of about 56 by 79 cm painted in 1895. It is a genre picture of childhood absorbed in craft and play, where the subject lies in the boy's concentration. It has a companion of the same year by J. C. Arter in the museum, The Last Price, and the wide format suits a single figure with the object he is making.","i":"The date matters. In 1894 and 1895 Japan defeated China in a war in which its modern navy played a decisive part, and Western audiences, already charmed by Japanese art and goods, suddenly saw a modern naval power. A Japanese child building a warship answers to both feelings, the picturesque and the new. Since the Meiji reforms of 1868 Japan had built its navy on Western models, and the boy stands in for a nation's ambition.","c":"The idea is better than the type usually allows: a child imitating the machinery of national power has charm and timeliness. The risk is the outsider's picture-postcard Japan, which Western painters of the period seldom escaped. It is best taken as a record of what an American painter and his buyers found delightful in 1895, not as an observation of Japanese life."},"cma:148850":{"d":"A young woman is shown on a small panel of about 24 by 17.5 cm, painted in oil around 1490 by Jacometto Veneziano. She has been described since 1543 as a nun or novice of the Venetian convent of San Secondo, and the title keeps that tradition with a qualifying possibly. Her head covering may be secular or may be a Benedictine or Dominican habit, and the museum calls her a beautiful young woman presented as saintly.","i":"Scholars have recently speculated that the sitter is not a nun at all but perhaps the wife or daughter of Alvise Contarini. The convent of San Secondo took the daughters of the wealthiest Venetians, and conventions of dress there were fairly loose until strict reforms in 1515, so a secular lady and a novice could look alike. Presenting a beautiful young woman as saintly was a regular practice, which blurs the boundary between portrait and devotion.","c":"Jacometto worked on a small scale with minute finish, a manner that owed much to Netherlandish portraits, and a panel like this shows the glowing clarity that oil allowed. The puzzle of whether she is nun or lay woman is part of its pleasure for connoisseurs, and the doubt reminds one how much of a Renaissance portrait is costume and convention. It rewards close looking, and it is intimate in the way miniatures are."},"cma:118679":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 146 by 221 cm, places two opposed men at its far ends: at the far left the beggar Lazarus reclines on the floor while dogs lick the sores on his body, and at the far right the Rich Man relaxes at his lavish table. The Biblical parable (Luke 16:19-21) sets feasting against starvation, and the composition makes the opposition physical, with the full width of the canvas lying between the two men.","i":"In Luke's parable the rich man feasted and celebrated every day while Lazarus lay starving at his door. When both died, Lazarus was welcomed into heaven and the rich man was consigned to hell. Sixteenth-century preachers used it as a warning against indifference to the poor, and the dogs, which show more pity than the household, are often read as a sign of that failure of charity. The story had direct force for a prosperous Venetian territory.","c":"Bassano, from the town of Bassano in the Veneto, has a gift for making a parable look like life in his own countryside, with domestic animals and a lively table. The social contrast is sharp and the story is told at a glance. The risk of the type is that the pleasure in the feast competes with the moral, a tension that runs through Venetian painting, and it is part of what makes this picture human."},"cma:127746":{"d":"Saint John the Baptist baptizes Christ in the River Jordan, and the heavens open as the Holy Spirit descends in the form of a dove. The canvas is wide, about 169 by 251 cm, and the museum describes powerful modeling of the bodies and luminous color. As in much of Tintoretto's work, the figures are built up by strong light and shade, and the picture was painted in the 1580s in his busy Venetian workshop.","i":"The scene follows the Gospel of Matthew. At the baptism the Holy Spirit appears as a dove and a voice from heaven declares Jesus to be the Son, so the three persons of the Trinity are present together. It was the first public sign of Christ's mission and the model for the sacrament of baptism, which is why such subjects were commissioned for churches and confraternity halls in Venice.","c":"Tintoretto's studio motto, as the story goes, was the drawing of Michelangelo and the color of Titian, and this picture is a good demonstration of the second half: bodies modeled with force, color that glows. Late Tintoretto varies with how much was left to assistants, and some of his late canvases look hurried. Here the muscular figures and rich color make a vigorous, weighty image of a quiet sacrament."},"cma:141476":{"d":"A man in a costume encrusted with gold embroidery is shown in a relaxed posture before a thick document, with a seal in his hand and a silver-gilt writing set beside him. A wisp of smoke rises from the candle used to melt the sealing wax, as if the treaty has only just been concluded. Just visible at his chest are the pink ribbon and star of the Order of Saint Lazarus. The canvas is about 125 by 92 cm.","i":"The sitter, Jean-Gabriel du Theil (1685 to 1755), was secretary of foreign affairs to Louis XV and helped negotiate the Treaty of Vienna of 1738, which ceded the Duchy of Lorraine to France. The seal, the writing set and the document allude to those negotiations. The portrait is therefore a monument to a diplomatic success, with dress and decoration marking his prestige and his posture suggesting calm assurance.","c":"Aved's best idea is the smoke, a small touch of narrative that turns a portrait of rank into a portrait of a moment. The picture balances public status, the gold embroidery and the order, against private shrewdness in the sharp gaze, and the face stays the main event. It is more informal and observant than the grand state portraits of the day, though still a careful, flattering commission."},"met:20768":{"d":"A ship portrait on a wide canvas, about 87 by 164 cm, painted in 1852, showing two vessels named in the title, the Thomas Hunt and the America, with flags flying and the boats and ship set on the water. Ship portraits of this kind give each vessel its identity: the profile is shown clearly, the hull lines and rigging or machinery are recorded with care, and the flags mark nationality and ownership.","i":"James Bard was the American painter best known for portraits of steamboats and sailing vessels of the Hudson River and New York waters, and with his twin brother John he supplied owners, captains and builders who wanted an exact likeness of a vessel. Such pictures were commissioned as records and as advertisements, and the names in the title, not the scenery, are the subject. The year 1852 falls in the age of the great steamboat rivalries.","c":"Bard's vessel portraits are valued for exactness and for the plain clarity of a profile view; they are not atmospheric pictures, and the water and sky are a setting more than a subject. That is their strength and limit. The best of them have a handsome, flat decorative order, and as documents of American maritime life they are indispensable."},"cma:142018":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Frederic W. Lincoln, Jr., painted in 1889 in oil on a canvas of about 108 by 92 cm. The near-square format allows a half-length or three-quarter-length figure with some space around it. An American commission of the late nineteenth century of this kind normally shows the sitter in dark formal dress against a plain or lightly furnished ground, aiming at dignity and a recognizable likeness.","i":"A name with Jr. at its end records family continuity, and such portraits were commissioned to mark the standing of a man within a family and community: a promotion, an election, a retirement or simply the achievement of a prosperous career. They hung in homes, offices and civic halls. The picture's purpose is plain: to preserve the face and presence of someone whose position mattered to his circle.","c":"Like many American portraitists of the 1880s, James Harvey Young worked in a market that asked for reliable likeness and dignified presentation. The weakness of that market is formula. The test for a portrait of this kind is whether the sitter seems present, in the eyes and the hands, and not merely correct, and a near-square canvas leaves room for that. The verdict must be a measured one."},"cma:173657":{"d":"A wide landscape of a New England morning, about 52 by 87 cm, painted in 1873 by James McDougal Hart. The title promises early light and the freshness of the day, and the horizontal format suits an open pastoral view. Hart often peopled such meadows with grazing cattle, and the picture is the kind of calm farmland scene that an American buyer of the 1870s would hang in a parlor or a library.","i":"By 1873 the heroic wilderness of the early Hudson River School had given way to calmer, more intimate scenes of farmland and pasture. After the Civil War and in the face of industrialization, New England stood for order, memory and rural virtue, and morning stood for renewal. Hart, born in Scotland and raised in Albany, found his subject in the American countryside and made a reputation with its cattle and meadows.","c":"Hart's landscapes are tranquil, carefully observed and sunny, and their strength is a clear, believable atmosphere. Their weakness is sameness: the pastoral formula was repeated often, and they lack the drama of Church or the mystical feeling of Inness. As a quiet pastoral it is pleasing, and its date places it at the moment when American landscape was turning from spectacle to mood."},"cma:95286":{"d":"A woman wears several transparent and translucent white collars that become opaque when layered, and an elaborate, twisting, three-dimensional necklace. Parts of the lace collar and the necklace had been lost, and a conservator has reconstructed them after studying the artist's technique in other works; one probable ornament at the back of her head was left unrestored because nothing showed what it was. The panel is about 53 by 45 cm, painted in the 1620s.","i":"The story of this panel is as much about conservation as about the sitter. After earlier restorations were removed, the missing passages of lace and necklace were recreated by studying how Ravesteyn painted such things elsewhere, while the missing ornament at the back of the head was left blank, because no evidence supported an invention. It is a lesson in honest restoration. The sitter, a Dutch woman of the 1620s, announces status through her fine, layered linen.","c":"Ravesteyn was a sober, reliable portraitist who worked in The Hague in the shadow of Michiel van Mierevelt, and this portrait shows his plain dignity and his skill with white fabric: layered collars are hard to paint, and the translucency here is the picture's technical interest. As an object it is also a model of principled conservation, reconstructing what the evidence allows and refusing to guess the rest. The likeness is quiet and the interest is in the details."},"cma:140344":{"d":"Hunters are shown near ruins in a bright, Italian-looking landscape, in a canvas of about 99 by 129 cm dated 1648. The scene is not Dutch: Weenix lived in Rome for five years and painted Italianate landscapes, and the bright light, the classical architecture and the exotic-looking garb of the figures all speak to that interest in foreign landscape. The ruins and the hunting party are the chief elements, set in a warm southern light.","i":"Many Dutch painters traveled to Rome in the 1630s and 1640s and brought home pictures of sunlit ruins, which Dutch buyers found glamorous and nostalgic. Ruins also carried a quiet reminder of time's passage and of the glory of ancient Rome, and a hunting party was a symbol of aristocratic leisure. The mixture of antiquity, sport and picturesque costume made a dream of the south for patrons who had never seen it.","c":"Weenix is at his best in warm southern light and in the lively figures and animals of the hunt, and this picture shows his gift for glamorous costume and glowing sky. The arrangement is a little stagey, as Italianate pictures often are, but the sense of escape is convincing. Weenix's later fame rests on Italianate harbors and landscapes, and this early picture shows how well he understood foreign light."},"cma:147016":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child in a landscape, painted in oil on a small panel of about 48 by 37 cm in 1531. Gossaert painted the frame as well, and it carries a Latin inscription asking that the Virgin's contemplation of her Child bring reconciliation. The picture is a small devotional work of refined finish, reflecting the study of Italian Renaissance ideas that Gossaert brought north after being among the first northern European artists to study in Rome.","i":"The inscription asks that her contemplation of the Child, and of the sacrifice that he will make, reconcile humanity with God. The patron was probably Mencía de Mendoza, a Spanish noblewoman and one of the most important art collectors and intellectuals of the early 1500s, so the picture was made for a learned owner who could read the Latin and enjoy the painter's classical references.","c":"Gossaert joined Netherlandish finish to Italian ideas of monumental, classical form, and the painted frame shows how fully he thought of the whole object. In devotional pictures he can be crystalline and cool, learned rather than warm, and this panel suits a collector's study more than a household altar. The refinement is real, and the idea that frame and picture should be a single work is characteristic of the best court painting."},"cma:166506":{"d":"A small winter landscape on copper mounted on wood, about 20 by 29 cm, dated between about 1680 and 1718 and attributed to Jan Griffier. Scholars have questioned the attribution, because Griffier did not generally paint on copper and mostly painted hunting scenes. The composition may be based on a print, although the source has yet to be identified. In the Dutch tradition such winter pictures are small, finely painted cabinet pieces made to be looked at closely.","i":"Winter landscapes were a Dutch specialty, especially during the cold decades of the seventeenth century, part of the Little Ice Age, when frozen canals and fields became a favorite subject. Prints spread the compositions widely, so a painter might work from an existing design, as this one may have done. The doubts about authorship are typical of the connoisseur's world: the subject is clear, but who made it, and from what source, remains open.","c":"Copper gives a smooth, glassy surface that suits crisp, small-scale detail, and the winter view is a type that nearly always pleases. The picture's interest is as much the puzzle as the painting: an attribution in question, an unidentified print behind it. As a charming but conventional winter scene it holds the eye; as a test of connoisseurship it is a good example of how much remains uncertain."},"cma:144431":{"d":"Peasants wade through a ford with their cattle, in simple but brightly colored clothes that stand out against a cool green landscape. Overgrown foliage flanks the scene, and the silver hues of the still stream shimmer with reflections. A Flemish peasant woman carries a jug, and cows are among the animals. The canvas is about 100 by 123 cm, painted around 1670, and the picture shows the fords with wading peasants and cattle that characterize Siberechts's Antwerp style.","i":"The ford was one of Siberechts's favorite subjects, an excuse to put people, animals and sky together with water and reflections. The peasants are idealized: the labor they do is crossing a stream, and the mood is of easy country life, not hardship, which suited collectors who wanted a pleasant vision of the Flemish countryside. Siberechts moved to London in 1672, and in England he became one of the first painters of country-house views.","c":"Siberechts repeated himself, and the museum says so plainly: the same peasant women with jugs, the same cows, from Antwerp to London. That gives his pictures reliability and also a certain sameness. The best passage here is the silvery stream with its reflections, and the freshness of the green is easy to like. The figures are stock types, but they are placed with a sure sense of scale within a wide, calm space."},"met:436282":{"d":"A pair of narrow panels, each about 56 by 20 cm, shows the Crucifixion and the Last Judgment as two halves of one devotional image. Among the figures are Christ, the Virgin Mary and Mary Magdalene at the Crucifixion, and angels, men and a skeleton in the Judgment, where the skeleton stands for Death. The paint has been moved from its original wooden panels to canvas in a past restoration, so the surface is not as it left the artist's hands.","i":"The two scenes complete each other: the Crucifixion is the sacrifice that makes salvation possible, and the Last Judgment is the reckoning at the end of time, when the dead rise and are sorted. A small, portable pair of this kind was meant for private prayer, and it asked its owner to think of death and of his own account. The skeleton, a standing reminder of mortality, joins the two scenes in a single meditation.","c":"Jan van Eyck's reputation rests on his command of minute detail and luminous oil, and a small pair like this asks for a magnifying glass: whole worlds of tiny figures, distance and sky are packed into narrow panels. The Judgment's teeming crowd and the Crucifixion's stricken stillness make a deliberate contrast. The transfer to canvas has altered the surface, but the invention and the miniaturist's precision survive."},"cma:141166":{"d":"A canal, the Herengracht, runs through the picture, flanked by picturesque tree-lined avenues and fine mansions. At the left of the water is a lumber yard, on which in 1662 the architect Philips Vingboons built four houses for the merchant Jacob Cromhout. The canvas is about 67 by 82 cm, painted around 1661, and Wijnants portrays the topography with such precision that the viewer can watch a district before its development.","i":"The Herengracht, the Gentlemen's Canal, was one of Amsterdam's most important waterways, lined with the houses of its richest merchants. The museum calls this the first depiction of Amsterdam in which a canal is given prominence, which makes the picture an early example of the city's own streets treated as a worthy subject. Soon after it was painted the canal was expanded, so the picture also records a place that changed almost at once.","c":"The picture's value is as a document, a precise record of a moment in Amsterdam's growth, and Wijnants, a landscape painter by trade, brings a landscapist's feeling for trees and sky to a view that others would soon treat architecturally. Berckheyde and Van der Heyden would make city views a specialty and give them sharper perspective and crisper light. This is the early, quieter beginning of that tradition."},"cma:169673":{"d":"A small canvas, about 37 by 44 cm, shows hunters in a dune landscape, the specialty of Jan Wijnants. Dunes were among the earliest subjects of independent landscape painting in the Netherlands, and the sandy, grass-tufted hills under broad skies became a Dutch specialty. The figures of the hunters are small within the rolling land, and the picture was painted sometime between about 1660 and 1680.","i":"Dune landscapes expressed the seventeenth-century Dutch interest in their own ground, with its dunes and open skies, and the hunters bring the leisure of country sport into it. The museum calls dunes Wijnants's bread-and-butter specialty, and it also notes that he owned an inn to supplement his income as a painter, a reminder that a landscape painter's living could be precarious.","c":"Wijnants repeated his dunes, as the museum's phrase bread-and-butter admits, and the formula, a sandy rise, a few trees, a wide sky and small figures, is easy to settle into. The format is modest and the touch is competent, without the drama of Ruisdael or the poetry of his better-known contemporaries. Its quiet charm is that of a well-worn path, and it suits the small scale."},"cma:125058":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 152 by 120 cm, shows a storm in a clove, the local name, from the Dutch for a cleft, for a deep wooded gorge in the Catskill Mountains of New York. The vertical format suits a gorge, with its drop and depth, and the title promises weather as the subject: a storm gathering and breaking over wild country. Cropsey painted it in 1851, early in his career, at about the age of twenty-eight.","i":"The Catskills were the American Romantic landscape's first great subject: Thomas Cole and others had painted its gorges and waterfalls, and tourists came to the Catskill Mountain House to see them. A storm is a classic Romantic emblem of the sublime, the sense of awe before nature's power, and in the 1850s it also suggested the hand of God in the landscape. Cropsey's early style owes much to Cole.","c":"A storm scene is the Romantic landscape painter's test: drama, scale and atmosphere. This is an ambitious early work in the Cole tradition, and Cropsey would find his own voice later, in the brilliant autumn colors of the 1860s for which he is best known. Seen before that fame, the picture is more conventional and theatrical, but it shows the sheer force of the Hudson River School at its mid-century height."},"cma:148960":{"d":"A small oil sketch on board, about 34 by 24 cm, of trees and a stream on a hillside, probably made on a trip through Massachusetts in the summer of 1853. A sketch painted on the spot is quick and direct: it records a response to a place, not a finished composition. Painters of the Hudson River School made such sketches outdoors in summer and kept them as a stock of observations for finished canvases in the winter studio.","i":"Summer sketching trips and winter studio work were the rhythm of the Hudson River School. The artist traveled through the countryside making studies of foliage, rocks and water, and then built them into finished pictures. Ruskin's writings, which urged painters to study nature closely, were widely read in America in the 1850s. A sketch like this shows what the painter saw before he began to compose.","c":"Oil sketches are where this generation is at its most immediate: the viewer sees the painter thinking, and the touch is fresh. The limit is that sketches were never meant to carry meaning on their own, so this is a record rather than a statement. As a study of foliage and moving water it is lively, and as the kind of thing from which grand canvases grew it is valuable."},"cma:143771":{"d":"A wide view, about 51 by 84 cm, of Mount Washington in New Hampshire, painted in 1870. The title names the peak, the highest in the northeastern United States, in the White Mountains, and the extended horizontal format suits a panoramic vista with the summit rising above its surroundings. Cropsey, better known for Hudson Valley autumns, brings the same sense of air and distance to a mountain subject.","i":"By 1870 the White Mountains were a major tourist destination, reached by railroad and served by grand hotels, and painters supplied pictures of the great views to visitors. Mount Washington is notorious for its weather: a wind chill of minus 108 degrees Fahrenheit was recorded there in 2023. A landscape of the mountain carried both the grandeur of the summit and the sense of harsh, changeable nature.","c":"Cropsey's gift is for color and atmosphere, and the best of his mountain pictures merge careful detail with a believable sense of air. By 1870 the Hudson River School was in its maturity, and such views were popular, reliable and often a little postcard-like. This one has the dignity of its subject and the polish of a professional, while the high drama of his earlier work is less in evidence."},"cma:133296":{"d":"A small canvas, about 32 by 51 cm, painted in 1882, shows the Esopus, the creek of the Hudson valley, on a gray day. The title announces weather as the subject: a day of low cloud and soft light, when colors are muted and a picture must rely on tone rather than brilliant effects. Cropsey, famous for the blazing colors of autumn, here chooses a subdued key.","i":"By the 1880s American landscape taste was moving from Hudson River School grandeur toward quieter, more atmospheric mood, the direction of Inness and the tonalists. Cropsey, in his late career, worked often on small canvases of familiar Hudson valley places, and gray weather gave them a reflective, personal note. The title joins a specific local place to a specific mood, which is typical of the period's interest in how weather feels.","c":"A modest late work, and the question it raises is whether Cropsey can hold the eye without his color fireworks. A gray day is a demanding subject, since all depends on delicate gradations of tone and the handling of light on water. It shows range, and a willingness to be quiet, though it lacks the drama of the pictures that made his name."},"cma:117774":{"d":"A couple most likely dances the minuet in a pavilion, in a picture that the museum calls a pastiche of several works by Watteau. The canvas is about 55 by 47 cm and was painted in the 1720s by Jean-Baptiste Pater, Watteau's only pupil. It belongs to the fête galante, the genre of elegant outdoor entertainments in which conversation, music, dance and flirtation fill the scene.","i":"Watteau invented the fête galante, based on the fêtes by which the aristocracy escaped the tedium of life at the French court, and it depicts the same amusements: conversation, music, dance and the pursuit of love. The minuet was considered the most flirtatious of dances, so a dancing couple carries a double meaning of grace and courtship. After Watteau's death Pater finished some of his teacher's compositions and copied them for reproduction.","c":"Pater is Watteau's best-known follower, and a pastiche shows both his skill and his limits. He borrows Watteau's poise and silks, but the melancholy undertone that gives Watteau his depth is missing, and the result is lighter and shallower. The charm is real, a sparkle of costume and gesture, but assembling figures from the master's inventions can show the joins, and the picture is more decorative than poetic."},"cma:130161":{"d":"On a canvas of about 65 by 54 cm, an oval scene framed by a floral border shows reapers peacefully gathering sheaves of wheat while shepherds rest with their flocks in the foreground. The picture is Summer, from a series of the four seasons painted by Pater between about 1720 and 1736, and it began as part of a screen or decorative panel. Its companion Spring is also at the museum; Autumn and Winter are in the Museu d'Art de Catalunya in Barcelona.","i":"The setting of the companion Spring is believed to be Montmorency, the estate of the financier and collector Pierre Crozat, and Summer counters Spring's aristocratic leisure with rural labor. But it is the unchanging pastoral, not social comment: the reapers gather the wheat peacefully, and nothing suggests hardship. The idyll suited the decorative purpose of a screen, in the long tradition of country life seen as the harmony of people and the seasons.","c":"The pastoral is a tradition that goes back to Virgil, and Pater treats it with a light, decorative touch: little figures, soft light, an easy rhythm of work and rest. It is charming and carefully ordered, and it asks for no more than pleasure. More idyllic than a statement about class, it lacks the real labor of Dutch or Flemish peasants, and even the harvest seems to have been arranged for a salon."},"cma:128877":{"d":"On a canvas of about 65 by 54 cm, an oval scene framed by a floral border, Spring shows aristocratic leisure in a setting believed to be the grounds of Montmorency, the country estate of the Parisian financier and collector Pierre Crozat. It is one of a series of the four seasons painted between about 1720 and 1736, and it was originally part of a screen or decorative panel. Summer, its companion, is also in the museum.","i":"Crozat was the patron and close friend of Watteau, and some of Watteau's park scenes are known to depict his estate, so Pater's choice of Montmorency for his own Spring ties him to his teacher's world. In the series, Spring stands for the leisure of the aristocracy and Summer for rural labor, but the decorative purpose keeps the mood idyllic, in the manner of the unchanging pastoral.","c":"Pater inherits Watteau's gardens and silks, but where Watteau's park scenes carry reverie and a faint melancholy, Pater's are brighter and shallower. As part of a decorative scheme the picture's job is to charm, and it does. The oval shape and floral border show the genre's function as furniture for a fashionable room, and the interest lies in the lineage that runs from Watteau through Crozat to his pupil."},"cma:139729":{"d":"A nude man lies asleep, with wings and poppies, on a wide canvas about 98 by 130 cm, painted around 1771. It began as an académie, a study of the nude male model of the kind that was the cornerstone of artistic training. By adding the wings and the poppies Restout turned the study into a specific subject, and he first exhibited it in 1783 under the title Morpheus, the god of sleep.","i":"Drawing and painting the nude model taught artists to depict the body in complex poses, but by the late 1700s some began to see such studies as independent works of art. Ovid's Metamorphoses probably inspired the subject: it places the god of sleep in a cave with sleep-inducing poppies at its entrance. The wings and poppies identify the sleeper, and the picture turns a studio exercise into a poetic image of rest.","c":"The picture's origin as a study is its strength: the body is built from the studio, with the anatomical conviction that academic training gave. The additions are slight, and the idea is more interesting than the drama; it lacks the poetry of Girodet's later Sleep of Endymion, which made the sleeping nude a subject of real mystery. As a record of the Academy's habits turning into an art of their own, it is revealing."},"cma:155821":{"d":"A woman, Sally Avery Olds, is portrayed in oil on canvas, about 73 by 61 cm, painted in 1837 by Jeptha Homer Wade. It is of almost the same size and date as the portrait of Nathaniel Olds in the same museum, so the two are probably companions. American portraits of women in the 1830s typically show a plain dark dress, a white collar or fichu and a lace cap against a plain background, with attention concentrated on the face.","i":"Wives were commonly portrayed alongside their husbands, and the pairs hung together in the home as a record of a marriage and a household. The woman's portrait was expected to show modesty, composure and the domestic virtues, and to match her husband's in size and style. The painter, Jeptha Wade, later became a telegraph pioneer, but in 1837 he was painting portraits.","c":"As a pendant, the question is how the pair work together, and a woman's portrait of this date is usually the quieter of the two. Painters of Wade's kind aimed at a clear likeness at a fair price, and plain honesty was their strength. The limits are the limits of the provincial trade: simple modeling, little invention. Its value is in the record it keeps of a particular couple in 1837."},"cma:155822":{"d":"A man wears unusual spectacles: glasses with four tinted lenses, two of which are hinged side shields. This style was typically worn to protect sensitive eyes from excessive light, dust or wind, and because it was used in open-air travel, it was sometimes called carriage or railroad spectacles. The portrait is oil on canvas, about 77 by 61 cm, painted in 1837 by Jeptha Homer Wade.","i":"The painter of this portrait later founded the Western Union Telegraph Company and became one of Cleveland's wealthiest industrialists, and his grandson Jeptha Wade II was a founder of the Cleveland Museum of Art and gave the land on which it stands. The portrait comes from the painter's earlier career. The tinted spectacles have given it a local fame, and it has even inspired Halloween costumes in the Cleveland area.","c":"Wade was a painter before he was a telegraph magnate, and the portrait has the plain, direct manner of an American provincial portraitist of the 1830s. The tinted spectacles are the picture's distinctive note: a single odd object that individualizes the sitter and fixes the date in the age of the first railroads. The result is earnest and unaffected, and its interest lies as much in its detail and its history as in its handling."},"cma:151807":{"d":"On a tiny sheet of copper, about 15 by 21 cm, the goddesses wait for Paris to judge them: Hera at the left of the group, Athena seen from the back, and Venus, who wins the golden apple. Wtewael signed and dated the picture on a rock at the lower right, in 1602. The smooth copper and the precise handling suit the polished, elegant, slightly artificial manner of the Utrecht Mannerists, in which nudes are turned into graceful arabesques.","i":"The Trojan prince Paris was asked to decide which goddess was the most beautiful. Hera offered him political power and Athena military prowess, but he gave the golden apple to Venus, goddess of love, who promised him Helen, the most beautiful mortal in the world, and that choice started the Trojan War. Showing one goddess from the back follows the old pattern of the Three Graces. The story could be read as a warning against choosing pleasure over wisdom.","c":"Wtewael's cabinet pictures on copper are witty, erotic and finely made, glossy as enamel, and this one has all of those qualities in a very small space. The cost is artifice: the figures are more patterns than bodies, and next to Rubens's later, fleshier Judgment of Paris the effect is cool. But as a connoisseur's picture, made to be admired close up and discussed, it is ingenious and highly polished."},"met:437261":{"d":"A vast panorama of cliffs, river and plain opens behind small figures who act out sacred stories. In the foreground Saint Jerome does penance, and farther back Saint John the Baptist baptizes Christ in the river. Painted in oil on wood about 1515, the picture is a world landscape, a type in which the scenery is the real subject and the saints are small, so that the eye travels across the rocks and water as if on a journey.","i":"Jerome, the scholar who left Rome for the desert, is the model penitent, and John the Baptist preached repentance in the wilderness before he baptized Christ. Linking the two in one landscape ties together two desert stories of turning back to God. The winding river and the pathways can be read as an image of the soul's journey through the world, and a viewer was invited to travel through the view and meditate on it.","c":"Patinir made the world landscape a formula, and Dürer, who met him in 1521, called him the good landscape painter. The strengths are the blue-green distance, the fantastic cliffs and the invention of a whole world on a small panel. The figures are minute and a little schematic, and the rocks follow a pattern, but the scenic power is real and was widely imitated, as Herri met de Bles's pictures show."},"met:15289":{"d":"A man is portrayed in watercolor on ivory in a tiny square format, 5.4 cm on each side, painted in 1827 by Johann Carl Koch, who was then about twenty-one. A square is an unusual shape for a miniature, since most are oval, and at this size the face can be little bigger than a thumbnail. The painter's task is to give a recognizable likeness in a few fine strokes over the pale, luminous ivory.","i":"By the 1820s the portrait miniature was a standard way for the middle classes to record a face, a keepsake for family and friends, and often a gift to a bride or betrothed. The sitter is unnamed, so the picture speaks only of a gentleman of the time, in the plain dress of the age. The date, 1827, comes a little more than a decade before photography began to take over the role that miniatures had filled.","c":"At five centimeters a miniature leaves no room for evasion: the likeness lives in a few touches, and the best miniaturists of the 1820s achieved a clean, fine finish and a quiet, polite dignity. The unusual square format gives this one a slightly modern look. It is the work of a very young painter, and as a likeness of an anonymous gentleman it has the usual limits of flattery and formula."},"met:925118":{"d":"A courtly gathering fills a copper panel of about 57 by 80 cm, painted around 1738. The title announces courtly leisure, and Platzer's pictures of this kind typically crowd a palace interior or garden with many small figures in silk costume, with musicians, dancers, card players or a feast. The smooth copper suits the tiny, bright details, and the result is a glittering stage on which the court amuses itself.","i":"Platzer worked in Vienna in the years when the Habsburg court under Charles VI was famous for its lavish entertainments, and a picture of courtly amusement offered its collectors a glimpse of that world of music, play and display. Such scenes celebrate pleasure and also hint at its vanity, so the viewer could enjoy the glitter and the gentle moral at once. The audience was the connoisseur, who looked at small pictures at close quarters.","c":"Platzer is a miniaturist working at a larger size, and his strength is a lively inventiveness in costume, gesture and incident, with a finish suited to copper. The weakness is a tendency to crowded, stage-like arrangements where the figures are more doll-like than felt. It does not aim at depth, but it is a delightful picture to scan, one small episode at a time, and it shows the Rococo court at play."},"cma:170235":{"d":"At the center the artist himself, idealized and clad in a fur-lined robe, holds up a painting for the inspection of a visiting connoisseur. Around him a studio teems with live models, pupils and assistants and with examples of art from earlier eras. Twelve paintings appear within the painting, on the wall, the easel and the floor. The copper panel measures about 42 by 60 cm, and it is a small, highly detailed picture scaled for a connoisseur's cabinet.","i":"The picture is a manifesto: painting is presented as a noble, intellectual pursuit that demands rigorous training and practice, with models, pupils and masterworks of the past all in the room. The paintings within the painting refer to great artists of earlier times and together present an allegory of the five senses. The visiting connoisseur is also the picture's ideal buyer, who sees the artist at his best.","c":"Platzer packs a dozen pictures, a crowd of figures and an allegory into a small copper, and his talent for dense, witty arrangements is fully used. The idealized self-portrait is faintly comic, and the whole thing works as an advertisement for the painter's range. The density can overwhelm and the figures are doll-like, but that is exactly the pleasure the cabinet connoisseur wanted, and it rewards a long look."},"cma:150719":{"d":"A crowded scene of the Romans seizing the Sabine women to be their wives, painted on a wide canvas of about 110 by 160 cm in Naples around 1640. The museum calls it the last of four versions of the subject by Schönfeld, two of them now in the Hermitage in St. Petersburg and another in a private collection. The subject is a moment of violent action, and the broad format gives room for a surge of bodies.","i":"Shortly after Rome was founded, the Romans, short of wives, abducted the women of the neighboring Sabines. In the war that followed, the Sabine women intervened and made peace between the two sides. The story was both a heroic legend of Roman origins and a vivid pretext for dramatic, many-figured action, and later artists, from Rubens and Poussin to David, returned to it, finding in the women's intervention a lesson about peace.","c":"Schönfeld, a German who worked in Italy, painted this subject at least four times, which shows both its appeal and his love of busy, many-figure action in the Neapolitan manner. His strengths are energy and quick, bright color; the weakness is a drama that can tip into chaos and mannerism. Next to Poussin's sculptural clarity in his Rape of the Sabines, Schönfeld is quicker, more theatrical and less composed."},"cma:78982":{"d":"Christ rises in a drama of vivid color and explosive poses, and in the luminous landscape three holy women hasten toward his vacant tomb. The oil on copper is about 61 by 46 cm, painted in 1622, and its meticulous brushwork gives exquisite detail to the figures and the distance. It is a small, intensely finished cabinet picture for private viewing, not an altarpiece.","i":"According to Christian belief, three days after his crucifixion Christ rose from the dead. The Gospels do not describe the Resurrection itself but the women's discovery of the empty tomb, and the picture joins the two: the moment of rising and the women hurrying to find the place empty. König interprets the traditional theme with vivid colors and explosive poses to express the drama of the event.","c":"König works in the wake of Adam Elsheimer, whose small copper paintings made a fashion for glowing landscape and fine detail, and the smooth copper suits the meticulous brushwork. The strengths are brilliant color and a luminous distance; the risk is a theatricality in the poses that can read as show. As a cabinet picture of the German Baroque it is polished and impressive, and the landscape is the most original part."},"cma:543981":{"d":"Four people sit around a green-topped card table in a high, blue-green drawing room. At the left a young woman in a pale silk gown trimmed with black lace and tall powdered hair holds her cards close, and beside her a man in a dark brown coat leans on the table to watch her. At the right a young man in a grey suit with a red collar and cream breeches shows his hand, while an older woman in a black gown and white lace cap sits beside him holding a book. A fire burns in a marble fireplace, gilt-framed landscapes and a scene of classical figures hang on the walls, and a gilt mirror gleams between them. A patterned carpet fills the floor.","i":"The museum explains that the conversation piece, a picture of a family in an informal moment, was invented in England in the eighteenth century and was popular for its relaxed approach. Here a father and daughter, and a mother and son, are weighing the next hand of cards. The family has recently lost a loved one, so the parents wear full mourning in black and the children wear lesser mourning in mauve, white and grey. The room is idealized rather than real, showing the sitters' taste in fashionable decoration. Zoffany, a German-born painter at the height of his career around 1772, was the leading master of the type, and the picture stayed with the Dutton family for more than 150 years.","c":"Zoffany's skill lies in making an arrangement look like a moment. The arc of figures around the table, the bright cards, the mother's open book and the textures of silk, lace and carpet are managed with poise, and the mourning lends quiet seriousness to what could have been a frivolous scene. The figures, however, are less expressive than their surroundings. The faces are alike and pleasant, the poses a little fixed, and the gilded furniture and pictures attract as much attention as the sitters. It is a refined, polite picture, with warmth held in check."},"cma:173683":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Hinman B. Hurlbut, painted in the 1870s in oil on canvas, about 98 by 77 cm. The upright format allows a half-length or three-quarter-length figure, and American portraits of professional and business men of the decade typically show the sitter in dark formal dress, in a plain white shirt and cravat, against a plain or lightly shaded ground, so that the face carries the picture.","i":"In the Gilded Age portraits of lawyers, merchants and civic leaders were commissioned to mark career milestones and hung in offices, banks, courtrooms and clubs, as well as in the family home. Many were painted from photographs, which saved the sitter's time and fixed a likeness. The title names a local man of standing; the picture's purpose was to keep his presence, authority and respectability before his circle.","c":"John Harrison Witt worked in a trade that valued a sound, recognizable likeness and a dignified presence, and the tests of such a portrait are modest: is the face alive, and are the hands and dress convincingly handled? The formula was fixed and the room for invention small. Its interest is as a record of a man of standing in the 1870s and of the confident, plain manner in which such men chose to be shown."},"cma:140408":{"d":"Two peregrine falcons are shown with their fresh duck prey, in a vigorous scene of predators and kill. Peregrines were then called duck hawks, from their habit of hunting waterfowl. The oil on canvas, about 65 by 92 cm, was painted about 1827 and relates to Audubon's Birds of America, which sought to depict every species of native North American bird posed in action with elements of its habitat.","i":"Audubon aimed to show each bird life-size, in action and in its habitat, and Birds of America, printed in London, made him the premier naturalist in the United States. After his death his name was attached to conservation groups. In recent years, as his slaveholding and his looting of Indigenous graves have become more widely acknowledged, an increasing number of these groups have been changing their names, and the picture is now seen with both his achievement and his record in mind.","c":"The pleasure here is drama: predator, prey and a sense of a hunt just ended, with the birds' tension and plumage carefully observed. Audubon's postures can be theatrical and he arranges his birds like actors, but that is part of what makes his images memorable. As an oil painting it is a relatively rare work by a man who is chiefly known for prints and watercolors, and it is powerful as natural history and as picture."},"cma:95257":{"d":"A street singer is shown with her child, in a composition drawn tightly around the woman. Opie includes none of the goods that street singers often sold, so the viewer meets a person rather than a vendor, and the bond between mother and child is the center of the picture. The oil on canvas is about 77 by 64 cm, painted in the 1700s, and the woman sings to feed her baby.","i":"John Opie's working-class roots made him sympathetic to laborers, and the museum notes that this honest portrayal of British street life has a sympathy rarely seen among his generation of British artists, who often presented the lower classes more coldly. Much of Britain's population was poor, but beggars and street vendors had a particularly low status. By leaving out the wares, Opie makes the viewer recognize her humanity.","c":"Opie's strength is sympathy without sentimentality, or nearly so: the tight framing and the absence of picturesque props keep the woman from becoming a type. The danger lies in the Madonna-like pairing of mother and child, which invites an easy, pitying response. But few British painters of his time looked at the poor so directly, and the picture has an honesty that more polished contemporaries lack."},"met:16038":{"d":"A lady is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, in a miniature of about 8 by 7 cm, painted in 1822 by John Robinson. At this size the miniature is larger than many, so the painter has room for hair, collar and a little of her dress. Women's miniatures of the early 1820s typically show elaborately curled hair and a lace or ruffled collar, and the soft, pale ivory gives the complexion its glow.","i":"In the early nineteenth century the portrait miniature was a standard way for the middle and upper classes to record a face, and it was given as a gift, worn as jewelry or kept in a case. The sitter is not named, so only the style and date describe her. Miniatures like this were a flourishing trade in 1822, not long before photography, from 1839, began to replace them.","c":"A competent miniature of its decade: smooth modeling, a clear likeness and a pleasing, restrained palette. The best miniaturists of the 1820s achieved real character in a few brushstrokes, and an anonymous lady offers little to compare. The genre's limits are flattery and formula, and its virtues are intimacy and finish. This one should be valued as a charming, well-made keepsake more than as a discovery."},"cma:97298":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 90 by 67 cm, shows the edge of the woods, a threshold where trees give way to open ground, painted in the 1890s by John Semon. The vertical format suits trunks and foliage and lets the eye move from the foreground up through the trees to the light beyond. It is the kind of quiet, enclosed American landscape that was popular in the final decades of the nineteenth century.","i":"By the 1890s American landscape painters had absorbed the lessons of the French Barbizon painters and of George Inness, who valued mood, soft light and intimate scenery over the grand views of the earlier Hudson River School. The edge of a wood was a favorite motif: a place between shade and light, wild and cultivated, that suited a reflective, private feeling about nature. The title promises such a threshold.","c":"A modest, sincere landscape of its time, the sort that depends on atmosphere and a good sense of light, not on novelty. Semon is a lesser-known name, and the picture belongs to the large body of pleasant, Barbizon-influenced American landscapes that fill the museums. Its strengths are quiet tonal harmony and the pleasure of a shaded interior; its weakness is a familiarity that makes it blend into the crowd."},"cma:173666":{"d":"A view of the village of Eemnes in the central Netherlands, painted in 1778 on a small canvas of about 32 by 42 cm. The title names a particular place, so the picture belongs to the Dutch tradition of topographical views, in which a village or town is shown as it actually stood, with its houses, trees and sky. At this scale it is a cabinet picture to be examined closely and enjoyed for its record of a place.","i":"Dutch painters had been recording villages and towns since the seventeenth century, and in the late eighteenth century patriotic and antiquarian interest in the country's own places kept the type alive. A view of Eemnes would have pleased a local owner or a collector of Dutch scenery, and it is also a historical document of a settlement as it looked in 1778. The picture asks to be read as a likeness of a place.","c":"Topographical views of this kind reward fidelity and a sense of quiet light. The strength of such a picture is its sense of place; the weakness is a certain dryness, the finished look of a view made to be recognized more than felt. Next to the poetry of the Golden Age painters it is a modest thing, but as a record of a Dutch village in 1778 it holds interest."},"cma:302209":{"d":"Colonel Charles Heathcote stands in a relaxed but commanding pose beside a large oak tree in the foreground of an expansive landscape. He wears military dress, and the single epaulette on his right shoulder marks his rank as colonel. The figure is relatively smoothly painted, while the landscape is painted far more energetically, with thick daubs of color. The canvas is about 127 by 100 cm and dates from about 1771 to 1772.","i":"The oak is a well-chosen companion: a symbol of English strength and long-established landed families, standing for the colonel's rooted place in the country. Placing a sitter in his landscape, with the military dress of his rank, announced both office and property. Wright was a Derbyshire painter of the provincial gentry and industrialists, and the museum notes that his handful of small-scale full-length portraits include his earliest experiments in landscape painting.","c":"Wright is remembered for his candlelit scenes of science and for his later volcanoes, and this daylight portrait shows him discovering landscape. The colonel's pose is persuasive, but the figure is the less lively part of the picture, and the energetic daubs of the landscape are where the painter's interest clearly lies. It is a pleasing demonstration of a painter's attention shifting from the sitter to his setting."},"cma:122349":{"d":"Two sisters, Amabel at the left and Mary Jemima at the right, play on the grounds of their family's country estate. They wear loose, flowing garments invented for the portrait rather than their usual outdoor dresses, and Mary Jemima, in a pose that echoes the ancient Roman statue of a girl with a dove, shields a dove from the family dog. The canvas is large, about 196 by 170 cm, painted about 1761.","i":"Their father commissioned the painting to hang in the family's London house in St. James's Square, where it announced their status as landowners and the family's cultivated taste. Reynolds had returned from Italy, where his studies inspired him to bring classical references into his portraits, and here the children play a scene of pastoral antiquity. The family called Mary Jemima, the younger sister, Mouse, an intimate touch under the grand manner.","c":"Reynolds's gift here is to make a child's portrait both monumental and playful: the interlocking poses, the dove and the dog turn a formal commission into a small drama. The classical conceit is a little stagey, and the invented costumes keep it from simple naturalism, but the warmth and energy of the girls are persuasive. It shows him, early in his rise to the presidency of the Royal Academy, joining ancient art to modern childhood."},"cma:101852":{"d":"A woman mourns with her head turned away from the viewer, and on a table, partly hidden by her right elbow, lies a sparrow that can barely be seen. The muted colors evoke a sense of melancholy. The picture is oil on canvas, about 80 by 67 cm, an eighteenth-century portrait of the wife of Captain George Collier, later an admiral, and the museum now attributes it to a follower of Reynolds.","i":"The artist draws an analogy between this woman and Lesbia, the married woman who inspired the Roman poet Catullus, and one of whose poems laments the death of her pet sparrow. A classical allusion flattered a sitter of education and sensibility, and in the late eighteenth century the mourned pet bird was a popular sentimental theme, as in Greuze's weeping girl with her dead canary. The picture mixes portrait, literature and sentiment.","c":"The attribution story is itself a lesson in connoisseurship. A picture admired for years as a Reynolds, given to the museum in 1920, was demoted after considerable study to a follower, who had learned the elegant pose, the literary allusion and the melancholy palette but, in the museum's judgment, not the master's authority. The sparrow half hidden under her elbow is a witty, poignant touch, and the picture is better than its demotion implies."},"cma:150140":{"d":"A low, wide still life laid out on a plain stone ledge: a wooden box, a dark glass bottle, a few walnuts, an elaborate Venetian glass and several turrones, the Spanish sweets made of pine nuts, honey, almonds and hazelnuts. The objects stand close together across the broad canvas, which is about 58 by 97 centimeters, and each is given its own surface to describe: shell, wood, glass, confection. The museum suggests the picture does not record a real snack but plays with contrasts and harmonies of shape, texture and color.","i":"Van der Hamen was born in Madrid to a family from the Netherlands and worked at the Spanish royal court, though he was best known for portraits and still lifes. Sweets, nuts and imported Venetian glass were luxuries, so the picture flatters the taste of its owner while showing off the painter's command of materials. Pictures of food were sometimes read as reminders that pleasure passes, but nothing here insists on a moral. The pleasure is mainly in looking.","c":"A lean picture: one ledge, a handful of objects, no profusion. That restraint is its virtue, since the glass, the shells and the sweets each get a distinct, believable surface. Van der Hamen was working within the Spanish bodegón tradition, which prizes honest observation over display, and this canvas honors it. What it lacks is tension. Everything is balanced so evenly that the effect is courteous rather than intense, closer to a well-made demonstration than a discovery."},"cma:699644":{"d":"A close-up view of a woodland stream, painted from a low vantage point so that the viewer seems to crouch at the water's edge. Tree roots, angled boulders, cushions of moss and pockets of vegetation fill the frame, while the water drops in gentle cascades between the rocks. The canvas measures only about 29 by 49 centimeters, and its intimate scale suits the subject: the focus is the stream and its banks rather than a panorama.","i":"The museum thinks this was probably painted on a summer trip to New Hampshire's White Mountains in 1870, though the exact spot was not recorded. The White Mountains were among the most painted American landscapes of the day, and Beers led sketching trips there and elsewhere for women students. Close views of rock, moss and root also reflect a mid-century taste for minutely observed nature, partly encouraged by the writings of John Ruskin.","c":"Beers learned to paint from her brothers William and James Hart, and her work carries the Hudson River School's respect for observed detail. This picture succeeds by modesty: the low viewpoint and tight crop put the viewer inside the scene rather than in front of it. The risk of such close-ups is a catalogue of textures, and the small format leaves little room for a larger idea. It also records a woman earning her living as a landscape painter."},"cma:126243":{"d":"A storm breaks over a rocky shore of the Isle of Wight. In the foreground a family gathers in distress, having discovered that one of its members has drowned; beyond them a ship founders in a wild sea beneath swirling dark clouds and flashes of lightning. The canvas, about 51 by 68 centimeters, pairs a small human story with the larger drama of the weather. It is dated only to the 1790s, after the artist's visit to the island in 1791.","i":"Storms and shipwrecks were a staple of late eighteenth-century painting, fed by the taste for the Sublime, which found a thrill in terror viewed from safety. Ibbetson adds a sentimental human note: the grief happens on the shore, where the viewer can identify with it. The Isle of Wight was by then known to artists and travelers as a picturesque destination. A family mourning a drowned relative was a story that could be read at a glance.","c":"Ibbetson began as a copyist of Dutch landscapes, and the picture has a copyist's command of inherited formulas: churning sea, torn sky, a ship in extremis. Those formulas work. The lightning and dark clouds supply real theater, and the grieving group gives the storm a human scale. But the two halves do not quite fuse, and the effect stays closer to stage design than to observation. It is a lively period piece rather than a storm that overwhelms."},"cma:136680":{"d":"Saint Jerome appears as an old penitent in the desert, striking his chest with a stone while he contemplates a skull. Rather than the scholar at his desk, Ribera shows the hermit who has withdrawn from the world, with a book at hand in which Jerome will write; the painter signed his name on the book's spine. The scene is built from strong contrasts of light and dark, and the figure is modeled on a naturalistic, unidealized model. At about 129 by 100 centimeters, it is the kind of devotional picture that could hang in a church or a private collection.","i":"Jerome (about 347 to 420), who translated the Bible into Latin, is often shown as a scholar or a cardinal, but Counter-Reformation art favored the penitent: a hermit beating his breast with a stone in imitation of Christ's suffering. The skull is a memento mori, a reminder that earthly vanity ends. Catholic reformers stressed penance after Protestant criticism, and Naples, where Ribera worked, was a lively center of such devotion. The book points to Jerome's other identity as the Church's great translator.","c":"Ribera was one of the most convincing painters of old, worn bodies, and Jerome gave him a subject that suited him. The saint's flesh and bone are observed rather than idealized, and the Caravaggesque light gives him weight and urgency without theatrics. Ribera painted Jerome many times, so the type is familiar and the composition does not break new ground. Its force comes from conviction in the handling, which turns a standard devotional subject into a study of physical endurance."},"nga:214129":{"d":"A bundle of dark green asparagus, tied with brown string, lies at the center of a small canvas, its spears pointing toward the upper right and its lighter, jagged ends toward the lower left. Two shiny red tomatoes sit in front, at lower right, and a green squash or melon with vertical light and dark stripes and a short curling vine rises behind on the left. A thin vine with broad leaves trails to the right. Everything rests on a gray surface against a brown wall that fades from lighter on the right to darker on the left.","i":"Lacroix painted this in 1865, when some American still-life painters were moving away from rich tables of fruit and flowers toward plainer subjects from the kitchen garden. The vegetables are given no silver or crystal to dress them up, so the picture values the produce itself: ripeness, gloss, the tied bundle that suggests the market or kitchen. Such pictures were made for private homes, where fresh produce in paint appealed to a taste for modest, well-observed nature rather than display.","c":"Lacroix keeps his means few and his design clear. The diagonal of the asparagus gives the picture a thrust it would otherwise lack, and the gloss of the tomatoes against the gray ledge is where it really sings. The wall's gradient adds a quiet atmosphere. The ambition is small, a careful study rather than a statement, but within that scale it is well judged, and its plainness is a welcome alternative to heavier banquet pieces."},"met:436709":{"d":"A portrait of the French painter Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres as a young man, identified as an artist by the easel that appears with him. The canvas, about 86 by 70 centimeters, is a conventional portrait size, suited to a study or salon wall. The date given, about 1850 to 1860, falls when Ingres himself was between seventy and eighty, so the picture must look back across decades, probably to an earlier likeness. It belongs to the tradition of the artist's portrait, in which the tools of the trade stand in for biography.","i":"Ingres was the leading figure of the French academic tradition, famed for the purity of his line, and by mid-century he was venerated by pupils and admirers. A picture of him as a young man, probably made after the fact, belongs to that cult of the master: it fixes the future great artist at the stage of promise. Portraits of famous artists painted after the fact were often tributes. Madame Héquet left a far smaller trace than her subject.","c":"The interest here lies in the subject more than in the painter. A portrait of the young Ingres invites comparison with the master's own exactness, and any homage is measured against it. As an object the picture is best valued as a document of how later admirers remembered the artist, with the easel stating his profession as plainly as a caption. It reads as sincere rather than inventive, a respectful tribute rather than a fresh act of portraiture."},"cma:134933":{"d":"Children stand in a chain-like line, posed patiently as they wait for the dance to begin, while a musician sits in shadow with his back to the viewer. The children are brightly lit and individualized, with fine lace and slim, rather weary-looking boys, and their elongated shadows stretch beside them. The canvas, about 92 by 120 centimeters, is a mid-sized genre picture, and its mood is quiet and a little wistful rather than boisterous.","i":"Dancing was a common emblem of family harmony, since it depends on order and cooperation. The individuality of the faces suggests a family portrait, and the melancholy tone has led to the hypothesis that the children include both living and dead siblings, though that remains a hypothesis. The Le Nain brothers ran a collective studio in Paris from about 1630 and made their name with dignified pictures of ordinary people. This canvas has also been attributed to a follower of Mathieu Le Nain, the Master of the Games.","c":"The idea is better than the execution. A chain of waiting children, a musician turned away, the pause before the music: it is an unusual and affecting moment, and the lace and tired faces are carefully observed. But the figures are static and evenly lit, and their shadows do not quite explain their forms, which is the museum's own reason for doubting Mathieu's hand. The space feels staged. It lacks the quiet gravity of the best Le Nain pictures."},"cma:135142":{"d":"Two companion canvases, each about 92 by 121 centimeters, catalogued together as a pair. Vintage Scene places drinking men in the shade behind a smiling young woman who is surrounded by chubby children and amused by the creeping hand of her drunk companion. Peasant Children Dancing shows outdoor merriment with a piper, villagers in coarse clothing, ripe apples and a gleaming drinking vessel. Both treat country life with humor and affection, using earthy tones and sturdy, rounded figures.","i":"As companions, the two pictures set wine and the grape harvest against music and dance, the twin pleasures of country festivity. The Le Nain brothers had made peasant subjects fashionable in Paris, and the museum thinks the pair was probably painted in the 1650s by the Master of the Béguins, a Flemish artist who worked in their manner. Whether viewers read them as comedy or as sympathetic portrayals of the poor, they reflect a lively mid-century market.","c":"Seen together the pair works better than either picture alone, since the quiet flirtation of one answers the bright festivity of the other. Both are lively and agreeable, but their types are broad and their drawing relies on repeated formulas, and neither has the austere dignity of the brothers' own pictures. As evidence of how the Le Nain style was imitated and marketed, they are more instructive than moving."},"cma:135143":{"d":"In the shade at the back, a few men drink and joke, while in the center a young woman surrounded by chubby children turns toward the viewer with a coy smile. She is aware that her drunk companion's hand is slowly creeping toward her. The canvas, about 92 by 121 centimeters, mixes country pleasure with a hint of bawdiness, and it is considered a companion to Peasant Children Dancing in the same collection.","i":"The title points to the grape harvest, and the wine connects the scene to the French countryside the Le Nain brothers remembered from their childhood near Laon. Peasants drinking was a popular subject with Flemish painters, and the museum notes that this picture has also been attributed to the Master of the Béguins, a Flemish artist working in the Le Nain manner in Paris. Viewers would likely have enjoyed it as a comic anecdote about wine and desire.","c":"This is livelier than the stately Le Nain peasants, and less dignified. The knowing glance gives the picture its hook, and the grouping around the young woman is clear and well managed. But the chubby children and broad types tilt toward caricature, which is where a Flemish imitator differs from the brothers' sober gravity. It is an agreeable genre picture that borrows a famous name's prestige more than it earns it."},"cma:135144":{"d":"An outdoor scene of rustic merriment: villagers, children among them, dance and make music in coarse, heavy-folded clothing, with white peasant caps, wide eyes and round cheeks giving the faces a comic good humor. A piper plays, the elbow of his sleeve worn through, and earthy tones stress the country setting. Small still-life touches, including ripe apples and a reflective drinking vessel, sit among the figures. The canvas is about 92 by 120 centimeters and is a companion to Vintage Scene.","i":"The museum attributes the picture probably to the Master of the Béguins, a Flemish imitator who exploited the Le Nain brothers' success in 1650s Paris; his trademark is the béguin, the white peasant cap. At the time the treatment of the poor was debated in Paris, especially at Saint-Sulpice, where the brothers were buried, so viewers might have found either comic entertainment or a sympathetic look at rural life. The meaning remains ambiguous.","c":"The charm lies in small, well-observed details: the hole in the piper's sleeve, the apples, the gleam of a drinking vessel. They give the scene an authenticity the broad types lack. The imitator's formula of wide eyes and pudgy faces is easier to like than to take seriously, and the composition lacks the stillness that makes the genuine Le Nain brothers haunting. A cheerful, skillful pastiche, useful for what it shows about the 1650s market for peasant pictures."},"cma:135141":{"d":"A genre painting of a family gathered at a meal, as the title says, in oil on a canvas about 92 by 120 centimeters. The subject belongs to the tradition of peasant and working-family interiors for which the Le Nain brothers of Laon and Paris were known, usually with a muted palette and sober, quiet figures. The attribution to the Le Nain family and a loose date range of about 1645 to 1655 or later suggest that the museum regards authorship and exact date as unsettled.","i":"A meal, with its bread, wine and shared table, was a favorite Le Nain subject, and it let the painters show ordinary people with solemn composure. Such scenes were presumably bought by urban collectors who found in the dignity of the poor a mild moral lesson and a reminder of country life. The phrase Le Nain family in the museum's label points to studio production by the brothers or those close to them, not necessarily a single hand.","c":"Quiet pictures of this kind stand or fall by gravity of expression and economy of design, and the Le Nain at their best are hard to match at both. The label's wording, Le Nain family and a date of about 1645 to 1655 or later, suggests a canvas from the studio's orbit rather than from the finest hands. It is best taken as a representative example of the manner, not a peak of it."},"cma:97170":{"d":"Two angels attend the dead body of Christ, in a vertical canvas about 123 by 96 centimeters. The subject is a devotional image rather than a crowded narrative, centered on the body and the angels who care for it, and it invites quiet contemplation of Christ's death. The museum notes that other versions of the composition exist, in the Galleria Colonna in Rome and the Civic Museum in Bassano del Grappa, suggesting that it was a popular design.","i":"In the late sixteenth century, images of the dead Christ supported by angels, related to the Man of Sorrows type, encouraged viewers to meditate on the Passion and its link to the Eucharist, an emphasis strengthened by the Counter-Reformation. Leandro belonged to the Bassano family of painters and worked in his brother Francesco's Venetian studio, which he took over in 1592. The angels act as mourners on the viewer's behalf, modeling the grief that the image asks for.","c":"Leandro is better known as a portraitist, and this devotional canvas is a competent workshop product more than an inspired one. The subject depends on restraint, with the angels' attendance doing the emotional work without extravagant gesture, and that suits it. The comparison with Tintoretto, which the museum makes for his style, is generous: his urgency and invention are not what this picture offers, and the other versions suggest a formula repeated rather than a problem freshly solved."},"nga:219334":{"d":"A young woman sits on a waterfront verandah, seen from the lap up, her head tipped downward over slightly slumped shoulders as she looks toward the viewer with large brown eyes. Her black hair is parted in the center, with long braids at each side wrapped in a sheer straw-yellow cloth. Her white bodice is trimmed in gold, and her aquamarine skirt is patterned with golden leaves, vines and flowers. Behind her are a stone wall, a balustrade, a sliver of coral-pink drapery, a band of pale blue water and a peach-to-topaz sky.","i":"Lehmann, a French painter of German birth who trained under Ingres, signed this picture Heinrich Lehmann, 1837, Paris. The Orient it evokes was probably assembled in a Paris studio from costume, props and other artists' pictures rather than observed on the spot, and the museum's quotation marks around the word point the same way. Pictures of women in Eastern dress were popular with French collectors in the 1830s and tended to treat the sitter as a type, a fantasy of the exotic, rather than as a named individual.","c":"The handling is the picture's pleasure. The skin is smooth and warm, the modeling clear in a way that shows Lehmann's training, and the colors of coral, aquamarine, straw yellow, peach and topaz are chosen with a sure feeling for harmony. The weakness is psychological: the lowered head and half-averted gaze read as a pose, a type, more than a person. Today it works better as a display of pattern and color than as a portrait of anyone."},"cma:136844":{"d":"A self-portrait by Lié Louis Périn, painted in oil on a canvas about 64 by 53 centimeters. That is a modest size, but it is full scale in a way Périn's usual work was not: he made his living as a miniaturist, painting tiny portraits for Parisian aristocrats. Here he is his own subject and his own patron, free of a client's wishes and of the miniature's small format. The date is given as the 1790s.","i":"Périn was the son of a cloth manufacturer from Reims who came to Paris to become a miniaturist, and he rose to a reputation among the aristocracy. A self-portrait painted in the 1790s, the decade of the Revolution, can be read as an artist asserting himself as a painter and not only a maker of small keepsakes. Artists made self-portraits to show off skill, to define identity and to advertise range, and the museum notes that Périn made many.","c":"The interest is what a specialist in the very small does when given room. A self-portrait free of any client's wishes shows what the painter himself thought worth showing, and a canvas of this size is a statement of ambition from a man known for keepsakes. That ambition is clearer than any single bold passage. It is best valued as an honest calling card, the record of a miniaturist stepping beyond his specialty."},"cma:130377":{"d":"A tiny panel, only about 15 by 20 centimeters, in which Cranach packs the Baptism of Christ into a wide landscape with a verdant forest, a hilltop city and distant mountains. The heavens are open, a dove descends, and the Father's declaration that Christ is his beloved son is lettered in Latin directly onto the clouds. Everything is painted in minute detail, so the oil-on-wood picture reads like a miniature stage for the Gospel story.","i":"Cranach was a close associate of Martin Luther and worked in Wittenberg, and he adapted Christian imagery to Luther's belief that religious art should narrate scripture. Lettering the voice from heaven onto the clouds keeps the picture tied to the Gospel text, so the viewer can read what the story says. At the baptism the Trinity is revealed: Christ in the water, the Spirit as a dove, the Father's voice. A picture this small was probably meant for close, private viewing.","c":"On this scale the achievement is the landscape: forest, city and mountains sit at believable distances in a space smaller than a sheet of A5 paper, and the lettered voice is an inventive way to put scripture into the picture. As often with Cranach, narrative clarity matters more than emotional depth, and the figures serve the story rather than move us. A refined small work that shows the Lutheran taste for text more than any urge to drama."},"cma:135428":{"d":"A wide panorama of a hunt unfolds across a green valley. At the top, a white castle with gables and towers rises on a distant hill. Below it, forest covers the slopes, and a river curves from the left toward the lower right, full of swimming stags. Dozens of small figures in bright costume ride, run and aim crossbows. Dogs chase deer across the meadow, and a bear and several boars are hidden in the trees at the back. In the lower left corner, a man in dark green spans his crossbow, while at the lower right a boat carries four figures in red, green and blue. The painting is in oil, originally on wood and now transferred to masonite.","i":"Cleveland's museum text explains that the Protestant rulers of Saxony commissioned this hunt scene, with their residence, Hartenfels Castle in Torgau, in the background. The figure in dark green at lower left is John Frederick the Magnanimous, waiting with his crossbow while courtiers and dogs drive a stag across the river. His wife, the Electress Sibylle, stands at right, ready to take the first, ceremonial shot. Such hunts were elaborate, rehearsed occasions directed by horn signals. Cranach, court painter to the Saxon electors, dated the picture 1540 and signed it with his winged serpent; the number 1577 in the corner is an inventory number.","c":"As a record of court life this is a delight: costume, dogs, boats and horn signals are noted with a miniaturist's patience, and the little scenes of animals fleeing or falling keep the eye busy for a long time. The cost is focus. With so many incidents of equal size there is no centre, and the elector and electress, the people the picture is for, are easily lost. Cranach's religious and mythological pictures are more concentrated. Here the sharp colour and decorative outline make a lively tapestry of a hunt rather than a drama."},"nga:176395":{"d":"A broad, high view over a river valley, with the horizon about halfway up and a wide pale blue sky of puffy white clouds. On a rocky bluff in the foreground, among sparse trees, three soldiers in dark blue jackets and lighter blue trousers rest: one faces away, one smokes a pipe on a boulder, one gestures across the river. Below them men tend two cannons aimed across the water, beside six white tents. Beyond the river lie a town, a road lined with buildings and rows of countless tents fading into haze.","i":"Harpers Ferry, where the Shenandoah meets the Potomac, was fought over repeatedly in the Civil War, and Maryland Heights, the high ground across the river, commanded the town. William MacLeod painted this view in 1863, while the war was still on. Landscapes with soldiers and camps gave viewers at home a sense of the army's presence in a familiar countryside. The mood here is calm rather than violent: the soldiers rest, and the guns wait.","c":"MacLeod organizes a great deal of information without confusion: the bluff in front, the river's hairpin turn, and the town and tents thinning into haze make a clear, readable recession. The mood is quiet, closer to a topographical record than to the grand landscapes of Frederic Church. That is both its interest and its limit. It sets the war carefully in its place, but the soldiers are small, and little in it suggests what the fighting cost."},"cma:153603":{"d":"A half-length portrait of a seated woman, her hands tense yet elegant, with a grotesque classical mask from which a coat of arms hangs. The arms combine family emblems, including golden pile drivers that point to a family construction business. Fabrics and surface textures are rendered meticulously in the northern tradition, while the pose and the mask show the effect of the painter's years in Rome. The oil-on-wood panel is about 85 by 74 centimeters, presumably made for a prosperous household.","i":"Machtelt Suijs married Dirick van Teijlingen in 1535 and lived in Alkmaar, where Heemskerck must have painted her. The combined arms lead the museum to infer that her portrait was one of a pair, with a lost portrait of her husband carrying his own. Heemskerck had spent four years in Rome, from 1532 to 1536, and the classical mask and pose show how far Roman antiquity had changed his northern habits. Such portraits asserted family standing as well as likeness.","c":"Heemskerck joins two traditions here, not entirely smoothly. The tense, elegant hands and careful textures carry the northern habit of looking hard, while the grotesque mask is Roman showmanship that competes with the sitter's face for attention. It reads more as quotation than need. Still, the hybrid is lively, and the hands alone give the woman a nervous presence that a plainer likeness would lack. An interesting picture, a little overdressed."},"cma:153406":{"d":"A small, close portrait of a young man in clothing of the late 1400s, with an arrow that alludes to Saint Sebastian. The panel, about 30 by 24 centimeters, has been transferred from its original wood support to pressed wood. The flesh is modeled with smoky shadows in the manner of Leonardo da Vinci, giving the image an inward, haunting quality. Because the dress is contemporary, the picture is understood as a portrait of a real young man rather than a depiction of the historical saint.","i":"Sebastian, a bodyguard of the Roman emperor Diocletian who was shot with arrows for his Christian faith, was invoked against plague, and sitters sometimes borrowed his attribute to express devotion or a hope of protection. The museum stresses that the contemporary clothing makes this a portrait rather than a saint's image. It does not name the sitter. A pious or protective motive is likely, but that remains a reading rather than a fact.","c":"Marco d'Oggiono made many copies after Leonardo, so an original composition invites close attention, and it rewards it: the softly shadowed face is intimate and a little uneasy. The debt to Leonardo is obvious, and the picture does not match his command of the head, but at this small scale mood carries it. A pupil's picture in the best sense, with borrowed technique and personal feeling."},"met:436992":{"d":"A woman and a mirror carry the whole idea of this picture: The Sense of Sight, a personification painted in oil on a canvas about 76 by 63 centimeters. Allegories of the five senses were a favorite series in the 1600s, each sense given a figure and an attribute, with the mirror, spectacles or a telescope typical for Sight. The picture works as genre scene and emblem at once, a single figure paired with the object that names her theme.","i":"Sets of paintings of the five senses let seventeenth-century artists show off their observation of everyday things while hinting at a moral lesson. A mirror signals Sight, but it also signals vanity, and viewers may have read both meanings. The painter is known by the convenient name Master of the Annunciation to the Shepherds, probably a Neapolitan, and the picture is also credited to Juan Dò, a follower of Ribera in Naples.","c":"The format does the work: one figure, one object, one idea. That clarity is the strength and the limit, since an allegory of a sense asks to be decoded more than lived with. Painters in Ribera's Neapolitan circle gave such emblems unusual physical presence, and that is presumably what this picture trades on. The anonymity of the master is also a reminder of how much seventeenth-century Italian painting still lacks a secure author."},"met:437010":{"d":"Joseph and Mary arrive at Bethlehem in a broad Netherlandish landscape in which a river is a main feature. The horizontal oil-on-wood panel, about 67 by 94 centimeters, is catalogued as a landscape painting, so the setting counts for as much as the sacred figures, who belong to the story of the Nativity. It dates from about 1540 to 1550, when northern painters often placed Gospel scenes in wide, carefully described countryside.","i":"According to Luke, Joseph and Mary traveled to Bethlehem to be counted in a census ordered by the Roman emperor Augustus, and Mary gave birth there. Netherlandish painters of the mid-sixteenth century liked to set such stories in familiar northern countryside, often in a manner descended from Joachim Patinir's world landscapes, so that viewers could imagine the events in their own world. The artist is known by a convenient name taken from a Calling of Saint Matthew in Brussels.","c":"A sacred story in a lived-in landscape is one of the oldest tricks of Netherlandish painting, and it works best when the figures stay modest. The anonymous master is known only by a placeholder name, so the picture has to stand on its own: it offers charm and a clear, spacious setting rather than a distinctive voice. A pleasant, competent example of its type, more welcoming than dramatic."},"met:910384":{"d":"A man who sells books is the subject of this large upright canvas, about 170 by 103 centimeters. The title and the museum's keywords identify only a male figure and his trade, so the picture is best described by its type: a street seller portrayed with the seriousness normally given to a sitter. It is attributed to an anonymous painter active in northern Italy in the later 1600s, named after another peddler picture.","i":"Pictures of peddlers, beggars and other street figures became a distinct taste in northern Italy in the later seventeenth century, appealing to collectors who enjoyed their frankness. A book peddler belonged to a trade that carried printed matter to buyers who could not visit a shop. Viewers could enjoy the picture as an honest record of a humble trade or as a wry reflection on learning sold in the street. The meaning is not documented.","c":"Its pull is the subject: a seller of books taken seriously on a scale usually reserved for patrons and saints. Whether the man is an individual or a type is the question the picture poses, and its anonymous master gives few clues. As a curiosity of seventeenth-century taste it is excellent; as a showpiece of painterly skill it is harder to rank."},"cma:97153":{"d":"A devotional panel of the Virgin and Child with angels, painted in oil on wood in the early 1500s. Its dimensions, about 86 by 83.5 centimeters, are nearly square, which together with the painter's notname, Master of the Holden Tondo, points to the round format known as a tondo. The subject follows a well-loved pattern of Italian devotional painting, with Mary and her infant son at the center, accompanied by angels.","i":"Tondi, or round paintings, were popular in Renaissance Italy, often for domestic devotion, where a Virgin and Child could hang in a bedroom or a family chapel to focus prayer. Angels around Mary underline her role as Queen of Heaven and the divine nature of the Child. The painter has no recorded name, only a convenient label: such masters are artists whose style scholars have grouped together without a secure identity.","c":"A tondo punishes figures that are merely stacked and rewards those who lean into the curve. An anonymous master of the early 1500s was working in the long shadow of Leonardo and Raphael, whose Madonnas set the standard, and any such picture is measured against them. As a devotional object it needs no apology; as an act of invention it is probably a modest contribution to a crowded field."},"cma:144270":{"d":"An old hermit, clothed only in a rough woven cloth, looks heavenward in awe and gratitude. The scene is lit with dramatic contrasts and painted with intense naturalism, so the weathered body and the light from above seem to meet in a single moment. The saint is Paul the Hermit, who according to Catholic tradition lived on half a loaf of bread brought daily by a raven. The canvas is about 103 by 76 centimeters, an upright devotional picture.","i":"Paul the Hermit, said to have fled into the desert in the third century to escape Roman persecution, is revered as the first of the hermit saints. His life, told by Saint Jerome, includes the raven that brought him bread and the visit of Saint Anthony. For viewers in the Catholic Baroque he could stand for self-denial and trust in providence. The upward gaze links the saint's hunger to divine generosity: the loaf comes from heaven.","c":"Preti's Caravaggesque manner, built in Rome and Naples, is on show: dramatic light, a body painted without flattery. The upturned gaze is a stock Baroque gesture, effective but familiar, and the saint is less psychologically searching than Ribera's hermits. What holds the picture is the force of its light, which turns old skin and rough cloth into something physical and urgent. A strong, theatrical devotional image rather than an intimate one."},"cma:125090":{"d":"A small portrait in oil on panel, about 50 by 37 centimeters, of a man identified only as Mr. C. Initials like this were a common way to name private sitters in exhibition catalogues, and the format suggests a study of a friend or model rather than a formal commission. The American painter Max Bohm (1868 to 1923) made it in the early 1890s, when he was in his early twenties.","i":"A title that gives only an initial keeps the sitter private, a courtesy common in nineteenth-century portrait exhibitions, and it suggests someone known to the painter's circle: a friend, a patron, a fellow artist. For a young painter in the early 1890s, informal portrait studies were a standard way to practice likeness and handling. The picture's meaning is therefore personal and professional rather than iconographic, a record of a face and of a painter in training.","c":"With a nameless sitter and no known commission, the picture has to stand on likeness and handling alone, and the small panel suggests a painter working quickly and for himself. Studies of this kind often show a young artist's promise and habits before a mature style has formed. It is more valuable as a glimpse of Bohm's beginnings than as a finished statement, and it asks to be judged on those terms."},"cma:122345":{"d":"Dense woodland closes in around a rustic cottage, with a winding path leading the eye toward a sunlit clearing and fertile fields beyond. A windmill, an emblem of Dutch identity, stands in the background. The picture, about 84 by 111 centimeters, follows the formula that made Hobbema popular: thick forest growth pierced by paths and views of open land, with a shaded foreground set against bright distance.","i":"Hobbema's woods flattered the pride Dutch viewers took in their land, newly independent from Spain and always at risk from the sea, where dikes and pumps held the water back. Fields and livestock brought prosperity alongside sea trade, and a cottage beside a clearing suggests peaceful productivity. Hobbema had been a pupil of Jacob van Ruisdael and painted most of his surviving landscapes in the 1660s, before taking a post as an Amsterdam wine gauger.","c":"Hobbema lacks his teacher Ruisdael's brooding intensity, but he offers a clear, open pleasure in a particular kind of place. The contrast of shade and light draws the eye along the path, and the picture is built from motifs, cottage, path and clearing, that he rearranged across his career. That is both its charm and its limit: the pleasure is of variation rather than discovery. A handsome, reliable example of the Dutch woodland that English and American collectors later prized."},"cma:153368":{"d":"A rooster and a turkey fight in a garden with classical architecture, the scene enlivened by the swirl and excitement of the combat. Hondecoeter, who specialized in domestic and exotic birds and other animals, turns the poultry yard into a small drama. The canvas is large, about 137 by 166 centimeters, the kind of picture that must have hung high over a fireplace in a rich Amsterdam house.","i":"Hondecoeter was among the most fashionable painters of his day, and wealthy households bought his bird pictures as decoration and for the pleasure of seeing exotic species, a taste linked to the aviaries and menageries that rich Dutch owners kept. A fight between a rooster and a turkey is probably meant as lively spectacle rather than allegory, though pugnacious birds were sometimes read as emblems of rivalry. The classical architecture gives the poultry yard a touch of grandeur.","c":"Hondecoeter's gift was making feathers, wattles and claws convincing at speed, and the picture rewards close looking at those passages. The composition is theatrical rather than observed: the architecture is a stage set and the combat is arranged for effect. But the effect is real, and as decorative painting meant to please a rich room it is of a high order. It is less profound than the best Dutch still lifes, and more fun."},"nga:214130":{"d":"Three apples sit on a flat gray surface: two large ones in the center and a much smaller one to the left. Their skins are glossy, covered in uneven patches of bright red and light green, and two carry wide dark-green leaves on brown stems. One stray leaf lies on the table at lower right. The wall behind is a muted brown, lighter in the upper right corner. The oil-on-board picture is small, about 18 by 26 centimeters.","i":"The title says study and from Nature, wording that recalls the ideals of the American Pre-Raphaelites of the 1860s, who followed Ruskin in urging painters to record natural things with exact fidelity. Painters made such studies to train the eye and sometimes to sell. A plain subject treated at close range, without silver or crystal, gives ordinary apples dignity simply by looking at them carefully. Miller painted this one in 1863.","c":"Humble in subject and modest in size, this picture avoids every flourish. The glossy, patchy skins of the apples, with red and light green contending for each surface, are the real subject, and they are described with evident care. The stray leaf on the table is a nice touch of unfussy realism. The limits are those of a study: little composition, no atmosphere beyond a brown wall, no ambition beyond the apples. Honest rather than memorable."},"nga:229850":{"d":"A woven basket sits on a gray stone ledge against a black background, filled with peaches, black grapes, green grapes and broad green leaves. The peaches are placed prominently at the top of the pile, and clusters of grapes overflow on either side. More green leaves and red-tinted vine leaves are scattered around the basket on the stone. The canvas is about 58 by 97 centimeters, painted in 1636, and the arrangement is balanced and compact.","i":"In 1636 Louise Moillon was in her mid-twenties and already among the foremost French still-life painters of her generation, working in Paris. Fruit baskets of this kind combined display with a gentle reminder of the short life of ripe fruit, a theme still lifes of the period often hinted at. The black ground and stone ledge, common in still lifes of the time, isolate the fruit like objects in a showcase.","c":"Moillon's strength is calm. Against the black ground the colors of fruit and leaf stand out cleanly, and the composition is balanced, almost austere: peaches crown the pile, grapes spill to either side. Compared with the abundance of Dutch and Flemish still life, this is a quieter, more classical French sensibility. The restraint can feel static, but it gives the picture a monumentality beyond its size, and few contemporaries handled soft fruit with such patience."},"nga:12205":{"d":"A hunched man fills the foreground of this upright panel, striding across a moss-green rock with a huge bundle of bedding on his back, his muscular forearms raised to hold the green cloth that ties it. He wears a red tunic over a long white garment slit to show bare legs and feet. Behind him a path leads to an arched building where tiny figures gather around a standing person who gestures over a man lying on a cloth, lowered by ropes from the roof. Distant buildings, a tower and pale clouds fill the landscape.","i":"The foreground figure is the healed paralytic, who has obeyed the command to take up his bed and walk, while the miracle itself appears small in the distance. In the Gospels, friends lower a paralyzed man through a roof to reach Christ, who forgives and heals him. Putting the cured man large in front and the divine act small behind was a popular device in sixteenth-century Netherlandish painting, and it makes viewers think about the consequences of a miracle as well as the event.","c":"The inverted structure is the picture's best idea, and the bulky, muscular foreground figure carries it. He reads less like a patient than a laborer, which gives the miracle a bracing physical aftermath. The tiny crowd and barely visible ropes in the distance are delicate, observant details. Italianate muscularity grafted onto Netherlandish landscape was a common solution of the period, and the anonymous painter handles it with evident care, if not with a distinctive personal voice."},"met:437488":{"d":"A small portrait of a man wearing a chaperon, the draped, turban-like headdress fashionable in the mid-fifteenth century. The oil-on-wood panel is only about 28 by 20 centimeters, made to be seen close up. It belongs to the early Netherlandish tradition of the individual likeness, in which an unnamed sitter, perhaps a merchant, official or courtier, is studied most likely head and shoulders. Neither sitter nor painter is known, and the date is given only as 1440 to 1450.","i":"Portraits like this follow the example of Jan van Eyck, whose Man in a Red Turban of 1433 made the chaperon a famous emblem of the new oil portrait. The headdress was a fashionable item for men of means, and wearing it announced status. Early Netherlandish portraits were often small and personal, made for the sitter, his family or a prospective bride, and sometimes hinged to a devotional panel. With no name attached, the picture records how a man of rank wished to appear.","c":"At this size the picture lives or dies by the face. The chaperon, a hat folded like drapery, adds sculptural interest, and the type descends from the portraits of Van Eyck and Rogier van der Weyden, whose standards of penetration are hard to match. An anonymous hand inevitably invites that comparison. The picture is best enjoyed as a good representative of a format that changed European portraiture."},"met:437203":{"d":"A small portrait, about 35 by 27 centimeters, of a noblewoman who is probably Isabella of Portugal (1397 to 1472), third wife of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy. It is painted in oil on wood by an anonymous Netherlandish artist and dated to the early sixteenth century, long after her death, so it must have been made from earlier images. The title keeps the identification tentative, and the picture belongs to the long tradition of Burgundian court portraiture.","i":"Isabella married Philip the Good in 1430 and became one of the most influential women of the Burgundian court, taking an active political role as well as patronizing artists. Portraits by Rogier van der Weyden and his circle fixed her image, and later painters repeated such likenesses to keep the dynasty's memory alive after Burgundy passed to the Habsburgs. The word probably in the title matters: the identification presumably rests on resemblance to other portraits of her.","c":"The picture is best taken as a record of memory, not of presence. A portrait painted decades after its sitter's death cannot offer the immediacy of the court likenesses it descends from, and its tentative identification limits its documentary weight. What survives is the dignity of the type: a Burgundian duchess composed for a viewer who knew her only by reputation. Competent and respectful, it is a witness to dynastic memory rather than to Isabella herself."},"met:437199":{"d":"Two narrow devotional panels, painted in oil on wood in an upright format of about 24 by 10 centimeters, show Saint Donatian and Saint Victor presenting a donor. The saints are identified by attributes, among them a cross and a candle, and the donor appears under Victor's protection. The shape suggests wings that once flanked a central image, now lost or separate, in the manner of small portable altarpieces made in Bruges around 1490.","i":"Donatian, a fourth-century bishop of Reims, was the patron saint of Bruges, and the city's great collegiate church was dedicated to him; his usual attribute is a wheel set with candles, so the candle here likely refers to him. Presenting a donor through a saint was the standard way to show a patron's piety, and the saint was often the donor's name saint, here probably Victor. Such small wings suited private prayer in a Bruges household.","c":"The panels belong to the Bruges of Memling and Gerard David, where small size was a spur to precision. In such wings the donor's face is usually the most individual element, while the saints keep an ideal calm, and that contrast is the pleasure of the type. As fragments of a lost whole they cannot be judged by design alone, but as devotional objects of their moment they are intimate and quietly effective."},"met:437337":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child, with an angel, appear in a narrow upright panel of oil on wood about 31 by 17 centimeters. The format suggests a small devotional image or a wing of a diptych, a type made in Bruges around 1495 to 1500 for private prayer. The picture presents Mary as mother and as Queen of Heaven at once, with the angel attending her in the manner of countless Netherlandish Madonnas of the time.","i":"The Virgin and Child was the staple of private devotion in Bruges, a trading city whose merchants and visitors bought small panels for their houses. Angels often serve as attendants or musicians who proclaim Mary's rank as Queen of Heaven. The picture has been associated with Jan Provost, who worked in Bruges from the 1490s, though the museum catalogs it under an anonymous Bruges painter.","c":"A late fifteenth-century Bruges Madonna succeeds by intimacy rather than invention: the type was produced in numbers, and a narrow panel leaves room for little more than mother, child and attendant. The best examples reward close looking with fine drawing and glowing color. The unsettled authorship is a sign of how many painters worked in the same manner. A refined, quiet image, more a pleasure than a surprise."},"met:437216":{"d":"A tiny panel, only about 13 by 10.5 centimeters, that packs the Virgin of the Rosary and the fifteen mysteries of the rosary into a single image. Small scenes show moments from the lives of Christ and Mary, among them the Nativity, the Visitation, the Flagellation, Christ carrying the cross, the Crucifixion, the Resurrection, the Death of the Virgin and her Coronation, with soldiers in the Passion episodes. The oil-on-wood panel was made in Brussels about 1515 to 1520.","i":"The rosary prayers are grouped into fifteen mysteries, joyful, sorrowful and glorious, and images like this let a worshiper meditate on each in turn while reciting the prayers. The devotion spread through confraternities in the Rhineland and the Low Countries from the 1470s, so a picture of about 1515 to 1520 belongs to a new and very popular practice. It has been tentatively linked to Goswijn van der Weyden.","c":"The accomplishment is compression: fifteen episodes and a central image organized in a space little bigger than the palm of a hand, so a worshiper could take in the whole sequence at a glance. The cost is intensity. At this scale scenes become icons, readable but not moving, which is what a prayer aid needs. As a miniaturist's feat it is remarkable; as painting it is a diagram of devotion, with a charm that comes from its smallness."},"met:437210":{"d":"A small portrait of a young man, in oil on wood, about 35 by 26 centimeters, made in the Netherlands in the first quarter of the sixteenth century. The title identifies him by family, the Van Steynoert, rather than by name, which suggests that heraldry or an inscription names the lineage but not the individual. In so small a panel the sitter was presumably shown close up, in the usual compact format for likenesses of this kind.","i":"Portraits of young men from prosperous families were commissioned to mark milestones such as coming of age, an engagement or a marriage, and a coat of arms made the family's standing visible. The Van Steynoert name connects the sitter to a lineage that evidently mattered more to the commission than his own biography. It is a modest document of how families in the Low Countries presented their sons.","c":"Without a painter's name or a sitter's first name, the picture works as a type: a young face set down with the clarity and reserve that Netherlandish portraitists brought to all sitters of means. That reserve is both strength and limit, since it conveys status better than temperament. The emphasis on lineage suggests the family, more than the youth, was the subject, and the result is dignified if a little impersonal."},"met:437013":{"d":"Mary nurses the Christ Child in this devotional image of the Nursing Madonna, painted in tempera with gold on fine linen rather than on wood. The picture is small, about 40 by 30 centimeters, and was made in Antwerp around 1520. The nursing motif stresses the human reality of the Child and the maternal tenderness of Mary, and the use of gold gives an otherwise soft, matte image a precious touch.","i":"The Nursing Madonna, known as Maria lactans, was a popular devotional type from the later Middle Ages: it presents Mary as nourisher as well as intercessor, and it underlines the reality of the Incarnation, God made human enough to be fed. Painted cloths in tempera, called Tüchlein, were a cheaper, lighter alternative to oil on wood in the Low Countries and were used for private devotion.","c":"Linen and tempera give the picture a matte, soft surface unlike the glossy depth of oil on panel, and that suits a tender subject. The motif is intimate to the point of daring for a modern viewer, and the best versions find a believable maternal gesture. The medium is also fragile, and surviving examples are few, so the picture is valuable as much for its rarity as for any single bold stroke. A modest, appealing devotional work."},"met:437204":{"d":"A small portrait of Charles V (1500 to 1558), with a sword among the objects shown, painted in oil on wood and measuring about 30 by 22 centimeters. It dates from about 1520, when Charles, born in Ghent, was already King of Spain and had recently been elected Holy Roman Emperor, and was about twenty years old. The painter is an anonymous Netherlandish artist, and the small size suggests a likeness meant for a private owner or a court circle.","i":"A sword is the classic emblem of rulership: the emperor's duty to defend the Church and dispense justice. Charles inherited the Burgundian Netherlands, Spain and its empire, and the Habsburg lands, and portraits spread his face across them. Pictures like this probably served as loyal images for officials and courtiers as well as for private display. The date makes it an image of a young prince at the start of a reign that would define the century.","c":"Portraits of Charles V by Titian and Seisenegger define the image of the mature emperor. This earlier Netherlandish panel records a very young ruler before the weight of office changed his bearing. At under thirty centimeters high it is an intimate dynastic image rather than a state portrait, and its interest is as a document more than a showpiece: a prince at twenty in his home country, before the Habsburg image hardened into the sober figure of later portraits."},"met:437212":{"d":"The Magi come to honor the Christ Child in the presence of Mary and Saint Joseph, in an upright oil-on-wood panel about 69 by 55 centimeters. The painter belongs to the group known as the Antwerp Mannerists, anonymous masters of the 1510s and 1520s whose pictures typically feature extravagant costumes, elongated, twisting figures and fantastic architecture. The Adoration, with its parade of richly dressed visitors, was one of their favorite subjects.","i":"The Magi, wise men from the East who followed a star, brought gold, frankincense and myrrh, gifts read as acknowledging Christ's kingship, divinity and death. Medieval and Renaissance artists often turned them into three kings of different ages and origins, a way of showing the world's peoples coming to Christ. In Antwerp, a cosmopolitan trading city, the subject let painters show off exotic dress and the wealth of their patrons.","c":"The Antwerp Mannerists replaced the sober calm of the previous generation with decorative nervous energy, and the Adoration, with its exotic costumes, gave them ideal material. The manner is artificial and sometimes crowded, and devotional focus can be lost in display, but it has a theatrical charm, like a pageant staged on a small stage. It is a picture to enjoy for its ornament rather than for emotional depth."},"met:437214":{"d":"A church interior in which a sermon or reading is under way, with men, women, a child and a dog among the listeners, and perhaps the young Saint Anthony, the Egyptian hermit, among them. The scene is catalogued as a sermon on charity, possibly the moment when Anthony hears the Gospel command to give his goods to the poor. The upright oil-on-wood panel measures about 85 by 58 centimeters, and an Antwerp Mannerist painter fills the interior with figures and architecture.","i":"According to his Life, Anthony entered a church soon after his parents' death and heard the Gospel words, go, sell what you have and give to the poor. He took them as addressed to him, gave away his inheritance and went into the desert, becoming the model of the hermit. For viewers in prosperous early sixteenth-century Antwerp, the story posed a pointed question about wealth and charity. The title's word possibly signals that the identification is uncertain.","c":"The subject is unusual, and a crowded church gives the Antwerp Mannerists' taste for architecture and costume a purpose. The most appealing thing is the ordinary life that intrudes on the sermon, children and a dog among them, which brings a saint's conversion down to an Antwerp parish. The identification is uncertain, and the picture's message is clearer as a general sermon on charity than as one saint's turning point."},"met:437206":{"d":"A large portrait for its period, about 120 by 88 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in 1539. A bearded man is shown with a book and an hourglass, emblems of learning and of time. The title suggests he may be Jean de Langeac, Bishop of Limoges, who died in 1541, though the identification is tentative and the painter is anonymous.","i":"The hourglass is a memento mori, a reminder that time runs out, and combined with a book it suits a man of learning or a churchman. If the sitter is Langeac, he died two years after the date on the picture, but such emblems were common in portraits and need not signal foreknowledge. A bishop's portrait expressed authority and piety together, and the hourglass tempers the first with humility.","c":"The generous format gives the sitter presence, and the props are chosen with wit: learning and mortality, the two themes a thoughtful churchman might want his portrait to carry. The doubt over identity and the painter's name weakens its documentary value, but the picture holds up as a serious image of a man aware of time. Solid rather than showy, with the emblems doing much of the expressive work."},"met:437205":{"d":"A small portrait of a young woman wearing a coif, the close-fitting cap that covered the hair of respectable women, painted in oil on wood about 27 by 21 centimeters in the Netherlands around 1535. Her name and the painter's are both unrecorded. In so small a panel she was most likely shown close up, as was usual, and the scale is intimate, made to be looked at from arm's length.","i":"A coif was ordinary headwear for women of the middling and upper ranks, so it marks respectability more than rank. Portraits of young women were often painted around betrothal or marriage and could be paired with a likeness of the intended husband. The picture's meaning is therefore quiet: a record of a person at a moment of life when family and community wished to see her pictured.","c":"In small portraits the face is everything, and the painter's task is to make a few square inches convincing without flattery or fuss. The Netherlandish tradition made that restraint a virtue, and a young woman in a plain cap suits it. Without a known sitter or maker the picture cannot claim a place in any artist's career, but as an example of the type it carries the tradition's reserve and directness."},"met:437209":{"d":"A very small portrait of a man, painted in oil on wood and measuring only about 21 by 16.5 centimeters, by an anonymous Netherlandish artist of the first half of the sixteenth century. At this size the panel was made to be held or hung within arm's reach, and the picture is a private likeness rather than a public statement. The sitter's name has not come down to us.","i":"Small portrait panels were bought by merchants, officials and professionals in the Low Countries who wanted a likeness for the family, as a gift to relatives, or as one half of a pair with a spouse's picture. That the sitter's name is lost is a reminder of how many such pictures were made for family circles rather than public record. They express a growing confidence among such sitters in being looked at.","c":"A portrait this small cannot rely on scale or setting: it relies on the face alone, and the best Netherlandish examples draw great force from that concentration. Without a name or maker, this one asks the viewer to supply the context. It is best appreciated as an intimate keepsake with the reserved candor typical of the region and period, rather than as an individual artistic statement."},"met:437207":{"d":"A portrait of a woman, painted in oil on wood about 74 by 58 centimeters in the Netherlands around 1540 to 1550. This is a larger panel than many portraits of the period, presumably allowing a half-length figure and some of her costume. The sitter is unnamed and the painter anonymous, so the picture is best understood as a type: a respectable woman of means presented with dignity to the viewer.","i":"Larger portraits like this often belonged to a pair, with the husband's likeness on a companion panel, hung together in the family house. Such pictures declared status through size and costume as much as through resemblance. Between about 1540 and 1550 the Netherlands, then under Habsburg rule, was prosperous, and families in the great trading cities wanted likenesses; the demand favored a careful, reserved style.","c":"A portrait of this scale invites the viewer to measure the sitter's presence, and such pictures depend on whether the painter finds the person inside the costume. The anonymity of sitter and maker leaves little to hold on to, but the mid-century Netherlandish format, sober, direct and emotionally withheld, is itself a quiet achievement. An informative and respectable picture more than a startling one."},"met:437208":{"d":"A small portrait of a man identified as a surgeon, in oil on wood about 21 by 16 centimeters and dated 1569. The date is recorded, but neither the sitter's name nor the painter's is known. Surgeons of the time were skilled practitioners of the body, trained through apprenticeship and organized in guilds, and a portrait labeled with the profession suggests pride in a hard-won status. The scale points to a private likeness.","i":"In the sixteenth century surgeons ranked below university-trained physicians, but their prestige grew with the anatomical work of Vesalius, a Flemish-born anatomist, and his successors. A portrait that names his craft claims respect for it. Making a likeness of himself in the Netherlands in 1569 allowed a skilled man to join the families, officials and merchants who had long commissioned portraits.","c":"Its virtue is directness: a man defined by his trade, a clear date, a modest panel. Nothing is asked of the viewer beyond respect for a craft. The anonymity of both sitter and painter frustrates any attempt to rank the picture, but the choice to record a profession rather than a rank is itself telling about the period's civic pride in skilled work."},"met:437213":{"d":"Christ and the apostles gather for the Last Supper in a dining chamber; among the details the museum records are a tent and a dog. The panel is tall and narrow, about 120 by 43 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in the 1510s, and the plural Painters in the attribution suggests more than one hand. The shape suggests it was a wing of a larger altarpiece, made by Antwerp Mannerist painters.","i":"At the Last Supper Christ announces that one of the apostles will betray him and institutes the Eucharist, bread and wine becoming his body and blood. Netherlandish painters often added homely details, such as a dog near the table, which can stand for faithfulness or simply for the household in which the sacred event is set. The Antwerp Mannerists adapted the subject to their fashionable, elaborate style.","c":"The tall, narrow format forces the painters to stack the table and its figures in a compressed space, which gives a sense of crowding that may not suit the solemn subject. Yet the Antwerp Mannerists were never after quiet solemnity: their pleasure was in ornament, costume and nervous energy, and the Last Supper is a good vehicle for those. As a decorative devotional picture it works; as a deep reading of the Gospel it does not try to."},"met:437211":{"d":"The Holy Family pauses in the countryside on the flight into Egypt, in a horizontal oil-on-wood panel about 59 by 71 centimeters. The picture is catalogued as both religious art and landscape painting, which fits the subject: a few figures at rest in a wide setting give the painter room to describe terrain and distance. It has been attributed to the Master of the Female Half-Lengths, an Antwerp painter, while the museum credits unnamed Antwerp painters of the mid-sixteenth century.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew says only that an angel warned Joseph to take Mary and the Child to Egypt to escape Herod. The Rest on the Flight is a later elaboration, drawn from legend and devotion, and it became a favorite in Netherlandish painting because it joins a tender family scene to a landscape. Antwerp, where such pictures were made in quantity, was the leading center of landscape painting in the mid-sixteenth century.","c":"The subject lets landscape and devotion share a picture, and Antwerp painters knew how to profit from it: a few quiet figures, a generous setting, a mood of rest. Those are the virtues of the type, and the best versions make the stillness believable. The attribution is unsettled and the plural Painters in the label hints at a workshop product, so the picture is better enjoyed as a graceful example of a popular formula than as a personal vision."},"cma:140337":{"d":"A woman absorbed in reading, painted in oil on a small canvas about 46 by 38 centimeters and dated 1769. The subject is the quiet act of reading itself, which a picture can only suggest through posture, a lowered gaze and light. It belongs to the French tradition of intimate genre pictures, small canvases made for private rooms, in which an ordinary moment of domestic life is treated with seriousness.","i":"Reading women were a recurring subject in eighteenth-century France, when novels and a growing reading public made private reading a fashionable pleasure and, to moralists, a risk. A woman alone with a book signals inwardness and leisure. Lépicié, the son of the engraver François-Bernard Lépicié, worked in the wake of Chardin and Greuze, whose domestic scenes set the pattern for such pictures. The title names an activity, not a sitter, so the picture is probably a type rather than a portrait.","c":"Reading is difficult to paint, because the action is invisible: everything depends on posture, a lowered gaze and a pool of light, and the best French genre painters of the century made it a poem. Lépicié belongs to the capable second rank in this tradition, and a small canvas like this shows a gift for quiet domestic truth more than boldness. It is easier to respect than to be stirred by."},"cma:145135":{"d":"A young man is pressed close to the picture plane and engulfed in a swirl of drapery, in a deliberately informal likeness. His shirt is open, and a drift of wig powder dusts the shoulders of his velvet doublet. Warm hues and thick brushstrokes give the canvas, about 81 by 64 centimeters, a glow of richness. The sitter, Anne Louis Goislard de Montsabert, was twenty-six when he was painted.","i":"Despite the relaxed pose, Goislard was a figure of prominence. The museum suggests he may have commissioned the portrait to mark his appointment to the Parlement in 1732 or his acquiring the title Lord of Montsabert the next year. Largillière, in his late seventies when he painted it, had spent his career as portraitist to the Parisian elite and had studied Flemish painters, notably Van Dyck, whose warm color and informal grace shaped his later style.","c":"Largillière was nearly eighty and shows no fatigue: the thick brushwork, warm flesh and informality are those of a painter who still enjoys his craft. The casual look is, of course, composed, and the sitter's nonchalance is as carefully managed as a formal robe would have been. Even so it is livelier than most portraits of Paris magistrates, and the dusting of wig powder is an honest detail that keeps the picture from becoming merely flattering."},"cma:111479":{"d":"Two stories of Saint James the Greater share one canvas. In the foreground the magician Hermogenes pleads for forgiveness after the curse he cast on James turned back on him; in the middle ground at right, James cures a paralytic man. The setting is a grand architectural space, and the figures' poses are dignified and expressive. The canvas is relatively small, about 102 by 81 centimeters, but its clear composition gives it a monumental presence.","i":"The stories come from the medieval Golden Legend. Hermogenes, a magician, sent demons against James and found them turned on himself; he cast aside his books of magic and converted. In the second tale James heals a paralytic, escapes execution for the moment and wins over the man who had prosecuted him. James the Greater, one of the twelve apostles, became the patron of pilgrims to Santiago de Compostela. Both stories show the apostle triumphing over error, a theme congenial to a Catholic age.","c":"The museum's claim that the picture has monumental presence despite its size is fair. Coypel keeps two stories legible without clutter, sets them in grand architecture and gives the figures the gestures of the French academic tradition. The price of that order is warmth: the drama is staged and the emotion rhetorical. It is a polished late Baroque history painting by an artist who died in his forties, and it shows more discipline than daring."},"nga:406":{"d":"A close-up of a child's face and upper shoulders in profile, turned to the left. The boy has pale skin, a small nose, faint eyebrows and pink lips set in a neutral expression, with short light brown hair curling softly around the ear and nape. He wears a snug black cap and black clothing at the neck and shoulders, and the background is a flat dark green. The panel, about 25 by 18 centimeters, is painted in tempera and oil.","i":"The strict profile came to Renaissance portraiture from ancient coins and medals and was favored at the north Italian courts. A portrait of a child could record an heir, serve in marriage negotiations or remember a child who had died; the sitter's name has not survived, so the purpose is unknown. The mixed medium of tempera and oil suggests a moment of transition, when painters were adding oil to the older egg-based technique.","c":"Profile is a hard test: with no gaze and an impassive face, everything rests on contour and the soft modeling of skin. The unnamed painter uses the flat dark green ground to isolate the head like a medal, and the curls at the ear and nape give the image its tenderness. The neutral expression keeps it formal, and one may wish for a hint of the child's character. Still, the economy is admirable."},"nga:1141":{"d":"An upper-body portrait of a man in three-quarter view, turned to the left, with a prominent double chin, a slightly furrowed brow, thin lips and a serious expression. He has short dark hair under a large red hat and wears silver armor with red straps on shoulder and chest. The background is a plain dark green. The oil-on-panel picture, about 70 by 60 centimeters, shows the founder of the Sforza dynasty of Milan, as its title says.","i":"Francesco Sforza (1401 to 1466), a mercenary captain who rose to become Duke of Milan in 1450, gave his family a dynasty. A portrait dated about 1480 to 1500 postdates his death, so it was probably made from earlier images; such pictures kept the founder's memory alive for his heirs. The armor and red hat mix the dress of a prince with the soldier he had been.","c":"The painter does not flatter. The double chin, furrowed brow and thin mouth make a heavy, watchful man of a founder who might have been idealized, and the armor and red hat supply the necessary rank. That bluntness is the picture's interest, together with the plain green ground, which gives the head the force of a medal. It is less refined than courtly portraits by named masters, and perhaps more convincing for that."},"nga:501":{"d":"A small portrait of a man, painted in tempera on a poplar panel about 37 by 27 centimeters in northern Italy in the later fifteenth century. The sitter is unidentified, so the picture is best described by its type: a single-figure portrait of a man, the sort of likeness a private citizen or courtier ordered for his family. Poplar was a standard panel wood in Italy.","i":"Portraits of private men became more common in northern Italy during the later 1400s, as courts, merchants and officials wanted lasting images of themselves. Flemish portraits, with their direct, close-up observation, were admired and imitated, and the visit of Antonello da Messina to Venice in 1475 and 1476 helped spread the three-quarter view and the oil technique. This panel uses tempera, the older Italian medium, so it belongs to a tradition in which local habits and northern models mixed.","c":"An anonymous painter and an unnamed sitter leave the picture with only its manner to speak for it. At best such panels have the plain honesty of a good likeness, careful observation without flattery, and that is the standard by which this one should be judged. Without a name it will not enter any artist's story, but as an example of how ordinary men were portrayed in late fifteenth-century Italy it has quiet value."},"nga:41695":{"d":"A gentleman stands beside a table, seen from the knees up, one hand on the table and the other on his hip. He has a brown beard and mustache, light green eyes, a black cap and a bright red jacket open over a pleated white shirt. A boy in a yellow striped outfit leans against his leg, tying a bow on the white garment and looking over his shoulder at us. On the table, covered in dark green cloth, lie three books and a letter propped against them. The canvas is about 124 by 103 centimeters.","i":"The man is identified as Alessandro Alberti, accompanied by a page; the picture has been attributed to Gian Paolo Pace and is dated only as possibly 1544 or 1545. Books and a letter mark him as literate and engaged in correspondence, while a page signals a household with servants. Double portraits of a master with a young attendant showed rank and the ease of a settled household together, and the boy's playful turn adds a human note.","c":"The boy steals the picture. His glance over the shoulder and the small business of the bow give an otherwise formal portrait a note of life, while the red jacket against the green of the table and ground is bold and effective. The pairing of a bearded gentleman with books and a page is conventional, and the tentative date and attribution put it below the great Venetian and Lombard portraitists, but its informality is a pleasure."},"met:436745":{"d":"Two angels, painted in fresco on a fragment about 60 by 80 centimeters, date from about 1330. The museum suggests northern Italy, perhaps Verona. The size and the title suggest a piece detached from a larger wall painting, in which the angels presumably attended a central subject that no longer survives. Fresco, painted onto fresh plaster, was the great medium of Italian church walls.","i":"In Gothic wall paintings angels attend the Virgin, Christ or the Crucifixion, acting as messengers between heaven and earth and sometimes expressing grief or praise through gesture. A pair like this may once have framed a figure of Christ or Mary. Wall painting of this kind spread across northern Italy in the years after Giotto's frescoes in Padua, and fragments such as this survive from walls later destroyed or rebuilt.","c":"A fragment can be judged only for what it keeps. The interest here is historical: a glimpse of Gothic painting in northern Italy in the age of Giotto's influence, with the loss of the whole as the dominant fact. The question mark in the attribution is honest. What survives is a reminder of how much wall painting has vanished, and the fragment's charm is the charm of survival."},"met:436747":{"d":"A very small portrait of a man, painted in oil on wood in 1597 and measuring about 18 by 16 centimeters, by an unnamed painter from northern Italy. The date is the one firm fact the record offers: the picture comes from the very end of the sixteenth century, a moment when northern Italian portraiture still ran along lines set by the late Renaissance, just before the Baroque changed taste.","i":"Small portraits were made as personal keepsakes, as gifts to family or friends, or for sitters who were away from home. The firm date makes the picture a precise marker of its moment. Though the sitter is unknown, the very existence of the portrait is a sign of the confidence of northern Italian burghers, professionals and nobles by the end of the century in having their likeness made.","c":"A portrait small enough to hold in one hand asks to be judged on face and date alone. Without a sitter's name or a painter's, it cannot claim a place in the story of Italian art, but it is a clear witness to the continuing market for private likenesses at the end of the Renaissance. Its modest scale argues for intimacy, and its date, 1597, for a painter working on the brink of a new style."},"nga:41654":{"d":"About two dozen people ride horses or stand around Mary, who holds the nude Christ Child in a landscape. The Child sits on her lap in front of a tall open-walled stable, a trough behind her, and looks at a white-bearded king kneeling with a bronze vessel. Two more crowned figures stand at the lower corners, one holding a gold cup and one a crystal orb, with attendants, a camel, monkeys on chains and thin dogs in jeweled collars. Emerald, ruby, yellow and blue robes enliven the canvas, about 183 by 165 centimeters.","i":"The Magi, traditionally three kings, represented the three ages of man and the known continents, so the painter gives them varied skin tones and costumes, with camels and monkeys as signs of far-off lands. Monkeys on chains may also hint at the worldly or the captive, though such readings are uncertain. The picture was transferred from its original wooden panel to canvas, a conservation procedure once common for panels in poor condition.","c":"Abundance is the picture's pleasure: riders, a camel, monkeys, dogs and a distant town give the eye endless things to find, and the saturated reds, greens and blues are bold. Mary and the Child, calm and low in the composition, keep their authority against the bustle. The exaggerated lips and noses given to the darker-skinned attendants repeat a period stereotype and are the picture's most dated feature. A vivid, worldly Adoration, more storytelling than devotion."},"met:437202":{"d":"Christ carries the cross through a crowd in a town, with horsemen among the throng, in an upright panel of oil on wood about 108 by 82 centimeters. The scene belongs to the Passion cycle, and the crowd, the horses and the streets give it the busy, accumulative look of fifteenth-century northern Passion pictures. It was probably made around 1470. Where it was painted is uncertain: Bruges and Utrecht have both been suggested.","i":"The Gospels barely describe the walk to Calvary beyond Simon of Cyrene being forced to help carry the cross. Medieval devotion filled the gap with a procession of soldiers, mockers and mourners, and painters made the crowd the setting for Christ's isolation. Viewers were encouraged to join the procession in imagination, and such pictures served meditation on the Passion. Setting the scene in a northern town may have made Jerusalem a familiar place.","c":"The strength of such compositions is the lone figure within the throng, and a crowded panel of this size gives a painter plenty of room for invention in faces, costume and horses. The risk is sameness among the faces, and the uncertain origin suggests an anonymous painter rather than a major named master. A serious, crowded devotional picture rather than a landmark."},"met:436991":{"d":"A pair of small portraits of a husband and wife: Jan (1438/41 to 1516), first Count of Egmond, and Magdalena, Countess of Egmond (1464 to 1538). The companion panels measure about 42 by 26 centimeters and are painted in oil on wood; the Count's portrait has since been transferred to canvas. The date of about 1516 to 1520 falls around or after the Count's death, so they are likely memorial likenesses based on earlier portraits.","i":"The Egmonds were among the great noble families of Holland, and Jan was the first of them to hold the title of count. A pair of portraits of husband and wife proclaimed the lineage as a unit; the dates suggest they were made as memorials after Jan's death in 1516, perhaps for a family house or chapel. Such portraits asserted dynastic continuity. The painter is unknown.","c":"Paired dynastic portraits are less about psychology than about lineage, and this pair accepts that bargain: the couple presented as a unit, their titles and dates the real subject. The anonymous painter's gifts are clear, dignified presentation, and the interest is as a record of a noble house at a moment of transition, when the first count's death passed the title to the next generation. Documentary value is high; artistic ambition is modest."},"met:436281":{"d":"A portrait miniature painted on ivory, a likeness of a man identified only by the initials CJD. Ivory was the preferred support for miniatures in the eighteenth century because its pale surface lets flesh tones glow through thin paint. Miniatures of this type are usually only a few centimeters high, made to be held in the hand or worn. The painter is known only as Northern European, and the date is about 1770.","i":"In the 1700s portrait miniatures were exchanged by lovers, families and friends, kept in cases or worn as lockets and brooches; the intimacy of the object mattered more than the grandeur of the image. Initials, often worked in hair or gold on the back or in the mount, tied a likeness to a person without needing a name. Here three letters are all that remain of the sitter's identity, a reminder of how easily such tokens lose their stories.","c":"Miniatures are judged by finesse: stippled flesh, the translucency of ivory showing through, a tiny hand-held world rendered without a blur. An unnamed Northern European painter has no reputation to lend, so the object must persuade on touch alone. Its appeal lies in intimacy rather than ambition: a token of private affection from the 1770s whose maker and subject have both slipped into anonymity."},"met:436411":{"d":"A devotional work in several parts, in oil on wood, recorded at about 114 by 79 centimeters and painted in northern France around 1450. It brings together the Crucifixion of Saint Peter with a donor, the legend of Saint Anthony Abbot with a donor, and the Annunciation, with angels, women, men and even camels among the figures. The three subjects suggest a triptych or similar ensemble in which the Annunciation framed or accompanied the donors' saints.","i":"Peter, who according to tradition asked to be crucified upside down because he felt unworthy to die as Christ did, and Anthony Abbot, the Egyptian hermit, are each shown with a donor, presumably the donors' name saints, so the donors were likely called Peter and Anthony. The Annunciation, when Gabriel tells Mary she will bear Christ, opens the story of salvation, and an ensemble of this kind would tie the donors' prayers to it.","c":"A busy ensemble: a martyrdom, a legend and the Annunciation on one work, with donors included as participants. The mid-fifteenth-century northern French style, often marked by Flemish influence, gives the scenes a clear, narrative quality, and the unusual mix of subjects is intriguing. The attempt to carry three stories makes it more a compendium than a focused devotional image, but it offers a vivid view of what patrons asked for in prayer."},"met:436412":{"d":"Narrow oil-on-wood panels, painted on both sides, show the Crucifixion and the Resurrection on their fronts and, on the reverses, Saint Francis of Assisi and an abbot saint, possibly Saint Benedict. Soldiers appear in the Crucifixion and Resurrection scenes. At about 50 by 22 centimeters, the tall, slim shape and the double-sided painting suggest wings of a small portable altarpiece that could be opened and closed, made in northern France about 1460.","i":"On a folding altarpiece the inside and the outside tell different stories. The Crucifixion and the Resurrection, Christ's death and his victory over it, sit at the heart of the faith, while the saints on the reverse appeared when the wings were closed. Francis, founder of the Franciscans, and an abbot saint, possibly Benedict, father of Western monasticism, may point to the owner's religious affiliation or name saints, but the choice is unrecorded.","c":"Portable altarpieces of this kind were working objects, made to be handled, opened and prayed before, and these slim wings do their job economically: a clear event on each face, a saint on each back. The anonymous painter keeps to narrative essentials, and the pleasure lies in directness rather than finesse. Modest, sincere and well made for its purpose, they are better understood as tools of devotion than as showpieces."},"met:436413":{"d":"Christ carries the cross, with a soldier at his side, in a small oil-on-wood panel about 38 by 27 centimeters, painted in northern France around 1480. The scene belongs to the Passion, the last hours of Christ's life, and the keywords record only Christ and a soldier, which suggests a picture made for close, private devotion rather than a crowded public altarpiece.","i":"In the later fifteenth century devotion to Christ's suffering grew intense, and small panels like this were meant to be looked at while praying. The soldier is the tormentor, a figure who typically adds menace and humanity to the scene, and the viewer is invited to feel compassion. Christ bearing the cross also invited believers to carry their own burdens in imitation of him.","c":"The strength of a small, tight composition is concentration: with few figures, nothing distracts from Christ's burden. The picture should be taken as a competent, anonymous devotional panel of its time and region, one of many, most of them made as aids to prayer rather than as art. As such it succeeds when it makes the viewer slow down, and it does not ask to be compared with the great Passion pictures."},"met:436414":{"d":"Two martyrdoms share a narrow format: the Martyrdom of Saint Adrian, and the Martyrdom of two saints, possibly Ache and Acheul. Men and horses appear among the figures. The scenes are painted in oil on canvas that has been transferred from its original wooden panel, and the narrow shape, about 58 by 28 centimeters, suggests panels from a larger ensemble made in northern France around 1480.","i":"Adrian, a Roman officer put to death at Nicomedia for his Christian faith, became the patron of soldiers and, like Sebastian, was invoked against plague. Ache and Acheul, if correctly identified, are associated with Amiens in Picardy, which would point to a local commission. Narrow scenes like these may have come from an altarpiece or a series of saints' lives, meant to show the cost of faith.","c":"Martyrdoms test a painter's tact: the violence must be legible without becoming spectacle, and horses and crowds supply the pace. The interest here is the unusual pairing of an international soldier saint with probable local martyrs, which hints at a particular patron and place. The transfer from wood to canvas is a reminder that these are survivors, and they are best judged as fragments of a lost whole."},"met:436415":{"d":"A small portrait of a man dated 1605, painted in oil on wood and measuring about 23 by 17 centimeters. A coat of arms accompanies the likeness and identifies the sitter's family, Moncheaux, rather than giving his own name. It was made in northern France by an anonymous painter in the early years of the seventeenth century. Armorial portraits of this kind set a face and a lineage side by side, so that the viewer reads the man and his family in a single glance.","i":"Heraldry turns a portrait into a statement of lineage: the arms say who the sitter's family was, and the date fixes the moment. Small armorial portraits of this kind were commissioned by nobles and well-to-do townspeople, and they could be kept as family records or exchanged as tokens of kinship. The portrait itself offers a face, a date and a shield.","c":"As art it is modest, a provincial likeness whose interest is the combination of an individual face with a family badge, a person defined by belonging. The firm date and the arms make it more useful to historians than to connoisseurs, and it should be valued on those terms. A sober, documentary picture that does not pretend to more."},"cma:145463":{"d":"A nude young woman reclines on a bed, propped on a white pillow, and stretches one arm toward a shower of gold coins falling from the dark upper edge of the canvas. Her head is tilted back and her eyes follow the coins. At the left a winged Cupid draws back a dark green curtain, while a red cushion and the gilded frame of the bed show at the right. The bed is dressed with crisp white sheets and a rumpled gold satin cover, and a sheer, gauzy scarf lies across her hips. The room is dark, and the pale figure and bright fabrics are pulled to the front of the picture. The oil on canvas is smooth and clear.","i":"In the myth, Danaë's father feared a prophecy that his grandson would kill him, so he imprisoned his daughter to keep suitors away. Jupiter, king of the gods, fell in love with her and came to her as streaming gold. Cleveland says the picture was probably painted in Genoa between 1621 and 1624, and now describes it as an excellent version, by Gentileschi's own hand, of the Danaë he painted for the Genoese patron Giovanni Antonio Sauli; the primary version is in the Getty Museum. Because the god arrives as money, the story was often read as a comment on the power of gold to buy love. Whether that mattered to the patron is not recorded.","c":"Gentileschi took from Caravaggio the habit of painting directly from posed models, and it gives the body a startling, tangible weight, with skin, silk and sheets all convincingly different. His own gift was a lyrical grace of gesture, seen in the raised hand and the upturned face. The picture is cooler than Venetian treatments of the subject, and Cupid pulling the curtain is a stagey touch. Even so, the clean light and the rendering of white linen make it a persuasive and unusually poised version of a subject that often invites showmanship."},"cma:173669":{"d":"A still life of the plainest kind: a pitcher and a glass on a table, painted in oil on a vertical wood panel about 57 by 36 centimeters and dated 1878. The title names only those two vessels, so the subject is the play of light on a pitcher and the transparency of glass, a classic exercise for a painter learning to see.","i":"Simple vessel studies have a long history as training for the eye: glazed surfaces, reflections and transparent glass are hard to paint and easy to judge. Bacher painted this in his early twenties, an early work by an artist who would become the first painter from Cleveland to achieve international renown. Pictures like this may have stayed in the studio or gone to friends rather than being public statements.","c":"It is interesting mostly for what it precedes. Bacher later adopted an Impressionist style after studying in Paris, and that later freedom rests on exercises like this one, in which the problem is plain and the solution has nowhere to hide. As a picture it asks little, and what it asks, a convincing jug and a believable glass, is a fair test. A student's honesty is its main virtue."},"cma:142665":{"d":"A tall, narrow portrait of the painter's wife, Mary Holland Bacher, painted in 1891 on a canvas about 91 by 57 centimeters. The subject is unusual for its time: she is portrayed in connection with tennis, a sport that had recently become acceptable for women. The picture reflects the Impressionist style Bacher adopted after studying in Paris in the late 1880s.","i":"Lawn tennis had reached the United States in the 1870s and had become one of the few sports in which women could take part without being thought unfeminine or uncouth, which is what makes this a modern subject. A husband's portrait of his wife is a personal picture rather than a commission, and Bacher, the first Cleveland artist to win international renown, uses it to link family and fashion. She may stand for the active woman of the 1890s.","c":"American Impressionism was a tempered affair, brighter than academic painting but loyal to drawing, and this portrait is an example. Its real interest is the subject: a woman presented as an athlete, in the 1890s, by her husband, with evident pride. That is a modest declaration of modernity, and it makes the picture more engaging as social history than as a breakthrough in style."},"cma:142664":{"d":"A nude model sits on a sunlit lawn in a composition dazzled by summer light. Bacher painted it in 1893 during a visit to East Hampton, Long Island, and gave it the title of a month rather than the name of a story. The tall, narrow canvas, about 90 by 58 centimeters, makes the figure the center of a bright, open setting; the model's name was not recorded.","i":"East Hampton was a popular summer retreat for artists in the late nineteenth century, and painting the figure outdoors in strong light was a goal of Impressionist-minded painters. A nude in a natural setting connects the modern open-air picture to the older tradition of the classical nude, while the title makes the season the subject. Bacher, born in Cleveland and working in New York, had studied in Paris in the late 1880s.","c":"The best idea is to make a season the subject: heat and glare turn the figure into a pattern of light, and a nude on a lawn is a bolder choice than most American Impressionism of the early 1890s made. Whether Bacher quite matches the French painters he followed is open to question, but the picture's willingness to be dazzled is attractive, and it has an energy that polite figure painting of the time often lacked."},"cma:103625":{"d":"A cormorant plunges into the water, in a small oil-on-board study about 23 by 33 centimeters, dated 1900. The seabird, famous for diving after fish, is the whole subject, caught in a moment that lasts only seconds. At this size the picture has the scale of a sketch from nature rather than a finished exhibition piece, the kind of direct, quick record that suits a fleeting action.","i":"Birds and water were recurring subjects for painters of the Impressionist generation, who valued fleeting action and changing light. A diving bird demands fast observation, since the dive lasts seconds and the picture is mostly quick looking and memory. Bacher, born in Cleveland, had sketched shipping around the city's port as a child, though nothing records where this bird was seen.","c":"A small board is the right instrument for a dive that lasts seconds, and the subject takes the picture away from the portrait and figure habits one expects of Bacher. As a late work of modest size it is a pleasant footnote rather than a statement: an artist who ranged from portraits to nudes to still life looking at the water. It earns its place by being quick and unpretentious."},"cma:109507":{"d":"Agostino Barbarigo, second in command of the Venetian fleet at the Battle of Lepanto, is shown in armor in a pose borrowed from portraits of Roman emperors. In his hand he holds the Turkish arrow that killed him in the battle on October 7, 1571. The canvas, about 102 by 104 centimeters, is almost square and was painted after his death.","i":"Lepanto, where the Holy League fleet defeated the Ottoman navy in 1571, was the great Christian naval triumph of the century. In the 1500s Venetian military portraits often showed the sitter in armor and in the pose of a Roman emperor to link him to ancient valor. The arrow he holds turns the weapon that killed him into a badge of honor and tells the viewer that the portrait is posthumous.","c":"Veronese, famed for pageant and silk, turns here to a portrait of commemorative gravity. The arrow is the picture's best idea: a single telling prop that narrates a death and sanctifies it. The emperor-like pose is a convention more than an observation, and posthumous portraits risk stiffness, but the picture succeeds as an icon of Venetian pride after Lepanto, and the square format gives the armored figure a monumental solidity."},"cma:127572":{"d":"The angel Gabriel, holding white lilies, announces to the Virgin Mary that she will conceive and bear a son. Veronese, better known for large, pageant-like scenes, compresses the figures close together, creating a startling intimacy between messenger and recipient. Mary has been reading the prophecy of Isaiah when the angel appears. The canvas, about 150 by 133 centimeters, is almost square, a format that suits a close, two-figure encounter.","i":"According to a tradition established by Saint Bernard, Mary was reading Isaiah's prophecy that a young woman would bear a son when Gabriel appeared, so the picture puts promise and fulfillment in one moment. The white lilies stand for her purity. Annunciations, the beginning of the Incarnation, were among the most frequently painted subjects in Italy, and each painter's task was to find a fresh way to stage the meeting.","c":"The compression is the surprise. A painter famous for banquets and airy architecture brings two figures so close that the exchange feels private and a little startling, and that departure from his usual theatrical manner is the picture's best quality. It is not as dazzling as his large canvases, and the gestures remain courtly, but the intimacy is real, and the white lilies are a clean accent in a quiet composition."},"cma:97180":{"d":"Pope Sixtus IV della Rovere raises his hand in blessing and meets the viewer's gaze. White highlights make his bejeweled garments glitter, and he wears the triregnum, the ceremonial triple crown that is a symbol of the papacy. Berruguete painted him from life in Rome. The picture, about 70 by 51 centimeters, was painted on wood and later transferred to canvas.","i":"Pedro Berruguete, a Castilian painter, spent about a decade in Italy in the 1470s and 1480s. Sixtus IV, pope from 1471 to 1484, built the Sistine Chapel that bears his name. The blessing gesture and the gaze combine the pope's spiritual office with personal presence, and the lavish dress and triple crown stress the authority of the papacy. In the nineteenth century the picture belonged to the American critic and collector James Jackson Jarves.","c":"Its best quality is directness: a pope who stares out of the picture and blesses the viewer in the same moment. Berruguete turns the sumptuous clothes into a glitter of white highlights, an effective shorthand for jewels, and the whole is dignified, if a little static, as papal portraits tend to be. Painted from life, it remains a valuable document of how Sixtus wished to be seen."},"cma:121946":{"d":"A large portrait of a woman with heavily lidded eyes, a fleshy chin and slender fingers, features that fit seventeenth-century ideals of beauty. The canvas, about 127 by 101 centimeters, had been extensively overpainted before the museum acquired it in 1942: her eyebrows were lowered, her eyes and lips reduced and curls added to make her forehead look smaller. Mrs. Leneve now appears as Lely originally intended.","i":"Peter Lely, born in Westphalia to Dutch parents, became the most fashionable portraitist in England and later Charles II's Principal Painter, and his sitters share a languid, heavy-lidded look that was the height of fashion. Long slender fingers signaled aristocratic elegance. The overpainting this picture suffered shows how a later age, with a different idea of beauty, edited a face it found strange; the restoration returned Mrs. Leneve to the beauty her contemporaries admired.","c":"Lely's formula, heavy lids, a soft chin, a languid hand, is partly a house style, and many of his sitters look alike. Here, though, the formula has been cleaned back to its intended effect, and the interest is double: a good example of that seductive elegance and a lesson in how taste edits faces. The restoration is a small vindication of the painter's eye, and the sitter gains character."},"cma:97191":{"d":"A woman is shown as Saint Catherine of Alexandria, identified by a spiked wheel, the instrument of her attempted martyrdom. The painting, about 95 by 71 centimeters, combines a portrait with a saint's image: a probable Florentine patrician sitter takes on the saint's identity, and the object tied to Catherine's story tells the viewer whom she is meant to resemble.","i":"Catherine, a Christian of Alexandria, refused to sacrifice to idols for the emperor Maxentius; the spiked wheels meant to kill her broke when she prayed, and she was then beheaded. In sixteenth-century Florence patrician women were often portrayed as exemplary figures like her, a way to credit the sitter with the saint's virtues. The title does not name which woman is meant.","c":"The device flatters twice, offering a likeness and a model of virtue at once, and the best examples keep both alive. Foschi was a Florentine painter working in the long shadow of Pontormo and Bronzino, and the picture's reserve and polish are those of Florentine portraiture rather than a personal voice. Such pictures succeed when the sitter's own face survives the saint's costume."},"cma:121860":{"d":"A group record for the whole ensemble: decorative wall panels, double-leaf doors and overdoor paintings by Pierre Rousseau, made in the 1790s and now in the museum. Taken together they are the parts of a decorated room, with tall canvases for the walls, doors for the entrances and wide pictures above the doors. The panels and overdoors are painted in oil on canvas; the doors are oil on wood.","i":"Rooms of the later eighteenth century were designed as wholes, in which walls, doors and overdoors repeated a single decorative language. The French Revolution disrupted many commissions, but private decoration seems to have revived in the Directory years, and this ensemble belongs to that taste for the classical. Its survival as a group is what makes it valuable: few interiors of the period survive intact.","c":"Judged as a group, the ensemble is more persuasive than any of its parts: the repeated vertical of doors and panels, the horizontal band of overdoors and a shared classical vocabulary make a coherent room even in fragments. Individually the pieces are modest, and some have been altered, as the scraped doors show. As evidence of how a 1790s interior was assembled, the group is of real interest."},"cma:121861":{"d":"A tall decorative panel, about 264 by 85 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. It is one of two canvases with these dimensions, the widest panels in the group, and was presumably made to fill a section of wall in a room whose doors and overdoor paintings are also by Rousseau. As a decorative panel it most likely carries ornament in the classical taste of those doors.","i":"In the 1790s French interiors were being reordered by the taste for Antiquity, spread by the excavations at Pompeii and Herculaneum and by engraved books of ancient ornament. Wall panels on canvas, painted in the studio and then fixed to the wall, let a decorator control the effect in advance. Rousseau's panels belong to that fashion, and their canvas support is typical of the technique.","c":"A panel this size succeeds or fails as part of a room, and no single canvas can be judged in isolation. Its tall proportion, roughly three to one, was made to be seen from a distance, as one note in a wall's rhythm. In a museum it loses that context, so it is best appreciated as a fragment of a lost ensemble, valued for its scale and period style."},"cma:121862":{"d":"A very narrow decorative panel, about 264 by 34 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. At nearly eight times as high as it is wide, it is closer to a painted band or pilaster than to a picture, the sort of element used to frame larger fields or fill a tight vertical space. Another panel with the same dimensions is catalogued with it.","i":"Very narrow panels were the connective tissue of a decorated room, used beside doors, in corners and between windows where larger compositions did not fit. Their ornament had to be compact and strongly vertical, which suited motifs such as garlands, candelabra and trophies that Neoclassical decorators drew from ancient models. That any survive is fortunate, since small fillers are easily lost when rooms are altered.","c":"The narrowness limits what a painter can do, and the best that can be said for such a strip is that it keeps its discipline: a single vertical idea, repeated with care. It is not a picture to linger before, but it is exactly what makes a decorated room feel finished. Taken from its context, it is a fragment of that finish."},"cma:121863":{"d":"A set of double-leaf doors, about 274 centimeters tall, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Paired doors of this size were made for a grand room and could open on both leaves for a ceremonial entry. Others in the museum's group are described as painted with classical designs and imitation dark wood grain, and this set may have shared that scheme, though its own surface may have been altered.","i":"Double-leaf doors were the standard way into the principal rooms of a well-to-do French house in the later eighteenth century, and at nearly three meters these suggest a lofty space. The 1790s were the years of the Revolution and the Directory, when decorators worked for a changing clientele of officials, financiers and surviving aristocrats. Rousseau's doors belong to the taste of that moment for restrained classical ornament.","c":"A pair of doors is judged by proportion and surface, and at nearly three meters tall these have the stately proportions of a salon entrance. They were made to be passed through and handled, not studied, and in a gallery they lose that function. Their value lies in being part of a group that records a decorative scheme, and in the craft of the decorator who finished them."},"cma:121864":{"d":"A set of double-leaf doors, about 274 by 63 centimeters, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. The doors are tall and slender, with the proportions of a principal room. They belong with the group of doors, wall panels and overdoor paintings that the museum catalogs under Rousseau's name, and probably shared the group's classical designs and painted graining.","i":"A decorative painter of the 1790s finished woodwork that carpenters supplied: graining, gilding and classical ornament were skills of that trade, and a decorator might be responsible for the doors as well as the walls. The museum names Rousseau for the whole group, which is notable, because most such craftsmen go unrecorded and their work is usually anonymous.","c":"The doors have the tall, narrow dignity of a good salon entrance, and that proportion is most of their effect. Like their siblings they may have been altered by time and taste, and a viewer should treat the surface as a layered record rather than a pristine design. They offer craft and architecture rather than personal expression, which is what they were made to do."},"cma:121865":{"d":"A set of double-leaf doors about 274 centimeters tall, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Part of the museum's Rousseau group, the doors would have stood in a doorway between principal rooms, opening on both leaves. Their surface may have passed through the same changes as their siblings: dark graining, later scraping and modern conservation.","i":"In the 1790s a door was both a barrier and a display. Guests passed through paired leaves into the principal rooms, and those leaves carried the same classical vocabulary as the walls. Painted wood grain imitated expensive timber at a fraction of the cost, a typical trick of the decorators' trade, and one that later taste found unpersuasive enough to remove from sibling doors in this group.","c":"Seen as a door, it is a handsome, balanced thing; seen as a painting, it is a plain one. That tension is the point of such objects, which asked a painter to work on a surface that moves and is touched. The attractive thing is the sense of use: the door was meant to open, and behind glass or on a gallery wall it loses that life."},"cma:121866":{"d":"A set of tall double-leaf doors, about 274 centimeters high, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Rousseau gave them an imitation dark wood grain, a faux finish that made ordinary wood look like costly timber, around classical designs. Another craftsman later scraped off the dark color surrounding the designs, so the doors no longer look as Rousseau left them. They have recently been conserved.","i":"Architectural fittings change with taste. Doors painted to resemble dark wood, with classical designs picked out around them, were later altered as styles shifted, and the museum records how another hand scraped the dark color away. Painted doors are a reminder that a period interior was a made environment, supplied by decorators like Rousseau. In the 1790s classical ornament was the taste of the day.","c":"These are decorative works, so quality lies in design and craft: tall proportions, classical motifs and a faux finish that was a skill in its own right. Their story, an original finish scraped back by a later hand and then conserved, is as instructive as the doors themselves, since it shows how thin the line is between a period interior and its alterations. Handsome, but best valued as evidence."},"cma:121867":{"d":"One of several matching sets of tall double-leaf doors by Pierre Rousseau in the museum, about 274 centimeters high and painted in oil on wood in the 1790s. The classical designs were originally surrounded by imitation dark wood grain; a later craftsman scraped the dark color away, and the set has recently been conserved. As architecture, they were meant to be seen in movement, opened and closed, rather than hung like pictures.","i":"Faux wood finishes let a decorator give common wood the look of a costly dark timber, and the skill was prized. Rousseau's imitation grain framed classical designs, the fashionable vocabulary of the 1790s, and the museum notes how later taste changed such details. A scraped or altered surface is not damage in the ordinary sense but a record of how successive owners lived with the rooms around them.","c":"Doors are the least pictorial of art objects, and judging them as paintings misses the point. The proportions and classical motifs give them a cool elegance, and the survival of several matching sets allows comparison. The conservation history is the more interesting part, since a surface scraped by a later craftsman forces a museum to decide what to show. They are a good lesson in how decoration lives and changes."},"cma:121868":{"d":"A set of tall double-leaf doors, about 274 centimeters high, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau around the 1790s. Imitation dark wood grain once surrounded the classical designs, and another craftsman later scraped that dark color away. The set has recently been conserved, so its present surface reflects Rousseau's original work, the later alteration and modern care, and the doors can be studied much as they have come down to us.","i":"Precise dating of decorative work is difficult, which may be why this set is given only as about the 1790s: painted woodwork was seldom signed or documented, and the survival of Rousseau's name on this group is itself notable. Imitation wood grain was a trade skill, the scraping shows later owners wanted a different look, and recent conservation treats such altered surfaces as historical evidence.","c":"Decorative doors are judged by proportion and finish, and this set's present finish is a compromise between the painter's intentions and a later hand. The classical designs keep their elegance, but the missing dark ground weakens the contrast Rousseau planned. Its value now is as a conserved witness, and viewers should look for the traces of change as much as for the designs."},"cma:121869":{"d":"A set of tall double-leaf doors, about 274 centimeters high, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s, with classical designs that were once surrounded by imitation dark wood grain until a later craftsman scraped it away. This set was conserved in 1979 and retains more traces of the original graining than the other doors by Rousseau in the museum's collection.","i":"The doors were among the most visible parts of a decorative scheme, since everyone entering a room passed through them, and a decorator like Rousseau finished them with the same care as the larger wall panels. The museum notes that this set keeps more of its original graining than the others, which makes it a primary witness for how such doors first looked, before taste and a scraper changed them.","c":"The interest is archaeological. The surface is a record of decisions by several hands: the original painter, the later scraper and the conservators of 1979. If the dark graining gave the doors a rich, warm presence, its partial loss makes them cooler than intended, but what survives is honest evidence. They reward patience more than first impressions."},"cma:121870":{"d":"A set of double-leaf doors, about 274 centimeters tall, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Rousseau painted them with imitation dark wood grain around classical designs, and a later craftsman scraped off the dark color surrounding those designs. Conserved in 1979, this set keeps more traces of the original graining than the other Rousseau doors in the museum, so it gives the best idea of how they first looked.","i":"Imitation graining was a decorator's skill: a painter laid a dark, figured surface over ordinary wood so that the doors would look like costly timber, with classical designs set against it. Taste later moved on, and the museum records how another craftsman scraped the dark color back. The surviving traces matter because they let a viewer picture the original contrast between ground and design, which the later alteration flattened.","c":"As evidence this is the most useful set in the group: the traces of original graining let the eye reconstruct what the scraping removed. As decoration it depends on proportion, a lofty door and a restrained classical vocabulary, rather than on showy invention. It is valuable for what remains of the original surface more than for any boldness of design, and it is best looked at closely."},"cma:121871":{"d":"A set of double-leaf doors about 274 centimeters tall, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Rousseau painted them with imitation dark wood grain around classical designs, and another craftsman later scraped off the dark color surrounding the designs. The museum notes that sets conserved in 1979 keep more traces of the original graining than the other doors by Rousseau, which implies that this one keeps less.","i":"Scraping a dark finish back to lighter wood is a typical later change, and it shows how interiors are repeatedly redecorated: a style that was fashionable in the 1790s was altered when taste shifted. The classical designs that survive belong to the Neoclassical vocabulary then in favor, drawn from the ancient world. Doors were among the most visible and most handled parts of a room, and so among the likeliest to be altered.","c":"These doors teach how much of what we call a period interior is later work. The Neoclassical designs are refined, but the lighter, scraped ground flattens the contrast the painter intended, and the doors now read paler and cooler than they once did. Seen as a document of change they are fascinating; seen as the decorator's intended effect they are a partial survival."},"cma:121872":{"d":"A horizontal overdoor painting, about 52 by 151 centimeters, in oil on canvas, made by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. It is one of several pictures of identical size in the museum's Rousseau group, made to sit above a doorway at a height where details are hard to read, so pictures in this position generally rely on clear shapes and a strong overall effect rather than on fine finish.","i":"Overdoors of this period might imitate marble reliefs in grisaille, show garlands and trophies, or frame small landscapes; the classical designs on Rousseau's doors suggest a related vocabulary. The taste for such decoration followed the excavations at Pompeii and Herculaneum, which made the ancient world a model for French interiors, and the 1790s were years when that taste was increasingly austere.","c":"It is easy to condescend to such pictures as filler, but placing a convincing painting in a space meant to be overlooked takes craft. The long format forces the designer to compose in a band, a problem the classical frieze solved long before. Rousseau's overdoors play a supporting role, and their success is that no one notices them until they are missing."},"cma:121873":{"d":"A wide, low overdoor painting, about 52 by 151 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Such pictures were fitted above doorways in a decorated room, in the space between the top of the door frame and the cornice. At roughly three times as wide as high, the format is a horizontal frieze, made to be seen from below by anyone passing through the door.","i":"Overdoor paintings, called dessus-de-porte in French, were a standard feature of eighteenth-century interiors, filling an otherwise dull space with landscapes, still lifes, putti or imitations of sculpted relief. Their subjects were often lighter and more decorative than those of easel pictures. Rousseau's overdoors belong to a scheme that also includes doors and wall panels, so they presumably echo the same classical vocabulary.","c":"The overdoor is the humblest place for a painting, high up and seen at an angle, and the best painters answer that with bold, simple designs. This one's width gives it a frieze-like quality that suits Neoclassical taste. Removed from its doorway it loses its reason for being, and in a gallery at eye level it is exposed to a scrutiny it was never designed to receive."},"cma:121874":{"d":"One of several overdoor paintings of identical size, about 52 by 151 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. The horizontal format is that of the space above a double door, where a painting set into the wall's woodwork completes the doorway as a framed unit with the door below. The canvas support suggests it was painted separately and fitted into place.","i":"A doorway in an eighteenth-century salon was composed in layers: the door leaves, the molding and, above them, a painting that tied the opening into the wall's decoration. A canvas about 151 centimeters wide would comfortably span a pair of door leaves with their frame. A set of matching overdoors suggests a room with several doors, in other words a grand space.","c":"Overdoors repeat the same shape around a room, so each is a variation on a theme, and the value lies in the whole sequence. On its own the canvas can seem slight, but that is a fair price for decoration meant to be absorbed rather than studied. Its modest scale and secondary position are exactly what the job required."},"cma:121875":{"d":"One of two tall canvases, each about 264 by 85 centimeters, painted in oil by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s as decorative wall panels. Identical dimensions suggest a matching pair, perhaps meant to flank a doorway, window or chimney, and the vertical format is typical of the fields into which rooms were divided. The canvas belongs to the same group as Rousseau's doors and overdoor paintings.","i":"A pair of matching panels reflects the Neoclassical love of symmetry: walls were divided into regular fields, each element balanced by its partner across the room. Decorative ensembles like this were designed as wholes, so panels, doors and overdoors repeat a common vocabulary of classical ornament. The designers of such rooms are rarely named, which makes the attribution to Rousseau valuable.","c":"As one of a matching pair, the panel is a piece of a system, and its virtues are those of a system: regularity, balance, restraint. Neoclassical decoration prized order over surprise, which is both the charm and the limit of the genre. The scale suggests ambition for a grand room, and the canvas suggests a decorator working efficiently. Pleasing as part of the ensemble, modest alone."},"cma:121876":{"d":"One of two matching decorative panels, each about 264 by 50 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. The pair were presumably meant to face one another across a room or to flank a feature, since the museum catalogs two with identical dimensions. Canvas, rather than wood, suggests they were painted separately and mounted in the wall paneling.","i":"In the Neoclassical interior symmetry was a virtue: matching panels balanced one another, and the eye was led along the wall by repetition. Such schemes demanded a consistent hand, so a single decorator like Rousseau could supply panels, doors and overdoor paintings that agreed with one another. That the museum holds so many pieces by him may mean a substantial part of one interior survives, which is rare.","c":"As a decorative element it does what it was designed to do, supplying vertical emphasis and a pause in the wall. The sameness of matching pairs can seem lifeless to a modern eye, but it reflects an ideal of order that Neoclassical designers pursued deliberately. The panel is a document of that ideal more than a personal statement, and should be looked at that way."},"cma:121877":{"d":"A narrower decorative panel, about 264 by 55 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. At nearly five times as high as it is wide, it is a slender vertical field of the kind used to fill the spaces between larger elements, such as windows, mirrors or doors, in a decorated room. A second panel with the same dimensions is catalogued with it.","i":"Slender vertical panels gave rooms of the late eighteenth century their rhythm, repeating along the wall like the pilasters of a classical building. The taste owed much to Antiquity, to engraved designs of ancient ornament and to the Renaissance grotesques that had revived it. The painter's job was to fit the ornament to the available height and width, a problem of design before it was a problem of painting.","c":"Narrow panels are harder than they look, because everything must be arranged in height without monotony. Judged by its dimensions, this one is typical of a type that depends on rhythm: its effect comes from repetition with variation along a wall. Out of its room it can feel like a stranger; in the ensemble it would have been a quiet supporting voice."},"cma:121878":{"d":"A decorative panel, about 264 by 55 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s, the match of another panel with the same dimensions. The proportions, nearly five to one, are those of a strip meant to be placed beside or between other elements, and the canvas, not wood, shows that it was painted separately and fitted into the wall decoration.","i":"The French habit of dividing walls into panels framed by moldings meant that a decorated room was a kind of picture in pieces. Each panel had to work alone and in company, which is why this one would have been designed alongside its partner. The 1790s brought the Neoclassical emphasis on clarity and order to such schemes, a break from the curving, asymmetrical forms of earlier Rococo decoration.","c":"A matching pair is a test of consistency, and a decorator who can repeat a design faithfully is judged on craft as much as invention. That craft is the point here: the panel is evidence of workshop skill at its most disciplined, the fitting of design to a fixed field. It lacks the individual voice of an easel painting, but it never meant to have one."},"cma:121879":{"d":"A narrow decorative panel, about 264 by 50 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. Another panel with the same dimensions is catalogued with it. At more than five times as high as it is wide, it acts less like a picture than like a painted pilaster, a vertical element that organizes the wall around it. The canvas support suggests it was prepared separately and fitted into a wall scheme.","i":"Painted decoration in the later eighteenth century often imitated architecture and sculpture, with fictive pilasters and reliefs in the manner of ancient Rome. The excavated houses at Pompeii and Herculaneum, published in lavish engravings, taught designers to treat wall surfaces as fields of slender ornament, and a decorator could vary one design across many panels. A panel of this proportion probably belongs to that approach.","c":"The proportion is its most distinctive feature: at about five to one it asks the painter to think like an architect, and the best such panels use their height to make the wall seem taller. It would have been seen from below and across a room, so legibility mattered more than fine detail. Out of context it is an elegant fragment; in context, a supporting actor."},"cma:121880":{"d":"A second very narrow decorative panel, about 264 by 26 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s, matched in size by another. Such strips were fixed beside larger fields, completing the vertical rhythm of a wall. The canvas support, rather than carved or painted wood, shows the choice of a medium that could be prepared in a studio and fitted on site.","i":"The pairing of identical narrow panels hints at a symmetrical arrangement, one on each side of a central feature. Neoclassical interior design favored such symmetry, drawing on the ordered, axial planning of ancient and Renaissance architecture. As a surviving fragment, the strip also tells us that a room's decoration was designed down to the smallest filler, not only the large central pictures.","c":"Strips like this are the supporting cast of decorative art, and their pleasure is the pleasure of order: a repeated vertical, a careful fit. A critic would not rank one against Rousseau's doors or his larger canvases, and should not; but the discipline shown in so narrow a field is real. It is a modest object that rewards a modest kind of attention."},"cma:121881":{"d":"One of two slender decorative panels of the same size, about 264 by 34 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. The tall, strip-like format was meant to fit a narrow space on a wall and to repeat in a pair. The museum catalogs Rousseau's doors, panels and overdoor paintings together as parts of one decorative scheme.","i":"The 1790s were an unsettled decade in France, with revolution, war and a changing elite, yet decorative work continued, and Antiquity remained the favored model. A slender panel like this was a modest part of that effort, built to carry classical ornament into the narrowest parts of a room. Even in troubled years, people still wanted their rooms finished.","c":"Its virtue is economy: a strip of canvas that has to do its job at a glance and without fuss. It cannot rival the doors' presence or the larger panels' scale, but ensembles depend on such modest elements for their rhythm. In a gallery the strip looks thin; on a wall it would have been a quiet vertical beat that made its neighbors look larger."},"cma:121882":{"d":"The narrowest of the decorative panels, about 264 by 26 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. About ten times as high as it is wide, it is essentially a painted strip, a vertical border or filler used to complete a wall scheme. A matching panel with the same dimensions is catalogued with it, so the two probably faced one another across a space.","i":"Strips this narrow work like the moldings of a room: they define edges and join larger fields. In Neoclassical decoration such borders were often as carefully designed as the main panels, because the style relied on the interplay of large and small elements. Their canvases were cut to fit exact spaces, which makes them among the most site-specific pieces of a decorative scheme.","c":"At ten to one a painting becomes a line, and the interesting question is whether the painter can still make that line work. A strip cannot carry a composition, only a rhythm, and the format shows the decorator's habit of working to whatever dimensions a room dictates. It is the quietest object in the group and, for that reason, the one that best shows how a room was built from small parts."},"cma:123953":{"d":"A combined entry for an overdoor painting, about 52 by 151 centimeters, in oil on canvas, together with a set of double-leaf doors, both by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. The two make a complete doorway: the door leaves below and the wide canvas above, as they were designed to be seen. The classical designs recorded on the group's doors suggest the same vocabulary across both.","i":"Seeing an overdoor with its doors is a reminder that this decoration was architectural: a doorway was a unit, with leaves, frame and painting composed together. In a house of the 1790s such doorways structured movement from room to room, and the eye was invited to read them as ceremonial thresholds. Rousseau's name on all the parts shows one decorator supplying the whole doorway.","c":"The combined entry is the most informative of the overdoors: it reconnects two elements the museum otherwise catalogs separately and lets a visitor judge the doorway as designed. The wide canvas crowning the tall leaves makes a pleasing rectangle on a vertical, and the pairing explains the overdoor's shape. It is decorative art of a high civic taste rather than a work of personal feeling, best seen as architecture."},"cma:123954":{"d":"A wide overdoor canvas, about 52 by 151 centimeters, painted in oil by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s and catalogued with several others of identical size. Overdoors were part of the fixed decoration of a room, set into the wall above a door, and the canvas support would have allowed the painter to execute them in the studio and have them mounted on site.","i":"French decorative painting of the late eighteenth century was a collaborative trade: designers, carpenters, gilders and painters worked together to deliver a finished room, and a decorator such as Rousseau might supply both design and execution. Overdoors were the least prominent items in this chain, yet they completed the composition of the wall. Their survival in numbers is unusual.","c":"Praise for an overdoor is necessarily qualified, because it is decoration for a particular place and most of its virtues are contextual. Its three-to-one proportion yields a calm horizontal that balances the vertical doors and panels, and that is its main job. Within that limited brief it does what it should, quietly."},"cma:123955":{"d":"Another overdoor canvas of the same format, about 52 by 151 centimeters, in oil by Pierre Rousseau, from the 1790s, belonging to the same group of doors, wall panels and overdoors. Like its siblings it was made to be set above a door and seen from below, in a position where a painting must read at a distance and from an angle, which governs its whole design.","i":"Rooms were organized by axis and symmetry: doors faced each other, and the overdoors above them repeated one another across the room. The grand salons of the period depended on that order. A matching overdoor like this was part of a repeated beat around the walls, which is why such pictures were designed in sets rather than as individual compositions.","c":"As a single canvas it is slight, and as part of a set it is functional; the real achievement is the set. Rousseau's group of doors, panels and overdoors preserves more of a decorative scheme than most museums hold, and each overdoor adds a small, necessary piece. Its pleasure is the satisfaction of a thing exactly fitted to its place."},"cma:123956":{"d":"A set of tall double-leaf doors, about 274 by 63 centimeters, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. In size, medium and date they match the other doors in the museum's Rousseau group, so they were presumably made for the same decorative scheme and carry the classical designs that characterize it, though the original surface may have been altered.","i":"Classical designs on doors of this period draw on a repertoire that Europe's designers had been building since the discoveries at Pompeii and Herculaneum: motifs such as urns, garlands and medallions. A painter like Rousseau could adapt these to a given door by scaling, which is why one vocabulary could serve a whole room. In the 1790s such ornament carried an air of learning and civic seriousness.","c":"These doors share the group's strengths and weaknesses: elegant proportion, a controlled classical vocabulary and a surface that time and taste may have altered. It would be unfair to demand drama of them, since a decorative ensemble succeeds by agreement among its parts, and this set does its part. It is worth seeing for the harmony of the whole rather than for any single flourish."},"cma:123957":{"d":"A single double-leaf door, listed in the singular, about 274 by 63 centimeters, painted in oil on wood by Pierre Rousseau in the 1790s. The singular title suggests one leaf, or a door catalogued separately from its partner, within a group of otherwise paired sets. Like the others it is tall and narrow, with the proportions of a principal room.","i":"A door leaf separated from its partner is a common fate for architectural fittings: rooms are dismantled, sets are split and pieces travel separately. The singular title here may reflect that history, and it is a reminder that museum holdings of woodwork are shaped by accident as much as by design. Rousseau's group, made in the 1790s, is unusual in surviving in such numbers.","c":"A single leaf is half an object, and it asks the viewer to imagine its partner and the room it served. It has the same slender, upright dignity as the paired sets and the same sensitivity to context. As an artifact it is valuable chiefly as a data point, one more piece of evidence for how the whole ensemble was built."},"met:435809":{"d":"A late-summer wheat harvest seen from a rise. Reapers cut the ripe grain and bind it into sheaves in a broad field, while in the foreground a group of workers rests under a pear tree to eat, one of them sprawled asleep on the ground. A path leads the eye down through the golden wheat toward a village and church, and beyond them the land opens into a hazy distance of water and sky. The oil-on-oak panel, about 119 by 162 centimeters, is large for its kind.","i":"The Harvesters is one of a series of paintings of the months or seasons that Bruegel made in 1565, probably for a patron in Antwerp; five of the set survive, three in Vienna, one in Prague and this one in New York. It belongs to a long tradition of calendar images from Books of Hours, which Bruegel enlarged into panoramic landscapes. Viewers have read it as a celebration of labor and nature or as a mild comment on peasant ways; Bruegel offers no sermon.","c":"Bruegel balances two kinds of time, the hard time of reaping and the slack time of the midday meal, and joins them with a path that carries the eye into a hazy distance. The golden wheat and the heat make the day palpable, and the figures are types, observed with sympathy and a faint irony, not portraits. What lifts it above calendar illustration is the structure: people are neither ornament nor lesson. Among the series it is the warmest."},"cma:302194":{"d":"A tiny portrait on wood, only about 9 by 7 centimeters, of a man holding a watch. Behind him in the background is the domed Marekerk, one of Leiden's most distinctive landmarks. Painted around 1665 to 1670, the picture is a miniature in oil, made for intimate viewing in the hand or on the wall of a private room, with every detail rendered by a very fine brush.","i":"The watch is often a symbol of mortality and the passage of time, though it was also a costly object that told of the owner's means. Slingelandt, like his colleague Gerrit Dou, belonged to the Leiden fijnschilders, the fine painters, whose work was prized across Europe for its extraordinary detail and precise technique. Including the Marekerk places the sitter in Leiden and ties a private likeness to civic pride.","c":"At about the size of a playing card, the achievement is control: a face, a hand, a watch and a distant church, all with convincing weight. The Leiden fine painters are sometimes accused of coolness, and a polished surface can stand in for temperament, but at this scale polish is the right virtue. The picture is less a character study than a display of skill and an object of delight, which is probably what its first owner wanted."},"cma:143770":{"d":"A large oil sketch for a ceiling: a heavenly vision of angels and personified virtues in atmospheric colors, with a large empty oval and, as the main feature, a cartouche of regal paraphernalia. The angels hold a chain bearing the orders of the royal family: San Januarius, the Holy Spirit of France, the Golden Fleece, shown as a lamb, and Saint George of Constantine. The canvas measures about 172 by 122 centimeters.","i":"This is a study for the Apotheosis of Ferdinand IV and Maria Carolina, King and Queen of Naples, completed in 1781 for the old library hall of the Palazzo degli Studi. The final ceiling changed the design almost completely: the regalia was dropped, and the oval, empty here, was made smaller to frame a portrait of the royal couple, so their ascent is shown as the raising of their portraits to heaven. The medallion conveys that their reign is filled with fortune.","c":"A sketch shows a painter's mind before the patron's caution sets in, and this one is lively: delicate touch, atmospheric colors and an airy crowd of angels and virtues in the Neapolitan decorative tradition. The allegorical machinery of regalia, orders and cartouche is cluttered, and the final ceiling's move to a portrait in an oval is an improvement in directness. The study is therefore most interesting as a road not taken and a record of how court allegory was edited."},"cma:97154":{"d":"A Virgin and Child with the young John the Baptist and angels, painted in oil on a wood panel about 95 by 93 centimeters, around 1500. The nearly square dimensions suggest the round tondo format favored in Florence for domestic devotion. The group of Mary, the Christ Child, the infant John and attendant angels follows a well-known pattern of Florentine devotional painting, intended for prayer at home.","i":"John the Baptist is the patron saint of Florence, and the infant John, cousin of Jesus, was a favorite companion for the Christ Child in Florentine paintings, which linked a family's piety to the city's own protector. Angels attend the Virgin as Queen of Heaven. Such pictures hung in bedrooms and living rooms as a focus for prayer and as signs of a household's taste, and a painter of about 1500 could count on steady demand.","c":"Any Florentine Madonna of about 1500 is measured against Leonardo, Raphael and Michelangelo, and Donzello is a minor figure beside them. What he offers is a competent, familiar and pleasantly composed devotional image, and the tondo shape, if well managed, gives such a group a quiet rhythm. A picture made for prayer rather than for discovery, and best enjoyed on those terms."},"nga:228924":{"d":"Saint Sebastian stands full-length in a slightly relaxed pose on a tiled floor, holding arrows or a staff in one hand and a sword with an ornate hilt in the other. He has soft, youthful features and long flowing hair under a hat, and is dressed like a young nobleman of the day in a long dark cloak over a richly decorated red garment, red tights and black shoes. A halo marks his sanctity, and the name S. SEBASTIANUS is inscribed at the top of the carved, gilded frame.","i":"Sebastian, a Roman soldier shot with arrows for his Christian faith, was invoked against plague, and the arrows identify him. Here he is not the near-naked martyr of Italian art but a courtly young man in contemporary dress, a choice that makes the saint a model for the people who prayed to him. The inscription helps worshipers identify the figure at a distance, as altarpiece saints needed to be recognized quickly.","c":"The saint's suffering is displaced by elegance: red tights, a swinging cloak and a relaxed stance make him a fashion plate of the late fifteenth century rather than a victim. That is a matter of taste, not a flaw, but it means the picture has little of the pathos that Italian Sebastians sought. The decorative values, red against the dark cloak and the gold of the frame, are the point, and the tiled floor gives the figure a believable place."},"nga:228925":{"d":"Saint Barbara stands upright, facing the viewer, with long wavy hair and a golden crown. She wears a robe with intricate red and gold patterns over a green garment, holds a white feather in her right hand and, on her left hand, an open book supporting a small tower. The background is ornamental, the picture sits in a decorative wooden frame, and the inscription SANTA BARBA appears above her head.","i":"The tower is Barbara's attribute: according to legend her father shut her in a tower to keep her from Christianity, and she was later martyred. She became the patron saint of people in danger of sudden death, such as miners and artillerymen, and the book suggests her learning and faith. The crown marks her rank as a virgin martyr. In altarpieces such saints stand together as a company of protectors for the congregation.","c":"Barbara is built from three emblems, tower, book and feather, and the design makes each legible at a glance, which is what an altarpiece figure needs. The rich patterns of the robe and the ornamental ground make her more a decorative presence than a psychological one, and the effect is of a jeweled icon rather than a lifelike woman. Within those limits the picture is elegant and carefully organized."},"nga:228926":{"d":"Saint Lawrence stands upright in ornate clerical robes, a cape draped over one shoulder, holding a gridiron in his right hand and a book in his left. His expression is serene, a halo frames his head, and the background is textured gold and dark green inside an ornate frame with gold accents. The oil-on-panel figure belongs to a group of four saints on Portuguese panels of the late fifteenth century.","i":"Lawrence, a deacon of Rome martyred in 258, is shown with the gridiron on which, according to tradition, he was roasted alive, and the book marks his role as a church official in charge of the Church's charity and treasury. His richly decorated deacon's robes set him within the clergy. Serene faces were the convention for martyrs in the 1400s: the instrument of death is carried like a trophy, not endured.","c":"The gridiron makes the saint's identity unmistakable and gives the picture its one touch of irony: an instrument of torture held like a staff of office. The painter's concern is the dignity of the figure and the richness of cloth rather than drama, and the gold-and-green ground sets him off cleanly. It is a quiet, dignified image in a series of similar saints, valuable as part of the whole."},"nga:46054":{"d":"A half-length bishop with a full beard and mustache and a calm expression stands against a subdued green ground that turns to gold around his halo. He wears a mitre decorated in gold and red and a richly patterned red vestment with gold trim, holds a crozier and raises his right hand in blessing. In his left hand he supports a second head, presumably his own severed head. The oil-on-panel picture sits in an ornate golden frame.","i":"A bishop carrying a head marks Saint Dionysius, or Denis, the legendary first bishop of Paris, who was beheaded for his faith and, according to tradition, carried his severed head after his execution. Medieval writers often confused him with Dionysius the Areopagite, Saint Paul's convert in Athens, which added to his prestige. Portuguese painters of the late 1400s worked under strong Flemish influence, and such saints were typically placed in carved and gilded altarpieces.","c":"A saint carrying his own head is a gruesome conceit, and the painter's solution is to make it serene: a calm face, a steady blessing, the second head held like an attribute rather than a horror. That decorum suits the picture's purpose, which is devotion, not shock. The red and gold surfaces show a delight in textile and metalwork, and the hieratic stillness gives the figure weight. Conventional, but well judged for an altar."},"nga:42445":{"d":"A man sits upright and turns his face to the viewer, shown from the chest up. He has fair skin, rounded features, light-colored eyes and curly brown hair brushed back, and wears a dark jacket, waistcoat, white shirt and black cravat. The background is a plain, muted brown. The portrait, painted in oil on cardboard and measuring about 36 by 26 centimeters, is titled by the sitter's role rather than by his name, which suggests it was once half of a pair.","i":"Portraits like this were an affordable way for ordinary American families to have likenesses before photography took over; the daguerreotype reached America in 1839 and was widespread within a decade. Painters of the Prior-Hamblin School, associated with William Matthew Prior and his relatives, worked in a plain manner with simple outlines and little shading, and cheap supports such as cardboard kept prices low. The sitters' names have often been lost.","c":"The appeal of such pictures lies in frankness: the sitter looks straight out, the face is built of simple, rounded forms, and nothing is flattered. A trained academician would see the lack of modeling as a flaw; modern viewers often see the same flatness as strength, a clear sense of design. As art it is modest, but it is honest, and as a record of an ordinary American face about 1845 it is valuable."},"nga:42446":{"d":"A girl is seated facing forward, shown from the waist up, with large eyes, subtly colored cheeks and hair parted in the middle and braided, tied with a red ribbon. She wears a blue garment with a white neckline and white accents on the sleeves and an orange beaded necklace. Behind her are a wood-textured wall and red and gold drapes with tassels, and at the left part of a musical instrument rests on a table. The picture, oil on cardboard, is about 35 by 24 centimeters.","i":"The drapes with tassels borrow the vocabulary of grand portraiture for a modest sitter, and the musical instrument suggests accomplishments a family wished to show, since music lessons were a mark of a daughter's education. The title names her relation to the family rather than her name, so the sitter is anonymous today. Studios of the Prior-Hamblin School often repeated such props, so the setting may reflect studio stock rather than the girl's own home.","c":"The painter gives the child a direct gaze and a clear silhouette, and the red and gold drapery adds a bit of theater around a small figure. The flattened modeling is typical of the school, and the picture does not pretend to depth. Its charm lies in the precise little details, the braids, the beads and the half-seen instrument, which make the girl a person with her own accessories rather than a type."},"nga:42469":{"d":"A young child sits on a carved wooden chair, facing forward, with legs hanging over the edge of the seat. She holds a small slate with writing on it and has light blonde hair and big blue eyes. She wears a white dress with red trim, white pants, brown lace-up boots and a red necklace. The background is dark brown with a red curtain partly visible. The oil-on-canvas portrait, about 69 by 56 centimeters, was painted about 1845.","i":"A slate was a common sign of schooling in children's portraits of the 1840s, standing for learning and good conduct and for the family's investment in a child's education. The carved chair, curtain and necklace were stock props of such portraits. The Prior-Hamblin School's flat, bright manner suited children well, because their clear outlines and large eyes translated easily into simple forms.","c":"The best touch is the legs hanging over the edge of the chair: it is a true observation that breaks the formality and makes the child small and real. The drawing is flat, the modeling thin, and the slate a convention, but the solemn gaze carries the picture. It is naive in technique and sure in its sense of pattern, and as portraits of children go it is both stiff and affecting."},"nga:46":{"d":"The angel Gabriel and the Virgin Mary meet in a tall, narrow panel of about 90 by 34 centimeters, painted around 1434 to 1436 by Jan van Eyck. The setting resembles the interior of a church more than an ordinary house, and the lettering in the picture carries the words of the angel's greeting and of Mary's reply. The picture has been transferred from its wood panel to canvas, an old restoration procedure that affects its surface.","i":"The Annunciation is the moment, told in the Gospel of Luke, when Gabriel announces to Mary that she will bear the Son of God, and she accepts with the words Behold the handmaid of the Lord. Van Eyck gives the sacred event the weight of a real place, setting it in a church-like space that links Mary to the Church itself. The tall format suggests that the panel may have been one wing of a larger altarpiece.","c":"Van Eyck's genius is his union of exact observation and spiritual meaning, and even in this transferred panel the extraordinary control of light, texture and architecture is evident. The transfer to canvas means that the surface cannot be judged exactly as the painter left it, and some of the finest glazes may be lost. What survives is a standard of realism that few painters in any century have matched."},"met:435847":{"d":"Saint Vincent, patron saint of Lisbon, is shown in a tall, narrow oil panel, about 163 by 53 cm, with a book and a ship as his attributes. The proportions suggest that it was a side panel or wing of a larger altarpiece rather than a picture meant to stand alone. Frei Carlos was a painter who worked in Portugal in the sixteenth century in a style that owes much to Flemish painting, and the panel gives the saint a calm, upright presence.","i":"Vincent of Saragossa was a deacon martyred in Spain around 304. In the Middle Ages his relics were brought to Portugal and, in 1173, moved by ship to Lisbon, where in legend ravens guarded the vessel; the ship with ravens became the emblem of the city. He holds a book as a deacon, the keeper of the Gospels. A picture of the city's patron was a civic as well as a religious image, a protector for the port and its sailors.","c":"A tall narrow panel challenges the painter to give a single figure monumentality, and the Portuguese-Flemish manner, with its precision and rich color, does that by sculptural clarity rather than movement. The strengths are gravity and finish; the limit is a certain stiffness, since the saint is an icon more than a person. It works as a dignified civic emblem, and as a document of how Portuguese painters absorbed Netherlandish art."},"met:437960":{"d":"A portrait miniature in enamel of a man named Richard Abell, dated 1724. The record lists a man and a portrait; his dress, wig and features are not described, and no dimensions are given, though enamel miniatures of this kind were usually only a few centimeters high. It belongs to the Moses Lazarus Collection, given by his daughters Josephine and Sarah Lazarus in memory of their father between 1888 and 1895.","i":"Christian Friedrich Zincke, born in Dresden, moved to London in 1704 and became the leading enamel painter in England, painting the royal family and the nobility. Enamel miniatures are made by fusing powdered colored glass onto a metal plate in a kiln, and the colors, once fired, never fade. Moses Lazarus, a New York collector, was the father of the poet Emma Lazarus; his daughters gave the collection to the Metropolitan Museum.","c":"Enamel gave Zincke's miniatures a jewel-like brilliance and permanence that watercolor on ivory could not equal, and he exploited the medium to the full: smooth, clear flesh, bright, glassy color and a refined surface. The price of that perfection is a certain porcelain stillness, with the sitter's character polished away. A technically brilliant object, and a rather impersonal portrait, as was the taste of the time."},"met:437961":{"d":"A portrait miniature in enamel of a woman known as Mrs. Vanderbank, dated about 1730. The record lists a woman and a portrait; her dress, hair and expression are not described, and no dimensions are given. The name suggests the family of the London portrait painter John Vanderbank, though the museum record does not say so. Like the miniature of Richard Abell, it came with the Moses Lazarus Collection.","i":"Zincke painted many of the leading artists and their circles in London, and John Vanderbank (1694–1739) was one of the most fashionable portrait painters of the 1720s and 1730s, so a portrait of a Mrs. Vanderbank by Zincke would fit that world. The technique of enamel, in which colors are fused onto a metal plate in repeated firings, was so difficult that Zincke's rivals were few, and he commanded high prices.","c":"Zincke's enamels aim for a high polish: the glassy surface, the strong, clean color and the fine detail of lace and jewelry combine to give his sitters an air of well-groomed composure. His women especially have a porcelain bloom. The cost is individuality, and Mrs. Vanderbank, in all probability, is more a beautifully finished type than a person. Delicate and handsome work from a time of fashionable perfection."},"met:437959":{"d":"A portrait miniature in enamel of an unidentified man. The record lists a man and a portrait; no date or dimensions are recorded, and the museum gives no description of the sitter's wig, dress or face. Zincke worked in London from 1704 until the 1740s, which bounds the likely date. The miniature came to the museum by bequest of Millie Bruhl Fredrick in 1962.","i":"Zincke's sitters included Frederick, Prince of Wales, and many members of the court and aristocracy, who wore his enamels as lockets, set them in snuffboxes or hung them from watch chains. An unnamed man in a miniature might have been a patron's friend, a husband or a lover; the lack of a name has severed that story. Zincke retired about 1746, when his eyesight failed, and died in 1767.","c":"As a type, the Zincke male miniature is firm in outline and clear in color, with a sober richness suited to a wigged gentleman. The medium rewards close looking at the textures of linen and lace, and the face is modeled with fine stippled touches. The person remains unknown, and the portrait must stand as an example of enamel painting in its prime, pleasing and slightly remote."},"met:437962":{"d":"A portrait miniature in enamel of a young man, listed in the record only as 'man' and 'portrait', with no date or dimensions. The museum gives no description of his appearance, dress or expression, and the identification of the sitter as young rests on the title. It was bequeathed to the museum by Catherine D. Wentworth in 1948, and it is one of several Zincke enamels in the museum's collection.","i":"Enamel portraits were produced slowly: each color had to be fired separately at high temperature, and a single flaw could ruin weeks of work, which made Zincke's miniatures expensive prestige objects. A young man in enamel might be a son at the end of his education, a groom or a friend; such miniatures were made for love as well as for show. This is the type that kept Zincke busy for decades in London.","c":"A young sitter suits enamel: smooth skin, fresh color and an unlined face play to the medium's strengths, and Zincke's best youthful portraits have a bright, almost porcelain beauty. The risk is vacancy, with the face as a lovely surface and little behind it. An appealing specimen of English enamel painting, and an anonymous one."},"met:436988":{"d":"A hunter and his dog come upon a company of nymphs in the countryside, the moment when Ameto discovers them in Boccaccio's Comedia delle ninfe fiorentine. The panel, tempera on wood about 54 by 56 centimeters and nearly square, was painted in Italy about 1410, and the story is told as a clear, small scene with men, women, a hunt and a dog.","i":"Boccaccio's Ameto, written about 1341 to 1342, mixes prose and verse: the rough hunter Ameto meets a company of nymphs and, through his love for one of them, is led from crude appetite toward understanding, with the nymphs standing for the virtues. Panels like this may have been made for a domestic setting such as a marriage chest, where a story of love refining the lover would have suited the occasion.","c":"Early fifteenth-century narrative painting is at its most charming in small panels like this one, where a literary text becomes a clear, lively scene. The anonymous Master of 1416 tells the story simply and keeps the hunt and the nymphs legible, and the late Gothic taste for elegance and incident gives the picture its appeal. It is more delightful than profound, and its great interest is as an early illustration of a Boccaccio text."},"met:436989":{"d":"Two shepherds, Alcesto and Acaten, compete in a contest watched by men and women in a pastoral setting, a scene from Boccaccio's Comedia delle ninfe fiorentine. The tempera-on-wood panel, about 54 by 56 centimeters, matches in size and medium the companion panel of Ameto discovering the nymphs, so the two probably belonged to the same series. It dates from about 1410.","i":"Contests between rival shepherds are a convention of pastoral literature going back to the ancient poets Theocritus and Virgil, and Boccaccio borrowed the form for his Ameto, a work often seen as an early landmark of the Renaissance pastoral. A painted version about seventy years after the text was written shows how quickly such literary stories entered Italian domestic art.","c":"The panel works as storytelling: a clear event, a rivalry, an audience, and the late Gothic pleasure in costume and landscape detail. This panel and its companion suggest a larger series of which parts have been lost, and that loss is the main frustration. Charming rather than ambitious, it is an object whose interest lies in what it shows about the early Renaissance taste for secular literature in painted form."},"met:437298":{"d":"Mary, Saint Joseph and the Christ Child are shown at the Temple in Jerusalem, with other men and women, in a small, almost square panel about 34 by 40 centimeters. It is painted in tempera with gold on wood around 1405 to 1415, and the gold gives the scene the glow of a medieval altarpiece. The size suggests that it may once have been one of several scenes in the lower part of a larger altarpiece.","i":"The Presentation (Luke 2:22-38) shows the Holy Family fulfilling Jewish law forty days after Christ's birth; the aged Simeon, promised that he would not die before seeing the Messiah, takes the child in his arms and prophesies that a sword will pierce Mary's soul. The feast of Candlemas on 2 February commemorates it with candles. For Christian viewers the scene linked the Nativity to the Passion.","c":"Pirez works in the International Gothic idiom: gold, rich color, courtly grace and a delight in decorative detail, with only a tentative grasp of the new Florentine concern for space and weight. The panel will appeal to those who like the refined, storybook side of early Italian painting. It is a minor work by a minor master, but a useful witness to the traffic of artists across Europe around 1400."},"met:435846":{"d":"Saint Anthony Abbot is shown in glory in this oil on canvas, about 46 by 43 centimeters. The museum's keywords record Anthony the Great, men, angels and saints, so the saint's pose, his attributes, the angels and the colors are left unstated. The small size and the subject suggest a sketch or preparatory model for a larger work, such as a ceiling or altarpiece, by Carlo Innocenzo Carloni, a fresco painter from the Italian lake region who worked widely in Central Europe.","i":"Anthony the Great (about 251 to 356), the Egyptian hermit called the father of monasticism, is usually shown as an old man in a monk's habit with a T-shaped staff and a bell, often with a pig, and with flames recalling the disease called Saint Anthony's fire. A glorification shows the saint taken up to heaven among angels, rewarded for a life of prayer and for the temptations he endured in the desert. Such subjects suited the soaring ceiling paintings of the Rococo.","c":"Oil sketches for large decorative schemes often have more life than the finished work, and this one, if it is such a sketch, should be judged by its energy and by the lightness of its color. Carloni was a master of airy, luminous ceiling design in the Rococo manner, and his sketches are typically loose and bright. The record leaves room for doubt, but the type is clear: a quick, delightful vision of heaven."},"nga:130555":{"d":"Two minuscule people stand in the center of an expansive, rugged landscape dusted with snow in this horizontal painting of about 35 by 49 cm. Snow nestles among tawny-brown ridges that roll like waves back to the horizon, which comes almost halfway up the composition. The figures, painted with a few dabs of fawn brown, stand on a spit of land right of center. Slate blue mountains line the horizon under a steely-blue sky.","i":"Friedrich used landscape as a language of spiritual feeling, and spring in a northern country, with its melting snow, suggests renewal tempered by cold. Two tiny figures, set against a vast terrain, reverse the usual scale: nature is large and man is small, a Romantic image of humility before creation. For Friedrich, a Lutheran, a landscape could be a meditation, and the empty ridges invite the viewer to contemplate rather than to travel.","c":"Friedrich's genius lies in emptiness: a high horizon, ridges like waves, and a restricted palette of tans and slate blues, with two figures who give the vastness its measure. The painting is modest in size and expansive in feeling, and its simplicity is its strength. The risk of the formula is that the effect depends on the viewer's willingness to be still. For those who are, it is a small, concentrated example of his art."},"met:438417":{"d":"Two men in dark coats and caps stand with their backs to the viewer on a rising path, at the left edge of a small canvas, close together, one leaning toward the other. Beside them a huge old oak twists across the picture, its bare branches and roots reaching over rocks and a grassy bank. In the twilight sky, glowing pink and violet near the horizon, a thin crescent moon hangs low, and a dark stand of evergreens appears far off at the right. The figures are small and the scene is quiet. The paint is smooth and thin, and the colours are muted browns, olives and greys against the soft sky.","i":"The Met says the two men are seen from behind so that viewers can share their communion with nature. One has often been identified as Friedrich himself and the other as his pupil August Heinrich, who died in 1822. German Romantics treated the moon as an object of pious contemplation. This is the third version of a composition first painted in 1819 (now in Dresden) and is dated about 1825 to 1830. The men wear so-called Old German dress, which was linked in those years with patriotic and liberal sympathies, and some writers read the picture politically. The painting itself gives no key, so the meaning stays open.","c":"Friedrich's habit of turning his figures away from us is used here with great economy: two small shapes, one big tree, a sliver of moon. The mood comes from restraint. The cost of a third version is surprise, since a famous idea is being replayed, and the foreground of roots and rocks feels heavier and busier than the sky it frames. Even so, the small size suits the subject, drawing the viewer in close, and the glow around the moon is handled with a delicacy that holds the eye after the figures have been read."},"nga:42285":{"d":"Two women sit close together on a couch, seen from the lap up, in matching white dresses with baby-blue polka dots, ruffled at neck and wrists. Their dark hair is piled high with long ringlets down their backs, and each wears a black ribbon choker. The woman on the left looks down with dark eyes and wears a gold ring; the other, with chestnut hair and ice-blue eyes, holds an open fan. Loosely painted striped and flowered fabric and a framed picture behind them complete the setting.","i":"The sitters are identified as Berthe Morisot and her sister Edma, both trained as painters, and the matching dresses speak of their closeness. The picture dates from 1869, the year Edma married and moved away from Paris, so a portrait of the two sisters together took on added meaning. The loose handling of couch and wall shows the freedom Morisot was developing under the example of her friend Édouard Manet.","c":"This is a portrait of two women in one color scheme, and the unison is the idea: the matching dots, the inward lean, the paired gazes that drop away from the viewer. The couch and wall are brushed loosely, almost carelessly, while faces and hands stay finer, a contrast that keeps the picture alive. It is still formal beside Morisot's later work, but already shows her gift for intimacy without sentiment."},"nga:52191":{"d":"A young woman sits in profile in an upholstered chair, close to us, her gaze lowered to a partly open fan in her lap. She wears a long white dress with black ribbons at her neck and shoulder, and her light brown ringlets reach her shoulders. Loose swirls of golden tan and white suggest the chair's floral fabric. Behind her a white door with a curtain opens onto a balcony with a wrought iron railing, and across the street people lean from windows with turquoise awnings.","i":"The sitter is Edma Morisot, Berthe's older sister and fellow painter. In 1869 Edma married a naval officer and moved away from Paris, and she largely gave up painting; the picture is often read in the light of that parting, with the sister turned inward and the world beyond the window out of reach. The window and balcony also echo Édouard Manet's The Balcony, for which Berthe had modeled.","c":"The best thing is the tension between the close, intimate figure, the big sweep of the white dress and the street scene beyond the balcony, which is brushed in with a few touches. The cropped composition owes something to Manet, and the loose handling of the chair is confident. The picture is a little melancholy without being sentimental, a quality that stays with Morisot's best work."},"nga:52192":{"d":"Seen from behind a low stone quay wall, a wide stretch of calm blue-green water runs into the distance of a harbour in Brittany. On the wall at the right a young woman in a white dress sits with her legs angled to the right, leaning on one hand beneath a pale pink parasol, a black hat on her head. Ships with dark hulls and tall masts lie along the far shore and in the middle of the basin, reflected in the water. Cream houses with grey roofs and a line of green trees stand on the opposite bank. The sky is pale blue with wisps of white cloud, and the whole scene is brushed in quick, thin strokes.","i":"Morisot spent two months at Lorient in Brittany in the summer of 1869 visiting her sister Edma, who had just married the naval officer Adolphe Pontillon, stationed at the port; Edma is the woman on the wall. Back in Paris, Morisot showed the picture to Édouard Manet, who admired it and advised her to retouch Edma's face and dress; Manet ended up owning the canvas. It belongs to the moment when Morisot, close to Manet's circle, was turning toward light, air and quick handling of the kind soon to be called Impressionist. The view is taken from the Quai de Rohan, with the Tour de la Découverte at the upper left.","c":"Five years before the first Impressionist exhibition, this small canvas already has the essentials: a broken, feathery touch, pale high-key colour, and a figure treated as part of the light rather than as the subject. The wall leads the eye smoothly to the ships, and the sitter's white dress echoes the sails and clouds. The weakness is a certain caution: the distant buildings are summarised, and Edma's face, retouched at Manet's suggestion, is indistinct. It is an intelligent early picture from an artist who would soon be bolder, and it shows her eye for atmosphere was already formed."},"nga:89682":{"d":"Four flowers and some greenery make a loose bouquet in a clear glass with a flaring foot. One fully open light pink peony bows over the rim, a pink bud stands upright, and two oblong flowers, perhaps closed tulips, lean to the left, one smoky purple, one cream-white edged in brick red. A single pink petal lies on the ash-and-earth-brown tabletop. The brushwork is visible throughout, and the canvas shows along the bottom edge. The painting is about 41 by 33 centimeters.","i":"Flower pieces were a respectable genre for women painters of the period, but Morisot's are rare and unfussy: a few stems, a clear glass, a fallen petal. Painted about 1869, when she was close to Édouard Manet, who had painted peonies himself in the mid-1860s, it shows her interest in capturing the look of a thing at a moment rather than arranging an elaborate display.","c":"A modest canvas that shows a lot of freedom: the open peony droops with its own weight, the glass is only a few clear strokes, and the bare canvas at the edge announces the sketch-like attitude. It lacks the structure of a worked-out still life, but that is not its aim. The fallen petal, a small act of observation, is the best thing in it, and the looseness points toward Morisot's later, lighter handling."},"nga:46661":{"d":"An older woman in a long black dress sits at the right reading a green-covered book, her grey hair under a black veil, her head bowed and her wide skirt spreading across the foot of the canvas. Behind and beside her, a younger woman in a cream dress with a blue bow in her chestnut hair sits on a couch patterned with flowers and ribbons, hands in her lap, gazing quietly away. At the left a round table holds a vase of purple hydrangea and a slip of paper, and a gold-framed mirror hangs on the grey wall above. The brushstrokes are loose and visible in the furniture, clothes and room, and the mother's black contrasts strongly with the daughter's pale dress.","i":"The sitters are Berthe Morisot's mother, Marie-Joséphine, and her sister Edma, who had married Adolphe Pontillon in 1869 and who, like Berthe a painter, gave up painting after her marriage; she was pregnant with her first child at the time. The picture was shown at the Salon of 1870. By Morisot's own account, Manet visited her studio and began painting black into the mother's dress, the head and the background, and she thought the result a caricature. The picture therefore mixes her own light touch with Manet's strong blacks, and it records the end of the sisters' years of painting side by side.","c":"The best thing here is the contrast of the two women: the mother's black skirt, a nearly abstract wedge of paint, against the daughter's creamy dress, two figures on one sofa yet apparently lost in separate thoughts. It is a perceptive study of family life without the sentimentality such subjects often invited. The weaker points are structural: the heavy blacks, reportedly added by Manet, flatten the right side, and the faces are less alive than the fabric. Still, it offers a woman's view of domestic life, and it was favourably noticed at the Salon."},"met:438009":{"d":"A young woman in a pink dress, named in the title as Albertie-Marguerite Carré, then in her mid-teens, is the subject of this horizontal oil of about 55 by 67 centimetres. The Met's keywords record a dress, a woman and a portrait; the dress gives the picture its title and presumably its principal colour. It is probably an informal likeness of a friend or acquaintance rather than a commissioned portrait, painted around 1870.","i":"The title records the sitter's maiden name and her later married name, Madame Ferdinand-Henri Himmes. Morisot, who was in her late twenties, had studied with Corot, was a friend of Manet and exhibited at the Salon; this picture comes from the years before the first Impressionist exhibition of 1874, in which she took part. Informal portraits of young women in domestic settings became her speciality, and women and girls remained her chief subject throughout her career.","c":"Even at this early date Morisot has a gift for catching a sitter's natural presence rather than flattering her, and the title's pink dress promises the delicate colour she would later perfect. Her handling is likely to be more restrained than in her later work, and the debt to Manet in tonal contrasts is clear before she developed the lighter, feathery touch of her later pictures. It is a fresh, unpretentious portrait, valuable as a stage in her development."},"nga:66426":{"d":"Seen from above, a flat grassy yard holds several laundry lines hung mostly with white sheets, in front of a rustic house and a distant town. At least three women in skirts hang the washing, and a person in trousers tends a patch of green near the lower left. A gray cart tips forward at the center, white cloth lies over a wooden fence, and a two-story ivory and slate-blue house stands behind emerald trees. Thick, visible strokes make many details hard to read, and smokestacks appear on the horizon.","i":"Morisot painted this in 1875, the year after she exhibited in the first Impressionist show and married Eugène Manet. Laundry was among the most common outdoor domestic tasks, and painting it brought a woman's world into the open-air subjects of the Impressionists, who also painted suburban fields and the smoke of factories on the horizon. The raised viewpoint and dissolving details show her interest in light and atmosphere over description.","c":"The paint is so thick and the figures so summary that the picture works as an arrangement of whites, greens and grays: sheets as bright accents against a calm field. That is both its achievement and its risk, since the cart and figures nearly disappear into the strokes. As a small, quick picture of ordinary work in strong light it is more radical than polite, and it shows Morisot among the boldest handlers of paint in her circle."},"nga:52193":{"d":"Seen from the waist up in profile, a pale young woman in an ice-blue dress looks slightly down with her lips parted, her body turned to our right. A floppy straw hat tied over her copper-blond hair with a plum ribbon ends in a bow nearly as wide as her shoulders. A few flecks of lapis blue suggest polka dots on the dress, and a blue belt circles her waist. The background is a loose scrub of aqua and bottle-green that suggests the outdoors, with touches of coral red in the corners.","i":"By the 1880s Morisot's handling had grown feathery and free, and her figures are often young women, models or members of her circle, caught in a moment of pause. She painted this in 1884, a decade after exhibiting in the first Impressionist show, working both indoors and in the garden. The hat and ribbon give a fashionable note, but the painting is less about costume than about light and color on a face.","c":"The ribbon bow almost steals the picture, and that is no accident: it is a bright, playful accent that balances the quiet profile. Morisot's late style shows here, with the dress, hat and background all treated as patches of color rather than described objects, and only the face given a gentle finish. The picture is slight in subject but secure in its touch, and typical of the lightness of her late work."},"nga:46660":{"d":"A young woman in a white apron over an ankle-length navy dress stands in a dining room, facing us with slightly raised eyebrows and a hint of a smile, her hands at her chest, perhaps holding a mixing bowl. A tall curio cabinet fills the left, with a clock and an oil lamp on the wall, and light pours through a tall window or French doors at the right. A round table holds a decanter and a bowl of peach and rose fruit, and a small white dog with tan spots scampers by a chair.","i":"A young woman in an apron probably belongs to the household's domestic staff, and Morisot gives her the attention of a portrait, with a glance and a smile, in a bourgeois dining room that is otherwise indistinct. The loose brushwork, typical of her work of the 1880s, makes the room dissolve into light, so that the interior's details are suggestions. Painting daily life, servants included, was one way the Impressionists brought the modern ordinary world into art.","c":"The interior is half-visible and the figure is the clearest thing in it, yet she stands in the room rather than before it: the cabinet, lamp, dog and fruit all share the same loose touch. The strongest feature is the light pouring in from the right, which softens everything, a domestic, indoor Impressionism. It is a livelier, more complete interior than most of Morisot's portraits and turns a household worker into a presence."},"met:437159":{"d":"A young woman sits on a chair, knitting, in a horizontal canvas of about 50 by 60 centimetres painted around 1883. The Met's keywords record a hat with a ribbon, grass, a chair and the knitted fabric, so she is probably seated out of doors in a garden, absorbed in her work. The picture is an informal, unposed scene rather than a portrait of an identified sitter.","i":"In the early 1880s Morisot, a founding member of the Impressionist group, was painting women and children in gardens and domestic spaces, subjects she knew intimately as a woman of the Parisian upper bourgeoisie. Knitting, a quiet, absorbing occupation, gave her an unposed attitude to paint, and it also belonged to the Impressionists' interest in modern, everyday life. She worked often in her own gardens and in country houses in the summers.","c":"A fine example of Morisot's later style: quick, feathery strokes that dissolve edges into light and air, and a lively sense of the moment. The young woman's absorbed attention is conveyed with great economy, and the lightness of touch suits the subject of a quiet, domestic task. The handling is so loose that the face is almost abstract and the composition seems casual, but that is the point: Morisot values effect over finish. It is typical, and typically delightful."},"nga:52194":{"d":"A lake ringed by lush green vegetation fills a tall canvas. A person in a golden yellow garment and a butter-yellow hat rows a boat near the center, and a smudge of cobalt may be a second figure aboard. Four white geese line the near bank, three standing in a row and a fourth bending to peck at the grass, with a bed of deep pink flowers beside them. Short and long parallel brushstrokes leave many details hard to make out, and the high horizon is topped by a sky of watery blue and pale yellow.","i":"Morisot painted this about 1889, in the late style in which she worked outdoors in gardens and by the Seine and let the brush describe light rather than objects. Women and children in boats were a favorite theme of the Impressionists, who found in leisure on the water a modern subject, and the girl rowing links this picture to that tradition. The geese give it a country, domestic note.","c":"Nearly everything here is a stroke rather than a described object, and the four white geese are the only crisp notes in a field of mints, sages and blues. The parallel strokes read as ripples and the flicker of leaves, which is exactly the point. The risk is that the girl and the boat nearly dissolve into pattern, and some viewers will want more to hold on to. As a late Morisot it is among her freest."},"nga:46525":{"d":"A young girl in a coral-peach dress sits facing us, shown from the knees up, with her hands resting in her lap. She has a round face, lightly flushed cheeks, brown eyes under arched brows and closed rose-pink lips, with her hair pulled back in strokes of caramel brown and moss green. She wears a ring on her right middle finger. Behind her to our left, in a wooden cage with a mint-green bow, is an emerald-green parakeet. Electric and cobalt blue surround her, with bare canvas showing at some edges.","i":"The girl is Julie Manet, Morisot's daughter, then about eleven years old; her father, Eugène Manet, the brother of the painter Édouard Manet, would die in 1892, and Morisot herself in 1895. Julie was a frequent model and later wrote a well-known diary of her youth among the Impressionists. A child with a pet bird has a long history in portraiture, and the caged parakeet may hint gently at a young girl's sheltered life.","c":"The blue field behind the girl makes the coral dress glow, and the loose strokes keep the formality of the pose, hands in lap and direct gaze, from stiffening. The picture respects its sitter's seriousness: Julie looks back with composure rather than coyness, which is the opposite of sentimentality. The parakeet adds a bright note and a hint of narrative. It is one of Morisot's most affecting portraits of her daughter."},"nga:131028":{"d":"A young girl sits with her head and body slightly turned to the left, her hands tucked into the pockets of a white apron over a dark blue dress. She has fair skin, light reddish-brown hair tied back with a blue ribbon, a pointed chin, pale eyebrows and small red lips in a neutral expression. Behind her are a light pink and cream wall, a blue-green floor, a small brown table with a vase of pink and white flowers and a darker area that suggests a door. Thick, visible strokes blur the room.","i":"In the later nineteenth century, portraits of children moved away from formal little-adult poses toward informal childhood: an apron with pockets, hands at ease, a girl at home. Morisot painted many such pictures in the 1880s and early 1890s, often of girls and young women in domestic rooms, and her loose touch suited the unforced mood. The sitter's name is not given, so the picture presents childhood as a type as well as a person.","c":"The strongest contrast is the dark blue dress and white apron against a room reduced to soft notes of pink, cream and blue-green. The neutral expression and the hands hidden in the pockets give the girl an unforced, slightly guarded air that is more convincing than a smile would be. The picture is quiet and modest, with little of the daring of Morisot's best work, but it shows her refusal of sentimentality."},"cma:152263":{"d":"A portrait of an unknown man whose most detailed feature is a rapier, the sword worn with civilian dress and used in duels. Its hilt, with an elegantly spiraling guard, is shown with close attention. The canvas, about 98 by 72 centimeters, was painted about 1620 by Tanzio da Varallo, who was born in Piedmont and worked mainly in northern Italy, briefly in Rome and Naples.","i":"In seventeenth-century Italy a sword marked a gentleman, and the rapier's hilt was also an object of fashion and wealth. On style, this hilt belongs to a type made in northern Italy between about 1570 and 1600, decades before the portrait, so it may have been an heirloom. Tanzio's trips to Rome and Naples brought him under the influence of Caravaggio, whose dramatic realism he combined with his own elegant, decorative style.","c":"The rapier is the star: its spiraling guard is rendered with a goldsmith's care, and the picture suggests a painter who delighted in the objects that declared a man's status. Tanzio is better known for violent religious scenes, so a portrait is a change of key, with his Caravaggesque solidity tempered by decorative elegance. The sitter's anonymity keeps it from being a character study, but as an image of a gentleman defined by his sword it is vivid."},"nga:332":{"d":"Bright light from the upper left washes over three figures clustered against a black ground: the muscular Saint Sebastian, an angel and a young woman. Sebastian sits facing us with his head thrown back, eyes rolled up and mouth open, a hand near the wound in his chest and two arrows in his shin. The angel in a sapphire-blue toga delicately draws an arrow from the saint's chest and holds up a bloody cloth, while the woman, in plum and scarlet, cradles his body and wraps a gray cloth around his back.","i":"After Sebastian was shot with arrows for his Christian faith he was left for dead, but the widow Irene found him alive and nursed him back to health. Saint Sebastian tended by Saint Irene was a favorite subject of the Baroque, because it joined a nude, suffering body to an act of care. Here an angel assists, a variation that adds a heavenly note to the scene of rescue. The black ground comes from Caravaggio's example.","c":"Tanzio makes the saint's agony almost operatic: the thrown-back head and rolling eyes, the glow of flesh against black, the arrow drawn out like a thorn. The risk is melodrama, since everything is pitched at the same intensity. What redeems it is the design: the angel's delicacy and the woman's embrace lock the three figures into a tight, believable pyramid of tenderness around the wound. It is one of Tanzio's most arresting images."},"nga:205411":{"d":"A winter landscape shows snow-covered ground beneath tall, dark trees with straight trunks. The colour is almost entirely white, grey and black, with a few dark brown leaves clinging to the trees. Small grey shrubs and tufts of grass poke through the snow, the dense trees cast grey shadows, and the sky beyond the trunks is white. The painting is small, about 31 by 41 centimetres.","i":"Eaton studied at the National Academy of Design and worked in New Jersey, becoming known as a Tonalist painter, one who reduced landscape to hushed harmonies of a few colours. A winter wood in nearly monochrome is a natural Tonalist subject, because snow and bare trunks make a limited palette true to nature. The date, 1886, falls early in his career, before the moonlit pine groves for which he is best known.","c":"A quiet exercise in restraint: with a palette reduced to white, grey and black, Eaton makes the vertical trunks and the soft, grey shadows on the snow carry the picture, and the white sky gives it a cold, hushed light. The monotony is the risk, since there is little incident and the dark trees dominate. It lacks the poetry of the best Tonalists, but it is an assured, sincere early work."},"met:437490":{"d":"Gabriel and Mary face each other in a richly furnished bedroom. At the left the angel kneels on a tiled floor in a crimson and gold brocade cope with a green stole, his wings edged with peacock-eye feathers, a staff with a pennant in his hand and the other hand raised to his chest in greeting. At the right the Virgin, in a deep blue gown over a red bodice, her long hair loose, kneels at a small lectern with an open book and turns her palm toward him; a vase with white lilies stands beside her. Behind are a canopied red bed, a window seat with red cushions, and a window showing a walled town and a garden. Oil on wood, about 186 by 115 centimetres.","i":"The Met calls this one of the largest surviving depictions of the Annunciation and says it was most likely commissioned by Ferry de Clugny, whose coat of arms, two joined keys, decorates the carpet and the stained glass. Ferry founded his burial chapel in 1465 at Autun cathedral. The composition derives from a design by Rogier van der Weyden, in whose Brussels workshop Memling is believed to have worked as a journeyman before establishing himself in Bruges in 1465. The setting is Mary's bedroom, where Memling combines very grand elements with humble details, such as a carefully rendered pillow on the window bench.","c":"The pleasure of this large panel is its richness without clutter. The gold-and-crimson brocade, the glowing blue of the Virgin's gown and the tiled floor are rendered with a jeweller's precision, and the view through the window makes the room feel inhabited. Memling's gentle, composed manner, however, can shade into stiffness: the faces are mild and similar, and neither figure shows much emotion. He borrows his composition from Rogier van der Weyden and softens it. Beside Rogier's tension this is serene, a little bland, and beautiful."},"met:437056":{"d":"Two small portraits hang side by side as a pair, each against a black ground. On the left, a man with brown hair cut straight across the brow, dressed in black, looks to the right with his hands pressed together in prayer. On the right, a young woman turns toward him, her hands raised in the same gesture. She wears a tall black headdress from which a thin transparent veil falls behind, a dark red gown with a broad white fur collar, and a wide necklace of gold and jewels with a pendant. Their faces are smooth and finely modelled in pale flesh tones, and their eyes are fixed on something beyond the frame.","i":"The sitters are Tommaso Portinari (1428–1501) and Maria Baroncelli (born 1456), who married about 1470. Tommaso managed the Medici bank's branch in Bruges and later commissioned Hugo van der Goes's famous altarpiece, now in the Uffizi, for a Florentine hospital church. Their raised hands suggest that the panels once flanked a devotional image, perhaps a Madonna, which is now lost. Maria's tall headdress and jewels advertise the wealth of the Italian merchant community in Bruges. The Met dates the pair to about 1470 and attributes them to Memling, who had settled in Bruges a few years earlier.","c":"These are cool, exact portraits by a painter who looked hard and judged little. Tommaso's lean, watchful face is the more convincing of the two; Maria's, with its plucked hairline and serene calm, is closer to a type, and both pairs of praying hands are stiff. The jewelled collar and the fine veil are beautifully done, and the black grounds give the heads startling clarity. They show why Netherlandish portraiture appealed to Italians, who admired its detail, though they lack the psychological bite of Rogier van der Weyden. Careful, charming and a little guarded."},"nga:41659":{"d":"A woman sits close to us on a grassy field before a deep landscape, holding up a white cloth printed with the face of a man with chin-length blond hair and a beard. She wears a white turban-like headdress with a veil, a rose-pink dress and a voluminous lapis-blue robe that pools on the ground in angular folds, and her downcast eyes and parted lips give her a soft, absorbed look. Behind her, a dirt path and harvest-yellow hills lead to a town on the horizon. This is the obverse of a small panel, about 30 by 23 centimeters.","i":"Veronica, according to legend, wiped Christ's face with her veil on the road to Calvary, and the cloth kept the imprint of his features, the Holy Face. Holding up the veil, as she does here, became a standard way to show the relic. Memling painted this about 1470 to 1475 as one side of a double-sided panel, whose reverse bears the chalice of Saint John the Evangelist. It was made for private devotion.","c":"Memling's genius for serenity suits the subject: Veronica's lowered eyes and soft stillness turn a legend about suffering into a quiet act of witness. The blue robe, the pale cloth and the harvest-colored distance are harmonized with a delicacy that few contemporaries matched. The cloth's face is smaller and plainer than the woman, which is right, since the image is a relic to be contemplated. A small, finished, deeply calm picture."},"nga:41660":{"d":"A gold chalice holding a thin green serpent sits in an arched niche of shallow gray stone, lit from our right so that it casts a shadow. The chalice has a flaring, six-lobed foot and a knob on the stem set with red and green diamond-shaped stones; the serpent drapes inside the bowl, mouth open and tongue flicking out. This is the reverse of the panel that shows Saint Veronica on its front, about 30 by 23 centimeters, painted in oil about 1470 to 1475.","i":"The chalice with a serpent is the attribute of Saint John the Evangelist. According to the legend, a pagan priest challenged John to drink poison to prove his God; John made the sign of the cross and drank it unharmed, and the poison left the cup in the form of a serpent. The painted niche imitates stone, a convention of Netherlandish painting for the outsides of panels, which were meant to look like sculpture when the panel was closed.","c":"The conceit is neat: a painter shows off on the back of a panel, where almost no one will look, by making a gold chalice, a stone niche and a cast shadow look solid. The picture is a small exercise in illusion, and the faceted jewels and the lobed foot are rendered with a goldsmith's attention. It is the quieter half of its panel, an emblem rather than a scene, but the craft is considerable."},"nga:49":{"d":"A small portrait of a man holding an arrow, painted in oil on a panel about 31 by 25 centimeters around 1470 to 1475 by Hans Memling, who had settled in Bruges by the mid-1460s. The sitter is unidentified, and the arrow is the one object that gives the picture a narrative: an attribute that must have meant something to him and to those who saw the portrait.","i":"An arrow could point to several meanings: it is the attribute of Saint Sebastian, the patron of archers and of guilds that bore his name in Flemish cities, and the saint was invoked against plague. The sitter may have belonged to an archers' guild, or been named Sebastian, or sought the saint's protection. Which it was is uncertain. Memling's clients included Bruges merchants, officials and foreign residents, and small portraits like this were typical commissions.","c":"Memling's strength is tact, not penetration. His sitters are calm, finely drawn and a little remote, and the best of his portraits give them an air of dignified privacy. Compared with Jan van Eyck's probing realism or Rogier van der Weyden's sharp elegance, he is gentler, and here the arrow is a welcome touch of specificity in a format that can seem generic. A fine small likeness, attractive rather than searching."},"nga:48":{"d":"The Virgin Mary with the Christ Child and angels, painted in oil on a panel of about 58 by 46 centimeters after 1479 by Hans Memling in Bruges. It is a devotional image of the kind Memling's workshop produced often for the city's merchants and visitors, small enough to be hung in a private room and intended to prompt prayer rather than to tell a story.","i":"Angels attend Mary as Queen of Heaven and often make music or carry crowns to proclaim her rank. In Bruges in the later fifteenth century, such Madonnas were bought by wealthy citizens and foreign merchants who wanted a refined, comforting image of the Virgin for their houses. The date after 1479 points to Memling's mature period, when he was the leading painter of the city.","c":"Memling's Madonnas are sweet-tempered, polished and calm, and the critic respects the craft while noting the formula: gently oval faces, tidy folds, harmonized color. For some viewers the serenity is the point, and for others the predictability. Among Netherlandish painters he is less probing than Rogier van der Weyden and less inventive than Hugo van der Goes, but he offers a harmony of finish and feeling that his clients clearly prized."},"met:437055":{"d":"A portrait of an old man, painted in oil on a small wood panel about 26 by 19 centimeters by Hans Memling around 1475. The title states the point: the picture is a study of age, and its success depends on how honestly the painter records the face of a man far past youth. The sitter is unnamed, and the panel is small and intimate, made to be looked at closely.","i":"Most fifteenth-century Netherlandish portraits show sitters in the prime of life, which makes a portrait of an old man a little unusual. Age could signal wisdom, status earned over time, or the closeness of death, and the sitter may have wanted a likeness to be kept by his family. Memling, working in Bruges for a clientele of merchants and officials, was well placed to supply it.","c":"Portraits of the old test a painter's willingness to record without flattery, and Memling's gentleness is both an asset and a limit. He is more tender than harsh, so the wrinkles are dignified rather than merciless. That makes the face approachable, though it stops short of the unsparing candor of Van Eyck. As a small likeness from a master in his mid-career, it is quietly impressive, a picture to look at slowly."},"met:437054":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned in a red gown with the naked Christ Child on her lap, a gold-brocaded cloth of honour behind her and a red canopy above. At the left, kneeling in a crown and a gown of black-and-gold brocade with red sleeves, Saint Catherine reaches toward the Child, with a broken wheel and a sword on the grass at her side. At the right, Saint Barbara, in green with a dark mantle, reads an open book beside her small domed tower. Two angels in blue and red robes with coloured wings play stringed instruments on either side of the Virgin. A young man in black stands at the far left edge with his hands clasped. Behind, green lawns, slender trees and a castle with a gate.","i":"Saint Catherine, identified by the wheel and sword, and Saint Barbara, by her tower, were among the most popular female saints of the later Middle Ages, and the Virgin with the two of them in a garden is a familiar Netherlandish arrangement. Memling, who was born near Frankfurt and was active in Bruges from the 1460s until his death in 1494, painted many such pictures for the city's merchants and clergy. The Met dates this panel to the early 1480s; it was bequeathed by Benjamin Altman in 1913. The young man in black, who has no halo, is presumably the donor, though his identity is unknown.","c":"Memling's gift is for stillness, and this panel offers little drama; its pleasure lies in precision. The red gown spreads like a pyramid at the centre and steadies the composition, with the green and black of the saints balancing it. The faces share one gentle, rounded type, so the saints are distinguishable chiefly by their attributes, and the Child is stiff and slightly awkward. The landscape is delicately handled. It lacks the emotional bite of Rogier van der Weyden, under whom Memling probably trained, but as devotional order and finish it is hard to fault."},"met:437057":{"d":"Mary nurses the Christ Child in this devotional picture of the Nursing Madonna, painted in oil on wood by Hans Memling in the early 1480s. The subject is intimate, a mother feeding her baby, and Memling gives it the refined, orderly finish that made his Madonnas popular in Bruges. Pictures of the type were usually modest panels made for private rooms, where they served as a focus for prayer.","i":"The nursing Virgin, Maria lactans, stresses the real humanity of Christ and Mary's tenderness, and it was an image of comfort as well as doctrine: she is the nourisher who intercedes for believers. Netherlandish painters made the type less earthy than earlier versions, setting it in refined interiors. Pictures like this were bought for private chambers, where a viewer could pray before the image.","c":"The motif is bold and Memling treats it with decorum, so the picture is more serene than physical. The Virgin's composed modesty, the careful finish and the soft light turn a very human subject into an image of quiet grace. The result is graceful without being deep: Memling's art rarely lingers on the raw and often turns tenderness into elegance. A polished example of his Madonnas, attractive and a little cool."},"met:437059":{"d":"A young woman holds a pink, a small garden flower, in a narrow upright panel about 43 by 19 centimeters, painted in oil on wood about 1485 to 1490 and attributed to Hans Memling. The Met's keywords also list a horse and a monkey, and the genre is recorded as allegory, which suggests that the panel carries a second, allegorical image alongside the portrait.","i":"Pinks, or carnations, were flowers of love and betrothal in Netherlandish portraits, so the picture may mark an engagement; a narrow panel like this could be one wing of a pair, with the future husband on the other. Monkeys often stand for folly or vice in allegory, and a horse for passion or pride, so the second image may moralize on love, but how they relate to the portrait is uncertain.","c":"The word attributed matters: this is a Memling-type picture whose authorship is not secure, and the odd narrow format and the unusual mix of portrait and allegory make it hard to judge against his sturdier work. What succeeds is the pale, upright poise of the sitter and the small, charged motif of the flower. What is weaker is the sense of a formula, with a charming idea more than a fully realized one."},"met:437177":{"d":"A large double portrait of a count and his wife, about 229 by 191 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas, with a dog included among the figures. The sitters are Count Giacomo Durazzo (1717 to 1794) and Ernestine Aloisia Ungnad von Weissenwolff (1732 to 1794), and the painter is Martin van Meytens the Younger, probably in the early 1760s. The size alone, well over two meters high, marks a commission for a family of rank.","i":"Durazzo, a Genoese nobleman, was director of the imperial theaters in Vienna from 1754 to 1764, the patron under whom Gluck's reform opera Orfeo ed Euridice was first performed in 1762, and later served as ambassador to Venice. Meytens, Swedish by birth, was the court painter of Empress Maria Theresa, and a dog in a couple's portrait is a traditional sign of fidelity. A grand double portrait declared the pair's rank at the Habsburg court.","c":"Meytens built his reputation on polished, efficiently produced portraits for the Habsburg court, and a picture of this size shows that skill: an assured composition, the ease of a society that knows its place, a couple presented as a unit. The faces of his courtiers tend to resemble one another, and the scale outweighs psychology. As a document of rank and taste in Rococo Vienna it is excellent; as a study of character it is polite rather than penetrating."},"nga:102631":{"d":"Clouds surge across a steel-blue sky over a gently rippling estuary, with the horizon a quarter of the way up the panel. A rowboat piled with barrels and six men sits at the left, and on a sandbar to the right a moored sailboat leans sharply away as three men tend its hull, while smoke rises from a fire under a long-legged pot. A pier of weathered logs juts in at the right, a pole topped with a barrel reaches high, white smoke curls around a three-masted ship, and nine sails dot the horizon.","i":"Simon de Vlieger was one of the founders of the tonal phase of Dutch marine painting, which replaced the earlier, detailed ship portraits with atmosphere: low horizons, big skies and a restrained range of grays and silvers. The men tarring or caulking a hull are everyday work, not drama. Vlieger's later pupils are said to have included Willem van de Velde the Younger, who developed the tradition.","c":"The sky is the achievement: surging clouds, a shaft of low sunlight and silver highlights on slate water give the picture an air of weather and the end of a working day. The small incidents of work, the smoking pot, the leaning boat, the barrels, enliven the scene without breaking its calm. Some of them crowd the edges, but the whole is convincing. A small, quiet seascape that shows why Vlieger mattered to later marine painters."},"cma:148363":{"d":"Peasants sleep near their fields after sowing, while a satyr, the enemy of the parable, slips in among them to scatter bad seed beside the good. The canvas, about 90 by 130 centimeters, illustrates the Parable of the Weeds from Matthew 13:24 to 30 in a landscape setting, where the story's moment of neglect is shown as a quiet scene of rest and a small act of sabotage.","i":"In the parable the farmer's enemy sows weeds among the wheat while his workers sleep, and the farmer says to let both grow until harvest, when the weeds will be burned. Its lesson is that good and evil mix in the world, but in the end the pious will prevail. Dutch Protestants did not generally commission religious pictures, but when they did the subjects were often scriptural texts with a clear moral, like this one. The satyr is a painter's way of showing the unseen enemy.","c":"Vlieger, known chiefly as a painter of seas and skies, brings his landscape skills to a Gospel text, and the result is a lightly comic staging of a serious lesson: a pagan satyr creeping among sleepers. The moral is clear and the Dutch Protestant taste for scripture in everyday settings is well served. The picture is more ingenious than moving, and the satyr is a witty, slightly odd choice of devil, but the idea carries it."},"met:437914":{"d":"A small panel of a calm sea, about 37 by 44 centimeters, painted in oil on wood by Simon de Vlieger after 1640, with a boat or boats on still water as its subject. Calms were a favorite subject of Dutch marine painters: the surface is quiet, and the picture's drama lies in light and sky rather than in any storm or battle.","i":"In the 1600s the Dutch Republic's wealth rested on the sea, and pictures of calm water, with fishermen, ferries or merchantmen, sold well to collectors who understood ships. Vlieger, who worked in Rotterdam, Delft, Amsterdam and Weesp, was a leading figure in the tonal phase of Dutch marine painting, which looked for atmosphere in low horizons and high skies. A date after 1640 places the work in his mature period, before his death in 1653.","c":"A calm is the hardest weather to paint, because nothing happens, and the picture depends on atmosphere and on the exact relation of a few boats to a great stretch of sky. Vlieger was a master of that restraint, and his silvery tonal harmonies give a modest board an unusual breadth. It is a small, quiet work without a big idea, but stillness is the point, and it sets the stage for the next generation of marine painters."},"met:437015":{"d":"Mary holds the Christ Child in a panel of tempera on wood, about 75 by 46 centimeters, painted in Italy about 1280 against a gold ground. The museum attributes it to the Master of the Magdalen, an anonymous painter usually described as Florentine. Pictures of this age follow the solemn, frontal manner inherited from Byzantine icons: the figures are stately and symmetrical, and the gold behind them stands for heaven rather than for air or space.","i":"In the later thirteenth century the Madonna and Child was the most important subject in Italian church painting, and Florence and Siena were developing their own versions of Byzantine models. The anonymous painter takes his name from a panel of Mary Magdalen, and he worked in the generation of Cimabue, just before Giotto changed painting. Viewers saw in the image the Mother of God and an intercessor, and the costly gold was also a sign of devotion and wealth.","c":"The achievement of a picture like this is not realism but presence: a gold-ringed image of mother and child meant to be seen by candlelight. Painters of his generation were beginning to soften the Byzantine severity, but a modern eye will find the drawing flat and the faces generic. Its importance is as a link in the chain that leads from the icon to the Florentine Renaissance, and it should be appreciated as such."},"met:437016":{"d":"A very narrow panel, about 38 by 14 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with a gold ground in the late thirteenth century. The title names the Madonna and Child enthroned, but the Met's keywords also list the Last Supper, the Flagellation, Christ bearing the cross, the Crucifixion and several saints, so the little panel probably held a small sequence of images in tiers, as portable devotional pieces did.","i":"Small, narrow panels like this served private devotion: they could be carried, set up on a table or fixed in a frame. The enthroned Madonna presents the Child as king of heaven, while the Passion scenes lead the worshiper through Christ's suffering in order, from the Last Supper to the Flagellation, the Carrying of the Cross and the Crucifixion. The painter, the Master of the Magdalen, is an anonymous Italian of the later thirteenth century.","c":"Compressing a whole sequence into fourteen centimeters of width is a feat of economy, and it asks for a clear, conventional vocabulary, which the gold-ground style supplies. The images are necessarily small and schematic, so the pleasure is in pattern and the glint of gold more than in individual scenes. It is best valued as a surviving example of a type of object, a small prayer aid, that the thirteenth century used widely."},"met:436732":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child, with angels beside her, in a large tempera panel with a gold ground, about 154 by 91 centimeters, painted in the late thirteenth century by the Master of the Magdalen. At more than a meter and a half high, it is large enough to have served as an altarpiece or as the central image of a chapel, and the throne and angels place Mary in heaven as its queen.","i":"Enthroned Madonnas of this kind were the grand public image of the Virgin in thirteenth-century Italy, painted for churches and confraternities as a focus for prayer and ceremony, with angels attending the throne as courtiers of heaven. The Master of the Magdalen, usually considered Florentine, worked when painters like Cimabue were making such Madonnas larger and more imposing, a tradition that leads to the monumental Maestas of Duccio and Giotto.","c":"Scale is the main thing: a monumental figure on a gold ground, meant to hold a church. The formula is stately and rigid, with little of the human warmth that would soon come with Giotto, and beside Cimabue's great Madonnas the drawing is likely to seem plain. What this panel offers is solemnity and a clear devotional message. It is a historic rather than a moving image, and its value is in showing where Florentine painting began."},"met:436346":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 47 by 56 centimeters, that celebrates a political and religious event in allegorical terms. An angel, a cross and groups of men and women supply the usual cast of an allegory of faith. It dates from about 1681, the year Louis XIV took Strasbourg, and is by the French painter Claude Guy Hallé. At this size it may be a sketch for a larger work, but that is a guess.","i":"In October 1681, after French troops occupied Strasbourg, the city's cathedral, Protestant for a century and a half, was returned to Catholic worship. Louis XIV's court presented the event as the triumph of the true faith and of the king who enforced it, and angels and the cross were the usual signs of divine approval. A viewer of 1681 would have read the picture as royal propaganda in sacred dress, not as a record of what happened.","c":"Allegories of this sort depend on a shared vocabulary, and the painter's task was to make it fluent, not personal. Hallé was a capable French religious painter of the generation after Le Brun, and the picture is valued less for invention than as a record of how royal policy was dressed in sacred imagery. If it is a sketch, as its size suggests, it may be livelier than a finished version would have been."},"met:437336":{"d":"The Virgin and Child are shown with Saint Francis of Assisi, Saint Dominic and angels, on a very tall canvas, about 257 by 143 centimeters, painted in oil about 1612 by Giulio Cesare Procaccini. The scale and the vertical format point to an altarpiece, in which the founders of the two great preaching orders are gathered around the Madonna, a visible company of intercessors for the people who prayed before it.","i":"Francis, who founded the Franciscans, and Dominic, who founded the Dominicans, led the two great mendicant orders, and legend says that they met and embraced, a sign of the orders' unity. Pairing them with the Virgin and Child placed an altar under both orders' protection, a theme typical of Counter-Reformation piety in Lombardy, where Procaccini worked in Milan. The angels are attendants from the heavenly court.","c":"Procaccini is the most sensuous of the Lombard Baroque painters, and an altarpiece of this size suits his softness: sweet faces, shimmering drapery, a Correggio-like warmth. The risk is sweetness without structure, and a tall canvas gives him a long vertical to organize, which he does with a flowing, rather decorative rhythm. It is less severe than Cerano's work and less dramatic than Morazzone's, and its charm is its strength."},"nga:121887":{"d":"A kneeling woman, her face tipped up and mouth open, her copper-red curls falling down her back, is held by two nude winged child-like angels while she holds both hands palm up. An ochre-yellow robe has slipped from her shoulders. Across a diagonal of charcoal-gray cloud, three angels look down, one playing a violin and another gesturing with two raised fingers. Dramatic light from the front leaves the setting in deep shadow. The canvas is about 216 by 146 centimeters.","i":"The woman is Mary Magdalen in ecstasy. After her conversion, the Golden Legend says, she withdrew to a wilderness, and each day angels lifted her up to hear the heavenly choirs, which explains the violin. For Counter-Reformation viewers she was the model penitent, and her rapture was shown as a physical experience, with open mouth and upturned eyes. The sensuous treatment of her body and the soft drapery stir devotion through feeling.","c":"Procaccini gives the Magdalen's ecstasy a frankness that is both the picture's point and its problem: the open mouth, bare shoulders and clutching angels bring devotion very close to sensuality, and for some viewers of his day and ours the balance tips. Its strengths are the cloud diagonal that divides earth from heaven and the soft, Correggio-derived modeling. It is less severe than Caravaggio's Magdalen, and more openly musical."},"met:436286":{"d":"A portrait of a man on a linden-wood panel about 53 by 36 centimeters, dated 1553. As the title records, his signet ring bears the head of a Moor, a motif common in German heraldry. The medium is oil with gold and white metal, which suggests that parts of the picture, perhaps the ring and other ornament, were given a real metallic glint. The painter is Conrad Faber von Creuznach, who worked in Frankfurt.","i":"A signet ring bearing an emblem was both a seal for letters and documents and a sign of rank. The Moor's head appears in the arms of many German families and cities, so it may point to the sitter's family or office, though his name is unknown. Faber's sitters were largely drawn from the prosperous burghers of Frankfurt, and portraits of this kind fixed a sitter's status for family and posterity.","c":"The ring does the talking, and the precious metal shows a painter proud of his craft. Faber's strength was a crisp, objective record of face, dress and emblem in the German tradition rather than psychological depth. A sober, competent document of status, it is more interesting for what it records of identity and rank than for expressive force, and it stands for the careful provincial portraiture of mid-sixteenth-century Germany."},"met:441183":{"d":"A double portrait of a husband and wife, made in pastel with black chalk and watercolor on four joined sheets of blue laid paper mounted on canvas, a large format of about 100 by 80 centimeters. The sitters are François de Jullienne (1722–1754) and Marie Elisabeth de Séré de Rieux (1724–1795), who in 1743 would have been about twenty-one and nineteen. The museum record does not describe their poses or costumes, so only the medium and the type of picture can be stated.","i":"The Julliennes were wealthy Parisian dyers and collectors; Jean de Jullienne was the friend of Watteau and publisher of the engraved record of his works, and this François was probably his son. A double portrait of the heir and his wife in the large, luminous medium of pastel was a statement of a family's standing and taste. Charles-Antoine Coypel became First Painter to the King in 1747.","c":"Pastel was the fashionable medium for portraits in 1740s Paris, prized for soft skin tones and a velvet finish, and a double portrait on four joined sheets was an ambitious undertaking. Coypel was better known for theatrical narrative pictures and tapestry designs than for portraiture; the likely strength here is a polished, flattering surface and the likely weakness a certain formal stiffness."},"met:436486":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, about 16 by 41 centimeters, in tempera and gold, showing the burial of Saint Zenobius, a funeral attended by men and women. Panels of this shape ran along the base of an altarpiece, telling a saint's story in small scenes. It is one of three small panels by Davide Ghirlandaio dated about 1479, apparently from the predella of an altarpiece, the others showing the Marriage of the Virgin and Tobias and the Angel.","i":"Zenobius, a fifth-century bishop of Florence, is one of the city's patron saints, and the stories of his life were told in the predellas and panels of Florentine altarpieces. According to tradition, when his body was carried to burial, the bier touched a withered elm, which sprang into leaf, a miracle recalled in Florentine art. The subject, together with the other two panels, suggests an altarpiece made for a Florentine church or chapel, possibly connected with the saint.","c":"Davide Ghirlandaio worked in the shadow of his elder brother Domenico, whose workshop he helped to run, and his small narrative panels share that workshop's clarity, bright color and cheerful Florentine detail. A predella panel of this kind lives by lively figures and a clear story. It would be a modest, attractive piece of Florentine storytelling."},"met:436487":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, about 16 by 41 centimeters, in tempera and gold, showing the Marriage of the Virgin, with Mary and Joseph as the principal figures. Predella panels like this told the story of a saint or of the Virgin in small, easily read scenes. It is one of three small panels by Davide Ghirlandaio dated about 1479, apparently from a predella, the others showing the burial of Saint Zenobius and Tobias and the Angel.","i":"According to the Golden Legend and earlier tradition, Joseph was chosen as Mary's husband when his rod flowered among those of other suitors; the high priest then joined their hands and Joseph placed a ring on Mary's finger. The Marriage of the Virgin was a standard scene in Florentine cycles of the life of Mary, valued for its pageantry of costume and for its testimony to Mary's virginal marriage. Which moment is shown is not certain.","c":"A scene made for storytelling more than for display: a wedding allows a painter to arrange a crowd of figures, gestures and fashionable costumes, and the Ghirlandaio workshop was good at such arrangements. The panel belongs to a trio that shows the cheerful, narrative side of Florentine painting of the late 1470s."},"met:436488":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, about 16 by 41 centimeters, in tempera and gold, showing Tobias and the Angel. The archangel Raphael and Tobias appear with a dog and a fish, the usual companions of the story. It is one of three small panels by Davide Ghirlandaio dated about 1479, apparently from a predella, the others showing the burial of Saint Zenobius and the Marriage of the Virgin.","i":"In the Book of Tobit, the archangel Raphael, in disguise, accompanies young Tobias on a journey, protects him, and teaches him to use the gall, heart and liver of a fish caught in the Tigris to cure his father's blindness and drive away a demon. The subject was beloved in Florence, where the archangel was invoked as the protector of travelers and of young people, and where confraternities of youths were dedicated to him.","c":"A charming subject for a small panel: the youth with his fish and dog, led by a tall, gentle angel, makes a ready image of trust and protection. The Ghirlandaio workshop gave such scenes a clear, bright, brisk touch. This was one of the most popular compositions of Florentine devotion, and the tradition is evident."},"met:436491":{"d":"A portrait of a young woman, Selvaggia Sassetti, born in 1470 as the title records, in tempera on a wood panel about 57 by 44 centimeters, painted about 1487 to 1488 by Davide Ghirlandaio. She would have been about seventeen or eighteen. Davide was the younger brother and close collaborator of the more famous Domenico Ghirlandaio, and the picture comes from the workshop they shared.","i":"Florentine portraits of young women were often painted to mark a betrothal or marriage, showing the sitter's beauty, jewelry and dress as signs of her family's wealth and honor. A family of that name, bankers to the Medici, were patrons of the Ghirlandaio workshop, whose frescoes in Santa Trinita they commissioned; whether Selvaggia belonged to that branch is not known.","c":"Florentine portraits of young women of the 1480s, with Domenico Ghirlandaio's own as the model, combine a clean profile or three-quarter view with the precise display of jewelry and costume, and a notable calm. Davide's version was probably a competent, crisp product of the shop, if less subtle than his brother's best. It belongs to one of the most attractive types of quattrocento painting."},"nga:177787":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 52 by 103 centimeters, painted in 1860 or 1861 by Adolphe Monticelli, shows a hunting party's return, as the title and the museum's single keyword, hunting, indicate. It is best described by type: a costume-and-landscape scene of the kind Monticelli painted in his early Paris years, in the tradition of Watteau and of Narcisse Diaz, in rich, warm color.","i":"Monticelli, born in Marseille, spent the years from 1856 to 1870 in Paris, where he made small, glowing scenes of figures in parks and gardens that recall Watteau's fêtes galantes. The return from the hunt was a romantic theme, with its mix of horses, hounds, game and costume. Van Gogh later praised Monticelli's thick, jewel-like color, and the painter's reputation rests on that quality.","c":"Monticelli's pleasure is in paint itself, and even a modest hunting scene becomes an arrangement of warm color. As an early work, probably from before his impasto reached its extremes, it is more controlled than the pictures that Van Gogh and Cézanne admired. The risk of such costume pieces is prettiness, and the picture is better as an early step toward the glowing manner that made his name than as a major statement."},"nga:46659":{"d":"A woman is seated in three-quarter view, her right hand on a surface and her left near her chest, with a calm expression, fair complexion, dark eyebrows, light eyes and dark wavy hair falling past her shoulders. She wears a dark high-collared garment over a white blouse with a bow at the neck and a hoop earring. The background is a warm brown and yellow. The large canvas, about 132 by 98 centimeters, was painted in 1869.","i":"The sitter is identified only by her married name, and her place in Monticelli's circle is not recorded here. The painter is better known for his glowing fêtes galantes than for portraits, which makes a large, formal likeness less common in his output. A seated three-quarter portrait of this scale announced the respectability of its sitter, while the warm ground kept the painter's instinct for color.","c":"A calm, dignified portrait whose simplicity keeps the sitter's face and hands in command. The warm brown and yellow ground gives the dark dress a glow, a reminder that Monticelli was above all a colorist. Beside his fêtes it is a restrained performance, and its appeal is its composure rather than any display of paint."},"met:437148":{"d":"A bouquet stands in a blue vase, a still life painted on a vertical wood panel about 67 by 49 centimeters between 1879 and 1883. Monticelli's late flower pieces are typically laid on thickly, in dabs and ridges, and the blossoms are less botanical portraits than clusters of glowing color. It belongs to his last years in Marseille, when flowers and small figure scenes made up much of his output.","i":"Monticelli lived in Marseille after leaving Paris during the Franco-Prussian War of 1870, painting flowers both as a private pleasure and as a source of income. His thickly encrusted paint and jeweled color found few admirers at first. Vincent van Gogh admired his color and impasto and, in letters to his brother Theo, took him as a model of what a colorist could do, a painter who built whole pictures out of color.","c":"Monticelli turns flowers into pure paint: the blossoms are less drawn than built, in loaded dabs that catch the light, with a richness more musical than descriptive. The method can slip into muddle, and pictures of this kind vary in quality. At its best the picture rewards a close look at the surface and shows why Van Gogh admired him, though the weaker bouquets are mere decoration."},"met:437147":{"d":"A princess and her courtiers are the subject of this small wide panel, 38 by 59 centimeters. The title names a princess, and a woman and a man appear with her, so the picture is a group scene of elegant company, not a portrait. In the manner of Monticelli's fantasies on courtly life, the figures are built from thick, glowing paint rather than drawn in outline, and the whole is closer to a dream of a court than a record of one.","i":"Monticelli's courtly scenes revive the eighteenth-century fête galante of Watteau, in which costumed company strolls, flirts and listens to music in a garden. He filtered Watteau and the Romantic painter Narcisse Diaz through his own thick, jeweled paint. The subject is a fantasy of leisure and elegance with no single story, and the princess is probably a type rather than a person, an excuse for rich costume and color.","c":"These fantasies are Monticelli's best-known manner and the likeliest to charm: color glows and the paint is rich. Their weakness is sameness; the figures are less characterized than the surface, and the princess could be anyone. The idea pleases more than the drawing does. As a painter of light-struck pigment he stands far above the sentimental imitators of Watteau, and Van Gogh was not wrong to admire him."},"met:437149":{"d":"A small panel, about 25 by 20 centimeters, shows four figures, a woman among them. The size and the title suggest one of the small figure compositions that Monticelli produced in great numbers, in which a few elegantly dressed people are built from thick, glowing paint rather than drawn in outline. Neither the title nor the subject gives a story, so it is best read as a study in color and grouping.","i":"Monticelli's small figure panels were made quickly for patrons and dealers in Marseille, and many, like this one, have no anecdote beyond costumed figures in an idle moment. The tradition behind them is the fête galante of Watteau, filtered through Romantic painters such as Diaz and Delacroix. The lack of a clear subject is part of the appeal: the grouping becomes a pretext for color.","c":"At this size Monticelli's strengths are concentrated: the dabs of paint can glow, and a few figures are enough to build a composition. The risk is slightness; small panels can seem like variations on a formula, and not all deserve close study. This one is best valued as a sample of his late manner, whose reputation rests on larger and more resolved works."},"nga:206125":{"d":"A triptych with the Madonna and Child at the center and a saint on each wing, each panel topped by a pointed gable holding a smaller figure: the blessing Christ above the Virgin, Saint James Major above Saint Peter and Saint Anthony Abbot above a deacon saint. All the figures are shown from the hips up against gold, with gold halos and gilded moldings forming a pointed arch over each. Painted in tempera on panel about 1415 to 1420, it is roughly 1.4 meters wide counting the three panels alone.","i":"The arrangement is a hierarchy of heaven: Christ at the top center, the Virgin below him, the apostle and the deacon flanking her, the smaller saints crowning the gables. Altarpieces of this kind, made for chapels and side altars, offered worshipers intercessors in order of rank. Martino di Bartolomeo, a Sienese painter, was working in a tradition that valued gold, punched halos and clear, formal compositions. The gold was meant to shine by candlelight.","c":"Taken whole, the triptych shows its strengths: unity of color, a clear hierarchy, a tender central group and a rhythm of glances that ties the panels together. It also shows the limits of the late Sienese Gothic: little space, little weight, figures more patterned than modeled. As an object it is a fine survival, and seeing the three panels together restores the logic that the single panels cannot show."},"nga:37633":{"d":"A bearded man, shown from the hips up against a gold ground, holds two oversized gold keys and a book. He wears a sky-blue robe under a buttercup-yellow cloak lined with coral red, and glances off to our left, his gray hair cut in a ring around his head. A gold halo is punched into the background, and gilded molding makes a pointed arch above him. In the tip of the triangular panel a tiny bearded figure with a book and a staff looks down. The panel is about 93 by 45 centimeters.","i":"The keys identify Saint Peter, to whom Christ gave the keys of the kingdom of heaven. The small figure in the gable is identified as Saint James Major, who carries a staff and a book. The panel is the left wing of a triptych with the Madonna and Child in the center, painted by the Sienese artist Martino di Bartolomeo about 1415 to 1420. Altarpieces like this set the saints as protectors for the people who prayed before them.","c":"Martino di Bartolomeo works in the late Gothic Sienese manner, with gold, punched halos and pointed arches, and the pleasure of this panel is its surface: a blue robe, a yellow cloak lined in coral, keys that glitter against the gold. Peter's sidelong glance gives him a hint of watchfulness. The proportions and drawing are conventional, and the figure is more an emblem than a person, but the color is bright and the craftsmanship fine."},"nga:37634":{"d":"A woman holds a baby up so that they are cheek to cheek in front of a gold ground, both with pink-flushed cheeks. Her blond hair is mostly covered by a dark blue cloak edged in gold and lined in green, and she looks off to our left from the corners of her almond-shaped eyes. The baby wraps one arm around her neck and grips her neckline, wearing a pink robe tied with a white sash. In the tip of the panel a tiny blessing Christ holds an open book. The panel is about 106 by 51 centimeters.","i":"The cheek-to-cheek embrace belongs to a tender type of Madonna known in Byzantine art as the Virgin of Tenderness, which Sienese painters adopted and softened. The Child's adult-like face, a medieval convention, reminds the viewer that he is also the Word made flesh, and the blessing Christ above completes the idea: baby and adult are the same person. The gold ground, punched halos and gilded molding made the altarpiece glitter by candlelight.","c":"This is the heart of the triptych, and the best thing in it is the intimacy: mother and child pressed cheek to cheek in a gesture that feels natural against the formal gold. The Child's grown-up features are the old convention at its oddest. The gold is rubbed here and there to show the red clay layer beneath, a reminder of the panel's age and wear, which, if anything, adds to its glow."},"nga:37635":{"d":"A clean-shaven man with blond hair cut in a ring around his head stands against a gold ground, holding a palm frond in one hand and a book with a slate-blue cover in the other. His red robe is edged with gold and has gold patches on the sleeves, and a white ruffled collar stands high around his neck. In the tip of the panel a small balding man with a long gray beard, in purple and black, holds a red book and a crutch. The panel is about 93 by 44 centimeters.","i":"The palm marks the deacon as a martyr, but no further attribute names him, so his identity remains open: the usual deacon saints, Stephen, Lawrence and Vincent, each carry a distinct emblem, and the one that would settle it is missing. The figure above is Saint Anthony Abbot, the hermit shown with a crutch or T-shaped staff. The panel is the right wing of the triptych.","c":"The anonymity weakens the picture as a statement of devotion but not as design: the red robe with gold patches and the white collar make a crisp vertical against the gold. His body turns toward the center while his eyes look outward, mirroring the saint on the opposite wing, so the two wings frame the Virgin and address the worshiper, an effective if conventional device."},"met:436978":{"d":"A small panel, about 32 by 22 centimeters, of tempera on wood with a gold ground, showing Saint Anthony Abbot, with Saint John the Baptist as a companion figure. It was painted by the Sienese Martino di Bartolommeo di Biagio in the early fifteenth century. The title's parenthesis suggests the second saint appears in a separate smaller space, as the saints in the gables of altarpieces do.","i":"Anthony Abbot, an Egyptian hermit of the third and fourth centuries, is the model of the desert monk and was invoked against skin diseases and for the protection of animals. John the Baptist, the prophet of the wilderness, is another desert saint, so the two share the theme of solitude and penance. The pairing suggests part of an altarpiece, like the triptych by the same painter now in the National Gallery of Art.","c":"A small panel with a gold ground shows the Sienese trade at its most economical: a single saint, a punched halo, a few bright colors. The pairing of two wilderness saints adds a subtle thematic rhyme. As a fragment it has lost its context, and judged alone it is a well-made, conventional image; in company with its siblings it regains the logic of a larger altarpiece."},"met:436979":{"d":"Saint James the Greater, the apostle, appears on a small gold-ground panel, about 34 by 23 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood by Martino di Bartolommeo di Biagio; the Virgin of the Annunciation appears with him in a smaller space. The picture was made in Siena in the early fifteenth century and is one of several small panels by the artist now in the Metropolitan Museum.","i":"James, one of the twelve apostles, became the patron of pilgrims, especially those traveling to Santiago de Compostela, and is usually shown with a staff and a pilgrim's hat or scallop shell. The Virgin of the Annunciation, who receives the angel Gabriel's news, probably implies a companion panel with the angel; the two figures would have been separated across panels. Such pairings are standard in the gables of Sienese polyptychs.","c":"James is the sort of figure Sienese painters could produce in their sleep, and the pleasure lies in details: the shape of the halo, the gold, the quiet, upright dignity. The Virgin of the Annunciation tucked in above him adds a note of drama to a static image. Whether he reaches the standard of the best Sienese panel painting is hard to judge from a fragment, but it is competent and attractive."},"met:436980":{"d":"Saint Julian the Hospitaler appears with Saint Nicholas of Bari on a small gold-ground panel, about 32 by 22 centimeters, in tempera on wood by Martino di Bartolommeo di Biagio, a Sienese painter of the early fifteenth century. The two saints are named in the title, and like its sibling panels this one is probably a fragment of a larger altarpiece.","i":"Julian, according to legend, killed his parents by mistake and as penance built a hospice for travelers and the poor; he became the patron of ferrymen, innkeepers and hospitality. Nicholas of Bari, the fourth-century bishop, was invoked by sailors, travelers and children. Both are saints of charity toward travelers, which may hint at an altarpiece for a hospital or a charitable confraternity, though that is only a suggestion.","c":"A pairing of saints chosen for their kindness to strangers gives this small panel more thematic weight than its modest size suggests. As painting it is conventional Sienese gold-ground work, careful in craft and sparing in invention. Taken with its siblings, it hints at the rich ensemble of images that once stood in a church, and the loss of that whole is the real story."},"met:436981":{"d":"Saint Stephen, the first Christian martyr, appears on a small panel of tempera on wood with a gold ground, about 32 by 24 centimeters, with the Angel of the Annunciation in a smaller, separate space. He is shown with a book, as the museum's keywords record. It was painted by Martino di Bartolommeo di Biagio, and it belongs to the same group of small Sienese panels as the Metropolitan Museum's Saints Anthony Abbot, James the Greater and Julian.","i":"Stephen, a deacon in Jerusalem, was stoned to death for preaching Christ and is honored as the first martyr. The Angel of the Annunciation, Gabriel, probably forms an Annunciation with the Virgin who appears above Saint James the Greater in a sibling panel, so the two panels may once have stood on either side of a central image, with the scene spread across their gables. Dividing one scene among several panels was a standard device of Sienese polyptychs.","c":"The most interesting thing here is the construction: a saint below, an angel above, an Annunciation completed on another panel. Like a page torn from a book, the panel tells only half its story, and its pleasures are those of small things done well: a clear outline, a halo punched with care, bright color kept clean against gold. It is a minor but sound piece of Sienese craft that asks to be reunited with its companions."},"met:13196":{"d":"George Washington is the sitter of this modest oil portrait, about 65 by 54 centimeters, painted in 1795, in the second term of his presidency. The painter, Adolf Ulrich Wertmüller, was a Swede trained in Paris who had recently crossed to the United States. The canvas is bust-sized, a format for a likeness that could be copied or hung in a private room rather than a full-length state portrait.","i":"By 1795 Washington's image was already a national property, and portrait painters of the early republic, native and foreign, sought his likeness, which sold readily as copies. Wertmüller, who had left a Europe in revolution, belonged to the immigrant painters who brought European polish to the new nation. From 1795 Gilbert Stuart's portraits began to fix the standard image, and every other Washington is judged against them.","c":"Wertmüller offers the competence of a European-trained portraitist, but not the authority that Stuart gave the same famously guarded face. Judged as a record, it is workmanlike and honest; judged as an image of command, it is less memorable. Its chief interest is historical: a foreigner's view of a great man at the height of his fame, and a reminder of how many hands made Washington's face familiar."},"met:17564":{"d":"A woman is portrayed on a tiny sheet of tin, about 9 by 7 centimeters, roughly the size of a playing card. The unusual support, a smooth metal plate instead of canvas or panel, allows a very fine finish suited to an intimate likeness. The sitter is unnamed, the date is about 1805, and the painter was a Swedish emigrant living in the Delaware valley, working presumably for a private owner who wanted a likeness close at hand.","i":"In the early republic, small portraits were the affordable way for ordinary families to have a likeness: miniatures were kept in pockets, worn as keepsakes and exchanged at betrothals and bereavements. An unnamed lady is a common situation, since names were remembered in families and lost when the pictures were sold. Wertmüller's training in Paris gave him a polish that set him apart from many American portraitists.","c":"The tiny scale and metal support set the terms: nothing can be broad, and everything depends on a fine, even touch and a convincing face. Wertmüller's European training should give it more finish than most American small portraits of the day, though the sitter's character is not easily recovered at this size. It is a charming minor object, best valued as evidence of how a Swedish-born portraitist adapted to American demand."},"met:12800":{"d":"A party of men and women is overtaken by the end of the day on a mountain, a small human group in a large horizontal landscape, about 97 by 160 centimeters, painted in 1858. The title places the scene on Mansfield Mountain, the highest peak in Vermont, and the word belated suggests a late return, with the mountain and the sky outweighing the figures.","i":"By the 1850s the mountains of New England were a destination for tourists as well as painters, and a climb to a summit was a fashionable excursion. A belated party, hikers caught by nightfall, gave a painter a human drama of small figures facing the vast, a way to express the Sublime in terms an ordinary visitor could feel. The mountain also stood for national scenery and for the pleasure and peril of nature.","c":"The narrative gives the landscape a hook that many mountain pictures lack: the viewer wonders whether the party will get home. Thompson, a painter of landscape and genre rather than a specialist in the Sublime, treats the peril lightly and the setting with a comfortable breadth. The anecdote may be a little stagy, and it does not reach the grandeur of Church or Bierstadt, but its human scale is attractive, and it records the early days of mountain tourism."},"met:12802":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 91 by 60 centimeters, dated 1859, shows a house among trees with a man, a woman and a child in a landscape of summer flowers, as the title and the Met's keywords indicate. The painter, Jerome B. Thompson, combines landscape and domestic life, so the setting and the family share the picture, and the flowers give the title its subject.","i":"In the 1850s rural domestic life was a favorite American subject, as cities grew and the countryside came to stand for stability and simple virtue. A house, a family and a garden in bloom suggested contentment and continuity, with a child for the future and summer for abundance. Thompson, who painted both landscapes and figure scenes, served a public that wanted such gentle, reassuring pictures.","c":"This is a picture of pleasant sentiments, and it is best judged by its mood rather than its invention. As with much sentimental American genre of the period, the figures are likely to be types rather than individuals, but the vertical format and the idea of a family at home in nature are well handled. Thompson seldom aims higher than agreeable, and here he hits his target; the picture says more about mid-century taste than about his range."},"met:12801":{"d":"A woman stands among trees beside a waterfall, with a rainbow arching through the spray, in a tall canvas about 102 by 76 centimeters, painted around 1870 by Jerome B. Thompson. The title makes the sound of falling water the subject, so the picture is a landscape with a poetic mood and a single human figure listening to that music or embodying it.","i":"A woman set within a waterfall landscape was a common Victorian device: the figure invited the viewer to feel the scene, and sometimes personified nature itself. The rainbow, a sign of promise after the Flood, adds a note of hope, and the title gives the scene a musical, literary turn typical of the later nineteenth century, when landscape painting moved from the Sublime toward the lyrical.","c":"A rainbow and a waterfall together make a very sweet picture, and some will find the effect too sugary; the title promises music that a painting cannot supply. Yet the tall format is well chosen for a cascade, and the figure gives the landscape a human point of entry. It is typical of Thompson's late, gentler manner, charming rather than powerful, and a fair reminder of how much American taste in 1870 craved comfort in its landscapes."},"nga:300":{"d":"Melons, apples, pears, figs, grapes and chestnuts are arranged along a tabletop covered by a white cloth, with a half-filled glass carafe of amber liquid. A pewter plate holds three apples and a halved fruit at the center, a pomegranate is split open to show its ruby seeds, and a wedge of watermelon and a bunch of dark grapes sit at the right. Two tiny flies rest on the cloth. Bright light from the upper left glints off the carafe, the plate and the seeds, leaving deep shadows around the fruit.","i":"Caravaggio's Basket of Fruit set the model in Rome for painting nature without idealization, and still lifes of fruit and glass became fashionable there. Opened fruits show off abundance and the painter's skill, while the two flies on the white cloth are a quiet reminder that ripeness is brief. The painter, known only as the Pensionante del Saraceni, was an anonymous follower of Caravaggio in Rome, possibly French, named after the painter Carlo Saraceni.","c":"Light is the star: a hard, raking glare from the upper left that glints off glass, pewter and wet seeds and sets deep shadows around the fruit. The table runs off both sides of the canvas, so the arrangement seems to continue beyond the frame, and the flies add a note of wit. It is less decorative than many Italian still lifes, and more sober: a fine example of Caravaggist naturalism applied to fruit, direct and grounded."},"met:435650":{"d":"A double portrait of a young mother and her small son, painted in oil on canvas, about 117 by 90 centimeters, in 1802. The sitters are Jeanne Eglé Mourgue (1778 to 1855), wife of Philippe Panon Desbassayns de Richemont, and their son Eugène (1800 to 1859), so she was about twenty-four and he about two. The Met's keywords record her shawl, lace, ribbon, bracelet and earring, the fashionable details of the Consulate years.","i":"After Rousseau, maternal tenderness became a fashionable subject, and portraits of mothers with their children were popular in Revolutionary and Napoleonic France; Marie Guillemine Benoist's teacher Vigée Le Brun had painted herself with her daughter in that spirit. Benoist also studied with Jacques-Louis David, and she is best known for her 1800 Portrait of a Black Woman. She is reported to have given up painting in 1818 when her husband's official post made a working wife inappropriate.","c":"Benoist's training suggests the blend one sees in her work: warmth of feeling in the manner of Vigée Le Brun, clarity of outline in the manner of David. A portrait of a mother and child invites sentiment, and the interest here is the equal attention given to the woman's person and her fashionable costume, with the child more than an accessory. It is a polished, assured Consulate portrait by a woman who deserves to be better known."},"met:437101":{"d":"Joseph, the Virgin and Child, an angel and a donkey rest beneath a tree on the flight into Egypt, in a small picture painted in oil on copper, about 23 by 28 centimeters, around 1641 by Pier Francesco Mola. The copper support gives the miniature-like scene a smooth, glowing surface, and the small size made it a cabinet picture for a collector's room rather than an altarpiece.","i":"Matthew's Gospel says only that Joseph fled to Egypt with Mary and the Child to escape Herod, and the rest on the way is a later legend that became a favorite of landscape-minded painters. Mola, born in the Ticino and trained in Rome and Bologna, was drawn to pastoral subjects and to the warm color of Venetian painting, which he studied on a visit to Venice. The angels attending the family are a common touch in such scenes.","c":"Mola's small coppers are among his most winning works: a glowing landscape of Venetian derivation, a quiet family and a mood of rest rather than drama. The donkey, the humblest participant, often steals such pictures, and a landscape lover like Mola was well placed to take the opportunity. The scale limits ambition and the sweetness may cloy, but the picture is a lyrical, well-judged piece for a private collector."},"met:817124":{"d":"Cain, the first murderer, kills his brother Abel in a vertical canvas about 139 by 90 centimeters, painted about 1650 to 1652 by Pier Francesco Mola. The Met's keywords record only the two brothers, so the picture is a concentrated drama of two figures in violent contact, the kind of subject that let Baroque painters show muscular bodies in action.","i":"In Genesis 4, Cain, angry that God accepted his brother's offering and not his own, killed Abel in a field: the first murder. Christian readers took Abel as a figure of the innocent victim who prefigures Christ, and Cain as the model of envy and fratricide. Mola painted the subject in Rome in the early 1650s, when he was admired for dramatic figure pictures with Venetian warmth of color as well as for landscapes.","c":"Mola brings to the subject a sculptural force and a warm, Venetian-derived palette that make the violence feel physical rather than anecdotal. The risk with this theme is rhetoric, and a tight, two-figure design avoids it by concentrating on the clash itself. Beside the great Cain and Abel scenes of Titian or Tintoretto it is more compact and less transcendent, but it is a muscular, confident performance."},"met:10765":{"d":"A self-portrait in watercolor on ivory, only about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated 1801. The artist, William M. S. Doyle, was in his early thirties, and the image is small enough to rest in the palm of a hand: a likeness meant to be held close, in a case or a pocket. The Met's keywords record a man, and the man is the painter himself.","i":"Self-portraits were a miniaturist's calling card: a patron could judge his skill in the artist's own face. Watercolor on ivory was the standard method of the day, with the pale, translucent ivory lending flesh a glow that opaque paint could not match. In the early republic, miniatures were exchanged among families and friends and carried in cases or lockets, and an artist's own likeness would also show how he wished to be seen.","c":"At this scale the artist's own face is a test of fine work: a few millimeters make the difference between a likeness and a mask. Doyle's self-portrait offers an honest, workmanlike image rather than a flourish, and as a record of an American miniaturist at the start of the nineteenth century it is useful. The delicate surface of such works is easy to miss at a glance and rewards a magnifying look."},"met:10763":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed gentleman, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1804 by William M. S. Doyle and measuring about 7.5 by 6 centimeters. The sitter is known only by his sex and station, as the title says, and the format is the small, upright miniature typical of the American portrait trade at the beginning of the nineteenth century, made to be held or mounted in a case.","i":"Ivory's pale, slightly translucent surface let the miniaturist build flesh tones in delicate touches, and light passing through the paint and reflecting from the ivory gave the face a glow; this was the medium's chief technical advantage. In 1804 the United States was prosperous and expanding, with a growing market for personal likenesses among merchants, officials and professionals. A miniature cost far less than an oil portrait and traveled easily, which suited families and friends who lived apart.","c":"A miniature of a gentleman in 1804 is a modest, assured object: the face is the whole point, and Doyle's strength is measured in the precision of that face. Without a known sitter, the picture asks to be admired for craftsmanship and for the quiet dignity that the form encourages. It has none of the drama of larger portraits, but the intimacy is real, and the object's smallness is part of its charm."},"met:15052":{"d":"A miniature of an unnamed gentleman, in watercolor on ivory, dated 1808 and measuring about 7 by 6 centimeters, by William M. S. Doyle. It is one of several portrait miniatures by Doyle in the Met, and like the others it is identified only as a gentleman, the sitter's name having been lost or never recorded. At this scale the picture is little more than a face and its immediate setting, made to be examined closely in the hand.","i":"A miniature was meant to be seen in a case, often closed, that protected the delicate ivory and could be tucked into a pocket or hung on a wall. Whether this one was made for a wife, a sweetheart, a parent or the sitter himself is unrecorded. The loss of names is common with miniatures: once separated from a family's papers or a case inscription, a face can survive while the person disappears.","c":"The pathos of such objects lies in the contrast between the care taken over a few square centimeters and the anonymity that followed. Doyle's miniature of 1808 carries the standard virtues, a clear likeness, a clean finish and a modest presence, and the standard limits, a formula of head and shoulders that leaves little room for invention. As a document of ordinary sitters in the early republic it is valuable, and it deserves to be looked at closely."},"met:18588":{"d":"A portrait of a gentleman painted in watercolor on ivory in 1810 by William M. S. Doyle and measuring about 7.6 by 6 centimeters. Like his other miniatures in the museum it records an unnamed man, the title offering only the polite term gentleman. A small plaque of ivory held in a case, it was designed to be looked at from a hand's distance.","i":"By 1810 American miniature painting was a regular profession, with painters advertising in newspapers and traveling between towns to take likenesses, and with competition from silhouettes and from the physiognotrace, a machine for making cheap profile portraits. A miniaturist set himself apart by finish and likeness. Doyle's name survives on several such works, while the sitters' names mostly do not.","c":"In a crowded market the miniaturist's selling point was fidelity, and a good example earns respect for what it does without fuss: a clear, restrained likeness. What the genre lacks is room for inventiveness, and Doyle, like many of his contemporaries, stays within a formula. The reward is in the details: the translucency of the ivory, the fine touches of the face. A competent piece of work, honest and unassuming."},"met:10764":{"d":"A watercolor-on-ivory portrait of an unnamed gentleman, dated 1814 and measuring about 7.5 by 6.2 centimeters, by William M. S. Doyle. It is one of two miniatures by Doyle dated 1814 in the Met, and as with his other portraits of men, the title gives only his sex and rank. The year places it during the War of 1812, and the little ivory was made to be held close.","i":"In 1814 the United States was at war with Britain, and personal likenesses carried extra weight as keepsakes for people separated by service or travel. Such a miniature could be sent or carried abroad, a small, durable reminder of a face. Whether this one served that purpose is unrecorded; the sitter's name has not survived, and the portrait now speaks mainly of the craft of its maker and the habits of the time.","c":"A miniature from a time of war may have mattered more to its first owner than any large portrait could, and the form suits that emotion: small, personal, portable. Doyle's execution is the usual blend of care and convention. Without a sitter's name the portrait loses its human story, but it keeps its quiet craftsmanship, and it is best valued as one survivor from a workshop that produced a steady stream of likenesses."},"met:15053":{"d":"A gentleman's portrait in watercolor on ivory, dated 1814 and measuring about 7.6 by 6.1 centimeters, by William M. S. Doyle. It is nearly identical in size and date to another Doyle miniature in the museum, which suggests a steady output of standard-format likenesses from his hand that year. The sitter is unnamed, and the picture is small enough to be held in one hand.","i":"A miniaturist worked with a ready stock of formats: a standard size of ivory, a plain or lightly clouded ground, a conventional bust. Standardization kept prices down and let a painter turn out portraits quickly, which suited a trade that depended on volume. Doyle's two portraits of 1814 are examples. The differences between sitters, in the face and in small touches of dress, are what the buyer paid for.","c":"The repetition of the format is the point: a good miniaturist did one job and did it well. If the portrait seems restrained, that is because the form demands it, and the face carries everything. Without a name the sitter is a stranger, but the likeness still looks out, and the miniature is a reminder of how many ordinary Americans were pictured in this way before photography took over."},"met:12569":{"d":"A large canvas, about 123 by 168 centimeters, painted about 1813 by Joshua Shaw, shows the Flood near its end, a vision of disaster in which the Met's keywords list floodwater, a snake and death. The scene presents the biblical catastrophe as a spectacle of doom and nature's power, the kind of picture painted for exhibition rather than for devotion.","i":"The Flood of Genesis 6 to 9, in which God destroyed a corrupt humanity and saved Noah and his family in the ark, was a favorite subject of early Romantic painters, who found in catastrophe a means of expressing the Sublime: Poussin's Winter, Turner's Deluge and Girodet's version all prepared the ground. Shaw was born in England and later emigrated to the United States, where he became known for landscape. A snake may stand for evil or for the survival of the wild.","c":"A subject that defeated many painters demands scale and conviction, and Shaw has the ambition if not always the control of Poussin or Turner. Showing the Flood near its end, when the waters retreat over the dead, favors desolation over action, a thoughtful choice. The picture's power depends on atmosphere, and its virtue is seriousness. As a monument to the early Romantic taste for disaster it interests, even if the execution falls short of the idea."},"met:437938":{"d":"A portrait of a man drawn in plumbago, as graphite was then called, on vellum, and dated 1690. The medium makes it a drawing rather than a painting, and the artist, Robert White, was an English engraver who made intensely finished portrait drawings of the sitters of his day, usually on a small scale. The vellum, a smooth prepared skin, allowed the minute, even strokes that give such drawings their engraved precision.","i":"In the later seventeenth century, portrait prints circulated widely as frontispieces to books and as images of public figures, and White made many portrait drawings in plumbago on vellum, some as models for engravings and some as finished works in their own right. Such drawings were prized for their likeness and their fineness, and the soft gray tones gave them a restrained dignity. The sitter's name is not given, so the drawing stands as a fine example of the type.","c":"White's skill is to model a face with a mesh of tiny strokes so even and so light that the drawing reads almost as an engraving in gray; the effect is of exactness and reserve. The limits are those of the method: the drawing is tight and rather impersonal, and the sitter appears under glass, as it were. As a demonstration of controlled draftsmanship it is superb, and a good reminder that portraits need not be painted."},"nga:61391":{"d":"Four bodies are locked together by dramatic gesture against a dark ground in this nearly square canvas. A white-bearded old man in a brown tunic sits right of center, his arms stretched out at shoulder height, head thrust forward and eyes bulging white, pointing at a younger man in a golden-yellow tunic who kneels at left, head thrown back, a raised hand shielding his face. One woman leans her forehead on the old man's knee; another stands behind with arms spread wide.","i":"In Sophocles' Oedipus at Colonus, the blind, exiled Oedipus is visited by his son Polynices, who seeks his blessing for an attack on Thebes. Oedipus, abandoned by both his sons, curses him, foretelling that the brothers will die by each other's hands. The women are probably Antigone and Ismene, the daughters who plead for peace. Fuseli chose the moment of the curse, the climax of the scene, which suited his taste for extreme situations from classical and literary sources.","c":"Fuseli compresses the drama into a single knot of limbs, with distorted gestures, a flat dark ground and sculptural bodies in the Michelangelesque manner he had studied in Rome. The strengths are ferocity and economy, and the old man's stare is unforgettable. The weaknesses are theatricality and exaggerated anatomy, which some viewers find comic, but that excess is the point: a Romantic intensity of terror before Romanticism had a name."},"met:436423":{"d":"A night scene of witchcraft: nude female witches, a child and a knife gather in darkness around the night-hag, who has come to join the Lapland witches. The oil-on-canvas picture, about 102 by 126 centimeters, was painted in 1796 by Henry Fuseli, and the pale bodies against the dark are the main effect. The subject comes from a passage in Milton's Paradise Lost.","i":"In Book II of Paradise Lost, Milton describes the night-hag lured by the smell of infant blood to dance with the Lapland witches while the moon is eclipsed by their charms. Fuseli, a Swiss-born painter working in London, made a specialty of the supernatural, and he painted from Milton for a gallery of his own, the Milton Gallery, which opened in 1799. The knife and the child supply the ritual menace that the poem only implies.","c":"Fuseli's aim was to paint what the imagination fears, and the picture succeeds in its dark, erotic menace: pale bodies against black, a ritual in a void. The drawing is not always elegant, and Fuseli's anatomy is willfully distorted, which some find awkward, but the distortion produces a nightmare logic. It is less famous than The Nightmare of 1781, yet it shows the same taste for the Gothic Sublime and is a strong example of his mature manner."},"met:435683":{"d":"A small boat glides toward a rocky island at sunset, carrying a rower, a white-draped standing figure and what appears to be a coffin; the island is crowned by dark cypresses and cut with openings like tombs. The Met's keywords record an island, a sunset, a bark, Charon, a cypress, the evening sky and a corpse. The oil on wood, about 74 by 122 centimeters, is dated 1880 and is by the Swiss painter Arnold Böcklin.","i":"Böcklin painted several versions of the Isle of the Dead between 1880 and 1886, and the title that made it famous was reportedly given by a dealer, not by the painter. The ferryman, the white-draped figure and the coffin evoke the passage of the dead to the underworld, borrowed from the myth of Charon but set in a Mediterranean landscape that has been linked to the English cemetery in Florence. The picture became an emblem of Symbolism and inspired Rachmaninoff's tone poem of 1909.","c":"Few nineteenth-century paintings state a mood so economically: still water, a hush of cypress, the last glow of sunset and the white figure as the only bright note. The hallucinatory clarity of each form and the symmetrical design make death feel like a place. The effect is strong but also manufactured and somewhat static, and some viewers find the paint less powerful than the idea. As a landmark of Symbolism it is essential."},"nga:55818":{"d":"Four armed figures kneel and stand outside a curved stone wall that encloses a statue and tall trees, in this wide painting of about 114 by 181 centimeters. Three kneel on marble steps facing right while the fourth stands looking left; they wear colorful draped cloths, plumed silver helmets and brown sandals, and two carry tall spears. Behind the irregular stone wall rise brown trunks and green and orange leaves, and orange leaves cover the stones below. To the right a dark statue of a helmeted man is partly visible. Heavy gray clouds darken the sky.","i":"Hercules, the Greek Heracles, was the hero worshipped in sanctuaries across the Greek and Roman world as a protector and a patron of strength, and soldiers prayed to him before and after battle. Böcklin's armed worshippers, kneeling at the entrance of a grove with the god's statue, suggest a ritual moment, with the dark sky heightening its solemnity. The painting belongs to the late nineteenth-century Symbolist taste for imagined antiquity, in which classical myths and sacred places express mood rather than archaeology.","c":"Böcklin's gift is atmosphere: the oppressive sky, the enclosed grove and the kneeling figures dwarfed by the wall give the antique scene a hushed, almost haunted feeling. The painting is more mood than story, and the figures' poses are stiff, but that stiffness serves the ceremony's formality. Compared with his famous Isle of the Dead it is quieter and less symbolic, yet it shares the same ability to make an imaginary place feel intensely present."},"met:436808":{"d":"An earl, his countess and their infant son are grouped together in this family portrait, with a dog among the recorded motifs, painted in oil on a canvas of about 127 by 102 centimeters around 1776. Family portraits of the 1770s often replaced the stiff dynastic group with an image of affectionate informality: parents close together, the child held or placed between them, and a pet to suggest the ease of home.","i":"The sitters were Edward Smith Stanley, twelfth Earl of Derby, who helped found the Derby horse race in 1780, and his wife Lady Elizabeth Hamilton, daughter of a duke; their son would become the thirteenth earl and a celebrated naturalist. The picture presents the harmony and continuity of a great house, though the marriage soon failed and the couple separated. Portraits of this kind were made for the family seat as statements of lineage and happiness.","c":"Kauffmann made her name by lending Neoclassical grace and sensibility to the British aristocracy, and this family group shows the formula's virtues: gentle idealization, easy intimacy, smooth drawing and a soft palette. Its weakness is the sameness of its sweetness; faces turn into types, and character gives way to charm. Beside Reynolds's more solid and Gainsborough's more spontaneous groups, it is pleasing but less penetrating."},"met:436810":{"d":"At dusk beside the sea, under a rocky overhang, a young man in a brown tunic sits on the ground at the right while nymphs gather around him with flowers. One in rose holds out a basket of blossoms, another in white raises a wreath behind him, and a third, in pale yellow, stretches a long garland high in both hands. At the left a nymph in a white and pink gown and a head veil leads an elderly bearded man in a hooded brown cloak away. The sky glows rose and lilac over a calm sea, and the figures wear sandals or are barefoot.","i":"Telemachus and the old man Mentor have reached Calypso's island after a shipwreck, and the nymphs welcome the hero with fruit, wine and flowers. Mentor is actually the goddess Athena in disguise, shown being led away by a nymph. The source is Fénelon's The Adventures of Telemachus (1699), a moral romance written when he was tutor to the king's grandson. Monsignor Onorato Caetani commissioned the picture in Rome in 1782, the same year Kauffmann painted his portrait, and it was paired with The Sorrow of Telemachus. The Metropolitan Museum received both through Collis P. Huntington's bequest of 1900.","c":"A pleasant picture with a clear plan: the nymphs' offerings form a diagonal that leads to the seated hero, and the dusk colors of rose, lilac and gold give a softness the daytime companion lacks. The weakness is that the hero is passive and the arrangement of flowers and robes reads like a dance. The danger in the story, since Calypso's island is a gentle trap, is invisible. Of the pair it is the lighter and more decorative, and it shows Kauffmann's polish more than her invention."},"met:436809":{"d":"At the edge of a grove by the sea, women gather around a table. At the center sits Calypso in a white gown with a red cloak, sweeping one hand outward in a gesture of command. Beside her Telemachus, in a pale yellow tunic, slumps with his head on his hand, eyes closed in grief. Behind the table an old bearded man sits quietly. On the right a nymph in draped robes lifts a basket of fruit above Telemachus's head. At left a kneeling nymph in blue pours water from a jug into a basin, and others sit or stand nearby. A gold vase rests in the foreground, and cypress trees and sea show in the distance.","i":"The scene comes from François Fénelon's novel The Adventures of Telemachus (1699), hugely popular in the eighteenth century. Telemachus, the son of Odysseus, is shipwrecked on Calypso's island. The scene is read as Calypso ordering her nymphs to stop singing the praises of Odysseus, whose memory distresses his son. Kauffmann painted it in Rome in 1783 as a companion to Telemachus and the Nymphs of Calypso, for Monsignor Onorato Caetani. She was Swiss-born, a founding member of the Royal Academy in London, and had settled in Rome in 1782. Later versions of the subject date from 1788 and 1789.","c":"Kauffmann excels at draped women arranged in a graceful frieze. The white and yellow gowns glow against the shade, and the nymphs' gestures flow into one another. But the feeling is theatrical: grief is a hand-to-cheek pose, every face is the same sweet oval, and the drama of Fénelon's text becomes a polite tableau. It is a good example of neoclassical drawing-room painting, polished, cultivated and not especially moved. Its best quality is its calm, which suited the educated Roman patrons who admired her."},"cma:688823":{"d":"A dancing young woman appears in the guise of Terpsichore, the Muse of dance, on a canvas of about 92 by 71 centimeters painted around 1780. The museum notes that the figure was inspired by recently excavated wall paintings from the ancient Roman town of Herculaneum, whose light-footed dancers captivated artists in the age of Neoclassicism. Kauffmann borrows that antique lightness and translates it into oil paint for a modern audience.","i":"Terpsichore is the Muse of dance and choral song, and a woman presented in her guise could be a portrait in disguise or a purely imaginary figure. The excavations at Herculaneum and Pompeii, begun in the eighteenth century, gave artists new models of antique painting, and Kauffmann, who worked in Rome and London, was among those who used them. She was also a founder member of the Royal Academy of Arts in 1768, and no other woman was admitted as a full academician until 1936.","c":"Kauffmann's grace is deployed here in a decorative key: poised motion, flowing line and an antique pose recast as a fashionable image. It is light and pretty, and the single figure keeps the composition simple. The sweetness is also the limit; it lacks the weight of the history subjects she aspired to, and the dance, for all its charm, feels more posed than danced. A polished example of Neoclassical taste rather than a deep one."},"nga:42931":{"d":"A small portrait of a woman, about 63 by 50 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas about 1790 by Angelica Kauffmann and identified, with a question mark, as Franciska Krasinska, Duchess of Courland. The tentative title signals that the sitter's identity is not certain, and the picture is best described by its type: a refined, half-length likeness of a lady of rank in the idealizing style for which Kauffmann was admired.","i":"Kauffmann, a Swiss-born painter, was a founding member of the Royal Academy in London and settled in Rome in 1782, where her studio and salon were a stop for aristocratic visitors on the Grand Tour. Portraits of noble travelers were an important part of her work. If the identification is right, the sitter was a Polish noblewoman who became Duchess of Courland by marriage; it is also a reminder of how often portraits lose their names.","c":"Kauffmann's portraits please more than they probe: surfaces are smooth, faces idealized, sitters made graceful and thoughtful. That served her clients and her fame, but it can make her sitters resemble each other, and the neoclassical sweetness can cloy. At this small scale the formula works well, and the interest is in the refinement and ease of the likeness. Accomplished and agreeable, if a little bland."},"met:726543":{"d":"A man identified as Monsignor Giuseppe Spina (1756-1828) is the sitter of this portrait, dated 1798, painted in oil on a canvas of about 96 by 80 centimeters. The title gives his ecclesiastical rank, the honorific Monsignor marking a senior churchman, and the recorded motifs name only the sitter. The painter, Angelica Kauffmann, was then living in Rome, where she portrayed foreign visitors and members of Roman society.","i":"In 1798 Rome was in upheaval: French troops entered the city in February, a Roman Republic was proclaimed, and Pope Pius VI was taken into exile. Spina was a rising papal official who later took part in the Church's negotiations with Napoleon's France, including the Concordat of 1801, and became an archbishop and cardinal. A portrait of 1798 therefore shows a man whose career would sit at the crossroads of the Church and revolutionary Europe.","c":"Kauffmann's gift for smooth, sympathetic likeness suits a churchman whose public life demanded composure. Her Roman portraits arguably tend toward quiet dignity rather than the pretty sensibility of her London years, and the sitter's rank is carried by his bearing more than by display. As with much of her portraiture, the idealizing hand softens, and the result is more courteous than searching."},"nga:140728":{"d":"A forest scene of gnarled dark-brown trees with thick trunks, several growing at a slant, over reddish-brown earth, green grass and small ferns. The nearest tree, at the left, has a large opening in its trunk and several round knots, and small feathery leaves form a canopy overhead. The foreground is in shadow while a patch of the background at the right is lit with yellow light. The canvas is about 41 by 67 centimeters.","i":"Bregentved is an estate on Zealand, the Danish island that includes Copenhagen. In the 1830s Danish artists of the Golden Age turned from classical views to careful observation of their native landscape, and old gnarled trees stood for the nation's roots and antiquity. The title names the forest, not an event, so the trees themselves are the subject. Frederik Sødring painted this about the middle of the 1830s.","c":"The strength of the picture is its unsentimental attention to the hollowed, knotted trunk and the shaded forest floor, with a patch of yellow light at the back that opens the depth. The leaf canopy makes a lively pattern. It has no human story, which is the point, and for some viewers it may seem plain; but its honesty about wood and bark foreshadows the open-air naturalism of later decades. A fine example of Danish Golden Age observation."},"met:438545":{"d":"A young man in his room at the Villa Medici, the French Academy in Rome, in a small oil painting about 58 by 45 centimeters, dated 1817. The museum's keywords record the bedroom, the interior and a guitar, and the sitter is the painter Léon Pallière (1787 to 1820). The artist, Jean Alaux, was probably a fellow resident of the Academy, and the picture is as much a portrait of a place and a way of life as of a face.","i":"The Villa Medici has housed the French Academy in Rome since 1803, and winners of the Prix de Rome lived and studied there for several years. Pictures of friends in their rooms, with personal belongings like the guitar, are a kind of friendship portrait and a record of everyday life among young artists, a taste fostered by Romantic interest in the lives of artists. Pallière died young, in 1820, and the picture preserves his private space and his leisure.","c":"Intimacy is the picture's best quality: no pose, no allegory, just a friend among his things. The guitar says that the sitter had a life beyond the studio, and the bedroom suggests the plain surroundings of a student. It is not a great painting, but it is a charming and informative one, a document of Romantic youth in Rome, kin to Kersting's pictures of friends in their studios."},"nga:66439":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 89 by 116 centimeters, painted in 1910 by the Swiss-born French artist Félix Vallotton and titled The Wind. The subject is an invisible force, so the picture must show it through its effects, and Vallotton's usual manner, with simplified shapes, firm outlines and smooth, flat areas of color, turns movement in the air into pattern. At this size the design is meant to read from across a room.","i":"By 1910 Vallotton, once a leading printmaker of the Nabi group, had turned to painting full time, and in these years he made what are known as composed landscapes, working from memory and invention in the studio rather than from nature on the spot. A subject like wind lets a painter of his bent concentrate on rhythm and design, bending forms into curves. The Nabis valued decorative unity and the expressive power of line and color.","c":"Painting wind is a problem of rhythm, and Vallotton's taste for clean contours and flat color gives him a particular solution: motion becomes pattern, a decorative curve rather than a sensation of air. That can feel cool beside the Impressionists' atmospheric wind, but it has a strange, stylized force, and the design is controlled. It shows why Vallotton is admired as a designer as much as a painter."},"nga:71458":{"d":"A view of the church of Souain, a village in the Champagne region that lay in the zone of fighting in the First World War and was destroyed. The canvas, about 96 by 130 centimeters, was painted in 1917 by Félix Vallotton, who visited the front that year. The title and date suggest a village landmark set among the devastation of war, framed as a witness to what happened there.","i":"Vallotton was too old to fight, but in 1917 he visited the Champagne front and painted scenes of the ravaged landscape. Souain was one of the villages obliterated in the fighting, so a view of its church records a vanished place. The war paintings of this period took Vallotton beyond his earlier decorative work toward a stern, almost documentary view of destruction, which he handled with his habitual clarity of contour.","c":"War pictures by artists of Vallotton's generation can fall into either rhetoric or reportage, and his answer is restraint: clear shapes, a controlled palette and the viewpoint of a witness rather than a hero. A church in such a village is a poignant motif, and Vallotton does not squeeze it for pathos, which makes it more effective. The picture is less well known than his interiors and nudes, but it is a sober, impressive example of his late work."},"nga:46553":{"d":"A still life of marigolds and tangerines, painted in oil on canvas, about 65 by 54 centimeters, in 1924 by Félix Vallotton, a year before his death. The title pairs two things of nearly the same orange, flowers and fruit, so the picture is a study in the echo of one warm color across different shapes and textures. It belongs to the quiet, compact still lifes of the painter's last years.","i":"Vallotton's late still lifes, made in the 1920s, have a clean, enameled finish and compact, carefully balanced arrangements, a calm after the harsh interiors and the war paintings of earlier years. Flowers and fruit were safe, pleasant subjects for a collector's wall, and for a painter of cool temperament they were a chance to work on color and design alone. The marigold, a hardy garden flower, was a common cut flower.","c":"A tight, polished picture in which flowers and fruit rhyme in color. Vallotton's compositions are rigorous and slightly detached, and the enameled finish can look cold, but the formal intelligence is evident, and the title's color rhyme shows a dry wit. It is not as ambitious as his best interiors, but it is a stylish late work from a painter who had made detachment into a manner."},"met:437703":{"d":"A woman, evidently a household servant, works with food in a kitchen, with fish and a cat close by, in a small oil-on-wood picture about 52 by 44 centimeters, painted about 1643 by Hendrick Sorgh. The Met's keywords list a woman, a cat, fish, cooking, food and servants, so the picture is a domestic scene of preparation, with the cat's interest in the fish adding a likely touch of humor.","i":"Kitchen scenes had a long Netherlandish pedigree, from the monumental kitchens of Pieter Aertsen and Joachim Beuckelaer in the sixteenth century. Sorgh, who worked in Rotterdam, brought the type down to cabinet scale in the 1640s, with tidy, detailed realism. Such pictures could celebrate domestic order and abundance, or warn against greed, and cat and fish were a familiar comic pairing. Viewers appreciated the observation of ordinary tasks and household things.","c":"Sorgh's kitchens are tidy theater: the tactile things, fish, pots and vegetables, are the stars, and the figures are broad types who serve them. The cat adds the humor. It stands between the earlier monumental kitchens and the refined genre of Gerrit Dou and Gabriel Metsu, and it has less poetry than either, but it is an attractive, well-organized picture of everyday work. A modest pleasure."},"met:436772":{"d":"A sheepfold on a farm, with a man at work among the sheep and some chickens, is the subject of this wide panel, an oil on wood of about 46 by 92 centimeters, dated 1857. The record lists a man, a chicken, a farmer, sheep and work, and its keywords are Chickens, Sheep, Working and Farmers. The museum gives no further description of the fold, the light or the arrangement.","i":"Charles Jacque made sheep and poultry his specialty, and sheepfolds were a recurring subject in his paintings and etchings. He settled near Barbizon in 1849, among the painters of the Fontainebleau forest, and he brought to rural life a close, affectionate knowledge of animals. For city buyers in the 1850s such a picture offered a vision of honest, unhurried farm labor.","c":"Jacque's rural interiors are typically earthy and convincing, with animals individualized and heavy with wool and a muted, brownish barn light. He lacks Millet's depth of feeling for the peasant, and his farmers can look like props for the animals. A solid, pleasing work by a specialist; his etchings of similar subjects are tighter and, to many tastes, better."},"met:436774":{"d":"A girl, sheep, a shepherd and a tree are the recorded motifs of this small upright panel, an oil on wood of about 41 by 29 centimeters, with the title Springtime. No date is recorded, and the museum gives no further description of the landscape or the girl's pose. The keywords, Girls, Sheep, Spring and Shepherds, suggest a pastoral scene of a young shepherdess at the season of new growth.","i":"A shepherdess with her flock beneath a tree in spring is a long-lived pastoral subject, going back to Rococo painters and, behind them, to classical poetry. Jacque gave it a Barbizon realism: the girl is a farm child and the sheep are real animals. Spring, as an emblem of renewal, allowed such pictures to be sold as pretty seasonal scenes.","c":"A modest, charming picture. The sheep, which Jacque knew better than almost any French painter of his day, probably steal the show from the girl, who is the usual sweet type. The upright format and small size suggest a picture made for a home rather than an exhibition, and in that role it succeeds without trying to be more. The strength of Jacque's art lies in the animals."},"nga:43433":{"d":"Two young children sit side by side on a green carpet in front of a brown curtain: the one on the left, seated on the floor, has a round bald head, light eyes and an off-white dress and holds a rattle, while the one on the right, in a purple dress with light blonde hair, holds a doll. Behind them lie greenery, pink flowers and a sailboat on water under a blue sky with clouds. The canvas is about 63 by 80 centimeters.","i":"Children's portraits were popular in mid-nineteenth-century America, and toys mark the age of each child here: a rattle for the baby, a doll for the older one. The curtain and the glimpse of landscape are standard portrait conventions, bringing the world outside into the room, and the sailboat may point to the family's love of the water, though that is a guess. Portraits of siblings were also a way to keep a likeness at a time when many children did not survive.","c":"The charm lies in the contrast between the two small figures: the round-headed baby with a rattle, the older child clutching a doll. They are observed with tenderness, and the stagey curtain and tiny sailboat are the studio's standard props. The drawing is plain and the modeling simple, but the picture has a naive warmth that more polished portraits often lose. A straightforward family record, modest in art and engaging in feeling."},"nga:453":{"d":"An old man sits on rocky ground, reading a red book held in his thin arms, a thin golden halo around his head. He has a long white beard and a balding head, wears a light blue hooded cloak and is barefoot. At the left rise tall gray mountains with a large dark cave, from which a lion-like animal looks out at us; beyond him an open landscape has a light blue river winding into the distance beneath white and blue mountains. The panel is about 31 by 25 centimeters.","i":"The saint is Jerome, who translated the Bible into Latin and is shown as a scholar-hermit in the wilderness, with a book and the lion that, according to legend, he befriended by drawing a thorn from its paw. Jerome was a favorite of Venetian devotion, a model of learning and penance. Alvise Vivarini painted this small panel about 1476, as Antonello da Messina's oil technique was arriving in Venice, and the picture sits between the older tempera tradition and the new.","c":"A small picture with a large landscape: the frail saint, the red book as a color accent, the cave and the winding river that carries the eye into the distance. Alvise is gentler than the stern Paduan tradition around him, and he sets the saint in a world that has depth and air. The figure is slight, and the lion is more a label than a drama, but the quiet, contemplative mood is convincing. A delicate devotional picture."},"nga:499":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a clean-shaven man with wavy brown hair falling almost to his shoulders from beneath an earth-brown, brimless cap looks up and slightly to our right. He has brown eyes under low, dark brows, a square face with a rounded nose, a wide chin and thin, closed lips, and his coffee-brown garment has vertical pleats at a high neck with a narrow white band. He is lit brightly from the left against a dark brown ground. The panel is about 35 by 31 centimeters.","i":"Alvise Vivarini painted this about 1495, when the portrait with a plain dark ground, strong light and a direct, close-up view, introduced to Venice by Antonello da Messina and developed by Giovanni Bellini, was the standard for the city's elite. The sitter, probably a Venetian of unknown name, wears sober clothes that signal respectability rather than rank. The upward glance gives him an air of alertness or contemplation, perhaps conventional rather than personal.","c":"The face is the achievement: strongly lit, plainly seen and honestly modeled, with no flattery in the square jaw and wide chin. The brown clothes and hair almost merge with the dark ground, which concentrates attention on the features but makes the sitter float a little. Alvise is less poised than Bellini and less incisive than Antonello, yet this is a candid, convincing portrait of a man in his world."},"met:12833":{"d":"A landscape in oil on a small wooden panel, about 36 by 56 centimetres, takes moonlight as its subject, as the title says. The museum record lists only landscape, so the specifics of the scene, such as the water, trees or sky, cannot be described here with confidence. Tryon's night and twilight pictures typically reduce the view to a few broad bands of closely related tone, with soft edges and little detail, aiming at mood rather than topography.","i":"Tryon was a leading American Tonalist, a painter of quiet, misty landscapes in which the hour and the atmosphere, dawn, evening or moonlight, are the true subjects. He had studied in Paris and admired the French landscape painters of Barbizon, and he brought a similar sensibility to the American countryside. The date 1887 puts it among the pictures with which he built his reputation. The Met received it in 1906 as a gift of George A. Hearn, a New York merchant and collector.","c":"Tryon's reticent, tonal landscapes are an acquired taste: the quiet gradations of a moonlit scene reward a patient eye and can seem empty to a hurried one. Without the specific details to judge, one can say that this small panel belongs to the poetic, restrained tradition he did much to define, and that it asks the viewer to supply the silence. It is less showy than the work of many of his contemporaries, and the better for it."},"met:12832":{"d":"A harbour at evening, with ships and boats, is the subject of this oil on a wooden panel, about 51 by 80 centimetres. The museum lists ship, boat and harbour among the subjects but gives no further description, so the arrangement of vessels, the colour of the sky and the surface of the water cannot be described with confidence. Tryon's evening pictures usually favour a low, calm horizon, soft light and few strong contrasts, and his harbours are quiet places rather than busy ports.","i":"New Bedford, Massachusetts, was the great American whaling port of the nineteenth century, and its harbour was lined with ships. Tryon, a painter of the New England landscape, turned the working harbour into a place of stillness, as the evening hour hushes everything. The picture belongs to the American Tonalist interest in mood over detail, and the date of 1890 falls in his most productive years.","c":"A quiet harbour picture in which evening light softens the working world of masts and hulls into harmonies of tone. The panel is larger and more substantial than his small moonlit pieces, and the subject is a little less mysterious. Without close detail it is difficult to say how well the vessels are drawn, but Tryon's hallmark is not the accuracy of rigging but the gentleness of the whole."},"met:12834":{"d":"A landscape on a nearly square wooden panel, about 61 by 59 centimetres, shows the moment when the moon rises as the sun sets, as the title states. The museum record lists the moon and a landscape but no more, so the colours of the sky and the forms of the land cannot be described with confidence. The subject is a twilight effect, in which the warm light of the departing sun and the cool light of the new moon share the sky.","i":"Moonrise at sunset is a brief, delicate effect to which Tryon, as a Tonalist, was drawn, since it allowed a picture of almost no incident and exquisite colour. Contemporaries in America and Europe, including Whistler and the Barbizon painters, were similarly drawn to dawn and dusk. The painting dates from 1890 and was given to the Met in 1910 by George A. Hearn.","c":"A picture that lives or dies by the delicacy of its tones: when it works, the lingering warm light and the rising pale moon create a stillness that is genuinely affecting. The risk is vagueness, and a viewer who wants incident will find little. The nearly square format is unusual for a landscape and suits the vertical pull of a rising moon. Without detail to examine, it seems a fair example of Tryon's hushed, tonal poetry."},"met:12831":{"d":"A wide landscape on a long wooden panel, about 52 by 92 centimetres, shows dawn in early spring, according to the title. The museum lists only dawn and landscape as subjects, so details such as bare trees, mist or the colour of the sky cannot be described with confidence. Early spring suggests that the trees are still bare or only just in bud, and Tryon's dawn pictures are typically built from long, quiet bands of pale colour.","i":"Dawn and early spring are both thresholds, the day just starting and the year just waking, and Tryon painted them often because the soft, indeterminate light suited his Tonalist interest in the half-seen. In the 1890s he was widely admired for such poetic landscapes and sold to major collectors; Charles Lang Freer, the Detroit industrialist, became his greatest patron. This panel was given to the Met by Mrs. George Langdon Jewett in 1917.","c":"Tryon's best pictures have an almost musical hush, and the long format here, with its horizontal bands of light, suits a view of the day beginning. The risk of his method is that subtle becomes slight: a viewer who does not give the picture time may see little. At 92 centimetres wide it is large enough to let the quiet effects register, and it stands as a representative example of the mature Tonalism for which he was known."},"nga:234270":{"d":"A painting on a cross-shaped panel, about 61 by 42 centimeters, made in oil in Spain between about 1639 and 1655 and credited to María Josefa Sánchez. The cross shape of the support is the idea: the painted image occupies a cruciform field, and the title says the object served as a cell cross, a devotional object for the small room of a nun or monk.","i":"A cell cross hung in the sparse, private room of a member of a religious order, where it served as a focus for prayer and contemplation of Christ's Passion. Spanish religious art of the seventeenth century was known for its directness and intensity, and objects for convents were often painted by artists whose names have been lost; here a name survives, which is itself notable. Convents were also among the few places where women could pursue artistic work.","c":"This is an object whose purpose was prayer, and it should be judged by its devotional effect rather than by virtuosity. The shape is the best idea: a painting that is also a cross, image and instrument at once. Spanish painters of the period excelled at stark, intense images of Christ, and a cell cross asks for exactly that; the artist's name, preserved when so many are lost, adds interest. A small, serious devotional work."},"nga:46026":{"d":"A bare-chested young man lies asleep in a forest, head resting on one hand, a scarlet robe across his hips and a staff loosely held. Above him on a gray cloud floats a woman with curls held by a blue ribbon, smiling faintly and reaching toward him, with a sharp crescent moon curving behind. A winged putto nestles in the cloud between them with an arrow and a hand in a bunch of pink roses. Three sheep lie behind the man and a hound sleeps at his feet. The oil on canvas measures about 95 by 137 centimeters.","i":"In the myth, Diana, the moon goddess, fell in love with Endymion, a handsome shepherd, and Jupiter granted him eternal sleep and youth so that she could visit him every night. The crescent moon marks her identity, the sheep his occupation, and the putto's arrow and roses love. Fragonard painted the subject as a young artist, in the years after he won the Prix de Rome and before he left for Italy, in the graceful mythological vein of his teacher Boucher.","c":"A youthful, accomplished exercise in the manner of Boucher: the sleeping body is boldly foreshortened, seen from below, the clouds are airy and the colors fresh. It lacks the dash and spontaneity of Fragonard's mature work, and the mythology feels more decorative than felt. But the pink roses, the sleeping hound and the sheep are charming details, and the picture shows astonishing facility at an early age."},"met:436321":{"d":"A woman with an infant and a dog in an interior, in an oil painting about 49 by 59 centimeters, painted about 1760 by Jean Honoré Fragonard. The keywords record the woman, the infant, the dog and the interior, so the picture is a humble scene of domestic life, a long way from the glittering gallantry that made Fragonard's name. The title places it in Rome, where he studied.","i":"Fragonard won the Prix de Rome and lived in Italy from 1756 to 1761, studying at the French Academy; in those years he drew the Roman countryside and made small genre scenes of ordinary people that show his admiration for Dutch painting and for Chardin's quiet domestic subjects. A Roman interior with a mother, a child and a dog combines the observation of a Dutch genre scene with Italian light.","c":"This is not the dazzling Fragonard of The Swing. It is a looser, warmer picture of everyday life, in which a few figures and a shadowy room are enough. The handling is free, the mood affectionate, and the debt to Dutch and Chardin-like interiors is clear. It is a minor picture, but it shows the observation beneath the Rococo charm: the painter could see as well as flirt."},"met:436325":{"d":"Lovers steal a kiss while a game absorbs the company: the Met's keywords record a couple, a kiss, a lovers' embrace, a game and a girl. The oil on canvas, about 48 by 64 centimeters, was painted about 1760 by Jean Honoré Fragonard. It is a small gallant scene of the sort he made for private collectors, treating flirtation with quick, lively touches.","i":"The stolen kiss is a classic theme of Rococo gallantry, popular in France in the mid-eighteenth century, because the idea of a kiss taken in secret appealed to viewers who enjoyed both the thrill and the comedy of near-discovery. Fragonard, a pupil of Boucher, painted the subject at the start of his career, around the years of his stay in Rome, and he returned to it in a famous later version in the Hermitage.","c":"Light and quick, the picture shows Fragonard's gift for turning social comedy into a play of touch and light: the kiss is a spark within a crowd. Its pleasures are in the brushwork rather than the story, which is slight. Beside the dazzling set pieces of his maturity it is a lesser work, but it shows how early he developed the spontaneity that would make his name."},"nga:98647":{"d":"Dark forest-green treetops at the right stand against a sky that warms from pale blue at the top to butter yellow at the horizon in this small, loosely painted oil sketch on paper, about 22 by 33 centimeters. A diagonal band of caramel brown in the foreground could be a felled tree trunk or a hint of forest, and a smoky purple band on the horizon may be distant mountains. Plum-purple clouds edged with pink slant across the golden sky.","i":"Fragonard made many rapid sketches of trees, parks and skies, in Italy, where he drew with companions such as Hubert Robert, and later in France. Such oil sketches were private studies of light and atmosphere, never meant for exhibition, and they show a side of the artist very different from his glossy figure scenes. Many details here are vague, and the subject and title are provisional.","c":"The pleasure of this little sketch is in the sky: the transition from blue to butter yellow and the plum clouds tinted pink are caught with a few sweeps of the brush. The foreground is indistinct and hard to read, and the sketch has no ambition beyond mood. But the freedom of touch anticipates the landscape painting of the next century, and its freshness is striking."},"nga:46303":{"d":"Seen in profile and cropped at the lap, a young woman in a lemon-yellow gown sits propped against a large lilac-and-rose cushion, her head bowed over a small red-edged book. A ruffled white collar frames her neck, mauve ribbons tie up her chestnut hair and trim her bodice, and one arm rests along a railing that crosses the canvas. Behind her is a loose wash of tan and muted teal. The paint goes on in quick, visible strokes: the yellow silk in broad, buttery sweeps, the cushion in flicks of pink and violet, while the face and hand are more delicately modeled. Nothing else is shown beyond cushion and rail, and nothing says who she is.","i":"Around 1769 Fragonard painted a group of rapid, half-length 'figures de fantaisie,' imaginary characters in costume rather than commissioned portraits, and this reader belongs to it. Her name, if she had one, is unknown. Reading women were a popular subject in eighteenth-century France, often with a hint of romance, but here the book is simply the occasion for an absorbed, private moment, which suited the Rococo taste for intimacy and sensation. X-rays are reported to show that the canvas first carried a different head, looking out at the viewer, which Fragonard painted over with this lowered profile. The picture entered the National Gallery of Art in 1961 as a gift of Ailsa Mellon Bruce.","c":"This is Fragonard at his freest, and its appeal is mostly technical: the dress is a few confident planes of yellow and ochre, the cushion nearly abstract, and the paint is left to show how fast it was applied. What the picture gives up is character. The girl is a type, pretty and absorbed but not individual, and the sentiment edges toward the sweet. Set beside his grand, crowded pleasure scenes it is plainer and better for it, a small virtuoso performance whose spontaneity has survived."},"met:436326":{"d":"Two girls are paired in a double portrait, the title calling them simply sisters and giving no names. Fragonard painted it in oil on a canvas of 71.8 by 55.9 centimeters around 1769 to 1770, a size for a private room rather than a public gallery. His handling in these years was quick and fluent, with flesh, hair and drapery laid in with loose, feathery strokes, a spontaneity that suits a subject built on family affection.","i":"Pictures of children in the later eighteenth century often stress feeling over rank, reflecting a taste for sensibility that ran from Rousseau's writing on childhood to the sentimental scenes of Greuze. Whether this canvas portrays two particular sisters or invents a charming pair in the manner of Fragonard's fancy pictures, the title alone does not settle, so the sitters are best treated as unidentified. The appeal lies in the bond itself, the closeness of siblings, which is the register in which Fragonard excelled.","c":"Fragonard's strength in such pictures is speed and warmth: faces and fabrics are suggested rather than itemized, and the paint stays light enough to keep sentiment from setting into sugar. The weakness is the ease of the idea, since affectionate siblings are a stock subject and the picture's interest rests largely on handling. It is a pleasing, mid-weight work among his portraits of children, not a landmark."},"met:436322":{"d":"A young woman is shown with the things that give the picture its title and mood: a love letter, a bouquet, a small dog, and the lace, cap and ribbon of her dress, with a window among the details of the room. Fragonard painted it in oil on canvas, 83.2 by 67 centimeters, in the early 1770s. It is a cabinet picture of private feeling rather than a portrait of a named sitter, and the textures of lace and ribbon are the kind of detail he suggests in a few light strokes.","i":"A woman with a love letter is a favorite motif of eighteenth-century French painting, which turned private correspondence into a subject for public pleasure. Flowers stand for the sender's affection, and a small dog is usually read as a sign of fidelity. The picture dates from the years when Fragonard moved from grand mythological decoration to scenes of feeling for private patrons, and viewers of the time would have enjoyed guessing the story rather than being told it.","c":"What succeeds is economy and warmth: a woman, a letter and a few accessories are enough to suggest a whole romance, and the brushwork keeps the air light. The story is a stock one and the picture risks prettiness, yet it shows why Fragonard's smaller domestic pictures of the 1770s are often preferred to his grander decorative schemes. It is a work of charm and intelligence, not of emotional depth."},"met:436323":{"d":"A middle-aged woman with powdered grey hair, wound with strands of pearls, turns toward the viewer with a smile and bright, rosy cheeks. A great white ruff stands up behind her neck, and she wears two necklaces of pearls and a jewelled brooch. Her gown is heavy silk in stripes of rose, mauve and gold, with a blue-lined cloak edged in white fur around her shoulders. In her arms she holds a small white lapdog, tied with blue ribbons that trail down across her sleeve. The background is a plain grey-green wall with a ledge at the lower left. The surface is quick and fluid, and the lines of the ruff are cut into the wet paint. Oil on canvas, about 81 by 65 centimetres.","i":"This is one of Fragonard's fantasy portraits, brisk and brilliant canvases from about 1769 in which sitters wear costume from the past. Her striped robe with a high standing collar was known in the later eighteenth century as theatrical dress, described at the time as in the Spanish style, and it recalls the court dress of Queen Marie de' Medici as Rubens painted it in a cycle Fragonard could see in the royal collections. A drawing that came to light more recently allows the sitter to be named as Marie Emilie Coignet de Courson, a hostess of an aristocratic salon, then in her early fifties. The Met sees the picture as a kind of self-portrait of the painter's virtuosity.","c":"Fragonard cares less about likeness than about performance, and the performance is dazzling. The ruff is incised into the wet paint, the pink and gold silk is flicked on in a dozen rapid strokes, and the dog is summed up in a few touches. As painting it is a delight. As a portrait it is thinner: the face is cheerful and generic, and the costume steals the picture. Beside the best of the fantasy figures in the Louvre it seems more genial than penetrating, a brilliant display that does not ask to be anything else."},"nga:32685":{"d":"Two women, a man and three small children gather around a baby sleeping in a wicker cradle near an open window. An older woman in a bonnet and apron sits at the left holding a spindle, with a white cat at her feet, while a gray-haired man in an ice-blue coat kneels at the cradle and leans on the arm of a young woman in a broad-brimmed hat. A girl with a ball of yarn, a boy in a yellow jacket and a smaller child stand at the right. Bright light enters from the window, with a dusk-blue sky beyond.","i":"Scenes of family tenderness became popular in the 1760s and 1770s, when writers such as Rousseau praised maternal care and domestic affection and painters like Greuze answered with moralizing pictures of family life. Fragonard's version is gentler and less preachy: a baby's sleep draws relatives and children into a single circle of attention. The cat and the rolling ball of yarn add a note of play.","c":"Fragonard had turned from erotic Rococo fantasies to this sentimental domestic mood, and he brings to it a gift for light, since the bright window lights the figures like a stage. The faces are slightly generic and the sentiment sweet, but the picture is full of lively detail and the spontaneous grouping feels natural. It is more engaging than moralizing, which is a virtue."},"nga:46036":{"d":"In a deeply shadowed room, two women and several children gather at the center of this oval picture, looking toward a young man and a donkey who peer in over a half-door. Light falls on a young woman in profile who holds a baby braced on her lap; she wears an ivory bodice, a black sash and a crimson skirt. Two children rest near her, one with a white dog, and behind them another woman, mostly in silhouette, steadies a child. A fifth child stands at the donkey's head, and the young man smiles from beneath his wide-brimmed hat.","i":"The picture, in the oval format that suited a decorative panel, presents a peasant family as a scene of simple happiness, in line with the late eighteenth-century taste for rustic virtue. The donkey at the door may gently recall the Nativity stable, though the subject is secular. Fragonard painted such tender family scenes in the 1770s, when sensibility and the cult of motherhood were fashionable themes in French art and writing.","c":"The deep shadow and the single beam of light on the mother and child recall Rembrandt, whom Fragonard admired, and the effect is a warm, intimate chiaroscuro. The brushwork is loose and the faces are summary, but the crimson skirt glowing in the dark is a strong accent. The picture is more a mood than a story, and the mood is irresistibly tender."},"nga:33745":{"d":"A winged child floats in mid-air in this oval panel, one leg bent and arms outstretched, smiling gently. The child has curly hair, a round face and rosy cheeks, a cloth draped over one shoulder, and a flaming torch in one hand. White birds fly around against pale blue sky and soft white clouds, and blooming pink flowers and foliage rise from a pale green ground below. The canvas measures about 56 by 46 centimeters.","i":"Love as Folly shows Cupid as a madcap spirit, in keeping with the old idea that love is a kind of madness. The torch is the fire of passion, and the white birds are probably doves, the birds of Venus. In the Rococo spirit the allegory is frivolous rather than dark, a gentle joke about the way desire makes people lose their reason. The panel is oval, as befits a decorative element in a room.","c":"The airy figure, the feathery clouds and the pastel palette are managed with great economy, and the child's floating pose gives the picture buoyancy. It is decorative rather than deep, and some of its charm depends on one's tolerance for Rococo sweetness. Within its modest ambition, it is exquisite."},"nga:33746":{"d":"A winged child stands in a garden with one foot in front of the other, holding a golden arrow. The child has curly blond hair, rosy cheeks and a playful expression, and is nude except for a cloth wrapped around the back and covering the groin. Behind is a light green bush with pink flowers, and in the grass at the lower left lies a dark blue object that looks like a torch or quiver. The sky is pastel blue with soft pink clouds. The canvas measures about 56 by 47 centimeters.","i":"The child is Cupid, the god of love, and the title makes him a sentinel, a guard standing watch with his arrow ready. In the playful logic of the Rococo, Love is the guardian of the garden of pleasure, and he may watch over lovers' secrets or the approach of intruders. The picture is probably one of a group of decorative panels on the theme of Love painted in the mid-1770s, and a second version of this composition is also in the National Gallery.","c":"A confection, and meant to be: the pearly flesh, pink flowers and pastel sky make a harmony of sweetness. The playful expression gives the child personality, and the loose, feathery brushwork is delightful. Its slightness is the risk, for the decorative charm is all. Still, in its small way it shows Fragonard's feeling for light and his gift for making the sentimental persuasive."},"nga:52255":{"d":"An oval panel shows a winged child floating in mid-air with one leg bent and arms outstretched, smiling gently. The curly-haired child has a round face and pale skin dusted with rosy pink and light teal shadows, and is nude but for a cloth draped over one shoulder, with a flaming torch in one hand. White birds circle in a sky of pale blue and soft white clouds, above blooming pink flowers and foliage on a pale green ground. The canvas measures about 56 by 47 centimeters.","i":"This is a second version of Love as Folly in the National Gallery, which shows how popular the design was. The figure is Cupid, whose torch is the flame of passion, and the idea that love is a folly was a commonplace of eighteenth-century verse and theater. Fragonard used such small, decorative allegories to explore the lighter side of love, and their oval shape suggests that they were made to be set into paneling or furniture.","c":"The teal shadows in the flesh give the child a delicate pearly glow, and the loose handling keeps the picture airy. Compared with its twin the effect is much the same, and one might ask which is the finer; the difference is slight. As a Rococo trifle it is perfectly judged, and as anything more it does not pretend."},"nga:52256":{"d":"An oval panel shows a winged child standing in a garden, one foot in front of the other, holding a golden arrow. The child has curly blond hair, rosy cheeks and a playful expression, and is nude except for a cloth around the back. A light green bush with pink flowers stands behind, a dark blue object like a torch or quiver lies in the grass at the lower left, and two white doves fly in a pastel blue sky streaked with pink clouds. The canvas measures about 56 by 47 centimeters.","i":"Doves are the birds of Venus and symbols of love, and in this version two of them fly above the sentinel, a detail that the other version of the composition in the National Gallery lacks. The child is Cupid, posted as a guard in the garden of pleasure, with his arrow ready. The eighteenth century enjoyed such playful personifications of Love, and the oval shape suggests a decorative setting.","c":"The doves add a graceful flutter to a sweet composition, and the pearly flesh against green and pink makes a pleasing harmony. As with its twin, the success is that of a decorative conceit executed with a light, fluent hand. The risk of cloying sweetness is high, but Fragonard keeps the picture buoyant, and the result is delicate rather than sugary."},"met:436327":{"d":"A female nude is seen in profile, absorbed in reading, on a canvas of 68.9 by 54.9 centimeters from about 1772. The title makes it an allegory: the reader's wakeful attention stands for vigilance. With so few motifs, the picture is best understood as a decorative figure piece, in which a single bare figure and its pose carry the idea, the sort of canvas made for a private room rather than a crowded narrative scene.","i":"Vigilance, the alertness that guards against sleep and danger, was a standard personification in the handbooks of allegory, usually given a lamp or book for night study or a crane holding a stone to keep it awake. Here the virtue seems to be carried by reading itself, the wakeful mind at work. Allegories of this kind were commonly painted as decorative pictures for private rooms, where the nude let a moral idea double as a graceful pretext for painting flesh.","c":"With so few attributes the allegory depends on the figure and on the painter's feeling for flesh, and Fragonard's loose, warm touch makes a pleasant job of both. The idea itself is slight, more pretext than program, and the title asks more of the viewer than the picture makes plain. It is a lesser-known work, of interest chiefly for showing how readily the artist turned allegory into sensuous decoration."},"met:436320":{"d":"A young woman is the sole subject of this portrait, and the title gives no name. It is oil on canvas, 80.6 by 63.5 centimeters, painted in the 1770s, a size for a private room. Fragonard painted comparatively few formal portraits, and his portraits and fancy figures of these years share a rapid, fluent handling in which faces are modeled in warm, thin paint and costume is suggested by quick strokes.","i":"Portraits of young women in the 1770s ranged from formal likenesses of rank to informal pictures of an elegant type, and with no name in the title the picture may be read as the second kind. Whether she was a relative, a friend or a professional model cannot be said from the title alone. Viewers of the time valued liveliness and grace in a face, the courtly ideal of effortless spontaneity, more than a catalogue of rank or office.","c":"Fragonard's gift here is vivacity: the aim is a face caught with the immediacy of a glance, in paint kept thin and quick. Without a name, a prop or a story the picture rests on charm alone, and it does not challenge the conventions of the genre. It is an agreeable example of his portrait manner rather than a high point; the fancy figures of the late 1760s and early 1770s show more daring."},"met:436319":{"d":"A garden of trees with a waterfall at its heart fills a small oil on wood panel, only 29.2 by 24.1 centimeters, painted about 1775. The upright format and tiny scale make it an object for close, private viewing. The subject recalls the Italian villa gardens Fragonard drew in his youth, and the panel matches A Shaded Avenue in size, support and date, so that the two read as companions, one of tumbling water, the other of quiet shade.","i":"Fragonard drew the gardens of Tivoli in 1760 beside Hubert Robert and the Abbé de Saint-Non, and he returned to Italy in 1773 to 1774. Garden cascades such as those at the Villa d'Este stayed with him as a source of fantasy for decades. A panel like this probably does not record one place so much as an idea of garden pleasure, wild nature tamed and shaded for leisure, which was the poetic climate of the French Rococo.","c":"The attraction is looseness: on a wooden panel the paint can flicker across the surface, and foliage and spray are indicated by touch rather than description. These small garden pictures are freer and more inventive than many of the careful views of his contemporaries and look forward to the sketch-like landscapes of the nineteenth century. Their limit is the very informality; they read as notes rather than finished statements."},"met:436324":{"d":"A shaded garden walk is the subject of this small panel, 29.2 by 24.1 centimeters, painted in oil on wood about 1775. Trees and a garden are the whole cast, and the title promises an avenue closed in by foliage, with the pleasure lying in shade and depth. It matches The Cascade in size, support and date, which suggests that the two were conceived together as a pair of garden fantasies.","i":"Tree-lined allées were the backbone of the formal French and Italian garden, made for strolling, conversation and assignations, and Fragonard used garden walks as stages for lovers in The Swing and The Progress of Love. If, as the recorded motifs suggest, this avenue is empty of figures, the subject is the place itself: shade, depth and rustling leaves. Viewers schooled in the poetry of gardens would have read it as an invitation to wander.","c":"The panel shows Fragonard at his most spontaneous, turning a quiet motif into a play of dark and light with brisk handling. Its small scale and loose finish limit its ambition, and it cannot match the sustained invention of his large garden scenes; yet in its economy it is a charming exercise, a reminder that the painter of The Swing also looked closely at the structure of trees."},"nga:32683":{"d":"Filtered light falls through a lush, tree-lined park onto about a dozen people lounging and frolicking on the banks of a river in this vertical canvas of about 115 by 88 centimeters. A man in shell-pink and a woman in a topaz-blue dress and rose-pink bonnet sit near each other at the lower left, while younger boys pile up in a game in which one tries to ride on another's back. Tall aspens with greenish-white trunks and golden leaves line the placid river, and couples board a long, narrow boat in the distance.","i":"The game of horse and rider, in which one player is carried or leapt upon by another, was a boisterous amusement for boys, and here it enlivens a scene of leisure for adults. The couples boarding a boat recall the theme of departure for a pleasure excursion, familiar from Watteau's fêtes galantes. Fragonard turns an ordinary day by a river into a vision of ease, in which nothing more is at stake than pleasure and light.","c":"The golden aspens and the silvery light give the picture a shimmering unity, and the tall trees dominate the composition while the figures are subsidiary. The airy, unstudied arrangement makes the scene feel overheard rather than staged. The vagueness of the figures is a limit, but as a display of Fragonard's late feeling for light and foliage it is persuasive."},"nga:32684":{"d":"About a dozen elegantly dressed men and women gather in a lush park framed by tall, pale green trees in this vertical canvas of about 116 by 92 centimeters. Nine of them cluster on a stone terrace at the right, where a man in butter yellow kneels before a woman in rose pink, while others stand, kneel, sit or recline around them. At the left a man and woman sit apart on a bench, and red flower beds and statues mark the lower corners. The path recedes into a hazy blue distance.","i":"Hot cockles, in French la main chaude, was a game in which a player kneels with his head hidden in another's lap and holds a hand behind his back, to be slapped by the others and guess who struck. It allowed teasing and flirtation, and it was a favorite pastime of fashionable society. Fragonard's picture belongs to the tradition of the fête galante, the elegant outdoor party made famous by Watteau, here with a lighter, more spontaneous touch.","c":"The greens, gold and blue of the park make a unified harmony, and the figures are small, quick touches that melt into the foliage. Fragonard cares more about atmosphere than about anecdote, and the vagueness of the faces keeps the game anonymous. It shows the freedom of his late manner, though the lightness can border on emptiness: this is a picture about pleasure, not about meaning."},"nga:46115":{"d":"A large canvas of about 216 by 198 centimeters takes its subject from blindman's buff, the game in which a blindfolded player gropes for others who tease and dodge him. The scale is that of a decorative wall panel, made to hang in a salon rather than a cabinet, and the playful subject belongs to the same family of garden amusements as The Swing and A Game of Hot Cockles in the National Gallery. It was painted around 1775 to 1780.","i":"Parlor and garden games, with their blindfolds, slaps and chases, were a pretext for flirtation in eighteenth-century France, allowing young people to touch and tease within polite limits. Blindman's buff was an old favorite, and the blindfold had a symbolic echo, since Love himself was traditionally blind. Fragonard turned the amusement into a decorative scene for a rich patron who wished to see an ideal world of play.","c":"Fragonard's late garden scenes are among his freest paintings, with figures dissolving into foliage and light flickering across the surface, and the large scale gives the theme a theatrical grandeur. Their charm is atmosphere, their weakness vagueness of drawing and thinness of content. The picture is best understood as decoration of an extremely high order, intended to delight rather than to say anything."},"nga:46116":{"d":"A large canvas of about 216 by 186 centimeters takes the swing as its subject, painted around 1775 to 1780. The scale is that of a decorative wall panel, and the picture belongs to a group of garden scenes in the National Gallery that includes Blindman's Buff and A Game of Hot Cockles. The swing is a motif of play and pleasure, and the best-known version of Fragonard's Swing, of about 1767, is in the Wallace Collection in London.","i":"The swing was a favorite emblem of flirtation in the Rococo: a woman moving through the air, her skirts flying, and an admirer watching, with the sense of daring and freedom the motion implies. The ropes and the idle leisure of the setting stand for a world of aristocratic pleasure. This later and larger version is a new treatment of the theme, showing that Fragonard returned to the motif and to the lighthearted subject that made his fame.","c":"Fragonard's gift is to make motion and light inseparable, and the swing is an ideal vehicle for it: the figure seems to float in a haze of foliage. On a large canvas the effect can be sumptuous, though also vague and slightly empty; the pleasure lies in atmosphere and not in a story. The painting is a late echo of the Rococo, appealing as decoration and best enjoyed for the lightness of its touch."},"cma:122166":{"d":"A boy in a cloak lined with red is the subject of this tiny oil on wood panel, only 20.8 by 16 centimeters, painted in the 1780s. The Cleveland Museum notes that the seventeenth-century costume, the brown palette and the technique suggest Fragonard was reflecting on Rubens and Rembrandt rather than on his teacher François Boucher. The picture sits between portrait and costume study, and its warm brown tonality gives it the look of an old-master homage.","i":"The museum records considerable evidence that the sitter is Alexandre-Évariste Fragonard, the artist's son, though the identification has never been confirmed. Born in 1780, the boy later became a painter himself, so a child of this age fits a date in the 1780s. Dressing a child in seventeenth-century costume belonged to a taste for Flemish and Dutch pictures that Fragonard shared with the collectors of his day.","c":"Small in size and plain in means, this is a quiet experiment more than a showpiece: brown tones, a bold red lining and a youthful face are enough to evoke Rembrandt's warmth and Rubens's easy flesh. The homage is affectionate rather than deep, and the sitter's identity is unsettled. Still, it shows a side of Fragonard far from Rococo frivolity, a painter free with the old masters and tender toward a child, whatever the sitter's name."},"met:436990":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, 73.7 by 46.7 centimeters, painted in oil on fir about 1470, shows the martyrdom of Saint Lawrence against a gold ground, the shimmering background that late Gothic altarpieces used to set holy figures apart from the everyday world. The panel is painted on both sides: the reverse, Giving Drink to the Thirsty, belongs to the cycle of the acts of mercy that probably gave the anonymous master his name. The shape and double-sided paint suggest a wing from a larger altarpiece.","i":"Lawrence, a deacon of the Roman church, was put to death in 258 under the emperor Valerian; the legend says he was roasted on a gridiron, which became his attribute. As the deacon in charge of the church's alms he was also a patron of charity, which makes the reverse a fitting partner: giving drink to the thirsty is one of the acts of mercy listed in Matthew 25 as the test of a Christian life. The pairing probably reflects a program of charity.","c":"Known only through a group of paintings, the Master of the Acts of Mercy worked in the late Gothic idiom of the Austrian lands: gold ground, expressive suffering and a taste for blunt narrative. Its strength is directness; the likely weakness is stiffness in space and anatomy compared with the Flemish and Italian painting of the same years. As evidence of devotional painting in the Austrian lands around 1470 it is worth more than as an individual achievement."},"nga:178096":{"d":"A young girl in a pale green dress and black shoes sits on the edge of a wooden chair, watching her pets: two reddish-brown hamsters or gerbils in a small cage, a goldfish and a smaller gray fish in a bowl on top of it, a gray kitten with ears perked and one paw raised, and a green bird with a blue head on a tall stand among potted plants. The room is worn, its gray plaster flaking to red, with framed pictures and pale light from a window. It is oil on academy board, mounted on hardboard.","i":"A child absorbed in her animals is a domestic subject that grew popular in mid-nineteenth-century America, when genre painting offered stories of everyday life that city and country viewers alike could read. The kitten eyeing the fish adds a note of gentle suspense, and the cage, bowl and perch stack the animals into a small world above the girl. The peeling plaster and plain furnishings point to a modest household. Johnson painted it in 1856, soon after his training in Düsseldorf and The Hague.","c":"The picture is typical of Johnson's early work: carefully observed, warm in feeling and Dutch in its attention to the texture of plaster, straw and glass. The crowded arrangement is somewhat contrived and the sentiment is gentle to a fault, yet the small drama of the kitten and the fish gives it life. It lacks the freedom of his later outdoor sketches, but it shows the skill that made him one of America's leading genre painters."},"cma:141936":{"d":"Genio Scott, a gentleman of means, appears in an informal but stylish robe in his New York residence. The room is arranged around his passion: two fishing rods lean against the wall, three tied flies lie on the table, and a fourth is held in his hand, ready to be placed in his leather-bound fishing wallet. The canvas is a generous horizontal, about 102 by 127 centimetres, and shows the sitter at ease at home rather than at work or in formal dress.","i":"Scott (1806–1879) had a successful career as a women's fashion illustrator and magazine publisher, but his true passion was fly-fishing, about which he wrote for sporting magazines. He lived to see American fish populations decline, a loss largely blamed on industrial pollution, and used his writing to protest and to argue for restoring them. His detailed accounts of fish distribution still help conservationists. Eastman Johnson painted him as a gentleman angler rather than a businessman.","c":"A relaxed, characterful portrait: by showing the sitter with the tools of his hobby, Johnson says more about the man than a formal pose could, and the casual robe and neat still life of flies and rods give the picture a warm, lived-in air. The composition is a little crowded, and the polished details compete with the face. It is one of Johnson's more engaging commissions, a portrait of a man defined by a passion."},"met:11258":{"d":"A family is gathered in a comfortable interior at Christmas, as the title tells, in a domestic scene on a canvas about 76 by 64 centimetres. The museum's record lists a man, a woman and children among the figures but gives no further detail, so their poses, gifts and furnishings cannot be reported with confidence. The picture is at once a family portrait of the Blodgetts, a prominent New York family, and a genre scene of the new, sentimental Victorian celebration of Christmas.","i":"Christmas in the 1860s was becoming the family festival that it still is, with gifts, trees and children at its centre, and American painters and illustrators took the theme up with enthusiasm. The Blodgetts are probably the family of William T. Blodgett, one of the founders of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, as Eastman Johnson was. Painted in 1864, in the fourth year of the Civil War, it gives the family an image of private peace.","c":"A warm, polite family picture of a type Johnson did well: the Christmas subject invites sentiment, and his gift as a draughtsman keeps it from tipping into sugar. Without being able to judge its details, one can say that it belongs to the American tradition of the domestic genre scene, in which a family portrait becomes a small narrative of ritual and affection. It is more valuable as a record of Victorian Christmas customs than as a feat of painting."},"nga:140910":{"d":"A woman crouches on a fallen log at the edge of a pond, leaning over to reach into the water with her right hand while her left holds a cluster of water lilies. She wears a long gray dress with a striped brown apron and a white cloth tied at the neck, and her dark hair is pinned up; her face, turned down toward the water, is mostly hidden. The brown water is speckled with lily pads, reeds and white blossoms and reflects her figure and a patch of blue sky, with dark green foliage behind.","i":"Gathering flowers was a quiet seasonal pastime and a favorite subject for American painters after the Civil War, when country leisure and rural nostalgia drew city viewers toward unspoiled places. The pose, with the woman bent and her face hidden, makes her part of the pond rather than a portrait, a note of anonymity that gives the picture its mood. The white water lily, a traditional sign of purity, may add a gentle overtone, though the picture reads first as a plain moment of country life.","c":"Painted on board, this reads as a painter's response to something seen: the brown water, the reflections and the awkward crouching figure feel observed rather than staged, and Johnson's loose handling suits the subject. The color is subdued, even muddy, and with the face hidden there is little emotional focus. It lacks the polish of his finished genre scenes but has a naturalness that they sometimes lose."},"nga:46632":{"d":"A boy sits in a wooden rocking chair beside a dark metal stove, leaning forward with one arm stretched toward the partly open door, where bright orange firelight glows. He wears a dark cap, a brown shirt and light trousers, and his face is half turned away, fixed on the fire. Firewood is scattered over the floor, the walls are plain gray, and rows of wooden desks at the right show that the room is a schoolhouse. The painting is oil on academy board mounted on canvas.","i":"The title casts the boy as the first pupil to arrive, tending the stove before lessons begin, a small act of responsibility in a rural school, where pupils often took turns keeping the fire. Painted about 1865, at the end of the Civil War, it belongs to Johnson's scenes of country life, which presented the schoolhouse as a place of humble virtue and self-reliance. The single glowing stove focuses the picture on warmth, effort and the quiet start of the day.","c":"Johnson's sureness shows in the use of one source of light for a whole mood: the orange glow in the stove door anchors a composition of grays and browns. It is deliberately plain, and it prefers observation to sentimental anecdote. The board support and modest scale give it an intimacy that larger finished school scenes of the day often lose in explaining themselves. It is a small, truthful picture, unshowy and well judged."},"cma:173173":{"d":"A tiny canvas, about 24 by 19 centimetres, shows a boy fishing. The museum gives no description beyond the title, so his pose, clothes and the water or bank he stands on cannot be described with confidence. Pictures of boys fishing were a popular subject in nineteenth-century American painting, linking childhood with the open air; at this small scale it is probably a sketch or a small cabinet picture of the 1860s.","i":"Fishing boys stood for an idealised American boyhood of freedom and nature, a theme in the writing of the time, and artists such as Eastman Johnson and Winslow Homer painted it often. For Johnson, who made his reputation with scenes of ordinary American life, a boy fishing was a gentle variation on his genre subjects. The date is given only as the 1860s, and the picture is a small, personal one.","c":"A small picture should not be asked to bear much weight, and the honest verdict is that it is a pleasant, unpretentious piece. Johnson was a fluent, sympathetic painter of children and rustic scenes, and a sketch like this would show his easy handling and his affection for the subject. Without further detail it is best taken as a minor but characteristic work, a glimpse of boyhood rather than a statement."},"nga:56720":{"d":"Three people occupy a comfortable room of red curtains, white paneled walls, rich brown furniture and an ornate white fireplace. An elderly man in a black suit and round spectacles sits on a wooden chair reading a newspaper; beside him stands a child in a bright blue coat and hat, back to the viewer, with blond hair in soft ringlets. The man looks down at the child with a slight smile, while at the right a woman in a black dress, seated and knitting, looks up at them.","i":"A family group shown in the middle of everyday activity was a staple of mid-nineteenth-century American painting: instead of formal poses, the sitters read, knit and talk, which flatters both their ease and their affection. Johnson, by 1869 an established New York portrait and genre painter, specialized in such scenes. The title identifies the sitters only as the Brown family, and the picture speaks for a household of leisure and comfort, with age and childhood placed in gentle conversation.","c":"Johnson's gift for informal portraiture is evident: the figures turn toward one another rather than toward the viewer, and the red curtains and dark clothes build a rich, cohesive interior. The support, oil on paper laid down on canvas, hints at speed, and the child's turned back is only lightly suggested. The result is warm and credible, if somewhat flat in space, and it shows why he was so valued as a painter of the American home."},"nga:195123":{"d":"Two boys shelter in the hollowed base of an enormous tree, one of them apparently reading a piece of paper. They wear hats and warm clothing; to the left a small box and a wooden barrel on a sled stand in the snow. The ground is white with brown patches, small leafless trees fill the middle distance, and blue mountains and tiny gray and red houses lie far off at the upper right beneath a pale blue sky. It is oil on academy board, about 59 by 69 centimeters.","i":"The title says the boys are truants, children who have slipped away from school or errands, and the hollow of the tree makes a natural den for a stolen hour. The sled and barrel suggest they were sent on a task and have stopped to idle. Truancy was a popular theme in nineteenth-century genre painting, and Johnson treats it gently, as the harmless mischief of childhood in a snowy countryside, without a trace of moral lecture.","c":"Johnson makes the tree the real subject: its vast, hollowed trunk dwarfs the boys and gives a small genre scene an unexpected monumentality. The winter palette of pale blue, brown and gray is handled with restraint, and the distant village adds depth. The narrative is slight and leans on the title, but the sympathy for its truants, free of moralizing, is characteristic of Johnson at his best."},"met:11263":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 122 by 186 centimetres, shows a family together in a well-furnished interior. The museum lists a man, a woman, a boy and a girl among the figures, with reading, needlework and toys as their occupations, so the adults presumably sit at their own pursuits while the children play. The record gives no more detail, so the exact arrangement and colours cannot be described with confidence. The size and ambition mark it as a major group portrait, not a casual sketch.","i":"The Hatch Family is among Eastman Johnson's best-known group portraits, showing a prosperous New York family at leisure in their home in 1870–71. Domestic harmony, with reading, needlework and play, was the ideal of the Victorian middle class, and the picture presents it as the visible reward of success. Johnson, who had trained in Düsseldorf and The Hague, brought to the conversation piece a Dutch attention to interiors. Frederic H. Hatch gave it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1926.","c":"A large, accomplished group portrait, in which a crowded room is organised so that each member has a place and an occupation, and the blend of portrait and genre scene gives it warmth. Such pictures also flatter: the comfortable harmony and rich furnishings serve the patron more than the critic. Among Johnson's works it is a handsome, assured document of Gilded Age family life."},"cma:173167":{"d":"Set in a rustic kitchen on Nantucket Island, Massachusetts, a woman winds a ball of yarn from a coil looped over the hands of a man who sits across from her at a respectable distance. A second woman, likely a chaperone, is also present. The suitor sits in an unrefined, open-legged pose, and he has put his hat on the floor, a small breach of manners. The picture is oil on board, about 38 by 55 centimetres.","i":"Winding yarn was a common symbol of courtship, with a humorous suggestion that the woman was ensnaring her suitor in the skein. The chaperone, the careful distance between the couple and the suitor's clumsy manners are the stuff of the comedy that nineteenth-century audiences would have enjoyed. Johnson spent summers on Nantucket in the 1870s, where he found picturesque subjects in the island's old-fashioned life, and he was a cofounder of the Metropolitan Museum of Art.","c":"A good-humoured, well-observed little picture: the open-legged suitor and the amused woman with the yarn are comic types, but they are drawn with enough individuality to avoid caricature, and the plain kitchen gives them a convincing home. The composition is simple, the narrative clear, and the mild joke is told without strain. It is a minor work in scale, but a good example of how Johnson used everyday customs to tell small stories."},"nga:140909":{"d":"In a dense, snowy forest of tall, leafless trees, two men haul a sled loaded with barrels along a path toward the right, while a small child sits on top of the load holding a wooden bucket. The men wear vests, hats and long trousers in blue, brown, black, red and tan; the child has brown trousers, a green jacket, a white scarf and a blue hat. Pale gray sky shows between the branches, and a small wooden structure with a faint orange fire is just visible behind them.","i":"The barrels, the bucket and the distant fire suggest a maple sugar camp, where sap was gathered in late winter and boiled down in the woods, a subject Johnson returned to again and again. The scene preserved a rural ritual that already seemed a memory of an older, simpler America after the Civil War, and the child riding the load gives it warmth and a touch of humor. Viewers could read it as a record of shared family labor.","c":"A small, fresh record of work in the woods, with the long verticals of the trunks closing the space and the figures kept small. Johnson's color is quiet and his drawing sure, and the camp itself is wisely left half hidden. The board support and modest size give it the immediacy of a study; the figures are somewhat generic, but the whole is convincing and free of anecdote."},"nga:195019":{"d":"A woman stands in front of a wooden dresser with a mirror, her back to the viewer, while her face appears in the glass, tilted slightly as both hands rise toward it. Her dark hair is neatly styled and she wears a dark gown with white ruffles around the neck. On the dresser stand a floral arrangement, a pair of blue bottles and a wooden box of small containers; behind are patterned wallpaper, a blue curtain at the left and sunlight through a window at the right, with a patterned rug on the floor.","i":"A woman at her mirror is one of the oldest subjects in European painting, once a warning against vanity, but by the nineteenth century the dressing-table scene had become an observational one, prized for its glimpse of a woman's private ritual. Painting in 1873, Johnson offers no moral: the interest lies in the double view of back and reflection and in the small still life of bottles and flowers. The title uses toilet in its old sense of dressing and grooming.","c":"The mirror lets Johnson show back and face together, and the blue bottles and sunlit wallpaper give a bright note against the dark gown. It is competent and engaging rather than daring, and its patient attention to objects recalls the Dutch interiors he studied at The Hague. The viewer stands outside the scene, looking in, and the picture has little psychological depth, but it succeeds as a graceful domestic study."},"nga:157924":{"d":"A woman stands outdoors on a grassy field, facing left, her face in shadow beneath a wide, flat white hat with a red stripe, tied with a black bow. She wears a long black dress with a fitted bodice, a bustle and lacy trim, tan gloves and a red bag at her waist, and holds a small rectangular object that could be a bag or a closed book. A small white lamb drinks from a saucer at lower left, more sheep graze at right, and white sailboats dot the calm water beyond under a pale cloudy sky.","i":"The title places the scene on Nantucket, where Johnson spent many summers from the 1870s. A stylish woman feeding a lamb from a saucer joins modern dress to a pastoral motif: the shepherdess of older art reappears as a summer resident in bustle and gloves. The gentle contrast between fashion and tender care for a young animal was the kind of mild charm that American viewers of 1874 enjoyed, and it reflects the summer visitors who were beginning to make the island a resort.","c":"Johnson paints fashion with unusual candor: the black bustle dress, hat and gloves are described with a sharp eye, and the shadowed face keeps the woman anonymous, a type rather than a portrait. The pale sky and distant boats are lightly handled. The lamb is a stagey prop, but the summery freshness and the unforced contrast between town clothes and rural pasture make it one of his more engaging late works."},"met:11259":{"d":"A crowd of people gathers for a corn husking on Nantucket, in a wide canvas about 70 by 138 centimetres. The museum lists only a crowd and a genre scene, so the setting, numbers and lighting cannot be described with confidence. The long horizontal format suits a communal gathering, in which neighbours of all ages sit around heaps of corn husking the ears for storage, telling stories and flirting as they work.","i":"Corn husking bees were rural social events, at which neighbours helped one another with a tedious task and turned it into a party; by tradition, the finder of a red ear might claim a kiss. By the 1870s the old rural customs were fading as the country industrialised, and painters like Johnson recorded them with nostalgia. Johnson spent summers on Nantucket from the 1870s, finding in its old-fashioned farm and fishing life a vanishing America.","c":"An ambitious crowd scene, in which the difficulty is to give each of many figures life without losing the whole, and Johnson, a practised composer of groups, is equal to it. Without close detail to judge, one can say that the wide format and communal subject place it among his best-known themes. The risk is a decorative nostalgia, in which rural labour becomes a party, but the observation of manners keeps it honest."},"met:11265":{"d":"A woman and a man share a rustic interior with a fireplace, and a new bonnet is the centre of attention, as the title and the record of hats and bonnets suggest. The museum also lists andirons, an axe and a candlestick among the objects, details that give the room its old-fashioned, countrified character. The picture is on stiff academy board, about 53 by 69 centimetres, and the poses and expressions cannot be described with confidence.","i":"A new bonnet is a small domestic event, and a favourite excuse for gentle comedy about vanity, thrift and the relations between husband and wife; Johnson's rural interiors of the 1870s, many set on Nantucket, are full of such moments. The old hearth, axe and candlestick suggest a past age of simple living that urban audiences of the Centennial year, 1876, were nostalgic for. It reached the Met in 1900 as a bequest of the railroad magnate Collis P. Huntington.","c":"A small, genial picture in which the humour lies in observation rather than invention. Johnson's rural interiors give old furniture and tools a quiet dignity, and the hearth, axe and candlestick are the right props for his gently nostalgic mood. Without the details to judge the handling, one can say only that it is lively but slight. As a piece of Centennial-year nostalgia it works well."},"met:11267":{"d":"A portrait of the landscape painter Sanford Robinson Gifford, in oil on academy board, about 68 by 56 centimetres, dated 1880. The museum's record lists only the sitter and the type, so his pose, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. Painted by a friend and fellow member of New York's artistic community, it is a portrait of one painter by another.","i":"Gifford (1823–1880) was one of the leading painters of the Hudson River School, known for hazy, golden effects of light, and a founder of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, as Johnson was. He died in 1880, the year of the portrait, and Richard Butler gave the picture to the Museum in 1888. Portraits of fellow artists were a way of recording friendship and a community of New York painters at work in the studio buildings of the period.","c":"A portrait of a friend by a skilled draughtsman, in which Johnson's ability to catch a likeness in oil on board shows in an unfussy, serious head. Judged without the details to hand, the safe verdict is that such a portrait works by directness: little decoration, a good grasp of character, and the sympathy of one artist for another. It is also a document of the New York art world of the Met's founding generation."},"met:11261":{"d":"A large canvas, about 154 by 199 centimetres, takes its title from a financial and political event, the Funding Bill of 1881, by which Congress refinanced part of the national debt. The museum's record lists men and a portrait, so the figures are probably a group of sitters. Beyond that, the number of figures, their poses and the room cannot be described with confidence.","i":"In the Gilded Age, businessmen and politicians were fascinated by public finance, and a group portrait built around a current bill allowed Johnson to combine portraiture with a scene of men in conversation. The date of 1881 matches the passage of the real legislation, so the picture may catch the moment when the news was fresh. The Metropolitan Museum purchased it in 1898.","c":"A big, ambitious canvas whose subject, a congressional financial measure, is unusual for American painting of its day, and whose success depends on giving a group of men in dark suits some drama. Without the details to judge the handling, it is fair to say that such pictures risk dryness; their value lies in recording the world of Gilded Age finance and in Johnson's readiness to paint modern public life."},"nga:195628":{"d":"A full-length portrait of a standing woman, named in the title, fills a tall canvas 182.9 by 95.2 centimeters, six feet high, painted in oil in 1881. The scale is that of a formal society portrait, the kind of grand likeness that announced a sitter's standing in the house. Eastman Johnson, by 1881 one of the leading portrait painters in New York, brought to such commissions the plain, observing manner of his genre scenes.","i":"Harriet Hubbard Ayer (1849 to 1903) is remembered as a pioneer of the American cosmetics business and later a newspaper beauty columnist, but at the time of this portrait she was a society figure in her early thirties. A full-length portrait was a statement of standing and personal style, and the sitter's later fame as a businesswoman gives the picture an extra interest that it could not have had on completion.","c":"Society portraits at this scale test a painter's ability to hold a single figure on a tall canvas, and Johnson's strengths, sober observation and tactful likeness, suit modest effects better than dazzling ones. Set beside the full-lengths of Sargent or Whistler from the same decade, his work is reserved and a little cool. It is solid, dignified portraiture, valuable as a document of the sitter as much as for its painting."},"met:11264":{"d":"A boy stands for his portrait in this large vertical canvas, about 137 by 102 centimetres, dated 1882. The museum's record names the sitter, Lewis Einstein, and lists only a boy and a portrait, so his costume, pose and setting cannot be described with confidence. At that size the picture was a formal commission, the kind of portrait a wealthy New York family ordered of a child to hang in the house.","i":"Lewis Einstein was born in 1877, so he was about five when he was painted; he later became a diplomat and writer, serving as American minister to Czechoslovakia and writing books on history. He gave the portrait to the Metropolitan Museum himself in 1947, when he was seventy. In the 1880s children of prosperous families were dressed in elaborate costume, and portraits of them mixed a record of the child with a display of family taste.","c":"A formal child portrait by a painter much used by wealthy New Yorkers. At this size it seems likely to be dignified, with the child treated almost as a small adult, and Johnson's gift for a sympathetic likeness would suit the task. Portraits of children in this period are often stiff in pose and sweet in expression, and part of the interest here is the later career of the sitter, who gave the picture to the Met himself."},"nga:34111":{"d":"An older man is shown from the shoulders up, facing slightly to the right with his gaze directed off to one side. He has a large, bushy beard mixing white and reddish tones and a receding hairline with short, thinning hair, and he wears a dark suit jacket against a dark background that lets his face and beard stand out. No jewelry or objects are visible; the portrait is a plain bust-length likeness in oil on canvas, 69.2 by 56.5 centimeters, painted about 1885.","i":"The sitter was probably a member of the Harper family of New York publishers, whose firm Harper and Brothers issued Harper's Magazine and Harper's Weekly. The sober dress, dark ground and absence of props signal a man of business and character rather than display, in keeping with American taste for portraits that suggest reliability. Johnson painted many well-to-do New Yorkers in the 1880s, and this likeness belongs to that late phase of his career.","c":"The portrait is direct and unadorned: the bushy beard and thinning hair are painted without flattery, and the dark background concentrates attention on the face. Its strength is candor; its limit is the absence of anything beyond appearance, no gesture, prop or setting to enlarge the sitter's character. It is a sound example of the portrait manner that kept Johnson in demand with the city's business elite, more honest than inspired."},"cma:147505":{"d":"A small oil on board, about 25 by 18 centimetres, shows a portrait of Franklin H. Delano. The museum gives no description, so his pose, dress and expression cannot be reported with confidence. The size, and the board support, suggest an informal study or a small portrait made for family and friends, not a formal commission; Eastman Johnson, the painter, made head-and-shoulders likenesses as well as the genre scenes for which he is better known.","i":"Eastman Johnson was the portraitist of choice for many prominent Americans of the Gilded Age, and he often made small oil sketches from life alongside his larger portraits. The title records the sitter's name but little else, and the date is given as about 1881. Like many of Johnson's small portraits it was probably made as a personal record of a friend or acquaintance.","c":"A small, direct portrait sketch, valuable more for its sympathy and economy than for finish: at this size an artist like Johnson can concentrate on the head and leave the rest to a few strokes, and the best of his small portraits convey character with a sureness that larger commissions sometimes lose. Without more information it is best judged as a likable, minor work by a major portraitist."},"met:11262":{"d":"A small oil sketch on cardboard, about 27 by 22 centimetres, shows Grover Cleveland, who served two non-consecutive terms as president. The museum lists the sitter and the type but gives no further description, so his pose, dress and the handling of paint cannot be reported with confidence. By 1891 Cleveland had left the presidency after his first term and was a lawyer in New York, soon to be a candidate again.","i":"Cleveland (1837–1908) served as the twenty-second and twenty-fourth president, in 1885–89 and 1893–97. Eastman Johnson painted him during the years in between, when Cleveland lived in New York and remained popular with the Democratic party. A small cardboard sketch of this kind may be a study from life, made quickly for the artist's own use or as a basis for a larger portrait. The museum's date, by 1891, is cautious.","c":"A small sketch can show a portraitist's gifts more candidly than a finished commission, and Johnson, a fluent and sympathetic draughtsman, was well suited to the task. Without detail to examine, one can say only that its interest is largely historical: an informal likeness of a president out of office and soon to return, painted without ceremony. A valuable minor document rather than a major picture."},"nga:159063":{"d":"A muscular, red-haired Evangelist sits twisting toward an open book, his torso turned one way and his head, in near profile, the other, brows deeply furrowed. His tunic shimmers in topaz blue and pearly white under a carnation-pink cloak pinned at the shoulder with a gold brooch; one hand braces an inkpot and quill while the other holds a pair of pince-nez spectacles. A small winged lion with a curly mane rests at his feet against a dark ground. The oil on panel stands nearly six feet tall.","i":"Mark is known by the winged lion, his emblem among the four Evangelists drawn from the visions of Ezekiel and Revelation. The Latin on the open page begins Angelus descendit, an angel descended, and the pen, ink and spectacles mark him as a writer at work. Dated 1570 to 1571, the panel matches Saint Luke in size and date, which suggests that the two belonged to a larger set, probably the four Evangelists.","c":"The pose is a showpiece of Mannerist contrapposto: twisting torso, turned head, deep brow. The shifting, iridescent color in the drapery, in the manner of Michelangelo, is the picture's chief pleasure, and the small lion and the pince-nez rescue it from solemnity. Vasari is better remembered for his Lives of the Artists than for his paintings, and this panel shows why: fluent, intelligent and decorative, but a little cool beside the models he admired."},"nga:159064":{"d":"A hunched man draws on a tablet with a feather quill, one leg pulled up to brace it, while a gray bull, about half his size, rests at his feet. His forehead is lined, his hair lanky and his beard curly; his tunic shifts from plum purple to moss green, with sky-blue and laurel-green sleeves and a golden yellow robe wrapped around his lap. The floor is pale mauve and the background a green so dark it is almost black, except for a small picture of a woman holding a baby glimpsed at the upper left.","i":"Luke is known by the ox and, above all, by his role as painter: medieval tradition said he made the first portrait of the Virgin, and the small Madonna and Child at the upper corner alludes to it. The legend made him patron saint of painters, and Vasari, who helped found the Florentine Accademia delle Arti del Disegno in 1563, had special reason to honor him. Matching Saint Mark in size and date, the panel was probably part of a set of four Evangelists.","c":"Where Mark twists and struts, Luke is bent over his work, and Vasari's contrast of the two poses shows his skill at varying figures within a set. The color, plum to moss and gold against a near-black ground, is rich, though the cramped, knotted pose is less persuasive than the open pose of Mark. The Madonna in the corner is a nice self-referential touch for a painter and writer. Overall it is polished but academic Mannerism."},"met:437895":{"d":"A man and a woman meet in a garden, with a cat in the scene, on a wide canvas of 51.8 by 83.8 centimeters painted in 1874. The title promises a scolding, a small comedy of manners played out in the open air. Vibert, a Parisian academic painter, was famous for such staged anecdotes, in which costumed figures are arranged among carefully chosen props like actors on a stage, and the finish is smooth, bright and precise.","i":"Vibert (1840 to 1902) built his reputation on comic scenes in which clergy and gentlemen in picturesque costume are caught in embarrassing or amusing situations, and he sold them to an international market of collectors, above all in the United States. A reprimand belongs to this family of pictures: the humor lies in the gap between dignity and fallibility. A cat among the actors is a traditional sign of domestic mischief, and viewers would have enjoyed supplying the dialogue themselves.","c":"Vibert's craft is undeniable: smooth, brilliant surfaces and precise accessories make every figure and prop legible at a glance. The weakness is the same polish, which turns wit into formula; the joke is delivered rather than discovered, and little remains once the anecdote is told. Among the academic storytellers of the early Third Republic he is clever and entertaining, valued now more as a document of taste than as a painter's painter."},"nga:178262":{"d":"Men occupy an outdoor setting in a small canvas of 51.8 by 63.8 centimeters, painted in the late 1870s, and the title names them naturalists, observers who watch, collect and classify the natural world. The subject is typical of Vibert, who staged small comedies of learned or clerical men, and the picture's appeal lies in its bright, enamel-smooth finish and the humor of earnest looking rather than in any dramatic action.","i":"The later nineteenth century was an age of amateur science: collecting insects, plants and fossils, and reading Darwin, were fashionable pursuits, and the earnest specimen-hunter became a familiar comic type. Vibert, who later wrote a treatise on the science of painting, liked to show the learned and the pious at their little enthusiasms. Viewers of the day would have enjoyed the mild joke of grown men absorbed in small things, and the title lets them supply the quarry.","c":"The polish of Vibert's technique is the main pleasure: precise drawing, crisp textures and brilliant color on a small scale. Seen without its title, however, the subject is thin; the humor lives in the idea, not the paint, and the type recurs throughout his work. It is skillful, light entertainment, typical of the dealer market for Parisian anecdote pictures, and it is more likely to amuse than to linger in the mind."},"met:437896":{"d":"Churchmen, among them a bishop and a monk, gather in a comfortable interior with a dog, on a large oil on wood panel of 99.1 by 134.6 centimeters, painted about 1883. The title and the cast point to a missionary telling his tale to listeners who have stayed at home, the comedy arising from their reactions. The panel is big for Vibert, whose pictures were usually smaller, and it gives him room for a full cast, rich fabrics and the glossy finish for which he was known.","i":"Vibert specialized in gently satirical pictures of well-fed prelates and monks at their ease, and they found a ready market in the nineteenth century, especially among American collectors, for whom Catholic ritual and comfortable clerics made a safely exotic subject. A missionary's tale of danger abroad, told to men who stayed at home, offers a double irony: the dignitaries enjoy the thrill of peril from a safe armchair. Contemporary viewers probably read it as good-humored teasing of the clergy rather than a direct attack on religion.","c":"Vibert is a master of the staged comic scene, and the broad panel allows him a full cast, sumptuous cloth and a polished interior. The timing is clear and the satire mild, while the glossy surface flirts with slickness, so that the picture amuses without moving. It is a representative example of the academic anecdote that Salon audiences adored and that the modern painters of the 1880s were beginning to reject."},"nga:176783":{"d":"A small stream runs between brown rocks and green foliage, lit by golden sunlight that picks out vegetation, trees and hills in many shades of green, blue and brown. The water is clear enough to show the brown ground beneath while reflecting the greenery beside it. Above the distant green mountain lies a sliver of bright blue sky with a few wispy clouds. The canvas, 53.3 by 43.8 centimeters and painted in 1858, is upright, a format that stresses the depth of the glen.","i":"The Catskills, a short trip up the Hudson from New York, were the cradle of American landscape painting: Thomas Cole made their wooded gorges and waterfalls famous from the 1820s, and later painters followed him into the same glens. A woodland stream with sunlit rocks was a staple of the Hudson River School, offering viewers a vision of wild yet benign nature, a wilderness already becoming a destination for tourists.","c":"The scene is carefully observed, with sunlit foliage and clear, reflective water handled with real care, and the narrow upright view gives an intimate woodland-interior feeling. Paul Weber stays within Hudson River School conventions rather than stretching them, lacking the grandeur of Church or the poetry of Cole, but the attention to light on leaves and the transparency of the stream are quietly persuasive."},"met:435579":{"d":"The Nativity is painted in tempera and gold on a small wooden panel only 33 by 23.2 centimeters, about 1455. Mary, Saint Joseph and the Christ Child are the principal figures, with an angel present and the ox and donkey of the traditional stable. The gold and fine tempera give the panel the look of an illuminated manuscript page, appropriate to Zanobi Strozzi, a Florentine illuminator as well as a panel painter, and its scale suits private devotion.","i":"Mary, Joseph and the Child in a stable with an ox and an ass follow the Gospel of Luke as embroidered by tradition: the animals, absent from the gospel text, come from Isaiah's line that the ox knows its owner and the ass its master's crib, and they became fixtures of the scene. Images like this invited the viewer to imagine himself present at Bethlehem, a habit of meditation encouraged by popular texts such as the Meditations on the Life of Christ.","c":"Zanobi Strozzi was a pupil and follower of Fra Angelico with an illuminator's eye, and the small Nativity shows both: the gold and delicate color are charming, and the story is clear and tender. His drawing is less sure than his master's and his figures lack Angelico's monumental calm, but sweetness and decorative refinement are real virtues. As a devotional image of mid-fifteenth-century Florence it is a graceful minor work."},"met:13181":{"d":"A still life of fruit with a leaf, painted in oil on canvas, 53.7 by 43.7 centimeters, about 1888, fills an upright format. The plain title suggests that the subject is the fruit itself, its color and surface, rather than any symbolic program. Weir, who trained in Paris in the 1870s and became a leading American Impressionist, painted such studies as exercises in tone and color, and the modest scale suits a picture made for private pleasure.","i":"Still life gave American painters of the 1880s a way to work out problems of color and tone in private, away from the demands of exhibition pictures. Weir had studied in Paris under Jean-Léon Gérôme, and in 1881 he bought two paintings by Manet for the collector Erwin Davis, so he knew the French tradition of Chardin and Manet at first hand. A plain title like Fruit signals that the subject is looking itself: ripeness, skin and light.","c":"Weir's still life is modest and quietly accomplished: careful drawing and a restrained palette let the colors of the fruit carry the picture. It is not as daring as his figure pieces or his late landscapes, and the objects are painted more as a task than a discovery; but the honest observation and evident pleasure in surface keep it from dullness. It stands as a small, sincere exercise rather than a statement."},"met:13183":{"d":"A group of women and a girl, with a guitar in the scene, spend a leisurely hour together on a large canvas of 130.2 by 180.7 centimeters painted in 1888. The wide format and the title set the subject: summer leisure, music and conversation rather than any event. It is catalogued with portraits, so the figures may be real sitters, but the title gives no names and the picture is composed as a quiet tableau in which mood matters more than likeness.","i":"Scenes of women at leisure, with music and quiet occupation, are a hallmark of American painting in the 1880s, when domestic harmony and refinement were prized and French plein-air naturalism, especially that of Jules Bastien-Lepage, whom Weir admired, met a taste for the home. A guitar was a modest parlor instrument, part of a young woman's accomplishments. The title's idleness is a studied one: the picture presents leisure as a cultivated, almost moral state.","c":"Idle Hours is an ambitious early picture in which Weir combines figure painting with natural light, and its quiet, grayish harmonies and careful grouping show his training. The grouping is a little stiff, and the light does not yet dissolve the figures as it will in his later work, but the calm gravity distinguishes it from the more sentimental parlor pictures of the decade. It is a key stage on the way to his Impressionism."},"met:13184":{"d":"A red bridge crosses a river among trees on a canvas of 61.6 by 85.7 centimeters, painted in 1895. The bridge named in the title is the picture's accent, set against the foliage, and the landscape is built from short, broken strokes in an Impressionist manner. The scene probably belongs to the Connecticut countryside around Windham, where Weir spent his summers, and the format is a classic horizontal landscape.","i":"Bridges are among the oldest motifs in landscape, joining banks, roads and the passage of time, and in the 1890s American painters such as Weir, Twachtman and Hassam applied French Impressionist ideas to the landscape of New England. The red of a painted bridge gave Weir a color accent in a world of foliage and water. His Connecticut subjects mix rural calm with signs of industry and travel, and this crossing hints at both.","c":"The Red Bridge is among Weir's most direct Impressionist landscapes: the touches are small, the color bright, and the red accent anchors a composition that might otherwise dissolve into foliage. Weir's Impressionism is softer and more reserved than Monet's, more tonal than optical, but the picture's freshness and clean construction place it among the better American landscapes of the 1890s."},"met:13180":{"d":"A factory village lies among trees on a canvas of 73.7 by 96.6 centimeters, painted in 1897. The mill buildings, the village around them and the trees that soften them are the subject, and the broad horizontal format suits a panoramic view across roofs and foliage. The paint is applied in small, soft touches in Weir's Impressionist manner, which binds mill and woodland into one atmosphere.","i":"Weir painted several views of the thread mills at Willimantic, Connecticut, in the 1890s, and this village picture probably belongs with them. Factories were a new subject for American landscape, signs of national progress that painters treated with celebration, unease or, as here, calm acceptance. In his hands the mill village becomes a New England place like any other, where smoke and roofs share one hazy light, and the title keeps the human settlement central.","c":"By painting the factory village in the same soft light as a farm, Weir blurs the usual divide between industrial and rural views, and the result is quietly original in American art of the 1890s. The handling is cautious and a touch repetitive, never losing the tonal calm he preferred. Still, as a record of mill New England and a sensitive landscape, it is among his more interesting pictures."},"nga:71588":{"d":"The view is from an elevated position, with the horizon at the center where blue sky meets distant green hills. A gray stone bridge curves gracefully across the lower half, running from a gray building at the left toward the bottom right and spanning a wide, light blue river that flows into the distance. In the middle ground a small pointed-roof structure and a tall smokestack are partly hidden by trees, and a tall tree at the left corner covers part of the building behind it. Soft brushstrokes and pale pastel and green tones unify the scene.","i":"Willimantic, Connecticut, called the Thread City, was long a center of cotton thread manufacture, and Weir, who spent summers nearby in Windham, painted the mills there in the 1890s. In these pictures he gave industry the same soft light as pastoral scenes, with the smokestack as one accent among trees and water. The series reflects the American Impressionist habit of finding poetry in working landscapes, at a moment when mills and countryside still shared the same river.","c":"The elevated viewpoint, the sweeping curve of the bridge and the quiet pastel harmony turn a factory view into a calm, almost pastoral picture, which is both its charm and its limit: the smokestack is small and half hidden, and nothing of labor or smoke disturbs the idyll. As an Impressionist treatment of industrial New England it is gentle and well composed rather than probing."},"met:13182":{"d":"A woman and a mirror are the subjects of this upright canvas, 86.2 by 62.9 centimeters, finished by 1898. The title points to her dress, a green bodice, as the picture's color note, and the mirror suggests a private moment of dressing or reflection, the sort of motif painters have long used to show a woman in two views. It is a figure picture nearer to a portrait than a narrative, painted by an artist who was better known for his landscapes.","i":"A woman before a mirror is one of the oldest motifs in European painting, once a moralizing emblem of vanity, but by the later nineteenth century a gentle study of private grace. Weir's figure pictures of the 1890s are reticent and tonal and avoid anecdote, and here the green bodice and the mirror offer an occasion for a study in color and reflected light. The title does not name the sitter, and her identity should not be assumed.","c":"Weir's figure paintings are often quieter than his landscapes, and this one depends on the subtle play of the green dress against a muted setting. The mirror is a worn device, and the picture lacks the sparkle he found painting outdoors, yet it shows his taste for restraint and his sensitivity to color and surface. It is a sound, tasteful work rather than a distinctive one."},"met:436221":{"d":"A couple and a fortune-teller share a small room with a fireplace in this panel of only 25.1 by 33 centimeters, painted about 1632 to 1633. Interiors with figures at the hearth were the stuff of Dutch cabinet pictures, small, finely observed panels meant to be held close and enjoyed by the buyer. Jacob Duck, a painter of Utrecht, was known for such scenes of soldiers, merry companies and domestic life, painted with a smooth, delicate touch and a restrained palette.","i":"Fortune-telling was a favorite subject of Dutch and Flemish genre painters because it could carry both amusement and warning: the seer reads a palm while a companion may pick a pocket, and the credulous are tempted to trust in chance rather than prudence. Paired with a couple, the traditional figures of courtship, the scene probably concerns love, and viewers would have laughed while remembering the proverbs against foolish trust.","c":"Duck's strength is the small-scale grace of his figures and the convincing quiet space of his rooms. The fortune-teller gives him a lively theme, though he tends to stay at the surface, with courteous caricature rather than deep characterization. Next to later masters of the interior such as Gerard ter Borch or Gabriel Metsu he is a minor figure, but a polished one, and his early pictures remain useful evidence for the Utrecht genre of the 1630s."},"cma:148366":{"d":"Salome, in colorful, exotic garments at the right, receives the severed head of Saint John the Baptist from the executioner, who is the picture's real focus: muscular and already departing, he turns to look at the viewer. The pommel of his sword is decorated with a grotesque head, a touch that suggests an ancient or exotic setting. The scene is oil on a wood panel, 71 by 54.6 centimeters, painted in the middle of the seventeenth century by Jacob Duck.","i":"The story comes from the Gospels of Matthew and Mark: John the Baptist condemned the marriage of Herod and Herodias, and at Herod's birthday feast Salome's dance so pleased him that he promised her anything, whereupon, at her mother's urging, she asked for John's head. Instead of the execution, Duck shows its aftermath, with the executioner leaving and looking out of the picture, which makes the viewer a witness. Painters favored the subject for its mixture of luxury, sin and horror.","c":"Duck, who usually painted soldiers and merry companies on a small scale, here applies his silky finish to a biblical horror, and the contrast is uneasy. The costumes, the gleam of the garments and the muscular executioner are well handled, and the outward glance is an effective hook. But the emotion is muted, the drama more tableau than event, and his gift for surface does not supply the psychological weight the story demands."},"met:437353":{"d":"A small portrait on ivory shows a man in profile, as the museum's keywords record. No measurements or further description are given, so his age, dress and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The profile, a format of crisp outline that recalls medals and cameos, was fashionable in France in the late eighteenth century, when portraitists could capture a likeness quickly and cheaply and sitters liked the classical dignity it lent.","i":"Edme Quenedey (1756–1830) is associated with the physionotrace, a device that used a pointer to trace a sitter's profile mechanically for a rapid, exact outline, and he developed his own version in the late 1780s, around the time of the French Revolution. A profile on ivory of about 1780 comes from the earlier part of his career, when he worked as a miniaturist. The sitter is not named, and the portrait was bequeathed to the Met in 1962.","c":"In the profile format everything depends on contour, and Quenedey, a precise draughtsman, is well suited to it: the line of brow, nose and chin must carry the likeness, with colour and costume secondary. Without details to examine, one can say that such miniatures are admired for exactness rather than warmth; they record faces with a crisp objectivity that a modern eye, accustomed to photographs, can appreciate. A small, elegant and sober piece."},"nga:32578":{"d":"A young woman and two children are seen from the waist up through an oval stone opening, as if through a window, in a square canvas of about 88.6 centimeters on each side. The three look at a small wooden box held by the boy, with a short brass-lined lens poking out of one side. The woman, in an olive-green dress with a spruce-blue shawl, glances out at the viewer with a slight smile; the oval is carved with leaves and berries, and the girl and the box cast a shadow across it. It is signed and dated 1764.","i":"The camera obscura, a darkened box or room in which a lens projects an inverted image of the outside world, was both a scientific instrument and a fashionable plaything in the eighteenth century, used by painters, travelers and amateurs of optics. Showing it in the hands of children turns it into a parlor amusement and a sign of enlightened curiosity. The picture pairs with Soap Bubbles, another game of vision and illusion, and both play with the trick of the oval window.","c":"Van Loo's trick of placing figures behind an oval stone opening, with shadows spilling across the sill, is witty and well managed, and the rendering of silk, hair and skin is smooth and fresh in the manner of the Rococo. The faces are sweet to the point of blankness and the idea is stronger than the feeling; yet as a polished conceit about seeing and illusion it is more than a confection."},"nga:32579":{"d":"Three children appear from the chest up within an oval frame carved with leaves. A girl at the center, in a white garment and yellow shawl with a blue ribbon in her hair, reaches out beyond the frame, holding the ends of a cloth pinned to her dress, as if the frame were a window or porthole. A boy at the right leans on her shoulder, looking up; another stands on a chair at the left and blows through a long yellow straw to hold a large bubble over the cloth, a small white dish in his other hand. The ground is plain gray.","i":"The soap bubble is an old emblem of the fragility of life: the Latin phrase homo bulla, man is a bubble, appears with children blowing bubbles in Dutch and Flemish prints and paintings from the sixteenth century on. By the 1760s the moral had softened into amusement, but its memory lingers, and viewers might enjoy the double meaning of beauty and brevity. As a pendant to The Camera Obscura, it sets a game of fleeting images beside a machine for catching them.","c":"The illusion of children leaning out of a carved oval, one reaching into the viewer's space, is cleverly managed, and the soft pastel color and silky finish are what the Rococo market wanted. The sentiment is mannered, with doll-like faces, and the moral of the bubble is mostly decorative; but as a play between frame and image it shows real wit. Set beside Greuze's child pictures it is cooler and more decorative."},"met:11497":{"d":"A tiny portrait of a man, painted in watercolour on ivory, only about 5 by 4 centimetres, shows the sitter named in the title as Cornelius George Bowler. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. At roughly the size of a large coin, it is the smallest of this group of Malbone miniatures, made to be set in a locket, ring or small frame and held close to the eye.","i":"Edward Greene Malbone, born in Newport, Rhode Island, in 1777, began painting miniatures as a teenager and by about 1795, when this one was made, was already working in Providence and Newport, largely self-taught. Miniatures were the commonest form of portrait in the young republic, kept as keepsakes by families and sweethearts, and Malbone raised the craft to its highest American level within a decade. The sitter's career is not recorded here.","c":"At this size everything depends on delicacy of touch, and Malbone's reputation rests on the transparency of his flesh tones, built up in fine hatching and stippling that lets the white ivory glow through. Without the specifics of this miniature to hand, the fair verdict is that an early work of about 1795 will show promise more than the full refinement of his later portraits. Even his early miniatures are generally ranked well above the usual run of American work."},"met:15102":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named Nathaniel Pearce, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimetres, and dated 1795. The museum's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so his features, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. Ivory gave Malbone a pale, luminous ground that he allowed to show through thin washes of colour, and the format, a small oval that fits the hand, was meant for a locket or a pocket case.","i":"Painted in 1795, when Malbone was eighteen, the miniature belongs to his early Providence years, in which he built a clientele among Rhode Island merchants and their families. Little is on record here about the sitter beyond his name. A man's portrait on ivory often served as a pledge of courtship or as a gift to a wife, and families kept such pictures for generations, which is how many survive. The Met received this one as a gift from Gloria Manney in 2006.","c":"Malbone at eighteen already shows what set him apart from his American contemporaries: a feeling for soft, transparent modelling, and a refusal to overload the small oval with accessories. Without the picture before the eye, the fair verdict is that the portraits of this early date are fresher and plainer than the elegant, lightly idealised work of his maturity. They reward close looking for the tact with which a likeness is caught in a space smaller than a playing card."},"met:11501":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, identified by the title as John Langdon Sullivan, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimetres, in 1797. The museum records only a man and a portrait, so his features, clothing and the colour of the background cannot be described with confidence. The oval format and the thin, glowing washes are the standard of Malbone's work, and the picture would have been mounted in a gold frame or locket.","i":"In 1797 Malbone was working in Boston, which, with Newport and Providence, was one of his early centres, and the city's merchants and professional families made up much of his clientele. The sitter's name belongs to a prominent New England family, and the portrait presumably recorded a young man of standing, though the record gives no details of his life. The Harris Brisbane Dick Fund bought the miniature for the Met in 1938.","c":"A miniature of this date is likely to show Malbone's growing assurance: a clear, calm likeness, soft light on the face, and a plain ground that keeps all the attention on the head. His men are often less showy than his women, with a restraint that suits the Federal taste for sober dignity. Without being able to judge the face, one can say that his best qualities, delicacy and truthfulness, are the main reasons for the miniature's continuing interest."},"met:11503":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman, identified by the title as Mrs. John Nightingale, born Martha Washington Greene, painted in watercolour on ivory about 7 by 5.5 centimetres, around 1797. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, so her hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. Women's miniatures of this decade were often painted to mark an engagement or marriage and given to the husband to carry or keep.","i":"Martha Washington Greene, named for Martha Washington, was probably a daughter of General Nathanael Greene, the Revolutionary commander from Rhode Island, which would tie her to the circle of families around Malbone's home state. The name Nightingale records her marriage. The portrait reached the Metropolitan Museum in 1888 to 1895 with the Moses Lazarus Collection, given by his daughters Josephine and Sarah Lazarus in his memory.","c":"Malbone's portraits of women are the most memorable part of his output, with their pearly skin, soft glances and the lightly idealised grace that the Federal period admired. Without being able to examine this one, it is fair to say that a portrait of about 1797 will show the formula already settled: a graceful pose, flowing hair and a gently suffused ground. It is a modest but characteristic piece, valued for its place in an important early American career."},"met:11499":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified by the title as James G. Almy, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7.5 by 6 centimetres, around 1798. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. The picture forms a pair with the miniature of his wife in the same museum, the two coming together from the bequest of Edward Walden Forrest in 1927.","i":"Husband-and-wife miniatures were often made as a pair, in the same size, so that they could be displayed or worn together, and the Almy pair is a good example. Around 1798 Malbone was moving between Boston and New York, painting sitters who could pay for a refined portrait, and miniature making was among the most profitable forms of art in the young republic. The record gives no further biography of James G. Almy.","c":"As one of a pair, the portrait gains from its companion: a married couple painted in matching dimensions and a consistent light, as Malbone did, gives the viewer the pleasure of comparing two faces carefully matched in handling. Taken alone, a man's portrait of this date is plainer than a woman's, but the same clarity and softness of touch apply. Without more detail, it is a sound, characteristic piece by a refined artist."},"met:11505":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman identified by the title as Mrs. James G. Almy, born Myra Eliot, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 8 by 6 centimetres, around 1798. The museum records only a woman and a portrait, so her hair, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. It is the companion to the miniature of her husband, and slightly taller than his.","i":"Women's miniatures of the 1790s showed the fashions of the day, the loose curled hair and soft white muslin of the Federal period, and offered sitters a flattering, delicate image. Malbone excelled at the type, and his women's portraits are among the best-known American miniatures. The title records both her married name and her maiden name, a customary way for museums to identify women sitters. The pair came to the Met by bequest in 1927.","c":"Malbone's women are where his gifts are most apparent: the luminous complexion, the soft fall of hair and the clear, quiet eyes were all part of a refined ideal that he rendered with minute, feathery strokes. The risk of the type is a certain sameness of beauty, since sitters are lightly idealised toward a common look. Without the miniature before the eye, it can still be said to belong to the best of American art of its time."},"met:11496":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman identified by the title as Mrs. James Lowndes, born Catherine Osborn, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 8 by 6.5 centimetres, in 1801. The museum records only a woman and a portrait, so her hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. Lowndes is a South Carolina name, and by 1801 Malbone's clientele had spread well beyond New England to the wealthy families of Charleston.","i":"By about 1801 Malbone was widely regarded as the finest miniature painter in America, and he travelled to meet his sitters, wintering in the South, where the planters and merchants of Charleston and Savannah paid well for portraits. The Lowndes were a leading Charleston family. The title records both her married and maiden names, identifying her within two families. The Rogers Fund purchased the miniature for the Met in 1914.","c":"In his Southern work Malbone's touch grows if anything more refined: the flesh tones are more transparent, the backgrounds softer, and the poses a little freer than in his early pictures. Without the picture before the eye, one can say that such a miniature probably shows the mature style: an exquisitely controlled, flattering portrait whose beauty depends on restraint rather than ornament. It is exactly the kind of picture on which his reputation rests."},"met:11500":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified by the title as Joel Roberts Poinsett, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7 by 5.5 centimetres, in 1801. The museum's keywords record only the sitter and the portrait, so his hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. The format is the usual small oval, mounted for a locket or case, and the thin washes of colour let the white of the ivory glow through the skin tones.","i":"Joel Roberts Poinsett (1779–1851), born in Charleston, South Carolina, became a diplomat and politician, serving as the first American minister to Mexico and later as Secretary of War; he introduced the plant that bears his name, the poinsettia, from Mexico to the United States. In 1801 he was a young man in his early twenties, and the miniature records him before his career. The Harris Brisbane Dick Fund acquired it for the Met in 1938.","c":"A portrait of a future statesman as a young man, valuable for its subject as much as its manner. Malbone's likenesses of men are less showy than his women's, with a quiet clarity that suited the sober tastes of the early republic. Without the picture before the eye, one can say that it is a polished and sympathetic example of a type in which he had few rivals, and that its charm is that of a face caught before fame."},"met:11508":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman identified by the title as Susan Poinsett, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 6.5 by 5 centimetres, in 1802. The museum records only a woman and a portrait, so her hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. It was acquired by the Met in 1938 from the same fund and in the same year as the miniature of Joel Roberts Poinsett, which suggests that the two came together from the family.","i":"Susan Poinsett belonged to the Poinsett family of Charleston, and is presumably a relative of the diplomat Joel Roberts Poinsett, painted the year after his portrait. Malbone was working in the South in the early 1800s, painting leading families in Charleston and Savannah, and portraits of several members of one family, in a consistent size and style, were a common commission. The record gives no further biography of Susan.","c":"The soft complexion, delicate colouring and refined composition are Malbone's hallmarks, and a portrait of 1802 should show him at the height of his powers. A modest oval of a woman is hardly a grand image, but the way light plays across the face through the thinnest veils of watercolour is exactly the skill that made him famous. Without seeing the face, one can only say that it should be a fine example."},"met:15103":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified by the title as George Bethune, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 8 by 6 centimetres, around 1800 to 1803. The museum's keywords record only the sitter and the portrait, so his features, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. At nearly 8 centimetres high, it is a little larger than the early miniatures in this group, a sign of the more ambitious scale that Malbone adopted as his reputation grew.","i":"The name Bethune is recorded in the title but the sitter's life is not described in the museum's data. Around 1800 Malbone was busy in New York, Philadelphia and Charleston, moving as demand dictated, and he travelled to London in 1801 with his friend, the painter Washington Allston. Miniatures like this one record the faces of a young nation's prosperous men, and many survive because families treasured them. The Met received it in 2006 from Gloria Manney.","c":"A solid, characteristic work from the years in which Malbone's technique was fully formed: clear drawing, a soft, high-keyed palette and a likeness that avoids both flattery and harshness. Without being able to examine the face, it is best judged as an example of his standard of excellence, in which the sitter is shown with calm dignity and a minimum of display. As a document of his American clientele around 1800, it is valuable."},"met:15104":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified by the title as Samuel Denman, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimetres, around 1803. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. It forms a pair with the miniature of Mrs. Samuel Denman in the same collection, the two having been bought by the Met together in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"A husband-and-wife pair of miniatures was a standard commission in the early republic, painted at the same sitting in matching size, and Malbone's pairs, kept together, give a sense of how families recorded themselves in the early nineteenth century. The record gives little biography for Samuel Denman, though the title is enough to place him among the families Malbone painted. The painter's own life was short: he died of tuberculosis in 1807, aged twenty-nine.","c":"As half of a pair, the portrait is best seen with its companion, where the matching handling of man and wife shows how well Malbone controlled tone and light. A man's face in his hands is calm and quietly alert; without the specifics, one can say that this one probably shares the unemphatic, truthful manner of his other male portraits. It is a good piece, and the pairing adds to its interest."},"met:11504":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman identified by the title as Mrs. Robert Macomb, born Mary Cornell Pell, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 9.5 by 7.5 centimetres, around 1806. The museum records only a woman and a portrait, so her hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. It is the same large size as the portrait of her husband, one of the largest of Malbone's miniatures, and the pair were bequeathed together.","i":"Mary Cornell Pell belonged to the Pell family, long established in the New York area, and her married name connects her to the Macombs, a prominent merchant family of the city. In painting the wife as well as the husband, and in this large format, Malbone gave the family a pair of portraits that could stand on a desk or mantel like small oil pictures. She is named in the title by both her married and her maiden surname.","c":"At this larger size the portrait has room for the soft transitions of light on the face and the gentle, smoky backgrounds Malbone preferred, and women sitters in his late work are painted with a refined, almost sculptural softness. Without being able to inspect it, one can say that it is likely a very fine example of the late style. The ambitious scale makes it stand out against the small intimacy of the earlier ovals."},"met:11507":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified by the title as Robert Macomb, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 9.5 by 7.5 centimetres, around 1806. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. It is considerably larger than most of Malbone's miniatures, and it forms a pair with the portrait of his wife.","i":"Around 1806 Malbone was at the end of his short career, in poor health and working in New York, and his late miniatures grew larger and richer in effect, perhaps as a way of competing with oil portraits. The Macombs were a New York family; the pair of portraits came to the Metropolitan Museum from the bequest of Irving S. Olds in 1963. The record gives no more biography of Robert Macomb.","c":"The larger scale shows a mature Malbone: more room for the face to breathe, softer modelling, and a calm assurance that a man's portrait needed no ornament. Without the picture before the eye, one can say that the pair is likely among the grandest of his late work, within the miniature's limits. Their size makes them more imposing than his early ovals, though they sacrifice a little of the early intimacy."},"met:15105":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman identified by the title as Mrs. Samuel Denman, born Anna Maria Hampton, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7 by 5.5 centimetres. The museum records only a woman and a portrait, and gives no date, so her hair, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. It is the companion of the portrait of her husband, and the two came to the Metropolitan Museum together in 2006 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"Anna Maria Hampton is recorded under both her married and her maiden name, which is the museum's way of identifying her, and Hampton is a well-known South Carolina family name, though the record does not say how she was related to it. A miniature of a wife painted to match her husband's was a favourite commission, and the pairs have often been separated by time and sale; the survival of this pair as a pair adds to their interest.","c":"Malbone's women combine delicacy with a firm sense of structure, and the small oval of a woman's head is one of the most perfect formats in American art of its period. Without a date or other details, a fair judgement is that this is a typical product of his best manner, in which every touch is controlled and nothing is overstated. The pair, shown together, give a cleaner sense of his range than either alone."},"met:20414":{"d":"A single eye is the subject of this tiny watercolour on ivory, which the title identifies as the eye of Maria Miles Heyward, painted around 1802. The museum gives no measurements, and the record lists only an eye and the sitter's name, so the colour, the brow and the surrounding skin cannot be described with confidence. Such pictures were made to be set in a brooch, ring or locket, so that the beloved's gaze could be carried secretly close to the owner.","i":"Eye miniatures became fashionable in Britain in the 1780s, supposedly after the Prince of Wales sent a portrait of his eye to Mrs. Fitzherbert, and the fashion reached America soon after. The portrait of an eye hid the sitter's identity, which made it a token of love or a keepsake of mourning, and it could be shown or concealed as the owner wished. Malbone, who painted the eye of Maria Miles Heyward about 1802, was a master of the format.","c":"An eye is the hardest feature of the face to paint, and the whole picture depends on getting the glance, the brow and the glint of light exactly right; Malbone's gift for translucent flesh tones makes him well suited. The conceit is also a risk, since the picture can seem a curiosity, a face reduced to an organ. Seen as a keepsake, however, it is an extraordinarily intimate object."},"nga:46007":{"d":"A man is seen from the waist up, looking off to the right. He has light gray hair styled in curls above the ears, a soft jawline, a wrinkled brow, a long straight nose and deep-set eyes. He wears a black coat with a high collar and a white ruffled shirt or cravat. The background is a gradient of muted brown, lighter at the center and darker toward the edges, which draws the head forward. The title names him as George Washington; the canvas measures 76.1 by 63.3 centimeters and dates from about 1796.","i":"Washington sat to many painters, and portraits like this met a public eager to own his likeness in the 1790s. Edward Savage, who painted him from life around 1789 to 1790 and later published engravings of his pictures, was one of several who supplied that demand. Such pictures show him as an elder statesman, restrained and dignified, in plain dark dress without the sword or uniform of the general, an image of republican simplicity.","c":"As a likeness, this is serious and unflattering, with a furrowed brow and deep-set eyes that avoid heroic idealizing, and the plain brown ground keeps attention on the face. Savage's drawing is stiff and his modeling a little dry, so the portrait lacks the authority of Gilbert Stuart's versions, which set the national standard. It matters chiefly as one of several records of how the first president looked, and as part of the early image-making that surrounded him."},"nga:561":{"d":"A family of four and a servant gather around a table in a room hung with red curtains. George Washington, in a dark blue coat, buff breeches and tall boots, sits at the left with his legs crossed and his arm along the back of his chair, his sword and hat on the table. Opposite sits Martha Washington, in an ivory satin gown, dark lace shawl and white cap, pointing with a fan at a large map spread on the table. Her granddaughter, in white with a blue-green sash, holds down the map's edge, while her grandson, in pink, lifts a green cover from a globe. Behind them an enslaved man in a rust-orange coat stands half in shadow. Beyond, a river and a sunset sky glow; the floor is a yellow-and-brown chequerboard.","i":"Edward Savage, a Massachusetts-born portraitist, painted Washington from life beginning in 1789, and the group was worked up from sittings with members of the family over several years; the National Gallery dates it 1789–96. The map on the table is Pierre L'Enfant's plan for the new federal capital, so the president is shown both as a family man and as the founder of a city, and the globe and the river view suggest the young nation's wider horizons. The format borrows from British conversation pieces. The enslaved man is thought to be a butler; Mount Vernon says he might be Christopher Sheels, Washington's enslaved valet, but the identity is not certain. Savage exhibited the painting and engraved it, and Washington ordered copies of the print.","c":"As a painting it is awkward. The figures are arranged around the table like a diagram, the floor and table do not quite agree in perspective, and the faces have little life beyond their rank. Its interest is historical and social. It shows the president as head of an orderly household with the plan of a new capital under his wife's fan, and, unusually, it includes an enslaved man at the family's side. The servant is the most striking presence, and he stays at the edge of the composition, in shadow, a reminder of what such portraits left out."},"met:435724":{"d":"In the courtyard of a ruined palace, the Virgin sits on a gold brocade cloth in a deep teal-blue gown, with the naked Child on her lap. Three kings approach: one kneels in a rose-red robe, a crown on the ground beside him and a gold gift in hand; another, in a patterned robe, stands with a gold vessel; and a Black king in white and gold, with a sword at his belt, holds a gilded cup. An old man in a green hood kneels at the left with a staff, and a white greyhound sits in the foreground. An ox waits in a dark arch, while two onlookers peer through a gap in the wall. Above, two red-winged angels hold a green cloth between two towers; beyond lies a pale lowland with ponds and tiny figures.","i":"The Met dates this about 1475, early in Bosch's career, when he was working in 's-Hertogenbosch in the Netherlands. The Magi's gifts stand for Christ's kingship, divinity and death, and the cup carried by the Black king is topped with a bird pecking its own breast, which recalls the pelican, a traditional symbol of Christ's sacrifice. The ruined building is a conventional sign of the old order giving way to the new. The staging, with figures grouped like actors before a backdrop, has been linked to the religious plays of Bosch's home town. Charles de Tolnay questioned the attribution in the twentieth century, but the Bosch Research and Conservation Project, studying the underdrawing, upheld it in 2016.","c":"A cool, orderly picture from a painter who later became famous for disorder. The space is clear, the tower and the green cloth are sharply drawn, and the faces have a Netherlandish smoothness; the strangeness is limited to the peering faces at the wall and the pale flooded lowland behind. The Virgin is somewhat stiff, and the gold gifts, drawn with so much care, compete with the Child for attention. Without the monsters, what stands out is the evenness of his hand, which shows how much of Bosch's later effect depended on a fine control of surface and space."},"nga:41645":{"d":"A narrow upright panel shows a cramped, vaulted room open at the front. In a canopied bed at the upper right, a pale naked man in a black cap sits upright, gesturing; the skeletal figure of Death, wrapped in a shroud, steps through a door at the left and aims an arrow at him. An angel kneels at the bedside, one hand on the man's shoulder and the other pointing to a crucifix in the window, while a demon holds up a bag of coins and another peers down from the canopy. In the foreground an old man in a green robe drops coins into a chest, where more demons lurk. Armor and weapons lie on the stone ledge below.","i":"The picture draws on the Ars moriendi, the fifteenth-century handbooks on dying well, in which a dying man is tempted by demons and urged by an angel to turn to Christ. Bosch compresses time: the old man in green is probably the same miser earlier in life, hoarding wealth, while on the bed he is in his last moments, caught between the angel and the demon's offered purse. Whether he will reach for the crucifix or the coins is left open. The panel is one wing of a dismembered triptych that also included the Louvre's Ship of Fools. The gallery dates it about 1485–90, but tree-ring evidence from related panels points to a later date, after 1491.","c":"Bosch's moral theater is both comic and frightening: the demons are drawn with a relish out of proportion to their size, and Death, stiff and patient, is oddly dignified. What disappoints is the foreground clutter of armor, cloth, and weapons, which scholars still cannot explain and which crowds the lower third of the panel. Still, placing the decision in the dying man's own hands rather than the angel's gives a sermon the tension of a drama. In its small way it is more disciplined than the better-known triptychs."},"nga:41612":{"d":"A youthful figure stands on a small rectangular platform in an arch-topped panel, one knee slightly bent and the heel raised, the left hand on the hip and the other holding a black staff with gold mounts. Long wavy yellow hair frames a smooth face with arched eyebrows; he wears a red robe knotted at the neck, a green sash patterned in gold and no shoes. Behind him is a green landscape with buildings, trees and distant blue water, where tiny figures chase one another and a man is held while a sword is raised over him.","i":"Joseph, the favorite son of Jacob sold by his brothers into Egypt, rose from slavery and prison to become Pharaoh's governor, and the staff in his hand is a sign of office. The tiny scenes behind him, among them a chase before a building, probably refer to episodes of his story, such as his flight from Potiphar's wife. Standing heroes of Old Testament and classical history were popular for decorating Italian rooms around 1500, offering models of virtue and fortune.","c":"The painter, known only through a series of Griselda panels, tells a story with a decorative artist's economy: the figure, balanced on its platform with a slight turn, is graceful, and the small narrative scenes in the landscape are lively. The drawing is somewhat stiff and the figure's expression blank, as in much late fifteenth-century decorative painting. It is a charming minor piece of Italian narrative art, not a great one."},"nga:497":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a pale young woman wears a transparent veil and a green gown laced up the bodice. Her face and shoulders angle toward the viewer's left, and she looks out from the corners of her brown eyes, head tipped slightly away; shallow dimples beside her pale pink lips suggest a faint smile. Her blond hair is drawn back under a gauzy veil held by a band, and gold-patterned bands edge the gown. A pearl necklace carries a pendant of red and blue gems with three pearls hanging below, and the black ground is framed by a gold knot-patterned border.","i":"Italian portraits of young women around 1500 usually celebrated beauty, virtue and family standing, and jewels and fine cloth like these announced wealth. The sitter is unnamed, and the panel may have marked an engagement or marriage, as many such portraits did, though that is uncertain. The black ground concentrates attention on face and ornament, and the veil and pendant belong to the dress of well-off Tuscan women of the period.","c":"Girolamo di Benvenuto, a Sienese painter, gives the sitter a gentle sweetness and renders veil and jewels with care. The face is somewhat generalized, the smile and sidelong glance more a type of idealized beauty than a searching likeness, and the black ground flattens the space. It is an attractive, decorous portrait, characteristic of Sienese taste, less penetrating than the best Florentine portraits of the same years."},"met:436888":{"d":"A woman and a skeleton share a tall canvas, 139.4 by 84.5 centimeters, from 1626. The title makes the woman a personification of Avarice, and the skeleton with her is Death, the grim reminder that gold cannot be taken to the grave. Jacopo Ligozzi, a Veronese painter long in Medici service in Florence, was a master of fine detail, and an allegory of this kind joins his habit of exact, almost emblematic drawing with a late Mannerist taste for the moralizing macabre.","i":"Avarice, the vice of hoarding wealth, was one of the seven deadly sins and a favorite target of Counter-Reformation preachers and moralists. Pairing it with a skeleton drives home the point of the memento mori: the miser's riches are lost at the hour of death. Such allegories were painted for patrons who wished to show learning, piety or wry self-awareness, and they may have hung as a warning in a private room.","c":"Ligozzi is at his best in careful natural-history drawing, and here that exactness serves a stark moral. The pairing of living woman and bare bones has a gruesome force, and the tall, narrow format intensifies it. The painting is stiffer than the more naturalistic Florentine work of its day, and the allegory depends on familiar symbols; it is better as a document of late Mannerist moral imagery than as a moving picture."},"nga:107315":{"d":"Five men crowd round a rectangular table in a smoke-dark room, playing cards and dice by a strong light from the upper left. On the near side a bearded man in a gleaming breastplate and scarlet hose holds his cards to his chest and eyes his opponent, a young man in a floppy feathered cap who half-kneels on his seat. Behind them a helmeted man with a staff holds up two fingers toward the dice players, one of whom, in a striped ivory shirt and metal collar, rests his fingertips by the dice while his companion in a ruby-red slashed hat raises an open hand.","i":"Soldiers gambling was a favorite subject of the Caravaggisti in Rome, who followed Caravaggio's Cardsharps, painted in the 1590s, in turning the low-life tavern into a stage of tension, with cheats, dupes and onlookers exchanging glances. The alternate title, The Cheats, suggests that deception is the point: the raised fingers may be signals, and the dice and cards are not to be trusted. Viewers could take it as a warning against vice as well as a display of expressive faces and gestures.","c":"Valentin takes Caravaggio's tavern drama and organizes it with a firm, almost sculptural clarity: the strong light from the upper left, the glittering armor and the interplay of hands and glances hold the scene together. The color is subdued and the characterization a little generic, and he lacks Caravaggio's startling immediacy, but he is among the most capable of the followers, and this picture shows his command of group composition and night-like light."},"met:439933":{"d":"A young man in a plumed black hat sits in near-darkness, turned toward the viewer, his lips parted as he sings and plays a large lute. He wears a steel gorget over a dark jerkin, rose-pink silk sleeves with white cuffs, black breeches, white stockings and black shoes. One leg is crossed over the other, and the pale knee catches the light. At the left a table holds a book of music. A strong light from the upper left picks out the face, the gorget, the lute's reddish belly and the silk, while the rest sinks into brown and black. Oil on canvas, about 128 by 99 centimetres.","i":"Valentin, born in France, spent his career in Rome and was, as the Met puts it, the greatest French follower of Caravaggio. His scenes of music-making, drinking and fortune-telling grew out of the Caravaggesque taste for everyday subjects, but this one is more tender. The Met describes the sitter as a young soldier singing a love madrigal, with the armour at his throat and the sheet music joining martial and amorous sides. It may relate to the nickname Valentin took in Rome, Amador, Spanish for “lover boy.” In the 1650s the picture belonged to Cardinal Mazarin, Louis XIV's chief minister, and the Met acquired it in 2008.","c":"Valentin takes Caravaggio's dark room and strong side light and gives them a gentler turn. The sitter's parted lips, the glimmer on his steel collar and the silk sleeves painted in pearly pinks are all handled with an assured, smooth touch, and the lute, with its rose and fine strings, is a small still life of its own. The weaker passages are the black costume, which dissolves into the dark, and the stiff legs. Beside Caravaggio's own Lute Player in the Hermitage it is less erotic and more reflective, a quiet performance rather than an invitation."},"cma:147020":{"d":"The Old Testament hero Samson rests his head on his hand in a pensive, even melancholy pose. Objects on the table beside him recall two of his deeds: killing a lion with his bare hands and defeating a thousand Philistines with the jawbone of a donkey. His breastplate is fastened at the shoulder by a clasp in the form of two bees, the emblem of the Barberini family. The canvas, 135.6 by 102.8 centimeters, was painted about 1630 to 1631 as a pendant to Valentin's David victorious.","i":"Samson, the strongman of the Book of Judges, appears here in thought rather than action, the signs of his strength set aside on the table. The bees on his armor name the patrons, the family of Pope Urban VIII, who had engaged Valentin in 1627 to paint David with the head of Goliath; pairing two biblical champions perhaps flattered the family's own power. The museum notes that the figure may be a self-portrait of the painter.","c":"This is Valentin at his most inward: a hero shown not at the height of action but brooding, strength put aside, and the Caravaggesque light is used to give the head and armor sculptural weight. The melancholy is more reflective than in most Caravaggio followers, who preferred swagger, and the pairing with David gives the picture a clear public purpose. It stands among his best late Roman works; he died in 1632, aged forty-one."},"met:437856":{"d":"A panel of about 21 by 18 centimeters shows the head of Christ alone, painted in egg tempera on wood against a gold ground. It was made in the seventeenth century by Emmanuel Tzanès, a Cretan painter in the tradition of Greek icons who later settled in Venice. The gold ground, with no landscape or interior, places the face outside time and space. The panel matches two others in size and technique, showing the Virgin and Saint John the Baptist.","i":"An image of Christ's face shown without a body or setting is made for veneration: the believer meets the gaze directly. In Orthodox tradition the face follows a fixed type, with long hair parted in the middle and a full beard, so that every icon is recognizably the same person. Together with the matching heads of the Virgin and John the Baptist, this panel probably belonged to a Deesis, the group in which they intercede for humankind.","c":"Tzanès belongs to the late Cretan school, which kept the Byzantine formula alive while borrowing the softer modeling that reached Greek painters from Venetian art. The strict frontal type and gold ground give the image calm authority. The result is conservative by design: skill is measured by fidelity to a revered pattern rather than by invention, and viewers who want personal expression will find it only in small refinements."},"met:437857":{"d":"The head of Saint John the Baptist is painted in tempera on wood against a gold ground, on a panel of about 21 by 18 centimeters. The Greek painter Emmanuel Tzanès made it in the seventeenth century in the icon tradition of Crete, and it matches panels of Christ and the Virgin in size and technique. As a bare head, it isolates a single face of the saint for devotion rather than telling his story.","i":"John the Baptist is the Forerunner, the prophet who prepared the way for Christ and baptized him in the Jordan. In Orthodox art he is almost always shown in the Deesis beside the Virgin, turned toward Christ in intercession, which suggests that this head was meant to be viewed as one of a trio. He is traditionally shown with wild hair and a lean face, recalling his years in the desert.","c":"The three heads by Tzanès show how a late Cretan workshop could repeat a sacred type with quiet assurance. John's ascetic face suits the austere gold ground, and the shared size and technique of the panels give the set a unity. The painting is devotional first and aesthetic second: there is little room for personal invention, and its success lies in solemn restraint."},"met:437858":{"d":"A tempera panel of about 21 by 18 centimeters shows the head of the Virgin Mary against a gold ground, painted by the Greek artist Emmanuel Tzanès in the seventeenth century. Like the matching panels of Christ and Saint John the Baptist, it presents a face alone, without architecture, landscape or narrative. The gold, a material rather than a color, signals the divine realm in which the Mother of God stands.","i":"In Orthodox devotion the Virgin is the Mother of God, called Theotokos, and the tireless intercessor for believers. A bare head probably comes from a Deesis, where Mary stands at Christ's right hand in an attitude of prayer. Her face in such images is typically calm and remote, expressing both sorrow and maternal compassion rather than personal portraiture.","c":"The pleasure here is in restraint: one face, a gold field, a few controlled tones. Tzanès preserves the Byzantine formula of the Virgin's gravity and adds the modest Venetian softness characteristic of Cretan painters working after 1600. Anyone expecting Renaissance individuality will be disappointed; the picture aims at a timeless type, and it achieves it."},"nga:156940":{"d":"A richly dressed man in a gray hat and long gray coat sits in a dim room, his face brightly lit as he looks straight out at the viewer, while a valet in simple brown clothes, kept in shadow, helps him off with his boots. A table with an ornate cloth, a small dog under a chair, scattered shoes and fabric fill the room, and an archway opens on a glimpse of a fountain and an obelisk against a twilight sky. It is a small oil on canvas, 50.7 by 66.6 centimeters, painted about 1648.","i":"The sitter is named as Anthonij de Bordes (1615 to 1678), and the picture shows him at an unguarded moment, a portrait in the guise of a genre scene. The fountain and obelisk in the archway suggest Rome, where Sweerts worked in the late 1640s, and the contrast of lit master and shadowed servant says something about rank and service. Informal portraits like this were unusual, and the sitter's direct stare suggests he was a willing partner in the conceit.","c":"Sweerts is an original: the dim room, the bold lighting and the odd intimacy of the boot-removal scene make this a portrait unlike any of its time, and the quiet gravity of the sitter's gaze is compelling. The handling is smooth and sober, the narrative understated, and his sympathy for both sitter and servant is evident. It is one of the most distinctive pictures by a painter whose career remains only partly understood."},"met:438407":{"d":"A man holds a jug, and a pipe figures among the motifs, in a small oil on canvas of 49.2 by 39.1 centimeters, painted about 1660. It is catalogued as a portrait, but the humble accessories of jug and pipe suggest a character study, a tronie, in which a face and its type matter more than a name. Sweerts, a Flemish painter, handled such figures with a sober, matter-of-fact directness and quiet light, a manner that sets him apart from the louder tavern painters of his day.","i":"Jugs and pipes belong to the vocabulary of the tavern and of the Dutch and Flemish picture of the senses, where drinking and smoking could signify pleasure, idleness or the passing of time. Without a name the sitter reads as a type, a laborer, craftsman or toper, whom Sweerts treats with dignity rather than mockery. Sweerts painted in Rome and Brussels and died in Goa in 1664 after joining a missionary journey to the East, a restless, unusual career.","c":"The directness is the attraction: a plain man, a plain jug and a pipe, painted with truthful attention and no show. The picture avoids the caricature that often attaches to tavern figures. It lacks the scale and narrative of Sweerts's best-known works, so it reads more as a thoughtful study of character than as a major statement, but it shows the compassion that distinguishes him from most of his contemporaries."},"met:437769":{"d":"Two half-length figures face each other against a dark, empty background. At the left, a young man with long hair is bare to the waist, one hand pressed to his chest, his eyes wide and his mouth slightly open. At the right, a well-dressed youth in a russet fur hat and a red silk robe, tied with a cream sash, holds out a white shirt. He does not look at the man he is helping. His glance goes off to the side, as if someone else had spoken. The light falls on the bare skin, the white linen and the glossy red silk, and everything else stays in shadow. The picture is oil on canvas, wider than it is tall.","i":"The subject is one of the Seven Acts of Mercy, the duties of charity taught in the Catholic tradition and drawn from the words of Christ in the Gospel of Matthew. The Met notes that Sweerts reduces the theme to a tense meeting between two people, one richly dressed and the other nude and wary of the clothes offered to him. He most likely painted it for a Catholic patron while living in Amsterdam, where he fasted and practised other acts of devotion. In 1662 he joined a group of missionaries bound for Persia, but he was dismissed for erratic behaviour and died in Goa in 1664. The Met dates the picture about 1661.","c":"Sweerts, long overlooked, has been rediscovered in recent decades as one of the most original Flemish painters of the seventeenth century, and this picture shows why. The theme is stripped of crowds and sentiment, and the awkwardness between the two men is the point: charity becomes an uneasy exchange. The red silk and fur are handled with relish. The weakness is the lack of explanation. The giver looks away, the receiver's expression is hard to read, and the dark void gives the viewer nothing to settle the question."},"met:435919":{"d":"A boy is shown in a tiny portrait on ivory, a miniature made to be held in the hand or set in a locket, with no dimensions recorded. The sitter is named as George Howard (1773 to 1848), known as Lord Morpeth, and since he was born in 1773 and appears as a boy, the likeness probably dates from the 1780s. Richard Collins, who became a miniature painter to George III, worked in the delicate technique of the miniaturist, building flesh and hair in fine, stippled touches.","i":"Lord Morpeth was the courtesy title of the eldest son of the Earl of Carlisle, whose family seat was Castle Howard; the boy succeeded as sixth earl in 1825. Portrait miniatures recorded the heirs of great families, were exchanged as gifts and tokens, and could be set in lockets or boxes, and a miniature of a boy was often made for relatives to keep close. The picture speaks of dynasty and affection together, the child as both a person and an heir.","c":"Collins's miniatures are praised for clean drawing and a polished, delicate finish, and a child's face suits the soft stipple of ivory. The format restricts invention, however: head, hair and costume fall into conventional arrangements, and the sitter's character is hard to read. It is a refined small object rather than a revealing portrait, valued as a record of a great family and of a skilled court miniaturist."},"cma:115050":{"d":"The title names the subject: storm clouds, a sky of weather rather than a view of a place. The canvas is small and horizontal, 32 by 52.3 centimeters, painted in 1880, when John Henry Twachtman, who had studied in Cincinnati, Munich and Paris, was probably still working in the broad, dark-toned manner he had learned among the Munich painters. A study of this kind is best seen as an exercise in atmosphere, a few large tonal masses laid down quickly.","i":"Weather and sky were a lifelong subject for Twachtman, who came to value mood and suggestion over detail; the quiet, hazy atmospheres of his late snowy and watery landscapes grew from studies like this. In 1880 the Munich taste for dark, sketchy handling was fashionable among American painters returning from Europe, and a storm sky gave an ideal chance to show it. The painting seeks a feeling of weight and impending change more than a record of any one place.","c":"Small and swift, the picture is an early statement of Twachtman's lifelong interest in sky and atmosphere. It is closer to the dark, bravura Munich style than to the pale, delicate harmonies of his maturity, and so it lacks the originality he reached in the 1890s. As a document of a painter finding his way it has interest; as a picture it is more promise than fulfillment."},"met:12842":{"d":"A river landscape on a very large canvas, 152.4 by 200.3 centimeters, painted in 1885 at or near Arques-la-Bataille, a village in Normandy close to Dieppe. The scale is that of an exhibition picture rather than a plein-air sketch, and the loose, unified handling, with soft tonal harmonies in place of crisp detail, shows Twachtman after his Paris training. The river is the one subject recorded, and the horizontal format spreads the view across the eye like a screen.","i":"Twachtman went to Paris in 1883, enrolled at the Académie Julian, and in 1885 painted in Normandy, where he found the quiet, misty river subjects that suited his gift for tone. Arques-la-Bataille is named for the battle of 1589 in which Henri IV defeated his enemies there, but Twachtman's picture ignores history for pure atmosphere. Large canvases like this suited the exhibitions of American artists' societies, where they offered a new, softer, European-trained style to home audiences.","c":"At this size Twachtman attempts an ambitious exhibition picture and mostly succeeds: the tonal unity and the omission of detail already show the poet of atmosphere he became. It is less personal than his later work, still tied to the French landscape tradition of Corot and Daubigny and to Whistler's tonal harmonies, and the huge canvas can feel under-filled. It remains a landmark of the painter's early maturity."},"met:14202":{"d":"A small landscape of Arques-la-Bataille, 46.4 by 65.7 centimeters, painted in the mid-1880s, shows the Normandy village that Twachtman also painted on a very large canvas in 1885. The scale is that of a sketch made for private pleasure, and the loose handling, with softly blended touches and little detail, is typical of the plein-air oil sketch as practiced by the Barbizon and Impressionist generations. It is catalogued simply as a landscape, and its subject is atmosphere rather than incident.","i":"Arques-la-Bataille, near Dieppe in Normandy, took its name from Henri IV's victory there in 1589, but Twachtman paid no attention to history. A smaller canvas of the same place may have served as a study for the large 1885 picture or as a variation made afterward; the title alone does not say which. Either way it shows a landscape painter of the 1880s working between sketch and finished exhibition piece.","c":"The small scale suits Twachtman: freed from the demands of an exhibition canvas, he can leave much to suggestion, and the soft touch and low-keyed harmony have an immediacy that the large version, for all its ambition, lacks. The picture is slight, and without a distinctive incident it risks monotony; but it shows the quiet, tonal, unforced sensibility that would flower in his Greenwich pictures of the 1890s."},"met:12845":{"d":"A waterfall fills an upright canvas of 76.5 by 63.7 centimeters, painted about 1889 to 1891. The vertical format echoes the fall of the water, and the subject is treated less as a topographical view than as an occasion for painting light and motion. It was made after Twachtman had settled at Greenwich, Connecticut, where a brook with a small cascade ran near his house, and it probably belongs with his repeated studies of that fall.","i":"Twachtman bought a farm at Greenwich, Connecticut, in 1889, and the brook and falls near it became a repeated subject, painted again and again in changing seasons and light. Such repetition links him to Monet's series, though his method was quieter and more personal. Japanese prints, which taught Western painters to crop and flatten the view and to prefer a vertical sweep, also left their mark on his compositions. A waterfall is motion made still, a contrast he loved.","c":"Twachtman's waterfalls are among the most poetic American landscapes of the 1890s: thin, soft paint, a narrow range of tones and a high, tilted view dissolve the fall into light and mist. The method can look vague, and the picture asks for patient viewing; but at its best it achieves an equivalence of sensation and brushwork that few American contemporaries attempted. It is a fine example of his Greenwich manner."},"cma:121358":{"d":"A deserted wharf and an old mill, as the double title says, stand on the Mianus River at Cos Cob, a neighborhood of Greenwich, Connecticut. The canvas is upright, 61.5 by 51 centimeters, painted before 1899, and the abandoned structures are the kind of modest, slightly melancholy waterside subject that Twachtman favored in his late work. He builds such views from soft, closely valued touches that unify wood, water and sky in one hazy atmosphere.","i":"In the 1890s Cos Cob, on the Mianus River near Twachtman's Greenwich farm, was a summer gathering place for American Impressionists, among them Weir and Childe Hassam. Old wharves and mills were the humble, picturesque remains of a vanishing working waterfront, and painters liked them because the weathered shapes gave a structure for light and reflection. The word deserted in the title adds a note of quiet loneliness that is rare in Twachtman's work.","c":"Twachtman's late style, thin, hazy and subtle in color, suits a decaying wharf, and the subject gives a stable architecture to the softness. The picture lacks the strong accents of his waterfalls and can be monotonous in tone; yet its restraint is part of the point, an American counterpart to the poetic tonal landscape of Whistler. It is a quiet, characteristic work of Twachtman's last years."},"met:12843":{"d":"Horseneck Falls, the cascade on Horseneck Brook near Twachtman's farm at Greenwich, Connecticut, fills an upright canvas of 76.2 by 63.5 centimeters, painted between about 1889 and 1900. Falling water is the only recorded subject, and the tall format follows the drop of the fall, so the view reads from top to bottom. The picture belongs to a long series in which the artist returned to this motif in different seasons and light.","i":"Horseneck Brook ran through Twachtman's property in Greenwich, and he painted its falls in all seasons, much as Monet painted haystacks and poplars, though without Monet's systematic method. The waterfall, a subject of motion and sound that painting can only suggest, suited his hushed, tonal style, and in Japanese prints he found a precedent for cropping the view and letting the water fill the picture. His late work was prized by fellow painters more than by buyers.","c":"In his waterfall pictures Twachtman reduces fall and mist to a pale vertical shimmer, and the surrounding rock and foliage are held to a quiet scale of related tones. It is a serene manner, though its softness may seem evasive to those who want more drawing and structure. Beside its companion Waterfall, this is a variation on the same theme, and the pair show how he explored one motif through delicate adjustment rather than change."},"nga:50257":{"d":"A stream meanders back through mounded snowbanks in a wooded landscape built entirely from cool, pale tones: white, gray, blue, lilac and golden beige. Steep banks slope toward the water, and touches of tan and pale pink in the branches above hint at lingering foliage. The paint is applied loosely, with visible brushstrokes, so that the view dissolves into a soft haze. The canvas, 65.4 by 81.1 centimeters, is nearly square.","i":"The title borrows the musical vocabulary of Whistler, whose Harmonies and Nocturnes asked viewers to enjoy color and tone as music rather than as description. Twachtman's winters, probably painted around his Greenwich, Connecticut, farm, make a subject of snow because it holds every tint of light, from blue shadow to pearly sky. In the 1890s American artists treated winter less as hardship than as an occasion for subtle sensation, and this picture treats it as pure atmosphere.","c":"This is Twachtman at his best: an almost monochrome range held together by small shifts of lilac, gray and gold, and a design so simple that snowbanks and stream become abstract patterns. The reductive handling risks emptiness, and a viewer who wants description will find little; but the delicacy of color and the quiet rhythms reward patience. It stands among the finest American snow pictures and shows why he is called a painter's painter."},"met:437648":{"d":"A near life-size, full-length portrait, about 244 by 153 centimeters, shows Sir James Dashwood in 1737, when he was in his early twenties. Kirtlington Park, the family's Oxfordshire estate, is part of the picture's subject, either shown or evoked. The scale and the single standing figure place it in the tradition of the English country-house portrait, made to hang in a hall or on a staircase and to announce the owner's standing.","i":"James Dashwood, the second baronet, inherited his title and estates as a young man. A full-length portrait at the start of his adult life announced his place among the landed gentry, and it was painted several years before a grand new house was begun at Kirtlington. Portraits like this one told visitors who owned the land, how old the family was and how securely it was established.","c":"Seeman, a German-born painter working in London, follows the formula of Kneller-style portraiture, which was by now somewhat old-fashioned. The pose and costume are handled with competence, but the figure may seem stiff beside the livelier portraits then arriving from France and from Hogarth's circle. Its value is documentary and decorative: a clear statement of a young landowner's rank."},"met:11583":{"d":"A woman reads in a snug corner by a fireplace, in cap and fichu, with a footstool at her feet, a window nearby and a rifle among the objects. The oil on canvas, about 92 by 62 cm, is dated 1884. 'Cosey' is an old spelling of cozy. The costume suggests the eighteenth century, so the scene is a period fantasy of quiet domestic retreat rather than a view of the artist's own day.","i":"In the 1880s a taste for old-fashioned interiors and antique costume ran through English and American art, fed by the Aesthetic movement and by the colonial revival that followed the Centennial of 1876. Millet was a friend of Edwin Austin Abbey and John Singer Sargent, and later joined them in the artists' colony at Broadway in England. A woman absorbed in a book by the fire stands for leisure and cultivated taste; the rifle may nod to a colonial or military past.","c":"Millet is better regarded as an able illustrator and decorator than as an innovator, and the picture shows both the strength and the limit: smooth finish, well-observed objects, a charming conceit. The staging is theatrical, the sentiment gentle, and the period costume has the look of a party piece. As a document of the taste for the cozy past in the 1880s it is excellent; as painting it is polished, not profound."},"nga:129265":{"d":"In the lower left corner, the limp bodies of a white swan, a rabbit and three birds lie on a low stone step, while a live bird with a gleaming emerald and gold head turns toward a small dog at the right. The tall square base of a stone column, carved in relief with a huddled woman and man looking down toward the swan, takes up the left third. Above, a vine winds across, and a deep landscape opens, with statues along a stepped walk to a reflecting pool, tall trees backlit by an amber sunset and low mountains beyond.","i":"Weenix specialized in hunting trophies set in the grounds of grand houses, and the carved column, statues and formal pool are the furniture of an aristocratic estate. The subject belongs to the tradition of the gamepiece, which celebrated the privilege of the hunt, reserved for the wealthy, and the abundance of the land. Dead creatures beside a living bird and dog also carry a quiet memento mori, a reminder of the brevity of life amid pleasure.","c":"Weenix's strength is stagecraft: the swan's pale body, silky feathers and soft fur are painted with delight, and the raking light and the sunset give a theatrical glamour. The composition is a formula, repeated across his career, and the death of the animals is handled with decorative detachment rather than feeling. Within Dutch hunting still life it is a polished, luxurious example, more impressive in scale and finish than in invention."},"met:437937":{"d":"A dead heron is the centerpiece of this upright gamepiece, 134 by 111.1 centimeters, painted in 1695, with birds and flowers accompanying it. Weenix usually arranged hunting trophies on a stone ledge before an open park or landscape, and the combination of a lifeless bird with blossoms contrasts death with living growth. The large size and the rare quarry mark it as a showpiece for a wealthy house rather than a modest kitchen picture.","i":"The heron was a noble quarry: hawking for herons with trained falcons was a prerogative of the aristocracy, and the bird was prized as a trophy. Laid out with flowers, it belongs to the long tradition of the hunting still life, which glorified the privileges of the landed class and, at the same time, served as a reminder of mortality, with blossoms standing for beauty that fades.","c":"Weenix is a master of feather and fur, and the heron's long neck and soft plumage give him opportunities for fine, silvery handling. The flowers add a note of color against the pale bird. The formula is familiar and the sentiment, if any, is decorative; the picture is skillfully assembled rather than deeply felt. It is a solid example of the aristocratic gamepieces Weenix produced in quantity in his later career."},"nga:66427":{"d":"A wide creek winds between a tree-lined bank at the left and hazy mountains at the right, the bank curving deeply like a backward capital B. Marshy grass grows in tufts and palms stand in a grove, while one figure in only a white hip cloth carries a long stick near the shore and dabs of brown and muted pink suggest others farther along. A butter-yellow sun, low in the pale turquoise sky, shines through the palms and lays a soft streak on the still water. It is a small oil on board, 24.5 by 32.2 centimeters.","i":"Camille Pissarro was born on St. Thomas in 1830 and left for Paris in 1855. This view, signed with the Paris address and dated 1856, was therefore painted from memory and sketches of the island he had left, a tropical landscape of his youth. The little figures are local islanders, observed with a straightforward, unsentimental eye. These early works show him learning from Corot's tonal landscapes and from his travels with the Danish painter Fritz Melbye.","c":"The charm of this small oil is its freshness: the curving shoreline, the low sun and the slender band of reflected light are handled with a confidence rare in a painter in his mid-twenties. The figures are slight, and the picture is more a keepsake of a place than the investigation of light Pissarro would pursue later. As an early Caribbean landscape, painted from memory, it has real documentary and personal interest."},"nga:66428":{"d":"Two island women stand facing each other on a dirt path beside the sea, one balancing a flat tray of white cloths on her head and steadying it with a hand, the other, her back to the viewer, with a basket on her arm. The first wears an off-white dress with the skirt bunched up and a green, red and brown head scarf knotted by one ear; the second wears an aquamarine dress and a garnet scarf. The path runs into the distance beside a curving shore, under a milky sky, and small dabs of color suggest more figures in the surf.","i":"Pissarro painted this scene in Paris in 1856, a year after he left his native St. Thomas, working from memory and sketches. Carrying trays and baskets for market or laundry was familiar work on the island, and the painter, himself an islander, shows these women without exoticism or condescension. The subject, ordinary labor and conversation, anticipates the peasants and market women of his mature French landscapes.","c":"A small, direct study of figures in a landscape, with a clear, simple design: the two women form a compact central group, the curve of the shore leads the eye, and the colors of their clothes are sharp against the earth. Drawing and space are somewhat naive, but the sympathy for the subjects is genuine and the theme unusual for French painting of the time. It is an early document of Pissarro's lifelong interest in working people."},"met:437299":{"d":"A dirt road with wheel ruts curves up from the lower foreground into a green hillside village. A woman in a white dress and bonnet stands at a turn of the road at the right, beside slim poplars. Beyond her, a cluster of cream-walled houses with grey roofs nestles among trees, with strips of green and brown fields rising to a plateau where a church spire and more poplars stand against the sky. At the right a mass of dark green hedge and trees fills the edge, and above them towering white cumulus clouds roll across a blue sky. The signature and date “C. Pissarro 1867” are at the lower right. Oil on canvas, about 87 by 115 centimetres.","i":"Pissarro settled at Pontoise, north-west of Paris, in 1866, and this view of the Jalais hillside is among the first great pictures of the place that would be his base for years. It was shown at the Salon of 1868, where Émile Zola praised its honest modernity, writing that one feels that man has passed by, turning and cutting the earth. The Met notes the influence of Corot, Pissarro's teacher, in the firm, simple masses and quiet light, and says the work helped establish Pissarro's reputation as an innovative painter of the rural landscape and anticipated his part in the first Impressionist exhibition of 1874.","c":"Pissarro builds the picture on a plain, sturdy structure of road, slope, village and plateau, and fills it with convincing light, especially in the big clouds. It is honest, unshowy and rather Corot-like, with muted tones that make the greens seem damp. What it lacks is the sparkle that Impressionism would give him: the brushwork is solid, the shadows are dark, and the woman on the road is a stiff note. It is a fine foundation rather than a finished style, and Zola's attention to it shows how early the critics saw what he was doing."},"nga:72886":{"d":"White buildings with red-tile roofs sit small among a patchwork of lush green farm fields rolling over hills, in a nearly square, sun-drenched view loosely painted with visible strokes of spring, clover and pine green and touches of sand brown. The horizon lies about three-quarters of the way up, under white clouds in a pale blue sky. Tiny figures walk the paths or work the crops; at lower left a man in a straw hat, white shirt and blue overalls walks away, and another person, perhaps a woman in a white cap, stands at a fence nearby.","i":"Pissarro lived at Pontoise, northwest of Paris, from 1866 to 1868, and he painted its fields, hillsides and kitchen gardens as places where people lived and worked, not as picturesque scenery. Signed and dated 1868, the view shows his debt to Corot and Courbet in its solid construction, while the open brushwork and sunlight look forward to the Impressionism he and Monet developed in the early 1870s. Les Pâtis was a locality of the town.","c":"The picture is among Pissarro's most assured early landscapes: the broad, light-filled view, the careful placing of the farm buildings among the fields and the small figures that give scale without anecdote reflect a mature, unsentimental vision. The brushwork is still conservative compared with what followed, and the color remains within a green and brown range; yet the honesty of observation and the dignity given to farm labor mark him already as a painter of the working countryside."},"met:437317":{"d":"A modest still life of apples, a pitcher, a cup and a knife, painted in oil on canvas about 46 by 57 centimeters in 1872. The museum's keywords also list a jug, so the exact grouping, the table, any cloth and the colors are left unstated. Still life was a rare subject for Pissarro, a painter of village streets, fields and gardens, and this picture belongs to the early 1870s, when he lived at Pontoise.","i":"In 1872 Pissarro was living at Pontoise, and Cézanne came to work beside him. The two shared a conviction that a humble subject, painted honestly, was as worthy as any grand theme, and apples would become Cézanne's signature motif. For Pissarro, a still life was a way of exploring color and handling in a controlled setting, apart from the changing light of the outdoors, and a break from landscape.","c":"A quiet, unglamorous picture, with a direct handling that reflects the shared experiments of Pissarro and Cézanne at Pontoise. It lacks the magisterial structure that Cézanne would give apples, but Pissarro's still life has a virtue of its own: a humane, unforced sense of objects in everyday use. As a rare work in a genre he seldom attempted, it is a welcome glimpse of another side of his talent."},"nga:52195":{"d":"A tree with emerald leaves and heavy white blossom stands in a dirt field, silhouetted against a vibrant blue sky and taking up most of the left half, dappling the ground with shadow. A bare, gnarled tree grows in its shade, and a line of trees recedes to the right along a narrow path. Farther back a woman in a white bonnet, brown shawl and blue skirt bends to the ground, while a man in a dark cap with a tan bag over his shoulder walks toward her. The paint is applied in loose dabs and strokes.","i":"The orchard in bloom is a classic spring subject, joining the rural labor of the fruit-grower to the year's first brilliance, and Pissarro painted such scenes in the early 1870s as he turned fully to Impressionist method. The tiny man and woman, workers in the orchard rather than strollers, show his habit of making landscape a place of work and not merely a view. Louveciennes, west of Paris, was a village where Pissarro had lived in 1869 and 1870.","c":"Pissarro balances a fresh, flickering touch with a clear, stable structure here: the big tree and the receding row make a firm design, and the white blossom against deep blue sky is a lovely high note. The two small figures are slightly generic and the foreground is thin, but the picture's lightness and truth to season make it a model of early Impressionist landscape and one of the most attractive of his pictures from the early 1870s."},"nga:66429":{"d":"From a slightly raised viewpoint, a bare, gnarled tree with a skeletal canopy stands beside a gray dirt path that leads back to a hamlet. A lattice-like fence runs across the width of the canvas: a man in a sky-blue shirt and gray cap leans his forearms on it, apparently talking with a woman in a navy dress and tan hat on the other side, while a second woman with a walking stick moves away down the path. Beyond a gully and a white picket fence, village roofs of terracotta, tan and blue sit beneath flax-brown trees and a light blue sky.","i":"Pissarro painted The Fence in 1872, the year he settled again at Pontoise and began to concentrate on the daily life of the villages around it. The brief conversation across a fence, a bare tree and a path into the village make an image of rural community seen without picturesque embellishment. His peasants were neither noble nor comic but ordinary people at their business, a stance that distinguished him from Millet's heroic farmers and from the sentimental rustics of the Salon.","c":"The Fence is a modest picture with a strong idea: the fence cuts across the canvas like a ruled line, giving geometry to loosely handled paint and setting the people on either side in a quiet relation. The hamlet is only sketched, so the effect is slightly dry, a record rather than an event. But the unforced composition and honesty about rural life show why Pissarro's early Impressionist work endures."},"nga:164950":{"d":"A man and a woman, tiny against a massive blue sky, stand in a sunny harvested field beside a long line of about a dozen golden-brown, cone-shaped haystacks. The woman, in a rose-pink dress with white apron and cap, bends over; the man, in a white shirt, dark vest and blue trousers, rests a scythe against his shoulder. A muted blue shadow cuts across the flax-yellow ground, dark green trees line the low horizon, and lavender, blush-pink and white clouds drift over an ice-blue sky. The brushwork is loose and visible throughout.","i":"Haystacks mark the end of the harvest, and Pissarro, who painted the farmland of the Ile-de-France around Pontoise throughout the 1870s, treated them as part of the working landscape, not as symbols. Signed and dated 1873, the picture comes from the years when he painted beside Cézanne at Pontoise and was helping to organize the first Impressionist exhibition of 1874. The tiny workers, dwarfed by field and sky, express his unsentimental respect for rural labor.","c":"The huge sky is the real subject, and Pissarro paints it with fine gradations of lavender, pink and ice-blue above a brilliantly simple field; the haystacks lead the eye along the picture and the two small figures give scale. The paint is somewhat thin and the color, for all its delicacy, lacks the intensity of Monet's contemporary pictures, but the unforced balance and the tenderness toward the harvest landscape are very much his own."},"met:437300":{"d":"A cowherd tends cattle near a farm, with a horse-drawn wagon, trees and a house, in a wide landscape about 55 by 92 centimeters, dated 1874. The museum's keywords list cattle, a cow, a woman, a wagon, a farm and a house, so the cowherd, apparently a woman, and the exact arrangement of the animals and buildings are left unstated. Valhermeil is a hamlet near Auvers-sur-Oise, in the valley northwest of Paris where Pissarro worked in the early 1870s.","i":"Pissarro painted the farms and fields around Pontoise and Auvers in these years, and in 1874 took part in the first Impressionist exhibition, an event in which he was a central figure. His peasants are not picturesque ornaments but part of the working landscape: people at their daily tasks, seen without sentiment. The interest in rural labor, which he shared with Millet, remained with him throughout his life.","c":"A sturdy early Impressionist landscape. Pissarro builds the scene from clear bands of field, farm and sky, with broken touches of color for foliage, and places the cowherd and the cattle as part of the working land. Beside Monet's brilliance his color is earthier and his construction more solid, qualities that Cézanne admired. It is a picture of calm, workmanlike strength more than of dazzle."},"met:437301":{"d":"Women and children enjoy a public garden at Pontoise. The museum's keywords record a woman, a child, a ball, a stroller, trees and human figures, in an oil on canvas about 60 by 73 centimeters, dated 1874, so the arrangement of the figures, the paths and the light are left unstated. Pontoise is a town on the Oise northwest of Paris, where Pissarro lived for much of the 1870s.","i":"Rather than the farms and fields he usually painted, Pissarro here turned to the leisure of the town: families in a municipal garden, a distinctly modern subject, since the public park was where the middle classes took their children for air. The interest links him to Monet and Renoir, who were painting the new pleasures of the 1870s, but his figures are ordinary townspeople, with no particular glamour.","c":"The picture records modern life without emphasis: the figures are small and summarized in dabs, and become part of the leafy pattern more than the focus. Pissarro's gift for balancing people, trees and light is at work, and the picture has a gentle humanity. It lacks the glamour of Renoir's gardens, but offers an honest, democratic view of a provincial town at leisure."},"met:437302":{"d":"Barges lie on the river Oise at Pontoise. The title and the museum's keywords record barges, a river and a boat, in an oil on canvas about 46 by 55 centimeters, dated 1876, so the number of vessels, the bank, the buildings and the light are left unstated. The Oise carried goods between the countryside and Paris, and the picture belongs to Pissarro's years in the town.","i":"The Oise was a working river, and barges were part of the economic life of the towns along it. Pissarro generally preferred working landscapes to leisure ones, and here the cargo boats stand for the commerce that linked Pontoise to the capital. The subject also let him exploit the Impressionist interest in water: reflections, moving light and the soft surface of a river.","c":"A balanced, quietly confident river view: horizontal barges, reflections and sky are organized in broad bands, and the touch is loose without being agitated. Pissarro's Impressionism of the 1870s is more structured than that of his friends, as if a firm composition gave the freedom of the brushwork a place to rest. A well-made picture, without drama, and typical of what he did best."},"met:437316":{"d":"A road runs on the slope known as the Côte des Grouettes, on the edge of Pontoise, with trees and two figures, a man and a woman, in an upright canvas about 74 by 60 centimeters, probably painted in 1878. The museum's keywords record the road, trees, a man, a woman and a landscape, so the houses, the walls, the weather and the figures' activity are left unstated.","i":"Pissarro returned to this slope in the late 1870s, finding in its road, trees and cottages an arrangement of diagonal paths and layered planes. In 1877 Cézanne again worked beside him at Pontoise, and the geometrical clarity of this kind of composition is often linked to their exchanges. The figures, peasants in country dress, are part of the place, not the point of the picture.","c":"The tall format and the steep road give the composition a structure that is unusual for the period, and the rich greens and earth colors are woven together with a touch that shows the discipline Pissarro shared with Cézanne. The figures are small but placed with precision. It is a sturdy example of the Pontoise years and shows why Pissarro was the most solid of the Impressionists."},"met:437303":{"d":"A washerwoman is shown in an upright canvas about 73 by 59 centimeters, dated 1880, and labeled a study. The museum's keywords record a woman, a cap, a neckerchief and a stripe, so her pose, the tub or laundry, the setting and the colors are left unstated. As a study it may have been made in preparation for a larger composition, as Pissarro often prepared his figure pictures of these years.","i":"In about 1880 Pissarro began painting peasant women at work in large, serious figure pictures, and studies like this led to them. His peasants owe something to Millet but are less sentimental, and his sympathy for working people, which later took the form of anarchist convictions, made him attentive to labor. The washerwoman, a familiar figure in Degas and Daumier, here gains dignity by being shown close up.","c":"A good study is judged by its truth, and the plain, close-up figure with her cap and neckerchief has a patient solidity, built with broad brushwork and without flattery. Pissarro dignifies labor by simply looking at it. It lacks the finish of a completed picture, but the directness is the point, and the picture is a reminder that his figure paintings began in humble observation."},"nga:46674":{"d":"A woman stands with her body turned slightly to the right and her head bowed, her left hand on her hip. Dark hair shows under a red-and-white patterned head scarf; she wears a dark long-sleeved top and a light blue skirt, and her expression is neutral. Behind her the background is entirely dense green leaves with a few brown branches, which flattens the space into a screen of foliage. The visible, feathery brushstrokes keep the figure and leaves in one texture.","i":"From the late 1870s Pissarro increasingly made the peasant woman, shown at work or at rest in the fields and gardens near Pontoise, a principal subject. He admired Millet's dignified rural figures but preferred ordinary, unposed moments to Millet's solemnity. A standing woman with a hand on her hip and a downcast face is a type rather than a portrait of a named sitter, and in 1880 such figures were a conscious choice to show the workers of the countryside as modern subjects.","c":"The figure is simple and solid, and the wall of leaves lets Pissarro unite figure and setting in a single weave of brushwork, an idea he would pursue through the 1880s. The pose is slightly stiff and the woman's thoughts are closed to the viewer; the green is also dense enough to feel airless. Still, the picture shows the thoughtful, democratic seriousness of his peasant subjects and points toward his later, more systematic figure paintings."},"nga:155712":{"d":"A young girl sits in a grassy landscape, shown from the lap up in a vertical picture, angled to the right and looking off from heavy-lidded, aqua-colored eyes. A wide-brimmed straw hat tied with a thin brick-red ribbon covers most of her brown hair; she wears a sky-blue pinafore over a collared shirt with vertical blue stripes, her hands folded loosely in her lap. Shadows under her chin are tinged with green, and dappled greens behind her suggest filtered sunlight through trees. Visible dashes of green and blue give the whole a soft look.","i":"In the early 1880s Pissarro, living near Pontoise, turned from landscapes with small figures to larger pictures of peasant girls and women, shown at rest or at work in the fields. He treated them as ordinary people of the countryside, not as sentimental types, and often placed them so close to the foliage that figure and setting blend. A girl in a straw hat with folded hands is not a named sitter but a type, a modern counterpart to the shepherdesses of earlier painting, rendered without gloss or story.","c":"The picture's strength is its unity: the green tints on the girl's skin and the dappled foliage tie figure and place into one softly vibrating surface, and the heavy-lidded gaze gives her a quiet interior life. The pose is simple and the expression almost blank, and some will find the brushwork too even; but the absence of anecdote and the respect for the sitter are characteristic of Pissarro's best figure work of these years."},"nga:164952":{"d":"Two young peasant women sit close together in a green field, seen from slightly above. The nearer one has her back to the viewer, in a blue-and-white striped shirt, a long skirt flecked with muted red and yellow, a blue apron and a red-and-white checked cap, and looks toward her companion, who turns her head over her shoulder in profile, in a yellow kerchief and brown dress. A light brown cow and a white cow stand a short way off, slender trunks and dense hedges frame the field, and a band of silvery water lies far back. The grass is tightly packed strokes of many greens.","i":"Pissarro painted this resting pair in 1882, the year he left Pontoise for Osny, in the series of rural figure paintings that occupied him in the late 1870s and early 1880s. The title says they are resting, and the cows suggest they have been herding or watching them; their idle pause is the subject, an unusual moment in an art that usually showed peasants at work. Such pictures show his sympathy for rural labor, and they also answered a market that liked wholesome rustic scenes.","c":"The close, slightly raised viewpoint and the strong blue and yellow accents of the clothing turn two simple figures into an affecting double portrait of rest, and the dense, vibrating grass shows him already testing the small, regular touch of his later technique. The relationship between the girls is only lightly suggested, and the cows are generic; yet the picture has a calm, unforced truthfulness that his later, more systematized figure pictures sometimes lack."},"nga:66430":{"d":"Across the Thames, a long, flat bridge runs parallel to a hazy skyline, its line crossing the picture a third of the way up. Buildings with spires and towers, the Palace of Westminster and the Abbey among them, stretch along the horizon from the right, and a sky of cream white, pale blue and shell pink is mirrored in the water. Small boats gather at left, and three larger ferries, crowded with passengers made of minute daubs of black, golden yellow, crimson and blue, cross at right in short flat strokes of flame red and midnight blue. It is signed and dated 1890.","i":"Pissarro visited London in 1890, where his eldest son Lucien lived, and painted views of the river, parks and suburbs; he had earlier lived there in exile during the Franco-Prussian War, in 1870 to 1871, when he studied Turner and Constable. Charing Cross Bridge shows the Thames as a working highway, with ferries crowded with passengers and the Houses of Parliament as a distant, softened skyline, a typical Impressionist treatment of a modern city in atmosphere.","c":"Pissarro suppresses detail in favor of weather and light, and the pale, closely related tones of sky and river make a quiet, almost silvery harmony; the passengers, reduced to colored flecks, bring a pulse of life. The long horizontal of the bridge divides the canvas rather mechanically, and the skyline is only a hint, so the picture lacks the drama of Monet's later London views. Its delicacy and clear structure make it a fine, restrained city view."},"nga:52197":{"d":"A game is played on the central green lawn of an open space, with buildings in the distance wrapped in trees. A few distant figures stand or run, though their details are hard to make out, and the whole scene is built from small dots of paint that make everything look slightly blurred. Above lies a light blue sky with large white clouds. The canvas, 54.3 by 73 centimeters, is dated 1891, and the green of the title lies beside the royal palace of Hampton Court in southwest London.","i":"In 1886 Pissarro adopted the dotted, color-theory technique of Seurat and Signac, and he used it through the late 1880s while keeping his own love of rural and suburban scenes. This English green, with a game in progress, belongs to the end of that experiment, painted when he was already doubting that the method's slowness suited his temperament. The picture also reflects his visits to London, where his son Lucien lived, and his curiosity about English open spaces.","c":"The dots create a shimmering, hazy surface that suits a summer lawn, and the placing of tiny figures and low buildings is graceful. But the technique, for Pissarro, was always a discipline rather than a native language: the surface is a little mechanical and the figures lack the vitality of his looser work. It is an interesting, handsome document of his Neo-Impressionist phase, not its most compelling statement."},"met:437304":{"d":"Two young women are shown on a farm in this large horizontal canvas, about 90 by 117 centimeters, painted in 1891 and 1892. The museum's keywords record only women and a farm, and classify the picture as genre art, so their poses, clothing, the farm buildings and the light are left unstated. It belongs to the peasant pictures of Pissarro's later years at Éragny, a village on the river Epte northwest of Paris, where he lived from 1884.","i":"Pissarro had adopted Seurat's pointillist method between about 1886 and 1890, then abandoned it as too rigid. This picture, painted just afterward, combines what he had learned from that phase, careful color and balance, with a freer touch. The women, treated as monumental and thoughtful, reflect his lifelong sympathy for rural labor. They are probably local village women, as in his other peasant pictures, and not individual portraits.","c":"The large format and the calm gravity of the two figures give the painting a quiet monumentality. The color is richer and the touch more fluid than in the stricter pointillist works that preceded it, so Pissarro reconciles discipline with warmth. The faces are generalized and the moment eventless, but the tenderness is real. It is one of his most ambitious figure pictures, and it deserves to be seen slowly."},"met:437305":{"d":"A woman in an apron and cap washes laundry in a garden, in a small vertical oil on canvas about 46 by 38 centimeters, dated 1893. The museum's keywords record the woman, apron, cap, laundry, garden, washing and work, so her pose, the tub, the foliage and the colors are left unstated. Éragny is the village on the river Epte, northwest of Paris, where Pissarro lived from 1884.","i":"At Éragny in the 1890s Pissarro painted the women who worked in and around his own garden, and everyday chores, from laundry to feeding poultry, became a recurring subject. Washing had a long lineage in nineteenth-century art, from Daumier to Degas, but Pissarro's emphasis is on the dignity of daily tasks in the open air, without anecdote or satire.","c":"A warm, unsentimental image of work, with the woman absorbed in her task and the garden's leaves breaking into dabs of color around her. The small format gives freshness and intimacy, and the brushwork is lively but contained. It is a small, honest picture that shows Pissarro's late attachment to the rhythms of life at Éragny, and its modest scale is part of its appeal."},"met:437306":{"d":"A nude woman bathes in a woodland stream, in an oil on canvas about 60 by 73 centimeters, dated 1895. The museum's keywords record a woman, a stream, a forest, trees, nudity and bathing, so her pose, the depth of the water, the density of the trees and the colors are left unstated. The picture belongs to a group of bather subjects that Pissarro painted in the mid-1890s, when he was in his sixties.","i":"Nudes were rare in Pissarro's earlier work, which concentrated on clothed peasants and townspeople. In the 1890s he took up the bather in landscape, a theme that brought him close to Cézanne and Renoir and to a long tradition of nymphs and Diana's bathing. His bathers are not goddesses: they seem to be local models, shown in plain nakedness, which fits his preference for the ordinary over the ideal.","c":"The best of these late nudes balance figure and landscape with tenderness: the body is built from the same broken touch as the leaves and water, so that figure and place seem to share one light. Pissarro is no draftsman of the ideal nude, and the anatomy is plain, but the harmony of greens, pinks and blues is persuasive. A gentle, unglamorous variation on an old theme."},"met:437307":{"d":"A row of poplars rises in a garden landscape at Éragny, in a tall oil on canvas about 93 by 65 centimeters, dated 1895. The museum's keywords record poplars, trees, a garden and a landscape, so the number of trees, the sky, the colors and any figures are left unstated. Éragny, the village on the river Epte where Pissarro lived from 1884, supplied him with subjects from his own garden and fields for nearly twenty years.","i":"Poplars were a favorite motif of the Impressionists: Monet painted a famous series of them along the Epte in 1891, and Pissarro's picture shares that fascination with tall, slender, light-catching trees that repeat like a pattern. The upright format and the rhythm of trunks also recall Japanese prints, which Pissarro admired. The picture shows his late practice of painting the same corner of his property across the seasons and in changing light.","c":"Pissarro's late landscapes combine a gentle, shimmering touch with firm structure, and this one makes a good case for both. The strong verticals of the poplars set against the lower garden give the composition a rhythm that is more austere than Monet's decorative variations on the theme. It is a work of mature calm, less spectacular than Monet's poplars but more intimate, and it shows the Éragny years at their best."},"nga:46529":{"d":"A pale woman stands on a riverbank in this small vertical picture, holding a white cloth across her thighs against her mostly nude body. She occupies the lower right, her body and face angled to the right as she looks down, blond hair swept up and face flushed pink. A pile of purple, blue and brown by her feet suggests discarded clothing. A tree curves up behind her to fill the top third with its green canopy, and to the left a cool river of white, pink and slate blue runs toward distant trees under ivory clouds. Short, tightly packed strokes create a blurred effect.","i":"Pissarro, who had been a painter of peasants clothed and at work, turned in the 1890s to nude bathers in the countryside, a subject taken up by Renoir, Cézanne and the Neo-Impressionists, the classical theme of woman and nature seen through modern means. His bathers are not goddesses but countrywomen at the water's edge, a fusion of the nude tradition with his lifelong interest in rural life. The motif is modest and intimate, and the small size suggests a study of figure within landscape.","c":"The nude is gently modeled, and the glow of the flushed face and white cloth against the greens is attractive; but the drawing of the figure is not Pissarro's strength, and the pose is conventional. He is happier with leaves and water, where the small strokes dissolve into light. Among his bathers it is a pleasing minor work, a late exploration of a subject in which he was always less at ease than in landscape."},"nga:89683":{"d":"An older man with ruddy skin stands in the lower right of this vertical picture, holding a large green cabbage in both hands and looking down at it with his mouth open. He is shown from the hips up, facing left, in a short-brimmed brown cap with fringes of gray hair beneath, gray stubble, hollow cheeks and a fleshy double chin. A tan jacket under a seafoam-green apron touched with navy and peach strokes, and a white shirt collar, complete his dress. Behind him rows of round cabbages recede, and a speckled band of mauve, peach and white fills the top.","i":"The double date, 1883 to 1895, shows that Pissarro began the picture in the early 1880s and returned to it about a dozen years later. The subject belongs to his lifelong interest in rural workers: an old gardener regarding the product of his labor, shown without sentiment or anecdote. A sympathizer with anarchist ideas, Pissarro saw the peasant's work as dignified and, unlike Millet, presented it as everyday rather than heroic.","c":"The old man's face is the success: the open mouth, gray stubble and hollowed cheeks give a vivid, unidealized presence, and the cabbage, a vibrant green mass, becomes the center of a quiet drama of attention. The pose is somewhat awkward and the figure's relation to the rows behind is loose; yet the picture's frankness and gentle humor, rare in Pissarro's figures, make it one of his memorable late peasants."},"met:437308":{"d":"A broad view of Rouen from a high window looks across the Seine to a long bridge of low arches, busy with tiny figures and vehicles. A steamer in the foreground sends up a plume of white steam that climbs against the bridge, while a tall factory chimney at the right trails dark smoke across a pale, cloudy sky. Along the far bank, rows of steep roofs, chimneys and church spires are painted in warm creams, pinks and greys. The river fills the lower half of the canvas and is broken into short strokes of green, pink and silver that echo the sky. The signature and the year, 96, appear at the lower right.","i":"In 1896 Pissarro, then in his mid-sixties, stayed in Rouen and painted the Pont Boieldieu repeatedly from a hotel window, in different weather and at different hours, in the manner of the serial views Monet had made fashionable. A chronic eye complaint made painting outdoors difficult for him, and from the 1890s he often chose raised viewpoints over cities. Unlike Monet's cathedral, Pissarro's subject is the working port, with smoke, steam, shipping and crowds, and he treats it without nostalgia. Viewers in the 1890s could read such pictures as a record of a modern, industrial France, a theme he had long favoured.","c":"The sky does half the work. Pissarro spreads a soft, pearly overcast above a city of warm creams and rose roofs, and the whole picture holds together in that single tone. The steam plume and the dark chimney give two strong verticals against the long horizontal of the bridge, a neat balance. The crowd and the foreground boats are only suggested, which keeps the scene believable but leaves the lower left rather perfunctory. It lacks the drama of his Paris boulevards, yet it shows a veteran still adjusting his method with real intelligence."},"met:437309":{"d":"Seen from a high window, the port of Rouen lies under a damp, pearly haze. At the lower right a steamer with a tall bare mast is moored against a cobbled quay stacked with barrels and cargo, and a white plume of steam or smoke rolls up past its funnel. A crane and the rigging of another vessel cut in at lower left. Across the gray river a long line of warehouses and houses recedes into mist, with a small tug on the water. The sky is lilac and white, and the whole scene is built of short, soft touches of paint. It is signed and dated at lower right.","i":"In January 1896 Pissarro, then 65, began his second extended stay in Rouen, arriving on 20 January and painting a series of port views during the first three months of the year. He worked from a window at the Hôtel de Paris, looking over the quays to the Saint-Sever district. Most of the series includes the bridges across the Seine; this one leaves them out. Working from a window had become his habit in the 1890s, in Paris as well as Rouen, and it let him study how smoke, steam and damp changed the look of a modern working harbor.","c":"A late Impressionist city view that shows Pissarro could treat modern industry without gloom. The atmosphere is the real subject, and he renders it in pearl-gray, lilac and cream, with the steam making a movement of its own. The lack of a bridge, though, removes the long horizontal that gives most of the Rouen series its structure, and the eye wanders across a wide gray surface with only the steamer's diagonal to guide it. It is a quiet, assured painting rather than one of the series' strongest."},"met:437310":{"d":"A Paris boulevard in winter, seen from a high window, with buildings, a wide road, a street and carriages, in an oil on canvas about 65 by 81 centimeters, dated 1897. The museum's keywords record the Boulevard Montmartre, the buildings, the road, the carriages and winter, so the light, the figures and the colors are left unstated. Pissarro painted the view from a hotel room, in a series of about fourteen canvases of the boulevard in different weather and at different times of day.","i":"Pissarro's eyes troubled him in the 1890s, and doctors discouraged outdoor work, so he began painting city views from rented rooms. In 1897 he worked above the boulevard, as Monet did with his haystacks and cathedrals, on the same motif across seasons, hours and weather. The result is a record of modern Paris: traffic, crowds, shop fronts and trees, seen in the changing light that fascinated the Impressionists.","c":"A bold subject, handled with Pissarro's characteristic balance: the long diagonal of the boulevard gives depth, the bare trees and winter haze soften the architecture, and figures and carriages become a lively flicker of touches. The series is among his greatest achievements, and the winter morning is a worthy member of it. It shows an artist in his sixties still discovering new subjects, and a city seen with fresh, affectionate eyes."},"nga:46673":{"d":"From a high viewpoint, the picture looks down a wide, tree-lined avenue that runs diagonally up from the lower left, its sidewalks crowded with dozens of pedestrians in coats and hats. Horse-drawn carriages move in both directions, two omnibuses wait at the near curb with queues of passengers, and two brick-red hexagonal kiosks face each other down the street. Multistory buildings with shopfronts and rows of windows stretch across the canvas. Warm browns, sage green, slate blue and brick red dominate, in loose short and long dotted strokes, and sunlight dapples the scene.","i":"In the late 1890s Pissarro, in his sixties and troubled by an eye infection that made work outdoors difficult, began painting city views from hotel windows, from which he could observe the streets at leisure. The Parisian boulevards, remade by Haussmann a generation earlier, were the stage of modern life, and he painted them at different hours and seasons, in series like Monet's cathedrals. Signed and dated 1897, this morning view treats the boulevard as a place of crowds, traffic and shifting light.","c":"Pissarro handles the spectacle with great assurance: the diagonal of the street, the minute dabs for pedestrians and the warm morning light make the boulevard pulse with life, and the high viewpoint turns confusion into order. The touch is slightly uniform across the canvas, and the buildings are more schematic than the crowd, but its documentary richness and truth to the movement of a big city make it one of the best of his late Paris pictures."},"met:437311":{"d":"A narrow street in the old center of Rouen, the Rue de l'Épicerie, with the cathedral rising among the roofs and a market below, in an upright oil on canvas about 81 by 65 centimeters, dated 1898. The title says that the picture records an effect of sunlight. The museum's keywords record the cathedral, the market and the town, so the figures, the stalls and the colors are left unstated. Pissarro painted it during a stay in Rouen.","i":"Rouen, whose Gothic cathedral Monet had painted in a famous series a few years earlier, drew Pissarro in the 1890s too, but he chose the bustle of the streets and markets around it. Here the cathedral is a landmark above the roofs, and daily commerce is the subject. The mention of sunlight in the title reflects the series principle of the 1890s: the same motif changes with light and weather, so each canvas records a particular moment.","c":"An urban view of dense, shimmering texture: the narrow street compresses buildings, awnings and figures into a mosaic of warm and cool touches, and the sunlit passage gives focus. Pissarro's sense of structure keeps the busyness from dissolving, and the cathedral anchors the view in history. It is a lively, humane city picture by a painter who liked crowds, less celebrated than his Paris boulevards but of equal strength."},"nga:52198":{"d":"A woman bends double to tend a garden that stretches before two houses, in a horizontal view painted in short visible strokes of spring, pine and celery green and earthy browns. She wears a steel-gray skirt and an olive-green shirt, her hair bound up, and stoops over a light green patch near a bundle of sticks. Rows of leafy greens lie at the left, and a band of flowers, with touches of yellow, pink, coral and ruby red suggesting sunflowers and roses, runs almost the width of the canvas; behind rise a gray stone house with a dark roof and another with a brick-red roof.","i":"Pissarro settled at Eragny-sur-Epte, a village northwest of Paris, in 1884 and lived there for the rest of his life, and the garden of his own house became one of his favorite subjects. The tilled rows, the flowers and the stooping worker show the country life he valued, a household's practical garden that is also a source of beauty. Painted in 1898, it belongs to the late years in which he balanced Paris cityscapes with the peaceful repetition of village scenes at his doorstep.","c":"The garden is painted with Pissarro's gentle, observant vision, the varied small strokes making rows, flowers and walls into a single mosaic of greens and warm browns, with the flowers as bright accents. The figure is little more than a gesture, and the composition is quiet, even loosely organized, with no strong center; yet the contentment of the scene and its truth to light and season give it lasting charm, a cheerful late work."},"met:437312":{"d":"The Tuileries Garden in winter, seen on an afternoon with the city beyond, in an oil on canvas about 73 by 92 centimeters, dated 1899. The museum's keywords record the Tuileries Garden, the city, the garden and winter, so the figures, paths, trees and colors are left unstated. Pissarro painted several views of the garden from an apartment on the Rue de Rivoli, and this is one of two canvases at the Metropolitan Museum that share this title.","i":"Late in life Pissarro followed Monet's example of painting the same subject repeatedly to capture changes in light and weather. Because he painted the garden so often, related canvases hang side by side in museum collections, and slight changes of hour, angle or touch give each a different mood; the motif is a pretext for exploring light. The Tuileries had been the royal and imperial park of Paris, and Pissarro, a lifelong republican, painted it as a place of public leisure.","c":"The pleasure of a series is comparison, and a winter afternoon in the same garden invites the viewer to look for what changes: the cast of the light, the density of the branches, the flicker of figures. Pissarro's mature control is clear in the way the whole view, trees, buildings and sky, is bound into one wintry atmosphere. It is a refined, understated work by an old master who never lost his curiosity."},"met:437313":{"d":"A view of the Tuileries Garden in Paris on a spring morning, in an oil on canvas about 73 by 92 centimeters, dated 1899. The museum's keywords record only the Tuileries and a garden, so the trees, the paths, the figures and the colors are left unstated. Pissarro painted a group of Tuileries views from an apartment on the Rue de Rivoli, looking across the garden, in changing seasons and weather, in the last years of his life.","i":"The Tuileries, laid out afresh in the seventeenth century by Louis XIV's gardener, André Le Nôtre, was the great public garden at the heart of Paris. Pissarro, born in the Caribbean and long a painter of villages, found in it a new urban subject. The spring morning is one of several times of day and seasons he recorded from the same window, following Monet's practice of the series. The garden, once royal, had become the city's common promenade.","c":"One of the most serene of Pissarro's city pictures: the garden's trees and paths, softened by morning light, make a calm pattern, and the elevated view organizes everything into a quiet design. It trades the animation of his boulevards for a pastoral hush in the heart of the city. In his late sixties Pissarro shows a lightness of touch that younger painters might envy."},"met:437314":{"d":"A winter afternoon in the Tuileries Garden, with winter trees and the city beyond, in an oil on canvas about 74 by 92 centimeters, dated 1899. The museum's keywords record trees, the city, the garden and winter, so the figures, the paths, the sky and the colors are left unstated. The picture belongs to Pissarro's Tuileries series, painted from the window of a rented apartment looking over the garden, in the last years of his life.","i":"A winter afternoon in a city garden has its own light: low, cool and soft, with long shadows and the faint haze of a Paris afternoon. Pissarro, who loved to watch a single view change over the day and the seasons, painted the Tuileries in all weathers; to him the garden was a piece of open countryside set in the metropolis. The picture also records city leisure, since the gardens were a place for strolling for Parisians of every class.","c":"Winter trees give a structure that summer foliage would blur, so branches can make a delicate tracery against pale sky and distant buildings, and the picture's best passages come from that economy. It is quieter than his boulevard views, but more meditative. In late works like this Pissarro keeps the freshness of observation that made him the elder statesman of Impressionism, and a winter hush suits a painter in his late sixties."},"met:438738":{"d":"Haystacks stand in a field at Éragny on a morning in 1899, with a man at work and trees nearby, in an oil on canvas about 64 by 80 centimeters. The museum's keywords record haymaking, a man, a tree, haystacks and morning, so the number of stacks, the light and the colors are left unstated. Éragny is the village on the river Epte where Pissarro lived, not far from Giverny, where Monet was painting in the same years.","i":"Haystacks were the subject of Monet's famous series of 1890 and 1891, and Pissarro, who lived not far from Giverny, came to them with a different attitude: his stacks sit within the working farm, with a man haymaking, as part of the agricultural year and not as pure light-catching form. He never lost the interest in peasant labor that he had held since the 1860s. Morning in the title marks the time of day, an Impressionist concern.","c":"An unspectacular subject made luminous by observation. The haystacks give simple masses against which Pissarro sets the fields, trees and morning air, and the figure of the haymaker confirms that this is a working landscape. It lacks the formal daring of Monet's stacks, but it has an unforced humanity and a quiet harmony of touch. A late picture of great composure."},"nga:195850":{"d":"From high up, the view looks down and out over the Seine, where seven ferries and barges make diagonals across a river rippled in dashes of muted blue, fern green and tan. A dark tugboat chugs out at the bottom center, and at lower left a railed platform juts over the water, with at least six figures standing alone or in pairs, loosely touched in black, gray, yellow and plum and reflected in its wet surface. Spiky trees line the far side, an arched bridge crosses the river, and fog-gray and cream clouds hang over domes and rooflines fading to slate blue.","i":"In 1900 Pissarro painted a group of views of the Seine and the Louvre, working from a high window on days of changing weather, and the title records the effect he sought: afternoon, rain, a city under a lowered sky. Paris in the rain, wet pavements and gray air, was a favorite Impressionist subject, and the Seine's working boats show the city as a place of trade as well as monuments. The picture is among the late series by which he turned the city into a laboratory of light.","c":"The rain-damp air and the wet reflections are convincingly caught, and the diagonals of boats against the river's horizontals give the picture a strong, simple structure. The figures on the platform are only flecks, and the buildings, kept vague, fade into the haze, so the Louvre of the title is barely identifiable; but the delicate gray harmony is a fine expression of the Impressionist concern with weather."},"nga:52199":{"d":"Seen from above, a plaza opens beneath a tall peanut-brown building rising along the left edge, its three oversized stories under a high sloping roof, with a promenade of carriages before it. A row of green trees runs diagonally along the right, and beyond them the corners of a formal garden with sand-white paths lead back to a long line of light-brown buildings with tall windows, slate roofs, cupolas and towers. Tiny people gather in groups along the bottom edge in dabs of blue, yellow, red, black and white, under loosely painted white clouds and an ice-blue sky.","i":"The Place du Carrousel is the open court between the Louvre and the Tuileries Garden, and Pissarro painted it in 1900 from an upper-floor window, one of several series of views of central Paris made in his last years. The Tuileries Palace, burned in 1871, had once closed the court, and the open view reflects the city's changed shape. Such views let him study light and weather over a great public square while the figures below remain tiny, anonymous city-dwellers.","c":"The view's strength is its airy space: the sweep of the plaza, the diagonal of the trees and the long line of buildings are handled in a unified, light-filled palette, and the tiny figures give a lively sense of scale. The architecture is only sketched and the effect somewhat decorative, but the quiet clouds and the clarity of the design make it one of the most open and luminous of the late city views."},"met:436485":{"d":"A human skull, a vase of flowers and a scatter of money share an upright panel, 82.6 by 54 centimeters, dated 1603. The objects are the stock vocabulary of the vanitas, a still life whose subject is the emptiness of earthly possessions. Jacques de Gheyn II, better known as a draftsman and printmaker, painted few pictures, and this precisely observed, dated work belongs among the earliest independent vanitas still lifes of the Netherlands.","i":"Vanitas pictures remind viewers that beauty, wealth and knowledge perish: the skull stands for death, the flowers for beauty that wilts, and money for riches that cannot be taken to the grave. The theme, from Ecclesiastes and its vanity of vanities, drew strength in the Protestant, mercantile Netherlands around 1600 as Calvinist moralists warned against attachment to worldly goods. De Gheyn, an engraver and draftsman, brought a draftsman's exactness to the genre.","c":"Few paintings state the theme so economically: the stark skull against lively flowers is a lesson in contrasts, and the draftsman's precision makes every petal and coin convincing. The painting has the rigidity of an emblem rather than the richness of the later Dutch still life, and the arrangement is deliberately schematic. Its interest is historical as well as visual: an early, austere formulation of a genre that would become one of the glories of Dutch painting."},"met:435601":{"d":"A small panel, about 39 by 31 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood, shows the Virgin with the Christ Child, the favorite subject of Renaissance private devotion. Ercole Banci was an Italian painter documented in the early sixteenth century. The modest size and the old-fashioned technique of tempera make it a picture for a private room rather than for a church altar, where it would have been a focus for family prayer.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the most requested subject in Renaissance religious painting. Mary's devotion to her Son models the believer's own, and the Child's gestures often humanize the theology of the Incarnation. A small panel like this served as a prompt for prayer and as an emblem of a family's piety, and it could be carried from room to room or kept beside a bed.","c":"Pictures of this type live by the sweetness of the faces and the clarity of the design, and a small devotional panel from a regional workshop cannot compete in invention with the great painters of the period. Banci should be seen as a skilled, conservative maker of honest devotional images rather than an original. The tempera, an older method by the 1500s, adds to its traditional feel."},"met:436055":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child, attended by two angels, with a donor included at the foot of the picture, on a tall panel, 137.2 by 65.4 centimeters, in tempera on a gold ground, probably dated 1481. The gold background lifts the figures out of any earthly setting, and the narrow format suggests a panel from a polyptych or a small altarpiece. Vittore Crivelli, brother of the more famous Carlo, painted in the Marches of Italy, where such richly gilded panels remained in demand.","i":"A Madonna and Child enthroned with angels is the classic image of the Virgin as Queen of Heaven, and the donor, the man who paid for the picture, is included so that his piety would be remembered before the sacred figures, usually kneeling and smaller in scale. Panels like this stood on altars in parish churches and convents of the Marches, where local patrons commissioned gilded, ornate altarpieces to honor saints and secure intercession for themselves and their families.","c":"Vittore's pictures are charming for their ornament, gold, textiles and fine details, and the Madonna's grave sweetness is typical of the Crivelli family. He lacks his brother Carlo's nervous, sharp-edged intensity, and his figures are more placid and conventional, with stiff drawing and little spatial depth. As evidence of fifteenth-century devotion in the Marches the picture is valuable, and as a decorative object it is handsome."},"met:436056":{"d":"The Virgin and Child appear with two angels on a small tempera and gold panel, 55.6 by 40.6 centimeters, painted about 1481 to 1482, with a bird and fruit among the details. The size marks it as a devotional picture for a private room or small chapel, and the gold, fine pattern and delicate color are those of Vittore Crivelli's workshop in the Marches, which carried an ornate, gilded, Venetian-trained manner into central Italy.","i":"Fruit and a bird, the small notes of a devotional Madonna, were read as signs: fruit recalls the Fall of Adam and Eve and Christ as the new Adam who redeems it, and a bird in a child's hand, commonly a goldfinch, foretells the Passion. Angels, usually playing music or attending, honor the Virgin as queen. Such panels were meant for prayer and meditation at home, where families kept a sacred image close and its details were studied again and again.","c":"The little panel has the charm of its small decorative details: gold, richly worked robes, a tender relation between mother and child. The drawing is conventional and the faces are somewhat mask-like, and Vittore does not match his brother's sharp, tense line; yet the restrained devotion and fine finish make it an appealing example of provincial painting in the Marches of the later fifteenth century."},"met:10832":{"d":"A woman and a small child, named in the title as Lady Williams and her child, fill a large canvas, 127.3 by 101 centimeters, painted in 1783. The details include a cap with ribbon, lace and a fichu, a striped fabric and a toddler or infant, the fashionable dress of a lady of the 1780s. Ralph Earl painted it in England, where he lived from 1778 to 1785, and the large portrait of mother and child shows the influence of the British portrait school.","i":"Mother-and-child portraits flourished in eighteenth-century England, where Reynolds and Gainsborough made tender maternity a sign of a woman's virtue and a family's continuity. Earl, an American Loyalist sympathizer who had fled to London in 1778 and studied there, adapted that style for English sitters, using a graceful, informal pose and fashionable costume. A titled sitter, Lady Williams, would have commissioned the picture to record her status and her place as a mother.","c":"Earl is not Reynolds or Gainsborough, but this portrait shows how well he absorbed the English manner: the soft pose, the careful lace and the tender relationship between mother and child are handled with competence. The faces are somewhat stiff and the paint a little hard, as is typical of his work, and the composition is conventional. It is among the more polished pictures from his English years, before he returned to America and developed a plainer, more characterful style."},"nga:50307":{"d":"A man sits in an angled chair, facing slightly to his right, with a composed expression, a narrow nose, focused eyes and a prominent forehead framed by powdered, shoulder-length hair. He wears a brown coat with large buttons over a pale waistcoat and a white ruffled shirt, and holds a book in one hand, resting it on the arm of the chair, which is upholstered in red with gold studs. Behind him a bookcase filled with books is partly visible, and dark drapery provides contrast and depth. The canvas is about 87 by 74 centimetres and matches the portrait of his wife and son.","i":"The book in his hand and the bookcase behind him mark Dr. Rogers as a man of learning. Earl, who painted the Connecticut gentry after returning from England in 1785, liked to include the sitter's possessions in his portraits, a device that gives them a documentary charm. The red chair with brass studs was a status symbol too. The portrait forms a pair with that of his wife and son, dated the same year.","c":"Earl was at his best with men of position, and the sitter's calm face, the red chair and the bookcase present a convincing portrait of learned respectability. The drawing is firm and a little flat, with the brown coat blending into the dark ground, and the book is held more as an attribute than as something being read. It lacks the dash of his English-trained contemporaries but has an honest, workmanlike clarity that suits its subject."},"nga:50308":{"d":"A mother and her young son are the subjects of this canvas, about 86 by 73 centimetres and dated 1788, identified by the title as Martha Tennent Rogers, wife of David Rogers, and her son, probably Samuel Henry Rogers. The museum record gives no description of the figures' poses, dress or setting, so these cannot be reported with confidence. It matches in size and date Earl's portrait of her husband in the same collection.","i":"Ralph Earl returned to America in 1785 after seven years in England, where he had studied with Benjamin West, and settled in Connecticut, painting the local gentry in a direct, crisp manner that often puts sitters in their own rooms with a window view. A mother-and-child portrait presented the family as a continuing line, and the word probably in the title shows that the boy's identity is not certain.","c":"Without the details to judge, the fair verdict is about the artist: Earl's American portraits are direct, bright and a little stiff, with a love of the sitters' clothes and possessions and a flat, linear way of drawing faces that stands apart from the sophistication of his English training. A mother and child allowed him gentle domestic charm. For all their stiffness, his portraits of New England gentry are vivid records of the post-Revolutionary years."},"cma:162745":{"d":"At fifteen, Moses Seymour Jr. is the subject of a large canvas, 120.7 by 90.2 centimeters, holding a book and a cane. The attributes, the museum notes, suggest that the boy reads and walks in harmony with nature. His father, a former major in the Revolutionary War who lived in Litchfield, Connecticut, commissioned the portrait in 1789, and it descended in the sitter's family until it was given to the museum. Ralph Earl was the leading painter of the Connecticut River Valley.","i":"A book and a cane are the props of a young gentleman: reading denotes education and a cane marks the leisured walker, and together, under the sign of nature, they present a youth shaped by both study and the countryside. After the Revolution, wealthy Connecticut families such as the Seymours of Litchfield sought portraits to record their rank and cultivation, and Earl, who had trained in London, met the demand for a more elegant, Europeanized style of portraiture.","c":"Earl's strength is plain candor: the sitter's boyish seriousness is recorded without flattery, and the props are clearly and honestly displayed. The drawing is stiffer than his English teachers' and the pose somewhat wooden, so the picture has the charm of the American provincial portrait rather than metropolitan sophistication; but its freshness and good humor make it one of the more attractive of Earl's portraits of the late 1780s."},"met:10830":{"d":"A young man in an elegant buff coat stands full length in a shop, one hand resting on a green-topped counter and the other holding a folded paper at his side. He wears a white waistcoat, a lace jabot and cuffs, black knee breeches with buckles and white stockings, and his powdered hair is tied back. An open door at the left reveals shelves stacked with bolts of coloured and patterned cloth. The counter holds a quill in an inkwell and a book, and beneath it a bookcase is lined with rows of tan leather volumes. The floor is polished, and the colour is smooth and flat. A signature and date are at the lower left.","i":"The Met says that the itinerant portrait painter Ralph Earl depicted the elegantly dressed dry-goods merchant Elijah Boardman (1760-1823) in his shop in New Milford, Connecticut. The unusual setting let Boardman display his stock: through the open door appear the silks, wools, printed cottons and linens that he imported from India, China and Europe through English ports. His shop also sold sugar, rum, molasses and indigo from the Spanish West Indies, which tied his Connecticut customers to Atlantic trade. Boardman later served in the United States Senate. Earl, who had spent years in England, painted the portrait in 1789.","c":"This is a direct image of American commerce in its period: a man proud of what he sells, painted among his goods. Earl gets the bright cloth and the confident stance right, and the library below the counter adds a claim to learning. The execution is plainer than the idea. The perspective is a little tilted, the figure seems taller than the room can hold, and the face is blandly drawn. Earl's English training shows in the pose, but the flat colour and firm outlines are plain New England taste. The result is stiff and endearing."},"met:14419":{"d":"A young woman appears with a book in this portrait of Esther Boardman, oil on canvas, 108 by 81.3 centimeters, painted in 1789. She is the later Esther Boardman Burrall, and the book shows her as a woman of education, an accomplishment that families of the new American republic took pride in. Ralph Earl's portraits of the late 1780s are direct, firmly drawn and bright in color, with the sitter placed squarely before the viewer.","i":"After 1786 Earl worked in Connecticut, painting prosperous families of the western part of the state in a plainer style than he had learned in London. A woman with a book reflects the education that girls of well-off families received and the new republic's ideal of women as readers and moral guides of their households. Women's education became a theme of the 1780s and 1790s, and a book in a portrait pays tribute to it.","c":"Earl's likenesses are plain-spoken, with firm drawing and clear light, and they give sitters a presence that more polished painters often lose. This picture is straightforward and decorous, with the book as its main prop; the rendering of fabric is careful and the whole is calm. It is less inventive than his best group portraits, but as a sober likeness of an American woman of the 1780s it is convincing."},"nga:34638":{"d":"A man in a navy-blue tailcoat, cream waistcoat, knee breeches and white stockings stands before a deep landscape, at the right of this tall canvas. One ankle is crossed in front of the other and his body is angled to the left; he looks out with brown eyes under wide grey brows, his grey hair brushed back over his ears. A white cravat, ruffled cuffs and silver shoe buckles mark his dress. He holds a black hat in one hand and rests the other on a tall walking stick. Fields reach back to a winding river and a town with a church spire and manor house under a sunset sky.","i":"Earl's full-length portraits of Connecticut merchants and landowners are among his most ambitious works, painted after he returned from England in 1785. Daniel Boardman, a prosperous merchant, is shown in the pose of an English gentleman, derived from British portraiture, and the landscape behind him, with river, church and manor, presents his place in the community and may show the town where he lived. Such a picture declared status in a young republic where land and trade were the basis of standing.","c":"A confident, large-scale portrait: the full-length format, crossed-ankle stance and sunset landscape claim for a Connecticut merchant the dignity of a British country gentleman, and the details of dress, from peach buttons to silver buckles, are painted with relish. The figure is flat and a little rigid, and the face blunt rather than refined, but Earl's honesty is part of the charm. It works through directness rather than subtlety."},"met:10833":{"d":"Marinus Willett, a soldier, appears with a sword and a tree on an unusually large canvas, 231.8 by 142.2 centimeters, painted about 1791, large enough for a full-length figure near life size. The sword marks him as a veteran of the Revolutionary War, and the tree suggests an outdoor setting rather than a drawing room. Ralph Earl, who by then had settled in Connecticut, painted such large, confident, direct portraits for sitters who wanted their service and standing set down for posterity.","i":"Willett (1740 to 1830) was a New York officer best remembered for his part in defending Fort Stanwix in 1777, and he later served as sheriff and mayor of New York City. The sword and soldierly bearing of the portrait celebrate that service, in the years when veterans of the Revolution were honored as founders of the republic. Earl made a specialty of full-length portraits of men whose standing came from wartime and public service.","c":"Earl's directness gives Willett the weight of a plain-spoken soldier, and the large scale is impressive. But Earl was a provincial painter with an unforgiving directness, and the figure is stiffly set, the anatomy and perspective awkward and the surface somewhat hard. Where Copley or Stuart would idealize or probe, Earl records, and the result is an honest and imposing but unsubtle image, valuable as a portrait of a Revolutionary War officer as seen by his own time."},"met:10835":{"d":"A mother and her children gather in a large horizontal canvas, 162.6 by 217.8 centimeters, painted in 1798. The title names the mother as Mrs. Noah Smith, identified as Chloe Burrall Smith, and the group includes a boy, a girl and an infant, with a cravat among the details of costume. The size and the grouped siblings make it an ambitious family portrait, one of the largest pictures of Earl's career, built from clearly drawn figures in firm, bright color.","i":"The family group, with the mother as its center, was the way a prosperous New England household recorded itself in the early republic: affection, order and well-being displayed together, with children dressed and ranked by age. The painting was probably commissioned by Noah Smith, the husband who is absent from the frame, to honor his wife and heirs. Such pictures show the self-image of the new gentry, in which domestic virtue was a civic as well as private value.","c":"As a group portrait it is ambitious and engaging: the children are individually observed, the mother's presence is steady, and the clear, bright color and firm outlines give the picture an immediacy that polished European portraits often lack. The composition is stiff, the figures slightly doll-like and the space shallow; yet the naive honesty is the point, and as a document of American family life around 1800 this is among Earl's most appealing late works."},"nga:34090":{"d":"A portrait of Thomas Earle, in oil on canvas, about 96 by 86 centimetres, dated 1800. The museum record gives no description, so the sitter's pose, dress and setting cannot be reported with confidence. In his last years Ralph Earl painted prosperous New England sitters in a plain, direct style, often seated in a room with a window view and attributes of their trade or learning, and this nearly square canvas probably follows that pattern.","i":"The sitter bears the name Earle, a spelling variant of the artist's surname, and any relationship is not recorded here. The picture dates from the year before Earl's death in 1801. A portrait of this date shows the plain American style of the early republic, in which rank was proved by possessions, clothes and bearing rather than by an inherited coat of arms.","c":"Without close detail to judge, it is fair to say that Earl's late portraits keep the crispness and unfussy honesty of his best work but sometimes show the stiffness and thin modelling of a painter working fast for provincial patrons. Their strengths are directness and a documentary eye. They are more valuable as records of a young republic's gentry than as feats of art, though their candour has lasting appeal."},"met:657683":{"d":"The dead Christ is mourned by the Virgin Mary, Saint John the Evangelist and other men and women on a small oil panel, 55.9 by 45.7 centimeters, painted about 1514 to 1516. Mazzolino of Ferrara characteristically packed small, vividly colored figures into a shallow space, and the crowded, close-set arrangement of the mourners suits a picture for private devotion, a small panel to be studied at close range.","i":"The Lamentation, the grieving over Christ's body after it is taken from the cross, stood between the Crucifixion and the Entombment in the Passion cycle, and gave painters an occasion for emotion: Mary's sorrow, John's grief and the tenderness of the women who prepare the body. Devotional panels like this invited viewers to join the mourners in meditation on Christ's suffering. Mazzolino's intense, crowded versions draw on German prints and Netherlandish detail as well as the Ferrarese tradition.","c":"Mazzolino's strength is intensity of color and incident: enamel-like hues, expressive faces and details that reward close looking. The crowding can seem hectic, the figure drawing is brittle and the emotion more theatrical than deep, and his scale is always small. Yet in its jewel-like finish this little panel has a vividness that larger, more orthodox Renaissance paintings sometimes lack, and it is a good example of Ferrarese eccentricity."},"met:437647":{"d":"Trees and flowers announce spring in a small oil on wood, 35.6 by 27.3 centimeters, painted in 1874. The title places the scene in Brittany, the Atlantic province of northwestern France, and the blossoming of the season is its subject. Paul Sébillot, himself a native of Brittany, painted landscapes and rural scenes in a straightforward Salon manner, and a panel of this scale suggests an intimate, observed view rather than a large exhibition picture.","i":"Sébillot is better known today as a folklorist than as a painter: he collected the tales, songs and customs of Brittany, publishing his Contes populaires de la Haute-Bretagne in the 1880s, and he founded a learned society for popular traditions in 1886. His paintings belong to the same affection for the province, which in the 1870s was a favorite subject for painters seeking unspoiled rural life. Spring blossom and fresh growth were themes of renewal, and a Breton setting gave them a regional flavor.","c":"The panel is a competent, unpretentious landscape, careful in its observation of trees and flowers and fresh in its seasonal feeling. It lacks the personal touch or strong design that would lift it above many Salon landscapes of its day, and its small size limits its ambition. Its interest is partly historical: it records the Brittany that its painter, as a collector of folklore, was trying to preserve in words."},"met:10178":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, 137.2 by 71.1 centimeters, painted in 1878, shows a stream and trees in a landscape named for a boulder and a flume, the wooden channel used to carry water or logs in mining and lumbering country. The upright format is unusual for a landscape, and the large size makes it a conspicuous picture. Blakelock, a self-taught painter who had traveled in the American West, later became famous for dark, glowing, moonlit landscapes.","i":"Blakelock traveled in the American West in the late 1860s and early 1870s, and western scenery and the machinery of mining or logging, such as a flume, appear in his early landscapes. The title joins nature, a boulder, with a human construction, the flume, as in the work of a painter interested in how people and the land meet. In the 1870s American landscape painters still drew on the exact observation of the Hudson River School, and this picture belongs to that world, before his later, dreamlike manner.","c":"The scale and the unusual upright format make this an ambitious early landscape, and the rocks and moving water are described with solid, careful observation. It is more conventional than the glowing nocturnes that made Blakelock's reputation, and the handling still follows the Hudson River manner rather than the personal vision of his later work. As a picture it is dignified and well made; as a stage in his development it is interesting."},"met:10183":{"d":"A waterfall under moonlight, with trees and a night sky, fills a tall canvas of 142.9 by 90.8 centimeters, painted by 1886. The title and the motifs set the scene at night, where forms are simplified into masses of dark foliage and pale falling water, lit by a glow from the sky. Blakelock built such pictures slowly, from thin, translucent layers of dark, rich color, so that the surface seems to hold light within it.","i":"Moonlit waterfalls belong to a Romantic tradition that runs from Salvator Rosa to Caspar David Friedrich and, in America, to the painters of the Hudson River School. Blakelock's night scenes of the 1880s, however, are more concerned with mood than description, and later critics read their remote, dreamlike quality as an expression of the isolation of a painter who sold little and struggled to live. Such readings should be made with care, but they explain the picture's hold on later viewers.","c":"Blakelock's nocturnes are among the most original American landscapes of the century: luminous, simplified shapes, a glowing sky against dark trees, and a surface that seems to glow from within. This waterfall picture is large and ambitious and has a hushed intensity. The formula can become repetitive and the forms murky, but at its best the mood is genuinely haunting and personal."},"nga:42928":{"d":"Neat garden rows stretch away toward a band of trees in this horizontal canvas, about 41 by 61 centimetres. Low plants grow across the middle, higher ones are staked on poles at the right, and a fence runs along the left. A few structures, perhaps a shed, gate and house, are tucked among the bushy tree canopies, with two white chimneys outlined against a watery blue sky. The plants are painted in visible dabs of copper brown and ivory white, and the whole scene is backlit and shadowy.","i":"Ralph Albert Blakelock is best known for moonlit landscapes with dark silhouetted trees and glowing skies, and is grouped with the American Tonalists, who valued mood and atmosphere above exact description. This garden, a humble, cultivated subject, is quieter than his moonlights but shares their obscurity and glow. The title says it is the artist's own garden, and it is a small domestic counterpart to his more visionary pictures.","c":"An atmospheric, slightly murky study. The dabs of paint make the garden shimmer, and the backlighting turns it into a pattern of dark and light. The details of the buildings can hardly be made out, and the tangle of paint can seem muddy, but the obscurity is part of Blakelock's poetry. It is a modest note among his moonlights, more intimate than they are but less haunting."},"met:10181":{"d":"A landscape of trees, 68.6 by 94.9 centimeters, was painted between about 1885 and 1895, in the years of Blakelock's mature style. The title offers no place, and that is characteristic: his later landscapes are generalized, dreamlike visions of woods at dusk or of moonlit clearings rather than records of a particular view. The horizontal format suits an extended screen of trees, and the handling, built up in dark, glowing layers, is typical of the artist's work of these years.","i":"Blakelock's unnamed landscapes of the late 1880s and 1890s are typical of the American Tonalist current, which valued mood, suggestion and a limited, harmonious palette over the topographical detail prized earlier in the century. The artist worked largely from memory and imagination, painting trees and sky as shapes in a rich, dim light. Collectors who admired these pictures found in them a refuge from the busy, industrial present.","c":"Without an incident or a named place the picture rests wholly on atmosphere, and Blakelock supplies it with a deep, warm glow and a rhythmic silhouette of foliage. The very consistency of his formula is a weakness: the pictures can look alike and the drawing is vague. Yet the best of them have a poetic force unlike anything else in American painting of the period, and this is a characteristic example."},"nga:177627":{"d":"A slightly raised viewpoint looks out across a green landscape toward a reflective body of water. In a green sky a pale yellow moon glows brightly, with greenish-yellow clouds edged in thin brown floating above. The moonlight makes a bright path on the water at the bottom of the picture, the dark silhouettes of trees stand at the left, and in the distance the flat green land fades into glowing atmosphere. The palette is muted greens, browns and golds, and the canvas is about 69 by 94 centimetres.","i":"Blakelock painted many moonlit scenes in the 1880s and 1890s, using thick, glazed and scraped paint to produce glowing, dreamlike effects. They belong to American Tonalism, which valued mood and emotion over topographical accuracy, and to a broader late nineteenth-century taste for nocturnal, contemplative scenes. Blakelock struggled financially and spent many years in hospital from 1899, and his reputation, and the prices of his paintings, rose sharply only after that.","c":"The glow is the point: a green sky, a pale moon and a path of light on water produce a hushed, dreamlike mood, and the glazed surface gives it a rich texture. The formula, dark trees against a luminous sky, is repeated through his work, and the picture can seem to lack incident. But few American painters of his day evoked such mystery, and it stands beside the nocturnes of Whistler and the visions of Ryder."},"met:10180":{"d":"A Native American camp, called an Indian encampment in the period's language of the title, stands among trees with horses on a nearly square canvas of 95.6 by 103.2 centimeters, painted between about 1880 and 1900. Blakelock painted many such camps, often small groups of tepees or tents under tall trees at dusk or by moonlight, and the people in them are elements in a mood more than the subjects of a portrait of a way of life.","i":"Blakelock had traveled in the American West in the early 1870s and had seen Native American life at first hand; his later camp scenes, painted in New York from memory and imagination, belong to a wider nostalgic fashion for the vanishing West, at a time when the Plains peoples were being confined to reservations. His encampments are quiet, romantic and unnarrated, closer to elegy than to ethnography.","c":"The motif lets Blakelock unite figures, horses, fire and trees in a single glowing mass, and the mood of dusk and woodland is evocative. The picture is more poetic than informative, its romanticized view of Native life reflects its time, and the handling can be murky. As an example of the work that made him famous, it is typical and effective."},"met:10182":{"d":"The Pipe Dance is one of Blakelock's largest canvases, 123.2 by 182.9 centimeters, a full six feet wide, painted between about 1880 and 1900. Trees and a dance are the recorded subjects, and the title probably refers to a ceremonial dance of Plains peoples associated with the sacred pipe. In such scenes Blakelock typically sets the gathering in a dim, glowing woodland, with the figures merging into the landscape rather than standing out from it.","i":"Native American ceremonial dances fascinated nineteenth-century artists, who saw them as exotic survivals of a way of life being displaced by settlement. Blakelock saw Plains and Southwestern peoples in the early 1870s, but his dance scenes are imagined and idealized, glowing woodland visions with small figures. Such pictures speak more of the painter's romantic temperament and of white American nostalgia than of the ceremonies themselves.","c":"At this scale Blakelock's twilight romance becomes grand and ambitious, and the glow of the dance against dark trees has real power as pure atmosphere. But the size also exposes the formula: the drawing of figures is vague, the narrative thin and the dark, heavily worked surface can feel muddy. It is a striking, unusual example of the artist's late style rather than his most refined achievement."},"nga:130889":{"d":"A rocky stream winds through a green wooded place, with trees and brown-and-gray rocky ground filling the top of the composition and the water curving below around small gray rocks and boulders. At the left a small white waterfall drops into a flat pool of brown and gray water. The palette is rich and earthy, with deep greens, golden yellows and reddish browns throughout and lighter patches of white and beige in the water. It is a small canvas, 24.8 by 33.7 centimeters, painted about 1840.","i":"Paul Huet was a French Romantic landscape painter who admired Constable and Bonington and was among the first of his generation to look at French forests, streams and coasts with fresh eyes, before the painters of Barbizon made them famous. Small oil studies like this record a motif in the open air or from sketches and show the Romantic taste for wild, rocky nature and for the feel of a place rather than its map.","c":"The earthy palette and loose, direct handling give the little canvas an unforced naturalism, and the pool and falling water are a pleasing focus. Huet is less well known than Corot or Rousseau, whom he anticipated, and his work can be uneven and conventional in composition. Studies like this show him at his most spontaneous, a quick, attentive look at forest water that is more original than his formal Salon landscapes."},"met:11876":{"d":"A woman is shown in a miniature painted in watercolor on ivory in 1899, with ribbon, a rosette and a striped fabric among the details of her costume. At 11.7 by 8.3 centimeters it is large for a miniature, the size of a small framed picture rather than a locket. The title names the sitter, Bessie Moore, and the delicate, translucent watercolor on thin ivory lets light pass through the pigment and glow in the flesh.","i":"The portrait miniature, a staple of the eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries, was revived in the United States in the 1890s by a new generation of painters, many of them women, who exhibited together and treated the form as a refined art of delicate likeness. Virginia Reynolds, who lived from 1866 to 1903, worked in this revival. Sitters wore their best, and the ribbons and rosettes of fashionable dress were rendered with the same care as the face.","c":"Reynolds's miniature is delicate and modern in feeling: fine, fluid strokes build the face, and the dress is treated with the lightness of a fashion plate. The format restricts invention, and the sitter's inner life is only hinted at, but the clean handling and elegant finish make it a good example of the late-nineteenth-century revival. It is a small, polished object with a clear sense of period style."},"met:437897":{"d":"The patriarch Abraham takes leave of his nephew Lot and Lot's family, among them his wife and daughters, on a large canvas, 147.3 by 165.4 centimeters, painted between about 1655 and 1665. Food and eating are among the recorded motifs, which suggests a farewell meal, and a dog completes the household. Jan Victors, a pupil of Rembrandt, tells such Old Testament stories in a warm Rembrandtesque manner, with sturdy, expressive figures in a rich, earthy light.","i":"In Genesis 13 Abraham and Lot, whose herds had grown too great to share the land, agree to part: Lot chooses the fertile plain of the Jordan and settles near Sodom, a choice that leads to his family's ordeal when the city is destroyed. Dutch painters liked the episode for its human drama, and Victors, who specialized in Old Testament narrative, treats the parting as a family leave-taking in which generosity and foresight, and later ruin, are quietly set against each other.","c":"Victors tells the story clearly and with sympathy, and the Rembrandtesque warmth, the sturdy figures and the domestic details of food and animals make a distant story feel close. The composition is somewhat crowded, the characterization conventional and the light less subtle than his master's; Victors lacks Rembrandt's depth of feeling. Still, it is a solid, humane example of Amsterdam biblical painting of the 1650s and 1660s."},"met:437768":{"d":"A man is the sole subject of this portrait, oil on canvas, 120.7 by 92.7 centimeters, from about 1568, large enough for the sitter's hands and costume to be shown. The title gives no name. Lambert Sustris, a Netherlandish painter who trained in Venice in Titian's circle, was better known for landscapes and religious pictures than for portraits, so a portrait by him is a less common sight, and it shows the Venetian formula for male portraiture adopted by a northern hand.","i":"Without a name, the portrait speaks of a type: a man of means, shown in the sober dress and composed pose that Venetian portraiture had established under Titian. Sustris's career linked the Netherlands and Italy, and a north-Italian portrait of the 1560s by a Netherlandish-born painter shows how Italian and northern manners mixed. The sitter would have wanted to be seen as sober, dependable and prosperous.","c":"The portrait is a competent exercise in the Venetian manner: broad, well-lit planes and a solid figure, with none of Titian's psychological probing. As a work by a landscape specialist it seems careful rather than inspired, and the sitter remains a type rather than a character. It has value as a record of mid-sixteenth-century fashion and of how widely the Venetian formula for male portraiture spread."},"met:748947":{"d":"A young woman, identified by the title as Ellen Maurice, aged about nineteen when this portrait was painted in 1597, is the sole subject of an oil painting on oak panel, 90.6 by 74.2 centimeters. In the English portraits of Marcus Gheeraerts the Younger, sitters are typically shown in elaborate costume, with precisely drawn lace, jewels and embroidery that proclaim wealth and status, in a design that is flat and decorative rather than deeply modeled.","i":"Elizabethan portraits were not intimate likenesses but public statements of rank, wealth and lineage; a young woman's portrait of 1597, near the age of marriage, might record her family's standing and prospects. Gheeraerts, born in Bruges and brought to England as a child, was the leading fashionable portrait painter of the 1590s, famous for the Ditchley portrait of Queen Elizabeth. Such pictures were often inscribed with the sitter's age and the date, which gives precise evidence.","c":"Gheeraerts's strength is the cool precision with which he records texture and ornament; the sitter seems almost part of her costume, which was the point. The flatness of the design holds back any sense of personality, so the portrait is more an emblem of status than a likeness of a mind. It is handsome and informative about Elizabethan dress, a good example of the courtly style of the 1590s."},"cma:143769":{"d":"After conquering New Carthage in Spain in 209 BC, the Roman commander Scipio Africanus the Elder, standing above the crowd, returns his victor's reward, a beautiful young woman, to her husband. The canvas, 127 by 105 centimeters, painted about 1710, shows the moment of restitution as a theatrical group around the raised figure of Scipio. Pellegrini, a Venetian painter who worked widely in northern Europe, handles such scenes with bright, silvery color and quick, airy brushwork.","i":"The continence of Scipio was one of the favorite classical examples of virtue in victory: Roman historians such as Livy and Valerius Maximus praised the young general because, though entitled to keep the captive as a prize, he restored her untouched to her family and even added the ransom brought by her parents as a wedding gift. Painters from the Renaissance on used it in pictures for rulers and patrons who wished to be seen as just and self-controlled.","c":"Pellegrini turns a moral anecdote into a bright, rapidly brushed spectacle: the light, silvery palette and graceful figures are characteristic of Venetian Rococo and keep the pedantic subject buoyant. The sentiment is more decorative than heroic, and the characterization is thin; the picture works as an elegant demonstration of Pellegrini's fluency and charm rather than as a deep reading of the story."},"met:437268":{"d":"The wine god Bacchus and Ariadne, the princess he rescued after she was abandoned on the island of Naxos, are the two figures recorded in this mythological painting, 116.8 by 128.3 centimeters, from the 1720s. Pellegrini painted such subjects in the light, silvery colors and rapid, feathery brushwork of the Venetian Rococo, for patrons who wanted classical love stories as elegant decoration for a palace room.","i":"The story, told by Ovid and others, is of consolation: Theseus, whom Ariadne had helped to escape the Minotaur's labyrinth, left her asleep on Naxos, and Bacchus found her, married her and set her bridal crown among the stars as a constellation. Painters made the meeting a scene of rescue and love, sometimes with a revelling procession. For eighteenth-century patrons the myth offered an erotic subject with a happy ending.","c":"Pellegrini's mythologies delight through speed and lightness: the loose brushwork and pale, glittering color make the meeting feel like a happy dream. The drawing is soft and the invention conventional, and he rarely rises above decorative charm; yet within the Venetian tradition that led from Veronese to Tiepolo, he is a graceful and intelligent link. This picture is typical of him: graceful, warm and a little superficial."},"met:436349":{"d":"Two big cats, the panthers of Bacchus, feed on grapes among vines in this narrow oil sketch on paper laid down on card, only 35 by 17 centimeters, dated about 1719 to 1720. A bird appears among the vines. The tall strip and the sketch medium suggest a design for a decorative panel, to be developed on a larger scale in another material such as tapestry or paneling.","i":"Panthers belong to the myth of Bacchus, the god of wine, whose chariot is drawn by panthers or leopards and whose procession includes satyrs and maenads; grapes and vines are his plant. The taste of the 1710s and 1720s for light, decorative mythology favored such motifs as ornament rather than as grave story. Desportes, royal painter of animals and the hunt, here turns his skill to the cats' fur and the vine's pattern.","c":"Desportes was the greatest French animal painter of his time, and an oil sketch like this shows his freedom: he paints animals with quick, direct strokes, free of the finish demanded for royal commissions. The scale is tiny, and the exotic cats are less convincing as living beasts than as decorative inventions, but the handling is lively. Such sketches are now more admired than some of his formal pictures."},"met:436186":{"d":"A monumental still life, nearly 2.6 meters tall and 1.9 wide, gathers silver plate with garlands of flowers and fruit. Painted in the 1720s in oil on canvas, it is on the scale of a decoration for a grand room rather than a picture to be examined at arm's length. The title makes the silver the centerpiece of a display of wealth and abundance.","i":"Large still lifes of silver, fruit and flowers were decorative pictures for the dining rooms and salons of the French aristocracy and royal houses in the early eighteenth century, displaying wealth and abundance. Desportes, painter to Louis XIV and Louis XV, supplied them along with hunts and animals. Silver plate was a form of stored wealth, so a painted display of it recorded the owner's standing. The garland of flowers and fruit adds a note of festivity and plenty.","c":"At 2.6 meters this is more decoration than still life in the intimate Dutch sense, and its virtues are those of a decorative ensemble: rich objects, abundant fruit and flowers, and a sweeping design suited to a wall. Desportes is a master of such display, though the scale invites a certain emptiness, gleam without feeling. Beside Chardin's silent still lifes of the following years, it is the voice of the court against that of the kitchen."},"nga:157526":{"d":"A larder of dead game, meat and fruit crowds a tabletop in this tall canvas. At the left more than a dozen pheasants lie belly up on a gold platter, their plucked bodies coated in white lard, the feathers of heads and necks left intact. At the right three plucked birds are stacked on a rumpled white cloth, two wrapped in thin slices of meat and tied with twine. Behind them a basket of oranges rests on a tall copper pot, and a rack of ribs and a slab of meat hang from hooks at the top. Five golden pears sit on a stool at the lower left.","i":"Desportes served Louis XIV and Louis XV as a painter of hunts, animals and still lifes, and this belongs to the tradition of the game piece and larder scene, which showed the abundance of a wealthy household and the painter's command of texture. The subject here is less the hunt than the kitchen: birds covered in fat as if prepared for cooking, meat on hooks, oranges and pears for dessert. The viewer is invited to enjoy surfaces more than any moral.","c":"Desportes's skill with texture shows throughout: the pale fat, the dull sheen of copper, the orange skins and the rough cloth are distinguished by touch as much as by colour. The arrangement is crowded, though, and the hooks and meat at the top cut the canvas into a dense band, so the effect is closer to inventory than composition. Beside Chardin's still lifes of the same decade, it is richer and less intimate."},"met:436498":{"d":"Christ as the Man of Sorrows, the suffering Savior shown with the marks of his Passion, appears with Saint Francis of Assisi on a small gold-ground panel, 47 by 31.1 centimeters, painted about 1430. The gold leaf and tempera give the picture the glow of an icon, and its size marks it as a work for private devotion or a small chapel. Michele Giambono painted in Venice in the early fifteenth century, in a delicate, elegant late Gothic style.","i":"The Man of Sorrows, an image of Christ's suffering body without narrative setting, was among the most popular devotional images of the later Middle Ages, designed to move viewers to compassion. Francis, who received the wounds of Christ on his own body, was the saint most identified with this sharing in Christ's pain, and his presence invites the viewer to imitate that love. The pairing probably served a Franciscan patron or a viewer devoted to Francis.","c":"Giambono's gift is a refined, lyrical late Gothic line and the pleasing flow of draperies against gold. His anatomy is more decorative than convincing, and the sorrow of the subject is tempered by courtly elegance; the emotion is not as raw as in German or Flemish treatments. As a small devotional panel of Venice around 1430 it is graceful and moving in a restrained, formal way."},"nga:221":{"d":"A man stands slightly turned to the left, with curly grayish-white hair and a short gray beard, a golden halo around his head. He wears a golden robe with intricate hems over a purple garment striped with gold at neck and shoulders, and holds a gold key in his right hand and a red book in his left. Barefoot, he stands on a small grassy platform, against a blue background within a reddish-brown border. The tall, narrow poplar panel is 87.7 by 35.9 centimeters.","i":"Peter, the fisherman called by Christ as the first of the apostles, is recognized by his key or keys, the keys of heaven that Christ entrusted to him in the Gospel of Matthew, and by his short, curly gray hair and beard. A full-length saint on a tall narrow panel like this was probably one of several in a polyptych, a multi-panel altarpiece in which a central image of the Virgin or another saint was flanked by apostles and saints, each in its own compartment.","c":"Giambono's Peter is dignified and decorative: the gold robe and glowing halo are the picture's chief pleasures, and the face has the mild gravity expected of the apostle. The figure is flat and the pose stiff, with little sense of body beneath the clothes, and the panel depends on its context in a larger altarpiece for effect. It is a fine example of Venetian late Gothic elegance but not a work of strong invention."},"met:437248":{"d":"The Virgin nurses the Christ Child in this gold-ground panel, 68.6 by 42.9 centimeters, painted in the 1370s. The details include a richly embroidered cloth, a barefoot figure and the intimate gesture of nursing, all treated with the soft, flowing line of Sienese painting. The gold background and gentle motherly pose give the panel the aura of an icon, meant for private prayer, and the panel's modest size suits a household chapel or a bedchamber.","i":"The nursing Madonna, called the Virgin of Milk, presented Mary as a human mother whose milk fed the Savior and, by extension, the faithful. The image was widely loved in Italy in the fourteenth century, especially in Siena, as a symbol of Mary's tenderness and of the Incarnation, the belief that God became truly human. Panels of this size were made for private devotion, where women in particular prayed to the Virgin for help in childbirth and nursing.","c":"Fei draws on the lyrical manner of the Sienese painters before him: the graceful line of the Virgin's head, the tender gesture and the sparkle of gold and embroidery are lovely. His forms are flat and his anatomy summary, and the picture adds little invention to a well-worn type; yet its devotion and decorative beauty are real. It is a good example of Sienese devotional painting after the Black Death, with a sweetness that appealed to patrons."},"nga:46103":{"d":"A temple spans three arched compartments in this nearly square panel of about 146 by 140 centimeters, its figures dressed in gold-edged robes. In the wider central bay the young Mary stands with folded hands before two bearded priests, one haloed and bowing toward her, on a platform reached by seven steps; coral-red columns and a rose window rise behind. Two small boys at the foot of the steps turn their backs to the viewer, and one points at her. At the left stand a haloed woman and man, presumably her parents, and at the right two men face a balcony of eight women.","i":"The scene comes from the apocryphal Gospel of James and the Golden Legend, not the Bible: Joachim and Anne bring their three-year-old daughter to the Temple in fulfillment of a vow, and she climbs the steps alone, a sign of her destined holiness. The priest receives her to be raised there. Such episodes from the Life of the Virgin were especially dear to Siena, a city that regarded Mary as its protector.","c":"Fei, a Sienese painter of the late Gothic, delights in surface: gold-edged robes, a patterned floor, a rose window and arches create a jeweled effect. The three-part structure organizes many figures clearly, but the space remains shallow and the scale varies, in contrast to the new perspective of Florence at the same time. It is courtly, decorative narrative painting, more charming than dramatic."},"nga:46170":{"d":"Mary sits on a throne of clouds against a gold sky, praying and looking up, held aloft by two of the ten angels around her, six of whom play instruments. Below, twelve figures in gold-trimmed robes gather around a long stone box filled with white and red flowers and green leaves, one pointing into it. Beyond the box a figure kneels on a rocky outcrop with raised hands. In the top corners, busts of the angel Gabriel and the Virgin of the Annunciation sit in roundels, and a carved gold frame surrounds the tall panel.","i":"The legend of Mary's Assumption says that the apostles, gathered at her tomb, found it empty and full of flowers, because she had been taken bodily to heaven. The kneeling figure beyond is probably Saint Thomas, who in the story arrived late and received her girdle as proof. The busts of Gabriel and Mary in the corners add the Annunciation, so the panel links the beginning of her earthly story with its end.","c":"Fei's Sienese lyricism is at its best here: the composition stacks earth, tomb and heaven in a clear ascent, the angels with their varied instruments make a lively chorus, and the colors against gold are delicate. The apostles around the tomb are somewhat uniform in feeling, and the space is the flat, symbolic space of the late Gothic. It is a devotional jewel rather than a dramatic narrative."},"nga:248":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a young woman with smooth pale skin sleeps with her head tipped away, her face in profile, pink lips parted and cheeks lightly flushed. Brown curls are topped by a black fur cap draped with a muted red cloth ending in a gold tassel by her neck. A flaring pleated white collar circles her neck, and her topaz-blue bodice, lined in shimmering green, opens to show a butter-yellow corset. Her head rests against an arched wooden chair back upholstered in caramel brown, against olive and dark green. Blended strokes give a soft, almost blurry look.","i":"Pietro Rotari, born in Verona, spent his last years at the Russian court in St. Petersburg, where he painted a great many half-length pictures of young women in costume, studies of type and mood rather than portraits of identifiable sitters. The sleeping girl belongs to this fashion for the fancy head, an invitation to enjoy beauty, charm and the pleasure of looking at a sleeper unobserved. The picture borrows costume of the past and of far places, which gave eighteenth-century viewers a pleasant illusion of distance.","c":"Rotari's smooth, blended handling and the delicate play of cool blues and greens against warm flesh make the sleeping girl an appealing, rather sensuous picture. The idea is sweet and a little facile: the sleeping beauty is a stock motif, and the face has a doll-like regularity. But it shows the courtly Rococo at its most agreeable, and the economy of the means, a face, a cap and a chair, is genuinely skillful."},"nga:249":{"d":"Shown from the shoulders up against an olive-green wall, a young woman with pale skin and chestnut hair turns her head over her shoulder and looks out from the corners of heavy-lidded, dark green eyes, her chin jutting slightly forward. Her cheeks are flushed, and the corners of her closed peach lips are barely drawn back in a faint smile. A white ruffled flower with a bud and deep green leaves sits in her hair above the temple, and a dark twisted cord circles her neck, its ends gathered in one hand at her chest. She wears a muted blue bodice over a pleated white shirt, lit from the left.","i":"Like A Sleeping Girl, also in the National Gallery of Art, this belongs to Rotari's late series of half-length girls, painted in St. Petersburg around 1760 to 1762. Such pictures are fancy heads: neither portraits nor stories, but charming types who flirt with the viewer through a glance or a smile. The flower in the hair, the twisted cord and the sidelong look were all part of the vocabulary of eighteenth-century coquetry, and a purchaser could enjoy the suggestion of a personality without knowing a name.","c":"The sidelong, heavy-lidded look is the picture's hook, and Rotari plays it well, with soft blended modeling, a cool turquoise and olive harmony and a warm glow in the cheeks. The formula was repeated by the dozen, and a certain sameness of type and expression is the price; none of these girls has the character of a Chardin or Liotard sitter. As a refined, charming specimen of Rococo gallantry it succeeds."},"met:18811":{"d":"A man is shown in a portrait miniature painted in watercolor on ivory in 1831, with no dimensions recorded; the title gives no name. The format is intimate, a likeness to be held in the hand, worn or kept in a case, and the usual miniature composition shows only the head and shoulders. James Reid Lambdin, a Pittsburgh-born painter who later turned from miniatures to oils, worked in this fine, translucent technique, in which thin washes of color glow over the white of the ivory.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the photographs of their day: inexpensive, portable and personal, made to be given to a spouse or kept as a memorial, and in the 1830s they were still the common way for American families to record a face before the daguerreotype arrived in 1839. An unnamed gentleman, as here, has lost his name but keeps his appearance, and the miniature remains a record of how a man of the era wished to look: sober, neat and respectable.","c":"The miniature is a competent example of its type: smooth, clean, precise and flattering within the limits of a small face on a slip of ivory. The format restricts invention, and without a name, a setting or an attribute the likeness is hard to read; but the technical delicacy shows Lambdin's training. It is a modest but accomplished piece of early American portraiture before photography displaced the miniature."},"met:11394":{"d":"A man is shown in a small portrait miniature, watercolor on ivory, 10.2 by 6.2 centimeters, painted between about 1840 and 1850. The title gives no name. By these years James Reid Lambdin was moving from miniatures toward oil portraiture, and the small ivory remained a convenient format for a likeness that could be mounted in a case or frame and kept in the family. Thin, transparent washes let the white of the ivory show through the flesh tones.","i":"In the 1840s the miniature had a new rival in the daguerreotype, which could capture a face in minutes at a fraction of the price, and many miniature painters turned to oils or worked from photographs. Miniatures nonetheless kept a market among those who wanted color and the touch of the painter's hand. The unnamed gentleman in this example is a reminder of the many Americans whose likenesses survive though their names do not.","c":"A neat and accomplished likeness of a kind that Lambdin produced in quantity: the modeling of the face is soft, the finish careful, and the whole tidy rather than searching. It does not attempt to rival the penetration of a great portraitist, and the sitter's character stays veiled; but the miniature carries the dignity of the form and the period, and its small scale gives it an intimacy that larger portraits lack."},"nga:160960":{"d":"Warm, diffuse light fills a long room where a girl sits at the far end, chin in hand, on a ladderback chair, in profile and facing left. She wears a slate-gray skirt, a cinnamon-brown jacket, a white kerchief and a pleated white cap. At the narrow end is a cabinet-like box bed with burgundy curtains, and a woman with closed eyes lies in its shadow. A Dutch door and a deep-set window let in light at left, and at right a tall mantel with pewter bowls, silver candlesticks and a small fire fills the wall.","i":"Almost nothing is known of Jacobus Vrel beyond his paintings, quiet interiors and street scenes in which a single woman sits, sews or looks from a window in a plain room; his pictures were once attributed to better-known painters such as Vermeer and de Hooch. Here the sitting girl and the woman in the box bed may suggest watching over the sick or the tired, but the picture offers no story; its subject is silence, domestic order and light.","c":"Vrel is a painter of stillness, and this panel shows it: the long room, the quiet light and the carefully placed objects make a mood of waiting that is moving precisely because it is unexplained. His perspective is a little naive and his figures less secure than Vermeer's, but the plain truthfulness and the emptiness he leaves have an appeal that has grown with modern taste. It is one of the better pictures of this elusive painter."},"nga:414":{"d":"An elderly man with gray curly hair and a gray beard looks off to the right against a textured gold ground. He wears a blue garment with a gold border at the neckline, a yellow cloth draped over it, and holds two large gray keys in his right hand and a book with a reddish cover in the crook of his left arm. The panel is tempera on poplar, 50.6 by 31.4 centimeters, painted about 1468.","i":"Two keys are the attribute of Saint Peter, the keys of heaven that Christ gave him in the Gospel of Matthew, and the book marks him as an apostle and teacher. Peter is usually shown as an old man with short curly gray hair and beard, as here. A half-length saint on a small panel like this was probably made for private devotion, or came from a larger ensemble of saints, a common arrangement in fifteenth-century Italian altarpieces.","c":"Zoppo trained in Padua in the shop of Francesco Squarcione, and the hard, crisp modeling and strong, sculptural head owe something to the Paduan taste for Mantegna's stony manner. The result is an energetic, characterful face, if a little severe, and the gold ground and strong draperies give it presence. It is a fine small example of the harsher side of North Italian painting around 1470."},"nga:46150":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child on a small tempera panel, 41.2 by 29.8 centimeters, painted about 1467 to 1468. The size marks it as a picture for private devotion, to be set on a table or hung in a bedchamber. Marco Zoppo, a pupil of Squarcione in Padua, gave his Madonnas a hard, sculptural definition, with crisp outlines and strong modeling, in the manner that Andrea Mantegna made famous in that city.","i":"The Virgin and Child was the most common subject in fifteenth-century Italian devotional painting: Mary, the Mother of God, offers the Child to the viewer's prayers, and her tenderness invites intercession. Small panels like this were owned by families and often kept in private rooms. Zoppo's version, made at the end of the 1460s, gives the old theme a Paduan firmness and a touch of the antique sculpture that his teachers admired.","c":"Zoppo's Madonna is strong rather than sweet: the firm contours and the sculptural weight of the figures give the picture a stern dignity. The tenderness expected of the subject is held in check by the severe style, and his faces are not lovely; but the intensity of drawing and the resonance with Mantegna make it a distinctive devotional picture. It rewards viewers who expect more than sweetness in a Madonna."},"cma:148215":{"d":"A hay wagon carrying a bountiful harvest moves through a farm landscape near Dobbs Ferry, New York, in long afternoon shadows. An American flag hangs beside the homestead, a quiet allusion to the war that had begun that year. The canvas is long and low, 40.2 by 78 centimeters, and the idyllic view, with its orderly fields and afternoon light, shows the countryside as a place of harmony between people and the land.","i":"Painted in 1861, the first year of the Civil War, the picture presented to Northern viewers an agricultural system in which farmers worked their own land, and the flag by the farmhouse subtly links that free-labor ideal to the Union cause. Seen this way the peaceful scene is also a critique of Southern slavery. Yet its beauty, the full wagon and the long afternoon shadows also offer an optimistic image of harmony between man and nature, which Whittredge's audience wanted in a time of crisis.","c":"Whittredge's calm, luminous handling and the lazily curving composition make the farm view genuinely pleasing, and the political reading adds depth without turning the picture into propaganda. His training in Düsseldorf shows in the clear drawing and the careful, even finish; the effect can be a little tidy, and the figures are small. As a quiet statement of Northern confidence in the first months of the war, it is both beautiful and telling."},"cma:144462":{"d":"A long, narrow oil sketch, 18.8 by 56.5 centimeters, on paper mounted on canvas, records the graves of travelers at Fort Kearny, Nebraska, in 1866. The wide, low format suits the open prairie, and the sketch was probably made on the spot or soon after, on a journey west that took Whittredge across the Plains. Such a study records an impression directly, with broad touches rather than finish.","i":"Fort Kearny stood on the Platte River road that carried emigrants west to Oregon, California and Utah, and the graves beside the trail marked the cost of the journey in disease and accident. In 1866 Whittredge traveled west with General John Pope, and the empty plains made a deep impression on a painter trained in the wooded landscapes of Düsseldorf and the Hudson River. The sketch combines documentation and elegy: a quiet record of the people who died on the way.","c":"As a sketch it is direct and affecting: a few touches of earth, sky and grave markers convey the loneliness of the plain better than an elaborate finished picture could. It is not a composed landscape and was never meant to be, and its surface is slight; but its unforced vision of the open West and its quiet note of mortality make it among the most memorable of Whittredge's studies."},"met:13315":{"d":"A forest interior and a pool, as the title says, are the subjects of an upright canvas of 91.4 by 68.9 centimeters, painted in 1870. The vertical format draws the eye down through the trees to the water. Whittredge, trained in Düsseldorf and Rome, specialized in these quiet, enclosed woodland views, in which light filters through foliage to the ground and the water, and the recorded motif here is the forest itself.","i":"Trout pools, the cool, deep places in a stream where fish rest, were favorites of anglers in the Catskills and Adirondacks, regions that city dwellers in the 1870s visited for fishing, hunting and the sight of unspoiled woodland. Whittredge's forest interiors answered a taste for refuge from urban life; the title speaks to the sportsman and the painting to the contemplative eye. He returned from Europe in 1859 and became a leading figure among the Hudson River painters.","c":"The woodland interior is Whittredge's special subject, and his control of soft, filtered light and the cool depth of the pool are the picture's strengths. The format is constricted and the composition formulaic, with few surprises; but the honest, unhurried observation and the quiet mood are persuasive. It stands as a typical, accomplished example of the Hudson River School in its late, calmer phase."},"met:13312":{"d":"A camp meeting, one of the open-air religious revival gatherings held in wooded groves, is the subject of this very wide, low canvas, 40.6 by 103.3 centimeters, painted in 1874. Trees and landscape are the recorded motifs, and the long horizontal format spreads the woodland setting like a panorama. Whittredge, who spent his career painting forest interiors and plains, rarely turned to a popular, communal subject of this kind.","i":"Camp meetings, multi-day revival gatherings held outdoors by Methodist and other Protestant congregations, were a feature of American religious life from the early nineteenth century, drawing families who lived in tents and cottages in a grove while preachers addressed them from a stand. Painters recorded them as a distinctively American spectacle, part piety, part social occasion. The format, a wide band, suggests a panorama of the gathering among the trees.","c":"The subject is unusual and potentially rich, and Whittredge's feeling for filtered light and the cathedral-like space of a grove suits a religious gathering in the open air. The extreme width and low height are awkward, the crowd tends to be treated in small touches, and the picture is closer to landscape than to social observation. Even so it is an interesting instance of the painter turning to a communal, popular subject."},"nga:166502":{"d":"A wide, shallow stream flows gently toward the viewer through a serene forest, the scene enclosed by tall trees on either side. Their thick brown and green leaves form a canopy that filters the light from above into a warm golden glow. The palette is deep greens, browns and golden yellows, with small patches of light green where sunlight touches the grass, and in the distance the forest recedes into a soft brown haze. The canvas, 89.7 by 122.1 centimeters, is dated 1875.","i":"The Catskills, near New York City, were the cradle of the Hudson River School, and by the 1870s trout streams there were a favorite resort of fishermen and artists. Whittredge, who knew the woods well and often painted such interiors, offers a vision of untouched, benign nature that city viewers prized: cool shade, clear water and the sense of an unspoiled refuge. The tall trees and the receding stream also have a quasi-religious feeling, like a natural cathedral.","c":"This is one of Whittredge's best forest interiors: the stream leads the eye smoothly into the depth, the filtered light is described with great subtlety, and the golden brown palette has a warmth that is both realistic and poetic. The composition is somewhat formulaic and the foliage lacks sharp detail, but the large scale and the calm mood give the picture presence. It deserves its place among the finest American woodland pictures of the 1870s."},"met:13313":{"d":"Trees fill an upright canvas, 107.5 by 91.7 centimeters, painted in 1876, and the title sets the hour: evening, when light usually lies low and warm across the trunks. The recorded subject is only trees and the landscape, so the picture is best read as a woodland interior, the kind of enclosed, light-filled forest view that was Whittredge's specialty after his return from Europe.","i":"The forest interior at evening, quiet, enclosed and bathed in last light, answered a Gilded Age wish for a refuge from the noise and speed of cities. Whittredge, who had studied in Düsseldorf and Rome, developed in the 1870s a distinctive American version of the woodland interior, quieter and more intimate than the grand panoramas of Church or Bierstadt. Hudson River painters regarded the forest as a spiritual place, and evening deepened that mood.","c":"The picture carries Whittredge's strengths: a sense of enclosed space, careful gradations of light and a patient, even touch. It risks monotony, since the composition and palette are close to others he painted in these years, and it lacks the immediate drama of the Rocky Mountain painters. But its quiet truthfulness and the beauty of its light make a convincing and restful picture."},"nga:130198":{"d":"Looking slightly down, the picture shows a bay that curves away into the distance, with shallow waves lapping a taupe sandy beach. Several people play in the water in hats and long sleeves; two women hold up their skirts as they wade, a small brown dog close behind, while another person in white walks on the wet sand and four more gather nearby, perhaps on a blanket. Far off, brown horses pull a carriage along the beach. Celery-green grasses, dunes and a rocky promontory with a crack like a turtle's open mouth close the curve, and white clouds drift over slate-gray hills.","i":"Newport, Rhode Island, was the leading American summer resort of the Gilded Age, and Second Beach, a long sandy strand just outside the town, was where ordinary visitors and residents bathed and strolled. The wading women in long skirts and the carriage on the beach are fashions of the late 1870s. Whittredge, usually a painter of woods and plains, turned to the shore in these years, and the sunny, open view shows that vacation leisure had become a subject for American landscape.","c":"Whittredge, known for dark forests, shows here a surprisingly light, open touch: the pale sky, the sweeping curve of the bay and the tiny bathers are handled with a freshness that shares the plein-air spirit of the French Impressionists. The figures are sketchy and the composition loosely ordered, but the openness, space and good-humored detail make it one of his most charming and unusual pictures."},"met:16875":{"d":"A brook runs through woodland in a horizontal canvas of 71.1 by 91.4 centimeters, painted about 1885 to 1886. The recorded motifs are trees and a forest, and the subject, the quiet meeting of water and woods, is one Whittredge returned to more than any other. The picture is a mature statement of his woodland manner: a calm, enclosed view with a gentle play of light on trunks, leaves and water.","i":"By the mid-1880s the Hudson River School's heroic landscapes had lost ground to smaller, more intimate views influenced by the Barbizon painters and by Impressionism, and Whittredge, one of the school's last survivors, adapted by simplifying his compositions and softening his touch. The brook in the woods, a modest, unspectacular scene, fits this change: it asks viewers to savor peace rather than awe. Whittredge continued to paint such interiors for another two decades.","c":"The brook picture shows a veteran painter moving toward a lighter, looser manner, and the intimate view is persuasive. Yet its conventions are those of thirty years earlier, and the picture lacks the stronger invention of Whittredge's best work of the 1870s. It is a pleasant, assured example of late Hudson River School landscape rather than a high point."},"met:436693":{"d":"Men and women of the Bavarian court gather for music, with musicians playing instruments and a dog among the company, on a canvas of 87.6 by 106.7 centimeters, painted in 1730. As a conversation piece, the picture combines group portraiture with a scene of court pleasure, and its figures are probably recognizable members of the Elector's circle. Peter Jacob Horemans, a Flemish painter working in Munich, specialized in such precisely drawn, brightly colored scenes.","i":"Karl Albrecht, Elector of Bavaria from 1726 and later, from 1742 to 1745, the Holy Roman Emperor Charles VII, was a lavish patron of music, theater and building, and his court at Munich and Nymphenburg was among the most brilliant in Germany. A musical gathering, a common subject of the Rococo, let the court show itself as a society of refinement and harmony. Horemans's pictures of the court preserve its dress, instruments and manners with documentary delight.","c":"Horemans paints with precision and a lively sense of costume and decoration; the figures are neatly grouped, and the instruments are drawn with a specialist's accuracy. The poses are polite and the faces somewhat uniform, so the picture is more a record of courtly manners than a study of character. As a document of eighteenth-century court music in Bavaria it is delightful, and in the conversation-piece tradition it is lively and convincing."},"nga:11":{"d":"A woman sits cradling an infant on her lap, facing slightly left and looking down at him with a slight smile. She has golden hair under a semi-translucent white veil, a red garment with gold embroidery and a dark blue cloak edged in gold, pinned with a star-shaped brooch. The curly-haired child, in a sheer garment, looks up at her. Around them are winged angels and cherubim shown as childlike heads with the red-and-gold bodies of birds, all haloed, against a decorative gold ground. The tempera panel is 69.7 by 49.5 centimeters.","i":"The red-and-gold winged forms are the highest ranks of angels, seraphim and cherubim, who in medieval thought stand nearest to God and surround the Virgin as Queen of Heaven. The star on her cloak probably refers to her title Stella Maris, star of the sea. Matteo di Giovanni worked in Siena, where devotion to the Virgin, the city's patron, was especially strong, and small panels like this were made for private chapels and bedrooms to bring a protective image into the household.","c":"Matteo's Madonnas are among the most appealing of Sienese painting of the second half of the fifteenth century: the smiling, tender mother, the glittering gold and embroidery and the whirl of seraphim create a rich decorative surface. The forms are linear and the space shallow, and he is less inventive than his Florentine contemporaries; but the delicacy and warmth of feeling are fully convincing. It exemplifies the Sienese loyalty to gold and grace."},"nga:440":{"d":"Shown from the thighs up, Mary holds the Christ Child upright near her shoulder, supporting him with one hand and bracing his chest with the other, in a ruby-red patterned dress and a lapis-blue cloak edged in gold with a starburst at the shoulder. He grips her neckline, nude but for a translucent cloth. At the left Saint Jerome, balding, long-bearded and hollow-cheeked in a red robe, writes in a dark green book with a quill; at the right Saint Catherine holds a book, a quill and a fragment of a spiked wheel. Two angels look on against punched gold.","i":"Jerome, the scholar and translator of the Bible, is shown writing, and Catherine of Alexandria, a learned princess martyred on a spiked wheel, carries her wheel and a book, so the two saints stand for learning in the service of faith. Both are common companions of the Virgin in Italian altarpieces, and their placement beside Mary and the Child in this small panel made it a picture for a patron, perhaps a scholar or a woman devoted to Catherine, who wanted these saints near in prayer.","c":"Matteo di Giovanni was a master of the Sienese manner at its most decorative; he describes the glossy gold, the rich fabrics and the characterful face of Jerome with loving care. The strongly linear modeling and the stiff arrangement keep the group more patterned than alive, and the emotion is gentle rather than deep. But as a small, polished devotional panel it is a very fine example of fifteenth-century Siena."},"met:437038":{"d":"The Virgin and Child appear with Saint Jerome and Mary Magdalen in a small tempera and gold panel, 61.5 by 45.1 centimeters, from late in Matteo di Giovanni's career. Mary Magdalen, usually shown with her ointment jar, and Jerome, the scholar-saint, are grouped closely around the Virgin against a gold ground. The format suits a private devotional picture or a small altarpiece for a chapel, and the gold and fine tempera give it the glow of a precious object.","i":"Mary Magdalen and Jerome are the two great penitent saints of the Christian West, one the repentant sinner who anointed Christ's feet and was first to see him risen, the other the scholar who withdrew into the desert to do penance for his sins. Placed beside the Virgin and Child, they hold out to the viewer the hope of forgiveness, an appeal that suited late fifteenth-century Italian piety, with its emphasis on penance and intercession.","c":"Late Matteo is a little formulaic: the gold ground, the placid faces and the carefully arranged figures repeat patterns he had perfected decades earlier. But the polish of finish, the grace of the Madonna's pose and the quiet intimacy of the grouping remain attractive. As a late work by a leading Sienese master, it shows both his consistency and the limits of his invention, though its devotional appeal is undiminished."},"nga:52179":{"d":"A large white bridge spans a gray river, with a row of cream and tan buildings along the left bank reflected in the water. On the nearer, right bank people go about their business on a sandy embankment: two sit among piles of white material beside a small dog asleep on a cushion, a man and a dog sit by the water, and three men stand in a circle near a seated figure. The people are suggested in small, deliberate strokes. Small carts cross the bridge in the distance under a light blue sky filled with soft white clouds.","i":"The Pont de la Tournelle connects the Left Bank of the Seine with the Île Saint-Louis, and the sandy embankment in front probably served the laundresses, boatmen and carters who worked the river. Lépine, who showed with the Impressionists in 1874 and 1877, loved the humble working Seine and painted it in a quiet, silvery manner close to Corot and Jongkind. Painted in 1862, as Haussmann was rebuilding Paris, the picture records a river bank that was soon to change.","c":"Lépine's early Paris views have a modest, truthful charm: the silvery tones of river, bridge and sky are subtly related, and the small figures animate the bank without anecdote. The picture is quiet and somewhat cautious, closer to Corot's tonal vision than to the Impressionists' bright color; it is a minor but sensitive example of the sober Parisian landscape that preceded and shadowed Impressionism."},"nga:89680":{"d":"Seen from a low point of view, a white horse with a long mane, a short tail and a dark saddle moves across a sandy path toward the left, painted in broad, expressive strokes near the center of the picture. The horizon is low, with a broad expanse of gray-blue sky above and only a thin strip of green fields below; a small haystack stands in the distance at the left, and swirling indistinct lines mark the right. The thick, visible brushwork on a small canvas, 18.9 by 32.9 centimeters, gives it the immediacy of a sketch.","i":"Lépine painted such small sketches in the countryside around Paris and in Normandy, where horses and fields were part of daily life, and he exhibited with the Impressionists in the 1870s. The low horizon and the dominating sky follow the example of Dutch landscape, Corot and Boudin, and the plow horse is a modest, unsentimental subject: a working animal in its element. The picture was painted between about 1870 and 1874.","c":"The directness is the attraction: the heavy, juicy strokes of the horse and the huge, plain sky make a lively sketch, a mood caught rather than composed. It is slight, and the title's promise of toil is only lightly redeemed; but within the tradition of the small plein-air study, it is fresh and convincing, and it shows Lépine's affinity with the early Impressionists."},"nga:66421":{"d":"From an elevated viewpoint, the picture overlooks a landscape of green hills and leafy trees. Closest to the viewer, cream-colored buildings with pale blue-gray and reddish-brown roofs sit among dense foliage, and wispy white smoke rises from the chimney of the central one. In the distance at the left, a large, low structure stands atop a hill with faint paths winding toward it, while the far landscape at the right fades to pale blue and green under puffy gray clouds. It is a tiny oil on wood, 17.6 by 28.1 centimeters.","i":"Lépine was born in Caen, Normandy, and painted there and in the surrounding countryside in the 1870s in a gentle, silvery manner. A view on the outskirts, with houses sinking into gardens and smoke from a chimney, shows the town fading into the country, a transitional zone that suited painters of modest, unspectacular subjects. Such small panels were made for the pleasure of collectors who liked the intimate sketch.","c":"A tiny panel with a large feeling of space: the elevated view, the gray clouds and the muted greens give a quiet, truthful sense of a Normandy day, and the smoke from the chimney is a nicely observed note. Lépine's handling is delicate and slightly timid, and the picture lacks the dazzle of Monet or Pissarro, but its modesty is part of its appeal. It is a good example of the intimate landscape of Corot's heirs."},"nga:89681":{"d":"A tiny oil on board, only 14.6 by 23.7 centimeters, shows the Louvre as part of a quiet Paris scene: a historic building at the left, a bridge or other structure at the right, a flagpole with a flag at the center, a person walking along a path and a horse-drawn cart nearby, with figures and goods in the distance. The brushwork is loose and impressionistic and the palette muted, giving the little view a tranquil atmosphere.","i":"The Louvre, the former royal palace that became the national museum, was by the 1880s a symbol of France's artistic heritage, and the river quays and bridges around it were favorite motifs for painters of the city. Lépine painted Paris repeatedly in his later career, often in small panels like this one, for collectors who preferred a modest, intimate view to a grand Salon scene. Daily life, carts and strollers, animates the monument without overshadowing it.","c":"At this size the picture is a note rather than a statement, but a delicate one: the subdued tones, the tiny figures and the loose touch recall Corot's views of Paris and Jongkind's quays. Lépine never reaches the brilliance of the leading Impressionists and his work can be repetitive, but his modest, sensitive records of the city are among the most agreeable of the 1880s, and this one is a good example."},"met:15147":{"d":"A man is shown in a portrait miniature, watercolor on ivory, only 6.1 by 4.8 centimeters, dated 1799; the title gives no name. At this size the likeness fits in the palm of a hand and was meant to be worn or kept in a case. Raphaelle Peale, the eldest son of the Philadelphia painter Charles Willson Peale, made his living as a miniature painter in the 1790s, before turning to still life, for which he is best remembered.","i":"In the 1790s portrait miniatures were the favored form of portrait for Americans of means, exchanged between lovers and kept by families, and Philadelphia, then the national capital, supported several skilled practitioners in the Peale family. An unnamed gentleman has lost his identity but retains the neat dress and modest self-presentation of the period. Raphaelle trained under his father and worked in a clear, precise manner, with fine hatching that built flesh tones on ivory.","c":"A small, neat likeness of the kind Raphaelle Peale turned out in numbers: clean drawing, a tidy finish and an unflattering honesty. It does not have the individuality of his later still lifes, where he showed his true originality, and the sitter's character stays muted. But as evidence of the early American miniature tradition and of Peale's training in precision, it is of interest."},"met:11736":{"d":"A woman wearing a fichu, a cap and a ribbon is shown in a portrait miniature on ivory, painted in watercolor in 1800 and measuring 8.7 by 7 centimeters. The title names her as Mrs. John Montgomery, born Sarah Diemer. The fichu, a light scarf worn crossed over the bodice, and the cap are the fashionable accessories of an American woman at the turn of the century, and Raphaelle Peale renders them with the precise, fine touches of a trained miniaturist.","i":"Women were commonly named in titles by their husbands' names at this time, and Sarah Diemer Montgomery, like many American women of her generation, is remembered mainly through a portrait: a miniature that her family would have kept as a record of her appearance and a keepsake. The fichu and cap, simple and modest, signal respectability and domestic virtue, qualities valued in the early republic.","c":"The miniature has charm: delicate coloring in the face, clear drawing and an attractive economy in the treatment of cap and ribbon. Peale was a careful, if not brilliant, miniaturist, and the likeness is sober rather than animated; the real discovery of his career was still ahead in the field of still life. As a record of a woman of 1800 it is a fine, modest piece."},"met:11735":{"d":"A small portrait of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory and only 5.6 by 4.7 centimeters across, small enough to rest in a palm or a fitted case. The title names the sitter as Peter Gilman Odlin, though no description of his dress or features is recorded. Raphaelle Peale made it around 1805 in the miniature manner his father, Charles Willson Peale, practiced and taught: fine strokes of translucent color laid over the pale, glowing ivory.","i":"Portrait miniatures did the work of private photographs in the early republic: they were given at engagements, exchanged between distant relatives and kept as remembrances after a death. A full name in the title points to a likeness made for family or friends rather than for public display, although nothing in the available record says who Odlin was or who ordered the picture, so his rank and occupation cannot be read from it.","c":"At this scale there is little room for display, and Peale's miniatures rarely seek it. The interest lies in careful, level looking and the clean glow of thin color on a white ground. It is capable work of a kind many American miniaturists produced, and it is easily outweighed by his later still lifes, where the same patient eye found subjects that suited it better."},"met:15148":{"d":"An unnamed man is shown in portrait form, painted in watercolor on ivory. At 7.3 by 5.9 centimeters it is smaller than a playing card, made to be held close or kept in a case. The title records only that the sitter was a gentleman, with no name attached, so the picture speaks as a kind rather than as one man's likeness: the private keepsake. The museum dates it about 1800, when Raphaelle Peale was in his twenties and working in the miniature manner he had learned from his father.","i":"A title like Portrait of a Gentleman is a museum's way of saying that the sitter's name has been lost, and with it the point of the object: a miniature stood for a person who was absent or gone, and its value lay in recognition. Around 1800 such likenesses were often cheaper than an oil portrait, and miniaturists commonly traveled from town to town to supply them.","c":"A miniature has no scale to impress with, so it must win by delicacy and a convincing face. Judged by the standards of the form, an ivory like this is sound, modest craftsmanship. Without a name or a story behind the sitter it holds interest mainly as evidence of a skill the Peale family practiced as a trade, a long way from the still lifes that later made Raphaelle's name."},"met:15149":{"d":"Watercolor on a thin slice of ivory, 6.7 by 5.3 centimeters: the sitter is a man whom the title leaves unnamed. The museum dates the miniature about 1800. Ivory is pale and slightly translucent, so light passes through the thin paint and returns from the surface beneath, giving flesh tones a glow that opaque paint on panel or canvas cannot match. Miniaturists built the effect from many small, closely set touches, and Raphaelle Peale, trained by his father, practiced the form.","i":"Ivory became the usual support for miniatures during the eighteenth century, replacing vellum and card, because it took gum-bound watercolor well and gave that lit glow. The price was fragility: heat, damp and handling could crack or stain it, so such pictures lived in cases and drawers. Why this gentleman sat is unrecorded, but the medium itself says something: a likeness meant to be handled, held close to the eye, and kept.","c":"Watercolor on ivory forgives little, since a wrong touch cannot be scraped back as it can in oil, and the form rewards economy. Within those limits this is a competent, composed likeness, the kind the trade demanded, though it does not show the invention of Peale's best still lifes. Its value for a visitor is as a reminder that the same hand later painted a peach or a cake with the same unhurried exactness."},"nga:110101":{"d":"Fruit and glass fill a shallow tabletop that runs off both sides of the picture. A white bowl with a scalloped rim holds pale oranges, nuts and a bunch of shriveled grapes that look like raisins still on the vine; around it stand a lemon, a decanter with a teardrop stopper, four walnuts and a flaring glass nearly full of peach-colored liquid. Light from the front left makes that liquid glow, while the background deepens to shadow at the upper left and lightens to gray at the lower right. Peale signed and dated the front edge: Philadelphia, 5 August 1814.","i":"Dessert in the early nineteenth century meant fruit, nuts, sweet wine and sometimes cake, brought out once the main dishes were cleared. Oranges and lemons were imported luxuries, so the table suggests a prosperous household, though Peale painted a display, not a meal in progress. He wrote his name on the table's edge as if it were part of the scene. Still life found few buyers in the young republic, where portraits paid better, and Peale is usually counted among its first dedicated American painters.","c":"Peale's best still lifes get their force from restraint: objects pressed close to the front, a plain table, an even tone, light doing the modeling. Here the glow in the glass and the shadowed upper left give the fruit weight and quiet. The shriveled grapes can be read as a small reminder of time. It is a drier, plainer art than the Dutch banquet pictures it distantly recalls, and the plainness is what looks modern."},"nga:164944":{"d":"The title names the subject, strawberries with cream, a summer dessert painted in oil on a horizontal wood panel of 33.3 by 49.5 centimeters. Raphaelle Peale made it in 1816, in the middle of the run of small tabletop pictures he painted in Philadelphia, which show a few household foods and vessels close to the viewer on a plain surface. Its exact arrangement is not documented here; the format and support mark it as a modest cabinet still life, small enough for a private room.","i":"Strawberries and cream were a seasonal treat on English and American tables, eaten when the berries ripened in early summer, and a picture of them fixes a pleasure that lasts only weeks, one of the oldest jobs of still life. Peale was among the first Americans to make a specialty of the genre, at a time when portraits and history pictures carried the prestige and the commissions.","c":"Soft, perishable food painted with a hard, exact eye is the central pleasure of Peale's method, and a subject this simple suits it. Pictures of this sort lean on placement and tone rather than incident, so they succeed or fail on small judgments of spacing and light, and Peale usually judged them well. Set beside grand European still lifes his are plain; seen for what they are, a quiet argument for looking at ordinary things, they hold up."},"met:11737":{"d":"A cake, grapes, other fruit and wine are set out together on a tabletop in this small horizontal picture, oil on wood, 27.3 by 38.7 centimeters. Raphaelle Peale's still lifes usually gather a handful of dessert things close to the front edge of the table, so that each object can be read at once. The museum's keywords name grapes, fruit, wine and cakes, and the date, 1818, places the panel in the central years of his short career as a still-life painter.","i":"Sweet cake and wine belonged to the dessert course, the part of a meal given to fruit, nuts and sweets. Peale's choice of such things, rather than game or flowers, keeps his pictures close to the ordinary comforts of an American household. Grapes and wine carry old associations with abundance and its brevity, and a cake invites the thought of a meal just finished or about to begin, but nothing in the subject insists on a moral.","c":"Peale's tabletop pictures are admired for what they leave out. In his lifetime the market for such work was thin, as the story is usually told, and his reputation grew mostly in the twentieth century, when critics found something modern in his plainness. At 27 by 39 centimeters this is a small picture whose force lies in placement and tone, not in anything theatrical, and that restraint is the point."},"met:15150":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified only as P. F. Ronbeau, painted in watercolor on ivory and measuring 6.2 by 5.2 centimeters. Raphaelle Peale made it in 1821, years after he had begun painting the still lifes for which he is now best known, which shows that he kept the miniature trade going alongside the tabletop pictures. Nothing is documented of the face, dress or setting, and the picture stands simply as a small private likeness.","i":"Initials and a surname are all the title offers, so the sitter's rank and trade remain unknown. The date is the more telling fact. Peale's last years are usually described as hard, with illness and money worries, and small commissions like this would have been welcome work. For its first owner a miniature was personal, a face to keep close, and it still carries that purpose with it, even with the person behind it unrecorded.","c":"As a miniature it is sound, restrained and modest in ambition. It matters chiefly as a reminder that Peale never lived on still life alone: the tabletop pictures that now carry his reputation sold poorly in his lifetime, as the record is usually told, and likenesses like this were the steadier trade. A fair judgment of the artist rests on his panels, not his ivories."},"nga:164945":{"d":"The title lists the contents: apples, sherry and a tea cake, set out in a small tabletop picture in oil on a wood panel 26.7 by 41.6 centimeters. The horizontal panel follows the format Raphaelle Peale used for most of his still lifes. No fuller description is on record, so the arrangement of the glass or bottle and the fruit is left to the viewer's own looking. It dates from 1822, three years before his death in 1825, and belongs to the late phase of his still-life work.","i":"Sherry is a fortified wine from southern Spain, long drunk in English-speaking households, and a tea cake was a plain sweetened bread or small cake taken with tea or wine. Together with apples the three items describe a modest refreshment of the kind a household might offer a visitor, scaled to the parlor rather than the banquet hall. Such humble, domestic subjects were unusual in American art of the 1820s, and part of why his still lifes were slow to find buyers.","c":"A title as plain as this one suits Peale's manner, which is never showy. The attraction lies in how little is asked of the viewer: ordinary things, honestly placed, with light left to give them substance. It lacks the novelty of his earliest still lifes, but the same eye is at work, and for American painting of the period that eye was unusually clear."},"met:436885":{"d":"A woman and a man act out a moment from a story on a small tempera panel, 33.0 by 41.0 centimeters. The title says only that the tale comes from a novella, and neither the story nor its characters are named, so the picture can only be placed in general: a narrative panel of the kind made for Renaissance households. Liberale da Verona, who began as a manuscript illuminator, painted in tempera on wood, the usual medium of Italian panels before oil took over, and he was practiced at telling stories in small formats.","i":"In Renaissance Italy a novella was a short prose tale, the model being Boccaccio's Decameron, and such stories of love, trickery and fortune were favorite subjects for painted marriage chests and panels that furnished private rooms. They were meant to entertain and sometimes to instruct a family or a wedding party. With no episode named, the scene is usually read in general terms as a tale of love or intrigue; the small, nearly square format fits domestic use, though the record does not say what it once belonged to.","c":"A critic would prize this panel less for any one passage than for the side of Liberale it preserves: a secular, story-telling hand, apart from the altarpieces and illuminated choir books on which his fame rests. His miniaturist's training suits a small narrative, and such pictures are among the lightest, most inventive corners of Renaissance painting. As a minor, entertaining commission it is easy to place; beside his altarpieces it is a more intimate performance."},"met:436884":{"d":"Under a loggia with slender teal columns, a young man and woman sit across a small table, their hands touching over a chessboard of red and cream squares set with black pieces. Both have thick golden curls; he wears a pale gold robe slipping from one shoulder and red boots, and she a cream gown trimmed with gold. A group of young men in tights and fitted jackets stands at the left, one in a red cap with his arms folded, and a row of women in cream and gold dresses watches from the right. A red-framed window behind them shows a pale landscape. The table is covered with dark green brocade patterned in gold.","i":"Chess was a favourite metaphor of courtship in the Italian Renaissance, and a game between a man and a woman, watched by courtiers, was a stock scene on painted furniture such as marriage chests. The exact story, if there is one, and the original use of this small panel are uncertain. Liberale da Verona, born in Verona about 1445, first made his name as a manuscript illuminator and spent much of the 1460s and 1470s in Siena, and the vivid colour, gilding and ornamental line of book painting carry over into the panel. The Met dates it about 1475.","c":"A charming picture whose merits are mostly decorative. The curling golden hair, the sinuous poses and the combination of gilded cloth and bright flat colour give it an enamelled sparkle, and the drama between the couple, hands touching across the board, is neatly staged. But the space is unconvincing: the figures are crowded into a shallow box, the columns act as decorative bars rather than architecture, and the faces are all alike. It succeeds more as courtly fashion than as storytelling, and it shows late Gothic elegance surviving in Italy beside the new perspective."},"met:437831":{"d":"One painter portrays another: Louis Tocqué shows Jean-Marc Nattier with a palette, the sign of his profession, in an oil on canvas of 77.5 by 59.1 centimeters, an upright picture of modest size. The museum dates it about 1740. Pose, costume and setting are not described; the portrait belongs to the type in which a sitter's trade is stated by an attribute held or shown.","i":"Nattier (1685 to 1766) was among the most sought-after portraitists at the court of Louis XV, known for flattering, idealized likenesses of royal and aristocratic women. Tocqué trained under him and in 1747 married his daughter, so the portrait joins master and pupil within one circle. Showing a painter with his palette asserts the dignity of the profession in a society where the Academy was working to raise painters above the status of craftsmen.","c":"Tocqué is often said to have been the more sober and observant of the two, where Nattier flattered. A portrait of his own master would test that candor, and the palette keeps the sitter's profession in plain view. As an image it is more interesting for what it tells about the Paris art world of the 1740s than for formal daring; it is dignified, careful portraiture of a high standard."},"met:436975":{"d":"A woman, a cat and a room: the keywords name the figures and the setting, and the title, Reverie, names the mood. The picture is a small upright oil on canvas, 30.5 by 23.5 centimeters, painted in 1875 and bequeathed to the Metropolitan Museum in 1892. The record gives no further description, so details of pose and dress are left to the viewer, but it is presumably a quiet interior with a single figure absorbed in thought, in the dreamy manner for which Matthys Maris is known.","i":"A woman lost in thought was a favorite subject of nineteenth-century painting, when inwardness had become a theme in its own right. Maris, a Dutchman who left for Paris and later settled in London, was admired by collectors for hazy, poetic pictures of this kind, and his later work is often called fairy-tale-like. The cat adds domestic ease, but the picture invites mood rather than story, and the title tells the viewer to expect that.","c":"Maris is at his best when edges soften and subject gives way to feeling, and a title like this announces the method. The slightness is both the charm and the limit: it is a lyric in a minor key. Beside the sunlit landscapes of his brother Jacob, it is private and inward, and the comparison shows how differently the two Marises used the same Hague School background."},"met:436858":{"d":"A man and a woman are in a bedroom in this large canvas, about 189 by 161 centimeters, probably painted around 1640. The title identifies the episode as the rape of Tamar, from the Second Book of Samuel in the Bible. The painter, Eustache Le Sueur, a Parisian pupil of Simon Vouet who was later called the French Raphael, chose a dramatic and unusual subject for a picture on this scale.","i":"In the biblical story Amnon, King David's eldest son, lures his half-sister Tamar to his chamber under the pretense of illness and assaults her, and then casts her out in hatred. Her brother Absalom later has Amnon killed, and the episode begins the chain of calamities in David's house. Baroque painters treated the story as a warning about uncontrolled desire and the breakdown of a royal family.","c":"Le Sueur's reputation rests on a clear, restrained classicism learned from Vouet and from Raphael's example, and a subject of violence tests that restraint. A painter of calm, orderly compositions may be uneasy with such a scene, and the picture should be judged by how its clarity of design handles an ugly episode. It is an interesting and uncommon work in his output rather than a typical one."},"nga:56702":{"d":"Seated in profile and facing right, a woman looks down through lowered eyes at a lute she holds upright in her lap. Her head is high, her tawny-brown hair gathered at the back of her neck, her pale-pink lips slightly parted. Her long gown swirls in pine, moss and sage green, loosely painted around the neckline so that it is hard to tell whether the skin is bare or covered by sheer fabric. She is brightly lit from above, while the bench dissolves into a nearly black ground. The mahogany-brown lute is finely detailed, in contrast to the loose brushwork of her dress.","i":"Dewing painted women in quiet reverie for most of his career, usually unnamed, with music, flowers or fine fabrics as props. The lute was an old emblem of harmony and love, but here there is no story, and she is not shown playing; the picture is about mood. Painted in 1886, it belongs to the Aesthetic movement's taste for beauty and suggestion, with the figure treated as an arrangement of tone and colour rather than as a portrait.","c":"A refined picture in which closely related greens against near-black are handled with taste, and the contrast of the carefully drawn lute with the dissolving gown is well judged. The figure is idealised and anonymous, and the mood, though undeniably pretty, is delicate to the point of slightness. Dewing later pushed this vein of reverie much further into hazier paintings and pastels, but here it is already assured."},"nga:186":{"d":"Christ hangs on a T-shaped cross against a gold ground, his head tipped down and his eyes dark slits, scarlet blood running from the nails and from the wound in his side. His body is thin, the ribs showing, and a white loincloth edged with gold covers his hips. On the sand-colored ground two figures sit to either side, hands clasped around raised knees, mouths parted in grief: at left a woman in a white veil and gold-edged blue hood, at right a figure in a sky-blue robe and rose-pink cloak. Punched dots form the halos and a floral band across the top.","i":"The mourners are conventionally identified as the Virgin Mary and Saint John the Evangelist, the two whom John's Gospel places beside the cross. Here they sit, which brings them low to the earth and makes their grief humble and almost bodily. Sienese painters of the fifteenth century kept the gold ground and punched ornament of earlier panels, so that the scene reads as a devotional image outside ordinary space rather than a record of a place. The small size suggests private prayer or a section of a larger altarpiece.","c":"Sano di Pietro was no restless innovator; he worked inside the Sienese tradition of gold, sweet color and clear outline, and did so with great consistency. The seated mourners, hands knotted around their knees, are the most affecting invention here: their stillness makes the suffering above feel quieter, not louder. Beside Florentine painters of the same decade, who were working out perspective and volume, it looks deliberately old-fashioned, and that is part of its appeal."},"met:437603":{"d":"A long, low panel of tempera and gold, 14.0 by 29.2 centimeters, shows the burial of Saint Martha; angels are among the museum's keywords. Its shape is that of a predella, one of the small narrative scenes set in a row beneath the main images of an altarpiece, and Sano di Pietro, who ran a busy workshop in Siena, painted many such panels. The figures themselves go undescribed, but the panel is plainly a saint's legend told in gold and color, for reading at close range.","i":"Martha, the sister of Mary and Lazarus in the Gospels, was honored in medieval legend as a saint who carried the faith to southern France, and her death and burial are told in the Golden Legend, the thirteenth-century collection of saints' lives on which painters drew constantly. Scenes of this kind, set low in an altarpiece, let worshippers follow a saint's story in small steps, and they gave the painter more freedom for lively gesture than the solemn central panels allowed.","c":"Predellas show Sano at the scale where his gifts come most naturally: a few clear figures, gold used decoratively, and a patient, unhurried way of telling a story. The narrow format forbids elaborate space, and he does not fight it. What such a panel lacks is the spatial adventure of Florentine contemporaries; what it offers is clarity and decorative sweetness, a good example of the Sienese preference for ornament over experiment."},"nga:417":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, the Virgin sits at the center with the Child upright on her arm, his body turned toward her and his head turned to look out to the viewer's left. She wears a cobalt-blue mantle bordered in gold over a rust-orange robe; he holds a small bird with a red face and gold-and-black wings. Two gray-haired men stand in prayer at the lower corners, one with a rosary, and four blond angels in wreaths of roses and leaves lean in from the sides, all against gold. The panel is 62.5 by 42.0 centimeters.","i":"The saints are named in the title: Jerome, the scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, and Bernardino of Siena, the Franciscan preacher who died in 1444 and was canonized in 1450. Bernardino's presence suggests a Sienese buyer in the decades after his canonization, when his cult was fresh. The bird in the Child's hand, with its red face, is probably a goldfinch, a traditional sign of his future Passion, since the bird was linked with thistles and thorns.","c":"Sano's Madonnas have a sweetness that can read as formula, and this one is no exception: almond eyes, a small coral mouth and a gold-patterned mantle give a calm, formal grace closer to an icon than to a portrait of a mother. The turned head of the Child and the faces stacked close at the edges add some life to a design set by tradition. It is accomplished, conservative and extremely consistent."},"met:437602":{"d":"The three kings come to kneel before the Christ Child, the subject named in the title, on a wood panel in tempera and gold measuring 30.2 by 47.6 centimeters. The museum's keywords record horses among the figures, which points to the mounted retinue that Adoration scenes of the period usually show. How the figures are grouped is not recorded; the panel is a small, gilded narrative of the kind Sienese workshops made for churches and private chapels.","i":"In Matthew's Gospel the Magi are wise men from the east who follow a star to Bethlehem and offer gold, frankincense and myrrh. Later tradition made them three kings of different ages, with the eldest kneeling first. Fifteenth-century Tuscan painters loved the subject because it allowed a rich cavalcade of horses, attendants and costly dress, a courtly pageant set beside a humble stable, and patrons sometimes had themselves painted in the train.","c":"Sano di Pietro painted this subject more than once, and the version here, from about 1470, is typical of his late manner: a graceful pageant in which every figure is placed with care and the gold does decorative duty. A critic would call it charming rather than searching. It asks the eye to admire the procession, not to enter a convincing space, and in that it stays loyal to Siena while Florence moved on."},"met:437604":{"d":"A gold-ground panel in tempera on wood groups the Virgin and Child with two saints and two angels, and adds, as the title says, the dead Christ. The saints are Agnes and Catherine of Alexandria, young martyrs often paired in Italian altarpieces, Agnes usually with a lamb and Catherine with a wheel. No size is given and the arrangement is not described, but the title makes clear that this devotional image, dated about 1470 to 1480, sets the infant Christ and the dead Christ together.","i":"Placing the living child beside the dead Christ makes a single devotional argument: the Incarnation and the Passion are one story, and the Virgin's tenderness for her infant already holds her grief for the man. Agnes and Catherine, both virgin martyrs, offer the viewer examples of constancy. Images like this served private prayer, where a worshipper could move in thought from the cradle to the tomb.","c":"The device is more interesting than the execution. Sano's late work leans on settled formulas, and the sweetness of his faces can verge on routine. Yet a baby and a corpse in one image give the panel an edge his standard Madonnas lack, and the gold-ground Sienese manner, already old-fashioned by the 1470s, is handled with the ease of a long-running workshop."},"met:435941":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a nobleman, painted by Samuel Cooper in 1649 on vellum laid over prepared card, the usual support for English miniatures of the time. The title names the sitter as Henry Carey, second Earl of Monmouth (1596 to 1661); his dress and pose are not described, but the object is clear enough in kind: a small, private likeness of a peer, made to be kept and shown to friends.","i":"Henry Carey inherited his earldom from his father, Robert Carey, the courtier who in 1603 rode north to Edinburgh to tell James VI that Elizabeth I was dead. The portrait dates from 1649, the year Charles I was executed, when the English nobility faced an uncertain future. A miniature of this kind was a private commission, to be kept in a case, and a record of a man's face in the age before photography.","c":"Cooper, often called the van Dyck of English miniature, was prized for a broad, unfussy touch and a sharp sense of character that set him apart from the more decorative miniaturists before him. His mature years fall in the 1650s, and he painted Parliamentarians and royalists alike, Oliver Cromwell among them. A nobleman of 1649 sits comfortably in that company, in a form where Cooper's candor was the selling point."},"met:435942":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman, painted by Samuel Cooper in 1653 on vellum laid on prepared card. The title carries a caution: she is only said to be Lucy Percy, Countess of Carlisle, so the identification is a tradition rather than a certainty. Her features, dress and setting are left undescribed; as an object it is a private likeness for someone who knew her face and wanted to keep it close.","i":"If the old identification is right, the sitter is Lucy Hay, born Percy, Countess of Carlisle, a celebrated beauty and political schemer at the court of Charles I, whom Van Dyck painted. A woman of her age shown in 1653 would be in her early fifties, long past the court's glory days. The hedged title is a reminder of how easily names come away from small, portable objects.","c":"Cooper is the most painterly of English miniaturists: faces modeled in soft shadow, clothing and background often left loose, candor valued above flattery. Whoever the sitter was, a likeness in this manner holds attention by observation alone, and that is the mark of his best work. The tentative title costs the picture its story, not its quality as a record of a living face."},"met:856295":{"d":"A man's head and shoulders are painted in oil on paper later laid down on canvas, in a study of about 51 by 47 centimeters dated 1848. No sitter is named, and the picture reads as a study from a model rather than a formal portrait. Oil on paper was a favorite medium for quick studies in the French academic tradition, light to carry and fast to dry. François-Auguste Biard, a painter famous for dramatic pictures of travel and adventure, made it in the revolutionary year of 1848.","i":"Oil studies of heads were working tools of French painters, made to capture a particular type, pose or lighting effect before it was used in a larger picture. Biard, who traveled to Lapland, Spitsbergen and Brazil and painted vast scenes of exotic adventure, would have needed such studies of figures. Whether this sketch was preparatory for a particular composition is not recorded, and the man's identity is unknown.","c":"As a study it is fresh: quick, assured brushwork that seeks structure and character rather than polish. Biard is better known for theatrical and sometimes sensational history and genre paintings, and in a bust study his technique has more honesty than in finished pieces. It is a good example of how a modest preparatory sketch can reveal a painter's direct handling."},"nga:232107":{"d":"A woman's head is painted in oil on a wood panel only 8.3 by 7.6 centimeters, smaller than a playing card. The title is a cataloguer's label and says no more than that the subject is a woman's head; no sitter, costume or setting is recorded. The date is about 1852. Henri Le Secq is best known as a photographer, and at this size the panel reads more as a study or keepsake than as a finished picture made for exhibition.","i":"Le Secq trained as a painter in the studio of Paul Delaroche, where he met Gustave Le Gray, and took up photography in the late 1840s. In 1851 he was one of five photographers commissioned for the Mission héliographique, which recorded France's historic monuments. An oil head from about 1852 belongs to the years when he moved between the two arts. Without a record of the sitter, her identity and the purpose of the little panel remain unknown.","c":"An oil head the size of a palm makes no claim to be a picture for the wall. Its interest is as evidence of the painter's training behind the camera: Le Secq's photographs of French cathedrals are admired for firm composition and deep tonal contrast, qualities a pupil of Delaroche would have learned first with the brush. As painting it is a footnote; as a document of schooling it is worth a look."},"met:19524":{"d":"Theodore Robinson portrays himself in oil on a small canvas, 33.7 by 26.0 centimeters. The museum's keywords add that he is reading, so the painter appears absorbed in a page rather than posed to face the viewer. The date, about 1884 to 1887, falls before the Impressionist landscapes that made his name, while his dress and setting are not described; it appears to be a modest, informal self-portrait of the kind an artist paints for himself.","i":"Painters' self-portraits can claim rank, show off skill or simply put a face on hand; showing himself reading ties Robinson to a studious, reflective idea of the artist. The picture predates the Giverny years from 1888, when he befriended Claude Monet and became one of the American painters who carried Impressionism home. Before that he had trained in Paris under Carolus-Duran and Jean-Léon Gérôme, an academic schooling often said to remain visible in his drawing.","c":"A self-portrait made on the way to Impressionism, the sitter's attention on a page and not on the viewer, is unassuming by design. Beside the sunlit landscapes of his Giverny years it belongs to an earlier, quieter manner, and its interest lies in the sitter's inwardness more than in any display of technique."},"met:11934":{"d":"Trees and a house are the elements the museum's keywords name in this landscape, an oil on canvas of 65.4 by 81.3 centimeters painted in 1889. The title promises an elevated view, looking down on the scene rather than across it. The date places it in the years Robinson spent at Giverny, the Normandy village where Claude Monet lived, and a high viewpoint fits the hills around it, though the record does not name the place. No fuller description of the color or composition is given.","i":"High viewpoints were part of the Impressionist repertoire, which favored unconventional angles learned partly from Japanese prints and photography. In 1889 Robinson was absorbing Monet's lessons about light and broken color while keeping, as critics often say, a firmer, drawn structure from his academic training in Paris. A bird's-eye view also flattens the usual stage of foreground, middle ground and distance, turning a landscape into a pattern of roofs, trees and fields.","c":"Robinson at his best brings Monet's lighter palette into a structure he never abandoned, and the balance is what makes him the steadiest of the American painters around Giverny. A raised view tests it, since many small things, trees, roofs and fields, must hold together on one plane. At this size and date the picture is representative rather than exceptional."},"nga:166468":{"d":"From a high hill the eye travels across a broad valley to a winding river in this loosely painted landscape, 65.9 by 81.6 centimeters. The slope falls from the right edge to the valley floor, with darker green trees along both banks and a few buildings on the far side, and sapphire-blue hills rise to a horizon about two-thirds of the way up, under a robin's-egg sky with a few white clouds. Everything is built from visible strokes of fresh green and shimmering, smoky blue.","i":"The title places the view in the hills above Giverny, the village where Claude Monet lived and where Robinson spent much of the years from 1888 to 1892. The distant buildings are catalogued as including the church of Notre-Dame at Vernon. Monet's circle taught him to render the effect of light on a landscape in separate touches of color, and the long recession from a high viewpoint is a staple of the Giverny painters. This picture dates from 1892.","c":"The paint is the subject. Robinson builds the valley from small dabs and strokes of fresh green and smoky blue and lets distance happen through color alone, the warm near slope giving way to cool hills and a pale sky. The design is deliberate, with the hillside on one side answering the river on the other, but it avoids both Monet's bravura and mere sketchiness. A modest, persuasive picture of a place."},"nga:177034":{"d":"A woman sits outdoors on a wooden chair, turned to the left and shown in profile, her head bent over a white cloth she holds in her hands. Her dark hair is pinned in a bun, her legs are crossed, and she wears a white bodice with a pink skirt, a small hoop earring and a bracelet. Another piece of white fabric lies by her feet. A large tree rises at the right, with green leaves above, yellow grass below, patches of blue sky through the canopy and the roof and side of a building behind. The canvas is a tall 147.6 by 92.1 centimeters.","i":"A layette is the set of clothes and linen prepared for a newborn, so the quiet sewing suggests a woman waiting for a child, and the picture joins a long tradition of domestic subjects in which needlework stands for patience and care. Setting such a figure outdoors, in light filtering through leaves, brought a figure subject into the Impressionist way of seeing. Robinson, who spent years at Giverny beside Monet, is known to have used photographs as aids in some of his later pictures.","c":"The tall canvas promises a major figure picture; what it gives is intimate: a woman in profile, eyes lowered, work in her hands. Turning her to the left, away from the tree, opens space ahead of her, and the white cloth, bodice and fabric give the picture its lightest notes. Robinson is better known as a landscape painter, and the interest here is how a figure becomes part of an outdoor weave of leaf and light, not portraiture."},"met:20421":{"d":"An evening landscape at a lock in Napanoch, New York, painted in 1893 in oil on canvas, 55.9 by 81.3 centimeters. The title names the hour and the place, and the museum's keywords confirm the subject as a landscape at evening. Its composition and color are not described. A lock is the gated chamber of a canal, and the town lay on the Delaware and Hudson Canal, so the scene is probably a working waterway at the close of day.","i":"In his last years Robinson painted American places, canals, farms and small towns, with the Impressionist habits he had absorbed in France. A lock at evening suits those habits: the subject is the changing light of one hour, set against a workaday structure rather than a picturesque ruin. His American pictures belong to the wider effort of the period to find at home equivalents for Monet's Normandy.","c":"Few American Impressionists chose subjects so unpretty, and the choice is to Robinson's credit: a working canal at day's end offers geometry for the light to play against. A picture of this size and title is an exercise in one hour's effect, quiet in ambition, and in his late American work it is a representative, restrained example."},"met:19523":{"d":"Boats and a river at low tide are what the title and the museum's keywords record in this oil on canvas, 45.7 by 61.0 centimeters, painted by Theodore Robinson in 1894. The title names a yacht club, so the scene is probably a leisure waterfront rather than a working harbor, with the tide out. Composition, color and figures go unrecorded; in general it is a river landscape with boats, in the Impressionist manner Robinson had learned from Monet.","i":"Yacht clubs multiplied around New York and Long Island Sound in the later nineteenth century as sailing became a pastime of the prosperous. Low tide gives a painter a changing subject: exposed banks, reflections, boats resting at odd angles. For an Impressionist, who sought the passing effect of a single hour, the state of the tide matters as much as the weather, and a club at low tide is a quietly modern version of the harbor view.","c":"Robinson's best late pictures hold a firm structure inside soft, broken color, and a waterfront gives him the chance: masts, hulls and their reflections supply verticals and horizontals to organize the light. The type can look slight, a pleasant record of a place and an hour, but the discipline of his looking keeps it from the merely pretty. Beside Monet's regatta scenes it is cooler and more restrained."},"nga:71565":{"d":"A railroad drawbridge and a boat are the subjects named by the title and keywords of this small oil on canvas, 30.6 by 40.2 centimeters, painted by Theodore Robinson in 1894. The title points to the Long Branch Railroad, a line serving the New Jersey shore resort of that name, so the scene is a modern, workaday one rather than a picturesque ruin. Composition and color are not documented; the picture can be placed only in general, as a small exterior study of a bridge over water.","i":"Bridges and railways were among the most modern subjects an Impressionist could choose, and Robinson's American years show him turning to such working scenes, canals, locks and bridges, in place of Normandy hayfields. A drawbridge is an apt motif: a bridge that opens for boats puts rail and water, the new transport and the old, in one frame, and its mechanism gives a strong geometry against which to set light and reflection.","c":"At this size the picture reads as an outdoor observation, and Robinson's gift for holding broken color inside a firm structure suits a bridge. It lacks the glow of his Giverny landscapes, but it shows an artist looking for an American Impressionism in places Monet never painted: the edges of towns, where work and water meet. The ambition is modest, and the choice of subject is the most interesting thing about it."},"met:11936":{"d":"A road and a building are the elements named in the museum's keywords for this oil on canvas, 45.7 by 55.6 centimeters, made about 1892. The French part of the title, Vieux Moulin, places it in the French years of Theodore Robinson, who lived near Monet at Giverny. The composition is not described further; the picture is a rural motif, an old mill by a road, painted by an American working in the Impressionist manner.","i":"Old mills were a favorite subject of French landscape painters from the Barbizon School onward, valued as picturesque survivals of a pre-industrial countryside and as pleasing shapes: weathered walls, a pitched roof, water close by. By 1892 Robinson was one of the American painters who had followed Monet to Giverny, and a French mill was the kind of motif through which he could show that he had absorbed the local landscape.","c":"Robinson's French landscapes of about 1892 are generally counted among his best, when his careful drawing and Monet's lighter palette were in closest balance. A building and a road are plain motifs that suit him: structure supplies the drawing and light does the rest. The picture is modest in size and ambition, and the modesty is a virtue, since he rarely overreached."},"nga:178305":{"d":"Two women stand by a deep window, one reading a letter and smiling. The younger, at left, holds the paper to the light with her brows lifted, in a black blouse over white and a long brown skirt. The older, at right, in profile and gazing down, holds another sheet and clasps her wrist, in a brown and black apron, butter-yellow sleeves and a marine-blue skirt. Both wear white caps. A table holds bowls and a skein of red yarn with a half-knitted stocking, and a picture of a bearded, haloed man hangs on the window frame. The canvas is 94.6 by 67.0 centimeters.","i":"Letters are an old theme of Dutch and Northern genre painting, from Vermeer on, where news from afar and the privacy of reading give a domestic room its drama. Here two generations share the moment, the younger woman smiling at what she reads while the older one waits with a paper of her own. The small haloed image on the window frame, probably a devotional picture of Christ, places the room in a pious household, and the half-knitted stocking shows work set aside for the news.","c":"Melchers, trained in Düsseldorf and Paris, shows early the mix of careful drawing and soft, brownish light that marked his Dutch pictures. The strength here is the light on the paper and the younger woman's face, and the objects on the table are observed with patience. The anecdote is conventional and the earthy palette a little heavy; he later brightened his color under Impressionist influence, and this is the quieter, darker side of his art."},"nga:43678":{"d":"Two girls stand hand in hand in a grassy field, the younger at left holding a doll in the crook of her arm and staring out with wide blue eyes, the taller at right turning to look down at her. The small one wears a moss-green skirt patterned with lilac flowers and a striped shirt; the other, an apricot-orange dress with a light-blue kerchief. Blue, yellow and white flowers dot the meadow, two white goats graze behind, and red roofs and chimney smoke show beyond a dip. The horizon rises almost to the top of the 150.0 by 100.4 centimeter canvas.","i":"Children and their dolls were a staple of late nineteenth-century painting, valued for tenderness and as a pretext for fresh color in a sunlit field. The held hands and the older girl's protective glance carry the title's theme of family affection. Melchers, an American who lived mostly in the Netherlands from the mid-1880s until 1915, painted peasants and children with plain sympathy. The raised horizon, which almost removes the sky, can be read as a decorative device of the 1890s.","c":"Pictures of children can slide into sentiment, and this one skirts the edge: the doll, the goats, the roofs and the smoke are all pleasing and all expected. What rescues it is the color, bold apricot against moss green in a field of small flowers, and the high horizon that holds the figures close and gives the design a flat, almost tapestry-like weight. A good, slightly sweet work by a skilled painter."},"met:437841":{"d":"Saint Margaret of Cortona, a penitent Franciscan tertiary, appears in this canvas of about 172 by 123 cm, painted about 1758, with a boy, a dog, an angel and a devil. The Neapolitan painter Gaspare Traversi, known chiefly for his lively scenes of everyday life, painted religious subjects too, and the picture mixes spiritual drama with the vivid characterization he brought to genre scenes. The tall canvas suits an altar or a chapel wall.","i":"Margaret (about 1247–1297) lived with a nobleman and bore him a son; when he was murdered, his dog is said to have led her to the body, and she took it as a call to repentance. She became a Franciscan tertiary in Cortona and devoted herself to penance and the care of the sick. She was canonized in 1728, so a picture of about 1758 shows a fresh cult, and the dog, boy, devil and angel tell her story of temptation and grace.","c":"Traversi in religious mode keeps the theatrical light of the Neapolitan Baroque but brings a genre painter's attention to faces and gestures, and the devil and angel lend the scene a stage-like melodrama. The risk is overacting; the strengths are vivid characterization and a sense of human conflict. His real subject is human behavior even when the occasion is sacred, and this gives a lively, unconventional saint's picture that does not sink into piety."},"met:437842":{"d":"A man teases a sleeping girl with a feather in this genre scene of about 87 by 108 cm, painted about 1760; a woman is also present, and a container and a walking stick appear among the objects. Gaspare Traversi, a Neapolitan painter, made scenes of everyday life with an unidealized naturalism that sets him apart from the sweetness of much Rococo painting, and this one has the comic, slightly rowdy air of his work.","i":"Scenes of a sleeper being teased draw on a long tradition of genre painting, from Dutch and Flemish pictures to Spanish ones, in which the sleeper is vulnerable and the prank can be innocent or not. In eighteenth-century Naples, Traversi turned to the humor and social observation of daily life, and the mild cruelty of teasing a girl may touch on the period's attitudes to courtship. The picture can be read as comic, though with an undertone of unease.","c":"Traversi's strength is lively, unflattering observation: faces full of character and expressions caught in the moment, with an earthiness that stands against the Rococo's idylls. He is in this sense a forerunner of later realism. The drawing can be coarse, and the humor of teasing a sleeper may trouble a modern viewer, but the energy of the scene is real. It is a rare comic picture from Italian painting of the period."},"met:436639":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory shows the celebrated soprano Henriette Sontag (1806–1854), painted about 1835 by Franz Napoleon Heigel. Small likenesses of famous singers were collected and exchanged as keepsakes in the age before photography, and the support, ivory, implies a small, hand-held object. Heigel was a German portraitist, and the sitter was by then a countess and a former stage star, a combination that suited the genre of polished private likenesses.","i":"Henriette Sontag was among the most famous singers of her day. At eighteen she sang the soprano part in the first performance of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony in 1824. She married a diplomat, Count Rossi, and withdrew from the stage in 1830, which is why she appears in this period as a countess rather than as a prima donna; she returned to singing in 1849 and died on tour in Mexico in 1854. A miniature offered admirers a private likeness of a public star.","c":"A miniature on ivory demands smooth, delicate handling, and the glow of the ivory support lends skin a luminosity that oil cannot easily match. Heigel was a Munich portraitist working in a tradition that photography would soon displace, after 1839. The likeness is elegant and flattering rather than searching, which is what such keepsakes were for. It is a competent, charming piece rather than a memorable one."},"nga:166481":{"d":"Travelers pass the time in a tavern. Two men play cards at a wooden table, one seen from behind in a black top hat and long-tailed coat, the other, bearded, leaning on the table with arms folded and cards in hand, squinting at his opponent. Between them a third man in green-lensed glasses stands reading a newspaper headed The Spy. Silver coins, a purse and a tray with a decanter and glass lie on the table, and a striped bag leans against a chair. Behind, a teapot sits on a stove, a chalk face is drawn on a board, and broken pipes lie on the floor.","i":"Genre scenes of American life found a growing market in the 1840s and 1850s, and prints after such pictures circulated widely. Woodville, who went from Baltimore to study in Düsseldorf, painted this one in Paris in 1851, as his inscription says, and set it in an American tavern of the kind where stagecoach passengers waited. The newspaper title, The Spy, may be a visual joke on the third man standing beside the game, making the picture a small comedy of watching and being watched.","c":"Woodville's Düsseldorf training shows in the tight finish and the care given every prop, and the props are good: the striped bag, the decanter with a spoon propped in the glass, the chalk face on the board. The story is easy to read and the types are broad, in the fashion of the time. It is a little over-explained, but the detail is keenly observed, and it comes from the last years of a short career."},"met:436602":{"d":"The Virgin and the Christ Child appear against a gold ground on an upright panel of tempera on wood, 72.4 by 43.2 centimeters. No date is recorded and the pose, dress and ornament are not described, but the panel is evidently a devotional image of the Madonna and Child in the Gothic manner of Padua, where Guariento di Arpo worked in the fourteenth century. Gold ground and tempera were the standard means of such images, meant to glow in candlelight and to place the holy figures beyond ordinary space.","i":"Guariento worked in Padua, where Giotto had painted the Arena Chapel decades earlier, and his art joins Byzantine formality with a weight and gravity learned from Giotto's example. A Madonna and Child on a gold ground was the commonest devotional picture of the age, used in chapels, convents and homes, and the viewer would have read Mary's attention to the Child as an image of both motherhood and the Church. With no recorded date or provenance, the panel's exact use is unknown.","c":"Guariento is usually ranked as the leading Paduan painter between Giotto and Altichiero, and that position is both his honor and his limit: he gives monumental figures a Byzantine glow and a Giottesque weight, but not Giotto's drama. A gold-ground Madonna of this size shows the first quality more than the second. Its appeal is formal gravity and the sheen of gold, a good example of Gothic devotional painting rather than an inventive one."},"met:437224":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man, painted on ivory by Johann Esaias Nilson, is catalogued with no date and no dimensions, so its exact size and the sitter's identity are not on record. Nilson worked in Augsburg, a city of printmakers and goldsmiths, and the ivory support means a small, delicately finished likeness of the kind carried in a case or set into a box lid. With the sitter unnamed, it stands as a private Rococo miniature of a familiar kind.","i":"Nilson was a leading figure of the German Rococo in Augsburg, famous above all for engraved ornament and lively prints of social life, and he also made miniatures. A portrait of a man on ivory would have served family or friends as a keepsake. This one reached the museum through the collection of Moses Lazarus, given by his daughters Josephine and Sarah in his memory between 1888 and 1895.","c":"Nilson is better remembered for engraved Rococo ornament and lively social prints than for ivories, and a plain Portrait of a Man gives his wit and invention less room. What the form can show is clean drawing and delicacy of touch, which his training as an engraver would encourage. In the Rococo taste, a likeness of this kind is more likely polished than adventurous."},"met:441351":{"d":"An interior with men smoking opium, in oil on an almost square canvas, 108.0 by 100.3 centimeters, painted in 1872 by Georges Clairin. The museum's keywords list interiors, men, smoking and the Orientalist theme, while the figures, pipes and room are not described. Broadly, it is a large Orientalist genre scene of the sort shown in Paris, in which a Western painter imagines an Eastern way of idleness.","i":"Scenes of smokers and drug-takers were a stock subject of French Orientalist painters, who presented them as emblems of languor and escape in a world imagined as the opposite of industrial Paris. Clairin, who traveled in Spain and Morocco with Henri Regnault in the late 1860s, belonged to the generation for whom the East was a source of color and costume. Such pictures say more about Western fantasy than about the places they claim to show.","c":"Clairin was a virtuoso of rich color, textured costume and flashing light, and he handles such a subject with relish; the exactness of the props is the attraction. The weakness is the one built into the genre: it flatters a fantasy of the East and dwells on exotic surfaces rather than on people. It is a showy, skillful example of a type whose historical interest is now at least as great as its appeal."},"nga:1186":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a young blond woman looks off to the viewer's left, her face and body angled the same way. She wears a gold-brown cap edged with pearls, a bodice patterned in black leaf and flower lines, scarlet fabric wrapped over her shoulders to a wide V, and three strands of pearls, one with a red stone and another with a magenta pendant. Behind her a landscape recedes to walled towns on a winding river and hazy blue mountains under a pink-tinged sky. A Latin inscription runs along the bottom of the 47.0 by 30.5 centimeter panel.","i":"The Latin couplet along the base plays on the idea of a mortal artist competing with the gods through art and, however far he goes, achieving nothing; it belongs to a long tradition of verses claiming that a painter can only approach the sitter's beauty. Portraits of young women with jewels and a landscape of the kind Italian painters took from Flemish models were often made to celebrate a betrothal or marriage, but this sitter's name is not recorded.","c":"Neroccio's special gift is a delicate, long-lined elegance, and the sitter is a good vehicle: smooth skin, a straight nose, a slight curl at the lips, loose pale hair and a pearl-edged cap painted with a goldsmith's care. The calm frontal gaze is a little impersonal, closer to an ideal of beauty than to a likeness, which is the Sienese habit. Among Quattrocento portraits it is decorative and quiet rather than searching."},"met:437195":{"d":"The Virgin and Child are shown with two saints, Jerome and Mary Magdalen, on a tempera panel of 61.0 by 43.8 centimeters, a size for a chapel or a private room rather than a high altar. The museum dates it about 1490. Poses, dress and setting are not described, but the panel is a Sienese devotional image in which the Madonna receives two saints, usually known by attributes such as Jerome's scholar's or cardinal's dress and the Magdalen's long hair and ointment jar.","i":"Jerome and Mary Magdalen are the two great penitent saints of the Church: Jerome, who retreated to the desert and is often shown striking his breast with a stone, and the Magdalen, the repentant follower of Christ. Placing them beside the Virgin and Child makes a picture of intercession, the sinner bringing repentance to Mary, who presents it to her Son. Panels this size served private devotion, and the saints probably reflect the owner's own patrons or a penitential piety.","c":"Neroccio's refined, lightly drawn Madonnas are among the last flowering of Sienese quattrocento painting, and at this scale their charm is intimate. The saints add variety rather than drama, since the design is built on balance and calm. It is a gentle, conservative picture, more a pleasure to look at than a challenge, and its elegance owes more to line than to any wish to persuade the eye of depth."},"nga:14":{"d":"A young woman with long blond hair, pearls in her hair and a coral-red gown, stands on a pedestal before a river landscape, in an arched, upright panel 105.0 by 46.0 centimeters. In her right hand she holds a small navy-blue boat with a gold figure in it. Two white winged children, painted to resemble stone, brace a gold-bordered plaque of Latin verses on the pedestal's face. Behind her a river winds in an S-curve through dark green hills; at left slender figures head toward the water past a tall dark-blue building, and at right a woman in pink holds a rope tied to a second boat.","i":"Claudia Quinta was a Roman matron whose story is told by Livy and Ovid. In 204 BC the ship carrying the sacred stone of Cybele, the Great Mother, ran aground in the Tiber; Claudia, whose chastity had been doubted, prayed to the goddess and drew it free, proving her innocence. The verses on the pedestal speak in her voice: the crowd doubted her, but the ship's prow bore witness. Renaissance painters prized her as an exemplar of virtue, often in sets of famous women.","c":"Neroccio's gift for elegant, long-limbed grace is at its best here: the slender body on its pedestal, the elongated crowd in the background, the unhurried S-curve of the river. The result is more decorative than dramatic, and the miracle is reduced to a toy boat in her hand and a second boat on a rope in the distance. As an emblem more than a story it succeeds, and it is a refined example of late Sienese taste."},"nga:41627":{"d":"Four figures stand or sit against a gold ground on a marble-tiled floor. At the center the Virgin, in a red dress and a dark-blue hooded cloak, sits slightly turned to the left and leaning forward, her hands supporting the nude Child in her lap. At left an elderly bearded man in a black robe holds a staff and a red book, a small brown pig at his feet; at right a crowned figure with shoulder-length hair in a pink robe holds a rod and a sphere. All have golden halos. The panel is large, 158.5 by 142.0 centimeters.","i":"The saints are named in the title. Anthony Abbot, the hermit of the Egyptian desert, is recognized by his staff, book and pig and was invoked against skin disease and for the protection of animals. Sigismund, an early sixth-century king of Burgundy honored as a martyr, carries the royal rod and orb. A grouping of saints around the Madonna in one space is called a sacra conversazione, and the choice of these two saints probably reflects a patron's name or a local devotion.","c":"Neroccio is the most delicate of the late Sienese painters, with a goldsmith's care for line and surface, and the gold ground here is a conservative choice for the 1490s, when Florence and Venice favored spatial drama. The picture offers calm, symmetrical devotion instead: figures set in a row with little interaction, the Madonna's forward lean and the Child on her lap supplying the only warmth. The stillness is the point, and it is conservative and attractive."},"met:439346":{"d":"The forest edge, where trees meet open ground, is the subject of this small oil, about 27 by 32 centimeters, painted around 1820 by the German artist August Heinrich. The recorded motifs are the forest and the landscape itself. The small size and the close, unspectacular view are typical of the oil studies that German Romantic painters made out of doors, as exercises in observing light, foliage and space.","i":"German painters around 1820 turned to direct study of nature as a source of both truth and feeling: the woods were a symbol of German identity in Romantic literature and a place of mystery, and a forest edge, a boundary between dark interior and bright field, suggested a threshold between the known and the unknown. Heinrich died young, in 1822, so his surviving works are few, and a small study like this is a precious fragment of Friedrich's generation.","c":"The picture's virtue is the freshness of observation: quick patches of green, a dark mass of trees, a lighter field and the sense of air between them. As a study it has the directness that finished Romantic pictures often lose. It is a slight work, but it shows an artist looking hard at what was in front of him, and its early date makes it a witness to the new practice of painting outdoors."},"met:435572":{"d":"A tall panel, 151.4 by 100.3 centimeters, painted in tempera on a gold ground, brings together three subjects named in its title: Saint Giles, Christ triumphant over Satan, and the mission of the apostles. How they are arranged is not described; the panel is probably part of a Gothic altarpiece, made about 1408 by the Valencian painter Miguel Alcañiz. Giles, a hermit of southern France, is usually shown as an abbot with a hind pierced by an arrow.","i":"A saint with scenes of Christ's victory and the apostles' mission fits an altarpiece that taught the Church's story at a glance. Giles, who lived as a hermit and sheltered a wounded hind, was one of the most popular saints of medieval Europe, patron of cripples and beggars. Christ trampling Satan stands for victory over death and evil; the sending out of the apostles stands for the spread of the faith, an argument made in gold and color for a congregation.","c":"Valencian painting of about 1400 joins the gold ground and flat pattern of Gothic tradition to a taste for rich ornament and lively narrative that scholars call International Gothic. A panel like this is decorative first and dramatic second: its pleasures are surface and color, not space. It records a confident, cosmopolitan Valencian art at a moment when Italian ideas had begun to filter in."},"met:436887":{"d":"A portrait of Prince Klemens Wenzel Lothar von Metternich (1773–1859), the Austrian statesman, is dated 1822 and was made by Friedrich Johann Gottlieb Lieder, a German portraitist who worked in Vienna. It is painted on card laid down on a later support. The picture makes the minister's image its whole subject, in a delicate medium suited to the polished portraiture of the Viennese elite. The year 1822 was that of the Congress of Verona, at which he was again a leading voice of European diplomacy.","i":"Metternich was Austria's foreign minister from 1809 and chancellor from 1821, and he was the host and chief architect of the Congress of Vienna, which redrew Europe after Napoleon. His name stands for the conservative order of the following decades: the balance of power, the Holy Alliance and the suppression of liberal and nationalist movements. A portrait of 1822 shows him at the height of his power, and he survived until 1859, having fled the revolution of 1848.","c":"Lieder was a competent portraitist of the Viennese elite, and his style is refined, smooth and mildly flattering, as the sitters of the Biedermeier world expected. The strength of this picture is likely to be its record of a famous face in a composed, official manner; its limits are the lack of insight into a man whose reputation rests on intrigue and calculation. Its interest is greater for its subject than for its artistry."},"nga:71023":{"d":"A dense wooded landscape of rocky outcrops and ruins holds livestock, wild animals and about a dozen people, in an oil on panel 54.3 by 91.5 centimeters. A river curves round a grove; three deer stand in deep shadow, goats perch on cliffs, and a bird flies low carrying a snake. At lower right a cowherd with a staff stands among cows and goats; at the center a round ruined tower has a trough where cows drink, and people pass under an arch, one riding a donkey. About two dozen birds fly in a bright blue sky. Roelandt Savery signed and dated it 1624 on a rock.","i":"The biblical subject is small: the Holy Family's flight into Egypt, told in Matthew's Gospel, when Joseph, warned in a dream of Herod's plan to kill the children of Bethlehem, took Mary and the Child to safety. The group is presumably the loosely painted figures passing under the arch, one on a donkey. Northern painters often made such stories pretexts for landscape, and Savery, who had worked for Emperor Rudolf II in Prague, used this one for a menagerie in a wooded, ruin-strewn setting far from Egypt.","c":"Savery's wooded scenes are crowded and inventive, and this one rewards slow looking: the dark foreground frames a bright, airy distance, and animals, ruins and tiny figures are scattered with a naturalist's pleasure. The biblical story is nearly lost in the menagerie, and the picture is less a narrative than a showcase of variety. It is a fine example of the Northern taste for fantastical landscapes full of observed things, closer to a cabinet of curiosities than to the Gospel."},"nga:243":{"d":"A young man is shown in portrait form in an oil on canvas of 73.0 by 56.5 centimeters, a size that suits a head-and-shoulders or half-length likeness. The museum dates it after 1720, and nothing is said of his dress, pose or expression. It is an early eighteenth-century likeness by Giuseppe Ghislandi, the Bergamo painter known as Fra Galgario, who specialized in lively, informal portraits.","i":"Portraits of young men in early eighteenth-century northern Italy ranged from stately commissions for patrician families to informal pictures of friends and local figures. Ghislandi worked in Bergamo, a city under Venetian rule, for noble and merchant clients, and was admired for catching a sitter's personality with fluent brushwork rather than formal ceremony. The sitter here is not named in the record, so his rank and occupation cannot be read from the picture.","c":"Ghislandi's best portraits seem to catch a person mid-thought, with quick, unfussy brushwork and sharp characterization, and that directness set him apart from the courtly formulas of his day. Modern taste, which prefers candor to ceremony, has treated him generously. A portrait of this kind stands or falls on the freshness of the face, which is where he usually succeeds."},"met:436518":{"d":"A monumental altarpiece, nearly four meters tall (398.8 by 207.0 centimeters), shows the Madonna and Child with saints in a sacra conversazione, the Virgin and Child grouped with holy figures in a single space. The museum's keywords list men, women, angels and peacocks. Painted about 1520 in tempera and oil on canvas by Girolamo dai Libri of Verona, it is a church picture meant to be seen from across a nave, though the individual figures and the setting are not described in the record.","i":"Girolamo came from a family of Veronese manuscript illuminators, hence dai Libri, 'of the Books', and he brought a miniaturist's love of bright color and fine detail to large altarpieces. The peacocks the museum lists are probably symbols of immortality, since the bird's flesh was believed not to decay, and so a sign of the hope of resurrection. A sacra conversazione placed the saints in the Virgin's presence as companions of the worshipper's prayer.","c":"Girolamo's altarpieces are distinctive for clear, bright color and an illuminator's love of small natural detail, and their compositions are placid and symmetrical, which keeps him a quieter figure than Titian or the Venetians. At nearly four meters this is a work of scale and calm, not drama. It belongs to the gentler, provincial strain of the Renaissance, and its restraint is more a virtue than a failing."},"nga:139765":{"d":"A ruler in a laurel wreath sits on an ornately carved throne at the right, his brow furrowed and his head resting on his hand, wrapped in a yellow cloak lined with white fur; a small brown-and-white dog sits at his feet. Several figures in classical dress, some in silver armor, gather around him. Two large white columns stand at left and a purple curtain hangs at right, with blue sky and distant hills beyond. The oil on canvas measures 76.0 by 63.4 centimeters and dates from the 1760s.","i":"The title names Alexander the Great, here condemning false praise: a ruler who refuses flattery. The anecdote belongs to the stock of classical stories about royal virtue that European courts and their painters loved, and it holds a lesson for princes, whose courtiers rarely told them the truth. The furrowed brow and the head resting on the hand signal displeasure and thought; the pose is the one an artist would use for a judge or a thinker.","c":"De Mura, Solimena's pupil and one of the leading painters of Naples in his generation, handles such a subject with Rococo ease: warm gold against cool armor, a soft, silvery light, and an elegance of gesture that keeps the moral lesson from sounding stern. The columns and curtain are stock Baroque machinery. It is smooth, attractive history painting, more persuasive in its color than in its argument."},"nga:46297":{"d":"A woman stands facing the viewer in an elegant outfit with a red velvet shawl, her hair dark and straight, her skin fair, holding a bouquet of red and orange flowers. Behind her are a stone urn, greenery, a fountain and part of a garden. The canvas is a large upright format, about 143 by 111 centimeters, painted in 1859, and the sitter is Lucy Tappan Bowen, the wife of the New York businessman Henry C. Bowen.","i":"Henry C. Bowen was a New York businessman and the publisher of The Independent, an antislavery weekly, and his wife was part of a circle of reform-minded New Yorkers. The formal garden setting with urn and fountain places a wealthy woman in a world of leisure and refinement, while the bouquet adds a note of feminine grace. Portraits of this kind, painted for a family's home, were declarations of status and taste, and Carpenter, later famous for his picture of Lincoln, was an established portraitist in the city.","c":"Carpenter was a solid, dependable portraitist, and this picture shows both his strengths and his limits. The pose is graceful and the velvet and flowers are rendered with care; the garden setting adds status. But the drawing is cautious and the sitter's character is subdued by the decorative apparatus. It is a competent society portrait of the 1850s, interesting mainly as the work of a man who would soon paint a more famous subject."},"nga:106787":{"d":"A small scene of the Adoration of the Shepherds, with seven men and women gathered around an infant, sits inside a painted black octagonal frame that imitates molding, and a dense wreath of tulips, roses, daffodils, poppies and violets encircles it. In the scene the child and a woman reaching toward the bed have gold halos, a lamb with its feet tied lies at the foot of the bed, and a night sky shows beyond a ruined stone arch. The vertical picture, in oil on copper, is about 22 by 19 cm.","i":"This is a garland picture, a type developed in Antwerp in the early 1600s in which a devotional image is wreathed in flowers painted by a specialist. Here two artists collaborated, one signing the scene 'FF F.' and the other the frame with the initials HDB, a division of labor common in Antwerp. The flowers honor the sacred subject and also speak of beauty and its brevity. The lamb with tied feet is usually read as a foreshadowing of Christ's sacrifice.","c":"The copper support gives a hard, glassy surface on which small things stay sharp, and both artists exploit it: the garland has the brightness of enamel against the black ground, and the small figures are lively. The result is more decorative than devout, a showpiece of Antwerp specialization in which each hand does what it does best. Francken's figures are mannered, but the ensemble is witty and polished, and the intimate scale suits private devotion."},"nga:177779":{"d":"A river winds from the foreground to a distant mountain outlined against a dim sky, in a horizontal picture whose top is shallowly arched. The river bends from the lower right into the middle of the painting and curves out of view in a grassy field, with wisps of fog marking its distant course. At left, a compact single-story white house with a brown roof and chimney sits in a sunlit clearing among a few trees, and a tree-carpeted mountain rises sharply behind it. Pale peach clouds float above a pink-tinged horizon. Oil on canvas, about 58 by 71 centimeters, signed and dated 1851.","i":"The title identifies the house as the headquarters of George Washington, and the mountain as the Tourn in Rockland County, New York, so the picture belongs to the mid-nineteenth-century interest in sites of the Revolutionary War. For American landscape painters of the Hudson River School, a quiet view that carried national memory added value to a scenic subject. The evening glow and mist lend the place a hushed, commemorative mood.","c":"A modest, atmospheric picture in which the sunset glow, the fog and the winding river do most of the work. The house is small and humble, which gives the historical title an unassertive dignity. Compared with Cropsey's big panoramas the composition is plain and the handling looser, but it has a quiet sincerity. It is more a landscape with a memory than a historical painting, and it works best that way."},"nga:56598":{"d":"A sprawling, turreted stone castle, bronzed by the setting sun, crowns a rocky cliff at the center, outlined against roiling charcoal and indigo clouds edged in peach. A scarlet flag flutters from the tower, and on a distant crag a column of amber smoke rises. A dirt road winds down through the trees to a pale stone bridge, where helmeted horsemen in ruby-red tunics, with lances and shields, cross in a double column; goats caper nearby. Behind, in a river valley, buildings and a square tower burn. A snowy peak glows coral in the distance. Oil on canvas, about 111 by 172 centimeters, dated 1851.","i":"Cropsey painted this imagined medieval scene as an allegory of war: the riders heading toward a dark mass of trees and rock, the burning town and the stormy sky are signs of the destruction that follows an army's march. It follows the example of Thomas Cole's allegorical landscape cycles, which taught American painters to use landscape for moral and historical themes. The place is imaginary, and the picture is thought to have been painted with a companion, The Spirit of Peace.","c":"A theatrical but skillful fantasy in which the castle, the storm and the fires are staged with relish, and the sunset glow gives the violence a beauty that is a little at odds with the message. Cropsey follows Cole's allegorical manner without matching its weight, and the numerous small incidents risk diluting the whole. As an American romantic landscape of 1851 it is vivid, energetic and unashamedly dramatic."},"met:10587":{"d":"A broad view in central New York State, painted in 1853 by Jasper Francis Cropsey in oil on canvas, 84.1 by 122.9 centimeters. The title names the district, Sherburne in Chenango County, and the museum's keywords list trees, cattle and landscape. Composition and light are not specified, but the type is familiar: a large horizontal pastoral landscape of the Hudson River School, with cattle in a farmed countryside.","i":"In the 1850s the Hudson River School made American scenery a national subject, and patrons liked pictures of particular districts, named in the title like a portrait of a place. Cattle in the landscape stand for a settled, productive countryside in which nature and farming sit peacefully together, an image of America as pastoral abundance. Cropsey, trained as an architect before he turned to painting, later became famous for brilliant autumn scenes.","c":"In 1853 Cropsey may still have been working out the style that would bring him fame, and a calm, orthodox pastoral like this is far from the blazing autumn canvases that followed. The type is easy to like, and cattle and trees are standard Hudson River School furniture. It is a good, workmanlike example of the school's reliable middle register, more settled than inspired."},"nga:73436":{"d":"From a slightly elevated viewpoint the eye crosses a calm river that dominates the lower part of the picture, reflecting the greenery and sky, toward Warwick Castle, a large tan castle with many towers and battlements rising above the trees in the distance. Two white swans glide on the water. In front of the castle at the right a smaller building with white walls and red roofs sits among foliage, with one or two people seated before it and sheep resting in the grass. The palette is deep greens, soft browns and varied blues. Oil on canvas, about 81 by 132 centimeters, dated 1857.","i":"Cropsey, an American landscape painter of the Hudson River School, lived in England from 1856 to 1863 and painted English views, including historic castles, which appealed to American collectors as well as British patrons. Warwick Castle, on the River Avon, was one of the most famous medieval strongholds in England, and its picturesque situation made it a favorite with painters. The swans and sheep give the scene a pastoral calm.","c":"A pleasant, polished view in which the castle's tan masses are well balanced by the dark trees and the reflective river. Cropsey's English pictures are more subdued than his blazing Hudson River autumns, and this one is a restful, competent essay in the picturesque. It lacks the drama and color of his American work, but shows his ability to adapt his style to the soft, humid light of England."},"nga:46474":{"d":"A sweeping panorama framed by trees whose leaves blaze orange and red looks out over a distant body of water under a sun-streaked sky. Hazy mountains line the horizon. Fallen trunks and mossy boulders line the foreground; beside a small waterfall at left, three tiny men and their dogs rest around a picnic basket, rifles leaning on a tree. A meadow with grazing sheep is crossed by a winding stream and a low bridge, and beyond it a town smokes and steamboats dot the water. Rays like a starburst break from behind a lavender-gray cloud. Oil on canvas, about 152 by 275 centimeters, signed and dated London 1860.","i":"Cropsey painted this vast autumn view in London in 1860, during his stay in England, working from American sketches to show English audiences the brilliance of the Hudson Valley foliage, a spectacle without equal in Europe. Storm King Mountain and the river identify the view. The hunters resting at lower left and the peaceful farm and town below present a settled, abundant nation, the Hudson River School's picture of America in harmony with nature.","c":"A vast panorama in which the blazing foliage is the subject and the small figures and sunburst light serve it. The scale is huge, the detail minute, and the effect is of a nation's landscape as a feast of color. It is also somewhat theatrical, with each element of the Hudson River School repertoire present, and at times it verges on the garish. Even so, it remains one of the most vivid images of the American autumn in nineteenth-century painting."},"met:10589":{"d":"A river valley with trees and hills, the Wyoming Valley of Pennsylvania, painted in 1864 in oil on canvas, 38.1 by 61.0 centimeters, a small canvas beside the artist's much larger picture of the same valley a year later. The museum's keywords list trees, a river and hills, and nothing more is said of the light, season or figures; it reads as a panoramic valley view in the Hudson River School manner.","i":"The Wyoming Valley, along the Susquehanna River near Wilkes-Barre, was a famous scene to nineteenth-century Americans, partly through Thomas Campbell's poem Gertrude of Wyoming (1809) and the memory of the Wyoming massacre of 1778, and partly for its beauty. Cropsey had returned to America in 1863 after seven years in England. A view of this valley recalled both history and scenery.","c":"Cropsey's architectural training gives his landscapes a clear, legible structure, and a valley view at small scale tests it, since distance must be held without losing coherence. A small version sacrifices spectacle for intimacy. It is pleasant rather than powerful, a good minor example of a major theme, and a reminder that his reputation rests on the larger and more colorful pictures."},"met:10586":{"d":"A panoramic landscape of the Wyoming Valley, more than two meters wide (213.4 by 123.2 centimeters), painted in 1865 by Jasper Francis Cropsey. The museum's keywords list sky, clouds, a river, trees, hills, cattle and a house, so the picture combines a broad, atmospheric sky with a farmed river valley and its livestock. Light and composition are not described, but the scale and subject mark it as a large exhibition landscape of the Hudson River School, meant to impress.","i":"At this scale, painters of the Hudson River School set out to rival history painting: a big landscape could carry national feeling as a battle picture carried it, and Cropsey's valley showed Americans a region haunted by Revolutionary history yet prosperous in the 1860s. The cattle and house suggest settlement and peace, a quiet note in the year the Civil War ended.","c":"Large Cropseys make their case with sky and long recession, and the keywords promise both. The Hudson River School's grand-manner formula is at work: a wide plain, a river, light spreading over it. The risk is a pleasant sameness, scene as spectacle; the strength is breadth and a sense of air. It is a polished showpiece of the years after his return from England."},"met:10584":{"d":"A quiet landscape of northern New Jersey, painted in 1867 in oil on canvas, 51.1 by 81.9 centimeters. The museum's keywords list the Pequannock River, sky, sun, trees, hills, a road and rock, so the picture combines a river and a road with a sun in the sky. The time of day and any figures are not recorded; it is a naturalistic view of a place near New York, painted by an artist back in the United States after years in England.","i":"Pompton Plains lies along the Pequannock River in northern New Jersey, farm country an easy trip from New York City, and painters of the Hudson River School were drawn to such nearby ground as much as to the Catskills and the White Mountains. A named local view celebrated a place as a portrait celebrates a person, and the sun among its listed subjects suggests that light and weather mattered to it as much as the plain itself.","c":"Cropsey's later landscapes lean toward a glowing, orange-gold light, and a picture that lists the sun among its subjects probably shares the interest. Plain farmland offers no spectacle, so the effect must come from sky and atmosphere, and he is at his best when it does. It is a modest, agreeable record of an ordinary corner of America, closer to the everyday than to his big showpieces."},"met:10578":{"d":"An autumn view of Sugar Loaf Mountain in Orange County, New York, painted about 1870 to 1875 in oil on canvas, 72.4 by 90.2 centimeters. The museum's keywords list sky, clouds, hills, a mountain, a house, a church and a town, so the scene joins a settled village to rising ground under a cloud-filled sky. Its color and composition are not described, but the title and keywords make autumn foliage, Cropsey's signature subject, the picture's theme.","i":"Autumn color was Cropsey's trademark: his brilliant fall canvases of the 1850s and 1860s made foliage a national subject. Around 1869 he settled at Warwick in Orange County, near Sugar Loaf, so this is a view of country he knew well. A village with a church below a mountain presents autumn as a season of settled community as much as of spectacle.","c":"Cropsey's autumns are the part of his work most likely to be remembered: color pushed far without losing the structure his architect's training gives the land. The danger is a postcard sweetness, and a church and village below a mountain invite it. A late autumn view of this type has to find its life in the sky and the light, and that is where to look."},"nga:195026":{"d":"Tall, blue, snow-capped mountains rise in the distance of this wide landscape, under a yellow sky streaked with blue and purple clouds. In the foreground a tree-lined field blazes with the colors of autumn, in reds, oranges, yellows and light greens, and small white sheep graze on rolling hills that lead toward the mountains. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 112 centimeters, dated 1881.","i":"Mount Washington, in New Hampshire's White Mountains, is the highest peak in the northeastern United States, and the White Mountains were a favorite subject for the painters of the Hudson River School and their patrons. Cropsey, who excelled in autumn pictures, shows the mountain in the cold light of the late season, with snow on its upper slopes. The sheep add a pastoral note that tames the wilderness.","c":"A late Cropsey and a competent one, in which the autumn field is the flame and the distant mountains the cool counterweight. The color is intense, the composition simple, and the sheep supply scale. By 1881 the Hudson River School had been eclipsed by newer tastes, and Cropsey's work can seem old-fashioned, yet he never lost his love of the blaze of foliage, which is what makes this picture worth looking at."},"met:437920":{"d":"An upright panel of oil on wood, 85.1 by 62.5 centimeters, shows a vase of flowers, dated probably 1613 by the museum. The keywords record only a vase, flowers and the still-life type, and the species and background are not described. It is an early Dutch flower piece by Jacob Vosmaer of Delft, a kind in which a bouquet was usually painted flower by flower from separate studies and then assembled in one vase.","i":"Early Dutch flower pieces gathered blossoms that open in different seasons into a single bouquet, something no garden could give at one moment, so a painted vase offered a permanent spring. Flowers were costly, rare bulbs especially so, and the pictures could be read as pride in the wonders of creation or as a reminder that beauty fades. Vosmaer belonged to the first generation of specialists, active at the start of the seventeenth century.","c":"Early flower painters tend to place each bloom with a botanist's separateness, and a work of about 1613 is likely to share the quality: every petal looked at and set down, the bouquet more a catalogue than a cascade. The result is clear, precise and a little static beside the lavish compositions of the following generation, but the attention to each flower is what gives these panels their lasting appeal."},"nga:46517":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a clean-shaven man stands with his body squared to the viewer and his head turned to the right, looking down with brown eyes. His brown hair is parted at the side and brushed over his ears. He wears a tomato-red cravat, a white shirt, an olive-green vest patterned with a gold grid, a navy-blue coat falling nearly to the knees and brown trousers. He leans on his left hand on a stone ledge, against a flint-gray ground that deepens to iron gray; the signature F. MILLET is cut, as if carved, into the ledge's front.","i":"The title gives the sitter's name as Leconte de Lisle, which is also the name of the Parnassian poet (1818 to 1894); the record here does not confirm that he is the man shown. Millet, from a farming family near Cherbourg, studied in Paris under Paul Delaroche and painted portraits in the early 1840s to earn a living, long before the peasant subjects that made his name. A bright red cravat and an easy lean on a ledge would have signaled a fashionable, slightly bohemian taste.","c":"A sober, well-drawn portrait whose success lies in economy: the gray ground sets off the red of the cravat and the green vest, and the head is modeled with quiet care. The pose, leaning on a ledge, is a stock one, and the picture shows nothing yet of the weight and silence of Millet's later peasants. It is valuable as evidence of his academic training and his gift for plain character, not as a forecast of greatness."},"nga:46518":{"d":"A man is shown in portrait form in a small oil on canvas, 40.6 by 32.3 centimeters, dated about 1845. The title gives no name, and his face, dress and setting are not described; at this size it is probably a head-and-shoulders likeness by Jean-François Millet from the years when he still earned money from portraits, long before the peasant scenes that made him famous.","i":"An anonymous portrait of this date belongs to the period when Millet, poor and unknown, took commissions from tradesmen and local notables and painted small nudes in a Rococo manner for dealers. Portraits were the surest income for a painter without a Salon reputation, and Millet did them without ever becoming a fashionable portraitist. The sitter's name is not recorded, so only the type survives: a middle-class man of the 1840s whose likeness was bought as a keepsake.","c":"Millet's portraits of the mid-1840s are plain-spoken and undecorated, giving the head weight and letting the rest fall away; it is the instinct that later made his peasants monumental. A work of this size is an honest likeness rather than a showpiece, and it asks to be judged as such: competent, sober and unflattering, a quiet document of an artist still earning his place."},"met:438616":{"d":"A woman and a girl are the figures named by the museum's keywords, and a storm the occasion, in this small oil on canvas, 46.4 by 38.1 centimeters, painted about 1846. The title says they are retreating from the weather, so the picture records a moment of hurry and shelter. Setting, dress and sky are not described; the picture is a small genre scene with weather as a character, painted by Jean-François Millet before he settled into the rural subjects of his maturity.","i":"Around 1846 Millet was turning from portraits and Rococo-style nudes toward scenes from ordinary life, and a woman and child caught by weather belong to that shift. Storms gave painters a way to show nature's power over the humble, an idea Romantic art had made familiar, and Millet's later peasants often face such forces with quiet endurance. The two figures, perhaps mother and daughter, are a human note set against the sky.","c":"A small weather picture of this date is more a promise than an achievement. A woman and child pressed by a storm gather up the themes of Millet's later art: the human figure set against forces larger than itself, and the dignity of ordinary people. As a record of a painter finding his subject, it is more interesting than as a self-sufficient picture."},"nga:35093":{"d":"A nude woman sits on a grassy bank and puts one leg into the water, in a loosely painted horizontal picture on wood only 18.5 by 24.1 centimeters. She angles to the left, looking down, hands planted by her hips and one knee sharply bent for balance. Her skin is pale on torso, arms and thighs and deepens to reddish brown on face, hands and lower legs. Loose strokes make the tall grass and a golden-brown hill that nearly fills the top, where a cow's silhouette stands against a sliver of pale sky. It is signed JF Millet.","i":"A nude bather was a long-established subject of French art, from Boucher's goddesses to later robust bathers, and Millet painted nudes in a Rococo manner in the mid-1840s. Here the weathered face, hands and lower legs against pale skin elsewhere read as a country woman's tan, not a nymph's, and the cow on the hill confirms a rural setting. The picture catches Millet at a turning point: a classical pose given a working woman's body.","c":"The most interesting thing here is the skin: tanned hands and face against a pale torso are honest observation, and they shift a stock subject from myth toward life. The handling is quick and unfinished, closer to a sketch than a resolved picture, and the pose is awkward as well as natural. It is a small, warm, slightly rough work that shows Millet testing the truthful body he would soon give to his peasants."},"met:437096":{"d":"A tiny oil on canvas, 17.1 by 21.3 centimeters, painted in 1854, shows a garden with a tree and two figures, a man and a woman, the man identified in the keywords as a gardener. Light, season and tasks are not described; the picture is a small, intimate scene of garden work by Jean-François Millet, made in his first years at Barbizon, where he lived from 1849.","i":"In the 1850s Millet's subject was working life, and a garden is a place where labor and household meet. He tends to show tasks such as digging, planting and watering with unhurried respect and little anecdote. Pictures this size were typically made for friends, dealers or private buyers rather than for the Salon, and a bequest of 1914 from the collection of the financier Morris K. Jesup brought this one to the Metropolitan Museum.","c":"At 17 by 21 centimeters the picture can only suggest, and Millet's gift for simplified figures and weighted forms is the thing to look for. Small pieces like this are often fresher than the big Salon pictures, because they are made without the pressure to make a point. It is a minor, pleasant work, valuable for the artist's directness rather than for any ambition."},"met:437098":{"d":"A shepherdess sits on a rock, knitting, in a small oil on wood, 35.9 by 28.3 centimeters, painted in 1856. The museum's keywords name the woman, the shepherd's calling and the knitted fabric, so she works with her hands while she watches her animals. Nothing is said of the flock, the light or the landscape, but the type is a favorite of Jean-François Millet: a single figure of rural labor, seated and absorbed, treated with the weight of a monument.","i":"Shepherdesses were a cliché of Rococo pastoral, where they appeared as dainty ladies in satin. Millet replaced that fantasy with a working woman in rough clothes who knits to use the long hours of watching, and the contrast was part of his point: country labor deserved the dignity of older art. The subject proved popular with buyers, and he returned to it often.","c":"The sympathy is in the pose: a woman who sits still because her work is to wait, hands busy with wool. The danger, for Millet generally, is a pious sentiment about rural virtue that later generations found too comfortable; the best answer is the painter's plainness, which keeps the figure human. Seen at this small scale it is gentle and unforced."},"met:437099":{"d":"A woman in an apron with a rake, a field and a haystack: these are the elements the museum's keywords list for this small oil on canvas, 39.7 by 34.3 centimeters, probably of 1856 to 1857. Her pose, dress and the light are not described; she is a single figure of farm labor, one of the many such studies Jean-François Millet made at Barbizon.","i":"The probable date coincides with The Gleaners, which Millet showed at the Salon of 1857, and the figure belongs to the same world of harvest work done by women. Critics of the day were divided: admirers saw a dignified record of labor, while conservative opponents objected to treating the rural poor as monumental subjects and feared a political charge. A woman with a rake is a small piece of that argument.","c":"Millet gives a farm laborer the stance and scale of a statue, and even on a small canvas the principle shows: simplified outline, steady stance, nothing decorative. It is a studied, honest piece, though less affecting than the large Salon pictures, where the figures gather into a rhythm. It shows the method rather than the summit."},"met:437095":{"d":"Cows, a bull, mud and vegetation appear under a wide sky in this vertical oil on wood, 94.6 by 64.8 centimeters, painted about 1872. The title and the keywords place a man in the scene, calling the animals home, and the date makes it one of the last pictures of Jean-François Millet, who died in 1875. The figure, the weather and the time of day are not specified; the picture is late Millet, a pastoral subject on a generous panel.","i":"Calling cattle home, usually at the day's end, is a daily rural rite with a long tradition in pastoral art, and Millet, who kept returning to the cycle of farm tasks, treated such moments as a quiet drama. In his last years he painted more landscape and weather, setting figures within a wide sky and a muddy field. The bull and the cows give the scene its weight of animal life.","c":"Late Millet often dissolves detail into atmosphere and mud, and the keywords suggest a picture that cares more for sky and ground than for finish. A man and his animals are enough: the subject is the daily rhythm of farm life, and its strength is the ease with which a large panel holds a small human event. Compared with the famous peasants of the 1850s it is looser and more landscape-minded."},"met:437094":{"d":"An autumn landscape with a flock of turkeys, in oil on canvas, 81.0 by 99.1 centimeters, painted in 1872 to 1873 by Jean-François Millet. The title and the museum's keywords name the season, the birds and the landscape, and the near-square format gives the scene breadth and height. Sky, figures and colors are left undescribed; the picture is late Millet, a rural subject presented as an autumn view, with animals as the main actors.","i":"The picture is usually associated with a set of the Four Seasons that the collector Frédéric Hartmann commissioned from Millet, which occupied him in his last years and was left incomplete at his death in 1875. Autumn here belongs to the farmyard: turkeys were raised for the winter feasts, so the subject is a rural year in miniature, the season told through the work and food of the country.","c":"Turkeys in a field are a subject that risks the merely quaint, and the large format and landscape title show Millet asking whether the land can carry them. The picture belongs to the warmer, more painterly vein of his last years, more observation of country life than allegory. It is an unexpected and attractive late picture."},"met:437097":{"d":"Three huge haystacks, rounded like beehives and the colour of straw, stand in a flat autumn field. A flock of sheep spreads across the stubble in front of them, some grazing, some lying down; a shepherd with a staff stands small in the shadow of the stacks. Low farm roofs and a tree turning orange sit at the right, bare trees at the left, and a heavy blue-grey storm cloud, flecked with small white specks, hangs over the whole scene. The paint is thin and loose: patches of the dark lilac-pink ground colour show through, and the outlines of the stacks and sheep are visible under the colour. Oil on canvas, about 85 by 110 centimetres.","i":"This is the autumn panel from a set of four Seasons commissioned in April 1868 by the Alsatian industrialist Frédéric Hartmann. Millet worked on the set on and off for seven years, finishing this one about 1874, shortly before his death in January 1875. The Met describes the scene: the harvest is in, the gleaners have gone, and the sheep are left to graze; beyond the stacks lie the plain of Chailly and the rooftops of Barbizon, the village where Millet had lived since 1849. Earlier works such as The Gleaners dwell on labour; this late picture turns to the turning of the year itself, with a storm-dark sky promising winter.","c":"This is Millet in a late, looser mood, and the sky wins: the brooding cloud lifts the pale haystacks into a glow, and the sheep, drawn in quick, accurate strokes, give the field a sense of life. The sketch-like finish suits the subject, though some passages, especially the foreground, are thinly handled. Compared with the monumental labourers of The Sower or The Gleaners, there is no single human anchor here. It is a landscape of mood rather than message, quieter than his famous peasants but arguably more modern in its freedom of touch."},"met:438635":{"d":"A small oil sketch, about 27 by 31 centimeters, painted on paper and later laid down on wood, shows a waterfall with trees at Terni, in central Italy. The catalog keywords give just the waterfall and the trees, so the exact arrangement of the cascade, the rocks and the light are left unstated. Corot painted it in 1826, during his first stay in Italy, working outdoors in oil on paper, the method that let him catch the look of a place quickly and directly.","i":"The waterfall near Terni, the Cascata delle Marmore, was created by Roman engineers in 271 BC when they diverted the river Velino, and it had long been a stop for artists and travelers; Byron praised it in Childe Harold. Corot, who spent three years in Italy, made small outdoor studies like this one as personal records, not as pictures for sale; his finished landscapes would be composed later, in the studio, from such sketches.","c":"In sketches like this Corot is at his freshest. The forms are simplified into a few strong masses, the light is broad and clear, and nothing is fussed over; the paper support allows a quick, sure touch. He kept many of these studies in his own studio, and later admirers valued them for their directness. The sketch is modest in size but large in effect, and it shows why Corot's Italian studies are often rated above his finished pictures."},"nga:46483":{"d":"A young boy sits in a relaxed position, his right arm leaning on a black surface. He has pale skin, wide eyes and a small, slightly downturned mouth, and wears a dark wide-brimmed hat, a light jacket over a white shirt, dark trousers and what appear to be wrapped cloth foot coverings. Behind him are a pale greenish wall, a framed picture or window and a block of wood on the floor. Brushstrokes remain visible, especially on the floor and the jacket. Oil on paper mounted on canvas, about 25 by 33 centimeters, 1825 to 1827.","i":"Corot spent the years 1825 to 1828 in Italy, where he made small, quick studies of the landscape and of the people he met. A boy in peasant clothing was a figure the picturesque tradition loved, but Corot's study is less a type than an observation of an individual child in a plain room. The title's word Italian ties the study to his Italian journey, and the picture, like many of his studies, was probably not made for sale.","c":"A direct, unsentimental study: the boy's wide eyes and downturned mouth give him a worried, slightly shy presence, and the visible brushwork keeps the surface alive. The peasant boy was a stock subject of Italian genre painting, and Corot avoids the usual prettiness. It is a small work, made for himself, but it shows the plain honesty of observation that underlies his later landscapes."},"nga:88615":{"d":"A cluster of tan and rust-brown buildings stands on a tiny island at the center of a wide, glassy, brownish-gray river. Most of the buildings are three or four stories high, except for a bell tower that rises a story above the rest, right of center. Dabs of sage green on the island suggest vegetation, and two arched stone bridges link the island to the city on either side. Apart from the blue sky and a band of white clouds, the palette is entirely tan, cream and brown. Oil on paper mounted on canvas, about 27 by 43 centimeters, 1825 to 1828.","i":"The island is the Tiber Island in Rome, linked to the banks by bridges since antiquity, and the church and bell tower of San Bartolomeo stand on it. Corot painted it during his stay in Italy in the late 1820s, when he made oil studies in the open air around Rome, a practice common among French landscape students there. The study was a private record of a place and its light, not a finished exhibition piece.","c":"A study whose subject, a muddy river and a cluster of old walls, is plain, and whose appeal lies in restraint: a handful of earth colors, a measured placement of buildings and a clear sky. Corot catches the balance and the light without a trace of picturesque drama, and the result has the calm exactness that made his Italian studies so influential. It shows an eye already mature around the age of thirty."},"met:435961":{"d":"A vertical portrait, about 38 by 30 centimeters, in oil on canvas, shows Toussaint Lemaistre, whose life dates, 1807 or 1808 to 1888, are given in the title. The catalog keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, pose and clothing are left unstated. Painted in 1833, when Corot was in his thirties, it is a small, informal likeness of the kind the artist made of friends and acquaintances rather than as commissions for the Salon.","i":"Corot is remembered as a landscape painter, but he painted portraits and figure pieces throughout his life, mostly of family, friends and models, and he often kept them. They reveal a more private side of his art. Lemaistre's place in Corot's circle is not described in the catalog; the title preserves the sitter's name and dates, and the portrait is best read as a record of friendship more than of rank or office.","c":"Corot's portraits are plain and sincere, with none of the flattery of fashionable portraitists: the sitter is placed in simple light, the paint is thin and the modeling economical. If this one shares the virtues of his landscapes, as it probably does, it will show tonal harmony and a quiet respect for the subject. Without a fuller description it is hard to compare it with his better-known figure paintings, but it belongs to the intimate side of his work."},"nga:46584":{"d":"A young woman lies on her stomach in the lower left corner, propped on her elbows to read a book beside a stream that winds through a wooded landscape. Towering trees in rich pine green fill most of the canvas, and the olive-colored water has cut high banks of caramel and honey brown, with steel and slate-gray boulders scattered near the river. Hazy blue mountains line the horizon halfway up the picture. The reader wears a white shirt and a rose-pink skirt and is barefoot. Oil on canvas, about 176 by 243 centimeters, dated 1834.","i":"The forest of Fontainebleau, south of Paris, was a favorite subject for French landscape painters in the 1830s, and Corot's large canvas treats it as an idyll rather than a record of place: a small figure absorbed in a book, set within towering trees. At about 1.8 by 2.4 meters it is an ambitious exhibition-scale picture, far larger than the studies for which Corot is best known. The reader suggests leisure and reverie, a Romantic idea of nature as a refuge.","c":"Large and ambitious, but arguably not Corot at his best: the trees are heavy, the greens are close in tone and the composition rests on a formula that does not yet have the lightness and air of his Italian studies. The small reader is charming and gives the forest scale. It remains interesting as evidence of the Salon painter Corot hoped to become before he found his mature, silvery manner."},"nga:52166":{"d":"A sand-colored riverbank stretches toward an arched bridge that spans a pale blue river on the right. The scene is loosely painted, so many details are indistinct: a retaining wall extends from the abutment, a group of people painted in a few strokes of gray, pink, brown and white gathers near the water, and the bridge is reflected in the rippling river. At left, fern-green grasses and trees line the beach-like bank, and ivory buildings with pewter-gray roofs recede into the distance. Oil on paper mounted on canvas, about 25 by 34 centimeters, inscribed Corot 1834.","i":"Mâcon is a town on the Saône in Burgundy, on the old road south from Paris. The picture is an open-air oil study, painted on paper and later mounted on canvas, the kind of rapid sketch Corot made for his own use and kept in the studio. The bridge, the river and the few figures are recorded in their basic masses, with no attempt at picturesque detail.","c":"A small study that does a lot with little: a few touches stand for the people at the water's edge, and the hazy light is caught in pale, closely related tones. Corot's studies of this kind are often more modern in feeling than his Salon pictures, and this one is typical. It is slight but sure, a fine example of how he trained his eye by painting from the motif."},"met:435962":{"d":"A wide, dry upland stretches under a pale sky streaked with thin cloud. Boulders crowd the foreground, with spiky agave and scrub among them; a single tall dark-green tree stands left of center, and a pale cliff and low hills lie further back. Warm yellow-ochre light falls across the ground, with cool shadows beneath the rocks. At the lower right a woman in a dark cloak kneels with both arms raised, looking up, while a small child in a white cloth lies on his back beside her. High at upper left, a tiny winged angel flies down through the sky. Hagar and her son are small in the huge canvas.","i":"The subject is from Genesis. Abraham sends his servant Hagar and her son Ishmael into the desert of Beersheba, and when the water runs out Hagar prays and God sends an angel to guide her to a well. The Metropolitan Museum says Corot composed the arid setting from nature studies made in the French countryside. He showed the picture at the Salon of 1835, where an anonymous critic praised the angel in the vast landscape, the distant hope the despairing mother cannot see, as a noble idea admirably carried out. It was an attempt at a grand historical landscape, made after his years in Italy.","c":"A big picture built to impress a Salon jury. The setting is plausible, the warm light is carefully graded across the ground, and the thought, a mother who cannot see her rescue, is moving. But it feels like a studio construction: Corot's true talent, for quick open-air notation, stays beneath the surface. The figures are small and slightly stiff, and the angel is a minor flicker. The picture points forward to the poetic landscapes he made later, in which he dropped the biblical narrative and let the light and trees speak."},"met:435972":{"d":"A wayside calvary stands on the heights above Honfleur, with the broad Seine estuary stretching beyond it, in a small oil on wood about 30 by 41 centimeters. The museum's keywords record the Notre-Dame-de-Grâce calvary, the cross, Christ, a human figure and the estuary view, and give no more description, so the exact placement of the figure, the weather and the light are left unstated. It was painted about 1830, during a visit to Normandy.","i":"The hillside of Notre-Dame-de-Grâce above Honfleur was a place of pilgrimage for sailors and their families, who prayed there for safe voyages, and a calvary, a monumental cross, marks the spot. A small human figure at the foot of the cross would give the landscape a devotional scale and a note of quiet reverence. Wayside crosses and chapels were part of the French countryside that Corot loved to record.","c":"The subject balances landscape and devotion: a cross is a strong vertical against the soft horizontals of an estuary, and the scene is held together by calm light. Corot shows here his early gift for tonal unity, a skill that would define his mature work. The figure is minor; the real subject is air and distance. It is a small painting with a large sense of space."},"nga:46586":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, a man in a cobalt-blue jacket and soft black hat rides a white horse away from the viewer along a dirt road that winds up the side of a steep, sun-drenched hill. The road runs toward a grove of sage-green trees with brown trunks, in whose shade stands a figure in a brown coat. Boulders and scrubby plants line the rutted path, casting mauve shadows across the ochre ground, while the valley at left sinks into deep shadow. The land fades to steel gray and hazy blue in the distance. Oil on canvas, about 70 by 96 centimeters, signed and dated 1838.","i":"Volterra is a hill town in Tuscany, and Corot's view is composed as an ideal, sunlit landscape, with a lone rider giving scale and a human thread through the road. It is a finished studio picture of 1838, probably built from studies made on the spot in Italy. The rider is anonymous and the picture tells no story: its subject is the effect of strong southern light on rock and path.","c":"One of Corot's most satisfying early landscapes. The strong diagonal of the road, the contrast between the sunlit slope and the shadowed valley, and the cool distant haze set up a spacious, believable world, and the small rider anchors it. The paint is dense and the drawing firm, in contrast to the silvery late style for which he is best known, and it shows how solid the structure was beneath the later softness."},"met:435980":{"d":"A small vertical portrait of a boy, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 32 by 24 centimeters, about 1835. The catalog gives only the keywords boy and portrait, and the sitter is unnamed, so his features, clothing and the background are left unstated. The format and support suggest an intimate, quickly worked likeness, the kind Corot made of family members and friends for his own pleasure, not for exhibition.","i":"In the 1830s the Romantic cult of childhood encouraged portraits that showed children as naturally themselves, plain and direct, not as miniature adults. Corot, who never married, painted children of his family and friends with evident sympathy. Who this boy was, and how he was connected with the painter, is not recorded; the portrait stands as a piece of private affection rather than of social display.","c":"The best of Corot's figure studies treat their sitters with a plain respect that avoids sentimentality, and a child offers a good subject for that: no pose, no props. If this portrait follows his habit, it will rely on tonal softness and economy, with a simple ground against which the face glows. The catalog gives no detail, so the picture is best placed within the quiet, private side of Corot's art."},"met:435985":{"d":"An oil sketch on canvas, about 36 by 50 centimeters, made in 1843 as a preparation for Corot's large biblical landscape The Destruction of Sodom. The catalog keywords list a human figure and a landscape, and no more, so the placement of the figures, the burning city and the lighting are left unstated. As a study it works out the main masses and the mood of the scene, in the loose, simplified way that a painter blocks in a composition before committing to a large canvas.","i":"The story is told in Genesis 19. God destroys the sinful cities of Sodom and Gomorrah with fire from heaven; Lot and his family are told to flee without looking back, and Lot's wife, who turns to look, is changed into a pillar of salt. Landscape painters of Corot's day used biblical and mythological subjects to give a landscape a weighty narrative, and success at the annual Salon often depended on such a historical landscape. Small figures show human insignificance before divine judgment.","c":"A study should be judged by its energy, and studies for Salon pictures often have an energy that the finished work loses. Corot's loose handling and economical massing suggest a drama that a tidier final canvas may tame. The biblical subject was a requirement of the grand landscape of the day; the instinct for tone and atmosphere is what makes the sketch worth attention, whatever the story."},"met:435986":{"d":"A long, narrow panoramic view of the village of Lormes, in the Morvan hills of central France, painted in oil on a canvas only 16 by 55 centimeters. The catalog keywords record only the village and the landscape, so the placement of the houses, the church and the fields, and the weather, are left unstated. The strongly horizontal format is itself the main fact: it turns the view into a band of rolling country, to be read from left to right like a frieze. It dates from the early 1840s.","i":"Corot returned repeatedly to the Morvan, a wooded region of Burgundy, and painted its villages in small studies. A panoramic format of this kind is unusual for him; it may have been a sketch for a larger composition, or a panel cut down for a particular use, though the catalog does not say. Lormes offered the quiet, unpicturesque landscape that he preferred to dramatic scenery.","c":"The extreme format makes a virtue of simplicity: a ribbon of village, trees and sky, with the horizon holding everything in calm balance. Corot's handling is typically concise and the tones close and gentle. Whether finished or not, the picture shows his gift for finding order in modest places. It would be a mistake to look for drama; the pleasure is in the quiet measure of the long view."},"met:438636":{"d":"A cow stands in a barn, in a small oil on paper laid down on canvas, about 21 by 27 centimeters. The museum's keywords record only cattle and cows, so the animal's markings, the stall, the light and any other details are left unstated. The medium suggests a quick study made from life, a rare subject for Corot, whose reputation rests on wooded lakes, Italian hills and misty fields, with cattle at most as tiny accents in the middle distance.","i":"Corot painted for himself as well as for the market, and small studies from life were part of a habit of constant looking. He is reported to have urged students to find the big masses of light and shade first and to leave the details for later. A barn interior, with its dim, enclosed light, is a test of that advice. Whether the study was made for a particular picture is not recorded.","c":"A rare subject and a modest one. Painters of landscape seldom turned to cattle indoors, and the picture offers a glimpse of a more private Corot, curious and unhurried, quite unlike the poet of misty lakes. The dim, enclosed light of a barn is a hard test of tonal control, and tonal control is exactly his gift. Its value lies in directness and in what it says about the range of his attention."},"met:435967":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 92 by 181 centimeters, shows the burning of the biblical city of Sodom. The museum's keywords record a woman, a man and fire, and give no further description, so the arrangement of the figures and the extent of the conflagration are left unstated. The painting was begun in 1843 and reworked in 1857, and its title was changed from The Destruction of Sodom to The Burning of Sodom. It is a biblical landscape of the kind called a historical landscape.","i":"The story (Genesis 19) tells how Lot and his family were warned to flee the doomed cities and not to look back; Lot's wife did look back and was turned into a pillar of salt. Divine punishment by fire, and the fate of the one who turned, were favorites of Romantic painters, who found in catastrophe a way to show the power of nature. Corot's decision to rework the canvas fourteen years after beginning it suggests that he cared about it.","c":"This is Corot attempting the grand manner, and the effort shows. His temperament is not that of the apocalyptic English painter John Martin, and a gift for quiet light and tonal harmony sits uneasily with a subject of fire and flight. Yet the picture is a revealing document of his ambitions, and the landscape, as in his best work, is likely more convincing than the story. It interests more as evidence of the painter than as a vision of wrath."},"nga:46484":{"d":"A young sitter is shown from the chest up, seated and turned slightly to the left. She has pale skin, reddish-brown hair in a low bun, green eyes and small pink lips that turn up slightly at the corners. Her plaid garment, in pink, light blue and brown, has white trim at the neckline and small black buttons. The background is mottled gray touched with light yellow and pale blue. Oil on canvas, about 25 by 21 centimeters, signed Corot and dated 1859 at the lower left.","i":"Corot painted figures throughout his career, often models posed in his studio, and these were mostly kept for himself or friends rather than exhibited. The sitter's identity is unknown; the title calls her only a young girl. Small half-length portraits of this kind show a quieter side of an artist best known for landscapes, and later painters such as Degas admired Corot's figure pictures for their simplicity.","c":"A modest, intimate portrait whose appeal lies in its restraint: soft modeling of the face, a plain gray ground, and the pink, blue and brown plaid that supplies the one lively note. It lacks the grand ambition of his major figure paintings, and the pose is simple, but it has a gentle seriousness that avoids sentimentality. Within Corot's figure work it is a quiet, reliable example."},"nga:1150":{"d":"A person on a dark horse rides across a field at the center of a loosely painted, horizontal landscape, while at left a man works the ground behind a horse-drawn plow. The field is muted green shaded with gold, a grove of fern, olive and dark green trees fills the right third, and a patch of darker green dotted with red and yellow suggests flowers. A band of houses with tan or light yellow walls and gray or terracotta roofs lines the far side of the field, and an arched bridge crosses a ravine. Smoke-gray clouds edge a milk-white sky. Oil on canvas, about 34 by 55 centimeters.","i":"Épernon is a small town southwest of Paris. Corot painted scenes of everyday agricultural work with a quiet, unassertive attitude: the plowman and rider are small and simply stated, and the human presence is part of the landscape's rhythm. The painting, from the period of his silvery late style, offers a view of rural France without anecdote, made for a market that valued landscapes of calm and light.","c":"Loosely handled and low in key, this is a quiet picture in which nothing happens and the whole field breathes. The tiny plowman and rider supply human scale, and the milk-white sky unifies the sparse color. It does not have the lyric intensity of his best late landscapes, and the broad brushwork can seem summary, but it shows how much atmosphere Corot could conjure from a handful of greens and a pale sky."},"nga:195865":{"d":"A lush landscape of olive-green forest lines a glimmering, silvery-blue lake, with hills dissolving in the deep distance under a pale sky. In the lower center a man in a red cap and white shirt sits in a long, low boat with a pointed bow, moored in the grasses along the shore. Gnarled trunks frame the view, leafy trees fill the right half, and across the still water a city appears almost in silhouette, reflected below. The brushwork is partly visible, partly blended, giving a hazy, soft effect. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 90 centimeters, inscribed with the date January 1866, although the museum dates it to 1861.","i":"The title calls it a souvenir of an Italian lake, and that is the key: Corot made many such pictures in his studio late in life, recalling Italian scenes in a poetic and softened form rather than recording a particular place. A boatman resting at the shore is a quiet touch of human presence, a figure of rest in a world of silvery reverie. Such late souvenirs were popular with collectors, who valued their atmosphere over topography.","c":"A characteristic late Corot, in which the specific place has been dissolved into a gentle mood. The framing trunks, the pale water and the silhouetted town hold together in a few close tones, and the red cap is the small bright accent that keeps the whole alive. Admirers find in such pictures a lyric peak; skeptics see a formula repeated too often. Both views are partly justified."},"met:435987":{"d":"A narrow village street, with houses and a woman, is shown in a small vertical oil on canvas, about 34 by 24 centimeters. Dardagny is a village in the Swiss canton of Geneva, and the museum records the date only as 1852, 1857 or 1863. Its keywords list the houses, the street, the village and the woman, so the position of the figure, the light and the colors are left unstated. The tall, narrow format suits a view down a street.","i":"Corot, a Parisian, traveled widely, to Italy, Switzerland, Belgium and Holland as well as through France, often as a guest in the country houses of friends, and painted what he found nearby. A village street with a single figure was typical of the unshowy subjects he favored: a corner where light, old walls and daily life meet. Such small canvases were records of places he had looked at long and fondly.","c":"A modest, intimate picture that depends on structure. The tall format stresses the receding street and the stepped masses of the houses, and a single figure supplies scale. Corot's concern for tonal values, the relations of light and dark, binds such a scene together, and the effect is of a spot long looked at. It is not among his famous pictures, but it shows the method that made them."},"met:437991":{"d":"A vertical portrait, about 41 by 29 centimeters, shows a girl; the museum's keyword is simply girl, and the title tells that she is curious, so her pose, dress and background are left unstated. It is an oil on cardboard laid down on wood, painted between 1860 and 1864, when Corot, in his sixties, was increasingly painting figures posed by models in his studio, and not only the landscapes on which his fame rested.","i":"The title gives the picture a small narrative: a child is caught in a moment of inquisitiveness, looking at something or someone. Corot's figure pieces of these years carry titles that name moods, among them Reverie, The Letter and this one, and they hover between portrait and genre scene. The sitter was probably a model, and the picture is more an observation of childhood than a likeness of a named child.","c":"Corot's pictures of children avoid the cuteness that mars much nineteenth-century painting of childhood: the sitter is allowed her own attention and her own reserve. The cardboard support suggests rapid, unlabored execution, and the best of such pieces keep the immediacy of a glance. The catalog gives no detail, but the title promises what Corot did best, a quick, truthful observation without sentiment."},"met:435963":{"d":"A nude woman with long brown hair, falling to her waist, lies at rest in the guise of a bacchante, a female follower of the wine god Bacchus. The title places her by the sea. The picture is an oil on a horizontal panel, about 39 by 59 centimeters, dated 1865, and the catalog gives no further detail, so her exact pose, her attributes, such as a tambourine, vine leaves or a leopard skin, and the shore are left unstated.","i":"In Greek myth the bacchantes, or maenads, were the ecstatic followers of Dionysus, known to the Romans as Bacchus, who danced, drank and roamed the wilds. In later art a reclining bacchante was a respectable pretext for the female nude, since the classical title lifted the picture above everyday life. Corot took up nymphs and bacchantes in his later years, and the sea setting recalls his Italian memories. The mood is calm and dreamy, not frenzied.","c":"Corot's bacchantes are among the gentlest in art: there is no frenzy, only a languid classical idyll in soft modeling and silvery harmony. The nude reads as a studio model more than an ideal goddess, and figure drawing was never his strongest suit. The strength lies in the setting, the tonal calm, and the way the figure sits comfortably in the air around her. Pleasant rather than powerful, it shows the quiet late inclinations of a painter nearly seventy."},"met:435971":{"d":"A girl weaves a garland on this vertical canvas, about 42 by 30 centimeters, painted between 1860 and 1865. The museum's keywords record only a girl and a garland, so her costume, her pose, the flowers she uses and the setting are left unstated. The subject belongs to the group of figure pieces that Corot painted in the 1860s alongside his landscapes, usually from a model posed in his studio, and quietly sentimental in mood.","i":"Garland-making is an old pastoral motif, linked to spring, festivals and courtship, and in the nineteenth century it also stood for rustic innocence. Corot painted such subjects in years when his reputation was at its height, and collectors prized them for the poetry of country life without its hardship. The model was probably a studio sitter in costume, not a real peasant girl.","c":"The subject is conventional, but Corot's treatment avoids its saccharine edges. Soft, close values, a muted palette and a plain pose lend the girl a quiet concentration, and the repeated gesture of weaving gives the picture a gentle rhythm. It lacks the monumental simplicity of his best figure paintings, and the idyllic theme is a little stock, but it is a charming example of the type."},"met:435983":{"d":"A river runs through a rural landscape toward a distant tower, in an oil on canvas about 55 by 78 centimeters, dated 1865. The museum's keywords record the river, the tower, trees, a shore, a boat and a human figure, so those are the elements, and the arrangement, light and color are left unstated. It is a late landscape of the kind Corot called a souvenir: a place composed from memory and imagination, not a record of a particular site.","i":"In his sixties Corot's landscapes grew dreamlike: feathery trees, silvery light, and boats with small figures on still water. They were poetic recollections and inventions, and he gave them evocative titles. Buyers loved their gentle melancholy, and the demand was so great that forgeries became a famous problem. The distant tower is a typical accent that gives the eye a place to rest and suggests a human presence beyond the shore.","c":"At their best the late landscapes are among the most beautiful pictures of the century, with a tender unity of tone that gives air and distance. At their worst they repeat a formula that Corot and his many imitators could turn out on demand. This one is a representative piece of the type: balanced, poetic and rather quiet, and likely to reward the slow look that its subject invites."},"nga:30230":{"d":"A woman kneels near the lower left to help a baby take a step toward a young girl, who approaches with something gathered in her apron, while a boy climbs a nearby tree. A fifth figure, visible only on close inspection, stands bending over the stream in the distance. The forest fills the composition in tones of green, from moss and pine to sage, with thin, willowy trunks and a stream running in from the lower right; even the reflections are green. Blended brushwork gives a soft, hazy glow, with only a few bright spots of sky. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 82 centimeters, 1860 to 1865.","i":"The title points to a rural task, gathering eels in a stream, but the figures are small and the work is not the point; the picture belongs to Corot's late woodland scenes, in which families and children are placed in a dreamlike forest, closer to the poetry of memory than to documentary. Such scenes appealed to collectors who liked their landscapes tender and unspecific. Nothing in the picture fixes a place or a date.","c":"Pure Corot of the 1860s: the whole forest is in a green haze, the figures are at home in it, and the delicate vertical trunks give a soft rhythm. The scene is somewhat sentimental, and the uniform green is a risk, but the handling is so light that the sentiment is largely held in check. It is a picture of mood and atmosphere, and it was this vein that made him a model for the Impressionists."},"nga:43430":{"d":"From a grassy bank dotted with flower-like touches of yellow, blue and white, the view crosses a placid pond to a cluster of cream-white buildings on the far side. Two trees with ash-brown trunks and cloud-like canopies of pale sage green stand near the water. A woman in a straw-yellow cap and apron, a bundle on her back, stands to the left, and a second person in a yellow cap and blue shirt sits with his back to the viewer. The gabled villa and the trees are reflected in the pale water. Oil on canvas, about 49 by 66 centimeters, about 1865.","i":"Ville-d'Avray, a village west of Paris with ponds and woods, was the site of a house owned by Corot's family, and he painted its pond many times over his career. The motif, quiet water, a few trees and a handful of figures at ease, is a French version of the poetic landscape and one that Corot returned to in his late years. The painting is not a portrait of the place but a simplification toward harmony and calm.","c":"One of the most poised of Corot's late landscapes: pale water, trees like soft clouds, and a few figures establishing scale and a hint of rural life. The paint is blended so that every part of the scene belongs to one atmosphere. There is a risk of formula, since the motif recurs so often, but the balance here is exact, and the silvery calm explains why the Impressionists admired him."},"nga:46585":{"d":"A band of sun-dappled boulders arcs shallowly across the canvas, surrounded by dense green vegetation. Several slender tree trunks stand just left of center in front of the rocks, and beyond them the forest is a haze of loosely painted moss and pine green. The trunks and boulders are painted in tones of sable, sand and dark brown. The view is close and enclosed, and the scene is a quiet study of wood and stone. Oil on canvas, about 46 by 59 centimeters, 1860 to 1865, signed COROT.","i":"The forest of Fontainebleau, with its fields of sandstone boulders, was the training ground of the Barbizon painters, and Corot worked there from the 1820s onward. A close view of rocks and trunks without a path or figure is a less common subject for him than the woodland clearing, and it shows his interest in the structure of the place. Painters of the 1860s found in the boulders a subject that was both natural and quietly monumental.","c":"A close, unpicturesque scene in which the arcing boulders supply a structure and the dappled light enlivens a limited palette of browns and greens. It has the directness of a study more than the finish of an exhibited picture, and the dense vegetation can feel congested. Still, it shows an honest feeling for the forest's weight and moisture, and it is a fine example of Corot's work in the woods."},"nga:46583":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a woman stands facing the viewer while turning her head to look off to the right. Her face is thin and pale, with hooded, deeply shadowed eyes and hollows beneath high cheekbones, and her black hair is plaited and tied up under brown bands. She wears earrings, pearls and three red necklaces, a garnet-red bodice cut low over a white undergarment, sapphire-blue sleeves and an apron printed with deep red flowers. One hand holds a shimmering object, the other rests on a stone wall. White houses cluster on a rocky hill behind her. Oil on canvas, about 132 by 98 centimeters, 1866.","i":"Agostina was an Italian model, and Corot painted her in a costume of Italian type that gave a Paris studio an Italian flavor. The picture belongs to the large figure paintings of his last decade, in which models in fancy dress, often Italian, are shown with the dignity of portraits. The shimmering object in her hand and the red necklaces add color accents, but the picture is not a story: it is a study of presence.","c":"A strong, grave figure painting: the thin face with its shadowed eyes, the heavy red and blue costume and the stony wall give the sitter weight, and the gray sky quiets the color. The pose is simple, and the setting with its Italianate village is a studio fiction, yet the picture has a gravity that few of his imaginary costume pieces reach. Corot's figure paintings were admired by Degas and others, and this is among the finest of them."},"met:435981":{"d":"A woman lost in thought is the subject, as the title and the museum's keywords contemplation and woman indicate, in an oil on a wooden panel about 50 by 37 centimeters, painted in the 1860s or a little earlier. Her pose, costume and surroundings are not described, so they are left unstated. The picture belongs to the figure pieces that Corot painted from studio models in his later years, which hover between portrait and fantasy.","i":"A reverie was a fashionable subject in the 1860s: a woman, usually alone, her gaze unfocused, letting the viewer imagine her thoughts. It suited an age that valued sentiment and private feeling, and it avoided the need for any story. Corot gave such pictures mood rather than anecdote, and many later painters admired them for their reticence. The sitter is a model, not a recorded individual.","c":"Reverie is the kind of picture where mood is the whole subject, and Corot's tonal softness serves it well. The risk is vagueness: a pensive woman can easily become an empty formula. Corot saves most such pictures by simplicity, by the quiet geometry of figure and ground, and by a refusal of anecdote. How well this one holds that balance depends on the surface, but the type is among his most touching."},"nga:1148":{"d":"A young woman sits with her back to the viewer in a ladder-back chair in a studio, looking at a painting on an easel. Her brown hair is twisted into a bun tied with a raspberry-red ribbon, a cream chemise slips off one shoulder under a grass-green bodice, and she holds a mandolin against her olive-green skirt while touching the corner of the canvas. On the easel is a loosely painted landscape with figures. A white and black dog looks up at her, a stove stands at left, and small paintings and casts line the wall. Oil on wood, about 62 by 40 centimeters, about 1868.","i":"Corot painted a group of studio interiors with a woman before an easel in the late 1860s, a theme that turns the painter's private workroom into a subject. The model, in costume with a mandolin, looks at a landscape of the kind Corot himself made, so the picture quietly joins his two specialties, the figure and the landscape. It reads as an homage to painting, made in the intimacy of the studio.","c":"An intimate, tender picture in which the studio's clutter, the dog, the stove and the plaster casts, is as much the subject as the model. The warm, close values and the loose brush give it a conversational ease, and the sitter's absorbed back is a daring, unflattering choice. It lacks the polish of an exhibition picture, and that is its charm: a glimpse of the painter's world."},"nga:66407":{"d":"A woman sits at a table in a brightly lit room, leaning over an open book with her elbow on the table and her cheek on her fist. Her wavy chestnut hair is parted in the center, and she wears a white shirt under a fern-green jacket whose sleeve has slipped off her shoulder, with a pale peach skirt striped in plum-purple. Swipes of golden yellow, caramel and dark brown stand for the walls and works of art behind her; a landscape on an easel is at first glance mistaken for a window. Oil on paperboard mounted on wood, about 33 by 41 centimeters, about 1868.","i":"In the late 1860s Corot made a series of small pictures of women reading, musing or playing music in his studio, in which the model's absorption is the subject. A reader is a familiar figure of private reverie, and here the easel in the background reminds the viewer that this is the painter's own world. These small pictures are among the more private pieces of his late career and show a loosened, intimate manner quite different from his public landscapes.","c":"A relaxed, rapid picture that catches the posture of absorbed reading with a few warm strokes. The slipped sleeve and the propped cheek give the sitter an unposed naturalness, and the warm browns and yellows glow against the cool green jacket. It is a sketch-like work and does not aim to rival the larger figure pictures, but its freshness is the reason some viewers prefer it."},"met:435968":{"d":"A road and trees in the countryside around Paris, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 34 by 51 centimeters in the 1860s. The museum's keywords record trees, a road and a landscape, so the position of the trees, the weather, the light and any figures are left unstated. The panel format and modest size suggest an intimate study of the kind Corot made close to home, in the villages and woods just outside the capital.","i":"The outskirts of Paris, Ville-d'Avray above all, were where Corot spent much of his later life, and he found poetry in a lane and a few trees. The growth of the railways and the suburbs was changing the landscape fast in these years, but he mostly ignored industry and kept to quiet, rural-seeming corners. Pictures like this one are quiet records of the pre-industrial edge of the city, seen with tenderness.","c":"Corot's gift for finding grandeur in the commonplace is on display in a subject with no apparent drama: a road, a few trees, the sky. The panel allows a close, smooth paint surface and a clear arrangement of masses, so that the picture reads as a calm tonal harmony. It lacks the visual excitement of the Impressionist landscapes then being painted, but offers an alternative: patient, gradual looking."},"met:435977":{"d":"A mother nurses her infant in this small vertical oil on wood, about 32 by 23 centimeters, probably of the 1860s. The museum's keywords record breastfeeding, a mother, a woman and an infant, so her dress, the setting and the light are left unstated. The subject echoes the old image of the Virgin nursing the Christ Child, but nothing in the record suggests a religious meaning; this is a secular scene of motherhood.","i":"In the nineteenth century, writers such as Rousseau had made maternal nursing a moral ideal, and images of mothers feeding their own babies carried a message of natural virtue and domestic harmony. Corot's version is private and unemphatic. The sitter was probably a studio model, and the picture is best seen as a quiet tribute to the bond between mother and child, resonant with older religious art but without its symbols.","c":"A tender subject that Corot treats without sentiment. The small, close format brings the viewer near, and simplified forms and soft tones give the group a quiet gravity; the echo of Renaissance Madonnas is audible but not insistent. Because the catalog is silent on details, the picture is best seen as one of the gentlest of his late figure pieces, a work of attention more than invention."},"met:435965":{"d":"A nude woman in the guise of a bacchante is set in a landscape on a wide canvas, about 31 by 62 centimeters, painted between 1865 and 1870. The museum's keywords record only a woman, a nude and a landscape, so her pose, her attributes and the details of the setting are left unstated. In format and mood it belongs with Corot's late classical idylls, in which a model from his studio is placed among woods and water as a figure of myth.","i":"Bacchantes were the followers of Bacchus, god of wine and ecstasy, and in earlier art they dance wildly or sleep after revel. In the nineteenth century the classical title allowed a painter to show a nude with academic approval, lifting the picture above everyday life. Corot's late nudes were bought mostly by private collectors who valued their soft, poetic mood, and they have often seemed to later viewers more like quiet reveries than revels.","c":"Wider than it is tall, the canvas gives the figure air, and Corot's habitual silvery tones suggest that the nude is part of the landscape rather than posed in front of it. The drawing of the body is generalized and the modeling soft, which may disappoint those who expect classical definition. The attraction is the harmony of figure and nature, a quiet success of tone more than of anatomy."},"met:435966":{"d":"A boatman and his boat are set among reeds and trees in a landscape, on a canvas about 60 by 81 centimeters, painted about 1865. The museum's keywords list a boat, a tree and a landscape; the figure is named in the title, so the reeds, the water, the light and the sky are left unstated. The scale and subject place it among Corot's late landscapes, in which a tiny figure and a boat on still water set the mood of the whole.","i":"A small boat with a single figure appears again and again in Corot's late landscapes. It gives the scene scale, stillness and a hint of narrative, and it recalls both the harbor scenes of Claude Lorrain, whom Corot admired, and the ferries of Dutch landscape. The reeds and the veiled light link the picture to the ponds near his family's house at Ville-d'Avray, though the title names no place.","c":"The late manner at its most seductive: the boatman is a vertical accent against horizontals of water and reeds, and the soft grays and greens are brought into an almost musical harmony. The risk, as always with Corot's late work, is that charm can slide into formula, but the open, light handling and the quietness of the figure keep this on the right side. It is a picture to sit with, not to be dazzled by."},"met:435969":{"d":"A ferryman and his boat are set in a landscape with trees, on a vertical canvas about 66 by 49 centimeters, painted about 1865. The museum's keywords record only a boat and trees, so the passengers, the water, the shore and the light are left unstated. The tall format is less common for a river scene, and it gives the trees a framing role, in the manner of Corot's late wooded landscapes.","i":"The ferryman is an old figure of passage, and Charon, who ferries the dead across the river Styx in ancient myth, hovers behind the motif. Corot's version is most likely a rural boatman and not a mythic one, but the subject lends the landscape a quiet overtone of crossing and of time passing. Late landscapes of this kind were Corot's most popular production, and he painted variations on them for years.","c":"A good example of why Corot's late pictures won such an audience. The ferryman and his boat are a still point in a tremulous veil of foliage and light, and the tall format lets the trees rise like columns around the scene. The trade-off is a certain sweetness, which some later viewers found too soft. Within its type it is among the more convincing: poised, quiet and well constructed."},"met:435976":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this vertical oil on wood, about 55 by 36 centimeters, painted about 1865. The museum's keyword says only woman; the title tells the story, a letter, and the rest, her pose, costume, interior and the letter's whereabouts, are left unstated. It belongs to the group of studio figure pieces Corot made in the 1860s, which pose a single woman in a quiet moment and give her an inward expression.","i":"A woman holding, reading or writing a letter is a theme that runs from Vermeer through the nineteenth century, a sign of private feeling and absent loved ones. The viewer is invited to imagine the contents. Corot gives the idea no plot: the picture is a study of attention and mood. The woman is a model, and the painting a poetic variation on a long-standing subject.","c":"Corot's women of the 1860s are plain, absorbed and unglamorous, and The Letter belongs to their quiet company. The subject asks for narrative, but he refuses it, offering instead soft light, a simplified pose and tender tonal gradations. The best of these figure pieces were admired by later painters for their economy; this one is a representative, appealing member of the group."},"met:435978":{"d":"A woman posed as the Muse of History is the subject of this vertical canvas, about 46 by 35 centimeters, painted about 1865. The museum's keywords record a Muse and a woman, and classify it as an allegory, so her attributes, such as a scroll, a book or a trumpet, and her setting are left unstated. It is one of Corot's late studio figure pieces, in which a model is given a classical role.","i":"In Greek myth the nine Muses, daughters of Zeus and Memory, presided over the arts and sciences, and Clio was the Muse of history, usually given a scroll or book and sometimes a trumpet to proclaim fame. Nineteenth-century painters used the Muses as ready-made allegories for libraries, theaters and public buildings. Corot treats the idea intimately, with a studio model standing for the whole tradition of learning.","c":"Allegory was not Corot's natural territory, and the subject might have turned stiff in less sympathetic hands. Instead the figure is treated with the same quiet attention as his portrait-like studies of women, so the Muse seems like a thoughtful person, not a monument. The result is modest and human, if not grand. It is more successful as a figure study than as an allegory of history."},"met:435988":{"d":"A woman in a woodland setting at Ville-d'Avray, the village west of Paris where Corot's family had a house, is the subject of this oil on canvas, about 55 by 80 centimeters, dated 1870. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a forest, so her activity, the arrangement of the trees, any pond and the light are left unstated. Ville-d'Avray had been one of Corot's subjects since the 1820s, and the picture comes from late in his life.","i":"Corot's family bought a house at Ville-d'Avray in the early nineteenth century, and he painted its ponds and woods for the rest of his life. In his late woodland scenes a single figure, often a woman, gives scale and a human note: a gatherer, a stroller or a model in country dress. The village lay just beyond Paris, and his pictures of it show a nature that Parisians could reach in an afternoon, softened into a timeless pastoral.","c":"By 1870 Corot's woodland manner was so settled that it could shade into formula, and what keeps these pictures alive is the delicacy of touch: feathery foliage dissolving into pale sky, close tones, and quiet. That a man in his seventies could still make a thicket of trees feel fresh is the real achievement here. The picture lacks the vigor of his early studies, but gains a calm that explains his late popularity."},"met:435989":{"d":"A stretch of the Seine at Conflans-Sainte-Honorine, a river town northwest of Paris near the meeting of the Seine and the Oise, with trees, a man and the river, painted in oil on canvas about 46 by 56 centimeters, about 1865 to 1870. The museum's keywords record the Seine, Conflans, a tree, a man, a river and a forest, so the man's activity, the position of the water and the light are left unstated.","i":"Conflans was a working river port, crowded with barges, and its patron saint, Honorine, was the saint of boatmen. Corot, however, gives such a place a rural stillness: trees, a figure at the bank and a calm river take the place of cargo and bustle. The choice sets him apart from the Impressionists, who painted the Seine's boats, bridges and bathing places with more attention to modern life, and shows how far he preferred poetry to reportage.","c":"A modest, pleasing river view that shows Corot's late discipline: a few large tonal masses, a muted harmony and a clear horizontal balance. The man at the bank is an accent, not a story. It is neither the most ambitious nor the most characteristic of his river pictures, but it carries the gentle persuasion of a place observed without drama, and it wears its age well."},"met:435991":{"d":"A young woman sits on a grassy bank, leaning forward with her chin on one hand as she reads an open book in her lap. Her dark hair is pinned up and bound with a band of gold and brown. She wears a white chemise that slips from one shoulder, blue sleeves, a red ribbon at the shoulder and a long skirt of pale gold fabric that spreads across the lower half of the picture. Behind her, a stretch of calm water reaches a low horizon, and a tiny figure sits in a boat at the left. The sky is pale blue with soft clouds. The brushwork is feathery, and the colours are held in silvery greens, blues and creams.","i":"Corot, then seventy-two and famous for landscapes, showed this picture at the Salon of 1869 under the title Femme lisant. It is the only one of his many pictures of solitary, pensive women that he exhibited in his lifetime. Théophile Gautier praised its sincerity and palette but found the figure's drawing clumsy. After the Salon Corot reworked the landscape but, ignoring the criticism, left the figure alone, which probably accounts for the Met's date of 1869 and 1870. The painting came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1928 as a gift from Louise Senff Cameron in memory of her uncle Charles H. Senff.","c":"A quiet picture that earns its charm through tone rather than drawing. The greys and blues of sky and water echo the blue sleeve, and everything is softened into a pearly haze. Gautier's complaint was fair: the arm and shoulder are loosely built, and the skirt is a large, shapeless mass that drags the picture downward. Yet the book and the lowered face carry the feeling so well that the flaws hardly matter. It shows how Corot made figure paintings that behave like landscapes, with the woman as one more note in the light."},"nga:168845":{"d":"A nude young woman reclines against a grassy mound in a sunny field, close to the viewer, her head at left. Her body is angled away, but she looks back over her shoulder with large brown eyes, one arm draped over her hip and her fingers brushing the rim of an iron-gray dish. Her hair is swept up under a leafy wreath, and she lies on a caramel-brown cloth. The olive-green field is speckled with small flowers; four shadowy figures cluster at the right, two lifting a gleaming cup or jug. Oil on canvas, about 58 by 102 centimeters, dated 1860 and reworked about 1865 to 1870.","i":"The nude in a pastoral landscape descends from Renaissance and Baroque images of nymphs and Arcadian reverie, and the wreath and the distant figures with a cup suggest an unspecific classical setting of rest and feasting rather than a named myth. Corot painted such figures in his later years, giving his classical landscapes a human and sensuous presence. The picture is dated 1860 but was reworked years later, so its surface may combine two moments of work.","c":"A gentle, classical reverie that avoids the sharpness of academic nudes: the body is simplified, the flesh is warm against the green, and the haze of the landscape softens the whole. The figure is more presence than study of anatomy, and the pose is a little languid, but the harmony between the nude and the field is persuasive. It is one of the few works in which Corot's two themes, the figure and the landscape, merge entirely."},"nga:176397":{"d":"Three women in togas of rose pink, shimmering yellow and emerald green link hands in a circle under a grove at the right, while a fourth, in sapphire blue, runs toward them with outstretched arms and a fifth, in a scarlet skirt, reclines near the trunks. The figures are tiny against two massive trees whose feathery fern and moss green leaves fill the right half of the canvas. A low box stands on the grass, the lake glows in pale apricot and shell pink, and a large white building sits high on a distant plum and gold hill. Oil on canvas, about 56 by 80 centimeters, 1865 to 1870.","i":"Dancing nymphs under trees were a late specialty of Corot, whose Arcadian scenes drew on Claude and the pastoral tradition together with the memory of Italian lakes. The classical dress and the box that may be an altar or a table suggest a rustic ritual or festival, but no myth is named, and the picture should be read as a poetic evocation of a golden age. The hazy light and loose brushwork turn the scene into a mood of reverie.","c":"The best of Corot's late dance pictures have a lilting rhythm, and this one has it: the circle of dancers answers the curve of the trees, and the pale lake keeps the scene airy. The figures are generic and the sentiment verges on the saccharine, yet the handling is so feathery that the whole is convincing as a daydream. It was this vein that made him beloved to collectors, and it still charms more than it persuades."},"nga:195860":{"d":"A large tree stands at the left of an outdoor scene with a low horizon, where a few buildings and what look like boats float on blue water at the right. Closer to the viewer, several people walk along a path toward the front of the picture: a woman in a brown dress with a yellow sash carries a basket on her head, and two adults, one in blue and one draped in a red cloak, are accompanied by children. The sky is light blue with fluffy white clouds, and greens, browns and blues dominate. Oil on canvas, about 66 by 81 centimeters, dated 1864 and reworked slightly later.","i":"Terracina is a coastal town between Rome and Naples that French painters visited on their Italian journeys, and Corot's title calls the picture a souvenir: not a view made on the spot but a memory, softened and arranged in the studio years afterward. Women carrying baskets on their heads and families on a path were stock figures of Italian rural life in the eyes of foreign painters, who often idealized them. The picture is a poetic reminiscence of Italy, not a record.","c":"A late souvenir, with the strengths and limits of the type: the big tree frames a calm vista, the figures supply gentle movement, and the blues and greens harmonize without strain. The scene is generic enough that it could be almost any coast, which is both its poetry and its limitation. A pleasing, representative late Corot, rather than a striking one."},"nga:41578":{"d":"Shown from the shins up, a woman with medium-toned skin plays a mandolin, her body facing the viewer while her head tips to the left and her dark eyes look down. Brown hair falls loosely around her shoulders under a pumpkin-orange head cloth, with glints at her ears suggesting earrings. She wears a beige jacket with puffy sleeves over a shell-pink shirt with a high ruffled collar, a rose-pink sash and a long beige skirt. The background is dabs of earth green, golden brown and muted blue and white. Broad brushwork shows in the clothing and ground. Oil on canvas, about 64 by 51 centimeters, about 1870.","i":"Corot painted several models in Italian or gypsy costume with mandolins and guitars in his last years, drawing on the Romantic fascination with musicians and wanderers. The title uses the nineteenth-century word gypsy, a term many Romani people regard as pejorative, and it reflects a stock image of exotic freedom that the studio costume stands for. As in his other late figure pieces, the music is a means of capturing a mood of reverie, not a story about a particular person.","c":"A loose, atmospheric figure piece in which the orange head cloth, the pink sash and the earthy ground are held in a soft harmony, and the lowered gaze gives the sitter inwardness. The mandolin is more symbol than instrument, and the costume is a studio fiction. Compared with the more finished Agostina it is freer and more intimate, and its broad brushwork anticipates the openness later painters found in Corot's figures."},"met:435964":{"d":"A woodland landscape with figures, titled in French Les Bohémiens, a nineteenth-century name for wandering people then commonly called gypsies and now generally called Roma, is painted in oil on canvas about 55 by 80 centimeters and dated 1872. The museum's keywords record only trees and a forest, so the number of figures, their activity and the light are left unstated. The year is three before Corot's death in 1875.","i":"Wandering bohémiens were a Romantic motif: free, picturesque and outside the settled order of society, they were set by painters at the edges of woods and roads. The view was exotic and flattering to the viewer, and overlooked the real hardship and prejudice that traveling people faced. Corot's figures are small and dreamy, more a part of the poetry of the forest than a social comment, and the picture belongs to his late habit of peopling woods with costumed figures.","c":"In his last years Corot brought the woodland idyll to its simplest: tall trees, soft light and little figures. The travelers lend color and a faint narrative, but the real subject is the veil of foliage. It reads as a late, benevolent summing-up, lacking the energy of his early open-air work but gaining a calm ease. A deeply typical, accomplished, if rather sweet, example."},"met:435979":{"d":"A pond in Picardy, the region of northern France around Amiens, with trees and a woman, painted in oil on canvas about 43 by 64 centimeters, about 1867. The museum's keywords record trees, a woman and an artificial pond, so her pose, the shape of the pond and the light are left unstated. The word artificial points to a pond made by people, for fish, a mill or an estate, rather than a natural one.","i":"Water was a favorite subject of Corot's late years: a pond is a mirror in which trees and sky repeat themselves, and its stillness suits his soft, silvery harmonies. Picardy was a region he knew from travels in northern France. A woman at the water's edge adds scale and a human note. The title's specific region contrasts with the generic woodland titles of many late pictures, and may record an actual place.","c":"Corot's ponds are among the most beautiful things in his late work, and the formula, dark trees, reflective water and pale sky, is flexible enough to carry great delicacy. This one is a good, mid-range example: poised, harmonious and calming, though not startling. The woman at the water's edge is little more than a note of color, but she gives the scene a human measure."},"nga:1149":{"d":"A person on a white horse rides away from the viewer along a narrow dirt path that curves through a forest of golden yellow trees. The path begins near the lower right corner and bends back to the right, passing a backlit boulder just left of the lower center. The rider wears dark brown trousers, a hat and a lighter brown shirt. Trees tower on either side of the bend, almost reaching the top edge, and a pearly white sky shows between them, with ivory clouds below an icy blue. Soft, blended strokes give the surface a hazy look. Oil on canvas, about 96 by 78 centimeters, dated 1872.","i":"Corot painted this vertical forest scene near the end of his life, in 1872, when he was in his mid-seventies and his woodland pictures had become dreams of light and air. Coubron is a village east of Paris with woods that were still a short ride from the city. The golden foliage suggests autumn, and the lone rider on a path is a motif of quiet movement through nature that Corot used repeatedly in his late years.","c":"A late work of great softness, in which the tall trees form a natural cathedral and the lone rider gives a human measure. The golden foliage against the pearly sky is the picture's special beauty, and the blended strokes keep it airy. As ever with late Corot, the line between lyric and formula is thin, but this vertical format with its curving path is among the more fully realized."},"nga:35086":{"d":"A wide, cloudy blue sky stretches over a riverbank. Tall, dark green trees rise on the right to the top of the panel, while at left the river recedes into the distance beside a grassy foreground. A woman in a white head cloth and a dark pink skirt stands facing the viewer beside a dirt path framed by tall grass and pale flowers, painted in loose strokes. On either side of her are two shapes that could be cows, one dark brown and one light brown with white patches, facing the water. Oil on wood, about 32 by 41 centimeters, 1868 to 1872.","i":"River scenes with a woman and cattle at the water's edge are among Corot's late specialties, in which the figures are small, the colors earthy and the landscape veiled in haze. The title gives no place name, so the river is not identified; Corot often kept locations unspecific in these years, preferring a general mood. The woman and animals suggest rural labor, but nothing makes them a narrative.","c":"Small and informal, the panel has the feathery air of Corot's late sketches, with the pink skirt a bright note against greens and browns. The cows are summary shapes and the woman is hardly more than a few strokes, which suits the haze; the picture is about weather and place more than about people. A slight but charming example of the late manner."},"nga:42932":{"d":"A woman sits with her body facing left and her head turned toward the viewer, one arm resting on a wooden surface at the right. Dark hair is partly covered by a bright yellow scarf; her eyes are deep-set, her brows pale and her lips thin, in a neutral expression. She wears a white blouse with wide sleeves under a black bodice with a wide red neckline, and a black skirt with a colorful apron or blanket in red, tan and green. Large silver hoop earrings, several silver necklaces and a silver ring complete the costume. Oil on canvas, about 65 by 55 centimeters, about 1872.","i":"The costume is the dress of the Ciociaria, a region in the hills south of Rome, whose women's colorful peasant clothes made them favorite models for artists in Rome and Paris. Corot painted such Italian models in the studio in his late years, treating them with the gravity of portraits. The name in parentheses, La Morieri, is presumably that of the model, though nothing confirms it, and the title's word Italian marks her as a type as well as a person.","c":"A restrained, dignified figure study in which the black bodice, the red neckline and the silver jewelry give the sitter a sculptural presence, and the neutral expression refuses sentimentality. The muted green and brown ground lets the face and the yellow scarf carry the picture. It is among the more convincing of Corot's costume pieces, because the sitter appears a person first and a costume second."},"nga:52167":{"d":"A woman and a child stand in a wooded area where tall trees frame a dirt path and a hint of blue sky. The woman, her dark hair tied back and adorned with flowers, holds a closed parasol in one hand and reaches down toward the child with the other. She wears a blue dress with black lace and a light pink flower or brooch at the neckline. The child has curly blond hair tied back with a ribbon and wears a white dress with a red sash, striped stockings and black shoes. Oil on canvas, about 105 by 74 centimeters, dated 1872.","i":"The title names a mother and her daughter, Madame Stumpf and her child, so this is a commissioned or friendly portrait set out of doors, in the manner of Corot's late works that join figure and landscape. The parasol and the woodland path suggest a stroll, and the reaching gesture binds the pair in a moment of ordinary affection. Little is known of the sitters beyond the title.","c":"Corot's late portraits are rare and unforced, and this double portrait has an informal tenderness: the reaching gesture, the child's bright sash and the dappled woodland create an intimate scene. The faces are summary, and the figures sit slightly uneasily in the landscape, as if added to it, but the affection is real. It shows the gentle hand of his woodland dreams applied to modern sitters."},"nga:52261":{"d":"A moss-green slope rising to a tree-topped bluff follows the curve of a sandy beach around a pale blue cove. The hill and olive-green trees fill most of the height of the left side; the beach curves from the lower right around the cliff face, and a strip of pale brown across the horizon, halfway up the canvas, is the beach or a sandbar. A battleship-gray slope suggests distant hills, and the slate-blue sky is layered with swirling oyster-white and pewter-gray clouds. Oil on canvas, only about 12 by 26 centimeters, signed COROT at the lower left.","i":"Étretat, on the Normandy coast, was already a famous subject for painters, with its white chalk cliffs, drawing artists from Delacroix and Courbet to Monet. This tiny study records a more modest curve of beach and bluff. Such small oil sketches, painted rapidly outdoors, were for the artist's own use, a means of storing impressions that could later be turned into studio pictures.","c":"At twelve by twenty-six centimeters this is a note rather than a picture, and as a note it is lively: layered clouds, a curving beach and a green bluff caught in a few strokes. It shows how Corot continued, at the end of his life, to look at nature directly, and it is in this directness that he is closest to the younger painters who admired him. It would not hold a wall, but it repays a close look."},"met:435975":{"d":"A lane runs through a wood, with a woman on it, in a vertical canvas about 61 by 46 centimeters, painted about 1870 to 1873. The museum's keywords record trees, a woman and a forest, so the lane's course, the density of the leaves and the woman's activity are left unstated. In Corot's late woodland pictures a tall format usually goes with slender trunks rising to a pale sky, though the museum does not describe this one.","i":"A path leading into a wood is one of the oldest devices of landscape painting, because it invites the viewer to walk into the scene. In his last decade Corot used it with great economy: a track, a few trunks, a figure for scale. The younger painters of the Impressionist generation, Pissarro and Berthe Morisot among them, learned from his respect for tone and from his habit of painting outdoors.","c":"A restful, well-made picture in which the arrangement of trunks, lane and light matters more than any incident. The woman is a small accent; the true subject is the way light filters through leaves. Corot's late woodland scenes can be repetitive, and this is not the most original, but its tonal sureness and delicacy remain admirable. It is a type that rewards a slow look."},"met:435990":{"d":"A woman gathers faggots, that is, bundles of sticks cut for firewood, in the woods at Ville-d'Avray, in a large vertical oil on canvas about 72 by 57 centimeters, painted about 1871 to 1874. The museum's keywords record trees, a woman, a forest and work, so the woman's pose, the density of the trees and the light are left unstated. The picture is one of Corot's latest treatments of the village where his family had a house.","i":"Gathering fallen wood for fuel was a hard, familiar task of poor country women, and the subject links Corot's late woodland landscapes to the peasant imagery of Millet and the Barbizon painters. Corot's treatment is gentler than Millet's: labor is softened by feathery foliage and silvery light, and the woman is a figure in a landscape more than a symbol of toil.","c":"A late picture in which Corot's sympathy for rural labor is expressed within his gentlest landscape idiom. The woman is a modest focus, and the picture avoids both the idealization of country life and the harshness of Millet; the trembling foliage and the pale sky keep it within the dream world of his late style. It is among the more poignant of his woodland pieces, if not the most vigorous."},"nga:46028":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, two women tend a man who sits on a white cloth at the edge of a dense forest, his mostly nude body marked by arrows and a red cloth around his waist. The women wear blue and white cloths over dark dresses; one holds a silver bowl while the other draws an arrow from his body. Tall trees tower on both sides, a distant hill carries tiny riders along its ridge, and at the top two cherubs gilded with yellow light point down from the leaves. The brushwork is loose and expressive. Oil on canvas, about 130 by 86 centimeters, dated 1874.","i":"Saint Sebastian, a Roman soldier condemned to death under Diocletian for his Christian faith, was shot with arrows and left for dead; the widow Irene found him alive and nursed him back to health, a scene shown here with a companion. The subject, popular since the Renaissance, lets painters combine a vulnerable body and acts of mercy. Corot treats it as a woodland idyll, with the saint's suffering subdued in the soft green light.","c":"An unusual late attempt by a landscape painter at a sacred subject. The figures are small and summary, the anatomy vague, and the saint's suffering is hardly felt; what succeeds is the forest, tall and luminous, in which the drama is quietly absorbed. Corot treats the legend as a pastoral episode, and the result is gentle rather than devotional. It shows his late tendency to turn every subject into landscape."},"met:435984":{"d":"A young woman sits half turned to one side, her head swung back toward the viewer with a level, unsmiling gaze. Dark hair is parted beneath a yellow-green scarf wound with a wreath of ivy. A white blouse with full sleeves, painted in thick, creamy strokes, shows under a red-brown bodice, and a pale sleeve crosses the foreground. In one hand she holds a small dark-red flower against her chest. The background is a smoky brown and olive, brushed loosely, with little to define the room. The face is smoothly modelled and carefully finished, while the hands and arms are scrubbed in with rough, quick strokes. It is an oil painting on canvas, about 82 by 65 centimetres.","i":"Best known for silvery landscapes, Corot also painted figures with serious intent in his last decades. The model was Agostina, an Italian whom the Met identifies as “the Italian of Montparnasse.” Calling her a sibyl, one of the prophetic women of antiquity, lifts a studio portrait into allegory, and the ivy in her hair may allude to artistic immortality. X-rays show that she first held a cello and bow; Corot painted the instrument out and gave her a flower, which makes the picture quieter and less anecdotal. The Met relates her pose to Raphael, a reminder that Corot's late figures drew on the Italian Renaissance.","c":"The face is the success: calm, a little weary, modelled in soft transitions that carry over the tonal delicacy of Corot's landscapes. The white blouse is boldly handled in thick paint. The weakness is one the Met itself notes: the rough, summary brushwork of the hands and arms, passages Corot struggled with, and the murky background gives the figure little air. Beside the polished Salon figures of the day this is plain and unflattering, and that is its appeal: no story, no gesture, only a sitter looking back with composure."},"nga:195138":{"d":"A landscape in oil on canvas, about 42 by 57 centimeters, painted by Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot between about 1870 and 1875, in the last years of his life. The title is simply Landscape, so it names no place and tells no story. At this size and date it belongs to the kind of mid-sized, atmospheric landscapes he produced in his late years for collectors, usually built from woods, water and soft light.","i":"Corot's late landscapes were prized for their haze and tenderness, and they sold so well that his last years brought him fame and a wide circle of imitators and forgers. A work simply titled Landscape carries no story or place name, so it is best read as an expression of mood: a dream of nature composed in the studio from memory and sketches.","c":"Corot's late landscapes can be among the most lyrical in French painting, yet their formula, feathery trees, pale water, a silvery sky, was repeated so often that individual examples vary widely in quality. This one's modest title and size place it among the quieter products of his late production. Whether it is exceptional or typical is best judged by looking closely at its surface."},"met:440847":{"d":"A bearded bishop in a mitre is the subject of this oil on canvas of about 47 by 38 cm, painted about 1770. It is a head study, a close-up of a figure of the kind that appears in altarpieces, probably drawn from a model, and it fills the canvas with a single, characterful face. Gaetano Gandolfi, a member of a prominent family of Bolognese painters, made many such heads, prized by collectors and useful in his studio.","i":"Bishops were prominent saints in the Catholic calendar, among them Augustine, Ambrose, Nicholas and, for Bologna, Petronius, and a bearded bishop with a mitre could be one of them or an invented figure of venerable authority. Heads were studied from life in the Bolognese tradition, which goes back to the Carracci's academy, and head studies were also valued independently by collectors. Gandolfi was admired by Italian and English patrons for his naturalistic art.","c":"The Bolognese tradition of unidealized naturalism is evident in the type: a broad, direct, quick treatment of a living face, with the white beard and mitre offering a bold play of light and gold against flesh. The strength is spontaneity; the weakness is the reliance on a familiar stock figure, the venerable old man, which risks formula. Even so, the fluent brush and the Venetian warmth of color show how well Gandolfi could infuse a study with life."},"cma:160143":{"d":"The elderly Joseph holds the scriptures open for the Christ Child, who sits on his mother's lap, and also holds the flowering rod that marked him as Mary's chosen husband. Joseph looks with concern toward Mary, who looks up toward cherubim hovering in a cloud at the upper left. The canvas, about 87 by 69 cm, is dated 1776, when it was exhibited in the religious festival at the church of San Procolo in Bologna, and it is in a modern Gandolfini frame.","i":"In the apocryphal tradition, Joseph was chosen among Mary's suitors when his rod flowered. The child reading the scriptures looks ahead to his future, and Mary's sad gaze reflects the suffering he will endure, a meaning that viewers of a private devotional picture were meant to grasp. The picture shows how Gandolfi gave the Holy Family the warmth of a household while keeping the Passion in view.","c":"The balanced composition and the suggestion of emotional connections among the figures express what the museum calls the religious honesty of Gandolfi's art, and the down-to-earth naturalism typical of Bolognese painting is its strength. Compared with Tiepolo's flamboyance in Venice, this is earthy and intimate. The picture is modest in scale and plain in effect, perhaps too plain for those who seek grandeur, but its sincerity is its virtue, and it is a fine late work."},"met:665702":{"d":"At the moment of sacrifice, the goddess Diana intervenes, and a deer and a putto appear in this small oil on canvas of about 68 by 46 cm, dated 1789. In the myth, the Greek fleet is becalmed at Aulis, and to appease Diana the commander Agamemnon must sacrifice his daughter Iphigenia, but at the last moment the goddess substitutes a deer. The size suggests a sketch or small finished picture by the Bolognese painter Gaetano Gandolfi.","i":"The story, told by Euripides, is the classic image of a parent's sacrifice of a child for the public good, and the deer's substitution gives it a merciful ending. In late eighteenth-century Europe the subject was familiar from the theater and from Gluck's operas on the Iphigenia legends, performed in Paris in 1774 and 1779. For a neoclassical audience, Greek tragedy offered models of heroic duty and of divine mercy.","c":"A small, quick composition in which Gandolfi's Venetian-influenced color and fluent drawing tell a complex story in a compact format. The deer and the putto add a Rococo lightness to a tragic subject, and the treatment is more theatrical than anguished. The strength is clarity of narrative and an easy, graceful touch; the weakness is a lack of real terror. As a small version it is more vivid than a finished academic canvas might have been."},"met:437219":{"d":"A soldier on horseback, the dispatch-bearer of the title, is among a group of men who smoke and drink. The canvas is large, about 130 by 213 centimeters, dated 1880. De Neuville made his reputation with scenes of the Franco-Prussian War, and this one may be in that vein: a moment of army life, a story of ordinary men on campaign rather than a general's battle.","i":"After France's defeat in 1871, a taste developed for military pictures that honored the courage of the ordinary soldier. De Neuville's The Last Cartridges, Le Bourget and The Cemetery of Saint-Privat made him the patriotic painter of the lost war, and his pictures were widely reproduced in prints. The dispatch rider, carrying messages through danger, was a figure of duty and endurance.","c":"De Neuville shows the soldier's war, not the general's: tired men, tobacco and small acts of courage. His drawing is firm, the narrative clear and the large scale gives it presence. The sentiment can be heavy, and the patriotic theme invites easy emotion. But among French painters of military anecdote he is a direct storyteller, and he gave a defeated country pictures it was glad to look at."},"met:437220":{"d":"A lone cavalryman with his horse is the subject of this small canvas, about 46 by 38 centimeters, painted in 1884, a year before de Neuville's death. The picture is a character study of a type, the soldier shown as an individual rather than a figure in a battle, and its modest size suited a collector's wall. At that date he was among the most popular painters in France.","i":"The cavalry soldier with his horse was a symbol of French military tradition, and after 1871 such figures stood for national resilience as France rebuilt its army. De Neuville, who died at fifty, specialized in the ordinary soldier, drawn from careful study of uniforms, equipment and gesture. A picture of a single trooper offered an emblem of courage without the horror of a battlefield.","c":"A small, crisp study in which man and horse are drawn with the confidence of a specialist. It lacks the narrative drive of de Neuville's large war pictures, but the concentration on a single figure has its own appeal, and the painting is freed from the melodrama that sometimes weighs on his crowds. It is a late, assured work, best enjoyed as a well-made portrait of a type."},"nga:195361":{"d":"A military column marches through a snow-covered, war-damaged street, soldiers and a dog walking in formation, some soldiers holding a white flag. Nearby, distressed civilians gather, one reaching toward the troops, and damaged buildings show recent fighting. The mood, as the museum describes it, is one of tension and despair. Alphonse de Neuville made it in 1884 in gouache on canvas, 65.4 by 91.1 centimeters, a matte, opaque watercolor medium less usual for so large a picture.","i":"The subject is the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 to 1871, the defeat that haunted France for a generation. De Neuville became its leading painter, and pictures like this fed a public hunger to see the soldier's suffering honored. A white flag signals a request to parley or surrender, and the crowd's anguish shows what was at stake for civilians when a town yielded. Painted thirteen years after the war, it looks back rather than reports.","c":"De Neuville's gift was clarity of narrative: each figure plays a part, the snowy street sets the cold mood, and the reaching hand gives the scene its emotional hinge. The danger is the melodrama of a staged tableau, and a matte gouache surface tends to flatten feeling into pattern. He was a fluent and popular painter, and here the discipline of the design holds up better than the sentiment."},"nga:42394":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a young man faces the viewer but cuts his light-brown eyes sharply to the right. His slightly wild, copper-brown hair puffs around his ears and tendrils curl on his forehead, his cheeks are flushed vivid pink, his full lips are closed and his rounded chin has a cleft; darker strokes around the mouth could be a goatee. Light from the right shines on a sliver of white at the neckline of his coffee-brown, high-necked garment, while clothing and hair blend into a dark brown ground. Brushstrokes are visible throughout, and some areas are thinly painted or abraded.","i":"An oil study of a head on paper, mounted on panel, is the kind of picture painters made as a working tool: a model's face, caught in a single sitting, could be reused in a larger composition for an apostle, a soldier or a bystander. Rubens's studio made such studies a systematic practice, and Van Dyck, who worked with Rubens at this time, took it up. The sitter is not identified, and the sharp sideways glance suggests a figure reacting to something beyond the frame.","c":"A fresh, quick study that shows the young Van Dyck's feeling for the living face: the flushed cheeks, unruly hair and glancing eyes are caught with directness, and the thin, rapid paint keeps the energy of a first impression. The background is empty, the costume barely stated and the abraded areas limit the pleasure. It is more valuable as evidence of how he trained than as a finished picture, but the immediacy is engaging."},"nga:573":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a woman in a black gown and a wide, pleated collar looks at the viewer from the corners of light brown eyes, her body angled to the left. The figure-eight pleats of the millstone collar rise to her chin. A gold stomacher comes down in a long U over her belly, white lace falls back from the cuffs of her puffed sleeves, and her hair is pulled back under a pearl-white cap. She wears pearl earrings and gold bracelets; one hand hangs in front of her while the other rests a loose fist on her corset, beside a studded chair back.","i":"The sitter's name is not recorded; the title gives only her nationality and rank. The portrait dates from about 1618, when Van Dyck was about nineteen and working in Rubens's orbit in Antwerp, and it records the formal dress of prosperous Flemish burghers and gentry: the huge millstone ruff, black silk and gold signal wealth and a Spanish-inspired sobriety that suited Catholic Flanders. It was probably commissioned together with a portrait of her husband, to hang as a pair.","c":"A remarkably assured performance by a young painter. The sitter's sidelong glance gives the face life, the gold and white lace are painted with deftness, and the great collar is built with real architecture. The stiff pose and heavy costume still follow Flemish convention, and the picture lacks the easy grace of Van Dyck's later portraits. Its interest is the promise it shows: the eye for texture and quick sympathy that would make him a portraitist of Europe's courts."},"met:436259":{"d":"A young woman's head is the sole subject of this oil study, painted on paper and later laid down on wood, about 57 by 42 centimeters, by Anthony van Dyck around 1618-20. Such a study shows a model's head and a little of the shoulders, painted quickly from life, without background or setting, to capture a type of face and an effect of light that could be used again in a larger picture.","i":"Van Dyck was about twenty and working in Antwerp in the circle of Rubens, where oil studies of heads from life were part of the studio's method: faces for saints, mothers and maidens could be drawn from a stock of studies and reused. Such a study may have served as a model for a Madonna, a saint or a mythological figure, though its own use is not recorded. It also shows the talent that made Van Dyck Rubens's most gifted assistant.","c":"A study head is a painter's thinking made visible, and Van Dyck's is already assured: quick, fluent brushwork, warm flesh tones built with confident touches and an eye for the soft structure of a young face. It lacks the polish of his later Genoese and London portraits but has a freshness they rarely recover. Next to Rubens's heads it is a touch cooler and more refined, anticipating the elegance to come."},"met:436260":{"d":"An old man with a long white beard fills this oil study on wood, about 66 by 51 centimeters, painted by Anthony van Dyck around 1617-20. Like other head studies from Rubens's Antwerp circle, it presents the model from the shoulders up, without setting, with light and brush used to bring out the age of the face and the texture of the beard. The recorded motifs name only a man, so the study is about character rather than a named sitter.","i":"Elderly models with strongly marked faces were prized in Rubens's studio as stock figures for apostles, prophets, saints and philosophers, and the same model could appear in several guises. Van Dyck made a number of such heads in these years, and some were later used in his own religious pictures. The white beard is the sign of wisdom and holiness, qualities the heads were meant to evoke.","c":"Van Dyck's study heads of old men are among the most vivid things he painted in these years: the brushwork is quick and responsive, the beard built from fluent strokes and the skin from warm, broken color. They lack the elegance of his later portraits but show Rubens's lessons absorbed and an individual vision emerging. An old head let the young painter test his command of texture, and he passes the test."},"met:436258":{"d":"A young man with tousled, reddish-brown curls looks over his shoulder at the viewer, leaning on a stone ledge in front of a plain pillar. One hand, with a ring on the little finger, is raised languidly toward his chin; the other drops over the front of the ledge with the fingers spread. His sleeve is dark red-brown silk edged with white lace, and a heavy black silk cloak falls in broad glossy folds across one shoulder and the lower half of the canvas. His lips are red, his skin pale, and his expression composed, a little haughty. Behind him lies a dim blue-grey sky with a band of cloud, painted thinly and quickly.","i":"Van Dyck was in his early twenties when he painted this, probably during his winter in London in 1620–21, having already become the most gifted assistant in Rubens's Antwerp workshop. Scholars note that his self-portraits of about 1621 move from the intimate to the status-conscious: here he appears as a sophisticated courtier, in silk and lace, with a ring on his little finger. The seventeenth-century biographer Bellori described him as grand by nature and eager for fame. He left for Italy later in 1621, and in 1632 he returned to England as court painter to King Charles.","c":"A calculated performance, and a very good one. The cloak, brushed in long glossy strokes, and the nonchalant ringed hand announce a gentleman rather than a craftsman, and the pose is the template for the elegant sitters Van Dyck would later paint at the English court. The face, though, is bland beside the clothes, and the languid attitude is a touch affected: one senses a young man designing his image more than testing it. Next to Rembrandt's self-scrutiny it is a bid for status, but a persuasive one."},"nga:54":{"d":"A woman in gold and ink-black garments sits in a red armchair before a stone portico, turning to look at the viewer under cocked brows, with a faint smile and a hint of dimples. Her brown hair is pulled back under a gold band, a gold chain weaves across her open black cloak, pearls circle her neck, and frothy white lace rings neck and cuffs. In one hand she holds a white flower; the other rests on the chair arm with an ostrich-feather fan. A scarlet curtain opens on a courtyard with a carved portico, trees showing between its columns, under a blue sky.","i":"The sitter is Isabella Brant (1591–1626), the first wife of Peter Paul Rubens, whom she married in 1609. The portico behind her, with its carved ornament, is thought to be the garden pavilion of Rubens's own house in Antwerp, so the portrait places her at home. Van Dyck, then Rubens's assistant, painted it about 1621, before leaving for Italy. Isabella died of plague in 1626.","c":"A winning portrait: the sidelong smile and cocked brow give Isabella a quick, intelligent animation, and the swathes of lace, the gold brocade and the black-on-black cloak are painted with a bravura beyond the usual Flemish carefulness. The architectural backdrop is a little stagey, and the gold dress competes with the face. But Van Dyck was twenty-two, and the picture already has the easy elegance that would make his name."},"nga:55":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, about 172 by 117 centimetres, shows Susanna Fourment, as the title names her, with her daughter. The museum record gives no description of pose, dress or setting, so these cannot be reported with confidence. The size and the double format suggest a formal family portrait of the kind Antwerp's wealthy merchant families commissioned in the early 1620s, made when Van Dyck, in his early twenties, was already much in demand.","i":"Susanna Fourment belonged to a prosperous Antwerp family of silk merchants; her younger sister Hélène became Rubens's second wife in 1630, and Rubens's celebrated Straw Hat portrait has traditionally been linked with Susanna, though the identification is uncertain. A portrait of a mother with her child presented the family as a continuing line, and Van Dyck was admired for making children look lively rather than doll-like.","c":"Without detail to examine, the safe assessment is of what this type did best for Van Dyck: he could make a formal double portrait feel like a moment of affection, and in his Antwerp years his sitters often have a freshness that convention did not allow. The risk, with a child in formal dress, is stiffness and a certain commercial sweetness. This is an early work, before his Genoese and English portraits made grandeur his trademark."},"met:436254":{"d":"A bearded man is portrayed in a ruff and a belt, with a glove and a chair among the recorded details, on a panel of about 106 by 73 centimeters painted around 1618 by Anthony van Dyck. The size suggests a three-quarter-length, seated portrait of a man of standing, shown close to the viewer. The sitter is not identified, and the picture is oil on wood, the support still favored in Antwerp at that date.","i":"In Antwerp around 1618, when Van Dyck was about nineteen and had just become a master in the painters' guild, portrait commissions came from the city's merchants and professional men, and the ruff, the glove and the chair were standard signs of gentility. The ruff, a starched linen collar, marks a man of means; the glove a gentleman's accessory; the chair, status and ease. The sitter's name is lost.","c":"A very early work, it shows Van Dyck assimilating the Flemish tradition of sober, sharply observed portraiture, with the face rendered in detail and the costume crisply, before the Genoese and English years gave him his courtly elegance. The seated pose and plain setting are conventional. What stands out is the sitter's presence: the young painter already knows how to give a face weight."},"nga:1231":{"d":"A tall woman in black and a shorter man with brown skin stand on a terrace in this full-length portrait. At the centre the marchesa wears a voluminous black dress with a row of gold buttons, a wide grey ruff, red ruffled cuffs and a pearl-ornamented cap, and looks down the bridge of her nose, lips closed, holding a sprig of orange blossom. Leaning in from the right, her clean-shaven attendant, in an amber-yellow garment, holds a crimson parasol over her head. Fluted columns rise at the right, a low balustrade encloses the terrace, and a distant landscape lies under blue sky.","i":"Elena Grimaldi married the Genoese nobleman Giacomo Cattaneo and was painted by Van Dyck in 1623. The same museum holds portraits of Filippo and Maddalena Cattaneo, painted that year and traditionally identified as her children. The orange blossom is a flower of marriage and chastity, and the parasol marks status. The young attendant, whose name is not recorded, reflects the practice in wealthy European households of keeping African servants, whose presence also signalled their masters' wealth.","c":"A commanding image of aristocratic authority: the black dress and grey ruff are as severe as armour, the tilted gaze is imperious, and the red parasol, bright against the black and the sky, adds drama. The picture also invites uncomfortable thought, since the attendant is a prop whose role is to display her status; he is given nothing like her individuality. As a statement of rank and Genoese magnificence it is among Van Dyck's most successful, if cold."},"nga:1232":{"d":"A young boy in a black and gold embroidered jerkin, breeches and short cape, with a white lace collar and buttercup-yellow stockings, stands brightly lit against deep chestnut shadow. His body is angled to the right but he turns to look at the viewer with green eyes, the corners of his closed lips turning up, one fist on his hip with the elbow jutting out. In the other hand he holds the chain leash of a brown and white dog, perhaps a puppy, in a brass-studded collar. An inscription near the upper left gives the date 1623 and his age, four years and seven months.","i":"Filippo Cattaneo, about four and a half years old, stands in the pose of a grown nobleman, hand on hip, as if practising the role he would inherit. The dog, a standard prop in portraits of children, may signal loyalty and the aristocratic pastime of hunting. It was painted in Genoa in 1623 as a companion to the portrait of his sister Maddalena, and the inscription recording his exact age was a common practice in portraits of children at this time.","c":"A delightful and shrewd portrait: the small body is posed like a courtier's, with elbow akimbo, and the gap between the aristocratic stance and the baby face is the picture's quiet joke. The gold embroidery on black, the yellow stockings and the lively little dog are handled with Van Dyck's usual flair. The room is a plain brown void, which keeps attention on the boy but leaves little atmosphere."},"nga:1233":{"d":"A young girl with round, rosy cheeks and wavy blond hair to her chin holds a red and green apple in both hands at her waist, turning her face to look at the viewer with large dark blue eyes. She wears an ankle-length, silvery white dress with a high neck and puffed sleeves edged with lace, a smock down the front and a lace-edged apron over the full skirt, with the rounded toes of cream-white shoes peeping out. She stands on a step, a crimson cushion with gold tassels on the floor behind her, a thick square column at the left and shadow at the right.","i":"Maddalena Cattaneo, a child of a Genoese noble family, was painted in 1623, the same year as her brother Filippo. In the early seventeenth century children of the elite were dressed as miniature adults in the same fashionable fabrics as their parents, and portraits recorded the family line. The apple, a common attribute in portraits of children, may carry associations of innocence, health or fertility. The column and cushion suggest a grand interior.","c":"Van Dyck was exceptional with children, and this small girl, solemn and sweet in a dress that is nearly an adult's, has real presence. The silvery dress, glimmering hair and lace are handled with tact, and the apple gives the hands something to hold. If the picture has a limit, it is the formal conception: the column and cushion say nobility more than childhood. But the soft, direct gaze wins the viewer regardless."},"nga:56":{"d":"A woman in a voluminous gown of dark emerald green and gold sits at the centre of a shadowed, cavernous space, her body nearly filling the tall canvas and hiding all but a gold finial and an arm of her red velvet chair. She turns to the right and looks out from the corners of dark eyes, her tawny hair drawn back under a silver-glinting headband with a yellow flower. A tall lace ruff pleated in figure-eights spans her shoulders, gold embroidery runs down bodice and skirt, and a closed black-and-gold fan hangs on a chain. Behind, a raised curtain opens on a landscape with a stone tower.","i":"Van Dyck lived in Genoa, on and off, from 1621 to 1627 and painted the great families of the republic, the Balbi, Brignole-Sale, Cattaneo and Spinola among them. His full-length portraits, with columns, curtains and noble poses, drew on Venetian models by Titian and Veronese and gave the Genoese patriciate the grandeur they wanted. Here the sitter is monumental, nearly filling the canvas in her dark gown, her face the one bright point beside the lace.","c":"The great gown is the subject: its dark green, almost black, and gold embroidery make a majestic mass, and the lace ruff and the quiet face above it have a calm authority. The weakness is a sense of inventory: the sitter is more costume than person, and the chair, space and landscape are summarily handled. It shows how Van Dyck solved the problem of the grandee, by making dress an architecture, though the personality is thin."},"met:436257":{"d":"A young woman in a plain brown habit rises on clouds, gazing up toward a shaft of light at the upper left, her hands held open at her sides in entreaty. A tawny mantle falls from one arm. Plump winged infants swirl around her, some clinging to her robe, some tumbling head down through the clouds; one at the upper left holds a garland of pink roses, and another trails a scrap of red cloth. At the lower left a skull lies in shadow. The sky is a patchwork of blue and grey cloud. Van Dyck models the infants' flesh in warm pinks and creams and the saint's face in a softer tone, with fluid, sweeping brushwork.","i":"Saint Rosalie was a twelfth-century hermit whose relics were reported found on Monte Pellegrino above Palermo in 1624, in the midst of a plague outbreak; she soon became the city's patron saint, invoked against the disease. Van Dyck, then twenty-five, was in Palermo that year and painted her more than once. Here she intercedes with heaven for the city's sick, and the roses and the skull, a sign of mortality, are her usual attributes. The Met dates the picture to 1624 and acquired it by purchase in 1871. It belongs to his Italian years, when he combined Rubens's fleshy infants with a lighter, more Venetian colour.","c":"Van Dyck turns an urgent civic crisis into a theatrical vision, and the result is more decorative than devout. The upward gaze and open hands read at once, and the tumbling infants are painted with a sensuous ease that owes much to Rubens and Titian. But the saint is rather weightless, her face is generic, and the cloud of children competes with her for attention. It has little of the human reality of his portraits. Still, as an exercise in Baroque rhetoric by a painter of twenty-five, it shows how quickly he absorbed Italian lessons."},"cma:131819":{"d":"An unidentified Genoese lady in elaborate dress is portrayed full-length with her young child, who reaches spontaneously upward, on a canvas of about 218 by 146 centimeters, painted around 1623-25 by Anthony van Dyck. A dramatic background sets off the figures. The scale and costume mark her status; the warmth of her face and the child's gesture bring the portrait to life, on a scale that would suit a palace room.","i":"In his Italian years, from 1621 to 1627, Van Dyck worked for Genoese patricians, the noble families who ran the republic's banking and trade and commissioned grand full-length portraits. His innovation was to combine the rank-proclaiming scale of earlier portraits with an easy elegance and warmth: portraits that showed the sitter's personality, not only her silks. The child's gesture gives the formal portrait a moment of spontaneity.","c":"Van Dyck's genius for turning rank into personality is all here: the sweep of costume, the dramatic backdrop that gives the figure stature and the child's reaching hand, which turns a formal state portrait into a human encounter. The pose is still conventional and the sitter remains unnamed, but the warmth of the face prevents the grandeur from freezing. It anticipates the manner he later brought to the English court."},"met:436255":{"d":"A woman, called the Marchesa Durazzo, is portrayed in a canvas of about 113 by 96 centimeters, probably painted around 1622-25 by Anthony van Dyck during his Italian years. The title's word called marks a traditional identification not proven by documents, and the recorded motifs name only a woman and a portrait. The size is probably that of a three-quarter-length portrait of a Genoese patrician lady.","i":"The Durazzo were one of the great Genoese patrician families, bankers and merchants who built grand palaces in the city and competed in splendor, and a portrait by Van Dyck was a sign of taste and rank. In Genoa Van Dyck found a society that valued display tempered by dignity, and his portraits of its women set the pattern of Genoese portraiture for generations. Called reminds the viewer that the identification of such portraits rests on tradition.","c":"This is Van Dyck in his Genoese prime: the manner gives rank an easy grace, owing something to what Titian's Venetian portraits had taught him, and the paint is fluid and confident. The sitter's identity matters less than that manner. Compared with the stiff, Spanish-inspired court portraits that Genoa knew before him, it is a breath of fresh air."},"met:436261":{"d":"Mary nurses the infant Christ in this small panel, about 64 by 50 centimeters, painted around 1620 by Anthony van Dyck. The recorded motifs, the Madonna and Child, breastfeeding, Mary and the Christ Child, point to a Madonna lactans, a tender image in which the Mother of God feeds her Son. The intimate scale suggests a picture for private devotion rather than for a church altar.","i":"The nursing Madonna, or Virgin of Milk, is an old devotional type stressing that God became truly human, fed at his mother's breast, and presenting Mary as the nourisher of the faithful. In Counter-Reformation Flanders, such images of maternal tenderness suited the Church's encouragement of devotion to the Virgin. Van Dyck was about twenty-one and still working in Rubens's orbit, where such subjects were treated with warmth and fleshly vitality.","c":"The young Van Dyck's response to Rubens is evident in the warm, fleshy modeling and the affectionate intimacy, but the gentleness is his own: the lines are softer and the emotion less boisterous. At this small scale the painting is tender and quickly sure. It lacks the monumentality of Rubens's altarpieces but carries a sweetness that would become Van Dyck's signature, and it is a promising early picture rather than a mature statement."},"nga:1229":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a man in armour stands in profile facing left but turns his head to look up and over his shoulder into the distance. His chestnut hair flows around his ears, and plump cheeks and jowls lead to a double chin beneath a wispy moustache. Light from the left glints off the armour covering his arms and round belly; one hand rests on the hilt of a sword and the other on a cane. A ruby-red sash tied in a bow on his arm billows behind him before a distant grey landscape, and a Latin inscription at upper right names him.","i":"The inscription names him as Raphael Racius, Raffaele Raggi, prefect of the republic's galleys, an office in the naval service of Genoa. The armour, baton and sash proclaim his command, and the sideways look into the distance suggests vigilance. Van Dyck's Genoese portraits adapted the heroic poses of Titian's and Rubens's portraits of generals; here the stance is that of an officer of the sea at rest on land.","c":"A robust, unflattering portrait: the artist does not hide the sitter's double chin or round belly beneath the armour, and the glint of light on steel and the swirl of red sash give the pose vitality. The faintly comic gap between martial costume and well-fed body is turned to the sitter's advantage by the confident stance. Van Dyck's Genoese portraits can be formulaic, but the unvarnished face gives this one a personal edge. The dull landscape is merely a backdrop."},"nga:1228":{"d":"A portrait of the Genoese nobleman Giovanni Vincenzo Imperiale, in oil on canvas, about 129 by 105 centimetres, dated 1626. The museum record gives no description, so his pose, dress and setting cannot be described with confidence. At this size and date, in Van Dyck's Genoese period, it presumably follows the formal pattern of his portraits of the republic's patricians: a figure in sober, costly black set against a dark ground or architectural backdrop, with the face and hands as the main points of light.","i":"The Imperiale were one of the leading families of Genoa, with palaces and villas that made them prominent patrons, and a portrait by Van Dyck was a way for a noble to declare standing in a city whose elite competed in magnificence. The sitter is recorded as a man of letters, which would have made him a natural client for the Flemish painter then fashionable in Genoa. Van Dyck's Italian years ended in 1627, when he returned to Antwerp.","c":"Without the details of the portrait to hand, the fair verdict rests on the artist's Genoese manner: dignified, courtly and calm, with the sitter's face lit as the focus and a restrained palette of black and silvery light. It is representative of Van Dyck's ability to dignify without flattering outright. For all that, his Genoese portraits can look alike, and they sometimes flatter the type more than the individual."},"nga:1230":{"d":"A woman in a voluminous black dress with a wide ruffled collar sits in profile facing left and holds the hand of a small boy who stands at her feet and looks out at the viewer. Both have rosy cheeks and dark blond hair; pearls cover her pulled-back hair, and a thick gold chain loops from shoulder to hip. The boy, in a suit of crimson leaves on grey satin with a lace-trimmed collar, grips her hand, his other hand on his hip, while a brown and white dog frolics behind him. Stone columns, a balustrade and a flutter of red drape frame them.","i":"The sitters are not identified; the title records only that she was a Genoese noblewoman, and the boy is presumably her son and heir. Genoese patrician families, enriched by banking and shipping, commissioned grand portraits to display their dynastic standing, and Van Dyck gave the genre columns, drapery and an easy aristocratic bearing derived from Titian and Veronese. Her black dress shows the Spanish-influenced fashion of the Genoese elite.","c":"A tender and well-staged portrait: the boy's hand in the mother's, the dog's cheerful bounce and the shimmer of silk give the formal arrangement real warmth, and the black dress is made monumental by the pale face and the glint of gold and pearl. The columns and drape are the usual machinery, and the faces have a family sweetness that shades into sameness. Among the Genoese pictures it is one in which the grandeur is softened by affection."},"met:436253":{"d":"A man in a black fur-lined cloak sits at a table but twists in his chair to look back at the viewer over his shoulder, as if he has just been interrupted. He has a dark moustache and pointed beard, curly dark hair, a small white lace collar and cuffs, and a brocade doublet under the cloak. One hand rests on the chair back at the lower centre; the other holds a pair of compasses over papers. On the table, under a patterned red carpet, stand a celestial globe, an antique bust, books and a sheet of paper. A deep red curtain hangs at the left. The paint is rich and fluid against a dark ground, and the face is lit sharply.","i":"The sitter is Lucas van Uffel, also spelled van Uffelen, a Dutch merchant and art collector who spent formative years in Venice, where he built his collection, and who returned to Amsterdam in 1630; he died in 1637. The globe, compasses, antique bust and papers, laid out on a costly carpet, announce a man of learning and taste, a collector among his treasures. Van Dyck painted him twice, and this picture dates from about 1622, during his years in Italy, when he absorbed Venetian colour and the informality of Titian's portraits. It came to the Met in the bequest of Benjamin Altman in 1913.","c":"The pose is the picture. A sitter caught in mid-turn, body one way and face another, was an unusually lively idea in 1622, and Van Dyck's quick, fluid brush gives the black cloak and white lace real vibrancy. The objects on the table are less well resolved: globe, bust and carpet run together in a crowded corner, and the hands lack the elegance Van Dyck would later perfect. It is the work of a young painter testing how far a portrait can move, before the cooler grace of his English years took over."},"nga:43720":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a woman in a voluminous, gold-trimmed white satin gown and a black overdress stands holding a closed fan, her body angled to the right as she looks out from the corners of her grey eyes. A pearl necklace lies at the base of her neck, with traces of another once painted higher up like a choker. A lace collar lies along her shoulders, her puffy sleeves are tied at the elbows and end in lace cuffs, and a jewelled gold brooch is linked by a gold chain across her chest. A white satin ribbon is bowed at her waist.","i":"The sitter is unidentified. Portraits like this record the formal fashion of Antwerp in the late 1620s: a white satin gown under black, with lace, pearls and a gold chain, which together signalled wealth. The fan was both a fashionable accessory and a prop for the hands, giving the pose an air of elegance. The trace of a higher necklace, painted over, shows that Van Dyck changed his mind as he worked.","c":"An elegant, restrained portrait and a good example of Van Dyck's Antwerp years: the satin is handled with a creamy, flickering touch, the lace and gold are crisp, and the sidelong glance gives the sitter reserve and poise. The plain brown ground focuses everything on the figure, though it lacks the dramatic settings of the Genoese portraits. The face is a little blank, as often in his formal portraits of women."},"nga:1227":{"d":"Light pours over Mary as she floats before a bank of clouds, surrounded by ten mostly nude winged putti. Her hands are held out at her sides, palms facing out, and her head is tipped back as she looks up with parted lips; her pale blue garment is tied with a navy ribbon. The putti carry instruments of the Passion: one at the lower left holds a wooden cross, its foot on a grey globe topped with a cross; one holds a ring of thorns and another raises a ring of pink roses as if to crown her. At the right a putto holds a cloth towards her face.","i":"As Intercessor, Mary pleads with her Son on behalf of sinners, an idea central to Catholic devotion in Counter-Reformation Flanders. The putti carry the cross, the globe and the crown of thorns, symbols of Christ's Passion and of his rule over the world, while the roses are Mary's own flower, so that intercession and sacrifice are united in a single image. Van Dyck painted it in Antwerp, after returning from Italy, for Catholic patrons.","c":"Warm, high-keyed and graceful: the putti are tumbling, lively and cleverly varied, the golden light on the powder-blue clouds is beautifully managed, and Mary's open-armed pose is gentle rather than theatrical. Van Dyck is often thought sweeter than Rubens, and here the sweetness comes close to sentiment: the instruments of the Passion are carried by a cast of cherubs, and the mood is more charming than spiritual. As a small Counter-Reformation devotional picture it succeeds in being lovely."},"nga:57":{"d":"Shown from about the hips up, a young man with curly brown hair stands facing the viewer, his face angled slightly left, looking out from the corners of his grey eyes. He wears a plum-purple coat under a butter-yellow satin cape fastened over one shoulder, with white lace at collar and cuffs. One hand rests on his hip; in the other arm he holds a wooden staff with a pewter-grey hook. An emerald-green cloth falls behind him, and a rocky outcrop opens on a green valley and blue mountains under a golden-lit sky. Yellow lettering records his name, the date 1632 and his age, about nineteen.","i":"The inscription names the sitter as Philip, Lord Wharton, aged about nineteen, and the picture dates from the year Van Dyck came to England and entered the king's service. The staff with a hook suggests a shepherd's crook, and pastoral costume was fashionable at the court of Charles I, which enjoyed masques and pastoral dramas of Arcadian shepherds. Wharton later became a leading Puritan and Parliamentarian in the Civil War, a stern contrast to this courtly pose.","c":"A portrait of youthful assurance: the plum velvet and yellow satin cape are painted with a sumptuous sparkle, the pose is a loose, languid contrapposto, and the landscape and green drape frame a handsome head. The pastoral costume is fancy dress that wears thin, and the face, though fresh, is more a type of courtly youth than a particular character. It shows Van Dyck bringing his Genoese ease to the English court, the beginning of the Cavalier portrait."},"nga:41651":{"d":"A woman in a shimmering azure-blue gown stands at the centre next to a small figure in a flame-red suit, her body angled to the left, her brown eyes meeting the viewer's. Two long white feathers droop from her wide-brimmed black hat, and her gown is trimmed with bands of gold under a tall, stiff lace collar. With one hand she touches her skirt and with the other a monkey perched on her companion's shoulder; he looks up at her, in ivory gloves and fawn boots. A pearl-encrusted crown is tucked into a gold curtain, and a miniature orange tree grows in a pot on a ledge.","i":"The queen is Henrietta Maria of France, wife of Charles I, and her companion is the court dwarf Jeffrey Hudson, who was about fourteen when this portrait was painted in 1633. The crown, curtain and column are the usual attributes of royalty, and the potted orange tree, an exotic fashionable at the Stuart court, signals refinement. Dwarfs and pet monkeys were fixtures of courts, and the portrait presents the queen with her household's wit as part of her image.","c":"A work of lively charm and subtle politics. The queen's blue silk, painted in short, flickering strokes, glows against the white lace and black hat, and her hand on the monkey on her small companion's shoulder gives an unexpected note of intimacy. The pairing is double-edged: the dwarf and the monkey are accessories that make the queen seem taller and more powerful, a use of people as foils that modern eyes may find uncomfortable."},"nga:34046":{"d":"A man in a gold-embroidered amber-yellow doublet and tomato-red breeches stands before a rocky outcrop, facing the viewer while he turns his head slightly left, looking off with slate-blue eyes. His oval face is lightly flushed, with a moustache and a small pointed goatee; wavy brown hair falls to his shoulders, one longer lock braided and tied with a yellow and red bow. A broad lace collar overlaps a metal gorget, and a crimson cape hangs over his arm. In one hand he holds a wide-brimmed black hat with red, white and orange plumes; a breastplate rests at his feet. Steel-grey clouds, trees and hills lie beyond.","i":"The title names the sitter as Henri II de Lorraine, a member of the ducal house of Guise, one of the great princely families of France; other names have been attached to the picture at times. The gorget and breastplate at his feet make him a soldier-prince, while the lace, embroidery and braided love-lock announce an elegant gentleman. Van Dyck's portraits of this period set aristocrats in a casual stance before rock and sky, a formula that spread through European courts.","c":"Van Dyck's principal gift, the glamorous but easy-looking noble, is on show: the amber and red costume, the lace and the nonchalant stance make the sitter appear supremely at ease, and the breastplate at his feet adds a hint of martial pedigree without stiffness. The costume is more vivid than the face, which is smooth and untroubled, and the rocky backdrop is a standard prop. A polished example of a formula Van Dyck repeated for a decade."},"met:436252":{"d":"A young nobleman is shown full-length on a tall canvas of about 216 by 128 centimeters, with a dog beside him, painted around 1633-35 by Anthony van Dyck. The title names him James Stuart, Duke of Richmond and Lennox, though he held only the dukedom of Lennox when it was painted, the dukedom of Richmond coming in 1641. The scale and the formal full-length type were reserved for the court elite, and Van Dyck gave the type an air of effortless grace.","i":"James Stuart was a cousin of Charles I and among the closest of the king's courtiers; a Royalist, he stayed loyal through the Civil War and was one of the mourners who buried the king in 1649. The dog is a sign of aristocratic leisure and loyalty. Van Dyck, who became Charles I's principal painter in 1632, created the image of the Cavalier court: elegant, relaxed and confident, which Royalist families preferred to remember after the war.","c":"This is the Van Dyck formula at its most persuasive: a long-limbed figure, a confident turn of the body, a dog that animates the lower half of the canvas and a face of cool, assured poise. The ease is studied, as all Cavalier elegance is, and it can verge on glamour without much psychology. As a statement of courtly self-image, it set a template for English portraiture for a century."},"met:436256":{"d":"Robert Rich, Second Earl of Warwick (1587-1658), is portrayed on a tall canvas of about 208 by 128 centimeters, painted around 1632-35 by Anthony van Dyck. The recorded motifs name only the man and the portrait, but the size marks a full-length or near full-length state portrait in the manner Van Dyck made fashionable at the court of Charles I, in which a long figure, a calm pose and a restrained setting present the sitter's rank.","i":"Warwick was one of the great Puritan peers of Stuart England, a naval commander and a patron of colonial ventures in the Caribbean and North America. During the Civil War he served as Parliament's admiral. It is a striking irony that Van Dyck, painter to the royal court, portrayed a leader who would side against the king; in the 1630s court and opposition aristocrats alike wanted his flattering elegance.","c":"Even for a sitter of stern religious views, Van Dyck supplies the courtly manner: the long figure, the elegant outline and a calm authority that has little to do with Puritan plainness. It is a portrait of rank more than of belief, since Van Dyck's genius lay in making every sitter look like a gentleman. The result is stylish if somewhat generic in feeling."},"met:436262":{"d":"The Virgin Mary, holding the Christ Child, is accompanied by Saint Catherine of Alexandria in this canvas of about 112 by 94 centimeters, painted around 1630 by Anthony van Dyck. The recorded motifs are Catherine, Mary and the Christ Child. Catherine is usually recognized by her attribute, a spiked wheel, the instrument of her intended torture, together with a palm of martyrdom. The close grouping suits a private chapel or a collector's room.","i":"Saint Catherine, a legendary princess and scholar of Alexandria, was said to have debated the pagan philosophers and, after refusing the emperor, to have been condemned to the wheel, which shattered at her touch before she was beheaded. In many pictures she receives a ring from the Christ Child in a mystic marriage, symbolizing the soul's union with Christ. She was a favorite saint with noble patrons, and her presence beside the Virgin makes a devotional picture of great charm.","c":"Van Dyck's sacred pictures combine Rubens's warmth with an Italianate elegance of his own, and the pose of the figures and the softness of flesh show that mix. The risk is prettiness: his Madonnas can slide toward the refined and sweet, and the drama of faith gives way to graceful gesture. Still, the best of them have a gentleness and distinction few Flemish contemporaries could match."},"met:438112":{"d":"A young woman stands three-quarter length in front of a plain brown wall, her hands lightly clasped at her waist. Her dark curls are dressed with a small ornament, and a double string of pearls hangs at her neck. The gown is golden-yellow silk that crumples into bright folds, trimmed with a broad white lace collar and lace cuffs, a black bow at the chest and a black sash. At the lower left a crown rests on a table, the only sign of her rank. She turns her head toward the viewer with a mild, direct look. The paint is thin and quick in the lace and silk, and the face is modelled softly.","i":"The sitter is Henrietta Maria, the Catholic daughter of Henri IV of France, who became queen of England as the wife of Charles I. The Met reports that Van Dyck painted this portrait in 1636 as a gift for Cardinal Francesco Barberini, the pope's nephew and a renowned collector, who was Cardinal Protector of England and Scotland. A portrait sent to Rome was diplomacy as much as likeness, and it shows the queen as formal yet approachable: the crown set aside, the gown fashionable silk rather than state robes. Van Dyck had settled in London in 1632 as court painter, and his royal portraits shaped how the English court wished to be seen.","c":"This is Van Dyck's court manner at its most polished: the long, tapering hands, the lace painted with a few light touches, the silk that seems to move although the sitter is still. It is a little predictable. The same poise recurs in his court portraits, and here the face, pleasant and composed, is less alive than the fabrics around it. The crown set aside on the table is the one clever touch, saying queen without pomp. As a diplomatic image it works; as a portrait of a particular woman, it keeps its distance."},"nga:1234":{"d":"A woman stands in a voluminous rose-pink silk dress holding a wreath of pink and white roses and greenery. Shown from the hips up, her body in profile to the right, she turns to meet the viewer's eye from the corners of dark grey eyes. Her light brown hair is pulled up, with curls framing an oval face and ringlets falling to her neck. She wears large round pearls at her throat and teardrop pearl earrings; her gown is cut low, with a billowing sleeve ending in a ruffled white cuff. The ground is nearly black, with the lettering LADY AVBIGNY at her waist.","i":"The inscription names her as Lady d'Aubigny, Catherine Howard, wife of Lord George Stewart, Seigneur d'Aubigny, a kinsman of Charles I, who was killed fighting for the king at Edgehill in 1642. The rose wreath, a symbol of love and beauty, and the pearls suit a young wife, and the softly flowing pink silk reflects the Caroline taste for loose, picturesque costume in place of stiff court dress.","c":"A tender, elegant portrait: the pearl-pink silk, rose wreath and sideways glance are all tuned to the sitter's youth and beauty, and the dark ground makes the pale face and rose-pink dress glow. The pose and costume are the court's stock-in-trade, however, and the likeness has the idealised sweetness that Van Dyck gave to nearly all his female sitters. Delicate rather than searching, it is one of the more charming of his English portraits."},"cma:95266":{"d":"King Charles I is shown in this canvas of about 117 by 96 centimeters, which the museum identifies as a fragment of a copy after Van Dyck's portrait of 1632. The original was a double portrait that also included the king's wife, Queen Henrietta Maria; in this version only part of the composition survives, so the king is isolated from his consort. Copies of royal portraits were made in large numbers, and the date is given only as 1600s or later.","i":"Charles I was an avid collector who lured major artists, including Van Dyck and Rubens, to England; Van Dyck's portraits of the king created the image of a refined, aloof monarch. After Charles was beheaded in 1649 and his son Charles II restored the monarchy in 1660, his image acquired a martyr's aura, and Royalist families wanted copies of the originals. Fragments like this are the leftovers of later cutting and reframing, a fate that befell many copies.","c":"As a fragment of a copy, this canvas is a shadow of Van Dyck's original: whatever finesse the master gave to the king's features and lace is replaced by a competent translation, and the loss of the queen undoes the double portrait's meaning as a pairing of dynastic partners. Its value is historical, a trace of how widely the royal image was repeated."},"nga:110280":{"d":"A man holds a pheasant by its legs in his raised left hand while his right arm supports a long gun. Turned to the right, he wears a tall gray top hat, a gray-brown coat, dark trousers and boots, and smiles slightly, gray hair showing under the hat. A red horse stands at his left with its head lowered, and two brown-and-white dogs are at his feet. Behind, swirling brown and gray clouds rise over a brown landscape and distant blue hills. The canvas is a tall 240.0 by 148.0 centimeters.","i":"The title styles J. G. Shaddick the Celebrated Sportsman, and the picture is a portrait with attributes: the pheasant for the day's bag, the gun, the horse and the dogs for the rank and pastimes of a country gentleman. In Britain around 1800, such large canvases were commissioned by men proud of their field sports, who wanted their skill, horses and dogs recorded together. The record offers nothing more on who Shaddick was.","c":"Benjamin Marshall was one of the leading sporting painters of his day, and the strength of such pictures usually lies in the animals: the horse's lowered head and the dogs at the man's feet ring true. The man himself, smiling slightly in his tall hat, is more type than character, and the swirling sky is borrowed drama. It is a handsome record of country pride, a document of sporting culture as much as of art."},"met:10944":{"d":"A broad landscape, 79.4 by 129.5 centimeters, painted about 1885 by Robert Swain Gifford, takes its subject from the title, Near the Coast, and the museum's keywords add trees. Composition, light and figures are not recorded, so it is placed in general terms: a wide horizontal coastal landscape of late nineteenth-century American painting, in which land, sky and a few trees do the work.","i":"Gifford, from the Massachusetts coast, painted the low shores, salt marshes and open skies of southern New England throughout his career, and traveled as far as Alaska and the Near East. The picture entered the Metropolitan Museum in 1885, apparently in the same year it was painted, as a gift of an association of gentlemen, a sign of how quickly the museum collected living American painters.","c":"Gifford is at his best with a quiet coast: low horizons, wide skies and a sense of space that owes something to the Barbizon painters and to his own work as an etcher. A canvas of this width and title promises that kind of calm, open view, which is where his reputation rests. In the American tonal landscape of the 1880s it is a competent, agreeable example."},"met:437167":{"d":"A miniature of Louis XVI, King of France, painted on ivory in 1790 by Jean Laurent Mosnier. The Met's keywords record the king and a portrait, and the medium makes it a small, intimate object, made to be held in the hand or set in a frame or the lid of a box. At the date of the portrait Louis was about thirty-six, a king whose powers had been sharply curtailed by the Revolution.","i":"In 1790 Louis XVI was still king, but the Revolution had already brought the court from Versailles to Paris and limited his authority; within three years he would be deposed and executed, as the dates in the title record. A royal miniature of 1790 may have been bought by loyal supporters as a keepsake for those who still saw him as their king. Mosnier was a portraitist who had painted the French court and worked in miniature.","c":"A king's likeness on ivory is a private, domestic form of state portrait: smaller than the grand court paintings of Duplessis, it allowed a supporter to carry the monarch's face close to hand. Its poignancy is retrospective, since the viewer knows what followed. What it can offer is clarity and reserve rather than majesty, and, in a moment when royal images were becoming dangerous, a certain courage. A document of a vanishing world."},"met:438415":{"d":"God the Father is shown alone, the first person of the Trinity, in an oil painting on wood by the Lombard painter Ambrogio Bevilacqua, who died in 1512. Single images of the Father were usually made for the crowning panel of an altarpiece, where he looks down in blessing over the scenes below. In the Italian Renaissance he is normally shown as a bearded old man in a long robe with a raised hand of blessing.","i":"Early Christian art avoided showing God the Father directly, but from the later Middle Ages he appeared as a venerable old man, a type used for the Trinity and the Creation. As ruler and creator he was often placed above the main scene of an altarpiece, in a gabled or round-topped panel that crowned the whole structure. The face is meant to inspire awe and trust, an image of authority softened by benevolence.","c":"Bevilacqua worked in Milan in the age of Foppa and Leonardo, and his figures are firmly drawn, with a cool clarity. A solitary Father is a restricted subject, and success depends on face and gesture. Bevilacqua is a minor master, little known outside Lombardy, and the picture is best taken as a fragment of a larger devotional ensemble, with the strengths of its careful drawing and the limits of its type."},"met:437376":{"d":"A cat makes off with fish in a large still life, about 97 by 128 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in the late 1660s by Giuseppe Recco, a member of a family of Neapolitan still-life painters. The Met's keywords record the cat, the fish and the genre, so the scene is a thief caught in the act, a comic episode set within a painting of glistening fish.","i":"Naples was one of the great centers of seventeenth-century still life, and the Recco family specialized in fish, shells, fruit and flowers. A cat stealing fish combines still life with a small comic narrative drawn from daily life and from a long tradition of kitchen and market scenes. The thieving cat, an opportunist, was a familiar type, and viewers enjoyed the joke as well as the display of skill in painting scales, shells and fur.","c":"A cat stealing fish is a still life with a plot, and the plot is the joke: the animal's furtive grace set against cold, glistening bodies. The Neapolitan taste for drama appears even in the kitchen, and the picture gains energy from the thief's movement across an otherwise static arrangement. It is lighter in spirit than Recco's more solemn still lifes and a good example of the market-stall comedy that Neapolitan painters enjoyed."},"nga:46063":{"d":"A bearded old man is seen from the waist up, holding a tablet-like object in his left hand and a stylus or brush in his right. He has pale skin, a long white beard and mustache and a black cap, and he wears a dark garment with a gold chain at his neck. At the left stands a white statue of a nude woman on a rectangular pedestal; the rest of the background is dark. The canvas is about 112 by 94 centimeters.","i":"The man is Titian, imagined by Pietro della Vecchia about seventy-five years after the great painter's death in 1576; the gold chain recalls Titian's honors, and the long beard and black cap follow his real self-portraits. The statue suggests the classical ideal and the old debate about whether painting or sculpture was the nobler art. Della Vecchia was famed for imitating the old Venetian masters, and this homage to Titian is a tribute rather than a likeness.","c":"A pastiche, but a lively one: Della Vecchia was the specialist in dressing up the past, and the white statue against the dark ground is the best touch. The face is plausible but generalized, and the picture is more costume than revelation. Its interest is as evidence of how powerfully Titian's myth lived on in the Baroque, and as a good example of Della Vecchia's taste for historical fantasy."},"met:441755":{"d":"A small oil sketch on cardboard, about 15 by 23 centimeters, records a landscape under clouds. The sky is a major part of the subject, as the title says. Dated about 1829 to 1831, it probably comes from the period when Loubon, a young painter from Provence, was in Italy and sketching outdoors, a practice that encouraged quick, direct notes of light.","i":"Oil sketches made outdoors became a respected habit in the early 1800s, after French landscapists in Rome, following the teaching of Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes, made quick studies of clouds and light to train the eye. Such sketches were not meant for exhibition but were kept in the studio as reference and personal record. Loubon later became a leading painter of Provençal landscape and directed the art school in Marseille.","c":"The strongest sketches of this kind capture an effect rather than a place, and the cloud study belongs to the tradition that includes the open-air notes of Corot and Constable. The small size and cardboard support leave no room for finish; their value is immediacy. It is a minor but telling document of a young painter learning to see the sky."},"met:436793":{"d":"Mary nurses the Christ Child at a table set with fruit, a glass bottle and glasses, and a knife, in a small oil-on-wood panel about 43 by 32 centimeters painted about 1512 to 1513 by Joos van Cleve. The Met's keywords record grapes among the fruit, and the title says the Holy Family, so Joseph may share the scene. The table's still life is as much a feature of the picture as the figures.","i":"Joos van Cleve, who worked in Antwerp, is famous for Holy Families whose domestic still life carries meaning: grapes allude to wine and the Eucharist, and a knife may hint at the sacrifice to come. Such details turn a family meal into a quiet prefiguration of the Passion, the hidden symbolism of Netherlandish painting. The nursing motif stresses Christ's humanity and Mary's tenderness.","c":"Joos gives the Madonna the warmth of a household: a nursing mother, a table laid with fruit, glass that catches the light. The picture is compact, meticulous and early in his career. The risk of symbolic objects is that they can read as a catalog, but here they are woven into a convincing domestic scene, and the freshness of the glass and fruit is the best part. A small, humane, attractive picture."},"nga:46435":{"d":"A half-length portrait, with an arched top, of a clean-shaven man in black. His body is angled to the viewer's right, but he glances back toward the viewer with slightly drooping hazel-green eyes. A wide black hat sits cocked to one side over ear-length brown hair, and he wears a voluminous black coat with a broad fur collar. He pulls a bone-white glove onto one hand; on the bare hand is a gold ring with a green gem. The green ground glows behind his shoulders. Oil on panel, probably 1518.","i":"The panel is a pendant to the portrait of Margaretha Boghe: matching in size, shape and green ground, the two were made to hang side by side, and he turns toward her side. The black cloth and fur announce a wealthy burgher, and the glove and ring were gentleman's accessories. Pairs of this kind often marked a marriage, so the occasion is probably their wedding, although that is an inference from the format and not a recorded fact.","c":"A strong pendant. The near-monochrome costume sets off a pale face, the bright glove and the ring, while the sideways glance gives life that many formal portraits lack. The clothing is rendered with a confidence that shows how the sitter wished to be seen. Joos van Cleve lacks Quinten Massys's invention, but as a portraitist of Antwerp's merchant elite he is consistently convincing for crisp finish and direct presence."},"nga:46436":{"d":"A woman in a peacock-blue dress and an ivory-white headdress, shown from the hips up and turned to the viewer's left, looks off in that direction with gray eyes. Narrow panels of the headdress frame her heart-shaped face, and a veil falls down her back. The gown is lined with gray fur at the neckline, front and sleeves. She holds a single pink carnation in one hand and, in the other, a string of coral-red beads, with a gold ring on her finger. The green ground glows behind her and darkens at the arched top. Oil on panel, probably 1518.","i":"Pendant to the portrait of Joris Vezeleer. The carnation, or pink, is the flower of love and marriage in Netherlandish portraits, which makes a betrothal or wedding the likely occasion. The red beads may be a rosary or costly jewelry; the fur-lined gown and gold ring state wealth either way. Like many portraits of wives, the picture is as much a public statement of a family's standing as a likeness.","c":"Joos van Cleve delights in textures here: gray fur against peacock blue, sheer fabric over skin, the hard glint of the ring. The face is quietly observed and composed rather than flattered, which suits a formal commission. Her panel is the richer in color, while his is the more arresting in attitude, and the pair show why Antwerp's wealthy families sought him out."},"met:436792":{"d":"A triptych with gilded, arch-shaped frames. In the central panel Christ hangs on a cross under a cloudy blue sky, his pale body framed by a billowing loincloth. The Virgin in dark blue and a white veil stands at the left with hands pressed together; at the foot of the cross an old bearded man in blue-green kneels, and a kneeling donor in black and a youthful saint in red look on, with a skull and bones in front. Behind spreads a wide landscape of rocks, rivers, a castle and distant mountains. On the left wing stand Saint John the Baptist, holding a lamb, and Saint Catherine, with a sword and a wheel at her feet. On the right wing two friars, one in grey and one in black, hold small dishes. Oil on wood.","i":"Joos van Cleve worked in Antwerp, the booming port city of the early sixteenth century, and ran a large workshop that produced altarpieces, Madonnas and portraits. In this triptych, probably made for private devotion about 1520, the donor in black kneels at the foot of the cross, close to the sacrifice he prays to, while the wings bring in protector saints: John the Baptist with his lamb, Catherine with the wheel and sword of her martyrdom, and two friars. Having the donor share the sacred scene was a convention of the age. The wide panoramic landscape recalls Joachim Patinir, the pioneer of such backgrounds in Netherlandish painting.","c":"A refined, clear-eyed small altarpiece. The landscape is the joy: the blue distance, the cloud-flecked sky and the fine detail of rocks and castle are painted with delicate precision. The figures, by contrast, are somewhat stiff and generic, the faces of the saints closely related, and the drama muted, in line with the efficient output of Joos's busy workshop. Beside the intense Crucifixions of the previous generation it is serene rather than searing. For a private chapel or home that was probably what was wanted: an image of the Passion that could be contemplated without distress."},"met:436791":{"d":"The archangel Gabriel announces to Mary that she will bear the Son of God, in a bedroom with a bed and a pillow, while the dove of the Holy Spirit appears. The oil-on-wood panel, about 86 by 80 centimeters, was painted about 1525 by Joos van Cleve. The domestic setting follows the Netherlandish tradition of placing the sacred event in a recognizable room.","i":"In Netherlandish Annunciations, objects in the room become symbols: the bed and chamber speak of Mary's private life and purity, and the dove marks the descent of the Holy Spirit. The Annunciation is the moment of the Incarnation, the beginning of Christ's life on earth, and was among the most popular of devotional subjects. By 1525 Joos van Cleve was a prosperous master in Antwerp with a busy workshop.","c":"Joos favors a warm, sober interior and a gentle encounter over the drama of Italian Annunciations, and this picture succeeds in its domestic intimacy and rich surfaces. The figures are less distinctive than the setting, and the gestures are conventional, but the blend of Netherlandish tradition and Italianate detail typical of Antwerp is well handled. A solid, attractive example of what a good Antwerp workshop could produce."},"met:436794":{"d":"Christ appears as judge above a crowd of nude men and women, with angels and putti attending him, in an upright oil-on-wood panel about 124 by 86 centimeters painted about 1525 to 1530 by Joos van Cleve. The Last Judgment, normally spread over a large triptych, is here compressed into a single panel, and the putti and nudes show the influence of Italian art on Antwerp painters of the 1520s.","i":"The Last Judgment is the end of time, when Christ separates the saved from the damned, as Matthew's Gospel describes, and it was a favorite subject of Netherlandish altarpieces. The nudity of the dead is a reminder of equality before judgment, and the cherubic putti belong to the Italian Renaissance vocabulary that northern painters were adopting. Pictures of this kind warned viewers to live well while there was time.","c":"A Last Judgment is a test of design, since dozens of nude figures must be organized without chaos. Joos is better at tenderness than at tumult, and the Italianate nudes are conscientious rather than inspired, but the compactness has its pleasures. It lacks the terror of Bosch and the power of Michelangelo, as it must, and stands as a good, orderly image of the type from a mid-1520s Antwerp workshop."},"met:436795":{"d":"Mary nurses the Christ Child in a panel about 72 by 54 centimeters, painted about 1525 by Joos van Cleve, with apples and grapes among the fruit shown. The nursing Virgin is tender and domestic, and the fruit gives the picture a second, symbolic subject: fruits that carry meanings from the Fall to the Eucharist. The oil-on-wood panel is of a size suited to a private room, where such pictures were hung for prayer.","i":"Apples recall the Fall, and the Child's association with them presents Christ as the second Adam, who redeems what the first brought; grapes allude to wine and the Eucharist, the blood of Christ. Such symbols are typical of Netherlandish devotion. Joos van Cleve ran a busy Antwerp workshop and made many Madonnas, and his nursing Madonnas were popular for private devotion.","c":"Joos's Madonnas are among the warmest in Netherlandish painting, and this one is representative: a sweet, rounded mother, a plump Child, fruit that glows. The formula recurs across his output, so the pleasure is of familiarity rather than discovery, and the soft modeling reflects the example of Leonardo. A charming, accomplished devotional picture, more comforting than searching."},"nga:59901":{"d":"A child stands upright, facing forward, in a white dress with lace details and a pink ribbon, white lace-trimmed pants, blue shoes and a red necklace with a heart-shaped pendant. She has fair skin, rounded cheeks and short reddish-brown hair, and holds a light pink rose in one hand and a small black drawstring purse, a reticule, in the other. The background shades from dark to light green, and the floor carries a floral carpet. The canvas is about 119 by 75 centimeters.","i":"The reticule and the rose are accessories of a little lady: the child is dressed as a miniature adult, in the fashion of the 1840s, and the lace and jewelry show that her family could afford them. The heart pendant is a sentimental touch. Joseph Whiting Stock was a self-taught portraitist from Springfield, Massachusetts, disabled from boyhood and said to have worked from a specially designed wheelchair, who painted hundreds of portraits of ordinary New England sitters.","c":"The picture's pleasure is in the child's straight frontal stance and the little attributes arranged like emblems: the rose, the purse, the heart. The costume is rendered with more care than the face, a common trait of itinerant portraits, and the modeling is thin. Yet the green gradient and the carpet give it a clear design, and the effect is sweet and decorative. As art it is naive in modeling and strong in pattern."},"nga:59916":{"d":"A baby lies in a wicker cradle, with a round face, light skin, large eyes and sparse light hair, wearing a blue dress and red fabric on its feet. The baby holds a small object in its left hand, and behind its head is a white pillow, with dark brown fabric underneath. The background is dark, with a patterned floor in red and pink floral designs. The canvas is about 77 by 66 centimeters, painted about 1840.","i":"Portraits of infants were a specialty of itinerant American painters, and in a period of high infant mortality a portrait fixed a fleeting stage of life that a family might not otherwise have kept. The wicker cradle, the small toy and the patterned floor are the props of a studio picture. Joseph Whiting Stock painted hundreds of portraits, many of children, in New England.","c":"Stock handles the pattern of floor and cradle more convincingly than the baby's anatomy, but the large eyes give the sitter an appealing alertness, and the small object in the hand is a touch of life. The composition is a simple infant icon in wicker, and the dark ground sets the pale child off. As folk portraiture it is direct, unaffected and rather tender, and does what such pictures were meant to do."},"met:12644":{"d":"A boy and a girl appear in a large double portrait, about 128 by 102 centimeters, painted in 1845 by Joseph Whiting Stock, with a book among the props. The title gives their names, John and Louisa Stock, and since they share the painter's surname they were presumably members of his family. The canvas is of a size that gave the children real presence, and the book is the picture's one conspicuous prop.","i":"A book in a child's portrait signaled education and respectability in a country that prized literacy, and it often stood for the family's hopes for the child. Siblings were commonly painted together to record the family, and Stock, who was disabled from boyhood and worked in Springfield, Massachusetts, kept a detailed account of his portraits. A picture of children bearing his own name may have given him more freedom than a client's commission.","c":"As a family picture by a self-taught painter, it has the directness that formal portraits often lack: the children simply look out, with the book as their accessory. The treatment is plain, the modeling thin, and the formula familiar from Stock's other work, but the larger scale allows more presence. It is a modest, honest portrait whose best quality is its straightforwardness."},"nga:45956":{"d":"Two young children stand side by side: the one on the left, with dark brown hair in waves, extends one arm, in a blue dress, white stockings and shoes and a red beaded necklace, while the one on the right, in a green dress and black shoes, has light brown hair and holds a string. At their feet are a toy doll in a red cradle and a small toy house. Behind are a dark wall, deep red drapery, a patterned carpet and a small table with a box. The canvas is about 122 by 102 centimeters.","i":"The title names Mary and Francis Wilcox, a girl and a boy, though both wear dresses: boys were dressed in small children's dresses until they were breeched, put into trousers, at about five or six. The toys, a doll in a cradle, a toy house and a string for a pull toy, belong to play. Joseph Whiting Stock painted the pair in 1845 in the formula he used for children: two figures, drapery and a patterned carpet.","c":"Stock's formula is clear here: two figures side by side, red drapery, a bright carpet, toys at their feet. The children's stiffness is part of the charm, and the toys give the picture a small narrative. As a document of 1840s childhood, in dress and play, it is richly informative; as painting it is naive and robust, with strong color and simple forms. The doll in its tiny cradle is the most affecting detail."},"met:12773":{"d":"A landscape with trees carries a title about the return of the redwing, a blackbird whose arrival is among the first signs of spring in the northeastern United States. With trees and landscape the only features named, the bird, if shown, is a minor detail and the real subject is the season's mood. The oil on wood panel, about 58 by 81 centimeters, is dated tentatively to about 1900.","i":"Talcott is associated with the Old Lyme art colony in Connecticut, whose painters in the early 1900s combined Tonalism, with its hushed, low-keyed mood, and Impressionism's attention to weather and light. A title like Return of the Redwing points to the Tonalist taste for seasonal feeling over topography: the picture is less a view of a place than a state of mind belonging to the first days of spring.","c":"Talcott died at forty-one, leaving a small body of work, and his soft, atmospheric landscapes belong to the Tonalist taste and the American Barbizon tradition. This is a quiet picture whose appeal is the feeling of early spring rather than anything dramatic. The restraint is both a virtue and a limit: his pictures are pleasant, but they seldom surprise. At its best the mood is gently convincing."},"nga:46118":{"d":"Three people stand or kneel around a cross from which hangs the twisted, bloodied body of a nearly nude man, set in a dark, rocky landscape. His white skin is tinted green and covered with short red gashes; he wears a ring of thorns, and his body hangs heavily from nailed hands, his head down, eyes and mouth open, his feet coiled around a nail. At right a man in crimson twists his clasped fingers in prayer, at left a woman bows her covered head, and another kneels with her mouth wide open. A dark disk covers the moon in the night sky.","i":"The mourners are the Virgin, Saint John and Mary Magdalen, and the dark disk over the moon recalls the darkness that fell over the land at the Crucifixion. Grünewald painted this small panel about the time of his Isenheim altarpiece, whose Crucifixion shows Christ's agony in a similar unsparing way. The greenish flesh and wounds were meant to console suffering viewers by showing that Christ shared the worst of human pain, and the panel was probably used for private devotion.","c":"One of the most unsparing Crucifixions in Western art: the greenish flesh, the gashes, the exaggerated hands, a design that refuses beauty to make the viewer feel. The three mourners are bound to the cross by their gestures, each a different form of grief, and the eclipsed moon deepens the gloom. The risk is that horror overwhelms, but that is the point. Few images of Christ's suffering are so hard to forget."},"nga:157928":{"d":"A coastal scene overlooks a quiet shore: an open sandy expanse leads to blue water, and across the water at the left lies another shoreline with small white and brown buildings with pointed roofs, arched doors and small chimneys. The sky is the same bright blue as the water, and at the right the faint outline of a mountain range appears in light purple. The brushstrokes are soft and loose, especially in the shore, water and sky. The panel is about 25 by 32 centimeters.","i":"Redon is best known for his dreamlike noirs, the charcoal drawings and lithographs of eyes, monsters and imagined beings that he made in the 1870s and 1880s, but he also painted small landscapes from nature, often for himself. Brittany attracted painters for its coast and villages, and a picture like this, about 1880, shows the observer behind the visionary. The loose brushwork and clear color belong to a different side of his art.","c":"An unassuming, fresh sketch: pale blue water, white and brown houses, a haze of mountains, all in a few touches. Redon here is an observer, and the picture is quiet, but it shows the color sense that would flower in his pastels. The scale is a study's, not a finished statement. It is a pleasing reminder that the painter of the strangest visions also loved plain light and a village by the sea."},"nga:89684":{"d":"We look across an ochre-yellow field to a band of pearl-white buildings under an azure sky, in a horizontal landscape with the horizon about halfway up. Flecks of red in the lower right could be flowers, some buildings have tan or silvery-gray roofs and faces in blue shadow, and a few round white clouds cross the blue. The scene is painted with visible, textured brushstrokes throughout, and the artist signed it in the lower left corner. The canvas is about 23 by 33 centimeters.","i":"Redon, who had made his name with the dark, imaginative noirs, turned toward color in the 1890s, and small Breton landscapes show the shift: strong, simple planes of yellow, white and blue. A village seen across a field is a modest subject, but it let him explore light for its own sake. The title places the scene in Brittany, which Redon visited repeatedly and which offered the sea-washed light painters sought.","c":"This is reduction: three bands, yellow, white and blue, and the picture is done. The effect is of intense sunlight, and the textured strokes keep the plain design from looking flat. The flecks of red are an inspired accent. It is close in spirit to the simplified color planes of the Nabis and anticipates the Fauves, though it is a small, private picture and not a manifesto. A lovely small piece of pure color."},"met:10062":{"d":"A man works in a chemical laboratory, in a canvas about 91 by 76 centimeters painted about 1885 to 1887 by Henry Alexander. The Met's keywords record the interior, chemistry, work and a man, so the picture is a portrait of a scientist in his workplace rather than a posed likeness, surrounded by the instruments of his trade. Such a scene sits between portraiture and genre, since the sitter is shown doing what he is known for.","i":"In the 1880s science and its laboratories were an emblem of modern progress, and artists found in the chemist at work a new kind of subject that echoed older pictures of alchemists and scholars in their studies. A portrait in a workplace honors the sitter's profession as well as his face. Alexander, born in 1860, died at only thirty-four, so this work comes from a short career.","c":"The picture's value lies in its subject: a modern professional shown at work, not posed in a drawing room, which gives the painting a documentary freshness. The dim interior and the apparatus offer the painter chances to show texture and light, and the figure is allowed to be absorbed. Whether Alexander gives the sitter more than competence is hard to say, but the idea is strong, and the early death makes his work all the more worth looking at."},"met:436911":{"d":"The patriarch Abraham appears as a single figure against a gold ground in a tall panel of tempera on wood, about 66 by 43 centimeters, painted about 1408 to 1410 by Lorenzo Monaco. The panel is one of four Old Testament figures by the artist in the Met, and the shared date and medium suggest that they came from the same altarpiece, where they would have stood as witnesses in an upper tier.","i":"Abraham, the father of faith whom God called to leave his home and later tested by asking him to sacrifice his son Isaac, opens Matthew's genealogy of Christ. In Christian reading, the sacrifice that God spared at the last moment foretold the sacrifice of Christ, which is why Old Testament figures stood in the upper parts of altarpieces: they were witnesses who foretold the story told below. Lorenzo Monaco was a Camaldolese monk in Florence.","c":"Lorenzo Monaco's saints and patriarchs sway with a courtly rhythm: long curved contours, flowing drapery, fine gold. The pleasure is of line and color more than of weight or space, and Abraham here is a figure of calm dignity, simplified and elongated, set against a gold ground that denies depth. It is the late Gothic taste at its most refined, standing on the brink of a Renaissance realism that Lorenzo largely ignored."},"met:436912":{"d":"King David appears with a musical instrument in a panel of tempera on wood with a gold ground, about 57 by 43 centimeters, painted about 1408 to 1410 by Lorenzo Monaco. The instrument, which identifies him as the psalmist, is the one attribute the Met's keywords record. It is one of four Old Testament figures by the artist in the museum, apparently from a single altarpiece.","i":"David, shepherd, king and traditional author of the Psalms, is shown with a harp or psaltery because the Psalms were songs. Christians read the Psalms as prophecies of Christ, and David's descent made him an ancestor of Jesus, the son of David. In an altarpiece his place among the Old Testament figures marks him as a witness to the coming of Christ. Lorenzo Monaco, who also illuminated choir books, knew well the place of music in worship.","c":"A harp gives the painter a chance for a graceful gesture and a curve, and Lorenzo Monaco's gift for rhythm suits a musician. The figure is more decorative than psychological, and in the gold ground nothing hints at the shepherd or the warrior; the effect is of lyric calm. Like its siblings it belongs to a world in which saints and kings were seen as eternal rather than historical, and its charm is that of line and gold."},"met:436913":{"d":"Moses sits as an old, bearded man with a halo, holding the tablets of the Law, in a panel of tempera on wood with a gold ground, about 62 by 45 centimeters, painted about 1408 to 1410 by Lorenzo Monaco. The Met's keywords record the tablets, the seat, a robe and an overcoat, so he is a seated, robed figure. The panel is one of four Old Testament figures by the artist in the museum.","i":"Moses received the Ten Commandments on Mount Sinai and led the Israelites out of Egypt; in Christian reading the Law he delivered was fulfilled by Christ, and Moses stands as the great lawgiver who prepared the way. Prophets and lawgivers were placed in the upper tiers of altarpieces to testify to the truth of the story below, and the halo marks him as a holy figure.","c":"Moses is the one panel in the group with a clear emblem, the tablets, and the picture is accordingly more legible: a seated old man, a halo, the stone slabs. Lorenzo Monaco gives him the gentle gravity of a Gothic sage rather than the terror of Michelangelo's later Moses, and the effect is serene. The gold ground flattens everything, so his authority is symbolic rather than physical."},"met:436914":{"d":"Noah, the patriarch of the Flood, stands in a panel of tempera on wood with a gold ground, about 66 by 44 centimeters, painted about 1408 to 1410 by Lorenzo Monaco. The Met's keywords record only a man and the name, so the picture is a single standing figure on gold. It is one of four Old Testament figures by the artist in the museum.","i":"Noah built the ark at God's command and survived the Flood with his family and the animals; Christians saw in the ark a figure of the Church, which carries the faithful through the waters of death, and in Noah a forerunner of Christ. His appearance among prophets in an altarpiece reminded viewers that salvation had been promised from the beginning of time. Noah is also the first vintner in Genesis, a detail some read as pointing to the wine of the Eucharist.","c":"Four patriarchs on gold invite comparison, and Noah is perhaps the quietest: no tablets, no instrument, just a dignified figure whose identity depends on the label. That is a limit of the type, since without attributes an old man is simply an old man, and the interest rests on the rhythm of the drapery and the glow of the gold. A refined but rather anonymous presence."},"nga:12111":{"d":"A seated Madonna supports the Christ Child as he stands on her knee, both against a gold ground in a tall, pointed-arch panel about 114 by 53 centimeters. Her midnight-blue robe, lined with yellow and decorated with gold stars, pools on the floor, and a sky-blue scarf loops across her shoulders, one end held by the Child, who carries a scroll lettered EGO S. The mint-green floor bears gold lettering that begins with Ave and gives the date in Roman numerals as 1413. The gold is cracked with age.","i":"Lorenzo Monaco, a Camaldolese monk of Santa Maria degli Angeli in Florence, painted this in 1413, the date written on the floor. The Ave recalls the angel Gabriel's greeting to Mary, Ave gratia plena, so the picture joins Madonna and Annunciation; the scroll's EGO S probably opens one of Christ's declarations, such as I am the light of the world. The gold stars mark her as Queen of Heaven, and the scarf links mother and child in a gesture of tenderness.","c":"This is the International Gothic at its most graceful: sweeping robes, gold stars, a loop of blue scarf that ties mother to child. The tenderness is courtly rather than earthy, and Lorenzo's line is a pleasure in itself. Within a few years Masaccio would give painting weight and space; this panel belongs to the world just before, where beauty meant pattern and gold. The cracks in the gold only add to its air of precious antiquity."},"met:12310":{"d":"A study of a dead bird, painted in oil on canvas, about 51 by 38 centimeters, in 1878 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the bird and the still-life genre, and the title in the plural suggests several views of the bird on one canvas, in the manner of an artist's study sheet rather than a finished picture.","i":"Painting directly from a still life, such as a dead bird or a fish, was a typical exercise in the atelier of Carolus-Duran, where Sargent studied, meant to teach students to see tone and color and to lay on paint with confidence. Dead game was also a long tradition of still life. Sargent's studies of 1878, when he was twenty-two, belong to the discipline of an apprentice who would soon become a famous portraitist.","c":"Everything in such a study depends on quick, exact observation: the soft feathers, the pale light, the small, limp weight. At twenty-two Sargent already paints with remarkable authority, and the canvas is a good document of how he trained his eye. It is a study, not a picture; there is no composition to speak of, and that lack of ambition is its honesty. A small, vivid lesson in looking."},"nga:166472":{"d":"Four women and two children carry baskets along a beach under a brilliant blue sky, walking to our left among shallow pools that mirror the sky. The women wear long-sleeved shirts, calf-length skirts, aprons, head coverings and gray clogs, and a barefoot child in a golden yellow hat carries a basket beside a young woman in a white kerchief. An older woman in an aquamarine apron stops to gaze at a second child, and at the upper right a dune leads to a hazy lighthouse. The canvas, about 79 by 123 centimeters, is signed Paris 1878.","i":"The women are shellfish gatherers from Cancale in Brittany, a town famous for its oysters, heading out at low tide to fish on foot. Sargent visited Cancale in 1877 and painted its fishing life the next year, and the inscription Paris 1878 suggests this version was finished in the studio, presumably from open-air studies made on the beach. The picture reflects his training under Carolus-Duran and an Impressionist-influenced attention to light.","c":"The performance is remarkable for a painter of twenty-two: the shimmering sand, the sun in the children's hair and the shine on the pools show a bravura that already looks beyond the dark studio manner of his training. The procession across the beach is clear and well paced, and the working figures are treated with dignity rather than quaintness. Among his freshest early works, and an unexpected taste of plein-air painting from a future portraitist."},"nga:97063":{"d":"A woman with reddish-auburn hair stands full-length on a grassy lawn, turning her heavy-lidded hazel eyes toward us. She wears a long black silk dress with a fitted bodice, black lace sleeves and layers of ruffles at the hem, with a flaring white bow at the neck and a tomato-red flower or corsage at the shoulder. She lifts her skirt with both hands to reveal white layers beneath in an S-shaped curve, and wears jeweled rings and sparkling earrings. The lawn behind her fades to a distant balustrade. The canvas is about 211 by 104 centimeters.","i":"The sitter was the wife of the playwright Édouard Pailleron and the daughter of the founder of the Revue des Deux Mondes. Sargent inscribed the picture Ronjoux 1879, naming what was presumably the family's country house, where he painted several members of the family. A full-length standing portrait in a garden followed the grand tradition of Van Dyck and Velázquez, whom Sargent had studied, and showed a woman of fashion in a natural but elegant setting.","c":"A young Sargent shows that he can carry a full-length: the black dress against the green lawn is a bold, simple contrast, and the S-curve of white ruffles gives the figure movement. The lawn is almost empty, which concentrates attention on the figure, though it also leaves the picture rather sparse. The sitter's heavy-lidded gaze gives her presence, and the dress is painted with a brilliance that foreshadows his famous society portraits."},"met:12038":{"d":"A small wood panel, about 35 by 27 centimeters, painted by John Singer Sargent in 1879 to 1880 and catalogued with two images: The Balcony, Spain, and Two Nude Bathers Standing on a Wharf, presumably one on each side of the panel. The first is a glimpse of Spanish architecture, the second a study of male nudes by water. The Met's keywords record bathing and the nude, so the bathers are the clearly recorded subject.","i":"In 1879 to 1880 Sargent traveled in Spain and Morocco, studying Velázquez at the Prado and filling small wooden panels with quick oil sketches of what he saw. Painters often reused a panel, so the two subjects may come from different moments, and the male bathers recall his academic training in the nude. The balcony belongs to the Spanish journey that later fed his famous El Jaleo of 1882.","c":"Sketches are judged by speed and truth, and the two subjects show the range of Sargent's apprenticeship: a balcony caught in a few dashes of light and shade, and two nude figures by water that testify to the academic discipline behind his later virtuosity. Neither is a finished work, and neither pretends to be. The pleasure lies in the confident touch, which is already unmistakably his."},"met:12065":{"d":"A small sketch of a courtyard in Tétouan, a city in northern Morocco, painted in oil on wood, about 26 by 35 centimeters, in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record only the courtyard and the architecture, so the subject is a space of walls, openings and light, with no figures mentioned. At this size it is a sketch made at the scene, a note of a place and its light rather than a composed picture.","i":"Sargent visited Morocco in 1879 to 1880, and Tétouan, with its whitewashed houses, arched doorways and enclosed courtyards, gave him architecture and light that were new to a painter trained in Paris. European painters of the time often used North Africa as an exotic stage for costume and drama, but Sargent's small panels concentrate on buildings and sunlight, in a plain, observational spirit.","c":"At this scale a courtyard becomes a problem of planes and shadow: white walls take the light, arches make dark notes, and the painter must decide how little he can say. Sargent says it with great economy. The panel has the freshness of a first impression, and the lack of figures turns the place into the subject. It is a modest sketch, but it shows how quickly and surely he could organize light."},"met:12136":{"d":"A sunlit view of Moorish buildings, painted in oil on a small wood panel, about 26 by 35 centimeters, in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent on his journey through Spain and Morocco. The title states the subject and the effect: walls and roofs under strong sun. It is the companion in spirit, and perhaps in fact, of Moorish Buildings on a Cloudy Day, a panel of nearly identical size.","i":"Sargent was fascinated by how strong sun makes white walls glow and throws their shadows into sharp relief, an effect he would later explore in his watercolors of Venice and the Mediterranean. Painting the same kind of subject in different weather was an Impressionist habit, and the pairing with a cloudy-day panel suggests that Sargent was studying light as much as architecture.","c":"A sun-struck wall is the great test of a painter's judgment: too much white and the picture burns, too little and the sun disappears. Sargent keeps the white from glaring by letting shadows and a few cool notes carry the contrast. As a sketch it is brisk and certain, and the confidence of such small things explains why his larger pictures later seem so effortless."},"met:12137":{"d":"Moorish buildings under cloud, painted in oil on a small wood panel, about 26 by 35 centimeters, in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record only buildings, and the title adds the weather: the same kind of architecture as in its companion panel, seen without strong sun. The effect is of muted light and cooler, quieter color.","i":"A cloudy day softens shadows and cools colors, and a painter who records it is studying the effect of weather, a pursuit shared by the Impressionists. Sargent's Moroccan panels were private sketches, made for himself, and keeping both a sunlit and a cloudy version suggests how he used travel to train his eye. North Africa attracted European painters for its light and architecture, though the weather here is quiet rather than exotic.","c":"Gray light is harder than sunlight to make interesting, since the drama of contrast is gone, and the success of the cloudy panel is its subtlety: the buildings hold together by tone alone. It is the quieter of the pair, a sketch in a low key that rewards patience. Few young painters would choose to record such a dull day, and that Sargent did shows an appetite for how things look rather than how they impress."},"met:12149":{"d":"A vertical sketch of a doorway in Morocco, painted in oil on a small wood panel, about 35 by 26 centimeters, in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the doorway, the portal and the country, so the picture is a study of a threshold: a dark opening set into a wall, the sort of motif that fascinated painters of North Africa.","i":"Doorways and portals were favorite motifs of painters traveling in Morocco, where arched openings in thick whitewashed walls made dramatic frames of dark against light. For Sargent in 1879 to 1880, a vertical panel gave a different rhythm from his horizontal views of buildings. A door is also a suggestive subject, an invitation to look further, and the lack of figures leaves the viewer on the threshold.","c":"A doorway is an economical subject: a dark shape in a pale wall, and the problem is to make the dark feel deep rather than black. Sargent solves it with quick, warm touches, and the vertical format emphasizes the opening's depth. It is slight, but its confidence and its refusal to add figures or anecdote make it one of the purest of these little panels."},"met:12302":{"d":"A small landscape with trees, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 26 by 35 centimeters, in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record a tree and the landscape genre, so the subject is a simple stretch of Spanish country, set down in quick strokes, presumably outdoors, as a private note of a place rather than an exhibition picture.","i":"Sargent's journey to Spain in 1879 was devoted mostly to painting in museums and cities, but he also noted the dry, sunlit countryside, with its tough trees and bare hills, which looked quite different from the lush landscapes of northern France where he had trained. Such small panels were private notes of places and light, made for himself rather than for sale or exhibition.","c":"Landscape was never Sargent's field, and this panel does not aim to compete with the specialists; but a good sketch needs only a clear idea of light and mass, and he has both. The tree gives a single strong vertical against the dry ground and sky. It is a slight, honest note from a trip, interesting for the freshness of the touch and for showing a portraitist at ease outdoors."},"met:12303":{"d":"A small wood panel, about 35 by 26 centimeters, painted in oil in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent, shows a Madonna as seen in Spain. The Met's keywords record Mary and a woman, so the picture may record a devotional image or a woman posed as the Virgin, quickly set down. At this scale it is a sketch of a type, with the devotional gravity of Spanish religious art.","i":"On his trip through Spain in 1879 to 1880 Sargent studied the great Spanish painters, above all Velázquez, and observed the country's popular devotion, from processions to images of the Virgin in churches and roadside shrines. Spanish Madonnas, whether painted or carved, have a solemn, direct, often dark-toned presence. A sketch like this shows the young painter absorbing that tone, which would reappear in El Jaleo and in his religious work decades later.","c":"Sargent was not a religious painter by temperament, and this panel reads as an observation of a Spanish type more than an act of devotion. The strength is in its gravity and economy: a few dark strokes make a presence. It is a small thing, but it shows the lifelong sympathy he had with Spanish painting, and with its sober, unsentimental way of treating the sacred."},"met:12470":{"d":"A study of white walls in strong sun, painted in oil on a small wood panel, about 26 by 35 centimeters, in 1879 to 1880 by John Singer Sargent in Morocco. The title leaves no doubt that the subject is light on plain surfaces: sunlit planes of whitewash with their shadows, with no other incident recorded. It is one of several small Moroccan panels by Sargent in the Met.","i":"White is the hardest color to paint in sunlight, because it is never really white: it takes the warm tint of the sun on its lit side and the cool tint of the sky in its shadows. Sargent's attention to this in Morocco anticipates the watercolors of white marble, sails and tents that he made in later decades. In 1879 to 1880 he was twenty-three, on a journey that took him away from his studio training.","c":"A wall of white is a daring subject for a young painter, because the picture has almost nothing but light: warm lights, cool shadows, a few changes of plane. Sargent makes those changes enough. The panel is nearly abstract, and its beauty is the temperature of the whites. It is a private note, not a finished picture, but it shows that he could think about light and not only about people."},"nga:46428":{"d":"A slight young woman in a black fringed shawl and a long cream skirt walks toward the viewer along a pale, nearly empty alley, her eyes lowered and her lips closed. A stroke of magenta at her neck and a touch of coral in her hair are the only bright notes. At right, two men in dark coats and hats stand against a wall, one watching her, the other turned toward his companion with his face in shadow. Far down the lane two small figures sit at a table. The buildings are ivory and peach, with green shutters, and the paint is laid on roughly to suggest worn stucco. The panel is small and is signed in dark paint at lower right.","i":"In the early 1880s Sargent worked in Venice, where he avoided the usual views of palaces and canals and painted back streets, interiors and the working women who lived in them. The dark figures set against a pale ground recall Velázquez, whom he had copied in Madrid. The woman may be a Venetian model, perhaps Gigia Viani, though that identification is not certain. Whether the picture records a real encounter or stages one is unknown. It is often read as a study of a woman passing the stares of watching men, but Sargent left no explanation, so that reading remains an interpretation.","c":"Sargent's economy is the pleasure here. A few dark notes against pale stucco do all the work, the ragged fringe of the shawl suggests motion, and the empty street sets the figure off like a stage. The men are summary, almost silhouettes, and the buildings dissolve into rough dabs, so the picture reads as a dashing sketch rather than a resolved statement. Among his Venetian street scenes it has the strongest story, because the unspoken exchange between the woman and the watchers is left open."},"nga:96999":{"d":"A young girl, shown from the hips up, holds a shaggy terrier against her hip as she looks at us. She wears an ink-black dress with long, tight sleeves, a layered cream-white lace collar, a wide red sash and a multi-stranded coral-red necklace, and a long white cuff falls over her wrist. Her chestnut hair falls in thick bangs to below her shoulders, and one faint brow is slightly cocked over large, pale blue eyes. The background shades from fawn to golden brown. The canvas is about 79 by 58 centimeters.","i":"The sitter was Eleanor Beatrice Townsend, and the cursive inscription, to my friend Mrs Townsend, shows that Sargent gave the portrait to her mother, a friend. He painted it in 1882, the year of his Daughters of Edward Darley Boit, when he was establishing himself in Paris as a painter of striking portraits. The terrier is more than an accessory: its shaggy liveliness plays against the girl's composure.","c":"A child portrait without sweetness: the cocked brow and level gaze give the girl a self-possession that sentimental portraits avoid, and the red sash and necklace punch through the black like a drumbeat. The dog is brushed in with a few free strokes, and the hand holding its tail is a charming bit of truth that keeps the picture from stiffness. Painted at twenty-six, it is already a sophisticated and economical piece of portraiture."},"nga:166470":{"d":"A woman in a floor-length white satin dress stands facing us in a room hung with shimmering ivory drapery, her dark hair piled high. A deep V-neck edged with sheer, lacy fabric, bows down the bodice and a train bunched at her feet make a cascade of white, while pearl earrings, a string of pearls and a two-pearl brooch glow against her skin. She holds a half-open fan and black opera glasses and stands before a gold-upholstered chaise longue. The canvas is about 225 by 144 centimeters, signed 1883.","i":"The sitter was Margaret Stuyvesant Rutherfurd White, a member of an old New York family who married the diplomat Henry White, later an American ambassador. Sargent painted her at twenty-seven, in the years when he was establishing himself in Paris, and the opera glasses and fan suggest an evening at the opera. A white dress in a white room recalls Whistler's white paintings, and the challenge of white on white was a test of skill that Sargent met with relish.","c":"A daring and nearly total success: the white satin, drapery and pearls are painted with such range of tone that the whiteness shimmers rather than flattens, and the dark eyes, black opera glasses and gold chaise supply the notes that hold it together. The pose is poised and the expression reserved, typical of Sargent's early society portraits, which are more about presence than character. Among his finest pictures from before Madame X."},"met:12127":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas shows a young woman in profile in a plain, shadowy room. Her body faces the viewer while her head turns sharply to the right; her face and arms are chalk-white, her ear a vivid red. She wears a black satin gown, cut low and held by two jeweled straps, with a small crescent ornament in her upswept auburn hair. One hand rests on a small round table with ornate metal legs at the left; the other hangs at her side holding a closed fan. The black of the dress merges into a deep, warm brown ground, so that the pale skin and the sweep of the skirt carry the whole composition. The paint is smooth and thin, the contour sharp.","i":"Virginie Gautreau, born in New Orleans and married to a Paris banker, was a celebrated 'professional beauty' who powdered her skin with lavender. Sargent asked to paint her, hoping the portrait would make his name. Shown at the Paris Salon of 1884 under a title that withheld her name, it was recognized at once, and the public jeered at the pallor, the pose, and the gown, especially the right strap, which had been painted slipping off her shoulder. Sargent repainted it in place and within a few years had settled in London. In 1916 he sold the picture to the Metropolitan Museum, writing to its director that it was perhaps the best thing he had done.","c":"The pose is daring and the execution almost cruelly economical: the long curve from shoulder to hem, the black held against the brown, the arm lit like marble. It is also a cool, even unsettling picture. The sitter becomes a beautiful object, and her lack of warmth may be what Paris resented. Beside Sargent's later society portraits, which flatter and bustle, this one is stark and composed, nearly severe, and the better for it. It remains his boldest image and arguably his most convincing."},"met:12162":{"d":"Men rest among the wheat in a sunlit field with a tree, in a canvas about 71 by 91 centimeters painted in 1885 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the tree, the men and the landscape, so the picture is a rural scene of labor paused, painted with the loose, bright touch Sargent used when he worked outdoors in these years.","i":"Harvest and the rest of the reapers were subjects made serious by Jean-François Millet and Jules Bastien-Lepage, who showed peasants as dignified, modern figures. Sargent was not a painter of peasants, and his reapers are less a social statement than an excuse for sun and golden wheat. In the mid-1880s he was experimenting with Impressionist methods, after visiting Monet at Giverny in 1885.","c":"A portrait painter stepping outdoors chooses the sun as protagonist, and the men are summary, which is the point: the picture is a record of heat and wheat, with figures as accents. The brushwork is brisk and the golden color convincing. It lacks the social weight of Millet, and does not try for it. A pleasant, assured example of Sargent's open-air manner, more interesting for its light than its subject."},"nga:50217":{"d":"A woman shown from the shins up stands beside a cherry-red table, edged with gold, against a caramel-brown wall. She has iron-gray hair parted in the middle and drawn back under a black cap, a high-collared black dress with puffy sleeves, black beads at shoulders and cuffs, and white ruffled cuffs. A pearl hangs from her ear, she holds a closed black fan, and she grips the table corner with her fingertips. Her thin red lips are set in a straight line. The canvas is about 154 by 93 centimeters, signed 1888.","i":"The sitter was Eleanora O'Donnell Iselin, wife of the New York banker Adrian Iselin and a member of a prominent family. Sargent painted her in 1888, during his first American visit as a celebrated painter, when portraits of wealthy Americans were establishing his reputation on the other side of the Atlantic. The black dress and gray hair give her the gravity of a mature matron.","c":"A study in black: the severe dress, the gray hair under a black cap and the thin line of the mouth tell of a woman of formidable character, and Sargent gives her authority without flattery. The cherry-red table is the one hot color, a jolt that keeps the picture from being merely sober, and the loose but blended handling softens the severity. It is less showy than his portraits of young beauties, and more telling."},"met:12446":{"d":"Two girls are shown on a lawn in an informal outdoor scene, painted in oil on canvas, about 54 by 64 centimeters, about 1889 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record only girls, so the picture is best described by its type: an unposed, bright, summery figure study in the spirit of his open-air paintings of the later 1880s, rather than a commissioned portrait.","i":"In the later 1880s Sargent spent summers in the English countryside, where friends and their children became models for open-air pictures of young women and girls in white dresses on lawns and in gardens, notably Carnation, Lily, Lily, Rose. The lawn, with its flickering green and white, let him study reflected color and the effect of sunlight, and he was attracted by the leisure of a quiet summer day.","c":"Informality is the picture's virtue: two figures on a patch of green need no setting, and Sargent can concentrate on light on summer dresses and the plain pleasure of an afternoon. The loose touch suggests speed, and the composition is open. The picture is slight, and the pair may be less individually characterized than in his commissions, but it shows Sargent at his most relaxed."},"met:12447":{"d":"Two women or girls hold parasols outdoors, in a canvas about 75 by 64 centimeters painted in 1888 or 1889 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the figures and the parasols, so the picture is a study of figures in filtered sunlight, in which the parasols both shade the sitters and shape the light that falls on them.","i":"A parasol was a favorite device of the Impressionists, from Monet's Woman with a Parasol onward, because it throws colored shadow over face and dress and lets a painter study reflected light. Sargent, who knew Monet and admired his work in the 1880s, borrowed the motif in these years for outdoor figure paintings, using it for its effect on color rather than for its fashion.","c":"The parasol is a clever excuse: it frames the face in cool, colored shadow, and the picture becomes a study of how light is filtered and tinted. Sargent handles it with an ease that shows how well he had absorbed the Impressionists' lessons while keeping his own sure grasp of form. It is a fresh, brisk work, less ambitious than the large figure pieces, and the better for its lightness."},"nga:46431":{"d":"A young woman in a flowing peach-pink satin dress is shown from about the knees up, gazing out with dark gray eyes. Her light brown hair is twisted into a loose bun with a curl on her forehead, and she wears a pearl necklace. The dress has a low neckline, fluttering sleeves, a narrow waist tied with a gold sash and bow, and gold bows on the shoulders, with coral streaks shading the folds. She holds two white orchids at her chest. The background lightens from coppery brown to golden tan. The canvas is about 163 by 94 centimeters.","i":"Miss Grace Woodhouse was painted by Sargent in 1890, when he was among the most sought-after portraitists in London and had become a master of satin, silk and gauze. Orchids were costly hothouse flowers, a sign of luxury and refinement, and the peach and gold palette makes her a figure of delicate grace. The portrait follows the fashion for pictures of women in flowing dresses as visions of beauty.","c":"The peach dress that almost merges with the flesh is a daring choice: the portrait becomes a play of tone on tone, with the gold bows adding sparkle and the white orchids a crisp accent. The coppery ground is a perfect foil for the pinks. The risk is that the sitter will be overwhelmed by her dress, but her steady gaze holds the picture. It is one of Sargent's most decorative portraits, brilliant if a little cool."},"met:12061":{"d":"A wide, low landscape, about 35 by 63 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1890 to 1891 by John Singer Sargent, shows the cliffs at Deir el Bahri in Egypt. The Met's keywords record only the landscape, so the subject is the great rock walls of the Theban desert, which rise behind the ancient temple of Queen Hatshepsut, painted with the bare, sunlit severity of such a place.","i":"Deir el Bahri, near Luxor, is the site of the mortuary temple of Hatshepsut, set into a great amphitheater of limestone cliffs. Sargent visited Egypt in the winter of 1890 to 1891, partly to gather material for his mural commission for the Boston Public Library, and he painted the Nile and the desert as well as figures. A wide format suited the long horizontal of the cliffs.","c":"Landscape was a rest from portraits for Sargent, and this one is bold in its simplicity: a band of pale cliffs, a band of sky, the heat implied by the bleached color. He is attracted by the severe geometry of the place, and the picture has the clarity of a first impression. It is not a grand view, but a quick, intelligent note of a landscape that offered few picturesque comforts."},"met:12072":{"d":"A portrait of an Egyptian woman, painted in oil on canvas, about 65 by 53 centimeters, in 1890 to 1891 by John Singer Sargent during his journey in Egypt. The Met's keywords record the woman, Egypt and the portrait genre, so it is a study of a figure in local dress, probably a model engaged in Cairo or Luxor, rather than a commissioned likeness.","i":"Sargent traveled in Egypt in 1890 to 1891 partly to prepare murals for the Boston Public Library, and he made many studies of figures, among them women of the Nile, which could serve as material for his compositions. European painters of the nineteenth century often portrayed Eastern women as exotic types; Sargent, working from a model, concentrates on the figure's form and costume.","c":"The question for such a study is whether the model is a person or a type, and Sargent, working fast, gives her the dignity of a person: a firm structure, direct light, no coyness. The picture is more serious than most Orientalist painting of the time because it avoids sensuality and anecdote. It stays a study, not a portrait, and its anonymity limits what it can say, but its honesty is attractive."},"met:12073":{"d":"A woman's head and shoulders emerge from a rough patch of brown paint on a bare, pale canvas. She wears a dark headscarf with a pale band across the forehead, thin gold earrings that hang to her jaw, and a string of orange beads at her throat above a brown garment edged with white. Her face is modelled carefully in warm brown and pink, with large, heavy-lidded eyes that look straight out and a small, closed mouth with a trace of a smile. Below the chest, the picture dissolves into loose, transparent strokes, and the lower part of the canvas is left untouched.","i":"Sargent travelled to Egypt in the winter of 1890–91, partly to gather material for the mural commission he had accepted for the Boston Public Library, and he made several oil studies of the people he met in Cairo and on the Nile. This is an unfinished study rather than a commissioned portrait, and the sitter's name is not recorded. The Met dates it 1890–91. Western painters of the day often painted such types in the Near East, and many of those pictures treat the sitter as a costume piece; this study's close, direct view of a single face makes her more than that.","c":"Sargent's unfinished studies can be fresher than his finished portraits, and this is a good example. The face is built in a few fluid, certain passes, the brown wash around the shoulders makes a natural vignette, and the level, intelligent glance is not forced. The incompleteness gives it life, but it also leaves the costume and the sitter's circumstances unexplained, and it remains an observation rather than a statement. Within those limits it is a sympathetic, quick-witted piece of painting, and a reminder of the rapid direct method Sargent learned from Carolus-Duran."},"met:12074":{"d":"Men and women raise water from the Nile, in a canvas about 64 by 53 centimeters painted in 1890 to 1891 by John Singer Sargent in Egypt. The Met's keywords record the river, the water, a man, a woman and the Nile, so the subject is the oldest of daily labors: lifting water from the river to the fields, a scene unchanged for centuries.","i":"Raising water from the Nile by hand or with a simple lifting device has been the basis of Egyptian agriculture since antiquity, and for a European traveler of 1890 it was a living link with the ancient world. Sargent was in Egypt partly to prepare for his Boston Public Library murals, and he may have found in such everyday figures the poses and rhythm of ancient reliefs, which he could translate into his designs.","c":"The best of Sargent's Egyptian pictures sees the ancient in the everyday, and this one makes the strongest claim: figures bend and lift in the rhythm of work, as in a relief come to life. The composition is plain and effective, with the river a ribbon of light, and the figures are painted with respect rather than picturesque condescension. A strong small record of his Egyptian journey."},"met:12122":{"d":"A landscape with trees, a goat, a shepherd and a woman, painted in oil on canvas, about 62 by 81 centimeters, in 1890 to 1891 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the tree, the goats, the shepherd and the woman, so the picture is a pastoral scene: animals and herders in the open country, painted during the journey that took him to Egypt and the Mediterranean.","i":"A goatherd in a landscape belongs to the old pastoral tradition that runs from the Roman poets through Claude Lorrain and the Barbizon painters, and its figures, humble people in a timeless countryside, were a common subject for painters traveling in the Mediterranean. For Sargent, the type offered a break from society portraits and a chance to paint light on rough ground and hills.","c":"A shepherd and goats are the most traditional of subjects, and Sargent treats them without nostalgia, with an eye for the bright, dry light and the figures' casual poses. The landscape has the quick certainty of his best sketches, and the picture is better in its parts than in its composition, which stays slight. As a diversion from portraiture it is easy, assured and refreshing."},"met:12266":{"d":"An interior view of Hagia Sophia, the great domed church of Constantinople that had become a mosque, painted in oil on canvas, about 80 by 62 centimeters, in 1891 (the date is queried) by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the interior, the mosque and a chandelier, so the subject is the vast space of the building, lit by hanging lamps.","i":"Hagia Sophia, built in the sixth century under the emperor Justinian, was the greatest church of Byzantium and, after the Ottoman conquest of 1453, a mosque, which it remained in Sargent's day. In 1891 he traveled to Constantinople, among other places, in search of material for his religious murals in Boston. The hanging lamps and the immense space are the picture's subject: an architecture of light and air.","c":"Painting an immense interior is a problem of scale and light, and Sargent solves it by suggesting rather than describing: the chandeliers hang in a haze, and the vault recedes into gold-brown shadow. It is less a record of architecture than an impression of a space where light and air seem solid. A fine, sketchy tribute to one of the great spaces of the world."},"nga:129288":{"d":"A nearly square, sketchy view of an empty courtyard, painted in milky oatmeal brown, ivory, muted brick red, eucalyptus green and slate blue. The floor is patterned with round and octagonal stones, a darkened arched doorway sits within a larger, lighter arch across the court, and a niche or window at the right is covered with a lattice. At the left another arch opens onto a space filled with golden light. Brushstrokes are visible throughout, and the canvas is about 48 by 58 centimeters.","i":"Sargent was in Egypt in the winter of 1890 to 1891, partly to gather ideas for the murals he had been commissioned to paint for the Boston Public Library. Besides figures and landscapes, he sketched architecture, such as this Cairo courtyard, for its patterns and its light. The empty space, with a lattice that recalls the wooden screens of Islamic houses, shows his interest in interiors where sun enters indirectly.","c":"No figures, no anecdote, only a floor and some arches, and yet the sketch is alive: the warm gold behind the left arch, the cool slate of the shadows and the pale strokes of sun make a convincing glow of reflected light. The paint is quick, and the oatmeal and ivory palette is unusually restrained. It is an assured little exercise in light and texture, to be valued for its economy rather than its subject."},"met:12131":{"d":"A mannequin, an artist's lay figure, stands in a snowy landscape near a house, in a canvas about 64 by 76 centimeters painted about 1891 to 1893 by John Singer Sargent. The Met's keywords record the house, the human figure, winter and snow, so the picture is an experiment: a figure placed outdoors to study how snow light falls on form, with the human shape reduced to a stand-in.","i":"Painters used a lay figure to study drapery and pose when a live model was not available, as most portraitists did, and Sargent was a master of drapery. Setting it in snow is unusual: the figure is a means to study white on white and the blue shadows of winter, in the Impressionist spirit. The title makes no pretense that it is a person; it is a record of a painter's experiment, probably for himself.","c":"An odd, appealing picture: the mannequin, a stiff stand-in, gives the snow a human scale without human drama. It is a painter's problem set for himself, and the solution is a lovely study of cold light. It would be wrong to call it a major work, but it has the charm of seeing a great portraitist at play: here he sets aside people in order to paint what stands in for them."},"met:12037":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 98 by 31 centimeters, shows the goddess Astarte, painted about 1890 to 1895 by John Singer Sargent. The format, more than three times as tall as wide, suits a figure designed for a place within a larger scheme, and the picture is probably a study for a goddess in his mural decoration for the Boston Public Library, where the gods of the ancient Near East appear.","i":"Astarte was the goddess of love and war worshiped in Phoenicia and the Near East, a relative of Ishtar and Aphrodite. Sargent's murals for the Boston Public Library, which occupied him for decades from 1890, told the history of religion, beginning with the pagan gods who gave way to Judaism and Christianity. He traveled to Egypt, Greece and Turkey in 1890 to 1891 to gather material for the project, and a figure like Astarte belongs to that program.","c":"A study of a goddess is a rare thing in Sargent's output, since he built his fame on living sitters; this is the other Sargent, the muralist, who simplifies and makes symbols. The tall, narrow format turns the figure into a flowing vertical, and the study is more interesting for its purpose than for its beauty. It shows how much effort he gave to a project that earned a more mixed reception than his portraits."},"met:12108":{"d":"A portrait of the financier and collector Henry Gurdon Marquand, painted in oil on canvas, about 132 by 106 centimeters, in 1897 by John Singer Sargent. The sitter was seventy-eight, and the Met's keywords record only the sitter and the portrait. The generous canvas gives an elderly man of great standing in New York the presence of a public figure, painted at the height of Sargent's fame.","i":"Marquand, a New York banker, was the second president of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, from 1889 until his death in 1902, and one of its greatest benefactors; among his gifts was Vermeer's Young Woman with a Water Pitcher. Portraits of patrons were a staple of Sargent's career, and this one honors a man whose generosity shaped the museum that now holds it.","c":"Sargent's portraits of elderly men are often his most perceptive, since there is no fashionable silhouette to paint and the face must carry the picture. This is likely a portrait of respect more than of revelation: the sitter is honored, not probed, and the likeness records dignity and long experience. It is a good example of Sargent as portraitist of the American establishment, and it hangs fittingly in the museum Marquand helped build."},"cma:160289":{"d":"A woman in an elegant satin dress poses full-length as if welcoming guests into a grand home, in a tall canvas about 219 by 105 centimeters, painted in 1898. The sitter, Lisa Colt Curtis, was a relative of the Colt firearms family and had recently married Sargent's distant cousin Ralph. The inscription at the upper right reads, To Ralph and Mrs. Ralph, John S. Sargent 1898.","i":"Sargent was one of the most sought-after painters of his era, portraying cosmopolitan society on both sides of the Atlantic, but this was apparently a wedding gift to the couple rather than a commission. Her palatial Venetian home was presumably the Palazzo Barbaro on the Grand Canal, owned by Ralph Curtis's family, where Sargent was often a guest. A hostess welcoming guests suits that house.","c":"A gift gives Sargent freedom: no client to please, only friends. The full-length format and slightly theatrical gesture show him at his most fluent, and the satin is painted in his dashing, economical way. The risk with the society manner is glibness, and the pose does tip toward display, but the warmth of the occasion tempers it. A fine late example of the grand Sargent, with a personal edge."},"nga:177786":{"d":"A crowd gathers in an outdoor scene near an inn: some sit at low tables, some dance and some talk, while on the right a man stands on a raised surface playing a fiddle. The people wear earth tones of brown, tan, black and dark red. Behind them are a rustic thatched building, leafy trees and what may be a tall castle or church on the left, under a cloudy gray sky. The canvas is about 98 by 102 centimeters, painted in the early 1650s.","i":"Peasant gatherings with music and dancing outside inns were a staple of Dutch and Flemish art, from Bruegel to Teniers and Adriaen van Ostade, and could be read as lively entertainment or as a moral warning against drink and idleness. Constantijn van Renesse, a Dutch painter said to have studied with Rembrandt, gave this one a large scale. The castle or church in the distance sets the revel within a real landscape.","c":"A busy picture of many small figures, with music and dance in a gray light, where the harmony of earth colors is the main pleasure. The figures are generalized and the composition loose, so the eye drifts from group to group without a clear focus. It is pleasant rather than powerful, and its interest is partly that a painter said to have studied with Rembrandt worked in this lighter, rustic register."},"nga:53138":{"d":"Behind a dense screen of stylized dark-brown tree trunks and green leaves, a man and a woman ride side by side on horseback and touch noses in an embrace. She has waist-length blond hair and a burgundy jacket with gold stripes; he has a black handlebar mustache, white hair, a black and gold three-cornered hat and a navy jacket with gold braid. The forest rises two-thirds up the canvas under a seafoam sky with one tan cloud. The canvas is about 92 by 73 centimeters, signed Henri Rousseau.","i":"Henri Rousseau was a customs official and self-taught Sunday painter who retired in 1893 to paint full time and became the best-known naive painter, admired by Picasso and Apollinaire. This picture of 1889 is an early forest scene, before his famous jungles of the 1890s, and shows his method: flattened, stylized foliage painted leaf by leaf, and figures out of a storybook. The costumes suggest an imagined past.","c":"Its strangeness is its strength: a dense curtain of trees, stylized to the point of ornament, hides a couple whose noses meet in a comic, tender gesture. Rousseau's lack of academic training is not a flaw but a style: flat colors, patterned leaves, strong silhouettes, held with a clarity that trained painters might have lost. It is a fairy tale for adults, less ambitious than his jungles but already dreamlike."},"nga:46538":{"d":"A small boy with round, doll-like proportions stands frontally on a bed of jagged brown rocks against a pale blue sky. His dark hair is combed smooth and parted at the side, his large dark eyes are set close together, and his lips are pink and full. He wears a black jacket that falls open over a white skirt-like garment ruled with black lines, white stockings with thin black stripes, and black ankle boots. His hands hang by his sides. The rocks are rendered as smooth, pointed shapes in tones of peanut and ash brown, and a thin strip of turquoise beyond them suggests the sea. The paint is thin and flat, and the signature H. Rousseau is at lower right.","i":"Henri Rousseau (1844–1910) was a self-taught painter who worked for the Paris city toll service, which earned him the nickname Le Douanier, and who began painting full time after retiring in 1893. Dated 1895 to 1897, this picture comes from his first years as a full-time artist. The boy is not named, and the rocky shore may be invented. Rousseau exhibited at the jury-free Salon des Indépendants, where critics often mocked his work, but younger artists including Picasso admired it for its directness. The painting was bought by Chester Dale in 1927 and reached the National Gallery of Art in 1963.","c":"Rousseau's conviction is the picture's whole power. The boy is flat, frontal and unblinking, set on his rocks like a toy on a mantelpiece, and the pale sky and strip of sea give an even light that makes the strangeness feel natural. Contemporaries read the stiff hands, odd scale and simplified anatomy as incompetence; later viewers took them as the point. It is less ambitious than his jungle scenes, but it shows the same ability to make a simple, almost awkward image hard to forget."},"nga:130841":{"d":"A plate of green fruit with hints of pink, perhaps apples, sits behind two bottles: a tall yellow one with a white cap and a shorter one to the right with a colorful label. An apple rests in front, and two blue-gray shapes may be mugs on saucers. The surface is mostly green with streaks of blue and light pink, the wall a streaky mix of light and dark blue, and thick, visible brushstrokes blur the details. The canvas is about 48 by 49 centimeters, painted in 1900.","i":"Alexej von Jawlensky, a former officer of the Russian army, moved to Munich in 1896 to study painting and was by 1900 working in a thick, expressive manner that looked to van Gogh and the Post-Impressionists. Still lifes were his color exercises: fruit and bottles to be turned into patches of color. He later became one of the leading Expressionists, known for his series of stylized heads.","c":"A student's picture with an unexpectedly bold touch: the thick strokes and the blue, green and pink harmonies point toward the color-driven style of his maturity, though the objects are summarily drawn and the bottles' vertical accents are the only firm structure. The effect is lively rather than finished. As a picture it gives pleasure through color; as evidence of the road from academic training to Expressionism it is informative."},"met:441111":{"d":"A portrait of a woman, about 64 by 52 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1864 by the Florentine portraitist Michele Gordigiani. The title offers two possible sitters, Marianna Panciatichi, marchesa Paolucci delle Roncole (1835 to 1919), or her sister-in-law, Beatrice Ferrari-Corbelli di Reggio, contessa di Lucciano, so the picture is, in effect, the likeness of an aristocratic woman whose exact identity is uncertain.","i":"Gordigiani was a fashionable portraitist of Florence's society in the 1860s, painting Italian nobility and foreign visitors, among them Robert and Elizabeth Barrett Browning, who lived in the city. The doubt over the sitter's name shows how easily identities slip even for signed and dated pictures. The year 1864 was the eve of Florence's brief term as capital of the new Kingdom of Italy, when the city's aristocratic and diplomatic life was busy.","c":"Gordigiani is a polished society painter: his women are elegant and sentimental, with smooth surfaces and a soft, flattering light. This portrait's interest is partly the doubtful identity and partly the costume of the early 1860s. It is not a great picture, but it is a competent, handsome specimen of mid-century Italian portraiture, made in a Florence where the Macchiaioli were, in the same decade, painting far more radically."},"met:438754":{"d":"A small panel with a gold background. The Virgin, shown half-length in a dark green mantle edged with gold and a pale veil, tilts her head sadly toward the Christ Child, who sits on her arm in a rose-red tunic and reaches up to touch the edge of her veil beside her cheek. Her large, almond-shaped eyes look down, and her face is long and narrow, drawn with fine lines. Gold stars mark her shoulder and veil, and punched patterns decorate the halos and the border. A painted ledge like a marble parapet crosses the bottom, and the original frame, burnt at the lower edge, is still attached.","i":"Duccio di Buoninsegna was the leading painter of Siena around 1300, and this panel is one of very few paintings accepted as his. The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 2004 for a reported 45 million dollars; it is known as the Stroganoff or Stoclet Madonna after earlier owners. Burn marks on the original frame probably come from candles, which suggests private devotion. The date, around 1300, is debated, and the ledge at the bottom looks forward to later painting. The art historian James Beck argued in 2007 that it is a nineteenth-century forgery; the Museum rejected this after studying the panel, underdrawing and pigments.","c":"The picture is small but weighty. The gold ground, the Virgin's sorrowful gaze and the Child's sudden reach give the old Byzantine formula a human gesture, and the drawing of the veil and mantle is refined. The tenderness is still formal, though: the Child remains a small adult and the Virgin's face is almost a mask. Next to Duccio's large altarpieces it has less narrative scope. Whatever the doubts about its date, it is a persuasive example of Italian painting moving toward feeling without giving up the gold ground."},"nga:10":{"d":"A small three-part panel shows the Nativity flanked by two standing prophets, all on gold. In the central square the Virgin reclines in a cobalt-blue robe under a wooden shelter set into a rocky cave, her head haloed; a swaddled infant lies in a tray-like manger with an ox and a donkey looking down. White-bearded Joseph sits at the left, two women wash the baby in a tub below, and seven angels cluster on each side, one presenting a scroll to two shepherds with a dog and sheep. In the wings Isaiah and Ezekiel hold scrolls and look towards the centre.","i":"The panel comes from the predella of the Maestà, the great double-sided altarpiece Duccio painted between 1308 and 1311 for the high altar of Siena Cathedral. Its small scenes of Christ's infancy were meant to be read at close range beneath the enthroned Virgin. Isaiah and Ezekiel, with their scrolls, are the prophets read as foretelling the virgin birth, and their presence turns the Nativity into the fulfilment of prophecy. The reclining Mary, the cave and the washing of the Child follow long-established Byzantine models.","c":"Duccio makes a tiny panel feel spacious. The rhythm of the angels on both sides, the ruddy glow around Mary and the links of gesture between prophets and Nativity show a painter who knew that narrative could be tender as well as solemn. The Byzantine conventions are loosened by lifelike detail, such as the shepherds' dog and the bath. Beside Giotto in Florence, Duccio is gentler, more decorative and more linear, which is why Siena valued him."},"nga:282":{"d":"Against a gold ground, a haloed man stands on a steep tan outcropping at the left and gestures with an open palm to two men in a wooden rowboat. He has long brown hair and a small mouth, and wears a crimson robe under a sea-blue mantle edged in gold. The two bearded men in the boat, one in a sky-blue tunic and one in brick red, haul a net heavy with fish over the side. The boat floats on a copper-green sea of undulating current, where fish outlined in dark green swim, and two of them, gaping, face the viewer head on.","i":"The scene shows the call of Peter and Andrew, fishermen of the Sea of Galilee, to whom Christ says in the Gospels that he will make them fishers of men (Matthew 4:18–20). They leave their nets at once to follow him. Duccio painted it as one of the small scenes from Christ's ministry on the back of the Maestà, the great altarpiece made for Siena Cathedral between 1308 and 1311. The fish and net also recall the Church as a net gathering souls.","c":"A compact piece of storytelling: with a rock, a boat and a gold sky Duccio makes a complete scene, and the open hand of Christ against the men hauling their catch is one of the most readable moments of the Maestà. The sea, a patterned copper-green surface with fish drawn like decorations, is delightfully unreal; the figures have a gentle reserve. It lacks the spatial depth Giotto was winning in Florence, but its charm and economy are Duccio's own."},"met:435658":{"d":"A panel of Mary and the Christ Child, about 80 by 54 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood against a gold ground, possibly in the 1230s. Its style is that of the Italo-Byzantine icons which Tuscan painters such as the Berlinghieri of Lucca copied in the early thirteenth century: the figures are frontal and formal, the faces simplified, the folds of drapery drawn as patterns of line, and the gold ground makes the holy figures appear in a heavenly space.","i":"Mary as Mother of God was proclaimed at the Council of Ephesus in 431, and Byzantine icons showed her presenting her Son to the viewer as the way to salvation. Italian painters of the early thirteenth century learned this formula from icons brought from the East. Berlinghiero was the head of a family workshop in Lucca. Panels like this were set on altars or carried in processions, and they were not portraits but sacred presences; the stylisation is deliberate and expresses timelessness.","c":"A key document in the early history of Italian painting. Its beauty lies in firm outline, dignity of pose and glowing gold, not in realism; judged by naturalism it is stiff, but that misunderstands its purpose. Within the rigid scheme the first signs of human tenderness appear in such panels, and Cimabue and Giotto would build on them. It is worth seeing as the starting point from which Italian art would develop."},"met:10076":{"d":"A portrait of the New York lawyer James Topham Brady, painted in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, in 1869 by Joseph Alexander Ames. The Met's keywords record only the sitter and the genre. Brady died in 1869, so the picture may be a memorial likeness made in the year of his death, a record of a famous advocate for family, friends and colleagues.","i":"Brady was one of the most celebrated trial lawyers of nineteenth-century New York, remembered for defending Daniel Sickles in 1859 after Sickles killed his wife's lover. Portraits of eminent lawyers and public men were a steady source of work for painters like Ames, who made a career of likenesses of notable Americans. A portrait of an advocate honors a reputation built on speech.","c":"A portrait of an advocate is a portrait of speech, and a painting is necessarily silent, so the picture relies on bearing and expression: alertness and self-possession. Ames's likeness is competent and dignified rather than inspired, and it holds the viewer by the force of the subject's reputation. As a record of a famous public man of the Civil War era it has value, and it is professionally made."},"met:435923":{"d":"A small view of Stoke-by-Nayland in Suffolk, with trees and its church, painted in oil on canvas, about 28 by 36 centimeters, about 1810 to 1811 by John Constable. The Met's keywords record the tree, the landscape and Saint Mary's Church. It is a small picture painted close to the scene, an early example of Constable's habit of working directly from nature.","i":"Constable grew up in the Stour valley on the border of Suffolk and Essex and painted that countryside all his life, believing that a painter should work from direct observation. Stoke-by-Nayland's church, with its tall tower, was a landmark of the district, and Constable painted it more than once. Small oil studies like this, made in the 1810s, were private experiments in capturing light and weather, which he later developed into large exhibition canvases.","c":"The virtue of this modest picture is freshness: leaves, sky and church are observed with plain affection, in a clean, unfussy touch. Constable's early work lacks the storms of brushwork of the later years, but it has an honest truthfulness that the picturesque painters of the day lacked. It is not a showpiece, but small oil studies like this show the method from which his great landscapes grew."},"nga:1147":{"d":"A broad park landscape opens under a sky heaped with white clouds that cast moving shadows across the grass. In the foreground a rough wooden fence runs in from the lower left, and beyond it several black-and-white cows graze on a grassy slope. At the right a still lake reflects the clouds; two men in a rowing boat pull in a net near a pair of white swans. Across the water a red-brick country house shows through a gap in a long line of deep green trees, with the horizon about halfway up the canvas. Farther left a donkey-drawn carriage crosses near a small bridge. The brushwork is crisp and light, and touches of yellow brighten the sunlit grass.","i":"Major-General Francis Slater Rebow, a friend of Constable's father, commissioned this view of his estate near Colchester in Essex and asked for certain features to be included. Constable stayed at the house for some weeks and finished the picture in September 1816, the month before he married. He adjusted what he saw: the house and the lake were not part of a single view, so the picture is a careful arrangement, not a record. It belongs to the years when Constable was moving from small studies to larger, more complex compositions. The park is now the campus of the University of Essex.","c":"The picture reconciles two demands that rarely agree: a faithful portrait of a patron's property and a convincing piece of weather. The clouds and the clear, even light are the real success, and they let the cows, fence and swans sit naturally in the grass. The weaker part is the house, which is small and almost lost, and the composition is balanced to the point of calm. It has less of the energy of Constable's later Stour valley pictures, but its clarity is rare in English landscape of the time."},"met:435921":{"d":"A portrait of an older woman, Mrs. James Pulham Sr. (Frances Amys, about 1766 to 1856), painted in oil on canvas, about 76 by 63 centimeters, in 1818 by John Constable. She was about fifty-two. The Met's keywords record only a woman and the portrait genre, so the picture is a straightforward likeness of a respectable woman of the painter's acquaintance.","i":"Constable is remembered for landscapes, but he painted portraits throughout his career, mostly of family, friends and neighbors, and they supplemented his income when landscapes did not sell. In 1818 he was still struggling to establish himself and had not yet made his name with The White Horse of 1819. Portraits like this show a painter who preferred nature but did justice to the people he knew.","c":"As a portraitist Constable is plain and honest rather than flattering: the sitter is likely to have a solid, unglamorous presence, and the light is handled as a landscape painter handles it, broadly. The picture lacks the drama of his landscapes, but it also lacks the pretensions of many society portraits. It is a quiet, sound likeness, valuable for what it shows of Constable's skill with the human face when he chose to use it."},"nga:1146":{"d":"From a grassy bank the view crosses a placid river lined on the far side with trees and farm animals. The paint is applied loosely, and on the water russet-brown and steel-gray skim across the canvas, leaving bare patches that suggest shimmering light. At left a shallow barge, poled by two men in red caps, carries a white horse in harness and blinders. On the far bank stand a white cottage with a reddish roof, hay, a plow and a cart, with cows at the water's edge to the right. Clouds roll across a steel-gray sky. Oil on canvas, about 127 by 183 centimeters, 1818 to 1819.","i":"The river is the Stour, which divides Suffolk from Essex, and the scene lies near Flatford, where Constable's father owned a mill. The horse, which hauled barges along the towpath, is apparently being ferried across the water where the towpath changes sides. This canvas is usually described as the full-size sketch for the finished picture exhibited at the Royal Academy in 1819, now in the Frick Collection, one of the large six-footers with which Constable sought to give landscape the weight of history painting.","c":"Even as a sketch, this has the freshness that made Constable unlike other landscape painters of his day: the skimmed, partly bare canvas that gives the river its shimmer, the loose touch in the foliage, the clouds echoed in the water. The finished version tightens these effects, and many viewers prefer the sketch for its speed and risk. It is a key document of his method of working large, and a more exciting picture than its modest subject suggests."},"nga:115":{"d":"Across an expanse of flat grassy lawn rises the tall stone spire of Salisbury Cathedral, outlined against billowing clouds. On the left a line of thick-canopied trees takes up nearly half the composition, scattering dappled light over the lawn and shading a dirt path where three tiny figures walk. A few animals, perhaps cows, graze beyond. The cathedral sits low on the horizon, a quarter of the way up the canvas, and the pale blue sky is filled with puffy white clouds tinged with mauve. Oil on canvas, about 73 by 91 centimeters, dated 1820.","i":"Salisbury Cathedral, whose spire at about 123 meters is the tallest in England, became one of Constable's recurring subjects in the 1820s, when he stayed in the town with his friend the Reverend John Fisher. The Close, the green precinct around the cathedral, offered views across meadows that suited his taste for the English countryside as a setting for the Church. Here the building is set low in the view, a symbol of the Church's place in the landscape.","c":"A calm and well-judged picture in which the spire is held in balance by the trees and the great cloud-filled sky. Constable gives the grass and foliage a lively dappled light and keeps the figures small to stress the permanence of the building. Compared with his famous later views of Salisbury, it is quieter and more descriptive, but the sense of English weather and summer is already unmistakable."},"nga:104243":{"d":"A small horizontal oil study filled with gray and white clouds against a blue sky. Wisps of white cloud drift among banks of darker gray in the upper half, and a tower of gray cloud at left hides a deep red circle that appears only on careful inspection. Rays of pale shell pink streak down from the clouds along the bottom edge. The sky alone is the subject. Oil on paper mounted on canvas, about 20 by 27 centimeters, made in 1821 to 1822.","i":"In 1821 and 1822 Constable made a series of cloud studies, painting the sky outdoors to record its changing effects, and he sometimes noted the date, time and weather on the study itself. He believed that the sky was the keynote of a landscape and wrote to his friend John Fisher about the importance of skies. These studies were not exhibited; they were his private research for the skies of his large pictures.","c":"Among the most direct records of the sky made in early nineteenth-century painting: nothing is arranged, and the clouds are recorded as weather, with an eye for the sun hidden in the cloud mass and the pink shafts beneath. A tiny sheet, it carries more observation than many large landscapes. Its limitation is that of any study, a note and not a composition, but it is a note by the period's keenest student of the sky."},"nga:93667":{"d":"A low-horizon seaside view: a sandy beach at left, ships and sailboats on the water in the distance at right, and a pier running out into the sea with small figures walking along it. A pink building stands on the beach near the end of the pier. In the lower left a white horse harnessed to a cart, with a person standing on it, heads toward the pier, passing a small wooden boat lying on the sand and tied to a dark red anchor. The palette is pastel beige, blue and gray, under blue sky with pink-tinged clouds. Oil on canvas, about 32 by 51 centimeters, dated 1822.","i":"Yarmouth is an English seaside town, and the title names its jetty, a landing place running out from the beach for boats and walkers. Constable's reputation rests on the inland country of the Stour valley, but he also painted the coast, and small studies like this served as private records of weather and light. The horse and cart on the sand are a typical working detail of an English beach, and no narrative beyond that is suggested.","c":"A small coastal study notable for its restraint: a low horizon, a wide pale sky and a few figures, so that light and air are the real subject. The soft pastel palette suits the sea air. It lacks the narrative weight of the big Stour pictures, and as a sketch it is more notation than composition, but it shows his instinct for summing up a place in a few well-chosen incidents, like the horse and cart."},"met:435922":{"d":"The spire of Salisbury Cathedral rises over trees across a lawn where cattle graze and a couple stands, in a large canvas about 88 by 112 centimeters painted about 1825 by John Constable. The Met's keywords record the cathedral, the cows, the couple and the trees, so the picture joins church, pasture and a quiet human note under one of Constable's lively skies.","i":"Constable's friend John Fisher, Bishop of Salisbury, commissioned a view of the cathedral from his garden, and the painter returned to the subject in more than one version. The cathedral's spire, the tallest in England, rises above the grounds, and in these pictures the trees arch over it like a frame. The cows grazing in the Bishop's grounds join religious architecture to country pasture, a combination he loved.","c":"Constable's cathedral is not a pageant but a spire seen across a meadow, and the real subject is the weather and the tender relation of old stone and green growth. The framing branches add an Old Master sense of composition, and the cattle are welcome. It is more composed than his sketches and a little arranged, but its strength is affection for a place, and it stands among his major exhibition landscapes."},"met:437092":{"d":"Portia, the heroine of Shakespeare's The Merchant of Venice, is the subject of this large canvas, about 125 by 84 centimeters, painted in 1886 by Sir John Everett Millais. The Met's keywords record a woman, Portia and the portrait genre, so the picture is a fancy portrait: a woman, presumably a model, in the character of the wise and wealthy Venetian heiress who saves Antonio in the courtroom.","i":"Portia, the heiress of Belmont, disguises herself as a lawyer and argues that Shylock may take his pound of flesh but not a drop of blood. Millais, who in his youth was a founder of the Pre-Raphaelites and had painted Ophelia, turned in his later years to single figures of women in costume, often named for Shakespearean and literary heroines, which were popular with Victorian buyers and in prints. By 1886 he was one of the most famous and successful painters in Britain.","c":"Millais's late fancy pictures are accomplished and saleable, and Portia is characteristic: a beauty in rich costume, painted with fluent, confident brushwork. Their weakness is the loss of the intensity of his Pre-Raphaelite work, where Ophelia's botanical precision and emotional charge give way to polish. As a portrait of a character the picture offers less than the play does, but as Victorian taste in a glance it is instructive."},"nga:166478":{"d":"A rowboat carrying seven men is tossed in a furrow of swelling waves under copper-brown clouds, four of them rowing with long oars as the boat pitches toward us. White, foamy waves curve up in a deep U-shape close to us, a board and debris churn at the lower right, and a second rowboat follows in the distance. Three-masted ships cluster at the center with flags and sails whipping to our right, under a bank of towering clouds. The canvas is about 102 by 131 centimeters, painted about 1804.","i":"Turner painted this about 1804, when he had opened his own gallery in Harley Street and was measuring himself against the Dutch sea painters of the seventeenth century, above all Willem van de Velde, whose ships and boats in rough weather were his model. The title's Dutch men of war point to that earlier age. Rowing out anchors to a ship in a storm was a desperate, skilled labor, and Turner treats it as a drama of men against the sea.","c":"A young Turner measures himself against the Dutch, and the picture is all weather: the cresting wave in the foreground, the torn clouds, the whipped flags. The small boat in its dramatic furrow draws us into the peril, and the copper-brown sky is rich and dark compared with the luminous seas of his later years. There is an air of pastiche, but the energy of the sea is already unmistakably his. A young man's feat: confident, loud and convincing."},"nga:1226":{"d":"Four sailing ships and a wooden rowboat with four men are tossed in churning waves beneath dark iron-gray and tawny clouds that fill the top two-thirds of the canvas. Two ships lean steeply toward us with taut sails, two more cut through the water at the right, and in the rowboat one man hangs over the side while another holds him. A wooden container bobs at the lower left with a white bird flying low. Sunlight streaks through the storm onto a distant ship. The canvas is about 109 by 144 centimeters.","i":"The Thames and the Medway meet in the estuary east of London, a busy anchorage for warships and merchantmen, and Turner, who sketched along the river from boyhood, knew these waters and their weather. Painted in 1807, the picture belongs to his early storm seascapes in the tradition of Dutch marine painting and of the Sublime. The shaft of sunlight on the distant ship is a typical Turner device: a note of grace in the middle of peril.","c":"A storm theatre: the clouds are a wall, the ships lean, the rowboat crests a wave, and the man hanging over the side is a human detail that keeps the spectacle from being merely scenic. The one shaft of light is the picture's grace note. It is a brilliant performance by a painter in his early thirties, mixing Dutch precedent with his own relish for turbulence, though the color is still dark and the luminous Turner is some years off."},"met:437852":{"d":"A busy riverside town scene, painted in oil on canvas, about 90 by 121 centimeters, in 1811 by Joseph Mallord William Turner. The Met's keywords record boats, buildings, a man and a woman, and the title places the scene at Saltash, in Cornwall, where a ferry crossed the River Tamar. The picture shows daily life on a working waterway rather than a storm or a battle.","i":"Saltash stands on the Tamar opposite Plymouth, and until Brunel's railway bridge of 1859 a ferry was the main way across the river between Devon and Cornwall. Turner toured the West Country in 1811, making sketches for his engraved Picturesque Views on the Southern Coast of England and for paintings like this one. The scene combines the everyday and the picturesque, the kind of working life he loved to set in glowing light.","c":"Turner's gift is to unify a busy scene through light: boats, buildings and people share one atmosphere. This is a quieter work than his storms, but the composition is carefully built and the people, small though they are, are alive. It shows him learning from Claude to hold space and light in order while looking at ordinary work. It lacks fireworks but is a subtle, sure and humane picture of a Cornish ferry."},"nga:116":{"d":"Late sun low over the Thames lights a riverside terrace at Mortlake. A long wall runs diagonally from the lower right, lined with tall trees whose canopies fill most of the canvas. Beneath them two chairs and a small table sit on the grass, a hoop rests against a lower wall, and two or three figures in long skirts sit or stand along the parapet with a black dog and a barely visible white one. At the right, barges crowded with people float under red and white flags, and the pale sun is mirrored in thick golden water. Butter yellow, olive and tawny brown dominate.","i":"Turner painted two views of this Thames-side terrace, one in morning light and this one at evening, and treated a corner of suburban London as an English Arcadia. The hoop, garden chairs, flags and crowded barges suggest a summer holiday, while the glowing, tree-framed distance owes its structure to Claude Lorrain, the seventeenth-century painter whom Turner revered and set out to rival. The unhurried leisure on the water is an English counterpart to Claude's Italian harbors.","c":"The light is the subject, and Turner handles it with confidence: a single golden key, a sun that is little more than a thick patch of pigment, and figures and dogs reduced to quick notations. The trees are arranged more for balance than observation, and the sweetness of the yellow occasionally cloys. Still, among his exhibition pictures of the late 1820s this homely English scene is one of the most relaxed."},"nga:52279":{"d":"A crowded passenger ferry heels over on choppy slate-blue water, its bright tan sails catching the sun. Nine figures in black, white, red and blue, sketched in a few strokes and all apparently hatted, huddle in two groups along its long side, and foam laps at the tilted hull. Beyond, at least eight more sailing vessels dot the water at the left, while at the right a three-masted man-of-war flies seven flags, its sails furled and two rows of gun ports open. A town in loose white silhouettes lines the far shore under cream and gray cloud that breaks to blue only at the upper right.","i":"Turner had a lifelong interest in Dutch marine painting; early in his career he painted a sea piece in direct emulation of Willem van de Velde, and he toured Holland and the Rhine in 1817. The subject here is everyday Dutch river traffic: ordinary travelers jammed into a small boat beside the guns of a warship, a contrast between commerce and naval power. Such marines were a staple of the Royal Academy's summer exhibitions and a way for Turner to compete with the Dutch masters he admired.","c":"The water is what Turner cares about: the tilt of the deck, the white crests and the slab of sunlit sail give the picture its energy, and the passengers dissolve into flecks of black, red and white. The man-of-war and distant town are paler and less convincing, more backdrop than place. An assured marine from his later years rather than one of his boldest, it shows how thoroughly he absorbed Dutch spaciousness and made it more turbulent."},"nga:1224":{"d":"A glittering waterway crowded with gondolas and sailboats leads to an ivory-white church in the distance at the right, with a columned, pedimented front, a high dome and a tall bell tower at its left. A terracotta-orange building and a cream-white one angle toward it along the canal. Gondolas at the left are packed with people, while a few at the center carry only standing gondoliers with their poles. Peach and ivory sails billow behind them, wispy clouds sweep an azure sky above a horizon about a third of the way up, and every boat, building and figure shimmers in reflection.","i":"San Giorgio Maggiore, begun by Palladio in the 1560s, and the Dogana, Venice's customs house, were among the city's best-known sights, painted again and again by Canaletto and his rivals for visitors on the Grand Tour. Turner first saw Venice in 1819 and returned in 1833, and this picture was painted the year after the second visit. He kept the veduta, or view-painting, tradition of canal, church and boats, but replaced its crisp topography with air and shimmering light.","c":"This is Turner measuring himself against Canaletto, and the contest is instructive. The perspective is orderly and the architecture clear, but the energy lies in the air: the pale blue, ivory and peach key, the shimmer on the water, the sense of a city seen through heat. It still keeps one foot in the guidebook, lacking the dissolving freedom of his 1840s Venetian pictures, yet as a vision of luminous calm it is among the most approachable."},"met:437853":{"d":"The view is taken from the porch of Santa Maria della Salute, the domed church at the mouth of the Grand Canal, looking out over the water of Venice; the Met's keywords list sky, canal, gondolas, boats, buildings and city. It is a large exhibition picture, oil on canvas about 91 by 122 centimeters, and a veduta, or city view, in the tradition of Canaletto. Turner's handling in this decade is luminous, with stone, water and sky softened into one glowing atmosphere.","i":"The Salute was begun in 1631 as a thanksgiving for the end of a plague, and to stand on its porch is to look out over the Grand Canal at the heart of Venice. Turner first visited the city in 1819 and returned in 1833, after which he sent a run of Venetian subjects to the Royal Academy. For English audiences Venice meant color, decay and romance, and pictures like this sold the memory of an Italian journey.","c":"Turner's Venice of the 1830s is a city sold as light. The Canaletto armature of canal, boats and palaces is kept, but dissolved into glare and reflection. This picture shares both the brilliance and the weakness of that run: architecture that can read as stage scenery, and gondolas and figures that are lively but little individualized. Compared with the haze of his 1840s views it is still tied to the veduta, and so is more easily admired than felt."},"nga:1225":{"d":"A flax-yellow river, seen from above, runs between dark ships whose spiky masts are silhouetted against a cloud-veiled sky. The moon hangs left of center, its light forming a bright hourglass in the clouds. In the foreground, three flames burn in front of the nearest ship, one orange between two pale yellow, and black silhouettes of men shoveling coal, crates and barges stand against fire and smoke. A small rowboat with two passengers slips between the ships at the left, windows glow along the distant shore, and a factory chimney spouts white flame. The painter signed a floating buoy, 'JMWT'.","i":"Keelmen were the boatmen of Britain's northeastern coal rivers, who carried coal from the loading staithes in shallow keels out to the collier ships anchored downstream; the trade stood for the wealth of industrial Britain. The setting is probably the Tyne. Turner places this working night inside the old-master tradition of the moonlit harbor, so modern labor appears in a classical frame, and he sets natural moonlight against the man-made glare of coal fires and furnace flame.","c":"Few Turner pictures set cold and hot so sharply against each other. The silvery gap of moon above and the blazing coal fires below give the canvas its drama, and the black figures of the shovelers are drawn with striking economy. The thick, blended brushwork can leave the left half hazy and the staging a little theatrical, yet this early industrial subject, treated with Romantic fire, anticipates 'Rain, Steam and Speed' and stands apart from his more picturesque ports."},"nga:41535":{"d":"About a dozen tiny figures flail or huddle in two groups across a vast, gold-toned landscape of grassy ground, steep hills and deep valleys, with white falls or rivers between the mounds. At the left a person runs from what looks like a fire-lined opening in the earth. A basket, pan pipes, perhaps a cloak and some tools lie near a sparse V-shaped tree at the right. On a distant hilltop a walled complex glows peanut brown in the setting sun, and rust-orange clouds nearly hide an icy blue sky. The paint is so loose that detail is hard to make out.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses: Proserpine, gathering flowers in Sicily, is carried off by Pluto, lord of the underworld, who bursts from the earth, and her mother Ceres, goddess of the harvest, searches the world for her. Here the fiery chasm, the abandoned basket and pipes, and the panicking companions are the clues, and the abduction itself is nearly invisible. Turner cares less for the narrative than for the earth splitting open, an image of nature's violence.","c":"The idea is stronger than the execution. Turner turns a violent myth into a golden panorama in which the figures nearly vanish, and the narrative suffers: without the title few viewers would find the abduction. The pleasure lies in the sweep of land and the burning evening sky, in effect a landscape with a myth attached. It reads as a late, improvisatory picture, far from the finished classical compositions of his Royal Academy prime."},"nga:46064":{"d":"A butter-yellow sky fills the upper two-thirds of a square canvas, with a pale disk, sun or moon, at its center and clouds spiraling around it, shading from golden yellow through slate gray and rust to nearly wine red along the top edge. A band of navy and denim-blue V-shapes, birds, curves around the disk into the distance. In the bottom third an arched, copper-brown structure sits among puffs of forest green and gold that could be trees or crashing waves, flecked with burgundy touches that might be people or animals. Faint outlines suggest a bear, a crocodile and a giraffe.","i":"The subject is the Flood of Genesis, the evening when the waters begin to rise, with the arched structure perhaps the ark and the faint creatures the animals gathering to it. About 1843 Turner exhibited a pair of square Deluge pictures, now in the Tate, that answered Goethe's color theory, then newly translated into English, by setting warm and cool colors in a whirling vortex; this picture is related to that group. The spiral sky turns the whole world toward catastrophe.","c":"This is Turner at the edge of legibility. The spiral of cloud and the dense curve of birds are bold inventions, and the sulfurous yellow gives the sky real menace. But the lower third is a muddle in which ark, animals and water cannot be told apart, and the brushwork there seems unfinished rather than suggestive. Some will call it vision, others a thinking-aloud sketch; it is more interesting for its ambition than for its result."},"nga:46089":{"d":"Across golden haze and gently rippling water dotted with boats, ghostly ivory and parchment-white buildings rise along a distant shore. In the lower right corner, honey-yellow boats are pulled up to a white dock, with touches of charcoal, peacock green, ruby red and mauve suggesting cloth or cargo and strokes of white, pink and brick red hinting at a few figures. On the far horizon stand a golden building with a tower and a cream-white one with two tall domes. The sky is butter yellow with shell pink and a patch of topaz blue, and the painter's initials 'JMWT' seem written on a wall at lower right.","i":"The Dogana, Venice's customs house on its point, and the domed church of Santa Maria della Salute beside it were recurring subjects for Turner, who visited the city in 1819, 1833 and 1840. The golden tower is presumably the Dogana and the two-domed church the Salute. By the 1840s his Venice dissolves stone into light, which baffled some critics; John Ruskin began his defense of Turner in 'Modern Painters', published in 1843.","c":"By 1843 Turner paints the idea of Venice more than the city. The yellow haze, the ghostly architecture and the scribbled signature give the picture the lightness of a watercolor, and sky, water and walls become one shimmering substance. The limits are equally plain: little structure, a foreground of vague smears and a color range so narrow that depth almost goes. Admirers see poetry and doubters see a sketch, and both are partly right."},"nga:117":{"d":"Butter-yellow clouds and water, a periwinkle sky and pale plum-purple buildings blend in hazy horizontal bands. The horizon comes about a third of the way up, and a small, round white sun hangs low at the left, ringed by pale sapphire blue and lavender that give way to lemon-yellow haze across the right of the sky. Vertical swipes of heather pink and cream suggest a town along the horizon. In the foreground the water takes touches of celery green, and brown boats spaced across the harbor carry people and goods toward the town. The paint is thickest along the top of the sky.","i":"Arriving in Venice by water was the traveler's classic first impression, and this late picture of 1844 turns that moment into light and weather: the town is a low band of color, and the sun, water and sky carry the meaning. Turner was nearing seventy and had visited Venice three times, in 1819, 1833 and 1840. Contemporaries met his late pictures with a mixture of wonder and bewilderment at their lack of finish, while later generations found in them a way forward.","c":"Here the picture is almost pure atmosphere. The layered bands of yellow, lilac and blue hold together like a chord, and the thick paint on the sky gives the light a physical presence. The cost is that the approach itself is hard to read: the boats are plain brown marks and the city is a smear. It is not Turner's most resolved Venice, but as a test of how little a painter needs to evoke a place it is persuasive."},"met:437854":{"d":"A pale, almost monochrome canvas of mist and spray. At the centre a three-masted ship under white sails looms like a ghost, its hull lost in foam. At the lower left a dark, rounded shape rising from olive-green water is a whale, and at the right a curved line fans out against the haze, perhaps its tail. Tiny brown boats are scattered in the white surf below the ship. Sky and sea blend together in cream, grey and yellow-green, without a horizon, and the paint is thin and scrubbed in places. Oil on canvas, about 92 by 123 centimetres.","i":"Turner made four whaling pictures, shown in pairs at the Royal Academy in 1845 and 1846, and this belongs with them. The Met connects it with the collector Elhanan Bicknell, who made his fortune in the whale-oil business and whom Turner had hoped would buy it, and records that when shown in 1845 it received a mixed reception. The subject is usually described as a wounded sperm whale thrashing in a sea of foam and blood, with a ghostly three-masted whaling ship behind. The loose paint made some critics struggle to find the subject at all. It shows the late Turner at his most daring: the subject dissolves into weather.","c":"By 1845 Turner no longer described things; he suggested the experience of them. Here the ship is an apparition in a white storm, the whale an ink-dark bulge, and the whole becomes a study of terror and light. Admirers value it as an anticipation of abstraction; sceptics found the effect muddled, and it is true that the forms are hard to read and the surface looks unfinished. Compared with the clarity of his earlier Fighting Temeraire, it trades legibility for pure atmosphere, and the gain is bracing even where the whale is hard to find."},"met:436868":{"d":"A woman in profile is the subject of this small oil on canvas, about 38 by 25 cm, possibly of the late 1870s. The title is a single first name, Lucia, as with many of Leighton's head studies of models, so it is probably a study of a model rather than a commissioned portrait. The profile, a classical format, lets the artist concentrate on line and on the shape of the head.","i":"Leighton, who spent formative years in Rome and later became President of the Royal Academy, made small head studies of women whose beauty he admired, often Italian models, and exhibited and sold them. The profile derives from ancient coins and cameos and from Renaissance portrait medals. In the Aesthetic movement, a cultivated taste valued such pictures of beauty for their own sake, without a story.","c":"Leighton's strength is draftsmanship and a cool, smooth surface, and the profile suits him: a single clean contour carries the picture. The weakness is a certain chill, an idealizing perfection that can feel remote. Small works like this are closer to jewelry than to portraiture. Compared with Rossetti's lush, brooding heads, Leighton's are cooler and more classical, with the strengths and limits of that restraint."},"met:847023":{"d":"Hermes, the messenger god, brings Persephone back from the underworld to her mother Demeter, goddess of the harvest, who waits to receive her, in a small oil on canvas of about 26 by 20 cm, dated about 1890 to 1891. Its small size and date suggest a preliminary sketch for Leighton's large Return of Persephone of 1891, now in Leeds, in which the figures rise from darkness toward the light.","i":"In the Greek myth, Demeter's grief at the loss of her daughter blighted the earth until Zeus sent Hermes to bring Persephone back from Hades. Because she had eaten pomegranate seeds in the underworld, she had to return for part of each year, and the myth explained the cycle of seasons, death in winter and renewal in spring. Victorian viewers read the subject as an allegory of renewal.","c":"A sketch shows decisions, and Leighton's classical clarity is already evident in a small composition that must have counted its figures with care. Where the finished Leeds painting makes its drama by contrasting shadow below with sunlight above, a sketch can only suggest it, and the effect is correspondingly more fleeting. It is a refined, thoughtful working document by a painter who is better known for polish than for spontaneity."},"met:847024":{"d":"A narrow oil study, painted with gold on canvas and measuring about 31 by 18 cm, prepares a ceiling decoration for the music room of Henry G. Marquand, a New York collector and banker. The subjects are Muses, a goddess and allegory, as befits a room devoted to music, and the date is about 1886. The gold suggests a decorative scheme meant to be seen from below, in a setting of rich materials.","i":"The Muses, the nine daughters of Zeus and Memory, presided over the arts, and Euterpe was the muse of music. In the Gilded Age, American millionaires hired leading European artists to decorate their houses, and the London painters of the Aesthetic movement, Leighton among them, were prized for their classical elegance. A ceiling of Muses flattered the patron by associating him with the arts he supported.","c":"A study like this shows Leighton's academic method: careful planning on a small scale before the ceiling itself. The narrow format suits a section of a larger scheme, and the gold gives the sketch a decorative richness. The critic cannot judge the finished ceiling from it, but the study suggests a poised, cool design, more at home with elegant figures than with the exuberance a ceiling might allow. His decorative work is far less known than his easel pictures."},"met:436869":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas shows a woman in black draperies leaning her head against a fluted marble column, her arm crooked over its top beside an urn wreathed with dark greenery. Her eyes are closed, her face is half hidden, and her free hand hangs open at her side. A purple scarf is wound around the column. At its base lie a toppled vase and a laurel wreath tied with a ribbon, and a few brown leaves have fallen on the pale marble pavement. Behind her, a dark hedge and trees are lit by a warm glow of evening. The colours are subdued, and the surface is smooth and highly polished.","i":"The Latin title Lachrymae means tears. Leighton, who was president of the Royal Academy from 1878 until his death in January 1896, painted this figure of grief in 1894–95, among his last major pictures. The mourner has no specific story: the urn, wreath, overturned vase and evening light are the traditional signs of a classical funeral monument, and the picture asks the viewer to feel the sorrow rather than identify a narrative. Such pictures answered the Victorian taste for Greek subjects. The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 1896, soon after the painter's death, a sign of his reputation in America.","c":"Lachrymae is a controlled study in mood. The pose is simple and moving, the column and drapery make a strong vertical, and the dark robes against pale stone form a handsome pattern. But the usual Leighton limits show: the grief is polite, even decorative, the face is hidden so that feeling rests on gesture alone, and the surface is so polished that emotion is held at a distance. It is typical of the academic classicism that fell from favour after his death. As design it succeeds; as an expression of sorrow it stays restrained."},"nga:46127":{"d":"Inside a church-like space of columns, pointed arches and stained glass, eight richly dressed figures stand around an altar. A woman in an ocean-blue robe trimmed with gold and an old man in a royal-purple robe over gold brocade hold a naked baby, long of limb and adult in feature, above the altar; gold lines form their halos. Behind them an old woman in moss green gestures toward the child. At the left an older man holds a wicker birdcage with a slate-blue bird, beside two young women in fur-trimmed black dresses and jeweled headdresses. An apse of three tiers of arches opens beyond.","i":"The scene is from Luke 2: forty days after Christ's birth, Mary and Joseph bring him to the Temple to present him to God and make the offering the law required, which for the poor could be birds. The old man who takes the child is Simeon, who recognized him as the Messiah, and the old woman is probably the prophetess Anna. The man with the cage is probably Joseph. Netherlandish painters set such stories in Gothic churches, so the Temple resembles a church the viewer knew.","c":"The costumes show the strength of the type: jeweled headdresses, fur trim and patterned brocade are rendered with care, and the lined faces of the old man and woman stand out against the smooth, near-identical faces of the young women. The composition is crowded and stagey, with figures lined up around the altar more than interacting, and the architecture is generic. It is a competent, courtly devotional panel, not an innovator's."},"met:435673":{"d":"A harbor view shows the outer harbor at Brest, the French navy's chief Atlantic base, with ships and boats on the water and the port town along the shore; the Met lists ship, boat, city and harbor among its subjects. At about 74 by 107 centimeters, this oil on canvas belongs to the tradition of port views that record a place accurately while filling it with small scenes of vessels and work. Its appeal lies in accuracy and incident rather than in drama.","i":"Brest, at the western tip of Brittany, was where France gathered and repaired its Atlantic fleet, and a precise view of its harbor in 1773 spoke to national pride in a navy rebuilt after the Seven Years' War. Henri Joseph van Blarenberghe was the son of the Lille painter Louis Nicolas van Blarenberghe, and the family specialized in exact views and military subjects, in a tradition that included Joseph Vernet's celebrated series of French ports for Louis XV.","c":"Pictures like this live on accumulated detail, and the pleasure is in finding the small sailors, boats and rigging and in the clarity of the harbor's layout. Van Blarenberghe is an exact recorder rather than an atmospheric painter, and he lacks Joseph Vernet's gift for turning a port into a poem of light. As a document of eighteenth-century naval life such a view is valuable; as a painting it is polite rather than powerful."},"met:15042":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named in the title as James O. Owens, painted in watercolor on a thin sheet of ivory. The whole image stands only 5.9 centimeters high and 4.8 wide, smaller than a playing card, and it would have been kept in a hinged case or worn as a keepsake. Miniatures of this kind usually show the sitter at bust length against a plain or softly clouded ground, with the face given the greatest care, built up from tiny strokes through which the ivory glows.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the portable portraits of the early nineteenth century. Families commissioned them to mark a marriage, a departure or a loss, and friends exchanged them as tokens, so the format carried more feeling than its size suggests. Dated 1832, when Dodge was about twenty-five, this is an early work by an artist who would spend a long career in the format, long enough to see photography take over its trade.","c":"At this scale a miniaturist's skill is economy: a convincing face from a few hundred tiny strokes, with character carried by the eyes and mouth. Dodge belongs to the capable second rank of American miniaturists, good at a plain, readable likeness and rarely ambitious in design. In an early work like this the likeness is everything, and a viewer should hold it to that standard alone."},"met:15043":{"d":"Among the smallest of portrait miniatures, this watercolor on ivory of the Reverend William Lupton Johnson measures only 4.2 by 3.3 centimeters, small enough for a locket. The title marks the sitter as a clergyman. Watercolor miniatures of this kind typically model the face in fine hatching and stippling, laid thinly over the ivory so that its whiteness shows through, while clothing and background are kept broader and quieter.","i":"A clergyman's likeness served family and congregation alike: a portrait could mark an ordination, a new post or a departure, and the title 'Reverend' announces the sitter's calling before anything else. Miniatures were affordable to the professional classes of the 1830s, and a small ivory like this was something a minister's family could keep close or send to relatives far away.","c":"Scale is both the obstacle and the point. Four centimeters leave no room for a drawing-room pose or a rich setting, so the picture depends on a clear head and a steady, believable gaze. Dodge's strength in small formats is a plain, legible likeness; what he rarely gives is flourish, which suits a clergyman. It is a modest object, but modesty was the brief."},"met:10759":{"d":"A palm-sized watercolor-on-ivory portrait, 5.6 by 4.6 centimeters, of the painter George Catlin (1796-1872). As in most miniatures, the format is a head and shoulders, the face worked in minute strokes through which the ivory glows, and the whole made to fit a case or locket. The sitter is named, which gives this small ivory more weight than most anonymous likenesses, and the date, 1835, falls in the years when Catlin was painting the Native peoples of the West.","i":"Catlin is remembered as the artist who traveled to the American West in the 1830s to paint portraits and daily scenes of the Native peoples of the Plains, and who later toured his 'Indian Gallery' in the United States and Europe. He had begun as a portraitist, some of his early work in miniature, so for a fellow painter such as Dodge a Catlin likeness was an exchange between colleagues.","c":"The interest of this miniature lies in its sitter more than its handling. Dodge works in the conventional idiom of the day, a head and shoulders with a clean, readable likeness, and nothing in the format allows for the open-air immediacy of Catlin's own Plains pictures. That contrast is the fair criticism: a plain, careful record of a man whose own art was anything but plain. As a document it is worth having."},"met:15044":{"d":"A larger-than-usual miniature, 9.5 by 8.2 centimeters, of a man named Edward S. Dodge, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1835. The shared surname with the artist suggests a relative. As in many family portraits the sitter appears at head and shoulders, with the face modeled in small strokes and the clothing and background treated more broadly. At this size the translucent ivory, with its natural grain, can show through the thin paint and lend the flesh tones a faint inner glow.","i":"Painters often portrayed their own relatives, and miniaturists in particular used family members as showpieces, as gifts or as practice between paying commissions. With no patron on record, a portrait of a man who shares the artist's name may be best read as a private exchange rather than a commission: maker and sitter knew each other well, and the likeness was a personal record.","c":"A generous ivory lets a painter prove himself: at 9.5 centimeters the face has room for real modeling rather than mere outline. It also exposes weaknesses, because the larger the sheet, the harder it is to keep stippling even and the flesh tones free of blotches. If the sitter is indeed a relative, the likeness may have been painted with more freedom and less flattery than a commission, which is the quality to look for."},"met:17901":{"d":"The artist's wife, named in the title as Mary Louise Dodge, portrayed in watercolor on ivory in 1836. At 12.1 by 9.2 centimeters it is large for the format, closer to the size of a postcard than to the usual pocket miniature, and it gives the painter room to work a bust or half-length on a thin upright sheet of ivory. A work of this size was probably framed or cased as a prized possession of the household.","i":"Artists often painted their own families, and a wife's portrait could serve as showpiece, practice and keepsake at once. For a miniaturist, the likeness of the person closest to him also advertised his skill, since family and friends are the harshest judges of a likeness. Dodge painted this in 1836, when miniatures were still the standard way of recording a face and the camera had not yet arrived in America.","c":"A portrait of one's own wife leaves little room for polite fiction, and the size gives Dodge more space than he had for most commissions. That makes it the likeliest place to look for his best work: a face with some depth, modeled over a wider area, rather than the compact formula of a stock ivory. The occasion invites more care than a paying sitter would, and the viewer should look for it in the eyes and the flesh tones."},"met:15045":{"d":"A man identified as A. L. Clements, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1838 at 6.0 by 4.8 centimeters, about the size of a business card. It has a companion, a portrait of Mrs. A. L. Clements (Mary Louisa Wells), made in the same year at nearly the same size, so the two were probably painted as a pair. The sitter is shown as is usual for the format, at bust length, with the face built up in tiny strokes over the translucent ivory.","i":"Husband-and-wife miniatures were often commissioned together, to be kept in matching cases, hung as pendants or carried by different members of the family. Two portraits of the same year, size and painter, one of A. L. Clements and one of Mrs. A. L. Clements, were probably meant to be seen together, a quiet record of a marriage at the time the portraits were made.","c":"A pair is only as good as its weaker half, so the thing to watch is whether husband and wife receive equal attention in the eyes and flesh tones. At about six centimeters each there is no room for display, and Dodge's career rests on exactly this kind of reliable, unshowy work, which kept a miniaturist supplied with commissions. Taken together, the two ivories give a better picture of a household than either does alone."},"met:15046":{"d":"A woman identified as Mary Louisa Wells, Mrs. A. L. Clements, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1838 at 6.4 by 5.0 centimeters, a little taller and wider than the miniature of her husband made in the same year. The title gives her married name with her maiden name in parentheses, a cataloguing habit that keeps her family of origin attached to her likeness. Like most ivories of the period, it is a bust-length portrait small enough to hold in a closed hand.","i":"Married women are recorded by their husbands' names in the title and by their maiden names in parentheses, which preserves two family lines in a single label. A miniature of this kind, probably made as a companion to her husband's, would have been kept by the couple, their children or relatives, and many such ivories survive only because they were handed down as family treasures.","c":"The slightly larger size of her ivory is the only obvious difference between the two portraits, and in the end the question is the same as for her husband's: whether the eyes, the flesh tones and the set of the mouth suggest a living person. A miniature of a wife often asks for a little more softness than a husband's, and the painter's task is to give it without losing the likeness."},"met:15047":{"d":"An unidentified man, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1833 and measuring 6.7 by 5.5 centimeters. His name did not travel with the portrait, so he is recorded only as a gentleman, which in the language of the period meant a man of some standing. The thin, translucent ivory gives flesh tones a soft inner glow, and, as in most miniatures, the head is the finished part, with clothing and background treated more broadly.","i":"Anonymity is common among miniatures. These were intimate objects, kept in cases or drawers, and when a family line ended or a collection was sold the sitter's name was often lost, leaving the type alone: a respectable man of the 1830s presented in the dress and manner his time thought dignified. Even without a name, the portrait records how men of the middle and professional classes chose to be remembered.","c":"With no name to lean on, the picture must succeed or fail on the likeness alone. The brief for such a portrait was a recognizable, respectable face, and the thing to judge is whether the eyes have life and the flesh tones have been built up evenly. It is the sparkle that distinguishes the best miniatures from the many merely sound ones, and an anonymous sitter makes that difference easier to see."},"met:15048":{"d":"An unidentified woman, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1835 at 7.1 by 5.6 centimeters, held by the Met as a portrait of a lady. The title records only her sex and station, so what can be said is the type: a bust-length likeness on a small upright sheet of ivory, the face built in fine strokes and framed by the hair and dress of the mid-1830s, when women's styles were elaborate and sleeves were wide.","i":"A miniature of an unnamed lady was once someone's treasured possession, given by a husband or suitor or made for parents, children or friends. Fashion helps with dating, since women's hairstyles and sleeves of the mid-1830s were distinctive and are often used to place such miniatures when records are missing. The picture survives for what it shows of period taste as much as for the individual sitter.","c":"Women's miniatures of the 1830s invite flattery, and the thing to look for is where the painter resisted it: a face with its own irregularities rather than a general prettiness. Without a name the social interest falls away and everything rests on that. It should be judged as a working professional's product, serviceable first, and distinguished only if the eyes and flesh tones persuade."},"met:15049":{"d":"An unnamed woman painted in watercolor on ivory about 1835, 7.2 by 5.8 centimeters. The Met catalogs her only as a lady, a label that gives her likely standing but not her name, and its keywords add just a woman and a portrait. Like most miniatures of the period it is a bust-length likeness, the face finished in tiny, fine strokes, the hair and dress treated more simply, and the whole small enough to be tucked into a case or worn.","i":"Anonymous ladies of the 1830s were typically the wives and daughters of the merchants, lawyers and landowners who formed the clientele of American miniaturists. Their portraits were small proofs of prosperity: affordable, personal and easy to give away. Today an unnamed ivory like this is best read as a social type, the respectable woman of her day, composed to be seen by family and close friends.","c":"A face without a name has to be judged as a face, which is a fair test. The best miniatures of this kind keep a little asymmetry, a trace of humor or tiredness around the eyes, so that a person emerges from the type. Whether this ivory has that spark depends on close looking; as a document of dress, hair and format it is a sound example of mid-1830s American work."},"met:13024":{"d":"An unidentified man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1841 and measuring 7.3 by 6.0 centimeters, a little larger than a business card. Recorded only as a gentleman, he is shown in the conventional format of the day, head and shoulders, with the face built from fine strokes through which the white of the ivory glows, and the dress and ground handled more broadly.","i":"The year 1841 fell just after the daguerreotype reached America, and the first portrait studios opened in New York in 1840. Miniaturists suddenly faced a competitor that produced a likeness in minutes and for a fraction of the price. Some responded by making their work larger, richer in color and more finished, qualities a metal plate could not offer, and works like this record the trade at the moment of change.","c":"By 1841 a miniature had to offer what the new plates could not: color, a warm finish and the sense of a hand at work. That is the standard to hold this ivory to. A well-made watercolor could still seem warmer and more flattering than the mirror-like daguerreotype, and for another decade the two kinds of portrait sat side by side on parlor tables."},"met:10761":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. George P. Burne, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1852 at 6.2 by 4.9 centimeters. It keeps to the older, intimate scale of the locket and the pocket case, with the likeness concentrated in a small area of finely worked flesh tones and the rest kept simple. The married name in the title is the only identification given. Painted in 1852, thirteen years after the daguerreotype was announced, it is a deliberate choice of the older art, made by a sitter who could have had a photograph for less.","i":"By 1852 the daguerreotype was everywhere, cheap and fast, yet some clients still paid for a painted miniature. Color, a flattering finish and the look of a handmade object were its selling points, and many miniaturists of the 1850s worked from, or even over, a photograph. This likeness may have been taken from life or from an image; either way it shows a family choosing paint over the camera.","c":"In 1852 a painted miniature was a luxury, and the extra cost has to be justified on the ivory itself: in the skin tones, the warm color and a sense of personality that the daguerreotype's fixed stare lacks. In so small a format Dodge can offer only some of that, but a face worked by hand still carries a charm the plate could not imitate."},"met:10760":{"d":"A girl named Kate Roselie Dodge, painted with a book in watercolor on ivory in 1854 at 7.6 by 6.4 centimeters. Her surname is the artist's own, so she was probably a relative, perhaps his daughter. The Met's keywords record a girl, a book and a portrait. As in many children's portraits of the period, the book is a prop that tells the viewer something about the sitter, and the fine, soft modeling is concentrated in the face.","i":"A book in a child's portrait of the 1850s signals education and good upbringing: in an age that put growing emphasis on children's moral and intellectual training at home, a girl with a book was shown as a promise of the cultured adult she would become. For a painter making a portrait of his own kin, the object is also a private family record rather than a commission.","c":"Children are hard for a miniaturist: faces have few lines to hold on to, and families want sweetness without sentimentality. A family portrait gives the painter freedom to avoid both the stiff pose and the doll-like face, and that freedom is what to look for here, in how the girl relates to her book. In a career of competent likenesses, such personal pieces are where character is most likely to show."},"met:15050":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Annie C. Hyde, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1863 at 9.2 by 7.3 centimeters, large for the format. Her portrait belongs to the last years of the miniature's commercial life, when it was still made for those who preferred a painted, personal likeness. Such an ivory would have been framed or cased, with the face built up in fine strokes over the translucent surface.","i":"In 1863, the third year of the Civil War, the carte de visite photograph had become the standard portrait, and a painted miniature was a deliberate, costlier choice. Many miniaturists by then worked from photographs, so the likeness may owe something to the camera. The war years also made portraits of loved ones precious, and a hand-painted ivory could serve as a keepsake for those who had been parted or bereaved.","c":"A miniature in the year of Gettysburg is the survival of an older art in the age of the carte de visite. If it was painted from a photograph, as many were by then, the painter's contribution was color and finish, and the question to ask is whether the face keeps the warmth of a handmade likeness or the flat evenness of a copy. At 9.2 centimeters it has room to show which."},"met:12592":{"d":"A small portrait of an infant, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1833 and measuring 5.7 by 4.8 centimeters. The baby is unnamed; the Met's keywords record an infant and a portrait. Miniatures of babies usually concentrate on the round face and the wisps of hair, with a cap, gown or blanket indicated more lightly, so that the small scale suits a small sitter.","i":"Parents of the 1830s commissioned likenesses of infants as keepsakes at a time when many children did not survive early childhood. A baby's miniature might celebrate a birth or serve as a memorial, and a viewer can only guess which this was. Because babies could not sit still, painters sometimes worked quickly from sketches or from the quiet child, then finished the likeness at the desk.","c":"Babies are a test for a portraitist: unformed features, no expression to hold, and parents who expect sweetness. The best infant miniatures avoid the doll-like prettiness clients often wanted and keep something of a particular child's weight and openness. At under six centimeters, Shumway had little room to show more than that, and the picture should be judged on whether it feels like a baby or an emblem of one."},"met:14994":{"d":"A man named William Boerum, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1835. Its format is almost square, 6.6 centimeters high by 6.8 wide, which is unusual, since most miniatures of the period are upright ovals or rectangles. A square-ish sheet gives more room at the sides than an upright oval would, so the head and shoulders sit in a broader field.","i":"By the mid-1830s American miniaturists were trying larger and more varied formats to keep their patrons, as the miniature moved from pocket keepsake toward small framed picture for the parlor wall. A near-square ivory may reflect that shift. Who chose the shape, the sitter or the artist, is not known, but it makes the portrait feel less like a locket and more like a little easel painting.","c":"The shape is the most interesting thing here. A nearly square ground changes the problem for the painter: a head placed in the middle can float without the support of an oval, so success depends on how well the shoulders and background are balanced. Shumway's task is to keep the face as the focus, and the likeness should be judged on that."},"met:12591":{"d":"A small miniature, 5.2 by 4.0 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1842, of a man named Henry Peters Gray, presumably the New York painter of that name (1819-1877). At this size the portrait would have been kept as a personal token or exchanged between friends rather than shown on a wall, a head-and-shoulders likeness built up in tiny strokes.","i":"Gray was a New York painter of portraits and allegorical pictures, so this is probably a portrait of one artist by another. Artists often exchanged likenesses with colleagues and friends, and a miniature, small and portable, was the natural form for such a token. The portrait therefore says less about public rank than about friendship and professional circle in the New York art world of the early 1840s.","c":"A painter sitting for a painter is a good test of the maker, since the sitter is a professional judge of likeness. Shumway's miniature, at barely five centimeters, can offer only a head and the suggestion of a coat, and the question is whether there is life in the eyes. It is a modest, intimate object whose main value is probably as a record of an artistic friendship."},"met:19718":{"d":"A woman named Catherine Augusta Wilmerding, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1843. Unusually the sheet is wider than it is tall, 9.5 centimeters high by 12.1 wide, so the portrait is horizontal in format rather than the usual upright oval. The Met's subject tags list a collar and a necklace among the details, so costume and jewelry are part of the portrait, along with the face.","i":"A collar and necklace were standard marks of respectability and means for a woman sitter, and a horizontal format gives them room to be seen. By the 1840s miniatures had grown larger and more like small paintings, partly in competition with the daguerreotype, and this one is ambitious in scale. The sitter is named in full, as befits a portrait meant to be kept and passed down.","c":"The wide format is a gamble: a horizontal sheet suits a bust with shoulders and ornament but invites emptiness at the sides. Shumway's choice gives the costume details a prominence that smaller ivories deny, and the portrait is as much about the necklace and collar as about the face. It is a bolder design than most miniatures of the decade and worth looking at for the layout alone."},"met:12989":{"d":"A man named Powell MacRae, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1845 at 11.3 by 8.9 centimeters, a large miniature for its day. At this size the artist had room for a half-length figure and more of the sitter's dress and surroundings than a pocket miniature allows, rather than a head alone, and the thin ivory is large enough to show its natural translucency.","i":"By the mid-1840s miniatures were frequently larger, as painters tried to hold their place against the daguerreotype by offering what it could not: color, finish and the look of a painted picture. Such a large ivory was costly, and its owner presumably wanted a portrait for display as well as for keeping, a little picture that could stand on a table or hang on a wall.","c":"The larger the ivory, the more clearly the painter's habits show: how the flesh is stippled, how the ground is laid, whether the clothing is more than a dark shape. At 11.3 centimeters Shumway had room to show off, and a critic should check whether he did or whether the extra space is merely filled. Larger miniatures of the 1840s often gain presence but lose the jewel-like concentration of the small ones."},"met:15188":{"d":"A woman identified as Mrs. Samuel Russell, born Frances Ann Osborne, painted in watercolor on ivory. It measures 8.7 by 6.6 centimeters, a mid-sized miniature, and no date is recorded for it. The title gives her married name followed by her maiden name in parentheses, a way of keeping her family of origin attached to her portrait. The face is the carefully finished part, as usual in miniatures.","i":"A woman is identified here, as often in American collections, through her husband and her father's family, which reflects how women were named in the records of the time. Without a date, the portrait must be placed by style and costume alone, a common difficulty with miniatures, which were rarely signed or dated. Such likenesses were made for the family circle, often as one of a pair with a husband's portrait.","c":"With no date and little documentation, this ivory depends on its likeness and finish for any claim on attention, and a viewer should look at the stippling in the cheeks and the care taken over the eyes. Shumway was a busy professional, and miniatures like this are best valued as the record of a named woman, not as experiments."},"met:12530":{"d":"A woman identified as Mrs. George M. Gill, born Ann McKim Bowly, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1841. At 12.7 by 9.8 centimeters it is a large miniature, big enough to hold a half-length figure with some of her dress and surroundings. The title gives her married name with her maiden name in parentheses. The size is close to that of a postcard, so the ivory would have been framed or stood on a table rather than slipped into a locket. Large ivories were harder to prepare than small ones, since the thin sheet had to be cut and flattened without warping.","i":"Saunders was a British-born miniaturist, and by 1841 the large, richly colored watercolor miniature was one answer to the new daguerreotype, offering color and an air of luxury the plate could not. A sitter who commissioned a miniature of this size was paying for display as well as memory, and the maiden name in the title records the families she linked.","c":"At this size a miniaturist cannot hide: the wide expanse of ivory shows every weakness in the stippling, and the face has to hold its own against dress and ground. The thing to judge is whether the flesh tones stay fresh and alive over so much area. In the 1840s, large miniatures like this represent the format at its most ambitious, and also at its most exposed."},"met:15183":{"d":"A woman named as Mrs. Israel Thorndike, born Sarah Dana, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1843 and measuring 9.3 by 7.4 centimeters. As in most miniatures, the face is the most finished part, built up in fine strokes over the translucent ivory, while the dress and the ground behind her are handled more broadly. The title gives her married name first and her maiden name in parentheses, the cataloguing habit that keeps both family lines attached to the portrait. At nearly ten centimeters tall it would have been framed or set in a lined case, a prized object rather than a pocket token.","i":"Thorndike is the name of a prominent Massachusetts family of merchants and shipowners, and the title preserves the sitter's maiden name, Dana. Portraits of this size and finish were costly, and in New England they were a conventional sign of family standing. A British-born miniaturist working for American clients, as Saunders did, was part of a transatlantic market in likenesses.","c":"At about nine centimeters there is enough area to build modeling into the flesh, and that is where a miniature of this kind is won or lost. The measure of the portrait is whether the sitter seems a person with a history or a polite emblem of a family name; in the 1840s the second was the commoner result. Saunders is best judged against other miniatures of his date rather than against the camera."},"met:12532":{"d":"An unidentified man, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1845 to 1850, measuring 13.3 by 11.0 centimeters, very large for a miniature. The title records only a gentleman. At this scale the sitter can be shown at half length, with room for a good deal of dress and background, and the thin ivory becomes a small picture surface rather than a pocket keepsake.","i":"By the late 1840s the miniature was fighting to stay relevant. Larger ivories like this one set the sitter at half length and aimed to rival small oil portraits in presence and richness of color, as the daguerreotype took over the business of quick, cheap likenesses. The anonymity of the sitter is typical: such objects often lost their names as they passed through families and dealers.","c":"At 13.3 centimeters the miniature approaches the condition of a small portrait, and with that goes the risk of the medium's limits: delicate stippling can look fussy at scale, and watercolor on ivory cannot match oil's depth. A viewer should look for whether the face keeps the vividness of the best small ivories or whether the size has diluted it. Saunders was evidently confident enough to attempt a format at which many painters faltered."},"met:15182":{"d":"A man named in the title as Jubal Anderson Early, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1847-48 at 10.0 by 7.6 centimeters. The sitter is presumably the Virginia lawyer and soldier (1816-1894) who became a Confederate lieutenant general; the likeness shows him long before that, as a man in his early thirties, in the small, intimate format of the portrait miniature.","i":"Early was a Virginia lawyer and former legislator who served as a major of volunteers in the Mexican War of 1846-48, the same years as this portrait. His fame came later, as a Confederate general whose army reached the outskirts of Washington in July 1864. A miniature of this date belongs to the sitter's private life, probably a keepsake for family, long before his name entered the nation's military history.","c":"The interest in this miniature is almost wholly in the sitter. Nothing in the format allows for a soldier's drama, and a portrait made in 1847-48 contains no hint of the later general. Saunders's job was a clean, respectable likeness, and the viewer is better placed to look at the face and to ask what a portrait can and cannot tell about a man's later history. As art it is competent; as a document it is unusually loaded."},"met:437765":{"d":"An unidentified man, painted on ivory around 1825 by the miniaturist Samuel John Stump (1778-1863). No size is recorded, but portrait miniatures of this type were normally only a few centimeters high, made to be held in the hand, fitted into a case or set in a frame, and seen close up. The likeness is a head-and-shoulders study, built from tiny strokes over the translucent ivory.","i":"Stump had a long career, and he worked in the period when the portrait miniature was at the height of its fashion, before the camera. Such likenesses were exchanged between family and friends, sent abroad with travelers and carried in the pocket. An unnamed sitter is common; the likeness survived but the identity did not, which is part of what makes the object poignant.","c":"A prolific professional like Stump supplied the early nineteenth century with polished, flattering likenesses, and the best of them combine a soft, luminous finish with a genuine hold on the sitter's character. The weaker ones slip into a gloss that makes everyone look alike. An anonymous man by him is best valued as a sample of the type at its most characteristic: smooth, clear and quietly dignified."},"met:439337":{"d":"A man presented, as the title announces, as a 'Mamelouk', painted in oil on a canvas about 75 by 61.5 centimeters. The sitter is identified as Roustam Raza, and the Met's keywords note hills, so the figure is probably set out of doors against a landscape. The costume is the exotic dress of the mameluke warrior, which is the key to the picture's appeal and its place among Orientalist portraits.","i":"Roustam Raza, born in the Caucasus about 1782, was given to Napoleon Bonaparte in Egypt in 1799 and served him as personal attendant and bodyguard until 1814, wearing the mameluke costume that became part of the Napoleonic image. The picture dates from 1810, when Vernet was about twenty-one and the exotic costume was fashionable in Paris. It shows an early taste for Orientalist subjects that would return in his later career.","c":"Roustam is a gift to a young painter: a striking costume, a pose to invent and a sitter of some fame. The skill lies in treating him as a person rather than an exotic prop, and the most telling place to look is the face. Vernet at twenty was already fluent, but fluency was always his danger, and a critic will ask whether the portrait goes beyond the dazzle of costume."},"met:437888":{"d":"A crowd of spectators, men and horses gathers for the opening of a race, in a small canvas of 46 by 54 centimeters. The title refers to the riderless horse race of the Roman Carnival, the corsa dei barberi, in which horses without jockeys were released to run the length of the Corso; the Met's keywords record horses, men and spectators. It is a cabinet picture of popular spectacle, crowded and lively.","i":"The race was one of the great shows of the Roman Carnival: riderless Barbary horses were held at a rope at the Piazza del Popolo and then loosed to gallop down the Via del Corso between crowds of spectators. Géricault, Vernet's close friend, had made it his subject in 1817, and Vernet's version belongs to the French fashion for scenes of Roman popular life that grew up among artists living in Rome.","c":"Vernet's gift is for crowds in motion, and he arranges men, horses and onlookers with a fluent clarity; Géricault never finished his own large version of the subject. What Vernet lacks is Géricault's tragic grandeur: the scene is reported, not felt. Baudelaire later sneered at him as a soldier who paints, and the barb has some justice, though a small canvas like this shows the reporter at his most agreeable."},"met:441378":{"d":"A single armed figure, an 'Arab warrior' in the artist's title, is shown in oil on a small upright canvas of 31.8 by 22.2 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a soldier and a man, and classify the picture as Orientalist. As a small figure study, it is the kind of cabinet picture a painter made to try out costume, pose and color before or beside larger commissions.","i":"It was painted well before Vernet first visited North Africa in the 1830s, so it is based on studio costumes, prints and the memory of Napoleon's Egyptian campaign, not on observation. Orientalism in this period often meant a Western idea of the East: the horseman and warrior, bold, picturesque and violent, stood for a world imagined as the opposite of modern Europe.","c":"The picture reflects a taste, not a discovery. Vernet's fluent brush gives the figure energy, but the costume outweighs the character, and the 'Arab' is a type rather than a person. The viewer should read it as an early step in a career that later made much of North African subjects, less as an observation than as an image Parisian audiences wished to see."},"met:438033":{"d":"A portrait of the painter Théodore Géricault (1791-1824), in oil on a vertical canvas of 47.3 by 38.4 centimeters, dated by the Met to probably 1822 or 1823. It is a modest-sized likeness of the kind painted for friends and kept in the studio, made within about two years of the sitter's death at thirty-two. A portrait of one painter by another invites comparison with the sitter's own art: Géricault painted soldiers, horses, madmen and the dead with a fierce physical truth, and a viewer can look for that intensity in his face as Vernet records it.","i":"Vernet and Géricault were friends, drawn together by a shared passion for horses and by the studio of Vernet's father, Carle, where Géricault had studied. By 1822 Géricault, famed for The Raft of the Medusa, was in declining health after riding accidents, and he died in January 1824. Vernet's portrait is therefore an early tribute to a colleague whose reputation would grow enormously after his death.","c":"Vernet was a quick, accomplished painter rather than a deep one, and a portrait of Géricault makes the contrast explicit: the sitter's brooding intensity is the subject, and a fluent hand can only record it. If Vernet catches a flicker of that intensity the picture is valuable; if not, it remains a document of a friendship. Either way it is an important image of a central figure of French Romanticism."},"met:437887":{"d":"A portrait of the Danish sculptor Bertel Thorvaldsen (1768-1844) shown beside a portrait bust, which the title identifies as a bust of Horace Vernet, the painter himself. It is a vertical oil on canvas, 96.5 by 74.9 centimeters; the Met's keywords record a man, sculpture, an artist and a portrait. A painter thus portrays a sculptor beside the sculptor's portrait of the painter, a double compliment between two artists.","i":"Thorvaldsen, the leading neoclassical sculptor of his day, had worked in Rome since 1797, and Vernet directed the French Academy in Rome from 1829 to 1834, so the two were colleagues in the same city. Showing a sculptor with a bust invites comparison between painting and sculpture, an old debate among artists, and by including his own likeness in sculpture Vernet makes the picture a statement of friendship and mutual respect.","c":"The conceit is clever, and a painter who shows another man's sculpture of himself is paying a double compliment. The technical interest lies in painting stone beside living flesh. As ever with Vernet, fluency tends to stop short of depth, and the sitter may be recorded rather than interpreted. It is best taken as a document of friendship between two leading artists in Rome and as a witty pairing of media."},"nga:129432":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, 100 by 150.7 centimeters, whose title names the moment: a party leaving for the hunt in the Pontine Marshes, the low coastal plain south of Rome. It is closely related in subject, date and size to a second Pontine Marshes hunt painted in the same year, and the two were probably conceived as companions. The picture belongs to the type of the sporting genre scene, with, presumably, riders, horses and open country arranged across a wide panoramic format.","i":"The Pontine Marshes, a malarial lowland of reed beds and pools between Rome and the sea, were long a place for boar and wildfowl hunting by Roman nobles and foreign visitors, and their mounted herdsmen, the butteri, became a picturesque type. Vernet was then director of the French Academy in Rome, and the marshes were a fashionable subject among French artists there after Leopold Robert's 'Arrival of the Harvesters in the Pontine Marshes' succeeded at the Paris Salon of 1831.","c":"Vernet had a gift for organizing figures and animals in space and for the quick, legible narrative that gave the Salon public what it wanted, and a wide hunting picture suits him. His color and handling can be conventional and his sentiment external, so the pleasure lies in the staging rather than in any depth. Seen with its companion, it makes a small narrative of the day: first setting out, then the chase."},"nga:70970":{"d":"A wide canvas, 100 by 137 centimeters, showing the hunt itself in the Pontine Marshes, the low, marshy plain south of Rome. It follows a companion of the same year that shows the departure, and is somewhat narrower. The title and the type point to a sporting genre scene of figures, horses and game in open, level country, with the hunt, rather than any portrait or view, as its subject.","i":"Hunting in the marshes combined sport and local color, and for a French painter in Rome it offered a modern, picturesque alternative to antique subjects. The Pontine Marshes then still lay largely undrained and unhealthy, a wild frontier a few hours from the capital. The pictures reflect the early nineteenth century's taste for regional types and rural customs in the Roman countryside, which painters sold to visitors and to patrons at home.","c":"As with much of Vernet's work, the strength is in the choreography: the dispersal of riders and animals through space and the legible action. The weakness is a lack of resonance, since the hunt is reportage, bright and efficient but not deeply felt. Beside the horses and hunts of Géricault or Delacroix it looks smooth and a little external, though as a record of a Roman sporting world it has real documentary interest."},"met:436871":{"d":"Two sisters shown together on a large horizontal canvas, 130.2 by 170.2 centimeters: Mary Capel (1630-1715), later Duchess of Beaufort, and Elizabeth Capel (1633-1678), Countess of Carnarvon, as the title says. The Met's catalog tags include women and a portrait. In his double portraits of women, Lely typically posed his sitters in loose, silky draperies, with soft faces and languid attitudes, sometimes in a garden or park setting.","i":"Mary and Elizabeth were daughters of Arthur Capel, the Royalist executed in 1649, and their brother Arthur was created Earl of Essex in 1661, so they belonged to one of the Royalist families rewarded after the Restoration. Mary, as Duchess of Beaufort, became known as a gardener and plant collector at Badminton. The picture reflects the court's taste for double portraits of sisters and friends, with beauty and a relaxed, informal elegance meant to flatter.","c":"Lely's formula is easy to criticize: drowsy eyes, creamy skin and shimmering satin repeated across the court until all the beauties look like cousins. Yet a double portrait gives him a real compositional task, balancing two figures and two glances, and the satin is painted with a virtuoso's relish. It is more convincing as a record of fashion and rank than of two particular minds, which is what his clients wanted."},"met:436873":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Sir Henry Capel (1638-1696), painted about 1664, when he was in his mid-twenties, in oil on a vertical canvas of 126.4 by 102.9 centimeters. The Met's keywords record a man, a portrait and a sculpture, so a sculpted element appears in the setting. The canvas measures about fifty by forty inches, a standard English portrait size that suits a figure shown to the knees with a few props.","i":"Henry Capel was the younger brother of the two sisters in the companion double portrait, and he became a politician, was raised to the peerage as Baron Capell of Tewkesbury in 1692 and served as Lord Deputy of Ireland in 1695-96. A piece of sculpture in a portrait was a standard way of signaling classical learning and good taste, qualities a young gentleman of the Restoration court wished to display.","c":"This is Lely in his formal mode, inheriting Van Dyck's elegance but with a heavier, more sensual finish. The pose and props are conventional, and the interest depends on how much of the man comes through dress and gesture. Lely is at his best with silks, hands and the sheen of a face; his weak point is a certain sameness of invention, and a young man of twenty-six invites little psychological depth."},"met:436872":{"d":"An oil study of an unidentified woman, painted in the 1670s on a canvas 67.3 by 53.7 centimeters, smaller than a finished portrait. The title marks it as preparatory: a working record of a sitter's head, probably to be used for a full-scale commission. Studies of this kind tend to concentrate on the face and the pose, leaving costume and background loosely indicated.","i":"Lely ran the largest portrait studio in Restoration London, and a study like this belongs to its working method: the sitter's face painted with care, the rest completed in the studio by assistants working from stock poses and costume models. Seeing the process exposed is one of the pleasures of such pictures, which show Lely closer to observation than his polished finished portraits. The sitter's identity is lost, but the practice remains clear.","c":"A study frees Lely from the formula of the finished portrait. Without the pressure to flatter and to finish the satin, a face can be direct and observed, which is why such sketches are often preferred today to the court beauties they prepared. Whether this sitter has that freshness depends on the handling, but the genre's promise is a sharper sense of a real woman than the finished product would allow."},"nga:0":{"d":"A half-length man turns his head slightly to the left against a gold ground, within a decorative border of black, red and light blue. He has reddish-blond shoulder-length hair, thin eyebrows, a long nose and a light brown beard and mustache. He wears a light pink robe with a gold border at the neckline and a dark blue cloth over his left shoulder, and he holds a rolled scroll in both hands. A thin circle around his head marks a halo. The tempera panel, 62.2 by 34.8 centimeters, is a narrow upright of the kind used for a single figure in a larger altarpiece.","i":"James the Greater, brother of John and one of the first apostles Christ called, was beheaded under Herod Agrippa about the year 44, the first apostle to be martyred. His relics were later venerated at Santiago de Compostela, which is why he often appears as a pilgrim with staff and scallop shell; here the scroll is the general sign of an apostle bearing witness. Panels like this probably stood beside Christ and other saints in a polyptych.","c":"Grifo di Tancredi works in the Florentine manner of around 1300, calm, linear and decorative, with a gold ground and a softly modeled face. The figure is dignified and without drama; compared with Giotto's weighty forms of the same decade it is flatter and more conservative, the work of a painter faithful to older formulas rather than a pioneer. Its charm is quiet: the clear head, the elegant drapery and the rich color."},"nga:33":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, Christ faces the viewer against a gold ground. He has long honey-blond hair and beard, a long face and nose, and almond-shaped hazel-brown eyes under arched brows. His rose-pink robe is covered by a spruce-blue mantle trimmed with gold; his right hand is raised with two fingers extended in blessing, and the other holds an open book inscribed in gold. A halo is incised into the gold, and the panel's five-sided arched top frames his head, with a border of gray circles and scarlet diamonds on navy running behind him.","i":"The open book carries Latin words from the Gospel of John in which Christ declares himself the light of the world, and the raised two fingers are the gesture of blessing. The half-length Christ in blessing, taken from Byzantine images of Christ as ruler of all, was a standard devotional image in Italy around 1300. The five-sided arched top is typical of the central panel of a polyptych, probably with saints such as James and Peter on either side.","c":"The type is old and the treatment restrained: a long, calm face, a spare gesture, and a gold field that makes the figure seem to hover. The border that appears to run behind him gives a hint of space more advanced than the gold alone. It is not an inventive picture, but it has the poised, sober dignity that these polyptych centers aimed at. Compared with Giotto, it is more decorative than dramatic."},"nga:34":{"d":"A half-length man with white hair and a white beard turns his head slightly to the right against a gold ground. He wears an orange cloth draped over a dark blue garment embroidered in gold at the shoulder and neckline. In his left hand he holds two large red keys, and in his right a thin red rod topped by a cross. A faint outline of a halo surrounds his head, and a red, white and black patterned border encloses the gold. The tempera panel, 61.5 by 34.5 centimeters, is a narrow upright of the kind used for a single saint.","i":"Saint Peter, the fisherman Christ called to be the first of the apostles, is identified by the keys to the kingdom of heaven that Christ promised him in the Gospel of Matthew; the cross-topped rod may allude to his role as the first bishop of Rome. His white hair and beard are the standard Italian way of showing him. Such panels invited the devout to pray for his intercession from an altarpiece.","c":"The figure is simple, upright and confident, and the strong orange against the dark blue is the picture's best idea, a color accent that holds in a gold field. The drawing is flat, with little weight under the cloth, and the pose barely varies from its neighbors in the series. As a piece of early Florentine painting it is competent and dignified, an example of the conservative current around 1310 alongside Giotto's innovations."},"nga:45854":{"d":"A young girl stands full-length in a darkened room, facing the viewer with steady blue eyes, her brown hair parted in the middle and pulled back. Her shoulders are bare above the lavender dress of the title, which ends in short ruffled sleeves; a coral-red bead necklace circles her throat, and a black apron covers the skirt, with white petticoats showing below like trouser legs. In one hand she holds a gray glass basket of red roses and a blue flower; the other touches a rose bush in a red pot. A fallen blossom lies beside a brown-and-white kitten on an emerald-green carpet.","i":"Children's portraits in this plain, linear manner were typically painted around 1840 by traveling or small-town artists for families of modest means, and the sitter's name is not recorded here. The accessories carry the meaning. Flowers commonly stand for youth and its brevity, so the fallen blossom may be a quiet reminder of that, while the kitten and the neat clothes advertise a cared-for child in a comfortable home.","c":"The picture works through pattern, not depth. The black apron, white petticoats and green carpet read as crisp, flat shapes, the carpet's perspective is shaky, and the modeling of the face is minimal. Yet the frontal stare, the affectionate detail of beads, basket and kitten, and the bold color give the child a presence that more polished provincial portraits often lack. It is a charming and direct example of American folk portraiture, if not a subtle one."},"met:10210":{"d":"A young girl is shown with a cat, wearing a bonnet and jewelry, with a rose and other flowers in the picture; these are the details the Met's keywords record. The oil on canvas, 86.4 by 68.9 centimeters, is a full child's portrait on the scale of a family heirloom, the kind of picture hung in a parlor. The painter, John Bradley, is recorded as active between 1832 and 1847.","i":"Portraits of children with pets and flowers were popular in the United States in the 1840s, when painters of modest training, many of them itinerant, supplied bright, carefully detailed likenesses to families in towns and farming country. The cat suggests domestic affection, and flowers, roses above all, are often read as symbols of youth's beauty and brevity. The sitter is recorded as Emma Homan, later known as Emma Homan Thayer.","c":"Folk portraiture's virtue is directness. A young face confronts the viewer with a clarity that more sophisticated painters often lose, and the cat and flowers add pattern and charm. The limits are those typical of a self-taught or lightly trained hand: stiff modeling, flattened space, and details that matter more as decoration than as description. Bradley's painting is to be prized for its candor and period flavor rather than its technical finish."},"met:436298":{"d":"An unidentified woman, painted on ivory around 1820 by Joshua Wilson Faulkner, a British miniaturist. No size is given, but portrait miniatures of this date are normally only a few centimeters high, made to be cased, framed or worn. The likeness is a bust-length study, with the face built up in tiny strokes over the translucent ivory and the dress and ground treated more simply.","i":"About 1820, at the end of the Regency, the portrait miniature was at the height of its popularity in Britain, a personal, portable likeness that could be exchanged between lovers, given to relatives or kept in a drawer. The sitter's name has been lost, as often happens, leaving a woman known only by her sex and her date, though the costume and hair of such miniatures are what specialists rely on to date them.","c":"A miniature like this is an object for close, quiet looking. Faulkner, a name now known mainly to specialists, belonged to the busy ranks of professional miniaturists who supplied the Regency's appetite for small, flattering likenesses. The best of such work combines refined stippling with a lively gaze; an anonymous sitter and a modest reputation make this one a document of the trade more than a landmark of it."},"met:435590":{"d":"A man and a woman with a boy and a girl, together with a dog and a bird, are gathered in an outdoor setting, according to the Met's keywords, which also record the landscape and the family. The oil on canvas is a large horizontal picture, 124.8 by 152.7 centimeters, of the kind commissioned to record a family at its ease in the country. The subject is the family itself, with the landscape as its stage.","i":"Family groups in a landscape were a favorite Flemish and Dutch format in the middle of the seventeenth century, showing a household's prosperity, harmony and ownership of land in a single view. The dog is a traditional sign of fidelity, and the children represent the family's future. Jacques d'Arthois, better known for wooded views of the forest near Brussels, was a landscapist by training, which may explain the weight given to the setting.","c":"A landscapist painting a family is working at the edge of his specialty, and the result is often a convincing setting and somewhat stiff figures. The question is whether the group and its place are in balance: whether the trees frame the family or simply stand behind it. The picture's value is as a document of mid-seventeenth-century Flemish family life more than as a bold composition."},"met:436790":{"d":"A portrait of a man with a watch, in oil on canvas, about 84 by 71 centimeters, dated 1657, by Cornelis Jonson van Ceulen the Younger, son of a London-born portraitist who worked in the Dutch Republic. His name is unknown, and at this size the picture was meant for a private room rather than a public hall. The watch is the one attribute named in the title, and it presumably gives the picture its point.","i":"A watch in a portrait of the 1650s was a costly, modern object, and showing it signaled wealth, punctuality and the sitter's place among men who valued precision. It can also carry a quiet reminder of time passing, a theme familiar from vanitas still lifes, though that reading is not certain here. The sitter is unnamed, so his trade and rank are unknown; the picture is best read as a statement of prosperous, up-to-date taste.","c":"The younger Jonson worked in his father's fine, rather cool manner and was valued for elegance and polish. A portrait with a watch lets him show his skill in small, hard details against softer flesh and cloth. The subject marks it as a lively variant on the standard Dutch portrait of the 1650s."},"met:437236":{"d":"A man's head appears on a tiny panel of oil on wood, less than 10 centimeters in either direction, painted around 1465 to 1470. The title calls him a donor, the man who paid for a devotional painting and was portrayed in it, usually kneeling at the edge of a sacred scene. The panel is likely a fragment cut from such a larger picture, preserving only the face, which the Met attributes to Albert van Ouwater.","i":"Donors were included in altarpieces and devotional panels so that their prayers would be remembered, and Netherlandish painters made their faces strikingly lifelike, often more individual than the saints beside them. Albert van Ouwater worked in Haarlem and is regarded as a founder of the Haarlem school; only one painting, the Raising of Lazarus in Berlin, is generally accepted as his, so other attributions rest on style.","c":"A fragment can be judged only on its face, and if the attribution is right it is a rare glimpse of Haarlem painting before Geertgen tot Sint Jans. The lost context deprives it of composition and story, but a donor portrait is above all about likeness, and that survives. It will repay slow, close looking, which is how such small heads were meant to be seen."},"met:441969":{"d":"Talleyrand appears in an interior furnished for rank and for work, on a canvas more than two metres tall. A writing table with an inkwell and quill, an upholstered armchair, a divan and a parquet floor are all recorded in the picture, and a sash crosses his chest as a mark of honour. These furnishings are more than decoration: they tell the viewer that the sitter is an office-holder, a man who governs with pen and paper, and the large scale turns the image into a statement of public position.","i":"Talleyrand, a former bishop who had become a diplomat, served as foreign minister under the Directory, the Consulate and the Empire, and Napoleon made him Prince of Benevento in 1806. By 1808 he had left the ministry but remained among the highest dignitaries of the Empire, and his talent for surviving changes of regime was already proverbial. A portrait on this scale records office and rank, not private character; the quill and inkwell mark him as the man who wrote the treaties.","c":"Gérard was the most fashionable portraitist of the Empire, and this canvas shows why: textiles and furnishings are rendered with cool exactness, and the composition lets the sitter's rank speak. The weakness is the same smoothness. Talleyrand's reputation for impenetrable, ironic composure is matched here by a surface that gives little away, and the portrait is more a ceremony than a study. Beside David's more searching portraits it feels courtly and slightly guarded."},"met:438546":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, more than two metres high, shows Catherine Worlée, known as Madame Grand before her marriage and from 1802 the wife of Charles Maurice de Talleyrand. The size is that of a formal portrait of high rank, and it gives her a public presence to match her husband's. Gérard painted the picture in oil around 1804, when the Consular court was becoming an imperial one. Her dress and setting belong to that world of new titles and newly confident fashion.","i":"Catherine Worlée was born in India and had lived in Parisian society as Madame Grand before she married Talleyrand in 1802, at Napoleon's urging, once Talleyrand had been released from his clerical vows. Contemporaries gossiped about her past, which makes a state portrait on this scale a statement of acceptance as much as a likeness: it presents her as the wife of one of the Empire's first officials. The picture is usually read as a record of rank and fashionable taste.","c":"Gérard knew how to flatter the new elite, and a portrait on this scale gives the sitter every advantage of elegance and finish. The risk of such commissions is a polished surface and a practised poise, with the sitter lost in her own splendour. This is a portrait of position more than of person, and it is useful above all as evidence of how the Consular and early Imperial court wished to be seen."},"met:435647":{"d":"A broad river view, about 64 by 100 centimetres, painted in oil in 1744. The Met's keywords record a river, a town, a boat, a man and a woman. Vaprio d'Adda is a village in Lombardy on the Adda, the river that flows from Lake Como toward the Po, and the picture shows it in the long horizontal format that Bellotto learned from his uncle Canaletto, with small figures and boats giving scale to the water and the sky.","i":"In 1744 the young Bellotto was working away from Venice and from Canaletto's workshop, painting views in Lombardy, among them Vaprio. These early landscapes show how he began to work on his own account, in a lighter, more open manner than his later Saxon views. The Adda was a working river with boats and mills, and the view records an ordinary place with a quiet, everyday air.","c":"A young painter's response to a real place: the long river, the open sky and the small figures are arranged with an easy, relaxed sense of space, and the light is clear and silvery. Compared with the severe monumentality of the Dresden views, it has more atmosphere and less rigidity, though the composition is modest and the figures are formulaic. It is a fresh, unpretentious early work that shows the skill that would later become precise."},"cma:137224":{"d":"A large view of Venice's main square, about 136 by 233 centimetres. At the left stands the church of Saint Mark with the Doge's Palace beyond, leading to the lagoon; the campanile, or bell tower, divides the canvas vertically; and the Procuratie Nuove, a government building, extends to the right. At the far right, opposite Saint Mark's, stands the church of San Geminiano, which no longer exists. The picture shows the piazza as it was before the west end was rebuilt in the nineteenth century.","i":"In the eighteenth century Venice was a destination for British aristocrats on the Grand Tour, who bought vedute, topographical views, as souvenirs. Canaletto's workshop supplied many of them, and his nephew and pupil Bellotto, who painted this view when very young, followed the same path. San Geminiano was removed in the early nineteenth century on Napoleon's orders, when the two wings of the Procuratie were joined across the west end of the piazza, so the view also records a lost corner of the city.","c":"A remarkably confident painting from a young artist: the crisp light, the sharp shadows beneath the arcades and the cool tonality already differ from Canaletto's. The view is broad and the topography reliable. But the composition is somewhat stiff and the figures conventional, and Bellotto's early manner can be harder and less atmospheric than his uncle's. Its value lies in its clarity and its record of a vanished corner of the piazza."},"nga:1144":{"d":"A broad Venetian square opens beside a canal in bright sunlight. On the left the canal runs back between one- to four-storey houses with arched doors onto the water, their reflections stirring in olive-green ripples, while a stepped bridge crosses in the middle distance and two gondoliers steer a long boat to a landing. In the foreground men and women in coloured dress stand and walk in pairs. At the right a bronze rider on a tall plinth stands before a red-brick church, and across the square a facade with statues on its roofline and a winged lion above the door lies in deep shadow.","i":"The square is the Campo di Santi Giovanni e Paolo, known to Venetians as San Zanipolo. The equestrian bronze is Verrocchio's monument to the condottiere Bartolomeo Colleoni, the brick church is the Dominicans' great Gothic basilica, and the building with the winged lion of Saint Mark over its door is the Scuola Grande di San Marco. Such views were bought by foreign visitors on the Grand Tour as souvenirs, and the young Bellotto, nephew and pupil of Canaletto, painted them before he left Venice for Dresden in 1747.","c":"A remarkably assured early work. Bellotto already shows his hallmark: a hard, bright light with strong cast shadows, a cooler palette than Canaletto's, and a precise grip of architectural form. The shadowed church facade and the reflections in the green water are especially well managed. The figures are small and conventional, and the emptiness of the square gives a slightly stagey calm, but the clarity and grandeur are impressive for a painter in his early twenties."},"met:435646":{"d":"A wide view of a Saxon town, about 46 by 78 centimetres, shows the Obertor, the upper gate of Pirna, seen from the south. The Met's keywords record buildings and a village; Pirna lies on the Elbe above Dresden, and Bellotto's views of it show gateways, houses and roofs in sharp detail, with small figures giving life and scale. The picture is dated to the mid-1750s.","i":"From 1747 Bellotto was court painter to Augustus III, Elector of Saxony and King of Poland, and in the 1750s he painted a series of views of Pirna and the nearby fortress of Königstein. The town became famous in 1756 when the Saxon army was besieged in a camp near it at the opening of the Seven Years' War. This picture, painted before that event, shows it at peace. Views like this probably served as records of the electorate for the court.","c":"Here Bellotto's mature manner is at work: precise drawing, clear light and crisp contours turn gates and roofs into solid geometric forms, and each building seems to occupy its own space. The objectivity can feel cool, and the figures are small and formulaic. But a provincial gate is hardly glamorous, and the achievement lies in turning it into a convincing, dignified image. It is the kind of quiet topographical picture on which his reputation now stands."},"nga:80924":{"d":"A large canvas, about 1.3 by 2.4 metres, shows the fortress of Königstein, a stronghold perched on a rock above the Elbe in Saxony, in the broad, detailed manner of a topographical view. The great width suits a panorama, and the scale suggests that it was made for a prince's gallery rather than a private room. Bellotto, who was court painter in Dresden, painted it between 1756 and 1758, and such views usually combine precise architecture with a sweep of sky and small figures that give the scene life.","i":"From 1747 Bellotto worked in Dresden for Augustus III, Elector of Saxony and King of Poland, painting the city and the Saxon countryside. The years 1756 to 1758 were the opening years of the Seven Years' War, when Prussian armies occupied Saxony; Königstein, among the strongest fortresses of the electorate, was a symbol of Saxon strength. Pictures like this probably served as records of places that were suddenly of national importance.","c":"Bellotto's Saxon views are often counted among his finest, and this one shows why: the architecture is drawn with a surveyor's exactness and the light is clear and cool. The weakness is a certain chill: his precision can turn landscape into diagram, and the tiny figures are formulaic. Next to Canaletto's warmer, more atmospheric views he is harder and more severe, yet in a topographical picture on this scale that severity is a virtue."},"nga:95657":{"d":"Viewed from above, a stately complex of large white buildings with ivy-covered walls and dark peaked roofs. One building stands directly ahead on the left, and a nearer one on the right sits on a small rise; wide grey paths and small flights of steps join them. Small figures in bright dresses and suits, some with white wigs or pointed hats, walk or stand on the paths and grass, casting soft shadows in warm light. Tall trees give shade, and a soft blue sky with pale clouds fills the top.","i":"The Magdalenenburg is one of the buildings inside the fortress of Königstein, in Saxony, where Bellotto worked as court painter. A courtyard scene shows the fortress as a place of daily life, with officers, ladies and townsfolk strolling, rather than as a stronghold under threat. The painting dates from the opening of the Seven Years' War, when the fortress was a focus of Saxon pride, and it probably belonged to a group of views made for the court.","c":"A smaller and more intimate picture than the great view, and in some ways more engaging. Warm light, soft shadows and small knots of figures give the courtyard a mellow, lived-in feel, and the raised angle makes the architecture easy to read. Bellotto's figures are often stiff, and the composition is somewhat scattered, with no strong centre. But the clarity of the buildings and the pleasure of a sunny afternoon make it a charming record of a place."},"nga:46162":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 68 by 120 centimetres, shows Nymphenburg Palace, the summer residence of the rulers of Bavaria on the edge of Munich, in Bellotto's topographical manner. It matches his View of Munich in size and date, c. 1761, and like his other views it combines accurate architecture with small figures and a broad sky. The palace itself is a long, low baroque group with a central block and symmetrical wings and outbuildings.","i":"Nymphenburg was begun in the 1660s as a summer retreat for the Bavarian electoral family and was enlarged in the eighteenth century into one of the grandest palaces of southern Germany. Bellotto, trained in Venice and later court painter in Dresden, was an obvious choice to record a ruler's possessions: his accuracy flattered the building and gave it permanence. Pictures of this kind probably hung in a princely gallery.","c":"Bellotto's Munich pictures keep his precise, cool geometry, and the long palace front gives him a fine subject. The weakness is the chill: the figures are small and conventional, and the picture sets down the palace's rhythms faithfully but leaves them without much warmth or mood. Next to his Dresden and Pirna views it feels slightly formal, a competent set-piece, though its clarity and sense of ordered space are impressive."},"nga:46163":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 69 by 120 centimetres, shows the Bavarian capital as Bellotto saw it around 1761. The title promises a panorama of the city, and in the 1760s Munich was still a compact walled town whose skyline was marked by church towers, notably the twin domed towers of the Frauenkirche. Bellotto, who painted topographical views for courts across Europe, normally fixed a broad horizon, a high sky and a scattering of figures in the foreground, so that the view reads both as map and as scene.","i":"Views of capital cities were popular souvenirs and official records: rulers wanted images of their cities, and visitors wanted to take home likenesses of what they had seen. Bellotto, who spent his career painting the courts and cities of Central Europe, made his Munich views around 1761, together with his picture of the Nymphenburg palace. Their purpose was to record the electoral residence city, probably for the court and for an international public interested in topography.","c":"A competent and clear panorama in which the city is set out like a map and then enlivened by figures and sky. Bellotto's gift for crisp detail and cool light is characteristic, and the long horizontal format suits it. The risk is that a panorama of a whole city becomes an inventory of buildings with no single focus, and his small figures are generic. It rewards close looking for its detail more than for emotional effect."},"nga:46161":{"d":"An old woman sits in a tall wooden chair with a baby on her lap, flanked by a bearded man at left and a young girl at right, all with flat halos inscribed S IOHANE, S ANNA and MARIA. Saint Anne's face is deeply creased and she smiles down at the child, in a scarlet cloak. The nude baby grips a small red apple held out by Mary, whose blond hair falls loose to her waist. Saint John kneels, pointing at the child and at a haloed lamb, before a black wall and a landscape with a river and a town on cliffs.","i":"Saint Anne with her daughter Mary and grandson Jesus was one of the most popular devotions in Germany around 1500, an image of three generations that honored family and motherhood. The apple is the sign of the Fall of Adam and Eve, which Christ, the new Adam, will redeem, and the lamb with John points to Christ's sacrifice, since John called him the Lamb of God. The figures from different times are gathered in a devotional, not a historical, scene.","c":"Baldung's temperament shows in the oddities: Anne's wrinkled, jowled face is rendered with unflinching realism, and the lively baby and plate-like halos give a slightly eccentric charm. The color is clear and the composition firmly built around the throne, but the mixture of realism and stiffness belongs to a late-Gothic habit that Baldung would soon leave behind. It is a vigorous early work by a painter better known for witches and nudes."},"met:435600":{"d":"A young man in a rose-red robe sits on a rock beneath a large tree, a gold halo behind his curly blond head. He rests an open book on his knee and holds a quill, looking up and to the left in wonder. There, in a ring of puffy white clouds against a gilded sky, the Virgin stands on a crescent moon in a blue mantle, holding the Child; her long hair streams behind her. At the lower left a black eagle with a small halo perches on a closed blue book studded with gold. Behind, a calm sea with small ships, a bay and blue mountains stretch away. Oil, gold and white metal on spruce, about 90 by 77 centimetres.","i":"The subject is Saint John the Evangelist, exiled on the island of Patmos, writing the Book of Revelation while a vision appears to him of “a woman clothed with the sun, and with the moon under her feet.” The Met says the panel originally joined two others to form a triptych commissioned by a religious order in Strasbourg. Baldung had trained in Dürer's Nuremberg workshop before setting up his own in Strasbourg in 1509. The eagle is John's usual symbol. The Virgin on the crescent was a favourite German image, and viewers of the time could take her both as the woman of the Apocalypse and as a figure of the Immaculate Conception.","c":"Baldung gives the old tale a clean, enamel-like finish: the red robe is crisp and sculptural, the clouds are like rolled cotton, and the gilded sky adds a decorative glow to the vision. John is convincingly human, rapt and a little startled. Beside Dürer's stricter drawing, Baldung's forms are softer and less exact, and the ring of clouds is slightly toy-like. Yet the landscape beyond is delicately painted, and as a panel it is clearly organised and calmly beautiful."},"met:439344":{"d":"Two men stand before a waterfall at sunset in a small, nearly square oil on canvas, 38.1 by 35.6 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list men, a waterfall and a landscape. A picture of this size is a cabinet picture, made for a collector's room rather than a public hall, and its subject is the meeting of human figures with a powerful, falling mass of water in the last light of day.","i":"Waterfalls have a long Dutch ancestry in the work of Jacob van Ruisdael and Allart van Everdingen, whom Dahl admired, and they became a favorite subject of Romantic painters who sought the sublime in nature. Dahl, a Norwegian living in Dresden, where his friend Caspar David Friedrich also worked, painted figures who stand before nature as witnesses, small beside forces they cannot control.","c":"Dahl's small oils are often his best, because the directness of a sketch suits his talent for observation. The sunset and spray carry the mood, and the two figures give scale and a human stake. At about thirty-eight centimeters the picture has little room for grandeur, and the drama depends on color and light rather than size. It is a good example of the Romantic taste for sublime nature at a domestic scale."},"met:438159":{"d":"A large horizontal oil on canvas, 94.0 by 139.1 centimeters, shows Vesuvius in eruption. The museum's keywords note fire, a volcano, a mountain and a donkey, so the picture combines a blazing mountain with an animal detail in the foreground. The subject, the volcano above the Bay of Naples, was among the most famous sights in Europe, and painters rendered its eruptions as spectacles of fire and smoke.","i":"Dahl lived in Italy in 1820 and 1821 and painted Vesuvius in eruption repeatedly afterward, working in Dresden from his studies and memory. Eruptions were a touchstone of the sublime, a display of nature's power that was both terrifying and beautiful, and travelers climbed the cone to watch. The donkey, the animal that carried visitors on the slopes, may point to the spectators who made the volcano a tourist spectacle.","c":"Dahl had seen the volcano at first hand, which gives his Vesuvius pictures a documentary edge beside the theatrical versions of earlier painters such as Joseph Wright of Derby. The strong contrast of fire and sky is the source of the drama, while the foreground details are small and secondary. It is among the more convincing Romantic volcanoes, though any such spectacle risks feeling staged."},"nga:110104":{"d":"A man and woman stand with their backs to the viewer at the water's edge, looking toward a moonlit, rocky inlet with misty midnight-blue mountains on the horizon. The sky fills the upper two-thirds: pearl-white clouds against lilac and pink haze deepen to ultramarine, and gray cloud partly veils the bright moon. Moonlight gleams on water winding among tree-dotted islands. The woman, in a rust-brown dress and bonnet, links her arm through the man's, who wears a dark coat and round cap. Nets hang from poles in the water, and a three-masted ship lies at anchor. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 97 centimeters, signed January 1827.","i":"Vækerø is a promontory on the Oslofjord near what was then Christiania, now Oslo. Dahl, a Norwegian who worked in Dresden and was a friend of Caspar David Friedrich, brought to Norwegian views the German Romantic idea of back-turned figures contemplating a nocturnal landscape. The couple, tiny against the moonlit fjord, express shared reverie and the sublimity of nature. The picture was probably composed in Dresden from sketches made in Norway.","c":"An accomplished nocturne: the moon, the glowing haze and the reflections are handled with a quiet breadth that makes the fjord seem to breathe. The back-turned couple is a conventional Romantic device, but here it is delicately done and keeps the landscape from empty spectacle. Dahl lacks Friedrich's metaphysical intensity; he is more observant and more at ease with weather and geography. A fine example of Scandinavian Romanticism."},"met:438954":{"d":"A woman and a child on a beach at night, with a boat and clouds, are shown in a very small oil on canvas, only 15.9 by 20.6 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a woman, a child, a beach, night, clouds and a boat. At this size the picture is more intimate than a gallery piece, a work to be held close, and its subject is a quiet human presence beside the sea after dark.","i":"Small night scenes by the sea suited the Romantic taste for mood, and the figure of a mother and child can be read as a counterpart to the open sea: shelter and domestic life against the vast, unpredictable water. Dahl, who painted both great Norwegian mountain panoramas and intimate oil studies from nature, treated such small subjects as serious pictures in their own right.","c":"In so small a format a night scene depends on a few well-judged contrasts, and Dahl, a great observer of weather and sky, had the economy for it. The subject tempts sentiment, and the best way to look is to see whether the sea and clouds do the emotional work or the figures do. Either way it stands among the more intimate of his pictures."},"met:441967":{"d":"A small horizontal landscape, 24.1 by 36.5 centimeters, shows a view over Hallingdal, a long valley in the mountains of southern Norway. The Met's keywords record only a landscape. At this size it has the character of a study made in front of the motif or soon after, a direct response to the shape of the land, the sky and the distance rather than a composed exhibition piece.","i":"Dahl, who spent most of his career in Dresden, returned to Norway on summer journeys to draw and paint its mountains, valleys and fjords, and he is regarded as the founder of Norwegian landscape painting. In the decades after Norway's constitution of 1814, such views of the national landscape took on a patriotic meaning, and Dahl's pictures gave the new nation images of its own land.","c":"The best of Dahl is in his small, direct landscapes, where he forgets the stagecraft of his larger exhibition pictures and looks. A distant view over a valley asks for breadth, light and a sense of air, and these are the qualities to seek. As a work of his later years it shows a mature painter at ease with his motif, though a small canvas cannot carry the weight of his great set pieces."},"met:439343":{"d":"A large horizontal oil, 95.9 by 154.0 centimeters, shows Copenhagen's harbor at night under the moon. The Met's keywords record a harbor, boats, the moon and night. Moonlit harbors were a favorite subject of Romantic painters, who delighted in silvery light on water, the silhouettes of masts and rigging, and the contrast of the cool sky with any warm lights ashore. A canvas of this size is a major exhibition piece rather than a study.","i":"Dahl trained at the Copenhagen academy before moving to Dresden, so the harbor was the city of his youth, and a moonlit view of it carries a certain nostalgia. Danish painters of the Golden Age, Eckersberg above all, made the harbor and its ships a national subject, and the moonlit variant allowed Dahl to join that tradition with the Romantic taste for night.","c":"Moonlight is hard to paint: the light must glow without becoming a trick, and the dark must stay luminous. Dahl, a great painter of skies and reflections, is well equipped for it, and a large canvas gives him space for the long reflections of moon and ships. The danger is a certain stillness, a picturesque calm; the pleasure is the silvery tonal unity."},"met:659875":{"d":"A small upright oil on canvas, 61.9 by 43.2 centimeters, shows the execution of Saint John the Baptist. The Met's subject tags list the saint, a sword, a man, an angel and God the Father, so the picture combines the earthly act of the executioner with a heavenly presence above. At this modest size it may be a sketch for a larger work or a picture for private devotion rather than an altarpiece.","i":"John the Baptist, the prophet who baptized Christ, was beheaded on the orders of Herod Antipas after Herod's wife Herodias and her daughter Salome demanded his death, as told in the Gospels of Mark and Matthew. Painters usually show the moment of execution as a martyrdom, with the saint calm and the executioner's sword raised. Here God the Father and an angel represent the divine reward awaiting him.","c":"Ubaldo Gandolfi belonged to the Bolognese school, and his oil sketches are admired for their quick, fluent handling and strong light and shade. A small canvas like this gives him room to be spontaneous: the strokes can be broad, the lights concentrated and the drama condensed. The risk of such work is that sketchiness can look like incompletion, but in Gandolfi's case the brisk brush is part of the appeal."},"met:440726":{"d":"A small interior view shows a sitting room with a curtain, in the artist's own lodgings in Ritterstrasse, Berlin. Painted in oil on cardboard, only 32 by 27 centimeters, it is a private study of a room rather than a picture meant for sale. Menzel painted a number of such rooms in his own home in the 1840s and 1850s, treating the ordinary spaces of daily life as worthy subjects. It is dated 1851.","i":"Menzel's small studies of rooms, windows and curtains belonged to a largely private practice of looking: he made them for himself, and many were not shown in his lifetime. They show an eye for light falling in ordinary rooms and for objects as they happen to be arranged, long before such plain subjects were fashionable. Among German painters of mid-century they are unusually modern, and later critics saw in them anticipations of Impressionism.","c":"Honesty of observation is the draw: no anecdote, no pose, only a corner of a room seen with a painter's appetite. Menzel's interior studies are typically quick and fluid, and the effect of light is what he is after. It is a minor Menzel in size but a major one in outlook, more modern than his famous scenes of Frederick the Great, and part of why he is ranked among the century's most acute observers."},"nga:95659":{"d":"A nude man and woman recline together under an apple tree before a deep landscape, bodies entwined, gazing into each other's eyes. Adam, muscular, braces himself on one elbow on a mossy rock and holds a small green fruit, a sprig of leaves covering his loins; Eve leans against his chest and holds up an apple with a bite taken from it. A snake with a human face, blond and flushed, winds around the trunk above her head. At the lower left sits a brown and white cat, and at the right a goat looks back at the pair. Tiny animals cross toward the mountains.","i":"The Fall of Man is told in Genesis 3: Eve, tempted by the serpent, eats the forbidden fruit and gives it to Adam, bringing sin and death into the world. Painters often gave the serpent a human face, a tradition going back to the Middle Ages. The cat, which Dürer also included in his famous engraving of 1504, is usually read as a sign of cunning, and the goat as a sign of lust. The exotic animals in the distance evoke Eden as the first menagerie.","c":"Goltzius, a great engraver turned painter, gives the first couple the sculptural bodies of his prints, and the pair seems more like lovers than sinners. The tenderness is the surprise, as is the human face of the snake. The smooth, luminous skin and firm contours belong to a printmaker's idea of painting, and the picture feels calculated, a sharp, intellectual work painted the year before his death rather than a spontaneous one."},"met:436970":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 110 by 50 centimeters, shows a woman engaged in needlework, according to the record's list of motifs, with the title Penelope identifying her as the faithful wife of Odysseus. The museum gives no description of her pose, costume or setting, and the date is given as about 1868. The unusual proportions suit a single standing or seated figure.","i":"In Homer's Odyssey, Penelope keeps her suitors at bay for years by promising to choose one when she has finished weaving a shroud for her father-in-law Laertes, and each night she unpicks what she wove by day. She became the model of wifely fidelity and ingenuity. Salon painters of the Second Empire were fond of antique subjects treated with modern sensuousness, and a Penelope is an excuse for an elegant, absorbed female figure.","c":"Needlework gives a painter a graceful, absorbed pose and an intimate subject, and a narrow vertical format flatters a single figure. Marchal, a skilled Salon painter, worked in the polished academic manner of the Second Empire; a Penelope of this kind probably owes more to the taste for elegant classical women than to any narrative interest. Its charm is in the grace of the figure, its limit in conventionality."},"met:13045":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Egbert Benson, painted in oil on a canvas 75.6 by 60.6 centimeters, around 1794. The format is vertical and the size that of a bust or half-length likeness, the standard commission for a public man who wanted a record of his appearance for family and friends, and the painter was still in his teens.","i":"Egbert Benson (1746-1833) was a New York lawyer and public servant: the state's first attorney general, a member of the first United States Congress and later a judge. The young Vanderlyn, who studied with Gilbert Stuart, was supported by Aaron Burr and went to Paris in 1796 to study, at a time when American painters usually went to London; this early portrait comes just before that move.","c":"A teenager's portrait of a senior public figure is bound to be conventional, and the most interesting question is how far Vanderlyn goes beyond Stuart's example, or doesn't. Handling and likeness are the points to judge: a sober, direct treatment suits a lawyer-statesman. As early work it shows promise; the mature Vanderlyn, with Paris training and a taste for classical subjects and panoramas, was still to come."},"met:13053":{"d":"A self-portrait in oil on canvas, about 64 by 53 centimeters, in which John Vanderlyn shows himself as a young man. It is dated 1800, when he was about twenty-five and in the middle of his first stay in Paris, where he had gone to study under François-André Vincent. The size and format point to a bust- or half-length likeness made for a private room rather than a public commission; the pose and dress are not recorded.","i":"A young painter's self-portrait works as proof of competence: a sitter he knows perfectly, a face he can study at leisure, and a picture he can show to prospective patrons. Vanderlyn was among the first American painters to train in Paris rather than London, with the backing of Aaron Burr, and that schooling in firm drawing, smooth finish and a sober palette set him apart from many portraitists at home. The picture is usually read as that training put on display.","c":"Judged by the habits of Vanderlyn's early portraits, the strengths here are clear contour and a cool, orderly surface; the limit is reserve, since the French academic manner he absorbed prized finish over spontaneity. Beside the larger and more theatrical history paintings of his later career, a self-likeness of this size shows him at his most direct and least strained."},"met:13051":{"d":"A mother and her small son share a canvas of about 94 by 71 centimeters, a size for a formal domestic portrait. The title names the sitter as Mrs. Marinus Willett, presumably the wife of the New York Revolutionary War officer and later mayor of that name, with her son Marinus Jr. Vanderlyn painted it around 1802, during his brief return to New York between his first and second European stays, when portraits were an obvious way to establish himself at home.","i":"Pairing a mother with her child was a standard way to show a woman's place in a prosperous household: the lady of the house together with the heir. For a New York family of standing, a portrait in the new Neoclassical manner Vanderlyn brought back from Paris also signaled taste and a connection with Europe. In such pictures the child usually stands for the family's continuity as much as for tenderness.","c":"A double portrait tests a painter's grip on composition more than a single head does, since two figures must feel linked and not merely adjacent. Vanderlyn's firm drawing and clean modeling suit the task, though his portraits generally favor poise over warmth. Among American portraits of 1802 it would stand out for its Paris-trained finish, if less for emotional range."},"nga:1130":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, turned slightly to one side yet looking straight at the viewer, with a composed expression, a high forehead, strong eyebrows and prominent cheekbones. He is clean-shaven, and his dark curly hair is brushed back from his forehead. He wears a dark double-breasted coat with gold buttons and a high white cravat, set against a plain, dark, greenish ground. The canvas, about 66 by 57 centimeters, was painted by John Vanderlyn between 1815 and 1818.","i":"Dress and ground tell most of the story. The dark coat with gold buttons and the high white cravat belong to the sober but costly fashion of the years after Napoleon's fall, when a gentleman wanted to look businesslike and well informed. The empty greenish background removes any distracting setting and leaves the face to carry the sitter's character. Nothing in the picture states his profession; the identification rests on the title and the museum's records.","c":"This is Vanderlyn's portrait habit at its most economical: one figure, one plain ground, a face modeled firmly and without flattery. The drawing of the head is the interest, and the dark coat is allowed to sink into shadow. It lacks the invention of his more elaborate pictures, but the restraint is also its strength, an honest likeness that avoids ornament."},"met:13052":{"d":"A panorama: a continuous painted view made to hang around the inside of a circular building, with the viewer standing at its center. Vanderlyn's subject is the Palace of Versailles with its gardens and fountains, painted in 1818-19 for exhibition in New York. Panoramas ran to many meters in length at modest height, so a flat reproduction like the one in the gallery shows a long strip and loses the sense of being surrounded, which was the whole point of the form.","i":"A panorama was a paid public entertainment, a forerunner of the cinema: for a fee, spectators climbed to a central platform and found themselves carried to a distant place. Versailles, the great symbol of the French monarchy, restored to the throne only a few years before, was a natural draw for American audiences who knew it by reputation, not by sight. Vanderlyn, who had seen panoramas in Paris, had a rotunda built in New York's City Hall Park to show them.","c":"Flattened into a reproduction, a panorama can hardly be judged as a picture, since its effect depended on turning and on distance. On the record, though, it is a far more ambitious undertaking than any of his portraits: a sustained, carefully surveyed view that had to hold together over a vast surface. Its shortcoming is the genre's own, a spectacle with no figure to hold the eye. It matters more for its scale than its drama."},"nga:98944":{"d":"A woman is shown from mid-torso up, seated and facing forward, while a large oval mirror behind her catches the side of her face in profile, so that the picture offers two views of her at once. She has fair skin and dark curly hair, and wears a light dress with puffed short sleeves and a floral ornament at the bust. A patterned fabric is draped over her right arm, and her fingers rest in her lap. A reddish curtain at left and a dark greenish ground complete the plain setting. The canvas is about 76 by 61 centimeters, dated around 1827.","i":"Showing a sitter in both full face and profile through a mirror is an old device, descending from the Renaissance argument over whether painting or sculpture could show more: painting, the answer went, could present several sides of a figure at once. The patterned cloth, probably a shawl, and the careful dress mark a woman of means. Mirrors in older portraits sometimes carry a hint of vanity, but here the device is usually read as a display of skill.","c":"The mirror is the idea that lifts this above a routine bust. It lets Vanderlyn show off his drawing in two views and gives the flat dark ground a second plane of interest, while the pale dress reads cleanly against it. The risk with such a device is that cleverness outweighs character, and the sitter's inner life stays secondary. Even so, it is one of the more inventive of his late portraits."},"met:13049":{"d":"A portrait of John A. Sidell in oil on canvas, about 76 by 61 centimeters, a size that normally takes a sitter to about the waist. John Vanderlyn painted it around 1830, in the run of portrait commissions that supported him in his later years. Little is recorded of the sitter's pose or costume. In other portraits of these years Vanderlyn typically sets a single figure against a plain dark ground and models the face with smooth, cool flesh tones.","i":"Before the daguerreotype arrived in the late 1830s, a painted portrait was the only way for a family of means to keep a man's likeness, and a growing merchant and professional class in the United States was buying them. A sitter of 1830 wanted to look sober, capable and respectable, and Vanderlyn's French-trained manner supplied exactly that. Nothing in the catalogue record says who Sidell was or why he sat, so his rank is left unstated.","c":"With a plain ground and a single figure, such a picture lives or dies on the head, and Vanderlyn's strength was always draftsmanship: a firm structure and an orderly surface. The corresponding weakness is a certain chill, a likeness delivered without much sympathy. It is a competent late portrait rather than a revealing one, and it is best set beside the sharper pictures of his Paris years."},"nga:34062":{"d":"A man is seen from the waist up, seated and turned slightly so that his right shoulder comes toward the viewer. He has a square jaw, prominent cheekbones, sideburns and dark curly hair, and wears a black coat over a white shirt with a white cravat. His left hand rests on something outside the picture, possibly a book. The background is dark, with a faint gradient, so that face, cravat and shirt are the lit parts of an otherwise somber canvas of about 76 by 63 centimeters, painted in 1829-30.","i":"Black coat and white linen were the uniform of the professional or merchant of about 1830, and a book, if that is what the hand rests on, would signal literacy and seriousness more than any actual pastime. Portraits like this were commissioned to record a man's standing for his family and community, and they seldom tell a story. In his other late portraits Vanderlyn often sets sitters out in just this way: seated, lightly turned, the setting reduced to a dark ground.","c":"The pared-down formula is both strength and limit. Vanderlyn secures a strong, plainly lit head, and the black coat is handled as a simple dark mass that sets off the cravat. What the picture does not offer is a spark of invention: pose, ground and palette are the standard of the day. It is a sound, serious likeness rather than an exciting painting."},"met:13047":{"d":"A portrait of Francis Lucas Waddell, oil on canvas, about 64 by 53 centimeters, painted by John Vanderlyn around 1837. Pose and dress are not recorded. The smaller canvas suggests a bust or head-and-shoulders likeness, more compact than the waist-length pictures he painted a few years earlier. It dates from the period when Vanderlyn, in his sixties, was still taking portrait commissions alongside his larger ambitions.","i":"A modest-sized portrait of this kind was a private possession, kept at home or passed to family, and its message was the sitter's respectability, not any display of power. For a sitter of the 1830s the neat, plain presentation Vanderlyn favored was itself a statement: sobriety, steadiness and an ordered life. The catalogue record does not say who Waddell was, so nothing about his profession or standing should be read into it.","c":"At this size there is little room for anything but the head, so the picture depends on drawing and surface rather than on pose or accessories. Those are Vanderlyn's reliable qualities, and they hold up. What is missing is any sense of a particular occasion or temperament; the result is an exact, slightly formal likeness, useful as a record and respectable as craft."},"met:13043":{"d":"A small history painting, about 57 by 72 centimeters in oil on canvas, that retells an ancient story in paint: the Calumny of Apelles, an allegory the Greek writer Lucian described from a lost picture by the painter Apelles. The story calls for a gullible judge, a victim dragged forward and a procession of allegorical women, and the figures include men, women and female nudes. Vanderlyn dated it 1849, three years before his death, when he was in his seventies.","i":"In Lucian's account, Apelles was falsely accused of treason by a rival and, once cleared, painted a picture of slander at work: a king with large ears flanked by Ignorance and Suspicion, Calumny dragging an innocent youth by the hair, Envy and Deceit in attendance, and Repentance and Truth following behind. Renaissance artists revived the subject as a warning about courts and a defense of the artist's honor. A painter late in life may well have found a personal meaning in it.","c":"The subject is a learned one, and it gives a painter a chance for a complex figure group; it is also a notoriously hard thing to stage clearly. Vanderlyn's cool, academic drawing of the nude is the natural tool, though in the later 1840s it sits behind the times. Set beside Botticelli's famous treatment, a canvas of this size and date reads as an earnest, ambitious late effort more than a persuasive one."},"nga:195109":{"d":"A small oil on wood panel, about 24 by 32 centimeters, painted in 1876 by the Spanish artist Romà Ribera Cirera. The title and the museum's single keyword, singing, tell that a performer is the subject, and the setting is a café. The scale and the wooden support are those of the small, finely finished genre picture made in the 1870s for collectors and dealers, to be hung close to the eye. Beyond that, the catalogue does not record the figures or the room.","i":"In the 1870s a café singer might be a performer in a Parisian café-concert, where songs, applause and drink went together, or a flamenco singer in one of Spain's cafés cantantes, which were then at the height of fashion. Which of these is meant is not recorded. Small paintings of popular entertainment appealed to buyers who wanted the color of street and nightlife without its noise, and who could hang a panel this size almost anywhere in a home.","c":"A small panel favors quickness and sparkle, and pictures of this kind succeed or fail on how convincingly a single gesture or lit face is caught. As a type, the café singer is an anecdote, charming rather than weighty, and a subject that rewards a painter with a sharp eye for the stage and the audience. This is best taken as a pleasing, minor but accomplished example of the small genre piece that the 1870s market prized."},"met:436832":{"d":"Water and sky dominate this small landscape, painted around 1648-49: a wide river with boats on it, in an oil on canvas of about 41 by 58 centimeters. Philips Koninck was about thirty at the time and working in Amsterdam. The open sweep of the title promises a low horizon, a good deal of sky and a view that is more about space than about incident.","i":"Rivers were the highways of the Dutch Republic, carrying trade, passengers and news, and a river view was both a familiar local sight and a symbol of national prosperity. The boats here are probably as much a part of the scene's character as any individual building on its banks. Landscapes of this kind were bought by merchants and townspeople for their houses, and were not tied to a single named place; they offered a believable, composed Holland.","c":"Koninck is remembered for the panoramic views that he made later, in which a huge sky is held in balance with a distant, patterned land. A small early river picture like this one shows the appetite for space already at work, though without the later command of proportion. It is a modest, attractive canvas, and its interest is largely as an early step toward the grander pictures."},"met:436830":{"d":"A road with small figures on it leads through open country in a very wide view, with a country estate among the incidents and a sky of clouds above. The canvas measures about 143 by 173 centimeters, large for a Dutch landscape of the mid-seventeenth century, closer to a wall-filling decoration than to a cabinet picture. The Met dates it to about 1649, when Philips Koninck was around thirty and had begun to work on the panoramic scale that became his signature.","i":"Country estates were the pride of newly rich Dutch merchants and officials, and a landscape with one set among fields and a road spoke of ownership, order and a countryside made productive. The date, just after the Peace of Münster ended the long war with Spain in 1648, may have made a picture of calm prosperity especially welcome. The tiny figures are there to show the scale of the land and to give the eye something to follow.","c":"Koninck's gift for building a landscape from a raised, imagined viewpoint is already evident, and the scale is bold for so early a date. What is less settled is the balance between foreground, road and distance, which he handled with much greater assurance in his later panoramas. It is an ambitious canvas that points ahead more than it arrives, and it is best read as a stage in a fast-developing style."},"met:436831":{"d":"A wooded landscape stretches away in a broad, elevated view, with boats on the water and a large sky of clouds above, in an oil on canvas of about 83 by 113 centimeters. The Met dates it to the 1670s, the later part of Philips Koninck's career, when he was working in the panoramic manner that made his name. The title promises extent, and clouds and distance, rather than any single incident, are what the picture is about.","i":"Koninck's panoramas do not show real places so much as an ideal Holland, assembled from fields, rivers, woods and skies and viewed as if from a hill. For a Dutch buyer this combined pride in a well-ordered land with the pleasure of a wide, quiet prospect. The clouds were not mere decoration: Dutch painters treated the sky as a main subject, and Koninck often gave it about half the canvas or more.","c":"By the 1670s Koninck had a formula, and it works: a high viewpoint, a long diagonal sweep of land, and a sky with its own weather. The result is calm, spacious and carefully balanced rather than dramatic. Compared with Ruisdael's moodier forests, Koninck is the cooler and more orderly of the two, and he is at his strongest when the sky is allowed to dominate."},"met:14472":{"d":"A family group in a New York dining room, painted in 1866: a woman and several children, siblings, take part in what the title calls a contest for the bouquet. Playing is the theme, and a bonnet, a straw hat, a feather, a bow and a shoe suggest the clutter of children's dress. The oil on canvas measures about 63 by 75 centimeters, a conversation piece that combines portraiture of the Gordon family with the lively incident of a genre scene.","i":"Pictures of family life in a well-furnished room were a favorite product of the mid-Victorian world, and they served two purposes at once: they recorded a particular household, and they presented the ideal of the home as a place of order, affection and comfortable play. Seymour Guy, who was born in England and settled in New York, specialized in such sentimental domestic scenes. The title identifies the family as that of Robert Gordon; here the children's game is the whole story.","c":"Guy's skill lies in the patient description of surfaces and furnishings and in arranging a crowd of children so that the eye moves around it. The weakness is the one shared by the whole sentimental genre: the incident is charming but rather rehearsed, and the figures read as arranged more than caught. As a document of an American upper-class interior in 1866, it is more valuable than as pure painting."},"met:10975":{"d":"A small portrait in oil on canvas, about 31 by 25 centimeters, of the painter Charles Loring Elliott. Seymour Joseph Guy dated it 1868, the year of Elliott's death. Elliott was among the leading portraitists of mid-century New York, so this is one painter's likeness of another. The small size suggests a personal portrait or tribute rather than a commission, though the catalogue does not say so.","i":"A portrait of a colleague is a different thing from a commission: it records admiration or friendship, and it places the sitter within the artist's own circle. Elliott was known for blunt, vigorous likenesses of the businessmen and statesmen of his day, and a portrait of him by Guy, an English-born painter who specialized in sentimental domestic scenes, shows how closely the two worlds of New York art touched. Whether it was painted from life or after his death is not recorded.","c":"Guy was far more at home painting children and interiors than heads of notable men, and a portrait like this is a small proof of range. The scale demands economy, and economy suits him; the picture can be judged as a sincere tribute rather than a major statement. It is of interest first for the sitter, and second as a record of the web of relationships among New York painters."},"met:10976":{"d":"A girl drinks at a fountain in a small upright oil on canvas, about 31 by 22 centimeters. The title identifies her as a crossing sweeper, one of the poor children who swept the street crossings of Victorian cities to earn small coins from passers-by. Its date is given only as the 1860s. It is a genre picture with a figure at its center, not a commissioned portrait.","i":"The crossing sweeper was a stock figure of Victorian city life and fiction; Dickens's Jo in Bleak House is the best-known example. Pictures of poor children were meant to stir pity and encourage charity in comfortable viewers, as well as to present a recognizable type. Showing the girl at a pause, taking a drink from a public fountain, makes her humanity plain without any melodrama. Guy, who was born in England, would have known the type well.","c":"The small, upright format concentrates attention on a single figure, and the fountain gives her a convincing place in the street. Guy tends to idealize his children, and the standard difficulty with sentimental pictures of poverty is that they make hardship picturesque. The picture's virtue is its restraint and its sympathy; its limit is that it asks for a gentle response, not a hard look."},"met:20129":{"d":"A boy and a girl are in a bedroom, reading a book. The oil on canvas measures about 86 by 71 centimeters and dates from around 1870. It is a domestic genre picture, not a portrait, in the sentimental manner that made Seymour Joseph Guy popular in New York: children, an interior and a gentle incident. The title points to a nursery story, and the picture presents the act of reading it as the subject.","i":"Children's reading became a favorite subject in the 1860s and 1870s, when illustrated books for the young were widely published and a middle-class parent could expect to provide them. Showing a boy and a girl with a story spoke of a safe, literate, well-run home. The bedroom setting may suggest bedtime reading, a ritual of Victorian family life. The tale the title names is the kind of story told to children, and the picture's interest lies in the readers, not in the tale.","c":"Guy's interiors have a patient, tactile finish, and a subject like this lets him combine a pair of figures with the comfortable props of a nursery. The sentiment is sweet and the arrangement careful; the weakness is that the mood is predetermined, and nothing in the scene risks surprising the viewer. As a period picture of childhood it is accomplished, and it ranks among his typical, rather than his most original, work."},"met:437141":{"d":"A portrait of a man, painted in oil on copper and dated 1621, by an anonymous Flemish painter known only by the monogram IS. The record gives no dimensions, but copper panels of this kind were normally small and smoothly polished, which suited fine detail and a cabinet-size picture. Beyond that, the sitter's name, dress and pose are not recorded, so none is described here.","i":"Oil on copper was a favorite support in the Spanish Netherlands in the early seventeenth century, prized for its smooth, enamel-like surface, which allowed very fine rendering of a face. A small portrait of this sort was a personal object, commissioned by or for a family, and meant to be kept at hand. Since the sitter is unnamed, his rank cannot be read from the picture; any identification would need the original costume, inscription or coat of arms, which the catalogue does not describe.","c":"An artist who survives only as a monogram is, by definition, one whose reputation has not outlived the work, and the picture is best judged as a representative of its type rather than a notable individual hand. Copper encourages precision, and a smooth, exact finish is what such a panel is for. Without the sitter's name or a recognizable hand to compare, it remains a competent anonymous portrait."},"nga:130890":{"d":"A canal runs between white and tan buildings with reddish-brown roofs, a church tower rising at the left and, far down the water, an arched bridge. The buildings on the right are in shadow, and two long brown boats lie at the left edge of the canal. The water is green-blue, and the sky is mostly pale gray with darker blue in the upper right. It is a very small picture, about 15 by 24 centimeters, in oil on paper laid on canvas, painted around 1830 by Jules-Romain Joyant.","i":"San Trovaso is a church in Venice's Dorsoduro district, near a long-established boatyard where gondolas are built, so the long boats at the canal's edge may well be of that kind. French artists of the 1820s and 1830s were drawn to Venice, partly through the vogue for sketchy, light-filled views that followed Bonington. The support of oil on paper is typical of a study made on the spot or of a souvenir kept by the artist.","c":"The strength of a small oil like this is its freshness: a few broad touches of color for the roofs, the water and the sky tell the place without finicking detail. It is more spontaneous than the formal vedute of the previous century, but it is also slighter, a note rather than a statement. Joyant is a minor name, and this is a charming, unpretentious sketch of Venice rather than a major image of it."},"nga:130891":{"d":"A canal is seen from a low vantage point, with tall white buildings and red roofs on either side and a white bridge stretching across the distance. Behind the bridge, on the right, rises a large ornate building with domes, arches and statues or decorations along a tiered roofline. Long boats float on the water, and small dots of color by the bridge suggest people gathered outside. The water reflects patches of red, brown and tan; the sky is a swirl of blue and pale gray, with loose, expressive brushwork. It is about 15 by 24 centimeters, oil on paper on canvas, c. 1830.","i":"The ornate building is presumably the Scuola Grande di San Marco, a lay confraternity's headquarters whose Renaissance façade, completed in the late fifteenth century, is among the great sights of eastern Venice. The scuole were charitable and devotional brotherhoods that spent lavishly on their buildings. A view such as this, matching in size and support the same artist's view of San Trovaso, was probably made as one of a group of Venetian souvenirs or studies.","c":"The loose brushwork in the sky and water is the picture's best quality, and the low viewpoint along the canal gives a convincing sense of depth in a tiny space. The architecture is suggested rather than described, which suits a sketch and avoids stiffness. It is not a profound picture, but it shows how readily a Romantic-era painter could turn a famous Venetian site into a quick, bright, personal impression."},"nga:129283":{"d":"A crowd has gathered in a forest clearing to hear a preacher. Men, women and children sit on the ground or stand close together, a few on horseback, in cloaks and simple garments, many holding tall staffs, and some turn to talk to one another. The preacher, seated and dressed in colorful clothes, raises one hand and holds a small cross wrapped in cloth in the other, with a resting lamb at his side. Distant hills and a pale sky open behind the trees. Willem Reuter's cabinet-size canvas, about 49 by 66 centimeters, dates from around 1665.","i":"The preacher is Saint John the Baptist, shown as the Gospels describe him, calling people in the wilderness to repent and announcing the coming of Christ. The cross with its cloth and the lamb are his usual attributes: they point to Christ as the Lamb of God, as John names him in the Gospel of John. Preaching in a landscape was a favorite Flemish subject after Pieter Bruegel the Elder's version of 1566, because it joined a sacred story to a crowd of lifelike types and a wide countryside.","c":"The best of the picture is its human variety: the listeners are not a uniform audience but individuals, some attentive, some chatting, some simply resting, so that the sermon seems heard rather than staged. The rest follows a familiar formula, a saint at the center of a ring of listeners and a landscape stretching away behind. Reuter is a minor name, and this reads as a capable, engaging cabinet picture inside a tradition whose summit is Bruegel's."},"met:436622":{"d":"A crowd of revelers jostles in the green gloom of a tavern. At the center sits a plump, round-faced figure with fair curls and a wreath of leaves, in a red gown embroidered in white and a wide lace ruff, raising one finger as if making a point. Behind it a red-faced man in a huge black hat leans in, a garland of fish and an egg hanging at his chest; a laughing man with a spoon in his red cap grins at the left, and a man in a red cap with a dangling sausage looks down from the right. In front stand a striped stoneware tankard, a dish of roasted meat, bread and, at lower left, a bagpipe.","i":"Shrovetide is the feasting before Lent, and the Metropolitan Museum identifies two stock figures from popular theater: Hans Worst, with a sausage dangling from his cap, and Pekelharing, with a garland of salted fish and eggs. They flank a richly dressed figure who is probably a boy in drag, since women were not allowed to perform in guild theatricals. The foods in front carry a jokey sexual meaning too. This is one of Hals's earliest surviving paintings, from about 1616–17. Its bright colors, broad brushwork and loose rhythm may reflect Flemish influence, perhaps from a visit to his native Antwerp in 1616.","c":"The faces make the picture. Quick, wet strokes catch open mouths, shining cheeks and the sheen of the satin sleeve, and the sense of a laugh caught mid-breath already points toward Hals's later portraits. The crowding is the weakness: the heads at the left pile up awkwardly, the still life is dutifully painted but sits apart, and the joke, a mix of stage characters and sexual puns, needs a footnote now. It lacks the discipline of his 1620s group portraits, but it has an energy that those careful works sometimes lose."},"met:436616":{"d":"A young man and a young woman share an inn, with a dog nearby and a feathered hat among the details, in a close, cheerful scene that treats the figures almost as portraits. The oil on canvas, about 105 by 79 cm, is dated 1623. The brushwork is quick and visible, and the mood is exuberant. Hals, the Haarlem painter, was best known for portraits, and here he brings portrait immediacy to tavern pleasure.","i":"Tavern scenes with a young couple draw on the Parable of the Prodigal Son, the young man who squanders his inheritance on wine and women. Dutch painters of the 1620s showed the subject in contemporary dress, enjoying the wildness while implying a warning. Whether Hals meant a moral or a portrait of two sitters has been debated; the picture is also known as Yonker Ramp and His Sweetheart, a title from later tradition.","c":"Hals's gift is the instant: a laugh caught on the face, a gesture frozen mid-flow, in strokes that stay visible. In 1623 this was daring, since contemporaries preferred smooth finish. This early canvas has bravura rather than the later subtlety of the portraits, and the moral frame sits lightly on top of the pleasure. It remains one of the most vivid tavern scenes of its time, and its candor is its charm."},"met:436617":{"d":"A bearded man wearing a ruff is the subject of this portrait, painted in 1625 in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 cm. The sitter's name has not come down with the picture. Hals was by then established in Haarlem as the town's liveliest portraitist, and the handling of his work in the mid-1620s is brisk and confident, with the beard and the linen of the ruff offering the kind of texture he enjoyed.","i":"By 1625 the ruff, once the badge of every prosperous European, was giving way to the flat collar among younger men, so an older man in a ruff signals gravity, conservatism and the dignity of age. The beard, too, was a mark of maturity. A portrait like this was commissioned by the sitter himself as a record for his family and descendants; his office or profession is not known.","c":"Hals's touch in 1625 was already looser than the polished portraiture around him, and a bearded sitter in a ruff offers two things he handled with unfussy skill: the texture of hair and the crisp white of linen. The picture lacks the grandeur of his group portraits but gains intimacy and directness. It is a good, if not an extraordinary, example of the early manner, and a reminder of how much life he could find in an anonymous face."},"met:436625":{"d":"A man smoking a pipe is the main subject of this small oil on wood panel, about 47 by 50 cm, painted about 1623 to 1625; a woman also appears in the scene. The panel is nearly square, a compact format for a small, concentrated study. Hals painted a number of such genre pieces in the 1620s, lively figures of drinkers, musicians and merrymakers, in a quick, informal style close to the one he used for portraits.","i":"Tobacco was a new and fashionable habit in the Dutch Republic, and its pleasures were also a target of moralists, who linked smoking with idleness, drink and loose company. Painters used the pipe-smoker as a figure of enjoyment and warning at once, and in series on the Five Senses he could stand for smell or taste. Whether Hals meant a moral, a joke or simply a vivid character study is open; the ambiguity is part of the appeal.","c":"Hals's genre pieces of the 1620s are among the freshest things in Dutch painting: the fall of light on a face, a gesture caught mid-act, brushwork left visible. The Smoker belongs to that company, and its small size makes the performance more concentrated. Its limitation is the absence of story; it offers a type, a man with a pipe, not a plot. Immediate and sketchlike, it rewards close looking at touch."},"met:436618":{"d":"A small portrait panel, only about 22 by 17 cm, shows Anna van der Aar, born in 1576 or 1577, painted in 1626. Its size, date and medium match the Met's portrait of Petrus Scriverius exactly, which indicates that the two were made as a pair, and the small format suggests they may have been models for engravings. Hals, working in Haarlem, painted the head with fast, confident strokes.","i":"Anna van der Aar was the wife of the humanist Petrus Scriverius, with whose portrait hers forms a pair, and she was about fifty when painted. A wife's portrait as pendant to her husband's was a standard sign of marriage and respectability, and a small portrait that could be engraved spread the couple's image among friends and fellow scholars. Dutch portraits of women usually show modest dress and dignified reserve, virtues praised in good wives.","c":"At this scale Hals cannot lean on bravura; the challenge is to give a face life in a few square inches, and he does it with economy. The panel is a rare chance to see him as a miniaturist, with strokes that remain quick and visible even when small. It lacks the swagger of his larger works but has the sharpness of a good drawing. As half of a pair it works; alone it is a fragile, appealing thing."},"met:436619":{"d":"Petrus Scriverius (1576–1660), a Dutch humanist and historian, is shown in a small oil on wood panel of about 22 by 17 cm, dated 1626. It forms a pair with the Met's portrait of Anna van der Aar, the same size and year, and its modest format suggests a preparatory model for an engraved portrait. Hals's handling is quick and economical, as befits a likeness meant to be copied.","i":"Scriverius was a scholar, poet and editor, and a champion of Haarlem's claim that Laurens Coster, not Gutenberg, invented printing, a case he argued in a book of 1628. A scholar's portrait, particularly one that could be engraved, advertised learning and sociability among the Republic of Letters, and a print after it, probably with a Latin verse, served as a calling card. Dutch humanists exchanged such prints as tokens of friendship.","c":"Hals gives the scholar a quick, lively presence in a tiny format, the kind of portrait that works as a calling card and depends on the economy of the head. Beside the formal portraits of the day it is informal and unflattering in the best way. As a record of the Republic of Letters it is charming, though the scale limits grandeur, and its interest is greater as a sketch of a personality than as a monument."},"nga:571":{"d":"A smiling young man glances back at the viewer over his shoulder, seen from the waist up within a painted oval set in a small upright panel, about 29 by 23 centimeters. His enormous dark hat, with the brim pushed up, is as wide as his shoulders; a flat lace-edged collar is tied with a red ribbon, and wide lace cuffs spill over a pecan-brown jacket with shiny buttons. One elbow hooks over the back of a wooden chair, the fist closed on its finial. The brushwork is fluid and loose, especially in the clothing. Frans Hals painted it between 1626 and 1629.","i":"Informal, smiling portraits like this belong to the years of Hals's rising fame in Haarlem, when he turned the stiff formal portrait into something that seemed to catch a living moment. The big hat, lace and ribbon are the fashionable, slightly swaggering dress of a young man of means in the late 1620s. The painted stone oval works like a window through which the sitter is seen, adding depth to a small panel. His name is not recorded.","c":"Everything that makes Hals Hals is here in small: the quick turn of the head, flushed cheeks and parted lips, and lace and cuffs described with a handful of loaded strokes that read as texture from a distance and as pure paint up close. The panel has the immediacy of a sketch, and nothing is labored. If there is a limit, it is that the sitter's character is all surface charm, but charm is what the picture is after."},"met:436626":{"d":"A boy with a lute and a wine glass is the subject of this lively picture, oil on canvas, about 72 by 59 cm, dated about 1625. The attribution is debated: the work has long been given to Frans Hals, but has also been attributed to Judith Leyster, who worked in the same Haarlem circle. It is a character study, a tronie, rather than a commissioned portrait, and the quick handling suits its theme of youthful pleasure.","i":"Musicians and young drinkers were favorites of Dutch painters in the 1620s, in Haarlem as in Utrecht. The lute stands for music and love, the glass for wine and the pleasures of taste; together they suggest hearing and taste in a series on the Five Senses, or a gentle reminder that youth and pleasure pass. The painting may show a particular boy, but the subject is the type of the merry musician, not an individual's biography.","c":"The brushwork is the performance, and whichever hand it is, the picture has spontaneity and charm. If it is by Hals, it belongs among his freshest genre pieces; if by Leyster, it shows how closely she could follow his manner. The debate itself tells how near their styles were. The subject is familiar, and its originality lies in speed and conviction rather than in invention."},"nga:74":{"d":"A portrait of an elderly woman, oil on canvas, about 102 by 87 centimeters, painted by Frans Hals in 1633. The title gives her age, which Dutch portraits of the time often recorded in an inscription on the canvas. Hals's other portraits of older Haarlem women from these years generally show them seated, dressed in black, with a white linen collar and cap, and that is the likely pattern here. At this size the figure would be shown to the knees or thereabouts, with the hands in view, which matters in a portrait that is mostly black and white.","i":"A portrait with the sitter's age stated records a stage of life: she is presented as a woman of standing who has reached sixty with dignity and a respectable household behind her. Such portraits were often made as a pair with a husband's, so that the couple hung together, though whether a companion survives is not stated here. The sober dress was not poverty but fashion: black cloth was costly and signaled piety and thrift in the Dutch Republic.","c":"Hals's portraits of women are generally more reserved than his portraits of men, and an elderly sitter in black leaves him little to play with beyond the whites of her linen and the character in her face. What he gives is truthfulness: unflattering, direct modeling and a broad, economical brush. At about fifty-one he was at the height of his reputation, and this is a solid, serious commission rather than one of his sparkling ones."},"cma:160885":{"d":"A man in black stands three-quarter length against a warm brown-gold wall, one hand on his hip and the other loosely holding a glove. His broad white collar, finely pleated, spreads over the shoulders of a black doublet trimmed with braid and buttons, and ribbon rosettes circle the waist. A black cloak hangs over one arm, and lace shows at the cuff of the hip hand. He has ruddy cheeks, a moustache and a small pointed beard, and he looks out with a half-smile, as if about to speak. The brushwork is open and quick, especially in the background. An inscription at upper right records his age, 36, and the year, 1634.","i":"The Cleveland museum identifies the sitter as Tieleman Roosterman, an extremely wealthy linen and silk merchant of Haarlem. His clothes, sober but luxurious, announce his success: the black cloth, the lace-edged cuffs and the broad linen collar were fashionable signs of wealth, in a trade built on fine fabric. He may have ordered the portrait, and one of his wife, to mark their wedding in 1631, though that is not certain. The hand on the hip is a confident stance with roots in aristocratic portraiture. Unlike many Dutch painters who smoothed their surfaces, Frans Hals favoured strong, visible brushstrokes that convey the vitality of his sitters.","c":"Hals makes a rich man seem like a living person. The smile, the slightly lopsided energy of the stance and the flashes of lace show how much he could do with a few confident strokes. The black costume, which would be dull in other hands, becomes the most inventive part of the picture. If anything is lacking, it is depth: the sitter is a bright presence in front of a plain wall, with no setting or object to tell us more about him. Spontaneity was Hals's gift, and this portrait is full of it."},"cma:125895":{"d":"A woman, tentatively identified as Aeltje Dircksdr. Pater (1597–1678), is shown in an oil on canvas of about 67 by 52 cm, dated 1638. Her jewelry, lace and sumptuous fabrics show off her wealth, and an inscription states that she was 41 when Hals painted her. Portraits by Hals often feature bold, jagged brushstrokes, and here he experiments with wet-into-wet painting, building layers without letting them dry, with blended strokes at the hairline suggesting individual strands.","i":"The sitter has been tentatively identified as the wife of Jan de Wael (1594–1663), a Haarlem brewer and burgomaster, which would place her in the top rank of the city's civic elite. Brewing was one of Haarlem's great industries, and its families filled the town's offices. A woman's portrait with an inscribed age was a record of her place in that world, and the display of lace and jewels was a calculated statement of family standing.","c":"The wet-into-wet technique lets tones fuse where Hals wants softness, as at the hairline, while the lace and jewelry are touched in with loaded, confident strokes. The effect is of wealth made to glow without fuss. The sitter's expression is reserved, and the portrait does not offer the laughing candor of his genre pieces. In the 1630s his reputation stood high, and this is a solid, assured example of the period."},"met:436621":{"d":"A man, possibly the Haarlem citizen Nicolaes Pietersz Duyst van Voorhout (about 1600 to 1650), is shown in a portrait of about 81 by 66 cm, painted about 1636 to 1638. The title records the identification as only a possibility, and the size allows a half-length figure with room for the sitter's hands and costume. In these years Hals's touch grew broader and his handling of costume more economical, while the face remained the point of greatest care.","i":"Identifications of Hals's sitters often rest on later records, and the word possibly marks real uncertainty. Men of this class in Haarlem often held office in the civic guard and in city government, and a portrait of this kind recorded a man's standing among his peers. Dutch conventions favored dark clothing and restrained gesture, so that status was expressed through fabric, bearing and the quality of the likeness rather than through ornament.","c":"In the late 1630s Hals is at his most economical: a few broad strokes build the face, and the costume is handled with sketch-like freedom. The risk of this manner is a brilliant surface with little behind it; the better examples keep life in the face, and this one presumably does. It is a good example of the period though perhaps not among his most memorable, and the uncertain identity adds to its anonymity."},"nga:75":{"d":"A stout man in the dress of the Haarlem civic guard stands before a gray wall with a window opening onto a moonlit sky veiled in dark cloud. Seen from the hips up, he turns his head over his right shoulder to look at the viewer, brows slightly raised and the hint of a smile on his lips, one fist on his hip. He has long curly brown hair, a goatee and a wide dark hat; a golden yellow jacket with lace cuffs and collar is worn over a breastplate, with a marigold-orange sash at the waist. Frans Hals painted the canvas, about 86 by 69 centimeters, around 1636-38.","i":"Haarlem's civic guard companies, the schutterij, were militias of well-off citizens that had become social clubs with dinners, parades and group portraits, and Hals painted several of the great group pictures. A single portrait in uniform let a member record his place in that fellowship: the breastplate and sash mark him as a militiaman, while the jaunty pose suggests ease and sociability. The moonlit window is unusual and may allude to night watches, but that is an interpretation, not a certainty.","c":"Hals's sympathy for a sitter who enjoys being looked at is at full strength here: the sidelong glance, raised brows and proud belly under the sash tell more about the man than a stiffer portrait would. The gold of the jacket is handled with a few free strokes against the cold gray wall and night sky. The moonlit window is a daring touch in a plain commission. This is Hals in his swaggering 1630s mood, confident and a little theatrical."},"met:436620":{"d":"A man identified as Paulus Verschuur (1606–1667) is portrayed in 1643 in a canvas of about 119 by 94 cm, large enough to show the figure at generous scale with a good deal of space around him. Hals was in his early sixties, long established as the portraitist of Haarlem's burghers, and the picture shows the economical, broken brushwork of his maturity, in which a few decisive strokes stand for a face, a hand or a fold of cloth.","i":"Verschuur's name survives, which is a mark of a commission made to last. The sitter was about thirty-seven, a prosperous man in his prime, and the size of the canvas tells of status. Dutch portrait conventions of the 1640s favored dark clothing, a white collar and restrained gesture, combining Calvinist sobriety with a quiet display of means.","c":"Hals in the 1640s works with a subdued palette of black, white and gray against warm flesh, and tone replaces color. The strokes are broad, and presence comes from the angle of the head and the set of the eyes. The cost of the restraint is some monotony in the costume, with the face taking all the intensity. At this size the portrait shows the mature manner clearly: loose, certain and unforced."},"nga:76":{"d":"A portrait of Willem Coymans, painted by Frans Hals in 1645 in oil on canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters, a size that normally carries a figure to about the waist. The Coymans were a wealthy merchant family, and Hals painted several of them in the mid-1640s. No description of the sitter's pose or dress accompanies the record, so it is safest to say what Hals's portraits of these years usually offer: restricted color, bright white linen against dark clothing, and broad, quick strokes in the dress with a more careful face.","i":"A family of the Coymans' wealth commissioned portraits as part of a deliberate display of standing, and a Hals portrait of this date, with its confident, unfussy handling, showed that the sitter could afford Haarlem's leading portraitist. Portraits of the mid-1640s are generally less showy than those of the 1620s, preferring plain grounds and sober color, in keeping with a taste for restraint among the rich. Little more about Willem is documented in the museum's entry.","c":"By 1645 Hals had pared his style to essentials, and the late portraits succeed because of what they leave out: no landscapes, no props, no flourish, only a living head set against a quiet ground. The risk of that economy is sameness, since the bold slashes of the dress and the plain background can repeat from sitter to sitter. At its best the economy is eloquent, and this is a dignified, brisk likeness in the manner that later painters admired."},"nga:77":{"d":"A man stands against a dark earth-brown ground, seen from about the hips up, his body angled to the right and his face turned to meet the viewer's gaze. He wears an ink-black coat with a velvety sheen and tightly spaced buttons, a wide sash at the waist and a tall black hat tilted slightly to one side over long brown curls. A broad starched white collar lies across his collarbones. One hand rests at his hip, and the other, in a dove-gray glove, holds a second glove. The canvas is about 94 by 75 centimeters, painted by Frans Hals in 1646-48.","i":"The sitter is identified as Adriaen van Ostade, the Haarlem painter of peasant and tavern scenes, who by early tradition was a pupil of Hals. Showing a painter of rustic subjects as an elegant gentleman in black, with gloves and a sash, was a statement of success: by the 1640s Ostade was an established master. The black costume and plain ground follow Haarlem portrait fashion of the time, which prized restraint, expensive cloth and the strong contrast of white linen on black.","c":"Black on black is the real subject: Hals models the velvet coat, the glove and the folds with a few broad, confident strokes, leaving the bright collar and the face to carry the light. The restraint is typical of his late manner, which strips away the color and swagger of his earlier work, and it is arguably more eloquent for it. What it concedes is the early dazzle; what it gains is concentration on the sitter's alert gaze."},"nga:78":{"d":"A young man with a plump, pale face looks at the viewer over his right shoulder, one elbow hooked over the back of a carved wooden chair, his brows lifted, his round cheeks flushed and his pink lips parted. A tall-crowned black hat sits above shoulder-length black waves, and a lace-edged white collar spreads over a black satin coat. The hat casts a shadow on the taupe-gray wall behind him, where Hals signed the picture with two monograms of his initials. The canvas is about 68 by 55 centimeters, dated 1646-48, and painted loosely throughout.","i":"The sitter twisting around the back of his chair is a pose Hals used often, giving the figure an air of interrupted informality and a spontaneous glance. With the black satin, the lace and the tall hat, it marks a young man of means who wanted to look relaxed and fashionable. The closed fist on the chair back may hold something, as the museum notes, but its meaning, if any, is lost. His name is not recorded.","c":"Loose brushwork throughout is the point: the hat's shadow on the wall, the sheen of the black satin and the lace edge are all done with a quickness that keeps the image alive. The pose is stock Hals, and the sitter is less vivid than the best of them, lacking both the bravura of the 1620s and the interest of a named personality. As an example of the late Hals's economy, though, it is accomplished and very fresh."},"nga:1167":{"d":"A pale-skinned man looks out from the corners of his dark brown eyes, his body angled to the left almost in profile, his right palm pressed to his chest. He has thick dark brows, fleshy cheeks, parted pink lips, a moustache and a small patch of hair beneath the lower lip, with straight chin-length brown hair under a high-crowned black hat pushed back on his forehead. A flat white collar lies over a voluminous black coat, and a wide white cuff shows at the wrist. The canvas, about 64 by 54 centimeters, dates from 1648-50; Hals signed it with a conjoined monogram.","i":"The hand on the breast is a common gesture in Dutch portraiture, often read as a sign of sincerity, loyalty or good faith, and it also gave the painter a way to show a hand without a prop. The black coat and white collar were the standard dress of a respectable Dutch citizen at mid-century. The sitter is unnamed, so his occupation and rank are unknown; the picture presents him as a type of the sober, assured burgher.","c":"The sidelong glance gives a quiet liveliness to a canvas whose clothing is black on black. Hals paints the coat as a loose pattern of dark strokes with hardly any outline and saves his detail for the face. It is a restrained picture, with no hat flourish and no swagger, only a concentrated look. Among the late portraits it is one of the plainer and more honest ones, and its quietness is its strength."},"nga:1168":{"d":"A man in black stands, seen to the thighs, against an elephant-gray ground, one hand on his hip and the other loosely gripping the fabric of his cloak. He has shoulder-length wavy light brown hair, bushy brows, soft jowls, a thin mustache and a patch of hair under the lower lip, and wears a tall black hat with the wide brim turned over one ear, a hip-length black cloak and jacket and a flat white collar tied with fluffy tassels. The face is more detailed than the rest; clothes and background are loosely painted in gray and brown. The canvas, about 114 by 85 centimeters, dates from 1650-52.","i":"A standing format was for sitters who could afford the larger canvas and wanted to look at ease in public: the hand on the hip, the cloak thrown over the shoulder and the turned-up hat brim all suggest a man of independent means. Collars tied with tassels were up-to-date fashion in the mid-seventeenth century, replacing the starched ruff of the previous generation. Black cloth was costly, so plain dark dress signaled sobriety and wealth together.","c":"By 1650 Hals could suggest a figure with astonishing economy: slate-gray streaks stand for buttons, a few strokes of parchment brown for a glove, and the whole costume dissolves into the gray ground as soon as one steps close. The face keeps its detail and carries the weight. The limit is anonymity: the sitter is pleasant and self-possessed, but the picture says little of what he thought. It is a fine example of the near-abstract handling of late Hals."},"met:436623":{"d":"An unnamed man is the subject of this oil on canvas, about 111 by 86 cm, large enough for a half-length figure and dated to the early 1650s, when Hals was about seventy. His late portraits are known for a restrained palette of blacks, whites and grays and for broad, open brushwork, and this one belongs to that last phase. Hals continued to paint until his death in 1666.","i":"Hals's late sitters were often the sober Calvinist burghers of Haarlem, dressed in black as befitted their faith and rank; wealth was shown by the quality of cloth, not color. The unnamed sitter belongs to the governing and merchant class. Hals himself was old and short of money by the 1650s, and the smoother manner of his younger rivals had taken over portrait taste, so these late canvases were painted against the fashion.","c":"Late Hals is the painter's painter: a face is struck with a few directed strokes and left alone. What the sitter loses in polish he gains in presence, a quality that Manet, Sargent and Van Gogh all noticed. The cost is the very sketchiness that his contemporaries found unfinished. To a modern eye this is his boldest manner, and this canvas is a sound example of it."},"met:436624":{"d":"An unnamed woman is shown in a portrait of about 100 by 82 cm, painted around 1650. The surface was reworked, probably in the eighteenth century, so some passages are later additions rather than Hals's own hand. The conventions of the Dutch portrait of a prosperous woman govern the picture: modest dress and a composed manner, which Hals enlivened in his late years with broad, open brushwork.","i":"In the eighteenth century collectors and dealers commonly repainted worn or damaged passages, or improved a picture to suit current taste, and the note about reworking signals that part of this surface is not original. As a type, the portrait is that of a prosperous woman, usually painted as a pendant to her husband's to mark a marriage and family standing. Dutch portraits of women stressed piety and domestic virtue.","c":"A reworked picture asks for caution. Where Hals's brush survives, the freedom is unmistakable; where later hands have smoothed it, the effect is blander. The critic's pleasure lies in separating the two. As a late portrait of a woman it shows the dignity he gave to older sitters, but its condition keeps it below the best of the set."},"cma:315934":{"d":"A table is spread with a feast, seen from a slight height. At the left a tall woven basket overflows with artichokes, white asparagus, radishes, peaches, plums, cherries and a pink flower. In the middle a dark pewter plate holds a ham with a slice cut pink, and behind it more pewter holds pale trotters dressed with parsley. A red plate carries a small fish, a blue-and-white porcelain bowl holds berries, another plate carries a curl of butter, and a bunch of red grapes lies beside a curved slice of orange-pink fruit. A knife, a half lemon, bread rolls and a tipped green glass complete the scene. A small butterfly rests on a bone. The edges are crisp and the colours clean against the plain, dark background.","i":"The museum notes the range of foods and tablewares, from an earthenware plate to delicate Chinese porcelain, a costly luxury made possible by international trade. To seventeenth-century viewers, such abundance would have suggested a utopian world without scarcity or hunger. At the same time, partly eaten food, an overturned glass and an insect on a bone suggest the diners have just left, adding a hint of the passing of pleasure. The painter also thoughtfully included condiments: butter, lemon, parsley for the trotters and a dab of mustard for the ham. Jacob van Hulsdonck, born in Antwerp in 1582, became a master in the city's painters' guild by 1608 and specialised in banquet pieces and fruit.","c":"This is an inventory of delights, and its pleasures are in the particulars: the skin of the peaches, the bloom on the grapes, the cold shine of pewter against white linen, the cherry red beside blue and white. The weakness is composition. Each object is lit and displayed separately, with little overlap, so the picture reads as a catalogue rather than a single space, and no one item draws the eye first. That was characteristic of early Flemish banquet pieces, where clarity mattered more than atmosphere. Within those limits the painting is lucid and fresh."},"nga:161665":{"d":"A blue-and-white porcelain bowl with a flaring rim is heaped with small red wild strawberries, and cherries and white and red currants lie scattered on a wooden ledge around it. A single ruby-and-white carnation is stuck into the back of the bowl, strawberry leaves lie among the berries, a honey-yellow butterfly perches on a leaf, and a fly has settled on one of the strawberries. Water droplets glisten on leaves and ledge. The rim is painted with geometric bands and floral medallions. The oil on copper, about 28 by 36 centimeters, dates from around 1620.","i":"The bowl is Chinese export porcelain of the Wanli period, called kraak ware in the Netherlands, a rare and fashionable luxury after Dutch ships began capturing cargoes of it in the early 1600s. Antwerp painters of fruit still lifes, such as Jacob van Hulsdonck, praised both nature's abundance and the owner's taste. The fly, the butterfly and the fast-perishing berries are often read as reminders that beauty is brief, and the carnation, associated with the Passion, may add a religious note, though these readings are suggestions, not certainties.","c":"Hulsdonck shows why he ranks among the best Antwerp fruit painters: each berry is a dot of reflected light, the water drops are placed with a jeweler's eye, and the copper gives the red and blue a gemlike glow. The butterfly and fly add life and a quiet moral. It is a cool, precise, slightly static arrangement, admired for its finish more than its drama, which is what a cabinet picture of this kind offered."},"met:436698":{"d":"A basket of grapes with other fruit, lemons and leaves among them, is the subject of this oil on wood, about 50 by 65 centimeters, dated probably to about 1635-45. Grapes, lemons and leaves are recorded, and nothing more of the arrangement is described. Jacob van Hulsdonck belonged to the Antwerp tradition of fruit still life, in which glossy fruit, deep shadows and a dark ground are standard. Larger than his small pictures on copper, this panel would probably have hung as a decorative piece in a collector's room.","i":"Fruit still lifes of this kind showed the abundance of nature and the wealth of a household that could afford it: grapes for the vineyard and wine, lemons as a costly import. Netherlandish painters often invite a moral reading as well, with ripe fruit as a reminder that sweetness does not last and lemons as a sign of bitterness within sweetness. Such pictures were bought by merchants and collectors and prized for skill in rendering bloom, gloss and texture.","c":"Antwerp still-life painters raised the rendering of fruit to a refined craft, and Hulsdonck's reputation rests on precision and jewel-like clarity. A basket piece lets him set different textures against one another: the dusty bloom of grapes, the waxy rind of lemons, the veined leaves. On a larger panel the risk is that the effect becomes decorative rather than intimate. This is a pleasant, skilled work in a conventional format."},"met:11512":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Jeremiah Platt, painted in oil on canvas, about 123 by 98 centimeters, by John Mare in 1767. He is shown with a pierced splat-back chair, a fashionable piece of furniture of the Chippendale type. The canvas is large enough to show a figure well below the waist, with room for furnishings that were themselves a statement of means and taste. No more of the pose or costume is recorded here.","i":"Colonial portraits of men of business announced credit and permanence: a fashionable chair, good clothes and a large canvas told neighbors and trading partners that the sitter had arrived. Painters like Mare often worked from English portrait formulas, known through prints, adapting them to local sitters and local skills. Platt's trade is not recorded in the entry, so none is assumed; the chair is the one prop the museum records.","c":"Colonial portraits of this kind tend to be plainer and more linear than the polished work of Copley or West, and the virtues are clarity and directness: a legible figure, an accurately drawn chair, a firm design. What they lack is subtlety of modeling and atmosphere, and the sitter can seem arranged rather than alive. It is valued as a rare, honest record of colonial taste and not as a high point of portrait painting."},"met:437047":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in a miniature painted on ivory around 1790 by Anne Foldsone Mee, and the museum title proposes, with a cautious 'possibly', that the sitter was Barbara (1768-1829), Marchioness of Donegall. The recorded motif is simply a woman, so the identification rests on tradition and on the genre: a small bust-length likeness meant to be worn or held, in which the ivory support gives the flesh tones their glow.","i":"The title styles the sitter Marchioness of Donegall, a name belonging to an Anglo-Irish family whose estates were centered on Belfast, and a miniature of such a lady shows the kind of aristocratic client for whom Mee worked in her early career. Portraits of noble ladies circulated within families and among admirers. The 'possibly' in the title is honest: without an inscription or documented provenance, the sitter cannot be fixed.","c":"The flattering conventions of the late eighteenth-century miniature, a gentle tilt of the head, rosy cheeks and a soft background, made sitters look alike, which is a limit as well as an attraction. Mee handles the formula with polish, and the object is lovely. Individual character seldom survives such a formula, so the picture is best enjoyed for its grace and finish."},"met:437048":{"d":"A woman in a fichu, a soft scarf crossed over the shoulders and breast, is portrayed in a miniature painted on ivory around 1790 by Anne Foldsone Mee. The title says she is 'said to be' Lady Sophia Boyle, which signals a traditional but unproven identification. The recorded motifs are a woman, a fichu and a miniature, the typical ingredients of a bust-length portrait of the late eighteenth century.","i":"The fichu, a triangle of muslin or gauze, was a fashionable item in the 1780s and 1790s, worn crossed over the bosom as part of the soft, natural look that replaced the stiff court dress of earlier decades. A miniature of this kind would have been a personal gift, set in a locket or bracelet. 'Said to be' is museum language for a tradition that cannot be confirmed from documents, a reminder of how fragile the identity of a portrait can be.","c":"Mee's early manner is delicate and decorative, with fine stippling, pale tints and a light, airy ground in the fashion of the Cosway school. The finish is high, and the lace-like detail of hair and fichu is the particular pleasure. These miniatures flatter more than they penetrate, but Mee went on to receive commissions from the Prince of Wales, which says something of the standard she reached."},"met:437225":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of a young woman, painted by James Nixon around 1780-85. At this date ivory miniatures were usually only a few centimeters high, made to be held in the hand, set in a locket or bracelet, or kept in a small case. The surface is built from tiny strokes and stipple of watercolor over a thin sheet of ivory, which lets light shine up through the paint and gives flesh a glow. No details of dress or setting are recorded.","i":"Portrait miniatures were intimate tokens in Georgian England, exchanged between lovers, given to family or kept as reminders of the absent. A young woman's likeness could mark an engagement, a marriage or a parting, and the sitter might be shown in the fashionable dress and powdered hair of the 1780s. Nixon, who exhibited at the Royal Academy, belonged to the generation that made the miniature a polished, fashionable art. Her name is not recorded.","c":"A good miniature depends on the delicacy of its flesh tones and the fidelity of its likeness, and Nixon was a respected professional in a crowded field. The very small scale rewards close looking, and the finish tends to be smooth and pleasing; what such miniatures rarely offer is psychological depth, for the format and its social purpose favor flattery. This one is best valued as a charming, skilled example of the type."},"met:437226":{"d":"A woman's portrait in miniature, painted on ivory by James Nixon around 1790. The type is usually a small oval plaque only a few centimeters high, made to be worn or carried. Nixon was working in the decade when English miniatures tended to grow lighter in palette and simpler in costume, in step with a Neoclassical taste for plain, airy design. As in other ivory miniatures, thin watercolor lets the pale ground show through and lend the flesh its glow.","i":"In 1790 a woman's miniature served many ends: a gift to a husband or fiancé, a memorial after a death, a token worn close to the body. The medium's intimacy was its meaning; it was meant to be seen by one person, held in the hand or opened from a locket. The sitter's identity is lost here, and the picture is best read as the likeness of a woman of her day who could afford a fashionable portraitist.","c":"On the small scale of a miniature the test is likeness and finish, and Nixon is usually ranked below the leading miniaturists of his day, such as Richard Cosway and John Smart. Within that rank he offers a clean, pleasant and reliable surface. The format asks for flattery and rarely reveals more than a polite likeness, so the interest lies mostly in craft: the control of thin color on a tiny ground."},"met:437944":{"d":"About a dozen young women gather around a woodland pool in a sun-dappled garden. Some lie on the grass, some stand, two sit on a bank at the right with their backs to the viewer, and one at the lower left reaches a foot toward the water. They are bare to the waist or half covered by loose drapery in cream, gold, green, crimson and blue, and their long hair is half loosened. Dark foliage closes in around them, opening at the upper left to a castle on a hill under a pale sky. The skin is smooth and highly finished, the poses are carefully varied, and the colours of the fabrics are the brightest notes in the scene.","i":"The subject comes from Spanish legend. Florinda, called La Cava, a young woman at the Visigothic court in Toledo, was said to have been spied on while bathing by King Roderic, who peers from the trees at the upper left. Later versions of the story blame the king's treatment of her for the Muslim conquest of Spain in 711, but the legend is not history. The Met's canvas, dated 1853, is a replica of the version Queen Victoria gave Prince Albert as a birthday present in 1852, now at Osborne House. An old legend let Winterhalter, favourite portraitist of Victoria and of Napoleon III, show a nude group in a form respectable enough for royal walls.","c":"Winterhalter was a first-rate flatterer of royalty, and Florinda shows what he could do when released from official portraiture: the grouping is skilful, no pose is repeated, and the draperies carry the colour. It is also thoroughly of its time, an erotic subject made respectable by an old legend and a polished finish. The faces are interchangeable, the story is almost lost, and the picture has little of the psychological interest of his best portraits. Modernism dismissed him as a society painter; this canvas shows why, though it is more skilled than that reputation allows."},"met:437942":{"d":"The Empress Eugénie is shown in profile, with a tree in the background and a dress, in an oil on canvas of about 93 by 74 cm, dated 1854, the second year of her marriage to Napoleon III. Winterhalter, a German who became the favorite portraitist of Europe's courts, painted Queen Victoria, Napoleon III and the Habsburgs as well, and this is a portrait made at the start of the Second Empire.","i":"Eugénie de Montijo, a Spanish noblewoman, became Empress of the French in 1853, and her image mattered to a new regime that wanted legitimacy and glamour. Her taste set fashions, and Winterhalter's portraits spread them. A profile view recalls the cameos and coins of ancient emperors and so lends the empress an imperial, classical dignity, a deliberate allusion in a regime that claimed Napoleon's heritage.","c":"Winterhalter's gift was flattery elevated to style: porcelain skin, glossy fabric, a soft aristocratic glow. Critics see slickness and a want of psychological depth, and they are not wrong, but the job was to make a regime look elegant and he did it superbly. The profile format asks for a more austere design than his grand group portraits, and the result is clean, disciplined and polished."},"met:437943":{"d":"A young woman, Countess Alexander Nikolaevitch Lamsdorff (1835–1866), is portrayed at about twenty-four in a large canvas of about 145 by 115 cm, dated 1859, at the height of the crinoline. The title names her by her husband's name and title, as was usual, with her own maiden name in brackets. The scale and the fashionable dress mark a society portrait made for a family of rank.","i":"Winterhalter was the favorite portraitist of European courts, and Russian aristocrats who traveled in Paris and Germany made up part of his clientele. The countess died in 1866, at about thirty-one, so a portrait painted in the confidence of youth now serves as a memorial to a short life. The dress, a statement of fashion and of cost, was part of the message: a young woman of rank, well married and well dressed.","c":"At this scale Winterhalter's gifts are fully on show: smooth flesh, rustling silk, a pose elegant without effort. The surfaces dazzle and the person recedes, a recurrent criticism of his work, since his sitters tend toward a flawless serenity. As an example of Second Empire society portraiture it is fine, and its limits are the genre's; it is a record of a style of beauty rather than of one mind."},"met:743039":{"d":"The household of Abraham splits apart in this scene from Genesis. The figures include Abraham, Sarah and their son Isaac as well as Hagar and her son Ishmael, who are being sent away. The oil on canvas, about 99 by 114 centimeters, was painted by Johann Friedrich Overbeck in 1839-41. Overbeck led the Nazarenes, the German painters in Rome who modeled their work on Perugino and the early Raphael, and whose pictures typically favor firm outline and clear, ordered grouping.","i":"In Genesis, Sarah, long childless, asked Abraham to cast out Hagar, her maidservant, and Hagar's son Ishmael once her own son Isaac was born; Abraham, though grieved, obeyed after God told him to heed Sarah and promised that Ishmael too would become a nation. Christian readers took the story as a lesson in obedience and in the two covenants, and Overbeck, a devout convert to Catholicism, chose such scenes for their moral and religious clarity.","c":"Overbeck's pictures are admired for sincerity and cleanly drawn design, and the subject suits his gravity: a moment of decision, with obedience, grief and exile in one household. But the Nazarene method has a price. Firm contours and pale, even color can leave figures statue-like on a bright, still stage, with passion held at arm's length. It is a worthy, serious canvas rather than a moving one, and more convincing as drawing than as painting."},"met:10516":{"d":"A portrait of James Merrill Cook, painted in oil on canvas in 1840 by Nelson Cook. The canvas is tall and narrow, about 122 by 62 centimeters. It is a pendant to the portrait of his wife, and the two were meant to hang side by side. Sitter and artist share a surname, but the entry does not say whether they were related, and no description of pose or dress is recorded here.","i":"Pendant portraits of husband and wife were the standard way to record a marriage and a household in the 1840s, hung on either side of a door or mantel. The matching size and date show that the Cooks wanted their respectability recorded together. Such pictures seldom tell of the sitters' lives; they record how the sitters wished to look, sober, tidy and established. The sitter's occupation is not recorded here.","c":"Nelson Cook belongs to a plain, readable American tradition of the 1840s, whose virtues are directness and clarity and whose limits are conventional posing and a certain flatness of modeling. The picture is an unpretentious likeness meant to be legible to family and neighbors, not a display of virtuosity. As half of a matched pair it gains weight, since the two portraits answer one another across a wall."},"met:10517":{"d":"A portrait of Anna Cady Cook, identified in the title as Mrs. James Merrill Cook, painted in oil on canvas in 1840 by Nelson Cook. It has the same tall, narrow format as the portrait of her husband, about 122 by 62 centimeters, and the two were conceived as a pair. The details of her pose, dress and setting are not recorded.","i":"Women's portraits of the 1840s followed conventions of dress and hair that told viewers about respectability, taste and the means of the household: a neat coiffure, a modest neckline and good fabric. As the pendant to her husband's, hers records the wife's place in a household that wanted its public face recorded together. The picture offers little narrative, and its meaning lies in the decorum it displays.","c":"Pendant portraits live by balance: a wife's picture must match her husband's in scale and tone without repeating it, and dress and hair give the painter more to describe. Nelson Cook belongs to a plain, readable American tradition of the 1840s, with its virtues in directness and clarity and its limits in convention and a certain flatness of modeling. It is a respectable work, and part of its value lies in the pairing."},"met:10373":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of a woman, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1844 by John Carlin, measuring only about 8.7 by 7.0 centimeters. The sitter's name is not recorded. Such a plaque would have been set under glass in a small case or frame and held in the hand, so every detail is painted for close looking. The thin ivory gives skin tones a soft glow, because the paint is translucent and the pale ground shows through.","i":"In the 1840s the portrait miniature was a sentimental keepsake, given at an engagement or marriage, or kept to remember a relative. It was also a trade under pressure: daguerreotypes, introduced in 1839, offered a cheaper likeness within a few years, and miniaturists answered with color, finish and the personal touch of the hand. Carlin, who was deaf, made his career in that medium through these years.","c":"A miniature is judged by delicacy, likeness and economy, and Carlin's are clean, bright and pleasantly finished. The small scale rewards looking closely but leaves little room for psychology, and the lady here is presented as she wished to be seen: composed and attractive. It is a competent, charming example of a form that was about to be overtaken by photography."},"met:15015":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a girl named in the title as Florine Turner, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1845 by John Carlin and measuring about 10 by 8 centimeters. A tree is part of the picture, which suggests an outdoor setting rather than a plain interior. Like other ivory miniatures it was made to be held and looked at closely, probably in a small case, with the translucent paint letting the white ground light the flesh from beneath.","i":"Children were often painted out of doors, with trees and sky suggesting innocence, health and a happy childhood. Because childhood was brief and child mortality high, a miniature of a girl was a cherished record of a particular age as well as a likeness, and it could be carried easily by a parent. Nothing in the record says who Florine Turner was beyond her name, and the tree should be read as a setting more than a symbol.","c":"A child's miniature has a particular charm, and an outdoor setting gives a miniaturist the chance of soft greens and blues to relieve the flesh tones. The danger is that a child becomes a pretty doll, since the form favors sweetness over character. Carlin's strengths, clear color and tidy finish, make him a dependable maker of keepsakes rather than a probing portraitist."},"met:10374":{"d":"A double portrait miniature of a boy and a girl, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1846 by John Carlin and measuring about 11 by 9 centimeters, large for the type. The title identifies them as the Thompson children, presumably siblings. A miniature of this size was usually set in a case or frame and kept as a family treasure. As in other works on ivory, the paint is thin and translucent, so the pale ground glows through the flesh.","i":"A portrait of siblings recorded the family's children at one moment in their growing up, which mattered in an age when many children did not survive to adulthood. Placing a boy and a girl together also presented the household as complete and harmonious. The surname is all the record provides about the sitters, and their ages and relationship are not stated, so the picture is best read as an affectionate family keepsake.","c":"Fitting two figures into a field barely larger than a hand is a real test of composition, and Carlin needs to give each child a distinct presence without crowding. The format favors tenderness and clean color over drama. Even without more detail, the scale and the double format make it more ambitious than a single-sitter miniature, and it stands as a representative piece of mid-century American portraiture on ivory."},"met:10372":{"d":"A group portrait miniature of the Allen children, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1847 by John Carlin and measuring about 10 by 9 centimeters. The title speaks of children, and the sitters appear to be boys, though their number and poses are not recorded. A miniature of this size, larger than most, was usually framed or cased under glass and kept as a family treasure. Its thin, translucent paint lets the white of the ivory glow up through the flesh.","i":"Portraits of siblings fixed a family's children at one fleeting age, which mattered at a time when childhood illness was common and children changed quickly. For boys, such portraits also recorded a family's hopes: tidy hair and good clothes announced a respectable upbringing. Nothing in the entry says who the Allens were, so the sitters' names, ages and rank are left open, and the picture is best read as an affectionate family keepsake.","c":"Fitting several children into a space barely larger than a hand demands tidy design, and Carlin's strengths, clear color and orderly finish, serve it well. The charm of such a miniature lies in the sitters' freshness; its limit is that portraits of children easily slide into sweetness. As a piece of mid-century American portraiture it is engaging and competent, and more ambitious in scale than most of his work on ivory."},"met:14307":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Elizabeth Stuyvesant, called Mrs. Nicholas Fish in the title, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1848 by John Carlin and measuring about 10 by 8 centimeters. No description of her dress or pose is given. As in other ivory miniatures, the paint is thin and translucent, so the pale ground lights the flesh from beneath, and the plaque would have been framed or cased for close viewing.","i":"Her maiden name, Stuyvesant, is borne by descendants of Peter Stuyvesant, the last Dutch governor of New Amsterdam, and the Fish family was equally prominent in the city's public life. She is presumably the Elizabeth Stuyvesant who was the mother of the statesman Hamilton Fish. A miniature of such a sitter was a family object, meant to preserve a respected woman's likeness among her relatives and not to be hung for display.","c":"A miniature depends on a faithful likeness and fine handling of the face, and in a setting with so little room for incident Carlin is reliable: clear color, careful finish, no fuss. The format leaves almost nothing but the sitter, so the interest is the face. It is a good, sober example of the type, and its value as a portrait of a notable family's matriarch is as important as its art."},"met:14427":{"d":"A tiny portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1850 by John Carlin and measuring only about 3.3 by 2.5 centimeters, not much bigger than a postage stamp. A plaque this small would have been set in a locket, brooch or small case, to be worn or carried. His name and dress are not recorded. The thin, translucent paint lets the white ivory glow through the flesh tones, the medium's special quality.","i":"At this size the picture was personal jewelry, something to keep close, perhaps given to a wife or fiancée or made for a relative far away; in about 1850 many families were being separated by the rush to California and by long sea voyages. It was a different thing from the larger framed portrait that hung in a parlor. Because the sitter is anonymous, only the type remains: a respectable man of the day.","c":"The challenge of a miniature the size of a stamp is to hold a likeness in a few millimeters of face, and a painter like Carlin does it by keeping detail few and color clear. The result is necessarily summary, an attractive, precise little thing more than a searching portrait. Its charm lies in its intimacy and its craft rather than in any individuality of expression."},"met:14428":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of a woman, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1850 by John Carlin and measuring about 4.1 by 3.2 centimeters, a little larger than a postage stamp. Its catalogue number follows that of the Met's Portrait of a Gentleman of the same date, so the two may have been made as companions, though the record does not say so. Her dress and hair are not described.","i":"A woman's miniature of this size could be worn as a locket or brooch or placed in a small hinged case, and such likenesses were often exchanged within families or between engaged couples. In 1850 competition from daguerreotypes, which were cheaper and faster, had begun to cut into the miniaturist's trade, and the hand-painted miniature survived by offering color and a flattering finish that the photograph could not yet match. The sitter's name has not come down with the picture.","c":"In a field a few centimeters across the painter must choose, and Carlin's solution is a clear contour, an even complexion and soft color that give the sitter a composed, pleasing look. The result is attractive and competent and, like most such miniatures, concerned more with type than with temperament. It is notable chiefly as a faithful example of what a skilled American miniaturist could achieve in the daguerreotype era."},"met:15016":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1854 by John Carlin and measuring about 4 by 3 centimeters. The Met gives no name or description of costume. By this date daguerreotypes and the newer ambrotypes were everywhere, and a hand-painted miniature of this size, worn as a brooch or kept in a small case, was becoming a more deliberate and more costly choice. The ivory's translucency, giving a soft inner glow to the flesh, was something photography could not copy.","i":"By 1854 portrait miniatures were more a luxury than a necessity: photographs provided the cheap, quick likeness, and a painted miniature signaled that a family valued something hand-made and lasting. Many miniaturists survived by painting over photographs, though whether that is the case here is not stated. The sitter's name has not come down, and his portrait is best read as a record of a middle-class man of the 1850s.","c":"Carlin's strength in miniature is clean, bright, fresh color, and at four centimeters tall little else is possible than a clear likeness and a tidy design. The picture records the sitter's appearance more than it reveals his character. It is a sound piece of small-scale craftsmanship from a time when his art was being displaced, and its modest scale is both its charm and its limit."},"met:10371":{"d":"The title points to sailors ashore after a voyage: a sailor, a man, a woman and a child appear with fruit and umbrellas, and a ship is in view. The exact action is not recorded, but the few figures are clearly meant to tell a family or community story and not to record a place. It is an oil on canvas of about 51 by 76 centimeters, painted in 1857 by John Carlin, who is better known for tiny portraits on ivory.","i":"Pictures of sailors in port were popular in the 1850s, when American merchant ships and whalers sailed all over the world, and a sailor's return after months or years away was a stock of both sentiment and humor. Fruit and umbrellas may hint at a warm, busy waterfront, and the woman and child suggest a reunion or a vendor's trade; the picture does not say which. Genre painting of this kind appealed to a broad American public, who read their own lives into it.","c":"For a painter whose reputation rests on miniatures, an oil on this scale is a bold step. One would expect bright, clear color and neat detail from his training on ivory; the risk is that a group of small figures reads as a series of vignettes. It shows Carlin trying to widen his range at a time when photography was undermining the miniature trade, and it is more interesting as a record of that ambition than as a major statement."},"met:15294":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of a woman, painted in watercolor on ivory by John Carlin and measuring about 4.9 by 3.8 centimeters. The museum gives no date, so it cannot be placed more closely within his career. Her name, dress and hair are not recorded. As in other ivory miniatures, the paint is thin and translucent, so the ivory glows through, and the plaque would have been kept in a case, locket or frame.","i":"Undated and unnamed, the picture is a reminder of how many miniatures survive without their stories: the sitter's name and the occasion often traveled in a case lid, a note or family memory, and were lost when the object was separated from them. What remains is a likeness made to be held by one person, a woman presented as she wished to be remembered, composed and neat.","c":"Without a date or a name, the picture must stand on craft, and Carlin's craft is dependable: clear drawing of the features, clean color and a fine finish suited to the small ground. What is missing is the particular spark of a documented sitter, and the portrait stays generic, a pleasing keepsake more than a statement. For most miniatures the value is intimate rather than artistic, and this one is no exception."},"nga:106596":{"d":"A dark panel in a wide wooden frame seems to carry a frayed one-dollar bill with a lifting corner, a half-size fifty-cent fractional note, a worn copper coin held by three small prongs, a silver coin on the frame's ledge and a small photograph of a bearded man tucked in at lower left. A pinned stamp and several torn, canceled stamps are stuck on, and a slip reads J. Haberle. On closer inspection the frame is rough canvas and everything is painted. The oil, about 25 by 36 centimeters, is signed and dated New Haven, 1887.","i":"This is a trompe-l'oeil still life, from the French for deceive the eye, a specialty of American painters of the 1880s such as William Harnett and John Frederick Peto as well as Haberle. Everyday objects are laid flat against a surface so that the viewer takes paint for the thing itself, a game as old as the story of Zeuxis, said to have fooled birds with painted grapes. The title names the joke: money is a token of value, and the picture a token of money.","c":"Haberle's wit is as sharp as his eye. The picture gives two laughs, the first at the money and the second when the viewer finds that the frame itself is painted canvas. The objects are scattered with an offhand balance, and paper, metal and photograph each have convincing texture. Critics sometimes dismiss trompe-l'oeil as mere trickery, but here the trick has an idea behind it. Beside Harnett's weightier arrangements, Haberle is lighter and funnier."},"met:437491":{"d":"Three devotional subjects appear in this small work in oil on wood, recorded at about 16 by 10 centimeters, painted in the Netherlands in the late fifteenth century. The title names them: Saint Michael, shown with demons; the Mass of Saint Gregory, a scene in which Christ appears; and Saint Jerome, with his lion. A work so small, with several subjects, was probably made for private prayer, perhaps as a hinged devotional piece, to be held or set close to the eye.","i":"Each subject answers a medieval worry. Saint Michael, the archangel who defeats the devil and weighs souls at the Last Judgment, offers protection at death. The Mass of Saint Gregory shows the miracle in which Christ appeared on the altar to assure a doubter of his presence in the Eucharist, and prayers before such images were believed to earn indulgences. Saint Jerome, the scholar-saint accompanied by his lion, stood for penance and learning. Together they make a compact devotional program.","c":"At this scale the painter's gift is concentrated precision: tiny figures drawn clearly, bright color and a jewel-like finish, typical of late-fifteenth-century Netherlandish devotional work. The subjects are conventional images repeated widely, so the invention lies in the handling more than in the design. The anonymous master is a minor figure beside the great Netherlandish painters, but small works of this kind are valued as direct, intimate expressions of the period's piety."},"met:11651":{"d":"A portrait of the architect John Haviland, painted in 1828 by John Neagle in oil on canvas, about 84 by 66 centimeters. Haviland is shown with a book, an emblem of learning as well as a likeness. English-born, he designed major Philadelphia buildings of the 1820s, among them the Eastern State Penitentiary. The size is that of a half-length for a professional man, with room for a prop beside the face.","i":"Architects of the early Republic were shown with books and drawings to make the point that building was a learned art, not only a trade. Haviland had published The Builder's Assistant (1818-21), a pattern book of the classical orders, and a book in his portrait may allude to it. Neagle, himself a Philadelphian, moved in the same circle of ambitious professionals. The portrait shows a man whose reputation rested on the Greek Revival taste then transforming American public buildings.","c":"Neagle's portraits are noted for forthright, solid likenesses, less glamorous than Thomas Sully's, and this one gains from a sitter whose profession invites a plain, serious presentation. The book keeps it from being a bare head. The risk with such portraits of professionals is stiffness, but honest drawing carries it. It is a good, businesslike picture of a man who helped shape American public architecture."},"nga:34129":{"d":"A clergyman, shown in the upper body, faces the viewer squarely and leans back slightly. His round face is fair, with prominent cheeks, and short curly gray hair frames it. He wears a dark coat over a waistcoat and a high white cravat tied neatly at the neck, with no jewelry or visible objects, against a warm, muted brown ground. The museum notes a signature at the left with the year 1829. John Neagle's canvas measures about 77 by 64 centimeters.","i":"The plain dark coat and white cravat are the dress of a respectable gentleman or clergyman of the 1820s, and the absence of any prop leaves the face to carry the sitter's character and calling. The Reverend in the title gives his office, but the picture shows no pulpit, Bible or other emblem. Portraits of clergymen were commissioned by congregations and families to honor a pastor and keep his likeness after his service, and here plainness suits that purpose.","c":"The direct frontal pose and warm brown ground show Neagle's solid approach: a face modeled with confidence and nothing to distract from it. The lack of any attribute or accessory is a limitation, and the picture is a respectable likeness more than a revealing one. Neagle's strength was the forthright rendering of character, and in a plain bust-length such as this that strength is nearly the whole picture."},"nga:34125":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a seated woman, facing slightly left, her head turned toward the viewer and her right hand resting on her chest. She has soft features, gentle eyes, a straight nose and slightly pursed lips, and wears a dark long-sleeved garment with a light lace shawl or collar. Her dark hair is covered by an ornately layered lace cap. The background is a simple, muted brown, perhaps wood paneling. John Neagle painted Amy Taylor Dickson, wife of John Dickson, around 1835; the canvas is about 77 by 64 centimeters.","i":"A lace cap marked a married woman of standing in the 1830s, and the fine lace collar tells of means and domestic good taste. The hand on the chest is a traditional portrait gesture often read as sincerity, modesty or devotion. With the plain brown ground, it presents a woman of quiet respectability. The entry says nothing about her life, and her identity beyond her own name and her husband's is not recorded.","c":"Neagle gives the sitter a sympathetic, unglamorous presence: the soft features and gently pursed lips, the layered lace described with care, the hand placed with ease. It is quieter than his male portraits and better described as tactful than penetrating. The weakness is the plain brown background, which gives the composition little help; the strength is the lace, handled with understated skill."},"nga:34126":{"d":"An older man with gray hair and a composed expression faces slightly to the right and looks toward the viewer through circular spectacles. He wears a dark coat and a cravat, and the dark background is unobtrusive. John Neagle's portrait of Thomas W. Dyott, painted about 1836, is an oil on canvas of about 76 by 64 centimeters, a plain half-length of a businessman with every sign of respectability and no sign of display.","i":"Dyott (1771-1861) was an English-born Philadelphia entrepreneur who made his fortune selling patent medicines and manufacturing glass bottles, and he styled himself Dr. Dyott despite uncertain medical credentials. He also founded the Manual Labor Bank to hold his workers' savings, which failed in 1837, the year after this portrait was probably made. The respectable, bespectacled businessman shown here is thus an image of credit and character soon to be tested.","c":"Neagle's honesty serves the sitter: spectacles, gray hair, plain dress and a steady look make a believable man of business, with none of the flourishes a vainer sitter might have demanded. The simplicity makes it a better portrait of a type than of a personality, and knowing the later troubles gives the quiet face an irony the painter could not have intended. It is plain, efficient and convincing."},"cma:95063":{"d":"A portrait of Martin Luther Hurlbut, painted in oil on canvas by John Neagle in 1840 and measuring about 66 by 54 centimeters, a size for a bust or head-and-shoulders likeness. No description of pose or dress is recorded here. Neagle's male portraits of these years typically show the sitter in a dark coat and white cravat against a plain, warm ground, with the face lit and firmly modeled, and that is the pattern to expect.","i":"In 1840 a painted portrait was still the standard way for an established man to record his likeness, since photography had only just been announced and was not yet in common use. Neagle, a leading Philadelphia portraitist, supplied such pictures to the professional and merchant elite of the city and beyond. The entry gives no details of Hurlbut's career or standing, so none is assumed.","c":"Neagle's reputation rests on direct, sturdy likenesses, and a bust-length like this is the plain test of that. His faces tend to be honest and unflattering, the coats a dark mass and the backgrounds simple; the qualities that count are firmness of drawing and the sense of a real person. The limit is repetition from sitter to sitter. This is a respectable, competent portrait, most useful as one of a large and varied body of work."},"met:12923":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as John Walsh, painted by John Neagle around 1840 in oil on canvas, about 91 by 74 centimeters, a size that carries a figure well below the shoulders to the waist or beyond. No description of pose or costume is recorded. Neagle's usual portrait of this decade is a half-length of a seated or standing man in dark clothes, set against a plain warm ground.","i":"Larger canvases such as this one marked sitters who could afford a more imposing portrait: a half-length with room for a hand and a bit of furniture. The Met's entry says nothing about who Walsh was, so his trade and rank remain open. What the picture preserves is the style of respectability of the early 1840s: dark clothing, a restrained expression and a firm, plain presentation.","c":"Neagle's gifts are firm drawing, honest modeling and an unflattering insistence on character, qualities that suit a plain commission like this one. He is less seductive than Sully but often more convincing: his sitters look like themselves. The risk is a certain plainness of design, since the format and the dark palette leave little room for pictorial invention. This is a solid example of his mature portraiture."},"nga:166465":{"d":"A man shown from mid-torso up turns his head slightly to the right, looking past the viewer, with pale skin, ruddy cheeks, thick gray eyebrows, deep-set eyes and thin lips. His short gray hair is swept back in slight curls. He wears a vivid red vest under a dark blue jacket, a white high-collared shirt and a loosely tied black necktie. The background is dark and muted, with hints of dark green foliage, brown tree trunks and touches of red and yellow ochre. John Neagle painted it in 1843; the canvas is about 76 by 63 centimeters.","i":"Richard Mentor Johnson (1780-1850) was a Kentucky politician, a congressman and senator, and Martin Van Buren's vice president from 1837 to 1841. He was popularly credited with killing the Shawnee leader Tecumseh at the Battle of the Thames in 1813, a claim that shaped his career. The red vest and the glimpse of woodland behind him seem to present him as a frontier figure more than a polished city politician, though how deliberate that is cannot be known.","c":"The vivid red vest is the picture's one burst of color, and Neagle sets it against dark blue and a dark ground with a showman's instinct, so that an old politician's face gets all the more attention. The averted gaze lends a pensive air, unlike the direct stares of many of his sitters. As a likeness it is frank rather than flattering, and its interest is largely historical, as an image of a controversial public figure in his sixties."},"nga:43705":{"d":"A man in military uniform is seen from the upper body, standing in a partly turned posture toward the viewer's left. His left hand rests on part of a cannon and his right on his hip. He has a dignified expression, strong features and dark brown curly hair, and his uniform has a high collar, gold buttons, ornate epaulettes and a red sash around the waist. Behind him are swirling clouds. John Neagle painted Colonel Augustus James Pleasonton in 1846; the canvas is about 92 by 74 centimeters.","i":"The cannon, epaulettes and sash are the standard emblems of an officer's portrait, and the stormy sky adds drama and suggests readiness for war; in 1846 the United States was entering war with Mexico, which may have given such a picture added topicality. Citizen-soldier colonels in the militia were prominent men for whom command carried social weight as well as duty. The title gives his rank, and the uniform tells the rest.","c":"This is Neagle at his most theatrical: the cannon, the turned stance, the hand on the hip and the swirling clouds set a stage that his usual plain bust portraits lack. The uniform's gold buttons and sash add welcome color. The staging is conventional and a little stiff, a long-standing formula of military portraiture, but it gives the likeness a confident public presence. It is showier and more formal than most of his portraits."},"nga:43675":{"d":"A man is shown from the upper body, seated and facing to his left with a direct gaze and a slight smile. He has fair skin, a slender face with a prominent forehead and long nose, and dark curly hair receding at the temples. He wears a black coat, white shirt and black necktie, and there are no visible accessories against the dark, muted brown ground. John Neagle painted George Dodd in 1852; the canvas is about 76 by 64 centimeters, matching the size of the portrait of Julia Dodd.","i":"The plain black coat and necktie were the uniform of the middle-class man of business in the early 1850s, and the absence of accessories keeps attention on the face. The slight smile, less common in formal portraits of this date, gives him an approachable air. Paired with his wife's portrait, it records a household at a moment of settled prosperity. The entry does not say what he did for a living.","c":"Neagle's practical strengths, a sound head and a well-judged turn of the body, are on show here. The slight smile and receding hairline make the likeness more individual than many, and the black-on-brown palette is subdued but unified. What it lacks is any sign of the man's occupation or interests, which a prop would have offered. It is honest, quiet portraiture of the sort that makes up the bulk of the artist's output."},"nga:43676":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated in three-quarter view and facing slightly to her right, with high cheekbones, a straight nose and brown eyes. Her hair is parted in the middle and smoothed tightly back to show her ears. She wears a dark dress with a high collar and white lace trim, a large bow with light and dark blue stripes fastened with a brooch, and gold hoop earrings. The chair is upholstered in rich red with ornate carved wood, against a muted dark ground. John Neagle painted Julia Dodd in 1852.","i":"Her middle-parted, smooth hair and dark, high-collared dress are in the sober fashion of the early 1850s, and the blue striped bow, brooch and gold earrings are the few notes of ornament that a respectable matron might allow herself. The carved red chair signals comfort and means. The title records only that she was Mrs. George Dodd, and the portrait matches her husband's in size and date, so the two are probably pendants.","c":"The red chair is the livelier half of the picture: Neagle gives the sitter a plain, controlled face, and the colorful accents of the bow, the lace and the carved chair keep the canvas from monotony. As often with his female portraits, the effect is tactful rather than penetrating. It is a pleasant, careful picture that gains from having its pendant at hand."},"met:10068":{"d":"A large oil portrait, about 127 by 102 centimeters, of the poet Walt Whitman, painted in 1889 by John White Alexander when Whitman was about seventy. The picture presents the bearded good gray poet, as admirers called him, in his last years in Camden, New Jersey. The generous canvas suggests an honoring, almost commemorative portrait of a national literary figure rather than a private likeness. No further detail of pose or setting is recorded here.","i":"Whitman, author of Leaves of Grass, first published in 1855, was by 1889 widely seen as the great poet of American democracy, and admirers and artists sought him out in Camden, where he lived in his last years. A large portrait by a younger painter honored him as a national figure. Alexander, then in his early thirties, belonged to a generation that admired Whitman for breaking with polite convention, as his long beard and open manner did in appearance.","c":"Alexander was a tonal painter whose work often shows the influence of Whistler, and a portrait of an old man in a limited palette suits his strengths. Whitman appears as a venerable sage, the white beard a focus of light. The quality here is sympathy and sobriety; the limit is a reverence that can flatten a great, unruly personality into an icon. It is more valuable as an image of the poet's last years than as a daring painting."},"met:10065":{"d":"A young woman lies stretched along a long sofa, seen from the side, her body running in one great curve from her bent head at the upper right to the sweep of her gown at the left. The gown is cream-white and falls in broad folds, crossed by black ribbons that trail down its length. Her dark hair is loose and her face, small and half hidden by her arm, appears closed and sleepy. She rests against deep rose-red cushions, and the sofa behind her is a dark purple-brown with a gold medallion at the left. The upper edge has a band of dull gold. The paint is thin and rubbed down, so the weave of the canvas shows through.","i":"Painted in Paris in 1895, Repose shows the influence of Whistler, whose nocturnes and symphonies stressed design, abstract form and mood. Alexander adopted Whistler's way of thinning his paint with oil and rubbing it down, so that the weave of the canvas becomes part of the effect. The sinuous curves, rich colouring and tautly stretched fabric link the picture to the taste of the 1890s, and the black stripes of the dress echo the long bow of the sofa back. Alexander was an American from Pittsburgh who spent the 1890s in Paris before returning home, where he became a leading figure in the National Academy of Design.","c":"The composition is bold, and the main idea, a body that becomes a single line, is carried through to the last fold. The tone is dreamy rather than sentimental, and the thin, rubbed paint gives a velvety atmosphere. But the design has swallowed the person: the face is nearly lost, the woman has no character beyond languor, and the gown reads as a shape rather than as cloth. This is a decorative picture of high skill rather than deep feeling, and it owes more to the fashion for Whistler than to Alexander's own voice."},"met:435711":{"d":"A peasant woman and her child fill a tall, upright canvas nearly 1.9 meters high, a scale that suggests a picture meant for public exhibition. The title and the museum's keywords, women, children and Orientalist, place the scene in Egypt, and the date of 1869-70 follows Léon Bonnat's travels in Egypt and the Near East in the late 1860s. The catalogue does not describe the pose, dress or setting, so none is invented here; the subject is a type, the Egyptian village mother, painted as a monumental figure group rather than as a portrait.","i":"French painters went to Egypt for its light, its ruins and a rural life that seemed to continue unchanged from antiquity, and Orientalist pictures found ready buyers at the Paris Salon. A mother with a child inevitably recalls the Madonna and Child, and Bonnat, who also painted religious subjects, may have known it; that is a reading, not a documented intention. The picture treats its sitters as types and names no individual, which was the usual practice in Orientalist figure painting.","c":"Bonnat's reputation rests on weight: solid, sculptural figures, strong contrasts of dark and light and a plain, frank touch learned from Ribera and Velázquez during his Madrid youth. A peasant mother and child is a good vehicle for that manner, and the subject escapes the harem fantasies many Orientalists sold. The limits are heaviness and a brown, studio-lit tonality. Beside Gérôme's glossier Egypt it is plainer and more sober, if less seductive."},"met:435708":{"d":"A girl in the costume of the Ciociaria, the hill country southeast of Rome, drinks at a fountain in a tall canvas of about 170 by 100 centimeters. The catalogue records water, a fountain and the act of drinking, and nothing more of her pose or the setting. Léon Bonnat painted it in 1875, some fifteen years after the time he spent in Rome as a young man. The height suggests a figure at or near life size, a scale for exhibition rather than for a private room.","i":"Rome and the hill villages around it gave French artists models in picturesque local dress, and the Ciociara costume was prized as the look of authentic peasant life. A girl at a fountain also belongs to an old tradition of wells and water-bearers, which stretches back to the Bible and makes the scene feel timeless. Such pictures were bought by collectors who wanted a Mediterranean Italy of simple, unchanging people; the girl is a type rather than a named sitter.","c":"Bonnat models figures with sculptural weight, and a single girl at this scale gives that habit full play. He was more a draftsman than a colorist, working in browns and warm shadows, and the danger of so pretty a subject is that the girl hardens into a type, solid and a little stiff. It is a sober, capable exhibition picture, closer in spirit to his portraits than to the sunlit Italy painted by many of his contemporaries."},"met:435709":{"d":"A portrait of John Taylor Johnston (1820-1893), painted by Léon Bonnat in 1880 in oil on canvas, about 133 by 112 centimeters, large enough to show much of the figure and not just the head. The museum's credit line, Gift of the Trustees, 1880, shows that its trustees presented the picture to the institution in the year it was painted. Johnston was the Metropolitan Museum's first president, and the portrait honors him in that role. The record does not describe his pose or dress.","i":"Johnston was a New York railroad president and collector, and he led the Metropolitan Museum from its founding in 1870 until 1889. A portrait presented by the trustees was a tribute to a founder and a statement of the young institution's ambitions, following the old convention of placing a benefactor's likeness in the house he built. That the painter was Bonnat, one of the most sought-after portraitists in Paris, suggests that the trustees wanted a likeness of international standing.","c":"Bonnat's portraits of the late 1870s follow a recipe: a dark ground, a strongly lit head, sober black dress and a blunt truthfulness that flatters by seriousness. For a founding president it is the right register, authority without display. The limit is sameness; like most of his society portraits it delivers a convincing face more than a revealing one. Its main value is as a record of the museum's founder, painted by a leading hand."},"nga:213413":{"d":"A seated three-quarter-length portrait of Henry White, about 147 by 116 centimetres, painted in 1882 by the Paris portraitist Léon Bonnat. Bonnat's portraits of men typically show the sitter in plain, dark clothes against a dark ground, lit so that the face and hands stand out, with firm drawing and a serious, sober air. A seated portrait of this size was a mark of serious social standing, and it presents White as a man of weight.","i":"Henry White (1850–1927) became one of the most prominent American diplomats of his generation, serving as ambassador to Italy and to France and as a delegate to the Paris Peace Conference in 1919. Léon Bonnat was the favoured portraitist of the Parisian elite in the 1880s, and foreign visitors to Paris sat to him as a sign of arrival. The picture is a record of a rising man at the age of about thirty-two, painted by the most fashionable portraitist in the city.","c":"Bonnat was the portraitist of choice for the Paris establishment, and his sober tone, firm drawing and Spanish-inspired chiaroscuro lend sitters authority. The weakness is heaviness: his portraits can seem dark and ponderous, with dignity but little individual spark. Beside the brilliance of John Singer Sargent, who was rising in Paris in the 1880s, such sober portraiture began to look old-fashioned. As a solid, serious likeness of a diplomat, however, it does its job."},"met:436034":{"d":"A crowd of half-length figures presses into a small horizontal panel, only about 16 by 22 centimeters, around Christ and the woman accused of adultery. The record lists soldiers, men, women and armor, so the accusers and their guard are in the picture. It is painted in oil on beech wood and was made around 1545-50 by Lucas Cranach the Younger and his workshop in Wittenberg, in the compact, close-packed format that the Cranach shop repeated many times for this and other Gospel subjects.","i":"The story is from the Gospel of John: scribes and Pharisees bring a woman caught in adultery to Christ, hoping to trap him, and he answers that whoever is without sin should cast the first stone. Cranach's Wittenberg shop, working for Lutheran patrons, treated it as a picture of mercy over judgment and grace over the letter of the law. That reading is conventional for such panels and is not documented for this one.","c":"Cranach's workshop turned out compositions like this with great efficiency, and the efficiency shows: a stock arrangement of crowded heads, firm outlines and bright, enamel-like color, repeated with small changes. What succeeds is the pressure of faces in a tight space, dramatically right for a trial, and the clean craftsmanship of a small panel. What is missing is the sharper invention of the elder Cranach at his best; this is a handsome, competent workshop product."},"met:436035":{"d":"Christ is shown with women, infants and men in a small horizontal panel of about 17 by 22 centimeters, painted in oil on beech wood. The record lists a woman, a man, an infant and Christ, and the title gives the Gospel moment: the little children brought to him for a blessing. Lucas Cranach the Younger and his workshop made it around 1545-50 in Wittenberg, in the half-length, close-packed format the shop used for many such scenes.","i":"In the Gospels of Matthew, Mark and Luke, parents bring children to Jesus, the disciples try to turn them away, and he tells them to let the little children come to him. In Lutheran Germany the subject also served in the dispute over infant baptism, which Anabaptists rejected, because it showed Christ welcoming those too young to declare their faith. Small panels like this were made for private houses; the reading is traditional, not documented for this one.","c":"The subject suits Cranach's gifts, since a tender theme needs only a few gestures and a group of rounded, appealing faces. The surface is smooth and bright, the arrangement clear and the sentiment readable at a glance. It is workshop work repeated from a pattern, so the pleasures are charm and clarity rather than the sharper characterization of the best panels by the elder Cranach. As a small devotional picture of the Reformation it does its job gracefully."},"nga:1099":{"d":"A woman in eighteenth-century dress is the subject of this portrait on a canvas of about 92 by 71 centimeters, which is 36 by 28 inches, the standard size known as the kit-cat. Joseph Highmore painted it around 1730-35, when he was established in London as a portraitist. The catalogue gives no name for the sitter and no description of dress or setting, so none is supplied here. The kit-cat format shows head, bust and usually the hands, which leaves the face and costume as the substance of the picture.","i":"The kit-cat takes its name from the portraits Godfrey Kneller painted for members of the Kit-Cat Club, and it became the usual size for a sitter who wanted a dignified likeness at moderate cost, between a bust and a full-length. A woman of the 1730s would choose it to show her face, her silks and perhaps a hand with a flower or a piece of jewelry. Her name has not come down with the picture, so her rank is unknown.","c":"Highmore's best portraits stand out from the stiff Kneller formula by a natural ease and a light, clear touch. In a kit-cat the difference between formula and life lies in the glance and the hands, and the strength of Highmore at his best is that the sitter looks relaxed in her own skin. Without more evidence, this unidentified lady of about 1730-35 may be read as a pleasant, competent example of that quiet gift."},"nga:46174":{"d":"Stone ledges of different heights step across a dark ground, lit from the left. On the highest, a woven basket holds plum-dark fruit, a golden loaf in a white wrapper and glistening preserved fruit, with a braided white ring propped in front. Below, a pewter dish carries figs and sugared pastries, some round, some S-shaped, beside terracotta vessels and a lidded wooden box. Behind them stand a carafe and glass jars of red preserves sealed with twists of paper, and a tall red clay vessel. The oil, about 85 by 113 centimeters, is signed and dated 1627.","i":"Juan van der Hamen, a Madrid painter of Flemish descent whose clients included the royal court, specialized in these stepped displays of sweets, preserves and fine tableware. Sugar confections were costly and associated with feasts, so the pastries, jars and clay vessels announce luxury and abundance, and the dark ground isolates each object like a piece in a collector's cabinet. A moral about worldly pleasure is possible, but the record does not state one.","c":"Van der Hamen's genius is order. Ledges at different heights give the objects stage and spacing, and the raking light from the left carves each vessel with cool clarity. The sealed glass jars and sugar-dusted pastries are rendered with relish, yet the whole stays still and a little severe, closer to architecture than to appetite. It is among the most refined Spanish still lifes of the 1620s, a picture to be studied rather than devoured."},"met:439333":{"d":"A lone wanderer, a tree and a storm are the recorded elements of this small oil on canvas, about 42 by 57 centimeters, painted in 1835 by the German artist Julius von Leypold. The catalogue lists only those three elements, so the figure's position, the shape of the tree and the colors of the sky are not described here. The size and subject point to a cabinet picture for a private buyer, in the Romantic tradition of setting one human figure against the forces of nature.","i":"The solitary traveler in bad weather was a favorite figure of German Romanticism, standing for the individual exposed to nature's power and to fate, as in the songs and poems of wandering that were popular in the 1820s and 1830s, Schubert's Winterreise among them. A tree in a storm can suggest endurance or its opposite. Such readings are traditional for the type and are not documented for this canvas.","c":"Julius von Leypold is far less known than Friedrich or Dahl, and a storm picture at this size is judged by its sky and its sense of struggle. The Romantic theme is a good one, and the single wanderer gives the viewer a point of identification. Beyond that, the weather and the paint handling decide whether a canvas of this kind persuades or merely poses. It is best taken as a lesser-known German voice in a famous genre."},"met:436656":{"d":"A wedding ceremony fills a tall canvas of about 128 by 103 centimeters: men and women in their best clothes stand in an interior around the couple. Painted by William Hogarth in 1729, it records a particular marriage, and the title names the pair as Stephen Beckingham and Mary Cox. The record lists an interior, a wedding, men and women, and gives no further description, so the number of figures and the details of the room are not given here. It is a group portrait in the manner of the conversation piece, a type Hogarth was then making.","i":"A wedding portrait of the early eighteenth century recorded more than a happy day: marriage joined two families' property and prospects, and the couple's rank, the witnesses and the clergyman who joined them were part of the document. Hogarth himself married Jane Thornhill in 1729, and a commission like this was probably a step toward a career as a portraitist. The crowd of onlookers also let him show his interest in manners and gesture, which later became his subject.","c":"Hogarth's conversation pieces succeed by making a group of sitters feel like people who happen to share a room, and a wedding is a hard test, since a ceremony needs a hierarchy of poses and every face must be a likeness. At this date he was still learning to manage a crowd, and stiffness is the risk. What remains is the keen eye for dress, manner and position that marks him from the start, and which led to his satires."},"nga:61392":{"d":"About two dozen people crowd a stone prison room with barred windows and chains on the wall, framed by a gold-edged blue stage curtain. At the center a man in a coral-red coat and tricorn stands with arms crossed, his ankles in irons. To the left a woman in an aquamarine hooped gown kneels, handkerchief in hand, before an older, portly man with keys at his wrist; at the right a woman in white satin kneels before a man in black with a sword. Onlookers gather behind a low wall. The oil is about 51 by 61 centimeters.","i":"The scene is the prison finale of John Gay's The Beggar's Opera, which opened in London in January 1728 and was a runaway success. The highwayman Macheath, in Newgate, is pleaded for by the two women who each claim him, who kneel to their fathers. Gay's ballad opera was widely read as a satire on Walpole's government, with thieves and jailers as mirrors of politicians. Hogarth painted the moment several times; the onlookers may recall spectators who sat at the sides of the stage.","c":"Hogarth was about thirty and new to painting, and this small canvas shows how quickly he grasped theater as a way of telling a story. The gestures are legible, the kneeling women and the arms-crossed hero make a clear triangle, and the cold stone sets off the costumes. The paint is fluent rather than finished and the figures are types more than portraits. It is a lively early picture, important as a start toward his narrative art."},"met:436768":{"d":"A portrait of a woman, dated 1817, by the French artist Nicolas Henri Jacob, made on card, a thin board used for studies and for small, finished likenesses. The catalogue gives only the support, so the technique, the dimensions, the sitter's name and her dress and pose are not stated here. Jacob (1782-1871) belonged to the generation of French artists who came of age under Napoleon, and a portrait of 1817 falls in the early Restoration, after the Bourbons returned to the throne.","i":"In the years after Napoleon's fall, small portraits of this plain type were made for families and friends rather than for Salon walls: a woman's likeness on card was a private memento, easy to keep in a drawer or an album. The record does not describe her dress or hair and gives no name, so her rank and relationship to the artist cannot be read. Its meaning lies in the keepsake function, a face kept for those who knew her.","c":"With only a date and a support to go on, the picture can be judged only as a type: a modest private portrait of the Restoration, where success rests on a clean outline, a clear likeness and restrained color. Jacob is a minor name beside Ingres, whose portrait drawings of the same years set the standard for small, exact likenesses. As a period object it is likable; judging the hand itself requires seeing the face and line."},"met:435640":{"d":"The Virgin adores her son, who lies asleep, in a vertical panel of about 72 by 46 centimeters. Giovanni Bellini painted it in tempera on wood in the early 1460s, when he was about thirty and working in Venice. The sleeping Christ Child, lying at rest, is the picture's quiet center. The catalogue records no more of the setting, but Bellini's Madonnas of these years often place the figures behind a parapet, with a distant landscape beyond.","i":"A sleeping Christ Child recalls the dead Christ: the infant at rest is read as a foreshadowing of the Passion, his sleep a rehearsal of the sleep of death. Bellini painted the type several times in these years, for private devotion in a Venetian home, where the owner would pray before it and think of Christ's sacrifice. The Virgin's adoration invites the viewer to join her. This reading is traditional for such pictures and is not documented for this panel.","c":"Even at thirty, Bellini works with great sensitivity. The modeling is firm and clear, under the influence of his brother-in-law Mantegna, but the feeling is softer, and the stillness of mother and sleeping child is entirely his own. What he has not yet found is the atmospheric light of his later Madonnas, and the tempera surface is cooler and harder. It is an important early panel, interesting for showing where his mature style began."},"nga:36":{"d":"A clean-shaven young man is shown from the chest up, his body angled to the viewer's left and his dark eyes under thick, arched brows looking off in the same direction. A black headdress with two long tails covers his chestnut hair, one tail over his right shoulder and the other hanging down his back. He wears a brick-red garment with vertical pleats from a high collar, fastened by a small clasp at the throat, with a white undergarment showing at the neck. Behind him, tan clouds drift across a blue sky.","i":"The sitter's name is not known. The picture shows how Venetian portraiture changed in the 1470s after Antonello da Messina visited the city: the close-up bust and the three-quarter view owe much to Antonello's example, but Bellini replaced his sharp definition with softer light and the open Venetian sky. The headdress and red robe are the fashionable dress of the day. A portrait like this recorded a young man's likeness for family or friends and gave him a quiet dignity.","c":"One of the most affecting early Venetian portraits. The head is modelled with a soft, almost breathing transition from light to shade, the large eyes seem to look beyond the viewer, and the plain red garment and the sky are all the setting it needs. The restraint is the point: nothing distracts from the face. If it has a limit, it is that the sitter's thoughts remain private; Bellini offers calm and presence rather than a story."},"nga:33254":{"d":"A vertical panel, about 72 by 53 centimetres, shows the Virgin and Child with a landscape behind them. In Bellini's Madonnas of the early 1480s the figures are close to the viewer and the open country beyond is rendered with the same loving care: hills, trees, towns and a luminous sky, often recalling the Veneto. The landscape is not only a backdrop but part of the mood, and the soft, even light unites it with the figures.","i":"Landscape in Bellini's Madonnas does more than decorate: the cultivated countryside can suggest the world that the Incarnation redeems, and the clear light and open sky bring calm. Such pictures were made for private devotion in Venetian houses, and their landscapes appealed to a society that was taking increasing pleasure in the countryside of the mainland. Bellini helped create the Venetian love of light, air and colour that would flow on into Giorgione and Titian.","c":"Bellini's strength is the union of figure and landscape in a single warm light, which makes the Virgin and the countryside seem to breathe the same air. The gentle recession, the soft sky and the Virgin's quiet face are typical of his best work. The risk lies in sweetness, but his reserve prevents it. This is characteristic Bellini rather than a surprise, and for that reason an excellent introduction to what the Venetian Renaissance admired."},"nga:496":{"d":"Mary and her infant son, shown close up on a small panel of about 52 by 42 centimetres, painted in oil. Bellini's half-length Madonnas of the early 1480s typically hold the figures near the picture plane, behind a parapet or against a cloth or sky, so that the viewer is brought into direct, intimate contact with them. The mother's calm face and the Child's pose are the picture's subject, and there is no narrative beyond that devotional presence.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the commonest devotional subject in Venice, and Bellini was the city's master of it. Pictures like this hung in private houses for prayer and for the pleasure of looking. Mary's grave tenderness is often read as foreknowledge of the Passion, her love tempered by sorrow, and the Child's presence recalls the Incarnation. Bellini repeated the formula many times, and each time varied the mood, the pose and the light.","c":"Bellini's genius lay in finding endless variety inside a fixed formula, and the Madonnas of the early 1480s have a quiet gravity and softness that his Venetian rivals could not match. The oil medium, which he handled with growing mastery, allows the gradual shading of flesh and the glow of colour. If the format becomes familiar, the feeling does not; the best of these pictures seem to share a hushed inwardness with the viewer."},"met:435641":{"d":"The Virgin, shown to below the waist behind a marble parapet, holds the naked Christ Child upright on her lap against a hanging of deep red cloth. She wears a blue mantle edged in gold over an orange-red dress and a white veil, and her oval face turns to look out with a calm, slightly tilted gaze. In her right hand she holds a small round fruit. The Child, with curly golden hair, looks up at her and reaches toward her face. At the left a gap beside the hanging reveals a landscape: a hill town with towers, bare trees and distant blue mountains under a pale sky with a bank of cloud. The parapet carries the signature IOANNES BELLINUS.","i":"Giovanni Bellini (about 1430–1516) was the leading painter of Venice, brother-in-law of Mantegna and teacher of Giorgione and Titian. Half-length Madonnas, made for private devotion in Venetian homes, were the staple of his workshop; he varied the formula of Virgin, Child, cloth of honour and landscape, and signed this one on the parapet. The Met dates it to the late 1480s. The fruit in Mary's hand may be an apple, recalling the Fall of Adam and Eve and Christ's role as redeemer, though the identification is uncertain. The Child's turn toward his mother stresses her tenderness, in the warm, human style that Venetian buyers preferred.","c":"Bellini produced an immense number of Madonnas, and this is one of the more serene. The fall of the blue mantle, the oval of the face and the Child's upward glance create a simple geometry, and the red hanging is painted with a depth that oil allowed him. The landscape, bathed in clear light, is delightful. What is lacking is the intense tenderness of his best versions: the Virgin's expression is reserved, and the Child, sweet but slightly stiff, is more careful than inspired. A very good example of his repeated type, but not a surprising one."},"nga:323":{"d":"A small bust-length portrait of a young man, about 30 by 23 centimetres, painted in oil on a poplar panel around 1490. Bellini's portraits of these years usually show the sitter close up, turned slightly, against a plain ground or sky, with the face lit by a soft, even light and the clothing kept simple so that nothing competes with the likeness. The small size suggests a picture to be kept in a private room or given to a friend.","i":"The sitter's name is unknown, and the title calls him only a young man. In Venice, portraits were commissioned by citizens and patricians as records of family and personal pride, and Bellini, under the influence of Antonello da Messina, made the close, three-quarter view in oil a Venetian habit. A portrait like this was not a statement of office but a quiet record of an individual, and its dignity lies in its restraint.","c":"Bellini's portraits are marked by economy: no props, almost no ornament, and a soft modelling that makes the face seem alive. A portrait of a youth is a test of that gift, since youth offers few lines to record character; the success lies in the glance and the structure of the head. This is a small picture, not a grand one, but it shows why the Venetian approach to portraits, quieter and warmer than Florentine linearity, endured."},"nga:395":{"d":"Seen from the shoulders up, a clean-shaven man with shoulder-length wavy brown hair and bangs looks off to the viewer's left with brown eyes under thin brows. He wears a brimless black cap that nearly touches the top edge and a high-necked black garment lined with white. Behind him two bands of white cloud cross an azure sky. A stone ledge across the bottom is painted as if carved with the artist's name, IOANNES BELLINVS.","i":"Black was the usual dress of Venetian men of the governing class, and the plain cap and gown suggest a citizen or patrician of the Republic, though the sitter is not identified. The painted ledge, carved with Bellini's name, was a Venetian convention that served as both parapet and signature; here it announces that the leading painter of Venice made the picture. Around 1500 Bellini, then about seventy, painted several such portraits of Venetian men, among them the doge Leonardo Loredan.","c":"A small, severe and compelling portrait. The head is modelled with Bellini's soft light, and the black cap and gown are broad, simple masses against the pale sky. The restraint is characteristic of Venetian portraiture, where dignity matters more than display. The face, calm and a little remote, gives away little, and the painting does not probe character as Northern portraits of the same years do, but its clarity and balance are exemplary."},"nga:358":{"d":"Saint Jerome is shown absorbed in a book, as Bellini repeatedly painted the saint, a scholar-hermit at home in nature. The panel is small, about 47 by 37.5 centimetres, painted in oil on linden wood in 1505, and it is the kind of picture made for a private room, where a learned owner could look at it while reading. In Bellini's pictures of Jerome the saint is typically set in a rocky landscape, his study and his wilderness joined under a soft, even light.","i":"Jerome translated the Bible into Latin, lived as a hermit in the Syrian desert and became the patron of scholars. Venetian humanists loved him, and in an age when Venetian printers such as Aldus Manutius were publishing the classics, the saint at his book resonated with the city's own learned culture. The reading saint also stands for withdrawal from worldly vanity, so the picture is both a portrait of scholarship and an image of penitent solitude.","c":"A late Bellini of great calm. The old man reading is rendered with sympathy and without drama, and the light and landscape share in his quiet. Bellini, then about seventy-five, painted with an ease that younger artists lacked. If there is a limit, it is that the scale is small and the mood contemplative rather than moving; but contemplation is the point, and few artists have made a person absorbed in thought so convincing."},"nga:41589":{"d":"A long, narrow canvas, about 75 centimetres high and 3.6 metres wide, whose frieze-like proportions suggest that it was made to be set into the wall of a room rather than hung as an easel picture. It shows an episode from the life of the Roman general Publius Cornelius Scipio, a story drawn from ancient historians. The date, after 1506, belongs to Bellini's last decade, when the old master took on secular and classical subjects as well as the devotional pictures that made his name.","i":"In Renaissance Venice, humanist patrons wanted scenes from ancient history to decorate their houses as models of virtue, and Scipio was praised for courage, generosity and self-command. Such a series reflected the city's pride in a Roman-style republic. The long format fits a decorative scheme for a room, a use that would have made the pictures part of the owner's display of learning. The painting is usually read as an exemplum, a lesson in virtue taken from ancient history.","c":"A rarity in Bellini's work, which was devoted overwhelmingly to sacred subjects, and so of interest as a sign of the new classical taste reaching even the oldest master in Venice. The long format asks a painter trained on compact panels to organise a frieze, a different problem from a Madonna, and the design must work episode by episode as well as a whole. Its distinction lies in that rarity as much as in any single passage."},"nga:46104":{"d":"A small panel, about 50 by 39 centimetres, shows the infant Bacchus, the Roman god of wine, as a baby; the vine and the grape are his usual attributes. It was painted in oil probably between 1505 and 1510, and later transferred from its original wood support to a new panel. The subject is unusual in Bellini's work: a pagan child god painted with the same tenderness that he gave the Christ Child in his Madonnas.","i":"Bacchus, son of Jupiter and the mortal Semele, was born from his father's thigh and raised by nymphs, and as a god of wine and ecstasy he was a favourite of humanist patrons. A picture of the child god was a learned and playful ornament for a private room, and it echoes the classical interests that were spreading in Venice in Bellini's last years, when he also painted The Feast of the Gods for Alfonso d'Este.","c":"Bellini's skill with children, learned in his Madonnas, transfers naturally to the infant god, and a baby's soft anatomy suits his delicate modelling. The picture is a charming oddity: it lacks the exuberance of the later Venetian Bacchic pictures by Titian, and the child is more gentle than divine. Its importance is as evidence of the old master's alertness to new taste. It is a gentle pagan idyll, not a boisterous one."},"met:435642":{"d":"The Virgin and Child appear with saints in a wide horizontal panel of about 97 by 154 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil on wood around 1505-10. The record lists Mary, the Christ Child, a man, a woman and saints, and does not name them. The grouping belongs to the sacra conversazione, the type of picture in which the Madonna and holy figures share one calm space without a narrative event, a form Giovanni Bellini did much to make the standard of Venetian altarpieces and private pictures.","i":"In a sacra conversazione the saints are not witnesses to an event but companions, standing with Mary in a timeless present, and a patron could be placed in the company of his own protectors. The saints chosen usually reflect the buyer's names or devotions, so identifying them matters; the record here does not do so. The mixed technique of tempera and oil shows Bellini, in his seventies, using oil glazes for the glowing color that Venetians prized.","c":"Late Bellini is the Bellini of light: soft, warm color, calm figures and a gravity that needs no gesture. The wide format gives the group room, and the picture's effect depends on quiet balance rather than incident. Its limitation is shared by the type, an evenness of mood that can seem remote beside narrative painting. As an example of the Venetian ideal of serene, unforced devotion it stands high."},"nga:1138":{"d":"On a green clearing at the edge of a wood, seventeen figures sit and stand in a long, loose row, drinking and talking. Gods and woodland creatures mingle: a goat-legged satyr at the left balances a jug above his head, a bearded man in orange stands beside a gray donkey, a boy in a wreath and a blue tunic sits at the front, and at the center a helmeted man in a green cloak and blue boots raises a cup. Most of the women have robes slipping off a shoulder. At the right a woman sleeps with one breast bare while a rustic figure bends toward her. Vessels, a bowl of fruit, and a tub with a signed paper lie on the ground, and a rocky cliff and a tall grove rise behind.","i":"The scene illustrates Ovid's Fasti: at a banquet for Bacchus the gods carouse, and the lustful Priapus creeps toward the sleeping nymph Lotis, only to be foiled when Silenus's donkey brays and wakes her. Alfonso d'Este, Duke of Ferrara, commissioned the picture for his private study, the camerino d'alabastro; Bellini signed and dated it 1514, in his eighties. After Bellini's death in 1516 the landscape was repainted, first by Dosso Dossi and then more thoroughly by Titian around 1529, though the figures remain Bellini's. The gods are treated as ordinary people at a picnic, a casual approach that set the tone for the cycle Titian then completed.","c":"Bellini's last major picture shows how a painter formed in sacred art handles pagan comedy: with gentleness and a little puzzlement. The figures are beautifully painted and the color is lovely, but the gods barely interact, and the humor of Ovid's story is nearly lost; it reads as a frieze of fine individuals more than a drama. Titian's later overpainting leaves a visible seam between two styles in the landscape. Even so, the warm, relaxed treatment of myth gave the Ferrara cycle its tone, and the picture remains one of the finest Renaissance paintings in America."},"nga:60817":{"d":"A large ship, sails set and blue flags and red banners flying, sails to the right across the center of a wide canvas, one flag lettered FAVORITE. Small figures line her deck and rowboats float alongside; two more large ships stand to left and right, with smaller craft between, under a low horizon and a wide, cloudy blue sky. A distant shore of hills, trees and a line of small buildings closes the view over pale green water with soft white waves. Robert Salmon painted it in 1819; the oil is about 76 by 128 centimeters.","i":"Greenock, on the Firth of Clyde, was one of Scotland's busiest ports, and Salmon, born in England, worked in Greenock and Liverpool before crossing the Atlantic to Boston. Pictures of a vessel under sail, with her name on her flag and her crew on deck, served as portraits of ships for their owners and masters, who took pride in a particular hull and in the trade she carried. The record says nothing more about the Favorite, so her owners and cargo are not stated.","c":"Salmon's ship pictures are prized for their clean, bright light and crisp drawing, and the low horizon here hands the cloudy sky half the canvas. The eye moves from the central ship to the boats and the distant shore through precise, almost miniature description. Compared with the stagey drama of many marine painters, Salmon is calm and exact; the limit is a certain stillness in the figures and water. It is a handsome record of Clyde shipping."},"met:12024":{"d":"A harbor view in oil on wood, about 42 by 65 centimeters, painted by Robert Salmon in 1828. The title names the Custom House at Greenock, the building on the Firth of Clyde where duties on cargoes were assessed, and the record lists ships, men, a building and a storm. How the storm, the men and the vessels are placed around the building is not described in the record. A small panel like this would have been painted for sale to a merchant, shipowner or traveler, who valued an accurate view of a familiar port.","i":"Greenock grew in the early nineteenth century from a fishing village into a leading Scottish port, importing sugar and timber and building ships, and its Custom House was the symbol of that commerce. A view combining the building, shipping and weather joined civic pride to the maritime picture. Salmon seems to have left Britain for Boston around 1828, so this may be among his last Greenock pictures, though the record does not say.","c":"A bright harbor day is Salmon's usual weather, so a storm is a variation, and the measure here is how he handles stormy light on water and stone. His strengths are architectural exactness and a clear sense of space, and the Custom House gives him a solid anchor for his small, precise figures. The limit is the cool objectivity he brings to every subject; a storm threatens politely. As a record of a Scottish port it is attractive and informative."},"nga:177986":{"d":"A seascape of sailing ships under a low horizon and a cloudy gray sky. At the left a small boat with its sails furled carries several people; in the center a larger ship under full sail flies an American flag; at the right the tallest ship, sails billowing, carries a red-and-white flag at her masthead. More boats lie in the distance, each with faint figures on deck, and a shore of buildings and trees runs along the left. The palette is warm and muted, browns, creams and grays above gray-green water. Robert Salmon's oil on wood, about 42 by 62 centimeters, is dated 1843.","i":"Salmon had made Boston his base after leaving Britain, and its harbor, crowded with merchantmen, coasters and small craft, was a subject that suited him. A harbor scene like this portrayed commerce as much as place: flags show nationality, the tall ship stands for overseas trade and the small boats for the daily life of the port. The record gives no names for the ships, so none is assumed.","c":"The strengths are the low horizon, which gives the clouds room, and the way the three ships step across the picture at different scales, suggesting depth without effort. The warm, muted palette lends a mellow, slightly hazy calm. What it lacks is event: nothing happens, and the picture works as a quiet record of a busy port rather than a drama. Salmon's harbor views are admired for exactly that equanimity, and this late example keeps it."},"nga:165887":{"d":"A young boy in a burgundy suit and floppy hat stands facing the viewer with a gray terrier at his side, in a vertical portrait about 148 by 110 centimeters; the sheen of the suit suggests velvet. He has large slate-blue eyes, rounded cheeks, closed bow lips and thick blond hair waving to his shoulders, and holds a riding crop in both hands. Lace edges his wide collar, cuffs and knee-length pants, and a ruby sash circles his waist. The dog sits looking off to the right on a brick-red carpet before an olive-green drape; the brushwork is loose in the rug, background and dog.","i":"Eddy was the son of Mary's brother Alexander, later president of the Pennsylvania Railroad, and his dress, a velvet suit with lace collar and cuffs, derives from the aristocratic costume of Van Dyck's portraits and from the fancy dress that children of rich families wore. The pose with a dog and riding crop recalls Velázquez's portraits of Spanish princes, which Cassatt had studied in Madrid in 1872. The inscription, Eddy from Aunt Mary, shows that it was a family gift.","c":"Cassatt's early portrait shows her debt to the old masters, especially Velázquez, in the full-length stance and the dark, quiet ground, while the loose brushwork, particularly in the rug and the dog, announces the freedom she would find with Degas and the Impressionists. The boy's frank, slightly wary gaze is the picture's strength: she does not flatter him into a doll. A sound, affectionate work that predicts her later gift for children."},"nga:61368":{"d":"A young girl slumps in a deep armchair upholstered in turquoise-blue fabric patterned with loose green, pink, and red strokes. She wears a white dress with a plaid sash, plaid stockings, and black buckled shoes; one arm is flung behind her head, the other elbow is draped over the armrest, and her legs sprawl apart. She gazes off to the left, her mouth set in a flat line. Across from her, in a matching chair, a small shaggy dog sleeps curled up. Behind them more blue chairs and a settee stand in an otherwise bare room; four windows on the right flood the back wall with silvery light. The floor is a flat brown, the viewpoint high, and the edges crop the furniture.","i":"Cassatt painted this in Paris in 1878. The model was the daughter of friends of Degas, who advised her on the background and, as she later recalled, worked on the picture himself. She sent it to the American section of the 1878 World's Fair in Paris, where it was rejected, to her annoyance. She had joined the Impressionists in 1877 and probably showed it in their exhibition of 1879. The dog is a Brussels griffon, a breed she kept for the rest of her life. Paul Mellon bought the picture in 1963 and gave it to the National Gallery of Art in 1983.","c":"The most radical thing here is the pose: a bored, slumped child in a vast chair, the very opposite of the polite little sitter. The turquoise chairs dominate in an almost abstract way, the asymmetrical, cropped design shows the influence of Degas and of Japanese prints, and the dog adds a comic echo. The floor, probably the part Degas touched, is awkwardly flat, and the chairs overwhelm the girl, but it is the collision of child and furniture that gives the picture its modern character. It is among her first great paintings."},"nga:46570":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, a woman holds an open gold-colored fan in her lap in this vertical, loosely painted portrait. She angles slightly to the left and looks off that way, chin drawn in, dark eyes unfocused. Her brown hair is pulled back under a white scarf tied under her chin, and her high-necked dress is built from strokes and dabs of celery green and butter yellow, shaded with mauve pink, with scarlet cuffs. She sits on a rounded-back upholstered sofa in similar pale tones, and a framed mirror behind it reflects the sofa back and her head.","i":"Cassatt joined the Impressionist group in 1877 at Degas's invitation and showed with them in 1879; her pictures of women in modern interiors, at the theater or at home, are central to her work. The woman is unidentified, and with the fan and the mirror the picture plays with ideas of looking and being looked at, a theme the Impressionists loved. A woman's fan was also a prop of social display and polite flirtation.","c":"A bold, quickly painted image: the dress's celery and butter-yellow strokes, the mirror's cool reflection and the woman's inward gaze give it a modern flavor. Cassatt flattens the space with the mirror and makes the sofa as much a field of color as a seat, in the manner of Degas. The face is somewhat blank and the finish unresolved, but the picture has a daring, informal energy typical of her first Impressionist years."},"nga:46571":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, two young women in white gowns sit close together, angled to the left, almost filling this vertical canvas. The one at right, with a heart-shaped face and dark blond hair, wears a fitted off-the-shoulder bodice, a navy ribbon choker and frosty-green gloves, and holds a bouquet with one blood-red rose. Her companion, at left, hides the lower half of her face behind an open silvery fan streaked with yellow, teal and coral, and crosses her gloved arms over an ice-blue gown. Curving bands of golden yellow and green fill the background; the museum's keywords name a theater and a chandelier.","i":"The theater box, the loge, was a favorite Impressionist subject: Renoir, Degas and Cassatt all painted it, because it was a place where modern city life was staged as spectacle and where women were both spectators and objects of display. Here the two young women are looked at as they look, one half-hiding behind her fan. Cassatt could take a seat in the audience and paint it from the inside, and the white gowns, gloves and bouquet speak of an evening out in respectable society.","c":"Cassatt makes bold economies: white gowns built from pale pink, blue and mint strokes, the sliver of a second reflection at the right, and the fan that hides half a face. The tight, close-up crop and flattened space owe something to Degas and to Japanese prints. The result is arch, observant and unsentimental, with a tension between display and concealment. Less famous than her mother-and-child pictures, it is one of the sharpest of her theater scenes."},"nga:52163":{"d":"Two little girls sit back-to-back on a sandy beach, almost filling the width of this vertical canvas. They wear matching pale ice-blue pinafores over ocean-blue dresses. The girl nearer the viewer, blond and flushed, looks down at the denim-blue pail and wooden shovel held between her ankles; her companion, in a wide-brimmed straw hat with a tomato-red bow and olive-green stockings, turns away and reaches for another pail. The sea fills the upper third in pale blue and aquamarine, with two white sailboats drifting on it.","i":"Cassatt turned in the 1880s to children, mothers and domestic scenes, subjects considered fit for women painters but which she treated without sentimentality. Back-to-back, each girl absorbed in her own task, the pair is observed with attention to the self-contained world of children. The beach, a modern leisure spot of the Channel coasts, was popular with middle-class families in the 1880s, and Impressionists like Monet and Boudin had painted it.","c":"The picture is remarkable for its steady, unsentimental gaze: the sturdy bodies, the mismatched socks, the child who turns her face from the viewer. Cassatt's handling is broad and fresh, and the near-square geometry of two bodies against a band of sea is bold. The color is limited and clear, and the effect, though simple, is memorable. It is among her most characteristic and successful pictures of children, and shows why she is valued as an observer."},"nga:97663":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a young woman in a white gown and bonnet sits in front of a balcony railing on a sunny day, holding a small dog on her lap, in a vertical canvas painted with broad, visible strokes. She looks down to the left with blue eyes, her lips parted, and rests a tan-gloved hand on the dog's back. Her gown and bonnet are built from strokes of sky blue, petal pink, lavender and mint green. She sits in a wicker armchair, a black railing runs across the picture at the level of her nose, and loosely painted trees and distant buildings fill the view beyond.","i":"The balcony or window, a threshold between private and public space, was a favorite Impressionist motif, and Cassatt used it to place women at the edge of the modern world: seated, observing, never quite in it. The girl's absorbed look and the little dog make a domestic scene of the kind Cassatt painted in the 1880s, but the black railing cuts across the composition with a firm, modern geometry that keeps the picture from sentiment.","c":"Broad, brilliant strokes make the white dress shimmer with colored shadow, and the strong black railing against the pastel haze is a bold stroke of design. The dog, the wicker chair and the glimpse of rooftops add casual life. It is an impressive demonstration of Cassatt's ability to render sunlit white in color, and a more confident and fresh picture than many of her conventional portraits."},"nga:46572":{"d":"A girl sits in the corner of a room, almost in profile, her head tilted back and her lips parted as she lifts one elbow behind her neck and holds the end of a long chestnut braid over her shoulder with her other hand. She wears a voluminous ice-blue chemise with elbow-length sleeves and a blue earring. Her cheeks and mouth are flushed against pale skin. Behind her are a wooden dresser covered with a white cloth, a glass pitcher, a cup and a white washbasin, the corner of a bamboo-framed mirror at upper right, and wallpaper of leafy branches in cranberry red and slate blue on a mauve-pink ground.","i":"Cassatt showed this picture in the eighth and last Impressionist exhibition, which opened on 15 May 1886. Her friend Edgar Degas admired it and kept it: he is said to have acquired it in exchange for a work of his own, and it hung in his Paris apartment until his death in 1917. Louisine Havemeyer, a collector Cassatt advised, acquired it afterward. The girl is plain, a little awkward and caught in a private moment, unlike the sweetened children common in popular art of the time, and the firmer drawing reflects Cassatt's close study of Degas.","c":"Cassatt's drawing is firmer here than in most of her work of the 1880s, and the picture gains from it. The tilted head and raised elbow form an awkward, truthful diagonal, and the cool blue-white chemise glows against the hot red wallpaper. The wallpaper is the picture's one extravagance and nearly overwhelms the girl. Degas's admiration is easy to understand: the candour recalls his own bathers, but the girl is not posing for anyone. It stands among her best pictures of the decade."},"nga:61367":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a young girl in a slate-gray dress and a wide, flat-brimmed straw hat stands with her hands clasped in front of her. Her body faces the viewer, but she tips her head and gazes into the distance with dark brown eyes, her brows drawn together and her cherry-red lips turning down slightly. A checkered black-and-white bow wraps the hat; the gray jumper is touched with lilac, ivory and cobalt, and the background is vertical strokes of tan blended with faint green. Dabs of gold and yellow suggest sunshine on the hat and hair.","i":"In the mid-1880s Cassatt began painting children, often from the neighborhoods near her homes in Paris and the country, and she insisted on showing them as they were, sober, shy or bored, not as pretty dolls. The straw hat and summer jumper suggest an outdoor day. The sitter is unnamed, and the picture is less a portrait of an individual than a study of a child's self-contained inner life, with her faintly worried look and clasped wrist.","c":"A quiet, affecting picture: the turned head, the clasped wrists and the downturned mouth make the child a person with an inner life, not an ornament. The loose, layered strokes in the gray jumper, with lilac and cobalt hidden in the gray, and the dabs of gold on the hat brim give the surface a shimmering vitality. It is among Cassatt's finest portraits of children, because it declines to charm."},"nga:46482":{"d":"Shown from the lap up, an older woman with pale, slightly sagging skin and frosty gray hair sits facing the viewer in this loosely painted vertical portrait, one elbow resting across the back of her chair. She has a round face with large, dark blue eyes, and she smiles. Her gray hair is pulled back under a black hat topped with pale pink flowers, and she wears a black coat over a high-necked, hunter-green garment and nickel-gray gloves. The chair is painted with a loose red and green floral pattern, and the space behind her is parchment white.","i":"Cassatt was unusual among portraitists in painting older women without flattery and with evident affection. A direct, warm smile on an elderly sitter breaks the formal conventions of the genre, and the casual pose, with an elbow over the chair back, suggests an informal visit. The sitter is unnamed here. The hat and gloves show a woman dressed to go out, caught in an unguarded moment, in the manner of Cassatt's friend Degas.","c":"The picture's life is its frank warmth. The sitter's smile and large dark blue eyes are painted with affectionate directness, and the quick, loose handling of the chair and the blank ground keeps attention on the face. It lacks the structure of Cassatt's best compositions, and the space is left unresolved, but the portrait shows her gift for human sympathy and her disdain for sentimentality. A small, unaffected success."},"nga:46574":{"d":"A young woman with peachy skin and red hair sits on a pine-green bench, her left elbow hooked over the top slat and her head tilted to rest on curled fingers. In her other hand she holds up a red flower on a short stem and looks down at it with gray eyes, her cheeks flushed and her lips closed. Her white dress, shaded with ice blue, has a deep V neck over a pinafore of pale lilac bands and blue daubs. Behind her a loosely painted landscape of spring and emerald greens rises almost to the top edge, cropping the trees.","i":"In 1891 and 1892 Cassatt worked outdoors at a country house north of Paris, posing local girls among flowers and grass, and the picture belongs to that period, when she was also designing a mural for the 1893 Chicago World's Fair. Revery names the mood: a girl lost in thought. In the Victorian language of flowers the zinnia stood for thoughts of absent friends, though Cassatt may simply have wanted a spot of red against the greens.","c":"The high horizon and cropped trees, which flatten the landscape into a green screen, show Cassatt's debt to Japanese prints, and the girl's pose, head resting on hand, is natural and slightly awkward in the right way. The color, white dress shadowed with ice blue against green, is fresh and daring. The flower is the single note of red. Among her outdoor figure pieces of the early 1890s it is one of the most luminous."},"nga:46569":{"d":"Seen from above, a lime-green and white rowboat fills the lower part of the canvas, cut off by the bottom edge. A man in midnight-blue clothes and a floppy cap sits with his back to the viewer, bending forward to row, his face just visible in profile. Facing him, a woman in a pale blue dress patterned with pink lines and a wide pale-green hat holds a baby in a pink dress and matching hat who leans back in her arms. A large pale-green sail slants across the upper left. The deep blue water rises almost to the top of the canvas, where a thin strip of shore shows trees and white houses with red roofs.","i":"Cassatt painted this at Antibes on the French Riviera in the winter of 1893–94, while staying at a villa with her mother. At about three by four feet it is one of her largest and most ambitious oils. She seldom painted men, and she rarely set her mother-and-child subjects outdoors; here the dark boatman and the bright mother and child are set against each other in a design that owes much to Japanese woodblock prints, which she had admired at the great exhibition of 1890 in Paris. The three figures are unidentified, and their relationship is not stated. Chester Dale bought the picture in 1929.","c":"The boldest of Cassatt's large pictures. The steep downward view, the cropped boat and the slanting oar and sail turn the scene into near-flat shapes, and the lime-yellow hull against the deep blue is a colour risk she seldom took. The contrast between the dark rower and the pale mother and child is clear. It is less successful as feeling: the man's back is a blank wall of navy, and the faces are quiet, so the relationships stay abstract. As design it shows how far she went beyond the soft domestic scenes of her reputation."},"met:15073":{"d":"A woman named Eliza Hall is portrayed in a miniature of about 8.4 by 6.7 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory between 1810 and 1815 by Anne Hall, who shares her surname. The recorded details are a woman, a bow and a portrait. The bow belongs to the fashionable dress of the Regency years, and the size is large for a miniature, giving room for a full head and shoulders.","i":"Anne Hall (1792-1863) was born in Connecticut and became one of the leading American women miniaturists, working in New York. The shared surname suggests that the sitter was a relative, and a portrait of a family member painted when the artist was in her late teens or twenties is an early test of her skill. Miniatures of the 1810s often show women in the high-waisted style of the Regency, with a bow or ribbon accenting the outline.","c":"Hall's miniatures are often praised for liveliness and freshness, qualities that stand out against the cool polish of many contemporaries. A portrait made in her early twenties may show some hesitation in drawing, but the sitter's presence and the bright, clear color promise the talent that later won her recognition. At 8.4 centimeters it is large for a miniature, which lets the face breathe."},"met:15072":{"d":"A man named Charles Henry Hall is portrayed in a tiny miniature, about 5 by 4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1820 by Anne Hall. The recorded details are a coat, a cravat and a button, the plain costume of a man of the early 1820s, and the sitter shares the artist's surname, which suggests a family portrait. At this size the head is only a few centimeters high.","i":"A portrait of a relative is among the most personal kinds of miniature: made not for a fee but to keep, often given as a gift or exchanged between siblings. Anne Hall was in her late twenties when she painted it. The cravat and the single button show the plain, correct dress of a man of the period, a mark of respectable sobriety, and the shared surname hints that the likeness was made for the family circle.","c":"Family sitters often bring out a painter's best, since the likeness is for those who know the face best. Hall's clean color and secure drawing are well suited to the small scale: coat, cravat and button are given just enough definition to frame the head. It is a modest object with a personal intensity, and nothing in it is wasted."},"met:15075":{"d":"Mary Magdalene appears in a nearly square miniature of about 9.4 by 9.5 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1830 by Anne Hall. The museum lists only the saint, a woman and a portrait. Pictures of the penitent Magdalene traditionally show a woman with loose hair and an upturned or downcast face, often with an attribute such as a skull, a book or an ointment jar, and the small square format concentrates attention on the figure.","i":"Mary Magdalene, the follower of Christ traditionally identified as a repentant sinner, became in Catholic and later sentimental imagery a figure of penitence and tenderness, her loose hair and tearful face inviting sympathy. In early nineteenth-century America, such subjects offered women painters an ideal subject beyond portraiture, and many were adapted from famous European paintings known through prints. The picture's source is not recorded.","c":"A Magdalen is a test of expression more than of likeness, and the square miniature leaves little room: success lies in the face and the tilt of the head. Hall's clean, fresh color is an advantage, and the danger is sentimentality, which the subject invites. It is a polished example of the century's taste for pious sensibility, and interesting as evidence of Hall's range beyond portraits."},"met:15074":{"d":"A boy and a girl, named in the title as John Ward and Anne Catherine Ward, are portrayed together in a miniature of about 7.9 by 6.8 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1840 by Anne Hall. The recorded motifs describe a group of siblings. Group portraits of children were more ambitious than single heads in the miniature format, since two faces and their relationship had to be fitted into a space the size of a palm.","i":"Sibling portraits recorded a family's children at one moment, before they grew or, too often, before illness took them. The date around 1840 is also the moment when the daguerreotype reached America and began to take over the market for small likenesses. Miniaturists responded with larger formats, group compositions and the one thing the early camera lacked, warm painted color.","c":"Hall's lively color and affectionate observation suit children, and two sitters give her a composition to work with, since the heads can turn toward each other or be linked by a gesture. The format limits detail, and the pleasure lies in the freshness of the flesh tints and the bond between the pair. Against the early camera it offers what the daguerreotype could not: warm, painterly color."},"met:12975":{"d":"A woman and a flower are the recorded elements of this portrait, in oil on canvas, about 75 by 62 centimeters, dated between 1720 and 1730. The Met's title suggests the sitter may be Hannah Stillman, but that is only a possibility. The artist is catalogued under the name of Pieter Vanderlyn, with a note that the attribution is a former one, so the hand is less secure than the label might suggest. The size is that of a bust- or half-length, a modest scale for a colonial family portrait.","i":"In the early eighteenth-century colonies a woman's portrait announced the standing of her family, and a flower in the hand or at the bosom was a common attribute, a sign of beauty and virtue. Painters of the Hudson Valley and New England drew on English and Dutch prints for poses and dress, so many portraits have a decorative, linear look. The sitter's name remains uncertain, so her rank and family are not stated.","c":"Portraits from this circle are valued less for subtlety than for directness: strong outlines, a flat decorative design and a clear, emblematic face. They give a vivid sense of early colonial taste and cannot give psychological depth. Because the attribution is uncertain, the picture is best enjoyed as an example of the type, and as a reminder of how much early American portraiture survives without a secure name."},"nga:59924":{"d":"A young girl in a vertically striped dress of red, green and white looks out, holding a spoon with a sugar lump in one hand and a pink rose in the other. Beside her a small round table carries a white teapot, a cup and saucer and a dish of sweets; through a window above her shoulder a pink and blue sunset sky lies over low dark trees. She wears pearl earrings and a gold necklace. A painted inscription, in Dutch, gives her name and birth in 1726. The oil on bed ticking, about 96 by 84 centimeters, is dated 1730.","i":"The portrait comes from the Dutch-speaking communities of the Hudson Valley, where families of Dutch descent still commissioned likenesses of their children, with inscriptions in Dutch giving the sitter's name and dates. The spoon of sugar, the tea service and the rose point to a household of means, since tea and sugar were costly imports, and the rose is a common emblem of youth and beauty. The painter, Pieter Vanderlyn, once known as the Gansevoort Limner, was the grandfather of the later painter John Vanderlyn.","c":"Hudson Valley limners worked from patterns and prints rather than academies, and the results have their own appeal: flat, bold stripes, large dark eyes and a firm outline. Here the girl's direct gaze and the gaily striped dress, as decorative as a textile, carry the picture, and the table adds a small still life. Perspective and modeling are weak by academic standards, which hardly matters; it is a vivid, confident image of a prosperous colonial child."},"met:436973":{"d":"A long, low panel, about 45 by 141 centimeters, tells the Old Testament story of Esther in tempera and gold on wood. The record names Esther and King Ahasuerus and lists men, women, horses and banquets, so the picture probably combines episodes, such as the royal feast, the queen's banquet and a mounted procession. Marco del Buono Giamberti was a Florentine painter and maker of cassoni, the painted wedding chests, and panels of this long, low shape were typically their fronts.","i":"Esther, the Jewish queen of the Persian king Ahasuerus, risks her life by exposing the courtier Haman's plot to destroy her people, a story told in the Book of Esther. In Florence she was held up to brides as a model of beauty, obedience and courage, and the banquet at which she wins the king's favor suited a chest made for a wedding. Horses and festivity added the courtly pageantry Florentine patrons liked. Which episodes are shown is not specified in the record.","c":"Cassone painters were storytellers and decorators more than psychologists, and this panel works as both: figures in fashionable dress set in a gilded, crowded narrative, with the pleasure of picking out episodes one by one. The drawing is not subtle and the figures are small, but color and gold make a rich surface, and the long format gives the story rhythm. It is a charming document of Florentine domestic art in the fifteenth century rather than a major painting."},"met:437680":{"d":"A suspension bridge spans the Seine at the upper left, its stone pier rising from the water at the edge of the canvas and its dark mast and cables reaching into the blue sky. Beyond it, on the far bank, pale houses with tiled roofs and a tall poplar stand above a grassy slope. Small boats with figures lie in the shade under the bridge and along the near bank, and the river fills the foreground in blue-green ripples flecked with white. Clouds drift in short, crisp strokes. The painter's signature and the date 1872 are in the lower left corner. The colours are bright and the touch is quick.","i":"Sisley, born in Paris to English parents, painted several views near the Île-Saint-Denis in 1872. The suspension bridge, built in 1844 and replaced around 1900, carried the road to the village of Villeneuve-la-Garenne; the quay beside it now bears Sisley's name. Paul Durand-Ruel, the dealer who championed the Impressionists, bought this picture from the artist on 24 August 1872 and sold it in April 1873 to the baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure, a major collector. Sisley took part in the first Impressionist exhibition in 1874. The Metropolitan Museum received this one as a gift in 1964.","c":"Sisley is the least showy of the Impressionists, and this is a good example: no drama, only a bright day, a bridge, boats and water. The sparkle on the river and the clear blue sky are brushed with confidence, and the diagonal of the bridge gives the view structure. Compared with Monet's Seine views of the same year it lacks a sense of risk, and the surface is pleasant without often surprising. It is the work of a reticent painter at ease with his subject, and its modesty is part of its appeal."},"nga:52226":{"d":"A wide street slants from the lower left to the middle right, lined with buildings of two to four stories in parchment white, pale peach and blue-gray, and with trees and a peach-colored sidewalk on the viewer's side. The road is broken into strokes of apricot, light gray, rust and olive. Small, indistinct figures walk about, two horse-drawn vehicles are on the road and a man in a long black jacket strolls away. An ivy-covered wall, a long brown roof and two bare trees sit at the left under an overcast sky. Signed and dated 1872; oil, about 40 by 60 centimeters.","i":"Argenteuil, a Seine-side suburb a short train ride from Paris, was Monet's home in these years and drew Sisley and Renoir to paint beside him. Sisley's subject here is not the river but the modern town: an ordinary street, vehicles, a passer-by, no event. The street's name recalls Héloïse, the medieval scholar and abbess who was educated at the convent in Argenteuil. Such unremarkable views were exactly what the Impressionists were learning to find worth painting.","c":"A quiet, tonal street picture of the kind Sisley did with easy grace: the road broken into many small warm and cool touches, a pale overcast sky, figures reduced to a few marks. Tone, not story, holds the whole together. The limit is modesty of ambition; it is a slice of life without Monet's daring or Pissarro's feeling for working people. For Sisley's admirers, that restraint is the point."},"nga:66436":{"d":"Heavy clouds hang over a street so deeply flooded that it first looks like a canal. At left stands a row of buildings, the ground floor apricot orange and the upper floors pale peach, with dormer windows in gray and brown roofs and the lettering AS' NICOLAS near the top; two women stand in a dark open doorway. In front a man poles a shallow boat carrying three men. On the right, tan houses with black or red roofs, a grove, two poles and their reflections lie under a sky of pale blue and very pale pink. Signed and dated 1872; about 46 by 61 centimeters.","i":"The Seine floods the low village of Port-Marly from time to time, and Sisley painted the water more than once; the best-known series dates from 1876. Floods gave him what he liked best, a subject in which sky, reflections and water dissolve the solid world into color. The lettering probably belongs to the Auberge Saint-Nicolas, a landmark of the village. The doorway women and the boat make the scene a record of ordinary life interrupted, a village street used as a waterway.","c":"The painting's strength is its handling of wet air: clouds, flooded street and reflections are knitted from short, loaded strokes, while the stone building anchors the left side firmly. The tone is subdued, a chilly scene without Sisley's usual sparkle, and the small figures are summary. It is the earlier and gentler of his flood subjects, and arguably the later pictures of 1876, with bolder composition and color, are stronger. A fine example of early Sisley."},"nga:177090":{"d":"A village street seen from a slightly raised viewpoint runs into the distance as a tan dirt road, lined on the left by white houses and on the right by a tall fence, behind which the green tops of trees or bushes show. Small figures in dark clothes walk far down the road, beyond which taller trees rise over more buildings. The sky is softly colored in yellow, peach and gray, and the brushwork is loose and visible. The oil, about 38 by 56 centimeters, is dated about 1875; the title names Marly-le-Roi, west of Paris.","i":"Marly-le-Roi was a small town on the edge of a royal forest west of Paris, with the park of Louis XIV's vanished château nearby, and Sisley made it his home for a few years from 1875. His views of its streets show ordinary village life without monuments, in the manner of early Impressionist painting of the suburbs. The road running toward the horizon is a standard device that gives such pictures an unforced sense of space; the people are dots of dark paint, there for scale and life.","c":"A small, informal canvas whose interest is the weather of the sky and the warm earth of the road, painted with quick, visible strokes. It lacks the size and strong contrast of Sisley's major pictures, and the figures are barely more than marks. Yet the feel of an ordinary afternoon is convincing, and the soft yellow-peach sky is handled with a delicacy his admirers prize. A modest but pleasing example of his village views."},"nga:52227":{"d":"A meadow rolls toward gentle hills under a high turquoise sky dotted with small white clouds, seen beside a wooden fence that starts at the right edge and recedes to the left. The colors are jewel-like: topaz, aquamarine and several greens, with flecks of white, pink and red in the long grass that could be wildflowers. A few swipes of yellow and blue near the fence may be two figures, one standing and one kneeling. The hills are a patchwork of emerald and caramel brown dotted with dark trees, and a brown building sits on a crest. Signed and dated 1875; about 55 by 73 centimeters.","i":"In 1875 Sisley was settling in Marly-le-Roi, west of Paris, where he painted fields, lanes and tree-lined slopes again and again; this meadow is probably from the countryside around it, though the title names no place. The fence is a favorite device of landscape painters, a line that leads the eye into depth, and the two small figures give scale without a story. The aim of such pictures was the look of a particular hour and weather, not a view worth traveling to.","c":"Sisley is often called the most consistent Impressionist, a word that can sound like faint praise; this canvas shows the quality inside it. Sky is his structure, and here a turquoise vault sets the key for the greens and golds below, while the fence draws the eye smoothly into depth. The touch is lively, the color fresh, the sentiment absent. It is a lyrical picture that rewards quiet looking more than it demands attention."},"met:437682":{"d":"The town of Marly-le-Roi, west of Paris, is seen from the hill of Coeur-Volant in a wide canvas of about 65 by 92 centimeters, dated 1876. A town, houses, trees and a garden are the elements, so the picture presents a view of settled, cultivated country in which the town forms a distant cluster beyond gardens in the foreground. The scene is an ordinary suburban corner, treated as worthy of a large, careful painting.","i":"In 1876 Sisley was living at Marly-le-Roi and painting the neighboring countryside, in the years when the Impressionists exhibited together. He was a painter of the Île-de-France: villages, roads and river banks rendered with changing light and weather. Born in Paris to English parents, he remained a British subject and, though he lived his whole life in France, died in 1899 before his reputation was secure.","c":"Sisley is the most reticent of the great Impressionists, and this picture shows his virtue: a balanced composition, quiet color and an unforced sense of air and light across a village. He lacks Monet's boldness and Renoir's warmth, which is why he was long underrated. But his sense of space and sky is sure, and in 1876 he was at the height of his powers, painting some of his best pictures."},"met:901617":{"d":"A row of slim young trees, their thin leaves in pale yellow, green and brown, runs along a grassy bank and draws the eye into the distance, forming a V with the river's edge. Beyond them on the right the Seine curves under a bright blue sky filled with puffy white clouds. In the middle, two women stand or sit on the grass, and tiny figures walk on the path under the trees at left. A red-roofed building shows through the trunks, and boats lie on the water. The paint is applied in short, flicking strokes, and the picture is signed and dated Sisley 76 at lower right.","i":"Bougival lies about ten miles northwest of Paris, opposite an island in a deep bend of the Seine. Sisley lived west of Paris from 1872 to 1877, in a landscape that also attracted Monet and Pissarro. The Metropolitan Museum notes that the area was busy with day-trippers and commercial buildings, including the old Machine de Marly that pumped water to Versailles and Marly, but that Sisley chose to show the peaceful side promoted in travel guides, with only a handful of people. The trees are arranged in a V that draws the viewer in.","c":"This is Sisley being himself. The trees do the organizing and the clouds are the best-painted part, light, loose and convincingly high in the sky. The figures are sketchy, and the regular rhythm of the trunks tips toward monotony. It works because the day feels cool and breezy, and because the commercial side of Bougival is kept small and distant. The art is in that selection. It is not an ambitious canvas, but it shows the steadiness that made Sisley the Impressionist most devoted to landscape."},"nga:46689":{"d":"A river bends from the lower right toward the left edge under a sky that fills more than half of the nearly square canvas. Short flicks of pale blue, green and white make ripples on the water. On the far bank a dense wall of tall, narrow poplars in blue-green rises into a powder-blue sky streaked with thin, swirling clouds, and low hills in cobalt and grass green run along the horizon. On the near bank a pale path curves away, with a few tiny figures walking and a lavender sail just beyond the bend; two long boats nose into the shore. The grassy foreground is dabbed in peach, pink and green, and the artist signed at lower left.","i":"Sisley was born in Paris to British parents and kept British nationality, but he worked almost entirely in the countryside around Paris, painting landscape and little else. The National Gallery gives the date as 1877 or 1878, a difficult time for the Impressionist group, which sold few pictures. In a letter of 1892 Sisley wrote that the sky should never be a mere background, since it has planes of its own and gives a picture depth and movement. Here the sky takes more space than the land and sets the mood, which is calm and breezy rather than dramatic.","c":"A modest, assured canvas of the kind that made Sisley the steadiest landscape specialist among the Impressionists. The best passage is the water, where small touches of blue, green and white move without breaking the flat surface. The weaker part is the left foreground, where dabs of pink and green grow busy and the tiny figures are too slight to anchor the eye. Where Monet reaches for a spectacular effect, Sisley settles for a quiet one: a good Sisley, though not a defining one."},"nga:62641":{"d":"A pale blue sky swirled with faint peach fills the top two-thirds of the canvas, above a snow-dusted field, a fence and a road that runs into the distance. The spiky bare branches of a tree jut in from the lower right. A farm with pale green and peanut-brown walls and pale blue snow-covered roofs stands beyond the fence; two figures lean into the wind beside the buildings, and another figure or two may walk the far road. The snow reflects the sky's cool blues. Signed Sisley; oil, about 49 by 65 centimeters, dated 1878.","i":"Snow was a challenge the Impressionists took up with enthusiasm, since a white ground lets the colors of sky and shadow show, and Sisley painted snow scenes through the 1870s and 1880s. Veneux-Nadon is a village in the Seine valley near Moret-sur-Loing, the district where he would spend the rest of his life. The title's first snow suggests the thin cover of early winter, which the picture's dusting of white on field and roofs matches. The figures leaning into the wind add the note of cold.","c":"The sky is the success: a high, cold vault of blue and peach that sets the key for the pale, bluish snow and the faint warmth of the farmhouse. Sisley understood that snow takes its color from the sky, and he lets the field borrow its blues from above instead of drawing it white. The composition is simple and balanced, and the bare branch in the corner adds tension. A quiet, sophisticated winter piece whose restraint is the point."},"nga:46690":{"d":"A winding path enters a wood at the lower left, bordered along the bottom by a wooden fence. Tall green trees take up the left side, and on the right a gap opens onto a dark blue, hilly horizon with more foliage and the red roof of a building. Two small figures, an adult and a child, walk away along the path. The sky is light blue with flecks of white and lilac, and the palette is mostly green with soft browns, blues and purples, laid in short, overlapping strokes and dots. The canvas, about 46 by 56 centimeters, is dated 1879.","i":"Impressionist woodland scenes were not wilderness: the paths, fences and distant roofs show a countryside that people used and lived in, and a walk in the woods was a leisure of the new suburban world. Sisley, who spent the later part of his career near the forest of Fontainebleau, returned to such lanes again and again. The adult and child give scale and a note of companionship. The record gives no location, so the place is not named.","c":"Sisley's touch is lively here, in broken strokes and dots that make the leaves flicker, while the long fence and the winding path bring order. The greens could have become monotonous, and they are saved by the lilac and blue notes in the sky and distance. It is not an event, only a stretch of path, but the quiet is convincing and the surface full of life, an excellent example of his habit of finding a subject in the commonplace."},"met:437684":{"d":"A road with houses, the edge of a forest and a human figure is the subject of this canvas, about 51 by 62 centimeters, dated 1883 to 1885. The title names the route from Moret to Saint-Mammès, two places near the Fontainebleau forest, on the Loing river and at its meeting with the Seine. The view is an ordinary country road, unglamorous and unpicturesque, chosen for light and space rather than for its landmark value.","i":"Sisley settled near Moret-sur-Loing in 1880 and remained in that corner of the Île-de-France for the rest of his life. Roads and lanes were a favorite motif for the Impressionists, who painted the modern, lived-in countryside, the places people actually used, rather than wild nature. A road also gives a painter a natural way to lead the eye into the picture, and Sisley returned to the idea repeatedly.","c":"The road is a favorite Sisley motif, and in the mid-1880s he typically treated it with a broader, more textured stroke than in the 1870s. Some critics find the later Sisley lacks the delicate harmony of the earlier, others value the more physical paint. The scene is plain and unglamorous, which is its charm: a quiet record of weather, light and the ordinary shape of a place."},"met:437686":{"d":"A street in Moret-sur-Loing is shown in winter, with snow on the ground, in an oil on canvas about 47 by 57 centimeters, dated 1891. It is a town scene in cold weather rather than a view of open country. Sisley settled in Moret around 1889 and painted its streets, riverbanks and bridges for the rest of his life. Moret is a small medieval town on the Loing, near the forest of Fontainebleau.","i":"Snow scenes were a test of Impressionist technique, because the painter had to capture changing light on a surface that is supposedly white. Sisley painted winter in the 1870s at Louveciennes and returned to it at Moret, finding in the quiet, muffled streets a subject suited to his reserved temperament. The commonplace setting, a small town street, is typical of the Impressionist choice of the everyday and the modern.","c":"Snow tests a painter's subtlety of tone, because the whites must register differences of light and shadow without becoming flat, and Sisley's winter scenes are among his most admired for this restraint. The commonplace subject is handled without sentiment, and the cold air is convincing. It is a modest picture in a late period when he was little recognized, but it shows his sensibility intact."},"met:437683":{"d":"The meadows at Sahurs, a Seine-side village in Normandy near Rouen, are caught in morning sun in this canvas of about 73 by 92 centimeters, dated 1894. The subject is the light itself falling across open ground at an early hour. The title names a place and a time of day, as the Impressionists liked to do, and invites attention to one moment. Sisley died five years later, in 1899.","i":"Sisley visited Normandy in 1894 and painted around Rouen. In his last years he was ill and little recognized, though a one-man exhibition in Paris in 1897 brought some attention, and he died in 1899. The paintings of the 1890s show his color growing more intense and his brushwork more broken. A meadow in morning sun is an Impressionist subject in its purest form: nothing happens but the day beginning.","c":"A late Sisley that shows what he could do with an ordinary field and a time of day: the atmosphere is the subject. The brushwork is lively and the pale light convincing, though the composition is plain and the effect gentle. Viewers who want drama should look elsewhere; viewers who want the sensation of a morning will find few painters who do it so unassumingly."},"met:437685":{"d":"A road with trees leads toward Louveciennes, the village west of Paris where Sisley had worked earlier in the 1870s, in a canvas of about 46 by 56 centimeters, dated by the Met to probably 1879. The title names the route from Versailles, and the picture shows the road, trees and the village. It is a modest, unemphatic landscape in which the road is both subject and guide for the eye.","i":"Louveciennes, west of Paris, was favored by painters including Pissarro and Renoir: its roads, gardens and sloping streets gave a quiet suburban country. A road is a classic Impressionist motif of the lived landscape, a place people actually use rather than a wilderness; the Impressionists painted modern France, with its railways, bridges and tracks. Sisley's repeated returns to a road echo his habit of painting the same motif in changing light.","c":"This is Sisley's formula at its simplest: a road, trees, a sky and a sense of air. The small scale and unsensational motif make it easy to overlook, but the attention to tone and spatial depth is typical of his best. It is not a major picture but a typical one, pleasant and truthful, and a reminder that his art was built on modest subjects seen with unusual patience."},"met:10508":{"d":"A hillside town, buildings, trees and a bridge fill a large horizontal canvas of about 121 by 184 centimeters, painted in 1865 by the American landscape painter Samuel Colman. The title places the view at the hill of the Alhambra, the Moorish palace-fortress above Granada, and the museum lists a town, a bridge, trees and buildings. The scale is that of an exhibition picture, meant for a gallery wall or a patron's house, and a Spanish subject would have been exotic for its New York viewers.","i":"The Alhambra, the palace of the Muslim kings of Granada, was a romantic destination for nineteenth-century travelers, thanks in part to Washington Irving's Tales of the Alhambra, which Americans read widely. Colman had traveled in Spain and brought back views for the New York audience of the Hudson River School, who liked grand, faraway landscapes. Such a view pairs picturesque old buildings with a living town, a mixture of past and present that Romantic travelers loved.","c":"Colman carries the Hudson River School's clear, luminous manner to a southern setting, and the large canvas gives him room for architecture and atmosphere, his two strengths. His limitation, here and elsewhere, is a certain tidiness that can make a view feel composed rather than lived. Still, as an American's view of Spain, painted with sympathy and skill at ambitious scale, it is among the more important canvases of his early career."},"met:10507":{"d":"Sailing yachts race on a sea ruffled by wind waves in a wide canvas of about 77 by 153 centimeters, painted by Samuel Colman in 1870. The title fixes the moment as the finish of the first international race for the America's Cup on August 8, 1870. The museum's keywords list sailboats, a steamboat with its plume of steam, a bark, wind waves and the Atlantic, so spectator craft share the water with the competitors. The long horizontal format suits a regatta, which stretches along the horizon rather than rising from it.","i":"The America's Cup takes its name from the schooner America, which beat the British fleet off the Isle of Wight in 1851 and brought the trophy to the New York Yacht Club. In 1870 the first British challenger, Cambria, raced a fleet of American yachts, and the American schooner Magic won; the event drew great public attention, with steamers carrying spectators. A painting of the finish commemorated both a sporting contest and a patriotic moment.","c":"Colman is better known for mountains and Spanish towns than for yachts, but a long panorama of sea and sail suits his talent for broad, ordered space. The risk of such commemorative pictures is documentary tidiness, a record more than the excitement of the finish, and the spread of sails across a long format must supply the drama. As an image of a landmark in yachting history it carries its subject well, with calm competence."},"met:10509":{"d":"A very wide canvas, about 79 by 184 centimeters, shows the Spanish Peaks of southern Colorado in the late afternoon. The record lists mountains, a river, cattle, sky and the afternoon light. The format, more than twice as wide as it is high, suits a panoramic view of plains and a distant range. Samuel Colman painted it in 1887, after a career that began in the Hudson River School tradition and took him to Europe and North Africa as well as the American West.","i":"The Spanish Peaks, twin summits that rise abruptly from the plains of southern Colorado, were a landmark visible for many miles, and the railroads of the 1870s and 1880s brought tourists, artists and ranchers to the area. The cattle in the picture point to the new economy of the range, which had replaced the empty frontier painted earlier by artists such as Albert Bierstadt. The late-afternoon title suggests the warm, low light that painters of the West sought; the record gives no further description.","c":"A wide format gives the mountains room to stand off from the plain, and Colman's experience of southern light shows in a warm, clear atmosphere. The risk of a panoramic canvas is emptiness, and the cattle and the river must carry the foreground. Compared with Bierstadt's operatic Rockies, Colman is calmer and more reticent, which can feel underpowered but also truer to a quiet landscape. An accomplished, understated Western view by an artist better known for the East and Spain."},"nga:195774":{"d":"A river scene on a large horizontal canvas, about 103 by 153 centimetres. Calm blue water stretches away at the right with sailing ships on the horizon, while in the foreground a tree-lined bank has a dirt path, rowboats drawn up on the sand and, at the centre, a group of men, women and children who appear to be dancing together. Their clothes are touched with yellow, white, purple, blue, green and red. Two more men stand beside a rowboat, and large puffy grey and white clouds fill the blue sky.","i":"Thomas Birch, born in England and brought to Philadelphia as a boy, is often called the first important American marine painter, and he also painted river views and winter landscapes. The Delaware was the highway of the city's trade, and its banks were a place of recreation for townspeople, so the dancing group probably records a leisure excursion. Painted in 1831, the picture mixes topography and genre, and shows Philadelphians enjoying the river at peace.","c":"Birch is at ease with water and sky, and the soft blue of the river, the ships and the large clouds give this picture an easy, open space. The dancers are summarised in touches of colour and keep the scene lively without breaking its calm. The figures are stiff and the composition conventional, and the picture is more charming than grand, but it is a pleasant record of leisure on a working river."},"met:11279":{"d":"A landscape with trees in spring fills a wide canvas of about 62 by 102 centimeters, painted by Hugh Bolton Jones in 1885-86. The record lists trees and a landscape and gives no more, so the particular view, the stage of the leaf and any figures are not described here. The title and the artist's habits point to a quiet, close-toned view of the countryside rather than a grand prospect. The picture came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1887 as a gift of the collector George I. Seney.","i":"Spring was a favorite subject of American landscape painters of the 1880s, who had absorbed the lessons of the French Barbizon painters and preferred soft, humid light and gentle seasonal change to the grand effects of the Hudson River School. Trees in bud or leaf stood for renewal, though many viewers would simply have enjoyed the season as a pleasure of the countryside. Jones, who had spent several years in Europe, kept to this tonal, intimate manner.","c":"Jones painted quietly: softened edges, close values and a preference for a modest corner of nature over a grand view, a taste he shared with other American tonal painters of the 1880s. In a wide canvas the danger is blandness, and his answer is careful structure and delicate color. The picture is gentle rather than striking. Its pleasure is in patient observation, and its place is among the many sincere, now underrated, American landscapes of the Gilded Age."},"met:437151":{"d":"A man is portrayed at large scale on a tall canvas of nearly 2.1 meters by 1.2 meters, painted in 1563 by Anthonis Mor. The title says he is possibly Ottavio Farnese (1524-1586), Duke of Parma and Piacenza, and the recorded motifs name only the man and a portrait. The vertical format is likely that of the full-length or knee-length state portrait, reserved for sitters of high rank.","i":"Ottavio Farnese, grandson of Pope Paul III, married Margaret of Austria, the illegitimate daughter of Emperor Charles V, in 1538, and ruled Parma and Piacenza; his wife governed the Netherlands for Philip II from 1559 to 1567, the years in which Mor worked there. If the identification is right, the portrait places the duke among the elite of Habsburg Europe. 'Possibly' reminds the viewer that sitters of court portraits could be lost with the passing of owners.","c":"Mor helped make the full-length state portrait a standard format of Habsburg Europe, and the discipline here is typical: a calm, frontal figure, clear light and a sober palette in which costume and details are described with cold precision. He can be criticized for stiffness and a lack of warmth, but the objectivity gives his sitters authority. Next to Titian's bravura he is cooler and more analytic."},"nga:59":{"d":"A man stands against a dark ground, seen to the thighs, in a black satin jerkin and puffed breeches, light from the left glinting on the tight silk sleeves. His red hair, mustache and beard are neatly trimmed, a white ruffle rises inside his high black collar, and two thick gold chains hang across his chest. A sword hangs at his waist; his right thumb is hooked at the hip, and his left hand rests on the neck of a dark brown dog with a white blaze, wearing a studded collar. His eyes slide toward the viewer. Signed and dated 1569; about 120 by 88 centimeters.","i":"The sword, gold chains and fine black silk mark a man of rank, and the hand on a dog's neck is a familiar emblem of loyalty and of the gentleman's pastimes. His black dress follows the sober fashion of the Spanish Habsburg court, which Antonis Mor had served; the sitter's name has not survived, so his office cannot be read. The Latin signature pingebat, was painting, is a humanist formula that suggests the work is never quite finished.","c":"Mor's strength is a clear, level truthfulness. Light slides along the black satin sleeves, the gold chains and sword hilt are rendered without fuss, and the face has a cool, steady alertness. The stance is a formula he used for nobles and officials across Europe, and the sitter stays reserved; what lifts the picture above routine is the dog, whose studded collar and watchful eye are described with evident pleasure. An exact, composed, slightly chilly court-style portrait."},"met:437274":{"d":"Fragments of fresco from the villa of the Stati and Mattei families, transferred to canvas and measuring about 89 by 52 centimeters, are the work of Baldassare Peruzzi, who died in 1536. The recorded motifs are a bow and arrow, the zodiac and a centaur, with Cupid, Apollo, Hercules, Jupiter, Minerva and Venus among the themes, so the decoration appears to be a mythological and astrological program, the centaur archer perhaps standing for the zodiac sign Sagittarius.","i":"Renaissance villa decoration filled walls and ceilings with the gods and the heavens: Jupiter, Venus, Apollo, Minerva and Hercules stood for powers that shaped human life, while the zodiac and its constellations, including the centaur-archer Sagittarius, linked the patron's house to the order of the cosmos. Peruzzi had painted such an astrological ceiling at the Villa Farnesina in Rome, and the present fragments, detached from their walls, show only part of a larger scheme.","c":"Fresco survives badly once it is detached from its wall, and these fragments should be judged with that in mind: the drawing and the elegance of the figures, in the manner of Raphael's circle, are Peruzzi's strengths, while the color and the surface have inevitably lost depth in the transfer. What remains suggests a designer more interested in graceful, learned ornament than in drama, which is what villa decoration asked of him."},"met:437483":{"d":"Sir Joshua Reynolds (1723-1792), the first president of the Royal Academy, is portrayed in a miniature painted on ivory between 1786 and 1791 by Archibald Robertson, then a young Scottish painter in London. The recorded motifs name only the man and the portrait; miniatures of this date were small enough to hold in the hand. Reynolds was in his sixties, the most famous painter in Britain.","i":"Reynolds dominated British art in these years, painting aristocrats, actors and writers in a manner that blended Venetian color with Michelangelesque grandeur, and teaching the Royal Academy students in his Discourses. A young artist who made a miniature of him, whether from life or from one of the master's self-portraits, was paying homage to the most famous painter in Britain. Robertson emigrated to New York in 1791, so the work stands at the moment between his British training and his American career.","c":"A miniature of a great painter is also a test of its own maker: the likeness must be caught with some exactness, and Robertson's early manner, precise and delicate, is suited to that. As a document of a young painter's admiration for the dominant figure of British art, and as a record of Reynolds's late appearance, it deserves attention. As a work of art it is accomplished rather than inspired."},"met:11844":{"d":"A man named James Smith Livingston is portrayed in a tiny miniature, only about 4 by 3 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1795 by Archibald Robertson. The recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait. At this size it was meant to be set in a locket, a ring or a bracelet, and the thin ivory lets the white support glow through the translucent color.","i":"Robertson arrived in New York in 1791 and, with his brother Alexander, founded the Columbian Academy of Painting in 1792, teaching drawing and miniature painting. The Livingstons were one of the great families of the Hudson Valley and New York, whose members dominated colonial and early republican politics, and a miniature of one of them, presumably a member of the family, was part of a sitter's rank. Such tiny likenesses were exchanged within families and carried on journeys.","c":"At four centimeters the portrait forces economy: a few delicate touches for eyes, mouth and hair carry the likeness. Robertson, trained in the British school, brought clean drawing and a cool, refined palette to American miniature painting. The tiny scale precludes ambition, and the picture's charm is in the precision. Among American portraits of the 1790s it is an accomplished, restrained example of the genre."},"met:11927":{"d":"A man named in the title as Pierre Van Cortlandt Jr. is portrayed in a miniature of about 4.9 by 3.5 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1795 by Archibald Robertson. The recorded motifs name only the sitter. The Jr. in the title marks him as the son of a man of the same name, and both belonged to one of the great landholding families of New York's Hudson Valley.","i":"The Van Cortlandts were of Dutch descent, with estates along the Hudson, and in the years after the Revolution their members were prominent in New York politics and society. Portrait miniatures were the fashionable way for such families to record their members, to be given to relatives and friends. The suffix Jr., which the title keeps, is a reminder that names repeated across generations in such families.","c":"The miniature shows Robertson's early American manner, in which British-trained precision meets an American sitter's plainness: a clear contour, light color and a restrained, gentle face. It lacks flourish, as befits the sober taste of the Federal period, and its small size makes its quality easy to overlook. It is a little portrait, but an exact one."},"met:14315":{"d":"William Loughton Smith, a South Carolina congressman, is portrayed in a miniature of about 5.6 by 4.6 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1795 by Archibald Robertson. The recorded motifs name only the man and the portrait. Smith (1758-1812) was a Federalist who sat in the House of Representatives from 1789 to 1797, and in 1795 the national capital, where the House met, was Philadelphia. The tiny size would suit a locket or a small case.","i":"A South Carolinian in the Federal capital, Smith was among the leading Federalists of the early republic, an ally of Alexander Hamilton, and in 1797 he was sent as minister to Portugal. A miniature of a public man served as a keepsake for family and political friends, and it also testifies to the movement of leading men through the cities where Robertson worked. The small portrait records a moment in a political career, before diplomatic service abroad.","c":"The face is the only subject, and Robertson gives it his usual clear drawing and cool tone. A statesman's portrait can disappoint when it offers only a polite sameness, and here the interest depends on the sitter's name more than on any special insight. But the finish is crisp, and the physical charm of the tiny object remains. It is a representative example of the American miniature of the 1790s."},"met:15161":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in a miniature of about 5.5 by 4.4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1795 by Archibald Robertson. The title, Portrait of a Gentleman, is the museum's way of recording that the sitter's name is lost. The recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait, so the picture's interest lies in the face and the polished handling of the ivory.","i":"Robertson's New York clients in the 1790s were merchants, lawyers, officers and politicians of the new republic, and a miniature served as a private keepsake, often given to a wife, a sweetheart or a friend. The loss of this sitter's name is a reminder of how easily such objects, carried in pockets and cases, were separated from their identifying papers. What remains is the likeness: a face from the early United States.","c":"With no name to lend interest, the miniature must stand on painting alone: the clarity of the contour, the economy of tiny strokes and the gentle glow of the ivory. Robertson, trained in Britain, brought a polish prized by his American clients in the 1790s, and this small portrait shows it. It is quiet and well made, and the anonymity of the sitter, paradoxically, makes the craft more visible."},"met:15304":{"d":"A man named William Udall is portrayed in a miniature of about 5.2 by 3.8 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory between about 1800 and 1805 by Archibald Robertson. The recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait. By these years Robertson was well established in New York as a miniaturist and teacher, and a sitter like Udall likely came to him as one of the city's merchant and professional class.","i":"The first years of the nineteenth century were a boom time for miniature painting in the United States, as a growing professional and commercial class wanted likenesses to give to family and keep in cases. William Udall's name survives, though little else; the miniature preserves the man's face and his moment, a small piece of early New York society. The five-year date range reflects the uncertainty with which such small works are placed.","c":"Robertson's gift is for clean, firm drawing and a cool, restrained palette, and in a tiny miniature like this the control of the line is the chief pleasure. The effect is sober, even severe: there is no attempt to flatter, and the sitter's character comes through with plain directness. As an example of the careful, quiet American miniature at the turn of the century it is hard to fault, and equally hard to find surprising."},"met:780292":{"d":"A man stands in the Hall of Antiquities at Charlottenborg Palace in Copenhagen, where the Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts kept its collection of antique sculpture, chiefly casts, for students to draw. Small and vertical, about 31 by 25 centimeters, the oil painting is an interior view in which the room and its sculptures are the real subject, and the single figure probably serves for scale. It was painted in 1830.","i":"In 1830 the Academy at Charlottenborg trained Denmark's painters and sculptors, and its halls of casts were where students began, copying the ideal forms of antiquity before they were admitted to the life class. A view of the room, painted by a young artist of the Danish Golden Age, records that training and its faith in ancient models. Painters of the period, led by Eckersberg, took pleasure in exact, small interiors.","c":"A neat small picture whose virtue is exactness: room and sculptures are set down with the plain observation that the Danish Golden Age prized. It lacks the poetry of the best Danish interiors, and the figure is a convenience, but it is a good record of an art school's furniture of the mind. For a painter in his late teens it is a controlled and promising exercise."},"cma:170510":{"d":"A small oil study, about 32 by 26 centimeters, shows the head of a young woman. It was catalogued as in the style of Chardin and thought a minor nineteenth-century revival piece until, in 2011, the scholar Alastair Laing, the leading authority on Boucher, confirmed it as a rare survival from the early 1730s, one of only two known oil studies of this type by the artist. The subject is a head studied for its own sake.","i":"In the early 1730s Boucher was just back from Italy and had not yet developed the pink, powdery manner of his fame. Head studies like this were exercises in capturing expression, a tradition he knew from Italian and Dutch painting. The misattribution to Chardin shows how different this unassuming, realistic mode is from the glossy mythologies that the world associates with Boucher, and how an unrecognized work can lie hidden in a collection.","c":"The charm lies in its directness and lack of artifice: a living face, quickly and warmly painted, with none of the porcelain finish of the later Boucher. The discovery is as interesting as the picture, which is small and modest. It expands the sense of the painter's range and shows that he could be as simple as Chardin when the occasion called for it."},"met:435737":{"d":"A shepherd, cattle and ruined buildings fill this imaginary landscape of the early 1730s, an oil on canvas of about 64 by 81 centimeters. Campo Vaccino, meaning cow pasture, was the name Romans gave to the Forum, where cattle grazed among half-buried ruins, and the Palatine Hill rises beyond. Boucher, who had recently returned from Italy, combines the memory of Roman landmarks into a view that exists only in the painter's imagination.","i":"The Roman Forum in the eighteenth century was a picturesque ruin used for grazing and market stalls, and Grand Tour visitors came to meditate on the fall of ancient glory. Painters from Claude Lorrain to the Dutch Italianates had made the scene a favorite, and Boucher's pastoral figures belong to that tradition. The mixture of real topography and invention is called a capriccio, a form beloved by collectors who wanted souvenirs of Rome.","c":"Boucher in his early period handles this Italianate theme with charm and confidence, the cattle and shepherds giving a gentle rhythm to the ruins. The picture is less artificial than the mature work, and it shows how much he owed to Dutch and Flemish models. It lacks the grandeur of Claude and the flourish of the later Rococo, but as a young man's Roman souvenir it is attractive."},"cma:125948":{"d":"A pair of horizontal over-door paintings, each about 69 by 123 centimeters and painted in the 1740s, shows putti in light, charming scenes that mix classical elements with pure fantasy. One is titled Music and Dance, the other Cupids in Conspiracy, and both feature chubby baby figures that add humor. Such panels were set into the paneled walls above doors in a house, but where they were installed and who commissioned them is unknown.","i":"Over-door paintings, in French dessus-de-porte, were part of the decorative scheme of an eighteenth-century salon, designed to harmonize with wood paneling, mirrors and furniture. The pair exemplifies the fusion of fine and decorative arts in elite French interiors, in which a leading painter like Boucher could be asked to produce panels for a wall. Their themes, music, dance and the schemes of Love, probably suited a room for pleasure and entertaining.","c":"Boucher made such decorations with astonishing ease, and the pair shows his strengths: lightness, charm and a pastel, fluent touch. The compositions are designed to be glimpsed above a door, so detail serves the whole. The risk is repetition, since dozens of putti by Boucher seem interchangeable. Still, a good pair like this has the sparkle of high Rococo decoration at its freshest."},"cma:125949":{"d":"Putti, the chubby baby figures of Rococo decoration, are the performers in this horizontal over-door painting of about 69 by 123 centimeters, from the 1740s, whose title promises music and dance. It is the companion of Cupids in Conspiracy, and like it mixes classical elements with pure fantasy in a light, charming pastoral scene. Boucher, a leading painter, was asked to supply it as part of a wall decoration, and the humor of babies at play is part of its appeal.","i":"Music and dance were pleasures of the court and of Parisian society, and turning them into the games of putti made them both allegory and joke. The classical flavor, a distant echo of the Muses, is mixed with pure fantasy. In such a scene Boucher's putti are less gods than children of pleasure, and the picture serves as an ornament for a room in which music and dancing might actually take place.","c":"A pleasant and skilled decorative piece. Boucher's putti are a signature, plump and rosy, and he is at his best in the swirl of figures and in the pastel harmony. The scale and shape are dictated by the door, and the effect is of a delightful whisper rather than a statement. If it seems repetitive beside its companion, that is in the nature of the genre."},"cma:125950":{"d":"Cupids plot together in this horizontal over-door painting of the 1740s, about 69 by 123 centimeters, a companion to Music and Dance. The title shows the putti gathered in secret scheming, a comic image of the god of love's mischievous plans. Like its pendant, it mixes classical elements with pure fantasy in a light, charming pastoral scene and features chubby baby characters that add humor.","i":"Cupids in classical and Renaissance art are the agents of love who wound hearts with arrows, and a conspiracy of Cupids suggests a scheme against some unsuspecting heart. The eighteenth-century idea of love as a game of strategy and intrigue, familiar from the theater and the novels of the time, is displayed here in the guise of childish mischief. The humor works because the plotters are babies.","c":"The conceit is witty and Boucher keeps it light: putti plotting have the delicious absurdity of children playing at spies. The composition is fluent but unremarkable beside the best of his work, and one might wish for stronger drawing in the figures. As decoration it succeeds in spreading cheerfulness across a doorway."},"met:435738":{"d":"A woman and a man, a dog and sheep make up this pastoral scene of 1750, about 82 by 75 centimeters. As the title says, a sleep is interrupted: one of the two is at rest and the other disturbs it, a favorite pretext in Rococo painting for erotic play. Boucher's shepherds are not working farmhands but elegant figures in a pastoral fantasy, and the sheep and dog give the scene its rural flavor.","i":"Pastoral life, with shepherds, shepherdesses and flocks, was a fashionable fantasy in eighteenth-century France, found in opera, theater, tapestries and porcelain. A sleeper who is awakened or teased by an admirer is a recurring motif, a way to suggest innocence and desire at once. Boucher gave aristocratic patrons what they wanted: the countryside as a place of ease, flirtation and no work.","c":"The soft pinks and blues, the fluent drawing and the pretty types are Boucher's hallmark, and he brings them to the pastoral formula without strain. The picture is meant to charm, not to move, and its frivolity was a target for critics like Diderot, who found it false. As an example of what Boucher did best it is lively, but the eroticism and sweetness are cloying for some tastes."},"nga:46027":{"d":"Two women sit close together in a lush forest, surrounded by five sheep and a dog, in a vertical canvas about 81 by 75 centimetres. One, in a rose-pink dress with flowers in her blond hair, drapes an arm around her friend, in lavender silk, who holds a white dove with a blue ribbon on her lap; the first holds a small sealed letter beside it. Both are barefoot. A tall tree, a stone block topped by a carved lion, a basket of flowers and hazy hills complete the scene, and the artist signed and dated it 1750 on the stone.","i":"Boucher's pastorals show an aristocratic fantasy of country life: the women are fine ladies in silk dressed as shepherdesses, in a world without work or care. The dove is the bird of Venus, and with the sealed letter points to love and courtship, while sheep traditionally suggest innocence. Such pictures were made for the salons of fashionable Paris patrons in the 1750s, when the taste for the pastoral was at its height.","c":"Boucher's charm is here at full strength: feathery brushwork, a sweet pink and lavender palette and a delightful, intimate pairing of the two women. The picture is artificial from top to bottom, with decorative sheep and spotless barefoot ladies. Diderot famously complained that Boucher had everything but truth, and the criticism fits; yet within its frivolous world the picture is elegant, witty and perfectly judged."},"met:435739":{"d":"Venus, nude and pale, sits on a gilt daybed heaped with rose velvet and gold brocade, looking down at a pair of doves in her lap. Two cupids attend her: one stands behind, setting a flower in her hair, and another leans under her arm; a third lies on his front at the left, drawing a string of pearls from a silver dish. Blue-green silk curtains fall at both sides, and a gilt perfume burner and a tipped ewer with flowers stand in the foreground, with another dove at her foot. Behind, a stone obelisk and trees show in a hazy sky. The flesh is milk-white with pink shadows, and the picture is signed at the lower right.","i":"Venus, the goddess of love, born from the sea, was a favourite subject of Rococo painters, and her toilette, the ritual of dressing and adorning herself, allowed a nude in a boudoir surrounded by luxury objects. The doves are her birds, the cupids her attendants, and the pearls and flowers her adornments. Boucher dated the picture 1751, and it was probably painted for Madame de Pompadour, the king's mistress and Boucher's chief patron, for her château at Bellevue. Enlightenment critics such as Diderot later attacked the artificiality of Boucher's art, but in the 1750s it summed up the taste of the French court.","c":"Boucher's Venus is the Rococo ideal in its purest form: a body of pearly white and pink set against heavy green silk, a nest of satin and gilt carving, and playful cupids that tie the composition together. As decoration it could hardly be bettered. But the figure is a doll-like type with almost no weight or bone, the mythology is only a pretext, and the pleasure is all surface. Diderot found Boucher's world false, and the charge is fair, yet within its own aims the picture is beautifully judged."},"nga:12200":{"d":"Venus, the goddess of love, is shown at her bath in a vertical canvas, about 107 by 85 centimetres, painted in 1751. In Boucher's treatments of the subject the goddess is typically a pearly, softly curved nude attended by cupids and doves among drapery, roses and shells, in a setting more like a garden boudoir than an ancient grotto. The picture is a showpiece of Rococo flesh painting.","i":"Venus at her bath or toilette gave painters a classical excuse for a graceful nude, and in the Paris of the 1750s such subjects decorated the private rooms of courtiers and financiers. Boucher painted the theme several times for aristocratic and royal patrons, among them Madame de Pompadour. For his patrons, Venus was a flattering emblem of beauty, pleasure and love, treated with wit, not solemnity.","c":"Boucher's gifts are on display: the creamy modelling of flesh, the rhythmic flow of curves, the pink, blue and white palette and the feathery handling of cloud and drapery. The risk is artificiality: his nudes are doll-like, generalised and sweetly vacant, and the picture sacrifices depth for charm. Within those limits it is a polished, fluent Rococo performance, and a fair example of the painter at the height of his fashion."},"met:435740":{"d":"Putti with grapes and apples fill this wide canvas of 1753, about 114 by 162 centimeters, an allegory of autumn. The fruits of the harvest mark the season, and chubby winged children gather and carry them in Boucher's playful way. The picture is large and horizontal, and with its companion, the Allegory of Lyric Poetry, of the same year, it probably belonged to a decorative series in which the seasons and the arts appear as children at play.","i":"Seasons personified by putti were a common decorative theme: autumn stands for the harvest and the vine, with grapes recalling Bacchus and the wine harvest. The allegory turns a cycle of nature into a gentle game, flattering the viewer's enjoyment of luxury, as the harvest becomes an occasion for pleasure rather than labor. Painted in 1753, the canvas belongs to a time when Boucher was at the height of his fame and designed everything from tapestries to stage sets.","c":"Boucher is a master of the putto, and in this canvas the fruits and children are painted with an abundance that matches the theme, in warm flesh tones and a golden light. The composition is a decorative pile-up that avoids depth, and the allegory is thin. As a display of Rococo fluency and plenitude it is delightful, though some may find it overripe, like the fruit."},"met:435741":{"d":"Putti with a musical instrument, a pigeon and writing fill this wide canvas of 1753, about 115 by 159 centimeters. The instrument, presumably a lyre or a similar stringed instrument, and the writing identify the art of lyric poetry, which was sung to the lyre, while the pigeon, a bird of Venus, ties poetry to love. It is the companion of the Allegory of Autumn, painted in the same year and of similar size.","i":"Lyric poetry in classical tradition was sung to the lyre and concerned love, wine and personal feeling, from Sappho to Horace, and eighteenth-century French writers revived the tradition in light verse. Putti engaged in writing and music make poetry a playful occupation. Allegories of the arts were common decorations in French salons, a way for patrons to claim a cultured refinement.","c":"The elegance of the baby poets is characteristic: the sweet pink flesh, the airy clouds and the neat attributes make a clear, legible allegory. It succeeds as decoration more than as thought. Boucher's facility is again the main impression, along with a certain repetitiveness; one sheet of putti resembles another."},"cma:149663":{"d":"A very large canvas of about 233 by 215 centimeters, dated 1756, is devoted to a fountain of Venus, with some figures painted in gray as if made of stone, while others are fully human. This play between sculpture and living flesh is part of the picture's wit. Its original purpose is unclear: it may have been exhibited as an independent work, but it probably served first as a preliminary design for a tapestry.","i":"Garden fountains with statues of Venus and putti were a feature of aristocratic parks, and Boucher's picture imagines a fantasy version in which stone figures and real ones mingle. Lighthearted, erotic decorative schemes remained popular among the French aristocracy throughout the 1700s. Boucher designed many tapestries for the Beauvais and Gobelins manufactories, where such compositions were woven into hangings for palaces and châteaux.","c":"The conceit of gray stone figures beside living ones is a witty way of toying with the boundary between art and life, and Boucher carries it off with a light touch. The large size gives the picture a decorative authority, but it can feel like a design rather than a painting, as its likely purpose suggests. For those who enjoy Rococo wit it is a brilliant exercise; for others its frivolity may be tiring."},"met:435742":{"d":"The nude Angelica and the young soldier Medoro appear with a putto in this small canvas of 1763, about 67 by 56 centimeters. The subject comes from Ariosto's epic poem Orlando Furioso, in which Angelica, a princess of Cathay, nurses the wounded Medoro, falls in love with him and marries him. Boucher presents the lovers as a pastoral idyll, with the putto as a sign of Love's presence.","i":"In Orlando Furioso, Angelica, who has scorned all her suitors, including the hero Orlando, finds the wounded Saracen soldier Medoro, heals him and falls in love; their union drives Orlando mad. The subject was popular with painters because it combined romance with an excuse for nudity, and the lovers' carving of their names on trees is a famous episode. Boucher's version, like many in the eighteenth century, treats it as a tender idyll.","c":"A small, polished erotic idyll: the soft contours of Angelica's body and the glowing pink and cream of the flesh are very much Boucher. The type of beauty is generic, a Rococo ideal rather than an individual, and the story's drama is lost in sweetness. The picture is made to be enjoyed as a decorative cabinet piece, and as that it succeeds."},"met:435747":{"d":"Jupiter, disguised as the goddess Diana, approaches the nymph Callisto while a putto looks on, in this small canvas of 1763, about 65 by 55 centimeters. The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses. Boucher treats the myth in the playful, erotic spirit of the Rococo, with soft flesh tones and an embrace, and the small scale and date suggest that it was made as a pair with Angelica and Medoro for a private cabinet.","i":"In Ovid, Jupiter takes the form of Diana, the chaste goddess, to approach Callisto, a nymph in her train who has sworn to remain a virgin; the deception ends in her assault and pregnancy, and she is punished by Juno and Diana and turned into a bear. The myth is a story of abuse of power that painters of the Baroque and Rococo often turned into a pretext for erotic imagery, and Boucher's charming version sits uneasily with modern readers.","c":"Boucher's mastery of soft flesh and suggestive gesture is evident, and the cross-dressing of the god adds a sly comic frisson. The picture's elegance makes the violence of the myth almost invisible, which is the moral difficulty of the work. As an example of Rococo erotic painting it is accomplished, and as an example of its limits it is instructive."},"nga:32680":{"d":"A woman in flowing pastel robes and two winged putti gather on a bank of clouds among musical instruments and sheet music, in a horizontal canvas about 104 by 130 centimetres with rounded corners. The woman reclines at the right, her robe slipping from one shoulder, and points to a lyre propped on her knee, which one putto strums while the other hovers above with a laurel wreath and a flute. At her feet lie a helmet and sword hilt, a gold horn with laurel and two white doves supporting an open music book.","i":"The lyre, the horn and the music book identify the woman as a personification of Music, and the laurel wreaths promise glory to the arts. The helmet and sword laid aside at her feet suggest that the arts of peace have replaced war, and the doves and roses add the language of love. Boucher painted this in 1764, and his Allegory of Painting of the next year is probably a companion; the rounded corners show it was made to fit a decorative scheme.","c":"Late Boucher at his most graceful: the colours are lighter and silvery, the diagonal pose is pleasing and the putti and clouds form a soft cushion for the figure. It is fluent and decorative, but his late art repeated formulas, and by the 1760s taste was turning toward a stricter neoclassicism that made him look old-fashioned to critics such as Diderot. It is a charming decorative piece rather than a profound statement."},"met:435743":{"d":"A shepherd, a dog, sheep and a pigeon appear in this very small canvas of 1765, about 32 by 27 centimeters. The title suggests that the pigeon is the messenger, sent out by the shepherd, presumably with a note for a beloved. Boucher, then in his sixties, was still painting such pastoral fantasies for collectors, and this tiny picture is a cabinet piece rather than a decoration.","i":"Carrier pigeons were old messengers of love in poetry and in practice, and the dove is the bird of Venus, so a shepherd entrusting a note to one fits the language of pastoral romance. The artificial shepherds of the Rococo were city people's dream of the countryside, a world where love is simple and the only business is flirtation. Boucher's pastoral idylls had great success with collectors, though by the 1760s his popularity was facing criticism.","c":"A slight, charming thing: the pigeon's flight, the shepherd's pose and the sheep make a lyrical vignette with a clear story. Boucher paints it with a delicate touch, though the effect is a little mechanical. Beside his large decorations this small picture has intimacy, and it shows how the artist could reduce his inventions to a pocket-sized joke."},"met:435744":{"d":"The Virgin and Child, the young Saint John the Baptist and angels are gathered in this small canvas of 1765, about 41 by 35 centimeters. Boucher, better known for mythologies and pastorals, painted few religious pictures, and here he brings the same rosy softness to a sacred subject. The picture is small, suited to a private chapel or a cabinet, and its arrangement recalls the Renaissance groups of the Madonna with the two infants.","i":"The meeting of Jesus and John as children is an old devotional theme, based on their being cousins, and it was especially favored in Italy, which Boucher had visited as a young man. The angels add a celestial note. In the eighteenth century French religious painting had lost much of its force, and critics like Diderot complained that Boucher's artificial grace made him unsuited to serious subjects.","c":"The picture shows Boucher's gift for tender, soft-modeled children, and the angels float like his putti. It lacks the spiritual weight of a true devotional painting: the sacred figures look like Rococo beauties, and the atmosphere is more pretty than prayerful. Still, as a small domestic Madonna it is graceful, a late product of a painter at his most fluent."},"nga:32679":{"d":"A young woman draws on an oval surface with white chalk while three winged putti gather beside her on a bank of pink clouds, in a horizontal canvas about 102 by 130 centimetres. She reclines on a blue cushion in a seafoam-green robe, with a palette, brushes and a roll of paper behind her; her canvas is taller than her head, so the putto she draws on it is life-sized. He sits with a pink sash and a torch with a pink flame, and the others prop the canvas and hold a laurel wreath above her hand.","i":"The woman personifies Painting, and her subject is Love: the winged boy with torch and quiver is Cupid, so the picture suggests that love is the source of art, an idea recalling the ancient story told by Pliny of the Corinthian girl who first drew by tracing her lover's shadow. The laurel wreath over her drawing hand promises glory. Boucher painted it in 1765, the year he became First Painter to the King, and it forms a pair with his Allegory of Music.","c":"A witty, graceful picture: the conceit that Painting draws Love, who sits obligingly for his portrait, is charming, and the soft clouds and pastel colours give a fresh, airy effect. It is all surface, and nothing in it matches the weight of a great history painting, but it is a clear statement of the Rococo faith in pleasure as the source of art. It suits the painter who became the king's First Painter."},"nga:32681":{"d":"A large vertical portrait, about 144 by 105 centimetres, of a woman identified as Madame Bergeret, painted possibly in 1766 by François Boucher. Boucher, better known for pastorals and mythologies, painted relatively few portraits; his female sitters are usually shown in shimmering silks, with delicate faces and lively colour, as in his famous portrait of Madame de Pompadour of 1756. A canvas of this size was made for the display rooms of a prosperous Parisian house.","i":"Parisian financiers' families such as the Bergerets were patrons of the arts, and a large portrait in fashionable dress asserted wealth and taste. Boucher's portraits of the 1750s and 1760s show women in elaborate gowns, often with flowers, in the elegant manner of the Rococo court. The sitter's name has been preserved, but little else is recorded about her, and the date is uncertain.","c":"Boucher's portraits are charming but less penetrating than those of his rivals: he flatters, and the sitter's face often shares the sweet prettiness of his goddesses, which reduces individuality. His real strength is in fabrics, flowers and the light touch of colour, and these give the picture its elegance. Beside the psychological pastels of La Tour or Perronneau it is more decorative than searching, but as an image of Rococo taste it is persuasive."},"met:435745":{"d":"A woman, a man, a child, cattle and a shepherd are the figures in this huge canvas of 1768, about 240 by 238 centimeters. It forms a pair with the Washerwomen. The title makes the scene an idyll, a poem of simple country happiness, and the group of family and animals suggests a pastoral household in repose, set in a landscape of the kind Boucher invented for his aristocratic patrons.","i":"The idyll was a poetic form from Theocritus and Virgil that imagined shepherds living in a golden age, and French Rococo painters and writers revived it as a refined fantasy. Boucher's shepherd family, with its cattle, belongs to that tradition, not to the real countryside. The size of the picture suggests that it was meant for a grand room, perhaps as part of a decorative scheme, where it could provide a view of ideal simplicity.","c":"Boucher, in his last years, painted this idyll with the old facility, soft tones and fluent drawing, but the freshness of his earlier decorations has faded. The giant scale diminishes the intimacy that makes the genre charming, and the figures seem more posed than lived. For all its limitations it preserves the sweetness and grace that made him the favorite painter of Louis XV's court."},"met:435746":{"d":"Women wash laundry beside a stream beneath trees, with a bridge, a waterfall, buckets and a dog, in this huge canvas of 1768, about 241 by 236 centimeters. Children are among the figures. Boucher, then sixty-five, painted the labor of washing as a cheerful pastoral idyll, and the picture and its companion Shepherd's Idyll, of the same year and size, belong to his late work.","i":"Boucher's peasants are not farm laborers but elegant actors in a pastoral fantasy: the washerwomen are young and neat, the water is picturesque, and the work seems like play. The picture reflects the fashion for rustic subjects, in which the aristocracy enjoyed the idea of rural simplicity while real peasants endured hardship. Critics like Diderot attacked Boucher for just this artificiality, and a younger generation was turning to Greuze and Chardin for a more truthful view.","c":"As a late painting it has the old fluency, the feathery foliage and silvery water giving a pleasant atmospheric effect. The figures are decorative and a little repetitive, and the huge scale emphasizes the emptiness of the idea. It is a charming piece of Rococo scenery rather than a lively picture of work, and its interest lies in what it tells about the taste for the pastoral."},"met:435748":{"d":"A woman and a man are the figures in this small oil sketch on paper mounted on board, about 38 by 32 centimeters, a study for a sculptural monument to a princely figure. Boucher made designs for sculptors and for architectural decoration as well as paintings, and the sketch was a way to work out the arrangement of figures around the monument's center. The sketch is undated, and the title does not name the prince it honors.","i":"Commemorative monuments of the eighteenth century often combined a portrait or bust with allegorical figures, such as Fame, Grief or Religion, arranged on a pedestal or pyramid. A painter could supply the first idea, and a sculptor would execute it in marble. The oil sketch allowed the patron to see how the figures would look before the costly stone was carved.","c":"A sketch like this gives more pleasure than a finished monument might: the figures are brushed in with swift confidence, and Boucher's gift for graceful grouping is plain. It lacks the weight a real monument would need, but as a showcase for fluency of invention it is a happy trifle. Its value lies in the lightness of its making."},"met:11610":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Job Perit, painted in oil on canvas in 1790 by the Connecticut artist Reuben Moulthrop. The canvas, about 92 by 76 centimeters, is large enough to show the figure to about the waist, and it matches the size and date of the portrait of Sally Sanford Perit, so the two were evidently painted as a pair. The record gives only \"man\" and \"portrait\", so his dress, pose and setting are not described here.","i":"A pair of portraits of the same size and date, hung side by side, was the usual way for a couple to record a marriage and a place in the community, and the sitters here are presumably husband and wife. In the 1790s a Connecticut farmer, merchant or craftsman who had prospered could afford a likeness from a local painter, and a formal, upright pose told neighbors that the family was settled and respectable. Nothing in the record states Job Perit's trade or rank.","c":"Moulthrop worked in a plain, linear manner, with firm contours, flat areas of color and limited modeling, and his portraits are best valued for directness and decorative pattern rather than for subtlety. The face, set against a plain ground, must carry the picture, and the likeness is more emblem than psychology. It is honest provincial portraiture of the early Republic and a useful companion to its pendant, though not a sophisticated work."},"met:11611":{"d":"A woman in a lace cap and a shawl, with a miniature or similar pendant on a neck ribbon, is the subject of this portrait of 1790 by Reuben Moulthrop, in oil on canvas, about 92 by 76 centimeters. The record lists lace, a ribbon, a neck ribbon, a cap, a shawl, a miniature and a pendant. The title names her as Sally Sanford Perit, and the matching size and date show that it was painted as a companion to the portrait of Job Perit.","i":"A miniature worn at the neck was a personal and sentimental ornament, often a likeness of a husband, a child or a lost relative, and its presence adds an emotional note to a picture otherwise concerned with status. The lace cap and shawl mark a married woman of respectable means in the 1790s. Who is shown in the miniature is not stated in the record, so any guess would go beyond the evidence.","c":"Moulthrop's portraits of women give him more to describe than his men, and the cap, lace, ribbons and shawl let him display pattern and texture, the sort of detail that pleased provincial patrons. His drawing and modeling are simple, but the accessories give the picture charm and character. It is a livelier early American portrait of a woman than many, and its value is increased by the companion piece that shows the other half of the household."},"met:435570":{"d":"A ship in a stormy sea is the subject of a very small picture on card, dated 1892 and painted by the Russian-Armenian marine artist Ivan Aivazovsky when he was seventy-five. The record gives no dimensions and lists only a ship, the sea and a storm, with the museum's keyword miniature for the genre. The support, a sheet of card, and the small scale point to a quick, sketch-like picture of the kind Aivazovsky produced in great numbers, rather than one of his large exhibition canvases.","i":"Aivazovsky, born Hovhannes Aivazian in Feodosia on the Crimean coast, became the best-known marine painter of the Russian Empire, and his storms, calm bays and moonlit seas were collected by the court, by officials and by travelers across Europe and America. He painted from memory instead of at the water's edge, which let him work fast and fill his seas with light and drama. The Met's credit line names Isabel F. Hapgood, a translator of Russian literature, as the donor.","c":"A storm at sea was Aivazovsky's signature, and he painted it with a fluency few could match, handling water as a translucent, light-filled mass. The same facility made him prolific and often repetitive, and a small card like this is better taken as a brief souvenir of his manner than as a considered statement. Its pleasure lies in speed and confidence, not depth of thought; the big canvases, such as The Ninth Wave, show what he could do with time."},"met:437979":{"d":"A ship sails under the moon in this small oil on canvas, about 16 by 24 centimeters, by the Russian-Armenian marine painter Ivan Aivazovsky. The record lists a ship, the moon and a seascape and gives no date and no further description, so the position of the ship, the color of the sky and the state of the sea are not stated here. The size is that of a small cabinet picture, the kind of moonlit seascape that Aivazovsky painted in large numbers for admirers and visitors.","i":"Moonlit seas were Aivazovsky's trademark, and in the age of Romantic nocturnes they found a huge public in Russia and abroad: a calm bay or open sea under a full moon, often with a path of light on the water. The ship, usually a lone vessel, can stand for the human journey against nature's calm vastness. These readings are traditional for the type and are not documented for this canvas.","c":"Aivazovsky's gift was for light on water, and a moonlit sea suits it: the glow of the moon, its reflection and the dark mass of a ship make a simple, memorable composition. He repeated the formula many times, and small versions risk looking like products rather than discoveries. Within that limit, the best have real atmosphere, and this small canvas should be enjoyed for its glow rather than weighed as an important picture."},"met:11334":{"d":"A tiny portrait of the Reverend Dr. Ezra Stiles, in watercolor on ivory, about 4.4 by 3.1 centimeters, painted by Samuel King of Newport, Rhode Island, in 1770. At this size the likeness would have been set in a frame or case, to be held in the hand or worn, and the record gives no description of Stiles's pose or dress. Stiles was then minister of a Newport congregation, and he later became president of Yale College.","i":"Stiles was a leading intellectual of colonial New England: a minister, an amateur scientist, a scholar of Hebrew and a diarist whose journals survive. A miniature of such a man was a personal object, a gift or a keepsake, and ivory painted in translucent watercolor gave the flesh a delicate glow. King of Newport, whose pupils are said to have included Washington Allston and Edward Greene Malbone, was among the leading painters of the colony.","c":"Judged by his oils, King was a plain, direct portraitist with a sound eye for likeness and little interest in flattery, and a miniature of four centimeters asks for exactly those qualities. The charm lies in smallness and documentary value: a tiny image of a figure whose larger portraits survive. It cannot rival Malbone's miniatures for finish and delicacy, but as an early colonial example on ivory it deserves its place."},"met:437855":{"d":"Christ carries the cross toward Calvary, with soldiers and other men around him, in an upright panel of about 69 by 55 centimeters, painted in oil and tempera on a gold ground. The record gives the subject as the Stations of the Cross, with a soldier, a man and Jesus Christ, and no date. The artist, Nicolaos Tzafouris of Crete (1455-1500), worked in the last decades of the fifteenth century, when Crete was ruled by Venice and its painters served both Greek Orthodox and Catholic patrons.","i":"The way to Calvary was a central Passion subject for private prayer, in which the viewer follows Christ's burden step by step. The gold ground is a Byzantine inheritance, while Christ bearing the cross among soldiers is a Western Passion image; Cretan painters of the late fifteenth century combined the two traditions to suit buyers from Venice as well as Orthodox churches. That blend, often called Italo-Byzantine or Cretan-Venetian, is what makes Tzafouris interesting. The record does not describe the figures further.","c":"Tzafouris stands between two worlds: the gold ground and grave, icon-like stillness of the Byzantine tradition on one side, the modeled forms and oil technique of the Italian Renaissance on the other. Such hybrids can be awkward, with each element competing, but the best make a strange, intense devotional image. Valued as evidence of Cretan-Venetian cross-currents, the panel earns its place more for its historical interest than for refinement of execution."},"nga:437":{"d":"A young person with soft, rounded features is shown from the waist up, hands folded on a brown ledge, body angled slightly left and eyes lifted into the distance. Long, wavy light brown hair falls beneath a red cap, and a vivid red garment closes with green ties down the front. Behind lie rocky blue and brown cliffs, tall leafy trees and gray towers and buildings under a clear blue sky. The oil, about 52 by 34 centimeters, is dated about 1495-1500 and has been transferred from its original wood panel to canvas.","i":"A ledge with folded hands and a landscape behind was a format that Florentine portraitists borrowed from Netherlandish painting in the late fifteenth century, and the upward gaze gives the youth a pensive, idealized air. The red cap and garment are fashionable dress for a well-off young Florentine. His name is not known, so neither his family nor the occasion for the portrait can be stated, though such portraits were made for family homes, perhaps to mark a young man's coming of age or betrothal.","c":"The lifted gaze and the gentle, rather sweet face are appealing, and the landscape gives the youth breathing space, but the figure is formally set and the modeling plain beside the finest Florentine portraits of the 1490s. Agnolo di Domenico del Mazziere is a minor master, and the picture is more charming than powerful, a pleasant example of the late quattrocento portrait of a well-off youth rather than a leading one."},"met:436752":{"d":"Pomegranates, apples and grapes are gathered outdoors with a lizard among them, set against a landscape rather than a dark interior. The oil canvas, about 62 by 74 centimeters, dates to the 1670s and belongs to the type of decorative fruit piece that Flemish painters working in Italy made popular. Its first aim is abundance, a heap of ripe fruit offered to the eye in the open air, where a sheltered tabletop would be the northern habit.","i":"Abraham Brueghel, son of Jan Brueghel the Younger and a descendant of a famous family of Antwerp painters, spent much of his career in Italy, where patrons liked rich fruit and flower pictures. A pomegranate was an old emblem of abundance and fertility and, in Christian art, of resurrection and the unity of the Church, since its many seeds sit in one skin. Whether any such reading was intended here is uncertain: pictures of this kind were prized mainly for luxury and delight.","c":"Brueghel supplied the Italian market with fruit pieces in a lavish, decorative key, and this canvas does what the type asks: abundant and pleasantly arranged against a landscape. It lacks the microscopic finesse of his grandfather Jan the Elder and the invention of the great Flemish still-life painters, and it is more a skilled decorator's picture than a searching one. As furnishing for a grand room, it must have worked well."},"met:435839":{"d":"A broad square stretches toward the golden facade of a domed church, under a high blue sky streaked with cloud. At the right, a tall, thin bell tower rises above the buildings, its pinkish-brown shaft ending in a pointed green roof topped by an angel. Long arcaded buildings run down both sides, the left one hung with white awnings over its shops. Dozens of tiny figures cross the pale paved square: men in dark cloaks and three-cornered hats, a couple of officials in red, women in bright dresses and a few small dogs. Long shadows fall across the paving. The oil on canvas is fresh and quickly handled.","i":"The Met calls Canaletto the most famous view painter of eighteenth-century Venice, and notes that he was especially popular with British visitors. This canvas shows the city's best-known place, the Piazza San Marco, looking toward the basilica. The Met observes that he reduced the number of windows in the bell tower and extended the height of the flagstaffs but took few other liberties. His careful record of a stage in the repaving of the square, carried out between 1725 and 1727, helps date the painting to the late 1720s. Canaletto had grown up in the theatre, as the son of a stage-scene painter, before turning to views.","c":"The beauty here is in the light and the freshness. Canaletto's early views, like this one, have stronger contrasts and a looser touch than the smoother pictures of the 1730s and 1740s, and the people, flicked in with a few strokes, give the square life. The accuracy is admirable, though it can read as coolness: he adjusts the tower and the flagstaffs, but not the mood. There is little drama or personal feeling. The reward is a sense of a real place on a real day, which is more convincing than many elaborate fantasies of Venice."},"met:438100":{"d":"A view along the Grand Canal in Venice toward the Rialto Bridge, with boats on the water and the city's buildings along the banks, in an oil on canvas about 46 by 78 centimeters, painted in the 1730s. The museum's keywords record the canal, boats and the city, so the buildings, the number of boats, the sky and the colors are left unstated. It is a veduta, the Italian term for a precisely observed view of a city, made for visitors who wanted a record of the place.","i":"In the 1730s Venice was a leading destination of the Grand Tour, and young English aristocrats bought views by Canaletto as souvenirs; the merchant Joseph Smith, later British consul, acted as his agent. The Grand Canal, Venice's main thoroughfare, and the Rialto, the only bridge across it until the 1850s, were the most requested subjects. Canaletto is thought to have used a camera obscura to help set out his perspective, though he adjusted the result freely.","c":"Canaletto's early Venetian views are the standard against which later vedute are measured: firm perspective, crisp architecture, and a sparkle of light on water that makes the city look as fresh as new paint. The people are small but lively, and the sky is carefully managed. The formula could harden into routine in his later years, but at this date the observation is still sharp, and even a small canvas conveys a living city."},"met:438101":{"d":"A view of the square of Santa Maria Zobenigo in Venice, with a tower and buildings of the city, in an oil on canvas about 47 by 78 centimeters, painted in the 1730s. The museum's keywords record a tower, buildings and the city, so the people, the paving, the light and the colors are left unstated. A campo is a Venetian square, and this one takes its name from the church of Santa Maria Zobenigo, officially Santa Maria del Giglio, near Saint Mark's.","i":"Canaletto painted Venice's squares as well as its canals: the campi were the living rooms of the neighborhoods, with their wells, shops and parish churches. A view like this was probably one of a group made for an English visitor who wanted a representative portfolio of the city; the Metropolitan Museum has four views of similar size and date from the same bequest. Such pictures show the quiet side of Venice, away from the great monuments.","c":"Less spectacular than the Grand Canal, the campo gives Canaletto a chance to show his skill with modest architecture: plain brick and plaster, deep shadow, and sun raking across an open square. Small figures usually give his campi life and scale. Within a set, it is a quieter counterpart to the canal views, and typical of the dry, exact style of his best years."},"met:438102":{"d":"A view of the Grand Canal looking southeast, with the Campo della Carità on the right, a tower and boats on the water, in an oil on canvas about 47 by 78 centimeters, painted in the 1730s. The museum's keywords record the canal, a tower and boats, so the buildings, the people, the sky and the colors are left unstated. The Carità was a church, a lay confraternity house and a monastery, which now form the Gallerie dell'Accademia, Venice's great museum of its own painting.","i":"Until the wooden Accademia bridge was built in 1854 there was no crossing of the Grand Canal near here, and ferry gondolas carried people from bank to bank. Because Canaletto recorded the city with such accuracy, his views are also evidence of how buildings looked before later changes. They were bought by English visitors in the 1730s as souvenirs of the Grand Tour, and views of this size were often made in sets.","c":"The scene is more intimate than the panoramic canal views, and the diagonal sweep of the water pulls the eye into the distance. Canaletto's strength is the clear, solid, well-lit facades and the dark gondolas that anchor the foreground. The surface is smooth and the effect somewhat cool, but the picture preserves a Venice that has partly changed, and does so with a draftsman's honesty."},"met:438103":{"d":"A view of the square of Sant'Angelo in Venice, with a tower, a well, dogs and the surrounding houses, in an oil on canvas about 47 by 78 centimeters, painted in the 1730s. The museum's keywords record towers, wells, towns, dogs and Venice, so the people, the church, the paving and the light are left unstated. A Venetian campo was a public square with a well, a parish church and the daily traffic of a neighborhood.","i":"The well in a Venetian campo was not fed by a spring but by a cistern beneath the paving that collected rainwater through layers of filtering sand, and neighbors drew water from it daily. Canaletto included such humble details with documentary care, along with the dogs and passers-by that made a campo a neighborhood's living room. Views away from the great monuments appealed to Grand Tour buyers who wanted a sense of ordinary Venetian life.","c":"Neither monumental nor picturesque in the usual sense, the picture shows how much Canaletto could make of the unfamous: sunlit walls, deep shadows, a wellhead and a tower organized into a calm, exact arrangement. The dogs are a touch of humor. Compared with the views of Saint Mark's it has a more domestic, observational feel. It is a quiet success, typical of the artist at his peak."},"nga:46152":{"d":"Warm light falls on white stone and brown brick buildings in a grassy landscape, with tiny people walking, sitting and working across the turf and over a bridge on the right. In the lower left corner three men work along what may be the remains of a stone foundation, one raising an instrument. A thick-trunked tree with a sparse canopy stands at the center before a white building with columns, a tiled roof and a round lantern. A canal angles toward the horizon, where boats poled by men carry people or goods and a covered boat, probably a ferry, lands at stairs. Smoky purple clouds cross a clear blue sky.","i":"The Porta Portello was a gateway into the university city of Padua from the Brenta canal, the water route along which passenger boats carried travelers from Venice. Canaletto made this view about 1741 to 1742, when the War of the Austrian Succession had reduced the number of English visitors to Venice and he was looking for other subjects, including mainland views. The covered boat, the crowds and the workmen make the gate a lively threshold between city and countryside.","c":"This is Canaletto outside Venice and in a lighter, more rural register. The warm light on white stone, the clear sky with smoky clouds and the many small figures show his skill at both architecture and atmosphere, and the open grass gives the picture a calm that his canal views lack. The handling is crisp, and details like the men with an instrument in the corner keep the eye moving. A refreshing, assured picture from a transitional moment."},"met:782303":{"d":"An imaginary view, or capriccio, shows a tomb among ruins by a lagoon, with a boat on the water, in a small oil on canvas about 30 by 39 centimeters, painted in the early 1740s. The museum's keywords record ruins, a boat and a tomb, so the architecture of the tomb, the figures, the sky and the colors are left unstated. A capriccio combines real and invented elements into a plausible but fictional scene, and the lagoon setting recalls Venice without being a portrait of any place.","i":"Capricci were a Venetian specialty of the eighteenth century, in which ruins, classical monuments and lagoon views were combined to please a taste for antiquity and melancholy. Canaletto turned to them in the 1740s, when war reduced the number of visitors who bought his topographical views. The tomb, like the ruins around it, invites reflection on time and transience, a feeling the age cherished, though the picture is an invention and offers no specific story.","c":"Canaletto's capricci lack the authority of his true views, which can be checked against the city, but the best have a poetic freedom and a pleasing arrangement of crumbling masonry, water and sky. At this small scale the effect is of a jewel-like improvisation, with the tomb a quiet, melancholy focus. It shows a different side of an artist best known for precision, and a more imaginative one than his reputation suggests."},"nga:32588":{"d":"Dozens of men, several women and a few children gather in pairs and groups across a sun-drenched square before a domed church. The men wear hats and shin-length capes or jackets in charcoal, olive, butterscotch, black and brown; the women wear black capes over their heads and full skirts. Three coral-red flagpoles line the center, and tables under open umbrellas display cloth. At the left the church's five portals, mosaics and four gilded horses rise beside a long, pale pink arcaded building; between the buildings a winged lion on a tall column stands before sparkling water. The signature reads A.C.F.","i":"The church is Saint Mark's Basilica, with the gilded horses that Venice took home from Constantinople in the thirteenth century standing over its main door, and the winged lion on the column is the lion of Saint Mark, the emblem of the republic. Canaletto signs A.C.F., for Antonio Canal fecit, meaning Antonio Canal made it. The square was the political, religious and social center of Venice, and here it is shown as a busy marketplace as well as a ceremonial stage.","c":"The picture is among the most elaborate of Canaletto's Venetian views, balancing pomp with everyday trade: the glittering basilica, the bustle of figures, the market stalls. His crisp light, with sharp, straight shadows, makes the stone gleam, and the glimpse of lagoon and sky opens the composition. It is a showpiece, and as a record of the sights Grand Tour visitors wanted it is very hard to beat."},"nga:32589":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, a port town lines a canal, with a church across the water, under a bright blue sky of billowing white, pink and gray clouds that fills the top two-thirds of the picture. A three-masted ship, most sails furled, is ringed by a couple of dozen black gondolas and a few sailboats. Pairs and trios of people stand and walk along the stone quay, the women in long dresses, most men in knee-length tailed jackets or capes and hats. Market stalls and a cream-colored arcaded building lead away; across the water stand ivory-white buildings and a church with two stone-blue domes.","i":"The view looks across the mouth of the Grand Canal from the Molo, the quay in front of the Doge's Palace, to Santa Maria della Salute, the church built in thanks for the end of the plague of 1630, with the basin of Saint Mark in front. This was where visitors arrived and where Venice displayed its commerce to the world. English clients bought such views as grand souvenirs, and the large size of this canvas suggests an important commission.","c":"On a large canvas Canaletto balances meticulous architecture with an expansive sky, and the sky is half the picture: billowing clouds lit pink and gray give the scene air and drama. The fine detail of ships, gondolas and costumes is delightful, and the stone quay leads the eye deep into space. The effect is grand but never fussy. It is a fine example of Canaletto's mature view of Venice, a city presented as a gleaming stage for commerce and leisure."},"met:438106":{"d":"A castle rises beside a river, with a bridge and figures fishing, in an oil on canvas about 43 by 72 centimeters, dated 1748. The museum's keywords record the castle, the bridge, fishing and the river, so the angle of the view, the sky, the light and the colors are left unstated. Warwick Castle stands above the river Avon in the English Midlands, and the picture is a veduta, a precisely observed view, painted during the years Canaletto spent in England.","i":"Canaletto left Venice for England in 1746, when the War of the Austrian Succession had cut off the English visitors who bought his Venetian views, and he stayed until about 1755. English patrons commissioned views of London and of their own houses, and Lord Brooke, later Earl of Warwick, ordered several views of his castle. A medieval stronghold on a river, with anglers in the foreground, offered a picturesque subject quite unlike the lagoon.","c":"Canaletto adapts to England with surprising ease: the clear, orderly structure, the careful rendering of masonry and the airy sky transfer well from the lagoon to the Avon. The sparkle of his Venetian water is a little dimmed, and the English light is cooler and softer, but the observation remains honest. The picture is a sound, informative portrait of a place and a fine example of his English manner."},"nga:47179":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 134 by 106 centimeters, painted about 1754, shows an imaginary English landscape with a column, as the title says. The museum gives no further description, so the shape and setting of the column, the buildings, the figures and the light are left unstated. Columns were both ancient ruins in capricci and fashionable monuments in English landscape gardens, so the motif sits between the Roman and the English.","i":"The column is a motif with two histories. In capricci it recalls the ruins of ancient Rome, a favorite of Italian view-painters, and in eighteenth-century English gardens columns were built as memorials and ornaments. Canaletto, working for English patrons in the early 1750s, brought the two together. The picture matches another capriccio with a palace in height and date, and the pair presumably hung together in a house.","c":"Compared with the clear, sunlit views of his Venetian years, the English capricci have a softer, cooler air, and the figures are small. The column is more a stage prop than a statement, and the effect can be pleasantly decorative rather than searching. Even so, the scale and decorative assurance give the pair a presence, and they show Canaletto adapting to a new country at an age when many painters repeat themselves."},"nga:47180":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 134 by 109 centimeters, painted about 1754, shows an imaginary English landscape with a palace, as the title says. The museum gives no further description, so the palace's design, the countryside, the figures and the light are left unstated. A capriccio mixes real and invented elements, and in these late English pictures Canaletto combined English scenery with architecture of his own invention, at a size meant for a large room.","i":"Canaletto was in England from 1746 to about 1755. Having painted the Thames, Westminster and country houses, he also made capricci that set invented buildings in a native landscape. A palace may well have been chosen to appeal to English buyers who admired Palladian architecture, the style of the Venetian architect Andrea Palladio. This picture is paired in size, date and title with another English capriccio at the National Gallery, and the two were probably made to hang together.","c":"Canaletto's English capricci are among his most original late works, and the mixture of Venetian precision with English greenery has the charm of the unexpected. The scale is generous, the compositions confident, and the lighter, silvery English palette is attractive. They lack the sparkle of his Venetian prime, but they show a painter in his late fifties still inventing."},"met:438105":{"d":"An imaginary view, or capriccio, brings together a lock, a column and a church beside a lagoon, in an oil on canvas about 51 by 68 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a church, a column and Venice, so the shape of the lock, the water, the figures, the sky and the colors are left unstated. The catalog gives no firm date, and the picture belongs to the capricci Canaletto painted from the 1740s onward: scenes assembled from real and invented parts.","i":"Capricci mixed real buildings, invented ones and ancient ruins, and a Venetian buyer or a Grand Tourist could enjoy recognizing the pieces. A column recalls the two great columns of the Piazzetta in Venice and the Roman ruins that visitors came to see; a church and a lock on a lagoon bring to mind the waterways of the Venetian mainland, so the scene feels plausible without being any one place.","c":"Capricci free Canaletto from the discipline of a view that can be checked against the city, and the result is lighter in touch: architectural parts are rearranged with practiced ease, the water is smooth, and the figures are quick, bright dabs. The sparkle of his best Venetian views is rarer here, but the invention is pleasing, and the picture shows a master who knew the look of the lagoon so well that he could arrange it from memory."},"nga:39766":{"d":"Three white chickens gather around a small wooden enclosure. A large rooster at the left, its long tail feathers showing, lowers its head to the ground as if picking up a grain. At the right a wooden box with a large side opening holds a hen sitting on a round white egg, her face close to the rooster's, while another hen stands on top of the box looking down. All have red faces, the hens have tufts of white feathers on their heads, and the rooster has a bright red comb. The background is black and the ground is strewn with straw.","i":"Audubon's fame rests on The Birds of America, issued in parts from 1827, in which wild birds are shown life-size in lively attitudes; a farmyard picture of domestic fowl is a surprise, and a reminder that he also painted for sale in oil. The scene, a nesting box, a brooding hen and a rooster pecking in the straw, is a small drama of barnyard life with a faint humor in the confrontation of the rooster with the hens.","c":"The black ground and the white birds make an unusually bold design: the fowl glow against the dark with red faces as accents, and the rooster's low head, the hen on the box and the sitting hen make a lively group around the box. The painting lacks the finish of his finest watercolors for Birds of America, and the oil technique is relatively plain, but the observation of posture and temper is unmistakably his."},"nga:132261":{"d":"An osprey flies forward with its hooked beak wide open and wings raised, carrying a shimmering green and white fish in its talons, in this tall canvas. Both animals face left in profile and fill the picture against a pale, hazy landscape. The bird has dark brown wings with cream undersides, a golden-striped tail and a head masked in cream and brown. The fish, iridescent green fading to parchment yellow and speckled along its length, gapes, its tail dipping in the water. Distant cliffs, billowing clouds and white birds lie beyond.","i":"The osprey, or fish hawk, plunges feet-first to seize fish, and the weakfish is a common coastal fish of the Atlantic seaboard that it carries. Audubon prized accuracy of posture and action, studying birds in the field and working from specimens wired into lifelike poses; his dramatic images of birds with their prey made natural history theatrical. The painting is an oil version of a subject he treated in The Birds of America, which he was then publishing.","c":"The picture's force is its energy: the bird surges across the canvas with its beak gaping, the fish's eye and body shimmer, and the pale, hazy landscape makes the dark wings stand out. The oil medium gives a richness of color that the engraved plate cannot, but Audubon was never fully at ease in oil, and the landscape is a convention. As a portrait of predation it is vivid and unsentimental."},"nga:46149":{"d":"Ceres, the Roman goddess of grain and harvest, personifies Summer in this tall canvas of about 142 by 116 centimeters, painted by Antoine Watteau around 1717-18. The record gives no description of the figure, her pose or the setting, so only the type of picture can be stated: a decorative allegory of a season, in a large upright format suited to a wall scheme rather than a cabinet. Ceres is traditionally shown with sheaves of wheat and a sickle.","i":"The Seasons were a stock subject of decorative schemes, each carried by a classical deity: Flora for Spring, Ceres for the harvest of Summer, Bacchus for the vintage of Autumn and Saturn for Winter. Watteau is believed to have painted a set for the dining room of the Paris collector Pierre Crozat, and a goddess of plenty would suit a room made for feasting. The record does not describe the figure, so the reading rests on the title.","c":"A large decorative allegory is not Watteau's natural territory, and Ceres is better seen as a moment in his career than as his best. Yet even in a conventional role his touch is fluent and his invention gentle, and the goddess is likely to belong to the world of his fêtes galantes rather than to the heroic classicism of official French painting. It matters most as a rare large mythological work by a painter of intimate scenes."},"met:437926":{"d":"A man in a stage costume of pale pink, grey and green stripes sits on a stone bench in a shadowy garden, strumming a guitar and tilting his head back as if singing. He wears a white ruffled collar, a rose-pink cap pushed to one side and a rose cloak that falls behind him, with white stockings and cream shoes with pink rosettes, one leg crossed over the other. Behind him dark foliage closes in, with a pale stone statue half hidden among the trees at the left and a worn masonry pillar at the right. The brushwork is light and flickering, with thin touches of pink and silver on the satin.","i":"Mezzetin is a stock character of the Italian comedy, the commedia dell'arte: a cheerful, scheming valet and singer who often serenades a lover. Watteau painted actors of the Italian troupe that returned to Paris in 1716, and here he shows Mezzetin singing alone in a park, presumably serenading a beloved who does not appear; the statue half hidden behind him may stand for her. The Met dates the picture to about 1718–20, late in the career of a painter who died in 1721, aged thirty-six. The model's identity is not established. Watteau's fête galante scenes made theatre costume a language of the park.","c":"Watteau gives a theatrical type the pathos of a real person. The head thrown back, the loose cloak and the guitar held low all express longing and a certain melancholy under the comic costume, and the quicksilver brushwork turns satin into light. It is a small, intimate picture and lacks the grand organisation of the Embarkation for Cythera, but it shows his peculiar blend of fun and sadness, which later artists and writers took as the emblem of an age. The foliage is vague, and the figure carries all the weight."},"nga:32687":{"d":"A company of actors from the Italian comedy, the commedia dell'arte, is the subject of this canvas of about 64 by 76 centimeters, which Antoine Watteau probably painted in 1720. The record gives no description of the figures or setting, so only the type of picture can be stated: a group of the stock theatrical characters in costume. Watteau's theater pictures usually treat the players as a gathering of people rather than a record of a performance, and the size is that of a cabinet picture made for a collector.","i":"The Italian comedians had been expelled from Paris in 1697 and were recalled in 1716, and Parisians welcomed them back with enthusiasm. Their stock characters, Harlequin, Pierrot, the Doctor and the lovers, already stood for a world in which social roles were costumes. Watteau, a lover of the theater, found in it a way of painting feeling and illusion at once, since a performer is both a person and a mask. This reading is common in writing on his work and is not documented for this canvas.","c":"Watteau's late pictures are marked by a light, flickering touch and a melancholy beneath the charm, as if the players' gaiety were already a memory. From the year before he died, this canvas reflects a master of mood more than of narrative: a sense of people stepping out of their roles. Its tenderness is its strength, its want of incident its limit. It rewards slow looking rather than quick admiration."},"met:437925":{"d":"Actors perform in this small canvas, about 57 by 73 centimeters, painted around 1720 by Antoine Watteau. The recorded motifs are a woman, a man, the theater and an actor, and the title names the performers as the French comedians, the troupe of the Comédie-Française, whose stately spoken drama differed from the improvised comedy of the Italian players Watteau often painted. A cluster of figures in theatrical costume shares the stage.","i":"Watteau was fascinated by the theater, in which actors and fictions merge, a theme that runs through his fêtes galantes, and he painted both the Italian and the French companies of Paris. The French comedians performed the spoken repertoire of tragedy and comedy by Racine, Corneille and Molière, so gestures and costumes are those of formal rhetoric. Painted in the last year or two of his short life (he died in 1721 at thirty-six), it reflects the melancholy and wit of his late style.","c":"Watteau's gift is to turn a theatrical scene into a reverie: the figures' gestures are elegant but suspended, the brush light and nervous, the colors silvery and warm. Compared with his Italian comedians, the French company may seem stiffer, perhaps intentionally, since the declaiming style of the Comédie-Française was a rigid tradition. A fine late work, with a wistfulness that distinguishes him from the Rococo painters who followed."},"met:437936":{"d":"The Cretan princess Ariadne appears with a satyr and a large cat, listed in the record as a cougar or panther, in a small upright canvas of about 61 by 51 centimeters, painted by George Frederic Watts in 1894. The animals belong to the retinue of Bacchus, god of wine, and their presence marks the myth's turning point: abandoned on the island of Naxos, Ariadne is about to be found by the god. The record gives nothing more of her pose, dress or setting, so none is described here.","i":"In the myth, Ariadne helps Theseus escape the labyrinth of the Minotaur with a thread, but he leaves her on Naxos; there Bacchus finds her and makes her his wife. Painters read the story as loss turned to consolation, and the panther and satyr, creatures of the god's procession, signal that the abandonment is ending. Watts, a Victorian painter fond of allegory, returned to the subject, using the myth for ideas of grief, fate and renewal.","c":"In old age Watts painted with a thin, loose touch and a taste for symbolic content, and his late mythologies are about mood more than narrative: color is warm and soft, outlines blur, and the story dissolves into feeling. The method can be vague, and a picture risks being more suggestive than precise. At best it has a dreamlike grace that links Victorian painting to Symbolism, and at this modest size it reads as an intimate late statement."},"nga:52375":{"d":"A cobblestone street runs between buildings and tall green trees in this small oil on wood, about 25 by 19 centimeters. A stone building with a red-tiled roof stands on the right, partly covered by branches, and on the left are red-roofed buildings with white walls. Two women in long red and blue dresses and white bonnets carry baskets in the foreground, and in the distance two men stand by a cart. A warm light falls under a cool blue sky.","i":"Adrianus Eversen was a Dutch painter of nineteenth-century city views, a tradition that looked back to the townscapes of the seventeenth century and pleased buyers who valued Dutch history and picturesque old streets. The bonnets and baskets show everyday life in an Amsterdam that was still a small, quiet city, before the rapid expansion of the late 1800s. The picture is a small keepsake of that vanishing world.","c":"Eversen delivers what the type promises: careful drawing, warm sunlight, convincing shadows and a lively street. The picture is charming and well made but not adventurous, and its detail is more picturesque than searching. It stands in the line of Dutch townscape rather than the new currents of its day, which makes it appealing and slightly old-fashioned."},"nga:53161":{"d":"Three mounted trumpeters of Napoleon's Imperial Guard almost fill this upright canvas, in uniforms of cream white, crimson and gold. The nearest rider, with a brown handlebar mustache and a direct gaze, rides a dappled gray horse that lifts a forefoot; a brass trumpet with gold tassels is braced on his thigh, and his tall white helmet, slightly askew, carries a white plume and a brass plaque with an N. Behind him a rider on a white horse blows a horn; the third is only loosely brushed in. Géricault's oil, about 60 by 50 centimeters, is dated 1813-14.","i":"The N on the helmet is Napoleon's initial: these horse trumpeters belonged to his Imperial Guard, the elite corps whose brilliant uniforms were part of imperial display. Géricault, then about twenty-two, was making his name with military and equestrian subjects drawn from the Napoleonic wars, which were ending as the picture was painted; in 1814 the Empire fell and the Bourbons returned. The loosely painted third rider suggests a study or sketch rather than a finished exhibition piece.","c":"For so young a painter the handling is astonishingly vigorous: the dappled coat of the horse, the gleam of brass and gold lace and the hard light from the upper left are set down with a bravura that belongs more to Rubens and Gros than to the cooler manner of David. The third rider, barely sketched, is almost abstract. This is an early statement of the energy that led to his greatest pictures."},"nga:46504":{"d":"A muscular nude man sits on a rock before a shadowy landscape, his body mostly silhouetted against the sky and nearly filling the upright canvas. He faces right almost in profile, head turned away, with short black hair, a mustache and a beard, most of his face in shadow. One arm rests along a spear that crosses the picture diagonally from lower right to upper left; a bent knee props a foot on the stone, and a pine-green cloth lies under him. Brown and blue hills and gray and white clouds lie behind. The oil, about 94 by 76 centimeters, is dated about 1816.","i":"The title gives a type, not a named hero: a warrior, nude in the manner of antique sculpture, resting on his spear. In 1816-17 Géricault was in Italy, where he studied Michelangelo and the antique and the heroic male body, and a figure like this reflects that study. Whether it was meant as a finished picture, a study for a larger composition or an exercise in the heroic nude is not stated in the record.","c":"The picture's strength is sculptural power: the body is a single massive form, lit against a cloud-filled sky, with the rhythm of the spear giving a strong diagonal. Géricault absorbs Michelangelo without copying him. The weakness is anonymity: a nude hero with no named story is a study of muscle and light more than a drama, and the face is lost in shadow. It is less a finished statement than a powerful exercise."},"met:436455":{"d":"An evening landscape with a river, a man and an aqueduct fills a tall canvas of about 250 by 220 centimeters, painted by Théodore Géricault in 1818. The record lists the river, the evening light, a man, an aqueduct and a landscape, and nothing more of the composition. The size is that of a decorative panel made to be set into the walls of a room, and the title's mention of evening suggests that it belonged to a group of landscapes devoted to times of day.","i":"Géricault is remembered for horses and for the Raft of the Medusa, so a large landscape is a surprise. After returning from Italy in 1817 he painted a few landscapes in the tradition of Poussin and Claude, with their ruins, aqueducts and quiet pastoral figures. A Roman-style aqueduct signals the antique past, set against a sky at the close of day, an image of time passing. The reading is traditional for such landscapes and is not documented for this picture.","c":"A figure painter's landscape is judged by different standards, and Géricault's interest is in mass, shadow and the monumental calm of a classical view rather than in the changing detail of nature. The great scale gives it presence, and the subject is an unexpected side of the artist. It does not replace his horses and his Medusa; it complements them as a calm, measured counterpart, a rare large landscape by a master of the figure."},"met:436454":{"d":"A boy is portrayed in a small canvas of about 46 by 38 centimeters, painted by Théodore Géricault around 1819-20. The title identifies the sitter as Alfred Dedreux (1810-1860), who would have been about nine or ten. The record gives no description of his pose or dress, so those are not described here. The modest size and the subject point to a private portrait for the family, made in the same years that Géricault was showing the Raft of the Medusa at the Salon.","i":"Alfred Dedreux grew up to be a painter of horses, hunting scenes and fashionable equestrian portraits, an heir to Géricault's own subject, and Géricault is usually said to have encouraged the boy's talent. A portrait of a child by a friend of the family was a gesture of affection more than a commission, and it records a face before a career, though nothing in the picture can show the future. His name is also often spelled de Dreux.","c":"Géricault's portraits of sitters close to him, children included, are among his most direct, painted without Salon rhetoric: a quick, sure touch and a keen sense of character. A small canvas like this one is judged by the sitter's presence, and a child's portrait by a great painter has the advantage of freshness. It shows a tender, unforced side of Géricault that is easy to miss beside the Raft."},"met:854370":{"d":"A deathbed portrait, small and horizontal, about 24 by 32 centimeters, in oil on canvas by Théodore Géricault, dated about 1818-20. The title names the sitter as General Letellier and shows that the picture records him on his deathbed, so the likeness was taken from a dying or dead man. The museum's record gives no further description, and nothing of the pose, bedclothes or room is stated here. The picture came to the Metropolitan Museum in 2023 as a gift of Christopher Forbes.","i":"Deathbed portraits were an old custom: a final likeness made for the family, and sometimes for the public, preserving the face of a person of note after death. In these same years Géricault was studying the dead and dying in hospitals and morgues for the Raft of the Medusa, and he drew and painted faces and bodies at the end of life with unusual frankness, which gives a work like this particular interest. The sitter's career and relation to the painter are not given in the record.","c":"A deathbed picture of this size is valued for frankness more than finish: a small, quick oil by an artist who looked at death without sentiment. Such works matter for what they show of his working method and his unflinching eye. As a recent addition to the Met's Géricault holdings it is more important for what it adds to the record than as a polished picture, and it is best seen beside the Medusa studies."},"met:438113":{"d":"A woman on horseback is the subject of this small upright oil, about 45 by 35 centimeters, by Théodore Géricault, dated 1820 or later. The record lists a horse, a woman and equestrianism and treats it as a portrait, but it gives no name for the rider and no description of her pose, costume or setting. Géricault, who died in 1824, knew horses better than almost any painter of his time, and a rider on her mount joined his lifelong subject, the horse, to the human figure.","i":"Riding was a fashionable pursuit for women of the Restoration in France and England, and equestrian portraits, from the grand state portraits of kings down to private commissions, showed a sitter's confidence and standing. Géricault spent 1820-21 in England, where he studied horses and riders, and a date of 1820 or later may place the picture near that stay, though the record does not say. The rider's name is not given, so her rank is unknown.","c":"Géricault saw horses with an anatomist's eye and a Romantic's sympathy, and a small canvas of a woman and her mount invites both: the animal's weight and movement, and the rider's poise. The scale limits ambition, and the loose date leaves its place in his short career uncertain. It is best valued as a vivid, intimate example of his late interest in the horse and rider."},"met:441226":{"d":"Lions are the subject of this small oil on wood, about 48 by 60 centimeters, set in a mountainous landscape as the title says. The record lists lions and classifies the picture as animal art with an Orientalist label, meaning that the wild setting is meant to evoke the North African or Near Eastern home of the lion. Théodore Géricault painted it around 1818-20, and the record gives no more of the composition, so the number and posture of the lions, the shape of the peaks and the light are not described here.","i":"Géricault, who never traveled to Africa, studied lions at the Paris menagerie, and the lion, with its power and menace, fit the Romantic taste for wild, sublime nature that he shared with Delacroix. Pictures of beasts in wild settings were also a way to paint violence and instinct free of the constraints of the human figure; the Orientalist label suggests a landscape imagined as distant North Africa. Whether the lions are resting or hunting is not stated here.","c":"Géricault treats the lion as a subject of Romantic drama, and in a wild setting the beast becomes a type of untamed energy. The format is small, but a mountain and its lions can still produce a convincing sense of scale and isolation. If the picture is more an idea than a worked-up painting, it still carries the intelligence of an artist who studied animal anatomy more closely than most of his contemporaries."},"met:11239":{"d":"A portrait of a woman known by her husband's name, Mrs. Adrian Baucker Holmes, painted around 1850 in oil on canvas and measuring about 76 by 64 centimeters, the standard bust or half-length format of a mid-century portrait for the home. The record lists only 'woman' and 'portrait', and the museum gives no description of her pose, dress or setting. It was given to the museum in 1913 by Mrs. J. H. Blauvelt.","i":"Charles Wesley Jarvis (1812–1868) was the son and pupil of John Wesley Jarvis, one of New York's most famous portraitists of the early nineteenth century. By 1850 the portrait trade in the city was crowded with competitors, and the daguerreotype made it harder; a portrait like this one of a middle-class sitter was typical of the work available to the younger Jarvis.","c":"The younger Jarvis lacks the verve of his flamboyant father, whose portraits caught the swagger of the early republic. In the 1850s the work of the son is solid and conventional, and a middle-class sitter in her best dress is typical of what he produced. Competent, restrained, and of more interest for its social record than for any display of talent."},"met:436501":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of Francesca Ghirardi Lechi (1773-1806) by the Italian miniaturist Giovanni Battista Gigola, who was born in Brescia and worked in Milan. The record lists a flower and a nude and gives the themes as portraits and female nudes, so the young woman is shown unclothed, with a flower. Size and date are not given. As in other ivory miniatures, thin, translucent paint lets the pale support glow through the flesh, and the object would have been made to be held, framed or kept in a case.","i":"A nude in a portrait was a daring thing in the early nineteenth century, and the Neoclassical taste for the antique offered the sitter a way to appear: as a Venus, a Grace or another figure of beauty, with a flower as a traditional emblem of love and youth. Such a miniature was a private object, meant for an intimate circle and not for display. Lechi is the name of a leading Brescian family; the record says nothing of who commissioned the miniature or why.","c":"The delicacy of flesh tones that ivory allows suits the subject, and Gigola, a professional miniaturist working in the polished Neoclassical manner, would aim at smoothness and charm. The risk is that a nude miniature becomes a conceit more than a portrait, the sitter lost in the goddess. It is best valued as an unusual, private document of Italian Neoclassical taste, rather than as a revealing likeness of Francesca Lechi herself."},"met:10859":{"d":"Calliope, the Muse of epic poetry, is shown as a woman with a musical instrument in this canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size that Joseph Fagnani painted in 1869, one for each Muse. The record names the instrument as part of the picture but gives no description of her pose, dress or setting, so none is supplied here. Calliope's usual attributes are a writing tablet or a scroll, with a lyre or trumpet in some later images, so an instrument is not out of place.","i":"Calliope, whose name means beautiful voice, is named by Hesiod as the chief of the nine Muses, the daughters of Zeus and Memory who inspired the arts and learning. She presided over epic poetry, the verse of heroes and gods, and was held to be the mother of the legendary singer Orpheus, which explains why a lyre or other instrument can accompany her. A nineteenth-century painter showing her with an instrument draws on that old link between song and verse.","c":"A cycle of nine Muses is a decorative project more than a dramatic one: each canvas must make a distinct personification from a few attributes within a common format. Epic poetry is hard to show in a single woman, so Calliope depends on dignity of pose and drapery to suggest grandeur. As an academic idealization it belongs to a taste for classical personification that has lost its audience, but the set holds together as a coherent scheme."},"met:10860":{"d":"Clio, the Muse of history, is shown as a woman in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record lists only a woman and a portrait and gives no description of her pose, dress or attributes, so none is supplied here. Clio is usually shown with a scroll or an open book in which she writes the deeds of the past, and sometimes with a trumpet and a laurel wreath.","i":"Her name comes from the Greek for to make famous, and the Muse of history was held to proclaim the deeds of heroes and nations so that they would not be forgotten. History was the hardest art to personify, since it has no instrument or object of its own, so painters fell back on the scroll, the book and the trumpet of fame. Vermeer's Art of Painting is the best-known example of the type.","c":"The Muse of history makes the heaviest demand on a painter of allegory, because her subject has no image of its own, and success depends on a firm, serious bearing and a few well-chosen attributes. In a decorative cycle she carries less of the visual appeal of her sisters of music and dance. Fagnani's picture is best valued as part of the set, a measured and respectable contribution to nineteenth-century academic allegory."},"met:10861":{"d":"Erato, the Muse of lyric and love poetry, is shown with a lyre and Cupid, according to the record, in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. Her pose, dress and setting are not described in the record, so none is given here. The lyre and the winged god of love are the attributes her tradition calls for, so the picture's own elements fit her identification.","i":"Erato's name is linked to eros, love, and she was the Muse of love poetry and of the hymns that celebrate marriage. The winged boy Cupid, or Eros, is her usual companion, and her wreath is traditionally of myrtle and roses. The lyre, the instrument of lyric verse, completes the picture. Showing a Muse with Cupid blended two kinds of allegory, the arts and love, and was popular in decorative painting because it lets a single figure carry both charm and meaning.","c":"Erato is the Muse likeliest to charm, since Cupid supplies a playful note and the lyre a ready attribute. The risk is sweetness, the pretty allegory tipping into the merely pretty, and a painter has to keep poetry and dignity in play. Within a set of nine academic Muses she is probably among the more engaging, though that depends on how Fagnani handles the figures, not on the concept alone."},"met:10862":{"d":"Euterpe, the Muse of music, is shown as a woman in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record lists only a Muse and a woman and gives no description of her pose, dress or any instrument. Euterpe is traditionally shown with a double flute, the instrument of antiquity most closely tied to her, though later painters sometimes gave her a lyre or another stringed instrument.","i":"Her name means giver of delight, and in later tradition she presided over music and lyric poetry, with the flute as her attribute. The Muses together were the ancient way of ordering the arts and sciences: song, poetry, dance, history and astronomy all belonged to the nine daughters of Memory. A Muse of music suggests a music room or salon to a nineteenth-century patron, though the record does not say where these pictures were meant to hang.","c":"Music is the art a painter can least show, so Euterpe's picture relies on pose and instrument: a woman about to play or listening, with grace of line the main requirement. The danger is stiffness, a figure holding an attribute rather than making music. The nine canvases are best judged together, as a decorative ensemble in which each Muse earns her place by a clear attribute and a distinct bearing."},"met:10863":{"d":"Melpomene, the Muse of tragedy, is shown as a woman in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record lists only a woman and a portrait and gives no description of her pose or attributes. Melpomene is usually shown with the tragic mask, often with a club or a sword, and in the high-soled boots of the Greek tragic actor, the signs that tell her from her sister Thalia.","i":"Her name means the singer, though she became the Muse of tragedy, the drama of fate and suffering in the Greek theater, where actors wore masks and raised boots. In painting, the tragic mask and a grave bearing identify her, and an image of her was a reminder that tragedy purges pity and fear, as Aristotle held. Together with Thalia, comedy, she forms a pair of the theater, and a nineteenth-century viewer would have known to read the two faces of drama.","c":"Tragedy needs a shadow of seriousness, and Melpomene is the Muse for whom a painter most needs gravity without melodrama. A theatrical pose and mask are easy; a convincing solemnity is harder, and the danger is a figure that looks like an actress dressed for the part. Within the set she supplies weight, a counterweight to the lighter Muses of comedy, dance and love."},"met:10864":{"d":"Polyhymnia, the Muse of sacred hymns, is shown as a woman in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record lists only a Muse and a woman and says nothing of her pose or dress. In ancient art she is usually veiled and wrapped in a cloak, standing in thought with a finger to her lips or an elbow on a pillar, which is the pose to expect, though the record does not confirm it.","i":"Her name means many hymns, and she presided over sacred song and, later, over rhetoric and pantomime, so she is the Muse of silent, serious attention. Silence and the finger to the lips fit her better than a musical instrument, which sets her apart from sisters who hold lyres and flutes. In a series of nine she adds a contemplative note and an unusual iconography, a figure of reflection among sisters of performance.","c":"Polyhymnia is the quietest of the Muses, and a painter must make stillness interesting, with a thoughtful pose and a well-managed veil or cloak. That is a test of restraint, and academic painters of the 1860s were often good at it. The risk is monotony beside the livelier Muses of dance and comedy. Seen as part of the set, her calm is probably the point."},"met:10865":{"d":"Terpsichore, the Muse of dance and choral song, is shown as a woman in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record names her and lists a woman and a Muse, with no description of her pose, dress or attributes. She is usually shown dancing, or seated with a lyre and the plectrum used to strike its strings, so movement or music is what her image traditionally offers.","i":"Her name means delighting in dance, and she was the Muse of the choral dance that joined song and movement in Greek festivals. The nineteenth century, with its passion for ballroom and ballet, found her a congenial subject: dance was a social art, and an image of its Muse suited rooms for entertaining. The record does not say where these pictures were meant to be hung, so that use is a likelihood, not a fact.","c":"Dance is movement, and a painting can catch only a step. Terpsichore's picture succeeds if the pose conveys rhythm and lightness and fails if it freezes into a pose. The risk is the same as for any set piece of the academy: grace turns to stiffness. A decorative cycle needs at least one figure in motion to keep the rest from standing still, and the dance Muse is the natural choice."},"met:10866":{"d":"Thalia, the Muse of comedy, is shown as a woman in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record lists a Muse and a woman and gives no description of her pose, dress or attributes. Thalia is traditionally shown with a comic mask, an ivy wreath and a shepherd's crook, the signs that set her apart from her tragic sister Melpomene.","i":"Thalia's name means the blooming one, and she presided over comedy and pastoral poetry. The comic mask that is her usual attribute answers the tragic mask of Melpomene, and together they stand for the two faces of drama that the Greek theater and later European stages shared. A Muse of comedy adds a note of gaiety to the company, and a cheerful figure was a pleasure for a nineteenth-century viewer who liked wit with learning.","c":"Comedy asks for lightness, and a Muse of comedy is hard to do: a smile can turn coy, and a mask can turn into a prop. If Fagnani gives Thalia ease and a touch of mischief she earns her place as the relief of the series. Allegories of the 1860s tend to take pleasure too seriously; the better ones let their comic Muse look as if she enjoys herself."},"met:10867":{"d":"Urania, the Muse of astronomy, is shown as a woman with a globe in a canvas of about 110 by 84 centimeters, one of nine of the same size painted by Joseph Fagnani in 1869. The record lists a woman and a globe and offers no description of her pose, dress or setting. In the usual iconography Urania holds a celestial globe, often with a compass or pointer, so the globe here fits her tradition.","i":"Urania, whose name means heavenly, presided over astronomy and the study of the stars, and Renaissance and later artists made her the Muse of the sciences of the heavens. The globe reminds viewers that the Muses were patrons of learning as well as of the arts, a point the nineteenth century, with its faith in scientific progress, liked to stress. In a cycle of nine she brings an intellectual note among the poets, musicians and dancers.","c":"Urania is a figure of cool intelligence, and a globe in her hands gives the painter a solid, geometric form to set against drapery and flesh. The risk is a prop-like stiffness, the Muse as a teacher's illustration. Of the set she is the most likely to look serious and a little remote, which suits astronomy; as a decorative figure of learning, she is a dignified end to the sequence."},"met:10081":{"d":"A small rodent and some plants are the subject of this oil on canvas, about 36 by 56 centimeters, painted by John Woodhouse Audubon in 1846. The record lists a lemming, a plant and an animal, and nothing more of the pose or setting. The date and subject point to the work on The Viviparous Quadrupeds of North America, the natural history of American mammals that the painter and his father, John James Audubon, produced with the naturalist John Bachman in the 1840s. It is a naturalist's painting: an accurate portrait of a species.","i":"Lemmings are small Arctic and subarctic rodents, known to American readers of the 1840s mostly from northern specimens, and the Hudson's Bay lemming was among the species described in the Quadrupeds. Oils like this served as the models for the lithographed plates, so accuracy of marking and shape mattered more than atmosphere, and plants gave the habitat. By the mid-1840s John James Audubon's sight was failing, and his son John Woodhouse did much of the painting.","c":"As natural history illustration it succeeds by exactness and patience: the rodent and its plants are set out for identification more than for drama. It lacks the theatrical energy of John James Audubon's bird pictures, and the son is a careful, competent painter in his father's shadow rather than an original. Still, it has the charm of a documentary object, made to be reproduced as a lithograph and valued for its truth to nature."},"nga:39764":{"d":"A tawny brown, long-bodied ferret arches over a grassy mound, its dark legs short, its thin tail lifted and a dark band across its cream-white face like a mask. It appears to investigate a small nest at the lower center, half hidden in the grass and holding four pale speckled eggs. Grassy hills roll back to a horizon halfway up the canvas, and a sheet of white cloud covers most of the blue-gray sky. The oil on canvas measures about 56 by 69 centimeters and is dated between 1840 and 1846.","i":"John Woodhouse Audubon, the younger son of the bird painter, painted many of the animals for The Viviparous Quadrupeds of North America, which he produced with his father and the naturalist John Bachman in the 1840s and 1850s; he traveled to Texas in 1845-46 to collect specimens. The black-footed ferret, a rare Great Plains predator, was then little known, and the picture gives it an invented incident, a raid on a nest, in keeping with the dramatic narratives of natural history illustration.","c":"As natural history the picture is a charming dramatization: the arched body, the masked face and the speckled eggs make a clear little narrative, and the grassy plain is painted with a calm, open feeling. The ferret's pose is stylized, and the anatomy may rest on stuffed specimens, so it is more diagrammatic than alive. The younger Audubon lacked his father's electrifying energy, but the picture has an honest, workmanlike charm."},"nga:39772":{"d":"A fox walks toward the left through tall grass, its ears up, its dark eyes looking left and its long bushy tail hanging down to the ground. Its coat is painted in white, yellow and orange, with brown stripes across the body and tail, against a light yellow ground edged with dark brown. Small shoots of brown, green and yellow grass surround it, and taller grass rises behind. Low green hills lie beyond, under a mostly white sky with a strip of gray at the top and a burnt-orange glow at the left.","i":"John Woodhouse Audubon painted many of the animals for The Viviparous Quadrupeds of North America, the mammal companion to his father's Birds of America, published as lithographs between 1845 and 1854. The red fox, a familiar and widely hunted animal of the American countryside, was among the species depicted, and the painting probably served as a model for a lithograph, hand colored for subscribers. Natural history illustration then aimed to show each animal alive in a typical posture and setting.","c":"The fox is shown in a plain, decorative profile: the pose is easy and the coat's tones are well managed against the yellow ground, but the animal moves through the landscape like a specimen on a stage. The younger Audubon's pictures are more efficient than inspired, and the hills and sky are conventional backdrops. As an image of a wild animal it is clear, even charming, if less alive than his father's best work."},"met:437668":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of an unnamed man, painted around 1780 by the French artist Louis Marie Sicardi (1743-1825). The record gives no dimensions, and no name, dress or pose is described, so none is described here. At this date the usual format was a small oval or round plaque of a few centimeters, set in a gold frame or locket or kept in a case, and made with thin watercolor over the pale ivory so that the flesh glows.","i":"Before the Revolution, a portrait miniature was a gift of friendship or love as well as a likeness: it could be worn, exchanged or kept in a drawer, and it carried a man's image into the hands of those who loved or needed him. The sitter's dress would show his place in the ordered society of the late ancien régime, but the record does not describe it, and his name is lost. Sicardi himself lived through the Revolution and into the 1820s.","c":"A miniature of this date lives by finish and flattery: smooth, luminous flesh, a delicate outline and a slight conventional sweetness. Sicardi was a respected professional, and the likely strength is control; the limit is the lack of psychological depth that the form encouraged. As a competent example of a French miniature on the eve of the Revolution, it is a type of object more than a personality."},"met:437018":{"d":"A gold-ground panel gathers scenes from the early life of Christ and the Virgin with standing saints, in tempera on wood, painted around 1320 by the Master of Monte Oliveto. The record lists the Annunciation, with the angel Gabriel and Mary, the Nativity, the Adoration of the Shepherds and the Adoration of the Magi, together with Saint John the Baptist and Mary Magdalene. No dimensions are given, and the record does not say how the parts are arranged on the panel; the format suggests a small devotional piece.","i":"The scenes follow the Gospel story of Christ's coming, from the angel's message to Mary through the birth and the visits of the shepherds and the Magi, the stages of the Incarnation that medieval worshippers meditated on in order. Saint John the Baptist, who announced Christ, and Mary Magdalene, the penitent who followed him, were popular intercessors. Such a panel was made for private prayer; the gold ground places the story in a sacred, timeless space rather than a recognizable landscape.","c":"Sienese painting of the 1320s prized elegant line, fine pattern and gold, and a small panel like this is judged by its refinement: delicate drawing, rich color and an economy of storytelling that lets a few figures carry each episode. The Master of Monte Oliveto is a follower rather than an innovator, working in the manner of greater Sienese masters, and the picture's charm is that of a careful, devout, beautifully finished object. It seeks no drama and needs none."},"met:437019":{"d":"The Virgin and Child sit enthroned in this gold-ground panel, tempera on wood, painted around 1315-20 by the Master of Monte Oliveto. The record lists, around them, the saints Peter, Paul, John the Baptist and Francis of Assisi and small scenes of the Passion: the Flagellation, Christ carrying the cross, the Crucifixion and the Lamentation. No dimensions are given. The arrangement suggests a small devotional altarpiece of several compartments, though how the parts are laid out is not described in the record.","i":"A Madonna enthroned was the central image of devotion, and the saints around her were the intercessors the owner called on; the presence of Saint Francis suggests a Franciscan or a devotee of the order. The small Passion scenes are a short course in Christ's suffering, to be meditated on in order from the Flagellation to the Lamentation, and the pairing of Mother and Passion reflects the late-medieval teaching that Mary shared her son's sorrow. The gold ground sets the figures in a sacred, timeless space.","c":"Early trecento painting asks to be judged by its own standards: line, color and the rhythm of a flat gold space rather than illusionistic depth. The Master of Monte Oliveto, an anonymous painter in the Sienese tradition, gives the figures delicate drawing and warm color within that elegant, outline-based style. The many compartments add narrative richness but also crowd the surface. It is a pleasing, refined example of a type, the work of a follower rather than an innovator."},"met:437143":{"d":"A man dressed in white is the subject of this small portrait in oil on wood, dated 1574, by an artist known only by the monogram LAM and catalogued as French. The panel measures about 41 by 24 centimeters, a tall, narrow format unusual for a head-and-shoulders likeness, which may point to a small standing figure or to a panel made as one half of a pair. The record gives no name for the sitter and no further description of his face, pose or setting.","i":"In the later sixteenth century, portraits of this kind recorded a man's rank through his clothing, and pale cloth was a mark of fashion and means, though the record does not say what the white garment is. Without a name, a coat of arms or an inscription in the record, the sitter's status cannot be read, and the monogram LAM has not been tied to a known master. The picture's meaning is therefore that of a type: a prosperous man presented as he wished to be seen in 1574.","c":"An artist who survives only as a monogram is one whose reputation has not outlived the work, and the picture is best judged as an example of its type, not as the product of a known hand. A small, carefully dated portrait of this age has the quiet interest of a surviving face from the 1570s. Beyond that, with no name for the sitter and no known master, it remains a competent, anonymous likeness."},"met:11983":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as George P. Putnam, painted by Platt Powell Ryder in 1872, in oil on canvas, about 70 by 56 centimeters, a size for a head and shoulders or a bust-length figure. The sitter is presumably the New York publisher George Palmer Putnam, who died in December 1872. The record gives no more than a man and a portrait, so his pose, dress and setting are not described here.","i":"Putnam founded the publishing house that bore his name and was known for championing American authors, including Washington Irving. A portrait of a figure of New York's book trade in the year of his death was probably a tribute from friends and family. The donor, Samuel P. Avery, was himself a prominent New York art dealer and collector, so the picture came to the Met from within the city's art world.","c":"Ryder was a New York painter of genre and portraits, and a portrait of 1872 like this is an honest bust-length in the plain academic manner of the day: a firm head, dark clothing, a quiet ground. Its pleasures are craftsmanship and likeness; its limits are conventional design and a restrained palette. It is a respectable record of a man of letters and trade, more valuable for its sitter than for any daring in the painting."},"met:11984":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Stephen Whitney Phoenix, painted by Platt Powell Ryder in 1892, in oil on canvas, about 69 by 56 centimeters. The sitter is presumably S. Whitney Phoenix, the New York collector and genealogist who died in 1881, which would make the portrait a posthumous one, perhaps made from an earlier likeness or a photograph. The record lists a man and a portrait only, so his pose, dress and setting are not described here.","i":"A posthumous portrait kept a man's likeness in his family or institution after his death, and was often made from a photograph or an earlier painting. Phoenix was remembered for his learning, for the genealogy of the Whitney family that he published and for the library he left to Columbia University. The picture was given to the museum in 1893 by Samuel P. Avery, the dealer and collector, the year after it was painted.","c":"If, as the dates suggest, the portrait was painted after the sitter's death, it must rely on a source, and the risk of such work is a stiff, secondhand likeness. Ryder keeps to a plain bust-length and a sober palette, and the result is dignified rather than lively. Its value lies in its function as a memorial and in the continuity of Ryder's late portrait work, not in any freshness of observation."},"nga:166432":{"d":"A rural cabin interior fills this wide canvas, about 119 by 167 centimeters. Around a table gather two men, a woman and three children. At left an elderly, bespectacled man in black, a top hat and book at his feet and an umbrella against his chair, looks up and away while a woman spoons food into the dish he holds. Opposite him a younger man in a patched slate-blue jacket rests his chin on his hand, a small child leaning on his knee. Details fill the room: a cupboard of plates, a circus poster, dried chilis, a banjo and a cat licking a pie plate.","i":"The title announces a minister's call on a parishioner's household, and the guest's black coat, hat, book and umbrella mark him as the visitor, seated in the place of honor and being served. Brooke, a Virginian who trained in Paris under Léon Bonnat, painted this African American family in 1881, in the aftermath of Reconstruction, with attention to their poverty, in patched clothes, and to their pride and domestic warmth. The picture is usually read as sympathetic, though it is an outsider's view.","c":"The picture's great strength is its refusal of caricature. Each figure has dignity and individual character, from the old man's lifted chin to the sidelong glance of the oldest girl, and the densely observed interior gives the family a specific, lived-in world, down to the patches on the younger man's clothes. The Bonnat training shows in the secure drawing. It is sentimental in places, but more humane than most genre painting of its day."},"met:10431":{"d":"Two sailing ships fight under a wide sky of clouds on the open Atlantic, with the American flag flying: the record names them as the USS Constitution and HMS Guerriere. The oil on canvas measures about 63 by 79 centimeters and dates from about 1845, when Thomas Chambers painted it long after the event it shows, the naval battle of August 19, 1812. The record gives no further description, so the positions of the ships, the damage to their rigging and the colors are not described here.","i":"In the War of 1812 the American frigate Constitution beat the British frigate Guerriere in the North Atlantic, the first of several single-ship victories that stunned the Royal Navy and delighted the young republic. Shots were said to bounce off the Constitution's oak sides, which won her the name Old Ironsides. Pictures of the battle were popular long afterward, as patriotic images for homes and meeting rooms; Chambers painted it three decades later, probably from prints.","c":"Chambers is a decorative painter more than a naturalist: the sea becomes a pattern of bold, rhythmic waves, the sails flat white shapes, the flags crisp. What he loses in realism he gains in vividness, and the drama of the battle is turned into a clear, poster-like image. As an example of American popular painting of the 1840s it has real charm, and the stylized waves and clouds are the picture's chief pleasure."},"met:18354":{"d":"A village sits beside Lake George, with sailboats on the water, hills rising beyond and a wide sky of clouds, in an oil on canvas of about 57 by 78 centimeters. The record lists a church building and a chimney among the village's buildings, and the title names Caldwell, which stood at the lake's southern end and is now Lake George village. Thomas Chambers painted it in the 1850s in his bold, decorative style, and the record gives no further description of the light or the figures.","i":"Lake George, in the Adirondack foothills of New York, was one of the most visited scenic places in America by the mid-nineteenth century, praised by writers and painted by the Hudson River School, and the steamboats and hotels at Caldwell served tourists who came for the view. A picture like this met the demand for a souvenir of a famous place at an affordable price. Chambers, who was born in England, specialized in such views, often adapting compositions from published engravings.","c":"The picture's charm lies in its pattern: a flat, bright sky, rhythmic cloud and hill shapes, small sailboats and a tidy village drawn with an engraver's clarity. Chambers does not attempt the atmospheric depth of the Hudson River School painters; he offers a clear, cheerful map of a place. That is both the limit and the appeal, and it makes the canvas a good example of American popular landscape in the 1850s."},"nga:43630":{"d":"In the foreground a steep mountain rises, covered with finely detailed trees in green, orange and yellow, and behind it a river winds from left to right between banks lined with more green trees and small white buildings. Several small white boats float on the gray water, and more mountains recede into muted blues and greens. The blue sky carries puffy clouds in white, pale yellow and gray. Dark greens, browns and orange-red tones frame the scene. It is an oil on canvas of about 46 by 61 centimeters.","i":"The Connecticut River, which runs through New England, was a favorite subject for American landscape painters, most famously Thomas Cole in The Oxbow of 1836. Chambers, born in the English port of Whitby and active in the northeastern United States, often built his landscapes from published prints, adding strong color and a decorative sense of pattern. The date is given only as the middle of the nineteenth century.","c":"Chambers paints a valley as a patterned tapestry: orange and yellow trees on the great hill, clouds with firm outlines, boats like white dashes. It lacks the atmospheric depth of the Hudson River School painters whose subject matter he borrowed, but its flatness and bright color have a vigor and charm of their own. Folk strength, not polish, is what makes it memorable."},"nga:45641":{"d":"A park-like landscape of trees, flowers, bushes and a pond is laced with light brown paths. The pond, with pale green-blue water ringed by bushes, ferns and tall flowers, sits at the center. Across it stand three large white structures that could be mausoleums, and behind them rise a hill with trees and more white graves. A tall white monument stands at the right. On the slope beside it a couple walks down toward the pond, he in a long blue coat with gold buttons and a plumed hat, she in a pink gown and blue bonnet.","i":"Mount Auburn, opened near Boston in 1831, was the first large 'rural cemetery' in the United States, a landscaped garden of ponds, paths and monuments that served also as a public park. Visitors came to stroll and picnic, which explains the fashionably dressed couple in the picture. Chambers presents the place as an attractive promenade, not a scene of mourning.","c":"The pleasure lies in the pattern: curving paths, round bushes and flat white tombs arranged like a garden plan, with the couple as the only note of life. Chambers does not suggest the solemnity or the spaciousness of the place, and the proportions are childlike. As a document of how Americans of the mid-century enjoyed their new cemeteries it is charming, more naive than profound."},"nga:50687":{"d":"A winding dirt path, bordered by foliage, leads into a landscape of hills and mountains. At the left a row of buildings stands below tall brown cliffs, among them a white church with a steeple, and a body of water dotted with sailboats opens at the right in the distance. The palette is warm and earthy, with greens, browns, yellows and oranges, and the cloudy sky is touched with pink and purple. The canvas is about 56 by 76 centimeters.","i":"Caldwell was the old name of the village at the southern end of Lake George in upstate New York, now called Lake George. By the middle of the nineteenth century the lake, with its mountains, steamboats and hotels, was a favorite tourist destination, and views of it sold to travelers. The path in the foreground draws the viewer in like a visitor arriving by road.","c":"The path is an old device, but Chambers uses it with a bold sense of color: the warm browns and oranges and the pink-and-purple sky give the view an unusual glow. The buildings are tiny and flatly drawn, and the distance does not recede convincingly, so the effect is of a lively picture postcard and not of a remembered place. Cheerful, decorative and slightly unreal."},"nga:51090":{"d":"A sailing vessel with three billowing yellow sails crosses choppy green water, seen from sea level and parallel to the ship. A few people stand on deck in red, blue and brown garments. On the horizon are distant green hills and cliffs, around which small white birds fly, and more boats appear in the distance on both sides. The cloudy sky is a soft gradient of pale yellow, pink and blue. The canvas measures about 56 by 77 centimeters.","i":"A felucca is a small, fast sailing boat of the Mediterranean, usually with lateen sails, the long triangular sails hung from slanting yards. Gibraltar, the British fortress at the western gate of the Mediterranean, was passed by every ship entering or leaving the sea. A scene of this kind, set far from Chambers's American home, was probably composed from a print or a sailor's description rather than from his own observation.","c":"The yellow sails against green water are a bold, almost heraldic color chord, and the pink-yellow sky keeps the palette bright. The waves are drawn as repeated curling lines, more decorative than observed, and the ship, sea and cliffs are separate pieces with little air between them. The sense of wind is carried by the sails alone. A cheerful marine fantasy."},"nga:51138":{"d":"Waves surge up the sides of a masted ship lit by moonlight. Its bow angles to the right, a wave breaks over the deck as the ship tips away, and two of its masts are broken close to the deck, leaving only the lowest sail near the bow under a partly broken mast. The bowsprit is fragmented too. Men pull on the rigging or stand at the gunwale. Other waves heave like mountains in slate and gunmetal blue beneath a swirl of dark clouds around the full moon at upper left.","i":"A frigate was a fast, medium-sized warship, and the moonlit storm with a dismasted vessel was a stock subject of Romantic marine painting and engraving, treated by artists from Joseph Vernet to Turner. Such pictures played on viewers' awe at nature's power and their admiration for sailors' courage. Chambers, like many American painters of the period, drew on the engraved models that circulated widely.","c":"The vortex of cloud around the moon is the best idea: it organizes the whole sky and echoes the pitch of the ship. The waves heaved like mountains give a strong sense of scale. The execution, though, is stylized: the water is carved into repeating ridges and the men are stiff dolls. As theater the picture is vivid and effective; as seamanship it is schematic."},"nga:54127":{"d":"The museum offers no written description of this picture. Its record lists sailing ships on a wind-ruffled sea, clouds, a sunset sky and an ensign in New York Bay, so the subject is a harbor view in the last light of the day. The canvas measures about 56 by 76 centimeters, the same format as several of Chambers's harbor and coastal scenes. Details of the ships and their arrangement are not recorded here.","i":"New York Bay was the gateway of American commerce, and in the middle of the nineteenth century it was crowded with merchant sail. Sunset views let a painter show off the sky, and Chambers's strong, almost theatrical color suited them. The ensign signals nationality and gives such views a patriotic note, consistent with their popularity among Americans proud of their rising port.","c":"Chambers's marine views are best judged as color designs, and a sunset in New York Bay gave him a ready excuse for bold ones. The usual limits of his harbor pictures apply: stiff ships, schematic waves and little sense of atmosphere. Their strengths are equally constant, in the warmth of the sky and the confident, decorative pattern. A cheerful, popular type of picture."},"nga:54128":{"d":"The view is taken from the water. Dark, billowing gray clouds mass above the horizon against a pale orange sky, and large sailboats ride the dark water with some smaller rowboats and a few pieces of driftwood. The water reflects the ships and the colors of the sky. At the left is a low stone building on a bit of green land, and at the right a green hill covered with houses and small buildings rises in the distance. The canvas measures about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The low stone building at the left is presumably Castle Williams, the round sandstone fort on Governors Island that helped guard the entrance to New York harbor and appears in several of Chambers's views. A threatening sky, with the storm still to come, was a favorite device for giving a routine harbor scene a hint of drama, and it let the painter play dark clouds against a glowing horizon.","c":"The dark cloud mass against pale orange is the best thing here, a strong, simple contrast that sets the mood. The boats are neatly drawn and the reflections tidy, but the landmarks are tiny and the storm is more announced than felt. A competent harbor view whose title does more work than the sky."},"nga:56728":{"d":"Several sailing ships are at sea in a scene viewed from just above the waterline, with sea, sky and land meeting at the horizon. The nearest ship has billowing sails that give a sense of motion, and the deliberate, smooth brushwork defines sails and water. A warm, colorful sky of pastel hues blends into blues and grays. On the right an American flag flies from a fortification, and other ships recede into the distance. The canvas measures about 56 by 76 centimeters.","i":"A pilot boat was a fast schooner that sailed out to meet incoming ships and put a harbor pilot aboard to guide them into port, and New York's pilot boats were famous for their speed and seamanship. The title names this one as the George Washington. The flag over the fortification and the other vessels tie the boat to the busy commercial harbor it served.","c":"The spreading sails and the warm sky give the picture an upbeat energy, and the flag is a clear patriotic accent. Chambers is at his best with such formal, high-keyed views; he is at his weakest in the hulls and rigging, which are drawn with more pattern than structure. A cheerful tribute to American shipping, attractive in color, light on technique."},"nga:59897":{"d":"The museum gives no written description of this picture. Its record lists sailing ships on a wind-ruffled sea under clouds, with the United States flag and an ensign flying, set in Boston Harbor. It is a canvas of about 56 by 77 centimeters, the same size as many of Chambers's harbor scenes, and, as with its companions, the subject is the busy port seen from the water. Details of the vessels are not recorded.","i":"Boston was one of the busiest ports of the young United States, and its harbor, dotted with islands, was a standard subject for marine painters. National flags and ensigns identify the ships' origins and give such views a note of civic and national pride. Chambers painted Boston and New York harbors for buyers who liked to see their port's trade celebrated.","c":"Chambers's harbor pictures are best taken as cheerful color arrangements: bright flags, patterned waves and a high-keyed sky. Their limits are consistent too, with hulls and rigging drawn more as pattern than as structure and little sense of weather. This is a straightforward member of a likable series, attractive in its flags and color without being searching."},"nga:59898":{"d":"The museum offers no written description of this picture. Its record lists a sailing ship on wind-ruffled water with clouds and an ensign, set in New York Harbor and passing Castle Williams, the round sandstone fort on Governors Island. The canvas measures about 57 by 76 centimeters. The title identifies the vessel as a packet ship, the type of ship that sailed on a fixed schedule.","i":"Packet ships carried mail, passengers and cargo between New York and Liverpool or Le Havre on fixed sailing days, a system begun by the Black Ball Line in 1818. They were the pride of the port and a regular sight at its fortified entrance. Passing a fort such as Castle Williams, completed in 1811, a ship becomes a symbol of both commerce and defense.","c":"A packet ship under sail past a fort is an economical emblem of American confidence in the era, and Chambers's bold color and simplified forms suit it. The risk is the usual one: the ship is a pattern and the waves are a decoration, so the picture states its pride without much feeling for weather or water. Likable, but more heraldry than seamanship."},"nga:214111":{"d":"A crystal vase filled with a bouquet of flowers stands against a dark brown background. The flowers are red, pink, yellow, white, peach and dark blue, with green leaves and stems among them. The vase rests on a gray surface, where some fallen yellow petals lie scattered at the right. The oil on canvas, about 42 by 33 centimeters, is by the American painter William Perkins Babcock, and the date is given only as the 1850s.","i":"Babcock, a Bostonian who settled in France and knew the Barbizon painters, brings the French taste for a dark ground and glowing color to a subject with Dutch roots. The cut-glass vase and the fallen petals recall the old still-life tradition, in which glass sparkles and flowers drop as a quiet reminder that beauty fades. Bouquets of this kind were popular domestic pictures in the mid-nineteenth century, easy to place in a parlor or dining room.","c":"Against a dark brown ground the bouquet's reds, pinks and whites glow, and the cut glass gives the painter a chance to show reflected light and transparency. The scattered petals are a gentle touch of transience. The painting is competent and appealing rather than daring, in the tradition of Dutch and French bouquet pictures, and it shows an American painter at ease with the European manner."},"met:10082":{"d":"Benjamin Franklin is shown in profile in a small oil on wood, about 31 by 20 centimeters, painted around 1876 by William P. Babcock. The record lists the sitter, a man, a portrait and the profile view, and nothing more of Franklin's dress or the ground. A small, upright panel in profile suggests a likeness made after an earlier image, such as a medal or bust, rather than from life, since Franklin died in 1790, long before Babcock was born.","i":"In 1876, the centennial of the Declaration of Independence, portraits of the founders were in demand, and Franklin, who served as American envoy in France and was hugely popular there, was a natural choice for an American painter based in France. The profile view recalls the medals and medallions made of him in the 1770s and 1780s, which made his face familiar across Europe. The picture treats him as a national emblem more than as an individual sitter.","c":"A profile portrait after a standard image is an exercise in tact: the face must read at once, and the painter's freedom is limited to touch and color. Babcock painted alongside the Barbizon artists, and his handling is likely to be softer than the stiff centennial likenesses. The picture is best valued as a small, affectionate homage and as a document of how Americans abroad remembered Franklin, not as a likeness with new insight."},"met:10083":{"d":"A very small portrait of Benjamin Franklin, in oil on wood, about 13 by 11 centimeters, by William P. Babcock, dated about 1870-80 with a question mark. The record lists only the sitter and the portrait, so his pose, dress and the background are not described here. The size is that of a cabinet-sized study or keepsake, closer to a sketch than to a finished presentation portrait, and it is one of three small Babcock panels, two of Franklin and one of Lafayette, given to the Met by William H. Huntington in 1883.","i":"Franklin's image was among the most reproduced of the eighteenth century, in paintings, prints, medals and even on snuffboxes and porcelain, and his fur cap and plain dress became a trademark in Paris, where he was celebrated as a simple American philosopher. A nineteenth-century painter making small versions, as here, belonged to a long tradition of keeping Franklin's face before the public. The date is uncertain, as the record's question mark shows.","c":"At 13 centimeters the panel is an intimate, spontaneous-looking object, and the interest of such small paintings is often in the freedom of touch that a painter allows himself away from a formal commission. Babcock's handling is soft and tonal, and the likeness depends on the familiarity of Franklin's face, which does much of the work. The picture is slight but pleasing, and best viewed together with its companions in the Met's Babcock group."},"met:10084":{"d":"The Marquis de Lafayette is shown in profile in a very small oil on wood, about 13 by 15 centimeters, painted by William P. Babcock between 1850 and 1880. The record lists the sitter, a man and a portrait in profile, with nothing more about his dress or the ground. The wide date range reflects uncertainty about when the panel was made. Lafayette died in 1834, so the likeness cannot have been taken from life by an artist born in 1826.","i":"Lafayette, the French aristocrat who fought alongside Washington and became a hero of American independence, was celebrated in the United States long after his death, especially after his triumphal tour of 1824-25. For an American painter living in France, a profile of Lafayette was a link between the two countries, like Franklin's. A profile suits a commemorative image, since it recalls coins, medals and busts, and gives the sitter the dignity of a monument rather than the immediacy of a living man.","c":"The profile reduces the face to outline and tone, so the success of the picture depends on the clarity of the head and the restraint of the paint. Babcock was a sensitive tonal painter, and a small panel like this is more tribute than portrait, a quiet exercise in remembrance rather than characterization. Its slightness is its limit, and its modesty is its charm; it is of interest chiefly as part of Babcock's small group of founding portraits."},"met:11433":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as William Popham, painted around 1845 by George Linen, in oil on canvas, about 31 by 25 centimeters, a small size for a head-and-shoulders likeness. The record lists only a man and a portrait, so his dress, pose and expression are not described here. Linen (1802-1888) was a professional portraitist of the mid-century, working in the years when the painted likeness was beginning to compete with the daguerreotype.","i":"By 1845 the daguerreotype had been in use for six years, but a painted portrait still carried a prestige that a photograph could not match, and a small oil like this served middle-class families who wanted a durable, colored likeness at moderate cost. Nothing in the record says who William Popham was or what he did, and the picture was given to the museum in 1897 by the dealer Samuel P. Avery. It is a likeness of a private man, remembered chiefly by his name.","c":"A small bust-length of 1845 is a test of drawing and likeness, and Linen's job is to keep the head firm, the dark clothes simple and the surface neat. The type has a plain virtue: it asks nothing of the viewer but attention to a face. The limit is the conventions of the day, which allow little individuality. As a competent, unadorned mid-century portrait it is respectable, not memorable."},"met:435624":{"d":"A small portrait of a man, painted in oil on a copper plate and dated 1627, by the Dutch artist David Baudringhien. The sitter is a man whose name is unknown. Plates of this kind were generally small, often hardly larger than a book page. Copper, with its smooth, hard surface, was favored in the Netherlands for small, finely detailed portraits and cabinet pictures.","i":"Dutch portraits of the 1620s recorded the prosperous burghers of the young Republic, who wanted a sober likeness in dark clothing with a white collar, and portraits on copper were small enough to be kept in a private room or carried. The sitter is unnamed. The type was a modest, intimate one, closer to a miniature than to a grand commissioned portrait.","c":"Copper rewards exactness, and the small portraits on it by minor Dutch masters like Baudringhien rely on clean contour, precise detail of lace and collar, and a sober likeness. The date and support place it in the tradition of the Dutch cabinet portrait. A modest but attractive document of Republican burgher dress and manner."},"met:435625":{"d":"A small portrait of a woman, painted in oil on a copper plate by the Dutch artist David Baudringhien, who lived from 1581 to 1650. The sitter is a woman whose name is unknown, and the portrait has no firm date, so it can be placed only within the painter's lifetime. Copper, smooth and hard, took fine detail, which suited portraits of lace collars and caps.","i":"Dutch portraits of women in the first half of the seventeenth century usually showed a sober dress of black silk with a white ruff or collar and a cap, signs of piety and prosperity, and portraits on copper were small and precious. The sitter here is unnamed, and without a firm date the portrait cannot be tied to a particular style of ruff or cap. It may once have been a pendant to a portrait of her husband.","c":"Like others of its type, this is a minor Dutch cabinet portrait whose appeal depends on exactness of detail and the sitter's quiet dignity. It can be judged only by type: competent, polite and restrained, as befitted the genre. It is worth seeing for its small scale and for the material, copper, that gives such pictures their lasting, jewel-like finish."},"nga:30218":{"d":"A woman sits on a chair, turned slightly to the right, looking down at a book she holds. Her short, wavy brown hair and bright pink lips are visible, but most of her features are unclear. She wears an ornate pink dress with ruffles down the skirt, a high collar and slightly puffed sleeves. The wall behind her is reddish-brown and the floor light gray, and the brushwork is thick and visible. The tiny oil on wood, about 24 by 14 centimeters, was painted around 1890-94 by Beatrix Whistler, wife of James McNeill Whistler.","i":"The pink gown, which may explain the title, and the figure absorbed in a book belong to the intimate, decorative interiors of the Aesthetic Movement, where a woman in a beautiful dress, quietly occupied, was a favorite subject. Beatrix Whistler, born Beatrice Philip, was the daughter of the sculptor John Birnie Philip and the widow of the architect and designer E. W. Godwin, a friend of Whistler's, before she married James McNeill Whistler in 1888. Who the sitter is is not stated.","c":"A tiny panel like this is closer in spirit to Whistler's own small, tonal oils than to a conventional portrait: a limited range of color, a few thick strokes and an unemphatic, partly dissolved face. The most successful note is the pink dress against the warm wall and gray floor, a color harmony in the Aesthetic manner. The sketchiness makes the picture slight, but it shows a real talent and a style learned from her husband's."},"nga:41596":{"d":"A bearded man kneels with hands pressed together before a bishop who raises his hand in blessing, in front of a church and a crowd. The bishop wears a tall white mitre edged with gold and jewels and a gold brocade robe, with four clergymen behind him, one holding a crozier. About fifteen men, women and children line up in a curving file across the square, and in the middle distance two men support a third lying on the ground, who stares at a green demon hovering overhead. The oil panel is about 62 by 47 centimeters.","i":"The title speaks of episodes because several moments of the saint's life share one picture: the petitioner who kneels for a blessing, the long line of those waiting, and the miracle of a possessed man freed from a demon. Such cures were the stock proof of a bishop's sanctity in medieval saints' lives. The record does not name the saint; a similar panel of the same hand and date, the Baptism of Clovis, suggests he may be Remigius of Reims, but that is a possibility, not a finding.","c":"The crowd is the picture's strength: each figure is distinct, colors pass in a lively chain from pink to mustard to green, and the demon is a small, vivid piece of theater that holds the eye. The narrative is told with a medieval love of simultaneous episodes, a style that can seem crowded to modern eyes. It is a delightful, finely painted panel, more attractive for its inventive detail than for any depth of feeling."},"nga:41597":{"d":"A nude man, Clovis, stands in a hip-high stone basin with his hands together in prayer while a bishop in a red tunic and brocaded cloak tips a golden bowl of liquid onto his head. Around the basin stand about ten people, among them a kneeling clerk with a thick clasped book, a woman in a gold veil and two men in fur-trimmed cloaks who touch the king's arm. Five men watch from a balcony with organ pipes, and a crowd gathers in the courtyard, where a peacock perches on an arch. The oil panel is about 62 by 46 centimeters.","i":"Clovis, king of the Franks, was baptized around 496 by Saint Remigius, bishop of Reims, in an event that French tradition treated as the birth of Christian France; the holy oil poured here recalls the anointing oil later used to crown French kings at Reims. Painters around 1500 set the scene in a contemporary church and dressed the figures in the fashions of their own day, so that the old story spoke to present rulers.","c":"The picture is a model of late Gothic narrative: richly brocaded robes, a lively crowd and architectural detail, all described with enamel-like clarity. The liveliest passages are the crowd of onlookers and the lavishly patterned bishop's cloak, while Clovis himself, a pale nude figure amid so much cloth, is the quiet center. The Master of Saint Giles is a brilliant describer rather than a deep psychologist; the drama is of display, and the pleasure is in looking."},"met:436862":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of an unnamed man, painted around 1790 by Jeanne Philiberte Ledoux (1767-1840), a French woman artist. The record gives no dimensions, name, dress or pose, so none is described here. At this date the usual format was a small oval plaque of a few centimeters, set in a frame or locket and painted in thin watercolor and gouache so that the white ivory glows through the flesh tones.","i":"In the years around the Revolution a portrait miniature was a private object, given between friends and family or worn as a keepsake, and women found a respected place among miniaturists. Ledoux, born in 1767, would have been in her early twenties, building a career at the moment when the old order was ending. Her sitter's name is not recorded, so no claim about his station can be made.","c":"A miniature of about 1790 asks for delicacy and likeness, and Ledoux, who is said to have studied with Greuze, would have absorbed a warm, direct way of rendering a face. The limits of the form are the limits of flattery and a small, polite range of expression. Without a name for the sitter, the picture is best valued as a competent work by a woman painter who built a long career in a field dominated by men."},"met:436537":{"d":"A small gold-ground panel, about 26 by 16.5 centimeters, shows the Madonna and Child together with the scenes of the Annunciation and the Nativity, painted in tempera on wood around 1310-15 by the Goodhart Ducciesque Master. The name means an anonymous follower of Duccio, the great Sienese painter, and the panel's tiny size suggests a portable object for private prayer. The record does not describe how the three subjects are arranged.","i":"The three subjects tell one story: the Annunciation announces that Mary will bear the Son of God, the Nativity shows his birth, and the Madonna and Child present the result for worship. Duccio and his followers made such small panels for private devotion, in which the owner could kneel before the Virgin and recall the beginning of salvation. The gold ground removes the story from time and place, so that the holy figures seem to stand in an eternal present.","c":"Duccio's followers inherited a language of soft, tender faces, graceful line and rich surface, and a panel of this size is a jewel more than a statement. The Goodhart Master is not Duccio, and the invention is derivative, but the finish is delicate and the tenderness is of the kind Sienese Madonnas do best. It is a refined, small-scale testimony to Duccio's influence, charming rather than original."},"nga:195807":{"d":"Notre-Dame rises in the central distance, its silhouette soft against a gray and blue sky, beyond a stone bridge crowded with tiny figures, horses and carts. At lower left a group stands by a staircase leading up to the bridge, and a cart and horse wait at the water's edge at right. The scene is painted in grays with touches of seafoam green and rosy pink, the people are only dots and dashes, and trees in green, red and ochre line the far street. The thick, fluid brushwork gives a hazy look. Robert Vonnoh's oil is about 76 by 91 centimeters, c. 1890.","i":"Vonnoh, an American trained in Paris at the Académie Julian, lived in France in the late 1880s, when the Impressionists' method of quick touches and misty light was spreading among foreign students. A view of Notre-Dame from the river was a Paris cliché, but in the softened, hazy atmosphere it becomes a picture about weather and the river quay rather than about architecture. The crowded bridge shows ordinary life, with the cathedral as a familiar, quiet landmark.","c":"The picture's success is in its atmosphere: the cathedral dissolves into gray-blue air, and the bridge crowd becomes a shimmer of tiny strokes, a lesson well learned from Monet and his circle. The risk of such hazy Parisian views is sameness, and Vonnoh, a talented follower, may not yet have a distinct voice. Yet the soft pinks and greens give it freshness, and it is an appealing example of American Impressionism in Paris."},"met:816520":{"d":"Apples, grapes, other fruit and nuts are heaped together in a wide still life, about 76 by 128 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by Giuseppe Ruoppolo between about 1660 and 1669. The record names apples and grapes among the fruit, and the title adds nuts, but it does not describe the arrangement, the table or ledge, or the lighting. The wide format and generous scale are typical of the large decorative still lifes made in Naples in the seventeenth century for the houses of collectors and nobles.","i":"Neapolitan still lifes of fruit, often arranged in profusion against a dark ground, expressed the abundance of the southern Italian countryside and the wealth of the patrons who could fill a wall with it. Fruit and nuts also carried a moral reading: ripeness is brief, and the bloom on a grape soon fades. These associations were understood, though the record does not tell how they were meant here. Ruoppolo was one of a family of Neapolitan still-life painters.","c":"Neapolitan still life is about weight and sensuous plenty, and a wide canvas of fruit gives a painter room to vary shape, color and texture: glossy apples against dusty grapes, the dull shells of nuts. The risk of such abundance is clutter, so the success lies in the arrangement and in a clear, strong light. Ruoppolo is a skilled, if less famous, practitioner of a rich tradition, and the picture is best enjoyed for its generosity."},"met:15086":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1821 by William Hudson Jr., measuring about 11 by 9 centimeters, large for the type. The record lists only a man and a portrait, so his name, dress and pose are not described here. At this date American miniatures were commonly framed or cased under glass and handled like small jewels; the ivory's translucency lends the flesh a soft inner glow.","i":"In the 1820s, before photography, a miniature was the portable, intimate form of portrait for a family or a friend: given at marriage, kept to remember a relative or carried on journeys. The larger scale here suggests a framed keepsake for display on a table or in a case. A gentleman of 1821 would be shown in the sober dress of the day, but the record does not name him or his profession, so his place in society is unknown.","c":"A miniature of this size has room for more than a face: a painter like Hudson can describe the collar, the hair and the background, and the successful miniature balances likeness, delicacy of color and a restrained sense of character. Hudson's reputation is modest, and without a name for the sitter, the picture is best valued as a skilled, typical American miniature of the 1820s rather than an individualized portrait."},"nga:214113":{"d":"A heap of cherries lies on grassy ground, on a small wooden panel about 17 by 22 centimeters, light enough to hold in two hands. The fruits range through shades of red, orange and light yellow, and a few have rolled away into the grass. Green leaves and blades fill most of the scene, set against a dark green background, with small patches of tan earth showing at the very bottom. The picture is an oil, painted close up, with no table, vase or cloth: only fruit and the ground it rests on.","i":"Fruit set on the open ground, not on a table, is characteristic of some mid-nineteenth-century American painters influenced by John Ruskin, who urged artists to study nature humbly and in detail. Cherries signify summer's brief sweetness, and in older still life fruit could carry reminders of transience, though Victorian buyers more probably prized the picture for its freshness and craft. Small panels like this suited the modest scale of domestic walls.","c":"The pleasure here is in plain, unshowy observation: cherries differing in ripeness from deep red to pale yellow, strays scattered in the grass, and no arrangement beyond a casual mound. The restraint is also a limit, as there is little strong design or drama, and the dark green ground leaves little room to breathe. Its charm lies in being unpretentious, and in a candid sense of summer that grander still lifes often miss."},"met:437024":{"d":"The Virgin and the Christ Child appear with the infant Saint John the Baptist and angels, with a dove among the elements, in a panel of tempera and gold on wood, about 85 by 60 centimeters. The record gives no further description of the poses or the setting. The panel is attributed to the Pseudo-Pier Francesco Fiorentino, a conventional name for an anonymous Florentine workshop of the later fifteenth century that produced many devotional pictures of this type for private homes.","i":"The infant John the Baptist, shown as a child alongside Christ, was a favorite of Florentine devotion, because John was the city's patron saint; the pairing of the two children foretells the Baptist's later role in announcing Christ. Angels attend the Virgin in the manner of a heavenly court, and the dove is traditionally a sign of the Holy Spirit or of peace and purity. Pictures of this kind hung in the bedrooms and family rooms of well-off Florentines as aids to prayer.","c":"The workshop's Madonnas are sweet, balanced and elegant, with graceful line and warm tonality borrowed from the great Florentine masters of the generation before, and the formula was repeated often enough that individual versions rarely surprise. The strengths here are decorative: a soft, gold-brightened surface and tender faces. The weakness is routine, an invention borrowed rather than found. It is a charming, competent example of the Florentine devotional trade."},"nga:91389":{"d":"From a slightly raised viewpoint, five dishes and bowls crowd a wooden tabletop against a dark ground, brightly lit from the left. Eleven glistening oysters lie on a round iron-grey dish in the left foreground, and a white porcelain bowl painted with blue flowers, piled with bread and pastries, stands at the right beside two seashells. Behind are sugared almonds, raisins and dried fruit, brown figs, a stack of round boxes with quince paste, a goblet of amber liquid and a decanter of gold-coloured wine.","i":"Beert worked in Antwerp, where he was one of the first painters to specialise in banquet pieces, tables of delicacies set out for display rather than for any meal. The oysters, sugar, dried fruit and imported porcelain were costly luxuries, and the picture is usually read as a celebration of abundance and refinement. For some viewers the shells and wine also hinted at sensual pleasure and its brevity, though the painter gives no explicit warning. Such panels were bought by merchants and collectors.","c":"Beert arranges his delicacies with the calm of an inventory: every dish keeps its own space, and the light picks out the wet gleam of the oysters, the frost of the sugar and the glass with exact care. The strength is clarity and visual appetite. The limit is a certain stiffness: objects stand side by side rather than melting into atmosphere, and the dark ground leaves them isolated. It is a precise, handsome banquet piece."},"met:436314":{"d":"A portrait of a man in plumbago, the old name for graphite, on vellum, dated 1700 and drawn by Thomas Forster. The record gives no dimensions, name, dress or pose. A man of 1700 would normally have been drawn with the long, full wig and the cravat or neckcloth that were then the mark of a gentleman, so those are the details to expect, though the record does not confirm them. It is a small, delicate image in silvery graphite lines, made to be framed like a painted miniature.","i":"Around 1700 a man's wig was the chief sign of rank and fashion, long, curled and costly, and a portrait that showed it announced standing as clearly as a coat of arms. Plumbago portraits like this were small keepsakes for family and friends, cheaper than miniatures but equally personal. The sitter's name is not recorded, so his rank and trade are unknown. The Met holds a portrait of a woman of the same year by the same hand, though the record does not say that the two were a pair.","c":"The wig is a test for a draftsman in graphite, since masses of curls must be drawn as a pattern of lines without losing the head beneath. Forster's discipline in such details is the quality that counts; the limit is the formal stiffness that the fashion imposed on every sitter. It is a fine, small, slightly austere portrait whose interest is more historical than personal."},"met:436315":{"d":"A portrait of a woman drawn in plumbago, an old name for graphite, on vellum in 1700 by Thomas Forster. No dimensions, name, dress or pose are recorded, but the type is well known: a small, finely worked likeness in silvery gray lines, made to be framed like a miniature. Plumbago portraits were the specialty of a few English draftsmen around 1700, and Forster was among its best-known practitioners. The sitter is unnamed in the record, and the medium is monochrome.","i":"A plumbago portrait was a cheaper, quicker alternative to a painted miniature and offered a different pleasure: clean outline, delicate shading and a likeness without color. Around 1700 such drawings were made as keepsakes and were framed or kept in small cases. The woman's name and rank are not given, so any reading of her dress or standing would go beyond the record; her portrait survives as a typical image of a woman of William III's day.","c":"Plumbago allows little correction, and a good plumbago portrait is a display of confident drawing: the hair, the folds of dress and the face built up from fine lines and stipple. Forster's accuracy of likeness is the point, and the limit is the monochrome that gives such images a reserved, slightly formal air. As a small, precise portrait from around 1700, it is a pleasure of line more than of character."},"met:436316":{"d":"A woman's portrait in plumbago, an old name for graphite, on vellum, dated 1701 and drawn by Thomas Forster, a year later than another portrait of a woman by him in the Met. The record gives no dimensions, name, dress or pose. The medium produces a small, precise image in silver-gray lines, made to be framed like a miniature. The accession number, 44.36.1, shows that the drawing belongs to a group of Forster portraits that the museum acquired together in 1944.","i":"Women's portraits around 1700 emphasized hair and dress, the elaborate coiffures and low necklines of the late Stuart court, which a plumbago draftsman could describe with great delicacy. Such drawings were gifts and keepsakes, and a woman might give her likeness to a husband, a suitor or a friend. The record does not name her or describe her dress, so nothing is known of her rank; she survives as an anonymous image of a woman of her time, fixed in fine graphite lines.","c":"Forster's strength is accurate, patient line, and a woman's portrait lets him add softness: smooth modeling of the face, fine hair, the flow of drapery. The limits are the formula of the age, a standard pose and a polite expression, and the near-monochrome that keeps the sitter at a courteous distance. Together with its companions it shows how consistent his output was; as a single picture it is graceful rather than revealing."},"met:436317":{"d":"A portrait of a man in plumbago, an old name for graphite, on vellum, dated 1705 and drawn by Thomas Forster, five years after the Met's portraits of 1700 by the same hand. The record gives no dimensions, name, dress or pose. The accession number, 44.36.4, places it with the other Forster portraits that the Metropolitan Museum acquired in 1944. As with the others, the small scale and silvery gray line suggest a keepsake meant to be framed and held close.","i":"By 1705 Queen Anne was on the throne and the long periwig was still the mark of a gentleman; a portrait of this kind would show the sitter at his best, in a neat wig and a neckcloth. The sitter is unnamed, and the record gives no occupation or rank. What the drawing keeps is a face from the first years of the eighteenth century, taken in graphite and meant to be remembered by those who knew him.","c":"Plumbago asks for patience, and Forster's technique is steady and clean, with the face carefully modeled and the dress and hair suggested with a sure but economical line. The weakness is sameness: the portraits of 1700 and 1705 look much alike, and the format leaves little room for personality. Valued as a small, precise record of a face from Queen Anne's early reign, it is a quiet, accomplished piece of English draftsmanship."},"met:10590":{"d":"Men, women and children skate on a frozen creek among trees in a wide oil on canvas, about 61 by 95 centimeters, painted in 1875 by Johan Mengels Culverhouse. The title names the Wissahickon, the wooded stream that runs through Philadelphia's Fairmount Park to the Schuylkill River. The record lists trees, a woman, a man, a child and ice skating, and does not describe the light, the hour or the number of skaters, so these are left to the viewer.","i":"Skating was a favorite winter pleasure of Victorian America, one of the few outdoor amusements open to women as well as men, and the Wissahickon's wooded valley was a favorite retreat of Philadelphians for walking, riding and winter sport. The year 1875 was also the eve of the Centennial Exhibition in Fairmount Park, when the city's parkland was promoted as a national attraction. A scene of families skating presents the park as a place of healthy, respectable leisure.","c":"Culverhouse's skating scene has the charm of a documentary: a believable crowd, a winter place and the lively detail of figures sliding across ice. The risk with such pictures is a cheerful sameness, as if all skaters were drawn from one pattern, and the painter is a competent hand, not a strong personality. It is of interest as a record of Victorian leisure in a park that was becoming a national symbol, valued more for its subject than its brushwork."},"met:436879":{"d":"A crowd fills an auction room in Clinton Hall, a New York hall used for sales, in a wide canvas of about 57 by 80 centimeters dated 1876. The record lists an interior with stairs, a crowd, men and women, and does not describe the lots on view, the auctioneer or the bidders, so these are left to the viewer. The Spanish painter Ignacio de León y Escosura, who gave the picture to the Metropolitan Museum himself in 1883, painted it as a genre scene of modern city life.","i":"Auctions were social theater in the Gilded Age: collectors, dealers, speculators and the curious crowded in, and a major art sale was reported in the newspapers as an event. Painters of the 1870s, especially those trained in the Paris tradition of detailed contemporary scenes, found in such crowds an ideal subject: many types, varied dress and a shared excitement. The record does not say which sale is shown, so the particular lots and buyers are unknown.","c":"Escosura worked in the tradition of precise, anecdotal genre associated with Meissonier and Fortuny, and a crowded room gives him scope for dozens of small, characterful figures. The danger is a dry, catalog-like finish, with too many details demanding equal attention. As a document of New York in 1876 it is lively and informative, and as a painting it is capable rather than inspired."},"met:437280":{"d":"A family group in an interior is the subject of this horizontal canvas of about 75 by 94 centimeters, dated 1732. The record lists a woman, a man, a girl, an interior, a family and a playing card, so the group is probably engaged in a genteel pastime such as cards. The museum gives no further description of their poses, costumes or the room, and names no figures beyond a man, a woman and a girl.","i":"The conversation piece, a small-scale group portrait of friends or a family in an informal domestic setting, was a fashion in Britain from the 1720s, practiced by Hogarth, Charles Philips and others. It allowed the sitters to show off their possessions, manners and relationships, and card games signaled leisure and sociability. The Strongs, known here only by their name, are among many English families recorded in this way.","c":"Conversation pieces depend on the smooth management of small figures in a room, and Philips, a skilled specialist, would have kept the arrangement graceful and the faces recognizable. The risk of the type is stiffness: figures arranged like dolls at a card table. The best of these pictures reward looking for their little domestic details, and as a window onto the 1730s the group is more valuable than most single portraits."},"nga:231677":{"d":"A rustic tavern interior is crowded with still life: ceramic pots and bowls on a wooden table and, in the foreground, cabbages, carrots and onions. On the left several people sit at a table. In the center a man with his arm around a woman stands before a stone archway among more pottery, and in the background another figure can be seen through a doorway. The palette is earthy and warm, built on browns, beiges and muted greens. It is a small oil on a wooden panel, about 33 by 45 centimeters.","i":"Dutch tavern scenes entertained buyers with the pleasures and follies of low life: drink, flirtation, music and cards. The standing couple is probably the center of the joke, and a moralizing viewer might read the embrace as a warning against lust and idleness, though many buyers enjoyed such scenes simply as lively genre. The kitchen-like foreground of pots and vegetables is a reminder that the genre overlapped with the still life and the kitchen piece, popular in the same decades.","c":"Buesem is a minor master, and this panel reads as a pleasantly competent example of a well-worn type. The foreground pots and vegetables claim the most attention, set against a loose, cheerful social scene; a viewer should not expect the psychological penetration of Ostade or the wit of Jan Steen. As a small, warm-toned period piece it has charm."},"met:437788":{"d":"Gaius Marius rides in triumph in a chariot drawn by horses, with the captured African king Jugurtha among the figures, in a vertical canvas nearly 5.6 meters high and 3.3 wide. Giovanni Battista Tiepolo painted it in 1729. The record lists a chariot, horses, men, a woman and the two named figures, Marius and Jugurtha, and gives no further description of poses or setting, so these are left to the viewer. The size shows that it was made for the wall of a palace room, not for a cabinet.","i":"Marius, the Roman general of humble origin who held the consulship seven times, celebrated his triumph in 104 BC after defeating Jugurtha, the Numidian king who had defied Rome for years; Jugurtha was paraded as a captive. The canvas is one of a group of Roman histories that Tiepolo painted in the 1720s for a Venetian palace, where the city's patrician owners saw in Rome's triumphs a model of civic glory, and where such pictures turned a room into a stage for ancient virtue.","c":"Even at thirty-three, Tiepolo handles a huge surface with command. His Roman histories are staged like theater, with figures typically ranged on steps and platforms against bright air, and the color warm and clear. The risk is that history becomes pageant, with little feeling for the captive's fate; the pleasure is the sheer ease of invention. It shows the grand Venetian manner already formed, and ready for the ceilings that would make his name."},"met:437794":{"d":"A battle scene on a vast scale, about 4.1 by 3.8 meters, shows soldiers, horsemen and the dead, with the record's keywords battle, equestrianism and death. The title identifies it as the Battle of Vercellae, the clash of 101 BC in which a Roman army under Marius and Catulus destroyed the Germanic Cimbri in northern Italy. Giovanni Battista Tiepolo painted it between 1725 and 1729; no further description is given, so the placement of the figures, the banners and the sky is left to the viewer.","i":"The victory at Vercellae ended the Cimbri threat to Italy and made Marius the savior of Rome, a story told by Plutarch and loved by Renaissance and Baroque painters as a pretext for dynamic battle scenes. For a Venetian patrician, battles of ancient Rome were also a way to praise a modern family's virtue and valor. This canvas and its companions were conceived for a palace room, where they were seen at some height and distance, which explains their large scale and strong gestures.","c":"A battle is Tiepolo's chance for diagonal energy: twisting horses, flung arms and fallen bodies make a mass of movement against a luminous sky. The surface is a lesson in his lightness of touch even in bloodshed; the picture's weakness is that its violence is theatrical, a pageant of death more than its grief. It is a splendid decorative performance that reveals the young painter's ambition, though it lacks the radiance of his later ceilings."},"met:437795":{"d":"Soldiers on horseback and on foot, men and a woman are caught in the fighting at the capture of Carthage, in a canvas of about 4.1 by 3.8 meters painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo between 1725 and 1729. The title names the Roman conquest of the city, Rome's rival in North Africa, and the record lists battle, soldiers, men, a woman and horsemanship without describing the composition. As a companion in size and date to the Battle of Vercellae, it was made for the wall of a palace room.","i":"Carthage, the Phoenician city that fought Rome in three wars, fell in 146 BC after a siege led by Scipio Aemilianus, and the city was burned; New Carthage in Spain had fallen earlier, in 209 BC, to Scipio Africanus. The record does not say which capture Tiepolo shows. The presence of a woman suggests the captives and civilians of a conquered city, a theme that gave painters of Roman history a chance to show mercy as well as conquest.","c":"Tiepolo turns a conquest into theater: strong diagonals, a crowd of fighters and onlookers and a bright, clear atmosphere. The woman and the civilians introduce a human note that a battle scene alone would lack, and the open air keeps the violence elegant. As a pair with the Vercellae canvas it shows him at the height of his early ambition; the drama is grand rather than deeply felt, and the pleasure is of brilliant organization."},"nga:46141":{"d":"Zenobia, the warrior queen of Palmyra, addresses her troops in a wide canvas of about 2.6 by 3.7 meters painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo between 1725 and 1730. The title tells the subject, a leader rallying her army, and the record gives no further description of the figures, the setting or the colors, so these are left to the viewer. The size is that of a decorative history painting made for the wall of a palace room, and it belongs with Tiepolo's Roman histories of these years.","i":"Zenobia ruled Palmyra, the caravan city in the Syrian desert, in the third century AD, rebelled against Rome and for a time held Egypt and much of the East before the emperor Aurelian defeated her and took her to Rome. In early modern Europe she was a favorite heroine, admired for courage and learning, and patrons liked her as an example of a woman ruler. The image of a queen speaking to her men evokes leadership and eloquence; how the scene is staged is the painter's invention.","c":"Tiepolo gives a woman the commanding center of a heroic scene, and a wide canvas is the sort of stage he handles easily. The painting has the confident ease that made him Venice's leading decorator, but heroic rhetoric can feel more theatrical than felt. It is an elegant piece of historical theater whose pleasure lies in the performance."},"cma:124796":{"d":"Saint Sebastian is bound to a tree and shot with arrows, flooded with radiant light, in a quick, sure sketch about 52 by 32 centimeters in oil on canvas. This is a modello, Tiepolo's first design for the altarpiece of 1739 at the Augustinian monastery church at Diessen in southern Germany, which he submitted for approval before beginning the final work. Its pair there was an altarpiece by his fellow Venetian Giambattista Pittoni, part of a program of architecture, painting, sculpture and stucco.","i":"Sebastian, a Roman soldier shot with arrows for his Christian faith, was invoked against plague, and his bound, nearly nude body pierced by arrows was a favorite subject because it joined suffering with ideal beauty. The radiant light in the sketch symbolizes his devotion and the glory awaiting him. A modello of this kind let a patron see and approve the composition before the huge altar was begun; here the Augustinian canons would have been the judges.","c":"A sketch like this is often more exciting than the finished altarpiece: the quick, assured strokes show Tiepolo thinking in light and gesture, and the radiant light and the bound figure make a clear, concentrated image. What the final work gains in finish, the sketch keeps in energy. It is a superb working document and a pleasure in its own right, and it shows why Tiepolo's oil sketches are prized as much as his finished pictures."},"nga:43610":{"d":"Saint Roch, seated on a cloud with his head tilted back and his arms raised, hands cupped, is lifted into the sky by three winged angels in billowing cloth, one of them perhaps a cherub. His garment is red and green; the angels have large blue and green wings. The background is blue sky streaked with cream and pink, and the whole is enclosed in a reddish-brown oval. The oil on canvas, about 41 by 34 centimeters, dates from about 1735-45 and is by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo.","i":"Roch, the French pilgrim of the fourteenth century who nursed plague victims in Italy and was said to have caught the disease himself, became a saint invoked against plague, and he was especially honored in Venice, where a great confraternity and church bear his name. Carrying him to heaven shows the reward of charity; the cupped hands suggest wonder or prayer. The oval format suggests that the picture was a design for a ceiling or the top of an altarpiece, though the record does not say.","c":"Tiepolo's sketches are where his speed and light show most: cloud, wing and cloth are swept into a single spiral of motion, and the airy blue sky does the rest. The oval is full of energy, and the figure's upward-turned, open-handed pose is conveyed with striking economy. As a small, free essay in his ceiling manner, the picture is a delight and a reminder that the Venetian grand style lived most freely in its sketches."},"nga:46060":{"d":"Bacchus sits astride a wine barrel at the center, crowned with grapevines and nude but for a claret cloth, a bowl in one hand while the other lifts a gold circlet set with tiny stars above Ariadne, who reclines beside him with wheat in her blond hair and flowers and wheat in the crook of her arm. Six putti crowd around, one drinking from a rope-wrapped bottle, one astride a cheetah, one with a tray. Imaginary cat-bodied creatures sit in the corners of a painted stone frame. Tiepolo's square oil, about 214 by 232 centimeters, dates from about 1743-45.","i":"Ariadne, abandoned on Naxos by Theseus, was found by the god Bacchus, who married her and set her bridal crown among the stars as a constellation; the starry circlet he holds above her is that crown. The grapevines, wine and wheat unite the gifts of Bacchus and Ceres, wine and bread, joy and plenty. For a Venetian house of the 1740s the story was an excuse for a luxurious, playful picture of love and festivity, with consolation after loss as its theme.","c":"Tiepolo's mythologies are Venetian in the best way: bodies soft and light, color pale and warm, the sky open and the mood amused rather than solemn. This is one of his most delightful, with the putti and the cheetah supplying comic invention and the whole a feast of pearly flesh and golden accents. If the allegory is slight and the figures decorative, the grace of the whole outweighs it. It is Rococo painting at its easiest and most radiant."},"met:438129":{"d":"The meeting of Mark Antony and Cleopatra is shown in a small oil on canvas, about 46 by 67 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo around 1745-47. The record lists Antony, Cleopatra, a flag and a dog, and does not describe the poses, the architecture or the colors. The size and quick-handled subject point to an oil sketch, a modello, made to plan a much larger work; it is generally linked to the fresco of the same subject that Tiepolo painted in the Palazzo Labia in Venice.","i":"Plutarch tells how Cleopatra, queen of Egypt, sailed to Tarsus in 41 BC to meet the Roman triumvir Mark Antony, arriving in a gilded barge with silken sails, dressed as Venus. It was a diplomatic meeting that became a love affair and a political alliance, later ending in defeat at Actium. Tiepolo, who painted the subject for a Venetian palace, turned the story into a scene of splendor and courtesy; the dog and flag are the supporting details of a historical pageant.","c":"As a sketch the picture has the freshness that finished frescoes lose: rapid strokes set figures in motion, and the whole is held together by light rather than detail. Tiepolo's Cleopatra is no tragic queen but an elegant Venetian lady, and the drama is of costume and gesture. It is a pleasing, brilliant essay, more attractive for its speed than its depth."},"met:438027":{"d":"A nude female allegorical figure is the subject of this oil on canvas, about 81 by 65 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo between 1740 and 1750 on what the record calls a gold ground. The record lists a nude and an allegory but does not say which idea she personifies, so her attributes, pose and setting are not described here. The gold ground makes this an unusual picture in Tiepolo's work, and it may have been decorative or experimental in purpose.","i":"Allegorical figures were the standard means by which Baroque and Rococo painters showed abstract ideas such as virtue, fame, wisdom or abundance, through a woman with identifying attributes. The female nude was an especially favored vehicle, since the body could stand for beauty and for the truth of the idea. Without her attributes the record leaves the specific meaning open; the picture is best read as a type, a lyrical personification set against a golden ground that gives it a precious, unreal light.","c":"Tiepolo's allegories are usually less about the idea than about the body and the light: luminous flesh, a few strokes of drapery and an airy setting do the work. The gold ground is an unusual note that adds richness and flatness together. As an elegant, quickly conceived figure it is a pleasure to look at, and it shows his gift for turning an abstract notion into a graceful, floating woman."},"nga:509":{"d":"Two women and an armored man nearly fill a vertical oval canvas, about 140 by 109 centimeters, before a pale yellow column and arch. At lower left a fair woman in a white gown sits on a moss-green cushion, eyes cast heavenward and lips parted, a gold belt looped over one shoulder. Behind her a crowned woman in a buttercup-yellow robe looks out at the viewer. At right, a man in scarlet armor, a lapis cloak and a helmet topped with a gold lion holds a sword and what looks like a lock of hair as he gazes down at the seated woman. Tiepolo painted it around 1750.","i":"The museum calls it simply a scene from ancient history because the story has not been securely named. A crowned woman, a seated woman who looks to heaven and a warrior holding a sword and a lock of hair might point to a tale of love, vengeance or sacrifice from Plutarch, Livy or Josephus, the sources Tiepolo and his patrons drew on, but any single identification remains a guess. The picture works as a type: costume, gesture and drama in an antique setting.","c":"The strengths are Tiepolo's usual ones: a rich, theatrical palette, the daring use of scarlet and lapis against pale yellow, and a vivid, slightly sensual play of glance and gesture among the three figures. The unresolved story is a weakness for the viewer, and the drama may be more decorative than moving. It is a beautiful example of Tiepolo's late grand manner, valued for color and staging more than narrative clarity."},"met:437790":{"d":"An oil sketch for a ceiling, painted as if the vault opened to the sky. A painted frame of stucco, with white sculpted figures sprawling in the corners, surrounds a pale blue and golden sky full of clouds. Near the centre Apollo, the sun god, glows with light as he prepares to set out in his chariot, whose dark and white horses rear at the right. Around him, gods of the planets and swarms of cherubs float on clouds in pinks, oranges and blues. The edges are crowded with figures in strong reds and blues, with an elephant and a horse among them, and a classical building at the bottom. Oil on canvas, about 185 by 139 centimetres, in an upright format.","i":"On 20 April 1752 Tiepolo presented this sketch to Carl Philipp von Greiffenklau, prince-bishop of Würzburg, as his proposal for the decoration of the ceiling over the grand staircase of the prince-bishop's new Residenz. The Met calls the resulting fresco the artist's greatest achievement and a landmark of European painting. The subject is Apollo beginning his daily journey across the sky, with deities around him standing for the planets and allegorical figures at the edges for Africa, the Americas, Asia and Europe. In the finished fresco, Würzburg and its rulers sit at the centre of the cosmos; the sketch shows the idea in miniature, for the patron's approval.","c":"As a sketch it is more spontaneous than the fresco could be, and it shows Tiepolo's gift at its purest: golden light, cloud edges that melt into air, and figures set at weightless angles. The pastel palette, from warm peach to cool blue, is airy and delightful. Yet the margins are busy beside the open centre, and the continents are stock exotic types rather than characters. As an exercise in cheerful illusion it is hard to beat, and as a sales pitch it plainly worked."},"met:437800":{"d":"A bishop is invested as a duke in a vertical oil sketch of about 72 by 51 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo around 1751-52. The record lists an arch, men, bishops and a musical instrument, and does not describe the figures' arrangement, the colors or the light. It is a modello, a preparatory sketch, for a fresco in the Kaisersaal of the prince-bishops' Residence at Würzburg, where Emperor Frederick Barbarossa was said to have invested Bishop Herold with the duchy of Franconia in the twelfth century.","i":"The Würzburg Residence was the palace of the prince-bishops, and the Kaisersaal, or Imperial Hall, told the story of the bishopric's glory: Barbarossa's wedding and the grant of the duchy, which gave the bishops princely rank. Tiepolo, summoned from Venice with his sons in 1750, painted the room and the great staircase ceiling in the following years. A modello showed the patron the design before the plaster was laid; the credit line, Purchase, 1871, marks it as one of the museum's earliest acquisitions.","c":"Tiepolo's Würzburg pageantry is a staged ceremony in light, brilliant paint, and the sketch keeps the spirit that the finished fresco, with its grandeur, partly replaces. It takes the story out of the Middle Ages and dresses it in Venetian splendor, which is the point. Not his deepest work, but one of his most buoyant, and the difference between sketch and fresco is the pleasure of watching a master think in paint."},"met:437798":{"d":"Putti, men and women float among clouds in a wide, low canvas about 2.4 by 4.7 meters in size, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo around 1750. The title and the record identify the figures as members of the Barbaro family, one of the old noble houses of Venice, raised into the sky in an allegory of family glory. The size and proportions are those of a ceiling decoration meant to be seen from below, and the record gives no further description of the individual figures, their dress or the colors.","i":"Venetian patrician families celebrated their lineages and virtues in ceiling paintings in which ancestors, allegorical figures and putti rise through a sunlit sky, a form of heraldry in paint. The Barbaro, who counted scholars, diplomats and patrons of architecture among their forebears, were among the oldest houses. Such a ceiling announced a family's standing to every guest who entered the room, and was often commissioned to mark a marriage or an alliance, though the record does not say what prompted this one.","c":"A ceiling must be read from below, at a distance, and Tiepolo is a master at making figures float in light against a pale sky: ascending figures, sparkling drapery and open space do the work. Hung flat on a museum wall, the picture loses its foreshortened effect and is best judged as a fragment of a decorative whole. In that context it is dazzling, flattering and a little empty, which is what family glorification wants."},"cma:97141":{"d":"A small, quickly painted oil sketch, about 41 by 34 centimeters, made by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in the 1750s as a study for a ceiling. The Cleveland Museum of Art notes that it is not linked to any of his finished decorations, so the project it was meant for is unknown. When such a design was carried out full scale, the ceiling opened like a vast window onto the sky, in which angels or mythological beings float among clouds. The record does not describe what this sketch shows, so no particular figures are named here.","i":"Tiepolo used sketches to work out design problems, to experiment with form and color, and to show clients how a finished project would look. A ceiling in the Venetian manner was a window onto heaven: figures were seen from below, foreshortened and lit from within, floating among clouds. Because this sketch belongs to no known commission, its subject remains unidentified, and it is best seen as a view into the process by which a large scheme was designed, with the help of a workshop that included two of his sons.","c":"A sketch is where a painter's hand is most readable, and Tiepolo's is brisk, light and sure: thin, quick strokes set clouds and bodies in motion with remarkable economy. The risk of any sketch is that it promises more than it delivers, and without a finished work to compare it with, the image is an idea rather than a statement. It is an engaging, spontaneous miniature of his ceiling manner, valuable chiefly as evidence of his method."},"met:437789":{"d":"The Magi adore the Christ Child while angels look on, in an oil sketch of about 60 by 48 centimeters painted in the late 1750s by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo. The record lists the Christ Child, the Adoration of the Magi, the Madonna and Child and angels, and gives no further description of the figures, the light or the setting. Tiepolo returned to the subject many times, notably in a large altarpiece of 1753 now in Munich, and a small canvas like this may be a design or a variant of that kind of composition.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew tells of Magi from the East who followed a star to Bethlehem, offering gold, frankincense and myrrh; later tradition made them three kings and gave them ages, from youth to old man, to represent the nations and stages of life paying homage to Christ. Tiepolo's Magi are usually Venetian in silks and turbans, and the exotic procession gave him a pretext for color and display. The angels add heavenly witnesses to the earthly scene.","c":"The subject was made for Tiepolo: costume, color and ceremony gathered around one tender center, the Child. A smaller version like this has the freshness of a sketch and a lightness of touch that the great altarpieces sometimes lose. What it cannot give is the grandeur of scale; what it keeps is the sparkle. It is a gracious, assured example of his late religious painting, more festive than devout."},"met:437803":{"d":"A woman saint prays above a scene of suffering in a tall, narrow oil sketch of about 81 by 45 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in 1758-59. The record lists a woman, men and women, angels, saints and God the Father, so a heavenly court above the saint looks down on the stricken below. It is a design for the large altarpiece Tiepolo painted for the cathedral of Este, near Padua, a town whose cathedral is dedicated to Saint Thecla.","i":"Saint Thecla, a follower of Saint Paul in the early Church, is honored as the first female martyr, and she is patron of Este, whose people turned to her in times of plague. Here she intercedes with God the Father for the sick, a classic image of the saint as a mediator between the afflicted and heaven. Plague was a recurrent fear in Italian cities well into the seventeenth century, and altarpieces of intercession remained a visible reminder of a town's deliverance.","c":"Tiepolo's late religious sketches show his gift for building a heavenly drama in a few lines: the narrow format drives the eye upward from the sufferers to the saint and the Father above. The sentiment is more grand than intimate, and the suffering below is a stage for the vision. A good example of his altarpiece manner, with the speed and fluency of the oil sketch, and a pleasure to look at, if less moving than it aims to be."},"met:437793":{"d":"A woman personifying Arithmetic stands in a tall, narrow panel nearly 3.7 meters high and 1.5 meters wide, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in 1760. It is a fresco that has been detached from its wall and transferred to canvas. The record lists a woman, sculpture and allegory, which suggests that the figure is painted to look like a statue; her attributes, which usually include a tablet or counting board marked with numbers, are not described in the record.","i":"Arithmetic was one of the seven liberal arts of the medieval and Renaissance curriculum, the art of number, and belonged with Geometry, Music and Astronomy among the four mathematical arts. Personified as a woman, she stood for learning and for the practical skill of counting on which Venetian trade depended. A set of such figures declared a household's interest in knowledge; she is one of several allegories of the arts and sciences that Tiepolo painted in 1760.","c":"In a tall, narrow panel the figure must make her effect through pose and drapery alone, and Tiepolo, who could make a lifelike figure float in air, here has to produce a convincing stone presence. The challenge suits his skill at suggesting volume with a few strokes of light. The allegory is conventional and the figure serves the architecture, but it is a graceful, confident piece of decorative fresco, with more charm than weight."},"met:437797":{"d":"A figure representing Geometry fills a tall panel nearly 3.7 meters high and 1.5 meters wide, painted in fresco by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in 1760 and later detached and transferred to canvas. The record lists a nude, sculpture and a fish, so the figure is nude and probably painted to resemble a statue; the fish is not among Geometry's usual attributes, which are the compass, the set square and diagrams, and it may be a decorative detail of the kind found on fountain statues.","i":"Geometry, the science of measure, was one of the liberal arts and the foundation of architecture, surveying and navigation, all of which mattered to Venice, a city built on water and ruled by engineers of the lagoon. Personified as a woman with compass and square, she appeared in libraries, schools and noble houses to show that the household valued reason and order. The nude treatment here, like a classical statue, gives the idea the dignity of antiquity.","c":"Tiepolo's gift for the figure is on display in a nude presented as a statue, the long, elegant lines of the body suiting the tall format. The risk of such decorative figures is that they are generic, standing for the idea rather than showing it, and a fish does not help. Even so, the draftsmanship and the flicker of light on the forms are those of a master at the end of his Italian career."},"met:437799":{"d":"Grammar appears as a woman in a tall panel nearly 3.7 meters high and 1.5 meters wide, painted in fresco by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in 1760 and later transferred to canvas. The record lists only a woman and gives no description of her pose or dress, so none is described here. Grammar's usual attributes are a book and a rod, the schoolmaster's tools for teaching letters and correcting errors, and she is often shown with children.","i":"Grammar, the first of the seven liberal arts, was the art of reading, writing and correct speech, and the foundation for all the other studies; medieval and Renaissance schoolbooks showed her teaching children or holding a rod. She belonged to the trivium, with Logic and Rhetoric. To include her in a decorative scheme was to honor learning in its most elementary and indispensable form, a compliment to a family that prized education.","c":"Grammar is the least glamorous of the arts, and Tiepolo's task is to give a figure of schoolroom authority the grace of a Venetian lady. The strength of the fresco is its large, calm design and easy handling; the weakness is the abstraction of the subject, which gives the painter little drama to work with. It is a fine, quiet decorative piece, valuable above all as part of a set."},"met:437801":{"d":"Metaphysics is personified in a tall panel nearly 3.7 meters high and 1.5 meters wide, painted in fresco by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in 1760 and later transferred to canvas. The record lists a nude, an allegory and sculpture, which suggests a nude figure painted to resemble a statue. Her attributes are not described. Because Metaphysics, the study of being beyond the physical world, has no natural object to hold, painters showed her with symbols of the heavens or of the mind.","i":"Metaphysics, the branch of philosophy that asks what being itself is, was not among the seven liberal arts but belonged to the higher study of philosophy and theology, and it was a favorite of the learned classes of the Enlightenment. Including her in a cycle of the arts and sciences told visitors that the house valued the highest, most abstract learning. How the ideas are signaled, by a gaze upward or an emblem, is not recorded, so the figure should be read as a type.","c":"Making an abstract science into a figure is a test of tact, and a nude presented as a statue is a graceful solution that gives the idea nobility without narrative. Tiepolo's economy of means is on show: broad, confident modeling and a clear light. The subject is too abstract to move the viewer; the delight is in the handling. It is a refined, light-handed late fresco."},"met:437804":{"d":"Two allegorical figures, Virtue and Abundance, appear with a putto in a fresco painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in 1760 and later transferred to canvas. The record gives no dimensions and lists nudes and a putto, so the figures are nude and accompanied by a child-like winged spirit. Virtue is often shown with a spear or a wreath, and Abundance with a cornucopia overflowing with fruit, but the record does not say which attributes appear here, so none is described.","i":"The pairing of Virtue and Abundance carried a simple moral and political message: prosperity follows good conduct. Virtue, the moral quality that earns honor, and Abundance, the material reward, were paired in decorations for houses and civic buildings to flatter an owner or a ruler as the source of both. The putto brings a note of play and fertility. A Venetian patron in 1760 could read the pair as a compliment to his family's merit and fortune.","c":"A pairing of idealized nudes and a putto gives Tiepolo what he did best: soft, luminous flesh, a quick, light line and a convincing play of light in an airy space. The allegory is stock and the meaning flattering, so the pleasure is in the painting itself and not in any new idea. A graceful late fresco fragment, more decorative than deeply considered, it shows how he could turn compliment into beauty."},"nga:41692":{"d":"A pale young woman looks out sideways from the opening of a black cloak that covers her shoulders, chest and part of her face, under a black tricorn hat set back on her head. Her face shows like a moon in the dark folds, with a glimpse of brown hair at her temple; a gossamer white glove emerges at her chin to hold a closed fan with coral-orange sticks and green, orange and cream fabric. The ground is a dark pine green. Giovanni Battista Tiepolo's oil on canvas, about 62 by 49 centimeters, dates from about 1755-60.","i":"The black cloak and three-cornered hat were the Venetian disguise worn at Carnival, which in the eighteenth century lasted for months, and on other occasions when anonymity was wanted; it let people of different ranks mingle in the city's gambling rooms, theaters and squares. The fan completes the costume's coquettish message. The sitter is unnamed; the picture may be a character study, a portrait or a bit of both, and the record does not decide.","c":"A study in black: the dark cloak, hat and green ground are separated by subtle changes of tone, and the white glove, the pale, moonlike face and the coral-orange fan are the few flashes that make the picture sparkle. The flirtatious sidelong glance tells the whole story in an instant. It is a modest-sized, brilliantly economical picture, one of Tiepolo's most intimate and a rare glimpse of Venice's social masquerade."},"nga:41693":{"d":"The Greek myth of Apollo and the nymph Daphne is the subject of this oil on canvas, about 69 by 87 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo around 1755-60. The record gives no description, so the poses, the landscape and the colors are not described here. The story, taken from Ovid's Metamorphoses, shows the god in pursuit of the nymph at the moment she is saved by being changed into a laurel tree, a moment painters could treat as a drama of speed and transformation.","i":"In Ovid's telling, Apollo, struck by Cupid's arrow, pursued Daphne, who had been struck by the opposite, repelling arrow; at the point of capture she called to her father, the river god Peneus, and was changed into a laurel. Apollo, in his grief, made the laurel his sacred tree, and its leaves crowned poets and victors ever after. The story was loved by painters as a metamorphosis in action: a woman's arms turning to branches. The moment of change is the heart of the tale.","c":"Apollo and Daphne is a subject for a painter of movement and light, and Tiepolo's late mythologies are at their best in such moments of grace and transformation. The risk is a prettiness that robs the story of its pathos; Tiepolo's characteristic remedy is speed and an airy palette. At this size and date it is a fluent, pleasing late canvas, a Rococo fable told lightly rather than a deep reading of Ovid's tragedy."},"nga:12137":{"d":"The viewer looks up through an opening ringed by a stone ledge into a blue sky where clusters of people and clouds swirl away. Two bearded, long-haired figures, painted to look like white stone, recline against gold medallions at the corners, framed by large shells. An obelisk, columns and a ship's mast rise into the air; some figures wear feather headdresses; a man restrains a rearing chestnut horse on the ledge, a nun with a cross floats above him, and far off a crowned figure sits on a throne. The oil, about 182 by 104 centimeters, is dated 1762 and sketchily painted.","i":"Tiepolo was called from Venice to Madrid by Charles III in 1762 to decorate the Royal Palace, where he worked until his death in 1770, and this is a design for a ceiling there. The feathered headdresses stand for Spain's American possessions, and ships, obelisks and columns evoke trade and power. In such allegories the monarchy appears as a bringer of wealth and benefits to its peoples, a flattering ideal that the figures in the clouds make visible.","c":"The sketch shows why Tiepolo was the most sought-after ceiling painter in Europe: from below, clouds, columns, horses and figures spiral into a believable sky, and the sketchy handling that looks rough up close is the secret of the illusion at a distance. The program is heavy with flattery, and the exotic detail is a European view of the colonies; the pleasure lies in the lightness with which a grand political message is made airy."},"nga:241":{"d":"A small winged cherub hovers in a swirling sky of soft blue and cloud, holding a round object, perhaps a crown or wreath, and a long thin one. Below, on reddish-brown ground, figures lie or sit, seen from the side and sketched so loosely that their features are indistinct: on the left a dark-bearded man and another cherub, on the right two women leaning toward each other. The brushstrokes are thick and visible. The oil on canvas, about 27 by 49 centimeters, is a study by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo, dated before about 1762, for a ceiling with the personification of Counsel.","i":"Counsel, the good judgment that guides rulers, was a favorite subject of ceilings in council chambers and princely rooms, where it flattered the men who met beneath it. A ceiling of this kind divides heaven from earth, with winged spirits above and mortals below, which is the arrangement the sketch seems to show. The wide, low format suggests a design for a long ceiling or a section of one; the record does not say which building the project was for.","c":"The thick, visible strokes show Tiepolo thinking aloud: a few dabs of paint set the cherub in swirling air and the mortals on the ground, and the whole is a proof of his command of space. A sketch like this is more appealing than many finished ceilings, because its vitality is intact. It is a slight object, a note to himself rather than a statement, and a lively one."},"met:437791":{"d":"The gods Apollo and Jupiter, with Jupiter's eagle, appear in this small oil sketch of about 84 by 69 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in the 1760s. The title names the subject as the Apotheosis of the Spanish Monarchy, and the record lists an eagle, Apollo and Jupiter, so the king's glory is imagined in the company of the gods. It is one of two sketches of this subject by Tiepolo in the Metropolitan Museum, probably both related to the Throne Room ceiling of the Royal Palace in Madrid. The record does not describe the composition further.","i":"Jupiter, king of the gods, with his eagle, stands for sovereign power, and Apollo, god of light and the arts, for enlightened rule; a monarchy that borrowed their company was claiming both force and civilization. The pairing was a common flattery of kings across Europe, and in Madrid it served to present Charles III as a ruler favored by Olympus. Two sketches of this theme in the Met show how Tiepolo tried out different designs before settling on the fresco.","c":"A sketch for Olympus is a pleasure for a painter of air: an eagle's wings, the gods' limbs and billowing cloud make an ideal subject for his quick, fluent touch. The program is less interesting than the paint: the gods are courtiers of a king, and Tiepolo plays them with Venetian elegance rather than Spanish severity. A lively, accomplished sketch, more ornament than statement, and best enjoyed beside its companion."},"met:437792":{"d":"A woman and putti appear amid a bright sky in a small oil sketch of about 82 by 66 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in the 1760s. The title names the subject as the Apotheosis of the Spanish Monarchy, a heavenly glorification of Spain's crown, and the record lists a woman, putti and sky, so the central figure is probably the personification of Spain or of the monarchy, though the record does not say. It is probably one of the designs for the ceiling of the Throne Room of the Royal Palace in Madrid.","i":"An apotheosis lifts a person or an idea into heaven, a form borrowed from ancient Rome, where emperors were declared gods. Charles III's Madrid palace used it to show the Spanish monarchy as a divinely favored power, with its virtues and dominions personified as figures in the clouds. Putti, symbols of blessing and fertility, accompany the central woman. A modello like this showed the king and his advisers the design before Tiepolo, in his late sixties, began the vast fresco.","c":"The sketch captures a Rococo ideal of the sky as a stage, with a few swift strokes lifting a woman and her putti into radiance. The allegory is stock and the ideology flattering, so the delight is in the lightness and the effortless grace of the floating figures. It is a lovely, spontaneous object, and the freedom of such sketches is exactly what the final fresco, with its demands of scale and etiquette, could not keep."},"met:437802":{"d":"Neptune, the sea god, appears with the winds, the gods Jupiter and Pluto and a putto in a small square oil sketch of about 62 by 62 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in the 1760s. The record lists Pluto, a putto, Neptune and Jupiter, and gives no description of the composition, the colors or the sea, so these are left to the viewer. The square shape and sketch-like scale suggest a design for a roundel, a ceiling section or a decorative panel.","i":"Neptune, Jupiter and Pluto were brothers who divided the cosmos among them after overthrowing their father Saturn: Jupiter took the sky, Neptune the sea and Pluto the underworld. Gathering them together suggests a picture of world order, and Neptune with the winds recalls the sea power on which a maritime empire depended. Many of Tiepolo's sketches of the 1760s were made for the Royal Palace at Madrid, but the record does not say that this one was.","c":"Tiepolo's mythological sketches are happiest when the subject is air and water, which suits Neptune and the winds, and the square format concentrates the energy of gods and putti into a small space. The weakness is the generic nature of the subject, which gives the painter a stock cast rather than a story. It is a slight object in scale but a delicate demonstration of what he could do with a few brushes, quickly."},"met:438026":{"d":"The goddess Aurora's chariot rides through the sky in this oil sketch of about 90 by 73 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo in the 1760s. The record lists horses, a chariot, a goddess, the gods Apollo and Bacchus and a swan, and gives no description of the composition, the colors or the light. In myth Aurora, the dawn, drives ahead of the sun god Apollo's chariot, so the gods in her train belong to a sunrise procession, and the vertical format suits a design for a ceiling or decorative panel.","i":"Aurora, who opens the sky each morning with rose-colored fingers, was a favorite of Baroque and Rococo ceiling painters, from Guido Reni's famous fresco in Rome to Tiepolo himself; her chariot carries the promise of light, renewal and the arrival of the sun. Bacchus and Apollo, gods of wine and of the arts, add the pleasures of civilized life to the dawn. A ceiling of this kind turned a room into the sky at daybreak, with guests below looking up at the procession.","c":"The subject suits Tiepolo perfectly: horses, gods and the flush of dawn are an opportunity for lightness, speed and warm, glowing color. A sketch like this has the sparkle of improvisation that the great ceilings often smooth away. The mythology is decorative rather than profound, and its pleasure is the effortless virtuosity of a master painting at ease with the sky."},"met:438128":{"d":"The Holy Family flees to Egypt in a small upright oil of about 60 by 41 centimeters, painted by Giovanni Battista Tiepolo around 1767-70. The record lists the Virgin and the infant Jesus, a donkey, an angel, a tree, mountains, a boat and birds, and does not describe the poses or the landscape, so how these elements are placed is left to the viewer. It is a late work, painted after Tiepolo had settled in Madrid, and it takes up a subject to which he returned many times.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew tells how an angel warned Joseph in a dream to take Mary and the child to Egypt to escape Herod's slaughter of the infants. The journey, with the donkey and a landscape of rest and fear, was a favorite subject because it combined tenderness and adventure. The boat and mountains suggest rivers crossed and wilderness passed, and an angel guides the family. The picture is also a gentle image of refuge, which spoke to viewers who knew the flight of exiles.","c":"Tiepolo's Holy Family is, as ever, a Venetian one: soft color, an airy landscape and gentle figures moving with a dancing lightness rather than pious gravity. The picture lacks the monumental power of his great altarpieces, but it has the intimacy of a late, small devotional piece, brushed with speed and tenderness. Its charm is its warmth, and its weakness a certain prettiness that skirts the story's menace."},"nga:101678":{"d":"A young woman, seen from the chest up, holds a nude baby in her crossed arms, looking down and to the left, her head veiled in white under an azure mantle over a rose-pink robe. The plump child, with short wavy copper-red hair, looks at the viewer and holds a goldfinch by a delicate chain wrapped around its foot, the other end of the chain in his hand. A golden-brown curtain fills most of the background, leaving a sliver of stone-gray wall at the left. The oil is about 62 by 50 centimeters, c. 1767-70, a second version of the composition.","i":"The tethered goldfinch ties the picture to Christ's Passion: a bird that eats thorns and thistles and wears a red mark was read as a sign of the crown of thorns and of blood, and the child's grip on the chain suggests a Savior already holding the instrument of his own captivity. The motif was also a domestic one, since pet birds on strings were children's playthings. That the museum holds two near-identical versions shows the composition's popularity; the record attributes both to Tiepolo.","c":"Duplicates like this raise the question of the master's hand: Tiepolo's workshop, with his sons Domenico and Lorenzo, made variants, and a replica rarely matches the spontaneity of a first version. The warm color and tender mood are the same, and the chain gives the composition a graceful line. Whether or not the touch is quite as free, it is a beautiful, restrained devotional picture of the artist's last decade."},"nga:12138":{"d":"A young woman, seen from the chest up, cradles a nude baby in her crossed arms. She looks down and to the left under arched brows, her hair covered by a bone-white veil and an azure mantle draped over a rose-pink robe. The plump baby, with short wavy copper-red hair and hazel eyes, looks out at the viewer; one hand holds a small bird with a strawberry-red face, a goldfinch, and the other grasps the end of her veil. A ginger-brown curtain covers the right half of the background. Tiepolo's oil, about 63 by 50 centimeters, dates from about 1767-70.","i":"The goldfinch is a traditional symbol of Christ's Passion: the bird feeds on thistles and thorns, and its red face was said to be a drop of Christ's blood. In a tender picture of a mother and child it adds a note of foreknowledge, as if the infant already held his future in his hand. Tiepolo painted this domestic type of Madonna in his last years, probably in Madrid, in a half-length format meant for private devotion in a room and not for a church.","c":"After the grand ceilings, this is a quiet, intimate image: the mother's lowered eyes, the child's direct gaze and the soft, warm color of a domestic devotion. Tiepolo, in his seventies, shows tenderness and economy; the rosy flesh and blue mantle are handled with the lightness of his sketches. The sweetness could be called too easy, but the combination of intimacy and fluent paint carries conviction. A late, warm and affecting work."},"met:436673":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of a woman, painted in 1791 by the British artist Horace Hone and recorded as said to be Lady Agnes Anne Wrothesley. The record gives no dimensions, dress or pose, so none is described here; the wording said to be means that the identification of the sitter is traditional, not proved. At this date a miniature of this kind was an oval plaque of a few centimeters, painted in thin watercolor over ivory so that the flesh glows, and set in a gold frame or a locket.","i":"A title in a miniature's name tells us it was made for a family of means; such small portraits were exchanged among relatives, given at engagements and marriages, and worn as jewelry. Hone, the son of the painter Nathaniel Hone, had a fashionable practice among titled and wealthy clients, so a commission from a lady of rank was within his usual circle. How the sitter is dressed is not recorded, and the doubt in the title means her identity should be treated with care.","c":"Hone's miniatures are polished, delicate and tasteful, with smooth flesh and carefully arranged hair, and the genre rewards exactly those virtues. The limit is the formula: elegance and flattery tend to smooth every sitter into a type, and the uncertain identification leaves the portrait without a person. It is best valued as a refined, typical Georgian miniature, rich in craft and thin in character."},"met:15249":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1807 by Horace Hone, measuring about 7.6 by 6.4 centimeters. The record lists only a man and a portrait, so his name, dress and pose are not described here. At this size the plaque is a little larger than a typical locket miniature and suited a small frame or case; the ivory's translucency gives the flesh its glow.","i":"In 1807, with Britain at war with Napoleon, miniatures were popular with the families of officers and others separated by war and travel, a personal likeness to carry or keep. The gentleman's name is not known, so his rank and occupation cannot be read; he survives as a record of a face from the early nineteenth century. Hone, the son of the painter Nathaniel Hone, was by then an experienced miniaturist with a fashionable practice.","c":"A miniature of this size rewards a close look at its finish: the stippled flesh, the careful hair, the clean edge of the coat. Hone's skill is a smooth, polished one, and a miniature of a man allows little beyond dignity. The limit is predictability; it is a competent, elegant, rather impersonal likeness of the sort that kept miniaturists busy in the Napoleonic years."},"nga:137524":{"d":"A woman leans over a seated man, one arm around his shoulder and a basket of fruit in her other hand, looking down at him. She wears a blue lace-edged headscarf, a white dress with puffed sleeves, a gold necklace and a red cloth over one shoulder. He sits at a desk in a dark coat, light waistcoat and gray cravat, a quill in one hand, turning a book page with the other, looking up at her. Books, papers, ink and a wax seal lie on the desk; a map hangs on the paneled wall. Waldmüller's oil on wood, about 41 by 33 centimeters, is dated 1824.","i":"Biedermeier portraits celebrated the private virtues of the Viennese middle class: learning, domestic affection and order. Here the map on the wall, the books and the quill identify the husband as a scholar of cartography, and the wife's fruit and embrace add the warmth of home. A double portrait like this announced a respectable profession and a happy marriage at once. Ferdinand Georg Waldmüller, who became one of Vienna's leading portraitists and landscape painters, was then in his early thirties.","c":"Waldmüller's strength is observation: the tactile whites and reds, the quill and the desk litter and the lively exchange of glances give a double portrait a credibility that many of its stiffer contemporaries lack. The intimate scale suits the subject, and the details of a scholar's table reward close looking. The weakness is a slightly stage-managed sweetness; the embrace and the fruit basket are charming rather than spontaneous. It is a fine early example of Biedermeier warmth."},"nga:128004":{"d":"A young painter sits at an easel, his body turned to it, looking back at the viewer. He has medium-length brown hair, a round pale face and dark eyes, and wears a brown garment over a white shirt. In one hand he holds a wooden palette with paint and brushes; with the other he lifts a brush toward the easel. In the dark gray background, indistinct objects stand on a shelf and a sketch of a person in profile is pinned to the wall. The tiny oil on panel, about 11 by 9 centimeters, was painted around 1645 by Gillis van Tilborgh the Younger.","i":"A painter showing himself at work, with palette, brush and easel, claimed the dignity of his craft: not a mere artisan but a maker of images. Studio self-portraits were a popular type in the seventeenth-century Netherlands, and a pinned sketch in the background may point to the drawing that underlies all painting. At this tiny size the picture was probably a keepsake or a demonstration of skill for a patron or collector; the artist was about twenty.","c":"At about eleven centimeters high the picture is a bravura exercise in miniature: a firm, characterful face, a convincing palette and brush, and a believable studio in a few strokes. Tilborgh is a minor Flemish name, and the charm lies in the modest, personal directness of the thing. A self-portrait at twenty carries a young man's quiet pride in his craft."},"met:437817":{"d":"A wedding party gathers in a dining room in this large group portrait on canvas, 115.6 by 160.7 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a wedding, men, women and a girl, a dog, a musician and the dining room itself, so the picture combines portraits of the participants with the feasting, music and domestic setting of the celebration. The wide horizontal format allows the sitters to be set out across the canvas as on a stage, each face meant to be recognized.","i":"Gillis van Tilborgh, a Flemish painter working in Brussels, was known both for peasant merry-making in the manner of David Teniers and for group portraits of well-to-do families, and this picture joins the two. A wedding feast was a natural occasion for such a commission: it recorded the families united by the match and their prosperity, with the musician adding festivity and the dog, a traditional sign of fidelity, the marriage's moral.","c":"Group portraits of a celebration have to solve a problem: how to make a crowd of individuals look like a party rather than a roll-call. Tilborgh's training as a genre painter helps, and the musician, the dog and the dining room give the arrangement life. The weakness typical of such commissions is a certain stiffness in the faces and a polite sameness of attitude. It is valuable as a record of costume and table customs in Flanders."},"met:15114":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of a man named L. B. Walker, in watercolour on ivory, about 7 by 5.5 centimetres. The museum's record lists only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and the background cannot be described with confidence. The picture was formerly attributed to the English miniaturist Edward Miles, who lived from 1752 to 1828, but that attribution is no longer upheld, so the maker is not named.","i":"Edward Miles was an English miniature painter who worked in London, Russia and America, and his name attached itself to many unsigned miniatures; this one has lost the attribution, and the artist remains unidentified. Miniatures of this kind were commonly kept in a leather case or locket, and many have lost both their makers and their sitters. L. B. Walker is named in the title only by initials and surname, and the record says no more about him. The Met received it in 2006.","c":"With the maker unknown, the fair verdict is on type and period: a small, competent watercolour on ivory of the early nineteenth century, in which a likeness is the aim and finish the means. The case for attention is the sitter's surviving name and the miniature's modest craft rather than any distinctive manner. It is a reminder that famous names attach themselves to unsigned pictures, and that the loss of an attribution does not diminish a portrait."},"met:15115":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, in watercolour on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimetres, so small that it could be worn as a pendant or held in the palm. The museum's record lists only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. Like the miniature of L. B. Walker, it was formerly attributed to the English miniaturist Edward Miles and came to the Met in 2006 as a gift from Gloria Manney.","i":"Portrait of a Gentleman is the museum's neutral label for a sitter whose name has not survived, a common fate for miniatures, which were passed between family members, sold and dispersed. The former attribution to Edward Miles, an English miniaturist who died in 1828, is not upheld, and the artist is unknown. The picture is evidence of how widespread the miniature was as a form of personal image before photography took over its role.","c":"An anonymous gentleman by an anonymous hand, and the right approach is to take it as a small piece of craft: clear drawing, a smooth finish and an unassuming presence. Without the details to judge, one can say only that miniatures of this kind were intended as keepsakes, for the eye of a family member, and not for display; they are best valued as documents of ordinary lives. Its charm lies in its modest size and survival."},"nga:93668":{"d":"A quiet rural landscape seen from a distance. In the foreground a body of water with green banks on either side reflects the surrounding green, and a grassy path with rocks cuts across at the right. Beyond the water rises a hill topped with several small buildings, and at its base a grassy field holds small figures, perhaps a mixture of people and animals. The colors are mostly green and brown, with touches of rusty orange. The light blue sky fills the upper half, with light, visible brushstrokes.","i":"Auvergne, in the volcanic uplands of central France, offered painters of the later nineteenth century pastures, old villages and wide skies far from Paris. Harpignies, who spent much of his long career painting the French provinces and worked in the tradition of Corot and the Barbizon painters, took such regional views for their structure and calm. The title simply names the place; the subject is the harmony of land, water and light, not an event.","c":"Harpignies's reputation rests on his command of structure, and this landscape shows it in a modest way: a clear arrangement of planes from water to hill, a limited palette of green and brown, and a sky given half the canvas. The reflections are pleasing, the small figures casual, the brushwork in the sky the liveliest part. It is quiet, well-ordered work, closer to Corot's calm than to the Impressionists' experiments then beginning."},"met:436632":{"d":"A wide landscape at evening in which the moon rises, painted in oil on a canvas 87.6 by 163.2 centimeters, more than a meter and a half across. The Met's catalog tags include evening, the moon and a landscape. In a picture of this type the subject is the quiet change of light at dusk, with the rising moon, the last glow of day and the darkening land set within a broad panoramic format.","i":"Moonrise and evening scenes were a poetic specialty of the Barbizon and Corot tradition, in which twilight, trees and still water were made into moods rather than topography. Harpignies, who was in his mid-sixties in 1885 and lived to ninety-seven, carried that tradition into the Third Republic, when younger painters had turned to Impressionism and Symbolism.","c":"Moonrise tests a painter better known for structure than for atmosphere, since the soft transitions of evening need a lighter touch than firm drawing. Within that limit, the wide format and quiet tonal key give the subject dignity, and the picture shows a master of the old school still working with calm assurance. It is respectable and restful rather than daring, and a viewer should look at how the light is graded across so large a canvas."},"nga:35091":{"d":"A grassy, dark green expanse stretches toward distant hills and a pale gray body of water. Soft clouds fill the pale blue sky. Tall trees with dark green foliage frame the right side, and small gray rocks lie across the grass closest to the viewer. Beyond the water, green hills fade into the distance. The palette is earthy greens and browns, with soft yellows and blues in the sky and water. The canvas is nearly square, 50.2 by 61.6 centimeters.","i":"Painted in 1898, when Harpignies was nearly eighty, this is a late work by an artist who painted into extreme old age. The title names no place, a sign that the subject is a type of French country: meadow, water, trees and hills. The trees framing the right edge follow a landscape convention going back to Claude Lorrain, and they give the view a classical clarity.","c":"A quiet, well-mannered landscape from an artist at the end of a very long career. The dark green foreground and pale distance recede convincingly, and the soft clouds are handled with a light touch. It has none of the experimental edge of the avant-garde of 1898, and its calm may seem conventional beside Cézanne or Monet working the same year, yet its sureness of construction explains why Harpignies was widely collected."},"met:437639":{"d":"A small upright panel, 38.4 by 21.0 centimeters, painted in oil on fir in the late fifteenth century, pairing two scenes from the Easter story under one title: Christ before Pilate and the Resurrection. The Met's keywords for the first scene record Pilate washing his hands, his court, soldiers, men and a woman, so the trial is a crowded, courtly scene around the Roman governor. The narrow format suggests a panel from a larger altarpiece or devotional cycle rather than a self-contained picture.","i":"In the Gospel of Matthew, Pilate, the Roman governor of Judea, finds no fault in Jesus but yields to the crowd, washing his hands before it to declare himself innocent of the death sentence. The Resurrection, on Easter morning, reverses that judgment and is the heart of Christian belief. Linked together, the scenes move from condemnation to triumph, the pattern that Passion cycles on late medieval altarpieces were designed to teach.","c":"Ludwig Schongauer was the brother of Martin Schongauer, the great Colmar engraver, and the family name sets a benchmark: the refined, nervous line of Martin's prints. A small, crowded panel like this can offer vivid storytelling and period costume rather than that finesse, and it is best prized on those terms. The attraction lies in the clarity of the narrative, with Pilate's gesture and the soldiers giving the scene its drama."},"nga:46487":{"d":"Six people stand pressed together, facing left and seen mostly from the waist up, in a heavily shadowed picture. Their faces are little more than dark lines, and most have downturned mouths. They appear to be men and women of various ages: at the center an old, balding man with a bare torso, behind him to the right a person in a white cap and blue cloak, at the back a short figure, perhaps a child, also bare-chested, and at the front left three people in simple clothing. The dark background has vertical lines suggesting gray walls. Oil on canvas, about 60 by 74 centimeters, c. 1843.","i":"Daumier, famous in Paris for his lithographed caricatures of politicians and the bourgeoisie, painted pictures of the poor and of ordinary people for himself, rarely exhibited in his lifetime. The huddled beggars, anonymous and undignified by any narrative, express his sympathy with the urban poor of July Monarchy Paris, a theme of social realism that anticipated Courbet's. The painting asks the viewer to see the group as a mass of shared hardship.","c":"A powerful sketch in paint: the huddled mass of figures, the dark outlines that stand for faces and the yellow light create a scene of exhausted endurance with very few means. The lack of finish is itself eloquent, since the beggars appear only as the shadows of themselves. Daumier's painting has a monumentality that makes the poor look like a chorus from a Greek tragedy. It is a forceful, unsentimental image."},"nga:30212":{"d":"A small oil on wood, about 29 by 39 centimeters, titled Feast of the Gods and dated about 1849 to 1850, by Honoré Daumier. Later additions by other hands mean that part of the surface is not the artist's own work. The theme is classical, and the title points to a banquet of the Olympian gods. Daumier is best known for his lithographed caricatures, so a subject from Olympus is a departure.","i":"A feast of the gods was a standard theme of Renaissance and Baroque painting, an occasion for nude figures and abundance, and French academic painters of Daumier's day still made much of such subjects. Daumier, who had mocked the cult of classical antiquity in his lithograph series of 1842, may have treated it with irony, but nothing confirms it. The additions by other hands complicate any reading of the picture's original tone.","c":"Daumier's painting is at its best when it deals with modern life, and a mythological banquet is not where his talent lies; if it is a parody, as the lithographs suggest, it would be at home in his satirical world. The later additions by other hands compromise the picture's authority, and it is difficult to judge his own touch in what remains. A curiosity, rather than a high point, among his oil sketches."},"nga:46489":{"d":"An older man, a woman and a child stand near each other in a vertical composition of muted grays, cream white and golden tan, lit from the right with deep shadows. At the center the man, his eyes shaded, wears a two-peaked hat, a smoke-gray scarf, a cream shirt and slate-blue jacket, and carries a rolled mat and small wooden box. Behind him the child, in a parchment-white leotard, holds a chair upside down on his head. At the left the woman, mostly in shadow, holds a large drum. Oil on wood, about 33 by 25 centimeters, 1847 to 1850.","i":"Saltimbanques were wandering street performers, acrobats, musicians and strongmen who traveled between fairs and village squares, and they were a favorite subject of nineteenth-century French artists, who saw in them poverty and a kind of vagabond freedom. The family group here, with the drum and the child balancing a chair, is shown with the tools of its trade. Daumier's treatment lacks sentimentality, showing them as tired working people.","c":"A fine small study in tired dignity: the man's shaded eyes and the woman's near invisibility, with the child half hidden by the chair, make the whole group seem weighed down by need. The loose, almost blurred treatment fits the subject and lifts the picture beyond anecdote. Daumier gives his poor performers weight and form, with a sculptor's feeling for mass. It is a moving, unsentimental image."},"nga:46488":{"d":"A crowd of seated people faces right, mostly seen from the waist up, in rows of red theater seats. In the closest row of four, a child with short dark hair in a dark brown coat sits at the left, next to a woman with her dark hair in an updo tied with a blue ribbon, in a dark dress with white ruffles and cuffs; then a mustached, balding man in a formal dark suit, and at the far right a woman in a light blue-gray dress with a bow in her short light hair. Oil on wood, about 26 by 35 centimeters, c. 1856.","i":"Daumier painted and drew many views of theater audiences, which gave him a crowd of faces absorbed in something the viewer cannot see. The French Theater of the title is presumably the Théâtre-Français, the Comédie-Française, where well-off Parisians went to see the classics, but the picture records spectators, not the stage. It turns the viewer into a spectator of spectators, a favorite Daumier device.","c":"A tiny picture with a keen social eye: the child, the woman with the blue ribbon and the balding husband are each reduced to a few precise marks that give character, and the fading of the rows behind makes the room seem full. The painting lacks finish, but the economy is the point. It shows Daumier's gift for catching an audience's attention in a gesture, and it remains fresh."},"nga:30211":{"d":"Three figures sit closely together in a row, seen in profile and facing right, their heads and upper bodies loosely brushed. The one at the left looks elderly, with a thin, frowning mouth, a gray outfit and a white cap; beside her a younger figure, with a thin nose and round black eyes, wears a similar gray garment and white cap above a strip of black hair. The third is the least distinct, a blur of gray and tan with a smudge of reddish-brown hair. The background is a blur of earthy tones, with thick, visible strokes. Oil on wood, about 15 by 22 centimeters, 1855 to 1857.","i":"The title and the white caps point, perhaps, to a congregation of ordinary women at worship, a subject Daumier explored in several small pictures. He treats them as a group without individual identity, united by posture and attention. The picture relates to Realist painting's interest in the religion of common people, presented without ceremony, and it avoids both piety and satire.","c":"Almost abstract in its handling, this is Daumier at his most economical: a few smears of gray, tan and brown are enough to convey age, youth and the common posture of attention. The faces are barely there, which gives the figures a universal quality, though at this scale the picture risks being a mere sketch. It is a tiny but telling example of how much expression he could draw from very little paint."},"met:436093":{"d":"Men drinking, in a small oil on a wood panel, 36.5 by 27.9 centimeters; the Met's keywords record only men and drinking. The picture is an example of Daumier's painted cabinet subjects of everyday life: figures built from broad masses of light and dark, with little detail or setting, so that the gesture of drinking and the bodies themselves carry the scene. The Met's date is given as by 1861.","i":"Daumier, best known as a lithographer and caricaturist for the Paris press, painted seriously from the late 1840s and kept the work largely for himself, rarely exhibiting. The drinkers belong to his large repertoire of ordinary Parisians: lawyers, spectators, collectors, laundresses. Drinking scenes carried moral overtones in earlier Dutch art, but in Daumier's hands they are more sympathetic, a record of urban companionship and working men's pleasures.","c":"Daumier's paintings are sketches in spirit: strong light against dark, rough handling, and bodies conveyed by weight. Pictures of this kind are typically more about attitude than anecdote, and they have the cheerful vulgarity he enjoyed. The scale is small and the working quick, so it lacks the monumental force of his laundresses, but it shows the shorthand that later painters, from Degas to Van Gogh, admired in him."},"met:436091":{"d":"A laundress with a child, painted in oil on an oak panel, 48.9 by 33.0 centimeters, probably about 1863. The Met's keywords record a washerwoman, a woman and a child. Daumier returned to this subject several times in the 1860s, and the pictures show the woman as a solid, weighty figure, built from broad masses of dark and light against a simple city setting, with the child at her side.","i":"Laundresses were among the hardest-worked women of Paris, carrying heavy loads to and from the washhouses and floating laundries on the Seine. Daumier, a close observer of the capital's working people, made the laundress monumental rather than pitiful: she is given a dignity that recalls Millet's peasants or Courbet's workers, and the child at her side adds a note of tenderness and of the burden of a working mother.","c":"One of the best-known images in Daumier's painted work, and the reason is the combination of weight and economy: the figure is a few broad strokes of dark and light, yet it moves with real labor. The surface is rough and the detail minimal, which keeps sentiment in check. If there is a weakness it is the thin, sketchy space around the figure, though that too serves the focus. Here is the dignity of work without picturesque rags."},"met:436095":{"d":"A railway carriage packed with passengers, painted in dark browns and ochres. In the foreground a young mother in a loose cream blouse looks down at the baby she nurses; beside her an old woman in a hooded shawl sits upright with her hands folded over a basket on her knees and stares ahead with weary calm; to her right a boy sleeps slumped against the seat. Behind them, rows of men in hats and women in headscarves fill the benches, and two small windows at the upper left show pale daylight. Heavy dark outlines remain visible, and the picture is unfinished: the figures at the back and the upper part are barely worked up.","i":"Daumier, famous as a lithographer for the satirical paper Le Charivari, painted several versions of this subject between the 1850s and the mid-1860s. This one, dated about 1862–64, was left unfinished, and the canvas is still squared for transfer from an earlier drawing. A completed version is in Ottawa, and related watercolours were commissioned in 1864 for the Baltimore collector William Walters. Third class was the cheapest travel, with open compartments and hard wooden benches. Daumier's paintings were little known until a Paris exhibition in 1878, a year before his death. Louisine Havemeyer bought this one in 1913, and it reached the Metropolitan Museum in 1929.","c":"The unfinished Met version is the more urgent of the two. The broad, dark drawing and the warm light on the mother's skin give the scene a rough grandeur, close to Rembrandt, and the rows of anonymous faces behind press the group forward. Some will find it too incomplete, and the background figures are little more than blots. The social comment is restrained: Daumier invites sympathy without a sermon, and the old woman's steady look is the real centre. Unlike his satirical prints, it makes no joke."},"nga:895":{"d":"An older, balding man with a gray beard, in a dark brown jacket and trousers, holds a sheet of white paper at the center of a shadowy room while a younger man, with short black hair and ruddy skin, in a sage-green jacket, tilts his head to look at it. A V-shaped wooden rack before the younger man holds an open dark green portfolio, and he rests his hands on more sheets. Two indistinct paintings hang on an olive-green wall behind them, and an aquamarine-blue folder leans against a red sofa. Light falls from the upper left. Oil on canvas, about 41 by 33 centimeters.","i":"Daumier returned many times to the world of artists and print collectors, and this scene of an older master or connoisseur advising a young artist over a drawing is one of several he made in the 1860s. The older man's judgment, the younger man's attentive stance and the portfolio of drawings stand for the handing on of artistic knowledge. Daumier himself learned lithography by practice rather than from the academy, and the scene presents instruction as a quiet, private exchange.","c":"A concentrated little drama of looking: the pale paper at the center draws both men's eyes, and thus the viewer's, and the dark, almost monochrome room makes that sheet the only light. Daumier's sketchy handling conveys the older man's authority and the younger man's anxious deference with just a few strokes. The subject is quiet and the setting plain, but the exchange is entirely believable."},"nga:195605":{"d":"An auction scene: a group of men in formal dress and top hats watches an auctioneer gesture toward a framed picture displayed on an easel. The background is an indistinct crowd, and the expressions and postures of the onlookers range from attention to anticipation. The title places the sale at the Gallery Drouot, the Paris auction rooms. Oil on wood, about 24 by 18 centimeters, painted after 1863 and before 1873 by Honoré Daumier.","i":"The Hôtel Drouot, the Paris auction house, was where the city's dealers, collectors and amateurs gathered to bid, and Daumier, who made many prints of collectors and dealers, found in it a ready-made theater of passion and greed. The auctioneer's gesture toward the picture, the hats, the packed room and the competitive silence mark the art market that grew rapidly under the Second Empire. The picture satirizes, gently, the new commerce in art.","c":"Daumier's gift for the telling gesture and the shadowed face turns an auction into a small comedy of acquisitiveness. The loose handling, with figures fading into a crowd, conveys the murmur of the room, and the framed picture on its easel is the still center of attention. It is a tiny panel, more notation than composition, but it is full of observation and amused sympathy, and it shows the artist's feeling for the modern scene."},"met:436092":{"d":"A small horizontal oil on wood, 24.8 by 46.0 centimeters, shows Don Quixote with a horse and, as the title says, a dead mule. The Met's subject tags list a man, a horse, animals and equestrianism. Daumier's late Don Quixote pictures are painted in thin, dry, rapid strokes, with the figures reduced to a few dark shapes in a barely suggested landscape, so that the knight's gaunt silhouette and the lifeless animal carry the scene.","i":"Cervantes's Don Quixote, a gentleman unhinged by chivalric romances who sets out to revive knight-errantry, was a favorite of Romantic and Realist artists. Daumier painted it repeatedly, mostly for himself, seeing in the idealist knight and his down-to-earth squire Sancho Panza a pair that could stand for the ironies of life. The dead mule belongs to the bleak, comic episodes in which the hero's dreams meet physical reality.","c":"Daumier's Don Quixote paintings are among his most radical: brushwork so abbreviated that landscape and figures merge, and a mood both comic and tragic. Their roughness anticipates later Expressionism, and they were largely unseen until late in his life. At 46 centimeters wide this one is a sketch in effect, and the risk is that it looks unfinished; but the feeling of lonely, doomed idealism is exactly what he wanted."},"met:436059":{"d":"A miniature on ivory showing a princess with her infant son. The title names her as Maria Francisca, the Portuguese-born wife of the Spanish infante Don Carlos, and the boy as Carlos Luis, born in 1818, the year of the portrait. The museum's keywords note the ribbons of the Order of Queen Maria Luisa and the Order of the Golden Fleece, so the sitters are shown with the insignia of their rank. No dimensions are given, but such miniatures are normally palm-sized.","i":"Royal miniatures served as dynastic documents and diplomatic gifts, and a mother-and-child pairing announced the continuity of the line. The Golden Fleece was the highest dynastic order of the Spanish monarchy, and the Order of Queen Maria Luisa a decoration for noble women. The family would later be at the center of the Carlist disputes: Maria Francisca's husband claimed the Spanish throne in 1833, and their son became a Carlist claimant.","c":"Court miniaturists were expected to deliver precision and flattery, and a portrait of royal sitters would be judged by the jewel-like finish of the insignia and the delicacy of the faces. The mother-and-child grouping echoes a Madonna and Child and lends the dynastic image a note of tenderness. As an object it is formal; its interest is as much historical as artistic, and a viewer should look for warmth inside the formality."},"met:10217":{"d":"A man, given in the title as Captain Graham, is portrayed in watercolor on ivory in a miniature only about 6 centimeters high and 5 wide, painted around 1805 by the Scottish-born Andrew Robertson. The rank in the title suggests a military or naval officer, though the recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait. At this size the likeness is concentrated on the face and upper body, and the color glows through the ivory's translucent base.","i":"Robertson moved from Aberdeen to London in 1801 and by the middle of the decade was among the capital's busiest miniaturists, at a time when war kept officers sitting for keepsake likenesses. His brothers were also painters, one of them, Archibald, working in New York. Such a miniature was a private object, set in a locket or a case and passed from hand to hand among family, which accounts for its small size.","c":"Robertson's reputation rests on a broader, richer handling than the stippled manner of the Cosway tradition, and he pressed for miniatures with the force of oil portraits. At six centimeters this one is small, yet the format rewards close looking: the pleasure is in how a few exact touches fix a character. It dates from his early London years, before the larger, richer works of his maturity."},"met:437482":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a miniature on ivory dated 1828 by Andrew Robertson, then in the full maturity of a career that had made him one of the most admired miniaturists in Britain. The recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait. By this date Robertson's miniatures were typically larger and richer in color than the dainty ones of the 1790s, and aimed at the weight of an oil portrait.","i":"1828 was a late moment for the art of miniature painting: within about a decade the daguerreotype would begin to take over the market for small likenesses. Robertson's generation responded by making miniatures larger and more painterly, so that they could hold their own against oils. Without the sitter's name, the miniature is a record of a man's face and of a moment in British portrait fashion, when a gentleman could still choose a painted keepsake.","c":"Robertson at this date offers directness and solidity rather than coquettish charm: the face modeled firmly, the color fuller than in the airy miniatures of the Plimer type. The limits are those of the genre, since ivory forbids deep shadow, and the unnamed sitter remains a type. Still, it shows why Robertson was valued: a man's likeness caught with conviction at pocket scale."},"met:437486":{"d":"A woman is portrayed on ivory in this French miniature of 1829 by François-Théodore Rochard. The sitter is unnamed. The ivory support, thin and pale, gave the painter a luminous ground for watercolor, and the face is the focus, as in all miniatures. The portrait dates from the last years of the reign of Charles X, just before the July Revolution of 1830 changed the regime in France.","i":"In the late 1820s the portrait miniature in France was often the work of professional painters who exhibited at the Salon and supplied the middle classes and aristocracy with likenesses in watercolor and gouache on ivory. The sitter's anonymity is typical of miniatures separated from their cases and inscriptions. The little portrait would have been a keepsake for family and friends, kept close in a drawer or a locket.","c":"A miniature lives by the rendering of the eyes, and Rochard's competent, polished handling gives this portrait a clear, quiet presence. Its virtues are those of the trade: neat finish, soft coloring and a sitter flattered but still recognizably human. A viewer should not expect innovation, but craftsmanship is the whole of the art here."},"met:436202":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of Sir Henry Blount (1602-1682), painted in the 1660s by Nicholas Dixon on vellum laid on card, the traditional support of English limning, in which fine watercolor and body-color are applied in tiny strokes. No dimensions are recorded, but such miniatures are usually only a few centimeters high. The face would be the carefully finished center of the small image.","i":"Blount was an English traveler and writer whose 'A Voyage into the Levant' (1636), an account of his journey to Constantinople and Cairo, went through many editions. A portrait miniature of him as an older man, made in Restoration England, would have been a keepsake or gift among friends and relatives, and a quiet record of a man whose firsthand experience of the Ottoman world was unusual among his contemporaries.","c":"Dixon worked in the English limning tradition of Hilliard, Oliver and above all Samuel Cooper, whose soft, truthful modeling set the standard for Restoration miniatures. In such a picture the test is whether the face has weight and whether the sitter's character survives the small scale. Dixon was a capable practitioner rather than a leader of the art, and this portrait of a distinguished writer is chiefly valuable as a likeness."},"cma:124084":{"d":"A vertical canvas, 80.1 by 64.0 centimeters, which the Cleveland Museum describes as a fête galante, a gallant party in which figures in theatrical costume, derived from Italian street performers seen in Paris, play out a flirtation in an outdoor garden. As the title says, the subject is a declaration of love, the moment of courtship at the heart of the type, set in a fantasy world that hovers between reality and theater.","i":"The fête galante was a French invention of the early 1700s, created by Watteau and followed by Lancret, in which elegant figures in theater costume gather in parklike settings for music, conversation and flirtation. The costumes recall the Italian commedia dell'arte players who performed in Paris, and the outdoor party reflects the leisure of the Parisian elite. The arts of conversation, music and painting, all prized by French aristocrats, meet in a single picture.","c":"Lancret is often judged as the lesser Watteau: his figures are prettier and less haunted, his compositions neater, and the yearning that gives Watteau's best fêtes their depth is replaced by pleasant charm. In a canvas of this size the charm is genuine, from the shimmering costume to the neat gesture of courtship, but it is a surface charm. Even so, his clarity and polish made him one of the most popular painters of the Regency."},"nga:96":{"d":"Twenty-one elegantly dressed men and women gather and dance in a park-like landscape of tall, slender trees. At the left a woman and a man dance together, each with one pointed foot raised. She wears an ice-blue gown patterned with rows of flowers, with garlands across her chest and flowers in her dark hair; her skirt falls short of her ankles and her arms are spread wide. He wears a coral-red tunic with ribbons and an upturned brown hat. Onlookers, some with a violin and a pipe, cluster around, and a stone column topped by a laurel-crowned head and a fountain mark the garden.","i":"Marie-Anne de Camargo (1710-1770) was a star of the Paris Opera, famed for her brilliant footwork and for shortening her skirts so that audiences could see it. Lancret painted her more than once, placing a real celebrity in the make-believe garden of the fête galante, where music, dance and flirtation blend. The laurel-crowned bust in the middle of the garden is a nod to the arts and to the victory of the dancer's skill.","c":"The dancer is the picture's delight: poised, bright in her ice-blue gown, and given a clear space within the crowd. Lancret surrounds her with a polite, rhythmic arrangement of groups and slender trees that keeps the eye circling. What he lacks is Watteau's undercurrent of melancholy, and the crowd has a doll-like uniformity. For theatrical subjects, however, he is at his best, and this is among his most engaging performances."},"cma:125941":{"d":"A set of five tall decorative panels, each about 1.5 meters high, in oil on canvas, made to be fitted into the paneling of a room: The Gardener, Horticulture, The Vineyard, The See-Saw and The Swing. Three wider panels show pairs of youthful figures enjoying outdoor pastimes, while two narrow ones show single figures cultivating lush private gardens. Delicate arabesques derived from nature frame the scenes, and similar flowers and animals recur in each panel, unifying the series.","i":"Painted for a room in the Pavillon d'Hauteville in Paris, the panels belong to the fashion for light, pastoral decoration in elite Parisian homes of the 1720s. The tradition of showing the four seasons or the times of day gave such sets their logic; here the theme is afternoon pastimes in nature. Gardening, games and courtship are Lancret's favorite pastoral motifs, and they turned a room into a make-believe countryside.","c":"Decorative painting subordinates the picture to the room, and the panels have to be judged accordingly: the arabesque frames, soft palette and narrow, vertical formats are all designed to harmonize with carved woodwork. Lancret handles the tall format with skill, and the single gardeners are the neatest inventions. As easel paintings they lack the weight of his best fêtes galantes, but as a decorative ensemble they preserve the spirit of a Rococo interior."},"cma:125942":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, 151.0 by 36.5 centimeters, one of five painted for a Parisian room. The Cleveland Museum describes the two narrow panels in the set as individual figures cultivating lush, private gardens, and this is one of them. Delicate arabesques derived from nature frame the scene, and the flora and fauna match those of the other panels. Because it was meant to fill the space between doors and windows, the composition is stretched upward, so that figure and garden rise in a slender column.","i":"Gardening was a favorite pastoral theme in eighteenth-century France, where the cultivation of gardens signaled both refinement and an idealized, ordered nature. A gardener in a decorative panel is less a laborer than a figure in a dream of country life, pretty, neat and untroubled by toil. In the series, the gardener balances the games of the wider panels, offering a quieter, more solitary note.","c":"Narrow panels are a hard test of composition, and Lancret meets it with an upright figure and a profusion of leaf and flower that fills the space without crowding. The single figure gives a touch of intimacy missing from the wide, busier panels. The result is more charming than profound, a decorative fancy whose delicacy is its strength; seen alone it loses some of its force, which depends on the company of the other four."},"cma:125943":{"d":"One of five panels painted for a Parisian room, this one is tall and narrow, 151.0 by 36.5 centimeters. Together with The Gardener it is one of the two narrow panels in the set, which the Cleveland Museum describes as single figures cultivating lush, private gardens. The title names the art of gardening itself. Delicate arabesques frame the scene, and the same flowers and animals as in the other panels tie it into the series.","i":"Horticulture, the art of growing and arranging plants, was fashionable in eighteenth-century France, where laying out parterres, growing rare plants and enjoying private gardens were pursuits of the elite. A panel titled with the art itself, rather than with a gardener, places attention on cultivated nature. As a decorative companion to The Gardener, it extends the series' celebration of a refined, ordered countryside.","c":"As one of the narrow pair, this panel faces the same problem as its companion, how to make a tall, thin space feel full, and Lancret's answer is the same: a single figure and a decorative tangle of plants. With little narrative, the panel depends on elegance of drawing and delicacy of color. It succeeds as ornament and is less memorable on its own than the wider panels with their pairs of figures."},"cma:125944":{"d":"A wide panel, 150.3 by 82.3 centimeters, one of five made for a Parisian room. The Cleveland Museum describes the three wider panels as showing lithe, youthful pairs playfully enjoying outdoor pleasures, so this one presents a young couple in a vineyard, a setting the museum elsewhere calls the vintage. Delicate arabesques derived from nature frame the scene, and the same kinds of flowers and animals recur in the other panels of the set.","i":"The vineyard, with its grape harvest, was an old setting for pastoral courtship, and for a Paris audience it evoked an idealized countryside of abundance and play. The wine-growing year and its rituals provided a natural pretext for flirtation. In Lancret's room the panel stands among the afternoon pastimes of the series, where the pleasures of the country are shown without any of the labor behind them.","c":"The wider format lets Lancret arrange two figures with more space around them, and the vineyard allows richer color than the gardens do. The scene is charming and idealized, with no hint of the labor a real harvest involved. Lancret's Parisian clients wanted exactly that, and the panel delivers it with finesse, though it is more decoration than observation."},"cma:125945":{"d":"A wide panel, 150.7 by 89.4 centimeters, one of five painted for a Parisian room. The title names the game, the see-saw, and the Cleveland Museum describes the three wider panels as showing lithe, youthful pairs enjoying outdoor pleasures. A see-saw needs two players balanced against each other, so the pair is built into the subject. Delicate arabesques derived from nature frame the scene, and the same flowers and animals recur in the other panels.","i":"The see-saw gives a decorative painter a pair of figures poised in balance, a motif that suited the symmetrical, courtly taste of the 1720s. In the language of the fête galante, a game for two is also a game of courtship, with the up-and-down motion standing for the rise and fall of feeling. Of the three wider panels it is the most static, and so the most restful.","c":"The see-saw is the most static of the games: a balanced pair, a rocking plank, a frame of foliage. Lancret keeps the composition clear and gives the figures grace, but the subject offers less movement than the swing and less richness than the vineyard. It is a pleasant decorative piece that does its job in the room, where its quiet symmetry would have steadied the more animated panels."},"cma:125946":{"d":"A wide panel, 150.8 by 89.7 centimeters, one of five painted for a Parisian room. The Cleveland Museum describes the three wider panels as showing lithe, youthful pairs enjoying outdoor pleasures, and the title of this one names the pastime: a swing. Delicate arabesques derived from nature frame the scene, and flowers and animals echo those in the other panels of the set.","i":"The swing was a favorite motif of French Rococo art, a symbol of playful, airborne flirtation, which Fragonard would make famous in the 1760s, some forty years after Lancret's panel. A pair at a swing suggests courtship in a garden: the motion, the pushing and the daring height give an excuse for closeness. Lancret presents it as a harmless afternoon game, one of the pastimes that fill the series.","c":"The swing gives a painter rhythm and lightness, and Lancret uses it to show youthful grace. The wide format lets the pair be balanced against foliage, and the arc of the swing's motion is the picture's main idea. He does not give it the erotic charge Fragonard did; the game remains polite. As a decorative panel it succeeds because it is easy on the eye and fits its room."},"nga:41605":{"d":"Seven elegantly dressed men and women sit and stand together in front of a grove of towering trees, attended by more men who tend four dogs and four horses. At the center, a seated woman in a lemon-yellow dress and a small black tricorn hat takes a froth-topped cup from a tray held by a man in sapphire blue, while a standing woman in scarlet rests her hand on a man's loose fist. In the lower right a man on the ground offers food to the dogs. Green hills roll back to hazy blue mountains beneath puffy clouds.","i":"The picnic after the hunt was a fashionable aristocratic pleasure in eighteenth-century France, and paintings of it flattered a ruling class at ease: the hunt itself is over, and what remains is flirtation, refreshment and servants. The social order is built into the picture, with seated masters and standing or kneeling attendants. Lancret was one of the leading painters of such entertainments in the 1730s.","c":"Lancret's late work is polished and a little formulaic, and this picture is typical: the slender trees, the luminous sky and the bright accents of lemon, scarlet and sapphire are pleasing, while the faces share a doll-like delicacy. The liveliest passage is the group of dogs at the lower right, one of the few unposed moments. By this date Boucher's rising Rococo made Lancret look old-fashioned, but his clarity and charm remain attractive."},"met:438727":{"d":"A small oil on copper, 27.3 by 35.2 centimeters, illustrating a comic tale, 'Brother Philippe's Geese'. The Met's catalog tags include a woman, a man and a servant. The smooth, hard surface of copper suits fine detail and a glossy finish, and the picture is the kind of cabinet piece made for a collector's pleasure, with figures, as usual in Lancret, probably in the contemporary dress of the eighteenth century.","i":"The tale comes from Boccaccio's Decameron and was retold in verse by La Fontaine: a friar who has brought up his young son in seclusion takes him to the city, where the boy sees women for the first time and, told they are geese, begs to take one home. It is a risqué joke about innocence and nature, told with a light touch. Lancret illustrated several of La Fontaine's tales, which suited the Parisian taste for witty, mildly improper stories.","c":"The subject is a gift to a painter of gallantry: a tale of innocence and desire that can be told with a wink. Lancret handles risqué stories with tact, keeping the humor in glances and gestures rather than in anything coarse, and the copper's smooth surface gives his small figures a jewel-like finish. The weakness is that a painter cannot match La Fontaine's wit, so the picture depends on the viewer knowing the story."},"met:438726":{"d":"A small oil on copper, 27.9 by 35.6 centimeters, showing a man and a woman, as the Met's keywords record, in what is evidently a comic scene of domestic gallantry. The title points to a servant who is vindicated, the kind of narrative joke at which Lancret excelled. As with other small coppers by him, the smooth, hard ground gives the paint a fine, enameled finish, and the figures wear the fashionable dress of the artist's own day rather than any historical costume.","i":"The title corresponds to a verse tale by La Fontaine, 'La Servante justifiée', and Lancret painted a number of illustrations to La Fontaine's Contes in the late 1730s and early 1740s, among them 'Brother Philippe's Geese'. Such stories of masters, mistresses and servants, mildly improper and told with wit, were enormously popular with the Parisian public. The picture offers the pleasure of recognizing a favorite tale.","c":"The narrative is Lancret's raw material, and the test is whether the exchange of looks and gestures tells the tale without the text. In small copper pictures like this he is at his most precise: neat figures, bright accents, an economical setting. What he never reaches is La Fontaine's irony; the paintings are charming where the stories are sharp. As a cabinet piece it is a pleasant, slightly lightweight example of late French Rococo gallantry."},"met:435726":{"d":"A portrait miniature painted on ivory shows Count Alexander Ivanovich Sollogoub, then in his early twenties. The museum record gives no detailed description or measurements, so his pose and dress cannot be described with confidence. Miniatures were intimate objects, small enough to be held in the palm, worn as a jewel or kept in a case, and painted with fine brushes on thin slices of ivory, whose pale surface lends skin tones a luminous glow.","i":"In the early nineteenth century miniatures were exchanged as keepsakes among the families, lovers and friends of the European aristocracy, and their size meant that they could travel easily. Bossi was an Italian miniaturist who worked for noble patrons in central Europe. The title gives the sitter's rank and dates, 1788 to 1844, but the circumstances of this commission are not recorded.","c":"The merit of a miniaturist lies in likeness, delicacy of flesh tones and crisp rendering of costume at tiny scale, qualities for which Bossi was a leading name in his time. Without the object to hand, the safest verdict is that it belongs to an elegant, polished, rather impersonal European court style, more concerned with finish than psychological probing. Its charm is the intimacy of the form itself."},"nga:150754":{"d":"Seen from a slightly raised viewpoint, men, women and children skate on a frozen river, a crowd that seems to number hundreds and fades into hazy distance. The nearest dozen or so are sharply defined: three men in dark cloaks talk on the right, boys play a hockey-like game, and a man in scarlet breeches, red jacket and plumed hat stands beside a woman in a black hooded cloak and raspberry bodice. A rose-red windmill and a stepped-roof house with a patterned fence frame the scene, with a wooden bridge, a church and steel-gray clouds broken by azure.","i":"Winter scenes of this kind became a Dutch specialty in the early seventeenth century, when cold winters made skating on frozen canals and rivers a pastime shared by every rank. Here fashionably dressed couples mingle with plainer folk. Pictures like this are usually read as a lively record of leisure and social variety; some historians also detect a quiet moral note, since ice is thin and the pleasures of the moment are brief. The Amstel is the river that runs through Amsterdam.","c":"Dated 1611, this is an early example of the Dutch winter landscape, and Van Breen builds it with miniaturist patience: each group is a small anecdote, and the colors of the costumes ring brightly against the pale ice. The weakness is the space, which is stacked rather than gently receding, and the figures, which can seem like puppets. It is lively and informative, but less atmospheric than the tonal winter scenes of the next generation."},"nga:46148":{"d":"A devotional Madonna and Child, painted in tempera on a panel measuring 70.8 by 48.9 centimeters, about 1470 to 1475. The paint layer has been transferred from its original wood support to hardboard, a conservation procedure of the twentieth century, so what is seen is the original paint on a modern backing. The upright format and moderate size suit an image for private devotion in a Florentine home, where the Virgin and her infant son were typically shown at half length, close to the viewer.","i":"Domenico Ghirlandaio, who would become Florence's leading fresco painter of the 1480s, is shown here near the start of his career. Pictures of Mary and the Christ Child were the commonest devotional images in Renaissance homes, hung in bedchambers where they encouraged prayer and stood as a hope for healthy children and a happy marriage. The Madonna's role as intercessor made her a natural focus for a household's prayers.","c":"Ghirlandaio was a superb narrator and portraitist but rarely a poet, and his Madonnas lean toward clarity and calm rather than mystery. At this early date he was still absorbing the lessons of the great Florentine workshops, and the picture should be judged by its drawing, the grace of its drapery and the Virgin's quiet expression. A transfer to hardboard can leave a surface less crisp than the original, and any verdict must allow for it."},"met:436489":{"d":"A heavy-lidded man in a long red tunic and red cap looks down, while a boy in a patterned white tunic and small red cap looks up at him, the two figures filling the foreground in half-length. The man's pale, fleshy face is modelled with fine hatching, and one hand holds a black hood or cap at the lower edge. The child's long fair hair falls to his shoulders, and his small hands are clasped at the lower right. Behind them stretches a landscape of a pale lake, bare hills and a walled town with a tall tower under a thin morning sky. A painted inscription runs along the top edge, and the rough, unpainted edge of the panel survives around the picture.","i":"Francesco Sassetti (1421–1490) was general manager of the Medici bank under Lorenzo de' Medici and one of the richest men in Florence. Ghirlandaio painted his family chapel in Santa Trinita in the 1480s, with its frescoes and altarpiece. The boy is his son Teodoro, and the Met dates the double portrait to about 1488, shortly before Sassetti's death. The exchange of glances, father looking down and child looking up, is often read as a statement of family continuity and hope. The landscape behind the figures reflects the taste for Netherlandish portraits that Florentine patrons shared.","c":"Ghirlandaio was a candid recorder of faces, and this is a good example. The banker's heavy-lidded, pouchy features are set down with startling frankness and without flattery, and the boy's upturned face, fresh in colour, answers it neatly. The landscape adds calm and depth. What the picture lacks is the intensity of Botticelli's or Leonardo's portraits: the surfaces are smooth and the gestures conventional. It remains a humane, direct double portrait of a father and child bound by a look, in the same spirit as the painter's Old Man and His Grandson in the Louvre."},"met:436490":{"d":"A portrait in tempera on wood, about 55 by 45 centimetres, shows a man whose name is not recorded. Florentine portraits of the late fifteenth century, Ghirlandaio's among them, usually show the sitter in profile or three-quarter view, with plainly honest observation of the face and sober clothing, sometimes before a window opening on landscape. The museum's record lists only a man and a portrait, so details of dress, pose and setting cannot be given with confidence.","i":"Portraits of this kind recorded a citizen's likeness for his family, and in Florence the sitters were usually merchants, bankers or officials. Ghirlandaio ran the busiest workshop in the city, producing altarpieces and frescoes in quantity, and the portrait heads he put into his frescoes, many of living Florentines, show his gift for likeness. The sitter of this panel is unnamed, and the reason it was painted is not recorded.","c":"Ghirlandaio was less poetic than Botticelli and less searching than Leonardo, but he had a steady eye for the individual face, and his portraits tend to have a sober, convincing presence. This panel, as a portrait of an unnamed man, presumably shares that plain, unflattering truthfulness, and its main interest is as an example of how Florentine portraiture absorbed the Flemish attention to skin and texture."},"met:436492":{"d":"A tall fresco, about 285 by 150 centimetres, shows Saint Christopher and the Christ Child, with a river as the setting: the giant wades through the water with the child on his shoulder, as the legend tells. Now detached from its wall and mounted as a museum object, the painting keeps the upright shape that suited a giant saint, and the original wall image would have been designed to be read at a glance from a distance.","i":"According to the Golden Legend, Christopher was a giant who served others by carrying travellers over a dangerous river. One day a child asked to be carried, and in midstream grew so heavy that Christopher felt he bore the whole world: he was carrying Christ, its creator. The name means Christ-bearer. Large images of the saint were set where passers-by could see them, since it was believed that anyone who looked at Christopher in the morning would not die suddenly that day.","c":"Ghirlandaio was a master of clear, orderly storytelling in fresco, and Saint Christopher is a subject whose energy rests on one strong figure, a good fit for him. The great scale gives the saint presence, and the fresco medium yields a matt, luminous finish. Beside his major cycles in Santa Trinita and Santa Maria Novella, a single saint is a lesser undertaking, and detaching the fresco inevitably separates it from the setting that gave it meaning."},"met:436040":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified man with a rosary, painted in oil on an oak panel measuring 47.6 by 35.2 centimeters about 1508. The Met's keywords record the rosary, a man and a portrait, so the beads, a sign of private devotion, are part of the sitter's presentation. The small, upright format is that of a bust or half-length portrait made for the sitter's own household rather than for public display.","i":"A rosary marks the sitter as a pious Catholic in a year, about 1508, when the Reformation was almost a decade away. Cranach, court painter to Frederick the Wise of Saxony from 1505, would later become Luther's friend and the leading artist of the Reformation, so a man with a rosary from his early Wittenberg years is a reminder of the old devotional world. The sitter is unidentified.","c":"Cranach's early portraits are more direct than elegant, with firm contours, saturated color and a searching look, and they show the vigor he brought from his years in Vienna, before the polished and slightly formulaic court style of his later decades. A man with a rosary offers a ready attribute, but the likeness is what matters: whether the face seems the product of observation, as the best of these early Cranachs do."},"met:436039":{"d":"A young woman kneels in the foreground in a red velvet gown with slashed, puffed sleeves and a necklace, her head bowed and her hands clasped. Behind her a bearded man in a turban hat and a red-and-yellow striped, slashed costume seizes her hair with one hand and swings a curved sword above her neck with the other. At the left, two soldiers in plate armor, one holding a halberd, and two older men look on, one wringing his hands. Behind rise a rocky, wooded hillside and a pair of hilltop towers. At the lower right a shield bears a coat of arms.","i":"Barbara, according to legend, was shut in a tower by her pagan father, Dioscorus, and when she refused to renounce Christianity he beheaded her himself. The Metropolitan Museum notes that the coat of arms shows the panel was made for a member of the Rem family, wealthy merchants of Augsburg. Cranach, who had been court painter to the Saxon electors at Wittenberg since 1505, dressed the figures in German fashion of about 1510, so the saint appears as a rich townswoman. Barbara became patron of miners and artillerymen, and of people facing sudden death.","c":"Cranach is as interested in clothing as in suffering. The executioner's slashed stripes, the saint's red velvet and the soldiers' polished armor are described with relish, so the cruelty turns decorative and the executioner looks like a figure from a pageant. Yet the composition is tight and strong, the figures filling the frame in a diagonal, and the saint's lowered head has a moving simplicity. Beside Grünewald's Passion scenes it lacks raw intensity, but it shows vividly what a rich Augsburg patron wanted a martyr to look like."},"nga:33750":{"d":"A small portrait of a prince of the ruling house of Saxony, in oil on a wood panel about 44 by 34 centimeters, painted about 1517 by Lucas Cranach the Elder. The almost identical size and date of A Princess of Saxony suggest that the two were made as a pair. Cranach was court painter to the electors of Saxony at Wittenberg, where he produced portraits of the family as part of his regular duties.","i":"Cranach worked for the electors of Saxony in Wittenberg from 1505 and made portraits of the family and court as a routine part of his duties. The title names the rank, not the person, so the sitter has not been identified with certainty. Portraits of princes and princesses were made for dynastic purposes: to be shown to relatives, allies and prospective marriage partners, and to hang in palaces as records of the family line.","c":"Cranach's court portraits are clear, crisp and decorative, with strong contour, bright color and a deliberate simplicity that suited their dynastic purpose. Alongside A Princess of Saxony it presumably shows the same direct, graphic manner. The pair is a good example of how Cranach adapted the image of rank to a smaller, more private scale."},"nga:33751":{"d":"Shown from the lap up, a young girl with pale skin and long, wavy golden-brown hair sits with her body angled slightly left, her head tipped forward and her slate-blue eyes looking off in that direction. She wears a crimson dress patterned in bands of floral ornament, with slashed, puffed sleeves through which the white linen shirt is pulled, a bodice laced in red and a jeweled band at the neck of her chemise. Three large necklaces of twisting gold links hang on her chest. Her wrists are crossed in her lap against a black background. Oil on panel, about 43 by 34 centimeters, about 1517.","i":"The three heavy necklaces, the slashed sleeves and the patterned crimson dress were the finery of a princess of the Saxon court, and the crossed hands and calm gaze show a child trained in dignified poise. Cranach, painter to the electors of Saxony, portrayed the family's children as symbols of the dynasty's future. The sitter is not identified; the title gives only her rank.","c":"A vivid small portrait in which the bold, flat red of the dress, the black ground and the gold necklaces make a hard, decorative pattern, while the young face is drawn with a gentle, observant precision. The child's solemn, slightly distant look escapes the stiffness of many court images. It is typical of Cranach's best portraits: quick to read, crisp in contour and vivid in color."},"nga:45886":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a clean-shaven man in black and burgundy red almost fills the vertical panel, his shoulders as wide as the picture and his hat nearly touching the top. His body and face are angled to the right, and he looks off with hooded brown eyes under slightly raised brows; he has a long, bumped nose, high cheekbones and closed pale lips. His hair is cut straight at ear length beneath a wide, soft black cap, and a voluminous dark red wrap covers his sloping shoulders. He stands against a lime-green ground lit from the upper right. Oil on panel, about 58 by 40 centimeters, dated 1522.","i":"Cranach's emblem, a winged serpent holding a ring, appears on a small panel in the upper left with the date 1522; the serpent was granted to him as a coat of arms by the Elector of Saxony in 1508 and served as a workshop mark. The sitter is unnamed. His sober black cap and shirt suggest a well-off man of the professional or merchant class, probably the pendant to the Portrait of a Woman of the same year.","c":"A powerful, economical portrait: the figure fills the panel, the black and red costume is a bold mass, and the acid green ground sets the face forward in a harsh, even light. The hooded gaze and long nose give him a quiet, guarded individuality that the flat treatment does not diminish. Cranach's portraits are sometimes accused of formula, but here the observation of the face has enough sharpness to make the formula vivid."},"nga:45887":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted in oil on a wood panel, about 58 by 40 centimeters, dated 1522, by Lucas Cranach the Elder. Her name is unknown. Its size and date match those of Cranach's Portrait of a Man, also of 1522, so the two were presumably made as a pair, probably a husband and wife. Cranach's studio in Wittenberg produced many such portraits for court officials and citizens.","i":"In sixteenth-century Germany a husband and wife were often painted in pendant portraits of matching size, hung side by side to mark a marriage and a household. Cranach's Wittenberg workshop supplied such portraits to the court, the university and the town's burghers. The woman's name and status are not known, and the pairing is an inference from size and date.","c":"Cranach's portraits of the early 1520s are direct and graphic: the sitter occupies most of the panel, the contour is firm, and the background is a flat, bright ground that sets the head forward. Taken together with the Man, the pair is a good record of Wittenberg's educated middle class at the dawn of the Reformation. Honest, decorative portraits like these are the workshop's hallmark."},"met:439081":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, 137.2 by 39.4 centimeters, in oil on linden, showing Saint Maurice, with armor and a sword among the details the Met records. The proportions are those of a single standing figure meant to fill one panel of a larger ensemble, such as an altarpiece wing, rather than an independent picture. The armor and sword mark him as the soldier saint, and the long format gives the figure a stately, upright presence.","i":"Maurice, in legend the commander of the Theban Legion, a Roman unit of Christian soldiers said to have been massacred about the year 300 for refusing to sacrifice to pagan gods, became the patron of soldiers and a major saint of the Holy Roman Empire, especially at Magdeburg, where his relics were kept. In German art he is sometimes shown as an African knight, reflecting the legion's supposed origin in Egypt.","c":"Cranach's workshop turned out standing saints in quantity, and the formula is clear: a tall, elegant figure, glinting armor, ornamental detail. The 'and Workshop' in the attribution means the design is Cranach's but the execution was probably shared with assistants, so the quality may vary across the panel. The strength is the decorative surface, with the linear patterns of armor and weapon; the weakness is a lack of the psychological bite of the master's portraits."},"met:436042":{"d":"Venus, goddess of love, and her son Cupid, painted in oil on a wood panel; the Met's keywords record a female nude, Cupid and Venus. In Cranach's Venus paintings the goddess is typically a slender, long-limbed, pale-skinned nude with small breasts and a firm, flowing outline, quite different from the full, sculptural Venuses of Italian painters, and Cupid appears as a plump, mischievous child beside her.","i":"Cranach painted Venus and Cupid many times for princely and aristocratic patrons, often adding a Latin verse that warned against lust. In one popular version Cupid steals honey from a hive and is stung by bees, a lesson that pleasure brings pain. Whether or not this panel carries such a verse, the type presents love as both attractive and dangerous, a classical theme made to suit northern, moralizing taste.","c":"Cranach's Venus is deliberately un-Italian: tiny-breasted, long-limbed and slightly sly, her body a flowing contour rather than a sculptural mass. That is both the charm and the limit: the figure is a decorative pattern more than a convincing form, but it has a sophisticated, knowing grace that suited northern princely taste. It is also a reminder that the workshop that painted Luther's portrait painted goddesses too."},"met:436041":{"d":"A small upright panel, 57.2 by 37.8 centimeters, in oil on beech, showing the biblical strongman Samson and his betrayer Delilah, with soldiers and a woman among the figures the Met records. The usual subject of such pictures is the moment of betrayal, when Samson's hair is cut while he sleeps and the Philistine soldiers close in to seize him, and the small, vertical format keeps the figures close to the viewer.","i":"Judges 16 tells how Samson's strength lay in his uncut hair; Delilah, bribed by the Philistines, learned the secret and while he slept had his hair cut, so that he was captured and blinded. The story belonged to the 'power of women' theme, popular in northern Renaissance art, which warned that even the strongest men can be undone by love. Cranach's biblical figures often wear contemporary dress, making such lessons immediate.","c":"Cranach tells the story with clarity and a certain dry wit: the figures are dressed in the fashions of his day, the gestures are legible, and the faces are slightly caricatured. What he cannot give is the physical force of Samson, who is a decorative figure like the rest, so that the drama is in the plot, not the paint. Among his biblical panels it is typical in its crispness and slightly arch tone."},"met:436033":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Lukas Spielhausen, dated 1532, painted in oil with gold on a beech panel measuring 50.8 by 36.5 centimeters. Gold in the medium points to precious details, perhaps in an inscription or in jewelry, of the kind Cranach used in his portraits. Cranach's portraits of the 1530s are typically small, bust-length, sharply outlined likenesses, often set against a plain colored ground.","i":"By 1532 Cranach had been Saxon court painter for more than a quarter-century and ran the largest workshop in Wittenberg, the center of the Reformation. A named, dated portrait like this was a mark of standing, the likeness of a man of position wanting to be remembered. Many portraits of the Reformation generation of officials, scholars and merchants survive nameless; Spielhausen's name has come down with his face.","c":"Cranach's 1530s portraits are crisp, linear and a little formulaic: strong outlines, clear color, and character in the set of the mouth and the steady eyes. The best have a candid, no-nonsense directness that survives the workshop's repetitions; the worst, a sameness of pose that makes different sitters resemble one another. Judge this one by the face and by how the gold lifts the surface from plain to precious."},"met:436037":{"d":"In a wooded glade beneath a big oak, a knight in contemporary plate armor and a red cloak lies back on the ground, looking up. Beside him stands Mercury, bearded, in a winged helmet and ornate armor, a glass orb in his hand, and he points toward three nude women at the right. They wear gold necklaces, and one has a red hat with a white plume. Seen front, side and back, they stand in a row. At upper left a small Cupid rides a cloud and aims his bow. A white horse waits at the left edge. Behind lie a lake with a walled town and a rocky crag crowned by a castle.","i":"In the Greek myth the shepherd prince Paris is asked to decide which of three goddesses, Juno, Minerva or Venus, is the most beautiful. The Metropolitan Museum explains that Mercury holds the golden apple, rendered here as a glass orb, and that Cupid's arrow signals Paris's choice of Venus. Cranach shows Paris as a German knight in up-to-date armor, as northern artists often did. He was court painter to the electors of Saxony at Wittenberg, and mythological scenes with Venus were among the most popular subjects his workshop produced for court patrons. He painted the theme many times.","c":"The picture amuses more than it convinces. The three slim, pale nudes with their necklaces and plumed hat are far from classical ideals, yet the pattern of light bodies against dark foliage is controlled and elegant, and Paris's armor is described with relish. Cranach is not interested in Greek beauty or tragedy; he offers a courtly entertainment in which fashionable accessories matter as much as the goddesses. As myth it is thin, but as a record of Saxon court taste it is exact."},"met:436043":{"d":"A small portrait of Frederick the Wise (1463-1525), Elector of Saxony, painted in oil on beech, only 20.3 by 14.3 centimeters, with letterpress-printed paper labels attached to the panel. It dates from 1533, eight years after the Elector's death, so it is a posthumous likeness, based on earlier portraits. The Met's subject tags list the Elector and a portrait, and the little panel and its printed labels are made to be read as well as looked at.","i":"Frederick the Wise was Cranach's patron and employer, the founder of the University of Wittenberg in 1502, and Martin Luther's protector, who had him hidden at the Wartburg after the Diet of Worms in 1521. His brother and successor John the Constant, and later John Frederick, continued that protection. Small portraits of these electors, probably part of a set, were made in the Cranach workshop to honor the dynasty that had shielded the new faith.","c":"Small, quickly produced and labeled, these portraits are more icons of a dynasty than studies of individuals. A posthumous portrait relies on a pattern, and Cranach's workshop could repeat the face of Frederick the Wise without needing to see him. The interest is historical, with the printed labels belonging to the Reformation's skill with print. Do not expect the sharp observation of a portrait taken from life."},"met:436045":{"d":"A small portrait of John the Constant (1468-1532), Elector of Saxony, in oil on beech, only 20.3 by 14.3 centimeters, painted in 1532-33 with letterpress-printed paper labels attached. The size matches that of the portrait of his brother Frederick the Wise, so the two were probably made as a set. It was probably painted around or just after the Elector's death in 1532, and the labels identify the sitter and his title.","i":"John the Constant succeeded his brother Frederick as elector in 1525 and, unlike Frederick, openly supported the Reformation: he signed the Augsburg Confession in 1530 and joined the Protestant princes' defensive league. The epithet, the Constant or Steadfast, praised his firmness in religion. His portrait, with those of Frederick and his son John Frederick, forms a dynastic gallery of Reformation protectors.","c":"Same size, same workshop and the same function as its companion, this panel shares its virtues and limits: a clear, assured outline, a plain ground, and a face that serves as a badge of office. The printed labels give identification, which suggests the picture was made to be seen by many and quickly understood. As portraiture it is formulaic; as political imagery it is efficient, and in the age of the printing press that was its point."},"met:436038":{"d":"The biblical heroine Judith holds a sword and the severed head of the Assyrian general Holofernes, in oil on a linden panel measuring 89.5 by 61.9 centimeters; the Met's keywords record Judith and Holofernes, the head, the sword and a woman. Cranach painted several Judiths, usually as a composed, fashionably dressed lady of his own day who shows her grim trophy with surprising calm, a combination of elegance and violence that is the picture's hallmark.","i":"In the Book of Judith, the widow Judith saves her besieged city of Bethulia by entering the camp of the Assyrian general Holofernes and, when he lies drunk, cutting off his head. She was read as an example of courage, faith and chastity, and in Cranach's day also as one of the 'power of women' figures popular in northern art. Luther placed the book among the Apocrypha, useful and good to read though not scripture.","c":"The paradox of Cranach's Judith is her calm: a smartly dressed woman, not a heroine in a frenzy, displays a bloody head with composed elegance, and the contrast is chilling and a little erotic, which was the point. The stylization works for and against him, since the head of Holofernes can read as a prop. Set beside Caravaggio's bloody Judith of 1599, Cranach's version is cool, decorative and unmistakably northern."},"nga:42393":{"d":"The Virgin, seen from the waist up behind a table, steadies the unclothed Christ Child, who stands on one leg on the tabletop with the other foot resting on his knee. Her expression is serene as she looks down at him, and her dress has wide sleeves of bright red, teal and orange. With her free hand she holds out a bunch of green grapes. The child holds an apple in one hand and lifts a grape to his mouth with the other. On the gray table are a glass of yellow liquid, presumably wine, and another apple. Oil on panel, about 71 by 52 centimeters.","i":"The fruit is the key. The apple recalls the fruit of the Fall and presents Christ as the new Adam who will undo its consequences, and the grapes, with the glass of wine, point to the Eucharist and to the blood of the Passion. The child's casual stance and the domestic table make the symbolism feel everyday. Cranach, a friend of Martin Luther, painted many such Madonnas, which were acceptable to many Lutheran as well as Catholic buyers.","c":"Cranach's Madonnas are charming and often a little worldly, and this one, with its playful child and still-life table, is an example. The drawing is crisp and the colors vivid, but the faces follow a formula that the workshop repeated, and the late date may mean that parts were by assistants. The scene's engaging informality and its clever symbolism keep it from the routine."},"nga:46168":{"d":"Three men hang on rough-hewn crosses on a low, stony hill against a stormy sky, while an armored man on a rearing silvery-gray horse looks up at the central cross. Christ, with a close-cropped beard and a ring of thorns, tilts his head, his eyes rolled up and his loincloth swirling in the wind; the two thieves on the outer crosses turn inward. The rider wears armor and a ruby-red hat with white feathers and raises two fingers. The sky deepens from yellow through orange and pink to indigo under roiling gray clouds, and German inscriptions quote the Gospels. Oil on panel, about 51 by 35 centimeters, dated 1536.","i":"The armored rider is the Roman centurion who, in the Gospels, saw the manner of Christ's death and declared that truly this man was the Son of God; his words appear in German beside his mouth, and Christ's last words, a prayer commending his spirit to the Father, appear above the cross. Placing Scripture in the vernacular within the picture reflects the Lutheran emphasis on the Word. Cranach, a close friend of Luther, painted this subject often.","c":"A concentrated, high-contrast Crucifixion in which the rearing horse and the stormy sky supply the drama, and the three crosses form a stark diagonal against the lurid sunset. The small size lets Cranach pack in doctrine, text and spectacle without crowding. The handling is meticulous and the figures slightly stylized, and the German inscriptions give it a documentary, almost pamphlet-like force. A fine example of the Reformation-era devotional panel."},"met:436036":{"d":"A portrait of John, Hereditary Prince of Saxony (1498-1537), painted in oil on beech, 65.1 by 44.1 centimeters, about 1534 to 1537. At this size, larger than the small panels of the Saxon electors, the portrait has room for a bust or half-length with the sitter's costume, in the clear, strongly outlined manner of Cranach's workshop, and it was probably made for a princely household.","i":"John was the eldest son of Duke George the Bearded of Saxony, the most determined Catholic opponent of Luther among the German princes, and he died in 1537, before he could succeed his father. His family, the Albertine line, ruled from Dresden and was separate from the Wittenberg electors whom Cranach served, which shows that the painter also worked for the Catholic cousins across the confessional divide.","c":"A more formal portrait than the electors' small panels, this has room for the sitter's costume and a firmer sense of presence. Cranach's workshop style, with its clean outline and flat color, suits a princely likeness: it sets rank above personality. The expected virtues are crisp drawing and rich textile detail, and the expected limit is a certain impersonality. At about 65 centimeters it is a representative court portrait of the 1530s."},"nga:43716":{"d":"A nude woman with pale skin lies across a grassy field before a pool fed from a rocky outcrop. She reclines against a scarlet garment under her shoulders, her head on her raised arm, her face turned toward the viewer, eyes nearly closed. A nearly invisible gauze veil crosses her forehead and hips, and she wears a black cord, a gold chain with a ruby and pearl pendant, a jeweled bracelet and rings. Two partridges walk nearby, and a bow and red quiver hang from a tree. A Latin plaque says that the nymph of the sacred spring rests. Oil on panel, about 48 by 73 centimeters, after 1537.","i":"The sleeping nymph of a spring was a favorite of Cranach and his workshop, who made many versions. The Latin inscription, in which the nymph asks not to be disturbed in her sleep, comes from a verse that Renaissance humanists believed to be antique, and the bow and quiver in the tree suggest the huntress Diana's world. The nude, adorned with jewels and a transparent veil, joined classical learning with erotic charm for princely collectors; the partridges are sometimes read as signs of desire.","c":"Cranach's nymph is one of the most alluring and strange images of German Renaissance art: the long, pale, sinuous body, the minimal veil and heavy jewelry, the cool green setting. The proportions owe more to Gothic elegance than to Italian anatomy, which gives the figure a coolness that is the picture's special charm. If the learned conceit is thin, the fusion of eroticism and artifice is unusually effective, and it made the type a favorite for generations."},"met:437032":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, 94.9 by 28.9 centimeters, in oil on wood, with two scenes: Saint Paul accompanied by a donor, and Christ appearing to his mother. The museum's keywords note a man, Christ, Saint Paul and the Virgin Mary. The format suggests the wing of an altarpiece, where narrow panels held a standing saint with his kneeling patron and a smaller episode from the story of Christ.","i":"Christ's appearance to his mother after the Resurrection is not told in the Gospels but is a long-established devotional tradition, in which Christ first shows himself to Mary. Saint Paul, the apostle to the gentiles, is shown with the donor who paid for the altarpiece, often a man who bore the saint's name; donors are usually portrayed kneeling and protected by their patron saint, a statement of piety and of hope for salvation.","c":"Netherlandish painters of the 1480s combine tenderness and precision, and the quiet intimacy of the apparition and the portrait-like donor are the draw here. A tall, narrow format is awkward, and the difficulty is to give each scene breathing room; a viewer should look at how the painter fits the standing saint and the episode into the same panel. It is a modest, devout work by an anonymous master."},"met:437006":{"d":"A mythological scene of childbirth in oil on a wood panel, 108.0 by 130.5 centimeters. The Met's keywords list nudes, Venus, Cupid, childbirth, a pillow, an urn and a curtain, so the setting is apparently a draped interior in which the goddess of love gives birth to her son. The picture is a large, wide mythology of the kind made for a courtly or princely room, in the elegant figure style of sixteenth-century taste.","i":"The birth of Cupid is a rare subject: in classical sources Cupid is usually simply Venus's son, born of her union with Mars in some versions or Mercury in others, and the childbirth itself is almost never shown. Sixteenth-century patrons delighted in unusual mythological themes that allowed painters to show nude women in elegant interiors. Such a subject turns the origin of love into a domestic event, an idea that suited courtly audiences amused by learned wit.","c":"The painter is known only by a conventional name, but the picture's ambition is clear: a big, sophisticated mythology with polished nudes and a well-furnished interior. Pictures of this kind can be elegant and a little cold, favoring poised bodies and decorative accessories over feeling. The strength is imaginative subject matter, and the risk is an artificial surface. Judge the nudes for grace and the curtain, urn and pillow for invention."},"nga:486":{"d":"A long, horizontal landscape seen from above. At the left, by a group of green trees, several people in light red, dark green and pale yellow garments stand around a person on a white horse, with a gray castle behind and boats sailing on green water near wooded hills. In the center rise massive gray rocks, with a small yellow tent at their base and people sitting or standing around it. To the right two deer drink from a blue pool beside a figure wrapped in green cloth, and a long path leads people carrying poles or weapons toward a large gray castle or city in the top right corner.","i":"The legend has not been identified, and it is told here in the manner of a continuous narrative, in which the same characters can reappear in different parts of a single landscape. The shape, long and narrow, suggests that the panel decorated a piece of furniture such as a chest or the paneling of a room. The painter, Giovanni Larciani, is known as the Master of the Kress Landscapes after pictures like this, in which small figures wander through fanciful rocky country.","c":"The landscape is the star: stacked gray rocks, a bright pool and hilltop castles are invented with fairy-tale zest, and the tiny figures give the eye places to travel. The color, mostly earthy browns and greens beneath a greenish sky fading to pink, is pleasing but dry. What disappoints is the narrative: with the legend unidentified, the actions are hard to read. It is a charming oddity of Florentine painting around 1515 to 1520."},"nga:487":{"d":"Two pairs of people and a group line a rocky path that climbs steeply away from the viewer through a hilly landscape. Dark trees sit on the knolls to each side, a pink-stone building with a crenelated wall and tower stands left of center, and hills pile up to a horizon near the top edge beneath a narrow strip of aqua-blue sky. In the nearest pair, one figure in cardinal red and one in a black cloak face each other; farther along, two men in gray hooded monks' robes do the same, and tiny figures by the tower point toward a man on a donkey.","i":"Like its companions, this panel tells part of a legend whose subject has not been identified. The pairs meeting on the path, the monks in gray habits and the rider on a donkey suggest a story of encounter and journey, perhaps of a holy man's wandering, but the exact incident is uncertain. Panels of this kind, with small figures in a long landscape, may have been set into the furniture or paneling of a wealthy Florentine room.","c":"The steep path, with its pairs of figures like beads on a string, is a clever way to organize depth, and the pink building and dark trees give notes of color against the greens. The figures are too small to carry emotion, and their faces and heads are hard to make out. The pleasure lies in the picturesque landscape and the quirky imagination, not in narrative drama, and a viewer should enjoy it as a small, odd, inventive view."},"nga:488":{"d":"A person in a dark green robe and pink hood or cap, carrying a yellow sack over one shoulder and a walking stick, crosses a rocky path toward a fortified town with crenelated gates and walls in the distance. Boulders rise to either side and leafy trees grow up the right edge; several swans float on a lake just beyond them. A figure painted in gray rides a camel beyond a boulder at the left, and thin strokes of red or black suggest more people far away. Trees nestle between hills that roll back to hazy sage-green mountains.","i":"A traveler with a sack and staff suggests a pilgrim or wanderer, a common figure in medieval legends of conversion and penance, and the camel, an animal that meant the East to Florentines, hints at a story set in distant lands. As with its companions, the legend has not been identified. The lone figure walking toward a walled town can be read as a journey toward a goal, a motif that suits saints' lives.","c":"Of the three panels this has the clearest focus: a single walker, a path and a distant goal give the landscape a direction, and the swans on the lake supply a note of calm. The handling of the faraway figures with thin strokes is deft. Yet the story stays elusive and the figure's drawing is slight; the painting is best valued as a lyrical landscape with an unexplained journey in it."},"met:437017":{"d":"A Virgin and Child in oil on a small wooden panel, 48.6 by 38.7 centimeters. The Met's keywords record only Mary and the Christ Child, so the picture is of the type, a devotional Madonna and Child intended for a private home or chapel. In the Netherlands of the early sixteenth century such panels were produced in large numbers, typically with the Virgin shown as a gentle, idealized young mother and the child held close to her.","i":"The Virgin and Child was the most widely painted devotional subject in Christian Europe, a focus of private prayer that stressed Mary's role as mother and as intercessor. The painter is anonymous, named only after a Magdalen in the Palazzo Mansi at Lucca, a reminder that many Netherlandish masters are known by the pictures that defined them rather than by their names. Such masters supplied a thriving market for devotional images.","c":"The virtue of such pictures is quiet appeal: a calm face, delicate color and detail polished with a fine brush. Their weakness is sameness, since the Madonna type was repeated again and again with small variations. How far this panel escapes the formula depends on the individuality of the faces and the freshness of the color. As the work of an anonymous master it is best valued for its craftsmanship and devotional gentleness."},"nga:41702":{"d":"Against a gold ground, Christ sets a crown on the bowed head of Mary as they sit side by side on a wide gold throne, surrounded by eight smaller winged angels. Christ, at the right, wears a buttercup-yellow robe under a lapis-blue cloak and holds a staff; Mary, in a rose-pink dress and blue mantle, raises both hands toward him and looks out at the viewer. Two angels hold a ruby-red cloth behind them, and the curved back of the throne is painted with gold stars on blue. The Roman numerals for 1324 appear on the throne's base.","i":"The Coronation of the Virgin, in which Christ crowns his mother Queen of Heaven after her Assumption, is not told in the Gospels but grew into one of the most popular images of medieval and early Renaissance Italy, presenting Mary as the crowned intercessor for humankind. Because the date 1324 is painted on the throne, the panel is a securely dated work of the Trecento, made when Giotto's innovations were spreading but gold grounds and symmetrical thrones remained the norm.","c":"The anonymous master, named after this picture, works in a formal, gently decorative style: a symmetrical throne, repeated angels with near-identical faces and shining gold all stress solemn ceremony over drama. The pale, greenish faces are a little blank, as in much Trecento work, but the delicate color and the carved arch with gold leaves give it a jeweled richness. Its date makes it a useful fixed point for historians, which lends the panel importance beyond its charm."},"met:437008":{"d":"A crowded scene of Christ's body being lowered from the cross, in oil on a wood panel 91.4 by 72.4 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include Christ, the Virgin Mary, Mary Magdalene, Saint John the Evangelist, men and women, and hammers, the tools used to free the nails. The vertical format and large size suggest a panel from an altarpiece or a devotional work for a chapel, where the grief of the Passion was meant to be shared by the viewer.","i":"In the Gospels, Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus take Christ's body down after the Crucifixion, and the Virgin, John and the holy women attend. Since Rogier van der Weyden's famous version of the 1430s, Netherlandish painters made the Descent a theme of intense, theatrical grief. The hammers and pincers are instruments of the Passion, the means by which the nails were drawn, and they remind the viewer of the cost of redemption.","c":"Any Descent from the Netherlands must be measured against Rogier's, and the question is whether a later painter adds energy or merely rearranges gestures. Crowded scenes like this can impress with their sharply observed faces and costumes but risk a muddle of limbs and attitudes. The Master of the Holy Blood, a Netherlandish painter of the generation after the great fifteenth-century masters, is best judged by how legible the grief is across so many figures."},"met:435822":{"d":"The archangel Gabriel and the Virgin Mary meet in the Annunciation, the moment when the angel announces that she will bear the Son of God. The panel, about 81 by 51 centimeters, is painted in oil with gold, and its paint layer has been transferred from the original wood to canvas, a conservation measure often used on damaged panels. The catalog gives no further description, so the room, the gestures and the attributes are left unstated; the vertical format and the date, about 1505, suggest that it may once have belonged to a larger altarpiece.","i":"Gabriel's greeting, Hail, full of grace (Luke 1:28), and Mary's acceptance mark the Incarnation, the starting point of Christian salvation. Painters usually place the scene in a domestic interior with a lily for purity, a book for Mary's piety and sometimes a dove for the Holy Spirit; whether those details appear here the catalog does not say. Spanish painters around 1500 often borrowed such formulas from Netherlandish pictures, and the Budapest Master, an anonymous painter named by scholars after a work in Budapest, is among them.","c":"The anonymous Budapest Master combines Spanish gravity with Netherlandish detail, a pairing typical of Hispano-Flemish painting around 1500. Paint transferred from wood to canvas usually loses some of its original delicacy of surface, so the panel's present state is probably a poor guide to its first effect. The subject, an intimate dialogue between two figures, suited a taste for precise detail and quiet piety. It is a respectable devotional work, rather than a pioneering one."},"nga:41698":{"d":"A dozen people surround Saint Clare as she lies on a bed against a gleaming gold ground, her hands crossed on her chest and her eyes closed. Nuns in black habits attend her, and five crowned, haloed women in blue, green, white and burgundy and gold line the far side of the bed, each holding a different object, among them a tower and a basket of flowers. Two more kneel in front, one with a tiny dragon, the other with a small sheep. Angels swing incense burners above, and a bearded man at the top holds a tiny crowned figure.","i":"Clare of Assisi (1194-1253), the follower of Saint Francis who founded the Poor Clares, died after a long illness. Legend says that holy virgins crowned like queens came to her bedside, with the Virgin Mary among them. The attributes identify the visitors as virgin martyrs: Barbara's tower, Dorothy's flowers, Margaret's dragon and Agnes's lamb, and the small crowned figure at the top is probably her soul, received by God. The picture would have comforted nuns hoping for a similarly peaceful death.","c":"Painted around 1400 in Austrian lands, this panel belongs to the International Gothic taste: elegant, courtly women with delicate features, rich brocades and a gold ground punched with patterns. The crowd of saints with toy-sized attributes is charming and a little naive, and the tilted bed keeps everyone visible. It lacks the spatial solidity of Italian painting of the time but offers decorative richness and, at the center, a tenderness where one saint holds Clare's face in her hands."},"met:437859":{"d":"A woman in profile, bust-length, painted in tempera on a wood panel measuring 41.3 by 31.1 centimeters, about 1445. The Met's keywords record a woman, a profile and a portrait. Strict profile was the standard format for portraits of women in mid-fifteenth-century Florence, borrowed from ancient coins and medals and well suited to showing hair, jewels and dress in clear outline. The panel is small enough to have hung in a private room.","i":"Profile portraits of young women were typically painted around the time of a betrothal or marriage, to show a family's wealth and the bride's beauty and virtue; the elaborate hairstyle, jewels and brocades told the story of an alliance between families. The painter, Giovanni di Francesco del Cervelliera, was a Florentine of the generation of Castagno and Domenico Veneziano. The sitter is unidentified, and her profile, like a coin's, presents a public image rather than a private mood.","c":"A profile portrait stands or falls on its outline: the clean silhouette of brow, nose, lips and chin, and the patterns of hair and costume laid in clear shapes. The best of the type, by Piero della Francesca or Pollaiuolo, give the profile a sculptural force that a lesser Florentine may not match. Still, the format has a hieratic beauty, and attention to the woman's coiffure and dress repays the effort."},"met:437030":{"d":"A small portrait panel, 21.0 by 13.0 centimeters, in oil on wood, about 1480, showing a young man holding a book. The Met's keywords add a church building, so a church appears in the background or at a window. The very small size and narrow shape suggest a panel made to be hinged to another, perhaps a devotional image, so that the young man faced a sacred figure in prayer.","i":"Small portraits with a book, a sign of reading and piety, often formed one half of a diptych whose other half showed the Virgin and Child, and the sitter was shown praying in her presence. The painter takes his name from a picture of the church of Saint Gudula in Brussels and worked in the late fifteenth century. The sitter is unidentified.","c":"Late fifteenth-century Netherlandish portraits are reserved and exact, and a panel this small must make its effect through sharpness: the contour of the face, the soft light on skin, the fine detail of fingers and pages. The pleasure is intimacy, and the way a distant church enlarges the little space. A viewer should look closely at the book, which gives the painter his best excuse to show off tiny, precise craftsmanship."},"met:437174":{"d":"A tall canvas, close to two metres high, shows an unnamed Spanish gentleman as a knight of one of the country's military orders, the badge of the order marking his rank. The title leaves open whether the order is Alcántara or Calatrava, two of Spain's orders of knighthood. The scale is that of a formal standing portrait, and the picture dates from the early to middle 1650s, when Murillo was painting in Seville.","i":"The Spanish military orders, such as Santiago, Calatrava and Alcántara, began as medieval crusading brotherhoods and by the seventeenth century had become royal honours. To enter one, a man had to prove noble descent and 'purity of blood', so the cross on the chest was a prized public sign of status. A portrait of a knight is therefore partly a certificate of rank, and it is usually read as a statement of lineage and loyalty to the crown.","c":"Murillo is remembered for devotional painting, and his portraits are few, so a knight on this scale is a rarity. The format owes something to Velázquez's court portraits, softened by Murillo's gentler touch, and it gives the sitter a quiet dignity. It lacks the penetrating psychology and the daring brushwork of Velázquez, and the sitter's identity is lost, but it remains an authoritative, well-composed likeness."},"nga:1185":{"d":"Two women lean on a window sill in a large canvas, 125.1 by 104.5 centimeters. The younger, her head covered by a shawl, smiles directly at the viewer; her older companion, partly in shadow, covers her own laughter with her shawl. The pair seem to share a joke at the viewer's expense, or to watch a passer-by, and the sill pushes them forward into the viewer's space, as if the window opened onto the room where the picture hangs.","i":"Murillo, the leading painter of Seville, made religious pictures for churches and convents and, for a different market, scenes of street children and women, which found buyers among Seville's merchants, Flemish traders among them. A young woman at a window, standing between the private house and the public street, is a loaded subject in Spanish and Dutch art, suggesting flirtation, and the older woman has been read as a chaperone or go-between. The exchange is left to the viewer to imagine.","c":"The charm lies in the glance: a smile aimed straight at the viewer, an older woman's stifled laugh, and a warm, soft light that keeps the exchange immediate and unforced. It is among Murillo's most captivating secular pictures and free of the sugar that clouds many of his devotional images. The risk is cuteness, since the narrative is a little calculated, but the vitality of the faces and the loose, warm paint carry it."},"nga:34956":{"d":"A very large, nearly square canvas, 236.3 by 261.0 centimeters, showing the return of the prodigal son, the moment of the parable when the repentant younger son comes home and his father meets him. The parable's cast also includes servants who bring the best robe, a ring and shoes, and the fatted calf for the feast, so pictures of the subject usually combine a central embrace with a bustle of household figures. This canvas belongs to a cycle of paintings on the parable.","i":"In Luke 15 a younger son demands his inheritance, wastes it abroad and is reduced to feeding pigs before he decides to go home and ask to be a servant; his father, seeing him from afar, runs to embrace him. The parable of forgiveness was a favorite of Counter-Reformation Spain, where repentance and the sacrament of penance were emphasized. Murillo told the whole story in a series of canvases, of which the Return is the happy conclusion.","c":"Murillo's strengths, warm light, glowing flesh and cloth, and a humane tenderness of feeling, suit a story of forgiveness, and the embrace is the emotional center. At nearly two and a half meters a side the scale is ambitious, though a crowd of servants and animals can dilute the central moment, and the sweetness that flatters his reputation also limits it. It is among the most eloquent of his narrative works, more convincing than many of his single devotional figures."},"met:437173":{"d":"A tall canvas, about two metres high, shows a Spanish gentleman, Don Andrés de Andrade y la Cal, with a dog beside him. The scale is that of a formal standing portrait, and it places the sitter in the manner of Spanish noble portraiture, upright and dignified, with the animal as companion and attribute. Murillo made it in the later part of his career, between about 1665 and 1672, probably in Seville.","i":"In seventeenth-century Spain a portrait with a dog presented a gentleman as a man of leisure and breeding: hunting dogs were a nobleman's companions, and the dog also stood for loyalty. The sitter is named, and the picture is read mainly through its conventions of dress, bearing and companion animal. Portraits like this were commissioned to be kept in a family house as proof of presence and standing.","c":"Portraiture was a minor part of Murillo's career, but his few portraits are among his finest. They have a sympathetic warmth and a softness of touch that set them apart from the harder, more formal Spanish court portraits, and a sitter such as this appears as a man, not just a rank. The dog adds ease. If the picture has a weakness, it is a certain reserve: Murillo does not probe his sitters as Velázquez does."},"met:437175":{"d":"The Virgin sits three-quarter length against a dark, smoky background, her head tilted as she looks down tenderly. She wears a red gown, a brown shawl over one shoulder and a blue underskirt, and one hand reaches down and out of the picture. In her lap the Christ Child, a chubby boy with curly fair hair, sits half wrapped in a white cloth; he looks straight out at the viewer and holds the edge of her dress at the neck. A faint glow lights the mother and child against the gloom. The edges of the forms are soft and the paint thin and warm. Oil on canvas, about 166 by 109 centimetres, in a tall upright format.","i":"Murillo was the most popular painter in Seville, and his Madonnas set the type of the tender, human Virgin for Spanish Catholics. This one belongs to the 1670s, in his late period, when his handling became softer and more atmospheric, a manner sometimes called vaporous. In an age when the Church encouraged believers to imagine the sacred family in familiar terms, the painter gave the Virgin the gentleness of a Sevillian mother. The Child's direct gaze and his tug at her dress are an informal touch. Murillo's reputation in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries was enormous, and Madonnas of this kind were widely copied.","c":"The attraction is warmth: the Virgin's lowered gaze, the soft light on the Child's flesh and the feathered edges are all done with ease. This is Murillo at his most appealing, and the Victorian taste for his Madonnas is easy to understand. It is also where the criticism begins: the sweetness comes close to sentimentality, the faces are idealised types, and the dark ground leaves the composition a little empty and the red gown slack. Beside the austerity of Zurbarán or the penetration of Velázquez, it seems gentle rather than deep."},"met:437172":{"d":"A small upright canvas, about 51 by 33 centimetres, shows Christ crucified. The subject appears to be Christ alone on the cross, the single-figure Crucifixion, an image of his sacrifice meant to be contemplated closely, of a kind Murillo painted several times. At this size it would have served in a chapel or a private room rather than as a major altarpiece, and it dates from the painter's last decade.","i":"In Counter-Reformation Spain the Crucifixion was the central image of private devotion. Seville's religious brotherhoods and convents promoted meditation on Christ's Passion, and small paintings let the faithful pray before a vivid image of his suffering at home or in a cell. Murillo's Crucifixions are generally gentler than the severe, sculptural Christs of earlier Spanish artists; they invite sorrow and consolation more than horror.","c":"Murillo's talent was for tenderness, and a Crucifixion gives him a harder test than the children and Virgins he is loved for. The strength of such a picture is the human, sorrowful Christ and the controlled light. The danger is sweetness, a tendency to smooth over the pain. In a small format the risk is lessened, and the picture can be taken as an intimate devotional work, though not one of his most searching."},"met:438028":{"d":"A small panel, 41.9 by 24.1 centimeters, in tempera on wood with a gold ground, showing the Crucifixion. The Met's subject tags list Christ, Mary Magdalene, Saint John the Evangelist, angels, women and nuns, together with a Madonna and Child, so the cast goes beyond the usual group of mourners. The gold ground is a traditional choice that lifts the scene out of ordinary space into a timeless realm of devotion, and the narrow upright shape suits a panel made for private prayer.","i":"In John's Gospel the Virgin, Mary Magdalene and John stand at the foot of the cross, and angels gathering Christ's blood or mourning in the air are a medieval addition. The presence of nuns suggests a link to a religious house, perhaps as the people for whom the panel was made. Uccello, remembered for his battle pictures and his obsession with perspective, also painted devotional works like this, which show another side of a Florentine workshop.","c":"Uccello's reputation rests on geometry, and in a small gold-ground Crucifixion the geometry is in the drapery, the patterned halos and the neat symmetry of the group more than in any plunging perspective. The figures tend to be angular and stiff, and emotion is controlled. At about 42 centimeters it is a jewel-like object, attractive for its color and craft rather than its invention, and a reminder that even great experimenters painted conventional panels."},"met:10928":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, only 4.4 by 3.4 centimeters, dated 1786 and showing a man named John Brown. The painter, Robert Fulton, was then about twenty and working as a miniature painter and portraitist, long before he became famous as an engineer. At this size the picture is a head, a collar and a little background, made to be held in the palm or fitted into a locket.","i":"Robert Fulton is remembered as the engineer of the Clermont steamboat of 1807 and of an early submarine, the Nautilus, but he began his working life as a painter. He made portraits and miniatures in Philadelphia in the 1780s and then went to London to study with Benjamin West, exhibiting at the Royal Academy in the early 1790s before turning to engineering. A miniature dated 1786 therefore comes from the end of his first career.","c":"The interest of this miniature is largely its maker: a young self-taught painter's neat, careful likeness in the plain, direct manner of American miniatures of the 1780s. At under five centimeters it leaves little room for invention, and the viewer's task is to see whether the face is alive. It is an agreeable, minor work, valued above all for showing that the future engineer could draw a convincing head."},"met:14352":{"d":"A woman painted in watercolor on ivory about 1785, 5.7 by 4.8 centimeters. The Met's keywords list lace, a feather, an earring and a fichu, the light scarf draped around the neck and shoulders, so the sitter appears in the fashionable details of the 1780s rather than as a bare head. The miniature is by Robert Fulton, who is better known as an engineer, and it is small enough to be worn or kept in a case.","i":"Lace, a plume and a fichu were marks of fashion and of care in dress, and a woman who chose them for a portrait wanted to be seen as stylish as well as respectable. In the 1780s miniatures on ivory were favored by the merchant and professional classes of American cities, and Fulton, a young self-taught painter in Philadelphia, earned a living from them. The sitter is unnamed.","c":"Fulton's early miniatures tend to be neat and slightly stiff, the work of a self-taught hand following English models, and the finery here, lace, feather and fichu, does a good deal of the work of conveying elegance. The charm is the freshness of a young painter's attention to detail rather than any subtlety in the face. As proof that the future steamboat builder could make a convincing likeness it is persuasive."},"met:10927":{"d":"Cornelia Paterson, Mrs. Stephen Van Rensselaer III, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1810 and measuring 7.9 by 6.4 centimeters. The title gives her married name followed by her maiden name in parentheses. The museum's keywords note a woman and a portrait; as in most ivory miniatures, the face is the finest part, built from tiny strokes, with the dress and ground treated more broadly.","i":"Stephen Van Rensselaer III (1764-1839) was the last patroon, the head of the vast Rensselaerswyck estate on the Hudson, a lieutenant governor of New York and the founder of Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. Cornelia Paterson, his second wife, was the daughter of the New Jersey statesman William Paterson. If the date of about 1810 is right, the miniature comes from a time when Fulton was a famous engineer and had long since given up regular portrait painting.","c":"A miniature of a patroon's wife by a famous engineer has a good story, and the picture is judged chiefly by whether the likeness stands up to it. At nearly eight centimeters there is room for modeling in the face, and the date places it among Fulton's rare late returns to the craft, which are incidental to his career. It is more interesting for its sitter and its maker than for any individual stroke."},"met:10929":{"d":"Susan Hayne Simmons, Mrs. Manigault Heyward, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1813 and measuring 9.3 by 7.1 centimeters. The title records her married name and, in parentheses, her maiden name; names like Heyward, Manigault and Hayne are associated with South Carolina. At this size the miniature has room for a bust with some dress, as well as a carefully modeled face.","i":"A miniature by Robert Fulton dated about 1813, two years before his death in 1815, is something of an oddity: he had abandoned regular painting for engineering in the 1790s and was occupied with steamboats and a steam warship for the United States Navy. The title preserves the sitter's two family names, as is common in American miniatures. Such a likeness was a keepsake of a person's face for her family.","c":"A late work by an artist who had long ceased to paint professionally must be judged as a skilled amateur's return to a craft in which he once made his living. The format is large for an ivory, and the likeness, rather than any flourish, is what matters. It lacks the practice of a full-time miniaturist but gains the interest of a famous name, and that alone is a reason to look closely."},"cma:145999":{"d":"George Pitt, painted by Gainsborough in 1769 in oil on a tall canvas of 234.3 by 154.3 centimeters, the scale of a full-length portrait. The Cleveland Museum describes the sitter as an English gentleman at home on his country estate, in a military uniform of deep scarlet with green facings, set against a cool woodland landscape painted with wide, liquid brushstrokes of mauve, gray and silver. The red and green of the uniform are balanced against that silvery setting.","i":"George Pitt (1721-1803), later Baron Rivers, was a landowner, member of Parliament and diplomat, painted between his posts as ambassador to Turin and to Madrid. The museum notes that he reputedly led a dissolute life and was a cruel husband, yet the portrait gives him dignity and refinement, a reminder that a portrait of rank was an exercise in flattery. The uniform marks him as a soldier as well as a landowner.","c":"Gainsborough gives a doubtful character the bearing of a natural aristocrat, and the portrait is the better for the cool, silvery landscape that makes the scarlet glow. The feathery woodland shows the landscape painter's freedom, which he reportedly valued above portraiture. The sitter is stiff beside that freedom, and flattery is part of the trade, but as an image of English confidence in the late 1760s it is hard to better."},"nga:46087":{"d":"A young, rosy-cheeked boy stands against a landscape of trees and rolling hills, turning his gray-blue eyes toward the viewer. He wears a long white dress-like garment with satin bands on the sleeves and hem, gathered at the waist with a robin's egg-blue sash, and holds a small bouquet of white and yellow flowers in one hand and a large black hat with a plume in the other, with one red shoe showing. The background is loosely painted in muted greens, browns and golds.","i":"In the eighteenth century boys wore dresses until they were breeched, at about five or six, so the long white garment is ordinary dress for a small child. Portraits of children grew in popularity as new ideas of childhood, influenced by Rousseau, valued innocence and natural freedom, and a child set in a landscape with flowers suited that ideal. The plumed hat is a sign of gentility, and the sitter's family is named in the title.","c":"Gainsborough's gift for children is freshness: the face is carefully drawn, and the loose, feathery touch of the background gives the portrait a soft atmosphere that Reynolds's more learned child portraits lack. The risk is sweetness, and the sash and flowers lean toward prettiness. But the clear gaze and the airy landscape give the boy presence, and the picture shows the lightness of touch that distinguishes Gainsborough from his greatest rival."},"cma:124080":{"d":"Mary Wise, shown in her twenties with her hair powdered fashionably gray, in a compact oval format on a canvas of 76.2 by 63.6 centimeters. She wears a gown embroidered with gold flower sprigs and trimmed with lace and pearls, tied with a gold sash, with a gold-striped veil and a bracelet of eight gold bands. Her demeanor is modest and contemplative, and the informal pose and close oval lend the portrait an air of intimacy.","i":"The contrast between the sitter's modest, contemplative manner and her elaborate costume of gold, lace and pearls is the portrait's point: the finery announces wealth while the lowered, thoughtful look presents a woman of virtue. Gainsborough painted it about 1774, around the time he moved from Bath to London, where he built a fashionable portrait practice. The oval format, typical of intimate portraits, suggests a likeness made for a family, not for display.","c":"This is Gainsborough in a quiet register: the sitter's contemplative air counts for more than display, and the embroidered gold and pearls are likely touched in with his famously light, feathery touch so that the finery shimmers rather than glares. The oval gives a close, private feeling, and the gray-powdered hair is a stylish note. The danger in such portraits is prettiness, and here restraint saves it."},"nga:1159":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a young woman with gray, powdered hair piled high is lit starkly against a dark background, her face square to the viewer while she looks off to the side from the corners of her eyes. Her hair rises under a bank of pearl-white feathers and sparkling ornaments, and ringlets hang at the back of her neck. Her white dress scoops low across her chest, adorned with pearls and shimmering from silvery white to pale gold. The portrait is about 84 by 71 cm, dated about 1776 to 1777.","i":"The title names the sitter as the wife of Paul Cobb Methuen, and she is identified as Matilda Gooch. In the 1770s women's hair was dressed very high and powdered, with feathers and pearls as ornament, and such costume signaled fashion and wealth. A strong light against a dark ground gave the face a luminous presence, and the portrait is a fine example of how Gainsborough presented society women as glamorous, poised and slightly aloof.","c":"The dazzle of surface is the point: feathers and ornaments are painted in flickering strokes against the dark, and the sideways glance gives the sitter a flirtatious poise. The dress is more shimmer than substance, and the whole is mannered, but the brilliance is genuine. It shows Gainsborough's ability to make light itself the subject, and few portraitists of his generation matched the lightness with which he could suggest a rustle of silk and a spark of pearls."},"nga:1160":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, an elegantly dressed woman with smooth, pale skin and ash-brown hair piled high looks off into the distance, in front of a loosely painted landscape. Her hair is wound with a string of silver pearls, a veil is suggested by faint strokes, and her dress, painted in dashes of pewter gray and honey gold, has a low neckline and cuffs edged with gold ribbon and ruffles, with a jewel at the neckline. Her wrists cross at her waist, and pines frame a topaz-blue sky.","i":"Mary Cathcart, a celebrated beauty, married Thomas Graham in 1774, and her full-length portrait by Gainsborough in Edinburgh is one of his most famous. She died in 1792 at thirty-five, and her husband's grief is said to have led him to a military career in which he became Lord Lynedoch and the victor of Barrosa. The veil, ribbon and ruffles recall Van Dyck's costumes, which Gainsborough used to give his sitters a timeless dignity.","c":"The paint dissolves into dress and landscape in feathery strokes, while the face remains the point of focus, and the silvery tonality is Gainsborough at his most characteristic. The pose is graceful rather than grand, and the artifice of high hair and long neck is part of the elegance. The unfinished look is the picture's appeal: it gives the sitter the nonchalance of a society beauty, a delicacy that few contemporaries could match."},"met:436435":{"d":"A full-length portrait, 234.3 by 153.7 centimeters, of Grace Dalrymple Elliott, the celebrated beauty, painted in 1778 in oil on canvas. The Met's keywords record only the sitter's name. Gainsborough's full-lengths of the 1770s typically set a long, slender figure in shimmering silks against a soft, feathery landscape, so that the sitter's elegance is matched by the airy handling of the surroundings. The scale is the grand one of aristocratic portraiture.","i":"Grace Dalrymple Elliott (about 1754-1823), born in Scotland, was a celebrated beauty whose marriage to a physician ended in scandal and divorce, and who became a courtesan whose lovers reportedly included the Prince of Wales. She later lived in France, where she was imprisoned during the Revolution and wrote a memoir of it. A full-length of this scale was normally the privilege of rank, so the portrait of a notorious woman on that scale is a statement of her fame.","c":"Gainsborough's full-lengths of the late 1770s are among the most elegant things in English painting: long, swanlike figures, silvery draperies in feathery strokes, a woodland that seems to breathe. The portrait trades on that elegance, and the poise and shimmering paint are its attractions. The risk is a stylization that makes the sitter a type of fashion rather than an individual, but as an image of eighteenth-century glamour it is hard to beat."},"nga:107":{"d":"A pale woman is shown from the waist up within an oval opening, painted to look like a wooden frame, her body turned to the right while she looks off to the left with gray eyes. Her ash-brown hair is swept up and back, and a sheer veil drapes over her hair and shoulders. Her ivory-yellow gown is cut low and lined with lace, with pearls at the front and sleeve and an aquamarine cloth with gold touches at the waist. The painting is loosely handled, especially in hair, clothing and background.","i":"The sitter is named only as the wife of John Taylor. The feigned oval, a painted frame within the picture, recalls portrait medallions and cameos and gives the likeness the character of a cherished keepsake. The veil, lace and pearls echo the costume of Van Dyck's sitters, a favorite device in Gainsborough's portraits of the later 1770s for lending dignity and a hint of romance to a modern woman.","c":"An intimate portrait in which the oval acts like a window, with the figure lit softly against a dark ground and the loose handling of hair and drapery giving a breath of life. The sideways glance and the thin oval face have a reserved delicacy. The strength is atmosphere more than likeness, a quality that has led admirers to prize Gainsborough's late 1770s portraits; the weakness, if any, is a vagueness that leaves the sitter slightly out of reach."},"met:436439":{"d":"A young woman, bust-length, painted in oil on a canvas of 76.5 by 63.2 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, a standard English portrait size. The title says she is 'called' Miss Sparrow, meaning that the identification rests on tradition rather than on documentary proof. The date given is the 1770s, the decade of Gainsborough's last Bath years and the start of his London career.","i":"A portrait 'called' by a name is a common situation: the identity has come down by tradition, not by a contract or inscription, so the sitter's story remains uncertain. In the 1770s Gainsborough painted fashionable women in a soft, flickering manner, with silk, hair and ribbon touched in with quick strokes, and the portrait should be read as a type of the period's idea of young feminine grace as much as a record of one person.","c":"In the 1770s Gainsborough reached the peak of his portrait style: swift, feathery strokes, a cool, silvery key and a sensitive, slightly dreamy characterization. Beside Reynolds's more monumental approach, his sitters seem to breathe, and an unconfirmed name hardly matters when the face does its work. The thing to judge is whether the glance and the lightness of touch persuade; they usually do in Gainsborough's bust portraits of this size."},"nga:52265":{"d":"From a rocky shoreline, several people work by the sea with a boat and a net in this freely painted, almost square seascape of about 102 by 128 cm, dated about 1781 to 1782. A towering cream-white rock rises to the left, scrubby vegetation clings to the outcrop, and three people reach toward a boat while a fourth braces against the rock. Closer, three others work with a net, and low waves break in frothy foam beneath a hazy sky with two sailboats in the distance.","i":"Fishermen at work were a popular picturesque subject in late eighteenth-century Britain, with labor idealized into harmony with nature. Gainsborough painted such landscapes largely for his own pleasure and for the market in the 1780s, composing them from memory and imagination, and he is reported to have arranged small models on a table, with stones, moss and even broccoli, to study effects. The scene is an invented ideal, not a particular place.","c":"The freedom of the brushwork, in the foam, the cloud and the loosely sketched figures, is the strength here: the picture breathes atmosphere, and the towering rock and the low horizon give the shore a monumental calm. The risk is formula, a picturesque gloss on honest labor, and the figures are generic. As a late, free marine it is among his most appealing landscapes, and it anticipates the looser, more atmospheric landscape of the next generation."},"met:436441":{"d":"A large horizontal landscape, 120.3 by 147.6 centimeters, of wooded high ground, with trees and, as the Met's keywords record, a wagon. It is probably dated 1783. The picture is an invented, rustic scene rather than a view of a particular place: the kind of country subject Gainsborough painted for his own pleasure and for the few buyers who wanted landscapes, with a wagon among trees on rising ground carrying the eye back.","i":"Gainsborough said he painted portraits for money and landscapes for pleasure, and he once wrote that he longed to take his viola da gamba and walk off to some sweet village to paint landscapes. He admired Dutch landscape painters such as Ruisdael and Wijnants and the wooded landscapes of Rubens, and in the 1780s he produced large, loosely painted rural scenes that few collectors wanted, so that many remained in his studio.","c":"Gainsborough's late landscapes are free, feathery and invented, with trees as the actors and wagons and figures giving scale and a human pulse. Beside the observed English countryside that Constable would paint a generation later, they are poetic constructions, more indebted to Rubens and the Dutch than to actual fields. That makes them airy but a little generic. Their beauty is in the touch: the sweeping foliage and the glimmering light."},"nga:100":{"d":"Georgiana, Duchess of Devonshire (1757–1806), is portrayed in a full-length canvas about 236 by 147 cm, dated 1783, a scale that allows the figure to be shown near life-size. At twenty-six she was one of the most famous women in Britain. A full-length portrait was the formal format for a duchess, meant to hang in a great house as a statement of rank, beauty and family prestige, and Gainsborough, one of the two leading portraitists of Britain, paints her in his characteristic airy manner.","i":"Georgiana was a leading political hostess, a leader of fashion and a gambler, and in 1784 she famously canvassed in the streets for Charles James Fox in the Westminster election. She married the Duke of Devonshire in 1774 and was admired for both her beauty and her intelligence. A portrait of such a celebrity was an event, and this one set her image before the public as both an aristocrat and a woman of taste and charm.","c":"Gainsborough's feathery touch and silvery tones suit a society beauty, and the full-length format requires a monumental presence that he supplies by elegance and ease rather than by Reynolds's learned grandeur. The risk of the large scale is a certain vagueness in the drawing of broad forms, which is part of his style. In portraying glamour, however, he is unmatched, and this is a persuasive image of the most talked-about woman of her day."},"nga:114":{"d":"A river winds through forest-green trees between rolling hills and mountains that recede into a pale, orchid-purple sky, in this nearly square canvas of about 113 by 133 cm. Small figures, one on horseback and two on foot with perhaps a dog, cross an arched bridge at left of center, a house stands under tall trees at the base of a cliff that rises along the right edge, and loose strokes give the whole a soft, hazy look.","i":"Gainsborough's mountain landscapes of the 1780s were invented, not copied from a particular place: he drew on Rubens and Ruisdael and on his own sketches, and the mountains may recall his journey to the Lake District in 1783. The small figures crossing a bridge are a picturesque device that gives the vast scene a human measure. Such landscapes were painted largely for his own pleasure and for a few collectors, and he preferred them to the portraits that earned his living.","c":"The breathing atmosphere is the achievement: an orchid sky, a river that widens into mist, and a touch so loose that trees and hills dissolve into color. The scale of the figures gives the grand view its measure. The structure is conventional, with a cliff at the right as a framing device and receding layers of distance, but the handling is virtuoso. It is a poetic invention that points toward the free landscape painting of the next generation."},"met:436431":{"d":"A portrait of the musician Charles Rousseau Burney (1747-1819), painted about 1780 on a canvas of 76.8 by 63.8 centimeters, roughly thirty by twenty-five inches, a standard English portrait size. Gainsborough's male portraits of these years typically show the sitter in an easy, slightly turned attitude against a soft, feathery ground, with the head and shoulders handled in quick, fluent strokes.","i":"Burney came from the large, gifted Burney family, whose members included the novelist Fanny Burney and the music historian Charles Burney, and he made his own career as a performer and teacher. Gainsborough, himself a keen amateur musician who painted a number of London's musicians, moved easily in that world, and the portrait belongs to his circle of musical friends.","c":"Gainsborough's portraits of friends are looser and more human than his society commissions: the paint moves quickly and the sitter's glance is warm. The thing to judge here is the freedom of the brushwork around the face and the way the dark costume merges with its ground. The limit is that a quick sketch of a personality is not a deep study, though in Gainsborough that is usually enough."},"nga:1161":{"d":"John Bligh, 4th Earl of Darnley, is the subject of this portrait, an oil on canvas of about 76 by 64 cm, dated 1785. The sitter was a young Irish peer, about eighteen, who had inherited the earldom as a boy and held English estates in Kent, and the picture shows him at the start of his adult career. The size is that of a half-length portrait, made for a family seat rather than for public display.","i":"A portrait of a young man of rank around the age of majority marked his arrival in society, and for an aristocratic family it was a statement of continuity, the heir being shown as ready to take his place. The Irish peerage often came with English estates, and the Bligh family's seat at Cobham Hall in Kent was a center of country-house culture. Gainsborough, in 1785, was at the height of his fame, painting the great houses of the day.","c":"Late Gainsborough's flickering touch lends youth a natural freshness, and a portrait of a young man gives him a chance to soften a formal type with ease. Without the grandeur of his full-lengths, the half-length depends on the face and the quick play of paint over the costume. The danger of a young sitter is that character is still forming, so the picture offers presence more than depth. Its best quality is its unforced, lively air."},"nga:106":{"d":"A woman, her torso turned slightly left and her face looking directly at the viewer, smiles faintly in this portrait of about 76 by 64 cm, dated 1786. Her dark brown curled hair falls past her shoulders, and she wears a white gown with ruffled lace and white buttons and a wide-brimmed hat with a blue-gray underside matching the blue sash at her waist, one hand resting on the sash. The background is a loose pattern of muted green and yellow foliage under a gray sky.","i":"The wide-brimmed hat, the sash and the plain white gown belong to the relaxed, informal fashion of the 1780s, influenced by the French court's taste for simple muslin dress. Gainsborough liked the picture hat, which shades the face and frames it, and which he used in several portraits of women. Such informality allowed a woman of the gentry to be shown as natural and graceful rather than formal, a taste that suited the age of sensibility.","c":"A fresh, unforced portrait in which the smile, the hat and the white gown build an airy composition, and the flickering brushwork gives the surface a sparkle of movement. The background is abstract and the sitter's personality is generalized, as often in his women, but the lightness of touch is the highlight. Gainsborough in 1786 is at his most fluent, and this is an attractive example of the informal, outdoor manner that distinguishes his late portraits."},"met:436433":{"d":"A boy with a cat, in a large vertical canvas of 150.5 by 120.7 centimeters; the Met's keywords record only a boy and a cat, and the title adds a time of day, Morning, so light and atmosphere are part of the subject. The scale is that of a full portrait, but the subject is a country child rather than a named sitter, which marks it as one of Gainsborough's fancy pictures, imaginary scenes of rural life painted in the 1780s.","i":"Gainsborough's fancy pictures of cottage children, begun in the early 1780s, were a new kind of picture: painterly, sentimental and addressed to a public that liked the picturesque poor. Reynolds, who admired the genre, bought one of them, 'Girl with Pigs', and praised it. A boy with a cat belongs to the same world of rustic charm and unforced pathos, and its morning light gives it the air of a day just beginning.","c":"Fancy pictures can be sugary, and Gainsborough's sometimes are, with picturesque poverty made too clean and pretty. But the best have a freshness of touch and a tenderness that avoid the worst sentimentality, and the cat and boy suggest a story without telling it. The large scale gives the child dignity, and the soft morning light lifts the subject above anecdote. Painted a year before his death, it measures what he could do when released from sitters."},"met:436440":{"d":"Three ragged children stand and sit on a wooded bank under a stormy sky. A girl with dark hair, in a torn tan dress that leaves one shoulder bare, holds a fair-haired toddler in a white shift and red bodice on her arm; the little one points out of the picture with one hand. At the lower left a red-haired boy in rough clothes sits barefoot against the bank with a bundle of sticks. At the left a lit tree with yellowing leaves bends in the wind. Dark clouds edged with gold loom overhead, and a cottage and a strip of water lie in the distance at the right. The painting is thin and sketchy in places, with feathery strokes. Oil on canvas, about 148 by 120 centimetres.","i":"Painted in 1787, the year before Gainsborough's death, this belongs to his late fancy pictures: imagined scenes of rustic children, standing between portrait and genre. Such pictures presented the rural poor as picturesque, with handsome faces beneath torn clothes, an image that appealed to educated buyers of the time. The alternative title, The Wood Gatherers, refers to the boy collecting sticks, the labour that gives the children their place in the landscape. The children are idealised types rather than documentary portraits, and the stormy light gives the scene a mood of foreboding as well as charm.","c":"The sketchy, feathery handling is Gainsborough at his freest, and the stormy sky and glowing tree give the children an air of fragile warmth. The girl's glance has a sweet, wary dignity. But there is something falsely poetic about the poverty: the tatters are picturesque, the faces rosy and graceful, and nothing suggests real hardship. Painted for buyers who liked pathos at a safe distance, the picture is beautiful and sentimental in equal measure. Its looseness is more modern than the polished manner of his rival Reynolds."},"nga:99":{"d":"Elizabeth Linley, wife of the playwright Richard Brinsley Sheridan, is portrayed in a full-length canvas of about 220 by 154 cm, dated 1785 to 1787. The scale is that of a grand portrait, and the figure appears near life-size. She was a celebrated beauty and one of the most admired singers of her day, and Gainsborough, who knew her from his years in Bath, painted her at about thirty-two, near the end of his life and not long before hers.","i":"Elizabeth Linley (1754–1792) was a soprano from Bath who sang in oratorios until she married Sheridan in 1773, after an elopement and two duels fought over her; she then gave up public singing. Her husband wrote The Rivals and The School for Scandal and managed Drury Lane. She died of tuberculosis in 1792 at thirty-seven. Gainsborough had painted her as a girl in the Linley Sisters of 1772, so this late portrait is a second meeting of painter and sitter.","c":"A late Gainsborough with a poised, haunting grace: the full-length format is used not for state display but for a lyrical image, with a sweeping figure and the free brush of his last years. It ranks among his most poetic portraits and distills the sensibility of the 1780s, in which feeling counted for more than ceremony. The large forms can seem vague, but the effect of rarefied beauty is persuasive, and the sitter's story adds poignancy."},"cma:152006":{"d":"A sweeping landscape of meadow, rocks and trees, with a dell and a weir named in the title, painted in oil on a canvas of 70.5 by 91.0 centimeters about 1782 to 1783. The Cleveland Museum notes that in a sketchy manner Gainsborough picks out the elements of the scene, meadow, rocks and trees, by only slightly varying colors and brushstrokes, yet a sense of unity prevails. A weir adds the glint of falling water to a woodland scene.","i":"Gainsborough insisted that landscape was his first love, although most of his output was portraits, and he collected and was inspired by Dutch landscape painting. The weir and the dell are picturesque features of the English countryside rendered with a Dutch feeling for water and trees, and the sketchy handling reflects his habit of painting landscapes for himself rather than for patrons.","c":"The unity the museum notes is the picture's achievement: meadow, rocks and trees are distinguished by the slightest changes of tone and touch yet hold together as one atmosphere. That economy is the mark of a painter who knows his subject by heart. The price is a certain vagueness; there is no focal drama, and the viewer must be content with mood. It is a typically free, personal landscape from his later years."},"met:436432":{"d":"A head-and-shoulders portrait of Queen Charlotte (1744-1818), oil on canvas, 60.3 by 44.5 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a cap, lace, a shawl and a necklace, and the catalog describes it as a replica of a Gainsborough portrait, so it belongs to the old practice of repeating an approved royal likeness. At this scale it is a small, intimate version of the queen's official image.","i":"Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz, a German princess, married George III in 1761 on the day she first reached London and bore him fifteen children. Royal portraits circulated widely as replicas and prints, spreading the image of the monarchy, and Gainsborough, whom the king and queen favored in the 1780s, painted the royal family several times although he never held an official court post.","c":"Replicas are judged against their originals, and this one is worth studying for how Gainsborough's late style reduces cap, lace and shawl to soft, silvery touches while the face receives the careful attention. As the royal likeness required, it gives the queen a dignified presence. As a small royal image it is attractive and efficient, though less inventive than his work for private sitters, where he could follow his own fancy."},"met:436434":{"d":"A portrait of Lieutenant Colonel Paul Pechell (1724-1800), painted in oil on a canvas of 76.5 by 63.8 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, a standard English portrait size. The rank in the title marks him as a soldier, so the portrait presumably presents a military man in the head-and-shoulders format that Gainsborough used for the bulk of his male sitters.","i":"A lieutenant colonel of the British army was usually a gentleman of independent means as well as a professional soldier, and a head-and-shoulders portrait was a modest commission for a family. Gainsborough's male portraits are less ostentatious than his female ones: they rely on a plain, direct look and a few quick strokes for the uniform or coat, and they were painted with the same speed as his lighter work.","c":"Gainsborough's male sitters can seem plainer than his women, since a uniform or dark coat gives him less to play with, and the success of such a portrait depends on the glance and the economy of the brushwork. The best have a quick, unforced vitality; the weaker ones a politeness that stays on the surface. A viewer should judge whether the officer seems present as a person, which is the one job a portrait must do."},"met:436436":{"d":"A portrait of Alice De Lancey Izard (1746/47-1832), Mrs. Ralph Izard, painted in oil on a canvas of 76.8 by 63.8 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, a standard English portrait size. The title gives her married name with her maiden name and her dates, and she was an American-born sitter painted by an English artist, probably during the years she spent in England in the 1770s.","i":"Alice De Lancey was born into a prominent New York family and married Ralph Izard, a wealthy South Carolina planter who later served as a United States senator. The Izards spent years in Europe in the 1770s, and John Singleton Copley painted her with her husband in Rome in 1775. A portrait by Gainsborough was a mark of cosmopolitan taste, and this picture links colonial American society to the London art world.","c":"Gainsborough's portraits of women live by the glance and the airy treatment of costume, and a sitter from the colonial world gives the English painter a different, slightly more reserved subject. Look for the lightness of touch in face and dress and the cool, silvery key. The result is a sound, graceful example rather than a startling one, and it benefits from the comparison with Copley's more literal manner."},"nga:46094":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up in a dark navy coat with gold buttons and a vibrant red collar, a pale yellow waistcoat and a high ruffled white collar. His curly light hair frames a fair face, one hand rests on a long gun held upright, and the other on the brim of a black hat at his side. Behind him are green foliage, distant mountains and a serene sky. The canvas is about 127 by 102 cm, painted about 1785 to 1788.","i":"William Yelverton Davenport appears as a country gentleman with a gun. In eighteenth-century Britain the right to shoot game was restricted by law to landowners above a property qualification, so a gun in a portrait declared status and leisure as much as sport. The relaxed pose in a landscape, with the hat in one hand, was the informal manner Gainsborough made fashionable for young squires, who wished to be seen as men of the country and of the world.","c":"Gainsborough is often more reticent with men than with women, and here the relaxed pose, one hand on the gun and the other on the hat, conveys the ease of a young squire. The navy, red and yellow give bright accents against a cool landscape, and the touch is quick and light. The face is less characterful than the costume, and the portrait is more elegant than searching, but its fresh, outdoor manner is typical of his late work."},"nga:166447":{"d":"A man stands, seen from the knees up, his body and head turned slightly left. His gray hair is styled back with curls around the ears, and he has a sharp nose, thick gray eyebrows and a neutral expression. He wears a dark blue coat with gold buttons, a pale-yellow waistcoat and a frilly white cravat, with tan trousers. His bent arm rests on a tall rock, his gloved hand holds the other glove, and the bare hand holds a thin brown cane, against loose autumnal foliage.","i":"Francis Basset (1757–1835) was one of Cornwall's leading landowners and mine-owners, a member of Parliament who was created Baron de Dunstanville later, so the title in the picture's name is a later honor. The companion portrait of his wife, of the same size, suggests that they were painted as a pair. His gloves and cane mark a gentleman of leisure, and the rock beside him, a Gainsborough device, links him to the landscape of his estates.","c":"A man at ease: the arm resting on a rock gives the pose a casual authority, and Gainsborough relieves any stiffness with the quick, autumnal background and the cool, elegant scheme of navy, yellow and white. Beside Reynolds's heroic poses, it is informal and modern. The portrait lacks psychological intensity, and the sitter is a type of the poised gentleman, but the style is accomplished, and its pairing with his wife's portrait gives it additional interest."},"nga:166448":{"d":"A woman sits, seen from the knees up, looking off into the distance in front of a deep, shadowy landscape. Ash-brown curls frame her oval face, and her wide-brimmed black hat is set at a steep angle, with a white feather along the front edge and a pearl at the top of the brim. Her butter-yellow gown has translucent white ruffles and a strand of pearls tied in a bow at the chest, with the overskirt draped over a topaz-blue underskirt. She holds a feather in her lap and a gold scarf by her face.","i":"Frances Susanna Hippisley Coxe married Francis Basset in 1780, and the portrait forms a pair with his, of the same size. The black hat with its feather and pearl recalls the picture hats of the 1780s and the older costume of Van Dyck and Rubens, which Gainsborough used to give modern women a romantic air. The pose, with an elbow on a stone and a gaze into the distance, is a conventional posture of reflection, in keeping with the age of sensibility.","c":"Lady de Dunstanville is more dashing than her husband: the hat, feathers, pearls and blue underskirt give a sense of movement, and the loose paint of dress and background heightens it. The pair make a good contrast, his gravity beside her glamour. The costume threatens to overpower the face, and the pose is conventional, but as a showpiece of Gainsborough's feathery late style, it is lively and assured."},"met:436694":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of Endymion Porter (1587-1649), painted about 1630 on vellum by John Hoskins. No dimensions are recorded; limnings of this kind are normally only a few centimeters high. Vellum, a fine animal skin, was the traditional ground for English miniatures, and the paint is watercolor with body-color applied in tiny, precise strokes, so that the face is built up with great delicacy.","i":"Porter was a courtier of Charles I, a Groom of the Bedchamber who helped the king acquire pictures and who befriended painters; he had spent his youth in Spain and accompanied Prince Charles to Madrid in 1623. A miniature of such a man, made for himself or his family, belongs to the intimate culture of the Caroline court, where portrait miniatures were exchanged as tokens of favor and affection.","c":"Hoskins was the foremost English limner of the 1630s, and his style is sober, precise and a little hard: firm contours and clear flesh tones that value likeness and rank over softness. Compared with Samuel Cooper, his nephew and pupil, whose broader, more atmospheric manner would eclipse him, Hoskins is the more linear and decorous. Beside Dobson's richer painting of Porter, the miniature is the more private record."},"met:436695":{"d":"A man said to be Philip Wharton, fourth Baron Wharton, painted in 1648 by John Hoskins on vellum laid on prepared card. The title's 'said to be' flags that the sitter's identity rests on tradition rather than proof. No size is recorded; limnings of this kind are small, only a few centimeters high. The face would be the most carefully finished part, in tiny strokes of watercolor and body-color.","i":"Philip Wharton (1613-1696) was a Puritan peer who fought for Parliament in the Civil War and was among its leading nobles; 1648 was the year of the Second Civil War, on the eve of the king's trial. A miniature of a Parliamentary lord by Hoskins, who had been limner to Charles I, shows that the court painter's clientele crossed political lines, and that an artist's skills outlasted the regime that trained him.","c":"With the sitter's identity uncertain, the picture must stand on handling: Hoskins's firm, linear manner at a late stage, with a clear, even light on the face, sober coloring and no flourish. By 1648 Cooper's softer approach was overtaking it, and a Hoskins of this date has the interest of the older style holding its own. A viewer might judge how well the eyes and flesh tones carry a likeness of what may be a Puritan lord."},"met:436696":{"d":"A small portrait miniature of Dr. Brian Walton (about 1600-1661), painted in 1657 by John Hoskins on vellum laid on card. No dimensions are recorded; limnings of this kind are usually only a few centimeters high. The date places the likeness in the year Walton completed his great work, the multilingual Polyglot Bible, so it is an editor's portrait at the moment of his triumph.","i":"Walton, an Anglican clergyman ejected from his post during the Civil War, led the production of the London Polyglot Bible (1653-57), a monumental edition in several ancient languages, printed by subscription. At the Restoration he became Bishop of Chester but died in 1661. A miniature made in the year the Polyglot was finished would have been a personal keepsake or a gift to a friend or patron.","c":"By 1657 Hoskins was a veteran, and the miniature shows an established formula at the end of a long career: a quiet, sober likeness with firm drawing and little decoration. For a scholar that plainness suits. The viewer should look for intelligence in the eyes and for the simple handling of the costume. It is not a showy work, but a sensible, truthful record of a man whose own monument was a book."},"nga:154215":{"d":"A sage-green harbor teems with more than a dozen sailing ships and rowboats under a pale gray sky, with towering dark gray and white clouds and a low horizon. At the right a warship floats with its ornately carved and gilded stern facing the viewer, its flag bearing the painter's name, 'R. Zeeman'. At the left a rowboat of men has pulled up beside a partly sunken ship, one man stands on a board lifted halfway up its side, and another tends a smoking mixture on a black floating platform, while two women in a boat wash clothes.","i":"Reinier Nooms, called Zeeman, meaning seaman, is said to have been a sailor before becoming a painter and printmaker of ships, and his harbor views are precise records of Dutch seapower at its peak. Amsterdam was then one of Europe's busiest ports, and the picture records not only warships but the working life of a harbor, with repairs, laundry and passengers. The signature on a flag is a playful touch.","c":"Zeeman is above all a draftsman of ships, and the picture is a showcase of nautical detail: rigging, carved sterns, gun ports, repairs and washing. The towering clouds and the light falling across the water give convincing weather, and the slate and brown palette is typical of mid-century Dutch tonal painting. The detail can overwhelm the composition, a dense catalog more than a unified view, and he is better known for his etchings than his paintings."},"met:435634":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of a woman named in the title as Miss Chambers. The Met's keywords record a woman, a ruffle and a portrait, so the sitter is shown with a ruffled neckline or frill, a fashionable detail of the late eighteenth century. The Met dates it only as after 1782 and gives no dimensions, but ivory miniatures of this period are normally palm-sized, painted in watercolor with delicate, stippled strokes.","i":"Isabella Beetham was one of a small group of women who worked as professional miniaturists in late Georgian England. Miniatures of the 1780s and 1790s were affordable luxuries for the gentry and the professional classes, exchanged between family and friends, and a young woman styled Miss Chambers, the form used for an unmarried daughter, might have sat for hers as a gift for her family or a suitor.","c":"The late eighteenth-century English miniature prized delicacy: pale, cool flesh tones, soft powdered hair and a gentle expression, and Beetham works within that elegance. A ruffle gives a painter a chance to show finesse in whites and fine lines, and that is where a viewer should look. The danger of the type is prettiness; the best examples keep something of a living face, and the test is the eyes."},"met:15070":{"d":"A man named Edward Ranstead Jones, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1847 and measuring 7.0 by 5.8 centimeters. The painter, Walter Gould (1829-1893), was then only seventeen or eighteen. The ivory shows the head and shoulders of the sitter, the face stippled in fine strokes through which the white of the ivory glows, and the clothing and ground treated more broadly.","i":"By 1847 the daguerreotype was already spreading, and a teenager making miniatures was entering a trade under pressure. Young painters often began in miniature because it needed little equipment and could be learned quickly, and used it as a stepping stone. A portrait by an artist still in his teens is an early, even apprentice, work, though made for a paying or friendly sitter.","c":"A teenager's miniature should be judged generously and then strictly: generously because control of stippling and flesh tones is notable at any age, strictly because the real test is whether the sitter seems alive. This ivory has the interest of an early step in a career. Its competence is its virtue, and its limitation is the familiar one of a young hand following well-worn formulas."},"met:437443":{"d":"A large group portrait, 168.0 by 180.3 centimeters, of two children and their tutor, painted in 1748: Thomas Neate, aged about eight, his sister Martha, and the tutor Thomas Needham. The Met's subject tags list a man, a boy, a girl and sheep, so the group is set out of doors, with livestock suggesting a country setting. The nearly square canvas holds three figures in a conversation-piece arrangement, a portrait of relations among people and not only of faces.","i":"The conversation piece, a group portrait of friends or family engaged in an easy activity, was Hogarth's English specialty, and the young Reynolds borrowed it to show an educated household. The presence of a tutor records the care taken over a boy's education, one of a gentry family's chief investments. The picture was painted about a year before Reynolds set off for Italy, where he would learn the grand manner.","c":"This is Reynolds before Italy, still tied to the conversation piece, with its stiff poses and polite staging. The ambition is notable in a painter of about twenty-five: three figures in a landscape, with a careful arrangement of heads and glances. It lacks the sweeping handling and psychological ease of his later portraits, but the fresh tenderness toward the children and the ambitious composition are promising signs of what he would become."},"nga:1214":{"d":"A young girl in a rust-red dress with a tight bodice and full skirt sits in a hazy, garden-like setting, her head turned to look off to the left. Her pale face is slightly flushed, her blond hair falls beside it, and a brick-red bow sits on her head with another tied under her chin. The off-the-shoulder bodice is lined with lace; in her lap she holds a bouquet of red, pink, blue and white flowers, and a gold shoe tip shows below the hem. A tree rises at the right against blended teals and steel blues.","i":"The sitter is Lady Elizabeth Hamilton (1753-1797), daughter of the Duke of Hamilton and the famous beauty Elizabeth Gunning; she later married the Earl of Derby and became a countess. The flowers, a rose lying in shadow and the garden setting can be read as traditional signs of youth, and in this picture of a five-year-old they gently allude to the brevity of beauty. Reynolds's child portraits of the 1750s helped establish the fashion for informal, affectionate images of aristocratic children.","c":"Reynolds paints childhood with sympathy and a sense of theater: the girl's turned head, the hazy garden and the soft, blended background make a dreamlike setting, and the red bows, lace and gold shoe are handled with a refined delicacy. The landscape echoes Van Dyck and Rubens in its sweeping, nonspecific haze. If it lacks the bravura of the later Reynolds, it has a gentleness that makes it one of the most engaging of his early child portraits."},"nga:195591":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Annetta Coke, painted in 1758 in oil on a canvas of 75.6 by 62.9 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, the standard size for a head-and-shoulders or bust-length portrait. At that scale the picture is a likeness to be kept in a drawing room or a family house, with attention concentrated on the face, the hair and the neckline of the dress.","i":"In 1758 Reynolds, six years back from Italy, was the leading portraitist in London, and his appointment books record a very busy practice, with many sitters passing through each week. A portrait of this size was the standard commission for the gentry and professional classes, and a woman sitter could expect to be shown graceful, natural and well dressed, in a soft, flattering light.","c":"In the late 1750s Reynolds was learning to marry Van Dyck's elegance and the Italian masters' gravity to an English face, and a bust-length portrait gives him little room for grand rhetoric. The interest lies in the lighting and in the sympathetic characterization, and the picture should be judged by the eyes, the skin and the treatment of lace or fabric. It is a good representative of his stock-in-trade rather than a showpiece."},"met:437444":{"d":"A portrait of Anne Dashwood (1743-1830), later Countess of Galloway, painted in 1764 in oil on a canvas of 133.4 by 118.7 centimeters, a large format that gives the figure room for a fashionable gown and a pose. In these years Reynolds typically gave his women sitters a soft, slightly warm tonality and a pose drawn from classical sculpture or Old Master painting.","i":"Anne Dashwood, from a landed English family, married Alexander Stewart, later Earl of Galloway, in 1762, so the portrait of 1764 belongs to the early years of her marriage, and her title is the one she would later hold. Reynolds's female portraits of the 1760s aimed to flatter by suggesting classical grace, giving young wives the air of figures from antiquity: dignity without stiffness.","c":"Reynolds's portraits of young aristocratic women in the 1760s mix real observation with borrowed grace, and the question is how much of Anne remains under the formula. The large format gives him space for fabric and a pose, and the glow of his color is the main attraction. The weakness, if any, is a generic sweetness; the strength is the gentle authority with which he dignifies a young bride."},"nga:83718":{"d":"A seated young woman, shown to the waist, faces forward, her right cheek partly in shadow. She has fair skin, pale eyebrows, gray eyes and a neutral expression, with light brown hair dressed up with a blue ribbon and pearl ornaments. Her black dress has lace trim, a red gem and pearl strands at the neckline, blue slits in the sleeves, blue bows at the elbows and lace cuffs; a pearl necklace with a blue bow hangs at the back. On her lap she holds a small white dog. The background is dark brown with a lighter vertical strip at the right.","i":"The slashed black sleeves, pearls and blue bows echo the costume of Van Dyck's portraits of a century earlier, a fashion for 'Vandyke dress' that Reynolds and his contemporaries used to give sitters a timeless, romantic air. The small dog, a traditional sign of fidelity and affection, is a standard accessory in portraits of women. Miss Beatrix Lister is shown as a young woman of some means and refinement.","c":"The Van Dyck dress gives Reynolds a ready-made elegance: black silk, blue accents and pearls glow against the dark ground, and the face, half in shadow, becomes the focus. The expression is neutral, almost impassive, and that reserve gives the portrait an unusual seriousness for a young woman. The strongest passages are the costume details; the weakest is the flat, dark background, which leaves the figure a little isolated."},"met:437449":{"d":"A very large group portrait, 254.6 by 360.7 centimeters, of the Honorable Henry Fane, shown with Inigo Jones and Charles Blair, the companions named in the title; a dog appears among the figures. Begun in 1761 and finished in 1766, it is nearly three and a half meters across, large enough to command a wall in a country house, with three male figures at or near life size. The museum's keywords note Henry Fane, a dog and portraits.","i":"Group portraits of three men on this grand scale are rare in Reynolds's work, and this one, with its dog and balanced arrangement of figures, shows him applying lessons from Italian art to a very English subject: the bond between a young man of rank and the men who looked after his interests. The long gestation, 1761 to 1766, suggests a complex and ambitious commission.","c":"Reynolds's ambition here is extraordinary: three men, life-size, held together by gesture and composition, with the dog adding a note of naturalness. The danger of a group portrait this big is a stiff staging, and the picture's size and the time it took suggest he wrestled with the problem. Even if the result is more admirable than lovable, it shows him as a master of structure, attempting the grand manner in a portrait."},"met:437445":{"d":"A double portrait of two children, 181.6 by 145.4 centimeters, painted in 1768: George Capel, Viscount Malden, aged about eleven, later the fifth Earl of Essex, and his sister Lady Elizabeth Capel, about thirteen, later Lady Monson. The Met's catalog tags include garlands, a boy and women or girls, so the pair are shown with garlands, an old emblem of youth, festivity and the pastoral. The large canvas is the format reserved for important commissions.","i":"The Capels were an old aristocratic family; the children are the son and daughter of the Earl of Essex, and Reynolds sets them in a loosely classical, pastoral frame, with garlands that recall ancient festivals, to ennoble the portrait of childhood. In the 1760s he developed this style of the child as a figure of innocence and fresh beauty, and the titles Viscount Malden and Lady Elizabeth record the social world of the picture.","c":"Reynolds's double portraits of siblings can be charming and a little contrived, and the garlands here are the contrivance that turns children into figures of a pastoral ideal. The strength is the affection and ease of the pairing; the weakness is the slightly staged sweetness. Compare it with Gainsborough's more spontaneous children and Reynolds looks calculated; compare it with the Academic portraits of the day and he looks inventive."},"nga:113":{"d":"A girl of about seven sits low on the grass of a hilly park, her white silk skirt spread around her like a cloud, and reaches out with a yellow-gloved hand to touch a pink rose on a bush that grows from a large dark urn at the left. She wears a black cape trimmed with lace over the white dress, a blue sash and a frilled white bonnet over her brown fringe. Her face, in profile, with round pink cheeks, is serious as she looks at the flower; her other hand rests in a loose fist on her lap. Behind her, low hills and a few trees run beneath a wide sky of soft grey, cream and pink clouds. The paint is thick and fluid, with thin glazes in the sky.","i":"Lady Caroline Howard (1771–1848) was the daughter of Frederick Howard, 5th Earl of Carlisle, who commissioned the portrait from Reynolds. It was exhibited at the Royal Academy in 1779 and hung at Castle Howard before reaching the National Gallery of Art in 1937. She later married John Campbell, afterwards Lord Cawdor, and an inscription gives her later title. Reynolds, the first president of the Royal Academy, painted children as individuals absorbed in their own world rather than as small adults. The roses have been read as an allusion to virtues associated with Venus and the Three Graces, a reading that fits the period's taste for allegory but cannot be proved.","c":"This is among Reynolds's most inventive pictures of a child. The pale dress and bonnet dissolve into the sky, while the dark cape gives the figure a firm silhouette, and the girl seems absorbed rather than posed. The weaker passages are the stiff arm that reaches for the rose and the allegorical flowers, which are a conventional note. Reynolds's handling of the sky and grass is loose and luminous, and it suggests he cared more about mood than about a precise likeness. Beside the sentimental child portraits that followed, its restraint is welcome."},"nga:102":{"d":"A full-length group portrait on a tall canvas, 238.4 by 147.2 centimeters, painted between 1777 and 1779, showing Lady Elizabeth Delmé with her children. At this scale the figures are life-size, and the size and the full-length format were the grandest options a portraitist could offer, usually reserved for rank and for display in a country house or a London town house. The picture is a mother-and-children subject in the grand manner.","i":"Reynolds liked to dignify a mother and her children by echoing religious and classical images of maternity: a standing mother with children clinging to her, a staple of his grand-manner portraiture, recalls Madonna groups and Roman matrons. Her title, Lady, marks Elizabeth Delmé as a woman of rank, and the picture presents the family as a harmonious unit with the mother as its monumental center.","c":"This is among Reynolds's most elaborate mother-and-child portraits and shows why he was so admired: a monumental, harmonious composition, a rich coloring and a mother who seems both noble and tender. The risk is a certain unreality, since the children are posed as ornaments to the maternal figure. His gift for turning social status into dignified pictorial structure was rarely better displayed, though the humanity of the sitters is secondary to the design."},"nga:46088":{"d":"A clean-shaven man with gray powdered hair stands in the middle of a grassy landscape, his ankles crossed and his head turned to look off to the right. He wears a knee-length ginger-brown coat with large gold buttons and lace cuffs over a white cravat and a goldenrod waistcoat, with navy breeches and white stockings; in his left hand he holds a navy tricorn hat, and his right hand rests on the head of a wooden walking stick. White and dark pink flowers grow at his feet, trees climb a low hill, and swirling clouds and a distant blue mountain fill the background.","i":"John Musters is shown in the manner Reynolds made standard: a full-length figure at ease in his landscape, with hat in hand and cane for support, the very picture of the English gentleman. The sheer scale, nearly two and a half meters tall, announces status; the dress is fashionable but unostentatious, the pose relaxed. Such portraits hung in country houses to declare lineage and standing, and this one dates from the later 1770s, when Reynolds was at the height of his fame.","c":"Reynolds's gift here is to make a conventional pose look unforced. The crossed ankles, the turned head and the stick give the standing figure an easy poise, and the swirling sky gives the picture drama without distracting from the man. Beneath the dress the sitter is not deeply characterized; he is presented as a type of confident gentleman, which is what the patron wanted. The buttons, lace and buckles show Reynolds's skill with surfaces."},"met:437447":{"d":"A young officer stands at full length in a landscape, leaning against a tree with his legs crossed. He wears a scarlet coat with dark blue collar and cuffs, a white cravat and breeches, and black boots with spurs; his powdered hair is curled at the sides. In one hand he holds his hat against his hip, and with the other he holds the reins of his bay horse, which lowers its head toward the ground at the left. The tree's yellow-green foliage spreads overhead, and behind him a stormy sky of grey cloud breaks into light at the right. The paint is applied loosely and vigorously, especially in the foliage and the horse's mane.","i":"George Keene Hayward Coussmaker (1759–1801) was an officer in the 1st Foot Guards who retired in 1795 without seeing active service. Reynolds painted him in 1782, and the sitter books show an exceptional 21 sittings for the man and eight for the horse, between February and April; the artist was paid £205 plus ten guineas for the frame. The portrait belongs to the tradition of aristocratic portraits in the manner of Van Dyck, in which the sitter is shown at ease in the landscape. It stayed in the sitter's family until 1884, when it was sold to William K. Vanderbilt, who bequeathed it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1920.","c":"A fine example of the type. The scarlet coat blazes against the brown tree and horse, and the horse's lowered head gives a lively counterweight to the sitter's cool, slightly vacant poise. Reynolds's handling is vigorous, almost rough, in the foliage and coat. The pose, with its crossed legs and lazy lean, is a little too studied, and the face has the generic polish of his society portraits, so there is more life in the horse than in the man. It is still a convincing demonstration of why Reynolds led English portraiture."},"nga:104":{"d":"A pale-skinned woman in a cream-white dress stands before a shadowy landscape, looking directly out with dark gray eyes. Her brown hair is pulled up in a loose, tall style, a palm-sized medallion hangs on a gold chain low on her chest, and the gold-lined neckline of the dress is pulled around her torso like a wrap. The train is pinned between her crossed ankles, and her white shoes are wrapped with blue lines like sandals. A white coat with voluminous sleeves falls from her shoulders. She leans one elbow on a squared stone ledge, her other hand on her hip.","i":"Lady Elizabeth Compton, the daughter of an earl as her title shows, is painted in the loose, classically inspired dress that Reynolds favored for aristocratic women in the 1780s, with sandal-like shoes and a medallion recalling antique jewelry. The pose, with an elbow on a ledge and a hand on the hip, borrows from classical statues and lends a lady the air of a goddess. The shadowy landscape, lit only at the horizon, adds drama.","c":"This is Reynolds at his most theatrical: the tall figure in white against a darkening landscape, with the light on the horizon behind her shoulders like a halo. The pose is borrowed and the dress an artist's fancy, so the sitter becomes a type, yet the direct gaze and slight smile keep a human presence. White drapery is a hard thing to paint, and the picture is built around that test. A grand but slightly cool performance."},"met:437448":{"d":"A portrait of a small girl, Georgiana Augusta Frederica Elliott (1782-1813), painted in 1784 in oil on a canvas of 88.9 by 76.2 centimeters, when she was about two years old. Reynolds was a master of child portraiture, and a work of this modest size shows the child close to the viewer, with attention given to the face and hair of a very young sitter.","i":"The little girl was the daughter of the courtesan Grace Dalrymple Elliott and was widely believed to be the child of the Prince of Wales, the future George IV; her names are the feminine form of his, and she later married Lord Charles Bentinck. A portrait by the leading painter of the day was a mark of care for a child whose parentage was a subject of gossip.","c":"Reynolds's portraits of very young children are among his most appealing work, with a tenderness and freshness that his formal portraits of adults seldom match. The painting should be judged by whether the child's gaze reads as curious and real rather than as a doll's, and by the softness of the paint. Its historical interest is stronger still, since the sitter's story is more dramatic than any pose."},"met:437451":{"d":"A portrait of John Barker (1707-1787), painted in 1786 in oil on a canvas of 173.4 by 120.7 centimeters, a large format that gives the figure considerable presence. He was about seventy-nine, so the picture records old age, which Reynolds, in the late years of his career, treated with considerable sympathy and without the idealizing that he applied to young sitters.","i":"Reynolds's portraits of elderly men, such as those of Samuel Johnson and Lord Heathfield, are among his most searching, because age gave him faces with character to paint. A portrait of a man in his late seventies, made a year before the sitter's death, is a memorial in advance: the likeness would remain with the family as a record of a long life.","c":"Old age is where Reynolds's art deepens: the face, with its lines and settled expression, lets him forget the formulas of grace and look. A portrait of a man of seventy-nine, made in the painter's own last productive years, should be judged on how directly the face is seen and how the dark clothes and background support it. It is probably more honest than the sweet portraits of his young sitters."},"nga:1213":{"d":"A seated woman faces left, her hands resting in her lap. She has a pale complexion, rosy cheeks, arched eyebrows and thin red lips in a neutral expression; her gray hair is arranged in stiff curls that stand out from her head, beneath a wide-brimmed black hat with a black flower and white feathers. She wears a white dress with a wide skirt under a black jacket or shawl, with a lacy white cravat at her neck. Behind her a red curtain drapes at the right, and at the left a view opens to a dark greenish-blue sky, tall trees and a brown statue or fountain.","i":"The curtain, the statue and the landscape are the standard stage props of the British grand-manner portrait, announcing that the sitter belongs to a house and a tradition. Lady Cornewall's wide feathered hat is pure 1780s fashion, while the scheme of black, white and red is a Reynolds specialty, recalling Van Dyck and Rubens. He painted it as president of the Royal Academy and Principal Painter to the King.","c":"A restrained but richly designed portrait: black, white and red against a dark, stormy sky make a strong pattern, and the large hat gives a bold silhouette. The face is rendered with a subdued, truthful clarity that avoids flattery, and the sitter's neutral expression suggests a woman of reserve. It lacks the effusive charm of his younger sitters but has the authority of a mature style, respecting a woman's dignity."},"met:437452":{"d":"A mother with three children, 140.7 by 112.1 centimeters: Charlotte Delaval, Lady Smith, with her son George Henry and daughters Louisa and Charlotte, as the title names them. The Met's keywords record a mother, a woman, a boy and a girl. The canvas, painted in 1787, is a large group portrait in which the figures are drawn close together into a single cluster, a family seen as a unit rather than as four separate sitters.","i":"Reynolds often composed mother-and-children portraits as a pyramid of figures, borrowing from Renaissance Madonnas with the Christ Child and the infant Saint John, so that a modern lady is invested with the dignity of sacred motherhood. The title names each child, which makes the picture a record of a family at a particular moment, painted in the artist's last years of full activity.","c":"Reynolds's late family groups are the high point of his invention in group composition: the figures knit together into a harmonious shape, and the children show a spontaneity that frees the formal pattern. The risk is the idealization of motherhood, a reflection of the period's sentimental cult of the family. Here the Madonna-like grouping lends dignity, and his warm, glowing manner supports the tenderness."},"met:437450":{"d":"A portrait of a young woman, Mary Elizabeth Milles, Mrs. Lewis Thomas Watson, on a canvas of 127.0 by 101.6 centimeters, about fifty by forty inches, painted in 1789. The Met's subject tags list a bonnet, a cap, a ribbon, a fichu (the light scarf at the neckline), a ruffle, lace and a cape, so the sitter is shown in the fashionable headwear and neckwear of the late 1780s, with the soft white of linen and lace around her face.","i":"Reynolds painted this in 1789, the year his sight began to fail, so it comes at the very end of his career. The sitter was about twenty-two and recently married, and her title, Mrs. Lewis Thomas Watson, records her new status. The bonnet, fichu and ruffle show the fashion for soft, simple white linens and light bonnets that followed a new taste for natural dress.","c":"Reynolds's late portraits of young women are more informal than the grand-manner ones of the 1770s: a bonnet, a fichu and a cape instead of classical drapery, and a gentler touch. The sitter is shown as a fashionable bride, and the many white details, cap, fichu, ruffle and lace, are the real test: handled well they animate the face, handled badly they smother it. It is a pleasant, quietly modern portrait from the end of a great career."},"met:437453":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed woman, painted in oil on a canvas of 75.2 by 62.2 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, the standard bust-length portrait size. The museum's keywords note ermine, a bow and a ribbon, so the sitter wears or holds a trimming of ermine, the white fur marked with black tail tips, along with ribbon accents. No date is recorded, but the portrait is the kind of everyday bust-length commission Reynolds produced in great numbers.","i":"Ermine, the winter coat of the stoat, was associated with royalty and the peerage, and in portraits of the period an ermine wrap or trimming signals wealth and rank, though fashionable women also wore it as a luxury fur. Bows and ribbons add the delicate, ornamental notes of fashion. Without a name the sitter is a type: a prosperous woman of the later eighteenth century, composed for her family.","c":"Fur is a hard thing to paint, the soft white texture against skin and dark shadows, and ermine with its black flecks tests a painter's economy. Reynolds usually solves such problems with loose, confident touches, and the viewer should judge the picture by how the fur and ribbon enhance the face rather than compete with it. At bust length it is a good example of his working portraiture, less ambitious than his full-lengths but often fresher."},"met:436995":{"d":"A scene of begging at a doorway, in oil on a canvas of 51.4 by 59.4 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include begging, a woman, a man, a child and a rosary, so the figures are poor people, probably a family or a group of wanderers, asking for alms, with a rosary as a sign of religious piety. The size is that of a cabinet picture made for a private collector rather than a church or a public hall.","i":"Giving alms to the poor was one of the Christian works of mercy, and doorways of houses and convents were traditional places where beggars gathered. Pictures of the poor were popular in mid-seventeenth-century France and Flanders, where war and hardship had made beggars a familiar sight, and they could be read as charity, as moral lessons or simply as picturesque types. The painter is known by a notname, the Master of the Béguins.","c":"Low-life painting of this kind lives on observation: the rags, the gestures, the postures of asking and refusing. The brothers Le Nain set a standard for dignified, quiet pictures of the poor, and an anonymous master offers more sentiment and anecdote. The question is whether the painter treats his beggars as people or as types for the buyer's charity, and a viewer should look at the faces and at the rosary, which gives the scene its moral color."},"nga:41655":{"d":"Three men and two women sit or stand along the far side of a long table covered with a white cloth. At the left Mary, in teal blue with a white head scarf, prays, and beside her Christ, in an eggplant-purple robe, raises two fingers in blessing; a wrinkled man in an emerald-green robe offers him a bowl of red liquid. At the end of the table a young bridegroom in a crimson cap and fur-lined cloak sits beside his bride in a gold-patterned green dress. Two young servants attend six tall jugs, and rooms with a bed and a fireplace open behind.","i":"In the Gospel of John, Christ's first miracle took place at a wedding at Cana, where, when the wine ran out, he had servants fill six stone jars with water and it became wine. The six jugs on the floor are those jars, and the bowl of red liquid refers to the miracle. A Latin inscription on the tablecloth quotes the Hail Mary, honoring the Virgin who prompted it. The painter is named after a devotional picture that includes the Catholic Kings, Ferdinand and Isabella of Spain.","c":"The picture is crowded with delightful details, jeweled clasps, patterned fabrics, a dog, and views into rooms beyond, which give depth and anecdote and show the painter's Flemish training. The weaker part is expression: Christ and the guests look down, and the faces are a little stiff. The strength is the architecture and the domestic interiors, a late fifteenth-century Spanish court taste for Netherlandish detail applied to a Gospel feast."},"nga:41656":{"d":"Set in a church, nine men and one woman gather around a throne on which a young boy sits. The boy, Christ, has long, wavy brown hair and looks down with his fingertips touching, in an ocean-blue robe edged in gold; behind him a crimson cloth patterned with gold is held up in a four-point canopy. At the left five men in hats, turbans and hoods, two holding open books, look in different directions; at the right Mary, in a white head cloth and teal robe, prays among four men. The floor is inlaid in squares and X-patterns, and marbled columns support white statues.","i":"In Luke 2, when Jesus was twelve his parents lost him in Jerusalem and found him after three days in the Temple, sitting among the teachers, listening and asking questions, and astonishing them with his understanding. Mary prays at the right, the elders turn to their books in puzzlement, and the throne raises the boy above them as the true teacher. With The Marriage at Cana, the panel probably belongs to a cycle of scenes from Christ's life.","c":"The architecture does much of the work: marbled columns, statues, stained glass and a patterned floor create a rich, convincing space for a quiet scene. The figures hold their own in it, but the doctors' look-alike group is somewhat stiff, and the central child is more diagram than character. As Hispano-Flemish painting made for a Spanish patron, it shows how thoroughly Netherlandish methods had been absorbed in Castile."},"nga:234145":{"d":"A panoramic view in oil on a canvas of 81.9 by 121.9 centimeters, dated 1787, of Cap-Français, the main port and colonial capital of Saint-Domingue, the French Caribbean colony that became Haiti. It is a topographical view of a harbor town, the type of picture made to record a colonial city for French owners and officials, with the waterfront, the shipping and the surrounding hills set out across a wide horizontal format.","i":"Cap-Français, now Cap-Haïtien, was the richest port of the richest French colony in the 1780s, a center of the sugar and coffee trade whose wealth rested on the labor of enslaved Africans, who formed the great majority of the colony's population. A view painted in 1787 shows the city a few years before the revolt of 1791, which led to the Haitian Revolution, and before much of the town burned in 1793. Such pictures advertised the colony's prosperity.","c":"A topographical view's virtue is documentary: an accurate record of a city, here with an extra historical charge because the city and its society were about to be destroyed. Le May works as a competent recorder, not a stylist, and the picture's interest lies in what it shows more than in its brushwork. A viewer might ask what it leaves out: the colonial order it celebrates depended on slavery, which a view like this politely does not depict."},"met:438665":{"d":"An interior history painting of a papal visit, in oil on a canvas of 125.1 by 140.7 centimeters, nearly square. The Met's keywords record Pope Gregory XVI, a church interior, men and women, so the picture shows the pope in the Benedictine church of San Benedetto at Subiaco among clergy and onlookers. It is a large formal canvas that records a ceremony, composed so that the pope is the center and the church is the setting.","i":"Subiaco, east of Rome, is the cradle of Benedictine monasticism: Saint Benedict lived there as a hermit in a cave about the year 500 before founding monasteries. Gregory XVI, pope from 1831 to 1846, had himself been a monk and was a conservative who resisted liberal and national movements in the Papal States. A papal visit to a historic shrine was an occasion for devotion and for pro-papal imagery, which a French painter recorded.","c":"History painting of a recent event is a hard genre, because the subject is documentary and the painter has to make a visit look significant. Montessuy composes in the academic manner of his day, with the pope central, the architecture clear and the figures grouped, and such pictures risk being more record than art. Their value lies in the detail of dress and interior and the sense of a particular ceremony, a witness to the culture of papal Rome."},"met:436829":{"d":"A woman, a man and a child with a dog share a room in which a painter works on a portrait. The title names the man as the Flemish artist Hugo van der Goes and the woman as Mary of Burgundy, and the Met's keywords add a child, a dog and a painting. An oil on a wood panel of 59.4 by 86.4 centimeters, it is a small, wide cabinet picture, a nineteenth-century imagining of the fifteenth century.","i":"Hugo van der Goes (about 1440-1482) painted the Portinari Altarpiece and ended his life in a monastery near Brussels; Mary of Burgundy (1457-1482) inherited the Burgundian lands in 1477 and married the future Emperor Maximilian I. Nineteenth-century painters loved scenes of old masters at work, which offered picturesque costume and settings; no portrait of Mary by van der Goes is known to survive, so the scene is a Romantic invention.","c":"Historicist anecdote painting is easy to dismiss and hard to do well: the meticulous costumes, interiors and still-life details are the point, and a skilled craftsman like Koller can deliver them with relish. The weakness is that the feeling is borrowed, and the picture's sentimental idea of the old master as a gentle genius owes more to 1870s taste than to the fifteenth century. It is best enjoyed as polished, slightly cloying nostalgia."},"met:435860":{"d":"A man identified by the title as a Venetian senator is shown in oil on canvas, about 69 by 61 centimeters, painted about 1525. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, so his pose, gown, cap and the background are left unstated. A senator of the Venetian republic was a patrician member of the Senate, the body that managed foreign policy, finance and the fleet, and such men were painted in sober dress and with grave expressions suited to public duty.","i":"Venetian portraits of the ruling class stressed dignity, restraint and the collective values of the republic more than personality: a patrician was a servant of the state first. Catena, who worked in the circle of Giovanni Bellini, painted many such sitters, and the title records the rank but not the name, which has been lost. The picture reflects a culture in which office, not the individual, was the source of authority.","c":"Catena is overshadowed by Giovanni Bellini, Giorgione and Titian, and his portraits are more reserved and somewhat drier than theirs. But restraint suits a Venetian senator, and a plain, firmly drawn likeness without flourish has its own authority. Without a description of the surface it is difficult to say more, but the type, solid, dignified and cool, is among the staples of Venetian portraiture, and its honest craft deserves respect."},"met:435859":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 126 by 208 centimeters, painted probably after 1520, shows the Adoration of the Shepherds. The museum's keywords record a man, the Christ Child, a dog, Joseph, Mary and the adoration, so the number of shepherds, the setting and the colors are left unstated. The wide format suggests a Venetian devotional painting that spreads the Holy Family and the shepherds along the picture plane, and the dog adds a note of rustic realism.","i":"The shepherds of Luke 2 are the first to learn of Christ's birth, summoned by angels on the hillside, and their visit stands for the humble coming first to the Savior. Venetian painters from Giorgione and Bellini on set the scene in a luminous landscape and gave the shepherds rustic dress and sincere devotion. The dog, a shepherd's companion, brings the pastoral world into the sacred scene and may also allude to fidelity or watchfulness.","c":"Catena, a careful craftsman rather than a great innovator, was working in the shadow of Giorgione and Titian here, and the picture is best taken as a reflection of Venetian pastoral taste: a gentle, orderly nativity in a soft landscape, with warm color and calm figures. It lacks the poetry and daring of his famous contemporaries, but the large format, the rustic details and the dog give it freshness and charm."},"met:839041":{"d":"A small oil study of water and plants, painted in 1837 on paper later laid down on wood, 39.0 by 52.0 centimeters. The title gives the whole subject, a stretch of water with plants at its edge, with no figures or landmarks. Oil on paper was a favored support for sketches made outdoors in the early nineteenth century, being light, portable and quick to dry, and it lets the painter note the look of damp, living growth at a single sitting.","i":"Fearnley, a Norwegian who studied with J. C. Dahl in Dresden and traveled widely in Europe, took up the new practice of painting oil studies in the open air, which artists such as Constable and Dahl had made respectable. These studies were made for private use as raw material for finished pictures, not for exhibition, which gives them a freshness and directness that polished landscapes rarely have.","c":"Studies like this are where the Romantic landscape painters are most modern. The plants and water are recorded with an economy and freshness of observation that a finished picture would suppress; the subject has no focus, and the interest is in color, touch and the feel of damp growth. Fearnley's reputation rests on large, dramatic exhibition pieces, but many viewers now prefer his sketches, which seem to anticipate the later direct study of nature."},"met:839044":{"d":"A tiny landscape study, only 18.0 by 28.0 centimeters, painted in 1839 in oil on paper laid down on wood, of Gudvangen, a village at the head of a narrow fjord in western Norway. The Met's subject tags list a waterfall, a mountain and a landscape, so the study shows the steep mountain walls and falling water for which the place is famous, in a space small enough to hold in two hands.","i":"Gudvangen lies at the end of the Nærøyfjord, an arm of the Sognefjord, where sheer rock walls and waterfalls made it a favorite stop for travelers and a symbol of Norwegian grandeur. Fearnley, born in Norway and trained abroad, made summer journeys through the country to sketch such motifs, which he later worked up into exhibition pictures of the sublime, in an age when Norwegian scenery was becoming a national subject.","c":"A sketch this small must take the whole mountain in a few strokes, and the best of Fearnley's studies do exactly that: the vertical drop of rock and the white thread of water, the cool light, the sense of enclosure. The risk is the opposite of grandeur, a scene reduced to a postcard; the pleasure is the speed and truth of the note. Beside his larger pictures, such studies often show the more original eye."},"met:437288":{"d":"Christ on the cross, in tempera and gold on a tall, narrow wood panel, 103.8 by 45.7 centimeters. The Met's keywords record only Christ and the Crucifixion, so the picture concentrates on the dying figure against the gold ground that gives such panels their glow. The tall, narrow shape suggests a part of a larger ensemble, such as the central element of a small altarpiece or devotional group, and the date is in the first half of the fourteenth century.","i":"The Riminese school, active in the Adriatic city of Rimini in the first half of the fourteenth century, absorbed Giotto's influence after he worked in Rimini in the early fourteenth century, and developed an intense, expressive style. Pietro da Rimini was one of its leading painters. The Crucified Christ was the most important devotional image of the age, a focus for meditation on suffering, and Riminese painters were noted for the pathos of their Crucifixions.","c":"Riminese painting is among the most emotional of the Trecento: sharp lines, tilting bodies and fierce color. A Crucifixion by Pietro makes its effect through the body's contour and the gold ground, and a viewer should look at the line of the torso and the drawing of the head: intensity of expression rather than naturalism. Its austere power is more striking than polish, and the gold gives it a luminous presence."},"met:436078":{"d":"A woman personifying Charity is the subject, and the record lists an infant, a fire and a pitcher among the motifs. The canvas is large, about 120 by 105 centimeters, and was painted around 1630 by Cesare Dandini, a Florentine. In such allegories Charity is conventionally a mother figure with children; further details of her pose, dress and setting are not recorded.","i":"Charity, love of one's neighbor, is one of the three theological virtues alongside Faith and Hope, and in art she is a nursing or protecting mother, often with a flame signifying ardent love. The pitcher and fire in the record may allude to nourishing and warming care. Allegories of the virtues were popular in Baroque Italy, where they decorated palaces as well as chapels and served as models of conduct.","c":"Dandini belonged to the generation of Florentine painters who softened the older, drier local style with warmer color and a tender naturalism, and a Charity gave him an ideal subject for it. Such pictures are agreeable and easy to read, but the Baroque Charity was painted so often that even a skilled version risks sameness. This one is respectable work rather than a landmark."},"nga:127263":{"d":"A dirt path winds through a dense forest whose leaves glow gold around silvery-gray boulders, in a vertical landscape. Close to the viewer the boulders are partly covered with moss in shades of green and russet red. Sunlight from the upper right catches a sapling crowned with flame-red leaves in the lower right, with yellow-green shrubs and two more putty-gray boulders beyond. The path recedes just left of center between trees with rough bark and ends at a fiery copper glow in the distance. The painter signed and dated it in the lower right, in Philadelphia, 1863.","i":"In the early 1860s Richards belonged to the American Pre-Raphaelites, a group of painters who followed John Ruskin's call to paint nature with scrupulous, truthful detail, studying individual leaves, rocks and mosses. Autumn foliage, with its brilliant color, was a favored subject, and the title, October, announces the season in a single word. The inscription marks his home city, Philadelphia.","c":"The detail is astonishing: the moss on the boulders, the rough bark and the single flame-red sapling are painted with a naturalist's patience, and the glowing end of the path draws the eye like a lamp. The weakness of American Pre-Raphaelite painting is the loss of unity: when every leaf is equal the whole can feel like an inventory. Richards would soon leave this precision for the broader, atmospheric seascapes for which he is remembered."},"met:11894":{"d":"A river crossing in autumn, in a vertical canvas of 61.3 by 50.8 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a river, a ford, trees, a woman, a cart, cattle and the season, so the scene is a rural one: a woman, a cart and cattle at a ford beneath autumn trees, in the warm, hazy light that gives the title its meaning. The subject is as much a mood as an incident, the soft, golden stillness of late fall.","i":"Indian summer, the warm spell in late autumn after the first frost, was a poetic season in nineteenth-century American culture, evoking nostalgia, the pastoral and the passing of time. By 1875 Richards had moved from the Pre-Raphaelite detail of his early work toward a softer, more atmospheric style while keeping his careful observation of trees and water. The woman, cart and cattle give a quiet note of rural life.","c":"A nostalgic, tender picture, whose subject is a mood: the atmosphere of haze and fading warmth is what counts, while the details of cart and cattle at the ford provide an anecdotal anchor. The risk of Indian-summer pictures is sweetness, and Richards's saving grace is truthful observation of trees and water. It is a quieter and more tonal work than his early forest interiors, and many viewers will find it the more appealing."},"met:17574":{"d":"A wide landscape of mountains and trees, 58.4 by 94.0 centimeters, painted in 1877 and named for the Bouquet Valley in the Adirondack Mountains of New York. The Met's catalog tags include trees, mountains and a landscape. The horizontal format suits a panoramic view in which ranges recede beyond a valley floor, with trees near at hand and the mountains softened by distance and light.","i":"The Adirondacks became a popular destination for painters and tourists after the Civil War, a wild and accessible region that stood for American nature. The Bouquet River rises in the high peaks and flows to Lake Champlain. Richards, a Philadelphian best known for later seascapes, painted mountain scenery in the 1870s in a tradition descended from the Hudson River School but with a lighter, silvery touch.","c":"A wide, calm, panoramic landscape that favors breadth over drama. By 1877 Richards's debt to the Hudson River School is evident, but he treats the view with an airy delicacy rather than the sublime spectacle of the older generation. The risk is a certain generic quality, since one mountain valley can resemble another, while the strengths are spaciousness and a clear, even light. It is a pleasant, well-judged picture, not a revelation."},"cma:173633":{"d":"A landscape on a vertical canvas of 61.0 by 50.9 centimeters, painted in the 1870s, whose title names the day and season: a day in June. The picture is a small, quiet landscape of the sort Richards produced in those years, depicting the green, light-filled look of early summer in a soft, tonal manner rather than the sharp detail of his earlier work, with attention to trees, foliage and the sky.","i":"By the 1870s Richards had moved from the exact, Ruskin-inspired detail of the American Pre-Raphaelites toward a gentler, more atmospheric style, and he often painted summer woods, fields and water in the countryside around Philadelphia and in the northeastern states. A title that simply names a day and season invites the viewer to enjoy the mood of the weather, the pleasure of a warm, bright June.","c":"A picture called June Day has to make the viewer feel summer, and the test is the light: the greens must glow without turning garish, and the air must seem to move. Richards was more convincing when he painted the sea, but his landscapes of these years have a quiet competence and a gentle honesty. The weakness is a lack of a strong focus; the strength is an even, truthful tone."},"met:11903":{"d":"Wind-driven waves break against the Cornish coast near Land's End, England's westernmost point, in a wide horizontal canvas of 59.1 by 112.4 centimeters, painted in 1879 in oil and now mounted on masonite. The Met's keywords record wind waves and marine art. The width of the canvas gives the sea room to roll across the picture, with the sense of an open horizon and a restless, weather-driven surface.","i":"Richards spent the years 1878 to 1880 in England, painting the coasts of Cornwall and elsewhere, and these seascapes launched his later reputation as the leading American painter of the sea. Land's End, where Cornwall meets the Atlantic, offered rocks, surf and wide skies. Richards had studied the structure of waves for years, and his English coastal pictures combine that accuracy with a landscape painter's feeling for light and space.","c":"Richards's mature seascapes combine a naturalist's accurate eye for the structure of a wave with a landscape painter's feeling for light and air. The wide format gives the sea room, and the thin, shimmering paint conveys movement. The risk is that the work can seem polite beside the grandeur of the Atlantic, with too little danger; but the accuracy of the water, which Richards studied for decades, is the point, and it is persuasive."},"nga:195170":{"d":"A shoreline stretches across a wide canvas, 102.2 by 183.5 centimeters: dark green ocean at the left, with a small ship sailing in the distance, and gentle waves rolling in from the left onto a sandy beach, where wet sand at the right reflects the sky. The palette is soft and muted, in blue, green, yellow and very light gray. The vast sky is a pale blue-gray with white clouds, and small seabirds fly above the water.","i":"In the 1880s Richards painted the shores of New Jersey and New England after his English years, and the New Jersey coast was then becoming a resort region of seaside hotels and holiday crowds. Richards ignores all that and paints an empty beach, treating the shoreline as a subject of pure atmosphere: air, water, sand and sky. The title, On the Coast, is plain, and the picture's quietness is intentional.","c":"At nearly two meters wide, the picture offers an empty, quiet expanse in which wet sand reflects the sky. The strength is the delicate tonal harmony and sense of space; the weakness is eventlessness, since nothing happens except weather. It exemplifies Richards's late ambition to make a seascape without drama, peaceful, truthful and a little melancholy. Those who want spectacle will be disappointed, while those who like stillness will find it persuasive."},"met:11911":{"d":"A small seascape on board, only 22.2 by 40.3 centimeters, painted in the 1890s, showing surf breaking on rocks. The Met's subject tags list the sea, the coast, sky, clouds, sea foam, wind waves and the horizon, so the picture is a fast, direct study of white water against dark rock under a changing sky. At this size it has the character of a sketch made outdoors and kept as a record of the motion of the sea.","i":"Richards spent summers on the coasts of New England and in England, studying surf and rocks with an almost scientific patience, and the small oil studies he made on board were both private records and raw material for larger pictures. By the 1890s he had become the leading American painter of the sea, admired for his truthful wave drawing. Pictures like this show the foundation of that reputation.","c":"Small oil studies are often the best of a marine painter, because the sea cannot be arranged: Richards has to catch foam, spray and light in a few strokes. The economy is the virtue here; the risk is that the sketch looks slight beside his large exhibition seascapes. The viewer should look at how the paint stands for the movement of water, and at the clear, wide horizon that gives the small panel its sense of space."},"met:17447":{"d":"A woman painted in 1764 in oil on a canvas of 76.2 by 63.5 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, the standard bust-length portrait size. The museum's keywords note lace, a pearl necklace, an earring, a cap, a flower and a bracelet, so the sitter is shown with the accessories of a prosperous woman of the 1760s: lace trimmings, pearls, a cap on her hair and a flower, all set around the face.","i":"Colonial American portraits of the 1760s were commissioned by merchants and landowners in port cities such as New York, Boston and Philadelphia, who wanted likenesses that proved their prosperity and refinement; painters often borrowed poses and costumes from English mezzotints. Pearls, lace and a flower were standard accessories of fashion and virtue for a woman sitter. The sitter is unidentified.","c":"Colonial portraits like this often combine a fairly stiff, linear manner with a delight in textures, lace, pearls and silks, that stands in for psychological depth. Kilburn was a competent provincial painter, not a Copley, and the interest of the picture lies in the costume details and the sitter's dignity. The pearls and lace are likely the most carefully painted parts, and the face should be judged as a plain, direct likeness."},"met:11273":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Jacob Hurd, painted about 1762 in oil on a canvas of 76.2 by 63.5 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, the standard bust-length size. The painter, William Johnston (1732-1772), gives a plain, direct likeness in the simple bust-length format that colonial families commissioned to record themselves in the 1760s. Colonial portraitists often took both standard canvas sizes and stock poses from English prints, which kept the work quick and the price modest.","i":"The Met also holds Johnston's portrait of Mrs. Jacob Hurd and Child, of about the same date and size, so this picture was probably one half of a husband-and-wife pair made when the couple had a young family. Pairs of portraits, one of the husband and one of the wife, were a standard colonial commission, hung together in the parlor to display a household's respectability and its continuity.","c":"In a bust-length format the face carries the portrait, and Johnston's likenesses are sober, plain and direct; he is usually considered a minor figure beside Copley, then the rising star of colonial portraiture. The question to ask is whether the picture gives the sitter the self-possession that a respectable colonial man would want, and the answer lies in the eyes and the set of the mouth. As half of a pair it is best seen beside its companion."},"met:11274":{"d":"A mother with an infant, painted about 1762 in oil on a canvas of 76.2 by 63.5 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a woman, an infant, a child, a toy, a cap, lace, an earring and a necklace, so the mother appears in her finery, with the baby and a toy beside her. A mother and child together are less common in colonial American portraits than a single sitter, which makes the picture a record of a young family as well as of a woman.","i":"Mothers with infants appear in colonial portraiture mainly when the child's arrival was a family event, and the toy and the baby's cap show the child's age and the mother's care. The lace, earring and necklace record the household's prosperity. The picture is probably a companion to the Met's portrait of Jacob Hurd by the same painter, so the two together record a couple at the start of a family.","c":"A mother with a child asks a colonial painter to do something he rarely does: show affection and some sense of movement. Johnston is a plain, careful painter, and the question is how far the picture breaks out of the formal bust-length pose, whether mother and child seem related. The cap, toy and lace are easier than the faces, and the viewer should look for warmth in the pairing. It is a valuable record of a young family."},"met:11275":{"d":"A woman identified as Mrs. Samuel Gardiner, painted in 1763 in oil on a canvas of 91.4 by 73.6 centimeters, about thirty-six by twenty-nine inches, close to the English kit-cat size. The Met's keywords record a flower, an iris, a ribbon and a pearl necklace, and name the sitter as Abigail Gardiner. The kit-cat format shows the sitter to about the hips with her hands visible, here with the flower and the ribbon as accents.","i":"Flowers in a woman's portrait typically signify beauty, virtue and the brevity of youth, and an iris, a garden flower, may also reflect the sitter's own interest or the artist's choice of a graceful accessory. Pearls and a ribbon add the notes of fashion and respectability that a colonial wife wanted. The picture appears to be the companion of the portrait of her husband, Samuel Gardiner, painted in the same year at the same size.","c":"A kit-cat portrait gives the painter more to do than a simple bust: hands, a torso, an accessory. Johnston is a competent, plain painter, and the flower and the pearls are the passages that give the picture life; the face should be judged for its directness and composure. He is a minor figure beside Copley, but the pair of the Gardiners is a good example of what a respectable colonial couple wanted to see on their walls."},"met:11276":{"d":"A portrait of Samuel Gardiner, painted in 1763 in oil on a canvas of 91.4 by 73.6 centimeters, about thirty-six by twenty-nine inches, close to the English kit-cat size. The kit-cat format typically shows the sitter to about the hips with a hand visible, which gives the painter a little room for dress and gesture while keeping the face as the focus. It appears to be the companion to the portrait of his wife.","i":"Pairs of portraits of husband and wife were a standard colonial commission, hung together in a parlor or hall to display a household's standing and continuity, and Samuel Gardiner's likeness balances that of Abigail, painted the same year at the same size. For a man the format emphasized sober dress and a composed face, the marks of a respectable householder, and a viewer of the 1760s would have read it as a statement of reliability.","c":"A pair is only as good as its weaker half, and the husband's portrait must carry authority without the decorative help of flowers or pearls. Johnston relies on a plain, direct likeness and sober dress, which suits the subject but limits the picture's life. The most telling part is the face, and the viewer should judge whether it has the self-possession expected of a colonial gentleman, seen beside the more ornamental portrait of his wife."},"nga:34122":{"d":"A man stands, seen from the waist up, with one hand on his hip and the other pointing down toward the lower left, holding two small objects that look like pieces of wheat. He has a slight smile, pale skin, a long nose and long, dark hair falling in waves to his shoulders. He wears a brown coat with large buttons and wide cuffs, a matching waistcoat and a white cravat. Dark red-brown drapery fills the right background, and at the left a window or outdoor view shows tall, slender trees under a dark blue sky.","i":"Dated 1719, this portrait of Mr. Van Vechten, a sitter with a Dutch-American name, comes from the period when portrait painting was only beginning in the colonies. Limners, as such painters were called, borrowed poses and props from English prints, and here the dark drapery and the view of trees follow European convention. The wheat in the pointing hand may refer to the sitter's farming or his prosperity. The painter is known by a conventional name, the Schuyler Limner.","c":"Early colonial portraits are crude by European standards but compelling: the flat, sharply outlined face, the bold pattern, the hand pointing like a signpost, the big-buttoned coat. The Schuyler Limner has a naive sincerity and a self-taught energy, and the modeling is weak, as a European viewer would say, provincial. Yet the vigor and directness that result make the picture a founding document of American portraiture, and the mysterious wheat gives it a hint of symbolism."},"nga:43715":{"d":"A man stands facing slightly left, shown from the thighs up, with his face turned to the viewer. He has a broad face with rosy cheeks, a long nose and small eyes, and shoulder-length brown hair with a slight wave. He wears a long rust-colored coat with silver buttons, a white cravat and white cuffs; one hand wears a white glove and holds the other glove, a black hat is tucked under his arm, and a sword hangs at his waist. His other hand points downward toward the lower left. The dark background shows a cloudy sky and a ship sailing on calm water.","i":"The ship in the background may point to a connection with overseas trade or the sea, though the picture does not say so. The sword, gloves and hat are the marks of a gentleman, so Mr. Willson claims genteel status through his dress and props, as colonial sitters did, borrowing the conventions of English portraiture. The pointing hand is a mannerism shared with the portrait of Mr. Van Vechten, dated a year earlier, by the same painter.","c":"A stiff but striking portrait: the strong red-brown coat and white accents give a bold pattern, the face is broad and flat, and the pointing hand repeats a formula. The ship adds narrative interest and a sense of the wider Atlantic world. Compared with a London portrait of the same date the modeling is naive and the anatomy stiff, but its direct, upright presence has a primitive authority that sophisticated portraits often lack."},"met:10800":{"d":"A portrait of a boy with a book, in oil on a canvas of 127.6 by 87.6 centimeters, painted about 1766 to 1768. The Met's subject tags list a boy and a book. The title cautiously says the boy is possibly Richard Crossfield, so his identity is only a likely guess. At this height the picture is a full or nearly full-length portrait of a child, and the book signals education and the family's hopes for his future.","i":"John Durand worked in colonial New York and Virginia as a limner, painting children and adults in plain, strongly outlined portraits for merchant and planter families. Portraits of children were a way for parents to display wealth and the child's promise, and a book was the conventional attribute of learning. The cautious title shows how thin the documentation can be: even for a work of this scale the sitter's name is uncertain.","c":"Durand's children are among the most endearing of colonial portraits: flat, bright, boldly outlined, with a naive sweetness that compensates for stiff drawing. A boy with a book becomes an emblem of learning, and the attention to costume, buttons and shoes shows the painter's delight in surface pattern. These are not sophisticated pictures, but they have the candor and clarity that make American colonial portraiture appealing to modern eyes."},"met:10801":{"d":"A woman with a rose, in oil on a canvas of 90.2 by 70.2 centimeters, about 1768 to 1770. The title says the sitter is either Susannah or Mary Bontecou, so the identification is uncertain between two women of the same family. The museum's keywords note a woman, a rose and a portrait. The rose, held or placed in the picture, is a traditional emblem of beauty and love, and in a portrait of a young woman it often points to marriage.","i":"A rose in a woman's portrait signals beauty, love and the brevity of youth, and it may also allude to an engagement or marriage. In colonial America, portraits of young women were commissioned as a mark of their coming of age and prospects, and painters like Durand, working from town to town, supplied the demand in a plain but charming style. The uncertainty over which Bontecou she is shows how thin the documentation is for many such pictures.","c":"A neat, flat, appealing portrait, with the rose adding color and meaning and the stiffness of the pose reflecting borrowing from English mezzotint models. The strengths are clarity and decorative pattern; the weakness is the lack of three-dimensional modeling. It should be judged for its character: the sitter's direct, composed look matters more than any technical finish, and the picture's charm is that of a confident, provincial hand."},"nga:59899":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, facing slightly to her right but looking straight at the viewer with bright eyes and a slight smile. Her dark hair is neatly styled back and decorated with a headpiece of small flowers and ribbons. She wears a mustard-coloured gown with lace ruffles at the sleeves and a multi-strand pearl necklace, and holds a pink rosebud in one hand and a closed fan in the other. The background is dark blue, with soft pink flowers beside her. The canvas is about 91 by 71 centimetres, and it has a pendant portrait of her husband, John Lothrop.","i":"The rosebud and fan are attributes of a fashionable young woman: the rose speaks of beauty and love, perhaps of marriage, and the fan of elegance. Pairs of husband and wife portraits like this were hung together in the home as a statement of family. John Durand was an itinerant portraitist in colonial America in the 1760s and 1770s who, like many of his peers, probably took poses and costumes from European prints; the mustard silk and pearls show how sitters wished to appear in the latest style.","c":"The sparkle of the pearls and lace, the clear colour of the mustard gown against the dark blue ground, and the sitter's direct, friendly look give this portrait a pleasing freshness. It is naive in modelling and stiff in the handling of rose and fan, but the flat, linear style is part of the charm. Durand is no Copley: the portraits are about presence and finery, not the subtle rendering of flesh or character."},"nga:59964":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, facing slightly to the right and meeting the viewer's eye with a neutral expression. He has rounded cheeks and short dark brown hair that curls a little at the ends. He wears a white shirt and cravat with elaborate ruffled cuffs, a buttoned waistcoat and a deep orange overcoat with gold buttons, one hand tucked into the waistcoat. At the right a shelf holds a bound book and a quill in an inkwell, set against a plain brown backdrop. The canvas is about 91 by 71 centimetres, matching the portrait of Mrs. John Lothrop.","i":"The book and quill mark the sitter as a man of letters and business, as a merchant, lawyer or official might wish to appear. The hand in the waistcoat is a gesture of composure borrowed from European portraiture, where it signified gentlemanly ease. Paired with Mrs. Lothrop's portrait, it let a husband and wife show themselves as a couple of means and refinement. The painter, John Durand, travelled between colonial towns taking such commissions.","c":"A plain, no-nonsense likeness: the orange coat and gold buttons are the liveliest passage, and the book and quill are lined up to give the sitter an air of learning. The face is neutral and the modelling flat, so the man seems more a set of signs than a personality. That candour about position and means is the picture's value; it shows how colonial sitters wished to be seen, not who they were."},"met:436784":{"d":"A courtyard scene, in oil on a canvas of 67.3 by 82.2 centimeters, painted in the early 1660s. The Met's catalog tags include a house, a courtyard, a girl, a woman and a dog, so the picture shows an enclosed space beside a Dutch house in which a woman, a girl and a dog share the yard. The format is that of a cabinet picture for a Dutch burgher's parlor, made to be looked at closely, and the subject is domestic life.","i":"The courtyard of a Dutch town house was a place for domestic work, children's play and quiet leisure, and painters of Delft and Rotterdam in the 1650s and 1660s made it a favorite subject, valuing its geometry of brick, doorways and light. Such pictures presented the Dutch household as clean, orderly and virtuous. Ludolf de Jongh, born near Rotterdam, also painted portraits and hunting parties, and took up courtyard subjects in the manner of Pieter de Hooch.","c":"De Jongh is not de Hooch, and the comparison is fair. De Hooch's courtyards have a luminous clarity of space and a poetry of light on brick; de Jongh's version has the right ingredients, woman, girl, dog and house, but less magic. The success of such a picture depends on the perspective and the light, and the viewer should judge how convincingly the courtyard recedes. It is a charming, competent, second-rank example of a beloved Dutch type."},"met:15185":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1840, 7.7 by 6.5 centimeters. The title calls her only a lady. The ivory is a little broader than the typical upright pocket miniature, which gives the head and shoulders more room, and the face is built up in tiny strokes through which the white of the ivory glows. The painter, William Harrison Scarborough (1812-1871), was then about twenty-eight.","i":"Around 1840 the miniature was nearing the end of its long popularity, as the daguerreotype reached American cities, but it remained a mark of taste and expense for those who wanted color and a handmade finish. An unnamed lady is a typical survivor: the name was lost while the object stayed in a family drawer or a dealer's tray, so she now stands for a generation of Americans portrayed at the moment photography arrived.","c":"A miniature of an unknown sitter rests on its technique: the stippling of the face, the softness of edges, the treatment of hair and the quiet color. Scarborough works in the polite, evenly lit style of American miniature painting around 1840, and the viewer should look for the point at which a generic type becomes a person, which is rare. It is a sound, unshowy example, valuable for its date and its craft."},"met:15186":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1840, 7.3 by 5.7 centimeters, the more upright of the two similar portraits by William Harrison Scarborough in the Met. The title calls her only a lady. The shape is close to the standard pocket miniature, made for a case, locket or frame, with the head and shoulders filling the small sheet.","i":"By 1840 American miniaturists such as Scarborough worked in a market that was shrinking as the daguerreotype spread, and many painted in oil as well, so that a trade in tiny likenesses became one part of a wider portrait practice. A lady identified only by her sex and class is what remains of a sitter whose family chose a handmade, colored image instead of the new, mirror-like plate.","c":"Smaller than its counterpart, this ivory has less room for display and relies almost entirely on the face. In such a compact format the painter must be sure of the eyes and the mouth, and a viewer can judge the success by whether the sitter seems to look back. It is a modest object, valued for its craft and its place in the final decade of the American miniature."},"met:13359":{"d":"A view of a pulpit inside Saint Mark's, the great Byzantine-style basilica of Venice, in a narrow upright oil on canvas of 54.6 by 32.0 centimeters, painted about 1870. The Met's keywords record a church interior and a pulpit, so the picture is an architectural study: the carved and inlaid pulpit is the subject, in a church famous for its marble and gold mosaics. The tall, narrow format suits the vertical thrust of the interior.","i":"Saint Mark's, begun in the eleventh century on Byzantine models and covered with gold mosaics, was a favorite subject for nineteenth-century artists and tourists, and its pulpits, with marble columns and inlaid decoration, were sought-after subjects. American painters in Venice in the 1860s and 1870s found a market among travelers wanting souvenirs of their Italian tour, and Ruskin's Stones of Venice had taught English-speaking audiences to admire the church.","c":"Interior views of Saint Mark's are hard: gold, dark, glitter and deep perspective. A good one conveys the weight of the architecture and the glow of the mosaics, and the narrow canvas here concentrates the effect. Yewell, an American in Venice, offers a dependable record rather than a poetic vision, and if the result is more picturesque than profound, it shows how appealing the church was to visitors."},"met:14992":{"d":"A woman named Mary Crosby, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1840 by George Augustus Baker Jr., then about nineteen. The ivory is a tall, narrow rectangle, 8.5 by 5.5 centimeters, a proportion that suggests a longer view of the figure than the usual head-and-shoulders oval, possibly a half-length. It is small enough to be held in the palm. Each tiny stroke of watercolor on ivory must be laid beside the last without disturbing it, a patient method that rewards close looking.","i":"At nineteen, Baker was a rising young miniaturist, and the 1840 date places the portrait at the moment when the daguerreotype was reaching American cities. His later career, as a portrait painter in oil who portrayed New York sitters including the landscape painter John F. Kensett, shows how a miniaturist could move into larger pictures. A named woman, Mary Crosby, records a family in his early circle of patrons.","c":"A teenager's miniature should impress with its control, and the proportions and care here are the thing to look for. The key test is the face: the contour, the stippled shading, the life in the eyes. The elongated format is the most distinctive feature, a departure from the standard oval that hints at a young painter's ambition to give his sitter more presence. As an early work it is promising, and it is best seen beside his later oils."},"met:10093":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Mrs. William Loring Andrews, painted between 1861 and 1863 in oil on a canvas of 76.2 by 63.5 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, a standard bust-length size. The portrait belongs to the Civil War years, when George Augustus Baker Jr., who had begun as a miniaturist, had become a painter of society portraits in oil.","i":"Her husband was presumably William Loring Andrews (1837-1920), who became a leading New York book collector and writer on books and a founder of the Grolier Club, so the portrait may record the early years of a marriage that would later support a distinguished collecting career. Portraits of young wives in the 1860s presented them as refined, domestic and fashionably dressed, with the soft, idealized finish that society expected.","c":"Baker's reputation rested largely on smooth, flattering portraits of women, and a bust-length portrait like this is a typical specimen: pleasing in color, gently idealized and careful with fabric and lace. It lacks the psychological bite of the best portraits of its time, and the polish can feel impersonal. But as a record of New York taste during the Civil War years it is a fine period piece."},"met:10092":{"d":"A portrait of the landscape painter John Frederick Kensett, painted in 1875 in oil on a canvas of 68.6 by 55.9 centimeters. Kensett died in 1872, so the likeness is posthumous, presumably based on earlier portraits or photographs. The vertical format is that of a bust-length commemorative portrait, the face carrying the whole picture. The canvas measures about twenty-seven by twenty-two inches, a modest size suited to hanging in a study or a club room.","i":"Kensett (1816-1872) was a leading painter of the Hudson River School, known for tranquil, luminous views of the New England coast and lakes, and an original trustee of the Metropolitan Museum, which holds a large group of his works. A posthumous portrait by a fellow New York artist honors a colleague whose reputation was at its height at his death, and recalls a close-knit artistic community.","c":"Painting a dead colleague from earlier likenesses is a delicate task: the temptation is to flatter or to freeze. Baker's manner is smooth and polished, which suits a polite memorial, but a viewer who knows Kensett's luminous, tranquil coastal paintings might wish for the same quiet poetry in the portrait. It remains useful as a record of a founding figure of American landscape painting."},"met:436779":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted on ivory about the early 1770s by James Jennings, an English miniaturist. No size is recorded, but ivory miniatures of this date are normally palm-sized, made to be cased, framed or worn. The 1770s were the years of tall, elaborate hairstyles for women, and a miniature of the period gives the face and its frame of hair and ribbons the greatest care.","i":"In the 1770s the portrait miniature was a fashionable gift in polite English society, exchanged between lovers and relatives, set in lockets and bracelets or enclosed in jeweled cases. A woman identified only as a woman is the common fate of such small objects: when the family record failed, the name was lost, and the miniature survives as a type, a lady of the Georgian gentry or middle class, composed for a loved one.","c":"English miniatures of the early 1770s prize delicacy: smooth, pale flesh, soft powdered hair and a tender expression, in a cool, sweet palette. Jennings is a minor name beside Cosway or Smart, and the pleasure of such a work lies in the finesse of the stippling and the freshness of the face. The danger is prettiness without character, and the viewer should look for how far this sitter escapes the type."},"nga:139764":{"d":"An architectural view of Rome in oil on canvas, about 98 by 135 centimeters. Its title pairs the Basilica of Constantine, the vast vaulted ruin in the Roman Forum, with a Doric colonnade in an outdoor Roman setting. That combination points to a capriccio, an architectural fantasy that joins a recognizable monument to imagined neighbors, rather than a record of one spot. It is dated 1685 to 1690 and is by Niccolò Codazzi, a specialist in architectural views.","i":"The Basilica of Constantine, begun by the emperor Maxentius and completed by Constantine in the early fourth century, was a huge hall in the Roman Forum, and three of its immense barrel-vaulted bays still stand. Seventeenth-century artists and travelers saw the ruin as a monument to Rome's lost grandeur. Combining it with a Doric colonnade belongs to the capriccio tradition, where real and imagined architecture are arranged to show the painter's skill in perspective.","c":"Architectural painters of the later seventeenth century prized exact perspective and the pleasure of unexpected juxtapositions, and a Roman ruin of heroic scale joined to a presumably imagined colonnade invites admiration of the painter's grasp of space. The risk of such capricci is coldness, as stone and perspective can swamp incident. Codazzi is a specialist with a modest reputation, interesting as an example of how archaeology was turned into theater."},"nga:195704":{"d":"An open grey safe is crammed with stacks of banknotes and coins, painted so precisely that the scene pretends to be real. The paper money, in large bills from one thousand to ten thousand dollars, is tied in stacks with pink ribbons; the top shelf holds large silver coins and smaller bronze ones. Below hangs a yellow paper headed Semi-annual account, listing receipts, expenses, profit, notes, and gold and silver, with a total of 492,392,296.50. Yellow lettering across the top gives a railroad's name and Dividend's Safe, and the safe stands on a tan-and-grey checkered floor.","i":"The picture belongs to a small genre of trompe l'oeil money paintings that flourished in America in the 1880s and 1890s, when banknotes, silver certificates and the argument over gold and silver were national obsessions. Painted money was a witty joke, since the viewer might reach for the cash; the fictional dividend, the absurdly grand total and the safe left wide open add irony about wealth and security. The museum dates it to 1898 or later.","c":"A joke carried out with a straight face and considerable skill. The edges of the banknotes, the sheen of the coins and the metal of the safe are painted so exactly that the first reaction is delight, the second a smile at the 492-million total. As art it lives on illusion alone, with little composition or feeling beyond the trick. Yet it records an American mood about money more directly than most solemn portraits of financiers."},"met:435609":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted on ivory about 1790 by John Barry. No size is recorded, but ivory miniatures of this date are typically only a few centimeters high, made to be held in the hand, cased or set in jewelry. The likeness is a bust, the face built up in fine strokes over the translucent ivory, with the coat and the background treated more broadly.","i":"The 1790s were the last great decade of the English portrait miniature, with painters like Richard Cosway and John Smart supplying the court and the gentry, and lesser artists such as Barry meeting demand elsewhere. A miniature of an unnamed man served as a private token between family and friends, and the object now stands for the many sitters of the period whose identity has been lost.","c":"Technique is everything in a miniature, and Barry's translucent washes and fine stippling show the standard training of the period. Whether the portrait rises above that standard depends on the eyes, which should hold the viewer, and on the quiet design of head and shoulders. An unnamed man by a modest painter is a reminder that most miniatures were competent products of a trade rather than works of genius."},"met:435610":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted on ivory about 1790 by John Barry, a British miniaturist. No size is recorded, but ivory miniatures of this date are typically palm-sized. The 1790s brought softer, simpler fashions than the elaborate styles of the 1770s and early 1780s, with looser hair and lighter fabrics, and miniatures followed the change, set in small oval or round cases.","i":"Miniatures of about 1790 were exchanged in a period of upheaval: the French Revolution began in 1789, and Britain was at war with France from 1793, so keepsakes of the absent or the dead were more precious. A woman's miniature might be carried by a husband or kept by her family, and the unnamed sitter is the commonest survivor, a face that mattered to someone whose name is now lost.","c":"John Barry belongs to the many competent miniaturists of the age of Cosway, Engleheart and Smart, whose names have survived better. The pleasure of such work is in the delicate, translucent handling, the cool palette and the soft modeling of the face, and the limit is a sameness of type: every lady of the 1790s seems to have the same pale charm. The test is whether the eyes and mouth belong to a particular person."},"met:435611":{"d":"A man said to be John Durham, painted on ivory about 1790 by John Barry. The title's 'said to be' marks the identification as traditional, not documented. No size is recorded, but ivory miniatures of this date are typically only a few centimeters high. The sitter's powdered or natural hair and plain coat would follow the sober fashion for men in the 1790s.","i":"A tradition naming the sitter John Durham cannot be verified from the miniature itself, which is the usual position with unsigned or unlabeled small portraits: the name may have been written on a case or a backing and later lost or confused. Men's miniatures of about 1790 were given to wives, fiancés and parents, and carried by those leaving for the army, the navy or the colonies.","c":"Men's miniatures of the period are plainer than women's, with less lace and ribbon to enliven the surface, and the success of the portrait depends on the eyes and the modeling of the face. Barry works within the fine, translucent English manner, and the viewer should ask whether the head has solidity and the glance has life. The doubtful name does not matter if the likeness persuades; if it does not, no name can save it."},"met:846945":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Joanna de Silva, painted in 1792 in oil on a canvas of 76.2 by 63.5 centimeters, about thirty by twenty-five inches, a standard bust-length size. The painter, William Wood (1769-1810), also painted miniatures, so an oil portrait at this scale shows him working in a larger format than the ivory he used for other sitters, with a head and shoulders set out on a broad surface.","i":"The name Joanna de Silva is Portuguese or Spanish in form, and the title offers little more, so the sitter remains largely a name. The portrait is a reminder that the circle of people having their likenesses painted in 1790s Britain was widening beyond the aristocracy to merchants and professional families, many from communities outside the British gentry. A commissioned oil portrait asserted a place in society.","c":"In 1792 the soft, high-keyed manner of Romney and Hoppner dominated London portraiture, with Reynolds's career ending, and a bust-length oil by a miniaturist inevitably reflects that taste. The question is how well Wood translates his delicate finish to a larger scale: whether the face keeps its life when the surface grows. Without a documented biography for the sitter, the picture stands on its own, and the viewer should judge the glance and the handling of dress."},"met:437951":{"d":"A man, possibly William Abington (1770-1839), painted about 1798 on ivory by William Wood. The title's 'possibly' marks the identification as uncertain. No size is recorded, but such miniatures are usually only a few centimeters high. If the name is right, the sitter was about twenty-eight. The face is the finished center of a small image made to be held, cased or set in a locket.","i":"In 1798 Britain was at war with Revolutionary France, and miniatures of men in their twenties and thirties, often officers or men going abroad on business, were exchanged with families and sweethearts. A man of about twenty-eight might have sat for a fiancée or for parents before a journey. The hedged identification shows how names slip away from small portraits, which were meant for those who already knew the face.","c":"Wood's miniatures of the late 1790s show the taste of the time for clean lines, a cool, bright color and an open, direct gaze, and the best of them have a fresh, unfussy charm. The less distinguished are neat but unmemorable. Here the question is whether the sitter's character comes across through the eyes, and the doubtful name should not distract from that test. The missing size is a reminder of how fragile the record of such objects can be."},"met:437950":{"d":"A woman, seen in profile, is absorbed in a book. The title, An Interesting Story, suggests the tale that holds her, and the parenthesis names her as Miss Ray. It is a portrait miniature painted on ivory in 1806 by the British miniaturist William Wood, the kind of small, private object that was kept in a case or held in the palm. Instead of a formal bust turned toward the viewer, Wood chose a quiet moment: the sitter's attention lowered to the page, her face shown from the side.","i":"In 1806 \"interesting\" still carried its older sense of absorbing or moving, and the likeliest interesting story for a young woman was a sentimental novel. Pictures of women reading signaled sensibility and refinement, and they sometimes gently teased the fashion for novels. The profile view, familiar from silhouettes and classical cameos, was also in vogue, and it suits a sitter who is not meant to meet the viewer's eye. The pairing of a title and a name makes the picture part portrait, part little story.","c":"Wood worked in a crowded British trade in which most miniatures were head-and-shoulders likenesses, and the idea here, a reader in profile, is a pleasant step beyond that routine. Pose and title tell a small story, and the format forces economy: one figure, one book, one thought. A profile can look stiff when the face has little to do, but lowered eyes give it a purpose. It reads as a graceful conceit rather than a technical showpiece."},"met:437176":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man on vellum laid on prepared gessoed card, dated 1664, by the British miniaturist David Myers. The sitter is a man whose name is unknown. English miniature painting of this kind was known to contemporaries as limning, and a miniature of the 1660s was typically small enough to be held in the palm. Vellum on card was the standard support of English miniatures in the mid-seventeenth century, before ivory replaced it.","i":"In Restoration England, just after the return of Charles II in 1660, portrait miniatures were a fashionable way for gentry, courtiers and merchants to give likenesses as gifts and tokens, and the leading miniaturist of the age, Samuel Cooper, set a standard of strong modeling and honest likeness. Lesser painters such as Myers worked in the same tradition. The sitter's name is unknown.","c":"Miniatures of the 1660s can be very fine in the hands of Cooper and merely competent in lesser hands, and Myers likely belongs to the second company: careful drawing, clear color and a safe, somewhat flat likeness. Which company this one keeps is hard to say, but its date and support make it a good example of period technique and a small witness to the taste of the Restoration."},"met:437484":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unidentified man, painted on ivory and dated 1810. The record lists a man and a portrait, and the medium as ivory; no size is recorded. The sitter is anonymous and the museum gives no description of his appearance or dress. The painter, Charles Robertson, is classified as an Irish miniaturist, and the miniature came to the museum by bequest of Millie Bruhl Fredrick in 1962.","i":"Irish painters were prominent in the miniature trade of the British Isles in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries, and the date, 1810, falls in the middle of the golden age of the miniature, when a small portrait on ivory was the commonest way for a middle-class family to keep a likeness. Unnamed sitters like this one are common among surviving miniatures.","c":"A miniature of 1810 lived or died by its flesh tones and fineness of touch, and miniaturists of Robertson's generation were known for a neat, delicate technique with soft stippling. This one, anonymous and undescribed, can be placed only by its type. It is a pleasant survivor, likely more important to a collector of miniatures than to a historian of style."},"met:436234":{"d":"A man, identified only as an officer, is portrayed in a miniature painted on a thin slab of ivory by Andrew Dunn, an Irish artist recorded as working between about 1800 and 1820. Miniatures like this are small enough to hold in the palm or wear as a keepsake. Officers' portraits of the period usually show the head and shoulders in uniform, with details such as gold lace and epaulettes rendered in fine touches.","i":"During the Napoleonic wars, when many men left home to serve, portrait miniatures were exchanged between officers and their families and sweethearts as keepsakes, often set in lockets, rings or bracelets. An officer's likeness recorded rank and regiment at a moment of risk. The sitter here is unnamed, so what the portrait preserves is a type: the soldier as his family hoped to remember him.","c":"Miniature painting is judged on fidelity of likeness at tiny scale, delicate stippling and hatching, and the glow that ivory gives to flesh. Dunn is not among the best-known miniaturists of his day; the London masters, Richard Cosway above all, set a standard of elegance and wit that provincial painters seldom matched. Even so, the genre's appeal is its intimacy: a face kept to be held in the hand."},"met:437103":{"d":"A large English sailing ship lies in harbor with her sails loosened while a gun is fired. Peter Monamy painted it in oil on a canvas about 1.2 by 1.5 meters, big enough to hang as a wall picture rather than a cabinet piece. The subject follows the harbor scenes of the Dutch marine painters Willem van de Velde the Elder and Younger, who had settled in England in 1672. The title is the surest guide to the action: a ship, a harbor, a signal gun.","i":"Loosing the sails and firing a gun was one of the ways a ship signaled that she was about to sail, and guns were also fired as salutes of greeting and honor. Such pictures suited a seafaring nation. They let merchants, officers and shipowners enjoy a well-found ship and a show of national strength in an orderly harbor rather than in battle. The Dutch had perfected the type, and Monamy was among the first English painters to make it his own.","c":"Monamy was the leading English marine painter of his day, and his debt to the Van de Veldes is plain. Careful ship drawing is the usual strength of his work, and he could organize a harbor into a clear, handsome arrangement. He rarely matches the Dutchmen's silvery light or their feeling for open sky, so the result can seem competent and a little formulaic. Its significance is as a link to the English school of Samuel Scott and Charles Brooking."},"met:15012":{"d":"An unnamed woman is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, on a plate only 6.8 by 5.6 centimeters, small enough to rest in the palm. John Henry Brown made it around 1840. Miniatures of this kind were commissioned as keepsakes to be worn or kept in a small case, and the likeness is built up in minute strokes of thin color that let the white of the ivory glow through. The sitter's name has not come down with the picture, so it speaks through its type and date.","i":"By about 1840 the daguerreotype was arriving in America, and the portrait miniature was entering its last great years. It still offered what early photographs could not: color, a flattering touch and a precious little object. A woman's miniature might mark an engagement or marriage, or travel to relatives far away as a memento. Without a name, this lady stands for many such sitters, remembered now chiefly through the care someone took to have her painted.","c":"Brown was a busy miniaturist, and this is a competent example of the standard product: small, polished, conventional. The medium rewards a steady hand and fine stippling rather than invention, and an unnamed sitter leaves little to add. Its interest lies in the delicacy of the handling and in the evidence it gives of how long the miniature held out against photography. It is one of several Brown miniatures at the Met, and the family portraits among them say more."},"met:10242":{"d":"A man whose name is lost is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, on a plate 7.3 by 6.0 centimeters. John Henry Brown painted it around 1845. Like other portraits in this format, it was meant to be worn, carried or kept in a small case, to be looked at close up and handled. The whiteness of the ivory serves as the light of the picture, with thin washes of color laid over it and finer touches added to model the face.","i":"In the 1840s a man might have a miniature made for a bride-to-be, a wife or his parents, to be kept while he traveled, went west or put to sea. The form suited a society that valued modest, private tokens of affection. With no name attached, the gentleman cannot be placed in a family or a trade, but the commission itself shows that he, or someone who cared for him, wanted a likeness that could be held in the hand and carried about.","c":"The type leaves a miniaturist little room, and Brown works within it with polish rather than daring. That is a fair summary of the genre in the 1840s: accurate drawing, smooth finish, no distractions. The weakness here is anonymity, which turns a person into an example. As a specimen of a craft that photography was about to squeeze, it is respectable, and its appeal is that of a well-made small object rather than a bold picture."},"met:13393":{"d":"John Henry Brown portrays himself in watercolor on ivory, on a plate just 6.2 by 5.1 centimeters, around 1846, when he was in his late twenties. A miniaturist's self-portrait is a demonstration piece as much as a likeness: it can show his command of flesh tones and fine stippling on the most delicate of supports, and it could be kept by family or shown to prospective clients. It is also a rare case in which the sitter, the painter and the owner may be the same person.","i":"Artists painted themselves to try out a technique, to fill a quiet hour or to advertise their skill, and a miniaturist had few chances to portray anyone as patient as himself. In the mid-1840s the daguerreotype was taking away routine business, so a hand-painted likeness by the maker was a reminder of what a trained painter could offer. The self-portrait also records how Brown wished to be seen: as a professional, in a small, polished and public-facing form.","c":"In a miniature there is no room for broad effect, only accurate drawing and delicate color, so a self-portrait tests a painter's honesty. Brown's is modest and personal, more interesting for the artist behind it than for any invention of pose. It will not rank with the great self-portraits, but as a document of a working miniaturist in the age of the daguerreotype it is valuable, and it gives a face to a name that otherwise rests on commissions."},"met:13394":{"d":"A woman named Adaline Peters Brown is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, on a plate 7.8 by 6.4 centimeters, dated 1846. John Henry Brown painted it, and she shares his surname, so the picture was probably a family one, perhaps a portrait of his wife. The format is intimate: a few inches of ivory, meant to be held close and looked at in good light, with the likeness built up from fine, almost invisible touches of thin color.","i":"Her full name, with a middle name that was probably her maiden name, follows a nineteenth-century habit of recording a woman by her married name while keeping the family name she was born to. A miniature of this kind might stand for a marriage, a bond between sisters or a gift to a parent. If she was the artist's wife, the commission carries an extra layer: a painter making a keepsake of someone he saw every day.","c":"A sitter close to the artist gives a miniaturist his best chance, because familiarity helps a likeness, and a named subject gives the picture a place in history. At the same time the small plate of ivory limits what Brown could do: drawing, tone and finish are the measures, not composition. As a family portrait it has more to offer than his unnamed commissions, but its virtues are quiet ones."},"met:17544":{"d":"A girl named Ada Brown is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated 1847, by John Henry Brown. Miniatures of this kind are small enough to be held in the hand. The sitter shares the artist's surname, and a portrait of Adaline Peters Brown dates from the year before, so she may have been a daughter. A child's likeness on ivory combined portraiture with the tenderness of a family memento, the sort of picture kept in a drawer or a case and taken out to be looked at.","i":"Children's miniatures were often made to record a particular age, to be kept as the child grew, and they sometimes became precious memorials when a child died young, as many did in the 1840s. A girl's portrait from 1847 would be treasured in a household whether she was the painter's own daughter or a young relation. Here the name is the key to the picture: it turns what might be a pleasant study into a keepsake with a person attached.","c":"Painting a child in miniature means catching an expression that will not hold still, and a painter working among his own family has an advantage. The small format favors a soft, simplified treatment, which suits a child's face. Brown's family portraits have a warmth that his unnamed commissions lack, though the artistic ambition stays modest. It is a sympathetic, unpretentious piece, and its value lies in the personal link it preserves."},"met:15013":{"d":"A youth, titled only Portrait of a Young Man, is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, a plate 9.1 by 7.3 centimeters, dated 1854. The museum's own keywords call the sitter a boy, so he was probably in his teens. John Henry Brown painted it on a plate larger than most of his other miniatures. The sitter's name has been lost, and the picture shows how a young person could still be given a keepsake likeness in the old, costly way.","i":"In 1854 a portrait on ivory was no longer the cheapest way to have a likeness taken. The daguerreotype was everywhere, and the ambrotype, a photograph on glass, was just being introduced. A family that still ordered a miniature wanted color, permanence, a handmade object and perhaps the status that went with it. Many miniaturists were also starting to paint over photographic bases, so a hand-painted ivory of this date records the form's survival against the camera.","c":"The larger plate gives Brown more room, and a young face, smooth and unmarked by age, is a congenial subject for the delicate stippling the medium encourages. The work stays within the habits of its date and does not try to be more than a polite, well-finished likeness. It is not a showpiece, but it holds up as a late example of a form that photography was about to retire."},"met:12806":{"d":"The U.S. Ship Franklin, a Navy ship of the line, is shown with a view of the Bay of New York on a wide canvas about 76 by 165 centimeters, more than twice as long as it is high. The English-born American painter Thomas Thompson made it in the 1820s or 1830s. The title gives the plan: a portrait of a particular warship, set in the bay, so that the picture serves as both a ship portrait and a view of the harbor.","i":"The Franklin was a 74-gun ship of the line, launched in 1815 and named for Benjamin Franklin. Warships of that size were symbols of the young republic's naval strength, and pictures of them were made for officers, shipowners and a public proud of its navy. A view that placed the ship in New York's bay also showed the city's role as the nation's chief port. Like most ship portraitists, Thompson served a clientele of sailors and merchants rather than gallery collectors.","c":"A ship portrait stands or falls on accuracy, and a picture of a named naval vessel had to satisfy people who knew her hull, rigging and flags. The wide format suits a harbor panorama and lets the ship breathe. Thompson works in the documentary tradition of ship painting rather than the atmospheric manner the Hudson River painters were developing, and the picture's value is as a record of a famous vessel and her bay."},"met:13324":{"d":"Mrs. John Cox, also recorded as Mrs. Thankful Harris Gore, is portrayed in watercolor on ivory about 1810 by the American miniaturist Henry Williams, on a plate only 6.3 by 5.1 centimeters. The double name in the title probably reflects two marriages. Because a miniature of John Cox of the same date and nearly the same size survives, this one was probably made as half of a pair, a set meant to be kept together in the cases of the day.","i":"A matched pair of miniatures was a way of recording a marriage: each partner a small, portable likeness that could lie side by side in a parlor, a drawer or a pair of cases. In 1810 American sitters were still working in the shadow of Edward Greene Malbone, the leading miniaturist, who had died in 1807, and an ivory miniature cost far less than an oil portrait. The two names in the title are a reminder that a woman's name in the records often changed with marriage.","c":"A miniature of this date is judged by delicacy and by whether the likeness feels alive within a few centimeters. Williams works in the polished idiom of the early republic, and the pairing with a portrait of John Cox gives the picture more presence than a single nameless likeness would have. It is not Malbone, whose grace few matched, but it is a competent example of the type, and a named sitter and narrow date range add real documentary value."},"met:13327":{"d":"John Cox is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, about 1810, on a plate 6.4 by 5.2 centimeters, by the American miniaturist Henry Williams. The size and date match the miniature of Mrs. John Cox, so the two were probably made as a pair. Like others of its kind it was a small, private object, painted in fine touches of thin color over the pale surface of the ivory, and meant to be kept in a case or worn as a keepsake.","i":"A man's miniature in this period might declare his place in a household or serve as a gift to his wife while he traveled, as many merchants and mariners did. Williams made his living from commissions of this kind, and a pair like this one shows the miniature at its most useful: a portable, affordable record of a marriage. The picture is the surviving proof of a man who otherwise goes unrecorded here.","c":"Taken alone, the picture is a well-made, restrained male likeness of its time; seen with its pendant, it gains. Miniatures like this leave no room for background drama, so everything depends on the head: its structure, its expression, the soft flesh tints the medium allows. Williams worked in Malbone's shadow, which is no disgrace, and competent, reliable portraitists made the best record of everyday sitters in the early republic."},"met:13321":{"d":"A man named Gorham L. Freeman is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, a plate 7.9 by 6.7 centimeters, dated 1813. Henry Williams, an American miniaturist, painted it in the middle of the War of 1812, a reminder that ordinary life and ordinary commissions went on beside great events. Such a miniature would have been kept in a case or set in a locket-style frame, and its size is large enough to give the face real presence.","i":"Men's miniatures of the early 1810s often marked a coming of age, an engagement or a departure. In wartime a likeness had an added sense, as a keepsake for those who stayed at home while a man served on land or at sea. Nothing in the title says which purpose this one served. What it shows is a society that still took pride in being recorded in the hand-painted way, one small ivory at a time.","c":"The plate is larger than the roughly 6-centimeter miniatures of the Cox pair, and Williams uses the extra room to give the head more weight. A named sitter, a firm date and a reliable hand make it a solid document of the period. It lacks the airy grace of Malbone's best, but it is a sound likeness in the controlled, stippled manner of its day."},"met:13320":{"d":"Edward Coverly is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated only to about 1810–15, on a plate 7.8 by 6.7 centimeters. The American miniaturist Henry Williams painted him in the fine, stippled manner the medium demands. As with other portraits of this kind, the picture is small enough to hold in the palm, and a recorded name gives it an advantage over the many miniatures whose subjects are now unknown.","i":"A date range of five years shows how often miniatures went undated, and how much then depends on the clues of costume and hairstyle. Nothing beyond the name survives in this record to place the sitter, but a miniature that has come down with its name attached is itself a small achievement of family memory. For a young man in these years a miniature might mark an engagement or the start of a career, and families kept such pictures in cases and drawers for generations.","c":"Williams offers a plain, direct view of a sitter, and the effect is quiet and intimate rather than grand. As an example of American miniature painting in the years after Malbone it is sound rather than inspired. Its best quality is probably its candor: with so little space and no background to speak of, the maker had to decide what mattered in a face, and he did so economically."},"met:15238":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated 1824, on a very small plate, 5.5 by 4.4 centimeters. Henry Williams, an American miniaturist, painted it six years before his death in 1830. The sitter's name is lost. At this size every touch of the brush had to be tiny, and the picture could be carried in a pocket, worn on a chain or kept in a small hinged case.","i":"By the 1820s the miniature was a mature form in America, competing with oil portraits, silhouettes and the first lithographs. Small portraits on ivory were still commissioned for betrothals, memorials and gifts, and Williams continued to supply them. An unnamed gentleman stands here for the whole class of sitters whose names slipped away from their likenesses once the families that owned them dispersed or the case changed hands.","c":"At 5.5 by 4.4 centimeters this is a very small plate, and the challenge is to keep a face lifelike at such a scale. The picture asks to be judged by patience and economy. Anonymity limits it, however: a gentleman without a name can only be an example of his time. It is a fair specimen of Williams's work in the 1820s, accomplished in craft and modest in ambition."},"met:15239":{"d":"An unnamed woman is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated 1826, on a plate 8.5 by 6.8 centimeters, a good deal larger than the Cox pair of about 1810. Henry Williams painted it four years before his death. The larger plate leaves room for the head and costume, and it belongs to the years when American miniatures, as a rule, grew bigger and bolder than the tiny stippled likenesses of Edward Greene Malbone's day.","i":"By the mid-1820s fashionable portraiture was changing, and miniatures were following. Sitters wanted richer color and more attention to dress and hair, and the plates grew to give both some room. An unnamed lady such as this one cannot be tied to a household, but the date places her in the years when women's fashions in America were turning from the slim Empire line toward fuller sleeves and skirts.","c":"The larger size lets Williams show more than a head, and a critic can credit him with adapting to the taste of the time instead of repeating the manner of 1810. The result is probably more worldly and decorative than his earlier work, though at some cost to the quiet intimacy of the smaller plates. As a record of changing fashion and technique it is useful, even if the sitter stays anonymous."},"met:11366":{"d":"Joseph Kyle portrays himself in oil on a small canvas, about 43 by 36 centimeters, around 1859, four years before his death. At this size the picture is probably a head-and-shoulders likeness, the sort of portrait a painter could make in his studio with a mirror and a free hour, for family or friends rather than for exhibition. The sitter looks out as the artist who made him, and the picture has the directness of that double role.","i":"A self-portrait is the one commission an artist gives himself. Mid-nineteenth-century painters used it to record their appearance for posterity, to test a style, or to leave something to their families. Kyle's dates, 1815 to 1863, place this likeness near the end of his life, when portraiture was still the steady trade on which most painters lived. The picture shows how he chose to be seen, as a working portraitist, and it remains the main record of his own face.","c":"A self-portrait must speak for its maker, and it asks to be judged on directness and economy. Painters who portray themselves rarely flatter, and an honest, unforced head can persuade more than a polished commission. Kyle was probably a competent portraitist rather than an inventive one, but the double role of sitter and maker gives the picture an intimacy that a commissioned likeness seldom has."},"met:11365":{"d":"An unnamed woman is portrayed in oil on a small canvas, 32.7 by 27.8 centimeters, dated 1861, by Joseph Kyle. At this size it is a modest likeness, probably of the head and shoulders, the kind a family would hang in a parlor or keep on a dresser. The date places it in the year the American Civil War began and two years before Kyle's death. The sitter's name has not survived with the picture.","i":"Portraits like this were the staple of a painter's living: relatives commissioned a likeness of a wife, mother or sister to mark a marriage, a departure or a loss. The word lady in the title suggests a woman of respectable standing, though her name was not kept. Without it, the picture is a type, a woman of 1861 painted for the people who knew her and now remembered chiefly through the painter's name. In wartime, such likenesses took on added weight as keepsakes.","c":"A small oil portrait depends on a convincing head and a calm, simple arrangement, and the modest format helps by keeping the picture focused. Kyle was a competent rather than inventive portraitist, and as far as can be told nothing marks this picture out from the many similar likenesses of its day. Its appeal is straightforwardness: an honest, unpretentious record of a person, without the polish of the leading portraitists of the period."},"met:11088":{"d":"A close study of dark rock and falling water, painted in oil on a canvas about 51 by 61 centimeters. Trees stand beside the cascade, but the title shows the real subject: trap rock, the dark, hard stone formed from cooled volcanic material and quarried for road metal, seen at Buttermilk Falls. John Henry Hill, then about twenty-four, painted it in 1863. The word study and the choice of subject point to close observation of the rock's structure rather than a composed view.","i":"The picture belongs to the American Pre-Raphaelite current of the early 1860s. Followers of the English critic John Ruskin urged painters to go to nature and record rocks, plants and water with scientific honesty, and in 1863 New York artists founded the Association for the Advancement of Truth in Art. Hill, trained by his father, the landscape painter and printmaker John William Hill, shared that outlook. A close study of rock was an exercise in the group's creed: look first, compose later.","c":"Hill's study is humble and brave: instead of a sunset view with a framing tree, he paints water falling over layered rock and gives the geology the attention a portraitist gives a head. Its strengths are close observation and a refusal to prettify. The price of such fidelity can be a certain dryness, and the picture is better understood as a document of the new truth to nature than as a finished composition."},"met:15096":{"d":"Louisa W. Dixon is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated 1833, on a plate only 6.0 by 4.7 centimeters, by the miniaturist William Lewis. She is recorded with her full name and middle initial, a useful survival, since many miniatures lose their sitters' names. At this size the likeness is close to a keepsake in the literal sense, something to be worn on a ribbon or pinned as a brooch, and it has to be looked at from a few inches away.","i":"By 1833 a miniature was a middle-class luxury, cheaper than an oil portrait and more personal than a print. A woman's likeness might be ordered by a fiancé, a husband or her parents, and exchanged at a wedding. The date places her in the decade of enormous sleeves and elaborately dressed hair, which fashionable sitters of the time often wore for their portraits. The picture's chief value now is that a name and a year are attached to a face.","c":"Lewis works at a scale where only the head can be fully resolved, and on a plate of 6.0 by 4.7 centimeters the success of the picture lies in the face. A named sitter with a secure date has more standing than the many unnamed ladies of the period, and it makes this miniature more useful to historians. As art, it is a competent miniature rather than a landmark, and its worth is in the precision of its craft."},"met:15097":{"d":"Lazarus M. Goldsmith is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated 1833, on a plate only 4.6 by 3.8 centimeters, by William Lewis. It is about the size of a matchbox, so small that three fingers could cover the whole picture. At that scale the face has to be drawn with the finest brush, and the plate would have been framed in a case or locket to be carried or worn.","i":"A miniature this small was a personal token, made to be kept in a pocket, a locket or a small case on a desk. The sitter's full name has been preserved, which is not true of many surviving ivories, and that makes this a more useful record than most. In the early 1830s such likenesses were exchanged at engagements and partings, and men carried a wife's or fiancée's portrait as readily as women carried a man's. The title now ties the likeness to a specific man.","c":"The tiny size is the main fact about the picture, and Lewis meets it with a compact, economical likeness. A plate this small leaves no room for background or accessories, so success rests on the drawing of the head and the transparency of the flesh tints. The result is a competent, unfussy piece. Anyone judging it should remember how hard it is to make a face read clearly at under five centimeters."},"met:15098":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, dated about 1835, on a plate 5.2 by 4.3 centimeters, by William Lewis. The sitter's name has been lost. The scale, smaller than a modern playing card, made it a pocket keepsake rather than a wall decoration, and the thin ivory, the fine brush and the careful finish are those used for named sitters of the period.","i":"Around 1835 the miniature had a firm place in middle-class life, and many families owned several. They were exchanged between lovers, ordered by parents when a son left home, and gathered into a single case as the generations passed. A gentleman with no recorded name has lost his place in that family chain, which is what makes anonymous miniatures poignant: the likeness remains while the identity that mattered most to its first owners has vanished.","c":"Without a name or recorded context, the picture can only be judged as craft: how convincingly a face has been made to live within five centimeters. By that standard it belongs with competent, professional work of its day, a trade object more than an artistic statement. It comes from a trade within a few years of being undercut by the daguerreotype, and its survival is a small reminder of what photography replaced."},"met:10984":{"d":"A small oil, about 36 by 51 centimeters, shows Hampstead Heath, the open heathland on a high ridge north of London that had long been a place for walks, views and landscape painting. James Hamilton, an American painter born in Ireland and best known for dramatic seascapes, made it in 1856. Hampstead Heath was a favorite subject of John Constable, who lived beside it, and a painter who chose it knew he was working on ground that English art had made famous.","i":"Hampstead had been a retreat for Londoners since the eighteenth century, and for painters it was an outdoor studio for studies of cloud and sky, above all in the work of Constable, who lived there for much of his later life. An American painter of the 1850s, better known for sea pieces, would have seen the Heath as a pilgrimage site of landscape art. The picture is in part a tribute to that tradition.","c":"Hamilton's reputation as the American Turner rests on his dramatic marines, and a modest landscape like this shows him in a quieter key. At 36 by 51 centimeters it has the scale of a studio piece, and its interest is in what an American romantic did with a subject that English painters had made their own. The risk is comparison: Constable's Hampstead pictures are hard to surpass, and a visitor's view can seem slight beside them."},"met:11683":{"d":"Mrs. Warren Rogers is portrayed in oil on a canvas 76.7 by 61.5 centimeters, about 1821, by John Paradise. The title gives her only as the wife of Warren Rogers, in the custom of the day, so that her identity comes through her husband. At about 77 by 61 centimeters the picture is a standard-sized portrait, probably of the sitter to the waist or thereabouts, made for display in a parlor or a family room.","i":"A portrait of a married woman commissioned in the early 1820s would present her as a respectable wife and mother, with dress and bearing that told of the family's standing. The custom of naming a woman as Mrs. plus her husband's full name shows how thoroughly a married woman's identity was folded into his. The likeness may well have been painted as a companion to one of Warren Rogers himself, though that is a guess.","c":"Paradise was not among the leading portraitists of his day and should not be measured against the elegance of Thomas Sully. The virtue of a portrait of this date and type is a believable, quiet presence, painted with an unfussy hand. It records a person of the period honestly if without brilliance, and its value is documentary as much as artistic."},"met:435618":{"d":"A tiny horizontal panel, only 13 by 26 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood. The subject is the Holy Trinity, apparently in the form known as the Throne of Mercy, in which God the Father presents the crucified Christ, with the dove of the Holy Spirit and attending angels. A panel of this size probably belonged to a predella, the row of small scenes beneath an altarpiece, and would have been seen at close range.","i":"The Trinity unites three persons in one God: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. In the Throne of Mercy the Father offers his Son's sacrifice to the viewer, and the dove between them completes the divine presence. Set in a predella close to the altar table, such an image may have reminded the faithful that the Mass renews Christ's sacrifice. Bartolomeo di Giovanni was a Florentine painter of the late fifteenth century who worked in the circle of Domenico Ghirlandaio.","c":"Bartolomeo's small panels have the neatness of the Ghirlandaio workshop, if not its authority. In a tiny format the miniaturist's care is an advantage, and the symmetrical subject is easy to read at a glance. The figures are repeated types and the emotion is formal, so the panel never reaches the force of a major altarpiece. It is a competent, charming workshop piece rather than an inspired one."},"met:11170":{"d":"A small upright landscape, about 36 by 31 centimeters, shows a mountain scene at morning, with trees in the view. Richard William Hubbard painted it in oil in 1856, when the Hudson River School was at the height of its popularity and painters sought out American mountains and the effects of early light. The title tells the story: the day is just beginning on the heights, and the painter's aim is to give that freshness a picture.","i":"Morning was a favorite subject for American landscape painters because it joined natural beauty with a hint of renewal, and the mountains were claimed as national scenery in a period when the country's wilderness was a source of pride. A picture titled Morning on the Mountain invites the viewer to share the sense of waking in a high place. Landscapes of this size, small enough for a parlor, were bought by city dwellers who valued calm views of the wild.","c":"The test of such a picture is its light: how cool and clear the early air feels, and how smoothly dark trees give way to the hazy distance. Hubbard follows the well-tested Hudson River formula of mountain, trees and early light rather than inventing a new one. A small upright panel can do this economically, but it also invites comparison with Church and Cole, whose mountains had far greater range."},"met:10907":{"d":"An unnamed woman is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, on a plate 9.8 by 7.3 centimeters, by George Freeman, about 1825. At almost ten centimeters tall this is a generous miniature, large for the type, and it was meant to be held and viewed close. The sitter's name has been lost, so the picture works chiefly as an example of a style and a craft, with the glow of the ivory showing through thin layers of color.","i":"In the 1820s miniature painters were working on larger plates and with stronger color than their predecessors, as taste moved toward fuller, more worldly likenesses. A woman of about 1825 who sat for a plate of this size was following that fashion, and the format could hold the head, the shoulders and a good deal of dress. Her name has been lost, but her portrait survives because the object was precious: a hand-painted, hand-held image.","c":"At nearly ten centimeters tall the plate invites a more ambitious, more worldly kind of picture than the tiny keepsakes of the previous generation, and the question is whether the painter filled the space with life. Freeman, a professional miniaturist, can be expected to supply polish; the absence of a name keeps the picture in the category of respectable craft rather than personal portrait."},"met:11473":{"d":"Henry Clay, the Kentucky statesman, is portrayed in watercolor on ivory on a plate only 3.0 by 2.4 centimeters, about the size of a large postage stamp, around 1840, by John Alexander McDougall. At this scale it is a keepsake or brooch-sized token, probably meant to be worn. Such tiny portraits of public men were often copied from engravings or existing portraits, and this one may have been.","i":"Henry Clay (1777–1852) was a senator from Kentucky, Speaker of the House and Secretary of State, and was called the Great Compromiser for his efforts to hold the Union together. As the leading Whig, he ran unsuccessfully for president three times. A likeness of about 1840 would have been treasured by admirers, and portraits of Clay, from engravings to ribbon badges, were everywhere in the political culture of the era.","c":"A statesman's face in three centimeters is a test of a miniaturist's concentration. The success of such a thing lies in making the familiar features and expression recognizable at a glance, and McDougall probably worked from a known image to do so. As a historical object it is more interesting than as an artwork: a small act of political devotion made with great craft. Nothing so small can say much more than that the man looked like this."},"met:15107":{"d":"A boy is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, on a plate 6.4 by 5.0 centimeters, by John Alexander McDougall. No date is known. McDougall's long life, 1810 or 1811 to 1894, spans the years from the miniature's height to its eclipse by photography, so the picture could belong to many decades. The sitter is unnamed, and the portrait is a quiet small likeness of a child, the kind of thing a parent or grandparent kept in a case.","i":"Children were frequent subjects for miniaturists because a likeness could mark a particular age and be kept as the child grew, or kept as a memorial if the child did not. With no name and no date, this picture belongs to the general class of family keepsakes: its first owners knew exactly who he was, and the information was never written where later viewers could find it. Such anonymous children are among the most common and most poignant survivals of the portrait miniature.","c":"A child's face in miniature wants softness and a light touch, and watercolor on ivory suits both. The same artist painted a stamp-sized Henry Clay, so scale held no terrors for McDougall, and the plate here is by comparison roomy. As an anonymous, undated likeness it is hard to place in his career, but it shows the kind of affectionate directness that small portraits of children invite."},"nga:34113":{"d":"John Peck is shown against a dark background, apparently from the chest up, in a plain oil portrait about 64 by 48 centimeters. He has fair skin, a straight nose, arched eyebrows and curly blond hair, and he wears a dark jacket and a white cravat. A hint of burgundy cloth appears at the right edge, and no jewelry or props distract from his face. John Johnston painted him around 1795, in a direct, unadorned manner that puts the sitter's face at the center.","i":"In the 1790s American sitters who wanted a likeness at modest cost turned to local painters like Johnston. The dark coat, white neckcloth and absence of any display suggest a sitter who wished to appear sober and respectable rather than grand, in keeping with the republican values of the new nation. The hint of rich cloth at the edge is the only touch of luxury.","c":"A portrait stripped to essentials, face, dark coat, white neckcloth and dark ground, depends entirely on the drawing of the head, and Johnston, a capable local painter rather than a brilliant one, meets that test honestly if without the fluency of Gilbert Stuart. The straightforward presentation has a certain dignity. What it lacks in refinement it makes up in plain candor, which many sitters of the new republic valued."},"met:16725":{"d":"A tiny portrait miniature, watercolour on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimetres, shows a man identified by the title as Jasper Ely Cropsey, painted about 1794. The museum record gives no description, so his dress, hair and pose cannot be reported with confidence. In the 1790s American miniatures were small, delicate and personal, often set in gold lockets or frames and worn or carried as tokens of affection; many were made without the sitter's name being recorded, but here the name has survived.","i":"In the 1790s miniatures served the same purposes that photographs would later: they marked betrothals, absences and mourning, and travelled with their owners. Portraits in watercolour on ivory, simple in line and light in colour, were produced by a handful of itinerant American painters such as Ebenezer Mack, who was active from 1785 to 1808. The sitter is named in the title; his career and the reason for this portrait are not recorded here.","c":"The pleasures of such a miniature are in the minute drawing and the glow of the ivory beneath the thin colour, and early American examples are charmingly plain beside their English or French counterparts. Without being able to judge this one's likeness or finish, the fair verdict is that it is a typical, attractive small work of the early republic, whose chief value is the preservation of a named face from the 1790s."},"met:437080":{"d":"A windmill stands on the hill of Montmartre, then an open, rural height at the edge of Paris, in a landscape about 74 by 102 centimeters. Georges Michel painted it in oil, probably around 1820. The broad, heavy handling and the emphasis on land and sky are typical of his work, which showed the windswept outskirts of Paris rather than polished views. The mill is a working landmark of the local economy, set in open terrain far from classical or Italian scenery.","i":"Montmartre had many windmills in Michel's day, grinding grain beside the quarries that supplied gypsum for plaster, and it was annexed to Paris only in 1860. Michel, who knew the Dutch pictures in the Louvre intimately and admired Ruisdael and Rembrandt, took their dark, brooding skies and applied them to the unglamorous plain around his own city. He was almost forgotten in his lifetime, but the Barbizon painters, who also looked to Dutch art, saw him as a forerunner.","c":"Michel's plain, dark landscapes were out of step with the polished neoclassical views of his day, which is both their weakness and their strength. The heavy handling can look murky and repetitive, since he returned again and again to the same bare hills. But the best of them, with their broad skies and a windmill as the vertical accent, anticipate Barbizon and even later landscape painting in their honesty about weather and ground."},"met:437079":{"d":"A plowed field and a village share this oil painting, about 51 by 70 centimeters, which Georges Michel probably painted after 1827, in his later years. The subject is the ordinary countryside near Paris, shown without heroes, ruins or classical figures. Michel preferred open, bare terrain beneath a large sky, and the plowed ground of the title is typical: a working landscape rather than a picturesque one.","i":"A plowed field was an unusual choice for a French landscape in the early nineteenth century, when academic taste prized classical or Italianate views. Michel, who studied the Dutch masters of the seventeenth century, brought their respect for ordinary land into French painting. Later artists, above all the Barbizon painters, would make fields and villages a central subject, and Michel is now remembered as a forerunner.","c":"The picture works, as Michel's best do, through breadth rather than detail: a few large masses of earth, village and sky, handled in a plain, heavy way. The danger of the method is monotony, and Michel can be dull when sky and ground fail to answer one another. His reputation rests less on any single canvas than on his place in a tradition that runs from the Dutch through him to Barbizon."},"met:437279":{"d":"A market, with a woman, a horse and a cart, is the subject of this tiny panel, only about 10 by 22 centimeters, by the Austrian painter August von Pettenkofen. The date is not recorded. The long, narrow format and small size make it a quick, intimate record of a moment in daily life: figures and animals grouped in a busy public space, painted in an informal, sketch-like manner that invites close looking.","i":"Pettenkofen is best known for small scenes of peasant markets, horses and carts in the Hungarian plain, especially around Szolnok on the Tisza, where he spent summers and where an artists' colony formed. His rapid, sketchy handling and fine color made humble subjects, a market stall, a cart, a woman at work, seem luminous. The subjects were not stories but fragments of life, a taste of mid-nineteenth-century realism in central Europe.","c":"Pettenkofen's tiny panels are admired for their economy: a few flicks of paint set a horse, a cart and a figure in the light, and the effect is of a moment seen, not arranged. The scale is almost too small for ambition, and the pleasure is in the touch. He has none of the grandeur of the Realists in Paris, but within his modest scope he is a keen observer. It is a delicate, truthful trifle."},"met:436838":{"d":"A richly dressed young man has his palm read by an old woman while the young women around him rob him. The old woman, her wrinkled face turned to his, takes his hand, and her companions press close, one cutting a medal from his chain and another picking his pocket, as calmly as if nothing were happening. The figures crowd the front of a canvas about 1 by 1.2 meters and are lit by an even, unshadowed light, their faces simplified into smooth, almost sculpted forms.","i":"Caravaggio's Fortune Teller of about 1595 made the subject famous, and it spread across Europe through his followers: a gullible youth, a Gypsy woman reading his hand, and a theft carried out under cover of the reading. Viewers enjoyed the comedy and took the moral, which warns against vanity and trusting strangers. The women are Gypsies in the language of the day, and the picture repeats a stereotype of Romani women as fortune-tellers and thieves that Romani people have long rejected.","c":"La Tour tells the story through glances, and the deadpan, almost puppet-like figures suit a comedy of deception. The clean contours and smooth, masklike faces that give his candlelit nocturnes their quiet can look cool in even daylight, but here they sharpen the satire. It lacks the spiritual depth of his best religious pictures, yet few seventeenth-century painters were as exact about the moment of being fooled."},"nga:54386":{"d":"Mary Magdalene, the repentant sinner of the Gospels, is shown in a quiet interior lit by a single candle, on a canvas about 1.1 by 0.9 meters. La Tour painted several Magdalens, and they share a plan: a lone woman, a small flame, and objects that speak of vanity and death, such as a skull or a mirror. The picture has no narrative incident. Its stillness is the subject, and the viewer is asked to join a silent meditation in which light and shadow carry as much meaning as the figure.","i":"Mary Magdalene was traditionally identified with the sinner who anointed Christ's feet, and she became the model of repentance. Counter-Reformation preachers promoted penitence, and pictures of the Magdalen meditating with a skull or a mirror urged viewers to turn from worldly vanity. In La Tour's Lorraine, which suffered war, plague and famine in the 1630s, meditations on mortality would have had a force that is hard to overstate. The candle is a symbol too: a fragile light that a breath can put out.","c":"La Tour's Magdalens are among the quietest pictures of the seventeenth century: he reduces the scene to a few simple, rounded shapes held in a warm pool of light. The poise and economy are admirable, and the lack of detail deepens the mood. The risk is a certain stiffness, since the forms can look carved rather than breathing. The Washington picture shares the serious calm that unites the series."},"cma:128842":{"d":"An old man sits in near darkness, looking up with his lips parted, his hands clasped in front of him. He has a bald head, a grey beard and a dark blue-green robe with a heavy cloak, and his eyes are red-rimmed. On a ledge behind him at the left, a cock stands on a plate beside a lantern whose glass sides glow softly. His sandalled feet reach into the glow at the bottom. The forms are plain and smooth, almost simplified into geometric blocks, and the palette is limited to deep greens, browns and warm creamy highlights. The painting is oil on canvas, made in 1645, and the figure fills most of the space.","i":"During Jesus's arrest on the night of the Last Supper, the apostle Peter denied knowing him. Jesus had predicted that Peter would do so before the cock crowed, which is why the bird is here. Cleveland notes that Christ forgave him, yet Peter was consumed by guilt, and La Tour shows him as an old man reflecting on his past in perpetual repentance, with red-rimmed eyes and an uncertain light that suggest sleepless nights. Peter's tears were a favourite subject of the Counter-Reformation. Unlike many artists inspired by Caravaggio, La Tour worked in relative isolation in northeastern France, and his connection to the Italian painter remains unclear.","c":"La Tour's restraint is what moves. There is no storm of grief, only an old man sitting with his own conscience, and the faint lantern light makes the scene feel intimate and private. The reduction of clothing and form to calm, rounded shapes gives the figure dignity. The picture's risk is dullness. The palette is dark and narrow, the rooster is almost comically calm, and a viewer may need time to find the emotion. Those who give it that time find a quiet and compassionate treatment of repentance."},"met:436839":{"d":"A young woman sits in profile at the left, her long hair loose over a white blouse with wide sleeves and a red skirt, the whole figure silent in near darkness. Her hands rest on a skull in her lap. Facing her on a table is a small mirror in an ornate gilt frame, and a single candle burns beside it, so that two flames appear, one real and one reflected, the only source of light. The warm glow touches her cheek, ear and shoulder and the pale cloth of her sleeve, and leaves her lower body in shadow. The mirror shows the candle but neither the woman nor the skull. On the floor at the lower right lie a few small, indistinct objects.","i":"Mary Magdalene, the follower of Christ whom Christian tradition made a repentant sinner who withdrew to a life of penance, was a favourite subject of the Counter-Reformation. The Met reads the mirror as a symbol of vanity, the skull of mortality and the candle, probably, of spiritual enlightenment. La Tour, who worked in Lorraine, painted several candlelit Magdalens, now in the Louvre, Los Angeles and Washington, as well as this one, sometimes called the Magdalen with Two Flames. The Met dates it about 1640. He was little known after his death and was rediscovered only in the twentieth century.","c":"La Tour is at his most economical here. A single light, an unbroken contour along the back, and the near-sculptural forms of the sleeve produce a calm that few Caravaggesque painters achieved. What the picture withholds is feeling: the face is half hidden and shows neither grief nor ecstasy, so the meaning is carried by the symbols. They are arranged with such care that the penitent can seem part of a still life. The best passages are small ones, the glow through the ear and the stillness of the hands, and they are enough to hold it."},"nga:12198":{"d":"A tall canvas shows a young woman standing full length in a white muslin dress, in front of a white curtain, on an animal-skin rug usually described as a wolf. Her red-auburn hair falls loose over her shoulders, and she looks out with a calm, slightly distant gaze, holding a single white lily in her lowered hand. Scattered flowers lie on the fur, and the animal's head, jaws open and glass eyes wide, stares up at the viewer from a blue patterned carpet. Whites, creams, and grays are laid on in thin, scrubbed layers, with strong color only in the hair, the flowers, and the carpet. The signature and the date 1862 are painted at the upper right.","i":"The model is Joanna Hiffernan, Whistler's companion. He began the picture in late 1861 for the Royal Academy, which rejected it. In London it was shown as The Woman in White, a title that tied it to Wilkie Collins's novel; Whistler objected, saying it was simply a girl dressed in white standing in front of a white curtain. In 1863 it drew crowds at the Salon des Refusés in Paris, beside Manet's Luncheon on the Grass. He later reworked it and came to call it Symphony in White, No. 1, to put the harmony of color above any story. Others have read it as a bride's lost innocence, or as the Virgin.","c":"It is easy to see why the picture caused argument: it was a portrait that refused to be one, a story that refused to tell. The painting succeeds when the white is left to vibrate in its own gradations, and less when the lily, the rug, and the face begin to mean something. The animal skin is a clumsy note, an obviously symbolic prop in a picture otherwise about tone. Still, the subdued, silvery handling is subtle, and the idea of making a harmony of color the subject points straight to Whistler's later Nocturnes."},"nga:61254":{"d":"Looking slightly down, the viewer sees a woman and two men at a table on a balcony above the Thames, with a crowded river behind them. The red-haired woman, in a dark green dress, leans back with her arm along the railing and gazes out of the picture, while the bearded man beside her turns toward a clean-shaven man in a cap who leans in from the right. Sailboats, rowboats and a steamship fill the tan river under a hazy pale blue sky, and only a corner of the table, with its dark red floral cloth, enters at the lower right.","i":"Whistler began Wapping in 1860 and worked on it until 1864, while he was etching the docklands of the Thames. Joanna Hiffernan, his companion and favorite model, is generally identified as the woman. The setting, a rough working waterfront, and the unexplained relationship among the three figures were unusual in British art, and they owe something to Courbet's realism, which Whistler had absorbed in Paris. The picture asks what the woman is doing there, and gives no clear answer.","c":"Wapping is a restless picture. The river, with its tangle of masts, is brilliantly observed, and the cropped corner of the table is a daring device that recalls photographs and Japanese prints. The figures are harder going, more stiffly placed, and the narrative never resolves. As a statement of Whistler's early ambitions it is gripping; as a complete picture it is uneven. The best of it is the river, which anticipates his later Thames pictures."},"nga:1238":{"d":"A gray river scene, with boats and buildings in the distance, in which a shadowy horizon divides gray sky from gray water. Closer in, boats lie at anchor with their masts tied up, their sails and rigging in earthy colors, and faint outlines of buildings mark the far shore. At the bottom, shadowy shapes that may be people seem to walk across a path or bridge. The paint is thin, soft and translucent, giving the whole upright canvas, about 61 by 46 centimeters, a blurred, misty quality.","i":"Whistler moved to Chelsea in the 1860s and made the Thames his subject, finding in its mist, mud and masts the quiet tones he later called arrangements and nocturnes. The title, Grey and Silver, announces the idea: a painting is an arrangement of color first and a view second. This was a radical position at a time when critics demanded stories and finish, and it explains why his river pictures looked unfinished to many contemporaries.","c":"This is early Whistler at his most Whistlerian: a nearly monochrome picture whose subject is atmosphere, with the few tied masts and the thin shapes of buildings supplying just enough structure. The restraint is its achievement, since a painter less sure of tone would have gone muddy. It is also slight, and some viewers will find it more an experiment than a finished picture. Among his river paintings it is a clear step toward the nocturnes of the 1870s."},"nga:82416":{"d":"Looking slightly down across a wide, boat-filled river under a cloudy sky, the viewer sees a far bank lined with low buildings in fawn, brown, red and cream. At the right, four long wooden boats lie side by side, their amber-brown sails tied to the masts and their prows cut off by the edge of the canvas. In the lower right corner, ghostly figures in long gowns and jackets stand on a walkway, and a slim boat with a narrow red sail has drifted into mid-river. Violet, slate and aquamarine wash the sky, and the water is smeared with reflections.","i":"Battersea Reach is the stretch of the Thames at Battersea, on the south bank in west London, across the river from Chelsea, where Whistler lived from about 1863. He treated the river as a subject of atmosphere and flat pattern rather than anecdote, and the cropped boats and strong diagonals owe something to Japanese woodblock prints, which he was beginning to collect. The loose handling and nearly transparent figures prefigure the thin, dreamy paint of his later nocturnes and set him apart from the detailed realists of Victorian London.","c":"A loosely handled river picture that is stronger as atmosphere than as drawing. The reflections and the sky are managed with a freedom unusual in 1863, and the ghostly figures are suggestions rather than people. The boats at the right are the firm anchor. It is one of the first pictures to show Whistler's mature gift for reducing a busy scene to a few bands of color and a handful of precise notes."},"met:13237":{"d":"A girl stands alone on a tall canvas about 2.2 by 1.1 meters, the full-length format usually reserved for grown sitters of rank. The sitter is Connie Gilchrist, a young London dancer known for her skipping-rope act, and the title announces what the painting is really about: a harmony of yellow and gold. Whistler painted it around 1876–77, treating the girl, her dress and the paint itself as notes in a single color scheme more than as a story about a child performer.","i":"Connie Gilchrist began as a child dancer in London's music halls and pantomimes, became well known for dancing with a skipping rope, and later married the Earl of Orkney. Whistler's choice of a young entertainer instead of an aristocrat is characteristic of his interest in modern London life. The musical title belongs to his campaign from the 1860s to make painting about harmony of color and form, a view that scandalized critics, notably John Ruskin in 1877.","c":"The picture is as much a portrait of a color as of a person: the tall, narrow format and the spare arrangement turn the girl into a vertical note of yellow. That is exciting when it works, and the sitter's natural, performing poise helps. The weakness of Whistler's harmonies of the 1870s is that likeness and character can recede, so the viewer remembers the scheme more than the child. It is a worthy sibling of his better-known adult portraits."},"met:13269":{"d":"A night picture in oil, about 64 by 77 centimeters, whose title announces its scheme: green and gold. Whistler gave such pictures musical names, harmony, arrangement and nocturne, to tell viewers to look at color and tone rather than at a story. Dated by 1877, it belongs to the series of nocturnes he began in the early 1870s. The title does not describe the scene, and Whistler meant it not to: the painting asks to be read as atmosphere.","i":"Whistler's patron Frederick Leyland suggested the word nocturne, borrowed from music, for his night scenes around 1871, and Whistler adopted it gladly. The term freed painting from the duty to describe: a night picture could be a few tones in close harmony, with forms barely visible. This approach angered critics, most famously John Ruskin in 1877, whose attack on another nocturne led to Whistler's libel suit in 1878. The title thus stands for a whole argument about what art is for.","c":"With so little description, the picture lives or dies by tone, and Whistler's best nocturnes make a few nearly matched darks feel like air. A viewer should look for the softness of the transitions and the delicacy of the lights. The risk is vagueness: a nocturne can slip from suggestion into emptiness. At their strongest these pictures are among the most original of the 1870s, and the series set a course for later tonal and abstract painting."},"met:13223":{"d":"A wide canvas, almost twice as broad as it is tall, about 69 by 135 centimeters, shows men and women in the Cremorne Gardens, a pleasure ground on the Thames at Chelsea. Whistler painted several Cremorne scenes in the 1870s, and the number in the title marks this as the second picture to bear the name. The subject is modern leisure: a public park with its crowd of strollers rather than a portrait of any one person.","i":"Cremorne Gardens, open from the 1840s until 1877, drew Londoners of every class for dancing, concerts, fireworks and, according to its critics, a good deal of drinking and flirtation. Whistler, who lived close by in Chelsea, painted it more than once, and The Falling Rocket, his famous nocturne of fireworks, is generally thought to show it. Painters of modern life, from Paris to London, found such pleasure grounds irresistible, and the Cremorne pictures belong with that wider interest.","c":"Whistler's best-known Cremorne pictures are nocturnes of dissolving light, and a wide figure scene like this one asks to be judged differently, as an arrangement of tone and space on a frieze-like format. The proportion, close to that of a Japanese screen, suits a crowd drifting through a garden. Without the drama of the fireworks pictures it can seem slighter, but it shows Whistler making a pleasure ground into something more like a dream than a documentary."},"met:13008":{"d":"A man in the costume of Philip II of Spain, in a small portrait about 49 by 36 centimeters, shows the English actor Henry Irving in a famous role. The Met lists the picture as formerly attributed to James McNeill Whistler, so it is no longer ascribed to him, and the date is given only as after 1872. It shares its subject with Whistler's full-length portrait of Irving as the king, but it is far smaller, at less than a tenth of the area.","i":"Henry Irving (1838–1905), the leading actor-manager of the Victorian stage and the first actor to be knighted, played Philip II in Tennyson's Queen Mary at the Lyceum Theatre in 1876. Philip II (1527–1598) was king of Spain and, by his marriage to Mary Tudor, briefly king of England as well. Whistler painted Irving in the role that year. Whatever its authorship, a small picture on this subject shows how quickly an image of a star actor could spread.","c":"Beside Whistler's own full-length, a small sketch of this kind has a different job: it tells the idea without the monumental presence. Its removal from the Whistler list is a reminder of how much his authority depends on subtle tonal control, which imitators can approximate but rarely hold. As a record of how Irving's Philip circulated it has interest; as a painting it should be taken without the Whistler name and judged more modestly."},"met:13211":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, nearly 1.9 meters high, shows a man standing in black evening dress against a dark ground, with a pink cloak draped over his arm. The sitter is Théodore Duret, the French critic and collector, and the title names the scheme, flesh color and black. The small area of pink glows against the black, and the face supplies the other warm note. Whistler painted it in 1883, in the full-length format of his best-known portraits.","i":"Théodore Duret (1838–1927) was one of the first critics to defend the Impressionists in print, a friend of Whistler, Manet and Monet, and a collector. Whistler's full-length portraits of the early 1880s, with their titles of arrangements in black, owed much to Velázquez, whose dark grounds and standing figures he admired. The pink domino, a masquerade cloak, adds a note of theater to sober evening dress and suggests a man on his way to an evening out in Paris.","c":"The pairing of pink and black is the daring stroke, and the tall format, the nearly empty ground and the sitter's easy stance recall Velázquez. The painting succeeds as an arrangement: the black figure holds the canvas, and the pink brings it alive. What it gives up is intimacy, since the scheme matters more than the sitter's character. Whistler's full-lengths can feel more like designs than likenesses, and this one is no exception, but the design is superb."},"met:13209":{"d":"A full-length portrait, more than two meters tall, shows the actor Henry Irving in the black costume of Philip II of Spain against a dark ground. The title, Arrangement in Black, No. 3, is the key: Whistler treats the black dress and shadowy setting as a study in tone, with the face and hands as the bright points. He began the picture in 1876, when Irving was playing the king in Tennyson's Queen Mary, and reworked it in 1885.","i":"Irving, already celebrated, played the cold, watchful king in Tennyson's play, and the portrait shows him as Philip II rather than as himself, a mixture of portrait and stage record. The theater, like Whistler's art, was a world of artifice and arrangement. The title belongs to a series of arrangements in black, full-length figures in dark dress, in which Whistler echoed the Spanish court portraits of Velázquez, an artist he revered, and in which the subject mattered less than the harmony.","c":"The long gestation shows in the result: the black is rich and varied rather than flat, and the figure has the commanding stillness of Velázquez's kings, though the tone is more dreamlike than severe. The weakness is a certain stage-play stiffness in the pose and an emptiness of expression, since Whistler cared more for the silhouette than for the man. Irving, famous for intensity, is given a quiet that suits the king more than the actor."},"met:13210":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 23 by 30 centimeters, shows a girl absorbed in her book. The title, Arrangement in Black, indicates that Whistler built the picture from dark tones, with the reader as its focus. Painted on wood, which he often used for small, quick pictures in the 1880s and 1890s, it is the kind of panel that could be finished in a sitting or two, and it has the intimacy of a private study rather than a public portrait.","i":"Reading was a favorite subject of nineteenth-century painters from Manet to Renoir, and for Whistler a girl reading offered a still pose and an inward mood that suited his idea of painting as a harmony of tone. The title Arrangement in Black carries the same message as his larger portraits: the picture is a composition in tone and shape, not an anecdote or a character study. Small panels let him try such arrangements quickly and cheaply.","c":"Panels like this show a lighter, more private Whistler than the big portraits, and the strengths are economy and sympathy: with a handful of dark notes and a bright face, a quiet mood is set down. The limits are those of the small panel, which can feel slight. Without the force of the full-length portraits, it is a minor companion to them, best valued for its immediacy."},"met:13225":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 29 by 18 centimeters, shows Edward Guthrie Kennedy, a New York print dealer who handled Whistler's etchings and lithographs. Whistler painted it on a small wooden panel in 1893–95. The upright proportions suggest a standing figure, a portrait reduced to the size of a book, which suits both the sitter, a dealer used to handling small works, and the painter's habit of working fast on small supports.","i":"Dealers were crucial to Whistler's career: Kennedy's firm exhibited and sold his etchings and lithographs in America and helped build his market among collectors. A portrait of a dealer is also a record of the business behind an artist's reputation. Small panels like this, informal in scale and handling, were the kind of picture a painter could give or sell to a friend and supporter, and they show Whistler among his allies rather than in the formal public mode of his big portraits.","c":"Small panels like this show Whistler's gift for suggestion: a shape, a few tones and a pose that tells of a person. The risk of such a format is that the likeness stays slight; with only a few centimeters to work in, the face is a touch rather than a study. As an affectionate document of a key supporter the panel is worth more than its size suggests, though it is a minor work beside the large portraits."},"nga:35081":{"d":"A young woman's head and shoulders fill a tall canvas, about 52 by 38 centimeters, against a tomato-red ground. Her body faces the viewer and her large dark eyes look out under nearly flat brows, with coral-red lips closed and a pale, gold-tinted face. Chestnut hair falls loosely down her back, tucked behind one ear. The brushwork is sinuous and visible, so her clothing blurs into a beige or brown mass, and three dark brown spots clustered against the background probably form Whistler's butterfly signature.","i":"Alice Butt sat to Whistler in the mid-1890s, when he was painting thinly and fluidly, so that a portrait is built from a few strokes rather than from careful modeling. The strong red ground recalls the flat, powerful backgrounds of Japanese prints, and the loose handling shows the late Whistler's interest in presence over description. The butterfly he used as a signature appears here as a part of the design instead of a mark in the corner.","c":"A deliberately reduced picture: the red ground, the pale face and the dark eyes do almost everything. The sketchy paint makes the sitter feel present rather than described, and the red is bold for a painter who preferred grays and browns. The cost is that her dress dissolves into vague masses, so it is more a presence than a portrait in the usual sense. Among Whistler's late heads it is one of the more direct."},"nga:30217":{"d":"A man stands upright, turned slightly to the right, on a tiny wooden panel only 22 by 13 centimeters. His face is indistinct, with a mustache and medium-length dark hair, and he wears a dark overcoat, dark trousers and a white collar, with his hands clasped in front. Behind him a textured beige wall meets a dark floor, and a reddish-brown object is partly visible at the right. The loose handling reduces the figure to a few simple shapes.","i":"Alexander Arnold Hannay is named only by the title, and he was probably one of the acquaintances Whistler painted in small, informal portraits in the 1890s. The pose, with hands clasped and a plain wall behind, reflects the artist's habit of treating a sitter as a figure in a space rather than as a face. Panels this small were quick to paint, and they let Whistler make a likeness out of a few tones and a pose rather than a detailed study.","c":"The panel is a study in how little is needed: the dark coat, the pale collar, the beige wall and a few brush marks establish a person in a room. The face, deliberately indistinct, makes the portrait more an impression than a likeness, which is both the point and the limit of the manner. It is a small, accomplished sketch of the late Whistler, light in touch and modest in aim."},"nga:45879":{"d":"Whistler shows himself from the waist up, standing almost in profile with his face turned to the viewer, one hand on his hip and the other held up, palm outward, near his waist. He looks out with dark eyes and one eyebrow lifted, a wide mustache and a small tuft of hair below the lip, his tousled brown hair merging into the sable background. A white collar shows against a dark vest, and the coffee-brown jacket is pulled back behind his hand. Three darker brown spots at the right edge form a butterfly.","i":"The title follows the pattern of Whistler's color titles, but the color is nearly all brown, in dark, closely related tones that hide many details. The self-portrait dates from his sixties, after the death of his wife in 1896, and shows him as the public Whistler: dandy, wit and artist, with arched eyebrow and confident stance. The butterfly he used as a signature is built into the background, as though the painter himself were part of the picture's design.","c":"A self-portrait that makes a virtue of darkness: the face emerges from a brown ground with the tonal subtlety Whistler learned from Velázquez, while the raised eyebrow and hand on hip supply the swagger. The paint is thin and the details are hazy, and some viewers will feel that the persona overwhelms the person. Still, the picture is among his most revealing, a performance by a man who understood publicity."},"cma:121261":{"d":"A violin player is the subject of this tall canvas, about 79 by 53 centimeters. The title names the figure by occupation rather than by name, an unusual choice for Whistler, who usually called his pictures arrangements, harmonies or portraits of named sitters. Painted around 1890–94, it belongs to the years when his figure pictures were thinly painted and low in tone, concentrating on a single musician rather than on setting or story.","i":"Music was the vocabulary of Whistler's art: he called his pictures symphonies, harmonies, nocturnes and arrangements to claim that painting, like music, could be about pure relations of tone and color. A picture of an actual musician therefore speaks to his central idea in a literal way. Violinists were also celebrities of the age, and Whistler had painted the Spanish virtuoso Pablo de Sarasate in 1884, so a violin player was a subject he knew well.","c":"Judged by Whistler's own principle, a few tones in harmony and a figure placed with tact, a musician is a sympathetic subject, since the pose itself suggests stillness and concentration. The late manner can be slight, and a viewer should expect suggestion rather than description. In company with the Sarasate portrait it shows how readily Whistler's ideas about music and painting could meet in a single image."},"nga:12197":{"d":"A woman stands with her back partly turned, her face in profile as she looks to the right, on a tall canvas nearly two meters high. She wears a silver and black gown: a black bodice with elbow-length silver ruffled sleeves under tiers of gauzy black, a broad pearl-white bow at her lower back, and a long silver skirt falling to the floor in a short train. Her features are soft and blended, her black hair is pulled into a bun, and behind her a smoky mauve wall meets a charcoal baseboard, with a butterfly signature in an oval.","i":"The sitter is identified as Ethel Whibley, Whistler's sister-in-law, the sister of his wife and the wife of the writer Charles Whibley. Whistler worked on the picture off and on, and the museum dates it from about 1888, the start being uncertain, to 1900. The title Andalusian is probably a nod to Spanish dress and to Velázquez, and Mother of Pearl and Silver names the pale, shimmering scheme in which Whistler dissolves the gown into light.","c":"The Andalusian is one of Whistler's most elegant late full-lengths, held together by the tension between the dark, nearly featureless wall and the shimmering silver gown. The brushwork is blended and soft, so the figure floats, and the profile is dignified, though the sitter's character is hard to read. The price of such refinement is distance: the picture is a vision more than a likeness. It ranks with his best late portraits, though few viewers will find it warm."},"met:437153":{"d":"On a rocky mountain ledge, a nude young man stands with a green mantle slipping from his shoulders and a long red-shafted spear planted beside him. A winged sphinx, a lion's body with a woman's head and torso, has leapt onto his chest and clings to him with her claws, her blue-gray wings spread behind her and her face lifted toward his. They look into each other's eyes. At the lower right lie the remains of earlier victims, a bare foot among the stones, and a carved urn on a column stands beside the ledge. Cliffs rise behind in a sky of pale green and gray. The tall, narrow canvas is finely detailed in the manner of an old master, and cool in color.","i":"The subject is the meeting of Oedipus and the Sphinx outside Thebes: the monster kills travelers who cannot answer her riddle, and Oedipus, who answers correctly, will free the city and become its king. Moreau showed it at the Salon of 1864, where it made his name; critics praised its learning, though some found Mantegna's influence too strong. It follows Ingres's version of 1808 but shifts the balance: Moreau's sphinx attacks, and Oedipus's victory is not yet certain. Many viewers have read her as a femme fatale, an early Symbolist type. Prince Napoléon bought it for 8,000 francs, and William H. Herriman bequeathed it to the Met in 1920.","c":"Moreau made a reputation with a picture that is more intellectual than sensual: every detail, the corpses, the urn, the gem-like surface, is arranged to carry a symbolic meaning. The figures are stiff and the composition almost static, a pose held rather than a struggle, and the archaic finish can feel airless. What works is the central idea of two faces locked in an intense, ambiguous look, which turns an old story into a drama of attraction and danger. It is a clear forerunner of Symbolism, even if the later Moreau was freer and stranger."},"met:782308":{"d":"A narrow panel, only 22 centimeters high, shows Salome with the head of John the Baptist, the grim climax of the story told in the Gospels. Gustave Moreau painted it in oil on wood around 1876, the year he exhibited his Salome pictures at the Paris Salon. At such a small size the panel is probably a study or a private work rather than a public showpiece, and it concentrates the drama on the dancer and her gruesome trophy.","i":"The Gospels of Matthew and Mark tell how Herod's stepdaughter danced at his birthday feast and, prompted by her mother Herodias, asked for the head of John the Baptist on a platter; they do not name her, and the name Salome comes from the historian Josephus. For Moreau and the Symbolists she became an emblem of dangerous beauty and cruelty. Huysmans praised Moreau's Salome pictures at length in his novel À rebours (1884), and Oscar Wilde's play helped make her famous.","c":"Moreau's Salome pictures are the clearest expression of his style: glowing, minutely worked and full of exotic ornament. On a panel this small the effect depends on glow and finish. The preciousness of the manner can become airless, and some critics find the blend of art-historical quotation and decadent perfume too sweet. Its strength is that it makes a violent story look like a dream, giving horror a strange stillness."},"met:10436":{"d":"George Washington is the subject of this preparatory painting for an engraving, in oil on paper mounted on paper and unstretched canvas, about 42 by 32 centimeters, made about 1860. As a design meant for a printmaker, it is a working object rather than a gallery picture: the image would be transferred and reproduced for wide circulation. Designs of this kind are usually kept simple in tone and clear in outline, since the engraver had to translate the image into lines.","i":"Alonzo Chappel made his living by supplying publishers with historical portraits and scenes that engravers turned into prints for books and for the home. Born in 1828, he never saw Washington, so his likeness necessarily drew on earlier portraits. In 1860, on the eve of the Civil War, Washington's image was a symbol of national unity, and demand for his likeness was strong.","c":"As a design for an engraving, this has the efficiency of its purpose: clear outlines, tonal contrast and a legible likeness that survives translation into line. It is not a personal vision, and the interest is historical, since it shows how the nineteenth-century public saw Washington, through prints. The oil design may have a freedom that the finished engraving lost, which is why such studies now appeal."},"nga:56148":{"d":"Three figures sit side by side outdoors: Saint Anne at left, the Christ Child at center, the Virgin at right. The nude child, with curly reddish hair and a red bead necklace, sits on a red cushion and turns his head to the right, while Anne, in a white head covering, green dress and red floral cloak, holds him. The Virgin, with long reddish hair and a white robe edged in gold, holds an open red book, and the child turns a page. Above, a white dove with gold rays and a crowned, bearded figure in red holding an orb break through the clouds.","i":"Saint Anne, the mother of the Virgin, was widely venerated around 1500, especially in the Netherlands and Germany, and an image of Anne, Mary and Jesus together, known in German as Anna Selbdritt, was a popular devotional subject. Above them the dove and the crowned figure, God the Father, join the group to form a Trinity of Father, Holy Spirit and Son. The book the Child turns probably stands for the Scriptures that foretell his destiny.","c":"The Master of Frankfurt, an anonymous Netherlandish painter known by a name of convenience, is a skilled colorist and a careful observer of textiles and faces. Here the reds and greens, the child's lively turning of the page and the gentle landscape give a human warmth that offsets the doctrinal scheme above. The grouping is somewhat static, three seated figures lined up like a relief, but the carefully described veils, cushion and necklace reward close looking."},"met:11347":{"d":"A man identified as Charles Henry Augustus Carter is portrayed in oil on a canvas about 92 by 76 centimeters, around 1845, by Nicholas Biddle Kittell. At this size the picture is a standard portrait, probably of the sitter to the waist or thereabouts, of the kind a man hung in his parlor or office. The long, three-part name was the nineteenth-century style of record, and it identifies a particular man.","i":"Portraits of men around 1845 were commissioned to record success and respectability: a businessman, a lawyer or a young professional would appear in dark coat and white linen with a serious expression. The full name suggests a person of some standing, remembered by his family. Portraiture was the staple income of most painters of the time, and a canvas of this size, a good deal larger than a miniature, was a more considerable and costly statement.","c":"Painted in the 1840s, when photography was starting to compete, a canvas of this size was a statement of means, and it asked for dignity more than flair. Kittell was a competent portraitist in the mainstream of his day rather than a painter of striking invention; the pleasures here are those of solid craft and a straightforward likeness, and the value is in the record of a particular man."},"nga:12133":{"d":"A kneeling angel at left and the kneeling Virgin at right face each other across a column-like partition, on a wide panel about 1 by 1.6 meters with curved upper corners. Gabriel, in a lavender robe tied with a gold cord and with gold and blue peacock-feather wings, holds a tall white lily; a dove hovers above him, with gold rays reaching toward Mary. She bows her head in a deep blue robe over a coral-red dress, holding a small teal book to her chest. Behind each figure is a slightly different room.","i":"The Annunciation (Luke 1:26–38) is the moment when the angel Gabriel tells Mary she will bear the Son of God. The lily is usually read as the sign of her purity, the dove as the Holy Spirit, and the book as the Scripture she was reading when interrupted. The glass vase on her shelf and the rays that pass behind the partition as through a window recall a familiar comparison: as light passes through glass without breaking it, so Christ was conceived without harm to Mary's virginity.","c":"Lippi's invention, setting the two figures in separate but linked rooms, makes the encounter feel both intimate and ceremonial, and the peacock-feathered wings are a delight. The wide panel gives the figures room to breathe, and the soft, clear color is typical of his best early work. The weaker side is the perspective, which does not quite resolve into one convincing space. The picture shows Lippi balancing Masaccio's weight with Fra Angelico's gentleness."},"nga:433":{"d":"The Virgin and her infant son are shown in tempera on a poplar panel, about 79 by 51 centimeters, painted around 1440 by the Florentine friar-painter Fra Filippo Lippi. Devotional pictures of this size were made for private rooms, chapels and convents, where they were looked at close to and prayed before. Lippi's Madonnas of this period have rounded, sculptural forms learned from Masaccio and Donatello, and a gentleness that is all his own.","i":"Lippi entered Florence's Carmelite convent as a boy, beside Masaccio's frescoes in the Brancacci Chapel, and the solid, weighted figures of that school mark his work. A Madonna and Child was the most common devotional picture in Renaissance Italy, a focus for prayer in a house or convent, with the mother's tenderness standing for divine love. The Virgin's gentle humanity, rather than a stiff, remote ideal, is what Lippi added to the Florentine tradition: she looks like a young woman a viewer might know.","c":"Lippi's Madonnas are among the most appealing of the early Renaissance, balancing Donatello's solidity with a tenderness that makes them human. Tempera, with its smooth modeling and crisp outlines, leaves little room for loose gesture, and the devotional formula of Virgin, Child and gently inclined heads recurs across his career. But within the formula, the delicate drawing of faces and hands gives each panel its life, and this panel of about 1440 shows the manner fully formed."},"met:436895":{"d":"The Virgin sits on a throne holding the Christ Child, with two angels beside her and a book among the details, in a tall panel of about 123 by 63 centimeters painted in tempera and gold around 1440. The narrow format suggests that the picture was originally part of a larger altarpiece, and it has since been transferred from its original wood. Fra Filippo Lippi's Madonnas are known for gentle, rounded faces and a tender, human feeling.","i":"Fra Filippo Lippi was a Carmelite friar in Florence who learned from Masaccio's solid, sculptural figures and from Donatello, and who made the Virgin look like a real Florentine woman. The throne and the angels set her in glory as Queen of Heaven, while the book may suggest her wisdom. Altarpieces of this type, with the Virgin enthroned, were the standard image for Florentine churches and chapels.","c":"Lippi's gift is to make holy figures human: the Virgin's face is tender and her pose natural, with an easy gentleness that earlier painters rarely achieved. The tall format forces a stacked composition, and the transfer from panel may have worn the surface. It is a typical and appealing product of his middle period, if not his most inventive."},"met:436896":{"d":"A young woman fills the foreground in strict profile, facing left, her pale face and long neck set against a dim room. She wears a red headdress with a tall veil falling behind, a pearl necklace and a rose-red gown with great black sleeves patterned in gold, and her hands are folded in front of her. Behind her at the left, a man in a red cap leans at an open window, seen in profile facing right, his hands resting on a shield striped in black and white. Through the window beyond her head are a winding road, a house and a small landscape in blue and green. It is a small panel in egg tempera, about 64 by 42 centimetres, with minutely painted details.","i":"Fra Filippo Lippi, a Carmelite friar who painted for the Medici, made what the Met calls the earliest surviving double portrait in Italian painting, the first to set its sitters in a domestic interior, and the first with a view onto a landscape. The man holds the Scolari family coat of arms, and the pair are usually identified as Lorenzo di Ranieri Scolari and Angiola di Bernardo Sapiti, who married around 1436. The woman's embroidered sleeve bears the word “lealta,” loyalty, so the picture probably marks their betrothal or marriage. Her fashionable dress and jewels display her family's wealth; the profile was the usual format for Florentine portraits of women.","c":"The man is a ghostly afterthought, but the woman is a triumph of line. Her profile is as clean as a cut cameo, and the stiffly patterned sleeve and veil turn her into a jewelled emblem of her family's standing. Lippi's real novelty is the room: for the first time in an Italian portrait, a window opens onto the world outside and light enters. It is not yet a living space, since she does not return his gaze and neither looks out at the viewer, but it points the way to the sympathetic realism of later Renaissance portraits."},"nga:422":{"d":"A small, wide panel, about 23 by 55 centimeters, shows the Nativity. Mary, in a rose-pink dress and a sky-blue robe lined with green, kneels with crossed hands before the swaddled infant, who rests against rolls of hay under a stone shelter while an ox and an ass look in from a trough. At the left Joseph sits dozing with his head on his hand, a staff and sack against his shoulder, before green rocky hills with a path leading to distant towns. Pale pink bands at top and bottom make the viewer seem to look through a frame.","i":"The kneeling Mary adoring her newborn child follows the visions of Saint Bridget of Sweden, whose revelations of the 1370s influenced fifteenth-century Nativities and replaced the older image of Mary reclining after the birth. Joseph's dozing, familiar in Italian art, may suggest his role as a bystander to a miracle that is not his own. The ox and ass come from Isaiah's prophecy and later tradition. A low, wide panel like this is typical of a predella, the strip beneath an altarpiece, though other uses are possible.","c":"Small as it is, the panel has a gentle clarity: the pink architecture, the sky-blue robe and the green hills are set out in fresh, easily read colors, and the napping Joseph adds a touch of humanity typical of Lippi. What it lacks is grandeur, since the format limits the figures to a quiet, intimate scale. As an example of Lippi's narrative gift it is more inventive than his large Madonnas, though necessarily slight."},"met:436897":{"d":"A young deacon sits enthroned at the centre of a gold-ground altarpiece, framed by a blue niche with a ribbed arch. Saint Lawrence is beardless and calm, with a gold halo, a grey-blue dalmatic carrying a gold panel on the chest, and a green mantle spread over his knees; he holds a red book, and his feet rest on the gridiron of his martyrdom. Two saints in pink cloaks, blue tunics and red caps stand at either side, each holding a palm. Three donors kneel in the foreground: two boys in red on the left and an older, close-cropped man in pink on the right, all with hands joined. The robes are edged with bands of pseudo-Arabic ornament, and the gold is worn along the arched top.","i":"Lawrence was a Roman deacon who, by tradition, was put to death on a gridiron in 258. The flanking saints are Cosmas and Damian, physician martyrs who were patrons of the Medici of Florence. The Met identifies the kneeling man as the merchant Alessandro Alessandri (1391–1460), shown with his two sons, and says the altarpiece was made for the family's church at Vincigliata, outside the city. The Medici saints probably signal the donor's ties to that family. Some scholars connect the work with the marriage of Ginevra degli Alessandri to Giovanni di Cosimo de' Medici in 1453 and so date it later than the Met's 1440s. Lippi, a Carmelite friar, was by then a favoured painter in Medici circles.","c":"Lippi's reputation rests on tender Madonnas, and this is a more formal, public picture in which he still keeps a human touch. Lawrence's smooth, faintly melancholy face, the careful pleating of the robes and the jewelled colour of pink, vermilion and green against gold are the real pleasures. The structure is less inventive: the flanking saints are near mirror images, the donors are tucked in at the foot, and the strict symmetry leaves the whole a little static. It is a polished, conventional devotional panel, best enjoyed for its surfaces rather than its drama."},"nga:41620":{"d":"A horizontal tempera panel, about 40 by 70 centimeters, illustrates an episode from the life of Saint Benedict, founder of Western monasticism. The title names three figures: Benedict, who gives the order; Maurus, his disciple, who is sent; and the young Placidus, who needs rescuing. Like Lippi's other small narrative panels of the 1440s, it is probably one scene from a larger set, and it would have been seen close up, as part of an altarpiece or decorative ensemble.","i":"The story comes from the Dialogues of Pope Gregory the Great: the boy Placidus fell into a lake while fetching water, and Benedict, in his cell, knew what had happened and sent Maurus to the rescue. Maurus ran across the water without noticing that he was walking on it and pulled the boy out by the hair. The miracle shows the abbot's spiritual insight and the obedience of his disciples, the virtue at the heart of the Benedictine rule.","c":"Lippi had a storyteller's gift for sequences of action, and a miracle in three stages, command, obedience and rescue, suits it. The small panel format rewards clear, simple staging. Such narrative panels are rarely as moving as his Madonnas, however, and the interest here lies in the telling rather than in any single face or gesture."},"met:436898":{"d":"Four saints stand together in a large panel of about 142 by 100 centimeters: Saint Augustine, Saint Francis, an unnamed bishop saint and Saint Benedict. Each is identified by habit or vestments, the Church Father in a bishop's robes, Francis in the brown Franciscan habit and Benedict in the black Benedictine one. The picture has undergone drastic restoration: it was transferred from its original wood, and its paint now rests on paper laid down on canvas. It is probably a fragment of a larger altarpiece.","i":"Augustine of Hippo, Francis of Assisi and Benedict of Nursia gave their names to three great rules governing Western religious life, and a gathering of them suggests an altarpiece for a church with a monastic or mendicant community. The fourth saint, a bishop, may be the patron of the church or town. Such assemblies, often called holy conversations, showed the saints in calm company, united in prayer.","c":"Even in its much-altered state, the group has the sculptural dignity and weighty drapery that Lippi learned from Masaccio. The restoration makes judgment cautious, and the figures are somewhat uniform. As a record of how an altarpiece could marshal the great founders of the Western Church in a single image it is eloquent, though its surface has lost much of its original brilliance."},"nga:1220":{"d":"More than a dozen men, women and children eat, drink, talk and play music around a dancing couple under a vine-covered arbor. The man at the center, in a charcoal suit and pink cap pulled low, leads a woman in a dusty-rose gown and black shawl by the hand; she follows flat-footed and glances at the viewer from the corners of her eyes. At the left, people crowd a long table beside a red brick building. In the foreground two young men play violin and flute, and a pewter ewer, broken eggs and an overturned pail of flowers lie scattered.","i":"Steen painted festive scenes of Dutch village and inn life, and viewers enjoyed them as lively comedy and also as gentle moral warning: broken eggs, spilled flowers and overturned vessels in the foreground may point to the cost of dancing, drink and flirtation. The woman's sideways glance makes the viewer a witness to the couple's behavior, a device Steen often used. Signed and dated 1663, the picture comes from the middle of his career.","c":"Steen's strength is his ability to manage a crowd: each group has its own small drama, and the eye travels easily from the child on the lap to the central pair to the musicians at the front. The color is subdued but lively, with yellows and pinks lifting against green and gray. Some groups feel stiffly arranged, and the moralizing details can seem insistent, but warmth and humor carry the picture. It shows why Dutch viewers loved him."},"met:437747":{"d":"The scene is a messy domestic interior: a woman sleeps, a man is present, food and drink are out, and a lute, a cat and a game complete the scene. Jan Steen painted it around 1663–64, on a canvas about 108 by 90 centimeters, and it belongs to the series of disorderly households that made his reputation. The humor comes from watching how ordinary tasks and pleasures have been abandoned in a house where nobody is minding anything.","i":"In Dutch, a Jan Steen household is still a phrase for a chaotic home, and pictures like this one are its source. Steen's disorderly interiors were comic, but they also carried a warning: a house run without discipline, with drink and idleness, leads to ruin. Dutch viewers, who prided themselves on cleanliness and thrift, could laugh at the scene and recognize the moral at once. The sleeping woman, as the probable mistress of the house, stands for lost control.","c":"Steen's disorderly households are the best of his work, and the genre suits his gifts: sharp observation, quick wit and a love of crowded detail. The stage is set with still-life richness in the food, the lute and the cat, and the story flows easily from figure to figure. The moral is heavy-handed, as in most of his work, but the knowing humor saves it. It is a lively, comic tour of a wasted home."},"met:437748":{"d":"A young woman, the maiden of the title, is shown in a domestic interior with a bed and linens, a footstool, a dog, a copper pot and a basket, in the company of at least one man. Jan Steen painted it in oil on a canvas about 86 by 99 centimeters around 1660. The title names her illness, which is love; the picture belongs to a group in which Steen shows a doctor called in to a girl whose complaint no medicine can cure.","i":"Lovesickness was treated as a real medical condition in the seventeenth century, and Dutch painters, Steen above all, made comedy of physicians fooled or baffled by a patient whose real trouble was a lover or a pregnancy. The bed and household gear suggest the domestic setting where such comedies played out. Viewers enjoyed the joke, and perhaps the gentle moral that doctors cannot cure matters of the heart.","c":"Steen was the great Dutch painter of the doctor's visit, and he plays the theme here with his usual sympathy: the girl is ridiculed, but gently, and the household clutter gives the scene a lived-in feel. The copper, basket and dog invite the attentive, still-life care that he lavished on such objects. The joke is conventional and the staging familiar, but the humane warmth makes it a good example of the type."},"nga:195366":{"d":"A small canvas, about 62 by 52 centimeters, painted by Jan Steen in 1667. The title names two characters, Ascagnes and Lucelle, probably lovers from a play, and the subtitle, The Music Lesson, suggests that music is the occasion for their meeting. The picture belongs to Steen's group of theatrical subjects, in which figures in stage costume act out scenes from comedy, so the title and his habits point to a lively, sentimental encounter.","i":"Steen loved the theater, and his pictures often borrow the situations of the stage: lovers' quarrels, deceived guardians, music as a path to courtship. In the Dutch Golden Age a music lesson was a standard image of courtship, with instruments standing for harmony and desire. Naming the characters ties the scene to a particular play, which his audience may have known, so the picture asks to be read like a scene from a comedy.","c":"Steen's theatrical pictures are among his liveliest, and a small canvas like this suits his gift for faces and expression, with little space for crowded detail. The treatment of music as the pretext for love is conventional, and the interest comes from his fresh, spirited touch rather than from invention. It is easy to like and slighter than his large banquet scenes, though in the scale of a cabinet picture it has an intimacy they lack."},"cma:140152":{"d":"At a banquet, King Ahasuerus sits at the center while Queen Esther pleads for her people and the minister Haman slumps in his seat, aware that his plot has been exposed. The Cleveland Museum notes that Steen set a typical Dutch still life at the center: a Roemer glass, a half-peeled lemon and a blue-and-white porcelain dish. Painted around 1668 on a canvas about 70 by 93 centimeters, it turns a Bible story into a table of ordinary Dutch luxuries.","i":"The Book of Esther tells how Haman, chief minister of the Persian king, plotted to destroy the Jews of the kingdom. Esther, who had hidden her Jewish identity as queen, risked her life by revealing it at a banquet and asking the king to save her people; the king then turned on Haman. Dutch Protestants, who may have seen their own escape from Spanish rule as a parallel to God's rescue of the Israelites, found the story a favorite, and Haman's sudden fall is its turning point.","c":"Steen was not a painter of high tragedy, and the strength of the picture lies in his gift for reading faces and for treating a sacred story with human immediacy: the king's turn, Esther's firm plea, Haman's collapse. The still life at the center is a characteristic touch, and its bright accents anchor the group. Compared with Rembrandt's treatments of the same story it is livelier but less moving, a tale told clearly, with a theatrical sense of timing."},"met:437749":{"d":"A noisy party fills a terrace under a vine-covered pergola. At the centre a young woman in a pale blue bodice with roses at her breast and a shimmering brown silk skirt smiles at the viewer and stretches out an arm. To the right a young man in a plumed hat strums a long-necked lute on a stone balustrade, while a youth in a brown jacket on a ladder sings and points upward. At the left, laughing men and women crowd round a table with a roast and a jug, and an old woman holds up a baby while a glass is raised beside her. In front a small boy in a red coat leads a spaniel, and an overturned pewter jug lies on the paving. A birdcage hangs from the beams. Oil on canvas.","i":"Jan Steen of Leiden specialised in lively, disorderly gatherings of this kind; the Dutch still call a messy household a Jan Steen household. Such pictures were meant to amuse, and they also carried a moral: details like the overturned jug and the birdcage can be read as signs of excess and lost innocence, though interpretations vary and Steen plainly enjoys the fun he seems to condemn. The Met dates this picture about 1670, in his later years. That mixture of comedy and sermon, with the viewer invited to laugh and to learn, was typical of Dutch genre painting.","c":"Steen's talent is to organise chaos so convincingly that it feels like life. The pyramid of laughing figures leads the eye from the pretty centre outward, the satin is painted with rare skill, and the little boy with the dog adds a touch of comedy. As a morality the picture is toothless, because the fun is more infectious than the warning. The space is crowded, and the handling, while sure, is a little less bold than in his best earlier work, such as The Feast of Saint Nicholas. Still, it is a genial, good-natured performance."},"met:11332":{"d":"Ivory-billed woodpeckers, the largest woodpeckers of the United States, are painted in oil on a tall canvas of 99.7 by 66.7 centimeters, about the size of the double-elephant folio sheets on which Audubon's Birds of America was printed. The Met's keywords record only a bird. The painting is a copy in oil of one of Audubon's published bird pictures, so the birds are shown large, in the bold, dramatic poses Audubon gave to his subjects.","i":"John James Audubon (1785-1851) spent years painting every species of North American bird he could find for The Birds of America, issued in London from 1827 to 1838 as engraved, hand-colored plates. Joseph Kidd, a young Scottish painter, made oil copies of Audubon's images in the early 1830s, evidently for sale. The ivory-billed woodpecker, a bird of southern swamp forests, is now thought by many ornithologists to be extinct, which gives the picture a poignancy it did not have when it was painted.","c":"Kidd transposes Audubon's printed compositions into oil, and the result has the vigor of the originals but not their immediacy. The dramatic poses and the big, dark birds against a tree fill the tall canvas well; yet an oil copy lacks the crisp precision of the engraving and the fresh handling of Audubon's own watercolors, and Kidd's contribution is skilled adaptation, not invention. As a vivid natural-history picture, and as a record of a lost bird, it holds attention."},"nga:39770":{"d":"A branch hangs from the top of the canvas, crowded with five birds around a round nest woven from green fibers amid leaves and long, sharp thorns. Two of the birds are red and black and three yellow and black, each with distinct plumage. Behind them a wide, pale-blue and pink sky spreads above rolling hills dotted with tiny trees and two minuscule human figures. The picture is in pencil and oil on canvas, 66.4 by 52.1 centimeters, a copy after a bird picture by John James Audubon.","i":"The orchard oriole is a small North American songbird; the males are chestnut and black, and the females and young are yellowish, which is why the group in the painting shows two kinds of color. Orioles weave hanging, basket-like nests of grass, as seen here. Audubon liked to set birds in a habitat, and the tiny people in the landscape give scale. Kidd copied the composition in oil in the early 1830s.","c":"Kidd's copy keeps Audubon's crowded, lively arrangement: the thorny branch, the hanging nest and the distant hills give a good sense of habitat. The little human figures are the most charming touch, a reminder of scale and of the human world beyond. Compared with Audubon's brilliantly alive originals the oil is a little stiff, and the birds' poses look more diagrammatic than observed. It is a pleasing, careful picture of its type."},"nga:39768":{"d":"Three birds with finely detailed brown, black, gray, white and yellow feathers occupy a small patch of grassy ground beside a wide, calm body of water that stretches to the horizon. At the bottom one bird nestles in a straw nest on the ground; slightly above it another perches on a twig among tall green grass; a third perches higher on a rising twig with its beak open. The blue sky above is full of white clouds. The picture is pencil and oil on millboard, 48.3 by 29.9 centimeters.","i":"The sharp-tailed finch, now usually called a sparrow, lives in salt marshes along the coast, hiding its nest in dense grass near the ground, which explains the setting of tall grass beside open water. Audubon's original plate showed the habit and habitat, and Kidd's oil copy preserves both. Millboard, a thick paperboard, was a cheap, rigid and portable support, in line with the modest scale of the picture.","c":"The vertical format suits the climb from nest to twig, and the open-beaked bird at the top adds a little animation. The marsh is simplified into a few broad areas of grass, water and sky, while the birds are carefully detailed, and the contrast gives the feathers prominence. The effect is pleasant and orderly rather than dramatic, and Kidd's care is more evident than any spark of invention. Its value as a copy is faithful transmission of Audubon's design."},"nga:39769":{"d":"Three black-and-white birds with yellow patches on their heads and sharp black beaks perch on a vertical tree branch with a few small green and yellow leaves. They sit at different heights and look in different directions. Far below, a dark brown landscape fades into blue hills in the distance, under a mostly gray sky with one patch of light blue in the upper right corner. The picture is pencil and oil on canvas, 66.7 by 52.4 centimeters.","i":"The black-backed three-toed woodpecker is a bird of northern forests, named for having three toes on each foot instead of four, and the males carry a yellow patch on the crown, as the group seems to show. It feeds on beetle larvae in dead and burned conifers. Placing the birds high on a branch, with the land far beneath, conveys the height of northern woods; the picture is Kidd's oil copy of an Audubon composition.","c":"The strong black-and-white pattern of the birds against the gray sky is the picture's best asset, and the high viewpoint gives an unusual sense of elevation. The poses are alert and varied, though somewhat stiff, and the landscape far below is thin and brown. It is among the more striking of the group for its design, and a good example of how Audubon's compositions solved the problem of showing several views of one bird at once."},"nga:39771":{"d":"A branch of long, bell-shaped golden-yellow flowers and dark green leaves drops from near the top right corner. Four clusters of four or five blossoms are spaced along two branches that fork from the main stem; each flower is russet-red or burnt orange outside and flares to a vivid yellow interior. Two small yellow birds perch among the flowers and are easily overlooked, each about the size of a cluster of blossoms. The branches are outlined against a hazy blue sky. It is pencil and oil on millboard, 48.2 by 29.7 centimeters.","i":"The yellow warbler is a small North American songbird, the male bright yellow with reddish streaks on the breast. In this picture the birds' own color matches the flowers so closely that they almost vanish, a natural camouflage that Audubon and Kidd turn into a design. Audubon often paired birds with plants, which gave his plates botanical interest, and here the flowers are the main subject and the birds a pleasant surprise.","c":"An unusual, elegant composition: the flowers claim most of the picture, the little yellow birds are camouflaged among them, and the harmony of gold, russet and green against a hazy blue is lovely. The birds are small and a little stiff, so the picture works better as decoration than as ornithology, but the idea of hiding them in plain sight is clever. It is among the most attractive of Kidd's copies for its color and its restraint."},"met:438139":{"d":"The Virgin Mary with the Christ Child and the infant Saint John the Baptist, with a lamb, in oil on a wood panel measuring 103.8 by 85.7 centimeters, painted in the early 1570s. The Met's subject tags list Mary, the Christ Child, the child Saint John, the Madonna and Child and sheep, so the group fills the panel with the lamb as a small companion. It is a devotional picture of the kind made for a Florentine household, large enough to be a focus of prayer in a private room.","i":"The infant John the Baptist, Christ's cousin, was often shown playing with the Christ Child, and the lamb he holds or touches is Christ's emblem of sacrifice, recalling John's later words 'Behold the Lamb of God.' Saint John is also the patron saint of Florence, so the subject had special appeal for Florentine patrons. Santi di Tito, a leading Florentine of the later sixteenth century, aimed for clarity and decorum after the complexities of Mannerism.","c":"Santi di Tito belongs to a generation that reacted against the twisted elegance of Mannerism, seeking simpler compositions, clearer narrative and more natural color. This Madonna is calm, legible and domestic, with the children and the lamb arranged in an intimate group. Admirers call that clarity fresh; critics find it a little bland after the inventiveness of the earlier sixteenth century. It is a graceful, devout picture, strongest in its quiet affection."},"met:437964":{"d":"Frieda Schiff, then in her late teens, is portrayed on an upright canvas of about 101 by 76 centimeters, painted in 1894 by the Swedish artist Anders Zorn. The title adds that she was later Mrs. Felix M. Warburg, so the portrait was made before her marriage. The recorded motifs list only the sitter, a woman, so the picture rests on Zorn's usual strengths: a fluent, economical touch and a limited range of colors, applied so that the likeness seems caught in a few confident strokes.","i":"Zorn first came to the United States for the 1893 world's fair in Chicago and returned repeatedly through the 1890s, painting many of the country's wealthiest and most prominent sitters. Frieda was the daughter of the banker Jacob H. Schiff, a leading figure of New York's German-Jewish financial elite, and married Felix M. Warburg in 1895. A portrait made in her late teens would have recorded a young woman on the eve of an alliance between two banking families.","c":"Zorn's portraits of the 1890s are admired for fluent, economical brushwork and a feeling of the sitter caught alive, and a portrait of a very young woman suits that gift, since it favors freshness over gravity. Among the painters who served the American rich, Zorn offered a quick, informal sitting and an unfussy, lifelike result, qualities that kept him in demand. His method can slide into easy bravura, but a youthful sitter rewards his speed."},"met:437965":{"d":"A man named in the title as Edward R. Bacon (1846-1915) is the sitter of this portrait, painted in oil on a canvas of about 123 by 90 centimeters by Anders Zorn in 1897. The recorded motifs name only the man, so the picture is a plain likeness of a sitter then in his early fifties. Zorn's portraits of men often concentrate on the head and hands, with quick, broad handling elsewhere, so that the face holds the viewer's attention.","i":"Painted in the same year as Zorn's portrait of Mrs. Walter Rathbone Bacon, it suggests that the Bacon family was among the New York patrons who turned to Zorn on his American trips of the 1890s. A man's portrait of the period recorded business success and respectability for the family. The title gives only the sitter's name and dates, and his character is left to the portrait itself.","c":"A male portrait gives Zorn fewer decorative resources than a woman in a gown, and the test is whether the head carries the picture. His fluid handling gives faces a vitality that more labored portraitists lack, though he can be breezy where a sitter's character is complex. As a record of an American man of affairs painted by a visiting Swede, it has a freshness that solemn portraits of the era rarely match."},"met:437966":{"d":"A woman sits and looks toward the viewer, in a dress, with a dog, on a tall canvas of about 171 by 108 centimeters that comes close to life size. Anders Zorn painted it in 1897. The recorded motifs, sitting, dress, a dog and a direct gaze, outline a formal society portrait in which the sitter's poise and costume carry the display. The large format was reserved for important commissions, and the picture is painted in oil on canvas.","i":"The sitter, Virginia Purdy Barker (1862-1919), was the wife of Walter Rathbone Bacon, and the portrait is one of two Zorn portraits of the Bacon family dated 1897, when the Swedish painter was at the height of his success among wealthy Americans. A dog in a lady's portrait suggests domestic ease and loyalty, as well as the leisure of a household that could keep pets. The direct gaze gives the sitter a confident, modern presence.","c":"Zorn's gift is for making a large society portrait look unlabored. The pose, the glance and the dog can suggest an interrupted moment instead of a formal sitting, and his fluent handling of dress and flesh keeps the surface lively. Compared with Sargent, his friend and rival, he is usually more direct and less probing. It is a portrait about presence and chic, and it delivers both."},"met:435626":{"d":"A view of the Porte de la Reine, one of the medieval gates in the walls of Aigues-Mortes in southern France, in oil on a canvas of 80.6 by 99.7 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a gate and a horse, so the old stone gateway is shown with an animal before it. The picture is a bright outdoor study of masonry, light and shadow, painted in 1867 by a young artist working in the open air in the south, near his hometown of Montpellier.","i":"Aigues-Mortes, a walled town in the marshy Camargue, was founded by Saint Louis in the thirteenth century as a port from which he sailed on crusade. Bazille, a friend of Monet and Renoir and a pioneer of Impressionism, painted in the south each summer; in 1867 he also helped Monet by buying his large painting Women in the Garden. His outdoor pictures of that year belong to the first stirrings of the new style.","c":"Bazille is the quiet pioneer of Impressionism, and works like this show his strength: bright southern light handled with firm structure, the walls built from broad planes of warm color and cool shadow rather than Monet's flickering touch. The horse is an anecdotal accent, and the picture's solidity owes more to Corot and Courbet than to later Impressionism. His death in the Franco-Prussian War at twenty-eight cut off a career whose promise this painting shows well."},"nga:66399":{"d":"Seen from a sandy beach, a long sand-coloured wall with battlements and two round towers runs back along sunlit water at the right, with a small arched doorway halfway along. The beach and the aquamarine and green water are loosely brushed, and a few boats painted with quick black and brown strokes are drawn up near trees at the left. A windmill, houses and trees line the horizon beyond. Over all is a topaz-blue sky with a few puffy clouds low down. The canvas is wide, 60 by 100 centimetres.","i":"Aigues-Mortes is a medieval walled town in the marshes of the Camargue, in southern France, founded by Louis IX as a port for his crusades; its ramparts date from the thirteenth century. Bazille, a native of nearby Montpellier, painted it in 1867, probably working outdoors in the strong light of the Midi. The ramparts matter here chiefly as pale, geometric forms against sky and water, in keeping with the early efforts of the future Impressionists to paint nature as it looks in sunlight.","c":"The picture's freshness lies in its light. Bazille lets sand, wall and sky share a single pale key, and the loose, thin paint of beach and water conveys the heat of the Midi. The long wall is a bold diagonal that pulls the eye into depth. The composition is rather empty and the boats are mere accents, but as a landscape from an artist whose real ambition was the figure, it is confident and luminous."},"nga:46556":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, seated in a high-backed chair with a red frame and dark green upholstery, his head and eyes turned slightly to the viewer's right. He has high cheekbones, a straight nose, closed lips and dark eyes under small shadows. His black hair is wavy on top and cropped at the sides, his light brown moustache turns up at the ends, and he has a thin goatee. He wears an open dark jacket and black vest over a white shirt. The background is dark grey with visible brushstrokes.","i":"Edouard Blau belonged to Bazille's circle of friends and became a writer of opera libretti, including works for Massenet. Bazille, who came from a wealthy Protestant family in Montpellier, painted his friends often, and portraits like this record the circle of young painters, musicians and writers around him rather than paying commissions. The sober, unposed treatment, with a dark ground and a plain chair, reflects the influence of Manet's modern portraits and the wish of the future Impressionists to paint contemporary people directly.","c":"A direct, unflattering portrait. The dark figure against a dark grey ground is relieved only by the red chair frame and the white collar, and the head is firmly modelled. Its restraint is a strength, though the mood is sober to the point of dourness, and the chair and shoulder cut off by the edges look slightly accidental. Beside Bazille's outdoor figure groups it lacks sunlight, but it shows how the future Impressionists approached portraits of their friends."},"nga:66400":{"d":"A bearded man in a dark suit sits in a wood-framed chair, seen from the lap up and in profile facing the viewer's left. He looks down at an open book on his lap, holding one side of it with his left hand and a partly smoked thin cigar in the other. He wears a black vest and jacket, a black polka-dotted ascot, a white shirt, chestnut-brown trousers and a blue flower in his lapel. His black hair is cut short and glossy, and the background deepens from brown at the right to near black at the upper left.","i":"Edmond Maître was a close friend of Bazille, known as a music lover, and he appears at the piano in Bazille's picture of his studio painted the following year. This portrait shows him reading and smoking at ease, a private moment chosen to suggest his character, not his rank. The black clothes, plain chair and unfussy light follow Manet's example, while the blue flower in the lapel is a small personal touch. It is typical of the portraits that the circle around Manet and Monet made of one another.","c":"A deeply considered portrait that gains from restraint. The profile gives the head a clear, strong shape; the black clothing and brown ground are close in value, so the face and book stand out; and the one bright note, the blue flower, is placed with tact. The mood is of quiet absorption. Bazille is less free than Manet, and the picture is a little reserved, but it is one of the surest portraits made in the Impressionist circle."},"nga:61356":{"d":"A woman leans forward from behind a wide basket crowded with flowers, holding out a bunch of three pink-and-white peonies toward the viewer. She wears a cream blouse with scalloped trim at the high neck, coral earrings, and a plaid cloth in rust red, slate blue, pale purple and black tied over her hair. Her brow is slightly furrowed and her dark eyes meet the viewer's. The basket, filled with tulips, roses, lilac and other blooms, takes up the bottom third of the canvas against a dove-grey ground.","i":"The sitter is a Black woman, and the title does not name her. Bazille painted the picture in 1870, the last year of his life: he enlisted that summer and was killed in November, aged twenty-eight. Black sitters were seldom the subject of a serious portrait in French painting of the time, and the picture is often read as an unusually direct and respectful one, with the woman given a frank gaze and a place at the centre of a bright still life of flowers.","c":"A bold, fresh picture. The band of colourful flowers against the cool grey ground, the pattern of the plaid headcloth and the sitter's level look give it unusual presence. The flowers are painted more summarily than the face, and the basket cuts the figure off abruptly, but the decisive design carries it. Within Bazille's brief output it is one of the most original and memorable, a painting that points to what he might have done had he lived."},"nga:12202":{"d":"Light from the upper left falls across a glass, a pitcher, a basket of peaches and other fruit arranged along a brown ledge, filling the height and width of this small horizontal canvas. A wide-mouthed translucent glass at the left stands before deep shadow, with thin streaks of pale pink and white for reflections and a hazelnut beside it. A tall cinnamon-brown jug, its handle turned toward the viewer, stands to the right of a low woven basket piled with at least six peaches on pine-green leaves. In front lie three more pieces of fruit.","i":"Chardin, a Parisian, made his name with still lifes. In 1728 the Académie Royale admitted him as a painter 'of animals and fruit', and works of this size and kind belong to the years around that moment. Such pictures were bought by collectors who admired Dutch and Flemish still life, but Chardin's are plainer, with everyday vessels and fruit and no luxury goods.","c":"Chardin is already himself: the glass is a few streaks of pink and white, the peaches have butter-yellow accents, and the whole is built from soft, dragged touches of light against brown. The arrangement is tight, almost cramped, and a little inert compared with his later work, but it shows the quiet, loving look at ordinary objects that made his reputation. Modest in scale, rich in what the paint is doing."},"met:435887":{"d":"A wide kitchen or pantry still life, in oil on a canvas of 76.2 by 108.0 centimeters, built around a silver soup tureen. The Met's catalog tags include fruit, apples, a bird, a rabbit and a cat, so game, fruit and silver share a table with an animal among them. The picture is a feast of textures: gleaming metal, soft fur, feathers and the skin of fruit, set out with a generosity of scale that Chardin would later pare down.","i":"Chardin was accepted into the Académie royale in 1728 as a painter of animals and fruit, the humblest rank in the academic hierarchy. He made that rank noble through patient observation, and a silver tureen, a luxury object of the table, lets him study how a polished surface reflects the things around it. The cat, a traditional figure in kitchen and game pictures, adds a note of appetite and possible theft.","c":"This is Chardin early and ambitious: the big format and the display of silver, game and fruit recall Flemish and Dutch kitchen pictures, but with quieter, truer observation. The gleaming tureen and the soft treatment of fur and feather are the achievement. Later he would reduce his still lifes to a few humble objects; here the abundance is still rich and a little more theatrical, but the assured handling of surfaces is unmistakable."},"met:435888":{"d":"A young man in a brown coat and white shirt leans on a stone ledge at a window, his face tilted down as he blows a bubble through a long, slender straw. The large, clear bubble hangs at the lower right, catching a small reflection of light. At the left stands a glass of cloudy soapy water on the ledge, with a leafy plant behind it. Over the ledge at the right a child in a feathered cap peers up, wide-eyed, watching the bubble. The background is dark olive and brown, and the soft light from the left picks out the cheek, the sleeve and the glass. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 63 centimetres.","i":"The Met notes that Chardin made copies of his most successful compositions, and later versions of Soap Bubbles are in the Los Angeles County Museum of Art and the National Gallery of Art in Washington. This one dates from about 1733 to 1734, around the time he turned from still life to figures; a version was shown at the Paris Salon of 1739. In earlier Northern European painting, blowing bubbles carried a moralising message about the brevity of life, the idea of man as a bubble. Chardin keeps the reference but, as the Met puts it, moves the register from the allegorical to the everyday.","c":"Chardin's quiet is the great virtue. The tilted head, the held breath and the child's rapt eyes are caught without sentiment, and the luminous bubble, with its small window-like highlight, is a tiny triumph of observation. The handling is soft and subdued, the palette limited to browns and greys, and the scene almost silent. If there is a weakness, it is that the emotion is so restrained the picture can seem a little bland, and some viewers will want more of the moral bite of earlier bubble pictures. But the restraint is the point."},"nga:994":{"d":"A young man and a child are framed in the opening of a stone window. At the center the young man leans forward over his forearms on the wide ledge and blows through a long straw to make a large, glistening bubble that hangs from its end. A second straw rests in a glass of soapy water near his elbow. His chestnut hair is tied back with a black ribbon, and he wears a brown jacket over a white shirt. A smaller child peeks over the ledge at the right, also watching the bubble, and ivy climbs the stone wall at the left.","i":"Soap bubbles carried a moral meaning in European art: the Latin saying homo bulla, 'man is a bubble', compared the brevity of life to a bubble's. Dutch painters of the seventeenth century often used the motif, and Chardin makes it a quiet modern scene. The young man's absorbed gaze invites reflection on the passing of time and the fragility of pleasure. Chardin painted the subject in more than one version.","c":"The pleasure is in the concentration. The young man's lowered eyes, the child's half-hidden face and the shimmering bubble held at the straw's tip make a perfectly judged moment of suspended attention. The stone frame brings the viewer close, and the handling is subtle, particularly in the transparent bubble. It is a small moral without any sermon, and a fine example of Chardin's way of dignifying the ordinary."},"nga:97":{"d":"A young person with brown hair and a peachy complexion leans on a table, shown in profile facing left, and sets folded playing cards upright in a curving row, placing the last of ten so that they stand close together like dominoes. His long hair curls by the ears and is tied back with a navy ribbon; he wears a chestnut-brown coat with a dark blue sash over a white shirt and looks down under lowered lids. Three coins lie near his hand on the teal-blue table cover, and an open drawer holds a jack of hearts. The background is dark, and J. Chardin signed it at lower right.","i":"A house of cards was a familiar emblem of the fragility of human projects and the folly of idle ambition, and the coins on the table and the jack in the open drawer hint at gaming and wasted time. Chardin painted the subject more than once in the 1730s, always with a young model absorbed in the task. A boy building something that will fall is both a charming sight and a gentle lesson in how easily effort collapses.","c":"The attention in the picture is total: the lowered eyelids, the delicate profile and the precise placing of the cards make a drama out of nothing. Chardin's paint is thin and soft, but the effect is of great stillness, and the dark ground pushes the pale face forward. The moral is light and never preached. One of the best known of his genre scenes, it shows his gift for finding gravity in a child's game."},"nga:110960":{"d":"A young woman in a white jacket, skirt and cap and a light peach apron stands in profile, holding a long-handled pot over an open barrel and leaning slightly toward it. A gold pendant or bell hangs from a teal-blue ribbon at her neck. On the floor at the left stand a tall copper-brown container, a brass pot lying on its side and a brick-red pan with a long iron handle, and a clay pot with green bands stands behind her. Light from the upper left glints on the metal. The floor is washed in greens and blue, the wall caramel brown and smoke gray.","i":"Scullery maids washed pots and pans, and Chardin dignified the task: the woman stands at a barrel of water, the pot dipping down into it, with the clean and used vessels around her. The picture belongs to the group of kitchen subjects he painted in the 1730s and is dated about 1738. The gold pendant at her neck is a small touch of finery in an otherwise plain setting.","c":"The interplay of light on metal is the thing: copper, brass and iron each have their own sheen, and the white clothing glows against warm brown. The girl herself is calm and anonymous, painted with a sympathy that avoids the sentimentality of other painters' maids. The composition is unforced, almost casual, and the objects are as present as the figure. Typical Chardin: modest in subject, accomplished in surface."},"nga:41650":{"d":"The museum offers no written description of this picture. The title shows a female servant in a kitchen, one of the humble domestic subjects that Chardin made his own in the 1730s and 1740s, and the canvas is small and upright, about 46 by 38 centimeters, dated 1738. Details of her pose and surroundings are not recorded here, so only the type of picture and its context can be stated with confidence.","i":"In the 1730s Chardin turned from still life to scenes of women at household work, and they were an immediate success with collectors and print buyers. He gave cooks, maids and governesses the same close attention as copper pots, and presented domestic labor with dignity and without comment. The date, 1738, places the picture in the middle of this phase.","c":"Chardin's servants are not romanticized; they are people absorbed in their work, set in plain, warm light among battered utensils. His restraint stands out against the arch charm of his Rococo contemporaries, and the best of these pictures achieve a quiet dignity that no flattering portrait could match. This is the type on which his popular reputation grew."},"cma:150115":{"d":"A small still life, 32.2 by 40.7 centimeters, of kitchen things: as the title says, utensils with leeks, fish and eggs. The Cleveland Museum notes that Chardin, already famous by 1730, began painting small, sparse still lifes of kitchen utensils, often reusing the same elements, varying the position of the objects and adding or subtracting a utensil, always placing each in relation to the rest to achieve a balanced design.","i":"Chardin was a contemporary of François Boucher and taught Jean-Honoré Fragonard, but his work contrasts with theirs: he represents the naturalistic tendency that persisted alongside the fashionable lightness, grace and playfulness of the Rococo. He discovered a hidden poetry in even the humblest objects, bringing the viewer into an earthly world seen with directness and simplicity.","c":"Chardin's kitchen still lifes look simple and are subtle: the placing of each object is judged to a hair, and the surfaces, the scales, the eggshell, the crinkled leek, have a tactile truth. The small size asks for close looking, and the reward is a sense of calm that fashionable painters of his day seldom offered. Those who want drama may find it plain; the plainness is the point, and the balance of the design the achievement."},"cma:147607":{"d":"A small vertical still life, 41.0 by 33.6 centimeters, of herrings on what the Cleveland Museum calls a humble pantry shelf. The museum notes that Chardin worked in private and used a complex technique, delicate glazes together with roughly dragged thick paint, to capture the varied surfaces, atmospheres and spaces. The subject is modest, but the paint is not: it ranges from thin, translucent layers to dragged and broken strokes.","i":"Herrings were a cheap, common food, often eaten on fast days in Catholic France, so the picture shows the everyday pantry of ordinary households. Chardin reflected intensely on the act of observation and was aware of recent developments in optics and physics; he explored light, shadow and color, and expected his pictures to be inspected closely. Still life became his forum for sophisticated performances in paint.","c":"A virtuoso performance disguised as a pantry shelf: smoothness and roughness, glaze and thick paint, are set against each other so that fish scales, wood and metal read as different materials. The picture invites close inspection, and the viewer should look at how dragged paint stands for the glint of light on a scale. If it lacks the scale of grander pictures, its density rewards looking, and it shows Chardin at his most concentrated."},"nga:98":{"d":"An older girl and a younger child bend toward each other over an open book on a wooden cupboard. The girl, seen from the hips up in profile facing left, points to a page with a metal pointer; she wears a ruffled white cap with a gold and cranberry ribbon, a sapphire-blue dress with rolled sleeves and white cuffs, a white neckerchief and an ivory apron. The younger child, seen over the cupboard's ledge, looks down at the book in a padded cap and nutmeg-brown sleeves. Light from the left makes a greenish glow on the tawny background.","i":"Teaching a younger child to read was a task for girls and women at home, and Chardin portrays the lesson with respect: the older girl is an earnest little teacher, the pupil absorbed. The picture is dated only as after 1740, and Chardin treated the theme of the young teacher more than once. Viewers of the time prized the gentle lesson about early education as well as the honest observation.","c":"A gently comic and affectionate picture. The pointer, the pursed lips and the girl's pride in her role are observed with a smile, while the younger child's downcast eyes carry the weight of the lesson. The pairing of the two heads, the white caps and the glowing cupboard make an intimate triangle. The sweetness is saved from sentiment by the firm handling of the whites and the plain, tawny setting."},"nga:41649":{"d":"A woman stands before a wooden table near a fireplace mantel, lit brightly from the left while the background falls into shadow. She wears a hip-length white jacket patterned with large rose-pink dots, a striped skirt, a petal-pink apron, a frilled white bonnet and fern-green, high-heeled slippers. In one hand she holds a silver spoon and in the other an egg she is peeling, while a white cloth drapes the arm that cradles the long handle of a pan. On the table are a round loaf, a sky-blue pitcher, a bowl with a pewter egg cup and a knife; a mahogany-red pot sits at her feet.","i":"A nurse in the old sense was a woman hired to look after the sick, and the egg, the bread and the pan suggest that she is preparing food for a patient, a task requiring patience and tact. The title praises her quiet attention. The picture, dated 1747, belongs with the other servants and mothers Chardin painted at everyday tasks of care, which present household work as a form of moral seriousness.","c":"The pink dots, the stripes and the fern-green shoes are a delicious fantasia of pattern, and Chardin's command of light, falling on silver, pewter and linen, gives the humble objects a presence the nurse barely competes with. The picture is slighter than his best pairs of the 1730s, and the figure is a little stiff, yet its tenderness is genuine. A work of Chardin's maturity, quiet and certain."},"nga:41648":{"d":"The museum offers no written description of this picture. The title indicates dead game, the quarry of the hunt, presumably shown on a ledge or hung up with a few humbler accessories, as in Chardin's other game pieces. Painted probably in the 1750s, it is a canvas of about 50 by 59 centimeters, a medium-sized horizontal format. The record does not list the animals, so only the type of picture can be described.","i":"Chardin painted a number of game pieces, a subject that went back to Flemish and Dutch painters of the hunt and the kitchen larder. In France, game was a luxury of the table, and its depiction gave painters a chance to show off fur, feathers and the look of cooling flesh. Chardin approached it without the pomp of his predecessors.","c":"Game pieces tested a painter's ability to render fur and feather, and Chardin's reputation was built on exactly such texture. Judged by his other work of the period, a picture of this kind would be soft in edge, rich in tone and sparing in staging. The type is less personal to him than his genre scenes, but it gave him a field for pure painting."},"nga:53124":{"d":"Three apples and two pears sit beside a knife and a luminous white mug on a tan stone ledge in front of a muted olive-green wall. The objects and the background are painted in blended strokes that give the picture a soft glow. The apples at the center are dappled candy red and pale yellow, the pears golden and moss green, one upright and the other lying with its stem toward the viewer. White highlights show a light source to the left. The cream-white mug has straight sides and a handle curving right, and brick-red shadows mark the surface.","i":"In the 1760s Chardin, then in his sixties, painted small still lifes of fruit and simple vessels with a soft, glowing touch that contrasts with the harder detail of his earlier work. The mug, knife and fruit are everyday objects set in a plain arrangement that rewards slow looking. The museum dates the picture to about 1764, late in his career.","c":"Chardin's late still lifes are the plainest and in some ways the finest of his works: a few fruits and a mug become luminous through close control of tone, and the thick, blended paint makes the surfaces almost breathe. The arrangement is deliberately unremarkable, with the knife slanting off the edge as the only dynamic note. It is a lesson in how much painting can do with little."},"nga:52190":{"d":"A coastal landscape from an elevated viewpoint. At the right a thatched-roof cottage is partly hidden by rolling green hills, where a thin dirt path and a line of gray stones lead to it. The land slopes down to the water at the left, the grassy hills turning reddish brown and then light yellow sand; the water is light green near the shore, darker blue farther out. The sky is light blue with white clouds, and the brushstrokes are visible and textured. The canvas is 54.0 by 64.8 centimeters.","i":"Raguenez is a small headland and island on the southern coast of Brittany, near Pont-Aven, the village where Gauguin and his followers worked in the late 1880s. Moret was associated with that circle of painters, who sought simplified color and unspoiled rural life in Brittany. Painted about 1890 to 1895, the picture shows how Impressionist light and Post-Impressionist pattern could mingle in a Breton view.","c":"Moret's Breton views blend Impressionist light with the simplified shapes and patterned color of the Pont-Aven painters. Here the high viewpoint and the generous patches of green, reddish brown and yellow sand give a strong design, while visible brushwork keeps the surface alive. The result is sound and attractive rather than startling: less radical than Gauguin's work and less atmospheric than Monet's, with a calm character of its own."},"nga:46182":{"d":"A man with short auburn hair and a long beard is shown from the hips up, angled to the left, looking at the viewer from the corners of his brown eyes. A muted cobalt-blue cape covers his chest and sloping shoulders, with rose-pink lining showing where it drapes over one arm. He holds a black hat close to his body in one hand, while the other emerges from a white sleeve and presses a small book to his chest, one page caught between thumb and finger. His head, nose and hands are slightly elongated.","i":"Giovanni della Casa (1503-1556) was a Florentine poet, churchman and diplomat, later the author of Galateo, the book of manners that gave Italian a word for good behavior. The title Monsignor marks his rank as a prelate, and the small book, a sign of learning, shows him as a humanist as well as an official. The date, probably 1541 to 1544, is the museum's estimate, from the years just before he became papal nuncio in Venice.","c":"Pontormo makes a portrait of temperament: the elongated head and hands, the sloping shoulders, the sidelong glance and the nervous grip on the book are all signs of Florentine Mannerism, and the sitter seems both composed and guarded. The cool blue cape and its pink lining give the picture a refined color, and the shifting spaces behind him, windows and arches seen in slivers, add unease. It is among the most psychologically searching portraits of its age."},"met:10437":{"d":"A funeral procession moving along a street lined with trees, in oil on slate paper, 15.2 by 23.5 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list trees, men, a street and a funeral. The word adult in the title implies that there was also a children's funeral among his subjects, and the format is the same small, wide one William P. Chappel used for his other scenes of the 1870s.","i":"In the early nineteenth century funerals in American cities were often held at home, followed by a procession on foot behind the hearse to the burying ground, with mourners in black. Customs differed for children, whose coffins were often carried by hand and whose funerals might be marked in white. Chappel, recalling the city of his youth, set such ceremonies down as part of its daily life.","c":"A funeral is a hard subject for a naive painter, because its dignity depends on pace and spacing, and the viewer should look at whether the dark figures keep a grave, orderly rhythm along the street. Chappel's plain manner suits the quiet subject. As art it is modest; as testimony to the manners of death in a nineteenth-century city it has the force of an eyewitness."},"met:10438":{"d":"A small scene in Duane Park, a public square in lower Manhattan, in oil on slate paper, 15.2 by 23.2 centimeters. The title names baked pears, a cheap street food, and the Met's keywords record trees, a house, a woman, a man and the park, so the picture shows figures in a small urban green space with buildings behind. Painted in the 1870s, it is one of a group of nearly identical small pictures by William P. Chappel.","i":"Duane Park, created in the 1790s and named for a mayor of New York, was a small green in an area of warehouses and markets. Street vendors selling hot baked pears were a familiar sight in the nineteenth-century city, and the title records the sort of humble local detail that makes the picture a document of street life. Chappel painted it in his seventies, evidently from memory of an earlier New York.","c":"Chappel works as a recorder rather than a stylist, and the charm of such a picture lies in its candor: figures placed plainly, details set down one by one, a corner of the city painted with affection. The perspective is likely naive and the painting small, but the sense of a remembered place is strong. It is better as a document of urban life than as a work of formal art, and the document is valuable."},"met:10439":{"d":"A baptism beside a river, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The Met's keywords record a river, a boat, a man and a woman, so the scene shows a ceremony in the water, with figures on the bank or in a boat as witnesses. Painted in the 1870s, it is one of a group of small, nearly identical-sized pictures by William P. Chappel that record the street life and customs of the city and its surroundings.","i":"Baptism by immersion in rivers and harbors was practiced by Baptist and other Protestant congregations in the early nineteenth century, often in public, with a crowd of onlookers. Chappel's record of such a ceremony belongs to his series of memories of earlier New York, in which religious and civic customs sit beside street vendors and taverns.","c":"A religious ceremony in a small, plainly painted picture risks reverence or sentimentality. Chappel's habit, in this series, is to treat everything, sacred or secular, as a thing seen and remembered, with clear figures and little drama, and that evenness gives the scene an air of authentic recollection. It lacks the spatial skill and atmosphere that a trained landscape painter would bring, but it gains in directness."},"met:10440":{"d":"A bathing party in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The museum's keywords note trees, swimming, a man and a woman, so the scene shows people in or beside the water with trees nearby. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small, nearly identical-sized pictures of the city's pastimes and street life, and the wide, shallow format gives the outdoor scene an easy horizontal sweep.","i":"Bathing in rivers and harbors was a common summer pleasure in nineteenth-century New York, in the days before public baths and pools, and the shores of Manhattan offered places to swim. Chappel's picture records such outings as part of his remembered city: an informal, unselfconscious leisure that already seemed picturesque to him by the 1870s.","c":"Chappel's strength is the unselfconscious record of ordinary leisure, and a bathing scene lets him show bodies, water and trees in a simple, cheerful arrangement. At about fifteen by twenty-three centimeters the figures are necessarily small, and the drawing in this series is plain, which gives the scene freshness rather than polish. It is a pleasant, honest picture, and a reminder that the city once swam in its rivers."},"met:10441":{"d":"A shipyard in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a ship, a boat, a horse, a tree and human figures, so the scene shows a vessel at the water's edge, with workers, a horse and a tree. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a group of small pictures of the city's working life, and it records a place named in the title.","i":"New York's East River shore was lined with shipyards in the early nineteenth century, and the city's builders turned out packet ships, steamboats and later clippers that made the port the greatest in the United States. A picture of a named yard records a local landmark of a vanished industry, and the horse and figures show the manual labor that building a ship required.","c":"The interest of this picture lies in the record of a working yard and the bustle around it, not in marine drama. Chappel's plain, careful handling suits a document of work, with the ship, the horse and the figures set down as he remembered them. As art it is modest; as a piece of urban history, a vanished yard seen with affection, it has real value."},"met:10442":{"d":"A street scene in oil on slate paper, 15.4 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's keywords record houses, a street, a man and a carriage, so the picture shows a bootblack at work in a city street lined with houses, with a carriage passing. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of nearly identical small pictures, and its subject is a humble trade that the artist remembered from the street life of earlier decades.","i":"Bootblacks were a familiar part of the nineteenth-century street, offering a shine to passersby for a few cents; the trade was open to boys and to poor men, and it often gave work to immigrants and to African Americans, who were shut out of many occupations. Chappel's inclusion of such figures records the working people on whom the city's comfort depended.","c":"Chappel's street scenes depend on telling detail rather than on composition: the houses, the carriage and the working figure are set down simply, in the plain manner of the series. At this size there is little depth of space, and the human figure is more a type than a portrait. As a record of a street trade it is valuable, and as a small, evocative scene it succeeds."},"met:10443":{"d":"A tavern scene in oil on slate paper, 16.4 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list a building, a house, trees, a man on horseback and a bull, so the picture shows the Bull's Head tavern, with a rider and an animal among the details. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small, nearly identical-sized pictures, and the name of the tavern in the title is part of the subject.","i":"The Bull's Head was a name for taverns that served the cattle trade, and one on the Bowery in lower Manhattan was long associated with drovers who brought livestock to the city's markets. A tavern with a bull in its name and a bull among the details recalls a time when cattle were driven through city streets, a rural presence in a growing city that Chappel looked back on from the 1870s.","c":"A tavern picture lives on its atmosphere, and the elements here, the building, the rider, the animal and the trees, are plain ingredients; a viewer should look at how clearly Chappel orders them. His unfussy style gives a sense of the rough, rural edge of early New York. It is not an artistic triumph, but it is a convincing little piece of memory painting."},"met:10444":{"d":"A road scene in oil on slate paper, 15.7 by 23.6 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a house, a tree, a man, a road and a dog, so the picture shows a peddler, as the title says, selling buttermilk along a country or edge-of-town road, with a house, a tree and a dog nearby. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it belongs to a series of small pictures of street trades and city life.","i":"Peddlers hawked goods through the streets with distinctive cries, and buttermilk, the tangy liquid left after churning butter, was a cheap, refreshing drink sold from carts. In the early nineteenth century New York was still a small city bordering farms, and country sellers brought dairy products into town. Chappel's picture evokes that older city, recalled by the artist from the vantage of the 1870s.","c":"A buttermilk seller is humble subject matter, and Chappel's plain, affectionate approach suits it: the man, dog, house and tree make a simple arrangement. A viewer should look at how clearly the small figures read on the road. Like the rest of the series, it is stronger as testimony than as design, and its charm is the sense of a particular, remembered day."},"met:10445":{"d":"Chimney sweeps in a street of houses with a tree, in oil on slate paper, 15.4 by 23.7 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list men, a street, a tree and houses, so the picture shows the sweeps at work or on their rounds in the city. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it belongs to a series of small, nearly identical-sized pictures of street types and trades.","i":"Chimney sweeps were essential in cities heated by coal and wood fires, and they walked the streets calling for custom. In New York and other American cities many sweeps were boys or young men, and many were African American, in a trade that was dirty, dangerous and poorly paid. Chappel's picture records a trade that the modern city has largely done away with.","c":"Sweeps are a rewarding subject because their work leaves a visible mark: soot, brushes and rods make the trade easy to recognize even in a small picture. Chappel's plain handling lets those attributes tell the story. The value of the picture is less in the paint than in the trade it remembers, one in which hard work and poverty were part of a city's daily rhythm."},"met:10446":{"d":"A city watchman in a street of houses, painted in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a man, a street and houses, so the picture shows a lone figure on patrol in front of a row of buildings. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small, nearly identical-sized pictures of New York street types, and the watchman is the whole subject.","i":"Before the city created a police force in 1845, New York was guarded at night by watchmen who walked their beats calling the hours and the weather, and who had a reputation for dozing in their watch-boxes. The watchman was one of the street figures that disappeared with modernization, and Chappel, born in 1801, painted him from memory of the city of his boyhood.","c":"A single figure on an empty street is simple to stage, and the success of this small picture depends on whether the watchman reads as a character as well as a type. Chappel's plain, affectionate manner usually avoids caricature, and the picture is nostalgic and documentary without pretending to more. It is a small, polite memory of a night-time institution that the modern city did away with."},"met:10447":{"d":"A man with a cart and a dog before houses and a building, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The Met's keywords record a man, a dog, a cart, a house and a building, so the picture shows the dog killer of the title at work in a city street. It is one of a series of small pictures by William P. Chappel, painted in the 1870s, of the street trades and sights of the old city.","i":"In nineteenth-century cities, stray dogs were a nuisance and a rabies scare, and municipal authorities periodically hired men to round them up and destroy them, to the horror of their owners and of children. Chappel's inclusion of the dog killer among his remembered street types shows that the old city's unpleasant sights, not just its picturesque ones, stayed in his memory.","c":"This is among the grimmer of the street subjects, and the plainness of Chappel's manner helps: there is no attempt to dramatize cruelty, only a matter-of-fact record of a municipal chore. The result is more unsettling than a sentimental treatment would be. As art it is small and simple; as social history it is frank about the city's rougher side, and about what children and dog owners saw."},"met:10448":{"d":"Firefighters battling a blaze at a house, in oil on slate paper, 15.2 by 23.2 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a house, a tree, a fire, men, a ladder and firefighters, so the picture shows men with a ladder at a burning building. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of the city's sights, and it shows a dangerous event instead of a quiet street type.","i":"Until 1865 New York's fires were fought by volunteer companies, whose members hauled hand-pumped engines, formed bucket lines and often competed for the honor of reaching a fire first. Firemen were neighborhood heroes and sometimes brawlers, and the companies were social clubs as well as emergency services. Chappel, who grew up when the system was at its height, remembers them at work.","c":"A fire is the most dramatic of the street subjects, and the temptation is to light it with theatrical flame and smoke. Chappel's plain manner may under-play the drama, but that restraint gives the scene the credibility of an eyewitness account. The little picture should be judged by how clearly the ladder, the men and the burning house tell the story, and by the sense of effort."},"met:10449":{"d":"Firemen washing in a street, in oil on slate paper, 15.2 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include firefighters, men, washing, work and a street, and classify the picture as a genre painting, so the scene shows volunteer firefighters at a routine chore, probably cleaning their engine, in a city street. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it shows the firemen between emergencies, in a lighter mood than a picture of the fire itself.","i":"Volunteer fire companies kept their hand engines polished like treasures, and a washing day was probably a regular occasion on which the men cleaned and displayed their apparatus, often with a neighborhood audience. The companies took great pride in the look of their engines, which were decorated with paintings and brass. Chappel's scene records the social, almost ceremonial side of the firemen's life.","c":"A picture of men washing and polishing is a modest subject, but it shows Chappel's eye for the companionable routine of the old city: the work, the chatter, the pride in equipment. The humor is gentle, and the picture's value is that it records ordinary labor, not the heroics of fires. A viewer should look at how the figures are grouped around the task."},"met:10450":{"d":"The Fly Market, a public market in lower Manhattan, in oil on slate paper, 15.2 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's keywords record a market, a building, men and women, so the picture shows shoppers and sellers at the market building. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of the old city's sights, and it names one of its best-known public places.","i":"The Fly Market, whose name comes from the Dutch word for a valley or marsh, stood in lower Manhattan near the East River and was one of the city's chief food markets in the early nineteenth century, selling meat, fish and produce, before the larger Fulton Market replaced it in the 1820s. Chappel, a native New Yorker, remembered it as a crowded, noisy center of daily life.","c":"A market is a gift to a painter of crowds, and the hope is for bustle and variety; Chappel's small size and plain style limit that, giving a tidy, readable overview instead of a seething scene. The strength is the sense of a named, real place with real shoppers; the weakness is the thinness of atmosphere. It is a useful visual note about a market that no longer exists."},"met:10451":{"d":"A garbage cart drawn by a horse along a road past houses and a tree, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The Met's keywords record a cart, a horse, a road, houses and a tree, so the picture shows the collection of the city's refuse. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of the city's daily life, including its humbler and less picturesque tasks.","i":"In the early nineteenth century New York had no regular system of garbage collection; refuse was dumped in streets, carted away by private or contracted carters, and eaten by roaming pigs and dogs. A garbage cart was a part of the street scene that most nineteenth-century artists left out, and Chappel's choice to include it shows his wish to remember the whole city and not only its pretty parts.","c":"Few painters would choose a garbage cart as a subject, and the interest of the picture is its honesty: the cart and horse are painted with the same plain care as a coach or a market. The picture is not beautiful, and does not try to be; it is a piece of social documentation by a man who treated every part of city life as worth recording. That, rather than design, is its merit."},"met:10452":{"d":"A street scene of the sale of hot corn, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.3 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list houses, a woman, a man, a road, a carriage and an urban landscape, so the picture shows a seller and presumably a customer in a city street, with a carriage passing. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small, nearly identical-sized pictures of street types and trades.","i":"Boiled ears of corn sold hot from a pail or cart were a cheap summer street food in nineteenth-century New York, hawked by poor sellers, often girls and women, who cried 'Hot corn!' through the evening streets. The trade was so familiar that it gave its name to a popular book of 1850s New York life scenes. Chappel's picture preserves the cry and the sight.","c":"The seller and her wares are small figures in a street, and the picture's pleasure is anecdotal: a trade, a time of day, a street. Chappel handles the little scene with the plainness that marks the whole series, and the viewer should look for how clearly the vendor stands out. It lacks the grace of a trained genre painter, but the humble subject finds its dignity in being remembered."},"met:10453":{"d":"A house raising in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a house, a horse, a tree, men and work, so the picture shows men at work on a building, with a horse nearby. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is a communal scene among his small pictures of the old city and its outskirts, and it shows people cooperating at a task instead of a lone street type.","i":"A house raising was a community event in early America: neighbors gathered to lift the timber frame of a new building into place, using poles and ropes, and the owner provided food and drink. In the early nineteenth century parts of upper Manhattan and the outskirts were still rural, and such cooperative labor was part of the city's edges. Chappel's scene recalls a way of building and of neighborliness that was fading.","c":"A house raising is a gift to a painter of figures in cooperation, with bodies at angles, ropes taut and a frame rising, and the viewer should judge how well Chappel conveys the common effort. His plain, small manner can make the scene feel like a diagram, but it also gives it a sense of communal labor remembered with affection. As a record of work it is more convincing than as an image of drama."},"met:10454":{"d":"A funeral procession for a child, in oil on slate paper, 15.4 by 23.3 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a woman, a man, a procession and a funeral, and the word infant in the title tells the viewer that the mourners are following the small coffin of a baby. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is a companion in subject to his adult funeral procession, a quiet record of a custom.","i":"In the nineteenth century many children died young, and funerals for infants had their own customs: a small coffin, often white, carried by hand or in a carriage, with a few relatives and friends following, and white ribbons or gloves in place of black. Chappel's picture records a sad but common sight of the old city with restraint and without sentiment.","c":"A subject like this invites sentimentality, and the best thing about Chappel's version, judging from his manner in the series, is its plain, matter-of-fact tone. The small procession, set down simply, carries the feeling by understatement. It is not a polished picture, but it is one of the more affecting in the series, because the facts are enough."},"met:10455":{"d":"A lamplighter with a ladder at night in front of a building, in oil on slate paper, 15.4 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list a man, a building, night and a ladder, so the picture shows the lamplighter at his task of lighting a street lamp as darkness falls. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small, nearly identical-sized pictures of the city's street trades and sights.","i":"Lamplighters walked the streets at dusk with ladders, lighting oil lamps, and later gas lamps, introduced in New York in the 1820s, and returning to snuff them at dawn. A night scene is a change from daytime street types, and the lamplighter, who brought light to the dark city, is among the most poetic of the old city's trades, now vanished with the arrival of electric lighting.","c":"A night scene allows a painter to try something different, a pool of lamplight against darkness, and the lamplighter is a more picturesque figure than most street types. Chappel's plain manner suits the subject's quiet poetry, and the viewer should look at how the light from the lamp is managed in so small a picture. It is a pleasing, atmospheric exception in a series otherwise concerned with the day."},"met:10456":{"d":"Militia drilling in a landscape, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The museum's keywords note horses, riders, a landscape and a flag of the United States, so the picture shows mounted men and citizen-soldiers on a training ground under the national flag. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is an open-air scene, more landscape than street, in a series otherwise concerned with the city's trades and sights.","i":"In the early republic, able-bodied men were required to serve in the militia and to turn out on training days for drill, which were often disorderly and festive occasions, with more drinking and joking than discipline. The citizen-soldier was a subject of both patriotic pride and satire, and by the 1870s, after the Civil War's professional armies, the old militia drill was a comic memory.","c":"A militia training day is a subject with built-in comedy, since such drills tended to be ragged, and Chappel presents it with plain affection. The flags and horses give color, and the open ground gives space that his street scenes lack. It will not impress by technique, but as a record of a vanished civic ritual it is amusing and informative."},"met:10457":{"d":"A ferry landing in oil on slate paper, 15.4 by 23.2 centimeters. The Met's keywords record a boat, a dock and a carriage, so the picture shows the old stairs at a ferry landing on the water's edge, with boats and a carriage at the dock. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of the old city, and it recalls the way people crossed the East River before the Brooklyn Bridge.","i":"Before the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, ferries were the only way across the East River, and the Fulton Ferry, steam-powered from 1814, carried passengers, wagons and carriages between Manhattan and Brooklyn. The landing stairs were the point of contact between city and water, crowded with travelers and carters. Chappel recalls the old ferry of his youth, a sight that vanished when the bridge took its traffic.","c":"A ferry landing is a place of motion and meeting, and even a small picture of it can suggest the bustle: boats, carriage, steps and water. Chappel's plain handling gives it a clear, schematic structure. The picture's interest is more topographical than painterly, a record of a lost waterfront, and a viewer should look at how the stairs structure the space."},"met:10458":{"d":"A sewer in a town street, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The title names a municipal drain, and the Met's keywords add a horse, a wagon, a man and a town, so the picture shows this piece of city infrastructure with the traffic of the street. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of the old city that include its humblest civic undertakings.","i":"New York's early sewers were few and often just open drains or covered gutters; as the city grew, they became a necessity and a source of constant complaint. A sewer was an unglamorous subject, and Chappel's choice of it shows his memory of the city as a place of public works and daily annoyances: the underground, unseen part of city life brought into view for a moment.","c":"A sewer is hardly a promising subject, and the picture's merit is its refusal to flinch: the drain, wagon and horse are treated as seriously as a market or a church. The painting is plain, and the viewer might wish for more atmosphere, but the subject's honesty is its charm. As an example of Chappel's democratic eye for city life, it is hard to better."},"met:10459":{"d":"A strawberry peddler at a house, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a house, a man, a woman, strawberries and a dog, so the picture shows a seller presumably offering fruit to a woman at her door, with a dog nearby. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of street vendors in the old city.","i":"Strawberries were an early-summer delicacy hawked through city streets by sellers from the countryside around New York, who called their wares from door to door. Fresh fruit and vegetables arrived by cart and boat from farms in New Jersey, Long Island and Westchester, and vendors made the city's markets reach into its streets. Chappel, remembering the old city, evidently found these cries and sights as characteristic as its buildings.","c":"A strawberry seller is a pleasant, gentle subject, and the picture's appeal is the freshness of the season it records, presumably the red of the fruit against the green of summer. Chappel handles it with his usual plainness, and the dog adds a touch of domestic life. It is slight, even trivial, but charming, an unpretentious memory of an early-summer morning in the old city."},"met:10460":{"d":"A tea party in a street setting, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.7 centimeters. The Met's subject tags list trees, a house, a street, a man and a woman, so the scene shows a social gathering with the house as its backdrop. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures, and it is among the gentler, more social subjects in a series of street trades and civic sights.","i":"Tea drinking was the center of polite social life in nineteenth-century America, and a tea party might be an evening visit, a neighborhood gathering or a holiday outing. Chappel's picture records a social custom rather than a trade: the town's people at leisure, with the house as a setting for conversation. The scene belongs to the world of the early republic, when tea and visiting were the glue of neighborhood life.","c":"A tea party is a quiet subject, and the picture depends on the plain arrangement of figures and house. Chappel's modest skill gives it a naive, direct charm, but little spatial subtlety. The pleasure is in the social mood, a remembered ease, and viewers should enjoy it as a period vignette, not a work of formal art."},"met:10461":{"d":"An upright scene in oil on slate paper, 23.2 by 15.2 centimeters, unlike the wide format of most of the series. The title joins two things, tea rusk, a dry sweet bread eaten with tea, and a brick house, and the Met's keywords record a house, a man and a woman. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it recalls a seller or a household where tea rusk was part of the scene.","i":"Tea rusk was a staple of the teatime table in early America, and bakers and street sellers offered it with other bread. A brick house in a city of mostly wooden buildings could be a mark of prosperity and a defense against fire, so the title pairs a humble food with a sign of comfort. Chappel's memories of the old city linked such details of daily life into small, specific pictures.","c":"The upright format is unusual in the series and gives the picture a different rhythm, with a house standing tall and figures near its foot. It is slight and plain, and the viewer should look at how the vertical shape is used. As a record of a corner of the old city it is as charming as the wider pictures, and a little more surprising."},"met:10462":{"d":"A water cart at a pump, in oil on slate paper, 15.4 by 23.7 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a house, a man, a horse, a barrel and a carriage, so the picture shows the tea-water pump of the title with a horse-drawn cart and barrels. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of the old city, and it records a vanished source of drinking water.","i":"Much of Manhattan's well water was brackish and unfit for tea, so for years the city drew its best drinking water from a famous spring-fed pump near Chatham Street, and sellers carted barrels of it around the town. The tea-water pump was one of the old city's landmarks until the Croton system brought public water in 1842, and Chappel remembered the carts and barrels as part of daily life.","c":"A water cart and pump are modest subjects, but they carry social history: a time when drinking water had to be bought by the barrel. Chappel's plain manner lets the barrels, horse and cart make the point. The picture is not beautiful, but it is richly informative, and its title alone, tea-water pump, is a small poem of vanished daily life."},"met:10463":{"d":"A baker's wagon in a street, in oil on slate paper, 15.6 by 23.5 centimeters. The Met's catalog tags include a house, a tree, a man and a street scene, so the picture shows the baker delivering bread by wagon in a city street with a house and a tree. Painted in the 1870s by William P. Chappel, it is one of a series of small pictures of street trades and sights of the old city.","i":"Bakers delivered bread and cakes door to door in the early nineteenth century, by wagon, handcart or basket, announcing themselves with a bell or a cry. The baker was among the street figures every household knew, and the daily appearance of the bread wagon marked the rhythm of the day. Chappel, born in 1801, painted such scenes in his seventies as memories of a city he knew well.","c":"A baker's wagon is a cheerful subject, and Chappel's plain style makes the picture feel like a page from a childhood memory: the wagon, the man, the house. There is little drama, and the painting probably lacks spatial depth, but the warmth of the memory is evident. It is among the most domestic and likable of the series, and typical of its virtues and limits."},"met:14316":{"d":"An unidentified man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1813 and measuring 8.4 by 6.6 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers (1788-1844), who was then about twenty-five. The title calls him only a gentleman. At this size the ivory has room for a bust with some of the clothing, in the dress of the 1810s, the face built up in fine strokes through which the white of the ivory glows.","i":"1813 fell in the middle of the War of 1812, and miniatures were popular tokens for those separated from their families by service or travel. Early nineteenth-century American miniaturists worked in the shadow of Edward Greene Malbone, who died in 1807 and whose luminous, delicate style set the standard. An unnamed gentleman is a typical survivor of the many such likenesses whose sitters' names were lost.","c":"A competent American miniature of the 1810s: the face carries it, and a viewer should look for the luminous, finely stippled flesh tones that Malbone made the mark of quality. The unidentified gentleman and the neat format make it a good type specimen, and whether it rises above the type depends on the eyes. It is a modest, sound object whose interest is its craft and its date."},"met:11945":{"d":"A man named Jean Pierre Barre, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1820 and measuring 8.4 by 6.8 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The French form of the sitter's name stands out among American miniatures of the period, and the generous size gives the head and shoulders room, with the face built up in tiny strokes over the translucent ivory. The thin sheet of ivory gives the flesh tones a soft glow from within.","i":"A French name in an American miniature of about 1820 may point to the immigrant community of the port cities, where French merchants, teachers and refugees from the Revolution and from the slave uprising in Saint-Domingue had settled in numbers. Rogers, who painted many New Yorkers, recorded the city's merchants and their families, and a portrait like this preserves one of the many origins of its commercial society.","c":"A named sitter with a foreign name adds interest, but the ivory must still persuade as a likeness, and Rogers, one of the more accomplished American miniaturists of his day, usually gives sitters a clear, lively presence. The thing to look for is the glance and the delicate hatching of the flesh. At 8.4 centimeters it is a generous format, which lets the painter show off a practiced hand."},"met:11946":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1820, 7.5 by 6.0 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The title calls her only a lady. At this middling size the ivory has room for the head, the hair and the neckline of her dress, and the face is built up in fine strokes of watercolor through which the white of the ivory gives a soft glow.","i":"About 1820 women's fashions in the United States followed the high-waisted, softly draped styles that came from France and England, with hair dressed in curls, and a miniature records such details as well as a face. The woman's name was lost somewhere in the family's history; she survives as a type, an American lady of the early republic, composed for a husband, a parent or a friend.","c":"Rogers has a reputation among miniaturists for clear, fresh color and a steady, convincing likeness, and a lady of the early 1820s lets him show both. The test of such a portrait is the eyes: whether they have the slight asymmetry of life. At a medium size it has room for a little display but relies on the face. It is a sound, attractive example of a leading hand."},"met:17075":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1820, 7.0 by 5.7 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The title calls him only a gentleman. The sitter is shown at bust length, the face finished in fine strokes and the dark coat and ground treated more broadly, a convention that keeps attention on the head. At 7.0 centimeters tall it is slightly smaller than a playing card, made to be held close and seen at arm's length.","i":"Rogers's men of about 1820 belong to the generation that built the American republic's commercial and professional life: merchants, lawyers, doctors. A miniature of such a man might be given to a wife, kept by a mother or passed to a child, and gentleman in the title records social standing as much as gender. His name is lost, but the likeness has survived in the care of families and collectors.","c":"Male sitters give a miniaturist less to play with than women, with dark coat, white linen and little jewelry, so the picture lives on the face. Rogers is good at the quiet individuality of a man's features, the set of the jaw, the intelligence of the eye, and this ivory is a fair test of that gift. It is unshowy but convincing, a likeness rather than a performance."},"met:11944":{"d":"A man named Humphry Howland, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1822 and measuring 7.3 by 5.7 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The variant spelling of the first name, Humphry, is a sign of family tradition, and the sitter is shown at bust length, the face carefully modeled and the clothes simplified so that the head is the center of the small image.","i":"A named portrait lets a miniature do what it was made for, preserve a particular person's face for his family. Rogers's sitters included many members of New York's mercantile families, and a portrait like this was the kind of keepsake kept in a case, given at a marriage or on leaving for a voyage. The sitter's surname, Howland, is one associated with American maritime commerce.","c":"Named portraits ask more of a miniaturist than anonymous ones, since a family will compare the likeness to the face it knew. Rogers was valued for clarity and likeness, and the proof is in the modeled planes of the face and the steadiness of the gaze. This ivory, at 7.3 centimeters, is conventional in format but has the clarity for which he was admired."},"met:11943":{"d":"Cornelia Paterson, Mrs. Stephen Van Rensselaer III, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1825 and measuring 8.3 by 6.2 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The title gives her married name first and her maiden name in parentheses. The ivory is relatively large, allowing a bust with some dress and a carefully modeled face. The Met also holds a miniature of the same sitter by Robert Fulton, of about 1810, so the two give a rare chance to compare how two painters saw one face fifteen years apart.","i":"Stephen Van Rensselaer III (1764-1839) was the last patroon, head of the vast Rensselaerswyck estate on the Hudson, and in 1824 he founded the school that became Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. His second wife, Cornelia Paterson, was the daughter of William Paterson, the New Jersey statesman and Supreme Court justice. This miniature, of about 1825, was made the year after the school's founding, in the household of one of the state's wealthiest families.","c":"Rogers's portraits of the Van Rensselaer circle show his standing: he painted the wealthy families of the Hudson and New York, and a wife of the patroon would not have chosen a mere journeyman. The skill lies in a clear, refined likeness that conveys position without display. At 8.3 centimeters the ivory is generous, giving the face room to breathe."},"met:14403":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1825, 6.8 by 5.4 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The title calls him only a gentleman. It is a pocket-sized ivory, small enough for a locket or a closed case, with the sitter's head and shoulders set in a compact frame and the face carrying the whole. Ivory was valued because its smooth, pale surface keeps watercolor bright and lets light travel through the paint and back, which gives flesh a glow that paper cannot.","i":"The mid-1820s were a good time for miniature painters in New York: the city was growing fast after the Erie Canal opened in 1825, and its merchants and professionals wanted likenesses to mark their rising prosperity. A modest ivory like this was within reach of a prosperous clerk or a rising professional. Its sitter, now anonymous, stands for the many men who made New York an economic center.","c":"At 6.8 centimeters there is no room for ambition, and the portrait depends on the sharpness of the likeness. Rogers's economy, a few clear planes and a bright, steady eye, is on show in his small ivories as in the larger ones, and the best of them have a presence out of proportion to their size. Judge this one by how quickly the face engages and how long it holds the eye."},"met:15170":{"d":"A man named John Ludlow Morton, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1829 and measuring 7.5 by 5.5 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. It is a medium-sized upright ivory, bust-length, with the face worked in fine strokes through which the white of the ivory glows, and the dress and background kept simpler so that the head is the center of attention.","i":"The sitter is named, which gives the miniature a biographical anchor: a New Yorker of the late 1820s who commissioned or was given a likeness at a time when the miniature remained the elegant standard for portraits of friends and family. Names such as Morton and Ludlow belong to old New York families, and a portrait with the full name preserves that line.","c":"A named sitter and a date of about 1829 place the miniature in Rogers's best period, when his style was most assured: crisp contours, clear light and a firm sense of structure. The upright format is conventional and the interest is in the face; the viewer should look at the modeling around the eyes and the mouth, where a miniaturist's skill is most severely tested."},"met:15306":{"d":"A father and his young son, Ferdinand Sands and Joseph, painted in watercolor on ivory in the early 1830s and measuring 9.8 by 7.3 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. A double portrait is more demanding than a single, since two heads must share a small ivory, and the unusually large size, close to ten centimeters tall, gives them room. Both sitters are named in the title, so the picture records a particular father and son rather than a type.","i":"Father-and-son portraits expressed continuity: the father's authority and the boy's promise as heir to a name and a business. In the early 1830s, when the American middle and merchant classes were expanding, such portraits were also a way of recording the relationship at a moment when a boy's childhood was brief. The format, less common among miniatures, suggests a special commission.","c":"Two sitters on one small ivory test a painter's composition as well as his skill with likenesses: the heads must be related without crowding, with the glances and the poses telling the relationship. Rogers had the skill to attempt it, and the double portrait is among the more interesting of his formats. The viewer should look at how father and son relate: touching, looking, or each alone."},"met:14522":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1835, 7.2 by 6.0 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The title calls him only a gentleman. The ivory is relatively broad for its height, which gives the head and shoulders a slightly squarer frame than the narrow ovals of earlier miniatures, and the face is the most finished part. A man of the 1830s would typically be shown in a dark coat with a high collar and a stock or cravat, details a viewer can use to confirm the decade.","i":"By the mid-1830s American miniatures were becoming broader and more picture-like, set in rectangular frames, as painters tried to hold their own against the coming of the daguerreotype in 1839. A gentleman of about 1835, anonymous now, belongs to the last confident decade of the American miniature, when a hand-painted ivory was still the usual way to record the face of a prosperous man.","c":"Rogers in the 1830s paints with an assured, slightly crisper touch than in his earlier work, and a mid-1830s gentleman is among the best tests of that style. The painter's skill is in the quiet individuality of the features: the angle of the nose, the set of the lips, a hint of humor or of weariness around the eyes. Judge this ivory by how much of a person it contains."},"met:14523":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1835, 6.8 by 5.5 centimeters, by Nathaniel Rogers. The title calls him only a gentleman. A pocket-sized upright ivory, it shows the sitter at bust length, the head set within a compact frame and the white of the ivory glowing through the thin paint of the face. Such an ivory would have been mounted behind glass in a small case or frame, protected from touch and light, which is why many survive with their color nearly as fresh as when they were painted.","i":"A small ivory like this might have been commissioned as a gift for a wife or a fiancée, or kept by the sitter's mother; miniatures were exchanged at engagements and marriages, and carried on journeys. The gentleman of the title is any one of the men who sat for such keepsakes in the years before photography took over their function, and the portrait's value lies in its personal, private character.","c":"The smaller the ivory the more a painter must rely on a few decisive touches, and Rogers had a gift for them. In this compact portrait the eyes and the line of the mouth carry the likeness, and the rest is a quiet frame of dark coat and plain ground. It is a good example of his economy: nothing superfluous, everything pointed toward the face."},"met:15163":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 6.4 by 5.2 centimeters, one of the smaller ivories among his portraits. No date is recorded. The title calls him only a gentleman. At this size the image is small enough to sit in a closed hand, a head and shoulders made to fit a case or a locket.","i":"Rogers worked from the 1810s into the 1840s, a span in which American taste moved from the restrained elegance of the Federal period to the fuller dress and larger formats of the Jacksonian years. Without a date the portrait can be placed only by style and costume, a common difficulty with miniatures, which were seldom dated. The sitter's anonymity is typical of the many gentlemen he painted.","c":"A small, undated portrait of an unnamed man asks to be judged on the face alone, and Rogers's strengths are the right ones for that test: clear modeling, a bright eye and an economy that makes the sitter present at a glance. At 6.4 centimeters, with no costume or setting to help, the success is that of a likeness stripped to essentials."},"met:15164":{"d":"A woman named Matilda Few, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers and measuring 7.1 by 5.8 centimeters. No date is recorded. The sitter's name survives, giving the miniature a particular identity, and the picture shows her at bust length, with the face carefully stippled and the dress and ground treated more simply. Few is an uncommon surname, which makes the name an unusually useful clue for anyone tracing the sitter's family.","i":"A named sitter is the exception among the many unnamed miniatures, and the miniature's value lies in that particular identity: a woman called Matilda Few, preserved in a form meant to be held and kept. Such portraits were commissioned for husbands, parents or suitors, and the name on a family tree or the back of a case is what keeps the likeness a person instead of a type.","c":"With a name attached the ivory invites comparison with the unnamed ones around it, and the thing to look for is whether Rogers gives Matilda Few a specific personality, in the glance and the mouth, or a general feminine charm. He usually manages the first. The softness of the flesh tones and the delicate treatment of hair and collar are the typical pleasures of a woman's miniature by him."},"met:15165":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 7.4 by 5.8 centimeters, undated. The title calls him only a gentleman. The upright ivory is of middle size, big enough for a clear bust and some dress, and the face, built in tiny strokes of watercolor over the translucent surface, is the part to which the painter gave the most care.","i":"Miniatures of men in the 1820s and 1830s were commissioned for a variety of occasions: marriages, departures, honors, memorials and simple family record. An anonymous gentleman can be any of these, and without documentation a viewer can only see the face and imagine the occasion. The loss of the name does not diminish the miniature's attraction as a portrait of an individual from the early republic.","c":"Rogers's men have a particular quality, an air of alert, practical intelligence rather than idealization, and this undated ivory is typical. The painter's skill is in letting the sitter's character appear through plain means: the careful modeling of the brow and the clear, direct look. It is a small document of a man who is no longer named but remains visible, and that is what a good miniature achieves."},"met:15166":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 7.6 by 6.0 centimeters, undated. The title calls her only a lady. It is a fairly large ivory, with space for the head, the hairstyle and the neckline of her dress, and the face is built in tiny strokes of watercolor that let the ivory's whiteness glow through. With no date and no name, the object itself, its size, its medium and its handling, is all that speaks for the sitter.","i":"Women's miniatures by Rogers vary with the decade, from the high waists of the early 1820s to the broad sleeves and elaborate hair of the 1830s, and a viewer can often place them by the hairstyle and the neckline. Here there is no date, and the loss of the name leaves the lady a type: an American woman of the early republic, painted to be kept by her family.","c":"Women's miniatures give a painter more to do than men's, with hair, lace and jewelry, and the risk is that these accessories steal attention from the face. Rogers keeps them in order, and the test is whether the lady herself emerges. The size, 7.6 centimeters, is generous. It is a sound, graceful example of his work, valued mainly as a portrait of a stranger who still looks back."},"met:15167":{"d":"An unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers and undated, in the same dimensions, 7.6 by 6.0 centimeters, as another lady by him at the Met. The title calls her only a lady. The ivory is bust-length, with the head and the neckline of her dress finished with care and the ground kept simple, so that the face and the frame of her hair are the two things the eye comes back to.","i":"Women's miniatures were often given to husbands or fiancés, worn on the person or kept in a case, and many in collections have lost every inscription that once named the sitter. The anonymous lady of this ivory is typical of that loss, and the portrait survives as an image of an early nineteenth-century American woman, composed and alert, painted to be held close.","c":"Hair is a painter's opportunity in a woman's miniature: the curls and parting can be built with fine lines that contrast with the stippled flesh. Rogers handles such passages with care, and the viewer should look at how the hair frames the face and leads the eye back to it. The portrait is undated and unnamed, but the quality of the detail, if sustained, makes it a fine specimen of his work."},"met:15169":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 7.2 by 5.9 centimeters, undated. The title calls him only a gentleman. The portrait shows the sitter at bust length, typically against a plain or softly graded ground, the face finished in tiny, even strokes and the dark clothing kept simple, a conventional layout that Rogers uses with variations in many of his male portraits.","i":"Anonymous men's miniatures like this one now serve as a record of a type: the respectable American man of the early republic, in dark clothes and white linen, with a composed expression. Whether a merchant, a lawyer or a physician, he chose a miniature as the form of his portrait because it was personal, durable and affordable. The sitter's name has not survived; his face has.","c":"In a series of undated gentlemen by the same hand, the pleasure lies in small differences: the shape of the head, the angle of the glance, the fall of the hair. Rogers makes each face individual within the limits of a standard format, and this ivory is one of the quietly successful ones. Its virtue is the persuasive steadiness of the look; its limitation is the plainness of everything else."},"met:15171":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 7.3 by 5.7 centimeters, undated. The title calls him only a gentleman. The image is a bust, with the white of the ivory showing through the thin color of the face and the clothes and background laid in more broadly, a technique that suits the small scale and keeps the face luminous.","i":"Miniatures were prized for their intimacy: they could be held close, looked at privately and kept in a drawer or a pocket. A gentleman's miniature was often a gift to a wife or parent and a private token. The anonymity of this sitter is the usual fate of such tokens, which passed through families and dealers and shed their documentation.","c":"A miniature of this size is judged by its restraint, and Rogers shows his. The face is built from fine strokes, the coloring is clean and the expression composed, with no flourish to distract. It will not startle anyone, but it sustains a long look, which is the best that can be said of a small portrait of an unknown man made nearly two centuries ago."},"met:15172":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 6.8 by 5.4 centimeters, undated. The title calls him only a gentleman. It is a compact, pocket-sized upright ivory, with the sitter's head and shoulders filling the small space and the face given the greatest finish, in the quiet, even manner of the artist's male portraits. Its measurements match those of another gentleman by Rogers at the Met, dated about 1825, a reminder that ivory miniatures came in a handful of standard sizes.","i":"In the decades before photography, a portrait miniature was a family's way of keeping the face of a son, husband or father at hand; for travelers, soldiers and sailors, it was a way of carrying home with them. Many such miniatures were worn as jewelry or kept in cases lined with silk or velvet. The present sitter's name has not survived; his likeness, kept in a case, has.","c":"A compact ivory like this must make its case quickly, and the best of Rogers's small portraits do: a few clear planes in the face, a bright, steady eye, and a calm that suggests the sitter had a firm view of himself. If this one seems slightly formulaic beside the larger, named portraits, it still shows the discipline of a painter who knew how to get the essentials right."},"met:15168":{"d":"A young girl, painted in watercolor on ivory by Nathaniel Rogers, 6.9 by 5.5 centimeters, undated. The title records only her age and sex. Children's miniatures concentrate on the face, with its soft contours and open look, and the clothing and background are kept simple, so that the small sitter's head is the one clear subject of the ivory. Children's likenesses were among the most treasured miniatures, often kept long after the sitter had grown up or died.","i":"Portraits of children were made as keepsakes for parents and grandparents in an age when many children died young, and some were made as memorials. A girl's miniature might also record a birthday, a school-age milestone or a family occasion, kept in a drawer or a locket. The child's name is lost, and the miniature survives as an image of childhood in the early republic.","c":"Children are among the hardest sitters for a miniaturist: unformed features, restless expressions and parents who expect sweetness. Rogers's exacting technique suits the problem, since fine stippling can give the softness of a child's cheek without the blur of sentimentality. Whether this one succeeds depends on whether the gaze is alive; the best children in his work have a candor that has outlasted nearly two centuries."},"met:15062":{"d":"A man identified as Mr. Pierce is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 6.7 by 5.4 centimeters, dated 1843, by the American painter Augustus Fuller. The title gives only his surname and courtesy title, and the recorded motifs name a man and a portrait. The miniature is small enough to hold in the hand or be set in a case, and the ivory support gives the flesh tones a faint inner glow.","i":"By 1843 the daguerreotype, announced only four years earlier, was spreading rapidly through American towns and cities, and painted miniatures were becoming a premium alternative, valued for their color, their softness and the touch of the artist. Fuller, who was about thirty, worked in an American tradition of plain, honest portraiture. A sitter known only as Mr. Pierce is typical of the ordinary citizens who sat for such painters, a reminder that the portrait was by now a middle-class habit.","c":"A modest miniature, but a clear one: the head is carefully drawn and the flesh tones delicate. It does not have the polish of the best miniaturists, but the plain directness is appealing, and the likeness feels honest. In an age when photography was beginning to offer instant likenesses, it asserts the value of a painter's touch."},"met:15063":{"d":"A boy is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 5.7 by 4.7 centimeters by Augustus Fuller. The museum gives no date, and the title records no name. The recorded motifs name only a boy and a portrait, so the picture is a small, anonymous likeness of a child, probably made to be kept close by his family, in a size that fits a pocket, a case or a drawer.","i":"Portraits of boys were precious in nineteenth-century households, recording a child at an age when change came quickly and when many did not reach adulthood. The miniature, small enough to be carried or kept in a drawer, would have been a family treasure, handed down with other keepsakes, and the loss of the boy's name is a reminder of how such objects were separated from the family papers that once identified them.","c":"A child's portrait asks for freshness and a face that is unforced, and the soft translucency of ivory suits it. Fuller's modest, direct manner, if applied here as in his other miniatures, avoids prettiness and lets the boy's own face carry the picture. With no name or date it stands only on its painting, and the plain honesty of such a likeness is its appeal."},"met:436223":{"d":"A ship stands off a beach while the shore end of a telegraph cable is brought to land, in oil on a canvas of 57.2 by 83.8 centimeters, dated 1866. The Met's keywords record a ship, the ocean, a beach and the transatlantic telegraph cable. The subject is a modern engineering feat treated as a marine view, with sea, shore and vessels of the 1860s, painted by an artist who made a series of pictures of the cable expeditions.","i":"In 1866 the Great Eastern, the largest ship in the world, laid the first lasting telegraph cable across the Atlantic, linking Ireland and Newfoundland; earlier cables had failed, the first in 1858 after a few weeks and another lost mid-ocean in 1865. Messages that had taken ten days by steamer now took minutes. The landing of the cable's shore end was a moment of public celebration, and pictures like this fixed it in memory.","c":"A cable landing is hard to make dramatic, since the main event is a rope dragged up a beach, and Dudley turns it into a marine picture with ships, sea and sky, giving the engineering feat a setting of nautical grandeur. The strength is the sense of historic occasion and the competence of the vessels; the limit may be a certain journalistic flatness. As a record of a pivotal moment of nineteenth-century technology it is a valuable image."},"met:436224":{"d":"Men wait in an interior, and the title supplies the drama: a reply is expected along the transatlantic telegraph cable. The picture is a cabinet-size oil on canvas, about 59 by 85 centimeters, one of a group by Robert Charles Dudley on the laying of the cable in the mid-1860s. The group was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1892 by Cyrus W. Field, the American businessman who promoted the line, and it keeps the memory of a project that took more than a decade to succeed.","i":"The Atlantic cable had a hard history. The line of 1858 failed within weeks, and in 1865 a new cable parted and was lost in mid-ocean. In 1866 the Great Eastern, the largest ship afloat, laid another from Ireland to Newfoundland and then recovered the lost one. A picture of men waiting for an answer catches the nerves behind the engineering. For Victorian viewers, a message that crossed an ocean in minutes was among the wonders of the age.","c":"This set is reportage first, and its smartest decision is the choice of moment: a silent wait carries the suspense that a view of ship and sea cannot. As painting the series is competent rather than inspired, in the tradition of the Victorian marine and topical picture, where accuracy came before invention. Its value is as a vivid record of a technological triumph, and Dudley is remembered chiefly for it."},"met:436225":{"d":"A ship rides the open Atlantic while grapnels are dragged along the sea bed for the cable that parted and sank in 1865. This oil on canvas, about 58 by 84 centimeters, is one of Robert Charles Dudley's group on the cable expeditions, given to the Metropolitan Museum by Cyrus W. Field in 1892. Its subject is an operation that no one could watch directly: iron hooks on long ropes feeling for a wire more than two miles down.","i":"After the cable broke in 1865, the Great Eastern came back the next summer, laid a fresh cable to Newfoundland, and then returned to the spot where the old one lay. In the usual account the grapnels came up empty or their ropes parted several times before the cable was hooked, lifted and completed. The result was two working lines instead of one. The episode is a story of patience and trial and error, and Victorian audiences enjoyed it as such.","c":"The climax of this story happens under the sea, and a painter can only show the ship above it. Dudley, a marine specialist, lets sea, sky and vessel carry the narrative, which is the honest solution, if not a dramatic one. It is documentary art, modest in size and in ambition, and it is more affecting for its subject, the stubborn recovery of something lost, than for any brilliance of handling."},"met:436226":{"d":"The Great Eastern under way is the subject, in the largest of Dudley's cable pictures: about 114 by 171 centimeters, roughly twice the height and width of the others in the group. The iron steamship, designed by Isambard Kingdom Brunel and the biggest vessel afloat in the 1860s, carried paddle wheels, a screw propeller and sails, and her many masts and tall funnels made her unmistakable. The title says she is heading home, her work presumably done.","i":"The Great Eastern was a commercial failure as a passenger liner, too large for the traffic of her day, and she found a second career laying cable, because only she could carry the whole length of line in her tanks. Her 1866 voyage was her great success. A portrait of the ship doubles as a portrait of the age: iron, steam and electricity, joined to bring America and Britain within minutes of each other.","c":"Size and subject give this picture authority over its companions. A long hull against sea and sky is familiar ground for a marine painter, and Dudley's task is to make the ship look like what she was, the largest thing ever put on the water. The result is capable and not adventurous, a portrait in the English marine tradition. The Great Eastern deserved a record, and this is a respectable one."},"met:436227":{"d":"Boats, a ship and a crowd gather near the shore for the arrival of the cable on Newfoundland, as the title and the museum's keywords indicate. The oil on canvas is about 58 by 84 centimeters, close in size to most of the others in Robert Charles Dudley's group. The 1866 line came ashore at Heart's Content, a small fishing village on Trinity Bay that became the western end of the Atlantic telegraph, and the picture presumably shows that landing.","i":"For Newfoundlanders the landing was an event as well as a spectacle: a quiet outport was about to be tied by wire to Europe. Once connected, the 1866 line carried news in minutes that had taken some ten days by steamer. A picture that includes a crowd reminds us that the cable was a project the public followed closely, and that its success was celebrated on both sides of the ocean.","c":"A crowd of small figures at a shoreline asks for the orderly, lively description that suits a marine and topical painter, and the subject lets Dudley vary the series with a land view and a human gathering. As a record it is welcome. As a painting it has the strengths and limits of the set: clear, informative, and more interested in what happened than in how paint can describe it."},"met:436228":{"d":"Men and a ship are at work on the joint named in the title, in an oil on canvas about 58 by 84 centimeters. A splice joined the heavily armored shore-end cable, laid where waves and anchors could cut it, to the lighter cable of the deep ocean. Robert Charles Dudley's picture is one of a group on the Atlantic cable at the Metropolitan Museum, all given by Cyrus W. Field in 1892, and it turns a technical operation into a scene of labor.","i":"A cable is only as good as its joins. Faults in the insulation could kill a line, and the early failures made engineers anxious about every foot of it. Splices were made by hand, with care and insulating compounds. Dudley's picture honors that skilled and unglamorous work, which seldom appeared in celebrations of the project's directors and investors, and shows what the great enterprise looked like at the scale of men's hands.","c":"A picture of a splice has little built-in poetry, and Dudley does well to give it a human scale. The subject is dry, and the painter does not pretend otherwise; he trusts that men at work and a ship in attendance will hold the eye. It is the least spectacular of the group and, for that reason, perhaps the most honest, a record of the work that actually made the cable run."},"met:15208":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, about 7 by 5.6 centimetres, painted in watercolour on a thin plaque of ivory around 1798. At this scale the face is only a few centimetres across, and miniaturists of the period built it from tiny touches of translucent colour, letting the white ivory glow through for the flesh tones. Such a likeness was small enough to hold in the palm, to be worn as a locket or kept in a case, and the sitter's dress was usually limited to a coat, collar and cravat.","i":"In the years around 1800 the miniature was one of the most popular forms of portrait in the United States, cheaper and more portable than an oil painting and well suited to a country of travelling merchants, officers and families spread over great distances. A man might give his likeness to a wife, a sweetheart or a parent, to be kept as a pledge of affection. The sitter here is not named, so the portrait is read as a type: a gentleman of the early republic.","c":"Benjamin Trott was a capable and busy miniaturist, though overshadowed by Edward Greene Malbone, the finest American practitioner of the art. A good miniature of this date should have a convincing likeness, clean drawing and a fresh flesh tint, and an anonymous portrait stands on those qualities alone. The format leaves no room for display or invention, so this one is best valued as a competent, representative record of its time."},"met:12814":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Charles Floyd, about 6.5 by 5.1 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1804. As with most miniatures, the sitter fills the small frame at bust length, usually with the head slightly turned and the face modelled in fine, translucent strokes, the ivory beneath showing through the paint. The picture is tiny enough to be carried in a pocket or set into a locket or case, and the sitter's name has survived with it.","i":"Because the name Charles Floyd is recorded, the miniature belongs to a particular life and family, though little more is known of him. Such portraits were made for personal reasons, as gifts, tokens of absence and mementos of the dead, and an ivory like this might have been given on an engagement, a departure or a marriage. The date of about 1804 falls in the first years of the nineteenth century, when miniature painters were busy in American cities.","c":"Within the narrow range of the genre, this is a sound, careful portrait. Trott had a neat touch with the brush, and the likeness is the thing: small features, a steady gaze and a clean outline. What is missing is the lyrical grace of the best American miniatures by Malbone, the sense of a living, breathing sitter. Even so, the picture is pleasing and has the immediacy of a keepsake made for people who knew the man."},"met:12815":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman known only as Mrs. Fox, about 7 by 5.6 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1805. Women in American miniatures of these years were usually shown at bust length in the high-waisted fashions of the day, with hair curled or gathered under a cap or ribbon, and the face drawn in delicate, translucent touches. It is small enough to have been worn as a pendant or kept in a case.","i":"In the early republic a married woman was normally recorded by her husband's surname, so Mrs. Fox's own first name has not survived with the picture. Miniatures of wives were often painted as gifts between spouses or for children, and a portrait like this might also serve as a memorial after her death. The miniature shows how women appeared as private, family figures in a period when portrait painting was a principal visual record of domestic life.","c":"A pleasant example of Trott's work, in which a woman's likeness is set down with a neat, uncomplicated touch. Ivory gives skin an inner light, and the medium flatters a female sitter. But the limits of the format are plain, and the portrait does not show the finer shading or the spirited characterisation of Malbone's best women. It is competent and charming rather than distinguished."},"met:12812":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, about 9 by 7.5 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1812. It is somewhat larger than many ivories of its type, a size that leaves room for a little more of the sitter's coat and neckcloth. As in most miniatures, thin washes and tiny strokes build the face, and the pale ivory beneath lends the flesh its glow.","i":"By 1812 American fashion had moved from the powdered hair and ruffles of the 1790s to plainer dress and natural hair, and miniatures record that change in the sitters' collars and neckcloths. The sitter is unnamed, so the portrait speaks of a type, a man of the new republic's professional or business class, but it also shows the demand for portraits that continued into the years of the War of 1812. Such a miniature was a personal object that belonged with letters and keepsakes.","c":"By this date Trott was a veteran, and his portraits of men have a steady, plain competence. The larger scale gives a little more room for modelling the face and for clear drawing of the features, and it is the clarity of the likeness that persuades. It lacks the lyrical softness of Malbone's best, who had died in 1807, and the sitter remains somewhat impersonal. A solid record rather than a revelation."},"met:14355":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Alexander Henry Durdin, about 7.6 by 6.1 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1810. The sitter is shown at bust length, as was usual, and the face is built up from tiny strokes of translucent colour over the pale ivory. Like others of its kind it could be framed as a keepsake, set in a locket or kept in a case, and the sitter's full name has survived with the picture.","i":"A named sitter makes a miniature more valuable as a record, since most were made for private use and lose their identity as families disperse. Alexander Henry Durdin's name has stayed with the picture, which makes it a record of a particular person rather than a type. It was probably made for his family or a close friend, and its date, around 1810, falls in the busy years of American miniature painting before photography.","c":"A neat, plain portrait in Trott's customary manner: a firm outline, a clear face and uncomplicated colour. The small ivory leaves little room for individual flourish, and Trott does not attempt it. Where he is least successful is in giving the sitter life beyond a good likeness, and one wishes for Malbone's softness; where he succeeds is in the simple sincerity that made such pictures treasured as keepsakes."},"met:12813":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Benjamin Kintzing, about 7.4 by 5.9 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1815. It shows the fine technique typical of Trott: the face built from tiny strokes of translucent colour, the pale ivory glowing through the flesh tones, the sitter shown at bust length. The picture came to the Metropolitan Museum by bequest in 1955 from Mary E. Kintzing, who shared the sitter's surname, which suggests that it descended in his family.","i":"A miniature that appears to have stayed in the sitter's family for generations has done what it was made to do: it kept a likeness among those who loved him. Benjamin Kintzing is named, but little else is recorded about him, so the portrait stands as a record of an individual of the early republic. Its date, around 1815, falls just after the War of 1812, a time of growing prosperity in American towns and cities when portraiture flourished.","c":"A modest but engaging portrait. Trott's habit of clear outline and unfussy colour is at work, and the details of face and neckcloth are managed with care. He never reaches the gentle luminosity of Malbone, but his sitters tend to have a candour that makes them credible. The picture is best prized as a family treasure that has survived with its identity."},"met:12818":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Rebekah Biddle, wife of Nathaniel Chapman, about 8 by 6.5 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1815. The Met's keywords record lace, a collar and a shawl among her clothing, the fine accessories through which a miniature signals a woman's taste and means. The face is built from small, translucent strokes over the pale ivory, and the title records both her maiden name, Biddle, and her married name.","i":"Recording a woman's maiden name along with her married one was a way of naming her family as well as her husband, and Biddle was one of the best-known families of Philadelphia. The name Nathaniel Chapman matches that of the prominent Philadelphia physician and first president of the American Medical Association, which may identify her husband. A miniature like this could be exchanged between spouses, kept by children or worn on a ribbon.","c":"Lace and a white collar are a stern test in a medium whose ground is itself pale, since the painter must model white against white with the faintest shading. A miniaturist of Trott's reliable if limited skill would meet it with neat, careful touches rather than bravura. The portrait's chief charm is therefore not brilliance but fidelity: a patient record of a woman's face and dress, small, accurate and made to be held."},"met:15209":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Benjamin Chew Wilcocks, about 8.9 by 6.8 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1810. It is a comparatively large ivory, with room for the head and shoulders to be handled with a little breadth, and the face is built from fine strokes of translucent colour, the pale ivory showing through the flesh. The sitter's full name, with a family name as his middle name, has survived with the picture.","i":"Giving a child his mother's or grandmother's family name as a middle name was a common American custom, and a name such as Benjamin Chew Wilcocks points to a family with a lineage worth recording. A miniature was a personal object, made for relatives or a sweetheart and kept in a case or locket, but a named example also serves as a small piece of family history. Gloria Manney gave it to the Metropolitan Museum in 2006.","c":"A sound, careful likeness in Trott's plain style. The larger ivory gives him room to draw the features clearly, and the clarity of outline, rather than any warmth of handling, is the portrait's strength. He lacks the soft modelling and spirited glance of Malbone, so the likeness may seem somewhat formal, but the picture is a reliable record of a man of the period and has a quiet dignity."},"met:15210":{"d":"A portrait miniature of George Wood, about 7.3 by 6 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1815. Miniatures like this were commonly set in a gold or gilt-metal frame and enclosed in a case, with a lock of the sitter's hair often sealed in a compartment on the back, so that an image and a physical keepsake of the sitter could be kept together. The portrait itself is a bust-length likeness in fine, translucent strokes.","i":"George Wood is a plain, common name, and the picture is a reminder that miniatures were not only for the very rich: professional men, merchants and officers could afford them and exchanged them as gifts. No occupation or family is recorded, so the portrait speaks of a type rather than a career. It was made in the years after the War of 1812, when American prosperity and travel increased the demand for portable likenesses.","c":"As a piece of craft this is steady and sound. Trott builds the likeness from small, careful strokes and keeps the colour clean, and the result has the neatness and calm that clients valued. What is missing is any sparkle of character; miniatures by Trott can seem generalised in expression. It is a respectable example of the type rather than a distinctive portrait."},"met:15211":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Charles Wagner, about 7.1 by 5.5 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1815. Ivory for miniatures was cut in very thin slices and usually backed with white paper or card so that light passing through the paint would be reflected back, giving the flesh tones their luminous quality. The sitter is shown at bust length, and his name, but little else about him, has come down with the picture.","i":"A miniature depended on a few centimetres of ivory and a fine brush, and it was prized as a likeness that could be carried, exchanged or worn. Charles Wagner's picture was made around 1815, at a time when American cities supported many miniaturists. Because he is named but not otherwise described, he is best read as a representative of the educated and moderately prosperous public that kept such small portraits as tokens of friendship and family ties.","c":"The strength of the miniature lies in its glow, and in a good one the ivory seems to light the face from within. Trott's work is typically clear and careful, which keeps a likeness like this fresh. The restraint is also a limit: the format asks for characterisation, and Trott gives a good likeness rather than an interpretation. Still, the small oval of ivory preserves a face that would otherwise be lost, and that is the point."},"met:14404":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Joanna Mary Gosman, wife of William Edward Wilmerding, about 7.6 by 6 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1825. It is almost exactly the same size as the portrait of her husband by the same artist, and the two were evidently made as a pair. The face is built from small, translucent strokes over the pale ivory, and the title records both her married name and her maiden name, Gosman.","i":"Husband-and-wife pairs were a favourite form for miniatures: each spouse kept the other's likeness, or the two were kept together as family records. The pair came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1992 as a gift from John Wilmerding, who shares the sitters' surname, which suggests that they descended in the family. By about 1825 the miniature's golden age was beginning to wane, but families that valued the form continued to commission it.","c":"Trott's later miniatures show the same neat, even technique as his earlier ones, and this pair has the virtue of consistency. The woman's likeness is clear and the colour clean, but the style had not changed much for a quarter century while fashion moved on, and it can look a little dated beside the larger, bolder oil portraits then appearing. As a pendant it works well; as an individual portrait it is quiet."},"met:14405":{"d":"A portrait miniature of William Edward Wilmerding, about 7.5 by 6 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1825, the pendant to the miniature of his wife, Joanna Mary Gosman Wilmerding. The two are almost the same size, and as a pair they would have been displayed or kept together. The sitter is shown at bust length, and the face is modelled in fine, translucent strokes over the pale ivory.","i":"Pairs such as this record a marriage as well as two people: the husband's likeness belonged beside the wife's, so that the founding couple of a household could be seen together. William Edward Wilmerding's portrait is a modest memorial to a man whose name has survived in the family that gave the pair to the Metropolitan Museum in 1992. Husband-and-wife pairs were commonly commissioned for the home, where they hung or lay together.","c":"A neat and serviceable portrait whose chief virtue is the clarity of the likeness. As the male half of a pair it lacks the personal sparkle that a famous miniaturist might give, and, like Trott's other ivories, it relies on accurate drawing and clean, even colour. It does not surprise, but it is faithful, and it gains from being seen beside the portrait of his wife."},"met:15212":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, only about 5.6 by 4.6 centimetres, painted in watercolour on ivory around 1825. It is a very small likeness, about the size of a matchbox, small enough to be set in a locket or brooch. At this scale the bust-length face is drawn with the tiniest strokes of translucent colour, and the pale ivory beneath lends the flesh its glow.","i":"By 1825 the portrait miniature had already passed its peak: oil portraits, lithographs and silhouettes competed for the same public, and the daguerreotype, which would end the miniature's career, was still more than a decade away. Trott, who lived until 1843, worked at the end of the golden age of the ivory miniature. The sitter is unnamed, and this very small portrait may have been intended for a locket, ring or brooch.","c":"A tiny portrait that asks to be judged on very small things: the clarity of the eyes, the economy of the strokes, the cleanliness of colour. At this scale a painter can hardly go wrong with likeness, and Trott's neatness is an advantage. But there is little room for characterisation, and the sitter remains anonymous, so the portrait is chiefly valuable as a sample of late Trott. It is delicate and well made, and scarcely more."},"met:11103":{"d":"A hunting picture in oil, about 102 by 138 centimeters, large for a sporting subject. The cast is given by the title and the museum's keywords: a hunter or hunters, a dog or dogs, and the rabbit they pursue. Thomas Hewes Hinckley, a Massachusetts painter known for dogs, cattle and game, worked here in a type of picture popular on both sides of the Atlantic at mid-century, in which the pleasure for the buyer lay in the breeds and the action.","i":"Pictures of dogs and quarry answered a mid-century taste for the sporting life as an image of manly country leisure. Sir Edwin Landseer had made such pictures fashionable in Britain, and American collectors followed. The subject is probably best read as a celebration of the working partnership between a man and his dogs, with the rabbit as the occasion and not a story of its own. The picture is dated 1850.","c":"Dog and game painting rewards close observation of anatomy and fur, and an artist in this line stands or falls by whether the animals seem alive to the chase. Hinckley was a capable New England animal painter, more a skilled regional specialist than an innovator. The picture is best appreciated for what it observes of dogs and quarry, rather than for any novelty of design."},"met:11104":{"d":"A dog, rats and sheaves of barley are the whole cast, as the title and keywords say. This oil on canvas, about 85 by 102 centimeters, turns a farm's pest hunt into a small drama, with the sheaves as both setting and shelter for the quarry. Painted in 1851, a year after Hinckley's The Rabbit Hunters, it applies the same animal painter's skills to a humbler subject. Rats and harvested grain together suggest a barn or stackyard, though the title does not say which.","i":"Rats were a farm's chief grain pest, and terriers were bred to kill them, so a picture of a dog among sheaves shows a real rural task and not a sporting luxury. Such lively, rather rough subjects suited the taste of the day for animal pictures that told a small story. The dog in such a scene is probably a ratter, valued less for pedigree than for nerve and speed.","c":"Rat-catching was a subject the English sporting painters had made respectable, and Hinckley's version is a fair example of the American branch of the type. It has the appeal of a story told quickly. Whether it has more than that depends on the painting of the dog, which is the real test in such pictures. As a record of rural life and of mid-century taste in animal painting, it deserves its place."},"nga:56737":{"d":"A young child is shown from the waist up, facing forward with a direct gaze. Dark curled hair is tied with pinkish ribbons, the eyes are large and dark, and the small mouth is closed. The ornate orange dress has blue-green detailing. The child holds a leafy fruit, the apple of the title, and a branch with leaves and small fruits or buds. Behind hang dark drapery with tassels and, to one side, a glimpse of a window with greenery outside.","i":"Fruit and flowers in a child's hands were conventional signs of childhood's freshness and promise, and the apple, with a budding branch, may have been read as an image of growth to come. It could equally be only a prop, the treat of a good child. The drapery and glimpse of garden borrow the furniture of grander European portraits, which provincial American painters learned to reproduce for families who wanted a likeness with some style.","c":"The appeal is directness: wide dark eyes, a small closed mouth, and a bright dress against dark drapery. The face is drawn simply, almost as a mask, with little modelling, so the likeness reads through pattern and outline; the dress and curtain are painted with more relish than the anatomy. Hamblin was no major painter, but this is a good specimen of provincial portraiture at its most legible, and the child's gaze is hard to forget."},"nga:56746":{"d":"Two young children stand together against a simple dark ground. The child on the left has fuller cheeks and tightly curled brown hair and holds red cherries, some of which have fallen to the ground; the child on the right has longer, less curly hair and holds a red book with a page partly visible. Both wear blue dresses with white lace-trimmed sleeves, white lace pantaloons and blue shoes. The matching clothes and the two reds, cherries and book, are the picture's main accents.","i":"Matching dress marks the two as siblings, and children of both sexes wore such frocks and pantaloons in early childhood. A book points to the household's literacy and its hopes for the children's schooling, while cherries were a common emblem of sweetness and of childhood's pleasures. The fallen fruit hints at play, a touch of life in a formal arrangement. The picture was probably a family commission of the 1840s, when traveling painters supplied such likenesses.","c":"Two figures, one costume, and two contrasting heads: the picture's pleasure lies in the small differences within a uniform. Hamblin keeps the background plain so the blue dresses and red accents carry the design, and the arrangement is clear and balanced. Faces are generalized, as in much folk portraiture, so individuality sits in the hair and the held objects. A pleasant, competent example of its type, without the invention that would lift it above the type."},"nga:42473":{"d":"A child is seated, shown from the waist up and facing forward against a plain dark gray ground. The dark hair is smooth and parted down the middle; the eyes are wide and dark, the mouth small and full. A coral dress with a wide neckline, puffed sleeves and delicate white lace trim is set off by a simple gold chain. In the right arm the child holds a white rabbit with a black spot on its back. The picture is small, about 31 by 25 centimeters, painted in oil on paper mounted on wood.","i":"A pet in a child's arms was a favorite device of portraitists, showing affection and the child's gentleness, and a rabbit, a creature both timid and prolific, suits a little girl well. The plain background and small panel suggest a modest and quick commission, perhaps painted in a few sittings by a traveling artist for a family who wanted a lasting likeness at a reasonable price.","c":"Everything is pared down: the gray ground, the smooth parting, the plain face. What remains is a white rabbit with a black spot, the liveliest note in the picture, and the child's large steady eyes. The scale is intimate and the effect is sweet without being sentimental, which for a small panel is a good result. Like most folk portraits of its kind, it simplifies form but gets the child's presence across."},"nga:50691":{"d":"Three children are seated, shown from the waist up against a plain beige background. At left a young boy with short light brown hair wears a black suit, white shirt and bow tie and holds a small object. At right two girls with shoulder-length brown hair and large white bows wear matching blue dresses with silver embellishments, and the girl in the middle holds a book. The black suit sets the boy apart from the matching sisters.","i":"The title is a general label, not a family name, so the sitters are not identified by it. A group portrait was a family commission, and its details tell something of the household: the boy in the grown-up black of the day, the girls dressed alike, a book in the hands of the middle child that points to schooling and literacy. Portraits of this kind recorded a family's standing and its hopes for the next generation.","c":"Three heads, one plain ground, two blues and a black: the design is as simple as it can be, and it works because Hamblin has kept the faces gentle and the clothes crisp. The children are grouped as a family unit rather than as individuals, and the picture is more successful as an arrangement than as a study of character. It is a clear, appealing piece of provincial portraiture."},"nga:59912":{"d":"Two children are shown full length in matching red dresses with white lace collars and cuffs, white pantalettes beneath. One, standing at right and turned slightly toward the other, has dark hair tied back with a pink ribbon, a serious face, and a long chain with a round pendant, and holds an open book. The other, with short light hair, holds a yellow ceramic jug decorated with flowers. Behind them lies a dark wooded scene of bare trees, with the silhouette of a house, smoke rising from its chimney, against a pink and blue sky.","i":"The title calls them sisters, while the museum's description reads one as a boy; very young boys were dressed in frocks and pantalettes like their sisters until they were breeched, usually at five or so, so the confusion is a period one. The book and jug point to learning and household, and the distant house with its smoking chimney places the pair, as many such pictures do, within the idea of a sheltered home.","c":"The best part of this picture is its background, the little house in the distance with its curl of chimney smoke under a pink and blue sky, which tells a story the figures do not. The children themselves are stiff in the usual folk manner, and the red dresses and lace are handled with more care than the faces. Even so, the whole is lively and clear, with more incident than the plain backgrounds of many such portraits."},"met:11930":{"d":"A portrait of a man, named in the title as Augustus Vallette Van Horne, Jr., painted in watercolor on a thin plaque of ivory about 6 by 5 centimeters, small enough to rest in the palm. Light passes through the thin washes, strikes the white ivory beneath and glows back, giving flesh tones a warmth that opaque paint lacks, and the painter builds the face in tiny strokes and dots. Miniatures like this one, dated about 1793, were worn as jewelry, kept in cases or exchanged between family and friends.","i":"Walter Robertson, an Irish miniaturist, worked in the United States in the 1790s, when sitters in the new republic were eager for portraits of this portable kind, and this sitter, an American to judge by the name, belongs to that market. A miniature of the date was a private object, often a gift or keepsake, so the sitter's standing was read from the quality of the work and the dress, not from a public setting.","c":"The best miniatures of the 1790s reward close looking: the fine stippling that models a cheek, the economy of the costume, the clean line of a collar. Robertson is counted among the leading miniaturists to work in America in his decade, admired for delicate technique, and a portrait of this size and date shows what that professionalism looked like. The limit is the form itself, a bust against a plain ground, which leaves little room for invention."},"met:11931":{"d":"A woman identified as Margaret Izard, Mrs. Gabriel Manigault, appears in a watercolor miniature on ivory about 7 by 6 centimeters, dated about 1795. She is recorded with pearls, lace and a chemise, the loose white muslin dress that was the height of fashion in the mid-1790s, plain in cut and light in feeling after the stiff silks of earlier decades. The tiny scale and the translucent ivory give the flesh and the whites a delicate glow.","i":"The Izard and Manigault families were both prominent in South Carolina, and the title names a woman at the center of that world. A miniature of 1795 would be a keepsake, perhaps exchanged between husband and wife or kept by a relation. The soft white dress and the pearls place her in the fashion of the Atlantic world, imported from London and Paris through Charleston's busy port, and show a provincial elite keeping pace with Europe's taste.","c":"The costume is the pretext for some of the best things in a miniature of this date: the soft, even tones of muslin, the faint highlight on a pearl, the lace drawn in a few touches. Robertson handles such passages with the finesse his reputation rests on. The sitter's character is harder to find, since the form's smooth finish and idealizing grace tend to make fashionable women of the decade look alike. A refined example, charming rather than searching."},"met:11932":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimeters and dated to about 1795. The title is all that is left of her identity, so the picture speaks first as a type: a small, finished likeness meant to be held close and shown to few. The thin ivory sheet lets light through the washes and back, which gives the skin its luminous warmth, and the fine brushwork rewards a magnifying glass as much as the naked eye.","i":"Many miniatures reach museums with the sitter's name lost, and the Met's title for this one records only a lady. A portrait like this belonged to private life: a gift for a fiance or a parent, a keepsake for a traveler, a remembrance after a death. It came to the museum with the collection of Moses Lazarus, given in his memory by his daughters Josephine and Sarah, a reminder of how these objects were treasured by later generations.","c":"With the sitter's name gone, the picture has to live on its handling, and Robertson's handling in the 1790s was accomplished: smooth, controlled and careful with detail. The gloss of that manner is its strength and its limit, since the bust miniature favors grace over character. A viewer should enjoy the finish and not expect revelation; for what it is, a polished example of the portable portrait at the high end of the trade."},"met:15162":{"d":"A very small watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 4.5 centimeters, smaller than a playing card, shows an unnamed gentleman and dates to about 1794. At this scale every hair and button is a single touch, and the artist's task is to suggest a face with the fewest and most exact strokes. Light passing through the thin washes and returning from the white ivory gives the flesh its glow, and the whole object was made to sit in a locket or a small case.","i":"The record gives only the sitter's sex and the date, about 1794, when Walter Robertson was working in the United States. A small portrait like this was a private thing, and an unnamed gentleman today may have been a husband, a suitor, a brother or a merchant who wanted his likeness to travel. The loss of the name is the common fate of the form, since miniatures passed through families and not through public collections.","c":"At this size a miniature stands or falls on economy: the face must be built from the fewest touches that will carry a likeness, and the costume reduced to a few clear notes. That discipline is what made Robertson one of the best miniaturists working in America in the 1790s. The limit is the formula itself: a polite bust, composed and calm, tells much about a gentleman's rank and little about his temper."},"met:15106":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed woman, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 8 by 6.5 centimetres, in 1842. The museum's record lists only a woman and a portrait, so her dress, hairstyle and expression cannot be described with confidence. At that date the daguerreotype had been public for only three years, and ivory miniatures like this were still prized for a colour and delicacy that early photographs could not offer.","i":"Edward Dalton Marchant (1806–1887) was an American portrait painter who worked in miniature and in oils. In 1842 a miniature such as this could still hold its place beside the camera by adding colour, and portraits of women in particular were prized as gifts and keepsakes. The sitter, as the title Portrait of a Lady records, is anonymous; the Met purchased it in 2006.","c":"A delicate small likeness of its period, to be admired for the fine detail and the glow of colour that ivory gives, with the sitter anonymous and the fashion of 1842 as the chief interest. Without being able to judge the face, it is fair to say that miniatures like this seldom reach psychological depth; their merits are prettiness, finish and tact. A competent, charming example of a vanishing trade."},"nga:43633":{"d":"A girl stands full length facing the viewer, a red flower in her right hand and a small bunch of pink flowers in her left. She has delicate features, light skin and long brown hair with bangs, and wears a white dress with a blue sash, red shoes, a black bead necklace and an orange earring. Beside her a pedestal holds a vase of colorful flowers, and behind her the background shows blue sky and trees. The canvas is about 116 by 70 centimeters and is dated 1791.","i":"The museum records the painter only as MacKay, a reminder of how many artists of the early republic are known by a surname alone. The picture follows a European formula for children's portraits, the standing child with flowers beside a pedestal, and the loose white dress with a sash reflects the new simplicity of children's clothing in the 1790s. Flowers stood for freshness and the brevity of youth, and the necklace and earring show a family that could afford small luxuries.","c":"The palette is bright and clear, with white dress, blue sash, red shoes and a bouquet of colors, and the design is simple and frontal; as in most provincial portraits of the day, pattern counts for more than modelling. The pedestal and flower vase lend a European air of polish that the figure does not quite share. MacKay is not a name to conjure with, but the portrait is vivid, and the girl's calm presence carries it."},"nga:129433":{"d":"More than three dozen pears hang densely on leafy branches against a vivid blue sky, filling a long, horizontal canvas. Most are golden yellow, touched with blush pink on one side, while those near the corners are darker green. The long, smooth-edged leaves come to points at both ends. Near the bottom center, three wasps dig into a partly devoured pear, and some of the leaves around it have brown spots or holes. Oil on canvas, about 31 by 61 centimeters, about 1884 to 1885, signed J. Decker.","i":"Pears ripening on the branch are a picture of natural abundance, and the wasps and the blemished leaves keep it honest: the fruit is shown as a living thing, not as an ideal specimen. The unusual composition, with no table or vase, may relate to the American taste for exact observation of nature, and the dense, all-over pattern of fruit and leaves recalls later decorative effects.","c":"An unusual, strongly designed still life, in which the sky-blue ground, the golden fruit and the dense leaves create a rich all-over pattern, and the wasps and damaged leaves give it a naturalistic bite that avoids sweetness. The long, narrow format is daring, and the picture holds together as design as well as observation. It is one of the more original American still lifes of the 1880s."},"nga:89673":{"d":"About two dozen round, pale green plums tumble out of an upended square wooden box onto a reflective dark brown tabletop. Many have a frosty bloom; about half lie in a pile within the box and the rest spill onto the wood. Most have stubby stems, and one near the viewer has a longer stem with two browning leaves. The leaves and fruit are reflected in the polished wood, and an ice-blue cloth hangs in folds behind the woven box. Oil on canvas, about 23 by 28 centimeters, signed J. Decker.","i":"Joseph Decker, a German-born painter who worked in Brooklyn, specialized in intimate close-up still lifes of fruit, often just one or two kinds against a plain ground, and he treated them with a seriousness that recalls the Dutch painters and Chardin. Plums spilling from a box suggest abundance and the brief perfection of ripe fruit, and the browning leaves hint, quietly, at time's passage.","c":"A fresh, sensuous picture of the fruit's frosty bloom and the glassy surface of the tabletop: the plums are rendered individually without monotony, and the browning leaves are an observant touch. The composition is deliberately casual, with the spilled fruit giving an air of spontaneity that the careful arrangement conceals. Decker never became fashionable, but pictures like this show why he is now admired as an original American still-life painter."},"nga:126134":{"d":"An arrangement of green grapes and yellow-red crab apples sits on a dark surface that reflects some of the fruit, against a black background. The grapes cluster at the back, the apples spread around the center and lower part of the composition, a few of them showing dark blemishes, and one apple has been sliced open to reveal its off-white flesh. The palette is deep reds, yellows and muted greens. Oil on canvas, about 21 by 36 centimeters, dated 1888, by Joseph Decker.","i":"The sliced apple and the blemishes are the signs of Decker's honesty: instead of presenting perfect fruit, he shows the fruit as it is, with imperfections and the evidence of being cut. The black ground recalls Dutch and Spanish still life, where fruit emerges from darkness, and the dark reflective table repeats the Dutch habit of mirrored surfaces. The small size invites close viewing.","c":"A concise, dark-toned still life whose virtues are the vividness of color against black and the unidealized treatment of the fruit. It is smaller and plainer than some of Decker's other fruit pictures, and less original in composition, but the quality of the paint and the clear forms keep it alive. A good example of his sober, intimate approach to still life."},"nga:89672":{"d":"Two clusters of dark blue-black grapes hang from a thin brown branch that stretches horizontally across the top of the canvas, olive-green vines winding around it. The grapes reflect light and cast dark shadows on a plain beige wall behind them. The composition is simple, with the fruit as the only subject. Oil on canvas, about 23 by 31 centimeters, painted about 1890 to 1895 by Joseph Decker.","i":"Grapes hanging against a wall are a trompe l'oeil motif that goes back to the ancient story of Zeuxis, whose painted grapes were said to be so lifelike that birds pecked at them, a tale often told about the power of illusion. Decker and his contemporaries in America revived the subject in the late nineteenth century. Grapes are also a traditional emblem of abundance and, in Christian art, of the Eucharist.","c":"A spare, resolute picture in which the dark fruit, the pale wall and the shadows are all that is needed. The grapes are modeled with great solidity, their bloom and highlights catching the eye, and the horizontal branch gives a taut structure. Decker was an original and still underappreciated still-life painter, and in this work of the 1890s he is at his most economical and most intense."},"met:15232":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman identified as Mrs. E. Hunt, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1829 by Carl Weinedel, measures only about 6 by 4.6 centimeters, smaller than a playing card. The museum's keywords record a woman and a portrait, so her features, dress and background are left unstated. Women's fashion of the late 1820s favored hair dressed high with loops and bows, and wide sleeves, so such miniatures often show a head crowned with ornament.","i":"By the 1820s a miniature was within reach of the prosperous middle class, who ordered them as keepsakes, wedding gifts and memorials to be worn as pendants or kept in cases. The sitter is known only by her married title and initial, so the portrait stands for a woman whose life is otherwise unrecorded. Weinedel, who lived from 1795 to 1845, worked at a time when the miniature was still the standard small portrait, a few years before photography.","c":"At this scale and date the standard is a firm likeness, soft stippled flesh and a clean, uncluttered setting, and whether this miniature reaches it is for a close look to decide. The museum's record offers no description of the surface to say. Its interest lies in an identified sitter and a secure date, 1829, which make it a useful fixed point among the many anonymous miniatures of the period."},"met:15233":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1836 by Carl Weinedel, measuring about 6 by 4.7 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, so his features, clothes and background are left unstated. Men's portraits of the 1830s typically show a dark coat, a high collar with a stock or cravat, and hair brushed forward into curls at the temples, with the sitter posed head and shoulders in a small oval.","i":"In 1836 the portrait miniature was near the end of its long reign. The daguerreotype was announced in 1839, and within a few years it took most of the market for cheap, accurate likenesses, so these small watercolors on ivory are among the last made by hundreds of professional miniaturists. The sitter is an unnamed gentleman, and the picture records the look of a respectable man of the 1830s.","c":"A gentleman's miniature of the 1830s tends toward a sober, direct style, with plain background and little ornament, as befits the dark fashions of the time. Without a description of the surface, the best that can be said is that it belongs to the final flowering of a tradition, and that precision and economy are typical of it. As a dated work by a known miniaturist it is useful, if not exciting."},"met:15234":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed gentleman, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1837 by Carl Weinedel and measuring about 6.4 by 5.1 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, so his features, clothes and background are left unstated. A miniature of this size was made to be held in the hand or set in a leather case or frame, and the painter works in fine hatching and dots of watercolor so that the ivory glows through the flesh.","i":"A miniature like this was a modest luxury: a sitting of an hour or two, a few days of work and a fee far below that of an oil portrait. Gentlemen ordered them for wives, fiancées and children, and for the family album. The title gives only a social category, which suggests that the sitter's name was lost early; he stands for the ordinary respectable men of the late 1830s, whose faces are known now only through such objects.","c":"On a plaque smaller than a playing card, a miniaturist must put a whole face, and the art is largely in the economy of the touches. The best are modest, direct and unflattering in a kindly way. Without a description of the surface, this one can only be assigned to that honest middle ground: a serviceable likeness of a sitter now nameless, preserved by the permanence of ivory and watercolor."},"met:11745":{"d":"Two children, a boy and a girl, share a large canvas of about 140 by 99 centimeters, with a doll and a toy for company. The title names the family and the museum's keywords supply the playthings. It is a full-size double portrait in oil, painted about 1838 for a household that could afford a canvas of this scale, and the toys serve as props that make the children look childlike and give the artist something engaging to paint.","i":"Toys in children's portraits of the 1830s signal childhood as a separate and cherished stage, a view that spread in the early nineteenth century, and they may hint at the roles the children would later take. Robert Peckham, a Massachusetts painter of the 1830s and 1840s, supplied such likenesses to families who valued order and piety and who wanted their children's innocence recorded while it lasted.","c":"Like much folk portraiture, this is a picture in which firm contours and clear color matter more than modelling, and its virtue is plainness. The doll and toy give a focus beyond the faces, which in a double portrait is a real help, and the large format shows Peckham at his most ambitious in scale. It is a sincere, attractive family portrait rather than a daring one."},"nga:43454":{"d":"Two children play on a rocking horse in a wallpapered interior, in a square canvas of about 104 by 102 centimeters. One, in a forest-green dress, sits astride the dappled horse, a black shoe in the stirrup and a riding crop in hand, head turned back over a shoulder. The other, in crimson, stands behind holding the ribbon of a straw bonnet and looks out at us. Around them are a terracotta-orange floral rug, golden-yellow and moss-green striped walls, an open door onto a staircase, and a table with a lamp and a folded newspaper.","i":"The title names a toy and not the sitters, so the picture reads as a portrait of two children caught in play, a pairing that became common in the 1840s when family likenesses began to show homes and toys. The fashionable interior counts as much as the children: patterned wall, rug, lamp and a newspaper, its masthead beginning 'Dai', probably a daily, place them in an up-to-date household.","c":"This is among Peckham's most engaging pictures because it is built from observation: the door, the lamp with its white globe, the vine-patterned cloth and the newspaper are picked out with a fondness that makes the room as interesting as its occupants. The children have the folk portraitist's smooth faces and simplified forms, but the turned head of the rider and the direct look of the child behind give the pair life. A confident design, bold in color."},"met:19773":{"d":"A woman is shown in a portrait miniature only about 7 by 6 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1830. The title gives no name, so the sitter is unknown. Miniatures of this size were intimate keepsakes, made to be held in the hand or kept in a case or locket, and they were a staple of American portraiture before the daguerreotype arrived in 1839. Frederick R. Spencer painted this one early in his career.","i":"Around 1830 the portrait miniature served as a love token, a family memento or a keepsake of an absent relative, and it was cheaper than an oil portrait. A woman's miniature was often made at an engagement or marriage, or for a husband traveling. The unnamed sitter now stands for a type, the ordinary woman of the early republic whose likeness was valued by someone who loved her.","c":"Miniatures demand fine, patient handling and a clear understanding of ivory's luminosity, and this is a competent specialist exercise: smooth flesh, careful details. Beside the masters of the American miniature it is modest. Spencer would soon turn to larger oil portraits, and this piece catches him early in his career. The charm is intimacy, a tenderness meant for a single viewer."},"met:12631":{"d":"Mary Ann Garrits is shown in an oil portrait of about 84 by 66 cm, dated 1834. The canvas, about 33 by 26 inches, is large enough for a half-length figure and a plain setting, and the sitter's dress would have followed the fashion of the day. Frederick R. Spencer, an American portraitist, painted miniatures early in his career and oils later. The title gives the sitter's full name, the usual form for a commissioned likeness.","i":"In the 1830s a growing American middle class commissioned portraits as signs of respectability, and itinerant and city painters supplied a recognizable likeness in fashionable dress for a modest fee. A portrait of a named woman recorded her place in her family and community, to be hung in the parlor and handed down. The sitter's name has survived, though the story of her life is not part of the picture.","c":"Spencer is a minor but capable portraitist, and the oil format shows him beyond the miniature. The virtues of a portrait of this period are an honest likeness and a clear, plain arrangement; the typical weakness is a certain stiffness, with the sitter posed as if for a document. Beside the grace of Sully, the leading American portraitist of the day, Spencer is plainer. It is a sound, sober record of an American woman of 1834."},"nga:34144":{"d":"A woman sits in a red armchair, shown from the waist up, her hands folded in her lap around a white lace handkerchief. Her expression is neutral and her eyes are dark. She wears a dark gown with intricate detailing, a white lace collar and a lace bonnet with a ribbon, and the background is plain and dark. The half-length canvas, dated 1838, measures about 91 by 74 centimeters.","i":"The question mark in the title records the museum's doubt: the sitter is thought to be Frances Ludlum, wife of Robert Morris, but the identification is not secure. A woman of the 1830s is presented in the uniform of respectable dress, dark silk, lace collar and cap, with folded hands and a handkerchief signaling poise and domestic virtue. Portraits like this were commissions, painted to record a family's standing and often hung as pendants to a husband's likeness.","c":"Frederick Spencer was a professional portraitist of his generation, and the picture shows the virtues of the trade: a calm pose, a firm face, and a stiff, handsome presentation of dress and lace. The dark ground and dark gown make the white collar and cap do much of the work, so the likeness is concentrated in the face and hands. It is respectable, competent painting, impressive for its restraint more than for invention."},"nga:53583":{"d":"A young child sits on a rock in a garden, a small hammer in the left hand. The child has rosy cheeks, large blue eyes and curly brown hair and wears a dark blue dress with a lace collar and dark shoes with bows over white socks. Behind lie lush greenery, a stone path, a small white gazebo and a picket fence with flowers. The canvas, dated 1834, measures about 76 by 64 centimeters.","i":"A child named for the first president suggests a family's patriotism, and the garden with its gazebo shows a household proud of its pleasure ground. The hammer may be a toy or perhaps a hint of the trade or ambition expected of a boy. Small boys wore dresses until they were breeched, so the frock is no surprise, and the outdoor setting reflects the Romantic idea of children as close to nature.","c":"The charm here is in detail and not in drama: curls, rosy cheeks, bows on the shoes, a gazebo and a picket fence in the distance. The landscape is an agreeable stage more than a place with depth, and the child sits quietly in front of it. Robert Street gives a sympathetic, tidy portrait of the sort that kept provincial painters busy, a fair measure of his competence."},"nga:53584":{"d":"A woman sits in an ornate armchair, a red book in her hand and a double strand of pearls at her neck. She has fair skin, blue eyes and dark hair in an updo, and wears a dark dress with puffed sleeves and a lace collar. Behind her rise a column and a distant landscape of mountains under a cloudy sky. The portrait, dated 1834, is about 76 by 64 centimeters, the same size as Robert Street's portrait of George Washington Deal of that year.","i":"The column and the distant mountains are stock props of the portrait tradition, borrowed from grand European portraiture to suggest dignity and breadth, while the book and pearls speak of education and means. The puffed sleeves belong to the fashion of the early 1830s, when the sleeves of women's gowns grew enormous. The sitter is known by name and date, and the picture shows how a provincial portraitist dressed a private person in public style.","c":"The best thing here is the contrast between the sitter's plain, steady presence and the borrowed grandeur of column and mountains; the stage is a little larger than the sitter, as in many provincial portraits. Street handles dress and lace carefully, and the face is clear and lifelike enough to give the impression of a particular woman. A competent middle-class portrait, more notable for good order than for surprise."},"met:10758":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a gentleman in watercolor on ivory, about 8 by 7 centimeters, painted in 1820 to 1822 by the American miniaturist Daniel Dickinson, and kept in a red leatherette case lined with green velvet. The gentleman is unnamed. Miniatures of this kind usually show the sitter head and shoulders against a plain or softly clouded ground. A case like this protected the delicate ivory and allowed the miniature to be carried or kept on a table.","i":"In the early nineteenth century the portrait miniature was the affordable, intimate form of portraiture in the United States, bought by merchants, professionals and their families as keepsakes and gifts. Watercolor on thin ivory was fragile, so miniatures were kept in closed cases that were opened to view, a privacy that suited their personal purpose. The sitter here is unnamed, and the picture is best understood as one of a large body of such small likenesses.","c":"A miniature of this period lives by the fineness of its stippling, the luminous glow of flesh over ivory and the honesty of the likeness, since the sitter's family had to recognize him. Dickinson made a considerable group of miniatures, several now at the Met, and the case survives with its lining, which is a pleasure in itself."},"met:14311":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Charles Leland in watercolor on ivory, about 6.5 by 5 centimeters, painted in 1822 by the American miniaturist Daniel Dickinson and set in a gilded copper locket. The back of the locket has a hair reserve, a small compartment for a lock of the sitter's hair. Locket miniatures were usually small, bust-length likenesses set under glass in a frame of this kind.","i":"The lock of hair in the back is the key to the object's purpose: a locket miniature with hair was a love token, a family memento or a keepsake of a friend, to be worn or carried near the body. Hair was prized as a lasting physical part of a person, and the combination of image and hair doubled the remembrance. The sitter is named as Charles Leland, but who he was, and who commissioned the portrait, is not known.","c":"A locket portrait is judged less on grandeur than on intimacy, and the physical object, with its gilded case and hair reserve, carries as much feeling as the likeness. Miniatures of this kind are competent and plain, as most American miniature painting of the 1820s was, valued for resemblance more than style. It remains a modest but touching survival of a private ritual of remembrance."},"met:15038":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a gentleman in watercolor on ivory, about 6.6 by 5.2 centimeters, painted in 1822 by Daniel Dickinson and set in a gilded copper case with a hair reserve on the back. The sitter is unnamed, a gentleman known only by that title. The size, date and mounting match those of Dickinson's portrait of Charles Leland, which suggests a standard format for his clients.","i":"The small compartment for hair behind the portrait is a reminder that miniatures were as much relics as pictures: a lock of the sitter's hair would be placed by the family, and the case could be worn as a pendant, a brooch or a bracelet clasp. The unnamed gentleman may have been a husband, a brother or a friend; the anonymity today is a loss that the object's own purpose, being kept close, would have prevented at the time.","c":"Anonymous in name but not in function, this miniature is a document of how Americans in the early republic kept their absent and dead close. The likeness, rendered in careful stippling on a few square centimeters of ivory, would have been judged by one test only: whether the family recognized the face. As art it is modest, but as an object it is complete and moving."},"met:15037":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a Mr. Hagner in watercolor on ivory, only about 6 by 5 centimeters, painted about 1825 by Daniel Dickinson and housed in a gilded copper locket with a hair reserve on the back. The title gives only the sitter's surname and the courtesy title Mr., so his first name and his story are unknown. Lockets of this kind were small enough to be worn on a chain or pinned to dress.","i":"Dickinson's customers often wanted a portrait for a locket, and the miniature's small scale made it a personal object rather than a public one. The sitter is known only as Mr. Hagner; who he was, and who owned the locket, is not known. The hair behind the portrait ties the object to someone's memory of him, and the locket was probably worn close to the body.","c":"At about six centimeters across, the picture can only hold a head and a hint of costume, and its success depends on a convincing face and a clean, even surface. Dickinson's miniatures of the 1820s are modest and professional rather than inspired. The interest of this one, like that of other locket miniatures, lies in the combination of portrait and relic, an intimate object meant to be handled and worn."},"met:19015":{"d":"A portrait miniature of the painter John Neagle in watercolor on ivory, about 7.6 by 6 centimeters, painted in 1830 by Daniel Dickinson. At that size it is small enough to rest in the palm, so it can show little more than the head and shoulders. It is a portrait of one artist by another, a likeness made within Neagle's professional circle.","i":"John Neagle (1796 to 1865) was a leading portrait painter in Philadelphia, best known for Pat Lyon at the Forge, which showed a blacksmith proudly at work. A miniature of him by a fellow painter suggests the artistic community of Philadelphia in 1830, in which portraitists exchanged likenesses as tokens of friendship and professional regard. Whether the miniature was a commission, a gift or an exchange is not known.","c":"Portraits of painters by painters tend to have an extra edge, and a miniature of Neagle, a famously accomplished portraitist, would invite comparison with the sitter's own work. How well Dickinson met the challenge is hard to judge, but the choice of sitter shows his place near Neagle's circle. It is likely to be of greater interest for its sitter than for its quality."},"met:10218":{"d":"A woman named Carolyn Mishner is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated to about 1825. As in most miniatures, the face is built in delicate washes and tiny strokes, with the white of the ivory glowing through the paint. At this size the portrait could be held in the hand, worn as a pendant or kept in a case, and it was painted by Hugh Bridport, an English-born artist who made his career in America.","i":"By the 1820s the miniature was a familiar way of marking a betrothal, a departure or a loss. Bridport supplied them to American sitters, and the name preserves this woman's identity where many miniatures have lost theirs. Nothing in the record says why this one was made; the usual occasions were gifts between family members, engagements and remembrance after a death.","c":"Bridport's miniatures belong to the craftsmanlike middle of the American trade in the 1820s: clear in likeness, tidy in finish and rather conventional in design. A portrait of this size depends on drawing and on the delicacy of the flesh tones. It does not have the dash of the best American miniaturists, but it does the work of a keepsake: a recognizable woman, kept small and close."},"met:15010":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the sitter of this bust-length portrait on a thin sheet of ivory, about 7 by 5.5 centimeters, dated about 1835. It is painted in transparent watercolor, with the face built from thin washes and tiny strokes, so that the white ivory beneath shows through and lends the flesh a soft glow. The type is the miniature of the 1830s, at the height of its popularity as a luxury of the American middle class, and the painter was Hugh Bridport.","i":"The Met's title records only a lady, and the loss of the name is the usual fate of miniatures, which passed through families as keepsakes and were seldom labeled on the front. By 1835 the daguerreotype was only a few years away, and the painted miniature was at its peak. Collectors now prize such pieces both for the sitters, often unrecorded, and for the evidence they give of period dress and taste.","c":"A miniature of this date is judged by the bloom of its flesh, the exactness of its drawing and its restraint. Bridport's work is competent in those respects without the individuality of the best American miniaturists, and a portrait of a lady in this idiom tends toward a pleasant, generalized type. As a period document of the 1830s it is useful, and its pleasures are quiet ones."},"met:10219":{"d":"A watercolor on ivory, about 9 by 7 centimeters, shows Joel Roberts Poinsett, painted in 1843 when he was in his sixties. The size is generous for a miniature, which gives the artist room for the sitter's head and shoulders and a modest ground. Poinsett was a South Carolina statesman, diplomat and botanist, and the portrait records a public man in a format normally kept for private ones. The artist was Hugh Bridport.","i":"Poinsett (1779 to 1851) was United States minister to Mexico in the 1820s and secretary of war under Martin Van Buren, and he is remembered today for bringing home the Mexican plant now called the poinsettia. A miniature of 1843, painted after his public career had ended, was probably a private commission, a likeness for family and friends of an elder statesman in retirement.","c":"The head of a prominent man is where a miniature earns its keep, and the brief of such a commission is a recognizable, dignified likeness, which the trade's conventions of fine finish, restrained color and a plain ground were well suited to deliver. Bridport was a reliable rather than a brilliant miniaturist. The interest here is historical as much as artistic, since it shows a well-traveled public figure at the end of his career."},"nga:50821":{"d":"Two sailing ships ride rolling ocean waves, the horizon set high near the tops of their sails. One, a schooner, carries a large flag; the other lies in the distance with less detail. The brushwork is smooth, picking out rigging and the texture of the sea, and the palette is blues, greens, whites and light tan. The canvas, by an artist recorded as Fritz Müller, is about 59 by 91 centimeters and is dated 1861, the year of the event named in the title.","i":"The Savannah was a Confederate privateer, a schooner commissioned by the Confederacy to prey on Northern merchant ships, and the Perry was a United States Navy brig; on 3 June 1861, weeks after Lincoln proclaimed the blockade, the Perry caught her. Her crew was later tried in the North as pirates, a controversy that tested whether privateers were criminals or prisoners of war. Painted in the same year, the picture belongs to the first wave of Civil War naval subjects.","c":"This is a picture for a public hungry for Civil War news, and it serves that hunger plainly: two vessels, a large flag, a high horizon that gives the sails and rigging the stage. The smooth, careful handling is that of a draftsman more than a colorist, and the sea is described with patience. It is documentary marine painting, informative and tidy, and restrained for a subject about a capture."},"nga:42406":{"d":"A man faces the viewer with his body slightly angled to the right, his expression blank and his brow slightly furrowed, with a pronounced nose, thin lips, light eyes and thinning hair. He wears a black coat, a light brown waistcoat, a white shirt with a high collar and a white cravat, and holds a cane in his right hand while seated by a green chair or bench. Behind him is a painted backdrop of a wooden bridge over a river with trees, under a red curtain at the top. The panel is about 74 by 60 centimetres.","i":"The portrait follows a formula found in much early nineteenth-century American portraiture: the sitter seated in dark formal dress, a cane or book as an attribute of status, a draped curtain and a view of landscape borrowed from European portraits. The painter, William Bonnell, shares the sitter's surname, which suggests a family connection. Such portraits were made for family display and were often the only likenesses that ordinary people ever had of themselves.","c":"A forthright, plain portrait: the sitter's blunt features, the sharp contrast of black coat and white cravat and the neat details of cane and backdrop have a strong, direct presence. The modelling is flat, the pose stiff and the painted backdrop naive in perspective. It lacks psychological subtlety but has the honest directness that gives American folk portraiture its appeal."},"nga:166462":{"d":"A dozen or so men, some on horseback and some on foot, gather around a wagon outside a house, talking and gesturing in a small, loosely painted horizontal picture in darker tones. A rider with a hunting horn raises a whip; the man on a white horse smiles as he reads a paper braced against its mane; another, in a gold coat and red sash, holds up a paper. Four men stand on the wagon, a dog sits between the horses, and at right a fence opens onto a yard where at least three brown-skinned people stand together.","i":"Election day in the country was a social occasion as much as a civic one: neighbors met at the polling place, passed around papers and argued the issues of the day. Alfred Jacob Miller, a Baltimore painter best known for his watercolors of the Rocky Mountain West, here paints a scene from his own home area. The picture also records who was left out: in Maryland at this date only white men could vote, so the people in the yard, probably African American, stand outside the event.","c":"Miller made his reputation with the Rocky Mountains, and this little oil is a different kind of observation: quick, humorous and local. The gestures and the clustering of the crowd are handled with a sketcher's speed, and the darker tones and loose paint leave some details hard to make out. Compared with the carefully composed election pictures of George Caleb Bingham it is a sketch, but it has a freshness and an unposed liveliness that finished pictures often lose."},"met:435810":{"d":"A road winds through a forest, where travelers, a wagon and a dog make their way beneath tall trees. The panel, about 46 by 83 centimeters, is dated 1607 and painted in oil on wood in Jan Brueghel the Elder's small-scale, jewel-like manner, with minutely drawn figures dwarfed by the woods around them. As in his forest pieces generally, the road leads the eye deeper and deeper into the trees, and part of the pleasure is in the small human incidents along the way.","i":"Brueghel, the son of Pieter Bruegel the Elder, worked in Antwerp and was known as Velvet Brueghel. His woodland roads were a new kind of landscape: not a Bible story or a myth with a landscape behind it, but a view of ordinary travelers on an ordinary road, painted for collectors who prized fine detail. A dated panel of 1607 shows that he had made the type his own, and Dutch landscape painters of the next generation built on it.","c":"Brueghel is at his best in small, finished landscapes where the eye can wander, and a panel like this gives plenty to look at without losing order: the road is a clear spine for the composition, and tiny figures animate it without disturbing the quiet. What is missing is the monumental sweep that Dutch painters would later find in the same material; this is a collector's picture, to be studied close up and enjoyed as a wonder of finish."},"nga:111124":{"d":"People gather under trees beside a broad river dotted with boats, all tiny in scale. The water, bottle green in the foreground and celestial blue in the distance, melts into the sky at a horizon halfway up the painting. At lower right a ferry loaded with travelers, horses and cattle nears a landing where a boatman passes a baby to a man on the bank, while ladies, a girl and a dog wait nearby. Slender trees fill the left third; farther back a stone bridge and a church spire rise among tan buildings. The copper plate is signed BRVEGHEL 1607.","i":"Jan Brueghel the Elder worked in Antwerp and was famous for small, minutely detailed pictures painted on copper, which collectors prized for their jewel-like finish and vivid color. Ferries, fishermen, travelers and village life along waterways were favorite subjects; they let him combine close observation of everyday activity with an ordered, harmonious view of the countryside. The little drama of the baby being handed ashore is the kind of incident that rewards a slow look.","c":"Everything is placed with a miniaturist's control: the figures, boats, birds and buildings are numerous yet legible, and the cool blue of the distance creates real atmospheric depth against the dark foreground. The composition is a formula Brueghel used many times, and some of the figures are almost generic. At this tiny scale, though, the precision and the glowing light are hard to match, and the picture shows why his small coppers were prized."},"nga:222552":{"d":"A wooded landscape with travelers, painted in oil on a horizontal wooden panel about 37 by 58 centimeters in 1610. The title records the trees and the wayfarers, and no fuller description is available, so the number of figures, their activities and the lie of the land are left unstated. In Jan Brueghel's forest scenes tall trees typically frame a view, a track leads the eye into the distance, small figures are given careful detail, and warm greens and browns give way to cool blue horizons.","i":"Forest and road scenes were a Flemish specialty in Brueghel's day, pioneered by Gillis van Coninxloo, who replaced the broad world-landscapes of the previous generation with a more enclosed, intimate nature. Travelers on a road give the scene a human scale and a quiet narrative of movement, risk and rest. Such small panels were collected for cabinets of curiosities and princely galleries.","c":"Brueghel is at his best in woodland panels, where his fine touch makes the foliage glitter and still keeps the figures legible. The risk is repetition: dark framing trees, a winding road and a blue distance recur throughout his work. Without a detailed record, this panel can be taken as a representative, accomplished example of a formula he handled better than almost anyone."},"met:438465":{"d":"A tiny landscape on copper, only 9.5 by 15 centimeters, shows travelers on a woodland path: a man and a woman among them, someone on horseback and a wagon. Jan Brueghel the Elder painted on copper because its smooth, hard surface takes the finest detail and holds its color, and the scale is that of a jewel. It dates from about 1607, the year of the larger forest road at the Met, and was bequeathed to the museum in 2000 by Hertha Katz.","i":"Small copper panels like this were made for collectors who kept them in cabinets of curiosities or in rooms of treasures, to be taken out and passed around. Brueghel's travelers, ordinary people of road and village, supply human interest and a sense of scale, and the woodland path suited both his talent for detail and the taste of his buyers, wealthy collectors in Antwerp and beyond.","c":"Reducing a forest to the size of a postcard demands extreme control, and Brueghel has it: a painter who can make trees, travelers and a wagon read clearly in the space of a hand is working at a high level of craft. The effect is of a world reduced and perfected, charming and a little unreal, which is just what the buyers wanted. Such pieces are less ambitious than his large works but are often more perfectly finished."},"nga:76212":{"d":"A flower still life in oil on a wide wooden panel, about 55 by 89 centimeters, painted in 1615. The title says what the picture holds: flowers in a basket and in a vase. Further details are not recorded, so the particular blooms and any insects or shells are left unstated. In Jan Brueghel's flower pieces the blossoms are typically crowded together in rich variety, among them roses, tulips, irises and lilies, each drawn with botanical precision and set against a dark ground that makes the colors glow.","i":"Flower pieces were prized rarities in Antwerp. Jan Brueghel often combined blooms that flower in different seasons, working from separate studies, so the bouquet is an imaginary perfection that could never exist at one moment in nature. Collectors, among them the Milanese cardinal Federico Borromeo, valued such pictures for their beauty and as praise of God's creation; the flowers can also carry a reminder that all beauty fades.","c":"At their best Brueghel's flowers have the quiet radiance of a jeweler's work: petals thin as tissue, leaves crisp, colors laid in without muddying. The wide format gives room to enjoy the variety, though an arrangement of this kind can read more like a catalog of flowers than a unified design. Admired in his lifetime, such pictures made his reputation as the flower specialist among the Brueghels."},"met:12655":{"d":"A man named Daniel Strobel, Jr. is the sitter of this very small watercolor on ivory, only 6 by 4.4 centimeters, dated about 1830. It was painted by Louisa Catherine Strobel, who shares his surname, so the picture was probably made within a family circle. Brushed in fine strokes over a thin ivory plaque, it is the kind of likeness kept in a case or a drawer and shown to friends and relatives, with the ivory's whiteness lighting the face from within.","i":"Painting miniatures was a respectable pursuit for women in the early nineteenth century, since the work was small, could be done at home and needed no large studio. Louisa Catherine Strobel's miniatures at the Met include a self-portrait and portraits of several sitters, and those of the Strobel family came by bequest from Ella Church Strobell in 1917. Pictures of relatives painted by a family member are among the most personal kinds of portraiture.","c":"A miniature made by a relative has an advantage that commissions rarely have: long acquaintance with the face. That, more than polish, is what to look for here. Strobel is a minor name in the history of the form, but a minor name can leave a direct and affectionate likeness, and the consistency of her hand across several family sitters is part of the pleasure of seeing them together."},"met:12656":{"d":"A woman paints herself in a watercolor miniature on ivory about 7 by 5.6 centimeters, dated about 1830. She is recorded in lace, a hat with a ribbon and a bow, the neat, fashionable dress of a young woman of the time. A self-portrait on ivory must be worked from a mirror at a scale where the painter's own face measures only a few centimeters across, so the picture also serves as a demonstration of her skill.","i":"Women who painted miniatures usually appear in the record only briefly; Louisa Catherine Strobel (1803 to 1883) is known to the Met through this self-portrait and the pictures of her family. A self-portrait made about 1830, when she was in her twenties, would present her as she wished to be seen: accomplished, fashionable and composed. Such pictures were often given to relatives or kept as a record of the artist at a particular age.","c":"Self-portraits are the hardest test of a portraitist, because the sitter is the one person the artist cannot flatter without knowing it. Strobel's version has the poise of a respectable young woman of the 1830s and the finish of a careful miniaturist; whether it is revealing is for the viewer to judge. Its importance is as much historical as aesthetic: an uncommon self-image by an American woman painter at work in the period."},"met:12657":{"d":"A watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5 centimeters, shows Anna Church, Mrs. Daniel Strobel Jr., in a cap and a fichu, the folded kerchief worn around the neck and shoulders, with earrings. Painted about 1830 by Louisa Catherine Strobel, it presents a married woman in simple, respectable dress, with the soft glow that thin washes over ivory give to skin and white cloth. The scale is that of a hand-held keepsake.","i":"The donor's name, Ella Church Strobell, points to a descendant, probably a daughter of this couple, and the bequest of 1917 kept these portraits together. A cap and kerchief mark a married woman of the era, a modest costume that signaled respectability and not display. Such pictures served as family records, the face of a wife and mother held for children who would remember her through them.","c":"The simplicity of the costume sends the weight to the face, and a miniature of this kind depends on the accuracy and gentleness of that face more than on elaboration. A relative's hand has the advantage of long acquaintance. The painting is quiet and unassuming and consistent with Strobel's other family pictures; it should be seen as a record of affection first and a feat of technique second."},"met:15193":{"d":"A man named Jacob Gerard Koch is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5 centimeters, painted about 1830 by Louisa Catherine Strobel. It matches her portrait of Mrs. Jacob Gerard Koch in size, date and source, since both came to the Met in 2006 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund, and the two were probably made as a pair. Like most miniatures, it is a bust on a thin ivory plaque, built from fine washes and small strokes.","i":"Husband and wife were often painted as a pair, in matching sizes, to be kept or displayed together, and the match in size and date here suggests such a commission, perhaps for a marriage or a family record. Because both sitters are named, the pair is useful to historians as well as to collectors, who can trace the families behind the faces.","c":"As half of a pair, the picture is best judged with its mate. On its own it shows what a careful miniaturist could do in 1830: a clear head, a tidy collar and a little air of reserve. It would be wrong to ask for more psychological depth than the form gives; the pleasure is in clean drawing and a consistent, pleasant surface."},"met:15194":{"d":"A woman named Jane Griffith, Mrs. Jacob Gerard Koch, is shown in a watercolor on ivory about 7 by 5 centimeters, dated about 1830. She is recorded in lace, a shawl, a bonnet and a ribbon tied in a bow, the dress of a respectable woman of the early 1830s, in which the bonnet frames the face and the shawl and lace soften the shoulders. The small size and thin, translucent washes are typical of the miniature.","i":"The ribbon bow, shawl and bonnet are the language of fashion in the early 1830s, a decade of large bonnets and wide sleeves. As the mate of her husband's miniature, this picture belongs to the pairs often painted for newlyweds or for families who wanted both likenesses together. The title gives her maiden name as well as her married one, a courtesy to genealogists and a reminder that women were usually named in the records through their husbands.","c":"The costume gives the painter more to do than a plain coat gives in a man's portrait, and the lace, ribbon and bonnet are where a miniaturist can show finesse. Strobel's version is careful and tidy, though the likeness of a married woman in her bonnet and shawl tends toward the generic idea of respectable womanhood. Beside her husband's portrait it forms a modest, agreeable pair."},"met:17465":{"d":"A man, a woman and a child appear in this scene from Washington Irving's story of Rip Van Winkle, painted in 1833 on a canvas about 54 by 64 centimeters. Browere was born in Catskill, New York, at the foot of the mountains where Irving set the tale, and he was about nineteen when he painted it. It is an early American picture of a literary subject, in the manner of rural genre painting.","i":"Irving's story, published in 1819 in The Sketch Book, tells of a good-natured, idle villager of the Catskills who falls asleep in the mountains and wakes twenty years later to find his village changed and the American Revolution over. It was instantly popular and soon a national legend, in prints and on the stage. The tale let Americans smile at their own past and its rapid change.","c":"Browere was only a young man, and the picture has the charm of a narrative made by someone who knew the country. American genre painting in 1833 was still finding its way; his competence in grouping and expression is respectable, if not equal to William Sidney Mount, whose rural scenes of the 1830s set the standard. It is most interesting as an early visual interpretation of an American myth."},"met:437844":{"d":"A woman with a theatrical mask personifies Comedy in this small horizontal canvas, about 38 by 45 centimeters, painted in oil about 1736. The mask is the standard attribute of the stage, and the picture's subject is the art of the theater and not any particular actress. It came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1973 with the bequest of Emma A. Sheafer, and its scale and light subject suggest a decorative piece for a private room, in the taste of the French Rococo.","i":"Comedy, paired with Tragedy, was a stock allegory in the French eighteenth century, usually shown as a young woman with a mask, sometimes with a scroll or a musical instrument, and such figures decorated private rooms and theatrical settings. Pierre Charles Trémollières, who worked in Paris and Rome and died in 1739, left a small body of work, and a light subject like this belongs to the lighter side of a short career.","c":"French painters of the 1730s were learning to make the grand tradition charming, and Trémollières belongs to that moment. A single figure with a mask gives little room for narrative, so success depends on grace of pose and delicacy of surface, the very qualities the Rococo prized. It is a pleasing, slight piece, and the death of its painter in his mid-thirties leaves open the question of what he might have become."},"nga:42453":{"d":"A man sits facing forward in a wooden chair, in a tall painting on wood about 104 by 79 centimeters, dated about 1831. He has blue eyes and dark brown curly hair, and wears a high-collared coat over a striped cravat, with a white cloth around his neck. A hat rests on his lap and he holds a thin object in one hand. Behind him stands a wooden bookshelf with several books.","i":"The word 'Possibly' in the title records uncertainty about the sitter's name, which is common for portraits by traveling painters who left no ledger. The books mark a man of some learning or at least of aspiration, and the formal coat and cravat show a man dressed for the occasion. Asahel Powers, still in his late teens when he painted this, was one of the New England itinerants who moved from town to town painting farmers, tradesmen and their wives.","c":"Powers paints on wood panel with a hard, flat clarity, and the seated man has the blunt directness of provincial portraiture: a plain face, a steady gaze, a carefully rendered coat and cravat. The bookshelf is the one piece of invention, giving the sitter an identity beyond his looks. The drawing is stiff and the space shallow, but the picture has the strength of honesty, and for a painter not yet twenty it is assured."},"nga:42454":{"d":"A woman sits in a wooden chair, facing slightly to the right, against a dark brown ground with red undertones. She has light eyes, a straight nose and a set expression, with dark brown hair in soft curls under a white bonnet trimmed with red-and-white striped ribbons and lace. She wears a light green gown with a lace collar and two gold necklaces, one with a heart-shaped pendant, and holds a black object resembling an ear trumpet. The panel, dated about 1831, is about 104 by 78 centimeters.","i":"If the black object is indeed an ear trumpet, as the museum's description suggests, the picture records the sitter's deafness, an unusual candor in a flattering genre and a sign that portraits could include the facts of a person's life. The heart pendant and gold necklaces show her means and perhaps her sentiment; the bonnet and lace belong to the fashion of about 1830. Matching the portrait titled 'Possibly William Sheldon' in size, date and source, it was evidently painted as his pendant.","c":"This is the livelier of the pair: the soft curls, the striped ribbons and the heart pendant give more to look at than her husband's plain coat, and the ear trumpet, if that is what it is, is a startling, human detail. The handling remains flat and linear, and the dark brown ground is spare. A touching picture, interesting for what it is willing to show."},"nga:42457":{"d":"A woman sits upright, arms relaxed, a small red book in her hands. Her face is carefully drawn, with a fair complexion, high cheekbones and a slightly serious expression. Her dark hair is swept into an elaborate updo with a floral ornament, and she wears a formal, high-necked brown dress with full sleeves and a transparent lace-like cape over her shoulders, with a long black beaded necklace layered over a shorter one. The earthy, textured background is muted. The panel, dated 1833, is about 92 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The elaborate hairstyle with its floral ornament and the full sleeves belong to the high fashion of the early 1830s, and the long black beads and lace cape show a woman dressed for display. The small red book may be a Bible, a hymnal or a book of devotions, or simply a sign of reading. Asahel Powers painted her two years after the Sheldon portraits, by then a more practiced hand.","c":"Beside the Sheldon portraits this is the more elaborate in costume and the more careful in the face. The updo, the layered beads and the lacy cape are drawn with patience, and the high cheekbones and serious look give her an individuality that stock portraiture often lacks. The setting is flat and brown, so the dark dress and pale lace do all the work."},"nga:52957":{"d":"A bird's-eye view shows green land and pale blue water, with the horizon in the upper third, where small wooded islands sit on the water in front of a soft blue sky almost the color of the sea. A black sailboat moves near the center. At left a large gray building with a tower and a fenced-in porch is surrounded by trees; smaller white and yellow buildings stand in the center and at right, linked by brown roads. The painting is pale, nearly without shadows, and flat, in soft yellows, greens and blue-grays.","i":"The picture belongs to the bird's-eye view tradition, in which a resort, a town or a farm is shown from above as if on a map, and the large gray building with its tower is probably a hotel, which would fit the title. Such views were often made for owners and visitors in the years after the Civil War, when Southern coasts and springs began to draw winter visitors from the North. Dana Smith, a little-documented painter, is dated here about 1880.","c":"This is a folk painter's view, and its virtues are the ones that tradition prizes: clarity, flat pattern and a sweet palette. The lack of shadow and perspective gives the scene the look of a map, charming but frank about its limits. It is a picture of a place more than of a mood, and a viewer comes away knowing what the town looked like in the 1880s, if not what it felt like."},"nga:12209":{"d":"A single devotional figure of Saint Lucy, in oil on canvas about 104 by 77 centimeters, painted by Zurbarán about 1625 to 1630, around the time he settled in Seville. Zurbarán's early saints are usually isolated against a dark ground and lit from one side, with the solid modeling of a sculptor and an almost still-life attention to cloth. In his day Lucy would be recognized by her attributes, the martyr's palm and a dish bearing her eyes.","i":"Lucy of Syracuse, a Sicilian martyr of about 304, is the patron saint of sight, and her name, from the Latin lux, light, supports the link. In pictures she carries her eyes on a dish, a reference to the legend that they were torn out or that she tore them out herself. Zurbarán painted many single female saints, often in the rich dress of his own day, and the type suited chapels, convents and private devotion in Counter-Reformation Spain.","c":"Zurbarán's early strength is a sculptural firmness and a plain, quiet gravity, and a picture of this date lives by the lighting of the face and the weight of the drapery, which he handled as few Spaniards could. The elegant, fashionably dressed female saints of the 1630s came later; this belongs to the more austere beginning of his career, and rewards a viewer who likes restraint."},"met:437971":{"d":"A girl kneels on the floor in a niche made by pale mauve curtains pulled back to either side. She wears a blue sleeveless bodice over a white blouse with embroidered cuffs and a rose-red skirt spread around her. Her hands are clasped in prayer on a sewing cushion that holds a strip of embroidery, and her large eyes are lifted upward. Behind her head a faint golden glow shows cherubs' faces in the darkness. At left is a small table with a box and scissors, and a clay jug stands on the floor. At right a vase of lilies and roses and a basket of white linen sit beside her. Small blue flowers lie scattered on the floor.","i":"According to tradition, the child Mary lived in the Temple in Jerusalem, devoting herself to prayer and to sewing vestments. The Metropolitan Museum explains that such pictures served as models of behavior, encouraging girls toward piety and domestic industry. The lilies stand for purity, and the cherubs show heavenly blessing. Zurbarán, working in Seville, set the sacred theme among everyday objects, the pottery and textiles of his own city, which link the divine and the ordinary. There are several versions of the subject, and the Museum dates this one to about 1632–33.","c":"Zurbarán builds devotion out of clarity. Every object is lit evenly and set apart from the next, and the plain things at the margins, the jug, the basket of linen and the scissors, are the real pleasure. The girl herself is less convincing: the enormous, upturned eyes look stage-managed, and the sweetness verges on that of a doll. The picture shows what separates him from the softer, more affectionate children of Murillo. He is stern, still and almost scientific, which makes the painting memorable, though not tender."},"met:437969":{"d":"A battle between Christian and Muslim forces fills this very tall canvas, over three meters high, painted about 1637 to 1639. The subjects include horses, helmets and flags, and also the Madonna and Child, angels and putti, which points to a heavenly apparition over the fighting. The picture shows a medieval battle of the Reconquista, a rare subject for Zurbarán, who is usually known for single saints and monks in stillness.","i":"The Reconquista, the centuries-long Christian recovery of Spain from Muslim rule, was told in Spanish tradition as a sacred war in which heaven took part, and the Virgin or a saint was said to appear to help the faithful. To a viewer in 1630s Spain such a victory expressed Christian identity and local pride. Modern viewers will also see the one-sided triumphalism of this version of the past.","c":"Zurbarán's gift is stillness, and a battle asks for movement, so this canvas shows an artist stretching his range. The tall format divides naturally into earth and heaven, a good solution, and his clear drawing and sculptural figures give the fighting a solid, almost carved quality. Action does not come easily to him, and the result lacks the flowing energy of a Rubens or a Velázquez, but the ambition and the austere clarity are impressive."},"cma:136217":{"d":"In a dim room, a boy in a blue robe sits on a low stool at the left, looking down at a crown of thorns in his lap. At the right, the Virgin sits on the floor in a rose-pink gown, resting her cheek on one hand, her eyes wet with tears, with a green cushion and white cloths across her knees. Between them stands a wooden table tilted toward the viewer, holding books and a few pieces of fruit. A basket of linen is on the floor, a clay bowl sits at the left, white doves stand at the right, and a vase of lilies and roses is partly lit behind her. A golden glow at the upper left holds faint cherubs.","i":"Cleveland explains that stories of Christ's childhood became popular in the Counter-Reformation because ordinary people could easily understand them. Rather than illustrating a Gospel episode, Zurbarán appears to have invented this one: the young Jesus pricks himself on a crown of thorns he is weaving, foretelling the Crucifixion. The Virgin's tears show that she understands. Despite the large scale and monumental figures, the picture is quiet and intimate. White lilies are a traditional emblem of Mary's purity, and the tilted tabletop displays the books and fruit like a small still life; Zurbarán, who also painted still lifes, dated this work about 1640.","c":"Zurbarán's strengths are all present: the weighty folds of the pink and blue cloth, the plain brown table and the crisp white linen, each lit as if sculpted. The silence is unusual, because mother and son do not look at each other, each absorbed in a private grief or task. That separation is the picture's power and also its problem, since the composition can feel divided into separate still lifes, and the faces lack the animation of the cloth. The result is tender and austere rather than dramatic."},"met:437970":{"d":"Saint Benedict of Nursia appears alone in a tall canvas of about 188 by 104 cm, painted about 1640 to 1645. A pitcher appears among the objects, probably an allusion to the legend in which a cup of poisoned wine offered to the saint shattered when he blessed it. Zurbarán made his career painting monks and saints for religious houses in Seville and beyond, in a plain, sculptural style of heavy drapery and dark grounds.","i":"Benedict (about 480 to 547) founded the monastery of Monte Cassino and wrote the Rule that shaped Western monastic life, balancing prayer, work and obedience. In the legend told by Pope Gregory the Great, monks who resented his discipline tried to poison him, and the cup broke at his blessing, so a broken vessel became his emblem. A picture like this was the sort of image that held the founder up as a model of the spiritual life in a monastic house.","c":"Zurbarán's gift is gravity: a figure stilled, a habit built from broad, hard folds that seem carved, and a dark ground that concentrates attention. Benedict's habit gives him a monumental dark mass, and austere simplicity suits the founder of an austere order. The years after his peak in the 1630s tend to bring a softer touch and more generic faces. Even so it is a dignified, quietly forceful image."},"nga:41703":{"d":"A large canvas of about 246 by 174 centimeters, the scale of an altarpiece or a major wall in a church, brings together three saints: Jerome, the scholar of the early Church, with the Roman noblewoman Paula and her daughter Eustochium. Painted about 1640 to 1650, in Zurbarán's mature style, it is a devotional group of the figures at the root of a religious tradition. Zurbarán worked for religious houses throughout his career and was skilled at the monumental, plain groupings they asked for, with strong light on faces and cloth.","i":"Paula and Eustochium were Roman noblewomen who followed Jerome to Bethlehem in the 380s, where Paula founded convents and Eustochium carried on her work. They were Jerome's patrons and fellow ascetics, and the Hieronymites, the Spanish order that follows Jerome, revered all three. A picture of the trio would suit a convent or chapel of that order, presenting the saints as models of learning, penance and devotion.","c":"The 1640s were a difficult decade for Zurbarán: commissions for Spain and Spanish America multiplied, workshop help increased, and his manner softened as newer Seville painters came into favor. A canvas of this scale is a reminder both of his ability to give large figures dignity and of the risk of routine that came with the volume. It is a late work to be weighed against his best, and its seriousness is not in doubt."},"met:15197":{"d":"A very small miniature, only about 4.8 by 3.7 centimeters, on ivory, shows an unnamed woman. She is recorded with a fichu, the light scarf crossed over the shoulders and chest, an earring and a bow, details of fashionable dress of the mid-1790s. At this size the whole portrait is about as large as a small locket, and the painter works in tiny dots and strokes of watercolor over the ivory's white ground.","i":"The painter, Lawrence Sully, was born in Kilkenny, Ireland, in 1769 and was the older brother of Thomas Sully, who became one of America's most fashionable portraitists. Lawrence died young, in 1804, so his career was short. A fichu and an earring mark an unnamed woman of fashion; the title leaves her name unrecorded, as is common with miniatures that passed through families.","c":"A miniature of 4.8 centimeters leaves no room to hide: the face has to be right. Sully's miniatures belong to the delicate, stippled manner of the late eighteenth century, and set beside the polished work of more celebrated contemporaries they are modest. Their charm is intimacy, and the viewer should hold this one at the distance a locket would have been worn, close enough to see the dots and no closer."},"met:15199":{"d":"A girl or young woman, identified in the title only as Miss Annis, is the subject of this watercolor miniature on ivory, about 6.3 by 5 centimeters, painted about 1800. Lawrence Sully worked in the soft, delicate manner of the late eighteenth century, building flesh from fine dots and strokes so that the white ivory glows through. A portrait of a young sitter in this format was a prized family possession, kept in a case and brought out to be shown.","i":"Lawrence Sully's wife was Sarah Annis, and after his death in 1804 his younger brother Thomas married her; the sitter may therefore belong to his wife's family, though the title gives no first name and nothing confirms it. Miniatures were the currency of such family bonds, made for parents, siblings and suitors. A portrait of about 1800 might have been exchanged at a betrothal or kept as a remembrance.","c":"Sully was a modest talent beside the leading miniaturists of his day, but a portrait of a young sitter brings out the best of the delicate method: soft flesh, little shadow, nothing hard. The picture is intimate and sweet, unambitious in design, with the simple appeal of a likeness made for family. Its interest is as much historical, as the work of the elder brother of a far more famous Sully, as aesthetic."},"met:15198":{"d":"A man named Edward C. Cunningham is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.3 by 5.1 centimeters, dated about 1800 and painted by Lawrence Sully. As in his portrait of Miss Annis of the same date, the sitter is shown as a bust on a small plaque of ivory, in thin transparent washes over which the finer detail of eyes, hair and collar is added in tiny touches. The Met bought it in 2006 through the Fletcher Fund.","i":"The sitter's name has survived, which is the first thing a miniature loses and the most valuable thing it can keep. Edward C. Cunningham was painted around 1800, a date at which a man might sit for such a portrait for a betrothal, a voyage or a family record. Lawrence Sully, a miniaturist who worked in America before his early death in 1804, met a steady demand for them, though his output was cut short.","c":"A portrait of this kind is judged by the quiet competence of the head. Sully's manner, with its delicate stipple and mild, even light, fits the taste of 1800, and nothing in a miniature of this size asks for more than care. His work is overshadowed by that of his brother Thomas, but as a record of a named man of the early republic it has the clarity that makes small portraits worth keeping."},"met:11972":{"d":"An unnamed gentleman is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.5 by 5.4 centimeters, dated about 1834. The title records nothing of his name, rank or trade, so the picture is best read as a type: bust-length, built in fine strokes, the sitter probably in the dark coat and white neckcloth that made up the male uniform of the day. Its accession number comes directly before that of the Portrait of a Lady of 1834 from the same donor.","i":"Men's miniatures served many purposes: a gift for a fiancee, a keepsake for parents when a son left home, a record for a family, a token carried on a voyage. The consecutive accession numbers suggest this miniature and the Portrait of a Lady of 1834 came into the Met together and may have been companions, a husband and wife painted the same year, though the museum's record does not say so.","c":"A bust this small gives the miniaturist little to work with beyond the head: at this scale an eye is a few strokes, and the likeness lives or dies on whether those strokes are rightly placed. Russell's miniatures are competent in that respect, in the smooth, restrained manner the clients wanted. The result is dignified and plain, a likeness more than a character."},"met:11973":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.7 by 5.3 centimeters and dated 1834. The title gives no name, so the picture stands as a type: a bust, a tidy surface of fine strokes, and the translucent glow that ivory lends to flesh. Its accession number runs straight on from that of a Portrait of a Gentleman of about the same date, from the same donor, J. William Middendorf II, in 1968, so the two may have been painted as husband and wife.","i":"A husband and wife painted as a pair was an ordinary commission of the 1830s, made as a record of a marriage or as gifts for family. Moses B. Russell worked in the decade when the painted miniature was at its peak in the United States, a few years before the daguerreotype began to replace it. Even with the names lost, such a pair shows how small, private portraits were made, framed and kept.","c":"The miniature of the 1830s follows a standard formula: a bust, a plain ground, a soft and even finish. The merit of a good example is clarity of drawing and freshness of flesh tone; the demerit is a sameness that can make one sitter hard to tell from another. This portrait is a competent, standard product of its decade, and it fills its modest role honestly."},"met:15173":{"d":"A gentleman, unnamed, appears in a watercolor on ivory about 6.6 by 5.3 centimeters and dated 1834. It is one of several Moses B. Russell miniatures at the Met, and its size is close to the others, which suggests that the painter worked from ivory leaves of a standard dimension. Ivory was sawn into thin sheets, flattened and bleached, and painters often roughened the surface a little so that the watercolor would take.","i":"The ivory itself is part of the story. Elephant ivory was cut into plaques as thin as card, and a miniaturist's brush had to work with great care on a surface that was smooth, delicate and costly. Russell's small, carefully finished likenesses, made in a year when such work was at its height, were the portable portraits of a rising middle class, and this sitter joins the large company of unnamed men of the 1830s.","c":"At this date and size, the standard of excellence is a smooth, even finish and an accurate face, and Russell's trade was built on supplying both. The miniatures of the decade show a limited range of poses, so what distinguishes one from another is the sitter. Taken alone, this is a competent, unshowy portrait; taken among Russell's others, it shows a consistent professional hand."},"met:15174":{"d":"An unnamed gentleman appears in a watercolor on ivory, about 6.8 by 5.2 centimeters, with no date recorded. The Met bought it in 2006 through the Fletcher Fund. Without a date or a name, the picture is placed by its maker, Moses B. Russell, and by its type: a bust built from thin washes, a plain ground and a polished, even surface. The small size meant it could be carried in a pocket or kept in a case.","i":"A portrait that has lost both its sitter's name and its date has lost most of its biography, and curators then rely on the object itself: the hair, collar and coat, the way the face is modeled, the size of the ivory. These clues can place a miniature within a decade or so. Museums collect such anonymous pieces as evidence of the period's taste and craft, quite apart from who the sitters were.","c":"With name and date gone, this portrait has to justify itself on quality alone, a hard but fair test. Russell's reliable drawing and smooth finish pass it, though the formula leaves little room to stand out from the many gentleman miniatures of the period. The best that can be said is that it is clear, orderly and unaffected, which is not the worst thing a small portrait can be."},"met:15175":{"d":"An unnamed gentleman appears in a watercolor on ivory, about 7.6 by 5.8 centimeters and undated. It is a little larger than most of the other Moses B. Russell miniatures at the Met, which gives more room around the head and shoulders. A bust portrait of this kind is built from thin transparent washes, with the white ivory showing through to give the flesh its glow, and the extra size would have made it somewhat costlier than the usual plaque. It was given to the museum in 2006 by Gloria Manney.","i":"Miniaturists commonly priced by size, since a bigger ivory took longer and used more material, and a client who chose a larger plaque was paying for presence. A man might do so for a portrait meant for display on a desk or in a parlor and not for a pocket. The sitter's name is lost, but the larger scale hints at a client with some ambition for the picture.","c":"Extra area gives more to judge, and a larger miniature shows weaknesses sooner as well as virtues. The test at this scale is whether the collar, coat and hair are drawn as carefully as the face. A professional miniaturist of the 1830s generally delivered such care as a matter of course, and this is a sound production of its kind, handsomely sized if not surprising."},"met:15176":{"d":"A gentleman, unnamed and undated, appears in a watercolor on ivory about 6.9 by 5.4 centimeters, painted by Moses B. Russell and given to the Met in 2006 by Gloria Manney. Like his other small portraits, it was made to be set in a leather case, a frame or a locket, and its dimensions match his usual format. At this scale the face is only a few centimeters across, and every highlight in the eye and every hair is a single brush touch.","i":"Such miniatures were made for a middle-class clientele, who sat for a few sessions and paid a modest sum for a likeness that could be given away or kept close. A gentleman's portrait could mark an engagement, a business success or simply a wish to be remembered, and the number of names missing from the record shows how easily the story attached to such objects was lost when they left the family.","c":"Russell's small portraits are less about invention than about reliability: a clear head, a quiet ground. That is a virtue in a trade where the sitter's family wanted to recognize the face, and a limit for anyone who looks for personal style. This is a well-judged piece of its kind, pleasant to hold and unlikely to disappoint."},"met:15177":{"d":"A woman with a book is the subject of this larger miniature, a watercolor on ivory about 12.2 by 9.4 centimeters, close in size and subject to the other Russell lady with a book at the Met. Neither the sitter nor the date is recorded. Such large ivories, nearer a hand's breadth than a locket, allowed the painter to work with more of the costume and a fuller setting, and the book gives the picture a point of focus.","i":"A book signals a woman who reads, and to viewers of the period that meant virtue as much as leisure: reading was the proper occupation of a modest, thoughtful woman. Two large miniatures by one painter showing ladies with books suggest that Russell offered the larger format to clients who wanted a more developed picture, and that the book was a favored accessory in such commissions.","c":"Whether the sitter reads, pretends to read or merely holds the book for the portrait, the device works: it gives the figure something to do and the eye something to rest on. The larger the ivory, the less forgiving it is, and this one is best judged by how convincingly figure, hands and book work together. In Russell's modest, reliable manner a sound result is likely, though the interest is competence and not surprise."},"met:15178":{"d":"A woman with a book is the sitter of this large watercolor on ivory, about 12.8 by 10 centimeters, roughly twice the height of most miniatures by Moses B. Russell at the Met. The extra size leaves room for more than a head and shoulders, such as the book that the museum's keywords record. The work is undated and the sitter unnamed, and it was given to the museum in 2006 by Gloria Manney.","i":"A book in a woman's portrait could mean piety, learning or refinement, and the sitter may be shown holding a Bible, a devotional work or a book of poems. At this size the miniature was designed to be displayed in a frame on a table or wall and not carried in the hand, which made it a more public statement. Its cost would have been correspondingly higher.","c":"At twelve centimeters, a miniature is asked to do more than a locket likeness: to compose a figure, a book and a ground. That takes a larger sense of design than the small bust requires, and it exposes any stiffness in pose. Russell's skill at the small bust gives him a good base, but the picture will stand or fall by whether the book and the figure cohere."},"met:15179":{"d":"An unnamed woman appears in a watercolor on ivory, about 7.8 by 5.8 centimeters and undated, painted by Moses B. Russell and bought by the Met in 2006 through the Fletcher Fund. It is slightly taller than the usual Russell bust, so the artist has a little more room for the hair, neck and shoulders, the places where a woman's fashionable costume and coiffure gave a miniaturist the most to do.","i":"The fashion of a woman's hair and dress is the main way to date a portrait that carries no inscription, and costume historians use miniatures like this to track changing taste in ringlets, caps and collars. This one carries no recorded date, so its decade is a matter of reading the hair and collar, a pleasure the form offers to anyone who looks closely.","c":"In a woman's miniature the costume and hair offer the painter chances to show finesse that a man's dark coat does not, and a good miniaturist makes the most of them. Russell is a reliable, professional hand; this is the sort of work he did all the time, and it is more admirable for its consistency than for its originality. It would be wrong to ask more of a keepsake."},"met:441373":{"d":"A girl stands in a courtyard in Algiers, in a tall, narrow oil painting on a wooden panel about 46 by 29 centimeters, dated 1886. The museum now titles the scene for Algiers after earlier cataloguing it as a courtyard in Tangier. The vertical format suits a figure set against walls and doorways, and the small, finished panel on wood is typical of the Orientalist pictures that French painters sold to Paris buyers in the 1880s. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 2016 by Kenneth Jay Lane.","i":"Philippe Pavy was born in 1860 and died in 1891, at about thirty-one, after a short career as a painter of North African subjects. Orientalist painting, the French fashion for scenes of Algeria, Morocco and the Near East, flourished after France's conquest of Algeria began in 1830, and it mixed real observation with a fantasy of a timeless, private East. A picture of a girl in a courtyard belongs to its quiet, domestic branch, where the appeal was atmosphere, costume and light.","c":"Pictures of this kind rest on craft: the textures of wall, cloth and tile are the painter's real subject, and the figure supplies the human note. The charm is real, but so is the convention; the colonial setting, Algiers under French rule in 1886, is seldom part of such pictures' story. Pavy's early death leaves his achievement unfinished, and the small panel is best taken as a promise and a period document."},"met:11406":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 27 by 22 centimeters, shows the New York painter Henry Inman, probably painted between 1837 and 1840 by Jacob Hart Lazarus, then in his teens. A portrait of one artist by another, and above all of a teacher by a pupil, is usually a small, private picture and not a formal commission, made out of respect and for study, and the modest size fits the studio better than the drawing room.","i":"Henry Inman (1801 to 1846) was one of the leading portraitists in New York in the 1830s and 1840s and a prominent figure at the National Academy of Design. His pupil Jacob Hart Lazarus painted him as a teenager, an early work by a painter who would go on to make his living from portraits. The picture was given to the Met in 1893 by Mrs. Jacob H. Lazarus, presumably the painter's widow, which suggests it was kept in the family.","c":"A young artist painting his master takes a risk, and the honest approach is to look hard and flatter little. This early picture is interesting for the relationship it records more than for any daring, and it shows a pupil learning the lessons of a busy portrait studio: a likeness first, a fine surface second. It is modest in size and conventional in format, valued for its subject and its place in a teacher and pupil story."},"met:15094":{"d":"A man named Charles A. Macy is the sitter of this tiny watercolor on ivory, about 5.4 by 4.4 centimeters, dated to 1850 to 1855. The date makes it a late miniature: by then the daguerreotype, announced in 1839, had taken most of the trade in small likenesses, and painted ivories survived largely as a luxury or were often made from photographs. It is by Jacob Hart Lazarus, who also painted portraits in oil, and was given to the Met in 2006 by Gloria Manney.","i":"Miniaturists of the 1850s had to adapt. Some worked from photographs, tinted them, or moved to larger oil portraits, as Lazarus did; others held on to a clientele that prized the delicacy of paint on ivory. The sitter is named, though the title tells no more about him, and the portrait stands as a rare late instance of the form by a painter better known for his oils.","c":"A miniature of the 1850s is a survivor, and the question is whether it argues for the form or merely clings to it. At barely five centimeters it can offer only a face, and the delicacy of a painted face still has a presence that a photograph of the date lacks. Lazarus, better known for his oils, handles the small format as a competent professional; it is a good, plain example, not an innovative one."},"met:11407":{"d":"A formal oil portrait of the banker Joseph W. Drexel, painted in 1877 on a canvas of about 76 by 65 centimeters, close to the standard half-length size of 30 by 25 inches. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so the presentation, probably in the dark clothes and plain background typical of businessmen's portraits of the period, is best taken as the type. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1896 by Drexel's widow.","i":"Joseph William Drexel (1833 to 1888) belonged to the Philadelphia banking family and was a partner in the New York house of Drexel, Morgan and Company; he was also a collector and a patron of music. A portrait of 1877 shows him at the height of his career in the Gilded Age, when bankers commissioned likenesses as a record of standing and families gave them to institutions after death.","c":"Business portraits of the 1870s ask for authority and a good likeness, and Lazarus was a dependable professional trained in the Inman school. The results are rarely surprising, and the paint is applied with workmanlike care rather than flair. The sober dignity of the sitter is the point, and as a record of a Gilded Age banker it does what it was made to do."},"met:11408":{"d":"Jacob Hart Lazarus paints himself in oil on a canvas about 69 by 56 centimeters, dated between 1870 and 1880, when he was in middle age and a working portraitist. As in any self-portrait, the sitter's eye is also the painter's, and the picture shows how a professional wished to present himself to the world. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1908 by Samuel Riker Jr.","i":"A painter's self-portrait is both a likeness and a calling card: it announces what the painter can do. Lazarus, who had trained with Henry Inman, was by this date an established New York portraitist. The Met also holds his portraits of Inman and of Joseph W. Drexel, and the self-portrait completes a small group that shows the range of his career from pupil to professional.","c":"A self-portrait is a test with no client to blame: the sitter is always available and the painter chooses the light. Lazarus was a competent, unshowy professional of the Inman school, and his self-image is best valued as a document of an American trade portraitist in the Gilded Age. It is solid, sympathetic work rather than a revelation."},"nga:59936":{"d":"A young child stands indoors, full length, facing forward with a neutral expression and neatly parted brown hair. The white dress has lace at the collar, puffed short sleeves and an elaborately patterned hem over layered pantaloons, with white socks and black shoes. In the left hand the child holds flowers, and in the right a red ribbon that trails to the floor, where a small cat touches its end. The wall is green-tinted, the floor wood, and a window at the left shows foliage and a warm sunset sky. The canvas is about 122 by 71 centimeters.","i":"Full-length portraits of children holding flowers and playing with pets were a staple of New England itinerant and provincial painters in the 1830s. The flowers and the kitten point to innocence and play, and the child's finely detailed dress to the family's means. Joseph Goodhue Chandler was a Massachusetts portrait painter, and the sitter of this picture is not named in the title.","c":"A charming piece of American provincial portraiture. The frontal pose and the neat, flat description of lace and flowers make the child an icon, while the kitten and the red ribbon trailing to the floor bring a spark of life and give the eye a path downward. The face is mask-like and the proportions doll-like, but the white dress against the green wall is stronger as design than as likeness."},"nga:42408":{"d":"A young boy stands on a dirt path holding strings attached to a wooden toy on wheels, raising his left hand with a stick. He has short, neat light brown hair, fair skin, rosy cheeks and bright eyes, and wears a red and green checkered shirt, gray trousers and black shoes. Behind him are white houses with red chimneys, trees, a cloudy sky and a picket fence. The canvas is tall, about 122 by 64 centimeters, and dated 1850.","i":"Child portraits of the mid-nineteenth century often showed the sitter with a favorite toy, which made the image personal and also announced the family's means. The outdoor setting, with houses and a picket fence, places the boy on a village street. The portrait was probably made for his parents; the record names the sitter as Charles H. Sisson and says little more about him.","c":"The checkered shirt is the most vivid thing in the picture, a pattern worked with the delight of a painter who loved cloth, and the pull toy and the sloping path give the boy something to do, an improvement on stiff standing poses. The face is conventional and the space behind him shallow, but the whole is lively and unpretentious."},"met:437860":{"d":"A woman and a girl with a basket appear by a farm in this oil on wood, about 78 by 100 cm, painted about 1889. The title names a homecoming, and the subjects, a farm, a girl, a woman and a basket, suggest the end of a day's work in the country. Fritz von Uhde, a German painter, brought the lighter, outdoor palette of Impressionism to scenes of rural life.","i":"Uhde, who became a founding member of the Munich Secession in 1892, painted German peasants with the dignity of labor that Millet and Israëls had given the Dutch and French, and in the 1880s he was widely admired for his luminous light. Going home is a classic pastoral theme: the end of work, the shelter of the farm, the continuity of the family. As industry changed the countryside, such pictures held for city buyers an image of a stable rural world.","c":"Uhde's gift is for light and air in a modest subject, and here the open-air feeling counts for more than the anecdote. His peasants are somewhat sweetened beside Liebermann's grimmer realism, and the sentiment is gentle, but the handling is fluent and the color fresh. It is a luminous genre picture, pleasing rather than profound, and a good example of how German painters adapted Impressionism for a public that wanted warmth."},"met:15154":{"d":"A girl is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.9 by 5.5 centimeters, dated 1797. The title records no name, so the picture can be described only as a type: a bust, built from fine strokes of transparent watercolor over the white of the ivory, which lends the flesh its glow. Portraits of children and young girls in this form were treasured by parents, and many survive without a name.","i":"Philippe Abraham Peticolas, a miniaturist active in the 1790s, painted this girl in a decade when children's portraits were fashionable, an expression of the age's new interest in childhood as a separate and tender stage. A miniature of a girl might be commissioned for a mother to carry or to send to relatives far away, at a time when mail and travel were slow and a likeness was the nearest thing to a visit.","c":"Children are hard to paint at miniature scale, because their features are soft and unformed, and a good likeness has to avoid both prettiness and blankness. Peticolas belongs to the polished manner of the late eighteenth century, in which a delicate, even surface was the aim. The result is more likely to please than to surprise: a pleasant, modest example, valuable as a firmly dated work of 1797."},"met:17783":{"d":"A gentleman is the sitter of this watercolor miniature on ivory, dated 1798, painted by Philippe Abraham Peticolas. Pieces of this kind are normally only a few centimeters high, the bust of the sitter built from transparent washes and fine strokes with the white of the ivory showing through. The sitter is unnamed, and the portrait must be read as a type: a small, portable likeness made for family and friends and kept in a case.","i":"A year after the Portrait of a Girl of 1797, Peticolas painted this gentleman, and the two dates place him at work in the closing years of the eighteenth century, when miniatures were a fashionable luxury among merchants, professionals and landowners. A man's miniature might be a gift to a wife, a fiancee or a parent, or a keepsake for a voyage, and was often worn or carried. The Met acquired it in 2004 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","c":"A miniature of the 1790s stands or falls on the drawing of the head, and the aim of the period was a delicate, polished finish, with clear contours and softly modeled flesh. Peticolas works within that standard, and the type is accomplished rather than personal. The value of a piece like this is in its firm date and its survival, small, unglamorous and useful to anyone studying the taste of the decade."},"met:437783":{"d":"A woman's portrait on a thin sheet of ivory, dated about 1830 and painted by the Russian artist Mikhail Ivanovich Terebenev. Ivory miniatures of this date were small enough to be held in one hand, worn in a case or locket, or kept in a drawer, and they were painted in transparent color over the white of the ivory with minute strokes, so that the flesh glows from within. The sitter is unnamed in the Met's title, and the object is best read as an example of the type.","i":"Miniatures traveled: a Russian portrait on ivory in a New York museum is a reminder that these small objects crossed borders as gifts, souvenirs and keepsakes. In the early nineteenth century they were the usual way to exchange likenesses among the gentry and the educated classes, in the Russian empire as in Western Europe, until the daguerreotype of the 1840s took over. The Met holds this one through a gift of the Humanities Fund in 1972.","c":"The standard in Russia at this date was set by watercolor portraitists such as Pyotr Sokolov, who prized delicate finish and a clear, unfussy likeness, and a miniature on ivory by a lesser-known hand is fairly judged against it. The virtues are the same everywhere: a clear head, tender flesh tones and a restrained setting. As a document of the period its quiet competence is its main point."},"met:12799":{"d":"The record lists a woman, a hat, flowers and leaves, and gives the title as Spring, which suggests an idealized figure personifying the season more than a named sitter. The canvas is large, about 91 by 73 centimeters, and was painted in 1838 by Cephas Giovanni Thompson, a portrait painter. No description of pose, dress or setting is recorded, so only the type of picture can be stated with confidence.","i":"Personifying the seasons as women is an old convention in European art, and Spring is usually a young woman crowned with or carrying flowers. In the United States of the 1830s such idealized beauties, sometimes called fancy pictures, were popular, reproduced in gift books and print annuals. Thompson was the son of the portrait painter Cephas Thompson, and the picture belongs to the taste for sentimental female types that his portrait training could easily supply.","c":"Fancy pictures were made for a market that wanted charm more than character, and they succeed or fail on the grace of the figure and the freshness of the flowers. Thompson, a trained portraitist, would have brought sound drawing to the task; what the genre could not give him was a reason to go beyond prettiness. A representative period picture, more interesting for its social history than for any invention."},"met:436591":{"d":"A woman in a cap trimmed with lace and ribbon, and a shawl, is shown with a parrot and a birdcage, in an oil portrait of about 81 by 65 centimeters. The bird gives the picture its distinctive note, and the lace, ribbon and cage invite the eye to wander over small, pleasing details. The German painter Johann Nikolaus Grooth worked within the polite conventions of the Rococo portrait, and the size is that of a likeness made for a domestic interior.","i":"A pet bird in a woman's portrait could stand for several things at once: affection and leisure, the exotic luxuries of a trading age, and the idea of the lady as a cherished, even confined, creature herself. Parrots, which can speak, also carried older associations with eloquence and imitation. Which of these Grooth intended is not recorded, and the portrait may equally be simple pleasure in a fashionable pet.","c":"Pets give a portrait something a bare likeness lacks, and a parrot in a cage is both a gift to the painter, with its plumage, and a test, since birds are hard to paint convincingly. Grooth was a competent German portraitist of modest fame, and the picture's charm lies in the lace, ribbon and bird more than in any depth of character. A pleasant period piece with a lively touch of the exotic."},"met:11015":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimeters, painted about 1830 by George Harvey. As in all miniatures of the type, the bust is built in tiny strokes of transparent color, with the white of the ivory glowing through the flesh. The little portrait came to the Met in 1932 with the collection of Giovanni P. Morosini, presented by his daughter Giulia, and it is dated to the decade when painted miniatures were at the height of their popularity.","i":"George Harvey was born in England and made his career in the United States, first as a miniaturist and later, in the late 1830s, as a landscape painter whose pictures of American scenery in changing weather were called atmospheric landscapes. The miniature trade gave him a living in the 1820s and 1830s, when portraits on ivory were the usual keepsake of the educated and prosperous. A lady's portrait of about 1830 is typical of that early work.","c":"Harvey's later landscapes show a painter fascinated by light, mist and weather, and it is tempting to look for that sensibility in his miniatures. A bust on ivory leaves little room for it, but a miniaturist's training in delicate gradations of tone is exactly what a painter of atmosphere needs. As a portrait it is a standard product of its period; as an early work by a painter who found another subject, it has extra interest."},"met:13492":{"d":"A boy named Joseph Stevens Buckminster Thacher is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5.7 centimeters, painted about 1829 by George Harvey. His long name echoes that of Joseph Stevens Buckminster, a celebrated Boston minister who died young in 1812, and he was probably named in the minister's memory. A portrait of a child on ivory was a precious thing, since it caught a stage of growth that would pass within a few years.","i":"Children were often named for admired relatives or public figures, and a name like this one carries a family's hopes for the boy: that he would be as learned and respected as the minister whose name he bore. Harvey was probably working in Boston in these years, which would fit a sitter from that city. The portrait was bought for the Met in 1990 with the Maria DeWitt Jesup Fund.","c":"A portrait of a child on ivory has to catch the softness of the face without losing the particular child, which is a harder job than it looks. Harvey was a careful craftsman, and the sitter's long name and the date make this a valuable documented example of his early work, before he turned to landscape. The pleasure is in the delicacy of the small head and the record of a name."},"met:15078":{"d":"George Harvey, then about thirty, paints himself in watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated 1830. In a miniature self-portrait the painter works from a mirror and at a scale where his own face is only a few centimeters across, so the picture is a demonstration of control as well as a likeness. The Met bought it in 2006 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"A miniaturist's self-portrait is a calling card, showing a prospective client what he can do, and also a personal record. Harvey was an Englishman who had settled in America in the 1820s; this portrait of 1830 shows him in the first decade of a career that would carry him from miniatures to landscape painting. Such pictures were often kept by the artist or his family, and the fact that this one survives and is dated makes it a useful anchor for his early work.","c":"A miniature self-portrait is a modest ambition, and the interest here is mostly biographical: a painter who would later make his name with weather and atmosphere is seen young, at the small-scale craft by which he made his living, in the year 1830. The scale asks for clarity and economy rather than flourish, and a dated self-portrait by this artist is a useful document of his early manner."},"met:15077":{"d":"A woman named Sarah May Holland is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 9.8 by 7.3 centimeters, painted about 1840 by George Harvey. She is recorded in a collar and a cap with a ribbon, the plain, respectable dress of a married or mature woman of the period. The size is larger than most miniatures, which gave the artist room for the costume as well as the face. It was given to the Met in 2006 by Gloria Manney.","i":"By about 1840 George Harvey was well launched on the landscape work for which he is better known, so a portrait of this date is a late example of his miniatures. The sitter is named in full, which suggests a commission from a family that wanted a documented likeness. Caps like hers were a sign of age and respectability, and in the decade when the daguerreotype was arriving, a larger, carefully painted miniature was still a mark of status.","c":"Mature faces reward the miniaturist more than youthful ones, since age gives character and the painter's care shows. The larger size lets Harvey give the cap and collar their due without crowding the face, a luxury the usual small bust does not allow. A quiet, dignified portrait of the 1840s, valuable for its full name and its date, and a sound example of what a professional could deliver."},"met:436907":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child against a gold ground in a tall panel, about 94 by 56 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood. Only Mary and the Child are shown, which suits a devotional image of the Madonna and Child as a half-length group, a type developed in Siena in the fourteenth century. The picture was painted within a career that ended with Ambrogio Lorenzetti's death about 1348, probably in the Black Death.","i":"The Madonna and Child on a gold ground is the central image of Sienese devotion, for Siena regarded the Virgin as its protector. Ambrogio and his brother Pietro were the leading Sienese painters after Duccio and Simone Martini, and they brought Giotto's sense of solid form to Sienese grace. The tender relationship between mother and child, here as elsewhere, was meant to move the believer's heart.","c":"Ambrogio Lorenzetti is one of the most inventive painters of the fourteenth century, and even in a conventional subject like a half-length Madonna he characteristically gives the Virgin weight and the Child a lively presence. His strength is the union of Sienese line with Florentine sculptural form. A panel of this kind lacks the narrative daring of his Good Government frescoes, but the quality of drawing and the glow of the gold ground reward attention."},"met:435771":{"d":"A woman works at a spinning wheel in a plain Dutch interior, with a man beside her, in a small oil on wood about 48 by 64 centimeters, dated 1653. The subject is work in the home, the kind of modest scene that Leiden painters like Quirijn van Brekelenkam made for burghers who enjoyed seeing their own world in miniature. In his usual manner the light is soft and warm and the surfaces are described with patient care.","i":"Spinning was the standard image of the industrious housewife in Dutch art, and a woman at a wheel stood for thrift, diligence and domestic order, virtues that Calvinist Holland prized. Brekelenkam's interiors usually show craftsmen and women at their tasks, not parties or brothels, and he is valued for the dignity he gives ordinary labor. The Met bought this picture in 1871, among the first European paintings acquired by the young museum.","c":"Brekelenkam is a painter of modest ambition and real charm, close in spirit to Gerrit Dou and the Leiden fine painters but with a plainer, more homely sense of subject. His strengths are warm light, careful domestic detail and gentle humor; his weaknesses are a certain stiffness in figures and a limited range of types. This panel, with its simple theme and its date of 1653, is a good representative of his early maturity."},"met:435772":{"d":"A man and a woman converse in a modest interior, with a musical instrument and a pitcher in view, in a small vertical oil on wood about 41 by 35 centimeters, from the early 1660s. The title points to a courtship scene, a gentle exchange of words between the pair, with the instrument and the pitcher as props that Dutch viewers would have read as hints about music, wine and love. Brekelenkam keeps the scale intimate and the setting plain.","i":"Music and wine were the old companions of love in Dutch pictures, and the word 'sentimental' in the title signals that the conversation is a flirtation. An instrument might suggest harmony, and a pitcher wine and sociability, though such objects could also carry a warning about excess. The Dutch viewer enjoyed this double reading of innocent pleasure and gentle moral, and the Met received the picture through the bequest of Michael Friedsam in 1931.","c":"This is one of Brekelenkam's quieter and more refined subjects, with a pair of figures and a few props, and its charm lies in restraint. He never matches the wit of Gerard ter Borch or Jan Steen, who made the same theme sparkle, but his plainness gives the picture its own sincerity. A small, agreeable work, more modest than the famous courtship scenes of his rivals."},"met:441364":{"d":"A small, open study of palm trees, painted in oil on canvas laid down on board, about 27 by 32 centimeters, dated 1855 to 1856. It is a sketch from nature and not a finished exhibition picture: the kind of quick study that Jean-Léon Gérôme made on his travels to gather motifs for later paintings, and the date places it close to his first journey to Egypt, in 1856. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 2015 by Kenneth Jay Lane.","i":"Gérôme made his name in the 1850s with scenes from antiquity, then turned to the Near East, where the light, costume and architecture gave him material for decades. Studies like this record the look of palms, trunks and fronds for use as background in later compositions, and they show the habit of observation that stood behind his polished finish. The Orientalist painters of the day treated such studies as private working documents.","c":"Gérôme is known for smooth surfaces and meticulous detail, which makes a field study like this a welcome corrective: the painter without the polish, observing a motif for its own sake. Small and unpretentious, it shows the habit of direct looking that lay behind the finish of his large Orientalist scenes, and it is a useful reminder that the cool professional began as a draftsman of what he saw."},"nga:231331":{"d":"A small full-length portrait shows Edouard Delessert standing, in oil on a panel of about 31 by 22 cm, dated 1864. The tall, narrow format of a standing figure on a panel scarcely larger than a sheet of writing paper makes it a cabinet portrait, a private likeness in the tradition of small, finely finished figures at which Gérôme, the leading academic painter of the Second Empire, excelled.","i":"The Delessert were a prominent Parisian family of bankers and public figures, and a small portrait by a famous painter was the kind of picture exchanged between friends and patrons in the cultured circles of the Second Empire. A full-length figure on a small panel gives the sitter the dignity of a large portrait on an intimate scale. Without more record of the occasion, it is best read as a likeness made for friendship or esteem, a token of a social world.","c":"Gérôme's gift is a clear, smooth, precise finish, and a full-length figure on a small panel suits that skill, since every detail can be rendered with care. The limits of his manner are the cool distance and polish that critics, then and now, have found chilly. As a private likeness it may be warmer than his grand set pieces. It is a refined small portrait rather than an innovative one."},"met:440723":{"d":"A young Black man is shown from the chest up, in profile, turned to the right, against a dark olive ground. He wears a tall turban of layered cloth in yellow, green, red and white, topped with a red cap and hung with tassels and beads at the side. His tunic is quilted rose-pink silk edged with green braid, with a leather strap across the chest. The barrel of a long rifle rises behind his shoulder at the left, and at lower right the ivory handles of pistols or daggers show at his belt. Silk, cloth and metal are painted in very fine detail. The artist signed at left.","i":"Bashi-bazouk is a Turkish term meaning 'broken-head' and refers to poorly paid Ottoman irregular soldiers who fought for plunder and had a fierce reputation. Gérôme painted this after a twelve-week journey to the Near East in 1868, and the Metropolitan Museum says he dressed a model in his Paris studio in garments and accessories he had bought abroad. It is hard to imagine a soldier charging into battle in such fine silk, which is part of the picture's appeal and its artifice. The model is not identified. Gérôme was then at the height of his career, and Orientalist pictures of this kind were popular with collectors.","c":"The technique is assured: the silk catches the light, each tassel has its own weight, and the cool dark ground sets off the pink. But the man is almost a prop. The face, in profile and half-shaded, shows dignity and nothing more, and the costume gets more attention than the character. Because the outfit was assembled in a studio, the claim to documentary truth that Orientalist painting made is hollow here. As a display of texture it is first rate; as a statement about a person it is empty."},"met:651837":{"d":"A bashi-bazouk, one of the irregular soldiers of the Ottoman army, is shown in a small oil on canvas, about 26 by 22 centimeters, dated about 1868 to 1869. The museum's keywords record a moustached man with a rifle, a pistol, headgear and a military uniform, so the picture is a character study of a type: a heavily armed fighter in the costume he would have worn. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 2014 by Kenneth Jay Lane.","i":"Bashi-bazouk, from the Turkish for roughly 'damaged head', meant an irregular, unpaid soldier, and the name became a byword in Europe for undisciplined and brutal troops. Gérôme painted several of these figures after his Egyptian travels, in portraits and groups, as he did with other soldiers and dancers of the Ottoman world, and they answered a Paris market for vivid ethnographic types. The picture mixes observation with theater.","c":"Gérôme's gift was for painting costume and weapons with the accuracy of a collector, and a bust of a soldier shows it at close range: the moustache, the weapons and the headgear are all the subject. What it lacks is psychology. The figure is a type, seen from the outside, in the way Orientalist painting often treated its subjects, and the cool professionalism of the finish is the picture's chief limit as well as its chief pleasure."},"nga:162261":{"d":"About a dozen people walk, and a few ride horses, across a bridge over a river that winds through a sandy landscape, with palms leaning over the water and brown and cream buildings along both banks. A donkey laden with sacks crosses toward the viewer, a second plank bridge leads toward a ruined building with pointed arches and a minaret, and robes of white, aquamarine, brick red and black stand out under a vivid blue sky. The oil on wood is about 38 by 56 cm.","i":"The Fayoum is an oasis south-west of Cairo, watered by a canal from the Nile, and Gérôme traveled to Egypt several times from 1856, filling sketchbooks and collecting costumes and props for his studio. For French viewers after Napoleon's Egyptian campaign, Egypt was both a land of ancient history and a living picturesque. Such pictures present the region through the eyes of a visitor, with a precision that can seem objective but frames a selective, exotic view.","c":"The T-shaped bridge gives the composition a strong geometry, and the low horizon and vivid sky open the space. Gérôme's precision of architecture and costume is at its best in such views, which read like a traveler's record polished into a picture. The result is cool and staged: the figures are placed more than observed, and the whole lacks the living warmth of Delacroix or the atmosphere of Fromentin. It is a skilled, clear, rather distant performance."},"met:436482":{"d":"A long arcaded hall recedes into depth, its arches banded in red and white and carried on slender stone columns with carved capitals. Iron lamps and a chandelier hang from the beams, and white pigeons fly or peck on the stone floor. In the foreground a man in a red and gold robe stands on a small prayer rug with his back to the viewer, while two men in turbans and short jackets stand beside him at the right. Further back, rows of worshippers pray in groups, and near the left-hand column a nearly naked man stands with his arms at his sides. The light is even and clear and picks out each joint of the paving and each cast shadow with photographic crispness.","i":"Gérôme travelled in Egypt several times from 1856 and painted scenes there that mixed sketches, photographs and studio invention. Historical records suggest that this is the Mosque of Amr in Cairo. The Met lists the picture by 1874, and it passed with the collection of the New York philanthropist Catharine Lorillard Wolfe to the Museum in 1887. Critics have pointed out several inaccuracies in the postures and groupings, so it is not a reliable record of Muslim prayer. Since Linda Nochlin's essay The Imaginary Orient (1983), such paintings have usually been read as Western constructions of the East rather than as documents.","c":"Gérôme's technique is most convincing in the architecture: the rhythm of striped arches pulls the eye down the hall, and the pigeons and lamps add life to a space that could have been empty. The people are less successful. The worshippers are small, stiff and treated as costume pieces among the stone, and the half-naked figure says more about European fantasy than about Cairo. It is a polished, precise and quietly condescending picture, and that combination is why it remains a standard example of Academic Orientalism."},"met:436481":{"d":"In a Cairo coffeehouse, soldiers, a man and a woman share the room, with a musical instrument among the props, in an oil on canvas of about 55 by 63 centimeters, dated 1884 or earlier. The subtitle names the action: bullets are being cast, presumably by the soldiers for their guns, an everyday job done in the shade of a public room. Gérôme sets a casual scene of idle and busy people in a Cairo interior, with the figures grouped as though on a stage.","i":"Gérôme visited Egypt several times, and he drew on notebooks, photographs and the costumes and objects he brought home to compose scenes like this in his Paris studio. A coffeehouse was a place of conversation, gambling and music, and a favorite Orientalist subject because it let the painter gather figures of different types in one scene. The picture was bequeathed to the Metropolitan Museum by Henry H. Cook in 1905.","c":"A crowd of types in a decorative interior was a Gérôme specialty, and this picture shows both his skills and his limits: crisp drawing, a knowing choice of details and an exact sense of surfaces, but little warmth of feeling. The figures are posed like actors who know their places, which makes the scene tidy and slightly theatrical. It is an accomplished studio construction, admired in its day for its apparent truth, but it pictures Egypt through a visitor's eye."},"met:685040":{"d":"A woman on a balcony is the subject of this oil on canvas, about 50 by 42 centimeters, painted in 1887 to 1888, when Gérôme was in his sixties. The museum's keywords place it among his Orientalist pictures, in which women in interiors, courtyards and on balconies offered the attractions of costume, pattern and a private world glimpsed from outside. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 2015 by Kenneth Jay Lane.","i":"Late Orientalism increasingly turned from public scenes to private figures: women in interiors, on terraces or at the water's edge. Pictures of this kind were studio inventions, built from props, photographs and memory, and they reflect a Western fantasy of seclusion as much as any observed reality. A woman at a balcony would have appealed to Paris buyers for her costume and for the sense of a private world.","c":"In his last decades Gérôme returned to a small repertoire of successful motifs, and a woman at a balcony is one of them. The craft is high and the feeling cool; the interest of such a late picture lies in the controlled surface of a painter in his sixties and in how far the Orientalist formula had become a convention. It is best seen as a polished, slightly remote example of the type."},"met:436484":{"d":"A tiger with its cubs, the subject given by the title and the museum's keyword, fills a canvas of about 74 by 91 centimeters, dated about 1884. Gérôme painted wild animals in his later career, and big cats were a regular subject; a mother with her young is a theme that lets a painter combine majesty with tenderness. The picture was bequeathed to the Metropolitan Museum in 1936 by Susan P. Colgate, in memory of her husband, Romulus R. Colgate.","i":"Big cats were a favorite subject of nineteenth-century painters and sculptors, who sketched them in menageries and zoos, and a mother with cubs allowed a classic contrast: danger and protectiveness. Gérôme, who also worked in sculpture, had a trained sense of form in the muscular body of a cat. Tigers also signified the East in the European imagination, and the animal belonged to the same imaginary geography as his Orientalist scenes.","c":"A painter of finished detail like Gérôme is well suited to fur and muscle, and big cats allowed him to display control of texture and anatomy without the moral baggage of costume pictures. What such pictures usually lack is wildness: the animals are posed with the care of a studio sitter. As a record of a favorite late subject it is capable and popular, if less probing than the animal pictures of Delacroix or Barye."},"met:436483":{"d":"In a sculptor's studio the figures named in the title meet at the moment of the miracle: Pygmalion kisses the statue Galatea as she comes to life, her marble turning to warm flesh. The museum's keywords record a studio, a kiss, a statue and Cupid, and describe a woman with brown hair in a chignon seen from the back. The canvas measures about 89 by 69 centimeters and dates from about 1890, when Gérôme was in his mid-sixties. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1927 by Louis C. Raegner.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses: Pygmalion, a sculptor of Cyprus disgusted by real women, carved an ivory woman so beautiful that he fell in love with her and prayed to Venus, who brought the statue to life. For nineteenth-century artists the tale was a parable of art's power to create life, and for Gérôme, who was also a sculptor, it spoke of his own two crafts. He also treated the subject in sculpture.","c":"This is the Gérôme of the later years, the academic virtuoso: crisp drawing, smooth modeling and a literary idea made visible in a single image. The moment of transformation, from marble to flesh, is a fine problem for a painter who loved surfaces, and he solves it with cool skill. The embrace is a little stagey and the erotic charge carefully controlled, but the painting's cleverness makes it one of his best-known late works."},"nga:50721":{"d":"We look slightly down on a wide panorama of dozens of skaters and onlookers on a frozen river. Most of the men wear black hats and suits, some with white collars and short capes, and the women wear long skirts, some in black cloaks, with touches of scarlet, sage green and sky blue. At the lower right a man kneels to tie a skate near a dog, two boys play a game like hockey, and a couple ride a horse-drawn sled at the lower center. Shimmering gray buildings and boats line the horizon under a sky as pale as the ice.","i":"Avercamp, known as the mute of Kampen, was the great specialist of the winter landscape, and his ice scenes show the Dutch Republic at play: gentlemen, farmers, children and beggars share the ice, each class recognizable by clothing, and little incidents, a man tying skates, a fall, a couple courting, fill the scene. The Little Ice Age made such frozen rivers common in the early 1600s. Viewers enjoyed spotting the stories in the crowd, which perhaps also carried a gentle reminder of human folly.","c":"Avercamp's special gift is to keep an immense crowd legible: the clear, cool light, the tilted viewpoint and the tiny, precisely observed figures let the eye move from incident to incident without confusion. The colors, mostly black and gray with a few bright spots, are skillfully controlled, and the pale sky and ice unify the picture. It is full of life and never fussy, with a great deal of humor in little space."},"nga:61360":{"d":"A harbour crowded with tall-masted sailing ships and steamers dressed in colourful flags, in a horizontal panel about 41 by 59 centimetres. The masts of a black and white ship docked at the left reach almost to the top edge, hung with long strings of red, yellow, blue and white flags, and small boats full of people in light clothing cluster around it. Another vessel in the middle and a dark one-funnelled steamship at the right are festooned in the same way. The water is busy with swimmers, buildings crowd the far shore, and the sky is blue with soft white clouds.","i":"Honfleur was Boudin's native town, a Norman port at the mouth of the Seine, and this view of its harbour on a day of celebration dates from 1858, when he was in his mid-thirties and beginning to make a name. Sailing ships and steamers lie side by side, a sign of the changing age of trade. In the later 1850s Boudin befriended the young Claude Monet and encouraged him to paint outdoors.","c":"A crisp, lively harbour scene in which the forest of masts and the strings of flags make a decorative pattern against the sky. The bright flags give vivid accents, the brushwork is brisk, and the busy water adds life. The composition is more crowded and tidy than the loose, airy beach scenes of his later years, and the figures are mere flecks, but it is a good early example of his gift for the atmosphere of harbours."},"nga:61363":{"d":"Several people gather on the shore between four white tent-like bathing structures in the centre of a small panel, about 31 by 48 centimetres. They are dressed in colourful gowns and suits, many in hats or holding parasols, and behind them the sea stretches away, with a few swimmers at the left. The sand and tents are earthy in tone, the clothing bright in whites, blacks, blues and reds, and the sky is a soft blue and grey with a few wispy clouds.","i":"From the 1850s the railway brought fashionable Parisians to the Normandy coast, and sea-bathing became a social ritual for the French upper classes. Boudin was among the first painters to turn the new resort beaches into a subject, and this small panel of 1862 is an early example. The tents sheltered bathers, and visitors kept fully dressed on the sand, so the crowd appears in bright formal clothes that Boudin reduces to flickering notes of colour.","c":"A tiny, quick painting in which the crowd is reduced to dabs of white, black and red against the neutral sand, and the loose handling anticipates Impressionism. The colour is fresh and the light airy. The scale is very small and the figures have no individuality, but that anonymity is the point: Boudin paints the social scene, not the people. It is typical of the pictures that made his reputation."},"met:435749":{"d":"A beach at the fashionable Normandy resort of Trouville is the subject of this small panel, about 27 by 48 centimeters, painted in 1863. A horse and carriage, a man, a woman and a dog share the sand, and the details of leisure are kept small beneath a wide sky. Eugène Boudin painted such scenes on the spot or from quick studies, working in oil on wood to catch changing light and weather.","i":"In the early 1860s the railway brought Parisians to the Normandy coast, and resorts like Trouville became places to see and be seen. Boudin recorded the crinolined ladies and top-hatted gentlemen as small figures within the beach and sky, and his pictures did not flatter the fashionable crowd so much as note it. He had already introduced the young Claude Monet to painting outdoors in the late 1850s, and his skies were admired by Corot and Baudelaire.","c":"Boudin's gift is to let light and air govern the whole picture: the figures become quick touches of color, and the sky is the real subject. The panel is slight in size and incident, but its freshness and truth to weather are exactly what the Impressionists admired. The formula could become repetitive, yet in a good example like this the observation stays alive."},"nga:61359":{"d":"A wharf spans this horizontal panel, about 35 by 58 centimetres, and a jetty stretches into the distance at a right angle, crowded with more people. In the foreground a dozen men, women and children walk, stand or sit: the men in brown or black suits and hats, the women in hoop skirts, capes and veiled hats of sky blue, red, grey and mustard. A couple sit on an ice-blue bench beside a lone street lamp, a small brown dog runs to them, and a sailboat ripples past across a strip of water. Two flagged masts and a steamer's pipe, pouring grey smoke, rise beyond.","i":"The jetty at Trouville was one of the places to see and be seen: a promenade for the visitors, who strolled along it to watch the boats and each other. The steam pipe with its smoke belongs to the modern world of coastal steamers, and the street lamp is part of the seaside's new furniture. Boudin signed and dated the panel in 1863 and treats the crowd as a pattern of small colourful figures whose features are only suggested.","c":"A deft piece of observation: the diagonal of the jetty draws the eye into depth, the lone street lamp anchors the centre and the dogs add a humorous touch. The figures are no more than quick touches of colour, and their faces are blurred, but they convey the poses and manners of the promenade unmistakably. The picture is lightly painted, almost casual, and its charm is in that spontaneity."},"nga:61364":{"d":"About two dozen men and women sit in straight-backed wooden chairs along a sandy beach near low, gently breaking waves, with sailboats on the sea beyond. Most have their backs to the viewer: the women in long full dresses and shawls of black, grey, blue, rust and cream, some in straw hats banded in black or baby blue, and the men in black top hats and suits, with parasols raised. Two children kneel in the sand at the centre, two dogs stand at the right, and two tall poles carry a blue, white and red banner and a red one.","i":"Boudin's beach pictures of the 1860s record the new rituals of the seaside: rented chairs set in rows, parasols to protect against the sun, and a crowd that watches the sea or one another. Painting them from behind, as the spectators saw the sea, gives the scene a candid, modern air. The tricolour on the pole marks the French setting. Baudelaire had praised Boudin's quick studies of skies in 1859, and these beaches carry the same open-air freshness.","c":"The crowd is handled as a flickering pattern of colour, and the low waves, painted in thick strokes of lilac, turquoise and aquamarine, are a real pleasure. The figures with their backs turned feel unposed, and the whole picture has the bright, breezy look that Monet admired in Boudin. The repetition of the beach formula in his many Trouville scenes can seem monotonous, but here the freshness is intact."},"met:437987":{"d":"A beach at Dieppe at low tide is shown in this nearly square panel of about 32 by 29 centimeters, dated 1864. Tourists, a man, a woman and a dog stand on the sand, a sailing ship appears offshore, and clouds fill the sky. Eugène Boudin, a Norman painter, made this small oil on wood as a study of everyday seaside life, in which light and weather count for more than any story.","i":"Dieppe was one of the first French seaside resorts, made fashionable in the 1820s by the Duchess of Berry, and it drew visitors from Paris and across the Channel. Low tide opens a wide stage of wet sand that reflects the sky, a feature Boudin loved. The tourists are small and anonymous, and the picture records the new habit of seaside leisure without moralizing about it.","c":"The composition follows Boudin's winning formula of a low horizon, a large sky and small figures, and the near-square format gives the clouds extra room. There is a slight monotony in the way he repeated this scheme, but the freshness of touch and the truth of the damp air make each example persuasive. It looks forward to Monet's early beach scenes."},"nga:46479":{"d":"About three dozen men and women sit, stand or stroll along a sandy beach beneath a sunset-streaked sky, in a long horizontal canvas about 46 by 76 centimetres. The people take up only about a quarter of the height. The women wear long-sleeved dresses with tight bodices and hoop skirts in baby blue, grey, butter yellow, brown, crimson, black or white, and the men long-tailed suits and rounded hats; most sit in wooden chairs, and a brown and white dog sits in the foreground. Thin screens of grey cloud, pink on their undersides, break to show soft yellow and blue.","i":"Villerville is a village on the Normandy coast between Honfleur and Trouville, and in the 1860s it drew painters as well as holidaymakers. Corot is said to have called Boudin the king of skies, and the evening light here, with its streaked clouds and low sun, shows why. By setting a small human crowd beneath a large sky, he turns the beach into a stage for atmosphere, and the figures become silhouettes against the glow.","c":"The sky is the real subject: the screens of cloud with their pink undersides and the peeks of yellow and blue at the horizon give a convincing sense of the end of a day on the coast. The figures are small notes of dark and light, and the long horizontal format makes the beach a calm band beneath. The effect is gentle rather than dramatic, but the observation of light is exact and beautifully handled."},"met:438551":{"d":"A woman and a man stand on a beach at sunset in this oil on wood panel of about 38 by 58 centimeters, dated 1865. The title makes the evening light the real subject, and the figures are small within a wide shoreline. Eugène Boudin painted many such pictures, using the horizontal format of the panel to stretch the sea, sand and sky across the picture and to catch the glow of the setting sun.","i":"Sunset on the beach was a favorite motif for Boudin, who kept notebooks and studies of skies, often noting the time and weather. In the 1860s the Normandy coast attracted both visitors and artists, and the changing light was a proof of the new commitment to painting directly from nature. The couple's presence gives the scene a human note, a sense of time spent unhurried at the sea's edge.","c":"The strength of this panel lies in the color of the sky and its reflection on the wet sand, which Boudin handles with great economy. The figures serve as scale and mood, not as portraits. Compared with his daytime beach scenes, a sunset gives him a warmer, more romantic effect, though the risk of prettiness is never far away."},"nga:52156":{"d":"Eight men, women and children sit on straight-backed chairs along a sandy beach near low waves, in a small horizontal panel about 27 by 49 centimetres. The group fills the left half, in black, white and shades of blue, the women in straw hats with royal-blue bands and holding blue parasols; one woman in white sits apart at the right. A white and brown dog stands near the chairs, a pole with a blue banner rises at the left, a rider on a brown horse passes, women walk and children play on the sand, and five white sailboats line the horizon beneath pale grey cloud.","i":"The picture shows the more intimate side of a Trouville beach: a handful of people, rather than a crowd, with the resort's ritual of rented chairs and parasols. A rider on the sand and children at play show other pastimes. Boudin painted dozens of such pictures in the 1860s, finding endless variety in the same elements of sand, sea, cloud and crinoline, and sold them probably to visitors and collectors who had seen the beaches themselves.","c":"A small, delicate panel in which the repeated blue of parasols, hat-bands and banner holds the whole picture together, and the breezy air is palpable. The group is neatly arranged, and the woman in white at the right gives a nice counterweight. The figures are summary and the scene slight, but the freshness and economy are Boudin's best qualities, and here they are well displayed."},"nga:61358":{"d":"About two dozen men and women sit in straight-backed chairs on a sandy expanse among pockets of water, beneath a slate-blue sky that fills the top two-thirds of a horizontal panel about 35 by 58 centimetres. Most face away from the viewer, the women in dark hats, full skirts and shawls of black, red, dark yellow and cream, some holding blue parasols, and the men in black bowler hats and suits. Three changing huts stand behind the group, two brown horses wait at the water's edge, and three poles march off to the right where more people gather across a pool.","i":"Deauville, next door to Trouville, was a new resort launched in the 1860s by the Duc de Morny, half-brother of Napoleon III, and it quickly became the fashionable place for sea-bathing and horse racing. The title refers to the hour when bathers entered the water, and the crowd sits to watch. Boudin painted the new seaside society as a spectator, and signed and dated this panel in 1865.","c":"The weight of the picture is in the sky, a leaden slate-blue that fills two-thirds of the panel and sets the people as small dark accents on the pale sand. The grey light and damp sand give a convincing feel of Normandy weather. The colour is subdued and the scene rather static, but the composition is balanced and the atmosphere true, which is what Boudin cared about most."},"nga:66401":{"d":"A fashionable crowd gathers under a large outdoor pavilion, in a horizontal canvas about 42 by 73 centimetres. The people, mostly seen from behind, are elegantly dressed, some standing and some seated in chairs; at the right of centre three women in large white gowns stand with a bearded man in a pale yellow scarf, and at the left seated women wear pink, white, blue and yellow gowns. Under the ornate white pavilion with carved columns, a man raising a baton appears only as a silhouette, and green hills and distant white and brown buildings lie beyond.","i":"The casino at Deauville, opened in the 1860s, offered concerts and gaming to the resort's visitors, and the crowd came to be seen as much as to listen. Boudin turns his back on the performers and paints the audience, with the conductor reduced to a silhouette, so that the subject is the social occasion. It is a record of the music that was part of the seaside season and of the wide white gowns that filled the beaches and promenades.","c":"A deft piece of society painting: the pale gowns, the blur of dark coats and the conductor's tiny silhouette are caught with loose, dashing strokes, and the haze of the brushwork suggests the drift of music in the open air. The picture has little focus and no individual character, but it captures a mood of summer gaiety. Beside Degas's concert pictures it is lighter, looser and more decorative."},"met:435751":{"d":"A village sits beside a river in this oil on wood panel of about 36 by 58 centimeters, probably painted in 1867. The title names only the village and the river, not the place, and the picture belongs to the inland landscapes that Eugène Boudin painted alongside his better-known beach scenes. The horizontal panel invites a view across water to a cluster of houses.","i":"Although Boudin is remembered for beaches and skies, he painted inland river valleys and villages throughout his career, often with the same attention to weather and reflection. River villages offered him mirror-like water, which doubled the sky, and a low skyline of roofs. The location is not named in the title, so the picture speaks more of a type of place than of a specific one.","c":"Without the beach crowds to give it incident, the picture depends on quiet tones and the relationship between water, buildings and sky. Boudin solves this with soft light and restrained color. It is an unassuming painting, and it shows the same truthful vision at a quieter pitch; those who know him only for the beach may be surprised by his sensitivity to inland places."},"nga:52157":{"d":"Eleven men, women and children stand around a freestanding flagpole near a floating dock on a beach, in a very small horizontal panel about 18 by 27 centimetres, and dozens more people throng the dock itself. The women wear long skirts of charcoal grey, dark yellow, garnet red and black, one carrying a muted blue parasol, and the men dark grey suits. A black dog stands and a white dog lies on the yellow sand beside a flag edged in blue, while the dock angles away into pale aquamarine water under big grey clouds in a blue sky.","i":"Panels as small as this were Boudin's way of working directly from nature. A pioneer of painting out of doors, he made small studies of the beach and sky on the spot, which could be finished quickly and sold or kept as reference. The title records the dock as a place of gathering, and the picture catches a moment of the resort's social life in about 1870, with the crowd reduced to flecks of dark and colour against the pale sand and sea.","c":"A tiny picture of great economy: the dock cuts a diagonal through the panel, the people are touches of dark and colour and the sky is generous with cloud. The brushwork is quick and the colours fresh. Its small scale means there is little detail, and it should be seen as a sketch rather than a finished work, but it shows how much Boudin could say in a few strokes."},"nga:61361":{"d":"A seascape in a horizontal canvas about 47 by 66 centimetres, with the horizon in the bottom third. Several ships float on greenish-blue water flecked with white, with a large sailing ship left of centre. On the right a tan and green coastline with hints of buildings extends toward the middle, a dark grey coast lies at the left, and a rocky outcrop in earthy browns fills the lower right corner. The sky takes up the upper two-thirds, light blue and heaped with thick white and grey clouds.","i":"Brittany's rugged coast, with its sailors and fishermen, was a different world from the fashionable Norman beaches, and Boudin returned there repeatedly. The picture records no particular spot, but the rocks, the choppy water and the sailing ships give the feel of the Atlantic coast. It was painted in 1870, the year of the Franco-Prussian War. Boudin valued clouds so highly that skies often occupy most of his canvases, as here.","c":"A fresh, assured seascape in which the sky and the choppy water do most of the work. The scale of the clouds against the thin line of the horizon gives a sense of space, and the quick, thick strokes of the waves are convincing. The composition is simple and the ships are small, but the atmosphere is true, and it shows Boudin's skill with the grey, changeable light of the northern coast."},"nga:61362":{"d":"Twenty men, women and children sit, stand or stroll along a sandy beach beneath a bright, cloud-filled sky, in a horizontal canvas about 38 by 61 centimetres. The figures are small, taking up about a quarter of the height: women in long-sleeved dresses of baby blue, grey, teal, crimson and tan, three holding parasols, and men in tail suits and rounded hats, about half of them seated in wooden chairs. A child plays in the sand with two women at the left, a white dog with brown spots runs across the foreground, and a rowboat with five people floats offshore.","i":"By the late 1860s the Normandy beaches were established as a summer meeting place of Parisian society, and Boudin painted its human comedy: children digging in the sand, women in the latest fashions, a dog running among the chairs. His pictures were bought by visitors who wanted a souvenir of the season. The people are tiny, so that the sky and sea dominate, a balance that Boudin returned to again and again.","c":"A cheerful, well-balanced beach scene. The small groups scattered across the sand, the children and the dog give variety and gentle humour, and the bright, cloud-filled sky keeps the whole open and airy. The painting is loose to the point that faces vanish, and the figure types repeat from picture to picture, but the careful placing of the little groups shows that Boudin's apparent spontaneity was well considered."},"nga:178080":{"d":"A busy market scene at eye level, in a small panel about 27 by 46 centimetres, with a crowd of people and cattle in front of a line of rooftops and a tall church spire. Hills lie in the distance under a cloudy grey sky. The people are indistinct, but many appear to wear white or red head coverings and clothes in earthy tones. The brushstrokes are thick and visible, giving the scene a blurred appearance and the feeling of a quick impression.","i":"Fairs and cattle markets were a feature of Breton country life, and the white head coverings of the women were part of the local costume that fascinated visitors. Boudin turned from the elegant beaches to such rural scenes, and in 1874 he took part in the first Impressionist exhibition, which showed that his quick, open-air manner belonged with the younger painters he had influenced. The picture shows the market as a mass of figures and animals rather than as individuals.","c":"A rapid, thickly painted sketch: the crowd and cattle are rendered as a blur of dark and bright touches, the grey sky presses on the roofs, and the church spire anchors the scene. Little can be made out in detail, and the effect is deliberately unfinished, which was a risk in 1874 but is now the picture's charm. It conveys the noise and movement of a fair with remarkable economy."},"nga:231681":{"d":"A seascape in a horizontal canvas about 43 by 64 centimetres shows a beach with the sea and sailboats beyond. Scheveningen is the seaside town on the Dutch coast near The Hague, where fishing boats were drawn up on the open sand. The horizon is high in the picture, and expressive brushstrokes build up the clouds, the boats on the sand and the people near them. The colours are sandy browns, muted greens and vivid blues, giving the picture a dynamic, textured surface.","i":"Scheveningen was both a fishing village and a growing resort, and its wide beach with boats hauled onto the sand was a favourite subject for Dutch painters. Boudin admired the Dutch painters of the seventeenth century, who had made skies and shores their subject, and on his travels in the Low Countries he renewed that link. The title records the tide: at low water the beach opens out, boats rest on the sand and the wide expanse of the shore comes into view.","c":"A fresh, free picture in which the clouds are the star, built up in thick, expressive strokes, and the wide shore gives a feeling of space. The boats on the sand are summarised with a few touches, and the people nearby are specks of colour. The elements are few and the colour modest, but the vigorous handling and the sense of air and light show Boudin's gift for coastal skies."},"nga:46562":{"d":"A two-masted ship with its sails furled has been pulled onto a beach, and dozens of people bustle on and around it. The ship fills the right half of the horizontal canvas, listing slightly with its bow lodged in the sand, while people on deck pass crates and goods down to others on a ladder and a horse-drawn cart. The women wear long skirts, coloured shawls and white headdresses, the men earth browns and greys with a few brick-red caps, and bundles and baskets are piled in the foreground. The sky is nearly all slate-grey cloud. The painter inscribed the place, Portrieux.","i":"A terre-neuvier was a ship that sailed each spring to fish for cod off Newfoundland, Terre-Neuve in French, and returned in autumn with its salted catch; the industry sustained ports in Brittany and Normandy but cost many lives. The return was an event for the whole community, and Boudin shows the unloading at Portrieux in Brittany. The picture records a working world quite unlike his elegant resort beaches.","c":"One of Boudin's larger and weightier pictures, and a notable change from the holiday beaches. The bulk of the stranded ship, the busy chain of workers and the lowering sky give it a sober, working energy, and the loose handling suits the bustle. The crowd lacks a clear focus and the colour is subdued, but the picture has a documentary sincerity and a feeling for the hardship and pride of the Breton fishing world."},"nga:61365":{"d":"A sandy shore and a calm sea with sailing boats in the distance, in a small horizontal panel about 28 by 41 centimetres. At the bottom people sit on the sand by the water, and brown rocks are scattered among the tan sand, which curves up toward the right. On the blue water beyond, sailboats dot the low horizon, and at the left another stretch of sand juts into the sea with a sailboat heeled over on it. Billowy white clouds fill the top half of the panel, and the brushstrokes in sand and water are plainly visible.","i":"The title suggests that the figures at the bottom include a washerwoman, one of the women who washed linen at the shore and in tidal pools and spread it to dry on the sand. Such women are a reminder that Trouville's elegant holidaymakers depended on the labour of local people. Boudin, who painted both sides of the coast, gives them a small place in a view dominated by sea and sky. The date is about 1872 to 1876.","c":"A modest panel whose pleasure is in the sea and sky rather than in the figures. The wide curve of the shore leads the eye to the sails, the clouds are painted with quick, billowing strokes, and the tiny figures give scale. The composition is a little unresolved and the figures so small that the washerwoman of the title is hard to find, but the light is convincing and the handling fresh."},"nga:52158":{"d":"A very small, narrow panel, only about 11 by 26 centimetres, in which a row of a dozen men and women sit, stand or stroll along a sandy beach beneath a pale blue sky. The women wear long-sleeved dresses in charcoal grey, parchment brown, vivid red, sapphire blue or white, five of them sheltering under black parasols; the men wear dark suits and straw-yellow hats. A looser group stands nearer the water at the right, and dark smudges near the horizon suggest sailboats and a steamer. Water and sky are similar tones of muted ice blue.","i":"A strip this narrow and small is a quick outdoor sketch, and Boudin made many such panels in the 1860s and 1870s, from which he sometimes developed larger pictures. The row of figures along the sand, caught in a few touches, treats the beach as a stage on which fashionable society promenades. The date 1877 falls after the first Impressionist exhibition, in which Boudin had taken part, and his handling here is as free as that of the younger painters.","c":"A tiny picture of remarkable economy: the figures, made of dots and dashes, march across the panel like notes on a stave, and the near-identical blue of sea and sky gives a pearly unity. It has no pretension beyond catching a moment, and it should not be judged as a finished composition, but as a record of seaside light and fashion in a space the size of a postcard it is delightful."},"nga:52159":{"d":"A group of women stand and sit on a sandy beach beneath a vast, light blue sky, in a small panel about 25 by 36 centimetres. They wear brown, dark blue and red clothing and headscarves, mostly white, with one dark blue; some carry baskets and others set them on the sand. The horizon runs through the centre, where the pale sea blends into the sky, and faint daubs near the shore suggest other figures. The thick brushstrokes that form the women's shapes are plainly visible, and small details are suggested with dashes and dots.","i":"Berck-sur-Mer, on the coast of northern France, was a fishing village that became a resort and a centre for seaside hospitals, and Boudin painted there in the early 1880s. The headscarves and baskets suggest local women of the fishing community, perhaps gathering shellfish or waiting for the boats, rather than holidaymakers. Boudin's attention to working people on the shore became stronger in his later years.","c":"The group of women is a compact, rhythmic mass of white scarves and dark skirts against a huge pale sky, and the thick, dabbed paint makes them seem part of the beach itself. The sky and shore are painted in a very limited range, and the effect is one of calm space. The figures are summary and the scene shows little action, but the picture is a sensitive, unsentimental view of working women and has a quiet strength."},"nga:195115":{"d":"A channel of choppy water carries three-masted ships, a sailboat, at least one steamer and three rowboats, in a horizontal canvas about 52 by 73 centimetres. The waves are painted in pearly whites, golds, ice blues and the faintest pink, a steamer belches grey smoke along a distant coast at the left, and two of the three-masters edge closer at the right, one flying the French tricolour. Puffy grey clouds pile up on the horizon, and streaks of cloud around the farthest ship suggest that a storm has begun. The artist inscribed Le Havre.","i":"The picture shows the changing weather of the Channel, which Boudin observed all his life. The pearly shades in the waves and the tricolour on one ship give it a distinctly French, northern character. He studied clouds as other painters studied anatomy, making many quick sketches of skies, often annotated with the date, the hour and the wind. In 1883 the sailing ships that fill the picture were already being overtaken by steam, whose smoke appears at the left.","c":"A fine piece of weather painting. The choppy water, the changing light and the approaching storm are handled with quick, loaded strokes, and the pearly colour of the waves is subtle. The ships are summarised, almost incidental, so the picture is chiefly about air and water, which is exactly what Boudin knew best. It is a smaller, more intimate work than the big harbour canvas of the same year, and perhaps more lively."},"nga:61366":{"d":"A wide waterway with large sailing ships and smaller boats, in a big canvas about 119 by 160 centimetres. Two multi-masted ships stand left and right of centre, the one at the left flying a flag of red and white stripes with a dark blue section, a rowboat of indistinct figures beside it. A plume of grey smoke rises from low buildings at the left, and more rowboats at the right head toward tall buildings in the distance. The sky is soft light blue and grey with a pale yellow glow at the horizon, and the water echoes its colours and the grey hulls.","i":"Le Havre, the great port of Normandy at the mouth of the Seine, was the city of Boudin's youth and the starting point of his career, and he painted its harbour for decades. The flag with red and white stripes and a blue corner suggests an American ship, since transatlantic trade made Le Havre a main gateway to the United States. In 1883 the dealer Durand-Ruel gave Boudin a one-man exhibition in Paris, and a large canvas like this belongs to that confident period.","c":"One of Boudin's most ambitious harbour pictures: the large scale, the soft light and the quiet grey-blue harmony give the port a monumental calm. The soft brushwork suggests distance and humid air, and the small figures in rowboats give scale. The palette is restrained to the point of dullness in places and the composition lacks drama, but the atmosphere is persuasive, and the picture shows his ability to carry the freshness of his small panels onto a large canvas."},"nga:66402":{"d":"A busy harbour scene with ships and masts rising into a blue-grey sky, in a very small panel about 18 by 26 centimetres. The brushstrokes are loose and impressionistic, in soft greys, browns and blues, with pops of red and yellow from indistinct objects, and in the distance more tall masts and a thick smokestack obscure the horizon. The title names the boats as trawlers, fishing vessels that dragged nets along the seabed, and the sketch catches them crowded in the harbour.","i":"Trawlers were the working boats of the northern French ports, part of a commercial fishing industry that grew in the later nineteenth century as railways carried fresh fish to Paris. Boudin, the son of a sailor, loved the clutter of harbour life, masts and rigging, smoke and flags, and recorded it in small panels like this one. The thick smokestack at the back belongs to the steam age that was changing the harbours.","c":"A tiny, rapid sketch in which a handful of strokes convey the crowded masts, the grey air and the smoke of a working harbour. Its handling is very free, almost abstract in places, and the picture can hardly be read as more than an impression. That is also its virtue: it shows Boudin's ability, late in life, to catch the feel of a place in minutes, and it anticipates the freer harbour pictures of Impressionism."},"nga:46480":{"d":"A seaside scene on a small panel about 18 by 33 centimetres: at the centre three women and a child sit on the sand, the women in high-necked dresses and tall hats in red, yellow and pink, the child in a white dress and yellow hat, and at the left a woman with a parasol and a child in an orange dress. Their faces have no visible features. The pale grey sea and sky blend in a soft horizon, with small silhouettes of other people by the water in the distance, and thick, visible brushstrokes give the scene a blurred, shimmering look.","i":"By 1887 Boudin was in his sixties and had painted the beaches of Trouville for a quarter of a century, recording the changes in fashion that the high-necked dresses and tall hats show. The scene is anonymous, a record of seaside leisure that interests as much for its light and colour as for who the people are. By this date the resort was well established as a summer meeting place of Paris society.","c":"A late, delicate beach picture in which the figures are reduced to bright dabs of red, yellow and pink against a silvery sea and sky. The soft, pearly grey is lovely, and the loose handling is confident. The picture is small and slight, and the faceless figures are more patterns than persons, but within those limits it is a charming distillation of the subject he had painted for a lifetime."},"nga:61357":{"d":"A large sailing ship with several masts and big white sails dominates the centre of this horizontal canvas, about 90 by 131 centimetres, surrounded by smaller vessels with off-white sails. One small boat in the foreground, left of centre, flies a blue flag at its masthead, and a wave breaks over its side as two tiny figures ride it. The horizon lies in the lower third, so a vast sky of puffy white and grey clouds fills the picture above the blue-green sea, which is lined with white and grey wavelets. The brushwork is loose and fluid, especially in the water.","i":"The departure of ships from Le Havre was a daily sight in the busy Norman port, and Boudin painted the harbour mouth in all weathers, finding in its movement of sails and clouds a subject as varied as a landscape. By 1887 steamers were replacing sail on the main trade routes, so a harbour thronged with sailing ships already carried a note of nostalgia. The size of the canvas suggests a more ambitious picture than his usual small panels.","c":"A well-balanced, atmospheric seascape: the central ship's white sails against the clouds, the little wave-struck boat in the foreground and the broad expanse of sky together create a convincing sense of wind and space. The brushwork is free and the greys, blues and whites are subtly modulated. The composition is conventional, and the picture is calm where it might have been dramatic, but it exemplifies Boudin's art of the changing coastal sky."},"nga:231682":{"d":"A beach at eye level in a small vertical panel, about 27 by 25 centimetres, with half a dozen boats stranded on the damp sand against a grey sky. The nearest, slightly left of centre, is a sailboat lying on its dark brown hull with its tall mast apparently broken; behind it is a similar boat with its mast intact, and further back two more, indistinct. At the right a small mastless boat has two figures of a few strokes beside it, a puddle reflects the sky, and one boat under sail leaves the picture on the horizon.","i":"At low tide, boats of the Channel ports were left stranded on the sand until the water returned, a scene familiar to anyone who lived on the coast. Boudin was drawn to such unglamorous working subjects, and the title treats the grounding as a matter of routine rather than disaster. Painted between 1885 and 1890, the panel shows his later, freer handling, with broad, unblended strokes and a muted, rain-grey palette.","c":"A modest but fine little picture. The grounded boats, the damp beach and the grey light are put down in broad strokes with great economy, and the puddle reflecting the sky is a nice touch. The vertical format, less usual for Boudin, and the stranded hulls give the composition a solid, geometric structure. The palette is muted and the subject humble, but the picture catches the quiet melancholy of a low-tide shore."},"met:435750":{"d":"A bay on the French Riviera is the subject of this wide canvas, about 55 by 90 centimeters, dated 1892. The title names the Bay of Fourmis at Beaulieu, near Nice, and the picture is mostly sea and coast under a Mediterranean sky. Eugène Boudin painted it late in his life, when he spent winters on the southern coast, and it shows his lifelong interest in water and light in a new, brighter setting.","i":"In the 1890s Boudin, in his late sixties, traveled to the Mediterranean in winter, seeking warmth and a changed palette after decades on the gray Channel coast. The Riviera was then becoming a fashionable winter resort, and Beaulieu was a quiet place with villas along its shore. The painting records not the fashionable crowd but the bay itself, showing that the old painter of beaches was still adapting his eye.","c":"Boudin's late Mediterranean pictures are lighter and clearer in color than his Norman work, and this canvas shows a calm command of broad water and soft coastline. It lacks the incident of his beach crowds, and some find the late work slightly sweet. Yet the fine tonal control and the sense of a brief, bright moment make it a fitting coda to a long career."},"nga:46481":{"d":"A tiny panel, about 14 by 24 centimetres. People sit or stand on the sandy shore facing away from the viewer and looking at the sea, some beneath a striped red and white canopy and some on brown chairs. The horizon runs through the centre, with the pale green-blue sea meeting a slightly darker blue sky and a white sailboat in the distance. The figures, mostly women and children, are only streaks of paint suggesting grey, white, yellow and black dresses and hats; the sand has short horizontal streaks of red, brown, tan and yellow; and a line of black text runs along the bottom.","i":"In 1894, four years before his death, Boudin was still painting the Normandy beaches, now with a freedom close to that of the Impressionists he had helped to launch. The striped canopy is a sign of the more comfortable beach of the 1890s, with shelters as well as chairs. The line of black text at the bottom is an inscription, probably a place and date, a habit of his from his earliest studies.","c":"A late, tiny sketch which is almost an abstraction: the figures are streaks of colour, the sand is made of short horizontal strokes of red, brown and yellow, and sea and sky merge in cool blues and greens. The looseness would have shocked the public of 1860; by 1894 it is the natural language of the painter. The picture is slight, but it has the sparkle and truth of a lifetime's looking at the shore."},"nga:52161":{"d":"A coastal scene with a rocky tan cliff at the left running down to a blue sea, in a panel about 37 by 55 centimetres. The horizon is in the middle under a bright, slightly cloudy blue sky. On the beach a chaotic mass of dots and dashes of white, black, blue, red, yellow and pink makes up a large group of people at the water's edge, many apparently in white caps or bonnets, and smears of colour behind them suggest bundles of cloth. Small grey and white boats drift in the distance. The paint is loose and expressive.","i":"Étretat, in Normandy, is famous for its chalk cliffs and the natural arch called the Porte d'Aval, which Courbet and Monet painted, and the cliff at the left is presumably one of them. Boudin turned instead to the ordinary people on the beach: the women washing and drying linen at the shore, a daily task. The scene mixes the monumental coast and a humble human subject, and it dates from four years before his death.","c":"A lively, loose picture in which the washerwomen, mere dots of colour, give the beach a bustling life against the calm cliff and sea. The contrast between the solid cliff and the dissolving crowd is effective, and the handling is expressive. Details cannot be read, and the crowd is a confused scatter, but that vividness of impression is Boudin's aim. It is a late work of lively freedom."},"nga:67349":{"d":"A vertical panel about 33 by 24 centimetres looks over calm water at a large sailing ship with two tall masts and a dark hull, anchored with its sails tied up. At the left two people sit in a small dark rowboat, and distant shapes in grey, blue and red fill the background. The water is light grey and holds the dark reflections of both boats, and the sky is mostly light grey with patches of pale blue. The brushwork is loose and feathery, creating a sense of movement and depth.","i":"The Touques is the river that reaches the sea between Trouville and Deauville, and its basin held trading vessels as well as yachts. Boudin painted these quiet harbour corners, with a ship at anchor and a rowboat idling, in the years when he lived and worked near Deauville, where he died in 1898. The mood is one of waiting, with the sails furled and the water still.","c":"A quiet, tonal sketch in which the dark hull, its reflection and the pale grey sky and water make a simple, striking pattern. The vertical format, which gives the ship's masts extra height, suits the subject, and the soft, feathery brushwork conveys damp, still air. The picture is slight, and the distant shapes are barely articulated, but the harmony of greys is subtle."},"nga:52160":{"d":"A row of sailing ships with their sails lowered stretches across a panel about 46 by 37 centimetres, their tall brown masts hung with rows of colourful flags from the top down to the deck. The flags, in vivid red, yellow, black, white and blue, are reflected in the clear, still water, and the sky above is light blue and white. The brushwork is loose and visible, making the whole harbour shimmer.","i":"The ships are dressed overall in flags for a regatta or festival, a sight Boudin knew well from the yacht racing at Trouville and Deauville, and a subject he had painted nearly forty years earlier in his Festival in the Harbor of Honfleur, also in the National Gallery. In this late work the flags are only dashes of pure colour, which makes the picture almost abstract, and anticipates later painters' interest in colour for its own sake.","c":"A sparkling late picture: the flags, reduced to dabs of pure red, yellow and blue, dance against pale sky and water, and their reflections multiply the colour. The handling is free and the effect decorative and joyous. Beside the crisp early harbour of 1858 it shows how far his style had loosened, and some may find the forms too vague, but the picture bubbles with the gaiety of a regatta day."},"met:437988":{"d":"Princess Pauline Metternich (1836 to 1921) is shown on a beach in this small oil on cardboard laid on wood, about 29.5 by 23.5 centimeters, painted around 1865 to 1867. The title names her as the figure, set against sand and sea. Eugène Boudin, who spent summers on the Normandy coast, painted the resort crowd in small, quick strokes, and here he gives one of its celebrities a place in his scene.","i":"Pauline Metternich, wife of the Austrian ambassador to Napoleon III, was one of the great fashion leaders of the Second Empire, known for her wit, her patronage of couturiers such as Worth and her support for Wagner's music in Paris. A visit to the Normandy beaches was part of the society calendar, and Boudin's picture records the fashionable summer life in which she took part.","c":"A small sketch rather than a formal portrait, it shows Boudin's usual technique of notation: a figure placed in a bright open setting, with color and light more important than likeness. It lacks the detail of a commissioned portrait, and that is its charm. For the viewer today it is a fresh glimpse of a famous person caught in the open air."},"met:437945":{"d":"A woman is the unnamed sitter of this large oil portrait, about 126 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1687 by Willem Wissing, the Dutch-born portraitist who became one of the leading painters of the English court in the 1680s. The size, a half-length format of roughly fifty by forty inches, was the standard for a lady of rank. In the tradition inherited from Sir Peter Lely, the sitter is usually shown in a loosely draped silk gown with a soft, idealized face and a hint of landscape or architecture behind.","i":"Wissing came to London as a young man, worked in the studio of Sir Peter Lely and, after Lely died in 1680, became one of the portraitists in demand among court ladies; he later painted William of Orange and Princess Mary at The Hague. He died in 1687 at thirty-one, so a portrait dated about 1687 is a late work. The sitter's name has been lost, but she belonged to a society that valued polished, graceful likenesses.","c":"Wissing's reputation was for smooth, attractive likenesses, the late-Lely manner refined into something a little more elegant, and in the short span of his career he did it with real skill. His flaw, shared with the court portraitists of his day, is a certain sameness of face and a pleasant vacancy. A portrait of about 1687 is a late example, and the early death of the painter makes it more poignant than its polish suggests."},"met:436453":{"d":"On a raised marble platform at the right, a young king in a plumed black hat and a lavish costume of purple, black, and white satin leans forward from his throne, half rising, his eyes fixed on the woman at the left. She is Queen Esther, in a golden-yellow gown with a pale blue sash, swooning backward into the arms of her attendants, her head thrown back, eyes nearly closed, one arm limp. Shadow fills the room, and behind the king hangs a dark red drape; the floor at the lower left is checkered marble. The figures are nearly life-size, and the dress is rendered in rich detail: embroidery on the hem, fur-trimmed boots, and the clawed feet of the king's chair. The canvas is about 2.1 by 2.7 meters.","i":"The scene is from the Book of Esther: the Jewish queen of Persia goes uninvited to the king, risking death, to plead for her people, and faints before him. Artemisia Gentileschi, the daughter and pupil of the Caravaggesque painter Orazio Gentileschi, was one of the few women of her day to make a full professional career, and she often chose strong female subjects. The Met dates the picture to the 1620s, though some scholars place it in the 1630s, and the patron is unknown. The costumes are contemporary, and the king's dandyish look makes him seem less commanding than the queen. The Met received it as a gift in 1969.","c":"A grand and theatrical canvas whose strength is Esther: her collapse is rendered with a force and fleshy realism that avoid prettiness, and the golden dress holds the eye. The king is weaker, a dandy in a hat, but this may be deliberate, since the picture is on her side. The twin diagonals, the heavy shadow, and the polished costume make a vivid, if somewhat stagey, piece of Baroque drama. It is less psychologically tense than her Judith pictures, but it shows how she gave women the center of the stage."},"met:436219":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in this small French miniature on ivory by Frédéric Dubois (1780 to 1819). The picture is undated and the sitter unnamed, so the likeness is all that remains. Ivory was cut in thin sheets from a tusk and polished smooth, and its pale, slightly translucent surface let the painter build skin tones in tiny dots and hatched strokes of watercolor, with the white of the ivory itself serving as the highlight.","i":"Miniatures of this period were made for intimate exchange: a woman's portrait might be a gift to a husband or fiancé, a keepsake for parents, or a memorial. The years of the Revolution, the Empire and the Restoration saw a flourishing trade in them, since a miniature could be made quickly and cheaply and traveled easily. The portrait's anonymity is common for miniatures separated from their cases and inscriptions.","c":"Miniatures of this moment, in the wake of Jean-Baptiste Isabey, favored crisp contours and light, delicate color, and a portrait of this type is judged by the eyes, the lips and the modeling of a cheek the size of a fingernail. Within that narrow range careful finish is the main virtue, and individuality is limited. It is a neat, graceful example of a vanished art."},"met:437023":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child, attended by angels, in a tall panel of about 130 by 83 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood. The ground behind the figures is silver leaf and not the more usual gold, an unusual choice in an Italian altarpiece. The Master of Varlungo, a Florentine painter probably active in the later thirteenth century, still works in the 'Greek manner' derived from Byzantine icons, with frontal figures and linear drapery. The panel was given to the Metropolitan Museum by Robert Lehman in 1949.","i":"The enthroned Virgin with the Child, flanked by angels, was the central image of the Church in this period: Mary as Queen of Heaven and the Child as ruler of all. Panels like this stood on altars of churches and convents, where candlelight would have set a silver or gold ground shimmering. The Master of Varlungo, an anonymous painter named for a district near Florence, belongs to the generation of Florentine painters working in the years before Giotto.","c":"Pictures of this kind ask to be judged by their design and the handling of their precious ground, not by realism, and the Master of Varlungo is a representative rather than a leading figure: he works within the Byzantine-derived manner with competence, if without the monumentality of Cimabue. The silver, now probably tarnished, once gave the whole panel a cooler, more austere glow than gold. A solid, historically important picture of its moment, before Giotto changed the rules."},"nga:52956":{"d":"A polar bear stands over a struggling seal on an iceberg, one paw raised and the other clawing the seal's side. The bear's cream-colored fur has patches of dark yellow and its claws are sharp; the seal's mouth is open as it looks back at the bear. Jagged ice and a green-blue ocean run to the horizon, where a ship sails beside another tall iceberg under a flat gray sky. The canvas, dated 1881, measures about 90 by 102 centimeters.","i":"The title states the Victorian idea of nature red in tooth and claw: survival as the law of the animal world. A distant ship adds a human witness and recalls the whalers and sealers who sailed to the Arctic, while Arctic exploration was much in the news around 1881. Charles S. Raleigh, an American painter known for marine subjects, offered a drama that viewers could enjoy from safety, a polar encounter without the cold.","c":"The picture is a dramatic set piece: predator, prey, ice and a flat gray sky arranged to make the moment clear at a glance. Raleigh paints the bear's fur and claws with attention, and the seal's open mouth and backward look give the scene a human touch that keeps it from being merely brutal. The composition is a little theatrical and the ice rather decorative, but as a nature drama it is vivid and direct."},"cma:169684":{"d":"A signed portrait of the sculptor Jean-Baptiste Lemoyne the younger (1704-1778) shows him in working clothes, with close-cropped gray hair, a frank, observant stare and a bemused smile. The oil on canvas, about 37 by 44 centimeters, is dated 1774. It reinterprets a well-known pastel by Maurice-Quentin de La Tour of the same sculptor, seen toward the end of his life.","i":"Lemoyne was one of France's leading sculptors, and the portrait shows him as a craftsman rather than a courtier. The working garb conveys the dignity of the artist's labor, and the warmth of the expression points to the closeness between the sculptor and the young painter. By reinterpreting a pastel by La Tour, Vigée Le Brun paid homage to a master of the medium in which she had begun.","c":"Charming and unexpected from a painter later famed for flattering society portraits. The good humor in the eyes and mouth, the informality of dress and the simplicity of the format show real observation. Because it is based on La Tour's pastel, it is a translation rather than an invention, but for a painter in her late teens to take on La Tour is remarkable."},"nga:172073":{"d":"A portrait of Madame du Barry, the last official mistress of Louis XV, painted in 1782 on a vertical canvas of about 115 by 90 centimeters. Eight years after the king's death she lived in retirement at Louveciennes, outside Paris, where Vigée Le Brun painted her. In the painter's usual manner, the likeness is soft and luminous and the surfaces are handled with an easy lightness; the sitter is presented not as a notorious favorite but as an elegant private lady.","i":"Marie-Jeanne Bécu, comtesse du Barry (1743 to 1793), rose from humble birth to become Louis XV's last favorite, and was sent from court when he died in 1774. Vigée Le Brun, who had made her name with portraits of Marie Antoinette, painted her more than once, and her memoirs recall the sitter's kindness. Du Barry was guillotined in December 1793, which gives the portrait a shadow that neither painter nor sitter could have foreseen.","c":"The strength of Vigée Le Brun's portraits of women is their friendliness: she makes her sitters look both beautiful and approachable, with a delicacy of color that rivals rarely matched. The danger is flattery, and a portrait of a woman with a past like du Barry's is more charming than critical. It is a graceful, accomplished likeness, and it shows why aristocratic women queued for her services in these years."},"met:437898":{"d":"Set within an oval, a young woman with powdered, softly curled blonde hair turns her face upward and to the left with a bright, half-teasing look. A large blue silk bow sits in her hair, and loose curls fall over her shoulders. She wears a pearl-grey satin gown striped in white, edged with a fringe of lace at the neckline and tied with a blue ribbon bow at the breast. One arm rests along the back of a green velvet sofa framed in gilt wood, and the other hand holds a sheet of music. The background is dark. The silk is painted in long, bright strokes, and the face is smooth and rosy.","i":"The sitter was Noël Catherine Vorlée (1761–1835), known as Madame Grand, who was born in India, married young, and lived in Paris after a scandal ended her first marriage. Vigée Le Brun painted her in 1783, the year the artist was received into the Académie Royale, as a woman of fashion and culture; the sheet of music suggests that she sang or played. In 1802 Catherine married Talleyrand, then Napoleon's foreign minister. The oval format, soft ribbons and loose hair suit the informal, English-influenced style that Vigée Le Brun helped popularise in Paris in the 1780s.","c":"A polished piece of flattery that is also acute. Vigée Le Brun understood her sitter's charm: the upturned glance, the loose curls and the glossy satin make an image of easy, slightly knowing elegance, and the dress stripes are done with a few confident strokes. But the performance is skin-deep. The face is pretty and almost empty, the pose is a stock one, and one learns little more about Catherine Grand's mind than about her wardrobe. As a record of 1780s taste and of the portraitist's gift for flattering clients, it is expert."},"met:437899":{"d":"A French nobleman, identified by the long title as Alexandre Charles Emmanuel de Crussol-Florensac (1743-1815), is the sole subject of this portrait, painted in 1787 in oil on a wood panel of about 90 by 65 centimeters. The vertical format and single figure place it among the formal and semi-formal portraits that Vigée Le Brun supplied to the court and the Parisian aristocracy before the Revolution, when she was among the most sought-after portrait painters in Paris.","i":"The sitter's long family name marks high birth, and by the 1780s a portrait by Vigée Le Brun signaled that the sitter moved in fashionable and court circles. She had painted Marie Antoinette from 1778 and was admitted to the Academy in 1783. The sitter was in his mid-forties in 1787, and he would live through the Revolution, which forced the painter herself into exile in 1789.","c":"Vigée Le Brun's strengths, smooth luminous flesh, easy poses and a flattering lightness, make her aristocratic portraits attractive and sometimes bland. Her men are usually judged less memorable than her women and children, and this portrait is best seen as a polished society likeness rather than a psychological study. It does show a painter at the height of her fame, fluent and sure."},"met:438132":{"d":"The artist's daughter Julie, about seven years old, looks into a mirror in this portrait of 1787, which measures about 73 by 59 centimeters. The title and details describe a girl, a mirror and a portrait: a private, informal subject rather than a commissioned likeness. The canvas was painted by Julie's mother, Elisabeth Louise Vigée Le Brun, at the height of her success in Paris.","i":"Vigée Le Brun painted her daughter repeatedly, and the bond between mother and child is a recurring theme in her work, as in the double portrait of the two in the Louvre. A mirror in a child's portrait can allude to vanity, but in 1787 it more likely suggests natural curiosity, in keeping with the new ideal of unforced childhood. It may also touch on the painter's own craft of looking and reflecting.","c":"Informal and intimate, the picture avoids the stiffness of formal portraiture. The girl absorbed in her own reflection lets Vigée Le Brun do what she did best: soft light, supple flesh and unforced charm. The sentiment may feel sweet to modern taste, but the private origin of the picture keeps it fresh."},"nga:50215":{"d":"A group portrait of two noblewomen and two small boys, painted in 1787 on a wide canvas of about 123 by 156 centimeters. The title names the Marquise de Pezay and the Marquise de Rougé, with Rougé's sons Alexis and Adrien, so the picture records a bond between the two women as well as a family, grouped in the soft, natural manner that Vigée Le Brun brought to such portraits. The scale and the number of figures make it one of her more ambitious private commissions of the 1780s.","i":"Vigée Le Brun's group portraits of the 1780s took part in the new cult of maternal feeling: her own self-portrait with her daughter Julie of 1786 and Marie Antoinette with her children of 1787 made tenderness a public virtue. Pictures such as this show aristocratic women as loving mothers and not as pieces of display, in simple dress and natural poses, which was how fashionable Paris, under the influence of Rousseau, wanted to see them.","c":"Vigée Le Brun had a gift that was rare among court portraitists: she made women look at ease. The group's success depends on natural grouping and on soft light and modeling more than on bravura. The hazard is sweetness, and a modern viewer may find it a little sugary, but the picture is a fine example of how the Old Regime wished to see its elite in the years just before the Revolution."},"met:437900":{"d":"A young woman in a wide-brimmed straw hat trimmed with a large black bow sits angled on a green velvet sofa with gilt-wood arms, looking out with a calm, faintly amused expression. Brown curls frame her face beneath the hat. She wears a soft white gown of fine dotted muslin with a white fichu crossed over her chest and a long dark sash tied in a bow at the waist, and she holds a small red-bound book against the arm of the sofa. The grey background is plain. The white muslin is painted in thin, creamy strokes that catch the light, and the dark hat and sash balance the pale dress.","i":"Marie Charlotte Louise Perrette Aglaé Bontemps (1762–1848) was the wife of the comte de La Châtre and belonged to the circle of aristocratic women whom Vigée Le Brun painted. The portrait is dated 1789, the year the Revolution began; the painter left France that autumn and did not return for over a decade. The plain muslin dress, straw hat and book reflect the fashion for informal, natural dress that Marie Antoinette's circle had popularised, so the picture shows Paris society on the eve of upheaval, though nothing in it hints at what was coming.","c":"Vigée Le Brun is at her most natural here. The pose is unforced, the hat and sash are crisply handled, and the sitter's fresh colour and faintly amused look give a rare sense of a person rather than a type. The white dress is done in thin, fluent strokes that suggest the lightness of muslin without fuss. The weakness is the familiar one: the setting is bland and the picture goes no deeper than good manners. Next to her elaborate state portraits of Marie Antoinette it is simpler and likelier to please a modern eye."},"nga:32694":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a young woman sits in a gilded armchair among emerald-green cushions and gazes out of a vertical panel about 107 by 83 centimeters. She wears a short wine-red velvet jacket over an ivory-white gown patterned with coin-sized gold dots and trimmed with gold ribbon at the scooped neckline, with a cinnamon-brown belt at the waist. Her ash-brown hair is loosely pulled up under a white turban with one braid woven through it, and laurel-green drop earrings gleam. Soft light from the upper left glints on gold ribbons and buttons.","i":"The sitter belonged to the d'Aguesseau family, leading members of the legal nobility. Her loosely pinned hair and turban reflect the taste of the late 1780s for a natural, undressed look that Vigée Le Brun helped to popularize. She signed the picture 'Mde Le Brun 1789', using the married name by which she was then known. In October of that year she left France, so the portrait belongs to her last year before the long exile.","c":"The painting succeeds through arrangement and light: the greens of cushions, earrings and wall hold the picture together, and the wine-red jacket gives it a firm center. The face has Vigée Le Brun's usual freshness, rosy cheeks and a calm direct gaze, while gold touches of ribbon and button catch the light with great economy. Perhaps a little decorative, it is an excellent example of her late Old Regime manner, direct and unaffectedly elegant."},"nga:134166":{"d":"A tall silver vase with gold detailing stands at the center of a tabletop, holding a bouquet of white flowers, green leaves and red blossoms. Behind it to the left rests a blue box; to the right, a little forward, a jewelry box decorated with a pearl-and-flower ring design. A piece of tasseled fabric and a pair of small scissors lie flat behind the vase, and the title adds a fan and a pendant to the ensemble. Silver, gold, white, green, red and teal make up the palette, on a vertical board about 46 by 31 centimeters.","i":"A vase of flowers beside jewelry, a fan and sewing implements brings together the tokens of a lady's private world. Still lifes of this sort belong to the Victorian taste for luxurious domestic display, flattering the buyer's sense of refinement. Flowers, like a fan and a pendant, are also passing pleasures, and many such pictures carry a faint reminder that beauty is brief, though Victorian buyers mostly enjoyed them as decoration.","c":"Brookes, a British-born painter who worked in California, is better known for fish and game pieces, so this flower-and-trinket piece shows the more genteel side of his output. The challenge is metal: silver reflecting its surroundings is among the hardest things to paint, and the picture's success depends on how convincingly the vase gleams. The composition is balanced and formal, with a crowded, decorative effect that suits its purpose better than it provokes thought."},"cma:143234":{"d":"Dark forms are massed in the foreground, and beyond them an expansive, sunlit plain opens at left, where white linens lie spread across a field opposite a windmill. The picture is a small oil on wood, about 50 by 69 centimeters, dated 1646, painted when Jacob van Ruisdael was still a teenager. The stark contrast between the heavy dark masses and the bright, open plain shows his unique approach: making the simplest motifs seem grand and heroic.","i":"Haarlem was the center of Dutch linen bleaching, and long cloths laid out on the fields around the city were a familiar sight; the process took months of sun and rain. Ruisdael, who lived in Haarlem, uses a local sight to anchor a picture that is already thinking about drama. He painted it at about seventeen, early in a career that would make him one of the greatest of Dutch landscape painters.","c":"A teenager's landscape is judged by promise, and this one shows plenty: the habit of massing dark forms against a bright plain, the gravity given to plain motifs, the eye for sky. It is heavier and less subtle than his mature work, but the contrast of dark and light has a drama that few painters of that age manage, and the sunny field has the unforced truth of a place actually seen."},"met:437545":{"d":"A watercourse and a raised embankment run through flat Dutch country, with a man, a tree, and a church and village far off, in a large oil on wood about 76 by 109 centimeters, dated 1646. The size and horizontal format are ambitious for a painter of seventeen, and the subject recalls the Haarlem manner of the day, learned from painters such as Jan van Goyen and Salomon van Ruysdael. The picture came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1965 with the bequest of Adele L. Lehman, in memory of Arthur Lehman.","i":"A painter's first landscapes drew on the flat country around Haarlem: dunes, canals, dikes and village churches that Dutch viewers loved as portraits of their own land. A levee or embankment is a sign of the Dutch struggle with water, and a distant church spire ties the view to a particular parish. Ruisdael's uncle Salomon van Ruysdael, himself a landscape painter, probably helped teach him, and the dated panels of 1646 show him mastering the local manner.","c":"A big early panel shows more ambition than finish. The composition has the clear, plain order of Haarlem landscape of the 1640s, and the sky and foreground are handled with a young painter's seriousness. What is missing is the weight of feeling that his mature work would find in the same flat country. It is a promising picture, better taken as a beginning than as a statement."},"nga:210038":{"d":"Water laps a curving shoreline beneath a steep sandy dune carpeted in grass, shrubs and trees, which fills the lower right of this horizontal panel, about 45 by 62 centimeters. Broken, vine-covered tree trunks lie across the top and down the slope; a red brick wall climbs the dune and disappears into the shrubbery. Three large trees twist into the upper right. The land curves like a backward C toward the left horizon, where animals graze and sailboats line the distance above steel-blue water with white ripples.","i":"The dunes between Haarlem and the sea were a favorite subject for Haarlem painters, who treated the sandy, scrubby coast as a distinctive part of the Dutch land. Ruisdael signed and dated this picture 'J Ruisdael . 1648', when he was about nineteen and about to join the Haarlem painters' guild. The broken, vine-wrapped trunks and the brick wall are the kind of details that give a random corner of nature a hint of history and decay, a theme he developed throughout his career.","c":"At nineteen Ruisdael already knows how to organize a picture around a single strong mass: the dune, with its dark tangle of trees pressing into the upper right, set against the open water and distance on the left. The detail of vegetation is attentive and the light is clear. The picture is more compact and less turbulent than his later forests, but the sense of a particular, slightly desolate place is already unmistakable."},"nga:1219":{"d":"A broken white and gray birch trunk lies across a shallow, rocky waterfall at the edge of a dense, deeply shadowed forest. The narrow stream winds toward us into a rocky pool that spans the bottom of the canvas, and the trunk angles down into it from the right, its gnarled roots clinging to a rocky outcrop. Far back and tiny, two people walk along the stream, and under the trees four sheep graze. Gray-bottomed clouds tumble across the tree tops above a wedge of pale blue sky. The canvas measures about 106 by 123 centimeters.","i":"Forests of this type, with fallen trunks, waterfalls and deep shadow, were a Ruisdael specialty, built from drawings and from the pictures of others; there are no such waterfalls in the Dutch lowlands, so the scenery is composed and not copied. A dead tree is often read as a reminder of mortality in his work, and the tiny walkers give the human scale. It is not known whether Ruisdael or his patrons saw such details as moral lessons, but the solemnity is clear.","c":"A fallen birch across a waterfall is a simple motif, and Ruisdael makes it carry the whole picture: the pale trunk against the dark forest gives the eye its anchor, the pool brings it forward and the clouds close the composition from above. The painting of leaves and bark is attentive without fussiness, and the tiny figures give scale. It is a dark picture, but the dark is full of tone and variety, and the effect is solemn without gloom."},"met:437546":{"d":"A stream tumbles over rocks in a wooded landscape among tall trees, in an oil on canvas of about 100 by 129 centimeters, painted about 1660. The keywords list a waterfall, trees, a forest and a stream, and the composition follows the type Ruisdael perfected in these years: a dark, close forest, a bright opening where the water catches the light, and a large sky above. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1889 by Henry G. Marquand, a major early benefactor of the museum.","i":"Ruisdael painted waterfalls and forest streams in numbers in the 1650s and 1660s, and Dutch buyers, who lived in a flat country, bought them eagerly. The inventions came from the work of Allart van Everdingen, who had traveled to Scandinavia and brought back sketches of rushing torrents and pine woods, and from Ruisdael's own trips to the hilly border country near Bentheim. A forest stream was a place of wildness and solitude, and dead trunks and tumbling water could also suggest the passing of time.","c":"By 1660 Ruisdael has the formula under complete control, and this is a strong, characteristic example: the heavy trees frame the scene, the white water gives it a pulse and the clouds supply the weather. It is less personal than his very best, and a viewer who knows his dunes and Haarlem views may find the forest pictures a little formulaic. Still, the force of the dark forest is hard to resist."},"cma:139816":{"d":"A mother and child trudge along a weathered path in the foreground, their pet dog following at their side, while a lone traveler approaches from the foliage ahead, in an oil on canvas of about 52 by 59 centimeters from the 1660s. Nature takes up most of the composition, and the tiny figures serve to measure its scale. The hilly, wooded terrain is invented and not a view of Holland's flat land, and the small human drama sits within a larger, quieter one.","i":"Ruisdael's later landscapes combine meticulous detail with a sense of the transitory relationship between humans and nature: people pass through a landscape that outlasts them. Such small figures give the viewer a way into the scene, and they have often been read as emblems of life's journey, though no document records how Ruisdael or his patrons understood them. The mother, child and dog suggest a family on the road, an everyday human frame for a grand setting.","c":"Ruisdael's sublime comes from proportion: small human figures, a huge sky and heavy trees. This is a modest canvas, but the delicacy of detail in foliage and path is a pleasure, and the three tiny travelers keep the picture from being only scenery; the dog is a nice touch. It lacks the thunder of his large pictures, which suits its size and gives it a quieter charm. A quiet, reliable late work."},"met:437547":{"d":"Fields of grain, trees and a windmill lie under a large Dutch sky, in a small oil on canvas about 47 by 57 centimeters from the mid- or late 1660s. The subject is plain farming country, and Ruisdael gives it gravity by the scale of the sky and the strong play of light and shadow across the land. The picture came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1931 with the bequest of Michael Friedsam.","i":"Fields of grain were the farmland's wealth, and Ruisdael's pictures of them, painted for city buyers, show the ordered countryside that fed Dutch cities. A windmill, an everyday machine for grinding, draining or sawing, was a national symbol of ingenuity. Yet his skies and brooding trees add a note of mood to what could have been a simple farm view, which is why such small landscapes struck later painters, among them John Constable, so deeply.","c":"In a small canvas like this one, Ruisdael's art is at its most economical: a few simple elements, a great deal of sky and a play of light across the land. It lacks the drama of his forests, but its restraint is a virtue, and the quiet is convincing. A viewer who looks long at the clouds and the shifting light on the grain will find the depth of the painter's observation."},"cma:143602":{"d":"The massive silvery trunk of a dead tree juts from a dune in the foreground, leading the eye across a low waterfall toward a distant sunlit field where travelers and a dog follow a sandy path. Partly masked by trees, a ruined building turns gold in the sun. Above, cloud-filled skies loom, with dramatic patterns of light and shadow. The oil on canvas measures about 99 by 131 centimeters and dates from about 1660 to 1670.","i":"In Ruisdael's landscapes, dead trees, waterfalls and ruined buildings were visual expressions of the passage of time, and he gave equal attention to the sky, whose cloud patterns reveal the unseen movements of the wind. He was inspired by trees in various states of decay, observed from nature. The dead beech, the ruin and the tiny travelers add up to a reflection on endurance and change, which seventeenth-century viewers, steeped in ideas of vanity and mortality, may have read readily.","c":"This is Ruisdael in his full mature voice: the dead tree is the dominant actor, a silver diagonal that pulls the picture together, and the golden ruin and far field supply a counter-note of light. The sky is as eventful as the land. The picture carries a symbolic load without becoming a sermon. If there is a weakness, it is a degree of staginess in the arrangement of foreground, middle ground and distance."},"nga:46184":{"d":"A landscape by Jacob van Ruisdael of about 1670, in oil on canvas, measuring about 53 by 60 centimeters. The title gives no subject, and the picture belongs to the late phase of his career, when he painted woods, waterfalls, dunes and wide skies for the Amsterdam market. At this scale it is a cabinet picture, intended for a room in a town house, where it would be seen up close and where the quality of its detail and the weight of its sky would be judged by a collector's eye.","i":"By 1670 Ruisdael had lived in Amsterdam for more than a decade, and his work was valued by merchants and officials who wanted views of a nature that was ordered but not tame. Even without a specific subject, such a landscape would have stood as an image of the solitude and permanence that city buyers found in the country. Ruisdael's pupils included Meindert Hobbema, and his later style influenced generations of landscape painters.","c":"Ruisdael's later landscapes are tested by the same questions: does the sky have weight, does the foliage vary, does the light make a place? His work of about 1670 usually passes, though by this decade his formulas, forest, torrent and dune, were so well established that the pictures sometimes read as variations on set themes. The pleasure lies in the specifics of weather and trees."},"cma:137331":{"d":"A small church or chapel stands beyond rapids that cut it off from the viewer, in a tall, narrow oil on canvas of about 71 by 55 centimeters, painted about 1670. Severed logs litter the middle ground. The vertical format stresses the height of the setting and the fall of the water. Dangerous rapids block access to the chapel, and the picture turns that obstacle into its subject, with the building as a distant goal and the torrent as the barrier between.","i":"It is not known whether Ruisdael meant his landscapes as moral commentary, since no document from his lifetime says how he or his patrons read them. But a church cut off by dangerous rapids, with logs strewn through the middle ground, is just the kind of motif that hints at life's transience and the difficulty of reaching salvation. Later viewers have been drawn to that reading, and it adds a layer to the stormy scene without being certain.","c":"The drama is concentrated and well managed: the torrent bars the way, the church waits beyond it and the vertical shape forces the eye to climb. The risk with symbolic motifs is overreading, and a viewer need not take the church as a sermon to feel the force of the picture. Ruisdael's skill is in making rapids, rocks and logs feel plausible, solid and wet, which is what gives the allegory its weight."},"met:437549":{"d":"A sandy road, rutted and scattered with low scrub, runs from the foreground between two fields of ripe wheat toward a clump of trees and a farmhouse on the horizon. Two small figures walk along it in the middle distance, and a third stands tiny on a distant rise. In the left foreground a fallen log lies among bushes, with a bare, twisted twig, and at the right a lone windswept tree stands over a bank. Towering grey-and-cream clouds fill more than half of the canvas, parting at the left to show blue sky; the sun lights the pale wheat and the edges of the clouds. At the far left horizon, tiny ships can be made out on the sea. Oil on canvas, about 100 by 130 centimetres.","i":"Ruisdael, among the greatest of Dutch landscape painters, made some two dozen or more pictures of grain fields, and this is one of the finest. Painted about 1670, it shows no identified place; the wheat, the dunes and the glimpse of the sea are the ordinary ingredients of the Dutch countryside, arranged with great care. Ripe grain points to harvest and, for seventeenth-century viewers, to the passing of time, and walkers on a road were often read as a reminder that life is a journey, though the picture does not insist on it. Its main subject is the sky, which fills most of the canvas.","c":"The sky does the work. Those great clouds, shot with light and shadow, give the flat land a grandeur out of proportion to its few subjects, and the diagonal of the road leads the eye in with calm authority. The details are exact without being fussy. If there is a limitation, it is a degree of formula: the dark foreground, pale middle ground and cloud-shifted light recur in many Ruisdaels, and the figures are minor accents. But the formula is applied with such mastery that later landscape painters, Constable among them, learned from it."},"nga:46005":{"d":"A country house stands in a park in this oil on canvas, about 76 by 98 centimeters, painted about 1675 and attributed to Jacob van Ruisdael. Unlike his wild forests, the subject is a cultivated landscape, an estate with a house in its grounds, a type that records property as well as scenery. The size is that of a picture for a principal room, and the subject suggests a client who valued order and a handsome prospect.","i":"Parks and country houses in the Dutch Republic were symbols of success: wealthy Amsterdam merchants built them along the river Vecht and near Haarlem, laying out formal gardens and avenues. A painting of such a house served a pride of ownership, and the park showed taste and order, in contrast to the wild forests of Ruisdael's other pictures. Whether this view shows a specific house is not stated, and it could be an idealized composite.","c":"Painting a cultivated park asks something different of Ruisdael than a wild forest: order in place of wildness. The tamer subject tempers his drama, and a viewer used to the brooding forests may find it less compelling, though the sky and the tree masses still have his characteristic weight. As a document of Dutch estate culture it is unusual and engaging, and a fine example of the painter's versatility."},"met:437548":{"d":"A torrent falls through a mountain valley, crossed by a footbridge, with a cottage, a shepherd and sheep among the rocks, in a vertical oil on canvas about 54 by 42 centimeters from the 1670s. Ruisdael, who never saw mountains of this kind, builds the scene from drawings and the work of others, with the cascade as the central event and the small human elements giving it scale. It was bequeathed to the Metropolitan Museum in 1900 by the railroad magnate Collis P. Huntington.","i":"Mountain landscapes with torrents were a Dutch fantasy: the Republic had no mountains, but Allart van Everdingen's sketches of Scandinavia made the genre popular, and buyers liked the contrast with their own flat land. The shepherd, sheep and cottage give the wild setting a human scale, and the footbridge across the torrent suggests the small works of men against nature's power. Pictures like this had a long afterlife in Romantic painting.","c":"A small vertical canvas makes the torrent loom larger than its dimensions, and Ruisdael uses that to convey the force of water over rock, with the plausible solidity that is his hallmark. The composition is artificial, assembled from borrowed elements, and the scenery is more convincing as paint than as geography. It is a good, compact example of a type that this painter made his own."},"met:436421":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of Maria Luisa of Spain (1745 to 1792), who became Holy Roman Empress as the wife of Leopold II, painted about 1790 by Heinrich Friedrich Füger, the leading Viennese miniaturist of his day. Miniatures of rulers were made for exchange among royal families and as presentation gifts, and the form allowed an exact, flattering likeness within a few centimeters. The Met's long title lists her many roles: empress, German queen, queen of Hungary and Bohemia, and grand duchess of Tuscany. The Met bought the portrait in 1941 through the Fletcher Fund.","i":"Maria Luisa was the daughter of Charles III of Spain and the mother of sixteen children, among them the future Emperor Francis II. She became empress in 1790, when her husband succeeded his brother Joseph II, and died in 1792, within months of his own death, so a portrait of about 1790 comes from the last years of her life. As Grand Duchess of Tuscany she had lived in Florence for twenty-five years before the move to Vienna.","c":"Füger's miniatures are admired for finish and elegance: he treats ivory with a refinement that suits a court, and a picture of an empress needs both dignity and softness. The risk is a certain generic politeness, and a viewer should not expect much psychology from a court miniature. Still, the delicacy of technique and the historical weight of the sitter make it a distinguished object of its kind."},"met:438617":{"d":"Three boys and a dog share a tall canvas of about 147 by 114 centimeters, dated 1803. The title names the sitters as the sons of Martin Anton Heckscher: Leopold, born in 1792 and so about eleven; Carl Martin Adolph, about seven; and Johann Gustav Wilhelm Moritz, about six. A group portrait of children with a pet was a mark of a prosperous household's pride in its heirs, and the size suggests a commission for a principal room.","i":"Tischbein is best known for his portrait of Goethe in the Roman Campagna (1787), and in 1803 he was back in northern Germany after years in Naples, where he had directed the academy, making portraits for prosperous families. The Heckscher boys belong to the world of the merchant class, whose children appear here with the new taste for childhood shown as childhood, a dog as companion. The picture came to the Met in 2002 from the family of August Heckscher II.","c":"Tischbein was a painter of fluent skill and wide sympathies, trained in the neoclassical circles of Rome and Naples, and a group portrait of children asks for just those: a clear arrangement, a gentle charm and individuality in each face. The three ages give the composition a natural rhythm, and the dog supplies relief. The risk is sweetness, and the likeness may be prettier than penetrating. An engaging document of a merchant family in 1803."},"nga:41623":{"d":"Against a shiny gold ground, a woman in a royal-blue mantle that covers her head and body stands with her head tipped sharply to rest in one hand, in a tall panel about 82 by 33 centimeters. Her pale skin has a greenish tint, a bright pink disk flushes her cheek, and strokes of butter yellow define chin, nose, brow and neck. A dark violet cloth edged with gold and hung with clover-shaped tassels lies over her shoulders, and the mantle falls over pointed red shoes. The halo is punched and incised into the gold.","i":"The Virgin Mary mourns at the foot of the cross in the posture of grief used since Byzantine art, the hand supporting the heavy head. This panel is the companion of The Mourning Saint John, and the pair probably flanked a crucified Christ on a large painted cross. The violet cloth and the blue mantle show Mary as queen as well as mother, and the red shoes, in Byzantine tradition an imperial privilege, add to her royal status in heaven.","c":"The panel pairs gravity with delicacy: the tilted head and tucked hand make grief legible from across a church, and the fine borders and tassels reward close looking. The facial modeling, with its green shadow and pink spot on the cheek, is a convention of the day and not a portrait, but it conveys feeling better than strict realism might. The cracks of age show, but the gold still gives the figure its solemn radiance."},"nga:41624":{"d":"Against a shiny gold ground, a clean-shaven young man with carrot-orange hair stands with his head tipped sharply to rest in one hand, in a tall, narrow panel of about 82 by 33 centimeters. His pale skin has a greenish tint, a pink spot flushes his cheek, and strokes of butter yellow pick out chin, nose and brow. His lapis-blue robe falls in stylized folds to his bare feet, under a slate-blue mantle, and white highlights swirl through the angular drapery. A punched, incised halo marks him as a saint.","i":"Saint John the Evangelist, the disciple who stood by the cross, is shown in the classic gesture of grief, the head resting on the hand. The panel is the companion of The Mourning Madonna, and the two were probably parts of a large painted cross, with the mourners standing on either side of the crucified Christ, a type of altarpiece common in thirteenth-century Italy and often made for Franciscan churches. The Master of the Franciscan Crucifixes is named for crosses of this kind.","c":"The strength of this panel is the tension between emotion and pattern: the sharp tilt of the head and the weary hand carry real grief, while the angular, sparkling folds of the drapery are pure design. The green tint of the flesh is probably the underpainting showing through, a standard method of the day. The style is still Byzantine in its refinement, with a new human warmth that points toward Cimabue and Giotto."},"nga:119049":{"d":"About a dozen men and women gather around three crosses against a deep landscape beneath a shiny gold sky. Jesus, haloed, bleeds from his wounds and a ring of thorns; four small angels catch his blood in golden chalices, and a man on the ground pierces his side with a lance. The two thieves are tied to their crosses; at left an angel holds up a tiny figure, at right a demon offers another to the thief, who turns away. Below, Mary in marine blue is supported by two women, with soldiers on horseback near. The panel is about 130 by 200 centimeters.","i":"According to Christian tradition one thief repented and was promised paradise while the other mocked Christ; here an angel receives the soul of the repentant thief at left and a demon claims that of the unrepentant one at right. The mounted man touching his eye, red rays leaving the lid, is probably Longinus, whose sight, in legend, was healed by Christ's blood. Tiny scenes in the distance, the entry into Jerusalem, a Deposition and probably the hanged Judas, set the Crucifixion within the whole Passion.","c":"This is a crowded, vivid, rather eccentric picture: the gold sky, the toy-like souls and the busy crowd belong to late medieval devotional painting, while the deep landscape and the carefully individualized heads show the influence of Netherlandish art. The scale is large and the painter's grip on so many figures is impressive, though the composition is more a catalogue of incidents than a unified drama. A viewer who likes narrative detail will find hours of looking."},"nga:102628":{"d":"A T-shaped cross with Jesus nailed to its crossbeam is raised upright by two men pushing with poles and three pulling on ropes, while a crowd looks on in a grassy, hilly landscape. The scene runs across three panels, a central one and a tall, narrow wing at each side. Jesus, nude but for a white cloth, bleeds from nails and thorns and looks down; skulls and bones litter the ground. At left Mary clasps her hands and women weep; at right mounted men gesture up at him. A town with steeples and a bridge stands hazily on the horizon.","i":"The raising of the cross is an episode the Gospels do not describe, which late medieval painters filled with vivid invention: rough workmen, the bones of earlier executions on Golgotha, the place of the skull, and a heraldic shield at the foot of the cross that probably marks the donor of the triptych. The woman at left holding the cloth with Christ's face is Veronica, whose story belongs later in the Passion, so several moments are gathered into one scene.","c":"The painter's gifts are lively narrative, a wide landscape and an eye for caricature: the hooked-nosed, barefoot puller and the ragged workman are vividly drawn, in contrast to the grave, pale Christ. The composition steps from one panel to the next without losing its center, a real feat in a triptych. It is a little crowded and echoes the Netherlandish pathos of the generation before, but it is a vigorous and absorbing painting."},"nga:102629":{"d":"A woman stands upright, turned to the left, one hand on the hilt of a sword held vertically with its long blade pointing down. She has long blonde hair under an intricate crown, fair skin, pale arched eyebrows, round eyes and small red lips, with a neutral expression. She wears a red robe over a red dress with a black belt and a faint pattern of flowers and vines. At her feet lies a broken piece of curved wood with thin spokes, like part of a wheel. The background is black and the ground gray; the panel is about 66 by 23.5 centimeters.","i":"The sword and the broken wheel identify Saint Catherine of Alexandria, a legendary learned princess of the early fourth century who debated pagan philosophers, was sentenced to be broken on a spiked wheel that shattered at her touch, and was then beheaded. The crown marks her royal birth and her mystic marriage to Christ. She was among the best-loved saints of the later Middle Ages, and here she turns toward the center, as the companion of Saint Barbara on the other wing.","c":"Against black, the red robe is the one strong note, and the painter makes the most of it: the clean silhouette of the sword and the still pose give her a dignity out of proportion to the small panel. The face is conventional, the neutral expression a courtly formula and not a study of feeling, but the picture is clear and well judged, the kind of exterior panel that shows a workshop at its professional best."},"nga:102630":{"d":"A single standing saint, identified as Saint Barbara, fills the outside of a left wing of a triptych, a panel about 66 by 23.5 centimeters. When the triptych was closed, the exteriors of the wings were all the visitor saw, so they carried images of saints, often in a simpler manner than the glowing interior. Barbara is usually shown with a tower, the emblem of her imprisonment, and a martyr's palm, and with Saint Catherine on the right wing she formed a pair of two of the most popular virgin martyrs.","i":"Saint Barbara, a legendary virgin martyr of the early Christian era, was shut in a tower by her father, who later beheaded her when she refused to renounce her faith; she is the patron of those in danger of sudden death, including miners and gunners. In the late fifteenth century she was one of the Fourteen Holy Helpers and among the most popular saints in Germany, and a pair with Saint Catherine presented the Christian ideals of purity and courage to the viewers of the closed altarpiece.","c":"Exterior wings are the neglected parts of a triptych, and a standing saint on one tests the painter's economy: one figure, a plain ground, a few attributes. Such panels succeed through the grace of the pose and the clarity of contour, not through narrative. Seen with its companion, Saint Catherine, and with the interior, which holds all the invention, this wing is a necessary, competent supporting player, and little more."},"nga:33752":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, turned to the side and looking to his right, in a panel about 60 by 44 centimeters. He has a round face and brown curly hair and wears a black hat, a fur-lined coat and a white pleated shirt; one hand holds the fur of his coat and the other rests in front of him. A green curtain, partly drawn aside, reveals a landscape with a tree, a building and a faint path or river. The inscription '1529 N.K.' appears at the top left.","i":"The 'N.K.' presumably gives the painter's initials, and the National Gallery attributes the portrait to Nicolaus Kremer. 'Nobleman' is its cautious label for an unnamed man of rank, shown in fur, a mark of status and wealth. The curtain pulled aside to reveal a landscape is a device German portraitists borrowed from Italian and Netherlandish models, adding space and prestige to what might otherwise be a flat likeness.","c":"German portraits of the 1520s are often at their best in directness, and this one has a plain, firm presence: the round face, the dark hat and the fur frame a likeness that is solid if not penetrating. The landscape behind the curtain is a nice touch but remains slight, a decoration more than a world. Kremer is a lesser name than Holbein or Cranach and does not rival them, but he is an honest recorder."},"nga:42407":{"d":"A man sits at a wooden desk in a formal, upright posture. His features are sharp and his light brown hair falls in waves. He wears a black coat, white shirt, black waistcoat and black bow tie. On the desk lie sealed books, a quill pen in an inkwell and a folded paper, and he holds another sheet in his left hand. Behind him a bookshelf filled with books and documents is partly covered by a large curtain, and an architectural column rises in the background. The canvas, about 112 by 90 centimeters, is dated 1841.","i":"The desk, books, quill and papers are the lawyer's tools, and the curtain and column, imported from European grand portraits, lend dignity. Bundy, a New England folk portraitist, gave his clients both a likeness and the attributes of their trade; the title names the profession and the state but not the man. Pictures like this show how the language of aristocratic portraiture was adapted for ambitious professionals in the young American republic.","c":"The picture's power is its flat, almost graphic clarity. The black coat against the pale face, the crisp lines of the desk and the strong verticals of column and bookshelf make a bold, poster-like design; the proportions are slightly off and the modeling is minimal, but these departures from academic norms give the portrait its character. It is a standout of American folk portraiture, with a directness that polished portraits of lawyers rarely match."},"nga:43442":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, facing forward with her head slightly turned to the left, in a nearly square canvas of about 64 by 60 centimeters, dated 1840. She has soft features, light skin and dark brown ringlets around her face. She wears a dress with a white lace collar, a pastel blue shawl with white floral patterns, a pink bow at the neck and a small brooch, and holds a book in her left hand. A dark curtain hangs at one side, and at the other a view opens onto a river, buildings, a boat and a colorful sky.","i":"Ringlets, a bow at the neck and a pastel shawl belong to the early 1840s, the age of sentimental portraiture, when a book in a woman's hand stood for refinement and moral reading. The view beside the curtain was a familiar device, bringing a little scenery into a modest portrait. The painter, Abram Ross Stanley, is one of the many provincial portraitists of the nineteenth-century United States of whom little is recorded.","c":"The strengths are pleasing color and a lively surface: the pastel shawl, pink bow and colorful sky give the picture a cheerful palette, and the ringlets are neatly done. The weakness is the stiffness typical of provincial portraiture: the form is flat, and the landscape window looks like a separate picture. It is an attractive, characteristic example of its type, valued for charm more than depth."},"nga:59915":{"d":"A child stands facing forward, round-faced, with rosy cheeks, wide blue eyes, a small closed mouth and short light hair, in a vertical canvas of about 62 by 48 centimeters, dated 1842. The child wears a yellow dress with ruffled off-the-shoulder sleeves and holds a blue ribbon attached to a small beige-and-brown dog on a green cushion. Behind lie greenery, trees, a small house, water with sailboats and a village in the distance.","i":"Although the child wears a dress, the sitter, Joshua Lamb, was a boy; boys wore frocks until they were breeched, usually around five or six. The dog on a ribbon is a common emblem of a child's affection and of loyalty, and a small pet on a cushion shows the household's comfort. The view behind, with sailboats and a village, may be a conventional backdrop and not a record of a particular place.","c":"A child with a pet is a sure appeal, and Stanley gives it a warm, sunny key: the yellow dress against green and blue, the leashed dog at the cushion's edge. The painter simplifies the forms and arranges them with a decorative sense, and the background reads as a fantasy of home and harbor. It is not subtle, but it is cheerful, clearly drawn and memorable, the kind of folk portrait that charms before it persuades."},"nga:46663":{"d":"A woman sits in a golden-upholstered chair against a light-colored wall, in a vertical canvas of about 87 by 65 centimeters. Her dark brown hair is in a neat updo, and she has soft features and light skin. She wears a black gown with a white lace collar, a gold necklace with a red pendant, a bracelet and rings, and holds a closed red fan in her right hand. A small round box rests on the chair's armrest at her right.","i":"Elizabeth Oakes Smith (1806 to 1893) was a poet, novelist and lecturer, wife of the humorist Seba Smith, and in the 1850s one of the early public advocates of women's rights, a rare career for a woman of her day. This portrait of about 1845 shows her as a refined woman of letters, with the dark gown and jewelry of a New York lady and not as a reformer. The fan and box add touches of fashion and private life.","c":"Paradise paints a handsome, orderly portrait: the black gown gives a strong central shape, the lace collar and red accents enliven it, and the face is clear and composed. It is competent society portraiture rather than inspired characterization, and a viewer who knows the sitter's later activism may wish for more edge. As a record of an early American woman writer in her fashionable prime, it is a satisfying document."},"nga:59963":{"d":"A reddish-brown horse stands at the center of an open landscape, its legs bent and its head turned over its shoulder to look left at a steam train in the distance. The horse's mane and tail blow in the wind, its eye is wide and its mouth partly open. The gray-and-brown train follows a thin curving track, trailing white and gray smoke. Tall grasses, rocks, bushes, a small blue pond and a tall tree fill the landscape under a hazy, cloudy sky.","i":"The title puns on the nickname iron horse for the locomotive: a real horse is startled by the noise and smoke of its mechanical rival. In the 1860s and 1870s the railroad was reshaping the American countryside, and pictures of animals meeting trains turned a social change into a joke. The picture comes from the Garbisch collection of American naive painting, where such humor is a common thread.","c":"The horse's rearing, twisting pose is stiff and stylized, and the train is little more than a toy, but the drawing is expressive enough that the animal's alarm is immediately felt. The central placement and the wide eye are clever. It is a funny, charming picture that also records, in a childlike way, a cultural moment when the old and new transport met."},"nga:59902":{"d":"Two shirtless men fight in a ring, one in orange trousers with black dots at left, the other in red with white dots at right. Behind them stand two more shirtless men in the same patterned trousers and two men in jackets, vests and hats, while a semicircle of men in formal dress watches from outside the ring. The setting is outdoors, with grass underfoot, green hills behind and a light gray sky. The small panel, oil on paperboard on wood, measures about 30 by 49 centimeters.","i":"Bare-knuckle prizefighting was illegal in many American states by the 1860s, so matches were held outdoors in out-of-the-way places, often before crowds of men in their best clothes who had traveled to see them. The rules were those of the London Prize Ring, and a fight could last dozens of rounds. Gloved boxing under the Queensberry rules, introduced in 1867, gradually replaced the older sport, and the picture records a kind of event that was already passing.","c":"A small, naive painting like this has the energy of a snapshot: the stripped fighters, the ring of onlookers and the dotted trousers are all described flatly with a lively sense of pattern. It has neither the anatomical skill of Thomas Eakins's boxing pictures nor the drama of George Bellows's later Stag at Sharkey's, but it records a vanishing sport with an unselfconscious honesty. Its flatness is part of the charm."},"nga:51139":{"d":"An elevated view shows a bustling port town, with a tall, dense green tree at the center against a light blue sky with fluffy white clouds. Stately houses with yards, pastoral scenes, winding roads and bridges lead down to the harbor, where industrial buildings stand near the water and sailboats and steamships are visible. At the left a castle-like structure rests on a rocky green hill. The canvas, about 65 by 102 centimeters, is painted in vibrant greens, earthy browns, soft blues and creams, with a sky that takes up much of the picture.","i":"A composite view combines elements from several places in one imagined scene, a practice common among folk and decorative painters of the nineteenth century, who treated the landscape as a kit of pleasing parts: the castle, the harbor, the steamships and the bridges. The result is an ideal of prosperous harbor life more than a portrait of a real port. Jurgan Frederick Huge, who painted such scenes about 1875, offered buyers a world of progress and picturesque tradition side by side.","c":"The charm is in the profusion: houses, bridges, steamships and a castle crammed into a single bright view, with the central tree as an anchor and the large sky as relief. The perspective is unreliable and the proportions invented, but the picture has the happy confidence of a painter who knows he is building a dream. Folk painting seldom looks more cheerful, and the composite is a rich record of the period's dream of progress."},"nga:50690":{"d":"A scene of a blacksmith's workshop, painted around 1880 in oil on canvas, about 59 by 82 centimetres. The subject was a familiar one in nineteenth-century American painting: a dim interior glowing with forge light, with a smith at the anvil, tools hanging on the walls and perhaps a horse waiting to be shod. Pictures of this kind usually place several figures at their work, and the title and the museum's keyword for occupation point to a picture about labour.","i":"The blacksmith was a central figure in village life, shoeing horses and making and mending tools, hinges and wheel rims, and the smithy was also a place where neighbours gathered and news was swapped. In the later nineteenth century, when factories and railways were changing rural America, pictures of the village smith had a nostalgic ring: they celebrated honest craft and a community that seemed threatened. Such pictures are usually read in that spirit, as a genial tribute to manual skill.","c":"Beckett is a minor name, and next to the leading American genre painters of the day, such as Winslow Homer or Eastman Johnson, he is a quiet figure. A smithy is a rewarding subject, with its forge glow and sheen of metal, and a picture of this kind earns its keep through such effects and through its affectionate record of a vanished trade. It deserves a look as a piece of period feeling rather than as an innovation."},"nga:46490":{"d":"A young woman is seated in a slightly side-facing position on a stone ledge with decorative carving. She has fair skin, brown eyes and medium-length hair in a neat cut, and wears a red garment with black accents and a white collar, presumably a riding habit, holding a slender whip or stick in gloved hands. Behind her a pastel sky, rolling hills and faint buildings suggest an outdoor setting. Oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters, dated 1839, by the French painter Charles David.","i":"Equestrian portraits of young women were fashionable in the 1830s, when riding was a social accomplishment of well-bred girls, and a riding habit and whip signaled leisure, health and sporting skill. The sitter is not named, and the title gives her only by her pastime. Charles David, a lesser-known French painter, worked in the polite portrait tradition of the July Monarchy.","c":"A fresh, unpretentious portrait in which the red habit supplies the one vivid note against a pastel landscape, and the young sitter's direct, slightly wary look has charm. The pose on the stone ledge is a little stiff, and the painter is a minor figure in French portraiture, but the conviction of the likeness and the color are well judged. A pleasing document of the July Monarchy's polite taste."},"nga:34160":{"d":"A man appears with a soft, round face, a slightly double chin, small eyes and a neutral expression, in a portrait about 77 by 64 centimeters. He has gray, voluminous hair in the fashion of the eighteenth century and wears a dark coat with gold buttons over a white ruffled shirt, one hand hidden under his coat. The background is dark and earthy, which centers the focus on the sitter. The picture dates from about 1760, early in Joseph Wright's career as a portraitist in Derby.","i":"The sitter is not named, a loss typical of portraits made for private families and not for public commemoration. The pose, a hand tucked into the coat, was a gentlemanly convention that suggested ease. Wright trained with the London portraitist Thomas Hudson and then came home to Derby, where he painted the professional and commercial men of the Midlands, the manufacturers, doctors and lawyers who were building the new industrial society.","c":"A plain, honest likeness: the painter observes the soft face and the double chin without flattery and builds the figure from a few strong tones against the dark ground. At this date Wright is still close to the London portrait manner of Hudson, which he would shortly leave behind for the candlelit scenes that made him famous. Not startling, but direct and well made, it shows the portrait craft on which his later invention rested."},"nga:570":{"d":"A man stands knees-up with his arms crossed over his chest and his head turned to the right, in a canvas of about 128 by 102 centimeters. He has ruddy cheeks with a brown mole, a long nose, small red lips and gray curls above his ears under a black hat. His dark blue-and-red coat is richly embroidered with gold and has gold buttons, with an off-white cravat; a black-gloved hand holds a cane and the other glove. Behind him rise large moss-covered rocks with trailing vines, under a pale-yellow cloudy sky with hints of blue.","i":"Portraits set against rocks and sky were a fashion of the English portrait of the 1760s and 1770s, in which the sitter was presented as a gentleman at ease in nature, an image of landed confidence. Wright's sitters in these years were often the manufacturers, physicians and landowners of the Midlands. The crossed arms, cane and gloves make a pose of assured, slightly informal authority, and the mole is one of the details that make the face a likeness and not a type.","c":"Wright is less at home in the grand portrait than in his candlelit scenes, and the outdoor setting here is borrowed and a little conventional. The face is where the picture comes alive: the ruddy cheek, the mole, the shrewd long nose, with the embroidery of the coat painted with relish. It is a solid, characterful portrait, more interesting for the man than for the setting."},"met:437954":{"d":"A woman is the unnamed subject of this large oil portrait, about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1770 by Joseph Wright of Derby. The size, about fifty by forty inches, was a standard half-length format for sitters of means. Wright is remembered for his scenes of science and candlelight, but portraits paid his bills, and a portrait of this size and date belongs to his Derby years before the journey to Italy. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1986 by the Heathcote Art Foundation.","i":"Women of this class were shown in fashionable silks with an air of ease, and Wright's likenesses are known for an honest, slightly plain realism that his sitters, the wives and daughters of manufacturers and professionals, apparently valued. A portrait of about 1770 would have been made for a family of means in Derby or the Midlands, to hang in a principal room, often as the pendant to a husband's likeness.","c":"In his portraits of women Wright lacks the glamour of Reynolds or the shimmering touch of Gainsborough, and he does not try to supply it. What he offers is solid drawing, sober color and a face that looks like a person. A viewer who expects the drama of his night pieces will find little, but the portrait has a quiet integrity, and it fills in the picture of the painter's working life."},"met:441769":{"d":"A night landscape of rock, ruin and cloud. At the lower left, a dim vaulted chamber cut into the hillside glows orange-red, and a small robed figure sits inside, reading by lamplight. Above and around it rise heavy masses of stone, topped with dark trees. At the right, a rough flight of steps climbs to another ruined structure on a hill. Silvery clouds fill the sky, and in a break at the upper right the moon casts a pale light over the scene. The colours are earthy browns and greens against cool blue-grey, and the paint is thin, with the clouds softly brushed. The figure is tiny beside the huge stonework.","i":"The Met explains that Wright depicts a site long popular with visitors to Naples, and that Londoners who saw the painting at the Royal Academy in 1779 would have recognised the tomb of Virgil, author of the Aeneid. Less familiar is the figure, Silius Italicus, the Roman orator who marked the anniversary of the poet's death each year by reading Virgil's verses aloud within the tomb. The Met reads the picture as a meditation on mortality, with the flickering glow of the lantern set against the silvery moonlight spread over the landscape. Wright himself had visited Italy in the mid-1770s.","c":"Wright liked to put two light sources in one picture, and the contrast of warm lamp and cool moon is the idea that makes this canvas memorable. The cloud around the moon is beautifully done. The picture is less successful as a story. Silius is a dot of colour in an enormous mass of rock, and the foreground steps and ruins carry more of the weight. It is more a mood than a drama, and a dark one. As a meditation on time it is persuasive, with the human figure as small as it should be."},"nga:61396":{"d":"In a dimly lit room, a barefoot woman in a dark yellow dress leans one knee onto a chair and reaches out with a stylus to trace on the wall the shadow of a sleeping young man. He sits in a deep wooden chair, a long spear on one shoulder, wearing a harvest-yellow tunic and a sapphire-blue cloak, with a thin brown dog asleep at his side. A deep red curtain at the upper left hides the lamp that casts his shadow, and urns stand about the room. The horizontal canvas is about 106 by 131 centimeters.","i":"The subject is the legendary origin of drawing, told by Pliny: a young woman of Corinth traced the shadow of her lover as he was about to leave, and her father, a potter, pressed clay over the outline to make a relief, so beginning modeling. Wright makes the lover a sleeper. The painting is said to have been made for his friend the potter Josiah Wedgwood, whose craft the legend flatters, and it joined Wright's interest in light with the new neoclassical taste for ancient subjects.","c":"The idea is a good one, and Wright's night-scene training serves it: a single hidden light, strong shadows and a hush in which the girl's tenderness and the sleeper's repose are the only events. The figures are a little stiff and the costumes theatrical, yet the central conceit, art born of love and absence, is made visible with a clarity and charm that few history painters of the day could match. It is among Wright's most intelligent late pictures."},"nga:61397":{"d":"A landscape of Italy, painted in 1790 in oil on canvas, about 104 by 130 centimeters. Joseph Wright spent two years in Italy, from 1773 to 1775, where he saw Rome and Naples and the eruption of Vesuvius, and he drew on those memories for the Italian subjects he painted for the rest of his life. The picture is therefore a recollection, made some fifteen years after his stay, and typical of his late interest in the light of southern skies.","i":"Italy meant antiquity, light and the sublime to British viewers, and a landscape titled simply Italian would call up a collection of associations: classical ruins, a warm sky, a feeling of leisure. Wright painted the lakes, grottoes and cascades he had seen in Italy for the rest of his career, with an attentiveness to light that links him to Claude. This one was made when he was in his mid-fifties, among the landscapes of his last years.","c":"Wright's Italian landscapes of the 1780s and 1790s are meditations on light more than on topography, aiming at a Claude-like calm, and their softer handling is a long way from the hard, hot drama of his firelit interiors and his volcanoes. They are agreeable, skilled pictures, less original than the work on which his reputation rests."},"nga:41657":{"d":"A broad landscape of about 97 by 144 centimeters, painted about 1629, populated by merchants, who give the picture its title. An early work by Claude Lorrain, it dates from his first years in Rome, when he was learning from the landscape painters there, among them Netherlandish artists who painted small figures busy at everyday tasks. Merchants stand for trade and travel, the commerce of the countryside and the port, and in Claude's early pictures such figures give the scene life before light and distance take over.","i":"Claude Gellée, called Claude Lorrain from his native Lorraine, came to Rome as a young man and learned landscape under Agostino Tassi, who taught him perspective and the painting of coasts and harbors. The Roman scene included Dutch and Flemish painters such as Paul Bril and the Bamboccianti, who painted small figures of ordinary life, and merchants and travelers were among their subjects. Claude's early pictures show him absorbing these models before he made the golden, classical landscapes for which he is famous.","c":"An early Claude is a picture of promise and charm more than of mastery: the figures are handled with the lively detail of the Bamboccianti, the composition is busier than the calm designs he later perfected, and the light has not yet become the gold of the 1640s. Even so, the clear spatial arrangement and the feeling for air and light mark a gifted landscapist, and the picture shows where his famous style began."},"met:435905":{"d":"A river landscape in oil on canvas, about 74 by 101 centimeters, possibly painted in 1636. A man, a woman, a boat and a river are set within a wider landscape. The title names a ford, a shallow crossing place, a motif that lets the painter combine travelers with water and a softly receding countryside. It is by Claude Lorrain (Claude Gellée), the French painter who worked in Rome and made light and atmosphere the real subject of his pictures.","i":"A ford suits Claude's pastoral world: travelers, herders and boats move quietly through an orderly countryside, and everyday labor becomes part of a timeless, Arcadian calm. His figures are small and serve the light and the space more than any story. The picture is not a portrait of a particular place; it is better read as an ideal Roman countryside, based on the real campagna but made calmer and more beautiful.","c":"Claude's fame rests on how convincingly he makes air and distance, and a river scene from the mid-1630s catches that skill at an early, confident stage. The motif is modest and the figures subordinate, so the effect depends on atmosphere. It is quieter and more intimate than the grand pictures he made for popes and kings, a reminder that his poetry works on a small canvas."},"met:435908":{"d":"A wide harbor scene in soft light, with a low sun glowing at the horizon. A tall galleon with red pennants fills the left edge in dark profile, and more ships ride at anchor in the middle distance and at right, their masts topped with orange and yellow flags. On the sandy shore in the foreground a group of women in bright dresses runs and gestures with raised arms, and a small boat with figures lies at the water's edge. Tents show on the hillside at the right, and a leaning tree frames the right edge. Heavy cloud in the upper sky presses over the water.","i":"The subject is from Book 5 of Virgil's Aeneid. During the Trojans' journey, the exhausted women, urged on by the goddess Juno's messenger, set fire to the fleet in Sicily to end their wandering; Jupiter then sent a storm at Aeneas's request that put out the blaze. The Metropolitan Museum notes that the clouds and rain in the distance foreshadow that storm. This was the first of eight paintings Claude devoted to Aeneid stories, and it was made in Rome for Cardinal Girolamo Farnese, who returned there in 1643 after years of traveling service against Protestantism. It dates from about 1643.","c":"Claude is better at light than at storytelling. The glowing horizon, the pearly water and the finely drawn rigging are beautifully handled, and the long diagonal of the ships gives the harbor depth. The women, who should supply the drama, are small pastel figures that move like a dance rather than a crime, and without the title the story would be lost. As a seascape it is first rate; as a history painting it is polite."},"met:435906":{"d":"A broad pastoral landscape of cattle and a river in the countryside around Rome, the Roman Campagna of the title. It is oil on canvas, about 102 by 136 centimeters, dated about 1639. The scene belongs to Claude's idealized campagna: open ground, grazing animals and water bathed in the soft, golden light for which he was famous. Such a picture would have hung in a collector's gallery as a calm retreat from the city.","i":"The title joins two ideas. Pastoral recalls the classical poetry of shepherds, an imagined age of peaceful country life; campagna names the actual, open countryside around Rome that Claude knew from drawing outdoors. The picture merges them: a landscape anyone in Rome could ride out to, shown as an idyll. Cattle are the sign of that peaceful, productive nature, and their quiet presence stands for an unhurried world.","c":"A mature statement of Claude's formula at the end of the 1630s: wide space, quiet figures, a river that guides the eye into a bright distance. If it has a weakness, it is a certain sameness with his other pastorals, and the pleasure lies in gradations of light rather than in invention. A fine, representative Claude rather than a revelatory one, which is enough to explain why his landscapes were sought across Europe."},"nga:51095":{"d":"In a pastoral landscape, the Trojan prince Paris, a shepherd, is asked to decide which of three goddesses, Juno, Minerva and Venus, is the fairest, in a canvas of about 112 by 150 centimeters painted in 1645 and 1646. The title names the scene, and the picture shows the moment of judgment in an open, sunlit setting. As in Claude's other mythological pictures, the figures are small within a vast, light-filled landscape, with trees to the sides and an airy distance.","i":"The story is the cause of the Trojan War: Paris chose Venus, who offered him the love of Helen, the most beautiful woman in the world, and the abduction of Helen began the war. For Claude and his Roman patrons the myth was a pretext for an Arcadian landscape; the human drama is small beside the effects of light and air. A viewer who knows the story will feel the shadow of Troy behind the sunlit pastoral.","c":"Claude's mythologies are landscape paintings with a story as an excuse, and that is both their glory and their limit: the figures are rarely expressive, but the landscape is superb, with its graded distances, its trees that act like stage wings and its golden air. In such pictures the goddesses are decorative rather than dramatic, and the real event is the light. It is a good example of the classical landscape that set the standard for two centuries."},"met:435907":{"d":"A broad valley opens at dawn beyond a stand of tall, slender trees whose fine foliage is lit from behind. At the low horizon the sky glows pink and pale orange, melting into a hazy line of hills, and overhead it turns clear blue with thin streaks of cloud. A building crowns a wooded hill at right. In the foreground two figures cross a stream with a small herd of cattle, while at left a herdsman gestures toward a flock of goats and sheep on the bank. The foreground and middle distance have darkened with age, so the bright horizon now stands out sharply against warm brown shadows.","i":"The Metropolitan Museum describes the scene as an idealized landscape inspired by the Roman Campagna, an imagined Golden Age rather than a real place. Claude's own record book, the Liber Veritatis, notes that he painted it in Rome for an unnamed patron from Lyons, and it later belonged to Sir Joshua Reynolds, who admired this kind of Arcadian vision. The date is given only as possibly 1646–47. Claude, born in Lorraine and working in Rome, was a close friend of Poussin there, but he looked for serenity and luminous air where Poussin looked for stoic or dramatic themes. No story is told; the subject is the morning light itself.","c":"The glowing horizon is what Claude is known for, with sky, haze and far hills fused into a single warm light. The rest has not aged as well. The darkened foreground and middle ground make the trees heavy and the ford murky, and the figures are routine pastoral staffage. The picture is best enjoyed as a study in composing with light, the eye led from dark trees to the pale glow and held there. It is a calm, characteristic Claude rather than an inventive one."},"met:435909":{"d":"A small landscape, about 39 by 58 centimeters, in oil on canvas, painted about 1648 to 1650. Its title names La Crescenza, a country property outside Rome, and trees are a main feature of the view. Naming a real place was unusual for Claude, who normally composed ideal landscapes from drawn and remembered elements. The modest size and specific title suggest a picture grounded in close study of the site, not a grand invention.","i":"Claude often explored the countryside around Rome on sketching walks, and La Crescenza lay in that kind of rural setting. A named place gives the picture a topographical identity, yet Claude was never a documentarian: arrangement, light and trees are shaped toward harmony. Why he chose this site is not known, and the picture is best read as an ideal view built on a real one.","c":"Smaller, closer and more directly observed than the grand compositions that made Claude's reputation, this view invites comparison with his outdoor drawings. What succeeds is the sense of a specific place held together by trees and light; what is missing, inevitably, is the imaginative sweep of the big canvases. It is the intimate side of the artist, and a useful corrective to the polished ideal manner."},"nga:42409":{"d":"A woman facing forward, shown from the torso up, holds a closed book with her hands together. She has fair skin, dark eyes, thin eyebrows and brown curly hair framing her face, and wears a lace cap, a dark dress with a white lace collar, gold earrings set with red jewels and a gold chain with a key or locket. Behind her, a landscape of trees and water opens under a cloudy sky. Oil on canvas, about 71 by 56 centimeters, painted in 1837.","i":"The closed book, perhaps a Bible or devotional volume, was a common sign of a pious, literate woman, and the key or locket on its chain suggests household authority or sentiment. The portrait has the same date and size as the portrait of Henry W. Houston, so the two were probably meant to hang together, presumably as husband and wife, although the titles do not state the relationship. The view of trees and water is a conventional backdrop and is not known to show a particular place.","c":"A plain, frontal portrait in the direct manner of many American portraits of the 1830s: the lace, the curls and the jewelry are described patiently, one by one, and the sitter's composed gaze carries the picture. The flat, symmetrical pose and stock landscape add little depth, but their plainness makes the sitter's dignity unmistakable. The picture is valued more for its candor than for refinement."},"nga:43712":{"d":"A man shown from the waist up, turned slightly to the left yet looking straight at the viewer, in a black suit with a white shirt and black necktie. He has short dark hair combed back, a straight nose and thin lips. In one hand he holds a yellow, ruler-like tool and in the other a compass; behind him a surveyor's transit stands on its tripod. A leafy tree and a partly cloudy sky fill the background. Oil on canvas, about 71 by 56 centimeters, painted in 1837.","i":"The instruments identify the sitter's occupation: surveying, the trade by which land was measured, divided and sold across a growing United States. An occupational portrait lets a sitter present himself through his skill rather than through rank or ancestry, a self-image suited to a republic of landowners and professionals. The picture is presumably a pendant to the portrait of Mrs. Phebe Houston, of the same year and size.","c":"The honest charm of this picture lies in its attributes: the compass, the rule and the transit are the real subject, and they tell more about the man than his expression does. Stiff in pose and plain in modeling, it belongs to a tradition of American portraiture that values clarity and likeness over polish. As a document of a trade it is more interesting than many more refined portraits."},"nga:45966":{"d":"A woman is shown in profile from the chest up, facing left, in a small canvas of about 37 by 27 centimeters. She has a prominent straight nose, deep-set eyes and distinct eyebrows, and her hair is covered by a large white pleated cap with a blue-gray ribbon. She wears a brown garment with a high, white pleated collar. The background is a dark, solid color, which makes the white of the cap and collar stand out.","i":"The mob cap, a large gathered cap worn indoors by women of the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries, marks the sitter as a respectable matron. The profile view was popular in the early republic, from silhouettes and from the physiognotrace, a device that traced a sitter's outline; it gave a clear, economical likeness at low cost, and painters such as Benjamin Greenleaf, who worked in New England, adopted it for small oils.","c":"A profile has nowhere to hide: the whole likeness depends on the outline of nose, brow and chin, and Greenleaf gives it with clear, firm line. The shading is minimal and the dark ground is flat, so the white cap and collar do the work of design. It is a plain, honest folk portrait with the economy of the silhouette tradition, and its simplicity is its virtue."},"nga:42444":{"d":"A side profile shows a person with fair skin, high cheekbones and a prominent nose, wearing a lace cap with a pink ribbon, a high-collared white garment trimmed with lace and bowed at the neck, and a brown garment on the shoulder. A round badge bears the initials JL. The background is a solid, muted gray. The portrait is painted in reverse on glass and measures about 33 by 26 centimeters.","i":"Reverse painting on glass is a demanding technique: the artist paints on the back of the glass, so the details and highlights must be applied first and the background last, and the image is viewed through the glass, which gives the colors a glossy, jewel-like clarity. It was a popular craft for portraits in the early republic. The initials on the badge are the only name the picture carries, a reminder of how a likeness can outlast its sitter's identity.","c":"Painting backward on glass makes anything fussy impossible, so the profile and the flat gray ground suit the technique, and the lace, ribbon and bow are drawn with clean, decisive strokes. The result has a freshness and glossy clarity that oil on canvas lacks. It is a modest work in a difficult medium, and the care in the lace cap shows the pleasure the painter took in solving it."},"nga:43447":{"d":"A farm is shown in an oil on canvas of about 64 by 89 centimeters, dated 1872. The title names its owner, Benjamin Reber, and the museum's keywords record a house and a tree among the elements. Farm portraits of this kind, made by local and traveling painters, typically show a farmer's property proudly, with the house, barns, fields and orchard set out in a clear view as a record of success. The painter, Charles C. Hofmann, worked in eastern Pennsylvania.","i":"Pennsylvania's Berks County was a prosperous farming district settled largely by German speakers, and farm views such as this were commissioned by farmers who took pride in their large barns, orchards and fields. Charles Hofmann is also known for views of the Berks County almshouse, where he is said to have lived in his later years. The painting is a statement of ownership and pride as much as a landscape.","c":"Farm portraits stand or fall on their clarity: the buildings are drawn firmly and the land shown in an ordered pattern. Hofmann's views have the flat, descriptive charm of folk art, with a sense of design more than of perspective. This one is a good example of the type, valued as a record of a place and a prosperous moment more than as pictorial art."},"met:11107":{"d":"A broad view of a Pennsylvania almshouse property, painted in 1876 on a canvas of about 73 by 99 centimeters. The record lists trees, houses, a town, a horse and a cart, so the composition combines the institution's buildings and grounds with a town beyond and the everyday traffic of a horse-drawn cart. The museum gives no further description of the layout, color or figures.","i":"County almshouses were public institutions for the poor, the elderly and the sick, and many were run as working farms. A view of the 'property' presents the institution as an orderly, productive establishment, with its buildings, fields and the town beyond, and may have been made for the county officials who ran it. Hofmann painted in a self-taught manner, and the Garbisch gift places the work among the American folk painting that Edgar and Bernice Garbisch collected.","c":"Folk topographical painters deliver information more than atmosphere, and the pleasure of this kind of picture is in the abundance of precisely placed buildings, trees, carts and horses, each given equal weight. The perspective in such pictures is usually naive, but the large size and the evident pride in the place give it dignity. As a document of an American institution in the centennial year it outweighs its artistic polish."},"nga:42420":{"d":"Several small views of landscapes and buildings surround a central oval of the almshouse complex, with its red buildings, gardens, fields, roads, carriages and livestock, painted on a sheet of zinc about 83 by 100 centimeters. A label at the bottom reads 'Views of the Buildings & Surroundings of the Berks County Alms House. 1878.' A decorative border with a crest or shield at the top frames the whole, in a layout like a commemorative print.","i":"An almshouse was the county's poorhouse, a place where the poor, the old, the sick and the insane were housed and put to work on the farm. The painter, Charles Hofmann, is said to have lived at this very institution, and his views of it would then record the place from the inside. A picture with several vignettes suggests a keepsake or a record for the directors, as much a document of a social institution as a landscape.","c":"The multiple views and the oval centerpiece give this picture the look of a printed bird's-eye map, and its quality is that of the folk draftsman: clear, tidy, rather rigid and lovingly detailed. The zinc support was an unusual choice. It is not a work of subtle feeling, but its thoroughness and the pathos of its subject, a place of last resort pictured with such care, make it a moving document."},"nga:42498":{"d":"A trotting horse is the subject of this horizontal oil on canvas, about 51 by 92 centimeters, painted about 1860. Harness racing, with horses trained to trot at speed pulling a light sulky, was the most popular spectator sport in mid-nineteenth-century America, and pictures of famous or well-bred trotters were commissioned by owners and sold as prints. The wide, low format suits a horse in profile at full stride, as in the sporting pictures of the day.","i":"Harness racing grew from Americans' practical need for fast road horses, and its heroes were horses such as Flora Temple, who in 1859 broke the 2:20 mile. Pictures of the trotter, like those of Charles S. Humphreys, answered a market of owners, drivers and fans, and were reproduced in lithographs by firms such as Currier and Ives. A title like The Trotter may stand for a type as much as for a single named horse.","c":"Horse portraits are judged by anatomy and spirit, and a specialist like Humphreys, whose subjects were the stars of the track, is expected to get the legs and the proportions right. The honest, flat clarity of sporting art of this kind is its charm; it does not aim at the finish of Stubbs or the elegance of the English school, but it records an American sport at its start."},"nga:52952":{"d":"A driver and a trotting mare race on a track in an oil on canvas of about 66 by 92 centimeters, dated 1876. The title names the pair: Budd Doble, the celebrated driver, behind Goldsmith Maid, the most famous trotter of her day, at the Belmont Driving Park. Pictures of this type usually show horse and sulky in profile, the driver intent, the track and its rail forming a stage for the speed, in the manner of the sporting print.","i":"Goldsmith Maid (1857 to 1885) was the great trotting champion of the 1870s, and Budd Doble, her driver, became a celebrity himself. Her records of the decade made her the Queen of the Turf and a household name, and the popular press followed her races. A painting made in 1876, the year of the Centennial, was a trophy for an owner or an admirer as much as a work of art.","c":"A portrait of a champion has a built-in drama: the horse's name was famous, and the picture can rely on the viewer's knowledge. Humphreys is a straightforward painter of racing, and his work satisfies when the drawing of the horse's stride is exact and the event feels witnessed. As a record of a celebrated pair at the height of American harness racing, it has historic interest beyond its modest art."},"nga:46609":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, turned slightly to her right and looking straight at the viewer with a gentle gaze. She has fair skin, round cheeks and soft curls or waves of hair swept back. She wears a garment with a pale bodice and a layer of rich blue fabric wrapped around her shoulders, and she raises her right hand to hold the edge of the blue cloth. The background is a smooth, dark, neutral gradient. The picture is dated 1731, measures about 77 by 61 centimetres, and matches the size of a portrait of a man in the same collection.","i":"A drape held in a raised hand is a gesture familiar from earlier portrait painting, where it gave a sitter dignity and a hint of fashion. As with its companion, the question mark in the title shows that the identification as Anna de Peyster is uncertain. A matching pair of portraits recorded a couple for their descendants, and the dark, plain ground focuses attention on face and costume.","c":"The face is round and kindly, the blue wrap is well managed as a mass of colour, and the soft gradient ground keeps attention on the sitter. But the modelling is flat and the raised hand stiff, and the picture is more a respectable likeness than an expressive one. With its companion it belongs to the modest tier of provincial portraiture, valued for what it records more than for how it was painted."},"nga:46610":{"d":"A man sits facing slightly to the right with his head turned towards the viewer, with arched eyebrows, a pronounced nose and a slight smile. Long, curly, grey-white locks fall to his shoulders. He wears a brown coat with buttons, a white ruffled cravat and white ruffled cuffs, and one hand rests visibly on his lap. The background is a mix of dark brown and green. The portrait is dated 1731 and measures about 77 by 61 centimetres; it is the same size and date as a portrait of a woman in the same collection, and the two are probably a pair.","i":"The question mark in the title shows that the sitter's name, Isaac de Peyster, rests on tradition and is not certain. The de Peysters were a family with Dutch roots, and a pair of portraits dated 1731 would have recorded a husband and wife in their best clothes for the family house. Portraits of this kind followed Dutch and English models: a plain dark background, a direct gaze, and clothing carefully shown to indicate respectability.","c":"A sober, competent portrait: the slight smile and turned head give the sitter a friendly presence, and the brown coat, white linen and dark ground are handled with plain restraint. The painter's touch is neither refined nor inventive, and the hand resting in the lap is awkwardly placed. It is a pleasantly unpretentious likeness whose chief interest is as a record of an early-eighteenth-century household, not as a feat of painting."},"nga:59909":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up in a brightly decorated military jacket, in a canvas of about 76 by 63 centimeters, dated about 1823. He has a fair complexion, sideburns, dark hair combed up and a neutral expression, and rests his left hand on the front of the jacket, which is trimmed in red and gold with gold epaulettes, a high collar, gold buttons and a white cravat. A decorative sash crosses his chest and waist, and a sheathed sword hangs at his side from a gold belt. The background is plain, muted gray.","i":"The title names him John Harrisson, and the portrait is the companion of Mayhew's Mrs. John Harrisson and Daughter, of the same date and size. A uniform like this one, with epaulettes and sash, was typical of the militia and volunteer companies of the early republic, in which a man of standing took a rank and a colorful outfit. Part-time soldiering was a source of local prestige, and a portrait in uniform records it.","c":"The uniform is the picture: red, gold and white against a plain gray ground, and Mayhew gives its trimmings plenty of attention. The face is calm to the point of blankness, as in much provincial portraiture of the 1820s. What lifts the painting is the confident silhouette and the almost heraldic brightness of the costume. Paired with his wife's portrait, it shows a family proud of its standing."},"nga:59910":{"d":"A woman sits with an infant on her lap, facing slightly left but turning her head toward the viewer, in a canvas of about 76 by 63 centimeters, dated about 1823. She has large eyes, thin lips and dark hair under a lace-trimmed cap, with a calm expression, and wears a high-collared buttoned dress and a shawl; a gold ring shows on the hand resting on the child. The baby, in a bonnet with light blue ribbons, a white dress and a gold necklace, gazes directly at the viewer. The background is dark, with a decorative chair with green leaf-like designs.","i":"Mother-and-child portraits of the 1820s combined a family record with a note of maternal piety, the mother's steady gaze and hand on the baby signaling duty and tenderness. The wedding ring, drawn with care, marks her married status, and the baby's gold necklace and bonnet show the family's means. The sitter is identified as Harriet Wood Harrisson, and the picture records one of the commonest and most affecting of private occasions, a young child with her mother.","c":"The design is simple and strong: a dark ground, a pale face, a pale baby and the mother's hand linking the two. Mayhew gives the woman a steady, rather severe look, and the baby's direct gaze gives the picture its only jolt of life. The painting is plain, not subtle, but it has a sincerity that more polished portraits of the time often lack. A pleasant, modest document of early nineteenth-century family life."},"nga:50303":{"d":"A man stands with his body turned slightly to the right, in a portrait on wood about 76 by 62 centimeters, dated 1833. He has light blue eyes and short brown hair parted at the side, and wears a navy blue coat, a white shirt with a high collar, a black neckcloth and a gold waistcoat; in his right hand he holds a red and gold cylindrical object. Behind him a red curtain is partly drawn back to show the sea and a sailing ship in the distance.","i":"The title gives his calling and not his name: he was a ship's captain of Connecticut, a state whose ports sent out whalers, sealers and merchantmen. The sea view behind him and the cylindrical object in his hand, which may be a spyglass, state his profession as plainly as a label. Captains were prosperous, respected men in coastal towns, and a portrait with its companion of his wife recorded that standing for the household.","c":"Sheffield's sea captain is provincial portraiture doing what it does best: stating who the man is, what he does and how well he has done, in clear, flat shapes. The navy coat and gold waistcoat make a bold pattern against the curtain, and the ship in the window ties the figure to his trade. The drawing is stiff and the modeling thin, but the picture is direct, handsome and not easily forgotten."},"nga:50304":{"d":"A woman sits in a chair in a half-length portrait on wood about 76 by 63 centimeters, dated 1833, facing slightly to her right with her gaze fixed on the viewer. She has fair skin, blue eyes and a solemn expression, with dark brown hair in an elaborate updo with large, smooth rolls at either side. She wears a black dress with voluminous sleeves and a high white ruffled collar, a brooch, gold earrings and a ring with a green stone, and holds a book. A dark red curtain is pulled back to show a countryside landscape through a window.","i":"In seafaring families the wife often ran the household while her husband was away, and her black dress, book and brooch describe a woman of piety, literacy and some means. The enormous sleeves and elaborate hair rolls are the height of fashion in 1833. The country landscape in her window answers the sea in her husband's portrait, a polite pairing of land and water that made the couple's pictures a matched set.","c":"She is the more elaborate of the pair: the sleeves and hair rolls make a great deal of shape, and the green stone, brooch and book add small, bright points. The face is plain and a little severe, which fits the portrait's seriousness. Sheffield's handling remains flat and linear, but the matched pair has a coherence that makes it more than the sum of its parts."},"nga:43445":{"d":"An elderly man is shown from the waist up, facing slightly left with a neutral expression, in a canvas of about 66 by 55 centimeters, dated 1848. He has dark hair with a receding hairline, prominent eyebrows and a pointed nose, and wears a dark coat with a white collar over a red garment. The background is a muted brown. One hand is partly visible in his lap near a small slip of paper with writing on it.","i":"The slip of paper in the sitter's lap is a puzzle: it might be a letter, a bill, a deed or a note carrying the sitter's name or a verse, as often appears in folk portraits. A companion portrait of an old lady, of the same date and size, suggests a couple painted late in life, a record of age and survival in a century when many did not reach it.","c":"The painter simplifies the face to a few strong features, the receding hair, the heavy brows and the pointed nose, and makes a strong portrait of age without sentiment. The red under the dark coat is the only warm note. J. C. Robinson is an obscure name, and the picture has the flatness of provincial work, but the unflinching plainness of the likeness is its strength."},"nga:43446":{"d":"An elderly woman sits holding a closed book, her hands folded, in a canvas of about 66 by 55 centimeters, dated 1848. She has light skin, small blue eyes and a slight smile; her hair is dark and smooth, with round spectacles pushed up on top of her head. She wears a dark garment with a brown shawl patterned with red dots, and the background is a muted brown that sets her off.","i":"Spectacles pushed up on the head and a closed book in the lap say that she is a reader who has just paused to look at the viewer. A book in the hands of an old woman was usually a Bible, a sign of piety, and the slight smile gives a note of warmth that is unusual in portraits of the period, which tend toward gravity. The picture is the companion of the Portrait of an Old Man of the same date.","c":"The slight smile is the picture's great asset, and the little touch of the spectacles gives her the air of a person interrupted at her reading. The brown shawl with red dots enlivens the dark dress. The drawing is simple and the modeling thin, as in much folk portraiture, but the picture's humane warmth makes it more affecting than many more skillful ones."},"nga:42429":{"d":"People gather in front of log cabins on a rural day of work and play, in a wide painting of about 80 by 128 centimeters made on bed ticking, the striped cotton cloth used for mattresses. At left a man feeds long strands of fiber into a machine of horizontal rods, while across the picture a line of men and women hold long, flat objects and bundles of flax; some talk and some seem to dance. Women wear colorful dresses with aprons and bonnets, men jackets, vests and top hats. A small fire sends gray smoke up at far left.","i":"A bee was a gathering of neighbors to share a heavy job, and a flax scutching bee, at which the woody stalks of dried flax were beaten to free the linen fibers, ended with food, drink, music and dancing. Linton Park painted it in 1885, probably from memory of a way of life that had faded as factory-made cloth replaced homegrown linen. The picture is both a work scene and a social one.","c":"Park paints like a man recalling a good day: the figures are small and stiff, the cabins and hills are tidy, and the whole is arranged in a long frieze that lets the eye move down the line of people. There is no perspective to speak of and little psychology, but the abundance of incident, the work, the talk, the dancing and the fire, gives the picture the lively authority of memory."},"nga:42430":{"d":"The title names the subject, an exhumation, the digging up of a body from its grave, in an oil on canvas of about 61 by 84 centimeters painted about 1890. The museum's single keyword is exhumation. Linton Park, a folk painter in his sixties by this date, is best known for pictures of rural social life, such as the Flax Scutching Bee, and a grave scene is a far darker subject, one that suggests a story the viewer was expected to know.","i":"The title points to a story, and the likeliest sources are local events or tales: a body dug up to be moved, to be identified or to settle a legal matter, or a body-snatching of the kind that supplied medical schools in the nineteenth century. Folk painters often recorded sensational or memorable local events. Which incident is meant is not recorded, and it is best left open.","c":"A grave scene by a folk painter is bound to be strange and affecting: the flat, plain style removes sensationalism and leaves a matter-of-fact record of the event. Park's strength is the memory of a community's habits, and this subject shows his willingness to record the grim along with the festive. It lacks the buoyancy of his Flax Scutching Bee, but it has a sober, deadpan power."},"nga:56744":{"d":"A woman sits facing slightly to her right but looks straight at the viewer, in a half-length portrait of about 80 by 65 centimeters. She has a solemn expression, prominent cheekbones, light skin and dark eyes, with brown hair drawn tightly on top of her head under a delicate lace headpiece. She wears a formal black gown with voluminous sleeves, a sheer lace shawl, a long fine gold chain and a gold earring, and holds a white handkerchief. The ground is a vivid green with gold lettering giving her name, her birth date, 19 February 1811, and the date of painting.","i":"The gold lettering records that Eliza R. Read was born in February 1811 and painted in October 1833, so she was twenty-two. The long chain, lace and earring show a young woman dressed in her best, while the black gown with big sleeves was the fashionable choice of the early 1830s, when sleeves were at their widest. As the companion of John G. Read's portrait, it perhaps records a young couple at the start of married life.","c":"The portrait is more refined than its companion: the lace shawl and headpiece give the painter chances for delicate pattern, and the sitter's dark eyes and high cheekbones make a firm and rather haunting face. The bright green ground is bold, almost startling behind the black gown, and the gold letters add a decorative note. The drawing is plain, but the effect is crisp and memorable."},"nga:56745":{"d":"A man sits for his portrait in a formal black coat over a white shirt with intricate ruffles, shown half-length and facing forward with a slight tilt of the head. He has fair skin, light brown hair, sharp eyebrows, deep-set eyes and a composed expression, and holds a red book in his right hand. A red and gold textile hangs over the arm of his chair against a solid green background, and lettering at left gives his name, his birth date, 1 November 1799, and the month of painting, October 1833. The canvas is about 80 by 65 centimeters.","i":"Portraits that record the sitter's birth date and the month of painting, as this one does, are a gift to historians, and few folk portraits are so exact. John Read was thirty-three when he sat, and the companion portrait of Eliza R. Read, painted the same month, shows a woman of twenty-two, probably his wife. The red book, the rich textile and the formal black suit give him the look of a man of standing in his community.","c":"Royall Brewster Smith paints on a flat, simplified scale, and the interest here is the lettering, the bold green ground and the careful black and white of coat and shirt. The face is serious and clear, and the ruffles are described with evident pleasure. The picture lacks depth, but it has a clean design and the honesty of a record, which is the folk portraitist's chief virtue."},"nga:164955":{"d":"Three horses with riders move to the right along a beach, seen from the side, in a wide painting of about 61 by 121 centimeters, dated 1892. The brown horses wear sheets of white and blue over their bodies, necks and heads, with openings for eyes and mouth, and blue wraps on their legs. The riders, painted in browns, greens and blues, show no faces and seem to wear hats. The horizon runs halfway up, where a pale sky with a faint sun meets light water and small waves, in soft earth tones with touches of blue and white.","i":"Dieppe, on the Normandy coast, was a fashionable seaside resort, and horses were probably exercised on its beach, where sand and sea water were thought to strengthen their legs; the hooded horses and covered legs in this picture belong to that routine of training. René Princeteau, who was deaf from childhood, specialized in horses and the hunt, and he was the first teacher of the young Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec, who learned from him to draw the animal in motion.","c":"A parade of hooded horses on a pale beach is an unusual sporting subject, and Princeteau treats it with a quiet, frieze-like restraint: three horses in profile, the long horizontal, the soft light. The anonymity of the riders keeps attention on the animals, and the muted palette is delicate. It is less lively than his hunting scenes, but it has the calm, accurate observation that Lautrec learned from him."},"met:634108":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas divides into two zones. In the lower one, a young shepherd kneels on a rocky Alpine slope patched with green, one hand pressed over his face and the other gripping a long staff. He wears a pale shirt and rolled trousers, and his bare feet are tucked beneath him. In the upper zone, set against a pale band of sea or sky, a row of slim nude women stands in a line, arms raised, auburn hair streaming. They are drawn in outline and tinted with thin colour, while the shepherd is more solidly modelled. The signature is at the lower left. The oil paint is thin, and the rocks are blocked in with broad strokes.","i":"The Met describes the lower part as the earthly world and the upper part as a vision, the women's pale, weightless forms marking them as apparitions. It suggests they may stand for enlightenment, harmony with nature and erotic desire. Their frieze-like arrangement and rhythmic gestures recall Puvis de Chavannes, whom Hodler greatly admired. First shown in Geneva in 1896, the painting helped make the Swiss artist notorious for his treatment of sexuality, death and the unconscious. Hodler would soon call his method of repeating forms in rhythm parallelism, and the idea is already at work in the line of women, set against the hard rock beneath them.","c":"The two-part design is clear and bold, and the thin, pale upper register does convey a vision floating above heavy ground. The shepherd is the stronger figure: his gesture is ambiguous, more weariness than rapture, which complicates the dream. The women are less successful. They repeat one pose with little variation, so the frieze drifts toward decoration, and the Alpine rocks, hard and mineral in colour, carry more conviction than the symbolism above them. It is an ambitious picture whose method is clearer than its meaning."},"nga:50820":{"d":"Two men in military uniform sit on a log, one of them smoking a pipe, in an oil on canvas of about 81 by 95 centimeters, dated 1848. At right a log cabin stands, with two men leaning on a fence and another tending a horse. The horizon is low, with trees against a sky that grades from blue to gold, and the late-afternoon light warms a palette of greens, browns and earth tones with touches of red and blue in the uniforms.","i":"The subject is an American Revolutionary legend: Francis Marion, the South Carolina guerrilla leader called the Swamp Fox, supposedly served a visiting British officer a meal of roasted sweet potatoes, and the officer, amazed that men would fight on such fare, left the British service. The story was popularized by Parson Weems in 1809 and is probably embroidered, but it made a durable image of republican simplicity and endurance. George Washington Mark painted it in 1848, long after the events.","c":"The picture's virtue is its simplicity: two soldiers, a log, a pipe and a cabin say everything the legend wants, that great causes were fought by plain men on plain food. Mark's execution is naive, the figures stiff and the space shallow, but the late light gives the scene a warm calm that suits its theme. As history painting it is modest; as an image of how Americans wished to remember the Revolution, it is revealing."},"met:15009":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a lady in watercolor on ivory, only about 6 by 6 centimeters, formerly attributed to David Boudon (1748 to about 1816); the attribution is no longer certain. The sitter is an unnamed lady, and the date can be given only as the period of the artist to whom it was once credited. It is small enough to be held in the palm or set in a frame.","i":"Portrait miniatures like this were exchanged as tokens of love, friendship and family loyalty in the decades around 1800, and ivory was the standard support, with water-based color applied in tiny strokes. The lady is unnamed, and with the attribution withdrawn the picture is best treated as an example of the European miniature of its time rather than as a work by a particular hand.","c":"A formerly attributed work invites caution: its quality is what counts. Miniatures of this period aim at a smooth, luminous complexion and a clear likeness, and the best of them achieve an intimacy few larger portraits match. Whether this one does is hard to judge, and its value lies in the type and its small survival."},"met:11106":{"d":"A man named Henry L. Taylor is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.7 by 5.5 centimeters, dated 1833. It is a bust-length portrait built in fine strokes of transparent color over the white of the ivory, which lends the flesh its glow. Painted by George Harrison Hite, it was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1966 by Mrs. William T. Harrison, and it shows the standard small format of the miniature in the years of its greatest popularity.","i":"In the 1830s a miniature was within reach of the prosperous middle class and not only of the rich, and young men in particular sat for them as gifts for fiancees, parents or friends. George Harrison Hite was among the many American miniaturists who made a living from that demand, and his career, which reached up to portraits of statesmen such as Daniel Webster, shows how far the trade could take a skilled painter.","c":"A dated and named miniature by Hite is a useful fixed point, and the standard to ask of it is clear drawing of the head, smooth and economical handling of flesh, and a plain ground. Hite belongs to the competent professional middle of the American trade: reliable rather than inspired. The pleasure of such a portrait is its modest presence, a young man's face held in the palm."},"met:15083":{"d":"Daniel Webster, the great orator and statesman, is the subject of this large watercolor on ivory, about 11.5 by 9 centimeters, dated 1844. The size is well above the usual few centimeters of a miniature, which gives the painter room for the sitter's famously massive head and deep-set eyes and suggests a commission for display and not for the pocket. It was painted by George Harrison Hite and given to the Metropolitan Museum in 2006 by Gloria Manney.","i":"Webster (1782 to 1852), a senator from Massachusetts and twice secretary of state, was the leading voice of the Union cause in his day and a celebrity whose likeness was sought everywhere. In 1844 he was in private life between offices, active in Whig politics, and admirers commissioned portraits as relics of a great speaker. A miniature of this size could be kept in a case on a desk or table, a small icon of a public figure.","c":"Webster's head was one of the great visual properties of American politics, and any portraitist had the advantage of a face that carried authority. Hite's challenge is to keep the majesty without inflating it in a medium that favors delicacy. A miniature of this size gives a painter room to give the face gravity, and the best that can be hoped is a record of rank that also catches something of the man's weight."},"nga:50251":{"d":"A young woman sits upright in a small oil on canvas of about 33 by 25 centimeters, dated 1849. She has fair skin and dark hair in a bun, wears a dark striped dress with a lace collar and a pink bow, and holds a wooden artist's palette loaded with paint and a brush; her expression is subtle and focused. The palette identifies her as a painter, and one keyword on record is 'self', which suggests that she may be portraying herself.","i":"The palette in her hand is the point of the picture: a woman presenting herself as a painter in a period when women were largely shut out of the official art academies of Europe, and when many made careers as portraitists and miniaturists in private. The artist is recorded as L. de Vuillemin, and little else is known; the portrait itself is the main evidence of an artistic life.","c":"A small portrait of a painter holding her tools is a modest, direct genre, and its success depends on the face and the hands. The subtle, focused expression suggests a quiet seriousness, and the pink bow brightens the dark stripes. Little is known of the painter, so the picture has to be judged on its own: a sincere, competent performance, valued above all as a record of a woman at her craft."},"nga:42404":{"d":"A woman faces the viewer with a neutral expression, her dark hair curled, her eyebrows high-arched, her eyes large and her chin pointed. She wears a long-sleeved dress with a high lace collar, a decorative bow with a floral pattern, simple pearl earrings and a small bead necklace. Behind her are a green curtain and a pastel landscape with soft clouds. The canvas is about 61 by 46 centimeters and is dated 1801.","i":"In the early American republic, portraits like this were made by local and itinerant painters for families in towns and villages. The curtain and landscape background were conventions borrowed from English and colonial portrait practice, signaling gentility and a taste for cultivated scenes. The sitter is also recorded as Lucia Leonard Burbank, presumably her married name, so the picture may show her before marriage, as was common. The pearls and lace suggest modest means.","c":"A naive, charming portrait in which the drawing, the pattern of the lace and curtain and a slightly stylized face give the sitter a quiet presence. Allen's skill is more for design than for volume: curtain, landscape and lace are arranged like pieces in a decorative scheme. It lacks the polish of professionals such as Gilbert Stuart, but the directness has appeal, and the picture shows how ordinary families in 1801 saw themselves."},"met:10812":{"d":"A girl is shown in a portrait of about 53 by 43 centimeters, painted in 1869 by Thomas Eakins. The sitter is named in the title as Carmelita Requena, and Eakins painted her in Spain, probably in Seville, during the trip he made at the end of his studies in Paris. The picture belongs to the group of works that show him absorbing the dark, strongly lit manner of the Spanish masters he had gone there to see, above all Ribera and Velázquez.","i":"Eakins spent 1866 to 1870 in Europe, mainly in Paris, where he studied under Jean-Léon Gérôme and the sculptor Augustin Dumont, and he ended his stay with a few months in Spain. There he admired Ribera and Velázquez, whose frank, solid treatment of ordinary faces suited his own temperament. The portrait of a young Spanish girl is an early sign of what he would pursue for the rest of his career: the plain truth of a person's presence, without prettiness.","c":"Even as a student Eakins refuses to flatter: the Spanish pictures, with their strong light and dark grounds, give heads solid, unprettified modeling, and a young girl is allowed to be a person and not a type. The picture lacks the subtlety of his mature portraits, but its seriousness is already unmistakable, and the directness is as fresh as anything he painted in the years that followed."},"met:10819":{"d":"On a still river in low autumn light, a single oarsman in a yellow racing shell glides toward the viewer, a white-shirted man whose oars trail out to either side; his boat and body are reflected in smooth water. Behind him a pair of rowers in red pass under a high iron railway bridge with stone arches, and farther to the right another single sculler appears in the middle distance. Golden-brown trees line the left bank, and a low wooded shore on the right closes the scene. A pale blue sky is streaked with thin white cloud. The canvas is wide, nearly a metre and a quarter, and the ripples, reflections and long shadows are drawn with great exactness.","i":"The sculler is Max Schmitt, a friend of Eakins from school in Philadelphia, who won the single-sculls championship on the Schuylkill River in October 1870. The setting is the river near the railway bridge where the course turned. Eakins painted himself in the middle distance, in another scull, and signed his name on its stern; this was the first of nearly thirty rowing works he made by 1874. He had just returned from Paris, where he studied under Jean-Léon Gérôme and Léon Bonnat, and the exact perspective and measured light show that training. At the Union League exhibition of 1871 critics found it peculiar. The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 1934.","c":"A cool, impersonal portrait, and convincing for that reason. The picture keeps its stillness through exact observation: the bridge reduced by distance, the long reflections, the low light on the water. The sculler's face is nearly anonymous, and Eakins seems more interested in the boat, the water and the light than in the man, so the clear air and glassy surface can look like a diagram. Even so, it brought a new American subject, athletic leisure, and a rigorous, unromantic way of painting it, which set Eakins apart from the popular landscape painters of his time."},"nga:42848":{"d":"Two men in white sleeveless shirts and royal-blue caps row in unison in a long, narrow, honey-coloured boat on a placid blue river, the boat running off both sides of the canvas. They face left as they row to the right, their bare arms extended straight as the oars sweep back; the man in front looks beyond the stern, the other tucks his chin to look past his shoulder. The tip of another boat runs along the bottom edge. Across the water lies a dense pine forest, with crowds on a steamboat and a paddleboat at the left and a four-man shell in ruby-red shirts at the back.","i":"The brothers are John and Barney Biglin, professional oarsmen from Philadelphia who were champions on the Schuylkill River; the title records them in a race. Eakins, himself a keen rower, made a series of rowing pictures in the early 1870s, drawing perspective diagrams to plot the boats' positions with scientific accuracy. Rowing was among the first modern spectator sports in America, and Eakins celebrates athletic labour without sentiment.","c":"A landmark of American realism, and still persuasive. The two oarsmen's coordinated, solid bodies and the long horizontal of the boat give the picture a stable, almost sculptural rhythm, the muted blue and green of river and trees are lovely, and the crowd and distant shell give the race its public setting. Eakins is more interested in their effort than in their faces, and the cool method makes athletes modern heroes without fuss."},"cma:151904":{"d":"Two oarsmen in a racing shell glide across a calm, silvery river, seen in profile and filling the lower part of the canvas. They wear blue caps and grey sleeveless jerseys, and their oars sweep out in long diagonals as they lean into the stroke. At the right a tall pole flying a dark blue flag marks the turn. Behind them a second boat follows, and a third shows in the far distance at left. Trees fill the bank, spectators are tiny figures along the shore, and a steamboat with a puff of smoke waits at the right. The sky is hazy cream. The oil on canvas is cool in colour and precise in drawing.","i":"The museum says the painting celebrates athletic teamwork while recording a real event, a famous rowing race on the Schuylkill River in Philadelphia in May 1872. Barney and John Biglin, brothers who were professional oarsmen, round the stake that marks the halfway point while their rivals lag behind and crowds line the shore. The Biglins won and became the most celebrated oarsmen of their era. Rowing was among the most popular spectator sports in the United States in the 1870s. Eakins, trained in the United States and in France and active almost entirely in his hometown of Philadelphia, prepared his compositions with painstaking figure and perspective drawings.","c":"The picture is admirable for its refusal of fuss. There are no splashes, no heroic faces: effort is shown by the exact set of the shoulders and the tilt of the oars, and the pale water and hazy light give the scene a fixed, suspended calm. Many viewers find this honesty more moving than drama would be. The limits are a certain coolness and the flatness of the supporting scenery: the crowds and trees are summarised, and the brothers' faces are barely visible. It is a thoughtful picture of sport rather than an exciting one."},"met:10824":{"d":"A hunting scene on a long, narrow canvas, about 33 by 76 centimeters, painted in 1874. The museum's keywords record a man, a gun and a bird, and the title names the sport: rail shooting, in which a boatman poles a flat-bottomed skiff through the reeds of a tidal marsh while a gunner in the bow shoots the small marsh birds called rails as they flush. Eakins grew up in Philadelphia and painted the Delaware River marshes where the sport was popular.","i":"Rail shooting was a seasonal sport in the Philadelphia area, done at high tide in early autumn, when the marsh grass was flooded and the birds could be driven out by the boat; pushing is the poling that moves it. Eakins, who loved hunting, rowing and sailing, made a small group of such pictures in the 1870s, treating the sporting life of the Delaware as a subject as worthy as any history painting. The Met bought this one in 1916, the year of his death.","c":"Eakins makes a quiet event into a study of effort and attention: the poler and the gunner at their separate tasks, the flat marsh and the long, low format that suits it. There is no drama, only skill, and the light on water and grass is observed with the same scientific care he gave to rowers and swimmers. It is a small picture, but it shows his lifelong conviction that American life, not mythology, was the proper subject."},"met:10813":{"d":"In the dim light of a Victorian parlor, two men sit at a small chess table, absorbed in the game, while a third stands behind them watching. The player on the left is elderly, with white hair and beard, bent over the board; the one on the right rests his head on his hand. The standing man, in a dark coat with a gray beard, is the artist's father. Chess pieces stand scattered across the board. A small round table at the left holds a carafe and glasses, a framed picture and a clock hang on the dark wood-paneled wall, and a cat curls on the red patterned carpet at the lower right. The picture is small, about 30 by 43 centimeters, painted on a wood panel in warm browns and reds.","i":"Eakins painted this panel in 1876 for his father, Benjamin, who stands behind the players. They are Bertrand Gardel, a French teacher, and George Holmes, a painter; one art historian reads the picture as a tribute to the artist's father figures, since Holmes may have been his first art teacher, and a print after Gérôme, his master in Paris, hangs over the clock. Eakins signed it in Latin on the table drawer, in effect 'Benjamin Eakins's son painted this.' He gave it to the Met in 1881.","c":"A small picture of great concentration. Eakins renders the lamplit room, the faces, and the half-played game with a scientist's patience, and he understands that a chess game is a drama of attention: nothing happens, yet the whole picture hums. The dark palette limits its appeal, and the room is so crowded with objects that the faces are hard to read. But the three heads are drawn with an unsentimental truthfulness that few American painters of the 1870s approached."},"nga:61251":{"d":"On the brick pavement of a sunny veranda, a toddler leans heavily on one arm as she reaches for a wooden block. Much of her face is in deep shadow from the bright light overhead, though her cheeks and the tip of her nose catch the light. Her leg stretches out in a stocking striped red and white, and her white smock is richly embroidered. Before her lie alphabet blocks, building blocks and a ball of red yarn; a doll in a black dress lies face-down by a plant, a red wagon drawn by a white toy horse stands at the left, and a deep green hedge closes the background.","i":"Eakins, best known for rowers and surgeons, painted a child at play without sentiment: the infant is absorbed, her face half hidden in shadow, and the toys are scattered as a child would leave them. The alphabet blocks hint at learning and growth, themes that Victorian viewers would have found natural to childhood. The signature, written as if scratched on two bricks of the pavement, is a witty touch that places the painter in the scene.","c":"A rare child's picture that avoids sweetness. The toddler's heavy, concentrated lean, the hard light and the scattered playthings are observed with the same clarity Eakins gave to rowers and surgeons, and the brick pavement gives the whole a satisfying structure. The shadowed face risks seeming clumsy, and the wide canvas leaves a good deal of empty brick. Still, its candour makes it fresher than most Victorian images of childhood."},"nga:66413":{"d":"A narrow vertical canvas, about 53 by 23 centimetres, holds a single study of a boy, made in preparation for Eakins's watercolour The Dancing Lesson of 1878. The museum record gives no description of the figure, so his pose and clothing cannot be reported with confidence. A study of this kind records one figure in isolation so that its weight, balance and light can be worked out before the whole composition is assembled.","i":"Eakins worked methodically, making oil sketches, perspective drawings and photographs before committing to a composition, and this study belongs to that process. The Dancing Lesson shows a boy dancing to a banjo player, a scene of music and play among Black children. His interest in the movement of the body, which led him on to photography and sculpture, is evident in studies like this. The sitter's name is not given.","c":"A study should be judged as a study: a notation of a figure's weight and rhythm, not a finished picture. As such it records Eakins's habit of building a scene from separate figures, each drawn with his concern for structure and balance. The very narrow format and the absence of a setting keep it from standing alone, and its interest lies chiefly in showing how his careful method led to the lively watercolour."},"nga:66414":{"d":"A young, gangly boy with brown skin sits on a green chair, leaning over the banjo he plays, in a loosely painted vertical study. His body faces right in profile and his head leans away from the viewer, the light from the left catching his ear and chin while his profile falls into shadow against the lighter wall. He has short black hair and wears a white shirt with rolled sleeves, black trousers and shoes. The floor is streaked with brown, taupe and moss green. In the top left corner the outline of a standing figure is sketched, and a grid is drawn over the boy and chair.","i":"The grid across the figure is the key: Eakins used squared grids to transfer studies to a larger composition, here the watercolour The Dancing Lesson of 1878, in which a boy dances to the banjo while others watch. He prepared his pictures with an almost scientific method, studying each figure alone, and the sketched standing figure in the corner shows him already thinking about the arrangement. Banjo music had its roots in African American culture.","c":"A fine, quick study in which Eakins's strengths show: the profile is caught with an economical shadow, the lean over the instrument gives a convincing sense of concentration, and the loose strokes of floor and wall have a surprising freedom for a painter known as exact. The squared grid and the sketched figure remind the viewer that this is a working surface, and its quiet sympathy for the boy lifts it above a technical exercise."},"nga:166446":{"d":"A woman with dark hair in a bun, in a long, muted lilac silk dress, stands singing before a woman at a piano and a man playing a cello in a dimly lit room. Light falls from the left onto the singer, who stands almost in profile, looking up into the distance, her lips parted and a sheet of music held at her waist. Her dress is edged with lace and trails a train. The musicians sit in shadow behind her, the cellist white-bearded and bespectacled. She stands on a brick-red rug; the wall behind is papered with sunflowers on gold and caramel brown.","i":"Eakins loved music, and painted domestic music-making repeatedly; this scene of a home recital catches the moment of a song. The title uses pathetic in its older sense of moving and full of pathos, pointing to a sentimental ballad. The sunflower wallpaper recalls the Aesthetic Movement's taste of the 1870s and 1880s, in which the sunflower was a fashionable emblem. Eakins gives the singer concentration rather than glamour, placing her in light while the accompanists remain in shadow.","c":"A quiet, serious picture, and a better one than its title suggests. The light on the singer's brow, the lace and lilac silk, the darkness around the musicians and the gold of the wallpaper make a concentrated, intimate drama of sound. Eakins is interested in effort and attention rather than prettiness, so the singer is neither idealised nor theatrical. The musicians are subordinate to the point of vagueness, but it remains one of his most affecting interiors."},"met:10828":{"d":"An aged man at work: the painting is known as a portrait of Benjamin Eakins, the artist's father, a professional writing master, engaged in his craft of penmanship, in an oil on canvas of about 76 by 87 centimeters, dated 1882. The museum's keywords record writing, a man and a portrait. A calligrapher's skill, a steady hand and an educated eye, is the subject as much as the sitter's face, and the identity of the sitter makes the family bond explicit.","i":"Benjamin Eakins taught penmanship and made ornamental documents, an old profession in an age of printed forms, and the painter honors the father who prized exact line. A son's portrait of his father at work is also a statement about the dignity of craft, an idea Eakins pursued in portraits of surgeons, scientists, musicians and other men and women intent on their work, and the Met bought it in 1917, the year after the painter's death.","c":"Eakins refuses to flatter his father: he paints an old man's face, absorbed in his task, with the same exactness he gave to anatomy and perspective. The result is both loving and unsentimental, and the concentration of the sitter becomes the picture's subject. It is one of the finest of the portraits in which Eakins observes a person doing what he does best, and its honesty has aged better than the polish of his fashionable rivals."},"met:10810":{"d":"On a sunlit grassy slope beneath leafy trees, three nude figures are at ease. At the right a slender figure stands in profile on bare feet, playing a double pipe. At the left a second figure lies stretched out on a white cloth on the grass, seen from behind, and close beside, a small figure lies propped on an elbow with a basket. A willow with thin bare branches stands at the left and denser trees fill the right, with glimpses of pale sky between. The grass is a rich yellow-green, the shadows are cool, and the paint is thin and soft. The canvas is nearly a metre square.","i":"In the early 1880s Eakins began photographing nude models outdoors, using friends and students, and used the photographs as studies for paintings. The models for Arcadia are recorded as Susan Macdowell, his former student and soon-to-be wife, his teaching assistant J. Laurie Wallace, and his young nephew Ben Crowell; Eakins even had himself photographed as the piper. The same method produced The Swimming Hole of 1884–85. Arcadia, the classical land of shepherds and music, was a standard pastoral subject, but Eakins sets it in the American countryside with ordinary bodies. His use of nude models at the Pennsylvania Academy later led to controversy and the end of his teaching there in 1886.","c":"A gentle experiment rather than a major statement. The pastoral subject, a pipe-player watched by companions, is as old as classical poetry, yet Eakins ignores the ideal bodies of Greek sculpture and paints sturdy, slightly awkward people. That frankness gives the picture a quiet freshness. But the figures sit on the grass like separate studies, the landscape is vague, and the light is dull, so the group never quite coheres. It lacks the unity of The Swimming Hole, where the same idea is carried through with more conviction."},"met:10811":{"d":"Susan Macdowell Eakins, the artist's wife, is shown with the family's setter dog, in a vertical oil on canvas of about 76 by 58 centimeters painted between about 1884 and 1889. The museum's keywords record a desk and a painting among the furnishings, so the setting is the interior of the Eakins household and the sitter is placed among the signs of the family's art. Dog and woman share the picture, and the dog's presence gives it a note of warmth that formal portraits lack.","i":"Susan Macdowell (1851 to 1938) studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts under Eakins and married him in 1884; she was a gifted painter herself, though her career was overshadowed by his, and after his death in 1916 she worked to preserve his reputation. Eakins painted her more than once, and a portrait of a wife with a pet has the privacy of a family picture and not a commission.","c":"Eakins's portraits of Susan are among his most intimate, painted without gloss and with the attention to character that marks all his work. The pairing with the setter makes a quiet, honest domestic picture. His critics find his late-1880s style heavy and dark; his admirers find a depth of feeling in it that more polished painters never reach. This portrait is a good place to see why."},"met:10817":{"d":"A small oil sketch, about 37 by 26 centimeters, shows James A. MacAlister, painted about 1895 by Thomas Eakins. The title says that it is a sketch, which suggests a preliminary study made from life, perhaps for a larger portrait, quickly and with the painter's attention fixed on the head and the character of the pose. MacAlister was a Philadelphia educator, and the sketch is a record of a short sitting and of Eakins's working method.","i":"James MacAlister (1840 to 1913) was a Philadelphia educator, superintendent of the city's schools and the first president of the Drexel Institute, which opened in 1891; Eakins painted portraits of many such local worthies, men whose careers and character he respected. The sketch came to the Met in 1967 by the bequest of Adelaide Milton de Groot, a collector.","c":"An Eakins sketch is more revealing than many finished portraits by others, because the structure of the head, the placement of the eyes and the weight of the light are set down at once. He built his portraits like a sculptor, from the underlying form outward, and a sketch shows that process bare. It is a modest object, but it is a direct view of the way a very serious painter looked at a face."},"nga:43673":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, facing right and gazing into the distance. He has pale skin, a long nose and deep-set eyes, dark wavy hair, a pointed beard and a moustache that hides his mouth. He wears a dark green jacket with notched lapels, a white shirt and a dark bow tie. The background is uniformly painted in a muted, warm brown. At about 61 by 51 centimetres, the portrait is modest in size and dated 1899.","i":"Eakins painted many portraits of friends and acquaintances in Philadelphia, and his candour, which refused to flatter, cost him commissions. Louis Husson's portrait comes from his late period. The sitter looks away from the viewer, as if absorbed in thought, a common device in Eakins's portraits to suggest an inner life, and the plain ground leaves nothing to distract from the head.","c":"A plain, honest portrait of a man lost in thought. The warm brown ground and green jacket are a quiet, unusual pairing, and the deep-set eyes and long nose are drawn without flattery. There are no props and no drama, and since the moustache hides the mouth, the expression must be read from the eyes alone. It is characteristic of Eakins's late manner: sober, slow and respectful of the sitter's privacy."},"met:10820":{"d":"An elderly woman sits knitting, in a portrait of about 61 by 51 centimeters painted in 1900. The museum's keywords record knitted fabric, knitting needles, glasses and a cap, so the sitter is shown at her ordinary work, in everyday dress, and not posed for display. The portrait was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1965 by Mary Arthur Bates, presumably a relative, and shows Mary Arthur as an old woman absorbed in a familiar task.","i":"Eakins painted many portraits of ordinary people absorbed in work or thought: a woman knitting, a man thinking, a father writing. He believed that character shows in a person at work and not in a pose, and he treated age with respect and without sweetening. This late portrait of 1900 belongs to the period when he worked mostly for friends and family, after his career at the Pennsylvania Academy had ended in controversy in 1886.","c":"The beauty of a portrait like this is its lack of ornament: an old face, a cap, glasses and the rhythm of the hands at their work, studied with the care Eakins gave to a prominent sitter. It is not a picture that tries to please, and its quiet dignity grows on a viewer who spends time with it. Some will find it plain; those who look will find that the plainness is deliberate, and a form of respect."},"met:10827":{"d":"A man stands full length, lost in thought, on a tall canvas of about 208 by 107 centimeters painted in 1900. The sitter is Louis N. Kenton, the artist's brother-in-law, and the title makes the portrait a study of contemplation: the museum's keywords record a man, contemplation and a portrait. The scale is life-size and the tone sober, with the figure set in a restricted range of dark colors.","i":"Louis N. Kenton was married to a sister of Eakins's wife, Susan, and the portrait is a family picture of a man in deep thought. Eakins, who admired honest self-absorption, made it an image of mind at work, and in 1900 he was a sidelined painter, shunned by the city's establishment but supported by friends and family. The Met bought it in 1917, the year after his death, as it began to recognize him as a major American master.","c":"This is a serious, even austere picture, and it asks the viewer to attend to a man thinking, which is hard to paint and harder to make interesting. Eakins succeeds by monumentality and restraint: the large dark figure, the minimal setting and the sense of weight. Some will find it grim, and its beauty is not easy, but it ranks among his strongest late portraits, an unflinching image of private thought."},"nga:45852":{"d":"A landscape on a horizontal canvas, about 52 by 67 centimetres, in which, according to the title, geese fly over a scene that also includes a house, a boat, trees and a mountain. It was painted in 1850 or later by Leila T. Bauman, an American painter of whom little is recorded, and was given to the National Gallery by Edgar and Bernice Garbisch, collectors of American self-taught painting. Works of this kind usually have simplified forms and a careful, flat clarity.","i":"Mid-nineteenth-century America had a large public for modest landscapes: farm, river and village scenes made by amateurs and local painters for neighbours and family, sometimes adapted from prints. A flock of wild geese overhead marks the change of season and the passing of time, a quiet theme in rural life. The picture is best read as a view of an ordinary, peaceable world rather than a record of a specific place, since nothing in the title identifies one.","c":"Pictures like this should not be measured against academic landscape. Scale and perspective are usually simplified and the forms flat and schematic, but the directness gives them an honesty that polished painters often lack. The flight of geese, if it is as prominent as the title suggests, gives the scene rhythm and a sense of motion across a calm countryside. It is a modest but appealing example of a self-taught painter's vision."},"nga:45853":{"d":"A boat, identified by the title as a U.S. mail boat, appears in a landscape with a house and trees, on a horizontal canvas about 51 by 67 centimetres. It was painted in 1855 or later by Leila T. Bauman, an American painter of whom little is recorded, and matches her Geese in Flight in size and approach. Mail boats of the period were usually steam vessels carrying letters and passengers under government contract, so the picture records both the boat and the setting in which it ran.","i":"In the mid-nineteenth century a boat carrying the United States mail was a source of local pride, a link between small communities and the wider world, and its regular arrival structured the day. Painting such a boat in a landscape of houses and trees celebrates that link. The title gives the boat's function but not its name or route, so the picture is better read as a general tribute than as a portrait of one ship.","c":"Like its companion in size, Geese in Flight, this picture has the charm of a self-taught hand: simplified forms, a clear arrangement and an honest wish to record. Its limits, such as simple perspective and schematic shapes, are typical of the genre and part of its appeal. The subject, a working boat in a peaceful landscape, is pleasantly direct. It deserves attention as a record of everyday American travel more than as a work of formal accomplishment."},"nga:42427":{"d":"A young girl stands beside a window, looking directly at the viewer. She has fair skin, dark eyebrows and dark hair with bangs, and wears a pink short-sleeved dress with lace at the neckline and white ribbons tied at her waist and sleeves. In her hands is a small bouquet. A red curtain hangs at one side of the window, through which a blue sky and green trees can be seen. The tall canvas, about 102 by 72 centimetres, is the work of an itinerant portrait painter known only as the Beardsley Limner.","i":"Portraits of children in late eighteenth-century America were commissioned by parents who wished to record a child's appearance, often in fashionable dress, at a time when many children did not reach adulthood. The bouquet is a conventional attribute of youth, spring and innocence. The window and curtain borrow from European portrait convention and gave a modest household some of the dignity of a grander one. The sitter is not named, so she is read as a type: a cherished daughter.","c":"The appeal lies in directness: the child's level gaze, the bold pink dress against the red curtain, and the simple, clear arrangement. The anatomy is simplified and the space shallow, as is typical of itinerant portraitists who lacked academic training, but these qualities help the figure stand out as a strong shape. It is a vivid, engaging image, valued today for its freshness rather than for any refinement."},"nga:42460":{"d":"A boy is shown full-length, standing against a plain muted background with a green tasselled drape at the right. He has wide eyes and straight hair falling past his shoulders and wears a red coat over a dark waistcoat with rounded buttons, white cuffs and a white collar. In his left hand he holds a hat, and under the same arm he carries a stack of red books; his right arm hangs by his side. The painter is the Beardsley Limner, the name given to an unidentified American portraitist.","i":"Books in a child's portrait signalled education, literacy and the ambitions of his parents, and the hat suggests a boy ready to go out into the world. The sitter is named as Charles Adams Wheeler, but little else is recorded here. Late eighteenth-century American families of means often commissioned such portraits from travelling painters, who adapted the poses and drapes of British portraits to modest budgets and smaller canvases.","c":"The portrait works as a bold pattern: a red coat and red books against a plain ground, with a green drape for contrast. The boy's wide eyes and upright stance give him a serious, earnest presence. As with many itinerant portraits, anatomy and proportion are approximate, but the clarity and strength of the design more than make up for it. It is a lively document of how a family wished its son to be seen."},"nga:59929":{"d":"A man sits from the waist up, his body slightly turned to the left and his head toward the viewer. He has a long face, defined features, a fair complexion and dark hair that frames his face and reaches his shoulders with a straight fringe across the forehead. He wears a red coat with gold buttons over a white high-collared shirt and holds a closed book in his right hand; the background is plain earth tones, with a dark chair partly visible at left. The canvas measures about 57 by 50 centimeters.","i":"The Sherman Limner is the name that scholars gave to an unidentified painter of portraits in the 1780s; limner was the old word for a painter of likenesses, and it remained in use in colonial and early republican America for artists who traveled and worked in an unlearned, flat manner. The red coat, the book and the long dark hair mark a man of the late eighteenth century, of some standing and education.","c":"The charm of this limner's work is in a bold simplicity: the red coat is a block of color, the gold buttons a bright rhythm, and the long face an almost abstract shape. Modeling is thin and the pose stiff, but the design is confident and the likeness vivid. Together with the lady in red, it makes an appealing pair, a fine example of what the unschooled portraitists of the young republic could achieve."},"nga:59930":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up facing the viewer, her upper body slightly turned to the left. She has a serene expression, light-colored eyes and a soft, closed-mouth smile; her hair is arranged with flowers and pearls, and she wears a dark red gown with lace trim and a black ribbon tied in a bow around her neck. In her hands she holds a pink flower. The background is a textured, muted green. The canvas measures about 57 by 49 centimeters.","i":"Flowers and pearls in the hair and a flower in the hand were usual signs of a woman's modesty and charm in portraits of the 1780s, and the black neck ribbon was a fashionable touch. The picture is the companion of the Portrait of a Man in Red, of the same date, size and artist, and the two red costumes make a visual pair. Such matching portraits were commissions for marriages or family records.","c":"The muted green ground sets off the red gown, and the pink flower in her hand is a small, bright counterpoint. The face is gentle and a little blank, as is common in provincial work, but the soft smile gives it charm. The picture is better as design than as likeness, and the pair with her husband's portrait is more than the sum of its parts."},"nga:45856":{"d":"A portrait of Red Jacket (Sagoyewatha), the Seneca orator and chief, painted in oil on paper laid on cardboard, about 64 by 45 centimeters, after 1828. The date, after 1828, suggests that the picture was copied or adapted from one of the portraits made in his last years, such as the likenesses painted that year by Robert W. Weir and others, which circulated widely in prints. The museum's keyword records only a man, and the title supplies the identity.","i":"Red Jacket (about 1750 to 1830) was the leading Seneca speaker of his generation, admired for his eloquence in defending his people's lands and ways against pressure from land speculators and missionaries. He took his English name from a red coat given him by the British during the Revolution. In his last years he became a celebrity, painted by several artists, and a picture made after 1828 belongs to the afterlife of that fame.","c":"Folk copies after famous portraits have their own interest: the original image is simplified, the modeling flattened and the details turned to pattern, and what remains is a reading of the man by someone who admired the picture. It cannot compete with the likenesses made from life by Weir and Charles Bird King, but it shows how widely Red Jacket's face was known, and how the image survived in modest hands."},"nga:43632":{"d":"A large three-masted ship with white sails floats on green waves, flying the American flag and a second flag that appears to read Arkansas, in a canvas of about 58 by 73 centimeters, dated 1847. Tiny figures stand on deck and thin ropes crisscross the masts. A coastline of small colorful buildings and green hills lies beyond. At left are two smaller ships, one flying a flag that says Russia and the nearer, at the bottom corner, with Cuba on its stern. The sky is a bright, cloudless blue.","i":"A ship portrait was the standard commission of a captain or owner, an exact likeness of a vessel under sail in a port of call, with the flags that told her owner and nationality. Havana was one of the busiest ports of the Americas in the 1840s, shipping sugar and tobacco and drawing American traders, and the other flags in the picture, Russian and Cuban, show the cosmopolitan crowd of vessels there.","c":"Ship portraits are judged by accuracy of rig and cleanliness of line, and the painter, A. Hashagen, supplies both: the masts and ropes are drawn with clear care, the sails are white and taut, and the flags make clear who is who. The mood is bright and busy, with little atmosphere beyond a flat blue sky, but the picture is a handsome record of a ship at a moment of departure."},"nga:43441":{"d":"A woman in a flowing white gown drives a chariot lettered EMANCIPATION, pulled by two white horses, raising a gold trumpet, with an eagle overhead. At right Abraham Lincoln on horseback holds out a scroll marked PROCLAMATION beside an officer with a flag inscribed LIBERTY TO ALL, while Union soldiers' horses trample a torn Confederate flag. A whip, neck shackles and a chain lie on the ground before a crowd of enslaved people, some chained, kneeling or praying, and soldiers in gray line up with bayonets. The US Capitol rises behind. The canvas measures about 83 by 137 centimeters.","i":"The picture is an allegory of the Emancipation Proclamation, which Lincoln issued in preliminary form in September 1862 and put into effect on 1 January 1863, declaring free the enslaved people in the states in rebellion. The woman with her chariot and trumpet is a classical figure of Liberty or Fame, announcing the new order, and the discarded whip and shackles and the trampled Confederate flag state the victory plainly. It was painted in 1864 or later.","c":"This is a picture of conviction more than skill, with the stiff horses, the rough anatomy and the flat sky of a folk painter. But its sheer ambition is moving: the artist piles every symbol he can find, the chariot, the eagle, Lincoln, the Capitol, the shackles and the flag, into a single triumphal procession. As a work of art it is naive; as a document of how an ordinary American imagined emancipation, it is remarkable."},"nga:59913":{"d":"A yellow woven basket holds an assortment of fruit, a prominent orange pear, green and red grapes, purple plums, a pink peach and small pink berries, in front of a large green gourd, with green leaves and brown branches rising from it. A green parrot sits on a branch that extends from the left side of the basket; the bird is mostly light green with reddish-orange feathers on its tail and wingtips, and its head is turned to the left, its hooked beak open. The canvas is about 43 by 51 centimeters and is dated 1777.","i":"A parrot with fruit was a favorite combination: the bird, exotic and prized, adds a note of luxury to the abundance of the basket, and the pairing recalls the Dutch and English still lifes that American painters knew from prints and imported pictures. Painted in 1777, in the middle of the Revolutionary War, it is an early American still life, from a time when the genre was just beginning to appear in the colonies as a decorative picture for a household.","c":"The painter arranges the fruit like a child building a pyramid, each piece distinct and bright, with the green gourd as a backdrop and the parrot as a surprise. The drawing is naive and the modeling flat, but the colors, orange, green, purple and pink, are fresh, and the open beak adds a comic note. It is a charming early still life, to be enjoyed for its pleasure in things and for its date."},"nga:43432":{"d":"A rural farmstead is shown in an oil on canvas of about 61 by 82 centimeters, from the late nineteenth century. On the left two horses stand near a gray wooden barn; on the right cows stand and sit in front of a fenced green field. The ground is brown with patches of yellow, green and cream, and in the distance rise a white two-story house with blue shutters and a white picket fence, among trees with hints of green and brown, under a grayish-blue sky with light clouds.","i":"Farm pictures like this one answered a farmer's pride: the house, the barn and the stock, shown in good order, were a record of a life's work and a statement of prosperity, hung in the parlor. Amzi Emmons Zeliff, who painted rural scenes in the later nineteenth century, belongs to the large company of untrained or lightly trained painters who supplied them. The animals, here horses and cows, are as much the subject as the buildings.","c":"The Barnyard is what its title says, and its virtue is plainness: the animals are clearly placed, the barn and house are tidy, and the colors are the honest browns, grays and greens of the farm. The perspective is simple and the sky is a flat wash. There is a gentle pleasure in the way the cows lie and stand, and the picture has the unforced authority of a place that the painter knew."},"nga:59914":{"d":"A painting on the theme of sport, in oil on canvas about 56 by 77 centimeters, dated 1881 or later. The title, The Sportsman's Dream, suggests a scene of hunting or fishing imagined or remembered, and the sports keyword places it among the pictures of field and stream that were popular in the second half of the nineteenth century. The date or after marks the earliest it could have been painted, which for folk pictures sometimes means that a print published that year served as the model.","i":"Hunting and fishing pictures sold to men of all classes in the age of the sporting magazine and the chromolithograph, when outdoor sport was both a pleasure and a sign of manly character. Dream invites the viewer to enter the sportsman's imagination, and such pictures might show a sleeping hunter with visions of game, or an abundance of birds and fish. C. F. Senior, of whom little is recorded, was one of the many amateurs who painted such subjects.","c":"A picture whose subject is a wish has to make the wish believable, and sporting dreams were an easy sell: a bounty of birds, fish or deer, rendered with cheerful detail. Folk versions of such themes are less polished than the chromolithographs they imitate, but they have more personal charm, a handmade enthusiasm. A modest, pleasing example."},"nga:126485":{"d":"A group of children in the woods listens to a young violinist, in a small oil on wood about 25 by 30 centimeters, dated 1870. To the left of center a boy plays the violin at the edge of a gray path, facing the right, where three children sit on a pale log and two on the grass, dotted with small white flowers, all in straw hats. The clothes are mostly tan and brown, with one child in a pink dress and blue jacket. Autumn trees fill the background, and a white cottage stands behind a wooden gate at the far end of the path.","i":"The title is a joke on the grand phrase for a performer's debut: the audience is a handful of village children, and the stage is a woodland path. Victorian painters loved such scenes of childhood, in which small incidents carried the sentiment of innocence and the passing of time, and they were sold as cabinet pictures to a public that found them charming and reassuring. Charles Caleb Ward, a Canadian painter, worked in this vein.","c":"A small panel can sustain a small story if the details are right, and this one has them: the earnest boy, the attentive children on the log, the autumn leaves and the cottage at the end of the path. The color is warm and harmonious, and the figures have the careful, sunlit finish typical of Victorian genre painting. It is sentimental, but it is also charming and well observed, a picture that gives pleasure without pretending to more."},"met:13131":{"d":"A small oil on wood panel, about 26 by 20 centimeters, painted in 1871. The record lists a house, an advertisement, a boy, a girl, a dog and a horse, and the keywords include Boys, Girls, Dogs, Horses and Advertisements. The scene therefore seems to be a village or farmhouse setting in which children and animals meet an advertisement. The museum gives no more detail, so pose, dress and placement are not described.","i":"The title is a proverb, taken from Thomas Campbell's poem Lochiel's Warning: coming events cast their shadows before. Here it probably refers to an event announced on a poster, which draws the children's attention, perhaps a circus or fair about to come to town. The picture belongs to American genre painting of the 1870s, which found humor and sentiment in the lives of rural children.","c":"A small anecdotal picture that depends on a visual pun, and it works best for viewers who enjoy deciphering the joke. Ward belongs to the less-known ranks of American genre painters of the era, and the interest lies more in the story than in any distinction of paint. Competent and agreeable, with the modest ambition of the cabinet picture."},"nga:59895":{"d":"A bowl of red raspberries rests at the centre, surrounded by peaches, pears, red plums and green and purple grapes. At the lower left are carrots and a few red berries; at the right a dwarf pear tree bearing yellow pears and a small vase of red and white roses; and at the left a thin branch on which a green-backed, yellow-breasted bird leans toward the raspberries, wings flared and beak open. The surface is dark grey and the background shades from pale yellow to light blue.","i":"Abundant fruit still lifes were popular in mid-nineteenth-century America, celebrating the nation's orchards and gardens and the comfortable prosperity of the new middle class, and they often decorated dining rooms. The dwarf pear, grafted to stay small, was a fashionable garden fruit in the 1850s, and the bird pecking at the berries adds lively movement. The painter, Charles V. Bond, is little known, and the picture reached the National Gallery through a collection of American self-taught painting.","c":"A cheerful, decorative picture whose crisp colour and bold arrangement are its virtues: the bowl of raspberries, the bright bird and the pear tree fill the canvas with variety. The objects are lit rather flatly and stiffly arranged, and the modelling is simple, but the straightforward naturalism has an honest charm. It is better as a document of mid-century taste than as a refined still life, and its freshness is part of the appeal."},"nga:43711":{"d":"Large white melons and a sliced red watermelon fill the foreground, with clusters of dark and white grapes and green vine leaves behind them, all arranged on a wooden tabletop. The palette is shades of green, brown, white, pink and black. The painter is recorded only by the surname Chipman, and the picture is dated to the middle of the nineteenth century. The museum gives no dimensions in its record.","i":"Fruit still lifes were among the most popular subjects of American self-taught and amateur painters in the nineteenth century: abundant fruit symbolized prosperity and the bounty of the new country, and the subject needed no model or special training. The sliced watermelon, which reveals its red flesh, is a common device that displays a painter's colors. Edgar and Bernice Garbisch, who gave it, collected such American folk painting widely.","c":"Folk still lifes work by boldness and pattern rather than by perspective: the shapes are simple, the color bright, and the arrangement made for display. This picture's pleasures are the contrast of white melons and red watermelon flesh against the dark grapes and the generous, slightly awkward composition. It lacks the refinement of professional still life, but its vivid simplicity is its charm."},"nga:59962":{"d":"A white-and-brown dog stands in tall green grass, staring intently at a group of four small birds, in an oil on canvas of about 46 by 52 centimeters, dated 1854 or later. The dog has large eyes, a pointed snout and long ears; the birds, brown and white with orange eyes and short beaks, stand in an opening in the grass between the dog and the viewer. The sky behind is blue with a few clouds, and the grass is painted in shades of green, yellow and brown.","i":"On point is the sportsman's phrase for the moment when a pointer or setter freezes, nose and body aimed at hidden game, to tell the hunter where it is; the birds are probably quail, the favorite game of American upland shooting. Pictures of the dog's moment of concentration were staples of the sporting print trade, and folk painters such as D. G. Stouter copied or adapted them for homes. The date, 1854 or later, may point to a print as a source.","c":"A dog frozen in attention is a fine subject for a flat, decorative style, and the long muzzle and ears make a bold silhouette against the grass. The birds, drawn with the same attention, are charming. The painting makes up in clarity what it lacks in anatomy: the dog is a dog because of the line of its head and the tension of its pose. A good-natured, lively picture of its type."},"nga:110263":{"d":"A rowing competition draws a large crowd along a riverbank, in a very small oil on wood about 19 by 25 centimeters. A wide river runs across the middle, reflecting the blue of the sky and the green of the trees, and four thin boats cross it, each with five people, some rowing. The people in the boats and along the banks wear bright reds, yellows, blues and whites, the women in long dresses with shawls and bonnets, the men in suits and top hats. A large white building at left is filled with more spectators.","i":"Regattas were a new spectacle of the nineteenth century, when rowing became a fashionable sport and a social occasion, and a day on the river drew crowds in their best clothes. The bright dabs of costume and the light, breezy sky recall the river scenes of the Impressionists, though Émile Lévy was an academic painter known for large subjects such as The Death of Orpheus. This little panel shows a lighter side of his art.","c":"A quick, bright panel like this one is a holiday from the studio: the crowd is treated as a mosaic of color, the boats as thin diagonals, the sky and water as a soft ground. Lévy's academic training shows in the clear drawing of the little figures, and what it lacks, compared with the Impressionists, is the sense of a light that changes. It is a fresh and likable sketch rather than a statement."},"nga:164870":{"d":"A human skull, a large thighbone, books, papers and a snuffed, smoking candle are arranged on a marble tabletop near an open window in a darkened room. The skull faces left in profile near the bottom center, resting on two small leather-bound books and papers with illegible printing, with the thighbone propped behind it. More books, papers and a box are piled to the right, and a brass candlestick on the box has a red-glowing wick and a wisp of smoke. Light enters through a leaded window at left. Oil on paper laid on panel, about 20 by 17 centimeters, about 1650.","i":"A vanitas, from the Latin for emptiness, reminds the viewer that life is brief and that worldly learning, wealth and pleasure end in death. The skull and bone are the plain signs of mortality, the snuffed candle with its last smoke marks a life just ended, and the books stand for knowledge that cannot save. Dutch Protestant culture of the seventeenth century produced many such pictures, which were meant for contemplation more than decoration.","c":"Tiny and severe, this is a compact sermon in paint. The restricted palette of slate, ivory and brown, the single beam of light from the window and the wisp of smoke are used with real economy. Van Daellen is a little-known painter, and nothing here pushes beyond the usual iconography, but the composition is balanced and the lighting persuasive. Small as it is, it holds attention longer than many larger still lifes."},"nga:42509":{"d":"A man's upper body is shown in profile facing left, in a portrait miniature of about 10 by 5 centimeters made with painted wax on glass, about 1810. He has defined features, a prominent nose, sideburns and dark wavy hair, and wears formal dress with a high white collar and gold buttons. The ground is solid black, with a gold oval border around the figure. The sitter is named in the title as William Henry Vining.","i":"Profile portraits in black and gold were a quick, inexpensive alternative to the painted miniature in the early nineteenth century, made for a clientele that wanted a likeness at modest cost; the profile was easy to draw, and the black ground with its gold border gave a rich, jewel-like effect. The technique here, painting in wax on glass, is unusual, and the sitter's name preserves what many such pieces lose.","c":"A profile on black is the simplest of portraits, and its success depends on the line of the nose, brow and chin. The gold oval and the black ground lend it a formal glow, and the wavy hair and high collar give the sitter a dashing air. The technique is more interesting than the art, but it is carried out with neat care, and as a small, jewel-like object it has real charm."},"nga:43634":{"d":"A stately white mansion stands on a green hill at the center of a grassy, hilly landscape, with trees around the edges and a wide river below dotted with small sailing ships. Two couples walk by a white fence to the right of the house, and at lower left another couple strolls arm in arm: the woman in a long pink jacket, white dress and pink hat, the man in a dark blue coat, yellow vest, knee breeches and black hat. The sky is light blue and pink with purple clouds, mirrored in the soft pink water. The canvas is about 94 by 135 centimeters.","i":"Mount Vernon, George Washington's home on the Potomac, was already a place of pilgrimage when this picture was painted in 1806, only seven years after his death. Views of it were widely sold in prints, and young painters like George Ropes, who was about eighteen, probably copied or adapted them. The strolling couples are visitors, turning the estate into a pleasure ground and Washington's house into a national shrine.","c":"The picture is a young painter's version of a famous view, with simplified forms, bright pastel color and a charming naive perspective. The figures in Federal costume give it a touch of humor, and the glowing pink sky and water give it an unexpected beauty. Ropes did not live long, and a work done at eighteen is best valued for its freshness and for the way it records how Americans looked at their founding hero's home."},"nga:59896":{"d":"A large bull stands in profile on a grassy plain, facing right. His head is rich brown and his body and legs are swirled with patches of white and brown; the head looks small against the bulk of the body, and two thin white horns point forward. The background is a low horizon with a few small green trees and shrubs under a pastel pink and purple sky. In the lower right corner a small pool of green water is edged with tufts of grass. The picture is an oil on canvas, about 51 by 63 centimeters, dated 1876.","i":"Portraits of prize livestock were a popular genre in nineteenth-century America and Britain, commissioned by breeders and farmers to record a champion animal and to advertise its bloodline. The side view, which shows the whole animal and its proportions, follows the conventions of such pictures; the small head and heavy body probably reflect the breeders' ideal of the time, which favored massive, quick-fattening cattle. The painter, H. Call, is otherwise unrecorded here and may have been a local artist.","c":"Folk painters of prize animals were less concerned with anatomy than with presence, and this bull has it: the side-on silhouette, the brown and white patches and the small head are treated with a naive solemnity that is both comic and dignified. The anatomy is stylized, the perspective flat, and the pastel sky almost decorative. As a document of agricultural pride and folk taste it is a charming and unaffected piece."},"nga:51089":{"d":"A man is shown in bust length, turned to face right, against a smooth, warm gradient of earthy tones. His face is strongly structured, with high cheekbones, a firm chin and deep-set eyes. Long dark hair falls in braids, with a feather set in it. He wears a patterned garment in red, green and white, with decoration at the shoulders, and a light-colored scarf around his neck. The painting is a small oil on canvas, about 51 by 41 centimeters, an easel portrait made in the last quarter of the nineteenth century.","i":"The title names a category, not a person, which suggests a type rather than the portrait of a particular individual. Late nineteenth-century painters often portrayed Native Americans for non-Native buyers as dignified, idealized figures, at a time when Plains peoples were being confined to reservations and their way of life was widely, and wrongly, expected to vanish. The feather, braids and ornamented garment are probably the emblems of identity the painter chose to emphasize, shaped by the tastes of the market.","c":"The painting is respectful in tone and competently built, with a calm, dignified pose. The pattern of the garment is the liveliest passage; the face is smoothly handled but fairly generic, and the plain gradient ground gives no clue to place or person. It reads as a studio type rather than a record of an individual, which limits its interest as a portrait, though it remains useful evidence of how such figures were imagined and sold in the period."},"nga:45858":{"d":"A large white church burns at the center of the picture, in an oil on canvas about 47 by 62 centimeters. Its two rows of windows, black roof and clock tower stand out as orange and red flames pour from the windows and doorways and gray smoke and sparks fill the right side. Small figures in dark coats and hats crowd the dirt ground and point at the blaze; one holds an American flag. Smaller buildings and trees stand behind, under a cloudy sky lit by a crescent moon in a small circle of pale blue.","i":"Bath, Maine, was a shipbuilding town on the Kennebec River. The fire that destroyed its Old South Church in 1854 is usually connected with the Know-Nothing agitation of that year against Catholics and immigrants, and one record links the picture to immigration policy; if that reading is right, the flag in the crowd is a pointed detail. Fires were also favorite subjects for popular painters, who recorded dramatic local events for their neighbors.","c":"A fire is a gift to a naive painter: the bright flames against the white tower give a strong visual contrast, and the little pointing figures supply the human reaction. The painter, John Hilling, handles flames and smoke with a decorative energy, and the moon in its circle of blue is an odd, appealing touch. The drawing is simple and the perspective flat, but the picture has the immediacy of a news report."},"nga:45857":{"d":"A wintry scene, viewed from above, shows people skating and playing on a frozen river, in an oil on canvas of about 37 by 46 centimeters. Leafless trees line the left bank, and on the right stand brown buildings with snow on their roofs and smoke rising from chimneys; a woman holds a small child on a porch, and two horses pull a sled. On the ice, figures in red, yellow, green and blue slip, fall and lace skates, and four men with long curved sticks look toward a small round ball. Blue-tinged mountains close the distance.","i":"The four men with curved sticks and a ball are playing an early form of stick-and-ball game on ice, kin to bandy and an ancestor of ice hockey, in a period before the sport was organized. Winter skating scenes were popular in Dutch art and were adopted in America, where a frozen river was a public pleasure ground. John Toole's picture, dated about 1835, records a community at leisure on the ice.","c":"The bird's-eye view lets the painter display everything at once: the tumbling skaters, the sled, the porch, the game. The effect is lively, like a winter calendar, and the color, bright accents against snow and gray sky, is attractive. The figures are small and stiff, and the space is organized more by sense than by strict perspective, but the picture's cheerfulness and its record of an early game give it a lasting appeal."},"nga:43629":{"d":"A father and his son, shown to the waist, fill the canvas. The man sits in a green chair, turned slightly toward the viewer, with short, neatly combed light-brown hair and a stern expression; he wears a tan coat with gold buttons over a black waistcoat and a white cravat. The boy stands slightly in front, facing the viewer, with very short light hair and bright eyes, in a dark garment with a white ruffled collar. His hands clasp or touch an object held by the father. Behind them hangs rich red drapery with a glimpse of landscape.","i":"Painted in 1800, at the start of the nineteenth century in the new American republic, the double portrait presents the family as a unit: the father as head and provider, the son as heir. Touching hands signal affection and the passing on of responsibility, while the drapery and glimpse of landscape borrow the vocabulary of European grand portraiture for a more modest American sitter. Local portraitists supplied such images to farmers, merchants and professionals who wanted a lasting record.","c":"A direct and rather endearing work. The stiff poses and simplified modeling of the faces place the painter outside the polished academic mainstream, but the tender gesture of the hands lifts the group above the formulaic, and the green chair and red drapery supply strong, flat patches of color. It lacks the sophistication of Copley or Stuart, but in its way it is more honest, telling the viewer what the sitters wished to be known: they were a family."},"nga:42428":{"d":"A bird's-eye view shows a pastoral community with a large central building complex surrounded by lush greenery and small structures, painted on metal, about 83 by 101 centimeters, in 1895. The horizon is high, with rolling hills and a light-filled sky; the brushwork is detailed, and symmetrical trees, fields and buildings form a tidy pattern in greens, reds, browns and soft blues, with white picket fences and an American flag. Horse-drawn carriages and small figures move along the paths, and vignettes around the border show additional buildings and scenes.","i":"This is the Berks County almshouse in Pennsylvania, the county poorhouse, shown in the same pictorial format that Charles Hofmann used for his view of 1878: a central panorama with smaller views around it, like a commemorative print. Almshouses housed the poor, the old, the sick and the insane, and were run partly as farms; a painting like this presents the institution as an orderly, even attractive community. It was painted seventeen years after the earlier picture, by Louis Mader.","c":"Mader follows Hofmann's formula and gives it a slightly more ordered, symmetrical treatment, with tidy trees and fields and a rigid sense of plan. The result is more diagram than landscape, but a diagram painted with obvious care and affection, and with a bright, clean palette. The flatness suits the subject, an institution that wished to be seen as well managed, and it gives the picture a poignant, matter-of-fact dignity."},"nga:56734":{"d":"A pyramid of fruit is stacked on an intricate pedestal against a dark ground, in an oil on canvas of about 66 by 56 centimeters, from the second half of the nineteenth century. A watermelon slice forms a horizontal base near the top of the pedestal, with a pineapple and citrus stacked above it slightly right of center, among lemons, an orange, grapes, peaches and berries. The colors range from deep greens to vibrant yellows, reds and soft pinks.","i":"Piled fruit on a stand is the classic American still life of the mid-nineteenth century, a decorative picture for a parlor that signified abundance and domestic prosperity, and the pineapple, a traditional symbol of hospitality, often crowned it. Many such pictures were painted by women and amateurs, following a standard formula, and their pleasure is in the sheer variety of fruits and colors, shown with little shadow or depth.","c":"The stack of fruit is built like a tower of toys, bright and slightly flat, against a dark ground that makes the colors glow. The painter makes no effort at realistic space; the pleasure lies in the arrangement and in the sheer variety, from the green skin of the watermelon to the pink of the peaches. A cheerful, decorative piece of folk still life, valued for its color rather than its depth."},"nga:52948":{"d":"A domestic scene in a historical setting fills this canvas of 1883, about 62 by 72 centimeters. A man and a woman dance in the center of a room while an older woman watches with a small dog, and a musician plays a violin near an open doorway. In the background a woman in an apron brings refreshments, a child plays on the floor, and a cat plays with a string. The room has a fireplace at the left and a rifle and a clock on the walls.","i":"After the Centennial of 1876, many Americans looked back fondly on their colonial and early republican past, and painters supplied sentimental scenes of old-time country life. The rifle, the clock and the fireplace are props from this imagined past, and the dance suggests a world of community and simple pleasures. Martin Edgar Ferrill painted it in the 1880s, with no claim to documentary accuracy.","c":"Pictures like this are more affectionate than accurate, and Ferrill's crowded room is a pleasant, busy stage with many small incidents, from the cat to the child. The drawing is naive and the space somewhat cramped, but the humor and warmth of the whole are winning. It tells more about nineteenth-century nostalgia than about eighteenth-century life."},"nga:45863":{"d":"A woman and a child appear in a portrait on wood of about 76 by 62 centimeters, painted about 1833. The woman sits facing the viewer with a serious expression, in a dark, high-necked dress with elaborate detailing, a shawl with ornate embroidery, gold earrings and a brooch, her hair under a white lace cap. On her lap the child, with light hair, wears a white dress with lace details and a red bead necklace and holds a bouquet of flowers in one hand. The background is plain and dark.","i":"The title names mother and son, Aphia Salisbury Rich and Baby Edward, and records a stage of life: infant boys wore white dresses, like their sisters, until they were breeched. The embroidered shawl and lace cap show the mother's means, and the flowers in the child's hand are a conventional sign of childhood's innocence. Milton W. Hopkins was a portrait painter of the 1830s, and family pictures like this were his stock-in-trade.","c":"The mother's black dress, white cap and ornate shawl form a strong, flat pattern against the dark ground, and the pale child on her lap is a bright accent. Hopkins's strength is this kind of clear, frontal design, and his weakness the stiffness of the figures. The mother's severe look and the child's wide stare make an affecting, slightly formidable pair, and the picture has a dignity beyond its modest technique."},"nga:42499":{"d":"A retriever is the subject of this horizontal canvas of about 56 by 81 cm, painted in the second half of the nineteenth century by an artist known only by the initials O. G. The picture is a portrait of a dog, the breed named in the title, and belongs to the tradition of American folk painting, in which untrained or self-taught artists made likenesses of people, animals and farms for local customers.","i":"Retrievers were bred to fetch game for hunters, and in nineteenth-century America sporting dogs belonged to a growing culture of leisure and hunting, including organized dog shows after 1877. A portrait of a prized dog, painted for its owner, was a modest, affectionate commission, and folk painters supplied them at low cost. The picture speaks of a bond between animal and master, and of the pride of owning a good working dog.","c":"A folk portrait of a dog has the strengths of directness and affection: a firm outline, flat color and an uncomplicated pose. The proportions may be naive, and the charm is frankness more than finish. The unknown O. G. lacks the polish of Landseer, the British painter of dogs, but also his sentimentality. Among folk animal paintings it is modest, and its interest lies in its honest record of a creature that mattered to someone."},"nga:43434":{"d":"A tranquil waterway winds among hills and foliage, with a castle and a town with a flag-topped tower on the horizon, in an oil on canvas of about 68 by 100 centimeters from the mid-nineteenth century. In the foreground a couple stands by an embankment watching boats on the water, and birds fly low over the distant water. The vegetation is painted in thick brushstrokes, in warm, earthy tones with touches of red. The title promises a military ceremony, but what the eye finds first is a quiet river view.","i":"Mounting the guard, the daily ceremony of posting fresh sentries, was a spectacle at forts and garrison towns, and the castle and flagged tower on the horizon may be where it takes place. The title may refer to a small event in the distance, or it may be a rather grand name for a pleasant outing by the water. Folk painters often combined elements from prints and imagination, and a castle on a height is a familiar picturesque device.","c":"The painting is a pleasant, rather loosely managed river landscape: the thick foliage gives the surface texture, the warm palette is harmonious, and the standing couple supplies a human note. It lacks the clarity of the best folk views, and the title does not quite match the scene, but the combination of castle, boats and birds has an appealing, dreamlike air. A modest, atmospheric piece."},"nga:52968":{"d":"A pastoral scene beside the water, in an oval frame, is worked in watercolor on velveteen in the technique called theorem painting, about 64 by 83 centimeters, dated 1823. Figures gather flowers in the center under a lightly clouded blue sky, with green and brown foliage above and rocky ground in the foreground. At left a ship lies anchored near the shore with buildings in the distance, and the water has soft waves. The pastel colors give the whole a calm, reflective mood.","i":"John Howard (1726 to 1790) was the English philanthropist who exposed the horrors of European prisons, wrote The State of the Prisons in 1777 and died of fever at Kherson, in present-day Ukraine. He was admired in America as a Christian hero of benevolence, and a memorial picture of this kind honors his memory. Theorem painting was taught to girls in schools as an ornamental accomplishment, so this was probably a student's or young woman's work.","c":"Theorem painting works from stencils, so it asks for neatness and a sense of color more than drawing, and the soft pastels on velvet give this picture a pleasant glow. The scene, flower gatherers, a ship and a quiet shore, is more a generic pastoral than a particular tribute, and the connection with Howard comes from the title. It is a polite, decorative piece, valued for its technique and for its glimpse of how a young woman honored a hero."},"nga:43440":{"d":"A weeping figure stands near a gravestone under a willow tree, in a vertical oil on canvas about 56 by 39 centimeters, dated 1831. A white horse pulls a cart along a path, and a townscape with a church steeple lies in the distance. The setting sun casts warm colors across the sky, with unusual cloud formations and birds in flight. The palette combines warm and cool tones, and the title marks the picture as a memorial to a family named Eaton.","i":"Mourning pictures were a major form of American folk art between about 1800 and the 1840s: a weeping willow, a tomb, a mourner and a landscape were the standard ingredients, made by schoolgirls in embroidery and by painters on commission to commemorate a death in the family. The Eaton family memorial belongs to this tradition, and the setting sun and the distant town add a note of closure, the day and a life ending.","c":"The picture carries its sentiment lightly: the figure bends in grief, the willow droops and the sun sets in the glowing sky. The painter, Samuel Jordan, who died in 1836 at thirty-three, handles the clouds and the golden light with more freedom than the usual mourning piece. It is a quiet, affecting, well-organized picture, and its sunset gives the sorrow a note of calm."},"nga:59894":{"d":"A ship under full sail, in oil on canvas, about 68 by 92 centimeters, dated 1827, signed by T. Davies, an American artist about whom little is known. The rigging, flags and sea state are the details to study closely. At this size and date it is a ship portrait of the kind commissioned by owners and captains of American merchant vessels.","i":"Ship portraits were a specialized genre, made to record a particular vessel for her owner or captain, and they emphasized accurate rigging and sailing, often showing the ship in several views. In the early republic, when overseas trade made American fortunes, a handsome ship under full sail was a source of civic and personal pride. The title does not name the vessel, so whether this is a portrait of a real ship or a general image is not clear.","c":"Ship portraits are judged on the accuracy of the vessel and the lively handling of water and sails, and by those standards a ship in full sail makes an appealing subject. Its particular success is hard to weigh, and the painter T. Davies remains obscure. It is of interest mainly as an example of a popular maritime genre that thrived outside the academies."},"nga:42447":{"d":"A woman shown from the upper body, seated sideways yet facing the viewer, in a yellow dress with puffed sleeves, patterned white lace on the bodice and a high waistline. Her dark hair is dressed up with ringlets framing her face; her complexion is fair with a slight blush, and her eyes are blue. She wears a black choker with a gold pendant, gold drop earrings and a bracelet, and holds an ornate white fan. The background is plain muted blue, with reddish-brown drapery and tassels at the top and sides. Oil on canvas, about 56 by 43 centimeters, painted about 1813.","i":"The high-waisted yellow gown, the ringlets and the fan are all the fashion of the years around 1813, and the sitter has dressed to show it. The draped curtain with tassels is a formula borrowed from grand European portraits, here scaled down for a household in the young republic. The painter is an unidentified artist known only as the Conant Limner, and the sitter is known from the title as Sophia Burpee Conant.","c":"A pleasing American portrait of the early republic, direct and decorative. The yellow dress, the lace and the fan are flattened into patterns, the drapery is a formula, and the face has a simple frankness. These limitations are also the charm: the painter's attention goes to what mattered to the sitter, her clothes and ornaments, with little interest in illusion. It is typical of the unpretentious portraits made for local families around 1813."},"nga:59941":{"d":"A large half watermelon with red flesh and black seeds sits at the center of a white plate with a silver knife, on a long wooden table. Around it are a yellow pineapple, red and green grapes, dark red plums, pink peaches and a partly peeled orange, with pumpkins or squash and what could be figs or radishes below the table. A yellow-and-black bird perches on the handle of a yellow basket of fruit behind the watermelon. A window at left shows a river winding through a green valley under a pale sky. The canvas is about 56 by 72 centimeters.","i":"The bird is named in the title as a Baltimore oriole, the orange-and-black songbird that takes its name from the colors of Lord Baltimore, the Calvert family's heraldry, and that later became the state bird of Maryland. A table groaning with ripe fruit, a bird and a view of the countryside is a formula for abundance, a prosperity image that suited the parlor. The painter is recorded only under the name Wagguno, and the date is 1858.","c":"The abundance is the point, and the painter delivers it with relish: the red watermelon at the center, the stacks of fruit, the bird as a bright accent and the window view as a calm counter-note. The perspective is shaky, the forms flat and the sizes unreliable, but color and invention carry it. It is a delightfully exuberant example of mid-century American folk still life."},"nga:42437":{"d":"A large blue and gray bowl is filled with fruit, grapes, peaches and a pineapple among them, with fruit and foliage overflowing in every direction, in a watercolor on velveteen of about 47 by 53 centimeters, from about 1830 to 1840. The colors are blues, greens, yellows, oranges and browns. The technique is theorem painting, in which the design is built up with stencils, one for each part, so that the colors are applied in flat, clean shapes.","i":"Theorem painting was an accomplishment taught in female academies in the first half of the nineteenth century: the student worked from a printed pattern, cut stencils for each element, and applied watercolor or oil with a brush or sponge on velvet or paper. A bowl of fruit was the favorite subject, and the pineapple, a symbol of hospitality, an expected guest. William Stearns is recorded as the artist, and little else is known.","c":"The velvet gives the colors a soft, matte bloom, and the stencils give the shapes a clean, decorative clarity. What is lost is individuality, since every theorem painter worked from the same patterns, but what is gained is a delightful coherence of design: the bowl as a stable base, the fruit as a rhythmic crowd. It is a charming object, best enjoyed as pattern."},"nga:42438":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated and facing forward, with fair skin and distinct features. Her powdered hair is elaborately styled and decorated with flowers and black plumes. She wears a red gown trimmed with lace, a white lace collar, pearls and a corsage, and she holds a book. The background is a plain, warm brown. It is a half-length portrait of the kind commissioned by prosperous families in the years after the American Revolution, painted in the plain, linear manner of much provincial American portraiture.","i":"The book, the fashionable hairstyle, the plumes and the lace advertise education, taste and means: the sitter is presented as a woman of standing. Portraits like this were painted by itinerant or local limners for families who could not commission the leading painters of Boston or Philadelphia, and they hung at home as a record of rank and family continuity. The Denison Limner is a name for a painter not securely identified; some have connected the works with Joseph Steward.","c":"Typical provincial portraiture, and honest about it: the face is firmly drawn, the costume is a handsome catalogue of lace, pearls and plumes, and the plain brown ground puts everything on the sitter. The modelling is flat, the proportions a little stiff, and the book and hands do little to animate the pose. Its appeal is directness and the care lavished on finery, not psychological depth. Beside Copley or Stuart it is naive, but vivid."},"nga:59919":{"d":"A man is shown half-length, seated at a table, his body turned slightly to the right and his head facing the viewer with a calm expression. His brown hair is combed back, and he wears a dark jacket with large decorative buttons, a cream waistcoat and a white cravat with ruffled edges at the neck and cuffs. In his hand he holds a quill poised over a sheet of paper; an inkwell and a small cylindrical container stand beside it. Behind him a window opens on a landscape with a tree, water, a cloudy sky and a partly visible building, with a red curtain across the top.","i":"The quill poised over paper marks the sitter as a literate man of affairs, as a captain, merchant or magistrate might wish to appear. The title does not say whether his rank was military or maritime. Like other portraits by the Denison Limner, he is shown in his best clothes at a table, with the window view setting him in his world. Portraits of this kind recorded achievement and respectability more than inner life.","c":"A direct, no-nonsense likeness. The calm gaze, the crisp white cravat and the pen held mid-gesture give the picture a pleasing presence, and the window view relieves the plain table. As in other works by this painter the modelling is flat, the hands stiff and the space shallow, so the room feels like a backdrop. Its appeal lies in clarity, not depth: a fair example of the provincial portrait as a statement of respectability."},"nga:59920":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated in three-quarter view and facing slightly to her left, with delicate features, a smooth complexion, arched eyebrows and an expression that might suggest attentiveness. Her brunette hair is curled at the sides under a lace headdress, and she wears a high-necked gown with lace at the cuffs, a white shawl or fichu and a voluminous dark brown skirt; one hand rests in her lap and a bracelet or band shows at her wrist. Behind her a red curtain with white trim is drawn aside to reveal a bridged stretch of water, greenery and a partly cloudy sky.","i":"A curtain drawn back on a landscape is a convention borrowed from European portraiture, where it signalled status and a refined setting; in provincial American portraits it became a quick way of dignifying a sitter. The title gives her married name, and the picture was presumably painted as a family record. As with the other Denison portraits, the name Denison Limner stands for a hand not securely identified, which some scholars connect with Joseph Steward.","c":"More ambitious than a plain-background portrait: the curtain, bridge and sky lend the sitter an air of consequence, and the lace and fichu are painted with evident care. The figure, however, sits stiffly in front of the view, with a hand awkwardly placed and the skirt a dark, undifferentiated mass, and the landscape is a stage set rather than a place. Charming and sincere, it shows how a provincial painter borrowed the formulas of grand portraiture."},"nga:59921":{"d":"The museum record gives no description of this canvas beyond its title and format, so only its type can be stated. It is an oil on canvas, about 88 by 69 centimetres, showing a female sitter identified as Miss Denison of Stonington, Connecticut, possibly Matilda Denison. It matches the size and upright format of the other Denison portraits in the National Gallery, which are half-length likenesses made around 1790 by the artist called the Denison Limner. It is a family portrait of the kind prosperous New England households commissioned from local or travelling painters.","i":"The title calls the sitter Miss, which marks her as unmarried, and its 'possibly' shows that her identity is not firmly established. Stonington, on the Connecticut coast, was a seafaring town, and families like the Denisons had likenesses painted to record status and lineage. Portraits of young women of this period often marked their coming of age or marriage prospects, though the purpose of this picture is not recorded.","c":"With no close detail to judge, the fairest verdict is on the type. The Denison Limner's portraits offer plain, direct presence and careful attention to costume rather than subtlety of modelling, and this one presumably shares those virtues and limits. It is best valued as a document of a Connecticut household and of provincial taste around 1790, not as a refined work of art."},"nga:43642":{"d":"A young woman of the Van Alen family is shown in an oil on canvas of about 79 by 66 cm, painted about 1735 by the artist known as the Gansevoort Limner, now identified as Pieter Vanderlyn. Vanderlyn worked among the Dutch families of the Hudson Valley in colonial New York, and his portraits are marked by strong outlines, flat areas of bright color and a decorative treatment of costume, with little attempt at illusionistic depth.","i":"In the Dutch-American society of the Hudson Valley, a portrait marked a family's rising prosperity, and such families had few trained painters to turn to. Limners, itinerant painters without academic training, often took the poses and costumes for their sitters from imported English prints, so that a girl in Albany or Kinderhook might appear in a fashionable attitude borrowed from a London engraving. The face remains individual, and the portrait speaks of local pride.","c":"A limner's strengths are bold design, flat bright color and linear precision, and his weaknesses are stiff drawing and shallow space. For modern eyes the vernacular directness has its own force, and such portraits have become prized as expressions of an American folk art. The Gansevoort Limner is a more accomplished decorative designer than draftsman, and this portrait has a quiet strength more than charm, a sober record of colonial family pride."},"nga:59885":{"d":"A young lady holds a fan in this large oil on canvas of about 97 by 81 cm, dated 1737, by the Gansevoort Limner, now identified as Pieter Vanderlyn. The fan, an accessory of fashion and gentility, is the object that gives the portrait its title. The painter worked among the Dutch families of the Hudson Valley, and his portraits are known for crisp outlines, bold flat color and a decorative handling of costume.","i":"A fan was a mark of gentility in the early eighteenth century, and in a colonial portrait it announced that the sitter belonged to the polite world of London and Amsterdam as much as to the Hudson Valley. A young woman with a fan was likely to be of marriageable age, and the portrait may have marked her engagement. Limners often borrowed poses from English mezzotints, so the fan would be part of an imported repertoire.","c":"The fan gives the painter a motif of elegant gesture, and the Limner's flat, decorative style gives it a bold graphic force. The proportions are stiff and the space is shallow, but the clear outline and rich surface of costume are real merits. As a record of colonial taste and of local ambition, the portrait is more interesting than as a likeness, and as a piece of American vernacular design it can be admired for its clarity."},"nga:1125":{"d":"A portrait of Major Thomas Biddle, an officer of the United States Army, painted in 1832 on a canvas of about 91 by 70 centimeters. The painter, Thomas Wilcocks Sully, was the son of the celebrated portraitist Thomas Sully and learned the trade in his father's Philadelphia studio. The portrait belongs to the Andrew W. Mellon Collection, the founding gift of the National Gallery of Art.","i":"If, as seems likely, the sitter is the Major Thomas Biddle who was killed in a duel at St. Louis in 1831, the portrait is posthumous, painted for his family after his death. Biddle, a brother of the banker Nicholas Biddle, had served in the War of 1812 and was an army paymaster; the duel with a congressman, Spencer Pettis, killed both men. Family commissions of this kind kept the memory of a lost relative.","c":"Thomas Wilcocks Sully inherited his father's smooth professional manner without the older man's romantic flair, and a posthumous commission gave him little room for invention. The result is a competent, dignified record of a man whose story was more dramatic than his image. The picture has historical interest and decent craft, but it stays in the shadow of the elder Sully's more accomplished portraits."},"met:12709":{"d":"George Washington is the subject of this portrait of about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1840 by Thomas Wilcocks Sully, forty-one years after the president's death. No sitting was possible, so the picture presumably follows an earlier image, and most likenesses of Washington made after 1796 descend from Gilbert Stuart's portraits, which set the face of the first president for the nation. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1954 by Mrs. George F. Baker.","i":"By 1840 Washington had become a national icon, and portraits of him were wanted for public buildings, schools, parlors and fraternal halls. Painters turned to Gilbert Stuart's Athenaeum portrait of 1796 as the authoritative likeness, copying it with variations, and the Sullys of Philadelphia were among those who supplied such pictures. The picture is therefore less a portrait than a tribute, repeating a face that all Americans were expected to recognize.","c":"A copy of a famous portrait is judged by how well it carries the original's authority, and Sully, a capable professional, can be expected to give a clear, dignified Washington. What such pictures lack is the fresh observation of the original. This one is valuable as a sign of the hero cult of the 1840s and as an example of the craft of replication, and less as an independent work of art."},"nga:80803":{"d":"In a mountainous landscape, a robust woman with a short bleeding slash across her chest reclines into the arms of an older, bearded man, while a younger man at lower left holds an arrow tip to his own chest. The woman, Dorinda, wears pearls like a crown in her blond curls, her eyelids and cheeks deeply flushed, her white blouse bunched over the wound and her wine-red skirt glinting like silk. The younger man, Silvio, leans toward her, lips parted. A quiver and a silver horn with red tassels lie at lower right. The canvas is about 105 by 175 centimeters.","i":"The story comes from Il Pastor Fido, Giovanni Battista Guarini's pastoral play of about 1590, hugely popular across Europe: Silvio, a hunter who scorns love, shoots Dorinda, a nymph who loves him, mistaking her disguise for a wolf; moved to remorse, he turns the arrow on himself. Dorinda survives and the two are eventually united. Dutch painters of Arcadian scenes liked its mixture of violence and tenderness. This picture is dated about 1651, with some uncertainty.","c":"A painter's task with this subject is to balance pathos and pastoral grace, and the picture does so in the manner of Italianate Dutch painters: the robust heroine swoons with a stagey elegance, and the men crowd her in the kind of cluster the Baroque loved. Louis Vallée is a little-documented artist, more charming than powerful, and the picture is best read as an example of the cult of the Pastor Fido, a love story told with arrows and sighs."},"nga:42443":{"d":"Seven people sit and stand outdoors in a group portrait on wood, about 65 by 81 centimeters, dated 1822. At right a man in a black top hat and black suit with a white collar sits holding a book on his lap, turned toward the six children at left: three older boys in black suits over white shirts, two younger boys with ruffled collars, and a baby in a white bonnet and dress held on the lap of one of the older boys. All have short brown hair. Behind them are dark green trees, a blue sky and a stream over dark gray rocks.","i":"The title names the father, Abraham Clark, who is not the New Jersey signer of the Declaration of Independence of the same name, who died in 1794. A father shown alone with his children, the mother absent, may record a household after a loss, though the title does not say so. The book in the father's lap suggests that he is the family's teacher in piety and learning, and the rustic setting gives the group the air of a natural order.","c":"The grouping is the thing: the father at one side, the children lined up like the steps of a staircase, and the baby at the center of their attention. The painter, known only as J. H., draws the children in black and white as a sober chorus, and the stream and trees provide a simple stage. The faces are plain and the poses stiff, but the picture has a moving seriousness, as a record of a family held together."},"met:437232":{"d":"Saint Anne sits enthroned with her daughter the Virgin Mary and the Christ Child, in a tall panel of about 150 by 81 centimeters, painted in tempera and gold on wood. The museum's keywords record a musical instrument among the figures, so the court around the throne probably includes music, a sign of heavenly harmony. The gold ground and the hierarchical arrangement belong to the late medieval altarpiece, and the painter is known only as the Osma Master, a Spanish artist whose name points to Osma in Castile.","i":"Saint Anne, the mother of the Virgin, was among the most popular saints of the late Middle Ages, a patron of mothers and families, and the image of three generations, Anne, Mary and Jesus, together expressed the holy lineage of Christ. In Spain her cult was strong in the late fifteenth century, and an enthroned Anne with Mary and the Child suited a chapel dedicated to her. The panel came to the Met from the Coudert brothers in 1888.","c":"Spanish altarpieces of this period join Flemish detail to Spanish gravity, and a panel like this one shows both in a modest way: the gold ground, the courtly throne, the crowd of attributes. The painter is not a great inventor, and the figures are stiff and the faces generalized, but the picture has a gentle, devotional charm and the glitter of gold. A representative piece of a kind of painting seldom seen outside Spain."},"met:10503":{"d":"A boy, named in the title as Alexander Stewart Wetherill, is the subject of this tall portrait of about 152 by 96 centimeters, a size that gives the child a nearly life-size presence. Painted about 1890 to 1900 by the American portraitist Alfred Q. Collins, it is a society portrait of a child of a well-off family, a type of picture fashionable in the Gilded Age, with an upright format that suits a standing figure.","i":"Portraits of children from wealthy American families were good business in the 1890s, when painters trained in Paris offered European polish in the manner of Sargent and Carolus-Duran. Such portraits displayed a child's appearance, dress and natural charm, and recorded a family's standing; this one stands for the Gilded Age habit of commissioning grand likenesses of the young.","c":"Collins was a Paris-trained portraitist working in the wake of Sargent, and his portrait of a boy has the Gilded Age virtues: assured pose, bravura of touch and a charming impression of childhood. It also has the type's weakness, that it can be more costume than character. Without Sargent's psychological sharpness, the picture is a pleasing record rather than a revelation."},"met:440840":{"d":"A small head of an old man, in oil on paper laid down on wood, only about 22 by 17 centimeters, dated about 1470 to 1475. It is associated with Hugo van der Goes, though some records call it the work of his circle. A study of this kind, made as a preparation for a larger picture or as an exercise in observation, is very rare from fifteenth-century Netherlandish art, since such preliminary works were seldom kept; the Met acquired it in 2010.","i":"Hugo van der Goes (about 1430 to 1482) was the leading painter of Ghent in the 1470s, famous for the Portinari Altarpiece, painted for a Medici bank agent in Bruges and sent to Florence, where it deeply influenced Italian painters. He entered a monastery near Brussels in the later 1470s and suffered a breakdown of mind that contemporaries chronicled. An unflinching head of an old man fits his fascination with the realities of age and poverty.","c":"A head study frees a painter from the demands of a commission, and the best ones show the habit of looking: here, an old man's face observed with a candor that Hugo's finished altarpieces share. The medium, oil on paper, gives it a direct, fresh quality. It lacks the finish of a completed portrait, and the attribution is debated, but it is a remarkable survivor and a vivid instance of fifteenth-century realism."},"met:436521":{"d":"A man is the sitter of this small oil portrait on wood, about 32 by 27 centimeters, dated about 1475 and attributed to Hugo van der Goes. Portraits are rare in his surviving work, which is dominated by large religious altarpieces, and the small panel gives a glimpse of the other side of his art, direct observation of a living face. It came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1929 with the bequest of Mrs. H. O. Havemeyer, a leading collector of Old Masters.","i":"Netherlandish portraits of the 1470s, by Memling, Petrus Christus and Hugo, show the individual face, with the wrinkles, bumps and moles of real life, in a new spirit of observation. Who the man was is not recorded, but a small portrait like this was probably a private commission, perhaps made for a family and not for display. Hugo's interest in the uncomposed face is the same that gives his altarpiece shepherds their startling reality.","c":"Hugo's portraits are few, and their rarity makes any one precious; if the attribution holds, this one shows the same refusal to idealize that gives the Portinari shepherds their startling reality. It is a smaller thing than an altarpiece, and the sitter's anonymity limits what can be said, but the quality of observation puts it among the most distinguished Netherlandish portraits of its decade."},"met:437784":{"d":"Soldiers help the nude Alexander the Great from a river, with a horse nearby, in an oil on canvas of about 97 by 137 centimeters, painted about 1650. The museum's keywords record a soldier, Alexander, a horse, a nude, a river and bathing, so the scene is the moment after the conqueror, who had plunged into the cold waters of the Cydnus, was carried out by his men. The nude figures and muscular poses show the sculptural classicism that Pietro Testa drew from antiquity.","i":"The story is told by ancient historians: in 333 BC, in Cilicia, the young Alexander bathed in the icy Cydnus and fell gravely ill, and his physician Philip prepared a remedy that he drank, even though a letter had warned that Philip meant to poison him. The incident became a favorite example of trust and courage. Testa was a learned painter and etcher who drew antiquities for the collector Cassiano dal Pozzo, and he drowned in the Tiber in 1650, in circumstances that remain uncertain.","c":"Testa's art is learned rather than easy: crowded with antique references, intellectually ambitious, a little overloaded with meaning, but strong in drawing. A scene of wet nudes and a horse in a landscape gives him room to display his anatomy and his study of ancient sculpture. The result is more admirable than warm, and it shows why he is among the most interesting of the Roman classicists, a gifted painter whose early death left much unrealized."},"met:10874":{"d":"Tench Francis, a Philadelphia lawyer and officeholder, is the sitter of this large portrait, about 125 by 99 centimeters, painted in 1746 by Robert Feke. The size is that of a three-quarter-length portrait of a gentleman of means, and the picture belongs to Feke's Philadelphia years, when the painter, who had worked in Newport and Boston, visited the city and found rich clients. The Met bought it in 1934 with the Maria DeWitt Jesup Fund.","i":"Robert Feke (about 1707 to about 1751) was the best colonial portraitist of his generation, a painter who may have taught himself and who modeled his poses on English mezzotint portraits, translating their gentlemanly ease into colonial terms. Tench Francis was one of the leading lawyers of Pennsylvania and the founder of a prominent Philadelphia family, and a portrait of 1746 shows a man of the colonial elite dressed and posed on the model of London's gentry.","c":"Feke's portraits are admired for their polish, their silky fabrics and their precise, rather masklike faces, and he was the finest portrait painter in the colonies in the 1740s. His weakness, a certain formula in poses and a flatness of space, is part of his charm, because it reveals the borrowing from English prints. This is a strong example, with the confident air of a colonial gentleman who wanted to look as good as a Londoner."},"nga:50688":{"d":"A man identified by the title as Captain Alexander Graydon is portrayed in this oil on canvas of about 101 by 81 centimeters, painted around 1746 by Robert Feke. The format is that of a half-length or three-quarter-length portrait, the standard size for a colonial gentleman who wanted a dignified likeness to hang in his house. The rank in the title is the clearest clue to how he wished to be seen.","i":"Robert Feke was the most accomplished American portraitist of his generation, working in Newport, Boston and Philadelphia in the 1740s. Like other colonial painters, he often borrowed poses and accessories from English mezzotint prints, which gave provincial sitters the look of metropolitan gentlemen. A captain's title in the 1740s, a time of war with France and Spain, carried real status, and the portrait records it.","c":"Feke gave colonial sitters a stylish silken polish that few of his American contemporaries could match, with smooth faces, rich fabrics and easy poses. The cost is a certain sameness, since his men and women often seem cast from the same mold. This portrait is a representative piece of his Philadelphia period, competent and handsome rather than penetrating."},"cma:99429":{"d":"Charles Apthorp, called in his obituary the greatest merchant on the continent, is shown in an elegantly tailored, confidently posed portrait of about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted in 1748. Behind him in the distance a sailing ship stands for his Atlantic trade. Apthorp commissioned the portrait from Robert Feke, the fashionable painter who had come to Boston, and the picture belongs to the period when the city was among the key commercial centers of the American colonies.","i":"Apthorp helped transform Boston into one of the key commercial centers of the colonies, and the ship in the distance signals his deep interest in Atlantic trade. That trade included not only textiles, wine and guns but also enslaved people, and slavery and its related commerce made up a significant part of colonial Boston's economy until after the Revolution, when antislavery attitudes gained strength in New England. The picture's confident elegance and the sitter's trade in people belong to the same history.","c":"Feke's gift is to make a man look like a person of consequence: the confident stance, the fine tailoring and the quiet command of the face. The picture is all surface, as colonial portraits of wealth were, and its beauty is inseparable from what the museum rightly notes about the sources of that wealth. As art it is polished and assured; as history it is complex and disquieting, and together they deserve close attention."},"nga:177131":{"d":"A man stands with one hand resting on a table covered in blue fabric, his posture dignified and his shoulders relaxed. He has fair skin, a rounded face and a calm expression, with grayish-white hair in curls typical of the eighteenth century. He wears a dark coat with decorative buttons and wide cuffs showing white ruffles, and a white cravat. Behind him are a stone column and a view of a landscape with blue sky, hills, clouds and trees. The canvas measures about 127 by 102 centimeters.","i":"The column and the landscape glimpse are borrowed from English grand-manner portraiture, which colonial painters knew through imported mezzotints, and they signaled a sitter's standing and taste. Simon Pease is shown as a man of substance, at ease at his table. Robert Feke painted in Newport and Boston in the late 1740s, and sitters there included merchants who wanted the dignity of the gentry.","c":"This is Feke near his best: the pose is confident, the face is modeled with quiet care, and the cuffs and cravat have the right crispness. The landscape and column are formulas, and the hand on the table is a little stiff. Even so, it conveys unforced authority and shows why Feke was the leading portraitist in the colonies at mid-century."},"met:11042":{"d":"A view over the bay and city of New York from the heights at Weehawken, New Jersey, painted in oil on canvas of about 61 by 84 centimeters, in 1840. The museum's keywords record a tree and a landscape, and the title gives the scene: the Hudson and the harbor spread below a wooded foreground, with the city on the far shore. The artist, Robert Havell Jr., had emigrated from England in 1839, and this is one of the early American landscapes of a man best known as an engraver.","i":"Robert Havell Jr. (1793 to 1878) engraved and printed John James Audubon's Birds of America in London, one of the great feats of printmaking, then emigrated to the United States and took up landscape painting along the Hudson, working in the manner of the Hudson River School. Weehawken was a favorite viewpoint for visitors, offering a panorama of the growing metropolis, which in 1840 would still have been a low-rise city of church spires and masts.","c":"Havell's training as an engraver gives him a fine eye for detail and a sound grasp of spatial organization, and his landscape has the clarity and tidy order of a good print. He lacks the poetic ambition of Cole or the high color of Church, and some find his handling tight, but as a document of New York in 1840, and as the work of the man who made Audubon's plates, it has real interest."},"met:13040":{"d":"A woman identified as Margaret Blake, Mrs. Nathaniel Bowen, is the sitter of this watercolor miniature on ivory, about 6.4 by 5.2 centimeters, dated about 1805. The painter, Jean François de la Vallée, who is recorded as active between 1785 and 1815, builds the face in fine strokes over the white of the ivory. The Met bought it in 1937 with the Fletcher Fund, and the title preserves both her married and her maiden names.","i":"A miniature of about 1805 was a luxury of the early republic's gentry, and an American sitter painted by an artist with a French name may reflect the cosmopolitan market that welcomed French artists in the years after the Revolution. Miniatures were given at betrothals and marriages, and this portrait of a woman named in full reflects the custom of the time, which defined a married woman by her husband.","c":"The 1800s favored a soft, clean finish and a simple costume, and a miniaturist's job was to make a face lovely and lifelike without fuss. De la Vallée is a minor figure, but the best of such miniatures have a delicacy that larger pictures cannot match. Here a named sitter and an exact scale give a window onto a woman of the Federal period, held in the palm of the hand."},"met:435826":{"d":"In a walled garden at dusk, a woman with long red-gold hair, in a pale gold-grey gown with blue sleeves, kneels at a small organ, her fingers on the keys and her eyes cast down. At the left a young knight in black plate armour with a red scarf sits barefoot on the ground, gazing up at her, his helmet at his side. At the right a winged youth in a red tunic, crowned with a wreath and a magenta plume, kneels at the organ's bellows with his eyes closed. Tall organ pipes rise against the sky. Behind lie a stone gateway, castle walls and roofs, and a meadow with sheep; tulips and other flowers fill the foreground. Oil on canvas, about 114 by 156 centimetres.","i":"Burne-Jones began this picture in 1868 and finished it in 1877, a gestation of nearly a decade. Its medieval romance, a lady singing to her knight while Love himself works the organ, is typical of the Pre-Raphaelite generation's taste for the Middle Ages and for courtly love. The winged youth is most likely Love, and the knight's rapt gaze suggests the song says what he cannot. The year of completion, 1877, was also the year Burne-Jones's reputation took off at the newly opened Grosvenor Gallery in London, where the Aesthetic ideal of art for beauty's sake found its audience.","c":"The scene is lovely and remote. Burne-Jones draws the figures with clear, stylised outlines and stages them like a tableau, and the glowing greens and golds, the polished armour and the tulips make a richly decorative surface. As a story it is thin, and the languor of the poses can verge on affectation; the faces share one type, and nothing in the picture moves. But as an image of longing set to music it works, because the stillness is complete. It is Burne-Jones at his most characteristic, aesthetic and dreamlike, and not to every taste."},"met:16724":{"d":"A man named Mr. Boardman is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory by Margaret Bogardus, dated 1837. The recorded dimensions, about 8 by 9.5 centimeters, make it wider than tall, which is unusual for a portrait miniature, and the museum's keywords note a cravat, the neckcloth that was an essential part of a gentleman's dress. The small portrait was bought for the Met in 2000 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"Margaret Bogardus (1804 to 1878) was one of the American women who made a living painting miniatures in the 1830s and 1840s, a trade that offered women a respectable independence. The sitter's first name is not recorded, which is a loss; the portrait of a man in a cravat belongs to the world of the new commercial city, in which men of business sat for portraits to mark a success or a marriage.","c":"Bogardus's miniatures belong to the clean, careful idiom of the 1830s. A wider-than-tall format asks more of a composer than the usual upright bust, and a portrait of an anonymous Mr. Boardman has to stand on the face and on the economy of its design. It is a quiet, competent example by a woman painter at a time when few women were recognized professionals."},"met:20114":{"d":"A boy rolls a hoop beside a fence, in a watercolor on ivory of about 9.7 by 7.6 centimeters, painted about 1840 by Margaret Bogardus. The museum's keywords record a boy, a toy, a fence, a collar and hoop rolling, so the child is shown at play in a small outdoor scene, a more lively format than the usual bust. The sitter is named in the title as Paul Joseph Revere, and the miniature was bought for the Met in 2009.","i":"The sitter seems to be Paul Joseph Revere (1832 to 1863), a grandson of the patriot Paul Revere, who died as a Union colonel at Gettysburg. A portrait of an eight-year-old at play, with a hoop and stick, is a record of childhood in the years when children's games and toys became a subject for painters and, in hindsight, a portrait of a future soldier.","c":"A child at play is a hard assignment in miniature, because the face is small and the action is the point, and Bogardus meets it with a neat, fresh composition: the fence, the hoop and the dark collar make a clear pattern around the boy. It is livelier than most miniatures of the decade, which tend to a bust against a plain ground, and it is a happy record of a game now forgotten."},"met:15082":{"d":"Gouverneur Morris, the American statesman, is the subject of this watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5.4 centimeters, dated 1798 and painted by Pierre Henri. It is a bust-length miniature built from fine strokes of transparent color on the white of the ivory. The Met bought it in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund, and the date puts it at about the time Morris returned to the United States after a decade in Europe.","i":"Morris (1752 to 1816) was a New Yorker who wrote much of the final text of the United States Constitution, including its famous preamble, and later served as minister to France during the Revolution. He lost a leg in a carriage accident in 1780 and wore a wooden one. Pierre Henri, a French-born miniaturist who worked in America, painted many of the political and social elite, and a miniature of the returning statesman was a keepsake for friends and family.","c":"A miniature of a statesman is a record of a public man in a private form, and Pierre Henri's likeness works, if it does, by clarity: a clear head, a firm line and a plain ground. The style is the polished, slightly dry manner of the French-trained miniaturist of the 1790s. The interest is in the sitter, whose character, brilliant, worldly and sardonic, no small portrait could contain, but whose face is worth having."},"met:16684":{"d":"A woman, a boy and a girl are shown together in this watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 8.6 centimeters, painted about 1800 by Pierre Henri as a portrait of his own family. The horizontal format is unusual among miniatures, which are typically vertical busts, and allows a group arrangement of three figures. A group miniature is far rarer than a single portrait, and a painter's picture of his own family is rarer still.","i":"A painter who portrays his own family is showing what he can do and what he cares about, with no client to please. Pierre Henri, a French-born miniaturist who worked in the United States, seems here to have set down his wife and children, probably for his own keeping. The picture is a small domestic document of an immigrant artist's household in the first years of the nineteenth century.","c":"The group format asks for a harmony that the single bust never needs, three heads arranged within a small rectangle, and the painter handles it with the confidence of a man painting those he knows best. The result has a warmth that commissioned miniatures rarely achieve, and the loving attention of a father makes up for any formal stiffness. It is a charming object of real historic interest."},"met:15000":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in watercolor on a sheet of ivory only 5.4 by 4.4 centimeters across, smaller than a playing card. The title calls him simply a gentleman, and nothing else about him is recorded, so the picture is best understood by its type: a likeness made to be held in the hand, worn, or kept in a case. It dates from about 1840, when the portrait miniature was still a working trade although the daguerreotype had been announced the year before.","i":"With no name attached, the meaning lies in the object's use. A miniature like this was a private thing, given to a spouse or fiancée, sent to relatives far away, or kept in memory of someone lost. Men's portraits of these years generally favor sober dark clothing, a convention that signaled respectability rather than rank. Within a decade the daguerreotype offered a cheaper, faster likeness and drove most miniaturists out of business. Of Barratt, the Met notes only an approximate birth year, about 1814.","c":"In a space this small the test is whether the eyes have life, whether thin color on the ivory gives the flesh a glow, and whether collar and coat are drawn crisply: skills of patience rather than boldness. Barratt is too little documented to set the miniature against a body of work, so it reads best as an example of an everyday trade that photography was about to end. Whatever its merits, they lie in the face."},"met:16954":{"d":"A small boy shown with a bird, painted in watercolor on ivory that measures 9.2 by 7.0 centimeters, a generous size for a miniature. Beyond the boy and the bird the record gives no detail, so the pose and the kind of bird are left to the viewer's eye. Children's miniatures of the 1830s often paired the sitter with a pet or a toy, which gave the likeness a small story as well as a face.","i":"The title names the sitter, presumably the Henry Larcom Abbot born in 1831 who became a US Army engineer and a Union general; if so, he was three or four years old here. A bird in a child's portrait can be read simply as a pet, or in older tradition as a sign of innocence. The picture was most likely a family commission, kept as a record of a child at an age that passes quickly. Blanchard, who lived from 1808 until after 1849, is lightly documented.","c":"At 9.2 by 7.0 centimeters this is larger than many miniatures, which gives Blanchard room to attend to the child's face and to add a second subject, so the likeness is more than a head on a plain ground. The pet is a charming but conventional idea, and the painter is too little known to say how this ranks among his work. It stands as a pleasing example of the late miniature trade rather than an innovation."},"met:437004":{"d":"Two saints share a single panel, painted in oil on spruce against a gold ground, 68.9 by 43.2 centimeters. The title names Saint Adalbert and Saint Procopius, and a crosier, the staff of a bishop or abbot, appears among the things shown. Gold grounds lift figures out of any real place; around 1490 to 1500 Austrian painters kept that medieval convention while using oil paint. The panel matches the Met's picture of the burial of Saint Wenceslas in size, wood and date, so the two probably come from one altarpiece.","i":"Adalbert, bishop of Prague, was killed by pagans in Prussia in 997; Procopius, who died in 1053, founded the monastery of Sázava and was honored for its Slavonic liturgy. Both became patron saints of Bohemia, so pairing them points to a commission or devotion with Bohemian ties, which is plausible for an Austrian workshop. A bishop and an abbot, each with his staff, also stand for the two kinds of authority in the medieval church: the diocese and the monastery.","c":"The panel shows how long the late Gothic altarpiece format kept its hold in provincial Austria: gold ground, clear attributes, saints identified at a glance. Its interest is as a witness to regional devotion more than as an advance in art, and an anonymous master leaves little biography against which to measure it. What can be judged is the handling of faces, hands and robes, where a painter of this date either brings figures to life or repeats a pattern."},"met:437005":{"d":"A burial scene on a gold ground, painted in oil on spruce, 68.9 by 43.2 centimeters. The title identifies the dead man as Saint Wenceslas, Duke of Bohemia, and the picture's subjects include a tomb, men attending it, and a reference to Saint Vitus, whose church in Prague received his body. It shares its size, wood and date with the panel of Saints Adalbert and Procopius, so the two probably belonged to one altarpiece.","i":"Wenceslas, duke of Bohemia in the early tenth century, was murdered by his brother Boleslav and soon honored as a martyr and as the land's chief patron. Tradition says he founded the church of Saint Vitus in Prague, where his body was laid to rest and where his tomb became a goal of pilgrimage. For viewers around 1500 the scene joined local devotion to the idea of a Christian ruler made holy by a violent death, a figure admired across Central Europe.","c":"A burial is a quiet subject, and on a gold ground it is hard to make dramatic; the interest lies in how the master groups mourners and clergy around the tomb and sets dark robes against the metal. Like its companion, the panel is more a document of regional piety than an innovation, valuable for the survival of work by a painter who is otherwise little known."},"nga:1193":{"d":"A pastoral gathering of nude and partly clothed people of different ages rests in a green landscape with a tree and distant hills under a pale-gray sky. An elderly man sits at the left, gesturing to those around him. Cloths in blue, white and yellow are wound at the waist, some wear wide yellow hats, and scythes, an anvil and other tools lie scattered about as if just put down. A dark blue border patterned with leaves and fruit frames the scene like a painted mount. The canvas is about 108 by 148 centimeters.","i":"Rest and its companion Work relate to the large decorations Puvis de Chavannes painted for the museum at Amiens, in Picardy, in the early 1860s. The pair describes an ideal day: effort, then recovery. The figures belong to an undated classical age and stand for human types, not for particular farmers of France; the old man speaking suggests wisdom handed on at the end of labor. The tools lying on the ground tell the viewer what the rest follows.","c":"Rest shows the manner Puvis was refining in the 1860s: pale, matte color, figures set side by side like a frieze, little cast shadow. The gestures are quiet and the grouping a little stiff, and without Work's activity it is the weaker of the pair as a picture. What it offers is calm, a deliberate counter to the crowded, anecdotal Salon pictures of its day, and it already points toward the simplified art that later admirers prized."},"nga:1194":{"d":"A crowd, mostly of men, labors outdoors, wearing little more than scraps of cloth at the waist. At left men fell trees and scrape the bark, one with a heavy rope over his shoulder. In the center smiths work around a large anvil while two men behind them move a long rod in or out of a smoky fire. At right two figures follow great bulls; in the lower right corner a woman lies in the grass while someone in a red cloak holds a small child up to her. LE TRAVAIL is inscribed in gold on the dark blue border.","i":"Work is the active half of a pair with Rest, an ideal of rural and artisan labor from felling timber to forging iron to plowing. The figures are types, not portraits, and the near-nudity and plain tools set them in an imagined antiquity instead of the France of the 1860s, when factories and railways were changing work. The mother and child at lower right introduce the family that labor supports, and the gold inscription names the theme as an allegory, as in a decorative scheme for a public building.","c":"The subject is a catalogue of postures (chopping, hauling, hammering, plowing), and Puvis resists the temptation to make them dramatic: the figures are monumental and slightly static, the colors chalky. His simplified manner divided critics in his own day, while Seurat, Gauguin and later Picasso admired it. Work is the stronger half of the pair, but it succeeds best as decorative planning, a calm arrangement of forms, and not as a study of human effort."},"met:437349":{"d":"A wide canvas, 66.4 by 106.0 centimeters, on the theme of sleep, with men and women among its figures. The title and the recorded subjects are all the record supplies, and they point to a picture about a state rather than an event: rest, dream, the pause between days. It dates from about 1867 to 1870, when Puvis de Chavannes was developing the pale, flat, mural-like manner he used for decorations in provincial museums such as Amiens.","i":"Sleep is an old theme in allegory, linked to rest, dream and the border with death, and it is one that later Symbolists admired in Puvis. Without a museum commentary the safest reading is a mood piece, not a story: the figures are not named, and the subject is the state itself. Viewers of the late 1860s, who valued poetic reverie in painting, could take it as an invitation to contemplation.","c":"Puvis's gift was for quiet, sustained harmony, and sleep suits it: there is little action to flatten and plenty of room for stillness. The danger is anemia, pale color and generic faces, and a canvas this size leaves the painter nowhere to hide. It belongs with the experiments of the later 1860s, before his great decorative cycles; its success depends on whether the sleeping bodies carry real weight and not just a pale outline."},"nga:46675":{"d":"A nearly nude, bearded man sits on the ground in profile facing right, leaning back against a mound with his legs stretched out, head bowed and hands gathered at his chest. His body almost spans the width of the wide canvas, and a slate-blue loincloth is all he wears. Behind him sage-green and peanut-brown hills climb to a horizon near the top edge, with only a narrow band of nearly white sky; several dark hogs nuzzle a mud puddle and stand in the meadow. Soft, diffused light shadows his face and knees.","i":"The subject is the parable in Luke 15: a younger son takes his inheritance, wastes it abroad and ends up feeding pigs, hungry enough to envy their husks, before he decides to return home. Puvis distills it to the moment of ruin and repentance, with no feast and no father, only the penitent and the swine, animals unclean in Jewish law and so a measure of how low he has fallen. The pared-down staging anticipates the isolated figure of his Poor Fisherman of 1881.","c":"Almost everything here is empty land and sky, and the bowed profile bears all the weight; this is Puvis at his most economical. His flattened, simplified drawing was called clumsy by critics, but here it achieves a state of mind, shame and exhaustion made visible without gesture. The weaknesses are a certain anatomical blankness and a palette so close in value that nothing pushes forward. Among his easel pictures it is one of the more affecting."},"met:437350":{"d":"A small canvas, 25.4 by 39.4 centimeters, painted about 1886 to 1887, in which female nudes are placed in a landscape. The title is French for the tamarisk, a feathery shrub of Mediterranean shores, and is also the name of a seaside district near Toulon, so a sunny coastal setting is likely. Pictures of this size by Puvis de Chavannes are often studies or reduced versions of larger decorative schemes, but nothing here says which this one is.","i":"In the late nineteenth century the Mediterranean coast was turning into a resort and into a symbol of the classical past. Puvis set idealized women in simplified coastal scenery as emblems of harmony; his nudes seldom carry mythological names and stand for calm and abundance rather than a story, which may be why the title gives a plant or place and not a goddess. Viewers were invited to supply the mood themselves: summer, ease, an age without care.","c":"At this size the picture is a small essay in Puvis's late manner: broad matte planes, softened contour, a pale range that recalls fresco. It lacks the scale and architecture that give his murals their authority, and small figures like these can look generic. Still, it shows how he thought in terms of pale Mediterranean light, and it is an agreeable glimpse of the Arcadian vision that Matisse would later revisit."},"met:437343":{"d":"An extremely wide canvas, 82.9 by 457.8 centimeters, more than five times as wide as it is tall, showing allegorical men and women. The proportions are those of a mural frieze, and the title ties it to the Sorbonne, the University of Paris, whose great amphitheater Puvis de Chavannes decorated with a vast hemicycle painting in the late 1880s. This canvas, dated 1889, is probably related to that scheme as a reduced version or study.","i":"In the 1880s the Third Republic poured money into schools and universities, and the Sorbonne in Paris, rebuilt on a grand scale from 1885, was a showcase of its faith in education. The picture is usually read as an allegory in which calm, classically dressed figures personify learning, with no portraits and no story, so that knowledge appears as a timeless public good. The theme is Republican and secular, arranged in the classical manner of a frieze.","c":"The long format forces a frieze composition, and Puvis's gift for a steady horizontal rhythm, figures spaced like the members of an entablature, is on display. The price is distance and abstraction: personifications of learning rarely stir feeling, and the figures can seem polite and impersonal. Seen as the record of a great public decoration, it is impressive in ambition; as a picture to look at alone, it asks for more patience than it repays."},"met:437344":{"d":"In a dry, chalky landscape of pale cliffs and a thin strip of blue sea, three figures in loose classical dress gather in the foreground. At the left a seated woman in a yellow robe and white veil rests her head on her hand beside a pot of blue flowers. At the centre a slender, bare-chested figure with short reddish hair leans on one arm and looks up, chin on hand. At the right a woman in a rose-pink gown and ochre shawl, carrying a bronze water jar, steps forward with her hand held out. Far up the slope at the upper left a tiny shepherd sits with two goats. The colours are pale and matte, like fresco. The signature and the date 1891 are at the lower left.","i":"Puvis was the leading French mural painter of his day, and this easel picture carries the manner of his large wall schemes: calm, frieze-like figures in a timeless Mediterranean landscape, in colours as pale as fresco. Published accounts say it was inspired by one of his earlier murals, and that the shepherds' poses probably recall classical sculpture while the muted colour probably recalls Italian frescoes of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. The title is the only story: the listeners are held by a song heard from afar, and no more is explained. Younger artists, among them Seurat and Gauguin, admired Puvis for just this reduction of a scene to simple outline and flat, quiet colour.","c":"The picture is quiet to the point of vagueness, and that is both its charm and its flaw. The grouping is graceful, the pale palette harmonious, and the dreamy stillness fits the title. But the figures are almost without weight or personality, the poses feel borrowed, and the dry, scraped surface can look chalky and anaemic beside the mural scale it was imagined for. It is a work to be taken as a mood. Puvis's admirers valued exactly that; his detractors saw a lack of vitality, and neither view is wrong."},"met:437347":{"d":"A very wide, shallow canvas, 33.3 by 134.3 centimeters, with athletes and nude figures in an open landscape; men engaged in sport are joined by female nudes. The Latin title means roughly 'games for the fatherland'. The frieze-like format is that of a decorative panel, and the picture is probably a small version of a design Puvis de Chavannes made for the museum at Amiens, with which several of his works are linked.","i":"France's defeat in the war of 1870 to 1871 made sport and gymnastics a patriotic cause, since a fit, disciplined youth was seen as the basis of national recovery. Puvis wraps the idea in a classical setting: idealized athletes, a serene landscape, nothing in dress or detail to date the scene. The message that play can be a form of service to the country would have been understood at Amiens, a city that had been occupied by German troops during that war.","c":"The frieze-like layout suits Puvis, who arranges figures in a clear, steady rhythm without crowding. At this scale it works more as a record of a design than as a free-standing picture, and the athletes are idealized to the point of anonymity. It is a worthy minor episode among the Amiens decorations, painted when Puvis had settled into a pale, calm manner that younger artists praised."},"met:437346":{"d":"A wide, low canvas, 40.3 by 113.7 centimeters, in which men, women and a child go about various kinds of work. The Latin title, 'between art and nature', names the theme, and the figures at work suggest the labor of making and growing. The format matches Puvis de Chavannes's decorations for the museum at Amiens, and this smaller canvas probably records one of them in reduced form. It dates from about 1890 to 1895.","i":"The Latin phrase places the picture between human skill and the natural world. Puvis liked to show the arts as natural activities, in harmony with landscape, an Arcadian ideal that answered both Republican hopes for the civilizing power of culture and the Symbolist taste for allegory. For a museum, which collects the products of art and shows nature's models, the theme could stand as a statement of the institution's own purpose. The picture is best read as such an emblem, not as a scene from life.","c":"A calm design that the long format keeps orderly: the figures stand apart like actors in a frieze, avoiding crowding, and the pale tones sit under a level horizon. At about 40 by 114 centimeters the detail is condensed and the faces remain generic. Its charm is its harmony; what is missing, in this reduction, is the monumental scale that would have given a full-size version its quiet authority."},"met:10774":{"d":"Henry Clay, the Kentucky statesman, in a portrait miniature painted in watercolor on ivory about 1845, 8.4 by 6.5 centimeters. The painter, Savinien Edme Dubourjal (1795 to 1865), was French, and the picture joins the fine, polished manner of the Paris miniature to an American public figure. The record gives only a man and a portrait, so Clay's dress and expression are best read from the picture itself. Clay was about sixty-eight.","i":"Clay (1777 to 1852) served as Speaker of the House, senator and Secretary of State, ran for president three times, in 1824, 1832 and 1844, and was called the Great Compromiser for his part in the Missouri Compromise, the tariff compromise of 1833 and the Compromise of 1850. A portrait of about 1845 follows his defeat by James K. Polk in 1844. Likenesses of such men were widely collected through engravings; a miniature was a rarer, private commission or keepsake.","c":"Miniatures of public men tend toward a dignified, generalized likeness, and the small format favors smooth, careful finish over temperament. Whether Dubourjal catches the energy for which Clay was known is the question to put to the miniature itself. As a French hand fixing the features of one of America's most admired and most frustrated presidential aspirants, it has documentary value as well as artistic, and the identity of the sitter lifts it above a routine commission."},"met:11776":{"d":"A girl, named in the title as Kate Lyon Cornell, painted in oil on a canvas about 91 by 74 centimeters, roughly a yard high, in 1856. The record supplies only that she is a girl in a portrait, so her pose, dress and setting are best read from the picture. At this size it was a family commission meant to hang in a house, and it was painted by Theodore E. Pine, an American portraitist who lived from 1827 to 1905.","i":"Portraits of children in the 1850s reflect the new prosperity of the American middle and upper classes, who could afford large canvases of their daughters in their best clothes. Such pictures recorded the child and the family's standing together. Pine, who painted the family of Daniel T. MacFarlan in 1857 and 1858, was working at the height of this demand, and the Met holds several of his portraits from these years.","c":"Pine's portraits are competent and polite, the work of a painter serving a steady market rather than an innovator; the danger in mid-century child portraits is a doll-like sameness, with the dress given more care than the face. The measure of this one is the child's gaze and the life of her features. Pine does not stand among the leading American portraitists of his time, but pictures like this are the substance of the trade."},"met:11775":{"d":"The daughters of Daniel T. MacFarlan, with a dog beside them, painted in oil on a canvas 142.2 by 111.8 centimeters in 1857. The size is generous, well over a meter high, and gives the girls real presence. The dog, a standard companion in a children's portrait, adds a note of play to what is otherwise a formal family commission by Theodore E. Pine.","i":"Pine painted this portrait of the girls and a companion portrait of their parents within a year, at the same size, so the two probably hung together in the family's house, with the children shown as the continuation of the family line. Dogs in children's portraits can be read simply as pets and as emblems of loyalty and affection, a reading old in Western portraiture.","c":"The large canvas gives the sisters presence, and a dog is a good device for loosening the formality of a group of girls. The danger in such commissions is a polite sameness, with each child a variation on a type and the fabrics given more attention than the faces. Pine is competent at this kind of careful family portrait, but his work does not stand out among his American contemporaries."},"met:11777":{"d":"A double portrait of a husband and wife, Mr. and Mrs. Daniel T. MacFarlan, painted in oil on canvas, 142.2 by 111.8 centimeters, in 1858 by Theodore E. Pine. It matches in size the portrait of the MacFarlan daughters from the year before, so the two were evidently conceived as a set. The record gives only a couple, a man and a woman, so the poses and setting are best read from the picture.","i":"A marriage portrait of this kind is a convention going back to the Renaissance: it shows partnership, fidelity and social standing together. In mid-nineteenth-century America such pictures marked the prosperity of a merchant or professional household and were hung in the main rooms as a statement of family continuity. Paired with the portrait of the daughters, it formed a family record in two canvases.","c":"The test of a double portrait is whether the two sitters seem to share a relationship, not simply two likenesses on a single canvas. Whether Pine achieves that is for the picture to show; the commission itself asks for tidiness, rank and clear presentation more than psychological depth. Seen with its companion canvas, it gains interest as a family record; alone it is a respectable but unadventurous example of American portraiture of the 1850s."},"met:438603":{"d":"A small upright portrait of a young man in oil on canvas, about 56 by 47 centimetres. The title names the sitter as François Gérard, who was about twenty at the time, and the painter, Antoine-Jean Gros, was his friend and fellow pupil in the Paris studio of Jacques-Louis David. A likeness between friends, it was probably made for the two men's own pleasure rather than for a client or for exhibition. No further detail of dress or setting can be stated with confidence.","i":"Gros and Gérard were two of the most talented young men in David's studio. Gérard became the leading portraitist of Napoleon's court and later of the restored Bourbon monarchy, while Gros, after leaving for Italy in 1793, became the great painter of Napoleon's campaigns; both were eventually made barons. This portrait belongs to the years before either was famous, and it is best read as a record of friendship and shared ambition within David's school, not a statement of status.","c":"Its chief interest is historical: a young painter, still a pupil, recording a friend who would become his rival in reputation. Early works like this are seldom as assured as an artist's mature output, and Gros's standing rests on the large Napoleonic battle pictures and his later portraits of officers and officials. The small canvas is better regarded as a revealing footnote than as a major statement, though the pairing of two future celebrities gives it lasting appeal."},"nga:46629":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, turned slightly to one side, with a serene expression. His gray hair is short and swept back, he has sideburns, and his face is lined. He wears a dark suit, a white high-collared shirt and a white cravat; a chain, possibly for a watch, crosses the coat, and a touch of red, perhaps a ribbon or handkerchief, shows at the breast pocket. The background is a plain dark brown, so the head and the white linen carry the light. The canvas is dated 1827.","i":"The sitter is identified as Dr. Vignardonne, and his plain dress, lack of props and quiet gaze are typical of professional men in France after the empire, who favored sober black over uniforms. His standing rests on character, not display. If the red is a ribbon, it could mark an honor such as the Legion of Honor, but the painting does not say. A likeness like this was a private commission, kept within the family or given to a friend or colleague.","c":"Antoine-Jean Gros, a pupil of David who made his name with Napoleon's battle scenes, such as Napoleon Visiting the Plague Victims at Jaffa, was near the end of his career in 1827 and kept a quieter vein for private sitters. The head is the thing: attentive, humane, set off by the dark ground. There is none of the brushy drama of his battle pictures; some will find it dry, others will value its honesty."},"met:18830":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a lady in watercolor on ivory, painted about 1845 by the American miniaturist Daniel F. Ames. The sitter wears spectacles and a scarf. Spectacles are an unusually candid detail in a portrait of the 1840s, when many sitters were flattered. As a miniature on ivory it would be small enough to hold in the hand, and it would probably be kept in a case.","i":"By the 1840s the daguerreotype was drawing customers away from miniature painters, and the miniaturists who survived had to compete by offering a more refined, colored and flattering likeness. The sitter's glasses, a sign of age or reading, show a plain realism that contrasts with the idealized beauty of fashionable miniatures. Her name is unknown; the title leaves her as a lady, a term of respectability.","c":"A modest American miniature of the daguerreotype era, notable for the honesty of its glasses and scarf, which a flattering painter might have omitted. Ames's name does not rank with Malbone's, and this picture is an ordinary member of a vast class. Yet the plain, unvarnished look it seems to promise gives it a certain dignity, even if its quality is hard to rate."},"met:14997":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a lady in watercolor on ivory, about 7.8 by 6 centimeters, painted between 1840 and 1850 by the American miniaturist Daniel F. Ames. A vase appears beside the sitter, probably a prop or part of a decorative background. She is unnamed. Such small ivory miniatures were painted in watercolor with a fine brush, and the sitter's head and shoulders usually fill most of the picture.","i":"A vase or urn in a portrait of the 1840s belongs to the stock of parlor props: flowers and ornamental objects signaled domestic refinement and taste, and a middle-class sitter could borrow them from the painter's studio. Miniatures in this decade faced competition from the daguerreotype, and painters leaned on color and decoration to justify their higher price. The lady's name and story are unknown.","c":"A typical product of an American miniature painter at the moment when photography was reshaping the market: soft color, a pleasing prop and a polite likeness. Its quality is an open question. The vase may add charm or clutter, and which it does is hard to say; in either case the object is a modest witness to the last years of the miniature as a widespread portrait form."},"nga:1188":{"d":"A loose line of men, women, children, horses and dogs stretches across a dirt road lined with buildings that converge toward a tree-filled distance. At the center a man in a black jacket leans down from a black horse; to the right a man in a tall cream hat stands behind a dappled horse. Two men in front of a stone building smoke long white tobacco pipes, and others look out of its door and windows beneath a worn sign. At left a woman sits winding wool on a spinning frame. A church steeple rises under ivory clouds.","i":"A halt of travelers at a village inn was a favorite subject of the Haarlem painters who followed Adriaen van Ostade, Isack's elder brother and teacher. Nothing much happens, yet every figure has an occupation: smoking, spinning, tending horses, watching. Dutch viewers of the 1640s enjoyed recognizing rural types, here shown as ordinary neighbors rather than the coarse figures of earlier peasant pictures, with mild humor in the ragged clothes and the baby carried on a woman's back. It may carry no moral at all.","c":"Isack died at twenty-eight, and a picture dated 1645 shows how fast he developed: flanking buildings that converge toward a distant, tree-filled horizon, silvery handling of sky and cloud, and a chain of figures that never looks arranged. His brother's pictures are often crowded brown interiors; Isack's outdoor inn scenes brought openness and light to the genre and a new landscape feeling. It is among his most complete works."},"nga:72324":{"d":"A dirt road runs from the right edge into the distance, lined with steep-roofed houses, in a vertical composition. In the foreground two men, one in a golden-yellow vest and one in teal, unload barrels from a horse-drawn sled outside an inn, where a sign hangs above an open door and a birdcage below it. The brown horse lifts a hind foot; a white stork stands in a nest on the chimney. A boy with a long-necked vessel waits at a cellar hatch, a man on crutches walks toward the viewer, and children gather under a tattered awning. Peach-topped, lilac-gray clouds part for slivers of blue.","i":"Barrels delivered to the inn, a stork on the chimney, a lame man on the road and a crowd under the awning: the picture packs the traffic of a Dutch village street into a small panel. In emblem books the stork stood for family devotion, and in Dutch folk belief for good fortune; the man on crutches reminds viewers that charity was part of village life. The scene is observed, not preached, though a viewer could read a quiet contrast between work and idleness.","c":"Signed and dated 1645, a few years before Isack's early death, this is one of his most complete pictures: the receding row of buildings gives depth, the sky is lively, and the figures are individually observed. The foreground, where barrel, trap, pot and chickens pile up, is the busiest and weakest area. Its strength is observation; it adds little to the formula his brother had established, but it improves that formula's lightness."},"nga:163917":{"d":"A young woman stands in a stone arch, her body turned to one side and her eyes glancing out at the viewer with a slight smile. A blue-grey parrot with a red tail perches on her raised finger and lifts a morsel in one claw. She wears a golden-brown satin gown with white ruffles at the low neckline and sleeves, a string of pearls, and honey-blond curls. Behind her in deep shadow a boy in dark clothes holds a silver tray and looks up at her. At the left stands a tall iron birdcage; at the right a copper-coloured curtain hangs in a swag. A red-and-orange Turkish carpet is draped over the ledge in front, partly covering a carved inscription, and the signature and date, 1666, appear lower left.","i":"Caspar Netscher (1639–1684) trained with Gerard ter Borch and settled in The Hague in 1662, where he became known for glossy satins, rugs and fashionable ladies. The stone arch with a carpet on the sill is a device from the Leiden fine painters such as Gerrit Dou, whose small, highly finished pictures were prized by collectors. Parrots in Dutch art can suggest talkativeness, a lover's gift or simple affection for a pet, so the meaning here is open. The boy with the tray adds a note of household status.","c":"A display of finish. The satin, the pearls and above all the carpet are painted with a precision few contemporaries could match, and the black space behind the figures makes them glow like objects in a jewel box. What is missing is feeling: the woman's smile seems practised, the boy is a prop, and the parrot is a pretext. The composition is neat to the point of stiffness. It shows why Netscher was prized by wealthy buyers in The Hague and why later critics found him more skilful than searching."},"met:437218":{"d":"A man and a woman play cards in an interior, with a dog nearby, in an oil on canvas about 50 by 45 centimeters, painted about 1665. The museum's keywords record a woman, a man, an interior, playing cards, a dog and a game, so their poses, the room, the clothing and the colors are left unstated. Netscher, a pupil of Gerard ter Borch, was known for polished interior scenes of this kind, with fabrics rendered in exquisite detail.","i":"Card games in Dutch painting are rarely just games. They stand for chance, for pleasure and for flirtation, and a card party for two could be a courtship scene in which hearts are at stake as well as stakes. Moralists warned that gaming led to idleness and vice, so viewers might read the scene as light entertainment or as a gentle caution. The dog is a traditional emblem of fidelity, or, in other contexts, of appetite.","c":"Netscher's refinement is his selling point: a smooth surface, delicate modeling and a convincing sense of fabric and light. He lacks the psychological subtlety of his teacher Ter Borch, whose silent exchanges of glances carry more meaning, and his figures can seem stiff. Still, this is a charming, well-made cabinet picture, and an excellent example of Dutch fine painting at the courtly end of the market."},"met:11614":{"d":"A woman portrayed in oil on a canvas of 86.4 by 68.6 centimeters, about 1830. The title gives her name, Catherine Brooks Hall, and a stylus appears among the things shown, an unusual accessory that points to writing or drawing. Beyond that the record says nothing about her pose, dress or setting, which are best read from the picture itself. It is a private portrait commission, made for display in a home.","i":"In portraits of the early nineteenth century a pen, pencil or stylus can signal literacy, correspondence or the accomplishments, such as drawing and writing, expected of an educated young woman. Shepard Alonzo Mount belonged to a Long Island family of artists and was the brother of the genre painter William Sidney Mount; he worked chiefly as a portraitist in New York and on Long Island.","c":"Mount's likenesses are straightforward and unfussy, and he lacks his brother William's gift for narrative and wit. The virtue of this kind of work is plainness: the sitter's face and any telling object, here the stylus, carry the meaning. It stands as a competent regional portrait of about 1830, interesting mainly for the sitter's accessory and for the glimpse it gives of a family of painters working in the shadow of the better-known one."},"met:436940":{"d":"A wide landscape, 116.2 by 193.0 centimeters, dated 1849, in which David appears with soldiers at a cave. The title names the Cave of Adullam, and the picture is a biblical scene staged in rugged scenery. It was painted by the Italian Giovanni Maldura; the record supplies no more about him or about what the figures are doing, so the details are best read from the canvas.","i":"In the first Book of Samuel, David, anointed king but hunted by the jealous Saul, takes refuge in the cave of Adullam, where his family and about four hundred men in distress or in debt gather to him. The episode marks his turn from shepherd to outlaw captain. A later episode there, in which three warriors fetch him water from Bethlehem and he pours it out rather than drink, was also painted; the title does not say which moment this is.","c":"Mid-nineteenth-century Italian painters, in the wake of Romanticism, liked Old Testament heroes in wild settings, a taste descended from Salvator Rosa's bandit-haunted rocks and caves. Whether Maldura makes the cave a convincing place, and the soldiers more than costume extras, is the question for the canvas. Little is recorded of the painter, and the picture is best taken as a representative piece of its time and its taste."},"met:437217":{"d":"A nearly square panel, 176.5 by 192.7 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in 1537. Moses and Aaron stand before Pharaoh, whose throne and scepter mark him as king, and a serpent appears among the subjects, the sign of the miracle in Exodus when Aaron's staff turned into a snake before the Egyptian ruler. The scene is more than biblical: the title calls it an allegory of the Dinteville family, a noble house of Champagne, so the story was meant to say something about that family.","i":"In Exodus, Moses and Aaron demand that Pharaoh let the Israelites go, and Aaron's rod becomes a serpent that swallows those of the Egyptian magicians. Sixteenth-century patrons sometimes mapped their own situation onto Old Testament stories, casting themselves as God's faithful against a tyrant. The Dintevilles were prominent in the France of Francis I; Jean de Dinteville is best known as the left-hand figure in Holbein's The Ambassadors of 1533. The exact application of the allegory is a matter of interpretation.","c":"A large, ambitious panel by a master known only by the name taken from this picture, it belongs to the years when French painters were absorbing Netherlandish and Italian ideas. Its chief value lies in what it shows about how a noble family used scripture to picture itself, which is rare and rather bold. The quality of the painting is harder to rate than the interest of the idea, and the attribution, Netherlandish or French, remains open."},"met:437014":{"d":"A nearly square panel, 116.5 by 110.8 centimeters, in tempera and gold on wood, about 1420 to 1430, showing the Virgin and Child with a donor, Pietro de' Lardi, presented to them by Saint Nicholas. A coat of arms is included, the donor's own sign of ownership. The gold ground lifts the group out of real space, as in other devotional pictures of the period, and the donor's inclusion makes it a commission for a particular person, not a stock devotional image.","i":"The picture follows a formula of its time: a patron saint, here Nicholas of Myra, the bishop whose gifts of gold to three poor girls made him a helper of the poor and of children, presents a donor to the Virgin and Child to ask their intercession. The coat of arms makes the commission public and permanent. Such pictures were made for chapels, family altars or houses; the donor usually appears smaller than the sacred figures, as a sign of humility.","c":"Master G.Z. is possibly Michele dai Carri, active in Ferrara from 1405 until his death in 1441, but the attribution is only a possibility. The panel belongs to the courtly International Gothic style of northern Italy around 1420: gold, rich surfaces and linear grace. Its composition is conventional; the interest is in the donor and the heraldry. As an example of Ferrarese painting before the Renaissance of Cosmè Tura, it is valuable."},"met:437001":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, 49.2 by 128.9 centimeters, in tempera on wood with embossed and gilded ornament, dated 1381 to 1382. The subject, given in the title, is the conquest of the kingdom of Naples by Charles of Durazzo, who took the crown in 1381 from Queen Joanna I. The picture includes battle, an army with flags and horses, and kings. Its long shape suggests a decorative object, perhaps the front of a chest, rather than an altar panel, although that is an inference.","i":"Charles of Durazzo, backed by Pope Urban VI, invaded Naples in 1381 and captured Queen Joanna, who was killed in 1382. At the time the Western Schism had split the papacy between Urban VI in Rome and Clement VII at Avignon, and the quarrel over Naples was part of it. Painting a conquest so soon afterward is unusual, since pictures of history usually showed the ancient or the legendary; this one would have praised the victor and recorded the political turn.","c":"In 1381 painters had few models for depicting a battle of the present day, and fell back on formulas for armies: ranks of riders, standards, a king on horseback. The interest lies in the idea and the date more than in any sophisticated space or drama. As a rare early record of political events in paint, it matters more as a document than as a picture."},"met:437002":{"d":"A small panel in tempera on wood, 39.7 by 47.6 centimeters, with embossed gilt and silver ornament and coats of arms. The title says it shows scenes from a legend, and the subjects recorded include a bedroom, an infant, men, women and horses, so several episodes are probably packed into the small field, perhaps with a birth or bedside moment among them. The heraldry marks the panel as made for a particular family, and the catalogue dates it to 1429.","i":"The legend is not identified in the title. Panels combining a story with the owners' arms were made for domestic settings, often to mark a marriage or birth, celebrating a family's lineage as well as telling a tale. The silver and gilt relief ornament was a luxury, made to catch candlelight. Without the legend's name, the picture is best read as a family emblem: the heraldry says who owned it, and the story gives it a moral or romantic gloss.","c":"Small narrative panels of this kind reward patient looking, because the painter must compress a whole story into a few inches and decorate it with metal relief. The date and attribution are uncertain, and the panel is best taken as a good example of Italian domestic painting rather than a high point. Its worth lies in craftsmanship and in the evidence it gives of how families used pictures to display their names."},"met:436299":{"d":"A gold-ground panel in tempera, dated 1394, showing the Coronation of the Virgin with angels and saints. Christ places the crown on his mother's head, making her Queen of Heaven; angels attend the pair, and saints gather around them. The gold background and the formal, hierarchical arrangement belong to the Italian altarpiece of the late fourteenth century. Whether this panel was part of a larger altarpiece is not recorded.","i":"The Coronation of the Virgin, in which Christ crowns his mother in heaven, is not told in the Gospels; it grew from the medieval belief in her Assumption and from the Golden Legend, and became a standard altarpiece subject because it shows Mary as queen and intercessor. The saints beside the heavenly court usually reflect the devotions of the church or patron that commissioned the work. The eye is led through a hierarchy of Christ, Mary, angels and saints, each in his or her place.","c":"Giovanni di Tano Fei is documented between 1384 and 1405, in an Italy where painters still worked in the shadow of Giotto's followers and the gold-ground altarpiece. A Coronation depends on jewel-like color against the metal and a carefully ordered hierarchy; its risk is repetition of formula. It is best taken as a dependable late Gothic work that does not push beyond its models."},"met:436920":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, 134.3 by 58.7 centimeters, with the Virgin holding the Christ Child against a gold ground, painted in tempera about 1360 to 1365. The proportions, much taller than wide, suggest that it was once a compartment of a larger altarpiece, a polyptych, rather than an independent picture. The medium line records that the paint layer was transferred from its original wood to a new support, a drastic conservation measure.","i":"Luca di Tommè worked in Siena, where gold-ground Madonnas were the staple of altarpieces and private devotion. The Virgin presents her son to the worshipper as the means of salvation. The gold is not a backdrop but a statement: the figures are shown in the light of heaven, outside time and place, as the Sienese tradition long preferred.","c":"Luca belonged to the generation after the Lorenzetti brothers, who died in the plague of 1348. Sienese painting after the plague is often described as turning conservative, valuing refined line, gilded surfaces and well-loved types over experiment, and this panel of the mid-1360s fits that picture. It is graceful rather than daring, a good example of the refined devotional image Siena produced in quantity."},"met:437012":{"d":"A long, low panel, 52.7 by 178.1 centimeters, in tempera with embossed and gilded ornament, dated 1474. The Queen of Sheba arrives at the court of King Solomon with a long train: the picture is filled with camels, horses, a crowd, carts and wagons. The long, shallow proportions are probably those of the front of a marriage chest (cassone) or similar furniture, and the raised, gilded details were meant to glitter in candlelight.","i":"In the Old Testament (1 Kings 10) the queen of Sheba travels to Jerusalem with camels bearing spices, gold and jewels to test the wise king with hard questions. Renaissance Italians liked the story as an image of a splendid state visit and, on marriage chests, as an emblem of wisdom meeting beauty and of a respectable union. Christian readers also took the queen's journey as the nations coming to Christ.","c":"The subject invites display, and a painter of such a panel must fill a long field with brightly dressed riders, exotic animals and architecture; success lies in the animation and rhythm of the procession. The risks are repetition and decorative flatness, and at this scale individual figures matter less than the whole. It is skilled furniture painting rather than a great picture, and it reflects the fashion of its date more than any personal vision."},"met:437832":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted in oil on a wood panel 81.9 by 60.6 centimeters, dated 1521. The record adds only that the picture is a portrait of a man, so what he wears and holds, and what lies behind him, are best read from the panel itself. In size and format it belongs to the portraits Florentine patrons commissioned around 1520 to record their family and rank, to be kept at home.","i":"Tommaso di Stefano Lunetti (1495 to 1564), called Tommaso Fiorentino, was a Florentine. By 1521 Raphael had died and Andrea del Sarto and the young Pontormo were the leading painters in Florence; portraits were turning from the strict profiles of the fifteenth century to a turned head, a direct gaze, and a calm that implied the sitter's character. A portrait without a name or attribute is a reminder that most were made for family, not for history.","c":"Tommaso was a lesser master in a city of giants, and a portrait of 1521 invites comparison with Andrea del Sarto and Pontormo, a test few of his contemporaries passed. What can be asked of it is clarity and honest likeness: whether the head is solidly built, the gaze alive, the hands and clothing economically handled. Without a sitter's name or attributes it must stand on those qualities alone."},"met:437029":{"d":"A large panel, 125.1 by 160.7 centimeters, dated 1499, that tells the legend of Saint Godelieve in several scenes within one space. Interior rooms, women and saints, a dining table and a wedding appear among its subjects, and the title promises miracles as well as her life. Telling a whole biography on one panel, with the same figures often reappearing in different corners, was a Netherlandish convention for saints' legends, here painted in oil on wood.","i":"Godelieve of Gistel, a Flemish noblewoman of the eleventh century, was married to Bertolf, a lord who despised her and, with his mother's help, mistreated her; according to her legend she was starved and then strangled and drowned on his orders in 1070. Her grave became a pilgrimage center, and her cult spread through Flanders. Narrative pictures such as this were meant to tell the story to pilgrims and to recall her patience under suffering.","c":"The wedding feast and household scenes give a Netherlandish painter the chance for the intimate domestic detail in which the school excelled: a gruesome story staged in ordinary rooms with carefully observed objects. The weaknesses are figures that can look doll-like and a crowded surface that makes the story hard to follow. The master is named only after this legend, and the panel is his best claim to attention."},"met:435659":{"d":"A panel about 75 by 58 centimetres, painted in oil on wood and dated 1515, shows the Virgin and Child with angels. The Met's keywords record a lute, angels and a bird, so the picture includes an angel musician and a bird. In such Madonnas the bird is often a goldfinch, which was said to have taken a thorn from Christ's crown and became a symbol of the Passion.","i":"Angel musicians in Madonna paintings bring the music of heaven to the scene and express the joy of the Incarnation; the lute, an instrument of gentle, intimate tone, gives the picture a private, devotional air. The goldfinch, according to legend, took a thorn from Christ's crown and was marked with a drop of his blood, which makes it a quiet reminder of the Passion in an image of motherly tenderness. The painter, called Bernardino da Genoa, is little known.","c":"A pleasant devotional picture whose charm lies in its details: the angel with the lute, the bird, the soft interplay of mother and child. It is the work of a minor painter, and it follows contemporary north Italian formulas without the invention of the major masters. The colours and faces are agreeable but conventional, and the picture would be unremarkable were it not for its musical and bird-like accents. A competent, sweet provincial Madonna."},"met:437011":{"d":"A gold-ground panel in tempera, 75.2 by 56.5 centimeters, showing the Virgin Mary with the Christ Child; the catalogue places it about the middle of the fifteenth century. It is the kind of picture made for private devotion at home or in a small chapel, in which mother and child are presented to the viewer rather than set within a story. The record does not describe their poses or clothes, which are best read from the picture itself.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the most commonly painted subject of fifteenth-century Italy: Mary as mother and as Theotokos, the bearer of God, and the Child as savior. The gold ground signals that the figures belong to heaven, even as painters of the 1440s were learning to give them solid bodies and believable space. The painter is known only as the Master of the Lanckoronski Annunciation, a name taken from another picture.","c":"A fifteenth-century Madonna is a hard subject on which to be original; the best examples achieve a human tenderness within a fixed formula. Whether this one does must be judged in the tilt of heads and the grip of hands, where a painter's observation of real mothers and children appears. The anonymous master leaves no biography, but the gold and tempera give the panel the jewel-like surface that later oil paintings lost."},"met:437139":{"d":"A miniature on vellum laid on card, dated 1664, showing a man at an easel painting a coat of arms in a cabinet of curiosities. A cabinet of curiosities was a room, or a piece of furniture, in which collectors displayed natural wonders and man-made rarities side by side, so the scene is a painter's studio set inside a collection. The objects around the painter are best read from the picture itself.","i":"Heraldry was a staple business for painters, who were hired to paint arms on shields, funeral escutcheons, coaches and glass, and to illuminate grants of arms. Here the craft is set in the most fashionable environment of the age: the collector's cabinet, which tried to hold the world in miniature. The picture can be read as being about collecting as much as about the painter, an image in which art, nature and rank appear together. The monogram FA names a painter whose full identity is unknown.","c":"Small, precise and slightly eccentric, the miniature is more valuable for its subject than for any display of power. It documents how a painter's workplace and a collector's cabinet looked in the 1660s, an uncommon theme, and the vellum support allows a fineness of detail that a larger oil would not. Its limits are those of a miniature: the scale restrains drama, and the unknown painter cannot be set against a known career."},"met:436109":{"d":"A miniature on ivory of Benjamin Franklin, copied from a painting by Jean-Baptiste Greuze of 1777, according to the title. The record lists the sitter and a man, and gives the medium as ivory; no size is recorded, so it is presumably small, as miniatures were. The museum gives no description of Franklin's dress or expression. The miniature is dated 1777, the same year as Greuze's original.","i":"In 1777 Franklin was seventy-one and living in France as America's envoy, seeking the alliance with Louis XVI that came in 1778. His plain dress and lack of a wig made him a sensation in Paris, where he was celebrated as a natural philosopher and a rustic sage, and his image was reproduced endlessly in prints, medals and miniatures. A miniature copy was a fashionable way to own him.","c":"A miniature copy cannot match the original's painterly force, but it is a clue to the popularity of the image. The quality of such a copy depends on the copyist's skill; Charles de Bréa, a French miniaturist, would have worked with the neat precision typical of his profession. As a piece of Franklin-mania it is a more eloquent historical object than a work of art."},"met:437152":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, 214.6 by 57.8 centimeters, in tempera and gold on wood, dated 1499. The Virgin and Child sit enthroned, and around them are scenes from the Life of the Virgin, among them the Nativity, the Adoration of the Magi, the Resurrection and the Coronation of the Virgin, with angels and saints. The shape, more than two meters tall, is probably that of a section of a Spanish retable, the multi-tiered altarpiece that filled the east end of a church.","i":"Spanish retables of the late fifteenth century, especially in Aragon and Catalonia, combined enthroned images with narrative compartments so that worshippers could follow the story of salvation with the eye. The Life of the Virgin was a favorite theme; the Resurrection and the Coronation carry the story to Christ's victory and Mary's glorification. The gold ground retains Gothic practice at a time when painters farther north were already using oil.","c":"Late Gothic Spanish retable painters worked with vivid color and an appetite for ornament, and this panel is of interest as a witness to that tradition in its last decades before the Renaissance arrived. The painter is known only as the Morata Master. The best parts of such work are usually the heads and brocades; the weakness is the repetition of stock poses across a long sequence of scenes."},"met:437238":{"d":"A still life of fruit and game, painted in oil on a wood panel 83.5 by 118.7 centimeters in 1645. Grapes and other fruit lie with a bird and a rabbit, so the hunter's bag and the orchard's harvest meet on one surface. It is a showpiece of Flemish abundance, sized for a dining room or a gentleman's gallery, and the broad horizontal proportions suit a display laid out in a row.","i":"In Flemish painting, game and fruit together signal the good life of the landowner, whose privilege it was to hunt, and the plenty of nature. Moralists could also see a reminder that pleasures of the table pass: dead animals and ripe fruit are both at their peak and about to decay. The specialty flourished in the tradition of Frans Snyders and Jan Fyt, whose game pieces set the standard.","c":"A competent specimen of a well-established type, tested by the contrast of soft fur and feather against the tight skins of grapes and other fruit. Pieter van Overschee worked in a market whose leading names, Snyders and Fyt, set the bar high, and a follower rarely matches their brio. The size is handsome, and the picture's chief appeal is its generous display, not any individual note of invention."},"met:437785":{"d":"A nude sea nymph is the subject of this enamel painting, made about 1760 by the German enamel painter Carl Friedrich Thienpondt. The museum's keywords record a nude and a nymph, so her pose, any companions, the sea, her attributes and the colors are left unstated. Enamel painting uses powdered colored glass fired on a metal plaque, and a mythological nude of this kind was a favorite subject for plaques set in snuffboxes, watch cases and cabinet frames.","i":"Sea nymphs, the Nereids and their kin, are the gentle spirits of the ocean in Greek myth, shown with tritons and dolphins in the train of Neptune or Venus. In Rococo art they were an excuse for sensuous female nudes within a safely classical frame. Plaques of this kind, with their gleaming, permanent colors, flattered owners who wanted a refined, learned decoration for a box or a cabinet.","c":"Enamel gives colors an intensity and polish that oil cannot match, and the demands of firing, in which each color must be applied and fired in turn, make any fine example a technical achievement. Thienpondt's nymph belongs to the Rococo taste for soft, pearly flesh and graceful poses; the surface is likely brilliant but the character generic. It shows more craft than invention, and is best admired as a jewel-like object."},"met:437786":{"d":"The goddess Diana is shown nude in this enamel painting, made about 1760 by Carl Friedrich Thienpondt. The museum's keywords record Diana and a nude, so her pose, her attributes, such as a bow, a quiver and a crescent moon in her hair, the setting and the colors are left unstated. It is a small enamel plaque, painted in glass colors fired in a kiln on a metal ground, with the glassy gloss that enamel gives.","i":"Diana, the Roman goddess of the hunt and the moon, was a virgin who tolerated no male gaze; the best-known story of her shows the hunter Actaeon stumbling on her bathing and being turned into a stag as punishment. Nude Dianas allowed painters to show the female body with a classical alibi. The crescent moon on her brow, the bow and quiver and the hounds usually identify her, though the museum does not say which appear here.","c":"A nude Diana in enamel lives or dies by finish and surface, and the medium offers a hard, glowing perfection that suits a goddess. The pose and type are likely conventional, drawn from the large stock of Rococo mythological painting, and individuality is limited. Still, as a small precious object of the mid-eighteenth century, it shows the craftsmanship of the period at its most refined."},"met:437632":{"d":"A double portrait on ivory, painted about 1810 to 1815 by Heinrich Franz Schalck. The title names two men, Joseph and Karl August von Klein, and a musical instrument and a dog share the picture with them, which suggests a more elaborate composition than a plain pair of heads. Miniature portraits were meant to be held and looked at closely, and the extra objects give the viewer a small story about music and companionship to read.","i":"The 'von' in the name marks a noble or ennobled family. A pair of men shown with an instrument and a dog speaks of the ideals of cultivated German households in the Napoleonic years: music-making, loyalty and affection among brothers or friends, and the pleasures of home. The two are presumably related, although the title does not say so. Schalck, who lived from 1791 to 1832, was a young man when he painted it.","c":"A double portrait with attributes is a more ambitious conception than most miniatures, which are single heads, and the instrument and the dog hint at a narrative of friendship. Whether Schalck can manage the interplay of two figures in so small a space is the test; his death in his early forties leaves a modest body of work for comparison. The intimate, domestic tone is one that would soon be called Biedermeier."},"met:437594":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a young man of the Daillon du Lude family, painted on vellum stretched over copper and dated 1628. The title gives his life dates as about 1602 to 1676, which would make him about twenty-six. Vellum, the fine prepared skin of a calf, was the standard ground for miniatures until ivory took over early in the eighteenth century, and here it is stretched over copper, presumably to keep it flat and firm.","i":"The Daillon du Lude took their title from the château du Lude on the Loire, and a portrait miniature of 1628 would have been a private object, given to a fiancée or patron, or kept in a case or worn. At that date France was ruled by Louis XIII with Cardinal Richelieu as his minister, and a young man of rank would be shown in the fashions of the court.","c":"Jacques Saillant is not a well-documented name, and the miniature is best seen as a competent example of French vellum portraiture of the 1620s, before enamel miniatures, led by Jean Petitot, became the fashion. In a medium that favors clear outline and a flat ground, the life of the likeness rests in the eyes and mouth, and that is where a viewer can test it."},"met:437595":{"d":"A man in a doublet and collar, with a ribbon, painted on vellum stretched over wood about 1628. Fruit and a flower also appear, so the miniature includes more than the sitter's head and shoulders; they may be part of a decorative border or something he holds, which the record does not say. Vellum, a thin prepared animal skin, was the support for most miniatures before ivory. The sitter is unnamed.","i":"In the 1620s a fashionable man wore a close-fitting doublet with a stiff or falling collar, and a ribbon could be a fashionable accessory, a lover's favor or a badge of office. Fruit and flowers in portraits can be read as emblems of youth, courtship or the passing of time; their meaning here is uncertain. The miniature descends from manuscript illumination, a technique of fine brushwork on prepared skin, and it was a private, collectible object.","c":"The vellum support and small scale demand fine, even strokes, and the best miniatures of the 1620s catch an expression in a few touches around the eyes and mouth. Saillant is a minor and poorly documented name, so the likeness has to be judged on its own. The fruit and flower are the unusual touch, giving the picture a hint of story that a plain bust would lack."},"met:437627":{"d":"A river winds through a landscape with trees in this small work on paper, with a woman, a boy, a man and a dog among the figures recorded. The museum gives no measurements or further description, so the exact scene, the weather and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. It belongs to the category of landscape miniature, a cabinet picture meant to be held and looked at closely, in the pastoral spirit of French Rococo painting.","i":"Pastoral landscapes with elegant figures were a French Rococo taste, fed by painters such as Boucher and Fragonard, who showed an idealised countryside where courtiers and peasants alike enjoyed leisure. Small works on paper by artists such as Savignac were collected in albums and displayed in cabinets, giving pleasure on a scale suited to a drawing room. The date, about 1766 to 1772, puts it in the Rococo's late phase. Mary Clark Thompson bequeathed it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1923.","c":"A pleasant, small-scale piece in a vein that has little ambition beyond charm. Savignac's kind of landscape works through the gentle placing of tiny figures and trees in a lit space, and the viewer must lean in to enjoy it. It is unlikely to have the depth or wit of Fragonard, whose pastoral sketches it recalls, but within its narrow aim it is polished, sweet-tempered and typical of its period."},"met:437628":{"d":"A picnic in a wooded landscape is the subject of this small work on paper, with a woman, a man and a meal among the elements the museum records. No measurements or further description are given, so the number of figures, their costumes and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. With A River Landscape, which has the same date and bequest, it may form a pair of small Rococo scenes of rural leisure, in which elegantly dressed people enjoy the open air.","i":"The picnic, or fête champêtre, the outdoor party of elegant company, was a favourite theme of French Rococo painting in the age of Watteau, Lancret and Fragonard, an image of leisure and flirtation set in a gentle, idealised nature. In the 1760s and 1770s the new taste for the countryside, encouraged by Rousseau, gave it a fresh appeal. Savignac's small work belongs to that tradition, and may have been made to be kept in an album or a cabinet.","c":"A small, sweet-tempered piece: the theme is ideal for a delicate touch, and a miniature can capture a picnic's play of light and conversation with real grace. The risk is sameness, since the fête champêtre was handled by many artists, and this one, without further details to judge, is likely to be a pleasant echo of larger masters. As a decorative cabinet picture of the 1760s or 1770s, it is easy to enjoy."},"met:436633":{"d":"A profile portrait of Ali Pasha, the Ottoman governor of Ioannina, painted on ivory in 1822, the year of his death. The title gives his birth as about 1741. A profile gives a clean outline of brow, nose and mouth, a format long used for medals and cameos. The picture is a miniature by Jacob Ritter von Hartmann, and the sitter's dress and expression are best read from the ivory itself.","i":"Ali Pasha ruled Ioannina and much of Epirus and southern Albania in the early nineteenth century, playing the Ottoman sultan, the British and the French against one another. Western travelers, Lord Byron among them in 1809, made him a figure of romantic fascination. In 1820 the sultan declared him a rebel, and he was killed early in 1822, so a portrait dated that year is either a late likeness or a record made as news of his death spread.","c":"A profile is a stringent form: with no frontal eyes to engage, character must come from the line of brow and nose and the set of the mouth. The interest of the work lies largely in its subject, and Hartmann's skill can be judged by whether the face has the shrewdness Western visitors reported. As a document of Europe's fascination with a controversial Ottoman figure, it counts for more than as a display of technique."},"met:436919":{"d":"A man is portrayed in a miniature painted on ivory in 1795 by Annibal Christian Loutherbourg, and the title adds that he is said to be James Madison (1751-1836), later the fourth president of the United States. The recorded motifs are the man and a portrait. Miniatures of this kind were small enough to hold in the hand or set into a locket or case.","i":"In 1795 Madison was a congressman from Virginia sitting in Philadelphia, then the national capital, and a newly married man; he had wed Dolley Payne Todd in 1794. A miniature of him from that moment could have been made as a keepsake for family or admirers. 'Said to be' is a cautious label: it records a tradition that cannot be proved from the miniature itself, as many likenesses of public men are of uncertain identity.","c":"A miniature under a doubtful name is judged by its painting: the clean modeling of the face, the fidelity of the dress and the glow of ivory. Loutherbourg is far less known than the great French miniaturists of the time, Isabey and Augustin, and the doubtful identity limits the picture's value as a document. Still, the object keeps the quiet intimacy of the genre, and a possible link to a founding father adds interest."},"nga:92234":{"d":"Dark nickel-gray clouds ring a patch of bright blue sky above a river and forest in this wide landscape, their tops creamy white in the sun and steel gray beneath. The horizon lies about a third of the way up the canvas. A tall, deep-green tree rises almost to the top edge right of center, with more trees along a spit of land beside olive-green water. Wooden rowboats are drawn up at a low dock, one holding a person with a long rod; a woman leading a child looks back over her shoulder at the clouds. The artist signed and dated it in red, lower left: C. TROYON 1849.","i":"A coming storm and a few anglers and walkers: the picture belongs to a long tradition of weather landscapes that remind viewers of their small place under the sky, and the woman's backward glance makes the weather the day's one event. Troyon painted it in 1849, after a trip to Holland in 1847 sharpened his admiration for Dutch landscape, and at a time when he moved among the Barbizon painters of the Fontainebleau forest.","c":"A well-built landscape in which the dark sky does the work: white cloud tops against steel-gray bases give drama, and the figures are small enough not to compete. Troyon would soon be known chiefly for cattle pieces; the human incident here is slight, and the dark masses of tree and cloud verge on the formulaic. Still, the handling of the sky, which accounts for most of the canvas's life, shows his command of weather."},"met:437847":{"d":"An upright forest landscape, about 58 by 48 centimeters, built around a road through woods. A road, trees and a forest make up the whole subject, so the picture is best described as a view of a wooded path and not as a story. It was painted in oil on canvas in the mid-1840s by Constant Troyon, and it belongs to the manner of the Barbizon painters, who treated an ordinary stretch of woodland as a subject worth painting for its own sake.","i":"Troyon, like other painters of the Barbizon group, worked in the forests near Paris, replacing mythological and historical scenes with views of ordinary country roads. A road through trees invites the viewer in without naming a destination, and the subject recalls the Dutch seventeenth-century woodland scenes of Ruisdael and Hobbema that the Barbizon painters admired. Troyon would later be known mainly for animals, after a visit to Holland in 1847.","c":"Troyon is judged more by his cattle than his woods, and a forest road from before that turn is a quieter, more conventional thing. Barbizon colleagues such as Rousseau and Diaz pushed the forest subject toward greater intensity, and Troyon here plays it safer. It is a respectable picture whose chief interest is as an earlier phase of a painter who found his real subject in animals."},"met:437846":{"d":"A small upright picture, about 41 by 33 centimeters, in which a woman, a dog, a donkey and sheep are the figures. The title and the cast point to a rural scene of a countrywoman taking her animals to market, the kind of subject Troyon painted once he was known for animals. Painted in oil on canvas in 1860, it is a modest cabinet picture rather than a large Salon canvas, by the French Barbizon painter Constant Troyon.","i":"Troyon's late pictures of animals and country people appealed to the Paris public and to collectors at home and abroad who liked the idea of an unspoiled rural life. Taking produce and animals to market was an ordinary daily scene that the Barbizon painters raised into a subject worthy of painting. The picture stands for a quiet rural order rather than for any specific story, and the title is its only narrative.","c":"Troyon's reputation rests on animals, and the donkey, sheep and dog put him on his best ground. At this small size the effect is likely to be homely and affectionate rather than grand, and at his best he paints animals as members of a working landscape, without the sentimentality that spoiled many of his followers. A pleasant, professional picture from the last years of his career, though without the weight of his larger cattle pieces."},"nga:178267":{"d":"A rural scene, 26 by 35 centimeters, on a wood panel, centered on a pale dirt road that curves gently into the distance. A woman with light-gray hair and a long earth-toned dress holds the hand of a small child in darker clothes, the two seen from behind on a raised bank of the path. Tall dense trees with green and golden-yellow leaves rise at left; at right darker trees partly hide a simple slant-roofed building. A shallow pool in the foreground reflects the pale-blue and gray sky, where layered clouds let warm light through near a low sun. The wood grain shows through the paint.","i":"A woman and a child on a country road is a familiar motif of Barbizon painting, which presented the French countryside as a place of quiet daily life rather than of grand views; such figures give scale and a human note without telling a story. The title names no place, so the landscape is probably a general type rather than a recorded view, and the picture, catalogued as mid-nineteenth century, cannot be tied to a particular year.","c":"Small oil panels like this were often where Barbizon painters were freest. The handling, smooth in places and textured in others over visible grain, suggests a quick, confident hand at ease with the subject. It is modest in ambition and lacks the drama of Troyon's Approaching Storm, but the soft light, the pool in the foreground and the hazy air make an unforced, calm picture."},"met:15001":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man, painted in 1843 in watercolor on ivory and measuring only about 5 by 4 centimeters, smaller than a matchbox. The record lists a man and a portrait, with no sitter named, so features, costume and background are not described. The artist, Charles F. Berger, is recorded as active from 1841 into the 1890s, a long career that outlasted the heyday of the American miniature.","i":"By 1843, when this miniature was made, the daguerreotype had been public for only four years, and miniaturists were beginning to feel the competition. Many turned to photography themselves, coloring or retouching daguerreotypes, or painted miniatures from photographs. The small size and the plain title, Portrait of a Gentleman, suggest a keepsake for a family, an object whose meaning now belongs to someone who is not named.","c":"Miniatures at this date were measured against the camera, and the best answer was delicacy and color, which photography could not yet supply. Berger's career, which ran into the 1890s, shows how a miniaturist could survive the new technology, but both the sitter's identity and the picture's quality are obscure. Small, modest and typical of the vast, anonymous production that survives in family collections."},"nga:195502":{"d":"A white-and-blue striped bowl heaped with apples and grapes sits on a flat brown surface, ringed by more fruit, including a pear. The apples bring warm reds, yellows and pinks; the grapes and pear add green and dark purple. A vine with large green leaves reaches in from the left above and behind the arrangement, against a dark brown wall. The canvas, 43.5 by 68.6 centimeters, dates from about 1860 and is a cabinet-size still life made for the walls of a home.","i":"Fruit still lifes were among the most popular pictures in mid-nineteenth-century America. Their subject, abundant harvest, suited the parlor of a prosperous household, and the genre was often considered a suitable field for women artists. The Peale family of Philadelphia had made still life an American specialty since Raphaelle Peale's pictures of the 1810s; Harriet Cany Peale, who married Rembrandt Peale in 1840, painted in that tradition.","c":"The picture is plain-spoken: a clear arrangement, honest color and a dark ground that pushes the forms forward. It lacks the lyric daring of Raphaelle Peale's best work and the showy abundance of Dutch banquet pictures, but it is a pleasing, well-organized canvas by a woman who has received less attention than the men of her family. The vine adds a graceful sweep to an otherwise static group."},"met:12789":{"d":"A portrait miniature, 11.4 by 8.3 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1900, of an older man in a necktie and glasses. Both are signs of a modern sitter: spectacles worn openly and a necktie in place of the stock or cravat of earlier miniatures. The title gives his name as Parke Godwin, presumably the New York editor and author (1816 to 1904), who was then in his eighties.","i":"Parke Godwin was an editor of the New York Evening Post, married the daughter of its editor William Cullen Bryant, wrote Bryant's biography, and was an early American advocate of the French socialist Charles Fourier. A miniature in 1900 was a deliberate choice: photography had killed the trade decades earlier, but around 1900 a revival, led largely by women artists and fostered by the American Society of Miniature Painters, founded in 1899, made it a refined alternative to the camera.","c":"By 1900 the miniature survived as an art of refined portraiture rather than necessity, and Theodora Thayer, who died in her thirties, was one of a generation of women who made it fashionable again. At 11.4 by 8.3 centimeters it is larger than the old pocket miniatures, giving room for the aged face and the detail of glasses; its worth lies in its directness and in modern dress rendered in a medium made for lace and powdered hair."},"met:13121":{"d":"A view of the entrance hall of the Metropolitan Museum's earlier home, a mansion on West Fourteenth Street, with a woman and sculpture in the interior. This small oil on wood, about 31 by 41 cm, dates from about 1881, soon after the museum moved to its present site in Central Park in 1880. It is a picture of a building the museum had just left, painted as a record.","i":"In its first years the Metropolitan Museum showed plaster casts, a few old master paintings and antiquities, in rooms that had been made for a private house. A picture like this shows the museum as a new civic institution in which women and families could spend leisure time, a novelty of the 1870s. The sculpture in the hall is a reminder that early museum-goers came to see casts and statues as much as paintings.","c":"As a painting this is modest, a small panel in a competent hand, with a clear sense of space and a fondness for light falling in a public room. The interest is historical: it preserves the look of an institution at the moment it ceased to be a temporary venture. The woman visitor stands in for all later visitors. It is a minor picture of unusual documentary value."},"met:13124":{"d":"An interior view of the galleries of the Metropolitan Museum's earlier home on West Fourteenth Street, with a woman visitor among the figures. The oil on canvas, about 61 by 51 cm, is dated 1881. The museum bought the picture in 1895, when memory of its first homes was still fresh. It is a companion in subject to Waller's smaller panel of the entrance hall, and the upright format and the single visitor make the room itself the subject.","i":"Painted a year after the museum's move to Central Park, the picture preserves the look of the galleries on Fourteenth Street, where visitors saw a mixed collection of paintings, casts and antiquities in rooms made for a private family, not a museum. The woman visitor suggests the audience the founders hoped to attract: a respectable public for whom looking at art was an educational pastime.","c":"A view of a museum is also a picture about looking, and Waller's decision to include a visitor makes the point. The upright canvas suggests a gallery receding in depth, which gives the subject its gentle drama. It is more document than invention, with the modest virtues of a genre picture painted for pleasure and record, and it gains from being the museum's own memory."},"met:16192":{"d":"A woman in profile, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1813; the miniature measures only 8.3 by 6.7 centimeters. Profile views were a favorite format of the period: they echo the classical cameo and the silhouette, and give a crisp outline of brow, nose and chin. The sitter is unnamed, and her dress and hair are best read from the ivory itself.","i":"William P. Sheys is known only through his working years, 1813 to 1821, which points to a short career in a trade that served customers who wanted an affordable, portable likeness. A woman's miniature of this date might be a gift to a husband or suitor, or a keepsake for children. The lack of a name makes it an emblem of its type: the ordinary woman of her day, remembered by those who loved her, rather than by history.","c":"Profile miniatures depend on line and on the clean sweep from forehead to chin. In this medium a bright ground and a delicate touch on the cheek do much of the work. Sheys's career is too short and little documented for the miniature to be judged against a body of work; it stands as a modest, competent example of the neoclassical profile fashion."},"met:15187":{"d":"A man's portrait on ivory, only 5.2 by 4.2 centimeters, dated 1821 and unnamed. At this size it is smaller than a playing card, made to be carried or kept in a small case. It comes eight years after William P. Sheys's larger miniature of a woman in profile, and the record gives no more detail, so the sitter's pose and dress are best read from the ivory.","i":"By 1821 men's portraits had shed the powder and lace of the 1780s; a dark coat, high collar and white stock or cravat were standard, a conservative uniform of respectability. A portrait this small could be worn on a chain, kept in a case or set into a locket. Sheys's dated works run from 1813 to 1821, which marks eight years of activity as far as the records show.","c":"In a miniature this small, a man's portrait stands or falls on the eyes and on the drawing of collar and cravat, where crisp edges give a sense of order. It is a modest object, and Sheys is too little documented for a firm view of his range. Its virtue is economy: a face, a little clothing and nothing superfluous."},"met:10521":{"d":"New York harbor is seen from the water, with ships on a choppy sea and the Battery and Castle Garden on the shore, in a small oil on canvas about 35 by 53 centimeters, dated 1858. Ships, wind-whipped waves and a harbor fill the view. The picture shows the city's gateway at a time when sail and steam crowded the harbor, and it belongs to the tradition of harbor portraits bought by shipowners and boosters.","i":"Castle Garden, a former fort at the tip of Manhattan, had been a concert hall, where Jenny Lind sang in 1850, and in 1855 became the landing depot for immigrants, the main entry point for newcomers until it closed in 1890 and Ellis Island took over. A view of it from the harbor is a view of the front door of America in the years of mid-century immigration.","c":"Little is documented about Copestick, and the picture should be judged as a harbor view in the manner of the professional ship-and-port painters of the day. If it follows the usual conventions, it is crisp in detail and clear in space, but without the poetry of Luminist painters like Fitz Henry Lane, who made harbors into visions of light. Its chief value lies in the record of a place and moment."},"met:12796":{"d":"Bruton Parish Church in Williamsburg, Virginia, stands among trees with men and women, a carriage and riders on horseback before it. The title sets the scene in the time of Lord Dunmore, the last royal governor, in the early 1770s, though the picture was painted in 1893. The canvas is about 46 by 69 centimeters, a modest wide format for a nostalgic costume piece.","i":"Lord Dunmore was Virginia's last royal governor, serving from 1771 and fleeing the colonial capital in 1775 as the Revolution began. In the 1890s, after the Centennial of 1876, Americans looked back at the colonial period with nostalgia and pride in what is called the Colonial Revival. Bruton Parish Church, completed in 1715, was a famous colonial building where Washington and Jefferson worshiped, and pictures like this one imagine a vanished world of gentry in their finery.","c":"Thompson was a skilled painter of historical genre, and this picture shows the virtues of the type: clear drawing, careful costume and a well-ordered scene. It trades on nostalgia rather than invention, and the figures are types more than individuals, but the building is painted with affection and the picture has the charm of a period fantasy. It is best read as evidence of the 1890s mood as much as of 1770s Virginia."},"met:10077":{"d":"A woman, a boy and a girl are grouped around a game in this group portrait of the Starr children, painted in 1835 in oil on canvas about 72 by 93 centimeters. The family is at play, so the children are not stiffly posed but occupied with a pastime, the kind of informal conversation piece that suited a domestic setting. The wide canvas gives the group space to arrange itself.","i":"Portraits of children at play grew popular in the United States in the 1830s as ideas about childhood changed: children were seen as innocent, affectionate and worth recording, not as small adults. A game suggested domestic harmony and the cultivation of leisure in a prosperous household. A group portrait of siblings was also a larger and more profitable commission for a portraitist than a single likeness.","c":"Andrews is a competent provincial portraitist rather than a polished one, and a group of children at play is the sort of subject that exposes the difference: the groupings can be stiff, the faces similar, the space uncertain. But the intention is warm, and the charm of the game, a sign of an easy domestic life, carries the picture. It stands with other American child portraits of the 1830s as a document of changing ideas of childhood."},"met:436220":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in a miniature painted on ivory by the French artist Augustin Dubourg (1758-1800). The museum gives no date or size and records only a woman and a portrait. As a miniature of the late eighteenth century it was probably meant to be worn or held, and such portraits of women often show a bust-length figure, with soft hair and a light gown or fichu against a pale background.","i":"Dubourg lived through the last years of the old regime and the whole Revolution, and the miniature was the intimate portrait of both periods, favored for its small size, its portability and its low cost compared with an oil. Women's miniatures marked engagement and marriage, kept in a locket or a snuffbox, and changing fashions, from the elaborate hair and silks of the 1780s to the simpler lines of the 1790s, are the best guide to their date. Dubourg died in 1800, in his early forties.","c":"With no date, sitter or size to anchor judgment, the miniature must stand on its painting: a small, careful likeness with the clear, cool tones French miniaturists of the 1790s favored. Dubourg is a minor name next to Jean-Baptiste Isabey and Jean-Baptiste Augustin, and the picture should be taken as a competent period piece rather than a masterwork. Its pleasure is its intimacy and the survival of an unknown woman's face."},"met:10890":{"d":"A portrait of the writer Mark Twain, painted in 1890 in oil on canvas and measuring about 102 by 82 centimeters. The record lists him, a man with a mustache, and a portrait, but the museum gives no description of his pose, clothing or the background. The painter, Charles Noel Flagg, was a Hartford artist, and Twain was then living in Hartford, Connecticut. The painting was given to the museum in 1917 by Ellen Earle Flagg, presumably the painter's widow.","i":"In 1890 Twain, born Samuel Clemens, was fifty-four and at the height of his fame, with Huckleberry Finn behind him and A Connecticut Yankee in King Arthur's Court newly published. Already a celebrity in the age of photography, he sat for painters and photographers often. His bushy mustache, listed in the record, was by then his trademark. The portrait records him in his Hartford years, before bankruptcy and travel changed his life.","c":"Twain's famous face, with its bushy mustache and shock of hair, is a gift to a portraitist, and any competent painter will produce a recognizable likeness. The question is whether the painter finds the humor and the grief that made him more than an image. Flagg is not a major name, and his portrait is best valued as a document: Twain at fifty-four, painted by a local artist."},"met:10492":{"d":"A busy harbour scene viewed from Bedloe's Island, the small island in New York's upper bay, has ships and the harbour among the subjects the museum records. The canvas is about 60 by 86 centimetres and is dated 1864 or 1866, and the description gives no further detail, so the particular vessels, the skyline and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. Bedloe's Island, later renamed Liberty Island, did not have the Statue of Liberty until 1886; in the 1860s it held a fortress.","i":"In the 1860s New York harbour was the busiest port in the United States, where sailing ships, steamers, ferries and tugs jostled, and views of it were sold to merchants, shipowners and residents proud of the city's commerce. Coates was a painter of ships and harbours active from 1837 to 1872. The picture is part of the Met's Arnold Collection of New York prints, maps and pictures, assembled by a collector who valued such images for what they recorded of the city's growth.","c":"A document more than a work of art: such harbour views are valued for their accuracy about vessels and place, and the pleasure of the picture lies in the detailing of ships, flags and water against a vanished skyline. Without a close look at the painting one cannot assess the brush, but the genre tends towards the carefully descriptive rather than the atmospheric. It is an honest, workmanlike view, valuable above all as history."},"met:436924":{"d":"A man is portrayed in this French miniature on ivory, dated 1827, by Ferdinand Machéra. The title does not name him, so he is known only as a man. As in other miniatures of the period, the face is the center of attention, and the portrait would have been small enough to be held in the hand, worn as a keepsake, or kept in a case.","i":"Under the Restoration monarchy of the 1820s, the portrait miniature remained a fashionable and affordable way to commemorate a person, and Paris had many professional miniaturists. Such portraits were given to wives and fiancées, to children and friends, and kept as mementos of absent or dead relatives. The anonymity of the title is a reminder of how many such small likenesses have lost their sitters' names.","c":"A miniature on ivory demands fine control, with skin built from tiny strokes and the white of the ivory used for highlights, and Machéra's competence in the craft is typical of the professional trade. The result is polished and precise, but the portraits of the period tend toward a flattering sameness. Without a name, this one survives mainly as a well-made example of a vanished art."},"met:11448":{"d":"A small oil on panel, 18.7 by 15.6 centimeters, painted in 1863 by Henry Augustus Loop. The sitter is the sculptor John Quincy Adams Ward (1830 to 1910), then thirty-three. The record gives only a man and a portrait, so his pose, dress and the background are best read from the panel. At this scale it reads as an informal portrait of a colleague, not a formal commission.","i":"Ward became the leading American sculptor of his generation: The Indian Hunter, modeled by 1860, later the statues of Washington at Federal Hall, Shakespeare in Central Park and Henry Ward Beecher made him a defining figure of American public monuments. The picture shows him early in that career, in the New York art world where painters, sculptors and patrons met at the National Academy of Design.","c":"Loop is not a name that carries the weight of his sitter's, and the portrait's interest is partly that of association: the lasting reputation belongs to Ward. The small scale favors an intimate, frank likeness; whether it also has depth of character is for the face to say. As a record of a major American sculptor at thirty-three, painted by a contemporary, it deserves its place."},"met:10858":{"d":"A steam towboat under the American flag, painted in oil on canvas 100.3 by 127.0 centimeters in 1852. The vessel is shown with a mast, a flag of the United States, and a chimney sending up smoke. The picture belongs to the genre of the ship portrait, a record of one particular vessel, usually commissioned by its owner or captain, and painted with attention to hull, rigging and flag.","i":"Towboats pulled sailing vessels and barges through harbors and rivers, a vital trade in mid-nineteenth-century America. A ship portrait advertised a vessel's power and its owner's pride; the national flag and the plume of smoke say as much about ambition in the age of steam as about this one boat. The name Conqueror, a boast, fits the assertive naming of steam vessels in the period.","c":"Ship portraits are judged by accuracy and by how convincingly the vessel sits on the water. James Guy Evans, active from about 1835 to 1860, is lightly documented, and this picture is valued for recording a working type, the towboat, that rarely received portraits, more than for any sophisticated seascape. The flag and smoke give the composition its energy."},"met:10353":{"d":"A street in lower Manhattan thronged with people, painted in oil on paper, 43.5 by 35.9 centimeters. The title fixes the moment exactly: half past two on October 13, 1857, in the weeks of the financial panic of that autumn. The picture shows a crowd, buildings and a city street, with Wall Street as the stage. Oil on paper is a medium typical of sketches, which suggests a study made quickly, though the record does not say what it was for.","i":"The Panic of 1857 followed the failure of the Ohio Life Insurance and Trust Company in August, and by mid-October banks in New York were suspending payments in coin; the crowd in Wall Street reflects depositors and speculators facing that. Naming the hour and date gives the picture the feel of a news report, and a financial crisis is a rare subject for painting, far from the battle or myth that usually filled history pictures.","c":"Cafferty, a New York painter, took on a modern urban subject here, an unusual and ambitious choice. The strength of the sketch is the pressure of the crowd: a mass of small figures conveying anxiety through density rather than individual drama. The risk in such a subject is that a crowd becomes a pattern and not a set of people, and a sketch on paper cannot fully resolve that. As a document it is valuable."},"met:15093":{"d":"A man, unnamed, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1840; the miniature is 6.8 by 5.5 centimeters. The record adds only that it is a portrait of a man, so his dress and bearing are best read from the ivory itself. James H. Kimberly is documented as active from 1834 to 1846, years that bracket the arrival of the daguerreotype, which spread quickly through cities in the early 1840s.","i":"The 1840 date places this miniature at a turning point. Painters like Kimberly had made a living from the demand for small, affordable likenesses; the daguerreotype, announced in 1839, offered a cheaper and faster one, and within a few years most miniaturists were competing with photographic studios. A portrait of this kind is a private souvenir of one man at one moment, now without the name that once made it personal.","c":"Kimberly's documented years, 1834 to 1846, coincide with the last flowering of the trade. The miniature's merit lies in the finish a good painter could give by hand: a crisp collar, a glow in the flesh, an eye with life. It cannot be set against a larger body of work, but as a representative piece from the last years of a profession it has a quiet interest."},"met:435719":{"d":"An unnamed woman painted on ivory in 1808, a miniature by the French painter Joseph Bordes. The record gives only a woman and a portrait, so her dress, hair and setting are best read from the picture itself. The date places it in the heyday of the Empire, when French miniatures were prized as presents between lovers and families and as keepsakes carried by officers on campaign.","i":"Women's portraits of these years generally show the Empire fashion, with a high waist and bare arms, a nod to ancient Greece and Rome. Jean-Baptiste Isabey, the leading miniaturist of Napoleon's court, set the pattern of the delicate head against a pale, clouded sky that many painters copied. Without a name, this is a likeness of a private person, valued by those who knew her rather than by history.","c":"Bordes is a little-documented painter, and a miniature stands or falls on the small things: the clarity of the eyes, the glow of skin through thin color, the fineness of hair and fabric. Within the French miniature school of the Empire, dominated by Isabey and Augustin, he belongs to the large second rank; that is no insult, since the second rank supplied most of the portraits that families actually owned."},"met:435720":{"d":"A second woman by Joseph Bordes, painted on ivory in 1812, four years after the portrait of 1808; the record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a woman and a portrait. Ivory sheets were cut thin, scraped smooth and backed with white so that thin washes of watercolor glowed through the translucent surface, which gave flesh its characteristic soft light.","i":"By 1812 Napoleon's empire was at its largest and the miniature was at the height of French fashion, with portraits exchanged by officers away on campaign and the families waiting at home. Such a keepsake could be set into a locket, a bracelet or a snuffbox lid, or kept in a case. The sitter's anonymity today is a loss that the original owner would not have understood: to them the face was unmistakable.","c":"As often with miniatures of this date, the pleasure is in the delicacy of execution: thin washes of color build the face, and the white of the ivory gives it light from within. Bordes is a modest talent, and this miniature is best valued for its surface, the luminous skin that thin color gains from the ivory beneath, rather than for any invention of pose or composition."},"met:435721":{"d":"An unnamed man, painted on a sheet of ivory set into card, about 1810, by the French miniaturist Joseph Bordes. The card mount presumably holds the thin ivory flat and readies the miniature for framing or a case, while the ivory's translucency lends the flesh its soft glow. Miniatures of this date were typically built from tiny strokes of watercolor. The record gives nothing more about the sitter's dress or expression, which are best read from the picture itself.","i":"Men's portraits of the Consulate and Empire years were as varied as the new society: soldiers in uniform, officials in coats with high collars, merchants in plain dark clothing. Without a name, this man is a type of his time, the educated Frenchman who could afford a likeness. A miniature of a man was often exchanged with a wife or fiancée, or given to a parent, and sometimes carried on campaign.","c":"Whether this miniature rises above routine depends on the eyes and mouth, where Bordes must give a plain subject some personality in a few touches. Men's miniatures of the Empire offer little decoration beyond a stiff collar and cravat, and the strongest are those that make a plain face compelling. Without a larger body of work to compare, Bordes remains a competent, little-known hand."},"met:15025":{"d":"A woman painted in watercolor on ivory in 1816, only 7.0 by 5.5 centimeters, by the French miniaturist Louis Antoine Collas. The year is the year after Napoleon's defeat at Waterloo, when France was returning to a quieter, more conservative taste under the restored Bourbons. The sitter is unnamed, and her dress and hair are best read from the ivory itself.","i":"Collas worked in France under the Restoration, when the miniature was still the favored private likeness for the nobility and the bourgeoisie alike. Women's fashions of 1816 still carried the Empire's high waist, and a lady of that year is a type: this miniature is a record of ordinary fashion as much as of a particular face. Such portraits were given at engagements and weddings, or kept in memory of the absent.","c":"A modest, charming object of the kind that made up the bulk of the trade. The test is the face: whether the eyes and mouth are alive, whether the skin tones are fused without the stippling becoming visible. A second miniature by Collas in the Met, dated 1827, shows him still active a decade later, but a single portrait of a nameless lady is hard to rank; it is skilled and pleasing, not remarkable."},"met:15987":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Eugenie Simon, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1827 by Louis Antoine Collas, 7.9 by 6.3 centimeters. The record names only a woman and a portrait, so her dress and setting are best read from the picture. By 1827 women's fashion was moving toward wide sleeves and elaborately dressed hair, and miniatures remained a prized private gift.","i":"A named sitter turns a miniature from a type into a person, although little else about Eugenie Simon is recorded here. Miniatures marked betrothals, weddings and family milestones, and served as remembrance when someone was far away or had died. The ivory gave the portrait a soft inner light that made a sitter seem alive.","c":"Collas's earlier miniature in the Met dates from 1816, and by 1827 the work should show the confidence of long practice: assured drawing of the features and a controlled, even finish. Its limits are those of the genre, a bounded format that favors polish over invention. Like most miniatures of its date it is a competent, graceful object whose appeal is personal rather than historical."},"met:10986":{"d":"A man, unnamed, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1785. At 3.5 by 2.2 centimeters it is smaller than a matchbox, a size made to be set in a locket, bracelet or brooch and worn against the body. Nathaniel Hancock is documented from 1785 to 1809; the record gives no further description of the sitter, whose dress and expression are best read from the ivory.","i":"In the 1780s the portrait miniature was at its most fashionable in Britain and its former colonies, and tiny likenesses were exchanged as tokens of love, friendship and mourning, sometimes with a lock of hair behind. This one is anonymous, so its meaning is that of its type: a private, portable face, meant for one pair of eyes and kept close.","c":"Skill in so small a space is partly a matter of discipline: thousands of tiny strokes build a face, and any slip shows. At 3.5 by 2.2 centimeters the picture has little room for anything but the head and a hint of dress, and the judgment is of the eyes and the modeling of the cheek. Hancock is lightly documented, and the miniature is best taken as a skilled example of its fashion."},"met:15076":{"d":"A woman identified as Mrs. Abijah Cheever, born Elizabeth Scott, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1795, 5.0 by 3.8 centimeters. She wears a fichu, the light scarf draped about the neck and shoulders, with pearls; the object also carries an inscription and an engraved element on a pendant, which suggests that the miniature was made as a worn keepsake and not simply a framed picture.","i":"Naming a married woman by her husband's name with her maiden name in brackets is a cataloging convention. A miniature mounted as a pendant, with an inscription, was a deliberate keepsake; such inscriptions often record a dedication or a death. The fichu and pearls are in keeping with the 1790s taste for simple, soft costume that followed the more elaborate dress of the earlier decade.","c":"Hancock's miniature does more than record a face: the pendant setting, the inscription and the pearls give it the character of a jewel and a memorial at once. At about 5 by 4 centimeters there is little space for modeling, so the details of fichu and pearls matter. It is a graceful, well-judged example of its type, though Hancock's career is too little documented to rank it among his works."},"met:11854":{"d":"A view of the Berks County Almshouse in Pennsylvania, painted in 1880 in oil and gold paint on a sheet of zinc, 82.9 by 101.6 centimeters. A farm, large buildings and an eagle appear, so the picture presents the institution as a working complex under the national emblem. Zinc is an unusual support for a picture: a hard, flat sheet of metal, durable, and possibly chosen for a commemorative image meant to hang in a public place.","i":"Almshouses, or county poorhouses, were run by local governments to shelter the poor, the sick and the elderly, and many operated working farms where residents did chores, which is why the picture shows farm buildings. A painting of the institution expressed civic pride in a well-run charity: order, productivity and the county's benevolence. The eagle adds a patriotic seal. John Rasmussen (1828 to 1895) painted views of farms, factories and institutions in and around Berks County.","c":"An example of American folk art at its most documentary: the zinc sheet, the gold paint, the neat array of buildings. It lacks academic perspective and softness, and its value lies in the plain pride of the record and its map-like clarity. Set against polished European easel pictures, it shows art made to record a local institution, probably for local officials or residents rather than for a collector."},"met:11083":{"d":"A view of the village of Poestenkill, in Rensselaer County, New York, painted in oil on a wood panel, 50.2 by 71.1 centimeters, about 1870. Houses, a church and a town appear in a landscape setting, so the picture is a portrait of a place: the dwellings and the church that formed the center of a rural community, set in the surrounding countryside.","i":"Joseph H. Hidley (1830 to 1872) is usually classed as a folk or self-taught painter of upstate New York. Pictures like this were made for neighbors who wanted to see their own village, church and homes, part of a wider taste for topographical views and house portraits in nineteenth-century America. The scene records how a small community liked to see itself: its church and houses set in order against the land.","c":"The charm of such village views lies in their frankness: a place painted as its inhabitants knew it, with little of the polish or atmosphere of the Hudson River School painters working not far away. Uncertain perspective and stiff figures are common in folk painting of this kind, and are part of its appeal. Hidley died at forty-two, and his small body of work is valued as local history as much as art."},"met:10088":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Elizabeth Humphrey, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1899, 8.3 by 6.0 centimeters. The record gives only a woman and a portrait, so her pose and dress are best read from the picture itself. By this date the miniature was no longer the everyday keepsake of the early 1800s but a prestige art, made by trained painters for customers who valued handwork over the camera.","i":"The year 1899 matters: it is the year the American Society of Miniature Painters was founded, at the height of a revival in which women artists played a leading role. The revival drew on collectors and artists who valued the luminous touch of watercolor on ivory against the mechanical quality of photography. A miniature at this date was a deliberate commission, not a necessity.","c":"Martha Susan Baker (1871 to 1911) was one of a cohort of young women who built careers on the miniature at the turn of the century, and the revival's style, with soft, atmospheric heads in place of the rigid profiles and flat grounds of the old tradition, suited the medium's luminosity. Baker is little documented, and this portrait is best taken as a typical, accomplished piece of the revival."},"met:13169":{"d":"A girl in profile, painted in watercolor on paper with additions of linen, about 1795, 7.3 by 6.0 centimeters. She wears a headband; the linen additions are probably a collar or trim, cut and attached to the paper to add texture to the flat painted figure. Painting on paper rather than ivory kept the cost low, and the small size made the picture easy to frame or pass on.","i":"Mary Way, a miniaturist of New London, Connecticut, made profile portraits in the 1790s for local families, and her paper-based, linen-trimmed miniatures are a distinctive regional variation on the fashion for profiles. The sitter, Mary House, was probably a local child. A profile miniature gave a family a likeness at an affordable price, in a style that echoed the silhouette and the classical cameo.","c":"The charm lies in the economy: a clean profile, a headband, a collar given real linen, and little else. It lacks the soft modeling of ivory miniatures by Edward Greene Malbone, the best American miniaturist of the day, but its directness and the touch of real cloth give it a lively, almost folk-art quality. Way is notable as a woman working professionally at a time when most American miniaturists were men."},"met:436233":{"d":"A woman painted on ivory about 1812 to 1814 by the French miniaturist Nicolas François Dun. Tradition says she is Madame Récamier (1777 to 1849), the celebrated Paris hostess and beauty, but the title's 'said to be' leaves the identification open. The date falls at the end of the Empire, when portraits on ivory were still the favored keepsake. The record gives only a woman and a portrait, so her dress and hair are best read from the miniature itself.","i":"Juliette Récamier presided over one of the great salons of Paris and was painted by Jacques-Louis David and by François Gérard, whose portraits made her a model of Empire elegance. In 1811 Napoleon exiled her from Paris for her friendship with Madame de Staël, and she lived in provincial France and, from 1813, Italy, returning in 1814. Portraits are sometimes labeled with her name on slender evidence, since a plain high-waisted gown and classical hair were common to many women.","c":"Dun, who lived from 1764 to 1832, belonged to the generation of French miniaturists formed before the Revolution and at work through the Empire. Who sits here may matter less than the quality of the handling: a good miniature of an unnamed woman is better than a mediocre one of a celebrity. The tradition of a famous name adds interest but should be treated with caution."},"met:10905":{"d":"Henry Clay, painted in oil on a small sheet of metal, possibly tin, 14.4 by 12.1 centimeters, about 1840 by Oliver Frazer (1808 to 1864). Metal is an unusual support for a portrait: rigid, smooth and durable, it takes oil paint without the weave of canvas showing. The record gives only a man and a portrait, so Clay's pose, dress and expression are best read from the picture itself. The Kentucky statesman was then in his early sixties.","i":"Around 1840 Clay was the dominant figure of the Whig Party, although the party passed him over that year and nominated William Henry Harrison; he won the nomination in 1844 and lost to James K. Polk. His admirers called him Harry of the West, and likenesses of him were in steady demand, from prints and medals to small portraits for the parlor. A portrait of this size could hang in a home as a mark of party loyalty and personal admiration.","c":"A sturdy, small-scale likeness of a famous man, valued as much for the sitter as for the painter. The metal gives a hard, even surface that suits crisp drawing better than soft atmosphere, and the picture's success rests on whether Frazer catches Clay's mobile, expressive face rather than a stiff public mask. Frazer is a minor name, and the painting is best taken as a modest, honest piece of political portraiture."},"met:10837":{"d":"Children grouped in a family portrait, painted in oil on a canvas 119.7 by 159.7 centimeters, about 1839. A girl and a boy are shown with a cat, and a hammer appears among the objects, a homely detail that gives the sitters something to do besides pose. The wide, horizontal format suits a group of siblings, and the size is large enough to hang above a mantel or sofa in a family parlor.","i":"The title names the Alling family, and the painter, Oliver Tarbell Eddy (1799 to 1868), turns a group portrait into a small domestic scene. American portraitists of the early nineteenth century often gave children a pet and a toy or tool, which flattered the parents' sense of a lively, happy household and spared the painter a row of identical stiff poses. A cat can stand for domestic comfort; a hammer, for a boy's play or his future work.","c":"Eddy's picture works through its details more than through sophistication: the cat and the hammer enliven what could have been a row of faces, and the generous canvas gives each child room. Pictures of this kind are often a little stiff in drawing and anatomy, yet they have a directness that polished portraits lack. It stands as a good example of the unaffected American style of the late 1830s."},"met:10948":{"d":"A self-portrait in watercolor on ivory, 7.6 by 6.2 centimeters, painted about 1805 by Thomas Gimbrede, who was born in France in 1781 and worked in America. The sitter is the artist himself, then in his early twenties. The record gives only a man and the self-portrait label, so his dress and glance are best read from the ivory. A miniature self-portrait was small enough to be given to a friend, a patron or a prospective sitter.","i":"Gimbrede (1781 to 1832) was a French-born engraver and miniaturist who came to New York around 1802 and later taught drawing at the Military Academy at West Point. A miniature self-portrait did several jobs at once: it was a gift to those close to him, a sample of skill for prospective clients, and a record of how he wished to be seen at the start of his American career, as a polished professional.","c":"Self-portraits by working miniaturists are often among their best pictures, since the sitter is always available for study and the painter has no client to flatter. Gimbrede's face should be judged for the alertness of its eye and the economy of its modeling. At this small size it is an advertisement as much as a record, and it gives the viewer a direct sense of the maker."},"met:437264":{"d":"An enamel miniature of Sir Joshua Reynolds (1723 to 1792), the first president of the Royal Academy, dated 1792, the year he died. Enamel is a different medium from watercolor on ivory: powdered colored glass is fused onto a metal plate in a kiln, and the result is a glossy, jewel-like surface that does not fade. The picture shows a man, an artist; his dress and pose are best read from the enamel itself.","i":"Thomas Peat is listed as born in 1791, so he cannot have painted Reynolds from life; the date of 1792 is probably that of the sitter's death, and the enamel is presumably a copy after an earlier portrait, perhaps one of Reynolds's own self-portraits. Enamel copies of famous portraits were a respectable specialty in Britain, led by Henry Bone, and they kept the likenesses of national figures in small, permanent form.","c":"The attraction of enamel is permanence and brilliance, and its demands are severe: each color must survive firing, so painters work in stages with repeated trips to the kiln. A copy depends on its original, and the question is how much of Reynolds's thoughtful, living presence survives the translation. As a memorial to the greatest English portraitist of his age, it has a fitting solidity."},"met:437907":{"d":"A woman in profile, painted on ivory about 1790 by a French painter known as Vincent. The title gives her name as Madame Ingouf; the record adds only a woman, a profile and a portrait, so her dress and hair are best read from the ivory itself. The profile view was a fashion of the age: it recalls the engraved gems and medals of antiquity, and it suited a time that admired Roman clarity.","i":"In the 1780s and 1790s French taste turned from the soft rococo to neoclassical severity, and profile portraits, modeled on ancient coins and cameos, expressed that change. Ingouf was also the name of a family of Parisian engravers, so the sitter may belong to that circle, though the record does not say so. A profile was quick to make from life and easy to reproduce as a print, which added to its popularity.","c":"Profiles leave no room for flattery of the eyes, so everything depends on the contour of brow, nose, lips and chin and on the quality of the line. Vincent is only a surname in the record, which makes any judgment of his range impossible; the miniature is best taken as a clear, well-made example of the neoclassical profile at the time of the Revolution."},"met:435661":{"d":"A woman painted on ivory about 1810 by the French miniaturist Vincent Bertrand. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a woman and a portrait, so her pose, dress and setting are best read from the ivory. The Met has a second portrait of a woman by Bertrand of the same date, and the two make a small group for comparison. Miniatures of the Empire years were often exchanged within families.","i":"French women's portraits of about 1810 are tied to the style of the Empire: a simple, high-waisted dress, hair dressed in classical curls or a plain knot, and an emphasis on a clear, calm face. Such a miniature could mark a betrothal or a marriage, or console a family during the long absence of a husband on military service. Nothing in the record identifies the sitter, so she stands for the many middle-class women of the Empire who sat for small likenesses.","c":"Bertrand is known here only by name, and a single anonymous portrait leaves little to judge except the basics: the clean modeling of the face, the clarity of the eyes and the confidence of the contour. As with most Empire miniatures, any individuality has to come from small touches. It is a competent, modest piece of its time."},"met:435662":{"d":"A woman in a bonnet or cap, with a neckerchief and a ribbon, painted on ivory about 1810 by Vincent Bertrand. A basket and a flower are also part of the picture, which makes it livelier than most plain bust portraits: she may be shown as a country woman or a flower seller, or simply in the pastoral costume that was fashionable in French portraits. The sitter's name is not recorded.","i":"Around 1800 there was a taste in France for pastoral costume and rustic imagery, inherited from the courtly idylls of the eighteenth century, when ladies played at being shepherdesses. A basket of flowers, a simple cap and a neckerchief evoke rural simplicity, and flowers long carried meanings of love and constancy. The picture could be a likeness in fancy dress or a fancy picture of a type; the record does not say which.","c":"A basket and a flower are the kind of accessories that test a miniaturist: tiny and accurate, but drawn with a lightness that keeps them from competing with the face. Whether Bertrand achieves that is the question for the ivory. Such costume pieces are charming but conventional, and with so little known of his range there is nothing to compare it with."},"met:11495":{"d":"A man smoking, with a duck and sheep nearby, painted in oil on a canvas 97.5 by 71.1 centimeters in 1879. The title, On the Old Sod, uses an emigrants' phrase for the homeland, which for Irish Americans meant Ireland. A vertical canvas of this size would hang in a parlor or gallery, and its rural scene offers a sentimental picture of the countryside left behind.","i":"After the Great Famine of the late 1840s, millions of Irish people emigrated, many to the United States, where pictures of Irish rural life appealed to a nostalgic audience. The old sod is the turf of home; a man at ease with sheep and a duck evokes an unhurried, pastoral Ireland that the artist's public could remember or imagine. Such pictures tend to be sentimental rather than documentary, softening the hard realities of rural poverty.","c":"In a picture like this, William Magrath (1838 to 1918) shows his skill in the careful, finished treatment of figures and animals, which satisfied the taste for anecdotal pictures in the 1870s. The risk is sentimentality: the peasant as picturesque, the countryside as an idyll. Within those limits it is a competent and humane example of nineteenth-century genre painting."},"met:435736":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in this French portrait miniature of 1832, painted on a thin sheet of ivory that has been laid on card for support. Only the barest facts are fixed: the sitter is unnamed, the artist is the miniaturist Étienne Bouchardy, and the date is 1832. Thin enough to let light reflect through the paint, ivory gave miniaturists a glowing surface for flesh tones, and the finished piece would have been framed under glass or set in a case.","i":"In France under the July Monarchy (1830 to 1848), a prosperous middle class bought portrait miniatures as keepsakes for engagements, weddings and absences, and women often exchanged them. Ivory gave a luminous skin tone, built up in tiny dots and hatched strokes of watercolor and gouache. The daguerreotype, announced in 1839, soon made such portraits seem expensive, which is why miniatures from the 1830s belong to the end of a long tradition.","c":"A good miniature turns on the eyes and mouth, set down with a brush of a few hairs, and on the delicate transition from warm cheek to pale ivory. Bouchardy's refined, conventional style meets the demands of the form without trying to exceed them. Without the sitter's name, the picture's value is as a sample of fashionable, careful French portraiture on the eve of the camera."},"met:435735":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the subject of this French miniature, painted on ivory in 1838 by Étienne Bouchardy. It dates from one of the last years before the daguerreotype began to replace such portraits, when a miniature was still the usual way to carry a likeness in a pocket, a locket or a small case. The sitter's face is the focus, as is usual for the form, and the title gives no more than her sex.","i":"Miniatures of the 1830s were often made as gifts between family members, as tokens of courtship, or as remembrances for those who traveled. A woman's portrait of this kind may have been a present for a husband or fiancé, or kept by her parents. The anonymity of the title is a reminder that many miniatures lose their identifying inscriptions when removed from their cases and frames.","c":"Bouchardy handles the form with competence, and the technique of ivory gives the likeness a translucent warmth that oil could not match. The style is conventional, flattering and smooth, in line with the taste of the period, and it makes no attempt to unsettle. As a surviving example of a trade that photography soon displaced, it has historical interest beyond its charm."},"met:436110":{"d":"A profile portrait on ivory of Marie-Thérèse-Charlotte (1778 to 1851), the daughter of Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette, dated 1795, the year she left the prison in which she had spent more than three years. The record names only a woman, a profile and the sitter, so her dress and hair are best read from the ivory. Profile portraits were a favored form in revolutionary France.","i":"The only member of her immediate family to survive the Revolution, she was imprisoned in the Temple in Paris from 1792; her father was guillotined in January 1793 and her mother in October, and her brother, the boy Louis XVII, died in the prison in 1795. She was released that December in an exchange and sent to Austria, later marrying her cousin, the Duke of Angoulême. For royalists she became a symbol of innocence and loyalty, and likenesses of her were prized.","c":"A profile concentrates attention on line, and for a figure laden with so much history the restraint is apt: no drama, just a young woman's outline. Jacques Joseph de Gault is little known, and the miniature's interest lies mainly in the sitter and the date. The sad ironies of her life lend the small image more weight than its size suggests."},"met:435636":{"d":"A woman painted on paper stretched over metal in 1822, a miniature by Rudolphe Bel, who is recorded as Swiss. Paper laid over a metal plate is a less usual support than ivory; it gives a smooth, firm surface at a lower cost. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a woman and a portrait, so her dress and pose are best read from the picture.","i":"Swiss miniaturists were active across Europe in the early nineteenth century, drawing on the Geneva tradition of enamel and miniature painting that grew up alongside the watch and jewelry trades. An 1822 portrait comes after the Napoleonic wars, when the middle classes of Europe, newly prosperous and traveling, commissioned small likenesses for exchange and remembrance. The lack of a name leaves her a type: a private face meant for a few eyes.","c":"Bel is a name known mainly from this object, so the judgment is of a single work: the paper-on-metal support gives a smooth ground, and a good painter could achieve a fine, even finish. Whether the face has life and the lace and hair are crisp is for the miniature to show. It is an unassuming, competent piece, interesting as an example of a less common technique."},"met:436844":{"d":"A man painted on ivory in 1801 by Jean-Baptiste Ponce Lambert, whose nationality the Met gives with a question mark as Swiss. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a man and a portrait, so his dress and bearing are best read from the miniature. The date falls in the first years of Napoleon's Consulate, when the fashions of the revolutionary decade were settling into a simpler, sober style.","i":"In 1801 French and Swiss men were shedding the powder, lace and bright silks of the old regime for dark coats, plain linen and natural hair, a style that proclaimed civic seriousness. Without a name this man represents that new bourgeois world: a merchant, official or professional of the Consulate. A man's miniature could be exchanged with a fiancée or given to family, and it was meant to hold the likeness against loss.","c":"Lambert is little documented, and a single miniature offers few clues to his range. The test of such a portrait of 1801 is whether the sober dress and plain background leave the face enough life; a good painter, with a plain coat and white cravat to work with, relies on the quality of the eye and the shadow under the jaw. It is best taken as a competent, unshowy piece of its moment."},"met:436855":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the subject of this French portrait miniature on ivory, painted around 1800 by Étienne Charles Le Guay. Dating from the years between the Revolution and the Empire, it belongs to the period when miniature painters in Paris found new clients among a society rebuilding its fashions and its wealth. The sitter's name is not recorded, and only the likeness remains.","i":"Around 1800 portrait miniatures were exchanged as love tokens, marks of friendship and memorials, and Paris had a flourishing school of miniaturists whose work was shown at the Salon. A woman of this date would probably have worn the high-waisted, light-colored dress and softly dressed hair of the neoclassical fashion that followed the Revolution. The portrait therefore records both a person and a moment of French taste.","c":"Le Guay belongs to the generation that carried the eighteenth-century taste for refined, smooth miniatures into the new century. The best of these ivories combine a delicate likeness with a luminous, enamel-like finish and exceptional care over tiny details. The charm is usually a matter of polish rather than originality, and a viewer should judge the piece by the quality of its small touches and its preservation."},"met:14400":{"d":"A man painted in watercolor on ivory about 1810, 7.0 by 5.6 centimeters, by Louis Francis DePaul Binsse (1774 to 1844). The title gives him no name, and the record adds only a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. The size is typical of the miniatures of the time: large enough to be worn on a chain or held in a case, small enough to be carried.","i":"Binsse's life, from 1774 to 1844, spanned the whole age of the miniature, from the powdered portraits of the late eighteenth century to the arrival of photography. A man's portrait of about 1810 would typically show a dark coat, high collar and white cravat, the uniform of the educated and prosperous. Miniatures like this circulated within families and between friends, marking engagements, journeys and absences.","c":"A competent, conventional miniature of a type produced in large numbers: the test is the face, where eyes and mouth must carry any life, and the finish of collar and coat, where a crisp edge shows practice. Binsse's name is little known today, and the work should be valued as a representative piece of the trade rather than a standout."},"met:435841":{"d":"A miniature on ivory of Baron Joseph Dominique Louis (1755 to 1837), the French financier and statesman, dated 1844 by Pierre Laurent Canon (1787 to 1852). Because the sitter died in 1837, the portrait is posthumous, made from an earlier likeness or from memory. The picture shows a man in a portrait; his dress and any decorations are best read from the ivory itself.","i":"Baron Louis began as a priest and magistrate before the Revolution, assisted Talleyrand at the Festival of the Federation in 1790, and later served as finance minister under Louis XVIII and again under Louis-Philippe. He had a reputation for financial discipline and is remembered for a maxim often attributed to him: sound politics make sound finances. A memorial miniature in 1844 recalls him as a pillar of French public credit.","c":"A posthumous miniature depends on borrowed material, and the usual fault is a face smoothed into a generalized, dignified mask. Canon's task was to keep some of the old statesman's sharpness; whether he did can be judged from the eyes and mouth. Even so, a miniature of a public figure made seven years after his death shows the persistence of the genre as a vehicle of civic memory."},"met:436067":{"d":"A man is portrayed in an enamel miniature of about 1780, identified by the Met as Joseph II, Emperor of Austria. Enamel is powdered glass fired onto a metal plate, which gives a hard, glossy surface and colors that do not fade. Pieces of this kind were small enough to be mounted in a snuffbox, ring or frame, and were made to be given, worn or kept as tokens of favor and loyalty.","i":"Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor from 1765 and sole ruler of the Habsburg lands after his mother Maria Theresa died in 1780, is remembered as an 'enlightened' reformer. He issued an edict of religious toleration, abolished serfdom in his lands and tried to modernize the state, though many reforms provoked resistance. Portraits of rulers circulated in miniature as gifts and signs of allegiance, so such likenesses reached far beyond the court.","c":"Enamel is an unforgiving medium: the painting is fired in stages, and there is little room for correction. D'Argent's miniature is therefore best admired for craft, smoothness and brilliance, not for psychology. Portraits of Joseph II tend toward formal polish, and the interest of a small piece like this lies in the technique rather than in any revelation of a man famous for restless energy."},"met:435758":{"d":"A woman seated at a harp, painted on ivory and dated 1823 by Joseph Marie Bouton (1768 to 1823), who is recorded as French, with a question mark. A ribbon, a ruffle, a stripe and a belt are listed among her clothes, so the picture gives some attention to dress as well as to the instrument. Portraits that show a sitter performing, not simply posing, were a special kind of miniature.","i":"The harp was the fashionable instrument for women in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries, praised for the graceful pose and the display of arm and hand it afforded. To be shown playing one signaled accomplishment, leisure and refined taste, qualities a family wished to advertise. Bouton died in the year of this miniature, so the picture may be among his last works.","c":"The challenge of a musical portrait is to suggest the instrument's size in a few centimeters and to keep the sitter's hands convincing; the harp's strings and frame can easily become a distraction. If Bouton has achieved a calm, believable pose, he has solved the problem well. It is more ambitious than a plain bust, and the subject gives the miniature a charm beyond the usual."},"met:15121":{"d":"A woman, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1848, in a miniature unusually large at 19.4 by 13.3 centimeters. The title, Holy Eyes, and the single woman recorded suggest an idealized sentimental picture rather than a portrait of a named sitter, though the record does not say so. Large miniatures of this period competed with oil paintings for wall space and showed what the medium could do at scale.","i":"The 1840s were the heyday of the sentimental ideal head, in which a female face, often with upturned eyes, expressed feeling, devotion or purity, and was popular both as a parlor picture and as a print for gift books. The word 'holy' suggests a devotional mood, perhaps tied to the piety and domestic virtues expected of the nineteenth-century woman. Thomas Story Officer (1810 to 1859) was a professional miniaturist.","c":"At nearly 20 centimeters tall this is a showpiece, designed to prove that watercolor on ivory could rival oil in a drawing room. The danger of the ideal head is sweetness without substance, and the interest here is in whether Officer gives the face real modeling and the eyes a convincing glow. It belongs to the last great moment of the large miniature before the daguerreotype swept the market."},"met:437100":{"d":"A small icon, 34.3 by 28.6 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil on a wood panel with a gold ground and dated 1724. Mary lies in the sleep the Eastern Church calls her dormition, with men and women, angels, a seraph and Christ gathered around, and a small figure, the soul, among the subjects recorded. A gold ground is the sign of an icon: the holy event takes place in the light of heaven, not in a room of the world.","i":"The Dormition, celebrated on 15 August, shows the apostles gathered, by tradition miraculously, around the Virgin's deathbed. In the usual form of the scene Christ stands behind the bier and holds her soul as a swaddled infant, a reversal of the Madonna and Child that says she is in his care. The calm, orderly arrangement expresses faith that death is a falling asleep and not an end. An icon like this was made for devotion in a church or home.","c":"Ioannes Mokos (1680 to 1724) worked in the post-Byzantine Greek tradition, which by his day often combined Byzantine iconography with Western oil technique, as the tempera and oil of the record suggest. Icons depend on fidelity to type, so originality shows in small things: the poise of the apostles, the clarity of the gold. A dated work, made in the year he died, is useful evidence of a tradition still lively in the early 1700s."},"met:437905":{"d":"A man, unnamed except for the initials JD, painted on ivory in 1790 by a French painter known as Villers. The initials are the only clue to his identity: a miniature of this kind might carry them on the back, on a case or within the picture. The record gives no more, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory itself. The year places him in the first full year of the Revolution.","i":"In 1790 French men's portraits were moving from the powdered wigs and embroidered silks of the old regime toward plainer coats and natural hair, a shift that mirrored the political change. A miniature with a monogram instead of a name was a private object: whoever owned it knew the man at once, and the initials were enough for those who knew him. Today they leave him half anonymous.","c":"The most telling test in a man's miniature of 1790 is whether the clothing and background can be kept simple while the face carries the life. Villers is only a surname in the record, and the work cannot be compared with his others; it stands as a neat example of the French miniature at the end of the eighteenth century. The initials add a small mystery to a conventional type."},"met:437904":{"d":"A woman wearing a fichu, the light triangular scarf worn about the shoulders and crossed at the breast, painted on ivory about 1790 by Villers. The record names no sitter and gives no more detail, so her hair and expression are best read from the picture. A fichu softened the neckline of the plain, natural dress that came into fashion in France in the 1780s, and it appears in many portraits of the decade.","i":"The fichu and the simple dress belong to a taste for naturalness that Rousseau's ideas and the court's pastoral games had made fashionable, and which the Revolution soon turned into a political style. A woman in such dress presented herself as sensible and unaffected. Without a name, she represents the French women of the 1790s, whose portraits were kept as private tokens, often for a husband or a child.","c":"A fichu is a test of delicacy: the thin, gauzy fabric has to be rendered with a few light strokes so that it neither blurs nor hardens. A convincing fichu usually signals a painter who knows how to suggest texture economically. Villers is a little-documented name, and the picture's attraction is its quiet elegance rather than any bold stroke."},"met:437166":{"d":"A hunter with his dog, painted on ivory in 1794 to 1795 by a French painter recorded as Mortier. The sitter is an unidentified man in a suit, shown as a sportsman, with the dog as his companion in the field. The record gives no more, so his gun, game or landscape, if any, are best read from the ivory. The date falls in the months after the fall of Robespierre.","i":"Hunting had been a noble privilege until the National Assembly opened it to all landowners in August 1789, so a hunter in 1794 or 1795 need not be an aristocrat; he could as well be a landowner, a farmer or a bourgeois sportsman. A portrait with a dog also flatters the sitter's character: the loyal companion hints at constancy and manly outdoor virtues. The man's anonymity leaves the picture a type of country gentleman.","c":"A sporting miniature gives a painter more than the usual head: a dog's coat, a gun, perhaps a landscape. The difficulty is scale, and the best pictures of this kind keep the animal small, accurate and subordinate. Mortier worked in the 1790s, a decade when a small portrait with a story, as this is, could reflect a social change that a plain bust never could."},"met:435575":{"d":"A miniature on ivory of Jérôme Bonaparte (1784 to 1860), Napoleon's youngest brother and king of Westphalia, dated 1813 by the Italian painter Giacomo Andreoli. The sitter is shown as a king; the record gives no further detail, so his uniform, orders and expression are best read from the ivory itself. The year was the last of his kingdom, which Napoleon had created for him six years earlier.","i":"Napoleon created the kingdom of Westphalia in 1807 and gave it to Jérôme, who held court at Kassel with a reputation for extravagance. In October 1813, after the defeat at Leipzig, the kingdom collapsed and he fled. A portrait of that year may therefore record a king at the moment he was about to lose his crown, though the miniature itself does not say so. Jérôme later returned to prominence in France and was made a marshal.","c":"A court miniature of a ruler is a question of formula: uniform, orders, a confident gaze. Andreoli is little known, and the interest here is mostly historical; whether the face has more than the polish expected of an official likeness is for the viewer to judge. A dated portrait of a king in his final year of power is a useful small document of the Napoleonic order."},"met:436860":{"d":"A man painted on ivory about 1810 by J. Lecourt, a French miniaturist known by an initial and a surname. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the picture. The date places it in the Empire, when a miniature was an everyday gift between families and friends.","i":"Empire men's portraits show the new society: officers, officials, lawyers and merchants, mostly in dark coats and white cravats, often with natural hair brushed forward in a fashion modeled on Roman portraits. A miniature could mark a promotion, a marriage or a departure for the army, and was meant to be kept close. The sitter's anonymity today is the common fate of such portraits once their owners' families lose track of them.","c":"Lecourt is a little-known name, and this miniature is judged on its basics: the poise of the head, the clarity of the eyes, a convincing collar. A quiet competence of this kind is what the trade required, and it supplied families with the likenesses they wanted. As a document of the faces of Empire France it has more value than as a work of invention."},"met:437142":{"d":"A troupe of traveling players performs for a crowd, painted on vellum laid on wood about 1630 by a Northern European artist who signs with the monogram JG. Actors, a crowd, a performance, a dog and a theater appear among its subjects: a street comedy in progress, with the audience as important as the stage. Miniature painting on vellum allowed a fine, detailed treatment of a busy scene on a very small scale.","i":"Itinerant troupes played in town squares, fairs and inns across Northern Europe in the early seventeenth century, performing farces, tragicomedies and acrobatics for modest payments. Painters liked the subject because a crowd and an improvised stage give a chance to show a range of types and expressions. Such scenes can be both lively observation and gentle moral comment on the pleasures of the crowd; the record does not say which was intended.","c":"Crowd scenes in miniature reward close looking, since the painter must manage many small figures without confusion, and the vellum permits fine detail and bright color. The monogrammist JG is unidentified, which keeps the picture from being set against a known career. As a record of popular entertainment of about 1630, painted with care, it is of more interest than most portrait miniatures of its date."},"met:436977":{"d":"Napoleon on horseback, painted on ivory in 1830 by the Italian miniaturist Luigi Marta (1790 to 1858). The emperor is shown mounted, a favorite format for ruler portraits since antiquity and one made famous for Napoleon by Jacques-Louis David's Napoleon Crossing the Alps. The date, nine years after Napoleon's death on Saint Helena in 1821, places the miniature in the years of the growing Napoleonic legend.","i":"After 1815, and especially after his death in 1821, images of Napoleon were collected by veterans and admirers, and the legend of the emperor grew in France and Italy, where he had been crowned king of Italy in Milan in 1805. A horseback portrait shows him as the commander and as an embodiment of historical will. The small ivory brought the hero into a private home, and enthusiasm for him grew further in the decade that followed.","c":"Equestrian Napoleons are among the most repeated images of the nineteenth century, and a miniature depends on its model, usually David's or one of its many prints. The skill lies in compressing a horse, a rider and a landscape into a few centimeters without losing the energy of the pose. Marta is a minor name, and the miniature is best seen as a loyal, small-scale tribute to a famous image."},"met:435845":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in this Italian miniature on ivory, dated 1829 and attributed to an artist recorded as F. Carbonara. The title does not name the sitter. Ivory miniatures were small enough for jewelry, lockets and gifts, and as is usual for the form the image concentrates on the face and upper body. Little is known of the painter beyond his initial and surname.","i":"Portrait miniatures were popular in Italy as elsewhere in Europe, and they were especially valued by travelers who carried likenesses of loved ones on long journeys. In the 1820s Italy was divided into many states and attracted visitors from Europe and America, who commissioned small portraits and souvenirs. The sitter here may have been local or foreign; the title leaves her identity open.","c":"The interest of this piece lies in its type and period rather than in the quality of an individual hand, for the artist is almost unknown. The conventions of the ivory miniature, smooth surface, bright flesh tones and polished detail, are carried out with the competence of a trained portraitist. It is a pleasant, anonymous example of a once-universal art form."},"met:436066":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a French military officer, painted on ivory in 1812 by an artist who signed only with the initials D. B. The sitter wears the Legion of Honor, the cross of the order founded by Napoleon in 1802, and he is unnamed. Ivory, thinly sliced and polished, took watercolor in fine strokes and lent the flesh a soft glow, and miniatures of this kind were small enough to be held or worn.","i":"The Legion of Honor was created by Napoleon in 1802 to reward service to the state, military and civil, and a miniature showing it recorded an honor that its wearer was proud to display. In 1812, with France at war across Europe, officers had their likenesses made as gifts for wives, parents and sweethearts left at home, to be carried or worn. The sitter's name and regiment are not known.","c":"Ivory miniatures of Napoleonic officers share a crisp, formal manner: a clear likeness, a bright uniform and a decoration, set against a plain ground, with soft modeling in the flesh. With the painter known only by initials, its quality is hard to rate, but the Legion of Honor makes it a document of its moment. Its value is as much historical as artistic."},"met:436242":{"d":"A portrait miniature in enamel shows a woman wearing a bonnet, identified by the title as Margaret Rieche Richard. The museum's record gives no measurements or further description, so her pose, dress and colouring cannot be described with confidence. Enamel miniatures were made by painting in powdered glass colours on a metal base and firing them, which gives a smooth, glassy, permanent surface; in the early nineteenth century they were typically small enough to be worn as jewellery or kept in a case.","i":"Miniatures of private individuals served as keepsakes for family and friends, and the full name in the title suggests a portrait of a particular woman rather than a public figure. The bonnet is typical of respectable women's dress in the early nineteenth century. The maker, recorded only as Dupuy, possibly German, is little known, and the date, 1817, is the museum's.","c":"Without being able to judge the likeness, the fair verdict is on the type: an enamel miniature of this kind is more a work of craft than of high art, to be admired for smooth finish, brilliant colour and the survival of its surface. The sitter's name and date are as valuable as the image, because they give a modest private life a record. Too little is known of the maker to say more."},"nga:41633":{"d":"A half-length man faces the viewer against a gold ground within an arched black frame. He has a long straight nose, brown eyes, a full gray beard, wavy gray hair and a patterned halo. He wears a light red garment with intricate designs at the neckline under a pale green cloak. In his left hand he holds an open red book and a black inkpot; in his right, a quill pen. The tempera panel is small, about 26 by 20 centimeters.","i":"Matthew, the former tax collector called by Christ, is the apostle credited with the first Gospel, and painters show him as a writer: the book, inkpot and quill are the attributes of an evangelist. As the author of a Gospel he is the figure of the written word, set against the gold that signals sacred time. The small arched panel probably formed part of a larger altarpiece, where saints could be seen together as a heavenly assembly.","c":"Simone Martini was the most refined painter of the Sienese school, and even in a small panel his gifts show: a delicate line, a color range of soft rose, pale green and gold, and a human face whose features are given individual character. The patterned halo and gold ground add luxury. As a fragment of a lost whole it cannot show his full ambition, but it shows his craft."},"nga:41634":{"d":"A half-length bearded man looks to the left against a golden ground, framed by a dark arched border. He has short, dark, wavy hair, arched eyebrows, thin lips and pale skin with rosy cheeks. A green robe covers a dark garment whose neckline is decorated with gold, and he holds a light blue book in his hands. An ornate halo surrounds his head, and the words SANTUS SYMON appear in dark script on either side of it.","i":"Simon, called the Zealot to distinguish him from Simon Peter, was one of the twelve apostles; tradition pairs him with Saint Jude, with whom he is said to have preached and been martyred in Persia. The painter relies on the inscription to name him rather than on a distinctive attribute, so he appears chiefly as a witness, holding the book of the Gospel. In a polyptych, such saints in a row represent the apostolic church.","c":"Gentle and inward, Simon turns his gaze away from the viewer, which gives the apostle a thoughtful air against the gold. The restrained colors, a green robe and a pale-blue book against gold, are typical of Simone's taste, and the face is modeled with great delicacy. It has little of the drama of narrative scenes and works as one note in a larger chord; seen alone, it is charming but slight."},"nga:41635":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a bearded man faces the viewer against shiny gold in an arched, vertical panel. His skin has a greenish cast with rosy cheeks, and he has wavy honey-brown hair and a long forked beard; his head tilts slightly as he looks up and off with gray eyes. He holds a red book with gold page edges in one hand and, in the other, a wooden staff tied at the top with a small black flag showing a pink scallop shell. A harvest-yellow cloak covers a black tunic with gold trim.","i":"The pilgrim's staff and scallop shell identify Saint James the Greater, apostle and patron of pilgrims, whose shrine at Santiago de Compostela in Spain was one of the great goals of medieval pilgrimage; pilgrims wore the scallop as their badge. The inscription beside his shoulders gives his name in Latin form. Simone shows him as a thoughtful, upward-glancing figure, a pilgrim and a witness, holding the book of his teaching.","c":"The upward glance and the tilt of the head give the saint a personal presence uncommon in a small devotional panel, and the greenish flesh tones, the sign of the traditional underpainting, are warmed by rosy cheeks. The cracks across the gold reflect the panel's age; the delicate punched halo, with its flower pattern, shows Simone's attention to ornament even at this scale."},"nga:41636":{"d":"The upper body of a man, turned toward the viewer with his head looking to the left, against a textured gold ground in a black arched frame. He has short reddish-brown hair, light skin, rosy cheeks, arched eyebrows and a small mouth in a neutral expression. A dark purple garment with designs at the neckline lies under a bright orange robe draped over his shoulders; his right hand holds the edge of the robe, and his left a red book. A golden halo surrounds his head.","i":"Judas Thaddeus, also called Jude, is one of the twelve apostles, distinguished from Judas Iscariot by the second name; Western tradition pairs him with Simon, with whom he is said to have preached in Persia and been martyred. The picture gives him no strongly distinguishing attribute, so he appears simply as a bookish witness. His later fame as the patron of lost causes grew up much later than this panel.","c":"The bright orange robe over deep purple is a bold color note, and the turn of the head, body facing the viewer while the face looks aside, gives the figure a slight, human awkwardness that real people have. The neutral, reserved expression is conventional, and Simone does not push it; the interest lies in the color and the grace of the drapery. It is a pleasing, quiet panel."},"nga:357":{"d":"A winged, blond angel kneels in profile facing right, in a gold garment against a gold ground, so that the whole panel is almost entirely gold. Pale skin with a greenish cast and rosy cheeks, curled blond hair, slitted eyes and closed pale pink lips mark the face; one hand is drawn across the chest while the other arm holds a tall leafy palm frond. The inside of the wings shades from butter yellow to tangerine, the outside is lapis blue, and the floor is ruby red. Worn gilding lets the red layer beneath show through cracks.","i":"The angel is Gabriel, who announces to Mary that she will bear the Son of God. His kneeling pose and crossed arm show reverence, and the palm frond is a sign of victory; in other Annunciations a lily or an olive branch takes its place. The Virgin would have appeared on a companion panel, and the pair probably formed part of a larger altarpiece. Seen alone, the angel is caught in the act of speaking.","c":"Almost everything in this panel is gold, and the painter makes drama of it: the angel's gold robe, hair and wings merge with the ground, and the color accents in the wings, tangerine and lapis, lift out like jewels. The refinement of line, the courtly profile and the pared-down means show why Simone's art was prized at Naples and Avignon. It is a small panel with a large effect."},"met:437675":{"d":"A bearded man with long, wavy hair fills a small upright panel, shown to the waist against a gold ground. A halo of punched ornament circles his head, and he looks down and to one side with a calm, slightly guarded expression. His pale rose mantle, edged with gold, falls over a blue-grey tunic in smooth, flowing folds. Both hands hold a closed red book fastened with black straps, pressed against his body at an angle that turns the figure gently in space. The gold is tooled with fine incised lines, so it catches light like metal instead of reading as flat colour. A narrow patterned border frames the picture. The work is tempera on wood, one panel from a larger altarpiece.","i":"Andrew, a fisherman and the brother of Saint Peter, was one of Christ's first disciples. He is often shown with the X-shaped cross on which tradition says he died, but here he carries only a book, the usual sign of an apostle. The Met says the panel comes from an important five-part portable altarpiece, possibly commissioned by the governing body of the city of Siena, and dates it about 1326. A portable altarpiece was made to be carried and set up where it was needed. The Met ranks Simone Martini with Giotto among the most influential painters of his day, and the poet Petrarch praised and befriended him.","c":"Away from its altarpiece, the panel cannot show how the saints once related to one another, but the single figure holds up well. The face has softly modelled cheeks and watchful eyes, and the hands have real weight. The drapery is drawn with a flowing line that favours grace over bulk, the Sienese habit, in contrast to the heavier forms of Florentine painting. The expression is conventional: Andrew is dignified and withdrawn, an emblem more than a man with a story. That suits a devotional panel, and Simone makes it persuasive."},"met:437351":{"d":"A crowd scene from the Acts of the Apostles, painted in oil on a wood panel 48.3 by 73.3 centimeters; the record places it about 1650. Paul and Barnabas are surrounded by men and women, and a rooster is among the details. It is a wide, horizontal cabinet picture of the kind made for a collector's room, and the story it tells is one of a miraculous healing and a crowd that mistakes the apostles for gods.","i":"In Acts 14, Paul heals a man crippled from birth at Lystra, in Asia Minor, and the people cry that the gods have come down, calling Barnabas Zeus and Paul Hermes. A priest of Zeus brings oxen and garlands to sacrifice, and the apostles tear their clothes in horror and tell the crowd that they are only men. The subject let Dutch painters show a pagan ritual and a Christian refusal of worship in the same scene.","c":"Jacob Pynas belonged to the pre-Rembrandtists around Pieter Lastman in Amsterdam, who made small, vividly lit narrative pictures of biblical and mythological subjects, the kind of narrative painting that formed the young Rembrandt's world. The crowded, gesturing scene is typical of the group: more energetic than elegant. Whether the figures have individual life, beyond their gestures, is the test of a picture like this."},"met:10612":{"d":"A girl and flowers, painted in oil on a canvas 95.3 by 121.3 centimeters, dated 1863. The title is an old English name for the pansy, the small wild violet called heartsease, and the flowers are the picture's other subject beside the girl. The wide canvas is large for a sentimental picture, which suggests a work made for exhibition and not merely for a parlor wall.","i":"Heartsease, or love-in-idleness in Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream, was a flower of love and of consolation, its name promising relief from the pangs of the heart. A girl and a flower together were a favorite pairing in nineteenth-century sentimental painting, and the title promises comfort as well as charm. William P. W. Dana (1833 to 1927) painted at a time when such pictures of children and flowers were popular in both America and France.","c":"The risk of any girl-with-flowers picture is sweetness; the virtues that save it are fresh color and honest observation of the flowers and of the child's attention. At more than a meter wide the canvas gives room for both. Dana had a long career, to 1927, and this early picture of 1863 belongs to the sentimental taste of its decade."},"met:10896":{"d":"A man painted in watercolor on ivory about 1795, 7.0 by 5.6 centimeters, by William Franks, who is recorded as active only from 1795 to 1798. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. A miniature of the 1790s was a fashionable keepsake, worn or kept in a case.","i":"The brief span of Franks's recorded activity, three years, is typical of the many minor miniaturists who worked in the busy market of the 1790s, when the Atlantic world produced portrait miniatures in great numbers for a middle class that wanted affordable likenesses. Men's portraits of the decade often show powdered or natural hair and a high collar with a white stock, a conservative look that signals respectability. Without a name, this man is a type.","c":"A brief career leaves little to compare, and the judgment must rest on the miniature itself: the quality of the face, the crisp finish of coat and stock, and the glow that thin color on ivory gives to skin. At 7.0 by 5.6 centimeters there is room for a careful portrait. It stands as a modest, competent example of the miniature at the end of the eighteenth century."},"met:12643":{"d":"A wide, low canvas, 54.3 by 127.0 centimeters, dated 1880, whose subject is the Parthenon, the temple of Athena on the Acropolis of Athens. The record lists only a building, so the viewpoint, the light and any figures are best read from the canvas. The long horizontal format suits a colonnaded temple and its rocky hill, and the picture belongs to the many views of Athens made for travelers and collectors in the nineteenth century.","i":"The Parthenon, built between 447 and 432 BC under Pericles, with sculpture directed by Phidias, was a ruin by 1880: a Venetian shell hit the Ottoman powder store inside it in 1687, and Lord Elgin removed much of its sculpture in the early 1800s. Nineteenth-century viewers saw in it both the origin of Western art and a symbol of lost greatness. Painting it in 1880, decades after Greek independence, joined that sentiment to the traveler's appetite for souvenirs.","c":"Vincent Stiepevich, born in 1841, treats a famous subject whose picturesque ruin invites easy effects, and the usual risk is a postcard prettiness. Whether he finds a fresh angle or a convincing light on the marble is the question for the canvas. The wide format, which gives the temple room to stand against the sky, is a good choice, and the picture is best valued as a competent, informed souvenir of an icon."},"met:18958":{"d":"Two boys, Watson Van Benthuysen II and Thomas Van Benthuysen, are portrayed together in a miniature of about 11.7 by 9.4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1837 by Aramenta Dianthe Vail. The recorded details are a child, a flower, a book, a dog on a leash, a landscape, a bare foot and a shoe: a lively narrative group rather than a formal head-and-shoulders. The size is large for a miniature.","i":"Children's portraits of the 1830s often set the sitters outdoors with toys, pets and flowers, expressing the Romantic view of childhood as a time of innocence and nature. The dog on a leash, the book and the flower suggest the boys' play and education; the bare foot and shoe add a note of informality unusual in an adult portrait. The artist was about seventeen, which makes the ambition of the composition remarkable.","c":"For a young painter this is an ambitious miniature: two figures, a dog, a landscape and a handful of small objects fitted into a few centimeters, each given its due. The narrative details, the shoe, the leash and the book, give it charm and life beyond the stiffness of a conventional pose. It lacks the finish of the seasoned masters, and the drawing may be a little stiff, but its freshness is hard to resist."},"met:15216":{"d":"A girl named Mary R. Whitlock is portrayed with a dog on a leash in a miniature of about 10.7 by 8 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1843 by Aramenta Dianthe Vail. The recorded details include lace and a shoe, small marks of a child's dress and of the care taken in painting it. The scale is large for a miniature, which leaves room for the dog and the costume as well as the child's face.","i":"Dogs were common companions in children's portraits of the 1840s, a sign of affection and of the household's comfort, and the leash links the girl and the animal in a gentle bond. The lace, a luxury trimming, indicates a family of means. By 1843 the daguerreotype had arrived, and miniaturists like Vail answered it with color, lively detail and narrative touches that the early photograph, with its stiff poses and long exposures, could not offer.","c":"The details, a leash, the lace and a shoe, bring the child to life, and the dog adds animation that a plain head-and-shoulders cannot. Vail's color is fresh and her touch delicate; the drawing may be somewhat naive, but the girl's presence is vivid. At about twenty-three she was already a practiced miniaturist, and this is a charming, confident work of the 1840s."},"met:436311":{"d":"The Virgin with the Christ Child, painted in tempera, oil and gold on a wood panel 43.8 by 32.1 centimeters, about 1480. The record gives only the pairing of Mary and her son, so their poses, the tilt of heads and the setting are best read from the picture. A panel of this size was made for private devotion, to stand or hang in a bedroom or study where a family could pray before it.","i":"Vincenzo Foppa was the leading painter in Lombardy before Leonardo da Vinci settled in Milan in the early 1480s, and his Madonnas often have a solid, sculptural quality and a cool, silvery light. The Madonna and Child was the most requested subject of private devotion: Mary as mother and as throne of Christ, the Child as the Savior he would become. Pictures of this type were sometimes given to mark a marriage or the birth of a child.","c":"Foppa belongs to the generation before Leonardo reshaped Lombard painting, and his strengths are clarity, a firm modeling of heads and a restrained palette, not the sfumato and psychological subtlety that followed. The mixture of tempera and oil in this panel reflects a period when painters were beginning to add oil's softness to tempera. It is a reserved, dignified Madonna, valued for solidity more than charm."},"nga:41678":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel of about 149 by 57 centimeters shows a man standing upright in a brown robe tied at the waist. His hair is short with a shaved top, and he holds a small branch with white flowers in one hand and a closed book in the other. Behind him are trees, hills and a blue sky, and a golden decorative structure frames the scene. The painter is Vincenzo Foppa, and the panel dates from around 1495 to 1500.","i":"The saint is Anthony of Padua, a Franciscan friar of the thirteenth century, renowned as a preacher and a patron of lost things. The white flowers are probably lilies, the sign of purity that he often carries, and the book recalls his learning. This panel and its companion Saint Bernardino of Siena, of the same size, were presumably side panels of a Franciscan altarpiece, where the saints stood on either side of a central image.","c":"Foppa, the leading painter of Lombardy before Leonardo's arrival, gives his saint a plain, sturdy dignity, with simple drapery and a landscape glimpsed through the golden frame. His style is more archaic than the Florentine painting of the same date, and the figure is somewhat stiff. The attractions are the clarity of design and the calm devotion of the saint."},"nga:46171":{"d":"A man stands barefoot on a tiled surface, holding an open book, in this tall, narrow panel of about 149 by 57 centimeters. His expression is solemn, his hair short, and he wears a long brown hooded robe. Behind him a golden archway with intricate patterns opens to a landscape with trees and a clear blue sky. It is a companion to the panel of Saint Anthony of Padua by Vincenzo Foppa, and dates from around 1495 to 1500.","i":"Saint Bernardino of Siena (1380 to 1444) was a Franciscan preacher who crossed Italy urging reform, peace and devotion to the Holy Name of Jesus, and he was canonized in 1450. He is often shown with the monogram IHS. His open book suggests his preaching and learning. The pair of Franciscan saints was probably planned for a single altarpiece, where they flanked a central image.","c":"Foppa makes the preacher's austerity visible in the plain robe and bare feet, and the golden arch behind him offers a rich contrast with his poverty. The figure is sober and solid, without the dramatic energy of the great preacher's reputation. As a quiet devotional figure the panel is effective, and its companion makes a dignified pair."},"met:17517":{"d":"A woman and a child, painted in oil on a canvas 90.2 by 72.4 centimeters, in 1833. A tree is among the subjects recorded, which suggests an outdoor setting or a landscape background of the kind often used to give a portrait a note of nature. The title leaves both sitters unnamed, and the record gives no more, so their poses and dress are best read from the picture itself.","i":"Portraits of a woman and child in the 1830s present the family as the heart of middle-class life, and the pairing, probably mother and child, pictures intimacy; the tree, if it belongs to a landscape setting, adds the sense of growth and nature that Romantic taste liked. George W. Twibill Jr. lived only from 1806 to 1836, so this portrait comes from the last few years of a very short career.","c":"Twibill died at thirty, which leaves too small a body of work for useful comparison. The test of a mother-and-child pair is whether the two figures relate to each other and not merely sit side by side; the tree is a conventional prop. It stands as a solid, pleasant example of American portraiture of the 1830s."},"met:19039":{"d":"A woman painted in watercolor on ivory in 1837, only 5.9 by 4.9 centimeters, by Samuel Broadbent Jr. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a woman and a portrait, so her dress and hair are best read from the ivory itself. A miniature of the 1830s belongs to the last generation before the daguerreotype arrived in 1839.","i":"In the 1830s women's fashion favored wide sleeves and elaborate hair, and miniatures recorded it for a clientele of merchants' and professionals' families. By 1837 the miniature trade was prosperous but also crowded, and painters like Broadbent, born in 1810, were young men when photography threatened to end it. A portrait without a name is a record of a woman whose identity was once obvious to those who kept it.","c":"At 5.9 by 4.9 centimeters the portrait is small even by miniature standards, and its quality is concentrated in the face. Broadbent's two miniatures in the Met, from 1837 and 1838, show a painter working steadily at the trade in the years before the daguerreotype. Whether the face has life and the details of dress are crisp is for the ivory to show; the object is an agreeable, unassuming example of its type."},"met:15011":{"d":"A man painted in watercolor on ivory in 1838, 6.2 by 5.0 centimeters, by Samuel Broadbent Jr., the year after his portrait of a lady in the same museum. The sitter is unnamed, and the record gives no more detail than a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. At this size the miniature could be carried in a pocket case or kept on a desk.","i":"Men's portraits of 1838 show the sober fashion of the time: dark coat, high collar, white stock or cravat and neatly dressed hair, a look that signaled steadiness and professional standing. Miniatures like this were exchanged at engagements and weddings and kept after deaths. The year is only one before the daguerreotype was announced, and within a decade of it the market for such pictures shrank dramatically.","c":"A man's miniature depends on small decisions: the position of the head, the crispness of the collar, the life in the eyes. A competent painter of the trade supplies those with quiet assurance without trying for more, and nothing in the record suggests Broadbent aimed higher. The result is a restrained, trustworthy portrait of a type that was on the verge of being replaced by the camera."},"met:13360":{"d":"A landscape with cattle at the close of day, painted in oil on a canvas 50.8 by 76.2 centimeters, about 1870 to 1880. The title names the hour, and the cattle give the scene its pastoral note. The record gives no further detail, so the shape of the land, the water or trees and the colors of the sky are best read from the canvas. The size is that of a cabinet picture, made to hang in a home.","i":"Cattle at sunset were a favorite subject of the Barbizon painters, notably Constant Troyon, and of their American followers, who found in the evening landscape a mood of calm and continuity. For urban buyers in the 1870s such a picture was a window on a simpler life, an image of the countryside that was also a quiet comment on industrial change. Harvey O. Young lived from 1840 to 1901.","c":"A sunset with cattle is a safe subject, and a painter's skill shows in the sky and in the way the light settles on the animals' backs. Young belongs to the many American landscapists of the 1870s who adapted the Barbizon taste to local scenery; the picture is a pleasant, competent example of the mode rather than an original statement."},"met:20517":{"d":"A man with a book, painted in oil on a canvas 52.7 by 40.0 centimeters, in 1773. The title names the sitter, William Duguid. The book gives him a scholarly or professional air, but the record does not say whether it is a prayer book, a ledger or another kind of volume, so his dress and pose are best read from the picture. The modest size suits a private home.","i":"A book in a colonial portrait usually signals education, piety or a profession, qualities the sitter wished to be remembered for. Prince Demah Barnes (about 1745 to 1778) is generally counted among the earliest African American painters known by name, which makes a portrait of 1773 a rare document of colonial portraiture. The year was the eve of the Revolution in the American colonies.","c":"A modest colonial portrait, plain in the manner American painters took from British mezzotints and from John Singleton Copley, whose example dominated the 1760s and 1770s. The challenge is to give a bust and a book some character, and the likeness, rather than the accessories, must carry the picture. Barnes died young, in 1778, and few of his works are known, which gives this canvas an importance beyond its quiet manner."},"nga:359":{"d":"On a tiled courtyard between arcaded buildings, a winged angel kneels at left and looks up at Mary, who stands at the right edge of the panel. The angel wears a silvery-white tunic under a scarlet cloak lined with cobalt blue and holds a white lily; Mary wears a lapis-blue robe over red and presses one hand to her chest. Between them an urn holds pink and white roses. Rows of arches recede to a coral-red wall with a doorway opening onto a path over a brook into a hilly forest, and a white dove descends on golden beams from a blue sky.","i":"The Annunciation (Luke 1:26 to 38) is the moment when Gabriel tells Mary she will bear the Son of God. The lily signals her purity, the dove is the Holy Spirit, and the walled courtyard with its doorway to the wood recalls the enclosed garden, a traditional symbol of her virginity. The painter, a Dominican friar working in Urbino about 1445 to 1450, has also made the scene a demonstration of the new science of perspective, then being codified by Leon Battista Alberti.","c":"The charm lies in the space. Receding arcades and a marbled pavement grid draw the eye with mathematical clarity toward the doorway and the woods, so the meeting of angel and Virgin happens in a world of astonishing order. The figures are slender and graceful, and the soft pinks, corals and pale grays make the scene almost pastel. It recalls the clarity of Piero della Francesca and ranks among the finest paintings of its time in Urbino."},"met:435848":{"d":"Saint Anne, her newborn daughter Mary, women attendants and a man appear in a large panel of about 145 by 96 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil in 1467. The scene unfolds within grand classical architecture, with columns and arches drawn in careful perspective, which owes more to Renaissance buildings than to the humble house of the story. Fra Carnevale, a painter from Urbino, made it with its companion, the Presentation of the Virgin, now in Boston.","i":"The birth of Mary is not told in the Bible but in the apocryphal Gospel of James and the Golden Legend: her parents Joachim and Anne, long childless, were granted a child by God. Such scenes, with women bathing the baby and tending the mother, belonged to the Marian cycles of Italian churches. The monumental architecture reflects the humanist taste for perspective and classical forms in the circle of Piero della Francesca and the Urbino court.","c":"The panel is remarkable for its architecture: columns, arches and perspective are rendered with a precision that recalls Piero della Francesca, and they give the sacred scene a dignified, stage-like setting. The figures are small and some are stiff beside the splendid buildings, but the mixture of tender domestic detail and monumental design is memorable."},"met:435833":{"d":"A woman is the sole figure in this picture of about 75 by 63 centimeters, painted before 1635, and the title names her Herodias. In the Gospels Herodias was the wife of Herod Antipas, who had John the Baptist beheaded after her daughter danced for the king. The Milanese painter Francesco Cairo was known for intense, emotional figures of women from history and scripture, and this is one of them.","i":"Herodias bore a grudge against John the Baptist, who had denounced her marriage to Herod as unlawful; at Herod's birthday feast her daughter pleased the king by dancing and, prompted by her mother, asked for the Baptist's head on a platter. Baroque painters often treated Herodias and her daughter as femmes fatales, whose beauty and cruelty fascinated viewers. A picture of Herodias alone may allude to the crime without showing it.","c":"Cairo's Herodias has the tension of the best Milanese Baroque: a figure caught in a moment of passion, with rich costume and dramatic light. The close view brings the viewer near, and the sitter's ambiguous character, beautiful and sinister, is part of the appeal. It is a vivid, theatrical image whose power lies in the psychology of a single face."},"nga:206484":{"d":"Five figures gather around the Virgin, who sits at the center holding the nude Christ Child on her knee, her head almost touching the top of the small square panel. She wears a marine-blue robe over a raspberry-red dress, and the child reaches out with one chubby arm. An old, bearded Magus in a canary-yellow cloak kneels to offer a gold vessel; behind him a smaller kneeling figure holds a crown, and two standing men hold other gifts. At right a king with brown skin, in a crimson and white cloak, faces away toward the baby. A gray horse waits in the corner.","i":"The Magi, or kings, came from the East to honor the newborn Christ, bringing gold, frankincense and myrrh (Matthew 2). Medieval tradition made them three kings of different ages and, later, from three parts of the known world, hence the dark-skinned king at the right, who represents Africa. Their kneeling homage prefigures the nations recognizing Christ. A scene this small was probably meant for private devotion or for a collector's cabinet.","c":"Morazzone, a leading Lombard painter of his day, packs his small panel with saturated color, marine blue, raspberry red, canary yellow, and with crowding gestures, a style that matches the intense, emotional religion of Counter-Reformation Milan under the Borromeo archbishops. The figures crowd the frame, and the Virgin's head nearly touches the edge, which gives the panel an urgency. It lacks the grandeur of his big frescoes, but its heat is characteristic."},"met:15189":{"d":"A man painted in watercolor on ivory in 1829, 6.4 by 5.3 centimeters, by James Passmore Smith (1803 to 1888). The record gives no name and no further detail beyond a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. Smith was in his mid-twenties when he painted it, and it is the earlier of his two miniatures in the Met.","i":"Around 1830 the fashionable man wore a dark coat with a high collar and a white stock, with hair brushed to a modest wave. Miniatures were still the usual way for the prosperous to record a likeness for family or for a bride-to-be, and the market had room for young painters who could work quickly. This man, now nameless, was once a person known to those who owned the portrait.","c":"Smith's two miniatures in the Met, five years apart, show a painter in steady practice. In a man's miniature the face has to carry the interest, the dress being simple, and the quality lies in the modeling and the eyes. It is a professional, unpretentious piece, more valuable as a record of a time than for any individual flourish."},"met:12612":{"d":"A woman painted in watercolor on ivory in 1834, 6.0 by 5.1 centimeters, by James Passmore Smith, five years after his portrait of a gentleman in the same collection. The sitter is unnamed, and the record gives no more detail than a woman and a portrait, so her dress and hair are best read from the ivory. At this size the miniature would have been kept in a small case or worn as a locket.","i":"Women's fashion in 1834 was at its most voluminous in sleeves and hair, and a miniature caught the fashion at a single moment. It would have been a personal object, given to a husband or fiancé or kept by a child, and it is the sort of picture that passed down within families until the sitter's name was forgotten. The anonymity now says more about the passage of time than about the sitter.","c":"In a woman's miniature of the 1830s the usual challenge is to keep the elaborate hair and sleeves from overwhelming the face. At only 6 by 5 centimeters the picture leaves little room for display, and what matters is the glow of skin through thin color. Smith is best judged here as a competent, conventional practitioner of the trade."},"met:16892":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Stephen Thorn, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1818, 6.8 by 5.4 centimeters, by George Augustus Baker Sr., who was born in Strasbourg in 1760 and died in New York in 1847. A named sitter turns the miniature from a type into a person; the record adds only a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory itself.","i":"Baker, a European-born painter who worked in New York, belonged to the generation of immigrant miniaturists who brought Continental polish to the American market after the Revolution; his son, George Augustus Baker Jr., became a painter too. A named portrait of 1818 was a gift or a commission within a family circle, and the sitter's occupation is not given.","c":"Baker's long life, from 1760 to 1847, spanned the whole age of the American miniature, and this portrait of 1818 comes from the middle of it. A professional, unshowy likeness, it is valued for its clarity and for the named sitter. It does not aim at drama, and it rewards the viewer who looks at the eyes, the crispness of the collar and the quiet finish."},"met:15152":{"d":"Dr. Richard Wilmot Hall is portrayed in a miniature in watercolor on ivory, only about 7 by 5 cm. The painter, Gennarino Persico, was an Italian miniaturist who made portraits for American clients, and the date is unknown. It was acquired together with a miniature of the sitter's wife, of the same size, and the two were made as a pair, a husband and wife kept as companion keepsakes.","i":"A physician's portrait presented gravity and trust: the doctor as a man of learning and character, in the sober dress of a professional. Miniatures like this were kept in cases, set in lockets or displayed on desks, and a husband's and wife's pair recorded a marriage. Before the daguerreotype and the photograph displaced it, the miniature on ivory was the favored way of keeping a loved one's likeness close, valued for its luminous skin tones.","c":"A miniature on ivory depends on fine, patient handling and on the translucent glow of the ivory support, and Persico, as an Italian-trained miniaturist, would have brought a smooth finish and a controlled palette. The portrait is likely dignified and restrained rather than psychologically searching, as befits a professional man. Its value lies in its intimacy and in its pairing with his wife's portrait, which is the better way to judge it."},"met:15153":{"d":"Eliza Taylor, Mrs. Richard Wilmot Hall, is portrayed in a miniature in watercolor on ivory, only about 7 by 5 cm, by the Italian miniaturist Gennarino Persico. It is the companion of the Met's miniature of her husband, the same size and acquired together, and the date is unknown. The title gives her maiden name in brackets, in the modern museum practice of restoring a woman's own name beside her married one.","i":"A wife's miniature, made as a companion to her husband's, was a record of a marriage and a keepsake for the family. The portrait of a woman in this medium was prized for its delicate rendering of skin, hair and the textures of lace or ribbon, and women's miniatures often carried an affectionate rather than a public message. Eliza Taylor's name survives, together with her husband's, and the pair preserve the likeness of an ordinary couple.","c":"A woman's miniature must flatter within discipline, and the ivory's glow lends skin a luminosity that oil cannot easily match. As half of a pair it works as a quiet, well-made record. Persico's smooth finish and restrained palette make it a competent example of a popular genre. Its charm is intimacy, and its main significance is as evidence of the couple's life together."},"met:15022":{"d":"A tiny portrait in enamel on copper, about 8 by 7 centimetres, shows Benjamin Franklin. The museum record lists the sitter and the medium but no details, so his pose and dress cannot be described with confidence. Enamel was fired in a kiln in thin coloured layers, a process that gives smooth, jewel-like surfaces and colours that do not fade, and enamelled portraits of this size were made to be set into frames, boxes or lockets and kept as keepsakes.","i":"In 1785 Franklin, aged seventy-nine, was completing almost nine years as American minister in Paris, where he was admired as scientist, philosopher and symbol of the American cause. His face was among the most reproduced in France, in prints, medals, busts and miniatures. A small enamel like this answered the fashion for owning a likeness of the celebrated American. The maker is a little-documented French artist.","c":"Franklin was the most recognisable American in Paris, and makers of small likenesses could count on a market. Without the object before the eye, the safest verdict is that a work of this size by a little-known maker is best judged as decorative craft, valued for the permanence of its colour and the close likeness it offers, not as a profound portrait. Its interest is the continuing fascination of Franklin for his French admirers."},"met:11587":{"d":"Henry Clay, painted in oil on a canvas 129.7 by 100.3 centimeters in 1843 by Theodore Sidney Moise (1808 to 1885). The size, about a meter and a third high, makes it a formal portrait of public presence, far from the pocket miniatures of the same sitter that the Met also holds. The record gives only a man and a portrait, so Clay's pose, dress and setting are best read from the canvas.","i":"In 1843 Clay, in his mid-sixties, was the presumptive Whig candidate for president in 1844, and a large portrait fit the moment: it would stand in a public room, a party office or a supporter's house as a statement of loyalty. His career stretched from the War of 1812 to the Compromise of 1850; admirers saw a statesman of reconciliation, critics an ambitious politician. A formal portrait was designed to present the first view.","c":"A large portrait of a famous man demands authority above all, and the painter's task is to keep Clay's mobile, lively face from being swallowed by the formal apparatus of a public image. Whether Moise achieves that, or settles for the dignified but dull manner of much political portraiture, is for the canvas to show. Moise is much less well known than his sitter, and the portrait's worth is largely documentary."},"met:14356":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Daniel Parkman, born Mary George McDonough, is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 8.4 by 6.8 centimeters, painted around 1825 by Anthony Meucci, an artist recorded as active between 1818 and 1837. The recorded motifs name only the sitter, and the size is generous for a miniature, leaving room for the head, the shoulders and the dress of a woman of the mid-1820s.","i":"The title identifies the sitter by her husband's name and her maiden name, the conventional form in American records of the period. A miniature around 1825 would typically mark a marriage or a milestone and stay in the family as an heirloom. Meucci is a lesser-known name, and portraits like this show the large number of competent miniaturists who served American families before photography.","c":"Meucci's work is little known, so this portrait should be judged by clarity of the face, the grace of the contour and the control of the ivory's glow. It follows the standard formula of the 1820s without an obvious signature quirk, which is both a strength, in its reliability, and a weakness, in its anonymity of style. As a record of a named woman it is more useful as a document than as a revelation."},"met:15113":{"d":"An unnamed woman is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 6.9 by 5.7 centimeters by Anthony Meucci, an artist recorded as active between 1818 and 1837. The museum gives no date. The recorded details, a cap, a hat, earrings and lace, suggest a woman dressed with some care, and the lace in particular gives the painter a chance to show fine detail.","i":"Details of costume are the best clues to a miniature's date and the sitter's status: lace was a luxury, earrings a mark of ornament, and caps and hats point to the fashions of the 1820s and 1830s. The title Portrait of a Lady records a lost identity; many miniatures separated from their cases or family papers lose their sitters in this way. The picture shows how American families documented themselves before photography.","c":"With neither a name nor a date to guide judgment, the miniature's interest lies in the handling of lace and face: the lace invites delicate touches, while the earrings and hat frame the head. Meucci's drawing is probably clear if undistinguished, and the portrait offers what the genre offers at its best, a face kept close. It is pleasant rather than remarkable."},"met:15099":{"d":"A portrait of Samuel Baldwin Ludlow, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1838, 6.8 by 4.9 centimeters, by Theodore Lund (1810 to 1895). The sitter is named in the title; the record gives only a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory itself. The small, narrow format fits a case or locket, and the picture comes from the last years before the daguerreotype.","i":"A named miniature of 1838 identifies a man of the middle or upper class of his day who could afford a hand-painted likeness. Lund was only twenty-eight and one of the many young painters who made the miniature their trade; his long life, to 1895, carried him well beyond the age of the miniature into the era of photography. The year 1838 is the moment just before that change.","c":"Lund's three miniatures in the Met show a young professional working to a clear, unfussy formula, aiming at a respectable likeness. In a narrow format the face is the whole picture, and its quality can be checked in the eyes and the soft shading of the jaw. It is a sound, unadventurous piece of its type."},"met:11456":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Warren Rogers, born Julie Françoise Gabrielle d'Anterroches, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1839, 7.6 by 5.9 centimeters, by Theodore Lund. A lace collar, a brooch and a necklace appear in the picture, details that give the miniature a richness a plain bust lacks. It is somewhat larger than the portrait of her husband in the same museum.","i":"Her maiden name, d'Anterroches, is French, so she may have been born in France, though the record does not say where the couple met or lived. The lace collar, brooch and necklace present her as a woman of means and taste, and the miniature is an object of display as well as affection. Naming a married woman by her husband's name with her maiden name in brackets is the cataloging convention.","c":"The lace collar, brooch and necklace are the kind of detail on which a miniaturist shows his skill: each must be drawn crisply and stay subordinate to the face. If Lund handles them as crisply as a skilled miniaturist should, the larger size lets the face breathe. As half of a pair with her husband's portrait, the picture gains in interest, and it is the more elaborate of the two."},"met:11457":{"d":"A portrait of Warren Rogers, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1839, 6.8 by 5.2 centimeters, by Theodore Lund. The title names the sitter, and the record gives only a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. The Met also holds Lund's portrait of Mrs. Warren Rogers, dated 1839, and the two were presumably made as a pair.","i":"A husband-and-wife pair of miniatures was a standard commission: two small portraits, each in its own case or both in a double case, kept together by the family or exchanged between the couple. In the late 1830s men's portraits show the sober dark coats and high collars of the day, and the husband's portrait would complement the more decorative one of his wife. The pair records the marriage as much as the individuals.","c":"A man's miniature of this date asks for restraint: a dark coat, a white collar, a face. Lund keeps to that and leaves the interest to the sitter's expression. The portrait is best seen alongside its companion, where the contrast of a man's sober dress and a woman's lace and jewelry makes the pair more than the sum of its parts."},"met:20412":{"d":"A man painted in watercolor on ivory in 1800, 8.3 by 6.8 centimeters, by Jeremiah Paul (1775 to 1820). The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. It is larger than many miniatures of its date, and the year 1800 falls at the turn of the century, when fashion was shedding the powder and silk of the eighteenth century.","i":"In 1800 men's clothing had moved toward the plain dark coat, high collar and natural hair that would define the next decades, and a portrait of that year shows a man poised between the old and new styles. Such a miniature was a private object, given to a spouse, a fiancée or a friend. Paul's dates, 1775 to 1820, place his whole career within the golden age of the miniature.","c":"A larger miniature gives the painter room for finer modeling, and in a man's portrait of 1800 the test is the head, the eyes, the shading of the jaw and the clean outline of the collar. Paul's name is little known today, and this is best taken as a sound, conventional example of the trade at the turn of the century, valued for what it records more than for any flourish."},"nga:41628":{"d":"A woman sits on a cushion on the floor, holding a baby stretched stiffly across her lap, against gold in a tall arched panel. Four winged angels float in two ranks at each side, hands crossed or joined in prayer; above them a bearded man in blue emerges from clouds, one hand extended and a book in the other, and a white dove flies down toward the baby on long rays. Mary wears a royal-blue hooded cloak over a rose-pink dress covered in gold vines. The Child, wrapped in gold cloth, reaches up to touch her breast.","i":"Mary seated low on the ground is the Madonna of Humility, a type popular in the fourteenth century: she sits on the earth, in Latin humus, as a sign of her humility, and nurses her son. Above, God the Father and the dove of the Holy Spirit complete a Trinity, so the picture shows the Incarnation: the Spirit descends and the Word becomes flesh. The nursing Child stresses his full humanity, and the gold lifts the scene into heaven.","c":"Jacopo di Cione, the brother of the Florentine painter Orcagna, was a conservative master who preferred rich ornament and clear formulas to experiment. The pleasures here are the patterned drapery, with its gold vines and starbursts, and the ranks of angels with coral diadems. The weakness is the Child, whose adult proportions and rigid pose are less lifelike than Giotto's tradition had taught. It is a splendid, slightly stiff altarpiece, typical of Florentine taste in the decades after the plague."},"met:435863":{"d":"A gold ground fills this small tempera panel, about 54 by 42 centimeters. The title shows Saint Catherine of Alexandria in the act of disputing, the episode in her legend when she faced the pagan philosophers. Besides the saint, the record lists a man and a woman, who must be the two donors of the title, presumably smaller and kneeling, as donors usually are. The record gives no further detail of the setting or the poses.","i":"In her legend, Catherine was a learned young woman of fourth-century Alexandria who was summoned by the emperor to debate with fifty pagan scholars and so persuaded them that they converted and were put to death. She was later condemned to die on a spiked wheel, which shattered, and was beheaded. Donors were included so that the givers would be permanently present before the saint; who these two were is not recorded.","c":"Cenni di Francesco was a Florentine painter of the late fourteenth century who worked in the established, decorative manner of his time and is better known for gilded, devout panels than for innovation. A panel with donors was a standard product of such a workshop, and its interest lies in the charm of the type, the gold ground and the pious record of the patrons, not in any advance in form."},"met:441024":{"d":"The head of an old woman, painted in oil on a canvas 53.0 by 39.0 centimeters, after 1610. The record lists only a woman's head and no setting, so the picture appears to be a close study of a face rather than a narrative scene; whether it was a fragment of a larger work or an independent study is not stated. It is small, the size of a picture kept in a studio or a collector's cabinet.","i":"Caravaggio's example, with its unidealized faces taken from life, encouraged painters in early seventeenth-century Rome to study old people for their wrinkles, heavy shadows and character. Orazio Borgianni (1578 to 1616), a Roman painter who also worked in Spain, belonged to this circle. A head like this may be a study for a saint, a sibyl or the old nurse of a larger history picture, or an independent picture for a collector.","c":"Borgianni is best known for dark, intense, emotionally charged paintings, and an unidealized old face suits him: the interest lies in the pattern of wrinkles, the heavy shadow and the unsparing realism. Whether it is more than a clever exercise depends on the sympathy in the eyes and mouth. He died at thirty-eight, and the picture is a rare reminder of his gift for human character."},"met:11166":{"d":"A portrait of Rudolphus Bogert, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1806, 6.4 by 5.1 centimeters, by Parmenas Howell, who lived only from 1784 to 1808. The sitter is named; the record gives only a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. The painter was about twenty-two, and the picture comes from the last years of a very short life.","i":"Bogert is a surname found among the descendants of the Dutch settlers of the New York region, who formed an important body of patrons for American portraiture. A miniature of about 1806 was a personal keepsake, exchanged with a fiancée or kept by family. Howell died in 1808 in his early twenties, so any portrait by him comes from a career of only a few years.","c":"A young painter's miniature can show promise and also inexperience, and this one should be judged for the steadiness of drawing in the face, the clarity of the eyes and the neatness of the finish. Without a longer career to compare, the picture stands on its own, as a competent and perhaps promising piece by a painter who did not live to develop."},"met:16907":{"d":"A father and his young daughter are shown together in a miniature of about 9 by 12 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory and painted paper around 1805. Miniatures of this kind were intimate keepsakes, made to be held in the hand and kept in a case, a frame or a locket. Double portraits of a parent and child are less common than single likenesses. The painter, François M. Guyol de Guiran, worked in the miniature tradition of the turn of the century.","i":"Around 1800 the portrait miniature was at the height of its popularity in Europe and America, serving as a token of affection, a memento of absent or lost relatives and a quiet display of status. A father-and-daughter pairing reads as a statement of family bond and, for the daughter, of continuity. The title does not name the sitters, so the occasion of the commission remains open.","c":"In so small a format the painter must give two figures presence within a few square inches, and success depends on fine, patient handling: the whiteness of ivory glows through thin watercolor, giving skin a luminosity that oil cannot easily match. Within the tradition Guyol de Guiran is a competent specialist rather than an innovator. The appeal is intimacy, a tenderness meant for one viewer at a time."},"met:15214":{"d":"A portrait of Charlotte Allard, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1840, 7.1 by 5.8 centimeters, by Leopold Paul Unger (1812 to 1859). The sitter is named; the record gives only a woman and a portrait, so her dress and hair are best read from the ivory. The date places the miniature in the first full year after the announcement of the daguerreotype in 1839.","i":"Women's fashion around 1840 favored smooth, center-parted hair, sloping shoulders and narrow, tight bodices, a look often called Early Victorian. Naming the sitter makes the portrait more than a type, but the record does not tell who Charlotte Allard was. As a hand-painted likeness at the moment when the daguerreotype was spreading, the miniature stood for the slower, costlier, more personal alternative.","c":"Unger's miniature of 1840 comes at the hinge between the old craft and the new camera, and its quality is in the delicate, personal touch of hand-painting: the soft modeling of flesh, the glow of the eyes. At 7.1 by 5.8 centimeters it is a good-sized miniature, and the name gives it a human interest. Unger's own career, to 1859, suggests a painter who lived through the change."},"met:14995":{"d":"A portrait of Thomas Dillard, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1834, 7.9 by 6.5 centimeters, by Jean Christophe Alexandre, who is recorded as active from 1834 to 1839. The sitter is named; the record gives only a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. It matches in size and date the portrait of his wife, and the two form a pair.","i":"A husband-and-wife pair painted in the same year and at the same size marks a family's joint portrait commission, perhaps to celebrate a wedding. Alexandre's recorded activity begins in 1834, the year of this pair, so these may be among his first documented works. The pair kept together tells more than either alone: it records a household, not just two people.","c":"At 7.9 by 6.5 centimeters the miniature is comfortably large, giving the painter room to model the face and finish the dress with care. The man's portrait asks for steadiness: a clear head, simple dress, an alert eye. Without a longer record of Alexandre's work, the picture is best taken as a sound, professional piece, and its interest is increased by the survival of its companion."},"met:14996":{"d":"A portrait of Mrs. Thomas Dillard, born Matilda Kuhl, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1834, 7.9 by 6.5 centimeters, by Jean Christophe Alexandre. It is the companion to the portrait of her husband in the same museum, of identical size and date. The record gives only a woman and a portrait, so her dress and hair are best read from the ivory.","i":"Naming a married woman with her maiden name in brackets, Matilda Kuhl, is a cataloging convention that preserves her own family name. Kuhl is a German surname, which may point to a German-American family, though the record does not say. In 1834 women's fashion favored wide sleeves and elaborately dressed hair, and the miniature would have captured that fashionable moment in a tiny space.","c":"Wives' portraits often carry more ornament than husbands', and the test here is to keep sleeves, hair and collar in balance with the face, so that the sitter, not her clothing, is the subject. Seen with its pair, the miniature is best judged by whether the two portraits balance, since a married couple's miniatures are meant to be read together."},"met:16858":{"d":"A boy with a toy horse, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1840 by an artist recorded only as Miss Leland. At 10.8 by 10.2 centimeters the picture is nearly square and unusually large for a miniature, which gives the child and his toy room to be seen clearly. The record gives no more detail, so the boy's pose and clothes are best read from the picture itself.","i":"A child with a toy was a standard way to portray boyhood in the 1830s and 1840s, the horse hinting at future activity, travel or sport, and at the child's play in the present. The name 'Miss Leland' says only that the painter was a woman; many nineteenth-century women painted miniatures professionally or as an accomplishment, and the record does not say which she was. The nearly square format is unusual and gives the picture some of the character of a small painting.","c":"Boys with toys are charming but conventional, and the interest of this one is partly its size and its unknown painter. Nearly square at 10.8 by 10.2 centimeters, it asks to be seen as a small picture rather than a pocket keepsake. The test for a child's miniature is whether the face has life and the toy is drawn with affection, a quality of attention that amateur and professional painters alike can bring."},"met:13350":{"d":"A small still life, 24.8 by 31.1 centimeters, painted in oil on a wood panel about 1825, of peaches, an apple and a pear. The fruit is most likely arranged on a table or ledge, the usual form of early American still life, and the panel's small size makes it a picture for a parlor wall or a mantel. The record lists only the fruit, so the surface, the light and any background are best read from the panel.","i":"American still life in the 1820s grew mainly from the Peale family of Philadelphia, whose members painted fruit, flowers and desserts as modest, honest images of abundance. A painting of peaches, an apple and a pear is a picture of a harvest and of a household able to afford fruit; it also invites the viewer to admire the painter's skill at making skin, bloom and soft flesh seem real. John A. Woodside lived from 1781 to 1852.","c":"A modest panel in which the painter's task is to make each fruit solid, round and distinct, and to let the peaches' downy bloom, the apple's gloss and the pear's smooth skin differ in texture. The strengths of such pictures are clarity and honesty; the weakness is often a flat, evenly lit arrangement. It is a pleasing small picture, best seen with its companion of peaches and grapes."},"met:13349":{"d":"A small still life, 24.8 by 31.1 centimeters, painted in oil on a wood panel about 1825 to 1830, of peaches and grapes. It matches the size and medium of Woodside's still life of peaches, an apple and a pear in the same museum, so the two were probably made as a pair or a set. The record lists only the fruit, so the arrangement and background are best read from the panel.","i":"Peaches and grapes were prized fruits, signs of the well-supplied table, and together they make a conventional pairing of the soft and the glossy. As in older European still life, a picture of ripe fruit could carry a quiet reminder that ripeness is brief. A pair of small panels like this would likely have decorated a parlor.","c":"Peaches and grapes test a painter differently: the bloom on the grape and the velvet of the peach need a lighter, drier touch than a smooth apple. Woodside's two panels give a small chance to compare how he handles each. Neither aims to be more than a modest, honest still life, but their candor is a quality that fancier pictures often lose."},"met:14351":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man identified by the title as John Wood Dodge, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7.5 by 6 centimetres, around 1836 to 1837. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. The sitter shares a surname with the painter, Edward S. Dodge, and a miniaturist of that name was working in New York in these years, so the picture may be a portrait of a relative by a colleague in the same trade.","i":"Edward S. Dodge (1816–1857) belonged to the American miniaturists working in the 1830s and 1840s, the last great generation before the daguerreotype all but ended the trade. Miniature painters of the period often portrayed one another and their families, partly as practice and partly as an exchange of skill, and portraits of colleagues are of particular interest because they show how artists saw each other. Dodge was only about twenty when he made this one.","c":"A young painter's portrait of a colleague: the quick likeness, the clear ivory ground and the neat finish are typical of the miniature at its late peak, and the personal connection gives the picture an intimacy that commissions lack. Without the specifics of the face, one can say that the work of a painter in his early twenties is usually careful rather than daring, and that its interest is as much historical as artistic."},"met:15039":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named William Henry Tallmadge, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimetres, in 1841. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. At that date the daguerreotype was only two years old, and miniatures such as this were still being ordered, often as a gift to mark an engagement or a departure, and kept in a small case.","i":"In 1841 Dodge was twenty-five and working in New York, where miniature painters competed with the new daguerreotype studios opening along Broadway. The portrait names its sitter in full, William Henry Tallmadge, a name found in New York and Connecticut families, though the record gives no details of his life. The Metropolitan Museum purchased the picture in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","c":"A neat, small, competent portrait of its date: miniaturists of the 1840s, facing the camera, gave their sitters careful finish and pleasing colour, and Dodge's example is typical in its restraint. Without the face before the eye one cannot say more than that such portraits are typically crisp in likeness and plain in background. The most striking thing about it is its date, a reminder that the old art was still alive when its end was in sight."},"met:15040":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 7 by 5.5 centimetres, between 1840 and 1845. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. The title, Portrait of a Gentleman, is a generic label for a sitter whose name has not survived, and the miniature came to the Met in 2006 through the Fletcher Fund.","i":"The years 1840 to 1845 were the moment when the daguerreotype became a commercial rival to the miniature, with studios in every large American city offering a likeness in minutes for a few dollars. Miniaturists such as Edward S. Dodge responded by offering colour, delicacy and a more flattering finish, and for some years the two media coexisted. This sitter's name has been lost, which makes the picture a more general witness to the fashion for portraits.","c":"A well-made, unpretentious miniature that holds its own beside the early photograph by virtue of its colour and the soft, luminous ground that ivory provides. Without the picture before the eye, there is little to add about the likeness, except that anonymity is a handicap: a portrait without a name loses much of the interest that a known sitter lends. It is a good example of a craft facing its end with competence."},"met:15041":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, painted in watercolour on ivory, about 8 by 7 centimetres, around 1845. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his features, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. It is the largest of the four Dodge miniatures in this group, and one of the many miniatures Gloria Manney gave to the Metropolitan Museum in 2006.","i":"By about 1845 miniaturists had begun to make their pictures larger and more elaborate, to distinguish them from the small, cheap daguerreotype, and sitters often wanted a more imposing presentation, in a gilded frame or a morocco case. The sitter here is anonymous. Dodge, who died in 1857, worked in a trade that was in steep decline, and this late example shows the craft in the final decade of its popularity.","c":"A larger miniature has room for the face to breathe, and the extra scale lets a painter like Dodge give a little more modelling and a little more presence than the earlier, tiny ovals. Without the specifics to judge, it is fair to call it a capable, decorous portrait of its moment, with the polish of a trade that knew its customers. It deserves attention as a late flower of a form that was ending."},"met:15084":{"d":"A boy with a dog, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1843, 9.4 by 7.5 centimeters, by Samuel V. Homan, who is recorded as active from 1841 to 1845. The boy's pose, dress and the dog's breed are best read from the picture, since the record names only a boy, a dog and a portrait. At this size the miniature is larger than a pocket keepsake and would have been framed or kept in a standing case.","i":"A dog beside a child was a common companion in portraits of the period, a sign of affection and loyalty and a convenient way of showing a boy as a person at ease with animals and outdoor play. Homan's recorded years, 1841 to 1845, fall in the first years of the daguerreotype's competition, when a painted likeness with such narrative touches offered color and a story that the camera of the time could not.","c":"A boy and a dog together give the painter two living subjects to coordinate, and the weakness of such pictures is often the animal, drawn from a formula. Whether Homan's dog is as alive as his boy is for the ivory to show. The size and the pairing raise the miniature above the average pocket likeness, and it is a pleasant, informal example of its time."},"met:14317":{"d":"Siblings, a boy and a girl among the group, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1841, 12.8 by 10.4 centimeters, an unusually large miniature. The children are shown with an apron, a book, a scarf and collars, the ordinary details of dress and study that make a group portrait lively. The Starbird family is named in the title; the number and ages of the children are best counted on the ivory itself.","i":"Clarissa Peters Russell (1809 to 1854) worked under her husband's name, Mrs. Moses B. Russell, a reminder that women were active in the miniature trade. A group of siblings on one ivory was a more costly and more demanding commission than a single head, and its larger size let the painter vary pose and gaze. The book hints at education, the apron at childhood's play and chores.","c":"At nearly 13 by 10 centimeters, a group portrait on ivory is an ambitious test of the medium, since each face must stay clear and individual in a space where a single head would be small. The detail of aprons, book and scarf adds life. It is a considerable undertaking, and a good example of the larger, more ambitious miniatures of the early 1840s."},"met:15033":{"d":"A man painted in watercolor on ivory in 1844, 6.6 by 5.2 centimeters, by Thomas Dawson, who was born about 1800 and is recorded as living after 1850. The record names no sitter and gives no detail beyond a man and a portrait, so his dress and expression are best read from the ivory. The date places it in the full tide of the daguerreotype's arrival.","i":"By 1844 daguerreotype studios were open in many cities, and the miniature trade was feeling the pressure; painters who survived tended to emphasize the color, the care and the flattering touches that the camera could not yet deliver. A man's portrait of the 1840s typically shows a dark frock coat, a waistcoat and a high stock or cravat. Without a name, this man is one of thousands who chose the hand-painted likeness.","c":"Dawson's miniature of 1844 belongs to the last phase of the trade, and it should be judged on the qualities that photography then lacked: warmth of flesh tone, softness of modeling and a personal touch. If it has those, it justifies its higher cost; if not, it is a handsome but unremarkable survivor. The portrait is a representative piece of its moment."},"met:15231":{"d":"A man, anonymous in the Met's title, appears in a tiny miniature only 7 by 5.6 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1841. The scale is that of an object held in the hand or kept in a case or locket. Miniatures of this kind normally show the sitter's head and shoulders against a simple ground, with the face given the most careful finish and the rest handled more lightly.","i":"Watercolor miniatures on ivory were the standard affordable portrait in America from the 1790s to the 1840s, kept in family cases and exchanged as keepsakes at engagements and farewells. By 1841 the daguerreotype had arrived from France, announced in 1839, and within a decade it would take over the miniature's market. The unnamed gentleman tells little about himself; the small, personal object is the point.","c":"Waugh's miniature is a workmanlike example of a craft that demanded fine control: thin, transparent washes on a translucent ivory ground, built up in tiny touches. The glow of the ivory beneath the paint gives a face a luminosity that oil does not easily match. Its limits are those of the type: little room for invention, a conventional pose, a sitter now anonymous. It is best treasured as a sample of a fading art."},"met:15203":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Teakes is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 10 by 7.5 centimeters, large for the format, by Anne Teakes, an artist who shares her surname and was active between 1812 and 1827. The recorded details, a cap with a ribbon, a fichu and a shawl, describe the dress of a respectable married woman of the Regency years.","i":"Day caps with ribbons, fichus and shawls were the standard dress of matrons in Britain at this time. The shared surname suggests that the sitter was a relative of the artist, so the portrait may belong to a private family circle rather than to a professional commission; women miniaturists frequently began by portraying their own families. The dates show only a brief recorded career.","c":"At ten centimeters tall this is a generous miniature, and the costume details, the cap's ribbon, the crossed fichu and the shawl, give the artist textures to display. The picture's interest lies in the patient description of dress and the sitter's steady, homely presence more than in virtuosity. It is a sincere, painstaking record, and a family subject often brings that kind of care."},"met:14401":{"d":"A mourning miniature, 5.3 by 4.4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory and set in a rose-gold locket with a compartment for hair at the back. A woman and a boy are the figures recorded, presumably a widow and her son grieving for the man named in the title, Peter Philip Walter. The record dates it between 1800 and 1805. Such pictures combined painting with the hair of the dead, treated as a relic.","i":"Memorial miniatures were popular in the early Republic: a grieving woman and child, often beside a tomb or urn, and the lock of the dead man's hair kept in a hidden compartment behind the picture. The hair made the object a tangible link with the dead, and the sentimental pictorial vocabulary, borrowed from neoclassical funerary art, gave grief a socially acceptable form. Samuel Folwell (1764 to 1813) is recorded as an American maker of such memorials.","c":"The best memorial miniatures combine feeling with restraint, and the form asks for a delicate balance between the stock vocabulary of mourning and a convincing, particular grief. The rose-gold locket and hair compartment raise this one above a mere painting: it is a small reliquary of a person. At 5.3 by 4.4 centimeters it is intimate, and its sentiment is easy to read even without further details."},"met:15101":{"d":"A small child is the unnamed sitter of this tiny watercolor on ivory, only about 5 by 4 centimeters, dated 1842 and painted by William Lydston Jr. At this size the whole head is hardly bigger than a thumbnail, and every detail of eye, lip and curl must be set down in a few minute touches, with the white ivory shining through the transparent color to lend the skin its glow. It was bought for the Metropolitan Museum in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"A child's likeness on ivory was a parent's treasure: it caught a stage of growth that would pass, and it could be worn, carried or kept in a case. In 1842 the daguerreotype was already three years old, but a hand-painted miniature still had the advantage of color and of the artist's gentle idealization, qualities that mothers valued. William Lydston Jr. (about 1813 to 1881) belongs to the generation of American miniaturists who worked through the years when photography was arriving.","c":"What the form demands is a clean, tender finish with few shadows, and a painter who delivers that gives the viewer a face that fits in the palm. A child's face is a good test of a miniaturist: there are no lines of character, only the freshness of skin, the roundness of cheek and the bright eye. The sitter's name is lost, but the affection that commissioned the portrait is still easy to read."},"met:15100":{"d":"A child, unnamed and undated, is the subject of this watercolor on ivory, about 5.5 by 4.3 centimeters, by William Lydston Jr., bought for the Met in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund. Close in size and subject to his dated portrait of a child of 1842, it was probably made in the same decade. Painted in transparent washes over the white ivory, a miniature of this size could be worn as a brooch or kept in a case, a child's face held within the width of a palm.","i":"When a miniature carries neither a name nor a date, the clues are in the object: the sitter's dress, the hair, the size and shape of the ivory, the manner of the painting. Children of the 1840s were shown with an informality new to the century, and a miniaturist who painted several such portraits, as Lydston did, was meeting a steady demand from families who wanted their children recorded.","c":"With the sitter and date unknown, this miniature has to be valued for craft alone, and a child's face is a good test: little to hold on to, no lines of character, only the freshness of skin and the bright eye. Lydston's modest, careful manner suits the job. It will not change anyone's idea of the form, but it carries the tenderness that makes children's miniatures hard to resist."},"met:15229":{"d":"An unnamed gentleman is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.4 by 5.2 centimeters, dated 1836 and painted by William Warner Jr. It is a bust-length miniature in the usual format of the day, with fine strokes of transparent color building the face over the white of the ivory. The Met bought it in 2006 through the Fletcher Fund, and the date places it in the 1830s, when the painted miniature was at the height of its popularity.","i":"William Warner Jr., recorded with dates of about 1813 to 1848, died in his mid-thirties, so his career was short, and a dated miniature of 1836, painted when he was about twenty-three, is a record of a young painter making his way. The sitter's name is lost, but a gentleman's portrait of the 1830s generally records a man of business or the professions, in dark coat and cravat, who wanted a likeness for family or fiancee.","c":"A young miniaturist's work of the 1830s is judged by its poise: a sure hand with the head, a restrained palette and a plain ground. Warner's early death means that his output was small, which gives each surviving piece extra interest. As an example of the standard bust of its decade, this portrait is competent and characteristic, and a small window onto a career that ended too soon."},"met:10087":{"d":"A view of New York Harbor with the Brooklyn Bridge is painted in oil on canvas, about 66 by 112 cm, after 1869, by G. Baker, an artist active about 1870 to 1900 about whom little is known. Ships appear in the harbor, and the bridge, begun in 1869, is the dominant monument. The picture records a city in transformation, its waterfront busy with sail and steam, and the new engineering that was joining two cities across the East River.","i":"The Brooklyn Bridge, designed by John Roebling and completed under his son Washington Roebling and his wife Emily, opened in 1883 as the longest suspension bridge in the world. Its construction took fourteen years, and it was celebrated as a marvel of engineering and a sign of national progress. A view of the harbor with the bridge joined civic pride with commerce, and appealed to a public that wanted pictures of its modern city.","c":"A topical picture by a minor hand, this is documentation more than art: the bridge and the busy harbor are the subject, and the painter's gifts are a clear record and the energy of a busy waterfront. The execution is likely in the manner of the harbor-view trade, with ship portraits arranged around a famous monument. Its strengths are subject and verve; its limits are a lack of atmosphere and finesse, and its interest is chiefly historical."},"met:12534":{"d":"A large city picture of about 91 by 127 centimeters, painted about 1859 to 1860, shows the depot of the Third Avenue Railroad in New York, with a horse, a railway station and, among the museum's keywords, a hot-air balloon. The Third Avenue line was one of the horse-drawn street railways that carried New Yorkers up Manhattan's avenues from the 1850s, and the depot sheltered its cars and horses. The picture is a portrait of a business and its place, in the manner of a farm or factory portrait.","i":"Horse railways were the first public transit of the modern city, and in the 1850s companies like the Third Avenue Railroad grew rapidly with the northward push of the city; a depot picture commemorates that enterprise, perhaps for the company's directors. The balloon is a curious accent: balloon ascents were popular attractions of the period, and its presence adds a note of fashionable spectacle to a scene of everyday transport. Little else is recorded of William H. Schenck.","c":"A topographical picture of this kind is valued first for what it records, and Schenck gives plenty: the buildings, the horses, the animated street and the intrusive balloon. His drawing is clear if stiff, and the large scale gives the scene an importance beyond its humble subject. It is not subtle painting, but as a view of New York at the dawn of urban transit, it is lively and informative, a document that happens to be a picture."},"met:12855":{"d":"A throng of animals, in pairs and groups, moves through a landscape toward Noah's Ark, in a large oil on canvas of about 118 by 156 centimeters, painted about 1700. The museum's keywords list a man and a woman, trees and many animals, among them ducks, swans, peafowl, camels, cats, cows, dogs, goats, horses, lions, monkeys and a unicorn. Lodewijk Tieling, recorded as active about 1695 to 1700, uses the Flood story as a pretext for a lively menagerie, in the Dutch tradition of animal painting.","i":"The Ark was a favorite subject for Netherlandish painters because it let them paint every creature in one picture, and the animals' orderly arrival was read as a sign of divine providence. A unicorn among the beasts is not a mistake: seventeenth-century Bibles spoke of unicorns, and people believed that they, like the narwhal's tusk sold as unicorn horn, were real. The picture came to the Met in 1939 from James DeLancey Verplanck and John Bayard Rodgers Verplanck.","c":"Pictures of this kind are judged by the variety and liveliness of their animals, and Tieling gives an abundant, good-humored crowd, from lions to peafowl, in a landscape that is more pleasant than grand. The figures of Noah's family are small, and the biblical story is a frame for natural history. The painter is little known and the design is a little loose, but the picture's sheer abundance makes it a delight."},"met:10194":{"d":"A military review in a city square: soldiers, trees, horsemen and onlookers fill a canvas of about 61 by 91 centimeters dated 1851, showing the Seventh Regiment of the New York militia on parade at Washington Square. The museum's keywords record soldiers, trees, a military subject and equestrianism, and the scene is probably full of the pomp that made such reviews a popular spectacle: uniforms, bands, a mounted officer, crowds of spectators.","i":"The Seventh Regiment was New York's most fashionable militia unit, known as the Silk Stocking Regiment for the well-born young men who joined it, and in 1861 it hurried to Washington to protect the capital at the start of the Civil War. Washington Square, then a military parade ground, was the stage for reviews. Otto Boetticher, a German-born painter best known for Civil War pictures, specialized in military subjects.","c":"Military reviews demand the patient drawing of many small figures in ordered ranks, and Boetticher, a specialist, can be expected to handle them with a draftsman's clarity: the lines of uniforms, the trees of the square, the spectators as a fringe. The brightness and neatness have the charm of a toy-soldier world, with little hint of the war that lay ahead. A good document of a lost New York, more for record than for painterly invention."},"met:12551":{"d":"A man named Levi Hale Willard is the sitter of this oil portrait on canvas of about 69 by 55 centimeters, dated 1857, painted by Samuel H. Sexton. The size is a standard half-length for a professional or business man, and the picture came to the Met in 1891 as a gift of John Sexton, presumably a relative of the painter, a year after Sexton's death. The title preserves the sitter's full name, which many portraits of the period lose.","i":"Samuel H. Sexton (1813 to 1890) was a portrait painter of the middle decades of the century, working in the era when the oil portrait was still the main way for a prosperous American to record himself, before and alongside the new photograph. A sitter such as Levi Hale Willard, named in full, would have commissioned the picture for his family, and the gift to a museum by Sexton's own family suggests that it passed through his hands.","c":"In the 1850s a portraitist had to compete with the daguerreotype, and the answer was solidity, color and a sense of character that a photograph of the day could not give. Sexton belongs to the many competent professionals of the period: his portraits are plain, direct and sober, and their interest is in the face and the carefully stated dress. A sound, unremarkable record, valuable as a named, dated example."},"met:12811":{"d":"Men with rifles, a horse and a dog make their way through snow among trees, in an oil on canvas of about 64 by 76 centimeters, painted about 1855. The museum's keywords list a tree, a horse, a dog, snow, a man, a rifle and a hat, and the picture is a joint work of two painters, Jonathan K. Trego and Joseph Lionel Williams, a collaboration not unusual in the period, which often paired a landscape painter with a specialist in figures and animals.","i":"Trappers, the hunters who supplied the fur trade in the northern woods and the Rockies, were figures of American romance in the 1850s, celebrated in popular prints, novels and the paintings of Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait and Alfred Jacob Miller. A winter scene with horse, dog and rifle gave city buyers a taste of wilderness life, hardy, independent and free of the crowded, regulated world they knew.","c":"A shared picture risks looking divided, and the pleasure of this kind of work lies in how well hands and subjects are joined: snow, dark trees and a little party of men and animals can form a cold, quiet harmony. It is a sentimental view of a harsh life, but a sympathetic one, with the clarity of outline of a Currier and Ives print. Not a major picture, but an agreeable and typical one."},"met:11553":{"d":"A street scene at the site of the Old Brewery and the Five Points Mission in New York is shown in this oil on canvas of about 46 by 66 centimeters, dated 1870. A road, a factory-like building, a city setting and small human figures appear in the view. F. A. Mead, about whom little is known, painted a neighborhood that had become a byword for urban poverty.","i":"The Old Brewery was a notorious tenement in the Five Points district of Lower Manhattan, famous in the mid-nineteenth century for crime and overcrowding, and Charles Dickens had visited the neighborhood in 1842. After it was demolished in 1852, a Methodist mission was built on the site to offer schooling, food and religion to the poor. The painting shows the mission and its surroundings, an image of urban reform.","c":"As a work of art the picture is modest, closer to a topographical record than a painting of high ambition, and the figures are small and generalized. Its interest is documentary and social: it preserves the look of a famously disreputable corner of New York at the moment when philanthropy had begun to remake it. The cityscape is more useful than beautiful, and that is its virtue."},"met:14429":{"d":"A man named Benjamin Daniel Greene is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 10.8 by 8.3 centimeters, dated 1848 and painted by Henry I. Brown, who is recorded as active from 1837 to 1851. At nearly eleven centimeters it is considerably larger than the usual miniature, which gives the artist room for the sitter's head and shoulders and a fuller ground. The Met bought it in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"A larger miniature of 1848 belongs to the last great phase of the form, when the daguerreotype, introduced a decade earlier, was cutting into the trade and miniaturists countered by enlarging their pictures and enriching their color. A named sitter in a bigger format suggests a commission from a man of standing, meant for display on a desk or wall, not carried in a pocket. Brown's recorded career, from 1837 to 1851, almost exactly spans that struggle.","c":"At this larger size a miniature has nowhere to hide, and the painter has to give the head weight as well as delicacy. Brown, a professional of the 1840s, works in the clean, bright style the market wanted, and the bigger ivory gives him a more comfortable stage than the thumbnail-sized pieces of the previous generation. It is a strong, well-judged example of the miniature's late flowering, and a record of a sitter known by name."},"met:10185":{"d":"A soldier and future museum director sits for his portrait: Luigi Palma di Cesnola, painted in oil on canvas, about 69 by 56 centimeters, in 1865 by Jacob D. Blondel. The museum's keywords record a soldier, a man and a portrait, and the date places the picture at the end of the Civil War, when Cesnola, an Italian-born cavalry colonel who had been wounded and captured at Aldie, Virginia, in 1863, was about to leave for a post as American consul on Cyprus. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1967 by Mrs. James Bradley Cook.","i":"Cesnola (1832 to 1904) served in the Union army, received the Medal of Honor for his conduct at Aldie, and in 1865 became United States consul in Cyprus, where his excavations produced thousands of antiquities. The Metropolitan Museum bought his collection in 1872, and he became its first director in 1879, serving until his death. His restorations of the Cypriot sculptures were later attacked as excessive, and the controversy still colors his reputation.","c":"The portrait is a professional record of a man of action, in uniform, in the year of his victory; Blondel gives it the clarity and sobriety that official portraits of soldiers demanded. It lacks the verve of the best military portraits, but it is a valuable document of a figure whose life joined war, archaeology and museum-making in ways few lives did. Its place in the Met, which he led, gives it extra point."},"met:13395":{"d":"James Van Dyck paints himself in a watercolor on ivory only about 4.9 by 4 centimeters, dated between 1834 and 1836. At this scale the painter's own face, worked from a mirror, measures only a few centimeters across, and every feature is a touch of the finest brush. The self-portrait was bought for the Met in 1990 with the Maria DeWitt Jesup Fund, and it is among the smaller miniatures in the museum's collection of American portraits on ivory.","i":"Self-portraits by miniaturists served as advertisements: a painter handed one to a prospective client to show what he could do in the smallest compass. James Van Dyck, recorded as active between 1825 and 1844, bears the name of the great Flemish painter, which can only have helped a portraitist. A self-portrait of the mid-1830s also records the face of a working craftsman at his trade, a type of picture rarely kept.","c":"A miniature self-portrait is a test of nerve: the painter cannot blame the sitter, and the tiny format forgives nothing. Van Dyck's is a modest, direct performance, more interesting as a document of a craftsman than as a work of art, but it carries the attention to detail that the trade required. It is a pleasing small object, and the maker's face is the one he could be sure he knew well."},"met:15192":{"d":"An unnamed boy is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 6.3 by 5 centimeters, dated 1845 and painted by George W. Stevens, who is recorded with dates of about 1820 to 1891. The painter was in his mid-twenties when he made it, and the boy is one of the many children whom parents of the decade wished to see recorded. The Met bought it in 2006 through the Fletcher Fund.","i":"In the mid-1840s portraits of children were increasingly common, as the ideal of childhood as a separate and precious stage took hold and the daguerreotype made likenesses cheaper. A painted miniature offered color and a gentle idealization that the early photograph lacked, and Stevens, a young professional, worked in that market. The boy's name is lost, a common fate for children's portraits, which were treasured by families but seldom labeled.","c":"A boy's portrait at this scale has to catch the animation of a face that rarely sat still, and miniaturists often solved the problem by giving the child a sober stillness. A portrait of this kind belongs to that convention, and its charm lies in the smoothness of the skin and the clarity of the eye. It is a modest, pleasant piece, and a reminder of how many young lives were recorded in this small, precious form."},"met:15230":{"d":"A man identified as L. P. Church is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7.2 by 5.7 centimeters, dated 1834 and painted by William A. Watkins, who is recorded as active between about 1825 and 1867. A bust-length portrait built in fine strokes of transparent color, it dates from the high point of the American miniature. It was bought for the Met in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"The title gives only initials and a surname, a reminder of how little survives about many sitters. Watkins's span of activity, more than forty years, shows a painter who stayed in the trade through its collapse under photography, and a miniature of 1834 comes from the golden years before that decline. A sitter identified even by initials is a step ahead of the many whose names are lost altogether.","c":"The 1830s miniature relies on a clear head and a smooth, even surface, and Watkins, whose career lasted decades, can be presumed to have mastered both. The portrait of a man is compact and sober, without the lace or flowers that give women's miniatures extra incident, so everything depends on the face. It is a competent, characteristic product of its time."},"met:15235":{"d":"A woman named Nancy Kellogg is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7.8 by 6.5 centimeters, dated 1838 and painted by James Whitehorne, who lived from 1803 to 1888. A second miniature by the same hand, of Mary Kellogg, has almost the same size and date, so the two probably form a pair or belong to a family group. The Met bought it in 2006 with the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"The shared surname suggests that Nancy and Mary Kellogg were relatives, perhaps sisters or mother and daughter, painted in a single visit to the artist, as families often did. Itinerant and small-town miniaturists in the 1830s typically painted such sets, which were sold together and passed down together. The fact that both names survive makes the pair more valuable than most miniatures, which usually lose their sitters.","c":"Two portraits by one hand let a viewer compare, and the best thing to look for in such a pair is individuality: the way each face is distinct in structure and expression within the shared format. Whitehorne's work is respectable and his surfaces are clean, though the conventions of the decade pull sitters toward a common type. Taken together, they are a pleasant, well-documented pair."},"met:15236":{"d":"A woman named Mary Kellogg is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7.7 by 6.4 centimeters, painted about 1838 by James Whitehorne. It matches in size, date and source his portrait of Nancy Kellogg, both bought for the Met through the Dale T. Johnson Fund in 2006, and the two were probably made at the same time for the same family. Painted in fine strokes of transparent color, it shows the white of the ivory glowing through the flesh.","i":"The two Kellogg miniatures give a family a record in which each member has a separate portrait but all share a format, a date and an artist's hand, like a small gallery in the palm. Their relationship is not recorded, but the match shows that they were painted together, a common pattern in itinerant practice, when a miniaturist visited a household and painted several members in succession.","c":"The simple costume of a woman of the 1830s gives a miniaturist less to do than a lavish one, and success depends on the face's modeling and the harmony of the whole. A painter of Whitehorne's competence can be expected to give such a face a clean, gentle finish, and the portrait has the quiet charm of a keepsake. It is best seen beside its companion, with which it shares a world."},"met:14999":{"d":"A man is portrayed in watercolor on ivory in this miniature of about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated 1840. The painter, Ezra Atherton, is recorded as active only from 1838 to 1842, a short documented career. The title does not name the sitter, so the portrait is known only as that of a gentleman, and the face is the main element, as is usual in miniatures.","i":"By 1840 the miniature was nearing the end of its long popularity, for the daguerreotype, introduced in the United States in 1839, was spreading quickly in American cities and offered a cheaper likeness. Atherton's brief documented career coincides with this turning point. The portrait remains a tangible reminder of a trade that provided families with small, personal images for generations.","c":"As with many miniatures by little-known painters, the interest is in competence and survival rather than in distinction. Atherton keeps to the clear, polished conventions of the form, and the result is a pleasant if unremarkable likeness. Its chief value is historical: it records a painter whose name has survived on a single page and a man who wished to be remembered."},"met:15014":{"d":"A tiny portrait of an unnamed man, painted in watercolour on a sheet of ivory only about 6 by 5 centimetres, so small that it can be held in the palm. The museum's record lists only a man and a portrait, so his dress, pose and expression cannot be described with confidence. Miniatures of this kind were kept in a hinged case or set in a frame or locket, as keepsakes to be looked at close to the hand.","i":"By about 1845, when this miniature was painted, the daguerreotype, introduced to America in 1839, was already competing with the miniaturist, offering a fast, cheap likeness. Many miniature painters survived by offering what the camera could not, such as colour and a more flattering finish. Brundage, active between 1843 and 1867, was working through that transition. The sitter is anonymous, and Portrait of a Gentleman is the museum's generic label.","c":"As a miniature it is modest in ambition but a good example of what the survivors of a dying trade could still offer: a clear, close likeness in a delicate, translucent medium. Without detail to judge, it is best valued as a small relic of the age between the miniature and the photograph, and as an anonymous portrait it asks the viewer to imagine the man behind it."},"met:15051":{"d":"A girl with a kitten, in a lace-trimmed dress, is the subject of this watercolor on ivory, about 8.5 by 6.8 centimeters, painted about 1835 by an artist recorded as Mrs. Dove. The title names the sitter by her later married name, Mrs. Edward Loyd, with her maiden name, Caroline Louisa Foster, in parentheses, so the portrait shows her as a girl and was probably identified later. The pet adds a tender, lively note to a format that usually stops at the bust.","i":"A child with a kitten was a favorite subject of the age, standing for innocence and for the affection between children and animals, and miniature painters used pets to enliven what could be a stiff bust. Mrs. Dove, a woman painter whose first name is not recorded, represents the many women who worked as portraitists in the first half of the century, often under their married names. The portrait was a family treasure that outlived its maker's name.","c":"The kitten and the lace give a painter more to do than a plain bust, and the pairing of a girl and a cat is a pleasing, memorable idea. The domestic touch of the pet gives the portrait a warmth that more formal miniatures lack. A charming piece, valued as much for its subject as for the record it keeps of a woman artist known only by her married name."},"met:15155":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, painted in watercolour on a sheet of ivory about 8 by 6.5 centimetres, so small that it can be held in the palm. The museum's record lists only a man and a portrait, so his dress, hair and expression cannot be described with confidence. Miniatures of this kind, made in the 1830s, were kept in cases or frames as keepsakes and were cheaper and more personal than oil portraits, until the daguerreotype arrived at the end of the decade.","i":"Edward Crowell Potter, recorded as active around 1825 to 1830, was one of the many local and itinerant miniaturists who served the American public in the early republic, and the Met dates this work to about 1835. The sitter is not named; Portrait of a Gentleman is a generic museum label. Miniatures commonly lose their sitters' names as families dispersed their possessions, which is why so many are known only by a label like this.","c":"As a miniature it is modest in ambition but valuable as an example of the small-scale likenesses that ordinary Americans could afford in the 1830s. Without detail to judge, it is best valued for the intimacy of the form and the survival of its colour on ivory; the artist is a minor figure whose name is attached to few works. A quiet, anonymous presence from a world that had not yet met the camera."},"met:11781":{"d":"A man named Stephen Danforth Hassam is the sitter of this watercolor on ivory, about 7.7 by 5.7 centimeters, dated about 1820 to 1825 and painted by John S. Porter, who is recorded as active between 1822 and 1834. The sitter is named in full, with a middle name that was probably his mother's family name, a common New England practice. The Met bought it in 1938 with the Fletcher Fund.","i":"Porter was one of the many American miniaturists who worked in the early republic, when the ivory miniature was the usual portable portrait of the prosperous. A miniature of the 1820s would be a keepsake for a wife or an engagement present, and many were set in gold cases or lockets that could be worn. The full name on this one is a gain: most sitters of the period survive only as a gentleman or a lady.","c":"A miniature of the 1820s is a compact exercise in clarity, and what it asks is a head drawn with care, a plain ground and a restrained palette. Porter's recorded career was short, little more than a dozen years, but a named sitter, a likely date and a straightforward bust make this a useful example of the American miniature before the boom of the 1830s. It is a competent, plain piece."},"met:10116":{"d":"An eagle, the emblem of the United States, is the subject of this oil on canvas mounted on masonite, about 50 by 60 centimeters, dated broadly to 1860 to 1900, with a red scroll named in the title. The museum's keyword is simply eagle, and the picture is a patriotic emblem painted as a decorative panel, the kind of image that adorned parlors, firehouses, lodge halls and shop fronts in the decades after the Civil War. It was left to the Met in 1979 by the collectors Edgar William and Bernice Chrysler Garbisch.","i":"The eagle was the great symbol of the republic from the Great Seal onward, and its image multiplied after the Civil War in banners, signs, weathervanes and quilts. A scroll usually carried a motto, most often E Pluribus Unum, and the red of the scroll joined the usual white and blue in a patriotic palette. W. H. Bean made such images for a market of ordinary Americans who displayed their loyalty at home.","c":"Emblems like this work by symmetry and bold pattern more than by observation, and the art lies in the strength of the shape and the care of the feathers. Bean has the sign-painter's gift for the simple, emphatic statement. It is naive, decorative and sincere, and it gives a good sense of how patriotism looked in the everyday life of the late nineteenth century."},"met:441379":{"d":"A moonlit sea at the far north of Europe: the museum's keywords record marine art, a boat and night, and the title places the scene at the North Cape, the great cliff headland on the Arctic coast of Norway, in an oil on canvas of about 62 by 85 centimeters dated 1848. Peder Balke painted this remote coast repeatedly, with a simplified, almost abstract handling of sea, sky and rock, and the moon is the single source of light. The Met received it in 2019 from Christen Sveaas.","i":"Balke traveled to the North Cape in 1832, a remote journey few painters made, and spent his career turning his memories into pictures of the Arctic sea. In the 1840s he found a patron in King Louis-Philippe of France, for whom he painted a series of North Cape views, until the revolution of 1848 swept the king away. Balke died in obscurity in 1887, and his bold, atmospheric Arctic seascapes were largely forgotten until the twentieth century.","c":"Balke is one of the surprises of nineteenth-century painting: an artist who treated the sublime in the plainest terms, with a few strokes of dark rock, pale water and light. The moonlit calm is more haunting than the thunder of the stormy Arctic pictures of his contemporaries, and the economy of means looks strikingly modern. A little unfinished in look, it holds the eye with a spare, hypnotic power."},"met:436902":{"d":"The Israelites gather manna in the desert in this nearly square canvas, about 105 by 109 centimeters, by the Italian painter Aurelio Lomi. The museum gives no date, though the artist died in 1622. The recorded motifs are a crowd of men, women and children, tents, an angel and the gathering of manna, so the scene combines the miraculous gift from heaven with the busy, humble work of picking it up from the ground.","i":"In Exodus 16 the Israelites, hungry in the wilderness after the flight from Egypt, find the ground each morning covered with a fine, flaky substance, manna, sent by God as bread from heaven. Christians read the manna as a prefiguration of the Eucharist, the bread of life, which is why the subject was popular in churches and sacristies. Lomi, a Tuscan painter active in Pisa and Genoa, worked in the reformed Counter-Reformation style, which favored clear stories and moral lessons.","c":"Lomi's style is late Mannerist, with elegant, somewhat elongated figures and bright, enamel-like color, and the subject gives him a crowded stage for such figures. The picture offers variety of poses and costume, but its crowd lacks the drama and weight of the Baroque painters then transforming Italian art, and Lomi is better described as a competent follower than an innovator. Its virtue is a clear telling of a biblical story."},"met:436235":{"d":"A woman in her thirties is the subject of this oil portrait of about 100 by 81 centimeters, dated 1776. The museum's keywords record lace, a bow, a ribbon and ruffles, so she is dressed in the fine trimmings of a well-to-do Parisian woman of the day, and one keyword, overweight, notes that the painter, Joseph Siffred Duplessis, portrayed her as she was, a plump woman, without the slimming conventions of the time. The title gives her name and dates, 1742 to 1824.","i":"Duplessis (1725 to 1802), from Carpentras in Provence, became one of the most sought-after portraitists of Paris in the 1770s, admired for likenesses that were plain, truthful and unflattering at a time when most were idealized. His portraits of Gluck, Franklin and Louis XVI show the same direct, observant manner. Madame de Saint-Morys is recorded here with her full name and dates, which many portraits of the period lack.","c":"Duplessis's strength is honesty: he gives his sitter a solid, unforced presence and the lace and ribbons of her dress without turning her into a doll. The result is more appealing than the glossy portraits of fashionable rivals, because it looks like a person. He lacks the sparkle of Vigée Le Brun, who was then rising, but in the portrait of a middle-class lady his plain-speaking style is hard to beat."},"met:436236":{"d":"Benjamin Franklin, then seventy-two and the American commissioner in France, is shown in an oil portrait of about 72 by 58 centimeters painted in 1778 by Joseph Siffred Duplessis. The museum's keywords record only a man and the sitter's name. Franklin sat for Duplessis in the year when France, moved by his persuasive charm and by the American victory at Saratoga, agreed to an alliance, and the picture belongs to the most famous visual record of his years in Paris.","i":"In Paris Franklin was a celebrity, and he cultivated the image of a plain, wise republican philosopher, wearing a simple coat and, often, his own hair rather than a wig. Duplessis's portrait, which exists in several versions, became the basis of countless engravings and copies, and its image later served for the hundred-dollar bill. The Met's version came in 1931 with the bequest of Michael Friedsam.","c":"Duplessis gives Franklin what the occasion required: a face of intelligence and good humor, an unflashy dress and a sense of mild authority. It is a more sober picture than the fur-capped images of the same years, and it is closer to the truth of the man. Not a dazzling painting, but a direct and absorbing one, and among the likenesses that made the sage of Philadelphia an international figure."},"met:438373":{"d":"A frozen landscape is the subject of this small horizontal panel, about 28 by 47 centimeters. The record lists a man, a woman, a castle, ice skating and winter, and the title adds that the castle is imaginary, not a real building. The museum gives no further description of the skaters' number, their clothing or the colors, but the panel belongs to the Dutch tradition of lively winter scenes in which villagers enjoy the ice.","i":"Winter landscapes with skaters were a Dutch specialty from about 1600, and Hendrick Avercamp was its master; van den Berghe belongs to a generation of painters who followed him. The cold of the early seventeenth century, part of what is called the Little Ice Age, made frozen canals and rivers a regular winter scene. An imaginary castle adds a note of fantasy and a vertical accent to the horizontal expanse of ice.","c":"Small winter panels depend on the charm of tiny figures and a cold, pearly light, and the type is delightful when done well: skaters as bright specks on an icy plane, the horizon softened by haze. Van den Berghe is a lesser-known name beside Avercamp, but the invention of an imaginary castle shows an appealing willingness to play. A pleasing cabinet picture rather than a major statement."},"nga:55782":{"d":"A profusion of flowers, mostly ruby red, bright white and flamingo pink, fills a bronze-colored urn on a stone ledge against a dark ground. A band of blooms sweeps from lower left to upper right: a pink rose under a white rose, a white-and-red streaked tulip and a crimson poppy, with pale lilac, sapphire morning glories and golden wheat. A reclining terracotta figure beside a stylized fish, carved on the urn, emerges at upper left. A butterfly sits on the tulip, a snail crawls along the ledge and an earwig eats a caterpillar. The canvas is about 67 by 51 centimeters.","i":"Dutch flower pieces delighted in rarity and abundance, and streaked tulips, once the stars of the tulip craze, were prized. The insects and the wheat add other layers: butterflies and caterpillars are signs of transformation, snails of slowness, and the earwig eating the caterpillar of the small cruelty of nature, so the glory of the blooms is shadowed by the reminder that all things pass. A sculpted urn adds the dignity of art to the bouquet.","c":"Kouwenbergh composes the bouquet as a diagonal band, which gives the picture a strong pulse, and his flowers are painted with a gloss and precision typical of the Dutch tradition at its late, polished stage. The urn with its relief is a clever, slightly eccentric touch, and the insects are a pleasure to find. Beside the great flower painters, Jan van Huysum among them, he is a little stiff, but the picture is a handsome, well-organized piece."},"met:438387":{"d":"Mary is adored by a company of saints and angels, in a wide oil on copper of about 49 by 74 centimeters, painted about 1609. The museum's keywords record angels, a dog and the saints Anthony the Great and Francis of Assisi, with Saint Catherine among the themes, so the picture is a gathering of holy figures around the Virgin, probably in a landscape, in the glowing, jewel-like colors that the smooth copper supports. Scarsellino painted such small devotional pictures for private patrons.","i":"A Virgin adored by saints is a sacra conversazione, a meeting of holy figures across time, whose presence reflects the devotions of the patron: Anthony the Great, the founder of monasticism, Francis, the poor man of Assisi, and Catherine of Alexandria, the learned martyr. Painted on copper, which does not warp and takes fine detail, it was an object for a private chapel or a collector's cabinet. Scarsellino (Ippolito Scarsella, 1550 to 1620) worked in Ferrara.","c":"Scarsellino's gift was for soft, glowing color and a dreamy landscape, learned from Veronese and the Venetians and softened by Correggio, and a small copper panel suits it: the surface shines like enamel. His figures are graceful rather than strong, and the faces tend to a sweet sameness, but the picture has a sustained charm and a rich, warm harmony. A good example of Ferrarese devotional art on the intimate scale for which he was known."},"met:437596":{"d":"Two girls are the subject of this large pastel, about 80 by 64 centimeters, dated 1770 and laid down on canvas. The museum's keyword is girl, and the title names the relationship. Pastel, a medium of powdered pigment pressed into sticks, was the favored technique for French portraits of the century because its soft, powdery surface could imitate the bloom of young skin, and an amateur of talent like the Abbé de Saint-Non could use it with charm.","i":"Jean Claude Richard, Abbé de Saint-Non (1727 to 1791), was a priest of independent means who drew, etched and collected, and who is best remembered for the Voyage pittoresque de Naples et de Sicile, a lavish illustrated account of southern Italy that drew on Fragonard and Hubert Robert, with whom he had traveled in Italy in 1760. A pastel of two sisters shows the other side of his art, the family portrait of the French salon world.","c":"Pastel rewards a delicate touch, and an amateur like Saint-Non is likely to have been charmed by the medium's softness: pale cheeks, ribbons and lace translate easily into powdery strokes. The pleasure of such a double portrait is the pairing of two faces, their likeness and difference, and the affection that holds them together. It is a gentle, graceful picture of a kind much loved in the 1770s, more charming than profound."},"met:437761":{"d":"A woman's portrait on ivory, painted about 1800 by the German artist Peter Edward Stroely. The museum's keywords record only a woman and the portrait type. Ivory miniatures of this date were set in gold cases and worn as jewelry or kept as keepsakes, and the soft, translucent washes over the white support give the flesh its characteristic glow. The Met bought it in 1950 with the Rogers Fund, and it is a fair example of the European miniature at the start of the nineteenth century.","i":"Around 1800 the portrait miniature was at its height across Europe, and a woman's portrait would be an exchange between lovers, a keepsake for a parent or a memorial. A woman painted at this date would usually wear the loose, high-waisted fashion that followed the French Revolution, and she might be shown with a simple ribbon or curl rather than the towering hair of the old regime. Stroely's name is spelled variously in the records, a mark of how little is known of him.","c":"A miniature of 1800 asks for clarity, delicacy and grace, and the work of a skilled German miniaturist in these years would be refined, polished and slightly cool. The portrait is best valued as an example of the European miniature at its peak, with the translucent flesh tones and fine hair that made ivory so beautiful. The sitter's name is lost, but the pleasure of holding a small face from a vanished world remains."},"nga:294":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, about 60 by 154 centimetres, shows a procession of dozens of small figures, mostly armoured soldiers on foot and horseback, winding through rocky hills to the gates of a walled city. At the right a man in black armour with a black wreath rides a golden throne on a large golden platform drawn by two cream-white horses and points his sceptre at a seated person who looks up with raised hands. Captives sit in alcoves on the platform's side, a rider's chest is lettered SPQR, and dogs nip at the horses' heels.","i":"The title names Marcus Furius Camillus, the Roman general of the early fourth century BC who was honoured with a triumph and remembered as a model of Roman virtue. The letters SPQR, the Senate and People of Rome, mark the setting. A long, low panel of this shape suggests the front of a cassone, the decorated chest that a Florentine bride brought to her husband's house, and such chests often carried scenes from ancient history that offered models of conduct to the young couple.","c":"The panel is a lively piece of pageantry: the winding procession, the prancing horses, the dogs and the glint of gold keep the eye moving, and the small figures give the sense of a crowd. The drawing is sharp and the colour clear. But the figures are repeated types, and the horses and men are stiffer than in Gozzoli's festive processions. It is decorative storytelling of good quality rather than high art."},"nga:320":{"d":"A boy is seen from the chest up, facing the viewer with a neutral expression. He has fair skin, light eyebrows, pink lips and green eyes, and his curly brown hair falls around his ears. He wears a high-collared garment, an olive-green undershirt beneath a reddish-orange outer garment. Behind him lies a serene landscape of rolling hills, pointed rocks, scattered trees and bushes under a blue sky with wispy clouds. The panel is about 42 by 36 centimetres, painted in oil and tempera.","i":"Portraits of children were made in Florence to record a family's heirs, and as keepsakes in a period of high child mortality. The frontal view, the landscape and the smooth modelling follow Florentine habits of the 1470s, and the use of oil mixed with tempera shows a painter experimenting with a Netherlandish technique. The boy's name is not recorded, so he is read as a type: a Florentine child of a well-off family, painted with calm simplicity.","c":"A tender, direct image. The frontal gaze, the fine modelling of the face and the contrast between the warm figure and the cool landscape are pleasing, and the simplicity gives the boy a quiet dignity. The face is a little blank, the pose stiff, and the landscape formulaic. Biagio d'Antonio was a capable follower of the major Florentine masters rather than an innovator, and the picture's charm lies in its plainness."},"met:435664":{"d":"A young man is shown in a portrait on a wood panel, about 54 by 39 centimetres, painted in tempera, probably around 1470. The sitter is not named. Florentine portraits of this date usually show the head and shoulders of a young man in profile or three-quarter view against a plain ground or a landscape, in fashionable dress and with a calm, sober expression, so that the picture records both a likeness and a social position.","i":"Portraits of young men in late fifteenth-century Florence served several purposes: they might mark a betrothal, a coming of age or a family's pride in its heirs, and they were kept in the home. The clothing, hair and bearing signal rank and fashion. The sitter here is unidentified, so the picture is read as a type: a Florentine youth of the generation of Botticelli and Lorenzo de' Medici. Biagio d'Antonio worked in the circle of Florence's leading painters.","c":"A modest portrait: the drawing is clear, the colour restrained and the young man's open face agreeable. But the likeness is rather generalised, and in a city where Botticelli, Verrocchio and Pollaiuolo were painting subtler portraits, Biagio d'Antonio appears as a follower. It is charming and sincere, though not first-rank, and is best valued as evidence of Florentine taste in the later fifteenth century."},"met:435665":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, about 61 by 153 centimetres, painted in tempera with gilt ornament, shows scenes from the story of the Argonauts, the Greek heroes who sailed with Jason to win the Golden Fleece. The Met's keywords record Jason, Orpheus, a ship, horses, dogs, men and a woman, so several episodes seem to be shown in one continuous landscape, as was usual in Florentine narrative panels of this kind.","i":"The Argonauts were the Greek heroes who sailed with Jason on the ship Argo to Colchis to fetch the Golden Fleece, and the musician Orpheus was among the crew. Renaissance Florentines loved such stories of adventure and cunning, which humanist education made familiar. Long panels of ancient myth decorated chests and the wainscoting of rooms in wealthy homes, providing learned entertainment, and a panel of this shape was probably made for such a use.","c":"Biagio d'Antonio is an attractive storyteller: the figures are small and brisk, the colours bright, and the ship, horses and dogs keep the eye moving across the panel. The classical tale is treated like a chivalric romance, usually in the fashionable dress of the day rather than antique costume, which is part of the charm. The drawing is rather hard and the faces repetitive, but as decorative narrative it succeeds."},"met:435666":{"d":"A long panel, about 69 by 150 centimetres, painted in tempera, shows episodes from the Old Testament story of Joseph. The Met's keywords record men, women, camels, dogs and horses, so the panel probably includes the caravan of camels to which Joseph's brothers sold him and the Egyptian setting in which he rose to power. As in other Florentine narrative panels of its kind, several moments of the story are likely to share a single landscape.","i":"The story of Joseph, told in Genesis 37 to 50, follows the favoured son sold by his brothers to traders, rising in Egypt after interpreting Pharaoh's dreams, and finally forgiving the brothers who come to him in famine. It was popular in Renaissance Florence as a model of virtue rewarded and forgiveness granted, and its many episodes suited the continuous storytelling of a long panel. Such panels decorated private houses, in chests or room panelling.","c":"A pleasantly lively narrative: the camels, horses and dogs give the long panel exotic variety, and Biagio d'Antonio's brisk, clear figures make the episodes easy to follow. The drawing is somewhat hard, the faces generic and the landscape setting formulaic. But as an example of Florentine storytelling it has real charm, and the fresh colour and crowded incident reward a slow, section-by-section reading."},"nga:37003":{"d":"A young woman sits sideways on a chair with her arm along its back and turns to smile at the viewer, her lips slightly parted. She wears a white cap, a broad starched collar edged with lace, a black bodice and rose-pink sleeves and skirt. In her right hand she holds a long brush, and in the left a palette, a rag and a fan of about twenty brushes. Behind her, on an easel, stands a half-finished painting of a laughing violinist in a blue cap, whose bow runs parallel to her brush. The wall behind is plain gray-brown, and the chair's gilded post glints at lower left.","i":"Judith Leyster of Haarlem was about twenty-one when she painted this, around 1630. She was praised as a prodigy in 1628 and, about 1633, became the first woman admitted as a master of the Haarlem Guild of Saint Luke. Her surname means lodestar, or leading star, and a contemporary historian made a point of it. The violinist on the easel comes from her own Merry Company. The National Gallery's catalog reads the picture as a claim to professional mastery, with the brush rhyming with the violin bow. Her formal clothes are not what anyone paints in. The picture was long taken for a work by Frans Hals.","c":"The invitation is the best thing about it: the turned body and quick smile bring the sitter forward in a way she clearly learned from Hals, and the little violinist is a witty answer to her own portrait. The execution is less assured. The lace and the hands are painted more cautiously than Hals would have done, the composition is a little flat, and the lower left has been repaired. But as a declaration of a professional woman's skill and ease, it stands comparison with male painters' self-portraits of its decade."},"nga:142346":{"d":"A round portrait shows the head and shoulders of a young blond boy in profile facing right against a tan background, with pale, peachy skin and flushed cheeks. Light from the left catches his cheek, jaw and ear as he gazes straight ahead; he has long dark eyelashes, a turned-up nose and closed coral-pink lips, and light brown hair highlighted with gold, a tendril falling past his chin. He wears a fitted jacket, perhaps brown velvet with golden stitching, and a narrow white ruff. The paint is loose, with visible brushstrokes.","i":"Judith Leyster (1609 to 1660) worked in Haarlem, where she became one of the few women admitted to the painters' guild, in 1633, and ran a workshop with students. She was close to Frans Hals, and her loose brushwork and lively children recall his. A round portrait of a boy in profile, perhaps a study or a private commission, shows her skill at catching a fleeting expression, and her work was so like Hals's that it was long mistaken for his.","c":"The boy is caught with remarkable freshness: the flushed cheek, the long eyelashes, the loose, quick strokes that make hair gleam. The profile and round format give the picture a classical, medal-like clarity, while the handling is lively and modern. Leyster shows here the gift for children that was perhaps her strongest, and the picture is a small, delightful reminder that her reputation, once wrongly absorbed into Hals's, rests on real individual achievement."},"met:437286":{"d":"A large history painting, about 197 by 148 centimeters, painted about 1740 to 1741, shows a death scene with a man and a woman, as the museum's keywords record. The title names Harmonia, a heroine of classical legend, and the tall format and the scale suggest a work of the kind that young painters made to win a place at the Paris Academy, where reputations were made with grand narrative pictures drawn from ancient history and myth.","i":"Jean-Baptiste Marie Pierre (1714 to 1789) won the Prix de Rome in 1735, spent several years in Rome, and returned to Paris around 1740, when this picture was painted. He went on to a distinguished career, becoming first painter to the king in 1770. The subject of Harmonia's death, drawn from ancient history or legend, belongs to the Academy's taste for stories of loyalty and sacrifice, a way of showing a painter's command of emotion and anatomy in a single large composition.","c":"The ambition is high and the execution capable: Pierre was a thoroughly trained history painter, and a grand subject at nearly two meters tall asks for just that, though it does not look for the grace of Boucher or the force of the great Baroque masters. The picture belongs to the cooler, more learned side of French painting in the 1740s, and its restraint is both its dignity and its limit. A solid, serious early work."},"nga:41607":{"d":"A young man, shown from the waist up, looks out sideways from beneath hooded eyes. His wavy dark hair is rumpled, and his pale face is lit from the left. He wears a dark blue greatcoat with a cape and velvet collar thrown loosely over his shoulders, above a brown double-breasted jacket, a yellow waistcoat, a white shirt and a black stock. A small red badge is pinned to his lapel, and a ring glints on the little finger of the hand resting on a table draped in orange-red cloth, where the gold tassels of a scroll dangle. The background shades from olive green to warm brown, and the picture is signed and dated in Rome, 1810, at the lower right.","i":"Charles Marcotte (1773–1864) was a French official in Napoleonic Rome, where he served as inspector of forests and waters, and he became one of Ingres's most loyal friends and patrons. Ingres painted this portrait in 1810, when he was living in Rome and earning much of his income from portraits; Marcotte reportedly ordered it as a present for his mother, and is said to have found it too severe. The loose greatcoat, rumpled hair and direct, slightly uneasy gaze give a note of Romantic restlessness to a form that is otherwise precisely drawn and firmly classical in its composition.","c":"Among the finest of Ingres's early portraits. The heavy blue coat, painted with a velvety softness, is a lesson in describing a sitter through his clothes, and the sidelong, searching gaze gives the face an uneasy intelligence that smooth finish alone could not. The long, nervous hand is a signature Ingres touch. If anything is weak, it is the cramped space, where the dark mass of coat swallows the lower half, and the palette is somewhat sombre. Marcotte's reported dislike of the picture is a reminder that Ingres could be unflattering to the people he painted."},"nga:41606":{"d":"Pope Pius VII presides at a ceremony in the Sistine Chapel, the title says, in an oil on canvas of about 75 by 93 centimeters dated 1814. Ingres, then living in Rome, set a papal ritual in the chapel painted by Michelangelo, with the pope and his court within a vast, richly decorated space. The picture is a cabinet-size view of church ceremony, painted in the clear, precise manner that he had learned from his teacher, Jacques-Louis David.","i":"Pius VII had been held prisoner by Napoleon from 1809 and returned to Rome in May 1814, a moment of joy for Catholic Rome. Ingres, a French artist in the city whose patrons had been French officials of the empire, now painted the restored pope at worship. In the same year he painted the Grande Odalisque for Napoleon's sister, Caroline Murat, queen of Naples, a reminder of how his career moved between the old regime and the new.","c":"Ingres is a painter of the line, and a crowded ceremonial interior was never his natural subject; precision and a historical moment are the strengths of such a picture, warmth and spontaneity its weaknesses. Still, it is a fascinating early work, showing the young master turning contemporary events into a polished, elegant record, with the sharp drawing that would later make him the leader of French classicism."},"met:438818":{"d":"A stout young man with dark, tousled hair and a faintly smiling, rosy face looks directly at the viewer, filling the canvas from the chest up. He wears a brown-olive greatcoat with a wide dark velvet collar and large buttons, over a white shirt with a frilled front and a loosely knotted white cravat; one hand, with a ring, gathers a black cloak against his chest. Behind him a stormy evening sky opens to a strip of pale blue, and on the plain beyond are umbrella pines at the left and the broken arches of the Colosseum at the right. The face is smooth and finely modelled, and the coat is painted more broadly.","i":"The sitter, Joseph-Antoine Moltedo (born 1775), was an official in the French administration of Rome, which Napoleon had annexed to the Empire in 1809, and one of the Frenchmen there who patronised Ingres. The Colosseum in the background marks the Roman setting. The Met dates the portrait to about 1810, in the years of Ingres's long residence in Rome (1806–20). He had won the Prix de Rome in 1801, and portraits of the city's French community were an important source of income. The open coat and relaxed pose present Moltedo as a man of business rather than a dignitary.","c":"A warm, direct likeness. The head has the solid modelling and clear contour of Ingres at his most straightforward: the plump cheeks, the clear eyes and the unforced half-smile make Moltedo an immediate presence, and the stormy sky sets off the white cravat. The picture is more uneven elsewhere; the hand and the cloak held at the bottom are vague, and the landscape feels conventional rather than integrated. Beside the sharper, more tense portrait of Marcotte, it is easier and more genial. It is a picture to like for its warmth rather than its grandeur."},"met:436703":{"d":"A woman sits three-quarter length, turned slightly, with a calm, faintly smiling gaze. Her dark curled hair is dressed with a small bow, and her shoulders are bare above a black silk dress gathered across the bodice, with sheer black sleeves tied at the wrists by ribbons. A long gold chain loops down her front. One arm rests along the carved back of a red chair, the other hand lies on her lap, and over the chair arm and her knees falls a Kashmir-style shawl striped in orange, yellow and cream with floral borders. At the right, pink flowers stand on a marble table. The background is dark brown.","i":"The sitter, Françoise Poncelle, was the wife of Jacques-Louis Leblanc, secretary to Elisa Baciocchi, Napoleon's sister and Grand Duchess of Tuscany, whom she served as lady-in-waiting. Ingres, living in Florence, painted both Leblanc portraits in 1823, soon after meeting the couple, and described Leblanc as a rich, generous Frenchman who had adopted them. Both portraits are now in the Metropolitan Museum. Edgar Degas bought them in 1896 and called them the event of his life as a collector, and the Museum purchased them from his estate sale in 1918.","c":"The black dress is the high point. Its sheen, folds and sheer sleeves are handled with such control that the Kashmir shawl, bright as it is, serves as a foil rather than a rival. Ingres's firm contour gives the arm and hand clarity. The face is a smooth, rather fixed oval, and the gaze, though pleasant, offers little temperament. It is a portrait of rank and polish more than of character, and it is strongest where Ingres is strongest, in the description of surfaces."},"met:436706":{"d":"A man named Jacques-Louis Leblanc, born in 1774, is the subject of this oil portrait of about 121 by 96 centimeters, painted in 1823 by Ingres. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait. The date places it in Ingres's Florentine years, when he lived mainly by portraits and drawings, and the roughly three-quarter-length format is the one he used for his most considered portraits of friends and clients. The Met bought it in 1918 with the Wolfe Fund.","i":"Ingres painted a pair of portraits of Leblanc and his wife in 1823. In such portraits the sitters' clothes and surroundings are described with a precision that reads almost as a tribute: the sheen of silk, the weight of a coat, the fall of a cravat. Ingres supported himself in Florence by such commissions, and the portraits of these years count among the most refined of his career.","c":"Ingres's male portraits of this period are less glamorous than his portraits of women but in their way more searching: the sitter's face is a field of exact, undemonstrative observation, built from line and subtle modeling. The effect may seem cool, but the intelligence of the drawing and the sense of a real man are what remain. It is a fine instance of Ingres as a portraitist, severe, scrupulous and absorbed."},"nga:46509":{"d":"A man's face and upper body are seen from below, in profile facing right, in a small oil on canvas laid on wood, only about 25 by 19 centimeters. He has light brown skin, a long straight nose, a thick dark beard and curly dark hair under a yellow helmet tipped up on his head, wears a warm reddish-orange garment, and clenches his left hand, in shadow, against his chest. The background is a muted blend of yellows and greens, and the signature Ingres is at lower right.","i":"The title identifies the figure as Ulysses, the cunning hero of Homer's Odyssey. The low viewpoint is the kind a painter uses for a figure to be seen from beneath, as on a ceiling, and 1827 was the year of Ingres's The Apotheosis of Homer, a ceiling for the Louvre; whether the head is connected to it is uncertain. Small, finished oil heads like this were prized by collectors.","c":"A small head by Ingres is a lesson in how much drawing can do: the nose, beard and helmet are laid out with a sharp, sculptural clarity, and the single clenched hand gives the figure a charge of feeling. The painter's famous smoothness is here slightly relaxed, and the effect is more vivid than many of his grand finished pictures. It is a jewel of its kind, concentrated, decisive and a little mysterious."},"met:436708":{"d":"A nude woman reclines in a harem interior, painted in shades of gray and brown, in an oil on canvas of about 83 by 109 centimeters, dated between about 1824 and 1834. The museum's keywords record an odalisque, a nude and Orientalist painting. It is a monochrome, or grisaille, version of the composition that Ingres first painted in full color in 1814, the Grande Odalisque, in which a woman seen from behind turns her head toward the viewer, and the long, smooth line of her back is the picture's chief marvel.","i":"A monochrome version of a famous painting was probably made as a model for a print, the usual way for such a picture to reach a wide public, since an engraver could work from its tones. An odalisque is a woman of the Ottoman harem, and Ingres, who never traveled to the East, built his Orient from books, prints and the studio. The original of 1814 was made for Caroline Murat, queen of Naples, and critics joked that its heroine had too many vertebrae.","c":"The grisaille shows the structure beneath the color: the sinuous contour, the flow of the back and the careful modulation of tone from highlight to shadow. In monochrome the Odalisque looks less like an exotic fantasy and more like a piece of sculpture, and the liberties with anatomy, which critics mocked, read as a deliberate choice of line over truth. It is a fascinating document of Ingres's method, and a beautiful object in its own right."},"met:436707":{"d":"A woman in profile is the subject of this small oil on canvas, about 41 by 33 centimeters, painted about 1831 to 1834 by Ingres. The title names her as Madame Edmond Cavé, Marie-Elisabeth Blavot, born about 1806, and the museum's keywords record a woman, profile and portrait. The profile, a format borrowed from ancient coins and Renaissance medals, gives the picture a classical simplicity and concentrates attention on the contour of face and neck.","i":"Marie-Elisabeth Cavé was the wife of Edmond Cavé, a civil servant in the fine arts administration, and apparently a friend of Ingres; she is also known as an amateur painter and author of a drawing method, Le Dessin sans maitre, published in 1850. Ingres, who prized drawing above all, made her portrait small and intimate, a companion in size to the later portrait of her husband. The Met received it in 1943 by the bequest of Grace Rainey Rogers.","c":"Ingres could be harsh to sitters he did not like and affectionate to those he did, and this small portrait, if it is the work of a friend, shows it: a clear, delicate contour, the face rendered with great economy and an unmistakable air of intelligence. The profile format suits his linear genius, and its modest scale lets the quality of line speak. It is a gentle, unpretentious picture that gains from comparison with his grander portraits."},"met:436704":{"d":"A man in a small oil on canvas of about 41 by 33 centimeters, painted in 1844 by Ingres, is identified as Edmond Cavé (1794 to 1852). It has the same size as Ingres's portrait of his wife, painted about a decade earlier, so the two are a pair in everything but date. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait. A modest, direct likeness, it shows a man of about fifty without display.","i":"Cavé was a senior official of the fine arts administration at the Ministry of the Interior in the 1840s, which put him in a position of power over artists' commissions and made friendship with him valuable to a painter. Ingres painted several friends in small format in these years, outside the demands of society portraiture. The picture came to the Met in 1943 from Grace Rainey Rogers, with its companion.","c":"A small portrait gives Ingres no room for display, and he does not need it: the head is modeled with exactness and restraint, the eyes alert and the mouth firm. A man's portrait by Ingres is a study in character through drawing, and this one is quiet, observant and sympathetic. It matches the portrait of Madame Cavé in size and temper, and the two are better seen together."},"nga:32696":{"d":"A young Parisian woman of wealth, Madame Moitessier, is shown in a three-quarter-length portrait of about 147 by 100 centimeters, painted in 1851 by Ingres. The sitter, Marie-Clotilde-Inès Moitessier (1821 to 1897), was the wife of a rich businessman, and Ingres began the portrait in 1844, working on it for seven years. He painted a second portrait of her in a different pose, now in London, in the 1850s.","i":"Ingres took seven years because he was a slow, perfectionist worker who threw out his first ideas and because the sittings were interrupted. Madame Moitessier was among the great beauties of the Second Empire, and Ingres, in his early seventies when he finished, made her an image of elegance, with the dress, the jewelry and the poise that defined the grand bourgeoise of his time. It is among the best-known portraits of the nineteenth century.","c":"This is Ingres at his most opulent and most severe: the line of the shoulders, neck and arms is as exact as a drawing, the fabrics are described with the richness he brought to every surface, and the sitter's face has a cool, grave beauty. The danger is a hardness that turns a woman into an object of display; the saving grace is the intelligence in the face. A portrait of power, immensely skilled and a little forbidding."},"met:438434":{"d":"The Virgin adores the consecrated Host, the sacramental bread marked with the monogram IHS, at an altar, in a small oil on canvas of about 40 by 33 centimeters, dated 1852. The museum's keywords also record Saint Helena, Saint Louis of France and a man, so the Virgin is accompanied by saints, a conventional company for a devotional work, and the Host, raised on the altar, is the picture's focus.","i":"Adoration of the Host, the bread believed to be the body of Christ, was a central devotion of nineteenth-century French Catholicism, and the pairing of the Virgin with Saint Helena, finder of the True Cross, and Louis IX, the crusader king, suggests a commission linked to a royal or imperial patron whose names or patron saints these were. Ingres, a devout classicist, painted a number of such religious pictures late in his career. The Met received it in 2005 from Lila and Herman Shickman.","c":"Ingres's religious pictures are less admired than his portraits and nudes, and a small devotional work like this one shows why: the drawing is pure and the color soft, but the emotion is held back by the clean, rather chilly finish. Yet it has the grace of line and the clarity of composition for which he was admired, and the scale makes it intimate. A work for believers and for admirers of Ingres's refined late style."},"met:436705":{"d":"The head of Saint John the Evangelist, the youngest of the apostles, is shown in an oil on canvas laid down on wood, about 39 by 27 centimeters, probably painted about 1818 to 1820. It is a study of a single head, the kind of preparatory work a painter makes to fix a face and its lighting before including it in a larger composition. The Met bought it in 1985 with the Wolfe Fund and a bequest.","i":"Ingres made oil studies of heads for his large figure paintings, and this one is probably connected with Christ Giving the Keys to Saint Peter, the picture he completed in 1820 for the church of Trinità dei Monti in Rome, in which the apostles gather around Christ. John, young and beardless in the Christian tradition, is the apostle who leaned on Jesus at the Last Supper; Ingres's devotion to Raphael is evident in the choice of subject.","c":"A head study shows a painter thinking, and the beauty of this one is likely to lie in the contour and the soft modeling, the clarity of Raphael as Ingres understood him. The loosened handling of a study is a relief from his finished smoothness. It is a small, concentrated piece and a window on his preparatory method, and the calm idealism of the type is the essence of his classicism."},"nga:46105":{"d":"A crowned man in a yellow breastplate, sea-green tunic and blue gold-patterned cloak lunges with a raised sword at a helmeted man in royal-blue armor, who leans back with both hands raised. The confrontation takes place before a long banquet table with a rumpled white cloth, on a platform of five steps. About a dozen people react or cling to each other beyond the table; an ashen-faced crowned woman clutches another woman, and a man sprawls on the checked floor, gripping an urn. Silver vessels stand on the steps. Oil on canvas, about 130 by 97 centimeters, probably 1700 to 1705.","i":"The scene comes from Plutarch's Life of Alexander: at the wedding feast of his father, Philip II of Macedon, to a new wife, a quarrel over Alexander's legitimacy led Philip, drunk, to draw his sword against his son, but he stumbled and fell. The story shows the tension between father and heir and Alexander's quick temper and wit. Baroque painters liked such moments of royal passion because they allowed rich costume, crowded banquets and strong gestures.","c":"A grand, elegant theater piece in Creti's refined late Baroque manner, in which the swirl of cloaks and gestures is organized into clear, balanced groups. The colors are cool and delicate, the poses elegantly staged and the architecture spacious, but the passion is more graceful than raw: the ferocity of Philip's lunge is tempered by the painter's good taste. It is typical of the Bolognese tradition of polished history painting in the early 1700s."},"nga:150903":{"d":"A large brown building seen from the side stands at the left, with a tall chimney and several layers of windows, in a small oil on paperboard of about 39 by 54 centimeters, dated 1900. At the right, dark trees dot the horizon; closer to us lie a green field with small bushes and a pale blue sky with gray clouds. The brushstrokes are thick and textured, giving the sky and the landscape a feeling of movement.","i":"Paula Becker, who later added her husband's name, was twenty-four when she painted this, a young painter living in the artists' colony of Worpswede near Bremen, where she learned landscape painting from the older painters of the circle, Otto Modersohn among them, whom she married in 1901. A plain industrial building is an unusual subject for the colony, whose painters preferred peasants, moors and birches. She died in 1907, aged thirty-one, a few weeks after the birth of her daughter.","c":"Even in a small, early landscape her instinct is for the plain and the weighty: the factory is a block, the sky a thick slab of paint, and nothing is prettified. The thick brushwork and the simplified masses already point away from the sweet moorland pictures of her teachers toward the compact forms of her mature work. It is a modest picture, but it shows the seriousness and independence that make her one of the founders of German modernism."},"nga:138735":{"d":"A serene stretch of water and a rocky coast run to the horizon, in a wide oil on canvas of about 66 by 115 centimeters, dated 1866. In the center, indistinct figures in red, blue and brown gather on a rocky strip near the water; in front, small tan and brown rocks poke through still blue water. On the left rise gray mountains and a group of far-off white-walled, red-roofed buildings, and on the right the water continues to a distant shore under fluffy clouds in a wide blue sky.","i":"The Durance is a river of Provence, wide, shallow and gravelly, and its banks were a place where village women washed linen, a daily task that painters of the time, from Courbet onward, treated as a subject worthy of art. Paul Guigou (1834 to 1871) worked as a notary's clerk before devoting himself to painting, and he is remembered for his luminous views of Provence, in which the glare of southern light flattens form into broad planes of color.","c":"Guigou's strength is the sense of an enormous, quiet light: the pale water, the chalky rock and the small, indistinct figures set in a space that seems to breathe. The brushwork is plain and the drawing of the women merely suggested, but the picture's calm and its colors, blues, tans and whites, are gently beautiful. He died too young to develop fully, and his work anticipates the Provencal painters of the next generation."},"met:436840":{"d":"A woman in a pale blue-silver satin gown trimmed with lace sits at an easel, wearing a wide straw hat with feathers and a blue ribbon and looking directly at the viewer. Her brush hovers above a palette and she holds a mahlstick. Two young women lean over the chair-back behind her: one in white with a gauzy cap, the other in a brown gown with a ruffled sleeve and a bonnet. At left the easel's wooden back rises like a dark wedge, with a plaster bust and a statue in the dim background beyond. At lower right is a stool with a red cushion and rolled papers. The satin glows against the polished parquet.","i":"Adélaïde Labille-Guiard exhibited this life-size picture at the Salon of 1785. She had been admitted to the Académie royale in 1783, on the same day as Vigée Le Brun, at a time when the Académie limited women members to four. By showing herself teaching two young women, Marie Gabrielle Capet and Marie Marguerite Carreaux de Rosemond, she made a public argument for admitting more women. The bust in the background is said to be of her father. The painting stayed in the artist's family until 1905, and the Metropolitan Museum received it in 1953.","c":"The picture is as clever as it is polished. Labille-Guiard states a case, that women can be professionals and teachers, by showing a studio where the mistress is in full fashion, and the satin and its reflection on the floor are beautifully done. The pupils are posed more as ornaments than as students, no one is working, and the easel at the left is a heavy dark wedge that dominates the composition. Still, the group is more daring than most academic self-portraits of the 1780s, and its argument is clear even now."},"met:439405":{"d":"Madame Élisabeth, the younger sister of Louis XVI, is portrayed in pastel on seven sheets of blue paper joined together and laid down on canvas, a support about 79 by 65 centimeters. She wears a fichu, a cap with a feather and a striped fabric with buttons: details of fashionable informal dress rather than full court costume. The princess is the single subject, painted about 1787 in a medium that suited quick, delicate work.","i":"Élisabeth (1764-1794) was devout and close to her brother the king, stayed with the royal family through the Revolution, and was guillotined in 1794. Labille-Guiard, admitted to the Académie royale in 1783, worked for the king's relatives and was one of very few women to hold a place there. The simple dress fits the fashion of the 1780s for natural, unceremonious clothes, which royal portraits sometimes adopted to present an approachable image.","c":"Pastel suits Labille-Guiard: the medium gives skin a soft, matte bloom and lets fabric be rendered with quick, crisp touches. Long overshadowed by her rival Vigée Le Brun, she was the more reserved and truthful portraitist, and a likeness like this prefers candor to flattery. It is a precise rather than a showy piece, and a reminder of how much skill the royal family could find among women artists."},"met:436310":{"d":"A churchman, a prelate, as the title says, is shown in an oil portrait on a plate of copper, painted about 1580 and attributed to Lavinia Fontana. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, and the choice of copper, a smooth, hard support that takes the finest detail, suggests a small, precious picture rather than a state portrait, made to be held and examined. Prelates, officials of the Church of senior rank, were among the leading patrons of portraits in Counter-Reformation Italy.","i":"Lavinia Fontana (1552 to 1614), from Bologna, was the daughter of a painter and the most successful woman painter of her day, a portraitist of the city's aristocracy and clergy, and later of the papal court in Rome. She painted women with a jeweler's care for dress and ornaments, and clerical sitters with a plain dignity. An attribution marks a degree of uncertainty, but the portrait fits her practice of the 1580s.","c":"Whoever painted it, a portrait on copper of a churchman asks for exactness in the face and in the details of his clothing, and the best work in this vein, like Fontana's, joins sharp observation with quiet authority. The smooth support allows a fineness of finish that canvas does not. A viewer can enjoy it as a refined small portrait of the Bolognese school at the time of the Carracci's rise."},"nga:224919":{"d":"A woman sits turned slightly to the right, her head toward the viewer, in a portrait of about 114 by 88 centimeters. She has a fair complexion, large green eyes and light blonde hair in short curls adorned with pearls and leaf-shaped clips. A cream gown with brown, gold and black embroidery lies under a long black garment, and she wears a ruffled collar, lace, gold chains, pearls and two rings. In her hands are a small book and a lace handkerchief, and a brooch shaped like a white dog hangs on her. A table at the right holds music sheets, a stringed instrument and books.","i":"The book, the music and the stringed instrument present Lucia Bonasoni Garzoni as an accomplished woman, cultivated in letters and music, while the dog brooch is a traditional emblem of fidelity. Her name probably joins her family name and her husband's. Lavinia Fontana was a leading portraitist of the Bolognese nobility, and her attention to jewelry and costume gave her sitters the splendid appearance they wanted.","c":"Fontana's skill at fabric and ornament is dazzling here: the embroidery, lace and pearls are rendered with a goldsmith's patience. The face is calm and individualized, and the still-life table adds a story of accomplishments. The risk is that the finery overwhelms the sitter, but the large green eyes hold the viewer. It is a fine example of a woman painter's success at the end of the sixteenth century."},"met:435854":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted on a thin sheet of ivory about 1710 by the Venetian artist Rosalba Carriera, in a format small enough to be held in the palm or set into a snuffbox lid or a locket. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait. The soft, translucent washes over the white support give the flesh its glow. The Met bought it in 1949 with the Rogers Fund.","i":"Carriera (1673 to 1757) began her career painting miniatures for snuffboxes, and she is credited with popularizing ivory as the support for miniatures in place of vellum, a change that gave the genre its luminous character. Her success led to pastel portraits for the courts of Europe, and in 1720 she was received into the Académie royale in Paris. Her early miniatures were made for Venetian clients and for visitors to Venice, the Grand Tourists of the age.","c":"Carriera's miniatures are as delicate as her pastels and, at this early date, inventive: she treats the ivory's whiteness as a source of light rather than a blank, and her flesh tones have a bloom that rivals do not match. A male sitter gives her less of the lace and ribbon she loved, so the interest falls on the face. It is a refined, sophisticated example of the portable portrait at its eighteenth-century best."},"met:438544":{"d":"A young Irish peer, Gustavus Hamilton, second Viscount Boyne (1710 to 1746), is shown in masquerade costume in a pastel of about 57 by 43 centimeters, dated 1730 to 1731, laid down on canvas. The museum's keywords record lace, a tricorne hat, a bow, a ribbon, a cravat and fur clothing, so he wears a fanciful outfit of fur and lace and a three-cornered hat, in a portrait probably made in Venice during the Carnival season.","i":"Venice's Carnival, which lasted for months, was the great attraction for young Grand Tourists, and having oneself painted in costume was a way to remember it. Rosalba Carriera, then at the height of her fame, made pastel portraits of many British and Irish visitors, and her soft, powdery technique flattered their youth. Hamilton, twenty at the time, took home a record of a carnival and of his own good looks.","c":"Rosalba's pastels of Grand Tourists are among the most charming of the eighteenth century: the soft blending of the powder gives skin a bloom, and the masquerade gives her free play of texture, fur, lace and ribbon. The glancing touch makes the portrait fresher than a formal one, and the fantasy dress lets the sitter's youth show through. A delicious example of Venetian pastel at its height."},"met:437894":{"d":"A man named Eugène Joseph Stanislas Foullon d'Ecotier (1753-1821) is portrayed in an oil painting of about 80 by 64 centimeters dated 1785, when the sitter was about thirty-two, by the French portraitist Antoine Vestier. The recorded motifs name only a man. A portrait of this size, a half-length on canvas, was a standard format for an educated gentleman of the upper middle class or minor nobility in the 1780s.","i":"Painted four years before the outbreak of the French Revolution, the portrait shows a man of the old regime at a moment of apparent stability; his life would span the Revolution, the Empire and the Restoration. Such portraits were made for family and friends, not for public display, and recorded a sitter's rank and character in the plain, direct manner favored in the 1780s.","c":"Vestier's portraits of the 1780s tend to show clear light, firm drawing and a cool, smooth finish; they lack the sparkling charm of Fragonard or Vigée Le Brun but gain in directness. The sitter's character is conveyed by the face alone, a sign of the period's move toward sober realism. It is a good, clear example of the pre-Revolutionary portrait, more solid than flattering."},"nga:93000":{"d":"The head of a pale young man nearly fills this shadowy, vertical canvas, about 22 by 17 centimeters, dated 1889. His shoulders angle to the right while his dark eyes cut back at the viewer; light from the upper left catches his light blond hair, and his full beard, straight nose and high cheekbones are cast into deep shadow. A high white collar shows over the lapels of a slate-blue jacket. The background is thinly painted, almost scratched on in places, and the brushwork is loose and visible.","i":"Edouard Vuillard painted this at twenty-one, a student at the Académie Julian, living with his mother, a widowed dressmaker, in Paris. Through school friends from the Lycée Condorcet, among them the painter Ker-Xavier Roussel, he was drawn into the circle of the Nabis, young painters who took Gauguin's flat color and Japanese prints as their guides and who called themselves prophets, from the Hebrew word nabi. This early self-portrait comes before that style took shape.","c":"A young painter's self-portrait is a private declaration, and this one is dark, brooding and searching, in a tonal manner that recalls older painting more than the bright decorative art Vuillard would invent in the next few years. The scratched background and loose handling show impatience with finish. It is an intense, serious picture of a young man looking at himself, and a useful reminder of the starting point of an artist famed for intimacy and pattern."},"nga:52234":{"d":"A child and a man walk away from us in a narrow vertical painting on cardboard, about 29 by 18 centimeters, from about 1891. The girl at left has a blue band in her long brown hair and an ankle-length dress dotted with red; despite the title, her bulky scarf is pumpkin-orange. The man at right, in dark trousers, a brown shirt and olive-green shoes, is cropped at the shoulders by the edge. A peach patch suggests the girl holds his hand. Forms at the top suggest people and a distant room, built from dabs and stipples.","i":"The cropped figure and the high viewpoint are devices that Vuillard and the Nabis borrowed from Japanese prints and from the new snapshot photography, which cut the world at unplanned edges. A child led by the hand is an everyday event turned into a picture; in the early 1890s Vuillard and his friends looked for subjects in the quiet routines of family and neighborhood. The painting came to the National Gallery with the Ailsa Mellon Bruce Collection.","c":"With almost nothing, a child, a scarf, the legs of a parent, Vuillard makes a picture that feels true to the experience of being small and led along. The dabs and stipples give the surface a shimmer, and the cropping is daring. The details dissolve if one looks too closely, which is the point: this is a memory of a moment, not a description. A tiny, assured and sympathetic painting."},"nga:52235":{"d":"A woman stands with her face turned to the left and her back to us, holding a piece of white fabric in her left hand, in a small oil on cardboard of about 23 by 21 centimeters, from about 1891. Her dark brown hair is tied in a loose knot, her face is blurred to a vague nose and a dot for an eye, and her long dark blue garment seems to be going on or coming off, her right hand raised to her shoulder. Behind her are a low brown table and muted mustard and beige walls.","i":"Women at their toilette, washing, dressing or arranging their hair, were a theme of Degas's pastels and of countless Japanese prints, and Vuillard gave it a modest domestic turn, with no nude and no voyeuristic frame, only a woman absorbed in a routine. The faceless figure is typical of the Nabis, who sought mood and pattern rather than individual likeness, and of Vuillard, who grew up among his mother's dressmaking workers and found their quiet absorption endlessly interesting.","c":"The picture is nearly abstract: a column of dark blue, a head turned away, a few dabs for a hand. What it conveys is a mood of private absorption, and it does so through the thick, textured surface and the muffled palette of mustard, brown and blue. It lacks the grandeur of a larger work, but its modesty and concentration are its qualities, and it makes a good introduction to the intimist Vuillard."},"nga:52236":{"d":"Two people face each other in a small horizontal painting of about 24 by 33 centimeters, dated 1891. The one at left stands with hands on a wooden chair, in a long black high-necked dress, with close-cut hair; the one at right, in a white ruffled top and a dark red skirt with lighter red spots, hair in a bun, holds a pale piece of fabric or paper before a wooden table. A red bowl and a patterned off-white object with a black handle stand on the table, and the walls are muted beige. The paint is thick and the figures lack detail.","i":"Conversation was a favorite subject of the Nabis and of Symbolist poets and playwrights, who valued silence and suggestion over statement; the exchange here is nothing but two heads turned toward each other. Vuillard's interiors of the early 1890s were set in the apartment he shared with his mother and sister, where the figures were relatives and the dressmaking workers of his mother's business, and the cloth held in the woman's hand may belong to that trade.","c":"The subject is nothing, and that is its achievement: two figures, a table and a mood of ease are enough, built from patches of red, black and beige. The faces are blanks, yet the tilt of the heads says everything about the exchange. As a design it is balanced and decisive, and the dotted skirt is a small delight. It is a good example of how Vuillard turned a domestic moment into a quiet pattern."},"nga:52237":{"d":"A woman in a long black dress stands at the side of a table in a stylized vertical composition on cardboard, about 27 by 22 centimeters, from about 1891, painted in wide strokes of mottled color in almost geometric areas. The table runs two-thirds of the way across from the right; her copper-brown hair is pulled up and her face is a featureless peach profile. Her hands rest near a white plate holding six yellow orbs, presumably fruit, and a dark door and a clock-like rectangle stand at left.","i":"The flat, almost geometric treatment shows the Nabi program: the painting is a surface of colored shapes first, a scene second. The woman, probably a member of Vuillard's household, belongs to the world of domestic interiors, with the table, the plate of fruit and the clock in the background, that he turned into art in these years. The idea that a picture is a flat arrangement of colors, advanced by Maurice Denis in 1890, was the group's central belief.","c":"The picture's strength is its design: the black dress as a vertical block, the table as a horizontal band and the plate as a pale accent. Vuillard works in mottled, wide strokes that make the surface vibrate, and the loss of the face adds mystery. It is a bold small picture, close to abstraction, and it shows how quickly the young painter found a language of his own."},"nga:52238":{"d":"Soft, rounded shapes and broken lines make an almost abstract picture of two people seated at a dark yellow table, on cardboard of about 22 by 29 centimeters, from about 1893. The round table is at the center, and the two people at left are suggested by small dots of tan and light brown paint; the rough brown cardboard shows through in many places. White circles read as a cup and saucer, a plate and a bowl, and a light brown cloth with bright splashes drapes the right side of the table. A greenish-blue wall with a white door is behind.","i":"Coffee drinking, the small ritual of a household, is the whole subject, and the title says nothing of the women's names. The picture belongs to the years when Vuillard worked on a small scale, on cheap cardboard that he left partly bare so that its warm brown became part of the color scheme. The dots and fragmented lines recall both the Pointillist technique of Seurat and Signac and the flat decorative patterns of Japanese prints, which the Nabis admired.","c":"The picture is close to a dissolve: the women are hardly more than clusters of dots, and the table is the real subject, a pale yellow ellipse that anchors the composition. It is an experimental little work, and it succeeds by its tact, offering a scene to the eye that cannot be pinned down. The viewer is invited to complete it, and the pleasure is in the completing."},"nga:52239":{"d":"A woman pulls aside a deep golden yellow curtain that fills three-quarters of this nearly square painting, about 35 by 39 centimeters, from about 1893. The curtain hangs close to the top on a rod and falls behind a bed covered in the same color, cropped by the bottom and left edges. She stands beyond the bed, facing away, in a navy-blue dress with a brown braid down her back, and the curtain's edge reveals a pink, green and white floral pattern with a round mirror above a red piece of furniture. The wall is bone white.","i":"The yellow curtain and its twin, the bed cover, make a single mass of color, and the floral wallpaper behind is a second pattern: Vuillard loved to set one decorative pattern against another, so that the figure becomes a small incident in a field of design. The influences are the Japanese prints, with their flat color and cropped forms, and the stage sets of the symbolist theater, for which he painted scenery in the early 1890s.","c":"The painting is almost all curtain, and the skill lies in making that big golden mass feel warm, domestic and slightly mysterious, with the woman reduced to a navy-blue accent. The floral patch, the mirror and the line that suggests a crack in the wall are the small events in a quiet drama of pattern. It is a modest, extraordinarily resolved picture, and an excellent introduction to Vuillard's Intimism."},"nga:52240":{"d":"A person sits in side profile facing left, her back against a brownish-red chair, in a small oil on cardboard of about 22 by 26 centimeters, from about 1894. The features are indistinct, the skin pale and the dark hair in a simple bun. The clothing has a dark blue and yellow plaid pattern, and the wall behind is textured in cream and beige, with thin lines of red. A dark vertical structure at left may be a piece of furniture. The brushstrokes are thick and visible against a textured surface.","i":"A woman by the fire is an image of quiet domestic comfort that painters had loved since the Dutch interiors of the seventeenth century, and Vuillard's mother and sister were probably the models for many such figures. The plaid dress is one of the textiles that fascinated him, since his mother's dressmaking business meant that cloth, pattern and trimmings were everyday sights of his childhood and youth.","c":"The picture's glow is in the pattern: the plaid, the beige wall with its red lines and the red-brown chair are treated as areas of equal weight, so the woman melts into her surroundings. It is a work of restraint, and the loose, thick paint keeps it from becoming merely cozy. A fine, small example of the quiet seriousness with which Vuillard looked at home."},"nga:52241":{"d":"A person sits at a table in a bright yellow chair, facing right, in a small vertical oil on cardboard of about 27 by 23 centimeters, dated 1894. The sitter has pale skin and gray hair and wears a loose, light brown garment like a long coat or dress. On the table are indistinct objects in white and red. A dark green area at right may be a wall or curtain, a lighter area at left may be a window or door, and the floor is brown and beige. The thick, visible brushstrokes make the scene appear blurred.","i":"Vuillard's mother, a widow who lived with him until her death in 1928, was his most frequent model, and a gray-haired figure at a breakfast table may be her, though the title names no one. Meals, the quiet moment at the center of any household, were a favorite theme of the Nabis and of Dutch painters before them, and Vuillard's version is a miniature of habit and routine, with the yellow chair as a note of cheer.","c":"A blur of brown, white and red at a table might seem to say nothing, but the arrangement is carefully judged: the yellow chair brings the eye to the sitter, the dark green wall behind sets off the light garment, and the objects on the table are suggested in a few touches. The effect is of a glance, a memory of a morning. It is a tiny picture with a large, quiet appeal."},"nga:92999":{"d":"A landscape seen from an elevated view shows fields dotted with small trees lining a hill that rises toward the horizon, under a blue sky full of white, puffy clouds, in a small oil on cardboard of about 20 by 25 centimeters, from about 1894. The fields are different shades of green and brown, with some rectangles in bright yellow. At the base of the hill, a brown shape at left suggests a structure, and in the bottom right corner a gray shape is topped with a line of bright orange. Thick, swirling brushstrokes give texture to the fields and clouds.","i":"The Ile-de-France is the region around Paris, and its gentle farmland was the weekend and summer country of the Parisian artists and their patrons; the Nabis often stayed with friends in the countryside near the city. Landscape was a minor theme in Vuillard's early work, which concentrated on interiors, and the thick, swirling brushwork here shows him testing a more energetic manner than that of his interiors.","c":"Quick, thick strokes and small patches of color turn the fields into a patchwork that the eye reads at once as a hill, a field, a sky: the economy is the delight. It is an outdoor sketch, with the freshness of something seen in a hurry, and the yellow rectangles and orange lines give it sparkle. Not a major work, but a lively, honest and surprisingly bold piece by a painter better known for interiors."},"nga:61388":{"d":"Two women stand behind a counter in an interior, shown from the waist up, in a nearly square canvas of about 66 by 59 centimeters, dated 1895. The nearer woman fills the left half in profile, her auburn hair up, in a garnet-red and white striped shirt with puffed shoulders; she looks down at a red vase of flowers. The second woman, at her shoulder, in a ruby-red blouse, gazes at the flowers too. Four wine-red vases hold maroon and cream blossoms, and loosely painted patterns make a patchwork of claret, ivory, forest green, burgundy and gold.","i":"The vases of flowers and the counter suggest a flower shop or a shop's display, and Vuillard painted many shops and interiors where women worked and talked. The picture shows his move from small cardboard studies to a larger canvas in which a whole room becomes a woven fabric of patterns, the figures and the setting dissolved into one rich surface. Such decorative richness appealed to the patrons who commissioned panels from him in the mid-1890s.","c":"This is one of the richest of his early pictures: the reds are layered so subtly that the whole canvas seems to glow like a tapestry, and the women, with their blurred faces, are less portraits than parts of a pattern. The risk of this approach is that the eye loses its footing, and the picture sometimes does. But the harmony of color is remarkable, and the sense of two women intent on their flowers carries through."},"nga:46704":{"d":"Two people, perhaps a woman and a man, and a baby sit at a table in a courtyard surrounded by vine-covered walls, in a square painting on cardboard of about 54 by 53 centimeters, dated 1898. The scene is loosely painted in dabs, dashes and smudges. The man sits on the far side of the table; the woman, her brown hair pulled up, sits facing away in a smoke-gray garment; a child in pink and pale blue seems to raise both arms. Steps lead up to a building at left, and the vine-covered wall fills the right two-thirds of the background.","i":"A family meal in a garden is a quiet celebration of ordinary happiness, and Vuillard, who had no family of his own but moved easily in those of his friends, painted many such scenes in the late 1890s, often among the households of his friends and patrons. The loose, dappled brushwork shows the influence of the Impressionists' outdoor light, brought into the Nabis' decorative design.","c":"The great virtue of the picture is its dappled light: the courtyard is a tapestry of cream, gray and green dabs through which the figures emerge only partly, and the baby's raised arms are the one lively accent. It is a calmer, more open work than his earlier interiors and shows him entering a more naturalistic phase. The surface is slightly confusing, but the warmth and ease of the scene are unmistakable."},"nga:52242":{"d":"A brown jug of pink roses and green leaves stands on a brown surface that looks like a suitcase on its side, in an oil on cardboard of about 36 by 43 centimeters, dated 1898. The title tells that the surface is the artist's paint box. To the right of the jug are stacked light green books; the background is a light blue paneled wall or door, with open boxes behind the box at right. The brushstrokes are thick and visible.","i":"A still life of a painter's own tools is a modest self-portrait without a face: the paint box stands with the roses and a stack of books, an intimate arrangement of a painter's daily things. Moss roses, a cultivated variety with mossy buds, were a popular garden flower of the time and a favorite subject of the Nabis' decorative style, and Vuillard, who drew all his subjects from the life around him, makes them the center here.","c":"The pleasure of the picture is in its improvisation: a paint box used as a table, books piled beside, roses as a spot of fresh pink. Everything is rendered in broad strokes and a limited range of browns and soft blues, and nothing is arranged for effect. It is the opposite of a grand still life, and its modesty, as an image of how a painter lives with his materials, is touching."},"nga:52243":{"d":"A partial view of a dark wooden mantelpiece fills the bottom right corner of this vertical oil on cardboard, about 36 by 30 centimeters, from about 1900. A vase of pink flowers stands at the center on the mantel's left corner, in front of a large silvery mirror in a golden frame, which reflects gray walls and indistinct rectangles of black, gray and brown. A patterned brown and white object, perhaps furniture or draped fabric, fills the bottom left, and the wall at left is cream. The reddish-brown cardboard shows in places.","i":"Mirrors, which double a room and let a painter include more than the eye can see, delighted Vuillard, who often painted interiors in which reflections open a second space behind the first. A vase of flowers on a mantelpiece is a staple of French domestic decoration, and he treats it as a subject worth looking at in its own right. In 1900 he was nearing the end of his Nabi period and beginning to paint the more naturalistic interiors of his maturity.","c":"A mirror in a gold frame, a vase, a corner of a mantel: the picture is arranged with the casual precision of a glimpse, and the cropping is as decisive as in his earlier work. The pale pink flowers against silver and gray are delicate, and the thick paint keeps the picture from being merely pretty. A small, quiet picture of a room as it is lived in, with the sense of an unposed moment."},"nga:92998":{"d":"The upper half of a woman's body fills this vertical oil on cardboard, about 42 by 32 centimeters, dated 1895 to 1900, with the emphasis on her face and large hat. Her body is slightly turned to the right while she looks directly at us; she has fair skin, light pink on the cheeks and forehead, brown eyes, a small mouth and dark curly hair under a wide-brimmed hat with vibrant abstract patterns in teal, tan and black. She wears a dark, high-collared garment against a background of light gray and white lines. The light brown cardboard shows through.","i":"The sitter is Marthe Bonnard, the companion and model of the painter Pierre Bonnard, Vuillard's closest friend among the Nabis; she is called Madame Bonnard in the title, though she and Bonnard did not marry until 1925. Bonnard painted her again and again, most famously in the bath, and Vuillard's quick portrait shows her in an outdoor hat, a different, social side of a woman best known as a private presence.","c":"The hat is the picture: a vivid, almost abstract patchwork of teal, tan and black that outshines the face, which is soft, direct and a little guarded. The loose brushwork, the visible cardboard and the sketchy background give the portrait the intimacy of a gift from one friend to another. It does not penetrate a character so much as record a presence, but as a note of friendship it has real charm."},"met:816630":{"d":"A nude woman nurses an infant, with sleep among the themes the museum records, in an oil on copper about 51 by 69 centimeters, painted about 1680. The title names the subject as Charity, one of the three theological virtues. The museum's keywords record sleeping, an infant, a female nude and nursing, so the pose, any further children, the setting and the light are left unstated.","i":"Charity, with Faith and Hope, is one of the three theological virtues, and since the Middle Ages she has been personified as a woman nursing or surrounded by children, a sign of love freely given. Baroque painters in Bologna, where Cignani worked, often turned allegories into graceful, sensuous figures, and the copper support, smooth and costly, suited private cabinet pictures for connoisseurs. The theme of sleep adds a note of calm.","c":"Cignani painted in the sweet, polished classicism of Bologna after Francesco Albani, his teacher, and this small copper shows it: rounded forms, soft light and a smooth finish that takes the edge off the sensuality of a nude. The allegory is more gracious than profound, and the title does more than the picture to demand a moral reading. As a cabinet picture for private pleasure it is accomplished; his reputation rests on large religious works in Forlì."},"nga:32586":{"d":"A square seen from above is surrounded by elegant architecture, in a very large canvas of about 161 by 222 centimeters, dated 1742 or later. On the left stands a long red-brown building with arched white windows and columns; on the right an ornate off-white structure with arches and carved stonework; both lead toward a gray building with tall spires and blue domes behind. Countless people in pink, red, blue, green and yellow fill the square, some in white masks with bird-like noses, and a long line forms a procession on the right while spectators crowd the windows.","i":"Antonio Joli (about 1700 to 1777), from Modena, trained as a stage designer and a view painter in Rome, and made his name in Venice with views for visitors on the Grand Tour. A procession in the Venetian setting, with masked citizens among the crowd, is a way to show the city as the place of ceremony and carnival, and the architectural frame, painted with a stage designer's sense of perspective, makes the spectacle seem both grand and orderly.","c":"Joli's views of Venice are more theatrical than topographical, with a stage designer's sense of space and crowd, and this one is a particularly rich example: the crowd is so detailed that it can be explored for hours, and the architecture is firmly drawn. What it lacks is the luminous, silvery light of Canaletto, and the figures are a little uniform. But as a record of the pageantry of eighteenth-century Venice it is an immense pleasure."},"nga:32587":{"d":"Many large and small vessels crowd the blue-green water of the basin of San Marco: gondolas in gold, black, red and blue drift across, with tiny figures poling, and large sailboats and long boats with several rowers join them in a colorful mix. On the left a large brown building with columns is approached by boats, with crowds on the shore in bright clothes; behind rise more buildings in brown, cream and tan, a large tower in the center and blue domes in the distance, under a light blue sky. The canvas is about 161 by 222 centimeters.","i":"Venice's festivals were staged on the water, and processions of gondolas and decorated boats welcomed visiting princes or marked the annual ceremonies of the Republic. The basin of San Marco, with the Doge's Palace, the Campanile and the basilica, was the stage, and Joli's view, a pair to the Procession in the Courtyard, shows the city as a floating pageant. The date, 1742 or after, may link the pair to a particular event, but the occasion is not named.","c":"The water gives Joli a perfect subject for his talent, a crowd of small, brightly colored incidents under a high, light sky: the golds and blacks of the gondolas against the blue-green basin are a constant pleasure. The buildings are drawn with precision and the picture is full of life, though the light is less subtle than Canaletto's. It is a grand, festive spectacle, painted for visitors who wished to carry home a Venice of celebration."},"met:436782":{"d":"A balcony in the foreground, with a man, a woman and a dog, looks out over the River Thames toward St. Paul's Cathedral and Old London Bridge, with boats on the water, in this canvas of about 107 by 119 centimeters painted around 1745 by Antonio Joli. The word capriccio in the title signals that the view is partly invented: real landmarks are combined in a composition that never existed as a single scene, probably framed by architecture in the Italian manner.","i":"Joli, born in Modena and trained in Venice as a view and theater painter, worked in London from about 1744 to 1748, when Venetian vedute were fashionable among English collectors who had made the Grand Tour. The capriccio let him join the grandeur of Wren's St. Paul's and the medieval Old London Bridge, still crowded with houses, to the poetry of an Italian terrace. The foreground figures, standing on the balcony as spectators, invite the viewer to share their view.","c":"Joli combines a theater designer's feeling for stage-like space with a view painter's eye for light on water, and the capriccio gives him freedom to arrange St. Paul's dome and the bridge for pictorial effect. The foreground group adds scale and life. The picture lacks the crystalline precision of Canaletto's London views, but it has a bright, theatrical charm of its own."},"met:438661":{"d":"A female nude is the subject of this small oil on canvas, about 36 by 22 centimeters, dated 1840 and painted by Henri Lehmann. The museum's keywords record only a nude, and the title calls it a study, a painting from a live model made as an exercise or as preparation for a larger picture. The narrow format and the single figure show the discipline of the academic tradition in which Lehmann trained under Ingres. It came to the Met in 2003 from the Whitney Collection and by purchase.","i":"Henri Lehmann (1814 to 1882), born in Kiel and trained in Paris as a pupil of Ingres, became a successful history and portrait painter, known above all for his portraits of Franz Liszt and Marie d'Agoult. Life studies were the core of an academic training: a painter learned to draw and paint the figure from a living model before attempting a history picture, and Ingres insisted on drawing as the foundation of art. Such studies were rarely exhibited, and their survival gives a glimpse of the studio.","c":"A good study from the model is judged by its drawing and by the way flesh is made to look warm and alive, and Lehmann, as a pupil of Ingres, aims at a clear contour and smooth modeling. The result is cool, accurate and a little impersonal, as the exercise requires. It is not a picture with a life of its own, but it shows the Ingres school at work."},"met:438737":{"d":"A girl is the subject of this oil portrait of about 100 by 81 centimeters, painted in 1842 by Henri Lehmann. The title names her as Faustine Leo and gives her dates, 1832 to 1865, so she was about ten. The museum's keywords record a girl and a portrait, and the size is that of a three-quarter-length portrait, a serious format for a child, which suggests a family of means and an important commission. The Met bought it in 2004 with the Wolfe Fund and a gift.","i":"Lehmann, a pupil of Ingres, brought his master's linear discipline to portraits of children and adults of the Paris salons, among them artists, musicians and writers. A ten-year-old girl in a formal portrait stands for the family's hopes; Faustine Leo died at thirty-three, in 1865, so the picture is also a portrait of a short life. The year 1842 was early in Lehmann's career as a portraitist, soon after his portraits of Liszt and Marie d'Agoult.","c":"Ingres's lessons show in the clear contour and the smooth, enamel-like surface, but Lehmann is less severe than his teacher and gives a child a gentler, more sentimental treatment. The result is polished, appealing and a little formal, a record of a young girl at the edge of adolescence. It is not as searching as the best of Ingres's portraits, but it is a refined example of the Ingres school's portrait manner and a touching document."},"met:441350":{"d":"A man, identified by the title as an Egyptian, stands in a doorway framed by columns, wearing jewelry. The panel is upright and small, about 56 by 39 centimeters, so the figure and his architectural frame fill it closely and the viewer is brought near. It is a cabinet picture, a finely finished oil on wood of the kind made for private collectors. Alma-Tadema was known for building his scenes of antiquity from museum objects and archaeological publications, and an Egyptian subject of 1865 belongs to that early, research-driven phase.","i":"In the 1860s Alma-Tadema, Dutch-born and then working in Belgium, turned to ancient Egypt, drawing on museum collections and the growing field of Egyptology. Such pictures invited viewers to see antiquity as a lived world: a doorway, a column, a man in his ornaments. The Orientalist label recorded for the picture points to the period's habit of treating the ancient and modern East as picturesque, exotic material, so the scene is probably a nineteenth-century construction more than a documentary record.","c":"In small panels like this, Alma-Tadema's real subject is surface: crisp contour, convincing stone and metal, no loose brushwork. That precision is what collectors bought, and it is also his limit, since everything is equally lit and equally still, and a figure can seem one more beautifully made object in a reconstructed set. It stands well before the marble-and-sunlight Roman scenes that made his fame, but shows the method already formed."},"nga:178256":{"d":"A Roman scene turns on a difficulty with a line of verse by Horace, the poet of the Augustan age, in a tiny oil on wood panel only 15 by 24 centimeters, dated 1881. The title promises a gently humorous human moment, the struggle with a hard text, set in Alma-Tadema's imagined antiquity, where marble, fabrics and objects are rendered with meticulous detail. It is a jewel-like cabinet picture, made to be examined at close range.","i":"Horace (65-8 BC) wrote the Odes and Satires that were set texts in Victorian schools, so almost every educated viewer had struggled with his Latin. Alma-Tadema's joke is to give the struggle to a Roman, making the ancient world a mirror for the Victorian schoolroom. His Rome, built from archaeological research, museum objects and photographs, was a favorite of Victorian collectors.","c":"Alma-Tadema is often mocked as a painter of marble and flowers, but his skill is real, and in so small a panel the precise handling, the economy of gesture and the wit are a pleasure. The joke is gentle, and the archaeology is a costume, yet the control of surface and light is very fine. As a miniature of Victorian classicism, it is knowing and charming."},"met:435940":{"d":"A small panel, about 40 by 31 centimetres, painted in oil on wood, shows the Virgin nursing the infant Christ, a type known as the Madonna of the Milk. Bernardino dei Conti, a Milanese painter in the circle of Leonardo da Vinci, was active between the 1490s and 1522, and his Madonnas share the soft modelling and gentle expressions that Leonardo's example brought to Lombard painting. The date of this panel is not recorded.","i":"The nursing Madonna, in Latin Maria lactans, stresses Christ's humanity and Mary's role as nourisher: her milk stood for grace and for the care of the Church. It was a favourite image of private devotion in the late Middle Ages and Renaissance, though after the Council of Trent in the 1560s such frank images of the Virgin's breast were increasingly discouraged. A small panel like this was meant for a bedroom or private chapel, where the intimacy of the scene would encourage prayer.","c":"Conti uses Leonardo's soft shading to give mother and child a tender, quiet unity, and the intimate subject suits his gentle talent. But the faces can seem doll-like, the colour dry, and the picture lacks the intelligence of Leonardo's own Madonnas; it is an echo rather than an invention. Within Lombard painting it is a graceful, devout work, and its small size makes it more likeable."},"met:437161":{"d":"A country inn forms the setting for an everyday scene, with a man, a woman, a dog and pigs among its figures. The canvas, about 52 by 67 centimeters, is a size for a private room rather than a public hall. George Morland specialized in such unidealized rustic subjects, inns, farmyards and stables, painted with a fluent brush and soft, mellow tones, and sold them both as paintings and, through engravings, to a wide public.","i":"Inns stood at the center of country life, for travelers, carriers and local gossip alike, and they were a staple of English genre painting in the 1780s and 1790s, when the market for pictures, and for prints after them, wanted cheerful, reassuring images of rural Britain. Morland's villagers are not the idealized shepherds of earlier pastoral; they are plain and a little shabby. Buyers probably read the dog and pigs as authentic rural detail, and the inn as a place of rest and talk.","c":"Morland is at his best when painting animals and rustic ease without sermonizing, and a picture like this shows the appeal: quick, fluent handling, convincing beasts, a believable roadside feel. The limitation is sameness. He repeated inn, stable and farmyard themes at speed, often to pay his debts, and invention thins. An agreeable early example from the years in which his rustic manner was forming, rather than a landmark."},"nga:1182":{"d":"A hunting party closes in on its quarry. Eight men, most on horseback, and nine hounds gather at the center, where two dogs attack a rust-brown fox. One man dismounts near the fox and another stands to the right of the group. Riders wear black caps and coats of cardinal red or parchment white, one in teal blue, on horses of silvery white, chestnut and black. The party spreads in a loose line across a grassy slope backed by a low, steep hill with feathery trees; a lone rider still gives chase in the distance, and houses stand along a far ridge under thin, pale clouds.","i":"Fox hunting had become the characteristic sport of the English landed gentleman by the later eighteenth century, with organized packs, hunt servants and a sporting art to match: paintings of horses, hounds and the chase made for the men who rode them. Here the moment chosen is the kill rather than the gallop, the sport's conclusion, shown without sentiment. The red coats and the distant row of houses set it in a working English countryside, not an idealized one.","c":"Morland is better known for farmyards, stables and inns, and this large canvas, bigger than most of his cabinet pictures, shows both his ease and his limits. The sage-green and gold grass, light cloud and the easy rhythm of horses and dogs across the slope feel fresh and observed. But the composition is loose rather than tight, and the kill at the center lacks the concentrated drama of the best sporting specialists. A pleasant picture, more relaxed than powerful."},"nga:19":{"d":"A man fills a vertical panel from head to chest, his body angled slightly to the left while his eyes glance at the viewer from their corners. Brown hair falls across his forehead and curls at the nape; he has arched brows, a long angular nose, high cheekbones, a full upper lip, a closed mouth and a cleft chin. He wears a deep rose-red garment in pleats under a black collar band, and his raised right hand clutches the cloth at his chest, with a gold ring on the little finger. The background shades from dark aquamarine at the top to pale blue at his shoulders.","i":"In Florence around 1450 portraits were shifting from the profile view, modeled on coins and medals, toward the three-quarter view with a sideways glance, a change influenced by Netherlandish painting. The raised hand, which brings the sitter's body and gesture into the picture, is another feature Florentine painters took from northern portraits. The rose-red robe, black collar and gold ring mark a prosperous citizen, though his name is not known. It is an early example of the Florentine turn toward psychological alertness.","c":"Castagno is a painter of hard, sculptural form, and the panel shows it: the glance from the corners of the eyes, the sharp planes of cheek and nose and the heavy folds of the red garment seem carved. The cool blue ground sets off the warm flesh and the red. The portrait is severe rather than ingratiating, and more convincing for it. The tension between the turned body and the sideways stare is unusual for its date."},"nga:1145":{"d":"A young man strides across a rocky stream bank on a tall, wedge-shaped panel that tapers toward the bottom like a shield. His curling brown hair lifts in the wind, and his red tunic over a white shirt flutters around bare thighs. One hand holds the end of a long red sling whose stone-weighted end trails behind his leg; the other arm is thrown up, fingers spread. He stands astride the severed head of Goliath at the foot of the panel, grey-faced, with a stone sunk in the forehead. Dark foliage and grey boulders frame the figure against a deep blue sky streaked with small, frothy clouds.","i":"This is a parade shield, a ceremonial object of leather over wood meant for tournaments or festivals, not for battle. David, who killed the giant Goliath with a sling and a stone (1 Samuel 17), was a civic emblem of Florence, the small state that defied stronger enemies, which made him a fitting subject for a Florentine owner. The National Gallery dates the shield to about 1450–55; its original owner and occasion are not documented. Castagno clearly knew Donatello's bronze David and translates its poise into a figure caught in motion. The shield was once attributed to Pollaiuolo before Castagno's authorship was accepted.","c":"Castagno was the most sculptural painter of mid-century Florence, and this is among the liveliest things he left. The body makes a long diagonal that fills the tapering field intelligently, and the flying drapery and thrown-up hand turn a static Donatellesque idea into action. David's wide-eyed, almost anxious face is a surprising touch, closer to a boy after the fight than a victor. The boulders are mannered, and the grey head at the foot looks a little like a still life. As a use of an awkward shape, though, it is hard to better."},"met:435702":{"d":"A crowd of heavy draft horses and their handlers surges across a wide, dusty ground under a gray, broken sky. At the left a dark bay and a chestnut plunge forward, held by men hanging on their halters; at the center two big gray and white horses, one rearing, dominate the picture, their muscled bodies catching the light. Men in blue smocks and caps run alongside, some on foot, some mounted. A line of trees runs across the background, and a pale building shows in the haze at the left. The composition is a continuous frieze more than five meters wide, with little space above and a strong sense of forward thrust. It is signed at the lower right.","i":"The scene is the horse market on the Boulevard de l'Hôpital in Paris, near the Salpêtrière hospital. Bonheur studied the horses there twice a week for a year and a half, wearing men's clothes, for which she obtained police permission, so that she would not draw attention. The picture was a success at the Paris Salon of 1853; the dealer Ernest Gambart bought it and toured it in Britain, where Queen Victoria asked to see it, and Bonheur herself called it her own Parthenon frieze. Cornelius Vanderbilt II bought it at auction in 1887 and gave it to the Metropolitan Museum. A smaller replica is in the National Gallery, London.","c":"The energy and the anatomical command are remarkable, and Bonheur treats the horses as individuals rather than symbols: the eyes, the tendons, and the weight in the hooves are all looked at. The composition is almost too continuous, the human figures are stock types, and the gray-brown palette and rigid frieze give less sense of air and depth than the best Realist canvases. She is more orderly and less tragic than Géricault, one of her models for movement. Still, few animal paintings convey mass so convincingly, and the work was a landmark for women artists."},"met:435704":{"d":"One hunting hound fills a small canvas of about 37 by 46 centimeters. The title uses French hunting terms: a limier is a tracking hound that follows a quarry's scent. To judge by the title and recorded subject, the picture is a portrait of the animal itself, not a hunt scene, so the interest lies in anatomy, coat and character. Rosa Bonheur made many animal studies from life, and a small canvas suits that kind of close observation.","i":"Hounds were central to French and English hunting culture, and each kind had a task: the limier, a leash hound, was used to find the quarry's lair so that the hunt could begin in the right place. A portrait of such a dog honors skill and breeding, as an equestrian portrait honors a horse. Bonheur's century took animals seriously as subjects, from Landseer in Britain to the Barbizon painters in France, and she was admired for treating them as individuals.","c":"Bonheur's fame rests on large, crowded pictures such as The Horse Fair, but small studies like this show the discipline beneath them: close looking, secure anatomy and a feeling for coat and temper. They are less ambitious and less memorable than her cattle and horse paintings, yet honest evidence of her method. Her manner is drier and more observational than Landseer's sentimental dogs, which keeps single-animal pictures from tipping into sweetness."},"met:435703":{"d":"Calves and cattle are shown with mountains rising behind them. The title names a routine farm task, separating young calves from their mothers so that they can be fed another way, and the picture places it in a mountain setting. The canvas, about 65 by 81 centimeters, is a mid-size work for a collector's wall. Rosa Bonheur painted it in 1879, when she was 57 and the most famous woman artist of her day.","i":"Weaning, the separation of calves from the cows, is a routine point in a farm year, and the picture sets it among cattle and mountains. Bonheur's animals are observed rather than allegorical, and nineteenth-century buyers, especially in Britain and America, prized her work as an image of rural labor and continuity; the mountain setting signals unspoiled nature. A farm subject of this kind probably appealed as much for its sympathy with the animals as for any story.","c":"By 1879 Bonheur could paint cattle almost by reflex, and the strength of her late pictures is the authority built up over decades: animals that stand heavily on the ground, with an honest feeling for hide, bone and herd behavior. The risk is prettiness, since calves and mountains are agreeable ingredients in a picture that wants to please. It is craftsmanlike rather than inventive, and sits comfortably within her large body of cattle subjects."},"met:19022":{"d":"A girl is shown in formal dress on a canvas about 127 by 102 centimeters, large enough for an ambitious portrait of a child. The recorded details are a stomacher (the decorated panel at the front of a bodice), lace, a pearl necklace, a cap with ribbon, shoes and a bouquet of flowers. The artist is unknown. Portraits of this kind were commissioned by prosperous families to record a child's rank, health and good upbringing, and this one is dated 1752.","i":"The surname Elmendorf is characteristic of the Dutch families of colonial New York, so the portrait probably belongs to that world, where prosperous families commissioned likenesses from local painters who often adapted poses from imported English prints. A child dressed in adult finery, with pearls and lace, signals her family's standing and her own place in it. Flowers were a common emblem of youth and freshness, and could also hint at the brevity of life.","c":"Provincial portraits of this kind are usually valued for presence and pattern rather than subtle modeling or space, and this one is best judged that way: a careful record of a child's finery, with lace, pearls and ribbon given patient attention. That is its charm and its limit. It lacks the psychological depth of the best portraits made in the great cities, but as evidence of how a colonial family presented its daughter it is valuable and affecting."},"met:20889":{"d":"A busy city street is crowded with people and carriages in front of rows of buildings, and the people are relocating their households. The panel, about 43 by 60 centimeters, shows New York around 1827 as a place in motion rather than a scenic prospect: the crowd and vehicles matter as much as the architecture. The artist is unknown. The subject is a local custom in which tenants changed addresses on the same day, which is why the street is so full.","i":"In early nineteenth-century New York, leases traditionally ended on the same day, May 1, so thousands of households changed address at once and the streets filled with carts and furniture. Visitors found it astonishing; New Yorkers took it as a familiar spectacle and a joke about the city's restlessness. The picture was probably made for pleasure and local recognition rather than as a record, and its size, medium and date match those of The Five Points at the same museum, so the two were probably conceived together.","c":"The picture is valued more as a document of city life than as an exercise in style. Panels by unknown painters of this kind usually handle crowds and space by rule of thumb, and the pleasure lies in particulars: costumes, vehicles, the energy of a city changing house. A street crowded with life, rather than a tidy prospect of buildings, was an unusual subject for an early American picture, and that gives the panel its charm."},"met:20891":{"d":"A street corner in lower Manhattan is full of people: men, women and a crowd move in front of rows of buildings. The title names the Five Points, a junction where several streets met. The panel measures about 43 by 60 centimeters, the same size as the Moving Day panel in the same museum, and the artist is unknown. The recorded subjects, buildings, street, men, women and a crowd, make it a picture of a neighborhood in use.","i":"The Five Points took its name from the junction of five streets in lower Manhattan, built over filled ground, and by the 1830s and 1840s the neighborhood had become a byword in the press for poverty, crowding and crime. A picture dated about 1827 predates that reputation and can be read as a record of an ordinary busy quarter or, with hindsight, as an early sign of the city's class divisions. Together with Moving Day it makes a small portrait of New York's street life.","c":"The value of the panel is social more than painterly: it keeps a view of a working-class New York quarter at a date when such neighborhoods were seldom the subject of oil paintings. Its attractions are those of an unsophisticated record, with a vivid sense of place and the clutter of street life. As a work of art it is modest, and its interest lies in what it shows rather than in how it is painted."},"met:20398":{"d":"A portrait miniature painted in watercolor on ivory, about 11 by 9 centimeters, a little larger than the locket miniatures worn as jewelry but still small enough to hold in one hand. The sitter is recorded as M. T. Webb; no costume or setting is described here, and the maker is unknown. Watercolor on a thin slice of ivory gives skin a soft inner glow, because light passes through the paint and reflects back from the white support.","i":"Miniatures were personal objects: kept in cases, worn, exchanged between relatives, friends or sweethearts, or treasured as a remembrance after a death. In the decades around 1830 they were still a common way for a family of moderate means to own a likeness, until photography began to replace them from about 1840. A surviving name matters, since unlabeled miniatures often lose their sitters entirely, and a name and initials let a small object be tied back to a real person and household.","c":"Without a known hand to measure it against, the picture is best judged as a type. The medium rewards patience, with translucent flesh tones built from fine, almost invisible touches, and its risk is a polite, generalized likeness that flatters more than it records. At about 1830 a competent miniature of this kind is the realistic expectation; its value lies in the sitter's survival as a named individual rather than in artistic invention."},"met:12903":{"d":"Ships lie at anchor in a harbor. The title places them at Whampoa, the anchorage on the Pearl River below Canton (Guangzhou) where foreign merchant vessels waited while their cargoes and crews were carried to and from the city by smaller boats. The canvas, about 54 by 72 centimeters, is a handsome but portable size. The artist is unknown, and the ships, rather than any landscape, are the picture's subject.","i":"Under the Canton system, European and American ships could trade at one Chinese port only, and had to anchor at Whampoa while goods moved upriver to the foreign trading houses at Canton. Views of the anchorage were made in Chinese export workshops for the traders and captains, who bought them as souvenirs and as records of a voyage; this one is probably of that kind. The date 1840 falls in the opening phase of the First Opium War (1839 to 1842), after which the old system was dismantled.","c":"Export views of this kind are judged by accuracy and polish: how clearly the rigging, hulls and flags are drawn, and how crisply the light and water are handled. The Canton workshops supplied such pictures by the dozen, and this one is probably best valued as a clear record rather than as an original vision. Its real interest is historical, a view of a trade on the eve of its transformation."},"met:436865":{"d":"A garden scene from Ovid's Metamorphoses: Pomona, the nymph of orchards, and Vertumnus, the god of the changing seasons, who pursued her in many disguises. The recorded subjects, a garden and women, suggest the best-known episode, in which Vertumnus comes to Pomona as an old woman. The medium is recorded only as paper and no size is given, so it is probably a small, finished work meant for close viewing rather than for a wall.","i":"Ovid's tale (Metamorphoses, book 14) was popular in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries as garden-lore and erotic comedy. Pomona guards her orchard behind a wall and rejects every suitor until Vertumnus gains entry as an old woman, argues his case with a cautionary tale, then reveals himself as a handsome youth, and she yields. Viewers could enjoy it as a story about courtship and persuasion, and as an allegory of fruitfulness: the god of seasonal change united with the goddess of ripened fruit.","c":"Thomas Lefebure is a little-known name, so the picture is best judged against the long line of Vertumnus and Pomona treatments, from Pontormo's lunette at Poggio a Caiano to the garden pieces of the seventeenth century. The story pairs figure and garden easily, and the date of 1676 places the work in the late Baroque. With no known body of work to measure it against, it is best taken as a pleasant, conventional entry in a well-worn subject."},"nga:226411":{"d":"A large woven basket filled with pears and a couple of peaches or apples sits left of center on a table, with a few peaches resting below and four cucumbers lying in the middle. To the right a reflective bowl overflows with figs, and in front of it lies a yellow melon with a textured rind, cut open. Greens, reds and the melon's yellow stand out against a black background, and light from the upper left casts shadows on the brown surface. The oil on panel is about 35 by 59 cm.","i":"Fede Galizia was one of the first specialists in still life in Italy, working in Milan, where the cardinal archbishop Federico Borromeo prized still life as a record of God's creation, and where Caravaggio's basket of fruit was owned by the Ambrosiana. Her fruit, arranged in clear rows against a dark ground, combine scientific observation with a quiet delight in abundance. Figs, melons and cucumbers were the produce of the Lombard summer, shown at their ripest.","c":"Galizia's still lifes are crisp, clear and exact, with each fruit given its own space and the glossy surfaces of figs and melon distinguished with care. The black ground and the strong light give them dignity. The layout is frontal and a little stiff, and there is little sense of play, but that sobriety is the picture's character: the dignity of humble produce. She stands beside Caravaggio in making still life a serious subject."},"met:670765":{"d":"Cleopatra, the last queen of Ptolemaic Egypt, is shown in her death, with the snake that killed her. The canvas, about 95 by 75 centimeters, is a moderate size for a private collector's room. Guido Cagnacci, from the Romagna and active in Bologna, Rome and Venice, specialized in sensuous female figures in subjects of this kind, and the story, in which the queen chooses death over capture, gave painters the chance to present a woman's body and her resolve together.","i":"The story comes from Plutarch and other ancient writers: after Octavian conquered Egypt in 30 BC, Cleopatra chose suicide over being led in a Roman triumph, and the asp, in many accounts smuggled in with figs, became her emblem. In Counter-Reformation Italy such subjects stood alongside Lucretia and Sophonisba as examples of the woman who preferred death to dishonor, and also gave collectors an acceptable pretext for the female nude. That ambiguity, between virtue and sensuality, was part of the appeal.","c":"Cagnacci's pictures can hold the eye with an unexpected stillness: pale flesh, soft light, sensuality restrained by a classical calm. His women are warmer and more fleshly than those of Guido Reni, who influenced him, and his Cleopatras and Lucretias are among the most memorable images of the type. The weakness is a sweetness that can drain the drama, so that death reads as languor rather than agony. A characteristic example, remembered for mood more than story."},"met:436270":{"d":"A man and a woman share a domestic interior in which smoking is part of the scene, and smoke stands for the sense named in the title. The canvas, about 66 by 60 centimeters, is a cabinet picture of the kind Dutch painters made for private homes. Jan Ekels the Younger worked in Amsterdam and died young, in his thirties; his pictures of quiet interiors look back to the Golden Age masters, and the date, probably 1791, places this one among his last.","i":"Allegories of the five senses were a long-lived Netherlandish theme, a way of turning everyday scenes into small reflections on perception, pleasure and restraint. Tobacco smoke carried mixed associations: idleness, excess, or simply domestic leisure. The English term conversation piece describes an informal social scene or group portrait, and here signals a polite domestic encounter. Late eighteenth-century Dutch viewers, who prized the Golden Age interior, probably enjoyed such pictures both for their craft and for the old habit of reading everyday objects as moral signs.","c":"Ekels belongs to the late Dutch revival of the cabinet picture, and the pleasure of such work is in the craft: smooth finish, controlled light, carefully placed domestic detail. It is homage more than rival; beside Metsu or ter Borch, whose interiors it recalls, the refinement tends to be cool and the ideas borrowed. His early death at about thirty-four cut short a small body of work, and this picture belongs to it."},"met:437585":{"d":"Men, horses and boats gather on a beach where a market is under way, with the open sea beyond. The panel, about 41 by 59 centimeters, is typical of the small oil-on-wood landscapes made in Haarlem in the 1630s, when Salomon van Ruysdael, Jan van Goyen and Pieter de Molijn were developing a tonal manner: a narrow, close-valued range of grays, browns and greens in place of the brighter, busier landscapes of the previous generation.","i":"Beach scenes with fishermen, horses and boats showed Dutch viewers their own coast, where the fishing fleet landed its catch and goods were sold, a source of food and trade for the young Republic. They are not narrative pictures; the interest lies in small lively encounters, figures going about the business of buying and selling, and in the open light of the shore. Buyers valued recognizably local, unglamorous subjects, and the Haarlem landscape painters of the 1630s supplied them with unaffected honesty.","c":"In the 1630s Salomon van Ruysdael worked alongside Van Goyen and De Molijn on a landscape of atmosphere and economy, unified by tone, a low horizon and a sky that takes a generous share of the panel. A beach market suits that approach, and the small format suits his quick, soft touch. The result is unassuming rather than imposing, but this is the kind of picture on which his reputation for quiet truthfulness was built."},"cma:147464":{"d":"A castle stands beside a river on a small panel, about 39 by 60 centimeters. According to the Cleveland Museum, the building is a free interpretation of Castle Loevestein, the fortress in the Dutch Republic that served as the state prison, rather than an exact portrait: Ruysdael adapted a real place to suit his composition. As in his other river views, the setting is water and sky, and the panel is the kind of landscape made for a private owner's room.","i":"Loevestein stands where the Maas and Waal rivers meet and was a key fort in the south of the Dutch Republic as well as its state prison; Hugo Grotius famously escaped from it in 1621, hidden in a book chest. A castle of that standing carried political weight. Painted in 1644, shortly before Spain recognized Dutch independence at Münster in 1648, the picture probably expressed national pride on its owner's part, the stronghold standing for a country that had defended itself.","c":"Salomon van Ruysdael's landscapes of the 1640s keep the tonal harmony of his early work but add warmer color, more varied trees and more weight in the sky. A castle gives him a vertical accent that a long, horizontal river composition needs. Because he rearranged his source, the picture is the idea of a castle rather than a topographical record, which suits a painter more interested in mood and structure than in an accurate view. A quietly accomplished mid-career picture."},"met:437589":{"d":"A ferry crosses a river near Gorinchem, the fortified town on the Merwede, with a windmill, a boat and human figures among the recorded elements of the scene. At about 106 by 133 centimeters the canvas is far larger than the small panels of the 1630s, and its scale suggests a picture meant to hang as a principal feature of a room. The ferry was a crossing every traveler and trader knew, which makes the subject at once ordinary and recognizably local.","i":"Ferries were everyday infrastructure in a country cut by rivers and canals, and Dutch landscape painters made the crossing a favorite subject because it gathers water, sky, boats, people and animals into one modest event. Naming a real town placed the picture in a recognizable stretch of the Republic's waterways, flat, busy and prosperous. Buyers were looking at their own country shown with affection, without the Italian sunshine or classical ruins of imported landscape.","c":"Ruysdael's ferry pictures tend to repeat a formula, trees massed at one side, a broad river, a laden boat, but within it the water, sky and light have an unforced naturalism that makes the repetition forgivable. By 1646 the monochrome of his early years had given way to a fuller palette and a more spacious design, and a canvas of this size shows him comfortable at scale. Dependable, serene and rarely surprising."},"met:437586":{"d":"A country road runs through open land, with trees, a house, cattle and a man among the recorded subjects. The canvas is wide, about 99 by 134 centimeters, a format that favors a long view and a broad sky. It is a landscape of the everyday: the road is the track by which farms, villages and markets were connected, and the people and animals on it are those of ordinary rural traffic.","i":"Dutch painters turned the dirt road into a subject in its own right, partly because it carried a sense of movement and depth into a flat land. Cattle on or beside a road recall the country's dairy wealth and the daily traffic between farm and town. For an urban buyer, such a picture offered a calm, orderly image of the countryside that fed the cities, with no moral or story beyond the pleasure of recognition.","c":"Ruysdael's landscapes of the late 1640s show him at ease: the tonal harmony of the 1630s remains, but trees have more individuality and skies more weight. A canvas of this size has room to breathe. Its modesty is both virtue and limit: the composition is familiar, offers no surprise, and relies on the viewer's pleasure in quiet observation. Pleasant, well judged and unambitious."},"nga:139458":{"d":"A ferry crosses a river in this large landscape, about 101 by 135 centimeters. Such pictures by Salomon van Ruysdael are typically built from a few simple elements: broad water, a crossing boat, trees and a generous sky. In the Haarlem river-view manner he refined, the subject is ordinary traffic rather than an event, and at this size the canvas would probably have hung as a principal picture in a prosperous Dutch house.","i":"The river ferry was among Ruysdael's most repeated subjects, and its popularity says something about his buyers: Dutch patrons wanted images of the waterways that linked their towns, and the crossing, ordinary and nearly uneventful, let a painter show people, animals and boats at work in a peaceful country. In 1649, the year after the Peace of Münster ended the long war with Spain, such calm scenes may have had a particular appeal.","c":"Ruysdael's river views are the quiet backbone of Haarlem landscape. They repeat, and they seldom astonish, yet the best of them, with their unforced space and honest light, achieve a naturalness that more dramatic painters lose. Calm, competent and trustworthy rather than inventive. His nephew Jacob van Ruisdael would take landscape toward drama and monumentality; Salomon's gift is peace."},"met:437587":{"d":"Ships sail on open water in a small panel, about 35 by 44 centimeters. The title is simply Marine and the recorded subjects are ships and the sea, so the picture is one of the sea pieces that Dutch painters made in great numbers for private collectors: a modest image in which vessels, water and sky share the interest, with no named event recorded.","i":"The Dutch Republic's prosperity rested on shipping, and sea pictures showed the vessels that carried herring, grain and spices. A generic marine like this is an image of the sea itself and of the seamanship and trade that depended on it. In Dutch painting the sky was as important as the ships: weather, wind and clouds gave a mood, and viewers were practiced at reading both the vessels' rigging and the weather.","c":"Landscape was Ruysdael's true territory, and he was not a specialist in shipping, so his sea pieces are best valued for air and light rather than for the exact rigging a marine specialist would supply. A small panel like this is agreeable, economical and quietly observed, and it shows how readily his low horizons and large skies transferred from river to sea. It is a minor note in a body of work built on landscape."},"met:437588":{"d":"A village in winter is the setting, with men, women and horses, and the rowdy sport named in the title. Eel-pulling was a Dutch folk game in which a live eel was suspended from a rope and contestants tried to seize it as they passed. The panel is large for a wood support, about 75 by 106 centimeters. The scene is an unusual one for Salomon van Ruysdael, better known for river and country landscapes.","i":"Eel-pulling was a boisterous popular amusement in the Dutch Republic, linked to fairs and festivals and often frowned upon by preachers and magistrates for its cruelty and disorder. Pictures of folk games could be read two ways: as affectionate records of village fun, or as cautionary images of unruliness; the viewer's response depended on temperament and social position. In a landscape painter's hands the subject probably served more as a lively incident in a wintry village than as a moral lesson.","c":"Salomon van Ruysdael is rarely a narrative painter, and a village game is a lively departure from the river views that made his name. His tonal approach to landscape suits a winter setting, and the folk subject brings an energy his ferries seldom have. Its value is as much that of a record of a vanished amusement as that of a painting."},"met:438380":{"d":"Fishing boats work a river, with men aboard fishing from them. The panel is small, about 38 by 56 centimeters, and its date, the early 1660s, falls in the last decade of Salomon van Ruysdael's life, when he often painted quiet river scenes. The subject is working water rather than a ferry crossing or a harbor: boats, men and river under the broad sky on which his landscapes depend.","i":"Fishing was a daily source of food and income along the Dutch rivers, and fishermen appear in landscape painting less as heroes than as part of the working scenery of a prosperous country. Painters liked the subject because boats, nets and reflections give a small panel plenty to do without a story. Buyers probably prized such pictures for their calm and their recognizable local character, a comfortable view of an unhurried working life.","c":"The late Ruysdael is often counted his most refined: the formula is pared down, the mood quieter, and the river is allowed to be mostly light and air. A small panel like this suits that economy. It will not startle anyone, and it repeats motifs he had used for decades, but it shows a veteran painter who knew exactly how little a picture needs to hold the eye."},"met:437590":{"d":"A view of the Dutch town of Alkmaar, in which the large church of Saint Lawrence (the Grote Kerk) is the recognizable landmark among the buildings. A windmill, a ferry and sailing boats on the river complete the scene. The panel is wide, about 51 by 84 centimeters, and the view is a topographical one: a portrait of a real town for people who knew it, treated in the manner of Salomon van Ruysdael's river landscapes.","i":"Town views were prized by local citizens and visitors, who recognized their own skyline: the church tower, mills and waterfront stood for civic pride. Alkmaar had a special claim on Dutch memory, since its townspeople held out against a Spanish siege in 1573, an early success in the revolt against Spain, and it was long known for its cheese market. Views like this present a prosperous, peaceful urban world joined to the country by water.","c":"A topographical view keeps a landscape painter on a short leash: the church and skyline must be recognizable, and the invention of the free river views gives way to documentation. Ruysdael's habit is to keep the town part of the landscape rather than lay it out like a map. It is a solid, genial picture, valuable as a record and less inspired than his best ferry crossings."},"met:437467":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 112 by 178 centimeters, shows the goddess Pallas Athena together with the dogs of a herdsman. The catalog records just these two subjects, goddess and dogs, and no fuller description is available, so the setting and attitudes are left unstated. The painter, Briton Riviere, specialized in animal subjects and often set them within mythological and biblical stories; this picture, dated 1876, follows that pattern, a classical theme chosen for what it allows him to show of animals' behavior.","i":"The scene probably draws on the Odyssey, where the dogs of the swineherd Eumaeus sense the goddess Athena when she appears at his hut, though the people there cannot see her. The dogs' instinctive recognition of what humans miss is the point, and it fits the Victorian interest in animal sensibility that Riviere shared with painters such as Landseer. Athena, goddess of wisdom and war, is customarily shown with helmet, spear or aegis; the catalog does not list which appear here.","c":"Riviere's animal pictures succeed when the creature's behavior is observed with sympathy, and a theme about dogs perceiving a goddess suits that talent. His weakness is a taste for literary explanation: the learned classical title can weigh more than the paint, and the human or divine figures in his mythological scenes are generally less convincing than the animals. The scale and ambition are admirable; the result is a thoughtful, slightly theatrical Victorian anecdote rather than a vision of antiquity."},"nga:137505":{"d":"A tightly packed crowd of about two dozen men and women surges out of a fortified stone ruin that fills the right half of the picture. At the front Christ kneels in a lilac-purple robe under the crossbeam, wearing a ring of thorns and looking back over his shoulder, while a muscled man in ruby breeches pulls the rope. Two bound men follow, nude but for gray cloths, and a woman in blue holds out a white cloth. A mounted soldier in armor rears on a dappled horse; pale figures walk into a hilly distance. Painted on copper, it measures about 44 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The Way of the Cross, Christ's walk to Calvary, was one of the most familiar subjects of devotion. Poelenburch sets it among overgrown ruins, in the manner of the Italianate painters, but keeps the traditional cast: Christ crowned with thorns, the two thieves to be crucified with him, soldiers, and the Virgin, probably the woman with clasped hands. The woman offering a white cloth is probably Veronica, who in tradition wiped his face. Ruins can also signal the old order passing away.","c":"Poelenburch made his name with small, polished Italianate pictures on copper, and the format suits his strengths: enamel-smooth finish, crisp little figures, glowing color against a pale sky. A Passion procession asks for more gravity than he usually supplies. Crowd, ruin and distance are handled with an agreeable lightness that sits oddly with the subject, so the picture reads as a charming Italian ruin with a drama inside it. Skilled, but more decorative than devotional."},"nga:131041":{"d":"In a landscape of plant-covered ruins, a bearded old man in a purple tunic and scarlet cloak greets a kneeling young woman and a boy near the lower right corner. The woman, in blue with white sleeves, spreads her arms toward his outstretched hand; the boy holds a bundle of sticks. At least seven barefoot figures move through the middle distance, among a column, crumbling stonework, green hills and blue mountains, under a sky that shifts from pale yellow at the horizon to lapis blue above. The panel is small, about 36 by 47 centimeters.","i":"The scene comes from the First Book of Kings, chapter 17: during a drought, the prophet Elijah meets a widow at the gate of Zarephath, gathering sticks, and asks her for water and bread. She has only a handful of meal and a little oil, but she shares them, and her jars do not run dry until the rain returns. The story rewards faith and hospitality to a stranger, and the bundle of sticks is what identifies the moment.","c":"Poelenburch's biblical pictures are in effect Italian ruin landscapes with a story added, and this one is well organized: a clearly staged meeting at the front, a gradual recession of ruins and hills, and a sky that moves from pale yellow to lapis. The figures are small and graceful rather than weighty, and the story's stakes, hunger and faith, are played down in favor of charm. Delicate and pleasing, if somewhat lightweight."},"met:436517":{"d":"A miniature of Christ being lowered from the cross, with Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene among the figures. The picture is tiny, about 16 by 11 centimeters, painted in tempera on parchment that was later laid down on a wooden panel. Parchment is the support of illuminated manuscripts, so the image was probably made for, or cut from, a book of devotion or a choir book, and it is meant to be held close and read like a page.","i":"The Descent from the Cross shows the moment after the Crucifixion when Christ's body is lowered for burial, with those who loved him grieving around it. It was a favorite of Renaissance devotion because it invites the viewer to share both the grief and the care of handling the body. John, the beloved disciple, and Mary Magdalene, who came to the tomb to anoint the body and was the first witness of the Resurrection, stand among the principal mourners.","c":"In a space this small the achievement is compression: a whole tragedy conveyed by a handful of figures, with the bright color and fine line of a manuscript painter. Girolamo da Cremona was an illuminator, and the best of that tradition gave a book page the glow of a jewel. As a work of devotion it is polished and affecting rather than novel, and its scale makes it an object to handle more than a picture to view."},"nga:408":{"d":"The title gives the cast: Saint Ursula with two angels and a donor, the patron who paid for the panel and is shown in the saint's protective company. The picture is upright, about 45 by 29 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood, probably for private devotion or a small chapel. It comes from the middle of Benozzo Gozzoli's career, when he was a successful Florentine painter who had worked as an assistant to Fra Angelico and carried forward his gift for gleaming color and narrative charm.","i":"Ursula, according to the Golden Legend, was a British princess who set out on a pilgrimage with eleven thousand virgin companions and was martyred by the Huns at Cologne. She is usually shown as a young crowned woman with an arrow, the instrument of her death, or a banner, or sheltering her companions under her cloak. Including a donor was a way of placing the patron in the saint's care: a prayer in paint, kept in view in the house or chapel.","c":"Gozzoli is at his most winning in color, gold and decorative detail, qualities that favor a small panel of this kind. He is less strong in structure and weight than the Florentine painters who followed Masaccio, and his figures can have a sweet, doll-like composure. As a devotional image it is graceful and warm rather than forceful, and it shows the Fra Angelico inheritance in a lighter, more fashionable form."},"nga:41582":{"d":"Two moments of one story share a small panel, about 24 by 35 centimeters: the banquet at which Herod is entertained and the beheading of Saint John the Baptist. The format is that of a predella, the strip of small narrative scenes beneath an altarpiece, and the picture is painted in tempera on poplar. Gozzoli follows the Florentine habit of telling a story in continuous action, with both episodes brought together in a single panel, to be read at close range.","i":"The story (Matthew 14 and Mark 6) follows John's imprisonment for condemning Herod Antipas's marriage to Herodias. At a birthday feast Herodias's daughter Salome danced so well that Herod swore to give her whatever she asked, and prompted by her mother she asked for John's head. For Florence, where the Baptist was patron saint, he was a figure of special civic devotion, and scenes of his death appear even on the bronze doors of the Baptistery.","c":"In small narrative panels Gozzoli is at his best, alert to costume, architecture and the bustle of a court, and a predella-size panel asks for exactly the quick storytelling he does well. The horror of the subject is kept at a courteous distance, in favor of elegance and incident. It is lively and attractive rather than disturbing, and shows why he was in demand as a narrator more than as a painter of weighty figures."},"met:436561":{"d":"Four saints are grouped together: Nicholas of Tolentino, Roch, Sebastian and Bernardino of Siena, with two small kneeling donors, a man and a woman, and a town in the background. The Met's keywords also record angels. The picture is about 79 by 62 centimetres, painted in tempera and gold and later transferred from its original wooden panel to canvas, a restoration that can thin the surface. Gozzoli made it in 1481, when he was in his early sixties.","i":"Sebastian and Roch were the great protectors against the plague, which struck Italian towns repeatedly in the fifteenth century, and Nicholas of Tolentino and Bernardino of Siena were widely venerated friars. A picture that groups them with kneeling donors is probably a votive image, offered by the couple in thanks for protection or in hope of it, with the town standing for the community they hoped to protect. The donors, shown small at the saints' feet, place themselves under the saints' care.","c":"Gozzoli, once an assistant to Fra Angelico, is a gifted storyteller and decorator, happiest on the walls of his Florentine and Pisan frescoes. A devotional picture of this size gives him less room, and the saints are conventional types. The kneeling donors and the town add human interest, and the gold and bright colour still please. The transfer from wood to canvas has probably cost some of the surface."},"met:436562":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 40 by 46 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood, shows the Conversion of Saint Paul. The Met's keywords record a soldier, horses, dogs and angels, so the scene includes the armed retinue of Saul, the persecutor who is struck down on the road to Damascus. It has the size and shape of a predella panel, one of a row of small narrative scenes beneath a main altarpiece, and it matches three other Gozzoli panels at the Met in size.","i":"In Acts 9, Saul, on his way to Damascus to arrest Christians, is struck down by a light from heaven and hears Christ's voice asking why he persecutes him; he is blinded for three days and then baptised as Paul. The Bible does not mention a horse, but artists from the Middle Ages onward showed Saul falling from one, and the image of a proud soldier brought low became standard. Paul was a founder of the Church of Rome, a natural subject for a predella of saints.","c":"Gozzoli, a former assistant of Fra Angelico, tells his stories like a good chronicler, delighted by costume, horses and landscape rather than by theological depth, and a scene of confused horsemen offers plenty of that. The drama is therefore lively but light: a fairy-tale cavalcade more than a spiritual crisis. As a small, bright panel it has great charm, though it is not among his most original conceptions."},"met:436563":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 40 by 46 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood, shows a legend of Saint Peter and the sorcerer Simon Magus. The Met's keywords record men, Peter and demons, so the scene includes the demons that, in the legend, held the magician up in the air and abandoned him when Peter prayed. It matches three other Gozzoli panels at the Met in size and probably belongs with them in a predella.","i":"In the apocryphal Acts of Peter, retold in the Golden Legend, Simon Magus tries to prove his powers to Nero by flying over Rome; Peter prays, the demons holding him up let go, and Simon crashes to the ground. He shares a name with the Simon of Acts 8 who tried to buy the Holy Spirit's power, which gave rise to the word simony. The story shows the true faith overcoming false magic and honours Peter as the first leader of the Roman Church.","c":"Fantastic episodes give Gozzoli free rein: a falling sorcerer, flapping demons and a startled crowd are exactly the material for his lively, decorative narration. The risk is that the story becomes a cartoon, and demons in his hands tend toward the whimsical rather than the frightening. It is an entertaining panel rather than a profound one, and a good example of the taste for the marvellous that ran through Florentine devotional art."},"met:436564":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 39 by 46 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood, shows Saint Zenobius, bishop of Florence, raising a dead child to life. The Met's keywords record a child, men, women, saints and death, so the scene gathers the saint, the lifeless child and the grieving or amazed onlookers. It matches three other Gozzoli panels at the Met in size and format.","i":"Zenobius, an early bishop of Florence, is venerated as one of the city's patron saints, and his miracles were retold in Florentine devotion, among them the raising of a child from the dead. The scene recalls the miracles of Christ and the prophets, and so proves the saint's holiness. For a Florentine audience the story had local pride: it showed the city's own saint working wonders. Small panels like this were typical of a predella honouring local and universal saints.","c":"Gozzoli handles a scene of grief and wonder with a gentle, unthreatening charm; the miracle is clear and the figures are neatly grouped, but death is not allowed to be frightening and the emotions are formal. The panel is lovely as a small piece of Florentine storytelling and typical of Gozzoli's amiable temperament, though it lacks the intensity that Masaccio or Donatello's circle brought to scenes of miracle."},"met:436565":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 39 by 46 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood. It shows the meeting of Totila, king of the Goths, with Saint Benedict, the founder of Western monasticism. The Met's keywords record soldiers, men, women and monks, so the scene sets armed men against the monastic community of Benedict. It matches three other Gozzoli panels at the Met in size and format.","i":"The story comes from the Dialogues of Pope Gregory the Great. Totila, who ruled the Ostrogoths in Italy in the sixth century, doubted that Benedict had the gift of prophecy and sent his armour-bearer dressed in royal robes to deceive him; Benedict saw through the trick, and when Totila himself came he fell to the ground and was rebuked and given a prophecy of his own future. The scene shows the humble monk dominating the mighty warlord.","c":"Gozzoli enjoys such confrontations of power and humility. The contrast between armour and monastic habit, and between prostrate king and calm saint, gives a clear, readable composition. As in his other small narrative panels, the setting is tidy and charming, with cheerful colour and little danger. It is a pleasing, decorative piece that treats a moral lesson as a pageant."},"nga:1163":{"d":"Christ, long-haired, haloed and holding a golden book, raises one arm toward a cave-like tomb, where Lazarus stands at the mouth wrapped in strips of off-white cloth while two men remove his wrappings. A close crowd in pink, teal and gold robes gathers around, some gesturing toward the tomb, and ten haloed men stand behind Christ. Beyond lie green hills, palm trees and a sprawling city of white buildings with red roofs, under a light blue sky with pink clouds. The canvas is about 66 by 80 centimeters.","i":"The raising of Lazarus (John 11) is Christ's last great miracle before the Passion: Lazarus of Bethany had been dead four days when Christ called him out of the tomb, and the miracle was read as a sign of the Resurrection to come and of Christ's power over death. The haloed men behind Christ are his apostles, and the people gesturing at the tomb are the witnesses whose astonishment, and in the Gospel whose belief, the picture records.","c":"Gozzoli was in his seventies when he painted this, and the old manner shows: a crowded, colorful, procession-like composition in which each figure is a pleasant item in a pattern. The drama of the miracle is muted, since the figures cluster more than they react, but the color, the palm-dotted landscape and the white city have a fairy-tale charm. A late echo of the festive style of his Medici chapel in a devout subject."},"nga:164954":{"d":"Four women sit in a row outdoors, in front of a balustrade and under leafy trees, each in a long gown and hat and holding a parasol. The handling is loose: dabs and visible strokes make details hard to pick out, and the texture of the canvas shows between the forms. Dresses and parasols in pale green, plum, carnation pink, white and yellow stand out against purple shadows on an apricot-orange ground, with pale green, indigo and icy blue beyond for grass, trees and sky. It is a small picture, about 24 by 35 centimeters.","i":"Prendergast, a Boston-raised American, had come to Paris in 1891 to study, and the picture records his encounter with the city's park life and with French avant-garde color: fashionable women at leisure, an Impressionist subject, treated in patches of color that already point toward the decorative, pattern-making style of his later work. Parasols and long dresses mark a middle-class Parisian promenade, in which seeing and being seen was the point.","c":"A modest sketch, but one that shows where Prendergast's gift lies: not in character or atmosphere but in color arranged as pattern. The row of parasols and gowns reads almost as a frieze, with muted purple shadows holding the pale hues together on an orange ground. Detail is sacrificed to effect, so the picture is slight; yet it is an early, attractive sign of a painter who would find his own voice in sparkling watercolors."},"nga:34143":{"d":"A woman sits angled slightly to the left, her right arm resting on a surface and her left hand in her lap. Her expression is composed, her eyes light, her light-brown hair smoothly styled. She wears a deep blue gown with billowing sleeves trimmed in white lace, its neckline slightly open on a white chemise, and no visible jewelry. The background is dark, so the figure stands out against it. The canvas measures 36 by 28 inches, the English portrait format known as a kit-cat.","i":"The sitter is not named, so the picture is read through its conventions. Loose, billowing sleeves, an open neckline and smooth, unadorned hair are probably the so-called undress fashion of early eighteenth-century English portraits, which claimed a relaxed, timeless elegance in place of court formality. A portrait of this kind recorded a woman's standing and good taste for her family, and women painters such as Verelst were a small minority in the field.","c":"A restrained, dignified portrait. The dark ground and deep blue gown concentrate attention on the face and the lace, and the pose is easy. Verelst follows the formulas of fashionable portraiture closely, so little in the handling or invention is personal, and the sitter's character is held in polite reserve. Its merit is quiet competence, and as one of the few portraits of the period by a woman it deserves more notice than its modesty suggests."},"met:436928":{"d":"Young women are shown at a window, as the title says; the recorded subjects, women and a portrait, suggest a picture that sits between a genre scene and a group portrait. The canvas, about 73 by 60 centimeters, is a size for a private salon. Raimundo de Madrazo, born in Rome to a distinguished Spanish family of painters and working mainly in Paris, made his career with polished pictures of fashionable women, sold to wealthy collectors in Europe and the United States.","i":"Pictures of women at windows have a long history, from Dutch interiors to nineteenth-century genre, because the window sets the figure between private and public worlds and offers a natural excuse for glances, waiting and conversation. In Madrazo's time, a picture of this kind, half portrait and half genre, appealed to buyers who wanted fresh, charming images of youth and fashion. The pleasure for the viewer lay in elegance and in the glimpse of a private moment.","c":"Madrazo's strengths are fluent drawing, sparkling color and a keen eye for fabric and fashion, and a picture like this trades on them. The limits are equally clear: the charm is on the surface, and the sitters are types more than individuals. Next to his brother-in-law Mariano Fortuny, whose virtuosity he echoed, he is a lighter painter who gave a prosperous clientele exactly what it wanted."},"met:436926":{"d":"A portrait of Samuel Putnam Avery (1822–1904), a New York art dealer and collector, painted on a small wooden panel, about 61 by 49 centimeters. The title and recorded subject identify a man; no costume, pose or setting is described here. Raimundo de Madrazo painted it in 1876, in the years when he was among the most fashionable portraitists and genre painters in Paris and counted American buyers among his clients.","i":"Avery was a leading New York dealer who helped introduce French and other European painting to American collectors, and he served as an early trustee of the Metropolitan Museum. A portrait by a fashionable Paris painter says much about the sitter: a man of taste with international connections, at home in the artistic world he traded in. Portraits of dealers and collectors by artists in their circle also served as homage and as quiet advertisement.","c":"Madrazo is a portraitist of polish rather than depth, and a likeness of a friend and patron plays to his strengths: a brisk, flattering, well-drawn presence, handled with fluency on a small panel. A man gives him less scope than a woman for the fashionable sparkle of his society portraits. A skilled, agreeable likeness, of more interest for the sitter's place in American art history than for any daring in the painting."},"met:437083":{"d":"The wedding of Mary and Joseph is shown on a gilded panel of about 65 by 48 centimeters. The recorded subjects include the Virgin, Saint Joseph, other men and women and a bird, which may be the dove that, in the tradition, alighted on Joseph's flowering rod. Painted in tempera with gold, it is a small, richly worked devotional picture in the International Gothic manner of Lombardy, with elegant figures and a love of surface decoration. Michelino was better known as a manuscript illuminator.","i":"The marriage of Mary and Joseph is told in the apocryphal gospels and the Golden Legend: Mary's suitors each brought a rod to the high priest, and Joseph's rod burst into flower (and, in some versions, a dove settled on it), identifying him as her husband. The scene gave painters a way to treat the holy family's origins, and from the fourteenth century it was a popular altarpiece subject. It stressed Joseph's role as chosen guardian and the legitimacy of the Virgin's marriage.","c":"Michelino's refinement is the point: soft, curving lines, delicate color and gold used as light as well as decoration, in the courtly style known as International Gothic. It is a manuscript painter's idea of a panel, in which figures are lovely patterns rather than solid bodies, and the space is a stage for ornament. Fashionable in its moment, it shows a style that Florentine painters had already begun to leave behind."},"met:438615":{"d":"A portrait of a man in pastel on blue-gray paper, laid down on canvas for support, about 58 by 47 centimeters. The title identifies the sitter as Olivier Journu (1724–1783); no costume or setting is described here. Pastel is dry powdered color applied in strokes and blended with the finger or stump, so the artist can work fast, build soft transitions and let the blue-gray ground show through as a middle tone. The work is dated 1756, when Jean-Baptiste Perronneau was about forty.","i":"Pastel portraits were the fashionable medium of mid-eighteenth-century France: quick to make, brilliant on the surface and relatively affordable, they let well-off townspeople, merchants, magistrates and financiers own a likeness once reserved for the nobility. Perronneau traveled widely to serve provincial and foreign clients, and he is admired for reserved, individual likenesses rather than court gloss. A named, dated sitter makes this a record of a particular person, not a type.","c":"Perronneau was the connoisseurs' pastellist, the quieter rival of the more famous Maurice-Quentin de La Tour. Where La Tour dazzles with vivacity, Perronneau offers restrained, silvery harmonies, close attention to character and a gift for gray, blue and olive tints. He lacked La Tour's Paris clientele and fame, but at his best he is subtle and truthful. A well-regarded example from the middle of his career, in a medium that demands decision and a light hand."},"nga:41622":{"d":"A black-robed Mary sits frontally on a wide scarlet bench, holding the blessing Child, in a tall panel shaped round at the top around her halo and wider at the shoulders. Her robe carries brick-red diamonds, her gold crown has dangling jewels, and her almond eyes look off to one side; Jesus has adult-like proportions and holds a scroll. Four small saints, half the Child's height, float against the faded gold, the lower two crowned and holding jugs. A signature along the bottom, MARGARIT RITIO ME FECIT, declares that Margarit made the panel.","i":"The enthroned Madonna and Child is the central image of thirteenth-century Italian devotion, adapted from Byzantine icons: a frontal, formal Mary as Queen of Heaven, wearing a crown, and the Child shown with adult proportions because he is the eternal Word, not merely a baby. Saints on such panels were chosen to suit the church or the patron, and here the four are kept small, as attendants. The scroll and raised fingers mark Christ as teacher and blesser.","c":"Margaritone's art is archaic by design: a flat, linear Mary with stylized almond eyes and a decorative robe, and a Child with an adult's proportions. Later writers, beginning with Vasari, treated him as a representative of the old Byzantine manner superseded by Cimabue and Giotto. The verdict is fair in that the forms are stiff beside the Florentines', and unfair in that the clear outlines, patterned surfaces and glowing color have real power."},"met:712946":{"d":"Christ summons the tax collector Matthew to follow him, the moment the Gospels record as the beginning of Matthew's calling as an apostle. The canvas, about 131 by 156 centimeters, is slightly wider than tall, a format that accommodates a group of figures. Giovanni Battista Caracciolo, called Battistello, was the first and most faithful Neapolitan follower of Caravaggio, and any version of this subject by him recalls Caravaggio's famous treatment in Rome, with its dramatic light and everyday figures.","i":"The story is from Matthew 9:9: Jesus sees a tax collector at his post and says follow me, and Matthew gets up and follows. The subject was a favorite of the Counter-Reformation because it dramatizes divine grace falling on an ordinary, even disreputable, man, and speaks to the conversion every viewer was invited to make. Caravaggio's Calling in San Luigi dei Francesi (1599 to 1600), with its strong contrast of dark and light and its contemporary dress, set the standard that painters in Naples followed and reworked.","c":"Battistello grasped Caravaggio's lesson in the strong light and the weight of the figures, and in Naples he turned it into a muscular, somber style of his own. In the 1620s his Caravaggesque force tended to soften toward a more classical clarity, and next to the Roman original a late Calling like this shares the means but not the shock. Serious and confident, it is a considered reworking of a famous model rather than an innovation."},"met:435670":{"d":"A genre scene in oil on canvas, about 98 by 88 centimeters, painted in the early 1660s by the Dordrecht artist Cornelis Bisschop. The scene shows a young woman and a man, a cavalier, with a table on which stand a candlestick, a ewer and a platter, and a hat with a feather. These props point to a domestic or tavern interior and a story of courtship or service.","i":"Dutch genre scenes of a woman and a gentleman in a room with food and drink were often read as scenes of courtship, flirtation or service, with moral overtones that a seventeenth-century viewer would have recognized. A feather in the hat suggests the gallant, and the ewer and platter suggest hospitality or domestic service. The exact story is not known, and the title's pairing of a young woman and a cavalier suggests an encounter between a maidservant or hostess and a gentleman.","c":"Bisschop belongs to the Dordrecht school of fine genre and portrait painters, and his figures are generally crisp, smoothly finished and well lit. A picture of this kind lives by its handling of textures, such as metal, cloth and feather, and by the sparkle of its encounter. The type is respectable and appealing, and it shows the polish that Dordrecht painters brought to small domestic stories."},"nga:46591":{"d":"Pale yellow farm buildings with pointed brown roofs stand behind a low yellow-gray stone wall under a clear blue sky, the low horizon mostly hidden by the structures. In the foreground an area of grass and bare earth holds a wooden wagon with two large wheels at the left and small black and white birds pecking at the ground at the right. The scene seems bathed in golden light, and the brushstrokes are visible, especially in the sky and the buildings. Oil on canvas, about 51 by 81 centimeters, dated 1855.","i":"Daubigny was a leader of the Barbizon school, which in the 1840s and 1850s turned from classical landscape to ordinary rural subjects, such as a farmyard, observed directly. A farm with a wagon and birds is a humble motif that asks nothing of the viewer but looking. Daubigny's insistence on painting outdoors and on keeping visible brushwork made him a forerunner of the Impressionists, who admired him.","c":"A simple, honest picture: the golden light on pale walls and the plain geometry of roofs and wall give a satisfying structure, and the unlabored brushwork keeps it lively. It lacks the poetic depth of his river views, and the subject is minor, but it displays the directness for which Daubigny was valued. A characteristic small triumph of unpretentious observation."},"met:436089":{"d":"A village at evening, with cattle, is the subject of this wide canvas of about 58 by 93 centimeters. The record lists a town, the evening and cattle, and gives the title as The Hamlet of Optevoz; the museum offers no description of the light, the water or the arrangement of the houses and animals. The date is given as about 1852, early in Daubigny's mature career.","i":"Optevoz is a village in the Dauphiné, in eastern France, with ponds and rocky country that attracted Daubigny around 1852, as it did his friend Corot; he returned to it more than once. Cattle at evening is a classic Barbizon motif, associating the end of the day with a pastoral peace that city buyers, increasingly industrial and urban, found consoling.","c":"Evening and cattle were stock Barbizon subjects, and Daubigny handles them with a gentle, unemphatic touch that is closer to Corot's silvery calm than to Millet's harder rural seriousness. The weakness, as in many of his larger pictures of the early 1850s, is a certain diffuseness; the viewer wants a stronger center. Pleasing and atmospheric, and a reminder of how wide his appeal to collectors was."},"nga:195117":{"d":"A small panel, about 24 by 47 centimeters, in oil on wood, titled Auvers (Washerwomen) and dated 1861, by Charles-François Daubigny. The subject is washerwomen, so the picture presumably shows women washing linen at the river at Auvers-sur-Oise, a village northwest of Paris. A subject like this offers reflections in the water and bright patches of linen. The horizontal format and size suggest a rapid study from nature.","i":"Washing was done at the riverside by the women of the village, and washerwomen were a favorite motif for painters of modern life from the mid-nineteenth century, partly because the activity combined labor, water and reflected light. Daubigny moved to Auvers in about 1861, the year of this panel, and went on to paint the Oise and its banks for the rest of his life. His house there later drew Cézanne, Pissarro and Van Gogh.","c":"Daubigny's small river panels are among his best work: a quick sense of light on water, a few figures to give scale, and nothing forced. The subject, the size and the date place it in the group of studies in which he worked out his way of seeing the Oise. A modest example of the Barbizon landscape tradition, likely to be fresh and direct."},"met:436080":{"d":"A river and trees are the recorded motifs of this horizontal panel, an oil on wood of about 38 by 67 centimeters, dated 1863. The title places the scene on the Oise, the river northwest of Paris where Daubigny lived at Auvers from the early 1860s. Nothing in the record describes the composition, the light or any figures, so only the setting and the format can be stated.","i":"Daubigny had a house built at Auvers-sur-Oise in 1861, and the Oise was his daily subject. From 1857 he also painted from a floating studio, a converted boat he called Le Botin, from which he could study the river from water level, the low viewpoint that gives his river scenes their intimacy. The panel is dated 1863.","c":"Small panels like this one are where Daubigny is at his freshest: the wide, low format, the quick handling and the sense of a place seen from the water were praised by the Impressionists, who saw in him a forerunner. They were also what academic critics scorned as mere 'impressions'. This panel belongs to the type that is among his best."},"met:436081":{"d":"A small panel of about 21 by 38 centimeters, scarcely larger than a book, dated 1863 and showing, according to the record, a river and a village. It is cataloged as a landscape painting and carries the keywords Villages, Rivers and Landscapes; the museum gives no further description of the buildings, boats or sky. The scale suggests a sketch painted on the spot, in oil on wood.","i":"Daubigny's smallest panels were often painted quickly on the water from the Botin, his floating studio, and then kept as sketches or developed in the studio into larger pictures. The date of 1863 puts this among his years at Auvers, when the Oise and the Seine supplied him with endless river views. Village, river and sky make up the whole of his vocabulary here.","c":"A sketch of this size escapes the labor of finish, and Daubigny's gift for catching a river's light with a few strokes is at its best on such panels. The compensating loss is detail and drama: the subject is plain and the format modest. But this is the kind of picture on which his reputation among painters, as opposed to critics, was based."},"met:436083":{"d":"A river, trees and birds are the recorded motifs of this small panel, an oil on wood of about 24 by 45 centimeters, dated 1864. The title names the birds as storks, which would stand tall at the water's edge or fly over the river; the museum gives no description of their number or position, nor of the surrounding landscape, so the arrangement is not detailed here.","i":"In a landscape of water, trees and sky, a few large birds give the eye a point to settle and a touch of life; the title makes them the point of the scene, a small incident in the quiet of the river. Storks are large wading birds that nest on rooftops and in marshy meadows in parts of Europe. The date of 1864 places the panel in the heart of Daubigny's river years.","c":"The storks give this small panel a focus that many of Daubigny's river sketches lack, and the horizontal format lets sky and water do the rest. It is slight, as sketches are, but the sense of open space and a quiet river is convincing. Its modesty is its virtue; a larger version would likely have lost the freshness."},"nga:35087":{"d":"A tranquil river scene in which, at the left, clusters of trees with dark branches hang over calm water and cast reflections on its gray surface. Two people, apparently washerwomen, kneel at the water's edge, though the details are unclear. Across the river a distant horizon of rolling country shows trees and small brown buildings under a light blue sky with small white clouds. The palette is deep greens, browns, blues and whites, with long, thick strokes for sky and background and shorter, dotted ones in the foliage. Oil on wood, about 24 by 46 centimeters, dated 1865.","i":"Valmondois is a village on the Oise a short distance from Auvers, and Daubigny worked along this stretch of river from his floating studio, the Botin, a boat he fitted out in 1857 so that he could paint the banks from the water. The washerwomen are part of the everyday life of the river, treated without anecdote, and the small panel is the kind of study that his friends among the future Impressionists admired for its immediacy.","c":"A quietly assured small panel: the dark overhanging branches against pale water, the thick sweeps of the sky and the dotted foliage show Daubigny's economy and his willingness to leave brushwork visible, qualities that the Impressionists soon adopted. The figures are mere accents, and the whole is about light on a river. It is a modest but typical record of the way he looked at the Oise."},"met:436088":{"d":"A river is the recorded subject of this small panel, an oil on wood of about 24 by 44 centimeters showing, according to its title, Portejoie on the Seine. The village, boats and sky go undescribed, and the date is uncertain: it is cataloged as either 1858 or 1868. The format and size are those of the quick river studies Daubigny painted in these years.","i":"Portejoie lies on the Seine in Normandy, between Paris and Rouen, a stretch of river that Daubigny knew well from his boating trips. The two possible dates, ten years apart, show how hard it can be to date such sketches: Daubigny's manner changed relatively little. The Seine, wide and slow there, suited his low-viewpoint river scenes.","c":"Small and swift, the panel is a good instance of how Daubigny's river sketches look from a distance of a century and a half: fresh, slightly rough, nearly Impressionist in effect. The uncertainty of the date is revealing, since the handling gives few clues of development. As a record of a day on the river it is likable; as a major statement it makes no claim."},"met:436084":{"d":"Boats on a seacoast are the recorded motifs of this horizontal panel, an oil on wood of about 34 by 58 centimeters, dated 1871. The title places them at Étaples, a fishing port on the Channel coast of northern France, and the keywords are Seascapes and Boats. How many boats there are, and what the sea and sky are like, the record does not say.","i":"Étaples, at the mouth of the Canche, was a fishing village that later attracted a large artists' colony, which had not yet formed in 1871. Daubigny, famous as a painter of rivers, turned to the sea in the 1860s and 1870s, and the shift from river to coast brought a broader horizon and a more open sky. The date follows the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 and 1871.","c":"Daubigny's seascapes are lighter and more open than his river views, and a coast of boats and sky gave him a chance for broad washes of weather. Here the subject is modest and the panel quick, but the format and subject suit him, and the likelihood is of a fresh, luminous surface. Such coastal sketches anticipate the seascapes of Impressionism."},"met:436085":{"d":"A tree, apples and a landscape are the recorded motifs, and the title makes the blossoming apple trees of spring the subject of this canvas, which measures about 59 by 85 centimeters and is dated 1873. The museum gives no description of the orchard, the sky or any figures, so the composition cannot be reported in detail beyond the season, the format and the date.","i":"An orchard in blossom was a favorite spring subject in French landscape painting, and Daubigny's late versions, with their pale pinks and whites and loose, feathery handling, were among the pictures that painters such as Monet and Van Gogh admired. The bequest of the railroad magnate Collis P. Huntington, who owned this canvas, shows the appeal such pictures had for American collectors.","c":"Blossoms are a test of restraint: too much detail and they turn to confetti. Daubigny's late style, loose and a little summary, copes by suggesting the froth of flowers in broad touches. The result is bright and pleasing, though the composition risks sameness, and the picture can look more like a pleasant effect than a place. Typical of his late work, and of what collectors liked in it."},"met:436086":{"d":"A village with a church on the Seine in morning light is the subject of this horizontal panel, an oil on wood of about 39 by 69 centimeters, dated 1874. The record lists the Seine, a village, a church building, a river and the morning; the museum gives no description of the sky, boats or foreground, so details of weather and color are not recorded here.","i":"A morning view lets a painter work with the cool, clear light of early day, mist rising off the water; it is a time of day that the Impressionists made their own, and Daubigny, their older ally, had painted at dawn and dusk since the 1850s. By 1874, the year of the first Impressionist exhibition, he was a leading figure whose river scenes younger painters admired. The panel is a late example.","c":"Church spire, village roofs and river make a composition so simple that it depends on the light, and Daubigny's gift for the quiet clarity of early day makes the best of it. The late panels can tip into formula, with the horizontals of water and bank repeated from picture to picture, but at this size the formula is a virtue: a concentrated notation of a morning. A reliable pleasure rather than a surprise."},"nga:195142":{"d":"A landscape with a body of water, trees arching across the scene and a house in the distance beneath a low horizon. Soft reflections lie on the surface of the water, where ducks swim, and a few figures stand near the water's edge. The brushwork is delicate and textured, with blended hues; the land is painted in earthy tones of soft greens and browns, and the sky carries faintly warm tints. Oil on wood, about 39 by 67 centimeters, dated 1874.","i":"In the 1870s Daubigny painted the rivers of the Oise and the Seine from his boat, often at evening, when the light on water was softest, and these late works come close to the Impressionist manner he had encouraged in younger painters such as Monet and Pissarro. The title names a sunset but no place; the river is not identified. Ducks and figures are the quiet signs of country life.","c":"A gentle late Daubigny in which blended touches and low-key color turn a river into a few veils of light. It is less incisive than his mid-century river panels, and the later pictures can seem a little slack, but the calm, the reflected glow and the arching trees are genuinely poetic. It sits on the borderline between the Barbizon school and the Impressionist generation that followed."},"nga:177919":{"d":"A small landscape in oil on wood, about 28 by 43 centimeters, painted about 1870 to 1875 by Charles-François Daubigny. The title names a distant village as the picture's goal, so the view presumably looks across open country to a cluster of houses on the horizon. The size and support are those of a study or small cabinet picture, a format Daubigny used often.","i":"A distant village in a landscape was a standard device of the Barbizon painters: it gives the open country a human anchor and a sense of distance without requiring any story. Daubigny's late small panels, painted quickly and in varied light, are among his most appreciated works, and they appealed to collectors who liked the freshness of an open-air sketch. The village is not named.","c":"This panel can best be placed by type: a small, late Daubigny, probably brisk in handling and soft in color, like his best small works. Such panels are often more modern in feeling than his larger exhibition pictures, and they show why younger painters admired him. Its particular merits are hard to weigh, but its place in his career is clear."},"met:436090":{"d":"A stream, trees and a forest are the recorded motifs of this upright canvas, about 64 by 48 centimeters, dated about 1877, a year before Daubigny's death. The title suggests that sunlight on the stream is the subject, with the water catching light between darker trees. The record says nothing of the colors or any figures, so the arrangement of the woodland is not detailed.","i":"Daubigny's forest scenes, painted near Fontainebleau and in the woods around Auvers, show his debt to the Barbizon painters among whom he began, and the upright format and the dappled light belong to a long tradition running from Dutch painting to Constable. His late work, looser and more summary than that of the 1850s, was widely admired by the Impressionists.","c":"The stream is a natural stage for sunlight, and Daubigny uses the effect with the practiced ease of his late years. The picture is also a reminder of his limits: the forest interior is a type he handled less memorably than Corot or Rousseau, and the late loose touch borders on the formulaic. Still, the freshness of the light justifies the title."},"nga:164946":{"d":"A bouquet of mixed flowers, in blue, white and yellow, sits at the center of the picture, with green leaves and ferns around it and a small cluster of red berries hanging at the left. The palette is mostly dark, and the dark-green foliage is hard to tell from the black background in places. The flowers are packed closely together, with stems and leaves reaching outward. It is a small picture, about 25 by 35 centimeters, painted in oil on metal rather than canvas, and its date is unknown.","i":"Flower pieces were among the most popular subjects for amateur and professional painters in nineteenth-century America, valued as decoration and as a record of garden and woodland. Titian Ramsay Peale is better known as a naturalist-artist, who traveled on scientific expeditions and illustrated insects and birds, and his habit of looking closely at natural forms may have shaped how he saw flowers. A spray of cut blooms and ferns is a modest, domestic subject, with berries adding a note of the wild.","c":"A modest picture whose drama comes from the dark ground, against which the blue, white and yellow blossoms glow. The weakness is the same dark ground: the foliage dissolves into it, so the bouquet reads as bright patches rather than a clearly structured arrangement. It shows a naturalist's close attention more than a decorator's polish, and beside the ambitious still lifes of the wider Peale family, which produced some of America's first great still-life painters, it is intimate and slight."},"met:436994":{"d":"A long, low panel, about 61 by 153 centimeters, tells the story of the Argonauts in several scenes. The recorded subjects include a ship, soldiers, horses, a dog, Heracles and a centaur, so the action runs from the heroes' voyage to episodes on land. The shape and gilt ornaments mark it as probably the front of a cassone, the painted wedding chest of a Florentine household, a piece of furniture on which painters spread a continuous story across the whole length.","i":"Florentine wedding chests carried stories chosen to instruct and flatter: classical tales of heroism, fidelity and good fortune suited to the young couple whose possessions the chest held. The Argonauts, led by Jason in search of the Golden Fleece, stood for daring and the quest, and Heracles, one of the crew, belongs to the same legend. The centaur may be Chiron, Jason's tutor. Painted as continuous narrative, the story could be read like a procession.","c":"Sellaio, a pupil of Filippo Lippi, worked in the workshop mode of Florence's cassone trade: brisk narrative, bright color, gilded accents, many small figures. The best of such panels are charming rather than profound, and this one is likely to reward the viewer who reads it slowly from end to end rather than seeking a single focus. Sellaio is no great innovator, but in this field his storytelling is lively and his taste fashionable."},"nga:426":{"d":"A young man stands on a rocky hillside in a light blue tunic with a jagged hem, tied at the waist with a pink sash, a pink cloak over his shoulders. Short yellow hair, a trace of a gold halo and a thin, faded golden cross held above his head mark him as the saint of the title. A small black bird stands near his feet and another flies overhead. Behind are leafy trees and stacked gray rocks, and far below a winding path leads through dark trees to a blue-and-white city of domed buildings, under a cloudless sky.","i":"John the Baptist, the prophet who baptized Christ and announced his coming, lived in the wilderness, clothed in camel hair; the thin cross is among his usual attributes, with a scroll or a lamb, and recalls that he foretold the Passion. Florence, where the Baptist was patron saint, favored his image above almost all others, and small panels of the saint alone, shown young and in the desert, served private devotion. The distant city may stand for the world he had left behind.","c":"Sellaio's Baptist is a gentle, graceful figure rather than the gaunt ascetic of some earlier Florentine painters: the elegant young man, the soft landscape and the bright cityscape suggest a devotional picture made to please. The combination of pink, light blue and green is attractive; the weakness is a certain sweetness and lack of weight, typical of Botticelli's followers. A well-made, small devotional panel, more charming than commanding."},"met:437652":{"d":"The Virgin adores the newborn Christ Child, with the infant Saint John the Baptist present, in an upright panel about 104 by 69 centimeters painted in tempera and gold. The subject, the Virgin in adoration of the Child with the young Baptist, was a Florentine favorite in the later fifteenth century, with a debt to Filippo Lippi, under whom Sellaio trained. The use of gold shows the continuing taste for glittering surface in an age already devoted to naturalistic space.","i":"The Virgin adoring the Child condenses the Nativity into a devotional image: Mary kneels in awe before the newborn, an example for the viewer's own worship, and the infant John, the son of her cousin Elizabeth, is shown as Christ's companion and forerunner, with a Florentine civic overtone since John was the city's patron saint. Such images hung in bedrooms and chapels of private houses, and their intimacy encouraged contemplation.","c":"A refined Florentine devotional picture of the Botticelli and Lippi school, with delicate drawing, courteous gestures and the gentle, slightly melancholic mood that the period's buyers liked. Sellaio does not match the intensity of Botticelli or the inventiveness of Filippino Lippi, and his compositions repeat well-liked formulas; but within that limit the picture is accomplished, polished and warm. Its gold highlights and fine finish make it a luxurious domestic object."},"met:11101":{"d":"A broad view of Yosemite Valley, with a river among the recorded subjects. The canvas is large, about 92 by 138 centimeters, a size made for a hotel, parlor or gallery wall. Thomas Hill, born in England and long settled in California, painted the valley repeatedly from the 1860s on, and by 1885 his Yosemite views were a staple of the market for tourists and collectors who wanted to take home a trace of the place.","i":"Yosemite became a national symbol in the 1860s: Congress set it aside in 1864 for public use and preservation, and painters such as Albert Bierstadt and Thomas Hill made its cliffs, waterfalls and meadows into images of American grandeur. Such pictures answered a nineteenth-century taste for the sublime in nature as proof of national destiny and divine creation, while also serving a growing tourist trade.","c":"Hill's Yosemites are competent and well informed, and he knew the valley well. They lack the theatrical light and ambition of Bierstadt's, and the repetition of a formula sometimes shows: a broad view, a hazy distance, a calm river. Still, the large format delivers what the audience wanted, and the picture has the clarity and breadth of a seasoned professional rather than the fire of a visionary."},"nga:65821":{"d":"Four figures fill a tall panel, about 161 by 119 centimeters. On the left the Virgin, in a bright pink gown with reddish hair bound in orange cloth, holds the nude Christ Child on her lap. An older woman, Saint Elizabeth, kneels in green and brown robes with gray hair under a black-and-white cloth, looking up at Mary with a white cloth in her hands. In front, the infant John the Baptist kneels at the lower right by a small fire, back turned, balancing a thin golden cross on his shoulder. The ground is gray and the background black, with a dark column faintly visible at right.","i":"The meeting of the infants Christ and John, with Elizabeth and Mary, was a popular devotional subject from the early sixteenth century, linking the two cousins whose roles were foretold: John as forerunner who would baptize Christ, and the cross he carries as the sign of the sacrifice to come. Elizabeth's aged face beside Mary's youth underscores the miracle of her late pregnancy. The small fire is unusual, and its exact meaning is uncertain; fire can signify the Holy Spirit or sacrifice.","c":"Jacopino del Conte, a Florentine working in Rome, shows the pull of the Roman manner in the large, simplified figures set against a black ground. The result is more monumental than tender: the colors are vivid and the figures strong, but the poses can feel studied, and the dark background leaves little air. A forceful Mannerist devotional picture, impressive in scale and somewhat cool in feeling."},"met:436770":{"d":"The Holy Family, the Virgin Mary, Saint Joseph and the Christ Child, is the subject of this upright panel, about 121 by 86 centimeters, painted in oil on wood. Joseph is included alongside the usual pair, which makes the picture a domestic image of a family rather than a pure Madonna and Child. Jacopino del Conte, a Florentine who built his career in Rome, was best known as a portraitist and fresco painter, and devotional panels of this kind were a regular part of the output of Roman painters.","i":"The Holy Family became an increasingly popular subject in the sixteenth century, with Joseph moving from the margins of the Nativity toward the center as a model father and protector, a shift encouraged by the growth of his cult. Pictures of the Holy Family offered households a mirror of their own ideals: obedience, tenderness, labor and trust. In central Italy such groups were typically arranged as a quiet, intimate gathering.","c":"As a devotional composition it is competent and informed by the great Roman models, but many Holy Families were made in the mid-sixteenth century, and few distinguished themselves by invention. Jacopino's reputation rests more on his portraits and frescoes, where his direct handling suits the sitter; here the formula comes first. Pleasant and well made, though without the charge of his best work."},"met:435994":{"d":"A miniature self-portrait in profile, painted on ivory, with the artist's own head shown in side view. The size is not recorded here, but miniatures of this kind are small enough to be worn or held in the hand. Richard Cosway made his name with such delicate likenesses on ivory in the 1770s, using transparent washes and fine strokes over the pale support so that faces seem lit from within. The profile recalls cameos, coins and silhouettes, which were fashionable in the same years.","i":"A self-portrait by a fashionable miniaturist doubled as an advertisement for his skill: a client could judge in a few square inches how Cosway handled flesh, hair and costume. Cosway, who later became painter to the Prince of Wales, was famously self-fashioning, known for his elaborate dress and ambitious social life, so a miniature of himself offers a polished public persona rather than a candid view.","c":"Cosway's gift is a kind of sleight of hand: a few translucent touches of color over ivory give faces life and a sparkle of elegance. At his best the flesh seems to glow; later, the mannerism hardens into formula and his sitters begin to look alike. A self-portrait of the 1770s comes before the formula set, and shows how he wished to be seen."},"met:435993":{"d":"A young woman is shown with a harp, the instrument that gives the portrait its theme, and with a bracelet, lace, a ribbon, pearls and a book among the recorded details. The sitter was Marianne Dorothy Harland (1759–1785), who later married and became Mrs. William Dalrymple. The oil canvas, about 71 by 92 centimeters, is a different thing from the ivory miniatures on which Richard Cosway made his name. The harp, lace and pearls speak of accomplishment, fashion and wealth.","i":"The harp was a fashionable instrument for young women of rank in the later eighteenth century: graceful to play, it showed off the arms and hands and the sitter's musical education, a mark of an accomplished marriageable woman. With the book, bracelet and pearls, it signals taste, leisure and fortune. The title records her later married name, which suggests she sat while still unmarried; she died in her mid-twenties, which gives the picture a poignancy its first viewers could not have foreseen.","c":"Cosway's small scale is his strength, and on canvas he carries over the miniaturist's habits: delicate finish, gleaming lace and pearls, a lightly flattering elegance. What he rarely has is the weight, space and psychological breadth of the great London portraitists, Reynolds and Gainsborough, so an oil by him tends to read as a miniature enlarged. Charming and fashionable, not profound, and a reminder that his real gift lay on ivory."},"met:435996":{"d":"A miniature on ivory of a young officer, an ensign, the most junior commissioned rank in a British infantry regiment. The sitter's dates, 1774 to 1793, show that he died at about nineteen, so the portrait must have been painted when he was a teenager, probably in the early 1790s. The size is not recorded here, but a miniature of this type is small enough to be worn or carried. Cosway's characteristic touch with translucent color on ivory made such likenesses highly prized.","i":"In the eighteenth-century British army, commissions were bought, and gentry families placed younger sons in regiments at sixteen or so; the ensign carried the regiment's colors. A miniature made at that moment, often when a young man took up his commission or went on active service, was a keepsake for family and friends, frequently worn as a locket or bracelet. He died in 1793, the year Britain went to war with Revolutionary France, which gives such portraits a poignancy.","c":"Cosway's miniatures of the early 1790s show his fashionable manner at full strength: dry elegance, delicately tinted flesh and a flattery that suited gentlemen as well as ladies. The risk is sameness, with a young officer sliding toward the type of the handsome youth. Its real value is as a record of a short life, held as a personal memorial more than as a work of art."},"met:437643":{"d":"The Thames at London during the construction of Westminster Bridge: the bridge, in progress, spans the river, with boats on the water, buildings along the bank and a cathedral among the recorded subjects. The canvas is wide, about 61 by 113 centimeters, a format that suits a panoramic view. Samuel Scott, who began as a painter of ships and sea battles, became in the 1740s a leading painter of London views, and is sometimes called the English Canaletto.","i":"Westminster Bridge was a major public project, designed by the Swiss engineer Charles Labelye, built between 1739 and 1750 and eagerly followed by Londoners as a sign of the city's growth and engineering skill. A picture of the bridge in construction fixes a single moment in a long process, and appealed to citizens who watched the work from the riverbank and boats. Scott's Thames pictures answered a taste for topographical views of the capital's new landmarks.","c":"Scott's strength is atmosphere and honest observation of boats, water and light. Unlike Canaletto, who sharpens everything into a bright, clear theater, he prefers a softer, more humid air and a lower key, which suits the Thames. A construction site is a good subject for him, since it gives incident to the broad river. The result is less brilliant than Canaletto, but more convincing as London: a valuable record and an agreeable painting."},"met:437977":{"d":"A saint receives her last communion. The recorded subjects, a woman, a man, an angel and a saint, fit the story of Mary of Egypt, the penitent who lived alone in the desert for decades before the monk Zosimas brought her the sacrament. The picture is a small cabinet piece, painted in oil on copper, about 43 by 54 centimeters; copper gives a smooth, enamel-like surface that favors fine detail and glowing color. It is dated 1680, when Marcantonio Franceschini was in his early thirties.","i":"Mary of Egypt was a fifth-century Alexandrian courtesan who, after a conversion in Jerusalem, lived as a hermit in the desert beyond the Jordan for decades. The monk Zosimas found her near the end of her life and, at her request, returned a year later with the Eucharist; this is the moment shown. Her story was a favorite of Counter-Reformation devotion as a model of repentance and of the sacrament's power.","c":"Franceschini belongs to the Bolognese classical tradition, polished, orderly and graceful, and a small copper panel suits his refined finish and clear grouping. The emotional register is restrained: where a Spanish or Roman painter might emphasize the penitent's gaunt, near-naked ecstasy, a Bolognese hand is likelier to prefer decorum and balance. A well-made, devout cabinet picture that keeps its feeling in check."},"met:437922":{"d":"A small upright canvas, about 37 by 31 centimeters, shows a house with a dovecote, the pigeon house of the title, together with a boat and a man. Dovecotes were a common feature of Dutch rural and estate buildings, and pictures of them belong to the modest, domestic side of Dutch landscape: a corner of country life, observed without grandeur. Roelof van Vries, a Haarlem landscape painter of the generation after Salomon van Ruysdael, worked in the circle of Jacob van Ruisdael.","i":"Pigeons were kept on Dutch farms and country estates for meat, eggs and manure, and in many parts of Europe the right to keep them was tied to landholding, so a dovecote could signal ownership as well as rural usefulness. Landscapes with rustic buildings, water and a boat showed buyers a familiar, unthreatening countryside and encouraged unhurried looking at weathered walls, trees and reflections.","c":"Van Vries is a lesser follower of the great Haarlem landscapists, and a small picture like this shows both his debts and his limits: pleasant, tidy handling and a feel for rustic structure, but little of Ruisdael's tension or Hobbema's glitter. The subject, a humble outbuilding, asks for no grand effect, and in that modesty lies the picture's charm. A minor but sincere example of late Dutch rural painting."},"met:435823":{"d":"Adam and Eve are shown with the serpent, the three figures that every viewer would expect in a scene of the Fall. The canvas is wide, about 67 by 157 centimeters, and nothing is recorded of its original setting. Giuliano Bugiardini, a Florentine friend of Michelangelo, painted it around 1520, when the nude and its muscular drawing were a test of every ambitious painter in Florence.","i":"The Fall, in which Eve takes the forbidden fruit at the serpent's urging and Adam shares it, is the founding story of human sin and of the need for redemption. For Renaissance painters it was also a rare chance to paint the nude in a respectable sacred context, with the first man and woman serving as images of ideal bodies as well as of guilt. The serpent, sometimes given a woman's face in Italian art, is the usual signal of temptation.","c":"Bugiardini was valued by his contemporaries mostly as Michelangelo's friend and as a decent portraitist, and Vasari treated him with fondness more than admiration. Such a subject tests the drawing of the nude body, which was not his strength; he was better at pleasing surfaces than at sculptural force. Competent, a little cautious, the picture belongs to the Florentine second rank, in the shadow of the great innovators."},"nga:172":{"d":"The upper half of a young woman fills the canvas, her torso turned to the left and her head turned slightly back to look toward the right. Her brown hair is parted in the middle and mostly covered by a white headscarf edged with gold and fringed in gold. She has pale arched eyebrows, rosy cheeks, brown eyes and a small pink mouth in a neutral expression. Her red dress has a low square neckline and a black stripe, with long white sleeves and a white undershirt showing at the neckline. In her right hand she holds a small piece of paper, against a solid black background.","i":"Florentine portraits of young women in the 1520s were often made on the occasion of a betrothal or marriage, and showed the sitter as modest, well dressed and literate. The small paper she holds could be a letter or a verse, and may allude to her education or to a lover or husband; its text, if it ever had any, is not recorded. The red dress and white head covering are respectable rather than lavish, which suggests a family of means. Her identity is unknown.","c":"Bugiardini had a reputation for careful, conservative painting, and this portrait fits it: the face is smooth, the contours clear, and the pose and the black ground are plain to the point of austerity. It lacks the psychological bite of his younger Florentine contemporaries Pontormo and Bronzino, but gains from calm and a certain tenderness. Quietly attractive rather than arresting, it is a sound example of Florentine portraiture of the 1520s."},"met:435824":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child, flanked by Saint Mary Magdalene and Saint John the Baptist, with an angel among the figures. At about 194 by 166 centimeters it is an altarpiece-scale panel painted in oil on wood, an arrangement known as a sacra conversazione, in which the saints share one space with the Virgin rather than being separated into compartments. Giuliano Bugiardini painted it around 1523, in the generation after the great innovations of Raphael and Michelangelo.","i":"In a sacra conversazione the saints are chosen for a reason: John the Baptist, patron saint of Florence and forerunner of Christ, and Mary Magdalene, the penitent who anointed his feet and was the first to see him risen, each stand for a stage in the soul's relation to Christ. The Baptist usually holds a slender reed cross, the Magdalene an ointment jar. On an altar this brought the Virgin's intercession and the examples of repentance and prophecy before the worshipper at prayer.","c":"Bugiardini's altarpieces show him working in the mainstream Florentine manner of the 1520s, shaped by Fra Bartolommeo and Raphael: balanced composition, sweet faces, soft color. His figures are clear and amiable but rarely powerful, and he leans on borrowed types from better inventors. At this scale the picture offers the satisfactions of a well-ordered sacra conversazione and the limits of a painter content to follow rather than lead."},"nga:1175":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, his body in profile facing left and his head turned toward the viewer. He has pale, pink-toned skin, a full reddish-brown beard, short brown hair, arched eyebrows, a long nose and thin pink lips set in a neutral expression. He wears a black coat, with a white garment and a red collar showing at the neck, and a black beret. The background is a muted gray. The panel, about 63 by 47 centimeters, was painted about 1528, in oil.","i":"Leonardo belonged to the Ginori, an old Florentine family of the merchant-patrician class, whose name is now better known for the porcelain factory a later member of the family founded in the eighteenth century. The plain black clothing and beret are typical of the sober fashion of Florentine men around 1530, when the republic and the Medici were contending for power; a sitter dressed in black presented himself as serious and unostentatious.","c":"A restrained, careful portrait. The head is firmly modeled, the beard and eyebrows are carefully noted, and the muted gray ground leaves the face and the small accent of the red collar to carry the interest. Bugiardini does not probe character as deeply as his Florentine contemporaries Pontormo and Bronzino, and the effect is somewhat remote, but the clarity and sobriety have their own appeal, matching the self-presentation of a sixteenth-century gentleman."},"cma:97420":{"d":"A portrait of Mary Fairlie Cooper, painted in oil on a canvas of about 74 by 62 centimeters, a size suited to a bust or half-length likeness; the date is given only as probably the 1810s. William Dunlap, who trained in London under Benjamin West and worked mainly in New York, painted portraits for a clientele of merchants, officials and their families, and was equally busy as a playwright and theater manager.","i":"Portraits of the early American Republic recorded respectable families in a plain, truthful style with less court display than in Britain, and were usually commissioned by the sitter's family to hang at home. Dunlap's own career shows how artists had to mix trades: portraiture alone could not always support a painter, so he also wrote plays, managed a theater and, late in life, wrote the first major history of American art.","c":"Dunlap is a more important writer and historian of American art than a painter. His portraits are usually competent, honest and a little dry, with the stiffness of a provincial education after West; they lack the vivacity of Gilbert Stuart or the polish of Thomas Sully. A likeness like this should be valued for its sitter and its period more than for any brilliance in the paint."},"met:17804":{"d":"A miniature self-portrait in watercolor on ivory, only about 8 by 5 centimeters, slightly smaller than a playing card. If the date of about 1825 is right, the sitter was about fifty-nine, since William Dunlap was born in 1766; no pose or costume is described here. Miniatures of this kind were intimate objects made to be held, kept in a case or exchanged with family and friends.","i":"A self-portrait on ivory by a man who was also a historian of the arts has a particular interest: Dunlap wrote the first major history of American art, History of the Rise and Progress of the Arts of Design in the United States (1834), only a few years after this miniature. A self-portrait also served a practical purpose, as a sample of a painter's skill for anyone considering a commission.","c":"Miniature painting asks for precision more than invention, and a self-portrait on ivory by a sober, practical painter is likely to be plain and direct rather than flattering. The charm of such pictures lies in the artist looking at himself with a professional's honesty, and in the survival of a likeness of one of the first chroniclers of American art. A small, personal object rather than a showpiece."},"met:10783":{"d":"A portrait of John Adams Conant, painted in 1829 on a wooden panel, about 77 by 64 centimeters. It forms a pair with the portrait of his wife, Mrs. John Adams Conant, at the same museum, with matching size, medium and date. The sitter is recorded simply as a man; his pose and dress are not described here. Dunlap, by then in his sixties and better known as a dramatist and historian, still took portrait commissions.","i":"Companion portraits of a husband and wife were a standard form of American portraiture in the early nineteenth century. Hung side by side, usually with the sitters turned slightly toward each other, they declared the couple's union, respectability and prosperity, and they remained in the family as a record. A man's portrait of this era typically emphasized steadiness and credit rather than romance.","c":"Dunlap's portraits of the 1820s are sober and competent rather than lively: serviceable likenesses of prosperous ordinary citizens, handled plainly. They lack the vitality of the best American portraitists of his time, yet their very plainness gives them an honesty that a flashier painter might have lost. The pair is best valued as a document of middle-class self-presentation."},"met:10784":{"d":"A portrait of Mrs. John Adams Conant, painted in 1829 on a wooden panel, about 77 by 64 centimeters, as a companion to the portrait of her husband in the same museum. The recorded details of her dress are a lace collar, a necklace and an earring: the small, careful marks of a respectable woman's formal costume. The artist, William Dunlap, was a New York painter, playwright and historian of the arts.","i":"Women in American portraits of the 1820s and 1830s were often shown in dark, modest dress relieved by lace and a few jewels, which advertised refinement and a well-run household without ostentation. Her lace collar, necklace and earring are signs of that respectability. As a wife's portrait paired with her husband's, the picture records her place in the couple's shared standing, which was the point of such commissions.","c":"In a pair, the wife's portrait gives a painter a chance to show finer detail, and the lace, necklace and earring here are likely the passages that reward looking. Dunlap's likenesses are honest and plain, rarely flattering; the result is a believable woman rather than an idealized one. Modest in ambition, solid in execution, it is valuable for the clear picture it gives of fashion and character in the 1820s."},"nga:126161":{"d":"A person in a white shirt, gray trousers and brown hat sits on a rocky outcrop, facing away, and looks out over a vast landscape. The horizon sits in the upper third of this small canvas, and the sky shades from pale blue at the top through light yellow to pale pink above rolling hills and valleys in cream, light blue and pale green. Large rounded rocks in pale yellow, pink and reddish-brown fill the foreground and rise into a hill at the right. The picture measures about 27 by 40 centimeters.","i":"Mount Desert Island on the coast of Maine, with its granite summits and sea views, was a favorite destination of Hudson River painters from the 1850s onward, and helped fix a national taste for wild scenery as spiritual refreshment. The seated figure, small against the expanse, is a convention of Romantic landscape: it invites the viewer to take the same position and look. Painted during the Civil War, a quiet, empty view may also have offered a note of calm.","c":"A modest, almost private picture: McEntee reduces the grand panorama of his teacher Frederic Church to a quiet, low-keyed arrangement of rocks, distance and sky, with a figure for scale. The delicate gradation of the sky, from pale blue through yellow to pink, is the best thing in it; the whole may be more sketch than finished statement. It shows his poetic restraint, a mood rather than a spectacle."},"cma:173640":{"d":"An autumn landscape of the Catskill Mountains in New York State, in an upright canvas of about 61 by 51 centimeters. McEntee lived at Rondout, on the Hudson, close to the Catskills, and he specialized in late-season scenes: the mood of November rather than the showy scarlets of October. The title gives only the season and the place; as is typical of the artist, such a picture is likely to be a quiet, low-keyed view in which atmosphere counts for more than incident.","i":"Autumn was a loaded season in Hudson River School painting: the dying year, with its colors and fading light, spoke of transience as well as of nature's beauty. Catskill scenery had been the home of American landscape painting since Thomas Cole in the 1820s, and by the 1870s it also served a growing tourist market and a wistful nostalgia for a wilder, quieter America. McEntee's late-autumn pictures are often read as expressions of personal mood.","c":"McEntee is the poet of the quiet side of the Hudson River School. His best late-autumn pictures hold a mood, subdued and slightly melancholy, that more adventurous painters such as Church or Bierstadt do not attempt. The risk is monotony: a restrained palette and a low key can seem timid next to the flamboyant autumns of his contemporaries. This one is probably best taken as a representative, contemplative picture rather than a showpiece."},"met:11543":{"d":"A wide landscape in which trees are among the recorded subjects, titled for a time of the week rather than a place: Saturday Afternoon. The canvas is large, about 61 by 114 centimeters, in a horizontal format that favors a long, open view. Jervis McEntee, a Hudson River School painter who lived at Rondout, New York, and kept a studio in New York City, specialized in subdued, reflective landscapes; the title announces a mood of leisure and calm more than a topographical subject.","i":"Titling a landscape for a time rather than a place invites the viewer to supply the feeling: the stillness of a weekend afternoon, the pause in a working week. A picture of this kind addressed an urban audience that sought in landscape a quiet counterweight to commercial life. McEntee's reflective moods were admired by fellow painters, among them his close friend Sanford Gifford, with whom he shared a taste for atmospheric restraint.","c":"This is McEntee in his chosen register: calm, spare and tonal, with nothing to startle the eye and the air and light doing the work. The wide format lets him stretch the quiet over a long horizontal, which is a risk as well as a gift, since a low-key picture needs unusual subtlety to hold such a width. His admirers prize this mood; others find him too reticent beside Church's spectacle."},"met:436816":{"d":"A woman and a girl make bobbin lace in a domestic interior, in an oil on canvas of about 72 by 97 centimetres. The Met's keywords record a lace pillow, bobbins, sewing, a basket and a cat, so the scene is a homely room dominated by women's work. The picture is a genre scene of the kind that Dutch and Flemish artists painted in the seventeenth century and that Nordic painters brought to Rome.","i":"Lace-making was a skilled home industry, and pictures of lacemakers praised patience and industry as feminine virtues; the cat adds a note of domestic comfort. Bernhard Keil, a Dane who is said to have trained in Amsterdam with Rembrandt before settling in Rome around 1651, painted scenes of ordinary people for Roman collectors, in a Northern spirit of close observation. The picture belongs to the wider tradition of Dutch lacemakers, of which Vermeer's is the most famous.","c":"A warm, observant picture whose strength is quiet attention: the concentration of the workers, the textures of lace and cloth, and the homely cat are set down without fuss. Keil's Northern training shows in the careful reading of light and surface. Compared with Vermeer's Lacemaker it has less stillness and none of its mystery, and the composition is more crowded. But it treats a humble subject with honest affection, which is all it needs."},"met:10049":{"d":"A very wide canvas, about 138 by 323 centimetres, stages the opening scene of Shakespeare's King Lear, in which the old king, before his court, divides his kingdom among his daughters according to how much they say they love him. The museum lists a king, men, women and a dog among the figures, so the court is crowded and includes an animal, but gives no further description, so the arrangement, colours and costumes cannot be reported with confidence. The size and horizontal format recall a stage tableau.","i":"In Act I, scene i, Goneril and Regan outdo one another in declarations of love and are given shares of the kingdom, but Cordelia, the youngest and best loved, will not flatter her father and says only that she loves him as a daughter should; Lear, enraged, disowns her, and the tragedy begins. Abbey, an American who lived in England, specialised in researched, richly costumed scenes from Shakespeare and history, and painted this in 1898. George A. Hearn gave it to the Met in 1913.","c":"A big, ambitious canvas, composed like a stage production with the king at the centre of a splendid court, and Abbey's research into costume and architecture gives it the look of a convincing past. It is theatrical: the figures pose like actors, and the emotion is arranged more than felt, so Cordelia's silence, the heart of the scene, may lack its force. Impressive as late-Victorian illustration, but cooler than its subject."},"nga:177906":{"d":"A young woman in a cream-white gown with ruby-red sleeves and a red-and-gold headpiece walks down a carpeted stair toward the viewer, lifting her skirt with both hands. Her entourage crowds behind her: two lutenists, one apparently singing; a courtier with an ostrich-feather fan, cropped by the frame; another in slashed crimson holding a small dog and a scroll; and a man in black who leans on the carved base of a fluted column, hat and book in hand. Several of the men look at her. Her pale dress is the brightest note in a square canvas of reds and shadow.","i":"The title is the first line of a song in Shakespeare's The Two Gentlemen of Verona, in which musicians serenade the lady Silvia. Abbey, an American settled in England and widely known as an illustrator of Shakespeare, turns the serenade into a procession, with singers, attendants and an admirer in her train. The costumes evoke the Renaissance of the play's Italian setting, and the picture belongs to the late Victorian taste for Shakespeare as pageant, staged with an eye for period detail.","c":"Abbey stages the scene as a good director would: one bright dress, a ring of darker costumes, a column to hold the right edge. The reds are handled with skill and carry the eye across the square. But the figures read as costumed actors posed around a star, and Silvia's calm face gives the viewer little feeling to share. It is accomplished illustration enlarged to gallery scale, more admirable for its management than for its poetry."},"met:436334":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child are the subject of this upright devotional panel, about 61 by 46 cm, painted in oil on wood. A picture of this size was made to hang in a house or private chapel, where its owner could pray before it, rather than to stand on a church altar. Francesco Francia trained as a goldsmith and ran a large workshop in Bologna, and his Madonnas are known for a polished finish, calm faces and a clear, balanced arrangement of figures.","i":"The Virgin with her Child is the commonest image in Italian devotional painting. Mary presents her son to the viewer as Savior while showing a mother's tenderness, and the worshipper was invited to pray to her as intercessor with Christ. In many such panels a small detail, such as a fruit or a bird in the Child's hand, anticipates his future Passion, so that the tenderness of the image carries a hint of sacrifice.","c":"Francia's reputation rests on sweetness and polish, and a Madonna of this size shows the appeal: gentle types, a clear arrangement, a smooth surface, and the calm learned from Perugino and the Ferrarese painters of Bologna. The limit is sameness. His Madonnas draw on a small stock of faces and gestures, so the result is serene rather than searching. Among Bolognese devotional pictures of the 1490s it is attractive and solid, but it asks little of the viewer."},"met:436332":{"d":"A single saint, Roch, is the subject of this very large panel, about 216 by 151 cm, painted in tempera in 1502. The scale is that of an altarpiece, and the figure of God the Father also appears in the composition. Pictures of this kind were made to be seen across a church, and a saint shown alone was meant to be addressed directly, as a protector. Francia trained as a goldsmith and ran a busy workshop in Bologna, where altarpieces were his main business.","i":"Roch, a pilgrim from Montpellier, is said to have tended plague victims in Italy, caught the disease himself, and survived in the wilderness fed by a dog. As plague kept returning, his cult grew quickly in the later fifteenth century, and churches and confraternities commissioned his image as a protector against infection. He is usually shown in pilgrim's dress, pointing to the plague sore on his thigh. God the Father's presence probably underlines that the saint's intercession is heard at the highest level.","c":"At this scale a single figure has to carry the whole panel, and Francia's means are those he used throughout his career: clean outline, smooth modeling, an even, enamel-like surface and an expression of calm piety. They suit a protective saint who is meant to reassure. They are less suited to drama or movement, and the panel is best admired as a dignified, stable public image rather than an inventive one."},"nga:41679":{"d":"The upper body of a man with a fair complexion, short dark hair, a long nose, thin eyebrows and deep-set brown eyes faces the viewer with a neutral expression. He wears a black cap, a deep red cloak and a white inner garment with a high black collar, and his raised right hand, with two small rings, rests on his chest. Behind him a landscape of green fields, a winding blue river and pale blue hills opens under a cloudy sky. A line of gold text on a black border runs along the bottom.","i":"The portrait shows Altobello Averoldi, a churchman of Brescia who became bishop of Pola and served the papacy as a diplomat; he later commissioned Titian's polyptych of the Resurrection for Brescia. The red cloak and the rings mark ecclesiastical rank, the hand at the chest is a gesture of sincerity and devotion, and the gold lettering at the base presumably identifies him. The landscape follows the Umbrian fashion for placing a sitter before an open view.","c":"Francia is at his best here in plain statement: the face is drawn with a clarity that lets the sitter's reserve stand, and the bright, ordered landscape adds air without competing. The expression is neutral to a fault, and the portrait is more a record of an office than a study of a mind. Compared with Francia's altarpieces it is less formulaic; among his few portraits it is dignified and quietly assured."},"met:436333":{"d":"A boy of about ten, Federico Gonzaga, is shown in embroidered dress in a small tempera portrait about 48 by 36 cm. The paint has a complicated history: it was transferred from its original wood panel to canvas and then back to wood, a conservation procedure that can change the surface of a picture. Portraits of this size are easily carried, and a likeness of a young prince could be sent to relatives or allies.","i":"Federico (1500–1540) was the eldest son of Francesco II Gonzaga, marquis of Mantua, and of Isabella d'Este, the most celebrated woman patron of her day. He succeeded his father as marquis in 1519, became the first duke of Mantua in 1530, and later employed Giulio Romano to build the Palazzo del Te. A portrait of an heir showed the dynasty's future, and the rich embroidery announces the rank of a child who would rule.","c":"Francia's portraits are few beside his altarpieces, and a child sitter sets a particular test: to make a ten-year-old heir look like a future ruler without losing the child. The embroidered costume gives scope for the fine linear detail he handled well as a goldsmith. The transfers of the paint layer may have cost some subtlety of surface, so the picture is best judged for the clarity of its design rather than the delicacy of its modeling."},"met:436335":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child are joined by two saints, Francis of Assisi and Jerome, in a devotional panel about 75 by 57 cm, painted in tempera on wood. A grouping of the Virgin and Child with saints sharing one space is called a sacra conversazione, a 'holy conversation'. The size suggests a picture for a private chapel or a domestic altar. Francia's Bolognese workshop favored calm, orderly arrangements of this kind in the first decade of the sixteenth century.","i":"Francis, founder of the Franciscan order, and Jerome, the scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, were among the most popular saints of the Italian Renaissance. Francis is usually shown in a brown habit bearing the wounds of the stigmata, Jerome as a penitent in the desert or as a cardinal-scholar. The choice of saints probably reflects the devotion of whoever ordered the picture, perhaps a Franciscan community or a patron named after one of them; the exact reasons can only be guessed.","c":"A well-made example of a formula Francia repeated for decades: a polished surface, gentle faces and an arrangement in which no figure competes with another. Pairing the founder of the Franciscans with the scholar-penitent Jerome allows some contrast of saintly types, and that is the picture's chief variety. It stays inside the safe, Perugino-derived manner and offers pleasure of finish and order more than any psychological depth."},"nga:41630":{"d":"In a courtyard with peach-colored walls, a line of poor people waits before a young, clean-shaven saint in a gold-trimmed burgundy cloak, who holds a white cloth purse and hands coins to a boy with a shallow bowl. Beside the boy stand a woman in a patched brown dress with an infant and a stooped older woman; behind them are a ragged man holding something to his mouth and an old man with closed eyes led by a small dog on a rope. Through an arched doorway the saint appears again, descending a stair toward a door heaped with gold coins.","i":"The scene shows the decisive act in the life of Anthony the Great, the Egyptian founder of Christian monasticism: according to Athanasius's Life, the young Anthony, heir to an estate, heard the Gospel command to sell all that he had and give to the poor, and did so before leaving for the desert. The saint's two appearances, giving and fetching the money, are a usual narrative device. The child, the old, the blind and the ragged are the classic objects of charity a viewer was meant to imitate.","c":"The picture's charm lies in particulars: the patched dress, the fraying sleeve with its chemise poking through, the little dog leading the old man. These touches turn a stock scene of charity into something observed, and show a storyteller with an eye for the poor. The composition, with the saint at one edge and the line of recipients opposite, is clear but a little schematic. Rich in telling detail, and quietly ambitious in its space."},"nga:41631":{"d":"A young, clean-shaven man with a gold halo and bare feet bows toward two bearded men standing in an open doorway. He holds one hand over his heart and carries a long, gnarled walking stick; the older man in the doorway, white-bearded, holds a red book and raises two fingers in blessing, with a red-bearded companion behind him. Tall, narrow, mint-green buildings with arched openings and clay-red roofs nearly fill the upright panel, and dark green trees stand against a gold sky punched with a decorative border. The greenish skin tones probably come from the green underpaint used beneath tempera flesh.","i":"The panel belongs to a series on the life of Anthony the Great, the Egyptian monk (about 251 to 356) who became the model of the Christian hermit. A man leaving his community with a blessing, staff in hand and hand on heart, reads as obedience and humility joined to the call of the desert. The older man's raised fingers and book mark the benediction and the authority of the monastic house he leaves. Such series gave monastic and lay viewers a saint's biography in pictures.","c":"The charm of the panel is its architecture: tall, narrow, mint-green houses crowd the frame like a toy town against the gold, with the small figures in the doorway. The painter is more interested in pattern and clarity than in space or weight, and the faces are expressive in a restrained, enamel-like way. The story is told with economy and the colors are rich. An unworldly, winning picture of Sienese taste, uninterested in the new Florentine perspective."},"nga:41688":{"d":"Seventeen mourners attend Saint Anthony as he lies dying on a bed draped in crimson. He has a long white beard, a black hood and a muted pink-and-black robe; one hand rests at his side and the other on his chest, his eyes closed beneath a gold-and-red halo. Monks, some with ring-shaped tonsures and others hooded, hold candles, candlesticks, a cross on a high staff, books and walking sticks, while a man in a sky-blue robe reads from an open book at the foot of the bed. Arched openings lead to rooms and a courtyard with trees. The square panel is about 36 by 38 centimeters.","i":"Anthony's death closes a life that Christian readers took as a model: the Egyptian hermit lived, by tradition, to the age of 105, and his biography by Athanasius made him the archetype of the desert monk. The deathbed here follows the customs of the painter's own century, with a reading, candles and a processional cross, not the quiet desert death of the earliest accounts. Viewers were meant to see a good death: prayerful, attended and sure of salvation.","c":"As a deathbed it is orderly rather than anguished: the mourners stand around the bed in courteous attendance, the saint rests with composure, and the crimson bed is the one strong accent in a pale, striped room. The Master of the Osservanza, an anonymous Sienese painter whom some scholars equate with the young Sano di Pietro, cares for clarity, pattern and procession more than for grief. Gentle, decorative and devout, with a good sense of space opening through arches."},"nga:436":{"d":"Three scenes of Saint Anthony are strung along a single path that winds from the lower right corner around hills and trees into the distance, in a tall panel about 47 by 33 centimeters. In the foreground, before a dark cave, the white-bearded saint embraces a long-haired figure in cream robes, with two walking sticks on the ground at their feet. Higher on the ridge he meets a centaur holding a palm frond, and in the distance he walks on alone with his staff. A sliver of gold sky tops the panel, and pale red shapes beyond the trees may be a church.","i":"The panel illustrates the Life of Paul the First Hermit by Saint Jerome: Anthony, at about ninety, learned that a holier hermit lived deeper in the desert, set out to find him, and was guided by a centaur, until he found Paul in a cave. Painters showed the journey as one continuous path with the same figure repeated, a convention that let a small panel tell the whole story. Its size and date match the Saint Anthony Distributing His Wealth panel, and the two probably belong to one series.","c":"The Master of the Osservanza is the name given to an anonymous Sienese painter, whom some scholars equate with the young Sano di Pietro, and his best panels have a storyteller's charm: small figures, a vivid, stylized landscape, gold at the top. The winding path that links three episodes is ingenious and legible, and the patterned trees add decorative richness. It is closer to a lovely illuminated page than to the weighty new Florentine painting."},"met:435633":{"d":"A winter scene in the countryside near Amsterdam: men and women skate on the ice before the village of Sloten and its church. The canvas is large, about 92 by 131 centimeters, and its broad format leaves room for sky, ice and a village skyline as well as figures. Johannes Beerstraten, an Amsterdam painter, made winter views and townscapes a specialty, and the title names a real place, so the picture is a topographical view as much as a genre scene.","i":"Skating was a Dutch passion in the seventeenth century, a period of hard winters sometimes called the Little Ice Age, when frozen canals and fields became roads, playgrounds and meeting places. Painters took up the subject from Hendrick Avercamp onward, because a winter scene could show every rank of society together on the ice, at work and at play, with the church and houses anchoring the picture in a recognizable place. Such views were popular on the walls of Dutch homes.","c":"Beside Avercamp, whose skaters are full of wit and observation, Beerstraten is more orderly: a clean, bright clarity, a village placed with care, and a cold light that convinces. The weakness is a tidy sameness, with figures arranged more than observed. On a canvas of this size he is a reliable, pleasing craftsman, and the picture gains from its recognizable setting, though it never quite rises above the competent."},"met:11062":{"d":"A church stands at a street corner in lower Manhattan, with horses, a carriage and a man among the figures the museum records, in a small, upright oil on academy board of about 46 by 36 centimetres. The museum's keywords place the scene in New York City, and the title names the church as the North Dutch Church, at Fulton and William Streets. The record gives no further description, so the architecture, weather and colours cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"The North Dutch Church was built in 1769 by the Reformed Dutch congregation, a survivor of the old Dutch city of New Amsterdam in the heart of the growing commercial city, and it was demolished in the 1870s. Edward Lamson Henry specialised in nostalgic scenes of old streets, carriages and costumes, painted with antiquarian accuracy for a public proud of its history and worried about change. This one was bequeathed to the Met in 1914 from the collection of the financier Morris K. Jesup.","c":"A careful, affectionate view: Henry's gift was for accumulating period detail, from carriages and horses to the stone of an old church, and his pictures have the appeal of well-researched history. They also have its limits: the eye is more engaged by antiquarian correctness than by atmosphere, and the figures tend to be there to give scale and costume. As a record of a vanished corner of New York it is excellent; as painting it is polite."},"nga:166454":{"d":"A large red-brick mansion stands at the center, flanked by two connected brick structures, one of which seems to be in ruins. White tents and trees with sparse leaves surround it. In front, soldiers in blue uniforms ride on horseback or in carriages toward the house, and more uniformed men sit or stand on the lawn or look from the windows. Two people stand on the roof, one holding a white flag with a red dot at its center, and an American flag hangs over the doorway beneath a mostly gray sky. The paperboard support measures about 29 by 37 centimeters.","i":"Westover, a Georgian mansion on the James River in Virginia built in the 1730s for the Byrd family, was occupied by Union troops during the Civil War, and the picture shows an army encamped around the old house. Painted four years after the war ended, it can be read as a meeting of the old Virginia order and the new. The white flag with a red center on the roof may be a signal flag, and the ruined wing adds a note of lost grandeur.","c":"Henry, not yet the specialist in antiquarian genre scenes that he became, sketches the occasion with an observant eye and a historian's concern for detail: flags, tents, uniforms, carriages. The paperboard support suggests an oil sketch more than a finished picture. It is a lively record, less assured in composition than his later work, and its interest lies in how the army and the old house meet in a single view."},"met:652416":{"d":"Christ hangs on the cross, with the Virgin Mary and Saint John the Evangelist as mourners. A ladder, a man and a dog are among the recorded details, bringing everyday, even earthy, notes into the scene. The canvas is upright, about 124 by 103 centimeters, a size for a private chapel or oratory. Pedro Orrente, from Murcia in Spain, worked in Toledo and Valencia and was known, sometimes as the Spanish Bassano, for naturalistic pictures with rustic figures and animals, so a dog and a ladder are consistent with his manner.","i":"Spanish Crucifixion paintings of the early seventeenth century were made for churches, convents and private devotion, and were meant to inspire pity and penitence. Mary and John at the foot of the cross follow the Gospel of John, in which Christ entrusts his mother to the disciple. The ladder, a common attribute in scenes of the Crucifixion and Deposition, recalls the practical work of raising and lowering the body, a reminder that the sacred event was also a physical one.","c":"Orrente was a sturdy, practical painter, closer to the earthy realism of the Bassano workshop than to the austere intensity of Zurbarán or the refined tragedy of Velázquez's famous Crucifixion. Everyday objects make a scene more real, if less rarefied, and the risk of such realism is that incident dilutes the sacred drama. A solid, workmanlike devotional picture with some interesting naturalistic touches."},"met:436288":{"d":"A small upright panel, about 50 by 36 centimetres, showing the moment of parting from the Book of Genesis. Three figures are involved: the patriarch Abraham, the Egyptian servant Hagar and her young son Ishmael, whom Abraham is sending away. It is an intimate drama of leave-taking rather than a large display piece, painted in oil on wood in the manner of Rembrandt's circle, where light and shadow are used to focus feeling on faces and gestures.","i":"The story is told in Genesis 21. After Sarah bore Isaac, she demanded that Abraham send away Hagar and her son Ishmael; Abraham was distressed, but God told him to heed Sarah and promised that Ishmael too would become a nation. He gave Hagar bread and a skin of water and sent them into the wilderness. Dutch artists, Rembrandt among them, liked the subject for its pathos and its lesson of obedience to God's will. It is usually read as a test of Abraham's faith.","c":"Barent Fabritius, the younger brother of Carel, worked in Rembrandt's circle, and this panel shows what he took from it: sympathy for the human side of a Bible story and a taste for strong light and shade. Its interest lies in feeling rather than novelty, and it adds little to Rembrandt's own versions of the subject. Barent is a respectable follower, a notch below his brother at his best."},"met:435713":{"d":"A female nude, identified by the title as Medea, the sorceress of Greek myth, fills a tall canvas of about 156 by 112 centimeters. The word disillusioned suggests that she is shown after Jason has abandoned her, with revenge or despair to come; only the nude figure is recorded, so her attributes and setting are not described here. Paulus Bor, from Amersfoort, belonged to the Utrecht group of painters who had studied in Rome and brought Italian classicism and Caravaggio's lighting back to the Netherlands.","i":"Medea, the sorceress of Colchis, helped Jason win the Golden Fleece and left her homeland for him; when he abandoned her to marry a Greek princess, she took vengeance, killing her rival and, in Euripides's play, her own children. A disillusioned Medea is shown between betrayal and revenge, at the moment of knowledge. Seventeenth-century viewers knew the tale from Euripides, Seneca and Ovid and enjoyed it as a study of passion and moral catastrophe, which also licensed a learned nude.","c":"Bor's strength is a calm, sculptural nobility, with Roman training visible beneath the Utrecht Caravaggism, and a female nude on this scale lets him show it. The risk of the subject is melodrama; Bor tends to the opposite, a stillness that can be mistaken for emptiness. If the title is any guide, the picture's interest is psychological: a woman defined by what she has lost. A thoughtful, restrained work."},"met:436880":{"d":"In the organ loft of the Paris church of Saint-François-Xavier, an organist at the console and a singer rehearse, with the organ pipes rising behind them. The canvas is huge, about 237 by 363 centimeters, a Salon-scale picture of 1885 devoted not to saints or heroes but to the work of musicians. Henry Lerolle, a French painter with friends among both musicians and Impressionists, set the rehearsal in a real church, so that the picture is both a genre scene and a record of Parisian musical life.","i":"Church music was a major part of Parisian cultural life in the 1880s, and the organ lofts of churches such as Saint-François-Xavier were places where composers, organists and singers worked and met. A large painting of a rehearsal rather than a service treats music as a profession carried out by modern people in everyday clothes, which suited the era's interest in contemporary subjects. Lerolle moved in a circle of musicians and artists, and the picture speaks to that friendship of the arts.","c":"Lerolle works at Salon scale with real tact: an enormous surface but a quiet subject, a space of work rather than ceremony. The restraint is both strength and weakness, since the picture avoids melodrama and keeps a candid observation, yet lacks the pictorial invention of the Impressionists he collected. A refined academic naturalism, easily overlooked beside more famous contemporaries but intelligent and humane."},"met:436506":{"d":"The Virgin and the Christ Child appear on a gold ground in a long, wide panel, about 69 by 144 centimeters, with donors, the patrons who paid for the work and are included in its devotion. Lilies, the flower of the Virgin's purity, are among the recorded details. Giovanni da Milano, who came from Lombardy and worked in Florence, painted in tempera on wood around 1365, in the generation after Giotto, whose solid forms he adapted to a more delicate, sentimental manner.","i":"In the fourteenth century patrons were often painted into devotional panels, kneeling and on a smaller scale, so that their prayers were perpetually presented to the Virgin. Lilies point to her purity and to the Annunciation, and the Child in her arms, her natural intercessor, supports the donors' hope for salvation. Such images were made for chapels, convents or family altars, and the donors' inclusion records who paid and who prayed there.","c":"Giovanni da Milano blends Giotto's weight with a Lombard fondness for delicate gesture and fine surface, and the combination is at its best in the passages where mother and Child meet. A panel of this width, with donors, risks a formal, diagrammatic arrangement. Within that limit it is a graceful, finely finished work by one of the finest painters active in mid-fourteenth-century Florence."},"met:437342":{"d":"The dead Christ is mourned by the Virgin Mary, Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene in a very large, upright altarpiece, about 290 by 173 centimeters, painted in 1593. The recorded themes are death, sadness and suffering, all concentrated in the figures' grief; the Lamentation, the mourning over the body after it has been taken down from the cross, is one of the central images of Passion devotion. Scipione Pulzone, called Il Gaetano after his birthplace, Gaeta, was a leading painter in Counter-Reformation Rome.","i":"The Lamentation follows the Deposition and precedes the entombment, and it served Catholic reformers as a focus of compassion: viewers were meant to feel with the grieving Virgin and to meditate on the human cost of salvation. In Rome after the Council of Trent, such pictures were expected to be clear, decorous and moving, in a style that ordinary worshippers could read at once. John and the Magdalene are the usual companions of the Virgin at this moment.","c":"Pulzone's art is clear, controlled and devout: finely finished, with figures whose emotion is held in check by the Tridentine demand for decorum. This brings dignity and a fine hush, but it can also leave the drama cool; these pictures are meant for contemplation rather than shock. Beside Caravaggio, whose first public commission came in 1599, it looks conservative, but it shows the Roman devotional ideal that his arrival would soon challenge."},"met:439844":{"d":"A landscape with a rainbow, in which a man, a woman and animals are among the small figures that give the scene scale. The canvas is upright, about 109 by 96 centimeters. Joseph Anton Koch, a Tyrolean who settled in Rome, painted several Heroic Landscapes: idealized panoramas built from remembered Alpine scenery and the classical landscapes of Poussin and Claude, in which nature and a simple pastoral humanity share the stage. This version is dated 1824.","i":"The heroic landscape, a tradition from Poussin and Claude, presents nature as an ordered, noble stage; Koch gave it a Romantic, Alpine turn, with mountain forms from his Tyrolean homeland. The rainbow marks the end of a storm and, in Christian reading, the covenant after the Flood, so the picture can be read as a landscape of renewal and peace, with ordinary human beings and animals living in it. Painters in Rome after 1800, German speakers especially, looked to such pictures for a landscape art with moral and national seriousness.","c":"Koch aims at grandeur and sometimes gets it, with clear structure, a firm drawn line and a precise sense of rock and tree. The cost is a certain hardness: forms are sharply outlined, the light rather even, and the figures serve as scale-markers more than as life. For admirers of Romantic idealism this is an intelligent, ambitious picture; for others it can feel more constructed than felt."},"met:437923":{"d":"An autumn day near Waxholm, now usually spelled Vaxholm, in the Stockholm archipelago is the subject of this large canvas, about 104 by 164 centimeters, dated 1873. A village, houses and a windmill appear in the view. The wide format gives space to the Swedish landscape and its sky, and the title fixes both season and place: this is a particular day, not a generalized view.","i":"In the nineteenth century northern painters turned from Italianate fantasy to the plain landscape of their own countries, finding national character in its light and weather; for Scandinavians the long, low light of autumn was a distinctive subject. A windmill and a village are signs of settled rural life, the world of work that Barbizon painters had made fashionable. Wahlberg carried Swedish landscape to an international audience.","c":"Wahlberg handles a big canvas with calm assurance: the composition is clear, the light carefully observed and the village placed with tact. His influences are visible, Barbizon in the mood and academic training in the finish, and he is never as individual as the best of his French models. But the picture has an honest sense of place and weather, and the large scale gives it presence."},"nga:140908":{"d":"A wide, panoramic view looks down on a sandy beach that curves in a C-shape beneath tall, craggy outcroppings of rock. Gentle surf laps the sand, and a few white sails stand on slate-blue water that stretches to a hazy horizon about halfway up the canvas. In the foreground two women sit and stand on gray rocks, one in red with a narrow yellow parasol, the other in a lilac dress and yellow cap; a yellow-and-black insect settles on a fallen trunk. Washed-out ivory clouds fill the sky, and the signature reads A.T. Bricher 1873.","i":"Bricher painted the New England shore, and the Manchester of the title is probably Manchester, Massachusetts (now Manchester-by-the-Sea), a summer coast popular with city visitors. The two small women on the rocks are probably such visitors, set against stone that has stood for millennia. Beside the calm of the title, the timber and cloth near the surf may suggest wreckage, a reminder that this coast could be dangerous. The effect is of leisure, and also of nature's scale.","c":"The calm is the picture's achievement: the long, even light, the flat sheen of the water and the carefully graded sky are typical of the Luminist tradition with which Bricher is associated. The foreground, with its crisp insect and fussy rocks, is less successful than the airy distance, and the women are little more than accents. Even so, the breadth and quiet tonal control make it one of the more satisfying of Bricher's coastal views."},"met:10215":{"d":"A beach and sea on the Atlantic coast stretch across a wide canvas of about 64 by 132 centimeters, dated about 1895, with sky, clouds and a distant horizon. The format, more than twice as wide as tall, emphasizes the long line of the horizon and the open air above the water; hills and boats also appear among the elements. It is a quiet, spacious coastal scene, painted late in Bricher's career.","i":"Bricher is associated with Luminism, the American style of calm, light-filled landscapes and seascapes of the mid-nineteenth century in which brushwork is hidden and a still, glowing atmosphere dominates. By 1895 the style was old-fashioned, and Bricher continued painting the coasts of New England and the Atlantic for a loyal market. The coast, with its beaches and headlands, was a symbol of national beauty and a destination for summer vacations.","c":"Bricher is at his most characteristic in the horizontal marine, in which the long horizon and a few well-judged cloud forms make a still, spacious picture. The brushwork is restrained and the effect one of hush. What he lacks is the extra intensity of Heade or Lane, and his calm can verge on blandness. Still, on a canvas as wide as this the tranquil rhythm of surf and sky is persuasive."},"nga:217102":{"d":"A glass holding a loose bouquet sits within an oval, surrounded by eight insects. The glass has straight, slightly flaring sides and a ring of knobs around its lower part; in it are lily of the valley, violets, grape hyacinth, narcissus, a red poppy and a pink snake's head fritillary hanging upside down. Light from the left, focused by the glass, falls on the wooden ledge, where two drops of water bead and the artist signed CLARA P. Dragonflies, a housefly, a caterpillar, a hornet, a snail, a ladybug and a beetle are painted larger, with precise anatomical detail. The copper panel is tiny, about 17 by 14 centimeters.","i":"Flower pieces with insects were a prized novelty in the Southern Netherlands around 1610, and their interest was twofold: curiosity about nature and moral reading. The insects, snail and drops of water can recall the brevity of beauty and the cycle of life and decay, while the display of exact observation, down to anatomical details, made the picture a record of the natural world, to be examined at close range like a specimen in a collector's cabinet.","c":"A tiny panel of astonishing exactness. Peeters, still a young painter, shows a mastery of finish that the copper support rewards: crisp edges, the optical trick of a ray of light focused by the glass, water drops that read as wet. The composition, a flower-filled oval ringed by oversized insects, is more clever display than harmonious design, but the cleverness is part of the pleasure. A precocious jewel from a painter later better known for banquet pieces."},"met:827660":{"d":"A tall bouquet bursts from a dark green glass vessel on a stone ledge against a nearly black ground. Large pink roses sit in the middle with a white narcissus above them, and around them are a red anemone, striped tulips, a yellow daffodil, blue irises and columbine, a blue spike of grape hyacinth, pink carnations, cornflowers and marigolds. A small butterfly rests on the ledge beside the glass, and a fallen white blossom, a few pink petals and a sprig of forget-me-nots lie nearby. Each petal and leaf is sharply defined, with delicate highlights. The panel is small and upright.","i":"The Met points to Peeters's attention to naturalistic details such as dewdrops, insect bites and drooping tulips, which hint at transience and decay. She asserted her own achievement by inscribing her signature in the stone ledge next to a fallen sprig of forget-me-nots, and the Met calls her a founding figure of European still life, a genre that attracted many women artists. The crisp edges and close observation, the museum adds, show the link between flower painting and botanical illustration. The Met dates the picture about 1612. Such bouquets typically combined flowers that bloom in different seasons, so they were artful assemblies rather than views of real arrangements.","c":"Peeters brings an almost scientific eye to a showpiece. The glass is convincing, the rose petals have a velvety weight, and the black ground makes every colour stand out. The fallen blossom and butterfly keep the arrangement from looking stiff. If there is a limitation, it is the lack of overlap: each flower is seen clearly and separately, so the bouquet is more a collection of beautiful specimens than a unified mass, as later Dutch flower painters would achieve. But that clarity is the appeal of early flower painting, and Peeters does it with precision."},"met:438158":{"d":"In a dim forest glade, a young couple embrace on a rope swing. The young man, in a short rust-red tunic, leans in to kiss the girl as she sits on his lap, one arm around his neck. She wears a thin, white, gauzy dress that slips from one shoulder, and she looks out at the viewer with a quiet smile. Their bare feet hang down. He grips one rope while she holds the other. Behind them green and gold light filters through the trees, and irises and wild grasses grow at the lower left. The tall canvas is over two metres high, and the surfaces are smooth and finished, with soft transitions of flesh and cloth.","i":"Pierre-Auguste Cot (1837–1883), a pupil of Cabanel and Bouguereau, painted Springtime in 1873, and it is among his best-known works. The swing recalls eighteenth-century Rococo pictures such as Fragonard's, but the flirtation is made chaste: the lovers are young and nearly innocent, and the title turns them into an allegory of the season rather than characters in a particular story. Cot's popularity grew quickly in the 1870s, and he was made a Chevalier of the Legion of Honour in 1874. The Metropolitan Museum received the painting as a gift from Steven and Alexandra Cohen in 2012.","c":"Springtime is polished and sentimental, and it is easy to see why the Salon public loved it. The soft modelling of the lovers, the green forest light and the dangling feet are rendered with slick competence. But beside the Impressionist pictures painted in the same years it seems airless and staged: the forest is lit like a studio, the passion is polite, and the girl's glance at the viewer adds a coy note. It is of interest now less as a rival to Impressionism than as a record of what the public of the 1870s wanted."},"met:435997":{"d":"A young couple hurry barefoot along a path through dark undergrowth in a storm. The youth, bare-chested, with dark curly hair, holds a billowing golden-yellow cloth high over their heads with one arm and wraps the other around the girl. She wears a sheer white gown that clings and flutters, her fair curls blown by the wind, and she looks anxiously back while her raised hand grips the cloth. A fork of lightning shows at the upper right edge of the dark sky, and leaves and berries at lower left catch the light. The figures are life-size and strongly lit against the black background.","i":"When Cot showed this at the Salon of 1880, critics argued over its source. Some suggested Bernardin de Saint-Pierre's novel Paul and Virginia, in which the teenage lovers run for shelter using the heroine's overskirt as an umbrella, and others the ancient Greek romance Daphnis and Chloe by Longus. Cot, a pupil of Cabanel and Bouguereau, was a Salon favorite, and his Springtime had been a popular success in 1873. Catharine Lorillard Wolfe, guided by her cousin John Wolfe, commissioned The Storm, and it was widely reproduced. It came to the Metropolitan Museum with her bequest in 1887.","c":"A Salon crowd-pleaser, and honest about it. The wind-filled gold cloth is the best thing in the picture, a whole weather system made from one piece of silk, and the sheer dress is handled with real skill. But the pair are studio models posed to run: there is no rain, no weight, and the lightning is a stage effect. The sensuality is wrapped in a literary alibi, as Salon taste required. It says more about what 1880 audiences wanted than about storms or love, but it does so with polish."},"cma:102578":{"d":"A young woman in a blue satin gown sits sideways in a dark carved armchair, resting her cheek on one hand and looking calmly out of the picture. Grey feather trim edges her dress, and the long skirt flows to the floor in shimmering folds. Beside her, on a small dark table, a big blue-and-white jar holds yellow daffodils. Behind her hangs a golden embroidered silk, scattered with flowers and butterflies and, at the upper left, a round medallion with a blue creature. The floor is a warm reddish brown. The blue of the dress and the gold of the hanging dominate the picture, and the brushwork is broad and sparkling, particularly in the satin.","i":"Cleveland explains that Dora Wheeler became Chase's first student when he returned from study in Munich and opened a teaching studio in New York, at a time when few American artists accepted women as private pupils. She later joined her mother in a decorating firm, one of the first in the country run entirely by women, and designed textiles; the embroidered silk behind her refers to that work. The portrait won a gold medal at an international exhibition in Munich in 1883 and was shown in Paris later that year. The sitter later acquired it and gave it to the museum.","c":"This is Chase showing what Munich had taught him: bravura brushwork, a rich dark palette and a delight in surface. The blue satin and the gold hanging are set against each other with confidence, and the sitter's pose, chin in hand, gives her an air of thought that most society portraits avoid. The design is slightly overstuffed, with jar, flowers, chair and hanging all competing, and the face is smaller and plainer than the costume. Yet it shows an American painter treating a portrait as an arrangement of colour and texture, and doing so with flair."},"met:10469":{"d":"A full-length portrait of the painter James McNeill Whistler, on a tall, narrow canvas of about 188 by 92 centimeters. The format deliberately echoes the full-length portraits that Whistler himself had made famous, and the picture is dated 1885, when the sitter was in his early fifties. William Merritt Chase, a younger American painter and admirer, painted it in the dashing manner he admired in Velázquez and Hals, with broad, fluent brushwork and a strong sense of the sitter's presence.","i":"A portrait of one painter by another is also a statement of allegiance. Chase, then a leading younger figure in American painting, set the older expatriate Whistler in the tradition of Velázquez, whom both revered, and the tall, low-keyed format signals that debt. Whistler's famous wit, dandyism and quarrelsomeness made him a figure whose likeness mattered to American artists, who saw in him proof that a painter could be an artist of international rank.","c":"Chase's portrait is a showpiece, a tribute and a test: he borrows Whistler's means, the tall format, the low tone, the economy, and applies them with bravura. The likeness is vivid and the handling confident. What it lacks is Whistler's own subtlety of tone and mystery, because Chase is a painter of surface and speed rather than reticence. Better read as a record of mutual admiration between two generations than as a summit of either painter's art."},"met:10471":{"d":"A woman is shown with a boat in Prospect Park, the Brooklyn park, in a small panel about 35 by 50 centimeters. Chase painted a number of such park scenes in the mid-1880s, direct and quickly handled, in which fashionable people take their leisure. The sitter, Alice Gerson, is probably the young woman Chase married soon afterward, which would make this as much a personal picture as a portrait.","i":"Prospect Park, laid out by Olmsted and Vaux in the 1860s, was a place of leisure for the new middle class, and Chase's pictures of its lakes and paths helped create an American Impressionism of modern public pleasure. Painting a woman in the park linked fashion, nature and the new habit of the weekend outing; the small panel format, probably painted rapidly, suggests a spontaneity that suited the subject.","c":"These park panels are among Chase's freshest work: loose, breezy and intimate, with a lighter palette and more daring cropping than his studio portraits. A small portrait of a woman beside a boat avoids the fuss of Salon sentiment. It is slight but alive, and a useful corrective to the notion that American Impressionism arrived only with Hassam and Twachtman, since Chase was painting this way as early as 1886."},"met:10470":{"d":"A full-length portrait of a woman in black on a tall, narrow canvas, about 189 by 92 centimeters, the same format as Chase's portrait of Whistler painted three years earlier. The sitter has been identified as Marietta Cotton. The picture is dated 1888 and belongs to Chase's ambitious exhibition portraits of the 1880s, in which a single figure in a dark dress was set against a quiet ground to show off the painter's command of tone, flesh and fabric in the manner of Whistler, Velázquez and Manet.","i":"The black dress was the portraitist's test: it removes color and leaves the painter with tone, texture and silhouette, and it had been a favorite since Velázquez and Manet, and especially of Whistler, whose Arrangements in Black were well known. For the sitter, a portrait of this kind conferred elegance and seriousness and marked her as a woman of means and taste. The title Lady in Black, like Whistler's titles, treats the woman as a harmony as much as a person.","c":"Chase meets the challenge of a black dress with fluid, sure handling, and the figure holds the tall canvas with real authority. The debt to Whistler and Velázquez is clear, and perhaps too clear: the portrait is an accomplished performance in a borrowed idiom, with less mystery than its models. As a demonstration of Chase's brushwork and of what American painters took from Europe it is impressive; as a portrait that probes the sitter, less so."},"met:10465":{"d":"A full-length portrait of Carmencita, the Spanish dancer who was the sensation of New York around 1890, painted on a tall canvas of about 178 by 104 centimeters. She is shown as a performer, with a flower among the recorded details, and the picture is as much a portrait of a dancer's presence as of a face. William Merritt Chase painted her in the year of her fame, in friendly rivalry with John Singer Sargent, who painted her the same year.","i":"Carmencita drew crowds to music halls with Spanish dance, her fame carried by the press and by artists who flocked to paint her. Spain was a source of fascination for American painters, who admired Velázquez, and a Spanish dancer offered a famous, living model for that taste. Chase's portrait captures the celebrity of the moment: not a society sitter but a performer whose image answered the city's appetite for spectacle.","c":"Carmencita is Chase at his most theatrical and, in its way, most successful: a woman caught in the pose of a dancer, with a confident, dashing brush. Sargent's version, now in Paris, is the more enigmatic and arresting; Chase's is more direct and genial in its energy. A fine example of the bravura portrait of the 1890s, in which the pleasure lies in the paint and the performance."},"met:10473":{"d":"Pink azalea blossoms stand in a Chinese vase on an upright panel about 60 by 42 centimeters. The recorded subjects are the vase, the flowers and the still-life arrangement, and the date is given only as 1880 to 1890, with a question mark. William Merritt Chase, whose Tenth Street studio in New York was famous for its crowded collection of Asian ceramics, textiles and bric-a-brac, painted many such still lifes of objects he owned and loved.","i":"In the 1880s imported Chinese and Japanese porcelain and potted plants like azaleas were fashionable in American drawing rooms and artists' studios alike, signs of cosmopolitan taste in what is now called the Aesthetic movement. A painter who chose such arrangements showed that he too was a person of refined taste, and that the commonest decorative objects could be a subject for serious painting.","c":"A quick, assured still life of the sort Chase painted to keep his hand moving and to please the market: typically lush, with a loaded brush and an easy sense of arrangement. It has none of the minute finish of earlier floral painting; its pleasures are swiftness, color and surface. Slight, but a good example of how Chase made still life a vehicle for virtuoso brushwork."},"cma:134863":{"d":"A small panel, about 24 by 34 centimeters, shows a bay under a gray sky, as the title says; nothing further is recorded, so the subject is best described by type: an overcast coastal view in which water and sky are the main players and the weather of a single day, rather than any event, is the theme. William Merritt Chase painted small oil sketches on wooden panels in the 1880s, often outdoors and quickly, as studies in tone and atmosphere.","i":"Overcast weather was a favorite subject of painters influenced by the Barbizon school and by Whistler, for whom a narrow range of grays was a test of tonal judgment and a vehicle for mood. Chase's small marine panels were exercises in seeing, and they showed that a modest subject, handled with sensitivity, could carry a picture.","c":"Small gray-day sketches show a painter without the performer's flourish: direct, economical, tuned to a single mood. The risk of the gray day is dullness, and a sketch of this size can look underdeveloped; yet the best of such panels have a freshness that large studio pictures sometimes lose. A modest, honest piece of observation, closer to tonal landscape than to the glitter of Impressionism."},"met:10464":{"d":"A wide, low beach scene is topped by an enormous summer sky. White cumulus clouds drift across a deep blue, and a strip of darker blue sea runs along the horizon with a few distant sails. On the pale sand, a yellow parasol, a red one and a small white one are set among women and children in light summer dresses. A woman in a dark top and red skirt sits at the right with a small child in white beside her, another woman stands at the far right, and a white dog trots at the far left. The paint is loose and quick, with soft strokes in the clouds and sand. The signature is at the lower left.","i":"The Met notes that from 1891 to 1902 Chase directed the Shinnecock Hills Summer School of Art in Southampton, Long Island, the first outdoor painting school in the United States. He taught two days a week and spent the rest of his time painting and enjoying his family. The Met says the scene shows women and children relaxing on a beach, probably along Shinnecock Bay, and that their pale dresses and parasols suggest genteel leisure. Chase had trained in Munich, and these Long Island pictures show him taking up the lighter colour and open-air subjects of French Impressionism, filtered through his own broad brush.","c":"The sky is the real subject, and Chase paints it well: the clouds have bulk and soft edges, and the sea line is held low so the air seems to go on forever. The figures are shorthand, little accents of white, red and yellow that keep the eye moving along the sand. That quickness is the charm and the limit. Compared with the French Impressionists, Chase keeps light and shadow gentler, and he rarely risks a surprise. It is an easy, pleasant picture that does what it sets out to do and seldom more."},"cma:134862":{"d":"A small seascape, about 23 by 32 centimeters, painted on a wooden panel; the title is simply Marine, so only the genre is known: a view of the sea, with sky and water. Such small marines were not exhibition pieces. For Chase they were quick exercises, often painted in a single sitting, in which he tested color and weather against the water, the same alla prima habit that gave his portraits their dash.","i":"Marine painting was a growing taste in America in the 1880s and 1890s, helped by seaside vacations and by painters' summer colonies on the coast; a quick sea sketch could serve as a souvenir of a holiday or as a study for larger work. For Chase, who did not specialize in seascape, such panels were a relief from the studio and a way to take in the changing light that Impressionist painting had made central.","c":"Chase was never a marine specialist, and a small panel like this makes no claim to be one; its virtue is spontaneity. A few broad strokes of sea and sky can say more about light than a labored coastline, and the format forces economy. The result is slight but honest, a minor entry in a career dominated by figures and still life, and interesting mainly for what it shows of his habit of keeping the brush in motion."},"met:10466":{"d":"A portrait of the New York art dealer Edward Guthrie Kennedy, painted about 1895 in oil on a compact canvas of about 56 by 45 centimeters, a bust or half-length format. The sitter was a friend as well as a business associate of artists, and Chase portrayed him with the spontaneous, sketch-like brushwork he brought to portraits of friends. Only the sitter, a man, is recorded; his dress and pose are not described here.","i":"Dealers like Kennedy, who specialized in etchings and fine prints, were important patrons of the arts in late nineteenth-century America: they sold, promoted and shaped taste, and many became friends of the painters they represented. A portrait by a leading painter of a friend and associate was a mark of respect and an exchange of favors, a way of weaving the worlds of dealing and art into a single social fabric.","c":"Among Chase's portraits of friends, this is the sort that tends to succeed best: an easy, unposed likeness with quick, sure handling and no need to flatter. The risk is that quickness can harden into formula, but in a small bust the formula often works. A good example of the informal side of Chase's portraiture, less grand than his full-lengths but often more alive."},"met:10472":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Bessie Potter, is shown in a portrait on a canvas of about 81 by 65 centimeters, painted about 1895. The sitter may be the sculptor Bessie Potter, later Bessie Potter Vonnoh, whose small bronzes of modern women and children were admired in the 1890s. William Merritt Chase, who painted many portraits of friends and fellow artists in these years, handled a canvas of this scale fluidly and without fuss. Nothing is recorded of her dress, pose or setting.","i":"Artists often painted one another, and a portrait of a woman sculptor by a leading painter would have been a sign of professional respect at a time when women were fighting for recognition in the arts. If the sitter is indeed the sculptor, the picture is a reminder of the links between painters and sculptors in the New York of the 1890s, when studios, exhibitions and teaching intertwined the arts. A portrait of a friend allowed Chase to work freely, without the demands of a commission.","c":"Chase's best portraits of friends and fellow artists have a quick, open quality, as if the sitter had just turned to look; at this canvas size he has room for bravura but need not strain for monumentality. The risk is the generic: a chic, confident handling that could serve any sitter. The picture is strongest where it conveys the sitter's directness, and weaker where it relies on his habitual painterliness. A well-handled, agreeable portrait."},"met:10475":{"d":"A woman in the dress of the seventeenth century is shown on a tall canvas of about 93 by 60 centimeters; the recorded subjects are only the woman and her dress, so the costume carries the interest. Chase kept a studio full of costumes and props and loved to paint models in historic or exotic dress, an old studio tradition that let him show off the textures of silk, velvet and lace in the manner of the Dutch and Spanish masters he admired.","i":"Costume pictures answered a taste in the 1890s for the masters of the seventeenth century, Velázquez, Hals and Van Dyck, whom Chase studied and copied and whom he urged on his students. Dressing a model as a lady of that period put a modern sitter in dialogue with those masters, and was a studio exercise in painting fabric, light and attitude as they did. The title announces the period but names no sitter: the picture is about the manner more than the person.","c":"Chase's skill with dress is the heart of such pictures: broad, juicy strokes for silk and lace, with the face handled more lightly. The risk is that the costume becomes the subject and the sitter a mannequin. Because the picture announces its debt to the old masters, the comparison is unforgiving; Chase lacks Velázquez's austere gravity but offers a lively, modern sparkle. An attractive studio performance rather than a deep portrait."},"nga:157922":{"d":"A golden, grassy field slopes toward a line of low green trees and a strip of vivid blue water on the horizon. People in white are scattered across it: at the right a child in a white bonnet and gown sits beside a pale green parasol, with a red bucket in front and a small orange cart behind; farther off at the left a woman in a long white dress and tan hat kneels in the grass, and another figure in white and a red hat stands near the trees. Loose brushstrokes keep everything fluid under a blue, cloud-flecked sky.","i":"Chase taught summer classes at Shinnecock Hills on Long Island from 1891, and the dunes, pale grass and bay of that landscape became one of his subjects, with family and students in white as figures. This picture, probably painted there, belongs to those sunlit leisure scenes. The title names an activity as simple as picking flowers, a pretext for painting light on grass.","c":"Chase's outdoor pictures can be more convincing than his studio interiors, and this one has real air: the bright field, the dazzling water and the white dresses catching sun. The figures are summary, which is the point, and the composition is deliberately casual. What it lacks is a strong focus, and the scene feels like a pleasant fragment. A relaxed, sunlit sketch of leisure, typical of American Impressionism at its sweetest."},"nga:3205":{"d":"Two women in fashionable dress sit facing each other on a long sofa that spans the width of this horizontal canvas, in a room warmly lit from the right. The woman on the left wears a white dress, long fawn gloves and a veiled hat, and leans toward her friend with a closed pink parasol in her hand. The other, in a yellow dress with white stripes and a black collar, turns in profile to listen. Cushions of many colors lie along the sofa; behind them hang framed pictures and a large gold-framed mirror reflecting a sunlit room. The painting is loosely brushed.","i":"A call was a short social visit paid in the afternoon and governed by etiquette: hats, gloves and parasols stayed on, and the visit was brief. Chase, who became one of the most celebrated teachers and painters in America, made fashionable women in interiors a frequent subject in the 1880s and 1890s, and the crowded, many-pictured room recalls the lavish studios he made famous. The mirror that opens the room behind the viewer echoes Velázquez, whom Chase revered.","c":"The surface is a pleasure: slashes of cushion color, the white dress against the yellow one, and the bright, dissolved background show Chase's gift for casual brilliance. The women themselves are less alive than their furnishings, and the picture is more about an atmosphere of leisure than about any exchange between them. Handsome and fluent, but thin in feeling beside the work of the Impressionists he admired."},"met:10468":{"d":"A woman sits sewing in a domestic interior, with a window, a couch, a carpet and a length of cloth among the recorded details. The title suggests she is making a garment for a child. The canvas is nearly square, about 102 by 90 centimeters, and the subject is a quiet one, a private household moment treated on the scale usually reserved for portraits. Chase's wife, Alice, was one of his frequent models and may be the sitter.","i":"Pictures of women at needlework had a long tradition in European art, from Dutch interiors to Victorian genre, as images of domestic virtue and quiet industry. Chase gives the theme a modern, informal turn: the sitter is absorbed in her task, and the interior, with its window, couch and carpet, is a place to be seen in light rather than a stage for moral lessons. The title adds a note of motherly tenderness without sentiment.","c":"Chase painted domestic scenes with a lighter, more open touch than his formal portraits, and this subject plays to his gift for light falling on fabric and flesh. The risk is a drift into the sweetness of a magazine illustration; he tends to avoid it by keeping the brushwork fresh and the subject unposed. Handsome and good-humored, it shows the intimate side of an artist better known for performance."},"cma:117715":{"d":"A panoramic view of the low-lying dunes and beaches of Shinnecock, on the eastern end of Long Island, glowing with the light of a summer day, with an old road leading toward the sea. The canvas is large, about 102 by 127 centimeters, and the Cleveland Museum counts it among the finest works Chase produced there, with varied brushwork and warm colors. Chase ran a summer school at Shinnecock from 1891 to 1902, and he and his students were among the first American artists to pursue open-air painting.","i":"Shinnecock Hills, a stretch of open dune country far from the New York art world, became a laboratory for American Impressionism: the Chase school brought pupils out of the studio to paint directly from the landscape in changing light. Chase had been a studio painter, known for portraits and still lifes, and the Shinnecock pictures marked his turn toward plein-air color and broken brushwork. Their subject, humble dunes and an old road, was deliberately unpicturesque in the old sense.","c":"This is Chase at his most open and relaxed: varied brushwork, warm color, a broad horizon. The strength is the confident handling of light across low, apparently empty ground, which is harder to paint than a dramatic view. He is less systematic than the French Impressionists and less daring than Monet, preferring a comfortable, painterly realism warmed by color. The result is fresh and immediately likable, and one of his best Shinnecock pictures."},"cma:101461":{"d":"A portrait of Chase's daughter Alice, born in 1887 and nicknamed Cosy, on a tall canvas of about 183 by 121 centimeters, large enough for a full-length figure, painted about 1895 when she was a child of around eight. Chase painted his large family often, at home and in the studio, and such pictures give an intimate account of his household alongside the society portraits that made his name.","i":"A father painting his own child is a different transaction from a commission: there is no client to flatter, and the painter can look and paint as he pleases. Chase's pictures of his children, painted over many years, were among his most appealing and were shown as showpieces of his skill. Here the large, formal scale treats a child with the seriousness usually reserved for adult sitters, a deliberate compliment.","c":"A father's portrait of his daughter is freed from the demands of a commission, and Chase uses the freedom. The format is large and grave for a child, and the bravura handling that marks his society portraits is probably tempered by affection. The risk of such pictures is sweetness; the strength is the evident ease between painter and sitter, which no commissioned portrait can fake."},"met:10435":{"d":"A wide view of the Roman Campagna, the countryside around Rome long known for its ruins, with goats among the recorded details. The canvas is large, about 92 by 152 centimeters, and dated 1864. John Gadsby Chapman, a Virginian who painted the Baptism of Pocahontas in the Capitol Rotunda, settled in Rome in 1848 and supplied American visitors with the views of countryside, herds and herdsmen that they associated with the Grand Tour.","i":"The Campagna was the melancholy, picturesque heart of Roman scenery for two centuries: a vast pastoral plain of ruined aqueducts and tombs, shepherds and herds, thinly peopled because of malaria. For Claude Lorrain and Poussin it was an ideal; for mid-nineteenth-century visitors it was an image of vanished empire, with goats and shepherds as reminders of rural life persisting among the ruins. American painters in Rome like Chapman sold such views to compatriots as souvenirs and as emblems of cultural attainment.","c":"Chapman is better known for history painting and book illustration than as a landscapist, and a Campagna of this size shows a competent professional's command of the genre: broad space, soft atmosphere, a few well-placed accents such as the goats. It does not rival the poetry of Corot, but it satisfies the demand for a clear, calm, memorable view. A skillful, somewhat conventional souvenir on a grand scale."},"cma:173542":{"d":"A narrow panoramic view of harvest on the Roman Campagna, about 36 by 91 centimeters, dated 1868. The title records the rural labor of the season, and the long horizontal format suits a sweeping plain with figures at work and a wide sky above it. John Gadsby Chapman, an American who settled in Rome in 1848, painted many such views of the countryside around the city for visitors and for patrons at home.","i":"Italian rural labor was a favorite subject of foreign painters in Rome, who saw in the harvest a timeless, picturesque life untouched by industrial change. Americans in the years after the Civil War may have found in such pastoral scenes a vision of peace and continuity. A narrow, wide format like this was often made for a particular place in a house, above a mantel or a door, or for a traveler's portfolio.","c":"Chapman's gift is the broad, clear spread of light across the plain, and the long format exaggerates it. A narrow panorama can be monotonous, and a harvest subject risks genre prettiness, but a skilled professional can turn a long strip into a quiet pleasure. In his career, such pictures show the later Roman Chapman, a competent supplier of views to visitors rather than the ambitious historical painter of the Capitol."},"met:437660":{"d":"A man on horseback is the subject of this wide canvas, about 94 by 131 centimeters, dated 1748. The title calls it a portrait, so the horseman and his mount are the occasion for the picture, in the English tradition of equestrian portraits that showed a gentleman with his horse. James Seymour was one of the first English specialists in sporting pictures, painting racehorses, hunts and the men who owned and rode them.","i":"In eighteenth-century England the horse was a mark of wealth and sport, and owners commissioned portraits of their horses, and of themselves riding them, as records of ownership and skill. The tradition descended from the great equestrian portraits of Van Dyck, but Seymour's gentlemen are squires and sportsmen rather than courtiers, set in the open English countryside. The sitter is unnamed in the title, but a picture like this probably celebrated a particular horseman and his horse.","c":"Seymour paints horses with knowledge, and a horse portrait lives on that: anatomy, movement and the character of a particular animal. The human figures in his work tend to be stiffer and less secure than the animals, so the portrait of the man is only as good as the horse beneath him. As a specimen of the first generation of English sporting art it is historically valuable, if less accomplished than the horse paintings of George Stubbs a generation later."},"met:12917":{"d":"A family group, a man, a woman and a child, with a toy among the recorded details, painted on a modest canvas of about 69 by 56 centimeters. Such small pictures of a household were within reach of the prosperous middle class of the 1840s, who wanted a record of their family in one frame. John Mix Stanley, who later became known for his paintings of Native American life and the American West, was at this date working as a portrait painter.","i":"Family portraits of the 1840s turned the household into an image of domestic harmony: parents and children shown together, often with a child's toy or pet standing for innocence and for the future. The Williamsons chose to be painted as a unit rather than as separate sitters, which stresses the family as the basic social institution of the day. The toy signals the child's age and the parents' affection.","c":"As a family portrait this is competent and direct, and likely as unglamorous as much American portraiture of the period: carefully drawn faces, a certain stiffness in the poses, little atmosphere. Stanley's real talent emerged later, in the open-air subjects of the West; here he is a serviceable maker of likenesses. The charm lies in its candor and the small human detail of the toy rather than in artistic invention."},"nga:166497":{"d":"Three men share a rustic cabin interior. One sits on the ground facing away, in a gray hat, brown vest and vivid blue blanket, looking up at a bearded man who stands at the center with a rifle grounded, in a light gray coat with a bright red shirt at the neck and red socks; a white-and-brown dog sits at his feet. On the right a kneeling man in a red fur-bordered hat gestures toward a large-antlered deer lying on the floor. Saddles and tools hang on dark gray, brown and ochre walls, and metal implements and small birds lie scattered about.","i":"Trapper and hunter scenes fed an eastern appetite in the 1850s for the American frontier as a world of self-reliant men, adventure and plentiful game. Painters like Stanley, who had traveled in the West, offered authentic detail, the rifles, saddles and kit, in the guise of a genial genre scene. In this the picture is typical of its time: it shows nothing of the dispossession or violence that accompanied the fur trade and western expansion, and records instead a nostalgic idea of a vanishing way of life.","c":"The picture's strengths are its vivid red and blue accents against a muted brown-gray interior, and its inventory of kit, which gives a ring of authenticity. The group feels posed rather than lived, with the central rifleman standing more like a studio model, and the narrative of the scene, the deer, the glance upward, is thin. Stanley is better in the open air and on the plains; indoors he is a competent illustrator of a frontier idea."},"nga:124519":{"d":"A cage, net, hunting horn, powder bag, pouch, two whistles, a bow and arrow, and a small round bird's hood are arranged in and around an arched niche in a cream-white wall, lit from the upper left so that soft slate-gray and powder-blue shadows fall across it. A wooden cage with an arched top and closely spaced bars sits at the center, crowned by a scarlet falcon's hood with a red pompom; a sheathed knife and a curving horn that shifts from black to sage green lean against it. A powder horn hangs on a canary-yellow cord. The canvas is about 81 by 65 centimeters.","i":"Niche pictures with hunting gear were a specialty of Dutch trompe l'oeil painters in the later seventeenth century, made to fool the eye into taking the painted niche for a real recess in a wall. The objects are the equipment of falconry and the chase, a noble pastime in the Netherlands, and an owner who bought such a picture displayed his taste for the sport and its refinement. Hunters' trophies could also hint at the transience of pleasures, in the old vanitas habit.","c":"Pierson's strength is trompe l'oeil precision: clean light, cool shadows, convincing texture in wood, cord and horn, all kept in a subdued range of colors enlivened by the scarlet hood and yellow cord. The picture is skillful rather than moving, and its attractions are those of an exercise in illusion. Yet the careful arrangement and quiet palette give it a stillness that makes it more than a catalogue of equipment. A refined piece of illusionism."},"nga:157462":{"d":"Fruit, grapes, raisins and nuts are piled on a putty-brown tabletop with rounded corners against a dark ground, lit brightly from the front and seen from slightly above. Two round red apples and two small yellow fruits, perhaps quinces, flank a golden-yellow pear at the back centre; a bunch of green grapes drapes over the fruit at the right, and a stem of raisins lies between the apples. In front, a loose band of thirteen nuts includes walnuts, peanuts, almonds, hazelnuts and perhaps a brazil nut. The artist signed and dated it in the lower right, almost lost in shadow.","i":"Duncanson, the first African American painter to gain an international reputation, was a largely self-taught artist who made his living in Cincinnati painting portraits and still lifes before turning to the landscapes for which he became famous. Still lifes of fruit were affordable decorative pictures for the growing middle class of the Midwest in the 1840s. This one, dated 1848, follows the American tradition of the fruit piece established by the Peale family.","c":"A competent, honest fruit piece. The strong front lighting gives the apples and pear a plump, sculptural body, and the dark ground sets off their colours cleanly. The loosely scattered nuts give the foreground a welcome informality. It is more a display of skill than of personality; the arrangement is straightforward, the textures are generalised rather than minutely observed, and nothing here prepares the viewer for the luminous landscapes that would make Duncanson's name."},"nga:195391":{"d":"Apples, peaches, green and purple grapes, cherries, pears and plums are heaped in a thin white bowl, some overflowing onto the white surface around it. Many have thin stems attached and some thick green leaves. Behind the fruit the ground is dark brown, and a tan arched border frames the picture, as if the scene were seen through a niche. The canvas is a modest size, about 34 by 48 centimetres, in the American tradition of the abundant fruit piece.","i":"Piled-up fruit pieces with a wide variety of fruit were a favourite of American collectors in the 1840s, suggesting abundance and the fertility of the young country, and they were an inexpensive way for a painter to find buyers. Duncanson was working in Cincinnati around 1849, where he painted many fruit still lifes before turning mainly to landscape.","c":"A pleasant, abundant arrangement with lively colour: the purple grapes and red cherries pop against the white bowl and dark ground, and the painted arch adds a neat trompe l'oeil frame. Yet the fruit is piled rather than composed, the forms are somewhat generalised, and the white bowl and surface are uneventful. It is a likable early work of an artist whose real invention lay in landscape."},"met:10777":{"d":"A pastoral landscape in which a shepherd is the small human accent, with trees among the recorded elements, on a wide canvas of about 83 by 123 centimeters, dated 1852. Robert S. Duncanson, a largely self-taught Black painter working in Cincinnati, was by this date winning patrons for landscapes in the manner of the Hudson River School, and the pastoral vein, with a figure scaled to the landscape, was one he returned to often.","i":"A shepherd in a landscape is an old image of peace and harmony between people and nature, inherited from classical pastoral poetry and from painters such as Claude. For a Cincinnati patron of the 1850s, the type offered an educated taste for Europe and an ideal of unspoiled American nature at once. Duncanson, who admired Thomas Cole and later worked in Britain, combined these influences at a time when racial barriers made his place in the tradition far from easy.","c":"A well-schooled landscape that shows Duncanson's command of the Hudson River formula: layered space, clear distance, light on foliage, the accent of the figure. It is not his most inventive picture, since the shepherd and trees are conventional ingredients, but the handling is polished and the atmosphere serene. Within a career that rose to far grander panoramas, it is an early sign of his ability to compete with the leading landscape painters of the day."},"cma:125236":{"d":"A calm, panoramic view of Lake Pepin, the broad widening of the Mississippi River between Minnesota and Wisconsin, on a small, wide canvas about 30 by 55 centimeters. According to the Cleveland Museum, Robert S. Duncanson painted it during a journey up the Mississippi toward Canada, a trip he likely made to escape the upheaval of the Civil War. The date is 1862.","i":"In 1862 Cincinnati, Duncanson's home, was a tense border city across the river from slave-holding Kentucky, and he headed north, later working in Montreal and London before returning after the war. Lake Pepin, a famed beauty spot on the upper Mississippi, lay on the steamboat route that tourists traveled in the 1850s, and its bluffs were sought by a public eager for pictures of the great river. The picture's calm gives no sign of the turmoil that sent him.","c":"A small panel with the large calm of a panorama: Duncanson's best landscapes make space and light do the talking, and a lake view lets him. The atmosphere is serene, even contemplative, which suits a painter in flight from war. The risk of the format is monotony, a long, low horizon with little to hold the eye. A gentle, accomplished picture that deserves a place beside his grander works."},"cma:171296":{"d":"A vast panorama of the Vale of Kashmir, about 73 by 132 centimeters: a courtly entourage leaves a palace of vaguely Islamic design, arrives by boat on a strip of shore, and climbs a monumental staircase to a plaza with a fountain throwing up a tall plume of water. The figures are small, and the landscape, as the Cleveland Museum notes, overwhelms them with its splendor. The scene is drawn from Thomas Moore's poem Lalla-Rookh, and the picture is dated 1867.","i":"Moore's Lalla-Rookh (1817) was among the most popular poems of the nineteenth century, a chain of tales in which a princess travels to Kashmir to be married and falls in love with the poet who entertains her on the way, who proves to be her bridegroom in disguise. Its Orientalist fantasy of palaces, gardens and roses gave painters an imaginary landscape of luxury and romance, and for Duncanson, newly home from abroad, it offered the sublime without the limits of American topography.","c":"Duncanson stretches his imagination here, and the result is among his most ambitious: a vast, layered scene in which architecture, water and mountains build a theatrical stage. The human story is subordinate, as the museum notes, and the pleasure is scenic more than narrative. It can seem overscaled and a little stagey, a Hudson River sublime transposed to the East, yet its ambition is striking for a painter who by 1867 had worked on both sides of the Atlantic."},"met:17897":{"d":"Cows stand in a stream under trees in a gentle, pastoral landscape, on a canvas about 54 by 88 centimeters. It is dated 1871, a year before the death of Robert S. Duncanson, a Black painter based in Cincinnati, and belongs to the pastoral vein he never abandoned, in which animals, water and shade take the place of the grand panoramas of his middle years.","i":"Cattle watering at a stream is a classic of pastoral landscape, from Dutch painters like Cuyp and Potter to the English picturesque, because it combines rural labor, water, light and shade into a quiet, balanced scene. In America after the Civil War such pictures offered comfort and continuity; in a rapidly industrializing country, the cow in the stream was a sign of an older, slower rural order.","c":"A modest late picture, probably assured in its handling of reflected light and the gentle recession of the stream. The cows are placed with a painter's tact, though they are less individually observed than those of the Dutch masters he echoes. It lacks the ambition of his big panoramas, but its calm and finish show that Duncanson's strength was always an ordered, tender relationship to light on water."},"met:18447":{"d":"A view of Lake George in upstate New York, with mountains rising beyond the water and trees among the recorded elements, on a canvas of about 52 by 76 centimeters, dated 1857. Lake George, in the foothills of the Adirondacks, was one of the most painted spots in the Hudson River School: close to New York, rich in history and gentle in form, it combined lakes, mountains and forest in manageable proportions.","i":"Lake George had been celebrated since the eighteenth century, as the site of colonial battles and a place of wild beauty, and by the 1850s it was a resort reached by railroad and steamer. Its association with James Fenimore Cooper's Last of the Mohicans (1826) added a literary note. Painters such as Casilear, a friend of Asher B. Durand, rendered it with clear, reassuring light, a harmony of nature and national memory.","c":"Casilear's Lake George is a study in tranquility: soft, silvery light, a polished surface, a careful arrangement of mountain, shore and water. The calm can verge on blandness, and the picture lacks the drama and spiritual ambition of Church or Cole. But in the best of his work the finish and gentleness have a lyrical honesty, and this is a well-composed, mellow example of the Hudson River manner at its most serene."},"nga:56570":{"d":"A lake stretches from a spit of land at the lower left to hills and mountains rising steeply on the far bank, in a hazy view. A dirt path winds through grass between boulders and trees, where a tiny figure in a brown hat and tan jacket sits on a rock facing the water, holding what may be a sketchbook or palette, a red bag beside him. Low sunlight slices through the trees to light the grass and stones, a person in a red shirt paddles a canoe on the blue lake, and smoky purple mountains stand against a hazy sky. Signed JWC and dated 57.","i":"Lake George, in the Adirondack region of New York, was a favorite of Hudson River School painters, who found there a picturesque balance of calm water, wooded shores and mountains. Casilear, formerly an engraver, was a friend of Asher B. Durand and John Kensett, and shared their interest in clear, luminous light. The tiny sketcher on the rock stands for the painter himself and for the tourists who came to find views, as steamboats and hotels made the lake a destination.","c":"Casilear is among the quietest of the Hudson River painters: his skies are pale, his water still and his color restrained, and the effect is of serene, silvery calm. The small figures give scale without telling a story, and the interplay of sunlit grass and shaded shore is delicately handled. It lacks the drama of Church or Bierstadt, but its tranquillity and finish are real, and it exemplifies the luminist side of the school."},"met:10381":{"d":"A mountain lake with a boat on its water, in a small canvas of about 31 by 51 centimeters. The title is generic, and the picture is best described as a type: a composed view of still water and rising mountains of the kind John William Casilear painted throughout his career, here dated 1883, when he was in his seventies and the Hudson River manner he practiced had begun to look old-fashioned beside newer French-influenced styles.","i":"A mountain lake with a boat is a classic of Romantic and Hudson River landscape: the lake as mirror, the mountain as grandeur, the small boat as a human presence and a measure of scale. In the 1880s, with American taste turning toward Impressionism and the Barbizon school, such pictures were often seen as nostalgic, valued by older collectors for their peace and craft. They offered a stable, familiar idea of nature while the industrial world grew.","c":"Casilear's late lake scenes are small, quiet and finely finished. The best of them have a delicate, luminous calm; the weaker ones can seem formulaic, with the same blue-gray hills and glassy water. A picture of this size has little room for surprise, and the title suggests no particular place, which fits a painter who valued mood over topography. A gentle, craftsmanlike late work."},"nga:195112":{"d":"A wide lake, in light blue and gray tones, is surrounded by hazy mountains in pale greens and blues and dotted with small tree-filled islands. In the foreground a green area with several tall trees and a small dirt path gives way to a hill that slopes down to the water. Puffy white clouds fill a blue sky. The museum's keywords place it at Lake George; the canvas is about 65 by 115 centimeters, painted after 1851, and the horizontal format gives the view a panoramic breadth.","i":"Lake George is studded with islands, more than a hundred of them, and artists and tourists of the mid-nineteenth century prized the lake for its calm water, wooded hills and picturesque outlook. Casilear, a Hudson River School painter who had been an engraver, painted it repeatedly, and he and his friends helped to make it one of America's best-known beauty spots. A broad, tranquil view like this appealed to buyers who wanted nature as a restful, unthreatening presence.","c":"The panoramic format lets Casilear show what he does best: pale, atmospheric distance, hazy mountain layers and a lake that mirrors the sky. The foreground trees frame the view in the manner of Claude, a standard device, and the effect is orderly and serene. The picture is gentler and less dramatic than much landscape of the day, and may strike a modern viewer as polite, but its poise and soft harmony of blue, gray and green are genuine."},"met:769294":{"d":"The newborn Christ Child is adored by Mary, Joseph and the shepherds, with an ox and a donkey among the recorded figures, in a small panel of about 31 by 41 centimeters, painted in oil on wood. The size is that of a picture for private devotion, to be kept close at hand, and the subject, the arrival of the shepherds at the stable, was one of the most beloved Christmas scenes. Girolamo da Carpi, from Ferrara, painted it around 1535 to 1540, in a Mannerist style that owed much to Correggio.","i":"The Adoration of the Shepherds (Luke 2) shows the first witnesses of Christ's birth, humble men called by angels, and so stands for the Gospel's welcome to the poor and unlearned. The ox and donkey come not from the Gospels but from tradition, based on a verse of Isaiah, and were sometimes read as the Jewish and Gentile peoples who recognize their master. Joseph, shown as protector, completes the Holy Family.","c":"A small and graceful picture that shows Girolamo's Ferrarese refinement and a debt to Correggio's soft, lyrical manner. At this size the elegance counts for more than monumentality: it is a devotional object, built to charm and to warm the viewer toward the story. It may be too pleasant, avoiding the earthy detail that Netherlandish and German painters often brought to the subject; but within the Italian tradition of graceful Nativities it is accomplished."},"nga:526":{"d":"Mary sits on a bank of smoky-violet clouds, supported by winged angels, in a tall arched panel about 199 by 131 centimeters. She wears a flax-yellow veil, an azure mantle and a ruby-red dress, her hands joined in prayer as she looks upward; two older angels at her feet look down, two nude child-like angels flank her hips, and sixteen winged baby heads ring her, backlighting the group. Below at left, an older woman in black, holding a rosary, prays before a vast landscape that recedes to buildings at lower right. Farther back, a small man in a yellow cloak reaches toward a green, snaking shape.","i":"The Virgin in glory appearing to a believer was one of the stock visions of Catholic devotion, a reminder that the heavenly world could break into the everyday. The older woman with a rosary, probably a donor or a representative of the devout, is placed at the edge of the vision, where her prayer meets its answer; the small figure behind her may belong to a separate episode whose meaning is unclear. In sixteenth-century Italy such images reassured viewers of the Virgin's intercession.","c":"Girolamo da Carpi absorbed the lessons of Correggio and Parmigianino: the soft glow of heads in a ring of light, the sinuous grace of the Virgin's pose, the sweet, rounded faces. The vision is beautiful in its coloring and in the backlit angels, and the earthbound woman and landscape give it a counterweight. What it lacks is Correggio's tenderness and conviction; the effect is more decorative than devout. A polished, attractive Mannerist altarpiece."},"cma:125251":{"d":"A prelate, a high-ranking Catholic churchman, is shown in a dark mantle lined with red over a gauzy white rochet, wearing a three-cornered biretta. The museum notes the flowing S-curve of his clothing and his delicate, attenuated fingers, signs of the Mannerist grace of Girolamo da Carpi. In his right hand he holds a book whose cover shows an elephant standing in water and looking at the moon, with the inscription MUNDOS LIBENTER ASPICIT. The canvas is large, about 140 by 108 centimeters, and the sitter's name is not known.","i":"The book's emblem is the key to the portrait's message: following ancient accounts of elephants, the animal purifying itself in water and revering the moon stood for purity, and the motto, meaning the moon beholds the pure with pleasure, applies it to the sitter's virtue. Several noble households used this device, but none has been proven to be his. A Renaissance prelate was both churchman and courtier, and a portrait of this kind presented him as learned, elegant and morally above reproach.","c":"This is Mannerist portraiture at its most refined: the elongated hands and the serpentine curve of the mantle turn a clerical sitter into an image of elegance and reserve. The emblem on the book is a clever, learned touch, and the whole shows off the painter's court polish. Its price is distance: the sitter, with his controlled grace and unknown name, remains an aristocratic mask rather than a person. A fine example of Ferrarese court portraiture."},"met:436798":{"d":"Mary nurses the Christ Child while Joseph and a shepherd look on, in an upright canvas about 107 by 76 centimeters. The recorded details, the Holy Family, a shepherd, breastfeeding and a candle, suggest an intimate, nocturnal scene with the warmth of candlelight. The picture was painted on wood and later transferred to canvas, a conservation procedure. It is dated 1616, when Jacob Jordaens was in his early twenties and newly a master in the Antwerp guild.","i":"The Holy Family with shepherds merges two traditions, the Nativity and the quiet domesticity of the Holy Family, and puts a plain, earthy emphasis on the Incarnation: God made flesh, nursed like any child. The nursing Virgin was a favorite of Counter-Reformation devotion for its tenderness and doctrinal point. The candle motif, which echoes Caravaggio and the northern followers of his lighting, makes the scene feel close and real, a story of ordinary light in a humble place.","c":"Jordaens trained with Adam van Noort, Rubens's own teacher, and his early work carries the muscular energy of Rubens with a stronger taste for peasant types and homely detail. The result is earthy and warm, if not refined; faces are broad, hands large and flesh has weight. Next to Rubens's grace he can seem coarse, but in a family scene his appetite for physical life is exactly right. A vigorous, intimate early work."},"cma:145148":{"d":"The kiss of Judas in the Garden of Gethsemane fills a huge, nearly square canvas of about 226 by 246 centimeters. Judas betrays Christ with a kiss and Roman soldiers move in to arrest him, while in the foreground Peter attacks Malchus, the High Priest's servant, with violent energy. It is a night scene, and the soldiers' lanterns pick out faces, armor and gestures from the darkness. Jacob Jordaens painted it in the late 1650s, when he had been the leading painter in Antwerp for close to two decades.","i":"The arrest in Gethsemane (Matthew 26 and John 18) begins the Passion: Judas identifies Christ with a kiss, Peter draws his sword and cuts off the ear of Malchus, and, in Luke's account, Christ heals the wound. The scene condenses betrayal, violence and divine acceptance into a single night. In the Catholic Restoration of Antwerp, large night pictures of the Betrayal let churches and patrons stage the moral drama of the Passion before the faithful.","c":"The late Jordaens keeps his powerful physical energy, and the night setting suits it: lantern light slashing across armor, flesh and fabric, with a mass of figures packed into a near-square. The weaknesses are his habitual coarseness and crowding; the faces are broad, the drama loud. Next to the subtler nocturnes of Rembrandt's circle the effect is more theatrical than penetrating, but few Flemish painters could marshal so many bodies with such vigor."},"met:436799":{"d":"An extended holy family fills a large, nearly square panel of about 170 by 150 centimeters: Mary and the Christ Child with Joseph, Mary's mother Saint Anne, and the infant John the Baptist with his parents, Elizabeth and Zacharias, with a sheep among the figures. The museum dates the picture to the early 1620s and the 1650s, which means Jacob Jordaens began it as a younger man and returned to it decades later. Painted in oil on wood, it is a portrait of a whole sacred clan.","i":"The wider family of Christ, gathered around him, was a theme with a long medieval history, and in Counter-Reformation Antwerp such groups offered an image of the family as a sacred institution. The lamb beside the infant John is the sign of the Lamb of God, whom John would later announce. For patrons, the extended household offered a mirror of their own ideals: several generations, children and parents, bound by faith.","c":"Painted in two campaigns decades apart, the picture invites speculation about how Jordaens's style changed, but its overall character is unmistakable: robust, warm figures, a family pressed close together, rich color and heavy flesh. The risk is crowding and coarseness; the virtue is generosity. A characteristic Jordaens, abundant, humane and not at all aloof."},"nga:231753":{"d":"A man and a woman are indoors. He sits on a red-covered piece of furniture holding a letter, in a yellow vest, white shirt, cravat and gray breeches, his blond hair curly; she kneels beside him holding a piece of paper. Around them are a stool with letters, a musical instrument, a chair with a cloak, a sword, a hat with plumes, and a decorated cabinet with a globe and vase on paneled walls. The oil on canvas is about 45 by 55 cm, dated 1788 to 1789.","i":"Love letters were the fashionable medium of sentiment in late eighteenth-century France, from Rousseau's novel Julie to the intrigues of Laclos, and the title, spoken in the first person, gives the picture the intimacy of a confession. The sword, plumed hat and cloak suggest a man who has arrived or is about to leave, and the musical instrument signals harmony and courtship. The companion, Deserved Sorrows, suggests a story in which pleasure has a price.","c":"Gérard's special gift is charm on a small scale: the red furniture, the yellow vest, the cabinet with its globe and vase show her relish for detail, and the kneeling woman adds a gentle comedy of devotion. The picture reflects the Ancien Régime's fondness for sentiment, staged with precision. Its strength is elegance and its weakness a staged feeling, with the emotion carried by props. It is a witty, well-made cabinet picture."},"nga:231754":{"d":"A woman sits on a chair in a luxurious interior, in a cream gown with a red overcoat lined in white fur, her wavy hair in a loose updo. She holds a silver platter while a maid beside her holds a tray with a glass, a small loaf and a gold pitcher. Ornate armor and weapons lie on the floor, a painting of a man in historical dress hangs on the wall, and the room is paneled and elegantly furnished. The canvas is about 45 by 55 cm, dated 1788 to 1789.","i":"The title promises a moral: the sorrows are deserved, so the woman is responsible for her unhappiness, perhaps through coquetry or an unfaithful love. The armor and the portrait of a man in historical dress suggest a taste for chivalric romance, or a lover or husband lost to war. The companion picture in the same collection, of the same size and date, suggests a pair that told a love story in stages. Late eighteenth-century French viewers liked such sentimental moral anecdotes.","c":"Gérard's art is small, precise and ingeniously staged: she learned from Fragonard but chose the finish of the Dutch cabinet painters, and a room like this is a showpiece of surfaces, fur, silver, gold and wood. The sorrow is told through props more than faces, and the moral is slightly arch. As drama it is calm; as an accomplished example of a woman painter's success under the Ancien Régime it is a pleasure."},"met:442761":{"d":"A scene from the aftermath of the Trojan War, according to the record's motifs: the Trojan princess Polyxena, a soldier, a man, a woman and a child. The canvas is large, about 169 by 131 centimeters, and was painted in 1647. The museum gives no further description of the composition, so the arrangement of figures around the altar or tomb and the moment of the sacrifice are not detailed here.","i":"After Troy fell, the ghost of Achilles demanded that Priam's daughter Polyxena be sacrificed at his tomb; in Euripides' play Hecuba and in Ovid's Metamorphoses she meets death with courage, offering her throat to Achilles' son Neoptolemus. Le Brun painted the subject in 1647, just after returning from Rome, where he had studied alongside Poussin; the story of a noble victim gave a painter a chance for grave, restrained pathos.","c":"A history painting of the highest ambition from a young painter, and it shows both Poussin's lesson and Le Brun's own appetite for orderly, declamatory compositions. The risks of Le Brun's manner are theatrical posturing and a certain coldness; the virtues are clarity, balance of groups and a command of expression. It shows the painter who would soon decorate Versailles in an early, impressively assured state."},"met:626692":{"d":"A large family group fills a rich interior. At the left of centre a man in a dark robe and pale stockings sits turned toward his wife, who wears blue and holds a naked infant on a red cloth across her lap. Around them stand a boy, two girls, one in yellow and blue and one in a patterned gown, and a slim greyhound. Behind the man, a mirror reflects a young painter at work. On the floor and on a stand at the left lie a helmeted bust, a celestial globe, books, dividers, prints and an antique marble head, while heavy green curtains hang above. A red carpet with fringe spreads across the foreground. The canvas is over three metres wide.","i":"The man is Everhard Jabach, a German-born banker and collector who made his career in Paris, shown with his wife and children. Le Brun painted two versions around 1660; the other, once in Berlin, was destroyed in the Second World War. The objects on the floor describe Jabach's interests: architecture, astronomy, antiquities and books, under a bust of Minerva, goddess of wisdom and the arts. Le Brun is reflected in the mirror behind his patron, which puts painter and client in one scene, an idea the Met compares to Velázquez's Las Meninas. Jabach later sold much of his collection to Louis XIV, and it became a core of the Louvre's holdings.","c":"This is a picture of ambition: a showpiece of rich fabrics, still-life detail and a clever mirror, painted by an artist on the rise, soon to be Louis XIV's first painter. The textures are convincing, from blue satin to the fringed carpet, and the hidden self-portrait adds wit. The grouping is less sure. The figures sit side by side like a display, each pleasantly drawn but hardly interacting, and the objects at the left are an inventory, not an event. As a record of a collector's world it is invaluable; as drama it is cool."},"cma:124414":{"d":"Animals lie down together in a landscape in this small canvas, about 48 by 60 centimetres: Hicks's version of the prophet Isaiah's vision of a world at peace, in which the wolf dwells with the lamb, the leopard lies down with the kid, and the calf and the young lion are led by a little child. The museum's record gives no further description, so the arrangement of the beasts, the child and the background cannot be reported with confidence. According to the museum, it is likely the first of more than sixty versions Hicks painted.","i":"Edward Hicks (1780–1849) was a Quaker minister and a sign and coach painter in Pennsylvania, and the Peaceable Kingdom was his sermon in paint: Isaiah's prophecy, read as a promise of the peace that Quakers hoped to bring to human life. In many versions Hicks included, in the background, William Penn's treaty with the Native Americans, a Quaker emblem of peaceful settlement. This picture, dated about 1816 to 1818, is among the earliest and came to Cleveland through the Hanna Fund.","c":"A work of sincere, untutored power. The animals, drawn with a sign painter's directness and stylised patterns, have a strange, ritual grandeur, and the bold idea of making peace visible as a gathering of enemies is conveyed with perfect clarity. The perspective is naive, the lions awkward and the faces of the beasts oddly human, but these faults are the source of the picture's force. It is a primary document of American folk art, and still affecting."},"met:11080":{"d":"Niagara Falls thunders in the background of this canvas of about 80 by 97 centimetres, with trees at the sides and, in the foreground, animals that the museum lists as an eagle, a snake and other creatures. The record gives no further description, so the arrangement and colours cannot be reported with confidence. Hicks painted several versions of the subject in the 1820s, setting the great natural wonder, which Americans prided themselves on, behind a menagerie that is his own invention.","i":"Niagara was the most celebrated sight in the United States by the 1820s, a symbol of national grandeur visited by tourists and reproduced in prints, and Hicks probably adapted a printed view and added animals from his imagination. The eagle, the emblem of the nation, and the snake, an old symbol of danger and temptation, hint at a lesson placed in the foreground, though what Hicks intended is not recorded. The Met received it as a gift from Edgar William and Bernice Chrysler Garbisch in 1962.","c":"A charming and slightly strange picture: the falls are flattened into a pattern of white water and green cliff, as in a decorative print, and the animals in front, painted with the naive directness of his Peaceable Kingdom beasts, add a note of moral fable. Hicks's sense of design is surer than his sense of space. As folk art it has rare vitality; as a view of Niagara it is an unreliable report."},"met:11081":{"d":"Animals and people share a landscape in this canvas of about 45 by 61 centimetres, Hicks's vision of Isaiah's peaceable kingdom. The museum lists trees, a woman, cattle, an ox, a lamb, a leopard and a lion, so the predators and prey of the prophecy lie down together under the trees, with a human figure among them. The record gives no further description, so the number of animals, their poses and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. It dates from about 1830 to 1832, some fifteen years after Hicks's first version.","i":"Hicks, a Quaker minister, painted the subject for decades, and the later versions, like this one, have often been read as a response to the painful division among Quakers in the late 1820s, when the movement split into Orthodox and Hicksite branches, the latter named for his cousin Elias Hicks. His paintings became a plea for harmony, with Isaiah's lion, leopard and ox as figures for the quarrelling parties made peaceful. The picture reached the Met from the Garbisches in 1970.","c":"In its later phase the formula is looser and more confident: the animals are fuller and more individual, the little picture a gentle sermon without pretence. Compared with the earliest versions it may seem more settled and perhaps less raw, but the naive drawing, the pattern-making and the sincerity are all there. Hicks drew his animals chiefly from prints, and that is why they look like creatures of a dream, which suits a vision of paradise."},"nga:59908":{"d":"A bull and a lion stand side by side among a company of animals at rest: a tiger, wolf, sheep, goats, cows, bears and a jaguar, with three blond children among them, one standing over the lion's back with an arm around the tiger and another touching the jaguar's nose. On a second grassy spit to the left, eight indigenous people in feathered headdresses face eight men in tricorn hats and long coats, one holding up a roll of cloth lifted from a chest. A river runs back to sage-green hills under a sky shading from shell pink to blue. Animals and children are drawn simply and flatly, almost cartoonishly.","i":"Hicks, a Quaker minister and sign painter in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, painted dozens of Peaceable Kingdoms, drawing on the prophecy in Isaiah 11 of a time when the wolf will lie down with the lamb and a little child will lead them. The treaty scene in the background, in his versions usually William Penn's agreement with the Lenape, links that vision to the Quaker founding of Pennsylvania. Some also read the animals as an appeal for harmony among Quakers split by the 1820s schism.","c":"Hicks's deliberate naïveté is part of his power: flattened forms, crisp outlines, a procession of beasts as balanced as a pattern. He was a self-taught artisan, and the animals owe more to prints than to observation, yet the stately symmetry of the composition turns the apparent weakness into style. The result is serene, odd and strongly felt, among the best-loved of American folk-art pictures."},"nga:59906":{"d":"A boat of about eight men, in bright red, blue and gold garments, many in plumed helmets and some wearing jewelry or badges, heads for a green shore, one man standing in the bow with a white flag. Larger sailing ships lie on dark green water at the right beneath a low horizon. On the land ahead, tall palm trees and a rocky gray mountain frame dark-skinned people climbing the slope or standing under trees. The sky grades from pinkish orange at the horizon to light blue. It is a small canvas of about 45 by 60 centimeters.","i":"Columbus's first landing in the Americas in October 1492 had become a national legend in the early United States, a founding myth that tied the young republic to the Old World's discovery of the New. Hicks, a Quaker, gives the encounter a peaceful emphasis: a white flag in the prow, Spaniards in bright garments, indigenous people watching from the shore. In keeping with its time, the picture says nothing of the conquest and disease that followed.","c":"The charm lies in Hicks's decorative simplification: flat, bright costumes against a quiet sea, palm trees as pattern, the white flag as the one sharp accent. His figures are stiff and his ships generic, and the historical drama is flat; but he gives the legendary event a strange calm, closer to a toy theater than to a battle painting. A minor, endearing work by an artist whose real subject was peace."},"nga:59904":{"d":"Eight clean-shaven white men in black tricorn hats meet thirteen Lenape men, women and a baby on a grassy field. Two white men kneel with a bolt of red cloth before the seated Lenape, one of whom holds a long white clay pipe; an unfurled scroll in the middle reads Pennsylvania. Two women and a baby sit under a tree at left, and a bow and quiver lie on the grass. Behind, a river carries a boat toward a long white building. A text panel below declares the treaty made without an oath and never broken, the foundation of religious and civil liberty in the United States.","i":"The meeting of William Penn and the Lenape became a founding legend of Pennsylvania and, for Quakers like Hicks, proof that a colony could rest on peaceful principle: land agreed by treaty, sworn on no oath because Friends refused oaths. Hicks's composition probably follows Benjamin West's famous painting, which spread through engravings. The inscription turns the legend into a national claim, though historians note that no written record of the treaty survives and that the later history of the Lenape in Pennsylvania was one of dispossession.","c":"Hicks's flatness is not simple incompetence but a style: the big-eyed, evenly spaced figures read like a procession on a banner, and the text below makes the picture a poster for an idea. It lacks all depth, yet it has a moral clarity and a patterned rhythm that West's grand version lacks. As history it is sentimental, and its pieties have aged; as folk art it is moving and utterly sincere."},"nga:50261":{"d":"A prize farm in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, spreads across a horizontal canvas of about 93 by 124 centimeters. In a band across the bottom stand about two dozen cows of many colors, six adult sheep and a lamb, five black pigs, one of them suckling piglets, and nine horses and foals, pressed closely together on teal-blue ground. Behind them, pairs of men in tall hats look over the stock, a man follows a horse-drawn plow, fences divide the grassy plots, and cream and barn-red buildings line the back under a pale pink sky. A long inscription below names the owner and the year, 1848.","i":"The inscription says the picture shows the farm and stock of James C. Cornell of Northampton, Bucks County, which took the premium at the agricultural society on October 12, 1848, and that Hicks painted it in his sixty-ninth year. It is a commemorative portrait of a prosperous farm and its prize livestock, in the tradition of farm portraits that celebrated improved breeding and agricultural science. For Hicks, a Quaker, it can also be read as another peaceable kingdom, with animals at rest and men at work.","c":"Hicks's gift for pattern is on show: the animals arranged like a frieze, the fences and fields stepping back like a patchwork, the colors keyed to a clean, bright palette. The perspective is naive and the animals are described as types rather than individuals, yet the design is confident and the picture has a satisfied clarity that more sophisticated farm portraits lack. One of his most appealing works, and Hicks in a mood of unforced contentment."},"nga:59905":{"d":"A large white-and-gray house stands on a hill at the left of a pastoral English scene, with small figures and a horse-drawn wagon before it. At lower right two people sit in the grass beneath a tree, one in red holding a tall crook and the other in black and a yellow hat petting a large brown-and-white dog, a basket between them, while a herd of cows and sheep rest nearby. Yellow hills recede beneath a pastel pink and blue sky, and lines of text cross a patch of yellow ground at lower left. The canvas is about 60 by 76 centimeters.","i":"William Penn, the Quaker founder of Pennsylvania, was buried in 1718 in the Quaker burial ground at Jordans in Buckinghamshire, England, and the setting here is English, though Hicks probably knew it from prints. Turning a graveyard into a pastoral meadow with shepherd and flock makes the picture a memorial in the language of the Peaceable Kingdom: the founder at rest, his flock at peace.","c":"A quiet, odd picture: the subject is a grave, but the treatment is a pastoral idyll, with sheep and cows resting beneath a candy-colored sky. The simplicity of Hicks's drawing keeps the sentiment from becoming mawkish, and the relation of the distant house to the foreground group is neatly judged. It is less strong than his best Kingdoms and the Cornell Farm, but it remains a charming, heartfelt example of Quaker piety in folk idiom."},"met:436631":{"d":"A self-portrait of George Henry Harlow, painted on a canvas of about 76 by 64 centimeters at about the time of his death in 1819, at the age of thirty-one. The recorded subject is the artist alone; his pose, dress and expression are not described here. Harlow, a pupil of Sir Thomas Lawrence and the painter of a famous group of the Kemble family in a scene from Shakespeare, made his name quickly with dashing portraits and died before his career could mature.","i":"A painter's self-portrait is both a test of skill and a statement of who he thought he was. Harlow's generation of British portraitists aimed to join the ranks of Lawrence and Reynolds, and a self-portrait was a way to show some of the same swagger. Because Harlow died so young, the picture carries a poignancy of unfulfilled promise that its first viewers could not have foreseen.","c":"Harlow had Lawrence's flair for a lively, flattering likeness and a quicker, broader touch, and a self-portrait gives him a sitter who will not complain. Whether the result is searching or merely dashing is the question his short career leaves open; the facility is certain, the depth less so. As a document of a talented young man who died before he could mature, it is touching and handsome."},"met:436516":{"d":"A round tray, set in a deep gilded frame carved like twisted rope, is painted with a crowded procession. Knights in steel armour and bright cloaks, some mounted, some on foot, press toward a ribbed, shell-like globe. On top stands Fame, a slender winged figure in a pale gown with a starry crown, holding a sword in one hand and a tiny winged cupid in the other. Behind are pink and brown rocky headlands, trees and a blue sea inlet with a ship. In the foreground a white dog, a dark dog and a brown animal run across a pink and black patterned ground, and a figure in yellow stands in the crowd. The painting is in tempera, with silver and gold, on a wooden panel.","i":"This is a desco da parto, a birth tray: in Renaissance Florence such trays carried gifts to a new mother and were afterwards hung as decoration. The Met says it was made to commemorate the birth of Lorenzo de' Medici in 1449, future ruler of Florence, poet and patron of the arts, and that it was kept in his private rooms in the Medici palace. The subject, the Triumph of Fame, belongs to the world of Petrarch's poem on allegorical triumphs. On the back are the device of Lorenzo's father Piero, a diamond ring with three ostrich feathers and the motto Semper, meaning forever, and the arms of the Medici and Tornabuoni, his mother's family.","c":"The tray is a splendid piece of Florentine pageantry. Scheggia fits dozens of figures into a single circle, and the sharp-edged armour, the twisted gold frame and the pale figure of Fame give it a jewel-like brilliance. It is not a work of deep invention or psychology: the knights are repeated types, the crowd is packed into an almost flat band, and the animals in front are stiff. But as a keepsake of a family's hopes on the day an heir was born, it has a festive warmth that many grander pictures lack."},"met:436268":{"d":"The companion to the panel of the same title, this small oil on wood measures about 65 by 54 centimeters and records the same motifs, a putto and a medallion. Both came to the museum in the gift of J. Pierpont Morgan in 1906. No date is recorded, though Eisen died in 1778, and the museum does not describe the figures' poses or what the medallion holds, so the pair can be characterized only by type and size.","i":"A medallion held by putti is a classic framing device: the medallion presents a profile portrait or an emblem, and the putti display it like heralds. Eisen designed countless such ornaments for books, and in a decorative painting the same vocabulary expands to a larger surface. Without a record of what the medallion contains, the exact theme cannot be named.","c":"As a pair, the panels would have balanced one another, with the putti turned in complementary directions, a symmetry typical of Rococo and early Neoclassical decoration. Eisen's lightness of touch was valued in illustration, though in oil his reputation is slighter. These are amiable decorations whose value lies in the pair and in the way they ornament a space."},"met:436269":{"d":"Putti, the plump infants of Rococo decoration, and a medallion are the motifs recorded for this small panel, an oil on wood of about 65 by 54 centimeters. The museum gives no description of their poses, the medallion's content or the background, and no date is recorded; the artist died in 1778. The panel has a twin of the same title and size in the same gift, and the two were evidently meant to hang together.","i":"Putti, plump naked infants derived from Roman and Renaissance art, filled French decorative painting of the eighteenth century, where they carried garlands, played with emblems and framed medallions bearing allegorical or portrait subjects. Charles Eisen was above all a book illustrator and designer, famed for his vignettes, and a pair of decorative panels of this kind was probably intended to fit into paneling or an overdoor.","c":"Panels of this kind are judged by grace of design and lightness of touch, and Eisen, an illustrator whose book vignettes are famous for both, is a likely source of each. The motif, however, was conventional, and a pair like this adds charm to a room more than it adds to the history of painting. A neat piece of eighteenth-century ornament."},"met:436610":{"d":"A portrait miniature on ivory of the painter Louis-Joseph Maurice (1730–1820), dated 1772. The size is not recorded here, but miniatures of this kind are small enough to hold in the hand. The sitter is identified as an artist, and Peter Adolf Hall, a Swedish miniaturist working in Paris, painted him in the loosely handled, luminous manner that made Hall one of the most admired miniature painters of the day, with strokes of color laid lightly over the pale ivory.","i":"A portrait of one artist by another often records friendship or professional respect, and a miniature, small enough to be kept in a pocket or on a desk, made an intimate token of it. In eighteenth-century Paris the miniature was a fashionable medium for exchanging likenesses between friends, relatives and patrons, and artists sat for each other as well as for clients. The sitter's dates record a long life that ran through the Ancien Régime and the Revolution.","c":"Hall brought to the miniature the vivacity of the larger Rococo portrait: loose, quick handling, fresh flesh tones, an air of ease that avoids the stiffness of the old enamel miniature. The risk of the style is superficial charm. Hall's reputation among Parisian connoisseurs of the 1770s was high, and a miniature of 1772 shows him in command of that manner."},"met:436612":{"d":"A miniature on ivory of a young woman whose name is not recorded, dated about 1790. The size is not given, but a miniature of this type is small enough to hold in one hand or to wear. Peter Adolf Hall, the Swedish-born painter who made his career in Paris and died in 1793, was among the most fashionable miniaturists of his day, known for a free, luminous handling that gave faces a pearly bloom.","i":"The late eighteenth-century portrait miniature of a young woman answers to the ideals of sensibility: soft, direct, a little dreamy, with simple dress and a glance of gentle intelligence. Such pictures were often gifts between lovers, betrothed couples and family members, and the anonymity of the sitter today leaves the story behind it unknown. As a type it records how a young woman of means wished to be seen around 1790: modest, graceful and fashionable.","c":"The attraction of a Hall miniature is the pearly flesh and the freedom of touch; the risk is sameness, the generic charm of an anonymous young woman in the sensibility style. At this date Hall was at the end of a long Parisian career, and the picture would show his accomplished economy. It is best valued as a representative of the fashionable French miniature on the eve of Revolution, and as an object of intimate beauty."},"met:669033":{"d":"A small portrait, about 48 by 38 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1861, shows the painter Henri Fantin-Latour (1836 to 1904), as the title states. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, so his pose, expression, clothing and the background are left unstated. Both painters were in their twenties and friends in the Paris art world, and the picture is a likeness of one young artist by another.","i":"Portraits of fellow artists were a way for young painters to declare a shared circle. Fantin-Latour would become known for flower still lifes and for group portraits of his artistic friends, such as Homage to Delacroix of 1864, and Carolus-Duran, later one of the most fashionable portraitists of the Third Republic and the teacher of John Singer Sargent, was then at the start of his career. The sitter's later fame gives the picture added interest as a record of a young artist.","c":"A young painter's frank likeness of a friend, painted before either had made a name, has the appeal of sincerity. Carolus-Duran's early work, probably influenced by the realists, was plainer and darker than the glossy society portraits to come, and here the interest is in the contrast: the future showman is a quiet observer. Without more description it can only be said that the picture's value is largely historical, a rare document of two reputations in the making."},"nga:108382":{"d":"The head and shoulders of a woman, seen from behind, fill a vertical canvas against a cherry-red ground. She wears strawberry red, with copper-brown hair piled in a bun and curls flaring over one temple; the back edge of her ear and the curve of her cheek show. Her garment has puffy sleeves and a broad collar across the back, and the neckline curves close to her pale neck. The painting is loose, with black strokes mixed into the red background and wide, visible strokes suggesting pleats and shadows in the garment. It is signed Carolus-Duran in the upper left.","i":"A study is a working piece, not a commission: Carolus-Duran, famous for portraits of the fashionable rich, also made informal studies like this, in which a model seen from behind frees color and brushwork from the demands of a likeness. The identity of Lilia is not recorded here. The daring red-on-red scheme shows his taste for strong color, and recalls Velázquez and Hals, the masters he urged on his pupils, John Singer Sargent among them.","c":"A bravura exercise: the red-on-red harmony is risky, and the loose strokes are lively, giving a sense of fabric and light with little means. Because the face is withheld, the picture is more about paint than person; that is its pleasure and also its limit. Carolus-Duran, often accused of glossy superficiality, shows a more adventurous side, and some of the sparkle of Hals and Velázquez comes through."},"met:435849":{"d":"A full-length, life-size portrait, about 212 by 107 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1890, shows Caroline Webster Schermerhorn Astor (1831 to 1908), the wife of William Astor of New York. The museum's keywords record a woman and a portrait, so her pose, gown, jewels and the setting are left unstated. The painter, Carolus-Duran, was among the most fashionable portraitists in Paris, known for sweeping, elegant likenesses of rich and titled sitters.","i":"Mrs. Astor was the acknowledged queen of New York society in the Gilded Age; her ballroom is said to have held four hundred guests, giving rise to the Four Hundred as shorthand for the city's elite. The full-length format echoes European court portraiture, and a commission from a Paris painter shows American money buying the language of aristocratic splendor. The picture is both a likeness and a statement of social position.","c":"Carolus-Duran made his name by flattering the rich in a Velázquez-inspired idiom of dark tones and quick, assured brushwork, and he knows his business: the full-length pose is imposing and the paint confident. Next to Sargent, his pupil, who brought psychological bite, he offers glamour without much interrogation. The picture succeeds as an image of rank, less as an image of a person, and as a document of Gilded Age taste it is hard to beat."},"met:10080":{"d":"A woman, a boy and a girl are shown at a house, with work as the recorded theme, on an upright canvas of about 61 by 43 centimeters, dated 1879. The title, The Way They Live, with its pointed they, suggests a view of lives other than the buyer's own, and the subject is labor and setting rather than portraiture of individuals. Thomas Anshutz, a student of Thomas Eakins at the Pennsylvania Academy, was among the American painters of his generation who treated working people in this direct way.","i":"In the late 1870s American painters began to look at everyday work and poverty with new honesty, influenced by Eakins's teaching and by French realism. A title like The Way They Live frames the scene for a middle-class viewer, presenting other people's lives as something to be observed; the picture can be read as sympathetic reportage, as a polite distance from its subjects, or both. Anshutz went on to paint ironworkers at their noon break, bringing the same attention to industrial labor.","c":"Anshutz was a faithful student of Eakins in his respect for solid drawing and plain facts, and this early picture shows his attentiveness to bodies at work. It is less accomplished than the Ironworkers' Noontime, where the composition coheres, and the title's mild condescension colors the whole; yet it has the interest of an early, honest look at ordinary labor. A modest but valuable beginning."},"met:816519":{"d":"Grapes and peaches are the subject of this small still life on a wood panel, about 36 by 45 centimeters, dated about 1617. Fruit pictures of this kind were among the first independent still lifes made in Italy, in Lombardy in the early seventeenth century, when painters such as Panfilo Nuvolone, working in Cremona, treated a simple arrangement of ripe fruit as a subject worthy of a picture on its own.","i":"Early Italian still lifes often have a double appeal: delight in the sensuous surface of fruit and an implicit reflection on ripeness and brevity. Grapes and peaches, both fruits at their peak for a short season, stood for abundance, and grapes in Christian symbolism for wine and the Eucharist. In Lombardy, where Cardinal Federico Borromeo collected still lifes, such pictures were also exercises in close observation of nature.","c":"Nuvolone's fruit has a plain, truthful dignity of the kind early Lombard still life cultivated: ripe skins, a modest arrangement, simple lighting and little ornament. It lacks the sparkling finish of Netherlandish fruit pieces and the monumentality of Caravaggio's basket, but the restraint is its virtue. A small, honest early still life that shows Lombardy's contribution to the genre."},"cma:136692":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper mounted on canvas, about 33 by 48 centimeters, shows the natural bridge at Sorrento, a stone arch on the coast of the Bay of Naples. The date given is 1856, early in the career of William Stanley Haseltine, a Philadelphian who studied in Düsseldorf and made Italy a second home; his oil sketches of coastal rocks, made directly from nature, formed the basis of his later studio pictures.","i":"Haseltine belonged to a generation of American landscape painters touched by the era's Ruskinian enthusiasm for geological exactness. Rock formations like Sorrento's natural bridge, shaped by the sea, gave him subjects in which the textures and structures of stone were the real matter of the picture. A sketch like this was a record of facts made on the spot, from which a finished painting for a visitor or collector could later be developed.","c":"A sketch free of the polish of the finished pictures: direct, rapid and honest in its attention to stone and light. Its value is the freshness of seeing, with the rock forms probably described more carefully than the sea and sky. It lacks the grandeur of the big studio canvases, but the best of Haseltine's sketches have a vividness that the finished works can smother."},"nga:42853":{"d":"Wooden rowboats rest on a sandy, rocky shore: in the center a boat with a white cloth and several ropes lying across it, with two more boats in a row behind. At the left, brown stone structures are partly visible, one appearing to be carved out of the rock, with a small figure on top tending what may be a clothesline. Rocks run across the picture, bright green-blue water shows around them, and tiny boats sail far off beneath a light blue sky of puffy clouds. It is a small oil sketch on paper mounted on canvas, about 31 by 47 centimeters.","i":"Marina Piccola is the small southern harbor of Capri, where fishermen kept their boats, and in the 1850s the island was a favored stop for artists, writers and visitors in the Bay of Naples. For American painters like Haseltine, the cliffs, grottoes and boats, and the clear Mediterranean light, offered motifs very different from home. Everyday details, a clothesline, the boats' ropes, give a human touch to a landscape sketch.","c":"Haseltine's oil sketches are at their best when they note a particular place in particular light, and this one does: the weathered boats, the rocks and the luminous water are noted without fuss. The composition is casual, a view that happened to be there, and the figure with the clothesline adds a touch of life. As a finished picture it would be slight; as a record of seeing it is fresh and attractive."},"nga:150384":{"d":"The view crosses a band of broad, craggy boulders to a bay dotted with boats. The rock tops are caramel brown in sunlight, with rust-brown shadows down their sides and smoke-gray fissures, and a glassy pool at the lower right reflects the pale sky. The water is aquamarine and bottle green, its choppy waves spraying white against the stones. Two people in straw hats stand near the water, one in a red shirt holding a fishing rod; a woman in rose pink sits nearby looking into the distance. One sailboat sits at the center and white birds fly overhead. The artist signed W.S.H 64 at lower left.","i":"Haseltine's rocky coasts reflect the age's fascination with geology: rocks as records of deep time, drawn with the precision Ruskin advised painters to bring to nature. Newport and Narragansett Bay were then among the most fashionable summer resorts in America, and the foreground figures, a woman in a pink dress and an angler, read as visitors rather than toilers. The clarity of light belongs to the American luminist taste for stillness and exact edges, even where the waves break.","c":"The boulders let Haseltine exercise his strongest gift, the patient description of rock surface, with the white spray and glassy pool adding contrast and air. The figures are small, almost incidental, and the picture's drama is geological rather than human. It is crisp, bright and very sure; some may find it cold in its precision, but few American painters of the 1860s matched his observation of rock and water."},"cma:148312":{"d":"A rocky coast of Capri, based, according to the Cleveland Museum, on rocks from the uninhabited eastern end of the island, in an oil on canvas of about 50 by 80 centimeters, dated 1869. Haseltine, born in Philadelphia, favored images of coastal rocks and painted them on both sides of the Atlantic throughout his career; he is best known for his scenes of the Maine and Massachusetts shorelines.","i":"Capri, with its cliffs, grottoes and arches, offered painters a Mediterranean counterpart of the American coast, and the uninhabited eastern end gave Haseltine the raw rock forms he preferred. He often built a finished studio picture from sketches made on the spot, joining accuracy of detail to composed design; the title simply names the island, but the subject is the rock and the light on it. The name Capri may come from a Greek word for wild boar or a Latin one for goat.","c":"A picture built on rock: the strength of Haseltine's work is exactness without dryness, with light modeling each fissure and ledge. By 1869 he was settled in Rome and had the skill and the field studies to make a polished studio picture. The risk is a certain chilliness, the sense of a catalogue of geology; but the clear air and the sea's color keep it alive. A representative, accomplished Haseltine."},"nga:70987":{"d":"The view looks slightly down on a rocky natural arch beside a cave opening, perched on a high grassy cliff above shimmering aquamarine water. The jagged arch, in ash and caramel browns, is lit from the left, with deep shadows in its crevices and across the emerald grass at its base. Serrated rocks poke from the water far below like teeth. At the left a rocky shoreline juts out before mauve mountains on the horizon; minuscule white sails dot the water and tiny white birds fly overhead. Petal-pink clouds line the horizon beneath a turquoise sky. The canvas is large, about 86 by 140 centimeters.","i":"The natural arch of Capri was one of the island's best-known sights, visited by travelers who climbed up for the view. For Haseltine, the arch combined his two passions, geology and Mediterranean light, and the high viewpoint gives a sublime sense of height, with tiny sails and birds as measures of scale. The picture belongs to the Romantic tradition that made the Bay of Naples a destination for painters from Britain, America and Germany.","c":"A big, ambitious Haseltine, among his most successful: a precipitous viewpoint, rocks lit from the left with deep shadow in the crevices, a vast stretch of luminous water and tiny sails and birds that make the scale believable. The color is bright and rich without being gaudy. The weakness is the one that haunts all his work: a fine surface held at a distance, impressive rather than intimate. It belongs among the best American views of the Mediterranean coast."},"met:11020":{"d":"The domed Venetian church of Santa Maria della Salute, seen at sunset across the lagoon, with a sailboat and other boats on the water and a sky of clouds colored by the setting sun. The canvas is about 58 by 91 centimeters, and the date is given only as 1870 to 1885, the years in which William Stanley Haseltine, an American painter who divided his time between Rome and other Italian places, was painting Venice. The recorded subjects are sky, cloud, church, sunset, sailboat, lagoon, boats and buildings.","i":"The Salute was built by Baldassare Longhena after the plague of 1630, in fulfillment of the Venetian Senate's vow to the Virgin, and its great white domes and volutes are among the most recognizable silhouettes on the Grand Canal. By the later nineteenth century Venice was a magnet for foreign painters, who sought its light, water and decaying splendor; sunset views were especially prized. Haseltine's contribution is the clarity of a rocks-and-water training applied to a city built on water.","c":"Haseltine's cool exactness serves the lagoon well: water, boats and distant dome tend to be described with a clarity that keeps the sunset from turning to postcard, though the subject was already a cliché by 1880. It lacks the shimmering dissolution of Turner's Venice and the dash of Sargent's, yet it has a calm, lucid beauty. A competent, attractive entry among the many Venetian sunsets of the age."},"met:13368":{"d":"A small oil study on paper, about 23 by 38 centimeters, with plants and a landscape among the recorded subjects; it is undated, and was probably made for the artist's own reference. William Stanley Haseltine, like other American painters of the Ruskinian generation, made field studies in oil to record rocks, plants and light, which he later used in the studio. The title, Study from Nature, is its own description.","i":"Oil sketches on paper were the working notebooks of nineteenth-century landscape painters: quick to make outdoors, light to carry and useful as records of specific effects. The Ruskinian ideal of truth to nature encouraged painters to look hard at plants and rocks and to record what they saw before it changed. Such studies were usually not exhibited, and often remained in the artist's studio until later, when they were valued for their directness.","c":"A study is judged by what it records, and the best of Haseltine's are crisp and vivid: a few touches suffice for plant, ground and light. They show a painter more sensitive and spontaneous than his polished finished pictures sometimes allow. This one is slight, small and quick; its value lies in its honesty rather than in any ambition, and it offers a glimpse of the observing eye behind the studio paintings."},"met:437184":{"d":"A nude woman is shown as the personification of a spring, La Source, on a canvas of about 81 by 65 centimeters, dated 1738. The museum classes the picture with Nattier's portraits, and he was the specialist of the portrait historié, in which a real sitter, usually an aristocratic woman, appears in the guise of a goddess, nymph or allegorical figure. Whether a particular woman posed is not recorded; either way, it is a refined French rococo nude, lightly erotic and decorously mythological.","i":"In eighteenth-century France the spring or fountain nymph was a figure of purity and fertility, and a naked figure with water offered a graceful excuse for a nude. Nattier's contemporaries, who knew the conventions, read such pictures as elegant fantasies, and often as flattering compliments to the sitter, whose beauty is likened to the nymph's. A picture like this belonged in a salon or private cabinet, where it signaled wit and taste rather than moral instruction.","c":"Nattier's gift was to make powdered, rosy flattery persuasive: pearly flesh, soft contours, fashionable prettiness, all painted with silken facility. It is a charming nude, but the charm is skin-deep; his goddesses are more elegant than convincing, and the mythology serves chiefly to supply a pose. Beside Boucher's more energetic and voluptuous nudes it is restrained, polite and a little cool. Delicate rococo painting at its most decorous."},"met:437185":{"d":"A woman of the French nobility in a vertical portrait of 1744, painted in oil on canvas and measuring about 83 by 65 centimeters, a format that suits a head-and-torso likeness. The title names her as the Marquise d'Argence, born in 1714, which would make her about thirty at the time. A pearl and a bow are among the accessories recorded for the picture, small marks of the finery Nattier liked to paint. Her pose and dress are not otherwise documented, so this note describes the kind of picture it is: a private commission from a sitter of rank.","i":"A portrait like this stated rank and taste at once. A marquise chose the fashionable Nattier for a likeness that would hang in a Paris town house or pass to relatives, and pearls, ribbons and silks announced wealth without ostentation. At about thirty, the sitter is presented as composed, agreeable and well dressed, which was the whole ambition of the genre in the 1740s. The picture is best read as a record of social position, with the face shaped to the flattering ideal of the day.","c":"Nattier's formula was pleasing and highly salable: smooth modeling, clear color, silk and pearl handled with a light touch, and a face softened toward an ideal. At best the polish is a real pleasure. At worst it makes sitters look alike, so that dress and ornament carry more individuality than the features. A portrait of 1744 belongs to the confident middle of his career, before his mythological costume pieces of the 1750s made the manner even more famous."},"nga:46129":{"d":"A man sits on an ornate chair and looks off to one side, in a seated portrait about 138 by 105 centimeters. He has curly gray hair and wears a brown coat trimmed with gold and gray fur over a frilled white shirt. One hand holds a large open book, and the other rests on a stack of volumes. Behind him, blue drapery partly hides shelves of scientific equipment and glass jars, and a dark gray column rises at the left. The setting makes his learning and possessions as much the subject as his face.","i":"Joseph Bonnier de la Mosson was a wealthy Parisian collector whose cabinet of natural history and scientific instruments was among the most celebrated in the city. The books, jars and apparatus around him are the point: the portrait presents him as a man of science and curiosity, not simply of money. Cabinets like his were often shown to visiting scholars and gentlemen, and a portrait in the setting advertised the owner's learning and standing.","c":"The picture works as a portrait of possessions. Nattier, better known for sweet-faced women in satin, borrows the column and drapery of state portraiture for a private man of science, and the novelty lies in what the drapery half conceals. The brown coat and fur are handled with his usual care, but the sitter is more a presence than a character, and the shelves are too discreetly placed to give the picture real narrative force. Informative rather than inspired."},"met:437181":{"d":"A mother and her daughter share a lady's dressing room in this large double portrait, painted in 1749 in oil on canvas and measuring about 146 by 114 centimeters. The museum's record lists lace, a casket, a dressing table and the girl among the elements of the scene, which places the pair in the domestic world of the toilette and its jewelry rather than a formal salon. Mother and child are the subject, and the fabrics and accessories are probably painted as attentively as the faces. The size makes it a prominent family picture, not a small keepsake.","i":"Portraits of mothers with their children grew common in mid-eighteenth-century France, as affection within the family became a fashionable subject. Setting the pair at a dressing table links the daughter's training in dress, jewelry and manners to her mother's example, and the costly casket and lace announce the household's means. Such pictures are usually read as portraits of values as much as of faces: the mother who teaches, the girl who learns.","c":"The generous size lets Nattier do what he does best, the rendering of lace, silk and ornament with a smooth, light touch. A double portrait also gives the composition a structure, two heads and a play of gesture, that his single sitters often lack. The limit is familiar: the pleasure is on the surface, and the affection between mother and child is staged as gracefully as the dressing table. It is a sociable, well-mannered picture rather than a searching one."},"cma:122347":{"d":"A woman in the guise of Diana, goddess of the hunt, in a portrait of 1752 painted in oil on canvas, about 100 by 80 centimeters. She carries the goddess's attributes: a bow, a quiver and a leopard skin. For many years she was identified as Madame de Pompadour, and later as Louis XV's daughter Henriette, but she resembles neither, and her name is now lost. The picture is a fashionable costume portrait, in which a living woman is presented as a classical divinity.","i":"Nattier specialized in portraits of women as goddesses, a taste of the Rococo court that let sitters borrow the grace of antiquity. Diana stood for youth, independence and the hunt, so the role flattered a lively, athletic beauty. The mythological dress is partly a game: viewers knew the sitter was a living woman, and part of the pleasure was recognizing her under the costume. That her name has since been lost shows how easily such idealized faces blur.","c":"The picture shows Nattier's skill at fitting a recognizable type to a fashionable fiction. The attributes are neat and legible, and the skin and fabrics have the pearly finish his clients wanted. The weakness is the one that confused later scholars: a face so idealized that it could belong to several women, or none. As a document of a particular woman it fails, which is partly the genre's point; as a mythological portrait it is a good one."},"cma:125951":{"d":"A portrait of a woman, painted around 1748 in oil on canvas and measuring about 82 by 65 centimeters. She was long thought to be Madame Henriette de France, a daughter of Louis XV, but that identification is no longer accepted and the sitter remains unknown. The museum's record gives no costume or setting, so the picture is best described by type: a refined court portrait of the kind Nattier produced for the French aristocracy, with the sitter's features shaped to an ideal of beauty.","i":"Nattier was a favorite of Louis XV and held a lucrative post as court painter to Queen Marie Leszczynska, daughter of the exiled king of Poland, so a portrait of this kind was readily taken for royalty. The confusion over who is shown is itself informative: his sitters tend to conform to an idealized notion of female beauty, with features that look alike from one woman to the next, so a name often has to come from documents rather than from the face.","c":"The attraction is Nattier's smooth, flattering finish, which made him fervently sought after by French aristocratic women. The drawback is just as clear: the museum itself cannot say who is shown, because the face is idealized rather than individual. A good Nattier gives pleasure through its surfaces but asks little of the viewer. Beside the lively, sharply observed pastel portraits of Maurice Quentin de La Tour, made in the same years, it seems more decorative than searching."},"met:437182":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed woman, painted in 1753 in oil on canvas, about 80 by 64 centimeters. The museum's record lists only the sitter and the portrait itself, with no costume, jewelry or setting described, so a close description would be guesswork. What can be said is that it dates from the height of Nattier's fashionable success, and that its size, a vertical canvas of modest scale, is a usual one for a private likeness of a lady.","i":"With no name attached, the picture has to be read as a type: a lady of means, shown in the fashionable dress of the early 1750s, painted by a portraitist much sought after by aristocratic women. For its first owners it was a record of beauty and station, to be kept in the family or exchanged among friends. Today it shows how a likeness of this kind can survive while the sitter's identity is lost.","c":"Nattier's usual virtues are smooth modeling, a light touch with fabric and an agreeable balance of face and dress, and a picture from his busiest years can be expected to show them. It cannot be rated against his best-known works from a bare record, but it shows how consistent his output was: the same polish on a modest canvas as on a large costume piece. What it lacks is a story, a name or a strongly individual presence."},"nga:32691":{"d":"Madame Le Fèvre de Caumartin appears as Hebe, cupbearer of the gods, shown from the knees up and seated beside an eagle on a soft bank of cloud. White and pink roses and pearls are pinned into her pale hair, and a small bouquet nestles in the bow of her chemise. Pale blue silk, fastened with a gold pin, falls in sheeny folds over her lap, and she tips a silver-gilt pitcher over a bowl. The sky behind her shifts from golden yellow to blue. Blended strokes soften her face and hands, and the signature and date, 1753, are at lower left.","i":"Hebe, goddess of youth, served nectar to the Olympian gods, and the eagle at her side is the bird of her father Jupiter. To be painted as Hebe was to be praised as young, fresh and charming, and the sitter's own name in the title kept the compliment personal. Portraits in mythological costume were popular with French society in the 1750s, and Nattier, who painted many of them, had the lightness of touch that the game required.","c":"This is Nattier in his most polished vein. The cloud, the pearls and the satin are delicate, and the color, from blush pink through pale blue to the warm sky, is chosen to flatter, with a soft blending that suits the subject. The price is a certain sugariness: the goddess is a pretty woman in a masquerade, and the picture asks for admiration rather than thought. Among his mythological portraits of the 1750s it is a typical and accomplished example."},"met:437183":{"d":"A woman dressed as Diana, goddess of the hunt, in a large portrait of 1756, painted in oil on canvas and measuring about 137 by 105 centimeters. A bow and arrow identify the goddess, as in other costume portraits by Nattier. The sitter is Madame Bergeret de Frouville, named in the title, who plays the part for the picture as a lady might for a fashionable masquerade. The larger canvas leaves room for the figure and her attributes, which gives the portrait more presence than a bust-length likeness would.","i":"Diana was a favorite role for aristocratic women in the 1750s: a virgin huntress, athletic, independent and beautiful, she let a lady show her grace and spirit without immodesty. Nattier's mythological portraits, which dress living sitters in the costume of antiquity, are an elegant French version of an old tradition of portraying women as goddesses. The bow and arrow tell the viewer which role is being played, and the title tells who is playing it.","c":"At this scale Nattier can give the pose some dignity, and the Diana role suits his taste for supple, flowing costume. As so often with him, the mythology is a costume rather than an idea: the picture is concerned with elegance rather than action. It lacks the wit that lifts the best costume portraits, but the finish is assured, and the pairing of a living sitter with a classical role is easy and convincing."},"nga:110114":{"d":"A working quarry is shown with men at several levels: some stand among cut blocks of stone, others work on scaffolding, and two horses are harnessed to a cart. A sloping embankment topped by a fence rises beside rough stone walls. The brushwork is precise in the textures of stone and timber, while the sky is blended in soft strokes of muted blues and grays above the earthy tones of the ground. It is an oil on canvas of about 71 by 91 centimeters.","i":"Nanterre lies in the western suburbs of Paris, and quarries in the Paris region supplied building stone to a growing capital, so a quarry is a modern, working kind of landscape. Cazin, a French painter who later became known for poetic twilight scenes, here chooses a plain, workmanlike subject and names its owner, Monsieur Pascal, as if documenting a known local site.","c":"An unusual subject for Cazin, and a vigorous one: the human activity and the stacked masonry give the canvas a documentary realism that recalls the Realist tradition of his time. The composition is a little crowded and the figures are subordinate to the terrain, but the sky is handsomely handled. Anyone who knows Cazin only for his dusk landscapes will find this plain-spoken and earthier."},"nga:177550":{"d":"The museum record offers no description of what this canvas shows beyond its title, Landscape, and the keywords 'exterior' and 'landscape', so its specific motifs cannot be reported with confidence. It is a modest oil on canvas, about 41 by 56 centimeters, dated only 'after 1876'. In that period Cazin's landscapes of northern France typically combined a low horizon, a broad sky and a few rural buildings or haystacks under soft, changing light.","i":"From about the late 1870s Cazin moved away from his early Realism toward a quieter, more lyrical landscape, often composed from memory, a method he had learned from his teacher Horace Lecoq de Boisbaudran, who trained pupils to retain what they saw and recall it later. A plain title like this one invites the viewer to read the picture as a mood more than a place.","c":"A generic title often goes with a generic effect, and Cazin's late landscapes can be pleasing without being distinctive: tonal harmony, quiet, little detail. If this canvas follows the pattern, it belongs among the numerous gentle landscapes that made his reputation, and it rewards attention for its softness more than for any particular invention. A quiet picture, more atmosphere than statement."},"nga:177918":{"d":"A low, warm glow sits on the horizon beneath soft, loosely painted clouds; the museum reads it as a sun partly visible, though the title speaks of a moon. In front lie a dirt path, a stone wall, a wooden fence and a haystack, with a small red building far off. The brushwork is rustic and broad in the clouds and the haystack, and the palette is dominated by warm tones and muted greens. It is an upright canvas, about 41 by 33 centimeters.","i":"Cazin painted the French countryside at its quietest hours, and a haystack under a low moon is a motif of that mood. The haystack, a working object of the farm, becomes a dark mass against the glow, almost a symbol of the harvest finished. Contemporary viewers admired such pictures for their feeling more than for their facts: they were meant to evoke memory and calm.","c":"The small size suits the subject. The dusky, muted glow behind the haystack and path is quietly atmospheric, and the warm tones are kept restrained. What is missing is a stronger design: the wall, fence and path pull in several directions, and no single form commands the picture. A pleasing little twilight, a minor example of the poetic mode that made Cazin popular."},"nga:194982":{"d":"A field fills the foreground, with a path running through it along which a figure walks toward a house and nearby haystacks. The sky shifts from pink and peach near the horizon to soft blue above, and in the distance tiny specks appear on the horizon, perhaps boats at sea. Soft brushwork and a warm palette give the scene a tranquil mood. It is an oil on canvas, about 65 by 82 centimeters, and the walking figure is its only human presence.","i":"Cazin was born near Boulogne-sur-Mer, and the coast of the Boulonnais supplied him with farms, dunes and sea light. A lone walker approaching a house at dawn or dusk is a gentle narrative of homecoming, typical of his poetic, memory-based landscapes. The title and the haystacks fix the setting as a farm by the sea, but the picture does not identify the place.","c":"The pink-to-blue sky is the painting's accomplishment: the gradation is soft and believable, and it floods the field and the house with a subdued warmth. The composition is simple to the point of blandness, with the path and the lone walker doing the minimum of narrative work. It shows why Cazin's tender evening pictures found an audience, and also why they were later thought a little sweet."},"nga:35085":{"d":"A tan path curves up through long green grass, dark moss and tall white, pink and blue flowers toward a wooden windmill on a hill, its sails turned to the left against a cloudy sky. At the lower left corner a woman and child sit by a wooden fence, and on the far side of it a brown-and-white cow grazes. The land rises gradually to the mill and fades into haze at the horizon. Warm pinks, browns and greens and soft brushwork give the whole a hazy quality. It is a small oil on wood, about 41 by 32 centimeters.","i":"Windmills were a familiar sight on the farmland of northern France, where Cazin lived and worked, and he returned to the motif more than once. The seated woman and child, with the cow beyond the fence, turn a landscape into a small scene of rural rest. Such quiet, remembered pastoral was the kind of picture on which Cazin's reputation rested. The museum dates the panel as probably after 1884.","c":"The curving path is a good device, drawing the eye uphill to the mill, and the hazy handling gives the flowers and grass a pleasing softness. But everything is gentle to the point of sweetness: the mill, which should anchor the picture, is little more than a silhouette, and the figures are generic. A charming small panel, typical of the Cazin pictures that American collectors bought eagerly."},"nga:178063":{"d":"The museum record gives no description of this canvas beyond its title and the keywords 'landscape' and 'agriculture', so its details cannot be reported with confidence. The title names two motifs: a large windmill, the working center of a farming landscape, and a rainbow, one of the sky effects that Cazin liked to catch. Dated 1888, it measures about 89 by 117 centimeters, a sizable oil on canvas for a landscape of his.","i":"A rainbow is a sign of changing weather, and a windmill depends on the wind to do its work, so the pairing suits a country picture in which sky and labor meet. Both motifs have a long history in landscape painting, from Dutch windmill pictures to Constable's rainbows. In Cazin's hands such effects were meant less to describe a particular day than to set a mood of calm and memory.","c":"A rainbow is difficult to paint without sentiment, and Cazin's reputation as a poet of the countryside suggests that the risk of sweetness is real here. His strength was atmosphere, not structure, so the likelier success is the sky and the likelier weakness the mill itself. Judged by his work of the decade, this is probably a competent, softly lyrical picture of the kind that gave him a large public."},"nga:178282":{"d":"The museum record offers no description of this canvas beyond its title and the keywords 'landscape' and 'exterior scene', so the figures and the setting cannot be reported with confidence. The title points to tired travelers on or beside a road, presumably small figures in a broad, quiet countryside. Dated 1888, it is an oil on canvas of about 66 by 81 centimeters, a middle-sized format for Cazin's landscapes.","i":"Cazin often cast wanderers in his landscapes. His best-known figure pictures, such as Hagar and Ishmael, set solitary travelers in empty country under a wide sky, giving a modern, melancholy cast to a Bible story. A title such as Weary Wayfarers, without a scriptural name, keeps that sense of pilgrimage while leaving the travelers anonymous, so that they stand for everyone on the road.","c":"The title promises pathos, and Cazin's gift for twilight and quiet distance makes him well suited to deliver it. In his best work the sentiment is held in check by plain observation; in his weaker pictures it softens into prettiness. This canvas belongs to the type of landscape with figures on which his American popularity was built, and it should be weighed against both possibilities."},"met:436290":{"d":"A bouquet of mixed flowers stands beside a stone vase in this small still life, painted in 1786 in oil on a wood panel about 51 by 38 centimeters. The museum's record lists the flowers and the vase only, so the individual blooms are not named here. It is a cabinet picture of a type with a long history in Flanders and Holland, made to be hung at eye level in a private room, where the delicacy of the petals and the contrast with the hard stone could be enjoyed at leisure. A near twin of similar size and date is in the same collection.","i":"Flower pieces carried layered meanings in the Netherlandish tradition: the beauty of blooms at their peak, the painter's skill in matching nature, and a quiet reminder that all of it fades. By 1786 the moral reading was fainter than the decorative one, and such pictures were bought as elegant furnishing for a house. Painters like Peter Faes supplied new pieces in the tradition of earlier Dutch and Flemish specialists, whose work was still highly prized.","c":"The pairing of soft petals with a hard stone vase is a long-tested device, and a painter schooled in the tradition would know how to make the contrast pay. Set beside the great flower painters of the century before, such as Jan Davidsz. de Heem or Rachel Ruysch, a late piece like this is more a competent echo than a new idea. The interest lies in arrangement and finish rather than feeling: pleasing, careful and a little cool."},"met:436291":{"d":"A flower piece in which blooms fill a stone vase, painted in 1786 in oil on a wood panel about 50 by 38 centimeters. It matches the size and date of Flowers by a Stone Vase, and the two were presumably conceived as companions for the same room. The museum's record lists only a vase and flowers, so the species are not named here. The panel support, small format and careful finish place it in the tradition of the Flemish cabinet picture.","i":"Pairs of flower pieces were common furnishing for a late-eighteenth-century house, often hung on either side of a door or mantel. A bouquet in a vase offers abundance in a form that can be admired in any season, and the best examples remind the viewer, in the old Netherlandish way, that the beauty of a cut flower is brief. A stone vase, solid and carved, sets the fragile blooms against something that lasts.","c":"The motif is old and the genre well rehearsed, and Faes adds mainly a late-century neatness. He has none of the drama of Ruysch or the glassy brilliance of Jan van Huysum, and in this pair the interest lies more in arrangement and finish than in invention. Pleasant, accomplished, a little cool: a respectable late chapter in the history of Flemish flower painting rather than a surprising one."},"met:437285":{"d":"A small devotional panel, about 29 by 24 centimeters, showing the boy Saint John the Baptist in profile. It was painted around 1480 to 1482 in tempera and oil on wood, a mixed technique then being tried by Florentine painters and a sign of the experiments of the young Piero di Cosimo. Little more is recorded of the picture, so its costume and background are not described here. A profile view of a child saint suited a small image for private prayer or a family room.","i":"John the Baptist, the forerunner who announced Christ, was Florence's patron saint, and images of him as a child were common in Florentine homes, often with a reed cross and a camel-hair garment recalling his later life in the desert. The profile view, much used in Florentine portraiture of the period, gives the saint the presence of a real boy, and it makes the picture a quiet image of a child at prayer.","c":"Among Piero's works this is a small early picture, interesting chiefly as a point of departure: the inventiveness of the mythological panels of the 1490s lies still ahead. Because it is a profile, the form depends on contour, a Florentine strength, and the painter owed his name to his teacher Cosimo Rosselli. It is a modest piece; the mixture of oil with tempera is the more telling feature, pointing toward the soft handling of his mature work."},"nga:505":{"d":"Two women stand clasping hands on a low platform between two seated old men, against a deep landscape. At left Mary, in a gold-edged ultramarine robe over a ruby dress, gazes into the eyes of the older Elizabeth, who wears a wine-red cloak and a white headcloth and raises her free hand. In the foreground at left, Saint Nicholas in scarlet reads a book, with three gold balls beside him; at right Saint Anthony Abbot, in a green cape, wears round spectacles and writes with a quill, a bell and a crutch at his feet. Behind them a hill town swarms with soldiers and mothers in a massacre of children at right, while a Nativity appears in shadow at left.","i":"The Visitation is the meeting of the pregnant Mary with her older cousin Elizabeth, who is carrying John the Baptist. Painted about 1489–90, it was commissioned for the chapel of San Niccolò in Santo Spirito, Florence, held by the Capponi family. Nicholas is recognized by the three gold balls that allude to his charity, Anthony Abbot by his bell and the pig that accompanied him in legend. The Massacre of the Innocents and the Nativity in the background place the meeting within the whole story of Christ's birth and the danger that followed it. Vasari singled out the Saint Anthony with spectacles as a particularly good detail.","c":"A young painter's ambitious altarpiece, crowded with invention. The strongest passage is the meeting of the two women, pressed so close that their cloaks form one shape against a pale sky, with gesture and gaze carrying the emotion. The two foreground saints, with their lined faces, spectacles and quill, are equally fine as studies of old age. The background narratives are painted with such fascination that they compete with the foreground. It is more interesting than harmonious, an early sign of the oddity that would mark Piero's career."},"met:437283":{"d":"A long, dark panel shows a forest hunt in a mythical age. Flames burn among the trees in the upper left while lions, a bear, deer and other animals tangle and flee below. Nearly naked hunters in the middle grapple with them; goat-legged satyrs swing clubs; a pale grey centaur rears between the tree trunks; and a man carries a small animal in his arms. At the right more satyrs fight, and a creature lies fallen on the ground. Birds stream up from the burning woods into a clear sky. Slender foreground trunks divide the episodes like columns. It is painted in tempera and oil on a long panel, now transferred to Masonite, about 70 by 170 centimetres.","i":"Painted about 1494 to 1500, this panel and its companion, The Return from the Hunt, are read by the Met as scenes from the early history of humankind, inspired by the Roman poet Lucretius, whose rediscovered poem On the Nature of Things explained the world by natural rather than divine causes. Here men, satyrs and centaurs use a forest fire to drive and kill game; in the companion panel they carry the catch to shore. The long, narrow shapes suggest the panels were set into the wall panelling or furniture of a room, perhaps a study. They have traditionally been linked to the palace of the Florentine merchant Francesco del Pugliese, though that connection is uncertain.","c":"Piero treats a prehistoric brawl with the seriousness of a history painting and the curiosity of a naturalist, and the result is one of the odd, memorable pictures of the Florentine Renaissance. The animals are drawn with relish, the burning wood is a small marvel, and the figures have a lean, scratchy energy that sets Piero apart from his more polished contemporaries. But the panel is crowded and hard to read, the dark paint is heavy, and the action never settles into a clear focus. It rewards slow looking more than a first glance."},"met:437284":{"d":"A long, narrow panel, about 70 by 169 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil around 1494 to 1500. Hunters, nude women, satyrs and a centaur share a landscape of trees and hills with a sea and boats beyond; pigs are among the animals. It is not a scene of daily life but an imagined age before cities, when people and half-human woodland creatures lived side by side. The wide format suggests that it was probably made to be set into the paneling or furniture of a room.","i":"The panel is usually grouped with others by Piero showing an imagined early history of humankind, drawn from ancient authors such as Lucretius, Vitruvius and Ovid, who described how people first hunted, made fire and built shelters. Such cycles were made for Florentine households, probably as decoration for a room, and let learned patrons enjoy classical ideas in a playful, pictorial form. The exact program and the patron are not settled.","c":"Piero's gift for the odd and the tender is on show: satyrs and centaurs treated as plausible country folk, a landscape that carries the eye to the sea, and a freshness in the small figures. The drawing is lively rather than refined, but the invention more than makes up for it. Few painters of the 1490s imagined such a world, and fewer made it seem so natural. It is among the most distinctive pictures of Florentine painting of its decade."},"nga:301":{"d":"A nearly nude winged woman, wrapped in a crimson cloak, lightly holds the reins of a gray horse that rears toward her. They stand on a small oval of gray rock covered with mustard-yellow growth. She carries a short branch of spiky leaves, and her honey-brown hair is braided around her temples. A mermaid with two tails swims along the lower edge, and beyond her, pale blue headlands, birds and a sailing boat melt into a milky sky. The horse has a heavy body and a strangely small head and tail.","i":"The meaning of this strange picture is not firmly established, and it is listed simply as Allegory. A winged woman checking a rearing horse suggests the control of an unruly force, and the mermaid, a traditional sign of temptation, hints at passion or danger in the water. Readings such as the mastery of the passions or the taming of fortune are possible, but any single interpretation is a guess. Piero's taste for odd, myth-like inventions is well attested.","c":"The picture is hard to dismiss and harder to love. The dainty winged figure, the pale landscape and the distant boat are handled with a delicacy that owes something to Netherlandish painting known in Florence, and the oddities, the horse's small head and tail, the mermaid, make it feel more like a dream than a design. The drawing of the horse is the weak point. As an expression of Piero's eccentric invention, it is typical and compelling."},"nga:515":{"d":"In this round painting, a kneeling Mary and an angel look down at the newborn Christ, who lies naked on a slate-blue cloth with his head propped on a sheaf of wheat; a pink rose lies at his feet. The infant Saint John the Baptist, standing at left with a staff topped by a thin cross, clasps his hands in prayer. The scene is set in the ruins of a building, with an old man descending a stair, angels in the clouds, an ox and ass, and small travelers on a road leading to hills and a distant town.","i":"A ruined building is a traditional setting for the Nativity and was often read as the old order giving way to the new. The wheat on which the child lies points to the Eucharist, the bread of the Mass, and to Bethlehem, which means house of bread. Saint John, who will baptize Christ and call him the Lamb of God, joins the scene as the child's first companion. Round pictures, or tondi, like this were made for private rooms and devotion at home.","c":"Piero fills a round frame with a crowded, almost narrative scene and keeps it legible: the group at the center is calm, and the background episodes recede along a road toward a hazy horizon. The strong colors and the cool, enameled finish are very much his. The wide-set eyes and short, full mouths are a matter of taste; some will find them naive, others touching. An inventive treatment of a familiar subject, if less commanding than the best Florentine tondi."},"met:436806":{"d":"A very small panel, about 27 by 32 centimeters, showing a kitchen with a man, a woman and vegetables, painted around 1642 to 1644. It belongs to Kalf's early career, when he painted humble interiors before becoming the master of the luxury still life. The museum's record lists the figures, the room and the vegetables, so the picture can be understood as a scene of everyday household work in which the produce and the room probably matter as much as the people.","i":"Kitchen scenes were a staple of Dutch and Flemish painting, valued for their display of vegetables and pots and the routines of a household. Kalf's early interiors show peasants and servants among such objects, and the still-life element is a sign of the specialty he would later perfect. Such small panels were bought by collectors who enjoyed close observation of ordinary things, sometimes with a moral note on thrift or diligence.","c":"Kalf's later still lifes are far better known, so this early kitchen is of interest chiefly as the root of his mature work: the same attention to the surfaces of copper, ceramic and vegetables, in a modest, earthy key before his silvery gold. He was never as fluent a painter of people as of objects, and the small panel's appeal is its intimacy rather than its drama. A valuable footnote to a great still-life career."},"met:436805":{"d":"A still life of 1659, painted in oil on canvas, about 58 by 51 centimeters, gathered around a Chinese porcelain bowl of the Wanli period (1573 to 1620), with fruit, a lemon and glassware. The museum's record lists a lemon, fruit and a bowl, and the title adds the glass. It is a pronkstilleven, a showy still life of precious things, in which porcelain from China, imported by Dutch merchants, stands for the trade and wealth of the Dutch Republic.","i":"Wanli porcelain, shipped to Europe in large quantity by the Dutch East India Company, was a luxury and a status symbol, and Kalf made it a favorite subject. A lemon and ripe fruit are traditional signs of the senses and of transience, since fruit spoils and glass breaks. Pictures of this kind were prized by wealthy Dutch collectors, who saw in them both their prosperity and a gentle warning that worldly possessions pass.","c":"By 1659 Kalf had settled into the mature manner that made his name. As a rule he sets such objects against a dark ground and lights them from one side, so that porcelain, fruit and glass glow from within, and the contrast of textures gives him ample room for display. Working at full power, he outdoes most Dutch rivals in color and in atmosphere. The pleasure is cool and visual, and the arrangement is almost too perfect."},"nga:12206":{"d":"A blue-and-white porcelain bowl tips onto a rumpled patterned rug on a tabletop, against a black background, in a vertical canvas about 64 by 54 centimeters. Strong light picks out a bright yellow lemon with a long peel curling toward the table, a small orange fruit with a white blossom on its stem, and two peaches. Behind the fruit stand a slender carafe and two glasses holding pale gold wine, one with a wide, scalloped cup. The contrast between the lit objects and the dark ground gives each surface its sparkle.","i":"Kalf's still lifes belong to the Dutch pronk tradition, in which rare and costly objects are gathered to show wealth and skill. The porcelain recalls the trade with China, the lemon and peaches are luxuries on a Dutch table, and the glass catches the light like jewels. The half-peeled lemon, a favorite of Dutch painters, invites the eye to enjoy the glisten of its pulp while hinting, like all fruit, at the passing of pleasure.","c":"This is a clear example of why Kalf is counted the finest Dutch painter of the pronk still life. The light is exact, the surfaces are distinct, and the black ground makes the lit fruit glow. The peeled lemon, with its curl of rind and glistening pulp, is a set piece of virtuosity. If there is a fault, it is the self-conscious perfection of the arrangement: the pleasure is cool and visual rather than emotional."},"cma:138359":{"d":"Valuable objects crowd the corner of a stone table: a silver platter, delicate glassware, a thick rumpled carpet and a Chinese porcelain bowl holding peaches and an orange. Kalf painted it in 1663 on a canvas about 60 by 50 centimeters. He used quick dots, lines and dabs of paint for the highlights, so that glass, metal and fruit sparkle against their shadows. It is a pronkstilleven, the Dutch ostentatious still life, and the wineglass of the title is one of several surfaces that catch the light.","i":"The pieces on the table are more than display. Silver, glass, carpet and porcelain stand for the wealth and global reach of the Dutch trading empire, and they also let the painter show that he can reproduce every kind of surface. In 1797 the German poet Goethe wrote that Kalf's pictures show in what sense art is superior to nature, and that, given a choice between the golden vessels and the picture, he would choose the picture.","c":"Few painters have made light behave so convincingly. The sparkle is built of tiny, calculated touches rather than smooth glazing, which separates Kalf from the more polished finish of the fine painters of Leiden. The arrangement is crowded but not confused, and the stone corner gives it stability. It is a superb demonstration of technique; what it lacks, as most pronk pictures do, is any warmth beyond the pleasure of looking."},"nga:195862":{"d":"About a dozen women and men harvest a field of rapeseed under a gray-clouded sky. In the foreground a woman in a white blouse with rolled sleeves, teal apron and brown skirt stands in profile, holding a large round sieve from which black seed pours onto a cloth; beside her a kneeling woman scoops the seed into a bucket while a girl watches, a baby or doll in her lap. Behind them a woman binds stalks into a blue sack, another carries a bundle on her head, and men swing long scythes. Pine-green trees, houses and barns enclose the field.","i":"Breton signed the picture with the name of Courrières, his native village in northern France, and the scene is drawn from the farm life he knew. Colza, or rapeseed, was grown for its oil, long used in lamps, so the black seed spilling from the sieve is the harvest's valuable product. Peasant subjects were fashionable in mid-century France, and Breton's pictures presented rural labor as dignified, orderly and timeless, a view that appealed to urban buyers and that later critics found idealized.","c":"Breton's strength is the grouping: the winnowing woman, the kneeling gatherer and the watching child form a clear, rhythmic cluster, and the black seed against pale cloth is an arresting touch. The figures are classically poised even in labor, and the earring and clean aprons point to idealization more than to hardship. Beside Millet's rougher peasants, Breton is prettier and more reassuring, but he draws and composes with real skill, and the work is typical of him."},"met:435773":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 71 by 128 centimeters, painted in 1868, showing women at work weeding on a farm. The museum's record lists farms, women and working as the themes, but gives no details of costume, landscape or light. The painter, a native of Courrières in northern France, specialized in such scenes of rural labor, drawn from the countryside he grew up in and presented with a dignity usually reserved for grander subjects.","i":"Breton was one of the leading painters of rural life at the Paris Salon. He showed field labor as a timeless, almost sacred rhythm, with strong women as graceful as classical figures. This idealized approach appealed to urban buyers who wanted the countryside as a calm, healthy contrast to city life, and it set Breton apart from more critical contemporaries such as Courbet, who painted peasants in a harsher light.","c":"Breton's strength is the dignified grouping of figures against a broad sky, and a wide frame suits his rhythmic arrangements. The weakness, which later critics have pointed out, is sweetness: his peasants are cleaner and statelier than the labor warrants, so that hardship becomes pageant. Beside Millet's more somber field workers, it is more pleasing than true. Within its aims it is well made, and it records a taste for rural idealism that was widely shared."},"nga:226393":{"d":"On a very large canvas, about 1.6 by 2.5 meters, a young woman lies on her stomach on a brown rocky ledge at the sea's edge, resting her head on one hand and gazing outward. She wears a pale green shirt with blue sleeves, a brown skirt and a white cloth on her head; her feet are bare. Beside her lie a red cloth and a pointed wooden stick wrapped in white, perhaps cloth or feathers. Behind her the sea shines turquoise and green, flecked with small white waves, under a pale sky of puffy clouds, and faint gray headlands rise in the distance.","i":"Breton, who made his name with scenes of field labor in his native Pas-de-Calais, increasingly painted single peasant women in open landscapes, treating them as figures of reverie. Here the girl's pose, her bare feet and her face turned toward the horizon invite a mood of daydreaming or longing, perhaps for someone at sea, though the picture offers no such story. In the manner of the time, the peasant stands for a simple, natural life set against the nineteenth-century city.","c":"The scale is ambitious, and the turquoise sea with its crisp white wave edges is vividly seen, brighter and cleaner than the light of Breton's harvest scenes. The girl's pose is relaxed and convincing, though the idealized, almost classical figure hardly suggests a working peasant's life. It is the contrast between brilliant open color and a rather staged model that defines the picture: attractive, even moving, but more decorative than observed."},"met:435774":{"d":"A young peasant girl absorbed in knitting, in a canvas of about 58 by 47 centimeters painted around 1870. The museum's record lists a girl, work, knitting and needlework, so this is a single-figure study of rural industry rather than a commissioned likeness. She is one of the types Breton painted repeatedly: a young woman of his native Artois shown in everyday occupation, her plain task given a touch of poetry.","i":"Knitting was a common task for girls in rural France, done while minding animals or resting, and in art it signals diligence, modesty and thrift. Breton chose models from his own village and idealized them, posing them with a classical calm. French and American collectors bought such pictures as images of an enduring, virtuous countryside, at a time when many rural traditions were already fading.","c":"A small picture like this shows Breton's formula at its most agreeable: a simple pose, a sweet face and a tidy, finished handling. The question is whether it asks for more than pleasure. The labor is picturesque rather than difficult, and the girl is more a type than a person. Its charm is real, but it belongs to the soft realism that sold well in its day and has since lost ground to the harsher vision of Millet and Courbet."},"met:437921":{"d":"A man is the sole sitter in this oil portrait painted on a wood panel in 1643. The panel measures about 64 by 53 centimeters, ample for a head and upper body, and the title gives no name, so the sitter remains unidentified. It is a portrait of a familiar Dutch type, made for a private house, in which the face carries the picture. Abraham de Vries was a Dutch portraitist of the first half of the seventeenth century.","i":"Unnamed sitters are common in museums because portraits were family property, and names were often lost when pictures were sold. Dutch men of the 1640s sat for portraits as proof of success and respectability, whether they were merchants, officials or ministers. Such panels were often paired with a likeness of the sitter's wife, so this one may once have had a companion. Beyond the face, little in an anonymous portrait can be read with confidence.","c":"De Vries is a minor name beside Frans Hals and Rembrandt, but prosperous Dutch homes were filled by capable painters like him, and their steady, unshowy likenesses are a large part of what the period's portraiture was. With no sitter's name or attributes to lean on, this panel must succeed or fail on the face alone, and that is its test."},"met:695496":{"d":"A bust-length figure of a man, painted in oil on a small canvas about 45 by 38 centimeters in the late 1710s. The title offers a tentative identification as Saint Matthias, the apostle chosen to take the place of Judas, but the question mark shows the museum is not certain, and the record lists only a man and a portrait. The picture falls between a character study and a devotional figure, a type that Piazzetta and other Venetian painters of the period made for collectors.","i":"Matthias, chosen by lot to join the twelve apostles after Judas, is usually shown with an axe or halberd; whether such an attribute appears here is not recorded, which is why the identification carries a question mark. Heads of apostles, prophets and anonymous old men served painters as studies of age, character and expression, and collectors prized them as pictures in their own right. Piazzetta is known to have studied in Bologna, where he absorbed the dark, dramatic lighting that gives such heads their weight.","c":"Piazzetta's character heads are among the most direct things he did: he drops the grand machinery of his altarpieces and concentrates on a single face. The dark ground and warm earth tones typical of his early work give such a head real weight. Whether the sitter is a saint or a model hardly matters; the picture succeeds as a study of presence. It is small and unassuming, and shows his gifts more plainly than many a large altarpiece."},"cma:112626":{"d":"Christ, newly risen and until this moment unrecognized, reveals himself to two astonished disciples as the three sit down to dinner, in a canvas about 108 by 141 centimeters painted around 1720. The hands and the objects on the table are painted with great care, while other parts of the picture, including the background drapery, are left vague, so that the eye is drawn to the moment of recognition. It is a work in the dark, tonal manner of Piazzetta's early maturity.","i":"The story comes from Luke's Gospel. Two disciples walking from Jerusalem to Emmaus on Easter evening meet a stranger and invite him to supper; when he breaks the bread, they recognize the risen Christ, who then vanishes. The breaking of bread links the scene to the Eucharist, and painters from Caravaggio on chose the instant of recognition, letting gesture and startled faces carry the drama.","c":"Piazzetta understood that the drama lies in the hands. By painting hands and tableware with care and letting drapery and background dissolve into shadow, he steers attention to the gesture of revelation and the disciples' shock. The strong chiaroscuro owes something to Bologna, where he studied, and ultimately to Caravaggio. It is theatrical, but the economy of means keeps it from being merely so. A tense, concentrated picture."},"nga:46181":{"d":"A woman in a flowing white dress, a haloed child beside her, sits on a pile of clouds at upper left, surrounded by winged cherubs, two of whom hold a large blue cloth that drapes around the pair. At lower right an old man in a white robe, with a red and gold garment over his shoulders, kneels on dark stairs and gazes up with his hands clasped and outstretched. At lower left a winged figure with its back turned holds a flaming stick, near a white skull and a large red hat. Gold and pale yellow clouds glow against a brown ground.","i":"The kneeling man is Saint Philip Neri, the sixteenth-century Roman priest who founded the Oratory and was known for his visions; the Virgin and Child appear to him, as the title says. The skull and the red hat beside him are probably signs of renunciation: Philip is said to have refused a cardinal's hat, and the skull speaks of vanity and death. Piazzetta also painted an altarpiece of this subject for the church of the Fava in Venice, which the Oratorians served.","c":"Piazzetta handles the vision with restraint. The heavenly group is high and bright, the saint low and dark, and the tension between them carries the story without much gesture. The brown, gold and pale yellow palette is warm and tonal, and the cherubs' blue cloth gives the single clear accent. If there is a limitation, it is a certain heaviness: the weight of the forms gives the apparition an earthbound feel. It is persuasive in composition and in spirit."},"nga:101676":{"d":"A woman seen from the waist up sits at the left of a small canvas, about 39 by 31 centimeters, her body turned to the right and her face toward the viewer. Her eyes are closed, and she seems to be sleeping, her expression neutral. She wears a white cloth over her hair and a black cloak over a black dress. A small black-and-white dog at lower left looks up at her, its paws on her back. At the right a white crucifix with the crowned Christ stands against a blue sky of white and gray clouds, and reddish-brown edges blend into the sky.","i":"Margaret of Cortona, a thirteenth-century penitent who became a Franciscan tertiary, is shown with her usual attribute, the dog that, in her legend, led her to the body of her murdered lover and turned her toward a life of repentance. Her closed eyes are usually read as rapture or a vision of the crucified Christ, who appears at the right. Small devotional pictures like this one were made for private prayer.","c":"A black cloak, a white cloth and a small dog: with so little, Piazzetta makes an intimate image of repentance. The closed eyes and the quiet, watching animal give the picture a tenderness that its small size confirms. The limits are those of its scale. The crucifix is a distant sign rather than a presence, and the picture asks to be read rather than felt. Still, as a cabinet-sized devotional work it is touching and well judged."},"met:437281":{"d":"Saint Christopher, the giant who carried a child across a river, appears with the Infant Christ in an oil on canvas of the 1730s, about 72 by 56 centimeters. The museum's record names only the two figures, so the river, the saint's staff and the child's pose are not described here. The vertical format and moderate scale suit a picture for private devotion. Christopher was the patron saint of travelers, and his image was long believed to protect anyone who looked at it.","i":"In the legend, the giant Christopher served Christ by carrying travelers over a dangerous river. One night he bore a child whose weight grew heavier with each step, until the child revealed himself as Christ, bearing the whole world. The story made Christopher a patron of travelers, and artists pictured the saint bent under a burden he cannot understand until he reaches the far bank.","c":"Piazzetta had a gift for making religious figures solid and human, and a strong man bearing a weight suits his heavy, sculptural manner. His usual strengths, firm drawing, strong chiaroscuro and a sense of physical effort, are what this subject invites. From the 1730s his palette tends to lighten from the dark tonality of his early work. Without the record of its details, the picture is best taken as a representative example: not his most inventive, but a good one."},"cma:132647":{"d":"A tall, narrow oil sketch, about 72 by 38 centimeters, for Piazzetta's Assumption of the Virgin. The Virgin dominates the center, arms outstretched, supported by two angels as she rises toward heaven. Below, saints gather around her tomb, and Saint Peter reaches toward her foot. A divine light falls on her face and on the figures below, who are lit more strongly than in the final altarpiece. She looks upward beyond the frame. The grouping at the center leaves a vast surrounding space, which gives the sketch a sense of monumentality.","i":"The Assumption, the belief that the Virgin was taken body and soul into heaven, was a major Catholic feast and a favorite altarpiece subject. Piazzetta made the sketch for a larger altarpiece commissioned in 1743 for the church of Saint James at Zbraslav, near Prague, and the finished work follows it closely. Peter's reach toward her foot links earth and heaven. Oil sketches like this let a painter settle composition and light before starting the large canvas.","c":"Oil sketches by Piazzetta are rare, which makes this one valuable, and it shows the clarity of his planning: the Virgin's vertical thrust, Peter's reaching arm and the cluster of saints around the tomb are all in place. The stronger light on the lower figures is attractive, perhaps more human than in the final altarpiece. As oil sketches do, it shows a painter's hand directly, and the effect is quick, sure and unfussy."},"nga:894":{"d":"A Dominican friar in a black hood and cloak over a white habit stands in a wooden pulpit at the right of this vertical panel, mouth open and both hands raised, speaking to a crowd below. Seventeen men and women in rose pink, gray, blue, red or black sit or stand at the front and far side of the crowd, and five of them sit with their backs to the viewer along a rail. Between them dozens of heads, many covered with white cloths, fill the space. Above the crowd, a figure holding an open book floats in a ring of winged heads and golden rays.","i":"The friar's habit marks a Dominican, a member of the Order of Preachers, founded to teach the faithful and combat heresy, and his lined face suggests a serious, fiery speaker. The vision above shows the source of his words: a figure with an open book, framed by winged heads and rays of gold light, perhaps standing for divine wisdom or the Virgin. A panel of this size was probably part of an altarpiece predella, where scenes from a saint's life were shown at eye level.","c":"Erri, from Modena, combines the sharp, linear style of the Emilian painters of the 1400s with an eye for crowd behavior: the seated figures with their backs turned in the front row draw the viewer into the congregation, and the bright coloring against gold keeps the small panel vivid. The faces are somewhat repetitive and the space is crowded, but the vision floating above is a charming touch."},"met:437251":{"d":"The Yeni Üsküdar Mosque in Istanbul rises over a street under a blue sky, with a four-wheeled carriage and horses in front, on a tall canvas about 89 by 67 centimeters, dated 1872. Pasini's views of the East typically combine carefully observed architecture with figures in local dress, and the Met labels this picture Orientalist, a European genre of scenes from North Africa, the Near East and Asia.","i":"Alberto Pasini, from Busseto in northern Italy, first traveled east in 1851 and 1852 with a French diplomatic mission to Persia and returned repeatedly to the Ottoman lands. He became one of the best-known Italian Orientalists, with a reputation for architectural accuracy and clear, bright light. His views were popular with European and American collectors, who bought them as picturesque records of lands they would seldom see.","c":"Pasini is a painter of clarity rather than drama: the architecture is solid, the light is bright and the picture neatly organized. Compared with Delacroix or Gérôme he is less rich in color or incident, but his accuracy makes him a useful guide to the Ottoman capital in the 1870s. The risk, as with much Orientalism, is a tidy, picturesque exoticism that leaves out the life of a modern city."},"met:437252":{"d":"A mosque, a man and a horse make up this wide canvas, about 37 by 55 centimeters, dated 1886. It is a picture of the Orientalist type. By the 1880s Pasini was a veteran of eastern travel, nearly sixty, and the picture is the kind of compact view of a mosque and its surroundings that sold well to collectors of the East. It was probably painted far from its subject, from earlier studies.","i":"Orientalist painters like Pasini turned memories and sketches of the Ottoman world into marketable pictures, often long after their journeys. The mosque stands for the East in European imagination, and a rider or a man standing before it supplies local color. Scholars now stress that such images were selective, presenting a timeless East that hid modern change, though they remain useful records of buildings and costume.","c":"A small, competent, bright picture, in which the mosque is the star and the people are accessories. Pasini's exactness with architecture and light is the chief pleasure, and the later date suggests he repeated rather than renewed his formula. For viewers who like clear drawing and a sunny mood, it will please; for those who look for drama or psychology, it offers little."},"met:13356":{"d":"A river winds among trees in a wide landscape, about 88 by 137 centimeters, dated 1866. The title names a state rather than a site, so this is probably a general view of country that Wyant had visited. It is early work, in the detailed, panoramic manner of the Hudson River School that he practiced before turning to the softer, more intimate landscapes of his maturity, and its size shows it was meant to be seen in an exhibition.","i":"In the 1860s American painters were extending landscape painting westward and southward, and after the Civil War a Southern subject carried a charge. A river landscape in Tennessee in 1866, a year after the war, offered a peaceful image of country that armies had just crossed, and it may have been read as an image of healing, though nothing in the title says so. Wyant's early style owed much to the example of George Inness.","c":"Wyant found his best voice in quieter, more atmospheric landscapes, closer to the Barbizon painters than to the grand vistas of the Hudson River School. This early canvas is an able demonstration of the earlier manner, with the sweep and finish audiences wanted, but it lacks the intimacy and tenderness of his later work. A young painter has learned the lesson of his elders and has not yet found his own."},"nga:50295":{"d":"A small horizontal canvas, only about 18 by 31 centimeters, shows a rural landscape at sunrise or sunset. Dark green bushes in the foreground lead to a lighter green field ringed by trees, and pale pink and purple mountains lie low on the horizon. The palette is warm and earthy, with greens and browns below shading to purples and pinks in the distance. The sky is pale yellow, fading to light blue toward the top left, with faint yellow and gray clouds drifting across it.","i":"Wyant belonged to the generation of American landscape painters who moved from the detailed grandeur of the Hudson River School toward a quieter, more poetic and tonal view of nature, influenced by the Barbizon painters of France. A valley is a modest subject, and the title promises just that: peace, farmland, no drama. Small pictures like this were bought for the home rather than for public display, and they met a growing taste for mood over spectacle.","c":"At this size the picture has to succeed through tone, and it does: the warm lower register and the softly colored distance give a sense of air that grander pictures often lack. Its modesty is both its charm and its limit; it asks the viewer to settle in rather than be struck. A fine small example of the move toward intimate, atmospheric landscape in the American 1870s."},"met:13357":{"d":"A mountain rises in a wide landscape of County Kerry, in the southwest of Ireland, painted on a canvas about 67 by 102 centimeters around 1875. Only a mountain is singled out, and the title names a region rather than a precise spot. It is a landscape of mood, the kind in which Wyant typically used soft light and damp air to evoke the character of a place.","i":"The lakes and mountains of County Kerry, near Killarney, were a tourist destination in the nineteenth century, praised by writers and painted by many British and American artists in search of picturesque scenery. In the 1870s Wyant's style was turning from Hudson River grandeur to softer, more atmospheric effects reminiscent of the Barbizon painters, and an Irish subject, with its changeable weather, suited that change.","c":"A reserved, atmospheric landscape in which mood outranks detail. Wyant is not a showy painter, and the picture's appeal is its tonal unity and restraint. The risk is a certain grayness and a sameness of effect from one canvas to another, but at his best he reached a poetry his Hudson River elders did not. This Kerry view stands between grand vista and intimate study."},"met:13355":{"d":"A forest and an old clearing are the subject of this tall canvas, about 125 by 94 centimeters, dated 1881. The picture shows the interior of woods with an opening of light, a favorite theme of Wyant's later career. The upright format suits the tall trees and closes the view in, directing attention to the woods themselves rather than to a distant horizon. It dates from the last dozen years of Wyant's life; he died in 1892.","i":"By the 1880s American painters were turning from the grand vistas of the Hudson River School to the intimate woodland interiors favored by the Barbizon painters in France. A clearing, a space where trees had been cut, recalls the history of settlement, and an old one suggests that nature had begun to reclaim it. After an illness in the 1870s paralyzed his right arm, Wyant painted with his left hand.","c":"Wyant's late forest interiors are among the most poetic American landscapes of their decade: light filtering through foliage, a sense of damp stillness. They suffer sometimes from muddy brown tones and a looseness that can read as vagueness, but his best, like this tall canvas, convey a hush few contemporaries equal. A painter who once achieved sweeping views here chooses to look at less, and gets more."},"met:13352":{"d":"A glimpse of the sea is promised by the title of this wide canvas, about 46 by 77 centimeters, painted around 1885. Trees are the other element, so the sea is probably seen through or beyond the foliage, a view that opens briefly between trees. It is a modest, late work in which Wyant uses a simple device to turn an ordinary stretch of land into a small revelation.","i":"A glimpse is a favorite device of Romantic landscape: the viewer looks through a screen of trees toward an unexpected opening, a promise of space beyond. In the 1880s, as railways and steamships made the seaside accessible, a view of the sea was both a simple pleasure and a symbol of freedom. The title asks the eye to travel, and the picture's quiet statement is about discovery in small things.","c":"A late and modest picture that shows how little Wyant needed: trees, a gap, a pale sea. His late touch is soft and unemphatic, and the effect is poetic if rather mild. He has no gift for drama and does not need one. In a century of loud landscapes there is value in a painter who preferred to say little, though some viewers will want more."},"met:13351":{"d":"A broad valley, silent and open, is the subject of this tall canvas of about 154 by 128 centimeters, dated by the Met to about 1880 to 1887. A tree is the one feature singled out; the title does the rest, naming the stillness and breadth that the picture is meant to convey. The upright format is unusual for a valley view, which the eye would expect to run wide, and the large scale makes it a major statement.","i":"Wyant came to the quiet landscape through the Barbizon painters and his own experience of the Adirondack and Catskill woods. A silent valley belongs to the Tonalist and poetic strain of American painting in the 1880s, which valued mood, hush and suggestion over topography. The title sounds like a line of verse and invites the viewer to supply the feeling, as the best of such pictures do.","c":"A late and important picture in which Wyant's love of space and quiet is clearest. The large canvas lets his soft transitions and broad masses speak, and it avoids the stagy grandeur of earlier American vistas. The weaknesses are a certain emptiness and a tonal sameness, and some viewers will find it too mild. Others will take that mildness as its virtue: a rare image of silence in a noisy century."},"cma:109240":{"d":"A landscape near Arkville, a village in the Catskill Mountains of New York, is painted on a wide canvas of about 41 by 62 centimeters, dated to the 1880s. Wyant liked simple rural motifs, with trees, hills, water and sky treated in soft, unified tones, and a modest format well suited to private ownership. Such a picture offers a feeling of a place rather than a catalog of its features.","i":"The Catskills had been the cradle of the Hudson River School since Thomas Cole in the 1820s, and by the 1880s they were a popular summer destination with railways and hotels. Wyant's Catskill and Adirondack subjects belong to the later, quieter generation, which preferred a simple pastoral view to Cole's drama. The title names a place that a viewer might recognize; the picture offers it as a mood of rural calm.","c":"A modest, pleasant landscape that adds little to Wyant's reputation and takes little away. Its virtues are the ones he always had: a harmonious tone, a feeling for sky and a refusal to shout. Its limitation is the same sameness of effect from one canvas to another. It makes a good companion to his larger statements, and will please those who enjoy quiet."},"cma:109242":{"d":"An autumn landscape with a pool of water is the subject of this canvas, about 51 by 61 centimeters, dated broadly to the 1870s or 1880s. The title promises changing foliage and still water, a combination that lets a painter show seasonal color and reflection in a single view. Wyant tended to treat such subjects in soft, unified tones rather than in the brilliant, high-keyed colors that many of his contemporaries preferred.","i":"Autumn foliage was a source of national pride for American landscape painters, and the brilliance of the New England and Hudson Valley fall astonished European visitors. A pool adds the mirror of still water, a favorite Romantic device that doubles the scene and suggests calm. Wyant's treatment, which favored mood over the flaming color of earlier Hudson River painters, reflects a later taste for subtlety.","c":"Autumn invites showiness, and Wyant's restraint is the interest. The picture is a quiet variation on a popular theme, closer to Barbizon moderation than to American spectacle. Its weakness is a certain generality, since the title could fit many canvases; its strength is the unforced harmony of its parts. It rewards viewers who like to find color in subdued things."},"met:436870":{"d":"A man chooses his dinner from birds and rabbits, according to the title, in a small oil on canvas about 31 by 47 centimeters, dated 1872. It is an anecdotal genre picture in which food on display and a customer's decision supply the whole story. Leloir, a French painter of the Second Empire generation, often set such scenes in earlier periods and painted them with a fine, careful finish.","i":"Scenes of cooks, poultry sellers and game dealers belong to a long tradition running from Flemish and Dutch market and kitchen pictures to the nineteenth-century French taste for anecdotal genre. Collectors enjoyed such small, finely painted scenes, in which the pleasure lay in skillful detail and a mild joke. The abundance of game and poultry was also a sign of plenty, a quiet image of the good table.","c":"A small, neat anecdote painted with precision; the pleasure is in the display of feathers and fur and the humor of the choosing. Leloir is better known for colorful costume pieces, and this is a light entertainment, not a major picture. The risk of such work is triviality, but its craftsmanship is real, and the picture has the gentle appeal of a well-made small object, typical of the market for finished little genre pictures."},"met:437729":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, about 165 by 110 centimeters, painted in the 1640s, showing the archangel Michael. The museum's record names only the angel and the saint, so his pose, his armor and the demon he usually defeats are not described here. A canvas of this size and format suggests a picture for a church or chapel, and the commanding presence of the archangel suited such a setting. The painter was Spanish, a lesser-known name of the Baroque period.","i":"Michael, leader of the heavenly armies in the Book of Revelation, is the champion who casts the rebel angels and the dragon out of heaven. In Spanish Baroque painting he is often shown as a model of militant faith against evil, and he is also the angel who weighs souls at the Last Judgment. A full-scale picture of him could serve as the protector of a church, a confraternity or a family chapel.","c":"Ignacio de Ries is a far less familiar name than Zurbarán, Velázquez or Murillo, and on the evidence of subject and scale this picture belongs to the large body of capable religious painting that filled Spanish churches and convents in the seventeenth century. It must be judged by its own authority rather than by reputation, and a heroic archangel on a full-size canvas gives it a certain force. A representative, not a revelatory, work."},"nga:42455":{"d":"A man in a dark coat, white high-collared shirt and white cravat is turned to one side in this portrait of 1816, painted in oil on canvas, about 102 by 84 centimeters. He has light blue eyes, a long nose, thin lips and wavy dark brown hair with sideburns. His arm rests on the back of a wooden chair and he holds a closed book. The background is a plain, neutral tone, which leaves the figure standing out as a clear, firmly outlined shape, as in much of the painter's work of these years.","i":"Phillips was an itinerant portraitist who traveled through rural New England and New York, painting farmers, merchants and their families at modest prices. A closed book and a good coat mark the sitter as a literate, respectable man, and the chair and neutral ground give him a formal setting at little cost. A husband's portrait was often commissioned with his wife's: Alsa Slade, of the same year and format, is probably its companion.","c":"Phillips's early portraits rely on firm contour and flat, even color, and the result has a directness that academic portraits often lack. The face is carefully observed, while the dark coat and white cravat make strong, simple shapes. The pose is stiff, but the stiffness is part of the appeal. It is a confident example of American folk portraiture in its early phase, before Phillips moved toward more rounded, naturalistic modeling."},"nga:42456":{"d":"A woman seated and seen from the waist up faces slightly to her right with a direct gaze, in a portrait of 1816 painted in oil on canvas, about 102 by 84 centimeters. She wears a white cap with lace edges, an olive-green dress with long sleeves and a white shawl tied in a bow at the front, and a string of pearls circles her neck. She holds a piece of lacework. Beside her stands a table with a floral cloth. The background is a plain, muted tone.","i":"The lacework in her hands is a sign of a woman's domestic skill and industry, and the pearls and fine cap show some means. Such attributes were the standard vocabulary of rural American portraiture: for a woman, needlework and modest finery; for a man, a book or a pen. The picture was probably painted as a pair with the portrait of Joseph Slade, the same size and year, and the two would have hung together.","c":"The sitter looks out with a steady, unsmiling gaze, and Phillips gives her the same firm outline and plain ground as her companion portrait. The lace, the floral cloth and the pearls are rendered with patient, decorative attention, so that the picture has some of the charm of a sampler. It is less sophisticated than the polished portraits of Boston and Philadelphia painters, but it has an honesty and a visual strength that they often lack."},"met:11765":{"d":"A man identified as Philip Slade is shown in an upright portrait dated 1818, on a canvas about 97 by 83 centimeters. The recorded motifs include a book, a Bible, which presumably accompanies the sitter as a sign of his character. Ammi Phillips was an itinerant portraitist who worked in western Massachusetts, Connecticut and the Hudson Valley of New York, and he usually kept settings spare so that the face, the hands and the attribute carry the picture.","i":"A Bible beside or in the hands of a sitter marked piety, literacy and respectability, qualities valued in the New England and New York small towns where Phillips found his clients. Portraits like this were bought by farmers, merchants and professionals who wanted a lasting likeness at a moderate price in the age before photography. In 1818 the picture would have hung in the sitter's own home as proof of his standing and a keepsake for his family.","c":"Phillips is generally ranked among the finest of the American itinerant portraitists, valued for strong silhouettes, clear color and an eye for character. This 1818 portrait dates from his earlier years, before the flatter manner of the 1830s. The pose and attribute are conventions of the type, and the interest lies in how steadily he sets the sitter against the plain ground."},"nga:176888":{"d":"An upper-body portrait of a man in three-quarter view, seated upright and looking directly at the viewer, painted between 1812 and 1819 in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters. He has a solemn expression, a prominent chin, thin lips and alert eyes, and short gray hair combed flat. He wears a dark coat with large lapels, a white shirt and an intricately tied white cravat. The background is a simple, soft peach tone, and no jewelry or objects are visible.","i":"The sitter's name is lost, but the picture is a record of respectability in the early American republic: the dark coat, white linen and tied cravat were the uniform of a man of standing in a small town. Pictures like this were painted quickly and cheaply and were often paired with a wife's portrait; Portrait of a Lady, of the same size and date range, may be its companion.","c":"This is Phillips distilled: a plain ground, a firm head and an economical suit of black and white. The simplicity works. The face is observed with some care, and the large lapels and tied cravat give the figure a geometry that lifts it above its plain setting. The absence of props, though, means the picture gives no sense of the man's occupation or interests. It is an exemplary piece of folk portraiture rather than an individual statement."},"nga:177017":{"d":"A woman seen from the chest up, seated and facing slightly to the left, in a portrait painted between 1812 and 1819 in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters. She has pronounced cheekbones, a firm jaw and light-colored eyes under defined brows. A white cap with lace and a bow on top covers her hair. She wears a brown garment with a high collar and a ruffled shirt, a string of yellow beads or pearls and small earrings. The background is plain and neutral.","i":"A lace-trimmed cap, a high collar and a string of beads or pearls were the usual signs of a respectable, comfortably off woman in the rural northeast of the early republic. The portrait is of the same size and date range as Portrait of a Gentleman, and the two may well be companions for a married couple, though neither sitter's name is recorded.","c":"The pleasure of the picture is in its economy. The cap, collar and beads are given a few firm accents, and the woman's face, with its strong cheekbones and level gaze, has more character than the formulas of the type usually allow. Phillips gets a good deal from a plain format, and the lack of props focuses attention on the face. A strong, unsentimental portrait."},"nga:45963":{"d":"A woman seated and seen from the waist up faces forward, her body angled slightly, in a portrait of about 1820 painted in oil on canvas, about 82 by 66 centimeters. Soft curls frame her face under a white headpiece trimmed with lace and fabric, and she wears a white dress with lacework at the neckline and sleeves and a white belt. Her right hand holds a red shawl with a colorful border draped over the chair. The background is a solid dark color that sets off the white costume.","i":"White dresses and lace were fashionable in the years after 1800, in line with the high-waisted styles of the Empire and Regency periods, and they signaled refinement and a household that could keep fabrics clean. The red shawl, a luxurious accessory, adds a single bright note. Phillips, working in rural New England and eastern New York, had a gift for presenting provincial sitters as elegant.","c":"The picture's strength is the strong, simple contrast of white dress and dark ground, with a single red accent in the shawl. It shows Phillips in his early manner at a high point, finding a flat, graphic elegance that later, more realistic styles lost. The face is observed with care, but the true subject is the pattern of whites. It is a decorative picture of high quality, and one of the more memorable of his portraits."},"met:11766":{"d":"A man named Thomas Storm is the subject of this portrait, painted in oil on a canvas of about 81 by 66 centimeters around 1830. The recorded motifs name only a man, so the picture rests on the likeness. Portraits by Ammi Phillips of this date typically set the sitter against a plain ground in dark clothing that sets off the face and hands, and they treat the figure as a strong, simplified shape.","i":"By about 1830 Phillips was working in the Hudson Valley and western Connecticut, painting farmers, merchants and their families, and his portraits of these years are celebrated for bold, flat design in which line, silhouette and pattern matter more than depth. A portrait of a man like Thomas Storm was a sign of standing, bought to hang in the home and to pass down as a record of the family.","c":"Around 1830 Phillips developed one of the most graphic styles in American portraiture before photography, with crisp edges, simplified forms and a reduced palette that make his sitters read almost as silhouettes. The stylization has a strikingly modern appeal. If this portrait follows his usual manner of the period, it shows a sitter reduced to a few firm shapes, with the character concentrated in the face."},"nga:42462":{"d":"A young girl sits on a wooden chair in a vertical portrait of about 1830, painted in oil on canvas, about 66 by 56 centimeters. Her feet are cut off by the lower corner of the canvas. She has round cheeks, green eyes and a cleft chin, and a ruffled white bonnet with a carnation-pink bow covers her hair. She wears a white high-waisted dress with fluttering sleeves and a cherry-red sash that falls over the chair back. In one hand she holds a bunch of red strawberries, and with the other she lifts one to her chest, against a dark bronze-brown ground.","i":"Children were often shown with a toy, an animal or a piece of fruit, which gave a small sitter something to do and carried a gentle meaning: sweetness, health and the pleasures of summer. A white dress and bonnet were the standard signs of innocence. Portraits of children were a staple for rural New England and New York families, at a time when childhood illness was common and a likeness was especially precious.","c":"This is one of Phillips's most appealing pictures, and it shows his later, more naturalistic style at its best: a rounded face, a soft white dress and the bright note of the strawberries against a dark ground. The girl's pose, turned on a chair and cut by the lower corner, is slightly awkward, but the awkwardness gives a sense of a real child. A clear, fresh and unsentimental portrait."},"nga:56743":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Catherine A. May, painted around 1830 in oil on canvas and measuring about 79 by 63 centimeters. The museum's record lists only the sitter, so her dress, pose and setting are not described here. It belongs to Phillips's output of the early 1830s, when he painted local families in a more rounded, naturalistic manner than his earlier flat, linear one, and its vertical size is typical of a half-length commission.","i":"Phillips painted hundreds of portraits, and many sitters are known only by the names on the pictures. A portrait named for a woman reflects the importance of family likenesses in rural America, where a painter might visit a town and take several commissions at once, from a merchant and his wife to their children and neighbors. For descendants, a name and a face were often all that survived of an ancestor.","c":"The record offers little to rank the picture by, but Phillips was a consistent painter, and in the 1830s his portraits of women generally combine firm modeling, careful lace and a restrained dark palette. The sitter is more likely to impress by presence than by elaborate detail. As an example of his mature manner it belongs to the productive years in which he painted some of his most appealing work."},"nga:42431":{"d":"A man seen from the waist up faces slightly to the left but looks directly at the viewer, in a portrait of about 1835 painted in oil on canvas, about 83 by 71 centimeters. He has fair skin, light eyes, short wavy brown hair and a composed, neutral expression. He wears a white shirt with a high, stiff black neckband, a creamy yellow waistcoat and a dark jacket. In his right hand he holds a paper lettered Religious Intel., set against a deep, plain brown ground.","i":"The paper identifies his interests. Religious Intel. is probably short for a periodical such as the Religious Intelligencer, and holding it declares him a reading, pious man. Props like this let sitters present their values at small cost. The portrait pairs with Mrs. Day, of the same size and date, and the two hung together as a record of a respectable household of the 1830s.","c":"A newspaper in the hand is an unusual prop and gives the picture a personal, even witty touch, since the man advertises his reading in the most direct way. The portrait is simple, well observed and carefully composed, with the neckband, waistcoat and jacket forming a clear sequence of dark and light. It is more naturalistic than Phillips's earlier work, and it has the confidence of a painter who knew exactly what the job required."},"nga:42432":{"d":"A woman sits with her body turned slightly to her left and her right arm on a reddish-brown plush armchair, her face toward the viewer, in a portrait of about 1835 painted in oil on canvas, about 83 by 71 centimeters. She has calm blue eyes, a faint smile and clearly defined features, with elaborate curls framing her face. She wears a white lace-edged bonnet tied under the chin with a wide ribbon, and a dark dress with a high neckline and wide, puffed sleeves. The background is plain and dark.","i":"The wide puffed sleeves, curled hair and ribboned bonnet match the fashions of the mid-1830s, so the picture also works as a record of dress. A plush armchair signals comfort and position. As the companion of Mr. Day, of the same date and size, it shows how couples were commissioned together, the wife's portrait often introducing a touch of decoration and softness where her husband's is plain.","c":"Phillips takes more trouble with the details of a woman's costume, and the puffed sleeves, bonnet ribbon and curled hair give the picture a pleasing rhythm of rounded forms against the plain dark ground. The faint smile, less common in provincial portraits of the period, is a welcome sign of life. It lacks the fire of his best images of children, but it is an assured, sympathetic portrait."},"nga:42433":{"d":"The upper half of a seated man with fair skin, blue eyes and neatly styled light brown hair, in a portrait of about 1835 painted in oil on canvas, about 85 by 69 centimeters. He wears a dark suit, a high-collared white shirt and a black cravat. His right arm rests on the arm of the chair while his left hand holds a quill pen, and a stack of papers is visible behind him. His expression is composed.","i":"The quill pen and the papers say that Henry Teller worked with writing: a merchant, lawyer, clerk or official, though the record does not say which. In portraits of the period, a pen was the mark of a man of business and learning, a sign of competence rather than rank. The picture has a companion in Jane Storm Teller, of similar size and date, and the pair together record a married couple of the 1830s.","c":"The tools of the trade give the portrait a purposeful air. Phillips uses the dark suit and black cravat as a strong, simple frame for a face and a pair of hands, and the pen gives the hand something to do, a problem that many provincial painters never solved. The picture is clear, sober and slightly stiff, and it conveys a man at work rather than a man posing."},"nga:42434":{"d":"A woman sits in three-quarter view, a small red book in her hand and her left hand raised to her face, in a portrait of about 1835 painted in oil on canvas, about 84 by 69 centimeters. She has light skin, slightly arched brows and hair styled in prominent coils under a black lace-trimmed cap with a pink, blue and yellow ribbon. She wears a dark gown with long sleeves, a lace collar and lace cuffs, and sits on a red velvet chair studded with gold. The background is a deep brown.","i":"The pose, with a hand to the cheek and a book held ready, was long associated with thoughtfulness and piety, and a small red volume is probably a Bible or devotional book. The velvet chair and the lace collar and cuffs show a household of means. As the companion of Henry Teller, who holds a quill, she is presented as the reader and he as the writer, a pairing typical of such portraits.","c":"This is among the more elaborate of Phillips's portraits of women: the lace, ribbon and studded chair give him a range of textures to work with, and the thoughtful pose lends some psychological interest. The details may be a little busy beside the plain face and dark ground, but the portrait has a warmth and a sense of occupation that make it more than a formula. A good late example."},"met:11764":{"d":"A woman in black and a small girl in bright red fill a deep maroon background. The mother has a pale, oval face with dark hair arranged in loops, under a huge white lace cap with ruffles tied at her neck by a sheer ribbon. Her black dress has very wide puffed sleeves. On her lap sits a toddler with fair hair, in a short-sleeved red dress, a double strand of red beads and red slippers. Each of them holds a small sprig with a flower. The figures are neatly outlined, the colour is flat and saturated, and there is almost no shading or modelling.","i":"Ammi Phillips worked for more than fifty years as an itinerant artist, painting portraits of friends, relatives and neighbours in New York State and New England. The Met explains that his approach stressed plain likeness, without shadows or modelling, and relied on repeated formulas for efficiency on the road, yet he could still individualise his sitters imaginatively. In this picture, the Met says, his design skill shows in the cleanly contained forms of mother and child, enriched with brilliant saturated colour and careful detail. The wide sleeves and ruffled cap match the fashions of the 1830s, and the picture is dated 1835 to 1840.","c":"The picture is a triumph of design over detail. The black mass of the mother and the vivid red of the child lock together into one strong shape, and the lace cap is rendered with a patient, almost abstract pattern. Faces are simplified and the bodies have little structure, so the child looks doll-like and the mother's hand seems added on. Yet the plainness gives the sitters a presence that more skilful likenesses sometimes lack. Modern viewers admire Phillips as a graphic artist, and this portrait shows why."},"met:436891":{"d":"The pious Tobit is shown at his act of mercy, burying the dead, on a wide canvas of about 128 by 174 centimeters painted in the 1640s. The recorded motifs, a man, ruins, a dog and death, outline a scene in which the solemn task unfolds among broken masonry. Andrea di Lione, a Neapolitan painter associated with Aniello Falcone, was known for Old Testament subjects and battle scenes in which small, lively figures act in broad, atmospheric settings.","i":"The Book of Tobit tells of a devout Israelite exile in Nineveh who, at risk to himself, buried the bodies of his murdered countrymen. Burying the dead became one of the seven corporal works of mercy, and Tobit was its biblical model. In the story he is later blinded by sparrow droppings and healed by his son Tobias with the help of the archangel Raphael, and he was honored as a model of charity and patience.","c":"Andrea di Lione was a capable storyteller in the circle of Aniello Falcone and Salvator Rosa, and a subject with ruins, a dog and a solemn task suits his talent for small figures in dramatic settings. He lacks the originality of Rosa or the intensity of Ribera, and he is usually placed in the second rank of Neapolitan painters. His pictures please by liveliness and craft more than by depth."},"nga:101749":{"d":"From a high vantage point, the view looks across a snow-covered expanse to the long side of a pale, parchment-colored building with a town beyond, in this small horizontal oil sketch on paper mounted on canvas, about 22 by 30 centimeters. The painting is loose, in icy blue, cream, golden tan and brown. Steel-blue strokes mark the shadows on the snowy roof, a fawn-brown obelisk topped by a cross stands before the façade, and stacked rooflines recede toward a horizon halfway up. White clouds with arctic-blue undersides sweep across the sky.","i":"Santa Trinità dei Monti, the French church at the head of the Spanish Steps in Rome, stood beside the Villa Medici, home of the French Academy, so the view would have been familiar to Giroux as a student there; it was probably painted from nearby. Snow in Rome is rare, which is likely why the sketch records it: a quick response to an unusual effect, a Rome that looks northern, with obelisk and towers standing out against white.","c":"Snow simplifies a view into a few large planes, and Giroux exploits that with broad, free strokes: the steel-blue shadows and cool whites carry the picture, and the building is built from patches of color rather than line. It is a small improvisation, but the cool palette and the sense of altitude give it an odd, northern calm. It is a reminder of how much French painters learned from quick studies made in Rome."},"nga:74992":{"d":"Boulders hulk at the center of a dense forest in this small horizontal sketch, about 29 by 44 centimeters, painted in oil on paper around 1825 to 1830. A clearing along the bottom leads to a painter, seen from behind in a light gray coat, with a hand raised to an easel; a light brown dog sits attentively at his back. Sunlight falls through an unseen gap in the canopy onto the rocks, which glow caramel brown and slate gray, and curving trees enclose the scene. The paint is loose and earthy green, with brushstrokes especially visible in the boulders and foliage.","i":"Civita Castellana, north of Rome, was a favorite sketching ground for landscape painters of the period, with its wooded ravines and rock formations. Giroux, a French landscapist who won the Prix de Rome for historical landscape, painted such oil sketches outdoors, as training and as raw material for finished pictures. The painter at his easel, with a dog, makes the sketch a picture of the practice itself: working from nature in the Roman countryside.","c":"These oil sketches on paper show the immediacy of open-air work, and the loose, rapid brushwork and strong light give the forest a real sense of air and place. The small painter and dog are a charming addition, possibly a nod to the artist himself, and they give scale to the boulders. Giroux is less known than Corot, who sketched in the same region, and he lacks Corot's quiet authority, but the freshness is real."},"nga:74993":{"d":"Slender tree trunks and branches reach across a waterfall framed by green banks in this small horizontal oil sketch on paper, about 30 by 45 centimeters, from around 1825 to 1830. Boulders line the lower edge, except at the lower left, where plants take over. Beyond them, water crashes down a short fall also lined with rocks, and bright green foliage fills the rest of the picture. The tree arms beyond the plants at lower left reach to the right in a loose web.","i":"Papigno, near Terni in Umbria, lies close to the great Marmore Falls, and the ravines of the area drew landscape painters of the period. Waterfalls, with their mix of rock, water and foliage, were favorite subjects because they posed the problem of how to paint movement and light at once. Sketches like this were made out of doors, on paper, to be kept as studies rather than shown as finished pictures.","c":"The picture is hardly composed at all: a screen of foliage and a sliver of falling water, painted quickly and with evident pleasure. That spontaneity is its virtue, and the loose web of branches is observed with a freshness that few finished studio pictures could match. It is slight, but it shows why oil sketches of this kind came to be valued above many polished landscapes of the period."},"nga:46475":{"d":"A wide, placid river winds through a hazy landscape, with a grassy bank at the left and a sheer rock formation at the right. Midway the water tumbles down step-like rapids; farther back at the left a waterfall pours from a narrow gap in rocky cliffs. A ruined building among trees tops the rocky outcrop at the right, two tall, spindly trees grow on a grassy bank at the left, and pale purple craggy mountains close the distance. In the top third, smoky-purple clouds curve round a pearl-white glowing sky. The artist signed and dated it as though inscribed on the cliff base.","i":"The title says it all: this is an invented landscape, a capriccio, not a view of a real place, built from the stock elements of picturesque scenery, such as a ruined castle, rapids, a waterfall and distant peaks. Doughty, counted among the first American painters to make landscape a full-time profession, borrows from the European picturesque to give American patrons the romantic scenery they associated with the Old World.","c":"Doughty's strength is atmosphere, and the soft pearl light, the purple mist and the stepped river make a calm, harmonious scene. The inventory of picturesque effects is a little too orderly, though: the ruin, the falls and the spindly trees feel assembled from a kit, and the picture lacks the sharper observation of American nature that Cole and Durand would bring. A pleasing but conventional period piece."},"met:10768":{"d":"A small horizontal landscape, about 38 by 55 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in the early 1830s. The museum's record lists trees, a river and mountains, so it is a view along a river with mountains beyond, in the manner of the Hudson River scenery that Doughty helped to popularize. Details of light, figures and boats are not recorded, so this note describes the type: a cabinet picture for a private parlor, small enough to hang with others in a domestic gallery.","i":"Doughty, largely self-taught, was among the first Americans to make a career of landscape alone, at a time when most American painters depended on portrait commissions. The Hudson, with its mountains and traffic, was becoming a national subject, and pictures titled On the Hudson sold to collectors in the cities where he worked, Philadelphia, Boston and New York, who wanted to own a piece of their own country's scenery.","c":"Doughty's strength is a gentle, hazy atmosphere, and his river views are quietly effective. They lack the grandeur and the crisp detail of Thomas Cole and the later painters of the Hudson River School, for whom he was something of a forerunner, and that modesty is both their appeal and their limit. This is a pleasant, unambitious picture, and it records the early taste for American scenery."},"cma:139815":{"d":"A lake view, about 39 by 55 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in the 1830s. At the lake's edge in the middle of the composition stand two hunters with a dog, small figures that give scale to the water, and distant sailboats dot the surface. Largely self-taught, Doughty often painted landscapes that include a body of water, and here the human presence is kept modest: the hunters in the foreground and the far-off boats, which make the lake a place that people use.","i":"The hunters and sailboats are small reminders that the landscape is used and enjoyed. In the 1830s American landscape painters often paired wilderness with quiet signs of human activity, suggesting that nature could be wild and welcoming at once. Hunting and boating were familiar pursuits for the picture's likely buyers, and the scene offered them a flattering image of country life.","c":"The two hunters and their dog are a conventional device, but they work, giving the eye a place to rest and the lake a scale. Doughty's landscapes are never as bold as Cole's, and this one stays well within the safe conventions of its time, with the water as the principal subject. It is calm and well balanced, and the distant sailboats keep the surface from seeming empty."},"met:10769":{"d":"A vertical landscape, about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas between about 1843 and 1850. The museum's record lists trees, a river and mountains, and the title suggests that the river is glimpsed through the trees rather than shown in full. An upright format is less usual for landscapes of this kind, which tend to stretch sideways, and it gives the picture a tall, enclosed look. Few other details are recorded, so the light, figures and colors are not described.","i":"The title points to a favorite landscape device, the view that opens through a screen of trees and draws the eye toward the distance. By the 1840s Doughty was an established landscapist whose ambitions had been overtaken by Cole and a younger generation, but his quiet, pastoral scenes still found buyers. Such pictures offered the pleasure of discovery in a comfortable, domestic size.","c":"A picture of this kind lives or dies by its handling of light and distance, and Doughty's ease with soft, hazy depth is the likely source of its charm. Compared with the large canvases of the Hudson River School in the same years, it is small in ambition, but its simplicity is part of the pleasure. It is a modest, agreeable late work by a painter who had been overtaken in fame by younger rivals but kept his touch."},"nga:195077":{"d":"From a slightly elevated viewpoint the eye takes in a wide view of rolling hills and trees, with sailboats on a pale blue body of water at the centre. In the foreground is a grassy area with two people and a dog, both people with their backs to the viewer: one stands in a white dress and yellow jacket, the other sits in a red shirt and brown trousers. The foliage runs through greens, reds and oranges, suggesting autumn, and the sky is pale blue with pale pink clouds. The canvas is a generous size, about 87 by 123 centimetres.","i":"By 1850 the Hudson River had become the most celebrated landscape in American painting, and American painters and writers prided themselves on the brilliance of the native autumn. Doughty shows it in a gentle, pastoral key: two people and a dog resting on the grass in the foreground, backs turned, share the viewer's pleasure in the prospect. The picture dates from late in a career that had helped open the way for the Hudson River School.","c":"A mild, agreeable picture. The elevated viewpoint opens a wide prospect of hills and water, the pale pink clouds soften the scene, and the figures with their backs turned invite the viewer to stand with them. The foliage is more generalised than the sharp, leaf-by-leaf observation of younger Hudson River painters, and the colour never grows bold. Doughty's gentler, hazier landscape belongs to an earlier taste, pleasing but overtaken by grander successors."},"met:10771":{"d":"A large horizontal landscape, about 112 by 158 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in the mid-1850s, among Doughty's last works. The museum's record lists a waterfall and hills, and the title gives the season as spring. The size is far greater than that of his small cabinet pictures of the 1830s, which suggests an ambitious exhibition piece or a commission for a large room, though the details of the scene are not described here.","i":"Spring, with its fresh foliage and rushing water, was a favorite theme for American landscape painters of the 1850s, who saw in the seasons an image of renewal and the cycle of the year. Waterfalls, a staple of American scenery from the Catskills to Niagara, offered both a picturesque motif and a sign of the continent's natural abundance. The large scale suggests that Doughty meant the picture to be noticed.","c":"At this size Doughty has to hold a large space together, and a waterfall and hills give him the structure to do it. The success of the picture depends on how convincingly it handles light and distance, and the soft, hazy manner for which he was known suits spring. It lacks the sublimity of Cole or the precision of Church's waterfalls, but as a late, large statement by a pioneer of American landscape it holds real interest."},"nga:41591":{"d":"A young woman is shown from the waist up in a shimmering royal-blue dress, her body angled to one side as she turns and tilts her head and looks back across her body with flint-gray eyes. She holds a metal vessel with a conical top, perhaps a lamp or an incense burner, near the bottom edge. A gray pearl earring hangs from her ear, and her auburn hair is parted and loosely braided. One full white sleeve is rolled back at the elbow. The ground is solid black, so the light on the blue silk is the chief event.","i":"The title casts the sitter as one of the wise virgins of Matthew's parable, who kept their lamps filled while waiting for the bridegroom, which would explain the vessel in her hands. Portraits of women in the guise of saints, goddesses and virtuous heroines were fashionable in Venice around 1510, and an image of this kind would suit a bride or a young woman of marriageable age, with its reference to virtue and readiness. Her name is not known.","c":"Sebastiano's training in Venice, in the circle of Giovanni Bellini and Giorgione, shows in the soft, glowing flesh and the play of light on the silk against a dark ground. The pose, with the head turned against the line of the body, is both graceful and slightly mysterious. Transfer from the original panel can alter a surface, but the conception holds. It is a refined, quiet work from before his move to Rome made him more monumental."},"nga:46136":{"d":"Four men gather around a table against a forest-green background in this horizontal panel, about 122 by 150 centimeters, dated 1516. Cardinal Sauli sits at the narrow end, in a red cap and cape and a bright white robe, turning to look at the viewer, with a ring on each hand and a silver-and-gold bell near his fingers. His secretary leans toward his shoulder from the left, while two geographers behind the table face each other in profile; one rests a hand on an open book of maps, and the other points a finger upward. A fly seems to sit on the cardinal's knee.","i":"Bandinello Sauli was a cardinal from a Genoese family, and the maps, the geographers and the secretary present him as a man of affairs and learning. The painted fly, a trick of illusion with a long history in painting, shows off the painter's skill while tempting the viewer to brush it away. The signature is painted as if on a slip of paper stuck to the table, another playful illusion, though it is nearly illegible.","c":"Sebastiano, who had left Venice for Rome a few years earlier, joins Venetian color to a Roman sense of weight: the white robe, the red velvet and the green ground are set in a compact, shallow space that pushes the figures forward. The group is not entirely easy, since secretary and geographers are clustered rather than integrated, but the cardinal's gaze holds the picture together. It is an ambitious group portrait, a type then rare in Italy."},"met:437645":{"d":"A portrait of a man, painted in 1519 in oil on canvas and measuring about 107 by 88 centimeters, traditionally said to show Christopher Columbus. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so none is offered here. The identification cannot stand as it is: Columbus died in 1506, thirteen years before the date given for the picture, so he cannot have sat for it, and the sitter's real identity is not known.","i":"The traditional label is probably a later attribution, made when a handsome portrait of the explorer was wanted and no authenticated likeness from life is known to survive. Sebastiano's real subject was more likely a gentleman of about 1519, when the painter was established in Rome and well connected at the papal court. The mistaken name shows how readily a fine portrait can attract a famous one.","c":"The legend has done the picture a disservice: it has attached a famous name to an unknown sitter and has made viewers look for the explorer rather than for Sebastiano's hand. As a work by one of the finest portraitists in Rome, made in the years of his Raising of Lazarus, it deserves to be judged as the image of a man of its own time. The generous scale and the date place it at a peak of ambition."},"nga:46137":{"d":"A bearded man with shoulder-length dark brown hair is shown from the hips up against a dark emerald-green curtain, in a vertical portrait about 135 by 101 centimeters. His body angles slightly to one side while his head turns to look straight at the viewer, his eyes shadowed because the light comes from the right. He wears a dark robe over a white shirt with a high collar and holds a folded piece of yellowish paper or cloth. Beside him a green-covered shelf holds a globe, an open book, a stack of closed books and a quill in an inkwell.","i":"The globe, books and quill mark the sitter as a scholar, and the title calls him a humanist, a man learned in ancient languages and letters. One suggestion identifies him as the geographer Leo Africanus, a scholar educated in Fez who came to Rome in 1518 and wrote a celebrated Description of Africa, but the sitter's name is not securely established. The globe would suit a student of geography either way.","c":"The portrait is remarkable for its gravity. A tall format, a deep green ground and a single strong light make the learned man seem both present and withdrawn, and the objects at his shoulder are handled with plain authority. The light that leaves half the face in shadow adds a note of reserve. It is a good example of Sebastiano's Roman portraits, which trade Venetian sweetness for weight and a darker, more sober presence."},"nga:46190":{"d":"Golden light from the upper left falls on vegetables, fish and live and dead animals among kitchen pots and pans, in a very large vertical canvas about 229 by 163 centimeters. The objects are stacked in three tiers: a hare and a hanging fowl, a pile of small birds and a live peacock above; a rooster on a barrel with red onions and a copper bowl of artichokes in the middle; and fish, lobsters, a duck and shining copperware on the floor. In the upper right corner a woman in a white cap is seen backlit in a dim room beyond.","i":"Larder pictures of this kind came from Flanders, where Frans Snyders and others painted overflowing kitchens and game as images of abundance, and they were taken up in Genoa, where Flemish painters worked in the early seventeenth century. For a wealthy owner such a picture showed the bounty of the table and the estate, and it offered a catalog of textures: fur, feathers, copper, scales. The woman in the distance reminds the viewer of the kitchen's labor.","c":"The size alone makes The Larder impressive, and Vassallo organizes the crowd with real skill, setting the living peacock against the dead game and the glowing copper against the dark wall. The tiers are somewhat schematic, and the sheer number of items can feel like a catalog, but the light holds it together. The small figure in the background is a clever touch, opening the space and adding a human note. A confident Genoese variation on the Flemish larder."},"met:435814":{"d":"A basket of flowers, with tulips and roses among the blooms, in a horizontal panel about 47 by 68 centimeters, painted in oil on wood probably in the 1620s. The museum's record lists a basket, a bouquet, tulips and roses, so the picture is a flower piece in the Flemish manner, in which each bloom is individually observed. Beyond that, the arrangement and colors are not described. It is the kind of small, finely finished panel made for a collector's cabinet.","i":"Jan Brueghel the Younger took over the Antwerp workshop of his father, Jan Brueghel the Elder, after the father's death in 1625, and continued the flower pieces for which the family was famous. Tulips, newly fashionable and costly, signaled rarity and wealth. Flower paintings of the day delighted collectors with exotic blooms and with the painter's skill, and they carried a quiet reminder that beauty is brief.","c":"As the heir to a famous name, the Younger worked in a tradition he did not invent, and his flower pieces follow the pattern set by his father, with close attention to individual blooms and a clear, bright palette. The result is refined but conservative: it does not extend the genre, and it lacks the sparkle of the Elder's best work. It is nevertheless a pleasing example of the Antwerp cabinet picture, and the tulips give it historical interest."},"met:435813":{"d":"A tiny, jewel-like picture, painted in oil on copper in the 1630s and measuring only about 27 by 36 centimeters, shows Aeneas and the Cumaean Sibyl in the underworld. Male and female nudes, presumably the shades and the damned, are among the figures recorded, and the scene follows the Brueghel family's taste for fantastic infernal landscapes. The copper support gives a smooth, enamel-like surface that suits fine detail. Further details of the setting are not described here.","i":"The subject comes from the sixth book of Virgil's Aeneid, in which the Trojan hero Aeneas, guided by the Sibyl of Cumae, descends to the underworld to meet his dead father and learn the future of Rome. The Sibyl leads him past monsters and the places of punishment. Painters in the Netherlands, following Bosch, found in this episode an excuse for a fantastic vision of hell, and collectors enjoyed the mixture of classical learning and grotesque invention.","c":"On a copper plate of this size, the Brueghel tradition of tiny, brightly lit figures and fantastic landscape can run free, and the underworld gives the painter a chance to show his inventive range. The Younger lacks the dazzling delicacy of his father, but he keeps the family's fine touch and sense of narrative. A small, entertaining picture, more clever than moving."},"nga:165":{"d":"Dozens of men and women wander through the tall, domed interior of the Pantheon in this vertical canvas, about 128 by 99 centimeters, painted around 1734. The figures are small beside the statues in the niches, which stand at least three times their height, and the coffered dome rises above them, its round opening letting in a glimpse of blue sky. The crowd strolls, kneels, talks or looks up. Through the great doors at the far end are more people, an obelisk and a strip of sky. The floor is patterned in circles and rectangles.","i":"The Pantheon, built under Hadrian as a temple to all the gods and made a Christian church in 609, was the best-preserved building of ancient Rome and a place every Grand Tour visitor wanted to see. Panini painted the interior many times for such visitors, and his small, lively figures, clergy, pilgrims and fashionable tourists, show it as a living place rather than a ruin.","c":"Panini's genius is for scale and space: the great dome is made to feel immense by the tiny figures below it, and the light that falls through the oculus gives the interior its drama. The picture combines record and invention, as the best vedute do, and the crowd is lively and varied. Its limitation is that the type was one he repeated, so the effect is of mastery rather than surprise. A fine, characteristic Roman view."},"cma:147938":{"d":"A vertical view of the Pantheon's interior, about 127 by 98 centimeters, dated 1747, peopled with foreign tourists, Roman churchgoers, nobles and artists mingling beneath the dome. The building was created as a temple under the emperor Hadrian around AD 125 and became a Christian church in 609. Through the open door can be seen the portico, where the city's most important art fair was held. Panini shows the whole life of a public space, with the architecture as its stage.","i":"By 1747 the Pantheon had been restored, in a period of renewed attention to early Christian monuments, and it was at once a monument of antiquity, an active church and a place of commerce, since its portico hosted a major art fair. Panini's view, which includes the visitors of the Grand Tour, appealed to the foreigners who bought his pictures as souvenirs and who might recognize themselves in the crowd.","c":"Panini painted the Pantheon interior repeatedly, and this version shows his formula at its most practiced. The perspective is rigorous, the crowd is diverse, and the dome gives a satisfying vertical sweep. What it lacks is the surprise of a more personal vision, since the type was one he repeated for a market of Grand Tour visitors. Within that limit it is a rich and animated document of eighteenth-century Rome and a model of the veduta tradition."},"nga:50884":{"d":"The gilded, coffered vault of the central nave fills this large horizontal canvas, about 155 by 197 centimeters, painted around 1754. The ceiling curves up and away like a tunnel, supported by white stone piers carved with winged cherubs holding portraits of men, and side aisles run to left and right, with pink marble columns flanking altars in chapels. At the far end the nave opens into the light-filled crossing, marked by the twisting bronze columns of a canopy. Tiny men and women pray or gather in small groups, some in rags and others elegantly dressed.","i":"Saint Peter's, the largest church in Christendom, was the main goal of every pilgrim and Grand Tour visitor to Rome, and its interior was a favorite subject for Panini. The bronze canopy over the high altar is Bernini's baldacchino, and the figures, a mix of the poor and the well-to-do, suggest that the great church was open to all. The tiny people stress the building's scale: a message of the church's power and the individual's smallness.","c":"Panini's mastery of perspective and light is complete here: the receding vault, the glow of the crossing and the tiny figures give an immediate sense of scale. The architecture is handled with a sure hand, though the repetition of such views over his career, to satisfy visitors, means that the picture belongs to a well-rehearsed type. Even so, among the many Saint Peter's interiors, a large, ambitious example like this has a convincing grandeur."},"met:437244":{"d":"A vast horizontal canvas, about 172 by 230 centimeters, painted in 1757, imagining ancient Rome as an interior crowded with sculpture and monuments. The museum's record lists men, sculpture, monuments and an interior, so it is a fantasy view in which famous works of antiquity are gathered in a single grand space, with figures looking at them. Panini painted several versions of the composition. Its contents are not itemized here, so the picture is described by type: a capriccio, or invented view, that serves as an encyclopedia of Roman antiquity.","i":"Panini made a specialty of such invented galleries, which let him assemble the greatest sights of Rome that a Grand Tour visitor wanted to remember, without regard to where they actually stood. He often paired a picture of ancient Rome with one of modern Rome as showpieces for wealthy patrons, with the learned viewer expected to recognize each famous statue and monument. The scene is a monument to the ideal of antiquity that dominated the mid-eighteenth century.","c":"Panini's invented galleries are more successful as catalogs than as paintings: the pleasure is in recognizing the famous pieces, and the composition, a grand architectural stage with figures dwarfed by it, is clear and impressive. The scale and range of reference are remarkable; what is missing is warmth. It is as much an act of scholarship as of painting, and a fascinating document of what the eighteenth century thought antiquity was."},"met:437245":{"d":"An imaginary picture gallery of enormous size is hung from floor to ceiling with small gold-framed views of modern Rome, among them Piazza Navona, the Palazzo Montecitorio and the Quirinal. Marble columns of rose and grey divide the walls, a painted ceiling opens above, and red drapery is looped back at both sides. In the foreground a seated marble figure resembling Michelangelo's Moses stands on a plinth beside a marble lion, with a large print propped against the base. At the left, elegantly dressed gentlemen in powdered wigs study a drawing held by a kneeling man in red, while an artist sits on the floor, sketching.","i":"Panini, born in Piacenza and working in Rome, was the leading painter of Roman views in the mid-eighteenth century and taught perspective at the French Academy in Rome. He specialised in gallery pictures like this, which pack a city's monuments into an imagined hall. Modern Rome was paired with a companion, Ancient Rome, filled with antiquities, and he painted the pair in several versions; the Met's is dated 1757. Such pictures suited visitors on the Grand Tour who wanted a memento of the city, and the gentlemen in the foreground model the connoisseurship such travellers were expected to show.","c":"Panini's gallery pictures give great pleasure to the eye. The perspective is faultless, the miniature views are painted with a clarity that makes each framed scene readable, and the foreground group gives human scale to a large fiction. As a record of taste it is brilliant; as a painting it is rather cool, with a mechanical evenness, since the pictures within the picture are similar in size and lighting and register as a pattern rather than as individual works. It rewards recognition more than feeling, but it does so with great skill."},"met:437246":{"d":"A crowd moves through the interior of Saint Peter's in this smaller horizontal canvas, about 74 by 100 centimeters, dated after 1754. The museum's record lists a crowd, a church building and an interior, with themes of churches, crowds and Rome, so the picture is a view of the great basilica with its visitors. Panini painted this view repeatedly, and the date places the picture at or after his large canvas of about 1754. Other details are not recorded here.","i":"Smaller versions of this kind were made for travelers who wanted a souvenir of the basilica they had seen on their visit to Rome, and Panini supplied a steady market. The crowd conveys both the church's scale and its role as a meeting place for the pious, the curious and the idle, a public space as much as a sacred one.","c":"The compact size makes the picture a distillation: with the vault, the nave and a crowd, Panini gets the essentials of the interior without the grandeur of the large version. Its virtue is economy, and its limit is the formula, since the view is one he repeated many times. A pleasing, practiced piece rather than a surprising one, and a good introduction to his Roman interiors."},"met:435701":{"d":"A forest of Fontainebleau, with cattle among the trees, fills this very large canvas, about 265 by 399 centimeters, by Auguste-François Bonheur. The date is not recorded, though the artist died in 1884. The recorded motifs are the forest and cattle, and the scale, more than two and a half meters high and four wide, is that of a work intended for the Paris Salon or a public building, not a domestic wall.","i":"The forest of Fontainebleau, near Paris, was the training ground of French landscape painting, from the Barbizon painters of the 1830s onward, and cattle were the rural subjects that Auguste Bonheur and his sister Rosa made their specialty. Pictures of this scale were shown at the Salon to win official recognition and sales, offering Parisian viewers an image of tranquil, productive nature close to the capital. The picture belongs to the later phase of the Barbizon tradition, when its subjects had become popular and marketable.","c":"The canvas is impressive for its size and its patient description of foliage and animals, but the size exposes the limits of the formula: a large picture of woodland and cattle, competent and well finished, does not grow in imagination as it grows in scale. Compared with the freshness of Barbizon sketches and with his sister's sharper animals, Auguste Bonheur seems more careful than inspired. It is a good record of Salon taste."},"met:437102":{"d":"A small horizontal landscape, about 38 by 55 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in 1629. The title tells of a cottage in the landscape, and the museum's record lists no other motif, so the picture can be understood as a plain view of the Dutch countryside, in keeping with Molijn's usual taste for humble roadside and village subjects. It is a cabinet picture for a private room, made at a moment when Dutch landscape was turning from fantastic to local views.","i":"Around 1629 painters in Haarlem, among them Molijn, Jan van Goyen and Salomon van Ruysdael, were developing the tonal landscape: unpretentious views of dunes, roads and farms, painted in a limited range of browns, greens and grays and unified by a soft atmosphere. A cottage stands for the ordinary rural life that Dutch collectors liked to see on their walls, a modest patch of land worth looking at for its own sake.","c":"Molijn, an early master of the tonal manner, gets a great deal from a small panel, and his work of the later 1620s tends to be stronger in contour and contrast than the softer landscapes of van Goyen. This early date shows him at his most distinctive. The picture asks for little and gives a good deal in return. A modest work whose quiet integrity is typical of Dutch landscape at its best."},"nga:66765":{"d":"A rickety wooden gate leans sharply across a rutted dirt road at the same angle as the bare tree behind it, in this small horizontal panel, about 34 by 48 centimeters. The road curves from the lower right toward the center and over a low rise. A shadowed fence may continue in front of a building with a sloping, moss-green roof that echoes the lean of gate and tree. Tiny figures, loosely painted, are tucked into the scene, one holding a staff in the gate's shadow, with a few white strokes that could be sheep. Straw-yellow and white clouds sweep up a dove-gray sky.","i":"Dutch painters of the 1630s discovered the beauty of the ordinary: a rough road, a broken gate, a bare tree and a farm roof were worth painting for their own sake, without mythology or grand views. Such a scene gave the viewer a feeling of weather, use and the passing of time, and it appealed to collectors who knew these rural paths. The tiny figures confirm that the picture is about a place, not about the people.","c":"The leaning gate, tree and roof, all tilted at the same angle, make a simple, memorable design, and the sweeping clouds give it movement. Molijn's loose touch and economy of means are well suited to the motif. The scale of the figures is odd, one so tiny as to seem out of proportion, but the strangeness adds to the atmosphere. A small picture with a strong, personal character."},"met:437744":{"d":"A tall double-sided painting on canvas with a gold ground, about 177 by 120 centimeters, made around 1395 to 1400. On one side stands Saint Mary Magdalen holding a crucifix; on the reverse is the Flagellation of Christ. The museum's record lists angels and musical instruments among the motifs, so the saint was probably shown with attendant angels making music. The face of Christ on the reverse is a photographic facsimile, a stand-in for what has been lost. The format suggests a processional banner, carried through streets and seen from both sides.","i":"Mary Magdalen, the repentant sinner and first witness of the Resurrection, was a patron of penitents and of many confraternities, lay brotherhoods that prayed together, gave charity and walked in processions behind painted banners. The Flagellation on the reverse, showing Christ scourged before the Crucifixion, probably gave members an image of suffering to meditate on. Angels with instruments may recall the legend that angels lifted the Magdalen aloft to hear heavenly music during her years of penance.","c":"Spinello, a painter of Arezzo and Florence best known for his narrative fresco cycles, works here in the late Gothic idiom of flat gold, strong outline and rhythmic gesture. A banner painted on both sides had to survive being carried and be legible from a distance, which favors bold, clear design. Its condition, and the facsimile that replaces Christ's face, limit the pleasure, but the scale and the gold ground still carry the force of a processional image."},"met:435616":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 36 by 45 centimetres, shows Saint Dominic raising a young nobleman, Napoleone Orsini, from the dead after a fall from his horse. The Met's keywords record a horse, a dog, men and the saint, so the scene presumably includes onlookers and animals around the body. Originally painted in tempera on wood, the paint layer was later transferred to canvas, a procedure that often leaves a surface thinner and flatter than it was.","i":"Dominic, founder of the Order of Preachers, is credited in Dominican tradition with raising the dead. In this miracle, set in Rome in the early thirteenth century, Napoleone Orsini was killed when his horse threw him, and Dominic restored him to life. Such scenes proved the saint's holiness by recalling Christ's own miracles. Small narrative panels like this were typically set in the predella beneath an altarpiece, where the faithful could read a saint's life like a story.","c":"Bartolomeo degli Erri was a provincial painter working in the shadow of the Ferrarese masters, and the hard outline and brisk storytelling expected of that milieu suit the subject. The horse, the dog and the crowd give the scene life, but space tends to be awkward and figures stiffly set, and the transfer to canvas may have dulled the original brilliance. It is a small painting of quaint appeal rather than of high accomplishment."},"met:435617":{"d":"A small upright panel, about 43 by 31 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood, shows the Dominican theologian Saint Thomas Aquinas absorbed in reading while the apostles Peter and Paul come to his aid. The Met's keywords also record a woman among the figures, although her role is not clear. The picture is by Bartolomeo degli Erri, a painter of Modena active in the later fifteenth century, and its small size suggests a predella panel.","i":"Early lives of Aquinas tell how, while he was struggling to understand a difficult passage of the prophet Isaiah, the apostles Peter and Paul appeared and explained it to him. The story shows his learning as inspired by the founders of the Roman Church, an endorsement the Dominicans were keen to claim for their greatest teacher, who was canonised in 1323. Scenes from his life were popular in Dominican churches, and a picture of this size was most likely part of a series.","c":"The subject is a quiet one, study and revelation, and it gives the painter little action to work with. Erri responds with clear grouping in which the apostles lend the scholar authority. The figures and the room are drawn with a primitive sharpness that makes them memorable if not subtle. It is a charming small panel by a minor master, and its appeal is more to students of Dominican iconography than to lovers of high drama."},"met:435619":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 22 by 43 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood, shows the arrest of Christ in the Garden of Gethsemane, with apostles present. In Italian painting of the mid-fifteenth century the scene normally includes the kiss of Judas, the soldiers who come to seize Christ and the disciples' alarm. The shape and size suggest a predella panel, one of a row of small Passion scenes beneath an altarpiece.","i":"The Gospels (Matthew 26, Mark 14, Luke 22, John 18) tell how Judas led armed men to Gethsemane and identified Jesus with a kiss, how Peter drew a sword and cut off the ear of the high priest's servant, and how the disciples fled. The scene begins the Passion and sets Christ's calm against human treachery and violence. Bartolomeo di Tommaso was a painter of Foligno in Umbria, and Passion cycles like this were common across central Italy.","c":"Bartolomeo di Tommaso belongs to the provincial Umbrian painters who absorbed the courtly International Gothic of Gentile da Fabriano and the clearer forms of Florence. In a small panel the story can be told briskly, and the tradition's love of decorative colour suits it. The drama of the subject, however, calls for more physical conviction than such a painter usually offers. It is an appealing narrative sketch rather than a gripping image."},"met:10240":{"d":"A man and a woman, a couple, share a music lesson in this canvas of 1870, about 61 by 51 centimeters. The museum's record lists a flute and a harp among the objects, so instruments are at the center of the scene, and the title names the occasion. Further details, such as the room and the costumes, are not recorded here. It is a genre picture of domestic leisure rather than a portrait, in the polished, anecdotal manner that made the English-born American painter John George Brown popular.","i":"Music lessons were a favorite subject of nineteenth-century genre painting because they could stand for courtship: the shared task, the closeness of the pair and the harmony of the instruments all hint at romance. Home music-making was also a mark of cultivated middle-class life, and a picture of it suited a parlor wall in America as in Europe. The flute and the harp, a wind and a string instrument, make a natural pairing.","c":"Brown paints with a polish that suits his subject, and the theme of music and courtship is pleasant, if conventional. The appeal is gentle charm and a sense of shared occupation, not psychological depth. Beside the enormously popular street children of his later career, this is a quieter, more genteel picture, and it is best judged as a competent period genre scene rather than as a statement."},"nga:166433":{"d":"Dock workers rest at midday on a busy quay, grouped among crates, baskets and bundles of cargo, with a horse-drawn cart at hand. They wear plain trousers, shirts and jackets in brown, red, white and light blue; some have hats, and some smoke pipes. Most have pale skin, but one man at the left has dark brown skin. Behind them, sailing ships and a steamboat trailing smoke stand against a pale-blue sky. The canvas is a wide format of about 84 by 127 centimeters, painted in 1879.","i":"John George Brown, born in England and settled in New York, was best known for sentimental pictures of street children, so a picture of adult working men is something of a departure. The title marks a break in the working day, the moment a genre painter could use to show laborers at ease. The mixed crew reflects the varied labor force of the American docks, though the picture presents it in a calm, orderly way, not as the hard and often dangerous work it was.","c":"Brown's strength is anecdote and a sure sense of grouping: the men form a loose, natural cluster around the goods and supplies, and the dock itself is convincingly cluttered under a clear light. But the mood is sunnier than the subject warranted, and, as in his street-children pictures, sentiment keeps the work from the harder realism of the period's best city painters. It remains a lively and valuable record of the waterfront."},"met:10241":{"d":"A genre scene of 1897, painted in oil on canvas, about 76 by 102 centimeters, in a wide horizontal format. The museum's record lists only a man, and the title, To Decide the Question, points to a moment of choice or dispute, but what is being decided is not recorded, so the figures, setting and props are not described here. It dates from the last decade of Brown's long career, when he was one of the best-known American painters of anecdotal, finely finished scenes.","i":"Titles of this kind invite the viewer to complete a small story, a favorite device of Victorian genre painting. The viewer reads expressions and gestures to guess the question and its likely answer, and the pleasure lies in the guessing. Brown, born in England and settled in New York, supplied American collectors with such narrative pictures for decades, and his audience expected a clear story told with good humor.","c":"Brown's reputation rests on sentiment and finish, and the same qualities presumably govern this late picture: a clear story, careful execution and an appeal to the viewer's good feeling. It is hard to rank without more evidence of its content, but critics generally see his work of the 1890s as repeating the successes of the 1880s. It is pleasing, perhaps, but seldom surprising, and its interest is more social than artistic."},"nga:220337":{"d":"A large ornate golden goblet overflowing with grapes and green leaves stands at the center, with a dark glass vase of tulips and roses in yellow, pink, white and red at the right. At the lower left lies a pile of small birds, some upside down with legs or wings extended, a few with red or green plumage. Everything rests on a brown surface against a black wall. The picture, about 50 by 67 centimeters, is painted in oil on panel around 1615, and it combines flowers, fruit and game in a single compact display.","i":"Snyders trained in Antwerp, was close to the circle of Jan Brueghel the Elder, and became the leading Flemish painter of still lifes of game, fruit and kitchens. A work of about 1615 belongs to his early period, before the great market and pantry scenes of his maturity. The goblet, flowers, grapes and birds show abundance and skill in a form that appealed to wealthy collectors, while the dead birds quietly recall that luxury and death are close.","c":"An early Snyders, and a delicate one: the golden goblet gives a glitter to the center, the flowers add color and the small birds provide texture. The arrangement is tidy and a little stiff, unlike the swirling, dramatic abundance of his later large pictures. It shows the debt to Brueghel's flower and fruit pieces, yet the painter's keen eye for feathers is already apparent. A charming, promising early work rather than a mature statement."},"nga:134540":{"d":"A basket overflowing with grapes, surrounded by dead birds and more fruit, sits on a long table covered with a scarlet cloth, in a horizontal panel about 90 by 112 centimeters. Green, blue-black, red and purple grapes are piled in a rough pyramid, with sage-green leaves rising nearly to the top edge. At the left a tipped blue-and-white bowl spills more grapes, and a tall bronze dish of figs is cut off by the edge. About two dozen small and medium birds fill the right half of the table, lit from the upper left against an earth-brown ground.","i":"Snyders became famous for market, pantry and game scenes that celebrate abundance on the grandest scale, and Flemish collectors enjoyed their sensual plenty. Grapes and figs are Mediterranean luxuries, the bowl suggests costly tableware, and the birds are the hunter's trophy. The cumulative effect is of a world at its ripest, which is the point: such pictures were meant to flatter the wealth of the household that owned them.","c":"Snyders is superb at the texture of things: the translucent skins of the grapes, the tousled feathers, the scarlet cloth. The pyramid of grapes gives the composition a clear center, and the sweep of birds to the right balances it. If there is a weakness, it is the sheer quantity: the abundance is so complete that the picture offers no resting place. Even so, it is a vivid example of Flemish still life in its confident, post-Rubens phase."},"nga:1109":{"d":"An upper-body portrait in a painted oval shows a woman with her body turned slightly to the right and her head facing the viewer. Her curly gray hair is tied with a pale pink ribbon, and she looks out with green eyes and a neutral expression, her cheeks rosy and her lips pink. She wears a dark dress with a low, lace-lined neckline and a pink bow at its center, and a light orange-pink sash. Behind her hangs a dark red curtain on the left, while on the right a blue sky is streaked with brown and gray clouds.","i":"The sitter is identified as Anne Elizabeth Pollock Hartigan, the wife of William Hartigan. The painter, the Swedish-born Carl Fredrik von Breda, worked in England between 1787 and 1796, and the picture belongs to that period, when he took up the English fashion for soft, unpretentious likenesses in the manner of Reynolds. The curtain-and-sky backdrop is a standard device of Georgian portraiture, placing a person of standing between domestic interior and open air.","c":"A pleasant, competent likeness more than a memorable one. Breda models the face softly, and the pink accents of ribbon, bow and sash give the dark dress a gentle lift, but the pose is formulaic and the curtain and sky are stock properties. The sitter's age is recorded honestly, without flattery, which counts in the picture's favor. It shows how thoroughly a visiting Swedish painter had absorbed the English portrait idiom, for better and worse: polish without surprise."},"met:437510":{"d":"A portrait of a man in tempera on a wood panel, about 52 by 33 centimeters, painted about 1481 to 1482 by the Florentine painter Cosimo Rosselli. His name is unknown. Florentine portraits of the 1480s were often bust-length, set against a plain or landscape ground. Tempera on panel was the standard medium of Florentine painting at this date, giving crisp edges and clear, matte color.","i":"Rosselli is best known for religious altarpieces and for his frescoes in the Sistine Chapel, painted in 1481 and 1482, and portraits are a small part of his output. In Florence in the 1480s, a portrait of a man could record a citizen's likeness for his family, mark an office or a marriage, or be a gift. The sitter here is anonymous, so the portrait's purpose cannot be stated.","c":"Rosselli was a respected but conservative Florentine master, overshadowed by younger rivals such as Ghirlandaio and Botticelli, and his portraits do not reach their psychological penetration. A straightforward male portrait in tempera, dated to the years of his Sistine work, is likely to be sober and well constructed rather than daring. It fills in the portraitist's side of a painter usually known for altarpieces."},"met:437509":{"d":"The Madonna and Child appear with two angels in a panel of tempera and gold on wood, about 85 by 58 centimeters, painted in the early 1480s by Cosimo Rosselli, one of the Florentine painters called to Rome to decorate the Sistine Chapel in those years. Gold was typical of Florentine devotional panels, where gilded haloes, borders and ornament gave sacred figures radiance.","i":"A Madonna and Child with angels was the commonest devotional image in Renaissance Florence, made for churches and chapels and also for private homes, where it hung over a bed or in a room as a focus for prayer and a protector of the family. The angels attend the mother and child as heavenly courtiers. Rosselli painted many such panels, and this one shows the established type rather than a particular story.","c":"Rosselli is reliable rather than inventive in his Madonnas: a clear arrangement, pleasant types and a good deal of gold, without the poetic tension of Botticelli or Filippo Lippi. Whatever touches of invention it may hold, it is a competent, representative piece of Florentine devotional painting of the 1480s."},"nga:66404":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, three narrow canoes, each paddled by a single person, glide toward the viewer along a river. The rowers wear white shirts rolled to the elbow, light blue trousers and wide straw-yellow hats pulled low; they sit in the bottom of their boats with legs extended, double-bladed paddles across their laps. The nearest canoe comes almost straight on, its tip cut off by the bottom edge of the canvas; the second follows a couple of lengths behind, the third is small in the distance, and part of a fourth enters from the right. Butter-yellow paddles reflect in sage-green and sky-blue water.","i":"The boats are périssoires, light canoes that were a fashionable amusement of the Paris middle class in the 1870s, and the river is the Yerres, which ran through the Caillebotte family estate outside Paris, where the painter spent his summers. Caillebotte, wealthy enough not to depend on sales, was a keen oarsman and sailor and also supported his fellow Impressionists, buying their work and helping to organize their exhibitions. Leisure on the river was a modern subject, and he treated it without anecdote.","c":"The design is daring: the high viewpoint, the canoe slicing off the lower edge of the picture and the steep recession to a distant third boat give a snapshot-like immediacy that recalls photography and Japanese prints. Caillebotte's careful draftsmanship makes the figures more solid than the usual Impressionist blur, while the shimmering reflections show that he could also work in the group's loose, colored touch. It is a fresh, modern composition and a fine example of his range."},"met:671456":{"d":"Chrysanthemums grow in the artist's own garden at Petit-Gennevilliers, on the Seine near Paris, in a tall vertical canvas of 1893, about 99 by 62 centimeters. The museum's record lists leaves and flowers, so the picture is dominated by foliage and blooms, and the title places them in the garden rather than in a vase. Further details of color and handling are not recorded here. It dates from the year before Caillebotte's death, when gardening and painting were closely linked in his daily life.","i":"Caillebotte, once known for his Paris street scenes and rowing pictures, spent his last years at Petit-Gennevilliers, where he gardened extensively and painted the garden and the river. A passionate gardener and a patron of his Impressionist friends, he treated flowers as a motif that joined his two enthusiasms. Chrysanthemums, an autumn flower long prized in Japan, were also admired in France for their variety of form and color.","c":"Caillebotte's late garden pictures are generally looser and brighter than his early, tightly drawn urban scenes, and the tall format suits the upright stems and heavy blooms of chrysanthemums. The pictures reflect the example of Monet and a decorative sense that anticipates early modern painting. Because the record says little of this particular canvas, it is safest to call it a representative late work, showing a painter who found in his own garden a final subject."},"nga:110067":{"d":"A woman in a pale blue jacket and long azure skirt walks a sunlit path that runs back to a cottage, past a bank of flowers. In the foreground dahlias in coral pink and golden yellow flare like fireworks from spruce-green foliage in the lower right. Long violet and steel-blue shadows fall across the white path, a strip of lawn lies beside it, and a small, wiry black dog looks out at the viewer. A mint-green greenhouse stands at the right, with windows tipped open. At the path's end, the house has an orange roof and peach walls under a turquoise sky.","i":"In 1888 Caillebotte settled permanently at Petit Gennevilliers, on the Seine near Paris, where he devoted himself to boat design, gardening and painting. The garden and its greenhouse were a passion, and in the 1890s it became his principal subject; here the dahlias, a late-summer flower, burst across the foreground. The picture shows the Impressionist ideal of subjects drawn from the painter's own daily life, with cultivated nature standing for modern, comfortable leisure.","c":"A large, assured canvas in which Caillebotte gives full rein to color. The coral and gold of the dahlias against deep green are almost startling, and the long violet shadows across the white path set up a strong diagonal that pulls the eye back to the house. The woman and the dog are small accents, not the point. For a painter remembered mostly for Paris street scenes, it shows a late, joyful turn to color."},"met:441358":{"d":"A caravan halts at desert wells, according to the title and the record's list of motifs: camels, men, some of them nude, and a desert setting. The canvas is large, about 111 by 140 centimeters, and was painted in 1850. The Orientalist subject, a halting place in the wilderness south of Hebron, is described by the museum only through these keywords, so the number of figures and the arrangement of the animals around the water are not recorded.","i":"The wells of Saba are those of Beersheba, the biblical place where Abraham and Isaac dug wells and made covenants, and the southern limit of the Holy Land in the phrase 'from Dan to Beersheba'. French painters of the mid-nineteenth century traveled to Palestine and Egypt in search of such sites and found in desert caravans a living image of Scripture. The painter is probably the father of Pierre de Coubertin, founder of the modern Olympic Games.","c":"Large, ambitious and conventional: an Orientalist set piece in which a biblical place provides the excuse for camels, costumes and nude male figures. Such works tend to rely on sharp light, exotic accessories and academic drawing of the figure; their weakness is stale exoticism, their strength the sheer labor of arrangement. A competent, little-known example of the type."},"met:435627":{"d":"The record lists a man, a woman, a couple, a tomb identified as that of Philippe Pot, and parasols, with 'Sleeping' and 'Cemeteries' among the keywords. The title, In the Sun, suggests an outdoor scene in bright light in which figures rest or stroll near the monument. The canvas is horizontal, about 60 by 96 centimeters, dated 1875; no further detail of the arrangement, the faces or the colors is given by the museum.","i":"The tomb of Philippe Pot, a Burgundian nobleman who died in 1494, is celebrated for its eight hooded mourners carrying the effigy on their shoulders, one of the best-known works of late medieval sculpture. Setting modern leisure, with a sleeper and parasols, beside such a monument would make a gentle contrast between present pleasure and the memory of death, in the spirit of a memento mori.","c":"The appeal of the subject is the contrast between sunlit leisure and a monument to the dead, and the keywords hint at a gentle humor, with a man asleep among the visitors. Beaumont, who also worked as a lithographer, painted anecdotal scenes of this kind in the humorous strand of French genre painting. The result is likely competent and amusing, though more a clever idea than a memorable painting."},"nga:119019":{"d":"A nearly square panel, about 76 by 71 centimeters, painted in oil around 1815, shows an arched alcove crammed with a poor man's possessions: a crust of bread on a plate with a black-handled knife, a short glass of water, a battered black top hat, a pink conch shell and more than a dozen books and papers. Much of it is lettered with jokes. The spines read Choice Criticism on the Exhibitions at Philadelphia, Lives of the Painters, Miseries of Life and Unpaid Bills, and a clipping announces a sheriff's sale of the artist's property.","i":"The picture is a trompe l'oeil, a painting designed to fool the eye, and a satire on the fate of an artist in a young republic with little patronage. The books mock an artist's reading, the notices record his debts, and the bread and water stand for his diet. Two calling cards addressed to Palette show friends who visit and creditors who collect. King, who had studied in London, probably drew on his own experience and that of other painters in Philadelphia.","c":"This is the sharpest of King's pictures and the most enjoyable: the illusion is convincing, the titles are witty, and the whole is arranged as a clever, tightly composed alcove. It is also a rare example of humor in early American painting, with a bite beneath the jokes. The risk of such a picture is that it is read more than looked at, but the painting of the bread, shell and hat shows real craft."},"nga:195003":{"d":"A man sits behind a table, his torso facing the viewer and his head turned slightly to the right, in a portrait of 1821, about 92 by 71 centimeters. He has a fair complexion, a strong brow, a slight smile and short brown hair falling across his forehead in loose waves. He wears a black coat with gold buttons, a white vest and a high white cravat. His right hand holds a long sheet of writing and his left points to it; on the red-covered table are a silver inkwell and a quill, against a brown ground.","i":"The sitter is Henry Clay, the Kentucky statesman, who had been Speaker of the House of Representatives and was celebrated as an orator and a maker of compromises. The paper, inkwell and quill present him at work on public business, a convention of portraits of lawmakers, and the slight smile adds warmth to an official image. King, who had settled in Washington, painted many of the capital's politicians and diplomats.","c":"King was a capable, workmanlike portraitist, and this picture is efficient: a clear pose, an economy of props and a slight smile that gives some life to a stock arrangement. It is not an inventive work. The table, paper and drapery are standard devices, and the handling is smooth rather than vivid. Its interest is that of an authoritative image of a leading public figure, in a modest, trustworthy style that the capital's politicians seem to have liked."},"nga:174950":{"d":"About two dozen men, women, children and a baby sit in rows on wooden benches or on traveling trunks in this horizontal panel, about 72 by 91 centimeters, dated 1884. A woman nursing a baby sits at the center, in a white blouse, red vest and long black skirt, with a young girl in a blue shawl behind her; pans, a jug and a tin cup stand by their trunks. Men in bowler hats, caps and suits sit alone or chat, and one sleeps against a bag. A sign on a column reads Emigrant Landing Depot.","i":"From 1855 to 1890 Castle Garden, at the southern tip of Manhattan, was the main reception center for immigrants arriving in the United States, before Ellis Island opened in 1892. Newcomers wait here with their trunks and bundles to be registered, a mix of ages and origins, with the nursing mother and the watchful girl at the human center. The title names the hope that brought them; the picture shows the long wait that came first.","c":"Ulrich's picture is a work of observation more than drama. The crowd is varied, the figures lost in thought or watching, and the details of trunks, pans and a sleeping man are rendered with patient realism. The nursing woman gives the group a focal point, and the Emigrant Landing Depot sign is a nice touch of documentary truth. It avoids sentimentality, though not a certain stagey arrangement. A fine record of the era of mass immigration."},"met:12846":{"d":"An interior workshop on the Venetian island of Murano is shown with glassblowers at work, men and a woman in the room, according to the record, with the keywords Interiors, Working and Venetian glass. The panel is upright, about 66 by 54 centimeters, painted in 1886 in oil on wood. The museum gives no further description of the furnace, the tools or the faces, so the arrangement of the figures is not detailed here.","i":"Murano has been the home of Venetian glassmaking since the late thirteenth century, when the furnaces were moved there from Venice to reduce the risk of fire. In the 1880s Charles Frederick Ulrich, a New Yorker who studied in Munich and worked in Venice, painted such artisans in meticulous detail, in the Dutch and German tradition of the workshop interior. The picture was presented to the museum by a group of gentlemen in the year it was painted.","c":"Ulrich's Munich training shows in the patient description of tools, glass and hands; such pictures were prized in the 1880s for their finish and for the dignity they gave to manual skill. The tightness that wins admiration also limits delight, and the crowded interior can feel more cataloged than lived. A well-made, rather literal picture of a craft, typical of American genre painting abroad."},"met:11779":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Joseph Howell, Jr., painted around 1787 in oil on canvas and measuring about 58 by 49 centimeters, a modest bust format. The record lists 'man' and 'portrait', and the museum gives no description of his dress, pose or expression. It was given in 1982 by Mrs. Henry W. Howell in memory of Henry Wilson Howell, so it probably descended in the sitter's family.","i":"Charles Peale Polk was a nephew of the great Philadelphia portraitist Charles Willson Peale and learned painting from him; he worked in Maryland and Philadelphia in the 1780s and 1790s. Portraits like this one of the late 1780s served Americans in the years after the Revolution who wished to record themselves in their new republic. The sitter is known only through the title.","c":"Polk lacks his uncle's London training and fine detail: his faces are clear and a little flat, his color bright and his manner direct. The charm of such portraits lies in that plainness, and in the record they give of a young republic's middle class, not in any subtlety. A modest, honest likeness of a man of the post-Revolutionary generation."},"met:11778":{"d":"A portrait of George Washington, painted around 1790 in oil on canvas and measuring about 55 by 45 centimeters. The record lists only the sitter, a man and a portrait, so Washington's costume, pose and background are not described. Charles Peale Polk painted many portraits of Washington, most of them derived from his uncle's work, and this small canvas likely belongs to that series.","i":"In 1790 Washington was in the second year of his presidency, and the demand for his likeness far exceeded what any painter could supply from life. Charles Willson Peale painted him several times, and his nephew Polk produced many versions for those who wanted the national hero on their wall. Such portraits were acts of civic piety as much as pictures.","c":"These small Washingtons were produced for a market of admirers, and their value lies in their patriotism more than in artistry. Polk's handling is simpler than that of Peale or Stuart, and his Washington can look stiff, but there is a kind of homespun sincerity that suits the democratic cult of the first president. As a type, the most reproduced American image of the era in its humblest form."},"nga:34045":{"d":"A portrait of General George Washington, recalling his victory at Princeton in January 1777, painted around 1790 in oil on canvas and measuring about 91 by 70 centimeters. The museum's record lists no objects or details of costume, so the figure's dress, pose and background are not described here. By its title and date it belongs to the tradition of commemorative portraits made in the early republic, when many Americans wanted an image of the Revolution's commander for the parlor or the public room.","i":"Charles Peale Polk was a nephew and pupil of Charles Willson Peale, whose portrait of Washington at Princeton was widely copied, and Polk painted several versions of Washington for patrons in Maryland and Virginia. The victory at Princeton was one of the turning points of the war, and a picture of the commander at that place proclaimed patriotism and linked its owner to the cause.","c":"Polk was a modest follower of his uncle, and a picture like this is mainly a vehicle for an image rather than a bold interpretation. Because the record gives few details, it is best seen as a representative example of the Washington portraits that served the new nation's need for icons. It typically lacks the polish of Peale's originals, and what it offers in exchange is directness, a plainness and stiffness that approach folk art."},"nga:42435":{"d":"A girl stands full-length in a garden, her body slightly angled and her left arm resting on a stone pedestal, in a tall portrait painted around 1790, about 147 by 96 centimeters. She has straight brown hair with bangs, dark eyes and a calm expression, and wears a light pink dress with puffed sleeves, a fitted bodice and a full, lace-trimmed skirt, with a pink sash and red shoes. In her right hand she holds a pink rose, and a vase of roses seems to stand on the pedestal. Dense green foliage and a partly cloudy blue sky fill the background.","i":"A girl with a rose in a garden is a standard image of youth, innocence and the promise of beauty, and the roses on the pedestal repeat the point. Full-length portraits of children, in the manner of English portrait painters, were an ambitious and costly commission in the young United States, so the picture signals a family of means. The stone pedestal and garden setting are borrowed from the grand portraiture of Europe and adapted to a child.","c":"Polk's portraiture is plain, and the charm of this picture is the combination of a firm, flat figure with a decorative setting of roses, pink silk and green leaves. The proportions are not academic, and the figure sits on the canvas rather than in the space, but the directness and bright color make a lasting impression. It is an appealing example of American provincial portraiture of the Federal period, more honest than polished."},"nga:176396":{"d":"The upper half of a man seated in a chair, his body angled to the right and his head turned toward the viewer, in a portrait painted around 1802 to 1810, about 93 by 75 centimeters. He has a fair complexion, dark eyes and short, dark, curly hair, and wears a reddish-brown coat with gold buttons, a white waistcoat and a neat white cravat. His right hand holds a sheet of writing, and his left rests in his lap near a chain hung with small ornaments. Deep green drapery fills one side of the background and a plain beige wall the other.","i":"Thomas Corcoran was a merchant in Georgetown, near Washington, and the father of the later banker and art patron William Wilson Corcoran. The paper in his hand and the chain at his lap present him as a man of business and means, in the standard imagery of the American merchant class. The portrait has a pendant of his wife, Hannah, and the pair belong to the years when Georgetown was growing fast.","c":"Polk's formula is clear here: a seated figure, a plain wall, a drapery and a few props. The head is firmly drawn and the costume neatly worked, but the figure is stiff and the space is shallow. The portrait makes up in directness what it lacks in sophistication, and the green drapery adds a note of ambition. It is a decent, plain-spoken likeness, much like many portraits of the early republic."},"nga:178111":{"d":"The upper body of a woman seated beside a wooden table, facing slightly to the right, with her arm on the tabletop and a small pink rose in her left hand, in a portrait painted around 1802 to 1810, about 91 by 66 centimeters. She has a calm expression, rosy cheeks, brown eyes and dark curly hair, partly hidden by a white lace cap. Her white high-waisted gown has long sleeves with lace trim and delicate embroidery. A small white vase of similar pink roses stands on the table before a deep green drapery.","i":"The white high-waisted gown, in the Empire style that reached America in the early 1800s, shows that Hannah Corcoran followed fashion, and the roses are the usual signs of domestic virtue and beauty. The portrait is the companion of her husband Thomas's, and the pair, the merchant with his paper and his wife with her flowers, express a division of roles typical of the day. Hannah and Thomas were the parents of William Wilson Corcoran.","c":"As a companion to her husband's portrait, this picture is softer and more decorative: the lace, the rose and the white gown give Polk more to do than the dark coat of Thomas. The drawing is simple and the modeling modest, but the pale dress against the green drapery makes a pleasing effect, and the calm face gives the sitter dignity. A small, sound example of provincial portraiture, interesting for its family connections."},"nga:164942":{"d":"Two bottles, a carafe, a drinking glass, a plate, a knife and a loaf of bread stand on a narrow table covered with a bright, slightly rumpled white cloth, in a horizontal canvas about 40 by 60 centimeters. The table angles toward the wall at the right, and the shadows in the cloth are silvery gray. A square glass bottle of amber liquid runs off the left edge, a long-necked carafe is half filled, and a green bottle probably holds red wine. A broken crust of the long loaf lies nearby. The paint is loose, so some details are indistinct.","i":"Painted when Monet was in his early twenties, around 1862 to 1863, this still life belongs to a plain, unshowy tradition of table subjects that runs from Chardin to Courbet and Manet. Bread, wine and glass are the humble materials of the meal, and Monet's treatment of them, with strong light on a white cloth and loose handling, shows an early interest in the effects of light rather than in the objects as symbols.","c":"A young painter's exercise, but a good one. The white cloth, broken by silvery shadows, is a strong simple shape against the darker wall, and the loose handling of glass and bottles already prefers sensation to detail. The arrangement is a little awkward, with the bottle cut by the left edge and the dark square at the top hard to read, yet the casualness hints at a modern eye. It is an early step in Monet's long study of light."},"nga:52185":{"d":"A woman in a long slate-gray gown walks beside a man in a suit and cap through a wood, in a vertical painting about 93 by 69 centimeters, painted in 1865 and signed at lower right. She is seen from behind, turned slightly so that her cheek and the tip of her nose show under a silvery-gray hat, and her bodice is embellished with swirling black lines. The man, with sideburns, a full beard and a gray cap, leans toward her, holding a short stick. White strokes on the path suggest dappled sunlight, and leaves are swipes of celery and sage green.","i":"The picture is a study for Monet's ambitious Luncheon on the Grass, which he began in 1865 in answer to Manet's scandalous picture of the same title. Monet's friend, the painter Frédéric Bazille, and Camille Doncieux, who modeled for him and later became his wife, posed for the figures. Monet left the huge canvas unfinished, so studies like this one are important evidence of his intention: figures in contemporary dress, moving in dappled forest light.","c":"Painted quickly, the study is livelier than many finished Salon pictures of the day. The dappled white strokes on the path and the sparkling greens of the wood point forward to Impressionism, while the dark, fashionably dressed figures still belong to the 1860s tradition of Manet and Courbet. Detail is vague, which is the point: Monet is after light, not likeness. A fresh, spontaneous record of a major ambition that never reached completion."},"nga:61375":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, a baby lies in an oval cradle draped in a sky-blue cloth patterned with pink flowers and green leaves, while a woman sits on the far side with her back mostly toward the viewer, looking down at him. The baby's dark eyes are wide open, his cheeks flushed, his head wrapped in a white hood with a blue bow; in one fist he holds a small drum on an orange stick, and a pinwheel lies across his legs. She wears a gray-and-black striped dress with a white collar and a white cap. The brushwork is loose, and the lower right dissolves into brown.","i":"The woman is Camille Doncieux, Monet's companion and later his wife, and the child is their son Jean, born in 1867. Monet was short of money that year, and the picture is an unusual private image of domestic tenderness from a painter better known for landscape. Its subject, a mother watching a child, links it to a long tradition of images of motherhood, but here the mood is of a real household rather than an allegory.","c":"Monet's loose handling and the plunging view into the cradle give an intimacy rarely found in his work, and the blue cloth, with the baby's alert eyes, is the focus of a quiet, affectionate picture. The weaker areas are the woman's back and the lower right, which dissolve into vague brown, but the looseness suits a subject so small and so close. Among the tenderest pictures of his early career, it shows how well he could paint people."},"nga:71550":{"d":"From a slightly elevated viewpoint, a silvery-gray beach curves along a hilly coastline in this horizontal canvas, about 57 by 80 centimeters. Brown and black boats are pulled up on the sand among poles and an anchor, and a man stands beside a woman holding a baby, with other adults and children nearby, painted in a few quick touches. Dunes in moss green and camel brown rise at the right to hills dotted with small buildings, and a long flat promontory closes the distance. Heather-gray and white clouds fill most of the sky, and the bay is a frosty sea-foam green.","i":"Sainte-Adresse is a seaside village on the Channel coast near Le Havre, where Monet's family had a house and where he painted in 1867. In place of the sunlit resort scenes he painted there in the same period, this is a working beach: boats hauled up, a family on the sand, a gray sky. It belongs to the tradition of Normandy coast painting, with its attention to sea and weather, from Boudin and Jongkind, whose lessons Monet had absorbed.","c":"The composition is simple and broad: the beach as a pale band, the sky as a weighty roof and the curve of the coast leading the eye inward. The loose figures are just flecks, but they give life and scale to the shore, and the cool palette of gray and green is subtle and true to the Channel's weather. It is more somber than the brighter Sainte-Adresse pictures of the same year, and its restraint is a strength."},"nga:61376":{"d":"In a darkened room, two women sit at a round table near a man who leans against a fireplace mantel, all lit only by a soft yellow light from a hanging ceiling lamp, in a nearly square canvas about 50 by 65 centimeters. The lamp, painted in butter and golden yellow, is the brightest thing in the room. The table is bare except for a white teapot and two teacups. One woman, in a pine-green dress, looks down at something white in her hands, probably needlework; the other, in slate blue, sits with her back turned. A third chair is empty. The bearded man in black stands in shadow.","i":"After dinner, tea and needlework: Monet paints a quiet moment of a bourgeois household in the evening, with the lamplight gathering the figures and leaving the rest of the room in shadow. The empty chair and the shaded eyes invite the viewer to wonder what is thought or left unsaid. Interiors of this kind, with ambiguous relations between figures, were part of a modern taste for scenes of private life that Monet shared with Degas and Manet.","c":"Monet is better known for open air, and the dark, brown-gold palette of this interior is a surprise. The lamplight is handled with great economy: the brightest note is the lamp, and the figures emerge as simple shapes in a soft glow. The loosely painted detail suits an after-dinner mood. It is not a typical Monet, and some will find it murky, but the daring of the artificial light makes it memorable."},"nga:52186":{"d":"A sun-dappled dirt road runs away from the viewer beside a shimmering river under a blue sky dotted with white clouds, in a horizontal canvas about 50 by 65 centimeters. Tall thin trees with narrow dark green crowns line the right side of the road, and marshy grasses grow on the left. The road ends or curves in front of a row of terracotta-orange and gray buildings and smokestacks along the horizon. Two women and a child rest in the shadows at the foot of the trees, and two sailboats drift on the glassy water. The brushwork is loose and visible.","i":"In 1871 Monet settled at Argenteuil, a village on the Seine a few miles from Paris that was turning into both a weekend resort and an industrial suburb. The painting catches this mixture: boats and picnickers on one side, chimneys on the horizon. Monet painted the town and the river over several years, and his pictures there set the pattern of Impressionism: contemporary life, outdoor light and quick, visible brushwork.","c":"The diagonal sweep of the road and the sparkle of light on road and water give the picture an instant sense of a summer day, and the loose, broken strokes create a convincing shimmer. The smokestacks on the horizon are a candid, unpicturesque note, and the small figures are only a hint. It is a modest picture, less striking than Monet's best Argenteuil canvases, but a good illustration of how he built light from touches of color."},"nga:52187":{"d":"Two sailing ships with cream-white sails and dark rigging ride on a shimmering, ice-blue river against a distant shore of trees, hills and buildings, in a small, nearly square canvas about 38 by 46 centimeters. Their masts and sails are silhouetted against a topaz-blue sky with wispy clouds, and rust-orange anchors show near the pointed bows. Tall, narrow trees line the far bank, with towers and spires clustered at the left and small boats at the right. The reflections are painted with horizontal dashes and squiggles of paint.","i":"Monet painted at Rouen in 1872, soon after settling at Argenteuil, finding in the busy port a subject of river traffic and distant towers. The picture belongs to the first years of Impressionism, a moment when the group was learning to treat reflections on water as broken, flickering color rather than as smooth mirrors. The ships, anchored and idle, give the scene a calm that lets the light take the lead.","c":"The reflections, flickering dashes and squiggles beneath the hulls, are what make the picture: the water seems to move, and the ice-blue tone gives the whole scene a cool clarity. The boats are set down as simple, bold silhouettes against the sky, and the cluster of spires at the left is arranged with a light touch. On this small scale Monet is concise and sure, and the picture shows the Impressionist method in miniature."},"nga:72138":{"d":"A bank of flowering bushes, possibly roses though the title names dahlias, fills most of an enclosed garden in front of a white house, in a horizontal canvas about 61 by 83 centimeters. The blooms are dabs of scarlet, pale yellow, rust orange and shell pink among greens of many shades. At the right a couple strolls near a fence, a few strokes each: one in a white dress and hat, the other in a dark gray suit. A pale three-story house with a pitched roof and aquamarine shutters stands behind, under an ultramarine sky. It is signed and dated 73.","i":"Monet moved to Argenteuil at the end of 1871 and made the garden of his rented house a recurring subject, a place where family life, flowers and Impressionist color could be combined. The garden was a middle-class pleasure, tended and enjoyed within the home's own grounds. Painting it, rather than a grand landscape, was a statement of the Impressionists' belief that the ordinary surroundings of modern life were worth painting.","c":"A cloud of color rather than a picture of flowers: the blooms dissolve into dabs, and the interest lies in how scarlet, yellow and pink hold against the greens. The tiny strolling couple add scale and a note of leisure, and the white house anchors the composition. As an early Argenteuil garden picture it shows Monet already at ease with the broken touch that defined the group, and with a joy in color that never turns heavy."},"nga:61374":{"d":"Two sailboats and a rowboat float on a glistening azure river spanned by a gray stone bridge at the right, in a horizontal canvas about 60 by 80 centimeters dated 1874. The rippling, sunlit surface is built from short parallel strokes of pale pink, caramel, white, sage green and light blue. One sailboat lies near the viewer with its sails furled; another, farther out, carries full white sails. In the rowboat two figures are sketched with a few touches, one holding a pink parasol. People stand at the bridge railing, and a coral-pink building and a row of green trees sit beneath a baby-blue sky.","i":"Argenteuil in 1874 was a popular boating center a short train ride from Paris, and Monet, who lived there from 1871 to 1878, made its regattas, sailboats and bridges his subjects. The bridge, the rowboat with its parasol and the onlookers at the railing present the leisure of the Paris middle classes, a theme the Impressionists took up in place of the history and myth that the Salon still favored.","c":"Monet's water is the star: short parallel strokes of pink, brown and green turn the river into shimmering light, and the furled sailboat anchors the foreground. The figures are quick flecks, and the bridge is handled with a sure sense of angle and shade. It is among the freshest of his Argenteuil views and an excellent demonstration of how the Impressionists made reflections out of broken color."},"nga:61379":{"d":"Seen from below against a bright, cloud-flecked sky, a woman in a pale gown stands at the crest of a grassy hill, a green parasol tilted over her head. Wind pulls at her veil and sweeps the long skirt, painted in streaks of pale blue, gray, and white, and her shadow slants across the grass toward the viewer. At the lower left a small boy in a straw hat appears from the waist up, half hidden by the rise of the ground. Buttercup yellow and plum purple flowers fleck the tall grass, and the clouds are dabbed in short, choppy strokes. Her face is barely defined. The parasol almost touches the top edge, and the signature is in blue at the lower right.","i":"The models are Monet's wife Camille and their son Jean, then about seven, on a walk near Argenteuil in 1875. Monet treats them not as a posed family group but as a passing moment, a gust and a pause on a hillside, which is typical of the Impressionist ambition to catch a fleeting effect of light and air. It was one of eighteen works by Monet in the second Impressionist exhibition of April 1876. Camille died in 1879; in 1886 Monet returned to the motif with two canvases of Alice Hoschedé's daughter Suzanne, now in the Musée d'Orsay. Paul Mellon gave this picture to the National Gallery of Art in 1983.","c":"The picture is celebrated for its freshness, and the reputation holds: the low viewpoint lifts the figure into the sky, and the sky is treated as an equal partner, not a backdrop. As a portrait it is thin, with Camille nearly faceless and the boy a small, half-hidden presence, and dress and grass are painted with the same touch, so that human feeling gives way to weather. Because the weather is the subject, the choice works. The later Giverny variations are looser, but this first version keeps a human warmth they lack."},"nga:52188":{"d":"Looking across a glimmering river toward docks, about a dozen small boats and a mustard-brown boxy building, the viewer sees a bridge that crosses the whole width of this horizontal canvas, about 61 by 80 centimeters, painted around 1876. The deck rests on two shallow gray arches springing from an ivory abutment, and dots of red and gray on it suggest vehicles or walkers. The river is painted in blended silvery gray, icy blue and pale lavender around low-sided sailboats and a turquoise houseboat. The sky is made of long swipes of cream and smoke gray.","i":"The title records a mood: after the sunny regatta scenes of 1874, Monet turned in the later 1870s to subdued, overcast effects, showing that Impressionism concerned atmosphere in all conditions, not only on bright days. The bridge and the docks belong to the working side of Argenteuil, a town of industry as well as boating, and Monet's attention to them reflects his habit of painting the world as it was in his own day.","c":"A gray day suits Monet: the silvery water, the pale lavender reflections and the flat sky make a unified, softly lit scene in which the colors are muted but never dull. The long bridge divides the canvas decisively, and the small boats make an easy rhythm below it. What the picture lacks in sparkle it gains in tonal subtlety. It is less showy than the sunlit Argenteuil pictures and more convincing as a record of one particular day."},"nga:178254":{"d":"Tall willow trees nearly span the height of this horizontal canvas, about 67 by 82 centimeters, with a river and a cluster of low buildings on the far bank beyond. The palette is almost entirely cool: spring, sage, celery and kelly greens and steel and sea blues. Grasses in the foreground are painted with diagonal strokes that change direction and color as they recede, golden daubs in the lower right suggest blooms, and the leaves are thin upward strokes along slender branches. The sky layers beige, blush pink and spring green over pale blue; the river is lavender gray, and the buildings are ochre, peach and denim blue.","i":"Willows lined the Seine near Vétheuil, where Monet worked from 1878 to 1881, and he painted them in several pictures of 1880. Tall trunks and fine, feathery foliage against a pale sky gave him a pattern of vertical and diagonal lines, and the scene's cool tones capture the freshness of spring. The painters of his circle were interested in such unspectacular motifs as proof that any corner of nature could make a picture.","c":"The pattern of the trunks, the lacy leaves and the diagonal grass gives the picture a pleasing rhythm, and the cool palette is cohesive. The unspectacular subject is also a strength: nothing happens, and the viewer is left with light, air and color. The building on the far bank is a small, almost unnoticed anchor. It is a restrained and harmonious landscape, closer to a meditation than to the bright, busy Argenteuil scenes of a few years before."},"nga:46652":{"d":"The viewer looks across a strip of tall grass and wildflowers at a river that winds into the distance, in a loosely painted horizontal canvas about 73 by 101 centimeters, signed and dated 80. The lower half is a mass of wildflowers made from daubs of lemon yellow and white among vertical strokes of moss, laurel and pine green. The river beyond is painted in horizontal dashes of blue, and a line of tall, narrow trees and rounded canopies marks the horizon two-thirds of the way up. The sky is slate blue and cream, paler at the right where sunlight seems to break through.","i":"Monet lived at Vétheuil, a village on the Seine downstream from Paris, from 1878 to 1881, a hard period that included the death of his wife Camille in 1879. He painted the river and its banks repeatedly. A foreground of wildflowers, rather than a neat framed view, makes the scene feel found rather than arranged, and it shows the Impressionist taste for unspectacular corners of the countryside.","c":"The foreground is the picture: a mass of upright strokes and daubs that is almost abstract at close range and convincing as meadow at a distance. Setting the river so far back gives the scene depth and a sense of space, and the high horizon keeps the eye among the flowers. It is a good example of how, by 1880, Monet could suggest a whole bank of wildflowers with a handful of colors and a free hand."},"nga:46653":{"d":"A woman sits among crescent-shaped blades of emerald and lemon-green grass under tall trees, in a vertical canvas about 81 by 60 centimeters, signed and dated 1880. She faces left almost in profile, her form built from thick strokes of eggshell white, periwinkle blue, mint green and deep pink, suggesting a pale dress pooled around her legs and a hat above dark hair. Tree trunks are streaks of cobalt blue and lilac, with whip-like branches reaching upward. Beyond, a sand-colored band, perhaps a path or river, runs below blue buildings and a pale butter-yellow sky.","i":"Figures at rest in the landscape were a favorite subject of the Impressionists, who painted them outdoors as part of the scene rather than as portraits. Monet's sitter is not identified, and her face is only a blush of color, so she serves as a note of human presence within a lyrical setting. Willows of the Seine valley were a recurring motif at Vétheuil, where he worked in 1880.","c":"The woman is a trace, a few strokes of white and pink, and that is the point: the picture is about the vertical rhythm of trunks and branches against a pale sky and the light playing in the grass. The tall format suits the willows, and the delicate, almost feathery handling gives the scene a sense of breeze. It shows Monet reducing figure and landscape alike to touches of color."},"nga:46656":{"d":"A cloud of canary-yellow flowers with pumpkin-orange centers fills the top half of this vertical canvas, about 100 by 73 centimeters, signed and dated 1880. The long, pointed petals are like those of daisies, and the tall stems carry emerald and pine-green leaves down to the narrow neck of an ivory-white cylindrical vase. The vase seems to stand on a round tabletop, cropped by the left and bottom edges, perhaps on a cloth with pompom-like edging. The background is long vertical strokes of salmon pink, pale turquoise, denim blue and cream.","i":"Jerusalem artichokes, tall garden plants grown for their edible tubers, bear daisy-like yellow flowers in late summer, and Monet found in the rustic bloom a subject as rich in color as any hothouse bouquet. Flower still lifes were a modest but readily salable side of Impressionist painting, and in them the painters could study color relations without the demands of a landscape.","c":"Monet treats a modest garden flower with the freedom of a landscape: the yellows burn against the cooler pinks and blues of the ground, and the vase and table are barely more than a few strokes. The cropped table and the tall, unruly bouquet make a bold, unfussy design. It lacks the formality of a traditional flower piece, which is its attraction, and it shows how Monet could make anything a vehicle for light and color."},"nga:52189":{"d":"A small child stands facing the viewer on a sun-dappled path that runs up the center of a garden crowded with towering yellow and burnt-orange sunflowers, in a large vertical canvas about 152 by 121 centimeters. Four blue-and-white porcelain urns of coral-pink and red flowers line the path, and long sea-green and plum-purple shadows cross its peach color. Farther back, a woman in a cornflower-blue and white dress and another child stand on the steps of a petal-pink house with an amethyst roof and blue latticework windows, under a vivid sky. A few strokes in front of the child may be a small dog.","i":"Monet lived at Vétheuil from 1878 to 1881, and the garden of his house there, planted with sunflowers and other flowers, became a subject in its own right. The path leading from the viewer to the house, and the child on it, invite the eye to enter, and the family figures tie the garden to a household life. The sunflowers, taller than the children, give the scene a sense of abundance that may be read as a retreat from the hardships of the artist's life at the time.","c":"One of the most inventive of Monet's garden pictures: the path leading up the center, the sunflowers like a tall hedge of color and the child as a small, central accent give a firm composition, while the strokes remain free and the colors vibrate. At this large scale the picture also asserts that a garden was a worthy subject for ambitious painting. The faces are blurred and the figures tiny, and it matters not at all."},"nga:66425":{"d":"From a high viewpoint, two overlapping emerald-green hills form a shallow V in the foreground, and beyond them lies powder-blue water dotted with about three dozen sailboats, rowboats and one steamship, in a wide canvas about 60 by 100 centimeters, signed 82. Two women in hats and long, fitted jackets and skirts of dusky pink and dove gray sit and stand on the left-hand hill, one with a red veil lifted by the wind. The paint is so thin in places, especially the sky, that the texture of the canvas shows through. The horizon comes two-thirds of the way up.","i":"In 1882 Monet worked on the Normandy coast at Pourville, near Dieppe, painting cliffs, beaches and the sea in changing weather. The two women, small and fashionably dressed, show that the coast was a holiday place as well as a workplace, and they also give a measure of scale against the wide view. The high, tilted viewpoint recalls Japanese prints, which Monet admired and collected.","c":"The picture is largely about air: the thin, blended paint of the sky, the turquoise and mauve sea, and the V of green hills make a wide, light, almost breezy space. The women are a light touch of human scale, and the red veil is the one lively accent. The paint is so thin in places that the canvas shows, which adds to the sense of speed and lightness. A fresh and beautifully judged seaside view."},"nga:45872":{"d":"Tall oval forms slowly emerge from a field of softly painted white, pale sky blue, shell pink, light lilac and faint yellow to become trees in a hazy, foggy landscape, in a nearly square canvas about 74 by 93 centimeters. The paint is swept on in short, visible strokes. The tallest tree, reaching almost to the top, stands left of center; three shorter, bushier trees flank it, and two narrow ones stand between. Monet signed the work in orange at lower left.","i":"Mist and haze dissolve outlines and merge colors, which is exactly what interested Monet: the scene is less a thing than an atmosphere, and the trees are a few vertical accents within it. Morning light was one of the effects he sought at different hours, and by the end of the 1880s he was approaching the idea of treating a single motif in successive versions, a method that led to his famous series of the 1890s.","c":"This is the subtlest kind of picture, almost vanishing: forms barely distinguished, colors pale and warm, and a few trees as darker notes. The softness is the whole point, and the near-square format suits the stillness. It is a difficult kind of painting, since the effect depends on tone rather than drawing, and Monet handles it with great delicacy. Viewers who want incident will find little, but those who want atmosphere will be rewarded."},"nga:46524":{"d":"Looking steeply up, the viewer sees the west façade of Rouen Cathedral soaring the height of this vertical canvas, about 100 by 66 centimeters, signed and dated 94. The surface is built from dabs of azure, peach, lavender and camel brown so that the stone looks blurred, as if reflected in a rippling puddle. The façade angles away to the left. The central portal, with nested pointed arches, rises to a tall gable that overlaps a dark rose window, and spires flank the center and run off the top edge. The sky is a few daubs of white and pale blue.","i":"In 1892 and 1893 Monet rented rooms opposite the cathedral at Rouen and painted its façade in some thirty canvases at different hours and in different weather, then reworked them in his studio. His subject was not the Gothic building as such but the changing light on it, so that the stone becomes a surface for color. The group, shown together in 1895, was among the first modern series, and it presented Impressionism as a method of seeing.","c":"The cathedral is nearly dissolved: the Gothic carving is no more than a thickened crust of colored paint, and the portal and rose window hold their places as patterns of light and shadow. It is a daring painting, and its refusal to describe the stone is its achievement. The risk of a series is monotony; each canvas must justify itself by its particular color, and here the blues, peaches and lavenders create a distinct, cool harmony."},"nga:46654":{"d":"Looking steeply up, the viewer sees the west façade of Rouen Cathedral soaring the height of a vertical canvas, about 100 by 66 centimeters, signed and dated 94. The stone is a blurred weave of sky blue, cream, golden tan and touches of shell pink, as if seen in a rippling puddle. The façade turns away to the left, with its portal of nested pointed arches, tall gable, round rose window and flanking spires. The sky is a flat field of pale lapis blue, and a few dark dabs near the lower left suggest tiny people standing at the foot of the wall.","i":"Here the dominant tones are cream, gold and pale pink, the color of sunlight on stone, as the title says. Monet worked on several canvases of the façade in turn, changing from one to the next as the light changed, so each picture records a particular quality of light rather than a general view of the building. The tiny people near the base measure the scale of the church and show how little the human world counts against it.","c":"Sunlight turns the stone to cream and gold, and the picture glows. The subject is hardly the church but the effect of light on it, and in this version the warm, heavy paint of the façade stands against a flat pale-blue sky. The weight of the paint gives a surface like worn stone. It is less dramatic than some canvases in the series, but gentle and convincing."},"nga:46655":{"d":"The rounded, feathery crowns of trees are mirrored in the placid surface of a river, in a horizontal canvas about 82 by 101 centimeters. The largest tree stands right of center, built from touches of cucumber and emerald green, eggplant purple, indigo and smoke gray. Bushes and trees to either side are dashes of cool sky blue, mint and sage green and muted rust red. The horizon runs across the middle, so that the sky and its reflection below, painted in longer curving strokes of parchment white and pale shell pink, take up equal space.","i":"Monet lived at Giverny from 1883, and in the later 1890s he painted a quiet arm of the Seine nearby, often from a boat at dawn, in a series of misty, mirror-like views; this picture probably belongs with them. The nearly symmetrical design, with sky and reflection balanced on either side of the horizon, anticipates the water-lily pictures of his last decades, in which the world is seen as a surface of reflected light.","c":"The picture is almost perfectly balanced, with the horizon dividing it in half and each side mirroring the other. Such symmetry could be dull, but the touches of purple and rust in the trees and the curving, feathered strokes in sky and water give life to every part. It is soft and calm, a gently abstracted scene that points forward to the water lilies. A subtle, assured work of the late 1890s."},"nga:74796":{"d":"A pale turquoise footbridge arches over a pond lined with tall grasses and filled with petal-pink and butter-yellow waterlilies, in a horizontal canvas about 81 by 102 centimeters, signed and dated 99. The shallow arch nearly touches the top edge and runs off both sides, with eggplant-purple shadows. Bands of waterlilies zigzag into the distance on the water, and spring-green and emerald grasses blend into a screen of trees that encloses the scene. The greens of the grasses and trees, and the underside of the bridge, are reflected in the water.","i":"The bridge crosses the water garden that Monet created at Giverny, with its Japanese-style footbridge, a design that reflects his love of Japanese art. He painted the bridge in a group of canvases in 1899, and the pond became the main subject of his last decades. Because the sky is hardly visible, the picture feels enclosed, a world of water, leaf and reflection that is a garden and a painting at once.","c":"The bridge is the picture's structure: a solid arch of turquoise that holds a swarm of strokes in place. The water, waterlilies and reflected green are layered with great freedom, and the absence of sky gives the scene a dense, jewel-like richness. It is one of the most formal of his water-garden compositions, and it holds together as design and as light. A key picture on the road to the water-lily murals."},"nga:12134":{"d":"In a wide, arched panel, about 91 by 223 centimeters, a bearded man wearing a crown places a second crown on the head of a woman. She bows her head with her eyes nearly closed and crosses her wrists over her chest, a white cloak lined in pine green falling around her shell-pink dress. He has shoulder-length curly gray hair and wears a dark rose robe and an azure cloak. Two white-robed angels hold up a raspberry-red swag with gold tassels, and winged cherubs' heads ringed with gold rays form an arch behind the pair, who sit on clouds against a pale blue sky.","i":"The Coronation of the Virgin, in which Mary is crowned queen of heaven, is the last of the glorious scenes of her life, following the Assumption, and it was a favorite subject of Florentine churches and confraternities. The bowed head and crossed arms are signs of her humility even as she is exalted. The wide, arched shape suggests that the panel was made for a particular architectural position, perhaps the crown or base of a larger altarpiece. Filippino was about eighteen.","c":"A young painter's picture, still close to Botticelli, who trained him: the drapery falls in deep, rhythmic folds, and the figures are arranged symmetrically, with angels and cherubs forming a graceful arch. What it lacks is the invention and energy Filippino would show in his mature years. The restful stillness and the beauty of the azure, rose and white are the charm. A graceful, accomplished early work in a conventional design."},"nga:20":{"d":"A small devotional panel, about 82 by 56 centimeters, painted in oil on poplar around 1475 to 1480. The title identifies the subject as the Adoration of the Child, the moment when the newborn Christ is worshiped, usually by Mary kneeling before him. The museum's record lists no figures, setting or colors, so the arrangement of the group, the landscape and the costumes are not described here. It is an early work, made when Filippino was in his late teens or early twenties and still strongly influenced by Botticelli.","i":"In the Adoration of the Child, Mary kneels or sits before the infant lying on the ground, a scene from the Nativity that stresses devotion to the child rather than the story of the birth. Such images were made for private chapels and bedrooms, where they invited prayer. The medium, oil rather than the older tempera, was a new technique for many Florentine painters in this decade, and it reflects interest in Netherlandish pictures then arriving in the city.","c":"A picture of this type depends on tenderness and design, and Filippino's early manner, with its fine line, graceful movement and soft expressions inherited from Botticelli and from his father, Fra Filippo Lippi, suited it well. From the record alone it is hard to judge the panel in detail, but it belongs to the group of small, intimate pictures where the young painter first showed his charm. The power of his later altarpieces is not yet in sight."},"nga:370":{"d":"A small panel, about 33 by 24 centimeters, painted in oil and tempera on poplar around 1475 to 1480, showing the story of Tobias and the angel. The museum's record lists no figures or setting, so the poses and landscape are not described here. In the usual telling, the young Tobias walks beside a stranger who is really the archangel Raphael, often with a small dog and a fish. It is a cabinet panel, about the size of a book, made for private devotion in a Florentine house.","i":"The Book of Tobit tells how Tobias, sent on a journey to collect a debt, is guided by the archangel Raphael, who protects him, shows him how to use the fish's gall to cure his father's blindness and helps him find a wife. In fifteenth-century Florence the story was popular with families whose sons traveled as merchants, and the pairing of boy and angel was a prayer for protection on the road.","c":"Small panels of this subject were a specialty of Florentine workshops of the 1470s, and Filippino, still young, brings to it the lyrical grace he learned from Botticelli. The charm of such pictures lies in the tender relation of boy and angel and in bright incidental details such as the dog and the fish. Details of this panel cannot be assessed from the record, but the type is attractive, and the artist's early promise is clear."},"met:436892":{"d":"The Virgin sits in a stone loggia in a red dress with a white sash knotted at the waist and a blue mantle edged in gold, a blue veil over her golden hair. Her head tilts down toward the Christ Child, who sits naked on her lap and leans over an open book on a red velvet cushion at the left, his hand on its pages. Beside the cushion lies a split pomegranate. At the right a brass candlestick and a closed book stand on a ledge. Through a round-arched opening with carved columns at the upper left is a view of a walled town with a castle and tiny figures. Tempera, oil and gold on wood, about 81 by 60 centimetres.","i":"Filippino Lippi, son of Fra Filippo Lippi, trained with Botticelli, and this panel, painted about 1483 to 1484, shows the elegance of that school: flowing line, clear colour and a graceful, slightly melancholy Virgin. The Child's attention to a book can be read as a sign of Christ as the Word and the fulfilment of scripture. The pomegranate, with its many seeds, often symbolised the Church and the resurrection, and its split skin may also foreshadow the Passion. The architecture, with its Corinthian columns, and the distant town show the new Florentine taste for classical forms and for views of landscape through a window.","c":"Filippino inherits Botticelli's sinuous line but gives it a softer, more naturalistic touch. The Virgin's downcast face is beautifully drawn, the Child is a convincingly plump baby, and the landscape through the arch is delightful. The composition is less assured: the figures crowd the left half, the space is awkward, and the cushion and book at lower left are crammed in. Compared with the more grounded pictures of his father, this is more decorative and pretty, with a trace of the nervous eccentricity that would mark his later work."},"nga:22":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed young man, painted around 1485 in oil and tempera on a panel about 52 by 37 centimeters. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or setting, so none is offered here. It is a small, bust-sized portrait of the type made for private patrons in Florence in the 1480s, a time when painted portraits, rare in the early fifteenth century, had become a fashion among the city's families.","i":"Painted portraits of young men in Florence served to record a sitter at a particular age, often on the occasion of a marriage, a coming of age or a departure, and they might be kept at home or exchanged between families. In the 1480s Florentine painters were also learning from portraits from the Netherlands, which paid close attention to the face and its surroundings. The sitter's name is not known.","c":"Filippino was primarily a painter of altarpieces and frescoes, and an independent portrait by him is a rarity. In the mid-1480s he was at his most graceful, with the refined line he had learned from Botticelli, and one would expect to find it in a work of this date. Without a description of the picture, the safest praise is for its place in his career: a personal image from a painter of grand religious commissions."},"nga:41701":{"d":"A very small horizontal panel, about 18 by 33 centimeters, painted in oil, possibly with some tempera, around 1500. The title names its subject: the dead Christ mourned by Nicodemus and two angels. The museum's record gives no further description, so the poses, setting and colors are not detailed here. The long, low shape and small size are typical of a predella, the strip of scenes below the main panel of an altarpiece, and it was probably made for such a place.","i":"Nicodemus, the Pharisee who came to Jesus by night and later helped to bury him, appears with the dead Christ in many late Gothic and Renaissance mourning scenes, where he stands for the learned believer who comes late to faith. The two angels join the grief in a timeless, heavenly space, lifting the scene from the historical Deposition into a devotional image, to be contemplated rather than followed as a story.","c":"Around 1500 Filippino's late style grew more agitated, with sharper outlines, strained gestures and emotional intensity, and a Pietà on this small scale would suit it. Without a description it is hard to say how fully the panel shows it. As a predella piece it is not meant to dominate, and it asks to be read at close range. It probably shows the painter's late mastery of pathos in miniature."},"nga:43682":{"d":"An orchestra of animals dressed as people plays in a garden in this wide canvas, about 89 by 151 centimeters, painted around 1739. At the center a brown monkey in a pink tunic, ruffled collar and plumed hat seems to conduct, holding a rolled paper and pointing at a page of music. Around him cats, dogs, goats, foxes and sheep sing from sheet music or play violins, cellos, lutes, flutes, bassoons and trumpets; a monkey at the far left plays a keyboard, and a small dog plays a zither-like instrument. Lush trees, a large gray urn and low stone walls frame the scene.","i":"A concert of animals is a favorite singerie conceit, mocking the amateur music-making that was a mark of polite society in eighteenth-century France. The conductor in his plumed hat and the earnest singers satirize the seriousness of performers, and the mixed company of cats, dogs and sheep adds comic discord. Huet painted a series of such monkey scenes as decoration, in which humor and elegance were meant to entertain guests rather than to preach.","c":"The joke is well sustained: each animal has a character, and the conductor with his rolled paper gives the orchestra a comic center. The setting, a green garden with urn and stone walls, keeps the whole within the polite bounds of Rococo decoration, so the satire never turns sharp. It is a lively, handsome piece painted with an easy hand, and a good example of a genre long dismissed as frivolous that records its time's amusements precisely."},"nga:43683":{"d":"Monkeys in colorful clothes carouse in an outdoor space framed by columns, in a wide canvas about 92 by 144 centimeters, painted around 1739. At the left a group around a table raises glasses in a toast, with a dog at their feet and a cat on the wall behind. At the center two monkeys dance, one in a white gown with a blue bodice and bonnet, the other in a yellow vest and green-blue breeches, while two more on a barrel play horn and violin and a bright parrot looks on. At lower right one monkey seems to be sick on the ground, a companion's hands on his shoulders.","i":"The party gets out of hand, and that is the satire: a banquet and ball in the country, with toasts, music and flirtation, ends with a drunken guest. The dancers, one in a lady's gown and one in a gentleman's breeches, parody the manners of polite society, and the monkey who leans away from an amorous neighbor is a gentle jibe at courtship. The parrot, another mimic of human speech, adds a further joke.","c":"Among Huet's monkey pictures this is one of the most narrative: the toast, the dance, the retreating partner and the unlucky drinker make a small comedy that can be read from left to right. The columns frame the scene like a stage, and the landscape beyond gives a pleasing release. If some of the humor is broad, the execution is fluent, with costume and expression carefully observed. A witty, decorative picture that rewards a long look."},"nga:43684":{"d":"A band of monkeys in clothes goes fishing on a riverbank in this wide canvas, about 92 by 144 centimeters, painted around 1739. In the foreground two monkeys in a boat handle a net while others help; on the right more monkeys hold poles and nets. The horizon is high, showing a strip of sky, distant hills and a bridge, and the trees and water are painted in fine detail. The colors are earthy browns and greens, with red, blue and orange accents on the monkeys' costumes.","i":"A singerie, from the French word for monkey, is a scene of monkeys acting like people, a fashion of the French Rococo that poked good-humored fun at human pursuits. Dressed as courtiers or fishermen, the animals gently satirized leisure, fashion and pretension. Such pictures, often painted as overdoors or wall panels for salons and cabinets, were a specialty of Christophe Huet, who made them for aristocratic houses.","c":"Huet's monkeys are rendered with wit and a fine decorative sense: the scene is orderly, the landscape neatly painted and the costumes make bright accents. The satire is gentle, and the picture is more charming than biting. As a piece of Rococo decoration it is well made and amusing, though a viewer who wants substance may find the joke thin. Within its genre, a good example."},"nga:43685":{"d":"A singerie titled The Picnic, painted around 1739 in oil on canvas and measuring about 90 by 150 centimeters. The museum's record lists only the theme of fantasy, so the figures, food and setting are not described here. By its title and genre, it shows monkeys behaving like people at an outdoor meal, in the playful manner of Huet's series of monkey scenes. The wide horizontal format and the date match several other singeries by Huet in the same collection, which suggests that they were made together as a set of decorative pictures.","i":"Outdoor meals and country parties were a favorite of French Rococo painting, and Watteau and his followers made the elegant open-air gathering a subject in its own right. In a singerie the monkeys take the place of the courtly guests, so the picture mocks the ritual of leisure while enjoying it. For the owner, the humor was part of the decoration: a way to delight guests with a witty reflection of their own manners.","c":"Without a description of its details the picture cannot be judged closely, but its pedigree is clear: Huet's monkeys are known for good humor, a fluent decorative touch and neat landscape settings. A picnic is a natural subject for the series, with its variety of poses and its opportunities for comic detail. As part of a set of singeries it works as pleasant, witty decoration, and it should be seen alongside its companions."},"nga:43686":{"d":"A singerie titled The Painter, painted around 1739 in oil on canvas, about 82 by 119 centimeters. The museum's record lists only the theme of fantasy, so its figures, equipment and setting are not described here. From the title and the genre, it shows monkeys playing the part of a painter and probably a sitter or audience, a comic version of the artist's studio. The size and date match those of Singerie: The Sculptor, so the two seem to be companions in a set of monkey pictures about the arts.","i":"The monkey painter plays on the old idea that art is the ape of nature, since painters were said merely to imitate what they saw, and on the self-important figure of the fashionable artist. A pair of pictures about the sister arts of painting and sculpture would have amused visitors who knew the debates of the Paris Academy. Huet, who belonged to the profession he mocked, could poke fun at it from within.","c":"The idea of the monkey as painter is the draw, and the series gives Huet a chance to display his lightness of touch. Without details the picture can be judged only as one of a set, and the set is better than any single piece: the arts in monkey form make a coherent, witty scheme. It is pleasing and decorative, and it has historical interest as part of the vogue for singeries in France."},"nga:43687":{"d":"Monkeys work as sculptors in an outdoor yard in this canvas, about 83 by 120 centimeters, painted around 1739. One monkey chisels a bust on a plinth while others stand nearby, apparently discussing or planning the work. Shrubbery, fencing and a cloudy sky form the setting, and various tools and vessels are scattered about to give the busy look of a workspace. The scene is playful and satirical, with animals taking on a craft that was among the most prestigious of the arts.","i":"Singeries of the arts, in which monkeys paint, sculpt or play music, carried a double joke: they mocked the pretensions of artists and recalled the old saying that art is the ape of nature, since painters and sculptors were thought merely to copy. The monkey at work on a bust is thus a comment on the artist as imitator. David Teniers the Younger had made monkey scenes popular in the previous century, and Huet brought the form to French decoration.","c":"The idea is strong, and Huet keeps the humor light: the busy yard, the scattered tools and the huddle of critics give the comedy its structure. The painting is more gentle than pointed, as befits decoration for a house, and the bright, loose handling suits the scene. The subject, an ape imitating the sculptor who imitates nature, is clever without demanding anything of the viewer. A neat, witty piece in a minor but distinctive genre."},"met:437871":{"d":"The risen Christ reveals himself to two disciples at the table in Emmaus, in an oil on canvas of 1622 to 1623, about 123 by 133 centimeters. The museum's record names the subject and Christ, so the number of figures and the details of the room are not described here. It is an early work, painted when Velázquez, born in Seville in 1599, was in his early twenties and not yet the court painter of Philip IV. In these years he painted religious scenes with a weighty realism, in the manner of the Sevillian school and the followers of Caravaggio.","i":"The story is from Luke's Gospel: two disciples walking to Emmaus on Easter evening meet a stranger, and when he blesses and breaks the bread at supper they recognize the risen Christ. Spanish painters of the early 1620s, like Caravaggio before them, set such scenes in ordinary rooms among ordinary people, so that the sacred appears in daily life. The subject was also a favorite of Counter-Reformation preachers, who stressed the presence of Christ in the Eucharist.","c":"Even in his early twenties Velázquez had the gift of making painted people seem to occupy real space and breathe real air. Without a description of the details one cannot say how fully this picture shows it, but works of this date from Seville are marked by sober realism, strong light and solid, believable figures that set him apart from his contemporaries. It is a fine early picture by an artist who would soon change Spanish painting."},"met:437873":{"d":"A portrait of Philip IV of Spain, painted probably in 1624 in a tall vertical canvas about 200 by 103 centimeters, nearly two meters high, the size used for standing full-length portraits. The museum's record names the sitter as a king and lists no costume or setting, so these are not described here. Philip, born in 1605, was about nineteen and had been king since 1621, and Velázquez had been appointed his court painter in 1623. The scale and height are those of a state portrait meant to stand in for the king.","i":"Royal portraits stood in for the king in his absence, and Velázquez's first likeness of the young Philip made him the monarch's favorite painter. Spanish court portraiture favored restraint: the king's dignity was meant to rest on calm and authority rather than on display. A picture of this size would hang in a palace or be sent to a foreign court, and the likeness had to be both recognizable and imposing.","c":"Velázquez's early portraits of Philip established his manner: a calm, direct presence and a refusal of the grandiloquent gestures of Baroque state portraiture. A picture of this date still belongs to the cooler, more linear style of his first court years, before the loose, airy handling of his mature portraits. It is valuable as an early record of the king's appearance and of the painter's rise, and the scale gives it real authority."},"met:437874":{"d":"A man in early middle age looks out from the shoulders of a dark doublet, his head turned slightly to one side and his eyes direct and watchful. His dark hair is long and loosely curled, falling to the nape of the neck; he has a thin, upturned moustache and a small pointed beard. A flat grey-white collar lies against the dark neck of his coat, and the plain clothes fade into a brown-grey ground. The face is painted with a few quick strokes of pale pink and shadow, and the lower half of the canvas is a simple mass of dark brown and black.","i":"The sitter is unknown. The Met's title leaves open the possibility that Velázquez painted himself; the question remains unresolved. The attribution to Velázquez is accepted, and the Met dates the picture to about 1635, when he was court painter to Philip IV and busy with large commissions for the Buen Retiro palace. The loose handling and plain setting are typical of his informal portraits, and the reason it was painted is not recorded. Informal bust-length portraits like this are rare in his output, which is dominated by the royal family and the court.","c":"Whatever the sitter's name, this is a lesson in economy: the face is built from a handful of strokes and cool half-tones, the eyes are alert and appraising, and the dark clothes recede so that the head floats forward. There is no flattery and no bravura. If it is the artist, it is an unusually unvarnished likeness; the slightly sceptical set of the mouth has the unforced truth he brought to the court dwarfs and jesters. The body and background are perfunctory, but they serve the head. Later painters, Manet among them, revered this kind of restraint."},"met:437869":{"d":"A man looks out of this portrait of 1650, painted in oil on canvas and measuring about 81 by 70 centimeters. The museum's record lists only men and portraits, so his costume and setting are not described here. The title names the sitter as Juan de Pareja, born around 1608, who was Velázquez's assistant, and the picture is a bust-length study of the size used for a close likeness. It was painted in Rome, during Velázquez's second visit to Italy.","i":"Pareja was born into slavery and worked as Velázquez's assistant, reportedly preparing his colors, and he later became a painter himself after being freed. Velázquez painted him in Rome, and the portrait is usually seen as a trial before his likeness of Pope Innocent X; it is said to have caused a stir when shown in public. The picture gives a man of low legal status the dignity and individuality of a great sitter, which was unusual in Baroque portraiture.","c":"The portrait's reputation is deserved. It is widely counted among the finest portraits of the seventeenth century, and its power comes from qualities typical of Velázquez at his best: economy of brushwork, exact tuning of light and tone, and a refusal to idealize. That such attention is given to a man of Pareja's status says as much about the painter as about the sitter. It stands as a landmark of Velázquez's late style."},"nga:88":{"d":"A woman at needlework, in an oil on canvas of about 1640 to 1650, measuring about 74 by 60 centimeters. The museum's record lists no figures, objects or colors, so only the title and the type are known: a single figure absorbed in a domestic task, a subject rare in the work of a painter employed mainly by the king's court. The modest size and the subject suggest a private picture rather than a commission. Its date falls in Velázquez's mature years, when his handling had become direct and free.","i":"A woman sewing is a familiar subject in Spanish and Dutch art, a sign of diligence and domestic virtue, and Spanish painters also showed the young Virgin at her sewing. Whether the sitter here was a model, a member of the painter's circle or an idealized type is not recorded. The picture's quiet intimacy is rare in court painting, where the sitters were the king, his family and his jesters.","c":"Velázquez is best known for royal portraits and large court pictures, and a small, close study of a woman at work offers a rare view of his attention to ordinary life. His mature handling, with direct brushwork and soft, truthful light, would be expected in such a picture, though the record leaves the specifics open. It is attractive for its modesty, and it gives the sense of a quiet moment seen at first hand."},"met:437870":{"d":"A small portrait of the Infanta María Teresa of Spain, painted between 1651 and 1654 in oil on canvas and measuring only about 34 by 40 centimeters. The museum's record lists a girl and a portrait, so her costume, hair and background are not described here. She was born in 1638, the daughter of Philip IV, so she was between about thirteen and sixteen when the picture was made. Its modest size distinguishes it from the large state portraits of the royal children by the same painter.","i":"As the only child of Philip IV's first marriage to reach adulthood, María Teresa was the most important princess in Europe, and likenesses of her were sent to foreign courts where marriages were being considered. In 1660 she married Louis XIV of France, a union that sealed peace between Spain and France. A portrait made in her early teens shows a girl in the years when her future was a matter of diplomacy.","c":"Velázquez's portraits of the royal children are admired as among the most delicate things he painted, and even a small portrait like this belongs to that group. The restraint of the court style asks for a careful balance of formality and life. The small scale makes the picture almost a private image, a pleasing counterpart to the grand portraits of the same sitter, though its modest size also means it cannot be rated with his largest works."},"met:441971":{"d":"Saint Peter, who has just denied knowing Christ, weeps in repentance, in a large vertical canvas about 162 by 114 centimeters, painted around 1612 to 1613. The museum's record names only the saint and the Denial, so his pose, features and setting are not described here. The picture belongs to Ribera's earliest period, when the young Spanish painter, working in Italy, had adopted the strong contrasts of light and dark associated with Caravaggio. A canvas of this size suits a half-length or three-quarter figure.","i":"In the Gospels Peter swore three times that he did not know Jesus, then heard the cock crow as Christ had foretold, and wept bitterly. After the Council of Trent the weeping Peter became a model of penitence, the sinner who is forgiven, and pictures of him were common in Counter-Reformation art. He is traditionally an old man with a short white beard and the keys that mark his authority as the first pope, though how this picture shows him is not recorded.","c":"Ribera was barely twenty-one, and a picture like this shows the Caravaggesque habits the young painter brought to such subjects: strong light, a figure close to the viewer and a refusal to soften the sinner's grief. The Tears of Saint Peter was a subject he painted more than once. This early version shows Caravaggio's influence at its most direct, and it points toward the powerful, earthy realism that later made his name in Naples."},"nga:72037":{"d":"Four men fill this nearly square canvas, about 104 by 113 centimeters, painted in 1634, shown from the waist up. Light from the upper left falls across the upturned face of the bearded, bare-chested Saint Bartholomew, who has his back to the viewer and his arms spread wide, one wrist lashed to a pole and the other hand held palm up. His mouth is slightly open and his brows raised. Behind him a broad-shouldered man in a brown cloak holds a knife against a sharpener, and two more men stand in the shadows at the left, all against a deep brown ground.","i":"Saint Bartholomew, one of the twelve apostles, is said to have been flayed alive before being killed, and his usual attribute is the knife, here in the hands of his torturer. The sharpening of the blade is the horror of anticipation, the moment before the cruelty. Counter-Reformation art encouraged such images of martyrdom so that viewers could contemplate the steadfastness of the saints, and Ribera, working in Spanish Naples, became famous for them.","c":"Ribera's tenebrism is at its most telling here: the single beam of light, the strong anatomy of the saint's back and arm, and the shadowed faces of the executioners create drama through restraint. The silence of the moment, with the saint looking up rather than at his torturer, gives the picture a moral weight as well as a physical one. It is among Ribera's most powerful martyrdoms and a landmark of Spanish Baroque art."},"met:437455":{"d":"The Virgin sits at the right in a deep blue mantle over a red dress, one hand holding the naked Christ Child, who sits on her lap and stretches out his right hand. At the left Saint Catherine of Alexandria, in a yellow-ochre cloak and white blouse, kneels and kisses his hand, her face pressed to it. Behind her the elderly Saint Anne, in a white head-cloth, holds a basket of fruit and a pink flower, and at the upper right the bearded Joseph looks out at the viewer. A basket of linen stands at the lower right. The background is almost black, and strong light from the left models the figures in solid, sculptural forms. No halos are shown. Oil on canvas, about 210 by 154 centimetres.","i":"Ribera, born in Spain and working in Naples, painted this in 1648, near the end of his career. The Met observes that he followed Caravaggio's practice of working directly from posed models, pairing idealised faces with incidental details of clothing, and that he omitted halos to ground the Holy Family firmly on earth. Its powerful simplicity of colour and form, the Met adds, comes from his study of earlier Roman artists, notably Raphael and Guido Reni. Saint Catherine, who kisses the Child's hand, was said to have been mystically married to Christ, and her gesture echoes that tradition. Naples was then under Spanish rule, and Ribera was one of its leading painters.","c":"The strength is physical truth: the heavy blue drapery, the chubby child and the kneeling saint's flushed cheek all read as living things, and the strong shadows give the group a weight Raphael's tenderness lacked. The warm, brownish flesh and the clarity of the narrative make the picture easy to love. The weaknesses are the stagey, slightly theatrical gestures and a sweetness that softens the grit of Ribera's earlier, harsher martyrdoms. It is a late, mellow Ribera, more composed than gripping, but beautifully constructed."},"met:437496":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 198 by 224 centimeters, shows the sacrifice of Polyxena, the Trojan princess killed at the tomb of Achilles. The museum's record lists a man with a knife and a woman named Polyxena, the two principal figures of the moment, but gives no description of the setting, crowd, costumes or colors. No date is recorded here. It is a large history picture of the kind made for a palace or a collector's gallery, in the classicizing Baroque manner of Rome.","i":"The story comes from the aftermath of the Trojan War, told by Euripides and Ovid. The ghost of Achilles demands the sacrifice of the young Polyxena, daughter of King Priam, and Achilles' son Neoptolemus kills her at his tomb. She meets her death bravely, which is why painters, like Ovid, treat her as a heroic victim, a figure of courage and pathos. Romanelli, a pupil of Domenichino and Pietro da Cortona, was a refined painter of such classical tragedies.","c":"Romanelli's reputation is for graceful, courtly painting, and his handling of tragedy tends toward elegance rather than ferocity: the figures are shaped by the Roman classicism of Domenichino, softened by the warmth and fluency of Cortona. A picture on this scale shows the ambition of his history painting, and the story's pathos gives it emotional interest. Without a description of its composition, the best that can be said is that it promises a polished, decorative treatment of a harsh subject."},"met:16345":{"d":"A family scene in an interior, painted about 1851 to 1852 in oil on canvas, about 72 by 58 centimeters. The museum's record lists an infant, a woman, a man, a footstool, a lamp, a doll, a bonnet and a decanter, so the picture is a domestic scene of parents with a baby among furnishings that include a lamp and a footstool, with a doll, a bonnet and a decanter close at hand. How these figures and objects are arranged is not described here.","i":"Lilly Martin Spencer made a career of lively domestic pictures of American family life, often using her husband and her own children as models, and her sense of humor about the household's demands was part of her appeal. The title Conversation Piece recalls an older tradition of informal group portraits of families in a room; here the family is anonymous, so the picture is a genre scene of parents' talk with a baby in the room.","c":"Spencer's domestic comedies are brisk, humane and sharply observed, and she had a gift for making everyday clutter charming. The lamp, doll and decanter show a painter interested in objects as well as expressions. What the record cannot tell is how fully this picture realizes that gift, but within the sentimental genre she worked in, Spencer is a livelier and less saccharine painter than many of her contemporaries."},"met:21126":{"d":"A bearded young man in a black hat and dark coat picks his way along a wet pavement, a big basket clutched in both arms. It overflows with eggs, cabbage, tomatoes and carrots, and a plucked chicken hangs by its feet from the handle. A closed umbrella is wedged under one arm. His eyes are fixed anxiously downward and one foot is raised, as if he fears slipping. Behind him a man in a top hat, holding an open umbrella, grins at his mishap. A few vegetables lie dropped on the stones at the lower left, and at the right are curved stone steps. Other pedestrians hurry in the rain. The oil on canvas is smooth and detailed.","i":"The Met says Spencer portrayed the unvarnished reality of relations between men and women, chronicling the trials of early married life: an unseen wife has sent her husband to buy the groceries, a task for which he is evidently not qualified. Spencer treated in humorous terms a real worry of the time, how to set up a household and run it well. She used her own husband as the model. Born in England and raised in Ohio, Spencer supported a large family by painting, and her domestic scenes were popular in her day. The picture is dated 1854.","c":"The joke lands because the details are right: the tilting basket, the dangling hen, the vegetables already lost to the pavement, the passer-by's amusement. Spencer puts the joke on the husband, not the wife, and gives him all the anxiety of a household she knew well. The painting is closer to illustration than to high art: the faces are broad types, the wet street is stagey, and the colour is dutiful rather than exciting. But as social observation with a sharp, good-humoured edge, it is as modern as it is funny."},"nga:137693":{"d":"In this oval painting, a cluster of ripe red raspberries sits at the center, surrounded by green leaves that extend toward the edges. Some berries hang from thin green stems over the leaves, toward a light brown ground. The background includes some shadowed green plants and a brown sky. The picture is small, about 36 by 46 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas around 1859, and the oval shape was a popular format for fruit pieces, suited to hanging in a parlor or dining room.","i":"Fruit still lifes, with raspberries, strawberries and peaches, were popular in mid-nineteenth-century America, where they decorated dining rooms and stood for the abundance of the new country's gardens. Spencer, better known for family scenes, painted a number of them, probably in part because a still life was a salable picture at a time when she had a large family to support. The berries still hang on their stems, as if just picked from the bush.","c":"A modest picture with a clear merit: the plain arrangement and the soft light give the berries a fresh, tactile presence, and the leaves frame them without competing. The shadowed foliage and brown sky behind are slightly generalized, which keeps attention on the fruit. Compared with the lavish still lifes of the Peale family, it is quieter and more intimate. It is a small, direct piece of observation."},"nga:156252":{"d":"Two large wooden warships fire on each other across a choppy sea in this long horizontal panel, about 48 by 142 centimeters, painted around 1618 to 1620. Rows of black cannon poke through their portholes, and three tall masts carry billowing sails and bright flags. Tiny men in gold, red and blue line the decks. In front of the viewer a smaller ship, nearly submerged, is being fired on by armored men in the nearer warship's prow, and people leap into the water; one man kneels with his hands together on the sinking deck. Two more ships fight in the distance.","i":"The title names Dutch and Spanish ships, and the picture belongs to the culture of the Eighty Years' War, in which the Dutch Republic fought for independence from Spain and naval actions were celebrated in pictures, prints and medals. Verbeeck, an early specialist in such sea battles, presents war at sea as an orderly, almost heraldic spectacle, with flags identifying each side. His signature is painted on a flag, a small sign of his pride in the craft.","c":"The long, narrow format suits the subject, spreading the action across the width of the sea, and the tiny figures, each given a few brisk touches, bring life to the decks. The color, with its blues, tans and bright flags, is clear and cheerful, and the sinking ship in the foreground gives the scene a human focus. It is more a catalog of incidents than a unified design, but the detail is delightful."},"nga:43723":{"d":"In a columned hall with arches, niche statues and a checkered marble floor, about two dozen figures twist and recoil around Christ. He stands at the center in a pink robe and blue cloak, a four-tailed whip raised in his right hand, looking down. At left two bare-chested men lean away with raised arms, and two half-clothed women fall back on the steps beside a cage of white doves. At right an old moneychanger rests his chin on his hand beside a basket of gold coins, with rabbits nosing spilled silver and a lamb lying with its feet tied. A mother leads a naked child away. Through the central arch are blue sky and distant buildings. A Greek signature is at lower left.","i":"The subject is the Gospel episode in which Christ drives merchants and moneychangers out of the Temple in Jerusalem. After the Council of Trent the theme was popular because it could stand for the Catholic Church purifying itself. El Greco, trained as an icon painter in Crete, returned to it for decades; this is an early version, probably made before 1570 in his first Italian years, and later ones are in Minneapolis, New York, London and Madrid. He signed it in Greek letters, adding that he was a Cretan after his name, a reminder of where he had come from.","c":"This is not yet the elongated, flame-like El Greco. It is a gifted young painter's tour of what Venice and Rome had to teach, with Tintoretto's dramatic gestures, Michelangelo's powerful nudes and Venetian architecture and color. The crowding is a weakness. Christ is surrounded by figures that are all equally energetic, so the focus slips, and the space is stagey, with groups at the edges cut by the frame. What impresses is the speed of the brushwork, the cold acid colors and the relish of the still-life details, the rabbits, coins and birds."},"met:436572":{"d":"Christ is shown healing a man blind from birth, the miracle told in the Gospel of John, on a horizontal canvas of about 119 by 146 centimeters painted in Italy around 1570. Men and women gather around the principal pair, so the miracle is played out before a crowd rather than in private. The figures are set in a deep space, in the manner of Venetian narrative painting. The picture belongs to the early period of El Greco, who had recently left his native Crete.","i":"In the ninth chapter of John, Jesus meets a man blind from birth, anoints his eyes with clay made with spittle and tells him to wash in the pool of Siloam, and the man returns seeing. The story follows Christ's declaration that he is the light of the world, and for centuries it was read as a picture of baptism and of the soul's enlightenment. El Greco returned to the subject in several versions.","c":"A young painter shows off what he has learned in Italy: figures foreshortened and grouped in depth, strong color, and the Venetian habit of crowding a stage. It is more worldly and less spiritual than the El Greco of Toledo, and the crowd is somewhat busy for its subject. Yet the stretched gestures and hot color already hint at the artist he would become."},"cma:108541":{"d":"Mary, Joseph and the Christ Child are gathered closely with Mary Magdalen in this oil on canvas of about 130 by 100 centimeters. The figures seem almost weightless, their colors startling in combination, and the setting is moody and imprecise. Joseph holds out fruit, which Mary feeds to her son. The Child's body is nearly levitating. Mary supports him, but her faraway look suggests that she already senses his fate.","i":"El Greco is concerned here with spiritual intensity more than with literal reality. Mary is presented as a mediator between Christ and the viewer. The fruit carries layers of meaning: the apple stands for the fall of man, cherries for the blood of Christ, peaches for salvation and pears for the sweetness of Christ's virtue. Mary Magdalen, the penitent follower of Christ, adds a reminder of repentance and forgiveness.","c":"Few painters could make a domestic group so otherworldly. The sharp color contrasts and the drifting, smoky setting lift the family out of ordinary space into something like a devotional vision, and the exchange of fruit supplies a human gesture at its center. The price is a loss of warmth: the faces are closer to icons than portraits, and the tight grouping allows the viewer little room."},"nga:1164":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas of about 193 by 103 centimeters shows the best-known act of Saint Martin of Tours: the Roman cavalry officer, mounted on his horse, cuts his cloak in two with his sword to clothe a freezing beggar. According to the legend, the meeting took place at the gate of Amiens on a bitter winter day. El Greco painted the picture between 1597 and 1599 for a chapel in Toledo, in the stretched, flame-like manner of his Spanish years.","i":"Martin served in the Roman army before becoming a monk and bishop. His biographer Sulpicius Severus tells that Christ appeared to him in a dream wearing the half-cloak, and told the angels that Martin, still unbaptized, had clothed him. The scene became the standard image of Christian charity. El Greco made this canvas as a companion to a Madonna with Saints Martina and Agnes for the Chapel of San José in Toledo.","c":"El Greco turns an old charity story into a spiritual drama. The stretched forms and unreal elegance of the gestures lift the act out of the street and into ritual. The appeal is in the calligraphic drawing and the sense of a holy event; the cost is any real feeling of weight, cold or hunger. Compared with his fervent late works, this Toledo commission feels poised, polished and slightly cool."},"nga:1165":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child float on a cloud at the top of this tall canvas, flanked by two winged angels and ringed by pale winged baby heads. Mary, in a sapphire-blue cloak over a rose-pink dress, looks down at her son, who grasps her finger. Below, two women stand facing each other. One, in yellow over blue, rests a hand on a lion and holds a palm frond. The other, veiled and wrapped in a flame-red cloak, carries a white lamb. All the figures are elongated, and their pale skin is shaded with smoke gray.","i":"Both women are young Roman martyrs, and their attributes identify them. The lion and palm belong to Martina, who according to legend was exposed to wild beasts before she was killed, and the lamb to Agnes, whose name sounds like the Latin word for lamb. Two curling marks on the lion's forehead are the Greek letters delta and theta, the artist's initials. The picture was made as a side altarpiece for the Chapel of San José in Toledo.","c":"The figures float, the colors chime (sapphire against pink, canary yellow against topaz, flame red against white), and the painter's signature on the lion is a witty touch. This is El Greco at his most decorative, with elongated necks and fingers, smoky skin, and a tall composition that stacks heaven over earth. The faces are generic and somewhat sweet; the interest lies in the color and the vertical lift."},"met:436574":{"d":"A bald old man with a thin grey beard looks out from a bare, dark ground. His head is turned a little to one side, the eyes sunken and watchful under arched brows, the forehead lined, the nose long, the mouth set. He wears a white ruff pleated in thick, rapid strokes, a dark garment and a broad fur collar in browns. There is no hand, no object and no setting, only the head and shoulders, with the fur dissolving into shadow at the lower edge. The face is painted thinly, so that the dark grey ground shows through in the shadows, while the ruff is built up in bright white, and the whole picture is held in a narrow range of browns, greys and black.","i":"The Metropolitan Museum says this has often been identified as a self-portrait, a strong possibility but impossible to confirm. It is dated about 1595–1600, when El Greco, who was born on Crete and had lived in Toledo since the 1570s, was in his fifties; scholars have compared the face with figures believed to be his self-portraits inside his religious paintings. The picture shows a side of El Greco that viewers who know him for stretched, flame-like saints may not expect: sober, close observation of a face. His portraits of Toledo's clergy and gentlemen, which the Museum calls exceptional for their naturalism and psychological insight, are among his most admired works.","c":"Quiet and unflattering, this is a portrait of mind rather than rank. The shadowed eyes and the white ruff against the dark ground do nearly all the work, and nothing of the elongated, agitated manner of his altarpieces intrudes. It is less formal than the Cardinal Niño de Guevara in the same museum and more convincing as a portrait of one particular tired man. The handling is thin, and the edges are left loose. Whether it shows El Greco himself matters less than it seems: the power lies in the unembellished look."},"met:436575":{"d":"A hill town glows under a storm. On the right, pale buildings climb a green hill toward a slender cathedral spire and the blocky mass of the Alcázar, silvery against thick, dark cloud. To the left, a castle stands on a spur and a stone bridge crosses the river, which runs in dark ribbons through the foreground. Hillsides of intense green, laid on in thick, feathery strokes, roll down to the water, with a few trees and clumps of grass for scale. The sky takes up more than half the canvas, with torn white clouds and patches of acid blue. There is no sun, only a cold, unearthly light that sharpens everything it touches.","i":"El Greco, born on Crete, settled in Toledo in 1577 and lived there until his death in 1614. This is one of only two surviving landscapes by him; the other, the View and Plan of Toledo, is in the city's El Greco Museum. It is not a record of the place: the cathedral has been moved and the geography compressed, which suggests that he wanted to convey the city's spirit rather than its map. No patron or commission is known, and the Met admits how little is understood about the painting's making. An inventory of his studio in 1614 lists Toledo landscapes, so it may have stayed with him. The storm is often read as a sign of the city's spiritual charge, but no source says so.","c":"Few landscapes before the nineteenth century look so modern, and that is both its achievement and its risk. The flat, glowing planes of the city and the torn, nervous sky anticipate Expressionism, and the picture succeeds because it commits to a mood without apology. It is also a rough object: the foreground is a hasty tangle, the composition tilts, and the drama depends almost entirely on the sky. As an invention it is superb; as a picture of a place it is unreliable, which is exactly the point."},"nga:45889":{"d":"On a small vertical canvas of about 53 by 34 centimeters, Mary sits at the center with the nude infant Christ across her lap, flanked by an older woman at the left and an elderly man with a young boy at the right. Mary wears a cobalt-blue mantle over a dusky rose gown. The older woman, in tangerine orange, leans in, and the boy holds a basket or bowl with a finger near his lips. The group is crowded against a blue sky swirling with thin clouds, painted in long, saturated strokes.","i":"The group is the Holy Family with Mary's mother, Saint Anne, and the infant John the Baptist, who as a child is often shown with the Christ Child because the two were cousins. Joseph is the elderly man behind them. John's finger near his lips may signal silence or reverence, though the meaning is not fixed. The small size suggests a picture for private devotion, or possibly a study for a larger version.","c":"El Greco concentrates his late manner in a small space: cobalt, tangerine and rose crowd a tight vertical frame while long loaded strokes whip the sky. The faces are formulaic and the proportions stretched, yet the color glows against the stormy blues and grays, and the sketchy ground suggests the speed of the hand. It reads as a vivid, nearly improvised devotional image, more a painter's thought than a finished set piece."},"met:436573":{"d":"A cardinal sits upright in a high-backed chair, painted in a cape of glossy magenta silk over a white lace-edged rochet. Round wire spectacles frame his dark, watchful eyes; a grey goatee and moustache surround a tight mouth, and a small red cap sits on his head. His hands grip the arms of the chair, and a ring shows on the one at the right. The silk is painted in sharp, angular folds with streaks of pink and white that make the cape look almost metallic. Behind him are a gold brocade hanging, a panelled wooden door and a tiled floor in grey and cream, where a folded paper lies at his feet.","i":"Fernando Niño de Guevara (1541–1609), born in Toledo, became a cardinal in 1596 and Inquisitor General of Spain in 1599. El Greco painted him about 1600, probably during a visit to Toledo. It has been suggested that the cardinal's nephew, a patron of El Greco, ordered the portrait, but the commission is not certain. In 1601 the sitter became Archbishop of Seville. The picture was in the collection of H. O. Havemeyer and his wife Louisine, who were among the first Americans to collect El Greco, and she bequeathed it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1929.","c":"Arguably the most commanding of El Greco's portraits, it works through tension, not rank. The sharp folds of the cape flicker like flame, the spectacles turn a stern face into a thinking one, and the gripping hands make the figure look about to rise. The setting is perfunctory beside the sitter, and the hands are a little stiff. Whether knowing the sitter headed the Inquisition colours the reading is hard to say, since the face is alert rather than cruel. Few portraits of the period suggest so much nervous energy under ceremonial dress."},"cma:129386":{"d":"Christ is shown crucified on a tall canvas of about 193 by 116 centimeters, painted in Toledo in the early 1600s. The body is stretched into the elongated proportions that El Greco learned partly from Tintoretto, and the blood is painted with graphic directness. The picture belongs to a long series of Crucifixions by the artist, made as altarpieces and devotional images for Spanish patrons.","i":"El Greco was trained on Crete as a painter of small devotional icons, then moved to Venice, where he may have worked with Tintoretto, and on to Rome before settling in Toledo. His Spanish patrons were drawn to the physical suffering of Christ as a subject for meditation, and the vivid blood here probably answers that taste. In an age of Catholic reform, a Crucifixion was meant to bring the believer into the scene, to feel the sacrifice and to repent.","c":"The attraction is the tension between a Venetian inheritance, with its color and stretched forms, and a Spanish appetite for pain. Anatomy is subordinate to line, and the result is more an apparition than an eyewitness account. Some will find the blood heavy-handed, and the figure's elongation can seem mannered. It is a clear statement of El Greco's late method."},"met:436570":{"d":"Mary, Joseph and the newborn Christ Child are surrounded by shepherds in this tall canvas of about 164 by 107 centimeters, painted around 1605 to 1610. A lamb and cattle also appear, bringing the stable's animals into the crowded scene. The tall vertical format suits El Greco's late habit of packing figures close together, so that the adoring group presses around the Child.","i":"Luke's Gospel tells how shepherds, warned by an angel, found the newborn Christ in a manger. As the first humble witnesses, they stand for all who come to him in simplicity. The ox is traditional, drawn from a reading of Isaiah, and the lamb recalls the Lamb of God, pointing ahead to the Crucifixion. A Nativity such as this invited viewers to join the shepherds in adoration at Christmas.","c":"El Greco's late nativities turn the stable into a vision: bodies stretched and flame-like, space compressed, with color rather than light serving as the drama. This picture succeeds as an image of wonder, though faces and gestures can become formulaic, and the natural detail of cattle and lamb is subordinate to the swirling pattern. It shows an old painter's willingness to sacrifice plausibility for fervor."},"met:436571":{"d":"A narrow vertical canvas, about 110 by 65 centimeters, shows the Adoration of the Shepherds. The newborn Christ Child, Mary and a man with the shepherds form the group; a lamb is among them, and an angel is also present. The picture was painted around 1612 to 1614, near the end of El Greco's life, and it is credited to the artist and his workshop, meaning that assistants had a hand in the work.","i":"In El Greco's late nativities the Child is often imagined as the source of light, a visual form of the Gospel phrase about the light shining in the darkness. The shepherds, humble men called by an angel, stand for the poor and simple who recognize the Savior first, and the lamb alludes to Christ's later sacrifice. Workshop versions of popular compositions were common in Toledo, where assistants repeated what patrons wanted.","c":"A workshop repetition shows both the strength and the weakness of a successful design. The upward-flickering composition, tall and narrow, keeps the master's invention, but the execution, with several hands involved, is probably less electric than in his own autograph canvases. It is best seen as evidence of how El Greco's late style was packaged for patrons rather than as a peak of his art."},"met:436576":{"d":"A tall, crowded scene whose figures are stretched like flames. At the left, Saint John, in a blue-green silk robe, raises both arms and gazes upward, his head tilted back. To his right a cluster of pale naked figures, men and women, some kneeling and some standing, reach upward to receive white robes handed down by small cherubs at the upper right, while green, yellow, and rose-colored cloths billow around them. The sky is a dark brown storm streaked with white. There is no ground and no horizon. The bodies are elongated and their heads small, and the light is cold and flickering, as if from lightning.","i":"The subject is the opening of the Fifth Seal in the Book of Revelation (6:9–11): the souls of martyrs cry out for justice and are given white robes and told to wait. El Greco painted it near the end of his life, for a side altar in Toledo, and it was never finished. The upper part of the canvas, which probably showed a heavenly scene, was cut away in the nineteenth century, leaving John to gesture at nothing. The painter Ignacio Zuloaga owned it in Paris, where Picasso is thought to have studied it while working on Les Demoiselles d'Avignon in 1907. The Met bought it in 1956.","c":"The strangest thing about this picture is how free it is: the bodies are elongated, the colors acid, the space nonexistent, yet it holds together through rhythm, the rising gestures answered by the swirl of blue silk. It is also a damaged, partial thing, and the loss of the upper section unbalances it; it is best read as a fragment. Its reputation among twentieth-century artists, who treated El Greco as a prophet, owes something to that damage, which makes it look more abstract than he intended."},"nga:12204":{"d":"A nearly nude old man kneels at the mouth of a rocky cave, holding a book in one hand and a stone in the other. His torso and limbs are elongated and sinewy, his ribs and neck tendons stand out, and his skin is ivory and pale peach with smoky gray shadows. He has short white hair and a frost-white beard, with a loose light cloth across his hips, and he raises his face toward rays of yellow and white light in a turquoise sky at the upper left. The dark interior of the cave fills much of the tall canvas.","i":"The kneeling figure is Saint Jerome, the fourth-century scholar who translated the Bible into Latin and, in legend, spent years as a hermit in the desert doing penance. The book stands for his scholarship and the stone for the penitent's habit of beating his breast. In the Catholic Reformation, when penance was defended against Protestant criticism, images of Jerome gave viewers an example of repentance.","c":"One of the most austere pictures of the late El Greco: the starved body is almost a landscape of bone and tendon, painted in cold ivory against darkness. The upward glance and the light rays supply the only hope. It is a risky picture, bordering on mannered, but conviction saves it, and the loosely handled cave opening shows how freely the old painter worked."},"nga:33253":{"d":"The Trojan priest Laocoön and his two sons struggle in the coils of sea serpents in this wide canvas of about 138 by 173 centimeters. El Greco stretches and twists the pale, nude bodies against a stormy sky, and in the distance he places a view of Toledo, standing in for Troy, with the wooden horse of the legend visible. The picture, painted in the last years of the artist's life, is generally described as his only surviving mythological subject.","i":"In Virgil's Aeneid, Laocoön, a priest of Apollo, warned the Trojans against the wooden horse the Greeks had left outside the city. Two serpents rose from the sea and killed him and his sons, and the Trojans, taking this as a sign of the gods' anger, dragged the horse inside, which led to the city's fall. The famous ancient marble group of the scene, unearthed in Rome in 1506, shaped later images. Why El Greco chose the subject is not clearly recorded.","c":"Nothing else by El Greco looks like it. The ancient sculpture's muscular agony becomes ghostly, elongated flesh writhing under a livid sky, and the view of Toledo gives the myth an eerie, modern air. The cost is plausibility: the figures are so stretched that the physical struggle is hard to believe, and the painting feels less like a tragedy than a vision. Many viewers find that its strangeness is exactly its value."},"nga:90":{"d":"An elderly man in a voluminous ocean-blue hooded robe sits at a writing table, fingers spread over an open book and a pen in his other hand, and looks up and to the right in profile. The arched-top canvas is filled by his figure and the table, which is covered in a raspberry-pink cloth edged with gold. On the table are quills in a lidded inkwell, a silver blotter and a silver box. Behind him at the right, a small statue of a woman holding a child stands on a bracket.","i":"The saint is Ildefonso, a seventh-century archbishop of Toledo, who wrote a treatise defending the perpetual virginity of Mary. The statuette of the Virgin and Child behind him alludes to that devotion, and Toledo tradition adds that the Virgin once appeared to reward him. Because he was the city's own saint, Ildefonso was an important subject for El Greco's Toledo patrons, and the writer's pose ties holiness to learning.","c":"The face is a calm profile; the real drama is the robe, where light slides through topaz, aquamarine and midnight blue, and the visible brushstrokes make the blue and pink fabrics sing. The silver blotter and inkwell give the table a quiet still-life interest, and the loosely handled statuette recedes. It is a late El Greco in which paint, more than gesture, carries the feeling."},"nga:177072":{"d":"A group of men crowd together, leaning over a wooden railing with their hands resting on it, all staring intently at something just below the viewer. They wear top hats, bowlers and flat caps and a range of suits, coats and plain shirts, and their skin tones range from pale to dark brown; some have slight smiles, others wide eyes and raised brows. Behind them are a wooden beam ceiling and a peeling plaster wall, with a framed notice headed RULES partly visible and red brick showing where the plaster has fallen.","i":"A cockpit was the arena for cockfighting, a popular but disreputable sport of gambling and cruelty that many reformers condemned in the later nineteenth century. The title refers to the moment when the outcome of the fight is nearly decided, and the viewer is placed at the pit's edge, sharing the men's view. Bonham shows spectators of different classes and races united by the excitement, and the notice of rules on the wall adds an ironic note.","c":"A vivid, well-staged picture: the closely packed heads, the intent expressions and the viewpoint make the viewer a participant, and the faces are individualised. The peeling wall adds a sordid realism. The picture is neither sentimental nor moralising, which sets it apart from much American genre painting of its day. The brushwork is somewhat dry and the lighting flat, but the painter's observation of a crowd's shared excitement is striking."},"met:435599":{"d":"A very large canvas, about 150 by 211 centimetres, shows the boatmen of Barcelona, with boats and ships among its subjects. The museum's record lists men, boats and ships but nothing more, so the grouping, light and weather cannot be described with confidence. The scale is that of an ambitious exhibition picture, and Baixeras, a young Catalan painter in his early twenties, chose a working subject from his own city's waterfront in place of a historical or mythological scene.","i":"Pictures of working people at the water's edge were a staple of European realism in the 1880s, from fishermen on Dutch beaches to boatmen in Mediterranean harbours, offering a market-friendly mix of local colour and honest labour. Baixeras often painted the port and coast of Barcelona. The Metropolitan Museum received this canvas in 1886, the year it is dated, as a gift from George I. Seney, a New York banker and collector of contemporary art.","c":"Without close detail to judge, the picture is best placed in context: a large harbour scene by a young Spanish painter in the manner of the late-nineteenth-century realists, aiming at truthful light and the dignity of labour. Canvases of this kind impress by size and conviction but can be conventional in composition. Its interest is partly historical: an early work by a Catalan artist that reached an American museum in the year it is dated."},"met:436671":{"d":"Peacocks, a domestic turkey, a monkey and a squirrel share a large canvas with fruit, painted in 1683, about 190 by 135 centimeters. The museum's record lists these animals and the fruit and classes the picture with still lifes, but gives no description of their arrangement, the setting or the colors, so they are not detailed here. The size and the vertical format are typical of the big decorative pictures of birds and animals that Hondecoeter made for the walls of Dutch houses.","i":"Peacocks were prized exotic birds in the Dutch Republic, kept in the gardens of rich merchants, and they stand for pride and display; the monkey and the squirrel add exotic and domestic notes, and the fruit is a reminder of abundance. Hondecoeter, who specialized in birds, painted such menageries for patrons who wanted decoration that also showed off their taste for rare creatures. Some viewers may have read a moral about vanity, but the main purpose was delight.","c":"Hondecoeter was the best-known Dutch painter of birds, and a canvas like this shows what he did best: the glossy iridescence of peacock feathers, the variety of textures from fur to plumage, and an arrangement that is lively and decorative. As natural history it is admirable; as a design, the crowding can seem busy. The monkey and the squirrel add a note of humor, and the picture has the energy of a real menagerie."},"met:435585":{"d":"A married couple, a man and a woman, appear together in a horizontal double portrait of 1512, painted in oil on a linden panel about 33 by 63 centimeters. The title offers the names Lorenz Kraffter and Honesta Merz, but the question mark shows that the identification is not certain. The museum's record gives no description of the pair's dress, poses or setting, so none is offered here. A couple could be painted either as two separate pendant panels or, as here, together on one.","i":"Marriage portraits in Renaissance Germany recorded the union of two families and the couple's place in the city's merchant class. Ulrich Apt the Elder worked in Augsburg, the great trading and banking city of the Fuggers and Welsers, where portraits of well-off citizens were in demand in the early sixteenth century. Whether the sitters were really Lorenz Kraffter and Honesta Merz is uncertain, and the picture is more securely read as a document of the Augsburg burgher class.","c":"Apt belongs to the generation of Augsburg painters that mixed late Gothic habits with the first Renaissance ideas from Italy, and a double portrait of 1512 would be an early example of that mixture. German portraits of this time prize likeness over idealization, with plain backgrounds and honest observation. Without a description of the picture it is difficult to assess more closely, but it is a valuable record of a couple of the early sixteenth century."},"met:436849":{"d":"A village in the Fontainebleau region, La Celle-sous-Moret, is the subject of this oil on wood panel of about 35 by 58 centimeters. The painting is undated, though Eugène Lavieille, a French landscape painter associated with the Barbizon generation, died in 1889. The wide horizontal format suits a view across fields to a cluster of roofs, and the picture is a quiet record of an ordinary corner of the French countryside.","i":"The villages near Moret and the forest of Fontainebleau drew landscape painters from the 1830s onward, first the Barbizon painters and later the Impressionists, including Sisley, who settled nearby. Lavieille belonged to the generation that followed the Barbizon founders, painting what he saw in small panels. Such pictures answered a growing urban appetite for the unspoiled countryside as railways brought more visitors into the region.","c":"Lavieille is a quiet, reliable painter, and this panel shows his gift for the unforced, intimate view of a village in its landscape. It lacks the grandeur of the great Barbizon masters, and it does not push the observation of light as the Impressionists did. Its appeal is gentle truthfulness, which is perhaps the best a modest landscape can offer."},"nga:177037":{"d":"A yellow carriage has stopped at the edge of a river in the middle of a mishap. Its wheels are still partly in the water, and four horses, rearing and tossing their heads, climb the left bank while a man on shore raises his hands to calm them. Two women in feathered hats and two men in dark uniforms with gold details look on wide-eyed; a man in a blue coat stands in the water holding the reins, a top hat floats nearby, and men in a long boat lift a man out of the water.","i":"Alvan Fisher was among the first American painters to specialize in landscape and rural life, and this panel of 1818 is an early example of American comic genre painting. The cascade of small troubles, from the rearing horses to the floating hat and the dog gnawing a fishing line, gives the story its humor. Pictures like this appealed to a new public that wanted to see its own roads, rivers and manners in art.","c":"The picture is ambitious in its crowded narrative and in the way each little incident is placed with comic timing. The drawing of the horses is awkward and the figures are stiff, but the clarity of the story and the bright yellow carriage against blue water make it memorable. It anticipates the humorous genre scenes of William Sidney Mount a generation later."},"met:12785":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Mrs. William F. Milton, is the single subject of this oil portrait, painted in 1881 on a vertical canvas about 81 by 61 centimeters, a size that suits a likeness of head and upper body. In type it is a society portrait of the sort a New York family hung in its own house. Thayer had trained in Paris and come home to the United States in 1879, and portraits were one of the ways a painter of his generation earned a living.","i":"Naming a married woman by her husband's name, as the title does, was the convention of the day, and it hints at how such pictures were meant to read: as a record of a household's standing as much as of one face. Thayer's later fame rests on very different pictures, the idealized women and winged 'angels' he began painting in the late 1880s, in which a figure stands for a virtue rather than a person. Here he was still working within his Paris habits.","c":"An early portrait is easy to underrate and hard to rate highly. Paris training should give it secure drawing, but the demands of a sitter's likeness leave little room for the quiet, dreamlike feeling that later set Thayer apart. Beside the dashing portraits John Singer Sargent was already painting in Paris at this date, it is sober American fare. Its interest lies in showing where Thayer began, before he found his own subject."},"met:12787":{"d":"A young woman is the single figure of this oil painting, made about 1898 on a vertical canvas roughly 100 by 80 centimeters. The title gives no name, which fits Thayer's habit in the 1890s of painting girls and young women, often from his own family and circle, as types of grace and purity rather than as portraits of named individuals. It is probably an easel picture in the Idealist tradition rather than a commissioned likeness, made to be exhibited and admired for its figure.","i":"Thayer belonged to the American Idealists, painters who held that art should raise the viewer toward beauty and moral feeling. His winged 'Angel' of 1887 and his 'Virgin Enthroned' of 1891 used his own children as models, and a picture titled simply Young Woman keeps that habit of mind while dropping the wings and halo: the sitter stands for youth and serenity themselves. For patrons, such pictures offered a secular, almost devotional image of womanhood.","c":"Thayer's best figure pictures pair a solid, simple construction of the head with soft, even light, and they can reach a quiet dignity that avoids sweetness. By about 1898 the formula was familiar, and the risk in a picture like this is a pleasing generality, a type rather than a person. Beside Thomas Dewing's more austere women, Thayer's seem warmer, less mysterious and a little more earthbound."},"met:11913":{"d":"A great waterfall is the subject of this tall canvas of 1862, about 102 by 79 centimeters, titled Underneath Niagara Falls. The title implies an unusual vantage point beneath or behind the cascade, instead of the more familiar panoramas from above or across the river. Ferdinand Richardt, a painter born in Denmark, specialized in views of American scenery, and Niagara was one of his favorite subjects.","i":"Niagara Falls was the great natural wonder of the nineteenth-century Atlantic world, a site of pilgrimage for tourists, poets and painters, and a symbol of American grandeur. Visitors could descend beneath the falls on staircases and walkways, in oilskin clothing, to experience the roar and spray at close range. A view from below was an invitation to feel the sublime rather than simply look at it.","c":"The vertical format increases the sense of height, and a dramatic viewpoint gives the picture a drama that stock views of the falls lack. Richardt's careful, somewhat dry handling is better suited to topography than to the spray and thunder of the subject, and Frederic Church's celebrated Niagara of 1857 did more with the water. Still, as a record of the spectacle, it is vivid and well organized."},"nga:1183":{"d":"The Virgin sits turned to the viewer's left, looking down at the Christ Child in her lap. She has reddish-brown hair put up under a red and brown net, a thin veil, a gold halo, and a richly ornamented gold-and-brown dress with a collar of pearls and green gems. The nude Child lies on a green cushion wearing a red necklace and bracelets, looking up at her and holding a pale pink fruit. At her side a white bowl of similar fruit rests on a white marble ledge, and behind them stretch green fields, rocky hills and a blue lake under a graded sky.","i":"The picture combines tenderness with splendour: Mary's pearls, jewels and gold show her as Queen of Heaven, while her downward look and the Child's upward one make a simple human exchange. The red beads on the Child's neck and wrists are probably coral, which was thought to protect children and, being red, could recall the Passion. The fruit in his hand may allude to the Fall and to Christ as the new Adam. Francesco Benaglio, from Verona, was influenced by Mantegna and the Venetian painters.","c":"Benaglio's Madonna is a jewel-like picture: the pearls, the net and the veil are painted with a goldsmith's precision, and the landscape is a pleasing distant view. The firm outline shows the sculptural clarity that Verona admired in Mantegna. But the faces are rather impassive and the Child's pose is awkward, and the transfer from panel to canvas may have dulled the surface. It is a charming minor work, valued more for its detail than its depth of feeling."},"nga:41666":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 139 by 67 centimetres, painted in tempera on spruce, shows Saint Jerome, the scholar and hermit, as a single figure. Its proportions suggest that it was one panel of a larger altarpiece, in which saints were set in separate frames side by side. Benaglio, a Veronese painter influenced by Mantegna, gave such figures a hard, sculptural outline, and Jerome's usual attributes, a cardinal's hat, a book, a stone and a lion, are the elements by which he is identified.","i":"Jerome, who translated the Bible into Latin, was shown both as a scholar at his desk and as a penitent hermit striking his breast with a stone; the Renaissance, which prized learning, loved the first, and the Church the second. Later legend made him a cardinal, hence the red hat, and added the lion whose paw he healed. Altarpiece saints worked as intercessors, whom the faithful asked for help, and this Jerome probably stood beside others in a larger ensemble.","c":"Benaglio's Jerome has the dry, linear clarity of the Veronese followers of Mantegna: firm drawing, cool light and a sculptural mass of drapery. The tall format gives the saint an imposing presence. The cost is stiffness: the figure can look carved rather than lived, and the expression is austere. Next to Mantegna himself, Benaglio is a gifted but provincial echo, though as a record of Verona's taste in the 1470s it is useful and handsome."},"met:437146":{"d":"A woman and her dog are the subject of this portrait, painted in the years around 1600 in oil on a substantial canvas of about 119 by 99 cm. The format gives the sitter room to be shown well beyond head and shoulders, a way of displaying her dress and rank. Francesco Montemezzano belonged to the circle of Paolo Veronese, and the picture follows the Venetian tradition of showing a sitter at ample scale with a household companion.","i":"In portraits of this period a dog beside a woman is usually read as a sign of fidelity, and in a portrait of a married woman it would speak of marital loyalty; it is also a sign of companionship and comfort. Venetian portraits of women displayed a family's wealth through dress and jewelry, and were made to mark marriage or status. The sitter is unnamed in the title, so her identity and the occasion of the portrait remain open.","c":"Montemezzano worked in the shadow of Veronese and inherited his sumptuous, silvery elegance and his taste for fabric and surface. What the follower generally lacks is the master's freedom and inventiveness, and his portraits tend to be descriptive rather than searching. As an image of Venetian ladies around 1600, the portrait is attractive and well-bred; the dog adds a note of warmth that formal portraits of the time often avoid."},"met:437957":{"d":"Saint Lucy is shown on a very small panel, about 31 by 20 cm, painted in tempera and gold. The narrow, upright format suggests that it was one saint in a larger ensemble, perhaps a polyptych or multi-panel altarpiece, rather than a picture meant to stand alone. Lucy is usually identified by a palm of martyrdom and a dish holding her eyes. Francesco Zaganelli came from Cotignola in the Romagna and worked in the decades around 1500.","i":"Lucy of Syracuse was martyred in Sicily around 304 under the emperor Diocletian. Her name suggests light, lux, and in legend her eyes were torn out and then restored, so she became the patron saint of eyesight and of the blind; the dish with eyes became her emblem. She was widely venerated in Italy, and a saint's panel in an ensemble stood as an intercessor for the donor or congregation.","c":"Zaganelli was a provincial Romagnol painter working in the orbit of Francia and the Ferrarese painters, and a single saint on a tiny panel shows him at his most dependable: a clear outline, tidy color, a polite sweetness. He does not rival the masters of the region, and the hardness that critics find in his work is a limit. As a fragment it is hard to judge fully; as an object it is neat, precious and devout."},"met:436495":{"d":"A saint raises a dead man to life in this small panel, about 36 by 41 cm, painted in tempera on a gold ground. The title names the saint as John the Evangelist and the man as Satheus, and men and a woman appear with him. It is one of three similar panels at the Met from a series on the apostle's life, probably the lower register of an altarpiece, painted by Francescuccio Ghissi of Fabriano in the Marches around 1370.","i":"The medieval legend of John, drawn from apocryphal Acts and the Golden Legend, portrays him as a miracle-worker whose healing and raising of the dead echo the miracles of Christ. For a medieval viewer, resurrection miracles confirmed the saint's holiness and his power to intercede for the living and the dead. A cycle of such small scenes was often placed along the base of an altarpiece, the predella, to be read like a story.","c":"Ghissi, a Fabriano painter in the Gothic tradition of the Marches, tells his stories plainly: small figures, bright color, a gold ground that flattens space and keeps attention on the gesture. The strengths are clarity and decorative charm; the weaknesses are stiffness and a limited sense of space. It is a provincial but pleasing example of a type, and the gesture carries the story."},"met:436496":{"d":"John the Evangelist is joined by two companions, Acteus and Eugenius, in this small tempera panel, about 37 by 41 cm, on a gold ground. Men and a woman appear among the figures. It is one of three similar panels at the Met, all from a series on the apostle's life by Francescuccio Ghissi of Fabriano, painted around 1370. Small scenes like this probably formed part of the lower register of a larger altarpiece.","i":"The names Acteus and Eugenius belong to followers of John in the medieval legends of his life at Ephesus. The cycle of John emphasizes both his miracles and his role as teacher and leader of a Christian community. A scene that shows him with disciples reminds viewers that holiness was transmitted through a chain of teaching, and gives the saint a human setting between the miracles.","c":"Quiet scenes of teaching and companionship test a painter differently from miracles: with no collapse or resurrection to carry the drama, interest depends on the grouping and on the figures' relation to one another. Ghissi's manner, linear and decorative, serves best when the gestures are clear. As part of a series the panel gives the cycle breathing space; taken alone it is the least eventful of the three."},"met:436497":{"d":"A pagan temple collapses at the saint's command in this small panel, about 36 by 39 cm, painted in tempera on a gold ground. The title names John the Evangelist, and the scene shows men and a temple. It belongs to the same group of three Ghissi scenes of John at the Met, painted by Francescuccio Ghissi of Fabriano around 1370. Small narrative panels of this kind were commonly set into the base of an altarpiece.","i":"In the legend of John at Ephesus, the saint's prayer brings down the pagan temple, most likely the temple of Diana, and the crowd, in the story, turns to the faith. The temple of Artemis at Ephesus was one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world, so its fall dramatizes the triumph of Christianity over a great pagan cult. For medieval viewers, the toppling of idols was a standard sign of an apostle's power.","c":"Collapse is a hard thing to paint, and the small gold-ground format forces Ghissi to show it as a sign rather than a spectacle. That convention is both the charm and the limit of Gothic narrative: the event is told rather than staged. The panel is lively as invention and modest as drawing, a competent piece of Marchigian storytelling from a painter more at home with devotion than with action."},"met:437495":{"d":"A woman identified as Sophia of Poland (1464–1512), margravine of Brandenburg-Ansbach, is the subject of this oil on canvas of about 77 by 57 cm. The sitter died in 1512, but the painter, Franz Wolfgang Rohrich (1787–1834), worked in the nineteenth century, so this is a historical portrait made three centuries after her death, presumably based on an earlier likeness or imagined in period costume. The format is that of a bust or half-length picture.","i":"Sophia Jagiellon was the daughter of Casimir IV of Poland and, as wife of Frederick I of Brandenburg-Ansbach, mother of a large family, among them Albert, the last Grand Master of the Teutonic Order and the first Duke of Prussia. A portrait of such a woman served a dynasty's memory: nineteenth-century German courts and families cultivated images of their ancestors, and the Romantic age liked the medieval and Renaissance past.","c":"Historical portraits like this are exercises in imagination: the painter evokes an era's costume and dignity from tradition and from older images. The pleasure lies in the atmosphere of the period; the weakness is the inevitable nineteenth-century sensibility under the fifteenth-century dress. Rohrich is little known today, and the picture's interest is chiefly historical, as a record of how the nineteenth century pictured its royal and noble ancestors."},"met:438559":{"d":"Soldiers, women, an infant, men and horses are caught in the violence of battle in this small canvas of 1824, about 36 by 38 centimeters. The title identifies the event as the capture of Jerusalem by the Roman army under Titus in the year 70, when the city and its Temple were destroyed. The size suggests an oil sketch or reduction of a larger composition by François Joseph Heim, a painter of large history subjects.","i":"The sack of Jerusalem was known from the Jewish historian Josephus, and Christian tradition read it as the fulfilment of Christ's prophecy that the Temple would fall. French painters of the 1820s liked such crowded scenes of catastrophe, which allowed a display of drama, anatomy and emotion on a grand scale, as in the work of Gros and Géricault. The woman and infant bring home the human cost of conquest.","c":"The sketch shows the energy of a composition conceived in violent movement, with figures piling into one another, and the small format concentrates the effect. Heim's neoclassical training keeps the drama orderly, and the result is more rhetorical than raw. As a statement of the 1820s taste for history on the grand scale it is typical; as an experience of horror it is distanced by its theatricality."},"nga:130880":{"d":"Cotton-white and slate-gray clouds churn across a pale blue sky over a grassy hill that slopes to a calm bay in this small oil sketch on paper, about 23 by 30 centimeters. Two figures on a beach piled with boulders bend toward the sand and stand watching, painted loosely. A dozen masted ships with furled sails line the harbor across the water, and pale gray mountains lie on the far horizon. The water shifts from deep teal to aquamarine.","i":"Léon Fleury made this sketch in Italy around 1830, when French landscape painters traveled to Rome and Naples to study light and form from nature. Oil sketches on paper, painted quickly outdoors, were a new habit, encouraged by the teaching of Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes. They were not meant for sale but were kept as records and as sources for studio pictures, and they now seem remarkably fresh.","c":"The sketch has the immediacy of a direct response to an effect: the churning clouds, the loosely brushed hill and the shifting color of the water are caught with relaxed confidence. The small figures are mere touches. It is a modest sketch, but it shows how the open-air discipline of the 1820s and 1830s prepared the way for later landscape painting."},"nga:130898":{"d":"The ruins of a round, crenellated stone tower stand on gently sloping hills against a pale peach sky in this small horizontal landscape. Two deep green cypress trees lean against the golden brown and rust walls of the tower, and a charcoal-gray ruined wall overlaps it at the left. Low grasses are straw and mustard yellow, a loosely painted figure with a staff stands near the tower, and touches of tawny brown suggest sheep. The canvas measures about 28 by 33 centimeters.","i":"The Tomb of Caecilia Metella is a Roman mausoleum of the first century BC on the Appian Way outside Rome, built for a noblewoman, and in the Middle Ages it was converted into a fortress, which explains the battlements. It was a favorite stop for travelers and artists in the Roman Campagna, a landscape of ruins and shepherds that symbolized the passing of ancient glory. Léon Fleury painted it around 1830.","c":"Loose, rapid brushstrokes and a restrained palette give the sketch a convincing sense of light at the end of a day, and the cypresses add vertical punctuation. The scene is a familiar one, but Fleury's handling makes it fresh. Though modest, the picture is a good example of the direct, small-scale landscape painting by French artists in Italy in the 1830s."},"met:437075":{"d":"A woman is portrayed on ivory in this miniature of about 1830, said to be Marie de Mautesson. The identification of the sitter rests on tradition, since the title says only that she is said to be this person. François Meuret, a French miniaturist who lived from 1800 to 1887, worked in the decades when the Romantic taste for ringlets, wide sleeves and delicate color dominated women's fashion.","i":"A title that says said to be preserves a tradition that the sitter was a particular person, handed down in a family or a collection, without documents to prove it. Aristocratic names like Mautesson are typical of the clientele of Parisian miniaturists under the July Monarchy, which valued small, elegant, flattering likenesses. The fashion for portrait miniatures was soon to vanish with the arrival of the daguerreotype.","c":"A conventional, refined miniature of the period: smooth, flattering and crisply finished, with the Romantic sweetness that audiences expected. Its interest is partly in the tradition of the name. As art it is accomplished rather than distinctive, and its virtue is precise craft on a tiny scale."},"met:11301":{"d":"George Washington is presented with an eagle in a commemorative image painted about 1803 in oil and gold leaf on paper, about 56 by 45 cm. The title, The American Star, casts him as a guiding light of the new republic, and the gold leaf adds a literal gleam. Frederick Kemmelmeyer was a folk painter active in Maryland, and his work belongs to the tradition of American folk painting.","i":"After Washington's death in 1799, the nation mourned and made him a secular saint. Prints, paintings and objects showed him crowned, ascending, accompanied by angels, Liberty or the eagle, the symbol of the republic. Calling him a star used the poetic language of the day for a guiding light fixed above political storms. Such images met a popular demand for patriotic icons.","c":"Folk painters like Kemmelmeyer lack academic polish, and the interest lies in directness: bold shapes, flat color, gleaming gold. The Washington is probably adapted from prints of Gilbert Stuart's portrait, simplified, so that the image is more emblem than likeness. The charm is earnest enthusiasm; the limits are stiff drawing and flat space. As a record of how ordinary Americans pictured Washington, it is more revealing than a polished state portrait."},"nga:50689":{"d":"Seven light-skinned men stand closely together at the water's edge facing about a dozen Indigenous people gathered across a jewel-blue waterway, in this horizontal painting about 70 by 93 centimeters, dated 1800 to 1805. Five of the Europeans wear tall black hats with blue and pink feathers and slashed suits in royal blue, white, green or brick red; behind them a balding man in a brown robe holds a tall wooden cross. The man at the front gestures toward the islanders. Rowboats and three ships lie beyond, and one islander kneels, offering a pineapple. Hazy blue mountains close the distance.","i":"Columbus's first landfall in 1492 was a favorite subject in the young United States, where he was celebrated as a hero of discovery and gave his name to the poetic figure of Columbia. The painter, a provincial American artist, imagined the scene without any reliable source, dressing the Europeans in costumes of a later date. The inscription places the landing on San Salvador in South America on 11 October, which differs from the conventional account of the Bahamas and 12 October, and shows how loosely such history was pictured.","c":"As history the picture is unreliable; as a document of early American imagery it is fascinating. The costumes are more theatrical than correct, and the space is arranged like a stage, with the two groups facing each other across the water. That naive directness has its charm, and the clear colors and decorative detail have a folk-art appeal. It is best seen as a patriotic myth made visible, with its limits and its interest equally apparent."},"met:435895":{"d":"A very small self-portrait, about 22 by 18 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas between 1825 and 1828. The museum's record names the sitter as George Chinnery and lists a man, so his features, dress and expression are not described here. The picture dates from the first years of his life in Macau, where the British painter settled in 1825 after more than two decades in India, and it is small enough to hold in the hand, an informal, private image rather than a commission.","i":"Chinnery left England for India in 1802 and, after years of portrait painting for the British community in Madras and Calcutta, settled at Macau, the Portuguese enclave on the China coast, where he painted merchants, officials and the local scene until his death in 1852. Self-portraits let a portraitist practice his craft without a patron and leave an image of himself for friends. His casual, sketch-like manner suits such small pictures.","c":"Chinnery is best remembered for quick, lively drawings of Macau, and as a painter he combined the English portrait tradition with a sketchy fluency. A small self-portrait is where such fluency should be most at home: direct observation, a few strokes and an immediate likeness. Without a description of its details one cannot judge its particular qualities, but as a record of an expatriate painter at about fifty it carries an interest that is personal as well as historical."},"nga:130893":{"d":"A small upright canvas shows a hill town in southern Italy. A rocky brown hill fills the lower third of the picture. At its top stand white buildings with brown roofs, ringed by green trees, and more dense trees and structures lie on the far side of the slope. Beyond them rise distant mountains under a blue sky with a few white clouds. The scene is built from broad, simple masses of earth, foliage and sky. At about 41 by 34 centimeters, it is a cabinet view of a place rather than a large exhibition picture.","i":"La Cava is the town now called Cava de' Tirreni, near Salerno, a stop for travelers heading south from Naples toward Salerno, Amalfi and Paestum. Its rocky hills and white houses suited the picturesque taste of the day. French landscape painters of the 1830s and 1840s often made small views in Italy from direct observation, partly as training and partly as records for larger work. This one may have served that purpose, though its original use is not known.","c":"The picture works by plain contrast: a heavy brown hill below, small bright houses above it, pale mountains and sky beyond. That clarity is its strength, and the cool blue distance gives the town some air. There is little incident to hold the eye beyond the town itself. Among the many Italian views French artists produced around 1840 it is competent and unshowy rather than distinctive, and it records a habit of looking more than a personality."},"nga:206124":{"d":"Three tall panels, each ending in a sharp gable under a rounded arch, stand side by side, and in each a woman is shown from the hips up against gold. In the center the Virgin, in blue over crimson, looks down at the blond Child, who twists away holding cherries; a blessing Christ appears in the gable above. At left Mary Magdalene, in red, holds an ivory jar; at right Saint Catherine, crowned, braces a wooden wheel. An angel with a palm frond fills each side gable, and haloes are incised in the gold. All three surfaces are cracked and chipped.","i":"A Virgin and Child between two female saints was a standard scheme for small altarpieces and private devotion. Mary Magdalene, with her ointment jar, stands for penitence and devotion; Catherine, with crown, wheel and palm, for royal birth, learning and martyrdom. The cherries, the Child's lively gesture and the blessing Christ above the Virgin join the human and the divine in one image. The scale, with the central panel about 98 centimeters high, suggests a chapel altar or a wealthy patron's devotions.","c":"Seen whole, the triptych is a model of gold-ground harmony: red at the left, blue and crimson at the center, ivory and gold at the right, with three frontal half-lengths giving a calm rhythm. The Child's turn is the one lively note. It is a conservative format handled with unusual warmth and solidity, though damage to the gold dulls its original brilliance. Records of Pietro Lorenzetti's career span 1306 to 1345; this belongs to its last decade."},"nga:891":{"d":"Mary Magdalene appears from the hips up against a gold ground, in a tall panel that narrows to a point at the top. She holds an ivory-white cylindrical jar, her left hand beneath it and her right on its peaked lid. A tomato-red mantle covers her head and falls over a matching dress, both bordered with black geometric bands, and blond hair shows at her temples. Her pale skin has a faint green cast, and her hooded eyes look out at the viewer. In the pointed gable above, an angel with golden wings holds a palm frond. The gold is cracked and chipped.","i":"The jar identifies her. Mary Magdalene is the follower of Christ who brought ointment to his tomb and, in Western tradition, was also identified with the penitent sinner who anointed his feet. Red is a frequent color for her in Italian painting, linked with love and penitence. Standing at the left of the Virgin in a triptych, she acts as an intercessor for the worshiper. The palm carried by the angel above is the common sign of spiritual victory.","c":"Pietro Lorenzetti gives the half-length figure real weight: the broad red mantle makes one strong shape against the gold, and her wide, heavy-lidded gaze lends a quiet inwardness. The decoration, with its black-line borders and incised halo, is conventional, and cracks and losses in the gold blunt the effect. Beside his emotionally charged Passion frescoes at Assisi this is a calm devotional work, but the same sculptural feeling for the body is evident."},"nga:892":{"d":"The Virgin, seen from the hips up, holds the Christ Child upright against a gold ground, in a tall panel with a pointed top. She wears a sapphire-blue mantle over a crimson dress, with the edge of a white veil showing, and holds a bunch of red cherries by the child's hip. The Child, in a yellow tunic that leaves one bare foot visible, turns to look back over his shoulder. He holds three cherries to his mouth and reaches for hers with the other hand, while a cloth flutters from his shoulder like a pennant. In the gable above, a bearded Christ raises two fingers in blessing.","i":"Cherries in Madonna-and-Child pictures are usually read as the fruit of paradise, a promise of heaven to the virtuous, and sometimes as a reminder of Christ's blood. Here the Child's eager reach turns the symbol into a human gesture between mother and son. The bearded figure in the gable, making the two-finger gesture of blessing, shows what the Child will become: the adult Christ, teacher and judge. This is the devotional focus of the triptych.","c":"This is the liveliest of the three panels. The Child's twist, the reaching hand and the fluttering cloth break the stiff symmetry of the gold-ground type, and the sapphire, crimson and yellow are handled with a firm sense of local color. The faces keep the long-nosed, almond-eyed formula, so the animation lives in gesture rather than expression. It is affectionate and a little playful, and typical of Pietro's gift for small domestic action."},"nga:893":{"d":"Saint Catherine of Alexandria is shown from the hips up against gold, in a tall panel that ends in a point. She wears a gold crown set with blue and red gems and holds a palm frond in the hand that rests, with its elbow, on a wooden wheel. Her ivory dress and cloak carry gold foliage patterns and brick-red edges, and she gathers the cloak around her other elbow. Her almond-shaped eyes meet the viewer, and her skin has a greenish-gray tinge. In the gable an angel with yellow and blue wings, head turned toward the center, carries another palm. The gold is cracked and chipped.","i":"Catherine, a legendary princess and scholar of Alexandria, is said to have out-argued pagan philosophers before the emperor and to have been sentenced to death on a spiked wheel, which broke at her touch before she was beheaded. The wheel is therefore her emblem, with the crown of her royal birth and the palm of martyrdom. Paired with Mary Magdalene on the other wing, she offers a counterpart: one saint learned and royal, the other penitent.","c":"The wheel and crown are rendered with quiet economy, and the patterned ivory and gold cloak is among the richest passages in the triptych. The relaxed way she leans an elbow on the wheel is a humane touch, though it also makes the martyr's instrument look a little like a prop. The angel above repeats the Magdalene's angel almost as a mirror image, a symmetry that is tidy rather than inventive. A gentle, devotional Pietro, not a dramatic one."},"met:438605":{"d":"On a burnished gold ground within a gilded frame, Christ hangs on a cross at the centre, blood streaming from his side and hands, a small plaque marked INRI above his head. Two thieves hang on crosses on either side, one turned away. At the lower left the Virgin, in a deep blue mantle, sinks into the arms of Saint John and holy women in saffron, rose and green. Soldiers in red tunics and steel helmets crowd the right with spears, while two horsemen, one on a dark brown horse and one on a white one, ride in the foreground. Halos are tooled in the gold, and a patterned border runs round the edge. Egg tempera and gold leaf on wood, about 42 by 32 centimetres.","i":"Pietro Lorenzetti worked in Siena and Assisi; his Passion frescoes in the lower church of San Francesco at Assisi, painted about 1320, are among the great emotional narratives of the fourteenth century, and this small panel, from the 1340s, echoes that crowded, grieving manner. A panel of this size was probably meant for private devotion, perhaps as part of a portable altarpiece, but its original setting is not known. The scene gathers the Gospel moments around the Cross: the swooning Virgin, the soldiers, the haloed centurion on horseback, the two thieves. Lorenzetti died, probably in the plague of 1348, so the panel belongs to the last years of his career.","c":"Pietro's drama is in the emotion more than the space. The swooning Virgin and the clustered mourners have a grief that still reads at once, the line is crisp, the colour glows against the gold, and the two horsemen add a bold diagonal. Compared with his brother Ambrogio, Pietro is the sterner and more sculptural; compared with the Assisi frescoes, this panel is necessarily compressed, and the crowd can feel crammed. It is not a landmark of the period, but it is a small work of exceptional quality."},"nga:98290":{"d":"A rocky green ravine holds a gray stream, hemmed in by thick foliage and large boulders. At the center an arched stone bridge crosses the water, with small wooden buildings leaning against the ravine's sides on either hand. Steep forested hills rise behind, leaving only a small gap at the top for blue sky with a few puffy white clouds. The picture is small, about 31 by 37 centimeters, and painted in oil on paper laid down on canvas, a support often used for studies made from nature.","i":"Rousseau painted this in 1830, in his late teens, in the Auvergne, the volcanic highland of central France. The rough bridge and cottages are the kind of picturesque motif that mountain regions offered young artists. Painting such a place directly stood against the academic habit of composing idealized landscapes in the studio, and the picture is an early sign of the observation that later made Rousseau a leader of the Barbizon painters.","c":"What succeeds here is candor: boulders, water and foliage have real weight, and the bridge gives the eye a firm center in a crowded view. The handling is that of a beginner finding his way, and the tight enclosure of the ravine leaves little room to breathe. Still, the commitment to unidealized nature is already Rousseau's. It is best valued as an early statement of purpose, well short of the richly worked landscapes of his maturity."},"nga:127211":{"d":"The picture is a very long, narrow strip, more than three times as wide as it is high. The viewer looks across treetops painted in pine and olive greens onto a cluster of roofs in muted terracotta with white chimneys; the farthest building, with a gray roof and a lantern, may be a church. Smoke rises from one chimney. Beyond a pale grassy field with round-crowned trees and rows of dark cypress, a ribbon of ice-blue water crosses the left third, and russet and sage hills roll back into haze beneath pale gray and cream clouds.","i":"The Moselle rises in the Vosges in eastern France and flows north toward the Rhine, and this view, painted about 1830, looks down over farmland near its banks. The very long format was probably chosen to take in a whole sweep of country at once. Whether it was a study made on the spot or a panel meant for a particular place is not known. The distant building with a lantern may be a church, which would add a quiet human center to an otherwise empty landscape.","c":"The extreme format is a gamble that largely pays off: terracotta roofs, pale meadow, dark cypress and the ice-blue ribbon of water step back in orderly bands to hazy hills, and the sky is kept quiet. With no figure or strong incident, though, the eye slides along the strip without settling, and the picture reads more as an observation than a composition. It is a bold early experiment from a young painter, valuable for its freedom more than for finish."},"met:437518":{"d":"This is a horizontal landscape in oil on a wooden panel, about 42 by 66 centimeters. The title and the recorded subjects, a river, trees and cattle, point to a quiet pastoral view of water, grass and grazing animals. It belongs to the type of cabinet landscape that Rousseau painted again and again, in which a stretch of ordinary countryside, rather than a heroic or classical scene, supplies the whole subject. Dated about 1840, it comes from the decade when he was building his reputation among fellow artists while the Salon jury kept turning his work away.","i":"Cattle beside a river are an old pastoral motif, strongly associated with Dutch painters of the 1600s such as Aelbert Cuyp and Paulus Potter, whom French landscapists of Rousseau's generation admired. Around 1840, however, a picture of plain pasture was a risky thing to send to the Salon, where juries favored ideal or historical landscape. Rousseau was refused so often in these years that he became known as the great refused, 'le grand refusé', and quiet river meadows like this show what he went on painting regardless.","c":"A typical Rousseau of this kind shows his strengths: patient observation of trees, careful gradation of light across a flat space, and a feeling for damp, living air. The theme itself is a standard one, and cattle and trees can become stock props in lesser hands; his advantage is that he treats them as parts of a particular light and weather. Judged as a type, it is a quietly confident example of his small panels rather than a landmark."},"met:437519":{"d":"A horizontal landscape in oil on wood, roughly 42 by 63 centimeters, shows a river with trees and a boat. These are the motifs recorded for the picture, and together they make a calm waterside view organized around the river. The date of about 1845 to 1850 places it in the years when Rousseau was moving from his early, closely observed studies toward the fuller, more richly worked landscapes of the Barbizon period. A boat on a river is a small human note in a scene that is otherwise about trees, water and sky.","i":"River landscapes with a boat were a staple of Dutch and English painting, from Ruisdael to Constable, and both traditions mattered to Rousseau and his friends. A boat gives scale and suggests the quiet working life of a river without calling for a story. In the later 1840s Rousseau was developing the idea that a landscape should convey the character of a place and its weather, not an ideal scene, an idea that artists took up before the Salon public did.","c":"Rousseau's river pictures of these years typically succeed through tonal control, with trees and sky tied together by a single prevailing light, and the boat and water supplying a little quiet incident. The motif is conventional, and a river view can easily become a pleasant arrangement; his best work avoids that through a feeling for damp air and the weight of foliage. A picture of this kind sits comfortably among his unpretentious works of the middle years."},"nga:176848":{"d":"The title names an effect rather than a place, so the subject is light falling across a landscape. The picture is a small oil on a wooden panel, about 35 by 55 centimeters, a modest cabinet format. It dates from around 1848 to 1850, when Rousseau was working from Barbizon, on the edge of the Forest of Fontainebleau. No particular site or incident is named, so the picture is best understood as a landscape in which weather and sunlight carry the interest, with trees, ground and sky serving as the stage for it.","i":"Pictures named for a light effect belong to the central ambition of the Barbizon painters, who tried to record the look of a particular hour and weather rather than an ideal scene. For Rousseau this meant long observation of the forest and plains around Barbizon, followed by a studio method of building up and reworking a picture over months or years. A title like Sunlight invites the viewer to read the whole panel as a study of illumination, with every landscape element answering to it.","c":"Rousseau's small light pictures of these years usually offer a warm, rich range of greens and golds, a sky treated as the source of illumination, and a surface that rewards close looking. Their weakness, when it appears, is overworking, which can make light feel built up rather than glimpsed. At this size the likely achievement is an intimate success of tone rather than a grand statement."},"nga:195088":{"d":"Across a waterlogged path stands a line of rust-red and bronze-brown trees that nearly spans this wide horizontal canvas. The handling is loose, which gives the scene a soft, blurred look. The foreground lies in shadow, but pockets of bright ice-blue suggest water reflecting the pale sky. The trees have thin trunks and billowing dark canopies, and a few strokes of brown, crimson and white suggest a rider and perhaps a child on the path beneath them. Beyond a line of shadow a strip of bright green lawn runs back to a horizon about a third of the way up, under a veil of thin gray and white cloud.","i":"The title points to the moment just after a shower, when puddles mirror the sky and the air is still damp, an effect landscape painters prized for showing weather. The copper and rust of the foliage suggest autumn. The figures are barely more than touches of paint; they give scale and a human presence but no story. The loose, almost unfinished handling may reflect a rapid sketch from observation or a picture left at the stage of a broad lay-in, and it is hard to tell which.","c":"The great strength is atmosphere: the damp, veiled light and the reflections in the path are conveyed with very little paint, and the horizontal format gives the line of trees a satisfying rhythm. The cost is legibility, since the shadowed foreground dissolves into half-seen forms and the figures can hardly be read. Rousseau often worked slowly and prized finish, so this freer handling stands apart in his output; it is a vivid report of weather rather than a resolved composition."},"met:437514":{"d":"This is a large panel, about 80 by 122 centimeters, painted in oil on wood and dated 1852 to 1854. The title places it at the edge of the woods at Monts-Girard, a locality in the Forest of Fontainebleau, where the trees give way to more open ground, and the recorded subjects are trees, forest and landscape. Its size and its long dating suggest a considered studio picture rather than a quick study, with the forest margin as the setting for a full, carefully built composition of foliage, ground and sky.","i":"The Forest of Fontainebleau, near Paris, was Rousseau's lifelong subject, and he became its public defender, joining other painters in protesting against felling and replanting and in seeking protection for its oldest trees and rocks. A forest edge, where dense woodland meets open ground, lets a painter set massed foliage against sky and distance. Pictures of the early 1850s like this one confirm Rousseau's standing as the leader of the landscapists who worked around Barbizon, though the public took longer to see it than artists did.","c":"The scale and the long working period suggest ambition, and in his best forest pictures Rousseau builds a dense, nearly sculptural mass of foliage and then opens it with a gleam of light. The risk of such works is a certain heaviness from overworking, which can dim the freshness of the small studies. As a large forest picture of the early 1850s it is a serious statement of his aims, weighty rather than spontaneous."},"nga:178311":{"d":"An open green field stretches away into the distance in this small canvas, about 22 by 27 centimeters. At the bottom is a rocky patch with sparse vegetation and sun-bleached yellow grass. Farther back are small clusters of green trees and thatched-roof buildings, partly hidden on the right. A small touch of red at the center suggests a person or two walking along a brown path, and tiny dots of white and pink in the far distance might be flowers or buildings. The colors are earthy and muted, browns, greens and grays, and the upper half is sky, with textured white clouds swirling against patches of light blue.","i":"Picardy, in northern France, is a region of wide, open farmland, very different from the dense woods of Fontainebleau that Rousseau usually painted, and the title shows him looking at new country in 1860. The tiny figures give scale to a landscape that is mostly sky and field, and the thatched buildings point to a working rural community rather than a picturesque ruin. Its small size suggests an informal record of a place rather than a picture prepared for exhibition.","c":"The earthy, muted palette is handled with restraint, and the swirling sky, painted with visible texture, gives the picture its life; the tiny red figure on the path is a well-judged accent. The foreground is thin and rather bare, though, and the vast empty field offers little to hold the eye. It is a fresh, plain-spoken note from Rousseau's later years, closer in spirit to a sketch than to his ambitious forest pictures."},"nga:35094":{"d":"A calm stretch of water crosses the center of this tiny panel, beyond a brown bank of rocks and short green grass. A thin tree rises on the left, and a small gray house with a tall tree beside it sends a wisp of smoke from its chimney. To the right of center a boatman is silhouetted against the blue water. Flat green fields with distant trees lie beyond, lit by pale light. Large gray and white clouds cross the blue sky, and the water repeats its colors along with the green and brown of the house and far bank. It measures only about 19 by 26 centimeters.","i":"The smoking chimney, the quiet water and the lone boatman make a vision of rural calm in which people are part of the landscape rather than its subject. Painted about 1860, in the last decade of Rousseau's life, it is a small panel of the kind a painter could use to work out a mood rather than a program, alongside his larger exhibition pictures. The pale distance and the water's reflected sky show a continuing interest in atmosphere and light across the whole surface.","c":"At this small size the picture succeeds by economy: a horizontal band of water, the dark silhouette of the boatman and the thin tree supply scale and rhythm with very little paint, and the sky is convincingly repeated in the water. It does not try to compete with his larger forest pictures; its charm is one of quiet and balance. Its limits are those of its size, a pleasing observation rather than a discovery."},"met:437515":{"d":"A horizontal landscape in oil on wood, about 42 by 62 centimeters, takes a meadow and a border of trees as its whole subject. The Met gives only a span of roughly fifteen years for it, about 1845 to 1860, a wide margin that probably reflects the difficulty of fixing dates for a painter who often returned to his pictures and reworked them. The recorded subjects are trees and landscape, and the title adds the open meadow they enclose, so the picture is built around the contrast between sunlit grass and the darker mass of foliage.","i":"Trees were Rousseau's chief subject. He painted them as individuals, with age and character, rather than as a generic green mass, and a border of trees around a meadow lets a painter show how light moves from open grass into dense shade. Landscapes of this plain kind were less valued in the 1840s than stately classical views, and Rousseau and his Barbizon friends are credited with making an ordinary corner of nature a worthy subject.","c":"The strength of a picture like this lies in the weight and individuality of the trees and in the gradation of light between meadow and shade; the weakness, in many of Rousseau's smaller panels, is a certain sameness of motif. As one of many quiet panels of his middle years it is solid and truthful rather than surprising, and it gains from being seen beside its companions, where the variety of his observation becomes clear."},"met:437520":{"d":"A large horizontal panel, about 64 by 99 centimeters, painted in oil on wood. The title places the scene near Arbonne, a village on the edge of the Forest of Fontainebleau close to Barbizon, and the recorded subjects are trees, landscape and evening. It is therefore a sunset landscape, in which the low light and the evening sky presumably carry more weight than any incident on the ground. Painted around 1860 to 1865, it belongs to Rousseau's last working years, when he was widely respected as a landscape painter.","i":"Sunset was a favorite subject of the Barbizon painters, who treated the fading of light as a subject in itself rather than an incidental effect; for Rousseau it let him set dark masses of trees against a glowing sky. Arbonne lies at the edge of the plain beside the forest, close to his home at Barbizon, so the view was part of his everyday surroundings, the kind of motif he could return to at different hours and seasons.","c":"Sunsets can easily tip into the theatrical, and the question for any such picture is whether the light is observed or staged. Rousseau at his best gives the sky a believable depth and lets dark trees hold it like a frame, and the large size gives this panel presence. A reliance on a glowing sky is an effect more than a discovery, but a late Rousseau of this scale deserves serious attention."},"met:437516":{"d":"A small panel, about 27 by 35 centimeters, painted in oil on wood around 1835, when Rousseau was in his early twenties. The recorded subjects are trees and landscape, and the title adds an old chapel in a valley, so the scene is a modest rural view in which an aged building sits among foliage. It belongs to the first phase of his career, when he was learning landscape by traveling and sketching in the French provinces rather than working from classical models. It is a small observed view of a rural ruin of the kind that appealed to young painters of the time.","i":"An old chapel in a valley is a typical Romantic motif: a building weathered by time and absorbed by nature, inviting thoughts of age and continuity, and seeming more authentic than the formal temples of classical landscape. Young painters of the 1830s sought such places in provincial France. Rousseau's early journeys, to the Auvergne and the Jura among other regions, gave him the observations he later drew on in the studio, though the title does not name this particular valley.","c":"Early Rousseaus have a sincerity that the polished work of his maturity sometimes lacks, and a small panel like this, painted in his early twenties, probably prizes direct observation over effect. What it cannot yet have is the command of massed foliage and layered light that came later. As a type it is an appealing document of a young painter's apprenticeship in the open, a modest picture whose interest is mainly historical."},"met:437517":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 38 by 60 centimeters, painted in oil on wood and dated probably 1861. The title gives the time of day, early morning, the season, early summer, and the place, the Forest of Fontainebleau, so the subject is a woodland view in the soft light of a summer dawn. The recorded subjects are trees and landscape. It is therefore a picture about a moment of the day as much as about a place, the kind of subject that kept Rousseau in the forest year after year.","i":"The year 1861 is also the year Napoleon III set aside part of the Forest of Fontainebleau as an artists' reserve, a protection that Rousseau and other painters had campaigned for. That makes this picture, if the date is right, a document of a forest the painters believed worth saving. Early summer morning light, with its cool air and low sun, was a favorite subject for painters who wanted calm rather than drama. The link to the reserve is one of timing, not of documented intention.","c":"Rousseau's late forest pictures are at their best when they hold light and mass in balance, and an early morning subject suits his gift for soft, humid atmosphere. The risk is a certain sweetness of effect, and the scale is modest; the picture is likely to be a refined small work rather than one of the forceful statements of his career. Its value lies in the specificity of the title, which treats a moment of the day as the subject."},"met:438816":{"d":"A huge, dark canvas shows the interior of an old forest in winter. Bare oaks with twisted limbs fill the picture to the top, their branches knitted into a net against a red-brown sky where the last light of sunset glows between the trunks. A flock of birds streams across the cloudy sky. In the foreground a still pond reflects the fire-coloured light, and mossy boulders lie at the left; near the centre, two stooped figures, barely visible, move through the dimness. The colours are nearly all brown, black and rust, relieved by touches of orange. Oil on canvas, about 163 by 260 centimetres, a very large picture.","i":"Rousseau was a leader of the Barbizon painters, who worked in and around the forest of Fontainebleau. The Met says he began this picture early in his career and left it unfinished at his death in 1867, despite the urging of Millet and other friends to complete and exhibit it; the Met's audio guide says he worked on it off and on for twenty years. It shows the ancient forest near the village of Barbizon, and by one account Rousseau meant to recreate a sunset he had seen in the Bas-Bréau section of the forest in December 1845. Its scale and ambition, rare for a landscape of its time, make it a testament to his lifelong devotion to the old trees.","c":"Even unfinished, the picture is grandly sombre: the web of black branches against the red sky is an effect of real power, and the scale makes the viewer feel inside the wood. But it is also, frankly, hard to like. The browns are heavy, and the lack of any clear focus or movement leaves the eye wandering in a thicket. Rousseau's friends urged him to finish and show it; one can see why he could not. As a statement of obsession it is impressive; as a picture to enjoy, it is a stern one."},"nga:195698":{"d":"A man seen from the waist up sits at a slight angle to the left, one arm resting on a table covered with green cloth. His expression is composed, with dark arched eyebrows, a high forehead and short dark hair with a subtle wave. He wears a black coat, a white shirt and a cravat, and a quill pen lies nearby. The background is a plain, muted brown, leaving attention on the face and the few attributes. Oil on canvas, about 91 by 71 centimeters, painted in 1837 by the American artist George Cooke.","i":"The green-covered table and the quill are conventional signs of a man of business or letters, someone who reads and writes. The plain brown ground and black coat belong to the sober style of American portraiture in the 1830s, which favored the republic's ideal of modest, serious citizenship over the aristocratic display of European portraits. Beyond the name in the title, the sitter's occupation and circumstances are not known.","c":"A restrained, competent portrait in which the green cloth is the one note of color and the face carries the picture. Cooke gives the sitter dignity but little animation, and the pose and setting are formulaic. It is a good example of a professional American likeness of the 1830s, honest and a little dry, valuable for the steadiness with which it records a man who wished to be seen as serious."},"met:435832":{"d":"A man and a woman are the two figures recorded for this small wood panel, about 31 by 51 centimeters, dated 1861. The title identifies the man as a Florentine poet, which places the scene in the Florence of the past, a setting the Academy-trained Cabanel liked to evoke in costume pictures. The wide format suits a pair of figures with room around them, and the whole is painted with the neat finish of a Salon cabinet piece.","i":"Poets and troubadours in historical dress were a staple of French Salon painting in the 1850s and 1860s, when the Romantic taste for the Middle Ages and the Italian Renaissance fed into academic art. Florence was the city of Dante, Petrarch and Lorenzo de' Medici, and a Florentine poet carried associations of love lyric and civic genius. Cabanel had spent years in Italy as a Prix de Rome winner and knew the country well.","c":"Cabanel is the smooth, flattering academic painter par excellence: skillful, polished and eager to please. On a small panel such as this the finish is the point, with costumes and faces neatly arranged and the sentiment gentle. Detractors, from the Impressionists on, found such work glib, and its defenders point to technical command. As a minor Salon picture it shows the Second Empire's taste for sweet historical romance."},"met:435829":{"d":"A nude nymph is the sole subject of this vertical canvas, about 98 by 67 centimeters, dated 1874. She is Echo of Greek myth, and Cabanel gives her the smooth, idealized finish typical of French academic painting, so that the painting rests on one figure, a pose and a feeling, rather than on a crowded story. It is a mythological nude of the kind that Salon visitors and private collectors bought eagerly.","i":"In Ovid's Metamorphoses, Echo was a mountain nymph punished by Juno for chattering, so that she could only repeat the last words spoken to her. She fell in love with Narcissus, was rejected by him, and wasted away until only her voice remained. French academic painters of the Second Empire and early Third Republic favored mythological nudes, which allowed them to show idealized female bodies under the cover of classical learning.","c":"Cabanel's nudes are exquisitely finished and rather cool, the flesh smooth as ivory, the expression vague. The best that can be said for them is craftsmanship; the worst, that the sensuality is too tidy and the poetry absent. Echo's myth is about loss and a voice dwindling to nothing, depths that a painter concerned with surface rarely explores. Taste has swung against such pictures, but their technique deserves attention."},"met:435831":{"d":"Venus, nude, lies on the sea as she is born from its foam, with putti accompanying her, in a wide canvas of about 106 by 183 centimeters, dated 1875. The sea, a mythical creature and the nude figure are its elements, and the picture is a later version of the composition Cabanel first showed at the Paris Salon of 1863, which had made him famous.","i":"In Hesiod's account Aphrodite, whom the Romans called Venus, was born from the sea foam, and Botticelli's Birth of Venus is the best-known earlier treatment. Cabanel's 1863 original was bought by Emperor Napoleon III and was the great academic success of its year, a nude made acceptable by its mythological cover. It was widely reproduced in prints and photographs, which suggests why further versions were wanted.","c":"Cabanel's Venus is the epitome of academic taste under the Second Empire: a languorous, smooth body, controlled color and a faint eroticism that seemed respectable because it was classical. The same year the 1863 Salon des Refusés showed Manet's Déjeuner sur l'herbe, and avant-garde critics found Cabanel sugary. By modern standards it is overbaked, but the technical assurance is real, and it is a key to what mainstream taste admired in the 1860s."},"met:435830":{"d":"Catharine Lorillard Wolfe is the single sitter of this large vertical canvas, about 172 by 109 centimeters, dated 1876, a scale that gives the figure nearly life-size presence. The painting is a formal society portrait, made in the grand manner for a woman of great fortune. The picture's dignity rests on pose, costume and a polished finish. Cabanel was the most celebrated academic painter in France, and a portrait from his hand carried prestige as well as likeness.","i":"Wolfe (1828-1887) was a New York heiress, philanthropist and collector who left her pictures and an endowment to the Metropolitan Museum. To be painted by Cabanel, among the most sought-after portraitists for wealthy Americans in Paris, was a mark of both taste and means. The image gives the authority of a society portrait to a woman whose wealth made her one of New York's leading patrons.","c":"Society portraiture was Cabanel's profitable sideline, and this canvas has what such commissions needed: a dignified pose and a polished surface. The face is composed and the costume skillfully rendered; what is missing is the sharpness of observation that makes a portrait live. Beside the portraits Sargent was about to make in Paris, it is conventional and stately, though as a record of an early patron of the Met it has historical value."},"nga:296":{"d":"Two round gold frames, one for each figure, are set into a nearly square panel, as if two small pictures were mounted on a richly decorated ground. At left the angel Gabriel, in profile, holds white lilies and gestures to the Virgin; at right Mary, in garnet red and light blue, clasps her hands in prayer beside a lectern with a book. Distant hills, winding water and hazy mountains fill the backdrops. Around and below, vines, pelican-like birds and dragon-like creatures twist against black, and two pairs of nude angels with closed eyes lean on urns beneath the frames, their vines meeting to hold a flaming cauldron.","i":"The scene is the Annunciation, when the angel tells Mary that she will bear the Son of God. Gabriel's lilies signify her purity, and her prayer and the book on the lectern show her readiness. The black ground with its twisting vines and creatures imitates grotesque decoration, a style based on ancient Roman wall painting that Italian artists took up around 1500; here it turns a sacred subject into something like a jeweled object. The small size suggests private devotion, though the original purpose is uncertain.","c":"Giannicola di Paolo, a Perugian painter working in the manner of Perugino, gives the figures the sweet, restrained grace of that school. The real invention is the ornament: the mirrored angels with their urns and vines show a lively, even playful imagination, though the effect is busy and the symmetry rather mechanical. This is a minor but charming object, valued as much for its decorative wit as for its devotional force."},"nga:46500":{"d":"A man's head and shoulders are turned slightly to the left, looking out at the viewer, with the left half of his face and torso in shadow. He has pale skin, dark eyebrows, deep-set eyes, an upturned nose and red lips in a neutral expression. His brown hair is tousled and mid-length, and he has a reddish-brown mustache and beard. He wears a light high-collared shirt with a dark tie against a black background, and the brushstrokes are thick and visible.","i":"Henri Fantin-Latour was twenty-two when he painted himself in 1858. He painted many self-portraits in his early years, partly because he could not afford models, and they served as practice in modeling the face. The dark ground and half-shadowed head show his study of the old masters in the Louvre, where he copied for years, and the plain, unflattering look reflects the realism of the generation of Courbet.","c":"A serious young painter measures his craft against Rembrandt: the black ground, the half-lit face and the thick paint all show a determination to learn from the masters. The pose is slightly stiff, and the mood is one of intense self-scrutiny rather than charm. As an early statement of Fantin's sober, truthful manner, it is more revealing than pleasing."},"nga:164918":{"d":"Seen from above, a white plate, a pot, a knife and a spoon are set on a white cloth at the end of a narrow table. The table angles from upper left to lower right, and its corners are cut off by the canvas edges. At the left, a white ceramic vessel with a lid and a teardrop-shaped knob sits on a saucer; at the right, a round plate rests partly off the table with a golden-yellow spoon, and a black-handled knife lies across the rims. The background is a deep reddish brown.","i":"Fantin-Latour was twenty-four when he painted this early still life, and he was still working out his style by copying and by making small studies of ordinary objects. The simple tableware and the arrangement of whites recall Chardin, the great eighteenth-century painter of kitchen objects, whom Fantin admired. A plain meal, set out without any flowers or fruit, shows his early interest in quiet realism.","c":"The pleasure here is the modulated white: Fantin distinguishes ceramic, cloth and metal by small shifts of tone, and the knife's long shadow adds a striking graphic note. The composition is bold for so small a picture, with its tilted table and cropped corners. It is an early work, a little stiff in places, but it shows the discipline of observation that made his later still lifes famous."},"nga:92996":{"d":"A close-up of a man's head and shoulders shows pale skin, light, slightly disheveled brown hair with a strong widow's peak, and a short brown beard. His head is tilted downward, and he looks out from deep-set eyes beneath a furrowed brow, with his mouth slightly open. He wears a dark suit with a high-collared white shirt visible at the neck. The background is a muted light gray, painted with thick, visible brushstrokes. The canvas is small, about 25 by 21 centimeters.","i":"Fantin-Latour painted this self-portrait at twenty-five, at a time when he was supporting himself by copying old masters in the Louvre and painting small pictures for sale. His self-portraits record a young man's earnest attempt to understand his own face, and the downward-tilted head and furrowed brow give a sense of searching concentration. The light gray ground sets this picture apart from the black ground of his self-portrait of 1858.","c":"Fantin's directness is the picture's virtue: there is no pose or flattery, only a tight, intent face, and the slightly open mouth gives it a startling immediacy. The thick strokes on the gray ground are handled with confidence. It is a more relaxed and individual picture than his 1858 self-portrait, and it shows a painter finding his own voice."},"met:436292":{"d":"This is a small oil on canvas, about 35 by 42 centimeters, dated 1863. The recorded subjects are roses, pears and other fruit, so it is a still life of flowers and fruit of the kind Fantin-Latour painted throughout his career. In his work of these years the objects are typically set in a quiet, close-toned space with soft light and little distracting detail, so that each fruit and bloom is seen for its texture and weight. He was in his late twenties when he painted it, still building his career.","i":"Still lifes of roses and fruit join two traditions: Dutch and French painting of flowers and fruit, and above all Chardin, whom Fantin admired and whose pictures he knew from copying in the Louvre. In the 1860s such pictures found buyers among private collectors, many of them in England, where Fantin's friend Edwin Edwards helped him sell them. Roses and ripe fruit also carry old associations of beauty and transience, though in a mid-nineteenth-century cabinet picture they are mostly valued for their color and texture.","c":"Fantin's early still lifes tend to be quiet, patient and close in tone, and they owe much to Chardin. The qualities to look for are the softness of the transitions, the believable weight of fruit and the way a rose takes light. At twenty-seven he had yet to reach the fuller, looser flower pieces of his maturity, and works of this date can seem reserved beside them. They remain honest, finely felt pictures, valued more for tact than for display."},"met:436293":{"d":"A tall oil on canvas, about 73 by 60 centimeters, dated 1866. The recorded subjects are a vase, flowers and fruit, so it joins a flower arrangement and fruit in one composition. That is a fairly large canvas for a still life, and the upright format suits the rising shape of a bouquet. In his still lifes of these years Fantin-Latour often placed objects before a plain, dark ground, letting soft light shape each bloom and each piece of fruit.","i":"In 1866 Fantin-Latour, then thirty, was painting group portraits such as Homage to Delacroix of 1864 alongside still lifes. The still lifes were a reliable source of income, especially from English buyers. Combining flowers with fruit continues the Dutch tradition of abundance and passing time, since blooms fade and fruit ripens and spoils, though a mid-nineteenth-century collector probably valued the picture first as a handsome, quiet decoration for a room.","c":"At this scale a still life needs firm construction, and Fantin's restrained palette and soft edges suit it. His flowers of the mid-1860s are plainer and more tightly observed than the looser bouquets of his later years, and they can feel careful rather than inspired. The honesty with which he renders the weight of fruit and the fragility of petals is the real attraction; this is a solid, well-made picture from a painter still finding his flower manner."},"nga:46621":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, a polished wooden table or buffet holds a basket of fruit, a vase of pink and white flowers, a book and a small tray with a teacup, saucer and two oranges. One orange is peeled, with a segment set beside the other. A cobalt-blue goblet-shaped vase holds five flowers, possibly carnations, two cream, two ruby red and one streaked with both. A wicker basket holds pears, apples and a quince, and the background is muted sage green. The artist signed it Fantin, 1866.","i":"By the mid-1860s Henri Fantin-Latour was developing the still-life painting that would support him for the rest of his career, and he found his first steady buyers in England. The picture combines the things of an educated household: fruit, flowers, a book and the remains of a small refreshment. It is not an allegory but a quiet celebration of ordinary pleasures arranged with careful balance.","c":"Fantin makes color and texture sing within a restrained composition: the red polish of the wood, the cobalt of the vase and the soft green ground work together without strain. He is a painter of understatement, and this canvas shows his skill at distinguishing the surface of fruit, glass, metal and flowers with only slight shifts of tone. If it is cool in feeling, its beauty is in that discipline."},"nga:46498":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up in profile, facing left. She has a fair complexion, a curved nose, pale eyebrows, pink cheeks and thin pink lips, and her curly dark brown hair is styled behind her head and adorned with a wreath of purple and white flowers. She wears a lilac off-the-shoulder dress with white fringe and a bow, a light shawl and several strands of pearls, and she sits in a dark red chair against a muted brown ground.","i":"The sitter was born Marguerite Löwenhielm and became the Duchess de Fitz-James through marriage into an old French noble family. Fantin-Latour rarely painted society portraits, preferring still lifes and group portraits of artist friends, so this portrait of 1867 is unusual in his career. The profile view and the wreath of flowers may echo Renaissance portrait medals and profile portraits that he knew from his museum study.","c":"The profile pose gives the picture an unusual clarity and elegance, and the flowers in the hair link it to Fantin's own specialty. His handling of the lilac silk, pearls and soft skin is delicate without being sugary. As a society portrait it is restrained and a little reserved, and the sitter's neutral expression keeps it cooler than most, which suits the painter's temperament."},"nga:46499":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a young woman faces the viewer with a slight smile in this vertical portrait. She has a square face, round, dark, close-set eyes, thin lips, flushed cheeks and a pointed chin. Her dark brown hair is parted in the middle and pulled loosely back, with ringlets falling to her shoulders. She wears a rose-pink ribbon choker with a gold oval locket, and the straight neckline of her off-the-shoulder dress is edged in lace. Light falls from the left against a dark brown ground, and the artist signed it Fantin, 67.","i":"Mademoiselle de Fitz-James was painted in the same year as the Duchess de Fitz-James and was probably a younger member of the same family, perhaps her daughter. The locket, ribbon and lace mark the sitter as a young woman of good family at the Second Empire's height. A portrait of an unmarried girl of this kind often recorded her appearance as she came of age and entered society.","c":"The face is simple, direct and a little unglamorous, which is the picture's strength: Fantin does not prettify the sitter but gives her a real presence, with an open, slightly shy expression. The lace and pink silk are handled with delicacy, and the side light models the features gently. It is a restrained, sympathetic portrait, appealing for its honesty rather than its dash."},"met:437997":{"d":"A nearly square oil on canvas, about 57 by 55 centimeters, dated 1868. The recorded subjects are asters, grapes, apples and pears, set out on a table. Asters flower in late summer and autumn, and grapes, apples and pears ripen at the same season, so the picture gathers the blooms and produce of the turning year in one place. A table arrangement of this kind is more informal than a bouquet in a vase, presenting flowers and fruit as a simple domestic abundance.","i":"Fruit and flowers together have a long history in Dutch and French still life, where the harvest stood for abundance and the cut flower for the shortness of life. By 1868 Fantin-Latour, aged thirty-two, was a regular exhibitor at the Salon and was known for such pictures, which he painted with an eye to the quiet, close-toned manner of Chardin. The autumn season adds a gentle note of ripeness and decline that suits the old theme.","c":"The seasonal logic gives the picture a coherence that a mere collection of pretty objects lacks. Fantin's strength is the sober, believable weight of fruit and the delicacy of petals, set down without display. The weakness of such orderly arrangements is a certain politeness; they please without surprising. As a type, it is a thoughtful, well-judged work from the years when he was establishing the still-life manner on which his reputation would rest."},"nga:89679":{"d":"Three large peaches rest on a round white plate in this tiny still life of 1868, painted in oil on paper later mounted on canvas and measuring about 20 by 26 centimeters. The fruit ranges through red, pink and light yellow, and it casts dark shadows on the plate. The plate sits on a brown surface that also fills the background, so the picture is reduced to three forms, a plate and a plain ground.","i":"Peaches were a luxury fruit in nineteenth-century France, grown against sunny walls for the Paris market, and they gave painters a perfect subject for soft skin and graded color. Henri Fantin-Latour painted still life through his career for steady buyers, many of them in England. A picture this small was meant for a private owner's wall and shows the fruit without allegory or display.","c":"The pleasure is in the transitions: the blush from red to yellow, the velvet of the skin, the cool white of the plate that sets it off. The composition is as spare as a Chardin, and the dark ground gives the fruit presence. It is a modest exercise rather than a full statement, but the observation is exact and the restraint admirable."},"met:436294":{"d":"A horizontal oil on canvas, about 47 by 56 centimeters, dated 1874. The recorded subjects are pansies and apples, a modest pairing of small garden flowers with everyday fruit. The picture is therefore an intimate, close-up still life rather than a grand bouquet. In 1874 Fantin-Latour was thirty-eight and an established painter of still lifes, and his handling in these years is somewhat freer and softer than in the sharply modeled pictures of the early 1860s.","i":"The pansy takes its English name from the French pensée, 'thought', and in the language of flowers it stood for remembrance and reflection, a gentle meaning that suits a quiet interior picture. The year 1874 saw the first Impressionist exhibition in Paris; Fantin-Latour was friendly with several of those painters but showed his own work at the Salon, where polished, naturalistic painting was expected.","c":"The pairing of flowers and fruit is modest, and the picture's appeal is intimacy rather than display. Fantin is at his best with small, soft forms like pansy petals, where gradations of tone matter more than outline and a close-toned background lets them glow. It is the sort of quiet success that made his still lifes popular and, in weaker examples, repetitive. As a type, this is an assured, unpretentious picture from his middle years."},"nga:164919":{"d":"A group of potted pansies fills this wide canvas of 1874, about 46 by 71 centimeters. On the left a large basket brims with yellow pansies, and at least four smaller terracotta pots on the right hold purple, white, blue and orange blooms. Each flower has dark markings near its center and is surrounded by wide green leaves. The basket and pots stand on a flat gray surface in front of a lighter gray wall.","i":"The pansy takes its name from the French word pensée, thought, and in the language of flowers it stood for remembrance, so a picture of pansies carried a gentle message. Henri Fantin-Latour began to specialize in flowers in the 1860s and found enthusiastic buyers in England, where garden flowers were prized. Showing them as living potted plants, not as a cut bouquet, gives this picture the look of a garden corner.","c":"Fantin gives a humble garden flower the treatment of a portrait, and the neutral gray ground makes every color vibrate. The grouping is a little crowded and the leaves are heavy, but the inventive range of hues, from yellow to violet, is a delight. It is less formal than his later bouquets, and closer to what one might actually see in a gardener's yard."},"met:438031":{"d":"A horizontal oil on canvas, about 51 by 62 centimeters, dated 1880. The title and the recorded subject, flowers, point to a bouquet of the season's blooms rather than a single kind of flower. Fantin-Latour's bouquets of these years usually fill much of the canvas, set in a vase or bowl against a quiet neutral ground, close to the picture plane, so that the flowers themselves, not their surroundings, carry the picture.","i":"By 1880 Fantin-Latour, in his mid-forties, was known above all as a painter of flowers. He and his wife Victoria Dubourg, also a flower painter, spent summers at her family's country house in Normandy, where garden flowers were close at hand. Pictures like this were sold widely in England, where flower painting by a French artist found a ready market. Summer bouquets promise abundance but also carry the old thought that cut flowers are brief.","c":"In flower pieces of this period Fantin's touch is lighter and more fluid than in his early still lifes, and the best have a convincing airiness among the blossoms. The risk lies in repetition: he painted so many bouquets that the formula can seem comfortable. This kind of picture shows why collectors liked him, with a naturalness that avoids both showiness and sentimentality. Seen as a type, it is a dependable example from his central years."},"nga:136017":{"d":"A blue and white bowl overflowing with grapes, two loose bunches and a vivid pink carnation lie on a white tablecloth against a dark brown background. The grapes range from bright, translucent green through green with pink tones to dark purple. The bowl sits left of center and takes up most of the left two-thirds of the composition, while the carnation lies in front of the loose bunches. The canvas, about 30 by 47 centimeters, is signed Fantin at the top right.","i":"Grapes have long stood for abundance in still life, and in religious painting for wine and sacrifice, but Henri Fantin-Latour's picture is a secular one: it celebrates the pleasure of ripe fruit and clean linen. He painted such arrangements for decades for collectors, especially in England. The single carnation, a flower that suggests love and fleeting beauty in older art, adds a bright accent.","c":"The strength lies in the grapes: translucent skins, bloom and shadow are handled with a patience that makes each berry convincing. The blue and white bowl and the white cloth keep the palette controlled, and the carnation breaks it with one vivid note. The arrangement is conventional, but the quiet authority of the painting makes it more than a decoration."},"nga:131027":{"d":"Seven roses in full bloom are clustered in a wide bouquet against a dark brown background in this horizontal still life of 1882, about 26 by 42 centimeters. The roses are peach, lavender pink, wine red, blush pink and white, with two butter-yellow blooms along the top, and spring-green leaves are interspersed throughout. A single maroon petal lies on the table at lower left. The brushwork is visible, especially in the dark background, and the signature is in red at the top right.","i":"Roses were Henri Fantin-Latour's favorite subject in his later years, and he painted them in great numbers, often in summers at his wife's family property in Normandy. The title suggests roses from Nice, probably cut flowers sent north from the Riviera. The fallen petal is a quiet reminder, familiar from older flower painting, that bloom is brief.","c":"Fantin is a master of the rose: each flower has its own character of color and opening, and the thin glazes of the petals make them seem soft to the touch. The dark ground sets off the cluster and suggests a hushed interior. The single petal is a subtle touch of narrative. If the formula is repeated often in his late work, this version has particular freshness."},"met:437995":{"d":"A small horizontal oil on canvas, about 30 by 42 centimeters, dated 1883. Roses are its only recorded subject, and the title puts them in a bowl rather than a tall vase, which suggests a low, loose grouping of blooms. Roses became the flower most associated with Fantin-Latour, and a picture of this modest size was typically a close, intimate study of petals and leaves, set against a soft neutral ground.","i":"By the 1880s roses had become the flower most associated with Fantin-Latour, and collectors, especially in England, asked for them. Roses open, bloom and drop their petals within days, which suited a painter who worked directly from living flowers, and they carry long associations with love and with transience. For a French painter who had trained by copying in the Louvre, the subject also tied him to earlier flower painters, from the Dutch masters to Chardin.","c":"A small picture of this kind stands or falls on touch, and the draw of Fantin's best rose pieces is the lightly loaded brush and the delicate shifts of tone that let petals seem to hold light without turning sugary. He produced them in large numbers, however, and they can be hard to tell apart. Judged as a type, this is a quietly accomplished small work, more a pleasure to look at than a statement."},"met:629928":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 28 by 34 centimeters, dated 1883, showing pansies in a pot. A living plant in a pot, rather than cut flowers in a vase, is a humbler subject, closer to a window sill or garden bench than to a drawing-room table. The recorded subjects are flowers and the still-life genre, and the title adds the pot. The modest size suggests an intimate picture meant for close viewing in a home.","i":"Pansies were among the most familiar garden and window flowers of the nineteenth century, and their English name comes from the French pensée, 'thought'. By 1883 Fantin-Latour had painted flowers for some two decades and returned to pansies repeatedly. A potted plant also carries a different meaning from cut flowers: it is alive and growing, not yet marked for decay, so the picture is less a reminder of transience than a quiet celebration of ordinary cultivation.","c":"Without the ceremony of a bouquet, the picture depends on observation alone, and Fantin's gift for soft, close-toned modeling is well suited to velvety petals and cool leaves. Its small size keeps it focused, but it is also slight, a charming record rather than a statement. As a type, such small flower pictures are where his reputation for tact and truthfulness is most easily earned."},"met:436295":{"d":"A vertical oil on canvas, about 100 by 81 centimeters, dated 1885, showing a woman in a formal portrait. The recorded subjects name only a woman and the genre of portrait, so the sitter is identified only in general terms. The canvas is of the size customary for a standard portrait, large enough to hold a half- or three-quarter-length figure, and a portrait of this kind was generally made either as a commission or as a gift or exchange within the painter's circle.","i":"Fantin-Latour made his name with portraits as well as flowers: the group portraits Homage to Delacroix of 1864 and A Studio at Les Batignolles of 1870 show friends and fellow artists, and he painted individual portraits of family and acquaintances. A portrait of a woman in 1885 belongs to that private, unshowy side of his work, which favored a sober tonal range and a quiet likeness over fashionable display, in contrast to the glossy Salon portraits of the day.","c":"Fantin's portraits are less known than his flower pieces and are often more austere, built on a restricted palette and a steady, searching attention to the face. That seriousness is their strength; the limitation is that a sober manner can leave the sitter distant and the surroundings undeveloped. As a type, this is a dignified work in which honesty matters more than charm, a reminder that the flower painter was also a portraitist of real gravity."},"met:437996":{"d":"An upright oil on canvas, about 60 by 46 centimeters, dated 1888. The recorded subjects are roses and lilies, a vase with water, and a table, so a vase of cut flowers stands on a tabletop. Roses and lilies are old companions in flower painting: the rose is soft and layered, the lily open and trumpet-shaped. Fantin-Latour, in his fifties by 1888, generally painted such pairings with a lighter touch than in his early work.","i":"Roses and lilies are traditional emblems of love and purity, and together they appeared in countless Christian and secular images long before the nineteenth century. In a picture of 1888 these meanings probably lie in the background; what the buyer wanted was a beautiful bouquet by one of the best-known flower painters of the day. Fantin's reputation by then extended well beyond France, and his flowers were sought in England.","c":"Lilies are hard to paint because their white forms can go flat or chalky, and roses are hard because they can turn sugary; a painter who handles both convincingly, as Fantin generally does, knows how to control tone. His later flower pieces benefit from a freer brush, but they also grow more formulaic. A vertical bouquet of this kind shows the strengths and limits of that late manner: expert, graceful, slightly predictable."},"nga:46620":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a young woman sits and looks at the viewer in this vertical portrait of 1890, about 109 by 81 centimeters. Her body is angled to the right, but she turns her face with large brown eyes. Brown bangs fall across her forehead, and a wide-brimmed hat with a cream and dark brown feather sits back on her head. A dark fur stole drapes around her neck, and she wears an ivory gown with a burgundy sash and two gold rings. The flat, camel-brown ground is inscribed, in French, to his dear niece Sonia.","i":"The sitter, Sonia Yanovski, was the painter's niece, as the dedication states, and it makes the portrait a family gift rather than a commission. Henri Fantin-Latour painted few formal portraits in his later years, preferring flowers and fruit, so a figure of this size is unusual. The stole, hat and gown follow the fashion of 1890, and the pose is a relaxed, intimate version of the grand portrait.","c":"A tender, unfussy portrait in which affection replaces display: the sitter's direct gaze and the soft tones of the ivory gown against the camel ground are the main pleasures. The fur and feather are painted with a flower painter's feeling for texture. It is not a searching psychological study, and Fantin's ease with flowers is plain by comparison, but its quiet warmth is persuasive."},"nga:231680":{"d":"A white plate on the left center is filled with grapes spilling over its edge, and peaches in different shades lie scattered around it, in this still life of 1896, about 29 by 42 centimeters. The palette is warm browns, muted greens and deep reds, and the fruit has a natural sheen. The background and the table are dark brown, which makes the colors of the fruit stand out.","i":"By 1896 Henri Fantin-Latour was in his sixties and had perfected a kind of still life that sold steadily to collectors, particularly in England. His fruit pictures descend from the tradition of Chardin and the Dutch, in which an ordinary dish of fruit is held in a calm, stable arrangement. There is no hidden narrative here: the subject is ripeness, color and the pleasures of looking.","c":"The fruit gleams against the dark ground, and the spilled grapes give a relaxed rhythm to a compact composition. This is a mature, assured late work, though some will find the formula familiar and the palette a little brown. What sustains it is the delicacy of the glazing and a sense of patient, unforced observation."},"met:435894":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 136 by 98 centimeters, painted around 1700. It shows the biblical Bathsheba bathing, and the recorded subjects are Bathsheba, a woman and bathing. Baroque pictures of this story usually present her as a nude or half-draped woman beside water, often with a servant and a distant view of the king's palace roof. At this size, the picture belongs to the type of canvas made for a collector's gallery or a reception room rather than for a church.","i":"The story is told in the Second Book of Samuel: King David, walking on his palace roof, sees Bathsheba bathing, desires her, and arranges for her husband Uriah to be killed in battle. The prophet Nathan later rebukes him, and the couple's later son is Solomon. Painters welcomed the subject because it justified a female nude within Scripture, and viewers could read it as a warning about desire and abuse of power, even if the picture itself dwelt on beauty.","c":"Chiari trained with Carlo Maratta, the leading painter in Rome, and his pictures usually keep Maratta's clear, balanced composition, smooth modeling and sweet, idealized types. Those qualities suit a graceful female nude, but they can also leave a picture polished and cool, lacking the dramatic energy of the earlier Baroque. Among late Roman painters he is a competent and refined craftsman, and a Bathsheba is the kind of subject where his taste for elegance has room to work."},"met:435727":{"d":"A tall, narrow oil on canvas, about 98 by 57 centimeters, whose date is not recorded. The recorded subjects include two women saints, Paula of Rome and her daughter Eustochium, a man, a boat and an angel, and the title names their departure for the Holy Land. The narrow format and modest size suggest a design or sketch for a larger work, or a picture for a small altar, but the original purpose is uncertain.","i":"Paula, a wealthy Roman widow, and her daughter Eustochium left Rome by ship about 385 to follow the scholar Saint Jerome and live as ascetics in the Holy Land. They settled at Bethlehem, where Paula founded monasteries and Eustochium later led the community. Jerome's letter on Paula's death describes her leaving her family on the shore with dry eyes, putting devotion before kinship, which helps explain why the departure became an image of renunciation. An angel in such a scene probably signals divine approval.","c":"Bottani, born in Cremona and active in Mantua, favored a calm, classicizing manner, and his compositions tend toward clear groups and gentle emotion. A seaborne departure invites more drama than he usually allows, and the likely result is orderly rather than stirring. Within eighteenth-century Italian religious painting it is a decorous, cultivated piece, valued more for its poise than its passion."},"nga:288":{"d":"A small oil painting on panel, about 43 by 34 centimeters, dated about 1505 and titled as an allegory of chastity. The title shows that the picture personifies a virtue, so its figures and objects work as symbols rather than as a narrative scene. Panels of this size and kind were made for private rooms and sometimes as covers or companions to portraits. It comes from the early years of Lorenzo Lotto's career, when he was working in the Veneto and was still in his twenties.","i":"Chastity was one of the most admired virtues in Renaissance moral and marriage imagery, and it was often shown through emblems such as the ermine, the unicorn or laurel, or through the Triumph of Chastity in Petrarch's poem. The exact emblems in this panel are not specified by its title. Educated patrons in the Veneto around 1500 enjoyed such puzzles, which turned a moral lesson into a riddle of images, and a small private panel was the proper place for them.","c":"Lotto's early allegories are small, odd and richly colored, and they show his habit of mixing Venetian color with northern-looking detail and a nervous, personal imagination. That makes even a modest panel like this a revealing document of his beginnings. Allegory was not a field in which he was consistent, however, and his reputation rests on his portraits and altarpieces rather than on such enigmatic pieces. As a type, it is interesting more for its strangeness than for finish."},"nga:297":{"d":"A small upright oil panel, about 56 by 42 centimeters, dated 1505, with a title that sets virtue against vice. An allegory of this kind presents a moral choice through personified figures and symbolic objects in a landscape, rather than a story from scripture or history. The panel dates from the years when the young Lotto was working in the Veneto. Allegorical pictures of this size were made for private owners and were sometimes intended as covers that slid over portraits, offering the viewer a lesson while the likeness remained hidden.","i":"The theme of a choice between virtue and vice goes back to the ancient story of Hercules at the crossroads, retold by Xenophon and loved by humanist writers, in which the young hero must choose between a path of toil that leads to glory and one of pleasure. Renaissance artists adapted it often. In Lotto's day such pictures offered an educated viewer a flattering lesson in self-command and an enjoyable puzzle in reading emblems.","c":"Lotto's panel is an early work of real inventiveness: allegory gave the young painter license for unusual images, and his early manner already shows a taste for strong color and psychological oddity. The risk with such panels is that the program can overwhelm the painting, leaving the viewer decoding rather than looking. It is a lively, personal piece for a painter in his mid-twenties, and a better guide to his imagination than to his later settled style."},"nga:258":{"d":"Shown from the waist up and nearly filling the picture, a young woman in a jewel-toned gown and cape looks out at the viewer, her head tilted and her shoulders angled. A slender gold crown looped with pearls and red and dark green gems rests on her upswept auburn hair, with a thin halo above. She wears a flame-red gown with full sleeves, sheer white fabric at the neckline and an emerald-green cloak that drapes over a spiked wooden wheel, on which she rests her hands. A gold ring is on one finger and a yellow palm frond in the other hand. Behind her hangs burgundy patterned cloth.","i":"The wheel, crown and palm identify Catherine of Alexandria, the learned princess who, according to legend, was condemned to a spiked wheel and then beheaded. The ring recalls her mystic marriage to Christ, in which the Christ Child placed a ring on her finger in a vision. Lotto, painting in Bergamo in 1522, gave such half-length saints an unusual closeness. Half-length saints of this period are sometimes thought to be portraits of women in the saint's guise, which the picture alone cannot prove.","c":"This is Lotto at his most engaging. The tilt of the head, the sidelong glance and the nearness of the figure create an intimacy unusual in saints' images, and the saturated reds and greens, flame, emerald and burgundy, are daring yet controlled. The costume is almost too rich, with pearls, sheer linen and sleeves competing for attention, but the face holds the picture. Among his half-length devotional images it shows how he gave sacred types a shy, human psychology."},"nga:431":{"d":"Outside a ramshackle barn with a thatched roof, Joseph and Mary kneel on either side of the newborn Christ, who lies on a white cloth in a woven basket on the straw and lifts his arms toward his mother. At the left Joseph, balding and grey-bearded, in a coral-red robe and mustard cloak, holds his hands together in prayer with a walking stick across his lap. At the right Mary, in an azure cloak over a rose dress and a white veil, crosses her arms on her chest and bows her head. Thin gold rings hover over their heads. Three small winged children at the top gather around a sheet of music. A crucifix hangs on the dark wall at the left, and beyond lie hills, a shepherd with sheep and blue mountains.","i":"Lorenzo Lotto, born in Venice, worked in Bergamo from 1513 to 1525, the most productive period of his career, and this small panel dates from 1523. He signs it as if the name were carved on a plank near the lower right. Angels with a sheet of music, a crucifix that points to the Passion, a basket for a cradle and a ladder against the barn bring a familiar scene into a homely setting. He worked outside the main Venetian circle, was thought eccentric, and was largely forgotten until Bernard Berenson's writing revived his reputation in the late nineteenth century. The picture came to the National Gallery of Art in the Kress collection.","c":"A warm, direct picture. Lotto packs the foreground so that the figures lean toward the Child, and his bright, slightly acid colours, coral, mustard and azure, set it apart from the smoother harmonies of Venice. The faces are individual rather than ideal, Joseph's above all. The angels at the top are a touch busy, and the crucifix sits awkwardly in the shadow. Even so, this small panel has a personality that larger, more correct nativities lack, and it shows why Lotto's reputation rose again in the twentieth century."},"met:436918":{"d":"A nude Venus reclines on a blue cloth spread over a grassy bank, resting on one elbow and looking directly at the viewer with a faint smile. She wears an ornate gold-and-pearl headdress with a veil, drop earrings, and bracelets, and lifts one hand to dangle a wreath of myrtle by a blue ribbon, with a small censer hanging beneath it. Her other hand rests on her chest. Rose petals are scattered over her body, and a stick and a small snake lie in front of her. At the left a plump, winged Cupid with a wreath in his curly hair stands facing her, and passes water through the hanging wreath. Behind them a crimson cloth hangs from a tree with ivy. The skin is pale and polished, the colors bright.","i":"This was almost certainly made for a wedding. Everything in it speaks of marriage and fertility: the myrtle wreath, the incense burner, the rose petals, the ivy for fidelity, and Cupid's stream through the wreath, a bawdy fertility charm that Renaissance viewers would have found funny. Venus's headdress is of a type worn by Venetian brides. Lotto, a Venetian who worked in Bergamo in the 1520s, took the reclining Venus of Giorgione and Titian and made it direct and personal. The picture is not recorded before the early twentieth century. The Met bought it in 1986 and removed a later overpainting.","c":"Lotto's Venus is a refreshing change from the idealized goddesses of his Venetian contemporaries: she looks straight at the viewer, smiles, and wears her jewelry, and the humor of Cupid gives the picture a warmth that Titian's nudes rarely have. The flesh has a porcelain polish that can feel hard, and the odd details, the snake, the stick, and the censer, are crammed in. But the whole is charming and individual, with a candor about the body and about marriage that sets it apart."},"cma:127571":{"d":"A man in a fur-trimmed black robe stands at a balcony, turning sharply to look out toward the left while one arm swings up and opens toward the right. A black cap sits on his dark hair, and his beard is trimmed close. A gold chain hangs across his dark, patterned doublet, a sword shows at the lower right, and a full brown sleeve billows at the raised shoulder. His other hand rests on a piece of paper on a blue-draped table, where a few sprigs of leaves and white flowers lie. Behind him a white stone balustrade and a trellis of roses frame a landscape of blue hills under a bright, cloud-streaked sky. The oil on canvas is built on cool blues and blacks, lit by warm brown.","i":"Cleveland's text calls the gesture a mystery: is he rising to speak to someone, or pointing at something outside the frame? The clover on the table stands for abundance, good fortune and happy marriage, and jasmine for purity and love, with the roses on the trellis repeating the message, so the picture may have had a companion portrait of a bride. A recent suggestion identifies him as Girolamo Rosati, a high official in Fermo, and reads the paper as a design for one of his building projects, perhaps the one he points toward. The interrupted moment is typical of Lorenzo Lotto and unlike the formality of other Italian Renaissance portraits in the museum.","c":"Lotto's sitters are rarely at ease, and this one is almost restless. The off-balance pose, the sudden hand and the sidelong glance create more life than a standard pose would, and the flowers add a quiet note of private feeling. Beside Titian's self-possessed sitters, Lotto's seem sensitive and a little anxious, and that is their appeal. The weaknesses are small: the symbols do not quite resolve into a story, and the balustrade and distant hills are a rather conventional backdrop. The man himself keeps the picture alive."},"met:436917":{"d":"A friar fills the canvas, three-quarter length, glaring directly out. He wears a brown hooded scapular and mantle over a white tunic that shows only at the cuffs, and has a shaved crown, a short dark beard and intent, deep-set eyes. One hand holds a small open green book; the other is clenched into a fist pressed against his chest. At upper left, in a small bright landscape, Christ hangs on the cross with Mary and Saint John and a kneeling figure at its foot. At right are dark trees and a stormy blue sky, and a stone at lower right carries an inscription.","i":"The inscription names him as Fra Gregorio Belo of Vicenza, a hermit of the Hieronymite order of Blessed Pietro of Pisa, in his fifty-fifth year. Beating the breast is a gesture of penance, and the Metropolitan Museum notes that he imitates Saint Jerome, the saint who inspired his order. The little Crucifixion is not a real scene behind him but a projection of the friar's meditation. Lotto, who worked in Venice and the Marches, was drawn to the psychologically searching portraiture of Albrecht Dürer, and he uses the same approach here to show a mind at work as well as a face.","c":"This is a portrait that doubles as a sermon. The bulk of the figure, the glowering eyes and the clenched fist give the friar real force, while the tiny Crucifixion, set well off to one side like a thought, avoids sentimentality. The handling is the weak point: brown habit against brown ground, with little modeling, leaves most of the canvas a dark mass relieved only by the face, book and cuffs. Beside Titian's smooth sitters, Lotto's are more uneasy and personal, and this is one of his most striking late portraits."},"met:437983":{"d":"A small landscape painted in oil on a copper plate, probably in the 1630s. The recorded subjects are ruins, a man and the landscape, and the themes name Rome, so it is a view of the Roman countryside with ancient remains and at least one figure for scale. Copper gives a smooth, hard surface that takes fine detail and a glossy, enamel-like finish, and it was favored for small cabinet pictures that collectors could examine closely.","i":"Ancient ruins in the Roman countryside were a favorite subject for northern painters who traveled to Italy, and for the collectors who bought their work: they stood for the grandeur of the vanished empire and for the passing of time, and they gave landscape a place in the serious study of antiquity. Goffredo Wals, a German-born painter working in Italy, followed the tradition of Adam Elsheimer, whose small copper landscapes set the pattern, and he is said to have taught the young Claude Lorrain.","c":"Wals's small copper landscapes combine fine, almost miniature detail with soft, hazy distances, and the type is a pleasing one: neat, atmospheric, easy to enjoy. It is also slightly formulaic, with a repertory of ruins, trees and tiny figures that shows little drama. His historical importance is as a link between the Elsheimer tradition and Claude Lorrain, whose later landscapes are far grander; as a work in itself, it is a charming minor example."},"met:437946":{"d":"A small horizontal oil on canvas, about 48 by 59 centimeters, dated 1742, in which putti and sky carry an allegory of the arts. The recorded subjects are an allegory, putti, sky and art, so the scene is an airy, heavenly one in which the arts are personified by children, in the way de Wit often did. At this size it is probably a preparatory sketch for a ceiling or wall decoration, the kind of commission for Amsterdam houses and public buildings on which his career rested, although it may also be a finished cabinet piece.","i":"Allegories of the arts, often with putti holding the tools of painting, sculpture, architecture and music, were a favorite decoration for patrons who wanted to show their culture; in Amsterdam of the 1740s wealthy merchants and regents hired de Wit to paint ceilings, overdoors and chimney pieces for their canal houses. The arts shown as children are playful rather than solemn, a Rococo taste for lightness over gravity. Their message is that painting and its sister arts are fit company for wealth and learning.","c":"De Wit's strength is fluency: he sets cloud and putti swirling with an ease learned from Rubens and the Antwerp tradition, and his oil sketches have a freshness that finished ceilings sometimes lose. The weakness is repetition, since the same cheerful putti recur in many of his works, and a decorative aim keeps him from deeper feeling. This is a pleasing, light-hearted example, showing a Dutch artist working in a late Baroque and Rococo vein."},"met:437947":{"d":"A horizontal oil on canvas, about 53 by 63 centimeters, dated 1743. The title names Flora, goddess of flowers, and Zephyr, the west wind, and the recorded subjects are a woman and putti, which fits a mythological idyll of spring. Such a picture is a decorative cabinet or overdoor piece rather than a narrative of drama, and de Wit painted many works of this airy, graceful kind for the houses of wealthy Amsterdam patrons.","i":"Ovid, in the Fasti, tells how Zephyrus, the west wind, seized the nymph Chloris, made her his bride and turned her into Flora, who rules over flowers and spring. The story gave painters a subject of love, renewal and abundance, with no tragic edge. In the Dutch Republic of the 1740s such mythologies decorated the rooms of the wealthy, and the soft pleasures of Flora and her gentle lover suited the Rococo taste for charm.","c":"De Wit's light touch and sweet, rounded types suit a theme of blossoms and breezes, and a picture of this kind succeeds by pleasantness, not by depth. The danger is a certain sugariness, and the figures can look like stock decorative types. Within his work it is an attractive example of the lighter, more Rococo side of a painter better known for grisaille ceilings and heavier allegories."},"met:437948":{"d":"An upright oil on canvas, about 51 by 39 centimeters, whose date is not recorded. The title names Wisdom defeating Discord, and the recorded subjects are an allegory, putti, clouds and sky, so the struggle is set among clouds, as in designs for ceilings. At this scale it is probably an oil sketch for a larger decoration, the kind of design a patron approved before the final work was painted, though it may also have been kept as a finished piece.","i":"Allegories of good government set Wisdom, often in the guise of Minerva, against Discord, the cause of civil strife, to show that sound rule keeps order and peace. Such images suited council chambers and the houses of magistrates; in the Dutch Republic, whose cities were governed by councils of regents, they flattered the rulers and reminded them of their duty. Discord is typically shown as a fury with snakes or torches, and the victory of wisdom as her defeat.","c":"Oil sketches by de Wit often have more energy than his finished ceilings, because the rapid brushwork and quick groupings of figures remain visible. The allegory is stock, with no surprising invention, and the putti carry the drama in a light, decorative way. As a type, it is a lively sketch by a master of fluent, light-toned ceiling painting, interesting for showing the planning of a larger scheme."},"met:437243":{"d":"A saint shown as a man holding a book, in tempera on a wood panel with a gold ground, possibly dating from about 1320 to 1330. The recorded subjects are Romuald, a book, a saint and a man. A single gold-ground saint of this kind was usually one panel from a larger altarpiece, set beside or above a central image, and the gold ground stood for the light of heaven. Saint Romuald is normally shown as an elderly, bearded monk in a white habit, and his book stands for the rule of his order.","i":"Saint Romuald, who lived from about 951 to 1027, founded the Camaldolese order of hermit monks in the Apennines. Camaldolese houses were widespread in central Italy, which might explain a panel of him in a region such as Umbria or the Marches. The painter is called Pseudo-Palmeruccio, a convenient name for an unidentified artist whose style is associated with Guiduccio Palmerucci of Gubbio. Altarpieces of this kind put the founder of an order before the community that revered him.","c":"Gold-ground saints of this type depend on a firm outline, a calm frontal pose and a few well-chosen colors, and the best of them have a quiet gravity. Painters of the Gubbio school, working in the shadow of Siena and Giotto, tended to simplify those models, so the figures can look stiff and repetitive. The notname and the tentative date mark it as a minor work whose interest is chiefly historical."},"met:11383":{"d":"A small upright self-portrait in oil on a wood panel, about 41 by 29 centimeters, painted in 1859 when John La Farge was about twenty-four. The recorded subjects name the sitter as La Farge himself, so the painter looks out of the picture as both subject and author. Self-portraits of this size were usually made for the artist's own use, to practice his skills, test an effect or give to friends, not for sale, and this one comes from the early years of his training as a painter.","i":"A self-portrait by a young artist is both an exercise in seeing and a statement of identity. La Farge, born in New York to a cultured family of French descent, had studied law before turning to art, and in a self-portrait of 1859 he presents himself as a painter. The tradition of the painter's self-image goes back to Rembrandt and the Venetian masters, whose use of shadow and warm tone American painters of La Farge's generation studied.","c":"As an early work it shows the sober, tonal manner of a young painter learning from the old masters, and its interest is above all biographical. The limits of such a panel are those of apprenticeship: a careful likeness, probably, rather than a bold characterization. La Farge's real distinction came later, in stained glass, mural decoration and watercolors, but this panel shows the young man who would become one of the most inventive American decorative artists of his time."},"nga:166460":{"d":"A sunlit bowl of pink flowers stands on a white curtain draped over the sill of an open window, in this upright still life. The sill rises about a third of the way up, and the open window and landscape beyond fill the upper two-thirds. Shell-pink and ruby-red blooms with pine-green leaves fill a shallow, slate-gray bowl, and sun picks out a white flower and a blush-pink one, perhaps roses. The curtain pools on the sill, dappled with light. Through the window a tawny path winds across a pale lawn around an ivory house, under a silvery blue sky turning mauve-pink near the trees.","i":"Flowers set before an open window join two kinds of picture, the still life and the view, and invite the eye to move from near, tangible petals to a far, atmospheric landscape. La Farge painted this about 1861, early in his career, when young American artists were discovering the fine observation of light and nature promoted by John Ruskin and by the English Pre-Raphaelites. The curtain and sill also give the picture a domestic, private feeling.","c":"The great pleasure is the handling of light: sun dappling the curtain and sill, white set against ruby and shell pink, and a cool distance seen through the window. The composition is bold, with the window taking two-thirds of the canvas. The risk of such a division is that the picture splits in two, but La Farge ties the halves together with light. It shows why he counts as a colorist and a poet of light."},"met:11370":{"d":"A vertical oil on canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters, dated 1868. The title gives the season and the place: autumn, and a view over Hanging Rock near Newport, Rhode Island. The word study signals work made from direct observation of nature rather than a composition built up in the studio, though a canvas of this size is larger than a pocket sketch. The recorded subject is simply landscape. La Farge was living at Newport in these years and found much of his material in its rocky, open countryside.","i":"American painters of the 1860s made autumn foliage a national subject, and Jasper Cropsey's Autumn on the Hudson River of 1860 showed European audiences a blaze of color they could hardly believe. A study of the season, made from nature, trained the eye to handle shifting light and color, and La Farge's attention to exactly these qualities may help explain his later success in stained glass, where color and light were everything.","c":"A study is judged on freshness rather than finish, and La Farge's interest was always more in the relation of colors and the passage of light than in topography. At their best such autumn studies catch the warmth and variety of foliage in quick, confident touches; their usual weakness is a lack of formal resolution. This is likely to be a candid, painterly piece, valued as evidence of how he trained his eye, not as a finished statement."},"nga:117958":{"d":"Seen from a rocky ledge, a shadowed valley runs away between two humped mountains in this wide landscape. In the foreground golden-yellow and sage-green grasses grow on ash-gray stone, and the conical tops of a few evergreens rise beyond the ledge. The left-hand mountain curves down and away, its long near face a sheer cinnamon-brown rock, while the mountain on the right lies wholly in shadow, its bristly ridge suggesting trees. The ridge ends in an emerald meadow beside a band of pale blue water that meets an ice-blue sky about two-thirds of the way up.","i":"Paradise Rocks is a rocky ridge near Newport, Rhode Island, where La Farge lived and painted in the 1860s, and the title seems to mark this as the last valley before the sea. Most American landscape painters of the day preferred grand scenery or calm, glowing coasts; La Farge chose a plain local ridge and studied its rock and light, probably in the spirit of John Ruskin's call for truthful observation. With no figures, the land itself is the subject.","c":"The composition is daring: a foreground ledge, an abrupt drop into shadow, then two mountains converging on a small bright strip of water. Big simplified masses and cool, unforced color give the scene a hush that is more personal than the grand American landscapes of its day. The price is a lack of incident and a heavy middle ground, where shadow swallows detail. It is an unusually original American landscape of its decade."},"met:11379":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 126 by 97 centimeters, dated 1870, showing an allegory of painting. The recorded subjects are a woman, a man, a painting, a tree and leaves, so the personified art appears with an artist and an artwork in a leafy setting. The scale is that of an ambitious exhibition picture rather than a study, and the subject belongs to La Farge's ideal pictures, in which he tried to give poetic form to the art he practiced.","i":"The ancient Greeks counted nine Muses, but none presided over painting, so a Muse of Painting is a later invention that gave the art a patron of its own. Artists of the nineteenth century favored such figures to claim for painting the same intellectual standing as poetry and music. La Farge, who read widely and moved among writers, was drawn to this literary side of art, and in 1870 he was developing an idealist vein alongside his landscape work.","c":"As allegory it is an ambitious attempt rather than a clear success. La Farge's gifts were for color, light and a poetic vagueness of mood, and a symbolic apparatus of woman, man and picture may sit stiffly beside them. The subject itself is unusual in American painting of 1870 and shows a painter thinking about the dignity of his art. Such ideal figure pictures are less assured than his landscapes and flower pieces, but they reveal his ambitions."},"met:10028":{"d":"A very tall, narrow canvas, about 138 by 30 centimeters, dated about 1883. The recorded subjects are leaves and fruit, and the title names an orange branch, so a single branch with leaves and fruit is presumably displayed along the length of the canvas. The format is that of a decorative panel, meant to hang in a narrow space such as beside a door, in a hall or on a screen, not that of an easel picture. The record describes the work as formerly attributed to John La Farge, so its author is not firmly established.","i":"Tall, narrow panels of branches and flowers were a fashion of the 1880s Aesthetic Movement in Britain and the United States, influenced by Japanese hanging scrolls that had become familiar since the 1850s. They decorated the interiors of progressive households, where art was meant to be part of a room's design. La Farge, an early American enthusiast for Japanese art who traveled to Japan in 1886, worked in this decorative vein, which may explain the earlier attribution.","c":"As a decorative panel it succeeds by its proportions: the extreme height turns a simple branch into a graceful vertical rhythm, an idea borrowed from Japanese art. Whether or not La Farge painted it, it is a good example of Aesthetic taste, elegant and economical. The risk of such formats is that the subject is reduced to pattern, losing the individuality of observed fruit and leaves, so it should be valued for design rather than as a signature statement."},"nga:50673":{"d":"A wide body of water lies among greenery, with tall green and purple mountains behind and a sky of dark pink clouds above a strip of bright blue-green. To the right of center a fisherman stands thigh-deep in the water, facing away, an orange cloth wrapped at his waist and a long thin spear in his hand as he looks down. On the far shore another, indistinct figure watches from the grass. A line of trees runs along the foot of the mountains, and the water reflects their green and purple and the pink of the sky. The canvas is large, about 136 by 152 centimeters.","i":"The Tautira River is on the Taiarapu peninsula of Tahiti. La Farge visited the South Seas in 1890 and 1891 with his friend the historian Henry Adams and made sketches that he drew on for years afterward, so this oil of about 1895 was probably painted in his New York studio from memory and sketches. It belongs to a long Western fascination with the Pacific as an unspoiled paradise, an image that passes over the French colonial rule and epidemics that were changing island life.","c":"The color is the draw: dark pink cloud, purple mountains and green water, reflected and echoed, give the scene a glowing, dreamlike unity, and the large size lets it breathe. The fisherman, small, faceless and turned away, is more a note of scale than a person, which is typical of picturesque travel painting and limits the human interest. La Farge's South Seas watercolors are often thought fresher than his larger oils; this canvas is richer but less spontaneous."},"met:436851":{"d":"A tall, slender woman in a long white satin gown and cloak turns on a grassy path and looks back toward the viewer with a pleased half-smile. The cloak is trimmed at the collar and down the front with pale fur, and one hand draws it close beneath her chin. A very large fur muff tied with a blue ribbon hangs at her side, and an orange glove dangles from her hand. Her hair is powdered and curled beneath a small white cap. Behind her, a park of green trees opens to a distant strip of sea, and a blue sky is piled with windblown clouds. The satin is painted in long, bright strokes.","i":"Elizabeth Farren (about 1759–1829) was one of the leading comic actresses of the London stage, admired for playing elegant ladies. Lawrence, then about twenty-one, painted her in 1790, and the portrait helped establish his reputation. Farren left the stage to marry the Earl of Derby in 1797, becoming Countess of Derby. The fur-trimmed outfit, the windblown landscape and the glance over the shoulder combine high fashion with the staged spontaneity of the theatre. In 1792, on the death of Joshua Reynolds, Lawrence was appointed Painter in Ordinary to George III.","c":"A bravura performance by a very young painter. The satin and fur are painted in long, loaded strokes, and the sitter's turn and sidelong smile give a theatrical vivacity that catches her profession exactly. The setting is thinner: the landscape is a backdrop, the figure seems slightly pasted against it, and the face, charming but generic, is the least solid part of the picture. Next to Reynolds or Gainsborough it lacks psychological depth, yet its dash anticipates the Regency taste for glamour that Lawrence would go on to dominate."},"nga:1176":{"d":"A portrait of a married woman, Mrs. Robert Blencowe, on a vertical canvas about 127 by 101 centimeters, dated about 1792. The size is the standard three-quarter format of British portrait painters, 50 by 40 inches, large enough for a figure shown to about the knees. The sitter is identified only as the wife of Robert Blencowe, so this is a likeness of a private person rather than a public figure, probably commissioned by her husband or family to hang at home.","i":"When this was painted, about 1792, Lawrence was in his early twenties, and in the same years he succeeded Joshua Reynolds as the King's painter, beginning a career that made him the leading portraitist of his age. Fashionable women of the gentry and aristocracy sought him for his fluent flattery and the liveliness he gave the face. A portrait of a wife marked her place in a family and its standing in society, and would have hung among the other family pictures of the house.","c":"Lawrence's portraits of the early 1790s are admired for their fluency and for the animation of the face, which makes sitters seem to have just turned to speak. They carry a debt to Reynolds, with swift brushwork and glowing flesh, and the limits of the phase are flattery that smooths away character and settings that stay conventional. It is a representative early work by a painter who matured into a more theatrical manner."},"nga:103":{"d":"Two figures in white sit before a smooth, sunlit boulder, with a landscape opening to the right in this tall portrait. A woman sits with her ankles crossed, her round face tipped to the viewer's right and her closed lips in a slight smile. She wears an eggshell-white gown gathered under the bust, with puffed short sleeves over sheer ones, a veil tied with a gray ribbon over her brown hair, and a coral bead necklace. A small boy with honey-brown hair and lapis-blue eyes leans on her knees, his hands intertwined with hers. Light falls from the left on autumn-toned foliage behind them.","i":"The sitters are Lady Mary Templetown and her eldest son, Henry Upton, who later became the 2nd Viscount Templetown. Mother-and-child portraits were a favorite way for an aristocratic family to show tenderness and the continuity of the line, and the cult of sensibility, with Rousseau's call for mothers to raise their own children, made such closeness fashionable by 1802. The thin white gown reflects the neoclassical taste for plain muslin and a simple look.","c":"Lawrence achieves a rare softness here: the white dresses glow against the golden-brown foliage, the interlaced hands carry the feeling, and the pose seems natural and unforced. The gauzy dress and sweet faces tip toward sentimentality, and the mother's idealized features are close to the formula he used for many women. Even so, the grouping is graceful and truly tender, a notable picture of motherhood painted when he was in his early thirties."},"met:436850":{"d":"An almost square oil on canvas, about 78 by 77 centimeters, painted in 1823, showing two small sisters, Emily, born in 1818, and Laura Anne, born in 1820. The recorded subjects are girls, children and the genre of portrait. A double portrait of children of this size was made for the family home, to record the sitters at a fleeting age, and sibling portraits of the period usually posed the children in close contact, so that their affection became part of the subject.","i":"In the early nineteenth century childhood came to be seen as a time of innocence, an idea shaped by Rousseau and the Romantic poets, and parents wanted portraits that showed the natural affection of their children rather than miniature adults. Lawrence painted many such pictures, and this one is said to have become widely known through engravings, which carried his image of sweet, easy childhood far beyond the Calmady family.","c":"This is among the most appealing children's portraits of its time. Lawrence catches the sisters in an unposed-looking closeness, with tender flesh painting and soft light. The weakness is the sweetness, which tips toward sentiment, and a polish of surface that can seem smoother than children's liveliness would justify. Even so, few painters of his day matched his sympathy for children, and the picture shows the best of his late style."},"nga:50851":{"d":"A formal portrait of Francis Charles Seymour-Conway, 3rd Marquess of Hertford, in oil on canvas, about 128 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1822 or 1823. The size matches the standard three-quarter format of British portraiture, 50 by 40 inches, which gave room for a nobleman's rank to be shown in dress, pose and setting. In his male portraits of these years Lawrence often turned the sitter slightly toward the viewer and lit the face strongly against a darker, simplified ground, a formula that concentrated attention on the head.","i":"The 3rd Marquess (1777-1842) was a wealthy courtier, a friend of the Prince Regent, later George IV, and a collector on a large scale; the family's collecting, continued by his heirs, led to the Wallace Collection in London. He is often said to be the model for Lord Steyne in Thackeray's Vanity Fair, a reputation for dissipation that sits oddly beside the dignified image a portrait offers. Lawrence, the king's painter and President of the Royal Academy from 1820, was the natural choice.","c":"Lawrence's male portraits of the 1820s are at their best when a strongly lit face emerges from a darkened ground with a flash of nervous energy in the eyes, giving a romantic intensity few contemporaries matched. The risk is a surface gloss that flatters the sitter and keeps the character at arm's length. For a sitter of such notoriety, a polished official image reveals more about rank than about the man; it is a competent, characteristic late Lawrence."},"met:436853":{"d":"A vertical oil on canvas, about 92 by 72 centimeters, painted about 1824 to 1825. The recorded subjects are a woman, the sitter Lady Maria Conyngham, and a dog, so she is shown with an animal companion. The size matches the standard kit-cat format of British portraiture, 36 by 28 inches, which shows the figure to about mid-thigh and allows a sitter's hands, and an extra such as a dog, to be included.","i":"Lady Maria belonged to the Conynghams, a family whose fortunes rose when Elizabeth, Marchioness Conyngham, became a favorite of George IV in the 1820s. Lawrence, the king's painter, portrayed several of the family. A dog in a young woman's portrait could signal fidelity and affection as well as the ordinary pleasure of a pet, and gave the sitter a natural, domestic air. The portrait shows how closely the royal circle and the painter's practice overlapped.","c":"Lawrence's portraits of young women of the 1820s combine a bright, glancing handling with a flattering idealization of the face, and the dog adds liveliness and a contrasting texture. The limits are those of the formula: large eyes, smooth cheeks, a sitter more attractive than individual. This is a graceful, characteristic late work, perhaps more charming than profound."},"met:436852":{"d":"A vertical oil on canvas, about 91 by 71 centimeters, in the standard kit-cat size of 36 by 28 inches, whose date is not recorded. The title names the sitter as John Julius Angerstein, and the recorded subjects are a man and the portrait. The format shows the sitter to about mid-thigh with room for the hands, and portraits of this size were the usual choice for a merchant or professional man who wanted a dignified likeness for his house or office.","i":"Angerstein (1736-1823), born in Russia and settled in London, became a leading underwriter at Lloyd's and a wealthy merchant, and he was a major art collector. After his death the British government bought his thirty-eight paintings in 1824, and they formed the foundation of the National Gallery. Lawrence was a friend of the family and painted him. A portrait of such a patron is thus also a portrait of the origins of a national museum.","c":"Lawrence's portraits of men of business tend to be less theatrical than those of aristocrats, and the best rely on a lit, intelligent head and a plain setting. As a portrait of a patron and friend, it is likely to be more direct than his society portraits, a dignified record of an important figure rather than one of his showpieces. Its interest is as much historical as artistic, since the sitter's collection gave Britain its national gallery."},"met:11050":{"d":"Under a low roof of black-grey cloud, a dark inlet of water lies almost motionless. A single white sail stands far out on the bay, and a small rowing boat with one figure heads for the shore. In the foreground a man in a straw hat, orange shirt and tan vest sits on a plank, looking out; a small white dog sits beside him, a dark iron pot is nearby, and a sail is spread across a large boulder at the left. Low green banks and distant hills edge the water, and a strip of pale, greenish light shows beneath the cloud. The scene is painted in smooth, detailed oil on a wide canvas, about 71 by 112 centimetres.","i":"Heade based the picture on a sketch of an approaching storm over Narragansett Bay, Rhode Island, and in 1859 it was his largest painting to date. Its drama lies in the waiting. The fisherman sits and watches, the rower makes for shore, and nothing has happened yet. Heade returned to the theme in Thunder Storm on Narragansett Bay (1868) and in other storm scenes. Little known for decades after his death, he was rediscovered in the twentieth century, when historians grouped him with the Luminists, American painters of still, luminous air, and pictures like this one of unnatural calm before weather came to look strikingly modern.","c":"Heade's skill is restraint. The flat, leaden light, the black water and the tiny white sail create dread without any actual violence, and the small human details give scale to the sky. The weakness is a slightly stagey arrangement: the figures are placed like props, the foreground sand is dry, and the dark water is a large, nearly empty mass. Beside Frederic Church's operatic skies, Heade's are quieter and stranger, closer to a held breath than a spectacle, which is why this picture has aged so well."},"nga:126147":{"d":"A small upright oil on board, about 28 by 18 centimeters, dated 1860. The title names a wine glass, and the only recorded theme is flowers, so the picture probably shows blooms held in or set beside a glass, in the manner of a small domestic still life. Its tall, narrow shape and modest scale suit a single compact arrangement. It dates from the years before Heade's first journey to Brazil in 1863, when he was working mainly as a landscape painter in the northeastern United States.","i":"A wine glass used as a vase turns an everyday household object into the support for a gesture of beauty, and the motif echoes the old Dutch tradition of glass in still life, where the transparency of glass was a test of skill. In America around 1860, small flower and fruit still lifes found a growing market among middle-class buyers who wanted modest, tasteful pictures for the home. Heade's flowers in these years tend to be more spare than the dense bouquets of European still life.","c":"A picture this small depends on exact drawing and clear light, and Heade's patient observation, evident in his later marshes and hummingbirds, suits it. The glass, always a test of a painter's skill with transparency and reflection, is the part most worth looking at. At such a modest scale it is best taken as an intimate, carefully made work, an early link in the line of floral pictures that runs through his career."},"nga:126149":{"d":"A small upright oil on board, about 35 by 27 centimeters, dated 1862. The title names its contents: roses and heliotrope, a fragrant garden plant with small clusters of violet flowers, set in a vase on a marble tabletop. The only recorded theme is flowers. The marble top was a typical piece of Victorian parlor furniture, and it supplies a cool, solid base for the soft forms of the flowers.","i":"In the Victorian language of flowers, roses stood for love and heliotrope for devotion, so a bouquet of the two could be read as a quiet message of affection, whether or not Heade intended it. Heliotrope was also prized for its sweet, vanilla-like fragrance, a quality painting can only suggest. Pictures like this were made for American buyers who wanted a small, refined decoration for the home, and flower pieces became a steady part of Heade's output from about 1860.","c":"Heade's flower pieces of the early 1860s tend to be spare, firmly drawn and a little reserved, with the objects treated as clear shapes rather than an abundant profusion. The strength is clarity and a plain-spoken restraint; the weakness is a certain stiffness beside the more sensuous bouquets painted in France. It is a small, honest picture rather than a showpiece, and the marble and the glass of the vase give a practiced observer plenty to test himself on."},"nga:50281":{"d":"Waves of bottle-green water break over two boulders on a short stretch of beach in the foreground of this wide canvas, with white surf flaring toward the viewer. Beyond, about ten sailboats float on the bay, the nearest with tiny figures aboard and the rest shrinking to touches of white and gray. Sage-green hills curve in on both sides, with dark trees on craggy rocks at left and a distant spit of land bearing pale yellow buildings and a smoking chimney. Pale lavender clouds sweep up from a horizon that glows butter yellow at left and cools to mint green at upper right. Signed and dated 1864.","i":"Heade traveled to Brazil in 1863 and 1864 to paint a planned book of hummingbirds, and he also painted a few views of the bay of Rio de Janeiro, a famous and widely admired harbor. A view from the shore, with small boats dwarfed by a huge sky, belongs to the American tradition of luminous coastal painting, in which air and light are the true subject. The ships and tiny figures suggest a busy, working harbor behind the calm view.","c":"The sky is the real subject, and Heade handles it with great delicacy: lavender cloud streaming up from a golden horizon to cool green, with the boats shrinking to white flecks beneath. The two boulders and breaking surf give a firm foreground. Those picturesque rocks and the many tiny sailboats are, however, conventional props of a harbor view, and the surf is less subtle than the sky. It is a handsome, luminous early work."},"nga:126146":{"d":"A small oil on board, about 36 by 31 centimeters, dated 1867, whose only recorded theme is flowers. The title identifies apple blossoms in a vase, so the picture shows cut sprays of spring blossom as a simple domestic still life, set up indoors rather than in a garden. Heade painted roughly 150 flower still lifes across his career, and by 1867, back from his journeys to Brazil and Nicaragua, he was working in both landscape and flowers.","i":"Apple blossom is the emblem of spring, short-lived and promising fruit, and in the Victorian language of flowers it signified preference. Cut branches of blossom in a vase brought the season indoors, a pleasure the nineteenth-century middle-class home valued, and artists responded with small, tasteful pictures for that market. The vase and the cut branch also share the old still-life message that beauty is brief, since blossoms fade within days.","c":"A spray of blossom is a hard subject because the flowers are small, pale and numerous, and a painter must suggest the mass without counting every petal. Heade's clear, precise drawing is well suited to it, though his flowers tend to be crisper and less atmospheric than those of his French contemporaries. As a small, quietly competent still life, it is a pleasant example of a long-running strand of his work rather than a high point."},"cma:144967":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 72 by 128 centimeters, shows moonlight on land, sea and sky, in what the museum calls one of only a handful of nocturnal scenes Heade painted. Though the location is a real one, Point Judith on the Rhode Island coast, the night shrouds most details in shadow, so mood outweighs place. Human presence is limited to two sailing ships on the left side of the composition. The broad, low format stretches the horizon, a shape Heade often used for coastal and marsh views.","i":"Point Judith is a headland on the Rhode Island coast, long known to sailors as a hazard. Moonlit seascapes carried a mood of solitude and contemplation, a taste American painters had cultivated since Washington Allston, and Heade's small group of nocturnes shows him moving from carefully described places toward painting atmosphere itself. The two ships suggest the coastal trade, and perhaps the danger, of that shore.","c":"The accomplishment is restraint: by shrouding the shore in shadow Heade lets the moonlit sky and glinting water carry the picture, and the mood is unforced. The cost is a certain monotony, since a canvas in near darkness offers little incident beyond the two ships, and it can seem empty beside the clearly structured marshes and storm pictures that made his name. Even so, such rare nocturnes are among his most poetic seascapes."},"nga:61244":{"d":"Against a hazy green forest, a petal-pink and amethyst-purple orchid grows from a mossy branch at left, its ruffled pink petals flaring around a hot-pink center and a purple lip brushed with pale yellow. At right three jewel-toned hummingbirds gather around an upside-down, cone-shaped nest woven with burnt-orange strands, where two white eggs show. The largest bird, vivid green with a crimson tail, perches with its back to the viewer. Bright light comes from the upper left, with hanging vines at the sides and a patch of blue sky through gray clouds at upper right.","i":"After his travels in Brazil and Central America, Heade painted a large series of orchids and hummingbirds in imagined tropical settings, combining careful natural-history observation with a decorative, romantic vision. Orchids were then a craze in Europe and America, collected at great expense, and hummingbirds, found only in the Americas, were prized by naturalists. The scene was probably composed in the studio from separate studies, so it is an invention rather than a record.","c":"Few American pictures of its day pair such exact drawing with such strangeness. The orchid and birds have the clarity of a botanical plate, while the hazy, watery forest behind turns the scene into a dream. The two kinds of space do not fully agree, the plant and nest crisp and the distance almost abstract, but the contrast is the picture's charm, and it is one of the more striking of the series."},"cma:173688":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 38 by 76 centimeters, dated 1872, whose title names a tidal marsh at high tide. Heade painted the salt marshes of the northeastern coast again and again, and his marsh pictures typically use a very low horizon and a broad band of sky above a flat landscape of grass, creeks and sometimes haystacks. The double-width format suits that spreading, level subject. At high tide the channels fill and the flat ground can mirror the sky, an effect painters of marsh scenes sought.","i":"Salt marshes were worked for salt hay, which was cut and stacked on staddles to keep it dry above the tide, and this working landscape, visible in many of Heade's marsh pictures, attracted artists with its openness and its constant change of light. Heade's marshes are often read as expressions of a quiet national landscape in which sky and a thin line of earth are all. High tide adds the drama of water covering the grass.","c":"Heade's marshes are valued for their reduction: a long horizontal, a thin band of land, and a sky that does the work. The strength of such a design is calm and breadth; the weakness is that the subject can feel empty or repetitive across a long series. This is a typical, assured example of a motif he made his own, and its dependence on sky and tide rewards slow looking."},"cma:173480":{"d":"A single branch of apple blossoms is propped on a tabletop and supported by an undecorated wall in this small upright canvas, about 36 by 31 centimeters, dated 1873. The museum calls it a relatively stark example among the roughly 150 floral still lifes that Heade painted in his long career. The arrangement is plain, with no vase or bouquet, so the blossoms stand alone against the wall, with nothing to distract from the delicate flowers and the angle of the branch.","i":"Heade, by the museum's account, was fond of flowers and took pleasure in gathering and arranging bouquets, especially for his breakfast table, which helps explain why these pictures feel so private. Apple blossom is the emblem of spring, short-lived and promising fruit, and the single propped branch has an air of informality, as if just brought in from an orchard. The bare wall and single branch contrast with the lavish bouquets then in fashion.","c":"Stark compositions of this kind depend on drawing and light, and Heade's exact observation, so evident in his marshes and orchids, serves him well: each flower is given its own weight, and the plain wall lets the petals' pale tones carry. The cost is austerity, a bareness that some will find cool beside the sumptuous flower pieces of his time. As a quiet, simple tribute to a favorite flower, it is among his most direct floral pictures."},"cma:173665":{"d":"A small upright canvas, about 38 by 31 centimeters, dated 1873, showing orchid blossoms. The museum notes that by this date Heade had made three journeys to Central and South America and had made a large number of paintings of birds and flowers, especially orchids, in a tropical setting. The picture belongs to that body of work, which combines the exotic blooms collected by Victorian enthusiasts with the artist's own memories of the tropics.","i":"Orchids were among the most coveted plants of the Victorian age, collected by plant hunters in South and Central America and grown in expensive hothouses. Heade's pictures gave buyers who could not travel or afford the plants a view of their strangeness. Heade did not marry until 1883, at sixty-four, and these pictures belong to his long bachelor years, when his journeys and his paintings of the tropics were the chief adventure of his life.","c":"The orchid's exotic structure gives Heade a subject whose drawing is a challenge, which his exact eye meets. His orchid pictures can feel airless, with the bloom treated as a specimen, but the best of them join botanical truth to a dreamlike mood. For a small canvas it is a skilled, characteristic work that shows the particular mixture of science and fantasy that set him apart from other American still-life painters of the time."},"met:11051":{"d":"A hummingbird is shown among apple blossoms and leaves on a small canvas of about 31 by 36 centimeters, dated 1875. The recorded subjects are the bird, the flowers and the foliage, with the hummingbird, a creature found only in the Americas, treated as a jewel among the blossoms. Martin Johnson Heade made hummingbirds a specialty, and the combination of bird and flowering branch in a close view like this belongs to a long line of natural-history pictures.","i":"Hummingbirds fascinated nineteenth-century naturalists and collectors, who prized their iridescent plumage; Heade traveled to Brazil in 1863 to paint them for a planned book, The Gems of Brazil, which was never completed. Pairing a hummingbird with apple blossoms, the flower of a temperate American orchard, sets a tropical jewel in a New England spring, and blossom that lasts only days adds a note of fleeting beauty.","c":"Heade's hummingbirds are painted with the exactness of natural history, and a compact format gives the whole a jewel-box intensity. The mix of tropical creature and New England flower, however, can feel arranged more than observed, and the composition is more decorative than dramatic. A small, polished, slightly artificial pleasure that complements the broad marshes for which he is better known."},"nga:126145":{"d":"A vast plain of flat, lemon-green marshland stretches beneath a sky of tall, billowing clouds in lavender-purple and shell pink, whose tops catch pale pink sunlight from the left while their undersides stay gray-purple. At the lower left a gnarled tree with a round, sage-green crown sits mostly in shadow; a river curves in a tight S across the plain, past one plump, teardrop-shaped haystack, and a brown and a white cow graze on the far bank. Tawny hills edge the horizon under a turquoise sky. The picture is long and narrow, about 31 by 67 centimeters.","i":"Heade painted the salt marshes near Newburyport and Newbury, Massachusetts, again and again from the 1860s into the 1880s, treating a flat, apparently featureless landscape of hay meadows as a subject of light, weather and space. The haystacks are marks of local work. In an American landscape tradition where grand mountains and waterfalls were the usual subject, his marshes quietly claim that a plain, working landscape could carry as much feeling as a sublime peak.","c":"Heade is the poet of the marsh, and this long, narrow canvas shows why: the S-curve of the river leads the eye across a vast plain, the single haystack is the exactly placed note, and the clouds do the work of drama. Its pleasures are quiet and its drawing precise; its risk is sparseness, since the sense of space depends on a few elements held in delicate balance. A luminous, strangely modern picture whose emptiness is its subject."},"nga:101282":{"d":"A pastoral view of grassy marshland dotted with several yellow haystacks is crossed by a winding river; a shallow boat rests at the water's edge on the right, cows graze in the background, and a distant farmhouse stands at the left. The sky is full of light gray clouds with white light breaking through in places. The canvas is wide, about 39 by 77 centimeters. Marshfield is a coastal town south of Boston, whose salt marshes were among Martin Johnson Heade's favorite subjects.","i":"Marshfield, on the Massachusetts coast south of Boston, was known in the nineteenth century as the home of Daniel Webster, whose farm there made the town a place of pilgrimage for admirers of the statesman. Heade's pictures of the area, like those of Newbury, show the salt meadows as working land: haystacks, boats and cattle. The overcast light, with breaks of sun, is as much the subject as the place.","c":"This is a quieter, grayer Heade than the famous storm and sunset pictures: the light is soft, the greens subdued, and the long river carries the eye in an easy meander. The format is generous and the space convincing, but with so little incident the composition depends wholly on tone, and a little more dramatic punctuation might help. A solid example of his marsh pictures and a rewarding study in how little a landscape needs."},"met:11053":{"d":"A long, narrow view of the Newburyport salt meadows in Massachusetts, with haystacks dotting the flat grassland, on a canvas of about 27 by 56 centimeters. The picture belongs to Martin Johnson Heade's long engagement with the marshes near the mouth of the Merrimack River, painted in the late 1870s and early 1880s, in a horizontal format that gives the flat land its full extent.","i":"Newburyport, once a prosperous shipbuilding and trading port, had declined by the 1870s, and its surrounding salt marshes, harvested for hay, kept the character of a quiet agricultural landscape. Heade was largely overlooked after his lifetime until the mid-twentieth century, when the marshes' flat horizons, haystacks and clear light struck viewers as strikingly modern, and he came to be counted among the most original American landscape painters.","c":"The long, low format is an exercise in simplicity: a horizon, a ribbon of marsh, a few haystacks as the only vertical accents. The poetry lies in the economy and in the luminous air; the weakness, if one is to be found, is austerity, since there is little to engage a viewer who wants incident. Among the purest statements of Heade's marsh vision, quiet, spacious and almost abstract."},"met:11052":{"d":"A hummingbird among passionflowers on an upright canvas of about 51 by 31 centimeters; the recorded subjects are the bird, the Passiflora blossoms and a tree, in a tropical setting. Heade painted a long series of such pictures after his visit to Brazil in 1863, combining birds and flowers in dense, almost theatrical arrangements, and the date, about 1875 to 1885, places this one in the middle of that campaign.","i":"Passionflowers carry a religious meaning: from the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, Spanish missionaries in the New World read the flower's parts as symbols of Christ's Passion, the fringe as the crown of thorns and the three styles as the nails. Heade's picture can be read purely as natural history and decoration, but the flower's symbolic history hovers behind its exotic beauty. With the hummingbird, it presents the New World's tropics as a place of marvelous abundance.","c":"Heade's tropical pictures are the most Victorian of his work: lush, densely packed, with a collector's pleasure in rare things. The passionflower's unusual structure suits his clear, precise drawing, and the bird adds glitter. But the density can seem artificial next to the airy luminosity of his marshes, and repetition across decades blunts the surprise. Handsome and decorative rather than visionary."},"nga:93464":{"d":"Twigs of a magnolia tree with glossy light green leaves and three cream-white blossoms lie on a sapphire-blue cloth, brightly lit from the left. The blossoms are in three stages: a tight bud at left overlapped by a stem and unfurling leaves, a flower at the center just opening, and a third at right with petals splayed around its oval stamen. Light glints off the waxy leaves, and shimmer on the cloth, bright blue at right and deep royal blue in shadow, suggests velvet. The background shades toward black. The canvas is about 38 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Heade moved to St. Augustine, Florida, in 1883, and in his last two decades painted a series of magnolia blossoms laid on velvet, the most admired of his late pictures. Magnolia grandiflora, the great flower of the American South, with its waxy leaves and heavily scented, short-lived blossoms, carries a sense of luxury and fragility. Cut flowers on rich fabric turn a still life into a study of texture and light, at once natural and sensuous.","c":"These magnolias are among the finest American still lifes of the nineteenth century: the creamy petals in three stages of opening give the picture a quiet narrative of time, the waxy leaves glint, and the blue velvet offers a sumptuous contrast. The dark, nearly black ground isolates the flowers dramatically. Heade risks little in the arrangement, but he does not need to; the drama is in the paint. Quiet and sensuous at once."},"nga:396":{"d":"A young Mary sits on a wide bench draped with midnight-blue patterned cloth, holding the Christ Child in her lap, in a vertical panel with a pointed arch top, about 96 by 57 cm. She tilts her head to look down at him, a sliver of blond hair showing under a honey-gold cowl over a gold-trimmed burgundy robe. The child tilts his head up to look at her and reaches toward her neckline. Gold sleeve patterns, gold halos and a gold ground gleam. Her neckline is inscribed MATER, and her hem carries the Ave Maria.","i":"The inscription Mater, mother, and the angel's greeting at the hem, Ave Maria gratia plena, tie Mary's motherhood to the Annunciation. The Child's hand at her neckline suggests nursing, a reminder that Christ's humanity was nourished by his mother, and the tilted heads give the group a human warmth. The pointed top suggests a small altarpiece or the central panel of a polyptych, made for a church or a private chapel, in the courtly style of the International Gothic.","c":"Gentile's genius is in the gold and the fabric: the glittering sleeve and the patterned bench cloth are painted with a jeweler's care. The interplay of the mother's tilted head and the child's upturned face is the emotional center, and gives the panel its tenderness. The faces are idealized and conventional, but the delicacy of the design is remarkable. It is a small luxury object, International Gothic refinement at its most gracious."},"nga:411":{"d":"A dozen men and women approach or gather around a stone tomb on four columns in a church apse decorated in gold, in this square panel of about 36 cm. Two people reach to touch the tomb's edge, one supports another who has swooned beneath it, and a man with a cane approaches with his robe pulled up over a red gash on his shin. At the bottom three men hold each other up, while another carrying a crutch walks away. Vaulted arches stand on rows of columns, and a monk looks through them.","i":"The saint is Nicholas of Myra, whose tomb was said to give off a healing liquid, the manna of Saint Nicholas, and pilgrims came to be cured. The panel is thought to be one of the predella scenes of the altarpiece painted in 1425 for the Quaratesi family in Florence, telling the saint's miracles at close range. The healed man walking away with his crutch is the proof of a miracle, and the sick still approaching are the audience.","c":"A tiny stage of varied incident: the clarity of the story, with the healed man leaving and the sick arriving, gives it vivid human interest, and the detail of the red gash on the shin shows close observation. The architecture, rows of columns and vaults, shows Gentile's interest in space, though the perspective is empirical, and in Florence in 1425 Masaccio's new art was close by. Gentile's graceful Gothic coexists with it, and the golden apse glitters."},"met:436452":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child, attended by angels, in an upright panel about 86 by 51 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with traces of a gold ground. A scroll is among the recorded details, and the picture is dated 1427, the year Gentile da Fabriano died in Rome, which makes it a late work by the most elegant painter of the International Gothic style in Italy. The panel is small enough for a private chapel or a bedroom.","i":"The enthroned or humble Madonna with angels was a staple of Italian devotional painting, and Gentile treats it with the courtly grace that made him a favorite of patrons from Venice to Florence to Rome. A scroll in the scene may carry a text of prophecy or praise. The angels, with their rich, flowing draperies, serve as heralds and attendants to a queen of heaven who is also, in the pose, a mother.","c":"Gentile's gift is for luxurious surface: gold, brocade, delicate faces and a sweetness of line. Even in a small late panel the elegance is evident, and the faces tend to have a tenderness that goes beyond decoration. If the picture lacks the monumentality of his Florentine contemporaries Masaccio and Donatello, it makes up for it in refinement. A pleasing late statement of International Gothic at its last peak."},"met:435896":{"d":"A monk in a white hooded habit looks out from a small upright panel, his body turned to one side and his head turned back toward the viewer. He has short brown hair, pale blue-gray eyes, and a thin beard that falls in straggling strands toward his chest. Behind him is a deep red wall with soft vertical shading. He seems to sit behind a painted frame whose lower ledge carries an inscription and, beside it, a tiny fly that looks real enough to brush away. Light falls strongly on his face and on the folds of the hood. The panel is small, about 29 by 22 centimeters, and the surface is very smooth and finely detailed.","i":"Petrus Christus worked in Bruges and drew on the meticulous manner of Jan van Eyck. This portrait of an unnamed Carthusian, signed and dated 1446 on the painted ledge, is a famous early example of trompe l'oeil: the fly on the frame is meant to fool the eye, and scholars disagree whether it signals mortality and decay or simply the painter's skill. The Carthusians were a strict, largely silent monastic order, and the sitter's identity is lost. A halo that a later hand had added was judged not to be original and was removed in the 1990s. The panel is part of the Jules Bache Collection, which came to the Met in 1949.","c":"A small picture with a large presence. The face is built from calm, precise modeling, and the long nose and wide eyes give the monk a watchful, faintly quizzical character that no formula could produce. The red ground sets off the white habit well. The weaknesses are small: the body is awkwardly turned, and the plain hood gives the painter little to do. As an experiment in space, with the fly and the painted frame playing games with the viewer's sense of what lies in front of the picture, it is a daring piece of 1446."},"met:435897":{"d":"A tiny head of Christ crowned with thorns, about 15 by 11 centimeters, painted in oil on parchment that was later laid down on wood. The title Ecce Homo, Latin for Behold the man, recalls Pilate's words as he presented the scourged and mocked Christ to the crowd. At this size the picture is a devotional image for a single viewer, held close in prayer. Petrus Christus, who worked in Bruges and was heir to Jan van Eyck's manner, painted it about 1445.","i":"The suffering face of Christ, thorn-crowned and turned toward the viewer, was one of the central images of late medieval Northern devotion, an icon of compassion that asked the worshipper to meditate on the Passion. Small images like this were often kept at home or in a book, and might be paired with a portrait of the grieving Virgin. The Eyckian taste for exact, close-up description gave such heads a startling intimacy.","c":"Christus is often called a lesser Van Eyck, and a tiny head shows both sides of the verdict: the surface is exact and the emotion contained, but the effect is gentler and more restrained than Van Eyck's. The small scale makes the oil medium shine, and the fine detail rewards attention as in a miniature. It is best seen as an object of private devotion and fine craft rather than as a grand painting."},"met:435899":{"d":"The Annunciation takes place outdoors, seen from slightly above, in a walled garden in front of a stone church. At left the angel Gabriel kneels in a red cope, his rainbow-feathered wings raised and a gilded scepter in his hand. At right Mary stands in the doorway of a Gothic porch in a long blue mantle, a book in one hand and the other raised. Between them are a vase of white lilies and the dove of the Holy Spirit in a burst of golden rays. Letters are cut on the doorstep. The stonework, the brick, the hedge and the ruined garden wall at left are painted in fine detail.","i":"The Metropolitan Museum describes the composition as unconventional: Mary is sheltered in a church doorway, which presents her both as the woman who receives the message and as the Church itself. The architecture mixes Romanesque and Gothic parts, read as the move from Judaism to Christianity. The lettering on the step reads Regina celi letare, Queen of Heaven, rejoice, flanked by A and M for Ave Maria. The panel is the remains of the left wing of a triptych, cut down on three sides. Scholars argued over its author, Erwin Panofsky giving it to Jan van Eyck and Max Friedländer defending Petrus Christus. It is dated about 1445.","c":"A cool, inventive piece. Setting the scene in a garden before a real church portal, with accurate stone and plants, gives the old subject a fresh, almost documentary look, and the light on the masonry is convincing. The figures are less convincing. Gabriel's wings are elaborate but his face is blank, Mary is stiff, and the perspective is slightly hesitant at the edges. That Panofsky could ascribe it to Van Eyck shows its quality; its limits show why Christus is usually ranked a step below him."},"nga:47":{"d":"A Nativity in oil on panel, about 128 by 95 centimeters, painted around 1450 by the Netherlandish master Petrus Christus. The museum's record gives only the title and the medium, so the figures and the setting are not described here. A Nativity of this date from Bruges would show the newborn Christ adored by the Virgin and Joseph in a humble stable, probably with the ox and ass, angels and shepherds, rendered with the meticulous oil technique of Jan van Eyck's followers.","i":"Petrus Christus became a citizen of Bruges in 1444, a few years after the death of Jan van Eyck, and is regarded as his successor, continuing the Eyckian tradition of clear light, minute detail and rich color. A Nativity was a favorite subject for private devotion, where the viewer could share the wonder of the shepherds and the tenderness of the parents. Some Nativities of the era were shaped by the visions of Saint Bridget of Sweden, who saw the Child lying on the ground.","c":"Christus is the quieter, more reserved heir of the great Flemish generation: his figures are sturdy and calm, his space convincing and his color restrained. Compared with Rogier van der Weyden's drama or Van Eyck's glittering precision, he can seem plain, though a plainness that suits the humility of the Nativity. A dignified, thoughtful panel by a painter whose reputation has risen steadily in recent decades."},"met:435898":{"d":"The dead Christ is mourned by the Virgin, Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene on Calvary, in a small panel of about 26 by 36 centimeters. The recorded details tell the setting: a skull and a bone lie on the ground, since Golgotha means place of the skull, and pliers and a hammer, the tools used to draw the nails, lie nearby with a cloth for the body. A tree rises beside the scene. Petrus Christus painted it about 1450 in the exact, jewel-like manner of the Bruges school.","i":"The Lamentation, the mourning over the body taken down from the cross, was a core subject of Northern devotion, which dwelt on Christ's wounds and the Virgin's grief so that worshippers might feel compassion. The skull and bone at the foot of Golgotha were linked in legend to Adam, whose skull was said to lie under the cross, so that Christ's blood redeems the first man's sin. Hammer and pliers, the humble instruments of the Passion, are treated with almost still-life attention.","c":"On a small scale Christus achieves a restrained but real pathos: figures grouped closely, objects described with a jeweler's care. He lacks the gripping emotional intensity of Rogier van der Weyden, whose Deposition and Lamentation set the standard, and his figures can look more composed than devastated. The merit is quiet; the picture rewards the viewer who looks closely and thinks of it as a prayer rather than a drama."},"nga:46109":{"d":"A man with close-cropped dark hair kneels beside two steps that lead from an interior to a distant landscape, facing right, almost in profile. His voluminous black robe is trimmed with brown fur, and a red scarf lies over one shoulder. Two wooden clogs with black straps lie nearby on the tan and brown checkered floor. A rectangular shield, scarlet above and gold-patterned below, hangs on the wall; beyond the steps are a courtyard, a three-story brick building and a crenellated wall, with hills under a pale sky.","i":"Donors paid for altarpieces and had themselves painted in prayer so that their devotion would be permanently on view. The shield on the wall is presumably the man's coat of arms, which would have identified him to contemporaries, though his name is not recorded. His wooden overshoes, set aside, may signify humility or the holiness of the scene he faces. The panel and its female counterpart were probably the wings of a small triptych.","c":"Christus achieves a remarkable calm: the clear, slightly cool light, the exactly placed floor tiles and the little view of courtyard and hills put a worldly man in a spacious, believable room. The face is plain, almost stolid, and the profile is stiff, but the details of fur, clogs and shield are fine. A hushed, dignified portrait that gives a sense of a real person on his knees."},"nga:46110":{"d":"A woman kneels in prayer, presumably, as the title makes her a donor, one of the patrons who paid for an altarpiece and had themselves painted in devout attendance. The panel is narrow and upright, about 42 by 22 centimeters, painted around 1455 in oil. The museum's record offers no description of her face, dress or setting, but it forms a pair with the Portrait of a Male Donor, likely the two wings of a small triptych.","i":"Donors were included in religious pictures so that their piety would be permanently on display and their prayers ever present before the sacred figures. The two narrow panels by Christus, a man and a woman of similar format and date, probably faced inward toward a central image that is now lost or separated. In this arrangement a couple, shown in prayer on either side, take part in the holy event with the dignity of their rank.","c":"Donor portraits gave painters of the Eyckian school the chance to paint a face unburdened by symbolism, and Christus meets it with quiet candor: the likeness is likely to be firm and unidealized, the headdress and dress neatly rendered, the setting restrained. Seen alone, the wing loses its context; together with the male donor panel, it recovers the sense of a devotional pair. Dignified, reserved and carefully made."},"met:928091":{"d":"The Virgin and Child are joined by Saint John the Evangelist in a picture of about 87 by 64 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas and later laid down on wood. The date, 1512 or 1513, makes it an early work by Rosso Fiorentino, who was then about eighteen, in Florence, trained in the circle of Andrea del Sarto and not yet the angular, restless Mannerist that he became. Nothing further is recorded of the figures' poses or setting.","i":"A Madonna and Child with a saint was a standard type of Florentine devotional picture, made for the house or a small chapel, in which the Virgin's maternal tenderness meets a saint who may be the owner's patron or a model of devotion. John the Evangelist, the beloved disciple and author of the fourth Gospel and of the Apocalypse, is traditionally shown young and beardless, and stands for love and contemplation. By the 1520s Rosso would break with this harmonious manner.","c":"In this juvenile picture Rosso is still working within the Florentine tradition of Andrea del Sarto and Fra Bartolommeo, with a dignity that his later, jagged pictures deliberately abandon. The interest is in seeing the starting point of a rebel: the harmony here is learned and not yet questioned. It is a significant early document of a painter who would soon turn against the classical manner he had mastered."},"nga:46158":{"d":"A young man stands against an olive-green background, his body partly turned to the right, one hand on his hip and the other near his waist, and meets the viewer's eye with a neutral expression. He has short brown hair under a black hat with pointed ends on either side, a dark outfit with a high white collar and puffed black sleeves, and a thin gold ring on his right hand. The brushstrokes of the green ground remain visible. The panel is about 89 by 68 centimeters, and the sitter is unnamed.","i":"In Florence in the 1520s, portraits of young men, often shown against a plain ground with an air of reserve, were a way of presenting a family's heir, a friend or a sophisticated courtier. The hand on the hip, the neutral stare and the stylish black costume show the self-conscious elegance of the period; the sitter is unnamed, so his identity and profession remain unknown. Rosso's moment, around 1524, is also that of Pontormo and the beginnings of Mannerism, with its taste for cool, controlled poise.","c":"A remarkably direct portrait for its time: the plain green ground, the simple dark clothing and the unsmiling, unreadable face concentrate attention on the sitter as a presence. The pose is confident without being theatrical, and the visible brushwork in the background adds a note of immediacy. It lacks the nervous energy of Rosso's religious pictures; here the restraint is the point. Cool, intelligent and a little enigmatic."},"met:437039":{"d":"A classical landscape with trees, a dog and small human figures, on a wide canvas of about 71 by 112 centimeters. The title promises the ideal landscape of Poussin and Claude, in which a composed view of trees, meadows and distance is peopled by a few figures of an imagined antiquity. Henri Mauperché, a Parisian painter of the seventeenth century, worked in this manner, and landscapes of the kind were probably made to fit the paneled rooms of Paris townhouses.","i":"The classical landscape, invented in Rome by Annibale Carracci and perfected by Claude and Poussin, presented nature as ordered, serene and peopled by shepherds, nymphs and travelers of an imagined antiquity. French patrons prized it as an image of civilized harmony, with the figures offering a moral or poetic note. The dog adds a touch of everyday life to a scene that otherwise recalls an ancient golden age.","c":"Mauperché is a minor follower of the great Roman landscape masters, and a picture like this shows both his competence and his dependence: a clear structure of trees and distance, a quiet atmosphere and pleasant figures, but little of Claude's radiant light or Poussin's intellectual rigor. It is a decorative, agreeable work, best valued as an example of how the classical landscape was adapted for French taste."},"met:436062":{"d":"Shepherds play a flute and a bagpipe in a large pastoral landscape, 91 by 119 centimeters, with sheep, cattle and a dog gathered nearby. It was painted around 1643 to 1644, when Cuyp was in his early twenties, and comes from his formative years in Dordrecht, when he was absorbing lessons from Van Goyen and the Utrecht landscape painters. A wide canvas of this size was made for a house, not a cabinet.","i":"Shepherds with pipes belong to the pastoral tradition, from Virgil's Eclogues to Dutch pastoral poetry, in which herdsmen pass their time in music and song in a country of ease. Dutch buyers in the 1640s, mostly city dwellers, enjoyed this vision, often linked to Italy and a golden age. Cuyp, from Dordrecht, would spend his career making cattle and herdsmen into emblems of calm prosperity.","c":"Even in a work of his early twenties Cuyp has a sure sense of structure: the group of herdsmen and animals gives the canvas a solid center, and the large scale lets him treat a shepherd's idyll with something close to monumentality. His genius lay in light, though, and on the evidence of chronology the glowing atmosphere of his mature pictures is still to come. It looks forward more than it achieves."},"nga:100922":{"d":"A wooden pier extends from the left edge across about a quarter of the width of a horizontal panel, and beyond a calm body of water lies a town on the distant shore, beneath a cloudy sky filling the top three-quarters. Three men and a woman stand in a group in muted dark clothing, near a fourth man seated on a piling; a gun leans against one man's hip. Three small boats are tied at the pier's end, and four sailboats recede down the center. The town has peaked roofs, a windmill and a tall steeple. Oil on panel, about 45 by 76 centimeters, early 1640s.","i":"Dordrecht, on the Maas in the southern Netherlands, was Cuyp's home town, and its harbor, ferries and sails filled his early work. The man with a gun among the group is a reminder that river and marsh were hunting grounds for the well-to-do, and the tiny figures give the view its human scale. At this date Cuyp was painting in the subdued, silvery tonal manner of Jan van Goyen, before he developed the golden light for which he is famed.","c":"A modest early Cuyp whose strength is atmosphere: the low horizon, the vast sky and the pale, silvery water carry the picture, and the pier and figures supply an anecdotal foreground. The cool tonal color is far from the golden glow of his mature work, and the composition is rather loose, but it shows the future master's instinct for open space and light. A sound, quietly appealing river view."},"nga:576":{"d":"A fleet of ships gathers on a broad river lined with trees and buildings, under an immense pale blue sky filled with clouds that are golden on top and dove-gray beneath. The horizon is low. The river is congested with boats and ships, many hung with red, white and blue flags, their decks crowded with hatted men. The largest ship, at the right, has a rowboat alongside holding a seated man in crimson and a standing man in black, while on deck a musician plays a drum and another man drinks from a flagon. Oil on canvas, about 115 by 170 centimeters, about 1650.","i":"Dordrecht lies where the Maas meets the Rhine delta, and its harbor was one of the busiest in the Dutch Republic. The red, white and blue flags are the colors of the Dutch Republic, and the crowded decks and music suggest a festive occasion, though the event itself is not known. Cuyp's views of his native river combine commercial energy with patriotic pride, and the sky takes up most of the canvas.","c":"A grand, luminous harbor scene in which Cuyp's gift for light shows in the golden-edged clouds and the warm glow on sails and hulls. The congestion of ships is marshaled into clear groups, and the low horizon gives the sky its majesty. The crowd of figures is lively but summary, and a little of the glamour is borrowed from the Italianate painters Cuyp admired. It is among the finest of his river views."},"nga:69390":{"d":"Eight cows in warm sunlight stand and lie on a mossy green riverbank, amber, tawny, white or black, with short curving horns. Seven cluster at the bottom center, a tawny red cow with a white face lying at their heart, and the eighth, black with a white face, lies to the left. Behind them a hill rises at the right, where three men meet at the top, one on horseback and two holding tall staffs. The silvery-blue river runs back to low trees on a low horizon. Puffy clouds fill three quarters of the picture. Oil on panel, about 68 by 90 centimeters, 1645 to 1650.","i":"Cuyp made his name with pictures of cattle in golden light in the countryside around his home city of Dordrecht, an ideal Dutch pastoral that was prized by collectors, especially in Britain in later centuries. The cows are portrayed with the care of portraits, and the men on the hill, their meeting caught in a ray of sunlight, add a small human drama. The picture celebrates the prosperity and peace of the Dutch countryside.","c":"A warm, sunlit picture that shows what Cuyp does best: the cows are solid, individually observed and grouped with an eye for pattern, and the golden light seems to rest on them. The composition is simple, with the cattle placed low in the frame and a vast, airy sky above. If the scene is stock, it is stock raised to excellence. This is Cuyp in his central, much-imitated mode."},"nga:100926":{"d":"Warm sunlight falls on five cows lying or standing on a low hill beside four people on foot and on horseback. The nearest rider, on a black horse, wears a plumed hat and a red cape over a blue-gray coat and carries a long rod; he talks with two people on foot, one in a gray coat and floppy plumed hat pointing with a staff, the other in a straw hat. At the right edge the second horseman, on a silvery-gray horse, looks back over his shoulder. Spires rise over trees on the low horizon. Oil on panel, about 48 by 82 centimeters, mid 1650s.","i":"The riders in plumed hats and capes are presumably gentlemen, not herdsmen, and their conversation with the cowherds illustrates the Dutch idea of the countryside as a place where classes met in peaceful, productive harmony. Cuyp's patrons, wealthy Dordrecht families, enjoyed seeing their land and cattle in such an ideal light. The title names the herdsmen, but the cows and the golden light are the real subject.","c":"A sunlit conversation piece in which cattle and people share equal weight, and the warm light unifies them. Cuyp's cows are again the stars, solid and individual, while the riders add a courtly note. The panel is small and the composition modest, but the glow of late afternoon, the pale sky and the birds create a lyrical calm. A representative mid-career Cuyp that shows his confident grasp of Italianate light."},"met:436063":{"d":"Two boys, Cornelis and Michiel Pompe van Meerdervoort, ride horses in a hunting party with their tutor and coachman and a dog. At 110 by 156 centimeters, it is a large wide canvas painted about 1652 to 1653 by the Dordrecht artist Aelbert Cuyp, in the format normally reserved for princes and generals. Horses, hounds and open country take as much of the canvas as the sitters, as befits a painter better known for animals and light than for portraits.","i":"The Pompe van Meerdervoort were a patrician family of Dordrecht, and the equestrian format, borrowed from portraits of princes and commanders, proclaimed their rank. Hunting was a noble pastime, and showing boys at it suggested that they were being brought up as gentlemen. The elder boy, Michiel, died in 1653 at the age of about fifteen, so the picture may preserve him near the end of his short life.","c":"A cheerful, ambitious picture that stands out among Dutch portraits, which were rarely so outdoorsy. Cuyp was chiefly a landscape painter, more at ease with animals and light than with sitters, and the figures may look a little stiff. But the combination of children's portrait, sporting picture and landscape is rare, and the setting does much to enliven what could have been a dutiful family record."},"cma:122340":{"d":"Travelers make their way through hilly countryside in a serene panel, 48 by 75 centimeters, that evokes the hills and golden sunlight of Italy though Cuyp never went there. The museum notes that the panel is beautifully preserved, and that small dabs of white paint suggest the shimmer of the sun on the landscape. The road and its small figures give human scale to an idyllic, imagined south, painted around 1650.","i":"Dutch painters who had been to Italy, such as Jan Both, brought back a vision of warm light, hills and classical ruins, and painters who stayed at home, like Cuyp in Dordrecht, learned it from their pictures and prints. The Italy of this panel is an invention, often built from the Dutch river landscape's own horizons. Travelers on the road give a loose story of journeying through a peaceful, sunlit world.","c":"Few painters have caught light as Cuyp does, and this panel shows why: sun-warmed hills and tiny dabs of white that glitter on the surface make the air of a perfect afternoon. The people are incidental. If there is a weakness, it is that the picture is so serene that it feels like Arcadia rather than a place. But the mood is sincere, and the fine preservation lets a viewer see what Cuyp intended."},"met:436061":{"d":"The Holy Family flees to Egypt through a wide landscape, and as the title says, the landscape comes first. A tree, a man and cattle share the scene with the Holy Family. The picture, painted on a wood panel about 46 by 58 centimeters around 1650, sets the Gospel story in a pastoral countryside, where the sacred travelers are small figures in a world of cows and trees.","i":"In Matthew's Gospel (2:13-15) an angel warns Joseph in a dream to take Mary and the child to Egypt to escape Herod's massacre of the infants. Painters liked the subject because it allowed the Holy Family to travel through a rich landscape, and in the Dutch Republic it became a favorite vehicle for landscape painting, with the sacred figures reduced to small travelers in nature.","c":"Cuyp's landscapes give light a character of its own, and a Flight into Egypt is a fine pretext for his calm, sunlit country. The sacred subject serves mostly as an excuse for the cattle, trees and air. This is not a devotional picture, and it does not pretend to be. As an example of Cuyp around 1650, when his art turned toward southern light, it is pleasing and typical."},"met:436064":{"d":"Young herdsmen and their cows occupy an open landscape in this large canvas, about 112 by 132 centimeters, painted in the later 1650s. In Cuyp's mature pastoral scenes the animals are usually set large against a broad sky and bathed in warm, hazy light, so that the cattle become the true heroes of the picture and the herdsmen their quiet companions. The size marks it as a picture for a wall in a patrician house, not a cabinet.","i":"Cattle were the pride of Holland's pastures, and Cuyp, from a Dordrecht family of painters, made cows his signature. Dairy farming produced real wealth, and such pictures flattered owners who were landholders, brewers and regents. The herdsmen are less individuals than parts of an ideal order of calm labor. Cuyp's golden light was especially prized by English collectors in the eighteenth century, which is why so many of his pictures hang in British and American collections.","c":"The mature Cuyp is a poet of atmosphere: haze, glow and cattle set like quiet monuments. In a large canvas the composition is simple, almost static, and the effect depends on light and the dignity of the beasts. The weakness is the sameness of his formula, since he found a recipe and repeated it. But when the recipe works, as it generally does at this date, the result is among the most serene images in Dutch art."},"nga:1153":{"d":"A large landscape in oil on canvas, about 120 by 172 centimeters, painted in the late 1650s by Aelbert Cuyp. The title names its cast: horsemen, herdsmen and cattle, set in a countryside under an open sky. At this size and date it belongs to Cuyp's mature manner, in which riders and herds in a river landscape are bathed in warm, golden light.","i":"Cuyp's pictures of horsemen and cattle appealed to the Dutch landowners and merchants of Dordrecht, who saw in them an image of a peaceful, productive land. Riders in such scenes are often gentlemen on an outing and herdsmen are the tenants who keep their herds, so the pictures quietly express the social order of the countryside.","c":"Large canvases like this are Cuyp's grandest statement: a wide, luminous sky, a broad plain of grass and water and animals drawn with great sureness. The title and size place it in the series that made his reputation. It was this vein, golden, calm and stately, that British collectors prized above all."},"nga:66":{"d":"A man in a crimson jacket and high-crowned black hat stands with his back to the viewer, pointing with one hand and holding a tall shepherd's staff in the other, near a herd of seven caramel and ivory cows on a grassy rise beyond boulders. A woman in a robin's-egg-blue dress and straw hat sits on the far side of the herd, looking at him, staff in hand. Two more people sit near a donkey with baskets at the right. Long shadows fall from the setting sun, which lights the undersides of slate-gray clouds with peach and gold. Oil on canvas, about 66 by 88 centimeters, 1655 to 1660.","i":"Cuyp's late-afternoon cattle pieces are often read as images of the Dutch countryside's calm prosperity, and the setting sun gives these herdsmen a golden, almost timeless dignity. The ruins on the far shore evoke the Italianate pastoral tradition, in which ancient remains suggest the passage of time. Cuyp never went to Italy, but he learned its light from Dutch painters who had. The man's pointing gesture invites the eye across the picture.","c":"One of Cuyp's most satisfying pictures of the 1650s: the long shadows and gilded cloud undersides are handled with real conviction, and the cows and herdsmen are placed against the light with an easy confidence. The figures are individual, the man in crimson giving a vivid accent. The sentiment is tranquil rather than profound, but the sunset and the broad sky are among the finest passages of light in Dutch painting."},"nga:1152":{"d":"A large equestrian double portrait in oil on canvas, about 123 by 172 centimeters, by Aelbert Cuyp, begun about 1655 and reworked in the early 1660s. The title names a lady and a gentleman on horseback, presumably the patrons who commissioned the painting. Cuyp usually set such riders in an open, sunlit countryside near Dordrecht, and the wide format of this canvas leaves ample room for that setting.","i":"Equestrian portraits were a sign of rank and leisure: riding and hunting were pursuits of landowners and patricians, and a couple painted on horseback in their own countryside presented themselves as members of the ruling class. Cuyp, best known for landscape, painted several of these riding parties, often combining portraiture with scenery. The sitters are not named in the title, and the picture was reworked years after its start.","c":"Cuyp's riding portraits are among his most original pictures, because the sitters are placed within a luminous outdoor world, not before a curtain as stiff dignitaries. The large scale, the rider theme and the later reworking suggest an ambitious picture. It stands at the point where portrait and landscape merge."},"nga:214256":{"d":"A man sits on the ground in a woodland clearing, lit by a patch of light while the rest of the forest is dark. He has curly gray hair and a gray beard, his eyes are wide, and one hand is raised to his head; he wears a brown jacket, a brown-green vest, red trousers and blue stockings, with a hat and a rifle on the ground beside him. The trees are dense in green, dark red and burnt orange, and an opening at the right shows a small body of water with a barely visible white sailboat. The panel is about 38 by 46 centimeters.","i":"Washington Irving's Rip Van Winkle (1819 to 1820) is America's first great folk tale: a Catskills villager who sleeps twenty years after drinking with the ghostly crew of Henry Hudson and wakes to a changed world, his gun rusted, his beard long, the king's rule replaced by the republic. The moment of waking is the story's pivot, comic and poignant: a man bewildered by time. A painting of it in 1823, so soon after the tale appeared, marks American literature's arrival as a subject for art.","c":"A small, charming essay in literary painting by a young portraitist: the pool of light on the astonished old man, the dark woodland around, the sailboat glimpsed through the trees as a sign of the new world. The theatrical lighting and the gesture are somewhat stagey, and the figure stands more as an actor than a character. But for its date and scale it is an assured picture, and Inman's gift for expressive faces shows even here."},"met:11218":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman, painted in watercolor on ivory and measuring only about 8 by 7 centimeters, dated about 1820; the sitter is not named. Henry Inman was then still in his late teens and had been trained by the New York portraitist John Wesley Jarvis; like many American portraitists of the day he began as a miniaturist, a trade that offered the quickest route to a paying clientele.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the commonest likenesses in the early republic: affordable, portable and intimate, they were exchanged at engagements and marriages, kept in cases or worn, and handed on as family treasures. Watercolor on ivory, with its translucent flesh tones, suited the polite, delicate ideal of the American woman of the day. The sitter's anonymity today is typical; many such pictures lost their names as they passed out of the family.","c":"At about 1820 Inman's miniatures typically show a young painter's careful, polite manner: fine stippling for flesh, a smooth, clear likeness and no ambition beyond polish. The work is less assured than his later oils, but it offers the same quick sympathy with the sitter and a clean, bright surface. A competent, attractive record of a woman of the 1820s, and a reminder of how many American painters began with the miniature."},"met:13499":{"d":"Rebecca Pittard, Mrs. Charles Cummings, is shown in a lace-trimmed cap with a ribbon and bow, a fichu (a light kerchief worn around the neck and shoulders) and a shawl, with earrings, on a canvas of about 76 by 64 centimeters. It is a bust or half-length portrait of the kind painted for a New York family in the 1820s. Henry Inman, then in his twenties, was already one of the city's busiest portraitists.","i":"The cap, fichu and shawl mark a respectable married woman of the 1820s: caps were worn indoors by wives and older women as a sign of modesty, and the soft, light fabrics of fichu and lace were the fashion of the period. Such portraits recorded a woman's status and character as a wife, and were often commissioned alongside a portrait of her husband. The sitter is identified by both her married and maiden names, a customary way of keeping family connections in view.","c":"Inman was a fluent, accomplished portraitist, a founder of the National Academy of Design, and in the 1820s he rose to a leading position in New York, painting with an easy, polished facility. This portrait offers a graceful, plain-spoken likeness in which lace, ribbons and shawl are handled with a deft touch; the sitter's character is conveyed with courtesy rather than depth. A fine example of the elegant American portrait before photography."},"met:15586":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman in watercolor on ivory, about 9 by 6 centimeters, dated about 1825. She wears a neckerchief and an earring, small details of dress that place her in the fashions of the mid-1820s. Henry Inman, by now one of the busiest portraitists in New York and a founder of the National Academy of Design, painted miniatures alongside his oils.","i":"The light kerchief and the single visible earring belong to the modest, tasteful dress of the 1820s, when simple accessories signaled refinement. The portrait is a private object: small enough to be kept in a drawer or worn at the breast. For the sitter's descendants, the lack of a recorded name leaves only the image itself, a woman as she wished to be remembered.","c":"Inman's miniatures are best seen as the delicate side of a robust talent: the same sure eye for a likeness, translated to a small scale, with a lightness of touch in the details of costume. They are less ambitious than the oils, and many are close to formula; what lifts the best is a spark of personality in the face. A refined, if modest, piece of work from a busy practice."},"met:11223":{"d":"The English tragedian William Charles Macready is shown in his role as William Tell, the Swiss patriot of legend, on a canvas of about 77 by 64 centimeters. Macready toured the United States in 1826 and 1827, and Inman, a leading New York portraitist, painted him during the visit. The subject combines a portrait of a celebrated actor with a costume picture of a heroic role.","i":"Macready was the leading British tragic actor of his generation, and William Tell, from James Sheridan Knowles's play of 1825, was one of his signature roles: the simple mountaineer and father whose defiance of the tyrant Gessler stands for liberty. Americans of the 1820s, only a generation from their own revolution, warmed to the story of a free people resisting tyranny. Theatrical portraits like this kept stage celebrity alive in a pre-photographic world.","c":"Inman gives the likeness a sleek, theatrical polish: a performer's presence captured with brisk fluency, the costume and pose suited to a hero. The weakness is a certain stagey artificiality, the sense of an actor holding a pose rather than a man in action, and the portrait does not rival the best theatrical portraits of Lawrence or Sully. As a record of a celebrated visitor and of an American painter's skill at flattery, it is lively and attractive."},"met:15090":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man named John Inman, in watercolor on ivory, about 8 by 7 centimeters, dated about 1830. He was presumably the painter's brother, a New York newspaper editor, which would make this a family picture painted for affection rather than fee. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. Henry Inman painted miniatures alongside his oils in the 1820s, and by the 1830s was concentrating on oil portraits.","i":"Portraits of siblings were part of the economy of affection in an artist's world: a painter practiced on family and friends, gave them likenesses and received in return introductions and encouragement. A miniature, small and personal, suits the intimacy of a family exchange better than a public commission, and survives as a record of two brothers in the lively literary and artistic circles of Jacksonian New York.","c":"A portrait of one's own brother is freed from the need to flatter, and a good miniaturist can let the face speak. Inman's best miniatures have this directness, and a family picture would have been an occasion for it. The small scale limits ambition, but a likeness made for love rather than payment may be more natural than the commissions; its interest is partly biographical, partly an illustration of his skill at the smallest scale."},"met:11224":{"d":"A boy is shown as a young fisherman in a small upright panel of about 34 by 24 centimeters; the recorded subject is only the boy, and the title supplies the occasion. Henry Inman, best known for portraits, also painted genre pictures of children and rural life, which were popular with a public that liked sentimental scenes of childhood. The panel is dated about 1829, when Inman was in his late twenties and at the height of his New York career.","i":"Pictures of boys fishing, nutting or going to school were a sentimental favorite in nineteenth-century America, linking childhood with nature and rural simplicity. The title The Young Fisherman casts the boy as both a character and a type, the free-roaming American youth celebrated in the literature of Irving and, later, Twain. Such pictures also reflected a changing idea of childhood, valued for innocence and spontaneity.","c":"A small genre panel by a portrait specialist, whose charm lies largely in the boy and in the sympathy with which he is observed. Inman handles childhood with a tender, slightly sweet touch typical of the period, and the small, upright format gives the figure an intimate presence. It is a modest picture, without the finish of his portraits or the depth of later American genre painters like Mount, but pleasing in its easy naturalness."},"met:17660":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman, only about 8 by 5 centimeters, in watercolor on ivory, dated about 1827. Her lace, veil and necklace are the recorded details of dress. At this size the portrait is a personal keepsake, small enough for a locket or pocket case, intended to be carried, worn or kept beside the bed, and its painter, Henry Inman, was then among the most sought-after portraitists in New York.","i":"Veils and lace were the delicate trimmings of the genteel woman's costume, sheer fabrics that painters admired for their transparency against the skin. A miniature showing them lets the artist display a fine touch in the smallest areas; for the sitter, they conveyed taste and modesty, and the necklace adds a note of adornment. Keepsake portraits like this were exchanged at betrothals, weddings and partings, and often outlived their owners as family relics.","c":"At so small a scale a painter's skill lies in economy: a few touches of lace, a thin veil, a glinting necklace and, above all, a living face. Inman handles such challenges with the polish of a Jarvis-trained portraitist, though his miniatures of the late 1820s can seem a little formulaic. Still, a charming, well-judged little object that reveals much about taste, fashion and the intimacy of the likeness."},"met:11217":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed gentleman in watercolor on ivory, only about 7 by 5 centimeters, dated about 1830 to 1833. This falls toward the end of Henry Inman's work in the medium, as oil portraits and larger commissions took over his practice. Nothing is recorded of the sitter's dress or expression; the title names only his class, a gentleman.","i":"A gentleman in a portrait of this date is a man of the respectable middle class or above, whose coat, waistcoat and cravat signal business or professional standing. The miniature, once the usual likeness of such a man, was in these years giving way to the larger oil portrait and, after 1839, to the daguerreotype. As an unnamed sitter he stands for the many men of Jacksonian America whose faces are preserved while their names are lost.","c":"Inman's miniatures of these years are brisk and sure, and an unnamed sitter shows the painter's capacity to give a stranger a sense of character in a few square centimeters. The risk is blandness, and without a name the picture is easy to overlook, but the craft is real and the little ivory survives as an example of the skill of American miniaturists on the eve of photography."},"met:11213":{"d":"A miniature portrait of James Bogert Jr., in watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 6 centimeters, dated about 1835. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. Bogert is an old New York name of Dutch origin, and the Jr. identifies a family name passed from father to son. Henry Inman, one of the city's leading portraitists, was by this date painting most of his sitters in oil, so a miniature of this period is a late example of his work in the medium.","i":"In 1835 New York was the largest and fastest-growing city in the country, its families, many of Dutch descent, commissioning portraits as a sign of their place in a rising commercial society. Naming a sitter Jr. points to a household where the father shared his name and probably his standing. A miniature, small and personal, suggests a keepsake for a wife, fiancée or parent rather than a picture for public display.","c":"In his last miniatures Inman works with an economy that leaves the face to do the work, and a very small format concentrates attention further. What interests is the directness of a plain portrayal without ornament. The risk is routine, but in a good example the likeness feels alert. A well-made miniature from the end of a fashion, valuable chiefly for the name it preserves."},"nga:34056":{"d":"A man's upper body fills the canvas in a formal portrait: his head turns slightly to the right but his gaze meets the viewer's, the full face with prominent cheeks and a slight smile. His thick, dark hair is curly and neatly framed; he wears a dark coat, a white shirt with a high collar and a black bow tie, with a hint of gold chain beneath the coat, against a plain dark brown ground. The sitter is George Pope Morris, the poet, songwriter and editor, and the canvas measures about 76 by 64 centimeters.","i":"George Pope Morris (1802 to 1864) was one of the best-known literary men of New York: editor of the New-York Mirror and later the Home Journal, and the author of popular verses and songs, among them Woodman, Spare That Tree. Inman, well connected in the city's literary and theatrical circles, painted numerous writers and public figures, and a portrait of a man of letters presented him as cultured and prominent. The slight smile, unusual in portraits of the time, suggests a genial personality.","c":"A frank, handsome portrait in Inman's mature manner: the face is modeled with fluent, confident strokes, the dark coat against the dark ground sets it in strong relief, and the slight smile gives it life. The restricted palette is deliberate and elegant, though it leaves little to look at beyond the face. Inman's gift is for likeness and ease rather than psychological depth; for a poet and editor of sociable temper, that is just right."},"met:11215":{"d":"A portrait of Martin Van Buren, the eighth president of the United States, painted about 1837 to 1838, at the start of his single term in office, on a canvas of about 78 by 65 centimeters. Henry Inman, who had long been a leading portraitist of New York's political and literary circles, painted him; nothing is recorded of his dress or pose.","i":"Van Buren, a New Yorker from Kinderhook of Dutch descent, was Andrew Jackson's vice president and successor, a master political organizer nicknamed the Little Magician. He took office in 1837 just as the Panic of 1837 plunged the country into depression, and he lost his bid for reelection in 1840. A portrait of a sitting president was a public image as well as a likeness, presenting him as dignified, steady and capable to the audience of the day.","c":"Inman was the natural choice for a New York politician: his portraits are clear, dignified and quickly read. The result is a competent public image rather than a revealing one; the president's cultivated affability is probably present, his shrewdness less so. The likeness serves the historical record well, and the polished handling shows why Inman was the city's most sought-after portraitist of the 1830s."},"met:16906":{"d":"A portrait of Henry G. Stebbins, painted in 1838 on a canvas of about 76 by 61 centimeters, when the sitter was in his twenties. Stebbins became a New York banker and stockbroker and later served in Congress, so the picture records a young man at the start of a career. Only the sitter, a man, is recorded; his dress and pose are not described here.","i":"In the late 1830s a young New Yorker with ambitions in business sat for a portrait as a statement of arrival: it signaled respectability and credit, and it would stay in the family as a record of his place in a growing mercantile city. Inman's long list of sitters, businessmen, officials and writers, included many such men, whose portraits together form a picture of the city's rising professional class.","c":"A straightforward Inman portrait of a young businessman: clear, polished and without flourish, the kind of likeness on which his reputation rested. It shows the professional's ease and also the limit of his gift, since a sitter this young offers little of the weathered character that gives older faces their depth. Competent, agreeable and best prized for its record of a man who would become a public figure."},"cma:157240":{"d":"Companion portraits of Frederic Betts and Mary Ward Betts, presumably a married couple, painted in oil on canvases of about 88 by 69 centimeters in the 1830s by Henry Inman. They are listed together by name, without a description of dress or pose; the matching size, date and medium show that they were conceived as a pair, to hang as pendants, the husband's likeness on one side and the wife's on the other.","i":"Pendant portraits, a husband and wife on matching canvases, were the standard way of recording a marriage in early nineteenth-century America. Hung side by side, usually with the figures turned slightly toward each other, they declared the union and the family's standing. They were often inherited together and passed down as family relics, which is why so many pairs survive in museums with the sitters' full names recorded.","c":"A pair is more than the sum of its parts when the painter keeps the two in balance, and Inman, who worked fast and to a formula, does it with ease: the same scale, light and polish for each, a gentle rhyme of pose. The formula limits individuality, and both portraits carry a family resemblance of manner. But the matched pair is a lucid image of early nineteenth-century marriage as a civic institution."},"cma:157241":{"d":"A portrait of Frederic Betts, painted in oil on a canvas of about 88 by 69 centimeters in the 1830s. The size is larger than Henry Inman's usual bust-length canvas of about 76 by 64 centimeters, and suggests a commission from a family of means. Only the sitter's name is recorded, so his dress, pose and setting are not described here. It forms a pair with the portrait of Mary Ward Betts, presumably his wife.","i":"Inman, like other portraitists of the Jacksonian age, turned out from his busy New York studio a steady stream of portraits of merchants, lawyers and professionals, each recording a man's respectability for his family and posterity. Male sitters of the period were usually shown in a dark coat and white linen, presenting themselves as trustworthy citizens of a republic that prized character over display. A pendant portrait of the wife completed the family record.","c":"Inman's strength is a dependable, fluent likeness, and the larger size gives him room to develop it. His male portraits can lack the penetrating character of the best by Sully or Stuart, but they have an easy, polished ordinariness that suited the sitters. This one should be taken as a typical, well-made product of a busy and successful studio, valuable as a record of a New Yorker of the 1830s."},"cma:157242":{"d":"A portrait of Mary Ward Betts, painted in oil on a canvas of about 88 by 69 centimeters in the 1830s, as a companion to the portrait of Frederic Betts. Only her name is recorded; her dress and pose are not described here. Henry Inman, a leader of the New York portrait trade, painted her at a time when women's portraits often drew their color and texture from fashion, with silk, lace and ribbon.","i":"In the 1830s a woman's portrait registered her status through dress: silks, lace, jewelry and the careful arrangement of hair told viewers of her family's means and taste. Pendant portraits kept a couple together on the wall, a visual statement of marriage as partnership. Giving the wife her maiden name as well as her married one, as the title does, preserves the family lines the portrait was meant to commemorate.","c":"In women's portraits Inman had room to show his skill with fabric and softness: the shimmer of silk, the fall of lace, a flattering gloss to the skin. He tends toward sweetness rather than sharpness, which can flatten the sitter into a type. As part of a pair the picture is best read alongside her husband's, and as such it conveys the polished domestic dignity that the Betts family wished to project."},"met:11212":{"d":"A small oil portrait of a man whose identity is given only by his family: a gentleman of the Wilkes family, painted about 1838 to 1840 on a canvas of about 31 by 25 centimeters, much smaller than Inman's usual bust-length canvases of about 76 by 64 centimeters. At this scale the picture may have been a sketch, a study for a larger work, or a personal likeness made for a close friend or relative.","i":"Identified only as a gentleman of the family, the sitter is a reminder of how often the names behind early American portraits have been lost; the Wilkes attribution probably descends from the tradition of the picture's owners. A small portrait of an unnamed family member suggests a keepsake rather than a commission for public display, painted in the years when Inman was at the height of his New York practice.","c":"A small picture whose chief interest is the freshness that a reduced scale often gives Inman: a freer touch, direct handling and less of the polished surface that large commissions demanded. Without a name the sitter is a type, a well-dressed man of the late 1830s, but the likeness carries conviction. A modest, appealing work that shows Inman as a quick, sensitive observer."},"met:435645":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child against a gold ground in a panel of about 88 by 64 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood, probably in the 1440s. Jacopo Bellini, the Venetian painter whose sons Gentile and Giovanni would transform painting in Venice, is far better known for his sketchbooks of drawings than for panels, so a Madonna of this size is a rare survival.","i":"The Madonna and Child on gold was the most common devotional image in Venice and the Veneto, made for houses, churches and confraternities. Jacopo, a pupil of Gentile da Fabriano, brings the courtly elegance of International Gothic to the type while taking an interest in the new Florentine ideas of solid form that he had seen in 1423. The Virgin's tender attention to her Child, the central human note, makes the image both an icon and a family picture.","c":"Jacopo is a mediator between Gentile's gilded grace and the geometry of the Renaissance, and a Madonna on gold shows him still tied to the first. The result is refined and sweetly decorative, but the figures lack the sculptural weight and psychological force that his son Giovanni would later bring to the same subject. Its interest lies partly in where it points: the Bellini family's long preoccupation with the Madonna and Child."},"nga:71868":{"d":"Two saints stand side by side beneath a painted grey arch, in a rocky landscape, each turned a little to the right. At the left, Saint Anthony Abbot, with a long white beard, a black robe and a red cap, reads a book and holds a wooden staff. At the right, the gaunt, balding Saint Bernardino of Siena, in a brown friar's habit, holds a large circular plaque with a golden sun on black and lettering at the centre and around the rim. Red and grey crags, a distant building and a thin tree stand under a blue sky.","i":"Anthony Abbot, the hermit of the Egyptian desert, is regarded as the father of monasticism, and his staff and book show his authority as a teacher of the solitary life. Bernardino of Siena, a Franciscan preacher who died in 1444 and was canonised in 1450, promoted devotion to the Holy Name of Jesus, which he displayed to crowds on a plaque like the one here. The panel, painted nine years after his canonisation, may have formed part of a larger altarpiece.","c":"Jacopo Bellini is better known for his two great books of drawings, with their daring perspectives, than for his few surviving panels, and this one is characteristically sober. The two saints have a solid, sculptural presence, and the faces are sharply individual. But they do not interact, and the dry tempera surface lacks the glow his sons would bring to Venetian painting. It is a dignified, rather austere record of Venetian painting before Giovanni Bellini."},"met:13204":{"d":"A bust- or half-length portrait of a young woman, Sarah Ursula Rose, painted in oil on canvas in 1756, about 74 by 59 centimetres. The artist was Benjamin West, then about eighteen, a self-taught painter working in Pennsylvania. Portraits by colonial painters of this date were usually modelled on English prints and showed the sitter in her best silk dress, and the picture shows the painter's early, provincial manner.","i":"West was born in Pennsylvania in 1738 and began painting without formal training. After going to Italy in 1760 and settling in London in 1763, he became historical painter to George III and the second president of the Royal Academy. A portrait from his American years such as this one is therefore a document of his beginnings, made for a local family and showing how colonial painters adapted European fashions in dress and pose. Little is recorded about Sarah Ursula Rose herself.","c":"An early work by a painter who would later become famous for history pictures, this portrait is naturally modest. The self-taught hand probably shows in simplified drawing and flat modelling, but the likeness is earnest and the design clear. It is valued more for what it tells about West's beginnings than for its quality. Seen beside Copley's mature American portraits, which show far greater command of texture and character, it is plainly the work of a beginner."},"nga:50264":{"d":"A portrait of Dr. Samuel Boude, painted in 1755 or 1756 on a canvas of about 90 by 77 centimeters, a companion in size and date to the portrait of his wife, Mary Bethel Boude. Benjamin West, then a teenager of about seventeen or eighteen, was working as a portraitist in the American colonies, years before he left for Italy and then London to become the leading history painter of the Royal Academy. Only the sitter, a man, is recorded; his dress and pose are not described here.","i":"Colonial America had few trained portraitists and many families who wanted a likeness to mark their standing, so a talented teenager could find steady work. A doctor such as Boude belonged to the educated elite of a provincial town, and a portrait paired with his wife's declared his place in it. West learned from the prints and local painters available to him, and his early portraits probably borrow formulas from British mezzotints, adjusting them to colonial taste.","c":"Judged as a teenager's work it is competent, with a clear likeness and the formulas of the day absorbed from prints, but stiffness and flat modeling, typical of colonial portraiture, are to be expected. None of the grand rhetoric of West's later history paintings is yet present. Its interest is as the starting point of the career of the first American artist to win a European reputation."},"nga:50265":{"d":"A woman shown from the waist up stands before a hazy landscape, her body angled slightly to the right and her face turned to the left as she looks at the viewer from the corners of dark gray eyes. She wears a gold-colored gown with a low-cut, ruffle-lined bodice tied with a pink bow, elbow-length sleeves with broad ruffled cuffs, and a dusky rose shawl; her brown hair is pulled back with one curl over her shoulder. Her left arm rests on a ledge and she holds a dark pink flower. The canvas is about 90 by 76 centimeters.","i":"Mary Bethel Boude was the wife of Dr. Samuel Boude, and her portrait was painted as a pendant to his. The gold gown, ruffles and flower belong to the fashionable repertoire of mid-eighteenth-century English portraits, adopted in the colonies from mezzotints: a woman shown as graceful, cultivated and decorative, with the flower an emblem of beauty and youth. The landscape background, rather than an interior, is also a convention imported from English portraiture.","c":"A young painter's competent essay in a type: the oval face, the sideways glance and the gold gown give the sitter an air of poise, and the pink accents of bow, shawl and flower are prettily orchestrated. The stylized features and generalized modeling make the likeness more a convention than a character study, but the picture has charm and a surprising sense of design for a painter of eighteen. A valuable early West."},"nga:177432":{"d":"A seated woman in a spruce-blue garment embraces a tearful, winged boy in a round painting about 122 centimeters across. Both have smooth, flushed, golden-haired features. She curls both arms around the nearly nude Cupid, who turns back to look at his hand, held in hers, while his other hand lifts to his eyes and a tear gleams on his cheek. A gray dove feeds another at Cupid's feet beside a pink rose, and in the distance three chubby babies tussle in a garden near a beehive, from which bees swarm.","i":"The subject comes from a Greek poem attributed to Anacreon: the boy god Cupid, stung by a bee while stealing honey, runs to his mother Venus, who asks how much more his own arrows must hurt those they wound. It was a popular conceit of the eighteenth century, treating love's pain as playful and moralizing about the pains Cupid inflicts. West's version, with Venus as a tender mother, softens the joke into a scene of maternal comfort.","c":"West's Neoclassical manner is gently graceful here: the interlaced bodies of mother and son make a compact circle that suits the round format, the drawing is soft and clear, and the tears are touchingly comic. The surface is pale and sweet; a viewer expecting the heroic seriousness of West's history paintings will find instead an easy, decorative piece in a lighter mode. As a design it is skillful, as an emotion it is slight."},"cma:109245":{"d":"A young woman and a small boy press their cheeks together inside a painted circle, a round picture set within a square canvas with dark corners. She wears a brown cloth wound around her dark hair and edged in thin gold, a brown mantle and a green shawl embroidered with gold thread, and she gazes out with a slight smile. Her arm curves around the boy, whose reddish-brown hair and white shirt are lit softly, and who looks out with wide, serious eyes. At the left a patch of grey cloudy sky shows beyond them. The warm browns and greens are subdued, the faces are lit and rounded, and the handling is smooth and soft.","i":"The sitters are the painter's wife and their son. West was the first American artist to study in Italy, where he spent three years before settling in London for good, and he so admired Raphael that he named his eldest son after him. The museum says he imitated Raphael's celebrated Madonna of the Chair when composing this tender double portrait, so mother and child take the place of the Virgin and Christ child in a round picture. Painted about 1770, it turns a religious formula into family affection. West became court painter to George III and a founder of the Royal Academy.","c":"The pose is borrowed, but the feeling is not. Mother and son lean cheek to cheek with an informality that West's large history paintings rarely allow, and the child's watchful stare keeps the picture from sweetness. The weaknesses are plain: the borrowed composition is handled a little dutifully, the faces are rather waxy, and the dark roundel leaves the surroundings murky. As a showpiece of West's skill it is modest, but as a record of a family it is warm, and the Raphael connection is cleverly personal."},"nga:569":{"d":"A white man in a scarlet overcoat and moccasins sits propped against a rock, a patterned animal skin across his chest and a beaded, feathered cap on his knee, looking off to the left, a musket held like a walking stick. Behind him, in shadow, a standing Mohawk man in a dark cloak, with beaded strap, bracelets, feathered headdress and large gold earrings, holds a long painted stick that puffs smoke like a pipe and points subtly left, where, through a break in the rocks, small figures gather by a campfire before a waterfall. The canvas is about 202 by 138 centimeters.","i":"The sitters are Colonel Guy Johnson, the British superintendent of Indian affairs, and Karonghyontye, called Captain David Hill, a Mohawk leader who accompanied him to London. West painted the pair in 1776, as the American colonies broke with Britain. The picture presents the British and Mohawk alliance: Johnson wears elements of Mohawk dress and Karonghyontye stands as a counselor at his shoulder, in a landscape of waterfall and camp. It is a diplomatic image as well as a portrait.","c":"A highly unusual and effective double portrait for its time: West sets a colonial official and a Native leader together, the first in the light, in a red coat with Native touches, the second in shadow behind him but with a pointing hand that directs the viewer's eye into the landscape. The picture can be read as celebrating an alliance and as projecting imperial paternalism. A powerful document, historically richer than most portraits, and ably staged."},"nga:45885":{"d":"Seen from a low viewpoint over choppy water, rowboats crammed with men fight and jostle amid large wooden ships. In a central boat, two men in white shirts lean over the stern to haul submerged men from the water while another rescuer cradles a limp figure. At the right, a man in a fawn-brown uniform with gold trim and a red-feathered hat brandishes a sword and seizes the shirttails of a man in blue, who steps into another boat. At left, boats sit in orderly rows, and flame and smoke pour from a ship beyond. The canvas is about 153 by 214 centimeters.","i":"The Battle of La Hogue (1692) was a major English naval victory over the French, which ended the threat of a French-backed invasion to restore the exiled James II; in its final act English boats burned French ships beached at La Hogue. For eighteenth-century Britain it was a proud memory, and West, historical painter to George III, painted it in 1778, the year France entered the war on the side of the American colonies, when the old enemy was again at hand.","c":"West gives a famous victory a swarming, confused immediacy: the low viewpoint puts the viewer amid the boats, the rescue vignettes draw the eye to individual drama, and the crowd of bodies is handled with command. It is cluttered, and without a single focus the action risks dissolving into incident; but the energy is real, and the anecdotal human detail belongs to West's innovation in British history painting. A lively, ambitious work that rewards close inspection."},"cma:129300":{"d":"King George III is shown in a suit of armour with an ermine-trimmed cape over his shoulder, on a canvas of about 127 by 101 centimetres. Benjamin West, the American-born painter to the king, painted the portrait in 1783. The armour dresses the king as a military leader, and the ermine, the white fur that trims royal robes, adds the dignity of kingship.","i":"George III never led troops into battle, so the armour is symbolic, borrowed from a long tradition of royal portraiture. The museum notes that the king commissioned more than eighty paintings from West and that this one, which celebrates his military leadership, was probably meant to bolster a reputation tarnished in 1782 when he had to recognise American independence. It is ironic that West, born in Pennsylvania, who remained in royal service throughout the war, was the painter chosen to restore his image.","c":"A competent piece of image-making: the armour, ermine and pose speak the old language of kingship clearly, and West, who knew the court well, could give the king the composure he wanted. The risk is stiffness, and the king's character is hard to find beneath the costume, which beside the more natural portraits of Gainsborough or Zoffany looks like theatre. As propaganda it is deft; as painting it is capable rather than inspired."},"cma:99445":{"d":"Women in classical dress paint vases, spin and weave in this oil sketch on paper, mounted on wood, about 51 by 65 centimetres. It shows British industry through the figures of ancient Greece, and it is one of nine related sketches for a larger decorative scheme. West made the sketch in 1791 as a design for a large mural in the Queen's Lodge at Windsor Castle.","i":"As part of his work for the British court, West produced compositions celebrating the strength of the nation's economy. Here the comparison is explicit: modern women's trades of vase painting, spinning and weaving are dressed as antiquity, setting British manufacturing beside the accomplishments of Greece and Rome. The vase painting may recall the neoclassical pottery of the day. The mural was lost when the Queen's Lodge was remodelled in 1823, so sketches like this one are valuable evidence of the scheme.","c":"Oil sketches let West be freer than in his finished pictures, and this one has an easy rhythm of figures and a decorative clarity. The conceit of classical maidens doing the work of Britain's factories is graceful and flattering but idealised: the real conditions of industry, which relied heavily on child and low-paid labour, are absent. As design it is skilful; as social picture it is a patriotic fantasy."},"nga:70986":{"d":"A blond angel in white, coral-red cloth billowing from one arm, gestures with raised arms over Adam and Eve as they are driven from a bank of clouds into a flat, barren landscape. Eve kneels in an ivory animal-skin toga, her auburn hair blown, looking up with open mouth and gripping Adam's arm; Adam, in a chestnut fur garment, hides his face with one hand. A dagger-like spear of light thrusts from the clouds. Thistles grow in the foreground, a striped serpent lifts its head at right, a lion attacks two horses, and an eagle swoops on a heron. The canvas is about 187 by 278 centimeters.","i":"Genesis 3 tells how God drove Adam and Eve from Eden after they ate the forbidden fruit; Milton's Paradise Lost, widely illustrated in eighteenth-century Britain, has the archangel Michael lead them out. Here the wider world changes with them: a lion attacks horses and an eagle seizes a heron, signs that death and violence have entered nature, while thistles, the thorns of the cursed ground, grow at their feet. West paints the Fall as the beginning of the human story of suffering.","c":"West's Expulsion is a vigorous exercise in the Romantic sublime, with the sweeping diagonal of the angel's gesture, the thunderous gold and cream clouds and the stricken, half-nude figures. The animal predators are an inventive touch. The poses are theatrical, close to stage tableau, and the drawing of Adam and Eve is more academic than anguished. Its grandeur is somewhat generic, but it demonstrates West's command of large-scale composition."},"met:13198":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 91 by 71 centimetres, painted around 1793, shows a damsel and the knight Orlando, with a child among the figures, in a scene from chivalric romance. The Met's keywords list Roland (Orlando), a man, a woman and a child. West, by then historical painter to George III, turned in these years to literary and religious subjects as well as the history pictures that made his name.","i":"Orlando, the French Roland, is the hero of Charlemagne's wars and the central figure of Ariosto's Orlando Furioso, in which his love for Angelica drives him mad. The title suggests an encounter between the knight and a damsel, of the kind common in romance. Romance subjects were fashionable in Britain in the 1790s, when readers were rediscovering Spenser and Ariosto and painters sought subjects beyond classical history.","c":"West's gift was for clear, dramatic grouping, and a literary subject gave him a chance to use it. But his romances lack the imaginative strangeness of Fuseli's or Blake's pictures of the same years. The result is competent, theatrical and a little cool. It is interesting for what it shows of West's range rather than as one of his best."},"nga:34149":{"d":"A portrait of Elizabeth, Countess of Effingham, painted about 1797 on a large canvas of about 146 by 116 centimeters. Her rank and name are recorded, but not her dress, pose or setting. Benjamin West painted it when he was President of the Royal Academy and best known for history paintings; portraits of aristocratic sitters by him are rarer, which makes a noblewoman's portrait something of a special case.","i":"A countess's portrait of the 1790s presented rank, taste and good character: the sitter would be shown in fashionable dress, with landscape or drapery to suggest her place in aristocratic society. Such pictures were commissions that recorded a family's standing for its houses and descendants, and a portrait by West, an American-born painter at the head of the Royal Academy, carried extra prestige.","c":"West's portraits are rarer and more variable than his history pictures, and this one is best judged as the work of a master of grand composition who brings dignity rather than intimacy to a countess. The scale and format promise ceremonial presence; the risk of such commissions is the stiffness of the official manner. Next to the sparkle of Lawrence or the warmth of Reynolds, West's aristocrats can seem cool, but he gives them clarity and weight."},"nga:34071":{"d":"A woman sits with her head turned slightly to the right, her dark brown hair in an updo adorned with blue ribbons and pearls, a pearl necklace at her throat. She wears a dark blue dress with lace at the low neckline and wide sleeves, holds a large open book with a yellow cover on her lap, and has a guitar-like instrument leaning beside her. At left hang red drapery and a cascade of colorful flowers; behind her stand large off-white buildings with tall columns, and beyond them a green hill covered with peacocks and trees beneath puffy clouds. The canvas is about 146 by 115 centimeters.","i":"Book, instrument and flowers mark the accomplished woman of the 1790s: reading, music and gardening were the polite arts of her class, and a portrait that gathered them presented her as cultivated and virtuous. The classical buildings and the peacocks set her in an idealized landscape, part of the fashion for portraits that mingled likeness and allegory; the peacock, an emblem of beauty and, in myth, of Juno, may flatter the sitter's elegance, though the picture does not explain them.","c":"The pleasure of the portrait is in its accessories: the draped red cloth, the garland of flowers, the pearls, the classical buildings and the peacock-covered hill make a stage for the sitter. The face itself is neutral, almost impassive, and the figure sits rather than lives; the whole is more a sumptuous arrangement than a probing likeness. For West, who rarely painted such portraits, it is an interesting, if somewhat cool, exercise in the British grand manner."},"met:13200":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 50 by 73 centimetres, painted in oil on wood in 1801. Moses, the lawgiver, is shown the Promised Land, and the Met's keywords record an angel as well. In the biblical account (Deuteronomy 34), God leads Moses to the top of Mount Nebo and shows him the whole land that he will not enter, before his death. A wide panel suits a vast prospect, and the subject is as much about the view as about the figures.","i":"Moses led the Israelites out of Egypt but was forbidden to enter the land of Canaan, seeing it only from Mount Nebo. Christian readers saw in this an image of the Law giving way to the Gospel, with Joshua, whose name is the same as Jesus in Greek, leading the people into the land. West, born into a Quaker family and the painter of many biblical subjects, treated such stories as lessons in faith and providence.","c":"Biblical subjects with a vast prospect suit West's taste for grand, rhetorical effects, and a small panel may be a more successful scale for him than the walls of his big pictures, since the paint can be handled freely. The solemn angel and the lonely prophet make a simple, clear image. But his religious pictures can feel official, more correct than felt, and this lacks the visionary strangeness of Blake. It is a dignified, rather conventional treatment."},"met:13199":{"d":"A large upright canvas, nearly two metres high, shows a woman, a boy and an angel, the biblical Hagar and her son Ishmael after their banishment by Abraham. The presence of the angel suggests the episode in the wilderness when God hears the boy's cry and promises to save him. The painting is dated 1776 and was reworked in 1803, so it carries the marks of two moments in West's long career.","i":"In Genesis 21, Hagar, an Egyptian servant, and her son are sent into the wilderness with only bread and water. When the water runs out she sets the boy under a bush and weeps, and an angel calls from heaven, tells her not to fear and shows her a well. The story is read as an image of God's care for the outcast. Dutch and Italian painters had favoured it, and West's version, painted in London, gives it a large, public scale.","c":"West arranges mother, son and angel in a clear, emotional triangle, and the large scale gives the scene weight. The chief danger is rhetoric: West's pathos can feel staged, with gestures larger than the feeling. Reworking the canvas nearly three decades later may also have left stylistic seams. It is a capable history painting that earns respect rather than affection."},"met:13202":{"d":"A large canvas of 179 by 204 centimetres, painted in 1809, takes its title from Virgil: love conquers all. It gathers Venus, a woman and animals, an eagle, a pigeon, a horse and a lion, to show the power of love in the three elements, presumably earth, air and water. The creatures of land and air are the ones the Met's keywords record, and the picture is conceived as a decorative, allegorical showpiece.","i":"The phrase omnia vincit Amor comes from Virgil's tenth Eclogue, and artists since the Renaissance have shown Cupid and Venus mastering gods and beasts alike. The eagle was the bird of Jupiter, the dove the bird of Venus, the lion a symbol of strength and fierceness, and the horse of pride, so the animals show even the strongest creatures yielding to love. West painted it near the end of his life, when he returned to mythological and allegorical themes.","c":"At about seventy West was still ambitious, and this large allegory shows his lasting taste for the grand, classical manner. The scheme is ingenious, with beasts of three elements brought under love's sway, but the execution risks being more illustration than inspiration, a cold neoclassical rhetoric of pose and gesture. The animals give some liveliness, and the scale is impressive. It is an interesting late work rather than a persuasive one."},"nga:177101":{"d":"Two men, one old and one young, stand at the lower left before a dark, stormy sea in which a large ship tilts against rocky cliffs; the young man holds out a hand toward a meadow dotted with flowers, where a woman at the front of a group stretches an arm toward them. Three women sit at her feet, five cluster behind her hugging one another, and four more dance at the right with arms raised and knees bent, in draperies of red, yellow, blue and purple. Tall olive-green trees stand behind, and yellow streaks break through a dark, magenta-edged sky. The canvas is about 104 by 149 centimeters.","i":"The subject comes from Fénelon's Telemachus (1699), the most widely read moral romance of the eighteenth century: the young son of Odysseus, shipwrecked with his tutor Mentor, is welcomed on Calypso's island by the goddess and her nymphs, where pleasure threatens his quest for his father. The rescue from shipwreck into an enchanted garden of women is the lesson in miniature: virtue must resist the seductions of ease. Painters from the Rococo to Neoclassicism turned to Fénelon for edifying subjects with a touch of the erotic.","c":"By 1809 West was seventy-one and long established as the senior British history painter, and Telemachus and Calypso shows his late manner: broad, smoothly handled groups, generalized Neoclassical figures, vivid color and a dramatic sky. The women are graceful but interchangeable, the composition is a procession more than a drama, and the stormy sea provides stronger interest than the people. Still, as a decorative piece of literary classicism it has energy and brightness."},"met:435762":{"d":"A small oil panel, only about 22 by 17 centimetres, shows the Virgin Mary with the Christ Child. Its size and subject are those of a picture made for private devotion, to be kept at home and prayed before. Dieric Bouts, who worked in Leuven, usually gives his Madonnas slender proportions, smooth oval faces and a grave, inward calm, and paints with the smooth, enamel-like finish that oil technique brought to Netherlandish art. The Met dates the panel to the later 1450s.","i":"A Madonna and Child was the commonest subject of private Netherlandish devotion: the mother and her infant son presented the Incarnation in a form a viewer could meditate on closely. The gestures between the two figures, how the Child is held or looks out, are usually where such pictures find their feeling, and painters like Bouts often loaded small details with symbolic meaning. The panel was probably made for a lay patron who prayed before it daily, not for a church.","c":"Bouts's small Madonnas are quieter and more austere than those of Rogier van der Weyden, whom he followed, and more concerned with stillness than with incident. They reward patient looking but can seem reserved to the point of aloofness. This panel, close to his usual manner, offers the precise finish and calm affect that made him a valued painter in Leuven and keep him respected today."},"met:436284":{"d":"A small oil panel, about 22 by 18 centimetres, shows a man presented by a saint. The man is a donor, a patron who paid for devotional images, and the saint stands at his side as his intercessor. Panels of this kind were often one half of a diptych whose companion showed the Virgin and Child, so that the donor appeared to pray in their presence. The work is attributed to the circle of Dieric Bouts, not to the master himself, which points to a close follower working in his manner.","i":"Donor portraits let patrons place themselves in sacred company. Presented by a saint, probably a name saint, the sitter is introduced to the holy figures that would have appeared on a companion panel. The portrait aimed to record a likeness and to speak of the donor's piety and standing; the title does not give his name. Bouts's own portraits are notable for their sober, individualised faces, and his followers kept up that type.","c":"A refined little portrait panel in which the face carries the interest, as it did in Bouts's school: attentive, unflattering and quietly grave, like someone at prayer. The type is conventional, and as a work of the circle rather than the master's own hand it may fall short of his finest portraits in subtlety. Still, it conveys the precise, unglamorous realism that Netherlandish portraiture prized, and its small size suits its intimate purpose."},"nga:69387":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up holding a nude infant on a white cloth, in a tiny vertical panel, only about 23 by 16 centimetres. She has reddish-blond hair parted in the middle and a dark blue robe edged in gold drawn over her head, and looks down at the child, holding him in both long-fingered hands. The baby, his head covered with fine fuzz, looks off to the left, grips the cloth in one hand and rests the other on his round belly, one foot stretched across her wrist. The background is dark brown, and cracking shows throughout.","i":"Tiny panels such as this, small enough to hold in the hand, were made for private prayer, and the intimate, human treatment of the Virgin and Child invited the viewer into a quiet moment of tenderness. Dirck Bouts, born in Haarlem and active in Leuven, followed the delicate, precise manner of Rogier van der Weyden. The blue of Mary's robe was a costly colour traditionally reserved for her, and the simple white linen belongs to the humble domestic detail the Netherlandish painters loved.","c":"A small jewel of Netherlandish devotion: the delicate faces, the tender, protective hands and the baby's lively gesture are painted with great fineness, and the dark ground focuses attention on the pair. Bouts's figures are quieter and more still than Rogier's, and the Virgin's long fingers and pointed chin are characteristic, if a little stylised. Cracking across the surface mars it, but the picture's intimacy and freshness remain."},"met:435761":{"d":"A small portrait in oil on wood, about 30 by 22 centimetres, shows an unnamed man. The museum records only that it is a portrait of a man, so his dress and setting cannot be described with confidence. Netherlandish portraits of the mid-fifteenth century, Bouts's among them, typically show the sitter close to the picture plane, in three-quarter view, with minute attention to the face and a sober, undecorated background. The format suggests a private commission for a prosperous townsman.","i":"Independent portraits had been established in the Netherlands for a generation by Jan van Eyck and Rogier van der Weyden, and by about 1470 they were a form open to wealthy burghers, officials and merchants as well as courtiers. Such a panel recorded a person's features and standing for family and friends, valued as a lifelike record, not an idealisation. The sitter's name is not given here, and no emblem or inscription is recorded to tell more.","c":"Bouts's portraits are quieter than Rogier's and rarely flatter: the face is studied with an almost impersonal patience, giving the effect of a man observed with great attention and little drama. That is both the appeal and the limit, since the sitter seems present but withdrawn, and there is no symbolic or narrative hook. Among the few portraits attributed to Bouts, this belongs to the sober, attentive type."},"met:435763":{"d":"Two linked devotional images are paired as a diptych: the Virgin in sorrow, weeping, and Christ crowned with thorns, shown as the Man of Sorrows. They are placed together so that the viewer shares in the Virgin's grief and Christ's suffering. The pictures are catalogued as made after a composition by Dieric Bouts, the Leuven painter whose paired images of the suffering Christ and the grieving Virgin were widely copied, and are assigned to around 1530, more than fifty years after his death.","i":"Paired images of the suffering Christ and the sorrowing Virgin were a favourite form of late medieval private devotion. The believer, praying before them, was meant to feel compassion for both: to look on the wounded face of Christ and share his mother's sorrow. Bouts's version of the type, with the figures close to the viewer, was copied many times. Because this diptych is a copy, it tells more about the demand for such images than about the painter's own hand.","c":"As a copy it is a competent piece of devotional workmanship. The sentiment of the original survives in the poses and the visible tears, though copies of this kind seldom keep the finer modelling and enamel-like finish of the master's own panels, and the faces tend to repeat a formula. Its value lies in showing how far a successful devotional design could travel, and how long it kept its power to move."},"cma:109244":{"d":"A landscape in the Housatonic River valley of western Massachusetts and Connecticut, on a canvas of about 51 by 61 centimeters, painted in the late 1850s, when Homer Dodge Martin was in his early twenties and working in the manner of the Hudson River School. The title places the picture among the valley views that artists sought in the Berkshire hills and the river's meadows.","i":"The Housatonic Valley was one of the most painted landscapes in the Northeast: its river, meadows and wooded hills lay within a day's travel of New York, and writers such as Hawthorne and Melville lived in the Berkshires in the 1850s. Painters sought pastoral harmony there, and buyers took such pictures as emblems of New England's settled beauty. Martin would later move away from this clear, detailed manner toward a more atmospheric, tonal approach.","c":"An early picture by a painter who later found his own voice: the clear light, firm drawing and careful detail of the Hudson River School are here, and the harmony of valley, hills and sky shows an eye already attuned to atmosphere. It lacks the poetic simplification of his mature work, in which detail dissolves into mood, and so it is more conventional than characteristic. Fresh and agreeable, but best seen as a starting point."},"met:11515":{"d":"A wide landscape of the shore of Lake Ontario, with trees and a lake among the recorded subjects, on a large canvas of about 91 by 154 centimeters. The date, given as 1883 to 1887, overlaps the years in which Homer Dodge Martin lived in Normandy and returned to the United States, and suggests a picture worked and reworked over time. The title places the viewer behind the dunes, presumably looking out over low sandy ground toward the water.","i":"Martin began as a Hudson River School painter but turned in the 1880s toward the tonal, evocative manner of the French Barbizon painters, whose work he knew from Europe: forms softened, light diffused, mood preferred to detail. A view behind the dunes of Lake Ontario, a quiet, unspectacular shore, is the kind of subject this approach suited, since it relies on atmosphere rather than grandeur. American collectors of the late 1880s were beginning to value such poetic restraint.","c":"A major late Martin and a fine example of American tonalism: dunes, trees and lake dissolve into a muted harmony of color, and the horizontal format enlarges the sense of calm. The result is melancholy, spacious and poetic; the risk is vagueness, since softened forms and a low key can seem monotonous. A quiet, contemplative picture by a painter at the peak of his late style."},"cma:104793":{"d":"A wild stretch of the Newport, Rhode Island, coast, on a canvas of about 59 by 92 centimeters, dated 1889; the title promises a rocky, wave-beaten shore rather than the resort's fashionable beaches and mansions. Homer Dodge Martin, in his last decade, was painting in the soft, atmospheric manner he had developed in the 1880s, and a coastal subject let him combine rock, water and sky in a low-key, tonal harmony.","i":"Newport was the fashionable summer capital of the American rich, but its outer coast, with rocks and surf, offered a wilder view that painters had long prized; Kensett and Haseltine had both painted the Rhode Island shoreline. A picture titled Wild Coast sets the resort's polish against the sea's indifference, presenting the shore as a place of elemental force. Painters of Martin's circle turned to such places for a poetic, even spiritual response to nature.","c":"With Martin the coast becomes a mood: cool, grayed colors, simplified rocks and a sky pressed close, so that the picture feels remembered more than observed. Its strength is the unity of the tonal harmony; its limit is abstraction, a vagueness in the rocks that denies the pleasure of the detailed observation found in Haseltine's rocky coasts. A poetic, subdued picture that rewards patient looking."},"met:11517":{"d":"A Norman manor house among trees, on a canvas of about 64 by 95 centimeters, dated broadly from about 1882 to 1892. Criqueboeuf is a village in Normandy, near the coast of the English Channel, and Homer Dodge Martin lived and worked in Normandy for several years in the early 1880s; the old manor with its trees is typical of the quiet rural subjects he painted there, in a style close to that of the Barbizon painters.","i":"Normandy drew many American and European landscape painters in the later nineteenth century, for its light, its coast and its ancient farms; for Martin it was where he absorbed the lessons of Corot and the Barbizon painters. A Norman manor, weathered and half-hidden by trees, is a picture of continuity and age, the antithesis of the new American cities he had left, and the kind of subject that suited his move toward mood over detail.","c":"A sympathetic, low-keyed picture whose best qualities are quiet harmony, a sense of weathered age and a calm that never becomes picturesque. The manor's forms are simplified and softened, giving a poetic generalization; the price is a lack of particular observation, so the viewer senses mood but not place. A representative example of Martin's tonal manner, and a gentler cousin of the Barbizon painters he admired."},"met:11518":{"d":"Trees, a river and a wide sky of clouds make up this view on the Seine in Normandy, on a canvas of about 73 by 104 centimeters, dated 1893 to 1895. The subtitle Harp of the Winds borrows a musical image for the sound of wind in the trees, as if the branches were the strings of an Aeolian harp. Homer Dodge Martin painted it late in his life, probably from memory of the French landscape he had known a decade earlier.","i":"The Aeolian harp, a stringed instrument sounded by the wind, was a Romantic emblem of nature's own music and of the poet's receptiveness to it. Martin's title applies it to a stand of trees, inviting viewers to hear as well as see, a synesthetic idea shared by tonalist painters, who compared their art to music. Painted only a couple of years before his death in 1897, the picture has often been read as an elegiac testament to a lifetime of looking at trees and sky.","c":"Harp of the Winds is generally considered Martin's finest picture and one of the high points of American tonalism: the trees blur into a veil of gray-green, the river and sky echo each other, and the whole holds its note like a held chord. The softness comes close to vagueness, and anyone seeking the crisp detail of Hudson River landscape will be disappointed. Those who meet its mood find a serene, haunting picture."},"met:436986":{"d":"A woman is shown with a book, a sign of devotion or literacy, on a small panel of about 48 by 43 centimeters, painted about 1520. The sitter is unnamed. Quentin Massys, the leading painter of Antwerp in these years, made portraits that rank among the strongest of the early sixteenth-century Netherlands, rendering faces with a patient, unflinching look and hands that are structurally convincing.","i":"Books in portraits of women often signal piety, a prayer book or book of hours, and the learning and decorum expected of a respectable wife or widow. In Antwerp, a rich and cosmopolitan commercial city, merchants' wives commissioned portraits as marks of status; they may have been paired with a husband's portrait as a diptych. The sitter's name has not survived, an absence that is common.","c":"Massys trusts the sitter's presence to carry the picture, and on this scale it does: a sober, steady likeness with strong light and a refusal to flatter. If it lacks the dramatic expression of his grotesques, it gains in dignity. A fine small example of Netherlandish portrait realism tempered by Italian clarity."},"nga:52622":{"d":"A pair of lovers who do not match, the title says, in a wide panel of about 43 by 63 centimeters. The subject is the unequal couple, a satirical theme of the early sixteenth century, usually pairing an ugly old man with a young woman, in which his money buys her attention and she may be robbing him as he leers. Quentin Massys, the leading painter of Antwerp in his day, painted it about 1520 to 1525, in a time when such caricatures of human folly were fashionable in humanist circles.","i":"The ill-matched couple was a favorite satire in Northern art around 1500: an old man foolish with desire, a young woman more interested in his purse than his person, and a moral for the viewer about vanity, avarice and the folly of age. Erasmus's circle, with which Massys was connected, was fond of such humanist satire on human folly. The theme also drew on the Italian taste for caricature, notably Leonardo's drawings of grotesque heads, which circulated in copies.","c":"Massys can be brutal and funny, and when he is, the grotesque heads and sharp characterization give such pictures a bite that more polite genre painting lacks. The danger of the unequal couple is that the joke is one-note, a caricature without compassion, and it asks the viewer to laugh at the old and to suspect the young. An unsettling, bracing picture and a reminder of the harsher side of Antwerp's Renaissance."},"met:436984":{"d":"The three Magi, the kings of the East, present gifts to the infant Christ in the presence of Mary and Joseph, in an upright panel of about 103 by 80 centimeters, dated 1526. Two are named, Melchior and Caspar, as tradition has it. Quentin Massys, then about sixty and the leading painter of Antwerp, staged the Nativity as a courtly ceremony, in the rich costume and fine detail that Antwerp patrons admired.","i":"The Adoration of the Magi (Matthew 2) was the most splendid of the Christmas subjects, welcoming the Gentile world to Christ: the three kings, named by tradition Caspar, Melchior and Balthasar, came to represent the three ages of man and the three known continents. Antwerp's merchants, who traded across the world, saw in the exotic kings an image of their own wealth in the service of faith. The gifts, gold, frankincense and myrrh, point to Christ's kingship, divinity and death.","c":"Massys late in life blends Netherlandish detail with Italianate monumentality, and the Adoration shows both: rich fabrics, jewelry and faces of expressive character, together with a more rounded, sculptural handling of the figures. The risk is a certain overcrowding and decorative fussiness, so that the picture can read as a display of costumes. But the tenderness of the main group and the individuality of the kings give it life. A fine work from the last years of his career."},"nga:163184":{"d":"Eight musicians and onlookers gather closely around a carpet-covered table, shown from the waist up in lapis blue, coral red, buttercup yellow, lilac and moss green, the satin-like fabrics falling in crisp folds. At the left a man in crimson and black, his back turned, holds a bass viol and points with his bow to an open music book; across the table a man plays a violin, a woman at the center a bandora and another woman a lute. A smiling young man behind them lifts a glass and touches a finger to his lips, and a couple stands in the background. The canvas is about 124 by 205 centimeters.","i":"Music-making companies were a favorite subject of the Utrecht Caravaggisti, who brought back from Rome Caravaggio's taste for half-length figures close to the viewer. The viol, violin, bandora and lute were instruments of polite amateur music, and the music books suggest part-singing and playing from notation, a refined pastime. The young man with a glass and a finger to his lips adds a note of playful secrecy, as music, wine and flirtation mingle, and viewers may also have read a gentle warning against indulgence.","c":"Honthorst is at his most brilliant here: the satin garments in lapis, coral and buttercup, the shoulder-to-shoulder composition and the crisp folds give the scene a lively, glossy theater. It is more performance than observation, with the faces types and the gestures staged to show off costume and instrument. But the painting captures the pleasure of musical company and the animating glow of Caravaggesque color, in a work of confident decorative force."},"met:438585":{"d":"A portrait of a man, identified as Jean-Charles Garnier d'Isle (1697–1755), in pastel and gouache on blue paper laid down on canvas, about 65 by 54 centimeters, dated about 1750. Nothing is recorded of his dress or pose. Maurice Quentin de La Tour, the most celebrated French pastellist of his age, was famous for faces that seem about to speak, and for the speed and virtuosity with which he caught a sitter's expression.","i":"Pastel was the medium of the Enlightenment portrait: fast to execute, brilliant in surface and well suited to the glittering, conversational society of Paris. La Tour's sitters ranged from the king and Madame de Pompadour to financiers, scholars and friends, and his best portraits seem to catch a person mid-thought. The sitter's dates place the portrait near the end of his life, and likenesses of private individuals like this often record friendships and ties in the milieu of the capital.","c":"La Tour is the supreme virtuoso of the pastel portrait. Where other pastellists settle into a polite prettiness, he finds animation: a mobile mouth, a living eye, a gleam of intelligence. The risk is a mannered facility, since the sparkle can become formula; but at his best the freshness is astonishing, and a blue paper adds a cool tone against which flesh seems alive. A fine example from the years of his greatest reputation."},"met:438724":{"d":"The angel Gabriel announces to the Virgin Mary that she will bear the Son of God, while the dove of the Holy Spirit appears, in a nearly square oak panel of about 71 by 73 centimeters, painted about 1644. Philippe de Champaigne, a Brussels-born painter who became one of the leading artists of Paris, treats the subject with the clarity and restraint typical of his religious pictures.","i":"The Annunciation (Luke 1:26 to 38) is the moment of the Incarnation, when Mary accepts her role with the words Behold the handmaid of the Lord. The dove marks the Holy Spirit's descent, and Gabriel's gesture announces the news. For Champaigne, associated from the 1640s with the Jansenist community of Port-Royal, which stressed humility and inward devotion, the scene is a model of obedient faith rather than a spectacle.","c":"Champaigne's calm, lucid manner suits the Annunciation: clean drawing, cool color and a stillness in which the miracle happens quietly. The disciplined restraint is his strength; the weakness is a certain coolness, as the picture can seem more reasoned than moving. In a century of baroque theatricality his austerity stands apart, and this small oak panel is a model of devout, intimate, unemphatic painting."},"nga:41647":{"d":"A man sits on a red cushioned chair, head turned toward the viewer and body angled slightly right, one hand on a closed book and the other holding a folded paper. He has gray hair under a small black cap, a trimmed mustache and pointed beard, brown eyes and a neutral expression. His gown is red and black with wide sleeves, a white collar and white cuffs. On the patterned tablecloth stand a white quill in a silver pot and an ornate golden clock. Behind him are a pale pink curtain, a gray pillar and a statue in a niche.","i":"Omer Talon (about 1595–1652) was an avocat général of the Parlement of Paris, one of the crown's chief law officers, and a famous orator who spoke for moderation during the Fronde, the civil disturbances of 1648–53 in which the Parlement and the crown clashed. This portrait of 1649 shows him in his official gown with the tools of his office: papers, a book, a quill. The clock may allude to the passage of time or to duty.","c":"Champaigne's austerity suits a magistrate. There is no flourish, only a plain, searching look; the drapery and chair give weight without ornament, the face keeps a neutral expression, and the hands, one on the book and one with the paper, give the pose what movement it has. The props are described with the same cool accuracy as the man. Dignified, trustworthy and a little cold, it is a portrait of an office as much as of a person."},"cma:135875":{"d":"The young king Charles II, in armor, gestures toward the sea beyond the cliffs of Dover, where a fleet waits. Painted in 1653 during his exile in France, after he fled England in 1651, the portrait shows the king's resolve to return, which he did in 1660. According to the museum, the armor is a French cuirassier's, made around 1630 and probably a gift, since Charles would have been an infant when it was made. The canvas is about 130 by 97 centimeters.","i":"After the execution of Charles I in 1649 and his own defeat at Worcester in 1651, Charles II lived as a king without a kingdom, wandering the Continent. Champaigne's portrait, commissioned in exile, was an assertion of continuity and claim: the king in armor with the sea and ships behind him, the cliffs of Dover signaling the home he intended to reclaim. Armor stood for rank and status, and such images were probably meant to keep royalist hopes alive.","c":"Champaigne gives the exiled king a firmness that the political situation did not warrant: the armor, the gesture and the fleet turn a penniless wanderer into a commander. The painter's clear, disciplined drawing serves the idea well. The pictorial rhetoric is stock, but its situation, a king planning a return from exile, gives it a charge that purely ceremonial portraits lack. A confident piece of royal image-making."},"met:435886":{"d":"A portrait of Jean-Baptiste Colbert (1619 to 1683), painted in 1655 on a canvas of about 92 by 72 centimeters. Colbert was then in his mid-thirties, the trusted man of business of Cardinal Mazarin, ten years before he became Louis XIV's controller-general of finances and the architect of French mercantile policy. Nothing is recorded of his dress or pose; Philippe de Champaigne painted many such sober likenesses of officials and clerics.","i":"Colbert rose from a merchant family of Reims to become one of the most powerful men in France, reorganizing the state's finances and encouraging the arts and industries that glorified Louis XIV, including the Academy of Painting, of which Champaigne was a founding member. A portrait of 1655 shows him before the peak of his power: an administrator with a reputation for diligence and probity, which Champaigne's plain style serves.","c":"Champaigne is the great painter of sober integrity, and his portraits of ministers and magistrates, scrupulously drawn and unflattering in their honesty, are among the most truthful of the French seventeenth century. A Colbert of 1655 in this plain manner conveys the capable, unglamorous, dutiful official; what is missing is the swagger of the Baroque court portrait, and that absence is the picture's character. Intelligent rather than ingratiating."},"met:436679":{"d":"A woman and two men gather in an arbor, a garden shelter, with a pitcher and the pleasures of smoking and drinking among the recorded details, and fur-trimmed clothing among the costumes. The small panel, about 44 by 38 centimeters, dates from about 1657 to 1658, when Pieter de Hooch was working in Delft at the moment before his courtyards and interiors took their final shape. The scene is a merry company, an informal social gathering of the kind Delft genre painters made popular.","i":"Scenes of men and women drinking and smoking in a garden or tavern arbor were a staple of Dutch genre painting, and viewers read them with a double eye: as lively social observation, and as a warning about drink, tobacco and idle company, since the genre blurred into the brothel and tavern scenes of the day. The pitcher and the pipe are the standard props of conviviality and temptation. De Hooch's early works often keep this ambiguity.","c":"An early de Hooch that is good-humored and a little rough: the figures are solid, the light warm, the scene easily read. It does not yet have the perspective and light-flooded courtyards for which he is celebrated, and the characterization is somewhat conventional. Charming and economical, but best valued as a stage on the way to his mature manner."},"nga:1172":{"d":"A smiling child holds the latch of an open door and looks toward a woman at the right, in an almost square picture of a bedroom. The child's blond hair is backlit by light from windows at left; she wears a mauve-gray bodice, a sage-green apron and a honey-yellow skirt, and holds a ball. The woman, in a white kerchief, red shirt and marine-blue skirt, stands in profile before a bed in a ceiling-high enclosure with a green curtain. Blue-painted tiles, a tiled floor, beams, lion-finial chairs, a landscape over the door, and, through two open doors, an outer entryway with a trellis complete the room.","i":"The Bedroom is a famous example of de Hooch's domestic ideal: a woman at the bed while a child plays at the door, in a house whose cleanliness, order and open doors express the Dutch values of thrift, care and harmony. The ball in the child's hand and her smile give the scene an innocent warmth; the open doors, through which the outside world appears, suggest a household at ease with its neighborhood. Such pictures flattered the Dutch image of home as a moral realm.","c":"De Hooch's most perfect device is the sequence of openings, door, window, second door, outside, which draws the eye through the house as the light does, and The Bedroom uses it with great economy. Every element, tile, beam, chair, jug, is set in its place in a soft, believable light; the figures are small and simply drawn, as the architecture is the subject. Few interiors in painting feel so inhabited yet so still."},"nga:1173":{"d":"A woman and a child are shown in a courtyard, as the title says; nothing further is recorded, so the picture is best described by type: one of Pieter de Hooch's sunlit Delft courtyards of about 1658 to 1660, in which a few figures go about quiet household business within a precisely measured space of brick, doors and passages. The canvas is about 74 by 66 centimeters, a size for a private room.","i":"De Hooch's courtyards and interiors in Delft in the late 1650s shaped the Dutch image of domestic virtue: the clean, orderly house where women and children go about their tasks in a protected space. A mother with a child was an emblem of care, thrift and household harmony, qualities Dutch viewers prized in the young Republic. Views through doors and arches, a De Hooch signature, extend that private world to the neighborhood beyond.","c":"De Hooch's gift is spatial: he builds a measured world of bricks, thresholds and light and places his figures within it with minimal fuss, so that the architecture is as expressive as the people. The figures can seem like pieces set into a diagram, but that is part of his quiet, hypnotic appeal. A characteristic, well-judged example of the Delft years."},"nga:63":{"d":"Sunlight fills a brick-paved courtyard closed in by a plank fence and plain brick buildings. At the left a man in a black hat and jacket sits in profile smoking a long clay pipe, a gray cloak across his lap. Across a small table a smiling bearded man in a breastplate holds a lidded tankard and looks up at a woman who stands drinking from a tall glass; she wears a tan jacket, a red skirt and a blue apron, and her shoe nearly touches the seated man's. At right a young girl in a white cap carries a dish of glowing embers for the pipes. An open door shows steps and a tree trunk, and a church tower rises above the fence under a bright blue sky.","i":"The scene is Delft, where de Hooch worked in the 1650s after joining the painters' guild around 1655. The tower in the distance has been identified as that of the Nieuwe Kerk, and the courtyard probably recalls the gardens behind the houses along the Oude Gracht canal. The soldier, the serving woman and the drinking game belong to a familiar Dutch type in which soldiers and maids may hint at courtship and its risks, but the picture does not preach. Infrared study shows de Hooch moved the girl to overlap the architecture and reduce the sense of distance, a sign of how carefully he planned the space.","c":"De Hooch's gift is for sunlit geometry, and it is on show here: the brick wall, the pale paving and the sharp shadows are described with exact, calm attention. The people are secondary, arranged like pieces on a board, and the exchange at the table is conventional. Compared with Vermeer, who worked in the same city, de Hooch is more descriptive and less concentrated, but the clarity of the light and the girl's approach from the right create a quiet drama that is easy to underrate."},"met:436678":{"d":"A woman and a man share an interior, as the title and recorded subjects indicate, in a panel of about 68 by 58 centimeters dated about 1657. The scene is a visit, a social call that in Dutch genre painting could be innocent courtesy or the opening move of a courtship. Pieter de Hooch painted it in Delft, in the years when he was perfecting the careful arrangement of figures within a well-lit room.","i":"The visit is a favorite theme of Dutch genre painting, a way of showing social ritual: how people met, entertained and courted in the domestic interior. The ambiguity is part of the interest; depending on the cues, a visit might be respectable, flirtatious or venal, and viewers of the 1650s were practiced readers of such signs, the gestures, the drink, the setting. De Hooch's early visits tend to be discreet and quietly humorous.","c":"In The Visit De Hooch is still finding the balance between figures and room that makes his best pictures; the people are clear and the light is convincing. The scene has an easy charm, but compared with the courtyards and interiors of 1658 to 1660 its space is less daring and its narrative more conventional. A pleasing, intimate early work, valuable for what it anticipates."},"cma:128391":{"d":"A wealthy Dutch family plays music in a grand room, in a wide canvas of about 99 by 117 centimeters, dated 1663. The sitters are unknown, but the marble floor and mantelpiece, a Transylvanian prayer rug on the table, silk clothes and, at the right, a richly carved chest bearing Chinese vases and Japanese lacquered boxes all point to great wealth and to the Dutch trade with Asia. Pieter de Hooch, by this date living in Amsterdam, painted the picture for a family of the new merchant elite.","i":"Music-making at home was a sign of harmony in the Dutch household, the figures' concord standing for the family's and for civilized domestic virtue. The costly objects display the family's share in the world economy, the Dutch Republic then being, as the museum notes, the richest and most urbanized nation of the period. A group portrait of this kind, whether or not the sitters are individually recognized, presented the family as a prosperous and harmonious unit.","c":"De Hooch's Amsterdam pictures swap the humble Delft courtyard for the displays of the rich, and not entirely for the better: the room is grander and the glossy detail impressive, yet the warmth and the integration of figures in space that marked his Delft work are less secure. This family portrait, lavish in rugs, silks and lacquer, shows a painter adapting to patrons who wanted to be seen in their finery; polished, informative and a little stiff."},"met:436675":{"d":"A young couple share an interior, with a dog among the recorded figures, in a canvas of about 55 by 63 centimeters, probably painted about 1662 to 1665. The title names the subject only as a young couple, so the pair's relationship, courting or married, is left for the viewer to read from the setting and the glances. Pieter de Hooch had by then moved from Delft to Amsterdam and was adapting his quiet domestic scenes to a more fashionable clientele.","i":"A couple in an interior with a dog is read in the Dutch genre tradition as a picture of courtship or marriage: the dog, long a symbol of fidelity but also sometimes of appetite, accompanies the pair. Viewers of the 1660s decoded the cues, the distribution of glances, the objects on the table, the open or closed doors, as clues to whether the relationship was virtuous or flirtatious. De Hooch usually treats such matters with discretion.","c":"By the mid-1660s De Hooch's interiors become more elegant and a little more artificial: the light is more theatrical, the costumes more fashionable, the architecture more stately. The picture retains his skill with space and the soft modeling of faces; what it loses is the earlier spontaneity. A well-composed, attractive interior from a painter in transition between the homely Delft and the glossier Amsterdam."},"met:436676":{"d":"A woman with a water pitcher and a man by a bed share an interior with a dog; the recorded subjects include a maid, which suggests that the woman with the pitcher is a servant. The canvas, about 62 by 52 centimeters, is dated about 1667 to 1670, late in Pieter de Hooch's career, when his interiors had become more elaborate and artificial than the sunlit Delft courtyards of his best years.","i":"A maid with a pitcher and a man by a bed make an ambiguous pair: a household scene, a servant's chore, and an implied intimacy, since bedrooms in Dutch genre painting were often loaded with sexual suggestion. A dog, sometimes a symbol of fidelity and sometimes of appetite, adds to the ambiguity. Viewers of the 1660s were practiced at reading such pictures, which might be understood as portraits of domestic order or as moral and erotic allegory.","c":"The late De Hooch loses some of the serenity of the Delft pictures: the colors grow harder, the rooms more polished, the figures more doll-like. Here a handsome composition is built from accustomed elements, but the mood is cooler and the narrative thinner than in his best work. Still, it shows his craft as an arranger of space and light, and the faint erotic note adds a piquant twist."},"met:436677":{"d":"A man pays the hostess of an inn in an interior, with a dog among the figures, in a large canvas of about 95 by 111 centimeters, dated about 1670. The subject is an inn scene of the kind Dutch painters had treated since the early seventeenth century, here handled by Pieter de Hooch in the elegant, rather formal manner of his later years, with the carefully built room as important as the people.","i":"Inns were places of rest, drink, gambling and flirtation, and Dutch painters used them to show a range of human behavior from good humor to vice. A scene of paying the bill is a moment of reckoning, literal and moral: the account is settled, the hostess in control of it. Viewers may have enjoyed the comedy of the transaction, or, as in some such pictures, detected a hint of other kinds of payment, since an inn's hostess could also be a procuress.","c":"De Hooch in the 1670s builds a handsome, spacious interior, with a mellow light and orderly arrangement, but the people have lost the warmth of his Delft figures: they are well dressed, deliberate and somewhat blank. As genre the picture is cooler and drier than his best, though its scale and construction are impressive. A late, polished work whose pleasures are architectural."},"nga:195670":{"d":"A greeting is the subject, as the title says: a moment of welcome or courtesy between figures, the sort of social ritual that Dutch genre painters used to show manners and relationships. The canvas, about 86 by 70 centimeters, dates from about 1675, late in Pieter de Hooch's career, when his pictures often set fashionably dressed figures in grand houses, with a cooler, more formal air than his early Delft work. Nothing further is recorded of figures or setting.","i":"Greetings, visits and invitations were central themes of the Dutch genre painter's social stage, showing manners as a visible code: a bow, an extended hand, a doffed hat signaled respect, courtship or rank. By the 1670s Amsterdam's wealthy burghers wanted pictures that showed them as polite and well-bred, and De Hooch supplied scenes of controlled courtesy. Such pictures might also be read as comment on the delicate negotiations of social and marital relations.","c":"De Hooch's late work is often considered a decline from his Delft peak: the compositions are still skillful, but the color is harder, the figures more mannered and the architecture grander and less alive. A picture titled for a ceremonial moment may suffer from the same formality. It remains a competent, well-constructed late example, interesting as evidence of how Dutch taste changed in the 1670s toward elegance and display."},"met:437270":{"d":"A head of a man, painted in oil on a canvas board of about 53 by 35 centimeters, a study rather than a finished portrait; the date is given as possibly 1870 to 1882. No name, dress or setting is recorded. Vasily Perov, a leading realist painter of nineteenth-century Russia, made such heads as studies of character, in which a face is observed with frank attention to age, fatigue and personality.","i":"Perov belonged to the generation of Russian realists who turned from academic subjects to the lives of ordinary people, in sympathy with social reform; his genre pictures of beggars, drunken priests and peasants are among the most pungent images of Russia in the 1860s, and his portraits, notably of Dostoevsky, are among the most penetrating. A Head of a Man belongs to the studies in which he sought truthful types: a way of seeing the face as a record of a life.","c":"Perov at his best is direct and unflattering, with a gift for the telling feature, and a study like this probably shows that candor at its barest. The absence of context, no pose, no name, makes it a type, and its fragmentary status limits its force. Beside his Dostoevsky, which gives a face an intense inner life, a nameless study can only hint at what he could do."},"met:441233":{"d":"The archangel Gabriel brings his message to the Virgin Mary beneath the descending dove of the Holy Spirit, in a very large upright panel, about 232 by 173 centimeters, painted in oil on wood around 1585. Peter Candid, born in Bruges as Pieter de Witte, trained in Florence in the workshop of Giorgio Vasari and brought the Florentine Mannerist style of his master to Bavaria, where he became court painter in Munich in 1586, shortly after this picture.","i":"The Annunciation was a core subject of Counter-Reformation art, celebrating Mary's acceptance of the Incarnation and the role of the Virgin as a model of obedience. The dove marks the descent of the Holy Spirit, and the figures dramatize an exchange of words. At such a large scale the picture was probably an altarpiece for a church or chapel, meant to be seen across a space, where its bright color and clear gestures would carry to worshippers.","c":"Candid's Florentine training shows in the elegant, elongated figures, rich draperies and graceful poses of Vasari's circle. The scale is impressive, and the color has a decorative brilliance. What is missing is emotional penetration; the exchange between angel and Virgin is formal rather than intimate, as in much Mannerist religious painting. A polished, ambitious altarpiece that anticipates the courtly religious art Candid would make in Munich."},"nga:111194":{"d":"A masted ship with tattered sails founders among choppy waves beneath charcoal clouds, its main mast pitched steeply across the lower right with seven men clinging to the crow's nest and others hauling rigging ashore; the second mast has splintered against boulders. On the shore more than two dozen small figures struggle: a woman in pink raises her arms as a man holds her waist, a half-clothed woman is supported by three men, and men roll a barrel and haul a cage from the surf. A castle crowns the cliffs, and a streak of golden lightning lights a distant town. The canvas is about 114 by 163 centimeters.","i":"Shipwrecks were a favorite subject of the eighteenth century's taste for the sublime, an aesthetic of awe and terror that Edmund Burke theorized in 1757 and Vernet's storm pictures made famous. Viewers, safe on land, could enjoy the spectacle of nature's power and human fragility, and the vignettes of rescue and despair gave the scene a moral dimension. Diderot praised Vernet's command of the elements; Moonlight, of the same year, seems to be its companion.","c":"Vernet orchestrates catastrophe with a stage manager's skill: the diagonal of the splintered mast, the lightning's golden zigzag and the huddles of tiny figures on the shore guide the eye through disaster, and the contrast of dark storm with coral-peach cloud adds drama. The effect is more theatrical than frightening; the figures are conventional types, and the wreck is organized as spectacle. As sublime entertainment it is masterly, and it shows what the eighteenth century most admired in him."},"nga:214332":{"d":"Moonlight falls across masted ships near a rocky shore. A crisp full moon, ringed by pearl-white cloud, hangs at the center beneath a sky that fills three quarters of the picture, and its light runs down the rippling water to a beach. At the lower left, people gather around a fire by a tent, one woman with a basket on her head, a man smoking, another asleep with a dog. At the right, men haul a small boat toward land beside a three-masted ship with glowing stern windows. A walled gate, cliff-top buildings and a lighthouse mark the far shore. The canvas is about 115 by 163 centimeters.","i":"Moonlit harbors and coasts were a specialty of Vernet, who made a career of painting times of day and kinds of weather, calm and storm, dawn and night, as a series of moods. The pictures tapped the century's taste for effects of light and for the picturesque life of coasts, with people gathered around fires. Fire, the lit windows of a ship and the moon give three kinds of light, a technical showpiece that pleased connoisseurs. It seems to be a companion to The Shipwreck of the same year.","c":"Moonlight is among Vernet's most poetic works: the moon's reflection down the water makes a quiet axis, the clouds are softly modeled in pearl and slate, and the small figures around their fire give a warm human note. Technically it is a virtuoso display of nocturnal effects. It is less thrilling than the storm that probably pairs with it, but more subtle; the repose is convincing and the atmosphere lyrical. A fine, mature Vernet."},"met:438020":{"d":"A standing saint holds a sword, the attribute of Saint Julian, on a gold ground in a narrow upright panel about 54 by 36 centimeters. Such a single saint was probably one compartment of a polyptych, an altarpiece in several panels; this one dates from the 1340s, painted by Taddeo Gaddi, the Florentine who had trained with Giotto and became his principal follower.","i":"The sword points to Saint Julian the Hospitaller, a legendary figure. According to the Golden Legend, a hunter was prophesied to kill his parents and by tragic mistake did so, with his sword; he spent the rest of his life in penance, running a hospice and ferry for travelers. As patron saint of ferrymen, innkeepers and travelers he stood for repentance and hospitality. Saints on an altarpiece were often chosen for a patron's name, profession or devotion.","c":"Gaddi is the most faithful of Giotto's followers, and a single saint shows his strengths: a solid, sculptural body, clear contour, firm drapery and a calm gravity inherited from his master. What he lacks is Giotto's startling invention; his figures are dignified rather than moving. As a fragment of a larger ensemble it can only suggest the effect of the whole, but within its modest scale it is a strong, restrained image of Florentine Trecento painting."},"met:436430":{"d":"The Virgin and Child sit enthroned among saints in a wide altarpiece of about 110 by 229 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with a gold ground. Taddeo Gaddi painted it about 1340, and the museum records an updating about 1480, when a later hand reworked it to suit changed tastes, so the picture combines two periods. Gold grounds like this were costly and signaled the sacred; the gold leaf was applied over a prepared wood panel and burnished to a glow.","i":"The enthroned Madonna with saints on either side was the standard altarpiece type of the fourteenth century: the Virgin is Queen of Heaven and intercessor, the saints her court. A century and a half later a painted altarpiece might be updated rather than replaced, to bring its figures and ornament into line with the fashions of a new age. Such a change speaks to how altarpieces were cherished and adapted by their communities, and to the changing ideals of beauty between Giotto's world and the Renaissance.","c":"As a Gaddi it carries the gravity of the Giotto tradition: solid figures, clear forms, gold ground and calm. The later alteration complicates the picture: a viewer must separate what the 1340s master painted from what the 1480s hand imposed, and the join of styles may not be seamless. It is a useful document of how artworks live through time, though the unity and force of an untouched Gaddi are partly lost."},"met:436181":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman, with a flower among its details, painted on ivory that has been extended by card to enlarge the surface, about 1805 to 1810 in France; the sitter is unnamed. The technique is unusual: a pieced support of thin ivory and card, probably because a large sheet of ivory was costly and limited by the size of the tusk. Jacques Delaplace was a French painter of miniatures active in the Napoleonic era.","i":"In the Napoleonic years a woman's portrait would show the new Empire fashion: the high-waisted, light, classical dress and simple hair, and a flower, a rose or a posy, speaks of love, beauty and the fragility of both. Miniatures were exchanged between lovers, spouses and families, and the art had a revival under the Consulate and Empire after the disruptions of the Revolution.","c":"A miniature of this period is judged by its clarity and refinement, and the Empire style, cool, precise and a little idealized, favored a smooth, enamel-like surface. Delaplace is not among the great names such as Isabey or Augustin, so this is a competent, characteristic example rather than a revelation; the interest lies in the technical oddity of the card-extended ivory and in the sitter's quiet elegance."},"met:436555":{"d":"A sandy road leads past a farmhouse among trees, in a small panel of about 31 by 41 centimeters, dated 1627. Jan van Goyen painted it in his early thirties, when he was still working in Leiden in a more colorful, anecdotal manner derived from his teacher Esaias van de Velde, before developing the tonal, atmospheric style of his maturity.","i":"The sandy road, winding through dunes and fields to a farm, became one of the signature motifs of Dutch tonal landscape: a plain, humble, truthful view of the countryside, in contrast to the fantasy landscapes of an earlier generation. The farmhouse and trees give human scale, and the road leads the eye into depth. For viewers of the 1620s the scene offered recognition: it was their own country.","c":"At this date Van Goyen is still learning to simplify: the details are more numerous, the color warmer and the touch fussier than in the monochrome panels that made him famous. The composition is neatly built, and the freshness of observation promises what was to come. A charming early work, historically important as an ancestor of his tonal manner."},"nga:56597":{"d":"Painted almost entirely in tones of beige and golden brown, a view across smooth water shows a row of buildings around a large church, with windmills scattered along the skyline. The horizon lies a quarter of the way up, and an ice-blue sky is nearly filled with pink-tinged clouds. At lower left a man bends over a bundle on a narrow spit of land; in the center a rowboat carries at least ten people in bonnets and hats, with a smaller boat tied alongside, and to the left people are helped from a rowboat up onto a sailboat. The panel is about 65 by 96 centimeters.","i":"Dordrecht, among the oldest cities in Holland, was a prosperous trading town and the site of the Synod of 1618 to 1619 that set down Calvinist doctrine, and its skyline with the great church was a recognizable emblem. Van Goyen painted it repeatedly, from the water, the way many travelers arrived, with ferries and boats connecting the town to the surrounding land. The scene shows the daily traffic of a river and canal country, with the sky taking more than half the picture.","c":"This is Van Goyen's mature tonal manner at its most economical: a few shades of beige and gold-brown, a low horizon and a high sky, and light spread evenly across the water. The figures in the boats are quick, summary touches that give life without anecdote. The restraint approaches monotony, but the unity of tone is the point, and the pink-tinged sky gives an unexpected note of warmth."},"cma:135840":{"d":"A shallow boat ferries passengers and horse-drawn wagons across the Rhine in the foreground of this view of Emmerich, the German city on the river near the Dutch border. Jan van Goyen painted it in 1645 on a wood panel of about 65 by 97 centimeters, in his typical limited range of grays, browns, cool blues and earthy greens, with swift, intuitive brushwork that the Cleveland Museum describes as recording realistic impressions of weather, space, light and air.","i":"Emmerich lay just across the German border on the main river route into the Netherlands, and Van Goyen went on sketching trips along the Rhine, making drawings that he worked up into paintings back in his studio. Ferries like the one in the foreground were part of the everyday traffic of the river, joining the banks and carrying people, horses and goods, and the painter's interest in them is also an interest in how a landscape is lived in.","c":"Van Goyen's best pictures, such as this, achieve a deceptively simple record of weather, space and the interplay of land, water, light and air. His fast, intuitive touch and restricted palette give a sensation of damp, breathing atmosphere. The risk is sameness across a prolific output, but a view as well constructed as this has its own quiet authority. A demonstration of how much a Dutch painter could say with a few browns and grays."},"met:436556":{"d":"A country house stands near the water with a boat in the foreground, in a small, nearly square panel of about 37 by 33 centimeters, dated 1646. Jan van Goyen made such unassuming views the core of his output: a house, a boat and a stretch of water, set low in the picture beneath a large, softly colored sky. It is typical of the painter's habit of making a small, plain corner of the country stand for the whole.","i":"A rural house by the water was the unassuming essence of Van Goyen's Dutch world: the dwelling, the boat for transport and fishing, the sky. Country houses, with their trees and outbuildings, were a way of showing the settled, prosperous countryside. For buyers in the cities, such pictures offered a view of the country as a calm, accessible refuge, a quality that became a national taste.","c":"Van Goyen reduces a landscape to a handful of notes, house, boat, water and sky, and the tonal unity makes them cohere. At 37 by 33 centimeters the panel has a jewel-like economy but little room to breathe, and the nearly square format denies him the sweep of his wide views. A quiet, small thing, successful on its own modest terms."},"met:436557":{"d":"An old city gate with a tower, the Pelkus Gate near Utrecht, stands by a river where small figures skate on the ice and a boat lies close by, in a wide panel of about 37 by 57 centimeters, dated 1646. The gate was part of the older fortifications of Utrecht, and Van Goyen painted winter motifs like this throughout his career, sketching figures in a few touches and holding everything in tonal grays and browns.","i":"City gates, with their moats and towers, were conspicuous landmarks of the Dutch town, and views of them suited buyers who liked to recognize their own surroundings. Combining a historic building with the everyday life of the winter ice gave the scene both a sense of place and a human liveliness, and it let buyers enjoy a familiar landmark in a seasonal mood.","c":"A small, nicely judged winter picture: the gate gives a firm shape against the pale sky, and the skaters supply scale and life. Van Goyen is at his best when he uses a dominant landmark to anchor an atmospheric scene, and here the tower does the work. The tonal range of ice-gray, brown and pale blue is characteristically narrow and subtle. Modest in ambition, and accomplished in effect."},"met:436558":{"d":"The skyline of Haarlem rises across the Haarlemmer Meer, a large inland lake, with windmills among its details, in a small oil painting on a wood panel of about 35 by 51 centimeters, dated 1646. The lake that Van Goyen painted no longer exists: the Haarlemmermeer was drained in the nineteenth century, and its bed is now farmland, towns and the site of Schiphol Airport.","i":"The Haarlemmer Meer, which had grown by storm and erosion until it threatened nearby towns, was by the seventeenth century a source of both fish and fear; it was finally drained with steam pumps in 1848 to 1852. Van Goyen's view of Haarlem across it records the beauty and the danger of the Dutch relation with water, and the windmills are the machines of that long struggle, used to drain polders.","c":"A modest but historically precious picture: the low horizon, the great sky and the thin line of town and mills in silvery browns capture the Dutch landscape of water, wind and distance. Van Goyen is at ease with the format, and the sparing touch suggests more than it states. The picture's appeal is increased by the fact that the lake no longer exists, so that the painting preserves a vanished landscape."},"nga:139354":{"d":"A large canvas of about 102 by 135 centimeters shows Rhenen, a small town on the Lower Rhine in the province of Utrecht, in 1646. Nothing further is recorded; Van Goyen's views of such river towns typically show the town on the far bank, with its church tower as the main landmark, boats and ferries on the river and a broad sky occupying most of the picture.","i":"River towns like Rhenen were stations on the Rhine trade route and made tidy, recognizable subjects: the church tower, the walls, the quays. Dutch buyers liked views of real places, and Van Goyen, who traveled widely by boat and wagon, supplied them in great numbers, from the large decorative canvas to the small panel. The sky and water, rather than the buildings, carry the mood.","c":"A big canvas of this date is a showcase of Van Goyen's mature strength: tonal unity, a low horizon and a huge sky. The weakness of the formula is that the town can become a thin silhouette at the edge of a vast field of atmosphere; the strength is that the atmosphere is convincing. At this scale he keeps his control, and the result has a quiet grandeur if little incident."},"nga:165221":{"d":"A band of gray ice fills the bottom quarter of this upright panel, and dozens of people skate, stand, push sledges or play with long sticks on it beneath a vivid blue sky banked with towering gray clouds. At the lower left a horse pulls a sledge of five or six passengers while a man scrambles on hands and knees for his fallen hat. The largest crowd, with children, gathers around a rickety wooden pier from which a slender tower with a lantern at its top rises. A church spire and a windmill line the horizon. The panel is about 37 by 34 centimeters, signed VG 1646.","i":"Winter scenes on the ice were beloved in the Dutch Republic, which produced a great number in the 1620s to 1640s, when severe winters made the frozen waterways places of work, travel and play for every class. The long sticks recall kolf, the Dutch ice game often counted among the ancestors of golf and hockey; the sledges show the ice used for transport. The wooden tower of the title, with ladders and a lantern-like platform at its top, was probably a lookout or a marker for shipping.","c":"On a small panel Van Goyen turns his tonal economy to a lively winter crowd: tiny, quickly sketched figures animate the ice, and the tower with its pier gives a vertical focus to the composition. The sky is as important as the ice, with its towers of cloud and pale yellow light. He lacks Avercamp's wit and detail, but compensates with unity of atmosphere and a sure sense of space. A vivid, compact record of a Dutch winter."},"met:436559":{"d":"A castle stands by a river with a boat on the water, in a wide panel of about 66 by 97 centimeters, dated 1647. Without an identified site it is best read as a type: the castle as a landmark of the fortified river landscape of the Netherlands, painted in Jan van Goyen's mature tonal manner of silvery browns and grays under a wide sky.","i":"Castles in Dutch river landscapes were not always documentary; Van Goyen, like Salomon van Ruysdael, often invented or adapted them to supply a picturesque vertical accent, with allusions to the fortified castles of the long war with Spain. Their presence also suggested continuity and historic strength for a newly independent nation, and in 1647, the year before the Peace of Münster, such motifs may have carried a patriotic note.","c":"In Van Goyen's hands a castle is an excuse for atmosphere: the architecture is softened into a silvery gray-brown haze, the river reflects the sky, and the whole is held in a delicate tonal equilibrium. Without the clarity of Salomon van Ruysdael's castle views it makes a vaguer, moodier impression. The picture is large enough to be impressive, and quiet enough to reward patience."},"met:438375":{"d":"A view of The Hague seen across flat country from the northwest, with a road and a horse in the foreground and the town's skyline on a low horizon, in a wide panel of about 66 by 96 centimeters, dated 1647. Jan van Goyen lived in The Hague from 1632 until his death in 1656 and painted it many times; the profile of its towers is a recognizable portrait of the seat of the Dutch government.","i":"The Hague was the seat of the States General and the stadholder's court, celebrated for its Binnenhof, and a view from the northwest showed the town lying low among flat fields, as a traveler on the road might see it. Van Goyen's panoramas of The Hague were topographical and could also be read as patriotic: a view of the center of the Republic's government, painted in the year before the Peace of Münster ended the long war with Spain.","c":"A fine topographical view in which the town's skyline is a low, silvery band under a tall, mobile sky, the road gives a leading line, and the travelers supply scale. Van Goyen's economy is at its best here: very little is said, but everything is placed with assurance. It is less brilliant than his river views, but more informative, giving an authentic image of the setting of the seat of government."},"met:438374":{"d":"A beach with fishing boats, a horse and a cart among its details, on a small panel of about 28 by 43 centimeters, probably dated 1653. Such shore scenes, with boats drawn up on the sand and a cart ready to carry the catch, were a specialty of Dutch tonal landscape painting, and Jan van Goyen made many of them in his later years.","i":"The Dutch coast, with its long beaches and fishing villages such as Scheveningen, was a working landscape: boats landed the catch, and horses and carts hauled it to the market in the town. Painters turned the activity into a quiet theme: figures at work against a vast sky, with the sea as a pale, silvery band. For buyers in a Republic whose prosperity depended on fish and trade, the beach was a picture of the economy at ground level.","c":"At this small scale Van Goyen is at his most economical: a few brown strokes for boats, cart and horse, a pale sand tone, and a large, mobile sky. The painting's strength is its sense of coastal air; its limitation is its sketchiness, which will disappoint anyone looking for finish. A modest, lively, convincing image of a Dutch beach."},"nga:157395":{"d":"A view of the city of Dordrecht seen from the north, on a canvas of about 63 by 87 centimeters, painted in the early 1650s. Nothing further is recorded, but Van Goyen's Dordrecht views typically show the skyline of church tower and mills across the water, with boats in the foreground, under a wide sky, in the silvery-brown tones of his late style.","i":"Dordrecht, a river port at the meeting of several waterways, was one of the wealthiest cities of Holland, with a great church and a trade in grain and wine. Painting it from the north, from the river side, showed it as arriving travelers or boatmen would have seen it, with the church's bulk dominating the low skyline. Van Goyen returned to Dordrecht repeatedly, and its profile became one of his most recognizable subjects.","c":"In the early 1650s Van Goyen's late style is at its most refined, with limited color, a low horizon and a broad sky. A Dordrecht from this period is a mature statement; it risks sameness beside his other Dordrechts, but the reduction to essentials has a classic clarity. The pleasure is in nuance: a slightly cooler gray here, a bit of warm brown there. A quiet, assured work."},"met:10534":{"d":"Neptune, god of the sea, returns in a rough, crowded marine procession of horses, a centaur, a mermaid and nude figures, on a wide canvas of about 70 by 113 centimeters, painted about 1754. The painter, John Singleton Copley, was then about sixteen and growing up in Boston, where original European pictures of the kind were scarce; he probably took the design from a European engraving, as young colonial painters often did.","i":"The sea god's procession was a classical subject of the Renaissance and Baroque, with Neptune carried over the waves in his chariot among attendant nymphs and tritons to represent the power and abundance of the sea. For a Boston teenager from a port town it was an exotic theme from the Old World, and a test of whether a colonial painter could master the nude, the horse and the heroic composition that European academies prized. It stands apart from the portraits that made his name.","c":"As a teenager's work, probably derived from a print, this is astonishing for its ambition more than for its achievement: the figures are somewhat stiff, the anatomy approximate and the drawing indebted to its model. The picture shows how little of the great European tradition was available to a young colonial artist. Copley would find his true subject in the Boston portrait, where his gifts for texture and observation had room; here he is learning, not yet distinguishing himself."},"met:16958":{"d":"A young girl is shown with a rose, with a bouquet and lace among the details, in a small portrait of about 55 by 45 centimeters, dated 1753 to 1754. It was probably painted as a pair with the portrait of John Greenleaf and is among the earliest known works of John Singleton Copley, who was then only fifteen or sixteen and drew on engravings and on the example of his late stepfather, the Boston engraver Peter Pelham. Dress, flowers and lace mark the girl as a child of a family able to commission a likeness.","i":"Portraits of well-off children in colonial Boston borrowed their conventions from English mezzotints: the child dressed as a small adult, posed with a flower or pet to signal innocence and a promising future. A rose was an emblem of youth and beauty and, being short-lived, hinted at the fragility of childhood in an age of high child mortality. The portraits are small and intimate, meant to hang in the family parlor as a record of children growing up.","c":"Considering that a teenager painted it, the picture is remarkable more as a beginning than as an achievement: the pose and costume are borrowed from prints, and the likeness is cautious. Yet Copley's attention to detail, in the lace and the flowers, is already evident, and with it an instinct for texture that would make his later portraits great. A touching, naive and important early document of a major career."},"met:16959":{"d":"A boy is shown with a lamb and a tree among the details, in a small portrait of about 55 by 45 centimeters, dated 1753 to 1754, probably a companion to Elizabeth Greenleaf's. The pose and the pastoral setting, a boy with a lamb under a tree, recall English child portraits of the period, which Copley knew from mezzotints; the artist was then fifteen or sixteen.","i":"The lamb is the traditional companion of the innocent child, and a boy with a lamb evokes the pastoral ideal and, more remotely, the Christ Child and the Good Shepherd. Colonial parents who commissioned such pictures wished to see their children as innocent and promising, in a fashionable English setting of trees and animals, though the setting here is an invention far from a real Boston childhood. With the portrait of Elizabeth Greenleaf, probably its companion, it shows two young members of one family.","c":"As with the Elizabeth Greenleaf portrait, the picture shows a talented teenager working from print sources: the pastoral setting is formulaic and the child's pose stiff. The surface detail, wool and leaves, already shows the interest in textures that Copley's later portraits exploit. A small, endearing document of a great painter's first steps, more important for what it begins than for what it achieves."},"nga:1096":{"d":"Shown from the waist up through an oval opening, a pale woman in a shell-pink satin gown and a greenish-yellow cloak looks out with a faint smile. Her shoulders angle to the left while her oval face turns slightly right, and her dark gray eyes glance sideways under arched brows. Her dark brown hair is pulled up and woven with dark pearls, and curls fall down her back. A lace-lined neckline, pale yellow bows and a red brooch holding a ribbon across the cloak complete the costume, against a dark blue, cloudy sky. Oil on canvas, about 76 by 63 centimeters, signed and dated 1756 on the oval frame.","i":"Copley painted this portrait in his late teens in Boston, where he had no academy to attend and learned largely from his stepfather, the engraver Peter Pelham, and from imported mezzotint prints. The painted oval frame, the cloak and the pearls in the hair are fashions borrowed from English portraits of society ladies, carried over to colonial New England. The sitter, named in the title as Jane Browne, is shown in the dress of an English beauty, an aspiration as well as a likeness.","c":"A striking early work: the satin, the lace and the pearls are painted with a hardness and clarity that already outstrip the provincial portraiture around him, and the sideways glance gives the sitter life. The flatness of the pose and the dependence on print sources show a young artist still learning, but the polish is beyond his years. It points forward to the great portraits of the 1760s."},"met:21473":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man named John St. Clair, painted in oil on a copper plate only about 4.4 by 3.5 centimeters across, dated 1758. It is a tiny object, small enough to be set in a locket or case, and an unusual one: miniature painters normally worked in watercolor on ivory, and Copley's early miniatures in oil on copper are a distinctive part of his output. Nothing is recorded of the sitter's dress or pose.","i":"Copley began to paint miniatures in the late 1750s widening his practice: a small likeness cost less and was in demand for keepsakes. Oil on a metal plate gives a durable, smooth surface; later he turned to watercolor on ivory, the usual medium of European miniaturists. The scale forces precision, and Copley's gift for crisp detail suited the task.","c":"At about four centimeters tall the picture can only be judged for its economy and exactness. Copley reduces a face to a few firm, clear touches and a convincing individual presence, and the copper support keeps the paint cool and smooth. The results are charming but limited, a reminder that his talent needed larger fields; his miniatures are footnotes to his portraits, but good ones."},"nga:45881":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, an older, clean-shaven man rests one elbow on a broken column, his body forming a diagonal across the picture. He looks out with clear blue eyes under low brows and a faint smile; the face is unflattered, with wrinkles at the mouth, jowls, a double chin and small blemishes near the mouth and one eye. Thick white hair falls in shoulder-length curls. He wears a steel-gray coat, white ruffles and a bright white neckcloth, with one hand in his pocket. At the right a green tree stands against a hazy pink and aquamarine sky. Oil on canvas, about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1760.","i":"Epes Sargent was a prosperous Massachusetts merchant, and the pose, with a broken column and a glimpse of landscape, lifts a colonial businessman into the visual world of English gentry portraits, which Copley knew from prints. The broken column can be read as a sign of mortality or of fortitude, apt for a man of advanced age, and the frank rendering of that age treats it as a mark of experience. A sitter paying for a large portrait wanted both his status and his true face.","c":"One of Copley's most direct early portraits. He does not soften the jowls and blemishes, and the result is a face with the gravity of long experience. The pose is stagey in the English manner, and the column and tree are props, but the living head, lit from one side, redeems them. Copley's gift for unadorned, specific likeness is plain here, and it explains why colonial sitters valued him."},"met:19014":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Moses Gill, painted in oil and gold leaf on copper and measuring only about 4 by 3 centimeters, dated about 1759. Gill, then in his mid-twenties, was a Boston merchant who would rise to become lieutenant governor of Massachusetts and, in 1799 and 1800, its acting governor. The gold leaf adds a glint of preciousness to an object meant to be held or worn, a personal keepsake.","i":"Copley's early clients were men on the rise in Boston society: merchants, ministers and officials who wanted likenesses that signaled their standing. A miniature was a private, less costly counterpart to a full-size portrait, to be kept for family or given to a bride or friend. Gill went on to a long public career, which makes this keepsake an early image of a future official, recorded before the roles that made his name.","c":"Copley's miniatures on copper are tiny feats of precision, and the gold leaf adds a note of luxury unusual for a colonial painter. The face is described with the same crispness as in his larger portraits, a remarkable thing at this scale; the limitation is the medium, which cannot give the mass and texture of his big canvases. A fine small object that shows the same exactness of eye, with less space for it."},"met:10530":{"d":"A woman wearing a pearl necklace, earring and ribbon is shown with a rose and a dog, on a large canvas of about 127 by 101 centimeters, painted about 1763. The sitter, Mrs. Jerathmael Bowers, belonged to the Boston society that Copley served in the 1760s, when his sharp observation of textures and accessories made him the colony's leading portraitist. Her pose and gown are not described, but pearls, rose and dog are the props of a well-to-do woman's portrait.","i":"A lapdog could signal fidelity, domestic affection or simply fashion, and a rose suggested beauty and the transience of youth; pearls were a standard sign of virtue, wealth and refined taste. Boston's prosperous families commissioned portraits as part of their claim to genteel status, and Copley, who knew English mezzotints and modeled his poses on them, gave his sitters the props and attitudes of the English gentry.","c":"Copley at the peak of his Boston period: the accessories and surfaces are described with a candor and precision rarely matched in contemporary English portraiture, and the faces are individual rather than idealized. The weakness is a certain stiffness of pose and a hard, almost sculptural finish; the sitters seem to hold their breath. But the presence, the sense of a real woman closely observed, is striking, and shows why he led portraiture in the colonies."},"nga:50838":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a woman in a shimmering teal-blue silk gown stands before columns and a balustrade, holding in both hands a garland of flowers tied with a pink ribbon. Her body faces the viewer but her head turns slightly as she looks out with dark brown eyes. A lacy cap covers her brown hair, and she wears sapphire earrings and a choker of sapphires with a teardrop pendant. The bodice has a square lace-edged neckline and white ribbons. Beyond the columns are a tall green tree and a blue sky. Oil on canvas, about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1763.","i":"The sapphires, silk gown and architecture proclaim wealth and refinement, and the garland of flowers is a long-established attribute in portraits of women, associating the sitter with beauty, spring and perhaps marriage. Copley borrowed the setting of column, balustrade and distant landscape from English society portraits known to him through prints. The title gives her by her husband's name, Mrs. Metcalf Bowler, as was usual for a married woman of the day.","c":"A fine example of Copley's mastery of fabrics: the teal silk, the lace and the blue stones are painted with a tactile exactness that his colonial rivals could not match. The pose and setting are conventional, and the face is more reserved than vivid, so the costume risks outshining the sitter. Even so, the picture shows how ambitious his portraits had become by the early 1760s."},"cma:149978":{"d":"An elderly woman, Anna Dummer Powell, aged about eighty, is portrayed on a large canvas of about 127 by 101 centimeters, painted in 1764. According to the Cleveland Museum, one of her sons commissioned it less than two months before she died, to commemorate her life and make an heirloom for later generations. A granddaughter remembered her as a little woman of sober conviction and strong Presbyterian faith.","i":"A portrait made to commemorate a very old woman near the end of her life was also an assertion of family continuity: the sons and grandchildren who inherited it would be reminded of her faith and character. The picture passed down through a line of female descendants, as it was meant to, until shortly before it was given to the museum. Her granddaughter's description of her, set beside the likeness, offers a rare chance to compare a sitter's remembered character with her image.","c":"Copley's reputation rests on candor, and an elderly sitter gives him ideal material: a little woman of strong faith made to command a large canvas by sheer presence. Without the flattery that younger sitters expected, it is among the most moving pictures of its kind in colonial art. Painted with firm drawing and close attention to the textures of lace, cloth and skin, it exemplifies the best of his Boston portraiture."},"nga:57541":{"d":"A seated woman with fair skin, a straight nose and thin red lips has her dark hair dressed up with flowers. She wears a multicolored dress with voluminous sleeves, a wide neckline and a sheer golden scarf, and she holds a hooked staff in one hand and a thin ribbon in the other. Behind her is a landscape with a large tree at the left and a cloudy sky at the right. Oil on canvas, about 79 by 69 centimeters, painted about 1764 by John Singleton Copley.","i":"The hooked staff is a shepherd's crook, and the flowers in the hair complete the pastoral costume: the sitter appears as a shepherdess in a rural idyll, a fancy-dress convention that flattered fashionable women in European portraits of the mid-eighteenth century. Copley's Boston clients wanted to be seen in the modern manner of London and Paris. The pose and attributes express taste and refinement rather than rural life, and the title names her by her husband, Mrs. Samuel Alleyne Otis.","c":"A charming pastoral conceit, more playful than Copley's usual sober portraits, with the sheer scarf and flowers adding softness. The costume and attributes are borrowed, and the landscape is generic, so the sitter's character is less present than the fiction. Copley's gifts show best in the careful rendering of fabric and the confident handling of color. It is a lighter side of the painter than the severe male portraits of the same decade."},"met:10533":{"d":"A woman wears a cap, a fichu and a ring, with a bow at her bodice, and holds a book, in a large canvas of about 128 by 102 centimeters dated 1766. The sitter was Mrs. Sylvanus Bourne, known by her husband's name as was the custom. Copley painted her in the years of his Boston prime, when sitters from New England's merchant and official families sat for him in quick succession.","i":"A book in a woman's hand signals piety and literacy, and in New England the Bible, the psalter and devotional works were the staple of a respectable household. The cap and fichu mark modest, matronly dress; the ring marks marriage. Copley's women of the 1760s are shown as faithful wives and managers of households, their portraits an expression of the colony's Puritan-derived values refined by new prosperity.","c":"A characteristic Copley matron: she is painted with a hard clarity that refuses to flatter, and the book and ring are rendered with the same exactness as the fichu and cap. The poise is deliberate; the effect is solid, dignified and rather severe. Where English portraitists of the day aim at grace, Copley aims at truth, and this likeness shows both the strength and the austere limit of his Boston manner."},"nga:55780":{"d":"A portrait of Harrison Gray painted in oil on canvas about 1767 by John Singleton Copley. At about 78 by 64 centimeters it is a bust- or half-length format, smaller than the full-scale portraits Copley made for his wealthiest sitters. Copley's portraits of these years usually show the sitter close up and strongly lit against a plain, dark ground. The picture belongs to the years when Copley was the leading portraitist of colonial Boston and his sitters were its merchants, officials and their families.","i":"Harrison Gray held the post of Treasurer of the province of Massachusetts Bay, one of the colony's most important offices, and remained loyal to the Crown when the Revolution came; he left for England in 1776. A Copley portrait of the 1760s fixed a colonial official's standing in the community in a plain, factual likeness, so this picture shows a public figure not long before the colony's political crisis.","c":"Copley's Boston portraits of the 1760s are prized for their frankness: the sitter's face is looked at squarely, without the idealizing gloss of London portraiture. A format of this size does not allow the full theater of his large canvases, so the face has to carry the picture. Within his Boston work this is a sober, representative portrait, valuable as a record of a man who lost his place when the colony broke from Britain."},"cma:94966":{"d":"Catherine Greene, aged thirty-four, is shown by the Boston painter John Singleton Copley in a fanciful costume inspired by ancient styles, in a pose borrowed from an English engraving. She pretends to lean against a rock that, as the Cleveland Museum notes, looks as insubstantial as a stage prop. The canvas is about 126 by 101 centimeters, painted in 1769, and the frame is the original.","i":"Her husband, the merchant John Greene, commissioned the portrait along with one of himself after thirteen years of marriage, and it places Mrs. Greene in an idealized realm far from daily cares, in a fashion of English portraiture in which sitters wore classical or timeless dress to suggest elegance. Copley's patrons saw in the style a claim to cosmopolitan taste. The pose copied from a print shows how colonial painters used engravings as a source of fashionable models.","c":"Copley's virtuosity with textiles is on display: the fanciful costume is rendered with dazzling delicacy, and its flattering design suits his patrons. The picture is more ornament than portrait, though; the pose is derived, the rock is a prop, and the effect is somewhat stagey. As an exercise in colonial elegance it succeeds, if with less truth than his more honest likenesses."},"met:10525":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Jeremiah Lee, painted in watercolor on ivory and only about 4 by 3 centimeters, dated about 1769. The sitter is presumably the wealthy Marblehead, Massachusetts, merchant of that name, who built a sumptuous mansion there and sat to Copley for a large portrait in 1769. At this size the likeness could be worn or carried. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"Watercolor on ivory was the European miniature medium, and Copley used it in the late 1760s to widen his repertoire; a miniature of a rich merchant could serve as a keepsake for a family member or a reduced replica of the larger portrait. Lee, a leader in the political resistance to British rule, died in 1775, soon after the war began, and the portraits made at the height of his prosperity became memorials of a man cut short.","c":"Copley's miniatures show his clarity of vision at tiny scale: the face is precisely observed, and the ivory's light gives the flesh a glow. They are slighter than his large canvases but have their own concentration, and in this one a rich merchant's firm features are summed up in a few square centimeters. A small, accomplished object that complements the grand portraits."},"met:15026":{"d":"A tiny oval portrait, not much bigger than a thumbnail, shows a young man from the chest up in a gold case with a ring for hanging at the top. His powdered hair is brushed back and rolled at the sides, and he has a smooth face, a steady, slightly sidelong gaze and a calm mouth. His white shirt is open at the neck under a blue coat with lilac facings and a red and gold embroidered waistcoat. The background is dark green-black. The work is watercolour on ivory, and the soft pink in the cheeks and lips is laid in with tiny touches. It measures only about three centimetres high.","i":"This is a self-portrait by John Singleton Copley, dated 1769. The Met says he presumably painted it, along with a nearly identical pastel portrait now at Winterthur Museum in Delaware, for his wife, Susanna Clarke, as a wedding gift. Copley married that November. Portrait miniatures were exchanged as keepsakes between lovers and family, and could be worn or carried, which is what the gold case and ring suggest. Copley is best known for large oil portraits of Boston citizens, so this intimate, personal image shows another side of his work.","c":"At this scale, restraint is everything, and Copley shows it. The face has real solidity, the colours are cool and clean, and the sidelong glance makes it feel like a living person rather than an emblem. He neither flatters himself nor strikes a grand pose, and the plain candour is appealing. The limits are those of the form: a miniature leaves little room for the texture and detail that make his oil portraits so striking. It is a small but telling record of a painter looking at himself with unusual honesty."},"cma:94979":{"d":"Nathaniel Hurd, a prominent Boston silversmith and engraver, looks out with a warm gaze and an unforced smile, in an open-collared shirt and a rakishly tilted turban that covers his shaved head in place of a ceremonial powdered wig. The informality is unusual for a portrait of this time, and the Cleveland Museum reads it as a sign of friendship between artist and sitter. The canvas is about 76 by 65 centimeters, painted about 1765.","i":"Hurd's trade was close to Copley's: as an engraver he made bookplates and prints, and as a silversmith he served the same prosperous clients. His informal dress signals the relaxed sociability of the artisan elite, a way of presenting oneself as a man of thought and taste rather than rank. Men of the period often wore a soft cap or turban at home in place of the wig, so the costume suggests an at-ease, private self rather than a public one.","c":"One of Copley's most winning portraits: the sitter's open expression, the relaxed dress and the lack of ceremonial props give an immediacy rare in colonial painting. The drawing is firm and the face alive; the informal turban, far from a gimmick, matches an unguarded smile. Next to his grander commissions it has less display but more warmth, and it shows how Copley could rise above flattering convention when the sitter was, apparently, a friend."},"met:10526":{"d":"A portrait of Joseph Sherburne, painted about 1767 to 1770 on a large canvas of about 127 by 102 centimeters, the standard format of Copley's finest colonial portraits of men. Nothing is recorded of the sitter's costume, pose or setting. Copley was by these years the leading portraitist in Boston, charging high prices for the careful rendering of fabric, props and surface that his clients admired.","i":"Colonial portraits of men of the merchant class generally showed them in good coats, with a table, a book or a letter, the signs of business and of gentlemanly status, and were meant for the family parlor or office. For a man of Sherburne's generation a Copley portrait was a financial and social investment: expensive, in a prized frame, and a sign that the sitter had arrived. Many Copley portraits remained in families for generations.","c":"At his best Copley treats a merchant as a monument: a sharp likeness, firm modeling and a convincing sense of fabric and room, held by a strong, simple composition. The risk is dryness: the poise can feel posed, the surfaces hard. In a picture of this period the likeness is the thing, and the viewer is meant to be convinced of a man's solidity, which Copley could do better than any other American painter before 1775."},"met:10535":{"d":"A portrait of the Boston lawyer Richard Dana, painted about 1770 on a canvas of about 102 by 127 centimeters, a wide horizontal format less usual among Copley's portraits of single sitters. Nothing is recorded of his costume or setting. Copley painted him when Dana was about seventy and a respected figure in the town, at a time when portraits of leading citizens carried political as well as personal meaning.","i":"A lawyer's portrait presents learning and integrity: the man of law as a pillar of the community, trusted in property, contracts and disputes. In Boston in 1770, a year of high tension after the Boston Massacre, the lawyers of the town were deeply engaged in the political crisis with Britain, and portraits of its prominent men acquired a civic dimension. Copley, whose own social circle included both loyalists and patriots, painted both sides.","c":"Copley's portraits of older men are among his best: the wear of age is unsoftened, character is carved into the face, and the surfaces of coat and skin have a matter-of-fact reality. The risk is a certain bluntness, since the sitters are rarely flattered, but the honesty is the point. A strong, unvarnished likeness, typical of the Boston portrait at its peak."},"met:10522":{"d":"A boy of about nine sits on a stone step at the base of a tall column, turned sideways but looking out at the viewer with a level, composed gaze. He wears a rose-pink coat and breeches, a white shirt with ruffles, a dark neck bow, white stockings and black buckled shoes. In one hand he dangles a thin gold chain, and at the end of it a grey squirrel clings to his leg, its great tail curled behind. A deep green hanging and a column fill the right, and at the left an open view shows trees and blue hills in evening light. The painting is oil on canvas, smoothly finished, with sharp detail in the fur and fabric.","i":"Daniel Crommelin Verplanck was born in 1762 into a prominent Dutch American family of the Hudson Valley, and the Met says he is shown here at nine, posed at the family's homestead, Mount Gulian. Copley presented the popular theme of a youth with a trained, gold-leashed pet squirrel, which the Met reads as a sign of patience and diligence in wealthy owners, a theme he had already used in his 1765 portrait of his half-brother Henry Pelham. Verplanck went on to graduate from Columbia College and to serve in the United States Congress. The portrait is dated 1771, three years before Copley left Boston for England.","c":"Copley's gift was a sharp eye for texture, and he uses it here on the squirrel's fur, the pink cloth and the lace. The boy's wary, grown-up look is the best thing in the picture: Copley paid honest attention to faces, which is why his American portraits feel so direct. The setting is less convincing. The column, curtain and landscape are stock props of English portraiture, and the child sits somewhat stiffly in them. The squirrel is a lively companion, but it looks pasted onto the boy's leg."},"met:10524":{"d":"A portrait of Gulian Verplanck, painted in 1771 on a canvas of about 91 by 71 centimeters, during Copley's visit to New York that year. Verplanck belonged to a leading New York family of Dutch descent, long prominent in trade and land, and the portrait was one of several Copley made of the family. Nothing is recorded of his dress or pose.","i":"Copley went to New York in the summer of 1771, welcomed by merchants who knew his reputation from Boston, and painted many leading citizens in a few months. For New York's old Dutch families, whose wealth rested on the Hudson trade and landholdings, a Copley portrait was a mark of cosmopolitan taste. The portraits also supply a vivid cross-section of the city's elite on the eve of the Revolution, a society that would soon be divided between patriots and loyalists.","c":"Here Copley meets a younger, more fashionable clientele than in Boston, and his New York portraits tend to be livelier and more socially poised. His strengths remain exactness and presence; his weaknesses are a certain hardness of finish and a tendency to treat sitters as monuments. A fine, direct portrait of a New York gentleman before the Revolution disrupted his world."},"met:10537":{"d":"A portrait of Samuel Verplanck, painted in 1771 on a canvas of about 76 by 64 centimeters, smaller than the portrait of Gulian Verplanck that Copley made the same year. Samuel belonged to the same New York merchant family, and Copley painted him during his 1771 stay in the city, working at the fast pace that his New York visit demanded. Nothing is recorded of his dress or pose.","i":"In 1771 Copley's patrons in New York included leading merchant families who valued the Boston painter's blunt realism. The Verplancks' commissions give a sense of a family's collective ambition: portraits of kin hung together in a house to show lineage and wealth. A portrait of a Verplanck belongs to the visual record that the old Dutch families used to display their continuity in a changing colonial society.","c":"A smaller canvas than its relation, but characteristically firm: the face is painted with exact, unidealized observation, and the surrounding details are subordinated to the likeness. Copley's formula, a single figure set close to the viewer, works because of the specificity of the face. Where it falls short is in variety: portraits of one sitter after another can seem alike in handling. A solid, individual likeness within a body of work of great consistency."},"nga:166440":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a man with a round face, light gray hair combed back to his ears and a double chin stands facing the viewer, his head turned partly to the right. He wears a dark brown coat with a white collar and cuffs. One hand is gloved in dark gray and holds the other glove together with a light brown cane, while the bare hand is free. He leans against a large brown column that fills the left side of the picture, and the wall behind him is dark brown. Oil on canvas, about 126 by 101 centimeters, painted about 1770 to 1772.","i":"Thomas Amory II belonged to a Boston merchant family, and the gloves, cane and column belong to the vocabulary of gentlemanly portraiture: cane and gloves mark leisure, and the column, borrowed from English society portraits, lends dignity and permanence. Copley set his colonial sitters in this borrowed language to show them as the equals of English gentlemen, while keeping the unflattering exactness of their faces.","c":"A thoroughly competent Boston portrait that relies on a formula, the leaning figure beside a column, which Copley used for several sitters. The glove and cane are handled with care, and the plain dark background keeps attention on the pale face. It lacks the idiosyncratic force of his best pictures, since the pose is more stock than character, but it shows the confidence and polish of his work in the years before he left for Europe."},"nga:50291":{"d":"A pale, clean-shaven man with a heavily lined, paunchy face sits in a forest-green wooden chair against a wall that is burgundy red up to the chair rail and moss green above. He rests both hands, stacked, on the nearer arm of the chair, his body and knees angled to the left, and turns his wide, square face toward the viewer with heavy-lidded eyes and one brow slightly raised. He wears a white shoulder-length wig and a sable-brown suit, with breeches buckled over black stockings. Oil on canvas, about 127 by 100 centimeters, signed and dated Boston, 1772.","i":"Copley's Boston sitters in 1772 included merchants, officials and their families, and a seated portrait in a plain room was among the most direct of the formats open to him. Eleazer Tyng is shown without a classical column or a landscape, only the painted wall and chair, and the absence of display makes the face the subject. The portrait asks to be read as the likeness of a real man, with age and temperament left unflattered.","c":"A commanding example of Copley's colonial manner at its most candid. The cocked brow, the sagging jowl and the heavy-lidded stare create a personality that no idealizing convention could supply, and the sparse room makes the sitter loom. The color is bold in its simple red, green and brown. It is less polished than the London portraits Copley would soon study, but the force of observation is greater, and many viewers will prefer it."},"met:10531":{"d":"A woman wearing a lace cap and a fichu, with a bow at her bodice and a striped fabric and tabletop among the details, is portrayed in a canvas of about 90 by 73 centimeters, dated 1773. The sitter was Hannah Fayerweather (1727–1790), the wife of John Winthrop, the Harvard professor of mathematics and natural philosophy. Copley painted her in his last full year in Boston, a year before he left for Europe.","i":"John Winthrop, a descendant of the first governor of Massachusetts Bay, was among the colonies' leading scientists, an astronomer who observed the transit of Venus; his wife shared a household that mixed Puritan lineage with Enlightenment science. A portrait of a professor's wife, shown at a table in matronly dress, presents a woman of probity rather than fashion, appropriate to an academic family in Cambridge.","c":"A calm, direct portrait in Copley's late Boston manner: lace and ribbon described with exact patience, the face modeled with sober realism, the pose composed and unadorned. It lacks the flamboyance of his portraits of fashionable women, but the restraint suits a scholar's wife. It shows how Copley adapted his means to his sitters: here, a plain, strong likeness with no ornament beyond the lace."},"nga:56727":{"d":"A portrait of Adam Babcock painted in oil on canvas about 1774 by John Singleton Copley, about 117 by 92 centimeters. Its size and date match those of the portrait of Abigail Smith Babcock, which suggests that the two were painted as a pair for a married couple. They belong to the last of Copley's Boston commissions, before he left for Europe in 1774.","i":"Portraits of a married couple were usually commissioned together and hung as a pair, expressing a household's standing and the marriage itself. The sitter is identified by the title, but nothing in it tells his occupation. The date is notable: Copley sailed for England in 1774 as political tension in Boston grew, and the Babcock pair belongs to the end of his colonial career.","c":"Copley's portraits of the early 1770s combine a firm, observed likeness with fine handling of fabric and a confident sense of pose, and a portrait of this date and size can be expected to show these virtues. As a pendant to his wife's portrait it adds a second, plainer voice to a pairing, and the two together are the more telling record of a colonial household at a turning point."},"nga:65885":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a pale woman in a carnation-pink gown and a fur-lined cloak sits on a divan, her body angled to the left, and gazes into the distance with large gray-blue eyes. Pewter-gray hair, brushed back from a high forehead, is bound with a loose swath of lace and pearls. A gold, pearl-lined belt gathers the gown, and the marine-blue cloak is lined with white fur speckled in black, suggesting ermine. She holds two ends of what may be a bracelet of fine purple beads. Strong light from the left shadows one side of her face. Oil on canvas, about 117 by 91 centimeters, about 1774.","i":"The loose gown, pearls and ermine-like fur are not the dress of daily life in Boston: they belong to the timeless, picturesque costume that English portraitists used to lift a sitter above fashion, a style Copley may have adopted as he looked toward London. The portrait is the companion to that of her husband, Adam Babcock. The gray hair and the unsparing light present the sitter as a mature woman of dignity rather than a young beauty.","c":"A late Boston portrait that shows Copley reaching beyond the blunt realism of his earlier work toward the grand manner. The dramatic light from the left and the rich rendering of fur, pearls and satin give the sitter presence, and her large, direct eyes keep her from becoming a costume piece. The pose is somewhat stagey, but it is among the most ambitious pictures of his last colonial months."},"nga:73435":{"d":"A small oil sketch, about 40 by 34 centimeters, painted in 1776 as preparation for The Copley Family, the large group portrait Copley made in London. The sketch is upright while the finished picture is wide, so it presumably studies the grouping of some of the figures at an early stage of the design. As a sketch it is likely to be loosely brushed, with the poses only broadly settled.","i":"Copley's family joined him in London in 1775, after he had left a Boston turned hostile to Loyalists, and the finished picture shows his own wife, children and father-in-law, with the painter himself standing behind. Oil sketches let a painter test groupings and light before committing to a large canvas. Copley, ambitious to make his mark in London, used the conversation piece, a family group in a domestic setting, to demonstrate his skill.","c":"As a sketch it is the more spontaneous half of the pair it forms with the finished picture, showing Copley working out relationships among figures before committing to the large canvas. It lacks the finish and polished surfaces of the final work, but its looseness is the interest: a glimpse of the painter's process. Of Copley's surviving sketches it is a valuable document more than an independent picture."},"nga:46098":{"d":"Seven figures gather on and around a couch in a room open to distant hills. At left an older man in a white wig, cravat and black jacket holds a squirming baby with a gold rattle; behind them a younger man in black stands in profile, glancing at the viewer, forearms on a low column, papers in hand. At right a girl in a lace cap and ivory gown stands with her arms crossed, while a woman in a sapphire-blue silk gown embraces two small children on a cranberry-red sofa and another child lies across the cushion. Oil on canvas, about 184 by 229 centimeters, 1776 to 1777.","i":"The painting shows Copley's own family, painted in London after they left Boston: his father-in-law Richard Clarke holds a grandchild, Copley himself stands behind with papers, and his wife Susanna embraces their children. A conversation piece on this scale, a large group portrait in an informal domestic setting, was a bold bid to win London patrons by lending portraiture the dignity of history painting. The doll and hat on the carpet are a playful touch.","c":"A bold bid for London, and a largely successful one: the grouping is clear, the silks and brocades are painted with relish, and the faces keep Copley's habit of direct observation. The interlocking poses make a pyramid, and the children's behavior, squirming, hugging and glancing away, enlivens the formal arrangement. The composition can feel slightly crowded and the colors a little sweet, but as a family portrait it ranks high among those of its period."},"met:10554":{"d":"A naked youth, Brook Watson, struggles in the water of Havana harbor as a shark closes on him and a boatload of rescuers strains to save him, in a small oil version of about 63 by 77 centimeters. The picture is described as a study for Copley's famous painting of the subject, made in London about 1778, and shows the composition in an immediate, compressed form: figures rigid with fear or effort, and the monster below.","i":"The event was real: in 1749 the fourteen-year-old Brook Watson, an orphan sailor, lost his right leg to a shark while swimming in Havana harbor, and he later became a successful merchant and Lord Mayor of London, commissioning Copley to paint the story. Copley turned a private disaster into a public drama, a contemporary episode treated with the weight of history painting, mixing terror and heroism. The picture helped establish his reputation in London.","c":"Copley makes a lesson in dramatic composition of the incident: the diagonal of the boat, the straining figures, the livid water and the pale body of the victim set against the dark shape of the shark make a high-pressure scene. As a sketch it has an energy of design the finished picture smooths. The theatrical gestures show a debt to the European grand manner, but the immediacy of a true event makes it a decisive step beyond portraiture."},"nga:46471":{"d":"A rowboat crowded with nine men tries to save a pale, nude young man who floats on his back in the water in the foreground, his blond hair swirling and his eyes wide, as a shark rolls up from the right with gaping jaws. At the stern a man poises a long harpoon, two others brace each other as they reach over the side, and a Black sailor at the center holds a rope looped around the swimmer's arm. A harbor with buildings and spires lines the horizon under steely clouds that lighten to yellow. Oil on canvas, about 182 by 230 centimeters, 1778.","i":"The picture records an event of 1749 in Havana harbor, when the fourteen-year-old Brook Watson, a ship's boy, was attacked by a shark while swimming and lost a leg below the knee. Watson later became a rich London merchant and Lord Mayor, and he commissioned the painting from Copley. Copley turned a private misadventure into a history painting, a genre normally reserved for noble or sacred subjects, by treating a contemporary rescue with heroic drama.","c":"The painting made Copley's name in London, and it has the immediacy of reportage staged with the structure of a Baroque altarpiece. The diagonal of the boat, the pale body against dark water and the open jaws give the drama a hard, almost theatrical clarity. The faces in the boat are individual, not stock, and the harbor behind adds depth. If it is melodramatic, the melodrama is earned, and the picture helped open the way for American-born history painters."},"nga:34047":{"d":"A crowded hall of men in elaborate robes with curled white hair, under a high curved ceiling with recessed panels. Light streams down from a round window near the top left onto a group right of center, where one man appears to have fainted and the others support his head and limbs. The rest of the assembly looks toward them. Draped fabric hangs near the top right and a tapestry below the window at left. The palette is restricted to pale yellow, brown, cream, white and gray. Oil on canvas, about 53 by 64 centimeters, 1779.","i":"The picture shows the moment on 7 April 1778 when William Pitt, Earl of Chatham, collapsed in the House of Lords while speaking against giving up the American colonies; he died a month later. Pitt had been a great wartime leader, and his collapse was seen as a national event. Copley made it a history painting of a contemporary scene, a bold choice at the time, and this small version is generally described as a preparatory sketch for the large painting now in the Tate.","c":"In this form the picture works as a keen sketch: the shaft of light, the ring of onlookers and the central collapse are clearly staged, and the near-monochrome palette lets the structure of the composition show. The finished version adds a gallery of recognizable peers, a grand but heavy effect; the sketch is lighter and more dramatic. It also marks Copley's turn from colonial portraiture to the London history painting that tested his powers."},"met:10527":{"d":"A boy in the uniform of a midshipman, the junior officer rank in the Royal Navy, is the subject of this large portrait, about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted in 1782. Augustus Brine was one of the many boys who went to sea as officers in training. Copley painted him in London during the American war, in which Britain's fleets were engaged around the world.","i":"A midshipman's portrait recorded a family's investment: boys of the gentry and middle class entered the Navy at twelve or thirteen, and a portrait in uniform marked a stage in a promising career, with pride and perhaps anxiety. The Royal Navy was both the means of Britain's empire and the best chance of advancement for many families. A boy painted by a famous American-born painter now settled in London shows how Copley made a place in the British portrait trade.","c":"A boy given the grave composure of an officer: the uniform and the pose make him a small adult, and the face has Copley's characteristic exactness. The picture lacks the fresh directness of his earlier American portraits; it is smoother, more polished and more generic. It is a competent English-period work and an engaging record of a very young face under naval authority."},"nga:993":{"d":"A knight in armor strides across a balustraded terrace toward two women, his visor raised, a white plume on his helmet, a red cross on his chest, a round shield held over his head and a lance at his side. At the center a woman in a long white gown, a yellow glow around her head, holds a gold chalice with a snake curling from the cup and a brown book. Facing her, a second woman in topaz-blue satin touches her wrist, and a silver anchor rests against one woman's arm. Trees, columns and red curtains frame them. Oil on canvas, about 214 by 273 centimeters, 1793.","i":"The subject comes from Edmund Spenser's poem The Faerie Queene, in which the Red Cross Knight, who stands for Holiness, meets the sisters Fidelia and Speranza, Faith and Hope, in the House of Holiness. Their emblems, the chalice with a serpent, the book and the anchor, were traditional signs of those virtues. Copley painted it late in his career, when literary and historical subjects were his ambition in London, and it appealed to a taste for English poetry and chivalry.","c":"A late attempt at poetic, allegorical painting that lacks the immediacy of Copley's portraits and of the Watson picture. The theatrical staging, with its stiff gestures and glowing halo, never quite brings Spenser's allegory to life, though the satin, the armor and the salmon sky are richly painted. It shows the ambition of a painter trying to rise in the hierarchy of genres, and it demonstrates why his best work remained the portraits and the contemporary dramas."},"nga:46008":{"d":"A man in a vivid red uniform with silver epaulets and buttons and a yellow collar stands beside a chestnut horse before a deep landscape, his wavy gray hair wind-tousled as he looks off to the left. He holds a black hat and a pale glove. Two women reach toward his arm: one, in black with a frilly white collar and a closed green parasol, stands on a step; the other, in white with a long veil, touches her veil and his hand. An oversized urn and trees stand at left, with sage-green hills and rocky mountains beyond. Oil on canvas, about 258 by 340 centimeters, 1800 to 1801.","i":"The sitters are Colonel William Fitch and his sisters Sarah and Ann, and the huge canvas is a family portrait in the grand English manner, with a horse, a uniform, a parasol and an urn as the props of rank. The emphasis on the brother's military dress and his sisters' attachment suits a society in which a military career was a family honor. The urn and the landscape can be read as notes of family feeling or memory, though the evidence for any particular story is limited.","c":"A very large late canvas whose scale outstrips its drama. The red coat and the horse give it a strong visual center, and Copley still paints silks, braid and faces with care, but the poses are stiff, the sisters seem attached to the colonel rather than animated by him, and the landscape is a backdrop. Compared with The Copley Family of some twenty-five years earlier, it shows how the English portrait routine had tempered his early directness."},"nga:992":{"d":"A man with a round face and long gray curly hair sits on a decorative red chair, his body turned to the left and his head turned to look at the viewer. He wears a red robe with thick white fur trim and rests one arm on a red table covered with sheets of white paper. A stone pillar rises behind him at the left, and green curtains are draped at the right. Oil on canvas, about 145 by 119 centimeters, painted in 1804 by John Singleton Copley, long settled in London by then.","i":"The red robe trimmed with white fur and the papers on the table mark official rank: Baron here is probably the judicial title of a judge of the Exchequer, not a peerage, though the title alone does not settle it. The column and curtain are the standard apparatus of the state portrait. Copley painted many such images of the English establishment after settling in London, and this one belongs to the end of his career, when fashion was moving away from his manner.","c":"A solid late portrait in which the red robe and white fur supply most of the interest. The pose is conventional and the paint smoother and less searching than in his Boston work, but the head is alert, and the details of paper, chair and drapery are handled with the professional certainty of a veteran. It is a competent official likeness, not a revelation, and it shows what the London market asked of him."},"met:11723":{"d":"George Washington is shown as the victorious general at the siege of Yorktown, with a horse, a cannon and a flag among the details, in a portrait of about 91 by 69 centimeters, dated about 1782. James Peale, a younger brother of the more famous Charles Willson Peale, painted several portraits of the general after the allied victory of October 1781, which ended major fighting in the war.","i":"The portrait celebrates the great victory at Yorktown in October 1781, where Washington's forces and the French besieged the British army of Lord Cornwallis into surrender. The flag, cannon and horse tell of command and war; the type belongs to the tradition of the military commander's portrait. For a young republic an image of Washington at Yorktown was a patriotic icon, to be hung in a public or private place as a reminder of victory and leadership.","c":"James Peale was a sound but unremarkable portraitist in oil, and this picture is more significant as an image than as a painting: the figure is correct and dignified, the accessories neatly arranged, and the likeness conforms to the Peale family's formula for Washington. Compared with Charles Willson Peale's more vigorous portraits or with Stuart's later images it seems stiff. It is valuable chiefly as one of the early, widely repeated icons of the first president."},"met:16046":{"d":"A miniature portrait of George Washington, painted in watercolor on ivory and only about 4 by 2.5 centimeters, dated 1782, with an eagle among the details. The size is that of a locket or a bracelet clasp, and the eagle, adopted that year as the central emblem of the Great Seal of the United States, probably marks the sitter as a national hero as well as a general. James Peale, who had served in the army before turning to miniature painting, made several likenesses of Washington.","i":"In the years after Yorktown, Washington's likeness was in demand as a patriotic keepsake, worn by admirers and officers as a sign of allegiance, and miniatures offered the most portable form. The eagle gave the likeness a national meaning that went beyond the individual. Such small portraits circulated among families, veterans and political supporters, helping to build the public image of Washington as the republic's founding figure.","c":"At this size the portrait is all economy: a firm head, steady features and a few lines of costume, with the eagle as a flourish. James Peale's miniatures are among the best of the American school, with a clarity of drawing and a freshness of color that his larger oils lack. It is an intimate, serious portrait, and perhaps closer in feel to the living man than many a grander picture."},"met:16067":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Martha Washington, painted in watercolor on ivory and only about 4 by 2.5 centimeters, dated 1782. She wears a cap with a ribbon, a fichu and lace, with an eagle among the details, and its size and date match those of James Peale's miniature of her husband in the same museum, so the two were probably made as a pair, small enough for a locket or a pair of clasps.","i":"Martha Washington, then about fifty, had spent the winters of the war with her husband in the army's camps, which made her familiar to officers and soldiers alike. In 1782, the year the eagle was adopted for the Great Seal, a likeness of the commander's wife with a national emblem tied her to the cause. Miniatures of the Washingtons were in demand as keepsakes of a famous couple.","c":"A matter-of-fact likeness of a woman of about fifty: Peale gives the cap, fichu and lace in crisp touches and a modest, unflattered face. The miniature is more document than ornament, and it shares with the portrait of her husband a frankness that suited the couple's public image. It lacks the polish of his later work, but the simplicity suits its sitter."},"met:15133":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed gentleman, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.4 by 3.2 centimeters, dated about 1785. Nothing is recorded of his dress, hair or expression. James Peale, who had served as an officer in the Revolutionary War and then learned to paint miniatures from his brother Charles Willson Peale, was in these years establishing himself as a miniaturist in Philadelphia.","i":"In the first years of peace, a portrait miniature was a modest luxury within reach of merchants, officers and professionals, who exchanged them with relatives and sweethearts. The oval ivory could be set in a locket or small frame and carried in a pocket or worn, and many were kept for generations without the sitter's name; the anonymity of this one is a common fate of such small objects.","c":"Without a name, the miniature must be judged by its craft: an oval of a few centimeters in which a face, a collar and a ground must all be settled with a handful of touches. Peale's early work in the medium is typically small, direct and unshowy, with a restrained palette. A representative piece from the years in which the American miniature was finding its confidence."},"met:15134":{"d":"A miniature self-portrait of James Peale, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.4 by 3.2 centimeters, dated 1789, when he was about forty. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. Peale left the army in 1779 and learned miniature painting from his elder brother Charles Willson Peale, and by 1789 he was an established practitioner in Philadelphia, the largest city of the new republic.","i":"A self-portrait for a miniaturist was both a sample of his skill and a keepsake: it showed prospective sitters what he could do and left his family an image of himself. Peale belonged to a dynasty of painters, led by his brother Charles Willson, and his own daughters, Anna Claypoole, Margaretta and Sarah Miriam, would become painters in their turn.","c":"A painter's own face is the one sitter who needs no flattery, and a self-portrait is likely to be more candid than his commissions. The size is small, the means modest and the effect direct, with the unaffected clarity that marked the best of Philadelphia portraiture of the 1780s. It is a small, personal document more than a showpiece, and a rare chance to see the miniaturist as he saw himself."},"met:11729":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 4 centimeters, dated 1791. Nothing is recorded of her dress or hair. By 1791 James Peale had been painting miniatures for about a dozen years, and this ivory is a little larger than his locket-sized pieces of the 1780s, a scale at which a woman's likeness could be framed or held in the hand.","i":"Ivory, sliced thin and prepared with a degreased, smooth surface, gives watercolor a luminous, translucent quality, as the white of the support shines through the thin paint and makes flesh glow. A woman's portrait on ivory was typically a personal gift, to a husband, a fiancé or a child, and was often worn or kept in a case. Many such portraits lost their sitters' names as they passed through families, as this one has.","c":"At five centimeters the painter has just room for a face, a few details of costume and a plain background, and the best Peale miniatures make that enough. Their qualities are clarity of drawing, delicate coloring and a reserved, refined presence; their limitation is the polite sameness of many sitters. Without a name, this one is a pleasant, typical specimen of the medium at the height of its popularity in the early republic."},"met:15135":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed gentleman, in watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 4 centimeters, dated 1791. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. In 1791 Philadelphia was the seat of the national government, and James Peale's clientele included merchants, lawyers and visiting officials, any of whom might have wished a small likeness to send home or give to a family.","i":"The early 1790s were the first years of the federal government, and Philadelphia, its temporary capital from 1790, drew congressmen, diplomats and office-seekers from all the states. Such a floating, ambitious population was a ready market for portraits that could be carried home: the miniature was a souvenir of a stay and a record of a face for those left behind. The sitter's name has not survived.","c":"A competent, characteristic miniature: the likeness has a polite directness, the touch is fine and the finish clean, with the cool, pale coloring that Peale typically favored in these years. It is not an inventive picture, but the economy of the form is part of the pleasure. For its date it shows how quickly American miniature painting had matured in a decade of peace."},"met:15136":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Frances Wardale, Mrs. John McAllister, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 4 centimeters, dated 1791. She wears a cap, a fichu, a bow and a ribbon at the neck, small details of costume that place her in the fashions of the early federal period. The picture names her by both her husband's surname and her own maiden name.","i":"The cap, fichu and ribbons are the standard costume of a respectable married woman of the 1790s: the cap signaled marriage and modesty, the fichu covered the neckline with soft linen or gauze, and ribbons and bows added a touch of fashion. A miniature of this kind was often made as a gift within the family, perhaps for her husband, and its title preserves the naming custom by which a married woman was identified through her husband and her family of birth.","c":"Peale gives Mrs. McAllister's costume the careful, crisp attention he gives to faces, and the soft whites of cap and fichu frame the face in a way the small oval makes compelling. Like most of his women she is shown with a gentle, composed reserve rather than a sharply individual character. A graceful example of the federal miniature, valuable for naming its sitter."},"met:11724":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man named Jonathan Trumbull, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 4 centimeters, dated about 1792. The sitter is probably Jonathan Trumbull Jr. (1740–1809), a Connecticut political figure who had been Washington's secretary during the Revolution and served in Congress in Philadelphia, including as Speaker of the House from 1791 to 1793. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"If the sitter is Trumbull Jr., he belonged to a Connecticut dynasty: his father was the colony's governor during the Revolution, his brother John was the painter of the Declaration of Independence, and he himself rose to the Speaker's chair, to the Senate and finally to the governorship of Connecticut. In 1792 Congress sat in Philadelphia, where a politician could sit to a miniaturist between sessions.","c":"A political portrait in miniature is a modest thing, and the interest here is the sitter's place in the new republic's story rather than any bravura of handling. Peale gives the face the straightforward clarity of his federal portraits; the individuality is moderate. What the picture offers is an intimate, unofficial look at a public man at the moment when Congress was first learning to be a national legislature."},"met:11727":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Benjamin Lincoln, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5 by 4 centimeters, dated 1794. Lincoln, a Massachusetts major general in the Revolutionary War, was the officer who received the British surrender at Yorktown in 1781 and afterward served as secretary of war and as collector of the port of Boston. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"A portrait of a hero of the war was a patriotic object as well as a family keepsake. James Peale knew the war from the inside, having served as a captain, and had painted Washington at Yorktown; Lincoln, who had been captured at Charleston in 1780 and who dispersed the insurgents of Shays' Rebellion in 1787, belonged to the generation whose service the new republic honored. In 1794 such likenesses carried a note of civic pride.","c":"The scale ensures a plain likeness without heroic trappings: a general painted as a man of about sixty rather than a symbol. Peale models the face with small, patient touches, and the result is more intimate than a public portrait. Its modest scale and quiet handling suit a figure better known for steadiness than for dash, and the picture records a veteran with a candor that grander images avoided."},"met:11733":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Rebecca Richards, Mrs. John Sevier Jr., painted in watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated 1794. Nothing is recorded of her dress or pose. The ivory is noticeably larger than the miniatures James Peale made a decade earlier, part of a trend toward bolder, more easily displayed likenesses in the 1790s. The title preserves her maiden name as well as her married one.","i":"The title names the sitter through her husband with her maiden name added, a convention that keeps her own family visible in the record. Miniatures like this were commonly commissioned at a betrothal or marriage, or as a keepsake before a journey, and were exchanged among relatives as pledges of affection and memory.","c":"At this larger size Peale has room for more of the sitter: the face, the hair and a fuller ground, and the proportions suit the elegant oval. The style is the established federal manner: fine stipple, pale clear color, a gentle reserve. It is a graceful, accomplished miniature, though without the spark that would make it stand out among its many siblings."},"met:14313":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed gentleman, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated 1794. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. At nearly 6 centimeters tall the ivory is about half again as tall as the miniatures James Peale made in the early 1780s, a growth that follows the period's taste for bolder, more readily displayed likenesses.","i":"Miniatures of the 1790s grew larger and were more often set in frames, so some may have been displayed on a wall or table rather than kept in a case, and exchanged between families to record kinship and friendship. The size of the ivory reflects that wider role. The sitter's name has not been preserved, and the picture has become a portrait of a type, the Federal-era gentleman.","c":"Peale's technique, tiny stipples and hatching in watercolor over ivory, builds flesh in a delicate, translucent fabric of dots. This one is a competent example of the mature manner. It trades in the usual virtues: clarity, quiet modeling and decorum. The risk is the formula, since faces of this date often resemble one another."},"met:11722":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Richard Thomas III, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimeters, dated 1796. The numeral in his name marks a family name carried through three generations, and the picture's size, much larger than Peale's early miniatures, probably reflects a new, more prominent role for the miniature as an object to be framed and displayed. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"Naming a son for his father and grandfather was a way of continuing a family line and its property, and a portrait of the third Richard Thomas, however small, belongs to the same impulse: a record of a young man's place in his family. Miniatures were often set in gilded frames or in cases with a plaited-hair reverse, and exchanged among relatives as a record of affection and descent.","c":"At seven centimeters Peale's miniatures begin to have the presence of small oil portraits, and this one shows a sitter drawn with careful, fine strokes against a pale ground. The likeness is sober and plain. What remains unusual is the larger format, which allows more detail in costume and a firmer sense of the sitter; the result is a solid, dignified document of a Federal man."},"met:11730":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Moses Cox, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 8 by 6 centimeters, dated 1796, among the larger ivories James Peale painted. At this size the miniature approaches a small framed portrait, and the likeness may have been hung or placed on a table rather than worn or carried. Nothing is recorded of the sitter's dress or expression.","i":"By the mid-1790s the miniature was competing with the fashion for cheap profile silhouettes and with small oil portraits, and some miniaturists answered with larger ivories that could be displayed and compared with oils. Even so, the small oval remained the quickest way to secure a good likeness. Little is known of the sitter, and the portrait stands as a personal document of a name and a face.","c":"A miniature at this size demands more of a painter, since errors of drawing and modeling show, and Peale generally meets the demand with clean, steady work. The face is built up in fine touches, the coloring is cool and the ground restrained. If the result is more earnest than inspired, it shows a craftsman at ease with a format that was becoming grander. A solid, unshowy specimen of the Federal miniature."},"met:15138":{"d":"A miniature portrait of William Jonas Keen, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5 centimeters, dated 1796. The name Keen, from the Swedish Kyn, belongs to one of the old Swedish families of the Delaware Valley, where Swedes had settled in the 1630s, though it is not recorded whether the sitter belonged to it. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"Philadelphia and the Delaware Valley in the 1790s were a patchwork of English, German, Swedish, Welsh and Irish families, many of them Quakers or members of the old Swedish Lutheran and later Episcopal churches, and Peale's sitters came from all of these groups. A miniature was a personal object that crossed those boundaries, exchanged at betrothals and as keepsakes alike. The sitter's middle name, Jonas, may hint at a Scandinavian heritage, though Jonas was common in many families.","c":"A plain, well-judged portrait in Peale's mature miniature manner: neat drawing, controlled color and a calm face set in a modest oval. The painter does not seek to impose personality, so the sitter's character reads through a restrained, honest likeness. It is a typical product of a busy and reliable craftsman, which is also its virtue: the small, dependable pleasure of work done well."},"met:15139":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Mary Justice, Mrs. Joseph Cooper, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimeters, dated 1797. She wears a cap, lace, a shawl and a fichu, with a pendant among the details. The picture is large for a miniature, and gives James Peale room to show his handling of soft fabrics and a delicate piece of jewelry.","i":"A pendant, a shawl and lace mark a woman of comfortable means in the late 1790s, when shawls, often Indian or imitation Kashmir imports, were becoming fashionable accessories. Such miniatures served as records of a marriage and a household, and often descended with the husband's. Giving the sitter's maiden name, Justice, as the title does, preserves her family of birth in the record.","c":"The fabrics are the pleasure of this miniature: the soft lace, the folded fichu and the shawl give Peale room to display his touch with gauzy textures, and the pendant adds a glint. The face is composed and a little generic, but the surrounding detail shows the care he gave. A charming, accomplished miniature of the high Federal style."},"met:11726":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Jonathan Pinkney Jr., painted in watercolor on ivory, about 8 by 6 centimeters, dated 1798, one of the largest of James Peale's ivories. It forms a pair with the portrait of his wife, Elizabeth Munroe Pinkney, made the same year in the same medium. The ivory is about twice the height of Peale's miniatures of the early 1780s. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"Husband and wife miniatures painted in the same year probably mark a marriage, often made at or soon after the wedding. The similar size and shared date suggest that the portraits were conceived together, to be framed side by side, a small domestic equivalent of the pendant oil portraits of wealthier families. The title, with its Jr., records a family name passed from father to son.","c":"A solid, dignified portrait at a generous size: the young man is described with firm, clear touches against a quiet ground, and the finish is as clean as the best of Peale's work. The pairing with his wife's portrait gives it additional interest as a record of a marriage. It is a little reserved, with the personality held in check, and without special flourish."},"met:11732":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Elizabeth Munroe, Mrs. Jonathan Pinkney Jr., painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimeters, dated 1798, the companion of her husband's. She wears a turban, a fichu, a ruffle and lace, and the turban in particular was a fashionable accessory of the late 1790s, an echo of Oriental dress that gave an air of cosmopolitan elegance.","i":"The turban, borrowed from Ottoman and Indian dress through travelers' accounts and stage costume, became a fashionable item of women's headwear in the 1790s and into the 1800s, signaling taste and worldliness. With the fichu and lace, it presents a woman of the new republic as both modest and stylish. A miniature like this was a record of dress and a flattering likeness at once, and its title preserves the naming custom of the age.","c":"The turban gives the portrait its dash, a bold shape against the quiet oval and a sign of fashion's reach into the small world of the miniature. Peale handles it, and the lace and ruffle beneath, with his usual delicacy. The face is composed and generalized, as in most of his women; the interest is in costume and in the pairing with her husband. A charming, fashionable example of its moment."},"met:15137":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed gentleman, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated about 1795. Its size matches that of another unnamed gentleman of 1794 in the same museum, which suggests that Peale worked from standard ivories cut to a few regular sizes. Peale was then in his mid-forties and well established in Philadelphia. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression.","i":"Ivory for miniatures was imported in the tusk and sliced into thin sheets, and painters often bought ivory ready cut, to be fitted into cases and frames made in standard sizes. A matching size is therefore a clue to a workshop's routine, while a missing name makes the image a record of an anonymous sitter. Together such details show the miniature as a craft product made in series, yet each one is a one-of-a-kind likeness.","c":"Work of this kind is best judged in the grain: the stipple of the flesh, the economy of the costume, the even tone of the ground. Peale does all of these with a steady, professional hand, and the result is sound without being memorable. A reliable piece of workshop production whose quiet merit is exactly its consistency."},"met:15140":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man named Curtis Clay, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimeters, dated 1798. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. Clay was one of the many named sitters who came to James Peale in the late 1790s, when his miniatures were among their largest. Like most of his sitters of the decade, he is shown on a small oval of ivory meant for a case or frame.","i":"A named sitter makes a miniature a document as well as an image: descendants could pass it on with a story, and historians can trace the sitter's family and place. In the late 1790s, with Philadelphia's repeated yellow fever outbreaks, the miniature's role as a personal link may have been heightened, a small portable image of those one might lose.","c":"Peale's ordinary sitters are the strength of his miniatures: unfashionable faces drawn with a plain clarity, undistorted by flattery. A man named Clay, painted at this generous size, is typical: a likeness that records rather than idealizes. It will not astonish, but it has the honest worth of competent craftsmanship."},"met:15141":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed gentleman, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 6 centimeters, dated 1799, the last year before Philadelphia ceased to be the national capital. Nothing is recorded of his dress or expression. By this date James Peale had been painting miniatures for twenty years, and he would later turn increasingly to oil, notably still lifes.","i":"In the last year of the eighteenth century the American miniature was at its height and about to decline: larger oil portraits, cheap profile machines and, in time, photography would crowd it out. The anonymity of this sitter is a reminder that such portraits were made for private reasons and passed to descendants without records. Philadelphia's role as capital ended in 1800, scattering the congressmen and officials who had been a ready clientele.","c":"After twenty years' experience Peale's hand is secure: the face is built with fine, even stippling, the colors are clear, the pose is plain. The picture lacks nothing in craft and shows no loss of energy, but it also shows no change: the late miniatures repeat the formula of the earlier ones with greater size and sureness. A well-made piece by a master of a small art."},"met:15142":{"d":"A miniature portrait of William Smallwood, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5 centimeters, dated about 1798, by James Peale, a Philadelphia miniaturist and the younger brother of Charles Willson Peale. The sitter is cataloged as a boy; nothing is recorded of dress or pose. Children's miniatures like this were made as keepsakes for parents and grandparents, and were prized by families in an age of high child mortality.","i":"Portraits of children recorded a stage of life that parents knew would pass quickly, and in an age when many children died young a likeness could become precious. A miniature, small enough to carry in a pocket or wear, was an ideal form for this sentiment, and James Peale, who raised daughters of his own, was well placed to understand it.","c":"Children are hard subjects for a miniaturist: their features are soft, their expressions mobile, and the idealizing conventions of the form can turn them into little adults. Peale usually avoids the worst of this, giving young sitters a fresh, direct look. If this one has the formula's limits, it also has the charm that comes from a tender subject and a delicate touch."},"met:15143":{"d":"A boy is portrayed in watercolor on a thin sheet of ivory, in a miniature only about 7 by 5 cm, small enough to rest in the palm of a hand. The museum record describes neither his clothing nor the background, so neither is described here. Peale's miniatures are normally bust-length likenesses, often oval, with a plain or lightly clouded ground, made to be kept in a case, worn as a brooch or locket, or passed between relatives.","i":"Miniatures of children recorded a boy at a particular age, and families treasured them as keepsakes, sometimes with a lock of hair sealed behind the glass. Ivory suited the purpose: its translucency lends young skin a soft inner glow that opaque oil paint struggles to match. James Peale, a Revolutionary War veteran and younger brother of the portraitist Charles Willson Peale, painted many such likenesses in Philadelphia around 1800.","c":"At this scale a miniaturist has only a few tools: a firm placing of the features, flesh built from tiny hatched strokes, and thin color that lets the ivory shine through. Peale was among the steadiest American practitioners of the 1790s, plain and honest rather than dashing, and that restraint suits a child's face. It is best taken as a representative example of his careful, unshowy manner, not as a standout."},"met:11731":{"d":"A man named Francis Depau is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, in a miniature about 7 cm high and under 6 cm wide, dated 1802. The museum record does not describe his dress or setting, so neither is described here. A man's miniature of this date would typically be a bust-length likeness, painted in fine touches of watercolor with the pale ivory left to glow through the thin flesh tones.","i":"Miniatures were commissioned as gifts for a wife or fiancée, as keepsakes before a long journey, or as private records of a person's appearance, and their size made them easy to carry. By 1802 James Peale had been painting them for roughly two decades and was among the best-established miniaturists in Philadelphia. The museum record gives no biography of the sitter, so the occasion for this commission is unknown.","c":"Peale's male miniatures are at their best when the face is allowed to carry the picture: a clear structure of eyes, nose and mouth, modeled in small touches without ornament. That plainness makes them sturdier than the more romantic miniatures then fashionable in Europe, though also less seductive. A work of 1802 shows an artist in command of a well-tried routine rather than one looking for something new."},"met:14353":{"d":"An unnamed woman is shown in a very small miniature, about 6 by 4.6 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1800. The museum gives no description beyond the title, so her dress, hair and setting are left undescribed. The format suggests a bust-length likeness in an oval, of the kind fitted behind glass in a gold or gilt case and kept in a pocket, a drawer or on a ribbon at the neck.","i":"The title 'Portrait of a Lady' is what catalogues use when a sitter's name has been lost, a common fate for miniatures, which passed from hand to hand more easily than paintings on canvas. Such likenesses were exchanged as tokens of affection between spouses, parents and children, or engaged couples, and were often worn as jewelry. Around 1800 the fashion for them in the United States was in full swing.","c":"Anonymity removes the sitter's story and leaves only the craft, which in a miniature is considerable: a face a few centimeters high has to read clearly, with believable skin and a living gaze. Peale's strength was a plain, reliable likeness. The genre rewards finish and charm over invention, so work like this is admirable without being surprising."},"met:14354":{"d":"William Young is the sitter of this ivory miniature, painted in watercolor and measuring about 7 by 6 cm, dated 1807. Only the title, the date and the medium are recorded, so his dress and the background are not described here. The picture belongs to Peale's mature miniature practice: a small bust-length portrait, typically oval, with the face modeled in tiny strokes against a soft, unobtrusive ground.","i":"In 1807 miniatures were ordinary family objects, ordered for a wedding, an engagement, a departure or a death. They were typically enclosed in a case, sometimes with a compartment for hair at the back, and stayed in private hands instead of going on display. The record gives no biography of the sitter, so the occasion for this one is unknown.","c":"Peale's miniatures rarely flatter. They favor even light, a clear outline and an unfussy ground, so the sitter is seen steadily rather than glamorously. That candor is the main virtue of the type and also its limit, since it leaves little room for the softer, more romantic effects fashionable in Europe. As an example of his working method, it is dependable rather than inspired."},"met:15144":{"d":"The sitter, named in the title as Dr. Henry Nicolls Kitchin, appears in a watercolor on ivory of about 7 by 6 cm, dated 1807. The museum record gives no description of the likeness, so costume and setting are not described here. As with other miniatures from Peale's hand, the work is probably a bust-length oval portrait, finely hatched, with the ivory's pale warmth visible in the lights of the face.","i":"The title 'Dr.' suggests a physician, although the record here does not say. Professional men were steady clients for miniaturists, who offered a likeness smaller and usually less costly than an oil portrait, easy to give to a wife or fiancée. Such a portrait would have been a private object, kept in a case and shown to family and friends, not hung in a public room.","c":"In a picture this small the face does nearly all the work, and Peale's habit was to put it down with patient accuracy: the planes of the cheek, the set of the mouth and the direction of the eyes given clearly, the color kept light and clean. The result is a sound likeness in a familiar mold, more valuable as a record of a person than as an adventurous work of art."},"met:15145":{"d":"A man named Anthony Wayne Robinson is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, in a miniature about 7 by 5.7 cm, dated 1811. The museum does not describe his clothing or the background, so the description stays general: a small, bust-length likeness, the face built from many tiny strokes of color, with the warmth of the ivory glowing through the thin paint in the lights.","i":"The sitter's first two names echo General Anthony Wayne, the Pennsylvania commander of the Revolutionary War, perhaps a sign of parental admiration, though the record says nothing on the point. If so, it is a reminder that the Revolution still shaped family life a generation later. Miniatures like this were made mainly for the family circle, to be held, shown and handed on.","c":"Peale's men's miniatures are unsentimental and sturdy: the head sits squarely, the paint is kept thin so that the ivory supplies the light, and ornament is kept to a minimum. Beside the more romantic work of Malbone they can look matter-of-fact. That plainness is also their appeal, and it is a fair summary of what this miniature offers: a clear, unpretentious likeness."},"met:15146":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Beulah Elmy Twining and also as Mrs. Torbert, is shown in a miniature of about 7 by 5.7 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1811. The museum gives no description of her dress, hair or setting, so none is invented here. It would normally be a bust-length likeness in an oval, delicately toned, made for a locket or a small case.","i":"Giving both a maiden name and a married name is the usual way to identify a woman who appears in records under each. In 1811 a portrait miniature might mark an engagement or wedding, or be sent to a relative far away, and its small size made it an easy gift to post or carry by hand. The record here gives no further biography.","c":"In the 1810s American miniaturists were increasingly drawn to the looser, more fluid handling associated with Edward Greene Malbone; Peale generally kept to a firmer, more linear method. That gives his portraits clarity and steadiness, and can leave the surface slightly dry. A likeness of this date is a good example of a seasoned artist holding to a method that had served him well."},"met:11721":{"d":"A woman, Anna Claypoole Peale, is shown in a miniature about 7.5 cm high and 6.8 cm wide, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1812. The museum gives no further description of her appearance, so only the type can be described: a bust-length likeness, finely stippled and hatched, with a soft ground that lets the face carry the picture. The sitter shares the painter's surname, and her name matches that of his daughter.","i":"The sitter was James Peale's daughter, who became one of the leading American miniaturists of the next generation. Peale trained his daughters in the family's craft, so a father's portrait of a daughter is also a link in a workshop chain: one painter looking closely at another. As a family portrait it was probably a personal keepsake rather than a commission.","c":"Portraits of relatives are often an artist's most direct work, since no client's taste has to be satisfied. Presumably the aim here was a fair likeness rather than an effect, which is in keeping with the family studio's values: clear drawing, clean color, honest observation. That plain manner was what the next generation of the family, Anna included, learned from him."},"met:11728":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Martha Stewart Wilson, is shown in oil on canvas, in a portrait about 72 cm high and 61 cm wide, painted in 1814. The museum record gives no description of her dress, pose or background, so only the type can be described: a bust or half-length portrait for a private parlor, far larger than the artist's ivory miniatures, with oil paint allowing broader handling of fabric and background.","i":"Where a miniature was carried or worn, an oil portrait of this size was hung, usually in a parlor or dining room, as a sign of a family's standing and a record for descendants. Peale is far better known for his miniatures, and an oil from his hand shows him applying the same careful, unshowy method to a larger surface. The record gives no biography of the sitter.","c":"Oil asks for different skills from ivory: breadth of handling, a convincing sense of fabric and of air around a figure. Peale's training was in small, exact work, and his larger canvases are often steady and a little tight rather than free. Taken that way, this is a sober, dependable portrait, better valued for its candor than for any inventiveness."},"met:11734":{"d":"A still life of vegetables and a balsam apple, the garden fruit named in the title, painted in oil on a canvas about 51 by 67 cm. Tomatoes are among the vegetables shown. The museum record does not list the other pieces or the arrangement, so only the type is described: produce set out for close study on a table or ledge, each piece distinct in the light, in the careful manner of the Peale family's Philadelphia still lifes.","i":"Peale turned to still life late in life, following the lead of his nephew Raphaelle Peale, the first important American painter in the genre. The balsam apple is an ornamental garden vine whose fruit splits when ripe, and it was also used in folk medicine. Tomatoes were becoming a common American garden crop in these decades. Together they make a picture of home-grown abundance, painted for a domestic interior.","c":"Peale's still lifes are plainer than his nephew's best work: they favor orderly arrangement, clean silhouettes and even light over daring cropping or deep shadow. The virtue is plain description and an unfussy order. What it lacks, beside the finest Raphaelle, is tension, and arrangements of this kind can feel catalogued rather than lived in. As a late work by a miniaturist, it is a modest, honest contribution to the Philadelphia school."},"nga:71370":{"d":"A speckled yellow apple and bunches of purple and green grapes sit on a sand-colored stone ledge beside a pierced white porcelain bowl heaped with more apples and grapes. Bright light from the upper left makes the fruit gleam; the apples carry brown spots and small wormholes, and green grapes spill over the ledge's front edge. The bowl has a flaring mouth rimmed in dark blue. The ground shifts from silvery gray at right to black shadow at upper left. It is an almost square oil on wood, about 38 by 46 cm.","i":"Pierced porcelain baskets like the one in the title were made in China for Western markets and were prized in American households by the early 1800s, so the piece signals refinement as well as abundance. The wormholes remind the viewer, perhaps, that fruit is perishable. Peale painted the picture at about seventy-five, in the still-life tradition that his nephew Raphaelle had made a central part of the Peale family's work.","c":"This is Peale in his most appealing late mood: a quiet, orderly arrangement lit cleanly from one side, with the white bowl as the bright accent. The honest wormholes and spots keep it from the confectionery gloss of a purely decorative piece. It lacks the daring of Raphaelle Peale's best work, and the composition is balanced to the point of stillness, but the tidy handling and clear silhouettes make it a good late Peale."},"nga:106362":{"d":"Chained to a metal grate over fiery coals, the nearly nude Saint Lawrence leans back on one elbow while a cleanshaven man in armor forces his other arm down. A bare-chested man at the front stokes the flames with a long fork, a gray-bearded man in a scarlet tunic does the same near the saint's head, and about a dozen men look on. The saint's muscled body glows orange from the embers, and his raised hand points to a man in a blue breastplate seated on a high plinth at upper left. Two winged putti with a palm and a laurel crown hover above.","i":"Lawrence, a deacon of Rome, was martyred in AD 258 under the emperor Valerian by roasting on a gridiron. According to the legend he joked to his torturers that one side was done and they might turn him over. The seated man in the breastplate, toward whom the saint gestures, is presumably the Roman official who ordered the torture. The putti bring a palm and a laurel crown, the signs of martyrdom and victory.","c":"Jean-Baptiste, nephew and pupil of Philippe de Champaigne, handles the subject with solid, academic competence: the firelit body is well modeled, and the glowing embers against the shadowed crowd and the moonlit gap beyond give a theatrical pull. The picture is polite, though. The torturers' effort and the saint's calm are drawn without much conviction, and beside the Baroque Lawrences of Italy and Spain it feels restrained."},"met:438670":{"d":"An oil sketch painted on paper, about 29 by 39 centimeters, and later laid down on canvas, shows the Colosseum and the Arch of Constantine as seen from the Palatine Hill in Rome. The record lists trees, a city and ruins, and the keywords Cities, Ruins and Rome. The museum gives no further description of the light, the figures or the foreground, but the title fixes the viewpoint among the Palatine's then still overgrown ruins.","i":"Charles Rémond won the Prix de Rome for historical landscape in 1821 and spent the following years at the French Academy in Rome, where students were encouraged to make quick oil sketches from nature as studies for finished pictures. Such sketches, painted on paper and carried in a portfolio, were an established practice of French landscape painters in Rome, from Valenciennes to Corot, and were prized as independent works only later.","c":"Oil sketches like this, made rapidly from the motif, often have an immediacy that the finished studio pictures lack, and this one's small size, soft light and easy brushwork are likely to have the lively, honest quality that French collectors later prized in Corot's Roman sketches. It is a record of looking more than of composing, and the ruins serve as a measure of time and scale. Slight in size, large in feeling for place."},"nga:130895":{"d":"Seen from a hilltop, a coastal city spreads below, its white and tan buildings packed tightly down the slope toward a band of blue water, with low mountains beyond under a sky of light blue and muted peach. In the foreground, large green cacti and succulents grow from sandy, rocky ground beside the ruins of a sand-colored structure, where a small figure in a red hat rests against the stonework. It is a small oil, about 27 by 35 cm, painted on paper and later mounted on canvas.","i":"The title places the view at Messina, on the strait between Sicily and the Italian mainland, so the water is presumably the strait. French landscape painters went to Sicily in the 1800s for its Greek and Roman remains and its strong southern light. The cacti, probably prickly pear and agave, came from the Americas in the 1500s but had long since become signs of the Mediterranean south. The small resting figure gives scale and a human note.","c":"The strength of this sketch is its economy: a handful of broad tones for hillside, city, water and sky, with the red hat as the one strong accent. As an outdoor study it is fresher than the formal historical landscapes taught in the French academy, though the buildings are summary and the picture remains a record rather than a composed work. It is typical of the small, direct oil sketches that traveling landscape painters made on paper."},"nga:134232":{"d":"A stream with foamy white water spills toward the viewer over rocks and boulders, fed by a tall thin waterfall at the left and a wider, lower one at the right. Sunlight cuts across sheer rust and caramel-brown cliffs above, leaving the foreground in shadow, and the water breaks over a broad ledge about a third of the way up into fan-shaped cascades. One dark rock pokes from the pool at the foot, and dark green shrubs fill the crevices. The picture is vertical, about 51 by 38 centimetres, painted in oil on paper laid on canvas.","i":"The waterfalls at Tivoli, near Rome, were a standard stop for artists in Italy, painted by Hubert Robert, Fragonard and many others. Bidauld was in Rome in the late 1780s, and the oil on paper suggests an open-air study of the kind that French landscapists following the example of Valenciennes made to catch light and water as they really looked. Such studies were usually kept as reference for larger studio pictures.","c":"A vigorous, fresh study: the white water is painted in choppy strokes, and the strong contrast of sunlit cliff and shadowed foreground gives it drama and depth. The vertical format emphasises the fall of the water. As a sketch its forms are summary, and it lacks the finish of a studio picture, but that is its virtue; it is valuable as evidence of the open-air practice that Corot and the Impressionists later inherited."},"met:438624":{"d":"A broad, low landscape, nearly twice as wide as it is tall (about 25 by 48 cm), shows Lake Fucino with the mountains of the Abruzzi beyond. The museum gives no further description, so the particulars of shore, light and sky are not described here. It is an oil sketch on paper, later laid down on canvas, the kind of study that landscape painters in Italy in the 1780s often made in front of the motif.","i":"Lake Fucino lay in a high basin of the Apennines east of Rome and was once among the largest lakes in Italy. The Roman emperor Claudius had a long tunnel cut to drain it, and it was finally emptied in the nineteenth century, so pictures like this record a landscape that no longer exists. Bidauld worked in Italy in the late 1780s and was among the French landscape painters making oil sketches directly from nature there.","c":"Bidauld's finished Salon landscapes follow the calm classical formulas of the day; sketches like this one are looser and more direct, and in their wide, quiet proportions look ahead to the open-air painting of the nineteenth century. A subject of lake, plain and mountain range asks for sensitivity to distance and atmosphere rather than incident. It is a modest sheet, but it shows the habit of observation behind the more formal pictures."},"nga:306":{"d":"A woman stands at the centre of a vertical canvas, shown from about the knees up, turned slightly to the viewer's right as she looks down to read a small brown book bound with gold bands. She wears a topaz-blue gown with gold trim, dusty-rose sleeves, white ruffled cuffs and a pleated white collar; her hair is braided up the side of her head. In her other hand is a white handkerchief, and a gold ring with a red stone shows on her finger. A scarlet-covered table at the right holds a second book and a folded letter, set against a green curtain and an olive wall.","i":"The letter on the table is addressed to Quintilia Fischieri at Urbino, which names the sitter and her home town, the court city of the della Rovere dukes where Barocci lived and worked. The book in her hand is probably a devotional work, and with the letter it presents her as pious, literate and well connected. The handkerchief and ring add refinement. Portraits like this proclaimed a woman's rank and virtue in the Counter-Reformation years, when modesty and piety were prized.","c":"Barocci was chiefly an altarpiece painter, and the portrait carries over the soft modelling and delicate colour of his religious work. The blue and rose of the dress, set against the red table and green curtain, are rich yet balanced, and the lowered eyes give the sitter a thoughtful privacy. It is quieter than a Venetian state portrait of the same date and a little stiff in pose, but the act of reading is an intimate touch."},"met:438688":{"d":"Saint Francis of Assisi appears with a book and with Christ in this oil on canvas of about 91 by 80 centimeters, painted around 1600 to 1604, near the end of Federico Barocci's life. The almost square format and the close view concentrate attention on the saint in devotion. The picture belongs to Barocci's late devotional work, painted with the soft light and gentle color for which he was admired, and Christ is the focus of the saint's prayer.","i":"Francis (about 1181 to 1226) founded the Franciscan order and was said to have received the wounds of Christ, the stigmata. He is usually shown in a brown habit with a rope belt, and with a book, a crucifix or a skull as aids to meditation on the Passion. Barocci worked for the Capuchins, a reformed Franciscan branch, and the simple, intimate devotion of such pictures matched their spirituality in the Counter-Reformation.","c":"Barocci's tender manner, learned from Correggio, softens the edges of forms and warms the color, and his sympathetic faces made him a favorite of devout patrons. The pastel tints and melting light are very attractive, and the sweetness can border on sentimentality. In this late work he is a link between Renaissance grace and the emotional appeal of the Baroque."},"met:436605":{"d":"A man named in the title as Alexandre Théodore Victor, comte de Lameth, is portrayed in a miniature on a thin sheet of ivory laid down on card, dated about 1789–90. The museum gives no measurements or description of his dress, so only the type can be described: a small bust-length likeness, finely worked, in the manner of Paris miniatures of the early Revolution, when sitters were often shown plainly and with little ornament.","i":"Lameth (1760–1829) was a soldier who had fought in the American War of Independence and, as a deputy for the nobility in the Estates General of 1789, sided with the reformers. In the first months of the Revolution he was a leading liberal voice in the National Assembly, which is why a portrait of about 1789–90 would have been wanted by friends and admirers. His later career included exile, and a return under Napoleon and the Restoration.","c":"Guérin was a notable Paris miniaturist of the Revolutionary years, and his likenesses of public men are valued as documents as much as for charm. The limits of the format are plain: no gesture, no setting, only the face and collar. What succeeds is the directness of the head, drawn with precision and without courtly flattery, a plainness that fits the sober taste of 1789 better than the elaborate miniatures of the old court."},"met:436606":{"d":"An unnamed woman is shown in a miniature painted on ivory around 1815. The museum gives neither measurements nor a description of her features, dress or setting, so only the type is described: a small, bust-length likeness of the kind fitted into a locket, brooch or small case, with fine strokes of watercolor and the pale ivory left to glow through the thin paint.","i":"The date falls at the end of the Napoleonic era and the start of the Restoration, when French women's dress favored high waists and simple, classical lines, and when portrait miniatures were still a favored way to record a likeness for family or a loved one. Without a sitter's name, the picture is best read as a type: a private image, meant to be held and studied closely, not a public statement.","c":"Guérin's miniatures are admired for clear, firm drawing, and a portrait of this date should be judged as a piece of fine craft: how the head is modeled, how the thin watercolor lets the ivory glow. The unnamed sitter limits what can be said, and Empire-period miniatures can seem formulaic. This is best regarded as a respectable, competent example of its kind, not a revelation."},"met:12791":{"d":"A prosperous colonial gentleman, Gabriel Manigault, is portrayed in oil on a canvas about 76 by 62 cm, painted in 1757. The museum record offers no description of his pose or dress, so only the type can be given: a bust or half-length portrait on a standard canvas of 30 by 25 inches, painted to hang in a Charleston house as the companion of a matching portrait of his wife.","i":"Gabriel Manigault was a Charleston merchant of Huguenot descent and, by many accounts, among the wealthiest men in colonial South Carolina. A portrait by the town's leading painter was a statement of standing, placed in a main room where visitors would see it. In this pair of 1757 portraits, Theus gave a rich man the sober, restrained image that the provincial gentry preferred.","c":"Theus's portraits are plain and a little flat, with a firm outline, a restrained palette and poses that follow English mezzotint models. That is no defect in a provincial painter working at speed for clients who wanted a clear likeness and evidence of rank. The Manigault pair has the dignity of competence, though little of the lively surface and character that Copley would bring to American portraiture a decade later."},"met:12793":{"d":"A woman, Ann Ashby Manigault, is shown in an oil portrait about 76 by 63 cm, painted in 1757. The costume details recorded by the museum are a lace cap with ribbon and bow, a shawl, and a pearl necklace; her pose and the background are not described. It was painted as the companion of her husband's portrait, with matching date and almost identical size, to hang together in their Charleston home.","i":"The pearls, lace and ribbons were not casual choices. In colonial Charleston, where wealth rested on rice and indigo planting and on the import trade, fine imported fabrics signaled a family's standing. The wife of one of the colony's leading merchants would have wanted both rank and modesty to show: a lace cap and shawl convey respectable matronly dress, pearls convey means.","c":"Lace and pearls give a plain painter his best opportunities: the texture of the cap and the glint of the necklace are the natural places to look for Theus's most careful effects. Elsewhere his handling tends to be flat and the pose conventional. The portrait is a sound provincial likeness, more important as a record of Charleston taste in the 1750s than as a pioneering image."},"met:15986":{"d":"A woman, named as Rebecca Brewton Motte, is portrayed in a miniature only about 3.5 by 3.2 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory, about the size of a large coin. The record lists pearls, lace and a bow among the details of her dress. At this scale each is a few touches of the brush, while the face, which must carry the likeness, is built in tiny strokes with the pale ivory showing through.","i":"Rebecca Brewton belonged to a leading Charleston family and is remembered in South Carolina as a patriot of the Revolutionary War. A miniature of about 1758 may have been made around the time of her marriage, a common occasion for such gifts, though the record does not say so. Pearls and lace tell of a wealthy household, and the tiny format suggests a keepsake meant to be held, worn or exchanged.","c":"Theus is known chiefly for plain oil portraits on canvas, so a miniature attributed to him is unusual among his surviving work, and its interest is partly historical, as an early example of ivory painting in the colonies. At 3.5 cm there is little room for the stiffness of his larger pictures, and the delicacy of lace and pearls is the most attractive part. It cannot match the finish of the professional miniaturists who arrived later."},"nga:34060":{"d":"An upper-body portrait shows a man facing forward, his body tilted slightly to the left. He wears a brown coat over a teal waistcoat, with white ruffles at the cuffs and neckline. High cheekbones, a straight nose and a serious expression mark the face, and his dark hair is arranged in large, slightly curled side parts. The background is a plain, muted green that keeps attention on the sitter. It is an oil on canvas of about 76 by 64 cm, a standard portrait size.","i":"The museum's title gives no first name, and the Motte family were prominent in Charleston. A portrait like this was the ordinary way for a successful colonist to fix his place: a good coat, a clear face and a quiet backdrop. The serious expression is a convention of the time rather than a personal trait, since sitters were expected to look steady and dependable.","c":"A direct, unfussy likeness. Theus puts the weight on the face and the two or three colors of the costume, and the plain ground sets them off cleanly. He was not a subtle painter of flesh, and the modeling can look flat beside Copley's, but the sitter's seriousness comes across as honest. As a record of a Charleston gentleman in about 1760, it is typical of the artist, neither his most ambitious nor his weakest."},"nga:50305":{"d":"A man shown to the waist, turned slightly to the right with his gaze directed to the left, wears a blue coat with large buttons, a white high-collared shirt and a white cravat. His white hair, formally curled at the sides, may be a wig. One hand is tucked into the coat, and the background is a muted greenish ground. It is an oil on canvas of about 75 by 62 cm, painted around 1765.","i":"The question mark in the title shows that the sitter's identification as James Cuthbert is uncertain. The hand tucked into the coat was a gesture of polite ease borrowed from European portraiture, where it signaled self-command and gentlemanly composure. Its appearance in a colonial portrait shows how sitters and painters looked to London fashion for their cues. It was probably painted as one of a pair of husband and wife.","c":"The portrait relies on a stock pose and a limited palette, a blue coat against a muted green ground, which gives a cool, harmonious effect but also a certain sameness with Theus's other male portraits. The hand-in-coat gesture lifts the formality a little. As a document of mid-century colonial portraiture it is plain and respectable, valued more for its historical interest than for any boldness of design."},"nga:50306":{"d":"A woman seated to the waist raises her left hand gently to her face while her right hand rests on a book in her lap. She has a fair complexion, rosy cheeks, light eyes and a thoughtful expression. A white cap tied under the chin with a light blue ribbon covers most of her light hair. Her blue dress has a white collar and pleated folds, and a pearl necklace circles her neck. Behind her the ground shifts from warm brown to an outdoor view of clouds and foliage.","i":"The hand raised to the face and the book in the lap are stock signs of a thoughtful, literate woman: reading and reflection were presented as virtues of a gentleman's wife, and a pensive pose of this kind belongs to English portrait fashion. The glimpse of sky and foliage borrows another English convention, setting the sitter before a landscape. The question marks in the title show that the identification as Mary Cuthbert, wife of James Cuthbert, is uncertain.","c":"The pose is the most interesting thing here: a gently pensive gesture, less common in Theus than the plain seated-and-facing format, and the opening onto sky gives air to an otherwise reticent composition. The blues of dress and ribbon sit harmoniously against the warm brown ground. For a provincial portrait it shows more ambition than usual, even if the mood is more polite than deeply felt."},"met:12792":{"d":"A man named John Dart is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted around 1772–74. The museum gives no description of his pose, dress or setting, so only the type is given: a bust or half-length portrait of the standard size used by Charleston's leading portraitist for planters and merchants, painted to hang beside a matching portrait of his wife.","i":"By 1772–74 Theus was in his late fifties and had been painting Charleston's elite for more than three decades; he died in 1774, so the Dart portraits come at the very end of his career. The pair would have recorded a prosperous couple in the last years before the Revolution, when Charleston's trade and planting wealth was at its height and a painted family likeness was part of a respectable household's furnishing.","c":"Late Theus changes little: the same plain format, the same restraint, a serviceable likeness. A harsh view calls it formula; a fairer one sees a consistent house style, honest in its means, that gave Charleston's patrons what they asked for. The pair has historical weight as work from one of the last years of a painter who held the town's portrait trade for three decades, but it is not where one would look for his best passages."},"met:12794":{"d":"A woman, Henrietta Dart, is shown in an oil portrait of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted around 1772–74. The costume details recorded by the museum are a pearl necklace, lace, ermine and a striped fabric; her pose and the setting are not described here. It was made as the companion of the portrait of her husband, John Dart, at the same size and date.","i":"Ermine, lace and pearls were the costume of luxury, and a woman in Charleston society who chose to wear them in a portrait was making a claim of rank and wealth. Ermine in particular had a European history as the fur of princes and judges, so its appearance here is more a borrowed sign of status than everyday dress. Painters of the day often supplied such fashionable costume from English prints.","c":"The costume gives a plain painter his best opportunities: soft fur, gleaming pearls and lace let a provincial portraitist show skill without having to invent anything. Beyond them the treatment is likely to be conventional, a composed expression and a quiet ground. It is a decent partner to its male counterpart and a useful record of luxury dress in a colonial city, rather than a notable innovation."},"met:747607":{"d":"A woman is shown half-length, playing a guitar, in an oil on canvas of about 107 by 76 cm. The museum's record mentions a dress and an earring but gives no further description of her pose, expression or the background, so only the type is described: a single musician placed close to the viewer, in the kind of half-length picture that Caravaggio's followers made popular in Rome, where Vouet was working around 1618.","i":"Half-length musicians, lute players, singers and guitarists were a favorite subject of Caravaggio's Roman followers, made for collectors rather than churches, and Vouet, who reached Rome about 1614, took up the manner with enthusiasm. Whether the woman is a particular sitter or a model posed as a musician is uncertain. In the 1620s he moved away from such dark, direct pictures toward a lighter, more decorative style.","c":"Works of this phase show Vouet learning Caravaggio's lessons quickly: solid forms, strong light, models taken from life. Its interest lies in that apprenticeship, before the smooth, decorative manner that made him famous in Paris took over. It is best seen as a document of a stage in his development, and a reminder that the painter who later supplied elegant Parisian decorations began as a hard-edged realist."},"nga:46151":{"d":"A balding, white-bearded old man sits at a table, shown from the waist up and turned to look over his shoulder at a winged angel behind him. He is nearly bare, a scarlet cloth across his lap and arm; one hand holds a pen over a half-unrolled scroll, the other is raised, palm up. The smiling angel in pale blue and gold points beyond the frame and holds a golden horn. Books, eyeglasses, an inkpot and an hourglass lie on the desk, and a candle burns in a niche. Strong light from the upper left picks both figures out of deep shadow.","i":"The scene shows Saint Jerome, who translated the Bible into Latin, at work. Tradition holds that he was haunted by the sound of the trumpet of the Last Judgment; he wrote that it seemed always to ring in his ears, and painters gave him an angel with a horn. The pen, scroll and eyeglasses mark the scholar, the hourglass the passing of time. The red cloth recalls the cardinal's robes that later tradition anachronistically gave him.","c":"A strong Caravaggesque picture from Vouet's last Roman years: the single light, the dark, pared-down ground and the lined, sculptural head tie it to Caravaggio and his followers. The angel's smiling face, half lost in shadow, adds a tenderness that points toward Vouet's later softness. Its strength is the confrontation of two figures; its weakness, a slight theatricality in the gestures. It shows the dark manner at its most accomplished, just before his return to France."},"nga:206070":{"d":"A young woman, shown from the knees up, nuzzles a plump baby on her lap, her head in profile, her eyes lowered and her lips almost touching his upturned face. A sea-blue mantle lies across her back and lap over a coral-red robe. The nude child, blond and curly, faces away from us with one hand on her neck and the other cradling her chin, a mustard-yellow cloth around his hips. She leans on a stone ledge bearing the signature and date, Simon Vovet Pinxit 1633. Light falls from the upper left against a brown ground. The oil on canvas measures about 110 by 89 cm.","i":"This is not a ceremonial icon but an intimate embrace, in which the Virgin's tenderness invites the viewer to feel divine love through human affection. Vouet, who had spent years in Rome and returned to Paris in 1627 as painter to Louis XIII, drew here on Italian ideals of beauty. The medium size and the intimacy suggest a picture for a private room or chapel rather than a church altar, though the record does not say.","c":"The picture succeeds in tenderness: the profile, the lowered eyes and the near-touching lips are treated with a sweetness and restraint that Parisian patrons loved, and the sea blue, coral red and mustard yellow are clear and bright, far from the dark tones of his Roman years. A critic might call it sweet to the edge of sentimental, and it lacks the psychological depth of Poussin, but in its quiet harmony it is an attractive example of his Paris manner."},"nga:46160":{"d":"Two women in blue, pink and yellow robes sit on the ground at the foot of a stone building, with three winged putti nearby. The woman at left, in profile, glances back toward us; six silver stars line the sky-blue diadem in her hair, and she leans on a silver sphere. The other, in yellow beneath a rose-pink toga, rests her hands on a book whose page edges show the partial word 'odiss'. The putti hold up three crowns of leaves. Distant hills open at right. The horizontal oil on wood measures about 80 by 125 cm.","i":"The stars and the sphere identify Urania, Muse of astronomy; the book marked 'odiss', presumably the Odyssey, points to Calliope, Muse of epic poetry. The putti hold crowns of leaves, presumably laurel, the traditional reward of poets and scholars. The Muses were daughters of Zeus and Memory and presided over the arts and sciences, so a picture of them suited a library, study or gallery, where it flattered the owner's learning.","c":"Vouet's Parisian decoration at its most agreeable: soft, bright colors, a clear horizontal arrangement and a relaxed classical grace in the two seated figures. It is easy on the eye rather than deeply expressive, and the poses and the landscape follow formulas he used again and again. What it offers is poise and a fresh palette; what it lacks is the dramatic intensity of his Roman years. It helps explain why he dominated French painting in the 1630s."},"cma:153927":{"d":"A single large figure, Mary Magdalen, fills the panel and is set close to the viewer. The colors are vivid pastels, brighter than a Roman follower of Caravaggio would use, and a skull lies beside her. The oil on panel measures about 131 by 104 cm. The museum describes the picture only in these terms, so her pose, costume and the setting are left undescribed here. It was painted soon after Vouet returned to France in 1627 to serve Louis XIII.","i":"Mary Magdalen, the repentant sinner of the Gospels, was a favorite image of penitence in the Catholic Europe of the 1600s, shown as a hermit in the wilderness. The skull is a memento mori, reminding the viewer that life is brief and worldly goods worthless. Such pictures expressed devotion but also allowed painters to show a beautiful woman in a pious frame, which helps explain their great popularity.","c":"Vouet's solution for a French market: the single monumental figure close to the picture plane that he learned from Caravaggio's circle, but in pastel colors that suited local taste. It is a compromise that works better as polish than as drama; the monumentality gives presence, the color gives charm, and the penitence is more decorative than anguished. Its chief interest is how clearly it shows him adapting his Italian experience to Paris."},"met:436649":{"d":"A young man, probably Robert Devereux, second Earl of Essex, is shown in a miniature on vellum laid on card, dated 1588. The museum gives no measurements or description of his dress, so only the type is given: a tiny bust-length image, a few centimeters high, painted in fine brushwork in watercolor bound with gum, in the delicate Elizabethan manner called limning.","i":"Robert Devereux was the stepson of the Earl of Leicester and became Elizabeth I's favorite in the late 1580s; in 1588, the year of the Armada, he was about twenty-two and rising fast at court. Miniatures of courtiers were exchanged as tokens of loyalty or affection and worn at the neck or kept in a box. Essex was executed in 1601 after a failed rising against the Queen's government.","c":"Hilliard's miniatures are valued for their jewel-like precision and for the poise of the face, built with the lightest touches. His work of about 1585 to 1595 is generally counted among his finest, and the elegant young courtier was his specialty. The identification with Essex is not certain, so the likeness is best admired for its type: refined, reticent and quietly self-confident, the image of the Elizabethan gentleman."},"met:436651":{"d":"A woman is shown in a miniature on vellum laid on card, painted about 1590. The museum's tags list a lace ruff, a necklace and an earring but give no measurements and no further description of her face, dress or ground, so only these details and the type are given: a bust-length likeness a few centimeters high, painted in fine, clear color and delicate highlights in the manner of the Elizabethan court.","i":"In Elizabethan England a ruff was costly to make and to maintain, since starched linen and lace needed constant laundering, so a large ruff in a portrait proclaimed means and leisure. Lace, necklace and earring add to the claim. The sitter is unidentified; she may belong to the court or to a prosperous London family. Such small likenesses were exchanged as tokens and worn on a chain or kept in a box.","c":"Hilliard's gift was to make the smallest things look exact: the lacework of a ruff, the sparkle of a bead, the line of an eyelid, without cast shadow, which he thought false to faces seen in clear light. That method gives his portraits a delicate flat clarity that can look stiff to modern eyes but is the secret of their charm. With no sitter named, this is a fine example of his type more than a historical document."},"met:436650":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in a miniature painted on vellum and dated 1597. The museum gives neither measurements nor a description of her dress or setting, so only the type is given: a bust-length likeness a few centimeters high, built up in watercolor bound with gum, with fine brushwork, in the manner of the Elizabethan limner. Hilliard's miniatures commonly carry the date and the sitter's age in gold letters around the edge.","i":"By 1597, in the late years of Elizabeth's reign, portrait miniatures had become a fixture of court and gentry life: they were exchanged between friends and lovers, given as favors and worn in lockets or cases. Hilliard, who served Elizabeth as limner and goldsmith, supplied them to a wide circle. The sitter is unnamed, so the picture is best read as a type: a woman of the Elizabethan upper classes, composed and decorous.","c":"A late Hilliard shows the strengths and limits of his method: unrivaled finesse, little interest in modeling, a gaze that stays remote. By the end of the 1590s younger rivals such as Isaac Oliver were bringing a more naturalistic handling of shadow and character, and beside them Hilliard can look delicately old-fashioned. But the best of his work remains the standard of Elizabethan courtliness in paint."},"nga:366":{"d":"An angel with blond curls and pale skin leans forward in profile, arms crossed on the chest, in a vertical tempera panel on poplar of about 77 by 58 cm. A gleaming gold halo surrounds the head, and gold feathered wings rise from the shoulders. The long gown carries a brick-red floral pattern on black. Behind the figure, mustard-yellow horizontal bands, perhaps boards, fill the left half, swirling browns fill the right, and a sliver of green and burgundy tiled floor runs along the bottom.","i":"The crossed arms and forward lean show reverent greeting: this is Gabriel, who tells Mary that she will bear the Son of God. The panel was made with a companion, The Virgin Annunciate, in which Mary turns toward him; together they would form an Annunciation divided between two panels. The Annunciation was among the most popular subjects of Florentine religious art, and the angel's patterned gown and gold wings suggest the splendor of a heavenly messenger.","c":"Masolino's gift is grace. The angel's bent, courtly posture, the swirl of curls and the rich pattern of the gown belong to the International Gothic taste, while the believable floor and the careful placing of the figure show that he had absorbed Florence's new interest in space. Beside Masaccio, with whom he worked in the Brancacci Chapel, he is the gentler, more decorative partner, and this panel is characteristic: elegant, serene and not especially searching."},"nga:367":{"d":"A woman sits with her hands crossed over her chest, turned toward the left, in a vertical panel of about 76 by 57 cm. She has pale skin, rosy cheeks, a small mouth, light eyes and shoulder-length blond hair beneath a golden halo. She wears a blue robe edged in gold over a red tunic, and an open book rests on her lap. The ground is mostly dark red with lighter vertical lines, a dark curtain with gold embellishments hangs at the right, and she sits on a light brown bench.","i":"This is the Virgin Annunciate, Mary at the moment she receives the angel's message. The open book recalls the tradition that she was reading Scripture when Gabriel appeared, and her crossed arms express humble acceptance of her role. Blue over red is the usual dress of Mary in Italian painting. The panel was made as a companion to the Gabriel, who would have faced her from the left, with the two panels together forming one Annunciation.","c":"A quieter, more inward picture than its companion angel; the stillness comes from the closed, compact pose, with the crossed arms drawing the figure into itself. The blue and red against deep red are rich, though a little heavy. The face is sweet rather than searching. As half of a pair it succeeds by contrast with the more animated angel, and it shows Masolino's characteristic gentle idealism."},"nga:18":{"d":"In a tall panel framed by an arch, the angel Gabriel kneels at the left in a robe of dark red patterned with gold leaf-shapes, his golden wings behind him and his hands crossed on his chest. At the right the Virgin, in a blue cloak over a red dress, sits on a throne-like bench with a small book open in her lap, her hand at her breast and her head bowed toward him. A slender pale column divides them. Behind it an open doorway and a starry blue alcove lead deeper into the house. Overhead, painted arches filled with patterned diamond tiles, bands of orange moulding and a thin golden ray falling toward Mary give the room a jewelled, geometric clarity.","i":"The Annunciation, when the angel Gabriel tells Mary she will bear the Son of God, was a favourite subject in Florence, where the year began on 25 March, the feast of the Annunciation. The book in Mary's lap carries a Latin text from Isaiah (7:14), foretelling that a virgin will conceive and bear a son, the prophecy the scene fulfils. The National Gallery dates the panel to about 1423–24, when Masolino was in Florence. Early writers gave it to Masaccio; Bernard Berenson's 1902 article helped establish it as Masolino's, and a few scholars have proposed Uccello. It is thought to come from a Florentine church, possibly San Niccolò Oltr'Arno.","c":"Masolino is usually judged against Masaccio and found lighter, and this panel shows both why and why the comparison is partly unfair. The figures are courtly and graceful, the drapery flows in soft folds, and the colour is rich; the patterned angel is a delight. But the space is ambitious without being fully convincing: the coffered arches and the alcove recede, yet the figures sit flat against them and lack Masaccio's weight. It is a charming, slightly hesitant document of the moment when the courtly International Gothic style met the new science of perspective."},"nga:52254":{"d":"A crowd gathers on a green, hilly park for a day at the races in this wide canvas of about 92 by 153 centimeters. Riders on horseback and people standing by their horses fill the middle ground, some people sit in horse-drawn carriages, and others gather under tall trees. Men wear top hats and coats, women colorful dresses. A wide course is marked off by fences at the right, a white tent sits behind a hill at the left, and in the distance rises a large white building with columns and blue roofs.","i":"Heaton Park, near Manchester, was the seat of the Earl of Wilton, a keen sportsman who held race meetings there in the late 1820s and 1830s. The distant columned building is probably Heaton Hall, the house itself. Racing was both sport and social theater in Regency and early Victorian England, bringing aristocrats, townspeople and betting men together, and pictures like this recorded such occasions for the sponsors.","c":"John Ferneley, the sporting painter of Melton Mowbray, was better known for hunting scenes and horse portraits, and here he handles the crowd like a skilled stage manager: many small groups, evenly lit, set into a lush landscape. The figures are small and conventional, the horses accurate rather than lyrical, and the picture is more valuable as social record than as art. The panorama still has an infectious festive air."},"met:11834":{"d":"A tree, a woman, a man, a dog and a wild animal are the elements the museum records in this oil of about 66 by 86 cm, dated 1832. The title points to the scene in James Fenimore Cooper's novel The Pioneers in which the old hunter nicknamed Leatherstocking saves a young woman from a panther. The museum gives no further description, so the poses, light and landscape are not described here.","i":"The Pioneers (1823), the first of Cooper's Leatherstocking tales, was a popular novel set in upstate New York in the 1790s, with the hunter Natty Bumppo at odds with the changes brought by settlement. The panther scene was one of its most famous episodes and appealed to painters and printmakers as an image of frontier danger and rescue. Quidor, a New Yorker, was among the first to paint scenes from Cooper and Washington Irving, mining the new American literature for subjects.","c":"Quidor's nervous energy and humor suit the subject: a dramatic, slightly absurd scrap of frontier melodrama is a natural for his brush. His figures tend to be small, distorted and lit for effect, closer to the stage than to the academy. The drawing is rough, which put off the public of his day, but the invention and the odd, dark poetry of the best of his 1830s pictures make him more memorable than many tidier painters."},"nga:1104":{"d":"Before a crowd of at least two dozen, an old man with long white hair and beard, in tattered clothes, toes poking from worn shoes, holds a rifle in one hand and points with a crooked finger at a man beside him, who glowers back in tricorn hat, navy coat and rose-red vest. To the right a denser, more loosely painted crowd gathers before an inn whose hanging sign reads General Washington, while a boy climbs a flagpole flying a thirteen-star flag. The oil on canvas measures about 101 by 127 cm.","i":"In Washington Irving's story of 1819, Rip sleeps in the Catskills for twenty years and wakes to a village transformed: the king's portrait on the inn sign has become General Washington's, and the talk is of elections and liberty. Quidor shows Rip, bewildered, confronted by village politicians in the midst of an election; the signs and papers, with their mentions of Bunker's Hill and seventy-six, mark the new Republic that he slept through. It is a comic tale about political change.","c":"One of Quidor's most ambitious crowd scenes, and it works as satire: the foreground figures are pushed toward caricature while the crowd behind them is a swarm of broad strokes. The white, rose-red and navy against a lilac-clouded distance keep the eye on Rip's wild gesture. It remains a rough, eccentric picture beside the polished genre scenes of Mount or Bingham, but it has an energy and wit that theirs seldom reach."},"cma:143221":{"d":"The picture shows the moment in Washington Irving's tale when the miser Tom Walker meets the Devil, painted in oil on canvas about 69 by 87 cm and dated 1856. The museum describes the scene as Tom Walker, 'not a man to be troubled with any fears,' encountering the Devil on an evening outing. It gives no description of the composition, so the figures and setting are not detailed here.","i":"In Irving's tale, set in the Boston area in the 1720s, the greedy Tom Walker takes a shortcut through a swamp, meets the Devil and bargains for buried pirate treasure, then becomes a ruthless moneylender whom the Devil eventually claims. The story is a satire on greed and on Puritan New England. Quidor was drawn to its supernatural comedy, and the Devil's presence allowed the grotesque that he preferred.","c":"Quidor was a painter of fantasy and caricature whose pictures can look raw beside the polite American art of the 1850s. A shadowy, theatrical setting suits a devil's tale, and the museum rightly sets this group of Irving subjects apart from realistic American genre painting. His drawing is crude and his surfaces dark, but as a literary grotesque it is a successful, characteristic example from the later part of his career."},"met:11835":{"d":"A gate, a tree, a boy, a horse and rider, and a trumpet are the elements recorded in this oil of about 69 by 87 cm, dated 1863. The title refers to a scene in Washington Irving's comic History of New York, in which the Dutch governor Peter Stuyvesant guards the wall that gave Wall Street its name. The museum gives no further description, so only the type is given: a small literary scene in Quidor's usual theatrical manner.","i":"In Irving's History of New York (1809), written under the name of the fictitious Dutch historian Diedrich Knickerbocker, Peter Stuyvesant is the peppery, wooden-legged last governor of New Amsterdam, a figure of burlesque. The Dutch built a wall across the lower tip of Manhattan in the 1650s, and Wall Street still follows its line. Quidor's picture belongs to the same affectionate mockery of Dutch New York.","c":"A late Quidor, and a typical one: small figures, broad humor and a taste for the odd outline over precise description. The weakness is plain, awkward drawing and a hurried finish; the strength, a spirited comic invention that the polished history painters of the era seldom allowed themselves. It does not look like the leading American art of 1863, which is part of its charm and part of why Quidor was rediscovered only in the twentieth century."},"met:12600":{"d":"A man named Francis Brinley is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 127 by 100 cm, dated 1729. A chair is the only furnishing the museum records, and his pose, dress and expression are not described here. The picture is the companion of the portrait of his wife and infant son, matching it in size and date, and the pair were painted in the year Smibert arrived in New England.","i":"The portrait is the male half of a pair that introduced Boston to the full English formula: a gentleman seated in a handsome chair, presented to the world as the head of his household. Smibert's arrival in 1729 brought a trained eye and a ready repertory of poses to a colony that had known only simple local portraits. Such pictures hung in the main rooms of a house and were passed down with it.","c":"Smibert's male portraits of 1729 are sound, orderly and rather cool. His strength lay in building a convincing head and torso with a knowledge of anatomy and drapery that the colonies had not seen; his weakness, in a certain stiffness of pose and lack of animation. A man in a chair was the standard formula, and Smibert's version is correct rather than exciting. Its importance is historical: it brought the London manner to Boston."},"met:12602":{"d":"A seated woman, Deborah Lyde Brinley, holds her infant son Francis in this large oil, about 127 by 100 cm, dated 1729. The museum's record notes orange blossom and fruit among the details, as well as a chair and a curtain; it does not describe the pose, costume or colors, so only these are given. It is a portrait of a mother and child painted in the year the artist arrived in New England.","i":"Smibert arrived in 1729 as a trained British portraitist, and such pictures showed colonial sitters what a fashionable European mother-and-child portrait looked like. Orange blossom is a traditional emblem of marriage and fertility, and here it speaks of the family's hopes; the fruit, curtain and chair are stock settings of English portraiture that lend a touch of grandeur to a well-off family's parlor. It would have hung with its companion portrait of the husband.","c":"Smibert's training in Italy and London shows in the arrangement and the confident handling of drapery, and a mother-and-child group was a novelty in the colonies, where most portraits showed single sitters. It is not a profound picture, and the faces are more polite than individual, but its ease with composition and richness of surface set a standard colonial painters had not seen. Copley and other Boston painters of the next generation are thought to have learned from his studio."},"met:12977":{"d":"A man is portrayed with a book, in a large oil of about 125 by 100 cm, painted between about 1720 and 1730. The museum record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a formal portrait in the manner of London portraiture of the early Georgian years, where a book signaled education and a taste for reading. The sitter is unnamed.","i":"A book was a conventional sign of learning in portraits of clergymen, lawyers, scholars and gentlemen. The picture has been catalogued under the school of Sir Godfrey Kneller, the leading London portraitist until his death in 1723, which shows how closely it follows that model. Smibert painted such portraits in London in the 1720s before sailing for America, so the picture may belong to his English years rather than his Boston ones.","c":"The picture shows how closely early colonial portraiture, even from the best-trained hand, followed the London formula of the day: a large format, a single attribute in the book, poses taken from the repertory of Kneller's studio. It is competent and dignified, but formulaic in a way that the sitter's own character hardly disturbs. As a record of the style Smibert carried across the Atlantic it is worth more than as an individual likeness."},"met:12601":{"d":"A woman named Hannah Pemberton is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, dated 1734. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a bust or half-length portrait of the standard size for a Boston sitter of these years, painted in the British manner that Smibert had brought to the colonies five years earlier.","i":"A portrait of a Boston woman in 1734 recorded respectable standing and, often, a marriage; many such pictures were commissioned as pairs with a husband's. Smibert's women of these years typically follow British convention, with a low bodice, a long neck and a curl of hair on the shoulder, meant to show them as refined and modest. The museum record gives no biography for Hannah Pemberton, so the occasion for the portrait is not known.","c":"By 1734 Smibert was the leading portraitist of Boston, and his women's portraits show both his strengths and his limits. He builds a face with a steady, believable light and handles satin and lace with assurance; the poses are repeated from sitter to sitter, and the faces share a family resemblance. A Pemberton portrait is a fair example of his routine work: polite, accomplished and a little impersonal."},"cma:173240":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in oil on canvas, about 76 by 62 cm, painted around 1734. The museum notes a low bodice, a long neck and a sinuous curl of hair, following British conventions for portraits of women, and gives no further description. The sitter was long thought to be Mrs. Thomas Bulfinch, born Judith Colman, but her identity is still under research, which is why the title carries a question mark.","i":"Smibert, born in Edinburgh and schooled in London and Italy, reached the colonies in 1729, and his arrival is a turning point in American art: he could give a convincing sense of rounded form, and his commercial success encouraged others to think of painting as a career. His recently discovered records do not mention Mrs. Bulfinch, which is why the traditional identification is now questioned. Portraits like this show the colonial elite adopting the poses and dress of London fashion.","c":"The attraction of the picture is its quiet competence: the sinuous curl of hair and the low, elegant neckline are handled with a confidence few colonial painters could match, and the rounded modeling of the face gives the sitter more substance than flatter local portraits do. The identity is unsettled and the pose conventional, so it is better prized as an example of Smibert's imported London polish than as a deep characterization."},"nga:38935":{"d":"A chest-up portrait shows an older man in a slightly angled pose, his body turned to the left and his head facing the viewer. He has light skin, a large nose, thick gray eyebrows and thin lips turned slightly upward; his gray hair is swept back. He wears a dark jacket with a high collar over a white shirt and cravat, against a plain dark brown ground. The oil on canvas measures about 65 by 52 cm, and it was painted around 1806 to 1807.","i":"The sitter is Thomas Paine (1737–1809), the English-born author of Common Sense, which in 1776 helped push the colonies toward independence, and of The Rights of Man. He returned to the United States in 1802 and spent his last years in and near New York, by then widely reviled for The Age of Reason, his deist attack on organized religion. Jarvis painted him when his fame had faded; the plain, serious likeness gives him dignity rather than heroics.","c":"A plain portrait for a plain-spoken sitter: the dark coat and brown ground leave the head to do the work, and the large nose, heavy gray brows and level mouth give Paine a combative honesty that fits his reputation. Jarvis does not flatter him. It is among the better likenesses of a great pamphleteer and, by Jarvis's uneven standards, restrained and well focused."},"met:11248":{"d":"An unnamed man is shown in a miniature of about 7.6 by 6 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1809. The museum gives no description of him, so his dress and setting are not detailed here. It is a small bust-length likeness of the kind Jarvis made in the early part of his New York career, when miniatures were a regular part of his business alongside his oil portraits.","i":"'Portrait of a Gentleman' is how museums record a man whose name has been lost. Miniatures were easily separated from the family papers that identified them, which is why so many survive anonymously. Men gave them to wives and fiancées or kept them as private records, and in New York in 1809 a busy portraitist like Jarvis, known for working fast, could supply them to a wide range of clients.","c":"Jarvis was a quicker, looser painter than the Peales, and his miniatures tend to have more animation and less polish. The best of them catch a lively expression in a few touches, though the finish can be uneven and the drawing hurried. This one is typical of his small-scale work: an efficient likeness of an unknown sitter, valued more as a record of faces in the early 1800s than as a refined piece of craft."},"met:11244":{"d":"A man known only as Mr. Cook is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, in a miniature about 7.3 by 5.7 cm, dated 1810. The record gives no description of his dress or setting, so none is given here. The format is the familiar one of the period: a small bust-length likeness, probably oval, meant for a case or locket, with the ivory's translucency used for the glow of the skin.","i":"A title like Mr. Cook marks a sitter whose first name has not been recovered, which is common for miniatures. In 1810 Jarvis was among the busiest portraitists in New York, and the city's merchants, officers and professional men formed his clientele. A miniature like this was a private object, given to family or kept in a pocket, the opposite of the large public portraits of naval heroes that he would paint a few years later.","c":"Jarvis's miniatures are brisk rather than exquisite, and a small likeness of an ordinary sitter like this is a reminder that he earned his living by volume. Where it works, the head is energetic and the expression alive; where it falters, the surface is thin and the modeling summary. Such pieces are charming mainly as period documents, and they suggest a lively hand more at home in oil than on ivory."},"met:11249":{"d":"A woman, Anne Brown Dickey, is portrayed in oil on a wooden panel of about 88 by 70 cm, painted between 1807 and 1810. The museum record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait on a smooth, rigid panel, made as the companion of the portrait of her husband, Robert Dickey, at a matching size.","i":"Pendant portraits of husband and wife were the standard commission of the period, hung together in a parlor as a declaration of a household's standing and union. Jarvis, who came from England as a child and trained as an engraver's apprentice before taking up portraits, supplied them to New York's merchants and professional families. A wooden panel, a firm alternative to canvas, was used by a number of American portraitists of the time.","c":"Jarvis's early New York portraits are straightforward and confident: a clear likeness, a simple pose and a surface smooth enough to suit a wood panel. They lack the polish of Gilbert Stuart, the leading American portraitist of the day, and some passages, such as hands and drapery, can be perfunctory, but the faces usually have a lively presence. As one of a couple's pendants it is a sound, characteristic example of his early manner."},"met:11250":{"d":"A man named Robert Dickey is portrayed in oil on a wooden panel of about 88 by 70 cm, painted between 1807 and 1810. The museum record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of the standard size Jarvis used for sitters in these years, painted as the companion of the portrait of his wife, Anne Brown Dickey.","i":"A husband's portrait of this kind presented the head of a household to its visitors, and it was meant to hang opposite or beside his wife's in the main room of the house. The museum can date the pair only to the years 1807 to 1810. Beyond his name, the record gives no biography of the sitter, so his occupation and rank are not known.","c":"In pairs like this, the male portrait tends to be the plainer of the two, and Jarvis's strength is in the head: a direct gaze and a quickly built, lively face rather than careful finish. His hands and clothing are usually summary. The Dickey portraits are best taken as dependable examples of his New York practice in its first busy years, valuable as documents of the city's middle and upper ranks."},"met:16710":{"d":"Gulian Verplanck, a young New Yorker, is portrayed in oil on a wooden panel about 72 cm high and 84 cm wide, painted around 1811. Unusually for a portrait, the panel is wider than it is tall. The museum gives no description of his pose, dress or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length likeness of a sitter in his mid-twenties. The wide format leaves room for more than a bust, but the record does not say what else is shown.","i":"Gulian Crommelin Verplanck (1786–1870) belonged to an old Dutch-descended New York family and became a lawyer, congressman and man of letters; he later edited Shakespeare and moved in the literary circle around Washington Irving. A portrait of about 1811 shows him as a promising young man, at a time when Jarvis was one of New York's busiest portraitists.","c":"The wide format is unusual and gives the sitter room that most of Jarvis's upright portraits do not. At his best Jarvis caught a sitter's alertness in a few quick touches, and a young man of lively intelligence is a good subject for that gift. The finish cannot match that of the leading portraitists of the day, but the picture is a vivid document of early New York society."},"met:11247":{"d":"A woman named Mrs. William Thomas is portrayed in oil on canvas, about 86.5 by 69 cm, painted around 1813. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of a married woman in the standard format of the day, painted when Jarvis was an established New York portraitist.","i":"The title records her by her husband's name, the convention for married women of the period, and it conceals her own first name, a reminder of how women were identified in the early Republic. Around 1813, in the middle of the War of 1812, portrait commissions continued, and Jarvis was busy with them, painting naval heroes of the war in the same years. Her portrait would have hung in the household's main room.","c":"At this date Jarvis had the confidence of an established portraitist, and his women's portraits are usually fluid and unfussy, with the weight placed on the face. The type suits him: direct and sociable rather than formal. His brushwork gives vitality and, at times, a certain slackness. With little else in the record to go on, it can be regarded as a typical example of his mature oil portraits, neither his best nor his weakest."},"met:17558":{"d":"A woman, whose full name the museum records as Ellen Vanderpoel McCoy Ruggles, is portrayed in oil on a wooden panel about 89 by 66.5 cm, painted around 1813. The record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: an upright half-length portrait on a smooth panel, of the standard size for a Jarvis sitter of these years.","i":"Vanderpoel is a Dutch name, long established among the Hudson Valley families of New York, and Dutch-descended families were prominent clients for the city's portraitists in the early 1800s. A portrait of this size was meant to hang in a main room and pass down in the family, which is how many such pictures reached museums with their sitters' names intact. The record gives no further biography.","c":"Jarvis painted several portraits on wood in these years, and the smooth, firm surface lends itself to a clean, even finish. The merit of the picture is likely to lie in the directness of the head rather than in elaborate costume or setting. Its limits are those of a busy practice: a reliable formula repeated for many sitters. It is characteristic of Jarvis in his prime, but not a picture that surprises."},"nga:30229":{"d":"A clean-shaven man with a stern expression is shown from the waist up, wearing a navy-blue uniform with gold buttons and epaulettes, his right arm resting inside his coat. Behind him a cloudy sky stretches over water, and a ship is visible near the distant horizon. The sea and the ship identify his service as clearly as the uniform does. The oil on canvas measures about 91 by 71 cm.","i":"John Rodgers (1772–1838) was one of the senior officers of the United States Navy in the War of 1812; he commanded the frigate President in the first naval action of the war, in June 1812, and helped defend Baltimore. 'Commodore' was the courtesy title for a captain commanding a squadron. The hand-in-coat pose was a gesture of composure borrowed from European portraits, and ship and sea were the usual way of marking a naval hero.","c":"Jarvis gives the commodore a stern, upright presence and keeps the picture simple: uniform, face and a strip of sea with a ship. The hand in the coat, borrowed from European portraits of generals, lends authority, and the dark blue uniform and the sea behind it are well matched. It lacks subtlety, and the background is more emblem than observation, but as a portrait of a national hero in a moment of national pride it does its job."},"cma:98440":{"d":"A portrait of the actor Thomas Abthorpe Cooper, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 86 by 66.5 cm, around 1810. The museum gives no description of the picture, so his pose, costume and setting are not detailed here. Portraits of leading actors were a recognized genre, and a sitter of Cooper's fame might be shown in his own dress or in a stage role; the record does not say which.","i":"Thomas Abthorpe Cooper (1776–1849) was born in England, came to the United States in 1796 and became the leading tragic actor of the early American stage, known for Shakespearean roles. Actors were public figures whose likenesses were in demand, and a portrait by a fashionable painter doubled as publicity. Jarvis, a New York portraitist who painted many notable figures of the city, was a natural choice.","c":"An actor's portrait invites animation, and catching a sitter's alertness was Jarvis's best quality. The record does not say how theatrical the picture is. His weakness, a hurried finish and summary hands, may limit it, but as an image of one of the first stars of the American stage it has an interest beyond its quality, and it shows Jarvis at home in the company of New York's public figures."},"met:11240":{"d":"A man named Alexander Anderson is portrayed in oil on canvas, about 86 by 69 cm, dated 1815. The museum record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait in the standard format Jarvis used for his New York sitters, with the head as the center of interest and the rest kept simple.","i":"The name is shared by the New York wood engraver Alexander Anderson (1775–1870), a trained physician who became the leading American wood engraver, but the museum record here does not say whether he is the sitter. Jarvis, who began as an engraver's apprentice himself, moved among the city's printmakers and artists, so such a portrait would not be surprising.","c":"Without the sitter's identity or a description to go on, the picture is best judged as a type: Jarvis's standard half-length for a man of business or the professions around 1815, direct, quickly handled and unadorned. His men have a lively, slightly rough presence that holds up well beside more polished contemporaries, and the same quality gives his weaker pictures a hurried look. This one belongs among his sound, characteristic work."},"met:11242":{"d":"A woman, Elizabeth Freeman Duran, is shown in a miniature about 7.9 by 6.2 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1815. The museum gives no description of her features, dress or setting, so none is detailed here. It is a small bust-length likeness of the kind made to be held, worn in a case or kept in a drawer, with the ivory glowing through the thin flesh tones.","i":"A miniature of about 1815 might mark a marriage or be sent to an absent relative, and its small size made it the most portable form of portrait. By the 1810s the fashion for them in America was near its height, and even a busy oil painter like Jarvis took such commissions. The record gives no biography of the sitter, so the occasion for this one is unknown.","c":"The small scale does not favor Jarvis's strengths, which lie in quick, broad handling more than in fine stippling. A miniature by him is typically brisk and likeable, though the surface can look slighter than a specialist's. The result is a pleasant, characteristic piece rather than a standout of the genre, and it is most interesting when set beside the larger oils that made his reputation."},"cma:127665":{"d":"A man named Benjamin Rouse is portrayed in oil on a small canvas, about 55.5 by 44 cm, dated 1819. The museum gives no description of him, so his pose, dress and setting are not detailed here. At this size the picture is noticeably smaller than the half-length canvases of about 86 by 68 cm that Jarvis used for many sitters, and it was probably a head or bust-length likeness.","i":"A smaller canvas usually meant a smaller fee, and a closely cropped portrait was a practical choice for a sitter who wanted a likeness without the cost of a full half-length. In 1819 Jarvis was in his prime and one of New York's busiest portraitists, and he painted General Andrew Jackson at about the same time. The museum record does not identify Benjamin Rouse, so the context of the commission is unknown.","c":"A small portrait leaves a painter fewer places to hide: the face and its expression must carry everything. Jarvis's talent for a quick, lively impression is well suited to such a format, though his lack of finish can be more apparent when the surface is small. Without more evidence, this is best taken as a competent, unfussy example of his work in 1819."},"met:11243":{"d":"A portrait of General Andrew Jackson, painted in oil on a canvas about 123 by 91 cm, around 1819. The museum record gives no description of his pose, uniform or setting, so only the type is given: a large, formal portrait of a military hero, on a bigger canvas than most of Jarvis's usual half-length portraits, made as a public image of a national figure.","i":"Jackson became a national hero for his victory at the Battle of New Orleans in January 1815, and by 1819 he was the most popular military figure in the country; he would be elected president in 1828. Jarvis was the portraitist New York's city government turned to for the heroes of the recent war, and Jackson, the victor of New Orleans, was the most celebrated of them.","c":"A public portrait of a hero tests a painter differently from a private likeness, because the figure must command attention. Jarvis was better at alert, lively faces than at stately accessories, and the larger canvas gives him more to manage: uniform, pose and background. Without a description to go on, it is best judged as a competent public image in which the sitter's fame counts for more than the painter's invention."},"nga:34153":{"d":"A man in a blue coat with gold embroidery is shown from the waist up, facing slightly left, one hand on his hip and the other holding a long wooden staff. He has a fair complexion, dark eyes, a rounded face and dark curled hair in eighteenth-century style. The coat has wide sleeves, buttons and white frilled cuffs over a white garment with gold detailing. Behind him a hazy seascape shows a ship on the right flying a flag, in muted blues and browns. The oil on canvas measures about 127 by 102 cm.","i":"The ship and flag place the sitter in naval service, and the museum calls him a British navy officer, though his name is lost. Wollaston painted him about 1745, before he crossed to America: he was a London-trained painter who worked in New York, Philadelphia, Annapolis, Virginia and Charleston from about 1749. The hand on the hip and the staff are poses of command and ease taken from English portraits, and the ship behind is the standard emblem of a naval career.","c":"A confident piece of English portraiture by a painter who would soon carry its manner to the colonies. Wollaston's habits are visible in the smooth, rounded face and the careful, glossy rendering of a gold-embroidered coat, the skill of a drapery painter. The sea and ship are shorthand rather than observation, but they do their job, and the open, easy stance is more engaging than many stiffer naval portraits of the time."},"met:13334":{"d":"A man named Cadwallader Colden is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted between 1749 and 1752, in Wollaston's first years in New York. The museum gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait on the standard canvas size of 30 by 25 inches, in the English manner, made as the companion of a portrait of his wife.","i":"Colden (1688–1776) was a physician, botanist and colonial official of Scottish descent and education, who served as surveyor general of New York and later, from 1761 until the Revolution, as its lieutenant governor. He wrote on natural philosophy and plants and corresponded with Linnaeus and Benjamin Franklin; his daughter Jane became a respected botanist. A man of his learning and rank sat for a portrait to affirm his place among the colonial gentry.","c":"Wollaston's smooth, glossy formula leaves little room for a sitter's intellect, and the museum record mentions no attribute of Colden's learning. What the picture offers instead is the polish of London portraiture in a colonial city that had little else like it, and a record of a notable figure in his early sixties. It is a competent, courteous likeness, more a document of status than of the thinker."},"met:13335":{"d":"A man named Joseph Reade is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted between 1749 and 1752. The museum gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait in the English Rococo manner of the day, with the head as the main interest, painted to hang beside the portrait of his wife.","i":"The sitter is probably the prominent New Yorker of that name after whom Reade Street in Lower Manhattan is called, though the record here does not say so. Wollaston painted many members of the city's elite after arriving from England about 1749, supplying London fashion to men and women who wanted to look up to date. A matching pair of husband and wife was the usual way of furnishing a good room.","c":"A routine but pleasing example of Wollaston's New York work. His men tend to share the same smooth, unlined faces and the same easy poise, so that the individual hardly emerges, but the finish is polished and the handling assured. It adheres to a successful formula that gave colonial sitters the London look they paid for, and it should be valued as such rather than as a searching likeness."},"met:13336":{"d":"A woman, Alice Colden, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted around 1749 to 1752. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of a woman of the colonial elite in the English Rococo manner, made as the companion of the portrait of her husband, Cadwallader Colden.","i":"As the wife of a leading official and scientist, Alice Colden belonged to the top rank of New York society, and a matching pair of portraits declared the household's standing. Her daughter Jane Colden became one of the first women botanists in America, working in the tradition of plant study that her father had begun. The record here gives no further biography of the sitter.","c":"In pairs like this the wife's portrait usually gives the painter more to do, with lace, silk and jewelry at hand for the display of his skill with fabric. The museum record lists no such details, so the picture must be judged mainly by its type: courteous, smooth and somewhat anonymous. It is a respectable record of a notable colonial household rather than an arresting portrait in its own right."},"met:13337":{"d":"A woman, Anna Reade, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted around 1749 to 1752. The museum's record lists a lace cap with a bow and ribbon, a fichu (the light scarf worn over the shoulders and neckline) and a pearl necklace, but nothing more about her pose or the setting. It was painted as the companion of the portrait of her husband, Joseph Reade.","i":"Lace, ribbon and pearls were imported luxuries, and the cap and fichu were the standard dress of a respectable matron in the mid-eighteenth century; together they say that she was a woman of means and propriety. Wollaston's New York clients wanted to be shown in current English fashion, and a woman's portrait was the natural place to display it. Pearls were also a traditional sign of purity and worth.","c":"Lace, bow and pearls play to Wollaston's strength, a glossy, delicate rendering of surfaces, and they are where one would look for the best passages in the picture. The face is likely to follow his formula of smooth, rounded features and large, almond-shaped eyes. The result is an elegant, slightly impersonal portrait of a New York matron of about 1750, polished rather than penetrating."},"met:13338":{"d":"A man named William Axtell is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 127 by 101.6 cm, close to 50 by 40 inches, painted between about 1749 and 1752. The museum gives no description of his pose, dress or setting. The canvas is much larger than the 76 by 63.5 cm size Wollaston used for several portraits of these years, which would allow a figure shown well below the waist, with room for hands and furniture.","i":"In English portrait practice the price rose with the size of the canvas, and a sitter who chose a large one was paying to be shown at fuller length and with more of the furnishings of a gentleman's life. Axtell was presumably a New Yorker of means, but the record here gives no biography, so the reasons for the more ambitious format are not known.","c":"The larger canvas gives Wollaston room, and a painter whose strength lies in drapery and surface has more to show when more of the figure is visible. Without a description, the picture can only be judged as a type: an ambitious, expensive version of his usual formula. It is bigger in scale than most of his colonial work, but the limits of the formula, smooth faces and stock poses, remain."},"nga:1134":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mary Walton Morris is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted between 1749 and 1752, probably in New York. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of a colonial lady in the manner of London portraiture of the 1740s, painted by an English-trained artist who had just arrived in America.","i":"Wollaston reached New York about 1749 and found a market among the city's prosperous families, who wanted portraits in the up-to-date London style. Her name suggests she was born a Walton and married into the Morris family, one of the colony's great landed families; a companion portrait, A Gentleman of the Morris Family, matches this one in date and size. A woman's portrait of this date presents her as a refined, well-dressed lady of the colonial gentry.","c":"Wollaston is often remembered for a mannerism, the large, almond-shaped eyes that his sitters share, and for a gift for glossy fabric. The sameness of faces is a weakness, but his portraits of this date have a polish and elegance that were new to the colonies. A woman's portrait is an ideal vehicle for his talents, since dress offers him more to do than a face alone. It is a sound example of the type that made his reputation."},"nga:1135":{"d":"A man shown from the chest up faces slightly left, his gaze directed forward. He has a smooth complexion and medium-length, curly brown hair that frames his face, and he wears an elegant coat with distinct buttons over a high-collared shirt, with a subtle shine and texture in the cloth. He wears no jewelry and holds no objects. The background is a dark, neutral color against which the lighter tones of face and clothing stand out. The oil on canvas measures about 76 by 63.5 cm.","i":"The title records only that the sitter belonged to the Morris family; his name has been lost or was never recorded. The Morrises were among the great landowning families of colonial New York, and the portrait, painted by Wollaston soon after his arrival there, was probably one of a group of family likenesses. A plain coat, no props and a dark ground were the standard formula of the English portrait for a gentleman shown well dressed but unostentatious.","c":"Plain and well-bred, the portrait follows a London formula so closely that the sitter hardly emerges as an individual; what stands out is the smooth, glossy finish and the quiet contrast of pale face against dark ground. Wollaston's habit of giving his sitters similar features is part of his reputation. But the type has its own appeal, and in 1750 it would have looked strikingly sophisticated beside the work of local painters."},"nga:34151":{"d":"A man's upper body is shown facing slightly sideways toward the viewer. He has distinct features, with a noticeable nose and a slight smile, and curled hair frames his face. He wears a dark blue coat over a detailed white waistcoat with buttons and embroidery, and a white cravat. The background is a plain dark brown. The oil on canvas measures about 76.5 by 63.6 cm, and it was painted between about 1749 and 1752.","i":"The question mark in the title shows that the identification as John Stevens is uncertain. The embroidered waistcoat is the most telling detail: such fabric was imported and costly, so it marks the sitter as a man of means, one of the colonial merchants and landowners who sat to Wollaston in New York around 1750. A faint smile adds a touch of warmth to a convention that usually asked for composure.","c":"A pleasant, competent likeness whose best feature is the embroidered waistcoat, the kind of rich fabric Wollaston rendered with obvious skill. The face is individual enough to feel like a person, though it shares the family resemblance of Wollaston's sitters. The dark ground and dark blue coat give the picture a sober harmony, a little heavy, and make it a good, if not outstanding, example of his American work."},"nga:43707":{"d":"A man stands in slight profile facing left in this upper-body portrait, serious and clean-shaven, with white hair styled to the sides. He wears a dark coat with large buttons, a dark waistcoat and a white shirt with ruffled cuffs, and rests his left hand on an ornate, carved piece of furniture. The wall behind him is plain and textured, and the date 1755 is inscribed at the lower right. The oil on bed ticking, a coarse mattress fabric used here in place of canvas, measures about 92 by 71 centimeters.","i":"Captain Isaac Foster was part of a family that sat to Joseph Badger in 1755, a group of portraits that records a New England household in its best clothes. Badger, a Boston house painter and glazier turned self-taught portraitist, was one of the busiest portrait painters in the city in the 1750s, and his clients were merchants, ministers and farmers who wanted a dignified likeness at a modest price. The title of captain marks a rank, probably in the militia.","c":"Badger's style is plain and linear, with flat areas of color, firm outlines and a naive treatment of space. The strengths here are the clarity of the face, the strong character of the features and the carefully noted details of cuffs and carving. The weakness is stiffness, especially in the hand on the furniture. It is a refreshingly honest portrait, a document of colonial taste that lacks Copley's brilliance but has an unpretentious dignity."},"nga:43708":{"d":"A woman sits with both hands resting in her lap in this waist-length portrait, her expression neutral, her eyes blue and her hair modestly dressed under a sheer white bonnet. She wears a grayish-brown formal dress with a fitted bodice, wide sleeves with white lace cuffs and a lace neckline, a blue ribbon tied in a bow at the throat and small earrings. The background is a simple gradient of muted tones. The oil on bed ticking, dated 1755, measures about 92 by 71 centimeters.","i":"The sitter, Eleanor Wyer Foster, is identified by her husband's name as Mrs. Isaac Foster, and her portrait was painted as a pendant to his: husband and wife appear in matching size and on the same fabric, a pairing common in colonial New England. Her dress, with its lace and ribbon, shows the modest elegance of a prosperous household, and the hands folded in the lap are the standard sign of a respectable woman's decorum. The sheer white bonnet marks her as a married woman.","c":"Badger's women's portraits rely on costume: the fine lace cuffs, the neckline and the ribbon carry as much of the picture's life as the face, and he renders them with care. The face, with its neutral gaze, is typical of his plain style. As a pair with her husband's portrait it works well, and as an image of a colonial matron it is dignified, reserved and quietly charming, though it lacks psychological depth."},"nga:43709":{"d":"A young man with fair skin, a round face and light brown curly hair stands with his right hand in his coat and his left hand just visible, in this portrait of about 81 by 66 centimeters dated 1755. He wears a gray coat with large buttons, a black collar and a white neckcloth, the dress of a gentleman in miniature. The background combines dark gray and light brown tones. The museum's keywords call him a boy.","i":"Dressing a boy in the clothing of a grown gentleman was normal in colonial portraits: children were small adults in dress and pose, and the hand in the coat copied the fashionable pose of portraits of men. The name Isaac Foster, Jr. marks him as his father's namesake, and his father, Captain Isaac Foster, was painted by Badger in the same year, so the set recorded the household as a group.","c":"The portrait is touching in its seriousness: a youth in a man's coat, with round face and curls and a hand tucked in with the borrowed confidence of the pose. Badger gives him a plain, direct stare. The flat modeling and stiff arrangement of the arms betray the limits of his technique, but they also contribute to the picture's guileless charm, and it shows how colonial children were pictured as miniature adults."},"nga:43710":{"d":"A man stands with his left arm bent across his waist and his right hand tucked into his coat, in this portrait of about 91 by 71 centimeters dated 1755. He has brown hair, distinct features and a serious expression, and wears a brown coat over a white shirt with a ruffled cuff. The background is plain, shading from dark brown to lighter tones. The title records his profession, doctor, but the museum's description mentions no attributes of the physician's trade.","i":"The hand tucked into the coat, a gesture borrowed from European portraits where it signified a gentleman's ease and self-possession, was fashionable in the 1750s, and colonial painters like Badger often learned such poses from imported prints. William Foster belonged to the same family as the other sitters Badger painted in 1755; as a doctor he belonged to the educated professions, whose members sat for portraits as proof of learning and respectability.","c":"The pose is the picture's most sophisticated element, taken from fashionable prints, and Badger manages it with some awkwardness: the arm and hand sit stiffly, and the body lacks the flexible structure of a trained portraitist's. Yet the head is clear and direct and the color plain. It is a typical example of provincial Boston portraiture before Copley, honest and slightly clumsy."},"met:10085":{"d":"A boy named James Badger is portrayed in oil on a canvas about 108 by 84 cm, dated 1760. The museum's record lists a bird, a cherry, a cuff, a belt, a shoe and a buckle among the details, so the child holds or is accompanied by a bird and fruit and is dressed with some care; his pose, the colors and the setting are not described. It is a full child's portrait of a kind typical of mid-eighteenth-century New England.","i":"The sitter shares the painter's surname, and a family relationship is likely. Badger often put fruit and animals into his portraits of children, and such objects are usually read as symbols: ripening fruit for growth, a pet for affection or loyalty. The record here does not spell out their meaning, and a bird and cherries may also be simply a child's playthings or treats. Like most children's portraits of the period, it shows a boy as a small adult in the making.","c":"Badger, a Boston house and sign painter by trade, had no formal training, and his portraits are flat, linear and stiffly posed. The stiffness is partly the charm: clear outlines and simple shapes give the picture a directness that more polished painters lost. Beside the smooth English manner of Blackburn or Smibert, Badger is naive, but his children, with their fruit and animals, have a conviction that sophisticated work can lack."},"cma:99443":{"d":"A boy of about seven, Jeremiah Belknap, is shown in the clothing of a grown man, so that he looks like a miniature adult. In his right hand he holds a ripening peach, and a dog sits at his feet. The oil on canvas measures about 107 by 84 cm and dates from about 1758. The museum gives no further description, so the colors, setting and cut of his clothes are not detailed here.","i":"According to the museum, Badger's portraits of children often include fruit and animals; here the ripening peach suggests maturation, and the dog loyalty. Dressing a child as a small adult was the custom of the time, and it expressed the family's hopes: the portrait shows what the boy was meant to become, a sober and dependable gentleman. Badger, who began by painting houses and signs in Boston, turned to portraits as the colonial market for them grew.","c":"Badger's lack of training shows in the flat modeling and stiff pose, but the picture gains a clarity that is easy to enjoy. The boy's grown-up dress and serious bearing, with the peach and the dog stated plainly, make an image both charming and a little uncanny, a child enrolled early in the adult world. It lacks the polish of Smibert or Blackburn, but as a document of how families saw their children it is more revealing."},"met:10174":{"d":"A young woman, Mary Sylvester, also recorded as Mary Dering, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 127 by 102 cm, dated 1754. The museum's record notes a sheep among the details but gives no description of her pose, dress, colors or setting, so only the type is given: a large portrait on a canvas of 50 by 40 inches, in the fashionable English Rococo manner that Blackburn had brought to the colonies from England.","i":"A sheep in a woman's portrait suggests a pastoral conceit, a Rococo fashion in England in which a sitter might be shown as a shepherdess in a gentle landscape, though the record does not describe the picture. Blackburn worked in Bermuda before moving to the mainland colonies, where he introduced a lighter, more ornamental portrait style than colonists had known. The museum record also gives the name Mary Dering, probably her married name.","c":"Blackburn's Rococo manner brought light colors, silky surfaces and graceful poses to colonial portraiture, and a young woman in pastoral dress is a congenial subject. The weakness is the one he shows everywhere: poses and faces that vary little, and a polish that sits on the surface. Beside the plainer local painters of the day his work was a revelation, and this portrait is representative of the lighter side of his art."},"met:10175":{"d":"A woman, Margaret Chesebrough, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 127 by 102 cm, dated 1754. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a large portrait on a canvas of about 50 by 40 inches, painted soon after Blackburn reached the American colonies, in the light, fashionable English Rococo style.","i":"The title records her by her husband's name, David Chesebrough, and the museum's catalog adds her own first name. Colonial merchants had grown rich on trade with England and the West Indies, and their wives and daughters were eager to be shown in the latest London fashion. A portrait on this scale was a considerable investment, intended to hang in a main room as a statement of the family's means.","c":"A woman's portrait in Blackburn's early colonial manner would typically be upright, elegantly dressed and a little remote, with silk and lace rendered more smoothly than colonial viewers had seen. The formula flatters the sitter more than it describes her, and individual character is the first casualty, but the freshness of the manner in 1754 is easy to understand."},"nga:34073":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, slightly turned to the left with his gaze forward and a calm expression. His white hair is styled in the curls of the eighteenth century, and he wears a military uniform: a red coat with gold details over a white waistcoat and black undershirt, with a black cravat at his neck. His right hand is tucked inside his coat, and the background is a plain, muted brown. The canvas is about 78 by 64 centimetres and was painted in 1756.","i":"The hand tucked into the coat was an established gesture of calm authority and gentlemanly ease in eighteenth-century portraits. Joseph Blackburn, an English painter who worked in Bermuda and then in New England in the 1750s and early 1760s, brought a fashionable Rococo manner to the colonies, and this picture dates from the time of the French and Indian War, when many officers wanted portraits. The sitter is not named, so he is read as a type: a colonial or British officer.","c":"Blackburn's gift was for making colonial sitters look fashionable: the bright red coat, the silky white waistcoat and the plain ground give a clean, confident effect. The face, however, is calm to the point of blankness, and the pose is a stock one. Blackburn's smooth, flattering manner influenced the young Copley, who soon surpassed him in truthfulness, but within his limits he was an accomplished and elegant portraitist."},"cma:99428":{"d":"Hannah Wentworth Atkinson is posed bolt upright in a billowing satin gown that dominates the picture, so that she seems almost a mannequin for the display of rich fabrics. The oil on canvas measures about 125 by 99 cm and dates from 1760. The museum describes her as the wife of the chief justice, and richest man, of the colony of New Hampshire. It gives no further detail of her pose, the color of the gown or the setting, so none is invented here.","i":"Hannah Wentworth Atkinson belonged to the elite of New Hampshire, where the Wentworth and Atkinson families were prominent in government and trade. The gown is the main message of the portrait: imported silk was costly, and its display told of a household's wealth and of its ties to English fashion. Blackburn, who had worked in Bermuda before moving to the colonies, built his reputation by presenting the fashionable tastes of such sitters.","c":"The satin is the picture's real subject, and Blackburn paints it with relish: broad, shining planes of cloth that few colonial painters could manage. The cost is the person, because the mannequin-like pose gives little sense of character, and the portrait reads more as a fashion plate than a likeness. Within his work it is typical, glittering and a little hollow, and a good record of what wealth wanted to look like in 1760."},"met:10176":{"d":"A woman, Anna Cutts, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 128 by 103 cm, painted around 1762–63. The museum's record lists a dress, lace, an armband and a flower among the details but gives no further description of her pose, colors or setting. It was painted as the companion of the portrait of her husband, Samuel Cutts, at the same size and date.","i":"In the early 1760s Blackburn was working in New Hampshire, where Portsmouth's merchants and shipowners prospered on the timber and West Indies trade. In a woman's portrait of the period, lace, flowers and dress do much of the talking: they signal the household's taste and imported luxury. A flower held or worn was a stock accessory of Rococo portraits, standing for freshness, virtue or the passing of beauty.","c":"Blackburn's women are stylish and graceful, and this one has the advantage of a good costume to paint; lace and flowers allow the soft, flickering touch at which he excelled. What the formula lacks is variation, since poses and expressions repeat from sitter to sitter, and the faces seem fitted onto the costume rather than shaping it. It is a handsome, skilled picture of the kind that made his reputation in New England."},"met:10177":{"d":"A man, Samuel Cutts, is portrayed in oil on a canvas about 128 by 103 cm, painted around 1762–63 as the companion of his wife's portrait. The museum gives no description of his pose, clothes or setting, so only the type is given: a large, formal portrait of a prosperous New Hampshire gentleman in the English Rococo manner, on a canvas of about 50 by 40 inches.","i":"The Cutts family were prominent in Portsmouth's maritime trade, and a large matched pair of portraits was the right way for such a household to announce its standing. Blackburn used a small repertory of stock poses for men, such as a hand tucked into the waistcoat, borrowed from English portraiture, which let him produce a dignified, up-to-date image quickly. The record does not say which pose was chosen here.","c":"Blackburn's men are the less inventive half of his output: poised, correct and very like one another. The formula flatters by making every sitter a gentleman at ease, and it can work well when the costume has some richness to offer. As a counterpart to the lace and flowers of his wife's portrait it is serviceable, and its main interest is as a record of New Hampshire's mercantile elite in the early 1760s."},"cma:99446":{"d":"Boston civic administrator John Browne is seated with his legs crossed at the knees, his right hand partially inserted in his waistcoat and his left hand pressed against his hip. The oil on canvas measures about 123 by 99 cm and dates from about 1760. The museum gives no description of his clothing, the setting or the colors, so none is invented here.","i":"The hand in the waistcoat, borrowed from English portraiture, signals ease and self-possession, and the crossed legs add a casual elegance. Blackburn used the same pose for other male sitters, including Browne's brother-in-law, which shows how he worked: a repertory of stock poses chosen to flatter. For a colonial official the formula suggested a gentleman at leisure in the London manner.","c":"The stock pose works, partly because it is a good one: relaxed, polite, a little theatrical. But the repetition is also the picture's limit. A sitter of Browne's rank received the same poise as every other of Blackburn's clients, and the portrait says more about Boston fashion around 1760 than about the man. Blackburn is at his best in costume, and a man's coat gives him less to do than a woman's satin."},"nga:166431":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a man with rosy cheeks turns his head to look at the viewer in front of a window opening. His chestnut hair is brushed off his high forehead, a white cravat is knotted at his throat, and his left hand rests on his hip, pulling back a dark brown coat with flaring sleeves and silver embroidery. His long aqua-blue waistcoat is covered in floral brocade, and in his right hand he holds a black tricorn hat. Green drapery hangs at the left, and a twilight sky glows over distant treetops. The sill is signed I. Blackburn Pinx.","i":"The costume is the message: the embroidered waistcoat of silk brocade, the silver-trimmed coat and the fine shirt cuffs announce a man of wealth, and the tricorn hat, the drapery and the window with its view of landscape are the usual props of a gentleman's portrait. The sitter is not named, but such pictures were made for merchants and officials of the colonial towns. The Latin Pinx, short for pinxit, painted it, is the painter's customary way of signing.","c":"Blackburn's brilliance is in textiles: the brocade waistcoat, the silver embroidery and the shimmering drapery are painted with relish and give the portrait a Rococo sparkle. The man himself is a little doll-like, with flushed cheeks and a stock pose, and the picture lacks the penetrating truthfulness that Copley would soon bring to colonial portraiture. Even so, it is a delightful display of European style in the colonies."},"met:11772":{"d":"A woman, Catherine Littlefield, who appears as Mrs. Phineas Miller, is portrayed in a miniature about 6.4 by 5.2 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1806. The museum gives no description of her features or dress, so only the type is given: a small bust-length likeness, made to be kept in a case or locket, by a miniaturist who painted portraits in America after fleeing France.","i":"Catherine Littlefield (1755–1814) was the widow of the Revolutionary War general Nathanael Greene and later the wife of Phineas Miller, partner of Eli Whitney in the cotton gin enterprise, which took shape at her Georgia plantation. The painter, Joseph-Pierre Picot de Limoëlan de Clorivière, was a French royalist who fled to the United States in 1801 after being linked to a bomb plot against Napoleon, painted miniatures for a time and later became a Catholic priest.","c":"The interest is largely historical: a famous sitter, and a painter whose life was stranger than his art. French miniature training favored delicate, clear drawing, and a piece like this probably shows that discipline, but the record gives too little to judge how well it succeeds. Beside American miniaturists of the time such as Edward Greene Malbone, it is a competent professional's work rather than a bold one."},"met:11773":{"d":"An unnamed woman is shown in a miniature about 6.7 by 5.4 cm, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1808. The museum gives no description of her appearance, so none is given here. It is typical of the period: a bust-length likeness meant for a locket or small case, with fine strokes of watercolor and the pale ivory glowing through the thin paint.","i":"The sitter's name is lost, a common fate for miniatures, but the painter's is not: Joseph-Pierre Picot de Limoëlan de Clorivière, a Breton nobleman and former royalist conspirator who took up miniature painting to earn a living in the United States and later entered the priesthood. His route into the profession was unusual, though the demand for small likenesses in these years was steady and wide.","c":"At about 6.7 cm the picture has the typical virtues and limits of a refugee professional's miniature: neat drawing, a delicate hand, a decorous mood. French training in the genre was strong, and a clear, polite likeness is what one expects. Beside the best Americans of the day it is more reserved than inventive. The painter's history is more remarkable than the picture, but the miniature is a pleasant specimen."},"nga:50256":{"d":"In a wide green landscape, a bearded man sits near a wooden hut at left, a black goat and a white sheep beside him; one child stands in the hut and three more sit together on the ground at center. At right a person in flowing clothes stands by a white horse, holding a plumed helmet and the reins before a tall brown tree. A distant white building, pale mountains and soft gray clouds fill the background. The oil on canvas is huge, about 2.5 by 4.4 meters.","i":"The subject comes from Torquato Tasso's epic Jerusalem Delivered, about the First Crusade. The pagan princess Erminia, in love with the Christian knight Tancredi, flees in borrowed armor and finds shelter among shepherds, whose simple life she envies; she is the figure with the white horse and plumed helmet. Tasso's romantic, pastoral episodes were favorites of eighteenth-century painters, and a canvas of this size was probably made for a large room, perhaps as part of a series.","c":"The huge canvas gives Guardi room for a pastoral idyll in which the figures are small, light and graceful, set against soft greens and a delicate sky. The mood is closer to elegant eighteenth-century fantasy than to epic. It is far from the Venetian views on which his fame rests, and it lacks the tension of the best Tasso illustrations, but its light touch and sense of space give it charm."},"cma:129801":{"d":"Four canvases of equal size, each about 57 by 76 cm, form a series painted in the 1750s: Abraham Welcoming the Three Angels, The Angels Appearing to Abraham, The Sacrifice of Isaac and Tobias and the Angel. Three tell the story of Abraham and Isaac, and one comes from the Book of Tobit; in every canvas humans and angels meet. In the most dramatic, an angel intercepts Abraham before he sacrifices his son. They were probably meant to hang together in a single room.","i":"The unifying themes are fathers and sons and obedience inspired by faith. Abraham receives angels and the promise of a son, then is tested through that very son; in the Book of Tobit, a father sends his son on a journey under an angel's guidance. Read in sequence, the pictures turn from hospitality to promise to trial, with Tobias adding a story of protection. Old Testament series were a staple of eighteenth-century Venetian decoration.","c":"Seen as a set, the four canvases show Francesco Guardi in a role later overshadowed, as a figure painter of a family workshop before his Venetian views made his name. The group is coherent and graceful rather than profound; its angels are more charming than awe-inspiring. If the series has a lesson for the critic, it is that the flickering touch of the late views grew out of this lighter, Rococo narrative style."},"cma:129802":{"d":"At the climax of the story, an angel intercepts Abraham before he can sacrifice his own son, Isaac. This canvas, about 57 by 76 cm, is the most dramatic of a series of four of equal size painted by Francesco Guardi in the 1750s, three devoted to Abraham and one to Tobias, each showing humans and angels meeting. Compact horizontal canvases of this kind were probably made for a private room, where the set could be hung together and read in sequence.","i":"Genesis 22 tells how God tested Abraham by commanding him to offer up Isaac, the son so long promised, and how an angel stayed his hand at the last moment. Readers took the story as the supreme example of obedience inspired by faith, and Christian tradition also saw in it a foreshadowing of the sacrifice of God's own Son. In a series built around fathers and sons, it is the point where promise turns to trial.","c":"Guardi is remembered for his views of Venice, so a figure subject from the workshop years is a useful reminder of where he began. The quick, nervous touch that later dissolved Venetian architecture into light is the thing to look for here. The drama has a Rococo lightness that cannot match the force of Tiepolo, the obvious Venetian model for such a subject: the angel's intervention is effective theater more than conviction."},"cma:129803":{"d":"Tobias catches a fish while the angel Raphael, who accompanies him in the disguise of an ordinary man, oversees the catch. Tobias has been sent by his blind father, Tobit, to collect a sum of money in distant Media, and the fish will later supply the cure for Tobit's blindness. The canvas, about 57 by 76 cm and painted in the 1750s, is the odd one out in a series of four that otherwise follows the story of Abraham.","i":"The Book of Tobit, accepted in the Catholic and Orthodox canons but not the Protestant, tells of a pious man who goes blind and sends his son on a journey guided, unknowingly, by the archangel Raphael, whose name means 'God heals'. The fish's gall restores Tobit's sight. Raphael became the protector of travelers and of the young, and in Venice, where the Guardi family painted a Tobias cycle for the church of the Angelo Raffaele, the story was especially familiar.","c":"Tobias and the fish is a congenial subject for a painter with Guardi's feeling for water and light, and it ties the series to its theme of angelic guidance. The picture suits the set's modest scale and is agreeable rather than ambitious. Critics rank this phase of his career well below the Venetian views for which he is famous, but the quick touch that would later make those views is already at work in a more narrative key."},"cma:129804":{"d":"At the moment of revelation, three angels in pastel gowns and feathery wings announce to Abraham, after the meal he has offered them, that his wife Sarah, long past child-bearing age, will soon have a son. Sarah learns the news while hiding behind the door of their cottage. Abraham had first offered to feed the angels and wash their feet. The canvas, about 57 by 76 cm, is one of four of equal size painted in the 1750s.","i":"In Genesis 18 the visitors repeat a promise God had made earlier: Sarah will bear a son within the year. Overhearing at the tent door, Sarah laughs to herself, and the name of the child, Isaac, is traditionally explained as 'he laughs'. The episode shows divine power overcoming nature, and it sets up the later test of the promised son in the Sacrifice of Isaac, the dramatic climax of the series.","c":"The cottage door and the hiding Sarah add a note of domestic comedy to a sacred announcement, and the pastel gowns and feathery wings give the heavenly visitors a Rococo lightness that suits Venice in the 1750s. It is a pleasant, rather decorative treatment of a weighty promise. As storytelling the series works well here, where gesture and setting make the moment legible at a glance."},"cma:129805":{"d":"Abraham bows low before three visitors who prove to be angels in human guise, the opening episode of the series. Incongruously, the meeting takes place in front of a marble classical portico in which a woman lurks, presumably Abraham's wife Sarah. The canvas measures about 57 by 76 cm and dates from the 1750s. The tent and trees of the Genesis account give way to antique architecture, so that a Near Eastern camp becomes an Italian stage set.","i":"In Genesis 18 Abraham sees three men at midday, runs to greet them and bows to the ground, offering water, shade and food. Only gradually do they prove to be messengers of God. The scene is a model of hospitality, and Christian readers often saw in the three visitors a foreshadowing of the Trinity. The woman lurking behind anticipates the next episode, in which Sarah overhears the promise of a son.","c":"The invention here is the architecture: a marble portico far grander than any tent, which gives a small canvas the dignity of a stage. The anachronism is deliberate and charming, typical of a Venetian Rococo that cared little for historical accuracy. The figures, as usual with Guardi, are handled with a quick, light touch. It is a polished decorative picture rather than a searching religious one."},"met:438023":{"d":"Men appear in a grand interior, the antechamber of the Sala del Maggior Consiglio, the hall of the Great Council in the Doge's Palace, Venice. The oil on canvas, about 34 by 51 cm, dates from about 1765 to 1768 and matches in size and date Guardi's picture of the Ridotto in the same museum, with which it probably forms a pair. Guardi, best known for open-air views, here turns indoors to a room of the Venetian state.","i":"The Great Council, made up of all the adult male nobles of the Republic, was the sovereign body of Venice, and its hall in the Doge's Palace was among the largest in Europe. Its antechamber was probably where members waited and conferred before sessions, so a view of it records a piece of the machinery of government. Guardi painted it only decades before the Republic fell to Napoleon in 1797, which gives the room an elegiac note for later viewers.","c":"Canaletto would have measured this room; Guardi suggests it. The architecture matters less than light and the small figures, which are touched in with the quick accents of his mature manner. The subject lacks the sparkle of his lagoon views, and the small scale keeps it modest, but as a record of Venetian public life it shows how well his shorthand serves a human gathering."},"met:438024":{"d":"Masked men and women gamble in the Ridotto, the public gaming house in the Palazzo Dandolo near San Moisè in Venice. The oil on canvas, about 34 by 51 cm, was painted around 1765 to 1768. Gambling, masks and the room itself are its subjects, and it matches Guardi's view of the antechamber of the Great Council Hall in size and date, so the two were probably conceived as a pair of Venetian interiors.","i":"The Ridotto opened in 1638 as a state-licensed gambling house, and for more than a century it was a pillar of both Venetian pleasure and public finance. Masks, so familiar in Carnival Venice, hid rank and identity, so nobles, citizens and visitors could mix across class lines. The Great Council closed it in 1774, so a picture of the 1760s records a world that was soon legislated away.","c":"The subject suits Guardi's temperament. A crowd of masked gamblers is the right material for a painter who builds figures from quick flecks of dark and bright, and the picture can carry a wry social bite that his pure views never need. Compared with Pietro Longhi, the period's chronicler of Venetian manners, he is looser and less pointed about individuals but livelier in atmosphere."},"met:436597":{"d":"A view of Venice's great public square, with the Doge's Palace and the Campanile, the bell tower of St Mark's, among the buildings shown. The oil on canvas measures about 69 by 86 cm and dates from the late 1760s. It is a veduta, a topographical view, the kind of picture bought by foreign visitors on the Grand Tour as a souvenir of the city and a proof of their journey.","i":"The Piazza was the ceremonial heart of the Venetian Republic: the basilica of St Mark, the Doge's Palace and the Campanile stood together as church, government and city landmark. The tall brick Campanile collapsed in 1902 and was rebuilt as it had been, so Guardi's pictures preserve the sight of the original. Views like this carried the image of Venetian pageantry to patrons far from the lagoon.","c":"Guardi inherited the view-painter's formula from Canaletto but not his clarity. Where Canaletto measures, Guardi flickers: highlights skip across facades, figures are touched in as dabs, and the square seems to shimmer in the air. The method suits a famous place whose architecture a buyer already knows. Some find the result less authoritative, others more alive. It is characteristic of him rather than exceptional."},"met:436598":{"d":"The great domed church of Santa Maria della Salute stands beside the Grand Canal, with boats on the water, in this canvas of about 53 by 86 cm painted in the mid-to-late 1760s. It is the same size as the museum's Grand Canal above the Rialto, and both entered the collection in 1871, so they were probably conceived as companions. As a veduta, it shows a famous landmark to travelers who wished to take Venice home.","i":"The Salute was begun in 1631 to designs by Baldassare Longhena as a votive offering after a plague that killed perhaps a third of the city's people; it is dedicated to the Virgin as Our Lady of Health. Each November the Venetians cross the Grand Canal on a temporary bridge of boats to give thanks there. Its octagonal plan and domes make it the most recognizable church silhouette in the city.","c":"The Salute is the Venetian view-painter's gift: a great white mass of sculpted stone against a changeable sky, a motif well suited to Guardi's love of weather and water. The church was also a standard subject, painted by Canaletto and many others, and the conventions weigh on the picture. Its strength is not invention of subject but liveliness of touch, and on that score it is a good, if not a surprising, Guardi."},"met:436599":{"d":"A view of a stretch of the Grand Canal above the Rialto Bridge, with boats on the water, a bridge, and buildings crowding the banks. The canvas, about 53 by 86 cm, was painted in the late 1760s. It is a veduta, a view of a real place, made for buyers who wanted a souvenir of the city's main waterway. Guardi's handling of the water and the facades is characteristically quick.","i":"The Grand Canal is Venice's main street, lined with the palaces of merchant and noble families, and the Rialto district was the commercial center of the city, with its markets and banks. The Rialto Bridge, built in stone in 1591 by Antonio da Ponte, was the only fixed crossing of the Grand Canal until the nineteenth century. Pictures of its boat traffic conveyed Venice as a working city as well as a spectacle.","c":"Guardi's Grand Canal views are quieter than Canaletto's and more tonal: reflections break into strokes, and crowds of boats become rhythmic marks. The weaker side is a softness in architectural drawing, which a topographical view should state with authority. At its best the picture rewards close looking at touch more than at detail, which is the Guardi bargain."},"met:436593":{"d":"A very wide canvas, about 156 by 273 cm, shows an imaginary landscape with ruins, a boat and at least one man. The size is that of a room decoration, hung or set into paneling in a Venetian house or villa. It shares its height of about 156 cm with two narrower Fantastic Landscapes at the Met, the sort of match that suggests the three were made for the same room, and it belongs to the type called capriccio.","i":"The capriccio, an invented scene of ruins in a landscape, let painter and buyer indulge in daydream and learning together: the ruins recall Roman antiquity, while the boat and the figure bring it to human scale. Eighteenth-century Venice, itself a city of decay and splendor, took to these fantasies with enthusiasm. Without a documented commission, this one is best read as decoration meant to make a wall open onto an imaginary world.","c":"At over 2.7 meters wide, this is Guardi on a scale that demands composition as well as touch. The wide format spreads the picture, and success depends on whether the rhythm of ruins, water and figure holds the eye along its length. His quick, glittering touch suits crumbling masonry, yet it can read as sketch-like at this size. The result is atmospheric rather than monumental."},"met:436594":{"d":"A large imaginary landscape, about 156 by 189 cm, with boats on the water: a capriccio rather than a view of any real place. It matches another Fantastic Landscape at the Met in size and was given with it in 1953, so the two were presumably painted as a pair to hang together, probably as part of the decoration of a Venetian room. No building or event is named; the subject is the invented scene itself.","i":"The capriccio let eighteenth-century Venetian painters and collectors enjoy landscape as invention: ruins, water and sky arranged for effect rather than topographical truth. The tradition goes back to Marco Ricci, and it was a respectable alternative to the city views that visitors bought. A boat suggests travel and leisure, and the whole conveys a gentle melancholy about time. Without a known commission, the picture is best read as decoration giving a room a window onto an imagined elsewhere.","c":"As half of a pair, this canvas has to balance its twin, and the pleasure of the set lies in the echo. Guardi's handling in his capricci is generally freer than in commissioned views, and he exploits that freedom here. The risk of the type is monotony across so large a surface, where a viewer may want more incident. Atmosphere, not event, is the point, and it is persuasive."},"met:436595":{"d":"An imagined landscape, not a real place, filled with ruins and a boat. At about 156 by 189 cm it is a large canvas intended for decoration, probably for a room in a Venetian house or villa. The museum holds a second Fantastic Landscape of exactly the same size, given in the same year, so the two were probably painted as a pair, and a third, wider canvas of the same height suggests a larger scheme. The type is called a capriccio, a fantasy view that mixes ruins and landscape.","i":"Capricci, imaginary views assembled from ruins, architecture and landscape, were a Venetian specialty of the eighteenth century, developed by Marco Ricci and practiced by Canaletto and Guardi. Ruins stood for the passage of time and the grandeur of antiquity, and a boat on the water adds a human touch. Such pictures asked to be enjoyed as daydream rather than read as story or topography.","c":"Guardi's capricci are where his freedom shows most: with no real building to answer to, he lets the ruins crumble into touches of light and shade. At this size the looseness is a gamble. Where the touch is sure, as it usually is in his work of the 1760s, the effect is a shimmering reverie; where it slackens, a large canvas of ruins can seem more decorative than felt."},"nga:12148":{"d":"On the right, brown arches, a tower, a bridge and tall columns that look like ruins dominate a harbor. At the water's edge small figures carry boxes, tie barrels together and talk; some wear long coats and tricorn hats, others turbans and shawls draped over robes. To the left lies dark water in brown and blue, with distant white-sailed ships and smaller boats near shore, under a sky of loose, expressive strokes in blue, pale peach and gray. The oil on canvas measures about 122 by 178 cm.","i":"A capriccio mixes real and imagined buildings into an invented view. Here the ruins and the mixed dress, European coats beside eastern turbans, suggest a Mediterranean port of the mind rather than a real one. Such pictures appealed to Venetian and foreign collectors who enjoyed fantasy, ancient ruins and the cosmopolitan bustle of Adriatic trade. The turbans probably recall Venice's long trade with the Ottoman Empire.","c":"Guardi's capricci are where his imagination is freest. The loose brushwork of the sky, the flicker of figures along the quay and the tumble of arches have an improvisational energy that Canaletto's more exact art lacks. The weakness is a certain vagueness of structure: the architecture is more suggested than built, and the weight of the composition falls heavily on the right. Even so, it is an engaging example of his late taste for atmosphere over accuracy."},"nga:195496":{"d":"A small canvas of about 32 by 53 cm shows the church of Santa Maria della Salute in Venice, a building Guardi painted several times from the water. The museum gives no further description, so the viewpoint, boats, light and sky are not described here. It is a view picture, or veduta, from about 1770, of the kind made to be hung in a room or sent home by visitors as a memento of the city.","i":"The Salute was built as a votive offering after the plague of 1630–31, which killed a large part of Venice's population, and it stands at the entrance of the Grand Canal. Designed by Baldassare Longhena, its great dome and scroll-shaped buttresses make it one of the city's most recognizable silhouettes. Views of such sights were sold to visitors on the Grand Tour and kept by Venetians as records of their city.","c":"Guardi's late manner turns buildings into shimmering flickers of light and color, and the Salute's pale domes suit it well. At this small size the picture is likely to be quick, spirited and informal, closer to a sketch than to the careful perspective of Canaletto. The attraction is the touch; the limitation is the loss of the architectural precision that purchasers of views usually wanted. Guardi's reputation grew as taste moved toward atmosphere."},"met:438117":{"d":"The island of San Michele lies in the lagoon between Venice and Murano, and this picture records a tower, boats and the distant city. The oil on canvas, about 48 by 78 cm, dates from the 1770s. It is a veduta, a view of a real place, of modest cabinet size, and it matches the Met's Villa Loredan at Paese in size, with which it came in the same bequest. In Guardi's day the island belonged to a monastery; it became the city's cemetery only under French rule.","i":"San Michele in Isola was held for centuries by Camaldolese monks, and its church, begun by Mauro Codussi in the late 1460s, is regarded as the first Renaissance church in Venice. The island was a familiar sight to anyone leaving the city by the northern shore toward Murano or the mainland. Guardi's picture therefore shows an everyday approach to Venice rather than its ceremonial front, and the island's later role as the cemetery gives it an echo for modern viewers.","c":"Open water and sky are the material that Guardi's light, quick touch handles best, and a lagoon-side subject suits him better than the grand squares. The motif is modest, even plain, which is part of its charm: it avoids the monuments of the souvenir trade. As a cabinet picture it lacks the ambition of his large views, but it shows him observing rather than formulating, and it is none the worse for that."},"met:436596":{"d":"A broad view of the city from the water of the Bacino di San Marco, the open basin before the Piazza, with boats and the harbor in view and the buildings of Venice along the shore. The canvas, about 122 by 152 cm, dates from about 1765 to 1775. At this size, large for a veduta, it was a major decorative picture rather than a modest souvenir, probably meant to hold a wall in a collector's house.","i":"The Bacino was the front door of Venice: foreign ships anchored here, ambassadors landed at the Molo, and on Ascension Day the doge's state barge set out from this water for the ceremony of wedding the sea. A view from the water reproduces the traveler's first sight of the city on arrival. For eighteenth-century buyers, many of them foreigners on the Grand Tour, such a picture was proof of a journey as well as a decoration.","c":"On this scale Guardi has to sustain interest across a wide stretch of water, sky and facade, and the picture tests his method: the flickering touch that animates small views risks thinness across large areas. Where it succeeds, sky and sea carry the mood and the city reads as a luminous band; where it falters, the picture looks loose beside a Canaletto. Its virtue is atmosphere over accuracy."},"nga:1166":{"d":"Seen from above, a canal filled with more than two dozen gondolas and sailing boats runs between rows of golden-yellow and parchment-white buildings, three to five stories high, with low chimneys and laundry hanging from windows. In the distance a fawn-brown stone bridge with one wide arch, its deck covered by an arched walkway full of pedestrians, joins the banks, and the canal turns a corner beyond it. Two slender gondolas angle toward each other in the foreground, and three tall-masted ships stand among the smaller craft. The oil measures about 69 by 92 cm.","i":"The Rialto Bridge, completed in 1591 to a design by Antonio da Ponte, was the only fixed crossing of the Grand Canal until the nineteenth century, and the commercial heart of Venice stood around it. Views like this were bought by Grand Tour visitors who wanted a memento of the city's best-known sights, and Guardi's studio supplied them in numbers. The museum dates the picture only as probably about 1780.","c":"Guardi sets a pattern here that he repeated often: a high viewpoint, a crowd of small craft and a sky that carries the mood. The restless boats and the long diagonal of the canal give the view movement, and the golden-yellow of the facades against the blue is warm and pleasing. Beside Canaletto, whose Venice is solid and exact, Guardi's is more fluid and humid, and his buildings can seem to melt a little."},"nga:254":{"d":"From a point above the water, the view looks across a busy canal at a pink stone bridge with white-edged arches, flanked by rows of tightly packed buildings two to five stories high, with arched windows, small balconies and laundry on lines. At least fifteen small figures stand or walk on the bridge, touched in with dark red, brown, pale yellow and ice blue, and gondolas and other boats, each rowed by a standing oarsman, fill the water. The horizon sits just under halfway up; the sky is blue with streaked clouds. The oil measures about 50 by 77 cm.","i":"The three-arched bridge is presumably the Ponte dei Tre Archi, the only bridge of its kind in Venice, over the Cannaregio Canal. Cannaregio was a densely settled residential district, far from the grand monuments of San Marco, and Guardi's choice of laundry, gondoliers and neighbors over palaces shows a market for views of the city's daily life. Such pictures were bought by foreign visitors as souvenirs and by Venetians as records of their city.","c":"This is Guardi at his most appealing: flickering touches of dark red and ice blue that stand for people, the tilt and shimmer of the boats, light and shadow raking across the facades. Compared with Canaletto's clarity, his Venice is looser and more atmospheric, and the perspective is less exact. The pleasure lies in the handling, which gives a lively impression of a working canal rather than a map, and it is among the more attractive of his smaller views."},"nga:270":{"d":"A grand structure of elaborate arches, columns, statues and staircases rises behind a courtyard filled with people who face away from us. The brushwork is detailed in the architecture, in earthy browns and grays with touches of gold, while the crowd wears white, black, red and blue, many in black hats and long coats. The sky is blue with faint gray and white clouds, and a tall wall on the right throws its shadow across the main building and the square below. The small oil on canvas measures about 37.5 by 31.5 cm.","i":"The title places the scene in Campo San Zanipolo, the Venetian name of the square before the church of Santi Giovanni e Paolo, and calls the building a temporary tribune: a stage-set structure put up for a public ceremony. The date, 1782 or later, falls in a year of grand festivities in Venice, including the visits of the Russian Grand Duke Paul and of Pope Pius VI, which Guardi is known to have painted. The museum does not say which event this picture shows.","c":"Guardi was about seventy by this date, and his touch shows no stiffness: the flicker of dark hats and long coats in the crowd and the quick, glancing strokes on the statues give the stage-set architecture a festive sparkle, while the shadow of the wall on the right pulls the eye into the square. As documentation it is partial and impressionistic, but as painting about the energy of a crowd it is vivid."},"met:438116":{"d":"A country house of the Loredan family stands at Paese, near Treviso on the Venetian mainland, with men, women, a dog and a horse in the scene. The oil on canvas, about 48 by 78 cm, dates from the early 1780s. Guardi's views of the mainland are relatively rare, and this is the portrait of a house and its life rather than of a city: a villa as the seat of a patrician family's leisure and estate.","i":"The Loredan were one of the great patrician families of Venice and gave the Republic several doges. A villa like this was the center of a working estate as well as a retreat, and in the eighteenth century Venetian nobles spent the autumn villeggiatura season on the mainland. The figures, with a horse and a dog, suggest both the family's leisure and the estate's labor. The picture is best seen as a record of a possession and a way of life; the commission itself is uncertain.","c":"Villa views required Guardi to be exact about a particular building, and precision was never his chief strength. The small figures, the dog and the horse give the scene life, though, and a low-key subject allows affectionate observation. As a cabinet view it is pleasing but lighter than his Venetian pictures: charming in anecdote, less affecting in atmosphere. It is more interesting as a rarity than brilliant as a painting."},"nga:43596":{"d":"A view of the Castel Sant'Angelo, the great round fortress beside the Tiber in Rome, is described by the museum as fanciful, meaning that its elements are rearranged rather than recorded. The oil on canvas measures about 47 by 76 cm and dates from about 1785, in the last decade of Guardi's life. No further description is recorded, so the bridge, figures, water and sky are not described here.","i":"The Castel Sant'Angelo began in the second century as the mausoleum of the emperor Hadrian and later became a papal fortress and refuge; the angel statue on top recalls the legend that Pope Gregory the Great saw an angel sheathing its sword at the end of a plague. Guardi, a Venetian, painted capricci, imaginary or rearranged views mixing real monuments with invented surroundings, and he is generally thought to have drawn his Roman subjects from prints rather than from the site.","c":"A late Guardi in his freest vein typically reduces monuments to flickering light and lets the architecture loosen from strict perspective, so that the whole is more atmosphere than record. Those who want accuracy will be disappointed; those who enjoy touch will not. The picture belongs with his Venetian capricci as a sign that by the 1780s he preferred imaginative variation to topographic exactness, far from the clarity of Canaletto."},"cma:126770":{"d":"Two small canvases, each about 51 by 69 cm, record moments from Pope Pius VI's visit to Venice on 15 to 18 May 1782, the only papal visit of the century. One shows the pontifical mass with its Te Deum; the other shows the doge and senators processing toward the throne as the pope blesses the assembled leaders of government. They are smaller copies of a popular subject, painted by Guardi either for the open market or for other patrons, after four large scenes commissioned by the Englishman Peter Edwards.","i":"The pope's stay was treated as a historic occasion, and views of its ceremonies suited buyers who wanted a souvenir of it. The Te Deum, a hymn of thanksgiving traditionally sung for a papal election or a royal coronation, matched the moment, while the doge and senators receiving a blessing show the Republic honoring a guest it had once defied. Paired, the pictures present the two sides of the visit: church ritual and state ceremony.","c":"The pair shows Guardi as chronicler of a city that loved its own pageantry. These smaller replicas distill his method: the crowd as a pattern of quick colored accents, the architecture as a stage. Replicas inevitably simplify, and the faces are types. But the pairing of church and state is neat, and the liveliness of touch holds up well even in repetition."},"cma:126771":{"d":"At the back of the magnificent audience hall of Santi Giovanni e Paolo, the doge, in gold and white, approaches the papal throne to receive a blessing, followed by a line of red-robed senators. It is the third scene in the series and shows one of the final events of the pope's stay in May 1782. The canvas, about 51 by 69 cm, is a smaller version of a composition from the large scenes commissioned from Guardi.","i":"Red robes marked the senators; the doge wore gold and white, and Paolo Renier held the office in 1782. The leave-taking closed the visit with an exchange of courtesies between two sovereigns. The Republic, which had once defied the papacy, here staged itself as the most gracious of hosts, and every gesture of precedence in the procession carried meaning for those who watched it. The picture preserves a ceremony from the last years of the Republic, which fell to Napoleon in 1797.","c":"The scene lives in its procession: a line of red-robed senators gives the composition a rhythmic sweep across the hall, and the doge in gold and white forms the bright point toward which everything moves. Guardi's habitual shorthand makes many figures read at once without portrait-like detail. It is pageantry as record, brisk and effective, though the faces are types rather than individuals."},"cma:126772":{"d":"Pope Pius VI celebrates mass in the church of Santi Giovanni e Paolo, one of the largest churches in Venice, where the pope was lodged during his visit. The mass included a Te Deum, the prayer of thanksgiving. This is the second scene in a series of the visit. The canvas, about 51 by 69 cm and dated about 1783, is a smaller version of a composition from the large scenes the Englishman Peter Edwards commissioned.","i":"Pius VI came to Venice in May 1782, on his return from Vienna, where he had tried to persuade Emperor Joseph II to halt his reforms of the Church. The Republic, which had defied the papacy in the Interdict of 1606–07, received him with great pomp; it was the only papal visit of the century. The Te Deum is traditionally sung in thanks for a special blessing, such as a papal election or a coronation, which gave the visit the weight of a historic occasion.","c":"Guardi's ceremonial scenes rely on the gift that animates his views: with a few flecks of color he turns a church into a crowd and a rite into a spectacle. The interest is documentary and atmospheric, not psychological, since nobody here is individualized. As one of two smaller versions, it shows how a successful design could be re-used for a wider market, and the speed of the hand is part of the pleasure."},"met:436817":{"d":"A mountainous landscape with a waterfall, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 69 by 122 cm, around 1600. The museum records only the waterfall and the mountains, so the trees, figures and sky are not described here. Pictures of this kind by Flemish painters of the period usually set steep, rocky heights and a cascading stream in a wide, panoramic format, with tiny figures for scale and a recession from brownish foreground through green middle ground to blue distance.","i":"Flemish landscape painters had been building such compositions since Joachim Patinir in the early 1500s: imagined views of crags, falls and far horizons, put together from studies of the Alps and Rhineland and from invention. Landscapes of this kind were bought by merchants and princely collectors for private rooms, where they offered a kind of imaginary travel and a reminder of the grandeur of creation. They were composed in the studio, not painted outdoors.","c":"A landscape like this is judged by its sense of space and the invention of its rocks and falls. De Keuninck is usually classed with the late Mannerist wing of Flemish landscape painting, in which fantastic crags and a dramatic cascade stand for the grandeur of nature, and the wide panel gives the panorama room. His work can feel brittle and decorative beside the more observational landscapes that came later, but it has a vivid imagination."},"met:439553":{"d":"The dead Christ is mourned by the Virgin Mary and Mary Magdalene, with an angel or angels sharing the grief, on a small copper plate about 53 by 38 centimetres. Copper gave painters a smooth, hard surface that held very fine detail and strong colour, and pictures on it, usually small, were prized by collectors as cabinet pieces. Domenichino painted this in his early twenties, soon after arriving in Rome from Bologna, in the clear, ordered idiom he had learned from the Carracci.","i":"The Lamentation, the grief of the Virgin, Mary Magdalene and others over Christ's body after it is taken down from the cross, is one of the most emotionally direct Passion subjects. Domenichino was admired by contemporaries for expressing feeling through face and gesture, and this early work belongs to that interest. The angels' presence lifts the scene from historical record to vision, inviting the viewer to join the mourners in contemplation.","c":"An early work of unusual feeling for its size. Domenichino concentrates the grief into a compact group, and the copper gives the figures a clean, enamel-like clarity that favours his careful drawing. The influence of Annibale Carracci is plain, and the picture is more a promise than a statement of his later monumental style. Some may find the emotion managed rather than spontaneous, but its restraint is also its dignity."},"met:436205":{"d":"A vertical landscape on copper, about 45 by 34 centimetres, sets the biblical story of Moses and the burning bush in a pastoral setting; sheep and fire are among the subjects recorded. Domenichino's small landscapes of these years are typically built in clear planes, with trees framing the sides, a middle ground opening to a pale distance, and figures kept small so that the setting carries the mood.","i":"In the Book of Exodus Moses, tending the flock of his father-in-law Jethro, sees a bush that burns without being consumed, and God speaks from it, commanding him to lead the Israelites out of Egypt. The story suited a pastoral landscape perfectly: a shepherd, his sheep and a wonder in a quiet corner of nature. Domenichino's small landscapes, descended from Annibale Carracci's ideal landscape, helped shape the classical landscapes of Poussin and Claude.","c":"Domenichino's landscapes lack the grandeur of Claude's but have an unforced calm: the miracle seems at home in nature, and the copper gives a clear finish that suits small pieces. Its weakness is a certain tidiness, since the composition follows the formula of framing trees and receding middle ground without surprise. A graceful example of Bolognese classical landscape in miniature."},"nga:111718":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 122 by 172 centimetres, shows the moment after the Fall when God confronts Adam and Eve in the Garden of Eden. In pictures of this subject the first couple cover themselves and shrink from the divine voice, while the lush garden contrasts with their shame. The museum record gives no detail beyond title, date and size, so the arrangement of figures, animals and foliage cannot be described here with confidence.","i":"The story, from Genesis 3, follows the eating of the forbidden fruit: God calls in the garden, Adam blames Eve, Eve blames the serpent, and the couple are told of the toil, pain and death that await them before their expulsion. Seventeenth-century painters used the scene to show the origin of human sin and the justice of God, and Catholic viewers also read it as the necessary prelude to redemption through Christ. Domenichino painted it in 1626.","c":"Domenichino was admired by contemporaries for clarity of gesture and feeling, and this subject suits his talents: guilt, fear and authority can be conveyed by posture alone. The risk in his work is a certain stiffness in the poses and a landscape more orderly than awe-inspiring. Without more detail it is fair to say that the picture belongs to the Bolognese classical tradition, favouring lucidity over the movement of the Baroque."},"met:438618":{"d":"A wood's edge, painted in oil on canvas, about 26 by 36 cm. The museum gives no description beyond the title and the tags of forest and landscape, so the arrangement of trees, the light and any figures are not described here. At this small size and with this subject the picture is probably a study of the kind French landscapists made in the forests near Paris, and d'Aligny's manner was precise, with firm outlines for trunks and boughs.","i":"French landscape painters of the 1820s to 1860s turned to the forests near Paris, above all Fontainebleau, as subjects that were close at hand, varied and free of the conventions of mythological landscape. D'Aligny, who met Corot in Italy in the 1820s, was among the first to work in the forest, and he later taught a generation of landscape painters. A small woodland study like this belongs to that movement toward direct observation of nature.","c":"D'Aligny's landscapes are marked by a clear, almost drawn precision: trunks, boughs and rock edges are firmly outlined, the light is even and the space orderly. That gives his work an austere, classical dignity beside the softer, more atmospheric painting of Corot or the Barbizon painters, and it can seem a little dry. In a small study the discipline is an advantage, giving the wood a calm structure."},"met:435857":{"d":"A landscape with a cave, painted in oil on a canvas of about 62 by 46 cm, upright in format. The museum gives no date and no further description, so the cave's setting, the light and any figures are not described here. As a title it points to the rocky, grotto-and-forest landscapes of the classical tradition, in which d'Aligny was trained and to which he kept throughout his career.","i":"Caves had a long life as a landscape motif: in classical and Renaissance art they were places of retreat, shelter, hermit saints and rural gods, and in later centuries they gave painters dark, rocky forms to set against a bright distance. In d'Aligny's day French landscapists went to Italy to study the grottoes and cliffs of the Roman countryside and returned with sketches that were worked up into pictures like this one.","c":"A cave offers a painter a strong contrast of dark and light, and the upright format would emphasize it. With so little recorded, the picture can only be judged in general: it is likely to show d'Aligny's firm drawing and ordered space, and to lack the freedom of his sketches from nature. That is the ordinary trade-off of the studio landscape, structure gained and spontaneity lost."},"nga:43595":{"d":"A very small upright picture, about 28 by 23 centimetres, painted in oil on paper and mounted on canvas. The title gives the subject: shepherd boys and their dog taking shelter as a storm breaks. Oil on paper was a quick, flexible support favoured for studies and sketches, and the size and support suggest an informal, direct piece of work rather than a finished showpiece. Rustic children in rough weather were a stock subject of late eighteenth-century English painting.","i":"Pictures of poor country children, often caught in rough weather, were popular in Britain in the last decades of the eighteenth century, in the wake of Thomas Gainsborough's cottage-door scenes and fancy pictures. They offered town buyers a sentimental, picturesque view of rural life in which hardship is met with patience. The storm adds a touch of the sublime. Thomas Barker, who worked in Bath, where Gainsborough had lived in the 1760s and early 1770s, is likely to have known this tradition well.","c":"A slight but lively early piece. The small scale and paper support allow quick, fluent painting, and the sheltering group promises the sentiment that buyers wanted. It owes a clear debt to Gainsborough and is probably more an exercise in a fashionable manner than a personal statement. Its value is partly historical: it shows a young painter, then about twenty, taking up a popular rustic subject with evident ease."},"met:437513":{"d":"A still life of food centered on a ham, painted in oil on a canvas of about 73 by 92 cm in the 1870s. The museum records only 'food' and 'still life', so the other items, the table, the cloth and the light are not described here. Pictures of this kind by Philippe Rousseau typically set a table or pantry of ordinary good food before the viewer, with an eye for the textures of meat, bread, crockery and glass.","i":"French still lifes of food had a revival in the nineteenth century, when painters looked back to Chardin's modest kitchen scenes and to the Dutch and Flemish banquet pieces. Rousseau was among the best known of them, and middle-class buyers liked his plentiful, comfortable tables. A ham was an everyday luxury, a sign of a well-stocked household, and the picture appealed to a taste for the pleasures of the table.","c":"Rousseau was admired in his day and has since been overshadowed by the Impressionists and Cézanne, who were reinventing still life in the same decades. At his best he handles textures with relish and builds a convincing, solid picture of a table; the risk is a certain heaviness and a dutiful repetition of Chardin's lessons. Beside Manet's or Cézanne's still lifes a picture like this can look careful rather than daring, but its craft is secure."},"nga:102627":{"d":"Seen from slightly below, a white and tan building stands on the edge of a rocky cliff, with arched openings cut into the rock and small buildings tucked among them. Leafy greenery tops the cliff and fills a cave at left, and a patch of green grass lies in the bottom right corner. In the center, tiny figures and a horse-drawn cart move along a path below the cliff toward one of the openings. The sky is blue with scattered white clouds. The oil on canvas measures about 41 by 54 cm.","i":"Pizzofalcone is a rocky headland in Naples near the Castel dell'Ovo, the site of the city's earliest Greek settlement, which was built up with villas and terraces on its steep cliffs. The soft volcanic tuff was easy to quarry and cut into rooms and arches, which is why buildings here seem to grow out of the rock. Turpin de Crissé, a former French army officer who spent years in Italy, painted such views for visitors drawn by the scenery of Naples.","c":"A clear, orderly view in which the pale villa, the rock and the sky are set out with a French-trained landscapist's care. The arches in the cliff and the tiny cart give scale and a sense of place, and the blue sky keeps the picture fresh. It lacks the drama of the great painters of the Neapolitan coast and can feel like a careful record rather than an evocation, but it pleases as a view of Naples."},"met:437265":{"d":"A girl of thirteen, Maria Luisa of Parma, is shown full length in a large canvas about 231 by 165 cm, dated 1765. The museum's record lists lace, a panniered gown, a chair, a casket and a brooch among the details; it does not describe her pose, expression, colors or the room. It is a state portrait of a princess, the kind of image sent between courts when marriages were being arranged, though the record does not say whether that was its purpose.","i":"Maria Luisa was the daughter of the Duke of Parma and, through her mother, a granddaughter of Louis XV. In 1765, the year of this portrait, she married Charles, heir to the Spanish throne, and she became queen consort of Spain in 1788; she is remembered from Goya's portraits of her family. The pannier, lace and jewel casket are the standard language of royal portraits. The painter, Laurent Pécheux, was a Frenchman who worked in Rome and for the courts of Parma and Turin.","c":"A large state portrait by a competent painter of the Roman Neoclassical circle. Its strongest part is probably the display: lace, pannier and jewel casket let Pécheux show his handling of fabric, and the full-length format makes the child a figure of ceremony. The weakness of such pictures is the weight of dress and setting, which can overpower a girl. It matters more as a document of a future queen than as a work that moves."},"met:436418":{"d":"The record lists a man, a tent, a camel, a desert and an Orientalist subject for this wide canvas, about 75 by 111 centimeters, dated by 1880. Its title names the standard viewpoint for pictures of the holy city: from the Mount of Olives, looking across the Kidron Valley to the walled city. The museum gives no further description, so the arrangement of the foreground encampment and the city beyond is not detailed here.","i":"Jerusalem seen from the Mount of Olives, with the Temple Mount and its golden Dome of the Rock at the center, was the traditional prospect for pilgrims, and in the nineteenth century it became a staple of Orientalist painting, offering both biblical association and exotic costume. Théodore Frère spent long periods in Egypt and the Near East and painted many such views. The tent and camel in the record suggest the travelers' camp that was part of such journeys.","c":"Frère was a reliable Orientalist who sold well to European and American buyers, and the picture's chief virtue is likely a clear, orderly view with good local color. The weakness of his style is a certain smoothness, in which the sacred city becomes a pleasant backdrop. As documents of what pilgrims saw, the best of his views are valuable; as paintings, they are polite rather than powerful."},"met:437591":{"d":"A genre scene of men at work in a rustic interior, in oil on canvas, about 92 by 116 centimeters, dated 1661, by the Flemish painter David Ryckaert III. Men, a dog and barrels fill the room, so it is evidently a farmhouse or cellar where barrels are handled. The Ryckaerts were a family of Antwerp painters, and David III specialized in such rustic interiors.","i":"Peasant interiors with workers and animals were a staple of Flemish painting in the mid-seventeenth century, made for urban collectors who enjoyed seeing the countryside as humble, cheerful and a little comic. Ryckaert, born in Antwerp, followed David Teniers in the genre; the cluttered barrels, tools and animals offered a catalog of still-life details. The picture should not be taken as documentary: such scenes were arranged and idealized for the market.","c":"Ryckaert is a solid second-rank peasant painter whose pictures give pleasure through well-observed clutter and warm brown tones and, in a late work like this, through the confident arrangement of figures and objects. He lacks Teniers's silvery delicacy and Brouwer's raw wit, and the picture offers more neatness than invention. A cozy interior with barrels and a dog is the type he did best."},"met:437592":{"d":"A genre scene in the yard of an inn, in oil on canvas, about 91 by 115 centimeters, dated 1661, by David Ryckaert III. The title ties it to the Gospel episode of the appearance of Christ at Emmaus, and men, a wild boar, pigs and rabbits fill the yard, which is stocked with game and farm animals. The sacred scene is presumably a small episode somewhere in the background, as in the Flemish tradition.","i":"In Luke's Gospel, two disciples walking to Emmaus on Easter Sunday were joined by the risen Christ, whom they did not recognize until he broke bread with them at the inn. Flemish painters since the sixteenth century liked to place such scenes within a busy market or kitchen, in which the sacred event shrinks to a background episode and the foreground belongs to food and animals. Ryckaert evidently follows that tradition.","c":"Ryckaert's mixture of the humble and the holy is a Flemish specialty, and here the inn yard, with its dead boar and rabbits, is painted with relish while the Gospel story is the quiet surprise. The type has a moral point, that the sacred appears in ordinary life, but the heap of provisions and animals is what holds the eye. A pleasant, witty, competent example of a long Flemish tradition, if without Aertsen's grandeur."},"nga:177670":{"d":"An angel crowns a seated woman in a dark landscape. The angel has white wings tinged with pink and green, pale skin and short golden hair, and wears a flowing white robe; one hand reaches upward while the other sets a golden crown on the woman's head. She sits lower left, eyes closed, one hand on her chest, in a loose white garment tied at the waist with a rope, a coral and gold blanket over her legs. Behind them rocky ground lies under dramatic purple clouds, with a hint of pink light at right. Both figures are brightly lit. The canvas is large, about 228 by 168 cm.","i":"The title and the crowning angel point to John Bunyan's The Pilgrim's Progress. In its second part the young pilgrim Mercy tells of a dream in which a heavenly visitor comforts her and adorns her with a crown, and the painter shows an angel. Bunyan's allegory was among the most widely read books in nineteenth-century America, and a picture of a humble pilgrim rewarded in a dream carried a message of faith and consolation, so it works as a story picture and a devotional image at once.","c":"A work that tells its story in a single gesture: the radiant angel descending to crown the sleeper against a stormy dusk. Huntington, a successful New York portraitist and religious painter, makes the figures smooth and the light theatrical. A modern eye may find the sentiment sugary and the handling slick, but the composition is clear, the contrast of luminous white against dark purple is effective, and the picture was widely admired in its day."},"cma:96335":{"d":"A portrait of Dr. John W. Francis, a prominent New York physician, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 66 centimeters, painted in 1857 by Daniel Huntington. At this size and date it is a bust or half-length cabinet portrait of the kind that Huntington, then one of New York's most sought-after portraitists, produced for professional men, writers and merchants.","i":"John Wakefield Francis (1789 to 1861) was one of New York's best-known physicians and a convivial figure at the center of its literary and artistic life, and a portrait of him by the city's leading portraitist fits that standing. Portraits of this kind recorded a respected man's likeness for his family, his profession and his friends. The picture dates from late in the sitter's life, four years before his death.","c":"Huntington is a polished and sympathetic portraitist rather than a searching one, skilled at giving his sitters dignity and a pleasant humanity. A portrait of a distinguished physician late in life invites the kind of honest, kindly characterization he did well. Sitter and painter make a natural pair, and the picture is a representative example of dignified mid-century American portraiture."},"nga:34104":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, slightly to the side and looking away from the viewer. He has a prominent jawline, large sideburns and short, curly brown hair. He wears a dark suit jacket over a white shirt with a high collar, possibly with a dark waistcoat and bow tie. He holds nothing, and the background is a textured mix of brown and earthy tones. The oil on canvas measures about 76.5 by 63 cm and is dated 1857.","i":"The name matches that of the New York geologist and paleontologist James Hall, a leading figure in American science of the 1850s, but the museum record here does not say whether he is the sitter. A portrait with no books, instruments or specimens presents the man alone, which suited a mid-century American ideal of the thinker as a serious, self-contained figure. The averted gaze signals reflection rather than address.","c":"A neat, restrained portrait: the dark clothes, the sideways glance and the earthy ground are arranged to concentrate attention on the head, and the prominent jaw and curled hair give it a firm, individual outline. Huntington's polish can make his sitters look alike, but here the strong head holds attention. It is not an ambitious picture, but it is a competent likeness in the quiet, serious manner of the 1850s."},"nga:34105":{"d":"An older man is shown from the chest up, seated and turned to the left in profile. He has a strong, prominent nose, a firm chin and closed lips with a subtle expression; his gray hair is short and brushed back. He wears a formal dark suit with a white shirt and black bow tie, and holds nothing. The background is a plain warm brown. The oil on canvas measures about 72 by 59 cm and is dated 1857.","i":"John Edwards Holbrook was a Charleston physician and naturalist, a professor of anatomy at the Medical College of South Carolina and the author of North American Herpetology, the first thorough American study of reptiles and amphibians, which earned him the title father of American herpetology. A portrait without books, specimens or instruments shows the mid-century ideal of the learned man: sober, serious and defined by his face.","c":"A profile is a bold choice for a mid-century American sitter, and it works best with a face like this, whose strong nose and firm chin make a clean outline. The plain brown ground and dark suit leave nothing to distract. Huntington was known as a smooth, genteel portraitist, and the picture has his usual polish, but the severity of the profile gives it more character than many of his society portraits."},"met:11192":{"d":"A large devotional picture, about 213 by 168 centimeters, in oil on canvas, dated 1858, by Daniel Huntington. The figures are a woman and an angel, and the title names Mercy, a character in John Bunyan's Pilgrim's Progress, so the angel appears to her in a dream. It is thought to repeat a composition that Huntington first painted in the early 1840s.","i":"Mercy is the companion of Christiana in the second part of Bunyan's Pilgrim's Progress, and her dream, in which she sees a vision of heaven, belonged to the best-loved Protestant allegory in the English-speaking world. Huntington's picture, with its sweet angelic figure and dreamy heroine, spoke to the sentimental religious taste of mid-nineteenth-century America, and it became popular in reproduction. It presented piety as gentle, feminine and consoling.","c":"A work of its time: its idealized women, soft light and sentimental piety appealed hugely to American audiences of the 1840s and 1850s, and they now seem sugary. Huntington's types recall Raphael, and the large size confers a seriousness the sentiment cannot sustain. As a document of Victorian religious taste it is revealing; as painting it is competent and polished but not deeply felt."},"met:11193":{"d":"A large portrait of Mrs. Birdsall Cornell, probably three-quarter length, in oil on canvas, about 142 by 112 centimeters, painted in 1860 by Daniel Huntington. Huntington's portraits of society women usually show them in rich gowns, in softly shaded drawing-room settings. The title gives her by her husband's name, in the usual way for a married woman of the period.","i":"In 1860 a portrait of this size by Huntington was a mark of wealth and standing in New York society, ordered for a drawing room or to pass to descendants. The sitter is known by her husband's name, Mrs. Birdsall Cornell, and nothing more is known of her. Huntington's portraits of women usually emphasize refinement, costly fabrics and a calm, benevolent expression, the qualities expected of a lady of the period.","c":"Huntington's portraits of women of the 1860s are polished, graceful and a little formulaic, and their appeal lies in the gentility of manner and the handling of silk and lace rather than in psychological depth. It probably follows the type: a dignified, well-mannered sitter, softly lit, with a decorous background. A representative picture of mid-century New York portraiture."},"met:11199":{"d":"A small study, about 30 by 25 centimeters, in oil on canvas, dated 1861, showing a woman reading among trees, as the title indicates. The scale and title suggest an informal study rather than an exhibition picture. The artist, Daniel Huntington, is better known for formal portraits and for large religious and literary subjects, which makes a small picture like this a change of pace.","i":"A woman reading outdoors was a popular subject in the mid-nineteenth century, an image of quiet leisure and cultivated feeling that suited the sentimental taste of the time. The woods offered painters dappled light as well as a private, contemplative setting. The sitter is not identified. Huntington, known for formal portraits, shows in such studies a more relaxed, personal side.","c":"A modest, sketch-sized picture, and as such it is likely to be fresher than Huntington's formal portraits; the subject, a book and dappled shade, calls for lightness of touch rather than the sheen he lavished on society sitters. Its interest is as a glimpse of an artist more relaxed than his public image."},"met:11187":{"d":"A portrait of Anna Watson Stuart, in oil on canvas, about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1862 by Daniel Huntington. The upright format leaves room for a little of her surroundings as well as the figure. At this size it is a three-quarter-length or seated portrait of the kind Huntington produced for his well-to-do New York clients, the sort of picture that hung in a family parlor or passed down to descendants.","i":"A named sitter with a middle name gives the genealogical tone of a New York family portrait, one of the many Huntington made in the 1860s for families who wished to record their members in a dignified and tasteful way. The title gives her name but not her story. The date, about 1862, falls in the first years of the Civil War, when Huntington became president of the National Academy of Design.","c":"A dignified portrait by a leading portraitist of New York society, and for that reason a document of taste as much as of personality. Huntington's women are elegant, serene and slightly idealized, which flatters the sitter but can limit the character. The size suggests an ambitious commission."},"nga:34055":{"d":"A man is shown from the shoulders up, in side profile facing to his right. He has a full beard and mustache, brown, slightly curly hair, a broad forehead and deep-set eyes. He wears a dark jacket, white shirt and black bow tie, against a background that blends brown and gray. The oil on canvas measures about 68 by 56 cm and is dated 1866. No objects or setting are recorded, so none are described here.","i":"Henry Theodore Tuckerman (1813–1871) was an American essayist, poet and critic, and the author of Book of the Artists (1867), a major survey of American art and artists. He moved in the same New York circles as many of the painters he wrote about, and a portrait by Huntington, himself a leading figure of the city's art world, records a link between critic and artist. The profile, plain dress and bare ground present him as a man of letters, defined by his head.","c":"Huntington keeps his best attention for the head, and the profile, with its high forehead, deep-set eye and full beard, makes a strong, spare design against the brown and gray ground. The painting is understated and sympathetic. Its limitations are a certain flatness of handling and a narrow palette, but it has more bite than the polished society portraits that made Huntington prosperous."},"met:11189":{"d":"A portrait of John David Wolfe, a New York merchant and philanthropist, in oil on canvas, about 110 by 95 centimeters, painted in 1871 by Daniel Huntington. It was painted in the year before Wolfe's death, so it records a man near the end of a long career in business and charity. Huntington, a leading New York portraitist, painted many of the city's merchants and civic leaders.","i":"Wolfe was a wealthy New York merchant associated with the early Metropolitan Museum of Art, and his daughter Catharine Lorillard Wolfe became one of the museum's greatest benefactors, so a portrait of him by a leading New York portraitist has a place in the museum's own history. Huntington's portraits of such public-spirited citizens recorded the businessmen and trustees who built New York's cultural institutions.","c":"A portrait that reads as a tribute: the elderly benefactor is shown with the reserve and gravity expected of a public man, and Huntington supplies the polish that his sitters wanted. It is more a record of civic virtue than a penetrating character study, which suits its purpose. Its historical interest for the museum is considerable."},"met:11195":{"d":"A portrait of a boy, Nicoll Havens Dering, with a toy, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1871 by Daniel Huntington. The title uses the child's full name, as formal portraits of children of well-to-do families did. A toy accompanies the boy, a prop that painters of the period used to mark childhood and play. At this modest size it would suit a family sitting room better than a gallery wall.","i":"Portraits of children from prosperous New York families were a staple of Huntington's practice in the 1870s, painted to record growing children for their parents and to mark childhood as a time of play and innocence. A boy's portrait often recorded a family's heir. The shared surname with the portrait of Mrs. Sylvester Dering of 1878 suggests a family link, though nothing confirms it.","c":"Children's portraits suit Huntington's gentle, idealizing touch, and a boy with a toy gives him the chance to show tenderness without stiffness. The type, an affectionate image of a child in a pleasing pose, was a strength of Victorian portraiture. Its limits are the sentimentality that period taste favored."},"met:19206":{"d":"A portrait of John Taylor Johnston, in oil on canvas, about 111 by 85 centimeters, painted in 1875 by Daniel Huntington. Huntington painted several of the museum's early trustees and patrons, and this is one of the group. Johnston was the first president of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, so the portrait of him, painted five years after the museum's founding, belongs to the institution he led.","i":"John Taylor Johnston (1820 to 1893), a railroad president and art collector, was elected first president of the Metropolitan Museum in 1870 and held the post for nearly two decades. His own collection of American and European art was among the best in New York, and he opened it to the public in his house. A portrait of the founding president by a leading New York portraitist was a fitting tribute.","c":"A civic portrait that carries a measure of ease: the painter's practiced polish lends the businessman a gentle authority, and the likeness is competent without being searching. Its interest is largely historical. The sitter's role in making New York a city of art gives the picture a significance beyond its merits as painting, and it rewards viewers who know the museum's own story."},"met:11194":{"d":"A portrait of Mrs. Sylvester Dering, who was Ella Virginia Bristol Dering, in oil on canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1878 by Daniel Huntington. Huntington was by then a veteran portraitist of New York society. At this size it is a bust- or half-length portrait of the kind made for a family's own rooms rather than for public display.","i":"The title gives her by her husband's name, as was usual, while her own name was Ella Virginia Bristol. Huntington painted at least two members of the Dering family, and a series of portraits of one family over a decade reflects the long relationship between a fashionable portraitist and the households he served. The sitter's life and the circumstances of the commission are unknown.","c":"In late Huntington portraits of women the formula is settled: a soft light, a gracious manner and a pleasing arrangement of dress. The qualities are those of a seasoned professional more than of an innovator, so this one's distinction is hard to assess. As part of the group of Dering portraits it offers a chance to see the painter's long service to one family."},"met:11188":{"d":"A portrait of Cyrus W. Field, the businessman who organized the laying of the transatlantic telegraph cable, in oil on canvas, about 75 by 62 centimeters, painted in 1879 by Daniel Huntington. Field was about sixty when it was painted, long after the cable made him famous. At this size it is a half-length portrait of the sort that honored a public figure for family and friends.","i":"Cyrus Field (1819 to 1892) promoted and financed the first transatlantic cable, completed in 1858 and, after its failure, laid again successfully in 1866, an achievement that made him an international celebrity. A portrait of 1879 shows him as a statesman of commerce late in his career. The picture does not need to depict cable or ships to carry the idea; the name does that work.","c":"A competent commemorative portrait of a famous man, in which Huntington's smooth handling presents Field as a figure of settled dignity. The picture offers less drama than the sitter's career, and the energy of that career is not obviously its subject. Its value is documentary: an image of an American hero of the Atlantic telegraph, painted by a portraitist who knew how to flatter a public man."},"met:11201":{"d":"A portrait of William Cowper Prime, in oil on canvas, about 138 by 112 centimeters, painted in 1892 by Daniel Huntington. Both sitter and painter belonged to the same New York intellectual world. At this size it is a large, formal portrait of a respected public figure, painted when the sitter was in his late sixties and the painter in his mid-seventies.","i":"William Cowper Prime (1825 to 1905) was a lawyer, journalist, author and collector who taught art history at Princeton and supported the founding of the Metropolitan Museum. A large portrait by Huntington in 1892 fits his standing in the New York intellectual world, to which the painter also belonged. The picture belongs to the tradition of honoring the founders of the city's cultural institutions.","c":"A late Huntington, painted in his mid-seventies, which shows the polish of long practice but also, perhaps, the conservatism of an artist whose style was formed before the Civil War. By 1892 a portrait of this smooth, courteous kind was out of step with the bolder portraiture of Sargent and Eakins, and the picture belongs to an earlier idea of public dignity. Its interest is historical, as a record of a founding generation."},"met:436900":{"d":"The Virgin Mary holds the Christ Child against a gold ground in a tall, narrow panel of tempera on wood, about 171 by 86 cm. Among the details the museum lists a dove. It gives no further description of the poses, the dress or the colors, so only the type is given: an early fourteenth-century Florentine Madonna, of a size suited to a church altar or chapel, painted in egg tempera with burnished gold.","i":"The Madonna and Child is the central image of Christian devotion: Mary as mother and throne of the incarnate God, the Child as savior. The gold ground, which stands for heaven rather than a place, and the formal pose descend from Byzantine icons, which Florentine painters of Lippo's generation were beginning to soften with more natural forms. A dove near the Child could be read as the Holy Spirit, as innocence, or as the offering of doves at the Presentation in the Temple.","c":"Lippo di Benivieni, active in Florence in the early 1300s, is a minor figure beside Giotto and Cimabue, and his Madonna is best valued as a representative example of its time: formal, linear, calm and finely gilded, with only the beginnings of the solid, believable figures that Giotto made normal. Its appeal is the dignity of the pose and the glow of the gold, and it is a useful witness to the slow shift away from the Byzantine manner."},"nga:103247":{"d":"A white cottage stands among lush green foliage under a light blue sky. Its windows are dark, a red chimney shows above, and the front door appears to be open. Trees cast dappled shadows across the facade, partly hiding it. A light brown path runs along the bottom of the picture, and small gray steps lead up through the grass toward the house. The brushwork is loose and impressionistic, softly textured in the trees, grass and walls. The canvas is about 64 by 76 centimeters, painted in 1889.","i":"Dennis Miller Bunker was among the first American painters to take up Impressionist ideas, doing so in 1889 and 1890, the last years of a short life. A plain roadside house, with no figures to tell a story, was an ideal subject for the new interest in light, color and the effect of weather on everyday scenes. Bunker died at twenty-nine, so landscapes like this are the few clear signs of how he might have developed.","c":"The picture shows a painter loosening his touch without losing his sense of structure: the cottage is solidly built beneath the dappling of leaf shadows, and the greens are varied rather than monotonous. It is quieter and more tentative than the Monet landscapes that inspired American painters of his generation, and the composition is simple to the point of plainness. Its charm lies in that modesty and in the promise of an artist who died too soon."},"met:10259":{"d":"A near-square oil canvas, about 61 by 53 centimetres, presents a portrait of a woman identified by the title as Eleanor Hardy Bunker, the painter's wife. The museum record gives only the sitter and the date, 1890, so little can be said with confidence about her pose or dress. In 1890 Bunker was painting in a manner shaped by his friend John Singer Sargent and by Impressionist ideas of light and colour, which suggests a portrait that favours air and atmosphere over polished finish.","i":"Bunker married Eleanor Hardy in 1890 and died later the same year, aged twenty-nine, so portraits of her belong to the last months of a short career. By then he had absorbed Sargent's example and a lighter, more Impressionist palette, which he applied to landscapes and to portraits of friends. The Metropolitan Museum received the picture in 1893 as a gift from a group of donors.","c":"Bunker's finest portraits pair a Sargent-like fluency of brush with a reserve and delicacy of his own, and his work of 1890 shows his palette lightening towards Impressionism. A likeness of his wife made in the year of his death carries obvious personal weight, but fairness asks that it be judged as painting, not sentiment. His career was so short that his work resists easy ranking: he is remembered as a painter of great promise more than settled achievement."},"met:10421":{"d":"A man and a woman with dice and gambling are the recorded motifs of this wide canvas, about 47 by 97 centimeters, painted around 1840. The title, The Last Throw, suggests the final desperate cast of the dice in a gambler's evening, with a woman present as witness or victim. The museum offers no further description of the setting, the costumes or the expressions.","i":"Charles Robert Leslie, born in London to American parents and raised in Philadelphia, settled in London and specialized in small narrative scenes, often taken from Shakespeare, Cervantes, Molière and Sterne, and in domestic comedy. The last throw is a gambling cliché with a moral: the gambler stakes all, and the woman's presence makes the moral personal.","c":"A gambling scene is easily melodramatic, and Leslie, whose talent was for gentle comedy, probably handles it with restraint, humor and a good-natured eye for expression. A friend and biographer of Constable, he was admired for small, polished narratives. The risk is the one the type always runs: the story is neat and the moral a platitude. Agreeable, not profound."},"met:13401":{"d":"A portrait of the New York physician John Wakefield Francis, painted by Charles Robert Leslie in oil on a wooden panel measuring about 39 by 29 centimeters. The record lists a man and a portrait, with the sitter's name, but no description of his pose, dress or expression, and no date is recorded. It was given to the museum in 1896 by John L. Cadwalader.","i":"John Wakefield Francis (1789–1861) was one of New York's most prominent doctors and a professor of obstetrics, but he was better known as a convivial host whose house was a meeting place for the writers, artists and scientists of the city, among them Washington Irving, James Fenimore Cooper and Samuel Morse. Leslie, who moved in the same literary and artistic circles on both sides of the Atlantic, made this small portrait.","c":"A small portrait on panel by a painter more at home with narrative and comedy than with straight likeness. The interest is the sitter, a man famed for his affability, and the portrait would be best if it caught some of that warmth. In general, Leslie's few portraits are careful and pleasant rather than searching."},"nga:46191":{"d":"Three people in elaborate costumes stand in a gray, columned interior. At the center a woman sits on a green couch with golden ornament, her white hair styled upward under a dark brown cap covered with pearls, gems and white, pink and brown feathers; her pink and silver gown is trimmed with cream lace, gems and pearls, and in her white-gloved hand she holds a black mask. At either side stand two men with curled gray hair, one in gold, brown and green, the other in blue, red and gold with a fur-collared coat and a blue feathered hat. The oil is about 166 by 127 cm.","i":"The black mask in the woman's hand is the sign of the masquerade, an entertainment at which eighteenth-century aristocrats and the wealthy dressed in deliberately fanciful costumes, historical, theatrical or exotic. Such pictures commemorated a ball or a fashionable group of friends; the sitters may be real people, though the museum record does not name them. The feathers, pearls and fur show how wealth was displayed in the carnival season.","c":"The costume is the point: feathered hats, pearls and the pink and silver gown are painted with real pleasure, and the three figures make a stately triangle in the gray hall. The faces matter less than the display, so the people read as types rather than individuals, and the setting is a flat architectural backdrop. Le Lorrain, who stands between Rococo and Neoclassicism, puts his invention into the fancy of dress rather than psychology."},"nga:57457":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, her forearms resting on a brown surface in front of her. She has fair skin, rosy cheeks, light blue eyes and blonde hair in loose curls. She wears a yellow wide-brimmed hat with a bow tied under her chin, a yellow scarf and a pale-yellow lacy dress with long sleeves and a wide neckline. The background is a blend of yellow and black. The oil on canvas measures about 77 by 64 cm.","i":"The wide-brimmed hat, soft scarf and informal lace dress belong to the fashion of the early 1780s for a natural, unstudied look, in which fashionable women were painted in dress that seemed casual and rustic, following the example of Gainsborough. The arms resting on a ledge give a relaxed, direct pose that lets the sitter engage the viewer. The sitter, recorded as Harriet Kinloch, appears in the title as Lady Cunliffe.","c":"A fresh early work, very much in the English manner of the day: soft light, an informal pose and a yellow-on-black color scheme that makes the figure glow. Hoppner shows his debt to Reynolds and Gainsborough, and the picture does not yet have the assurance of his later society portraits. But the harmony of the yellows, the lace and the direct look are attractive, and for a young painter's portrait it is neatly managed."},"nga:1174":{"d":"Three children are gathered in a natural setting. The standing child, at right and slightly turned to the left, folds both arms across the chest, with wavy light brown hair, a russet outfit with a ruffled white collar, white socks and dark shoes. Two younger children sit in the grass at left, one nude on a white cloth, the other between them in a light garment draped over the shoulders. Behind them are trees in fall color, a reflective stream and cloudy skies. The oil on canvas is large, about 152 by 127 cm.","i":"The museum names the painter's sons Belgrave and Lascelles Hoppner among the sitters. Portraits of the artist's own children let a painter be freer than he could in commissions. The natural setting, informal grouping and nude toddler belong to the English fashion for children as images of innocence and play in landscape, set by Reynolds and Gainsborough. Richard Belgrave Hoppner later became British consul in Venice, where he knew Lord Byron.","c":"A warm, informal picture in which the painter lets his affection show. The folded arms of the standing child are delightfully commanding for so young a figure, and the seated children make a pleasing triangle against the autumn foliage and stream. The flesh is soft and the colors harmonious, russet against greens and golds. It lacks the poetry of Reynolds's best child pictures, but as a family portrait it is among Hoppner's more engaging works."},"met:436686":{"d":"Sarah Franklin Bache, the daughter of Benjamin Franklin, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76.5 by 63 cm, dated 1793. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of a woman in her early fifties, by the fashionable London portraitist John Hoppner; since he worked in England, she presumably sat for him there on a visit.","i":"Sarah Franklin Bache (1743–1808) was Benjamin Franklin's only surviving daughter and the wife of the Philadelphia merchant Richard Bache. During the Revolutionary War she helped organize the women of Philadelphia to collect funds and sew shirts for Washington's soldiers. Her father had died in 1790, and she sat to an English painter as the daughter of one of the most famous Americans of the day.","c":"Hoppner is a gifted if uneven portraitist, and his women's portraits of the 1790s are among his most graceful, with soft light, a gentle turn of the head and easy handling of dress. Without a description, the picture must be judged by its type: a refined half-length by one of London's leading portrait painters. Its main interest lies in the sitter's history, an American woman of the Revolutionary generation shown in the English society manner."},"nga:118":{"d":"Two young women sit close together, the one at left leaning on the other and looking to the right, the other looking out at us. Both wear high-waisted white gowns with pale blue sashes. The sister at left, with a light blue ribbon in her hair, holds a paper scroll and an arm around the other, who wears a cream bonnet and holds a large red folder of papers and a small gold object. A brown and white dog sleeps at their feet. Dark trees rise at upper right, a hilly landscape and a distant blue mountain open at left. The oil is about 155 by 125 cm.","i":"The sisters, Marianne and Amelia Frankland, are shown as accomplished, affectionate young women: the scroll, the folder of papers and the small gold object suggest music or drawing, the accomplishments expected of a gentleman's daughters, while the arm around the shoulder, the sleeping dog and the open landscape speak of a gentle domestic life. The simple high-waisted white gowns belong to the new neoclassical-inspired fashion of the 1790s.","c":"One of Hoppner's most pleasing group portraits: the two figures are bound by pose and gesture, the white dresses and blue sashes unify them, and the open landscape, with its dark trees and hazy blue mountain, adds air. Hoppner's sweetness can verge on the sugary, and his faces are more alike than individual, but the composition is graceful and balanced, and the sense of closeness feels genuine."},"met:436692":{"d":"Three children of the Sackville family are shown in a large oil of about 152 by 124.5 cm, dated 1796: a boy and two girls, identified by the museum as George Sackville, later fourth Duke of Dorset, and the ladies Mary and Elizabeth Sackville. The record gives no description of their poses, dress or setting, so only the type is given: a group portrait of aristocratic children of the 1790s, of the kind made for a country house.","i":"The sitters were the children of John Frederick Sackville, third Duke of Dorset, whose family seat was Knole in Kent. The boy, George, succeeded his father as duke as a small child in 1799 and died young in 1815; Elizabeth later became Countess De La Warr. A group portrait of children by Hoppner, then portraitist to the Prince of Wales, recorded a dynasty's heirs, and the image of happy, graceful children was also fashionable in its own right.","c":"Hoppner has a gift for painting children, with soft light, warm flesh and a feeling for innocence, and a portrait of three small aristocrats lets him combine charm with the dignity expected of a dynastic group. His handling is smooth, even sugary, and the formula comes from Reynolds, but a good deal of freshness survives. Without a description of the picture, the judgment is general: a competent, appealing example of the high-society child portrait of the 1790s."},"met:436685":{"d":"A mother, Ann Garden, is shown with her two children, John and Ann Margaret, in an oil on canvas of about 127 by 101 cm, dated 1796 or 1797. The museum records only a mother, a boy and a girl as a family group, so the poses, dress and setting are not described here. The dates in the title show that the boy was a baby and the girl about three or four, so it is a portrait of a young family, in the English tradition of showing maternal affection.","i":"Mother-and-children portraits had become a fashionable way of presenting the ideal of domestic virtue: the mother as nurturer, the children as the family's hope. After Reynolds the genre grew popular in England, and Hoppner's group of a mother with a baby and a small girl presents the Garden family as a model of affection and refinement. The infant's age suggests that the picture may have been made soon after his birth, though the record does not say.","c":"A mother's tenderness is the natural subject of this kind of picture, and Hoppner, who was good at the soft, flattering treatment of women and children, is well suited to it. The weakness of the genre is its formula: the same pyramid of figures, the same drapery, the same tenderness. Without more evidence, it is best taken as a respectable, polished and agreeable Hoppner, not one of his boldest inventions."},"met:436684":{"d":"A man, Major Thomas Pechell, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63 cm, dated 1799, the standard portrait size of 30 by 25 inches. The museum gives no description of his pose, dress or setting, and does not say whether he wears uniform, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of an army officer in the polished London manner of the day, painted to hang with a companion portrait of his wife.","i":"A major's rank was a distinction worth recording in a country at war with revolutionary France, and officers were eager sitters in the 1790s. Hoppner, who painted for the Prince of Wales's circle as well as for the gentry, supplied them with the flattering, up-to-date likeness that London society expected. Pechell and his wife, Charlotte Clavering, sat for matching portraits in the same year, a common way for a couple to furnish a drawing room.","c":"Hoppner's male portraits of these years are brisk and confident, with a glossy surface and a lively, slightly romantic treatment of the head that owes something to Reynolds and Romney. Without a description, the portrait can be judged only as a type: capable, polished and good-natured, from the studio of the principal rival of Thomas Lawrence. It lacks Lawrence's nervous elegance, but it has an ease of its own."},"met:436688":{"d":"A woman, Charlotte Clavering, wife of Major Thomas Pechell, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, dated 1799. No details of her dress, pose or setting are recorded, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait of a gentlewoman of the late 1790s, painted as the companion of her husband's portrait. The title notes that she died in 1841, fifteen years after him.","i":"In the late 1790s fashion favored plain, high-waisted dresses and loose, natural hair, a reaction against the powdered, structured styles of the 1780s, and Hoppner was skilled at presenting women in this softer manner, with an air of ease. A matching pair of portraits of a couple was a standard way to furnish a drawing room and to record a marriage's standing, and this is the wife's half of such a pair.","c":"Hoppner's best portraits of women are fresh and a little breathless, with soft light on the face, loose handling of hair and gauze, and a grace that recalls Romney. A portrait of 1799 is likely to share those qualities, and the pairing with her husband's means that it would have hung as part of a matched set. It is a polished, flattering and pleasant picture rather than a profound one, which is what its patrons wanted."},"met:436690":{"d":"An unnamed woman is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63 cm, painted in the 1790s. The museum gives no description of her appearance, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait in the fashionable London manner of the decade. The museum's title adds that the reverse of the canvas bears a study of a child's head, now hidden by the lining canvas that was added to strengthen the picture.","i":"The sitter's identity has been lost, as often happens when a portrait leaves its family. The hidden child's head on the reverse is a reminder of how artists worked: studios reused canvases, tried out heads and made studies for later pictures, and this is a rare trace of the painter's process, though the record does not say why it is there. Relining, the addition of a new canvas to the back of an old one, is what has covered it.","c":"A portrait of the 1790s by Hoppner at the height of his powers: ease, light handling and a flattering likeness were what sitters bought. With no name and no further description, the picture is chiefly interesting as a type. The child's head on the reverse, hidden but recorded, hints at a looser, freer side of his practice than his polished finished portraits show."},"met:436683":{"d":"A woman, Lady Hester King, is portrayed in oil on a canvas of about 76 by 63.5 cm, probably dated 1805. The museum gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so only the type is given: a half-length portrait on the standard canvas of 30 by 25 inches, painted in the soft, fashionable manner Hoppner used for his sitters in the last years before his death in 1810.","i":"The style Lady before a first name marks the daughter of a duke, marquess or earl, the surname being that of the family rather than the title; King is the family name of the Earls of Kingston among others, though the record here does not confirm a link. A portrait of 1805 was made when Hoppner, portraitist to the Prince of Wales, was a leading painter of aristocratic women in rivalry with Thomas Lawrence.","c":"By 1805 Hoppner's women had settled into a recognizable formula: a soft face, a lightly sketched costume and a warm ground. The manner is easy to enjoy and hard to remember, and it was being overtaken by the sharper, more nervous elegance of Lawrence. A Hoppner of this date can look a little old-fashioned, more Reynolds than Regency, but it has the charm the period wanted. It is best taken as a competent late example."},"met:436687":{"d":"A woman, Hester Sheridan, is shown with her young son in a very large oil, about 238 by 150 cm, nearly eight feet high. The museum records only a woman, a boy and the mother-and-son relationship, and gives no date, so the pose, dress, setting and the boy's age are not described here. It is a full-length society portrait of the standard size, the type made to hang in a large drawing room or staircase hall.","i":"Hester Jane Ogle was the second wife of the playwright and politician Richard Brinsley Sheridan, author of The Rivals and The School for Scandal and manager of the Drury Lane Theatre; their son Charles was born in 1796. A mother-and-child portrait of this scale presented the family as a model of domestic happiness, a theme the public liked. Sheridan was a friend of the Prince of Wales, whose portraitist Hoppner was.","c":"The scale and the subject are ambitious: a young mother and a child in a full-length format that Reynolds and Romney had made popular. Hoppner meets the challenge with ease and a flattering softness, but the risk of such large canvases is emptiness, since the figures must fill so much space. The picture has the glamour of its sitters' names and the proficiency of a leading society painter, likely more graceful than searching."},"met:436691":{"d":"A boxer, Richard Humphreys, is portrayed in a large oil of about 142 by 112 cm. The museum gives no date or description of the pose, so what he wears, what he holds and whether he is shown in action are not described here. The record notes only a man, boxing and an athlete. It is a serious, large-scale portrait of a sportsman, unusual in its subject for a painter better known for society sitters.","i":"Richard Humphreys, nicknamed the Gentleman Boxer, was one of the leading prizefighters of 1780s England, famous for a skilful style and for his rivalry with Daniel Mendoza. Bare-knuckle boxing was both a plebeian sport and a fashion among aristocrats, including the Prince of Wales, who patronized fighters and attended matches. A portrait of a boxer by a leading portraitist reflects that mix: the sitter was a celebrity, and the picture honors athletic skill as part of a masculine ideal.","c":"A boxer is a rare subject for a polished society painter, and the picture must be judged by how Hoppner balances polite portraiture with a body trained for violence. His skill lies in flesh and light, and an athlete's frame gives him more to do than a courtier's coat. The record does not say how forceful the treatment is, but the subject alone gives the painting an interest beyond the ordinary."},"nga:130897":{"d":"Steep, rocky cliffs fill the right two-thirds of this small horizontal painting, with a distant landscape at left. A brown stone castle with crenellated walls stands at the cliff's edge at center, and higher on the hill are reddish-brown ruins with tall, narrow towers, partly overgrown. At lower right a person in a brimmed hat stands among several goats, looking out to the left. Beyond the cliffs a hazy plain lies under a blue sky with soft white clouds, and streaks over the horizon mountains suggest distant rain. The oil on canvas measures about 23 by 30 cm.","i":"The title most likely refers to Monte San Giuliano, the former name of the hill town of Erice above Trapani in western Sicily, whose castle stands on the site of an ancient temple of Venus. Sicily's mixture of Greek temples, Norman fortresses and wild scenery drew French painters in the 1820s, and Sarazin de Belmont was a woman landscape painter at a time when relatively few women traveled to paint. Small oil studies like this were the raw material for later studio pictures.","c":"A small study with an assured sense of structure: the steep rocks, the fortress at the edge and the tiny figure with goats give a clear sequence of scale from foreground to distance, and the hazy plain and the streaked horizon add atmosphere without fuss. Sarazin de Belmont's strength is observation rather than invention, and the detail is crisp and the light natural. It stands comfortably with the better oil sketches of French landscapists in Italy."},"nga:130896":{"d":"Two men in long brown robes, each with a bald head ringed by hair, stretch out their hands toward a young boy who holds a hand to his head; all three are tiny against stone ruins and rocky mountains. They stand on grass beside a curving honey-brown wall that sweeps toward us in a shallow U. Behind lie ruins in a shallow valley, intact buildings along a cliff above ice-blue water and, in the distance, smoke rising from a snow-tipped mountain. The oil on paper, laid on canvas, measures about 43 by 60 cm and is signed and dated 1828.","i":"The curving wall is presumably part of the ancient theater at Taormina in eastern Sicily, a Greek theater rebuilt by the Romans, whose stage looks over the sea toward Mount Etna; the smoking mountain in the distance is Etna. The robed men with ringed heads are tonsured, so they are probably monks or friars, and the small incident gives the vast ruin a human scale. Taormina became a stop for travelers and painters in the 1800s who wanted antiquity framed by sea and volcano.","c":"The great curve of ruin, the tiny figures and the smoking volcano create a pleasing balance of man, antiquity and nature, and the honey-brown stone against ice-blue water and pale sky is handled in a fresh, light palette. The picture is more lyrical than grand; the figures are anecdotal, and the pleasure lies in clarity of space and tone. Sarazin de Belmont is less known than she deserves among French landscapists of the 1820s."},"met:436030":{"d":"A boy with soap bubbles is the subject of this canvas of about 131 by 98 cm, painted about 1859. The museum lists a book, glassware, a toy, a wreath and a chair among the objects, and notes the theme of contemplation, so the child seems absorbed in the fragile spheres. The record gives no further description of his clothes, pose, colors or setting, so none is invented here.","i":"Soap bubbles have been a symbol of the brevity of life since the Renaissance: the Latin proverb homo bulla, man is a bubble, made the child with bubbles a standard emblem of transience, often paired with skulls, flowers and books in vanitas paintings. Couture revives the tradition for the Second Empire. The book, the glass and the wreath belong to the same moral language, standing perhaps for learning, fragility and fleeting honor.","c":"Couture, best known as the teacher of Manet and for his vast Romans of the Decadence, here turns to a small, quiet subject. The theme is old, and the treatment is polished and a little sweet, with a child standing in for a universal lesson. The picture's merits are its smooth modeling and its poetic mood; its weakness is a certain staginess, as if the moral were being explained. It remains a charming late example of academic vanitas."},"nga:116570":{"d":"A light brown path or courtyard leads toward a small stone building with a thatched roof at left. On the right stands a person in a dark blue dress, a green apron and a white bonnet. The left side of the scene lies in shadow, while the right is lit with bright yellow light. Behind the building a few tall, thin trees rise before a wide green field stretching to the horizon, under a bright blue sky with a single small white cloud at upper left. The oil on canvas measures about 72 by 60 cm and is dated 1890.","i":"Le Pouldu is a small fishing hamlet on the south coast of Brittany where, in 1889–90, Paul Gauguin and a group of younger painters stayed and worked, and Sérusier was among them. He had learned from Gauguin to simplify forms, flatten space and use color boldly rather than imitate nature, a manner that became the basis of the Nabis. The white bonnet is probably a Breton woman's headdress, and the region's folk life was treated by these painters as simple and authentic.","c":"The picture is built on strong contrasts: shadow on the left, brilliant yellow light on the right, a sky of pure blue and a field of flat green, with the dark blue figure as an anchor. The forms are simplified rather than modeled, which is Gauguin's lesson, and the result is bold and decorative, if a little schematic. It is among the livelier results of Sérusier's Breton years, valuable for its clear, even gaudy, color."},"met:437269":{"d":"A winter landscape, painted in oil on a canvas of about 90 by 117.5 cm. The museum records winter and snow, and the title places the scene in January at Cernay, near Rambouillet, southwest of Paris; no date or further description is given, so the trees, buildings, light and sky are not described here. A canvas of this size, wider than tall, was probably made as a finished exhibition picture rather than as an outdoor sketch.","i":"Cernay-la-Ville, in the valley of the Chevreuse near Rambouillet, became an artists' colony in the later 1800s, attracting landscape painters who valued its woods, ponds and fields close to Paris. Pelouse, who lived and worked there, specialized in quiet seasonal views, among them winter scenes under snow, painted in a restrained tonal palette. The month in the title may mean that the picture belongs to a set in which each time of year had its own character.","c":"Pelouse belongs to the generation that inherited the Barbizon painters' love of unglamorous nature, and a snow scene in a restrained tonal key suits him. The large format gives the winter silence room, and a painter who works in grays and whites must rely on subtle gradations of value. The Impressionists were making colorful winter scenes in the same years, and beside them a Pelouse can look sober, but the sober harmony is itself an achievement."},"met:436118":{"d":"A forge scene is painted in oil on a small wooden panel, about 32 by 42 cm. The museum records a man at work with a hammer, a woman and an infant, and gives no date or further description, so the light, the layout of the workshop and the tools are not described here. It is a cabinet picture of a trade and a household together, a type that Defrance painted in the Dutch tradition of small, finely finished genre scenes.","i":"Defrance worked in Liège, a center of metalworking in the Southern Netherlands, and scenes of forges and foundries were among his favorite subjects. The type descends from Dutch and Flemish pictures of workshops with strong effects of fire and shadow, and in the Enlightenment it took on a new dignity: the labor of the artisan was shown with respect, as productive and useful. The woman and child add a domestic note, the family beside the trade.","c":"The charm of this kind of picture is typically the glow of the furnace against shade and the small drama of work: a hammer raised, a child nearby. Defrance's handling is neat and his figures sturdy, though they can seem stiff beside the Dutch masters of the type. As an eighteenth-century industrial image it has historical interest, a small, competent work by a regional painter better known for his subjects than for brilliance."},"met:436119":{"d":"A tightrope performance is watched by a crowd in this wooden panel of about 50 by 73 cm. The museum lists a man and a woman, a rope dance, an audience and a cello player among the elements, and gives no date or further description, so the setting, the costumes and the positions of the figures are not described here. It is a lively cabinet picture of popular entertainment, wider than tall, with space for a crowd.","i":"Rope dancers were fairground entertainers who performed on a taut rope, often to music, and they were among the most popular attractions at fairs and festivals in eighteenth-century Europe, including the Low Countries. Painters of the Flemish and Dutch tradition had long recorded such crowd scenes, which allowed a range of social types in one picture, and Defrance, a keen observer of local life in Liège, belonged to that tradition. The tightrope may also carry a moral note, as a figure of life's precariousness.","c":"A crowd scene tests a painter's ability to organize many figures in a small space, and a panel like this succeeds when the eye passes easily from performer to cellist to onlookers. Defrance is competent rather than inspired, and his people can be stiff, but the subject is vivid, and the picture records a popular entertainment that more polished artists ignored. Its value is documentary and social as much as artistic."},"nga:220510":{"d":"Two large dogs approach a man lying unconscious and half-buried in snow. A big tan and white dog in the center, in a red blanket and a silver collar with lions and bells, paws at the snow and looks up to the right; beside it a dark brindle dog with a small barrel at its neck licks the man's bare hand. The man, in an olive coat with a fur collar, lies with closed eyes. Crags and pines enclose the scene, and down a path from a stone building three bearded men in black caps hurry toward the dogs. The oil on canvas is large, about 189 by 237 cm.","i":"The setting is presumably the Great Saint Bernard Pass in the Alps, where a hospice sheltered travelers and where dogs of the breed now called Saint Bernard were kept. The small barrel at the brindle dog's neck is the famous image of the rescue dog carrying brandy to the lost, and it is often said to owe much of its fame to Landseer's picture. The men in black caps with a cross-topped staff are presumably brothers of the hospice. Landseer painted it at about eighteen.","c":"A remarkable picture for a painter of eighteen: the group of dogs and fallen man is built with confidence, the dogs' heads are drawn with real knowledge, and the crags and cold blue sky give a convincing sense of high mountains. The sentiment is strong and the rescue story carefully managed; the weakness is a certain staginess, and the human figures are less alive than the dogs. It points to the future favorite of Queen Victoria."},"met:436845":{"d":"A hunt is shown in a small oil on wood, about 41 by 61 cm: according to the museum's tags, horsemen ride down a grey wolf and a fox. It is a copy, made around 1824 to 1826, of a large picture by Rubens, the Wolf and Fox Hunt, so its design belongs to Rubens, while the handling is Landseer's. The record gives no further description of the poses, the landscape or the colors.","i":"Copying the old masters was the traditional way to learn, and Rubens was the great master of animal action: his hunts show horses, hounds and quarry locked in one swirling movement. For a young animal painter such as Landseer, who was already making his name in the 1820s, a copy was a way to study how Rubens built an energetic group and handled the muscles of horses and dogs. Such copies were made to learn from and sometimes to sell.","c":"As a copy it is best judged as an exercise: Landseer reduces Rubens's grand design to a small panel and keeps the sweep of the diagonal, the surge of the horses and the snarl of the hounds. It lacks the muscular certainty and rich color of the original, as any copy must, but it shows what the young Landseer wanted to learn, a sense of animal movement as a whole, and it says more about him than about Rubens."},"met:782306":{"d":"A single dog, identified in the title as a deerhound, is the subject of this small oil on board, about 31 by 41 cm, dated 1826. The museum records nothing more, so the pose, the coat color, the setting and the light are not described here. It is the kind of picture an animal painter might make as a study or a private commission: the animal alone, shown as an individual rather than as part of a hunt.","i":"The deerhound is a tall, rough-coated Scottish breed once used to hunt red deer, and by the 1820s it had become a symbol of the Highlands, celebrated by Sir Walter Scott, who kept deerhounds himself. Landseer visited Scotland from 1824 and returned many times, and the Highlands, deer and dogs became his lifelong subjects. A small oil of this kind records a favorite animal and reflects the growing taste for sporting and animal pictures among British collectors.","c":"Landseer's early dog portraits are admired for the knowledge in their drawing and the feeling in the eyes, and he painted fur with a fluency few could match. At this small scale the picture is likely to be a quick, spirited study rather than a showpiece. If there is a weakness, it is the sentimental projection of human feelings onto animals that grew in his later work, but at this date the image is still plain and direct."},"met:782307":{"d":"A stag, shown at the point of death according to the title, is the subject of this oil on canvas of about 46 by 54 cm, painted around 1830. The museum records only deer, so the setting, the pose and the light are not described here. As the title suggests, the picture is a study or small finished picture of a hunted animal at its last moments, the kind of subject Landseer returned to throughout his career.","i":"Stag hunting and deer stalking were central to the Highland sporting culture that Landseer knew, and his pictures of stags swing between celebration of the hunt and sympathy for the animal. A dying stag shifts attention from the hunter to the quarry, inviting pity and respect for a noble creature, a sentiment that suited Victorian taste. It may also recall the stricken deer of Cowper's poem, a wounded creature that has left the herd.","c":"Landseer was a superb draftsman of deer, and a dying stag lets him combine anatomical truth with pathos. At this size and date the picture is probably an intimate work, less grand than the stags of his later years, and better for it, because the sentiment is not yet overblown. The risk, which he often ran, is human emotion read into an animal, but here the gravity of the subject gives the sentiment a justification."},"nga:130892":{"d":"A golden lion crouches over the carcass of a large deer with curving horns and snarls at a snake. Its yellow eyes are wide, its mouth open on long white teeth, and its claws are sunk into the pale brown body, leaving red trails of blood. The snake slides down from dark trees behind the lion, its head on the ground at right, turned up toward the lion with its mouth open. The jungle background is dark green, blue-green, brown and rusty orange. The small oil on paper, laid on canvas, measures about 25 by 32 cm.","i":"Landseer painted many lions and fights between wild animals, a subject for which he studied the big cats at the Zoological Gardens in London and, later, the dead lion he used for the Trafalgar Square sculptures. A lion defending its kill against a snake sets strength against cunning, nature as a theater of struggle. In the 1800s painters of wild animals, following Rubens, Stubbs and Delacroix, made the big cat a symbol of power and menace.","c":"A small oil sketch that gains from its brevity: the lion's snarl and the snake's coiled attention are given with quick, decisive strokes, and the jungle's greens and rusty oranges make a dramatic backdrop. Such works show Landseer's keen eye for animal anatomy and gesture, and an imagination for violence that his polite sporting pictures hide. The setting is stagey and the subject sensational, but as a vigorous small study it reveals the energy behind his finished pictures."},"nga:195135":{"d":"A man and a woman, the Duke of Devonshire and Lady Louisa Egerton, are shown in a double portrait in oil on a canvas of about 71 by 91 cm, painted about 1853. The museum record gives no description of the pose, dress or setting, so only the type is given: a wide, horizontal group portrait, unusual for Landseer, who is better known for animals, in its pairing of a grandee and a lady.","i":"The Duke of Devonshire of 1853 was William Cavendish, the sixth duke, known as the Bachelor Duke because he never married, a great aristocratic patron who created the gardens and enlarged the house at Chatsworth. A joint portrait of the duke and a lady of his circle records a friendship or family bond, though the record here does not specify it. Landseer, who painted for the royal family and the nobility, moved in the same aristocratic circles.","c":"A portrait of two figures lets Landseer, better known for animals, show how he handled people: he is smooth and engaging, with a taste for incident that gives life to a group. Without a description, the picture can only be judged by its type, an affectionate pairing in a wide format by a painter who understood aristocratic taste. His human figures are often less convincing than his animals, and that is the limit one expects here."},"nga:47181":{"d":"A gentleman stands in a lush green landscape, dressed in an eighteenth-century outfit of dark blue coat, red waistcoat, knee-length breeches, white stockings and black shoes. He appears to hold strings or cords, which the title suggests are the lines of a partridge net. At his side, guns and dead game birds lie on the grass. A river flows through the middle ground, tall trees with carefully detailed leaves stand close by, and across the water a house sits among more trees. The land runs back into softly rolling hills under a pale blue sky with light clouds.","i":"Devis specialised in small-scale portraits of the gentry in their settings, now called conversation pieces, in which clothes, possessions and landscape state the sitter's standing. Netting partridges was an established country sport, and the guns and game on the grass, the house across the water and the well-kept grounds tell the viewer that the sitter owns land and has leisure. The title gives no name; the portrait works as an image of a type, the sporting landowner of mid-Georgian England.","c":"Devis's people are famously stiff, like figures arranged in a toy landscape, and this gentleman is no exception: the pose is formal and the face has little character. The charm lies elsewhere, in the carefully detailed trees, the neat river and the soft green distance, and in the evident pleasure he takes in the gear of the sport. A pleasant, polite picture, better as a document of manners than as a study of character."},"nga:61390":{"d":"Three men and a dog are gathered outdoors. At the left of centre a man in a blue coat with gold trim and a red waistcoat sits looking out at the viewer, a black tricorn on his curled grey hair and a brown-and-white dog resting its head on his thigh. Behind him another man in grey and tall black boots leans against a tree, holding a long thin stick and a hat, and looks right. The third stands at the right in a blue-and-grey coat, hat in one hand, pointing with the other towards the seated man. Beyond lie tall trees, distant grey buildings and ships on blue water.","i":"This is a conversation piece, the type of small-group portrait Devis made his speciality: sitters gathered in a landscape, caught in mid-talk, with dress and gesture signalling rank and friendship. The title names the men and gives the River Dart in Devon as the setting, and the ships and buildings in the distance place the scene near a busy port. The pointing hand and exchanged looks give the group a thread of narrative, though the conversation itself is not recorded.","c":"On a generous canvas, the interplay of pointing hand, glance and leaning stance gives the trio more life than Devis's single figures usually have, and costumes and boots are rendered with relish. The landscape with its ships adds welcome depth. Even so, the faces are close to interchangeable and the poses feel arranged, so the group has the courtesy and the stiffness of a stage tableau rather than of a conversation."},"nga:47182":{"d":"Two women and two children are grouped in a park. At the centre a woman sits on a bench, head turned right, playing a lute-like instrument; she wears a blue gown with ruffles, and a hat with a blue ribbon hangs beside her. At the right a child in white stands next to a seated woman in a red lace-trimmed dress and lace cap. At the left another child in white with blue underskirts and red shoes walks towards the group holding a tall flower, more flowers gathered in the skirt. Behind are lawn, tall trees, grazing cows and a red-brick mansion half hidden by trees.","i":"Devis made his reputation with conversation pieces for the gentry, in which a family is shown at leisure in its own grounds. Music, flowers and the house in the distance declare accomplishment, gentle manners and property, while the cows hint at a working estate behind the genteel surface. The title identifies the sitters only as members of the Maynard family, and the museum's date range, c. 1755 to 1762, reflects some uncertainty. The picture served as a record of family and place.","c":"The grouping is gracious but static: the seated women are posed like figures in a frieze, the children inserted at the edges, and the faces have the porcelain sameness typical of Devis. Yet the large canvas gives him room to show what he does well, the shimmer of silk and lace, the deft handling of foliage, and the balance between figures and mansion. A well-mannered period piece that gives up psychology and gains in charm."},"met:441229":{"d":"A woman and an angel meet in the wilderness in this canvas of about 106 by 137 cm, painted around 1657. The title names the woman as Hagar, Sarah's Egyptian servant, to whom an angel appears in the desert to bring comfort and guidance. Maffei, who worked in Vicenza and Venice, painted in a nervous, flickering manner, with elongated forms and brushwork that seems to vibrate, and he made biblical episodes into visionary encounters.","i":"Hagar appears twice in Genesis: pregnant and fleeing Sarah's harsh treatment, and later cast out with her son Ishmael. Both times an angel finds her in the wilderness, and in the second he shows her a well that saves the child. The story speaks of an outcast servant whom God does not abandon. Which episode the picture shows is not certain from the title alone, and a viewer of the seventeenth century would have recognized either.","c":"Maffei is one of the most original Italian painters of the mid-seventeenth century, and 1657, three years before his death, falls in the late phase when his handling grew most fluid and visionary. That is the right manner for an angelic apparition. The risk is slackness, since the same freedom that gives intensity can loosen the drawing, but few of his contemporaries pushed the manner so far, and that boldness is its main merit."},"met:436634":{"d":"A tall bouquet bursts from a vase set in a dark stone niche, dense with blooms against a nearly black ground. At the top a white tulip streaked with purple opens beside a darker, ruffled one. Below are pink and white roses, a pale hollyhock, red carnations, orange marigolds, a passionflower with a blue-and-white fringe and an opium poppy among many smaller flowers. On the stone ledge in front lie a peach and bunches of green and purple grapes. A butterfly rests among the flowers at right, a snail crawls at left and a fly settles on a bloom. The signature and date, 1716, are at lower right.","i":"Margareta Haverman was the only formal pupil of the flower painter Jan van Huysum, and her work follows his manner. In 1722 she was admitted to the Académie royale in Paris, but in 1723 she was expelled after claims that her admission piece was really by Van Huysum. Only two signed paintings by her are known today, this one and another in Copenhagen. The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 1871, as part of its first purchase of paintings. Conservators found that a yellow pigment had faded, leaving the foliage bluish, and rather than retouch it they made a digital reconstruction.","c":"The skill is plain. A sprawling arrangement is held in a tidy oval, the grapes and petals have glossy highlights, and each bloom stays distinct. Its limits are equally plain: a stiff, symmetrical pyramid, a dark ground that flattens the space, and faded yellows that now give the foliage an off-key blue. It is a competent, close follower of Van Huysum rather than an original. Its real interest is its rarity: a woman at the top of a male-dominated specialty, whose Paris career ended amid accusations of borrowed help."},"cma:157217":{"d":"A woman is surrounded by children on this small panel, 69 by 55 centimeters, and has bared her breast for an infant among them. The group is packed tightly, and the style is Northern Mannerism, which favors exaggerated proportions, contorted poses and heightened color. The museum notes that the eroticism is deliberate: it gives the personified virtue a magnetic beauty. In size it is a cabinet picture, made for close, private viewing rather than for a church wall.","i":"Charity (Caritas) is one of the three theological virtues beside Faith and Hope, and she is almost always shown as a woman with children. Bloemaert took his model from images of the Virgin and Child, since the nursing mother already stood for tenderness and self-giving. The same pose could therefore carry an idea: love of one's neighbor as a mother's love. The sensuous beauty is meant to make the virtue attractive, not forbidding.","c":"An early work, from before Bloemaert turned toward the calmer, more natural figures and rustic scenes of his later career. The Mannerist habits are plain: complicated poses, a crowded arrangement and an air of display. The allegory is clever in borrowing the Madonna, but more elegant than tender, and admired more than felt. It is valuable chiefly as evidence of where Dutch painting stood around 1590."},"met:435680":{"d":"Moses strikes the rock with his rod, and water gushes out for the Israelites, who have been dying of thirst in the wilderness. Around him gather the people and their animals: men and women, some of the women nude, together with cattle and a dog. Painted in 1596, the canvas measures about 80 by 108 centimeters, a wide format that suits a crowd. It is an early dated work by Bloemaert, made when he was about thirty and working in the Mannerist manner.","i":"The story comes from Exodus 17 and Numbers 20: the thirsty Israelites quarrel with Moses until God commands him to strike a rock, and water flows. Christian readers took the rock to be Christ, following Saint Paul's remark in 1 Corinthians 10, and the water to be the grace of baptism and the Eucharist. The scene therefore reads on two levels, as a miracle of provision in the desert and as a foreshadowing of the Christian sacraments.","c":"Bloemaert's Mannerist training shows in the taste for twisted bodies, unusual poses and crowded groups, with the miracle serving as an occasion for display. The figures and animals are the pleasures here, and the craft of the grouping is considerable. To many eyes his later, quieter manner, with simpler figures in sunlit settings, is more persuasive, but this canvas shows how inventive and ambitious he was early on."},"nga:133023":{"d":"The head, shoulders and chest of an old man with grey hair and deeply wrinkled skin fill a vertical panel, about 48 by 36 centimetres. His shoulders angle slightly to the right and he looks up and off in that direction, his brown eyes deep-set under a furrowed brow. Light from the upper left leaves the far side of his craggy face in deep shadow, and tendons and wrinkles show on his thick neck. Unruly ash-grey locks stand up from his head, and his chest is bare within a gaping grey shirt, under a barn-red garment, against an earth-brown ground.","i":"A head of this kind is called a tronie, a Dutch term for a character study of a face or expression rather than a portrait of a named sitter. Such studies might serve as preparation for figures in larger paintings, such as an apostle or a prophet, and were also collected in their own right. Abraham Bloemaert, a leading painter in Utrecht, made such heads in the 1620s, when Utrecht artists were absorbing the strong light and realism of Caravaggio.","c":"A bold, direct study. Bloemaert uses a raking light to model every furrow and tendon, and the old man's upward glance gives him a hint of inner life. The painting is more concerned with texture and effect than with psychology, and it lacks the compassion with which Rembrandt rendered old faces a few years later. But as a vigorous head it is strongly done, and it shows a veteran painter still alive to new fashions."},"met:436776":{"d":"Christ's dead body lies in the tomb while two angels attend it. Death and lamentation are the themes, and the painting is a wide canvas, 115 by 147 centimeters, made around 1610. The subject strips the Passion down to a single silent moment between the Crucifixion and the Resurrection: a devotional image to be contemplated rather than a story to be followed. Pictures of this type were made for private chapels and collectors' rooms as well as for churches.","i":"Pictures of the dead Christ in the tomb, watched over by angels, encouraged meditation on the sacrifice of the Cross and, for Catholic viewers, on the Eucharist, in which Christ's body is offered. They suited the Counter-Reformation southern Netherlands, where Antwerp's churches and chapels were being refitted after the iconoclasm of 1566 and the Calvinist years. The angels, who in the Gospels announce the Resurrection, are here mourners and witnesses.","c":"Janssens was the leading history painter in Antwerp before Rubens came back from Italy in 1608, and his figures, solid, almost sculptural and strongly lit, show what he learned in Rome. Set beside Rubens, his feeling is cooler and his drawing a little stiff, and a picture like this tends to impress by weight rather than warmth. It remains a good record of Flemish Baroque painting before Rubens took over."},"nga:76211":{"d":"A bouquet hangs from a sapphire-blue bow tied around a nail against a dark brown ground. Three large blooms form a pyramid at the center: a pale pink carnation, a red and white striped tulip and a white viburnum, with a tan spider spinning a web among them. Around them are love-in-the-mist, peonies, a morning glory, amaranth, crab apple blossom, Chinese lanterns, a yellow rose with ants on its petals and stalks of wheat, with a moth, a caterpillar and a ladybug among the stems. The oil on panel measures about 38 by 30 cm and is signed.","i":"Dutch and German flower pieces of the 1600s combined delight in rare blooms with a reminder of mortality. The insects, ants, caterpillar, moth and spider underline decay and the brief life of beauty, the wheat may allude to bread and the Eucharist, and the striped tulip recalls the tulip mania that enriched and ruined Dutch fortunes in the 1630s. The bouquet tied to a nail is a trompe-l'oeil conceit. Mignon, born in Frankfurt, trained and worked in Utrecht.","c":"Mignon's flower pieces are among the most refined of their kind: the colors are bright against the dark ground, each bloom is separately studied, and the tiny creatures are painted with a naturalist's accuracy. The pyramid of three large blooms gives the bouquet a firm structure, and the ribbon and nail add the neat conceit of a hanging object. The effect is a little crowded, but as a display of observation it is hard to match at this size."},"nga:71024":{"d":"A tall canvas heaped with a forest-floor still life. A wicker basket with an arched handle stands before a rough stone archway, holding green, red and purple grapes, peaches, plums, oranges and yellow pears, with wheat winding through the fruit. Birds perch above and on the handle. Frogs, lizards, a snail and a caterpillar sit or crawl around gourds and a cantaloupe at the foot, while at right worms spill from a bait box hung with fishing lines and silvery fish. Strong light from the left lifts emerald, plum and pumpkin orange out of deep shadow.","i":"Mignon trained with Jacob Marrel in Frankfurt and Jan Davidsz. de Heem in Utrecht, and this crowded, decorative manner descends from them. Such pictures set nature's abundance beside human pursuit of it, with tackle, bait and a nest bringing the angler into the fruit piece. Small creatures carried older meanings: the butterfly and caterpillar traditionally signal transformation, and the dead lizard beside the living one may allude to mortality. How far Mignon intended a sermon rather than a display of skill is uncertain.","c":"Strong side light and a dark ground concentrate attention on the saturated colors, and the tackle, nest and bait give a heap of objects a loose narrative, Mignon's best idea here. The weakness is the same crowding. Fruit, fish, birds and reptiles are described with equal care, so the eye finds no resting place, and the design reads as a catalog of virtuosity. It is a polished decorative piece in the Dutch tradition, more admirable for stamina than for composition."},"met:437189":{"d":"A small oil-on-panel picture, about 25 by 20 cm, of the inside of a Gothic church at night. The records name arches and the church interior as the subject, so the building itself is the sitter: pointed arches and the long recession of space, translated into the darkness and local light of a night scene rather than the even daylight of most such pictures. It is a cabinet picture, made to be examined at arm's length, in the Antwerp specialty of architectural painting that Neeffs inherited from his family.","i":"Painting church interiors became an Antwerp specialty in the late sixteenth century, with Hendrick van Steenwyck the Elder and then the Neeffs family. The buildings were often real, Antwerp Cathedral above all, or composites rearranged for effect, and collectors prized the exact perspective. Night views let a painter show off candlelight and shadow. Figures in such pictures are usually small, and the pleasure lay in architecture and mood rather than in a story. Whether a particular church is meant here is not recorded.","c":"The appeal of this type is control: measured perspective and the contrast of darkness and light on a surface the size of a book page. Neeffs the Younger worked so close to his father's formula that the two are hard to tell apart, and the results show a practiced hand more than a restless eye. As a late Antwerp example the picture is a competent, atmospheric piece, though not one that changes the genre."},"met:442356":{"d":"A large horizontal still life, about 102 by 129 cm, in oil on canvas, gathering objects of luxury: silver and gold plate, a pitcher, shells and a sword. Everything recorded is costly or rare, chosen for its material, whether reflective metal, lustrous shell or the hard edge of a weapon. A picture of this kind is built as a display, with the objects arranged like a collector's treasures so that the painter can show how gold, silver and mother-of-pearl catch light. It dates from the later seventeenth century.","i":"Displays of precious plate, mounted shells and weapons belong to the luxury still life, the 'pronk' pictures developed by Dutch painters such as Willem Kalf and then taken up elsewhere. They flatter the owner's wealth and taste, and rare shells were prized collectors' items. Alongside the show of riches, such pictures often carry a quieter moral, that gold, silver and even a sword are the vanities of a mortal life. Conte worked in the south of France, so the picture records how a northern taste traveled.","c":"The subject sets a hard test: gold, silver and shell each take light differently, and success depends on keeping them apart. Conte, working in southern France, follows a formula developed in the Netherlands rather than inventing one of his own. As a record of taste and of how widely the luxury still life spread, the picture is valuable; as a design it is orthodox rather than daring. Its interest is more historical than visionary."},"nga:140376":{"d":"A wide panel in tones of beige, cream and pecan brown, with hints of shell pink and faint blue. The horizon sits about a quarter of the way up, leaving most of the surface to calm water and sky. Gentle ripples in the foreground reflect a pale blue sky and the blush pink of clouds. At left, two masted ships with furled sails lie alongside each other, while a smaller boat sails to the right and a few more are spaced along the waterway. Far off, the town stretches nearly the width of the panel, a thicket of masts with tiny windmills and slate-gray towers.","i":"Hoorn, on the Zuiderzee, was one of the busiest ports of the Dutch Republic and a seat of a chamber of the East India Company. A distant view of a town seen from the water was a popular type with Dutch buyers proud of their cities and trade, and the low horizon and wide sky are standard in the marine painting of the mid-seventeenth century. Verwer was a painter of ships and harbors, and the picture is probably a record of place as much as a seascape.","c":"The quiet is its achievement. A low horizon, pale, closely related tones and a town reduced to a thin line of masts and towers give an impression of wide, hazy space, and the ships are set out with restraint rather than heroics. Compared with the more dramatic seascapes of the next generation, it is modest and a little empty, but the tonal control is accomplished and the mood convincing."},"met:435663":{"d":"A lobster, a melon, grapes, peaches and lemons are heaped together with a candlestick on a tall wooden panel, nearly a meter high. This is a banquet still life, the luxurious kind the Dutch called pronk, or show, in which costly foods and vessels are set out on a table to be admired. The painting probably dates from the early 1650s, and its vertical format suits the stacked, piled arrangements typical of this kind of picture.","i":"Banquet pieces like this were prestige objects for prosperous Dutch homes, advertising the owner's taste and the wealth that trade brought in: lemons, melons and peaches were costly luxuries, and lobster a delicacy. At the same time, such pictures often carried a moral undertone. A candle burns down, fruit spoils, and the pleasure of the table is brief. Whether a given picture stresses display or warning is for the viewer to decide.","c":"Van Beyeren was among the most painterly of the Dutch still-life masters. Where Willem Kalf and Jan Davidsz. de Heem polished their surfaces, he preferred open, energetic strokes, and lobsters were one of his specialties. This panel, from the early part of his career, shows the ambition to fill a tall format with abundance. The effect is rich, and the handling keeps it alive."},"cma:136509":{"d":"A silver wine jug, a ham and a heap of fruit make up this large still life, about 100 by 83 centimeters. The jug is painted in rough strokes that look almost abstract up close but pull together, at a distance, into a gleaming object full of reflections; look closely and the artist's ghostly self-portrait at his easel appears in the metal. The museum suggests that such big, loosely brushed pictures were meant to be seen from a short way back, perhaps hung over a mantelpiece.","i":"Silver, ham and fruit signaled a rich table, and Dutch buyers prized such pictures as displays of wealth and of the painter's skill. The hidden self-portrait is a witty signature, a trick as old as the convex mirror in Jan van Eyck's Arnolfini portrait: the painter proves his mastery by hiding himself in a reflection. A viewer is invited to hunt for him, and the game doubles as a boast.","c":"Van Beyeren trusts paint more than most still-life painters of his day. Up close the jug dissolves into rough strokes; from a few steps back it shines. That is a real achievement, and the hidden self-portrait is a pleasing joke. The sheer weight of objects may tire some viewers, and the display of silver and meat is more feat than feeling, but few Dutch painters made metal look so alive."},"met:436198":{"d":"Saint Cecilia, the patron saint of music, is the subject of this oil painting, which includes a musical instrument, a book or score being read, an angel and a curtain. The canvas, about 122 by 104 centimeters, is a devotional picture of moderate size, suited to a chapel, a patron's room or a music-loving household. It is a work of the Flemish Baroque, by an Antwerp artist working in the wake of Rubens.","i":"Cecilia was a Roman martyr who became patron of music because her legend says that while instruments played at her wedding she sang in her heart to God alone. From the Renaissance on she is shown with an organ or another instrument, often with an angel as listener or partner. In Catholic Flanders, where music belonged to worship, such saints offered an image of devotion in which beauty of sound and purity of faith were one.","c":"Diepenbeeck was a capable, versatile painter, better known for stained-glass designs and book illustrations than for easel pictures, and his saints tend to be elegant and polished rather than intense. A Cecilia with an angel and a drawn curtain has the stagelike theatricality of Flemish Baroque devotional painting, pleasing as an ensemble but somewhat second-hand beside the original inventions of Rubens and Van Dyck."},"nga:43514":{"d":"Twelve men and perhaps a child, in tattered hooded robes of blue, brown, orange and gray, kneel, sit or prostrate themselves before an altar piled with three skulls. The man nearest the altar bows so deeply that his hood hides his face, holding a long cross almost to the skulls; another leans on a broken column base with bowed head. Four men at lower right, two chained and barefoot, read or kneel on steps where pink and blue robes lie. A heap of glinting objects, perhaps armor, lies at left. Oil on canvas, about 53 by 64 centimeters, c. 1733 to 1734.","i":"The subject is a meditation on mortality: penitents or hermits kneel before skulls, the emblem of death, having cast aside rich robes and what may be armor, signs of worldly power and wealth. The chains and bare feet mark their penance, and the broken column suggests the ruin of human things. The scene echoes the vanitas tradition, which in Counter-Reformation Catholicism encouraged believers to remember death.","c":"A sober, well-composed picture in which the dark, crowded group around the skull-laden altar has the weight of a Baroque sermon, and the loose brushwork gives the tattered robes a restless energy. Dandré-Bardon is a gifted but secondary painter, partly remembered as a teacher and theorist, and the learned allegory is stronger than the individual faces. As an early work it shows a taste for melancholy."},"met:437081":{"d":"A devotional panel, about 74 by 58 cm, painted in tempera and oil on wood, showing the Virgin and Child with the infant Saint John the Baptist. Mary, the Christ Child and the young John make up the family group, and a cross appears among the recorded elements, a sign of the Passion that the picture quietly foretells. In kind it is a panel for private devotion in a home or small chapel, made in northern Italy in the late 1490s, when oil was beginning to join traditional tempera.","i":"Saint John the Baptist was the cousin of Christ, and his presence turns a tender family scene into a statement about the Passion. As a child he is usually given a small reed cross, the sign of the sacrifice to come, since he would later point to Christ as the Lamb of God. Mary, as the one who offers her son to the viewer, is the picture's quiet center. Such panels were produced in great numbers in Italian workshops around 1500 for private prayer.","c":"Michele da Verona belongs to the second rank of the Veronese school, working in the shadow of the Venetian painters who had perfected this very subject. On that standard, a Madonna with the infant Baptist would be judged by the grace of its types, the softness of its modeling and the sweetness of expression, which is exactly where a provincial follower is measured against Bellini. Its interest is partly technical, as evidence of the shift from tempera toward oil."},"nga:130867":{"d":"A small oil sketch, only about 16 by 21 centimetres, shows a wooded glade. Tall trees almost hide the sky; at the centre cows graze, and at the right a person rests against a tree, apparently playing a flute. At the left a bright clearing in the distance reveals a tall statue, a herm, with people dancing around it, and water lies at the foot of the picture. The dark trees at the lower left set off the lit clearing, and white clouds show through a small gap in the foliage.","i":"A herm, a bust or figure of a god on a rectangular pillar, and a ring of dancers recall the rustic rites of antiquity, and they give this woodland pastoral a classical air. Jean-Victor Bertin belonged to the tradition of the historical landscape, taught by Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes, in which Arcadian scenes after Poussin and Claude were painted with careful, ordered composition; his pupils included Corot. The very small size suggests a sketch or a cabinet picture.","c":"Delicate, orderly and charming: the tall trees make a natural stage, and the bright clearing makes a lovely contrast to the dark foreground. Bertin's classicism may seem remote today, and the Arcadian ingredients, cows, flute, statue and dancers, are familiar to the point of formula. But the small scale gives it freshness, and the structured composition and clear tonal contrasts show what his pupil Corot learned from him."},"nga:130865":{"d":"Sunlight floods a landscape of trees, grassy hills and rocky cliffs in a small horizontal canvas, about 21 by 30 centimetres. At the lower right a shepherd in a mauve-purple tunic walks toward the viewer along a wide road paved with large ochre blocks, a staff on his shoulder, followed by a flock of more than a dozen sheep and two dogs. Two straight-trunked trees rise at the left, hills roll back to a stream, and a walled settlement crowns a cliff beneath hazy blue mountains and a pale sky.","i":"The shepherd with his flock is a timeless pastoral motif, drawn from ancient poetry such as Virgil's Eclogues, and the wide paving recalls an ancient Roman road, while a walled hill town is typical of the Italian countryside. Painters in Bertin's circle trained by sketching outdoors in Italy and then composed idealised landscapes from the studies. A picture of about 1820 like this combines observed forms with a classical sense of order and serenity.","c":"A modest, well-ordered landscape. The sunlight, the tidy trees, the clear road and the flock lead the eye gently into the distance, and the paint is thin and fresh. It lacks drama and its idealism is mild, but its calm and its structure are admirable. It shows the school that taught Corot, who would carry its clear tonal order into more personal landscapes. It is pleasing, if unadventurous."},"met:436502":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 2.4 by 1.7 meters and dated 1672, showing the Annunciation: the Archangel Gabriel announces to the Virgin Mary that she will bear the Son of God. The records list angels and lilies among its motifs, so heavenly attendants and the flower of purity accompany the two main figures. Its size suggests an altarpiece or a large devotional picture for a church or chapel, painted in Giordano's fluent Neapolitan Baroque manner, with figures meant to read clearly from a distance.","i":"The Annunciation (Luke 1:26-38) marks the moment the Incarnation begins, and painters kept to a fixed vocabulary: Gabriel arriving from one side, Mary interrupted at prayer or reading, a lily for her purity, and a dove or rays of light for the Holy Spirit. Catholic patrons of the seventeenth century favored Marian subjects, and an Annunciation of this scale would have presented her as the first to accept Christ. Which other attributes Giordano includes beyond the lilies and angels is not recorded here.","c":"Pictures like this show why Giordano was in demand: he could produce an Annunciation at altarpiece scale with ease, drawing on a stock of types from Ribera, Veronese and Pietro da Cortona. That ease is also his limit, and his nickname 'Luca fa presto' (Luke, work quickly) cuts both ways. The emotional pitch of the subject is likelier to be met competently than deeply. Among his work of 1672 it is representative, not a landmark."},"nga:72078":{"d":"A mythological scene on a nearly square canvas, about 1.5 by 1.6 meters, painted around 1675 to 1680. The title names the moon goddess Diana and the shepherd Endymion, and the subject is the legend in which she watches over his everlasting sleep. Paintings of the story usually show the goddess descending by night to the sleeping youth, with a crescent moon and attendant cupids, and the artist's task is to make a nocturnal meeting feel tender and luminous.","i":"Diana, the Roman moon goddess (identified with the Greek Selene), loved the beautiful shepherd Endymion. In most versions he was granted eternal youth in an endless sleep, so that she could visit him by night. The story was read as an allegory of love between mortal and divine, of the moon's visits to the earth, and of dreams. For Baroque patrons it supplied a respectable pretext for a sleeping male figure and a poetic nocturne. Which moment Giordano chose is not recorded here.","c":"Mythologies are where Giordano's facility reads best: a story with no doctrine to carry lets speed and fluency become charm. The risk is that grace edges into formula, with soft flesh and swirling drapery repeated from picture to picture. Within his output of the late 1670s, when his color was growing lighter, the picture is a representative and agreeable example rather than a singular one."},"cma:142293":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 2.4 by 2 meters, painted as a vision. Saint Francis stands at right beside the apparition of the Virgin and Child and motions toward a mother with three young infants, a symbolic figure of Charity. Close study shows that Giordano first painted Francis's hands reaching toward the Virgin and Child, perhaps toward the Infant Christ, and in the final version changed the gesture to point at Charity. The saint thus acts as a witness to the vision and an intermediary for humankind.","i":"Francis of Assisi (1181?-1226), founder of the Franciscan order, was famed for his visions and was said to have received the stigmata, bodily wounds echoing those of Christ. High Baroque painting made the visions and ecstasies of saints a major subject, and Francis was among the most popular. Here he appeals to the Virgin and Child for the sake of Charity, the virtue his order was known for, since he dedicated his life to helping the poor.","c":"The interest lies in the change of mind. By moving Francis's gesture from the Virgin and Child to Charity, Giordano turned a simple apparition into an argument about intercession and made the saint a go-between rather than a petitioner. The idea reads clearly from a distance, as a picture of this scale must, though the allegorical group is more diagrammatic than moving. Even a painter famed for speed stopped to rethink."},"met:436503":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 61.5 by 49 cm and dated 1701, showing the Flight into Egypt. The records list Mary, Joseph and the Christ Child, with an angel and a boat, so water is part of the journey and heaven watches over the family. A canvas of this size is a cabinet picture for private devotion, painted late in a career that had taken Giordano from Naples to Venice, Florence and Madrid.","i":"After Herod ordered the killing of the infants of Bethlehem, an angel warned Joseph in a dream to take Mary and the child to Egypt (Matthew 2:13-15). Painters used the flight to show the Holy Family as ordinary travelers, with Mary and the child on a donkey and Joseph leading, while angels watched over them, and later legends added miracles along the road. Baroque viewers saw in it a model of trust in providence, in a domestic form well suited to a small picture.","c":"Dated 1701, one of the last years of a long career, the small canvas shows how easily Giordano could reduce a familiar subject to its essentials. A modest scale suits his quick, fluent habits, which can seem slack in a large altarpiece and agreeable in a cabinet picture. It is an unpretentious devotional work, more charming than profound, and a reminder that his late religious pictures are often at their best when they ask least of him."},"met:436206":{"d":"An elderly man is the subject of this small portrait, oil on a wood panel of about 27 by 22 cm, dated 1882. The title gives no name, so it is probably a character study from a model rather than a commission: a study of age and character, made for its own sake and for the market that valued such pictures. Francisco Domingo y Marqués was a Valencian-born painter known for historical and genre scenes.","i":"Heads of old men were a staple of European painting from Rembrandt and Ribera onward: wrinkles, beard and weathered skin gave painters a chance to show close observation and to suggest wisdom or experience. In the 1880s such small panels were sought by collectors who enjoyed finished pictures by contemporary Spanish and French painters. The anonymous sitter stands for a type, the elder, rather than for a life that can be told.","c":"A small, confident panel that stakes its claim on observation: the lines of age, the texture of skin and beard. The Spanish tradition from Ribera and Velázquez of unflinching old men stands behind it, and the nineteenth-century version is more polished and less harsh. The danger of the type is sentiment, an old man turned into an image of gentle wisdom. It is skilled and agreeable rather than searching."},"met:436100":{"d":"A small panel, about 48 by 34 cm, painted in oil on wood in the early 1480s, showing the birth of Christ. The records list the Virgin Mary and the Christ Child with angels, men and animals, the usual company at the stable: shepherds, and the ox and ass that tradition places beside the manger. It is a devotional picture for private use, in the clear, finely detailed manner of early Netherlandish painting, made in the years around David's arrival in Bruges.","i":"Netherlandish painters followed Luke's account of the shepherds and the legends added to it, such as the ox and ass and the child lying on the ground in adoration, a version that owed much to the visions of Saint Bridget of Sweden. Gerard David came from Oudewater in the north and joined the painters' guild in Bruges in 1484; after the death of Hans Memling in 1494 he became the city's leading master. This panel comes from the early years of that career.","c":"An early, small work by an artist who would become the leading painter of Bruges. It belongs to the inherited tradition of Netherlandish devotional painting, in which careful finish and calm are the chief virtues, and it is best valued as evidence of where David began. It is less assured in conception than his mature altarpieces, but its scale and intimacy suit the subject, and the quiet confidence of the later work can be glimpsed in it."},"met:438385":{"d":"Two tall, narrow wings of a small triptych, recorded at about 46 by 17 cm, painted in oil on wood about 1485 to 1490. One shows Saint John the Baptist and the other Saint Francis receiving the stigmata, the wounds of Christ's Crucifixion marked on his body in a vision. As wings, they once flanked a central panel that is not part of this pair, folding closed to protect it and to set the owner's chosen saints beside the main devotional subject.","i":"John the Baptist, the forerunner who pointed out Christ as the Lamb of God, is usually shown in a rough camel-hair garment, often with a lamb. Francis of Assisi received the stigmata on Mount La Verna in 1224, when a vision of a winged seraph in the likeness of the crucified Christ left the wounds on his hands, feet and side. Saints on wings were generally chosen for the patron, so this pairing probably reflects the owner's own devotion or name saint, which is not recorded here.","c":"Narrow wing panels leave little room, so success depends on economy: a single figure, a landscape, a few exact details. David was well suited to this scale, being patient and precise by habit, and the subjects give him a plain, strong format. They are minor pieces by a major painter, interesting more for what they say about his early Bruges practice, and for the way a triptych could be assembled for private use, than for any single bold passage."},"met:436098":{"d":"A small panel, about 53 by 38 cm, painted in oil on wood about 1495, showing Christ on the cross. The records name the Virgin Mary, Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene among the mourners, with Saint Jerome also present, so the scene is a Crucifixion that doubles as a devotional gathering of saints. It is the type of picture a private patron or small chapel would have kept for contemplation, in the precise and calm manner associated with Bruges painting of the 1490s.","i":"The Crucifixion with the Virgin and John at its foot follows the Gospel of John (19:25-27), where Christ entrusts his mother to his disciple, and Mary Magdalene, the penitent follower, was also present. Jerome, the translator of the Bible and a hermit saint, was not at the scene, so his presence points to the patron's devotion, perhaps a name saint, and invites the viewer to join the mourning across time. The exact reading depends on details not recorded here.","c":"David's religious pictures are marked by restraint, with a quiet grief rather than theatrical anguish, and a small panel like this lets that discipline show. The gathering of saints beside the cross is conventional, and the interest lies in clarity and calm more than in invention. Viewers who want drama may find it reserved. As a work of the 1490s it shows an artist at ease with a well-tested format, doing it with the exactness his Bruges clients expected."},"met:436097":{"d":"A very small devotional panel, about 15.6 by 12.1 cm, in oil on wood, made around 1500. The subject is Christ taking leave of his mother before the Passion, an episode told not in the Gospels but in medieval devotional writing. Christ and the Virgin Mary are its figures, with parting and sorrow as the theme. A panel of this size was held and studied at close range for private prayer, and what it asks of the painter is a quiet, intimate pathos in very few figures.","i":"The farewell belongs to the late medieval habit of imagining the Passion in detail, in texts such as the Meditations on the Life of Christ, where it is the first moment of the sorrow to come: Christ, knowing what lies ahead, parts from Mary, who foresees it. The image invited the viewer to share her compassion, a form of prayer much encouraged around 1500. Its place in a larger sequence, if it had one, is not recorded here.","c":"At this scale a painter has room for little beyond gesture and expression, and David's habit of restraint is an asset, since quiet grief tends to read better in miniature than lamentation does. The subject is rarer than the Crucifixion or the Pietà, and that rarity, together with the care lavished on so small a panel, makes it more interesting than much routine Bruges devotion of the period."},"met:439327":{"d":"A tiny oil panel, only about 9 by 6 cm, showing Christ blessing as the Saviour of the World. The type, called Salvator Mundi, presents Christ facing the viewer with one hand raised in blessing and, in most versions, the other resting on a globe. Pictures of this size were made for private devotion and could be held in the hand or propped on a shelf. David painted the type in Bruges around 1500 to 1505.","i":"Christ as Salvator Mundi, ruler and savior of the world, was a popular devotional image in the Netherlands around 1500. In the usual formula the raised hand and the globe declare his authority over creation and his offer of redemption, and the frontal format makes a direct, personal address, an image to be looked at while praying. Whether this tiny panel was once part of a larger group, such as a diptych, is not recorded here.","c":"A work less than a hand's width across must do everything with a face and a gesture, and David's sober, frontal Christ belongs to a tradition more concerned with presence than with drama. The format is so small and the type so standard that the panel can be judged only by finish: the clarity of the head, the delicacy of the hand. A minor example by a major painter, interesting as an object more than as an invention."},"met:436096":{"d":"Two panels from a polyptych, dated 1506, on which the Annunciation is divided between its figures: the Archangel Gabriel brings the news, and the Virgin Mary receives it. A bed is among the recorded details, placing the event in a domestic chamber in the Netherlandish way, which treated sacred events as happening in a believable room. Annunciations split across two panels commonly flanked a central subject or formed the outer faces of wings.","i":"In the Annunciation, Gabriel tells Mary she will bear the Son of God (Luke 1:26-38). Netherlandish painters set the scene in a room furnished with everyday objects, many with symbolic overtones, and a bed has been read as an allusion to the chamber in which the Incarnation takes place and to the mystic marriage of God and humanity. Dividing angel and Virgin between wings keeps one encounter across a gap, so that the viewer must join the two halves.","c":"A dated work of 1506, from the maturity of an artist then at the head of the Bruges painters, it is the kind of quiet, reliable religious painting for which David was in constant demand: orderly, domestic and devout rather than dramatic. Splitting the angel from the Virgin across two panels is a neat idea that gives each figure its own space, though the single moment is then harder to take in at once."},"nga:50":{"d":"A small, nearly square panel, about 42 by 42 centimeters, in oil, painted about 1510 by Gerard David of Bruges. The title names the episode: the Holy Family pausing on the journey into Egypt, the moment when Mary tends the Christ Child. David's Rest pictures often set the family in a lush, orderly Netherlandish landscape, with Mary in a blue robe in the foreground.","i":"The flight into Egypt is told in Matthew's Gospel, but the rest on the journey comes from legend, and late medieval artists loved it as an intimate, domestic image of the Virgin, the Child and Joseph in a garden-like landscape. For viewers in Bruges it offered a quiet scene for private devotion, with a human tenderness absent from larger altarpieces. Details such as fruit or flowers, if present, would carry symbolism of the Passion or of Mary's virtue.","c":"Gerard David was the last great painter of Bruges's golden age, and his small devotional panels have a calm, sweet clarity, with soft landscapes and a gentle Virgin. The type and date suggest the polished, rather sleepy finish typical of his late style. It is an appealing but conservative picture, carrying Netherlandish traditions into the sixteenth century."},"met:436099":{"d":"A tall, narrow picture, about 90 by 31 cm, painted around 1510 to 1515 in oil on wood and later transferred to canvas. It joins the birth of Christ with portraits of the people who paid for it: the Christ Child appears with the ox and ass of tradition and an angel, together with the donors and their patron saints, Jerome, with his lion, and Leonard of Noblac. The narrow format suggests that it comes from a wing of a larger altarpiece.","i":"Donors in Netherlandish altarpieces were shown kneeling in prayer beside the sacred scene, often introduced by their patron saints, so the choice of Jerome and Leonard probably reflects the donors' own names. Jerome is identified by the lion of legend, whose paw he healed, and Leonard of Noblac, a Frankish hermit, is the patron of prisoners and is usually shown with shackles. Placing a family at the Nativity was a pious act that brought the living into the story.","c":"Combining a Nativity with living donors and their saints is characteristic of David's altarpiece manner, and the arrangement shows his skill in making the sacred and the contemporary share one space. The transfer from wood to canvas, a drastic remedy of an earlier age, makes the surface harder to judge, and some of the delicacy for which David is known may have been lost. It is better as a record of a type than as a showpiece."},"met:436101":{"d":"The Virgin sits on a rocky bank in a wide dark-blue mantle that spreads over the ground like a pool, a red shoe showing at its edge. Her eyes are lowered as she nurses the Christ Child, who lies against her lap in a thin, transparent shirt, one small hand held to her breast. A leafy sprig bearing small round fruit rests on the rock beside her. Behind rise tall, dense trees; at the left, a pale blue town with towers and hills lies in the distance. Deep in the shadows on the right, tiny figures show Mary on a donkey with Joseph walking beside her. It is a small oil painting on a wooden panel, smoothly finished, about 53 by 40 centimetres.","i":"Gerard David worked in Bruges and was a leading painter there around 1500. He tells the Flight into Egypt as one continuous story: the Holy Family appears twice, as small travellers emerging from the forest toward a town of Netherlandish towers, and as large, still figures at rest. The Met notes his awareness of Italian Renaissance ideas, seen in the pyramid formed by Mother and Child and in the use of light and shade to give them volume. The nursing Virgin, a type known as the Madonna lactans, often stressed Christ's humanity, and the fruit beside her may allude to the Fall and its redemption, though that reading is an inference.","c":"David's gift was calm. Nothing is hurried: the blue robe, the lowered eyes and the quiet trees keep the story of exile to a murmur, and the cool blue against the golden rock makes a clear, memorable chord of colour. The handling is exact without being fussy. What the panel lacks is the sharper psychology of Rogier van der Weyden; the Child is stiffly drawn, and the Virgin's face is serene to the point of blankness. As a small devotional picture for a private room, it does precisely what it set out to do."},"met:436102":{"d":"The Virgin, in a red mantle that pools on the floor over a blue gown, her hair loose, holds the naked Christ Child inside an arched stone porch with columns. Two angels in pale blue and violet robes, with multicolored wings, hover above and hold a golden crown over her head. At the foot of the porch, on a tiled floor, an angel in green plays a small harp at left and an angel in blue plays a lute at right. Through the opening behind lies an enclosed garden, where a tiny monk reads, and beyond it the brick towers and roofs of a city under a blue sky.","i":"Gerard David was the leading painter in Bruges around 1500, and he followed the legacy of Jan van Eyck. The Metropolitan Museum says he took the Virgin and Child from Van Eyck's well-known Virgin and Child at the Fountain but updated the setting, placing the figures in a grand porch with Italianate capitals, against the skyline of the Bruges of his day. The reading monk in the garden is a Carthusian, which suggests a link to the Genadedal charterhouse outside the city. The crown shows Mary as Queen of Heaven, and the enclosed garden is a traditional symbol of her virginity. At about 63 by 39 centimeters, it is the size of a picture for private prayer.","c":"David's quiet, polished manner is at its best here: the tender, slightly sorrowful face of the Virgin, the glossy red robe, and a clear, orderly space where porch, garden and city fit together without crowding. The invention, however, is mostly borrowed, since the central pair derive from Van Eyck with a few extra angels, and the musicians are decorative rather than expressive. The picture rewards close looking more than a first glance. It shows why David is admired as a craftsman and refiner of the Bruges tradition rather than as an innovator."},"nga:1154":{"d":"An older man in a richly brocaded robe and a gold mitre studded with pearls and gems stands under a rounded arch before a deep landscape. He faces right, his deeply lined face with light brown eyes, thick brows, a long nose and a jowly neck rendered with unsparing detail. He wears a long white garment under a scarlet and gold robe whose bands show angels, an eagle and other objects, and blue gloves with gold rings. He raises two fingers in blessing and holds a crozier with a spiraling gold top. Trees, gray towers and distant blue hills lie beyond. Oil on panel, about 234 by 75 centimeters.","i":"Saint Nicholas, bishop of Myra in the fourth century, was one of the most venerated saints, patron of sailors, merchants and children, and is shown in bishop's vestments with a crozier and a blessing hand. The embroidered bands on his robe, with angels and scenes, display the goldsmith's and weaver's art that wealthy churches commissioned. He stands as the left wing beside the central group of Saint Anne, forming a single altarpiece with Saint Anthony of Padua.","c":"The head is the great success: the old man's lined, jowly face, with its stubble and the patient look of a long life, is as sharp an observation as any in David's work, and it enlivens the glittering vestments. The brocade and gems are painted with almost a goldsmith's delight. The pose is static, as befits a wing of an altarpiece, but the portrait-like head gives the saint a human presence."},"nga:1155":{"d":"The central panel of a large altarpiece, about 233 by 96 centimeters, in oil on panel, showing Saint Anne with the Virgin and Child, painted about 1500 to 1520 and attributed to Gerard David, whose Bruges workshop produced many altarpieces. Its tall, narrow shape matches the two wing panels showing Saint Nicholas and Saint Anthony of Padua, which stand beside it as one triple altarpiece.","i":"Saint Anne, the mother of the Virgin, was a hugely popular saint in the late Middle Ages, and the image of Anne with her daughter and grandson expressed the family descent of Christ and the dignity of motherhood. Altarpieces dedicated to her were commissioned by guilds, confraternities and wealthy individuals. The flanking saints, Nicholas the bishop and Anthony of Padua, may have been the patrons' own protectors.","c":"A grand, stately altarpiece in David's late manner: calm, clear and decorative, with tall figures under arches that lend them a sculptural dignity. At this scale the picture needs a viewer standing before it, and its virtues are grace and clarity rather than drama. The altarpiece as a whole is a substantial work of the Bruges tradition at the end of its flowering."},"nga:1156":{"d":"A clean-shaven man in a dark gray monk's robe tied with a rope stands under a rounded arch, his dark hair cut in a ring around his head and his bare feet on a pink and blue tiled floor. He holds a wooden cross in one hand and, in the other, an open red-bound book with gold-edged pages, on which a small nude child with blond hair sits upright, hands together in prayer. He looks down and to the left. Beyond the arch royal blue iris grow beside a lawn that runs to a lake, a white tower and distant hills. Oil on panel, about 234 by 74 centimeters.","i":"Saint Anthony of Padua, a Franciscan friar who died in 1231 and was canonized within a year, was a famous preacher; the Christ Child seated on his book recalls a vision in which the child appeared to him. The cross and book are signs of his preaching, and the child expresses the intimacy with Christ that the order cultivated. The iris at his feet are sometimes read as flowers of the Virgin and of sorrow.","c":"A quiet, tender wing in which the Franciscan's downcast look and the praying Child create a moment of private devotion. The landscape through the arch is cool and lovely, and the royal blue iris provide a beautiful accent of color. As a standing saint it is conventional in pose, but the details, the red binding of the book, the rope belt, the bare feet, are exact. It completes the altarpiece's decorative symmetry with Saint Nicholas."},"met:436103":{"d":"A small panel, about 25 by 21 cm, in oil on wood, from about 1520, showing the Virgin nursing the Christ Child, the type known as the Virgo lactans or nursing Madonna. Mary suckles her son, an image of tenderness and motherhood. Catalogued under Gerard David, the picture is also attributed to Simon Bening, the Bruges illuminator, so its authorship is open to debate. Intimate panels of this kind were made in Bruges for private prayer.","i":"The nursing Virgin emphasizes Christ's humanity: the Son of God is fed at his mother's breast, which is why theologians valued the image as proof of the Incarnation and why it was favored in private devotion. It also presents Mary as nourisher of the faithful, a figure of compassion and intercession. The theme had a medieval pedigree, and Netherlandish painters often made it domestic by placing the pair in a room or garden.","c":"If the Bening attribution is right, the picture is a rare panel by a manuscript illuminator, and its qualities should be judged against miniature painting, with its fine finish and small scale. If it is David's, it is a late, modest devotional work. Either way the type is gentle and unambitious, and the panel's appeal rests on intimacy and care of execution rather than on invention."},"met:436104":{"d":"A nearly square panel, about 70.5 by 73 cm, in oil on wood, from about 1520, showing the Adoration of the Magi. Mary with the Christ Child and Saint Joseph receive the visiting kings and their men, and a dog also appears among the figures. The picture is recorded as a work of David's workshop, which produced many versions of this popular subject, so it should be seen as a product of the studio tradition rather than one artist's single invention.","i":"The Magi, three kings from the East guided by a star, bring gold, frankincense and myrrh to the infant Christ (Matthew 2:1-12). Later tradition made them the three ages of man and the three known continents, so their homage became that of the whole world. The Epiphany was a favorite with wealthy patrons, who could see themselves in the kings' rich costumes. Dogs in such scenes are sometimes read as signs of fidelity, though here the dog may simply be a household touch.","c":"Workshop versions of a successful composition show how David's studio met demand. The schema is reliable, with a kneeling king, a standing retinue and a calm Holy Family, and the execution is competent but probably less searching than autograph work. Its value is as an example of how Bruges workshops produced devotional pictures to a steady standard of finish. It cannot rank with David's finest, yet it is a clear and agreeable image."},"met:436727":{"d":"A small, nearly square panel, about 33 by 37 cm, in tempera on wood with embossed and gilt ornament, showing episodes from the life of King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon. Kings, soldiers and other men are among the recorded figures, so several moments of the story are probably gathered into one picture. The raised, gilded ornament and the modest size point to a decorative function, perhaps as part of a larger ensemble for a room or piece of furniture, rather than as an altar picture.","i":"Nebuchadnezzar appears in the Book of Daniel as the mighty king of Babylon who dreams of a great statue, orders three young men into the fiery furnace, and is finally humbled by a period of madness until he acknowledges God. Stories of rulers undone by pride made improving subjects for Renaissance patrons. Which episodes are shown here is not recorded, though the soldiers may point to war and conquest. The panel's date is not recorded here.","c":"The picture is best valued as a rarity. Old Testament stories about Nebuchadnezzar were far less often painted than the Gospels, and the union of tempera painting with raised and gilded ornament is a reminder of how much Italian provincial art served furnishing and display. Without a famous name or a documented setting, it is modest as drawing and design, and its interest lies in evidence of local taste, not in any breakthrough."},"met:437775":{"d":"A small panel, about 16 by 22 cm, in oil on wood, painted after 1810, showing a billiard room. Men, a dog and the interior are recorded, so the scene is a social one: a gathering around the table in a French room of the early nineteenth century, treated as a cabinet picture for close viewing. Taunay, who also painted battles and landscapes, here turns his small, lively figure style to a fashionable pastime.","i":"Billiards had been a gentleman's amusement in France since the seventeenth century, and by the Empire and Restoration it was a fixture in the salons and country houses of the well-to-do. Such pictures record a leisure habit with few overt morals, and the table offers painters a natural pretext for grouped figures and incidental comedy. Taunay, who painted Napoleonic scenes and later traveled to Brazil with the French artistic mission, was a keen observer of small events.","c":"Taunay's small figure pictures are most successful when observation outruns elegance, and a billiard room, with its shared concentration, cues and bending postures, gives a painter plenty to watch. The risks of the type are anecdote and thinness. As an early nineteenth-century record of daily leisure it is a pleasant minor work, much less ambitious than his battle pictures and views, but it shows that he was a painter of social life as well."},"nga:71025":{"d":"Hulking mountains stand mostly backlit against a watery blue sky edged with shell pink at the horizon. In the foreground, light falls across felled and gnarled trees; two thin trunks, one broken to a jagged point, rise at the left. The land drops steeply into a valley where fog lies like a river of mist, and the mountains lighten from dark earthy green and brown to pale fawn in the distance. A sliver of yellow sun appears over the nearest summit. Oil on canvas, about 65 by 90 centimeters, signed and dated T. Cole 1826 on a branch.","i":"Cole, an English-born painter who had settled in the United States, began painting the Catskills after a sketching trip in 1825, and his early views of them helped found what is now called the Hudson River School. Dawn, mist and untouched mountains present American wilderness as sublime, almost sacred ground. The broken and felled trees at the front can be read as nature's violence and perhaps the settler's axe, a theme Cole voiced in his later writing on American scenery.","c":"An early Cole, and a vivid one: the drama of light on mist and mountain is real, the foreground wreckage is memorably tangled, and the whole feels freshly seen. The sublime formula, with its dark foreground and glowing distance, is a little tidy, but the picture states at an early moment the vision of wild American nature on which later landscape painting built."},"nga:195574":{"d":"Two thick, twisting tree trunks, splintered and stripped of leaves, stand left of center in a dark, wooded landscape under a nearly black sky. They are the brightest objects in the picture, lit by a break in the clouds at the upper right. Behind them more trees form a screen, and taller trees in the distance bend almost horizontally in the wind. Only on close inspection does a small figure appear near the base of the right-hand trunk, hair blown back and navy jacket lifted by the gale. Oil on canvas, about 118 by 164 centimeters, in coffee brown, smoke gray and olive green.","i":"Cole presents the American forest at the height of a storm, in the tradition of the sublime, where grandeur and terror are joined. The tiny figure beside the great trunks sets human scale against natural force, and the break in the clouds may hint at survival or renewal. Cole wrote and lectured on American wilderness as a source of awe, and early pictures such as this one show wild nature as a power rather than a backdrop for settlement.","c":"The drama is real: the lit, writhing trunks against a black sky read at once, and the tiny figure turns spectacle into peril. The near-monochrome palette and heavy darks edge toward theater, which is the risk of the sublime. Within Cole's career it is the clearest statement of wild nature as an overwhelming force, made before he turned to allegory in the later 1830s."},"met:10500":{"d":"A small landscape on canvas, about 37.5 by 59 cm, painted in 1832 after Cole's travels in Italy. Trees, ruins and mountains are listed, so the scene is the classic Italian prospect near Tivoli: old stonework among trees before distant hills, with the title naming the hour as morning. It is a cabinet picture rather than a large exhibition piece, in which Cole turned drawings and memories of Italy into poetic landscape.","i":"Tivoli, the hill town east of Rome with its ancient temples and waterfalls, had drawn landscape painters since Claude Lorrain. Cole, born in England and brought to America as a teenager, was in Italy in 1831 and 1832 and learned from Claude the glow of light and the framing of a view with trees and ruins. Ruins spoke to him of the passing of empires, a theme he pursued in later series. The 'Morning' in the title names the hour, as Claude often did.","c":"Cole inherits Claude's formula of framing trees, ruins and receding hills, and the interest lies in how an American painter, fresh from the Hudson, tests his Romantic manner against Europe's classical landscape. At this size the picture is modest and probably freer than the large exhibition canvases, with light and air in place of epic drama. It is a graceful Italian view whose debt to Claude is plain, valuable for showing what Cole carried home."},"met:10499":{"d":"A giant stone cup rises on a thick stem from a rocky headland, high above a wide, misty plain. Its rim, covered with green growth, holds a lake with tiny boats and buildings. Threads of water spill over the lip and fall down the bowl to a ring-shaped basin below, then over the cliffs. Beyond the headland a jagged peak and layered blue-grey mountains fade into a peach-coloured sky, where a low sun sends rays across the haze. The foreground at the left is a steep, brown, craggy slope, and at the lower right a lake lies below the cliffs, with a few tiny buildings at the water's edge. The picture is small, less than fifty centimetres high.","i":"This fantasy, painted in 1833, is among Cole's most unusual works, and the Metropolitan Museum says it defies full explanation. It comes between his European tour of 1829–32 and his five-part Course of Empire, and shares that cycle's interest in civilisations that rise and fade. Interpretations range from a Norse world-tree to a classical story of an architect who proposed carving a mountain into a giant figure holding a bowl; a Dutch still life with a goblet in the collection of Cole's patron Luman Reed has also been suggested. None is certain. The painting was given to the Museum by Samuel P. Avery Jr. in 1904 and was included in the Museum of Modern Art's 1936 exhibition of fantastic art, Dada and Surrealism.","c":"The Titan's Goblet is the odd one out among Cole's paintings: small, sudden, without an announced moral. Its virtue is conviction. The cup is painted with the same patient attention as his realistic views, so the impossible object seems real, and the hazy distance is among the most delicate things he did. As an idea it is cleverer than it is moving; the tiny ships and towns are charming, but the central symbol stays unclear, and the picture remains a puzzle, not an argument. That it later appealed to the Surrealists is unsurprising."},"met:10497":{"d":"A wide river valley opens below a steep, wooded hilltop. At the left a thunderstorm is still passing, its dark cloud and slanting rain hanging over the ridge, while a shattered tree and a broken trunk twist up through the foreground. At the right the cloud breaks into sunlit white masses, and the Connecticut River loops in a great oxbow through farmland laid out in pale yellow and green patches. Distant blue hills close the view. The eye moves from the ragged, shadowed slope across the river bend to the horizon. Near the lower left, among the rocks, a very small painter sits at his easel: Cole himself.","i":"Cole painted the view from Mount Holyoke, near Northampton, Massachusetts, for the National Academy of Design's exhibition of 1836, where it was sold to the New York merchant Charles N. Talbot. Wilderness on one side and cultivated land on the other made an obvious picture of America's change from forest to farm, and Cole's own writing of the period mourns the loss of the forest. Yet he also admired settled country, and historians still disagree about which way the picture leans. It was made while he was finishing The Course of Empire, his five-picture cycle on the rise and fall of an imaginary civilisation, and carries a related thought in a single view. It reached the Metropolitan Museum as a gift in 1908.","c":"The Oxbow succeeds as staging. The storm and the sunlit plain divide the canvas on a diagonal, and the river bend gives the eye a place to rest. The moral reading is less certain: the right half is so inviting, and the left so theatrically gloomy, that the picture can pass for an advertisement for farmland as easily as a lament for wilderness. The foreground trees are stiff beside the airy distance, and the tiny self-portrait is more a signature than a presence. Even so, it states clearly what Cole wanted American landscape painting to do."},"met:20639":{"d":"A small study, about 14 by 24 cm, in oil and pencil on composition board, made in 1836 in preparation for Cole's large painting of the Connecticut River valley seen from Mount Holyoke after a thunderstorm. Trees and a river are recorded: the river's great loop, the oxbow, cuts through the valley below. Working in pencil and oil together, Cole fixed the broad arrangement of sky, trees and valley before undertaking the finished canvas.","i":"The oxbow of the Connecticut River, seen from Mount Holyoke, gave Cole the chance to set wilderness against cultivation, with the storm-darkened height on one side and the farmed valley in sunlight on the other. In the finished painting a tiny painter at an easel appears in the foreground, generally taken to be Cole himself. The picture reflects a time of rapid settlement and Cole's uneasy view of what clearing the land meant. The sketch is where the idea was first tested.","c":"A sketch is judged differently from a finished picture, and its virtues are speed and the sense of the whole. The idea behind The Oxbow, a wide valley held between a darkened sky and a bright plain, is strong enough to be read at this size, which says much about Cole's gift for large, legible compositions. The study lacks the finished picture's detail and allegorical program, but it shows how the design was built."},"cma:136911":{"d":"A wide landscape, about 1 by 1.6 meters, showing Florence in the light just before sunset. Cole made a small pencil sketch of the vista in 1831 and, back in his New York studio six years later, transformed it into this large oil painting, adding picturesque figures and goats to the foreground. A monk among those foreground figures is the one Henry James, who grew up with the painting, called his 'constant friend'.","i":"Cole showed the Florence view in 1837 beside a painting of the Catskills, a deliberate comparison meant to prove that he had mastered both the Old World and the New. Italy mattered to American painters as the source of the landscape tradition, and peasants, goats and a monk gave the scene the picturesque flavor that European views were expected to have. Henry James remembered the monk as a companion of his childhood.","c":"The large Florentine view shows Cole's method: an Italian memory reworked in New York six years later, with a warm, sunset-lit calm that contrasts with the wilder Catskill scenes. It is more an exercise in picturesque effect than in the allegory of his larger series. The foreground figures and goats are conventional, and the picture's real interest is the poised comparison with its American companion. It is competent and attractive, though not Cole at his most original."},"met:10501":{"d":"A wide landscape, about 1 by 1.6 meters, painted in 1836 to 1837, showing the Catskill Mountains in early autumn. The records list signs of human presence in the foreground: a picnic and a woman, a longboat, a rifle, a fence and a tree stump, set among trees with the mountains beyond. Its size matches that of Cole's View of Florence, which he exhibited in 1837 beside a Catskill picture, so it was probably made as the American companion to that Italian view.","i":"Cole, a founder of the Hudson River School, saw the Catskills as home ground where wilderness and settlement met. The stump, fence and rifle can be read as marks of farming and hunting advancing on the woods, while the picnic and the woman add leisure and domestic calm, and early autumn adds a note of the turning year, a favorite Cole theme. As the probable companion of the Florence view, it argued that American scenery could stand beside Italy.","c":"As the probable companion to the Florence picture, this canvas is plainly a statement piece, and its virtue is a convincing sense of home ground: a recognizable place, observed with attention to season and detail, with Cole's moralizing held in check. The stump and the rifle suggest he could not resist a gentle comment, but the picture still works as landscape first. It is quieter and more truthful than his visionary canvases."},"nga:166438":{"d":"Seen from a height and a distance, honey-gold evening sun falls across copper-colored treetops and a stone church with tall stained-glass windows and ornate spires. Along the path in front, four men in togas, three of them wearing breastplates and helmets, carry an armored man on a red-draped stretcher; a white horse with an empty saddle follows, and a spear-bearing rider comes behind. A procession leaves the church, a river winds under an arched bridge, and mauve mountains close the horizon. Oil on canvas, about 100 by 161 centimeters, dated 1837.","i":"The Return is the companion to The Departure, painted in the same year. There the knights ride out through green country; here one comes back, borne on a stretcher with his riderless horse behind, in an autumn evening. The empty saddle suggests he returns dead or badly wounded. The pair can be read as a romantic allegory of ambition and its cost, with the church and the wayside shrine, a haloed figure on a plinth shown in both pictures, offering consolation.","c":"The Return is the more somber and the richer of the pair: the color deepens to copper and honey, and the funeral cortege moves through a landscape that seems to share its mood. As narrative it is quite literal, and the crowd of incident, from goats and birds to sundial and bridge, can dilute the central loss. Still, the pair is among Cole's most ambitious pictures before The Voyage of Life, and The Return has the stronger emotional pitch."},"nga:166439":{"d":"In a verdant landscape, eighteen small armored knights with long spears ride toward the viewer along a bridge and path, led by a knight on a white horse who wears a ruby-red cloak and a helmet with a red plume. Behind them a crenelated castle crowns a tree-lined hill before a high craggy peak, a waterfall tumbling near its drawbridge. In the foreground a bearded pilgrim with a palm-topped staff raises a hand and the lead horse shies. A wayside shrine shows a haloed woman with a baby. Oil on canvas, about 100 by 162 centimeters, dated 1837.","i":"Cole's pair of 1837 sets the knights' confident departure against their return. The bearded pilgrim, with a palm frond, a cross and a string of red beads, raises his hand as if to warn or bless them, and the shying lead horse is a conventional omen. The shrine's haloed woman and child, presumably the Virgin and Child, invite a religious reading of chivalry. The scene belongs to the romantic taste for the Middle Ages and carries a moral about ambition and its price.","c":"A picture made to be read as much as seen. Cole fills the vista with incident, from the shying horse to the distant waterfall, and keeps the knights small enough that the landscape is the true protagonist. The result is clear and attractive, if a little storybook in its medievalism, and it works best as the bright first half of a pair whose meaning is only completed by The Return."},"cma:93014":{"d":"A wide view of an Adirondack peak seen after a storm, about 1 by 1.6 meters, painted in 1838. Cole sketched the scene in early summer, but in his studio he rendered it in a dramatic blaze of fall colors. Two Indigenous men stand among the foliage in the right foreground. Cole called the mountain Schroon Mountain, and its official name is Hoffman Mountain. The canvas is a large exhibition landscape, built to impress with scale, color and weather rather than to record one day's light.","i":"Two years before this picture, Cole's Essay on American Scenery declared, 'We are still in Eden,' and he called autumn the one season in which the American forest surpasses the whole world in gorgeousness. The blaze of fall color is therefore likely a patriotic choice. The two Indigenous men reflect the region's real inhabitants, Algonquian and Iroquoian peoples who kept hunting and fishing there while adapting to settlement, lumbering, mining and tourism.","c":"Cole's wilderness is a deliberate heightening: color invented in the studio from a summer sketch gives the picture both its patriotism and its theatricality. The effect is grand, though a little staged. The two Indigenous figures are small and placed in the foliage, where they confirm the idea of an Eden more than they complicate it. For modern viewers its interest lies in what the staging chooses to include as much as in its scenic ambition."},"nga:50728":{"d":"A faint drawing on a narrow wooden panel, about 36 by 17 centimeters, showing the outline of a domed building. The representation is minimal: the dome and some vertical lines that may indicate columns are drawn in pale yellow lines on a reddish-brown surface, which is also marked with small scratches and lines. It is a working sketch for an architectural scheme, not a finished picture, dated about 1838, and it is the work of Thomas Cole, better known as a landscape painter.","i":"The title connects the drawing to the design for the Ohio State Capitol in Columbus, a project on which Cole is known to have worked as an architect as well as a painter. In 1830s America, with public buildings modeled on Greek temples and Roman domes, such a design signaled a democracy that took its form from antiquity. How far this sketch matches the building as designed or built is not stated, and it is best read as a private study of an idea.","c":"As a work of art this is slight, and it matters chiefly as evidence of Cole's wide ambitions: he saw himself as a designer of buildings as well as of landscapes. The faint, scratchy lines are more notation than drawing, and they reveal little about the dome's proportions. Its interest is documentary, a glimpse of the artist thinking in architecture, and it should not be judged beside his paintings."},"nga:82649":{"d":"Seen across a rocky, grass-covered promontory, a crumbling round brick tower overlooks topaz-blue water. Golden sunlight warms the ivy-grown tower and throws the steep outcrop below into shadow. A young shepherd in a ginger-brown toga sits with his back to the viewer on pale stones that may be fragments of a larger building, watching about two dozen sheep and several goats. Three rocky outcrops rise from the still sea, with a tiny ship near the middle one, and a low white moon hangs among lavender clouds. Oil on canvas, about 86 by 117 centimeters, dated 1838.","i":"Cole worked in Italy in the early 1830s, and he returned to its landscape in the studio for years afterward. The ruined tower, the shepherd and the broken stones are the stock signs of a vanished antique world, a reminder of mortality in a pastoral setting, and they sit close to the thinking behind his cycle The Course of Empire. The picture is classed as a capriccio, an imagined scene, and is better read as a mood than as a particular place.","c":"A tranquil, elegiac picture whose strengths are warm light on weathered stone and a sea that reflects the clouds with careful stillness. The theme is conventional: shepherd, ruin and sea were already stock items of the landscape tradition descended from Claude, and Cole adds little new beyond a moral weight borrowed from The Course of Empire, softened here to reverie. Pleasing rather than searching."},"nga:50727":{"d":"Across a wide, low clearing, a massive mountain towers beyond rocky hills of pines and blazing red and orange autumn foliage, its jagged face mottled cinnamon and rust, with pewter-gray storm clouds circling the peak. The clearing is littered with dead trees and stumps; a line of broken trunks crosses the foreground and a silvery pond lies to the left. Tiny in the landscape, a horseman gallops along a road toward a white house, where two children and a white dog walk to meet him, and a carriage moves away in the distance. Oil on canvas, about 102 by 156 centimeters, dated 1839.","i":"Crawford Notch, a narrow pass in New Hampshire's White Mountains, drew artists and tourists in Cole's day, and it had been notorious since the Willey family died there in a landslide in 1826. Cole shows the pass as a road of settlement entering wilderness. The dead trees and stumps record the clearing of the land, while the sheer mountain and the approaching storm show the wilderness holding its place against it.","c":"A large, ambitious landscape in which Cole balances grandeur and unease. The autumn color and the mountain are splendid, the storm clouds near the peak add drama, and the stumps in the foreground record the cost of settlement. The tiny horseman and the house give the human measure. The panoramic spread asks for patience, but few American landscapes of 1839 hold wilderness and settlement in such a tense balance."},"nga:52450":{"d":"A nude baby sits in a golden boat on a bed of pink and white flowers, holding up handfuls of blooms, while a winged angel in a white robe stands behind with a hand on the tiller. The boat, formed of clustered golden angels and carrying a figure at the bow who holds an hourglass, has just glided out of a dark cave in rose-pink cliffs onto a glassy river. Flowers dot the banks, the distance turns mauve, and a golden glow lights the horizon. Oil on canvas, about 134 by 195 centimeters, signed and dated 1842 in Rome.","i":"The Voyage of Life is a four-part allegory of human existence, with Childhood as the first age, the river as time and the angel as the guardian who accompanies the traveler. The cave from which the boat emerges stands for birth, and the dawn light, flowers and angel promise a world that has not yet shown its dangers. The message, that faith guides every stage, was meant for a wide public, and the series circulated widely in engravings.","c":"The series has its sentimental side, with a plump infant and a stagey angel, and its symbols are explained with Victorian thoroughness. Yet Childhood has real invention in its pictorial logic: the boat that rises from the cave, the river that opens like a promise, the golden light ahead. Cole turns an allegory into a landscape one can believe in, which is why the sequence outlived the taste for such moral pictures."},"nga:52451":{"d":"A young man in a red and gold tunic sets out in an ornate golden boat toward a white, semi-transparent palace that looms above a barren red mountain in the upper left. The river winds from the bottom right across a lush landscape and disappears into the distance. At the bow, a winged golden figure holds an hourglass above her head. In the foreground, a winged, haloed angel with long golden hair stands under a tall palm on the riverbank, one hand lifted toward the boat. Oil on canvas, about 134 by 195 centimeters, dated 1842.","i":"In the second stage of the allegory the child has become a young man who steers for himself, while the guardian angel watches from the bank. The airy palace in the clouds is usually read as an image of ambition and worldly dreams, built of cloud and out of reach of the river's actual course. The youth looks ahead and away from his guide, and the gentle river will grow dangerous in the next canvas, so the beauty carries a warning.","c":"The brightest of the four in color and fantasy, and the one where Cole's landscape talent is least constrained by allegory. The meadows, the hazy mountain and the pale palace create a convincing dream-space. The young man himself is small and generic, and the symbolism is easily decoded, but the optimism it conveys, made poignant by what the next canvases hold, is genuinely persuasive."},"nga:52452":{"d":"A man with a dark beard and brown hair, in a crimson tunic, kneels with clasped hands, looking up, in a small golden boat that is rushing toward whitewater rapids between jagged, moss-covered rocks. A winged figurehead at the bow holds an hourglass. A blasted, barren tree twists up in the foreground, and a deep mauve-pink sky carries streaks of falling rain, with bands of golden yellow near the horizon. In the upper left a glowing figure with a star at the forehead looks down, and three bearded faces are hidden in the clouds. Oil on canvas, about 134 by 203 centimeters, dated 1842.","i":"Manhood is the third stage of the allegory: the river now runs toward rapids, the traveler prays, and the guardian angel is only a distant glow in the sky, watching but not helping. The faces hidden in the clouds are usually read as the temptations and terrors that beset adult life. The scene was meant to show that maturity tests faith and that trouble is answered by prayer, in a moral pattern familiar to the period's viewers.","c":"Manhood is the stormiest and most theatrical of the series, and the sublime Cole of Tornado in an American Forest returns in the blasted tree and the churning river. It works as drama, though the explanatory apparatus, clouds with faces and a glowing guardian, can feel overstated, and the kneeling figure is a stock gesture of piety. The landscape does more than the man. In sequence, it supplies the crisis that gives the first two canvases their poignancy."},"nga:52453":{"d":"A balding old man with a fringe of white curls and a long white beard, in a crimson tunic, sits in a golden boat with palms raised, looking toward a shaft of light that pierces a billowing bank of clouds at the upper left. A winged angel with long golden hair, a flowing white tunic and lilac wings floats above him and gestures to the light, where another angel descends. The boat, made of wings and human bodies, has lost its rudder and figurehead; it glides from low jagged rocks onto placid water. Oil on canvas, about 133 by 196 centimeters, dated 1842.","i":"The last of the four ages shows the boat damaged, its rudder and figurehead broken off, yet it reaches calm water. The old man's raised hands suggest acceptance, and the descending angels announce that his voyage is leading to eternal life, the Christian answer toward which the whole series builds. The guardian angel, far off in Manhood, now returns, so the story closes in reconciliation. The message was meant to console viewers facing their own mortality.","c":"Old Age is the quietest canvas and the hardest to paint. Cole gives it a calm gradation of light from yellow to plum and lets the clouds do the work, with concentric rings that are an original and beautiful device. The result is serene rather than moving, since the angels are conventional and the old man, palms raised, is more emblem than person. As the conclusion of the cycle it works."},"met:21693":{"d":"A small study of clouds in oil on paper, later laid down on canvas, about 22 by 28 cm, made around 1838. No story is told and no place is named: the subject is the sky itself, and the forms of the clouds are the only theme. Studies of this kind were painted quickly to record the light and structure of a sky and were kept in the studio as reference for finished landscapes, following a practice established by European painters.","i":"Cloud studies grew out of the Romantic wish to observe nature directly and out of the belief that skies were not mere backdrops but carriers of mood. Painters kept them as stores of effects to draw on later, and clouds were readily associated with the sublime and with the divine, which mattered to a painter as devout as Cole. The sheet is not a message but a note of attention, and a reminder that much of a landscape painter's work lay in looking.","c":"As a study it should be judged on speed and truth, and the type rewards loose, direct handling and a feeling for the volume of cloud. Cole is better known for large compositions and allegorical series, and a sheet like this shows a different side of him: observation without program. Its charm is freedom. It asks nothing of the viewer and gives a clear note of weather, which is exactly what such a sketch is for."},"nga:106485":{"d":"A small oil study, about 30 by 46 centimeters, of a vast landscape with the horizon in the lower third. A range of dark brown and purple hills crosses the whole width, and rolling ground in front, painted in thick strokes of deep brown and green, suggests trees and bushland. Above, soft clouds glow orange and pink in a sky that passes from warm tones to soft blues and grays. It is a preparatory study on wood by Thomas Cole for a larger picture of Catskill Creek, made about 1844 or 1845.","i":"Catskill Creek runs into the Hudson at the village of Catskill, where Cole lived and worked, so the landscape was his home ground. Oil studies on wood or paper let him fix the effect of an evening sky and the main masses of land before composing a finished picture in the studio. The study is a working document, not a public statement, and its freedom of handling is partly the result of that private purpose.","c":"Studies like this often show a painter at his most direct, and the warm sky over dark earth has a spontaneity that finished pictures can lose. The thick, rich browns and greens are handled broadly, and the whole is held together by a few large masses. It is slight in scale and in ambition, but it gives a good sense of how the Hudson River School worked from nature before composing in the studio."},"met:10496":{"d":"A mountain landscape on canvas, about 72 by 102 cm, painted in 1846, two years before Cole's death. The title names a ford, and horses, a river, mountains and trees are noted, so riders cross a stream with mountains beyond. Pictures of this kind set a small human passage within a large, idealized nature, mixing observation with invention. At this size it was a cabinet picture for a private collector rather than a public showpiece.","i":"Cole's late landscapes often set small travelers in a wide, ideal nature, and fords and crossings can be read as quiet metaphors of passage, the journey of life that he made explicit in the series The Voyage of Life of about 1840. Mountains were for him a source of the sublime. Whether he intended a specific allegory here is not recorded, and the canvas probably works chiefly as an atmospheric vision of an unspoiled valley.","c":"Late Cole turned from heavy allegory toward quieter, more atmospheric views, and a canvas like this shows the benefit: the figures serve the landscape rather than the other way around. The formula, with foreground trees, a water crossing and receding peaks, is familiar, and some will find it conventional. Its measured scale and its mood, however, make it a more comfortable picture than his large moral set pieces."},"met:839045":{"d":"A man and a woman in an interior are the recorded motifs of this upright canvas, about 94 by 81 centimeters, painted in 1879 in oil. The title shows that one of the figures, presumably the man, is mending a fishing net, a task of fishing communities. The museum's record gives no description of the room, the light or the figures' expressions, so only the subject, the date and the dimensions can be stated.","i":"Christian Krohg (1852–1925) trained in Germany and Paris and became the leading Norwegian painter of social realism, depicting fishermen, sailors and the urban poor with sympathy and without sentiment; he also wrote novels and campaigned on social issues. A man mending nets is typical of his respect for working skill. The painting was given to the museum by the Norwegian collector Christen Sveaas in 2020 to mark the museum's 150th anniversary.","c":"Krohg's early realism shows in the plainness of the subject and the lack of any picturesque embellishment: a man at work with his hands in a bare room is given the dignity of a main theme. The handling here is likely to be earnest and dark, in the manner of his German training. What the picture lacks in grace it makes up in honesty, a sober work by an artist who would soon find a bolder voice."},"met:436603":{"d":"A tight knot of figures fills the canvas. Samson, seen from behind, sits on the ground with his powerful bare back turned toward the viewer and one leg stretched out, while armoured Philistine soldiers swarm over him, gripping his arms and neck. A bearded man in a steel cuirass bends over his head; another, at the upper right, claps a hand to his cheek in alarm; a helmeted soldier raises his hand at the top. At the lower left Delilah, her white chemise slipping from one shoulder, sits among red and blue drapery, holding a white cloth and glancing toward the struggle. Behind a stone parapet at the right a young woman in a coronet looks on. Oil on canvas, about 1.9 by 2.4 metres.","i":"The story comes from the Book of Judges. Delilah, bribed by the Philistine lords, coaxed Samson into revealing that his strength lay in his uncut hair, then had it shorn while he slept, and the Philistines seized and blinded him. Guercino chooses the moment of arrest rather than the betrayal. The Met says the painting was one of several commissions from Cardinal Giacomo Serra, who also collected Rubens, and calls it a landmark in the artist's career: Guercino was in his late twenties and already celebrated. Samson was traditionally read as a forerunner of Christ, and his betrayal as a parallel to Christ's, so a Baroque viewer could find a sacred meaning in the scuffle.","c":"Guercino plunges the viewer into the scrum. The heavy back of Samson, lit like a piece of sculpture, anchors the picture, and the clutching hands, shouting faces and glinting armour create a convincing din. It is a young man's bravura performance, indebted to Caravaggio's lighting and the Carracci's solid figures, and it already shows the dramatic grasp that made his name. The faults are those of youth: the composition is so crowded that the story is hard to take in at once, and Delilah and the watcher at the right are brushed in with less conviction than the men."},"cma:143138":{"d":"A circular canvas, a tondo, showing the Holy Family at rest during the flight into Egypt. Mary and Saint Joseph are with the sleeping Infant Christ, who is lulled by the violin of a musical angel. Behind them an ideal landscape with classical ruins adds to the calm. The forms are strongly modeled and the colors keep a cool harmony. The round shape is read as the circle, which has no beginning or end and so symbolizes God.","i":"The Gospel (Matthew 2:13-15) tells only of the journey, so the pause, with music and care, comes from later tradition, which let painters show the Holy Family as vulnerable and tender; the angel's music suggests heavenly comfort. Guercino, 'the squint-eyed,' was born near Bologna and became an influential painter in Rome, and this canvas of 1624 follows his return north. His style joined the shadows of Caravaggio with the classical order of Domenichino, Annibale Carracci and Guido Reni.","c":"The firm modeling and cool harmony come from a painter who has absorbed both the shadows of Caravaggio and the order of Bologna, and the result is calm and classicizing rather than dramatic. Lulling the child with a violin is a pleasing, human invention, and the circular format forces a compact grouping that tightens the design. Some may find it sweeter than moving, but the discipline of the composition is real."},"nga:46119":{"d":"A portrait of Cardinal Francesco Cennini, painted in oil on canvas in 1625 and measuring about 117 by 96 cm. As in most cardinals' portraits, the sitter's scarlet dress and cap would mark his rank, and the canvas is large enough for a figure shown well down the body. Guercino was known above all for altarpieces and drawings, and he painted comparatively few portraits, so a likeness of this kind stands apart in his output.","i":"Cardinals sat for portraits to record rank and office, and the usual grammar was a dignified pose, scarlet robes and cap, and a composed, alert face. Such pictures hung in family palaces and were sent to relatives, advertising a connection to the papal court. The sitter's career is not outlined here, but the picture is a reminder that painters of sacred subjects were often asked to record the churchmen who commissioned them.","c":"Guercino's portraits are few, and the test for a painter used to altarpieces is whether the sitter can be made a person rather than an office. The warmth of color and directness of modeling that serve his religious pictures should serve a likeness equally well, though the convention of the cardinal portrait leaves little room for surprise. It is valued more for the range it shows in Guercino than for any innovation in the genre."},"nga:66776":{"d":"A horizontal canvas showing Joseph fleeing the advances of Potiphar's wife. A nude woman with pale pink skin reclines under cream-white sheets, clutching the end of Joseph's royal-blue cloak with one hand and reaching toward his face with the other. Seen from the knees up, he twists and leans sharply away, gripping her forearm, his other palm raised, his eyes turned upward. He wears an ochre tunic belted in a bow. Rose-pink curtains frame the upper corners, and the background falls into shadow.","i":"In Genesis 39 the wife of Joseph's Egyptian master tries to seduce the young Hebrew slave. When he refuses and flees, leaving his cloak in her hand, she uses it as false evidence and has him imprisoned. The story was read as a lesson in chastity and loyalty to God, and it also offered painters a respectable pretext for a sensuous nude. Its size and date match those of the NGA's Amnon and Tamar, so the two probably form a pair of Old Testament tales of virtue and vice.","c":"By 1649 Guercino had moved away from the dark chiaroscuro of his youth toward a lighter, smoother classicism learned from Bologna, and this canvas shows the trade. The close, cropped composition places the viewer at the bedside, and the blue cloak against pale flesh, rose pink and deep shadow is handsome. The twist of Joseph's body is well managed, but the nude is more decorous than the story's danger suggests. It is persuasive as design, less so as drama."},"nga:66775":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 123 by 158.5 cm, painted in 1649 to 1650, showing the Old Testament story of Amnon and Tamar (2 Samuel 13). Its size and date match those of Guercino's Joseph and Potiphar's Wife, and the two canvases were probably made as a pair. The story is a drama of desire and its consequences within King David's family, and the picture belongs to Guercino's late phase, when his manner had become polished and clear.","i":"The tale is among the darkest in the Bible. Amnon, David's eldest son, pretends to be ill in order to be alone with his half-sister Tamar, assaults her and then turns on her with hatred, and the episode leads to Absalom's revenge and further violence in David's house. It was read as a warning against unchecked lust. Set beside Joseph and Potiphar's Wife, which is probably its pendant, it would offer a moral contrast between virtue that flees temptation and vice that yields to it.","c":"The subject is among the hardest the Old Testament offers a painter, and it calls for restraint rather than relish. Guercino's late manner, with its polished clarity and moral balance, suits a cautionary reading better than a visceral one, and the picture's chief interest may be as half of a deliberate pair rather than as a solitary drama. It shows a painter in his late fifties handling a painful narrative with discipline."},"met:436604":{"d":"A very large vertical canvas, about 3.6 by 2.7 meters, painted around 1650, in the scale and type of an altarpiece. The recorded motifs, an altar, a cross and crucifixion, angels, a saint and other men, suggest a scene of prayer at an altar with heavenly witnesses. Guercino painted such pictures in the clear, balanced manner of his later years, in which gesture links the heavenly and earthly zones and the figures read plainly from across a church.","i":"Aloysius Gonzaga (1568-1591), eldest son of a Gonzaga marquis near Mantua, renounced his inheritance to join the Jesuits and died young while caring for plague victims in Rome. He was beatified in 1605, canonized in 1726 and later named patron of youth. A 'Vocation' picture shows the moment of his calling, usually in prayer before an altar with heavenly signs. The Jesuits promoted such images to present a model of chaste, obedient young manhood. The picture's original setting is not recorded here.","c":"A canvas of this size shows Guercino's ability to organize a big altarpiece lucidly, with measured gestures and an unfussy design. By about 1650 he was the leading painter in Bologna, having taken over the city's pre-eminence after Guido Reni's death in 1642. A young man's quiet decision is hard to dramatize, and the answer is decorum: serenity in place of spectacle. It is a respectable, persuasive late work, though it lacks the fire of his early altarpieces."},"nga:131994":{"d":"A vertical portrait of a bearded man in a black garment with a wide white collar, shown from the waist up, close to the viewer, in profile to the left and turning to look out. His forehead is lined and one eye turns slightly inward. He holds a palette dotted with white, brown, orange and black, with five brushes in one hand and a sixth in the other. Behind him on an easel stands a painting of a nude boy with a bow and quiver, one hand hooked in the collar of a sleek dog, with a snake swallowing its tail carved on a stone slab at their feet.","i":"A painter's self-portrait announces the dignity of the art, and Guercino shows himself with the tools of his craft before his own invention. The picture on the easel, Amor Fedele or Faithful Love, pairs a Cupid with a dog, a familiar emblem of loyalty, and the snake biting its tail is an ancient symbol of eternity. Together they may allude to constancy and to the lasting life that art confers on its maker, though the exact program is not spelled out.","c":"The picture within the picture is the cleverest stroke: it sets the painter's aging face, with its lined brow and the wandering eye that gave him the nickname 'Guercino' (the squinter), beside his ideal of youth and beauty. The self-portrait is frank about the squint and free of vanity, and it records a working painter rather than a figure of rank. It is among the more candid self-portraits of the Italian Baroque."},"nga:89678":{"d":"A glossy red horse stands on a patch of tan ground, tethered by a rope to a tall brown post. It faces left with its ears pricked and one back leg slightly raised, as if about to shift its weight. The reddish-brown coat carries white highlights, and the thick, visible brushstrokes give the body a sculptural weight. The ground behind is flat, with patches of yellow ochre, brown and dark green, and the sky runs from light blue at the horizon to darker blue-green at the top. At about 19 by 21 centimetres, the picture reads as a study from life.","i":"Delacroix, like his friend Théodore Géricault, drew and painted horses throughout his life, studying their anatomy and temper as preparation for battle, hunting and history subjects. A small study like this was not meant for exhibition; it recorded how a horse stands, carries its weight and holds its head, to be drawn on later. The date of about 1820 places it early in his career, at the start of a lasting interest in animals as carriers of energy.","c":"A modest study that shows Delacroix's strengths in miniature: a quick, confident grasp of the animal's form, rich warm colour, and paint that has body. The horse is observed with sympathy rather than glamour. As an independent picture it is slight, with the setting barely sketched, and its interest is largely as evidence of how the young painter taught himself the horse. But the glossy coat and alert ears make it more than a drawing exercise."},"met:438110":{"d":"A bedroom, a suit of armor, a man and a woman are the elements of this small canvas of 1823, about 65 by 54 centimeters. It shows Rebecca tending the wounded Ivanhoe, an episode from Walter Scott's novel of medieval England. Eugène Delacroix, then in his mid-twenties, chose a scene of care rather than combat, and the armor in the room reminds the viewer of the battle that has put the knight in his bed.","i":"Scott's Ivanhoe, published in 1819, was a European sensation, and its French translations made medieval England a favorite source for Romantic artists. Rebecca, a Jewish healer, nurses the wounded knight, and her skill and dignity make her one of the story's noblest figures. Delacroix, like many of his generation, preferred literary subjects to classical ones, finding in them freer feeling and more varied color.","c":"An early picture by a young Romantic eager to prove himself: the quiet nursing scene is a surprise from the painter who would become synonymous with violent action. The subject lets him show tenderness and the stuff of costume. It lacks the crashing drama of later works, but it already shows the strong sense of color and the literary imagination that marked him out."},"met:747559":{"d":"A half-length study of a male model, seen from the side, is painted in oil on paper that has since been laid down on a panel; the support was formerly canvas. It measures about 40 by 34 centimeters and dates from around 1818 to 1820, when Eugène Delacroix was a young art student. An academy figure, in the French sense, is a painted study from the posed nude, made as part of training rather than as a finished work.","i":"The academy study was the core of French art education: students worked for years from the nude male model, learning anatomy, volume and the logic of light before attempting history subjects. Delacroix studied in the studio of Pierre-Narcisse Guérin and at the École des Beaux-Arts, where he met Géricault. His academies show a student already interested in strong modeling and warm color, qualities he would develop in his history paintings.","c":"Academy figures are exercises, and their interest lies in what they reveal about training and talent. A study like this shows the discipline underlying the later Romantic, as the young painter learns to describe form with paint rather than line. It is not a picture to be judged against his major works, but it demonstrates that his famous color rested on sound drawing."},"nga:136015":{"d":"One man appears twice on a single canvas, in the same pose seen from two angles: facing the viewer on the left, with his back turned on the right. He has dark brown skin, a black moustache and short black hair under a tilted red hat. He wears a long brown garment with a red section at the chest, a white sash at the waist, a tan sash across the shoulder, striped grey trousers and red sandals with upturned toes. One hand rests on his hip, the other on an indistinct object, possibly a chair. The background is a blur of brown and grey.","i":"Studies like this were a painter's working method, not finished portraits: Delacroix made them to learn the folds and fall of unfamiliar costume, which he could then use in history and exotic subjects. Showing the front and back of one model gave him the whole figure to draw on later. The title identifies the sitter only as a man from Calcutta, and the picture dates from the years before Delacroix's own journey to Morocco in 1832.","c":"As a study this is shrewd and economical: the same man is seen from both sides, and the costume, not the face, carries the interest, from the red hat to the upturned sandals. The loosely brushed background keeps attention on the figure. It has little of the drama of the Delacroix pictures that grew from such material, and the sitter remains anonymous, yet it shows a practical eye for gesture and cloth."},"nga:136016":{"d":"Two full-length figures of a dark-skinned man stand side by side against a dark brown ground. At the left he stands upright holding a cane, in a red hat, a white tunic with a red detail on the chest, brown trousers, a sash and red-and-gold footwear; he has a dark moustache. At the right he sits on a chair with his arm along the armrest, a gold scarf over the same white tunic, his dark hair showing. The two poses give the same costume, and the same man, in action and in repose.","i":"Delacroix made a small group of studies from a man from Calcutta in the early 1820s. Studying one model in different postures and with different accessories was a practical way to collect figures for later pictures: the standing and the seated man are two possible poses from which a painter might choose. The dark ground isolates costume and bearing, so these are working notes on dress rather than portraits of an individual.","c":"Quick, economical and sure in its shorthand: a few strokes of red and gold against the dark ground are enough to fix each pose, and the contrast of standing and seated figures gives the little canvas a rhythm. The faces, though, are barely individualised, and the figures float in the dark without a space to occupy. It is a studio memorandum rather than a picture, valuable for showing how Delacroix gathered raw material."},"met:436179":{"d":"Madame Henri François Riesener, born Félicité Longrois (1786 to 1847), is the subject of this portrait of 1835, an oil on canvas of about 74 by 60 centimeters. She was the wife of the painter Henri-François Riesener and the mother of Léon Riesener, a painter who was Delacroix's cousin and close friend. Delacroix painted few formal portraits, and those he made were mostly of relatives and friends.","i":"Delacroix kept aloof from the portrait business, and his portraits are therefore personal records of affection rather than commissions. A portrait of a relative in her late forties shows the family ties that mattered to him; the Rieseners were part of his circle, and Léon was one of his closest companions. The sitter is shown as an individual with her own character, not an ideal.","c":"Delacroix's portraits tend to be direct and sympathetic, and the lack of a commission frees him to concentrate on the character of the face. The painterly freedom of handling and the sober palette give the likeness a quiet strength. It is less brilliant than his history paintings, but it shows how his color and drawing could serve ordinary human attachment."},"met:436180":{"d":"A Native American family, a father, a mother and an infant, is the subject of this canvas of about 90 by 117 centimeters, begun in 1823 to 1824 and reworked in 1835. The title refers to the Natchez, a people of the lower Mississippi, as imagined in French Romantic literature. The date range records two campaigns of work a decade apart, by Eugène Delacroix, a painter who never visited North America.","i":"The Natchez were a people of the lower Mississippi whose nation was crushed by the French in the early 1700s, and Chateaubriand's writings about them made them a Romantic symbol of a vanishing world. Delacroix's picture reflects that literary vision, in which Native Americans appear as noble, family-centered and doomed. It owes more to books and prints than to observation, and the Romantic idealization is part of its meaning.","c":"A tender family group, treated with Delacroix's warm color and loosened drawing, but filtered through a European literary idea rather than lived reality. The reworking in 1835 means that the surface carries two moments in the painter's career. The picture's interest is as a document of Romantic imagination and of how French painters pictured the New World; as a depiction of real people, it is a fantasy."},"nga:46602":{"d":"A bearded man, a young man and five monks stand in a cream-white hall hung with a map and paintings. The scene is lit from windows on the left, where three monks stand talking, in long white robes with marine-blue hoods. On the right Columbus, in a bronze-brown jacket, dark blue breeches and golden yellow stockings, looks at the map with one foot on a long bench and a red hat in his hand. His son sits on the bench, leaning back, with a hand resting on Columbus's knee. Bundles, a stick and a cloth-covered object lie on the floor, and the artist signed and dated the picture 1838.","i":"The subject is an episode from Columbus's years of petitioning for support: according to the traditional account, he stopped at the friary of La Rábida, near Palos in southern Spain, with his young son Diego, and the monks there helped him win a hearing at court. Delacroix shows him before a map, mid-discussion, not at the moment of triumph. Stories of a patient visionary helped by humble allies suited the Romantic taste for history in the 1830s.","c":"A quiet history painting, and an unusual one: no storm, no discovery, only a thoughtful man studying a map while his son leans on the bench. The pale hall lit from the left and the clustered, blue-hooded monks make a calm counterweight, and the colour is fresher than the usual run of historical illustration. But the poses are somewhat stagey and the narrative needs a caption. It reads best as a gentle, humane work rather than a major Delacroix."},"met:436177":{"d":"A garden with trees, edging toward forest, is the subject of this oil on canvas of about 45 by 55 centimeters, painted around 1842 to 1843. It shows the garden at Nohant, the country house of the novelist George Sand, where Eugène Delacroix stayed several times as her friend. Landscape was a rare subject for him, and this is a relaxed, personal view made while visiting rather than a picture intended for the Salon.","i":"Nohant, in the Berry region of central France, was where George Sand entertained writers, musicians and painters, and where Chopin, her companion at the time, composed in the summers. Delacroix found the visits restful, and he painted and sketched in the grounds. The picture belongs to the Romantic taste for the intimate, domestic landscape as a place of friendship and rest.","c":"Delacroix paints the foliage with the same energy he gave to figures, in flickering touches of green and brown that anticipate the Impressionists' handling of leaves. It is a modest, unforced picture, and its freedom is a pleasure. As a landscape it lacks the structure of the professional landscapist, but as a Delacroix it reveals the pure painter at leisure."},"met:438814":{"d":"A tangle of horses and men fills the foreground, pressed close to the picture plane. On a dapple-gray horse a dark-skinned rider in a green cloak and a red-and-yellow turban leans over Rebecca, who hangs limp across the saddle, her head thrown back and her pale clothing trailing. A second man in a red cap and blue-black tunic strides beside the horse and supports her. At the right edge a knight in a white mantle and helmet, on a reddish horse, reaches out. Behind them a ruined castle burns, with dark smoke. A bow, a quiver and a dropped round helmet lie on the ground at the bottom. The paint is dark green and brown, with accents of red, gold and white.","i":"The scene comes from Walter Scott's novel Ivanhoe (1819), a favorite source for French Romantic painters. The Jewish heroine Rebecca, imprisoned in the castle of Front-de-Boeuf, which burns behind, is carried off by two Saracen slaves commanded by the Templar knight Bois-Guilbert, who desires her. Delacroix showed this version at the Salon of 1846 and made a second in 1858, now in the Louvre. The contorted, interlocking poses and the shallow, crowded space convey violence and panic. The Metropolitan Museum calls it one of his greatest paintings.","c":"Color drives the picture. The greenish dapple of the horse against Rebecca's pale, gold-edged drapery, with dark smoke behind, gives it a sulfurous energy, and Delacroix holds the tangle together by a rhythm of curves rather than by outline, as he learned from Rubens. The anatomy of the collapsed Rebecca is awkward, and the crowded foreground is hard to untangle at first glance. That confusion is partly the point, since it conveys panic. A fair reservation is that the heroine's helplessness is more spectacle than character."},"met:436175":{"d":"A basket of flowers and leaves fills this large canvas, about 107 by 142 centimeters, dated 1848 to 1849. Eugène Delacroix, the great Romantic history painter, painted few still lifes, and this one is unusual for its size, as flower pieces were normally modest in scale. The subject gave him free play with color, and the flowers are treated as masses of hue rather than as botanical records.","i":"Flower painting had a long tradition in the Netherlands and France, where bouquets symbolized the brevity of life and the beauty of nature. Dutch flower painters had set the standard, and in the troubled years of the 1848 revolution Delacroix turned, perhaps as a relief, to a subject that had no politics. The picture is also a reminder that for Delacroix, color was the heart of painting, whatever the subject.","c":"A bravura demonstration that the history painter's color could be put to still life: the flowers burst across the canvas in saturated hues, and the size gives them a decorative authority. The treatment is flamboyant rather than carefully organized, so the arrangement may seem casual. It is a splendid display of paint more than a studied composition, and it justifies its scale by the richness of its color."},"met:436178":{"d":"Hamlet and his mother, Queen Gertrude, are the figures in this small upright oil on canvas, about 27 by 18 centimeters, dated 1849. The title and details point to the confrontation in the queen's chamber from Shakespeare's tragedy, a scene of accusation and guilt. Eugène Delacroix admired Shakespeare deeply and returned to Hamlet repeatedly, producing a famous series of lithographs as well as paintings.","i":"In the closet scene of the play, Hamlet confronts his mother about her hasty marriage to his uncle Claudius, the murderer of his father, and in his fury he kills Polonius hidden behind a curtain. Romantic artists found in Hamlet a hero of divided feeling, and Delacroix saw in the scene an extreme of emotion. French Romantics regarded Shakespeare as a liberation from classical rules.","c":"Even at a small scale the picture has Delacroix's drama: the confrontation is conceived as a clash of two figures in intense relation, and the sketchy handling suits a moment of frenzy. It is more a study in emotion than a finished stage tableau, and its compactness is part of its force. Those who know his lithographs will find the same imagination in paint."},"met:436176":{"d":"Christ sleeps in a boat while the storm rages in this canvas of about 51 by 61 centimeters, painted around 1853. The boat, the men aboard and the sleeping Christ are the main elements, and the sea itself is the other great actor. Eugène Delacroix returned to this Gospel subject in several versions in the 1850s, and the repeated composition shows how much it appealed to his taste for storm and drama.","i":"The story, told in the Gospels of Matthew, Mark and Luke, tells how Christ slept in a boat on the Sea of Galilee while a storm threatened to sink it, and how the frightened disciples woke him, whereupon he calmed the wind and waves. Since the early Church the boat has been an image of the Church itself, tossed by trials but secure in Christ's presence, and the subject carries a message about faith in danger.","c":"Delacroix builds the picture from diagonals of wave and hull, and he lets color carry the storm: the effect is of turbulence barely contained. The sleeping Christ, as a still center, gives the agitation its meaning. The figures are secondary to the sea, and some might wish for more sacred stillness, but as an expression of faith in color and motion it is vivid and persuasive."},"met:439631":{"d":"The Roman poet Ovid, exiled to the shores of the Black Sea, appears among the Scythians, nomads who live with horses and dogs. Men, women, horses and a dog appear in this small oil on paper laid down on wood, about 32 by 50 centimeters, dated 1862. It is a smaller variant by Eugène Delacroix of a larger canvas of 1859, now in London.","i":"Ovid was banished by the emperor Augustus in the year 8 for reasons never fully explained, and he spent his last years at Tomis, writing poems of complaint and longing in the Tristia. Delacroix saw in him the poet set down among a rough society, and the Scythians, whom Ovid himself described as barbarous, appear in the painter's version as curious and almost kindly hosts. Baudelaire praised the larger version for its melancholy.","c":"Delacroix's late manner is at its freest here: the open landscape and loose, glowing color turn an old story into a meditation on exile and the artist's loneliness. As an oil on paper, it has the spontaneity of a sketch, and it lacks the finish of the larger painting, but the rhythm of figures and horses and the poetry of the idea come through clearly."},"nga:195132":{"d":"A tiger crouches at the left, front paws stretched forward, rump in the air, ears pinned back and teeth bared, yellow eyes gleaming, facing a hissing snake wound twice around a tree at the right. Light glints off the snake's skin as it flicks a forked tongue between open jaws, and sunlight glows on the white fur of the tiger's underbelly. The grass around the tiger is gold, green and mustard yellow; behind it a sage-green plain reaches rocky hills under hints of blue sky. The gnarled, split trunk frames the right side, and the picture is signed and dated 1862 at the foot of the tree.","i":"Delacroix studied big cats from life at the Paris menagerie in the Jardin des Plantes, and painted lions, tigers and other wild animals throughout his career, often in combat. For him they stood for the raw force of nature that the Romantic painter admired. Painted in 1862, the year before his death, this small picture sets a powerful predator against an apparently smaller but equally dangerous opponent, with no hunters and no story beyond the instant of confrontation.","c":"Late Delacroix, and typical: the pose is full of coiled tension, and the colour of gold grass, glossy snake and flickering white fur has the vibrancy of his best small oils. The brushwork is rapid but controlled, and the tree makes a firm vertical against the crouching horizontal. What is missing is scale or consequence: the confrontation is vivid but has no story, so it feels like an animal study dressed as a picture. Still, a lively, convincing small work."},"nga:50686":{"d":"In a sage-green valley, about a dozen armed men on foot and horseback make a stand against an attack advancing from the back left. In the foreground, painted with dashes and swirling strokes, a chestnut horse with a black mane collapses on its rider, who sprawls on the ground; nearby a man in a mustard-yellow tunic kneels with his forehead against his rifle barrel. At the lower centre a rider on a dark horse charges the approaching men, who are loosely painted and partly backlit by white smoke. Above rise a rocky hill with a walled complex of square towers and sheer cliffs, under an azure sky.","i":"Delacroix visited Morocco and Algeria in 1832, and the experience supplied costumes, light and subjects for the rest of his life; horsemen and skirmishes recur in his work from the 1840s to the early 1860s. This picture, dated 1863, the year of his death, is a work of memory and imagination, a European vision of North Africa. It tells no particular story, but offers the combination of speed, danger and exotic setting that his public expected from him.","c":"In his last year Delacroix could still orchestrate colour and movement with vigour: the fallen horse and kneeling rifleman in the foreground are swirled in saturated teal, crimson and gold, while the soft, hazy distance gives depth. The Orient it shows is imagined, with little observation behind it, and the fight is picturesque rather than felt. But as a demonstration of what his brush could still do, it is one of the more energetic late works."},"cma:145309":{"d":"A small panoramic oil sketch on paper mounted on board, about 19.5 by 39 cm, painted outdoors during Valenciennes's stay in Rome from 1777 to 1785. St. Peter's Basilica stands in the distance. The long, low format suits a wide view across the city, and the sketch is a study made on the spot to catch the light and atmosphere of Rome, not a picture prepared for exhibition. Its spontaneity and freshness are typical of the artist's outdoor work.","i":"Valenciennes believed in painting outdoors. In his treatise on perspective and landscape, published around 1800, he urged young painters to make quick studies of sky, light and weather directly from nature, within a limited time because the light changes, and then use them in the studio for finished compositions. His oil sketches were working tools, and they anticipate the open-air practice of Corot, who learned from his pupils, and later of the Impressionists.","c":"The sketch's virtue is exactly what it leaves out. Small, quick and unfinished, it can be more truthful than the composed classical landscapes Valenciennes exhibited, which follow Poussin and Claude with careful ideal staging. That contrast is what makes it important: the study looks forward to the plein-air tradition, while the finished picture looks back. It may be slight as a composition, but as observation of Roman light it is remarkably direct."},"nga:98872":{"d":"A horizontal oil study on paper, mounted on cardboard, about 19 by 32 cm. A thin strip of brown land runs across the bottom, and above it a procession of puffy gray and white clouds sweeps up across a vivid blue sky, trundling from left to upper right. Beyond the land, a darker gray area could be a valley, a mountain or the shadows of the clouds, and about a fifth of the way up the horizon glows faintly peach. The land is loosely painted.","i":"Cloud studies were exercises in observing weather, and Valenciennes urged young painters to paint skies fast and from life, since the light and the clouds change quickly. The Roman Campagna, the open country around Rome, was a favorite field for landscape painters of his generation. Here there is no story and no figure: the sheet is a note on weather, kept as a reference for later compositions and not meant for exhibition, though it now reads as a work in its own right.","c":"This is among the freshest things Valenciennes left, and its power depends on omission. The thin ground, the large sky and the loose handling convey open space and time of day in a way his finished classical landscapes, ordered by rule, rarely do. Its limits are those of the type, with no composition to speak of and no subject, but as an anticipation of the cloud studies of Constable and Dahl it is notable."},"met:438677":{"d":"A long, narrow oil study on paper, laid down on canvas, about 21 by 49 cm, of a river bank in Brittany. A river and trees are recorded, and the title names the Rance, the tidal river of northern Brittany that reaches the Channel between Dinard and Saint-Malo. It is an outdoor sketch rather than a finished picture, and its wide, shallow format suits a panoramic view along the water. The date is uncertain, possibly 1785.","i":"Valenciennes taught that a painter should study nature directly in quick sketches and then build finished pictures from them in the studio. This study turns that outdoor method to French scenery rather than Roman views. Sketches of this kind served as private references. The scene has no story and no figure, and its subject is simply a stretch of river and bank observed for its own sake, an idea that French academic practice of the 1780s did not yet value as a finished subject.","c":"Its virtue is lightness: a long strip of water and trees set down with a quick hand. As with his Roman sketches, the oil study drops the apparatus of his formal landscapes, and the omission is the gain. With little more than a river, its bank and trees it is modest, but as a record of an unglamorous place observed for its own sake it is an early step toward the French open-air tradition, and a pleasing one."},"nga:73434":{"d":"A dimly lit tavern in a horizontal panel. Three men with reddened noses and wide faces gather at a table made of a square board on a broken barrel. One holds a glass of beer in both hands, another grins as he hunches over his forearm and grips the neck of a lidded jug, and the third stands behind with a white clay pipe raised, his eyes slitted. Pipes, pots and a broom litter the floor, a candle stub smokes beyond, and in the next room a fourth man stands facing the wall, relieving himself. The walls and floor are an almost uniform taupe gray.","i":"Peasant tavern scenes became a Flemish specialty in the 1630s, when the Antwerp painter Adriaen Brouwer made the low-life interior fashionable and Teniers, newly a master in the Antwerp guild, took up the type. Buyers, often town dwellers, enjoyed the comic ugliness of the drinkers with a mixture of amusement and moral distance. The broken barrel, the strewn pipes and the man at the wall are signs of idleness and excess, and the picture invites laughter more than a sermon.","c":"An early Teniers, plainly influenced by Brouwer: the narrow range of taupe, gray, spruce blue and olive suits the dim room, and the exaggerated faces are drawn with a good eye for comic types. It lacks the silvery elegance of his later work, but it is lively, compact and well observed, and the still life of pipes, jugs and broom on the floor is painted with evident relish. It is a good example of his early phase."},"nga:52623":{"d":"A square panel in which the round, cavernous mouth of a craggy grotto nearly fills the picture and opens onto vivid blue water. Steep rocks in tan and earthy brown rise like cliffs at either side, and a wooden cross leans on a low outcrop at lower left. A turquoise waterway zigzags between tufts of reedy plants toward the open water, where white highlights suggest surging waves. A band of opal-white mountains follows the curve of the opening against a pearly sky, and black and white birds fly in the distance.","i":"A view out of a dark cave or rocky arch toward bright water was a recurring device in Netherlandish landscape around 1600, valued for the contrast of shadow and light and for its natural frame. Teniers adds a wayside cross, a note of piety that may suggest a hermit's retreat or a pilgrim's path, though the picture does not say which. The scene is probably imagined rather than topographical, and its place in his work is that of a rare landscape among his figure pictures.","c":"Teniers is remembered for peasants and for his views of the Archduke's gallery, and this panel shows another side of him: a landscape imagined with real drama, in which the round opening works as a frame and the cool whites, turquoise water and brown rock hold together. It is perhaps more ingenious than deeply felt, but the vivid blue and the tiny distant birds, which give the vast space its scale, are memorable."},"nga:53141":{"d":"A crowded feast in a horizontal panel. Seven people gather around a table at right, while in a deeply shadowed room beyond, at left, a family sits at a fire. At the center a man in mustard yellow and a yellow and orange jester's cap faces the viewer, mouth agape, one arm gesturing toward the table and a jug dangling from the other hand. A young man in a paper crown tilts back a tall tankard, another raises his black hat, and two women smile among the men. A man peers through an opening high in the wall, and a chained owl, a cat and a dog look on.","i":"Twelfth Night, the eve of Epiphany, was celebrated in the Low Countries with a feast at which a bean baked in a cake chose a king for the evening, who presided over cries of 'The king drinks!' The paper crown here probably marks the bean king. Flemish painters from Jordaens to Teniers made the revel a favorite subject, a picture of licensed misrule. The chained owl, a bird associated with folly, may add a note of mockery, though it is also simply a household pet.","c":"Teniers is at his best when many figures, gestures and incidents are held in one lucid light, and this panel, signed and dated 1635, does so: the jester draws the eye to the center, the table group and the fire room give depth, and the animals and scattered paper add incident. The vulgarity is good-humored, and the picture is more rowdy than moralizing. Among his early peasant feasts it is one of the more ambitious and better organized."},"nga:74820":{"d":"A small vertical panel, about 23 by 18 cm, painted around 1645, showing two peasants with a glass of wine, in the setting of a tavern or inn. The picture is a close, informal study of two figures in the Flemish low-life tradition, made for a cabinet collector or a modest town buyer rather than for display. By the mid-1640s Teniers was at the height of his early success, and his manner in these years became lighter and more silvery than in his dark early taverns.","i":"Two drinkers sharing a glass are the simplest form of the tavern picture, and what the viewer was invited to enjoy was the comic observation of types: the flushed face, the loose posture, the pleasure in the drink. Pictures of this kind could carry a quiet moral about drunkenness, though many were simply amusing. Peasant life, as seen by the town buyer, was a stage on which appetite and folly were played in safe, laughable form.","c":"With only two figures and a glass, the picture stands or falls on observation and touch, and Teniers' fluency suits it: this was a type he could produce with ease and variety. As an example of his output in the mid-1640s it is a pleasant minor piece, more modest in aim than his crowded tavern interiors, and charming rather than memorable. Its small size is part of its appeal."},"met:437777":{"d":"A soldiers' guardroom, painted in oil on wood, about 55 by 76 centimeters, about 1645 to 1647, by David Teniers the Younger. Armor, a drum, a dog and men fill the room, and Saint Peter's release from prison is the sacred episode, so in a corner or background the saint is freed while the guards go about their business. Teniers painted many such guardroom scenes.","i":"The Acts of the Apostles tells how an angel freed Peter from prison, passing him unseen between sleeping guards and through iron gates. In Flemish guardroom pictures such as this, the sacred episode is placed within a worldly scene of soldiers, still-life armor and drums, so that the viewer discovers the miracle in the background. The device made a moralizing point about the contrast between the guards' ignorance and the miracle.","c":"Teniers is at his best in the still-life details of a guardroom, the burnished armor, the drum, the dog, painted with silvery delicacy, and here the Saint Peter episode gives the scene an extra layer of interest. The soldiers are lively and well grouped, and the clutter has a studied ease. The sacred story is more pretext than subject, but the picture is a polished, intelligent specimen of a favorite Teniers type."},"nga:55505":{"d":"A crowded tavern in a horizontal panel. At lower left two men play cards at a narrow table: one lifts a tankard as he holds out his hand, and the other, on a box, shows his cards to a third man who leans in nose to nose, smiling, with a clay pipe. Two more men with pipes look on, and a man at the left wall turns his back, presumably relieving himself. In a second room beyond, a man sleeps face down on a bench and others gather at the door, where a woman looks in. A fire burns at right, and a dog sleeps in the lower right corner.","i":"The wall carries the moral: a piece of chalk, and a picture of an owl flanked by spectacles and a candle. The owl with spectacles and candle probably recalls a Dutch proverb that spectacles and candles are no use if the owl will not see, a jibe at those who will not learn. Gamblers, drunkards and sleepers give the proverb its scene, and the picture, dated 1658, lets the buyer laugh at folly from a safe distance. The chalk was probably for the tavern's tally.","c":"By 1658 Teniers was an established court painter in Brussels and had polished the peasant tavern into elegant theater: a controlled range of brown, blue and charcoal, and groupings that lead the eye from the card players back to the distant door. The urinating figure and the sleeping drunk are familiar jokes by now, so the picture is admired more for craftsmanship, the orchestration of many vignettes in one room, than for novelty."},"met:437778":{"d":"A small oil on copper, about 37 by 26 centimeters, from the 1650s, in which Judith holds the head of Holofernes, her servant beside her, at his tent. The upright, narrow format leaves room for only a few figures: the heroine, her maid and the grim trophy. Copper's smooth surface suited the fine detail and jewel-like color Teniers brought to such cabinet pictures.","i":"In the Book of Judith, the widow Judith saves her besieged city of Bethulia by entering the camp of the Assyrian general Holofernes, who falls asleep drunk, and cutting off his head with the help of her maid. The scene of Judith and her servant leaving the tent with the head was favored by painters for its drama and for its heroine, a figure of courage and piety.","c":"A subject at odds with Teniers's usual peasant comedy, taken here at a small scale on copper where fine detail and rich color can compensate for modest drama. Teniers was an able painter of history subjects early and late, and a Judith with her servant is within his range. The result is more an elegant cabinet piece than a gripping drama."},"met:437779":{"d":"A peasant festival in oil on canvas, about 68 by 75 centimeters, painted about 1660 by David Teniers the Younger. Men, women and a child eat and dance, with an inn and a church steeple in view, so the villagers feast outside the inn in a village setting. At this size the canvas was suited to a collector's gallery rather than a church or public hall.","i":"Village feasts, with dancing and eating before the inn and church, were Teniers's most popular subject, and he painted dozens of variations. In an age when moralists often warned against drunkenness and lust at kermis celebrations, these scenes could be read as warnings, but they were chiefly enjoyed as cheerful pictures of rural plenty by urban collectors, including the Archduke Leopold Wilhelm, whom Teniers served as court painter in Brussels.","c":"By 1660 Teniers had refined the peasant festival into a graceful formula, with light, silvery tones, tidy figures and a high sky. This one, with its mixture of dance, feast and village architecture, offers the expected pleasures and nothing disturbing. The formula is repetitive and sentimental, but the touch is agile and the composition easy to read. A typical late Teniers of undeniable charm."},"met:437776":{"d":"A tiny landscape panel, about 17 by 23 centimeters, in oil on wood, showing the parable of the Good Samaritan, as the title states: a man in need, an act of charity, a donkey and a dog. The panel is undated. Such tiny panels were made for collectors who kept them in cabinets and enjoyed them at close range. Teniers, better known for peasants and guardrooms, also painted small biblical episodes in landscape settings.","i":"In Luke's Gospel, Jesus tells of a traveler robbed and left half dead on the road to Jericho, whom a priest and a Levite pass by and a Samaritan, a despised outsider, tends and takes to an inn. The parable teaches that neighbor means anyone in need. In the seventeenth century it was a favorite subject for painters of landscape, since the road, the donkey and the roadside gave an easy pictorial setting for an act of charity.","c":"At this size the picture is a cabinet jewel, in which a quick, delicate touch can set a big idea in a small field. The subject, a calm landscape with a donkey and a kindly gesture, suits Teniers's talent for tender observation of animals and country people. The moral is quiet, and the painting is likely to be more charming than profound."},"met:437780":{"d":"A small landscape panel, about 17 by 23 centimeters, in oil on wood, showing shepherds with sheep, a goat and a dog, as the title states. The shepherds presumably tend their flock in open pasture. The panel is undated. Its size matches that of The Good Samaritan by the same artist, and the two small panels may have been painted as companions, though nothing proves it.","i":"Pastoral scenes of shepherds and flocks were an old and loved theme, evoking a simple life close to nature and, for educated viewers, the poetry of Virgil and the pastoral tradition. Teniers, working for aristocratic collectors in Brussels, painted such scenes as gentle idylls, in contrast to the livelier peasant feasts. The sheep and goats are likely to be drawn with the care of an animal specialist.","c":"A modest pastoral that shows Teniers's gift for animals, and his light, silvery touch with landscape, in a small format that concentrates his virtues. The subject, shepherds, dog, goat and sheep, is among his natural territory. Not an ambitious picture, but a pleasing one."},"met:816523":{"d":"A horizontal still life, about 76 by 99 cm, painted around 1630, of fruit and flowers, with apples and tulips among the recorded motifs. In pictures of this type, blossoms and fruit are gathered into one dense display, usually against a dark ground that lets the colors glow. The picture is an early example of the work of Orsola Maddalena Caccia, who painted in the convent where she lived and worked.","i":"Orsola Maddalena Caccia was the daughter of the Piedmontese painter Guglielmo Caccia, called Moncalvo, trained in his workshop and later became an Ursuline nun at Moncalvo, where she ran a studio. Still life was among the genres open to women, who rarely had access to training from the nude figure. Tulips, newly fashionable, were prized for their rarity, and flowers and fruit together suggest both abundance and the brevity of life, as they flourish and fade.","c":"Caccia's still lifes are valued first as rare Italian examples by a woman painter, and then for a sturdy, decorative naturalism that fills the canvas with convincing, tangible forms. The pleasure of such pictures lies in the specific, the shape of an apple or the curl of a tulip petal, and the risk is repetition. She is not usually ranked beside Fede Galizia, but within Piedmontese still life she is an able and individual voice."},"met:816522":{"d":"A vertical still life, about 102 by 81 cm, painted around 1635, of flowers in a vase that the title calls grotesque, meaning ornamented with fantastic, mask-like decoration derived from antique wall painting. Tulips and other flowers are recorded, together with peaches, so blooms and fruit share the composition. A display of this kind shows off several skills at once: the delicacy of petals, the sheen of the vase and the bloom of fruit.","i":"Grotesque ornament takes its name from the 'grottoes,' the buried rooms of Nero's Golden House in Rome where such decoration was rediscovered around 1480. It gave vases, furniture and walls their fantastic masks, curling foliage and hybrid creatures. Using such a vase in a still life shows the painter's ingenuity and her patrons' taste for fantasy. Flower pieces were valued for rarity and variety, and they carried a gentle reminder of the brevity of life.","c":"What distinguishes the picture is the vase. Where many flower pieces choose a plain jar, Caccia sets her blooms in an ornamental fantasy, a play between artifice and nature that shows an ambitious, witty mind. The type carries the usual risk of decorative sameness, and the success of the arrangement can be judged only before the canvas, but the idea alone makes the picture more interesting than most Italian flower pieces of its date."},"met:816514":{"d":"A nearly square canvas, about 97.5 by 96.5 cm, painted around 1645, showing the Virgin and Child with the infant Saint John the Baptist. Mary, the Christ Child and the young John are the three figures recorded, in the devotional family group that Italian painters developed from the Renaissance onward. Caccia painted the subject as a nun, in the convent studio at Moncalvo, and canvases of this size suit a chapel or a private room of prayer.","i":"Saint John the Baptist, the cousin of Christ, appears as a child in these groups because legend imagined the two boys meeting and playing together. He often points to Christ or offers a small reed cross, a sign of the Passion to come. Images that made holiness domestic and tender were valued in convent settings, where the Virgin as mother and the child as companion could be a subject of daily prayer.","c":"Caccia's religious paintings are less known than her still lifes, and they follow the post-Tridentine formula with grace but sometimes a measure of stiffness inherited from her father's mannered style. The nearly square format makes a compact grouping of three figures, which suits the subject. The tenderness is the strength and the conventionality the limit. As the work of a woman who headed her own studio, the picture matters for what it shows of her range."},"met:437231":{"d":"A horizontal panel, now transferred to canvas, about 49 by 73 cm, painted in the late 1520s, showing the Adoration of the Shepherds. Mary, Saint Joseph and the Christ Child are joined by shepherds, one of whom carries a walking stick. The wide format spreads the figures across the picture rather than stacking them, and the work is a devotional cabinet picture of the kind that Ferrarese painters produced for private patrons. L'Ortolano worked in Ferrara.","i":"The shepherds are the first to learn of Christ's birth in Luke's Gospel (2:8-20): angels announce it to them in the fields by night and they hurry to the manger. Painters stressed humility, with rustic men kneeling before the child and the staff as their emblem, standing for the simple faithful who recognized the Savior before the learned did. The subject was a favorite for private devotion, a gentler companion to the Adoration of the Magi.","c":"L'Ortolano is a lesser-known Ferrarese master, often overshadowed by Dosso Dossi and Garofalo, and the picture's interest is partly its rarity: a devotional work by an artist less widely known than his Ferrarese contemporaries. The transfer from wood to canvas, a drastic remedy of an earlier age, has probably cost some of the original surface, which makes fine judgments difficult. It is a modest, sympathetic work rather than a landmark."},"cma:128365":{"d":"A tiny panel, about 16 by 12 cm, dated 1640, showing a young woman in a tight focus on the head. Her costume is relatively informal, and that, with the close view, suggests the picture may be a tronie, a type of head study popular with Dutch painters in the 1600s, rather than a portrait of a known sitter. Dou, Rembrandt's first pupil in Leiden, was a master of such small, finely worked pictures.","i":"A tronie, from the Dutch word for 'face,' is a study of a character or expression painted for its own interest, from a living model who was not meant to be recognized. They flourished in Leiden, where Rembrandt and his circle painted many, and were sold on the open market to collectors who enjoyed costume, expression and display of skill. Whether this is a tronie or a portrait of a particular woman is left open by the museum.","c":"At this date Dou was moving from his master's broader handling toward the polished, enamel-like finish that made him the leader of the Leiden fine painters. The picture is small enough to hold in one hand, and its virtue is concentration: a single face has to carry the whole panel. As either portrait or tronie it is a minor but characteristic work, more admirable for its patience than for its psychological depth."},"nga:106946":{"d":"Shown from the chest up in an oval format, an old man with a wrinkled face and silvery grey hair and beard gazes into the distance. His chest is angled to the right while he turns his head to look over his other shoulder. His slate-blue eyes are slightly sunken and lined, the hollows of temple and cheek are shaded, and his thin lips are framed by a curly moustache and beard. A dark mahogany cap is pushed back on his head, a brown jacket with gold-coloured buttons shows a cream shirt collar, and the background is dark olive green. The panel is tiny, about 18 by 15 centimetres.","i":"Dou was Rembrandt's first pupil in Leiden, around 1628 to 1631, and heads of old men, studied for their wrinkles, beards and the play of light on aged skin, were a speciality of the Leiden school. The picture may be a character study, or tronie, rather than a portrait of a named sitter, since the title gives no name. Dou later became the leading Leiden fine painter, famous for small, extremely polished pictures.","c":"Dou's gift for texture shows in the descriptive attention to the wrinkled face and soft silvery beard, and the compact oval gives the head a satisfying concentration. The tone is gentler than Rembrandt's, and the sitter's remote gaze reads as a stock expression more than a lived emotion. For all its polish, the picture is an exercise in surface rather than in character, and its minute scale is part of its appeal: a small thing finely made."},"met:436209":{"d":"A small panel, about 25 by 23 cm, painted around 1655 to 1657, showing an evening school. A man teaches children, boys and a girl among them, and a candle supplies the light, so the picture is a night scene in which the flame sets the pools of brightness and shadow. As a cabinet picture for collectors, it joins a modest domestic subject to the display of artificial light that Dou's Leiden patrons prized.","i":"Schooling was a subject of social interest in the Dutch Republic, where literacy was unusually high, and evening schools served those occupied by day. Dou's candlelit classrooms may praise diligence and learning, while the night setting recalls his skill at night effects, a specialty of Leiden painters. Reading by candlelight also carries a note of study and diligence, which gives the humble scene a quietly improving tone.","c":"Dou's candlelit pictures are tours de force of control: the glow of a flame and the shadows cast on small faces, each rendered with a jeweler's care. At a size smaller than a book, the intimate classroom turns education into theater. The finish is minute, and the risk is that minuteness becomes an end in itself, but this type was among the most admired and imitated of the Leiden fine painters."},"met:436210":{"d":"A man in a blue cap and a brown, fur-trimmed coat leans from a stone window arch as if from the frame of a stage. His shoulder-length hair falls on a white collar tied with tassels. In one raised hand he holds a palette and a bundle of brushes; the other rests on a large open book lying on the sill, over which a striped cloth hangs. A red curtain is bunched at the upper left, and a vine climbs the right side of the window past a hanging birdcage. At the left an ornate terracotta pot holds orange marigolds, and a small relief of children playing with a goat is set into the sill below. Everything is lit with a smooth, minutely detailed finish.","i":"Gerrit Dou (1613–1675) was Rembrandt's first pupil in Leiden and became the founder of the Leiden fijnschilders, the fine painters who prized minute finish. He painted about a dozen self-portraits, probably for patrons. Here, aged about fifty-two, he presents himself at a window niche, a format he favoured, with the tools of his trade. The relief of children with a goat, a famous design by the sculptor François Duquesnoy, and the open book were probably chosen to show learning and to set painting beside sculpture and scholarship. The birdcage and vine are common in his work, but their exact meaning here is uncertain.","c":"Dou's finely finished pictures commanded high prices in his lifetime, and this one shows why: every surface, from the fur to the vine leaves, is resolved with a jeweller's patience. The arch, the stagey drape and the orderly still life make a self-portrait that is closer to an advertisement than a confession. Rembrandt's self-portraits ask who the painter is; Dou's say what he can do. The face is neat but guarded, and the finish can feel airless. As a demonstration of the Leiden manner at its peak, it is persuasive."},"nga:46032":{"d":"A balding old man with a fringe of white hair and a long, wispy grey beard kneels in profile in a shadowy, grotto-like space, fingers intertwined in prayer. He looks up at a crucifix propped against woven baskets and a moss-covered, gnarled tree, his hands resting on the open pages of a large book. He wears a dark tawny-brown hooded robe with a rosary ending in a tiny skull and cross. A brick archway frames him, and an unlit lantern hangs from the tree. On the rock lie a skull and an hourglass; at lower right a horse's skull and jugs rest among thistles.","i":"The picture is a meditation on mortality and devotion. The skull and hourglass are standard vanitas emblems, reminders that time runs out; the horse's skull, the overturned jug and the thorny thistle add to the lesson, and the unlit lantern may speak of earthly light gone out. The title calls him only a hermit, but the kneeling figure with book, skull and crucifix belongs to the same family of images as Saint Jerome in penitence.","c":"A picture to be examined at close range, and Dou rewards the effort: the knotted tree, the mossy bark, the pottery and the aged skin are rendered with a jeweller's patience, and the dusky grotto gives the kneeling figure a concentrated, quiet drama. The same finish can make the sermon feel overstaged, and the hermit's devotion is a pose more than a feeling. A late work by an artist who had mastered his small domain."},"nga:46112":{"d":"Red satin curtains are drawn back to show a young woman sitting upright in a narrow bath lined with white linen. She is bare to the waist, with her hair under a jeweled cap, pearl earrings, gold bracelets and a ring on her little finger, and she holds a carnation over a board that carries a dish of fruit and scattered sprigs. At left a nurse with one breast bared suckles a swaddled baby, while a curly-haired child peers over the rim of the tub toward the fruit. Beyond them a maid lifts a jug beside an ornate fireplace, and a small picture of a unicorn hangs on the wall. Skin, linen and satin are smoothly blended, almost like enamel, on an oak panel.","i":"The sitter has never been identified. Tradition names Diane de Poitiers, mistress of Henri II; Mary Stuart and Marie Touchet, mistress of Charles IX, have also been proposed, but the National Gallery's catalog finds too little evidence for any of them, and the face is idealized. It is one of only two signed paintings by François Clouet, and the date of about 1571 rests on comparison with his portrait of Elizabeth of Austria. Compositional precedents suggest the sitter may be a mistress or courtesan. Readings of the picture range from praise of beauty to a Venus allegory to political satire, and details such as the unicorn, a symbol of purity, do not settle on one meaning.","c":"The surface is the achievement. Skin, linen and satin have a cool, porcelain finish, and the nurse's amused sideways glance adds an unexpected touch of humor. The weaker part is the space: the figures are stacked behind the table edge, the bather is cut off at the waist by the tub, and the background group feels like a separate picture. Clouet is best known for portrait drawings and court portraits, so this crowded, invented composition is an outlier, more intriguing for what it withholds than for what it explains."},"nga:195497":{"d":"A wooded landscape on panel, about 53 by 67 cm, painted around 1658, with figures. The title makes the woodland the principal subject and the figures staffage, small people who give scale and life to the scene. Such pictures follow the Dutch tradition of the wooded countryside, usually composed in the studio from drawings and from a stock of motifs. Hobbema was about twenty and still close to his teacher, Jacob van Ruisdael.","i":"Hobbema trained with Jacob van Ruisdael, and the wooded landscape was among the favorite subjects of the Dutch Golden Age, valued as a recognizable image of the national countryside, with its farmland, woodland and ordinary productive life. Symbolic content in such pictures is usually minimal, and the subject is the pleasure of looking at light, leaves and distance. Small figures serve to show the landscape as lived in rather than wild.","c":"Early Hobbema is close to Ruisdael in subject and weaker in structure; his mature pictures of the late 1660s and 1670s, and The Avenue at Middelharnis of 1689, show a clearer organization of space and brighter light. A work of about 1658 should be read as promising rather than accomplished, with its interest in freshness of observation more than grandeur of design. It shows how much he had absorbed before he found his own manner."},"nga:1170":{"d":"A wide landscape in which two riders, small against the scenery, come toward the viewer along a rutted country road: one on a chestnut horse in a red jacket and a black hat pushed back, the other on a silvery gray horse in brown, with two dogs trotting ahead. At left a wooden bridge crosses a river to a squat watermill with a timbered, canary-yellow wall, a partly tiled, partly thatched roof and a tall water wheel, and ducks float below. Trees in moss, sage and golden green fill the right half, with a church spire behind them, under rolling white and gray clouds.","i":"Hobbema's watermills, with their timbered walls and wheels, were a motif he repeated throughout his career, and they stand for the working, prosperous Dutch countryside. The riders, travelers on a rural road, give the picture its title and its human scale, and the tiny figures in the shade, a man with a sack resting and a man and child walking into the trees, make the road a way for all kinds of people. No sermon is intended. The date is read as the early 1660s, its last digit uncertain.","c":"The Travelers shows Hobbema's characteristic virtues: a clear spatial plan, with a road that leads the eye in a curve and a sunlit mill set against shadowed trees, and a delight in textured materials such as thatch, tile and timber. The riders are tiny, but their lace cuffs show attentiveness. It lacks Ruisdael's drama and relies on charm, and precedes the richer pictures of the later 1660s, yet it is a very good picture of its moment."},"nga:68":{"d":"A rutted dirt road curves from the bottom center toward a grove of tall trees at left, under a pale blue sky with fluffy white clouds that fills the top two-thirds of the picture. Broad canopies throw deep shade over twisting trunks, and a narrow stream mirrors the leaves' greens. At right a sloping bank leads back to open fields with hedgerows to the horizon. Tiny figures are sprinkled through the scene: strolling couples, a man talking with a woman, a fisherman at a pond, a man carrying a pole. Signed and dated 1663 at lower right.","i":"Pictures like this present the Dutch countryside as ordered and peaceful: road, stream, field and hedge, together with small activities such as walking, talking and fishing, form an image of leisure and productive land. Hobbema did not usually moralize, and buyers valued the recognizable, orderly landscape. The wooded landscape was a genre developed by Ruisdael and others, and Hobbema's variants cluster on the road and the stream, in a Republic whose prosperity created the market.","c":"The large sky and deep shade give the picture a measured grandeur, and the winding road leads the eye well, while the tiny figures keep it domestic rather than heroic. Hobbema's trees are not always as convincing in structure as Ruisdael's, and his foliage tends toward pattern, but the open feeling and the soft light across the fields are persuasive. This is a representative, dated example of a painter at his best before the later 1660s."},"nga:1169":{"d":"A dilapidated cottage nestles among leafy trees beside a winding dirt road in a square landscape. It sits to the right of the road within a low rustic fence, with holes in the roof, a slight lean, walls of gray, mustard yellow and brick red, and some panels and windows missing; other windows have diamond-shaped panes. Gnarled trees and scrubby underbrush surround it. Beyond lies a spring-green lawn and a butter-yellow house. A woman holding an orange bundle and a child stand in the road with a thin dog, and a man on horseback rides away.","i":"Poor rural dwellings were a favorite motif for Dutch landscape painters, and Rembrandt etched them as well as Ruisdael painting them. They could suggest neglect or hardship, or simply the picturesque charm of age. Hobbema sets his hut among trees, with a neat yellow house in the distance, so social contrast may be present, though no message is spelled out. The picture most likely invited the viewer to enjoy the well-observed decay and the walk down the road.","c":"The decay is well observed: the leaning wall, the holes in the roof, the patches of mustard and brick give the cottage character, and the square format and winding road make a compact composition. The figures are anecdotal. Hobbema's feeling for ordinary, shabby places is the picture's charm, and it is less conventional than the mill-and-avenue scenes for which he is better known."},"nga:69":{"d":"A wide landscape on canvas, about 93 by 128 cm, dated 1665. The title names a high road, presumably a main route through the countryside, as its subject. Hobbema painted such roads as winding tracks through trees, with small travelers and a cottage or two, and the picture is best described by that type: a road that gives the view its depth, a screen of trees, and a broad sky, built from studio sketches rather than a single recorded spot.","i":"Roads were a favorite subject for Hobbema because they lead the eye into depth and bring figures into the landscape. They also reflect the traffic of a prosperous republic, with its network of tracks linking farms, villages and towns. The title alone does not say where the road runs, and Hobbema's landscapes are generally composites rather than portraits of places, so the picture should be read as an image of Dutch country life, not a topographical record.","c":"Dated 1665, the picture comes from the middle of Hobbema's best decade, when he turned a few formulas, a winding road, a screen of trees, a distant cottage and a bright sky, into very persuasive landscapes. The risk of such a method is repetition, and the pleasure lies in variations of light and order. It is a sound and attractive example rather than a surprising one."},"nga:67":{"d":"A vertical landscape looking across a stream and through a loose screen of shadowy trees at a sunlit farm under a powder-blue sky with towering clouds. The nearest ground is painted in olive green, sepia, brown and slate gray. A group of six people, nearly lost in shadow, gathers at the water's edge at lower left. Beyond, a low picket fence winds between gnarled trunks, and the sun pours from the upper left into a field with tan-walled farm buildings and peach-colored roofs. A church steeple stands in the deep distance, and birds cross the clouds.","i":"The picture dates from 1668, the year Hobbema married and took a post as wine gauger in Amsterdam, after which he painted much less. Its subject is the Dutch farm in sunlight, a prosperous, peaceful countryside seen through a frame of dark trees, with small figures at the water and a church in the distance. As in his other work, the pleasure is in the observation of light and farm life, with no moral or story attached.","c":"The strongest effect is the contrast between the shadowy foreground and the sunlit farm, a classic Dutch device that Hobbema handles with confidence. The upright format is less usual for him, and the sun falling from the upper left gives the field a bright, almost theatrical glow. The figures at the water are tiny and dim, but they serve their purpose of scale. It is one of the more effective and quietly dramatic of his canvases."},"met:436652":{"d":"A wide panel, about 75 by 110 cm, painted around 1665, of the approach to a village. A road leads past trees, a shed, cottages and houses toward a church, with a man on the road as the recorded figure. The title makes the threshold the subject, the point where open country meets settlement. Hobbema painted such views from studio compositions rather than from one spot, and the wooden panel is of a size suited to a domestic interior.","i":"Village entrances, with rutted roads, cottages and a church tower marking the community's center, were stock subjects for Dutch landscape painters and were valued as images of settled rural life. A church gives the village its identity, and a group of houses and sheds suggests ordinary prosperity. The composition usually moves from a foreground screen of trees to a bright middle distance. No story is told, and the viewer is invited simply to enter the place.","c":"The appeal lies in quiet naturalness: no drama and no single idea beyond the pleasure of a path into a village. The device of trees framing the way in and a lit patch beyond is Hobbema's staple, and few painters make a village seem so plausible. If it has a fault it is sameness, since the formula recurs through his work, but the panel shows his skill with air and light in a modest subject."},"nga:1171":{"d":"A landscape of buildings near a body of water. At lower right a person in red leans over a wooden fence to cast a fishing rod, among tall grass and bushes with small pink flowers. A path climbs on the right toward buildings, and two people with several dogs come down it from tall dark trees, one on a white horse, the other holding a stick with birds on it and offering one up to the rider. The path then curves past two large white buildings with pointed red roofs toward a village with a church steeple. The sky is pale blue-gray with fluffy clouds.","i":"A village beside a pool, with a few figures fishing, riding and walking, shows ordinary country pastimes, and the rider with dogs and the man offering a bird may belong to the hunting life of the countryside, though these figures are in shadow and their details are hard to discern. Hobbema's village scenes were built from studio compositions, and the date of about 1670 puts the picture among his mature work. The subject is the quiet life of a settled landscape.","c":"A mature Hobbema, in which the winding path leads the eye past buildings to a distant village, and the limited, rich range of dark greens, browns and grays is handled with some restraint. The pale sky and fluffy clouds give an open feel. It is quieter than his famous mill pictures, and the fishing figure and the rider are minor incidents, but the composition is orderly and pleasing. As a picture of c. 1670 it is representative rather than surprising."},"met:436653":{"d":"A wide landscape, about 95 by 130 cm, painted around 1670, in which a road winds through woodland. Trees and a road are the recorded subjects, and the picture belongs to the familiar type of woodland view, in which the road gives depth, the trees frame it and the sky opens above. By about 1670 Hobbema had settled into the large wooded landscapes on which his reputation rests, in canvases of this generous size made for the Amsterdam market.","i":"The wooded road is the Dutch version of an old landscape formula, familiar from Ruisdael, Wijnants and others, in which a track through trees leads the eye into depth. The trees are the real subject. Such scenes were sold to merchants and regents who liked the look of their own countryside, and the road, a sign of passage and trade, adds a hint of life to a view that might otherwise be only foliage.","c":"After he became a wine gauger in 1668, Hobbema painted much less, so pictures from around 1670 are rarer and often more deliberate. A large canvas of this kind shows a landscape painter who generally managed to keep a leafy woodland airy rather than heavy. The formulas are familiar, but they were executed with conviction, and the picture is a good example of the mature manner."},"met:14399":{"d":"A man, unnamed, is portrayed in a miniature in watercolor on ivory, about 1835. Its size is not recorded, but works of this kind are only a few centimeters high, made to be held in the hand or kept in a case. The sitter fills the picture, with nothing else to distract. A miniature like this centers on the face, rendered in fine, delicate strokes, with the rest of the figure reduced to a simple suggestion of coat and collar.","i":"Agate worked as a miniaturist in New York in the 1830s, and in 1838 he joined the United States Exploring Expedition under Charles Wilkes as an artist, making portraits of Pacific Islanders and Native Americans that are among his best-known works. This gentleman belongs to the more conventional early work, when a miniature cost modest sums and served as a family keepsake or a gift between engaged couples.","c":"A competent example of the miniature at its late-Jacksonian best. At such a small scale the aim is likeness: a clear face, a calm expression, a simple ground. Agate's later fame comes from the expedition portraits, which show a more alert, ethnographic eye than commissions like this one. The small gentleman is the usual product of the trade, pleasant and unrevealing, and a sound introduction to a vanished art."},"met:14993":{"d":"A young woman, identified only as Miss Bruce, is portrayed in a miniature of watercolor on ivory, about 6.7 by 5 centimeters, dated 1837. The title uses the form of address for an unmarried woman, which fits a portrait made for her family or for a betrothed. At this scale the whole picture is a face and a little costume, and its beauty depends on fine detail and the glow of the ivory support.","i":"In the 1830s a miniature was the usual way for a family to preserve a young woman's likeness, to be kept in a case, worn as a locket or exchanged at an engagement. Painted on thin ivory, the colors gain a translucent glow from the pale support beneath. Agate, who later became an artist on the United States Exploring Expedition, supplied such portraits in New York before the daguerreotype began to replace them.","c":"The very small scale rewards close looking: the sheen of the ivory beneath the paint gives the face a gentle luminosity, and the eye is invited to read tiny details of hair and dress. Agate was a better draftsman than many miniaturists, though the genre limits invention to the sitter's expression. It is a pleasing and tender object, and a reminder of how intimate portraiture could be."},"met:437624":{"d":"A long, narrow canvas, about 49 by 117 cm, painted in the early 1780s, showing the Triumph of Bacchus and Ariadne: the god of wine rides in a chariot attended by putti. The wide format suits a frieze or overdoor, and Sauvage was celebrated for decorative panels, often painted to look like sculpted relief. Mythological processions like this were popular in neoclassical interiors, where the classical past supplied gracious, light-hearted ornament.","i":"The triumph of Bacchus, the god's procession with chariot and revelers, was a classical subject known from Roman sarcophagi and revived by eighteenth-century decorators for dining rooms and salons, where wine and revel suited the setting. Ariadne, abandoned by Theseus on Naxos and consoled by Bacchus, shares the triumph, while putti add playfulness. Such pictures asked for nothing more than recognition of the classical theme and enjoyment of its elegance.","c":"Sauvage's reputation rests on illusion, painting that persuades the eye it is marble or plaster. If this canvas follows that manner, its value lies in graceful, compact invention and neoclassical clarity rather than in psychological depth. It is decorative art of a high order for a limited purpose, easily admired and quickly exhausted, but it shows how classical myth was kept alive in late eighteenth-century interiors."},"met:437618":{"d":"A narrow, frieze-like canvas, about 26 by 75 cm, painted in the 1780s and recorded as a work of Sauvage's workshop. It shows an infant bacchanal: putti with grapes, a satyr and goats. The format suits an overdoor or panel in a decorated room, and the subject is a playful revel in which children take the place of the adult followers of Bacchus.","i":"Infant bacchanals, in which putti play at the revels of Bacchus with grapes, goats and satyrs, descend from ancient sculpture and were taken up by Renaissance and Baroque artists. In the eighteenth century they became a staple of decoration, as in the work of Clodion, because they offered the charm of classical antiquity without any moral weight. They were the kind of subject that adorned dining rooms, with wine, grapes and abundance as the theme.","c":"As a workshop product, the picture is valuable chiefly as an example of a decorative formula that Sauvage's studio could repeat to meet demand, not as an individual statement. Its charm lies in the lightness of the idea and the plump, playful figures, and its limitation is that of any ornament: it asks to be enjoyed, not studied. A competent and pleasing piece of neoclassical decoration."},"met:437625":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 36 by 84 cm, painted in the 1780s and recorded as a work of Sauvage's workshop, showing the Triumph of Bacchus and Ariadne. Bacchus rides in a chariot with putti in attendance, the usual elements of the theme. The proportions suit a decorative panel or overdoor in a neoclassical interior, in the manner of the imitation reliefs for which Sauvage was celebrated.","i":"The procession of Bacchus, with his chariot, putti and revelers, was a classical subject that eighteenth-century decorators revived for salons and dining rooms. Ariadne, consoled by Bacchus after Theseus abandoned her, joins the triumph, so the scene joins love and wine in one celebratory image. Sauvage's studio repeated this subject in more than one size, and the repetition suggests how steady the demand for such classical decoration was.","c":"A workshop version of a successful design, the canvas shows how a decorative formula was maintained: a stock of poses and putti recombined to fit different spaces. The result is pleasant and clearly arranged, though necessarily less personal than work from the master's own hand. It is best seen as part of an interior ensemble, and as a record of neoclassical taste, not as a picture to compete with major mythologies."},"met:437620":{"d":"A long, narrow panel of slate, about 24 by 60 cm, painted in oil with a nymph holding a wreath and putti with garlands of flowers. Slate is an unusual support, dark and smooth, used for small decorative pieces and suited to crisp, controlled painting. The picture is undated, and its subject, a nymph attended by putti, is a stock theme of neoclassical ornament, made to decorate a wall, a piece of furniture or an overdoor.","i":"Nymphs, the female spirits of woods, streams and meadows, and putti with garlands of flowers belong to the vocabulary of classical decoration that was fashionable in Europe in the later eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries. They carry no narrative. A wreath and garlands suggest festivity, spring and abundance, and the oil-on-slate technique may reflect Sauvage's interest in painting that imitates carved or molded ornament.","c":"Painting on slate gives a cool, dense, smooth ground that suits crisp, controlled drawing, and a nymph with putti and garlands is a charming and tidy idiom. The object is more an elegant ornament than a picture to ponder, and it is best valued for craft and for what it shows of late neoclassical decoration. Without a precise date or setting it is a minor, agreeable work by a specialist of the genre."},"nga:355":{"d":"A devotional panel, about 80 by 60 cm, painted in tempera around 1475 and since transferred to canvas, showing the Virgin with the Christ Child. Pictures of this kind, made in great numbers in Florentine workshops, usually place Mary close to the viewer with the Child in her arms, and they were meant for the home or a small chapel, where they served daily prayer. The Virgin and Child are the only figures recorded.","i":"The Madonna and Child was among the most common devotional images in Renaissance Florentine homes, hung in bedrooms and reception rooms as a focus for prayer and as protection for the household. Images of mother and son also carried a quiet hint of the Passion, since the Child's destiny is sacrifice. Pier Francesco Fiorentino worked in Florence and the towns of Tuscany, and supplied many such pictures to private and church patrons.","c":"Pier Francesco belongs to the large group of Florentine painters who worked in the wake of the leading masters of the mid-century, turning out graceful, well-made Madonnas for an eager market. His reputation is modest, and his pictures are valued for sweetness and competence more than invention. The transfer from panel to canvas, a drastic treatment common in earlier restoration, can limit how much of the original surface survives."},"met:436519":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 101 by 76 cm, painted in 1873, of a woman before a mirror. Only the woman and the mirror are named, so the picture belongs to a familiar academic type: a single female figure absorbed in her reflection, with the mirror letting the painter show the face a second time. It was a typical Paris Salon subject, probably made for a private buyer rather than for a public building.","i":"A woman at her mirror is among the oldest themes in European art, read in earlier centuries as vanity or as Venus admiring herself. By 1873 the moral had usually thinned into a polite pretext for a beautiful figure in a well-appointed room, and Salon audiences enjoyed it as such. Glaize, the son of the painter Auguste-Barthélemy Glaize and trained in the academic system, painted many such subjects, and the title suggests a quiet, private moment rather than a story.","c":"Glaize is little remembered, and pictures of this kind show both the appeal and the limit of Salon painting: polish and an agreeable subject, with little to disturb the audience. A woman and a mirror tests how a painter handles flesh, fabric and reflected light, and success depends on those passages. As a record of taste in the early Third Republic it is a useful, competent piece rather than an innovation."},"met:436422":{"d":"A large upright panel, about 141 by 103 centimetres, painted in oil and gold, shows the Nativity. The Met's keywords record Mary, Joseph and the infant Christ, and in Sienese altarpieces of this period the Holy Family typically adore the Child, who lies on the ground, while the ox and ass look on and the landscape stretches behind. Gold, used in the haloes and rays, adds to the festive feeling of an altarpiece.","i":"The Nativity (Luke 2) shows the birth of Christ in a stable at Bethlehem. Since the visions of Saint Bridget of Sweden in the fourteenth century, painters often showed Mary kneeling in adoration of the newborn Child lying on the ground, rather than holding him, to emphasise his humility and her reverence. The Child's nakedness on the earth anticipates his sacrifice. Bernardino Fungai worked in Siena, where altarpieces of this kind were commissioned by churches and convents.","c":"Fungai is a charming, conservative Sienese master who blends Sienese elegance with the sweetness of the Umbrian painters then fashionable in Siena. The Nativity gives him the opportunity for gentle devotion, bright colour and pleasing landscape. His figures are neat, even doll-like, and the composition rather formulaic, but it has a quiet harmony. The picture is more graceful than dramatic and is a fair example of Sienese painting in the years around 1500."},"met:435903":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man in military dress, painted on ivory and dated about 1838. The record lists a military officer, a man and a portrait, and the medium as ivory; no size is recorded. The sitter is anonymous, and the museum gives no description of his uniform, rank or face. It was given to the museum in 1925 by Mrs. Louis V. Bell in memory of her husband.","i":"Officers sat for miniatures before leaving on campaign or on appointment, and a miniature of an officer was a keepsake for a family or a sweetheart. In France, the July Monarchy of the 1830s kept a large army occupied in Algeria, where the conquest began in 1830, and a uniform brought status. Little is recorded of the painter, Charles Pierre Cior, and the sitter's anonymity makes the picture a type more than a person.","c":"French miniatures of the 1830s prized a smooth, bright, enamel-like finish, and a uniform with its braid and buttons offered a chance to show it off. The danger of the type is impersonality; a uniform can hide a man. An anonymous example of a form that was near the end of its life as the daguerreotype spread."},"met:437253":{"d":"An enamel portrait miniature of the abbé and mathematician Charles Bossut (1730-1814), dated 1772 and shown in profile. No size is recorded, but enamel portraits were small objects, made to be held in the hand or set in a frame, box or locket. Enamel is painted in powdered-glass colors fired onto a metal plate, which gives a smooth, glossy surface that does not fade, and it demanded precise drawing from the painter.","i":"Bossut was a priest and one of the leading French mathematicians of his day, a member of the Académie des sciences who taught at the military engineering school at Mézières and wrote widely used textbooks. A profile portrait, in the manner of antique coins and medals, suited the neoclassical taste of the 1770s for sitters presented with the gravity of Roman worthies, and it made the scientific man a civic figure. Whether the miniature was a gift or a keepsake is not recorded.","c":"In enamel, colors can hardly be reworked after firing, so the form demands exact drawing and nerve, and the profile, with its single clear line, leaves nowhere to hide. Pasquier gives his sitter the dignity of a medal, though a profile by nature limits expression. As an object it matters for craft more than for psychological insight, and it is a good example of the Parisian enamelist's art in the 1770s."},"met:437254":{"d":"An enamel portrait miniature of a man named Gérard de Vesme, dated in the 1770s with the last digit uncertain. No size is recorded, but enamels of this kind were small, made to be held in the hand or set into a frame or case. In kind it is a likeness, usually a bust, painted in fired colors on a metal plate, a costly and lasting medium for a private portrait.","i":"Enamel miniatures were prized because their colors do not fade, and they were commissioned as keepsakes and gifts, worn or kept in boxes. A portrait of a named gentleman probably records a private commission rather than a public image. Pasquier belonged to the Parisian circle of enamelists who served courtiers and officials. Nothing about Vesme himself is recorded here, so the portrait's meaning is mainly that of the object: a durable token of a person.","c":"With the sitter little known, the miniature has to be judged as enamel: clarity of likeness, smoothness of flesh, control of color after firing. Pasquier worked in a demanding craft in which accuracy and polish were virtues and invention was not expected. The result should be a polite, well-made likeness, valuable as an example of its medium more than as a work with a story to tell."},"met:437290":{"d":"A gold-ground panel, about 78 by 56.5 cm, painted in tempera and dated 1420, showing the Virgin and Child attended by angels. A harp and a mandolin are among the instruments recorded, so the angels make music for the pair. The gold background marks a sacred, timeless space in the manner of late Gothic painting in Italy, and the picture was made as a devotional panel for a chapel or a private room.","i":"Angel musicians around the Virgin were a favorite device in late medieval Italy: they presented her as Queen of Heaven and suggested the harmony of the heavenly court. The harp and the plucked string instrument were familiar from courtly and civic music, which linked the sacred scene to sounds viewers knew. Pietro di Domenico da Montepulciano is a little-known painter, and the date of 1420 is a useful anchor for his work.","c":"A rare dated work, it should show the refined line, decorative color and gold of the International Gothic, a style soon to be overtaken in Florence by Masaccio and his circle. Judged against that coming revolution it looks backward, but its charm lies in its music and ornament, and in the survival of a regional painter's work. It is valuable as a document of its moment more than as an innovation."},"met:436976":{"d":"A vertical forest-floor still life, about 68 by 53 cm, painted around 1670, in which a poppy is shown with insects, snails, lizards and snakes. The type, called sottobosco, Italian for undergrowth, sets small creatures among weeds, thistles and flowers at ground level, often against a dark ground that makes their bright forms stand out. The picture is a cabinet piece for a collector of natural curiosities as much as of paintings.","i":"Marseus van Schrieck, who spent years in Italy and was nicknamed 'Snuffelaer' (the sniffer) by his fellow Dutch artists in Rome, was said to have kept snakes, lizards and insects in his garden in order to paint them from life. The poppy, source of opium, is linked with sleep and death, and the reptiles and insects may carry thoughts of mortality, but collectors probably also enjoyed the sheer strangeness of nature.","c":"These pictures live by contrast: a dark ground, bright, slick skins and the odd beauty of leaf and wing. Marseus pioneered the type, and Mignon and others followed him. The weakness is the staging, which can feel like a taxidermist's arrangement, and an airless dark ground. As the formula in its pure form the picture is more curious than moving, but the curiosity is the point."},"met:437657":{"d":"A tiny oil panel, about 17.5 by 26 cm, mounted on a second wooden panel, painted in 1878 or 1879 when Seurat was about twenty. The title places it at Saint-Ouen, a suburb of Paris on the Seine, and it is recorded as a landscape: an outdoor sketch rather than a finished picture. Small wood panels like this were what a young painter carried into the fields and suburbs around Paris, to be painted quickly and kept as study.","i":"In these years Seurat was an art student, enrolled at the École des Beaux-Arts in 1878, who sketched in the countryside and suburbs around Paris. His early sketches owe much to the Barbizon painters and to Corot, years before the dot method of A Sunday on La Grande Jatte. The edges of Paris, where city and country met, would remain a favorite subject, and a modest suburban corner like this is the kind of scene his generation found modern.","c":"A sketch this small and early is tentative, and it should not be judged against the famous dot paintings. Its interest lies in what it shows of the young painter's habits: quick seeing, simplified masses and a modest subject taken seriously. Seen on its own, it is a direct, sincere and unshowy panel, and collectors prize it as an early link in the chain that leads to La Grande Jatte."},"met:437655":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 79 by 62.5 cm, painted in 1881, showing a forest at Pontaubert, a village in Burgundy. Tree trunks and the understory of the wood are the recorded motifs, and the upright format suits the tall trunks. It is an early, comparatively large landscape by Seurat, from the years before he developed his dot method, when he was still learning from the Barbizon painters and Corot.","i":"In 1881 Seurat was in his early twenties and still working in a style close to the Barbizon painters, who had made the forest interior a subject for serious landscape, and a wood like this recalls Rousseau and Diaz. Over the next few years he would develop his technique of small juxtaposed strokes of color under the influence of color theory, including the writings of Chevreul. Pontaubert is a rural place near Avallon, away from Paris.","c":"The forest interior, a tall, closed composition with little sky, is an unusual choice for the Impressionist era, which preferred open light. The picture is probably more a study of mass and rhythm, with trunks building a pattern, than of atmosphere, and it lacks the clarity of structure Seurat would soon achieve. Its seriousness and early feeling for design make it more interesting than its modest reputation suggests."},"nga:164958":{"d":"A small, loosely painted panel, about 16 by 25 cm. Two people stand side by side in a field, raking, with their backs to the viewer. Soft, textured brushstrokes and loosely rendered details give the scene a hazy look. Both wear long skirts, shirts and head coverings in pearl white, periwinkle and sky blue, and each holds a long-handled tool and looks down at the ground. The field is fresh green with strokes of teal and muted yellow, and a darker green band at the top suggests a line of trees.","i":"Around 1882 Seurat painted a series of small outdoor panels of farm workers near Paris, in the manner of Millet's laborers, whose drawings he admired, and probably with the simplified figures of Puvis de Chavannes in mind. The work of the field is treated without anecdote: figures reduced to a few touches and blended with the ground they work. The panels were probably private studies, made to work out harmony of color and form rather than to be shown.","c":"These small panels are less about haymaking than about touch: soft strokes in pearl white, periwinkle and sky blue against fresh greens give the whole a hazy shimmer. The loose handling is closer to Impressionism and the Barbizon manner than to the dots to come, but the economy of the two bent figures already shows Seurat's gift for reducing a scene to a few large, quiet shapes. They are sketches, and charming ones."},"nga:164959":{"d":"A small panel, about 15 by 24 cm. The background is built of horizontal bands of browns and greens in short, overlapping strokes. In the center a person bends forward, facing right, and raises a thin tool above the head. The figure wears a blue shirt and brown trousers, with a patch of pinkish gray on the head that may be hair or a hat, and is painted in the same short strokes as the ground, so that the figure appears blurred, with little detail.","i":"Peasant labor was a major subject of French art in the second half of the nineteenth century, associated above all with Millet, and the hoe, the humblest tool, became a symbol of the toil of the land. Seurat treats the worker as an anonymous, bending shape in the field, painted in the same strokes as the soil, so that figure and ground blend. The effect is of rhythm and pattern more than of social comment, though the dignity given to the humble gesture is plain.","c":"The merging of figure and ground is the point: bands of brown and green, a blue shirt as the one cool note, and a single repeated stroke for everything. The panel is tiny and has no fine details, which keeps it from sentiment. It is one of the group in which Seurat tests how little drawing a figure needs when color and touch do the work, and as such it is more important than its size suggests."},"nga:164960":{"d":"A small panel, about 15 by 24 cm, in which short, angular brushstrokes suggest a person in motion. The figure bends forward, facing left, with a large, thin object raised over one shoulder. The person has pinkish skin and wears a white shirt, blue trousers and a black hat, and the face has no defined features. Yellow strokes like piles of hay or dry grass surround the figure against a dark green background. The thick, overlapping handling gives the whole a hazy look.","i":"The stone breaker, who smashed rocks into gravel for roads, was a standard emblem of the hardest, worst-paid manual labor, and Courbet's Stone Breakers of 1849 had made it a symbol in French painting. Seurat's panel has none of Courbet's confrontation: the worker is anonymous, the face undefined, and the motion of the raised tool is the real subject. Laborers of road and field kept appearing in the art of the 1880s as a record of modern rural life.","c":"The best thing here is the sense of motion: short, angular strokes make the raised arm and bent back read at once, and the yellow strokes around the figure, like hay or dry grass, give a warm halo against the dark green. The thick, overlapping handling is hazy and the figure has no features, which keeps the panel from sentiment. It is a study of labor in action, more than a portrait of any worker."},"nga:164968":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 16 by 25 cm, of haystacks in a field. Five large golden-brown stacks, shaped like tall cones, stand in a line that cuts diagonally across the green field from left to right, with more hay on the ground beneath them. The field has patches of yellow, purple and light blue. Above it, long horizontal strips of white cloud cross a light blue sky, and at the horizon a line of purple and dark blue shapes suggests distant trees or low buildings. Short, textured strokes make the landscape look blurred.","i":"Haystacks were a standard sign of the harvest and of the rural economy in French landscape, painted by Millet and the Barbizon artists long before Monet made them a series in 1890 and 1891. Seurat's version dates from about 1882, when he was making small outdoor panels around Paris and experimenting with color. The patches of yellow, purple and blue in the field suggest an early interest in pairing complementary colors, though no message is intended beyond the pleasure of the scene.","c":"The geometry is the interest: five cones in a rising diagonal turn an ordinary farm sight into a rhythm, and the lined horizon and bands of cloud steady it. The soft, blurred touch is still far from the dot technique of his mature work, and the picture is slight. But it already shows his instinct for reducing nature to simple solids and for treating a small panel as a complete composition."},"nga:164969":{"d":"A small panel, about 16 by 25 cm, showing a person bent over and working the land outdoors. The figure stands in the center, surrounded by green grass and dark trees, with no sky visible, and a section of brown earth fills the lower left corner. Leaning over the edge of that patch, the person puts a long-handled tool to the ground. The clothes are gray and the hat is dark. Broad, short strokes of greens, dark blues, yellows, browns and ochres overlap almost like a kaleidoscope.","i":"A man with a hoe working a patch of earth recalls the peasants of Millet, whose Man with a Hoe was widely known, and the countryside near Paris where Seurat painted in these years. The panel has no story and no sky, only a figure and the ground he works, which gives it an unusual, enclosed intimacy. Seurat's small panels of 1882 were private experiments with color, and this one shows him layering small strokes of different colors over one another.","c":"The kaleidoscope of overlapping greens, blues, yellows, browns and ochres gives the panel a vibrating surface in which the man almost dissolves, and that dissolving is its interest: it shows color doing the work of drawing. A viewer who expects the later dots will find the touch broader and looser, but the principle that colors mix in the eye is already at work. As an experiment it is more suggestive than complete."},"met:437656":{"d":"A small oil panel, about 16 by 25 cm, painted in 1882 to 1883, showing a gardener in a garden. A man, a garden and the gardener's task are the recorded subjects, so the picture belongs to the group of small outdoor panels in which Seurat studied workers in the fields and gardens near Paris. Such panels were painted quickly, in loose strokes, and kept as private studies rather than shown.","i":"Laborers in fields and gardens suited Seurat, who admired the peasants of Millet and wanted to give modern workers the same quiet dignity. A gardener is an unassertive figure, tending a patch of the suburban landscape, and the panel records an ordinary task without comment. In the year after this sketch Seurat began his first large canvas, and the small panels of 1882 and 1883 show him testing his touch and his color on humble subjects.","c":"On the evidence of Seurat's panels of this date, the figure is likely reduced to a few quick, blended touches, and the interest lies in how little drawing is needed to suggest a body at work. The picture belongs to a transitional moment, when he still painted in a Barbizon manner but was beginning to think systematically about color. It is a modest sketch, worth seeing for what it prepares."},"met:437659":{"d":"A small oil panel, about 16 by 25 cm, painted in 1882 to 1883, showing a stretch of the Seine with boats. Rivers, boats and Paris are the recorded subjects, so the view is of the river in or near the capital. Like Seurat's other small wood panels of these years, it is an outdoor sketch, painted quickly to catch a passing effect of light on water and sky, rather than a finished picture for exhibition.","i":"The Seine and its banks were Seurat's chief subject for several years, from sketches of boats and barges to the big canvases of Asnières and La Grande Jatte. For Parisians the river was both a working waterway and a place of Sunday leisure, and the suburbs along it were where the city's industry and its recreation met. Whether this panel records a particular spot is not recorded here.","c":"A river panel lets a painter practice the problem that mattered most to Seurat: how water, sky and boats can be made of the same few touches of color. The sketch is small and probably quickly done, yet the type is one that led to the cool, still river of Bathers at Asnières. It is a minor but telling step, and its interest is mostly in the development it records."},"nga:164961":{"d":"A small vertical panel, about 25 by 15 cm, painted around 1883. A large gray watering can sits at the bottom right, on the edge of a dirt path that extends from the lower left up toward a white wall running across the scene. Bushes on the right are speckled with dots of pink, yellow, blue, orange and purple that suggest flowers, and above the wall dark green trees leave a small patch of light blue sky. The paint is applied in small visible dots and short strokes.","i":"A watering can in a suburban garden is an unheroic, domestic subject, and the choice shows Seurat's habit of finding pictures in the ordinary corners of his surroundings. Le Raincy, east of Paris, was a leafy suburb, and a garden is a place of private leisure, unlike the public leisure of the river banks he would paint in his large canvases. The dots of color that suggest flowers are an early use of the small, separate touches he later made his method.","c":"The unusual feature is the object: a watering can almost fills a corner and gives a still-life weight to what would otherwise be a garden view. The wall and path divide the panel into a few simple planes, and the speckled bushes hint at the dot technique. It is slight, but the combination of plain geometry and glittering color is characteristic, and it makes a modest garden corner into a composed picture."},"nga:164962":{"d":"A small panel, about 16 by 25 cm, of expansive grassland under a textured blue and white sky. The horizon is high, set off by a thin line of dark trees and small white buildings, and a tall tree rises at the far left. A small dark silhouette of a person stands in the grass at left. Tiny dashes of blue and peach decorate the grass, and streaks of red and peach lie in the sky. The short, feathery strokes give the scene a blurred, shimmering quality.","i":"Painted in 1883, while Seurat was at work on his first large canvas, Bathers at Asnières, this panel belongs to the series of small outdoor sketches he made around Paris as private experiments. The flat open land, the high horizon and the tiny silhouetted figure place the human presence at the edge of a large field. Dashes of blue and peach in the grass and red in the sky are early signs of the broken, complementary color he would soon systematize.","c":"What succeeds is the openness: a wide field, a high horizon and a sky of textured strokes give the little panel a surprising sense of space. The feathery touch is looser than the later dots, and the figure is a mere mark, but it is enough for scale. Without the ambitions of the large canvases, the panel is a relaxed, fresh record of a summer day, and a good place to see the young painter's eye at work."},"nga:164963":{"d":"A small panel, about 15 by 25 cm, of several people spaced across a grassy expanse. The horizon is high, lined with white buildings with red roofs and green trees. Closest to the viewer are dark silhouettes of people at leisure: one seems to sit on a grassy hill, while others walk in opposite directions down a path, two holding umbrellas. The figures are painted with thick dark strokes and have no detail inside their forms. Beyond them a wide pink stretch could be a river or a path, in a palette of warm earth tones, muted greens and soft pinks.","i":"Parisians at leisure on suburban grass, strolling, sitting and carrying umbrellas, are the subject Seurat would make his own in Bathers at Asnières and A Sunday on La Grande Jatte. In this small sketch of about 1883 the figures are silhouettes without interior detail, an early step toward the anonymous, stylized figures of the large canvases, which present the modern crowd as a pattern more than as a set of individuals.","c":"The figures are blunt, dark silhouettes, and that is the interesting choice: Seurat reduces people to shape and lets the loose, layered strokes of green, pink and blue carry the movement of the landscape. The picture has more energy than the calm, frozen large canvases that came after, which turned such sketches into stillness. It is a lively sketch rather than a finished idea, and valuable for showing the starting point."},"met:437658":{"d":"A sunlit riverside park is seen across a broad lawn, painted in small dabs of colour. At the left the Seine glitters with sailboats, and a woman in a long russet dress fishes from the bank. Figures rest in the shade at the lower left; a child in white stands at the centre, and a woman with a red parasol stands behind. At the right a tall woman in a dark dress stands with a small monkey on a leash at her feet, and a brown dog sniffs the grass at the centre. Tree trunks and leafy shade cut across the lawn in stripes of light. A border of tiny coloured dots frames the scene. Oil on canvas, about 70 by 104 centimetres.","i":"This is one of the studies Seurat made for his large canvas A Sunday on La Grande Jatte, which shows Parisians relaxing on an island in the Seine just outside the city. The study dates from 1884, when he began the big painting; he worked on it in two campaigns, from 1884 to 1885 and from 1885 to 1886. At first Seurat mixed ordinary paints with earth colours; only in the second campaign did he break his colours into small dots of pure pigment, meant to blend in the viewer's eye. The finished picture, about two by three metres, was shown at the last Impressionist exhibition in 1886 and made Seurat the leader of Neo-Impressionism.","c":"Next to the finished painting, which freezes its crowd into stiff silhouettes, the study is loose and sunny. The fisherwoman, the dog, the monkey and the child in white are all in place, but the figures move more naturally, and the lawn has a breezy vitality that the final version trades for order. What is missing is the monumental calm that makes Seurat Seurat; this is the Impressionist beginning of his Sunday afternoon, not its classical end. It is valuable as a record of an idea taking shape, and charming in its own right."},"nga:164964":{"d":"A horizontal sketch in which a grassy riverbank cuts across the lower left corner and a river fills most of the panel. Three people sit along the steep bank, in a marine-blue shirt, a plum-purple garment and a white shirt, all in caps. Three more stand in the water near the bank, two bare-chested and one immersed to the chin, and the head of a white horse enters at lower right. Across the river, trees are painted in criss-crossing strokes of moss green and azure, with two sailboats as vertical strokes of white.","i":"Seurat made a series of oil sketches for Bathers at Asnières in 1883 and 1884, each fixing one element of the composition: the bathers on the bank, the figures in the water, the horse and the boats. The subject, working-class men bathing in the Seine at the edge of the industrial suburbs, was new for a large painting and deliberately contemporary. In the sketches faces are indistinct and figures simplified, anticipating the anonymity of the final work.","c":"The loose strokes and the visible handling of the water, in horizontal marks of blue, green and tan, make this a more painterly object than the polished final painting. What is striking is how much the scene is already organized: the seated group, the standing bathers, the diagonal bank and the white horse as an accent. It is a study, and the thing to notice is its economy, a whole social world suggested in a handful of touches."},"nga:164965":{"d":"A small riverbank scene, about 16 by 25 cm. In the foreground three people in dark blue clothes sit on a grassy slope that leads down to light blue water, facing right; one wears a yellow wide-brimmed hat, and their faces are indistinct. In the water behind them a person in a white shirt appears to swim, and small boats glide beyond, one with a red, white and blue striped flag. A brown horse stands in the river at left. Short, thick strokes give the whole a hazy look.","i":"This panel is one of the oil sketches Seurat made in 1883 and 1884 for Bathers at Asnières, his first large painting, which shows working men resting and bathing on the Seine bank at the industrial edge of Paris. The horse standing in the river, the boats and the figures on the grass are elements he worked out in separate studies before combining them in the large canvas, which the official Salon rejected in 1884 and which was shown at the Salon des Indépendants instead.","c":"The sketch is where the large painting's logic can be seen at work: the group on the bank, the slope, the flat plane of blue water with small boats as accents. Its short, thick strokes are hazy and unfinished, yet the placement is already confident. More spontaneous than the finished canvas, whose frieze-like calm took long reworking, it is a lively and informative piece."},"nga:164966":{"d":"A study of the river, about 17 by 26 cm. Nearest the viewer a pile of objects sits on a grassy bank: an indistinct white bundle with a gold and black object perched on it, surrounded by smaller round brown lumps. A green slope stretches along the left with a rectangular yellow section, and on the right the river is painted in light blue with lines of green, red and tan. In the distance a large pale bridge spans the water, with vertical shapes like smokestacks, one trailing pale pink and purple smoke, and tall dark trees surround light shapes that may be buildings.","i":"In the finished Bathers at Asnières, the men have left their clothes on the bank, and across the water a bridge and factory chimneys give the scene its modern setting at the industrial edge of Paris. This sketch isolates that setting and the still life of clothing, implying the absent bathers. Seurat made such separate studies of single elements before assembling them in the studio into the large canvas.","c":"A study with no figures can be more striking than the crowded ones: the pile of clothing, the pale bridge and the pink and purple smoke give a quiet, wry image of an industrial suburb at leisure. The thick, visible brushwork blends color and edges, so details are hard to identify, which is the character of a rapid study. It anticipates the cool, clear air of the finished painting."},"nga:164967":{"d":"A small panel, about 15 by 24 cm, in which thick, overlapping strokes build the blurred image of people standing and sitting on grass. In the center two people in pink and dark purple sit on the green ground facing left; the one in front holds a parasol and the one behind a red flower. At top left two people seem to stand, one apparently a child in white holding a red object, beside a column of pink that suggests an adult whose head lies beyond the edge. Strong shadows turn parts of the grass dark green.","i":"Seurat composed La Grande Jatte in the studio, assembling it from many separate studies of landscape and of figures like this one. The parasol, the flower and the child are the props of a Sunday in the park, and the poses of the seated and standing figures are worked out here before being fixed in the large canvas. The cast shadows, rendered as areas of dark green rather than black, show his interest in the effect of sunlight on color.","c":"The figures are stiff, blurred and reduced to a few strokes, and the sketch is of interest for what it settles: poses, the placement of a parasol, the pools of shadow on the grass. It is a modest working panel, not a picture meant for display. Compared with the large canvas it has little of the later calm, but it shows how the final order was built from small, practical decisions."},"nga:52225":{"d":"A small horizontal sketch painted with dots and dashes of fresh green, shimmering blue, amethyst purple, shell pink, pale yellow and cream white, with the brown wood of the panel visible throughout. Loosely painted as it is, a scene comes into focus: a grassy area hemmed in at left and along the back by water, a person sitting near the bank at left, violet daubs that could be more people walking among trees, and an arc of white that suggests a sailboat near the shore. The panel is signed 'Seurat' in red at lower left.","i":"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte, painted over two years from 1884 to 1886, shows Parisians at leisure on an island in the Seine, and Seurat prepared it with dozens of drawings and small oil panels like this one. Each sketch worked out a problem of light, grass, water or figures. Using dots and dashes with the panel's warm wood showing through, he tested on a small scale the divided color he would apply to the huge final canvas.","c":"The visible brown of the wood works as a warm middle tone against which the cool greens, blues and purples shimmer, and the result is almost abstract until the bank, the water and the sailboat declare themselves. The sketch has the freshness the large canvas had to give up in return for order. That Seurat signed it may suggest he valued it as more than a study, and it deserves attention as a small, complete picture."},"met:438123":{"d":"A very small vertical panel, about 16.5 by 12.4 cm, painted in oil on wood around 1881, showing a man leaning on a parapet, with trees in the scene. Like Seurat's other early panels, it is an outdoor sketch of a figure at rest, usually painted in a few broad touches to catch a posture and a patch of light rather than a face. Nothing more of the setting is known.","i":"A man leaning on a parapet, whether a quay wall or a bridge, is the kind of casual urban figure that painters of modern life noticed in the 1860s to 1880s: the idler, the stroller, the onlooker. Seurat's early sketches of figures in Paris and its suburbs, made before the large canvases, record such unassuming types. The man is a type, not a portrait, and his identity is not recorded.","c":"The panel is small and early, and its pleasure lies in economy: a posture, a tree and a wall are enough. In the context of Seurat's career it shows the young painter learning to see figures as simple masses before he developed his dots, and it is the kind of unpretentious sketch a viewer might overlook beside his famous works. Its value is as evidence of habits of observation."},"nga:61383":{"d":"A horizontal seascape painted entirely with small dots of pure color, mostly slate and azure blue, olive green and cream white. A sandy beach stretches to a tall, slender lighthouse at the center, speckled with azure, ivory and peach, and at right a four-story building with three levels of windows and a row of dormers extends off the canvas. A denim-blue shed with an orange roof stands in front of it, with a rowboat on the sand. At left a pier runs along the horizon beside a sailboat, and sunlight shimmers on the sea beneath a clear, ice-blue sky.","i":"In the summer of 1886, after showing La Grande Jatte in Paris, Seurat went to the Normandy coast and painted a group of seascapes at Honfleur, a harbor town already associated with Boudin, Jongkind and Monet. Painted in his mature dot technique, these pictures replace the crowds of his Paris subjects with empty beaches and quiet architecture. The lighthouse and the building mark a plain, human structure in a vast, bright setting.","c":"The picture is a study in stillness: lighthouse, building and shed stand at calm intervals in a composition built on a horizon about two-thirds of the way up, and the dots make the sea shimmer without fuss. The dotted method can look mechanical, but here the effect is of silence and clear air, and the orange roof and blue shed give a precise complementary accent. It is a quietly accomplished example of Seurat's mature seascape."},"met:437654":{"d":"Under a row of gas lamps like flaming crowns, a street show is staged on a platform. In the centre a tall trombonist in a pointed hat stands rigid, his instrument held vertically. At the left three musicians in bowler hats stand in a line behind a rail, playing. At the right a small boy in a high white collar waits beside a moustached man in a long coat, shown in profile. A leafless tree rises at the left, and across the bottom edge the dark silhouettes of the audience's heads and hats pile up. The whole surface is built from tiny dots of colour, in oranges, ochres, blues and greens, which gives the scene a shimmering, dusty light. The figures are flat, stylised and almost motionless.","i":"Seurat painted this in 1887–88 and showed it at the Salon des Indépendants in 1888. It records the Corvi Circus advertising itself with a free sideshow, or parade, at the Gingerbread Fair, held each spring in a working-class district around the Place de la Nation. The Met's research identifies the moustached man at the right as the owner and ringmaster, Ferdinand Corvi. Seurat left out the animals the circus was known for. His method, based on the colour writings of Michel-Eugène Chevreul and Ogden Rood, set small dots of unmixed colour side by side to be blended by the eye. Whether the stiffness is satire of mass entertainment or a formal exercise in rhythm has been debated.","c":"Few pictures hold a noisy subject so still. The row of heads at the bottom, the lamps at the top, the rigid vertical of the trombone and the slant of the rail form a grid in which the fairground falls silent. The cost is warmth. The figures are expressionless, and the dots dull the colour into a dusty haze, so the picture can feel airless. It is one of Seurat's most schematic works, admired for its design more than for any feeling in its faces."},"met:438015":{"d":"A river view painted in Seurat's dot technique, about 70 by 86 cm, from about 1886 to 1888. A river, boats, a house and trees are recorded, and the title gives the weather: a gray day, an unusual choice for a painter so often associated with bright sunlight. The view looks across the Seine from La Grande Jatte, the island in the river that was also the setting of his best-known picture.","i":"La Grande Jatte, an island in the Seine at Neuilly and Levallois, was a place of weekend leisure and, increasingly, of industry, and Seurat returned to it after finishing the large painting of 1884 to 1886. The title puts the picture in the Impressionist tradition of painting the same place under changing conditions of light, though Seurat applies his own method of small, separate touches of color, in which grays are made from many hues rather than from a mixed gray.","c":"A gray day tests the divided-color method harder than a sunny one, since there are no strong contrasts to exploit, and the muted harmony of river, sky and boats should show Seurat's mastery of tone. The result is likely calm, quiet and a little melancholic, lacking the monumental stillness of La Grande Jatte but gaining a lyrical softness. It is a sophisticated landscape of his last years, and rather underrated beside his figure pictures."},"nga:53139":{"d":"Green hills roll back to a turquoise-blue sea in an almost square canvas. The scene is built of layers of small dots in lavender, lilac, cool blue, warm green, yellow and peach. A hill angles from near the top left corner to the lower right, filling the bottom left half. It is mostly variegated greens, with two cliff-like purple surfaces at left and a lighter yellow patch at right. A pole and a cross-shaped structure atop the hill could be utility poles, and a distant object on the water may be a buoy or a boat. Peach lines the horizon, and straw yellow and cornflower blue ripple across the sky.","i":"Seurat spent the summer of 1888 at Port-en-Bessin, a small fishing port on the Normandy coast, and painted a group of seascapes there, finishing them in his Paris studio from small studies made on the spot. This one looks at the countryside above the sea rather than at the harbor. The poles and the cross-shaped structure may be telegraph or utility poles, a sign of modern infrastructure in a rural view, though their meaning is not recorded.","c":"The big dotted hill is less a picture of a place than a study of how many colors can sit in one patch of green, and the lavender, lilac, warm green and peach give the surface a vibrating richness. The composition is bold, with a diagonal slope cutting the square in half and the small sea and sky as counterweights. It is quieter and emptier than La Grande Jatte, but the emptiness is chosen."},"nga:157929":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 16 by 25 cm, made entirely of dots and touches of sky and royal blue, butter and sunshine yellow and spring green. Seen from a distance, it resolves into a sandy dune in front of the sea. Bands of blue against a yellow field above the horizon read as blue clouds against a sunny sky. A darker border of dots in navy blue, army green and a little rust orange runs round the edge and forms a painted frame.","i":"Seurat painted at Gravelines, a fortified port on the North Sea coast of northern France, in the summer of 1890, the last he would live to see: he died in March 1891 at thirty-one. The painted border is his own device. By surrounding a picture with dots chosen to contrast with the colors inside, he tried to isolate it from the white or gilt frames of the time. The scene itself is almost empty, a dune, the sea and the sky, which makes it a pure study of light.","c":"The painted frame is the surprise: it treats the picture as an object whose edges matter, and turns a tiny sketch into a finished, self-contained thing. Within it, a few bands of blue, yellow and green are enough for sun, sand and sea, and the economy is remarkable. It is a late, spare work in which the method is under full control, though its quietness may disappoint those who come for crowds and parasols."},"nga:206126":{"d":"A small altarpiece of three arched panels on a gold ground, dated 1354 in its inscription. The wider central panel shows the Virgin and Child enthroned among saints and angels, and the narrow wings show Saint Anthony Abbot at left and Saint Venantius at right. Spiraling gold columns separate the panels, and triangular gables above are filled with stylized leaves and animals on black. Small shapes at the top of the wings hold an angel and a haloed figure, and a crucified figure fills the central gable.","i":"Triptychs of this kind were made for chapels and convents, and the format, a central holy image flanked by saints, allowed the patron's devotions to be written into the picture. Here the saints are Anthony Abbot, the hermit linked to the Antonine order and to protection against skin diseases, and Venantius, a young martyr of the Marches. A damaged inscription records a friar named Giovanni and the date 1354. The altarpiece is recorded as the work of Puccio di Simone with Allegretto Nuzi.","c":"Seen whole, the triptych is a handsome example of Florentine Gothic: gold, lapis, brick red and decorative gables, arranged in a clear, symmetrical court with the wings turned inward toward the center. It is conservative, with figures stacked rather than placed in space, as in the generation after Giotto, but its richness and clarity as an object are real. Where single panels may seem thin, together they achieve an effect of ceremonial splendor."},"nga:6":{"d":"The left wing of a gold-ground triptych, a narrow arched panel about 89 by 34 cm, painted in tempera in 1354. A balding, bearded man in a brown cloak over a black robe stands in near profile facing right, his hood across his shoulders. He holds a wooden crutch with a curved top and raises his other hand, palm out. His halo is incised in the gold and inscribed with his name, and a black pig nearly disappears against his dark robes at his feet. The floor is patterned like brocade, with gold three-lobed designs on brick red.","i":"Saint Anthony Abbot, the hermit of the Egyptian desert (about 251-356) often called the father of monks, is recognized by his crutch or T-shaped staff and by the pig, an emblem of the demons that tempted him and, later, of the hospital brothers who bore his name. His halo here calls him Anthony 'of Vienne,' pointing to the Antonine order, which cared for the sick. The central panel's inscription mentions a friar, so the choice of saint may reflect the patron.","c":"As a single panel the wing is austere and grave: the ash-gray face, the dark robe and the raised hand are set against burnished gold with real conviction, and the near-profile turn directs attention to the center. The pig, almost lost in the dark robe, is a small failure of legibility, and the brocade floor is more decorative than convincing as space. It is a competent, conservative figure, best understood as one part of a larger design."},"nga:7":{"d":"The central panel of a triptych, about 108 by 58 cm, tempera on a gold ground, dated 1354. The Virgin and Child sit enthroned on two wide steps, attended by four saints and eighteen angels whose plate-like halos overlap. Mary, in a lapis-blue mantle edged and starred with gold, gently touches the Child's chin as they gaze at each other. Jesus, in blue and gold on dark orange, holds a bird in one hand, touches her robe with the other, and wears a piece of coral on a cord at his neck. A damaged line along the bottom mentions a friar.","i":"The Virgin enthroned among saints and angels presents Mary as Queen of Heaven in her court, a standard image for Italian altarpieces. The Child's bird, in later art usually a goldfinch, is read as a sign of the Passion, and the coral at his neck was both a protective charm and a symbol of Christ's blood. Mary's halo calls her Mother of God. The damaged line at the bottom records the year 1354 and a friar named Giovanni, probably the patron, though the rest of his name is lost.","c":"The central panel shows Florentine Gothic taste at its most decorative: gold, lapis blue and brick red, and overlapping halos that make a rhythmic pattern across the picture. The touch under the chin brings a human tenderness to a ceremonial court. The attendants, however, are interchangeable, and the stacked figures give little depth, so the effect is richness more than space. It is a refined, conservative work of the generation after Giotto."},"nga:8":{"d":"The right wing of a gold-ground triptych, a narrow arched panel about 89 by 33 cm, painted in tempera in 1354. A blond, clean-shaven young man stands turned toward the center, looking at Mary and Jesus. He wears a long orange tunic decorated with blue flowers, a peacock-blue cloak and matching boots, and holds a staff from which flags in gold on black or red flutter, his other hand on a gold belt. The floor is patterned like brocade, and his halo is inscribed with his name as a martyr.","i":"Saint Venantius, a young martyr of the third century, is the patron saint of Camerino in the Marches and is usually shown as a youth with a banner, the sign of a martyr's victory. His presence beside an Antonine saint may hint at the patron's connections, perhaps to central Italy, where Allegretto Nuzi, recorded as a collaborator on this altarpiece, was active, but the patron's own history is not recorded here. A figure holding a hand to the chest appears in the gable above.","c":"Of the three panels, this wing is the most colorful. The orange tunic with blue flowers and the peacock-blue cloak are fashionable, courtly dress, and the young saint's glance toward the center connects the panels. He is an elegant, slightly dandyish figure, more Gothic page than martyr, and the flags give a flutter of movement against the gold. A fine example of the decorative taste of mid-fourteenth-century painting."},"met:438541":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 34 by 45 cm, painted around 1838 to 1840, showing an episode from Caesar's Gallic War: the Gaul Litaviccus, who has betrayed the Roman cause, flees to Gergovia to join Vercingetorix. Men, horses and a battle are named among its subjects, so the scene is one of riders in the manner of academic history painting. At this size it is probably a sketch or composition study in which a very young painter works out the movement of figures and horses.","i":"The story comes from Caesar's account of his campaign in Gaul in 52 BC. Litaviccus, an Aeduan noble serving with the Roman army, deceived his troops by claiming that Caesar had massacred their leaders, led them to revolt and fled to Gergovia, where Vercingetorix held out; the siege of Gergovia was one of Caesar's few failures in Gaul. In the 1830s French writers had begun to treat the Gauls as national ancestors, which made such subjects timely.","c":"The picture shows a very young painter, trained by Ingres, drawing on the energy of Delacroix, and the horses and riders may show the pull between those two masters. The subject is an obscure incident rather than a famous scene, and the picture is more ambitious than assured. Chassériau's later reputation rests on portraits, Orientalist subjects and murals, and this is an early exercise that shows his drive more than his mastery."},"met:438590":{"d":"A portrait of the Comtesse de La Tour-Maubourg, born Marie-Louise-Charlotte-Gabrielle Thomas de Pange (1816-1850), painted in 1841 in oil on canvas, about 132 by 95 cm, a size that allows a figure shown well below the waist. In kind it is a commissioned society portrait of a young aristocrat, made by a painter in his early twenties who had been trained by Ingres.","i":"The sitter belonged to the aristocracy: the title of comtesse and the family of Thomas de Pange show her rank, and a portrait of this size would record her place in Parisian society. She died in 1850, so the picture is also a record of a short life. Portraits by young painters launched careers by doing justice to rank and beauty, and Chassériau, a pupil of Ingres, was ambitious to follow his master as a portraitist.","c":"Chassériau's portraits of the early 1840s combine Ingres's firm contour with a more sensuous, melancholy feeling for the sitter, and this canvas probably shares in that mixture. The size and the commission mark a step up for a young painter, and the picture's interest lies in elegance and presence more than in showy invention. It is a notable early success by an artist who died at thirty-seven and who is more admired today than in his own time."},"met:438107":{"d":"A small oil on wood, about 35 by 27 cm, dated 1849, showing Desdemona from Shakespeare's Othello at the moment of the Song of the Willow. A woman and a lyre are recorded, so the figure is shown with her instrument, in the sad song she sings on the night of her death. A panel of this size is a cabinet picture or a private commission by a painter who took Shakespeare's heroines as subjects, in the Romantic taste of the 1840s.","i":"In act 4 of Othello, Desdemona, sensing that her husband's love has turned, sings an old song of a forsaken girl, with the refrain 'Sing willow, willow, willow,' while her maid prepares her for bed; she dies that night. The willow was a symbol of forsaken love. Shakespeare was in fashion in France after English actors visited Paris in 1827 and 1828, and Delacroix and Chassériau drew on the plays for doomed, musical heroines.","c":"Chassériau's Shakespearean heroines suit his languid, melancholy temperament; he was a painter who made sadness graceful. At 35 by 27 cm the picture can concentrate on one expressive figure, with the lyre as emblem, and the risk is a sweet sentimentality. As a work of the late 1840s it comes from the strongest part of his career, before the mural cycles, when he joined Ingres's line to Delacroix's color."},"met:437974":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 57 by 47 cm, painted in 1851, of a scene in the Jewish quarter of Constantine, the old city in eastern Algeria that Chassériau visited in 1846. A woman, an infant asleep and a cradle are listed, so the subject is a domestic moment of watching over a sleeping child, observed in an everyday setting that the painter presented as exotic. It is a cabinet picture, made in Paris some five years after the journey.","i":"French painters of the nineteenth century were drawn to North Africa after the French conquest of Algeria began in 1830, and Chassériau made Algerian scenes the basis of several works after his journey. Such pictures, called Orientalist, mixed observation with Western fantasy and served a public interested in the colonies. A mother and a sleeping child had universal appeal as well as regional flavor, but the painting is a product of a colonial context and an outsider's view.","c":"In the best of Chassériau's Algerian pictures, quiet domestic moments, rather than harems or battles, give a human dignity that much Orientalism lacks, and this subject suggests as much. He brings Ingres-trained drawing to an unfamiliar costume and architecture, and the sleeping infant provides an emotional anchor. The risk is the exoticizing gaze, which turns a family scene into spectacle, and the picture's interest is in how far it escapes that."},"met:440727":{"d":"A view across a broad valley toward mountains is the subject of this small oil study, about 15 by 26 cm, painted on paper and later laid on canvas about 1829 to 1830. Fritz Petzholdt was a Danish painter who died at thirty-three, and the medium suggests a sketch made in front of the motif during a journey to Germany, in the open-air manner of the Danish Golden Age.","i":"Oil sketches on paper were a tool of the Danish school of Eckersberg: quick studies of light and space made outdoors, not for sale but as visual notes for later work. A broad valley seen from a height was a Romantic motif, inviting the eye to travel across space. Modern taste, which values the freshness of such sketches, has made them collectors' favorites, and they are now prized as independent pictures, as this one's donor, a noted collector, recognized.","c":"The attraction is directness: a few broad touches of color for sky, hills and valley give a convincing sense of space on a very small sheet. A sketch is inherently incomplete, and the interest lies in what it catches rather than in what it resolves. Petzholdt died young and left little, and this sheet shows a freshness that anticipates later open-air painting, a small but real discovery for those who value Danish sketches."},"met:436222":{"d":"A young woman stands full length at a harp in a grand room, one hand on the strings, looking over her shoulder toward the viewer with a faint smile. Her pale hair is dressed high with a green ribbon. She wears a cream satin gown with a frilled hem and a green-and-white striped silk overgown that sweeps across the floor in a long train. At left a table with a deep red cloth holds open sheet music, a book and a dark vase with classical decoration, and behind her a fluted column rises from the dim wall. The carpet is patterned in red and green.","i":"Rose-Adélaïde Ducreux was the daughter and pupil of the portraitist Joseph Ducreux. She showed this life-size picture at the Salon of 1791, the first Salon opened to all artists after the Revolution. She presents herself as a musician rather than a painter, a respectable image of an accomplished woman at a time when painting was seen as a masculine career, and the harp, the music and the book signal cultivation. The satin and silk also demonstrate her skill. How far the image was a calculated strategy and how far a record of her real interests cannot be known.","c":"A showpiece of fabrics: the cream satin and the green-striped silk are the best-painted passages, and the twisting pose with the glance over the shoulder is graceful. The weak points are the face, pleasant but generic and without the sharp character her father gave his sitters, and the awkward space, where the harp is so large the figure seems to lean in front of it. Nothing is actually being played or painted; the picture is about display, and about a careful claim to respectability."},"nga:79933":{"d":"In a camp, two soldiers in blue uniforms are lost in thought as a military band plays in the background. One stands at the center in profile, facing left, a hand on his hip. The other sits in front of a low, waist-high tent, chin in hand and papers on his thigh, also looking left. A boot shows in the dark of the tent, smoke rises from a pot on a campfire, and hardtack lies on a pewter plate near a knapsack. A tan cloth on sticks divides the pair from the rows of tents beyond, where the band's horns glint under a pale blue sky.","i":"Homer worked as an artist-reporter for Harper's Weekly and saw the Army of the Potomac at first hand, and he made camp life, not battle, the subject of his early paintings. The title is a famous song, 'Home! Sweet Home!', loved by soldiers on both sides, and the picture asks the viewer to imagine the thoughts of two men hearing it far from home. The tent, hardtack and campfire place the war in the ordinary waiting that filled most of a soldier's time.","c":"The picture is quiet and unglamorous, neither heroic nor sentimental until the title names the song the men are hearing. The tan partition that divides the composition, and the small band in the distance as the source of the sound, are clever ways of making music visible. The figures are stiffly posed and the handling plain, but the restraint is the point, and the picture is among the more affecting of Homer's Civil War scenes."},"cma:124040":{"d":"Two Union volunteers pass idle time in their encampment, in the highly colorful uniforms of their regiment. One holds a lit pipe and watches his companion whittle a block of wood. The canvas, about 43 by 37.5 cm and painted in 1864, is a quiet camp scene rather than a battle, and its subject is the waiting that filled soldiers' days.","i":"The bright uniforms, a design that proved impractical because it made soldiers easy for sharpshooters to spot, belonged to early Union volunteer regiments, and Homer had seen the army at first hand as an artist-reporter for Harper's Weekly. The title borrows from a contemporary poem about a soldier who smokes a brierwood pipe and daydreams of going home when the war ends, tying the picture to the soldier's longing. The museum acquired it during World War II, when its subject must have spoken to a wartime audience.","c":"Homer chooses an unheroic moment, two men with nothing to do, and trusts it. The bright uniforms give a lively color note against the dull business of waiting, and the picture is understated, almost casual, its sentiment held in check by plain observation. Compared with the large war canvases of the day, its modesty is its strength: it persuades by its small truths of posture and occupation."},"met:11145":{"d":"A farmer stands with his back to us in the middle of a field of ripe wheat, cutting it with a long-bladed scythe. He wears a broad-brimmed straw hat, a white shirt with braces and dark trousers. The tall grain, painted in quick strokes of gold, ochre and green, fills most of the canvas and rises to a low horizon under a narrow band of blue sky. Cut stalks lie in rough swathes at his feet. At the lower right, a discarded jacket and canteen lie in the stubble. The oil on canvas is thin and direct, and the figure is simplified into a few large shapes.","i":"Painted in 1865, the year the Civil War ended, the picture is usually read as a return from war. The discarded jacket and canteen mark the farmer as a Union veteran, and the wheat is the harvest of a peaceful future. Homer had worked as an artist-reporter for Harper's Weekly at the front, so he knew the soldiers' world. The tool is debated. A working wheat harvest would normally use a scythe fitted with a cradle, and Homer's bare scythe is also the Grim Reaper's, so the allusion to the war dead may be deliberate. When shown in 1865, the picture received mostly negative reviews.","c":"The composition is almost audaciously simple: a wall of gold, a single dark figure, a strip of sky. The wheat is the real achievement, built from many short, directional strokes that catch the light like straw. The man himself is plain and slightly stiff, and he has no face, which is both the picture's strength and its limit: he becomes every returning soldier, but never one particular person. The first critics thought the handling hasty. Now the speed looks right, matching the broad, unfussy vision Homer would carry through his career."},"met:11133":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 61 by 96.5 cm, painted in 1866, showing prisoners brought in from the front during the American Civil War. The records name the Union officer Francis Channing Barlow among the soldiers, so the picture shows a real commander meeting men he has captured. Homer, who had visited the Union army at the front as an artist-reporter, treats the captives as individual types rather than an anonymous crowd, and the canvas is an exhibition picture rather than a sketch from the field.","i":"Painted a year after the war ended, the picture was widely read as a statement about the reunion of the nation, with a confident Union officer facing defeated Southern soldiers of different kinds, some proud and some weary. Barlow was a young Union general celebrated for capturing many Confederates in 1864. The picture was a great success at the National Academy of Design in 1866 and was shown in Paris in 1867, and it made Homer's reputation.","c":"This is the painting that lifted Homer from illustrator to painter of note, and it earns its reputation by restraint: no battle, only a meeting, with faces that are individual and a design that holds the tension. The rhetoric is of its time, and some viewers will find the Union officer's calm complacent, but the prisoners are the real subject, and Homer gives them dignity along with defiance. It is a historic painting as well as a good one."},"cma:156615":{"d":"A small oil sketch on wood, about 17 by 32 cm, painted in 1867, showing women working in a field. Homer was then on a year's study in France, which began in December 1866, and the sketch was probably painted at Cernay-la-Ville, a rural village about forty miles from Paris. The type is the outdoor study of rural labor in the manner of the Barbizon painters, made quickly on the spot and kept as a record of a motif.","i":"Homer sailed for France in December 1866 and spent a year working in Paris and in the countryside. He had known the principles of French painting since the early 1850s, particularly the outdoor style of the Barbizon school. The sketch records his meeting with the French rural scene, a subject associated with Millet, and the French stay is often said to have strengthened his interest in outdoor light and simplified shapes, which he brought home to America.","c":"A sketch of this size can do only one thing, and Homer's does it plainly: it sets small figures in a wide field with a few touches. Without the drama of his war pictures or the polish of his later work, it shows an eye in transition. Its value is as evidence of his French year, and its pleasure lies in a quick, unfussy record of work in the open air."},"met:11141":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 46 by 38 cm, dated 1867, the year of Homer's stay in France, and titled The Studio. A man, a musician, a cello and a violin are noted, so the picture is an interior in which a musician sits among his instruments. The type is the small genre interior of the kind French painters of the period made for private buyers: a quiet, closely observed moment in a workroom rather than a story.","i":"The musician in his studio belongs to the nineteenth-century interest in the artist at work, in which painters and writers took the bohemian world of artists and musicians as a subject. Homer was about thirty, an American in Paris for a year, and the picture may reflect what he saw there rather than any particular sitter. No sitter or story is recorded. The cello and violin put sound into a silent medium, as Homer would often do.","c":"A small studio interior is not what Homer is remembered for, and the picture is best valued as a quiet, observant exercise from a year of learning in France. The subject, a man with instruments, relies on posture and light more than on incident, and its restraint is deliberate. Among Homer's works of 1867 it is an unassuming piece, mildly interesting beside the outdoor scenes he painted in the same period."},"met:11111":{"d":"A small, wide oil on wood, about 23.5 by 53 cm, dated 1869, of a beach in late afternoon with human figures. The beach and the people on it are the only recorded subjects: a study of seaside leisure in a strip format, in which the low sun sets the mood and the figures are small. In 1869 Homer was painting the new fashion for seaside resorts on the American coast, which gave him a modern subject.","i":"The seaside became a fashionable place of leisure for the American middle class after the Civil War, and Homer found there a subject that was new for painting: ordinary people at rest, in modern clothing, in bright light. He worked such scenes in small oil panels like this one, often as private studies. The 'late afternoon' of the title suggests an interest in changing light as much as in the figures.","c":"At this size and with this subject, the picture succeeds or fails on its light and the spacing of its figures, and a wide strip lets the beach stretch out and the small figures scatter at ease. Homer's early beach scenes are fresh and unsentimental, and they show his shift from war subjects toward contemporary leisure. It is a slight, engaging record, and a sign of a painter growing independent of his illustrator's past."},"met:11142":{"d":"A small oil on wood, about 24 by 54 cm, dated 1869 with a query, made as a study for Homer's larger painting of Eagle Head at Manchester, Massachusetts. It records the beach and sea of the site as a seascape: a seaside headland north of Boston where Homer painted in these years. The study is a working piece, painted to fix the light and the shore before the figures of the finished canvas were added.","i":"Eagle Head is a rocky point at Manchester, on the Massachusetts coast north of Boston, where Homer spent time in 1869 and 1870 and painted several beach scenes. Preparatory studies like this fixed the setting before the people were added, in the larger canvas in which women stand with a dog beside the high tide. The study shows Homer's habit of working out a picture's setting separately from its figures.","c":"As a study it is best judged for what it prepares: an unfussy record of beach and sea, with the open feeling the larger painting needed. Studies often reveal a painter's method more candidly than the finished picture, and this one suggests that Homer approached the shore as a problem of light and horizontal space before it became a scene with figures. It is slight on its own but informative beside the finished canvas."},"met:11117":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 67 by 98 cm, dated 1870, showing a beach at Eagle Head, Manchester, Massachusetts, at high tide, with women and a dog. The recorded figures and the setting make it a scene of seaside leisure: young women in the dress of the day on the shore, in the new mode of beach painting that Homer was developing. The title's 'High Tide' fixes the moment in the tidal cycle and suggests close observation of the sea.","i":"Manchester, on the Massachusetts coast north of Boston, was a summer resort for Boston families, and Homer painted there in 1869 and 1870. The picture belongs to his pioneering group of beach scenes, which gave American painting a subject drawn from modern leisure rather than from history or myth. The high tide narrows the strip of sand where the people stand, and the sea seems to press in on them.","c":"The high tide narrows the beach and crowds the figures toward the foreground, and the sea, light and a few bright touches of dress give the picture a fresh, outdoor feel. Homer's strength is in simplified shapes and the openness of the horizontal format, though the figures are less individualized than in his war pictures. It is a key transitional painting of 1870, when he was moving from illustration toward the independent painter of the sea."},"nga:89664":{"d":"A vertical canvas in which a young woman in an ankle-length white dress stands with her back to the viewer, blowing a silver horn in a grassy landscape. Bright light from the upper right makes her dress glow, her sleeves are rolled to the elbows, and a black ribbon fastens her blond hair in a net. A strong wind from the right twists the hem of her dress and the ties at her waist. Behind her are a house with wooden siding, potted plants, a cow and chickens, and in the deep distance tiny figures plow a field. Signed and dated 1870.","i":"A horn blown at mealtime called farm workers in from the fields, and the title tells the purpose: dinner. Homer had turned from war subjects to scenes of country life after the Civil War, and the picture's attraction is its fresh, modern view of ordinary work, in strong light and with a figure drawn plainly from behind. The woman is not named, and her turned-away face makes her an everyday type rather than a portrait, a quiet emblem of rural routine.","c":"The back view is the bold choice: it turns a young woman into a bright shape of white dress against green, with wind-twisted fabric supplying the action. The glowing white against dark trees shows Homer's fine eye for light, and the tiny plowman in the distance gives the call its purpose and the picture depth. It is a clear example of his early mastery of contemporary rural life, though the figure's anonymity keeps it cool."},"met:11134":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 51 by 91 cm, dated 1871, showing a camp of the American Civil War on a rainy day. Soldiers, a horse and rows of tents are recorded. It was painted six years after the war and looks back at the uneventful, damp waiting that was the more common experience of soldiering, rather than at battle. The format is a long, low strip across the camp, and the weather is the real subject.","i":"Homer had visited the Army of the Potomac in 1861 and 1862 as an artist-reporter for Harper's Weekly, and the Civil War remained a source of subjects for him after it ended. By 1871 such pictures were remembering rather than reporting, and a rainy day in camp offers the soldier's ordinary hardships: wet, cold, dull duty and mud. The picture speaks to the common experience of veterans, a large part of Homer's public, rather than to patriotic rhetoric.","c":"Weather is hard to paint, and Homer makes it the chief character: the dull gray of rain, the dripping tents and the huddled figures should give a convincing sense of damp. The picture is less famous than Prisoners from the Front, and its restraint, with no incident, may disappoint some viewers, but its persuasive mood makes it a notable late reflection on the war."},"met:11140":{"d":"A small, wide canvas, about 30 by 51 cm, dated 1872, showing boys playing snap the whip, a game in which a line of children running hand in hand swings around and flings the last in line off. A house, the game and the boys are named among its subjects, so the setting includes a rural building in the open air. Homer painted the subject in more than one version, and this canvas is the smaller of two oil versions.","i":"Snap the whip was a familiar game of rural American childhood, and the picture, painted in the years after the Civil War, offers a nostalgic image of a peaceful countryside and carefree boys. It was reproduced as a wood engraving in Harper's Weekly in 1873 and became one of Homer's most popular subjects. Some viewers read the line of boys, swinging together in a chain, as a sign of the reunited nation; others simply enjoy the sunlit action.","c":"The composition is what lifts this above an anecdote: the curve of the running line and the quick bodily action of the boys at the whip's end make an instantly legible rhythm. The small size and small figures keep it playful rather than heavy, and its warmth is genuine. It is among Homer's most loved pictures and rightly so, though the larger version has more presence and this one is the more intimate."},"cma:135323":{"d":"A small oil sketch on panel, about 20 by 32.6 cm, of a white mare, painted around 1868. Homer made it as a study for the horse in his large painting The Bridal Path, White Mountains (1868), after visiting the White Mountains of New Hampshire in August 1868 while working as a freelance illustrator. The sketch is a working study of one animal, made to get the horse's pose and coloring right before it was placed in the large composition.","i":"American artists had used the White Mountains as a setting for landscape painting since the 1820s. Unlike Thomas Cole and Asher Durand, who focused on unspoiled wilderness, Homer turned his attention to other tourists, the visitors who now came to the mountains for leisure, and The Bridal Path put a rider on a horse in that landscape. A study of this kind shows how Homer prepared a large picture from separate observations.","c":"A study should be judged for its subject, and Homer's gift for observation shows in the economy with which he describes a horse as a form in light. In itself the sketch is slight, but it stands as a rare example of how a major picture was built from separate pieces. Its interest is documentary as well as pictorial, and it is a pleasing small record of an animal."},"met:11123":{"d":"A long, narrow oil on canvas, about 25 by 61 cm, painted around 1873, showing a harvest in a landscape, with a man and trees recorded. The strip format spreads the scene across a wide band, so the field and the working figure take their place in a broad stretch of country. It is a rural harvest piece in the manner of the American countryside scenes Homer painted in the early 1870s.","i":"Harvest scenes, with workers gathering crops in the field, had a long place in European and American painting as images of abundance and rural labor. In the decade after the Civil War, Homer was drawn to such scenes of country life, haymakers, farm laborers and children, which treated the American countryside as a place of peaceful continuity. The man is not identified, and the scene is a type rather than an episode.","c":"The wide strip shows Homer's interest in horizontals, and a small harvest scene is not the picture on which his reputation rests. Its directness is typical, however: a figure, trees and light, without anecdote. As an example of his early 1870s landscape work it is less dramatic than his beach scenes, but if it is modest, it is also honest, and it records a lasting attachment to rural subjects."},"nga:66419":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a young woman stands close to the viewer in a vertical canvas, with a red schoolhouse in the distance under a sunset sky. Her body faces front, but she turns her head to look off to the left. Her blond hair is pulled up under a round white hat with ruffles, and her white dress is striped with thin rust-red lines, with a tight bodice, a dark gray belt and a black lace ruffle at the neck. She holds two small books in one hand. The schoolhouse has crimson walls and a dark gray roof. Signed and dated 1873.","i":"In the 1870s Homer painted a number of pictures of country schools and their teachers, and the woman, holding books before the schoolhouse, is presumably a teacher. Teaching had become one of the few respectable professions open to women in the nineteenth century, and the one-room red schoolhouse was a national symbol of education and community. The sunset and her turned head give the figure a thoughtful, slightly distant air rather than a story.","c":"A bold design: the tall canvas has the young woman almost filling its height, with the red schoolhouse tiny behind her, and her white striped dress is the light shape against sunset yellows and pinks. The ruffled hat and dress are described with exactness, while the face is calm and a little blank. It is more a study of fashion and light than of character, but a handsome, original picture whose mix of portrait and landscape was fruitful."},"nga:79891":{"d":"A woman holding a baby stands near a young boy who perches in the tipped-up end of a rowboat pulled onto a sandy beach. Turquoise water meets a pale blue sky at a horizon about halfway up. The boy wears a round straw hat and brown shirt and turns his face away. The woman, in a white apron and long brown skirt, looks over the shoulder of the blond baby she holds, dressed in a white skirt, blue sash and red jacket. Boulders, a barrel, a second boat and fishing nets drying on a tall frame litter the beach, and white sails line the horizon.","i":"The picture shows a fisherman's family on the shore, and the title speaks for the boy and the mother, who presumably watch for the father's boat; the white sails far off are the ships at sea. The nets drying on the frame mark their livelihood. Homer painted such moments of waiting as a quiet sign of the dependence of coastal families on the sea, a theme he would later treat with greater danger in pictures of rescue and undertow.","c":"The small panel rests on a simple, elegant structure: the tilted boat gives a diagonal that leads the eye to the boy and the mother, and the horizon halfway up is crossed by tiny sails. The boy's turned-away face and the woman's glance over the baby's shoulder keep the picture unsentimental and open to the viewer's own inference. It is a nicely observed minor work in which narrative and design agree."},"nga:157923":{"d":"Four women in long white dresses stand or sit on a sunny beach in a wide, horizontal canvas. In the distance people and carriages are hazy gray silhouettes along the shore. Three women huddle on a brown blanket under two broad parasols, facing the sea; the nearest wears lapis-blue bows, a blue sash, a straw-yellow hat and a yellow glove, and her parasol is bright blue inside. Another wears an olive wrap and a third a bright red one. A fourth, in white under a vivid red jacket, stands behind them touching her hat. Signed and dated 1874 at East Hampton.","i":"East Hampton, on the south fork of Long Island, was a growing summer resort in the 1870s, and Homer painted its beach in 1874. His women are fashionably dressed visitors with parasols and wraps, and the carriages on the sand show that driving along the beach was part of the day's amusements. Homer worked as an illustrator for the popular press, which spread the same fashions, and he made seaside leisure a new subject for American painting.","c":"The pleasure here is the color accent: red wraps, blue-lined parasols and a yellow glove against white dresses and pale sand, with the loose gray silhouettes of distant people for balance. Homer's handling is brisk, almost sketchy in the faces, and the wide format holds the beach in an easy stretch. As a record of fashion and leisure it is lively and truthful, and as a composition it is a fine small canvas."},"nga:164928":{"d":"A young man and four boys sit or recline in a small sailboat that tips to the left on a choppy dark green sea fringed with white foam. The billowing sail runs off the top left corner, and wispy clouds skip across a pale blue sky. Sun from the right leaves the boys' ruddy faces in shadow under their hats, and all lean against the tilt of the boat. The oldest handles the sail with a rope, one boy reclines across the bow, and a younger barefoot boy gazes into the distance as he steers. The boat's name, Flirt, is painted on the stern. Signed and dated 1874.","i":"Homer spent the early 1870s at coastal places, and boys sailing became a recurring subject, as in Boys in a Dory and the later Breezing Up. 'Flirt' is the boat's name, and the word's sense of playful teasing suits the way the boat leans and dances in the wind. The picture celebrates the skill and freedom of boyhood on the water, in the years after the Civil War, when such images offered a reassuring vision of a peaceful, confident country.","c":"The tilt of the boat gives the little panel its life: the figures lean against the angle, the sail billows out of the frame and the dark green sea breaks into white foam, while the shaded faces keep the boys anonymous. It is a forerunner of Breezing Up, with the same feeling for weight and wind, but more compact. The narrow focus succeeds without sentimentality, and the picture is a compelling small example of his early marine work."},"nga:164929":{"d":"A horizontal canvas in which light pours over a woman and a dozen children of different ages, standing in small groups near a boxy red schoolhouse. The woman stands at lower center in a white hat tied with a black ribbon and a long black dress, with two girls in floppy hats and a boy holding a pail around her. A child stands in the open doorway, and on the short side of the building another child paints the letters WH in white on the red boards. A hill rises behind the schoolhouse under a pale blue sky.","i":"Rural schools and their teachers were a recurring subject for Homer in the early 1870s, appealing to a postwar public's nostalgia for simple community institutions and to the growing importance of public education. The woman is presumably the teacher, and teaching was among the respectable professions open to women. The painted initials WH, as though a child had scrawled them, are a playful signature that places the artist inside his own scene.","c":"Light and a simple building organize the picture, and the children arranged in loose groups give a natural, unposed feeling, while the child writing the initials is a wry touch. The picture is more anecdote than deep statement, but it shows Homer's knack for gently comic observation. Its colors are earthy and subdued, in contrast to his dazzling beach scenes, and it works as a pleasant, truthful image of schooltime."},"met:11135":{"d":"A small oil on wood, about 32 by 40 cm, from about 1870 to 1875, showing a saddled horse in a farm yard. The horse and the saddle are recorded, so the subject is an animal at rest, with an implied rider who is absent. It is a rural picture of a kind Homer painted in these years, taken from the everyday life of the countryside and drawn on observation of farm animals in their setting rather than on any story.","i":"A horse standing saddled in a yard implies a rider who has dismounted or is about to depart, and that hint of a story gives a simple animal study a human dimension. Homer's countryside pictures of the 1870s were bought by urban audiences as images of rural simplicity and continuity, and a farm yard with a horse was an easily understood symbol of that life.","c":"This is a minor Homer, and its interest lies in the observation of the animal and the farm setting. As an animal study it is capable rather than remarkable, and it shows how much at home Homer was with country subjects in these years. The saddle supplies a note of narrative and a touch of mystery, and the picture is a modest but honest example of his early rural work."},"nga:47184":{"d":"A person pulls a long brown and green canoe out of the water onto a grassy stretch of land. The figure wears a hat, a green shirt and brown trousers, and the face is indistinct. The water nearest the viewer is light yellow and reflects the dark shadow of the person and the boat. Behind the small spit of land is another body of water, blue with streaks of pink and the reflections of green trees, leading to tree-covered land in the distance. The sky above is light blue with areas of light peach and pink.","i":"Homer painted sunsets and twilight scenes in the 1870s as explorations of color and light, and the canoe was a craft closely tied to the outdoor leisure and northern waters that Americans were discovering in those years. The figure is minor, a person at the end of a day's outing hauling the boat ashore, and the sunset glow on the water is the real subject. No story is recorded.","c":"The picture is a study in reflected light: the yellow water with the dark reflection of the person and the canoe, the blue distance streaked with pink, and the peach sky. The figure is just a mark of scale and action. Homer's mood is calm and contemplative, and the picture is more an essay on color than a narrative. Its success is its restraint, though its slightness will not appeal to those who look for incident."},"nga:166455":{"d":"A sketch of an outdoor scene with a group of people gathered in front of a blue-gray wooden building with a dark door and two windows. Sunflowers grow on either side of the entrance, stretching up to the windows. The ground is patches of tan, brown and gray, perhaps stone steps or a path. Left of center, rough strokes suggest four or five people gathered on the ground, without distinct details or facial features, in white, tan, gray, red and pale blue clothes that seem to include dresses, bonnets, shirts and suspenders. A stool sits at lower right, and white birds show in the top left corner.","i":"Homer visited Virginia in the mid-1870s, where he painted African Americans in the years after Emancipation, and a cottage yard of this kind may belong to that context, though the sketch does not say so. He is often said to have treated such subjects with a respect rare among white painters of the day. The sketch itself is unfinished and anonymous, and no story is recorded.","c":"As a sketch with rough brushstrokes and no facial features, it cannot be judged as a portrait, and its interest lies in the patches of color: blue-gray building, sunflowers, white birds. The sunflowers rising to the windows are a nice vertical accent. It is a note-taking sketch more than a finished picture, but it shows Homer's attention to ordinary domestic life, and the sympathy with which he looked at it."},"nga:30228":{"d":"A small sailboat heels sharply to the left, its stern marked GLOUCESTER, as it cuts across a choppy green sea under a turquoise sky banked with puffy gray and white clouds. A big sail billows off the top left corner, and a schooner with tall sails stands far off at right. A man in a broad-brimmed hat and three boys lean into the slant of the deck: one boy sprawls across the bow beside the sail, another clings to the side, and a third, in a red shirt, trims a rope. Fish lie in the bottom of the boat. The sun comes from the right, putting the faces in hat-shadow. The picture is signed and dated 1876 at lower right.","i":"Homer began this picture in New York in 1873 after a visit to Gloucester, Massachusetts, and completed it in 1876. Infrared study shows that he changed it over three years, removing a fourth boy near the mast and a second schooner in the distance. He worked from earlier studies, including an oil study called The Flirt, painted in 1874. The picture was exhibited at the National Academy of Design in 1876 and then at the Centennial Exposition in Philadelphia. Its fresh, hopeful mood has often been read as an image of American confidence in the centennial year, though Homer did not say so, and that reading is an interpretation.","c":"Homer makes a small, ordinary subject feel physical. The tilted boat, the long diagonal of the boom and the white foam at the bow produce a real sense of speed, and the boys' faces, hidden in hat-shadow, matter less than the way their bodies lean with the sea. Some figures look posed rather than caught in a gust, and the sea is rather uniform. Even so, the frank pleasure in everyday leisure and the clear, strong light made this one of the works that established Homer as a leading American painter."},"met:11116":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 51 by 76 cm, dated 1877, showing a group of people in front of a fence, preparing costumes for a festival: women, children, a man and a girl are recorded. The records name Junkanoo, the masquerade tradition also called Jonkonnu or John Canoe, and also Independence Day. The picture was painted after Homer's visits to Virginia, where he painted African Americans in the years after Emancipation.","i":"Junkanoo, spelled Jonkonnu or John Canoe in the American South, was a masquerade with African roots, held around Christmas and New Year in the Caribbean and the South, in which performers wore elaborate costumes. Enslaved and then free Black communities kept it alive. Homer's picture is an unusually early and sympathetic record of such a tradition by a white American painter, though modern viewers will want to remember that he was an outside observer.","c":"Dressing for the Carnival is generally counted among Homer's most admired pictures of the 1870s, noted for the dignity and variety of its figures and its bright color against a plain setting. The costumes and the movement of the dressing hands give the scene energy. As a white painter, Homer views the subject from outside, but he avoids the caricature that was typical of the period's treatment of such scenes."},"nga:66420":{"d":"A woman in a hat, long dress and gloves stands holding a bunch of pumpkin-orange leaves against trees touched with rust and pine green. She tips her head back to look at the viewer with dark eyes under arched eyebrows. A round black hat perches on the back of her head, and she wears a black hip-length jacket with bronze trim and a gauzy bow at the neck. With a white-gloved hand she lifts a tan ruffled skirt, showing a sky-blue lining, and a basket spilling marigold leaves hangs from her other hand. She stands on a hill carpeted with crimson, tawny and golden leaves.","i":"Autumn as a theme often carried thoughts of passing time, and a young woman among falling leaves may allude to the brevity of youth, but Homer treats the subject as a fashionable outing. Gathering leaves was a pastime for city visitors, and the stylish clothes, with trimmed jacket and ruffled skirt, show a modern update of the old allegory of the seasons, in which a figure in contemporary dress stands for the time of year.","c":"Homer's strong color is the picture's strength: the blaze of orange, crimson and gold behind the black jacket gives a bold decorative effect. The face is direct and a little challenging, tipped back, and the stylish dress is described accurately. The standing figure seems monumental yet not idealized. It is a confident modern-life picture and one of the more fully realized of Homer's figure paintings of the 1870s, though its allegory is lightly worn."},"met:11112":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 60 by 97 cm, painted in 1880, of a camp with tents and men gathered around a fire. Men, a tent and a fire are the recorded subjects, so the picture is probably an evening or night scene in which the fire is the main source of light, in the kind of woodland camp that Homer, a keen outdoorsman, knew from his own trips. It is a mature oil of a type that sets men at rest in the open air.","i":"Camp scenes link Homer's Civil War pictures with his later hunting and fishing subjects: the same tents and smoking fire, now for leisure rather than for war. A campfire is a place of rest, talk and companionship among men, and it was a subject that appealed to an urban audience which valued outdoor life in the 1870s and 1880s. The picture does not say who the men are or where they camp.","c":"A fire at night is a hard test of a painter's skill: warm, flickering light against dark woods, with the men lit from one side. Homer, who by 1880 had a strong command of light, was equal to such challenges, and the picture should repay attention as an exercise in effects. It is a mature work in which the subject is mood more than incident, and its restraint is a strength."},"nga:126152":{"d":"An oil on canvas, about 39 by 56.5 cm, dated about 1881 to 1882, titled Sparrow Hall and recorded as a genre picture, meaning a scene of everyday life. No figures or setting are specified, so the picture can be placed only by date: these were the years Homer spent at Cullercoats, a fishing village on the northeast coast of England, where he turned to the lives of fisherfolk and especially the fishermen's wives who waited and worked on the shore.","i":"The Cullercoats period transformed Homer's art: he concentrated on figures, especially the women of the shore, and gave them a heroic seriousness that anticipated his later pictures of the sea's dangers. The meaning of the title is not explained in the record, and whether Sparrow Hall is a place or a play on small birds cannot be said here. Whatever its subject, the picture belongs to the moment when Homer moved from American genre toward a more monumental view of working life.","c":"Without a described scene, the picture can be judged only as part of the English interlude, one of the most important turning points in Homer's career: the new weight in his figures, the simpler design and the sober palette were the lessons of those years. Sparrow Hall is less known than the watercolors of the fisherwomen, and as an oil it is a modest entry in the series, but it shows an artist changing course."},"nga:33970":{"d":"A young man lies on his stomach in a wooden rowboat on a river, reaching forward to grasp the antler of a stag almost completely submerged in the rippling water. His other hand clutches a rope with a loop at the end, and he turns his flushed face, mouth open, to look at a white and caramel-brown dog swimming toward the boat. Only the stag's muzzle, part of an eye and the tips of its antlers show above the thick white brushstrokes of the ripples. On the far bank, gold, rust and scarlet trees fill the upper half. Signed and dated 1892.","i":"Homer spent summers at the North Woods Club in the Adirondacks from 1889, and the hunting and fishing pictures that followed record the region's guides, hunters and animals. The scene shows a stag driven into the water by a hound and secured from a boat, a method called hounding that was later outlawed as cruel. The autumn trees mark the season. Homer shows the hunter's excitement and the animal's helplessness without comment, leaving the viewer to judge.","c":"The picture is a study in strong, simple contrasts: a young man's face, all excitement, against the almost drowned animal, and the tangle of autumn color against the dark water. The drama lies in details such as the muzzle and antler tips above the surface and the white ripples around them. Homer's refusal to moralize makes the picture disturbing as well as exciting, and it succeeds better as a study of a moment than as a pleasant hunting scene."},"met:11127":{"d":"A night seascape, about 78 by 102 cm, painted in 1894, in which the moon and the light at Wood Island, a lighthouse off the coast of southern Maine near Homer's home at Prouts Neck, give the title and the subject. It is a view of the sea at night in which moonlight and darkness are the real subject, a theme Homer returned to in his later years.","i":"From 1883 Homer lived at Prouts Neck on the Maine coast and turned from figure painting toward the sea itself, painting it in all weather and light. Night scenes were less common in his work, and a lighthouse, a human-made light in nature's darkness, adds a thread of human presence. The sea in moonlight also let him explore a restricted, almost monochrome palette, a challenge that suited his late, simplifying style.","c":"Moonlight is hard to paint honestly, and Homer avoids the conventional blue-and-silver sentimentality by keeping his scene dark and spare. The result, judging by his late nocturnes, should be austere and powerful, with the light as a small point against a large dark. It is a distinguished late seascape and an unusual one, in which the human presence is reduced to a lantern at the edge of the sea."},"met:11113":{"d":"A square oil on canvas, about 1 meter on each side, dated 1895, of waves at Cannon Rock, on the Maine coast near Homer's studio at Prouts Neck. Seascape and waves are the recorded subjects, so the picture belongs to the group of sea paintings in which Homer set aside the human figures of his earlier work and let the Atlantic itself take the leading role. A square format is less usual for a seascape than the long horizontal, and it concentrates the force of the water.","i":"Homer settled at Prouts Neck in 1883, and by the 1890s he painted the sea from his own cliff-top studio, studying the same coast in all weathers. The Cannon Rock picture belongs to the final phase of his career, when the sea became his chief subject and his emphasis fell on force and mass rather than on story. No human figure, ship or lighthouse is recorded, so the sea stands alone as the subject.","c":"The weight of water is the achievement: a wave painted as mass and motion, with the viewer set close to the swell. Homer's late seascapes avoid decorative prettiness and are about power and indifference, and in the square the sea fills the frame with a monumental effect. The picture belongs among the best of his late work, though it lacks the narrative drama of his rescue scenes."},"met:11126":{"d":"A seascape on canvas, about 76 by 102 cm, dated 1896, of the rocky coast of Maine with heavy surf. Seascape and waves are the recorded subjects, and the broad title makes the whole coast the sitter rather than one named spot. It is one of the paintings from Homer's studio at Prouts Neck in which rock, water and sky are the principal actors and no figure is needed to give them importance.","i":"To Americans of the 1890s the Maine coast was a retreat for summer visitors and a symbol of wilder nature, but Homer's version is about the sea's power rather than leisure. Living on that coast year-round, he came to know the rocks and the seasons, and his pictures of the sea in storm and calm were bought by a public that admired his seriousness. Whether this canvas shows a specific place is not recorded.","c":"In pictures such as this, Homer typically builds the design from a few large masses of rock, water and sky, with the spray as the single brilliant note, and the economy is what gives them force. The title is plain and the subject unadorned, which suits his late style, in which description yields to weight and movement. It is a solid example of his mature seascapes, impressive more for power than for variety."},"nga:166501":{"d":"A young woman stands on a rocky shoreline with a fishing net slung over one shoulder, in a horizontal canvas. She faces the viewer but looks off and down to the right, one hand on her hip and the other clutching the gray net, her chin tucked toward her shoulder. Her brown hair is pulled back, and she wears an ash-brown shirt with white bands and a mauve-pink and silvery gray skirt that a stiff breeze sweeps to the left, showing red stockings. White water breaks over boulders, light flashes along the horizon, and a white bird flies in a pewter sky. Signed 1897.","i":"The monumental fisherwoman, a figure Homer first explored at Cullercoats in 1881 and 1882 and returned to in the 1890s, stands for the women of coastal communities whose lives were tied to the sea. She is neither idealized nor a portrait. The net signals work, and the wind-swept skirt and stormy light tie her to the weather. The title's 'light' may refer to the bright strip along the horizon, a contrast with the gray sky.","c":"The figure is placed against the sea with sculptural solidity, and the wind, skirt and net give her movement, while the light on the horizon supplies a grand tonal contrast. As a work of 1897 it shows Homer's late preference for large simple forms and a restrained palette. Her expression is withdrawn, which makes her a type rather than a person, and viewers who want drama may prefer his rescue scenes, but the mood of quiet vigilance is memorable."},"met:11040":{"d":"A portrait of a young woman, Molly Wales Fobes, painted in 1790 in oil on canvas, about 87 by 81 cm. A woman in a hat is shown with a chair, a hand fan and a feather, accompanied by a cat and a bird, and butterflies also appear among the recorded themes, so the portrait is crowded with emblems of her taste and household. It is a provincial portrait of the kind painted for families in rural New England.","i":"Portraits like this were commissioned by country families to record a young woman's status, often at the threshold of adulthood or marriage. The fan, the feathered hat and the pets display fashion and refinement; a cat and a bird may be household pets or signs of domestic life, and butterflies traditionally suggest the soul or the brevity of beauty. Rufus Hathaway, who later became a physician, was about twenty when he painted it. The sitter's story is not recorded here.","c":"The charm of such pictures lies in directness and decorative surplus: bright patterns, an eager display of the sitter's finery, and the pets and butterflies for incident. As in most provincial portraiture, drawing and modeling are probably simple, but the painter's evident delight in detail gives the picture a vitality that more correct work often lacks. It is an engaging record of a young woman's world and a good example of New England portraiture of its date."},"met:437240":{"d":"A small gold-ground panel, about 62 by 40 cm, in tempera, dated about 1320, combining several sacred subjects: Saint John on Patmos, the Madonna and Child enthroned, the Death of the Virgin and the Crucifixion. Angels and saints, among them Francis, John the Baptist, Paul and Mary Magdalene, are listed, so the panel appears to belong to a small, portable altarpiece in which a central devotional image is surrounded by scenes of the Virgin's life and Christ's sacrifice.","i":"Pacino di Bonaguida was a Florentine painter and manuscript illuminator who made small, jewel-like devotional works in the decades after Giotto. A tabernacle of this kind joined the Madonna's majesty to scenes that framed her role: her death and Christ's crucifixion show her bond with his sacrifice, and Saint John on Patmos, writing the Book of Revelation, was often linked with the Virgin through the vision of the woman clothed with the sun. The presence of Saint Francis may point to a Franciscan patron.","c":"Pacino's training as an illuminator should show in delicate drawing, lively little narratives and crisp color against gold, with the Florentine taste for storytelling. The picture is less monumental than Giotto's work but more intimate, and its charm is detail. Its limit is a repetition of types, and the crowding of subjects can make it harder to read. As a small example of Florentine devotional art of the early fourteenth century it is attractive and informative."},"met:436008":{"d":"A portrait of the musician Alphonse Promayet (1822-1872), painted in 1851 in oil on canvas, about 107 by 70 cm, a size that allows the figure to be shown well down the body. A man, a violin and a musician are noted, so the sitter appears with his instrument, in the plain, unidealized manner that Courbet brought to the likenesses of his friends and companions.","i":"Courbet, the leader of French Realism, painted his friends, among them musicians and writers of the Parisian bohemia, and a portrait with a violin identifies the sitter by his profession rather than by rank. Realism insisted on painting contemporary people as they were, without the idealization or historical costume of academic painting, and a musician in everyday dress made the point. The portrait was probably an act of friendship as much as a commission.","c":"The portrait's honesty is its strength: Courbet gives the sitter weight, presence and a quiet dignity. In 1851 he was at the start of his fame, and his portraits of friends show his gift for solid, unflattering truth. As a likeness it is competent and warm, and as an image of the artist's circle it is valuable. It is less audacious than his big canvases but shows the same refusal to flatter."},"met:438820":{"d":"Three young women in summer dresses and straw hats walk down a grassy slope in a rocky valley. The one in pale pink carries a basket and holds out her hand to a barefoot girl in a patched dress and head cloth, who stands at the left; the second shelters under a small parasol, and the third, in yellow, looks off to one side. A small black-and-white dog trots at their feet. At the right two cows, one buff and one brown and white, stand on the hillside. Behind rise a dark hollow in the rock and a bare grey limestone ledge under a pale sky streaked with thin cloud. The picture is very large, about two metres high and 2.6 metres wide.","i":"The Metropolitan Museum identifies the three women as Courbet's sisters Zélie, Juliette and Zoé, strolling in a small valley near their home village of Ornans, in eastern France, with one offering alms to a young cowherd. Courbet showed the picture at the Salon of 1852, and critics attacked it as tasteless and clumsy, mocking the plain faces and countrified dress of the so-called ladies, the small dog and cattle, and the lack of a unified perspective. X-rays show that he later enlarged the cattle, perhaps in answer to the jibes. The picture began a series on the lives of women.","c":"The critics of 1852 were partly right. The three women and the girl stand in a flat row, the cows seem to belong to another picture, and the light is dull, so the large canvas feels more assembled than seen. But the awkwardness is also the subject. Courbet painted provincial girls as they were, neither idealised nor mocked, and gave a pasture the scale usually kept for history painting. As a work it is stiffer and less sure than A Burial at Ornans of two years earlier, but it shows what his Realism had set out to do."},"nga:30231":{"d":"Dense canopies enclose a forest interior along a stream. Only a couple of small patches of ultramarine sky break through the foliage, which is built from dabs and strokes of pine, olive and spring green. Dark trunks rise straight or curve up through the leaves. The stream starts in the lower right corner and runs into the distance along the right half of the picture, its rippling water painted in muted green, ice blue and very pale pink, while grassy patches line the dirt ground at left. Oil on canvas, about 104 by 137 centimeters, signed and dated 1855.","i":"Puits-Noir, the Black Well, is a wooded stream in the valley of the Loue, near Ornans, the Franche-Comté town where Courbet was born. He painted the countryside of his home region again and again, treating its rocks, woods and streams as worthy subjects on the scale of history painting, a stand that made him the leader of the Realist movement. The picture presents the place as damp, dark and real, without a classical or picturesque frame.","c":"A dense, tactile picture that makes the viewer feel the shade and moisture of the forest, with thick paint and greens kept close in value. The lack of a clear focus, and the scale, go against landscape convention, and that is the point: Courbet treats the wood as physical substance. If the closeness can feel claustrophobic, the effect is powerful, and the picture shows why he mattered to later painters from Cézanne to the Impressionists."},"met:436025":{"d":"A landscape of Ornans, Courbet's native town in the Franche-Comté near the Swiss border, probably painted in the mid-1850s, in oil on canvas, about 73 by 92 cm. The town and the river are named among its subjects, so the view shows the houses and the water of the Loue below the cliffs that enclose the valley, in a close, earthy treatment typical of the painter's attachment to his home country.","i":"Ornans was Courbet's home town, and he painted its valley, cliffs and river throughout his life, a proud provincial among Parisians. His Realism was rooted in the particulars of the Franche-Comté: the limestone cliffs, the damp valley, the river. Ornans appears in his major pictures, A Burial at Ornans and After Dinner at Ornans, and views of the town found buyers. The date, probably mid-1850s, is uncertain.","c":"Courbet's landscapes are heavy with paint, and he used the palette knife for rocks and foliage, so his Ornans views feel earthy and unromantic. Compared with the contemporary Barbizon painters, his landscape is more physical and less atmospheric. A view of a town is a more conventional subject than his dramatic rocky valleys, and the result may be less distinctive, but it shows his deep attachment to place, and the honesty of the record is persuasive."},"nga:53126":{"d":"A green landscape under a bright blue sky. Trees fill the grassy ground, a small blue stream winds across the grass at the left and reflects the light, and a hill with small rocks and light green trees rises on the right. A few wispy white clouds cross the sky. The scene is best read as a quiet pastoral view named for La Bretonnerie, a place in the Indre department of central France. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 73 centimeters, dated 1856.","i":"The Indre is a department in the Berry region of central France, far from Courbet's native Franche-Comté, and the picture probably records a visit to the countryside during one of his journeys. Courbet's landscapes of the 1850s adopt the Realist approach: an ordinary stretch of land, painted directly with no classical frame. The title names the property, La Bretonnerie, rather than a famous view, which fits his refusal of the picturesque.","c":"A fresh, unassuming landscape in which the colors are clear and the handling direct. The bright blue of stream and sky and the rich greens suggest a day in good light, and Courbet manages a sense of breadth with few elements. It is slighter than his big Franche-Comté landscapes and has less of their physical density, but it shows the plain, honest eye that made the Realist landscape possible."},"met:436014":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 93 by 149 cm, dated 1857, showing hunting dogs with a dead hare in a forest. Dogs, a rabbit or hare, trees and hunting are the recorded subjects, so the picture is a scene of the chase's aftermath, in which hounds are posed around the kill amid the trees. Courbet painted such pictures of hunting and game from his home country of the Franche-Comté.","i":"Courbet, the son of a landowner in the hunting country of the Franche-Comté, painted hunting scenes throughout his career, and the forest and its animals are shown with a physical directness. For a Realist, the hunt was part of rural life, and the dead game piece had a long tradition in still life and animal painting. His version is less courtly than the eighteenth-century hunting pictures: not an aristocratic pastime but a countryman's pursuit.","c":"The strength is the physical presence of the animals and the thick, earthy paint: the hounds have weight, the forest is dark, and the dead hare has a still-life poignancy. Dogs and game were popular with buyers, and Courbet's hunting pictures at the end of the 1850s were less radical than his figure paintings but in some ways more consistently successful. Without softening, the sight of the dead hare is hard to forget."},"met:436015":{"d":"A large portrait, about 149 by 107 cm, dated 1857, of the tenor Louis Gueymard (1822-1880) in the costume of Robert in Giacomo Meyerbeer's opera Robert le Diable. It is a 'historiated portrait,' meaning that the sitter is shown in the character of a role rather than as himself: a singer of the Paris Opéra, painted by the leader of French Realism, in a theatrical subject that is unusual in Courbet's work.","i":"Robert le Diable (1831) was one of the great successes of French grand opera, the story of a knight, son of a devil and a princess, torn between his demonic father and his mortal love. Gueymard was a leading tenor at the Paris Opéra, and a portrait in the role celebrated both the singer and the part. Courbet, who loved music and moved among musicians, may have painted it as a tribute to a friend's art.","c":"The portrait is a tribute to a friend's art and a meeting of two reputations, made when Courbet was receiving larger commissions. Yet the stage costume and grand pose sit uneasily with his plain-spoken Realism, and the picture reads more as a role than as a man. It succeeds as a vivid, solid image, but it is not among his best-known pictures, and its interest is in the contrast between theater and truth."},"met:436016":{"d":"A woman in a black, flounced dress sits close to life-size on a tall canvas, her body slumped slightly to one side and her head tilted toward her shoulder. A green shawl with orange-gold bands falls from her shoulders, and red coral bracelets circle both wrists. One hand, in a white glove, touches the lace at her throat; the other, bare, hangs by her side with a white handkerchief. Her feet rest on a green embroidered footstool. A small veneered table at the left and a red patterned curtain at the right are painted in dark, thin tones, while the black silk of her dress is built up in long folds. Her eyes look at the viewer with tired, level calm.","i":"The sitter was Mathilde Desportes (1827–1910), who married Auguste Cuoq. The Met dates the portrait about 1852–57. Her husband is said to have rejected it, feeling that it did not do justice to his wife's beauty, so Courbet kept the canvas and exhibited it in 1867. The large scale and the intimate, unguarded pose make it one of Courbet's most unusual portraits, and it fits his Realist principle of not prettifying what he saw. It came to the Metropolitan Museum from the collection of H. O. Havemeyer and his wife Louisine, who collected Courbet, as part of her bequest in 1929.","c":"This is a frank portrait, and one can see why the husband objected. The black dress takes up most of the canvas and leaves little to look at but the face, to which Courbet gives no flattering beauty and no pose of authority, only a tired, direct presence. The body is hard to read under the black silk, and the interior is a vague dark stage. But the colour scheme of black, green and red coral is bold, and the picture has an honesty that fashionable Second Empire portraits seldom allowed."},"nga:46587":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a young sitter faces the viewer with her body while her head turns to the right in three-quarter view, looking away. She has pale skin, ruddy cheeks, dark eyes, short wavy brown hair and pink lips in a neutral expression. She wears a broad-brimmed brown hat with a brown bow or flower at the right and a ribbon hanging at the left, and a red-brown cloak with a large white collar over dark garments. The background is light gray, with soft, visible brushstrokes. Oil on canvas, about 60 by 53 centimeters, dated 1857.","i":"Courbet painted portraits of friends, patrons and local acquaintances throughout the 1850s, and the title leaves the girl unnamed, so she may belong to that circle. The broad hat and cloak read as everyday dress rather than a costume piece, and the plain gray ground follows the Realist rule of showing a sitter without props. The averted gaze lets the viewer study her at length.","c":"A direct, unflattering and rather appealing study: the cheeks are ruddy, the head is solid, and the soft gray ground avoids all ornament. Courbet's portraits of this kind lack the bravura of his large pictures but have a sturdy, plain presence. The averted gaze and neutral mouth keep it clear of the sentimentality that was the norm for portraits of girls in the period."},"met:436000":{"d":"A portrait of Madame Frédéric Breyer, born Fanny Hélène Van Bruyssel (1830-1894), painted in 1858 in oil on canvas, about 91 by 73 cm. No dress or setting is described, but the type is clear: a society portrait of a married woman in her late twenties, on a canvas large enough to show the figure well down the body, painted by Courbet, whose portraiture joins frank observation to the solid, earthy handling of his other work.","i":"The sitter is identified by her husband's name and her own maiden name, the usual way of recording a married woman in the nineteenth century, and the portrait would have recorded her place in a prosperous household. Courbet painted a number of portraits of patrons and friends in the 1850s, to earn a living and to show that Realism could produce likenesses as well as scenes of rural life. Nothing more is recorded of her family.","c":"Courbet's commissioned portraits can be a tug of war between his Realism and a client's wish to be flattered, and the best of them keep a sober, direct gaze and broad paint. On the evidence of his other portraits of the period, his approach to a female sitter was frank and unromantic, and this one is probably competent and honest. It is not among his most memorable pictures, but it shows how he handled the commissioned likeness."},"met:436017":{"d":"A portrait of a man known as Monsieur Suisse, painted in 1861 in oil on canvas, about 59 by 49 cm, a bust-size canvas of the kind a painter makes of a friend or acquaintance. No dress or setting is recorded. The title gives only the name: Suisse is a French surname, and also the word for a church beadle, but the record does not say which is meant.","i":"Courbet was in these years a prominent, controversial painter with a wide circle of admirers and a short-lived studio of students, and his portraits of male friends and patrons are among his most direct works. The sitter's identity is not further recorded here. A small, direct portrait of a modest man shows the democratic sense of Realism that anyone, whatever his rank, is a worthy subject.","c":"Small portraits like this are where Courbet's Realism is most intimate: the head modeled in thick, confident paint, free of flattery. Without a described face or costume little more can be said than that it is a typical, sound example, valued for the plainness and weight that he gave to ordinary sitters. It is a modest work, and its interest lies in its honesty rather than in any novelty of design or invention."},"met:436009":{"d":"A forest landscape, about 53 by 65 cm, probably painted in 1862, with trees and a brook running through a clearing. The title suggests it may be the study recorded as Brook, Valley of Fontcouverte, which would place it in the Saintonge, the region of western France where Courbet stayed with his patron Étienne Baudry in 1862. Forests and a stream are the recorded subjects, so the picture is a woodland interior rather than a view.","i":"In the Saintonge in 1862, Courbet worked on landscapes in the open, filling canvases with the forest's mossy greens and the play of water, and his patron's estate gave him freedom to paint nature in a physical rather than a picturesque way. A forest interior with a stream was a favorite Courbet theme. Whether this canvas is the study mentioned in the title is a question of attribution of the title, not of the painting itself.","c":"Courbet's forest interiors are among his most original landscapes: dense, damp and physical, with the paint laid on in broad patches. As a study it is probably freer than a finished picture, and its interest lies in how he builds the clearing's light from dark greens. Its seriousness about plain nature, without incident or anecdote, was a new thing in French landscape, and the picture is a good example of that attitude."},"met:436022":{"d":"A naked woman stands with her back to the viewer, knee-deep in a shallow stream at the foot of a small waterfall. Her dark hair is pinned up in a loose bun, and she leans forward with her arms stretched out over a mossy rock and a curving branch, almost embracing the falling water. The figure fills the center of a tall, narrow canvas, lit softly against deep green and brown foliage, with spray and pale ripples at her feet. Her flesh is rendered in warm, creamy tones with dark shadow, and the signature appears at the lower right.","i":"The title means a spring or the source of a river, a theme of allegory since antiquity. The Met suggests Courbet may have been answering Ingres's The Source, which had been shown in Paris the year before: Ingres's smooth, idealized girl pours water from a jar, while Courbet's is an actual woman, seen from behind and unflattered, with a loose bun and a solid, ordinary body. The rejection of the classical ideal was the heart of Realism. The model is unknown. Louisine Havemeyer acquired the painting and left it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1929.","c":"The back view is unusually frank: Courbet declines the face, the pose, and the ideal. The flesh, with its soft shadows and the plausible bulk of the haunches, is superbly handled, and the dark wet rock gives the figure physical presence. The weaknesses are in the setting: dark, rather muddy, more backdrop than place, and the gesture of embracing the waterfall feels contrived. As a reply to Ingres it is polemical more than poetic, but its honesty still has force."},"met:436018":{"d":"A female nude with a flowering branch, painted in 1863 in oil on canvas, about 75 by 61 cm. It is a cabinet nude, sized for a private room, in which the woman is given no mythological name but is simply a woman, in keeping with Courbet's insistence on painting what was real in the years when he provoked the Salon with such subjects.","i":"Nudes in the Salon needed the pretext of myth or allegory to be respectable, and Courbet's habit of painting real women without that pretext was provocative and sold well to private collectors, as with his Woman with a Parrot of 1866. The flowering branch may be a sign of spring and fertility, an old attribute of Flora or Venus, but nothing in the title assigns the woman a role, and the picture offers frank, worldly beauty.","c":"Courbet's nudes are earthy rather than idealized, and that frank flesh is their point: the body is a physical presence in a bold, direct light. This one is modest in size and not among his boldest, but it is pleasing for its simplicity. Where classical nudes are cool, his are warm, heavy and individual, and the flowering branch is a decorative accent in a design that depends mainly on the body."},"met:436007":{"d":"A very large canvas, about 2.4 by 1.9 meters, painted about 1859, on the subject of a hunter's return. Men, dogs, birds, a fox and rabbits are recorded, so the picture shows the spoils of a hunt and the people and dogs who took part. At this size it is a major exhibition picture rather than a cabinet piece, a hunting subject treated with the scale usually reserved for history painting.","i":"Courbet's hunting pictures of the late 1850s grew from his visit to Germany in 1858 and 1859, probably, where he joined hunts in the forests near Frankfurt, and from his boyhood in the hunting country of the Franche-Comté. The large scale is a Realist statement: the countryman's pursuit given the dimensions of a history painting. The subject also met the taste of the Second Empire's elite for hunting scenes, one of Courbet's reliable markets.","c":"At two and a half meters, the picture invests an ordinary game bag with epic weight, and the contrast between heroic scale and plain subject is both its boldness and its problem: the figure and the dead animals may feel more laborious than lively. Courbet's brushwork is thick and the color dark and earthy. The picture shows his determination to treat rural life as a major subject, and it is impressive, if somewhat ponderous."},"met:436023":{"d":"A large landscape, about 100 by 142 cm, dated 1864, of the source of the Loue, the river that issues from a cave at the foot of limestone cliffs near Ornans in the Franche-Comté. A river is the recorded subject, so the picture shows water emerging from the rock in a gorge, in the dark, damp, enclosed manner of the painter's home country.","i":"The source of the Loue, a resurgence from a cave in a limestone gorge, was a natural wonder of Courbet's region, and he painted it repeatedly from 1864 into the 1870s. For Courbet the cliffs and the cave were a form of his homeland, and they grounded his Realism in the particulars of the Franche-Comté. The subject suited both the period's taste for dramatic scenery and his own for matter: wet rock, moss and the mass of stone.","c":"The picture is a triumph of material painting: rock rendered with the palette knife in thick, wet strokes, with Courbet's love of mass and texture, and the result is more physical than the usual Romantic view of such grottoes. The effect can feel heavy and dark, but it is solid and convincing. Landscapes like this influenced the Impressionists, who admired his direct, unidealized handling of nature."},"met:436024":{"d":"A portrait of a woman in a riding habit, called L'Amazone, painted about 1855 to 1859 in oil on canvas, about 116 by 89 cm. The sitter's name is not recorded here. The riding habit, a close-fitting jacket with a long skirt, was a fashionable costume that let a woman be shown as active and elegant, and the type of picture is a half- or three-quarter-length society portrait with a hint of sporting life.","i":"'Amazone' was the French word for a woman rider and for the dress she wore, a term that recalled the mythical warrior women and gave equestrian ladies a touch of glamour. Under the Second Empire, riding in the Bois de Boulogne was fashionable, and a portrait in riding dress announced rank and leisure. Courbet, who rejected the idealizing portrait, paints her in ordinary realistic terms.","c":"As a portrait of a woman in costume, the picture has the usual Courbet virtues, a firm sense of form and a dark, earthy palette, with the habit supplying a mass of dark cloth for the painter to model. The risk of such a picture is stiffness, since a commissioned pose imposes constraints. Her identity remains open, and as a type the picture is accomplished though not especially original."},"nga:43681":{"d":"A pair of arched cave openings gape in a steep, rocky cliff face that fills the canvas. The cliff is painted with dabs and textured strokes of slate gray, peachy tan and muted blue. The larger opening, left of center, is about four times the size of the smaller one beside it. Murky green water reflects the shadowy openings and the stone pier between them, and churns white over boulders at the lower right. A tiny barefoot man, trousers rolled to the knee, stands with his back to the viewer before the larger opening, holding a long stick. Oil on canvas, about 98 by 130 centimeters, 1864.","i":"The Loue rises from a cave in the limestone cliffs of the Jura near Ornans, Courbet's birthplace, and the place was a landmark of his home country, which he painted repeatedly. The tiny figure gives the cliff scale and suggests human insignificance, a Romantic note, but Courbet treats the rock as a solid, textured mass painted in thick strokes, in accord with his Realist insistence on matter.","c":"One of Courbet's most physical landscapes. The cliff is treated almost as a wall of paint, with a few colors scumbled and dabbed into a rough surface that has the weight of stone, and the dark mouths of the caves give the picture a black center. The tiny figure is all that keeps it from pure abstraction. It is a bold, even harsh picture, free of picturesque charm, and it shows the freedom with paint that later painters admired."},"met:436012":{"d":"A fishing boat lies tilted on a shore of rocks and sand, filling the right half of the picture. Its sails are loosely gathered and slack, with rigging running up to a small pennant at the masthead, and the dark hull has gear heaped inside. In front, rounded boulders and bare beach lead to a gray-green sea breaking in long, low waves. Above the horizon a bank of storm cloud sits under a sky of peach and blue-gray. The paint is thin and quick in the sea and sky and thicker on the hull. The artist's name is signed at lower left.","i":"Courbet painted this at Trouville on the Normandy coast, during an intensely productive stay with James McNeill Whistler from September to November 1865. He boasted to his father that he had finished thirty-five paintings, which he said stunned everybody. Where other coastal painters such as Boudin and Isabey treated boats as small accents in a view, Courbet made the vessel itself the subject. He was the leader of French Realism, which gave ordinary people and objects the full scale once reserved for history painting. This was the first of his paintings to enter the Metropolitan Museum, in 1899.","c":"It is a portrait of a boat, and Courbet gives the hull the same stubborn solidity he gave his peasants. The sea and sky, handled thinly and fast, give an open-air feeling that the boat's weight sets off. The rigging and sails are the weaker part, a tangle of dull strokes that does not read clearly. Beside the bright, airy harbors Boudin painted on the same coast, it is heavier and plainer. Its value lies in that bluntness: nothing is prettified, and the boat looks tired and real."},"met:436001":{"d":"A portrait of Joanna Hiffernan, called Jo, the Irish model who posed for Whistler, painted in 1865 to 1866 in oil on canvas, about 56 by 66 cm. She is shown with a mirror, in the pose of a woman at her toilette, and her long red hair is the picture's principal glory. Courbet painted several versions of the subject, and this is one of them.","i":"Courbet met Jo at Trouville in 1865, where she was with James McNeill Whistler, and the title La Belle Irlandaise reflects her Irish background and her beauty. The mirror joins the old theme of vanity to a modern portrait of a woman's presence, and it let Courbet show female beauty without the pretense of Venus. Jo is also believed to be the model for his controversial picture of sleeping women of 1866.","c":"A coup of color and sensuality: the red hair against the dark ground, the soft flesh tones, and a brushwork unusually tender for Courbet. The conceit of the mirror doubles the face and lets the sitter be absorbed in herself, which helps the picture escape the heaviness of some of his work. It is among the most sensuous of his portraits, and a rare moment when his earthy Realism turns lyric."},"met:436002":{"d":"A nude woman lies on her back across white bedding, her head thrown back so that her long, tousled hair spills over the edge at the lower left. One arm is raised to a green and yellow parrot, which spreads its wings above her hand, while her other hand rests low across her thigh. A tall perch with a feeding cup stands at the far right, and dark foliage and drapery fill the background. Crumpled cloth and ribbons lie in the shadow at the lower right. The flesh is smooth and warmly modeled against the dark ground, the sheet and hair handled with broader, freer strokes. The canvas is wide and low, nearly two meters across, and signed at the lower left.","i":"Courbet sent this to the Salon of 1866, the first of his nudes to be accepted there, after a nude he had submitted in 1864 was refused. The pose borrows from the long tradition of the reclining Venus, but the model's disordered hair, discarded clothes, and ordinary body place her in the present, not in myth. Joanna Hiffernan, Whistler's companion, is thought to have posed. The parrot adds an exotic note. Édouard Manet painted a clothed counterpart the same year, Young Lady in 1866, which also has a parrot; both pictures are now in the Metropolitan Museum. Louisine Havemeyer, a keen collector of Courbet, left this one to the museum in 1929.","c":"As pure painting this is Courbet at his most persuasive: the flesh is built in soft, unbroken gradations, and the white sheet and the hair are handled with real bravura. It is also one of his more conventional nudes. The pose is as polished as any Salon Venus, and the parrot reads as a prop. Its freedom lies in what it refuses: no goddess, no allegory, only a woman who has taken her clothes off. That honesty, wrapped in an acceptable format, is probably why the jury let it through."},"met:436003":{"d":"A large canvas, about 130 by 97 cm, dated 1866, of a nude young woman bathing in a stream in the countryside. The records list a stream, a rural setting, a low shrub on the ground and, notably, her waist-length red hair. It is a nude without a mythological pretext, in an open-air setting, which was bold for the time and typical of Courbet's challenge to the conventions of the Salon.","i":"The subject descends from the old theme of the bather, Susanna or Diana, whose nudity was excused by myth. Courbet drops the myth and gives a modern woman in a French landscape. His nudes were part of his challenge to the academic rules of the Salon, and some were bought by private collectors, though his Bathers of 1853 had caused a scandal. The Young Bather continues that line with a lighter touch.","c":"The red hair and pale body against the dark foliage make a strong color design, and the figure's ease is natural, whereas the Bathers of 1853 was a scandal for its heavy flesh, so the Young Bather may be lighter and more charming. It is a confident, accomplished nude by a painter at the height of his powers, if less radical than his large canvases. Its appeal is in directness, not in myth."},"nga:154844":{"d":"A beach lined with black rocks and boulders opens onto the sea beneath a brick-red sky that dominates the nearly square canvas. The sky is turquoise at the top edge, fading to salmon pink and then deep red along the low horizon, with steel-gray clouds rippling across its width. Two sailboats float far out on water painted the same turquoise as the sky, with reflections of pink and red. In the foreground water breaks around jagged black rocks above a strip of mustard-yellow and tan sand. Oil on canvas, about 50 by 61 centimeters, 1865 to 1866.","i":"Trouville, on the Normandy coast, was a fashionable resort by the 1860s, and Courbet worked there in the mid-1860s on seascapes and beach scenes. A sky dominated by red was a challenge for painters, and Courbet's version is bold and unnaturalistic in its intensity. The black rocks are the picture's anchor. Here the leader of the Realists uses a natural phenomenon as a vehicle for color and mood.","c":"A startling picture for the 1860s: the huge red sky pressing on a low horizon and a hard-edged turquoise sea give the scene a raw, theatrical intensity, and the jagged rocks add a sullen weight. Courbet's seascapes were criticized as empty, and this one is as spare as any, but its audacity of color now looks prophetic. It is among the most arresting of his coastal pictures and a forerunner of later painters' freedom with color."},"nga:66408":{"d":"A narrow strip of beach gives way to a vivid turquoise stretch of water beneath a towering, hazy sky in a nearly square canvas. The beach is painted in strokes of tan and peachy brown skimmed with dark brown or black to suggest rocks or shells. The water runs to a low horizon, a quarter of the way up, in topaz and powder blue, and piles of fluffy cloud above are brushed loosely in cotton and gossamer white. Oil on canvas, about 54 by 64 centimeters, signed and dated 66 in red.","i":"Courbet painted a series of seascapes on the Normandy coast in the mid-1860s, working at Trouville and later at Étretat, where sea and sky filled most of the canvas and the human presence vanished. The title speaks of calm, a counterpoint to his stormy waves, and the picture reduces a seaside view to three bands of sand, water and cloud. The empty, wide horizon anticipates the seascapes of Whistler and the Impressionists.","c":"Spare to the point of austerity, this is a picture of almost nothing, and that is its strength: the turquoise band, the stretch of tan sand, the piled clouds, all handled in a few strokes. It has a modern clarity that few painters of 1866 dared. If it seems slight beside the great waves of his later seascapes, it deserves attention as a daring experiment in flat, wide space."},"met:436019":{"d":"A small portrait of an unnamed man, painted probably around 1862 in oil on canvas, about 41 by 33 cm. It is a small bust portrait of the kind Courbet made of friends and acquaintances, a head rendered with direct, solid brushwork, without flattery or elaborate setting. A head-and-shoulders format keeps attention on the face, and the picture's interest rests on the sitter's presence and the painter's plain, unidealized way of seeing.","i":"An unnamed sitter on a small canvas is a reminder that portraiture, for Courbet, was a form of Realism: a person painted as he was, without rank or symbol. Such small portraits were often made for friends or as studies, not as commissions. The sitter's name, profession and relation to the painter are not recorded here, and the picture's meaning is therefore that of a plain, honest likeness.","c":"Small and spare, the portrait relies on the head: modeling, expression and honesty. Judging by his other portraits of the period, the face would be treated with direct, solid brushwork and little concession to polish. It is modest and probably a study or a gift rather than a commission, and its interest is as evidence of Courbet's habit of looking at ordinary faces with serious attention."},"met:436004":{"d":"A woman rises from the sea, seen from the waist up, her arms raised and folded above her head so that they form a loop around her face. Her wavy auburn hair is loosely pinned, and she looks aside to the right with an absent gaze. White foam and green-gray water slap around her waist in thick, quick strokes. Behind her the sky darkens to a storm, with a pale band at the horizon and a tiny sail at the right. The flesh is smooth and carefully graded, a trace of underarm hair visible, in sharp contrast to the rough handling of the water. It is a small upright canvas, signed in red at the lower left.","i":"Courbet's interest in the sea and its waves grew in the late 1860s, and this picture, dated 1868, joins that interest to his taste for the nude. Standing nymphs and sea-born Venuses were Salon staples, and the figure here recalls them, but Courbet offers no shell, no attendants, and no myth, only an actual body in actual water, painted with a bather's directness. The model's identity is unknown. The picture was shown at the Ghent Salon of 1868 and again in Brussels in 1872. It entered the Metropolitan Museum with the Havemeyer bequest of 1929.","c":"A sensuous and technically bold picture whose weakness is a certain placidity. The sea is wonderfully painted, with spume dashed on in fat strokes, but the figure is a studio pose, sweetly lit and rather inert; the looped arms are stagey, and the echo of a Venus rising from the waves never resolves into either parody or conviction. The meeting of honest observation and the academic nude is the interest, and the limit, of the picture. It is less confrontational than Woman with a Parrot and less ambitious than the wave pictures that followed."},"nga:46589":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a woman reads a book with one elbow propped on a grassy bank, her body angled to the right and her gaze lowered to a pale green-covered volume. She has dark brows over hooded eyes, a round face, and dark brown hair falling loosely behind her shoulders; with her free hand she touches her hair, the little finger delicately crooked. A strap of her pearl-white chemise has slipped from her shoulder, and the loosened dress leaves much of one breast bare. Leafy trees and gray rocks are loosely painted behind. Oil on canvas, about 60 by 73 centimeters, about 1866 to 1868.","i":"Courbet painted a series of reading women and bathers in the 1860s, and the combination of a book with a partly bare body belongs to that sensual theme. The book signals leisure and education, while the slipping chemise offers the viewer an easy intimacy; Salon audiences of the Second Empire, who accepted nudes only when classical, found such frank modern flesh provocative. The sitter is not identified.","c":"A frank, warm picture in which the pale flesh, the green book and the loosely brushed foliage set up an easy, relaxed harmony. The pose is natural, and the little finger crooked at the hair is a delicate, observant touch. The sensuality is direct and earthier than the classical nudes of the Salon, and it avoids both coyness and coarseness. Painted with Courbet's firm, unfussy handling, it is typical of his late sensual manner."},"met:436005":{"d":"A vast sky of heaped cumulus fills almost two thirds of the canvas, painted in thick cream, grey and pale yellow with patches of blue showing through. Below it the horizon sits low, where a band of green-blue sea meets the sky and a few tiny sails stand far out. In the foreground, wet beach sand stretches in flat bands of beige and pink. Two small boats lie beached, one at the right with its mast and ropes lying along the sand, the other farther away. There are no figures. The paint is buttery and heavy in the clouds and thinner on the sand, and the signature G. Courbet is at the lower left.","i":"Courbet painted this on the Normandy coast in 1869, part of a group of seascapes made that year. It is among the barest: sky, water, sand and two boats, with no warships, no storm and no people. Courbet was best known for large figure paintings of ordinary life, such as A Burial at Ornans, and the sea pictures show the same refusal to idealise, applied to weather. Later painters, Monet among them, worked the same coast. The Metropolitan Museum owns it through the bequest of Louisine Havemeyer, who with her husband collected Courbet, in 1929.","c":"The drama is all in the clouds. The sea is a thin strip, the boats are little more than marks, and the sand is a plain slab, and that austerity is both the picture's strength and its limit. The buttery paint makes the cloud feel solid, and the cool light is convincing. But the foreground is flat and the beached boats are placed a shade too neatly to look chance. It is a bold, nearly abstract image of weather, and it points toward what Monet and others would do with the same coast."},"met:436006":{"d":"A seascape, about 69 by 100 cm, dated 1870, showing a waterspout, a whirling column of water and cloud that forms over the sea in a storm. Seascape and waves are listed, so the picture is a scene of rough weather painted in Courbet's broad, physical manner, in the group of Normandy coast pictures he made around 1869 and 1870.","i":"Courbet had painted the Normandy coast at Trouville in 1865 and at Étretat in 1869, and his waves, with their palette-knife thickness, became a staple of his late work. A waterspout is an unusual natural event to choose, and its drama suited the Romantic tradition of the sublime, but Courbet's rendering stays close to observation rather than allegory. The title calls it a marine, the French term for a seascape.","c":"The sea is not an ornament but a mass: thick paint conveys weight, and the water is a physical, resistant substance, typical of Courbet's late marines, which were widely admired and which influenced Monet and Manet. The waterspout's drama is unusual for him, and the picture makes something spectacular from his plain method. It is more powerful than pretty, and it earns its place among his best seascapes."},"met:436011":{"d":"A winter landscape, about 75 by 92 cm, painted about 1865, with deer in the snow. Deer, snow and winter are noted, so the picture shows animals in a cold, quiet forest, a subject close to Courbet's hunting and snow scenes of the 1860s, painted with broad strokes in muted, snowy tones. It is a cabinet picture for a private collector, not a public showpiece.","i":"Courbet's winter pictures of deer, often set in the snowbound woods of the Jura, came from his love of the hunting country and its animals, and here the game appears as wild creatures at rest. Deer have symbolic associations, a hunted stag suggesting suffering, but Courbet's emphasis is on physical presence: the animals as part of the landscape. His hunting and snow scenes of the mid-1860s sold well.","c":"Snow is a demanding subject because it must be painted in many shades of gray and blue, and Courbet's palette-knife technique suits its heaviness, so his deer are solid rather than graceful. The picture has the quiet of the winter forest and an insistence on matter. It is an attractive, representative piece of his later animal and landscape painting, though neither as dramatic nor as inventive as his large canvases."},"nga:46485":{"d":"Towering, sheer cliffs loom at the left over a sunny beach where four empty wooden boats rest on the sand. The chalky faces are streaked with olive green, dark brown, gray, rust red and mustard yellow, and their mossy tops catch sunlight from the upper left, with a lone leafy tree leaning in between the nearest two. One boat is stripped and dilapidated; another carries a ginger-brown sail and a small blue, white and red flag. Two people sit or stand farther off, and a few sailboats drift on the bright blue water. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 90 centimeters, about 1872 to 1875.","i":"The beach is Étretat on the Normandy coast, famous for its white cliffs, which Courbet painted in 1869 and later. The wooden fishing boats, hauled onto the sand and left with sails and ropes, are the working life of the place seen without its workers, so the picture has the quality of a still life as much as a seascape. By painting a working beach with thick, direct strokes, Courbet gave a modest scene the monumental treatment once reserved for history.","c":"The picture combines monumentality with plain observation: the cliffs press in like a wall, their surfaces built from thick patches of color, and the beached boats supply a human counterpoint. It lacks the focus of his best coastal works, and the late date shows in a certain routine of handling, but the sunlit sand and the rich cliff colors are persuasive. A solid late Courbet, not a revelation."},"nga:53125":{"d":"Four boats with low masts sit on a sandy beach beside rocky cliffs under a cloud-filled sky. Three of the boats cluster near the center and the fourth lies a short distance to the left. The beach is painted in visible dabs and strokes of tan and cream with gray shadows; the cliffs, scarcely taller than the masts, are smeared and smudged in warm brown and black. Dark shoals mark where the beach meets the water, with two white-sailed boats in the distance, and bands of parchment white, gray and smoky lavender gather in an icy blue sky. Oil on canvas, about 65 by 91 centimeters, about 1872 to 1875.","i":"Étretat's cliffs and the Porte d'Amont, one of its natural arches, were already among the best-known sights of the Normandy coast, but Courbet's attention here is on the humble boats of the fishing community. This is a late version of a subject he first painted in 1869; by then he was in political trouble, imprisoned after the Commune of 1871 and exiled to Switzerland in 1873, and the Normandy beaches may have become a memory he returned to.","c":"A subdued, smudged picture in which the beached boats become quiet, almost sculptural objects against low cliffs. The handling is thick and sometimes careless, and the late date perhaps shows in a certain weariness; yet the pale sand and layered sky have a modest, shabby grandeur. Compared with the confident Étretat pictures of 1869, it feels like an echo, but an honest one: a minor late Courbet that still shows how much he could make of a plain subject."},"met:436010":{"d":"A small forest landscape, about 50 by 61 cm, painted between about 1868 and 1877, showing a brook running through woodland. It is a damp, enclosed woodland interior in which the water is the quiet focus and trees close the view. Courbet returned to the motif again and again, and a canvas of this size was a cabinet picture for a private buyer.","i":"The forest brook was one of Courbet's favorite motifs, drawn from the wooded valleys of his native Franche-Comté, where streams run through dark, mossy gorges. The wide date range includes his last years, after the Commune, when he was in exile in Switzerland and painted landscapes to earn his living, so the picture may belong to that period, though the record gives no place. No figure is recorded, and the subject is the interior of the wood itself.","c":"Courbet's forest brooks reject the picturesque in favor of dense, physical matter: dark greens, wet stone, light seeping through leaves. The theme is quiet and unadorned, and the effect depends on his handling of the paint, which makes foliage and water feel heavy and real. The picture is a representative example of a late motif he repeated with variations, valuable for its consistency and its refusal of drama, though it may seem monotonous to some viewers."},"met:436013":{"d":"A landscape on canvas, about 59 by 76 cm, painted about 1873 to 1877, whose title names a hidden brook. A river or stream is the recorded subject, so the picture shows water tucked among rock and foliage, partly concealed by the banks and the plants around it, in the shaded manner of Courbet's late forest and gorge scenes. It is a cabinet picture, made for a private collector.","i":"Courbet's Jura valleys are full of hidden streams, running in gorges among rocks and overhanging trees, and he made variations of the theme in the 1860s and 1870s. In the years after the Commune, when he was fined, imprisoned and then exiled in Switzerland, he painted landscapes in order to earn money. The title's idea of concealment suited his taste for enclosed places, though whether the picture was painted in France or Switzerland is not recorded.","c":"The idea of a stream half hidden gives the picture a quiet intimacy, and Courbet's heavy, tactile paint makes the foliage and wet stone convincing. Late landscapes of this sort are sometimes formulaic, repeating a set of dark greens and browns, and this one is probably no exception, but within the formula the sense of a particular, enclosed place is persuasive. It is a modest but characteristic late work, honest and unshowy."},"met:436020":{"d":"A landscape on canvas, about 50 by 61 cm, painted between 1873 and 1877, whose title gives the subject as a river and rocks. A river is listed, so the picture shows water running between or beneath rocky masses, with stone as the principal subject. It is a late work of Courbet's, from the last years of his life, when he lived in exile in Switzerland and continued to paint landscape subjects.","i":"Rock was a favorite Courbet subject because he could build it up with the palette knife in thick, tactile layers, and the limestone of his native Franche-Comté gave him many models. In his exile in Switzerland after 1873 he painted the lakes and streams of the region, and a river among rocks suits both landscapes. The title does not specify a place, and the picture is best read as a study of stone and water.","c":"Stone as monumental mass is what Courbet does best: dense, heavy and textured, as against the airy atmosphere of the Impressionists he influenced. A picture of a river and rocks gives him a sober, physical subject, and the result is powerful if somewhat dark. It lacks the drama of his gorge and cave pictures, but it shows the late style at its simplest and most direct, with little concession to charm."},"met:436021":{"d":"A seascape on canvas, about 51 by 61 cm, dated 1865 or later, of the sea and sky with clouds. Sky, seascape and clouds are recorded, and the title is simply The Sea, so the picture is a pure marine without ships or figures, in which water and weather are the whole subject. It belongs to the group of seascapes Courbet made on the Normandy coast from the mid-1860s and kept painting for the rest of his life.","i":"Courbet worked on the Normandy coast at Trouville in 1865, alongside Whistler, and again at Étretat in 1869, and his empty seascapes of sea and sky became a staple of his later output. The subject came to him as a new kind of landscape, with no story and no human presence, and it sold well to collectors. The date, 1865 or later, indicates that the exact year is uncertain.","c":"The empty seascape is a test of a painter's honesty, since there is nothing to hide behind: only the horizon, the movement of water and the clouds. Courbet meets it with broad, thick paint, and the sea has weight and mass rather than glitter. The result is austere and physical, closer to Boudin's skies in subject than in sentiment, and it was a model for later painters of the Impressionist generation."},"nga:96688":{"d":"Six men crowd into two small boats that together span the width of a wide canvas. On the left, Christ sits in profile in the stern with gold rays about his head, raising a hand toward a bald, bearded man in marigold orange who kneels in prayer before him. A younger man in pink and emerald green steps forward with outstretched arms. In the other boat, two bare-chested men haul in a net while an old white-bearded man sits in the bow, holding a half-submerged oar. Blue water stretches back to a distant mountain, and the horizon is set high.","i":"The scene is from Luke 5:1–11. After a night of failure, Simon Peter lets down his nets at Christ's word and takes so many fish that the boats begin to sink. Peter falls to his knees, confessing himself a sinful man, and Christ tells him that he will now catch people. The miracle is read as the call of the first disciples and as an image of the Church, with the boat as the ship of the faithful and the catch as the souls gathered in.","c":"An early work in which Bassano already shows his gifts: bold colour, a lively sense of bodies at work and a taste for plain, earthy types over idealised heroes. The crowded boats and high horizon push the figures toward the viewer, which gives the scene energy. Yet the composition is lopsided, with Christ at the very edge, and the muscular poses recall Mannerist prints. It lacks the unified atmosphere of his later pictures, but it is vigorous and fresh."},"nga:267":{"d":"At the foot of a steep grassy hill, shepherds, cows, sheep, a goat and a dog crowd the lower half of this vertical night scene. A man reclines at the lower left with a long pipe, a woman bends beside a cow with a pail, two boys look on, and a bearded man in pink shields his face with one hand as he looks up. Above, in a shaft of golden light breaking through navy-blue clouds, a winged angel in a grey robe leans down on a dark cloud and points toward him.","i":"The subject comes from Luke 2:8–20. On the night of the birth, an angel announces the Saviour to shepherds watching their flocks, and they are the first to hear the news. Their simple lives made them an image of humility, and Venetian painters liked to treat the scene as a rural idyll. Jacopo Bassano, who lived and worked in the small town of Bassano del Grappa, gave the shepherds the look of local country people, with their animals, tools and worn clothes.","c":"Bassano is at his most original in nocturnes like this. Instead of idealised herdsmen he offers peasants and farm animals observed with affection, and a single burst of golden light sets off the darkness. The weaker point is the link between the two halves, the angel above and the group below, which have little interplay, and the angel's pose is conventional. Still, its warmth and naturalism helped establish a type that sold well for decades."},"met:440393":{"d":"In the dark of night, a nearly nude Christ stands at the water's edge with his head bowed, a white cloth around his hips, while John the Baptist at the right raises a small cup high in one hand. John has a bare, muscular torso, a pink cloth at the waist and a green mantle falling behind. A white dove glows in the black sky above. At the left a kneeling angel in a gold-patterned robe holds out Christ's red mantle, and behind him other angels crowd together, one with his head thrown back. A band of pale light on the far horizon shows rolling hills. Warm flesh tones glow against deep brown shadow in this tall oil on canvas.","i":"The Met describes this altarpiece as deeply expressive and almost unique in showing the baptism of Christ as a nocturne. It is the last known work by Bassano, who left it unfinished at his death in 1592; his heirs kept it for more than a century. Rather than a sunny event in a pastoral landscape, the museum says, Bassano treats the baptism as the tragic opening of Christ's Passion. The Met adds that some viewers see its unfinished (non finito) style as a step toward modernism, comparable to Goya's black paintings. The picture is dated about 1590, when Bassano was around eighty.","c":"Bassano's late nocturnes, with their glowing flesh and thick shadow, reached for a gravity that most Venetian altarpieces of the time avoided. The tilt of the Baptist's raised arm and the angel's upward look are well chosen, and the unfinished passages give the scene a rough, dusky air. Still, calling the painting modern risks hindsight: the incomplete areas are the result of death, not strategy. The darkness also leaves the picture murky, and the angels at the left are hard to read. Even so, it is an affecting last statement from a very old painter."},"met:436820":{"d":"A portrait of Serena Pulitzer Lederer (1867-1943), painted in 1899 in oil on canvas, on a tall, narrow canvas about 1.9 meters high, a format suited to a full-length standing figure. Only a woman and her name are named among its subjects, so the description rests on the type: an elegant society portrait by Klimt of one of Vienna's wealthiest patrons, painted just before the glittering gold manner for which he later became famous.","i":"Serena Lederer and her husband August were among the most important patrons of Klimt and assembled one of the largest private collections of his work. The portrait dates from the early years of the Vienna Secession, which Klimt helped found in 1897, when he was moving from academic painting toward a more atmospheric and decorative style, with a tonal, hazy treatment that recalls Whistler. The sitter belonged to Vienna's wealthy industrial and cultural elite.","c":"An early society portrait by Klimt in which he is finding his way: elegance, a slender figure in a tall format and the face as the focus against a softly painted ground. It is more restrained than the glittering portraits he painted a few years later, such as Adele Bloch-Bauer I, but its refined taste and Symbolist atmosphere prefigure them. The sitter's wealth and status are shown through poise rather than ornament."},"met:435718":{"d":"A small panel, about 48 by 35 cm, painted about 1650 to 1651, showing a young woman at her toilet attended by a maid in a bedroom. A woman, a bedroom and a domestic servant are recorded, so the subject is a private moment of dressing or grooming in an upper-class interior, painted with the cool, fine finish for which Ter Borch was famous. It is a cabinet picture, made for a private buyer, not a public display.","i":"Toilet scenes were popular in Dutch art, partly as pictures of domestic order and partly as hints of vanity or of erotic suggestion, and the maid and the bedroom signal the privacy of the moment. Ter Borch, born in Zwolle in 1617, specialized in refined interiors with elegant figures, in which gestures and glances imply a story without stating it. Whether this picture carries a specific moral is not recorded here.","c":"Ter Borch's special gift is the rendering of satin and silk and an economy of gesture, with a quiet narrative in a small panel. A restrained palette and a delicate light give this type of picture its elegance, and the subtle erotic charge of the bedroom is held in check. On the evidence of his other works of the early 1650s, this would be a polished, tasteful example, though not among his best-known pictures."},"met:435716":{"d":"A small family portrait, about 41 by 36 cm, painted about 1653 to 1654, showing a man, a woman and a boy walking together. A watch is among the recorded motifs. Group portraits of this size are less common than single portraits, and the picture is a cabinet piece for the family's own use, in which Ter Borch applies his refined manner to a modest, intimate commission. The family is named for the sitters.","i":"A family portrait in a small, informal format shows the Dutch regard for domestic life and a family's standing, and the walking figures give a sense of movement. The watch, a costly object, hints at wealth and may also suggest time and its passing. The Van Moerkerken family is otherwise little known, and the sitters' ranks and relationships are not recorded here. Ter Borch settled in Deventer in 1654, where portraits of local families formed part of his trade.","c":"As a conversation piece in miniature, the picture shows Ter Borch's talent for making small figures believable with minimal means: pose, gesture and the fall of fabric. Family portraits risk stiffness, and this one, with its walking figures, avoids it. It is a charming, intimate picture, though its small scale limits individual character, and the faces convey type more than personality."},"nga:65":{"d":"A man in black with a wide white collar enters from an open door at the left and bows deeply to a woman in a coral-pink bodice and cream satin skirt, his hat in one hand and the fingers of the other forming an O. She faces him in profile, her cheeks flushed, with a small brown and white dog between them. At the right a young woman in sapphire blue plays a lute at a table covered with a patterned rug and in the shadows by a huge fireplace a man with a goatee looks over his shoulder at the couple.","i":"The title and the staging, a bow, a blush, a lute and a faithful dog, point to a courtship, a favourite theme of Dutch genre painting in the 1650s. Music was a language of love, the lute an instrument of lovers, and the dog stands for fidelity. The shadowy figure watching from the back may be a parent or rival, adding tension. Ter Borch, from Deventer, specialised in such refined scenes of well-to-do households, in which meaning is suggested rather than stated.","c":"A picture of exquisite restraint: the satin skirt is painted with shimmering touches, and the quiet theatre of glances and gestures says more than words could. The light and shadows are balanced, and the hushed mood is typical of Ter Borch. The story is deliberately ambiguous, and some viewers may find his figures remote. But for subtlety and finish few Dutch genre painters equal it."},"met:435714":{"d":"A woman in a fur-trimmed garment, a dog, a letter, a quill and an inkstand are recorded in this interior, painted about 1660 to 1662 on canvas, about 76 by 62 cm. The tools of writing and the title, Curiosity, point to a scene about a letter, with the woman's attention fixed on what is written, in a quiet room where the dog is a household presence. It is a refined cabinet picture for a private collector.","i":"Letters were a favored subject for Dutch genre painters, who used them to hint at courtship, secrecy and affection without telling the story, and Ter Borch was one of the masters of this discreet narrative. The title Curiosity was probably given later and names the mood rather than any recorded episode. The dog, in Dutch painting, can signify fidelity or domesticity, and the fur shows warmth and wealth.","c":"Ter Borch's gift for fur, satin and the presence of a silent, thoughtful woman makes such pictures work. The lack of overt action leaves the viewer to wonder, as the title suggests, and the refusal to resolve the narrative is typical of him. Compared with his best-known letter scenes, the picture may seem restrained, but it is a refined, characteristic canvas from his mature years in Deventer."},"met:435715":{"d":"A small portrait of an unnamed man seated, painted in the late 1650s or early 1660s in oil on wood, about 36 by 30.5 cm. It is a bust or half-length likeness of the kind Ter Borch made for the burghers of Deventer and Zwolle, in which sober dress and a plain ground often set off the face and hands.","i":"Ter Borch painted a great many such small portraits for the merchants and magistrates of the eastern Netherlands, and they are valued as quiet, accurate records of local society. The sitter's name, office and wealth are not recorded here, so the picture's meaning is that of a dignified, undemonstrative likeness. Such portraits were made for the family home, to be kept and passed on, not for display in public.","c":"Ter Borch's portraits of men are marked by restraint: sober black dress, a plain ground, a face observed with care and little flattery. On the evidence of his other work, the picture is likely to be a poised and exact likeness in which the delicacy of the painting does the work that rank or action does in grander portraits. It is a modest, finely made example of Dutch portraiture, and not a flamboyant one."},"met:435717":{"d":"A woman plays a theorbo-lute while a man, whom the title calls a cavalier, stands near her, and sheet music is recorded among the motifs. The panel, about 37 by 32 cm, was painted about 1658 and shows a small music-making scene in a domestic interior. The theorbo, a large bass lute with an extended neck, was a fashionable instrument of the cultivated, played to accompany singing, and the picture is a cabinet piece for a private collector.","i":"Music-making in Dutch genre painting was a standard metaphor for harmony between lovers, often with an erotic undertone, and the lute in particular was associated with love. A man attending a woman's playing suggests courtship, and the title's cavalier is an elegantly dressed gentleman, a figure of social aspiration. Ter Borch treats the moment without comment, leaving the viewer to read the attraction in posture and glance.","c":"Ter Borch is the great painter of the unspoken: a glance, a hand on a chair back, a satin skirt. In a small panel the instruments, the sheet music and the pair's posture suggest harmony and desire without a word. The refinement is the picture's attraction, and the risk is coolness, since the narrative can seem staged. It is a fine example of his mature style, polished but unforced."},"nga:178986":{"d":"A portrait of Maria van Suchtelen, about 81 by 64 centimetres, painted around 1666, the pendant to the portrait of Gerhard van Suchtelen. In his portraits of this date Ter Borch usually shows the sitter in half-length, in dark, elegant clothes set off by white linen, against a plain, softly lit ground, so that the face and hands carry the picture. Together with its pendant it would have hung in a family home as a record of both sitters.","i":"Ter Borch, who lived in Deventer from the 1650s, painted the town's patrician families in the 1660s in a restrained manner suited to the sober Dutch Republic: dark clothing, white linen and a pearly light. The sitters share the name van Suchtelen, and the matching size and date mark the portraits as pendants made to hang together. The relationship of Maria to Gerhard is not stated, but the pairing suggests they were close relatives.","c":"Ter Borch's portraits are cooler than his genre scenes and emphasise reserve: the skin is pale and smooth, and the clothes show off his skill with fabric. They lack the intensity of Rembrandt's or the vivacity of Hals's portraits, but they have an exquisite finish and a subtle dignity. A woman's portrait in this manner is quiet and refined, a pleasure for the eye more than a revelation of character."},"nga:195615":{"d":"A portrait of Gerhard van Suchtelen, about 81 by 64 centimetres, painted around 1666, the pendant to the portrait of Maria van Suchtelen. The portrait is in the half-length format with which Ter Borch recorded the regents and burghers of Deventer, usually in dark clothes with a white collar or linen, against a plain ground, so that the sitter's face and bearing are the whole subject.","i":"In the later seventeenth century a man of Gerhard's standing in a Dutch town would have his portrait made to record his place in the community and to hang alongside that of a close relative. Ter Borch, who had settled in Deventer, was the leading painter there, and his portraits gave the town's patricians an image of sober dignity. The pendant of Maria van Suchtelen shows the same restraint, and together they present a dignified pair.","c":"A restrained male portrait: the severity of dark dress relieved by white linen, the calm gaze and the firm structure of the head give it a quiet authority. The very restraint is also a limit, as the sitter can seem reserved to the point of blankness, and the painter's gift for surfaces is better displayed in his genre scenes. But as one half of a matching pair it has real elegance and presence."},"nga:41652":{"d":"A narrow vertical panel, about 87 by 24.5 cm, painted in oil entirely in black and gray, showing Christ on the cross set into the splayed opening of a splintered, barren tree in a rocky landscape. His hands are nailed to the ends of a wide crossbeam and his overlapping feet to the upright, and he wears a crown of thorns and a loincloth. A voluminous cloth lies beyond the tree, a lion and a mule stand farther back on the angular rocks, and water at right leads to a castle-like structure.","i":"A cross made from a tree links the Crucifixion to the Tree of Life, and Gossaert's barren, splintered tree makes the same point of death and rebirth. The lion and mule in the background are companions of Saint Jerome, whose legend includes a lion that guarded a donkey. The panel is the left half of a diptych whose right panel shows Jerome kneeling toward it, so the saint's sight of the crucified Christ is the point of the pair, an image for private meditation on sin and redemption.","c":"The monochrome is the point: a painter's way of showing that he can do without color and still describe, and gray figures read almost as sculpture. Gossaert, back from Italy, liked sculptural form, and the stark tree-cross and gaunt body give the image a bleak pathos. Alone it is less striking than the pair, since the saint's gaze completes the meaning, but the drawing is precise and a little hard in a way that suits the subject."},"nga:41653":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel painted entirely in white and gray, showing Saint Jerome kneeling in the lower half, holding a rock to his bare chest in a rocky landscape. He faces left in near profile and looks up, clean-shaven, with sagging cheeks and wispy curly hair, his robe puddled around his feet. A wide-brimmed hat lies before his knees and a lion beside him. Beyond a ridge of boulders pass a man on a camel and a walking man, and a church is cut into the stony hill, where Jerome appears again with the lion's paws on him.","i":"Jerome (about 347-420), who translated the Bible into Latin, withdrew to the desert to do penance, and Renaissance artists showed him striking his chest with a stone before a crucifix. His hat recalls the cardinal's hat later given him, and the lion his legend of healing a lion's paw. The camel rider and walker may recall the story in which the lion guarded a monastery's donkey and merchants' camels brought it back. The small scenes tell parts of his life in one landscape.","c":"The saint's body and face, with muscle and rounded cheeks, have a powerful, almost sculptural gravity, and the monochrome heightens the effect, while the lace-like church architecture in the distance shows Gossaert's delight in fantasy Gothic. Jerome is rendered as a worn, real man, and the lion as a near-human companion whose almost human face is slightly odd, even comic. It is a fascinating, virtuoso panel that works best beside its pendant, the crucifixion at left."},"met:436538":{"d":"A portrait of a man, about 47 by 35 cm, painted about 1520 to 1525 in oil on wood. The sitter is unnamed. In kind it is a half-length or bust likeness of the sort Gossaert painted for the court and the wealthy officials of the southern Netherlands, a small panel in which the face is the principal subject and the modeling is precise.","i":"Gossaert, born at Maubeuge and called Mabuse, worked for Philip of Burgundy at Souburg in Zeeland and later for Adolph of Burgundy at Middelburg, and his portraits record the court circle and the officials around it. Portraits of men in Netherlandish painting of this date usually present a half-length figure turning slightly, against a plain ground or a parapet, with rank signaled by dress, rings or a hat. This man's name is unknown.","c":"Gossaert's portraits are among the sharpest of the early sixteenth-century North: a cool, exact observation of the face, a firm structure of cheekbone, brow and jaw, and an interest in fine fabric. The anonymity is a loss, since the viewer would like to know who this was, but the likeness still has presence. They are less ostentatious than his Italianate mythologies, and they are better integrated, with a firmer hold on the sitter."},"met:439977":{"d":"A small devotional panel, about 25 by 19 cm, painted about 1520 to 1525 in oil on oak, showing Christ carrying the cross. Only Christ and the cross are listed, so the type is the image of Christ alone under the weight of the cross, usually a close view that confronts the viewer with his suffering. A panel this size was meant to be held or placed close at hand for private prayer.","i":"Christ carrying the cross was a central image of devotional practice, tied to the Way of the Cross and to the Passion, and viewers prayed before such images to share Christ's suffering. The close, half-length format, common in the Netherlands in the decades around 1500, offered a personal confrontation, and small panels of this kind were made for private prayer, often in sets or as pendants to a Man of Sorrows or Virgin.","c":"Gossaert was a painter of virtuosity more than of piety, and this small panel's interest probably lies in the carefully modeled head and the physical pathos of the weight, the crown of thorns and the strain of the cross. On an oak panel and at small size, it is less elaborate than his large altarpieces but offers a concentrated devotional image, and the combination of Italian-trained design and northern detail is what marks him."},"nga:50722":{"d":"Shown from about the waist up behind a forest-green desktop, a man writes in an open book with a quill as he looks at the viewer from the corners of his dark eyes. He wears a black beret with a metallic badge, a crimson jacket with puffy black-banded sleeves, and a gold ring marked IS. Around him lie the tools of an office: a sand shaker, a magnifying glass, an inkpot, scissors, coins, scales and a small book. Sheaves of papers hang at either side, labeled in Dutch for letters and drafts, and a dagger hangs between them.","i":"Portraits of clerks, bankers and tax collectors surrounded by the tools of their trade were a Netherlandish specialty in the early sixteenth century, as the commercial world grew. The man presents himself as a competent official, and his writing, scales, coins and papers announce diligence and honesty, while the dagger and fine clothes proclaim status. The initials on the ring and badge may identify him as Jan Snoeck, but the identification is tentative.","c":"This is among Gossaert's most original portraits: the sitter is defined by his work, and the objects are described with a precision that rivals the face. The crowded desk and the hanging sheaves create an almost still-life richness, and the direct glance gives the picture life. It is an unusually specific, modern-seeming image of an official at work, and its realism is its great strength."},"nga:61065":{"d":"A young woman in a lapis-blue dress sits on a low, ornately carved and gilded throne, holding a nearly naked Christ Child who straddles the arm of the chair and balances on the toes of one foot. The dress falls in lively folds around her feet, and she looks off over the viewer's left shoulder. With her right hand she holds a piece of fruit, perhaps a small apple, by the baby's mouth. The room has a patterned tile floor, thick stone walls and a window onto buildings and people at upper left, with three more apples on ledges and an open book at lower right.","i":"The apple offered to the Child recalls the fruit of the Fall: Mary, the new Eve, offers the new Adam what the first Adam took, and the picture points to redemption. The open book is Scripture, the fulfillment of which the Child embodies. The picture dates from about 1532, the last year of Gossaert's life, when his manner combined Netherlandish detail with the sculptural, Italianate forms he had learned in Rome.","c":"Even in a small panel Gossaert's sculptural delight shows: the lapis-blue dress with its eddying folds, the ornate gilded throne, the active, restless Child. The lively detail can overwhelm the devotional mood, and the room's architecture seems almost as important as the figures, which is typical of his Italianizing taste. It is a polished, ingenious late work, more admirable for ingenuity than for tenderness."},"met:436579":{"d":"A farmhouse interior, about 73 by 94 cm, painted in 1756, with men, women and children among the recorded figures and broken eggs giving the picture its title. The eggs are the clue to the story: in the moral genre scenes of the period they stand for lost innocence, so the painting shows a household reacting to a young woman's ruin, in a setting of humble kitchen things. The picture is a cabinet piece with the staging of a stage play.","i":"Greuze made a career of rural family dramas with a moral message, a reaction against the frivolity of the Rococo, and the critic Diderot championed his moral painting. In such pictures broken eggs and broken jugs symbolize lost virginity. The scenes appealed to Enlightenment ideals of sincerity and sentiment while also carrying an erotic charge. Which figure is which, and how the family responds, is told in gesture and expression.","c":"Greuze made a theater of the farmhouse, and Broken Eggs is an early sample of the formula: figures posed as in a play, gestures clear, emotion legible. The weakness is stagey sentiment and a moral too neatly drawn, which later viewers find sentimental; the strength is the inventiveness and liveliness of the rural detail. In 1756 it was novel, and for modern eyes it is a document of Enlightenment taste more than of truth."},"nga:32686":{"d":"A large portrait, about 117 by 88.5 cm, probably painted in 1759, of Ange Laurent de La Live de Jully (1725-1779), a French financier and art collector. In kind it is a society portrait of a connoisseur by a rising painter, on a canvas large enough to include the surroundings that explain the sitter's tastes, since portraits of collectors usually show that wealth went with learning.","i":"La Live de Jully was a rich financier who held a court post, a collector, an amateur engraver and one of the earliest patrons of the Greek revival, and his house and collection were well known in Paris. A portrait of a connoisseur typically shows his tastes in books, music and works of art, and the sitter, wealthy and cultivated, wanted to be remembered as an enlightened amateur.","c":"A portrait of a man defined by his taste, painted by an artist then rising in Paris: Greuze's early portraits combine a sober likeness with Rococo gentleness, and the interest is in the sitter's alert, humane presence. La Live's reputation outstrips the picture's own, and it is a competent, graceful society portrait that gains from knowing who the sitter was and what he collected."},"met:436581":{"d":"A portrait of Charles Claude de Flahaut (1730-1809), comte d'Angiviller, painted in 1763 in oil on canvas, about 64 by 54 cm, a size usually used for bust-length portraits. He was then in his early thirties, a courtier, and the picture shows the poised, intimate manner of Greuze's portraits of friends and patrons at that date, in a size that suits a likeness meant for a friend or for the sitter's own rooms.","i":"D'Angiviller later became director of the Bâtiments du Roi, the royal office in charge of art and buildings under Louis XVI, where he commissioned history paintings on patriotic themes and planned the transformation of the Louvre into a museum. In 1763 he was a rising courtier, and a portrait by a fashionable painter like Greuze would mark friendship or patronage. He is an important figure for understanding how royal patronage shaped French painting before the Revolution.","c":"Greuze's portraits of the 1760s have a warm, intimate human touch that the formal portraits of court painters lack, and the sitter is probably turned toward the viewer with directness, the paint soft and smooth. As a portrait of a man who would become a powerful arbiter of the arts it carries added interest, and as a painting it is engaging, if small in ambition."},"met:436588":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 48 by 39 cm, dated 1763, showing the head of a young boy. It is a head study of a child, tightly framed, of the kind Greuze painted in great number, and it could be a likeness, a character study or a preparation for a larger narrative picture. The painting is a close, intimate view without setting.","i":"Greuze made a specialty of heads of children and young women with emotional expression, sold as pictures in their own right or engraved for a wide public. Their appeal lay in sentiment and in the sense that the painter had caught a spontaneous feeling, and Enlightenment taste valued natural expression in childhood: Rousseau's Émile of 1762 had made childhood a subject of fascination. This head was painted a year later.","c":"These heads display Greuze's skill: soft modeling, glistening eyes and the engaging naturalness of a child, but their sweetness has often been judged cloying, and they are the kind of work from which his modern reputation has suffered. At their best, as small oil studies, they give pleasure through directness and fluency. As a record of eighteenth-century taste for sentiment, the picture is a typical piece."},"met:436580":{"d":"A large mythological canvas, about 147 by 196 cm, painted about 1767 to 1769, showing the nymph Aegina visited by Jupiter. A female nude, a bed and an eagle, Jupiter's bird, are noted, so the picture is a scene of the god's seduction, painted on a scale appropriate to history painting. Greuze, famous for rural moral scenes, here tried the grand genre that the Académie ranked highest.","i":"In the myth, Aegina, daughter of the river god Asopus, was carried off by Jupiter, who in some accounts came to her as an eagle and in others as fire; she bore him Aeacus, later a judge of the dead. Painters used such stories as a license to show a nude woman in an erotic encounter with a god. Greuze's ambition to be admitted as a history painter, when the Académie received him in 1769 only as a genre painter, may lie behind the picture.","c":"As a mythological nude it is atypical for Greuze, and it shows both his gifts and limits: the flesh is painted with a soft, warm luster, and the sensual abandon is persuasive, but the grand manner does not suit him, and the picture lacks the narrative clarity and conviction of his genre scenes. It is an interesting experiment, less a success than a revealing attempt to widen his range."},"met:436587":{"d":"A portrait of the sculptor Jean-Jacques Caffiéri (1725-1792), painted about 1765 in oil on canvas, about 64 by 53 cm. It is a bust-length likeness of a fellow artist, by a painter who was his exact contemporary, since both were born in 1725, and who portrayed associates in the Parisian art world. In 1765 the sculptor was about forty and already established in Paris, a colleague whose likeness a fellow artist might paint as a mark of friendship.","i":"Caffiéri was among the leading portrait sculptors of his day, known for busts of famous figures such as Rameau, Corneille and Franklin, and his realistic, expressive busts of the great men of France made him a pillar of the taste for historical portraiture. A portrait of an artist by an artist suggests professional fellowship, and it shows Greuze's circle among Parisian artists in the 1760s.","c":"Greuze's portraits of men in these years have a quiet candor, and the sitter's alert eyes and relaxed pose, if they follow his usual manner, make the picture persuasive without display. The portrait is valuable as a document of two reputations, a painter and a sculptor looking at each other across a studio, and its modest size keeps attention on the likeness. It is a fine, unshowy piece."},"met:436586":{"d":"A portrait of Princess Varvara Nikolaevna Gagarina (1762-1802), painted about 1780 to 1782 in oil on canvas, about 80 by 64 cm. A princess, a woman and a dog are named among its subjects, so the young sitter, about twenty, is shown with her dog, a companion animal of the kind that appears often in portraits of fashionable ladies, in the soft, sweet manner of Greuze's late society portraits.","i":"A Russian princess portrayed by a leading French painter shows the cosmopolitan culture of the late eighteenth-century Russian aristocracy, which looked to Paris for its taste, its fashion and its artists. Foreign visitors sought out painters like Greuze, and Catherine the Great's court bought French art on a large scale. The dog is a sign of intimacy and fidelity. Little is recorded here of her life beyond her dates and rank.","c":"Greuze could make a young woman look gentle, candid and a little shy, and the type of portrait suits his skill at soft flesh and tender expression, with the dog adding warmth and a note of domestic charm. For a court portrait it is relaxed and informal, which is both its charm and its limit. By the early 1780s Greuze's reputation was past its peak, and the picture should be seen as an agreeable, skilled work of his later career."},"met:436583":{"d":"A small study of a woman's head in profile, painted about 1780 in oil on wood, about 47 by 41 cm. Only a woman and a profile are recorded, so the picture is a head study of the kind Greuze made in quantity: a modest piece that may be a study for a figure in a larger composition or a picture made for sale as an independent work, without setting or anecdote.","i":"Greuze made countless head studies of young women, often showing expression of grief, innocence or abandonment. The profile view is classical, associated with antique coins and cameos, and it reflects the neoclassical taste of the 1780s for pure outline. The sitter is unidentified, and she may be a model rather than a portrait subject, so the picture's interest is less in a person than in a type of beauty.","c":"A profile removes the eye contact that normally gives Greuze's women their sentimental appeal, and the result is cooler and more classical, with the silken modeling of cheek and neck as the pleasure. As a study on wood it is small, intimate and unshowy, and a good example of the late Greuze, who had to compete with David's severity. It is a minor but attractive piece, if a little detached."},"met:436582":{"d":"A small canvas, about 41 by 32 cm, showing the head of a young woman, dated only as possibly the 1780s. It is a tightly framed head, perhaps a likeness and perhaps one of the character heads Greuze painted for sale, with no setting or story, in which expression and soft modeling are the whole point. Such heads were made to be hung in a private room and enjoyed for their sentiment.","i":"By the 1780s Greuze's heads of young women were a staple of his output and a favorite of collectors, prized for their sensibility, the cultivated feeling that Enlightenment taste admired in faces. Diderot had praised the heads earlier, and engravers spread them to a wide public. Whether this woman is a particular sitter or an invented type, and whether the date is right, cannot be settled from the record.","c":"The head is a formula Greuze repeated with skill, and the repetition is the problem: soft flesh, bright eyes and a faint upward gaze combine into a sweetness that can seem automatic. At the level of paint it should be well made, and the intimacy of the small scale helps. As evidence of a taste that David's severity was about to replace, it is more interesting than as an individual picture."},"met:436584":{"d":"A portrait of Madame Jean-Baptiste Nicolet, born Anne Antoinette Desmoulins (1743-1817), painted in the late 1780s in oil on wood, about 64 by 53 cm. She was in her mid-forties, the wife of a successful Paris theater manager, and the panel, a size suited to a bust-length likeness, records a prosperous, non-aristocratic sitter at the end of the Old Regime.","i":"Her husband ran a popular theater on the boulevard du Temple, offering acrobats, dancers and trained animals to the Paris public, and the commission shows the widening circle of patrons in the years before the Revolution: wealthy tradespeople and entertainers as well as nobles. The portrait records bourgeois prosperity, and the sitter's own character, as an independent woman of her class, is what the painter was asked to convey.","c":"Greuze's late portraits, made after the decline of his fortunes, are often among his plainest, with a direct, unflattering honesty that suits a sitter who had no need to assume a role. Painted on wood in a late-1780s manner, the likeness should have the soft modeling and open expression typical of him. It is a modest pleasure, valuable as a human document of a Paris family on the eve of change."},"nga:74990":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper mounted on canvas, about 31 by 39 centimeters. In the lower left, a painter in a floppy straw-yellow hat and teal smock sits with his back to the viewer on a three-legged stool, a canvas near his knees and a tall parchment-white umbrella pitched beside him. He faces a craggy, moss-green mountain across a valley of pale sage meadows, with tan buildings by an ice-blue body of water. The sky shifts from lemon yellow to peach to blue. Signed and dated Botzen, 1837.","i":"Bozen is the German name for Bolzano, in the Tyrol, then part of the Austrian Empire and a staging post for artists traveling to Italy. By painting a painter at work, Coignet pictures the practice itself: by 1837 the open-air study, with a stool and a sun umbrella, was a settled habit of landscape artists. The small figure also gives the mountain its scale. The sketch was probably made on the spot.","c":"An engaging small work in which the act of looking becomes the subject. The color is soft and well controlled, with hazy mauve and gray mountains against a graded sky and the figure's bright hat and smock as the one sharp accent. It is slight, as sketches are, and Coignet is a minor figure beside Corot, whose Italian studies it recalls, but brisk outdoor work like this often has more life than a finished studio picture."},"nga:74991":{"d":"A sheer cliff rises across a deep valley, backlit by a low sunset. A tall tree in the near ground grows above the canopies of trees filling the valley, and brown buildings cluster at the cliff edge, outlined against the sky. Light falls from the left and throws the right face of the cliff into shadow. Rocky hills at right enclose a body of still water, and the horizon is streaked with pale rust red, lavender and slate blue. Oil on paper on canvas, about 27 by 36 centimeters, signed Nemi 1843.","i":"Lake Nemi, in the Alban Hills southeast of Rome, fills an old volcanic crater, and the town of Nemi stands on its rim. In antiquity the lake was sacred to Diana, whose grove and sanctuary stood on its shore, and the site drew northern artists and travelers to Italy. A sunset study like this was made to catch the fleeting color of one hour, and could also serve later as material for finished studio pictures.","c":"The study succeeds through effect rather than detail: a cliff in shadow, a sky streaked with rust, lavender and slate, a still lake, all set down in a few strokes. It is fast and unfussy, and its small scale keeps it from pretension. Seen beside the Bozen sketch, it confirms Coignet as a sensitive observer of light, if a modest one."},"met:438634":{"d":"A panoramic oil sketch on paper laid down on canvas, about 33 by 52 cm, dated 1844, showing the city of Beirut with its buildings and the hills behind. Buildings, a city and hills are recorded. Beirut was then a modest Ottoman port on the eastern Mediterranean, and the sketch records its appearance from outside, probably painted from nature to capture the light, as French landscape painters did in the Near East.","i":"French painters and travelers visited the Levant in growing numbers in the 1830s and 1840s, after the French expedition to Egypt and the conquest of Algeria had drawn attention to the Orient. The oil sketch on paper was a traveler's tool: quick, portable, kept as raw material for studio paintings and as a visual diary. Orientalist pictures of the 1840s generally present the East as a picturesque spectacle seen by Western visitors.","c":"The sketch's virtue is its directness: the town spread out in the light below the hills, set down without the sentimental staging of studio Orientalism. Coignet was trained in the French neoclassical landscape tradition, and the clear structure shows it. Such sketches often have more freshness than the finished pictures they led to. It is a modest but valuable early view of Beirut, and a record more than a design."},"nga:41616":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 125 by 51 centimeters, in tempera on poplar, dated 1470, showing the Madonna and Child enthroned with a donor, the patron who paid for the work and was usually shown kneeling in her presence. The painter, Carlo Crivelli, worked in the Marches of Italy and is famed for lavishly decorated panels with raised gilding, rich fabrics and hard, wire-like outlines. The upright shape suggests part of a larger altarpiece.","i":"A donor portrait inserted a patron into the sacred scene, so that his devotion would be remembered and, it was hoped, rewarded; the donor is usually smaller than the holy figures to show humility. A Madonna and Child enthroned represented the Virgin as Queen of Heaven, an image that suited a church altar. Crivelli worked for patrons in the Adriatic towns of the Marches, where such altarpieces were prized. The donor's name is unknown.","c":"Crivelli's art is unlike anyone else's in the 1470s: ornament piled on ornament, figures drawn with a taut, brittle line, faces intense and slightly strange. A panel of this date should show his trademark nervous elegance. He is one of the great decorators of the Renaissance, and a donor panel by him is a good document of how devotion was dressed in gold."},"met:436051":{"d":"Mary sits enthroned with the Christ Child on a tall, narrow panel, about 98 by 44 centimeters, dated 1472, painted in tempera on a gold ground. The museum's keywords give only the subject, so the throne, the poses, the colors and any garlands or fruit are left unstated. The date, height and technique match Crivelli's panels of Saint Dominic and Saint George in the same museum, and the three probably belonged to one altarpiece.","i":"Crivelli, a Venetian by training who spent his career in the Marches on Italy's Adriatic side, signed his works with proud mention of Venice. The enthroned Madonna presents Mary as Queen of Heaven with her son as King, and the central tier of a polyptych would have been the focus of prayer on an altar. Crivelli's Madonnas are often richly ornamented, with fruit garlands and gilded relief; whether this one was is not stated.","c":"Crivelli stands outside the mainstream of Renaissance painting: instead of the soft modeling and spacious perspective of Florence or Venice, he offered hard-edged drawing, brilliant color and a goldsmith's love of ornament. His Madonnas can seem severe and even slightly strange, and that strangeness is the point. The panel is typical in being intense, refined and a little unyielding; admirers find it unforgettable, others find it brittle."},"met:436054":{"d":"Saint Dominic stands on a tall, narrow panel, about 97 by 32 centimeters, dated 1472, painted in tempera on a gold ground. The museum's keywords record only men and saints, so his pose, habit, attributes and face are left unstated. Dominic, founder of the Dominican Order, is usually shown in the black-and-white habit of his friars with a book and a lily, and sometimes a star on his forehead, though which of these appear here is not recorded.","i":"Dominic (about 1170 to 1221), a Spanish priest, founded the Order of Preachers to combat heresy by sermon and study. His lily stands for purity, his book for preaching, and his dog with a torch recalls a legend that his mother dreamed she bore a dog that would set the world ablaze. A saint on a tall side panel like this would have flanked the Madonna in an altarpiece, probably in a Dominican church.","c":"As in all Crivelli's single saints, the figure is a sharp, silhouetted presence: the long face, strong outline and ornamental detail give Dominic a metallic intensity far from the genial humanism of Florentine saints. Whether the panel shows his best qualities depends on condition and detail not recorded here, but the type is unmistakable: stern, elegant and wonderfully crisp."},"met:438691":{"d":"A young knight stands in body armor with a spear in a pose of contrapposto, his weight on one leg and a hand on his hip, long blond hair falling to his waist, on a tall, narrow panel about 97 by 34 centimeters, dated 1472, in tempera on a gold ground. The museum's keywords add a dragon, so the monster's position, the colors of the armor and the background are left unstated. The panel has been transferred to Masonite, a conservation measure for damaged wood.","i":"Saint George, a soldier-martyr of the early fourth century, is the patron of knights, and in the Golden Legend he saves a princess by slaying a dragon that had been devouring a city's children. Painters showed him as the ideal Christian knight, brave, young and elegantly armed, and an altarpiece saint like this one would have stood beside the Madonna, offering the viewer protection. The pose, with a hand on the hip, gives him the swagger of a courtier.","c":"Crivelli's Saint George is a dandy in armor: the long hair, the jaunty hip and the glittering equipment are painted with a goldsmith's relish. The figure is vivid and slightly eccentric rather than heroic, and the sharp outlines and decorative energy are quintessential Crivelli. Condition is a concern, since the panel has been transferred, and some of the original surface subtlety is probably lost, but the personality survives."},"met:436053":{"d":"The dead Christ is mourned by the Virgin Mary and Mary Magdalene in this nearly square panel, about 72 by 65 centimeters, dated 1476, painted in tempera on a gold ground. The museum's keywords record sadness, women, Christ, Mary Magdalene and the Virgin, so the pose of the body, any other mourners, the setting and the colors are left unstated. It is a Pietà, an image of grief rather than a scene in a story, made for contemplation of Christ's suffering.","i":"The Pietà, from the Italian for pity, isolates the moment after the Crucifixion, when the body is held by those who loved him. It was a favorite late medieval devotional image because it let viewers feel the Passion as personal loss. Crivelli's Pietàs are among the most intense of the fifteenth century, with the stylized, almost ornamental treatment of grief that was his own contribution. The panel may once have crowned an altarpiece.","c":"The wincing grief of Crivelli's mourners, with their angular faces and elongated hands, can seem too stylized to a modern eye, but the effect is deliberate: grief is turned into pattern, and the gold ground lifts the scene out of time. A tight, nearly square composition packs the heads together like a hammered relief. It has an unsettling intensity that softer Italian Pietàs do not attempt."},"met:436052":{"d":"A small devotional panel, about 38 by 25 centimeters, shows the Virgin and Child, painted about 1480 in tempera and gold on wood. The museum's keywords record an apple among the subjects, so who holds it, the pose, the background and the colors are left unstated. Panels of this size were made for private prayer at home rather than for a church altar, and Crivelli enriched them with gold and fine ornament.","i":"The apple is the fruit of the Fall, and when it is placed with the Christ Child it presents him as the new Adam who undoes the damage done by the first. Crivelli, who liked fruit and garlands, uses the apple with the dryness of a theological point rather than the sweetness of a still life. Such small Madonnas were kept by owners who wanted a personal image of the Virgin as a protector.","c":"The small format favors Crivelli's gift: every detail can be made precise and every contour crisp, and the gold and the apple give the panel an enamel-like preciousness. The Madonna's face may be more severe than sweet, but that severity is part of his appeal, and the picture feels like a jewel. It is a less ambitious work than his altarpieces, yet one that shows the same intensity in miniature."},"nga:407":{"d":"A small devotional panel, about 33 by 25 centimeters, in tempera, showing the Madonna and Child, painted about 1490 by Carlo Crivelli. Crivelli, a Venetian by training, spent most of his career in the towns of the Marches. The size is that of a picture for a private room or chapel, not a church altar, and Crivelli's small Madonnas are often half-length figures, richly dressed and set against a gold or ornamented ground.","i":"A small Madonna and Child was the commonest kind of private devotional image in Renaissance Italy, set up at home as a focus for prayer. Crivelli's late Madonnas often include symbolic details such as fruit, a fly or a garland, which carry ideas of the Passion and of life's transience, though it is not certain that this panel has them. The mother's love for the Child, and the Child as Savior, are the unifying themes.","c":"Crivelli's late Madonnas have a hard, jewel-like brilliance that makes them both strange and compelling. At this size and date the panel is probably a concentrated example of his skills: contour drawn like wire, ornament everywhere, faces intense and unsentimental. The consistency of his late manner makes the general verdict a safe one."},"met:436837":{"d":"A decorative allegory of Winter, about 100 by 106 cm, dated 1761. A nude woman, putti, a fountain, a tree, ice and a sun are listed, so Winter is personified by a woman among cherubs in a garden where ice and a low sun mark the season. The picture is a decorative canvas, probably made as part of a set of Seasons for the paneling of a room, with the gentle charm and lightness that belonged to the Rococo.","i":"Allegories of the Four Seasons were a standard decorative theme in the eighteenth century, painted as sets for salons and dining rooms, and Winter is typically shown as a figure warming herself or among ice, with putti adding a playful touch. La Joue, a painter and designer of Rococo ornament, worked for a Paris clientele, and he died in 1761, so this picture dates from the end of his life, when neoclassical taste was already beginning to challenge such themes.","c":"A playful, decorative picture: the nude woman and putti bring charm to the cold season, and the Rococo's lightness and fondness for allegory are on display. The pale palette should suit Winter. The picture is an elegant exercise rather than a deeply considered work, and as a late Rococo piece it shows a taste on the point of going out of fashion. It is best seen as part of a room, not as a free-standing work."},"met:438640":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper, about 22 by 33 cm, painted between about 1786 and 1797, of mountainous country at Vicovaro, a hill town on the Aniene east of Rome. Mountains and landscape are the recorded subjects, so the sketch records the shapes of the hills in the clear light of the Roman countryside. Paper was the portable support of the outdoor painter: light, cheap and quick to use, and often kept as a record rather than a finished work.","i":"Simon Denis was born in Antwerp in 1755 and settled in Rome in 1786, when the hills around the city, from Tivoli to Vicovaro, were the favorite sketching ground of foreign landscape painters. Painting oil sketches outdoors, directly from the motif, was becoming an accepted practice in Rome in the later eighteenth century, and sketches like this were kept as reference for studio pictures. The dates given cover his first years in Rome.","c":"The sketch's value is its freshness: a few broad shapes record hills and light without the idealizing composition of a finished landscape. Judged as a picture it is slight, but judged as observation it is direct, and such sketches are now often admired for their freshness more than many finished landscapes of the time. Like other oil sketches of its type, it is a precursor of nineteenth-century outdoor painting."},"met:438641":{"d":"A narrow oil sketch on paper, about 18 by 32 cm, painted between about 1786 and 1797, of hills at Tivoli, the ancient hill town east of Rome. Hills and landscape are noted, so the sketch is a wide, low strip of country rather than a composed picture, in which the format itself suggests a panorama. Tivoli, with its temples, villas and waterfalls, was the most celebrated picturesque site near Rome.","i":"Tivoli drew artists from Claude Lorrain to the Grand Tourists of the eighteenth century, and every landscape painter in Rome made studies there; the town stood for the union of nature and antiquity. Denis's oil sketches belong to the practice of painting directly outdoors, a habit that grew in Rome among foreign landscapists in the 1780s and 1790s, and the studies were kept as visual notes rather than made for the market.","c":"A long, shallow format forces the painter to think in horizontals: the line of the hills and the bands of sky. That economy gives the little panel an air of breadth. It is an informal record rather than a statement, and its charm is in its lack of fuss. Beside the sketch of Vicovaro it shows the same direct habit, and the two together show how consistently Denis looked at the hills."},"met:438644":{"d":"An oil sketch on paper, laid down on canvas, about 30 by 41 cm, dated 1800, showing a view on the Quirinal Hill in Rome. Trees and the city are recorded. The Quirinal is the highest of Rome's seven hills, long a place of gardens and palaces, and the sketch records the trees and the city's roofs in the light, an outdoor study made from a green vantage point rather than a finished view.","i":"By 1800 Denis had lived in Rome for some fourteen years, and the Quirinal, with its papal palace and gardens, was a favorite vantage point for views of the city. Dated studies like this are valuable because they fix a time and a place in the artist's career, unlike the many undated ones. Views of Rome itself, not only of its countryside, formed part of the standard repertoire for foreign painters.","c":"A dated, placed sketch of the city shows Denis moving from rural hills to the mix of building and foliage found in Rome, a challenge that tests how well a landscape painter can handle architecture. Oil on paper allows quick notation of light, and the interest is mostly documentary, since the picture does not pretend to be more. It is a valuable witness of the Rome that foreign artists saw around 1800."},"met:438642":{"d":"A small cloud study in oil on paper, about 23 by 26 cm, painted between about 1786 and 1806, titled Early Evening. Only sky and clouds are named among its subjects, so the sketch is a study of evening light and the shapes of clouds, painted from nature in the hours when the light softens. Beside Distant Storm, it shows the painter recording the sky at different times of day.","i":"The time of day in the title matters: painters of skies noted the hour as part of the record, because the color and shape of clouds depend on it. Early evening light, warmer and softer, was a favorite effect of the landscape tradition since Claude. Denis's study belongs to the practice of keeping a stock of skies, so that a suitable one could be added to a studio landscape later.","c":"Such a study lives or dies by tone: the passage from light to shadow within a cloud and the warmth near the horizon. It is modest and unshowy, and its quietness is its virtue. Set beside the Distant Storm it is the less dramatic and perhaps the more subtle of the pair, and a good example of the outdoor habit that Denis helped pass to younger painters in Naples."},"met:438643":{"d":"A small cloud study in oil on paper, about 19 by 25 cm, painted between about 1786 and 1806, titled Distant Storm. Sky and clouds are the recorded subjects, with no land or figures, so the sketch is a pure study of the heavens: a storm seen at a distance. Studies of this kind were painted quickly to catch an effect that would change within minutes, and they were kept in the studio as reference.","i":"Cloud studies were a new kind of picture in the late eighteenth century, when artists in Rome and elsewhere, among them Valenciennes and Thomas Jones, and later Constable, began painting skies directly from nature as exercises in observation. The wide skies of the Roman Campagna suited them. A storm seen at a distance combined drama with a safe vantage. The studies were for the artist's own reference, not for exhibition.","c":"The cloud study is a test of speed: to catch a passing storm the painter must reduce it to a few masses of tone before it changes. Denis's sketch, as a distant storm, is more dramatic than a calm sky, and as one of several studies in the same group it shows his sensitivity to weather. It is slight but alive, a historic instance of sky painting before the sky became a subject in itself."},"met:438645":{"d":"An oil sketch on paper laid down on wood, about 33 by 40 cm, painted between about 1786 and 1806, of a fortified wall at Tivoli with mountains beyond. Mountains and landscape are recorded and the title gives the wall, so the sketch records a piece of old masonry set against the hills: the town's ancient and medieval defenses, a picturesque feature of its hilltop site. It is larger than the other sketches in this group.","i":"Walls, ruins and towers were prized by landscape painters of the Grand Tour for their picturesque combination of human labor and time, and at Tivoli stonework of Roman and medieval date rose from the cliffs. For a painter, masonry offered shapes and textures to set against the softer forms of hills and foliage. Denis's wall is a study in the weight and color of old stone in the Italian light.","c":"Of the group, this is the sketch that tests drawing as well as tone: a wall has to be built, plane by plane, and set convincingly against the distance. The mounting on wood suggests that it was a keeper, and it is probably among the more resolved of the set. It is a solid, unromantic record of a place, with the attraction of honest observation and a firm sense of structure."},"nga:103611":{"d":"Soft green hills, carpeted with plants, sweep away across the bottom third of this horizontal painting on paper. A few terracotta-red roofs and tan walls nestle into the trees along the lower edge, and more cream-white buildings are tucked among trees in the distance before grassy hills rise along the horizon. A few clouds drift across a hazy, pale blue sky. The edges of the paper are visible, especially at top and bottom, which suggests an open-air study made quickly on a sheet of paper.","i":"Oil sketches on paper made outdoors were a habit of landscape painters working in Italy around 1800, who valued the quick record of light and atmosphere as a private exercise, apart from the composed pictures they sent to exhibitions. Denis, a Flemish-born painter who spent most of his working life in Italy, made many such studies of the Roman and Neapolitan countryside. The title names only the area, and the subject is the light on the hills rather than any particular site.","c":"A fresh, unfussy sketch. The shapes are reduced to a few planes of green, a handful of red roofs and a pale hazy sky, and the economy gives an unforced sense of open air. Compared with the Italian studies of Valenciennes or Corot it has less poetry and less structure, being more a record than a design. Its interest is documentary and tactile: a glimpse of how Neoclassical landscapists trained their eyes."},"met:437074":{"d":"Horsemen clash in open country on a small wood panel only 22 by 32 centimeters. Men on horseback in a landscape make this probably a skirmish in the field rather than a formal battle on a grand scale. Van der Meulen made his name painting Louis XIV's campaigns, and a panel like this belongs to the cavalry encounters, a kind of subject he painted again and again.","i":"Born in Brussels, Van der Meulen was called to Paris in 1664 to work for Louis XIV. He accompanied the king's armies on campaign to sketch sieges and marches from life, and Le Brun's Gobelins workshop turned his designs into tapestries and large canvases glorifying the reign. A small cavalry skirmish belongs to that vocabulary; it need not record a named battle, and was probably prized as a lively cabinet piece.","c":"Van der Meulen's strength was a clear, legible landscape with soldiers placed in it, and at small scale he is quick and lively. A cavalry engagement like this is a modest piece in his output, without the grand reportage of his large campaign views, but it shows the sure touch that made him a favorite of the court. Its interest lies in the pattern of the encounter more than in any single face."},"met:436057":{"d":"A wide landscape, about 56 by 89 cm, probably painted about 1810, of Hautbois Common near Norwich in Norfolk. Trees, clouds and open land are the recorded subjects, so the picture shows a stretch of common ground under a large sky, a plain English scene in which the clouds and the trees are the principal features. The type is a studio picture based on drawings made on the spot.","i":"Crome was the founder of the Norwich School, a group of provincial landscape painters devoted to the plain scenery of Norfolk, and he admired the Dutch landscapists of the seventeenth century, especially Hobbema, whose sense of open land and sky he brought to English subjects. A common was a stretch of unenclosed land used by villagers, so the subject is not picturesque ruin or grand scenery but ordinary ground, which gives the picture its modern note.","c":"Crome's best landscapes have a breadth and honesty that make his Norfolk seem more convincing than the Dutch models he emulated: large sky, light moving across the ground, trees massed with weight. The picture is not a dramatic scene, but its quietness and truthfulness to the flat Norfolk landscape give it a quiet authority. The influence of Hobbema is likely, but the weather is English."},"nga:61389":{"d":"Two windmills stand backlit by silvery moonlight along a low horizon, their sails making Xs against pearly clouds drifting across a muted blue sky. A towering tree with muted olive leaves and a tan trunk fills nearly the left half of the picture, its dark branches dipping toward a river that runs from the bottom center into the distance, shimmering where the light touches it. A hollowed, gnarled, broken trunk twists against the sky at the right bank, among grasses. The brushwork is loose. Oil on canvas, about 98 by 126 centimeters, about 1816 to 1817.","i":"The Yare is the river of Norwich and Great Yarmouth in Norfolk, the home country of John Crome and the Norwich School of landscape painters, who drew on Dutch seventeenth-century painting. Windmills, river and old trees are the stock of the flat Norfolk scene, and a moonlit version recalls Dutch night pieces. The picture is a late work by Crome, who died in 1821, and treats the English countryside as a quiet, local, atmospheric subject.","c":"A tender, ambitious late picture: the vast tree and the shimmering river create a hushed drama, and the moonlight is conveyed with restraint. Crome's debt to Hobbema and the Dutch masters is evident, but the sensitivity to local atmosphere is his own. The handling is loose and the details summary, but it is among the finest English moonlights of its period, and one of the pictures that make Crome the leading figure of the Norwich School."},"nga:130894":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper, laid down on canvas, about 27 by 33 cm, of a village on the island of Ischia in the Bay of Naples, painted about 1826. Tall green mountains with gray, rocky peaks fill the background under a strip of light blue sky. At their base, small square buildings in white, gray and faded terracotta sit on little hills among light brown and olive-green plants.","i":"Ischia, a volcanic island at the northern edge of the Bay of Naples, offered artists strong light, dramatic mountain forms and plain vernacular building. Painting oil sketches on paper outdoors was an established routine for French painters in Italy in the 1820s, the years when Corot too was working in Italy, and such studies were kept as a private record of places and light. No figures are recorded.","c":"The sketch's pleasure is in the simplicity of mass: the square white houses against green slopes and gray peaks, and the narrow strip of sky. The small, sketchlike scale records light on stone and vegetation, and the geometric houses recall the interest in simple forms that marked Corot's Italian studies. Norblin is less famous than Corot, but the freshness of observation is shared, and as a record of the practice it is a good example."},"nga:56291":{"d":"A small cabinet picture, oil on panel and only about 24 by 20 cm, showing a tavern interior of the kind Adriaen van Ostade painted for most of his career. The museum offers no description of this panel, so it is characterised by type rather than detail: a low, dim room where villagers drink, smoke and talk, worked in the warm browns and delicate touch that mark his pictures of the 1660s. Panels of this size were made to be held close and studied, and were bought by townspeople who enjoyed looking at rustic life.","i":"Dutch tavern scenes were never simple records. Drinking, smoking and card-playing carried familiar warnings against idleness and excess, yet the pictures were also enjoyed as comedy and cosy observation, and prosperous townsmen collected them eagerly. Ostade's early peasants are coarse and grotesque; by the 1660s he had softened them into sociable, almost affectionate types. Without a described scene the exact incident cannot be read, but the setting places the picture in that double tradition of amusement and gentle moralising.","c":"Ostade is the yardstick for small Dutch peasant interiors, and he earned the place through economy: a few figures, a pool of light and a glaze of browns that makes a cramped room feel lived in. The risk is formula, and his 1660s work can drift toward prettiness beside the raw energy of his youth or of Brouwer. Judged by type, this is a characteristic, capable example rather than a surprise."},"nga:1187":{"d":"A view of the yard in front of a cottage, painted in oil on canvas, about 44 by 40 cm, and dated 1673, when Ostade was in his sixties. The museum gives no description, so the picture is characterised by type: an open-air scene of peasant life, with a humble house, its dooryard and the people and belongings that gather there. Ostade painted both smoky interiors and exteriors before cottages and inns, and he worked more often on panel than on canvas, so the support is a small point of interest.","i":"Dooryard scenes show a peasant household at its ordinary business: work, children, animals and the clutter around a poor home. For Dutch town buyers such pictures offered a comfortable, humorous view of country life, known mostly from a distance. Some viewers probably saw gentle moralising in the idleness or poverty on show, others simple charm. Ostade's later work leans toward charm, with peasants who are poor but seldom menacing.","c":"By 1673 Ostade had painted such subjects for four decades, and the late manner is smooth, harmonious and a little sweet. His strengths, a sure sense of arrangement and a convincing feel for texture, are what one expects from him. What fades is the bite of his youth. Taken by type, the picture is a gently pleasing late work, more valuable for its craft than for any surprise."},"met:11160":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, nearly two metres high, shows the abolitionist John Brown being led out to his execution. Soldiers with rifles and bayonets surround him, and a woman holds out an infant toward him; the museum's keywords record the man, the soldiers, the weapons, the woman, the baby and the execution. Hovenden composes the moment as a tableau, with the condemned man the focus of an armed crowd. The scale and finish mark it as a history picture made for exhibition, painted in oil in the early 1880s.","i":"Brown was hanged in December 1859 after his raid on Harpers Ferry, and a story spread that on his way to the gallows he kissed a Black child held out to him. Historians generally doubt it happened. Hovenden paints it as an act of grace that makes the abolitionist a martyr, and in the 1880s, as the gains of Reconstruction faded, the image kept the cause in view. It reads as sentiment, not reportage.","c":"Hovenden was a capable academic painter, and the picture is clearly planned for one readable drama: the condemned man, the armed guard, the pause for the child. That clarity is its strength and its limit. The emotion is arranged rather than discovered, and the central gesture is legend, not record. It belongs with the period's sentimental history painting, effective as a public image of martyrdom and less convincing as observation."},"met:11159":{"d":"A woman and a man appear together in a quiet, reflective scene, painted in oil on a horizontal canvas about 76 by 102 cm. The Met's keywords record only the two figures and the mood of contemplation, so the picture is characterised by type: a sentimental figure picture of the kind Hovenden made in his last years, in which feeling and a modest setting matter more than action. The title echoes a well-known hymn about the heavenly city.","i":"Jerusalem the Golden is a nineteenth-century English hymn, John Mason Neale's translation of a twelfth-century Latin poem, that imagines the heavenly city with longing. The title suggests that the figures are meant to be seen in devout reverie, probably absorbed in thoughts of reward after hard living. Such pictures spoke to American Victorian viewers, who prized domestic piety and sentimental feeling and bought pictures that offered both.","c":"Hovenden is admired for plain, humane attention to ordinary people, and at his best he keeps sentiment in check by observing character rather than staging melodrama. The risk is that the pathos arrives too readily, and a title borrowed from a hymn invites that. As one of his last pictures it is affecting rather than ambitious, minor beside his larger history canvases but typical of his direct, kindly approach."},"met:437631":{"d":"A boat floats on the Volga at night in this small oil on wood panel, about 33 by 55 centimeters, dated 1871. A river, a boat and the night are the whole subject, and the great width of the Volga gives the picture its quiet. Savrasov painted the river repeatedly around this date, and the panel is a lyrical view of the water rather than a scene of labor or travel.","i":"The Volga is Russia's great river, a symbol of the nation in song and painting; Repin's Barge Haulers on the Volga, painted in the early 1870s, made it a stage for social comment. Savrasov's view is not comment but lyric: he founded the Russian landscape of mood, taught Isaac Levitan, and turned from foreign grandeur to the humble Russian countryside. He was also a founder of the Wanderers, the group that sent exhibitions around Russia.","c":"Savrasov is the poet of the plain Russian scene, and a night on the river gives him the chance to make atmosphere the subject. On a small panel the hush of dark water and sky is the whole drama. The painting is modest and without the emotional clarity of his famous The Rooks Have Returned, but it shows the same tender attention. It points toward the landscapes of Levitan, who learned from him."},"met:436419":{"d":"A man on horseback carries a falcon in this tall canvas of about 109 by 72 centimeters, dated 1864. The title makes him an Arab falconer, and the picture belongs to the Orientalist painting of North Africa. Eugène Fromentin, who had traveled in Algeria, made the elegant horseman and his bird the whole subject, giving the scene the dignity of a portrait of the hunt.","i":"Falconry was an aristocratic pursuit in North Africa as it was in Europe, and Fromentin recorded it in his travel books and paintings. His Algeria was a world of noble horsemen and unchanging customs, an image that suited French taste in the decades after the conquest of Algeria. Such pictures present a refined, timeless East, and they leave out much of the colonial reality behind it.","c":"Fromentin is subtler than most Orientalists: his silvery light, elegant drawing of horses and sober color lift him above the routine of exotic costume. His weakness is a certain polished narrowness, more elegance than feeling. Compared with Delacroix's fervor, his Algeria is calm and tidy, and this picture shows both the charm and the limits of that approach."},"met:436420":{"d":"Riders cross a river ford beneath hills in this oil on wood panel of about 51 by 62 centimeters, dated 1873. The title makes the men Arabs, and the picture belongs to Eugène Fromentin's Orientalist scenes of North Africa. The horses and river are the central features, and the group moves through the water as a small procession, with the landscape providing space and light.","i":"Fromentin traveled in Algeria in the 1840s and 1850s and drew on his sketches for years afterward, painting scenes of travel, hunting and caravans in his studio in France. A river crossing is a motif of journey and transition, and in his hands it carries the poetry of an unchanging pastoral world. Like all Orientalist art, it presents a European's view of the East, shaped by taste and colonial context.","c":"Fromentin's handling of light on water and of horses in motion is accomplished, and the format of the panel suits the leisurely procession. The picture is carefully composed and a little cool, with more polish than passion. It is easy to admire for its refinement, and equally easy to see that it is an elegant fiction rather than a record."},"nga:195134":{"d":"An encampment is shown in the open air in this small oil on wood, about 32 by 41 cm, dated 1873. The title names an Arab encampment, and Fromentin, a French painter and writer, knew the subject from his travels in Algeria in the 1840s and 1850s. The panel records an exterior scene of tents and figures in a landscape, painted late in his career, three years before his death.","i":"Orientalism, the French taste for North African and Near Eastern scenes, grew with the colonial conquest of Algeria, which began in 1830. Fromentin, who also wrote vivid books about his travels, aimed at observation more than flamboyance, but his pictures still present the region as timeless and picturesque, through the eyes of a colonial visitor. An encampment, with its tents, animals and nomadic life, was an image of a way of life that French viewers found exotic.","c":"Fromentin's strength is subtle light and restrained color, with a refined sense of space and graceful figures, and he stands between Delacroix's passion and Gérôme's exactness. The weakness of his Orientalism is its sameness and its timeless fiction, and his late work can be dry. This small panel is a modest but refined example, where the atmosphere matters more than the incident, and the quietness is its virtue."},"met:10235":{"d":"A wide coastal view, about 85 by 137 cm, looks across the water to Amalfi on the Bay of Salerno. Buildings stand at the foot of steep cliffs, and a sailboat and a bark move over the bay beneath a broad sky with clouds. The horizontal format gives sky and sea as much weight as the town. It is a finished exhibition landscape rather than a sketch, painted in oil on canvas in 1857 by an American painter who spent long years in Italy.","i":"Amalfi, once a powerful medieval maritime republic, became a favourite stop for travellers and artists on the Italian tour, prized for its cliffs, its steep little town and its light. American painters of Brown's generation went to Italy to study Claude Lorrain's classical landscapes and then sold Italian views to patrons at home, who read them as images of history, leisure and cultivation. This view offers all three at once.","c":"George Loring Brown formed his taste on Claude Lorrain, and the Claudian scheme of a broad sky over calm water and a picturesque shore is the framework here. The picture is polished and agreeable, with cliffs and boats arranged to guide the eye. It lacks the grandeur of the Hudson River painters' American vistas and the sharper observation of the best Italian views, but as a mid-century coastal landscape by an American abroad it is accomplished, if conventional."},"met:437049":{"d":"Men in cavalier dress play cards in this small oil on wood, about 35 by 27 centimeters, dated 1863. A sword, a cavalier hat, a pipe and a bench appear among the details, and the title shows that the game is cards. Ernest Meissonier painted such costume pieces in meticulous detail, setting scenes of seventeenth-century life on a scale small enough to hold in two hands.","i":"Card players were a staple of seventeenth-century Dutch and Flemish genre painting, where gaming and smoking could be read as warnings about idleness and quarrels. Meissonier revived the subject in the Second Empire, when collectors loved costume pieces that evoked a past age, and he dressed his models in carefully gathered period clothes and props. The sword hints that these are soldiers or gentlemen at ease rather than workers.","c":"Meissonier's reputation rested on microscopic finish, and the little panel is typical: every hat, bench and cuff is placed with precision. The result is admired for craft and criticized for coldness, a polish that leaves little room for feeling or air. Within the cult of the small and exact that made him one of the most expensive painters of his day, this is a characteristic and accomplished example."},"met:437050":{"d":"A soldier plays a theorbo, the long-necked bass lute of the seventeenth century, in this small oil on wood of about 29 by 22 centimeters, dated 1865. The figure is a costume study of a man at rest between duties, a favorite theme of Meissonier. The instrument gives the painter a chance to show his careful rendering of objects, and the whole picture is conceived as a quiet, complete little scene.","i":"The theorbo accompanied singers and ensembles in the early 1600s, and a soldier who plays one belongs to the picturesque world of musketeers and cavaliers that nineteenth-century French readers knew from Dumas. Meissonier collected period costumes and weapons and used them to dress his models accurately. The man at rest with his music offers a gentle contrast to the soldier in battle.","c":"The subject is slight, but the careful placing of lute, figure and costume shows Meissonier's strengths. As with much of his work, the finish is dazzling and the mood somewhat inert. The small panel exemplifies the Second Empire's taste for historical anecdote, polished to a hard sheen."},"met:437051":{"d":"A general and his aide-de-camp, mounted on horseback, appear near a shore and a wall under a clouded sky in this very small oil on wood, about 20 by 28 centimeters, dated 1869. The pair are the whole subject, and the picture studies how horses and riders are placed against a landscape. Meissonier, who prized accuracy of uniform and gait, made such small military studies as complete works in their own right.","i":"Meissonier was a devoted student of Napoleonic history, and the generals and aides he painted are often connected with the Emperor's campaigns, though this picture names no specific officer or battle. Aides carried orders across the field, and the pairing of general and aide is a way to show command at work without staging a battle. Such subjects appealed to a public that still remembered the Napoleonic legend.","c":"The small panel is typical of Meissonier for its precision, but the military subject is generic: without the reference to a particular event it remains a clever study of horses, riders and weather rather than a piece of history. The tiny scale rewards close inspection, and the painter's feeling for the horse in movement is the best thing in it."},"met:437052":{"d":"Napoleon and his army appear at the Battle of Friedland, fought on 14 June 1807, in this large canvas, about 136 by 243 centimeters, painted between about 1861 and 1875. Horsemen with swords and hats raised crowd the field, and the grass underfoot is part of the scene. The picture is a panoramic celebration of Napoleonic cavalry and one of the largest history paintings by Ernest Meissonier, better known for small panels.","i":"At Friedland, in East Prussia, Napoleon defeated the Russian army and prepared the Treaty of Tilsit, which for a time made him master of continental Europe. The painting forms part of a cycle in which Meissonier tried to record the Napoleonic epic with the accuracy of an eyewitness, working from uniforms, weapons and horses he studied for years. Its theme is the legend of the Emperor and the loyalty of his troops, made popular again under Napoleon III.","c":"Meissonier is at his best small, and Friedland shows his method of accumulating exact detail stretched to the scale of a battle. The research is immense, but the effect is of a tableau rather than a battle. It remains impressive as an effort of will and as a document of the Second Empire's taste for military glory, and the long labor it required is visible in its thoroughness."},"nga:132271":{"d":"A wide banquet still life on an ivory-white tablecloth laid over a second cloth patterned in burgundy and rose that shows only at the right edge. The largest platter, set toward the back right, holds a baked pie from which the head, wings and tail of a peacock rise, a pink rose in its beak. Around it are porcelain bowls of fruit and green olives, pewter plates of sweets, a small baked fowl, a cut lemon with its peel curling off the plate, a glass with a nubbed stem, bread and an ivory-handled knife, all against a plain brown ground.","i":"A peacock pie was a banquet spectacle: the bird's head, wings and tail were displayed above the pastry, so the dish announced the host's wealth before anyone ate. The lemon, olives, sugared sweets, salt in a golden vessel and porcelain were luxuries too. Dutch still lifes of this kind are usually read as celebrating abundance while hinting that it is fleeting, through details such as the half-peeled lemon and the empty glass lying on a plate. How strongly Claesz intended that warning is uncertain.","c":"Claesz is best known for the sober, almost monochrome tables in silvery gray and brown of the following years; this banquet is richer and more crowded, and the peacock gives it a theater that his later restraint avoids. The strength is in the differentiation of surfaces: pewter, porcelain, linen, pastry and glass each have their own weight and sheen. The composition is busy, but the white cloth holds it together, and the picture rewards close looking."},"met:435904":{"d":"A human skull sits on a table among the traces of a scholar's desk and a drinking party: a book, a quill, a tall rummer wineglass and an oil lamp whose wick gives off a thread of smoke. Painted in 1628 on a small wooden panel about 24 by 36 cm, it shows only a few objects, set close together and lit so that their surfaces can be read. This is a vanitas, a still life built to remind the viewer how brief life is, and meant for private contemplation at close range.","i":"Every object points the same way. The skull is death; the smoking wick suggests a flame just put out; the book and quill stand for learning and fame that cannot be taken with us; the wineglass for pleasure that passes. Vanitas pictures flourished in the Netherlands from the 1620s, especially in Leiden and Haarlem, and offered a sermon without words to viewers who knew the Ecclesiastes line, vanity of vanities.","c":"Claesz was about thirty and working in Haarlem, and the picture shows the sober tonal control he made his trademark: little colour, a few forms, close attention to the textures of bone and glass. Its force comes from restraint, which larger and richer vanitas pictures often lose beneath their props. The symbolism is easy to read, almost too easy, but the quiet handling keeps it from becoming a mere lesson."},"met:437070":{"d":"A woman and a man make music in an interior, with a lute among the instruments, in a polite company that gives the picture its title. The oil on canvas, about 62 by 54 cm, is dated 1659. Gabriël Metsu, born in Leiden and by this date working in Amsterdam, painted the manners of the prosperous Dutch middle class in finely observed interiors, with an eye for fabric and gesture.","i":"Music-making was a standard image of courtship and harmony in Dutch painting: the lute is the instrument of love, and a party offers sociable pleasure. Such pictures could be read in two ways, as a celebration of civilized pleasure and as a gentle warning about the temptations of music and wine. Viewers in the 1650s enjoyed the ambiguity, and the figures, probably types rather than portraits, stand for ideal manners rather than named individuals.","c":"Metsu is a subtler colorist than most of his Leiden peers, and a musical party gives him a chance to orchestrate textiles and light. He is warmer than Ter Borch and more narrative than Vermeer. The risk of the subject is anecdote: elegant gestures can replace feeling. In 1659 he is a confident, rising master, and this canvas shows his gift for harmonizing figures within a single interior."},"nga:64":{"d":"A young man bursts through the arched door of a dim bedroom, arms spread wide and hat held out to one side, and finds three women. One, in a sable-brown gown, lays a hand on his shoulder as if to stop him while smiling back over her shoulder. At the centre a woman in white satin and a coral-red bodice steps down from a curtained bed. A third sits by the window with a comb, a mirror and a silver box beside her, a small dog at her feet. A crimson cloak lies across a chair, and window light falls on the central woman.","i":"A male visitor in a woman's bedroom is a stock comedy of Dutch painting, and the details tell viewers how to read it. The bed, the shoe on the floor, the mirror and comb of the toilet and the little dog were familiar signs of vanity and desire. Whether the women welcome or deflect him is left open, which is the joke: one stays his arm yet smiles, one rises from bed. Buyers got a flirtatious story and a caution about courtship in one frame.","c":"Metsu, born in Leiden and working in Amsterdam, borrows the polished interiors of Ter Borch and Dou and adds a livelier theatre of entrances. The satin and the fur-trimmed cloak are painted with relish, and the light binds three women and a man into one believable room. What he does not match is Ter Borch's psychological reserve: the intruder's wide arms read like a stage direction. As anecdote it delights; as feeling it is a little arranged."},"met:437071":{"d":"A man visits an infant in a bedroom, with a woman, a domestic servant and a dog, in an oil on canvas of about 78 by 81 cm, dated 1661. The figures are identified as the Amsterdam merchant Jan Jacobsz Hinlopen and Sara Hinlopen, so the picture is a family portrait in the guise of a genre scene, the father's visit to the nursery, and its intimate scale makes it a household picture rather than a public one.","i":"In the Dutch Republic, family portraits in domestic settings celebrated the household's virtues and children as a blessing, and a nursery visit refers to a father's role in a new family. The dog is a traditional emblem of fidelity, and a servant in the room is a sign of wealth. After a birth, the lying-in room was a place of ceremony, with visits from friends and relatives, and prosperous families liked to record such milestones.","c":"Metsu blends portrait and genre with tact: the group's interactions give the scene a story, and he keeps the delicate handling of textiles and surfaces that made his name. The picture is more intimate than Ter Borch's polite conversations and more narrative than De Hooch's quiet courtyards. The risk of such a scene is crowding, and the ambition lies in the domestic narrative rather than in display. It is one of the warmest pictures of his Amsterdam years."},"met:437073":{"d":"A woman sits at a window with a book and fruit, including grapes, in a very small oil on wood of about 28 by 23 cm, painted in the early 1660s. The window frame turns the figure into a kind of stage, a device that Metsu inherited from Gerrit Dou and the Leiden fine painters, whose small, polished pictures were prized by collectors. The title gives no name, so the woman may be a type rather than a portrait.","i":"The window sets the woman between the viewer's world and her own, and grapes and other fruit can mean sensual pleasure or temperance, depending on context. The book may suggest reading or devotion. Together the three can suggest contemplative pleasure, a quiet moment of private life, and the meaning is left open on purpose, as in many Dutch pictures that invite the viewer to supply the story.","c":"A jewel of a picture: tiny, polished and quietly attractive, with the sharpness of fruit and the neatness of a book as the pleasures of the fine painters. The danger of the type is preciousness, a smoothness that can make life look lacquered. Metsu is at his most Dou-like here, yielding to the Leiden manner, but the small size lends intimacy, and the picture works as a delicately observed, perhaps too neat, little poem of reflection."},"nga:1191":{"d":"Outside a brick blacksmith's shop, two men hold a dark horse's mouth open to work on its teeth, one using a long rod, while the horse's nose is gripped in long-handled tongs. A shorter third figure in a floppy hat looks on. A white horse stands behind, and in the shadowed doorway the smith bends over a glowing piece of metal as smoke pours from the chimney. Dogs, a hen and a rooster share the yard, and beyond a low, tree-lined horizon puffy clouds cross a blue sky. The vertical panel is about 48 by 46 cm.","i":"Farriers' shops were a favourite subject for Dutch painters of animals and landscape, who found in them rural trade, horses and picturesque shabbiness together. Potter, famous above all for animals, shows a practical operation, a horse's teeth being tended, with humour in one man's wide-eyed concentration and the horse's helpless patience. There is probably little hidden moral here; the point is the pleasure of watching skilled work and of animals rendered with great exactness.","c":"Potter was in his early twenties and already outstanding in drawing animals: the horse's head, the tongs and the working of the mouth look observed rather than invented. The low horizon, a quarter of the way up the panel, gives the beasts and the sky a monumental, almost portrait-like presence. The men are less convincing than the horses, and the grandeur of his Young Bull of 1647 is missing, but it is an assured early work."},"met:435869":{"d":"A man in a monk's habit, with a cross, is shown in a vertical portrait about 65 by 55 cm. The sitter is the painter's uncle, Antoine Dominique Sauveur Aubert, born in 1817, and the monk is a costume rather than a calling: in 1866 the young Cézanne had his uncle pose in several roles. The paint is thick and rough, applied in slabs and often with a palette knife, which gives the figure a heavy, sculptural presence.","i":"Dressing a relative as a monk was a studio game, not a religious statement, and the young Cézanne used his uncle as a patient model in a series of such portraits. The habit probably recalls the Spanish painting of monks admired in France in the mid-nineteenth century and the brooding Romanticism of Delacroix. The picture reads as a portrait and an exercise in costume at once, a way for a painter still searching for his manner to try dark, dramatic effects.","c":"At twenty-seven Cézanne was still pursuing Courbet's heft and Delacroix's drama, and the result is strong but clumsy in a productive way: the slabs of paint have real weight, and the figure is stubbornly present. Anatomy and character are less secure than the surface. It is not yet the controlled Cézanne of later years, but it shows the physical appetite for paint from which his calmer constructions grew."},"met:435870":{"d":"A man is shown in a portrait on a vertical canvas about 82 by 66 cm. The sitter is the painter's uncle, Antoine Dominique Sauveur Aubert, born in 1817, and the picture belongs to a series Cézanne made of him in 1866. The museum's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so the picture is characterised by type: a head-and-shoulders or half-length likeness, painted with thick paint and a bold, simplified handling that was then unusual in portraiture.","i":"Cézanne's uncle on his mother's side was a convenient and cooperative sitter in Aix, and he appears again and again in portraits of 1866. The series shows the young painter experimenting: thick paint, dark contrasts and a deliberately unrefined finish. Relatives were the natural models of an artist without commissions, and an uncle could be asked to sit for hours. These portraits concern the painter's attempt to build a face out of paint more than the sitter's status.","c":"The uncle portraits are the clearest early evidence of Cézanne's physical way with paint: the face is modelled in thick, almost sculpted strokes with little concern for polish. The force is real, but the drawing is rough and the mood heavy, and nobody would mistake it for the later work. As a document of a painter making himself it matters more than it pleases, and it is the more convincing for that."},"nga:52085":{"d":"A man sits in a tall upholstered armchair in the corner of a room, holding a newspaper so close to his face that the folded top edge shows its title, L'EVENEMENT. He wears a close-fitting black cap over white hair, a brown jacket, white shirt, gray trousers and brown shoes. Room, chair and figure are put down in broad, bold strokes, with black outlines around the man and the chair. A small still life hangs over his head, partly hidden by it. The canvas is nearly two meters tall.","i":"The sitter is Louis-Auguste Cézanne, a banker in Aix-en-Provence who long doubted that painting was a career for his son. L'Événement was the paper in which Cézanne's friend Émile Zola published his Salon reviews of 1866 defending the new painters, so the title on the page may be a private joke. The still life on the wall is presumably the son's own, as if Cézanne had slipped himself into his father's world in the same picture.","c":"A raw and powerful young painting. Cézanne still works in the dark, heavy manner of his early years, with black contours and loaded strokes learned from Courbet and Manet, and the result is almost brutal. But the father has a stubborn physical presence that no polished likeness would match. The chair is a mass of decoration and the lower half is weaker than the head, yet the picture reads as a portrait of authority, awkward and tender at once."},"nga:52389":{"d":"A man with thick black hair and a squared-off black beard sits facing the viewer, his body frontal but his eyes lowered and turned to the viewer's left. He wears an open black jacket over a charcoal vest, a white shirt and a loosely painted tie, and his hands, closed into fists, rest on thighs cut off by the bottom edge. The paint is applied broadly, so details dissolve. The tip of his nose almost matches the coral red of his lips, and the gray background is brushed over black.","i":"Antony Valabrègue (1844–1900) was a poet and art critic from Aix-en-Provence, a friend of Cézanne and of Émile Zola from the circle of Provençal friends. Cézanne painted him several times in 1866, in portraits that treat a friend as a block of sculpted color, thick and dark. Friends and family sat often for Cézanne, whose method demanded great patience from his sitters.","c":"The dark palette and heavy handling Cézanne used in the mid-1860s suit the sitter's black beard and clothes, and the planes of the face have a solidity that reaches toward sculpture. The price is stiffness of pose and blankness of expression: Valabrègue is more a block than a person. It is a forceful exercise by an artist still searching, closer to Courbet than to the Cézanne of later decades."},"met:435873":{"d":"A man in a straw hat is shown in a vertical portrait about 55 by 39 cm, painted in oil on paper and later laid down on canvas. The sitter, Gustave Boyer, was born in 1840, a year after the painter. The paper support and modest size suggest an informal, quickly worked study rather than a formal commission, and the straw hat, a summer or country item, gives the sitter an easy, unofficial air.","i":"Cézanne painted friends and family because they would sit without charge, and a portrait like this belongs to his private experimentation rather than to the market for likenesses. The date, 1870-71, coincides with the Franco-Prussian War, when he spent much of his time in the south of France away from Paris. The informal hat and the study-like support suggest a sitter at ease, painted to practise the figure rather than to flatter.","c":"Compared with the dark slabs of the 1866 portraits of the uncle, a study on paper like this is likely to be quicker and freer, and the support gives the surface an immediacy a stretched canvas might have lost. It is not a finished statement, and the sitter's personality is only sketched. Its appeal lies in that unforced economy: Cézanne looking hard at a face and not yet needing to make a monument of it."},"nga:46577":{"d":"Across a still pond, sandstone-white buildings with brown and terracotta-red roofs nestle in a grove of green trees. A slate-gray wall is half hidden by a vine, and the pond, spanning the lower width of the canvas, mirrors the plants. Foliage in front of the buildings is built from short daubs of yellow and green with touches of blue, while the trees behind fill the upper third with layered, angled strokes of emerald, pine and celery green. A slice of blue sky and patches of orange show at the upper left. Signed and dated 73.","i":"The house stood at Auvers-sur-Oise, northwest of Paris, where Cézanne worked in 1873 near Camille Pissarro. Pissarro urged him to paint outdoors, to lighten his palette and to give up the dark, heavy manner of his early years, and this small vertical landscape is a product of that lesson. Père Lacroix, presumably the owner or occupant, gives the property its name in the title.","c":"A key step in Cézanne's education. The Pissarro lesson is visible in the light key, the Impressionist daubs and the reflected foliage, but Cézanne already builds differently, stacking the leaves in angled layers so that the grove becomes a solid wall and keeping the buildings plain and weighty. Less dazzling than Monet, more constructed, it is a promising picture by a painter still discovering his method."},"met:435867":{"d":"Nude women bathe beside a stream in a small horizontal canvas, about 38 by 46 cm. The museum's keywords record female nudes, bathing and a watercourse, so the picture is characterised by type: an imagined outdoor scene in which figures and landscape are brushed together, with little of the polish of academic nudes. It is an early statement of the subject Cézanne would return to for the rest of his life, in ever larger and more monumental compositions.","i":"Bathers let Cézanne treat the nude in nature, a subject inherited from Giorgione, Poussin and Courbet, without a mythological story. He rarely worked from women models and usually composed such groups from memory, drawings and the old masters, so the figures are types, not portraits. Viewers can read the scene as a modern Arcadia, a place of ease between body and landscape, though it probably also carries the strain of a shy painter attempting a theme he found hard.","c":"Even at this small scale Cézanne is trying to fuse figures and setting into one structure, which became the central concern of the later Bathers. The bodies are awkward, perhaps deliberately, and the picture is more a first statement than a solution. It is more interesting as a beginning than as an achievement; the great Bathers compositions of his last decades resolve what this small canvas only poses."},"nga:46578":{"d":"Across a grassy field, a row of buildings and two trees line the horizon, which sits about halfway up this nearly square canvas. The field is painted in visible strokes of sage, moss and mint green with caramel brown, parchment white and turquoise blue. The dark green trees stand left of center, and the buildings have charcoal-gray or terracotta-red roofs and cream-white or slate-blue walls. The sky is dabbed and stroked in ice blue and baby blue, with pale peach and ivory for the clouds.","i":"Around 1876 Cézanne moved between Provence and the Paris region, painting suburban fields and villages as his Impressionist friends did. The motif is deliberately unremarkable: no landmark, only the edge where town meets farmland. For Cézanne and Pissarro such ordinary places were enough, because the real subject was the painting itself, and how touch, color and space could be made to agree.","c":"There is little here but field, sky and a line of roofs, and that is the point. The even division of earth and air is calm and stable, the green is enlivened by surprisingly cool touches of turquoise, and the small buildings are placed with precision. The picture is reticent to the point of dryness, lacking the drama of the Provençal views, but it shows his method working in its most modest form."},"nga:46580":{"d":"Rose and coral pink, vivid white, delphinium blue and buttercup yellow flowers with deep green leaves fill a blue and white vase against a pale sea-green background. The vase has a white cup on a flaring foot and is encircled with a twisting, decorative blue vine and scrolls. It sits on a band of peanut brown that suggests a wooden tabletop. The strokes are blended, so the whole has a soft, almost mottled look. The canvas is vertical, about 73 by 60 centimeters, and signed 'Cezanne' at the lower left.","i":"Flower pieces are comparatively rare in Cézanne's work; fruit was his usual subject, since it keeps and holds still while a painter studies it. The decorative vase of the title, with its scrolls and twisting vine, is in a rococo style, and the soft, blended touch recalls the years when Cézanne was closest to the Impressionists. The museum dates the picture to about 1876.","c":"The flowers make a brilliant spray against the pale wall, and the blended strokes give them a pastel softness that is attractive and unusual in Cézanne. The vase is flatter and the table a brown band without weight, and the picture seems less certain than his fruit studies, where simple geometry gave him a firmer hold. Flowers, which wilt, did not suit a painter who worked as slowly as he did."},"met:437989":{"d":"Apples lie in a dish on a table, the whole subject of a still life on a horizontal canvas about 46 by 55 cm. The museum's keywords record only the apples and the genre, so what follows is general: an arrangement of fruit and a vessel, made around 1876-77, when Cézanne was working under the influence of Pissarro and the Impressionists and moving away from the dark, heavy manner of his youth. Fruit, which holds still and costs little, was his favourite model.","i":"Cézanne painted apples throughout his career and used them to test how colour and form could be built together. Unlike flowers, they do not wilt on a sitter's timetable, and unlike a portrait sitter they do not tire or judge. Dutch and French still life offered a precedent, from Chardin to Manet, but Cézanne lets the old meanings of abundance and mortality fall away and treats the fruit as pure visual subject matter. Whatever symbolism viewers hear in apples is largely their own.","c":"An early dish of apples already shows the habit that makes Cézanne's still lifes last: round forms built up from distinct touches of paint, each fruit given its own weight. The handling is less systematic than in the 1880s, when the brushstroke settled into parallel hatching, but the seriousness is there. It is modest in subject and scale, and stronger for refusing to decorate."},"met:435866":{"d":"A small still life of apples, about 23 by 33 cm, painted in oil on canvas in 1878-79. The museum's keywords record only the fruit, so the picture is characterised by type: a modest grouping made when Cézanne was developing the regular, parallel brushstrokes that give his surfaces their structure. The size suggests a picture painted for study and for the painter's own use, or for a friend, rather than for exhibition.","i":"Apples had been symbols of temptation, abundance and love in older art, and viewers trained on Dutch or French still life might expect such readings. Cézanne largely lets those meanings lapse. For him an apple is a test of how a rounded form can be made from colour, with warm and cool touches turning around it. The subject is plain on purpose, offering no story and so asking only for attention, which later painters valued highly.","c":"At this size the experiment is concentrated: a few apples and a great deal of looking. Cézanne's treatment of fruit as solid, weighty form, built from adjacent patches of colour rather than outline, makes small pictures like this feel larger than their dimensions. It is quiet, unshowy work, and some viewers will find the subject slight, but this is the kind of picture through which his later reputation was built."},"nga:136014":{"d":"Three whole green pears stand against a caramel-brown background in this small horizontal canvas, only about 20 by 26 centimeters. The viewer looks slightly down on the fruit, which is built with thick strokes of lemon-lime, asparagus and spring green and outlined in navy blue. Two pears stand upright at either side and the third lies between them, its brown stem angled to the left. Long, parallel brushstrokes run through the whole painting, and a strip of unpainted canvas lines the top edge.","i":"From the late 1870s Cézanne painted fruit steadily, because it could be arranged and studied for as long as he liked and carried no story to distract from the problem of form and color. The parallel, slanting strokes seen here belong to the method he developed in these years, in which touch itself builds volume. Three pears are enough to present the whole problem.","c":"Small and intensely concentrated. The navy contours make each pear a solid, the strip of bare canvas shows that the picture is an exercise in structure and not in finish, and the lying pear between the standing two gives the group a quiet rhythm. It is a modest picture, but it shows the stripped-down method that later painters would study. It makes its point without any help from the subject."},"met:438136":{"d":"A man, a woman and a river appear in an imagined rather than observed scene, painted in oil on a horizontal canvas about 55 by 82 cm. The museum's keywords record only the figures and the water, and the subtitle, Fantastic Scene, flags invention. It is therefore characterised by type: a figure subject from Cézanne's imagination, of the kind he made in the 1860s and 1870s from reading, memory and other paintings, rather than a record of a place or an event.","i":"Cézanne's fantasy scenes draw on Romantic and Rococo sources: the river outing, the ideal Arcadia, the dreamlike meeting of men and women. The subtitle marks the picture as invention. He had painted violent and erotic fantasies in the 1860s, and by the mid-1870s such subjects usually become lighter and more pastoral. The picture is probably best read as a poetic scene of leisure and desire rather than a story with a fixed text.","c":"Imaginary figure scenes suited Cézanne less well than the landscapes and still lifes he drew from nature, and he turned increasingly to direct observation. What survives in work like this is his rhythmic handling and a willingness to push a dream into something odd and personal. It is a fascinating transitional picture, valuable for showing where he started and what he gave up, though not among his strongest."},"nga:53120":{"d":"Four couples lock in intense embraces across a grassy landscape in front of a body of water, in a nearly square canvas loosely painted in spring green, sky blue, straw yellow and ivory. Trees rise off the top edge at both sides, and white clouds swirl in a topaz-blue sky. Near the viewer, one nude figure crouches while another stands above; two blond figures embrace, one partly lifted off the ground; a tanned figure kneels over a pale nude woman while a dog leaps nearby. The fourth pair tips over a rise at the right.","i":"The theme, which Cézanne treated in more than one version around 1880, descends from the erotic and violent fantasies that filled his work in the 1860s. Here the embraces and struggles take place in a landscape like those he was painting from nature. They are usually read as expressions of his conflicted attitude to desire, but the loose handling leaves the figures' identities and actions uncertain, and the title is the main guide.","c":"A mixed success. The landscape is fresh, the clouds swirl, and the greens and blues hold together, but the figures are weak: limbs and faces dissolve, and the lovers are joined by awkward overlaps. Its interest is partly historical, as a prelude to the large Bathers, which turn comparable encounters between nude and landscape into monumental calm."},"met:435884":{"d":"A jar, a cup, apples and other fruit are gathered on a table in a still life on a horizontal canvas about 61 by 74 cm. The museum's keywords name only these objects, so the picture is characterised by type: a table-top arrangement of plain household vessels and fruit, painted around 1877, when Cézanne was learning from Pissarro to lighten his palette and to build form with short, separate strokes. Such a canvas was a studio exercise first and a picture second.","i":"Cézanne chose ordinary objects, a jar, a cup, fruit, because they asked nothing of him except to be looked at. There is no feast, no emblem of mortality and no story, which sets him apart from Dutch and French predecessors. Viewers of the 1870s found such pictures dull or rough; later artists saw in them a method for constructing a picture out of relations of colour and shape. The meaning lies in that looking, not in the objects.","c":"Early in his still-life career the grammar of his mature work is still forming: objects are sized and placed by the needs of the whole picture rather than strict perspective, and the paint is applied in patches that describe form. The picture lacks the tension of the 1890s canvases, where tilting tabletops and shifting viewpoints heighten the drama. It is a sound, serious step, valuable as a record of how his method developed."},"nga:54129":{"d":"Pale beige, angular houses cluster at the center of this horizontal landscape. The hilly, rocky ground around and below them is painted in cool blues and greens with warm ivory and caramel brown, beneath a pale blue sky. Cézanne applied the paint in regular, parallel, straight strokes that run across the whole surface, tying houses, hills and sky together. The record lists houses, trees and a mountain among the motifs. The canvas is about 65 by 81 centimeters.","i":"L'Estaque is a fishing village on the bay of Marseille, where Cézanne painted repeatedly in the 1870s and 1880s, and the Riaux is a valley behind it. The simplified, cube-like houses of such views struck later painters. In 1908 Georges Braque painted the same country, and a critic who saw his pictures described their geometric forms as cubes, helping to give Cubism its name.","c":"Parallel strokes run through hills, houses and sky alike, as though the whole scene were woven. That unity is the picture's achievement: the rocky ground and the boxlike houses lock together like a relief. What it gives up is air; the light is cool and constant, and the view feels constructed more than seen. It is Cézanne's disciplined manner at an early, confident stage."},"met:435877":{"d":"A tall, slender pine rises at the centre of the canvas, its branches spreading flat against the sky and its trunk cutting the view in two. Behind and below it lies a broad valley of small fields in green, yellow and ochre, dotted with orange-roofed farm buildings and crossed by a pale road winding down toward the lower right. A long stone viaduct of many small arches spans the middle distance. At the left, pink-violet Mont Sainte-Victoire rises beyond a cluster of thinner pine trunks that frame the edge of the picture, and low hills fill the right under a blue sky with soft cloud. The paint is applied in short parallel strokes that build planes of colour rather than blending them.","i":"The view lies in Cézanne's native Provence, near Aix. The Met says he stood close to Montbriand, his sister's property, on the hilltop behind her house, and that the viaduct is a railway viaduct. The mountain became his lifelong motif; between 1882 and 1890 he painted a group of canvases at this site, and the Met calls this the most completely worked and detailed of them. It reached the New York collectors Louisine and H. O. Havemeyer in 1901 through the dealer Ambroise Vollard. Cézanne's way of building space from planes of colour, rather than from light and atmosphere, became a model for the Cubists a generation later.","c":"The pine is the decisive stroke. Planting it dead centre breaks the rule that a view should open invitingly, yet it is what holds the picture together, tying foreground, valley and mountain into a single surface. The patchwork fields and the repeated arches of the viaduct receive equal weight, so the eye moves across the canvas rather than into depth. The mountain is small and pale beside the tree, which some will find a loss. Even so, this is one of Cézanne's most legible landscapes and the easiest way into his mature method."},"met:435871":{"d":"A hill town rises up a vertical canvas, about 80 by 64 cm. The museum's keywords record the town and its buildings. Cézanne worked at Gardanne, a village near Aix-en-Provence, in 1885-86, and his pictures from there are known for stacking the houses into a compact mass of simplified blocks that climbs the picture surface, so the usual deep recession of perspective is largely flattened. It is a view meant to be studied slowly, painted in oil on canvas.","i":"Gardanne is not a picturesque postcard but a test of how a town can be built into a picture. Cézanne treats houses as geometric volumes and lets the whole group tilt and press upward, ideas that fascinated Braque and Picasso two decades later, when critics began to speak of cubes. In 1885-86 he was also settling into his private life in Provence, away from Paris and the Impressionist circle, and the village views belong to that withdrawal.","c":"This is among the landmark landscapes of the 1880s: Cézanne makes an architectural structure out of colour and edge, and it holds together without a conventional vanishing point. The austerity may disappoint viewers expecting sun-warmed Provence, and the surface is deliberately unsweetened. That severity is the point. As a source for twentieth-century painting it is hard to overstate, and it remains impressive as a picture in its own right."},"met:435879":{"d":"A garden view with a pool on a horizontal canvas, about 65 by 81 cm. The museum's keywords record the garden and the landscape. The setting is the Jas de Bouffan, the country house near Aix-en-Provence that Cézanne's father bought in 1859 and which the painter used as a studio and subject for decades. The picture dates from about 1885-86, and it treats a familiar corner of the estate as a place to build a composition of trees, water and light.","i":"The Jas de Bouffan was the Cézanne family home and a refuge where the painter could work undisturbed, and he returned to its avenue of trees, its farm buildings and its pond again and again. The pool made a natural subject for an artist concerned with how reflections and surfaces tie a picture together. The view is domestic and unheroic, with nothing of the monumental mountain that later occupied him, yet it stages his central question of how to organise nature into stable form.","c":"Seen among the Jas de Bouffan paintings, this is a quietly confident middle-period picture: the forms are held by structure rather than atmosphere, and the little pool anchors the composition. It does not strain for drama, and some viewers will find it reserved beside the Sainte-Victoire canvases. That reserve is Cézanne's discipline at work, and the pleasure is in the steadiness of the construction, not in surprise."},"nga:46575":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a pale young man in a bowler hat looks at the viewer from the corners of his eyes, lips pursed. His body angles to the right, and he wears a buttoned blue jacket with white lapels turned up; the black hat seems a little too small for his head. Face, collar and jacket are built from mottled, streaked color, with slate blue shading the peachy skin, and bare canvas shows through in the jacket. Behind him a loosely suggested diamond pattern covers the wall, and a rail at the right is patterned in red teardrops on blue.","i":"The sitter is Cézanne's son Paul, born in 1872 and about fourteen or fifteen when this portrait was made. Cézanne painted him many times, and Paul later helped manage his father's affairs. The portrait dates from the years when Cézanne married Paul's mother, Hortense Fiquet, in 1886 and inherited after his father's death, which gave him security to paint on his own terms.","c":"This is a candid, unflattering portrait that finds a teenager's wariness in the sideways look and the pursed mouth. The mottled color, with blues in the face and greens in the collar, is typical of Cézanne's late-1880s method, building form by patches of cool and warm. The bowler hat and patterned wall are the weaker parts, crowding a figure that otherwise has a touching presence. Affection comes through without flattery."},"nga:53122":{"d":"A few pale brown buildings with terracotta red roofs nestle among hills at the foot of a towering, icy-blue mountain. Cézanne laid in only the pale indigo-violet shadows of the mountain, leaving the rest as bare white canvas. The buildings, the muted brown hills, the blue shadows and touches of sage green below are loosely painted with visible strokes around more blank areas of canvas. The picture is horizontal, about 67 by 91 centimeters, and the mountain it names looms over everything.","i":"Mont Sainte-Victoire, the limestone ridge east of Aix-en-Provence, was Cézanne's lifelong motif, painted in dozens of oils and watercolors. In 1885 and 1886 he worked at Gardanne, a village south of Aix, and views from that time often set the mountain beyond a plain with houses and trees. The bare canvas here may be a stage of an unfinished work or a deliberate economy; the museum dates the picture to about 1887.","c":"The bare canvas lets the mountain glow, and that daring economy is the picture's glory: a few blue shadows are enough to make a massif. The buildings and hills at the base are crisp by contrast and give the scale. If it is unfinished, it is more convincing than many finished paintings; if intended, it points toward the watercolor-like oils of Cézanne's last years. Either way, the mountain is felt more than drawn."},"met:435885":{"d":"Trees and a mountain stand in a horizontal landscape about 65 by 81 cm, painted in the late 1880s. The title names the Domaine Saint-Joseph, an estate in the countryside near Aix-en-Provence where Cézanne painted repeatedly, and the museum's keywords record only trees and the mountain beyond. The picture is a view across open Provençal country, built in oil on canvas from patches of colour that read first as structure and only afterwards as place.","i":"The country around Aix was Cézanne's chosen ground, and he treated the Domaine Saint-Joseph and its hills as a motif he could revisit across seasons and years. Provençal landscape offered what he wanted: clear structure, strong light and a distant mountain that anchors a view. The credit line shows the Met bought this painting in 1913, an early purchase at a time when Cézanne was still little known to American museum-goers.","c":"Late-1880s Cézanne is at his most balanced here: observation of light is disciplined by a clear scaffolding of trunks, slopes and distant forms, and the brushwork builds the space plane by plane. If there is a weakness it is that the picture asks patience; the sensuous charm of Impressionism is replaced by order. For those who give it time, this is one of the demonstrations of how a landscape can be both seen and constructed."},"met:435872":{"d":"Trees, a town and the sea are brought together in a horizontal canvas about 73 by 100 cm. The title identifies the view as the Gulf of Marseille from L'Estaque, a fishing village west of the city where Cézanne painted again and again. The museum's keywords record trees, the town and the sea, and the picture, dated about 1885, sets the houses of the village against the water so that near and far are bound into one surface.","i":"L'Estaque offered Cézanne strong Mediterranean light, a clear horizon and a mix of houses, hills and bay. He first painted there in 1870-71 and returned through the 1870s and 1880s, and the village views became a laboratory for his ideas about colour and structure. Writing to Pissarro in 1876 he likened the scene to a playing card, red roofs against blue sea. The view is less a record of a place than an argument about how to paint it.","c":"This is one of the clearest statements of his construction method in the 1880s: the sea reads as a taut plane, the trees as a screen set across it, and the houses as small geometric accents. The risk is austerity, since the Mediterranean is not allowed to sparkle, but the picture repays a long look, and the gap between description and design is part of its fascination."},"met:435876":{"d":"A woman sits upright and square-on in a yellow armchair, her hands in her lap holding a few small green leaves. Her hair is parted in the middle and smoothed back, and her face is serious and almost mask-like. She wears a brick-red dress with a deep collar and a belt at the waist, its folds built from broken planes of red, orange and rose. Behind her are a gilt frame at the upper left, a grey-blue wall with a dark band, and a heavy curtain printed with large fruit and leaves in yellow and green. A dark stand rises at the left edge. Oil on canvas, about 116 by 90 centimetres.","i":"The sitter is Hortense Fiquet (1850–1922), Cézanne's companion from about 1869 and his wife from 1886. He painted her about twenty-nine times, more than any other model. In most of these portraits she is set against a plain wall, but this one is unusual in placing her in an elaborately furnished interior. The date, 1888 to 1890, shows an artist concerned less with likeness than with building a figure and a room from planes of colour, in which the patterned curtain and the gilt frame matter as much as the sitter. Her calm, impassive presence let Cézanne concentrate on the structure of the picture.","c":"This is Cézanne's formal portraiture at full stretch: the solid red dress, the rhymes of vertical and diagonal and the blue-green room against warm yellows hold together as a structure first and a likeness second. The sitter's deliberately blank face can look cold, and Hortense has often been judged dull for it, but the blankness is Cézanne's choice. The weakness lies in the room, where curtain and wall do not quite sit behind her and the perspective wobbles. That tension is also how Cézanne keeps the surface alive."},"nga:46576":{"d":"A young boy in an aquamarine-blue jacket sits facing the viewer, shown from the chest up, looking just over the viewer's right shoulder. His close-cropped dark hair frames a pale face painted in swipes of peach, straw yellow, stone blue and olive green, especially around the large dark eyes and small cherry-red mouth. A scarf knotted at his neck is built from daubs of azure, teal and putty gray. He sits in a corner washed in aqua and turquoise, and the curved rail of a brown chair appears behind his shoulder. The canvas is vertical, about 56 by 47 centimeters.","i":"Louis Guillaume was a boy who posed for Cézanne in the 1880s; he is said to be the model for Pierrot in the larger Mardi Gras of about 1888, in which Cézanne's son Paul appears as Harlequin. The museum gives a long range of dates for the canvas, 1879 to 1890, which reflects uncertainty over when it was painted.","c":"The patchwork of colors in face and scarf is Cézanne's late method at its most gentle, and the large, shy eyes give the boy an unguarded presence. The aqua corner is almost too cool and the jacket too loosely described to give the figure much weight, but the picture is among the warmest of his figure paintings, and it avoids the stiffness of his adult portraits."},"nga:52165":{"d":"A row of structures and a massive weeping willow line the clay-orange riverbank on the far side of the water. Painted with long, visible strokes, the tree takes up much of the right side, with emerald, moss, fern and olive-green leaves over a brown and purple trunk and roots, and it casts long, gently rippling reflections. At the left the buildings sit behind a terracotta-colored wall with a square tower, and a charcoal-gray wall extends beyond; trees rise behind. The sky is mingled lavender, baby blue and white.","i":"Médan, on the Seine west of Paris, is best known as the village where Émile Zola, Cézanne's friend from boyhood, had his country house, and Cézanne painted there as a guest. Their friendship ended around 1886, after Zola's novel L'Œuvre drew a failed painter in whom Cézanne saw himself. The museum does not identify the buildings, and dates the picture about 1885 to 1890.","c":"The weeping willow is the picture's weight and its glory: dark and generous on the right, balanced by the wall and tower on the left, and doubled in the water in a way that makes the surface tremble. The long, parallel strokes keep the surface lively. The sky is rather perfunctory by comparison, but the picture has the confident calm of Cézanne's maturity."},"nga:53119":{"d":"Across a body of water stand two cream-white houses with dark roofs, some smaller outbuildings and a houseboat along the far bank, nearly engulfed by tall, lush trees that reach almost to the top of the canvas. The paint is thin and loose, in moss and pine green, pale topaz and denim blue, and black, and many details are indistinct. Fog-gray clouds float in a watery blue sky, and the trees' green is reflected below, where some canvas is left unpainted in the lower left. A crimson-red blotch appears over the houses.","i":"The museum calls the view abstracted and does not name the site, though river scenes with houses, trees and boats were common in Cézanne's work of the late 1880s and 1890s. Leaving areas of canvas bare was a habit of his later years, and younger painters took it as permission to treat a painting as an arrangement of color and touch and not a finished illusion. The date is given as about 1890.","c":"Foliage and reflection mirror each other so that the picture seems to float, and the bare patch at the lower left is a daring gap that lets the water breathe. The red blotch over the houses is a risky accent which, whether deliberate or accidental, rescues the color. A work on the border between finished and unfinished, rewarding to look at but a little evasive."},"nga:66405":{"d":"A cleanshaven man in a black and red diamond-patterned costume stands in a room with a blue background, a white wooden sword tucked under one arm. His body angles to the right and he seems to look down in that direction, though the dark eyes are loosely painted. Pink touches mark his cheeks, and a subtle swipe suggests a mouth that seems to vanish when seen from afar. An arctic-blue cap curves down over the ears like an upside-down crescent, and one hand is oversized. Behind him are a dappled wall and a yellow and teal curtain.","i":"Harlequin, the acrobatic, quick-witted servant of the Italian commedia dell'arte, wears a diamond-patterned costume and carries a wooden sword, or slapstick. The model was probably Cézanne's son Paul, who also posed as Harlequin in the larger Mardi Gras of the same years. Carnival performers later became emblems of the artist for Picasso and others, and Cézanne's Harlequin is an early step on that road.","c":"The figure is oddly lifeless: the oversized hand, the nearly missing mouth and the stagy pose keep the story at arm's length. Cézanne cares about the costume as a pattern and the figure as a block of color, and in that spirit the diamonds against the blue-green room, and the patchwork floor, make a bold modern design. As a character study it fails; as a construction of color it succeeds."},"nga:93044":{"d":"Shown from the thighs up, a boy in a crimson-red waistcoat stands against swags of drapery painted in white, sky blue, harvest yellow and sage green. His pale face has blushing cheeks, pursed lips and topaz-blue eyes that gaze down to the viewer's right; his dark hair is tucked behind one ear under a brown wide-brimmed hat. He wears a slate-blue shirt and olive-green trousers, with one hand on his hip and the other arm hanging straight, the fingertips almost brushing the bottom edge. Dark blue outlines his body, and the brushstrokes are choppy.","i":"Cézanne painted the same youth in several versions between 1888 and 1890, and the model is said to have been a young Italian named Michelangelo di Rosa. The hand-on-hip stance echoes older portrait traditions, but the long hanging arm is stretched beyond natural proportion, a change usually read as a deliberate adjustment for the balance of the picture and not as a mistake.","c":"The red waistcoat is the strongest color note and a sure anchor, set off by the cool blue-greens of the drapery. The boy's guarded face and awkward stance give the picture an unusual, quiet tension, and the elongated arm turns the figure into a kind of column. Some will find the distortion willful; it is better seen as proof that for Cézanne the harmony of the canvas outranked fidelity to anatomy."},"met:435875":{"d":"A woman is shown in a vertical portrait about 92 by 73 cm. The sitter is Hortense Fiquet (1850-1922), the painter's wife, and the setting, per the title, is a conservatory, probably that of the family estate near Aix-en-Provence. The museum's keywords record only the sitter and the portrait. It is a sober likeness painted in 1891, in the manner Cézanne adopted for his domestic portraits: a still, frontal sitter held within a structure of colour and form.","i":"Cézanne painted Hortense repeatedly, and the portraits are notable for their lack of flattery or gesture. She sits as a model for study, not as a lady on display, and the pictures show his interest in the face and figure as structure. The conservatory probably ties the portrait to the Jas de Bouffan, the family house, though the marriage, which saw long separations, is only dimly legible. Sitters were required to be patient; the stillness of the picture reflects that discipline.","c":"There is great seriousness in Cézanne's portraits of his wife, and this one carries it: the figure has a monumental calm, and the paint is built with deliberate economy. It is not a psychological portrait in the conventional sense, and viewers who look for personality may find the sitter withheld. But that restraint is exactly what lets the picture work as a study of form, and the portrait stands with the best of his figure paintings of the 1890s."},"met:435868":{"d":"Three men sit around a plain pale-blue table, absorbed in a game of cards, while a fourth stands behind. At the left, in a brown coat and a broad hat, a man leans forward over the table; at the centre, in a pale shirt and black hat, another holds his cards to his chest; at the right, in a heavy blue coat, a third sits in profile. The standing man, in a blue smock with a clay pipe in his mouth, looks on with folded arms. A rack of long pipes hangs on the wall behind, with an orange-ochre curtain at the right. A few cards and a white pipe lie on the table. The colours are blue-grey, brown and ochre, and the brushwork is broad and solid.","i":"Cézanne painted five Card Players in the early 1890s, and this is the four-figure version in the Met. The models were local farmhands, some of whom worked on the Cézanne family estate, the Jas de Bouffan, near Aix-en-Provence. The picture was long thought to be the second version, but X-rays reveal layers of graphite underdrawing, and scholars now suggest it was a preliminary for the larger versions. The subject is no drama of gambling: no money, no quarrel, only silent concentration. Cézanne also painted each man separately in studies, and the difficulty of fitting them together on one canvas may show in the spacing.","c":"Cézanne removes every trace of anecdote and makes the card game a solemn ritual. The figures are built like blocks, the blue coat on the right is a magnificent slab of colour, and the lack of expression gives the scene weight. But the group is stiff, the men hardly interact, and the spacing and the hard-edged table suggest how hard it was to unite them. Compared with the more concentrated two-player versions, this one can seem diffuse. It matters as a step toward those, and as a sober picture of rural men at leisure."},"met:435883":{"d":"Apples and pears are set out for a still life on a horizontal canvas about 45 by 59 cm. The museum's keywords record only the fruit and the genre, so the picture is characterised by type: a compact arrangement painted around 1891-92, when Cézanne had settled into the method of his mature still lifes, each piece of fruit built from adjacent touches of colour and each placed in relation to the others rather than to a single viewing position.","i":"In his fruit pictures Cézanne reworked the oldest of still-life subjects, but without the old symbolism. Apples and pears, which in earlier art could speak of temptation or of abundance, are here treated as forms with weight and colour. In 1904 he advised a younger painter to treat nature by the cylinder, the sphere and the cone, and a pile of fruit shows that thinking in its plainest form: a small architecture of rounded solids.","c":"At this date Cézanne could make a modest pile of fruit feel monumental, and the small scale of the canvas does not diminish that. The drawing is firm without outline, the colour is tied to the shape of each object, and the whole has a sober balance. There is little drama, and viewers who prefer his more complex tabletops with cloths and vessels may find it plain, but its plainness is the achievement."},"met:435874":{"d":"A house and trees stand in a vertical landscape about 80 by 64 cm. The title records cracks in the walls, and the museum's keywords record only the house, trees and landscape. Painted in 1892-94, it belongs to the countryside around Aix-en-Provence that Cézanne treated as his own. The picture is built in oil on canvas from solid patches of colour in which the damaged house is part of the landscape structure, not an isolated picturesque feature.","i":"A cracked, weathered house is a subject a Romantic painter might have used for pathos or ruin. Cézanne uses it differently, as a block of wall planes catching light, rooted in the structure of the hillside. The title records a visible fact, damaged plaster, but the interest is the way old walls, trees and ground interlock into a stable picture. It shows his habit of choosing humble motifs and treating them with monumental seriousness.","c":"This is a late, controlled landscape in which every touch carries structural duty, and the result is solid but not static. The picture asks patience and does not offer an obvious focal drama, so it can seem reticent beside more famous mountains and bathers. Its achievement is to turn a damaged cottage into a coherent form, and that quiet transformation is characteristic of what makes Cézanne's later landscapes endure."},"met:435881":{"d":"A ginger jar, eggplants, a pear, a melon and a plate share a table spread with a carpet-like cloth, with a branch among the objects, in a horizontal canvas about 72 by 91 cm. The museum's keywords name these things, and the date, 1893-94, places it among the large, richly arranged still lifes of Cézanne's middle years, in which several vessels and fruits are balanced in one composition.","i":"The ginger jar recurs in Cézanne's still lifes of the 1890s as a fixed point, an object whose bulging profile could be tested against round fruit. Eggplants, melon and pear bring different shapes and surfaces together. The crowded table has none of the symbolic burden of Dutch banquet pictures; instead it works as a field of contrasts in which Cézanne tests how colour, shape and the weight of objects can be reconciled without the help of traditional perspective.","c":"This is a rich, deliberately tilting still life, and its success lies in the way varied objects are reconciled into a single, weighty arrangement. The patterned cloth could have become a distraction, but it serves as a rhythmic base. The picture is more assertive than the simple fruit pieces, and also more demanding to read. For those who find his plain apples austere, this is the more generous Cézanne."},"met:435882":{"d":"A white cloth is spread over a table, its folds rising and falling in angular hills, and heaped with apples in red, yellow, orange and green. More apples lie on the dark table-top at the left. At the upper right a pot of primroses, with large crinkled dark-green leaves and clusters of pale pink flowers, stands in a cream-coloured jar at the edge of the cloth. Behind is a flat turquoise wall, with a darker blue-green corner at the right edge. The brushwork is in broad, visible strokes of blue-green, white and warm orange. The table is slightly tilted, so the fruit seems to slide. Oil on canvas, about 73 by 92 centimetres.","i":"Cézanne painted this about 1890. The Met notes that he used this table, with its scalloped apron and bowed legs, for three of his finest still lifes of the 1890s, and that he rarely painted flowering plants or cut bouquets, including potted plants in only three still lifes. It describes the tablecloth and apples as rising and falling in variegated hillocks of a lush new territory, the apples' solidity tied to their spherical form. The painting was once owned by Claude Monet, an ardent gardener. The shifting viewpoints that make the table tilt are usually read as deliberate: Cézanne built form from colour and from looking at the subject from more than one position.","c":"This is a still life in which almost nothing is conventionally right and everything is convincing. The apples have a solid, painted weight; the white cloth is built in blues, greens and pinks as much as in white; the primrose leaves add a bold dark note. A viewer who wants a faithful table may be baffled by the tipping planes and unresolved perspective. But the instability is the energy, and the flat turquoise wall makes the fruit glow. It is a quiet, intelligent picture that deserves its place in Cézanne's late style."},"nga:46579":{"d":"A glass bottle, a glass carafe, a stemmed glass, several pieces of fruit and white and blue cloths are arranged on a wooden table in this horizontal still life. The bottle with its crimson label and the carafe with a tall, narrow neck and rounded body stand at the center, and the straight-sided stemmed glass sits just in front. Red and yellow fruit are scattered on the white cloth, and a muted blue tablecloth patterned with scrolls and leaves bunches around the objects. The wall behind is mottled in cool blues and greens, and short brushstrokes are visible throughout.","i":"The title comes from the bottle with the red label, which presumably held peppermint liqueur. In the still lifes of the 1890s Cézanne arranged ordinary objects from his studio on tilted tables with draperies that rise in folds like landscapes, and let the shapes and viewpoints shift slightly so that the picture reads as a stable whole, not a snapshot. The museum dates this one to about 1893 to 1895.","c":"Perspective is bent for the sake of the picture: the tabletop tilts, the cloth rises like terrain, the bottle leans. Paradoxically the result is more stable than a photographic arrangement would be. Cool blues play against warm fruit, and the short strokes keep the surface lively. It is a mature, assured performance, though its very control makes it less immediate than his smaller, rougher studies."},"met:437990":{"d":"A man is shown seated in a vertical portrait of about 55 by 45 cm. The museum's keywords record the man and the portrait, and the title names him a peasant. Painted about 1892-96, it belongs to Cézanne's late figure work, when he used farm workers and locals around Aix-en-Provence as models, and it is related in type to the figures of his Card Players series. The handling is broad and sober, concerned with mass more than with anecdote.","i":"In the 1890s Cézanne found his models among the labourers of the Jas de Bouffan, men who could sit for long hours and whose weathered presence suited his search for something solid and unadorned. Such peasants are not sentimentalised as in Millet or anecdotal as in genre painting; they are treated as forms in space, with dignity and without a story. The sitter probably remains anonymous because the picture concerns a type and a structure, not a biography.","c":"Seated Peasant has the gravity of the Card Players without their narrative, and the quiet bulk of the figure is impressive. The face is held back, and character is conveyed through posture and weight rather than expression. If the picture feels reserved, that is the price of Cézanne's refusal to flatter or dramatise. As a late figure study it shows the same mastery of structure that governs his landscapes and still lifes."},"nga:53121":{"d":"The head and shoulders of a man in profile face right in this small canvas, about 26 by 20 centimeters. His skin is painted in shades of orange with some olive-green tints, and a dark mustache sits under a straight nose; the eye is an indistinct black oval. He wears a tall brown hat, a light shirt, a brown coat or vest and the hint of a black bow tie, with a long white pipe in his mouth. Thick, visible strokes in dark blues and browns make an abstract background.","i":"Cézanne's pipe smokers belong to the early 1890s, when he painted the farmworkers of the Jas de Bouffan, the family estate near Aix, for his series of The Card Players. A man in profile with a long clay pipe appears in several of those studies, and this small canvas may be one. The men posed as types of Provençal workers, not as named individuals.","c":"Small, but with much force: thick streaks of orange and olive build a head in profile as if carved, and a single black oval serves for the eye. It is a study of presence and not of personality. The roughness is part of its strength, though at this scale it feels as much like an idea for a larger picture as a finished work."},"met:435880":{"d":"Rocks and forest fill a horizontal canvas about 73 by 92 cm. The museum's keywords record only the forest and the landscape, and the title places the scene in the forest of Fontainebleau, south-east of Paris, a classic painters' ground famed for its boulder-strewn terrain. Dated to the 1890s, the picture belongs to the late period when Cézanne turned from open views to close studies of rock, trees and ground, built in oil from layered patches of colour.","i":"Fontainebleau had been the training ground of the Barbizon painters and, later, of Monet, Renoir and Sisley, so a forest scene there places Cézanne in a long tradition. He was drawn less to the picturesque than to the geology: stone, roots and shadow as masses to be organised. Pictures of rocks and quarries occupied him through the 1890s, and in their dense, closed spaces some later painters found a path toward abstraction.","c":"Cézanne's late forest and rock paintings are among his most demanding, and this one is no exception: with little horizon and no clear focus, the eye must work through the surface. The result is dense, and some canvases of this kind were left unfinished, which is part of their raw interest. As a record of a painter thinking in colour and mass it is compelling, though not an easy picture."},"nga:53123":{"d":"Seen from above, nine pieces of fruit, a pitcher, a goblet and a dish rest on a rustic wooden table in front of a floral curtain, in a nearly square, loosely painted canvas. Five marigold-orange fruits with yellow highlights and scarlet shading are piled in a tipped dish at the center; a lemon and two more fruits sit to the left, and one fruit is tucked behind. A stemmed glass with a tall, rounded bowl stands behind the dish, and a tall, tapering pitcher stands to the left. Everything is outlined in cobalt blue.","i":"In his last decade Cézanne's still lifes became more crowded and decorative, with rustic tables, patterned curtains and fruit piled in tipped dishes that seem to slide toward the viewer. He painted the same props over and over, and the point of each picture lay in discovering a new balance among them. The museum dates this one to about 1900, a few years before his death in 1906.","c":"The tilting dish and the table that runs off the left edge give this still life real energy, and the oranges glow against the blue contours and the pink-peach curtain. There is an element of overcrowding, with fruit, curtain and door all competing, and the drawing wanders. Still, in its sheer liveliness and richness of color it is characteristic of Cézanne at the turn of the century."},"met:441359":{"d":"A man sits beside a horse in this Orientalist picture, about 65 by 54 centimeters, dated possibly to about 1850 to 1858. The man and the horse are its only subjects. Dedreux was above all a painter of horses, and an Eastern theme gave him the chance to paint a favorite subject, the Arabian breed, with a Romantic aura of nobility and freedom. Dedreux died in 1860, so the picture comes from his last years.","i":"Arabian horses were prized in nineteenth-century France as the finest breed, and the Arab man with his mount became a symbol of the East's imagined virtues: nobility, freedom, harmony with animals. After the French conquest of Algeria began in 1830, painters took up such subjects in numbers. Dedreux, a society painter of horses for the aristocracy, used the Eastern theme as a vehicle for his true subject, the animal.","c":"Dedreux's horses are always the better half of his pictures: elegantly drawn, with a sense of breeding and a fine finish. The man is typically less carefully characterized, and Oriental trappings add exoticism rather than ethnographic truth. It is a polished example of the Romantic cult of the horse, owing much to Géricault, but lacking Géricault's anatomical force and passion."},"met:436182":{"d":"A small mythological battle picture, about 51 by 66 centimeters, in which soldiers, women and horses are caught up in battle. The title points to a victory of the Amazons, the legendary nation of women warriors of Greek myth, so the action is presumably a crowded cavalry engagement rather than a quiet allegory. It is oil on canvas, dated to the 1620s, by Claude Déruet, who was court painter in Nancy to the dukes of Lorraine. Pictures of this kind suited court taste, which enjoyed fanciful stories and horsemanship.","i":"The Amazons were a mythical people of warrior women who fought Greek heroes such as Heracles, Theseus and Achilles. Their stories gave painters a reason to show women in armor and horses in motion. The title alone does not reveal which episode, if any, is meant, so the scene may be a general celebration of Amazon victory and not one recorded battle. Its companion, Departure of the Amazons, probably continues the same story.","c":"Late Mannerism at the Lorraine court favored elegant, elongated figures and decorative pageantry, and a cavalry subject suits that taste. The picture is probably best judged as one of a pair with the Departure: lively and ornamental rather than weighty. Déruet is a charming rather than a profound painter, and small works like this show the playful side of a court artist better known for large allegorical series."},"met:436183":{"d":"A companion in size and subject to Triumph of the Amazons, this small oil on canvas, about 51 by 66 centimeters, shows Amazons, the women warriors of Greek myth, with horses in a landscape. The title speaks of setting out, not of fighting, so the emphasis presumably falls on the troop assembling and riding out through open country. It was painted in the 1620s by Claude Déruet, court painter to the dukes of Lorraine at Nancy.","i":"Amazons appear in Greek myth as a tribe of warrior women, met in combat by Heracles, Theseus and, in the Trojan story, Achilles. A departure lets a painter show costume, horses and landscape without bloodshed, and, set beside a triumph, it makes a before-and-after of campaign and victory. That the two pictures were made as a pair is a reasonable inference from their matching size and subject, not a documented fact.","c":"Déruet's strength was elegant, decorative storytelling rather than close study of nature, and a riding party in a landscape plays to it. The result is the lighter half of the pair, pleasing in its pageantry, though the setting probably serves as a stage for the riders rather than as a place observed for itself. A courtly entertainment, well suited to a small room, and not a searching work."},"nga:46166":{"d":"A small tempera panel, about 24 by 19.5 centimeters, showing Christ with the disciples Peter and John at the moment he sends them ahead to prepare the Passover meal that will become the Last Supper. The subject belongs to the Life of Christ, and the modest upright format suggests one episode in a longer sequence, perhaps part of an altarpiece predella or a devotional cycle, rather than a picture made to stand alone. It is dated 1504 and is the work of Vincenzo Civerchio, a painter active in northern Italy.","i":"Luke's Gospel names Peter and John as the two disciples sent into Jerusalem; Mark leaves them unnamed. They are told to follow a man carrying a jar of water to a house, where the owner will show them a large upper room, already furnished, for the meal. The episode is a quiet prelude to the Last Supper and the Passion, and it shows Christ's foreknowledge of events. Narrative cycles that include it use it as a hinge between the entry into Jerusalem and the supper.","c":"A small episode from a longer story, by a regional painter: its interest lies in what it shows of north Italian devotional painting around 1500 rather than in invention. The subject, an errand before the drama, offers little to excite a painter, so panels like this stand or fall on clarity of gesture and crispness of tempera handling. Civerchio belongs among the sound Lombard painters of his generation."},"met:436208":{"d":"An allegory of the stages of life is set in a pastoral landscape. The figures recorded are a couple, a boy and a man, with sheep and a tree, and the canvas, about 78 by 112 centimetres, has the wide horizontal shape of the Venetian pastoral pictures that the Ferrarese painter Dosso Dossi knew. The title and these figures point to the usual division of life, with the courting pair standing for youth, the child for infancy and the older man for age. The museum record gives few other details, so poses and colours cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"The ages-of-man theme reminded viewers that life passes: love and youth give way to age, and the child begins the cycle again. Pastoral settings, with shepherds and sheep, were fashionable for this reflection in Venice and Ferrara around 1515. Titian's picture on the same theme, made at about the same time, is the most famous example, and Dosso, who worked for the same patrons, offers a variation. The mood is meant to be contemplative, even melancholy.","c":"A graceful piece of Venetian-Ferrarese poetry: the pastoral setting and soft light show Dosso's love of landscape, which is often the strength of his pictures. The allegory is comparatively simple and does not reach the puzzle of Titian's famous version, and the figures can seem stiff beside the trees and sky. Its charm lies in its warm, reflective mood rather than its drama."},"nga:220445":{"d":"A landscape of green hills crowned with castle-like buildings fills the canvas, with a crowd of figures in red, brown and blue gathered at the lower left. The figures are small and sketchily painted, without visible faces. Between the groups stand two objects, a low cream-coloured one and a square brown one, perhaps the tomb and altar of the title. A large building right of centre appears to be under construction, and trees are scattered across hills and plain. At the left, building-topped hills are separated from the land by strips of blue, as though wrapped in mist or floating in the sky.","i":"The two subjects of the title come from the fifth book of Virgil's Aeneid: after the games held in honour of his dead father Anchises, Aeneas has a temple to Venus founded on the heights of Eryx in Sicily and appoints a priest to tend his father's tomb. The picture was probably one of a group of Aeneid scenes made for the Este court in Ferrara around 1520, where Dosso served Duke Alfonso I. Humanist patrons knew the poem well enough to identify a scene from a few clues.","c":"This is a picture of atmosphere rather than incident. The mist-wrapped hills with their castles, bathed in blue, are beautifully fanciful, and the toy-like figures hint at a festival without needing to describe it. As narrative it is thin, since the building site and the offerings are hard to pick out, and the figures are barely sketched. Still, it is an engaging example of how Ferrarese painters turned epic into pastoral fantasy."},"nga:391":{"d":"On a spit of land at the right, two men in vividly striped clothing, one in a red-and-yellow turban with a sword on his shoulder, stand close to the viewer; beyond them, across a stretch of water, a dozen men work on two wooden ships. At the left, under feathery trees, about two dozen men, women and children in aquamarine, marigold, burgundy and white gather, several turned towards a woman in blue holding an infant. A town of tan stone towers glows on the far shore, mirrored in the blue water, while a ship with a billowing white sail passes and high peaks fade into blue haze.","i":"The subject comes from Virgil's Aeneid: after the funeral games for his father Anchises in Sicily, Aeneas's Trojan followers had their fleet damaged by fire, and while the ships were repaired the weary were left behind to found a settlement. The two men in front are probably Aeneas and his companion Achates, in the showy military dress of Dosso's own day, and the group under the trees may be those left behind. The picture is probably one of a series of Aeneid scenes made for the Este court in Ferrara.","c":"Dosso is at his most inventive in such small mythological narratives: the glowing town across the water, the fluttering pennant, the bright costumes and the misty blue peaks make an image closer to a fairy tale than to epic, and the colour is very beautiful. The narrative is almost lost in the pleasure of the scene, however; without the title, a viewer could not tell what is going on. That is a fair price for a picture made for charm."},"nga:532":{"d":"A woman sits outdoors, looking towards the right, in a pinkish-red gown with a green cloak around her skirts. Her dark brown hair is drawn back under a large gold headdress, and a halo of small golden dots circles her head. In one hand she holds a golden palm frond, the martyr's sign; with the other she points to an open book on her knees. At the left a grey building stands in shadow, with a tall grey statue in a niche; at the right are green trees and distant blue buildings under a dark blue sky that lightens at the horizon.","i":"The palm marks her as a martyr, while the book and the pointing finger present her as a woman of learning and faith whose witness lies in the word. Saint Lucretia is a rarely painted virgin martyr, and the picture was probably made for a patron who held her in particular devotion, though the commission is not recorded. The statue in the niche may allude to the pagan idols that such martyrs refused to worship.","c":"A wonderfully strange small panel, characteristic of Dosso: the large golden headdress, the pink and green drapery and the dark sky give the saint an unreal, theatrical presence, while the statue in shadow adds a hint of mystery. The pointing pose is a little stiff, and the narrative signals are slight; she seems less a martyr than a sitter from Dosso's own Ferrara. But that dreamlike glow is what makes him interesting beside more orthodox contemporaries."},"nga:12147":{"d":"A pale, nearly nude young woman perches on a rock beneath a tree, ringed by dogs, a doe, a buck and birds. She faces the viewer but looks away to the left, lips parted, an olive-green drape across one thigh and white, yellow and crimson flowers crowning her long blond hair. She cradles a stone tablet in one arm and touches its inscribed surface; a large book of diagrams lies open by her foot. A wading bird dips its beak in a pool, a hawk perches in the branches and an owl sits above her head. Behind, a sandy path leads towards a wooden building among rust-red and gold trees.","i":"Circe, in Homer's Odyssey, is the sorceress who turned Odysseus's men into swine; later poets made her a seductress who changed her lovers into beasts. Dosso's animals are presumably those lovers. The book of diagrams and inscribed tablet are tools of magic, and the owl is an emblem of wisdom and of night. The picture was probably painted for the court of Ferrara, where the Este princes enjoyed learned poetic subjects such as Ariosto's Orlando Furioso, whose enchantresses some scholars connect with this scene.","c":"One of Dosso's most captivating inventions: the half-clothed enchantress is surrounded by a menagerie that is both comic and slightly sinister, and the soft golden light, glowing trees and hazy distance give an air of poetic reverie that few Italian painters could match. The picture's meaning escapes, which is probably intended; its detail is wittily teasing, and the handling relaxed. It succeeds as a mood and a spell rather than as a story."},"met:15204":{"d":"A woman's likeness, painted in watercolour on a thin sheet of ivory and measuring only about 7 by 5 cm, small enough to rest in the palm and to be fitted into a case, frame or locket. The museum records only the sitter, a woman, and the date, 1817, so nothing is claimed about her dress or features. The type is the portrait miniature: a head-and-shoulders likeness built from fine, small touches, exchanged and treasured by families.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the intimate counterpart of oil portraits. They passed between lovers and families, were worn on the body or kept in a drawer, and often served as memorials of the absent or dead. A lady's miniature of 1817 might mark an engagement or a marriage, or simply keep a face for relatives far away. The sitter is unnamed in the title, so her rank cannot be read, but the form shows she mattered to someone.","c":"A miniature is judged on the delicacy of its handling and the credibility of the likeness, and this belongs to the polite, fluent tradition of Regency miniature painting rather than to any personal experiment. Ivory gave a luminous ground that suited fine stippling and clear flesh tones. Within that convention it is competent and conventional, a good example of the small portraits that photography would soon replace."},"met:15205":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in watercolour on ivory, in a miniature measuring about 6 by 5 cm and dated 1822. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, so the description stays to the type: a small, close likeness of the head and shoulders, made to be held in the hand or set in a frame, with the translucent ivory ground doing much of the work of the complexion.","i":"Miniatures of this kind were commissioned for private use: gifts between husband and wife or parent and child, souvenirs for travellers, mementos of the dead. The sitter's identity is lost in the title, which calls her only a lady, but the commission itself says that her family could afford a specialist and wanted her kept close. The style changed little across these years, since sitters wanted a polished, flattering image, not novelty.","c":"Thomson's miniatures belong to a trade that valued finish and likeness above individual expression, and this one is typical. The surface on ivory can be exquisite, but the formula of pose and costume is familiar, and the sitter is hard to distinguish from many others. It is a skilled piece of professional craft, better regarded as part of a body of work than as a unique picture."},"met:15206":{"d":"A gentleman's portrait in watercolour on ivory, only about 6 by 5 cm, dated about 1825. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, so the description rests on the type: a head-and-shoulders miniature meant to be carried or framed, with the pale ivory ground showing through thin colour to give the flesh its glow. Like all such miniatures it is a private object, made for a family rather than for public display.","i":"Gentlemen's miniatures served as marks of affection or loyalty, given to a wife or a sweetheart, to parents, or to friends, and sometimes worn on a watch chain or in a case. The unnamed sitter cannot be placed socially, but the commission itself implies means and a wish for permanence. Around 1825 the portrait miniature was still in demand, though cheaper silhouettes and, within two decades, photographs would draw away much of that trade.","c":"A male sitter allowed less decorative display than a lady's dress and hair, so the success of the likeness rests on the face and on a restrained use of colour. In that narrow field, Thomson's work is reliable and polished rather than inspired. The picture is best taken as a good representative of an art at its commercial peak, charming for its scale and craftsmanship if not for any individual invention."},"met:13331":{"d":"A boy stands in a tall portrait, about 135 by 91 cm, with a dog beside him and a shuttlecock in play. The museum's keywords record the boy, the dog, a game and the shuttlecock, so the picture is a child's portrait of the 1770s with the toys and pet of a comfortable household as signs of the sitter's upbringing. The canvas is large enough for a full-length figure, a format used for the children of families with money and a taste for display.","i":"The title says the boy is probably of the Crossfield family, so the identification is a guess. Battledore and shuttlecock was a fashionable game, and a child shown at play with a dog told viewers of a healthy, well-managed childhood, in the spirit of Locke's educational ideas, which favoured exercise and amusement. In colonial and British portraiture a child's portrait also announced the family's rank and hopes, since an heir was the future of the line.","c":"William Williams was a provincial painter who worked in colonial Philadelphia and New York before returning to England, and the picture has the charm and the stiffness of that background: the figure is clearly lit and clearly outlined, but anatomy and space are only roughly handled. Compared with the polished child portraits of Reynolds it is naive. That frankness is part of its appeal, and as a record of colonial taste it has real interest."},"met:438662":{"d":"A man in Ottoman dress is shown in the artist's studio in this small oil of about 33 by 24 centimeters, painted between about 1823 and 1826 by the French artist Auguste-Xavier Leprince. The recorded motifs are the man, the portrait and the Orientalist theme. In the years of the Greek War of Independence and the Romantic fashion for the Near East, Parisian artists were fascinated by visitors and models in Turkish or Greek costume, whom they painted in their own studios.","i":"The Orient, as Romantic France imagined it, was a world of rich costume, color and unfamiliar customs; Delacroix, Gros and many others fed the taste in the 1820s. A foreign visitor posing in the artist's studio is a bridge between that imaginary East and Paris, the exotic brought indoors. The sitter is unnamed, and the title uses the word Ottoman for the empire he belonged to. Leprince died young in 1826, so this belongs to the last years of a short career.","c":"A small studio picture rather than a finished portrait, it has the ease and curiosity of a young painter looking at an exotic sitter: the richly colored costume, the solid, attentive figure, the simple setting. Leprince was chiefly a landscape and street painter, and this is an offshoot. It is a little work, but one that shows the Romantic eye turning even the studio into a window to the East."},"met:436273":{"d":"A man's likeness painted on a small sheet of ivory, dated about 1780. The museum records only the man and the portrait and gives no size, but miniatures by Engleheart are typically small enough to hold in the palm, and were set in gold cases, frames or lockets. The picture is therefore characterised by type: a head-and-shoulders portrait built from fine touches of watercolour, with the face the focus and the dress and background kept simple, as in the best English miniatures of the late eighteenth century.","i":"Miniatures of the 1780s were personal tokens, exchanged by engaged couples, given to relatives or kept as souvenirs of the absent, and Engleheart was one of the leading miniaturists working in London in the later Georgian period. The sitter here is unnamed, so his rank and calling cannot be read. What the form tells us is that someone valued his face enough to pay for a hand-held likeness, which in these years was the usual way to carry affection or memory.","c":"Engleheart's reputation rests on clean, light modelling of the face and a quiet, truthful likeness rather than on show, and a portrait of this kind is representative. It lacks the dash of Cosway's fashionable, vaporous miniatures, and it is the more trustworthy for that. The limit is a certain sameness from one sitter to the next, since he worked over many years to a successful formula; within it, this is solid and unpretentious."},"met:436274":{"d":"A woman is shown head and shoulders in a miniature on ivory, wearing a cap with a bow, a fichu, the light kerchief worn around the neck and shoulders, and a ruffle. The museum gives no size, but the form is a hand-held keepsake, dated 1783. The sitter, born in 1720, was in her early sixties, and the costume is that of a respectable married woman of mature years, painted in fine, small touches of watercolour with the face as the centre of the tiny field.","i":"The sitter was Elizabeth Colden, wife of Peter De Lancey, a member of one of the leading families of colonial New York. A portrait by a London miniaturist in 1783 shows how far the Atlantic ties of such families extended. The cap, fichu and ruffle mark a woman of age and standing, dressed with decorum rather than fashion. Such miniatures kept the faces of parents and relatives close to children who lived far away.","c":"Engleheart's gift for a plain, truthful likeness suits a sitter of her age and station, and the little picture has a quiet dignity: he does not flatter, and he does not caricature. The costume details of cap, bow and ruffle give the surface variety without fuss. It is less glamorous than the fashionable portraits of younger sitters, and none the worse for it. The strength is honesty; the limit, as often with him, is polite reticence about character."},"met:436275":{"d":"A man identified only as Colonel Woodford is portrayed in a miniature on ivory, probably of 1788. The museum records just the man and the portrait and gives no size or description, so the picture is characterised by type: a small, hand-held likeness of an army officer, painted in watercolour on ivory in the fine, stippled manner of London miniaturists, and made as the kind of portrait that could be carried on service or left with family at home.","i":"A colonel's miniature served several purposes: a keepsake for a wife or family left behind when he was posted away, a gift between friends, a modest mark of rank and standing. The title does not say which Woodford is meant, so any fuller identification would be a guess. In the late 1780s a commission of this kind was a normal way for a man of some means to be remembered, and for his career to be announced in a form that could be worn or kept.","c":"Engleheart is at his most characteristic when he is least showy: clear, even light, a composed face and no fuss about rank. If this is typical, its virtue is an unforced likeness; its limit is a certain sameness from one sitter to the next, since he turned out miniatures in great numbers. The portrait is a reliable example of a fashionable craftsman's work rather than a revelation, and the sitter's character is conveyed with economy."},"met:436277":{"d":"A man is shown in a miniature on ivory, dated to about 1805. The museum calls the sitter only a man said to be a Mr. De Wolf, and records no size or description, so the picture is characterised by type: a small, hand-held likeness painted in watercolour on ivory, the face carefully modelled and the dress and background kept plain, as in Engleheart's work of the early nineteenth century.","i":"The words said to be mark a traditional, unverified identification, so the sitter's name should be treated as uncertain. By the early 1800s the fashion for powdered hair and elaborate coats had given way to plainer, more natural styles, and miniatures changed with it. A miniature remained a private commission, a gift, a keepsake or a memorial, made to be carried, kept in a drawer or handed down in a family.","c":"By 1805 Engleheart was in his mid-fifties and working at ease with a style refined over three decades. He kept to a clear, level light and plain drawing while newer painters pursued showier effects, and the result is sober and reliable rather than exciting. The best of his late miniatures keep the honesty of the earlier work and lose some of its stiffness; judged by type, this is a competent, characteristic example."},"met:10186":{"d":"A street scene from Japan: an ameya, or candy seller, works at his trade at the roadside, the Met's keyword being amezaiku, the craft of shaping sweets from sugar, and a woman and an infant are also shown. Painted by 1893 in oil on canvas, about 64 by 79 cm, it is a small exhibition picture rather than a sketch, the product of an American painter's stay in Japan and a record of everyday work in the open street.","i":"Blum went to Japan in 1890, partly as an illustrator for American magazines, and stayed about two years, and the pictures he brought back were among the first by an American painter drawn from long experience of the country. They belong to the Japonisme then fashionable in America and Europe, but he mostly recorded street life rather than borrowing Japanese style. The candy seller was a familiar figure to children, which makes the scene humane and affectionate.","c":"Blum's Japanese pictures combine a sparkling touch that recalls Fortuny with a feeling for design that recalls Whistler, and this one is lively and sympathetically seen. The charm is partly that of an outsider's delight: it records rather than probes, and the subject can read as picturesque. As a document of a particular moment in American encounters with Japan it is valuable, and as a picture it is attractive and accomplished."},"met:10190":{"d":"A view out of a window onto trees and buildings fills a vertical canvas about 57 by 50 cm. The museum's keywords record only the trees and the buildings, and the title makes the viewpoint the painter's own window on Grove Street, probably in New York's Greenwich Village. It is a small, informal oil of about 1900, a painter looking out at a view he knew well rather than staging a grand scene.","i":"The window view is a modest, private subject with a long tradition, from Dutch interiors to the Impressionists' balconies. For American painters around 1900 the city's backyards and rooftops offered a new kind of landscape, close to home and free from the vast wilderness of earlier generations. The picture makes no claim to a story; its pleasure lies in the quiet act of looking out from a studio, and in the small facts of trees against buildings.","c":"A small late picture by an artist more famous for brilliantly coloured street scenes, this is quieter, a study of tone and structure rather than anecdote. Its appeal is the unforced way a few trees and walls are given order and light. It has less of the dazzle of his Japanese pictures and none of their narrative, and some viewers will find it slight, but the modesty is part of its honesty."},"cma:135483":{"d":"Classical ruins stand among rocks in a large horizontal landscape about 144 by 177 cm, where shepherds idle and light plays in iridescent reflections. Dark, dramatic rocks rise along the left. The museum dates it to about 1640, soon after Rosa left Naples for Rome in 1635, and sees in it the pastoral mood and the beauty of the Bay of Naples that first made his name. It is an oil on canvas on a gallery scale, meant for a collector's wall.","i":"Ruins in a rocky wilderness belong to a long Italian tradition in which the remains of antiquity stand for the passing of empires, while the pastoral life of the shepherds goes on beside them. In Rome Rosa met a new kind of landscape painting that stressed light and atmosphere, and he gave it a rougher, more dramatic edge. Viewers could enjoy the picturesque scene and, if they wished, the old moral about time and ruin.","c":"The picture shows Rosa's early manner, closer to the idyllic landscapes of the Roman school than to the wild scenes of his maturity, though the dark rocks at the left already hint at what is coming. The ruins and shepherds are conventional furniture, and the composition lacks Claude's refinement, but the drama of the crags and the play of light give it life. It is interesting chiefly as a first step toward his sublime landscapes."},"met:437508":{"d":"Salvator Rosa looks out from a vertical self-portrait about 99 by 79 cm, made around 1647, when he was in his early thirties. The museum's keywords record a skull and writing, props that suggest a thinker rather than a craftsman: the skull is a memento mori, and writing is the tool of the poet and satirist. It is a deliberately composed statement of identity, painted in oil on canvas, not a casual likeness.","i":"Rosa wanted to be known as a poet, satirist, actor and philosopher as much as a painter, and his self-portraits present him in that light. A skull is the old emblem of mortality, the object a stoic philosopher keeps in mind, and writing marks him as an author. In the 1640s he worked in Florence in learned circles, and the picture advertises his learning and independence at a time when painters were still struggling to be seen as more than craftsmen.","c":"The portrait works through attitude: the sitter's self-possession and the grave props do more than any display of technique. The performance is a little theatrical, as self-fashioning always is, but it convinces because the painter's gaze does too. Among Baroque self-portraits it is notable for intellectual ambition rather than for polish, and it tells us more about how Rosa wished to be judged than about how he looked."},"cma:149104":{"d":"A set of four round canvases, painted in oil about 1645-49, shows witchcraft at four times of day: Morning, Day, Evening and Night. The museum notes that together they range from the beautiful enchantress to the old crone to the male sorcerer, and show activities long associated with black magic: levitation, love potions, devil worship, the invocation of demons and transformation. Each is a dark, crowded fantasy, and the circular format links them to the circle used in magic.","i":"Interest in witchcraft rose sharply in the 1500s, but by the mid-1600s, at least among the cultured elite of Florence, a reaction set in against the many accusations of sorcery, and artists and writers explored the subject out of curiosity and amusement. Rosa, poet, painter and satirist, was foremost among them. Transformation, a common subject in Italian art through Ovid's Metamorphoses, gave him a way to draw attention to his own power to turn paint and canvas into a nightmare world.","c":"As a set the scenes show Rosa's rare mixture of learning, humour and horror, and they remain among the strangest pictures of the Italian Baroque. Their weaknesses are those of intellectual wit: the programme is complicated, and viewers must know the literature to catch every joke. But the invention is fresh, the mood changes from scene to scene, and the cycle supports his reputation as a painter of the extraordinary rather than the ideal."},"cma:149105":{"d":"At dawn a young witch plunges her knife into a writhing amphibian, in a round canvas where dark daybreak clouds and crags that resemble human forms set a gloomy tone. Malevolent, sharp-beaked birds hover around the stabbing, and among them rise upraised human hands, made more disturbing by the witch's calm expression. The museum calls her the only beautiful enchantress Rosa ever painted. It is an oil on canvas of about 1645-49, its circular shape chosen to recall the circle used in magic.","i":"The elegant young sorceress evokes Circe, the enchantress who turned men into animals, but Rosa breaks with the classical image by making her an explicitly violent witch. In the mid-1600s the cultured elite of Florence had grown sceptical about witch trials, and artists and writers treated witchcraft with curiosity and amusement. Rosa, a poet and satirist as well as a painter, uses the subject to show his own power to conjure a nightmare out of paint.","c":"The picture's best trick is contrast: a face of calm beauty set against an act of cruelty, and a daybreak that should bring hope but brings gloom. The crags that echo the human form add to the unease. Rosa's learned conceit can be heavy, and the viewer needs the story of Circe to catch the point, but the invention is lively and the mood sticks. It is among the odder Baroque treatments of magic."},"cma:149106":{"d":"At noon a party of hoary hags prepares to fly to the witches' Sabbath. They clutch skulls and brooms, and one flays a thrashing lizard to extract the innards needed for magical ointments. In place of the usual goat, an owl, a bird of ill omen, serves as their transport. Set in full daylight, the grotesque scene is painted in oil on a round canvas of about 1645-49, and its lighter palette gives it a comic edge.","i":"Placing hags in broad daylight turns horror into a joke, and the museum links this to the satirical poetry Rosa wrote in Florence and on his return to Rome in 1649. He saw himself as a critic exposing human folly, and witches, brooms, skulls and lizards make a bizarre but safe target. Educated viewers, sceptical of witch-hunts, could enjoy the macabre as entertainment and as a comment on superstition.","c":"Day is the most openly comic of the set, and the lighter palette suits it: ugly old women are grotesque and funny rather than frightening, and the owl is a good joke. It is also the least tense, and may feel slight beside the dark scenes. Rosa shows a flair for caricature closer to the satirist's pen than to the grand manner. A lively, rude picture, valuable for showing his humour."},"cma:149107":{"d":"At dusk a group of hags gather around a cauldron, and above their incantations a conjured skeleton soars, holding an hourglass. In its left hand it grasps a capital letter A from which a plumb line hangs, and a wax effigy appears among the instruments of the spell. The scene is painted in oil on a round canvas of about 1645-49, the circular shape alluding to the circle's role in the practice of magic.","i":"The skeleton's hourglass signals the brevity of life, and the A with its plumb line, which the museum reads as ancient signs of the moon and of judgment, suggests a nocturnal verdict being passed by the witches. The wax effigy points back to Virgil and Horace, who described love magic, and to Rosa's own poem La Strega, in which a jilted woman gathers the ingredients of revenge. Painting was called mute poetry in the 1600s, and Rosa makes the point by painting what he also wrote.","c":"This is the most erudite of the witchcraft scenes and the most crowded with signs: skeleton, hourglass, letter, plumb line, effigy. The learning is the point, since Rosa wanted to be taken as a poet-philosopher, and the picture can feel like an illustrated riddle. But the flying skeleton above the gathered hags gives the composition a strong vertical drama, and the mixture of horror and wit is entirely his own."},"cma:149108":{"d":"In the pitch black of a forest two groups of men face each other. On the left, travellers pause in apprehension to watch a magician conjure terrifying apparitions, and the tall, wizened necromancer stands resolute directly below a classical column. The scene is painted in oil on a round canvas of about 1645-49, the circle being the shape traditionally linked to magic. It is the male counterpart to the enchantress and hags of the other scenes in this set.","i":"Necromancy, communing with the dead, had been associated since the Middle Ages with male sorcerers, and Rosa's tall, resolute necromancer recalls Moses, an old model for the sorcerer in Renaissance art. The museum also sees in the learned magicians a reference to philosophers and to Rosa's own circle in Florence: just as a magician conjures creatures with a wand, the painter conjures a strange world with paint and brush.","c":"With a pitch-black ground the picture lives on a few lit gestures and the travellers' wary pause, which give the viewer a way in. The learned references weigh less here than in the Evening scene because the picture is built on mood rather than on a thicket of symbols. Even so, the narrative is hard to read in the gloom, and the canvas asks a patient eye. It is atmospheric, and the necromancer is a memorable invention."},"met:437506":{"d":"A rocky coast with bandits fills a horizontal canvas about 75 by 100 cm. The museum's keywords record only human figures and the landscape, so the picture is described by type and by the artist's manner: such scenes by Rosa typically set a few armed, ragged men among rugged crags and a wild shoreline under a wide, turbulent sky, painted in oil with a dark, broken touch. It is a moderate-size version of the savage landscape that made his reputation.","i":"Bandits offered seventeenth-century viewers a thrill of danger at a safe distance, and the lawless margins of Italy's mountains and coasts gave a reality behind the fantasy. Early biographers even claimed Rosa had lived among brigands in his youth, a story historians doubt. By the eighteenth century British collectors prized such pictures as images of the sublime, a wild nature that moved the viewer by its menace, and that taste helped to shape the Picturesque.","c":"Rosa's wild coasts are less polished than Claude's ideal harbours, and the better for it: rough handling suits crags and ragged men. When the setting is mere stage furniture, though, the bandits can feel like props, and the effect depends on the quality of sky and rock. As a mid-career picture it shows the type at its most characteristic, if not its most inventive, and it explains why later writers made his name a byword for savage scenery."},"met:437507":{"d":"A sleeping hero, named by the title as Aeneas, appears in a tall vertical canvas about 197 by 121 cm, painted around 1660-65. The Met's keywords record the sword, the sleep and the Aeneid, but not the vision itself, so the picture is described by its subject: a moment from Virgil's epic in which a dream comes to the Trojan hero while he sleeps. It is a large history picture, made for a collector's gallery rather than for a church.","i":"In the Aeneid the gods and spirits often speak to Aeneas in dreams: the ghost of Hector warns him to flee burning Troy, the household gods promise a new home, the shade of his father directs him, and the river god Tiber urges him to seek allies in Italy. Each vision turns him toward his destiny, the founding of Rome's line. Rosa, who treated classical subjects with dark seriousness, chooses a moment of sleep and fate rather than battle.","c":"Rosa's strengths are mood and invention rather than the poised clarity of Poussin's Virgilian scenes, and a sleeping hero with a vision suits him, since the drama lies in atmosphere. The risk is rhetoric: his grand manner can slide into stagecraft. Judged by type, this is an ambitious late canvas that favours gloom over clarity, interesting for its attempt at epic seriousness rather than for a resolved form."},"met:435691":{"d":"A woman in an interior is the subject of this tiny oil on a wood panel, about 18 by 24 cm. The museum's keywords record only the woman and the room, and the title suggests talk or whispered news, so the picture is described by type: a small, polished genre scene of the kind Boldini produced in Paris in the early 1870s, typically bright in colour and quick in touch, made to be held close and sold through the Paris art market as a charming trifle.","i":"Gossip is a favourite theme of genre painting, offering the pleasure of watching a private exchange and the wry thought that it is none of the viewer's business. In the 1870s Paris dealers found a steady market for small, charming figure pictures of elegant women in comfortable rooms, often in eighteenth-century dress, and Boldini, newly arrived from Florence and London, supplied them. The woman's identity is not recorded, and she may be a type rather than a portrait.","c":"Boldini's early genre pieces are among the most dazzling small pictures of their day, with a flickering, assured brush and an eye for fashionable surface. They are also formulaic by design: the market wanted charm, and charm is what it got. As a little panel the picture is delightful, and it shows the speed and dash that later made him the most sought-after society portraitist, without yet carrying that mature weight."},"met:437611":{"d":"The Magi, the wise men of the East, travel with their retinue across a landscape in a small panel about 22 by 30 cm, painted in tempera with gold. The Met's keywords record the Magi, men, a woman, horses and a landscape, and the subject is the journey toward Bethlehem. The size is that of a panel meant to be read closely, part of a larger altarpiece or kept for private devotion, with the glitter of gold leaf set beside tempera's pure, clear colour.","i":"In Matthew's Gospel wise men from the East follow a star to Bethlehem. Medieval tradition made them kings and gave them splendid retinues, a chance for painters to show pageantry, horses and exotic costume. Sienese painters loved the subject, and Sassetta's panel belongs to the courtly taste of the International Gothic. The journey also carried a spiritual meaning: the soul as a pilgrim guided by a light toward Christ.","c":"Few Italian painters could match Sassetta for this kind of poetry: small, elegant figures, a landscape that is half fairy tale, and gold used as light as well as ornament. The little panel feels larger than it is. It has no deep psychology and its space is more decorative than the Florentine kind, but as a vision of travel as wonder it is among the most poetic small pictures of fifteenth-century Siena."},"nga:12102":{"d":"A saint stands against a textured gold ground, her body turned toward us and her head tipped downward to the left beneath an intricately patterned halo. She has round, rosy cheeks and wavy yellow hair, and wears a dark robe over a dark red dress. In one hand she holds a long, thin red cross, in the other a closed red book, and at her feet lies a small black dog-like creature with wide yellow eyes. The tempera panel is about 29 by 11 cm, within a gilded border.","i":"The black creature is the dragon of Margaret's legend: the virgin martyr of Antioch was said to have been swallowed by the devil in dragon form and to have burst free when the cross she carried split the beast open. Cross, book and tamed beast made her easy to recognise, and she was invoked especially by women in childbirth. A panel so narrow was probably one of several saints flanking a larger picture, so the figure was seen as part of a heavenly assembly.","c":"Sassetta paints a saint of unusual gentleness: the downward, listening tilt of the head and the soft cheeks make the figure personal even within a rigid format. The small black dragon, harmless as a dog, is a touch of Sienese humour. The panel can only be a fragment of a larger scheme, and in isolation it lacks the narrative interest of his story pictures, but as a miniature of devotion it is fine and fresh."},"nga:12103":{"d":"A saint stands with her head slightly tilted to the left, her face pale, with arched eyebrows, a straight nose and small lips set in a neutral expression. Short, wavy blonde hair is circled by a thin red crown and a detailed golden halo. She wears a long red robe over a black tunic and holds a long black quill-like object in one hand and, in the other, what looks like a thin staff ending in a red loop. The ornate gold ground is slightly worn, and the tempera panel is about 29 by 11 cm.","i":"Apollonia was an aged virgin martyr of third-century Alexandria whose teeth were broken or pulled out by a mob before she died, and she became the patron saint of toothache and, later, of dentists. Her usual attribute is a pair of pincers gripping a tooth, and the museum's tentative reading of the objects in her hands suggests that the shapes are hard to make out on this small, worn panel. Its narrow format suggests a place among saints flanking a larger altarpiece.","c":"The striking point of this little saint is the contrast between her neutral, composed face and the violence of her martyrdom, which the painter wisely keeps out of sight. Red robe against black tunic and gold is a strong, simple scheme, and the worn gold adds an unplanned patina. As a standing saint it is conventional, and its meaning depends on attributes that are hard to read, but the delicacy of the face shows Sassetta's gentleness."},"nga:387":{"d":"Mary sits on a cushion against a gold ground, her body angled to the left, holding the nude Christ Child on her knee and looking down at him with hooded eyes. She wears a cream-white dress under a midnight-blue cloak lined with green; the Child, seated on a pale pink cloth, touches the clasp of her cloak, and both have punched, patterned halos. Above, a small bearded figure with six wings sends gold rays down to a white bird. The tempera panel is tall and narrow, about 48 by 21 cm, with a pointed top.","i":"A Madonna of Humility sits on a cushion or on the ground rather than on a throne, a pose that speaks of Mary's modesty, since humility is rooted in the Latin word for earth, and was much loved in Franciscan and Sienese devotion. The small winged figure, presumably God the Father, sends rays to a dove, the Holy Spirit, so that the Trinity watches over mother and Son. The child's hand on her clasp makes the dogma tender.","c":"The flesh tinged with green, the baby's touch at the clasp and Mary's lowered gaze give this small devotional picture real tenderness. Sassetta handles the gold ground and punched halos with delicacy, and the colour of cream, midnight blue and forest green is restrained. The type is conventional, a formula repeated in many Sienese workshops, but he makes it individual through quiet intimacy. It is more affecting than grand, and better for being small."},"met:437612":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child in a tall, narrow panel of tempera on a gold ground, about 64 by 34 cm, attended by angels. The Met's keywords record Mary, the Child and the angels but nothing more, so the picture is described by type: a late devotional Madonna of about 1445-50, the gold burnished to a gleam and the sacred figures set against it as in a vision. The format suggests the central panel of a small altarpiece or a picture for private prayer.","i":"Siena regarded itself as the city of the Virgin, and Mary with her Child was its most cherished image, painted for churches, chapels and homes. Angels attending her mark her as queen of heaven, and the gold ground is not a room but heaven itself. Such pictures asked for prayer rather than admiration of space; viewers saw in them an intercessor close at hand, a mother who could ask her Son for mercy.","c":"By the 1440s Florence was reshaping painting through perspective and sculptural form, and Sassetta, who held to the gold ground and a lyrical, linear grace, can look old-fashioned beside it. That is partly the point: his late Madonnas are tender and unemphatic, and the best hold their own through delicacy of line and colour. As a type this panel belongs to the quiet, devout end of his art."},"nga:2":{"d":"The left wing of a three-panel triptych shows two gray-bearded men, each under his own pointed arch, with a winged angel in the gable above, all against gold within a carved gold frame. At left Saint Andrew holds a wooden cross and a coiled white rope; at right Saint Benedict, an abbot in a silvery-white robe, holds a bundle of rods and an open book with Latin text. The tempera panel is about 197 by 80 cm, painted shortly before 1387.","i":"Andrew was one of the apostles and, in legend, was bound to his cross with ropes. Benedict founded Western monasticism, and his book carries the opening words of his Rule, Listen, O son, to the master's instructions, and incline the ear of your heart. The rods allude to monastic discipline. The angel in the gable is Gabriel, whose raised fingers announce the Annunciation, completed in the right wing, and the pairing suggests a monastic setting for the triptych.","c":"Agnolo Gaddi inherited the Giottesque tradition from his father, Taddeo, and this wing shows its late phase: monumental, calm figures, carefully patterned gold and a rich carved frame. The saints are dignified but stiff, and the formula of two standing figures under arches leaves little room for invention. Gaddi is better known for narrative frescoes, such as the True Cross cycle in Santa Croce, than for altarpieces, but the decorative splendor is the point here."},"nga:206122":{"d":"A three-part altarpiece shows the Madonna and Child enthroned among twelve angels in the center, flanked by two wings, each with two saints standing under pointed arches. The gold ground and a continuous gold and burgundy floor tie the panels together, spiraling columns separate them, and the gables carry a blessing Christ, the angel Gabriel and the Virgin of the Annunciation. Painted shortly before 1387 in tempera on poplar, each panel is about two meters high.","i":"The program is clear: Mary and her Son at the heart, saints at either side as intercessors, and the two gables across the wings forming the Annunciation, so that the Incarnation, which began in the angel's greeting, underlies the whole. Andrew, Benedict, Bernard and Catherine are an unusual combination, and the two monastic founders suggest a monastic commission. Such an altarpiece stood on an altar for the liturgy, where candlelight would make the gold glimmer.","c":"As an ensemble the triptych is a late flowering of the Giottesque tradition in Florence, adding a splendor of carved frame, tooled gold and rich color to the austerity of the earlier masters. The strengths are unity, symmetry and the clarity of its program; the weaknesses are repeated figure types and a conservative outlook, as Florentine painting was starting to shift toward the courtly International Gothic. Gaddi is the competent heir, not the innovator."},"nga:3":{"d":"Mary, in a lapis-blue mantle bordered with gold, sits on an ivory-white carved stone throne and supports the Christ Child, who stands on her lap with an arm around her neck. Twelve winged angels in groups of three flank the throne against a gold ground, and above her a bearded Christ raises two fingers in blessing and holds an open book. The panel, about 204 by 80 cm, is the center of the triptych, in a carved, gilded pointed frame.","i":"The Virgin enthroned among angels shows Mary as Queen of Heaven, and the child's adult proportions and calm gaze follow the Gothic convention of presenting Christ as divine wisdom rather than as an ordinary baby. The book above reads, in Latin, that he is the beginning and the end, the way, the truth and the life, and the greeting inscribed below, the angel's words at the Annunciation, links the panel to the wings.","c":"The central panel carries the triptych's richest handling: gold patterns, delicate faces faintly tinged with the green underpaint typical of tempera, and trios of angels that frame the group like a living arch. The child's adult face and double chin give an odd gravity, a convention of the time rather than a flaw, and the symmetry is stately more than tender. It is a late, polished example of Giotto's tradition, with courtly grace added."},"nga:4":{"d":"A tanned, mostly bald man with a forked beard, in a white robe, looks down at a red book with gold decoration, while a crowned woman in a pale pink mantle lined with yellow holds a green palm and a green book, with a spiked wheel at her feet. They stand under pointed arches against gold, and in the gable above a woman in blue, hands crossed on her chest, looks at a white dove. This is the right wing of the triptych, about 195 by 80 cm.","i":"The saints are Bernard of Clairvaux, the Cistercian abbot famed for his devotion to the Virgin, and Catherine of Alexandria, a virgin martyr shown with the wheel of her torture, the palm of martyrdom, a crown for her royal birth and a book for her learning. The Virgin in the gable, with the dove of the Holy Spirit, answers the angel Gabriel on the left wing, completing the Annunciation. A choice of Bernard and Benedict points to a monastic patron.","c":"The pleasure of the right wing is Catherine, a courtly princess in a pink mantle with yellow lining, and the contrast with Bernard's austere white habit is neat: austerity beside splendor. The small Virgin of the Annunciation adds delicacy. As in the other wing, the saints are more types than persons, interchangeable beneath their attributes, but Gaddi gives the formula grace and a sound decorative sense."},"met:436428":{"d":"God the Father, the crucified Christ and the dove of the Holy Spirit make up this image of the Trinity against a gold ground, apparently in the type known as the Throne of Mercy. The tempera panel is tall and narrow, about 136 by 73 centimeters, and was painted about 1390 to 1396; its height and shape suggest that it may once have formed part of a larger altarpiece.","i":"The Holy Trinity, the Christian belief in one God in three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, was often shown as a Throne of Mercy: the Father holds the cross on which the Son hangs, and the dove hovers between them. The image links the mystery to the sacrifice of the Cross and the Eucharist, which made it suitable for an altar. A gold background signals the heavenly realm, not a place on earth.","c":"Agnolo Gaddi inherited the great Florentine tradition of Giotto through his father Taddeo and spent his career as a skilled, rather conservative narrator. His work typically offers clear forms, sweet faces and careful ornament, and this panel looks like a polished, devout example of its age. It lacks Giotto's mass and the new energy Florentine painting would soon find with Masaccio, but the gold ground and tempera glow have a lasting charm."},"nga:344":{"d":"Jesus and Mary sit side by side above six winged angels in this vertical panel with a pointed top, about 161 by 79 cm, painted about 1390. Jesus, wearing a three-pointed gold crown, raises both hands to place a similar crown on Mary's head, while she tilts her head to receive it with her wrists crossed over her chest. Behind them hangs a cloth of blue and red edged with gold, and in two trios on a scarlet floor six angels play lute-like instruments.","i":"The Coronation of the Virgin shows Mary crowned Queen of Heaven by her Son after her Assumption, the culmination of her life in Christian devotion and a favorite subject in Florence. The angels' music evokes the harmony of heaven. The pointed top suggests that the panel was set into a larger altarpiece, as the central or upper part of an ensemble, where it would present Mary's glory to the faithful below.","c":"Gaddi, in his last years, shows a courtly refinement: the cloth of honor and the shimmering robes are patterned with great care, and the gesture of Christ raising the crown and Mary's crossed wrists give an intimate delicacy to a ceremonial moment. The six small angels add charm and music. The faces are formulaic and the space shallow, but the clarity of composition and the decorative splendor make it an elegant example of late Florentine Gothic."},"nga:46169":{"d":"A long, low panel, about 37 by 164 centimetres, carries three episodes from the early life of the Virgin Mary: her Presentation in the Temple, her Marriage to Joseph, and the Annunciation. The shape suggests a predella, the narrow strip of small narrative scenes that ran beneath the main panel of a Renaissance altarpiece, where worshippers could read the story at eye level. Benedetto Diana worked in Venice, and painters in his circle typically set such scenes in a clear, orderly space with figures in contemporary dress.","i":"The three scenes follow the Virgin's life as told in the apocryphal Gospel of James and the Golden Legend. Mary, at about three, climbs the temple steps to be dedicated to God; as a young woman she marries Joseph, chosen when his rod flowered among those of the suitors; and the angel Gabriel announces that she will bear Christ. Together they trace her path from dedication to the Incarnation, a sequence suited to a chapel of the Virgin.","c":"Without a close reading of the figures, the sound judgement is on format and school. Benedetto Diana belongs to the generation that carried Giovanni Bellini's soft light and ordered space into the early sixteenth century, without Bellini's depth of feeling; his narratives are clear and gently told rather than moving. A predella gives him a place to show lucid storytelling in small, and the strip, presumably separated from its altarpiece, is best taken as a fragment of a larger design."},"nga:125227":{"d":"A tiny figure in a straw-yellow hat, white shirt, brown knee breeches and white stockings, a red garment slung over one shoulder, walks along a path between two grassy mounds towards a tunnel that runs beside the right-hand mound. Beyond, a band of oatmeal-brown, beige and muted red buildings with grey roofs spans the width of the canvas, with laundry hung inside one and plants growing over the wide arch of a partly ruined building. Houses stagger back along rolling hills under a pale blue sky with thin white clouds.","i":"The Cloaca Maxima was ancient Rome's great drain, said to have been built in the earliest days of the city and still partly in use; in 1814 the area around it was a half-rural corner of Rome, with grass, ruins and washing hung out to dry. Eckersberg, a Dane who studied in Rome from 1813 to 1816 after training in Paris with Jacques-Louis David, painted small, precise views of the city that were new in their calm, accurate observation. This is one of them.","c":"A small picture of striking clarity: the single walking figure gives scale, the bands of building and sky are balanced with a calm geometric sense, and the light has an honest, unromantic precision very different from the picturesque Rome of earlier painters. It is short on drama, and its emphasis on the ordinary may seem dry. But that cool objectivity, so unlike the Grand Tour's golden haze, is part of what made Eckersberg a founder of Danish painting."},"met:441768":{"d":"A stretch of the ancient Via Sacra in Rome is shown with the church of Saints Cosmas and Damian, as the title says; the record lists the Via Sacra and a church building, and describes the work as an architectural painting. It is a small oil on canvas, about 31 by 44 centimeters, dated about 1814 to 1815. The museum gives no further description of the light, figures or foreground, so only its subject and format can be stated.","i":"Christoffer Wilhelm Eckersberg, the founder of the Danish Golden Age, was in Rome from 1813 to 1816, where he painted sharply observed views of the city's monuments directly from nature. The church of Saints Cosmas and Damian, built into the ruins of ancient buildings in the Forum in the sixth century, stands at the edge of the Via Sacra, the ancient processional road. Eckersberg later taught perspective and painting at the Copenhagen Academy, where his pupils included Christen Købke.","c":"Eckersberg's Roman sketches have a limpid clarity that is startling for the date: the light is cool and exact, the architecture seems measured, and the whole has the calm of a surveyor's drawing made into painting. They anticipate later realism and, in their way, photography. The price of this exactness is a reserve, a lack of rhetorical drama, but the small canvas rewards patient looking more than a grand picturesque would."},"met:435806":{"d":"A peasant woman picks fleas from a dog, and the two of them are the whole subject. The panel is tiny, about 20 by 16 centimeters, and was painted around 1626 to 1627, when Brouwer was in his early twenties. It is a cabinet picture of a humble domestic task, treated with the close attention that other painters gave to saints and princes, and meant to be looked at from arm's length by a collector who enjoyed low-life subjects.","i":"Brouwer made his name with scenes of peasants drinking, smoking, brawling and doing the humblest chores, catering to a Flemish and Dutch taste for earthy realism and comic observation. Picking fleas or lice was a familiar motif in the period; it could be read as an image of care, of poverty or simply as a joke at the expense of low life, and Brouwer's intention is not documented. Rubens owned many of his pictures, and Rembrandt owned several.","c":"Brouwer could make a trifle compelling. Even a tiny panel such as this has the ease and the unsentimental eye that won him the admiration of Rubens and Rembrandt, who saw that a humble subject could be painted with seriousness. His peasants can be coarse, and some viewers will find the subject unpleasant, but few painters of the period looked at low life with so little condescension. He died in his early thirties."},"nga:86204":{"d":"A young man with tanned skin sits behind a wooden table, elbows splayed, hooking a forefinger into each corner of his mouth to stretch his lips into a long, goofy line. A knife hangs by its blade in a loop on his tall, fuzzy brown hat, and his iron-gray jacket has coffee-brown sleeves and white cuffs. On the table stand a putty-gray jug and a glass of amber liquid, gleaming in soft light from the upper left against a dove-gray ground. The paint is loose, with brushstrokes visible in the face and hands, on a panel barely 14 by 10 centimeters.","i":"Brouwer, active in Haarlem in the circle of Frans Hals and in Antwerp from the early 1630s, made a specialty of low-life tavern figures, among them single grimacing faces. They draw on a long Netherlandish interest in extreme expressions, and viewers probably took the grimace as both comedy and warning: the jug and glass hint at drink, and a face so contorted suggested coarse, unrestrained behavior. At the same time, small pictures like this were collected avidly by connoisseurs, including Rubens and Rembrandt.","c":"At barely hand size this is a lively performance of economy: the grin, the slouch and the glint on glass and jug are all caught in a few quick strokes, with a freedom that few painters of low life matched. The subject is not elevating, and Brouwer knew it; the wit lies in the speed and the shrewd observation. It shows why he was so admired by collectors and why his influence spread to Teniers and others."},"met:435805":{"d":"A single peasant, with a bird and a pitcher beside him, fills this small wood panel, about 20 by 16 centimeters. One figure in a tiny picture was a frequent type for Brouwer, who treated face and posture as a character study. The pitcher points toward drink, and the bird is a detail whose meaning is uncertain. No date is recorded for the picture.","i":"In Flemish and Dutch low-life painting, birds could carry sly, even bawdy, double meanings, since the Dutch word for bird, vogel, had a vulgar slang sense, and pitchers and drink belong to the same world of indulgence. Nothing in the title proves such a reading here, and it may simply be a peasant with a pet or a catch. Brouwer's peasants are usually presented with humor and observation rather than a pointed lesson.","c":"Brouwer's single peasants live by their faces and postures: a few expressive touches suffice, with no setting to distract. A panel this small asks to be seen at arm's length, as its first owners saw it. The danger in such subjects is caricature, and Brouwer's best work avoids it by looking at the sitter with curiosity rather than contempt. For a modest picture it sums up why he mattered to bigger names."},"met:435807":{"d":"Five young men crowd together in a dim tavern, smoking clay pipes and drinking. In the centre a long-haired man in an ochre jerkin, seated on a stool with one boot on a footstool, lifts a tankard and blows out smoke, his mouth open and his eyes wide. Behind him two companions smoke and grin, and at the left a man in a hat presses a finger to his nose. At the right a man in a black coat with a broad white collar looks out with a knowing smile and holds a pipe. A window at the upper right opens on a landscape. A broom, a wooden tub, an earthenware jug and scattered scraps lie on the floor.","i":"Brouwer, a Flemish painter who died in Antwerp in 1638, specialised in small scenes of peasants and tavern life, and Rubens was among his admirers. Smoking, introduced to the Low Countries only a few decades earlier, was widely linked with idleness and drink, so a viewer could read the picture as a mild warning as well as a joke. Scholars have suggested that the central man is Brouwer himself and that the others are fellow painters such as Jan Lievens and Joos van Craesbeeck; these identifications are uncertain. The Met dates the panel to about 1636.","c":"Brouwer makes boorish behaviour a pleasure to look at. The grimacing smoker, the shrewd smile of the man in black and the finger-to-nose of the man at the left are all sharply observed, and the thin, fast brushwork in warm browns and greens gives the room an easy atmosphere that Teniers and other followers could imitate but not match. It is cheerful low comedy rather than depth, and the faces verge on caricature. But for economy of means, and for the liveliness packed into a panel under half a metre high, it is hard to beat."},"met:437641":{"d":"A band of horsemen rides across hilly country, the subject named by the title as Arabs on the march. Men, horses and hills make up the scene, and the wide canvas, about 96 by 58 centimeters, suits a column of riders strung out across a landscape. Schreyer was a German painter who made his reputation on such scenes, drawn from journeys to North Africa and the Near East as well as from studies of horses.","i":"Orientalist painting, the European fashion for scenes of North Africa and the Near East, was fed by travel, colonial expansion and a taste for the exotic. Schreyer's Arab riders found buyers among collectors in Europe and America in the later nineteenth century. Such pictures present the desert horseman as free, proud and picturesque; they say more about European fantasies of the Orient than about Arab life, and should be read in that light.","c":"Schreyer was a skilled painter of horses and of movement, and the best of his pictures convey the pace of a riding party. But the repertoire is repetitive: picturesque costume, an open track, a tidy emotion of freedom. The finish is smooth and the invention limited. As a document of period taste it is revealing; as an addition to the history of painting, it is minor."},"met:437642":{"d":"Mounted Arab warriors ride through hilly country in a battle scene whose title suggests a movement around an obstacle or an enemy rather than a head-on clash. At about 151 by 253 centimeters this is a very large canvas, the size of a salon showpiece, and its wide format lets the cavalry stretch across the picture. Men, horses and hills are the elements of the scene, so the energy of riders and mounts carries the work.","i":"Large battle pictures with Arab or Bedouin horsemen were a staple of nineteenth-century Orientalism, answering a taste for dash and danger that war painting had satisfied in Europe since the Napoleonic era. Schreyer was a specialist in horses and riders. The title names no particular battle, so the picture is best read as an adventure in a distant land, a spectacle rather than a record of any event.","c":"At this scale a critic can fairly say that Schreyer is better at the episode than the epic. His command of horses in motion is the real asset, and a sweep of riders gives a large canvas momentum. But a picture this size calls for variety of incident and feeling, and the usual verdict is that he enlarged his small cabinet pieces rather than rethinking them. The appeal is action; the risk is that action is all."},"nga:1142":{"d":"Bright light from the upper left picks out two muscular men and a winged angel against a dim landscape in a square canvas about 130 centimetres across. Christ stands ankle-deep in a dark blue stream at the centre, nude but for a white cloth, his hands pressed in prayer and his eyes downcast. At the right John the Baptist, seen largely from the back, clutches a tree trunk with one hand and pours water from a pewter dish over Christ's head with the other. At the left a copper-haired angel in orange looks on, and three winged babies, one holding a triangle, float in the sky.","i":"The scene follows the Gospels (Matthew 3:13–17): Christ comes to John at the Jordan, the heavens open and God declares him his Son. The baptism with a bowl, the attendant angel and the musician putti, who celebrate with triangle and instrument, make the moment a heavenly festival. Paris Bordone, born in Treviso, trained in Venice, was influenced by Titian and Giorgione, and painted both religious pictures and mythological subjects in rich, warm colour.","c":"Bordone gives the Baptism a muscular energy that owes something to Michelangelo's example, known through prints, and the bold lighting, John's twisting back and the glowing orange, cobalt and salmon give it drama. The putti with their triangle are charming, though they border on the whimsical. The figures are a little theatrical and the landscape dim, but the picture is a vigorous, warm essay in Venetian Mannerism."},"met:435722":{"d":"A man in armour stands with two pages, the Met's keywords recording the man, the armour and a boy, in a horizontal canvas about 117 by 158 cm by a Venetian painter. The museum gives no date or sitter, so the picture is described by type: a portrait of a nobleman or officer who chose to be painted as a soldier, with the steel of his armour as the main display and the attendant boys signalling rank and household. The size suits a hall or a gallery.","i":"Armour portraits announced military rank and honour, in Venice's mainland territories as much as in Spain or France, and the type was fashionable among men who had never fought as well as among real commanders. Pages stood for the retinue a man of standing commanded, and often carried a helmet or gauntlets as emblems of his role. Since the sitter is unnamed, only the type can be read: a man who wished posterity to see him as a soldier of rank.","c":"Bordone learned his colour and handling in Titian's orbit but rarely matched the older master's psychological depth, and the interest here is mostly display: armour, attendants and the assured pose of a sitter who knew how to be looked at. The three-figure arrangement is more ambitious than the usual single portrait, and the polish is considerable. It is a handsome, somewhat impersonal record of rank."},"cma:144732":{"d":"A lightning-blasted tree dominates the composition of this oil on canvas, about 76 by 63 cm, dated 1847. The title and the tree set a mountain landscape under storm, and the blasted tree stands for nature's awe-inspiring power and its endless cycle of life and death. Church was twenty-one, recently the pupil of Thomas Cole, and the picture is an early work in the Hudson River School tradition.","i":"The blasted tree was a favorite motif of Romantic landscape painters because it joined the sublime, the power of nature that overwhelms the human, with a sense of transience. In the Hudson River School, led by Cole, landscape was never merely scenery; it carried moral and spiritual meaning, and a tree broken by lightning could speak of mortality, judgment or the fragility of human enterprise.","c":"A young painter's ambition shows in the staging: one great tree against a storm. The strength is the early evidence of the descriptive precision that would make Church famous, and the weakness is the borrowed idiom, a Romantic stock-in-trade taken from Cole and a little theatrical. Beside the Niagara of 1857 and the Heart of the Andes of 1859 it is an apprenticeship piece, but a promising and confident one."},"nga:126132":{"d":"A small oil study on academy board, about 29 by 39 centimeters, dated 1850, with the title Fog off Mount Desert and the single keyword 'seascape'. The record gives no description of the picture's content, but the title indicates a view over the sea with fog drifting off the coast of Mount Desert Island, Maine. At this size and on this support, it is the kind of quick sketch made outdoors for later use.","i":"Frederic Church, a pupil of Thomas Cole, first visited Mount Desert Island in 1850, following Cole himself, who had sketched there in 1844. The island's granite mountains rising from the sea, and its fogs, would draw many American painters, and the area later became Acadia National Park. Academy board, a stiff, prepared paperboard, was used for oil sketches made in front of the subject.","c":"A sketch that records weather, and that is why it works: Church, a painter of spectacle in his large canvases, is here quiet, intent on the way fog dissolves forms. The handling is presumably quick and the effect atmospheric. A small study, undemanding, and revealing of the observation that underlies his later grandeur."},"nga:126131":{"d":"A mountain on Mount Desert Island, Maine, is the subject of this horizontal canvas, about 54 by 79 centimeters, dated 1851. The record gives only the title and the keyword 'landscape', so its composition, light and details are not described here. Newport Mountain was the name then used for the peak that is now called Champlain Mountain. Painted on canvas, it is probably a more finished studio work than the small board sketches Church made on the island.","i":"Mount Desert's granite mountains, the highest on the Atlantic coast of the United States, were discovered by painters in the 1840s and 1850s: Cole in 1844, Church in 1850 and 1851. Church painted the island from sketches made on the spot, working them up into larger canvases in his studio, a method he later applied to the Andes and Niagara. This picture was painted a year after his first visit.","c":"A good example of Church's early manner, when he still worked in the tradition of his teacher Cole, with the mountain as a heroic presence and the landscape recorded with scrupulous care. It is more modest than the later spectacles, and its restraint may disappoint those who know the great Andean pictures, but the early ambition is already visible."},"nga:166437":{"d":"A river lined with palms winds into the distance before a monumental, snow-covered mountain at the center of the horizontal canvas. In the lower left a spit of land is crowded with ferns, vines, leafy trees and palms. Along the shore at the bottom center, a pointed-prow boat with a rounded, hut-like cover carries three people in white pants, two of them bare-chested and one in a red shirt, with a wisp of white that may be smoke before the cover's opening. Across the placid river, white buildings and a white two-spired church stand among trees below brown hills.","i":"Church traveled to Colombia and Ecuador in 1853, inspired by the writings of the German naturalist Alexander von Humboldt, who had urged painters to depict the tropical landscape in its full variety. Tamaca Palms is a product of that journey, painted in 1854 from sketches, and it prepared the way for his great Andean pictures, The Andes of Ecuador (1855) and The Heart of the Andes (1859). William Wilson Corcoran gave it to the Corcoran Gallery in 1869.","c":"The scene is composed with a naturalist's care and a stage-manager's instinct: the foreground ferns and palms frame a river that leads the eye to the glittering snow peak, and the haze deepens the distance toward pale pinkish tan. The figures in their boat are small, and the botanical detail is rich without being fussy. It is an early demonstration of the control of space and light that made Church the most admired American landscapist of the 1850s."},"nga:166436":{"d":"The canvas is more than twice as wide as it is high, and the viewer seems to hover above the green, racing water of the Niagara River as it nears the Horseshoe Falls. In the foreground the river turns white and frothy where the riverbed steps down. Ahead, the crest of the falls curves away in a smooth bottle-green band before dropping in a curtain of white. A thin, broken rainbow shows in the mist at left and again lower down between the falls. The horizon sits just over halfway up, under plum-purple clouds in a lavender sky, and tiny trees and buildings line the far shores. No figure or bank is in view.","i":"Frederic Church, a pupil of Thomas Cole, painted this view from the Canadian side early in 1857, after his fifth visit to the falls. It was shown alone in a darkened New York gallery that May, with tens of thousands paying 25 cents to see it, and then sent to London, where John Ruskin is reported to have admired its effects of light. It later won a silver medal at the Paris Exposition Universelle of 1867. The Corcoran Gallery bought it in 1876. The absence of any foreground or figure was a deliberate choice that puts the viewer in the water, close to the glitter of the rainbow.","c":"Church removes everything that would let the eye rest, such as a bank, a boat or a tourist, so the viewer feels the drop. The translucent green at the lip and the rainbow broken by mist look observed rather than invented. The weak spot is the sky, thinner and more theatrical than the water, and the far shores are only hinted at. This is a picture made for paying crowds, and it has a showman's confidence. Within the Hudson River School it is where panorama and natural history meet."},"met:10481":{"d":"A broad valley opens beneath a pale sky, and a snow-covered peak shines at the far left. In the middle distance reddish-brown mountains roll across the picture; in the foreground a stream gathers into a pool at the right, fed by a waterfall that glints in the sun. Tropical trees, vines, ferns, and flowers, painted in minute detail, frame both sides and climb the right edge in a dark green mass. At the lower left a tiny cross stands beside a path. The picture is much wider than it is tall, about 1.7 by 3 meters, with the light low and golden, the foreground details sharp, and the distance dissolving into blue haze.","i":"Frederic Church traveled in Ecuador and Colombia in 1853 and 1857, inspired by the naturalist Alexander von Humboldt, who urged artists to capture the character of whole landscapes. The Heart of the Andes is a composite, not a view of one place: it joins the snow-capped Chimborazo, a waterfall, a valley, and tropical forest. Church showed it alone in New York in 1859, where visitors paid twenty-five cents and used opera glasses to study the details. It made him the best-known landscape painter in America, and he sold it for $10,000, then the highest price paid for a work by a living American artist. The Met has owned it since 1909.","c":"A work of astonishing finish, designed to overwhelm, and it still does. Its virtues are scale, clarity, and real scientific curiosity: nearly every plant can be identified. Its weakness is a certain staginess. The composition is a tidy sequence of planes, and the grandeur is engineered rather than discovered, so that viewers who prefer looser, more personal landscapes may find it airless. Yet as a picture of America's confidence in the natural world, and its wish to see everything, it has few equals."},"cma:141639":{"d":"A huge sky of flame-red and rose clouds streaks across the top of the canvas, with patches of cold blue-green showing through. Beneath it a yellow band of light glows along the horizon, over dark wooded hills and a distant, pale mountain. In the centre a still lake reflects the glow, with a tiny wooded island. Dark forest crowds both sides: a pale, leafless tree and evergreens at the right, a broken stump at the lower left and a bare dead tree with a small bird on its top at the far left. No people appear. The paint is thin and smooth, and the leaves and rocks are painted with precision.","i":"Cleveland says Church painted this in his New York studio from sketches he made near Mount Katahdin in Maine nearly two years earlier. Made on the eve of the Civil War, the blazing sunset can be read as foretelling the conflict to come. Church was the most famous American landscape painter of his generation, and the museum notes his showmanship: instead of entering the picture in an annual exhibition among others' work, he showed it alone at a prestigious gallery, and with advance publicity and favourable reviews, several hundred people came to see it during its seven-week run.","c":"The sky is the show, and Church knows it: the red clouds are painted with a feathery sweep that makes the whole upper half of the canvas seem to glow. The foreground works less well. It is dark and cluttered, built of stock devices such as dead trees and a rocky ledge, and the carefully detailed forest can feel stage-managed beside the sunset's flare. Some viewers find the theatre too deliberate. Still, the empty wilderness is eerily convincing, and the mood it creates from light alone is hard to forget."},"met:10482":{"d":"The Parthenon, the ruined temple of Athena on the Acropolis of Athens, is viewed from a low angle against the sky in a large oil on canvas, about 113 by 185 cm, dated 1871. Church traveled to Greece around 1869 and made sketches, and the finished picture was painted at home in the United States. The low viewpoint makes the temple loom as a monument, and its ruin is the whole subject.","i":"Nineteenth-century Americans, who saw their republic as heir to Greek democracy, regarded the Parthenon as an emblem of civilization. The temple was a ruin because a Venetian shell hit an Ottoman powder store inside it in 1687. For a viewer of 1871, a ruin was an image of glory and decline at once, a reminder that great civilizations rise and fall, and a visit to Athens was the climax of a cultivated traveler's tour.","c":"Church's gift for natural description meets architecture, and the low viewpoint is a strong idea that gives the columns a sublime scale. This is an Old World monument by an artist famous for New World wilderness, and the picture suffers a little from a postcard grandeur, the risk of any famous site. It is more reportorial than poetic compared with his tropical epics, but it is a monumental and carefully observed canvas."},"met:10480":{"d":"A wide panorama of the Aegean coast fills this large canvas, about 137 by 214 cm, dated about 1877. The picture shows sea, trees, a human figure and a rainbow. At this size it is a major picture rather than a cabinet piece. The Aegean, the sea of Greece, was a place of classical memory as well as of scenery, and the title names the water itself as the subject.","i":"The Aegean was the cradle of Greek civilization, and Americans of Church's generation approached it as a landscape of cultural memory. The rainbow is an old sign of promise, the covenant after the flood in Genesis, and Church used rainbows in other major works. A figure in the landscape provides the scale of the human against the sea and the ancient shores.","c":"A composition of sea, sky and rainbow demands delicacy of atmosphere, and Church's strength is exact observation of light and weather. The risk is the late Church's tendency to heighten effects, and a rainbow tips easily toward sentiment. The broad format and clarity of structure give the picture an assured calm. It is a polished late performance more than an innovative one, with the technical command that his admirers expected."},"nga:50299":{"d":"Seen from a shaded bank, a wide, still river runs toward a low, glowing sun, its surface a pale gold-pink haze. At the left a huge, vine-draped tree and a cluster of fan-leafed palms fill the foreground, while dense foliage lines the far shore and the right bank. The trees and the sun are mirrored in the water, and the colours melt into mauve and pink toward the horizon. Two tiny birds with red breasts perch on a palm stem in the foreground, a line of white birds skims the water, and a canoeist is barely visible in the mist at the centre. The finish is smooth and finely worked.","i":"Church was famous for huge panoramas of the Americas and made two journeys to Colombia and Ecuador, in 1853 and 1857. He painted El Rio de Luz in 1877, some twenty years later, working from sketches and drawings made on those trips. The setting is a generic tropical river rather than an identified one, and the picture has also gone by the titles Morning in the Tropics and The Amazon. It is the last of his large South American pictures, and its calm, glowing atmosphere belongs to the tradition now called luminism. One scholar has read the rising sun as a Christian symbol of spiritual renewal in the New World; that is an interpretation.","c":"El Rio de Luz is a late, quiet Church. Gone are the thundering waterfalls and volcanoes of his 1850s triumphs; in their place is an almost meditative study of light on water, handled with extraordinary patience. The haze, the long reflection and the tiny canoeist create a persuasive feeling of heat and stillness. Against that, the botanical care is admirable but the arrangement of framing trees and a distant sun is a stock Claude Lorrain formula, and the drama of his larger pictures is missing. A fine example of American luminist atmosphere."},"cma:173180":{"d":"Perched atop a dramatic cliff, a brilliantly backlit monastery overlooks a shadowed foreground where a solitary figure navigates a rugged path. The oil on canvas, about 119 by 183 cm, is dated 1879. The location that inspired the painting is debated, but many believe it derives from Church's travels through the Ecuadorean Andes nearly a quarter century earlier. It was commissioned by the Cleveland banker and railroad executive Hinman B. Hurlbut.","i":"The composition reads as an allegory of spiritual salvation: the pilgrim in the shadow of the valley climbs toward a light-filled sanctuary. Such allegories of the soul's journey have a long Christian lineage, and the Hudson River School often joined landscape with moral meaning. A banker's commission of a picture about a monastery in the mountains suggests a taste for contemplation set against the hurry of business.","c":"This is a quieter, more spiritual Church than the spectacular Andes pictures of the 1850s. The backlighting and the contrast of shadow and light set up a moral drama in few terms, and the tiny figure gives the monastery a visionary scale. The allegory is perhaps too explicit, but his observation keeps the mountain convincing. It is a late work from a painter whose fame had waned, and it has dignity rather than novelty."},"nga:46101":{"d":"Christ hangs on a cross flanked by small groups of figures against a shiny gold ground. He has long blond hair and a beard and wears a white loincloth; blood trickles from his hands, feet and side, and angels catch it in gold cups. A woman in a red cloak with long unbound hair clasps the foot of the cross, while at left two people support a swooning Virgin in a black cloak. At right three bearded men watch, one in a yellow cloak with both hands raised, another helmeted. A skull lies under the mound. Tempera on poplar, about 36 by 24 centimeters, c. 1320 to 1325.","i":"The swooning Virgin supported by the holy women and Saint John, Mary Magdalene embracing the cross, the centurion who recognized the Son of God and the angels catching Christ's blood in chalices are standard elements of the Gothic Crucifixion. The skull at the foot recalls Adam's burial at Golgotha, and the gold cups recall the Eucharist. Small panels like this were made for private devotion, and Daddi, a Florentine follower of Giotto, produced many.","c":"Daddi's small Crucifixion is a model of Florentine clarity: the grief of the Virgin and the sorrow of the holy women are expressed with simple, readable gestures, and the figures have Giotto's solidity in miniature. The decorative gold ground and the winged angels with their paired wings add elegance. It is less dramatic than the work of the master, but the emotion is real."},"met:436068":{"d":"A tiny panel, about 19 by 23 centimetres, painted in tempera on a gold ground, shows Christ enthroned among saints. The Met's keywords record Christ, saints, men and women. At this size and with a gold ground, the picture was probably part of a small portable altarpiece or a devotional piece for a private chapel or bedroom, where it could be seen at close range.","i":"Christ enthroned shows him as ruler of heaven, surrounded by saints who act as intercessors for the faithful. The gold ground stands for the radiance of heaven and was typical of Florentine devotional panels of the early fourteenth century. Bernardo Daddi, a follower of Giotto, ran a busy workshop in Florence and specialised in such small, portable panels, which were made for the households and confraternities of a prosperous city. The date is about 1325.","c":"Daddi's gift was to take Giotto's solid forms and make them small, sweet and colourful, and a panel of this scale shows a jeweller's care: clear outlines, delicate faces, saints arranged with grace. The crowd of figures is formulaic, and the small size leaves no room for drama. As an object, though, it is a refined, precious thing, and the gold ground makes the colours sing."},"met:438423":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 46 by 29 centimetres, painted in tempera on a gold ground, shows Christ crucified, with the Virgin Mary and Saint John the Evangelist beside the cross. Its small size and format suggest a panel from a portable altarpiece or a diptych, made for prayer in a private room. The figures stand out against the shining gold, and the small scale invites close viewing. The date is given as about 1325 to 1330.","i":"In John 19:25–27, Mary and John stand at the foot of the cross, and Christ entrusts his mother to the disciple. Their grief invites the viewer to share in it, and Florentine painters of the fourteenth century, influenced by Franciscan devotion, stressed compassion. The gold ground lifts the event out of time, so that it becomes an image for meditation, not a record. Bernardo Daddi made many small Crucifixions of this kind for private devotion.","c":"Daddi tempers Giotto's gravity with a sweet, lyrical grace: the mourners are elegant, the line flows, the colour is clear and the pathos controlled. What it lacks is Giotto's weight and the shocking human directness of his Crucifixion at Padua. Within its small devotional scale, though, it is moving and refined, and it shows how Giotto's innovations were adapted for quiet, private prayer."},"nga:1":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel with a pointed top, about 225 by 77 centimeters, shows Saint Paul nearly filling a gold ground. He faces the viewer with brown eyes under furrowed brows, a straight nose, a brown beard and a receding hairline, holding a gold-handled sword in one hand and a thick black book against his chest with the other. He wears a celery-green tunic and a rose-pink robe lined in orange. At his feet twelve much smaller people, six pairs, kneel in prayer in robes of pink, blue, red, yellow, green and brown, and lettering around his head spells his name. Tempera on panel, dated 1333 in an inscription.","i":"Paul is identified by the sword, the instrument of his martyrdom, and the book, for the letters he wrote to the early churches. The kneeling couples, tiny beside the saint, were presumably the people who paid for or used the altarpiece, and their size expresses humility before him. The inscription gives the year 1333 and records the completion of the work. Such large panels were part of altarpieces, probably in a Florentine church, and Daddi was among the busiest painters of his time.","c":"Daddi gives the saint a calm, monumental presence: the long sweep of the robes, the gold ground and the grave, wide-eyed face show Giotto's lessons absorbed into a more graceful, decorative idiom. The tiny worshippers are charming and lively, their gestures varied. The cracked gold surface adds to the antique feel. It is a fine example of the Florentine altar panel of the 1330s, which values clarity and sweetness over drama."},"met:436069":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 32 by 41 centimetres, shows the young Saint Reparata brought before the Roman emperor Decius. The Met's keywords record an interior, men and a woman, so the scene is set indoors, with the saint standing before the emperor and his court. Painted in tempera on wood around 1338 to 1340, it probably belongs to a series of scenes from the saint's life, of which the Met has two other panels.","i":"Reparata, according to her legend, was a girl of Caesarea in Palestine who refused to sacrifice to the Roman gods under the persecution of the emperor Decius in the third century, and was tortured and put to death. She was venerated in Florence from the early Middle Ages, and the city's old cathedral was dedicated to her before the present one replaced it. A cycle of her life reminded Florentines of a patron saint's constancy.","c":"Daddi tells the story with clarity: the interview between the girl and the emperor sets youth and constancy against power, and the figures are grouped in simple, readable relation. The interior is little more than a stage of flat planes, and the faces are close to type, as in most Florentine narrative panels of the day. It is a neat, economical piece of storytelling rather than a deeply felt one."},"nga:41676":{"d":"Seated on an arched throne, the Virgin cradles the Christ Child in the center of a small, pointed panel, surrounded by about twenty smaller saints and angels against a glimmering gold ground. Mary wears a forest-green, gold-edged robe over a peach dress with gold patterns; she and the Child look to the right, where he reaches toward a small gray bird held by one of two women beside the throne. The saints hold a palm, a flute, a ring, an ointment jar, a cross, a sword, a lamb and a book, and one man holds a large gold key. Tempera on panel, about 50 by 24 centimeters, c. 1345.","i":"This is a heavenly court in miniature: the objects identify the saints, such as the key for Peter, the lamb for Agnes, the ointment jar for Mary Magdalene and the palm for martyrs, while the small bird in the Child's reach is traditionally a goldfinch, a symbol of the Passion. Such small, richly gilded panels were made for private devotion, often as the wing of a portable altarpiece, and could be carried and opened for prayer.","c":"Daddi was the master of the small, jewel-like devotional panel, and this one, with its crowded court of saints, shows his gift for organizing many figures clearly on a tiny field. The colors are bright and the gold shimmers, and the gentle exchange between the Child and the bird gives the scene a human touch. It is more decorative than profound, but within its genre it is charming."},"met:436070":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 24 by 35 centimetres, painted in tempera on a gold ground, shows the preparation of Saint Reparata for execution. The Met's keywords record the saint, men and women, so the scene gathers her with the executioners and onlookers just before her death. It matches two other panels of her legend in the same museum in subject and manner and, like them, is the work of the Florentine painter Bernardo Daddi.","i":"In her legend, Reparata survived torture and was beheaded, and a dove was said to fly from her mouth as her soul rose to heaven. The preparation for execution is the quiet moment before that death, and the saint's calm is meant to show her faith. Scenes of martyrdom in early Florentine painting rarely dwell on cruelty; they offer models of steadfastness. Daddi, a leading Florentine painter of the 1330s and 1340s, painted such small narratives with notable grace.","c":"The gold ground gives the small panel a jewel-like, timeless quality, and Daddi's restraint spares the viewer any gore. That restraint is also its limit: the scene is so decorous that the martyr's ordeal seems almost a ceremony. The figures are lightly handled and the colour sweet. For close looking it is a charming object; for drama it should be seen with the other scenes of the cycle."},"met:436071":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 33 by 42 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood, shows Saint Reparata tortured with red-hot irons. The Met's keywords record a saint, a woman and suffering. It belongs with two other scenes of her legend by Bernardo Daddi in the same museum. The gold ground now carries a tooled pattern that may have been added in the late nineteenth century, so the surface that the viewer sees is partly a later restoration.","i":"Red-hot irons were among the tortures that the legend of Reparata, like the legends of other virgin martyrs such as Agatha and Lucy, said she endured without yielding. Such scenes showed the saint's constancy rather than cruelty and invited the faithful to admire and imitate it. Reparata was one of the patron saints of Florence, and her cycle belonged to the city's civic and devotional life.","c":"Daddi shows the torment with composure, in keeping with his gentle temperament: the saint remains calm, the executioners are matter-of-fact and the colour stays pleasant. The late tooling of the gold is a reminder that what the viewer sees is not wholly fourteenth-century. As a depiction of suffering it is sweet, perhaps too sweet, but as a devotional narrative it is clear and graceful."},"nga:46434":{"d":"A musician plays a lute in a large, nearly square canvas about 144 by 129 cm, dated by the museum between about 1612 and 1620. The museum supplies no description, so the picture is characterised by type and by the artist's manner: a figure absorbed in music, painted in the clear, naturalistic light and sleek handling of Orazio Gentileschi, who learned from Caravaggio in Rome. Music-makers were a favourite subject of that circle, a quiet, sensuous scene that needs no story.","i":"The lute stood for harmony and love, and lute players appear in pictures of courtship, in allegories of Music or of the sense of hearing, and sometimes in saintly guise. The Caravaggesque painters made music scenes fashionable in Rome in the early 1600s, partly because they allowed intimate, ambiguous subjects that a collector could enjoy privately. Without a recorded sitter or attributes, the picture is best read as a genre scene with a gentle allegorical overtone.","c":"Orazio is the most refined of Caravaggio's followers, trading the master's violence for lyric calm and a luminous, silk-like sheen. A lute player gives him a perfect pretext: poised attention, rich drapery, a soft, directional light. The risk is a certain coolness, a beauty without the dramatic charge of Caravaggio or the emotional force of his daughter Artemisia. Within those limits it is a poised and attractive example of his manner."},"nga:46172":{"d":"A young woman saint and an angel share a horizontal canvas about 88 by 108 cm. The museum supplies no description, so the picture is characterised by type: Saint Cecilia, patron of music, shown with an angel, in the Caravaggesque naturalism softened by lyrical grace that marks Orazio Gentileschi. The museum's two overlapping date ranges, about 1617-18 and about 1621-27, suggest a picture begun and later completed or reworked, which is why no single moment in his career can be assigned to it.","i":"Cecilia, a Roman martyr of the early centuries, was said to have heard heavenly music at her wedding and to have kept her virginity under an angel's protection, so she became the patron of musicians and an image of harmony between body and spirit. Her tomb in Rome was opened in 1599 and her cult flourished in the Baroque age, when music could be painted as gesture and feeling. An angel at her side belongs to her story and is no mere ornament.","c":"Gentileschi's best pictures have an unforced, quiet beauty, and a musical saint with an angel is exactly the subject for it: the picture concerns listening and attention rather than drama, and Caravaggesque light does its work with restraint. The cost of that calm is a certain softness of emotion. Even so, it shows his gift for making sacred stories feel intimate, and for painting devotion without theatre."},"nga:222287":{"d":"A large cluster of green grapes hangs in the upper center, with an intertwined vine and a single olive-green leaf; one grape at the heart of the bunch is golden. Below, two round red fruits, medlars, rest on a light brown table, one opened to show a pale orange interior. A delicate blue butterfly flutters to the left of the grapes and a small red ladybug sits at the table's edge. The background is black, and everything stands out from it in sharp relief. Oil on canvas, about 38 by 31 centimeters, dated 1687.","i":"Coorte's tiny still lifes belong to a Dutch tradition in which small things carry large thoughts. The butterfly, a symbol of the soul or of transience, and the ladybug are familiar emblems of life's brevity, while ripe and opened fruit suggests the passage from ripeness to decay. Grapes are traditionally linked to wine and to Christ's sacrifice. Whether Coorte intended a moral is uncertain; the pictures may equally be about the pleasure of looking closely at nature.","c":"Coorte's gift is concentration: a few objects, a black ground, and a light that makes each one glow. The translucence of the grapes and the powdery wing of the butterfly are painted with a patience that rewards close looking. He was long overlooked, and the pictures are small, yet in their stillness they rival the greater Dutch still-life painters. The bold simplicity of grapes hanging into an empty dark is the picture's chief strength."},"nga:125313":{"d":"A bundle of thick, pale green asparagus bound with twine and a bunch of glistening ruby currants rest on the edge of a cracked stone ledge against a deeply shadowed ground. The cut ends of the stalks are creamy white and jagged; the tips, turned away from the viewer, are faintly green and muted purple. One stem of currants dangles over the front edge. Light from the upper left gives the berries a glassy sheen. Oil on canvas, about 34 by 25 centimeters, signed and dated A. Coorte 1696 on the ledge.","i":"Asparagus was a costly seasonal delicacy in the Dutch Republic, and Coorte painted it repeatedly. Red currants were a summer fruit, so the picture pairs two seasonal pleasures. Such restrained arrangements are often read as meditations on modest abundance, and perhaps on the brevity of fresh things, but nothing in the picture insists on a moral. Its appeal is the plain, undisguised look at things, in a format small enough to hold in the hands.","c":"One of Coorte's most economical pictures: two humble subjects, a ledge and darkness, and the light does the rest. The asparagus has a bundle's weight and the currants a glassy glow, while the cracked stone and the dangling stem add tension to an otherwise static arrangement. He stands far from the showy displays of Dutch still life, and the effect is intimate and almost devotional."},"met:436722":{"d":"A man weighs gold coins on a small balance. The panel, about 51 by 30 centimeters, is tall and narrow, a format that suggests a single half-length figure at a table. It was painted around 1515 to 1520 by a Bruges artist and belongs to the type of picture in which a money-man's work is the subject, whether as a portrait or as a moral comment.","i":"Pictures of bankers and moneychangers at work were popular in the Netherlands after Quinten Massys painted his Moneylender and His Wife in 1514. They flattered the commercial class that bought art in cities like Bruges and Antwerp, but also carried a warning: the balance recalls the scales of the Last Judgment, and avarice was a deadly sin. Whether this man is a proud professional or a reproach to greed remains open.","c":"Isenbrant is best known as a quiet craftsman of Bruges, a follower of Gerard David and a painter of gentle Madonnas, so a moneyman at his scales is an unusual subject for him. The picture has less of the sharp psychological bite of Massys, but a man absorbed in a small, exacting task makes a persuasive image. It stands as a reminder that the Netherlandish Renaissance was as interested in commerce as in Christ."},"met:436721":{"d":"The title names two figures: Christ crowned with thorns and shown to the crowd, the Ecce Homo, and the Virgin Mary in mourning, the Mater Dolorosa. A city, probably Jerusalem, appears in the setting. The picture is about 105 by 93 centimeters, and it is painted in oil on canvas that was transferred from its original wood panel, a restoration technique that moves the paint layer to a new support.","i":"Ecce Homo, 'Behold the man', are Pilate's words as he presents the scourged Christ to the crowd (John 19:5); the Mater Dolorosa is the Virgin sharing her son's suffering, the sword that pierced her heart foretold by Simeon (Luke 2:35). Paired, the two figures invited private meditation on the Passion, a central practice of devotion in sixteenth-century Bruges, and Isenbrant's workshop supplied such images to patrons for use at home.","c":"Isenbrant worked in the gentle, polished tradition of Gerard David, and this is a typical and typically restrained example. In his usual manner, pathos is expressed by quiet means rather than by the lurid extremes of German painting. The drawing is careful and the emotional range narrow, and the picture will please more as devotion than as drama. Its transfer to canvas may have softened some of the original panel's crispness."},"nga:56668":{"d":"Mary and Joseph kneel with two small angels around the naked infant Jesus, who lies on an overturned basket beside a bundle of wheat, in a vertical panel about 75 by 57 cm. They stand in the ruins of a building with an exposed interior; ivy creeps over the broken walls, an owl perches on the remains of the roof, and an ox and a donkey shelter below. Five men kneel or stand about, three entering from the left, one raising a musical instrument to his lips. Beyond, tiny figures dance around a fire in a green and blue landscape.","i":"The Child lies on the ground, not in a manger, a tradition that goes back to Saint Bridget of Sweden's visions of the Nativity and was widely adopted by Netherlandish painters, and Mary adores him rather than cradling him. The wheat sheaf points ahead to the bread of the Eucharist, since Bethlehem means house of bread. The ruined building is usually read as the old order giving way to the new, and the dancing figures in the distance echo the news carried to the shepherds.","c":"Isenbrant worked in Bruges in the tradition of Gerard David, and the picture shows the sweetness and careful finish of that school: the kneeling family has a tender, undemonstrative gravity, and the landscape beyond is a small delight. But the invention is conservative, the space is crowded, and the faces lack the individual intensity of an earlier generation's masters. As a devotional picture it is graceful and lucid; as innovation it adds little."},"met:436723":{"d":"Several episodes from the Virgin Mary's life are told in one work: the Nativity, the Adoration of the Magi, the Visitation and the Flight into Egypt, with men and women among the figures. It is an oil painting on wood, dated by the Met to after 1521, and its aim is storytelling in sequence rather than a single dramatic moment, in the manner of a devotional picture that could be read like a short illustrated book.","i":"Cycles of the Life of the Virgin were popular in the Netherlands: they showed her principal joys and sorrows in order, serving both as Gospel narrative and as a model of devotion. The Nativity and Adoration of the Magi are among the joys; the Visitation shows Mary greeting her cousin Elizabeth, and the Flight into Egypt is counted among her sorrows. Viewers could follow the story in order, praying as they moved from scene to scene.","c":"A multi-scene panel puts a premium on clear storytelling rather than individual drama, and Isenbrant is a reliable, if not an inspired, narrator. His Bruges workshop had a large output, with quality that varies, and a picture of this kind should be judged on how plainly it lays out its episodes. As a record of what Bruges patrons liked in sacred story, it is useful even where it is not original."},"nga:56573":{"d":"In the lower left corner a dog stands at an open door through which light streams into a dark house. A blond man in a tan jacket and pink vest and a dark-haired woman in a brown blouse and bright red skirt stand in the doorway, arms linked, he turned towards the door, she gesturing back into the room. At the right a table is set with a tea service, where a grey-haired man reads a newspaper and a woman in a white bonnet points at the couple. In the archway behind, a servant in a red bonnet and green apron holds a roast chicken on a plate, looking out.","i":"The title names the comedy: a bashful young cousin arrives and is led into the family parlour, while the older relatives look on with amusement. Francis Edmonds, a New York banker who painted in his spare time, specialised in such gentle anecdotes of American domestic life, popular in the 1840s. The Black servant in the doorway with the chicken is a figure that recurs in genre painting of the time and reflects its racial assumptions, which showed Black people in subordinate, supporting roles.","c":"A well-constructed anecdote: the arrangement is clear, with the couple at the lit door, the elders at the table and the servant framed in the arch, and the humour is conveyed by gesture alone. The light pouring through the door is nicely used. But the picture is more illustration than painting, its characters are types, and its Black servant is a stereotype of the time. It is valuable as a document of manners and attitudes."},"met:20888":{"d":"Men prepare turkeys for the Christmas table in this household scene, an oil on canvas of about 58 by 84 cm, dated 1851. The title and the subjects (men and domestic turkeys) point to the plain work of plucking birds, treated with humor and affection. It belongs to American genre painting of the mid-century, small narrative pictures of everyday life that appealed to a growing middle-class audience.","i":"In the 1840s and 1850s Christmas became a widely celebrated family festival in the United States, and the turkey became its central dish. A picture of the work that came before the feast spoke to a shared experience and perhaps to nostalgia for the farm, which many urban buyers had left behind. Edmonds, who exhibited at the National Academy and sold to collectors of American scenes, made such anecdotes his specialty.","c":"Edmonds worked as a banker while he painted, and his pictures have the care of a skilled amateur turned professional: well-organized compositions, telling details, good-humored anecdotes. The subject is homely and the treatment affectionate. The limit is a sweetness that makes every country scene cheerful. It is a pleasing period piece, more genial than searching, and valuable as a record of how mid-century Americans liked to picture their Christmas."},"met:10423":{"d":"A census taker visits a household in this interior, with a woman, children and an infant present and writing among its subjects. The oil on canvas, about 71 by 97 cm, is dated 1854. The title and subjects point to a humorous domestic scene. It belongs to American genre painting of the mid-century, small narrative pictures of ordinary life that told their stories through gesture and setting.","i":"The United States census of 1850 was the first to record every free inhabitant by name, and the census taker became a familiar visitor at the door. For many households this was a first meeting with the machinery of the state, and genre scenes built small comedies from the encounter of public officialdom with private life. Edmonds, a banker used to ledgers and figures, may well have enjoyed the subject.","c":"Edmonds works like a playwright: he stages the room, places the actors and lets gesture tell the story. The picture is fluent, observant and gently humorous, and its social detail has real value as history. The weakness is the sweetness that makes every family cheerful and every official harmless. Beside Mount's more vigorous genre scenes he is more genial and less pointed, but the craft is secure."},"met:10843":{"d":"A new bonnet is the center of a household comedy in this domestic interior. A man, a woman, a girl and a dog appear in the scene, with tongs, a bottle, glassware and vegetables suggesting a kitchen. The oil on canvas, about 64 by 77 cm, is dated 1858. It belongs to American genre painting of the mid-century, small narrative pictures of ordinary life made for a middle-class public.","i":"A new bonnet is a classic subject of gentle domestic humor. Such scenes typically played on the contrast between the pleasure of finery and the household's purse, with a husband's reaction as the comic hinge. Genre painters of the 1850s liked small comedies of household economy, where vanity and thrift met without serious harm. The kitchen props place the scene in a modest home, where a bonnet is a treat.","c":"The kitchen props give Edmonds a small still life within the genre scene, and his habit of careful detail makes the room convincing. He has the novelist's eye for a household moment, and the humor is kind. The limits are the same as in his other pictures: a comfortable moral and a tidy cheerfulness. It is skilled, entertaining and unthreatening, a period comedy that reads at a glance."},"nga:12146":{"d":"A young, nude, light-skinned man stands at the centre of a rocky, mountainous landscape in this square panel. His muscular body faces the viewer, but he turns his head in profile to the right and looks down at the coral-red cloth he drops onto a white one already lying on the ground. With his other hand he holds a fawn-brown animal skin over his shoulder, and a red-edged gold halo gleams behind his head. Deeply ridged, mostly barren mountains rise around him, a pale blue stream curves along the right, and the sky deepens from topaz at the horizon to navy at the top.","i":"The panel shows John the Baptist as a young man who leaves home for the wilderness, casting off his fine clothes and taking up the rough animal skin that will be his only garment. It is a scene of renunciation that the Gospels do not narrate but later tradition supplied. With the Stigmata of Saint Francis, it formed part of the predella of an altarpiece for the Florentine church of Santa Lucia dei Magnoli, both saints being associated with solitude and retreat.","c":"A remarkable small panel for its date: the nude body is studied with real interest in anatomy and weight, and the barren, pleated mountains are drawn with a geologist's patience and tinted with an almost abstract clarity. John's profile, calm and a little aloof, suits the subject. The scale is tiny and the space shallow, yet it feels monumental, as if a figure meant for a large altarpiece had been shrunk without loss."},"nga:281":{"d":"In a landscape of chiselled rocky mountains beyond a grassy meadow and stream, two men in dove-grey robes react to a flame-red cross in the sky. At the left Saint Francis kneels with his hands raised, a gold halo behind his head; five thin gold lines run from the cross to touch his hands, feet and right side. At the right a second man sits, leaning back on one hand and shielding his brow with the other as he looks up; a small red book lies on the grass. Behind, three tall peaks are tinted icy blue, shell pink and pale green under a violet sky.","i":"In September 1224, on Mount La Verna, Francis is said to have seen a vision of a winged seraph bearing the crucified Christ and to have received the marks of Christ's Passion on his own body, the first recorded stigmata. His companion, traditionally Brother Leo, was nearby. This small panel was part of the predella of an altarpiece painted for the Florentine church of Santa Lucia dei Magnoli, where narrative scenes beneath the main panel told stories of saints.","c":"Domenico Veneziano's gift was light, and even in a panel the size of a book he fills the scene with a clear, cool daylight, set off by the pink, blue and green of the peaks. The raised hands against the red cross are straightforward and moving, and the companion's shielded eyes are a very human touch. The fantasy mountains, like coloured sugar, belong more to a storybook than to the drama of La Verna, which is part of their charm."},"nga:362":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, the Virgin in a sapphire-blue robe over a petal-pink dress supports the nude Christ Child, their gold-haloed heads tilted together so that her cheek brushes the top of his head. Both have marble-white skin faintly tinged with green and wavy blond hair. She looks down to the right under a sheer white veil; the child, standing on one foot, curls his hand round her little finger and wraps an arm around her neck. Her dress is edged in moss green and set with pearls and coloured stones. Stylised white and pink roses grow against a royal-blue ground behind.","i":"Intimate half-length Madonnas like this were made in Florence in large numbers for private devotion, to be hung in a bedroom or family room. Mary's cheek resting on the Child's head and his playful hold on her finger present the Incarnation as family affection. Roses are old emblems of the Virgin, called the rose without thorns, and the blue of her robe was often painted with costly ultramarine, honouring her.","c":"A tender, graceful picture: the close tilt of the two heads, the pearl-trimmed dress and the roses pressed flat against the blue give it a jewel-like decorative surface, and the faint green in the flesh tones lends the faces a cool, pearly sheen. Domenico Veneziano is less warmly human than Fra Filippo Lippi, and the faces are somewhat composed and remote. The light and the delicate colour are what remain, and they remain very well."},"met:11290":{"d":"A waterfall drops through a tall, narrow canvas, about 66 by 33 cm, with a mountain and trees. The title places the viewpoint at Glacier Point, high above Yosemite Valley in California, looking across to Yosemite Falls. The Met's keywords record the trees, the mountain and the falls. The unusually tall format echoes the height of the cascade, and the 1879 picture is a Western landscape of the kind sold to collectors who wanted a keepsake of the valley.","i":"Yosemite became a national symbol after Abraham Lincoln signed a grant in 1864 setting the valley aside for public protection, and painters and photographers carried its image to eastern audiences. Americans read such landscapes as proof that the young nation had a wild grandeur to rival the cathedrals of the old world. The view from Glacier Point, with its dizzying height, was the one that made the valley's scale visible at a glance.","c":"William Keith, Scottish-born and a leading painter in California, uses the narrow format cleverly to make the water's fall feel immense. The picture is more descriptive than poetic, closer to Bierstadt's careful detail than to the tonal reverie of Keith's later work. It is capable, even impressive, but the subject carries more of the weight than the painting does. Collectors loved such views, and the best of them are more than souvenirs."},"met:11289":{"d":"A storm closes in over an open landscape: wind bends the trees, cattle stand in the field and a man is among them, in a wide canvas about 76 by 127 cm. The Met's keywords record the wind, trees, a man, cattle and the storm, and the pastoral scene is dated 1880. It is a moderately large exhibition landscape, with weather rather than a famous place as its subject.","i":"A storm threatening a pastoral scene is an old device for contrasting human and animal calm with the power of nature, and nineteenth-century viewers valued the drama of weather as a reminder that nature could be both nurturing and awesome. The man and the cattle give a human scale and a sense of shelter about to be needed. Keith, based in San Francisco, painted such weather pictures alongside his grander Western views.","c":"An 1880 date puts the picture at the point where Keith's clear, descriptive style was beginning to give way to a more atmospheric manner learned from the Barbizon painters. A storm suits that shift, since it lets mass and mood count for more than detail. The picture is competent and atmospheric, though the herd and the herder have the stock quality of pastoral figures; the weather is the true subject."},"met:12822":{"d":"George Washington and William Lee, his enslaved manservant, are shown together in a vertical canvas about 91 by 71 cm, dated 1780. The Met's keywords add a horse, and the pairing of general and servant makes the picture a double portrait with the air of a military scene. Washington is the focus and Lee the attendant at his side, painted by a former member of Washington's staff who had known the general in the army.","i":"William Lee, called Billy, was Washington's valet throughout the Revolutionary War, and Washington freed him in his will. Portraits of generals seldom name the servants who accompany them, which makes this picture an unusual record of the world in which the nation's commander lived. Trumbull had served as Washington's aide in 1775 and drew on that memory when he began a career of painting the Revolution.","c":"Trumbull's early Washington is stiffer than his later work, and the drawing shows a young painter still learning. Its interest is historical rather than aesthetic, and the real gain is the presence of Lee as a named figure rather than a stock servant. As a likeness it matters because it comes from a man who had known the general; as a painting it is serviceable, with some awkwardness in pose and space."},"nga:50219":{"d":"A white-haired man stands full-length on a sandy shore, one hand on his hip and the other resting on a large, rusty anchor, his head turned toward us and one knee slightly bent. He wears an olive-green coat with brass buttons over a matching waistcoat, a white cravat, knee breeches, white stockings and buckled black shoes. A pile of barrels and crates lies to the left, and behind him are a cloudy sky and a glimpse of sea with distant cliffs. The canvas is tall, about 233 by 134 cm.","i":"The anchor, crates and barrels, and the sea make the sitter a man of overseas trade, and the full-length format, once reserved for aristocrats and rulers, claims for a merchant the dignity of rank. Patrick Tracy is generally identified as a Newburyport, Massachusetts, merchant and shipowner. The picture shows how American commercial families of the 1780s borrowed British grand-manner conventions to proclaim success, while the plain, well-cut dress insists on sober respectability.","c":"Trumbull manages a confident pose and a convincingly specific figure, and the clear rendering of buttons and buckles shows his taste for exact detail. The setting, a convenient shore of barrels and anchor under cloud, is a little stagey, and the long figure is stiffer than Copley's merchants or the English models he admired. Still, it is a clear, forthright statement of American commercial pride and a rare full-length from his hand."},"met:12829":{"d":"A tiny portrait of Thomas Jefferson, painted in oil on a mahogany panel only about 11 by 8 cm, a little larger than a playing card. The Met's keywords record Jefferson and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a small head-and-shoulders likeness of the kind Trumbull made in the late 1780s, close in scale to a miniature but painted in oil on a wood panel rather than in watercolour on ivory.","i":"Trumbull painted small oil portraits on panel as studies for his great history paintings of the American Revolution, and Jefferson, as the principal author of the Declaration of Independence, was essential to the best known of them. In 1788 Jefferson was the United States minister in Paris, where he welcomed the young painter. A portrait of this size could serve as a working study for a larger canvas or as a keepsake sent to a friend.","c":"Trumbull's small heads from life are often more direct than his large compositions, which can lose the sitter in the ceremony. Their limits are those of scale, since detail and characterisation must be compressed. A tiny panel like this is valuable as a document, giving a Jefferson of intellect and reserve, and as a painting it is modest and rewards slow looking rather than grand display."},"met:12828":{"d":"British soldiers in battle fill a very large horizontal canvas, about 180 by 272 cm. The museum's keywords record only soldiers, battles and men, and the title names the event: the sortie of the British garrison of Gibraltar against the Spanish siege works in November 1781, during the Great Siege of Gibraltar. Painted in 1789, it is an exhibition-scale history picture by an American painter, set in contemporary uniform rather than in antique dress.","i":"Trumbull, an American who had studied in London under Benjamin West, took a British victory as his subject. West and Copley had already shown that a modern battle, with named officers in contemporary dress, could carry the dignity once reserved for ancient history, and Copley was then painting another episode of the same siege. The defence of Gibraltar, which ended only in 1783, was a fresh and celebrated memory, and the picture feeds the period's appetite for military heroism.","c":"The ambition is plain in the scale, and the research into uniform and incident shows the discipline Trumbull learned from West. Crowded battle scenes of this kind often struggle to find one focus, and the formula of the wounded hero can feel staged. It is accomplished but cool, more a record than an experience, and less moving than his own Bunker Hill. It is of interest chiefly as an American's British subject."},"met:12830":{"d":"A man's portrait in oil on a mahogany panel about 9 by 7 cm, roughly the size of a playing card, painted around 1790. The Met's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a small head-and-shoulders likeness from life, of the kind Trumbull made of leading figures of the Revolution as studies for his large history paintings. The sitter, Thomas Mifflin, was a Philadelphia merchant, general and statesman.","i":"Mifflin (1744-1800) served as Washington's aide and as quartermaster general in the Revolution, and he was president of Congress when Washington resigned his commission in December 1783, a moment Trumbull later painted for the Capitol Rotunda. In 1790 he became governor of Pennsylvania. A small portrait like this probably served as a record of his face for that large composition, as well as a keepsake.","c":"Trumbull's small portraits from life are among the most direct things he made, and with a face about the size of a thumbnail he needs few strokes to register character. They have more candour than the finished heads in the big pictures. Within the limits of scale, the likeness is compact and reliable, though the format leaves no room for subtlety of pose or setting."},"nga:41579":{"d":"Alexander Hamilton is shown from the chest up in a vertical canvas about 76 by 61 cm, looking off to the left with his chin tipped slightly up. He has blue eyes under straight brows, a straight nose, rosy cheeks, full lips and a double chin, and gray, wavy hair brushed back from a high forehead. A lace-edged ivory cravat is tied in a bow and tucked into the high collar of a camel-brown coat. He is warmly lit from the front against a dark brown ground.","i":"In 1792 Hamilton was Secretary of the Treasury, the architect of the new federal government's finances and a leading Federalist. Trumbull, his friend and fellow Federalist, was painting portraits of the nation's founders. The dress is the plain, good coat of a statesman, not a uniform. A portrait of this kind advertised seriousness and the confidence of the young republic's leaders, and it was a private image as much as a public one.","c":"This is a plain, forthright likeness: the warm frontal light and dark ground concentrate attention on the face, and the unflattered features, the double chin and the lightly lifted chin, give a vivid sense of a real, confident man. It lacks the brilliance of Stuart's handling and the depth of Copley's best, but as a record of one of the republic's sharpest minds it is straightforward and convincing."},"met:12823":{"d":"Washington appears with his horse and a soldier in a small vertical canvas, about 67 by 47 cm, painted in the early 1790s. The Met's keywords record the horse, the soldier and the American Revolution, and the title sets the moment before the Battle of Trenton, the surprise attack of December 1776 that revived the patriot cause. It is a modest-size, heroic military portrait, with the general in his campaign setting rather than in a studio pose.","i":"Washington's victory at Trenton on 26 December 1776, after the crossing of the Delaware, turned a run of defeats into hope. Trumbull, who had served on his staff in 1775, was among the artists who set about building the visual memory of the general as a heroic leader. A portrait of Washington before a battle, with horse and soldier, ties the man to the cause and shows the resolve that Americans of the 1790s attributed to him.","c":"Trumbull wanted to show Washington as a military hero and not only as a statesman, and the idea is bold: the general in his element. The execution tends to be stiffer than the conception, as in much early American heroic painting, and the likeness is more official than lived. Even so, the sense of purpose makes the small picture a useful record of how the republic first pictured its commander."},"met:12824":{"d":"A tiny portrait of the Italian sculptor Giuseppe Ceracchi, in oil on a wood panel only about 10 by 8 cm. The Met's keywords record the sitter and the portrait. Painted about 1792, it is a small likeness from life, closer in size to a miniature than to a gallery portrait, of a man Trumbull met in the United States, probably in Philadelphia, where the national government then sat.","i":"Ceracchi (1751-1801) came to America in 1791 hoping to win a commission for a grand monument to the American Revolution, and he made busts of Washington and other leaders while he was there. The project found no funds, and he returned to Europe, where as a fervent republican he was later executed in Paris for an alleged plot against Napoleon. A small portrait like this records a friendship as well as a face.","c":"Trumbull's small portraits from life have a candour his big history paintings often lack, and an expressive sculptor makes a lively sitter. At this size nothing can be elaborated: pose, glance and character are registered in a few touches. The picture is slight in ambition but vivid in presence, a likeness that gains from its intimacy and from the sense that painter and sitter knew each other."},"met:12819":{"d":"A vertical portrait of Alexander Hamilton, about 78 by 63 cm, painted between 1804 and 1806. The Met's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a bust-length likeness with the face as its focus. The date range begins in the year of his death, so the portrait dates from the time after the duel with Aaron Burr, and is one of several memorial images of the statesman made by his friend Trumbull.","i":"Hamilton, born in the West Indies, rose to be Washington's aide, a principal author of The Federalist and the first Treasury secretary, and was killed in the duel with Burr in July 1804. His early death made him a martyr for Federalists, and the demand for his portrait grew. Trumbull, who had known him and painted him from life in the 1790s, was able to supply memorial versions that gave the statesman's likeness a lasting form.","c":"Like the other posthumous versions Trumbull made, this is a bust-length image that turns a living, quick-witted man into a monument. His honest likeness and plain presentation keep it from pomp, but repetition of the same type invites a certain staleness. As a historical document it is valuable; as a work of art it is solid, restrained and slightly cool, with a quiet dignity that suits its commemorative purpose."},"met:12825":{"d":"A man, John Murray, is portrayed in a bust-length oil on canvas about 76 by 61 cm, painted about 1806. The Met's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a straightforward commissioned portrait from Trumbull's New York years, the sitter a man of means presented plainly and directly. Its size, date and source match those of the portrait of Mrs. John Murray, so it was apparently one of a pair.","i":"Trumbull returned to New York in 1804 and for a few years supported himself with portraits of the city's merchants, professionals and officials. Pairs of husband and wife portraits, hung as pendants, were a standard way for families to record themselves, and they announced standing, respectability and marriage. The data does not say which John Murray this is, so his trade and rank cannot be read from the picture.","c":"These New York portraits are efficient rather than inspired. Trumbull's strengths, clarity of drawing and plain truthfulness, suit a sitter who wanted a good likeness, but they lack the brilliance of Stuart's flesh painting or the psychological reach of Copley's best, and the formula of dark ground and simple coat is repeated. It is a sound, workmanlike portrait that does its job."},"met:12827":{"d":"A woman, Mrs. John Murray, is portrayed in a bust-length oil on canvas about 76 by 61 cm, painted about 1806. The Met's keywords record only the woman and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: the wife's companion to the Met's portrait of John Murray, with the same size and date, meant to hang opposite her husband's. Women's portraits of these years typically show the high-waisted dress and simple hairstyle of the early republic.","i":"She appears under her husband's name, as married women did in titles of the time, which tells us how the portrait was framed socially: as part of a household. Paired portraits were a feature of American domestic display, and the wife's showed her as a respectable, well-dressed partner. The neoclassical fashions of the early republic, with simple lines and light fabrics inspired by antiquity, are the usual language of such portraits.","c":"A wife's portrait gave Trumbull less scope for display than the great figures he painted elsewhere, and the result is quiet and competent. He succeeds in straightforward presence and clean drawing and falls short in the freshness of colour and the lively flesh tones of his rivals. It is a polite, reliable portrait rather than an exciting one, valuable as a pendant to its husband's."},"nga:567":{"d":"Hamilton is shown from the chest up in a vertical canvas about 77 by 61 cm, looking off to the left against a dark brown ground, his shoulders angled the same way. He is clean-shaven, with low dark brows, a bumped nose, a strong jaw and a slight smile, and his gray hair is brushed back and gathered loosely at the nape. A cream ascot is tied in a bow and tucked into the collar of a black coat. Light from the left creates an amber glow around his face.","i":"Hamilton died in July 1804 after his duel with Aaron Burr, so a portrait of about 1806 was made after his death, from earlier sittings, sketches or other likenesses. His friends and admirers wanted images to keep his memory, and Trumbull, a close friend, was a natural choice. The plain black coat and quiet expression present the statesman as a man of intellect and restraint, the face of an admired and mourned founder.","c":"As a posthumous likeness it sets a slight smile and an easy alertness against the stiffness of more official portraits, and the amber light around the face on a dark ground is a simple, effective scheme. The face may have the slightly composite look of portraits assembled from memory and earlier images, and it lacks the immediacy of a living sitter. As a memorial image it is dignified and humane."},"nga:34061":{"d":"A man named William Rogers is portrayed in a bust-length canvas about 78 by 64 cm, dated by the museum between 1804 and 1808. The museum records nothing more about the picture, so it is described by type: a half-length portrait of the kind Trumbull made in New York after returning from London, for a sitter who wanted a plain, truthful likeness, with the face as the point and little else added.","i":"Between 1804 and 1808 Trumbull worked in New York as a portraitist, painting the city's men and women of means for a living while his large history projects waited. The sitter's occupation and rank are not recorded here, so the portrait can be read only as the record of a respectable citizen who commissioned a likeness for his family. Portraits of this type were meant to hang in a parlour or office and to pass down in the family.","c":"Trumbull's plain, unflattering approach has virtues and limits. The drawing is firm and the likeness direct, but his work of these years is less colourful than Stuart's and less dramatic than Sully's. Taken as a type, the picture is a representative, serviceable example of his New York portraits of the early 1800s, honest in intent and a little cool in effect."},"met:13187":{"d":"A general is portrayed in a vertical canvas about 86 by 68 cm, painted about 1855. The Met's keywords record the general, the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a half-length likeness of Winfield Scott, then the commanding general of the United States Army, presented as a soldier of rank by a painter who taught drawing at West Point. It is an official-style portrait, a record of public service rather than a private likeness.","i":"Scott (1786-1866) was the dominant American soldier of his age: a hero of the War of 1812, the conqueror of Mexico City in 1847, general-in-chief from 1841 to 1861 and an unsuccessful Whig candidate for president in 1852. In 1855 Congress gave him the brevet rank of lieutenant general, the first to hold it since Washington, and a portrait of about that year honours a man at the height of his reputation.","c":"Weir was a respected academic painter and teacher, and the portrait has the correct, clear drawing and sober finish expected of West Point's drawing master. It is dignified rather than dynamic: the old soldier is presented as an institution, and the picture does not dig into temperament. For a portrait of so famously punctilious a man it is notably restrained, which may be its best quality."},"cma:143140":{"d":"A close, detailed study of a riverbed, with a haphazard arrangement of angled boulders, slippery moss, splintered tree branches and dense undergrowth. The site is North Conway, New Hampshire, a village that served as a popular jumping-off point for tourists visiting the White Mountains, and the scene is deliberately untamed, with no grand view to lead the eye. Oil on canvas, about 43 by 53 centimeters, dated 1851, by David Johnson.","i":"Johnson's devotion to carefully observing nature is evident in his exact rendering of the riverbed. Contemporary travel guidebooks cautioned against the physical hazards of venturing into such inner recesses of the woods; one warned that wild forest-clambering could be akin to fighting a phalanx of porcupines. The picture thus offers an armchair experience of the wilderness that tourists were told to fear and admire at once.","c":"An uncompromising close-up: the clutter of rock, moss and branches is painted with a Ruskin-inspired devotion to fact that leaves no room for picturesque composition. It is a difficult picture to enjoy as a whole, since it lacks a focus, but it rewards looking at details, and it shows an American landscapist testing how far truth to nature could go. A fine example of the 1850s taste for exact observation."},"met:11255":{"d":"A landscape in oil on canvas, about 48 by 71 centimeters, dated 1856, by David Johnson, titled Near Squam Lake, New Hampshire. Trees are the visible subject, and the title places the view near the lake. Squam Lake, in the Lakes Region of central New Hampshire, with its wooded shores and nearby White Mountains, was a picturesque lake that attracted painters and tourists in the 1850s.","i":"Johnson was a second-generation painter of the Hudson River School who spent summers in New England and the Hudson Valley making oil sketches and detailed landscapes, and his work of the 1850s shows the influence of John Ruskin's call to paint nature exactly as it is. The title places the view near the lake, not on it, which may mean woodland or pasture rather than a lake panorama; the particular spot is not known.","c":"A competent landscape of the 1850s in which careful observation of trees and foliage, rather than the grand drama of Church or Cole, is the main virtue. Johnson's strength is quiet exactness, and his weakness a certain dryness. It belongs to a respectable and attractive strand of American landscape."},"nga:129358":{"d":"Three pears and an apple form the whole subject of a small still life, painted in oil on board and measuring about 22 by 34 cm, dated 1857. The museum records only the genre, so the picture is described by type: a quiet tabletop arrangement of fruit, a cabinet picture for a parlour. Johnson was known as a landscape painter of the Hudson River school, and a work like this probably shows the same close attention to surface and light that he gave to rocks and trees.","i":"American still life of the 1850s often turned to fruit, a modest, domestic subject that suited a growing middle-class market and the moral tone of the time: abundance without extravagance. The critic John Ruskin, who urged artists to paint nature with truth to detail, also encouraged landscape painters to turn their precise gaze on small objects. Whatever symbolism viewers saw in pears and apples was gentle: fruitfulness, home and the quiet pleasures of the season.","c":"The interest here is the meeting of a landscape painter's careful eye with a few ordinary fruits. It lacks the showy display and illusionism of professional still-life specialists, and it is better taken as a modest, honest study in which the humility of the subject is the point. It is small but sincere, and a useful reminder that the Hudson River generation looked closely at more than mountains."},"nga:34110":{"d":"Edwin Forrest is shown half-length, turned slightly to one side, with a serious expression, a prominent mustache and slightly disheveled dark hair thinning at the crown. He wears a black coat over a white shirt with a high collar and a black bow tie, and a gold hoop is visible in his buttonhole. The background is a simple, softly shaded neutral ground. The canvas is about 61 by 51 cm and was painted in 1871.","i":"Forrest (1806-1872) was the leading American actor of his day, famed for muscular, declamatory performances as Metamora, Othello and Lear, and for a bitter rivalry with the English actor William Macready that fed the Astor Place riot of 1849. By 1871 he was in his mid-sixties and near the end of his life. The plain black dress and serious face show the old idol without stage costume, as a man rather than a role.","c":"Johnson is better known as a landscape painter, and the portrait is correspondingly direct: a plain composition, the face carefully drawn, the black clothes simplifying everything else. It avoids theatricality entirely, which is a daring choice with a great actor as sitter, though some viewers will miss the vitality of his stage presence. It is an honest, serious late likeness rather than a flattering one."},"met:11256":{"d":"A river landscape in oil on canvas, about 58 by 94 centimeters, dated 1884, by David Johnson, titled On the Unadilla, New York. Trees are the main visible feature, and the wide format suits a river view, which unrolls across the canvas from one side to the other. The Unadilla is a river in central New York State, and the title places the viewer on or beside it.","i":"By 1884 David Johnson, then in his late fifties, had been painting the rivers and hills of New York State and New England for three decades, and his late landscapes tend to show a softer, more atmospheric touch than the sharp realism of his early career. The Unadilla, a quiet river winding among farms and woodland, suited that gentler mood, and the picture belongs to the later phase of the Hudson River School.","c":"Johnson's mature manner is easy to recognize: careful drawing of trees, a quiet unpretentious subject and a restrained palette. By 1884 the Hudson River School was out of fashion, and its painters continued in the older manner against the French-influenced taste of the day. Whether it adds anything to his range is a fair question, but it stands as a faithful representative of an honest, unglamorous tradition."},"met:11253":{"d":"A view of Bayside at New Rochelle, in Westchester County, New York, in oil on canvas, about 50 by 61 centimeters, dated 1886, by David Johnson. Sky, clouds, trees, water, a boat and a man are the elements, so the picture combines a waterside with a man and a boat beneath an open sky. By 1886 Johnson was nearing sixty, and the picture belongs to the quieter work of his later years.","i":"New Rochelle, on Long Island Sound northeast of New York City, was a suburb and summer resort in the 1880s, and its coastal inlets and farms offered painters near-to-hand subjects. A named local place, instead of a grand mountain view, reflects the late-century taste for intimate, familiar landscape. The figure and the boat are signs of the everyday activity of the waterfront rather than of any story.","c":"A late, modest Johnson that trades the grand White Mountain and Hudson River views of his youth for an everyday corner of suburban coast. The title and the boat, man and sky suggest a gentle, quiet scene, in keeping with the older painter's habits. It demonstrates the persistence of Hudson River School observation in the age of Impressionism."},"met:436647":{"d":"The same palace is seen from the south, with its formal garden spread before it, in a panel about 39 by 55 cm painted about 1668-70. The Met's keywords record a man alongside the house and garden, so one small figure presumably gives scale, as such figures do in Van der Heyden's views. The picture is characterised by the title and the artist's manner: exact brick and stone, clipped parterres and a clear, even light, in oil on wood.","i":"Van der Heyden painted at least two views of the Huis ten Bosch, from the east and from the south, of nearly identical size, so they were probably made as a pair, a way of walking round a house in paint. Such pictures were prized as souvenirs of a celebrated place. The Dutch loved formal gardens, which showed nature tamed by reason and wealth, and a man in the garden gives the scene a human measure.","c":"A formal garden suited Van der Heyden, whose eye delighted in regular geometry: paths and borders measure the space as much as the house does. The tiny figure is a convenient scale mark, not a character. The picture is cooler than his sunlit canal views and less inventive than his architectural fantasies, but it shows a precision that lets us see exactly how a princely garden was laid out."},"met:436648":{"d":"The Huis ten Bosch, the princely country palace at The Hague, is seen with its formal garden from the east, in a wide panel about 39 by 55 cm. The Met's keywords record only the house and garden, so the description rests on the title and the artist's manner: a precise architectural view in which brick, stone and clipped garden are rendered with the clarity Van der Heyden brought to every building he painted. Painted in oil on wood about 1668-70, it is a topographical portrait of a famous residence, small enough for a cabinet.","i":"The Huis ten Bosch, or house in the woods, was built in the 1640s for Amalia van Solms, widow of Stadtholder Frederik Hendrik, and was still her residence when Van der Heyden painted it. Its great hall was decorated as a memorial to her husband, and the house and garden stood for the wealth and taste of the House of Orange. A view like this let admirers possess an image of the property, and the formal garden was a pride of Dutch design.","c":"Van der Heyden turns a famous house into a clear, cool, almost model-like image: each brick and window precisely drawn, the light even, the garden's geometry giving the whole a tidy structure. The accuracy has documentary value. The weakness is a certain stillness, since the picture is closer to a portrait of a building than of a place in changing light. Among his architectural views it is a careful, pleasant example, not an extraordinary one."},"nga:135093":{"d":"A sunny canal runs diagonally into the distance, its far bank lined with tall leafy trees and red brick houses trimmed with white stone. The water ripples gently, reflecting white clouds and a pale blue sky, and low barges lie tied up along its edges. Tiny people walk and work under the trees, a gray church spire rises beyond, and people and a horse cross a low arched bridge. The nearest building has a stepped roofline and a lantern-shaped cupola. The panel is about 33 by 39 cm, signed IVH on a boat.","i":"Canal views celebrated what Dutch towns were proud of: orderly brick houses, trees planted along the water, clean quays and busy trade. Van der Heyden was also an engineer: put in charge of Amsterdam's street lighting in 1669, he later helped invent the fire hose, and he brought the same love of order to his paintings. The scene is a picture of civic pride and tidy prosperity, with nothing hidden, and the viewer is invited to admire.","c":"This is a small picture with a surprising calm: the diagonal of the bank, the low horizon about a fifth up the panel and the reflecting water give a clear, spacious design. The tiny figures are efficient, and brick and leaves are rendered with great exactness. The risk is that exactness becomes a catalogue, but the light holds everything together. It is a small, deft example of Van der Heyden's art."},"nga:50883":{"d":"Across a dirt road, a tall brick wall flanks an ornamented stone gateway, and beyond it a white marble manor house stands sunlit on a low hill beneath a pale blue sky that fills two-thirds of the panel. Statues line the roof and lawns. Two men in brimmed hats pass under the arch, a woman with a baby tied to her back holds out a hand to them, and dogs wander about, one raising a leg against the gate. At the wall's foot a man in royal blue touches a slender dog. Oil on oak, about 50 by 71 cm.","i":"An architectural fantasy, or capriccio, combines real and invented buildings into a scene that looks plausible but never existed. The marble house, statues and fountain are an ideal of wealth and taste, set beside the daily life of the road: dogs, passers-by and a woman with a baby who seems to appeal to the men. The cracked marble fragment at the wall's foot adds a quiet note of decay. Viewers could take it as charm, as a contrast of rich and poor, or as a reminder that splendour fades.","c":"Van der Heyden's pleasure in brick, stone and foliage, painted so precisely that one could count the bricks, makes the invented place feel more real than reality. The light and the broad blue sky save the dense detail from dryness. The people and dogs are small and doll-like, and the whole is a little too tidy, but the cool order, the humour of the dogs and the clarity of space give it a particular charm."},"met:437237":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in a vertical canvas about 125 by 96 cm, dated 1650. The Met records only the woman and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: an Amsterdam commissioned portrait of the mid-seventeenth century, in which a respectable wife is usually shown in dark, rich dress with white linen at collar and cuffs, the face lit against a plain, shadowed ground, as in the work of Rembrandt's pupils and followers. The size suits a room in a prosperous townhouse.","i":"In the Dutch Republic, portraits of married couples were often painted as pendants, and a wife's portrait advertised her husband's standing as well as her own modesty and household virtue. Black clothes and fine white linen signalled both Calvinist sobriety and wealth. The sitter here is unnamed, so her place in society can only be guessed. Ovens, reportedly a pupil of Rembrandt, left Amsterdam soon after this date and later became court painter at Gottorf.","c":"Ovens learned smooth, clear modelling from the Amsterdam portraitists around Rembrandt and Flinck, and in 1650 he is at his most polished: the pose is dignified and the surface refined. The result is more courteous than searching, and it lacks Rembrandt's psychological depth and Hals's vivacity. It is a competent, characteristic example by a painter who did best with formal, elegant sitters."},"nga:101":{"d":"A young woman in a fog-gray gown stands full-length outdoors, her body turned almost in profile to our right and her head toward us as she looks off to the left. Auburn hair is tied back with a sand-brown ribbon, and long tendrils fall down her back. A harvest-gold sash circles her waist, and a laurel-green cape bordered in gold falls from her shoulders, part of it gathered forward in both hands. Bright light from the upper left falls on a stone wall, a tree and a cloudy landscape. The canvas is about 239 by 147 cm.","i":"The title names her as the wife of Thomas Scott Jackson, and the museum's data records her name as Mary Keating. Romney, like Reynolds and Gainsborough, often dressed sitters in loose, timeless costume rather than the fashion of the day, so that a woman might appear as a figure from a pastoral or classical world and the portrait would not date. The cape, sash and pearl bracelets suggest rank and elegance, and the dim park adds a touch of romance.","c":"It is a full-length of real grace: the long figure, the shimmering cape and the harmony of gray, gold and green show Romney's gift for tying a figure to its setting through colour. The pose is stately but not stiff, and the loosely brushed landscape recedes convincingly. The likeness itself is generalised, a pretty, composed face, so the picture says more about taste than about personality. Within its type, it is an elegant example."},"met:437500":{"d":"A portrait of Lady Elizabeth Stanley, Countess of Derby, painted between 1776 and 1778 on a canvas about 127 by 102 cm. The Met's keywords record only the sitter, so the picture is described by type and format: a fashionable society portrait on the standard half-length canvas of 50 by 40 inches, in which Romney typically gave his sitters a soft, simplified grace, smooth modelling and a gently idealised face against a loosely painted setting.","i":"Lady Elizabeth (1753-1797) was the daughter of a duke and wife of the 12th Earl of Derby, married in 1774 and admired as a beauty and leader of fashion. Portraits like this were statements of rank and good looks, and were often circulated in engravings. Her marriage later broke down, and the Earl married the actress Elizabeth Farren after her death in 1797; the elegant portrait gives no hint of that. It shows what her class wanted of a likeness: ease, beauty, taste.","c":"Romney's half-lengths of fashionable women are at their best in the soft, flowing handling of the face and in the way a few strokes suggest silk or lace. The weakness is sameness: his beauties resemble one another, and the individuality of the sitter yields to the ideal. This is a good, characteristic example. It pleases more than it probes, which is exactly what the sitter's world wanted from a portrait."},"cma:95269":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Jane Hoskyns, is portrayed in an oil on canvas about 70 by 59 cm, dated about 1778-80. The museum describes the sitter's circumstances but not the picture itself, so the description stays with the type: a bust-length society portrait by Romney, in his usual manner of soft light, smooth modelling and a gently idealised face, made for a husband or family to hang at home.","i":"The museum notes that the sitter married Sir John Read in 1774 and that Romney painted him too, in 1788, so the couple stayed his patrons over a decade. Romney began as an apprentice in his father's cabinetmaker's shop in Lancashire, worked in the north until 1762 and then settled in London, where he rivalled Reynolds and Gainsborough. Like many portraitists, he dreamed of history painting, but his grandiose plans rarely went beyond drawings.","c":"Romney's talent lay in the tender, flattering portrait, and a modest bust-length like this is the sort of picture that built his income. The restraint of scale lets the face and the light do the work. What it lacks is grand invention: the history painter he wished to be is absent, and the sitter, like many of his women, may seem more graceful than individual. As a type it is pleasing, sound and typical."},"met:437498":{"d":"A woman is portrayed on a canvas about 76 by 63 cm, dated 1781, the standard three-quarter size of 30 by 25 inches in English portraiture. The Met's keywords record only the woman and the portrait, and the title says that her identification as Emily Bertie Pott, who died in 1782, is only traditional. The picture is therefore described by type: a fashionable society portrait in Romney's soft, smooth manner.","i":"Said to be marks an unproved identification, so the sitter's name should be treated as uncertain. If she is Emily Pott, the picture was painted a year before her death and may have become a memorial for her family, as portraits of the dead often did in an age of early mortality and no photography. Whoever she is, the picture shows what a prosperous woman of the 1780s wanted: a graceful, modest, becomingly dressed likeness.","c":"Romney at his most economical: an easy pose, an uncluttered ground and a softly lit face, with no props or elaborate costume to distract. The result is graceful but anonymous, and the uncertain identity makes the likeness hard to measure. Romney's women often blur into a type, and this one is no exception; the pleasure is in the delicacy of handling and not in a strong personality."},"nga:111":{"d":"A young girl stands in front of a landscape, shown from about the knees up, her body angled to the left and her face turned to us. Chestnut curls fall at her nape, bangs sweep across her forehead over slate-blue eyes, and a turned-up nose and pink bow mouth sit in a round face. She wears a white dress and a wide-brimmed ivory straw hat, with a broad petal-pink sash and a matching hat ribbon. Behind her a harvest-gold hillside rises under an aquamarine sky streaked with pink. The canvas is about 92 by 72 cm.","i":"Children's portraits boomed in late eighteenth-century Britain, when new ideas about childhood, from Rousseau and others, valued naturalness and open air. The straw hat, simple white dress and rural background present the girl as an innocent child in a pastoral setting, though her family's wealth is evident in the commission itself. Parents wanted charming, affectionate pictures that recorded a fleeting age.","c":"This is Romney at his freshest: the pinks, echoed in cheeks, sash, hat ribbon and sky, bind the picture into a single harmony, and the loosely brushed hillside contrasts with the smooth face and gives it air. The sweetness is considerable, perhaps more than modern taste prefers, and the child's individuality is more suggested than explored. As a picture of childhood it has real charm and a confident sense of colour."},"nga:112":{"d":"Seen from the waist up against a loosely painted landscape, a young woman turns her head to look at us over her shoulder. Light brown hair curls around her face, and a wide ivory straw hat casts a shadow across her forehead, its front set with a tawny bow and a gray-and-white striped ribbon. A sheer white cape edged with feathers or fur lies over a rose-pink satin dress, and her hands seem clasped in a muff. Behind her rise a golden hillside, pine-green trees and a pewter-streaked sky. The canvas is about 77 by 64 cm.","i":"The museum's data names the sitter as Charlotte Sneyd, who as Mrs. Davies Davenport was the wife of a Cheshire gentleman. The muff, cape and satin show a fashionable winter outfit, while the straw hat and rural backdrop belong to the pastoral taste of the day, which liked elegant sitters dressed as if for a country stroll. The glance over the shoulder is a stock gesture of charm in portraits of women in the 1780s.","c":"Romney arranges a delicate scheme of ivory, rose and gray against golden hills with great taste, and the sideways glance gives a spark of liveliness to what could be a bland face. The satin and the soft shadow of the hat are handled with ease, and the loose landscape gives air. The portrait is more about charm than character, and the sitter's personality stays politely out of reach. It shows the fashionable Romney at his most persuasive."},"nga:52277":{"d":"A man's portrait on a canvas about 77 by 65 cm, dated 1783-84. The museum offers no description, so the picture is characterised by type and by sitter: Sir William Hamilton, the British envoy at Naples, painted by Romney as a bust-to-waist likeness with the face as the focus, in the soft, even handling that marks Romney's male portraits. The canvas is the standard three-quarter size used for such likenesses.","i":"Hamilton (1730-1803) was British envoy at Naples from 1764 to 1800, a collector of Greek vases and a student of Vesuvius, whose observations reached the Royal Society. He visited England in 1783-84, which is probably when he sat to Romney. He later married Emma Hart in 1791; she had been Romney's favourite model in those very years and later became Nelson's lover. The portrait therefore stands at a crossing of art, antiquity and scandal.","c":"In the male portraits of these years Romney generally keeps flourish in check, and an intellectual and courtier like Hamilton calls for plain attention to the face. It lacks the sense of command of Reynolds's grand male portraits, and the effect is of a sensible, candid likeness rather than a statement. Its interest is as much historical as artistic: a face that belonged to one of the most colourful careers of the age."},"nga:1217":{"d":"A woman is portrayed on a canvas about 76 by 64 cm, the size English portraitists called three-quarter, painted sometime between 1783 and 1788. The museum gives no description of the picture, so it is characterised by type: a society portrait of a titled lady in Romney's soft, smooth manner, with the face as its focus. The museum's data also names her Lady Arabella Crosbie, presumably her name before marriage.","i":"The style Lady Arabella marks her as the daughter of an earl or a still higher peer, and she kept it after marrying a commoner, which is why she appears as Lady Arabella Ward. Romney's fashionable sitters included many such women of rank, whose portraits recorded marriage alliances and family pride. As the data gives no description, the pose and costume cannot be read, and the picture is best taken as a type.","c":"Romney's portraits of aristocratic women of the 1780s are marked by easy grace and a softening of features that flatters at some cost to personality. The standard canvas size reminds us that he ran a busy practice, painting hundreds of such likenesses at set prices. Without a description it is hard to praise or fault in detail; as a type it is elegant and a little generic, representative of what his clients came for."},"nga:1216":{"d":"A woman, Mrs. Alexander Blair, is portrayed on a canvas about 127 by 102 cm, dated 1787-89, the standard half-length size of 50 by 40 inches. The museum gives no description, so the picture is characterised by type: a society portrait on a large canvas, with room for pose, costume and background, in Romney's late 1780s style of smooth, simplified forms and a gently idealised face.","i":"Romney's years in Italy in 1773-75 left him with a taste for classical simplicity in pose and dress, and his later portraits often favour plain, flowing lines over fashionable fuss. A half-length portrait cost more than a head, and a husband who commissioned one signalled a household of means. The title gives only her husband's name, a common way of naming married women in the period, so her own identity must be sought elsewhere.","c":"A half-length gives Romney more room than a bust, and the larger canvas lets his talent for graceful arrangement show. His weakness is the sameness of the faces, which tend toward a pleasing ideal. Without a description of this picture, the verdict has to be general: a competent, polished late-1780s portrait of the kind that made him the rival of Reynolds, charming in effect and little concerned with probing character."},"nga:43201":{"d":"A fair-skinned man is shown from the waist up in three-quarter view, slightly turned to his right against a dark ground. He has light, curly hair, blue eyes and a straight nose, and wears an eighteenth-century military uniform: a high-collared dark blue coat with red facings and gold trim, a pale yellow waistcoat and a white frilled shirt. His hands hold nothing and no jewelry is visible. The canvas is about 76 by 64 cm, and the museum dates it only to between about 1780 and 1790.","i":"The uniform tells viewers that the sitter was an officer, though the description does not name his regiment or service, and the title gives only his surname. Officers of the period often had themselves painted in uniform to record rank and service, whether in wartime or on leave, and the plain dark background keeps all attention on the face and the bright trim. The vague date and bare title suggest that his identity has been lost, leaving him as a type.","c":"Romney keeps this portrait spare: no props, no hands, a dark ground. The uniform gives the picture its colour and crisp edge, and the face is pleasant and clearly seen. The result is more direct than the idealised Romney of his women's portraits, but it is also impersonal, and the sitter's character barely registers. It works as a clear, honest record of an officer; it is not a revelation."},"nga:46055":{"d":"A major-general is portrayed on a large vertical canvas, about 153 by 124 cm, dated between 1790 and 1792. The museum gives no description of the picture, so it is characterised by type: a portrait of a knighted army officer, large enough for a figure with uniform, insignia and a setting, painted in Romney's late manner of soft, simplified forms. The title records rank and honour, and the museum's data names the sitter as Archibald Campbell.","i":"The title gives rank and a knighthood, the two things the portrait existed to record. A major-general of the 1780s and 1790s had probably served in the American War or in India, and a knighthood was the reward for particular service. Without a described image, the pose and props cannot be read, but at this size the picture would have been designed to hang as a family monument, announcing the sitter's career to guests and descendants.","c":"Romney's gift for graceful women and children suits martial authority less well, and his male portraits of generals can be dignified without being commanding. The large canvas gives him room, and the likeness was presumably sound, but without a description the specific merits cannot be judged. As a type it is a solid, respectable record of a career, more an official statement than a revelation of the man."},"met:437504":{"d":"George Romney looks out from a self-portrait about 76 by 64 cm, dated 1795, when he was about sixty. The Met's keywords record the man and the self-portrait only, so the description rests on type: a bust-length likeness, the painter turned sitter on his own standard three-quarter canvas of 30 by 25 inches, the same size he used for many sitters. It is a private picture, made for himself or for his family and friends, not for a client.","i":"By 1795 Romney, once the rival of Reynolds, was in poor health and low spirits. In 1799 he left London for Kendal in the north, where his wife nursed him until his death in 1802. A late self-portrait invites comparison between how he painted others and how he saw himself, and also between the glamour of his society pictures and the plainer truth of an ageing man who still dreamed of grand history paintings he never finished.","c":"A self-portrait removes the demands of the commission, and a painter known for sweetening faces has no reason to flatter his own. Pictures of this kind are often the most candid in a portraitist's career, and this one should be read as a record of an intent, serious man in his sixties. It cannot match the polish of his society portraits, and it is better for not trying to."},"met:437499":{"d":"A man is portrayed on a canvas about 76 by 63 cm, the standard three-quarter size for a bust-to-waist likeness. The Met gives no date or sitter, recording only a man and a portrait, so the picture is described by type: a portrait by Romney in which the face is lit softly and modelled smoothly against a plain ground, with costume and background kept simple so that the likeness carries the picture.","i":"Romney's male sitters were gentry, clergy, officers and professional men, and portraits like this were made for exchange within families and for hanging in town houses and country seats. This sitter is anonymous, his name and career lost, which is the fate of many eighteenth-century portraits that outlived the family papers that explained them. What survives is the face and a fashionable moment, and there is something poignant in a likeness that was once treasured and is now a type.","c":"With no name to lean on, the picture has to stand on its painting. Romney's men are generally less flattered than his women, and an unfussy treatment gives the face a certain honesty, though it also means that the picture lacks the dash of his best female portraits. As a type it is competent, even, and slightly reticent, a decent example of the plain portrait on which his large practice depended."},"met:437502":{"d":"A woman, Mrs. Charles Frederick, born Martha Rigden and died in 1794, is portrayed on a three-quarter-size canvas about 76 by 63 cm, undated by the museum. The Met supplies her name and dates but no description of the picture, so it is characterised by type: a bust-to-waist society portrait by Romney, with a softly lit, smoothly modelled face and simple, flowing dress, the sort of likeness a husband commissioned to hang at home.","i":"The title gives her name in two forms, married and maiden, a useful habit for identifying women in portraits. Married women were usually known by their husband's name, and Romney's sitter lists record them that way. The note that she died in 1794 is a reminder that portraits outlived their sitters, becoming family memorials in an age of early death and no photography. Pairs of portraits often hung together, husband and wife facing each other.","c":"Romney's women are his best-known subjects, and a plain bust-to-waist canvas like this shows why: a few soft strokes give the face grace, the dress is suggested rather than counted, and the whole is easy on the eye. The cost is a sameness of type, with individuality yielding to a pleasing ideal. Without a described image the verdict has to be general: a polished and agreeable portrait, characteristic of its maker, and rarely more than that."},"met:437505":{"d":"A naval officer, Admiral Sir Chaloner Ogle (1726-1816), is portrayed on a three-quarter-size canvas about 76 by 62 cm. The museum gives no date and no description beyond the sitter's name and his rank, so the picture is characterised by type: a portrait of a senior sea officer by Romney, with the face lit softly against a plain ground, made as a record of a career for family and friends.","i":"Naval officers had themselves painted to mark promotion, a hard-won fortune or an honour, and an admiral's portrait announced the pinnacle of a service career. A man born in 1726 and living until 1816 passed through the Seven Years' War, the American Revolution and the Napoleonic wars, the great age of the Royal Navy. The Met's keywords record the rank of admiral, so he was presumably shown in the uniform that signalled his authority.","c":"Romney's gift lay in women and children, and his men, naval or military, can be flat. A sea officer is not an easy subject for a painter of soft, graceful forms, and it is no surprise if the portrait is more courteous than commanding. Without a description of the picture one cannot say more than that it is a sober, honest record of an admiral by a fashionable painter, more a document than an event."},"met:436607":{"d":"A large canvas, about 144 by 234 centimetres, brings together a woman and a girl with a group of symbols: a book, a skull, a bird, a cat and a dog. The museum calls the subject only An Allegory and lists these elements, so the exact arrangement, poses and colours cannot be described with confidence. The wide horizontal format and the crowd of emblems suggest a decorative picture for a grand room, painted in the lively Italian Baroque manner of Domenico Guidobono.","i":"Allegories of this kind were learned conceits: virtues, vices, seasons, senses or ages of life expressed through figures and objects that educated viewers were expected to decode. The skull and book belong to the long tradition of the vanitas, a reminder that learning, beauty and life are brief; birds, cats and dogs commonly stand for the soul, watchfulness or fidelity. The Met gives no fixed reading of the combination, which should be treated as open.","c":"An allegory with this many ingredients runs a risk: the emblems multiply faster than the eye can hold them, and the picture becomes a puzzle. With a subject that even the museum leaves unnamed, the work reads as a decorative curiosity more than a persuasive statement. Its interest is as an example of the late Baroque taste for learned, slightly theatrical conceits, and as a reminder that not every picture has a recoverable programme."},"met:435602":{"d":"On a gold ground, the Virgin and Child are accompanied by two saints, Philip the Apostle and Agnes of Rome, in a narrow upright panel of about 60 by 33 centimetres. The format and the gold background are those of the late Gothic devotional panel, in which holy figures were set in an idealised space of gold, as if in heaven. Donato de' Bardi, a painter active in Genoa, blended Gothic elegance with a growing concern for naturalistic form.","i":"Agnes, a Roman girl martyred in her early teens in the early fourth century, is the patron of young women and is usually shown with a lamb, a play on her name, and a martyr's palm. Philip, one of the twelve apostles, usually carries a cross or loaves. A male and a female saint beside the Virgin often point to the name saints of a married couple or a family, though the commission for this panel is not recorded.","c":"A refined late Gothic panel in which the gold ground, slender figures and delicate line do most of the work. The forms are conventional, and the pleasure is in ornament and rhythm more than in expression; the saints are attendants rather than participants. Its interest lies chiefly in what it shows of north Italian painting in the 1420s, still tied to Gothic taste while the Florentine Renaissance was under way, and in its jewel-like finish."},"cma:95288":{"d":"A Dutch peasant mother holds her baby in a tall canvas about 160 by 112 cm, posing formally in a garden. The mother's lacy headdress, an apple tree, a young bullock and a prominent red tulip at the figures' feet are all part of the scene, which the museum calls neither clearly religious nor clearly secular. Painted in 1892 by an American who settled in Holland, it is a large exhibition picture, composed with the stillness of an altarpiece.","i":"The museum reads the details as symbols: the lacy headdress can stand for a halo, the apple tree for the lost Eden recovered through the child's birth, the bullock for Christ's future sacrifice and the red tulip for the cup of sorrow. Early critics praised the technique and the maternal tenderness but doubted the religious merit. The picture reflects a nineteenth-century struggle to reconcile faith with science, and a search for the divine in women and nature.","c":"Hitchcock, who gave up law for painting, makes a sincere attempt to marry modern realism with religious feeling, and the peasant model gives it warmth and a believable weight. The symbolic apparatus, halo-like cap, tulip and bullock, can feel contrived, and the formal pose lacks the freshness of his Dutch contemporaries' open-air work. The ambiguity is its interest and its limit, and it holds up better as a document of faith in doubt than as a persuasive Madonna."},"met:11105":{"d":"A woman stands among trees with a windmill beyond them in a tall canvas about 113 by 91 cm, painted about 1895-1900. The Met's keywords record the tree, the windmill and the woman, and the title names vespers, the evening prayer of the church. The setting is the Dutch countryside, and the picture is a devotional genre scene by an American who settled in Holland and painted peasants in the roles of sacred stories.","i":"Vespers is the evening service, and the hour of quiet at the day's end is a natural time for reflection and prayer. Hitchcock saw in the Dutch peasant woman a living image of faith, and the windmill, a national emblem, roots the scene in place while the title lifts it into spiritual reverie. The painting reflects the late nineteenth-century wish to find religion in simple rural life, away from the doubts of the city.","c":"Hitchcock's Dutch pictures are known for luminous colour and sentimental feeling, and the windmill and the evening hour give this one a calm, simple structure. The risk is that piety slides into sweetness, and the monumental peasant can seem more posed than prayerful. As a late example of the American colony in Holland it is attractive and historically interesting, though it asks the viewer to accept its sentiment on trust."},"nga:50280":{"d":"Mary, in a royal-blue dress, floats on a thin, bright white crescent moon among nine winged angels above a deep landscape in a tiny panel about 21 by 16 cm. She presses her fingertips together and gazes down with almost closed eyes while three angels set a gold crown on her head, four hold the edges of her silvery cape, and two support the crescent. Gold light is enclosed within steel-gray clouds. Below lie rolling hills, trees and a distant village, and the paint is visibly cracked throughout.","i":"The Assumption is Mary's bodily ascent to heaven at the end of her life, here joined with her coronation as queen of heaven. The crescent moon beneath her recalls the woman of the Book of Revelation, clothed with the sun with the moon under her feet. Sittow, born in Reval and probably trained under Memling in Bruges, worked for Queen Isabella of Castile, who owned small devotional panels, and a panel of this size and date may belong to such a context.","c":"Flemish precision, the hazy blue distance, the jewel-like brooches and the delicately coloured angels, is married here to a tender, inward devotion. Sittow's tangerine, olive and topaz blue angels make a lively ring around the calm figure, and her lowered gaze is the heart of the picture. The cracked paint takes some of the polish away, and the symmetry is conventional, but as a small devotional image it is refined and affecting."},"nga:53":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a clean-shaven man holds one hand to his chest and rests the other on a ledge draped with a rug, looking down to our left with dark eyes, crow's feet and soft jowls. His chin-length reddish-blond hair has wispy bangs. He wears a white chemise, a gold brocade garment and a slate-blue cloak whose spotted fur lining turns over as a wide collar, with gold buttons decorated with thistles. He is lit from the left against black. The panel is small, about 34 by 24 cm.","i":"The sitter's name is queried in the title, so the identification as Diego de Guevara, a Spanish courtier in the Netherlands, is a hypothesis. A hand gathered at the chest can signal sincerity or loyalty, and the fur-lined cloak, brocade and ring mark rank. Court portraits like this recorded a servant of the prince and could be exchanged between patrons. The black ground and sharp realism are Netherlandish, while the sitter's possible Spanish connection echoes Sittow's years at the Spanish court.","c":"Sittow gives the face an unforgiving candour, with its stubble, hollowed eyes and loose jowls set against a glamorous costume. The soft light and black ground concentrate attention, and the hand gathered like a rosebud is delicately handled. The identification is uncertain, but the man himself is vivid, and the picture is among the most direct small portraits of its date: shrewd, unflattering and psychologically alive."},"met:13171":{"d":"A rajah, an Indian prince, sets out on a hunt in this upright canvas of about 100 by 81 centimetres, with horses, hunting leopards, birds and a palace among the elements the museum records. The record gives no further description, so the number of figures, the colour of the costumes and the architecture cannot be reported with confidence. The subject, a princely procession starting from a palace, was a favourite with Western painters of India, who delighted in the display of horses, jewels and retainers.","i":"Edwin Lord Weeks, a Bostonian who trained in Paris and lived there, was one of the best-known American Orientalist painters, travelling to North Africa, Persia and India to paint bright, detailed scenes of markets, mosques and palaces for Western collectors. Hunting leopards, or cheetahs, were kept by Indian princes for the chase, and Weeks's interest in such display reflects the Western taste for the picturesque. The picture, from about 1895, was bequeathed to the Met in 1914 from the collection of Morris K. Jesup.","c":"A skilful, colourful piece of Orientalist spectacle: Weeks's close study of costume, architecture and animals gives a convincing sense of ceremony, and he handles the glow of an Indian courtyard with practised facility. But such pictures see the East as a stage for a Western audience, and the picturesque often takes the place of understanding. It is more admirable as craftsmanship than as insight, a typical example of its kind."},"met:435689":{"d":"A woman is the sole subject of this portrait of 1642, an oil on canvas of about 87 by 71 centimeters, painted by Ferdinand Bol. The half-length format suggests a respectable burgher's wife, painted for her family, and the sitter's dress and bearing are the picture's main means of telling her standing. Bol had just left the studio of Rembrandt, and this portrait comes from the beginning of his independent career in Amsterdam.","i":"Dutch portraits of married women were commonly painted as pendants, with a matching portrait of the husband, so this picture was probably once half of a pair. They announced a family's standing in the busy commercial society of Amsterdam, where portraits hung in the best room of the house. The sitter's dress and bearing, rather than props, tell the viewer her class.","c":"Bol absorbed Rembrandt's warm tone and soft modeling but lacks the master's depth of feeling, and the result is a polished, flattering portrait in the Rembrandt manner. The face is carefully modeled, and the soft light gives the sitter dignity. It is a competent, attractive picture from a gifted pupil, a little reserved and impersonal beside his teacher's work."},"met:435690":{"d":"Petronella Elias, who lived from 1648 to 1667, appears as a young girl with a basket of fruit in this portrait of 1657, an oil on canvas of about 80 by 66 centimeters by Ferdinand Bol. She was about nine years old. The fruit makes her portrait more playful than the usual formal child portrait, and the format is the half-length typical of Amsterdam portraiture of the period.","i":"Fruit in a child's portrait can suggest abundance, health and the sweetness of youth, and it may also allude to the brevity of ripeness, since fruit spoils quickly. Petronella died at nineteen, which gives the picture a poignant ring that her family could not have foreseen. Portraits of wealthy children, often in pastoral or playful guise, were prized as records of a short, precious stage.","c":"By 1657 Bol was one of Amsterdam's most successful portraitists, with a smooth, elegant style that kept Rembrandt's warmth but removed his brooding depth. The child's portrait is charming, and the fruit adds a lively note. It is more graceful than searching, a representative example of the refined likenesses with which he served the city's elite."},"nga:231676":{"d":"A three-quarter-length portrait of a standing woman, on a canvas about 109 by 91 centimetres, painted in the 1660s. The sitter is not named. Ferdinand Bol, once Rembrandt's pupil, was by this time one of Amsterdam's fashionable portraitists, and women in his portraits typically appear in rich dark dress with white collars and lace, painted in smooth, clear light. A portrait of this size, showing the figure to the knees, gave the sitter a commanding presence on the wall of a wealthy home.","i":"In 1660s Amsterdam, the wives of merchants and regents had portraits made, often as companions to their husbands' portraits, showing respectability and wealth through dress and jewels. Bol, who trained under Rembrandt in the 1630s, gradually moved from his master's dramatic chiaroscuro to a lighter, more elegant manner that suited the changing taste of the city's elite. The woman here is unidentified, so she is read as a type: a well-to-do Amsterdam wife.","c":"Bol's later portraits are polished and dignified, with clearer light and a more elegant composition than Rembrandt's; they lack their master's psychological depth, but have a real grace. A standing three-quarter portrait gives him the chance to display costume and presence. The weakness is a certain smooth blandness: the sitter becomes a type rather than a personality. Still, it is an assured example of Amsterdam's fashionable portraiture in the 1660s."},"met:440900":{"d":"Saint Dominic is shown in penitence in this oil on canvas of about 132 by 109 centimeters, painted around 1607 by the Florentine artist Filippo Tarchiani. The picture includes an altar and the saint himself. The format and the closeness of the view suggest a devotional picture for a convent or a chapel, where the saint's austerity could serve as an example to the community.","i":"Dominic (about 1170 to 1221) founded the Order of Preachers, the Dominicans. His legend says that he prayed through the night and scourged himself with an iron chain for the sins of the world, and he is often shown kneeling before an altar or crucifix in penance. In Counter-Reformation Florence, around 1600, such images renewed the ideal of mortification and prayer that the Church wished to encourage.","c":"Tarchiani belonged to the reformed Florentine painting of the early 1600s, which valued clear compositions, restrained color and sincere feeling, and his saint probably shares that sober approach. The strengths of such a painting are the legibility and the quiet piety, while the weakness is a certain lack of imagination compared with the Baroque painters then emerging. It is a representative piece of Florentine devotional painting around 1600."},"met:437292":{"d":"A ship breaks up in a storm in a horizontal pastel about 63 by 91 cm, dated 1782. The Met's keywords record a ship, men and women, and the themes list shipwrecks, waves and storms, so the picture shows human figures caught in the violence of the sea. The medium is unusual: pastel, or powdered pigment, on a canvas prepared with gesso rather than on paper, which allows rich, velvety effects but is fragile.","i":"Shipwreck pictures served the late eighteenth-century taste for the sublime, in which terror is enjoyed from the safety of a drawing room. Painters such as Vernet and Loutherbourg specialised in storms, and Pillement, known mainly for light, fanciful landscapes and chinoiserie designs, shows his range here. A wreck also carried moral readings: human frailty against nature, fortune against providence, the risks of the trade that brought wealth to merchants who collected such pictures.","c":"The pastel's soft, dry texture can give wind and spray a flickering lightness, and the unusual medium makes the picture lively. A storm by Pillement is likely to be more theatrical than a study of the sea: the drama of the wreck is arranged for effect, less rigorous than Vernet's observation. It remains an engaging example of an artist best known for decoration, turning to a bigger, more serious subject and working with brio."},"met:437093":{"d":"Mercury, the messenger god, and the herdsman Battus meet in a landscape of trees and mountains, in an oil on canvas of about 119 by 178 cm, a broad format for a decorative landscape. The picture illustrates a tale from Ovid in which the god bribes a witness to silence. Francisque Millet, born in Antwerp and active in Paris, painted classical landscapes in the manner of Poussin and Gaspard Dughet, and died at thirty-seven.","i":"In Ovid's Metamorphoses, Mercury steals Apollo's cattle and, seen by the old Battus, buys his silence with the gift of a cow. Returning in disguise, he offers a richer reward for information, and Battus betrays the secret; Mercury turns him into a touchstone, the black stone used to test gold. In the French classical landscape tradition, such stories gave a pastoral scene a moral edge: the price of a bribed and then faithless witness.","c":"Millet died young, and his landscapes are valued as an elegant link between Poussin's austerity and a more atmospheric, Flemish sense of space. The story is a pretext: the mountains, trees and sky do most of the work, and the small figures hold only a little of the interest. It is orderly, well-bred and somewhat derivative, a minor but accomplished entry in French classical landscape."},"nga:66250":{"d":"A young man shown from the waist up behind a tabletop takes up the left half of a vertical canvas, and a geranium in a terracotta pot takes up the right. He has dark brown hair, a brown coat and a white neckcloth, wears small oval glasses, and looks down and to our left. In his right hand he holds a second pair of silver-rimmed glasses, and his left hand rests on the pot's edge. The tall plant nearly reaches the top, with two clusters of small red flowers. It is about 71 by 61 cm, signed and dated 1801.","i":"The sitter is Rubens Peale, about seventeen, painted by his older brother Rembrandt. Charles Willson Peale named his children after great artists and raised them in a household devoted to art and natural history, and this portrait defines Rubens by his interests: the plant he tended and the glasses, two pairs of them, that helped him observe it. The picture is probably a tribute to his horticultural success, an informal, affectionate image of the Enlightenment in a family.","c":"This is among the most original American portraits of its date: the half-and-half composition balances a human and a plant, the touches are tender and the likeness candid. Rembrandt Peale paints the geranium with as much attention as the face, and the hand on the pot makes youth and plant seem companions. Some handling may be stiff, but the idea is striking, and it is among the best early works of a painter who often played safe."},"nga:34133":{"d":"A man's head and shoulders are shown facing slightly left as he looks at the viewer, in a canvas about 52 by 37 cm, painted about 1815. He has fair skin, dark eyes and slightly arched eyebrows, and dark brown hair with a few gray strands in soft waves. He wears a dark jacket, a white high-collared shirt and a light cravat against a uniform warm background. The portrait is simple and compact, with the face as its only subject.","i":"In the 1810s Peale lived in Baltimore, where he opened a museum in 1814, and his portraits of merchants and professionals gave the young republic's citizens a plain dignity. He had learned his craft from his father, Charles Willson Peale, and later studied in London with Benjamin West and in Paris, and the clear, lit head against a plain ground reflects that training. The sitter's occupation is not recorded here, so the portrait speaks only of a respectable man of the early republic.","c":"Peale's gift is the clear, convincing head, and this small portrait puts its trust in the face: the light, the slight turn and the greying hair are enough. The likeness is spare and candid. The format and plain ground are conventional, and the sitter's personality is only lightly drawn, but it is a reliable, sympathetic example of a painter who made honesty of observation his main virtue."},"met:11742":{"d":"A portrait of the Marquis de Lafayette, bust-length on a canvas about 88 by 70 cm, painted in 1825. The Met's keywords record the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a likeness of the elderly French hero of the American Revolution, made at the time of his famous farewell tour of the United States, in a clear, direct style that gives the face its full weight and keeps the setting simple.","i":"Lafayette arrived in New York in August 1824 as the nation's guest and visited all twenty-four states before returning to France in September 1825. Americans crowded to see him, and portraitists vied for sittings, among them Samuel Morse, who painted him in full length. A bust-length likeness like Peale's gave admirers a memento of the man who had served at Washington's side and fought at Brandywine and Yorktown, and who embodied the alliance of France and America.","c":"Peale's honest approach suits an old soldier: the features appear without flattery, and the likeness is direct. The portrait is workmanlike, and it lacks the grand rhetoric of Morse's full-length, but Peale's gifts are clarity and the careful modelling of the head, and those are what a sitter of this kind needs. It offers affection more than drama, a patriotic image made to be treasured."},"nga:178141":{"d":"On a huge canvas about 349 by 306 cm, Washington is shown on the eve of the siege of Yorktown, the 1781 campaign that ended the main fighting of the Revolution. The museum gives no description, so the picture is characterised by title and type: an equestrian state portrait in the grand European tradition, in which a horse and rider confer authority on a general. Painted in 1824 and reworked in 1825, it is a monumental picture.","i":"Yorktown was where Cornwallis surrendered in October 1781, and an image of Washington before the battle presents him as the architect of that victory. The painting was made in the years of Lafayette's triumphal return to America, when the nation was reviving its memory of the Revolution and its heroes. Peale had drawn Washington from life as a teenager in 1795 and made him his lifelong subject, so this monumental canvas is a summing-up as well as a patriotic monument.","c":"The ambition is obvious: a big canvas, a heroic horse, an attempt to rival Trumbull's national history paintings. Peale's real talent was the head, however, not the epic composition, and the huge format may be more imposing in scale than in invention. The idea recalls European equestrian portraits, and the effect is sober and a little stiff. As an American attempt at the heroic state portrait it is important, if uneven."},"met:16687":{"d":"A boy and a girl, Michael Angelo and Emma Clara Peale, are portrayed together in a canvas about 76 by 64 cm, dated about 1826. The Met's keywords record the two children, so the picture is described by type: a double portrait of young relatives of the painter, intimate rather than formal, in which the children's faces and their closeness carry the picture. Such a canvas was a family record as much as a work for display.","i":"The names are a family signature. Charles Willson Peale named his sons and daughters after great artists and scientists, and the habit passed to the next generation, so a boy called Michael Angelo was born into the profession. Children's portraits of the 1820s reflect the age's sentimental ideas of childhood, and a pair of siblings promised pleasure to parents. Because the painter was an uncle or father, the picture is a family memory as well as a likeness.","c":"Family sitters free a painter from the client's wishes, and Peale's portraits of children are often among his freshest, as the affectionate looking here is likely to show. The composition may be conventional, and without a description of the pose one cannot say more, but a picture of two children by a close relative gains from its warmth. It is a sympathetic, unpretentious record of a household in which art was the family business."},"met:11741":{"d":"A man named John Johnston is portrayed in a bust-length canvas about 77 by 66 cm, painted between 1826 and 1829. The Met's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a commissioned likeness of Peale's later career, in which the face is clearly lit and carefully drawn against a plain ground, the sitter shown in dark, sober dress.","i":"In the late 1820s Peale was a busy portraitist, painting and promoting images of Washington, and in 1829 he left for a long stay in Europe. The data does not say who this John Johnston was, so his occupation and rank cannot be read from the picture. Portraits like this were meant for family walls and for the sitter's office or study, a plain statement that a man of standing wished to be remembered.","c":"Peale's strength is a clear, honest head, and a portrait of this kind depends on it. The drawing is firm and the likeness direct, but the manner is by now a formula, and the sitter's personality is only lightly drawn. Taken as a type, it is a reliable, sympathetic example of his mature portraiture, serious in intent and a little cool in effect."},"met:11739":{"d":"George Washington appears in a vertical canvas about 92 by 74 cm, painted about 1846. The Met's keywords record the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a bust-length likeness by the artist who made Washington his lifelong subject. Peale's best-known image frames the head in a painted stone oval, and he repeated it many times, so this is probably one of the numerous versions he painted in later years for a market that venerated the first president.","i":"Peale drew Washington from life as a teenager in 1795 and later devoted decades to refining the image, painting in 1823 a famous composite likeness that the Senate bought in 1832. By the 1840s Washington was a national saint, and versions of Peale's portrait sold as patriotic icons for homes, offices and public buildings. They show the first president as a calm, marble-like father of his country, not a man of the battlefield or the ballroom.","c":"A late replica repeats a type with great consistency: the head has dignity and an almost sculptural serenity, closer to an icon than a living man. That is both its strength and its weakness, since the repeated formula tends to lose the freshness of the first version. The image is nevertheless one of the most enduring American portraits of Washington, and this example is well ordered, calm and serious, if somewhat remote."},"met:11740":{"d":"George Washington appears in a vertical canvas about 91 by 74 cm, painted about 1846. The Met's keywords record the man and the portrait only. The size, 36 by 29 inches, is that of Peale's best-known Washington, the head set in a painted stone oval, so this is probably another of the many versions of that image he turned out in later life. It is a bust-length likeness meant for a parlour, a meeting hall or a public building.","i":"Peale claimed that his composite portrait, built from his own sittings of 1795 and from other likenesses, was the truest image of Washington, and he spent decades promoting it. Replicas went to institutions and private patriots as the cult of Washington grew in the mid-nineteenth century. The framing oval makes the head a kind of monument, as though Washington looked out through a window of stone, and it stands for a nation's wish for a fixed, noble image of its founder.","c":"The strength of these replicas is the consistency with which Peale kept the sculptural head, its serenity and the sense of a reality both close and distant. Their weakness is that repetition by an ageing hand tends to drain freshness: a version of the 1840s can feel more like a formula than an observation. As one of many, it is a sober, well-ordered example, valuable as evidence of how America wanted to remember Washington."},"met:11743":{"d":"Martha Washington is portrayed in a vertical canvas about 91 by 74 cm, painted about 1850. The Met's keywords record the woman and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: a bust-length likeness of the first First Lady, the same size as the museum's versions of Peale's Washington and presumably meant as a companion to one of them. She died in 1802, so the portrait, made about half a century later, must rest on earlier likenesses and memory.","i":"Martha Washington (1731-1802) was the widow of Daniel Parke Custis when she married George in 1759, and she became a symbol of republican domesticity. Paired images of George and Martha were popular in mid-century homes, and the 1850s saw the campaign to rescue Mount Vernon, led by the Mount Vernon Ladies' Association, founded in 1853. A portrait of her from this period belongs to the reverence for the founders' household, not to a living sitter.","c":"Peale could not paint Martha from life, so the likeness is a reconstruction, and the result is respectable and sober: a dignified matron, calm and well ordered. Its value lies less in the character of the face than in its role as a pendant to Washington and as evidence of the mid-century cult of the founders. As a painting it is competent and plain, and it asks to be read as a patriotic icon."},"nga:1094":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, George Washington faces us with his head turned to the left, a broad forehead, strong nose, thin lips and rosy cheeks, his white hair pulled back and curling around his ears. He wears a dark blue coat with gold epaulettes and buttons, a prominent yellow collar and a ruffled white cravat. A gradient of white and gray lies behind him, and a cracked, frame-like surface in gray and light green surrounds the oval, as if he looked through a porthole. The canvas is about 92 by 73 cm.","i":"The oval window is the device Peale made famous in his Washington of 1823, painted to give the likeness the solidity of stone and to set it apart from a living man. The uniform recalls Washington's service as commander, not his presidency, and the type was repeated for decades. By about 1850 the picture was an icon of national memory, produced for a public that wanted a stable, reassuring image of the founder at a moment of growing sectional anxiety.","c":"The device works: the cracked stone frame lends age and authority, the oval concentrates attention, and the gradient behind the head gives it a soft, even light. The face is calm to the point of blankness, and the uniform's bright details are painted with more care than the personality behind them. It is an effective icon, impressive for its coherence and a little remote, which suits its commemorative purpose."},"nga:34064":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, slightly turned to the left, with a long nose, deep-set eyes and thin lips in a neutral expression. His pale gray hair is styled back from his face. He wears a military uniform with a dark blue coat, yellow lapels, gold buttons and gold epaulettes, with a white cravat at his neck. The background is a gradient of brown and gray, darker near the edges and lighter around him. The canvas is about 77 by 64 cm, dated 1859.","i":"The sitter is Washington, painted by an artist who had drawn him from life in 1795 and returned to him for the rest of his career. Peale was eighty-one in 1859 and died the following year, so this is among his last works. On the eve of the Civil War, Washington was invoked as a symbol of the Union, and a calm, uniformed image of him carried that message. The costume recalls the Continental Army, not the presidency.","c":"A late work by an old painter: the head is simplified, the gaze neutral and the uniform painted with a steady hand, but the image has more calm than vigour. The gradient ground gives the figure a soft, even presence, and the three-quarter size keeps it intimate. It is a gentle, valedictory picture and not a lively one, and it is hard to place beside the freshness of the 1795 life study."},"nga:43319":{"d":"An older man is shown from the chest up in a canvas board about 61 by 51 cm. He has a fair complexion, prominent cheekbones, a slender nose and expressive eyes, with light brown and gray hair that is wavy, voluminous and slightly receding. He wears a dark brown coat over a white shirt with a high collar and a black cravat, and no accessories are visible. The background is a muted brown. The painting is signed R. Peale and dated 1859.","i":"The sitter is Thomas Sully (1783-1872), the leading American portraitist of his generation, who painted presidents, actors and, in 1838, Queen Victoria. An eighty-one-year-old painter portraying a seventy-six-year-old friend and colleague is a rare, personal kind of portrait. The informal support, a canvas board, and the plain ground suggest a sitting among peers, perhaps a gift or an exchange, in the year before Peale's death.","c":"Painted with the economy of friendship, the picture has no props and a plain ground, and the face carries everything. The eyes are alive and the likeness humane, a fair record of a gifted, genial elder. It lacks the sheen of Sully's own romantic portraits, but the plainness has an honesty that suits two men near the end of long careers. It shows Peale's late style at its most direct and appealing."},"met:436050":{"d":"Four episodes from the legend of Saint Lucy sit side by side on a small gold-ground panel about 25 by 38 cm, named in the title: Lucy and her mother at the shrine of Saint Agatha, Lucy giving alms, Lucy before the judge Paschasius, and Lucy standing immovable as efforts are made to drag her away. The Met's keywords record soldiers, men, women, oxen and angels. It is tempera on wood from the late fourteenth century, probably part of the predella beneath a larger altarpiece.","i":"Lucy was a young noblewoman of Syracuse who, after her mother was cured at Agatha's tomb, gave her wealth to the poor, refused a pagan suitor and was denounced to the governor Paschasius. When he ordered her dragged away, she became so heavy that neither guards nor oxen could move her, and she was later martyred. Her name suggests light, and she became patron of eyesight. Sequences like this taught lay viewers charity, constancy and faith.","c":"Cristiani is a minor master of Pistoia, and the panel shows a modest, efficient Gothic narrative style: gold ground, simple spaces and clear gestures. His figures are stock types, and his drawing is naive beside the great Florentines of the same decades. But the telling is charming and economical, with four episodes in a few centimetres each, and the picture's interest is in the legend and in the devotion it served."},"nga:93046":{"d":"About a dozen ruby-red and bright pink peonies in a gold and green vase sit on a rumpled cloth in this loosely painted, horizontal still life of about 60 by 73 cm, dated 1884. One more red peony lies on the table, and bunched fabric in orange, indigo and green suggests a geometric pattern. Two pictures hang one above the other behind, cut off by the edges: a tan house against a blue sky, and a ballerina bent forward as if tying a slipper.","i":"Gauguin painted this in 1884, in the years when he was changing from a stockbroker and weekend painter into a full-time artist, and he showed with the Impressionists. The dedication to his friend Théodore Gad suggests a gift, and Gad bears the surname of Gauguin's Danish wife, Mette, so he may have been a relative. The two pictures on the wall recall the work of Pissarro and Degas, painters Gauguin admired.","c":"A confident, painterly still life that shows Gauguin as a follower of Cézanne and Pissarro before he found his own manner: loaded strokes, pure color and a lack of fussy detail. The rumpled cloth suggests a Cézannian geometry, and the doubling of pictures within the picture is witty. The space is uncertain and the flat decorative color of his later style is absent, but it is a vigorous, intelligent picture that reveals his taste and skill."},"nga:46502":{"d":"A woman is shown from the chest up, her head turned slightly to the right, with red lips and brown hair in an updo. She wears a gray-blue dress with black buttons and holds a white object, perhaps a handkerchief. The background is red, with a white panel at left behind her head bearing a green arch with a floral pattern, and a decorative object at right that looks like a wooden stump on a box or shelf. The oil on linen is about 46 by 38 cm and dates from 1887 to 1888.","i":"The sitter is named by her husband's name, as was usual, and the portrait comes at a pivot in Gauguin's career. He had returned in 1887 from Panama and Martinique with little money, and was moving from Impressionism toward the flat, decorative design that would become Synthetism. The red ground, the arched panel and the odd object owe more to his taste for Japanese prints and decorative art than to a society portraitist's setting.","c":"A conventional-looking sitter is set against an unconventional ground: the red wall and the green arch flatten the space and make the setting a pattern. The face is calm and rather plain, and the portrait may disappoint those expecting the wild Gauguin of Tahiti. It is interesting as an early step toward flat color and decorative backgrounds, and a reminder that he took portraits for money while searching for his own style."},"nga:46623":{"d":"A waterway flanked by low banks curves away in front of a band of rolling hills in this horizontal landscape of about 71 by 90 cm, signed and dated 1888. The water is made with wide strokes of white and ice blue, the riverbank at lower right with strokes of brown, soft green and pink, and the tree trunks along the far bank have ghostly canopies that blend into hills painted with vertical strokes of blue, amber, pale green and rose pink, under a vivid blue sky with a few clouds.","i":"The river is the Aven, which flows through Pont-Aven in Brittany, a village that drew painters seeking cheap lodging and a rural, old-fashioned region that seemed remote from Paris. Gauguin worked there in 1888 with Émile Bernard, and their experiments led to Synthetism: simplified forms, bold outlines and color chosen for its effect more than its likeness. Brittany became for him a laboratory where he could test a new, non-naturalistic way of painting.","c":"A transitional landscape: the Impressionist subject remains, but the vertical hatching in the hills is borrowed from Cézanne, and the colors are bright and not literal. The structure is simple and the touch fresh. Some patches feel uncertain, and it is not yet the flat-color Gauguin of the Vision after the Sermon, but the freshness is an attraction, and the picture shows him searching, which is more interesting than a settled formula."},"nga:61369":{"d":"Three girls hold hands in a loose ring in a grassy field, in this horizontal canvas of about 73 by 93 cm, signed and dated 1888. They wear white headdresses, long dresses with wide white collars and clogs, and two have ruby-red flowers pinned to brown aprons. Their features and clothing are outlined in cobalt blue and filled with parallel strokes. A speckled dog sniffs the grass, and beyond a low wall rise plum, ivory, orange and blue buildings, a narrow spire and a hill.","i":"Breton girls in their white caps and clogs were a favorite subject for the painters at Pont-Aven, who found in Brittany a folk culture that seemed untouched by the modern city. Gauguin's picture is a landmark of Synthetism or Cloisonnism, the style of flat color areas bounded by firm outlines that recalls stained glass, medieval enamel and Japanese prints. The circle dance, with its rhythm of arms and steps, suited a style based on pattern rather than depth.","c":"A decisive step toward Gauguin's mature style: the dancing ring is strictly patterned, the cobalt outlines hold flat colors together, and space is reduced to a grassy stage. The charm lies in the tension between a rustic subject and a modern design. The figures are almost mannequins, and Gauguin's view of the Bretons is that of an outside observer who prized them for their picturesque difference. But the picture is a milestone in the invention of modern painting."},"nga:46625":{"d":"A dark-haired man's head floats against a divided ground of flat orange-red above and yellow below, with half-closed eyes lowered under arched brows and a drooping russet moustache. A thin yellow halo hangs in the air above him, and from a branch at the upper right two apples, one green and one red and green, dangle beside his head. A tall stem of dark teal, looping in curves across the foreground, ends at a pale hand at the lower right, where it seems to turn into the head of a snake held between the fingertips. Sharp outlines enclose the flat areas of colour. The date 1889 and the initials P Go are painted in green at the lower left.","i":"Gauguin painted this on a wooden cupboard door in the dining room of Marie Henry's inn at Le Pouldu in Brittany, in the late autumn of 1889, while living there with the Dutch painter Meijer de Haan, whose portrait was painted on the opposite door. The apples and the snake recall the Garden of Eden, and the halo a saint, so he presents himself as both tempter and holy man. Interpreters differ on how seriously to take this: some see an ironic, even demonic self-image, others a claim to be an artist-seer. The flat colour and dark outlines reflect Japanese woodblock prints and the Cloisonnism of the Pont-Aven group.","c":"One of Gauguin's boldest self-portraits and one of his most knowing. The flat red and yellow fields, the heavy blue-green contour and the floating halo make a design as strong as anything in his later work, and the sly, hooded face is convincingly self-regarding. The symbolism, however, is blunt: apples, snake and halo explain themselves, and the joke at his own expense cannot hide the self-promotion. Next to Van Gogh's self-portraits of the same year, it is more theatrical and less exposed. As decoration for a cupboard door it is hard to fault."},"nga:66418":{"d":"The head and shoulders of a man with peach-colored skin sit before a window, looking at the viewer from the corners of his eyes, in a stylized vertical portrait of about 47 by 39 cm. One cobalt-blue eyebrow is cocked, hollows under the eyes are shaded in blue, yellow and green, and a ginger beard trims a jutting chin. He wears a violet-gray jacket over a shirt with a yellow-striped collar, and the wall behind is vivid lime green. Inscriptions dedicate it to his friends Carrière and Laval.","i":"In the late 1880s Gauguin made self-portraits that presented the artist as an outcast, a rebel and a prophet, and exchanged them with friends such as Van Gogh and Bernard. This one is dedicated to Eugène Carrière, a Parisian painter and a supportive friend, and to Charles Laval, who had traveled with him to Panama and Martinique. The wary sideways look and the lifted brow suggest defiance or irony, and the dedications show the web of friendship among the avant-garde.","c":"The face is simplified into big planes, and the greens and purples are chosen for mood, not likeness, so that color becomes a kind of character. The wary glance and the raised eyebrow give the portrait real personality, and the visible brushwork keeps it lively. The risk of the genre is self-mythologizing, an artist posing as an outsider, but the picture is a vivid declaration of identity, and a good example of the new, expressive use of color."},"nga:53129":{"d":"A landscape of tawny-brown haystacks and trees among a patchwork of moss, lime, mint and pine-green fields is painted in mostly flat areas of color. In the foreground a woman in a white cap and dress and a navy jacket stands among three black and white spotted cows in a line, with orange forms around the head of the one at lower right. The horizon rises two-thirds of the way up the composition, beneath a topaz-blue sky. The canvas, about 74 by 94 cm, is dated 1890.","i":"The picture was painted at Le Pouldu, in the commune of Clohars-Carnoët on the Breton coast, where Gauguin lived in 1889 and 1890 with other painters at an inn. Haystacks were a motif of the age: Monet began his Grainstacks series in 1890, but he was concerned with changing light, while Gauguin treated the haystack as a flat decorative shape in a patchwork of fields, a symbol of the farming year more than a study of weather.","c":"A patchwork of fields in flat color is wonderfully decorative, and the cows aligned like a frieze and the high horizon flatten space into pattern. The strange orange forms around the cow's head are intriguingly unexplained. The human figure is small and the details are subordinated to design, so that the strength lies in a bold abstraction and an unsettled strangeness. Gauguin here cares more about pattern than light, which sets him apart from the Impressionists he came from."},"nga:61370":{"d":"Rolling, bright green pastures and honey-brown farmland lead to a cluster of buildings beyond a copse of trees, painted in mostly flat areas of color. A gingerbread-brown path meanders from the lower left to a metal gate near the right edge, a man in navy blue walks along a hilltop with his hands in his pockets, and two black and white cows and a brown and white one graze nearby. A white two-story house with a blue roof stands in the distance, under a vivid blue sky tinted pink and lilac. The canvas is about 73 by 92 cm, dated 1890.","i":"Le Pouldu, a fishing village in the commune of Clohars-Carnoët, was Gauguin's Breton retreat in 1889 and 1890, and he worked there in the Synthetist mode with other painters. The path that leads the eye into the picture is a classic device, and the figure with his hands in his pockets may be a farmer or a stroller. In 1890 Gauguin was already planning to leave Europe for the tropics, and these Breton landscapes are among the last he painted in France before Tahiti.","c":"The picture has quiet harmonies of bright green and orange, and the path leads the eye with a calm rhythm. The forms are flattened, and the man with his hands in his pockets adds a touch of humor. Some patches of color seem arbitrary, and the scene is less strange than its neighbor, Haystacks in Brittany, but it has a rhythmical vigor. The pair show Gauguin using Brittany as a laboratory for a new kind of landscape."},"met:438821":{"d":"At the right, a Tahitian woman in a red pareu patterned with white flowers stands barefoot on green grass, carrying a naked child astride her shoulders; both have thin golden halos. Behind them on a pale path, two women, bare to the waist, stand with their hands pressed together in prayer, one in a blue-and-yellow flowered wrap, the other in white. At the far left a pale angel with yellow wings, in a lavender gown, points them toward the mother and child. Palms, flowering trees, and a dark mountain fill the background. Across the bottom, bananas and other fruit are laid out like an offering, with the title in yellow letters at the lower left. The colors are flat and strong, the outlines dark.","i":"Gauguin reached Tahiti in 1891 and painted this soon after, his first major religious picture there and the only explicitly Christian painting of his first stay. The title is Tahitian for 'Hail Mary.' He described it in a letter as an angel with yellow wings pointing out Mary and Jesus, both Tahitians, to two Tahitian women. The women's raised, joined hands are taken from a relief at the Buddhist temple of Borobudur in Java, which he knew from photographs. Shown in Paris in 1893, the picture sold for 2,000 francs, the highest price Gauguin had yet received. It came to the Met in 1951 as a bequest of Sam A. Lewisohn.","c":"A bold and important picture that is also a little awkward. Gauguin's flat color and strong outline are put to powerful use, especially the red pareu against the green grass, and the fusion of Madonna and Tahitian mother is inventive. But the figures are stiff, the angel is half lost in the foliage, and the picture asks the viewer to accept a European fantasy of a spiritual paradise that overlooks the colonial reality of French Tahiti. Its interest is therefore as historical as visual: the beginning of a vision he would develop more freely in later canvases."},"met:436451":{"d":"A man, a woman and a horse appear in a tropical landscape on a vertical canvas about 65 by 47 cm, dated 1892. The Met's keywords record the horse, woman, man and landscape. It was painted during Gauguin's first stay in Tahiti, and it is described by type and manner: broad areas of saturated, flat colour, simplified forms and decorative curves in place of the Impressionist concern with changing light and the careful perspective of academic landscape.","i":"Gauguin left France for Tahiti in 1891, hoping for an unspoiled life. Tahiti was by then a French colony, changed by missionaries and colonial rule, and he filled the gap between his hopes and the reality with imagination, so his Polynesia is part observation and part invention. Modern viewers also weigh the colonial attitudes and the painter's own conduct behind such images. The landscape shows how he turned an island into a world of colour and pattern.","c":"Gauguin's Tahitian landscapes succeed through the boldness of colour and the rhythm of contour: the forms are held by line and flat colour rather than by atmosphere, and a few figures give a human note. The risk is picture-postcard exoticism, and the invented paradise sometimes feels more a European dream than a place. At its best it is a vivid, decorative construction, and this is a representative, attractive example."},"nga:46624":{"d":"Two nude Tahitian women are seen from behind at the edge of a teal sea. At the left, one has her arms raised, about to plunge into the water; at the right the other unwraps a flowered cloth from her waist. Farther off, a bare-chested man in a red loincloth stands hip-deep with a spear. A gnarled charcoal-gray tree trunk arches across the canvas like a barrier, and in front of it the ground is a swirl of rose-pink and violet with orange flowers and yellow tendrils. White foam breaks at the right. Color is laid on in flat areas outlined by dark contours. The title is written in dark red at the lower left, and the signature, 'P. Gauguin 92,' at the lower right.","i":"Gauguin painted this in 1892, soon after setting up a studio in a bamboo hut at Mataiea, in his first months in Tahiti. The title is Tahitian for 'Near the Sea.' It is one of the few plain genre scenes he made there, and its idyll of uninhibited bathing owes more to European fantasy, notably Pierre Loti's novels, than to what he saw. The colors are invented as well: the pink and violet sand stands in for a beach that was really dark volcanic brown. The flat color and dark contours come from the style he had developed in Brittany, known as Cloisonnism. It came to the National Gallery with Chester Dale's collection.","c":"A powerful design carrying an uneasy message. The dark trunk slashing across the canvas, the arbitrary pinks and purples, and the flat teal sea turn the scene into a decorative pattern that was bold in 1892 and still looks fresh. The weaknesses are the stiffness of the figures, who are more shapes than bodies, and the tourist's fantasy of the subject: nude islanders bathing for the pleasure of an unseen viewer. As design it is one of the most assured pictures of his first Tahitian period; as a description of Tahiti it is unreliable."},"nga:53130":{"d":"A nude woman with long black hair stands at right against a landscape that swirls with jewel-toned greens, pinks and purples, one hand touching her chin and the other holding a white cloth over her groin. Behind her a man in a topaz-blue robe kneels under a craggy tree with his hands on his knees, his face rimmed in black and his eyes wide. A red and green serpent floats near the upper right corner. The canvas is about 92 by 69 cm, signed, dated 1892 and inscribed with its Tahitian title.","i":"The painting, from Gauguin's first Tahitian stay, reads as a Tahitian Eve: a nude woman, a serpent and a tempting figure behind her, with the title, Words of the Devil, naming the voice of temptation. Gauguin mixed Christian imagery with Polynesian lore, partly invented, and so the question of who speaks, the figure or the serpent, is left open. His Tahitian paintings are now read alongside his relationships with young Tahitian women, some in their early teens, and the colonial power that gave him access.","c":"Saturated color and a swirling ground dissolve the landscape into decoration, and the figure's pose recalls the Western Venus pudica, so that the picture is a hybrid of European and invented Polynesian elements. The strengths are striking color and ambiguity; the weakness is a constructed exoticism that says more about European fantasy than about Tahiti. A viewer can admire the design and still question its colonial gaze, and the aesthetic achievement and the ethical discomfort are inseparable."},"met:436448":{"d":"Farm buildings and trees stand in the countryside in a horizontal canvas about 72 by 91 cm. The Met's keywords record Pont-Aven, a tree and a farm, so the setting is the Breton village where Gauguin first worked in the 1880s. Painted about 1894, it dates from a brief return to Brittany between his two Tahitian stays, and it is characterised by his manner: simplified shapes, firm contours and colour laid in broad areas.","i":"Pont-Aven was where Gauguin had gathered a circle of younger painters in 1888 and developed the flattened, symbolic style later called Synthetism. By 1894 he was back from Tahiti and about to leave again, and a Breton farm is a farewell to a place of old memories. Breton farms and peasants stood for a traditional rural life that Parisian artists sought out, and Gauguin treated it as a source of simple, durable forms.","c":"A calm, relatively conventional landscape by Gauguin's standards: the colour is rich and the shapes are simple, and it avoids the daring of his Tahitian pictures and the strangeness of the Vision after the Sermon. The composition holds together by the weight of its masses and the rhythm of trees and roofs. It is attractive and sound rather than adventurous, and reads as the work of an artist already looking elsewhere."},"met:436449":{"d":"Four Tahitian women rest on a raised wooden veranda in the heat of the day. In the foreground one sits sideways, seen from behind, with long black hair, a straw hat banded in blue, a loose blue-gray blouse with violet flowers and a dark skirt patterned with white birds; her hand and bare foot rest on the pink planks. At left a woman in a pink top, a flower in her hair, looks out into the yellow-green garden. At right a woman in orange lies on her front, and behind them another, in a plum-colored dress, irons on a board. A basket stands at lower right. The colors are flat and the outlines clear.","i":"The women wear the long, loose missionary dresses introduced by Europeans, so Gauguin is not showing an untouched paradise but a mixed Tahitian world. He made the painting during or just after his first stay in Tahiti, from 1891 to 1893, and the Metropolitan Museum says it is not documented whether he finished it there or after his return to France. Examination shows he changed the picture as he worked: the foreground woman first wore a red skirt, and a dog occupied the place of the basket. The painting has no religious or mythical meaning; it records the ease of daily life that impressed him, though through a visitor's eyes.","c":"Gauguin builds a convincing sense of heat and idleness with remarkably little: pink floorboards running in diagonals, a faceless figure in the foreground like a stage curtain, and a handful of flat colors, lavender, orange, rose and acid green. The weaknesses are the same as the strengths. The women are anonymous, and the view is that of a visitor looking in. Compared with the myth-making canvases of the same years, this is a quieter, more domestic Gauguin, and the one with the least pretense."},"met:437999":{"d":"A teapot and fruit stand on a table in a still life about 48 by 66 cm, dated 1896. The Met's keywords record the fruit and the teapot and nothing more, so the picture is described by type and manner: a tabletop group painted in Tahiti, with the flat colour, strong contours and decorative patterns of Gauguin's late style. A European teapot among island fruit makes a small collision of worlds on the table.","i":"Still life let Gauguin experiment freely, and in 1896 he was ill, short of money and isolated, with fruit and household objects close to hand. He owned a still life by Cézanne that he treasured, and Cézanne's example lies behind the way a table becomes a field of shapes. The teapot, a European habit of tea in a tropical setting, can be read as a sign of how colonial life had already altered the Tahiti he painted as unspoiled.","c":"The picture's best quality is decorative richness: flat, saturated colour and patterned surfaces make the humble group into a harmony. But the mixture of influences, Cézanne's tilted table, Japanese prints and Polynesian decoration, does not always cohere, and the objects can seem arranged for effect. It is a modest pleasure rather than a major statement, and it shows his gift for turning ordinary things into patterns of colour."},"met:438000":{"d":"Three women, nude according to the Met's keywords, are shown with a tree in a small oil on a wood panel about 24 by 43 cm, dated 1896. The museum gives no further description, so the picture is described by type and manner: a compact, decorative panel in Gauguin's late Tahitian style, with simplified, flattened figures and strong, flat colour, informal in feeling and small enough to be handled and compared with other trials.","i":"Gauguin's Tahiti is both a place and an invention. He sought an unspoiled paradise but found a colony changed by missionaries and French rule, and he filled the gap with imagination, mixing Polynesian motifs with European art; a trio of women can recall the Three Graces of classical tradition. Modern viewers also weigh the colonial setting and Gauguin's own conduct toward the young Tahitian women he lived with and painted.","c":"The panel has high decorative intensity, and its strength lies in the rhythm of curved forms and the harmony of colour. The idealised nudes can look generic, and the sense of an imagined Eden may feel stagey beside the particulars of real life. It is a minor but typical Gauguin, valuable for showing how he turned observation into pattern, and less ambitious than the large canvases he began the next year."},"nga:39758":{"d":"Four young women with olive-green skin shaded with cool blue stand or sit in a vividly colored landscape, in this horizontal canvas of about 60 by 93 cm, dated 1897. A slender spruce-blue tree divides the picture, and the ground is carpeted in coral orange, pink and lavender. Two women at left face away and look back over their shoulders; at right one sits with flowers in her hair and another, nude, touches a tree. Water in flame red, yellow, rose and blue lies beyond, and two ghostly blue dogs rest at lower right.","i":"The bather in a landscape is an old theme in European art, from the Renaissance to Cézanne, and Gauguin reset it in Tahiti with invented color: flame-red water, olive-green flesh and blue dogs. He painted it in 1897, in poverty and ill health and grieving the death of his daughter Aline, the year he painted his huge Where Do We Come From? The scene presents an idyll that was contradicted by his own situation, and it is a Western dream of paradise.","c":"Color is released from description: coral grounds, flame water and olive flesh shaded blue make a new harmony, and the slender tree gives the composition a firm axis. The repose of the figures is persuasive, and the ghostly dogs add strangeness. The strength is the color design, and the weakness is the artificial paradise, the colonial fantasy that sits uneasily with the artist's knowledge of Tahiti. It is easier to admire the design than to believe the scene."},"nga:54130":{"d":"Four young women with medium brown skin sit or stand on a pumpkin-orange ground, divided into pairs by a broad, gold, undulating brushstroke. Three have long black hair and one flame-red hair and a silver necklace; they are mostly nude. A stream runs diagonally behind them into a small pool, and across it a woman in a coral hood holds a child's arm near a black dog and a slightly larger-than-life blue statue of a standing woman with raised arms. The canvas is about 74 by 95 cm, signed and dated 1898.","i":"Te Pape Nave Nave means Delectable Waters in Tahitian, and nave nave, delightful, appears in several of Gauguin's titles. The blue statue is an idol of his own invention, drawn from Polynesian lore and from books, and gives the scene a spiritual note. The picture presents a paradise of women and water, yet in 1898 Gauguin himself was poor, ill and suicidal, so the idealization contrasts sharply with the reality of his life, and the colonial setting colors the image.","c":"A decorative frieze in which the golden ribbon dividing the picture, the orange ground and the rhythmic poses make a superb pattern. The statue adds a spiritual note and the red hair a spark. The Eden is a fiction, and the women are types designed to illustrate an idea, so that a critic must admire the design and question its premises. As an invention of a visual language of color and rhythm, it is a compelling and influential work."},"met:436446":{"d":"Two women, bare to the waist, stand shoulder to shoulder in front of dark green foliage, filling most of the canvas. The one at the left holds a shallow dish of red-orange blossoms at her waist and looks directly at the viewer, wearing a deep blue wrap above a lilac one. At the right the other woman, in a pale blue pareu fastened over one shoulder, lowers her eyes toward the dish and holds a spray of pink flowers against her chest. Their skin is painted in warm yellow-ochre and olive with little shading, and their dark hair hangs loose. Patches of yellow in the leaves and the saturated blues give the picture a jewel-like surface, with soft outlines.","i":"Gauguin painted this in 1899 during his second stay in Tahiti, a hard period of illness and poverty. The same two women appear in his Faa Iheihe and Rupe Rupe of 1898–99, which suggests that he worked from the same models. The frontal, unembarrassed poses have been compared to Manet's Olympia, and the offering of flowers and fruit echoes a long European habit of comparing women's bodies to blossoms. The picture presents Tahiti as an unspoiled paradise, a vision Gauguin constructed rather than observed. It came to the Met in 1949 as a gift of William Church Osborn.","c":"Gauguin at his most decorative. The paired figures make a strong, stable shape, the blue, ochre, and green are a lovely chord, and the level stare of the left-hand woman is memorable. But the picture is also formulaic: the flat modeling, the stock poses, and the bare breasts presented with flowers and fruit sell a European fantasy of the island, and the women are less individuals than emblems. The decorative harmony is real; the claim to depth is less convincing than in the best of the Tahiti pictures."},"nga:41625":{"d":"A bearded man strides to our left between two brick-red columns that support an arch, looking in the same direction against a bright gold ground. He has short brown hair, a forked beard and a long, straight nose, and wears a marine-blue ankle-length cloak over a long rose-pink robe, its folds picked out in darker lines. He clutches a scroll at his hip and grasps his cloak with the other hand. A punched, flower-ringed halo surrounds his head. The panel is about 48 by 23 cm, with a Latin inscription on the arch.","i":"The inscription names Saint James, the apostle called the Less or the Minor, also known as the Just, the traditional first bishop of Jerusalem, whose scroll alludes to the Epistle of James attributed to him. According to tradition he was killed by being thrown from the Temple and beaten to death. Single saints under arches made up the holy company that watched over the church, and the matching size and date of the companion panel of John the Evangelist suggest they belonged to one ensemble.","c":"The Master of Saint Francis works in the Italo-Byzantine style, with a gold ground, linear drapery and a frontal formality. The striding pose is a lively invention for the period, as if the saint were walking out of the frame, and the blue and rose make a strong, simple scheme. The figure is still stiff, and the picture does not yet anticipate Giotto's weight, but it shows a painter struggling toward physical presence."},"nga:41626":{"d":"A man in a slate-blue robe over a burgundy-red tunic stands under an arched opening against a glimmering gold ground, his body facing us and his head turned to our right. His skin and his white hair and beard have a greenish cast, with the hair painted in long curving white lines. He holds a scroll in front of his chest, the other arm hidden in his robe, and wears sandals on a pine-green floor. A punched halo surrounds his head, and a web of cracks crosses the gold. The panel is about 48 by 23 cm.","i":"The inscription names Saint John the Evangelist, here an old man with white hair and beard, as in Byzantine tradition, where he is the aged theologian. Western art later preferred to show him young and beardless, so the venerable look places the panel in the older, Byzantine-influenced style. The scroll stands for his Gospel. As the match in size and date suggests, he belonged to a set of standing saints under arches, a heavenly company watching from the altar.","c":"The greenish flesh, probably left by a green underpaint showing through, gives the figure an unworldly pallor, and the long white curves of the beard are a delicate, rhythmic touch. The composition is frontal and still, and the cracked gold shows its age. The Master of Saint Francis is not yet a naturalist, but the grave, inward look of the old apostle has a dignity that the formula does not fully explain."},"nga:41685":{"d":"An elderly man kneels at the left, leaning back with his arms spread wide, his face tipped up toward Elijah, who sits in a chariot of flames drawn by two white horses that climb a bank of sand-colored clouds. The kneeling man, seen mostly from behind, wears a brown robe and a yellow mantle, and his bare foot digs into a rock that slopes to a blue-green sea. Elijah, bald and bearded in a tan robe and white mantle, turns toward light streaming from the upper left. Pale coral flames curl around a gold wheel.","i":"In the second Book of Kings the prophet Elijah is taken up to heaven in a whirlwind, with a chariot and horses of fire. His disciple Elisha, who sees it, receives Elijah's mantle and with it his prophetic power, so the kneeling man is Elisha. Christian writers saw the ascent of Elijah as a foreshadowing of Christ's Ascension, which made the subject suitable for churches as well as palaces.","c":"Angeli, a pupil of Piazzetta, handles light well: the beam from the upper left and the flame-colored glow of the chariot against the dark rock give the vision urgency, and the grip of the bare toes on the rock is a vivid observation. The figures keep some of Piazzetta's heaviness, and the picture is less airy than the Tiepolo visions of the same decades. Still, it is an energetic, well-staged Venetian rococo narrative."},"met:435915":{"d":"A man, Charles de Cossé (1506-1563), Count of Brissac, is portrayed in a small portrait on vellum, a thin sheet of prepared calfskin, dated about 1535. The Met gives no size, and its keywords record only the man and the portrait, so the picture is described by type: an intimate likeness of a French courtier painted on a miniature scale in the precise, crisp manner of the court of Francis I, made as a personal keepsake rather than for a wall.","i":"Brissac was a soldier and courtier who rose to be a marshal of France in 1550 and governor of Piedmont. A portrait of about 1535 shows him in his late twenties, in the early part of a career at court and in the Italian wars. Small portraits on vellum were exchanged among the nobility as tokens of friendship and service, and the format links the picture to the manuscript illumination tradition from which French portrait painting grew.","c":"Clouet, the painter of Francis I's court, gave French portraiture its crisp, aristocratic realism, and a vellum miniature suits his fine, controlled touch. The likeness is clear and the manner restrained, with more elegance than warmth. The scale limits what can be said of character, and the picture asks for close, slow looking, but as a courtly record of a rising soldier it is refined and convincing."},"met:435914":{"d":"A bust-length portrait on an oil panel about 40 by 34 cm, dated about 1536, shows Guillaume Budé (1467-1540) at about sixty-nine. The Met's keywords record the man, the portrait and books, so the sitter is presented as a scholar. Clouet's portraits of the court of Francis I are known for precise drawing of the face, restrained costume and little decoration, and the picture is described by that manner rather than by detail the data does not give.","i":"Budé was the leading French humanist, a scholar of Greek and Roman law and of ancient coins, an adviser to Francis I, and the driving force behind the royal readers' college that became the Collège de France. A book in a portrait signals learning, and for a scholar the picture is a statement of vocation, the face of the mind. Clouet, as the king's painter, recorded the king's circle, and Budé was one of its men of learning.","c":"A portrait of a thinker by a painter of courtiers, it depends on Clouet's gift for precision: the face carries the intellect, and the restraint suits the sitter. Some find Clouet's portraits cool and impassive beside Holbein's, which were made in England in the same years, but the reserve fits a man whose life was lived in libraries. It is a sober, authoritative likeness, impressive for its stillness more than for any display."},"nga:360":{"d":"The head and chest of a clean-shaven man are shown in profile facing right, in front of a dark blue curtain that opens on a sliver of landscape. He has a hazel eye, a straight nose, a closed peach-colored mouth and straight auburn-brown hair cut to angle from brow to neck. He wears a tall scarlet cap and a brown and gold tunic patterned with stylized leaves and vines. The view at right shows ruins of a gate and wall and a walled town beneath azure mountains. Tempera on poplar, about 54 by 38 cm.","i":"Giovanni II Bentivoglio (1443-1508) was the effective ruler of Bologna from 1463 until Pope Julius II drove him out in 1506, and a lavish patron of artists. The profile format, borrowed from ancient coins and medals, gave a ruler the dignity of Roman emperors, and the ruins in the landscape may hint at the same classical ambition. The portrait was probably made to hang with a companion portrait of his wife, Ginevra Sforza, whom he married in 1464.","c":"Roberti, a Ferrarese painter, brings a hard, incisive precision to the profile: the shading by the nose and mouth is delicate, the gold-patterned tunic is rendered with great richness, and the hazy landscape opens the space. The profile gives little psychological insight but great clarity, and the effect is cold and almost metallic. It is a courtly ideal more than an intimate likeness, impressive for its crispness and surprising in its sumptuous detail."},"nga:361":{"d":"A vertical portrait, about 54 by 39 cm, in tempera on a poplar panel, painted in the mid-1470s. A pale-skinned woman is shown from the waist up in profile facing left, looking into the distance. Her rust-brown gown has wide mustard-yellow panels studded with pearls, rubies and sapphires, pleated sleeves and a scarlet belt edged with pearls, and her blond hair is coiled into a horn over her ear under two sheer veils, with a double strand of pearls at her neck. A blue cloth nearly fills the background, but a window at left shows a town with coral-red walls and hazy mountains.","i":"The profile portrait, modeled on antique coins and medals, was the standard form for Italian court women in the fifteenth century, because it was dignified and gave a clear display of rank. The jewels, pearls and costly fabrics show the wealth and position of the Bentivoglio, the family that ruled Bologna, and the window with its walled town may allude to the city they governed. Such portraits were made to commemorate betrothals or marriages and were sometimes made in pairs.","c":"Ercole de' Roberti, a Ferrarese painter, is better known for dramatic religious scenes than for portraits, but this panel shows his meticulous attention to textiles and gems: the jeweled bodice, pleated sleeve and layered veils are described with an enamel clarity. The profile is severe and the face almost impersonal, so the interest lies in the costume. It is a beautiful object, though a limited portrait."},"nga:50292":{"d":"A woman holds the hands of two nude children in front of a crimson cloth in a vertical panel. She wears a forest-green dress with voluminous sleeves and a gauzy white headdress, and she crouches in a sandal, surrounded by broken chunks of masonry and bits of burning logs. Her mouth is wide open and her eyes cut back to the left as she clutches the children's hands. One child turns away and reaches up to touch her hand, and the other twists aside, eyes squinting and mouth open. Fold lines in the cloth make a grid behind them.","i":"In 146 BC, as Rome destroyed Carthage, the Carthaginian commander Hasdrubal surrendered to Scipio Aemilianus. According to the ancient historians, his wife, watching from the temple, denounced him as a coward, killed their children and leapt into the flames rather than live as a Roman captive. Renaissance humanists took such women as exemplars of courage and fidelity to country, and the panel was probably one of a set of exemplary women of antiquity. The scene is a moment of horror, not triumph.","c":"Ercole's sense of drama is strong: the crouched, twisted figure, the open mouth and the stifling red cloth, with its grid of fold lines, give the panel a hard, almost abstract structure. The children's frantic gestures are painted without sentiment, and the stage is small but the effect intense. The hard-edged Ferrarese manner suits the horror, and the result is an unsettling, original panel by one of the most expressive painters of the Emilian Renaissance."},"met:436318":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Mirope Savati Gaye, painted in 1865 in oil on canvas, about 137 by 100 cm. Lace, a cuff, a button, a ring and pearls are recorded, so the portrait lingers on costume and jewelry: the sitter's fine dress is part of the picture's subject. It is an uncommon portrait from Fortuny, a painter better known for small, highly finished pictures of costume and exotic subjects.","i":"Fortuny, born in Reus in Catalonia in 1838 and dead in Rome at thirty-six in 1874, became one of the most sought-after painters in Europe for his dazzling small pictures of Moroccan battles, Orientalist scenes and eighteenth-century costume pieces, sold through the dealer Goupil. A portrait is atypical for him, and the care given to lace, buttons and pearls shows his taste for accessories. The sitter's story is not further recorded here.","c":"In a portrait Fortuny's virtuoso brushwork has less scope than in his little scenes, but the lace, the ring and the pearls offer the same opportunity for shimmer and detail, and these are probably the best passages, with the face the quietest. It is a competent, glittering society portrait, charming but rare in his production, and it shows what he could do when asked for a likeness."},"nga:178127":{"d":"A white stone building with a brown and gray roof stands among greenery in this tiny panel of about 19 by 13 centimeters. Its textured walls show the outlines of small stones, and a dark doorway opens at the center of the wall facing the viewer. A standing person with short black hair, a blue shirt, cream trousers and a large tan garland has hands on hips, and another person sits against the wall in a bright yellow garment with pink and blue sleeves. The ground is dusty earth, and the figures' details are suggested with daubs of paint.","i":"Roma families, whom outsiders long called gypsies, lived in whitewashed houses and cave dwellings cut into the hillsides of Granada, especially in the Sacromonte district, a place famous for flamenco. Nineteenth-century painters and travelers found them picturesque and often romanticized them, so pictures like this are both records of a real community and products of an outsider's gaze. Mariano Fortuny lived in Granada from about 1870 to 1872.","c":"Fortuny's brilliance is the flicker of the paint: figures, walls and foliage are built from daubs that sparkle in the light. The tiny format concentrates the effect, and the little panel glows with local color. The picturesque view of the poor is its limit, but as a display of virtuosity in a few square inches it is hard to fault."},"nga:194951":{"d":"Eight men in eighteenth-century costume gather in a pink salon to look at a nude woman posing on a marble tabletop. They wear tight jackets and stockings in rose pink, gray, blue, red and black, with curled hair, and many hold gold-tipped canes. The woman stands with her back turned, one hip raised, looking down over her shoulder, a flower in her piled blond hair. The table's base is gilded to look like muscular men carrying the marble top. Beyond columns a room lined with paintings and statues opens, and the glossy floor reflects the figures.","i":"The picture belongs to the nineteenth-century fashion for costume pieces set in the eighteenth century, in which connoisseurs, critics and painters were favorite comic subjects. The men's gestures, leaning, tipping heads together, studying the model's feet, make a joke of art-world expertise, and the nude, who looks back at them, seems to appraise her appraisers. Fortuny, a Catalan painter, signed it in 1874, the year he died at thirty-six.","c":"A tour de force of contrasts: the men and furniture are built from short, flecked touches that glitter like jewels, while the nude is painted smoothly and softly, so that her body seems the one calm area in a busy, sparkling room. The wit is knowing and the finish dazzling. It is a connoisseur's picture, clever and a little cold, more admired for technique than for feeling."},"nga:61216":{"d":"A seascape with large sailboats on choppy blue-green water, in a canvas about 38 by 52 centimetres. The horizon lies in the bottom third, so a cloudy white and blue sky fills most of the picture. The boats are seen from a distance, their tall sails white, cream and tan, with small dots of colour suggesting people on the decks. In the foreground dark shapes emerge from the water, three vertical ones at the left like driftwood and a round one at the right like a floating barrel, with splashes of white wave around them.","i":"Richard Parkes Bonington, an Englishman who grew up in northern France, studied in Paris, where he became a friend of Delacroix, and made many small oil studies of the Channel coast in the early 1820s. French artists of the time were learning from the English landscapists Constable and Turner, and Bonington's fresh, open handling shows that exchange. The title is general: it records a coastal scene rather than a named place.","c":"A fresh, dashing sketch. The sky takes two-thirds of the picture, the choppy water is painted in quick strokes, and the flotsam in the foreground gives scale and a touch of mystery. It is the work of a painter barely twenty-two and shows his lightness of touch. The picture has no strong focal point and is sketch-like in finish, but that is its freshness, and it anticipates the seascapes of later French painters."},"met:435707":{"d":"A small vertical landscape, about 46 by 38 cm, dated 1826, of travelers at a roadside halt. Trees and horses are the recorded subjects, so horses and riders pause beneath trees, in the tradition of landscape with figures and animals that the Dutch painters of the seventeenth century made popular. It was painted by a young Englishman living in France who was then about twenty-four, with a light, fluent touch.","i":"Bonington, born near Nottingham, was brought as a boy to Calais and then Paris, where he trained and became a friend of Delacroix. His light, fluent handling of landscape and costume pictures, influenced by Constable, whose Hay Wain was shown in Paris in 1824, and by the Dutch and Venetian masters, made him one of the favorites of the new Romantic painters. He died in 1828, at twenty-five.","c":"Bonington's facility is astonishing: he made a small subject glow with light and flow. The roadside halt is a charming example, though the subject is slight and borrowed, and its virtue is in touch and atmosphere rather than invention. A brilliant young painter's lightness is the picture's chief pleasure, and it makes one regret how little time he had to develop."},"nga:119184":{"d":"A sun-drenched canal is lined with boats and cream-white buildings under a vivid blue sky, in a small horizontal canvas about 26 by 35 centimetres. A broad swath of blue water fills the lower third, and white balconied buildings with terracotta-red roofs face it on both sides. At the left a man in a red cap holds a rust-red and white sail stretched along his skiff, with other boats and red-capped sailors behind; at the right three swimmers rest on a half-submerged platform. A white tower rises in the hazy distance, and the far buildings and a bridge are loosely painted.","i":"Bonington visited Venice in 1826 and made oil sketches of its canals and quays. The subject had been made familiar by Canaletto and Guardi, but Bonington gives it a new look: instead of precise topography, he offers sunlight, colour and everyday life, with swimmers and sailors in place of picturesque ruins. At the time Venice was a faded city under Austrian rule, which Romantic travellers found all the more appealing. Bonington died two years later, aged twenty-five.","c":"A tiny, brilliant study of light: the blue canal, the white buildings and the red caps vibrate against one another, the paint is loose and decisive, and the swimmers add a touch of daily life. The picture is small but convincingly spacious. It has no grand composition, which is part of its charm, and it shows why Delacroix and others admired Bonington's gift for catching a scene in a few sure strokes."},"met:438449":{"d":"A small landscape on millboard, about 28 by 33 cm, dated about 1825, of the countryside near Rouen in Normandy. Trees and landscape are listed, so the picture is an outdoor study of the Norman scenery, painted on cheap, portable board rather than canvas. The choice of support suggests a quick, direct sketch made in the field, and the view is a landscape without figures or story, in which light and foliage are the subjects.","i":"Bonington spent much of his short life in northern France, and the Norman countryside around Rouen and the Seine was one of his favorite sketching grounds. English painters from Turner onward had found the region picturesque, with its river, churches and half-timbered towns. Oil on millboard was a practical medium for quick studies outdoors, and Bonington's sketches were admired by French friends such as Delacroix and by dealers who recognized their freshness.","c":"The virtue of such a sketch is immediacy: Bonington makes a few touches stand for foliage, ground and air, and the softness and speed of the handling anticipate the freshness that later painters of the century prized. It is slight, and it makes no pretense of being a finished picture, but it shows his gift of sight. A small, fresh record of a place, and a good example of a type of picture in which he excelled."},"met:438427":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 95 by 173 cm, painted about 1582, showing the Lamentation over the dead Christ. The Virgin, Mary Magdalene and Saint John the Evangelist are recorded as the mourners, gathered around the body in the moment after the Crucifixion. The horizontal format suits a long, laid-out body with the mourners ranged around it, in an intimate scene that invites the viewer to join the grief.","i":"The Lamentation shows the grief of the Virgin and Christ's closest followers over his body before burial, an image encouraged by Catholic piety as a focus for meditation on the Passion. Ludovico worked in Bologna, where around 1582 he and his cousins Agostino and Annibale began their reform of painting, rejecting the artificial elegance of late Mannerism for direct observation from life, strong emotion and clarity.","c":"Early Ludovico is more daring and moody than the polished later works: a Lamentation of about 1582 uses dark tonality, naturalistic bodies and expressive grief. The picture hints at the reform that would shape Bolognese painting, and the weight of the dead Christ and the compressed grouping give it power. It is not a perfect picture, but a historically important one, from the formative years of the Carracci."},"nga:41674":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 139 by 111 centimeters, painted in oil about 1593, shows the dream of Saint Catherine of Alexandria. The museum's record gives no description of the picture itself, so how the saint is posed, how the vision appears and the colors are left unstated. Lodovico Carracci, the eldest of the Carracci family of Bolognese reformers, painted devotional pictures like this one with soft light, emotional warmth and naturalistic faces.","i":"In the legend of Catherine, a princess and scholar of Alexandria, the saint is converted after a vision of the Virgin and Child, and, according to the later tradition, she dreams that the Child places a ring on her finger, her mystic marriage to Christ. Dream pictures gave painters a chance to show a heavenly vision and a sleeping woman together, a challenge of light and mood that suited the intimate emotion Counter-Reformation art favored.","c":"Lodovico is the most emotional of the Carracci, and a dreamy, tender subject suits him: soft modeling, warm color and a mood of hushed inwardness set him apart from the clarity of Annibale. His pictures can lack firm drawing, and he is sometimes eclipsed by his cousins, but at his best he shows a sensitivity that anticipates the later Baroque. A picture on this theme gives scope for it."},"met:438776":{"d":"A tiny devotional painting on copper, about 30 by 25 cm, dated 1607, showing the Virgin and Child with saints, among them Peter, Andrew and Catherine. A copper support is smooth and durable and was used for jewel-like cabinet pictures. The Virgin and Child are shown with the saints in a shared space, in the type known as a sacra conversazione, and the small scale suits private prayer or a collector's cabinet.","i":"A sacra conversazione brings the Virgin and saints together in one space, as if in quiet conversation, and the saints are chosen for the patron's devotion: Catherine of Alexandria, a patron of young women and philosophers, and the brothers Peter and Andrew, apostles, who may reflect the patron's names or family. Copper panels were prized by collectors and for private devotion, small, portable and brilliant in color.","c":"On copper, Ludovico's late style, warm, tender and a little sentimental, shows in the affectionate interplay of the group, and the smoothness of the support suits fine finish. As a miniature of his altarpiece manner it works well, though its conventions are familiar. It is a fine, conventional work, more charming than powerful, which gains from the jewel-like quality of the copper."},"met:438561":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 172 by 116 cm, painted about 1616, showing the Denial of Saint Peter, the moment in the high priest's courtyard when Peter, questioned about knowing Christ, denies him three times. The Gospels set it at night around a fire, with a servant girl and bystanders as accusers and a cock whose crow will shame him. Pictures of this subject are often a drama of a few figures shown close to the viewer.","i":"Peter's denial (Luke 22:54-62) follows Christ's arrest: Christ had predicted that Peter would deny him three times before the cock crowed. The story, with Peter's tears afterward, was favored in the Counter-Reformation as an image of human weakness and the repentance that redeems it, and of the sacrament of penance. Night scenes by firelight were also popular after Caravaggio, offering painters a chance to show dramatic light.","c":"By about 1616 Ludovico's style had become more emotional, darker and freer in handling than the early classicism of the Carracci reform, and the Denial suits that late manner, with its psychological drama of guilt and exposure. A large canvas of this kind should show how he combined Bolognese structure with a more atmospheric, dark-toned expressiveness. It is a serious, powerful late work, if less polished than his earlier ones."},"nga:76210":{"d":"A bouquet of flowers in a glass vase stands on a wooden surface in front of a dark gray wall. The bouquet has a pink-and-white striped tulip, large pink and white roses, green leaves, and smaller white and blue bell-shaped flowers. A dragonfly perches on a tall stem at top right, and a colorful butterfly or moth rests on a leaf in the lower left corner. The panel is small, about 32 by 23 cm, and its plain dark ground makes the colors stand out.","i":"Striped pink-and-white tulips were 'broken' tulips, their stripes caused by a virus unknown at the time, and they were the most prized and expensive, symbols of rarity after the Dutch tulip mania of the 1630s. The dragonfly and butterfly add naturalistic touches and traditional signs of transience and transformation. Flower pieces suggested the beauty and brevity of life, and the glass vase suits transparency and fragility.","c":"The attraction is the clear, fresh color of the flowers against the plain dark wall, the transparent glass vase and the small creatures. Van Thielen was a pupil of the Jesuit flower painter Daniel Seghers, and the precision shows. The arrangement is simple, even sparse beside the lavish bouquets of Brueghel, and the dark ground concentrates attention. It is a modest, delicate work, well observed."},"nga:46179":{"d":"Three nude women, three men and a child stand or sit under tall trees in the foreground of a wide landscape. Paris, seated with his back to the viewer on a ruby-red cloth, holds out a golden apple, and the central woman takes it while a putto with a bow reaches toward Paris. Another woman wears a gold helmet and holds a spear, the third a tiara. Mercury, in winged cap and with a staff of serpents, leans on a stump, and a river god sits beside an overturned urn. In the deep distance run deer, a donkey rider, a tomb with obelisks and hazy buildings.","i":"The Judgment of Paris tells how three goddesses quarreled over a golden apple inscribed 'to the fairest,' and Paris, a shepherd who was a prince of Troy, awarded it to Venus, who had promised him Helen, so beginning the Trojan War. Renaissance painters loved the subject as a chance to show three female nudes in different poses, and as a moral about choice between power, wisdom and love. Sons, a Netherlandish painter who worked in Italy, sets the story in a vast imaginary landscape.","c":"The landscape is the real subject: a panorama with a tomb complex and obelisks, deer, a bridge and small figures, a world picture in the Flemish manner. The figures are firm but static, derived from Italian models, and their stiffness reveals the painter's northern origins. The picture's charm is encyclopedic detail and Mannerist elegance rather than drama, and it is interesting as a hybrid of north and south."},"met:436641":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man on a wooden panel, about 67 by 55 centimetres, painted in 1647. The size suits a bust-length or half-length portrait, the kind an Amsterdam citizen of means would commission for his home. Bartholomeus van der Helst, then in the years of his rise as the city's fashionable portraitist, usually presents his sitters in clear light, with smooth finish and carefully rendered dress.","i":"In Holland in the 1640s a portrait recorded a citizen's face and his place in the community, and clients often liked to be shown composed, well dressed and at ease. Van der Helst, Haarlem-born, rose in Amsterdam as Rembrandt's portraits grew more individual and, it is usually said, less fashionable. In 1648 he painted the large civic-guard banquet celebrating the Peace of Münster, now in the Rijksmuseum. Sitters like this unknown man chose him for just that combination of likeness and polish.","c":"Van der Helst offered what many patrons wanted: a clear likeness, tidy construction and a pleasing polish. That polish is both his strength and his limit. Compared with Rembrandt's portraits of the same decade, the sitter is seen from outside, as a fine citizen, not as a person with an inner life. Still, within that aim he is accomplished, and a portrait like this shows why he was so popular."},"met:436642":{"d":"A woman appears as a musician on a large canvas, about 138 by 111 centimetres, with a lute and a cello among the instruments that identify her pastime. The picture was painted in 1662 by the Amsterdam portraitist Bartholomeus van der Helst. In Dutch painting a woman at her music was a familiar subject that could serve either as a genre picture or as a flattering portrait in character, and the large, finished scale here suggests a costly commission.","i":"Music in Dutch seventeenth-century painting carried several meanings. It could stand for harmony, for the refinement of a cultivated household, or for love, since the lute was a long-standing emblem of amorous feeling. A woman with instruments was therefore both a compliment and a quiet moral. It is not clear whether the picture records a particular sitter or an idealised type, and it may combine the two.","c":"Van der Helst's polished manner suits a subject of silk and wood, and his gift for the gleam of satin and the shine of instruments would have found plenty of scope here. What is missing is the intimacy and silence of the great Dutch music scenes: Vermeer's women seem to listen, whereas van der Helst's sitters are presented. It is a handsome decorative canvas, a brilliant surface rather than an absorbing moment."},"met:435759":{"d":"The severed head of Saint John the Baptist is shown on a charger, a flat dish, as a single devotional image on a panel of poplar wood. Herod's banquet, the scene that produced it, is left out entirely, so that the viewer faces the saint's face directly, as in a private picture meant for prayer. It was painted around 1500, in the years when Aelbert Bouts, son of the great Dieric, ran his father's workshop in Leuven.","i":"After Herod Antipas had John the Baptist beheaded at the request of Salome, his head was brought to her on a platter (Mark 6:14-29), and that gruesome gift became a devotional image in its own right. Late medieval viewers contemplated the head on a charger as an image of martyrdom and of the sacrifice that foretold Christ's own. Aelbert Bouts, son of Dieric, ran a workshop in Leuven that supplied such private panels.","c":"The Head of Saint John is a test of nerve: it must not be sentimental, nor merely gruesome. A picture of this type depends on the calm of the severed face and the plainness of its presentation. Aelbert Bouts is a modest talent beside his father, whose figures have an authority that his cannot match, but within a familiar type his craft is clear, and the image remains arresting."},"met:435573":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, about 213 by 147 cm, painted in the 1730s, showing Flora, the Roman goddess of flowers, with Zephyr, the west wind. A goddess, putti, flowers and a landscape are noted, so the picture is a mythological idyll, the god of the gentle wind and the goddess of blossoms among flower-bearing cherubs. At this size it suited a salon or staircase, and the soft, decorative figures are meant to please.","i":"Ovid tells in the Fasti that the nymph Chloris was seized by Zephyrus, who married her and gave her dominion over flowers, after which she was called Flora. The theme of spring's gentle wind awakening the flowers was ideal for Rococo decoration: sweet, sensuous and light. Amigoni, a Venetian who worked in London in the 1730s and later in Munich, Paris and Madrid, brought a decorative Venetian Rococo manner to northern courts.","c":"Amigoni's Rococo lightness is the point: the Venetian taste for light color, silky flesh and billowing drapery, with figures arranged effectively as decoration. As painting it is more charming than profound, and it does not seek depth of feeling, but it is skilled and pleasing, typical of his courtly manner, and its large scale gives it presence. It is a pleasing, decorative work in the best sense of the word."},"nga:70329":{"d":"A bust-length portrait, about 76 by 63.5 cm, painted about 1670 to 1680, of a man named John Eldred, shown facing slightly aside with his head turned toward the viewer. He has wavy, light brown or blond hair falling to his shoulders, a round face with a prominent nose and a light complexion, and wears a dark robe with a white collar. No jewelry is visible, and the hands are not shown. The background is a muted brownish tone that forms an oval frame around the figure.","i":"The dark robe with a plain white collar may be the loose undress costume that Restoration portraits often used to give a sitter a timeless, informal dignity, free of showy armor or finery. The painted oval, a framing device favored in later seventeenth-century English portraits, gave a neat, formal shape. Riley became one of the leading portrait painters of England after Lely's death in 1680. Nothing about Eldred himself is recorded here.","c":"John Riley's portraits are quieter and more observant than the glamorous formula of his predecessor Lely: he lets the sitter's face speak, the palette is muted and the likeness frank. This restrained bust-length portrait shows his talent at its calm best, though the generic oval and robe offer little individuality. It is a sound example of a painter often overshadowed by Kneller."},"met:438098":{"d":"A vertical panel, about 66 by 48 cm, painted in 1872, of a woman at tea. A tea set, a chair, a table, a hat with a ribbon, and a view of the River Thames with a ship are named among its subjects, so the scene is a London interior with a window onto the river. It was painted soon after Tissot settled in London, and it shows the new setting and subjects, the fashion and domestic ritual of English society, that he made his own.","i":"Tissot, a Frenchman, moved to London in 1871 after the upheavals of the Franco-Prussian War and the Commune, and quickly became the chronicler of fashionable English life: tea, boating, parties and the Thames. Tea-drinking was a signature domestic ritual of the English middle and upper classes, an ideal subject for a painter of manners. The Thames view places the picture in the modern city, and the ship adds a touch of London's commerce.","c":"Tissot's strength is detail: fabrics, accessories and fashionable poses, each depicted with a tailor's care, and the window with its river life gives depth. The figure risks being a fashion plate, and the psychology is thin. It is a characteristic piece of early London Tissot, quietly sophisticated and observant, more social record than drama, and it shows the confidence of a painter who had found a market."},"nga:56669":{"d":"A young girl whose blond curls catch the sunlight in an otherwise dim living room kneels on all fours on a patterned rug, looking up, while the faces of three other children peek over furniture behind her. She wears a white dress with lacy ruffles and scarlet ribbons. Behind her stand a leather chaise covered with a tiger pelt and armchairs draped in furs, and a young woman in the back corner reads a newspaper with her feet on a footstool. A glass door opens onto a conservatory, and mirrors catch the sun. Signed J.J. Tissot.","i":"Tissot's pictures of the late 1870s show the comfortable surroundings of upper-middle-class London: furs and tiger skins, signs of empire and wealth, the conservatory, a Victorian status symbol, and the tea set. The game of hide-and-seek brings a note of play to a room whose formality is suggested by its furniture, and the adult absorbed in her newspaper at the back is the oblivious foil. The room probably represents Tissot's own house in St John's Wood.","c":"The picture is cleverly constructed: the bright child in the foreground against the dim interior, the faces peeking over furniture like a hidden picture, and the cluttered, cozy Victorian room with its furs and mirrors described with Tissot's love of detail. The mood is sweet, but the detail is controlled so that it never goes cloying. The pleasure is in the observation, and the reading woman's unconcern adds a touch of humor."},"met:440729":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 56 by 43 cm, painted about 1875, of a woman with a parasol in a garden on a spring morning. A woman, a garden and a parasol are recorded, so the scene is one of fashionable leisure in a private garden, of the kind found at the villas of St John's Wood. The picture is described by type: a single figure in light clothing, with the parasol as the sun's shield and the garden as the setting of her morning.","i":"Tissot's garden pictures use parasols and light dresses to mark leisure in the 1870s. The title evokes freshness and the season, and the parasol is both a sun-shield and a sign of the fashionable pale complexion. Degas invited Tissot to join the first Impressionist exhibition in 1874, and he declined, preferring his polished, detailed manner and the profitable market for pictures of English society. No sitter is recorded here.","c":"A fashionable woman in a garden is a staple subject of the Impressionist era, as in Monet's Woman with a Parasol of 1875, but Tissot's version is more polished and descriptive, and less interested in the effects of light than in costume and manner. The seasonal freshness is appealing, and the picture is pleasant, if lightweight, and a good example of how Tissot kept his distance from the Impressionists."},"met:438887":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 25 by 35 cm, painted about 1881, of a garden in full sunlight, as the French title En plein soleil says. A woman sewing, a boy, a girl and parasols are listed, so the scene is a quiet domestic moment in a garden, with the adult at her needlework and the children near her under the shade of parasols. It is a small, bright cabinet picture of the comfortable life of a London household.","i":"Tissot's garden scenes of the early 1880s, made as he approached the end of his London years, are among his most Impressionist in light. The title refers to the interest in outdoor light, and the sewing woman and the children show quiet domestic life; the parasol is again a shield against the sun. His garden pictures of these years often show his companion, Kathleen Newton, and children of her family, though this record names no sitters.","c":"Small, bright and relaxed, the panel feels more spontaneous than his big society pictures. The sunlight is bright rather than shimmering, and Tissot remains more illustrator than Impressionist: the figures are precisely drawn, and the light is described rather than dissolved. It is a charming minor work, and its modest scale suits the quiet, summery subject, though it does not carry the novelty of the true Impressionists."},"met:924787":{"d":"A small portrait of the artist's sister, Sophie Købke, in the hall of the family's home at Kastellet, the Citadel of Copenhagen. The oil on canvas measures about 31 by 21 centimeters and dates from about 1830. The record carries only the keywords Portraits and Women, so her pose, dress and the arrangement of the hall are not described. It was acquired by the museum in 2025, and the title notes that she later became Sophie Krohn.","i":"Købke grew up at Kastellet, where his father was a master baker supplying the garrison, and he painted the fortress, its surroundings and his family and friends again and again, with an unforced intimacy that is the hallmark of Danish Golden Age painting. He studied under C. W. Eckersberg at the Royal Academy in Copenhagen, who taught his pupils to paint directly from what they saw. Sophie, born in 1807, was three years older than her brother.","c":"At 31 by 21 centimeters this is a small, quiet picture, and Købke's gift for the unshowy is what makes it work: he seems to have looked at his sister as he looked at a wall or a doorway, with patient attention to light and surface, and without flattery. His portraits of family have a hushed intimacy that is rare in their day. Modest in size and scope, and exact in feeling."},"met:436825":{"d":"A portrait of the artist's younger brother, Valdemar Hjartvar Købke, painted around 1838 in oil on canvas and measuring about 54 by 46 centimeters. The record lists a man and a portrait, so Valdemar's pose, dress and expression are not described. The museum acquired the portrait in 1990 through the Catharine Lorillard Wolfe Collection fund. Valdemar, born in 1813, was three years younger than his brother.","i":"Købke's portraits of his relatives, painted in the 1830s, are among the most affecting of the Danish Golden Age, which prized the quiet observation of ordinary life. His brother sat for him more than once. In 1838 Købke was twenty-eight, and he left for Italy that year, an experience that opened his landscape painting to new light; the portrait dates to about that time.","c":"A family portrait by a painter whose gift lay in direct, unglamorous looking. The strongest Købke portraits give the sitter room and time, and a brother is the ideal subject for such patience: no need to flatter, no wish to impress. The result is typically a quiet, firmly drawn head against a plain ground, with understated color. Not a showpiece, but a faithful, human picture."},"met:437764":{"d":"A horse, a groom and a dog stand in a landscape, painted in 1768 on a canvas about 102 by 126 cm. The title names the third Duke of Dorset, a young aristocrat and sportsman, as the owner of the hunter. Trees, a man, a dog, a horse and a landscape are noted, so the picture is a commissioned portrait of a horse with the groom who cares for it and a dog, set against a quiet, open country background.","i":"Stubbs, a self-taught anatomist whose Anatomy of the Horse was published in 1766, built his reputation on the study of the horse. Sporting pictures for aristocrats, with horses and their grooms, were a thriving trade, and the groom and dog place the horse in the working world of the stable and the hunt. His pictures show horses as individual animals with character, and the owner's name in the title confirms that this was a portrait.","c":"Stubbs's horses are the best in British art: anatomically convincing, calm and individual. The landscape is a quiet backdrop, and the composition is formal and still, almost like a portrait of a person, which gives the animal dignity. The groom and dog are secondary. Some will find the pictures cool, but their exactness gives them lasting authority, and this one is a fine, characteristic example."},"nga:42287":{"d":"A group portrait, about 100 by 127 cm, painted in 1769, of Captain Samuel Sharpe Pocklington, his wife, Pleasance, and possibly his sister, Frances. The picture is a conversation piece, a portrait of several members of a family or friends in a landscape or interior, informal in pose. Stubbs, known for horses, painted few such pictures, and he sometimes set the groups in a landscape with horses, but the details of this one are not given.","i":"Conversation pieces were fashionable in Georgian England as a way for gentry families to portray themselves at leisure, in their own estates, rather than in formal poses. The 'possibly' in the title signals that the third sitter's identity is uncertain. Such pictures recorded a family's status and affections, and Stubbs's commissions for them came from the same landed class who bought his horses.","c":"Stubbs is not at his best with people: his gift lies in animals, and his figures in group portraits can look stiff beside the vivid presence of a horse. But the clarity of arrangement and the calm, sunlit landscape make the picture a distinguished record of a family. Its interest is partly in what it reveals of Stubbs's range, the animal painter treating human sitters with the same cool, exact attention."},"nga:110281":{"d":"A white poodle sits in a punt, a flat-bottomed boat, on calm water, in a vertical canvas about 127 by 101.5 cm, painted about 1780. The title names the subject and little else is known, so the picture is placed by type: a pet portrait on a large scale, in which a fashionable lapdog is given the dignity usually reserved for a horse or a person. It was made for an owner who wanted his poodle's likeness.","i":"Dog portraits were a thriving business for English sporting painters, and a poodle, a fashionable breed, would belong to a wealthy owner who wanted its likeness. The punt suggests water retrieving: poodles were originally water dogs, and the name comes from the German 'pudeln,' to splash in water, so the picture may play on this heritage. Stubbs's pet portraits show the same attention to character as his horses.","c":"A dog in a boat is a quirky subject, and Stubbs treats it with a solemn clarity: the animal's alert pose against the water, and the white coat as the picture's bright note. The picture avoids sentimentality, though the humor comes from the dignity. It is one of the more charming of his later works, and a reminder that the painter of the Anatomy of the Horse could give a poodle the same seriousness."},"nga:195072":{"d":"Dozens of people stand, skate, play a game like hockey or ride horse-drawn sleds on a frozen river that winds along a town at left and into the distance, in a nearly square panel. The horizon is about a quarter of the way up, and ivory clouds billow against a pale blue sky. Men wear tall hats, capes and breeches, and women wear pleated ruffs, fur muffs and long dresses. A man balances on one skate near the lower right and a girl pushes herself along in a sled. Bare trees line the snowy bank. Signed on a fence post, AV DN 1645.","i":"Winter scenes with skaters were a Dutch specialty from Avercamp onward, and the frozen river and canals brought all social classes together on the ice. They celebrate the pleasures of the cold winters of the so-called Little Ice Age, and the game like hockey is an early form of the Dutch game of kolf. Some viewers read such scenes as social comment, since the ice can be dangerous, but mostly they are delightful observation. Van der Neer, born in Gorinchem, is better known for moonlight.","c":"Van der Neer's winter pictures are later than Avercamp's and more atmospheric, with big skies and soft light, and the energy of the figures here is lively. The pale clouds and low horizon are classic. The picture is crowded with incident but ordered by the winding river, and its charm is high-spirited observation rather than the moody lighting for which he is famous. It is a fine example of the genre."},"nga:71369":{"d":"In almost complete darkness, the viewer looks across reeds and grasses lining a river spanned by a narrow, arched stone bridge. The horizon is about a third of the way up, and the sky is filled with clouds that glow blush pink, flint gray and lavender purple. A small, porcelain-white moon casts an opalescent gleam on the water under the arch. A path leads to a copse of tall trees, and a man in crimson and a woman in green stand near the gate of a walled enclosure, moonlight glinting on their white collars and gold buttons. Spires and stepped rooflines are outlined against the sky.","i":"Moonlit scenes were Van der Neer's specialty, along with winter scenes. Night views offered a challenge: painting light and its effect on color and reflection. His moonlight is not the Romantic sublime but a quiet atmosphere, and the small couple by the gate gives human presence. Dutch viewers probably valued the realism of dusk and the reflective water. The record calls the picture topographical, but the scene is likely a composite rather than a particular place.","c":"A tour de force of controlled darkness: the pink, gray and lavender clouds against the black foreground, the little moon's pearly reflection under the bridge, and the tiny figures glinting at collar and button reward close looking. It is among the best Dutch nocturnes, though the darkness can make the picture hard to read. The effect is quiet, even melancholic, rather than dramatic, and the tonal control is remarkable."},"met:437190":{"d":"A farrier's forge glows in a night landscape, a panel of about 48 by 61 centimeters painted in the early or mid 1650s. Fire, night and a blacksmith's trade make up the subject, so the picture is about the contrast between darkness and the glare of the fire, with the shoeing of horses as its pretext. It belongs to the specialty for which Van der Neer was best known: scenes lit by moonlight and fire.","i":"Van der Neer specialized in moonlit landscapes and night views lit by fires, which Dutch collectors enjoyed for their atmosphere and technical bravura; painting convincing darkness and glow was a recognized test of skill. The blacksmith's forge, where fire and iron meet, was a familiar rustic subject, and the farrier, who shoes horses, is an ordinary trade. The picture, however, is less about labor than about the glow itself.","c":"Van der Neer is the Dutch master of the night. His mixture of firelight and cool darkness is subtle, and he gives the dark itself a sense of air that few contemporaries match. A forge scene lets him show how an intense glow dies away into gloom. He is limited by the small repertoire of his scenes, but he repeats them with great skill, and for atmosphere few Dutch painters persuade so well."},"met:437191":{"d":"A river landscape at sunset holds trees, cattle and a rider on horseback. The canvas, about 51 by 71 centimeters and painted in the 1650s, has a wide horizontal format that suits the long sweep of a river and a glowing evening sky. The animals and rider are small figures that animate the scene and give it scale, while the light does the principal work.","i":"Evening and night effects were Van der Neer's trademark. Sunset pictures presented the Dutch countryside bathed in a glow, an image of a calm and prosperous homeland with grazing cattle and waterways. The cattle and rider are staffage, the small figures that give a landscape scale and life. His generation learned from Van Goyen's tonal unity and then added more color and more drama of sky.","c":"A competent and charming example of Van der Neer's evening landscapes, in which the sky does most of the work and the land sinks into soft shadow. His strength is atmosphere; his weakness, a certain sameness of device. As a sunset it lacks the drama of his moonlit nocturnes, but it keeps their gentle poetry, and the generous proportion of sky to land gives the picture its calm, typically Dutch breadth."},"met:437192":{"d":"Figures skate and play a stick-and-ball game on a frozen river in an evening light. This small panel, only 23 by 35 centimeters and painted around 1660, is crowded with tiny people enjoying the ice. The subject is communal sport on a frozen waterway, seen in the cold clear air at the end of the day. Games with sticks and a ball on the ice were a favorite Dutch winter pastime.","i":"Winter scenes were a Dutch specialty in the 1600s, a time of unusually cold winters when skating was a popular pastime for all classes. Such pictures show the country at leisure and the frozen waterways as public ground, and the crowd of ordinary people is part of their appeal. Van der Neer, who painted winters throughout his career, set his skaters in an evening glow, joining his gift for light to a favorite theme.","c":"On this small panel the pleasures are of detail and atmosphere: tiny figures, a glowing sky, the cold clarity of evening. Van der Neer is less lively with figures than Hendrick Avercamp, the great earlier painter of ice scenes, but his sense of light across the ice gives his winters a quietly luminous mood. As a pocket-sized picture it is easy to enjoy, and a little hard to forget."},"met:436997":{"d":"A gold-ground panel, about 60 by 43 cm, painted about 1400, showing the Crucifixion with the Virgin Mary, Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene, among the women recorded. The paint layer has been transferred from its original wood panel to a modern support, which can dull a surface. The type is a small Passion scene from a German altarpiece, with a gold ground and the soft, flowing style of late Gothic painting in Westphalia.","i":"The Crucifixion with the Virgin and John at either side follows the Gospel of John (19:25-27), and Mary Magdalene, often shown embracing the foot of the cross, is also part of the group. The picture is probably part of a series of Passion scenes. The Master of the Berswordt Altar is a modern name for an anonymous painter who worked in Dortmund in Westphalia around 1400 in the 'soft style,' with sweet faces and flowing draperies.","c":"Conrad von Soest and his circle brought a new human sweetness to Westphalian painting, and the Berswordt Master's Crucifixion probably shows less inspiration by comparison. It conveys the grief of the mourners with graceful, flowing line, however, and the gold gives the scene a quiet splendor. The transfer and modern support limit the surface, and the picture is of historical rather than aesthetic importance for most viewers, though technically interesting."},"met:438466":{"d":"A gold-ground panel, about 58 by 43 cm, painted about 1400, showing the Flagellation. Men and Christ are named among its subjects, so the scene follows the usual type: Christ stands bound while tormentors lash him with rods and whips. The paint layer has been transferred from its original wood to a modern support. Its date, size and manner match those of The Crucifixion by the same painter, so the two panels probably belonged to the same Passion series.","i":"The Flagellation (Matthew 27:26, John 19:1) is the Passion episode in which Pilate has Jesus scourged before the Crucifixion, a standard scene in Passion cycles that stressed his humble endurance. Late medieval devotion encouraged believers to share his pain, dwelling on the lashes and the instruments. The Master of the Berswordt Altar worked in Dortmund around 1400, and the panel belongs to the soft, elegant style of the region.","c":"The brutality of the subject is softened by the gentle, flowing style: the tormentors' attitudes can be almost graceful. This is typical of soft-style painting, where even violence is elegant. As a result the picture is more decorative than harrowing, and its interest lies in the narrative economy and in the survival of such early panel painting. It is a fine small document of the Gothic Passion cycle, rather than a powerful image."},"met:437000":{"d":"A profile portrait of a woman, about 40 by 27 cm, probably from the 1450s, in tempera and gold, transferred from its original wood panel to canvas. It is a Florentine likeness, with gold used for jewels and ornament, the sitter probably shown against a plain ground as a record of status. It would have been made for a betrothal or marriage.","i":"Profile portraits of women were fashionable in Florence in the mid-fifteenth century and were often made for betrothals and marriages. They present a young woman as an ideal of beauty and a bearer of her family's prestige, and costly dress, jewels and hair arrangements display wealth. The painter is an anonymous Florentine known only through an altarpiece, the Castello Nativity, and the sitter's name is not recorded.","c":"Profile portraits seek elegance of line rather than psychology, and the ornaments and outline are the point. The picture's interest is partly its anonymity, since both artist and sitter are unknown, and the transfer to canvas may have cost it something of its original surface. It is a good example of a type that inspired later portraits and carries the poise of its period, but it is modest as an individual work."},"met:437662":{"d":"A portrait of a boy, William Archer Shee (1810-1899), the painter's son, shown at about ten years old, painted about 1820 in oil on canvas, about 76 by 63 cm. It is a family portrait of a child by his father, a professional portraitist, probably kept in the home rather than made for a client. A canvas of this size is commonly used for half-length portraits, so the boy is probably shown to about the waist.","i":"Shee, born in Dublin, became one of the leading portrait painters in London and succeeded Sir Thomas Lawrence as President of the Royal Academy in 1830. Portraits of a painter's own children are intimate, kept in the family rather than sent for exhibition, and they allowed him to work without a client's demands. The sitter lived to 1899, long after his father's death in 1850, and is identified in the title as the artist's son.","c":"A father's portrait of his son often has an easy affection that commissioned portraits lack, and Shee, a polished, competent society portraitist in the Lawrence mold, may have relaxed in a family subject. As a Regency child portrait it is probably sound and graceful, though Shee never reached Lawrence's brilliance. The personal context makes it more interesting than the average portrait of its date."},"met:435672":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 122 by 176 cm, probably painted in the early 1630s, showing the lovers Angelica and Medoro from Ariosto's epic Orlando Furioso. A couple and male and female nudes are recorded, so the scene is the lovers' union in a pastoral setting, painted with the warm color and soft flesh that Blanchard learned from the Venetian painters during his years in Italy.","i":"In Ariosto's poem, Angelica, the princess of Cathay who had spurned all suitors, finds the wounded pagan soldier Medoro, nurses him and falls in love, and their idyll in the countryside drives the hero Orlando mad with jealousy when he finds their names carved on trees. The episode was loved by painters for its pastoral romance and nudity, and the pairing of a female and a male nude was a way to display both.","c":"Blanchard's Venetian sensuality is the interest: soft flesh and warm color, an unusual openness of tone in French painting of the 1630s, when the classicism of Poussin was still ahead. The lovers are graceful if idealized, and the picture delights without depth. It should be seen as part of a brief, brilliant career, cut short by his death in 1638 at thirty-eight, and it is a charming, sensuous work."},"met:437221":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 133 by 42 cm, in tempera on a gold ground, showing Saint Paul. The apostle stands alone, as in a side panel of a polyptych, a multi-panel altarpiece in which saints flank a central image of the Virgin or Christ. Paul is usually shown bearded and balding, holding a book for his letters and a sword for the manner of his martyrdom. The panel dates from the later fourteenth century.","i":"Paul, the apostle to the Gentiles, wrote the letters that form much of the New Testament, and the sword refers to his beheading in Rome. Sienese painters, working in the tradition of Duccio and Simone Martini, gave saints a sweet formality. Niccolò di Buonaccorso worked in Siena in the second half of the fourteenth century, in the generation after the Black Death of 1348, when Sienese painting was conservative and devotional.","c":"A solitary saint on gold works by the dignity of its pose and the line of its drapery, and the conservative Sienese manner prized refinement over novelty. Niccolò's panel is competent and attractive but derivative of earlier masters. It is valuable as a reminder that, after the plague, Sienese painters sustained a tradition rather than renewing it, and that a single standing figure could carry a good deal of devotional force."},"met:13058":{"d":"A lone man holding a firearm stands near a tree in a landscape, on a small upright oil on canvas about 36 by 22 centimeters. The title calls him an African sentinel, a guard on watch. The few elements point to a single figure rather than a crowded narrative, and the painting dates from 1865, the year the American Civil War ended.","i":"A sentinel stands for vigilance and isolation, and Vedder, an American painter in the 1860s, often chose solitary figures and dreamlike settings for pictures meant to suggest a mood. Viewers in 1865 might have connected a guard with a rifle to the war, but nothing in the title fixes a topical meaning. The word African probably adds an exotic, remote note, in the way many Western artists of the day imagined Africa.","c":"The picture rests on a single idea, the watcher alone in a landscape, and it depends on mood rather than incident. It anticipates the brooding figures of Vedder's best-known works, though on a small scale and with less ambition. As an indicator of where the painter's imagination was heading, it is more significant than as a finished statement."},"met:13067":{"d":"A woman is the sole subject of this tall, narrow canvas of about 99 by 59 centimeters, and the title says what ails her: the loss of reason. She is an imagined figure rather than the portrait of an identifiable sitter. The picture dates from 1864 to 1865, the closing years of the American Civil War, when Vedder painted a group of strange, dreamlike pictures that made his early reputation.","i":"Madness was a favorite subject of Victorian art and literature, usually embodied in a woman, from Shakespeare's Ophelia to the heroines of popular novels. Vedder's picture may also reflect the grief and loss of the war years, but the title is the only certain guide. The painting asks the viewer to read feeling from a figure alone, with little narrative to explain it.","c":"The idea is ambitious: a single standing figure carries a heavy symbolic load, and the success of the picture depends on whether the viewer accepts that burden. The theme is serious and the tall format suits it. It is an early step in Vedder's search for a personal symbolic language, which he later developed in his designs for the Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám."},"met:14474":{"d":"The first of nine small canvases, each about 16.5 by 27 centimeters, shows a man, a boy and a donkey, with a flight of stairs in the scene. As the numbering signals, the nine were meant to be read in sequence, like panels of a story. The small horizontal format is the sort used for sketches, and the paint was applied on canvas around 1867 to 1868, when Vedder was settled in Rome.","i":"The tale is told in the tradition of Aesop and in La Fontaine's Fables: a miller and his son take a donkey to market and are criticized by everyone they meet, whatever arrangement they try, walking, riding or one riding while the other walks. If the numbering follows the story, this opening panel sets out the characters before the quarrels begin. The moral is that trying to please everyone pleases no one.","c":"Vedder is remembered for solemn, symbolic pictures, so a series of comic fable panels is a welcome surprise. The small size suits the humor, which depends on gesture and on the stubborn donkey. These panels are minor, but they show a light touch and a narrative sense that the grand allegories seldom allow."},"met:14475":{"d":"A woman, a man and the donkey appear together in a landscape on a small oil on canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters. It is the second of a numbered series of nine panels illustrating a fable, painted around 1867 to 1868. The fable depends on onlookers who comment on the travelers, and a new figure such as the woman here may represent one of them, though which episode is shown is not certain.","i":"Public opinion is the real antagonist of the fable. Each person the miller meets has a different complaint, that the boy rides while the old man walks, or that both walk beside a perfectly good donkey, and the miller yields to each, ending by pleasing no one. A viewer of the 1860s would have known the story from schoolbooks, La Fontaine or Aesop, so a few figures sufficed to evoke it.","c":"With only a few figures and a modest landscape, Vedder has to tell the joke through placement and gesture, which is exactly what makes these panels amusing. The scale is slight, and the painting reads as a quick comic episode rather than a finished composition. As an entry in a sequence, it works better than it would alone."},"met:14476":{"d":"The third panel of the series brings together a man, a boy, a woman and the donkey, with a tree in the scene. It is a small oil on canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters, painted around 1867 to 1868. The cast is varied in age and role, which suits a story about a family on the road and the strangers who pass judgment on them.","i":"The tree and the group of travelers and onlookers may suggest a roadside meeting, the kind of encounter in which the fable's critics speak up. The moral that follows from these encounters is that a person who bends to every opinion ends by satisfying none, and loses the donkey into the bargain. Vedder's choice of a simple outdoor setting keeps the focus on faces and gestures.","c":"The humor depends on contrasts of age and role, man, boy, woman and animal, and the small format keeps each figure brief. Vedder does not linger over any one character, and the effect is that of a lively anecdote. The series is a trifle beside his visionary work, but it shows him as a quick, genial storyteller."},"met:14477":{"d":"This panel adds a mother with an infant and a dog to the man and the donkey, on a small oil on canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters. A woman carrying a child is the kind of everyday passerby who fills the roads of a fable, and the dog adds a lively touch of rural life. It is the fourth of nine numbered panels and dates from about 1867 to 1868.","i":"In the usual versions of the fable, strangers on the road offer their opinions on how the miller and his donkey should travel, and the miller changes his plan each time. A mother with a child makes the encounter feel like ordinary village traffic. The humor works because anyone watching can recognize the mixture of well-meant advice and pointless fuss.","c":"This is a busier scene than its neighbors, and the group of mother, child, dog and donkey gives the little picture a human and domestic warmth that the allegorical Vedder seldom allowed himself. Within a small frame the figures must stay brief, and the effect is anecdotal rather than deep. It is likable evidence of a gentler, more observant side of the painter."},"met:14478":{"d":"The fifth panel is one of the sparest of the group, showing only a man and the donkey, on a small canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters. With the cast cut to two, the picture concentrates on the relation between man and beast, which is the comic engine of the fable. It forms part of a numbered series of nine painted around 1867 to 1868.","i":"The fable's moral grows out of the animal's patient role: the donkey is the object of everyone's advice and the one who pays when the advice goes wrong. A man alone with his donkey recalls the story's beginning or its ending, depending on where in the sequence the panel falls. Aesop's tradition used animals to make human folly easy to see, and Vedder keeps that clarity.","c":"Reduction helps: with only two figures, Vedder can concentrate on pose and attitude, and the small format gives the little scene a droll intimacy. The panel is slight, and without its neighbors it would be hard to place, but within the sequence it works as a quiet beat between episodes. These are charming minor pictures."},"met:14479":{"d":"A man, a donkey and a dog share this sixth panel, which measures about 16.5 by 27 centimeters, from a series of nine painted around 1867 to 1868. The dog, a lively note in a story about a patient animal, joins the figures on the road. As elsewhere in the series, the format is small and horizontal, so each figure has to be set down in a few touches.","i":"Aesop-style fables use plain animals and plain people to display human foibles, and the miller's story is a lesson in the folly of taking every opinion to heart. Dogs are not essential to the fable, which suggests that Vedder added this one for liveliness or local color, a choice in keeping with a painter telling an old story in his own way.","c":"The dog is a clever addition, a lively contrast to the stolid donkey. The picture shows how Vedder could vary a limited set of characters from panel to panel, though such small studies are more amusing than ambitious. As part of a complete sequence they gain a cumulative charm."},"met:14480":{"d":"The seventh panel shows a man, a boy and the donkey, the three principal parties of the fable, on a small oil on canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters, painted around 1867 to 1868. The trio matches the core of the story, in which father and son take their animal to market, and the panel concentrates on them. It is one of nine numbered pictures made as a sequence.","i":"The father and son are the fable's real subjects: each choice they make, walking together, riding by turns, riding together, is a reaction to someone else's remark. The story warns against being governed by public opinion, a lesson that held for the 1860s as much as for La Fontaine's seventeenth-century readers. The donkey, meanwhile, is a patient bystander to the human argument.","c":"The three-figure group is the economical heart of the series, and Vedder's small, quick touch is adequate to keep man, boy and donkey distinct. The pictures are small in ambition, and their charm lies in wit rather than finish. Taken together they show a painter more relaxed and humorous than his symbolic reputation suggests."},"met:14481":{"d":"In the eighth panel a boy and a man appear in a landscape, on a small oil on canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters. The open setting stresses the long road to market on which the fable's episodes take place. It is one of nine numbered panels, painted around 1867 to 1868 while Vedder was based in Rome. The picture shows two figures in an open setting, and the numbered series invites the viewer to supply the episode from the fable.","i":"Landscape matters in the fable because it is a journey: the miller and his son are on the road, and every stage brings a new critic. If the numbering follows the story, this late panel may fall near the ending, where the pair lose control of the situation and the donkey. Whether the animal's fate is shown here is not clear.","c":"The landscape gives the panel breathing room, and Vedder, who had spent years in Italy among landscape painters, can paint it with relaxed fluency. The figures are brief, and the joke is quieter than in the panels with more characters. It is a modest picture, but a pleasant one, and it gives the series a change of pace."},"met:14482":{"d":"The last panel in the numbered set shows the donkey alone in a landscape, on a canvas of about 16.5 by 27 centimeters. After eight scenes with human figures, the animal stands by itself, and the empty setting changes the tone. Painted around 1867 to 1868, it closes a series in which the humans quarrel and the donkey simply endures.","i":"If the numbers follow the story, a landscape with only the donkey may point toward the ending, when the miller has lost control of his animal. The sole donkey could also be read as the story's patient victim or its quiet survivor, and the panel itself does not fix which. The moral of the fable, that trying to please everyone satisfies no one, is left to the viewer.","c":"A donkey in a landscape is the simplest picture in the set, and the quietest. It gives Vedder a chance to show a sympathy for animals and for open country that sits beside his larger fantasies. As the closing note of a joke, it works; as an independent painting, it is too slight to stand on its own."},"cma:146071":{"d":"A very small upright landscape, oil on card laid on canvas and about 22 by 12.7 centimeters, takes three trees in Italy as its subject. Vedder painted it in Rome in 1871, and an inscription shows that he gave it as a souvenir to Edmund A. Ward of New York, who had bought a large painting from him. It is a keepsake rather than a major exhibition piece.","i":"Artists in Rome often gave small oil sketches to the patrons and visitors who bought their larger works, and Americans traveling in Italy valued them as mementos. The inscription turns this study into a gift, a courtesy between painter and buyer. Trees were a favorite subject for oil sketches because they let the painter study light, structure and air with a few strokes.","c":"The narrow format and the choice of a single motif give the little picture a clean design, with simple vertical rhythms, and the reduction hints at Vedder's later habit of turning landscape into a quiet symbol. It is a trifle beside his large symbolic pictures, but a pleasant and honest one, more valuable for what it shows of his daily practice than for ambition."},"met:13062":{"d":"A long, low canvas of about 46 by 149 centimeters shows women bathing, and the title marks them as Greek. The wide, shallow format recalls a classical frieze, a shape well suited to a row of figures. Dated 1872 to 1877, it belongs to the Roman period of Vedder, who by then had lived in Italy for years and often turned to antique themes.","i":"Bathing women in a Greek guise were a popular device in the nineteenth century, because an antique setting let painters show the nude without breaking the moral rules of the time. Vedder, living in Rome among ancient sculpture and wall paintings, could draw on classical sources for pose and composition. The scene is probably less a story than an idyll, a vision of an ideal ancient world.","c":"The long format asks for rhythm more than drama, and a row of bathers lets Vedder indulge his taste for line and calm classical order. The result is graceful and decorative rather than searching, and the Greek label is more an excuse than archaeology. For a painter better known for brooding allegories, it shows a sunnier, more sensuous side."},"cma:157434":{"d":"A tiny horizontal oil on panel, about 9 by 18 centimeters, carries a title that promises company and music. It dates from about 1875 and is smaller than a paperback book. At this size, forms are necessarily summarized into small touches, and the picture reads as a keepsake or private sketch rather than an exhibition piece. The scene is a gathering for music, a subject of domestic pleasure rather than of grand themes.","i":"A music party was a gathering of friends for singing or playing, a form of home entertainment that has appeared in painting since the seventeenth century. Vedder's Roman circle was full of artists, writers and visitors, and informal evenings were part of that life. A picture this small was probably made for personal pleasure or as a gift to a friend.","c":"At a size that fits in the hand, this is a sketch-like trifle: a few touches of paint to suggest a gathering in an interior. Its pleasure is its intimacy and speed, not the grandeur Vedder showed elsewhere. It is a sociable, unsymbolic piece, a relaxed note from a painter who was usually serious."},"met:13070":{"d":"Nude female dancers fill this canvas of about 61 by 96 centimeters, dated 1885. They represent the Pleiades, the seven sisters of Greek myth who became a cluster of stars, here imagined as a dance. The wide horizontal format suits a group in motion. The classical subject, the nude figures and the sense of a celestial dance are typical of Vedder's mature allegorical painting.","i":"In Greek myth the Pleiades were the seven daughters of Atlas and Pleione, pursued by the hunter Orion until, in one version, Zeus set them among the stars as the cluster in the constellation Taurus. Of the seven stars, only six are easy to see, which gave rise to tales of a lost sister. Dancing stars also echo the ancient idea of the music of the spheres.","c":"The theme is made for Vedder: a group of weightless nudes lets him indulge in flowing line and classical grace, and the subject relieves him of the need for literal anatomy. The effect is poetic, coolly decorative and somewhat generalized, prizing rhythm over the solid modeling of academic nudes. A pleasing picture rather than a profound one, it shows his late idealism clearly."},"met:13060":{"d":"A man, a female nude and Cupid are the figures noted in this small oil on wood, about 25 by 30.5 centimeters, dated 1887. The title makes a cup the center of the allegory. Vedder had published his illustrated edition of the Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám in 1884, a book in which a wine cup recurs, and by this date he was fond of such pictures of love and fate.","i":"Cupid, the classical god of desire, signals that this is an allegory of love rather than a portrait of lovers. The cup may stand for the draft of passion or of oblivion, as in the Rubáiyát, where the cup is a symbol of life's brief pleasure. Vedder's pictures of this kind present love as a force beyond reason, sweet and potentially dangerous.","c":"Small, allegorical and slightly mysterious, the picture suits the cabinet scale of its wood panel. Vedder gives the idea more weight than the incident, and without a strong narrative the viewer is left with a mood of sensual reverie. It is a pleasant piece of late Victorian classicism, more ornamental than moving."},"met:13061":{"d":"A wide, low landscape of about 25 by 51 centimeters, painted around 1880 to 1890 in oil on canvas, takes its subject from the title: cypress trees and poppies, probably in an Italian setting. It is the kind of small-scale landscape Vedder painted alongside his larger symbolic works, using the plants of the countryside as a quiet subject in their own right.","i":"Cypress and poppy carry old associations. The cypress has long been a tree of cemeteries and mourning, and the poppy a flower of sleep and forgetfulness, both used in classical poetry. Vedder, who knew the classical authors and the Rubáiyát, was attracted to such emblems, so the pairing may be read as a gentle reflection on time and death, though the title alone does not require it.","c":"Landscape was Vedder's relaxed mode, and the long format suits an open view. The symbolism is light enough not to spoil what is essentially a pleasant record of the countryside. It is a modest picture, more appealing as a quiet sketch than as a statement, and it shows the painter at ease away from his allegories."},"met:13065":{"d":"A long, low canvas of about 30.5 by 76 centimeters, painted around 1899, shows a beach and a snake: the serpent of the title, resting at its lair by the sea. The shoreline is the picture's stage, and the monster is the only actor. It is apparently Vedder's late return to a subject he had first painted in the 1860s.","i":"Sea serpents were a popular marvel in the nineteenth century, when newspaper reports of sightings kept the legend alive, and Vedder turned the rumor into a dreamlike scene of a monster at rest rather than in battle. The lonely beach gives the creature an air of mystery. It is tempting to read the snake as a symbol of the unknown or of nature's dormant power, but the picture is also a fantasy for its own sake.","c":"Vedder's fantasy works best through understatement, and a serpent asleep on an empty shore is more unsettling than a battle would be. The wide horizontal format stretches out the stillness. A late repetition of a subject always risks losing the surprise of the first, but the idea remains potent."},"met:442183":{"d":"A small oil on copper, about 39 by 37.5 cm, dated 1621, showing a cardinal's procession. Men and a procession are listed, so the picture is a ceremonial scene in which a cardinal and his attendants move through the streets of Rome. Copper, smooth and durable, suits the finely painted detail that small, crowded pictures need. It was made as a cabinet picture for a collector, not as a public display.","i":"In baroque Rome cardinals were princes of the Church, and their processions, with horses or carriages, servants and retainers, were displays of power seen daily in the streets. Ottavio Leoni, called Il Padovano, was the favorite portraitist of Roman society, famed for hundreds of chalk portraits of artists, prelates and nobles. The date, 1621, is the year Gregory XV, Alessandro Ludovisi, was elected pope.","c":"Leoni's strength lay in the direct observation of faces rather than in stage direction, and a procession gives scope for a crowd of small portraits. The copper's smooth surface suits the detail. The picture is lively in its small figures but lacks the large-scale grandeur of Roman altarpieces, and it is best valued as a record of ceremonial Rome rather than as a major painting."},"met:13105":{"d":"A self-portrait by Samuel Lovett Waldo, painted about 1815 in oil on wood, about 84 by 57 cm, when he was in his early thirties and established in New York as a portrait painter. In kind it is a self-presentation of a professional painter, made for his family or possibly as a sample of his skill. The panel is the size of a half-length portrait, and a painter's own likeness was also a way of advertising his hand to sitters who visited the studio.","i":"Waldo, born in Connecticut, studied in London with Benjamin West and others before settling in New York, where he became one of the city's leading portraitists and later partnered with William Jewett. A self-portrait lets an artist show his own skill and status. The sitter is the artist himself, and the identification is secure, though the occasion for which it was painted is not recorded here.","c":"Self-portraits by the American portraitists of the period are valuable for their directness. Waldo's style, trained in the English academic manner, is competent, polished and a little formal, and if the picture shows the same strengths it is an assured, plain, professional self-image, more informative than flashy. It records a painter at ease with his craft and with his place in New York society."},"met:13093":{"d":"A portrait of Andrew Jackson, painted in 1819 in oil on canvas, about 64 by 53 cm, when he was a famous general and not yet president. At this size it is a bust or head and shoulders portrait, a format that could be repeated, copied and engraved for the public that wanted an image of the hero. The date places it between his victory at New Orleans and his presidential campaigns, when a face like his had become something of a national possession.","i":"Jackson was the hero of the Battle of New Orleans in January 1815, which made him a national figure, and by 1819 he was a major general and a political celebrity, in his early fifties; he was elected president in 1828. Portraits of Jackson were in demand as images of national heroism, and Waldo painted him more than once. The image was crafted to show a strong, sober and determined man.","c":"Waldo's Jackson is a straightforward, competent record of a famous face, painted with the plain firmness of the American portrait tradition, the head as the focus. It is solid but not inventive as art, though valuable to historians as a likeness made from life of the general before he became a president. It captures a determined presence without flattery, which suited the republican taste of the time."},"met:13100":{"d":"A small portrait on wood, about 50 by 37.5 cm, painted about 1819, of a poor man known as Old Pat, the Independent Beggar, apparently a New York street figure. It is a head-and-shoulders likeness of a beggar treated as a serious portrait subject, which was unusual in the period. The word independent in the title suggests how he was seen.","i":"Waldo, the fashionable portraitist of New York's prosperous families, painted this beggar, possibly a man named Patrick MacGregor, and the image was sold in many copies as an engraving, as the Cleveland Museum records for its version. The subject made a point about the poor who were part of the street life of a growing commercial city. The picture hovers between portrait and genre, and it exists in more than one version.","c":"Beggar portraits had a long history, but Waldo's treatment is respectful: the sitter is individualized rather than caricatured, and the small, intimate wood panel suits a close study. As a society painter's departure from fashionable sitters, the picture is more memorable than his routine portraits, and its interest comes from the sitter's evident dignity, which resists the easy sentimentality of charity pictures."},"met:13103":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man, painted about 1820 in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 cm, whose name has not survived. It is a typical American half-length portrait of a prosperous professional or merchant, in sober dress against a plain ground, of the kind Waldo turned out for his busy New York clientele in the years after he settled in the city.","i":"In early nineteenth-century New York, portraits were commissioned by merchants, lawyers and clergy as records of standing and as keepsakes for their families. This sitter's name has not survived in the record, which makes the picture a representative of a type: a sober, upright citizen of the early Republic. Waldo's trade depended on such sitters, and he supplied them with likenesses at a steady pace.","c":"Plain, honest and competent, the portrait reflects the lessons Waldo learned in London in sober modeling and simple backgrounds. The anonymity is a loss, since the sitter's character cannot be linked to a career or a family, and the picture's value is as an example of a professional's standard product. It shows how well a trained painter could make a plain face seem dignified and credible."},"met:13104":{"d":"A portrait of the Reverend John Brodhead Romeyn, a Presbyterian minister in New York, painted about 1817 to 1820 in oil on canvas, about 77 by 64 cm. It is a half-length portrait of a clergyman, the kind of image a congregation or family would commission to honor its pastor, and one that might hang in a family home, a church parlor or a meeting room.","i":"Clergymen were among the principal sitters of American portraitists. A pastor's portrait honored a congregation's leader and could be engraved for distribution, and Romeyn's name appears in the title with his clerical rank. Sober black dress and perhaps a book would identify his office, though the record does not say how Waldo presented him. The picture belongs to the stream of professional portraiture that served the city's churches and families.","c":"Waldo's clerical portraits show his gift for gravity, and the sitter's intelligence is the point. The likeness is probably solid and unflattering, and as an example of Waldo's New York portraits it is likely among the better ones, with the plain presentation suited to a minister. It is a modest, reliable picture, valuable as a record of the pastor and of the way American portraitists honored religious leaders."},"met:13096":{"d":"A portrait of Matthew Clarkson, dated 1823, in oil on wood, about 84 by 65 cm. The larger size on a panel suggests an important commission for a man of standing. By 1823 Waldo had a thriving New York practice, and the picture is probably a half-length likeness of an older gentleman in dark clothes against a plain ground, made for his family or an institution.","i":"The sitter's name is that of a leading New York family, and the portrait presents an older man of the Revolutionary generation, a reminder of how the founders' circle was commemorated in the early Republic. Portraits of such men hung in family houses and institutions and gave the new nation a sense of continuity. Waldo's competent, dignified likenesses suited this commemorative purpose, though the record gives no further biography of the sitter.","c":"Waldo's best portraits of older men have a quiet strength: a head modeled with plain, solid paint, free of ornament, with the character carried by the face. This one, on a panel, should show that economy. It is not a work of invention, and its value is as a record of an individual and of a type of commemorative portrait, a plain and honest likeness in the best American tradition of the time."},"cma:97421":{"d":"A study of a disheveled man, perhaps named Patrick MacGregor, painted about 1819 in oil on wood, about 48 by 37 cm. The sitter's gaunt, careworn features suggest sympathy, but his forceful gaze presents him as someone who cannot easily be made into an object of pity. Waldo sold many copies of an engraving of the figure and also painted a finished portrait of him, The Beggar's Dessert, with a meager bone and a bowl as attributes.","i":"Waldo, a popular society portraitist, took a beggar as a subject and so challenged assumptions that successful New Yorkers may have made about their city's prosperity. The widely sold engraving suggests a public interest in the poor, and the picture belongs to an early nineteenth-century attention to the humble sitter, linked to charitable concerns. Waldo began his formal art training at sixteen, and by this date he was a well-established painter of the city's elite.","c":"The forceful gaze is the picture's key: the beggar is not a pathetic type but a personality. Waldo's sympathetic but unsentimental realism avoids the condescension common in beggar pictures, and the study is more arresting than many of his polished portraits of the prosperous. It is an unusual, empathetic subject for the period, and its restraint makes it an important and moving small picture."},"met:13099":{"d":"A portrait of the painter's wife, Mrs. Samuel L. Waldo, painted about 1826 in oil on wood, about 77 by 65 cm, when he was in his early forties. A painter's portrait of his own wife is a family piece rather than a commission, and the panel size is that of a half-length likeness, made without a client's demands on the pose or the finish.","i":"Family portraits allowed painters freedom from sitters' wishes, and a wife is the most accessible of models. By 1826 Waldo, in his early forties, was an established New York portraitist who had worked with William Jewett since 1818. The picture is a record of domestic life as well as of a likeness, and the sitter's own name is not given in the title beyond her husband's.","c":"Familiar sitters tend to bring out an artist's warmth, and the portrait likely has the intimacy of a family picture, with fewer concessions to fashion than a commissioned one. As Waldo's portraiture goes, it is probably among the most personal, though it is not tested here against his public work. It is a modest, affectionate record and an interesting complement to his professional portraits."},"nga:1132":{"d":"A portrait of Robert G. L. De Peyster, dated 1828, in oil on wood, about 84 by 64 cm, showing a man from the waist up, seated, slightly turned to his right but facing the viewer. His expression is composed, with prominent cheekbones and a slightly pointed nose, and his dark brown hair is curled at the sides and forehead in the style of the period. He wears a dark blue coat with gold buttons, a yellow waistcoat, a high white collar and a decorative pin, and he holds a folded paper in his right hand. The background is an indistinct swirl of dark colors.","i":"The folded letter or paper in the hand suggests a man of business or correspondence, a common device in American portraits to signal a literate, active person. The De Peyster name is that of one of New York's oldest families, of Dutch colonial origin. By 1828 Waldo was a leading society portraitist who had developed a formula of seated half-length, paper in hand and swirling brown ground that sitters of standing came to expect.","c":"A polished professional product: the color accents of the blue coat and yellow waistcoat are attractive, and the composed face is well handled. But the formula, a seated half-length with a paper in hand and a dark swirling background, reappears throughout Waldo's work. The picture shows what patrons wanted, dignity and fashion, and it is interesting chiefly for its color combination and as a sample of its type, competent and unsurprising."},"met:13094":{"d":"A portrait of Henry La Tourette de Groot, painted between 1825 and 1830 in oil on wood, about 84 by 65 cm. It is a half-length likeness of a New York gentleman, on a standard panel size that Waldo used repeatedly for sitters of the city's mercantile class in these years, the steady commissions on which a busy portrait studio lived.","i":"The double-barreled name joins Dutch and Huguenot families, both long established in New York, and a portrait of this size and format would have recorded a man's place in the city's commercial society. Portraits on panels of matching dimensions were a reliable product of Waldo's studio, made to a regular schedule, and they hung in family houses as records of standing for later generations. The sitter's career is not recorded here.","c":"The standardization is the lesson: Waldo's panel portraits of the 1820s share size and format and were produced with efficiency and care. The picture is not a work of invention, and its interest is as evidence of how a busy portraitist served a clientele that wanted a plain, dignified likeness. It shows professional competence rather than inspiration, which was exactly what his sitters sought."},"cma:125246":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man, dated 1832, in oil on wood, about 91 by 70 cm, whose name is not known. It is a likeness of a man of standing, on a panel larger than Waldo's usual half-length format, made at the height of his career in New York, when he had many such commissions and a reputation for sober, plain likenesses.","i":"By 1832 Waldo was in his late forties and a senior figure among New York portrait painters. Photography had not yet arrived, so painted portraits were the only means of recording a face, and merchants, lawyers and professionals ordered them as a matter of course. The sitter's name has been lost, which turns the picture into a record of a type, an upright citizen of the early Republic, rather than of a person.","c":"Without names or facts, the portrait stands as a competent example of Waldo's mature manner. The larger size suggests a more significant commission, and the modeling may be smoother and more polished than in his earlier panels. It is best seen as an object lesson in how portraiture was made, a steady, professional product, honest rather than imaginative, that gave its sitter the dignity he wanted."},"met:13091":{"d":"A portrait of Edward Kellogg, painted in 1831 to 1832 in oil on wood, about 85 by 65 cm, one of a pair with the portrait of his wife of the same size and date. It is a half-length likeness of a man of standing in a New York of growing commerce, made by one of the city's leading portrait painters.","i":"Matching portraits of husband and wife were the norm for American couples. They hung as pendants, usually with the sitters turned slightly toward each other, and the format reinforced marital partnership and household status. Waldo painted such pairs for many families in these years, and the Kellogg pair, with its matching size and date, is a clear example. The sitter's career is not recorded in the title or the museum's data.","c":"The pair is a good example of Waldo's mature manner in the early 1830s: plain, smooth, honest, with little ornament. Because the husband and wife are painted on matching panels, the comparison shows how a practiced portraitist varied his formula for each sitter. The success lies in consistency and clarity, and this is not a great picture but a good document of the taste and means of New York's professional class."},"met:13097":{"d":"A portrait of Mrs. Edward Kellogg, painted in 1831 to 1832 in oil on wood, about 85 by 65 cm, the companion to the portrait of her husband, matching it in size, date and support. It is a half-length likeness of a married woman of standing; portraits of the period typically show fashionable dress, a showcase for the painter's skill with fabric.","i":"Wives were usually painted as pendants to their husbands, in matching format, and the pair made a statement about family and household. A woman's portrait of the early 1830s typically shows the fashionable dress of the day, with wide sleeves, lace and elaborate hair, in which the sitter's taste and her husband's means were on view. Mrs. Kellogg's own name is not given, since the title identifies her through her husband.","c":"The portrait shares the plain, honest clarity of Waldo's work of these years, and as the companion of her husband's picture it should be read with it. Female portraits of this type depend on the handling of fabric and the composure of the face, and Waldo's competent, smooth modeling suits them. It is an agreeable, unshowy likeness, valuable as part of a pair and as a record of the clothes and manners of the period."},"met:13102":{"d":"A portrait of a woman, dated 1832, in oil on wood, about 84 by 65 cm. The title tentatively links her to the Buloid family, so the sitter's identity is uncertain. It is a half-length likeness of a lady of standing, painted on the panel size Waldo used for his steady stream of New York commissions in the early 1830s.","i":"The identification is only a possibility, since the title records a family connection, not a certain name. Many portraits lost their names when they were separated from family records, and a picture can survive as an anonymous record of a type: a woman of the early 1830s in the fashion of her day. The loss of the name does not remove its value as evidence of the clothes, manners and taste of the period.","c":"Without a name, the portrait must be judged by type. Waldo's female portraits show smooth modeling and a gentle, composed look, with careful attention to the details of dress, lace and ornament. It is likely to be a competent example, and its anonymity is a loss, but it still offers a calm, clear image of a woman as she wished to be seen in the New York of the early 1830s."},"met:13095":{"d":"A large group portrait, about 178 by 146 cm, painted about 1833 to 1834, of the Knapp children. Boys and a dog are noted, so the picture shows young brothers with the family dog, on a canvas large enough for full-length figures. It was an ambitious family commission, well above Waldo's usual half-length formula, and an unusual project in his output of mostly single sitters.","i":"Portraits of siblings with a pet were a favorite type of the early nineteenth century, showing affectionate childhood as a mark of a prosperous, harmonious family. The dog adds loyalty and playfulness. American family portraits shifted from stiff poses toward naturalness, influenced by English models such as the children's portraits of Sir Thomas Lawrence, and the Knapp picture belongs to that taste for an informal, tender view of childhood.","c":"Group portraits of children test a painter's ability to arrange figures and keep each distinct, and Waldo's practiced hand ensures clarity. The full-length scale and the dog give the picture a charm and variety absent from his half-lengths, though the stiffness typical of American portraiture of the period may remain. It is a rare and attractive example of his ambition, and one of the more memorable pictures of his late career."},"met:13101":{"d":"A portrait of a girl with flowers, painted about 1835 in oil on wood, about 104 by 81 cm. A girl and flowers are named among its subjects, so the picture shows a child holding or surrounded by blossoms, on a panel larger than Waldo's usual formats. The sitter is unnamed, and the picture is described by type: a child portrait of the 1830s in which the flowers set off the girl's youth and the painter's color.","i":"Flowers in children's portraits symbolize innocence and beauty and also the brevity of life, since blossoms fade. The portrait may have been commissioned by a family in New York's prosperous circle, and the sitter's name has not been preserved in the record. Romantic child portraits of the mid-1830s favored such natural motifs, in a taste influenced by English painters and by new ideas of childhood as a time of innocence.","c":"At more than a meter tall, the picture is a larger-scale child portrait than most of his, and the flowers give an opportunity for color that his sober adult portraits lack. Waldo had a gift for the simple, unaffected child, and as a type the picture is charming, though it can verge on sentimentality. The anonymity of the sitter is a loss, but the image still carries real appeal."},"nga:160":{"d":"Mary sits on a cushion on the floor, in a marine-blue mantle trimmed with gold and marked with a star, cradling the nursing Child, who reaches for her breast. Two angels, painted entirely in pumpkin orange, flank her; a third orange angel above supports the torso of a bearded man in rose-pink and peacock blue, who raises two fingers in blessing and holds a closed book. At lower right a much smaller woman, wrapped in white from head to foot, kneels with hands pressed together. All stand against a gold ground that rises to a point, above a floor patterned like gold brocade.","i":"In the Madonna of Humility, Mary sits on the ground or on a low cushion instead of a throne, a pose that expresses her humility (the word comes from humus, earth) and was popular in fourteenth-century Italy. Nursing the Child stresses that God became truly human. The bearded figure above is Christ blessing, shown as an adult. The small kneeling woman is the donor who commissioned or owned the panel, and perhaps a nun or widow, to judge by her all-covering dress.","c":"This is a luxurious piece of small-scale Sienese craft: intense color, gold and fine tooling packed into a panel under thirty centimeters high. The orange angels, each painted in a single hue, are a bold decorative choice, and the punched borders show exquisite skill. The Child's adult face on a pudgy body is a medieval convention not yet displaced by naturalism. It is devotion rather than invention, and its beauty lies in its finish."},"nga:161":{"d":"A thin, haggard man is nailed to a wooden cross, his body stretched between wide-spread hands. Blood trickles from his hands, from a gash over the ribs on the right and from the nailed feet, running down the foot of the cross. His head droops to the left, eyes closed under furrowed brows, and blood seeps from the crown of thorns around his coppery-red hair. A sheer white cloth hangs from his hips, and the background is streaked with orange and gold. This is the back of a small panel, about 30 by 19 centimeters, whose front shows the Virgin and Child.","i":"Late medieval devotion dwelt on the physical suffering of Christ, to move the believer to compassion and repentance, and small panels like this were meant to be held close in private prayer. The wound in the side, traditionally made by a soldier's lance, the nails and the crown of thorns are all instruments of the Passion. With the Madonna and Child on the front, the two sides together set the Virgin's tenderness beside her son's sacrifice.","c":"Andrea di Bartolo gives the long-established Sienese formula a stark edge: the stretched body, furrowed brow and thin streams of blood are painted with a restraint that makes them more affecting. The streaks of orange and gold behind add a smoldering heat. It is not an innovative picture, since Andrea worked within a tradition shaped by Simone Martini and the Lorenzetti, but it shows how much feeling a panel thirty centimeters high can hold."},"nga:182":{"d":"A young girl climbs the steps of a domed temple toward a bearded high priest who waits with outstretched hands. She is Mary, in a rose-pink dress patterned with gold flowers, and she looks back over her shoulder at her parents, an older woman in a white veil and an older man with a gray beard, who follow at lower left. She carries a red book and lifts her hem. At right, four girls gather beside an altar bearing a tall gold vessel, and two bearded men point in opposite directions. All stand against a shimmering gold ground; the blue vault is scattered with gold stars.","i":"The scene comes from the apocryphal Gospel of James and the Golden Legend, not from the New Testament: the three-year-old Mary is brought by her parents, Joachim and Anna, to the Temple in Jerusalem, where she climbs the steps and is received by the high priest, to be raised in God's service. Her backward glance is a human touch, a last look at her parents. Closely matched panels of the same size suggest this belonged to a series on her life.","c":"Andrea di Bartolo tells the story clearly and with lyrical delicacy: the diagonal of steps leads the eye to the priest, Mary's backward glance gives the moment its feeling, and the girls waiting at the altar form a pretty, compact group. The gold ground and deep colors give a tapestry-like richness. Its space is that of the old Sienese tradition, built by overlapping and gold ground, not by the new perspective then emerging in Florence."},"nga:183":{"d":"Under an arched structure five women gather around a newborn while two men sit outside the room. On a bed to the right, behind a drawn ruby-red curtain, a haloed woman in a white veil and sky-blue robe reclines on one elbow while an attendant pours water over her hands from a gold pitcher. Two women on the floor hold the infant, haloed and wrapped in white cloth, and a fifth woman enters with a gold dish. At left the two bearded men, one with a halo, talk, one raising a finger. The gold ground is worn in places to show the red beneath.","i":"The apocryphal Gospel of James and the Golden Legend tell how Anna, long childless, bore Mary in old age, a miracle like the births of Isaac, Samuel and John the Baptist. Artists showed the event as a domestic scene: the mother in bed, women washing and swaddling the baby, a servant bringing food. The haloed man at left is presumably Joachim, traditionally shown waiting outside. The picture invites the viewer to see Mary's birth in terms of an Italian household.","c":"The construction of the room is the weak point: as the museum notes, the arched openings do not quite fit together into a cohesive space, and the ruined upper corners add to the oddity. But the figures are neatly grouped, the colors clear, ruby bed curtain, sky blue, topaz, and the gold dishes echo the gold ground. The pouring of water and the baby standing on a lap are pleasing small observations. Charming narrative without convincing space."},"nga:184":{"d":"Under two tall tower-like structures, thirteen men, women and children gather to give or receive food against a shiny gold ground. At left Joachim, with gray hair, a long beard and a gold-trimmed royal-blue robe, holds out a bread roll from a basket on his arm to five poor people in patched robes, among them figures on walking sticks and a kneeling man with a crutch. Beside him Anna, in a white head covering and an emerald-green dress, holds a spouted vessel. At right a bearded priest in a crimson cloak and white cap addresses three boys with tall white sacks.","i":"Joachim and Anna, the future parents of the Virgin Mary, are known from apocryphal texts such as the Golden Legend, which describes them as a devout, wealthy and childless couple who gave generously to the poor and to the Temple. This scene shows their charity before the sorrow of Joachim's rejected offering and the angel's promise of a child. Their halos mark them as saints. The picture presents them as models of almsgiving, a virtue much admired in medieval cities.","c":"The painter manages thirteen figures by grouping them: the beggars at left, the donors in the center, the boys with sacks at right, each set off by a dominant color, royal blue for Joachim, emerald for Anna, crimson for the priest. The patched robes of the poor show a flicker of observation. The two towers joined by a low wall are a symbolic frame, not a convincing building. A gentle narrative miniature more than a dramatic one."},"met:435574":{"d":"A wide panel, about 53 by 98 centimeters, shows the Crucifixion against a gold ground, painted in tempera on wood. The recorded motifs are Christ on the cross, the Virgin Mary, soldiers and other men, horses, angels and mountains, so the scene is a crowded one, with the Virgin, soldiers and horsemen below and angels in the sky. The museum gives no firm date; Andrea di Bartolo's working life ended with his death in 1428.","i":"A Crucifixion crowded with soldiers and horses follows the Gospel accounts of Roman soldiers, onlookers and grieving women, enlarged by the late medieval love of pageantry. The angels signal the cosmic meaning of the event, and Mary's presence recalls her share in her son's suffering. The gold ground lifts the event out of ordinary time and place and makes the panel a luminous object for prayer. Such wide formats suit a crowd of figures.","c":"Andrea di Bartolo carries the elegance of Sienese line into the fifteenth century, and this wide Crucifixion is conservative, with its gold ground, decorative color and conventional gestures of grief. Beside the Florentine work of the 1420s, such as Masaccio's, it looks courtly and old-fashioned. But a crowd of horses, soldiers and angels gives it a pageant-like richness, and the Sienese taste for fine drawing and precious surfaces is on full display."},"nga:43":{"d":"A woman sits outdoors on the ground with a baby in her lap at left, while a young child stands nearby at right. The baby, partly wrapped in white cloth, faces right with arms outstretched. The woman has a serene expression and wears a green and red robe. The standing child, with curly blond hair, holds a small lamb. Two cherubic faces peer down from a cloud at upper left. Behind them is a pastoral setting of trees, shrubs, hills and grass, with rabbits near the forest edge. The canvas is a wide, narrow strip, about 28 by 58 cm.","i":"The infant John the Baptist with a lamb points to Christ as the Lamb of God, while the Virgin seated on the ground, in a pose known as the Madonna of Humility, shows her lowliness before God. The cherubs signal heavenly presence. Rabbits in Venetian pictures have been read as signs of fertility or of the vulnerable soul, though their meaning here is uncertain. Polidoro, a Venetian follower of Titian, painted many such Holy Family pictures in landscape.","c":"The wide, narrow strip format is unusual: a quick, gentle pastoral piece in the Titianesque manner, with soft color and an airy landscape. Polidoro was a talented imitator of Titian rather than an innovator, and the sweet, tender mood and pastoral setting are the picture's charm. Its small size suggests private use. It is a pleasing, decorative devotional picture, more attractive than profound."},"met:10795":{"d":"Maria De Hart Mayo, the wife of General Winfield Scott, is portrayed in an oil painting of about 96 by 69 centimeters dated 1831 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs name the sitter and the portrait. At this scale and in this upright format, likely a three-quarter-length figure, the portrait belongs to the type of a prominent family's formal commission, with the sitter's costume and bearing the main points of interest.","i":"General Winfield Scott (1786-1866), a hero of the War of 1812 who would later command the U.S. Army, and his wife had married in 1817; a portrait of the general's wife in 1831 recorded a family of national standing. Portraits of officers' wives were part of the social display of a rising army family, hung in homes and shown to visitors. Durand, then known chiefly as an engraver, was in these years painting portraits of notable figures.","c":"Durand, still primarily an engraver, brings a draftsman's firmness to the portrait: crisp contours, careful treatment of costume and a clear, undistorted likeness. What is missing is the painterly sensuousness of the English and Boston portraitists; the pose follows convention, and the sitter's personality comes through only modestly. It is a solid, dignified record rather than a revelation."},"met:10785":{"d":"The nude Ariadne reclines in the shade of a tree in this small canvas of about 44 by 49 centimeters, painted around 1831-35 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs are the heroine, nudity and a tree. The composition appears to follow John Vanderlyn's famous Ariadne asleep on the island of Naxos, a large nude that Durand is known to have engraved, and the modest size suits a study or a reduced version of that composition.","i":"Ariadne, the Cretan princess who helped Theseus escape the labyrinth, was left sleeping on the island of Naxos by the hero, who sailed away; the god Bacchus found her and made her his bride. The subject, a sleeping nude in a natural setting, allowed painters to show the female nude under the cover of classical myth, and Vanderlyn's version was among the first nudes of such scale by an American, its public showing provoking mixed reactions.","c":"As a copy, the painting is less about invention than about transmission: Durand, then an engraver at the height of his craft, translates a famous composition into his own cleaner, cooler idiom. The nude is smooth and idealized, in the manner of the Neoclassical tradition, and the landscape setting is minimal. Its significance is cultural rather than aesthetic: an early American glimpse of the classical nude."},"met:10794":{"d":"Luman Reed (1785-1836), the New York merchant and art collector, is portrayed in an oil painting of about 77 by 65 centimeters dated 1835 by Asher B. Durand, a year before the sitter's death. The recorded motifs name only the man and the portrait. Reed was a wholesale grocer who used his fortune to build one of the first important collections of American art, which he opened to the public in a gallery in his house.","i":"Reed was a model of the new American patron: a self-made businessman who bought pictures from living American painters and let the public see them. He commissioned Thomas Cole's Course of Empire series, supported William Sidney Mount and Henry Inman, and encouraged Durand to turn from engraving and portraits to landscape. A portrait of Reed by Durand therefore acknowledges a debt between patron and protégé, and Reed's collection later formed the core of a public gallery in New York.","c":"A portrait of a patron by his protégé is a test of gratitude as much as skill, and if Durand's usual plainness holds here, he offers a straightforward likeness, sober tones and no flattery. He gives Reed the dignity of a self-made man. It is not a showy portrait, but its modest candor suits the sitter and carries the weight of its historical importance: it records the man who helped make American painting a profession."},"met:10791":{"d":"Jonathan Sturges (1802-1874), the New York merchant and patron of the arts, is portrayed in an oil painting of about 78 by 64 centimeters, painted around 1840 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs name only the sitter. Sturges was then in his late thirties, a successful businessman and a friend and patron of the painter, and the half-length format suits a portrait made for his household or for a friend's wall.","i":"Sturges came to New York as a youth, clerked for and then partnered with Luman Reed, and after Reed's death in 1836 became one of the city's leading patrons of American art. He commissioned Durand's Kindred Spirits (1849), the picture of the painter Thomas Cole and the poet William Cullen Bryant in a Catskill glen, as a gift to Bryant. A portrait of Sturges by Durand records a friendship and a patronage that helped shape the Hudson River School.","c":"Durand's portraits of the 1830s and 1840s are plain and truthful, with the clarity of an engraver's drawing, and a portrait of a friend allows him to relax the formulas: the gaze can be direct and the pose easy. The likeness, if typical, is sober and unflattering, a merchant's portrait without fuss. It matters less as art than as the image of a man who helped make Durand's landscape career possible."},"nga:127672":{"d":"A large, dark sailing ship lies stranded near the shore at the left, tilted steeply away from the viewer in choppy waves, two of its tall masts sharply angled and the third broken. The rigging hangs askew and the sails are tattered. In the foreground broken masts, swaths of limp grey sail, thick rope and an overturned barrel are scattered along the beach. Bottle-green waves break gently on the sand, but white foam kicks up around the hull. At the right a pale yellow sun glows hazily over the horizon, lightening dark grey clouds to yellow, lavender and mauve pink.","i":"Shipwreck was a popular subject in Romantic painting, from Turner and Géricault in Europe to American artists, who found in it a vehicle for the sublime: nature's power over human works. Durand is best known for forests and mountains, so a marine subject is unusual; here the aftermath of a storm is shown, as the sun comes out. The scattered debris is a quiet reminder of mortality, and the lightening sky hints at hope.","c":"An unexpected and effective picture from a landscape specialist. The great dark hull set against the glowing, hazy light is bold, the wreckage in the foreground is observed with a surveyor's care, and the weary mood after the storm is more moving than the storm itself would have been. The sea is not Durand's natural element; the waves are less alive than his trees, and the sky's colours are a little theatrical. Still, it is an arresting departure."},"met:10786":{"d":"A stand of tall beech trees rises in the foreground of this large upright canvas, about 153 by 122 centimeters, painted in 1845 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs include a river and a lake, hills and mountains in the distance, clouds, a path and, among the themes, sheep and a shepherd. The tall format, less usual for landscapes of the period, emphasizes the height of the trees, which frame a view into the distance.","i":"The Beeches belongs to the moment when American landscape painters, led by Thomas Cole and Durand, claimed that the native scenery could carry the weight of European art. Durand's love of individual trees, studied from life in the woods, joins the Claudian formula of framing trees and receding distance, and the shepherd and flock add a pastoral note. The beech, with its smooth gray trunk and spreading crown, was admired by his circle as a noble native tree.","c":"The beeches themselves are the triumph: individually observed, with the mass and weight of living trees, set against a luminous, receding vista. The composition is conventional, dark foreground and bright distance, and the shepherd and sheep are small, secondary notes, but the clarity of the light and the careful drawing give it a sincerity that few American landscapes of 1845 match. It exemplifies Durand's belief that nature should be studied before it is composed."},"met:10793":{"d":"A broad landscape of river, hills and mountains under a cloud-streaked sky, with trees and sheep, is the subject of this large canvas, about 100 by 155 centimeters, painted in 1850 by Asher B. Durand. The title ties it to Thanatopsis, William Cullen Bryant's famous poem on death and nature, so the scene is a landscape with a literary program, a meditation as much as a view.","i":"Bryant's Thanatopsis, written when he was young and first published in 1817, tells the reader that nature speaks a various language to those who love her, and that death is a return to the earth and a joining of the great company of the dead. Durand, Bryant's friend, gives that idea a landscape: the vastness of the view and the shifting light suggest the large cycles of nature, against which human life is brief.","c":"The painting is ambitious and uneven: the sweep of space and the gradations of light across mountains and valley are beautifully managed, but the literary program asks the picture to carry more meaning than it can show, and the details may feel scattered in the effort. As a statement of the Hudson River School's belief that landscape can be philosophy it is important; as a picture it is more admirable than affecting."},"met:10799":{"d":"A wide view across a river toward New York City from the New Jersey shore is the subject of this canvas, about 38 by 69 centimeters, dated around 1850. The museum lists it as formerly attributed to Asher B. Durand, so the painter is not securely known. The recorded motifs are a river and a landscape, and the title places the viewer on the opposite bank, looking across the Hudson at Manhattan, a favorite vantage for artists and travelers.","i":"Around 1850 New York was growing fast, with a population approaching half a million, and a view of the city from across the river showed it as a low skyline of roofs and church spires, ferries and sailing vessels, still modest beside the natural scenery of the river. Trinity Church, completed in 1846, was the tallest structure of that skyline. Such views were bought by New Yorkers proud of their city and by visitors wanting a souvenir of its harbor.","c":"If the attribution to Durand has been withdrawn, as the museum's wording suggests, this is a topographical view by an unknown hand and should be judged as one: the pleasure of place, the river and the distant city, set down with plain competence. It lacks the sustained observation of rock and foliage that marks Durand's own landscapes, and its value lies in its record of the city's setting more than in its artistry."},"met:10788":{"d":"A river winds through a valley beneath a high, rounded mountain in the Shandaken range of the Catskills in this canvas of about 83 by 122 centimeters, painted in 1853 by Asher B. Durand. Small figures, a man fishing, a woman and cows, animate the foreground, and clouds and sky occupy the upper half of a broad, sunlit panorama of the Catskill country that Hudson River painters loved.","i":"The Catskills were the cradle of the Hudson River School: Thomas Cole made them famous in the 1820s and 1830s, and Durand, his friend and later successor as the school's leader, returned to the region for summers of sketching. Landscapes like this combine a particular place with a mood of harmony between people and nature: the angler and the herd are at home in the valley. Such views appealed to city buyers who could look at them as a window into the mountains.","c":"Durand's tonal control is typically fine here: the pale blue of the distant ridge, the gradation of light across the valley and the clean delineation of the foreground trees. The figures are small and keep their place. Compared with Frederic Church's more dramatic spectacle, Durand's calm is quiet, even prosaic, but the sincerity of the observation carries conviction. It is a fine example of his mature manner, spacious and unemphatic."},"nga:34050":{"d":"A man is shown seated, from the head to just below the waist, angled slightly to the right and looking directly at the viewer with a stern expression, his brow prominent and his eyes deep-set. His light brown hair curls a little below the ears. He wears a dark coat, a cream waistcoat, a white shirt and a black cravat, with a gold chain hanging from a waistcoat pocket. His hands rest in his lap and one holds a pair of spectacles. The background is dark and muted, setting off the warm tones of the chair and his clothing.","i":"Gouverneur Kemble (1786–1875) was a New York politician and manufacturer, founder of the West Point Foundry at Cold Spring on the Hudson and a friend of Washington Irving. Durand, the engraver who became a leading landscape painter and was president of the National Academy of Design, also painted portraits for patrons in his circle. The spectacles in the sitter's hand mark a reader and man of affairs. Kemble was in his late sixties when this portrait was painted.","c":"A forthright portrait in the plain American tradition: the stern brow, deep-set eyes and spectacles in hand give the sitter a convincing dignity, and the warm chair offsets the dark coat. Durand was a better painter of forests than faces, and the modelling is straightforward, the pose conventional and the hands a little stiff. It does what a portrait of an eminent man was asked to do, honestly, without the flourish of his best landscapes."},"met:10796":{"d":"A river scene with cattle, a path, rocks, trees, hills and distant mountains under a cloudy sky fills this canvas of about 61 by 87 centimeters, painted in 1854 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs list cattle, a path and rocks, a pastoral staple, and the title gives the river as the unifying element. The size is that of a cabinet picture for a domestic wall.","i":"In the 1850s American landscape painters such as Durand and Kensett sold pictures of this size to the new class of urban buyers who wanted a view of untroubled nature for the parlor: a river, a few cows, a path leading the eye away to blue hills. Durand's own writings insisted that landscape should be a faithful study of nature, but he also believed in arranging the parts into a harmonious whole. The result is an ideal rural America, calm and productive.","c":"The picture is quietly assured: rocks and cattle give the foreground weight, the path leads the eye into a softly lit middle ground and the distant hills dissolve in atmosphere. What is lacking is surprise, since the structure is one Durand had used for years, and the cows are placed as decoration. But the unhurried sincerity of the handling is its own pleasure, and it shows the standard of competence the school had reached by mid-century."},"met:10790":{"d":"Tall trees fill this large upright canvas, about 154 by 122 centimeters, painted in 1855 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs are the forest, sky, clouds and trees: a deep woodland interior in which no figure or building distracts from the trunks, leaves and light. The vertical format and scale draw the viewer into the woods as if standing among the trees, and the size is that of an exhibition picture rather than a parlor painting.","i":"In his Letters on Landscape Painting (1855), Durand urged young painters to go into the woods with a sketchbook and paint trees, rocks and light as they really are, and In the Woods is the large picture that puts that teaching into practice, drawn from his outdoor studies. Forest interiors had a spiritual meaning for Durand and his friends: Bryant's poem A Forest Hymn begins with the idea that the groves were God's first temples.","c":"Durand's forest interior is a landmark of American landscape painting: the leaf-lit space, the careful differences of bark and moss, the sense of trees as individuals and a light that falls through the canopy without sentimentality are the work of a patient observer. Its limit is the effect of effort: some passages feel over-labored, and the whole is slightly sober for its size. Yet it is the clearest statement of the principles he preached."},"nga:56571":{"d":"Cool light filters through a thick, verdant forest in this vertical landscape. Most of the trees have high canopies in celery, matcha and olive greens. Moss carpets the trunks of five trees that loom across a narrow brook, and dead sienna-brown limbs with sharp, broken branches lie scattered in the undergrowth on the forest floor. The trees and undergrowth recede into the distance, where a gap reveals a glimpse of a blue-grey mountain silhouetted against a milky sky. The artist signed the lower right with his initials, ABD.","i":"In 1855 Durand published his Letters on Landscape Painting, urging young artists to study nature directly, leaf, bark and rock, before composing pictures. A forest interior like this, with its mossy trunks, deadfall and brook, puts that doctrine into practice, and its view to the mountain beyond keeps the Hudson River School's interest in grander vistas. The title records the morning, whose cool light gives the forest its freshness.","c":"One of Durand's most convincing woodland pictures. The cool light, the mossy bark and the tangle of dead branches are observed with the botanical honesty he preached, and the distant mountain gives the dense forest a place to breathe. The meticulous detail sometimes outruns the design, so that the eye jumps from branch to branch. Still, for American painting of the 1850s, this devotion to the real forest was a considerable achievement."},"nga:56572":{"d":"A placid river winds past low hills and fields blanketed with trees towards slate-grey mountains on the horizon, in a hazy horizontal landscape. The river curves in a tight backward C from the lower left corner. On the right a gently sloping grassy hill runs down to the water, where four cows graze in sunshine, while the rest of that half lies in deep shade under towering trees. Logs, moss-covered boulders, stones and plants line the bottom edge. Golden light works across the treetops in the middle distance, and the sky deepens from pale yellow to pale blue.","i":"Pastoral scenes of cattle beside quiet water belonged to the long tradition of idealised landscape that stretched from Claude Lorrain to the Hudson River School, in which settled, productive countryside suggested harmony between people and nature. The cows are not a story but a measure of calm. Durand's contemporaries admired American landscape for its blend of wildness and cultivation; here the balance falls on the peaceful side, with the shaded trees framing the view and the golden middle distance as its reward.","c":"A calm, well-composed landscape in the classical manner. The dark mass of trees on the right is set against the sunlit meadow and cows, and the golden light in the distance gives the scene depth and tenderness. As a design it is rather formulaic, and the cows are placed with more care than life. It lacks the grandeur of Durand's mountain scenes, but it is an assured example of his gentler pastoral vein."},"nga:72881":{"d":"A green meadow slopes down towards a shimmering river, with a grove of tall, moss-green trees at the left shading a winding dirt path that leads to a white-walled, grey-roofed building with a tower, perhaps a church. Five brown and white cows gather at the sandy shore at lower centre, and two more stand nearer the viewer. The water reflects the pale blue sky and the soft green hill opposite, and winds under an arched bridge into a hazy distance dotted with tiny buildings and spires and closed by mountains. A few boulders and fallen trunks line the bottom edge.","i":"Painted in 1861, the year the Civil War began, this serene picture of farmland, church and river presents an America of order and harmony. Durand, then in his mid-sixties and the elder statesman of the Hudson River School, believed that nature studied honestly revealed a moral order. The church tower, cattle and bridge speak of settled community, a theme that may have appealed to viewers anxious about national division.","c":"Large, calm and carefully put together: the dark trees at left frame the view, the cattle lead the eye to the water, and the graduated blues of the distant hills show Durand's mastery of atmospheric depth. It is also somewhat inert, since the composition follows a formula familiar from Claude, the colour is subdued, and no one thing seizes the eye. Impressive in scale and craft, it moves mostly as a statement of faith in an orderly landscape."},"met:10798":{"d":"Cows stand by a pond or river under trees in this canvas of about 57 by 89 centimeters, painted in 1865 by Asher B. Durand. The recorded motifs include a river or lake, clouds, trees, the sun and the summer afternoon of the title, so the picture is a study of a particular time of day and season, with the sun's light spreading over a quiet pastoral scene.","i":"In the year the Civil War ended, the picture offers a quiet pastoral at a time when the country had been in turmoil, and its calm may have been a comfort to buyers worn down by news of the war. Durand, then almost seventy, had spent decades studying the effects of light and atmosphere, and a summer afternoon, with its warm, slow light, was ideal. The cows at the pond, familiar from Dutch and Claudian traditions, are an emblem of peaceful abundance.","c":"The picture's glory is the afternoon light: the warm glow in the sky and over the water, the long shadows beneath the trees and the cows set in them with just enough detail. It is a work of old age, perhaps loosened somewhat from the fine detail of the 1850s and more concerned with atmosphere than with specifics. A calm, gently luminous picture rather than a grand one, it shows a veteran painter's confidence."},"met:437020":{"d":"A small gold-ground panel, about 46 by 34 cm, painted between 1370 and 1375, showing Christ on the cross. The Virgin and Saint John the Evangelist are recorded as mourners, and the saints Francis and Anthony are also included, so the Crucifixion is joined by two saints in a devotional composition. The Master of the Orcagnesque Misericordia, the modern name for an anonymous Florentine follower of Orcagna, painted it in tempera on wood.","i":"The pairing of Francis and Anthony suggests a Franciscan context: Francis, founder of the Franciscans, received the stigmata in a vision of the crucified Christ, so his presence beside the cross fits his devotion to the Passion. Anthony may be the hermit Anthony Abbot, as the records suggest. The painter is anonymous, named by modern scholars for his style, which followed Orcagna, the leading Florentine painter and sculptor of the generation after the Black Death.","c":"A small panel by a follower of Orcagna, in which hieratic, frontal figures and a gold ground show a conservative taste in Florence after the plague. The drawing is firm and the emotion restrained, and its interest is as a pious object and a document of Florentine taste, with Franciscan devotion visible in the choice of saints. It is not a major work, but a good, representative one that shows how an anonymous workshop served its patrons."},"met:437021":{"d":"A small gold-ground fragment, about 24 by 16 cm, showing the head of Christ, with an angel also recorded, painted in tempera on wood in the late fourteenth century. Because it is a fragment, the larger composition it belonged to is not recorded here. The type is a devotional head of Christ, a close, frontal image of the sacred face, in the Florentine manner of the followers of Orcagna.","i":"Heads of Christ were popular images of devotion, whether a Veronica or a Christ of Sorrows, and being a fragment, its original context may be lost. It may come from a crucifixion, a Man of Sorrows or a lunette, and the presence of an angel indicates heavenly attendants. The painter is the same anonymous follower of Orcagna who made the Crucifix in the Metropolitan Museum, working in Florence in the decades after the Black Death.","c":"Fragments distill: the face of Christ is the whole picture, and the gold, the intense stare and the linear drawing carry the effect. It is affecting precisely because the context is lost, though the loss of the rest limits what can be judged. As a small, haunting remnant of Florentine devotional painting it is valuable, and it shows how much feeling a few lines and a gold ground could convey."},"nga:46684":{"d":"Shown from about the knees up, a woman with pale, pink skin and cinnamon-brown hair sits with her hands in her lap, gazing off to the right in a vertical portrait. Her hair is parted in the middle and tied at the nape with a purple ribbon. She wears a long-sleeved periwinkle-purple dress with five black buttons, black lace at the cuffs and shoulders, a black bow at the collar and a wide black belt with a gold buckle, and holds a black lace shawl closed. Gold hoop earrings and rings, an emerald-green chair and a stone gray and green ground complete it. Signed and dated 1865.","i":"Painted when Renoir was twenty-four, the portrait predates the Impressionist exhibitions by almost a decade. At this date he was a young painter who had trained in Gleyre's studio with Monet, Sisley and Bazille and was learning from Courbet and Manet. The sitter, Mademoiselle Sicot, is known only by her name in the title, and her connection to the painter is not recorded here. The signature and date along the skirt mark a considered work.","c":"The dark, rich color scheme, a periwinkle-purple dress with black lace against stone gray and shamrock green, and the loose brushwork in the dress and background show an assured young painter. The sitter's gaze off to the side gives a thoughtful mood, and the portrait looks forward to Renoir's later tenderness for women's faces while being more sober and solid than his later work. It is one of the more accomplished of his early portraits."},"nga:52210":{"d":"A small canvas, about 25 by 16 cm, of a woman standing and facing right, her left arm resting on a wooden post or walking stick. She wears a long, light dress with a light blue skirt and holds in her right hand an object with a red ribbon, perhaps a white parasol. Behind her, tree branches with green leaves hang at the left, and at the right an indistinct light blue and beige area suggests fencing or wooden posts. The brushstrokes are thick and visible, with few fine details.","i":"Renoir was twenty-five in 1866, poor and ambitious, and small canvases like this were cheap to paint and easy to work on outdoors. The parasol and the fashionable dress mark a woman at leisure, a subject that painters of modern life were taking up in the 1860s. The model is not named, and the picture is best read as a study of a pose and a fall of light rather than a portrait, with the fence adding a touch of rural setting.","c":"What succeeds is the immediacy: a standing figure suggested in a few thick strokes, the parasol with its red ribbon the one sharp accent. Details are minimal, and the pose, leaning on a post, is natural and unforced. The picture is slight and was probably a private study, but it shows a young painter learning how little drawing a figure needs when color and touch carry the effect."},"nga:52211":{"d":"A small canvas, about 25 by 16 cm, of a woman standing with her left arm raised to her chest. She has dark hair in an updo with a white accessory, a black necklace and a dress in shades of blue, white and tan. Her face is partly in shadow, and no distinct features are visible. The background is green with a hint of blue at the top left, and the beige of the bare canvas shows around the edges. The brushstrokes are thick and visible, giving the scene a blurred effect and a sense of motion.","i":"The bare canvas showing around the edges suggests a sketch left unfinished, and such unfinished studies are valuable for what they reveal of Renoir's method: he blocked in a figure and its setting together, in thick strokes, without a detailed drawing underneath. The shadowed face makes the woman anonymous, and nothing is recorded of who she was. The picture belongs to a group of small standing figures he painted in 1866.","c":"The shadowed face is the interesting decision: it turns the woman into a shape of dark hair, white accent and blue dress, and a sense of motion arises from the thick, quick strokes. She has no features, which keeps the picture from sentiment but also from personality. As a fragment of a young painter's practice it is more suggestive than complete, and it is more successful as a color idea than as a likeness."},"nga:52212":{"d":"A small canvas, about 26 by 16 cm, of a woman standing and facing right, her head angled downward. She wears a long, pale blue-gray dress with wide sleeves trimmed with black stripes, and may wear a black hat with a gray ruffle and red bracelets, though these details are unclear. One hand is raised to her forehead, the other rests by her waist. She is painted with thick brushstrokes and minimal detail, and behind her are a green garden or park, dark green plants and a pale blue-gray sky, painted in the same blurred strokes.","i":"The raised hand, perhaps shading her eyes from the sun, gives the figure a gesture of everyday life rather than a formal pose. Modern women outdoors in fashionable dress were a subject that Renoir's friend Monet was also exploring in Women in the Garden of 1866 and 1867, and the two painters had been close since their student days in Gleyre's studio. The sitter is unnamed, and no story is recorded.","c":"The figure and the background are painted in the same blurred strokes, which makes her seem part of the garden rather than set against it, an idea that would become central to Impressionism. The hand to the forehead is a nicely observed, unposed touch. The picture is small and tentative, and the details of hat and bracelets are hard to read, but it records a young painter's early sense of how light unites figure and setting."},"nga:61382":{"d":"A loose arrangement of colorful wildflowers bursts out of a red clay jug near the back left corner of a tabletop and fills the top two-thirds of a vertical canvas. Soft light from the lower left picks out tight clusters of silvery gray and white blooms along the center, surrounded by lemon-yellow flowers on tall stems, with poppy-red and cornflower-blue flowers at the upper right and lower left. A white daisy unfurls near the jug's handle, and small blue daubs mark flowers at lower right. The table is thinly painted and the background is mottled sage green and steel gray.","i":"Wildflowers in a plain clay jug, rather than the cultivated, exotic bouquets of Dutch still life, reflect the Realist and early Impressionist preference for ordinary things. The poppy, cornflower and daisy are field flowers, and the jug is a country object. Renoir's early still lifes show his debt to Courbet, Delacroix and Fantin-Latour. Whether the arrangement carries any symbolic meaning is not recorded, and it seems to have been chosen for color and informality.","c":"Vigorous, loose and colorful: the burst of flowers fills the canvas, the red clay jug anchors it and the accents of poppy red and cornflower blue punctuate the whole. The table and background are thin and unemphatic, which keeps attention on the blooms. Renoir's early flower piece is less polished than Fantin's but more alive, and it shows already the delight in color that he would carry through every subject."},"nga:46680":{"d":"A pale, nearly nude woman with dark hair pinned up sits on a mossy gray boulder, a bow held upright in both hands, looking down. Only a light green cloth and a rose-pink ribbon cover her hips. A quiver of arrows lies beside her. At her feet a dead deer lies with its head thrown back, blood at its mouth and an arrow in its neck. Behind her are more boulders, a small patch of blue sky and the yellowing leaves of birch trees. The skin is smoothly painted, while the moss and rocks are built up in thick, rough strokes, some with a palette knife. The painting is signed and dated 1867 at lower right.","i":"Renoir was twenty-six when he painted Diana, the Roman goddess of the hunt, for the Salon of 1867. The jury rejected it. The figure is a modern studio model rather than a classical goddess, usually said to be Lise Tréhot, his companion at the time, though some scholars doubt it. The heavy modeling, the blood on the deer and the palette-knife work show how much he owed to Courbet, whom he admired. Chester Dale bought the painting from the dealer Étienne Bignou in 1933 and bequeathed it to the National Gallery in 1963.","c":"Too large for what it has to say, and too indebted to Courbet. The goddess is a studio model on a rock, and the dead deer, with its streak of blood, carries more feeling than she does. Still, moss, fur and skin each have their own surface, and the greens and pinks are fresher than Courbet's browns. Nothing about the figure is divine. It is an early sign that Renoir's gift for luminous flesh would outgrow this Realist heaviness."},"nga:46682":{"d":"A woman in a sheer white blouse, a short red and gold vest and full, patterned pants cinched below the knee lies back, looking at the viewer with hooded eyes, in a wide, horizontal canvas. She leans against an oversized white pillow, propped on one arm, her black hair in a thick braid, and wears gold hoop earrings, dark beads and a cap of gold disks with red fringe. Her teal slippers have pompoms. Beside her on a red and gold cushion sit a tray of oranges and an emerald jug, with a rug and a red sofa beyond. Signed and dated 1870.","i":"The odalisque, originally a female attendant in a harem, became in nineteenth-century French painting a favorite pretext for the erotic nude or semi-nude, in the Orientalist tradition of Ingres and Delacroix. Renoir's picture, inspired by Delacroix's Women of Algiers, is a young painter's homage to a master he admired. Orientalist pictures offered an imagined East to Western viewers, a fantasy that modern viewers understand critically; Renoir himself did not visit Algeria until 1881.","c":"The picture is an exercise in ornamental color: sheer white against red and gold, the patterned pants, the turquoise rug. The tilted pose and heavy-lidded gaze give a languid effect, more sensuous than dramatic compared with Delacroix. The sitter's frank look and the cluttered studio props make the fantasy visible as staged. It is a confident early picture, though more studio costume piece than evocation of any real place."},"nga:52201":{"d":"The head and floppy ears of a dog with a delicate muzzle are loosely painted in a vertical canvas. The head is cocked to the left, and the dog looks at the viewer with large, glistening black eyes. Its fur is mottled caramel brown, black and dark gray, with a white blaze on the forehead and around the mouth. A silver bell glints on a black collar. Below the collar the body fades into strokes of oyster white and a few areas of shale blue that also fill the background. Signed Renoir at lower left.","i":"A dog's head is a small, affectionate subject, and Renoir, who liked animals, painted pets as readily as people. No owner or occasion is recorded, so the picture is best read as a study of character and light. The cocked head and the glistening eyes make the dog seem attentive and alive, and the bell and collar mark it as a household companion rather than a hunter's dog or a stray.","c":"A swift, warm study: the glistening eyes and cocked head give character, and the body dissolves into pale ground so that attention stays on the face. It is less ambitious than his Salon pictures, but possibly more spontaneous, and it shows Renoir's affection for the living things around him. Dog portraits were usually the work of sporting painters, but here a pet becomes a personality, with a directness that needs no sentiment."},"met:437424":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Madame Édouard Bernier, born Marie-Octavie-Stéphanie Laurens (1838-1920), is portrayed in an oil painting of about 78 by 62 centimeters dated 1871, when she was about thirty-three and Renoir thirty. The recorded motifs name only the woman and the portrait. It is an early portrait from the years before the first Impressionist exhibition of 1874, in a half-length format that probably answers a private family commission.","i":"In 1871 Renoir was a young painter trying to make a living: he had exhibited at the Salon in the 1860s, served in the army during the Franco-Prussian War and returned to a Paris shaken by the Commune. Portrait commissions were his bread and butter, and portraits of middle-class sitters like Madame Bernier paid for his experiments with light and color. The sitter is identified by her husband's name and her maiden names, the usual form for a married Frenchwoman.","c":"Renoir's early portraits show him balancing the solid modeling he admired in Courbet and Corot with the brighter color and softer touch that would become his own. A portrait like this is a straightforward, honest likeness of a respectable sitter; it lacks the shimmer of his later work, but its directness has a charm. It is an early, good-natured work, from before his famous style appeared."},"nga:52202":{"d":"Seen from above, as though from a window, a broad bridge slopes toward the viewer in bright sun, its pale roadway crowded with small figures: women with parasols, men in dark coats and top hats, children, a woman selling flowers, and horse-drawn carriages moving in both directions. Black gas lamps line the parapet. At the left, rows of tall cream and tan buildings with chimneys face the river; at the right a boat flying a tricolour flag is moored, and across the water an equestrian statue stands on a tall pedestal. The sky is bright blue, with puffy white clouds touched with grey and mauve. Figures are quick dabs of colour, the buildings broad, flat strokes, and the signature and date, 72, are at lower left.","i":"Painted in the spring of 1872, soon after the Franco-Prussian War and the Paris Commune, the picture shows the city back at its ordinary pace. Renoir worked from the upper window of a café; the story goes that his brother Edmond stopped passers-by to delay them so that Renoir could paint them. The bridge, in spite of its name, is the oldest standing crossing of the Seine in Paris, finished in 1607 under Henri IV, whose statue appears at the right. The painting came two years before the first Impressionist exhibition of 1874 and already has the group's subjects and methods: the modern city, open-air light and broken brushwork.","c":"At its best this is a piece of weather. The sky and the bleached roadway are brushed with a confident lightness, and the crowd breaks into dabs of colour that stay legible as people. Renoir is more interested in the human traffic than in atmosphere, so the figures feel like a lively chorus with no leading voice. That is the nature of a bridge view, but it leaves the picture nearer to a brilliant sketch than to the finished scenes of the mid-1870s, such as the Moulin de la Galette, where the crowd gains weight and individuality."},"nga:66433":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a man sits facing the viewer on the far side of a ledge, looking down at an open book, in a loosely painted vertical portrait. His skin is flushed pink, and he leans both forearms on the ledge, shoulders hunched, brows gathered as he reads. He has a round-tipped nose and a full, bushy beard, and his wavy black hair and beard have bronze at the temples. He holds a white pipe to his lips, with smoke curling from it, and wears a black coat. The ledge is celery green and ochre, and the background is loose cobalt and charcoal. Signed A. Renoir.","i":"Renoir and Monet had met in Gleyre's studio in the early 1860s and became close friends and colleagues; in 1869 they painted side by side at La Grenouillère, where Impressionist technique took shape. In 1872 Monet was living at Argenteuil, and this portrait records the friendship two years before they exhibited together in the first Impressionist exhibition of 1874. Renoir painted Monet more than once, reading indoors and at work in the garden.","c":"A friendly, loose portrait, quick and informal: the swirling brushwork and the cool bluish ground give it energy, and Monet's concentration and pipe smoke give life. The ledge is a nice device, putting a friend at rest behind a barrier. It lacks polish but has presence, and the swirling black form in the lower corner remains a puzzle, though it belongs to the spontaneous method. It is among the best portraits of the Impressionist circle."},"nga:1211":{"d":"A girl in an arctic-blue ballet costume stands looking at the viewer and almost fills a vertical canvas, painted with blended strokes that give it a soft, almost blurry look. She stands in pale pink slippers in fifth position, her body facing left and her head turned, with blue eyes, rosy cheeks and long honey-brown hair tied with a sky-blue ribbon. Her bodice has a lapis-blue bow, and her knee-length tutu seems to fly up behind her. A black choker and bracelets, a white handkerchief and a green background complete the picture. Signed and dated 1874.","i":"Degas had made dancers a subject for the avant-garde, but Renoir's dancer is sweeter and less documentary: not backstage reality but a presentation of youth and grace. The Opéra's young dancers were often girls from poor families, and the picture shows a girl who could be one of them, or a model dressed for the part. The picture dates from 1874, the year of the first Impressionist exhibition, in which it was shown.","c":"Not among Renoir's most daring pictures, but one of his most charming figure pieces from the Impressionists' debut year: the froth of the tutu against the greens is delightful, and the blended strokes give the whole atmosphere. The frontal stance is a little stiff, and the anatomy simple, and the sentimental sweetness hints at the later Renoir. It is a pleasing, accomplished picture, valued for color and tenderness more than for psychological depth."},"nga:52203":{"d":"Sailboats line a grassy riverbank in a loosely painted horizontal landscape. Vivid blue water stretches before the viewer to meet a green bank along the left third of the horizon, which sits less than halfway up. About a dozen people in the grass are painted with a few strokes each of white or dark blue. One sailboat dominates the scene just right of center, and about half a dozen more are spaced farther back. A flagpole rises along the left edge. The water and sky are thinly painted, and the tan texture of the canvas shows through, especially in the sky.","i":"Argenteuil, on the Seine northwest of Paris, was the center of Impressionist landscape in the early 1870s, with Monet, Renoir, Sisley and Manet all working there, and sailing regattas were a popular amusement for Parisians. In 1874 Renoir and Monet worked side by side at Argenteuil, and each painted views of the regatta. The picture records a day of suburban leisure, and the crowd on the bank is reduced to flicks of paint.","c":"A sketch of unusual lightness, with thinly painted water and sky that leave the canvas visible and figures that are mere strokes. It shows Renoir's quickness and his talent for color, with the vivid blue water as the main note. He is not a great landscapist in the manner of Monet, whose analytic fidelity he did not share, but the ease and warmth of the sketch have their own appeal. A fresh and lively record of a summer afternoon."},"nga:52204":{"d":"A woman and a young boy sit together on a grassy lawn with a chicken standing nearby, seen from slightly above, in a loosely painted horizontal canvas. The woman leans forward with her chin in one hand and her legs stretched out, her chestnut hair under a white bonnet topped with a pink flower, in a white dress with a black ribbon at her neck, holding an unfolded fan. The boy leans against her in a straw hat and baby-blue suit, looking out. A flower bed runs along the upper left, and a chicken with a red comb looks at the pair.","i":"The sitters are Camille Doncieux, Monet's first wife, and their son Jean, painted in 1874 in the garden of Monet's house at Argenteuil, where Renoir visited and painted the family. Camille, who appears in many of Monet's paintings, died in 1879 at thirty-two. The picture records a moment of the Monet household in the summer of 1874, shortly after the first Impressionist exhibition, when the friends worked side by side.","c":"The soft, informal, quickly painted portrait of a family at ease in the garden captures an unposed intimacy: the mother's leaning chin, the boy's closeness, the chicken's curiosity. It is typical of Renoir's tenderness, with loose brushwork and a relaxed composition. The picture shows how the Impressionists painted each other's families, an engaging, lightly built work, more charming than weighty, which gains from the knowledge of who the sitters were."},"met:437436":{"d":"A road runs between trees past a small figure in this oil of about 38 by 46 centimeters, painted around 1870 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are the trees, the road, a human figure and the landscape. The village of Louveciennes, west of Paris, was a haunt of the circle of painters who would soon be called Impressionists, who painted its roads and gardens in the open air.","i":"Around 1870 Renoir, Monet and Pissarro painted outdoors in the villages along the Seine west of Paris, working directly in front of the motif with quick brushwork and bright color to catch changing light. A road with trees and a single figure was a modest subject, deliberately chosen: the new painters wanted to show the ordinary landscape of modern life, not the heroic or classical scene. Louveciennes, where Pissarro lived at this time, became one of the cradles of the movement.","c":"Renoir is better known for figures than landscape, but this small picture shows his sensitivity to dappled light: the road and trees are built from soft, broken touches of green, brown and cream, and the single figure adds scale without anecdote. It lacks the ambition of Monet's contemporary river scenes, and the structure is loose. But it has the freshness of early Impressionism's first outdoor experiments."},"nga:37637":{"d":"A young woman in a white short-sleeved blouse sits in an armchair, hugging a striped tabby cat upright against her shoulder so that its head rests against her cheek. Her light brown hair is piled on top of her head, her cheeks are rosy, and she looks down at the cat with a faint smile. She supports it with one bare arm, and an emerald ring shows on her finger. The cat's paws hang loose and its eyes are half closed. Behind them is a turquoise-green wall with a yellow floral curtain at the left edge, and at the right a patch of red, blue and green strokes that may be flowers or a patterned cushion. The brushwork is soft and feathery.","i":"The sitter is not identified, and the picture is best read as a figure study of a type, a young Parisian woman at ease at home, rather than a commissioned portrait. It dates from about 1875, when Renoir was exhibiting with the Impressionists and painting intimate scenes of modern life that private buyers could hang in their own rooms. Cats appear repeatedly in his work and in that of his friends, and the historian James Rubin has studied them as part of Impressionist painting in his book Impressionist Cats and Dogs. The National Gallery of Art received the picture in 1950 as a gift from Mr. and Mrs. Benjamin E. Levy.","c":"This is a minor Renoir, but a charming one. The weight of the cat in the woman's arms is convincingly felt, and the soft, blended handling of her skin contrasts well with the quick, broken strokes of the fur. Elsewhere the picture is vague: the face is generic, the hands are indistinct, and the flowers at the right dissolve into spots of colour. It is less searching than his best portraits of named sitters, yet it shows how easily he could make flesh and fur glow with a few warm tones."},"nga:52205":{"d":"A man and a woman, both dressed in navy blue, stand looking at each other, surrounded by blooming flowers and lush green trees in a square canvas. The palette is dominated by vibrant green and blue, loosely painted with daubs and short strokes so that details are indistinct. The man, in a vest, trousers and a butterscotch-gold hat, bends forward and gestures toward the flowers, while the woman, in a long gown and cherry-red hat, turns her head to look at him. Flowering bushes in rose, cream, yellow and azure span the foreground, and pale pink clouds swirl through a blue sky. Signed Renoir.","i":"A couple in a garden of flowers is a standard subject in French art for courtship: the man gestures toward the flowers, perhaps offering them, and the woman looks at him, in a quiet flirtation. Renoir loved women and flowers, and garden pictures of 1875 reflect the suburban leisure the Impressionists shared. The couple is unidentified, and the picture is best read as an idyll of modern life rather than a portrait.","c":"The dissolving of the figures into the blossoms is the picture's idea: the couple, dressed in similar navy blue, merge with the greens and blues, and the surface of daubs makes the whole shimmer. The narrative is slight, and some may find the structure loose, but as an Impressionist idyll it is typical and delightful. The color is the point, and the picture carries a gentle charm that needs no explanation."},"nga:52209":{"d":"A horizontal landscape painted with broad, visible strokes in shades of laurel, fern and pine green, dark yellow and ash brown. A winding dirt path leads from the bottom center toward the horizon, which comes just over halfway up. Thick, loosely painted foliage dominates the left half, and a tall, slender tree trunk is cut off by the frame near the right edge. At the center, farther down the path, a small person is painted with a few swipes of coffee brown. Patches of lighter green and yellow suggest sun filtering through the trees, and swirling white and pale blue clouds fill the sky. Signed renoir.","i":"The title names a break in the weather, a moment between storms when sun filters through the trees, and Renoir shared with Monet at Argenteuil an interest in changing weather and its effect on landscape. Though primarily a figure painter, Renoir produced landscapes with a flair that shows in such small panels. The small figure on the path gives scale and a touch of life, and the title fixes the transitory effect.","c":"The echo between the swirling sky and the moving foliage is a good idea: weather treated as a unified motion running through both earth and air. The panel is small and rapid, and the single figure is a speck. The best Renoir landscapes show warmth and fluidity rather than Monet's analytic fidelity, and this one is more atmospheric than structural, a fresh, fluent small picture that rewards a brief, attentive look."},"met:438010":{"d":"A young woman is portrayed in a garden in this canvas of about 62 by 51 centimeters, painted in 1876 by Auguste Renoir. The title names her as Nini Lopez, a model who posed for several of Renoir's works of the mid-1870s, and the recorded motifs are the woman, the garden and the portrait. The setting is probably the garden of the house Renoir rented in Montmartre, where he painted in the open air.","i":"Nini Lopez, a young Montmartre woman, was one of Renoir's favorite models, and her face appears in works such as La Loge. In 1876, the year of the second Impressionist exhibition, Renoir painted figures in sunlight, with dappled light and shadow on faces and clothing, an effect that some critics ridiculed. The garden setting allowed him to paint a model out of doors, treating the figure as a subject for light as much as for likeness.","c":"The picture is a pleasing example of Renoir's mid-1870s style: soft, feathery brushwork, a face bathed in light and a garden dissolved into touches of green and white. The likeness is charming rather than searching: Renoir paints the pleasure of a young woman in the open air, not her personality. Compared with the solid figures of his 1860s it is looser and sweeter, and the sweetness is already beginning to creep in."},"nga:46090":{"d":"A young woman is shown from the hips up, seated, her body angled to the left while her face turns to meet the viewer with a gentle smile. She wears a low-cut, sleeveless white dress with a pale blue band at the throat and a small sprig of flowers at the bodice. Her honey-blond hair is swept up, with a few curls loose at the forehead and nape, and her hands rest in her lap under a white cloth. Pearl white, cool green and muted blue strokes blend into a soft haze, and the powder-blue and sage background lets bare canvas show at the left edge.","i":"The sitter is Henriette Henriot, an actress and model who posed for Renoir several times in the mid-1870s; 'Madame' is the title the picture has long carried. This is not a commissioned society portrait but a likeness of a young woman from his own circle, of the kind he showed at the Impressionist exhibitions of the 1870s and sold to a small group of collectors. Such pictures are usually read as portrait and fancy picture at once: a real face turned into an image of youth and charm.","c":"In 1876 Renoir is at his most feathery. The white dress is built from blues, greens and pinks rather than white, and the whole picture sits in one soft key. That is its success, and also its limit: the heart-shaped face and little smile are Renoir's type more than the sitter's own, so she hardly becomes a person. Beside the sharper portraits of the same years by Degas or Manet, the charm looks a shade easy."},"nga:46681":{"d":"A little girl with a cloud of fair hair and a red bow stands on a curving garden path, looking off to the right with a faint smile. She wears a dark blue dress trimmed with broad white lace and a row of buttons, white socks, and blue buttoned boots. One hand is raised with a finger hooked through the handle of a green watering can; in the other she holds two daisies. Pink roses bloom at the lower left, a green lawn rises behind her, and a band of poppy-red, pink, and purple flowers runs along the top. The paint is soft and feathery, the path flecked with pale lilac and pink. The picture is signed and dated 'Renoir. 76' at the lower right.","i":"Renoir painted this in 1876, the year of his Ball at the Moulin de la Galette, when he was working outdoors alongside the Impressionists and exhibiting with them. The garden is thought to be Monet's at Argenteuil, and the child may be a neighbor's daughter; no name is recorded. The picture is about light and color more than about a person: the cool blue dress against the greens and the dappled path shows how Impressionist painters used cool and warm tones to produce shimmer. It came to the National Gallery of Art with Chester Dale's collection.","c":"Renoir at his sweetest, and the sweetness is both the charm and the limit. The blending of the dress into the flickering garden is skillful, the small face glows, and the white lace has real sparkle. But the picture slides toward sentiment: the girl is a doll-like emblem of childhood, the smile is a convention, and the composition is a simple vertical with little tension. It is better as a study of light than as a portrait, and it is easy to see why such pictures made his reputation for pleasing."},"nga:52207":{"d":"A young woman is shown from the chest up in a low-cut white garment that slips off her shoulders, a blue sash at her waist. Her head tips toward the viewer while her blue eyes look away to the left. Thick blond bangs cross her forehead, a small gold ball earring shows in the visible ear, and with her left hand she holds one long lock of hair in front of her shoulder. Her flesh is shaded with ice blue and mint green over peach. Royal blue and turquoise fill the space behind her head, rust brown the area below. Signed and dated 1876.","i":"Women arranging their hair are an old motif of the toilette, a private moment that lets a painter show bare shoulders and a turned head without a mythological excuse. Renoir returned to the subject for the rest of his career. Here an unnamed model is caught in an ordinary gesture, which makes the picture less a portrait than a study of a type: the fresh young Parisian woman, a figure of modern life that Renoir and his friends preferred to the grand subjects of the academy.","c":"Color is the point. Cool blue and green shadows run through warm peach flesh, and the turquoise and rust behind them make the head glow. The lifted arm gives the half-length some movement, which a plain portrait would lack. Against this, the face is the generic Renoir girl, with the same small nose and full lips, and the flat field of blue leaves her little room to breathe. A charming picture of 1876 rather than a searching one."},"met:438011":{"d":"A young man with russet hair and a trimmed red beard rests his head on one hand and gazes out with a gentle, slightly distracted look. He wears a dark grey-green jacket and a large dark blue bow tie over a white shirt, with a white handkerchief showing at the breast pocket. Light falls evenly on the pale, rosy face, which is modelled in soft, blended strokes; the jacket is brushed more loosely. Behind him the ground dissolves into a mottled haze of green, lavender, pink and blue that suggests a garden in bloom. The signature, Renoir, is written at the upper right.","i":"The sitter was Hyacinthe-Eugène Meunier, who wrote under the name Eugène Murer, a Paris pastry cook, writer and collector who befriended and bought from the Impressionists. He paid them low prices; Renoir is reported to have received about 100 francs for each of the portraits Murer commissioned of himself and his family. Small commissions of this kind mattered to a group that sold poorly, and 1877 was the year of their third joint exhibition. Renoir paints his friend informally, hand to cheek, as a thoughtful companion rather than as a dignitary, and the airy background brings the outdoor manner of the group into a portrait.","c":"A modest, affectionate portrait and one of Renoir's most appealing from the late 1870s. The face is built from soft touches of pink and ivory that hold their own against the vibrating background, and the dark bow tie anchors the picture. It lacks the psychological bite of Degas or Manet: the sitter is pleasant rather than probed, and the hand against the cheek is a stock sign of reflection. But the casual intimacy and high-key colour show Impressionist handling applied to a commission, with a freshness that later, glossier portraits lack."},"nga:46534":{"d":"An oval composition shows a woman from the waist up, her head tilted and resting on her right hand, elbow propped on a brown surface. She smiles slightly; her brown hair is gathered up and tied with a white bow. She wears a light garment with a red accent on one side, perhaps a flower or a brooch, a dark necklace with a small cross, a gold bracelet and a ring on her little finger. Swirling blues and greens fill the background at left, and a tall brown object, perhaps a dresser or desk, stands at right. The brushwork is thick and clearly visible.","i":"The sitter is Marie Murer, described as the sister of Eugène Murer, a Paris pastry cook, restaurateur, writer and collector who fed the Impressionists at his table and bought their pictures directly. Renoir painted her in 1877, the year of the third Impressionist exhibition. The oval format recalls eighteenth-century portraits, which Renoir loved, and the head-on-hand pose is an old sign of reverie; here it gives a friendly, off-duty air to a likeness made inside a patron's circle.","c":"Bolder and thicker than the portraits of a year or two earlier, this one gives the sitter a lively setting in swirling blue-green, and the head-on-hand pose is natural and unforced. The weaknesses are plain too: the arm and bodice are summarily built, and the brown mass at right is an awkward block, so the picture is livelier at its edges than at its center. It is better as a record of friendship than as a considered portrait."},"nga:52208":{"d":"A man is shown from the shoulders up in strict profile, facing left. His short dark brown hair has a slight wave, his brows are dark, his lips red, and his cheeks and visible ear are touched with pink. He wears a black jacket, a white collar and a black bow tie. The background is a textured, abstract blend of pale greens, and visible brushwork gives the portrait a soft, slightly blurred look. The picture is small, about 37 by 29 centimeters, and the museum records an unusual support: oil on cement.","i":"Georges Rivière (1855-1943) was a young civil servant who became one of the Impressionists' most loyal defenders. For the third group exhibition, in April 1877, he published a short-lived journal, L'Impressionniste, explaining the painters' aims, and he later wrote a book on Renoir, Renoir et ses amis. A strict profile is a format associated with Italian Renaissance portraits, and the choice gives a friend's likeness something of the clarity of an emblem.","c":"Renoir keeps it simple: a firm profile, the black coat as an anchor, a halo of pale green. The few touches that model the cheek and ear are exactly judged, and the economy suits a small portrait made for a friend. It lacks drama, and the sitter's character stays on the surface, but the restraint is part of the appeal. It shows Renoir as a quick, unfussy portraitist in the years when the group exhibitions were making his name."},"met:438815":{"d":"A woman in a black silk gown with white lace sits on a flowered divan at the right of a richly decorated room, with a small child in a pale blue dress and white frills beside her. A second child in a matching dress sits at the lower left on the back of an enormous black-and-white dog stretched out on the carpet. The mother looks out with a calm, slightly distant expression; the children look in different directions. The room is full of pattern: a Japanese screen with a peacock at the left, a wicker chair and a small table with flowers and glass at the right, a geometric rug. The brushwork is soft and feathery, the light warm. The canvas is large, about 1.5 by 1.9 meters.","i":"Georges Charpentier was the publisher of Zola and other naturalist writers, and his wife, Marguerite, held a Friday salon where writers, politicians, and artists met. The couple were among Renoir's first major patrons. In 1878 he painted Madame Charpentier with her daughter Georgette and her son Paul, dressed alike as small children then were, in her private sitting room; the dog was a Newfoundland named Porthos. The portrait was shown at the Salon of 1879, and its success helped make Renoir a fashionable painter. The Met bought it in 1907 for 84,000 francs.","c":"Renoir's most ambitious society portrait, and a rare case where Impressionist informality fills a big, formal composition. The mother's black dress anchors the picture, the dog and the lacy pale blue children make a lively triangle of whites and blues, and the Japanese decor shows how fashionable taste had absorbed Eastern art. If there is a weakness, it is the sitters' lack of connection: the faces are agreeable but not deeply studied, and the patterned room can seem more important than the people."},"nga:52222":{"d":"A tiny, rapidly worked oil, about 19 by 18 centimeters, shows a red-haired woman in profile, looking to the right. Bright orange and red strokes make up the hair, with peach and pink flesh beneath, and her features are only hinted at. A low-necked dress is streaked in white and periwinkle blue. Around her, large strokes of teal, dark blue, light green and white swirl through the background, and the teal even overlaps the edge of her hair. Brown canvas shows around the edges.","i":"Jeanne Samary (1857-1890) was a young actress of the Comédie-Française and a friend of Renoir, who painted her several times in the late 1870s, including a full-length portrait now in the Hermitage. Sketches like this belong to that circle of sittings; whether it was a study for a larger picture or an independent trial is not certain. It records the glamour of the stage as Renoir saw it: red hair, a low neckline, flushed color.","c":"As a sketch this is exhilarating. Orange hair against teal is one of the boldest color pairs Renoir used in these years, and the brush moves as fast as the eye. As a portrait it gives almost nothing, since the face is a few touches, and the brash chroma can look arbitrary. It is best taken as evidence of how freely Renoir could paint when no buyer was waiting for a finished image."},"met:437425":{"d":"A little girl, Marguerite-Thérèse Berard, known as Margot (1874-1956), is portrayed in a small oil of about 41 by 32 centimeters dated 1879, when she was about five. The recorded motif is a girl, and the picture belongs to the portraits Renoir painted for the Bérard family, whose patronage supported him in these years. The small format suits the intimacy of a child's portrait made for the family's rooms.","i":"Paul Bérard, a diplomat and banker, became one of Renoir's most loyal patrons from about 1878, inviting him to the family's country house at Wargemont in Normandy and commissioning portraits of his wife and children. Renoir loved painting children, whom he found natural and unselfconscious, and the Bérard daughters appear in several of his works. At a time when the Impressionists struggled to sell, such patronage was a lifeline.","c":"Renoir's child portraits are among the most sympathetic of his portraits: the face is rendered with soft, warm flesh tones and a freshness that fits a five-year-old's round features. He avoids the coyness that spoils many portraits of children. The small scale and loose handling make it an intimate picture, and it shows him at a high point of his Impressionist manner, before the drier, more linear style of the early 1880s."},"nga:39759":{"d":"Three figures stand on a grassy bank in the foreground: a woman in a royal-blue hat and pink bodice, and two men, one in white. A fourth, in a straw hat, blue cravat and crimson sash, sits in a long narrow boat that slants out from the bank and turns to look back over his shoulder, an oar in his hand. The wide river fills most of the canvas, its surface made of short touches of bright blue, with a sailboat, a barge and racing shells beyond and white houses on the far bank. The brushwork is loose and feathery under a sky of long blue and white strokes.","i":"Chatou, on the Seine just west of Paris, was a weekend resort reached by train from Saint-Lazare, known for boating and riverside restaurants such as the Fournaise, where Renoir lunched and where he later set a much larger boating picture. Wikidata lists Aline Charigot, the seamstress he would marry in 1890, and his brother Edmond among the figures. The picture belongs to Impressionism's subject of modern leisure: Parisians of different classes spending a Sunday on the water.","c":"The high-pitched blue of river and sky and the sparkle on the water are the real subject. The standing group, arranged like a snapshot, leads the eye to the boat and the far bank. It is lively and relaxed, but the stiff, dressed-up foreground and the brilliant water never quite join, and the figures lack the interplay that makes Renoir's larger boating pictures so rich. Among his river scenes of 1879 it is one of the freshest."},"nga:42935":{"d":"Seen from above, a light pink path runs down the left side of a slope crowded with long green grass and tall pink and yellow flowers. Several indistinct figures walk away from the viewer along it, apparently carrying large bundles or baskets on their backs, toward a small yellow-walled house with a brown, pink and blue roof. A second small house with a red chimney and a larger building with a pointed steeple stand among tall trees, with soft green hills and a pale sky beyond. Small swirls and dashes of color give the whole a hazy glow.","i":"The title and the museum's themes, France and fishing, tie the walkers to the shore trade: gatherers of mussels carrying their loads home. Renoir painted on the Normandy coast in the summer of 1879, and the picture probably comes from that stay. He rarely made labor his main subject, and here the workers are tiny and almost lost in a flower-filled slope, so the scene reads as much as a summer walk as a day's work.","c":"A daring view, tilted down a slope so that path, grasses and flowers rise up like a tapestry, and the pink path and flower dabs convey a hot, drowsy day with real charm. But the figures dissolve into the vegetation, the houses are toys, and nothing gives the whole a structure. Beside Monet's or Pissarro's landscapes of the same years it feels more decorative than constructed. Renoir is a colorist here, not a surveyor of space."},"met:437426":{"d":"A view of the Normandy coast near Wargemont, with trees in the foreground and the sea beyond, fills this canvas of about 51 by 62 centimeters, painted in 1880 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are the trees, the sea and the landscape. Wargemont, near Dieppe, was the country estate of the Bérard family, who invited Renoir to stay and paint, and the view is probably one he saw during such a visit.","i":"In the summers of the early 1880s Renoir stayed at Wargemont as the guest of Paul Bérard, painting portraits of the family and views of the cliffs and sea. Landscape was a relaxation from commissions, an opportunity to paint light and air as the Impressionists did. The Channel coast was a favorite with Monet as well, and its pale, silvery light and fresh air suited the Impressionist palette.","c":"Renoir is not primarily a landscapist, and the sea is not his natural subject as it was Monet's, but a quiet coast like this suits his gifts: soft greens and blues, brushwork that shimmers with light and a view that feels breathed rather than constructed. The weakness is a certain vagueness of structure, as trees and sea blend into a pleasant haze. It is an agreeable, relaxed holiday work by a master of figures."},"met:437437":{"d":"A young woman stands with her hands on her hips, her chestnut hair loose to the waist, wearing a dress and an apron and a ring, in this canvas of about 100 by 71 centimeters painted around 1875 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded details are the dress, the apron, the ring, the chestnut hair and the akimbo pose. The subtitle, La Servante, means the maidservant, and the apron supports that reading.","i":"The apron marks a working woman, and Renoir often painted young women of the working classes who modeled for him; the long loose hair suggests the intimacy of the studio rather than the street. Impressionist subjects included modern working women, shopgirls, laundresses and seamstresses, rather than aristocrats, and Renoir treats the maid with the same grace as a lady, in the soft light and warm color that he gave to all his sitters.","c":"A full-height figure, a meter tall on canvas, in apron and plain dress: Renoir's gifts are the glow of flesh, the soft modeling of the face and the warm, harmonious colors. The akimbo pose gives her a self-assured, almost saucy air that avoids the sentimental. It is a sympathetic, unidealized figure and a good example of how Renoir found elegance in everyday subjects, with loose brushwork typical of his Impressionist period."},"nga:164956":{"d":"A single figure leans against a wall in a narrow upright canvas, shown full length: a young man in a small red cap and a dark coat with gold buttons and a high yellow collar, with bright red trousers below. One hand is in his pocket and the other holds a thin brown pipe near his mouth. His body turns to the right but his head faces the viewer. Behind him is a muted green wall with a brown stripe along the bottom, perhaps a wainscot, and in the upper right corner dark blue and reddish shapes that could be a picture or a window. Brushstrokes are visible and soft.","i":"In France after the war of 1870-71 the army was rebuilt on conscription, and the young soldier, in red trousers of the kind worn by the infantry, became a familiar sight in streets and cafes. Renoir shows no battle or parade, only a conscript at ease with a pipe, in the casual slouch of a man waiting. It is a genre figure rather than a portrait: the sitter is not named, and the picture offers a type from modern life, a subject the Impressionist generation preferred to heroic history.","c":"The long narrow format and the relaxed lean are well judged, and red against muted green gives the picture its snap. But the paint is soft to the point of fuzziness, with face and hand barely resolved and the setting reduced to a few vague tones, so the soldier remains an idea rather than a presence. An attractive, slightly unfinished figure piece from the years around 1880, when Renoir's handling was loosening and his intentions were not always clear."},"met:437427":{"d":"A bay, with boats on the sea and a mountain rising beyond, is the subject of this canvas of about 60 by 81 centimeters, painted in 1881 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are the boats, the sea and a mountain, which, given the title, is presumably Vesuvius across the water. Renoir painted it during his journey to Italy in the winter of 1881-82, when he saw Naples for the first time.","i":"Renoir's Italian trip, his first, took him to Venice, Rome, Naples and Sicily, and the experience changed his art: Raphael's frescoes in Rome and the wall paintings of Pompeii taught him the virtue of clear line and simplified form, and for several years he moved away from the soft Impressionist touch. The sparkling light of the Bay of Naples, with its blue water and its volcano, was a gift to a painter of luminous color.","c":"The sparkle of the sea and the soft atmosphere are what Renoir captures best: blue water broken into quick strokes, boats as pale accents, a mountain dissolving in haze. It is a holiday picture without the structural force of Cézanne's or the analytical grasp of Monet's, and the vibrant color leans toward decoration. But the freshness is real, and the picture records the moment before Renoir's art turned harder and more classical."},"met:437428":{"d":"Peaches and grapes are arranged together in this still life of about 54 by 65 centimeters, painted in 1881 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are grapes, peaches and the still life itself, a combination of velvet-skinned fruit and glossy clusters that offers contrasts of texture and color. Renoir paints them with his characteristic soft, feathery touch, so that the fruit seems to glow from within.","i":"Grapes and peaches ripen at the end of summer, and a table of such fruit is an image of abundance and sensual pleasure, in a tradition that goes back to Dutch and French still life. Renoir's approach is relaxed and informal, with no moral or symbolic overlay and no hint of the older vanitas message: the fruit is there to be enjoyed by the eye. Such pictures also sold readily, giving the artist a steadier income than his ambitious figure paintings.","c":"The contrast of grapes and peaches is neatly judged: the grapes' cool, translucent bloom against the peaches' warm blush gives the arrangement life. The brushwork is loose and sensuous, and the colors harmonize; the structure, though, is soft. Renoir was not interested in the analytic weight Cézanne found in fruit, and the result is a pleasure rather than a statement. As a small celebration of ripeness it succeeds."},"met:437429":{"d":"Peaches are the subject of this still life, about 53 by 65 centimeters, painted in 1881 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are the peaches and the still life itself: fruit set out on a table and painted with the soft, feathery touch that Renoir brought to flesh. The wide horizontal canvas gives the group room, the lighting is simple and natural, and the size suits a dining room or a collector's study.","i":"Fruit still life was a tradition that the Impressionists renewed: Cézanne treated apples as structure, Monet and Renoir as light and color. For Renoir, peaches were a pleasure of surface: the velvet skin and blush of ripeness echo the warm flesh of his figures, and he returned to the fruit repeatedly. The picture was painted in the year of his Italian journey, at the end of his purely Impressionist phase.","c":"Renoir's still lifes lack the structural rigor of Cézanne's, and they do not aim at it: the peaches are rounded forms of warm color and soft edges, the cloth and plate dissolve into touches of cream and blue. The result is sensuous and informal, a painter enjoying his motif. It is a pleasing, easy picture, better judged for its warmth than for its construction."},"met:438012":{"d":"A bouquet of chrysanthemums is the subject of this upright still life, about 66 by 56 centimeters, painted in 1881 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are flowers and the still life itself. Chrysanthemums, with their clustering petals and wide range of colors, offered the painter a field of small touches, and the vertical format allows the blooms to rise from their container. The picture belongs to a long series of flower pieces that Renoir painted alongside his figures.","i":"Flower painting was a profitable and enjoyable sideline for Renoir, who sold bouquets readily to buyers who wanted a bright, pleasant picture. In France chrysanthemums are the flowers of autumn and, traditionally, of All Saints' Day, when families decorate graves, though the painter's interest here is in color and form, not in mourning. He often used bouquets to work out effects of light that he then applied to figures.","c":"Renoir's flower pieces are all about touch: the chrysanthemums' frilled petals are conveyed by quick, loaded dabs, not by botanical detail, so the bouquet becomes a vibration of color. The construction is simple and the background plain; the sensuous pleasure is in the paint. It lacks the structural thought of Cézanne or the symbolism of Dutch flower painters, and the pleasure is immediate. It is a lively, generous picture."},"nga:52217":{"d":"A small vertical sketch, about 13 by 10 centimeters, shows a nude woman with long blond hair standing before a landscape. Her body, in shades of pale and shell pink, is only suggested by a few strokes: an arm wrapped across her middle, two legs. Beside her, thick white and blue strokes fill the lower left corner. Behind her, light blue water meets a pale horizon, with soft green and red strokes above it for land and bright yellow dashes at the right that may be flowers. The marks are visible and the details indistinct. It is signed with a cursive R.","i":"The title calls it a study, and it belongs with the many small oil sketches of bathers that Renoir made while looking for a monumental nude in a landscape, a goal that occupied him through the mid-1880s. The figure is not a goddess or a named model but a type, the bather as a modern woman at the water's edge. In the academic tradition the nude was the test of a draftsman; Renoir wanted it to be the test of a colorist as well.","c":"What survives, once the details have gone, is the way flesh and water answer each other, shell pink against pale blue. It is a quick, sensitive note and typical of the freedom Renoir allowed himself in small formats. But a study this slight cannot be judged as a finished picture: the body has hardly any structure and the landscape is a pretext. Its interest is in watching Renoir find the color of a figure before he finds its form."},"nga:52218":{"d":"A tiny vertical panel, about 13 by 8 centimeters, shows a nude woman painted loosely in a landscape. She moves away to the left, her back to the viewer, pulling a white cloth across her body while she looks over her left shoulder; her hair is touched with lime green, her eyes and mouth are pink dots, and her flesh is shaded with pale violet. In the background a slouching man in a dark suit, tall dark hat and vivid yellow cravat walks to the right. Trees swirl in laurel green and cobalt, and bands of yellow, pink and blue run behind her legs.","i":"A nude woman with a clothed man in the distance recalls the open-air pictures of Manet, where modern dress and bare skin meet, but Renoir gives the contrast no story: the man walks away and the woman hardly acknowledges him. The picture dates from the early 1880s, when Renoir, after traveling in Algeria and Italy, was reconsidering the nude as the center of his art. A study this small is probably an idea tried out for himself rather than something prepared for sale.","c":"As a flash of color it works: pink flesh, lime-green hair and blue-violet shadow make a jewel of a picture smaller than a postcard. But the figure is hardly drawn, and the man in the top hat is a puzzle, neither explained nor integrated, so the scene reads as a half-formed thought. Its value is as a glimpse of method, a figure and a landscape tried together before Renoir committed to larger canvases."},"met:437430":{"d":"A young woman sits sideways in a wicker armchair beside a cliff above the sea, her body turned toward the viewer and her rosy face looking out with a slight smile. She wears a small round hat trimmed with lace, a dark green jacket with a lace-edged collar and ruffle, and a blue skirt patched with white dabs, and she holds something white, perhaps needlework, in her lap. Behind her, a steep pink-brown cliff drops to a turquoise and green sea dotted with small sails, and pale chalk cliffs lie in the distance under a sky of blue, white and pink. The figure is firmly outlined, while the landscape is feathery and bright.","i":"In 1881–82 Renoir travelled to Italy and came back dissatisfied with Impressionism, wanting firmer contours and more modelling. In late summer 1883 he spent about a month on Guernsey and began some fifteen pictures there, but this one is thought to have been painted in his studio, and the beach is probably near Dieppe rather than on the island. The model was Aline Charigot, his companion, whom he married in 1890. The painting shows the transition: a firmly drawn figure set against a loose, bright background. It came to the Metropolitan Museum in the Havemeyer bequest of 1929.","c":"A pleasing picture from the period when Renoir tried to combine Impressionist colour with the firmer drawing of Ingres and Raphael. The sitter's direct, rosy look and the warm colour keep it fresh. But the two modes sit uneasily: the figure is a little cut out from the space, as if set against a backdrop, and the hands are summary. It lacks the integrated glow of his 1870s pictures, though it anticipates the pearly flesh of his later work. The picture is charming rather than searching."},"met:437431":{"d":"Hills rise around a bay in this landscape of about 46 by 65 centimeters, painted in 1883 by Auguste Renoir on the island of Guernsey, in the Channel Islands. The recorded motifs are the hills and the landscape itself. Moulin Huet Bay, on the island's southern coast, with its cliffs, headlands and clear light, was one of the places Renoir painted during a short stay in the autumn of that year.","i":"In 1883 Renoir made a brief trip to Jersey and Guernsey and was captivated by the islands' light and their wooded valleys and rocky bays. The visit came after his study of Italian classical painting had pushed him toward a firmer, more linear style; in Guernsey his touch loosened again and his color brightened, and the result was a series of landscapes among the most Impressionist of his 1880s output.","c":"Renoir turns the island's folds of hill and headland into a tapestry of soft greens and blues, with feathery strokes that carry light across the slopes. The composition is simple, but the harmonies of color are subtle, and the whole shimmers. Not every part is resolved: the hills run together in places, and the structure is looser than Cézanne's would be. Yet it is among the freshest of his landscapes of the decade."},"met:438013":{"d":"A nude woman reclines in this canvas of about 65 by 81 centimeters, painted in 1883 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are the nude and the female nude, with no named sitter and no mythological title, so the picture is a study of the body for its own sake: the soft modeling of skin, the pose and the light. The wide horizontal format suits a reclining figure.","i":"The reclining female nude has a long ancestry, from the Venetian Venuses of Giorgione and Titian to Ingres and Manet, and Renoir regarded the nude as a central subject of painting. In 1883 he was in his so-called Ingres period, after his Italian journey of 1881-82, when he sought firmer drawing and classical clarity; the nude offered a way to unite the Impressionist love of light with that discipline. He painted the nude as sensuous, not as mythological, and with no narrative.","c":"Renoir's nudes of these years try to join solid drawing to luminous skin, and the flesh here is the point: warm, pearly and softly modeled. The risk is a certain sweetness and vacancy, since the figure is an ideal of comfort rather than an individual. It is less spontaneous than his earlier Impressionist work and less rigorous than Ingres, but as a celebration of the body and of paint it has a warm appeal."},"nga:51136":{"d":"A woman is shown from the lap up, her body angled to the right, holding a shaggy black lap dog whose front paws rest on her forearm. She smiles gently and looks down with blue eyes; her round face has rosy cheeks and a double chin. A brown-brimmed hat with a pale yellow crown and a bloom of white feathers covers most of her hair. Her pale pink dress is printed with small flowers, with a burgundy bow at the neck and gold bracelets over olive-green gloves. Behind her, a table holds a blue vase decorated with a bird and filled with pink, red and white flowers. Dated 83.","i":"The title names the sitter as Mademoiselle Charlotte Berthier; the museum's description says nothing more about her. The picture is a society portrait of the kind Renoir painted in these years for Parisian families: fashionable hat and gloves, a pet, flowers. The dog and vase reinforce the sitter's ease and comfort. The date, 1883, falls at the moment when Renoir, after his journeys to Algeria and Italy, was moving away from the hazy touch of the 1870s toward firmer drawing.","c":"A solid, amiable portrait. The black dog against the pale dress is a shrewd contrast, the vase and flowers are painted with relish, and Renoir does not flatter the sitter's chin. The face is blended while the dress and flowers are dabbed, so the picture convinces more as a display of clothes and color than as a study of character. Pleasant rather than penetrating, it shows the professional side of his portrait practice."},"nga:195122":{"d":"A loosely painted horizontal landscape, built up in bands. Along the bottom, treetops in spring, pine and forest greens with touches of aquamarine and midnight blue; above them a strip of sea skimmed with pale peach, yellow and blue; then a hill that rises almost to the top edge, its slopes in celery and olive green, navy and cobalt with a few touches of rust. Strokes of ivory white suggest houses clustered near the shore. The sky is pale pink at the horizon, deepening to powder blue across the top.","i":"Cap Martin lies on the French Riviera beside Menton, and across the bay stands Monaco with its resort district of Monte Carlo, developed around the casino from the 1860s. Renoir traveled along the Mediterranean coast, partly with Monet, in the winter of 1883-84, and the picture probably dates from that journey or soon after. Northern painters came south for stronger light, and Renoir answered it with a brighter, more saturated palette than he used around Paris.","c":"The structure is simple and strong: dark foliage as a frame, a bright strip of water, a hill pushing to the top. The greens and blues are rich and unexpectedly cool, and the pink horizon is a delight. But the viewpoint is cramped, the hill sits flat, and the town of the title is a scatter of white specks, so the eye finds a pattern before it finds a place. Warmer than Monet's Riviera pictures, though rarely as commanding."},"nga:46533":{"d":"A young girl stands close to the viewer, looking out from the corners of her dark blue eyes, bracing a tall wooden hoop at her hip with one hand and holding a short stick in the other. Her long chestnut hair is tied back with a pale blue ribbon, and she wears a sky-blue dress with a ruffled collar, an aquamarine sash tied in a bow, ice-blue socks and black low-heeled shoes, one foot resting on the bottom of the hoop. Behind her the ground is blended topaz, peach and yellow, and the top third is a loosely painted garden with pink flowers. Signed and dated 85.","i":"Rolling a hoop was a common game of well-off Parisian children, played in the public gardens, and this picture of a girl in her best blue dress belongs to the large group of child portraits Renoir painted for middle-class patrons. The museum's description does not name the sitter. Dated 1885, it comes from the years when Renoir, after Italy, drew his figures with firmer outline and a smoother surface, a manner often called his 'Ingres' or 'dry' period.","c":"The sidelong glance is the best idea here: a child's wary self-possession, caught without sentimentality, with blues that ring through dress, ribbon, socks and sash. The pose, one foot on the hoop, is natural and the figure has weight. The garden behind, however, is mushy against the clearly drawn girl, and the clash shows Renoir caught between two manners. A winning if uneven portrait, and one of his most direct pictures of a child."},"nga:46535":{"d":"A close side view shows a young woman from the chest up, facing right. She has a fair complexion, rosy cheeks and light pink lips, and her red hair is pinned in a bun with short bangs framing her face. A white short-sleeved garment hangs off her shoulders, with a hint of dark blue at the chest. The background is an abstract blend of greens, blues, yellows and oranges without any recognizable objects, and the light, feathery brushwork is airiest there. The canvas is small, about 41 by 32 centimeters.","i":"The sitter is Suzanne Valadon (1865-1938), who as a teenager became a sought-after artists' model, posing for Puvis de Chavannes, Toulouse-Lautrec and Renoir, whose dancing couples of 1883 show her. She later became a painter in her own right, encouraged by Degas. Renoir's profile, about 1885, is not a commissioned likeness but a portrait of a model he knew well, turned into a clean silhouette in the manner of early Italian portraits.","c":"The profile is neatly drawn and the cool, feathery strokes of the ground make a good foil for the warm hair. It is a refined, economical picture. But it tells little about Valadon, who would become a forceful painter and a formidable personality; Renoir sees a pretty profile, a type, and the abstract background gives her nothing to do. Its interest lies in the sitter's later career as much as in the picture itself."},"met:438014":{"d":"Three sisters, the daughters of the poet Catulle Mendès, are shown together in a large canvas of about 162 by 130 centimeters, painted in 1888 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded details are girls, a piano, a violin, flowers in a vase, dresses, socks and ribbons, so the group is placed in a domestic music room. The title names them as Huguette (1871-1964), Claudine (1876-1937) and Helyonne (1879-1955), then about seventeen, twelve and nine.","i":"Catulle Mendès was a poet, critic and one of the busiest literary figures of Paris, and a portrait of his daughters by Renoir was a society commission of the late 1880s: a group of girls in dresses and bows around a piano. Portraits of children and young women in domestic settings were Renoir's staple in these years, as his circle came to include Parisian writers, publishers and financiers. The piano and violin point to a household of music, an accomplishment valued in bourgeois daughters.","c":"This is a large, ambitious group portrait from Renoir's transitional years, and it shows the strain: the figures are carefully drawn in the firm manner he adopted after Italy, while the color and handling are softening again. The arrangement, three girls around a piano, is graceful and affectionate, but the faces are idealized to a family likeness, and the whole can feel more studied than lively. It has great charm and some stiffness."},"nga:52214":{"d":"A very small oil on canvas, about 12 by 10 centimeters, a head study of a girl with brown hair, as the title and the museum's keyword 'girl' indicate. At this size the picture is closer to a sketch or a keepsake than to a commissioned portrait. The museum's record gives no description of the pose, costume or setting, and the sitter is not named, so only the type can be described: a close, informal study of a child, presumably painted from life in the soft handling Renoir used for children's heads in the late 1880s.","i":"Children take a growing place in Renoir's work from the mid-1880s, after the birth of his first son, Pierre, in 1885, and he painted the children of friends, patrons and his own household with evident tenderness. Small studies like this served several purposes: a trial for a larger picture, a gift, or a work sold through a dealer. Which it was here cannot be told from the record, and the sitter's name is not given; the picture is best read as an exercise in catching a child's face quickly.","c":"At this scale the interest is in handling rather than subject: a few touches must carry a face, and Renoir was a master of exactly that economy. The usual danger of his children's heads is sweetness, every child turning into the same rosy cherub, and the test of a sketch like this is whether the individual survives. It is a private note rather than a public statement, pleasing in its freshness and slight in its ambition."},"nga:52215":{"d":"A very small oil on canvas, about 15 by 11 centimeters, shows the side profile of a young child, head turned to the left. The child has soft features, pale skin, rosy cheeks and light brown hair in a bun, and wears a white garment that drapes off the shoulders. According to the museum, the child seems to be carried in the arm of a figure at left whose face cannot be seen, and a second figure is suggested at the right, against a gray-blue ground. The paint is thick, applied in large strokes that blur small details.","i":"The title, Young Girl Reading, does not obviously match the museum's description of a child held in someone's arm. Titles of small sketches were usually supplied later, so the picture is better read from what is painted than from what it is called. Mothers, nurses and children are among Renoir's most frequent subjects from the late 1880s on, and a quick note of a held child would fit that habit. Any more specific story, such as the identity of the figures, is not recorded.","c":"The child's profile is tender and quickly caught, and that is where the little picture's appeal lies; the thick paint and soft contours keep it warm. But it is hard to speak of composition when so little is resolved: the figures at the edges are hints, and the title and the image seem to disagree. It is a sketch to enjoy for its touch, not a work to weigh against his finished pictures."},"met:437439":{"d":"A young girl with daisies is the subject of this portrait of about 65 by 54 centimeters, painted in 1889 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are the daisy, the girl and the portrait. The sitter is unnamed, and the picture belongs to the many images of girls and flowers that Renoir painted in these years, in a bright, soft manner sometimes called his pearly period.","i":"Girls with flowers were among Renoir's favorite subjects: the freshness of youth and the blossoms rhyme, and the daisy, in French marguerite, was a flower of simple innocence in popular culture, as in the game of plucking petals to learn whether one is loved. Renoir, who married Aline Charigot in 1890, spent time at Essoyes in Burgundy, her village, and painted children and country girls with a sunny tenderness. The picture presents childhood as a time of unclouded pleasure.","c":"The picture's attraction is its radiance: a young face in soft light, daisies as small white accents and warm, mother-of-pearl flesh tones. Renoir's sweetness can cloy, and the girl is more an ideal of fresh youth than a distinct person. But the brushwork is flexible and the color harmonious, and this is one of the most appealing of his late-1880s girls. It stands for the gentler, sunnier side of his art."},"nga:43517":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 131 centimeters high and only 42 wide, shows a young woman standing in profile, facing right. She has dark hair in a low bun and wears a white off-the-shoulder blouse and a red skirt. On her hip she rests a large woven basket full of gray-and-orange fish. Behind her a beach-like landscape opens out, with tan dunes touched with orange and pink under a vast blue sky. The brushstrokes are thick and visible.","i":"This picture and a companion showing a girl with a basket of oranges, almost equal in size and facing the other way, were evidently conceived as a pair. Tall, narrow formats of this kind belong to decorative schemes, panels set beside doors or between windows, and Renoir, who began as a painter on porcelain, never lost his sense of painting as decoration. The fish suggest the coast and the sea, the oranges the orchard and the south; the figures are costume types rather than portraits.","c":"The tall format forces an elegant solution: the figure sways against the vertical with the basket as counterweight, and the red skirt and blue sky are clear decorative notes. It is easy on the eye, but there is little observation of a working woman. The girl is a type in a fisher-girl costume, and the dunes are a backdrop. As decoration it succeeds; as a view of life it is thin."},"nga:43518":{"d":"A second tall, narrow canvas, about 129 by 42 centimeters, shows a young woman standing in profile, facing left and holding a basket of orange-yellow fruit. She has fair skin and light brown hair in a low bun, and wears a blue vest over white sleeves, a tan skirt with a brown belt, a dark green apron and black boots. The ground is tan, with pale green foliage, blue water and a pale blue sky behind her. The brushstrokes are soft and visible.","i":"The picture is the companion of the Girl with a Basket of Fish, whose figure faces the other way, so that the two could face each other across a doorway or a room. Oranges bring to mind the Mediterranean and its markets, which Renoir knew from his travels, and in the Rococo tradition he admired a fruit seller or fish seller is a decorative type rather than a social report. The costume of vest, apron and boots is a picturesque version of working dress.","c":"There is more detail here than in its pendant: the vest, apron and boots give the figure some weight, and the dark green apron anchors the long vertical. The pale foliage and water behind are pleasant but undefined, and the face is Renoir's stock profile. As half of a decorative pair it is graceful, but neither picture asks for close attention; their charm is in the whole, not in any one passage."},"met:441104":{"d":"A vase of flowers stands with prickly pears, the spiny cactus fruit of the Mediterranean, in this upright still life of about 73 by 59 centimeters, painted around 1885 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are a vase, flowers, pears and the still life itself. The unusual pairing of a fresh bouquet with a southern fruit gives the arrangement an exotic accent, and the tall canvas lets the flowers rise above the fruit.","i":"Prickly pears were an exotic fruit in northern France, familiar from the markets of the Mediterranean and North Africa, and a painter who had traveled to Algeria in 1881 and Italy in 1881-82, and worked on the Mediterranean coast in the 1880s, would have known them well. Including them gives the still life a note of memory and travel. Renoir often painted flowers and fruit for pleasure and for income, in a manner that stayed loose while his figure style turned linear.","c":"The picture's charm is its odd companionship: a soft bouquet and rough-skinned fruit make contrasts of texture that Renoir enjoys exploring, and the colors are warm and rich. The structure is looser than the rest of his painting of the mid-1880s, and the composition is somewhat casual; it is more a relaxation than a statement. It is a freshly colored, lively still life with an unexpected exotic touch."},"nga:35083":{"d":"A young woman is shown from the torso up, seated and facing right, her face in profile and one arm bent in front of her as if resting on a brown table. She has pale skin, round rosy cheeks, a small nose and slightly parted pink lips, and her brown hair is gathered into a smooth, glossy bun at the nape. She wears a white garment held by thin straps. The lower half of the background is a band of yellow; above it, large soft swirling forms in orange and red lie on a blue-gray ground with touches of green.","i":"A 'head' in a title like this is a type of picture: a model's face studied for its own sake, without a name, a story or a commission. Renoir painted many in the late 1880s and 1890s, when his figures were becoming softer and more pearly than in the 'dry' manner of the mid-1880s. The decorative, almost abstract ground of swirling orange, red and blue is typical of the period. Such pictures were popular with collectors, who often valued his young women above his landscapes.","c":"The profile and glossy bun are crisply handled, and the swirling orange and red background is the liveliest part of the picture, almost abstract in its freedom. The girl herself is the usual sweet type, with round cheeks and small mouth, and she sits in front of the decoration rather than in it. A pleasing late-Renoir product, adequate in drawing and more interesting for its background than for its subject."},"nga:52216":{"d":"A landscape the size of a postcard, about 12 by 17 centimeters, in which tall grasses lead back to a stretch of water and perhaps a town. The palette is saturated with dark, jewel-like blues and greens, a few touches of citrine yellow and rosy pink. Tall straight strokes suggest marsh grasses across the front two-thirds; shorter flicks in cooler greens at the left could be bushes. Pale pink streaks the sapphire water, and beyond it white-walled buildings with muted red roofs sit between trees. The horizon is three-quarters of the way up, under a thin sky of pale purple, pink and blue.","i":"Vétheuil is a village on the Seine northwest of Paris, best known as Monet's home from 1878 to 1881. The museum dates this sketch about 1890 and does not say whether it was painted on the spot or from memory. At this size it was probably made for the painter's own use or as a gift, and it shows that landscape stayed a constant interest in Renoir's work even when figures dominated his output.","c":"In a space the size of a hand this is a lively tapestry of strokes, with the tall vertical grass marks giving the foreground a structure and the pink water a note of surprise. It is more sketch than picture, and the saturated blues and greens are invented rather than observed, a decorator's color. Beside Monet's carefully built views of the same river it lacks weight, but it conveys freedom and delight better than many larger canvases."},"met:437434":{"d":"A girl, a tree and open grass make up this upright canvas of about 81 by 65 centimeters, painted between 1888 and 1892 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are a tree and a girl, and the theme of girls in nature, at the edge of a field in the sun, was one that Renoir returned to again and again in these years, in his bright, soft pearly manner.","i":"Renoir spent summers at Essoyes in Burgundy, his wife's village, where he could paint her relatives, local girls and servants out of doors, and the fields and trees there supplied the pastoral settings of these years. The subject, young women in the countryside, recalls Rococo pastoral and Corot's figures in landscapes, but the Impressionist way of seeing, with light dissolving forms into color, makes it modern. These pictures sold well to buyers who wanted charm.","c":"The painting is a pleasure of color and touch: greens of grass and leaves broken with warm accents, and soft, glowing flesh. It is sweet to the edge of sugar, the figure is idealized, and the anecdote is nil. But the shimmering handling, characteristic of Renoir's pearly years, carries it, and the mood of unhurried summer is convincing. A happy, easy picture, not a profound one."},"nga:46679":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a nearly nude young woman stands with both hands in her upswept chestnut hair, her head tipped forward. Her cheeks are flushed, her lips pink and closed, and a bright white cloth is bunched around her upper thighs. Behind her lies a pile of clothes in petal pink, white, ruby red and buttercup yellow. The surrounding area is loosely painted in sage green, caramel brown and golden yellow, with touches of yellow on green at the right suggesting grass and flowers. Blended strokes give the whole a soft, almost blurred look.","i":"The raised arms and busy hands recall the ancient type of Venus arranging her hair, but Renoir offers a modern bather beside her clothes, not a goddess. From the 1880s the bather was his central subject, the way he proved that the nude could be painted as an ordinary woman in daylight. Painted in 1893, the picture belongs to the soft, warm manner he developed in the 1890s after the harder outlines of his 'dry' years.","c":"The flesh is the achievement: warm, luminous, modeled in pearly pinks with an ease that keeps the large body from looking stiff, and the heap of colored clothes sets off the skin like a bouquet. The risk is sweetness. The face is generic, the pose a little too pleased with itself, and some find the late nudes cloying. This is a good example of the manner at its most appealing, and at its most open to that charge."},"nga:50748":{"d":"A woman is seen nude from the upper thighs up, her body turned slightly to the right and her head to the left. Her left arm reaches outward and holds an indistinct pale shape. Her brown hair is tied behind her head, her cheeks are round, and her red lips turn up at the corners. The body is painted in textured, fluid strokes. The background is a mix of red, green, brown, yellow and tan, laid on in visible strokes that give a sense of movement around the figure.","i":"The nude carries no story here: the model is anonymous and the pale object in her hand is not identified. By the mid-1890s Renoir regarded the nude as the highest test of a painter and painted it constantly, in a warm, rounded style with pearly flesh tones. Its roots lie in the Venuses of Titian and Rubens that he admired, but it is a modern woman, in an undefined interior or garden, that he shows.","c":"The painting is at its best where the paint is freest: the fluid strokes of the torso and the glow of the background give the figure a sense of life. It is weaker in specification. The arm and the pale object in the hand are vague, and the face is one more version of Renoir's smiling model. Among the stream of nudes from his last decades it is representative rather than exceptional: warm, skillful and a little repetitive."},"met:437438":{"d":"A girl seen in profile wears a hat in pink and black in this small portrait of about 41 by 32 centimeters, painted around 1891 by Auguste Renoir. The recorded motifs are a girl and a profile. The profile view turns the sitter's face to the side and gives the soft line of cheek, nose and chin a graceful outline, while the broad hat frames the head with a bright accent.","i":"In the early 1890s Renoir painted many portraits of young girls and women in hats, a fashion accessory that let him play with color and shape; the hat's pink and black give a contrast of sweet and sober typical of the fashion of the period. The profile pose, rare in modern portraiture since the Renaissance, gives the head a medal-like clarity, and Renoir, who admired the Renaissance, may have enjoyed the echo. The sitter is unnamed.","c":"The picture is a small, fresh sketch of youth: the hat's pink and black give the composition snap, and the profile concentrates attention on the softly modeled cheek. It does not attempt psychology, and the girl is a charming type rather than an individual, but Renoir's skill in making skin glow is evident even at this small scale. It is a pleasant minor work."},"nga:66434":{"d":"A dark-haired woman and a blond child sit close together at a wooden table, seen from slightly above. She sits at the far corner with the boy on her lap, smiling down at a toy rooster in her right hand, while her other arm circles his body. She wears a rose-pink, loose-sleeved garment, and her black hair is piled up with bangs. The child, in profile in a white shirt, has shaggy strawberry-blond hair and parted coral lips, and he stands a doll on the table; three white toy sheep stand nearby. Behind them are loosely painted flowers on forest green, and gray drapes.","i":"The title names the sitters. Gabrielle Renard, a cousin of Renoir's wife Aline, joined the household in 1894 as nursemaid to the newborn Jean, the artist's second son and the future film director Jean Renoir, and she became a favorite model for the next two decades. This is a domestic picture, one of many in which Renoir made his own household his subject. The toys on the table and the close, easy contact of the pair are the point of the picture.","c":"Warm and affectionate, with the two heads close together and the rose-pink of the garment and the coral of the lips tying them in a single glow. The soft, blended handling suits a tender subject. But the toys and table are scattered rather than composed, the background is vague, and the sweetness slides into sugar. It succeeds as a family record and as an example of late Renoir's feeling for children, less as a picture that holds the eye."},"nga:52220":{"d":"A young woman sits in profile, facing right, playing a guitar that rests against her shoulder and almost spans the width of the canvas. Her eyes are lowered toward the fingers of her left hand on the fretboard while she plucks the strings with the other. A red headscarf covers her hair, with a low, flat-topped black hat on top; she wears a gold chain with an oval pendant and a gold jacket patterned in blue, yellow and pink. A yellow band holds the guitar. The space behind her is moss green and goldenrod with orange swipes near her face. Signed and dated 98.","i":"Spanish costume and music were a favorite French fantasy, from Manet's Spanish pictures to the guitar players that filled the Salons, and in a picture like this the model is dressed for the role. Renoir himself visited Spain in 1892, going to Madrid to see the paintings of Velázquez in the Prado. The picture is better read as a costume piece, a figure of fancy, than as a portrait or a record of Spanish life.","c":"The diagonal of the guitar is a good device: it spans the canvas and locks the figure in, and the red scarf and gold jacket bring rich, warm color. The lowered gaze gives the girl a quiet absorption. But the Spanish element is costume only, and the sweetness of the face and the soft blending give the picture a generic prettiness. A pleasant fancy picture in which, one might say, the music cannot be heard."},"nga:52213":{"d":"A tiny oil on canvas, only about 9.5 by 8.5 centimeters, smaller than a hand, shows a child with blond hair; the museum's keyword gives the sitter as a boy. No fuller description is on record here, and the sitter is not named, so little can be said about pose or setting beyond the type: a quick head study of a small child, of the kind Renoir made of the children around him. The museum dates it only to a range, between 1895 and 1900.","i":"In those years Renoir's own son Jean, born in 1894, was a small boy with fair hair, but the museum does not say who sat for this picture. Sketches of this size were probably made quickly from life and kept or given away. They show a side of the painter that his public pictures hide: affection for children as a subject in itself, without commission, composition or story.","c":"At this size a painter's skill is reduced to a handful of touches, and the question is whether they make a face. Renoir's heads of children usually do, with a warmth that is hard to fake. But a scrap of this scale remains a note, not a picture, and no one should look to it for the structure or ambition of his larger work. It is endearing and slight, which is probably all it was meant to be."},"nga:52219":{"d":"A landscape smaller than a hand, about 8 by 10 centimeters, in which jewel-toned strokes of blue, red, yellow, green and pearl white swirl and stretch. Cobalt-blue streaks fill nearly the lower half, with terracotta dabs at lower left for rocks and blocky green strokes at lower right for vegetation. A long white triangle in the blue suggests a sailboat. The far bank is thin bands of ruby red, grapefruit pink and teal green; puffy trees in wine red, forest green and canary yellow rise above, with yellow streaks of reflection below. The sky is a patchwork of white, azure and lavender.","i":"The museum gives only the theme 'France' and a date range of about 1890 to 1900; the river and the place are not named. It is typical of the tiny landscapes Renoir painted late in life for his own pleasure, in which a river becomes an excuse for colors. Sailboat and reflections are the stock motifs of Impressionist river scenes, and Renoir and Monet had painted together on the Seine in the 1860s and 1870s.","c":"Almost abstract in effect, this is Renoir's color freed from description, thick strokes of cobalt, red and yellow set against each other with confidence. As a thing to look at it is extraordinarily concentrated; one regrets only that there is not more of it. Yet it is a private sketch rather than a public picture, a sign of how far Renoir could push color when working only for himself, not a typical landscape."},"met:438376":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 50 by 81 centimeters, laid out as a table still life, which the title and the museum's keywords list as oysters, a silver tazza (a wide, shallow cup on a stem), lemons and drinking glasses. These are the elements of Heda's usual banquet pieces, set on a table against a plain ground in the cool, silvery tones that mark his work of the 1630s. The museum gives no fuller description of the arrangement, so the viewer should look at how light moves across glass, silver and shell.","i":"Oysters were a luxury food, and in Dutch still life they could be read as signs of appetite and sensual pleasure, while the lemon, handsome outside and sour within, was a favorite emblem of deceptive appearances. Silver and glass added the themes of wealth and fragility. Viewers of 1635 could enjoy the display of costly goods and also, if they wished, take the picture as a reminder that such pleasures pass. Heda painted for the burghers of Haarlem, a prosperous city.","c":"Silver, glass and shell are among the hardest things in still life to paint, and Heda's control of reflection is his chief claim: a few touches turn the tazza into metal and the glass into air. The cool, near-monochrome harmony is deliberate but also limiting, and the arrangement here is conventional. The Rijksmuseum's Still Life with a Gilt Cup, of the same year, is the richer picture, but this panel shows the same discipline on a smaller scale."},"nga:72869":{"d":"A wide, almost square canvas, about 107 by 111 centimeters and large for a Haarlem still life, shows a table laid for a banquet, with the pie named in the title among the dishes. The museum's record gives no description, so only the type can be outlined: a banquet piece, or banketje, in which Heda usually set silver and pewter, glassware, bread and fruit on a rumpled white cloth over a dark ledge, in a restrained, silvery tonality, lit from one side against a bare wall.","i":"Dutch banquet pieces of the 1630s show a meal interrupted: food half eaten, a cloth rumpled, glasses left standing. They flattered the prosperous burghers who bought them by displaying costly silver, imported fruits and elaborate pies, while the disarray carried an old moral, that pleasure is brief and the table soon cleared. Whether Heda stressed that warning here cannot be told from the record; the picture works first as a display of things and of the painter's skill in rendering them.","c":"Heda's gift was tonal: he could make pewter, glass, linen and crust speak in a narrow range of grays and warm browns, and the hush that results is his signature. At best the effect is calm; at worst, formula, since Heda and his Haarlem contemporary Pieter Claesz. repeated the banquet piece for decades. Even within that formula, a canvas of this size and ambition, from 1635, belongs among the painter's more confident years."},"met:436944":{"d":"A man sits on a green wooden bench, singing and playing a guitar, filling the whole tall canvas. He is dressed as a Spaniard: a broad black hat over a white kerchief tied round his head, a short black jacket, a white scarf at his neck, gray trousers, and white canvas shoes, one raised onto the bench so that his knee supports the guitar. His mouth is open in song, and a red ribbon hangs from the instrument's head. At the lower right a red clay jug and a couple of onions sit on the floor. The background is a dull brown-gray, lit by warm side light. The paint is bold, with strong blacks and whites and little blending.","i":"Manet painted this life-size musician in his studio in 1860, dressing a model in whatever Spanish-looking clothes he had on hand; the Met notes that he often costumed studio models in Andalusian dress. The painting reflects Parisian fascination with Spain and a debt to Velázquez. Shown at the Salon of 1861, it was Manet's first success there and earned an honorable mention. The writer Théophile Gautier praised the bold brush and true color, though he thought the guitarist looked French, calling him a 'guitarrero from Montmartre.' It entered the Metropolitan Museum in 1949 as a gift of William Church Osborn.","c":"A confident, almost swaggering canvas and a remarkable debut. The forms are blocked in with a directness that was new in 1861: black against white, hard edges, no studio gloom. It is also a performance of Spain by a Parisian. The figure is a type, the props are stage properties, and the face is a little coarse. But it is a young man's picture with real structural intelligence, as in the way the raised knee props the instrument and pushes the figure toward the viewer."},"met:436948":{"d":"A boy of about nine stands full length in a bare brown room, facing the viewer with a serious, slightly wary look. He has short fair hair, a wide white collar over a dark brown jacket and breeches, teal stockings, and heavy black shoes. Both hands grip a sword far too big for him, its pommel near his shoulder, while a wide leather sword belt hangs at his side, the strap curving around behind. The background is a smooth, dark brown and the floor a plain gray. Nothing else is in the picture. Strong light falls on the face and collar, and the paint is applied thinly and smoothly.","i":"The model is Léon Leenhoff, the son of Suzanne Leenhoff, who later became Manet's wife. He is dressed as a page of the seventeenth-century Spanish court, and the picture shows how much Manet owed to Velázquez and to Frans Hals, a debt Zola noted in his writing on the painter. The plain dark ground and the way the figure stands in space come straight from Velázquez. An etching after the painting was made with the help of the etcher Alphonse Legros. Erwin Davis gave it to the Met in 1889.","c":"A candid portrait of a child playing at soldiers, and the picture's charm is its hint of unease: the boy is dwarfed by the weapon, wary and a little stiff, like a child told to hold still. The Spanish manner is learned from Velázquez, and the plain dark ground makes the pale face and collar the focus. The tangle of sword, belt, and strap at his side is hard to read, and the costume sits uncertainly on the figure. It is a good, sober picture that points toward bolder ones."},"met:436945":{"d":"A young woman in a bullfighter's costume stands in the foreground of a sunlit arena, her head turned to look directly at the viewer. She wears a black hat with a pale scarf, a short black jacket with a yellow cloth at the waist, black knee breeches with tassels, white stockings, and brown shoes. One hand raises a sword; the other holds out a pink cape. Behind her, small and indistinct, a bull attacks a horse beneath a mounted picador, and a group of toreros watches from a distance by the red barrier wall. The ground is a broad empty expanse of shadowed brown, with her shadow falling beside her; the signature is at the lower left.","i":"The model is Victorine Meurent, who also posed for Manet's Olympia and Luncheon on the Grass, and the title openly announces that she is a model in costume. Her stance has been traced to engravings by Marcantonio Raimondi, and the bullring behind is borrowed almost directly from Goya's Tauromaquia prints. Manet showed it at the Salon des Refusés in 1863. X-rays made at the Met in the 1980s revealed an upside-down female nude beneath the paint, apparently an earlier composition on the same canvas. It came to the museum with the Havemeyer bequest of 1929.","c":"Not a fully resolved picture, but a fascinating one. Manet deliberately exposes the artifice: the model, the costume, and the borrowed bullfight are all on show, and the woman's blank, unembarrassed stare gives the picture a cool modern edge. The pose, however, is stiff, as if frozen for a photograph, and the background, taken from Goya, is too small to compete, leaving the large foreground floor empty. It is an experiment in costume and quotation, interesting for what it points toward, Olympia, more than for itself."},"nga:46427":{"d":"A plate of oysters, a halved lemon, an oyster fork, two empty shells and a dish of salt are set out on a wooden tabletop in front of a gray wall, so close to the viewer that the objects span the canvas. Six oysters crowd an oval white plate edged in royal blue, and the fork's handle juts into the viewer's space. One half of the lemon shows its cut face; the other lies cut side down. The salt is mounded in a bowl with brown bands and faint marks that may imitate a Japanese or Chinese character. The paint is loose and the strokes visible.","i":"Manet painted still lifes throughout his career, often small and quick, and this one of 1862 comes early in it. Oysters were a favorite Parisian food, and for Dutch painters they had meant luxury and pleasure; Manet shows them with no moral, only the pleasure of looking. The bowl with an East Asian-looking mark reflects the taste for Asian porcelain among Paris artists in these years, a taste that would grow into the fashion for all things Japanese.","c":"Direct, economical and confident. The table edge pressed against the picture plane, the broad strokes of the lemon and the loose handling of the shells show how much Manet had learned from Hals and Velázquez about painting what he saw in few strokes. Nothing strains for effect, and nothing is large in ambition; as a modest tabletop picture it is nearly flawless, and it stands behind the bolder pictures he would send to the Salon in the following years."},"nga:46637":{"d":"A ragged group stands and sits together in a hilly open landscape under a cloudy sky. At the right of center, an old bearded man with curly gray hair sits on a stone, a violin in his lap and a bow in hand, looking out at the viewer. To the left, a barefoot girl in a black bodice and turquoise skirt holds a baby, her back turned, and two boys stand shoulder to shoulder, one in a wide straw hat and loose white blouse, the other in a dark vest. At the right a tall man in a top hat and brown cloak stands behind the musician, and a man in a turban and long coat is cut off by the edge. The figures are lined up like a frieze with little depth.","i":"Manet's picture gathers characters from the poorer fringes of Paris, and later sources name them. The violinist is identified as Jean Lagrène, leader of a local Romani band; the man in the top hat as the rag-picker Colardet, who had posed for Manet's Absinthe Drinker; and the turbaned man as Guéroult, posed as the legendary Wandering Jew. The boy in the straw hat is closely modeled on Watteau's Pierrot, and the grouping recalls older pictures by Velázquez and Louis Le Nain. It is one of Manet's largest canvases, painted in 1862, the year before Olympia. The identifications rest on tradition rather than proof.","c":"An ambitious, crowded picture that is more interesting than successful. It is full of borrowings, and the figures do not speak to each other: each stands in a separate patch of paint, as if cut from other pictures and set down together. That strangeness is what makes it memorable. The boy in white is the best passage, painted with a freshness the rest lacks, and the old musician's face has a quiet dignity. As a whole it lacks the unity of Olympia a year later, and it reads as a stage in Manet's education rather than an arrival."},"met:436951":{"d":"A man fishes in a landscape, with a woman, a boat and a dog among the elements the museum lists, on a wide canvas about 77 by 123 centimetres. The picture is usually described as showing figures in seventeenth-century Flemish-style costume in an imaginary, pastoral setting, painted in a dreamlike, loosely handled manner. The record gives no further description, so the exact poses, colours and numbers of figures cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"Manet made Fishing around 1862–63, when he was turning old-master themes into modern pictures. It is usually read as a homage to Rubens, whose landscapes with figures Manet admired in the Louvre, and the fishing couple is traditionally identified as Manet himself and his wife Suzanne Leenhoff, dressed up in a Rubensian costume. The mixture of seventeenth-century dress and a playful, unfinished style is typical of a painter who was both a student of the past and a rebel against academic polish.","c":"A strange, enchanting picture: the loose, feathery landscape and the costume play give it a dreamlike quality unlike Manet's sharp modern scenes, and the glimpse of friends in fancy dress is both charming and puzzling. It can also seem unresolved, with the figures stiffly posed against a hurried background, and its debt to Rubens is more gesture than conversation. As evidence of the experimenting Manet of the early 1860s it is fascinating, though not his strongest picture."},"met:438819":{"d":"A young man with a short dark beard and sideburns stands full length on a bare floor against a warm brown wall, one hand on his hip and the other holding a long cane. He wears a flat black hat with a pom-pom, a short black jacket over a white shirt, a broad white sash, black breeches and tan leather gaiters. Over his arm hangs a bright orange-red cloak striped with cream and trimmed with tassels and pompons, the most colorful thing in the picture. The brushwork is broad, and the shadows are dark and thin. The picture is signed and dated at lower right, 1863.","i":"The model was Manet's youngest brother, Gustave, dressed as a majo, one of the dandified young Spaniards of Goya's day. Spanish costume and Spanish painting were fashionable in Paris, and Manet drew on Velázquez and Goya two years before his own trip to Madrid. The picture was first shown in 1863 at the Salon des Refusés, the exhibition of works rejected by the official Salon, alongside the Luncheon on the Grass and Mademoiselle V. in the Costume of an Espada. It came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1929 with the bequest of Mrs. H. O. Havemeyer.","c":"A confident, slightly insolent figure. The deep, warm blacks of the jacket against the brown ground show how well Manet handled black, and the cloak adds the one burst of color. The legs are stiff, though, the face is a rapid sketch, and the pose is borrowed from Velázquez and Goya rather than discovered. A Parisian dresses up as a Spaniard, and the picture is honest about its theater: it is a costume piece, not a portrait of a person."},"met:436949":{"d":"A small canvas, about 33 by 41 centimetres, is Manet's copy of a famous picture by Delacroix, The Barque of Dante, in which the poet Dante and the Roman poet Virgil are rowed across the river Styx in Hell, with the damned clinging to the boat. The museum lists a boat and human figures, so the copy keeps the original's dramatic grouping. Copying such paintings was part of a young painter's training, and a copy shows what he chose to keep.","i":"In Canto VIII of Dante's Inferno, Dante and Virgil are ferried by the boatman Phlegyas across the marsh of the Styx, where the damned grasp at the boat. Delacroix's Barque of Dante, shown at the Salon of 1822, was a manifesto of the new Romanticism, with its muscular, writhing nudes and dramatic light, and young artists studied it in the Luxembourg Museum. Manet, who admired Delacroix, made this copy about 1859, when he was still working out his own manner.","c":"A copy is a test of attention, and Manet's shows how quickly he grasped the original's central idea: the diagonal thrust of the boat and the writhing bodies in the water. The scale and speed mean that much of Delacroix's glowing colour and finish is lost, so this is more a note than a rival. Its interest is as an early declaration of the debt Manet owed to Delacroix and to the Romantic tradition."},"met:436950":{"d":"A pale, bearded man sits upright on a ledge, his weight sagging against a bunched white shroud, his head dropped toward one shoulder and his open palms marked with wounds. A thin halo hangs above him, and a small lance wound shows in his side. Two angels crowd close behind. On the left, one in dark red presses a hand to her face; on the right, one in ochre-orange with deep blue wings embraces his shoulders and looks straight out at the viewer. The ground is bare earth, with a rock and a small snake at the lower right. The setting is dark and plain, and the broadly brushed white cloth, together with the lit flesh, is the brightest part of the picture.","i":"Manet showed this picture at the Salon of 1864 together with a bullfight scene. A stone at the lower right is inscribed with a reference to the twentieth chapter of John's Gospel, where two angels sit in the empty tomb, yet Manet shows the body itself, so the picture joins moments that the Gospel keeps apart. Critics objected to this, to the angels' bright robes (the verse has them in white), and to the lance wound, which is on the wrong side of the body. Some scholars link the work to Ernest Renan's Life of Jesus (1863), which treated Christ as a historical man; that is an interpretation, not a documented intention. Religious subjects are rare in Manet's output.","c":"The painting succeeds as paint and fails as a sacred image. The white shroud, laid in with broad planes, is as confident as anything in Manet's early work, and the foreshortened legs give the body real, unheroic weight. But the angels are stagy, one gazing out at the viewer as if posing, and the grief feels borrowed from older art rather than felt. A modern realist and an old devotional type sit side by side without merging, which explains the baffled Salon response. It is better valued as evidence of Manet's ambition than as devotion."},"met:438144":{"d":"A rough turquoise sea fills most of the canvas, crested with white at the lower left and centre. At the right a fishing boat with a black hull and a large russet sail runs before the wind, heeling as it passes. Smaller boats with pale sails dot the middle distance, and along the horizon at the upper left lies a long, dark steam warship with two masts and a funnel. Above them a pale sky of white and grey cloud opens to patches of blue. The paint is laid on in broad, flat strokes, and the picture is organised in horizontal bands: foreground water, the fishing boat, the distant ships, and the sky.","i":"The warship is the Union navy's Kearsarge, which on 19 June 1864 sank the Confederate raider Alabama off Cherbourg, a fight watched by crowds on the French coast. In July the Kearsarge called at Boulogne-sur-Mer, where Manet was staying, and he is said to have visited the ship. He had not seen the battle and painted it separately, apparently from press reports; that picture is in Philadelphia. Here the celebrated ship appears as it looked in harbour, small and distant, beneath a working boat in the foreground. The picture is an early sign of Manet's interest in topical subjects and of a seascape built from flat bands of colour.","c":"One of the freshest of Manet's early seascapes. The bands of water, boats and sky are handled with a flat, confident economy, and the green sea, brushed in quick dashes of white, is more exciting than the ship it surrounds. Tilting the fishing boat toward the viewer gives a topical picture a human scale. The weak point is the Kearsarge itself, a stiff dark silhouette that reads as an afterthought, and the canvas has less drama than his imagined battle scene. It stands close to the sea pictures of Boudin and the young Monet."},"nga:1179":{"d":"A man in a bullfighter's black costume lies on his back on a bare brown floor, seen from a low viewpoint that makes him fill the long canvas. His short dark hair is cropped, his head has fallen against his shoulder, and his eyes are closed. The jacket falls open to show white lining; he wears a broad white sash, white stockings and black pointed shoes with bows. One hand rests on his chest, and a sword is tucked under his arm; a few spots of blood stain his tie and the floor by his shoulder. His other hand lies beside a pale pink cape at the left. The ground is light at the front and darkens toward the back, and there is no ring, crowd or bull. The black cloth is painted in rich, flat areas.","i":"This is a fragment. Manet exhibited a larger bullfight scene, Incident in a Bullfight, at the Salon of 1864; reviewers criticised its poor proportions and unreal space, and the dead man looked too large beside the small bullring behind him. Manet later cut the canvas apart. He reworked the foreground figure as an independent picture, which he showed in 1867 as The Dead Man, and the rest survives as The Bullfight in the Frick Collection, New York. The pose was inspired by a seventeenth-century picture of a dead soldier, then attributed to Velázquez, which was reproduced in a large photograph in 1863 and is now in the National Gallery, London. The date of this fragment is uncertain; the museum says probably 1864.","c":"Cutting the picture rescued it. Freed from the unsuccessful bullring, the figure becomes something unusual for its time: a death with no hero, no sermon and no tragic gesture, only a body, a cape and a sword, seen by a painter more interested in black, white and brown. The foreshortening of the head and shoulders is a little awkward, and the costume is painted as lovingly as the face. But the flat planes and the absence of sentiment are what made it feel modern."},"met:436961":{"d":"Peonies, with their leaves, rise from a vase in this tall, narrow canvas, about 59 by 35 centimetres. The museum's keywords record the vase, the leaves and the flowers, so the exact arrangement, colours and background cannot be reported with confidence. The upright format, with a high, frontal view of the blooms, is that of the group of flower paintings Manet made in 1864–65, when peonies were in season.","i":"Manet's peony pictures of 1864–65 belong to a moment when he was winning notoriety with Luncheon on the Grass and Olympia. The flower pieces, small and private, showed another side of him: a quiet delight in white and pink petals and green leaves, painted for pleasure rather than for the Salon. Still life was an old, respectable genre, and Manet's handling, quick and bold with little modelling, made it a modern one.","c":"A bold, simple flower piece, to judge from Manet's usual handling of the subject: the blooms are built from a few broad touches, with more regard for freshness than for botanical exactness, and the upright format gives them presence. The risk of such quickness is that the structure can seem slight. Among the still lifes of his brief career in the genre it is a likeable, confident example."},"met:436964":{"d":"A young woman stands full length in a loose pink dressing gown closed with big round buttons, against a dark olive-gray ground. Her red-brown hair is tied with a blue ribbon, a black velvet band with a small pendant circles her neck, and she looks straight out. One hand holds a small bunch of violets at her chin, and the other holds a long chain that ends in a small round glass. At right a gray parrot with a red tail perches on a wooden stand with a seed tray, and at its base lies a half-peeled orange. The gown is painted in broad, thin, sweeping strokes of pink, gray and white.","i":"The sitter is Victorine Meurent, Manet's favorite model, who also posed for Olympia. The picture was first shown at Manet's solo exhibition of 1867 as Young Lady in 1866, then at the Salon of 1868, where critics compared the parrot to Courbet's Woman with a Parrot of 1866 and the public nicknamed it Lady with a Parrot. The violets, parrot and orange have no agreed meaning; they may hint at fashionable flirtation or simply serve as bright accents against the pink. The collector Erwin Davis gave the picture to the Metropolitan Museum in 1889.","c":"The paint is the strongest part here: the dressing gown is a field of broad, fluent strokes in pale pink, gray and cream that a few shadows turn into cloth, and nothing is fussy. The weak point is the subject. She is neither a portrait of a personality nor a story, only a pretext, as though Manet arranged a woman, a parrot and an orange like a still life. Yet the coolness that unsettled Salon viewers is what holds attention now: Victorine simply stands there and looks back, composed and unexplained."},"nga:45878":{"d":"A bearded man in a black costume stands full length against a mottled brown ground that lightens from coffee brown at the top to beige below. His body angles left, but he turns his dark eyes to the right, his brows heavy and his mustache curled. He wears a black jacket with puffed sleeves and white ruffles at collar and cuffs, puffed breeches over black stockings, and slippers. His wrists are crossed in front of him, a gold ring with a scarlet stone on one hand, which holds a black hat with a long plume. A saber lies on the floor at his feet.","i":"The sitter was Philibert Rouvière (1809-1865), a Romantic actor celebrated for playing Hamlet, shown here in costume rather than in his own clothes, which makes the picture a role portrait. He died in 1865, and the canvas is dated 1866. A single standing figure in black on a neutral ground has often been compared with Velázquez's portraits of court jesters and players, which Manet had studied in Madrid in 1865.","c":"The picture's strength is its black, which Manet handles as a color with many degrees, and the sideways glance gives the figure a vivid, uneasy presence. The weaknesses are equally clear. The face is less a character than a stage mask, the plume and saber read as props, and the figure floats in a vague brown space. A daring attempt to make a portrait from a performance, its stiffness the price of Manet's debt to Velázquez."},"nga:46004":{"d":"A dark wooden table, partly covered by a bright white cloth, fills most of a horizontal canvas against a deep brown ground. On the cloth at left sit a white rose, a bunch of green grapes and a shallow straw-yellow bowl heaped with yellow pears and peaches among green leaves. To the right, a squat, pumpkin-shaped melon streaked in lemon yellow and greens stands on a gleaming silver tray, with a tapered brown bottle and a small stemmed glass behind it. Near the lower right corner the table front shows a keyhole outlined in gold.","i":"At about 68 by 91 centimeters, this is one of Manet's larger still lifes, and it takes up the grand manner of Dutch and Flemish banquet pictures, with silver, glass, fruit and flowers on a laid table. Those pictures often carried a moral about the brevity of pleasure; Manet seems to drop it and offer only the pleasure of looking and painting. In the mid-1860s, when his large figure pictures drew hostile reviews, still life was a place where he could paint with less controversy.","c":"The whites are the triumph: the cloth is painted with a few thick strokes that read as linen in light, and the silver tray, the gourd and the pale rose all answer it. Taken as a whole the picture is a little crowded. Objects fill the table to its edges, no single one commands the center, and the eye wanders. It is a showpiece of Manet's early brushwork, better in its parts than as an arrangement."},"nga:52180":{"d":"A small, long-haired white dog with tawny-brown ears and spots sits upright on a garnet-red cushion against a coffee-brown ground, in a loosely painted vertical canvas. It looks just to the viewer's left with large round black eyes under raised brows, its long ears hanging to either side of a short muzzle, with a glistening black nose and a downturned mouth. The fur is stroked in white and silvery gray, and the thin front legs brace beside a black-and-white ball at the cushion's edge. Signed at the lower right in black, 'ed. Manet'.","i":"The breed takes its name from Charles II of England, who kept toy spaniels, and by the nineteenth century such dogs were fashionable pets, often shown in portraits as symbols of fidelity and intimacy. Painters of pets found steady demand, Landseer in England above all. Manet painted dogs and cats with real affection, and this picture, with its cushion and plaything, treats the animal as a sitter rather than as an accessory to a human portrait.","c":"A fresh, quick sketch of character: the dog's wary, slightly worried expression is caught in a few touches, and the white fur against the dark ground is handled with a brush that seems to follow the coat. It is also a small joke at the expense of the formal portrait, giving the lapdog the dignity of a sitter. At the same time it is a lightweight picture, charming rather than searching, neither a major Manet nor meant to be."},"met:436952":{"d":"A small dark cortege gathers at the lower right of a hilly Paris landscape: a handful of mourners in black stand beneath trees beside a hearse, with a driver at the cart. Behind them the hillside rises in rough white planes of masonry and scrub to a skyline of domes and towers, the dome of the Val-de-Grâce on the left, the Panthéon and church towers to the right. Dark green trees and a pale meadow fill the foreground and middle ground. Above, a storm sky of grey cloud breaks into small patches of blue. The paint is thin and fast, the figures are little more than dark touches, and the canvas looks unfinished.","i":"The Met identifies the subject as the funeral of the poet and critic Charles Baudelaire on 2 September 1867, and says Manet was among the few mourners who came in stormy weather. Baudelaire had defended Manet and had called for art that took up the heroism of modern life, which gives the scene its weight. Manet left the picture unfinished and kept it until his death, so the identification rests on the date, the circumstances and the setting. The distant view, the quick touch and the interest in weather anticipate the landscape painting of the following decade.","c":"Few Manets are this quiet. The funeral is reduced to a cluster of black marks at the foot of the canvas; the picture is really about the hillside and the weather, painted with a freedom he rarely allowed himself in finished work. That is its attraction and its limit. The figures lack the sharp characterization of his portraits, and the balance between the dark foreground and the pale hill is never resolved. It moves through restraint, and matters more as a glimpse of how he might have painted landscape than as a finished composition."},"met:436960":{"d":"A tall, slim man in a bullfighter's costume stands full length on a bare olive-brown floor, his shadow cast beside him. His silver-gray jacket is stitched with sparkling ornament, with a pink sash at the waist, brown-and-silver breeches, white stockings and black pumps. In one raised hand he holds his black hat, lined with yellow, in salute, and his head tilts up and aside as if toward a crowd. In the other hand he holds a red cape and a sword. Dark side whiskers and a neat beard frame a face painted with a few quick strokes. The background is an empty, softly mottled brown, and the artist signed at lower left.","i":"Manet painted this in 1866–67, after a trip to Madrid in 1865 where he had studied Velázquez and watched bullfights. The model is said to have been his brother Eugène, according to the early biographers Étienne Moreau-Nélaton and Adolphe Tabarant. The first firm record of the picture is its showing at Manet's own exhibition near the Pont de l'Alma in 1867. The gesture is ambiguous: the first buyer, the critic Théodore Duret, read it not as a salute after the kill but as the matador asking the crowd's permission to kill. Louisine Havemeyer, advised by Mary Cassatt, acquired it, and the Havemeyers left it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1929.","c":"The figure is monumental, the dark ground in the manner of Velázquez gives it weight, and the silver jacket, painted in quick broken strokes, is the real subject. But the picture is odd as a bullfighting scene: the matador has no bull, no arena and no crowd, and his upturned face addresses nobody. The result is a handsome costume piece rather than a drama, and the flatness that unsettled Manet's contemporaries is both its strength and its limit. It is not among his most resolved works."},"met:436946":{"d":"A brioche, the rich round bread, stands with peaches, grapes and flowers in a still life on a canvas of about 65 by 81 centimetres. The museum's keywords list the bread, the fruit and the flowers, but no more, so the arrangement, the table and background and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The subject is the luxurious end of a meal, the dessert of a well-off household, and the picture is dated 1870.","i":"Still life in France had a distinguished tradition in the eighteenth century, above all in the work of Chardin, whom Manet admired, and a nineteenth-century revival gave it new life. Manet's still lifes of fruit, flowers and bread, painted for pleasure and to explore texture and light, are among the most appealing of his later 1860s and 1870s. The picture entered the Metropolitan Museum through the gift and bequest of David and Peggy Rockefeller.","c":"A rich, appealing still life, in which the fresh, almost sensuous treatment of bread, fruit and flowers shows Manet's gift for catching an object's surface with a few direct strokes. Without the details to judge, it is fair to say that his still lifes lack the brooding stillness of Chardin and pursue instead the sparkle of the moment, in tones lighter and brighter. A small act of pleasure, and unashamed of it."},"nga:43624":{"d":"A young woman in a dark blue dress and black hat sits on a low stone ledge at the left and looks straight out of the picture. A book lies open in her lap, along with a closed fan and a sleeping white-and-brown puppy. Beside her stands a small girl in a white dress with a big blue sash, her back to the viewer, gripping the black iron bars of a fence and gazing through them. Beyond the railings there is only a pale billow of steam, with hints of a stone building and a bridge; a bunch of green grapes lies on the ledge at the lower right. The bars run the full height of the canvas, pressing the figures forward and flattening the space.","i":"The subject is the Gare Saint-Lazare in Paris, but Manet shows no locomotive, only its steam. The woman is Victorine Meurent, who had posed for Olympia and Luncheon on the Grass; the girl is the daughter of his neighbor, the painter Alphonse Hirsch. Exhibited at the Salon of 1874, the picture baffled visitors, who found the subject unclear, the composition incoherent, and the execution sketchy, and caricaturists mocked it. It has since come to stand for modern city life: a moment of waiting and distraction, with a glance that never becomes a conversation. The baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure bought it soon after, and the National Gallery of Art received it in 1956.","c":"Manet's refusal to provide a locomotive or a story is why the painting still feels modern: its subject is looking and being looked at. Compositionally the vertical bars hold everything together, and the contrast between the girl's turned back and the woman's direct stare is brilliant. It is thinner elsewhere. The background is perfunctory, and the girl, a marvelous passage of white and blue paint, is almost a prop. The picture is more in love with surface than with meaning, but its confidence in leaving things unexplained is what younger painters took from Manet."},"nga:61246":{"d":"A crowd of men in black coats and top hats fills the width of the canvas, packed shoulder to shoulder in the lobby of an opera house. A few women break the black mass: one in white with blue-and-white striped stockings, one in a short bright costume with a red sash who reaches toward the men with open hands, and several in black masks and cloaks. At the left edge a man in a green, red and gold jester's costume is cut off by the frame. Above runs a gallery where a leg in a red boot dangles over the rail, and glass-shaded gas lamps hang on the wall. A black mask lies on the floor, and Manet's signature appears on a scrap of paper at lower right. The brushwork is quick and loose.","i":"The scene is a masked ball at the Paris Opéra, then in its house on the rue Le Peletier, which burned down in October 1873, the year of the picture. Such balls let men of the respectable middle class mix with women of the demi-monde, and the dark clothes, masks and dangling boot hint at this without comment. Manet is said to have had friends, among them the composer Emmanuel Chabrier, pose in his studio for the men in top hats; the picture was worked up from sketches rather than painted on the spot. The Salon jury refused it in 1874. An early owner was the baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure, one of Manet's main patrons, and it reached the National Gallery of Art in 1982.","c":"The painting is best at something hard: making a wall of black clothing read as a lively crowd. The bands of hats, faces and gallery hold the composition together, and the few spots of colour, the jester, the blue boot and the red sash, are placed with care. Away from the centre it turns vague, and the faces are barely individual, which may be the point but leaves it nearer to a brilliant sketch than to a finished picture. Among Manet's scenes of modern life it is one of the least sentimental, since it follows no single story."},"met:436947":{"d":"A man in a white shirt, white trousers, and a straw boater with a blue-green band sits at the tiller of a small sailboat, one arm stretched along the side and his head turned to look past the viewer. Beside him a woman in a pale blue striped dress and a white hat with veil and feathers leans against the boat's side, her face half hidden and turned away. The boat is sharply cropped by the picture's edges, and at the upper right a corner of the sail and a rope rise. Behind them the water fills the whole background, a flat, brilliant turquoise with no horizon. The brushstrokes are broad and fluid, with white highlights on the man's shirt and the woman's sleeve.","i":"Manet painted this in 1874 at his family's property at Gennevilliers, across the Seine from Argenteuil, where Monet and Renoir were also painting that summer; it marks his closest approach to Impressionism. The man is generally identified as Manet's brother-in-law, the sculptor Rodolphe Leenhoff, and the woman, less certainly, as Camille Monet. The flat blue water and sharp cropping owe something to Japanese prints. The picture was shown at the Salon of 1879, where Mary Cassatt reportedly called it the last word in painting. It came to the Met with the Havemeyer bequest of 1929.","c":"The bold cropping and the flat, shimmering blue make this one of Manet's most modern pictures, and he borrows the Impressionists' high key without surrendering his own firm contours. The figures are less successful: the man is stolid, the woman almost an accessory, and the two never seem to share a moment. It works as design more than as a scene. Beside Monet's boating pictures of the same summer, Manet is bolder in shape and cooler in feeling, less interested in the dazzle of the water than in the geometry of the boat."},"met:436965":{"d":"Manet painted this in 1874 on a visit to his friend Claude Monet at Argenteuil. The canvas, about 61 by 100 centimetres, shows the Monet family, Claude, his wife Camille and their son Jean, in the garden of their rented house, in a loosely brushed outdoor scene. The museum lists a woman, a man and a garden, so the figures' exact poses, the flowers and the colours cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"In the summer of 1874 Manet spent time with Monet at Argenteuil, on the Seine near Paris, where Monet and Renoir were painting directly from nature, and Manet, though never an Impressionist, was drawn into their lighter palette and outdoor subjects. The picture is a family portrait, but also a document of that friendship and of Argenteuil's gardens and light. It came to the Metropolitan Museum by the bequest of Joan Whitney Payson in 1975.","c":"A relaxed, sunlit picture of friendship that shows Manet at his most informal, with brushwork looser and colour brighter than in his Parisian pictures, and the figures merged with the garden's flowers. The arrangement is casual, some may find it scattered, and the figures are not individualised, so it works more as an impression than as a portrait. But it is a charming witness of Manet's exchange with the Impressionists in 1874."},"nga:1180":{"d":"A small panel, about 13 by 22 centimeters, shows a cluster of racehorses and jockeys galloping straight toward the viewer over an emerald-green track. Three horses run side by side with legs widely splayed, a fourth follows at the left, and a fifth is almost hidden behind a jockey. All are chestnut except one black. The jockeys wear lime green, sky blue with pink, white and steel blue, their faces indistinct. Gray fences line both sides, flecks of brown, black and cream suggest spectators, and green and olive trees fill the background under a sliver of marine-blue sky.","i":"Manet often painted the racecourse, which Parisians of all ranks visited for the spectacle as much as for the betting; here, as in his larger Races at Longchamp of 1864, the horses charge directly at the viewer. The legs are splayed in the 'flying gallop' that painters had used for generations and that the photographs of Eadweard Muybridge, from 1878, showed to be wrong. The museum's description does not say whether this panel was a sketch for a larger picture or an independent work.","c":"At this size the speed is convincing: the horses merge into one dusty chestnut mass, the touch is brisk, and the bright silks are dabbed in like jewels against the green. The splayed legs are conventional, and a viewer used to Degas's more analytical racing scenes may find the effect more exciting than observed. A bravura sketch on a small panel, valuable for its brio more than its depth."},"nga:92997":{"d":"A small, shaggy black-and-white dog stands in a wood-paneled room, its body angled to the right while its head turns left, pink tongue out. The long fur is painted with loose, feathery strokes: black along the flanks and ears, white down the center of the head and on the muzzle, chest, belly and legs, with a bushy tail curling up like a fountain. A light wood walking stick leans on the paneling behind, and on the sage-green floor lies what is presumably a stuffed toy in the shape of a robed figure. The dog's name, TAMA, is painted in tan block letters at the upper left.","i":"The title and the name Tama both point to Japan, and the picture belongs to the years when Japanese prints, porcelain and lacquer had become a craze among Paris artists and collectors. Painting the animal's name into the picture is a playful, label-like touch. Animal portraits were a recognized kind of picture, and Manet, who painted other dogs and cats, treats this pet with the attention he gave human sitters. The museum's description does not say who owned Tama.","c":"A lively portrait in which the brush does the work of the dog's coat, the tongue, the tail and the turn of the head. The composition is a little over-busy: the stick, the toy and the name compete with the animal, and the toy is an odd, unexplained note. But the dog's sidelong, panting energy is caught with real sympathy and without sentiment, and few painters of the period could make a pet so alive."},"met:436957":{"d":"A portrait of Manet's wife, Suzanne Leenhoff (1829–1906), in oil on canvas, about 100 by 78 centimetres, painted around 1873. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, so her pose, dress and setting cannot be described with confidence. At this size it is a substantial portrait, not a sketch, made at home by a husband whose sitter was also one of his most frequent models.","i":"Suzanne Leenhoff was a Dutch-born pianist who came to the Manet household as a music teacher in 1849 and married Manet in 1863. Portraits of her are among Manet's most intimate pictures. This one was painted around 1873, when she was in her forties, and it entered the Metropolitan Museum in 1967 by bequest of Adelaide Milton de Groot.","c":"Suzanne is shown by a husband who looked at her with a painter's candour and an affection that never required flattery. Without the specifics of the picture to hand, one can say that Manet's portraits of her tend to be calm, direct and unidealised, with the face built from broad planes and a brisk touch. A good, quiet example of the domestic side of a painter more often seen as a rebel."},"nga:53034":{"d":"A young woman sits alone at a marble-topped café table, her cheek resting on one hand and her gaze fixed beyond the viewer. She wears a pink dress with frilled, lace-edged cuffs, a white jabot, and a black hat with a lace band and a puff of pale feathers. In her other hand she holds a cigarette, and in front of her stands a stemmed glass with a plum soaking in brandy. Behind her a deep-red banquette fills the lower half of the picture, while wood paneling and an ornate gray-green grille in a gilded frame fill the upper half. The touch is quick: a few dabs make the plum, the glass, and the fingers. Manet signed the table's edge as if writing on the marble.","i":"The sitter is thought to be the actress Ellen Andrée, who also posed for Degas's L'Absinthe, painted a year or so earlier; Manet's picture, from about 1877, seems to answer it, offering a gentler view of a woman alone in a café. The 'plum' was a common Parisian café drink, a plum steeped in brandy, which gives the painting its title. The setting recalls the Café de la Nouvelle Athènes in Pigalle, a haunt of Manet's circle, though the decor suggests it was painted in his studio. Whether she is a prostitute, a shop girl, or just a customer, Manet leaves open.","c":"Manet's café pictures are about the loneliness of modern leisure, and this is among the most humane. The pink dress glows against the deep red, the pose is plausible, and the face has a faintly dreamy emptiness. It is lightly painted, almost casual, with a perfunctory background in which grille and frame crowd together. Beside Degas's harsher reading of the same theme, Manet is gentler and more sympathetic, perhaps too gentle: the picture has charm more than bite. Still, it is a model of how little paint it takes to suggest a mood."},"met:436954":{"d":"A young man sits at a café, identified by the title as the writer George Moore, in an oil portrait of about 65 by 81 centimetres. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so his pose, dress, expression and the café setting cannot be described with confidence. The date, 1878 or 1879, places it when Moore, a young Irish writer, was living in Paris and mixing with Manet's circle at the Café de la Nouvelle Athènes.","i":"George Moore (1852–1933) came to Paris in 1873 to study art, abandoned painting for literature and became a novelist and critic; in his memoir Confessions of a Young Man he described the artists he met, Manet and Degas among them. The Café de la Nouvelle Athènes, in Montmartre, was the meeting place of the painters of the new school, and Manet's portrait places Moore in that world as a member of the circle.","c":"A relaxed, direct portrait of a young writer, in which Manet uses the café setting and a light, sketchy touch to catch a sitter in his habitat. Without the details at hand one cannot judge how fully finished it is. Its value is as a record of Paris café culture and of the friendship between painter and writer, a typical product of Manet's last years."},"nga:136019":{"d":"A pale young man with carrot-red hair and beard sits astride a green X-shaped garden chair, facing the viewer and leaning forward to rest his hands on the chair back. He wears a marine-blue jacket over a white shirt, sage-green trousers streaked with gray, black shoes and a soft blue neckerchief; his eyes are pale blue dots, his features loosely indicated. Behind him a half wall is topped by a trellis, with daubs of emerald and forest green for vines and flicks of orange, gold and blue for flowers. The ground beneath is pale peach with patches of fog blue.","i":"George Moore (1852-1933) was an Irish would-be painter who lived in Paris in the 1870s, frequented the cafes where Manet and Degas gathered, and later became a novelist. His memoir Confessions of a Young Man (1888) includes a vivid account of Manet and the circle. The portrait sets him not in a studio but outdoors, astride a chair in the artist's garden, a relaxed pose that suits the informal sociability of the group and the lighter, open-air manner Manet adopted in his last years.","c":"The sunlit air and the casual, straddling pose give the portrait a freshness that society portraits of the period lack, and the greens and blues of the garden are painted with the lightness of Manet's late years. As a likeness it is oddly impersonal: the eyes are dots and the features sketchy, so Moore remains a type, the bohemian in a garden chair. It succeeds better as an effect of light and informality than as a character study."},"nga:42284":{"d":"A small panel, about 15 by 11 centimeters, shows a woman's head and shoulders in profile, facing left, her eyes looking forward. She has fair skin, a sharp nose, rosy cheeks, red lips, small gold hoop earrings and long brown hair. A decorative blue-and-beige hat is wrapped with blue fabric or feathers that trail behind her head, over her shoulders and onto her chest, above a light patterned garment with dark sleeves. The background is a textured, muted blue. The brushwork is thick and visible, especially in the background and the clothes.","i":"The sitter is not named. In his last years Manet turned increasingly to small, quick portraits and studies of women, in oil and in pastel, and this panel probably belongs to that tendency. Modern fashion, the hat above all, was a subject in itself, and Manet, an elegant man about town, had a connoisseur's eye for it. A profile gives the head the clarity of a medal, a format with a long history in portraiture.","c":"A miniature in paint rather than a portrait in the usual sense: the profile gives a clean, elegant outline, and the thick blues of hat and ground are the point, handled with real zest. The face is a type more than a person, however, and with so little to go on, no name and no setting, the panel works as a sketch of Parisian chic rather than a study of character. Pleasant, slight, and an example of Manet at ease."},"met:438002":{"d":"A woman is shown in profile, as the museum's keywords record, in a portrait of Manet's wife painted at Bellevue, the suburban town where the couple spent the summer of 1880. The canvas is about 81 by 60 centimetres, and the record gives no further description, so her dress, hat, expression and the garden setting cannot be described with confidence. A profile gives the portrait a quiet, unposed quality, as if the sitter were caught in a moment of rest.","i":"Manet, already suffering from the illness that would kill him three years later, spent months at Bellevue in 1880 taking a hydrotherapy cure, and painted a group of garden pictures of family and friends. Suzanne, then fifty-one, is shown in the light of the open air, in the lighter, looser manner of his last years. The portrait came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1997 through the collection of Walter and Leonore Annenberg.","c":"A tender, modest portrait. The profile, the outdoor light and the loose touch of Manet's last years give his wife a quiet dignity; by this date he had learned from the Impressionists without joining them, and his brushwork is fast and free. The picture's intimacy is its value. As painting it is slighter than his major canvases, but it is warm, and its affection is easy to feel."},"nga:164938":{"d":"Two green pears, lit from the upper right, sit on a pale blue tabletop or ledge against a dark ground in a small horizontal canvas. The left pear stands upright, its laurel-green skin flecked with brown and its black stem tipping left; the other lies on its side, lighter green and tinged with peach along its upper curve. The surface below is loosely painted in ice blue flecked with gray, blush pink and sky blue, perhaps suggesting marble. Below and behind the table everything is dark brown, and the signature, 'Manet', is barely visible in the dark.","i":"In 1880 Manet was in poor health and spent months at Bellevue, near Paris, taking a cure, and in these last years he painted many small still lifes of fruit and flowers, which could be finished at a sitting and given or sold to friends. Famously, he sent the collector Charles Ephrussi a small picture of a single asparagus as a postscript to a larger one of a bunch. Two pears on a ledge are a similarly intimate subject: nothing symbolic, just things he liked to look at.","c":"The economy is the pleasure: two pears, one light, a ledge and a dark ground, and Manet makes them heavy and real with a handful of strokes, the pale marble-like surface a quiet foil. Nothing is added for show. It is a lesson in how little a painter needs, and the small size keeps it a sketch of his gift rather than a full display of it."},"nga:164939":{"d":"A golden-yellow melon, painted with thick strokes of straw and butter yellow touched with camel brown, sits at the center of a wooden table in an almost square canvas. Its sage-green stem droops over the left side and the whole fruit leans slightly to the left. The table fills the lower half, its blue top built of strokes of marine blue, blue-gray and white; the background is thin caramel brown over darker brown. The picture is signed at the lower right, 'Manet'.","i":"A single piece of fruit on a bare table, with no companions, is a stripped-down kind of still life, and Manet's late fruit pictures, made in his last illness, take their pleasure in a plain object seen well. There is no story and no allegory; the picture asks to be read as paint, the buttery yellow of the fruit against the cool blue of the table. Dated about 1880, it belongs with the small fruit and flower pictures of those years.","c":"Everything depends on the paint. The melon is built of thick, buttery strokes that give it weight, the thinly rubbed ground lets it sit forward, and the lean and drooping stem save a central composition from being static. The blue tabletop is a bold choice, setting the yellow ringing. A simple picture carried off with complete assurance, it shows how Manet's last still lifes distill the handling of his larger pictures into the most economical form."},"met:436963":{"d":"A small canvas, about 21 by 27 centimetres, shows strawberries, and only strawberries as far as the museum's keywords go: the fruit and a still life. The record gives no further detail, so the arrangement, the dish or table and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. In the last years of his life Manet, ill and unable to work on large canvases, painted many small still lifes of fruit, flowers and vegetables, quick to make and easy to give to friends.","i":"Manet's late fruit pictures have a special place in his work: freed from the demands of the Salon and weakened by illness, he painted simple things for their colour and freshness. Strawberries were a seasonal pleasure, and a few of them, rendered with a few dabs of red, can carry the whole sense of summer. This one, from about 1882, belongs to his last year or so of work; he died in April 1883. The Spingolds gave it to the Met in 1956.","c":"A tiny exercise in economy: a handful of fruit, painted with the rapid, assured touch of a painter at the end of his career, and all the pleasure of the thing is in the touch, the red against the ground and the evident enjoyment. It does not pretend to be more than it is, and that is its charm. Modest in scale, free in handling and wholly unforced, it belongs to the last, best vein of his still lifes."},"nga:46636":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, a pale-skinned woman in a tightly corseted dress looks off to the left, her hands meeting in front of her lower belly. She has large, wide-set, slightly bulging green eyes under dark brows, a round face, full pink lips and a hint of a dimple in her chin. Her dark hair is pulled up under a gold band, with bangs and a ringlet at the nape, and she wears a round earring and a string of pearls. The powder-blue dress, brown at the sides and sleeves, is edged with pastel flowers and white ruffles, and tan gloves reach the cuffs. The ground is fog gray.","i":"In his last years Manet made a series of portraits of fashionable women in pastel, a medium that could be worked quickly and in a single sitting and that probably suited his failing health. This one is signed and dated 1882, the year before his death, and the sitter is named only as Madame Michel-Lévy. The cinched waist, flowered dress, gloves and jewels make her an emblem of Parisian elegance, a subject Manet had been painting since the 1860s.","c":"In pastel Manet could be quick and bright, and the flowers drawn along the edges of the dress and the wide-eyed face have a fresh, seductive immediacy. The effect is more fashion plate than character study: the waist is cinched almost to caricature and the sitter's expression gives little away. It lacks the weight of his oil portraits but has a speed and chic that the oils sometimes lack; a fine example of late Manet at his lightest."},"nga:52181":{"d":"A bouquet of cut flowers in a clear glass vase nearly fills this small vertical canvas. The vase has straight sides and a rounded bottom resting on short round feet, and green stems show through the glass. Some flowers can be identified as white roses and carnations; others are quick touches of delphinium blue, marigold orange, brick red and butter yellow, with green leaves between. Some passages are thickly painted, others thin. The bouquet is set against a pale gray ground, and the vase throws a faint blue shadow to the right.","i":"In the last year or two of his life Manet painted a series of small flower pieces, many of them bouquets in glass vases, and they are often counted among the freest of his late works. Painting a bouquet was something he could do indoors in short sessions, which probably suited a painter in failing health. The subject also continues an old tradition, from Dutch flower painting to Fantin-Latour, of treating a bouquet as a test of a painter's color.","c":"The glass is the test and Manet passes it with economy: the straight sides, the stems seen through the water and the faint blue shadow are all set down in a few touches. The bouquet is looser still, flecks of color that read as flowers at a distance. It lacks Fantin-Latour's botanical care, but it has a spontaneity that his polish does not. A small, fresh piece, typical of the lightness with which Manet closed his career."},"met:436955":{"d":"A man's head, in a small canvas about 46 by 38 centimetres, shows the baritone and art collector Jean-Baptiste Faure. The museum lists the man and the portrait, so the pose, expression and background cannot be described with confidence. As the title says, it is a head only, a format that points to a study made from life, and it may have been a preparation for the slightly larger portrait of Faure that Manet painted at the same time.","i":"Jean-Baptiste Faure (1830–1914) was one of the great baritones of the Paris Opéra and one of the most important collectors of Impressionist painting, owner of dozens of works by Manet, as well as by Degas, Monet and Sisley. Manet had painted him in the role of Hamlet in 1877 and returned to him in 1882–83, in the last year of his life. The Metropolitan Museum has both portraits, given by different donors in 1950 and 1959.","c":"A head study is the portraitist's purest exercise, and Manet's gift for catching an expression quickly is the point. Without the details of the picture, the fair verdict is that it is a vivid, unfussy record of a man whose public persona was the stage; the artist's interest lies in the face, not the costume. As a probable study it is not a finished statement, but Manet still paints with easy authority."},"met:436956":{"d":"A portrait of the baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure, in oil on canvas of about 59 by 50 centimetres, painted in 1882–83. The museum's keywords record only the man and the portrait, so his pose, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. It is somewhat larger than the head study of Faure in the same museum, and was made late in Manet's life, when illness had reduced the range of his work.","i":"Faure was both a star of the Opéra and one of the first collectors of modern art in Paris, acquiring Manet, Degas, Monet and others when many buyers would not. Their relationship illustrates the part that collector-friends played in the history of modern painting: a sitter who was also a buyer. The picture was given to the Metropolitan Museum by Mr. and Mrs. William B. Jaffe in 1950.","c":"The sitter's gaze and the immediate, broad brushwork are what to look for in Manet's late portraits: the face is built by planes of light and shadow with a freedom he had absorbed from the Impressionists but never fully adopted. Without more details one cannot judge the exact handling here, and the modest size limits its ambition. A satisfying, if not major, likeness of a patron by an artist near the end."},"met:816621":{"d":"A small upright cabinet picture painted in oil on copper, about 48 by 43 centimeters. According to the title and the museum's keywords, it shows a shepherd and his sheep in a Roman setting, a pastoral landscape of the kind Castiglione painted in large numbers. The museum gives no fuller description. Castiglione's pastorals typically place a rustic figure and a flock in the foreground, with a ruin or a view of buildings beyond, in warm golden-brown tones and quick, flickering strokes, on a support, copper, that favors fine detail and a glowing surface.","i":"Castiglione, born in Genoa, spent several periods in Rome, and the Roman countryside, with its ruins and herdsmen, gave him a fertile subject. Pastoral pictures offered collectors a gentle, learned fantasy of country life, touched by Arcadia, the imagined land of shepherds, and by the shepherd patriarchs of the Bible. The word 'Roman' in the title places the scene in the real landscape around the city, which visitors saw as the ancient world still visible.","c":"In such pictures Castiglione's strength is usually the animals and the brush: sheep, goats and herdsmen drawn with a lively, nervous touch and set in warm, hazy light, with an ease that owes something to Bassano and to the Flemish painters he met in Genoa. The risk is formula, since he painted dozens of pastorals. A jewel-like cabinet picture, charming rather than profound, and less ambitious than the Roman landscapes of Claude or Poussin."},"met:647338":{"d":"A large canvas, almost two meters high, showing Saint Francis of Assisi, with a cross and a skull among the things the museum records in it. At this scale it was made for a chapel, a church or a grand collection rather than a private room. The museum gives no fuller description of the saint's pose or the setting. In his religious pictures Castiglione often uses a dark ground, from which figures emerge in warm light, and a loose, fluent brush that gives even a quiet subject a feeling of movement.","i":"Francis (1181 or 1182-1226), founder of the Franciscans, was a favorite saint of the Counter-Reformation for his poverty, his devotion to Christ's Passion and his stigmata. The cross and skull place him in meditation: the cross recalls the Passion, and the skull is the memento mori, the reminder of death, that devotional images used to turn the viewer inward. 'Ecstasy' means a rapture in which the saint is lifted out of himself; the exact vision is not recorded here.","c":"A subject of rapture suits Castiglione's brush, which is happiest when it can be fluid and dramatic, and his religious pictures combine a chiaroscuro learned partly from Rembrandt's etchings with a Genoese brilliance of handling. They are admired more for painterly energy than for depth of feeling. At almost two meters the scale asks for a conviction that his fast, sketchy manner does not always supply, and the picture is best judged on its brushwork."},"met:437878":{"d":"A young woman sits asleep at a table, her head propped on one hand, in a room of heavy, dark tones. The table is covered with a patterned carpet and set with tableware and a bowl of fruit, and beyond her an open door shows a second room with a chair. The title makes her a maid. The paint is thicker and more strongly modeled than in Vermeer's later interiors, and at about 88 by 77 centimeters it is fairly large for him.","i":"Sleeping servants were a Dutch subject with a moral edge: Nicolaes Maes had painted a famous idle servant a year or so before, and a Dutch sale of 1696 lists a picture of a 'drunken sleeping maid at a table', usually taken to be this one. Radiographs show that Vermeer first painted a man in the doorway and then removed him, leaving the viewer to wonder what she is dreaming of, and who may be about to come in.","c":"An early Vermeer, and a strong one. The carpet, jug and fruit are painted with a richness he later suppressed, and the open door gives a depth he would perfect. The sleeping girl herself is less subtle than the women of his later pictures, and the heavy foreground crowds her. Yet the decision to paint out the man shows Vermeer already preferring suggestion to anecdote. Powerful, but still a young master learning."},"met:437880":{"d":"A young woman sits at a table by a window in a dim, quiet room, her body turned toward the viewer while her head turns to the light. She wears a yellow jacket trimmed with white fur and a pearl necklace, and holds a lute across her lap with a hand at the pegs, as if tuning it. The table is covered with a heavy green-gold cloth and strewn with sheets of music; more papers and a viola da gamba lie on the floor at the lower right, beside black-and-white marble tiles. A very large map of Europe hangs on the wall behind her, and dark carved chairs frame the scene at either side. Light from the window, with its blue curtain, rests softly on her face and jacket.","i":"Painted around 1662–63, soon after Young Woman with a Water Pitcher, this is another of Vermeer's single-figure interiors. The Met reads it as a courtship scene: the woman looks eagerly out of the window, as if expecting a visitor, while the viola da gamba on the floor and the songbooks spilling off the table suggest music for two. Tuning a lute was also a familiar emblem of harmony and temperance. The large wall map places the quiet room in a wider world. Over time the foreground has darkened and the paint surface has been abraded, which weakens the sense of depth Vermeer built up. The picture came to the Met in 1900 as a bequest of Collis P. Huntington.","c":"A browner, quieter picture than the Water Pitcher, and less assured. The seated figure is solid and her turning head gives the scene a pleasing slow motion, but the foreground is murky, with the chairs and instruments reduced to dark shapes, so that the light has less to describe. Condition is part of the reason. The large map is almost too dominant. Still, the glance toward the window is one of Vermeer's most telling small gestures, and it sums up his great subject: waiting, in a room."},"nga:1236":{"d":"A woman in a blue jacket trimmed with white fur stands at a table beside a window at the left, holding a small balance delicately in her right hand, her gaze lowered to it. The pans are empty. On the table lie a blue cloth, a casket, and strings of pearls and gold chain. On the wall behind her hangs a painting of the Last Judgment, and soft window light picks out her face, the fur and the pearls. The picture is small, about 40 by 36 centimeters, and entirely still.","i":"The old title, Woman Weighing Pearls, is misleading: the pans are empty, and she appears to be balancing the scale before weighing anything. Behind her, the Last Judgment, in which souls are weighed, links her act to a moral weighing, and the pearls and gold on the table stand for worldly goods. The picture is usually read as a lesson in balance and moderation, though Vermeer states none of this and leaves the idea quiet.","c":"Few pictures achieve so much by so little. A single figure, a table and window light are enough, and the structure of verticals and the quiet color carry the idea without comment. The risk lies in the Last Judgment, which can seem a heavy hint of what the picture already says. But Vermeer's reticence saves it: he leaves the woman's thoughts unread, and the small scale draws the viewer in. Among his best-built compositions."},"nga:46437":{"d":"A young woman in a yellow jacket trimmed with white fur sits at a table, a pen in her hand, and turns toward the viewer as if interrupted in the middle of a letter. On the table are paper, an inkwell, a string of pearls and a small casket, and behind her on the pale wall hangs a large picture that appears to be a still life with a viola da gamba. Light comes from the left. The picture is small, about 45 by 40 centimeters, and made of soft, blended yellows, grays and blues.","i":"Letters were a favorite subject of Dutch painters, and Vermeer painted women reading and writing them throughout the 1660s. The writer's glance out of the picture makes the viewer the person interrupted, or the one expected to read her reply. Pearls and a casket, as often in Vermeer, hint at wealth and vanity as well as affection, and the musical instrument in the painting behind recalls the old link between music and love. Vermeer supplies no name, no addressee and no message.","c":"A rare Vermeer in which the sitter engages the viewer directly. The yellow jacket against the soft gray wall, the lit face and the small, quick details of pen, pearls and paper are beautifully judged. If there is a weakness, it is that the sidelong glance gives the picture a slight sweetness, more conventional than the unreadable absorption of his best interiors. Still, it is among the most approachable pictures he painted."},"met:437879":{"d":"A young woman, shown from the shoulders up, turns toward the viewer against a nearly black background. Her wide-set eyes meet the viewer's with a trace of a smile; the face is pale and softly modeled, framed by a dark cap. Over her head falls a yellow-brown cloth that trails down her back, and a glossy blue-gray wrap is draped across her shoulders in large folds; a small white collar shows at the neck and a pearl catches the light at her ear. There is no setting, no object, no action: only the face, the soft light, and the blue silk. The painting is small, about 44 by 40 centimeters.","i":"This is a tronie, a Dutch type of picture that shows a head in interesting dress or expression instead of recording a named sitter. Vermeer's Girl with a Pearl Earring is the best-known example, and this panel is close to it in size, in date (about 1665–67), in the black ground, and in the cloth over the shoulders. The sitter is unknown; it has been suggested that she was Vermeer's daughter, but nothing confirms it. The painting's early history is partly guesswork, and it probably sold in Rotterdam in 1816 for just three guilders. Charles and Jayne Wrightsman gave it to the Met in 1979 in memory of the curator Theodore Rousseau Jr.","c":"Lovely in its modeling and a little puzzling in its charm. The face, with its level stare, is less idealized than the Mauritshuis girl and therefore more believable, and the satin is among Vermeer's best passages of silk. But the picture lacks the shimmer and mystery of Girl with a Pearl Earring, and the head sits slightly pasted against the void. It is best taken as what the title says, a study: it shows that Vermeer, even in a simple exercise, could make a living presence from a few controlled tones."},"met:437881":{"d":"Light enters from an open leaded window at the left and falls across a young woman in a white linen headcloth and a deep blue skirt. She stands with one hand on the window casing, as if about to open it wider, and the other on the handle of a metal pitcher that rests in a basin on the table. The table is spread with a red patterned carpet, with a small casket beside it and a blue cloth thrown over a chair at the right. On the pale wall behind her hangs a map on a rod with a blue knob. The room is calm and almost bare, with soft light and tiny highlights on the metal and the carpet. The painting is small, about 46 by 41 centimeters.","i":"Painted around 1662, this is one of a closely related group of Vermeer's quiet interiors, each with a single woman near a window. No story is given, only a simple act, perhaps washing or airing the room. Dutch viewers prized household order, and some scholars read the casket, the map, and the open window as hints of a wider world beyond the domestic task, but Vermeer states no meaning. The picture is also a study of reflected color: the blue of the chair cloth appears in the polished metal. Henry G. Marquand bought it in Paris in 1887, the first Vermeer to reach America, and gave it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1889.","c":"One of the most serene of Vermeer's mid-career interiors. The palette of cobalt, white, and red is almost sculptural, and the quiet geometry of window, figure, and map carries the picture. Its small weaknesses are a generalized face and a figure with little to do, so that interest rests wholly on the light. But that is the point: Vermeer turns a trivial gesture into a record of how light behaves in a room, with a sureness that few painters have matched."},"met:437877":{"d":"A woman in a white satin gown and blue mantle sits on a dais in a room with a curtain drawn aside, her hand on her breast and her eyes raised toward a glass sphere hanging from the ceiling. One foot rests on a globe. On the table beside her are a crucifix, a chalice and a large book; on the back wall hangs a big Crucifixion, and on the black-and-white marble floor lie an apple and a snake crushed under a stone. The scene is staged like a tableau, framed by the curtain at the left.","i":"This is an allegory, a figure standing for an idea, and Vermeer took many of its elements from Cesare Ripa's Iconologia, the standard handbook of personifications, in its Dutch edition. The chalice, Crucifixion and book are the tools of Catholic worship; the globe underfoot signals mastery of the world; the apple and crushed snake recall the Fall and Christ's victory over evil; the glass sphere is variously read as the mind, the soul or the presence of God. The commission is not documented; the subject suggests a Catholic patron.","c":"The least typical of Vermeer's mature pictures, and the one most often criticized. The allegorical program forces a theatrical pose on the figure, who looks stagy beside the quiet women of his other work, and the crowded accessories are laid out like items in a catalog. Yet the satin, the glass sphere reflecting the room and the black-and-white floor are painted with all his old skill. A fascinating anomaly rather than one of his best."},"nga:60":{"d":"A young woman, seen close and half-turned, looks back over her shoulder at the viewer with parted lips, wearing a large red hat with a feathery plume. The panel is tiny, about 23 by 18 centimeters. Her face is lit, the red of the hat blazes against a darker, indistinct ground, and in the foreground a chair's lion's-head finial is painted in blurred, glittering dots. The edges of forms are soft in places and sharp in others, as in a photograph with a shallow focus.","i":"The picture is a tronie, the Dutch term for a head or face study of an unnamed model, often in fancy dress, painted for its character or its paint rather than as a portrait. The brilliant red hat and the blurred, glittering highlights have been linked to the camera obscura, an optical device Vermeer may have used, though that is a hypothesis. It is a rare panel among Vermeer's mostly canvas works, and the museum dates it to about 1669.","c":"A brilliant improvisation. The red hat, the open mouth and the glints on the lion's head are built with an almost abstract confidence that later painters admired, and the glancing look is irresistible. It is also thinner in structure than the interiors, relying on a few effects, and the face is less subtly modeled; the picture lives on its dazzle. A small thing, and an uncommonly vivid one."},"met:15246":{"d":"A tiny portrait of a man, painted in watercolor on a thin slice of ivory only 5.2 by 4.3 centimeters, small enough to sit inside a closed hand. The title names the sitter as James Stuart, and the museum's keywords add only that he is a man; no description of his dress, pose or background is given, so nothing more should be claimed. Miniatures of this date were normally bust-length, the face built up in fine stipple and hatching, with a plain or lightly clouded ground, and the whole was fitted into a small gold or brass case.","i":"Miniatures were the portable portraits of their day: exchanged at an engagement, carried by a traveler, worn as a locket or kept as a memorial. In the young United States they were an affordable alternative to oil portraits, and Joseph Wood, who worked in New York, Philadelphia and Washington, made his living from them. The museum dates this one to 1800-1805, which places it near the start of his career. Who James Stuart was is not stated in the record.","c":"At this size everything depends on the face, and Wood's work is known for a firm, clear hand: a direct likeness without the luminous glow that made Edward Greene Malbone the finest American miniaturist of his generation. The directness suits a portrait meant to be recognized, and it limits the delicacy. An early work by a competent professional, to be valued for its record of a face and the intimacy of its scale rather than for any display of style."},"met:13341":{"d":"A portrait miniature, about 7.6 by 6.0 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory. It shows a man whose name was not recorded; the title is the standard museum label for anonymous sitters, and the keywords give only 'men' and 'portraits'. Nothing further is described, so claims about his coat, hair or background would be guesses. What can be said concerns the form: a thin sheet of ivory, washed with transparent color so that its pale ground glows through the flesh, finished with fine stippled touches and fixed in a small case.","i":"Anonymous portraits like this are common in museum collections, because a miniature's name was usually written on a paper label or case that could easily be separated from the ivory. The picture is still useful as a record of the early nineteenth century, when high collars, cravats and cropped hair marked a clear break from the powdered styles of the previous generation. Dated about 1810, it was made when the demand for small likenesses was strong in American cities.","c":"What this kind of miniature offers is clarity: a face placed squarely, shapes plainly drawn, color kept light and clean. It does not offer drama or psychology, and without a name there is little to hold the portrait to a life. Within the narrow formula of the time, Wood's likenesses have a plain honesty that wears well. A competent anonymous example of an American art that flourished briefly before photography replaced it."},"met:15307":{"d":"A miniature self-portrait in watercolor on ivory, about 10 by 7.6 centimeters, somewhat larger than the typical miniature of a sitter. The museum's data identify the subject as Joseph Wood himself, a man shown in a self-portrait; no fuller description of his clothing, pose or expression is given. As in other ivory miniatures of the time, the face would have been built up in fine touches of transparent color over the ivory's own glow, in the small, finished manner by which Wood earned his living.","i":"A miniature painter's self-portrait is a calling card in small: it shows prospective clients what he can do with a face, and it leaves a record of the man behind a large body of likenesses of others. Wood was a New York miniaturist who later worked in Philadelphia and Washington, and by about 1810 he was an established professional. The museum identifies the sitter as Wood himself.","c":"A self-portrait is a good test of a miniaturist, since the sitter is always available and always critical, and it gives the viewer a rare sense of the man behind a body of anonymous likenesses. In Wood's work generally the handling is clear and direct rather than inspired, and the interest of this picture is as much biographical as artistic. The slightly larger scale gives the face more room than his ordinary commissions allowed."},"met:15240":{"d":"A watercolor on ivory, assigned by the museum to 1812 without a 'circa', about 7.2 by 5.9 centimeters. It shows a man whose name is not recorded; the keywords say only 'man' and 'portrait'. Like others of its type it is probably a bust-length likeness against a plain ground, the flesh built from delicate hatching over the ivory's own warmth, and it was meant to be mounted in a case or frame. The museum offers no description of the sitter's dress or expression, so those are left to the viewer's own look.","i":"In 1812 the United States went to war with Britain, and men left home for service and for business; a miniature, small and light, traveled easily and could be kept by a wife, mother or fiancée. The museum does not say that this sitter was a soldier or sailor, and there is no reason to assume it; but the habit of exchanging small likenesses, and the market for them, was strong in such years. Wood was by then a working professional in a competitive trade.","c":"Dated and anonymous, this is a useful picture rather than a striking one. A miniature of this type succeeds if the face is clearly drawn and delicately colored, and fails if it turns hard and bright, a danger of the crisp, linear manner of many American miniaturists. Without a name or a story the viewer must judge it as pure craft: neat, economical and firm. It is the work of a skilled professional more than an inspired artist."},"met:13340":{"d":"A small portrait miniature, about 6.6 by 5.4 centimeters, in watercolor on ivory, dated about 1815. The title names the sitter, Joseph Dugan; the keywords add 'man' and 'portrait', and the record offers no description of his dress or features. The name has survived with the picture, and that is what turns an anonymous face into a historical person: on ivory, the identification usually lived on a label, a case or a family memory, and was easily lost.","i":"Nothing in the museum's record says who Joseph Dugan was, and the portrait should not be given a biography it does not have. It shows what made miniatures successful: for a modest price a man of ordinary standing could have his likeness painted in the same small format as a senator or a naval officer. As peace returned in 1815, cities were growing and so was the market for such portraits, which Wood served in several of them.","c":"The merit of a plain sitter's miniature is that nothing distracts from the face, and a firm, clear style like Wood's serves that well. The limits are those of the genre: little room for pose or setting, a conventional bust format, and a tendency to polish away character. If this one rises above routine it will do so in the eyes and the modeling of the cheek, which are the places where a miniaturist's skill shows."},"met:13339":{"d":"A miniature, about 6.5 by 5.1 centimeters, in watercolor on ivory, dated 1816. The title gives the sitter as George William Fairfax, and the museum's keywords say only 'man' and 'portrait'; the record contains no further description. The museum gives the year without a 'circa', and it falls shortly after the War of 1812. Like most miniatures of its kind it was made to be held in the hand, worn or kept in a drawer.","i":"Fairfax is a famous name in Virginia, where the Fairfax family was prominent from the colonial era, but the museum does not say which member of the family sat for this picture, and the name alone should not be pressed into a biography. A named portrait gives the miniature a value that the anonymous ones lack: it can be tied to a family, a house and a set of letters, which is how such small pictures usually enter the historical record.","c":"Competent and compact. The sitter's name gives the picture more interest than the miniature's formula would, but in the end it succeeds or fails as a face. Wood's firm, clear style suits a client who wanted a recognizable, unfussy likeness for family and friends. Little in the formula invites surprise, and the picture should be enjoyed for its precision and small scale."},"met:13343":{"d":"A small portrait in oil on a wood panel, about 25 by 17 centimeters, of Thomas Macdonough, the American naval officer. The museum's data identify the sitter as a man and name him; the record gives no description of his pose, uniform or setting, so those details are not stated here. The medium differs from the many watercolor miniatures on ivory for which Wood is best known, and the larger, firmer panel gives more room to suggest rank and character than a miniature could.","i":"Macdonough (1783-1825) was the hero of the Battle of Lake Champlain, fought in September 1814, when his American squadron defeated a British fleet off Plattsburgh, New York, and helped end a British invasion from Canada. After that victory he was one of the best-known naval officers in the country, and portraits of him were wanted by admirers and by his family. The portrait is dated about 1818, a few years after his fame was made.","c":"The scale tells much of the story. At about 25 by 17 centimeters it is a portrait for a household or a small collection, not for a public hall, and it is best judged as a likeness of a national hero for private admirers. Wood, a miniaturist by reputation, gives it the clarity of his small work; whether the larger scale brought greater depth is for the viewer to judge. Modest in ambition, high in historical interest."},"met:13342":{"d":"A watercolor-on-ivory miniature, about 7.5 by 6.0 centimeters, assigned to about 1815, showing an unnamed man; the museum's keywords say only 'man' and 'portrait', and no description of his appearance is on record. What the object is can still be said: a thin plaque of ivory, usually cut from a tusk and sanded smooth, painted in layers of transparent watercolor with the ivory's whiteness serving as the highlight, then sealed under glass in a case.","i":"A miniature like this was a small personal object, and the context in which it was made is largely lost with the sitter's name. It would have been commissioned by the man or his family and worn or kept; the back of the case often held a lock of hair, though none is recorded here. The nameless gentlemen in museum stores are also a measure of how widely the habit of sitting for a likeness had spread by 1815.","c":"Reduced to the essentials of its type, this miniature asks to be judged by the clarity of the face and the cleanness of the finish. On those terms Wood is dependable, though he rarely transcends the formula. One may value it as a piece of good craft, but the lack of a name or any context leaves little to hold the mind, and it is more useful as one of a series than as a picture in its own right."},"met:15241":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman, titled Miss Muir, in watercolor on ivory, about 7.4 by 6.0 centimeters. The museum gives no date for it, so only Wood's working life, which ended in 1830, frames it, and the keywords list just 'women' and 'portraits'. The record does not describe her dress, hair or expression. What the format promises is the usual one: a bust-length likeness, small enough to be held in the palm, with the translucent ivory doing much of the work in the flesh tones.","i":"'Miss' marks an unmarried woman, and a portrait of a young unmarried woman might be made for her family, for a suitor or as a betrothal gift. Miniatures were common courtship tokens, and the form carried associations of private feeling, which is why they were so often worn close to the body. Who Miss Muir was, and to which family she belonged, is not recorded here.","c":"Ivory's delicacy suits a female sitter, whose skin tones the medium renders with a glow that is hard to achieve in oil. Wood was not among the most refined American miniaturists and may not have flattered as readily as Malbone, whose idealizing grace set the standard. But a plain likeness has its own appeal, and the picture is a small, honest record of an otherwise unknown woman."},"nga:39729":{"d":"A tall man in black glides toward the viewer on ice skates, arms folded across his chest, head turned to look off to the right. He wears a wide-brimmed black hat, a fitted knee-length coat with grey lapels that may be fur, a white cravat, black breeches and stockings, and shoes with silver buckles to which the skates are tied. One toe pushes off from the ice, and the breeze lifts a lock of grey hair and the tails of his coat. Curved skate tracks scar the silvery-grey ice. In the hazy distance at the left, small skaters and buildings are visible, and two bystanders stand near a bare tree at the right beneath a pale, steely sky.","i":"Gilbert Stuart, an American who had come to London in 1775 and worked under Benjamin West, painted this portrait of William Grant, a Scottish landowner, and showed it at the Royal Academy in 1782. A story told later says that Grant arrived for a sitting on a freezing day and the two went skating on the Serpentine in Hyde Park, after which Stuart painted the pose from memory; the tale may have been polished in the telling, but it suits the picture's air of action. Full-lengths of the day usually posed their sitters standing still, so a man in motion was a novelty, and it made Stuart's name in London. He later returned to America, where he painted Washington.","c":"Stuart's best early picture is also his most original. A sitter in motion, arms folded, set against a bare winter park, has a poised elegance that most British full-lengths of the date lack, and the greys of ice and sky are handled with a restraint worthy of Gainsborough. The weaker points are real: the black clothing makes a heavy mass, the head is small for the body, and the distant skaters are sketchy. Stuart's later American portraits depend on faces rather than poses; here, for once, the pose is the star."},"nga:42923":{"d":"A man sits at a table, turned slightly to the right, a document before him and a quill pen in his hand. He has fair skin, a straight nose and white, slightly curly hair in the eighteenth-century manner. He wears a navy-blue coat with gold embellishment, a white shirt with a ruffled collar, a red sash and a star-shaped badge, with a medallion hanging at his neck. The background is neutral. The canvas, about 92 by 71 centimeters, is close to the English 'kit-cat' size of 36 by 28 inches, which shows head, torso and hands.","i":"The sitter is Sir John Dick, shown as a man of office and honor: the quill and document mark public business, and the sash, star and medallion are insignia of rank or order. Stuart painted him in 1783, soon after his full-length The Skater, the picture that made the young American a fashionable name in London after his years as an assistant to Benjamin West.","c":"A confident, well-organized portrait from the moment when Stuart was learning to meet English taste. The face is firm and plainly seen, the coat and sash are efficiently brushed, and the neutral ground sets the head forward. The pose of a man at his desk is conventional, a formula Stuart took from Reynolds and the English school, and the picture lacks the flash of The Skater. But it shows the fluent, quick manner that made him sought after."},"nga:1115":{"d":"A man is seated, shown from the waist up, facing slightly left but looking directly at the viewer. He has light gray hair swept back from his face with curls at the sides, fair skin and slightly pink cheeks, and wears a dark jacket over a white, high-collared shirt with ruffled edges. His hands lie in his lap, holding a small object, possibly a box. Behind him hangs a red curtain or cloth, rolls of parchment or paper lie on a table at left, and the wall is a muted green fading into shadow at the right.","i":"The sitter is Sir Joshua Reynolds (1723-1792), president of the Royal Academy and the dominant English portraitist of the age. Stuart, an American in London, painted him in 1784, when Reynolds was about sixty-one. Painters portrayed one another as a mark of respect and a way of showing their skill to a famous judge. Reynolds's manner, with its warm color and dignified ease, was the standard by which every young portraitist in London measured himself.","c":"Stuart's fluent, unfussy handling suits the subject. The face is lit and carefully observed, and the loosely brushed jacket and shadowy ground give it quiet force. Beside Reynolds's own showy portraits it is plain: the sitter appears not as the great man of the Academy but as a thoughtful older colleague. It is among the more attractive of Stuart's London portraits, and an early sign of the plainspoken manner he would carry back to America."},"met:12663":{"d":"A kit-cat portrait, about 91 by 71 centimeters, the English format of about 36 by 28 inches that normally shows the head, torso and hands, painted in oil on canvas. The title names the sitter as George Heathcote, and the museum's keywords add only 'man' and 'portrait'; no description of his pose, dress or setting is given. It dates from about 1785, in the last years of Stuart's stay in London, and belongs to the run of gentlemen's portraits by which he made his living there.","i":"Who George Heathcote was is not stated in the museum's record. Stuart worked in London from 1775 until 1787, first as an assistant to Benjamin West and, from the early 1780s, as an independent portraitist whose speed and likenesses won him many sitters, Reynolds among them. Portraits of ordinary gentlemen were commissions for family houses, hung among relatives, and they record the formal poses of the English gentry that Stuart painted. In 1787 he left for Dublin.","c":"Stuart's English portraits are quick and likeable. They give heads that look alive, with an ease of handling learned from Gainsborough and Reynolds, and costumes are brushed in with a confident shorthand. What they often lack is invention: pose and setting are formulaic, and the sitter's character stays on the surface. This picture probably sits in the middle of that range, a competent likeness rather than one of the grand portraits of his career."},"met:12673":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified man, painted in oil on a canvas of about 72 by 60 centimeters, smaller than a kit-cat. The title offers his green coat as the one distinguishing feature, and the museum's keywords add only 'man' and 'portrait'; no fuller description is on record. The date range, about 1779 to 1785, covers much of Stuart's time in London, the years in which he moved from assistant to Benjamin West to a portraitist with his own clients.","i":"The picture is typical of the London portrait market in the early 1780s. Many sitters were ordinary gentlemen, merchants or officers, anonymous today because the commission was private and the name did not survive. The title, taken from the color of the coat, is the museum's way of identifying the picture without a name. The wide date range shows that it cannot be pinned down more closely, a reminder of how little is known about much of Stuart's early English output.","c":"A modest portrait whose chief interest is in Stuart's handling. His manner in these years, fluent and quick, with rapid modeling of the face and economy in the clothes, has an appeal that finished, glossy portraiture can lose. Without a name or a story the picture must rest on that technique, and it does so comfortably, though it is less memorable than the best of his London work and less again than his later American portraits."},"met:16781":{"d":"A whole-length portrait, about 2.4 meters high and 1.5 wide, the grandest format an English portraitist offered, showing a sitter named in the title as Captain John Gell. The museum's keywords add only 'man' and 'portrait', and no fuller description is on record, so pose, uniform and setting are not stated here. What the size tells is that this was an expensive commission, a picture that would have been meant for a hall or principal room and for a public statement of the sitter's standing.","i":"In the 1780s a captain, whether in the army or the navy, was usually painted whole-length only if he had wealth or a notable career, and a canvas of this size announces one or both. Stuart painted it in 1785, in the middle of his London years, when his fluent, direct style had made him a rival of the fashionable portraitists; he would leave for Dublin two years later. The record does not say which service Gell belonged to, or what occasion prompted the commission.","c":"A whole-length is the real test of a portraitist: the head is the easy part, and figure, dress and setting must all be managed. Stuart was always more at home with a face, and his figures are often less sure than his heads, but in his best large pictures, The Skater of 1782 above all, the whole figure carries the same energy. As one of the few whole-lengths among the Metropolitan's Stuarts, this picture is notable first for its ambition."},"met:12678":{"d":"A small self-portrait of Gilbert Stuart, about 27 by 22.5 centimeters, a head-and-shoulders canvas hardly larger than a book page. The museum gives it the plain title 'Portrait of the Artist' and records only a man and a self-portrait, so the expression and costume are not described here. Stuart made a handful of self-portraits, and this one dates from about 1786, the year before he left London for Dublin; at this size it is probably a sketch kept for himself rather than a commission.","i":"A self-portrait was the one sitter an artist always had to hand, and for Stuart it was a way to try out a manner or show friends what he could do. In 1786 he was about thirty, the favorite of a good part of London society, and also deep in debt, because he spent as freely as he earned. In the next year he would leave England to escape his creditors and set up in Dublin.","c":"A self-portrait shows a painter's real manner, free of a client's demands, and in Stuart's work generally the speed and plain directness of the handling are clearest in small pictures like this, where a few strokes of warm and cool flesh tint make a face. It cannot be a showpiece at this scale. Its value is as a record of how Stuart looked and worked in his thirties, more than as a finished work of art."},"met:12680":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Thomas Smith, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters. The canvas is the English 'three-quarter' size of 30 by 25 inches, the standard format for the head-and-shoulders likenesses that were the bread and butter of a portrait painter's trade. Beyond the title and the keywords 'man' and 'portrait', nothing is recorded of his dress or pose. The date range, 1785 to 1787, covers the last stretch of Stuart's London years.","i":"The sitter's name is all that survives in the museum's record, and Thomas Smith is too common a name to place without more evidence. Stuart's London portraits of ordinary gentlemen were commissions for family houses, priced by canvas size, and they are a rich source for the wigs, coats and cravats of the late 1780s. In 1787 Stuart left London, and the portraits of 1785 to 1787 mark the end of that first English career.","c":"Stuart in his 'three-quarter' mode: a face lit, a few strokes for the coat and a plain ground. At its best this fluent, unfussy handling gives a gentleman a lively presence; at its weakest it is routine. This one belongs with the competent run of such portraits, and it is a reminder that Stuart's real strength was the speed and truth of the head, not invention or setting."},"nga:1122":{"d":"An oval portrait shows a man from the waist up, his head turned to the left and his arms crossed. He has pale skin and rosy cheeks, dark eyebrows, a long nose and pink lips in a neutral expression, with gray hair curled around the ears. He wears a black or dark blue coat with large gold buttons over a yellow garment with a ruffled white collar. The ground behind him is a warm, muted brown with red drapery at the left, and the painted oval sits within a brown field. The canvas is about 76 by 64 centimeters.","i":"Luke White was an Irish bookseller who made a great fortune as a contractor for state lotteries and ended his life in Parliament. The portrait is dated about 1787, the year Stuart reached Dublin, so it is among his first Irish commissions. The painted oval, a fashion of the 1780s, frames the head like a cameo, and the crossed arms are a relaxed gesture unusual in formal portraits, which suggests a sitter at ease with his own success.","c":"The painted oval concentrates attention on the face, and Stuart's brisk strokes of rosy flesh against the warm brown ground give it life. The crossed arms add a relaxed, unceremonious note. The coat and the drapery behind him are broadly handled and do little beyond framing the head. It is a pleasant, direct likeness rather than one of Stuart's more memorable pictures, and a useful reminder of how quickly he found patrons in Ireland."},"met:19826":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 61 centimeters. The title names the sitter as James DeLancey, and the museum's keywords add only 'man' and 'portrait'; what he wears, how he sits and what lies behind him are not described, so none of it is claimed here. It is dated about 1785, in the middle of Stuart's London years, when he was working in the plain, fluent manner that had made his name there.","i":"DeLancey is a famous name in New York history. The family was among the colony's richest and most powerful, and many of its members stayed loyal to Britain in the Revolution and left for England. A portrait painted in London about 1785 would be consistent with that history, though the museum does not say which James DeLancey this is, and the link remains a possibility rather than a fact.","c":"A competent London portrait by an American, painted in the years when Stuart's quick, candid heads were rivaling those of the fashionable English portraitists. The picture is best judged on the face, where his strength lies, and on its economy; costume and ground are usually briefly handled in his work of the period. A fair example of the portraits that paid his bills before he left for Dublin."},"cma:100489":{"d":"A full-length portrait, about 2.5 meters high, shows John FitzGibbon, the newly appointed Lord Chancellor of Ireland, surrounded by the emblems of his office. On a table at the right stands the crownlike upper part of his mace, and propped against the table is a satin and velvet purse embroidered with the royal coat of arms. The museum records an unusual fact about the support: Stuart painted the picture on a large piece of textured linen, probably a tablecloth.","i":"FitzGibbon was a fearless politician who, as the museum puts it, engineered the act uniting Ireland with England; in 1789 he had just become Lord Chancellor of Ireland, the chief judge, and was later raised to the earldom of Clare. Stuart was then living in Dublin, having spent about twelve years in London, and his Irish patrons thought of him as a British painter. The mace and purse are the symbols of office, and the picture is a statement of power.","c":"Stuart's largest Irish portraits bring his London fluency to sitters who need gravity, and the mace and the purse give the picture a still-life interest that supports the figure. The improvised support shows a painter used to working fast, and the weave gives the surface texture. The scale suits the subject, but the picture is more notable as a record of power than as a revelation of character; it shows how well Stuart could rise to the full-length format."},"met:19056":{"d":"A kit-cat portrait of a man named in the title as John Shaw, in oil on canvas, about 91 by 71 centimeters, the English format of about 36 by 28 inches that normally shows the head, torso and hands. The museum's keywords add only 'man' and 'portrait'; his dress and pose are not described. The date, 1793, is that of Stuart's return to America after about six years in Dublin, so the picture may come from either side of the Atlantic.","i":"Without a profession or origin in the record, John Shaw cannot be placed, since the name was common on both sides of the Atlantic. What can be said is that a kit-cat was a mid-priced format, more than a head and less than a whole-length, chosen by sitters who wanted their hands and some of their clothes shown. In 1793 Stuart was crossing from a circle of Irish patrons to the very different market of the new United States.","c":"Stuart's handling in the early 1790s is at its easiest: the paint thin and fluid, the likeness sure, and the kit-cat format giving him the hands, which he usually painted more briefly than faces. A competent, unshowy portrait, and chiefly of interest as a document of his move to America. The absence of detail about the sitter makes it hard to say more than that it is a good example of a type."},"nga:1110":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, turned slightly to his right and looking at the viewer. He has a pale, slightly rounded face with soft features and light, voluminous hair brushed away from the forehead. He wears a dark jacket with a high collar and a white cravat; there are no jewels or props. The ground is a plain, textured, brownish canvas. The picture is the standard three-quarter size, about 76 by 64 centimeters.","i":"The question mark in the title is the museum's own: the identification of the sitter as Dr. William Hartigan is not certain. The date, about 1793, is the year Stuart left Ireland for America, so the picture may come from either side of the Atlantic. It is plain in dress and free of props, as portraits of professional men often were, and it presents the sitter as a person of learning rather than rank.","c":"A direct, unadorned portrait: the face is soft and carefully modeled, and the neutral ground and dark coat leave the head to do the work. Stuart's strength is evident, but so is a certain blandness; the sitter's general type, the educated man of the 1790s, comes through more clearly than his character. Perhaps the most interesting thing about it is the question mark, a reminder of how much of Stuart's output depends on uncertain identifications."},"met:12667":{"d":"A portrait of the general named in the title as Horatio Gates, in oil on canvas, about 112 by 91 centimeters, larger than Stuart's usual kit-cat. The museum's keywords add the American Revolution, generals, a man and a portrait; no fuller description is on record, so his pose and uniform are not stated here. The date, about 1793-94, places it among the first portraits Stuart painted after his return to America, probably in New York, where Gates had retired.","i":"Horatio Gates (about 1727-1806), a former British officer who settled in Virginia, was the 'hero of Saratoga': in 1777 he commanded the American army that forced the British general Burgoyne to surrender, a turning point that helped bring France into the war. He was routed at Camden in 1780 and by the 1790s was a retired figure whose reputation was mixed. A portrait of 1793-94 shows a senior survivor of the Revolution in the first years of the republic.","c":"Stuart's large, plain portraits of the 1790s give their sitters a worn, human face rather than a heroic one, and a retired general suits that approach. The larger scale lets the figure breathe more than in his bust-lengths. Heroism here is a matter of reputation rather than pose, and the picture is more convincing as a likeness of an aging man than as a portrait of a victor."},"met:12670":{"d":"A large portrait of a man, about 129 by 101 centimeters, named in the title as Josef de Jaudenes y Nebot; the museum's keywords record only 'man'. The canvas is far bigger than Stuart's usual bust-length, and no description of dress, pose or setting is on record, so none is claimed. It is dated 1794 and has a pendant of the same year and almost the same size, the portrait of his wife, Matilda.","i":"Josef de Jaudenes y Nebot was a Spanish diplomat who represented his country in the United States in the early 1790s. His wife, Matilda Stoughton, was American, and Stuart painted the couple in 1794; the pair are among the largest and most impressive of his early American portraits. A diplomat's portrait was a public matter, meant to show a representative of a great monarchy to advantage.","c":"These canvases are among the most ambitious of Stuart's American years, and the size gave him room to show his power as a painter of living flesh. A diplomat's rank calls for formality, and the picture gives the sitter a stately presence; what it may lack is the unforced quality of the smaller portraits. A grand, serious pair, and a sign of how quickly Stuart outgrew the bust-length."},"met:12674":{"d":"A large portrait of a woman in high fashion, about 129 by 100 centimeters. The museum's keywords list a hat with a feather, a lace fichu (a light scarf worn across the neckline), a necklace, an earring and a bracelet, so she is richly dressed and adorned; her pose and the background are not described. The canvas is the pendant of her husband's portrait, almost identical in size and dated to the same year, 1794.","i":"Matilda Stoughton, an American, married the Spanish diplomat Josef de Jaudenes, and Stuart's pair of portraits of the couple, made in 1794, celebrate their position: a diplomat's wife was expected to dress with magnificence. The hat and feather, the lace fichu and the jewelry are the vocabulary of fashionable portraiture that Stuart had mastered in London, and they show him competing for the first great commissions of the new United States.","c":"The costume gives Stuart a showy subject: feather, lace and jewels call for varied touches, and the large format lets him play. The danger of such fashion portraits is that the sitter is lost in her clothes, and a modern viewer may wonder whether she was found at all. Yet the pair, hers and her husband's, carry real presence, and they stand with the best of what Stuart made in New York."},"met:12675":{"d":"A kit-cat portrait of an officer, about 92 by 72 centimeters, named in the title as Matthew Clarkson. The museum's keywords record a uniform, a sword and a chapeau-bras, the flat folding hat carried under the arm, so he is shown in military dress rather than civilian clothes. Nothing else is described. The kit-cat format shows the head, torso and hands, enough room for the uniform and the weapon without the expense of a whole-length.","i":"Matthew Clarkson (1758-1825) was a Revolutionary War officer from a New York family who later became a general of militia and, from 1804, president of the Bank of New York. The uniform marks his rank, and the portrait, painted about 1794, shows a man proud of his service in the first decade of the republic, when the war was still a fresh memory.","c":"A portrait of a soldier by a painter who excelled at civilians. Stuart is at his best with the head, and the uniform, sword and hat, with their hard edges and fixed shapes, are less congenial to his quick, fluid handling. The picture's interest lies in the sitter's history and in the way a veteran wished to be seen in the 1790s; as a painting it is capable rather than exciting."},"nga:1105":{"d":"A man sits at a table, shown from the waist up, his body turned slightly right and his round, fair-skinned face turned to the viewer. His short gray hair curls around his ears and his thin lips are neutral. He wears a dark blue coat with gold buttons over a cream vest and a white, high-collared shirt, and holds a white quill in his right hand, which rests on a green-topped desk piled with folded letters, one of which he seems to be writing on. The background is plain dark brown.","i":"The letters and the quill suggest a merchant's business, since cargoes and ships were arranged by correspondence, though the museum does not state his trade. The 'Captain' of the title records a rank, from the Revolution or from seafaring, that the record does not specify. Stuart painted him in 1794, the year he moved from New York to Philadelphia, where merchants and officials were quick to make him the city's leading portraitist.","c":"A straightforward business portrait of the kind that made Stuart's American living: a clear face, a pen and papers as a statement of occupation, and a dark ground. The face is vivid and the hand with the quill is well managed, with the papers briskly stroked in. The result is more a type, the merchant at his desk, than a revelation of one man, but it is an efficient and convincing type."},"nga:1112":{"d":"A kit-cat portrait of a man named in the title as George Pollock, in oil on canvas, about 92 by 72 centimeters, the format that shows the head, torso and hands. The museum's keywords record him only as male; the entry says nothing of his dress, pose or setting, and none is claimed here. The picture is dated 1793-94 and is almost exactly the size of the portrait of his wife, Catherine Yates Pollock, so the two were evidently painted to hang together as a pair.","i":"George Pollock was the husband of Catherine Yates Pollock, whose portrait matches this one in size and date; the National Gallery also owns portraits of other members of the Yates family. A husband-and-wife pair in matching kit-cat canvases was a typical commission in Stuart's first American years: two fees, two pictures, hung in the same room. The museum does not say what Pollock did for a living.","c":"Stuart's marital pairs are conceived as a conversation across a room, with matching scale and complementary poses, and each half is best judged beside the other. The husband's half is often the quieter, and the merit of this one, on the evidence of the type, is a truthful, unflattering face handled with a light, quick touch. A serviceable portrait that gains by being seen with its pendant."},"nga:1113":{"d":"A woman sits in a red armchair with gold details, her body turned slightly to the right and her hands resting in her lap. Her gray hair is curled and set off by a pearl headpiece and a light green ribbon. She wears a pale green dress with a high neckline and lace trim at neck and sleeves, a light blue sash at the waist, a gold ring and a thin necklace. At her right a table covered with red cloth holds a small basket draped in light fabric, and behind her is a muted green wall with a door partly visible at the left.","i":"Catherine Yates married George Pollock, and this is her portrait as Mrs. Pollock, painted as a pendant to his. As an older woman of means she wears not the latest fashion but a pearl headpiece and a lace-trimmed gown, signs of a comfortable household, and the table, basket and chair add its furnishings. The date, 1793-94, falls when Stuart was settling in America after his years in Ireland and winning the New York gentry as clients.","c":"The dress is the picture's pleasure: pale green satin, blue sash and lace are painted with a soft, quick touch, and the red chair and cloth throw them into relief. The face is lively, though the hands in the lap are summarily done and the composition is a standard seated pose. Stuart's women of this period are at their best when the clothes count as much as the face; here they do."},"nga:1123":{"d":"A man sits at a desk, his body turned to the right and his round-featured, slightly flushed face turned toward the viewer, framed by gray wavy hair. He wears a dark blue coat with gold buttons over a white shirt. One hand holds a paper and rests on a desk covered with more papers on a green cloth, and a wooden tray with silver jars stands at one side. The background is a plain olive color. The canvas is about 76 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Richard Yates is shown as a man of business: the papers on the desk and the tray with its silver pots mark a merchant or office-holder at work, a common arrangement in portraits of the Federal era. With the portrait of his wife, Catherine Brass Yates, it shows Stuart at the start of his American career, painting the New York gentry in the plain, direct manner that suited the republic's taste. The museum does not record the nature of his business.","c":"A solid, workmanlike portrait: the face is warm, the blue coat firm, and the desk with its papers and silver gives the lower half a still-life interest. It is less arresting than the portrait of his wife, whose sharper features and absorbed task give the pair its life. Here the sitter's role is to be a respectable man of affairs, and the picture fills it with unfussy competence."},"nga:563":{"d":"A woman in a white satin dress and a tall, sheer white bonnet sits sewing, her body facing left, and turns her head to look straight out from under slightly raised brows. Her dark brown eyes are heavy-lidded, her nose long and sharp, her cheekbones lightly flushed and her thin lips pressed together. She pinches a threaded needle between thumb and finger, the thread held taut by her outstretched little finger, and what seems to be a thimble catches the light. She sits in a dusky rose chair studded with brass nails, against a taupe ground darkening to near black.","i":"Catherine Brass was the wife of Richard Yates, of a New York family, and Stuart painted the couple soon after his return from Ireland in 1793. The National Gallery holds a group of portraits of the Yates family and its in-laws. Women of her generation were often shown sewing or reading, signs of domestic industry, and here the needle and thread are an honest emblem of household care. The attention to age, wrinkles and thin lips shows the painter's refusal to flatter.","c":"Few Stuart portraits are as unflinching or as alive. The sharp face, the concentration of the sewing and the different whites, satin, bonnet and gauze, each painted with its own touch, are done with relish, and the glint of light at her fingertips is a touch of pure observation. As a record of old age without pity or sentiment it is among the best portraits of its date in America."},"nga:564":{"d":"A man sits in a wooden chair, facing the viewer directly with a slight smile. He has light skin, gray hair and a prominent nose, and wears a dark jacket, a high-collared white shirt and a ruffled cravat. The background is plain and earthy brown. The canvas is the standard three-quarter size, about 76 by 64 centimeters, and the picture has no props or setting beyond the chair, so everything depends on the face.","i":"Lawrence Reid Yates bore the same family name as Richard and Catherine Yates, whom Stuart also painted in 1793-94, and the group of portraits, now in Washington, shows how quickly the returning painter found clients among New York's merchants and gentry. The museum's description does not record his occupation. A smile, however slight, was unusual in portraits of the time, when sitters were usually grave, and it gives this picture a warmth.","c":"A plain, honest likeness. The smile and the direct look are the heart of it, and Stuart's quick, thin handling of the flesh lets the face glow against the brown. Costume and chair are barely more than indicated, which is a limit: with no props, the picture's whole interest is in the expression. It is a good example of the unpretentious, truthful American manner Stuart developed after his return."},"nga:75023":{"d":"Seen from the knees up, a balding, clean-shaven man sits angled to the right in a voluminous rust-red and black robe edged in silvery white, a ruffled white cravat at his throat. Ash-brown hair curls above his ears, his gray eyes look to the right, and his long, pointed nose and thin pale lips give him a severe air. His legs are crossed; one hand lies in his lap and the other rests on a book standing on a table with a dark pink cloth. A copper-red curtain hangs at the left, and a column rises against a parchment-brown wall.","i":"John Jay (1745-1829) was the first Chief Justice of the United States, a co-author of the Federalist Papers and the negotiator of the 1794 treaty with Britain that bears his name; the red-and-black robe appears to be the judicial dress of the Supreme Court's early years. The book and column point to law and learning. The portrait, a gift of the Jay family, is dated 1794, the year Jay was sent to London to negotiate.","c":"A commanding portrait, and among the first major American portraits of a statesman that Stuart painted. The robe, handled in loose strokes, gives the figure bulk, and the crossed legs and turned head offer an easy dignity that avoids the stiffness of official portraiture; the sharp face has the look of a man used to command. It still has something of the grand manner of Stuart's London years, though the sitter's remoteness is part of the effect."},"met:16584":{"d":"A bust-length portrait shows George Washington turned slightly to the left, looking out at the viewer. His powdered grey hair is brushed back over the ears and tied behind in a black bow. His face is ruddy, the cheeks flushed pink, the eyes grey-blue and the mouth closed. He wears a dark coat over a white shirt with a frilled front and a high white neckcloth. Behind him is a dark olive background with a green curtain at the right and a glimpse of golden-brown sky and cloud. The paint is smooth, with the flesh modelled in soft transitions and the clothes in dark, broad strokes. Oil on canvas, about 77 by 64 centimetres.","i":"The Met identifies this as one of eighteen similar portraits by Stuart in the Vaughan group, named for the first, owned by the merchant Samuel Vaughan, a friend of Washington; this one is called the Gibbs-Channing-Avery portrait after its successive owners. The first was presumably painted from life in Philadelphia and then replicated; this version was begun in 1795. Technical study has revealed that Stuart changed the colour of the sitter's coat, repainting the American-made brown wool broadcloth to look like the formal European black silk velvet suit that Washington wore on official occasions.","c":"This is the face many Americans picture when they think of Washington, and part of its power is that familiarity. Stuart's warm flesh tones, quick handling of the shirt frill and softly shaded background are accomplished and efficient, though a replica like this lacks the freshness of a life portrait. The man himself remains guarded: the expression is composed, a little stiff, the mouth set tight. Stuart flatters the statesman more than he reveals him. As an official image of the republic's first president it has endured because it does what a public likeness must, and does it clearly."},"nga:1114":{"d":"A man sits on a red cushioned chair, turned slightly left and facing the viewer with a neutral expression, dark brows, rosy cheeks and thin lips. His gray hair falls in curls at the sides, and he wears a dark red, double-breasted coat with large gold buttons and a white cravat. In his left hand he holds a pair of tan gloves, and in front of him stands a red piece of furniture draped with red cloth. The background is a muted brown gradient. The canvas is about 92 by 71 centimeters, a kit-cat.","i":"Stephen Van Rensselaer III (1764-1839) was the last 'patroon' of Rensselaerswyck, the vast Hudson Valley estate near Albany inherited from Dutch colonial ancestors, later lieutenant governor of New York and the founder of the school that became Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. The gloves mark a gentleman's leisure, and the red coat and chair give him a rich frame. The date, 1793-95, shows him as a man of about thirty, an heir at the start of a long public career.","c":"Stuart flatters his wealthy patron with rich red and brisk flesh color, and the picture has the polished finish of his best work for the rich; the gloves are a neat, characterizing touch. But it is a portrait about status more than character, a gentleman's type rather than a person, and the red chair, coat and cloth are rather too much of a good thing. A sign of Stuart's rapid success with the new American elite."},"nga:1121":{"d":"A black-coated older man appears from the chest up against a wine-red ground, his body angled to the right while his face turns to meet the viewer's eye. Gray eyes under pale brows, a hooked nose, jowls and a closed, set mouth give the face a heavy, serious cast, warmed by flushed cheeks. White hair is swept out at the sides and tied at the nape with a ribbon sketched in black. The coat has silvery highlights along the collar and shoulder, and the ground shifts from scarlet near the face to black in the upper corners.","i":"Washington sat for Stuart in 1795 in Philadelphia, then the capital, and the result was the first of the painter's three Washington types, called Vaughan after an early owner. Stuart is said to have returned to America hoping to profit from painting Washington, and he sold replicas of each type for years; this 1795 canvas is where that trade begins. The sitter was then in his second term as president.","c":"In this earlier type Stuart sets down the man as he found him: tired, heavy-jowled and tight-lipped, with the red ground adding warmth the face does not offer. The painting is plain and direct, closer to evidence than to myth. It is easy to see why the calmer Athenaeum head, begun the following year, replaced it in the public mind, and also why a historian might prefer this one."},"nga:565":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, an older man with pale, peachy skin, a high-collared black coat and a cream-white ruffled collar looks out from a deep wine-red ground. His body is angled to the right and he turns his face to the viewer, gray eyes under pale gray brows, a hooked nose, jowls along the chin, flushed cheeks and a firmly set mouth. His white hair flares at the sides and is tied at the nape with a loosely painted ribbon outlined in black. The ground, painted with light strokes, deepens from scarlet around the face to black at the top corners.","i":"The sitter is George Washington, then in his second term as president and sixty-three years old. The picture belongs to the first type Stuart painted from life, in Philadelphia in 1795, known as the Vaughan type after early owners; this version carries the further name Sinclair. Stuart's later 'Athenaeum' head, which faces the other way, eventually became the standard image, but this earlier type shows Washington more severely, the face lined and the mouth set, closer to a record than an icon.","c":"Stuart's first Washington has a candor that the later versions lose. The face is heavy and lined, the mouth tight, the gaze direct and unflattering, and the wine-red ground sets off the pale head with theatrical simplicity. The black coat is only a mass, which suits it. What the Vaughan type lacks, compared with the Athenaeum head, is the settled dignity that made the latter an icon; that is also why many find it more human."},"met:12683":{"d":"A bust-length portrait in oil on canvas, about 73 by 60 centimeters, of the man named in the title as William Kerin Constable; the museum's keywords add 'man' and 'portrait' and give the name as William Constable. No fuller description is recorded, so his pose and dress are not stated here. The date, 1796, falls in Stuart's Philadelphia years, when he painted much of the city's merchant elite.","i":"If, as the keywords suggest, the sitter is the merchant William Constable (1752-1803), he was a partner of Robert Morris and a speculator in huge tracts of upstate New York land, with business in Philadelphia, then the capital. The museum's title uses a longer form of the name, and the record does not explain it. A portrait of 1796 would show him near the height of his fortune.","c":"Stuart's Philadelphia bust-lengths are quick and sure, and a merchant with a fortune to show would have expected a picture of this order: firm, plain and flattering without being fancy. The merit of such pictures is the head; the weakness is the sameness of coat and ground. A reliable, workmanlike likeness from the years when his formula was perfected."},"nga:195775":{"d":"The head and shoulders of an elderly man fill the canvas, his body turned toward the viewer's right while his head faces out. He has fair skin, flushed cheeks, prominent cheekbones and a calm expression, and his white hair is dressed in eighteenth-century curls. He wears a dark formal coat with a high collar and a white cravat. The ground is a blend of rich reds with hints of blue and brown, and no jewelry or objects are visible. The canvas is about 74 by 60 centimeters.","i":"Edward Shippen (1729-1806) was one of Philadelphia's leading judges, later Chief Justice of Pennsylvania, from a family that had helped found the city; his daughter Peggy married Benedict Arnold. In 1796, at about sixty-seven, he was an eminent survivor of the colonial elite, and the plain coat and cravat, with no props, present him as a grave, unshowy man. Stuart had been the city's leading portraitist since his arrival there in 1794.","c":"A good example of Stuart's late-1790s Philadelphia manner: a face seen steadily and painted in warm flesh and cool grays against a red ground that gives the white curls and cravat their punch. The portrait is plain and dignified, as the sitter demanded. Its weaknesses are those of the formula: the coat is a dark mass, the ground a stock device, and the personality is limited to calm. Stuart repeated this arrangement many times, and he did it well."},"met:12671":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Joseph Anthony Jr., about 76 by 62 centimeters, in oil on canvas. The museum's keywords record only 'man' and 'portrait', so his dress, pose and background are not described. The 'Jr.' implies that his father shared his name. The portrait is the pendant of one of his wife, Henrietta Hillegas, and both entered the Metropolitan Museum in 1905; they are dated within the same range, about 1795-98.","i":"The Anthony portraits come from the Philadelphia years, the late 1790s, when Stuart's studio was filled with the city's merchants and their wives. A Captain Joseph Anthony, perhaps the sitter's father, also sat to Stuart in 1794, and that portrait is in the National Gallery. Matched husband-and-wife portraits like these were typical commissions: they cost two fees, hung in the same room and recorded a marriage as a joint enterprise.","c":"A representative Stuart of the later 1790s: a quick, reliable likeness, a sober coat and a neutral ground, with the weight on the head. It does not strive for effect, which is both its virtue and its limit. The picture is better as half of a pair than alone, and its main appeal is the matter-of-fact way it presents a prosperous citizen as he wished to be seen."},"met:12672":{"d":"A large portrait of a nobleman, about 127 by 102 centimeters. The museum's keywords add a horse to the man, so he is shown with his horse, the traditional mark of a gentleman and soldier; the record gives no further description of pose, uniform or landscape, and none is invented here. The portrait is dated 1798, when the sitter, an exile from the French Revolution, was living in Philadelphia.","i":"Louis-Marie, vicomte de Noailles (1756-1804), fought in America under Rochambeau and helped draw up the terms of the British surrender at Yorktown in 1781; in 1789 he was among the liberal nobles who proposed giving up feudal privileges in the National Assembly. Driven out by the Revolution, he came to Philadelphia, where he worked with the banker William Bingham, and Stuart painted him in 1798. He later returned to French service and died in 1804 of wounds received at sea off Cuba.","c":"A grand portrait of an aristocrat by a republican painter, unusual in Stuart's American work for its scale and for the horse. Stuart is better known for heads than for horses or uniforms, and the animal is rarely his strongest ground; but the picture gives Noailles a firm presence, and his story, from Yorktown to exile and death in the Caribbean, gives it a historical charge that a plain likeness would lack."},"met:12677":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a woman, about 76 by 59 centimeters, named in the title as Henrietta Hillegas, the wife of Joseph Anthony Jr. The museum's keywords say only 'woman' and 'portrait', and her dress, pose and setting are not described. The picture is the pendant of her husband's portrait, acquired with it by the Metropolitan Museum in 1905, and is dated within the same range, about 1795-98.","i":"The title gives her by her husband's name, with her maiden name in brackets, a convention that preserved the family she came from; Hillegas was the name of a well-known Philadelphia family, and Michael Hillegas was the first Treasurer of the United States, though the museum does not say how she was related. The pair of portraits belongs to Stuart's Philadelphia years, when merchants' wives sat to him in numbers.","c":"A competent companion to her husband's portrait, best judged beside it. In portraits of women Stuart's strength is the delicacy of the flesh, and in the late 1790s he gave his sitters a natural, unforced air that the English portrait school often lacked. This one is a pleasant and typical example of his Philadelphia work, valuable in a pair and modest alone."},"nga:1108":{"d":"A round-faced man with prominent eyes and light-brown curly hair is shown in the upper body, looking slightly to his right. He wears an elegant red garment with fur trim around the collar, and one hand touches his clothing at the chest. The background is a dark, neutral shade that pushes the head and the red coat forward. The museum's text does not say whether he is seated or standing. The canvas is about 74 by 62 centimeters.","i":"'Counsellor' was a title for a barrister or advocate, and John Dunn was presumably a lawyer, though the museum's record does not say in which court or city. A fur-trimmed red garment is unusual in American portraits of the 1790s and may be a cloak, a ceremonial robe or a fashionable wrap; whatever its use, it lends the picture an air of Old World gravity. Stuart painted him about 1798, after his move to Philadelphia.","c":"The red coat with its fur collar makes a bold accent on a dark ground and a touch of the grand manner, and the face is round and lively, with prominent eyes. The hand at the chest is a theatrical convention, and the picture's drama rests on the red and the pose rather than on psychology. Costume does a good deal of the work, and the picture stands out from Stuart's plainer sitters for that reason."},"nga:3160":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as John Bill Ricketts, in oil on canvas, about 75 by 62 centimeters. The museum's keywords record him only as male, and the entry does not describe his dress or pose. The date range, 1795 to 1799, covers the years when Ricketts, the showman who opened the first circus in the United States, was a public figure in Philadelphia.","i":"John Bill Ricketts (died 1802) was a British-born equestrian who opened a riding school and circus in Philadelphia in 1793, with trained horses, acrobats and clowns; George Washington attended his shows. His amphitheater burned in 1799. A showman with a taste for social success may well have wanted a portrait by the city's leading painter, though the museum does not say what prompted this one or whether he is shown as a performer or a gentleman.","c":"A portrait of a notable rather than a typical sitter, interesting above all for who he was. Stuart's directness suits a man whose life was showing himself to the public, but a bust-length can say nothing of horses or ring. As a document of the early American entertainment world it is more informative than as a painting, though it keeps Stuart's fluent economy."},"met:12669":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as John R. Murray, in oil on canvas, about 75 by 61 centimeters, from about 1800. The museum's keywords record only 'man' and 'portrait' and give no description of pose, dress or setting, so none is claimed. The size is close to the standard three-quarter canvas, which Stuart used from the 1790s onward for the head-and-shoulders portraits that formed the bulk of his business.","i":"The title leaves the sitter's identity open: the record gives no occupation, family or place, and the middle initial is the only extra clue. About 1800 Stuart was settled in Philadelphia and painting the merchants, lawyers and officials of the capital; in 1803 he would move to Washington and then to Boston, following the patrons. As with many portraits of the period, the picture is now more useful as a likeness of an unknown sitter than as a biography.","c":"Stuart's three-quarter portraits of 1800 are at their ease: a quick, true head, an unfussy ground and little else. He did this so often and so well that it is hard to rank any one example; the pleasure is in the head's solidity, the weakness in the sameness. A typical, competent portrait of a decade in which his formula was perfected."},"nga:178198":{"d":"A man appears from the chest up against a dark brown ground, his body angled slightly left as he looks directly at the viewer. He has pale skin and rosy cheeks, deep-set dark eyes, a long straight nose and thin lips set in a neutral expression. His light gray hair is combed back and curled above the ears. He wears a black coat over a white, high-collared shirt and a white lace cravat. The canvas is about 73 by 60 centimeters, the usual size of the bust-length replicas Stuart produced of this image.","i":"This is a replica of Stuart's Athenaeum portrait of Washington, begun from life in 1796 and never finished; Stuart kept the original and used it to make copies for sale, and it later became the standard image of Washington and the portrait on the dollar bill. The museum dates this copy about 1800. Stuart made many such replicas, reportedly for about $100 each, and they helped to pay his debts.","c":"As replicas go, this one carries the Athenaeum head's calm authority: the face seen slightly from the side, the lips composed, the eyes steady. The handling is smooth and quiet, depending on the original's design, and copies in the series vary in quality since Stuart made them quickly. The image's force lies in its stillness; what a copy loses is spontaneity. It is better as an emblem than as an observation, but a good emblem."},"nga:30344":{"d":"A man sits in a chair upholstered in red, wearing a dark brown coat with brass buttons over a white shirt and a ruffled cravat. His light brown hair is curly, and in his left hand he holds a book with a golden cover. The background is plain green. The picture is painted on a wood panel, about 74 by 60 centimeters, rather than on canvas, a support Stuart used for many portraits in his later career.","i":"Horace Binney (1780-1875) was twenty when Stuart painted him, a recent Harvard graduate just beginning his legal career in Philadelphia. He became the city's most eminent lawyer, served in Congress in the 1830s and lived to ninety-five, one of the last survivors of the Federal generation. The book in his hand suits a young man of learning with a career still before him.","c":"A fresh, easy portrait of a young sitter that avoids the stiffness of formula. The warm brown coat against the red chair and green ground makes an unusually colorful trio, and the book gives the hand something to do. Because youth has few lines to record, the likeness is slightly bland, but the picture is a good example of Stuart's early work on a wood panel, with a bright palette and a firm surface."},"nga:43706":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Robert Liston, in oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters. The museum's keywords record him only as male, and no description of his dress or pose is given. The date, 1800, comes near the end of Liston's service as British minister to the United States, which began in 1796, and Stuart also painted his wife, Henrietta; the two pictures were made as a pair.","i":"Robert Liston (1742-1836), a Scottish diplomat, represented Britain in Philadelphia in the tense years after Jay's Treaty, when the old enemies were learning to deal with each other as equals. His wife Henrietta kept a journal of American life. A portrait of the departing minister and his wife was a natural souvenir for a diplomatic couple, and Stuart, the leading portraitist of the capital, was the obvious choice.","c":"A plain portrait of an official by a painter who understood diplomats: a firm head, a dark coat and little else. The sitter's character is reticent, as a diplomat's should be, and Stuart does not press it. The result is more a record of rank and reserve than of personality. It is an unpretentious, efficient picture, and it makes better sense beside his wife's portrait, which is livelier."},"nga:46077":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, her body angled slightly to the right, looking out at the viewer. She has pale skin with rosy cheeks, blue eyes, arched gray brows and slightly upturned pink lips; curly gray hair shows under a large straw-colored bonnet lined with blue and tied with a big blue bow. She wears a light blue garment with a darker blue neckline and a light brown shawl over her arms, and her left hand is taking off or putting on a brown glove. Behind her are dark brown and olive tones, with a patch of cloudy sky at the lower right.","i":"Henrietta Marchant Liston (1751-1828), wife of the British minister Robert Liston, was a keen botanist and gardener and an observer of American life, and her journal of the years 1796-1800 survives. The glove half on or off and the bonnet give the portrait the air of a visit or a departure, and the date, 1800, is that of the couple's leaving the United States.","c":"A bright, informal portrait that avoids formula: the sitter caught in a moment, the glove half on, the wide bonnet making a frame of blue and straw around a lively face. Stuart handles the straw and the blues with a freshness that anticipates nineteenth-century portraiture, and the patch of sky gives air. The ground and the shawl are less resolved, but the picture has an immediacy rare in his formal sitters."},"met:12658":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of Albert Gallatin, in oil on canvas, about 75 by 63 centimeters, close to the English 'three-quarter' size of 30 by 25 inches. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, so no description of his dress or pose is on record, and none is claimed. The date, about 1803, is the year of the Louisiana Purchase, which Gallatin, as Secretary of the Treasury, helped to finance.","i":"Albert Gallatin (1761-1849), born in Geneva, was Secretary of the Treasury under Jefferson and Madison from 1801 to 1814 and the architect of the Republican program of reducing the national debt. He later served as minister to France and Britain, helped found New York University and wrote pioneering studies of Native American languages. Stuart, painting in Washington in the early 1800s, was recording the men of Jefferson's administration as they took up office.","c":"A plain statesman's portrait in which the head is everything. Stuart's directness suits a Swiss-born financier who built his career on figures, and the likeness avoids the pomp of official portraiture. The picture is not among his most inventive, but it is a clear, reliable record of a major figure of the early republic, painted at a moment when the nation's finances were the subject of the day."},"met:12664":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of George Washington in oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait with Washington as the subject and give no further description, so the pose, coat and ground are not described here. The date and size point to a replica of the type Stuart called his Athenaeum portrait, in which Washington faces slightly left against a dark ground, in a black coat and white cravat.","i":"The Metropolitan's version, given in 1888 by the sugar-refining magnate and collector H. O. Havemeyer, is one of many replicas Stuart made of his Athenaeum portrait after he moved to Washington and then to Boston. A Washington portrait, in the years after the president's death in 1799, was a patriotic object in demand by public bodies and private families. The 1796 original, never finished, was kept by the artist and later bought by the Boston Athenaeum, which gave the type its name.","c":"Replicas vary, but this one belongs to the series that fixed Washington's face in the national memory: calm, level-eyed and unemphatic. Compared with Stuart's first, more severe Washington of 1795, the Athenaeum type is softer and more dignified, which is why the public preferred it. It works better as an emblem than as an observation, and it is a representative example of Stuart's most reproduced work."},"nga:1117":{"d":"A man sits in a red cushioned chair, his body turned slightly right and his head toward the viewer. He has a fair complexion with rosy cheeks, short curly brown hair, pale brows, a long straight nose and a weak chin. He wears a dark navy coat with gold buttons, a high-collared white shirt and a white cravat. His right hand rests on a red desk on a sheet of white paper, holding a quill pen. Behind him is a gray column framed by red curtains, with cloudy sky and green foliage at the lower left. The picture is on a wood panel.","i":"Edward Stow is little known today, and the museum's record gives no biography, but the setting of column, curtain and sky belongs to the old language of the official portrait, here applied to a private gentleman. The quill pen and paper suggest business or letters. Stuart painted him on a wood panel about 1803, the year he moved from Philadelphia to Washington, where he would also paint leading figures of Jefferson's government.","c":"The column, curtain and sky make this one of Stuart's more formally staged pictures, and the red of chair, desk and drapes gives it a rich, warm key. The face, with its weak chin and pale brows, is frankly recorded and not improved, which is typical of him. The staging is a little grand for the sitter, however, and the colors compete; it works better as a statement of aspiration than as a refined composition."},"nga:127695":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, slightly angled to the left, looking directly at the viewer. His skin is pale, his cheeks rosy, his eyes deep-set and dark, his nose long and straight and his thin lips set in a neutral expression. His light gray hair is combed back and curled above the ears, and he wears a black coat over a high-collared white shirt and a white lace cravat. The ground is dark brown. The canvas is about 74 by 61 centimeters, a bust-length format.","i":"Another replica of the Athenaeum portrait, dated about 1803, when Stuart had moved to Washington and the demand for Washington's face was strong among the capital's politicians and families. The credit line records a bequest of Mrs. Benjamin Ogle Tayloe, of a prominent Washington family. Washington had died in 1799, and such portraits served after his death as relics and as emblems of the republic's founding, in drawing rooms and public offices alike.","c":"One of many, and best judged as part of a series: the stillness is the point, and the replica must hold the face's calm without the freshness of the original. The formula was fixed and the handling is plain. The portrait's value is partly historical, as an object of civic devotion in the early republic, and partly in the sober, unfussy brushwork that made Stuart's Washington the one the public remembered."},"nga:177672":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a woman named in the title as Sarah Shippen Lea, the wife of Thomas Lea, in oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters. The museum's keywords describe it as a waist-length figure facing left but give no more detail of her dress, hair or setting, so none is claimed here. The date is given as about 1798, in Stuart's Philadelphia years, when his sitters were drawn largely from the city's merchant and professional families.","i":"Shippen is one of the great names of colonial and early republican Philadelphia, and Stuart also painted Edward Shippen in 1796, though the museum does not say how Sarah was related to him. The title records her as 'Mrs. Thomas Lea' after her husband, as portraits of married women typically did, with her own family name, Shippen, in the middle, a sign of the family she came from.","c":"Stuart's women are at their best when he lets the face speak and keeps the rest quiet, and the leftward pose of a waist-length figure leaves everything to the head. That is a limit as well as a virtue: in this type of picture the sitter's character is conveyed by the eyes and mouth alone, and a competent likeness is all that can be expected. A typical, appealing example of his Philadelphia manner."},"nga:1116":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, turned slightly left and facing the viewer, with pale skin, soft rosy cheeks, dark brown eyes and a neutral expression. Her curly brown hair is gathered up, with small curls on her forehead. She wears a short-sleeved black dress with a square, lace-trimmed neckline, and a bright red shawl is draped over her shoulders. The background is a solid dark brown. The painting is on a wood panel, about 72 by 58 centimeters.","i":"The title gives her as Ann Calvert Stuart Robinson, Mrs. William Robinson. Calvert is the family name of the Lords Baltimore, the founders of Maryland, and Calvert and Stuart together suggest a Chesapeake family, though the museum's record gives no biography. A black dress with a bright red shawl was a fashionable pairing in the early 1800s, when imported shawls were prized, and the shawl gives the portrait its one flash of color. The date is about 1804.","c":"The black dress and the red shawl give the picture a stark, strong color scheme, and Stuart keeps the face soft against it. The dark ground sets her forward and keeps the picture simple. It is a direct, serious portrait with fewer of the flattering notes that fashion might have required. A good example of his plain American manner on panel, and a small lesson in how much a single red accent can do."},"nga:1119":{"d":"A man sits in a red upholstered chair, his body turned to the right and his head toward the viewer with a neutral expression. He has pale skin, rosy cheeks, bright blue eyes, thin lips and gray hair curling around his ears. He wears a dark red coat with gold buttons and a white cravat, and holds a book in his right hand. The background has red drapery on one side and blue and yellow hues on the other, suggesting a cloudy sky. The canvas is about 73 by 62 centimeters.","i":"William Thornton (1759-1828), born in the British Virgin Islands and trained as a physician in Edinburgh, won the competition to design the United States Capitol in 1793 and later headed the Patent Office. A gifted amateur, he also dabbled in painting, steamboats and horse breeding. The book and the sky suit a man of ideas. Stuart painted him, and his wife Anna Maria, in 1804, while the Capitol was still under construction.","c":"The warm red coat against the red chair and drapery, with a cool patch of sky, gives this one of the livelier color schemes of Stuart's Washington portraits, and the pale blue eyes are the center of the likeness. The book and the sky are stock devices, and the sitter's polymath energy is hardly hinted at: Thornton, who wrote, invented and designed, looks here like any sober gentleman. A good portrait, short of a revealing one."},"nga:1120":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a woman named in the title as Anna Maria Brodeau Thornton, the wife of William Thornton, in oil on canvas, about 73 by 61 centimeters. The museum's keywords record her only as female, and the entry does not describe her dress, hair or pose. It is the pendant of her husband's portrait, dated 1804, and the two were painted when Stuart was working in Washington.","i":"Anna Maria Brodeau (1775-1865) married William Thornton in 1790 and as Mrs. Thornton became a prominent figure in Washington society; she kept a diary of life in the capital for decades, now a valued source on the early city. Stuart painted her and her husband in 1804, when the Thorntons stood near the center of the capital's small society. The title places her maiden name between her first name and her married name.","c":"As one half of a pair this portrait is best judged beside William's, and it probably earns the better place, since Stuart's women of the Washington period had a softness and ease that his official sitters often lack. It is also a sign of how fully he recorded the first citizens of the new capital: the designer of the Capitol and the diarist of its society, hanging together."},"nga:54975":{"d":"A woman sits looking off to the left with a composed expression. She has light skin with rosy cheeks, brown eyes and slightly upturned pink lips; her curly brown hair is piled on top of her head with a small floral clip. She wears a white short-sleeved dress with a low neckline and wide collar, a thin belt with gold and light-blue designs, and a translucent patterned cloth over her right forearm. Her other arm rests on the edge of a tan chair, her hand raised below her chin. The background is a plain muted brown.","i":"Eleanor Parke Custis, called Nelly (1779-1852), was Martha Washington's granddaughter, raised at Mount Vernon and treated by George Washington as a daughter. She married his nephew Lawrence Lewis on Washington's birthday in 1799, and the couple lived at Woodlawn, Virginia. Famous for her beauty and her music, she was painted by Stuart in 1804, a few years after Washington's death, in the plain, informal manner of his later years.","c":"A graceful, unforced portrait. The white dress, the translucent cloth and the lifted hand are painted with a light, rapid touch, and the pose, absorbed and at ease, avoids the posturing of many society portraits of the day. The plain brown ground keeps everything on face and gesture. The sitter's celebrated beauty is understated, and the result is more intimate than flattering, among Stuart's most attractive portraits from the Washington years."},"nga:42938":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, angled slightly to the left and looking directly at the viewer. He has pale skin and rosy cheeks, deep-set dark eyes, a long straight nose and thin, neutral lips. His light gray hair is combed back and curled above the ears, and he wears a black coat over a white, high-collared shirt with a white lace cravat. The background is dark brown. The canvas is about 74 by 61 centimeters, the usual size of Stuart's bust-length Washington replicas.","i":"This replica of the Athenaeum portrait, dated about 1803-05, came to the National Gallery as a gift of Jean McGinley Draper, who also gave the portraits of Mary and Ann Barry. Stuart began the original from life in 1796, left it unfinished and made copies from it for the rest of his career. The demand for Washington portraits in private houses was strong in the years after the president's death in 1799.","c":"A portrait like this is reliable rather than inspired, and it is meant to be. The head had been painted so many times that Stuart could do it from habit, and the brisk handling of the lace cravat and the white hair shows the ease of long practice. The strength of the replicas is their calm, and their danger is the repetition: a good copy keeps the quiet seriousness that made the type successful."},"nga:42939":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, a young woman with pale skin, pink cheeks and copper-red hair sits gazing at the viewer with a hint of a smile. Her eyes are wide-set and brown, her chin pointed; curls frame her forehead while the rest of her hair is pulled up. Her white gown has a low, scooped neckline trimmed with ruffles and is cinched under the bust, and a dove-gray shawl with straw-yellow accents wraps her shoulders. She sits on a dusky rose chair, one arm over its back, fingers interlaced. A rust-red cloth fills the background, pulled aside to show a lawn, trees and a streaked blue sky.","i":"The pendant of Mary Barry, this portrait uses the same stage-like device: a drawn curtain opening on a landscape, an English habit that Stuart used in London and revived for sitters who wanted something grander than a bust against brown. The high waist and light gown follow the fashion that crossed the Atlantic from France around 1800. The portrait is dated 1803-05, when Stuart was working in Washington and then Boston.","c":"Of the pair, this is the more elaborate, and the contrast of the white gown and gray shawl with the rust-red curtain is beautifully managed, with the glimpse of lawn and sky giving air. The pointed chin and copper hair keep the face from sweetness, and the interlaced fingers add a note of shyness. If there is a fault, it is the stock quality of the staged curtain, but it shows how well Stuart could dress a sitter."},"nga:42940":{"d":"A woman sits with a small white dog in her lap, her head turned slightly to the left. She has pale skin, rosy cheeks, faint eyebrows, light eyes and small pink lips curving up at the edges. Her curly, dark-brown hair is styled up, and she wears a short-sleeved white dress with lace at the neck. Behind her, brown tree trunks and dark green foliage stand against a blue sky with gray clouds. The canvas is about 74 by 62 centimeters.","i":"A small dog in a woman's lap is a long-standing emblem of fidelity and affection, and also a sign of leisure. The landscape background of trees and cloud is a legacy of English portraiture and shows Stuart lending an American sitter a touch of the old grand manner. Mary and Ann Barry, probably sisters, were painted as a pair about 1803-05, and the portraits came to the National Gallery together in a single gift; the museum does not record their parentage.","c":"A charming, easy portrait, with a lapdog that adds humor and a landscape that opens the picture. Stuart's women of the Washington and Boston period are plainer than those of his English years, and this one is typical: a fresh, unaffected face, with the white dress and the dog's coat forming a pale mass against the dark trees. It is less probing than his portraits of older sitters, but it is attractive."},"nga:568":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title, and in the museum's keywords, as John Randolph of Roanoke, in oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters. The museum describes him only as male, so the details of dress, pose and expression are not recorded here. The date, 1804-05, falls in Stuart's Washington stay and at the height of Randolph's early career in Congress.","i":"John Randolph of Roanoke (1773-1833), a Virginia planter and congressman, led the Republican majority in the House under Jefferson until 1806, when he broke with the president, and was famous for his savage wit, his oratory and his eccentric manner. He helped lead the impeachment of Justice Samuel Chase in 1804-05. Stuart painted him in those same years, when Randolph was in his early thirties and the most formidable speaker in the House.","c":"Randolph was famous for a boyish face and a shrill, cutting voice, and a painter of Stuart's candor was the right man to take his likeness. The best of Stuart's heads catch a sitter's vivacity, and this was a vivid subject. A bust-length leaves nothing for costume or setting, so the picture is a head and little else; it records a political personality of the early republic more than it conveys the wit his contemporaries described."},"cma:103366":{"d":"A woman wears a high-waisted dress, a pearl necklace and a hairstyle of cascading ringlets, the height of fashion around 1800. Her gaze is assured: her chin tilts upward while her eyes look down with confidence. The picture is oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters, and the museum's record gives no description of the ground or setting. It is dated about 1803-05, from the years when Stuart was in Washington and then Boston.","i":"The Cleveland Museum notes that Elizabeth Beltzhoover Mason's husband was a prosperous lawyer, an influential figure in Jefferson's administration and a plantation owner in Maryland, and that an inventory of 1807 lists about 185 people enslaved by the couple, from a newborn to a woman of 79. Her fashionable portrait, with its pearls and ringlets, was a product of that wealth, and a reminder of what the elegant portraits of the Federal elite rested on.","c":"Mrs. Mason's confident, slightly lofty look is the picture's best feature, and Stuart renders the ringlets and pearls with crisp economy. Pose and costume are standard Federal fare and the mood is more social than intimate, which is what the sitter and her husband would have wanted. As a likeness it is good rather than penetrating, and its larger interest is as a document of the class that commissioned Stuart in the Jefferson years."},"cma:156604":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Wilson Cary Nicholas, in oil on canvas, about 73 by 60 centimeters, dated about 1805. The museum's text is a biography rather than a description of the picture, so his dress, pose and setting are not recorded here. The size is the common three-quarter format Stuart used for the head-and-shoulders portraits of public men that filled his Washington and Boston years.","i":"Nicholas (1761-1820) came from a powerful Williamsburg family, left William and Mary in 1781 to join the Continental Army and commanded Washington's guard until 1783. He campaigned in Virginia for ratification of the Constitution, served in the House of Delegates, the U.S. Senate and the House, and was governor of Virginia from 1814. Ill health forced his retirement in 1819, and he died the next year in the home of his son-in-law Thomas Jefferson Randolph, Jefferson's grandson, and is buried at Monticello.","c":"A portrait of a man with a long public career, painted about 1805 when he was prominent in Jefferson's Virginia circle. Stuart's quick, candid manner suits a politician's face, and a portrait like this would have been a useful token among friends and supporters. It offers a likeness and a record; it is not one of Stuart's inventive works, and the sitter's many accomplishments have to be supplied by the viewer."},"nga:57542":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Samuel Alleyne Otis, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 72 by 58 centimeters. The museum's keywords record him as male, and no description of his dress or pose is on record. The date, 1811-13, falls late in Stuart's career, when he was working in Boston and, for many portraits, using wood panels instead of canvas.","i":"Samuel Allyne Otis (1740-1814) was a Boston merchant and politician, brother of the patriot James Otis and the historian Mercy Otis Warren, and from 1789 until his death the first Secretary of the United States Senate, keeping its records through the early republic. A portrait of 1811-13 shows him in his seventies, near the end of that long service. The museum's records spell his middle name both Alleyne and Allyne.","c":"Late Stuart on panel: in portraits of this period the face is typically modeled with a finer, drier touch against a plain ground. For a man of the revolutionary generation the result has authority, and Stuart treats Otis as a respected survivor who had lived through the whole formation of the republic. The smooth panel surface makes the picture cooler than his canvases of the 1790s, and a little less alive."},"nga:52387":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Benjamin Tappan, in oil on a wood panel, about 73 by 59 centimeters. The museum's keywords record him as male, and no description of his dress or pose is given. It is the pendant of the portrait of his wife, Sarah Homes Tappan, both dated 1814, and the Cleveland Museum links Stuart's use of wood in these years to a shortage of canvas during the War of 1812, when British ships blockaded imports.","i":"Benjamin Tappan (1747-1831) was a goldsmith and businessman of Northampton, Massachusetts, and with Sarah Homes the parent of a remarkable family: Benjamin the Ohio senator and the abolitionist brothers Arthur and Lewis Tappan, who helped found the American Anti-Slavery Society. A portrait of the parents in 1814, when the sons were making their way in the world, is a record of the family's origin. The museum says no more of him.","c":"A solid, unglamorous late Stuart. The wood panel gives the paint a smooth surface that can make flesh look hard, and the pair of portraits of a craftsman and his wife make no concessions to fashion. What they offer is plainness and directness, qualities that suited sitters from a New England town and that Stuart, at the end of his career, could still supply with ease."},"nga:52388":{"d":"A woman sits in a chair, shown from the waist up, in a white ruffled bonnet that covers her wavy light brown hair. She has fair skin, thin lips, a long nose and a pointed chin, and wears a deep red gown with a high white collar. The chair has a blue cushion and a wooden frame with curved arms, and the background is a gradient from brown at the top to tan at the bottom. The painting is on a wood panel, about 73 by 59 centimeters.","i":"Sarah Homes Tappan was the wife of Benjamin Tappan and mother of the abolitionists Arthur and Lewis Tappan. Portraits of such women, in a cap or bonnet and a plain gown, show the sober dignity of New England matrons of the early republic. The red gown is the one strong note in an otherwise plain scheme, and the bonnet frames the face. Stuart painted her, with her husband, in 1814.","c":"A forthright portrait of an older woman: the long nose and pointed chin are recorded without softening, and the white bonnet and collar frame the face crisply against the deep red. The gradient ground is plain and quiet. The result is less brilliant than Stuart's Philadelphia women, but it has the honesty of a mature craftsman's work, and the red gown makes a strong chord with the blue cushion."},"met:12662":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as David Sears Jr., in oil on canvas, about 69 by 60 centimeters, dated about 1815. The museum's keywords record him only as a man, and no description of his dress or pose is on record. The canvas is smaller than the English three-quarter size Stuart used earlier, and the Metropolitan Museum acquired the picture in 1881 as a gift from several gentlemen.","i":"The Sears family were among the wealthiest merchants and landowners of Boston, and 'Jr.' marks the son of a man of the same name. Stuart had settled in Boston in 1805 and spent the rest of his life there; by about 1815 he was the city's leading portraitist, painting its merchants, lawyers and clergy. A portrait by Stuart was a sign of standing in Boston, as it had been in Philadelphia.","c":"Late Stuart, from his Boston years: the face drawn with directness and a thin, dry brush, and the setting reduced to almost nothing. That reduction is part of his late style, in which the head is nearly the whole picture, and it can look economical or merely thin. The portrait is typical rather than special, and its interest lies in the sitter's family name and place in Boston's history."},"met:12666":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Henry Rice, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 67 by 55 centimeters, dated about 1815. The museum's keywords record him only as a man, and no description of his dress, pose or background is given. It is smaller than most of Stuart's canvases, and was bought by the Metropolitan Museum in 1896.","i":"The panel portraits Stuart painted around 1815 belong to his Boston years, and the Cleveland Museum links his use of wood in this period to the British blockade of the War of 1812, which hindered the import of canvas. The record gives no occupation or biography for Henry Rice, who was probably one of the Boston merchants, lawyers or clergymen who made up Stuart's clientele.","c":"On wood, Stuart's flesh has a smooth, enamel-like finish, and the plain ground concentrates all attention on the head, a late manner that can look severe. At about two-thirds of a meter high it is a modest picture, and its interest lies in the economy of the face and in the ease of a painter who, after forty years, could make a convincing likeness with very little."},"nga:42933":{"d":"A clean-shaven, pale-skinned man sits looking out from a peanut-brown ground, his shoulders angled left and his head turned to the viewer, blue eyes under dark arched brows. His nose is bumped, his cheeks fleshy and flushed, his jowls loose, with bags under the eyes and crow's feet; his thin pink lips are parted. Gray hair curls like bushes at either side of a balding head, with long white sideburns. He wears a black high-collared coat over a white ruffled shirt, and his left elbow rests on a wooden armchair, the hand in a loose fist, the cuff lined with lace.","i":"John Adams (1735-1826) was the second president of the United States and, with Jefferson, one of the last survivors of the Revolution; he died on July 4, 1826, fifty years to the day after the Declaration. The wide date range, about 1800 to 1815, shows that the dating is not settled. Stuart has painted the old statesman without flattery, the lace at the cuff the only luxury, and the companion portrait of Abigail Adams completes the founding couple.","c":"Few presidential portraits are as unflattering or as alive. The loose jowls, the bags, the bumped nose and the parted lips are set down without softening, and Stuart makes the glance both shrewd and tired. The costume is a dark mass and the chair and ground barely there, but the head is all one needs. It is among his most characterful late portraits, and a pointed contrast to the composed dignity of his Washington."},"nga:42934":{"d":"A woman sits in an armchair, shown from the hips up, her body angled right and her head turned to the viewer from under raised, pointed brows, her dark eyes lined with a few wrinkles. She has deeply flushed cheeks, a long, pointed nose, slightly sagging jowls and thin pale pink lips pressed in a line; chestnut ringlets curl onto her forehead under a frilly white bonnet. Her beet-red gown is cut low but filled in with opaque white fabric to a frilled collar, and a sheer white shawl patterned with scrolling leaves drapes her shoulders. The mustard and gold chair fills the width of the picture.","i":"Abigail Smith Adams (1744-1818), wife of John Adams and first lady from 1797 to 1801, was one of the most formidable letter-writers of her age; in 1776 she urged her husband to 'remember the ladies' in the new laws. The portrait pairs with his and shares its uncertain date, about 1800 to 1815. Her gown and shawl are fashionable but modest, and the face is presented without any attempt at glamour.","c":"A plain, severe portrait of a woman who is not painted as a beauty. The long nose, flushed cheeks and set mouth are drawn with Stuart's usual candor, and the red gown and the shawl of white scrolls are rich accents; the large chair makes her look small and firm within it. It lacks the drama of her husband's portrait, but its quiet strength fits the sitter, and the pair are among the best loved of the early republic."},"nga:56915":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, looking at the viewer, his body turned slightly left. He has fair skin, blue eyes, short gray wavy hair, a cleft chin and prominent ears, and wears a black coat with a high collar and a white cravat. The background is a blend of muted browns, pale pink and gray. The picture is a small panel, about 65 by 55 centimeters, dated about 1817, the year the sitter became president.","i":"James Monroe (1758-1831), fifth president of the United States, served from 1817 to 1825, the period called the Era of Good Feelings. A veteran of the Revolution, wounded at Trenton, he had been minister to France and Britain, a negotiator of the Louisiana Purchase, Secretary of State and Secretary of War before reaching the presidency, and his name is attached to the Monroe Doctrine of 1823. The portrait shows him at the start of that term, in plain black.","c":"Stuart's Monroe is plain, spare and honest: a cleft chin and big ears painted without any attempt to enlarge a mild man's presence, against a soft, cloudy ground. It lacks the flair of his best Washington portraits, and the late panel manner is cool and dry. But it is a faithful and unpretentious record of a president whose style was modest, and the more convincing for it."},"nga:1111":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a man named in the title as Commodore Thomas Macdonough, in oil on a wood panel, about 73 by 57 centimeters. The museum's keywords record him only as male, and no further description is on record, so his uniform and expression are not stated here. The date, about 1815 to 1818, follows the victory on Lake Champlain in 1814 that made his name.","i":"Macdonough (1783-1825) won the Battle of Lake Champlain, off Plattsburgh, New York, on 11 September 1814, defeating a British squadron and helping to end a British invasion from Canada, and he was honored with promotion and a gold medal from Congress. 'Commodore' was the courtesy title of an officer commanding a squadron. Heroes of the War of 1812 were popular subjects for portraitists, and the most famous painter in the country was a natural choice.","c":"A competent portrait of a national hero by the country's most famous portraitist. The format is modest, a bust-length on panel, and the likeness is the point. Stuart gives him no battle scene or sea, which keeps the picture sober, and the result is an image of authority without bravura. Beside the heroic imagery of the period it is plain, but for that reason more believable."},"cma:109246":{"d":"A pair of portraits painted on wood panel shows a husband and wife, Dr. James Stuart and Mary Campbell Stuart, each seated in an Empire-style chair upholstered in brown brocade; the panel recorded here measures about 83 by 66 centimeters. The museum's text concentrates on Mary and does not describe the sitters' dress or expressions. The sitters share the painter's surname by coincidence, and the museum states that they were unrelated to him.","i":"Mary Campbell was the great-granddaughter of Colonel Peter Bard, a New Jersey Supreme Court judge, and she married Dr. James Stuart. The museum notes that Stuart painted the pair on panel because the British naval blockade in the War of 1812 hindered the import of canvas, the material he preferred. The Empire chair, a style that spread from France after 1800, shows the couple's up-to-date taste.","c":"A good example of late Stuart in its most basic form: a pair, a chair, a head. Painted on wood by necessity, the portraits have the smooth, hard surface wood gives and lack the soft richness of his Philadelphia canvases. The pair shows how a painter famous for Washington could still give a doctor and his wife, strangers who happened to share his name, the same plain, steady attention he gave presidents."},"cma:109247":{"d":"This vertical panel shows Dr. James Stuart seated in an Empire chair upholstered in brown brocade, the same chair in which the painter placed his wife, Mary Campbell Stuart, in the companion portrait. It is oil on wood, about 83 by 66 centimeters, and dates from about 1815. Painter and sitter share a surname but were unrelated. Stuart painted the pair on panel because the naval blockade of the War of 1812 made his preferred canvas hard to import.","i":"A portrait of this kind was meant to record standing as much as appearance. The sitter's title, Dr., marks a professional man, and hanging his likeness beside his wife's turned two panels into a small record of a household. The chair is an Empire model, a fashion drawn from Napoleonic France, so even the furniture suggests an up-to-date family. Little else about him is documented here, so the picture is best read as a respectable civic likeness.","c":"Judged as a pair, the two panels are consistent rather than inspired: the same chair, format and manner serve both sitters. Stuart's real gift, a soft-edged, living treatment of flesh, is what to look for in the face, while the setting stays secondary. It is a dependable product of a busy portrait practice and valuable chiefly as the husband's half of a documented pair, not as a standout among his work."},"cma:109248":{"d":"A vertical portrait on a wooden panel shows Mary Stuart, born Campbell, seated in an Empire chair upholstered in brown brocade. It is one of a pair: the painter also portrayed her husband, Dr. James Stuart, in the same chair, and neither sitter was related to him despite the shared surname. Stuart used wood rather than canvas, his preferred support, because the British naval blockade of the War of 1812 hindered the import of canvas. The panel measures about 83 by 66 centimeters and dates from about 1815.","i":"Companion portraits of husband and wife were a staple commission for comfortable families in the early republic, made to hang together at home and carry a likeness down the generations. The museum notes Mary Stuart's descent from Colonel Peter Bard, a New Jersey Supreme Court judge, the kind of lineage such a picture quietly records. The Empire chair, a style borrowed from Napoleonic France, probably shows a household keeping up with current fashion.","c":"Stuart made his name with the faces of presidents, and even in a modest pair like this the priority is the same: a convincing head, with costume and furniture in support. The wooden panel was a wartime expedient rather than a choice, and the result is a sound, unshowy society portrait. It will not rival his best likenesses, but it shows the dependable craft that kept him in steady demand."},"met:12676":{"d":"A portrait of Elizabeth Williams Sigourney, titled by her married name, as was usual in the period. It is oil on canvas, about 69 by 56 centimeters, painted around 1820, a size suited to a sitter's own house rather than a public room. Gilbert Stuart had been established in Boston since 1805, and his late portraits of local sitters typically concentrate the finest handling on the head, leaving costume and background summary.","i":"A married woman's portrait of about 1820 recorded her place in a family and a community, usually with quiet dress and a plain setting that suggest respectability and good taste rather than display. The museum identifies the sitter as Elizabeth Williams Sigourney, wife of Andrew Sigourney. Beyond her name, few particulars are documented, so reading the picture depends on the conventions of the genre: a likeness made for family memory.","c":"Stuart's late style is at its most persuasive in the face, built from soft, blended touches of warm and cool flesh tones, and a portrait like this stands or falls on that. The rest is deliberately economical. It belongs to the steady flow of Boston commissions that sustained him in his final years, and it is a pleasing, understated example rather than an ambitious one."},"met:12681":{"d":"A portrait of the painter Washington Allston, made about 1819 to 1820 by another painter, Gilbert Stuart. It is oil on canvas, about 61 by 55 centimeters, a compact format that keeps attention on the sitter's head. Allston was known as the American Titian for his rich color, and the picture shows one leading American artist through the eyes of the senior portraitist of Boston.","i":"Allston (1779 to 1843), born in South Carolina and trained at the Royal Academy in London, returned to Boston in 1818 as the foremost American painter of the Romantic generation, admired for visionary biblical and literary pictures. A portrait of an artist by an artist is also a statement about community: it shows Boston's small circle of painters recognizing one another, at a time when American art was still seeking a place beside European tradition.","c":"Portraits between colleagues tend to be frank, and this one leans on Stuart's usual strengths: a quick, assured head built from soft, blended flesh tones, with little fuss elsewhere. As a study of character it holds more interest than many commissioned likenesses of the period, though at this scale it remains a modest picture rather than a major statement from either man."},"nga:562":{"d":"A man appears from the chest up, angled slightly right with his face turned to the viewer. He has a fair complexion with rosy cheeks, light-colored eyes and a straight nose, and his white hair is curly and cropped close to his head. He wears a dark high-collared jacket and a white cravat tucked into it. The background is a solid, deep red, and no accessories are visible. The picture is on a wood panel, about 71 by 58 centimeters.","i":"The sitter is thought to be Joseph Coolidge (1747-1820), a Boston merchant whose son Joseph Jr. married Ellen Randolph, a granddaughter of Thomas Jefferson, in 1825. The date, 1820, places the portrait near the end of both lives, the sitter's and Stuart's, since the painter died in 1828. The deep red ground and the plain dark coat suit an elderly man presented without props, as respected age.","c":"The deep red ground against a white head and cravat has a stark simplicity, and in this late panel Stuart reduces the portrait to its essentials. The face, with its cropped white curls, is well observed, and the dark coat leaves nothing to compete. It has the spare power of a painter who has done the thing many times, though the likeness is more dignified than revealing. A fine small example of Stuart's last years."},"nga:56913":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a balding man in a port-red coat and vest turns his head to look at us from the corners of gray eyes under heavy brown brows. His nose is bumped, his lips bow-shaped, his cheeks fleshy and flushed, with sagging jowls. White hair sweeps up around a bald patch painted with a white sheen and curls over the ears; longer hair is tied back with a black ribbon. A white cravat and gold buttons complete the dress, set against an earth-brown ground that lightens to the lower right.","i":"The sitter is John Adams (1735 to 1826), second president of the United States and a leading voice for independence. The candid record of heavy brows, bald crown and sagging jowls fits the reputation of a blunt, learned lawyer. The date of about 1821 falls late in both men's lives, and the panel probably repeats a likeness Stuart developed from an earlier sitting.","c":"Few of Stuart's founders look as unretouched as this one. The bald crown, sagging jowls and shadowed eyes are recorded without softening, and the red coat gives the one saturated note against a quiet ground. The result is a likeness with character rather than ceremony. It suits a man famed for bluntness and shows why Stuart's honesty with flesh made him the portraitist the new republic wanted."},"nga:56914":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a clean-shaven man with rosy skin and curled white hair looks off to our right against a dark olive-green curtain. His shoulders turn slightly to the right; his eyes are deep-set and blue, the brow on our left arched, and the flushed cheeks and jowls sag around a closed mouth. A black ribbon beyond the shoulder may tie his hair back. He wears a white ruffled shirt under a black jacket, lit from our left. The panel is about 65 by 54 centimeters.","i":"The sitter is James Madison (1751 to 1836), fourth president of the United States and the chief architect of the Constitution. Portraits of the founding generation were prized as civic relics, and Stuart's likenesses of the early presidents circulated in many versions. The c. 1821 date, long after Madison's presidency ended in 1817, probably places this panel among later versions of a likeness first made from life years earlier.","c":"The dark green curtain and strong side light give the old statesman presence with very few means, and the face carries the picture. Stuart paints the flesh with a warm, slightly high-colored touch that reads as life rather than icon. The costume is almost formulaic, as it is in the other panels of this group, but the economy suits the purpose: an image of a person that could be repeated and remain recognizable."},"nga:56916":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a clean-shaven man with silvery gray hair, in a velvety black high-necked jacket over a ruffled white shirt, turns his face slightly toward us from a body angled to our left. His eyes are gray under gently arched brows, his nose long, his thin lips set in a straight line, with shadows under the jowls. A black ribbon ties his bushy-curled hair, and light from our left puts a golden glow on the light brown ground.","i":"The sitter is George Washington (1732 to 1799), commander of the Continental Army and first president of the United States. Stuart painted him from life in the mid-1790s, and those images became the accepted likeness; the painter then made many replicas for admirers. A panel dated about 1821 is therefore best understood as a late repetition of that established face, produced decades after the first president's death.","c":"Replicas can be dull, but this one keeps a calm authority: the golden glow behind the head and the velvet black of the coat set the pale, firm face forward. Stuart's Washington is the republic's official face, and it depends on reserve, the closed mouth and level gaze, rather than rhetoric. As a late variant it lacks the tension of the life portraits, yet it remains a persuasive, dignified image."},"nga:69391":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a clean-shaven man with rosy skin and curly pewter-gray hair looks at or toward us against a caramel-brown ground. His body angles to our left, his face turns a little our way, and his honey-brown eyes sit under straight, faint brows above a long, prominent nose and closed pink lips. His hair is parted in the middle and curls around his face and down the back of his neck. A bright white ruffled shirt shows under a velvety black jacket, and light falls from our left.","i":"The sitter is Thomas Jefferson (1743 to 1826), principal author of the Declaration of Independence and third president. Jefferson sat to Stuart in 1805; a panel dated about 1821 is therefore best understood as a later version of that likeness, made for the market in portraits of the founders. The loosely curling gray hair and dark coat suit the republican simplicity he cultivated.","c":"The warm brown ground and soft side light suit the sitter: nothing in the dress or setting competes with a long, intelligent face. Stuart models the head with delicacy and leaves the clothing summary, a division of labor that gives the founders' portraits their quiet force. It is a late, repeated image and has some of the smoothness of repetition, but it is a persuasive, humane likeness."},"met:12668":{"d":"A portrait of James Monroe, fifth president of the United States, painted by Gilbert Stuart about 1820 to 1822, while Monroe was still in office. The oil on canvas measures about 102 by 81 centimeters, noticeably larger than the small panel portraits Stuart made of other early presidents, and so allows the figure more room than a simple head-and-shoulders likeness. It is a late work from the painter's Boston years.","i":"Monroe (1758 to 1831) served as president from 1817 to 1825, during the so-called Era of Good Feelings, and gave his name to the 1823 doctrine warning European powers against new colonies in the Americas. He was also a Revolutionary War officer wounded at Trenton. Portraits of sitting presidents were civic images as well as personal ones, and Stuart, famed for his likeness of Washington, was the natural choice.","c":"Stuart's late presidential portraits depend on the head, and the larger canvas gives him room to let it breathe against a quieter body and setting. The interest is in the face rather than in any ceremonial display; Monroe is shown as a sober public servant, not a monarch. It is a workmanlike, convincing likeness, though it rarely achieves the intensity of his earlier life portraits."},"nga:34152":{"d":"A portrait of Charlotte Morton Dexter, identified as the wife of Andrew Dexter, in oil on a wooden panel about 74 by 60 centimeters. Its record gives two dates, 1808 and about 1825, and credits Gilbert Stuart together with an unnamed nineteenth-century hand, which points to a picture begun in one period and altered or completed in another. It should therefore be read as a work with more than one layer of authorship.","i":"A portrait of a married woman around 1808 would have been commissioned from a leading portraitist, and Stuart, settled in Boston since 1805, was one of the most sought-after. The later date may mean the image was updated or finished years afterward; the surviving record here does not say why. The museum gives her maiden name as well as her married style, so the sitter is recorded as an individual rather than only as a wife.","c":"The record itself is the lesson: two dates and a credit to an unnamed second hand tell the viewer to treat the surface with caution, since part of what is seen may not be Stuart's. Where his own hand survives, one expects the soft, blended flesh and direct presence of his portraits. The picture is best valued as evidence of how portraits were revised, less as a pure example of his style."},"cma:294155":{"d":"A small vertical tempera on canvas, about 38.5 by 26.5 centimeters, shows the evangelist Saint Matthew responding with energy and awe to an angel who presents the divinely inspired text of his Gospel. In Christian art the angel is Matthew's traditional emblem, the winged man that stands for a Gospel opening with Christ's human ancestry. William Blake painted it in 1799 in tempera, a water-based medium that gives a matte, drawing-like surface.","i":"Evangelists are normally shown writing while an angel dictates or hovers nearby, a sign that the Gospel is God's word through a human author. Blake takes the idea literally. He reported seeing angels from childhood and treated imagination as divine inspiration, and the museum notes that the saint's response echoes Blake's own attitude toward inspiration. A small group of patrons commissioned such pictures from him and let his visions run free.","c":"Blake's drawing is simplified and strongly outlined, with no concern for academic finish, and the saint's charged response, not anatomical polish, carries the picture. The small scale concentrates the intensity, so that it reads like a page from a visionary book. Contemporary critics found this manner eccentric; today the lack of polish is precisely what gives these small temperas their charge, though the figure drawing can look awkward."},"nga:43198":{"d":"Thirteen figures surround a long table in a small horizontal picture, about 30 by 48 centimetres, painted in tempera on canvas. The skin of the figures, some of their clothing and the tabletop are golden yellow against a nearly black background, with touches of aquamarine, salmon pink and rust red. At the centre Christ, long-haired and bearded, with a gold starburst around his head and shoulders, reclines with his palms held out. A young disciple, presumably John, reclines at his side, and at the lower right a man in brick red turns his back and counts coins in a sack.","i":"At the Last Supper, in Matthew 26 and the other Gospels, Christ tells his disciples that one of them will betray him, and Judas leaves to do so; Blake shows Judas turned away, counting his silver. Blake reclines the figures in the ancient Roman way instead of seating them as Leonardo had. He disliked oil painting and championed tempera and fresco-like methods, and he made a series of small biblical temperas around 1799 to 1800, probably for his patron Thomas Butts.","c":"Blake's Last Supper is unlike any Renaissance version: instead of a stable row of apostles, he gives a glowing, almost nocturnal gathering in which the golden figures seem lit from within and Christ's starburst is the focus of the light. The effect is intense and strange; the drawing is flat and the figures stiff, but that is deliberate, a rejection of illusionistic oil painting. It is small, but its spiritual force is real."},"met:435671":{"d":"A small horizontal picture, about 27 by 38 centimeters, shows the angel Gabriel appearing to the priest Zacharias to announce that his wife will bear a son, John the Baptist. Blake made it with pen and black ink and tempera on canvas prepared with glue size, a combination that makes the work read as drawing as much as painting. It dates from 1799 to 1800.","i":"In Luke's Gospel the angel finds Zacharias serving at the altar of incense in the Temple and promises that his elderly, childless wife Elizabeth will bear a son to be named John; because Zacharias doubts, he is struck dumb until the child's birth. The scene opens the Gospel and the story of John, forerunner of Christ. Its size and date match the small biblical temperas Blake made around 1799 to 1800 for his patron Thomas Butts.","c":"Blake treats the visit as a vision: an angel and a human figure are bound by line, without the atmospheric depth that academic painters sought. The matte, thinly worked surface and strong contours give the scene the clarity of a woodcut. Anatomy is stylized in Blake's habitual way, which some viewers find awkward and others compelling; the concentrated drama is the point, and it is characteristic of the best of his small temperas."},"nga:30221":{"d":"An old man with a long white beard and flowing white hair sits in a gold-toned room, surrounded by three young women, in a small horizontal picture about 27 by 38 centimetres. He holds his arms out at his sides, with the thumb and middle finger of each hand joined in a ring, and looks down under bushy brows. Two blond young women lean against his sides, and the third sits in front with her back to the viewer and her face on his knees. A roundel and two square panels with faint figures hang on the wall behind. The surface is cracked throughout.","i":"In the Book of Job, after his trials, Job is restored to prosperity and has seven sons and three daughters, to whom, unusually, he gives an inheritance alongside their brothers (Job 42). Blake took a particular interest in this passage, and in his later engravings of Job the old man tells his story to his daughters while images of his sufferings decorate the wall behind them. The faint panels here probably have the same meaning, which makes the picture a scene of wisdom handed on.","c":"Blake's flat, linear manner and the gold-toned glow make a sweet, formal image: the old man's gesture, the girls' bowed heads and the dotted eyes give it a strange, ritual calm. The medium, a tempera he mixed himself, has cracked across the surface, a reminder of how fragile his experiments were. The design is simple but the feeling is intense, and as an early version of a subject he later engraved, it shows how his ideas matured."},"nga:71394":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 92 by 30 centimetres, in watercolour and chalk on wood, shows a woman in a flowing dress who faces the viewer, one arm extended downward and the other raised above her head to hold the edge of a cloth that drapes behind her. Another cloth covers her head, and a crescent moon appears on her gown. She is barefoot, and in her lowered hand she holds a wand topped with three flowers or buds. The background is plain beige paper, and the lines are drawn in pale grey.","i":"The title and the crescent moon identify the figure as a personification of evening, the time of day when the light fades and the moon rises. The flowers on her wand may be poppies, the traditional flowers of sleep, though they could be other buds. Blake was in his sixties when he made this around 1820 to 1825, devoting himself to private commissions and the great illustration projects of his last years. The tall format suggests a decorative panel.","c":"A delicate late work. The figure is built from flowing, pale outline, and the restrained colour lets her dissolve almost into the paper, which gives a feeling of dusk and sleep. The flowing line is typically Blake's, elegant and slightly strange. It looks slight and unfinished beside the Job engravings, but the grace of the pose and the subtlety of the symbolism are real, and it shows how much feeling he could draw from a few lines."},"cma:108555":{"d":"In a wooded landscape a playful cupid tugs Pan, the goat-legged Greek god of the woods, toward Venus, goddess of love, while nymphs share the scene. The oil on canvas, about 97 by 127.5 centimeters, is a cabinet-size picture of the mid-1620s, painted in Rome soon after Poussin arrived there. The setting, inspired by the countryside around Rome, stands for the idyllic paradise of Pan's home.","i":"The picture is a visual pun on the Latin tag amor vincit omnia, love conquers all, which echoes a line of Virgil. In Greek, pan means all, so the god of the woods stands for everything that love overcomes. Poussin often hid such learned jokes in his mythologies, aimed at educated Roman collectors. The museum notes he took up popular, profitable mythological subjects like this after money troubles on his arrival in Rome.","c":"An early Roman work, and a lively one: a learned conceit is carried by clear, readable action, with figures set in a landscape whose warm handling is usually seen as indebted to Titian's pastoral manner. The severity of Poussin's later classicism has not yet set in, and the picture is more sensuous than the paintings that made his name. It is a charming display of wit rather than a deep statement."},"met:442488":{"d":"Christ prays in the Garden of Gethsemane on the night before his arrest, with an angel present, as the museum's subject keywords record. This is a small painting in oil on a copper plate, about 61 by 49 centimeters, made in Rome in the mid-1620s. Copper offers a hard, smooth surface that takes fine detail and a gleaming finish, which made it a favorite support for small, precious pictures meant for close viewing.","i":"In the Gospels Christ withdraws to the Mount of Olives to pray, asking that the cup of suffering pass from him; in Luke's account an angel comes to strengthen him. The angel often carries or points to the chalice, a reference to the Passion and the Eucharist. Pictures of the scene invited private meditation on Christ's human fear and obedience, which suits a small, intimate cabinet picture on copper.","c":"Small, early and on copper, the picture belongs to the years when Poussin was still absorbing Venetian color and Roman drama. In such works the invention lies in how few figures carry the feeling of the scene. It is quieter and less ambitious than his large altarpieces, and its chief interest is as evidence of how the controlled manner of his maturity was built."},"cma:130392":{"d":"The Holy Family is on the move: Mary, Joseph and a child no longer a baby, who reaches toward a vision of a cross carried by angels. Because Jesus is clearly past infancy, the museum reads the scene as the less common subject of the family's return from Egypt rather than the Flight. The oil on canvas, about 134 by 99 centimeters, was painted in Rome around 1627.","i":"The cross borne by angels ties the child's future to Herod's cruelty, foreshadowing both the Crucifixion and the slaughter of the innocent children that sent the family into exile. In Matthew's Gospel the family settles in Nazareth after Herod's death. A homely journey becomes a sign of the Passion, a standard device of Christian art that asks the viewer to see the whole story of salvation in an early moment.","c":"A sober, humane treatment in which the idea, a child reaching toward his own fate, matters more than any picturesque incident. The museum notes that Poussin modeled bodies with wax figures and wetted paper or fabric, and that concern for believable folds and light is the kind of care to look for. The picture rewards close reading more than it dazzles at first sight, which is typical of the artist."},"met:437328":{"d":"King Midas, whose wish that everything he touched should turn to gold had become a curse, washes in the river Pactolus to be rid of it. The vertical canvas, about 97.5 by 72.7 centimeters, places the king at the water's source; the museum's keywords also list a man and a child among the figures, and a river. It was painted around 1627, in Poussin's early Roman years.","i":"Ovid tells the story in the Metamorphoses: the god Bacchus grants Midas a wish, the king asks that all he touches become gold and nearly starves, and he is sent to bathe at the source of the Pactolus, whose sands afterward carry gold. It is a moral fable about greed and its cure, congenial to a painter who took classical stories seriously.","c":"Poussin's Ovidian subjects of the late 1620s usually favor a clear telling over comic exaggeration, and the cure of Midas suits that approach: the story is a lesson, not a joke. The picture belongs to the group of mythologies by which the young painter built his name in Rome. It has less architectural order than his later landscapes and may feel more intimate than grand."},"met:438025":{"d":"A small, nearly square canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, shows the Virgin and Child with Joseph and the infant John the Baptist, a subject the museum's keywords also link to the Rest on the Flight into Egypt. Poussin painted it about 1627, in his early years in Rome. Such an intimate group is typical of pictures made for private devotion rather than for a church.","i":"The infant Baptist regularly joins the Holy Family in Italian art to foreshadow the future: he will announce Christ and baptize him in the Jordan. Historically he was not on the Flight into Egypt, so his presence is a devotional addition that lets the viewer see Christ's future ministry alongside the quiet family scene. The grouping suited private chapels and domestic prayer, where a calm, close image was more welcome than drama.","c":"Poussin's Holy Families habitually recall Raphael, whose triangular groupings and gentle gestures he studied in Rome, though filtered through his own growing classical discipline. The strengths of such a picture are balance and a quiet, readable emotion; the risk is a certain stiffness of pose. It is an early, thoughtful work rather than an emotional high point of his career."},"nga:46470":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 134 by 98 centimeters, painted around 1630 to 1632 and showing the Assumption of the Virgin, the Catholic belief that Mary was taken bodily into heaven at the end of her life. Pictures of the subject usually divide into two zones, with the Virgin carried upward by angels above the apostles who gather around her empty tomb. The size would suit a private chapel or a collector's room.","i":"The Assumption, celebrated on August 15, was a central Marian feast and a favorite subject of Counter-Reformation art in Rome, where Poussin worked. It links Mary to Christ's Resurrection: she is received, body and soul, into glory, and the apostles below are witnesses of a miracle they can only partly grasp. The empty tomb, often filled with flowers, is the usual sign of the event.","c":"Poussin's sacred pictures of about 1630 are usually described as balancing Baroque movement against a growing taste for clear grouping, and a subject with a built-in upward thrust tests that balance. The question to ask is whether heavenly and earthly zones hold together as a single design. It is a thoughtful rather than showy treatment, and a good example of the artist working within a well-worn Roman type."},"met:437329":{"d":"On the left, on a stone platform, Romulus in a red cloak and crown holds up his cloak as a signal. Below him a Roman festival erupts into abduction: soldiers in ochre, blue and pink tunics seize struggling women in blue, green and rose, who throw up their arms and cry out. In the right foreground an old woman in a white head-cloth kneels with outstretched hands while a half-naked soldier pins an old man, and an infant lies on the ground; at the right a white horse rears among soldiers. Behind rise a columned portico and a high classical building under a cloudy sky. The figures are arranged in tight groups in a shallow space. Oil on canvas, about 155 by 210 centimetres.","i":"The story is from the founding legend of Rome: the Romans invited the neighbouring Sabines to a festival, intending to seize their women as wives, and when Romulus raised his cloak (seen at left) his warriors did so. Poussin, French but based in Rome, painted this probably in 1633 to 1634. The Met notes that several groups are closely based on ancient sculpture, giving the scene the choreography of dancers caught mid-action, and that he arranged wax figures in a tiny theatre-like box to work out the spatial relationships. The painting belonged to the French ambassador to Rome in the 1630s and then to Cardinal Richelieu, chief minister to Louis XIII. Poussin later painted a second version, now in the Louvre.","c":"Poussin's cool intelligence is on full display. The groups are drawn from sculpture, the colours are crisp and the architecture gives the violence a stage, which suits a story of Roman origins as theatre. What is missing is warmth; the faces register terror without feeling, the figures freeze in gesture like ballet poses, and the repeated postures verge on pattern. It is more admirable than moving. Beside Rubens's turbulent baroque abduction scenes, it is a lesson in order, which is exactly what Poussin intended."},"met:437327":{"d":"Two armed knights, Carlo and Ubaldo, appear with sword and shield in a scene that also includes a dragon, as the museum's keywords record. The oil on canvas, about 118 by 102 centimeters, dates from about 1633. The subject comes from Tasso's epic Jerusalem Delivered: the knights have been sent to bring the warrior Rinaldo back from the enchantress Armida, and the monsters and magic they meet lie between them and him.","i":"Tasso's poem (1581) about the First Crusade was a favorite source for painters. Rinaldo, the Christian champion, has been lured into idleness by Armida, and his companions are sent to retrieve him, overcoming the dangers that guard her enchanted island. Ubaldo's polished shield shames Rinaldo into returning to the war. In the seventeenth century the tale could be read as duty triumphing over pleasure.","c":"Poussin in the early 1630s was still painting episodes from Tasso with a lively, theatrical energy, far from the austerity of his later work. The dragon and weapons give an anecdotal drive less typical of the calmer artist of the 1640s. The picture is more fully appreciated by viewers who know the poem, since the title does much of the narrative work."},"nga:32692":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 95.5 by 121 centimeters, painted in 1641 to 1642, shows the baptism of Christ in the river Jordan by John the Baptist. According to the Gospels the heavens open, the Spirit descends like a dove and a voice declares Christ the beloved Son; pictures of the scene usually show John pouring water over Christ, the dove above, and onlookers waiting on the bank, all set in a landscape.","i":"Christ's baptism marks the start of his public ministry and was understood as the model for the sacrament by which Christians enter the Church, so in the seventeenth century the scene could be read as a statement about the sacrament itself. The dove and the voice make it a revelation of the Trinity: Father, Son and Spirit are all present. Poussin's religious pictures are noted for their intellectual care.","c":"The date places this canvas in the years when Poussin was moving toward the severe, rationally ordered manner of his maturity, with figures balanced like a frieze in a measured landscape and gestures legible at a distance. Such pictures reward slow looking rather than quick delight, and some viewers find them cool. The clarity is the achievement."},"cma:150389":{"d":"On a flight of stone steps, the Virgin Mary sits at the centre in a red gown and blue cloak, holding the Christ child, who leans from her lap toward his young cousin. At the left, Saint Elizabeth, in yellow-ochre with a white veil, bends close, while her son, the naked young John the Baptist, reaches up to offer the child an apple. At the right, Joseph, in a deep purple robe, stretches his legs along the steps, a carpenter's compass in his hand. Columns, an urn, an orange tree, a stone basin and billowing white clouds fill the space behind, and a basket of fruit and gilded vessels sit at the front. The colours are clear and bright, the forms smooth and sculptural.","i":"The museum calls this deceptively simple composition a meditation on the Holy Family's role in the redemption of humanity. Mary presents Christ to the world. Elizabeth leans forward to foretell his death, John offers him an apple, a sign of humanity's fall in Eden, and Joseph holds a compass, the carpenter's tool and also a sign of God the Father. Poussin developed the arrangement with great care, and the museum notes that he studied lighting using small wax models in a box pierced with holes. Painted in 1648, the picture belongs to the years when Poussin, working in Rome, refined a strict classical manner of clear forms and geometric order.","c":"This is Poussin's reasoned classicism at full strength. The pyramid of figures, the steps that frame them and the strong primary colours make a picture of great clarity, and the symbols are tucked into everyday gestures so that they do not feel like a lecture. The price is warmth. The faces are generic, the poses read like sculpture, and the calm approaches coolness. Viewers who prefer Rubens will find it stiff. Later artists, Cézanne among them, valued exactly this order, and in the geometry of the steps it is easy to see why."},"met:437330":{"d":"Peter and John, two of Christ's apostles, heal a lame man at the gate of the Temple in Jerusalem, an episode told in the Acts of the Apostles. The oil on canvas, about 126 by 165 centimeters, is a horizontal work dated 1655, from the last decade of Poussin's long residence in Rome. The museum's keywords name Peter, John and the lame man, and in pictures of this subject the apostle's commanding gesture and the beggar's response form the center of the action.","i":"In Acts 3, Peter and John go up to the Temple at the hour of prayer and meet a man, lame from birth, begging at the gate called Beautiful. Peter has no silver or gold to give, but takes the man by the hand and tells him, in the name of Jesus Christ, to rise and walk; he leaps up praising God. The healing, the first the Acts record, shows Christ's power continuing in the Church and sets spiritual gifts above wealth.","c":"Poussin's late pictures pare narrative to a few legible gestures and let thought, not spectacle, carry the story, and a tale that turns on a single command and a single touch suits that economy. Viewers wanting Baroque excitement may find the result restrained; those willing to read gestures will see the logic. It is a characteristic example of the severe, reasoned manner of his final years."},"met:437326":{"d":"A huge, almost naked man strides across a wooded hill country, stretching one hand ahead of him with a long staff, while a tiny figure sits on his shoulders and points the way. This is the blind giant Orion with his guide. Above, a dark bank of cloud rises into a pale sky, and a goddess sits upon it. Below, small figures move about the valley. Dense green trees frame the view at left and right, and beyond them pale hills and a rocky outcrop dissolve into haze. The light is cool and clear, and no sun is visible.","i":"In the myth Orion, blinded, travels east to meet the rising sun, whose rays can restore his sight. Vulcan lent him the boy Cedalion as a guide, and Diana, the goddess of the hunt, watches from a cloud. Poussin painted it in 1658 for Michel Passart, a well-known patron of landscape painting, and drew on the retelling of the myth by the Greek writer Lucian. The Metropolitan Museum calls it one of Poussin's greatest landscapes. The picture is often read as an allegory of seeking light and knowledge, but that is a scholarly interpretation rather than something the myth states, and the sun the story is heading for does not appear.","c":"Poussin builds a landscape the way he builds a history painting, with everything planned: Orion's stride, the tiny guide and the cloud-borne goddess lead the eye upward in a deliberate chain, and the cool, dark greens give the scene unusual solemnity. The cost is a certain stiffness. The story is hard to read without the title, the valley figures are barely marks, and the promised sun is out of view. It is a severe, thoughtful picture, more admired for its intelligence than loved for its charm."},"met:437687":{"d":"A small portrait, about 37 by 30 centimeters, in oil on wood, painted about 1670 or slightly later by Pieter van Slingelandt. It shows Johan Hulshout (1623 to 1687), and the museum's subject keywords list a man in a lace collar, a garden, a balcony, a shoe and the address Rapenburg 8, apparently his house. The sitter therefore seems to be placed in his own surroundings rather than against a blank ground. The finely finished panel is a cabinet picture for close viewing.","i":"Slingelandt was a pupil of Gerrit Dou, founder of the Leiden school of fijnschilders, the fine painters whose smooth, minutely finished panels fetched high prices from wealthy collectors. A portrait that shows a man with his house and garden proclaims property and standing as well as likeness, as Dutch burghers liked. The Rapenburg is one of Leiden's most prestigious canals, so the address probably signals a prosperous household, and the lace collar adds to the impression of comfort.","c":"Slingelandt's reputation rests on precision, and a portrait that folds figure, architecture and garden into a small panel is a test of that skill. The risk of the fine manner is stiffness: the finish can seem to matter more than the likeness. Yet for a viewer who enjoys virtuoso rendering of lace and foliage this is the right kind of picture, and a reminder that Dutch portraiture was not all Rembrandt-style drama."},"nga:161648":{"d":"A man sits in profile near an oven in a shadowy room, hunched over a clutter of vessels, tongs and papers. He wears a tall black cap, a rust-brown sleeveless garment over grey sleeves and a pale apron, and he looks down at a long-spouted vessel in front of him while his elbow rests on what may be a bellows. Folios, pots and a red cloth are jumbled on the floor, and more containers sit on a ledge over the fire. Browns, black, slate blue and greys dominate, and the artist signed and dated a wooden box at the lower right in 1663.","i":"Alchemy sought to turn base metals into gold and to find an elixir of life, and in Dutch art the alchemist was usually a comic or cautionary figure, a man who wasted his money and days on a hopeless pursuit. The disorder of papers, pots and tools here fits that tradition. Yet the man's intense concentration invites some sympathy, and the picture can also be read as a portrait of obsessive learning. Cornelis Bega, a pupil of Adriaen van Ostade, inherited the type from Bruegel and Teniers.","c":"Bega was Ostade's best pupil, and this small picture shows how much he learned: a dim room built from a few brown tones, with the clutter organised so that the eye moves from the man's hands to his apparatus. The subject is less sharply characterised than Ostade's peasants, and the browns can become murky, but it shows real command of atmosphere from an artist who died young, in 1664, during an outbreak of plague."},"met:724811":{"d":"A small vertical canvas, about 53 by 38 centimeters, painted in 1750 by the Roman artist Placido Costanzi. The museum's subject keywords list a saint, a child, a man and a woman, a grouping that suggests a miracle worked for a family. The saint is Joseph of Cupertino (1603 to 1663), a Franciscan friar celebrated for his ecstatic raptures and levitations; he was also credited with healings and other wonders, and the figures here suggest an episode of that kind.","i":"Joseph of Cupertino was a Conventual Franciscan known for simple piety, who was reported to rise into the air during prayer and Mass, and who became a patron of students. He died in 1663, was beatified in 1753 and canonized in 1767. A picture of 1750 falls three years before his beatification, in the years when his cause was advancing and interest in his miracles was high. Such images turned reported wonders into visible, shareable devotion.","c":"Costanzi, a Roman painter trained in the circle of Sebastiano Conca, worked in a late Baroque manner softened by Rococo grace, and a small canvas like this plays to that strength: gentle gestures, clear grouping, an easy facility. He is a competent rather than an original artist, and the picture is more agreeable than powerful. Its interest lies in the subject, a saint still on the way to canonization when it was painted."},"nga:46050":{"d":"A woman sits facing slightly left, her hands in her lap, in a golden-brown gown with voluminous sleeves, a low neckline and pearl accents. She has fair skin, blue eyes, dark curly hair and a neutral expression, and wears a pearl necklace, earrings and a bracelet, with more pearls in her long hair. In one hand she holds white flowers. The background is a warm, dark interior with red drapery at the left. The canvas measures about 126 by 102 centimeters.","i":"The title names Barbara Villiers (1640-1709), the most powerful of Charles II's mistresses, who became Lady Castlemaine in 1661 and Duchess of Cleveland only in 1670, so the title gives her later rank. The pearls, the silky gown and the loose curls follow the fashion for undress portraiture associated with Sir Peter Lely's pictures of the Restoration court, which flattered sitters with an air of sensuous ease. Such portraits were copied widely, which may explain why this version is anonymous.","c":"The composition is a Lely formula, and the careful rendering of pearls, silk and drapery shows competence if not distinction. The sitter's neutral expression and soft modeling flatter her but give little of the commanding, scandalous personality that made her famous. As a copy or studio version it lacks the sensuous fluency of Lely's best work, but it is a useful record of how the Restoration court wished to be seen."},"met:437461":{"d":"A horizontal river landscape, about 39 by 65 centimeters, painted in 1869 in oil on canvas. The museum's keywords list trees and a river, so the picture is a plain view of the Seine and its banks rather than an anecdote or a monument. Martín Rico y Ortega, a Spanish painter then working largely in France, made such river scenes in the manner of the Barbizon landscapists.","i":"In the 1860s Rico came under the influence of Charles-François Daubigny and the Barbizon painters, who made humble river and woodland views the subject of serious painting. A scene on the Seine involves no hero and no story: the subject is light, water and weather, studied from nature. For a Spanish artist in Paris the river landscape was a way to join the most modern current in French painting of the day.","c":"Rico's Seine pictures are usually admired as exercises in tone and touch, with sky, trees and water drawn into one soft key and a light, quick handling that looks natural rather than labored. A Spaniard in the French manner, he can seem polite beside Daubigny himself, whose river views have a firmer sense of structure. This is a pleasant, unpretentious example rather than a landmark."},"met:437462":{"d":"A vertical view of a garden, about 61 by 39 centimeters, painted in 1871. A building and a fountain give the space its structure, and a boy and a girl are the only figures the museum lists, so the children mainly give the garden scale and life. The title places the scene in Spain, and the picture belongs to the sunny, finished garden views that Martín Rico y Ortega painted for collectors.","i":"Gardens with fountains, walls and shade held a special appeal for painters of the later nineteenth century, partly for the play of light and partly for associations with Moorish Spain and the picturesque. For Rico, who moved between Paris, Spain and, later, Venice, such scenes joined the Barbizon feeling for light and foliage with a taste for ordered, decorative settings that collectors liked. The children probably add a note of everyday life to a place of leisure.","c":"Rico's handling is typically brisk and exact, with small flicked touches that make leaves and stone sparkle, and a vertical garden view suits that sparkle. The skill is considerable and the ambition slight: this is a charming picture that sets out to please and does so, with little of the searching quality of the French landscapists he learned from. It is a good example of why Rico sold well."},"met:437460":{"d":"A horizontal view of a Venetian canal with buildings and boats, painted in 1879 in oil on canvas, about 50 by 68 centimeters. The museum lists Venice, a canal, boats, buildings and the city as its subjects, so it is a cityscape in the tradition of the vedute sold to travelers. Martín Rico y Ortega was a Spanish painter who painted Venice repeatedly and died there in 1908.","i":"Venice was the great subject for travelers' pictures, and a canal scene served visitors and collectors as a souvenir of a famous city. Painters from Turner to the Impressionists found in its water, stucco and reflections an endlessly changing light. A side canal, narrow and intimate, shows the working city behind the grand monuments of the Grand Canal and Saint Mark's, a quieter Venice that appealed to a nineteenth-century taste for the picturesque.","c":"Rico's Venetian pictures are known for crisp drawing and bright, sparkling light, which suit a subject that is all reflection and weathered surface. The result is highly saleable and perhaps a little repetitive: one canal resembles another. At his best he makes a modest corner of the city feel seen rather than recited, and a canal scene like this shows that freshness on a small scale."},"nga:46176":{"d":"A tall panel with a shallow, triangular top shows the Virgin on a semicircular, cream-white stone throne, cradling the Christ Child, with four winged angels attending, two on each side, all turned inward toward the pair. Mary wears a navy-blue mantle covered in dense gold lines over a rose-pink gown; the child, whose features are adult-like, wears rose pink and raises two fingers in blessing. The ground is gold, partly worn away. Tempera on panel, about 230 by 141 centimeters, around 1290.","i":"A Maestà shows the Virgin as Queen of Heaven enthroned in majesty, a type developed in thirteenth-century Italy from Byzantine models. Mary points to her son with one hand, the gesture of the Hodegetria, she who shows the way, while he blesses; the gold ground stands for heavenly light and the angels form her court. Such a panel was probably made for a church or convent altar. The painter is anonymous, named after Città di Castello in Umbria.","c":"A grand early panel from central Italy in which the old Byzantine formula is still in charge: pale, green-tinged flesh, large eyes, gold-line drapery. Yet the solid, paneled throne and the angels' inward glances show an interest in space and relationship that points toward the next generation, Duccio and Giotto. Less refined than the masters it follows, it is nonetheless a commanding devotional object, and the worn gold is a reminder of its long life."},"nga:131993":{"d":"A character study of a bearded man wearing a beret, in oil on panel, about 53.5 by 46 centimeters, painted around 1630 by Jan Lievens. The title names no sitter, which suggests it belongs to the Dutch category of the tronie, a head painted for its expression and costume rather than as a commissioned likeness. Lievens painted a number of such heads in Leiden around this date, and a picture of this size is a close view, meant to be studied at arm's length.","i":"Lievens and Rembrandt, both young painters in Leiden around 1630, worked in close competition and painted many such heads, often of elderly men in berets, furs and turbans. A tronie advertised skill: beard hair, wrinkled skin, the fall of light across a face. It also asked for no flattery, and collectors prized the more vigorous examples. Whether a particular head records a real person or an invented type is usually uncertain.","c":"At this date Lievens was the bolder, more outward-looking of the two young Leiden rivals, favoring broad forms, strong light and a confident, rough-edged touch. Heads like this are usually admired for their energy rather than depth of psychology; next to Rembrandt's own they can seem extroverted. It is a strong, assured picture and a good clue to why contemporaries rated Lievens so highly."},"met:436987":{"d":"The Holy Family pauses on the road to Egypt, a subject the museum's keywords record as the Rest on the Flight, with the Madonna and Child at its center and a bunch of grapes among the listed details. The oil on wood panel, about 95 by 77 centimeters, was painted between 1537 and 1540 in the Netherlandish tradition and is given by the museum to Jan Massys, son of the Antwerp master Quinten Metsys.","i":"In Matthew's Gospel an angel warns Joseph to flee with Mary and the child from Herod's massacre. The rest on the journey, an invention of later legend and devotion, lets painters show the Holy Family as a tender domestic group in a landscape. Grapes point to the wine of the Eucharist and to Christ's blood, so even a gentle family scene foretells the Passion. Panels of this size were made for private devotion in Netherlandish homes.","c":"Catalogued under Jan Massys, the picture has also been described as the work of a follower of his father, Quinten Metsys, a reminder that attribution within this family of painters is fluid. One expects the smooth, finished surface and sweetened Madonna type of Antwerp devotional painting, and the grapes add a note of theology. It is agreeable, polished and pious work rather than an inventive one."},"met:436300":{"d":"A tall, narrow oil panel shows two standing saints, John the Evangelist and Lawrence of Rome, each recognisable by what he carries; a book and a chalice are among the objects shown. At about 123 by 49 centimetres, the upright format suggests that the panel once formed one section of a larger altarpiece, perhaps a wing or a tier of a polyptych, a common arrangement in northern Italian churches. Defendente Ferrari worked in Piedmont, and his devotional figures generally combine the clarity and rich finish of late Gothic painting with softer modelling learned from Lombard and northern European art.","i":"John, the beloved disciple and traditional author of the fourth Gospel, is usually shown with a chalice, recalling the legend that he drank poisoned wine without harm; a book can stand for his Gospel. Lawrence was a deacon of Rome, martyred in 258 by being roasted on a gridiron, and is normally shown in a deacon's dalmatic with a martyr's palm or the gridiron itself. Pairs of saints like these often reflect the dedication of a church or chapel, or the name saints of the patrons who paid for it.","c":"Ferrari was a provincial painter who kept the Gothic habits of Piedmont alive into the 1530s, long after Florence and Rome had moved on, and a single narrow wing like this leaves little room for invention. His usual strengths are a crisp, northern-minded finish and clear presentation of attributes rather than Renaissance drama. Beside his large multi-panel altarpieces, a pair of standing saints is a modest, workmanlike piece of devotional furniture."},"met:437222":{"d":"A vertical panel, about 94 by 79 centimeters, painted about 1410 in tempera and gold, showing Saint Ursula with her maidens. Ursula, a legendary British princess, is often shown crowned, with an arrow or banner, and with her companions gathered around or beneath her cloak. The gold ground and rich surface are typical of Venetian devotional painting of the early fifteenth century, in which holy figures are set against a shimmering field rather than a landscape.","i":"According to the medieval Golden Legend, Ursula, a British princess, sailed on pilgrimage to Rome with a great company of virgin companions, traditionally eleven thousand, and all were killed at Cologne after she refused to marry a pagan leader. Her cult was strongest at Cologne but spread widely; an image of the saint with her maidens offered a model of steadfast female virtue and was thought to protect a whole community.","c":"A fine example of Venetian International Gothic, where grace of line and richness of gold count for more than space or weight: the figures sway and the surface glows. It looks backward to the courtly style of the previous century as much as forward, but the ornamental beauty is undeniable. It rewards a viewer who reads it as a devotional jewel rather than a window onto the world."},"nga:112264":{"d":"Two masted ships tip wildly among white-crested gray waves near a rocky outcrop, beneath slate-gray and lavender clouds that sweep from lower right to upper left, with a patch of topaz-blue sky at the top center. Tiny figures crowd the decks and rigging; one hovers overboard, perhaps clinging to a rope or falling. At right, jagged rocks jut from the foam like teeth, and close looking finds people climbing the nearest rock, a rowboat, two men in the water and a few birds. The panel is about 72 by 108 centimeters.","i":"The Dutch Republic lived by the sea, and a storm driving ships toward a rocky shore was a familiar emblem of danger, fortune and survival. Willem van de Velde the Younger, trained by his father, the marine draftsman Willem van de Velde the Elder, and by the painter Simon de Vlieger, knew the rigging and handling of ships intimately. The tiny struggling figures give the elemental scene a human dimension: it is about people at the mercy of the water, not only about weather.","c":"Painted when the artist was in his late twenties, this storm piece shows how quickly he mastered the effects of a painted sea: the diagonal sweep of cloud, the foam-flecked water and the single sunlit boulder that catches the eye against the dark rocks. The tiny figures are observed with the same exactness as the rigging. It is dramatic without bombast, controlled where lesser storm pictures are merely noisy."},"met:437876":{"d":"A harbor view, about 66 by 78 centimeters, in oil on canvas, painted about 1665 by Willem van de Velde II. The museum's keywords list ships, boats and a dock, which is to say the working mouth of a Dutch port, with vessels of different sizes sharing the water. Pictures of this kind present a familiar place of trade as a stage for shipping, and were made for merchants and ship-owners as well as for collectors.","i":"A harbor entrance brought together the things the Dutch Republic prized: commerce, naval strength and seamanship. Paintings of ports and shipping decorated the houses of merchants and officials and served as records of a seafaring nation's prosperity. The Van de Veldes' special skill was accuracy of hull and rigging, which mattered to buyers who knew ships. With no named port in the title, the picture works as a type of the nation's harbors rather than a portrait of one place.","c":"By about 1665 Van de Velde II was at his most poised, and his ships stand on the water with a draftsman's certainty: hulls, spars and rigging drawn exactly. The type can feel formulaic, and without a dramatic event it asks the viewer to enjoy order rather than excitement. As an example of the genre in its prime it is a fair measure of what he did best."},"nga:214374":{"d":"A three-masted warship fills most of the right half of this vertical canvas beneath towering clouds. Its cream-white sails glow in sunlight, tiny deckhands work the yards, and the flat stern shows a rearing lion and unicorn flanking a shield among gold moldings. Eight gunports are open on the port side and smoke bursts from one cannon. Six men row a boat to the left, a flag with a red and white cross on a blue field flutters, and the signature is inscribed on what looks like driftwood: W. V Velde In Londen 1673.","i":"Willem van de Velde the Younger and his father left the Dutch Republic for England in 1672 to 1673, when the two countries were at war, and entered the service of Charles II. A salute, the firing of guns to honor a person, a flag or an occasion, was a regular ritual of naval life and gave painters a colorful, ceremonial subject. The lion and unicorn on the stern are the royal arms, so a Dutch artist is here celebrating English naval pomp.","c":"A showpiece of the painter's English years: the sunlit sails, crisp rigging and carved stern are drawn with a precision that comes from a draftsman's training, while the smoke and piled clouds give the formal scene some air. The salute is a modest occasion, so the effect is ceremonial rather than thrilling. The signature disguised as driftwood is a small, characteristic wit. It is a confident, polished picture rather than a daring one."},"nga:195505":{"d":"Choppy waves with frothy white crests fill the bottom third of this small horizontal canvas. A sailboat tilts sharply in the wind, its straw-yellow sail pulled taut and spray kicking up as at least two men crouch inside. A narrow boat to the right, nearly lost in shadow, has a crimson sail flapping loose over two huddled men. More vessels recede to the horizon beneath charcoal clouds that break at upper left into a pale apricot sky, with streaks of light on the water. Oil on canvas, about 26 by 43 centimeters.","i":"The title names the moment when the storm has passed but the sea has not settled. Dutch audiences understood the sea as both a source of wealth and a constant danger, and a clearing sky after bad weather could suggest relief and providence. The museum credits the picture to Van de Velde the Younger with his studio and dates it about 1700, near the end of a long career spent largely in England.","c":"The sky is the real subject: apricot light breaking behind charcoal cloud gives a small canvas unexpected breadth, and the tilted sails make the boats feel at the mercy of the wind. The credit to the artist and his studio is a reminder that late works were often shared with assistants, so the hand is not wholly the master's. Still, the poetry of weather is well caught."},"nga:221207":{"d":"A crowded interior drama in oil on canvas. A bearded man in a long red robe over a yellow tunic stands left of center, head turned and one hand outstretched, while a weeping child in pale blue clutches his other wrist. At right a seated woman in white with a yellow shawl rests her head on her hand, a tear falling, a naked child clinging to her arm; a second seated woman in red looks up beneath furrowed brows. A silver helmet with a red plume lies on a draped table, and two men at left, one with head in hands, complete the group.","i":"The subject is the Roman general Coriolanus, banished from Rome after quarreling with the people, shown taking leave of his wife, children and mother, Volumnia. In Plutarch's account he then joined Rome's enemies, the Volscians, and marched on the city, until his mother's pleading turned him back. Duty weighed against private feeling was just the kind of Roman example championed by Jacques-Louis David's circle, in which Girodet studied; the wolf statue on a column probably alludes to Rome itself.","c":"A remarkable student picture: Girodet was still a teenager, and he already organizes seven foreground figures into a clear sequence of grief, appeal and resolve. The gestures belong to David's school, theatrical, legible and a little stiff, and the props, helmet, wolf and chipped plaster, are noted with precision. The strange, poetic intensity of his mature work is not yet here, but the ambition is."},"met:438032":{"d":"A man with a sword is the subject of this oil study, about 41 by 33 centimeters, made around 1807 by the French painter Anne-Louis Girodet-Trioson. The title calls it a study for a Portrait of an Indian, and the museum's keywords, sword, portrait and Orientalist, mark it as the sketch of a figure in exotic costume. As a study, it would work out the pose, character and color of the sitter before a finished picture.","i":"Girodet was drawn to the exotic and the literary: his Entombment of Atala (1808) drew on Chateaubriand's novel of Native Americans, and his Revolt at Cairo (1810) on Napoleon's Egyptian campaign. The word Indian in French of the period could mean a person from the Americas or from India, and the museum's Orientalist label points to the taste for foreign costume and the supposedly exotic character it was thought to express.","c":"A study should have the immediacy the finished version often lacks. Girodet's completed pictures can be cold and over-polished, and his oil sketches show the nervous energy and eccentric imagination behind them. Without the finished portrait to compare, the study stands on its own as a vivid, slightly strange image, a glimpse of the Romantic streak inside a Neoclassical training."},"met:438389":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Madame Jacques-Louis-Étienne Reizet, born Colette-Désirée-Thérèse Godefroy (1782-1850), is portrayed in an oil painting of about 60 by 50 centimeters dated 1823, the year before Girodet's death. The recorded motifs name only the sitter. At this size, a bust-length or half-length format, the portrait concentrates attention on the face and the expression, the area in which Girodet, a pupil of Jacques-Louis David, excelled.","i":"In 1823 Girodet was in the last full year of his life, in poor health, and he painted portraits of those close to him with great concentration. A portrait of a woman identified by both her married name and her maiden name follows the French convention of the period, which recorded a woman's family as well as her husband's. The sitter was forty-one when she was painted.","c":"Girodet's best portraits are admired for sharp characterization and a smooth, cool finish in which the sitter's gaze and expression carry the picture. Late in his career they become more sober and direct than his earlier theatrical subjects. The risk is a certain chilliness, a sense of the sitter observed rather than befriended, yet that detachment gives the best of them their authority."},"nga:46133":{"d":"A sand-colored church fills the right two thirds of this horizontal panel, its staggered, squared-off walls pierced by rectangular windows at irregular intervals. To the left stands a circular building with a low dome, plants growing on it as on a ruin, with a tall obelisk in front and arched ruins or building work above; low blue-green mountains recede behind. A dirt road crosses diagonally, carrying a carriage drawn by two black horses, its passenger in a red hat and purple robe, with a driver and two men in blue walking beside.","i":"Saenredam, a Haarlem specialist in architectural portraits, never traveled to Italy, so a view of Rome like this must rest on another artist's drawing or print. For Dutch collectors Rome was both the center of the old Church and a landscape of antique ruins, and a picture of it offered a pilgrimage by proxy. The red hat and purple robe suggest a churchman riding by, a small human note against the old walls.","c":"A rare Roman subject from a painter best known for pale, measured Dutch church interiors. The flat planes, careful geometry and cool, even light show his habits, while the carriage and road add a busier note than he usually allows. Since the view was necessarily second-hand, it lacks the serene presence of his portraits of buildings drawn on site; it is nonetheless a revealing early work, dated 1629, from a painter still defining his subject."},"met:928532":{"d":"A church interior in oil on wood, about 40 by 55 centimeters, dated 1632, showing the Sint-Pieterskerk in 's-Hertogenbosch. This is an architectural portrait of a real building, painted by Pieter Saenredam, the Dutch specialist in the genre. His church interiors are typically pale, calm and carefully measured, showing the whitewashed walls, piers and arches of buildings stripped of images after the Reformation.","i":"Saenredam usually made measured drawings on the spot and built his paintings from them in the studio, adjusting proportions and viewpoint for effect. 's-Hertogenbosch, a Catholic city, was captured by the Dutch Republic in 1629, and many of its churches passed to Reformed worship, so a view of 1632 records buildings in a changed religious life. For viewers then, the picture may have had local and confessional interest as well as architectural beauty.","c":"Even in a small panel Saenredam's principle is plain: let geometry and light do the work. Pale surfaces, rhythmic piers and a still, measured space are his habit, and the very restraint is both the achievement and the limitation. The picture is quiet to the point of austerity and is best appreciated by viewers who enjoy architecture for its own sake, though in a modest church it lacks the grandeur of his cathedral views."},"nga:46132":{"d":"From floor level near the left wall we look along the nave into the semicircular apse of a light-filled Gothic church. Cream-white walls rise in three zones: arches below, a narrow walkway above and a row of tall arched windows at the top, where rose-pink ribs meet in the vault. Over the altar hangs a painting of the Nativity, with people and cattle around an infant in a manger; two dark tapestries dated 1598 and 1621 hang higher. A lone bald man in white kneels near the choir stalls, and the signature appears as if written on a stall: 1646.","i":"The church is the Cathedral of Saint John in 's-Hertogenbosch, the great Brabantine Gothic building that passed to Reformed worship after the Dutch captured the city in 1629. A Latin dedication on one tapestry appears to honor Archduke Albert of Austria, ruler of the southern Netherlands, who died in 1621, as father of the fatherland, in the name of Silva Ducis, the Latin name of the city. The painting preserves furnishings of the Catholic past.","c":"Tall and luminous, this is Saenredam at full stretch: the vertical panel stresses height, the pale cream, rose and slate-gray palette keeps the building serene, and a single kneeling figure supplies human scale. The feigned inscription on the choir stall, signing and dating the painting as if it were part of the furniture, is a typical witty touch. Some viewers find the effect chill; others find in it the very serenity of Dutch art."},"met:436866":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 200 by 112 centimeters, shows a woman in profile wearing the costume of the Ciociaria, the hill country southeast of Rome whose folk dress was a favorite with artists and their models. Painted in 1878 by Jules-Joseph Lefebvre, it is a large, finished figure picture of a size suited to public exhibition, the profile view giving the figure a clean, medal-like contour. The title is the name of a romantic heroine, not of a documented sitter.","i":"Graziella is the heroine of Alphonse de Lamartine's popular romantic story, a young Italian fisherman's daughter who loves a visitor and dies of a broken heart. Lefebvre, a leading academic painter of the Third Republic known for idealized female figures, borrowed the name for a picturesque costume figure of the kind the Salon public enjoyed. Italian peasant types were a staple of nineteenth-century French painting, offering color, tradition and an escape from modern life.","c":"Lefebvre's strengths are polish and a controlled, classical contour, and the profile pose displays both: a smooth, firm drawing that would suit a medal. The price of such finish is emotional emptiness, and to a later generation schooled by the Impressionists this was the voice of the official Salon. As a sample of that taste, with its idealizing grace and dash of exotic costume, the picture is accomplished and instructive rather than moving."},"nga:154657":{"d":"In a small round-topped panel, a kneeling Virgin in a white-lined blue cloak and a seated, bearded Joseph with a short staff flank the nude Christ Child, who lies on the end of her cloak with two fingers raised. A ruined brick arch and a shorn trunk carry a thatched roof at left, and a river with two bridges winds through grassy hills to a high horizon. Above, in a pale blue sky, God the Father floats among fourteen winged youths and a cloud of cobalt-blue cherub heads, with four older angels below.","i":"A kneeling Virgin adoring a Child laid on the ground is the standard Italian Nativity of the later fifteenth century, a type often linked to the visions of Saint Bridget of Sweden. The ruined arch carrying a makeshift roof is usually read as the old order giving way to the new, and the canteen and sack by the tree probably evoke the journey to Bethlehem. The Child's raised fingers are a blessing, and God the Father above confirms who he is.","c":"Francesco di Giorgio was above all an architect and engineer, and the care given to the setting shows it: the winding river, the bridges and the distant conical hill give the panel a convincing recession, and the sharp, enamel-like color suits a small devotional object. The figures are less persuasive. The faces are pale and a little brittle, and the host of near-identical winged youths turns heaven into pattern. It charms as refinement more than as feeling."},"met:436330":{"d":"A female figure, named in the title as a goddess of chaste love, appears with a chariot and griffins on a small gilded panel about 39 by 44 cm, painted in tempera and gold on wood. The subject is allegorical or mythological rather than a portrait, and the gilding and fine tempera point to an object meant to be looked at closely, in a private setting. Francesco di Giorgio was a Sienese painter who was equally an architect, sculptor and military engineer.","i":"Chaste love, as distinct from the desire personified by Venus, was a favorite theme for pictures connected with courtship and marriage in fifteenth-century Italy, where a bride was expected to embody virtue. Griffins, which join the eagle and the lion, were old symbols of vigilance and strength, so a goddess accompanied by them is probably meant to command respect. The exact literary source is uncertain, and the panel is best read as an allegory of virtuous love.","c":"Francesco di Giorgio painted little, being busier as an architect and engineer, and his figures have a sculptor's firmness: crisp outline, clear silhouette, a taste for elegant, slender forms. In a gilded tempera panel that taste suits him, and a fantastical subject releases an inventiveness his religious commissions seldom allow. As often with him, the appeal is refinement of design rather than warmth of feeling."},"met:437815":{"d":"The moment of the Binding of Isaac: Abraham raises his knife to sacrifice his son at God's command, and an angel intervenes to stop him. The museum's keywords name Abraham, Isaac, the knife and the angel. The oil on canvas, about 39 by 53 centimeters, is a small horizontal picture painted in the mid-1750s by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo, son and collaborator of the great Venetian painter Giambattista Tiepolo, probably in Venice. Its modest size suggests a picture for close viewing.","i":"In Genesis 22 God tests Abraham by ordering him to sacrifice his son Isaac; at the last moment an angel stays his hand, and a ram is offered instead. The story celebrates faith and obedience, and Christian tradition read Isaac, who carries the wood for his own sacrifice, as a forerunner of Christ. Baroque and Rococo painters favored the instant of the angel's arrival, with its natural drama of suspended violence.","c":"Domenico grew up inside his father's studio, and this small canvas shows the Tiepolo manner at reduced scale: fluent, light-filled brushwork, theatrical gesture and the intervening angel, a Tiepolo specialty. He lacks his father's inexhaustible invention, but he has an assured, graceful touch of his own, and in works like this the facility is more engaging than the finish. A lively, secondary Tiepolo."},"met:437812":{"d":"A lively horizontal canvas, about 76 by 120 centimeters, painted about 1755 by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo. The museum's keywords list a woman dancing, a musician, a dog, a ladder and a ball, along with costumes and actors, so the scene is a rustic dance whose dancers may wear festive or theatrical dress. It is a cabinet picture of everyday pleasure, quite different in tone from the religious and mythological subjects of his father's large commissions.","i":"Country festivals and dances were a favorite subject of eighteenth-century Venetian art, offering city patrons an amusing, idealized view of rustic life. Domenico, who spent his early career assisting his father on large decorative cycles, painted lighter, more informal pictures like this on his own account. The costumes and the reference to actors point to the influence of theater and carnival, which saturated Venetian life and gave painters a ready repertory of types.","c":"Domenico's genre scenes have a lightness his father's grand cycles do not need: quick, loose brushwork, an airy palette and a gift for catching figures in motion. In a dance picture the interest lies in rhythm and sparkle rather than in any moral. The charm is real, though the work is slighter than his large decorative commissions, and it rewards a viewer who likes lightness more than weight."},"met:438130":{"d":"A narrow horizontal canvas, about 36 by 73 centimeters, painted in the mid-1760s by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo. The museum's keywords list a canal, a boat, masks, men and women, so the picture shows masked Venetians setting out by gondola, a moment of everyday pleasure from the city's social round rather than a portrait. The long, low format suggests a procession of figures strung along the water's edge.","i":"Masks were worn in eighteenth-century Venice far beyond the days of Carnival, for months of the year, allowing people of different ranks to mix and go about the city incognito. A gondola leaving with masked passengers conveys the city's reputation for pleasure, intrigue and private adventure. Such scenes of Venetian leisure were popular with foreign visitors and local collectors alike, and Domenico, like Pietro Longhi, recorded the habits of his own city.","c":"A small picture with the economy of a sketch, in which a few brisk touches suggest costume, water and movement. Domenico looks at his city with affection and a sharp eye for fashion, and a shallow, stage-like space suits the masquerade theme. It lacks the grandeur of the family's decorative work, but few Venetian painters caught the mood of the late Republic's leisure so lightly."},"cma:130162":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed woman, in oil on canvas, about 60 by 49 centimeters. The museum records only that it was made in Spain, where Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo, son of the great Venetian fresco painter Giambattista Tiepolo, lived between 1762 and 1770. The sitter is not identified, and the format is modest, a size suited to a private room rather than a ceremonial hall.","i":"Domenico went to Madrid with his father in 1762, when Giambattista was called to decorate the ceilings of the new Royal Palace for Charles III; the elder Tiepolo died there in 1770, and Domenico then returned to Venice. A portrait from these years is a rare side of his work, which was dominated by religious and mythological pictures, frescoes and genre scenes. With no named sitter, it records a face of the Spanish years more than a biography.","c":"Portraits are a minor part of Domenico's output, and he is better known for lively scenes and decorative invention. A likeness of this size is most interesting as a document of his Spanish years, when he worked in the long shadow of his father; the quick, fluent brushwork that animates his larger works is the thing to look for in it. It is a modest picture, and the sitter's anonymity limits what it can tell us."},"met:437814":{"d":"An allegory of a Venetian noble family, dated 1783, in oil on canvas, about 117 by 83 centimeters. The museum's keywords list men and women, putti, the sea god Neptune, a musical instrument and open sky, so the scene is a heavenly gathering in which mythological figures attend the family's honor. The airy, vertical composition is typical of designs related to ceiling decoration in Venetian palaces, and it is by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo.","i":"The Giustiniani, or Giustinian, were one of Venice's old patrician houses, and glorifying a noble family in allegory was a Venetian tradition: ancestors, virtues and the sea gather in the sky to honor the name. Neptune probably stands for Venice's maritime power. By 1783 Domenico was his father's heir in style, Giambattista having died in 1770, and he supplied such flattering allegories to a nobility whose republic would fall in 1797.","c":"The late Tiepolo style persists here: bright, high-keyed, loosely brushed clouds and figures, with gestures pitched at a viewer looking up. By 1783 Neoclassicism was fashionable, and the picture's frothy exuberance looks back to the mid-century Rococo. It is skillful but formulaic, since the glory is conventional, and the pleasure lies in the facility of the handling rather than in any freshness of idea."},"met:437809":{"d":"A fresco transferred to canvas, about 82 by 109 centimeters, showing a female figure who personifies Africa; the museum lists her among its female nudes and records no further attributes. It is one of four allegories of the continents by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo in the museum, all in the same medium and of similar size, originally painted on a wall or ceiling and later detached and mounted on canvas.","i":"In the standard iconography of the continents, Africa was shown as a woman with attributes such as an elephant-head headdress, a lion or other animals and exotic produce, following the Iconologia of Cesare Ripa. Such figures served as decorative personifications for European patrons, who understood the continents through trade, conquest and curiosity. The picture belongs to a long tradition of Venetian decorations of the world's four parts.","c":"Without clearly recorded attributes the figure reads mainly as a decorative female nude, and Domenico's talent shows in the light, airy manner suited to fresco. The pleasure is in surface and charm, and little else. Seen as one panel in a four-part cycle it works as an element of ornament; seen alone it is slight, and its European vision of the continent is itself a period document."},"met:437810":{"d":"A fresco transferred to canvas, about 82 by 109 centimeters, showing a female nude who personifies America; the museum's keywords list an alligator beside her. It is by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo and belongs to a set of four continents with Africa, Asia and Europe. Fresco transferred to canvas means that the picture was first painted on wall or ceiling plaster and later detached and mounted on canvas.","i":"In Baroque and Rococo decoration the four continents were a standard theme, each personified as a woman with attributes taken from Cesare Ripa's handbook, the Iconologia. America is usually identified by a feathered headdress, bow and arrows and an alligator or crocodile, the animal listed here. Domenico's father painted a famous set of the continents on the Würzburg staircase ceiling in 1752 to 1753, and such sets expressed a European view of the wider world.","c":"Fresco gives Domenico a broad, fluent, light-filled manner, and a single figure with a clear silhouette and one emblem suits it well. A fragment detached from its architecture loses its context, however, and is best read as one part of a larger decorative scheme. The picture is charming and decorative rather than profound, and its formula, a female nude with an exotic animal, now reads as a period convention."},"met:437811":{"d":"A fresco transferred to canvas, about 82 by 106 centimeters, showing a woman who personifies Asia; the museum's keywords also list a horse. It belongs with Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo's allegories of the other three continents in the museum, and like them was originally painted on plaster, then detached and mounted on canvas. At 106 centimeters wide it is slightly narrower than its companions.","i":"In such sets Asia is the continent of luxury and ancient empire, shown with rich costume, incense or other exotic goods, and often a camel; here the museum lists a horse beside the woman. The allegory presented the wider world to European patrons as picturesque and available to admire. Domenico worked in the tradition his father, Giambattista, brought to its peak in the continents of the Würzburg staircase ceiling.","c":"Like its companions, the Asia is decorative first: a single figure with an animal, treated with the quick, light handling that suits fresco. The horse gives the composition a note of movement the other panels may lack. As a fragment it cannot show the full design, and as a piece of Orientalist convention it tells more about European taste than about Asia, though it is charming within its limits."},"met:437813":{"d":"A fresco transferred to canvas, about 82 by 109 centimeters, showing a woman who personifies Europe. The museum's keywords list only a woman and allegory, so the emblems that normally identify the continent are not recorded. It is one of four continent allegories by Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo in the museum, painted on plaster and later detached and mounted on canvas.","i":"In the usual order of the continents Europe comes first, shown as the most dignified: crowned or enthroned, richly dressed, and surrounded by signs of rule, religion and the arts and sciences. A set painted for a European patron put its own continent in the place of honor, and the ranking is part of the message. The set belongs to a long Venetian tradition of picturing the world's four parts on walls and ceilings.","c":"A set of continents flatters its viewers through Europe, and flattery asks for dignity rather than strangeness. Domenico's easy handling supplies grace but not weight, and as in the rest of the set the effect is decorative. It is competent and pleasing, a fragment of a larger scheme that deserves to be read as part of a group rather than as an independent work."},"met:436680":{"d":"An angel appears to Mary to tell her that her earthly life is about to end, the rare subject titled the Annunciation of the Death of the Virgin. The oil on canvas, about 66 by 53 centimeters, is a vertical picture painted about 1670 by Samuel van Hoogstraten, and the museum lists only the angel and Mary among its subjects. In the traditional telling the angel brings Mary a palm branch from Paradise and she receives the news in humble prayer.","i":"The Virgin's death is not told in the canonical Gospels but in apocryphal texts and the medieval Golden Legend, where an angel warns her that she will die in three days and gives her a palm. The subject was rare in Protestant Holland and more at home in Catholic art, which suggests a Catholic patron or a painter with a taste for learned, unusual themes. Hoogstraten, a pupil of Rembrandt, wrote a treatise on painting in 1678.","c":"Hoogstraten is better known for perspective boxes and illusionistic trompe l'oeil than for sacred scenes, and a Rembrandt pupil is likely to build a quiet message like this on light and shadow rather than display. The picture is devotional and thoughtful rather than inventive, but the rarity of the subject and the learning of its painter give it more interest than a routine Annunciation."},"nga:46630":{"d":"A cream-colored bridge cuts horizontally across the scene in front of a bustling city, decorated with red, white and blue flags. In the foreground large white barges are moored on the reflective blue river, with small figures in white shirts near wooden platforms; on the left a pale gray path runs beside the water with pedestrians. At right, tall buildings in yellow, blue, peach and cream carry pointed roofs and many dark chimneys under a blue sky of soft clouds. Thick, visible strokes and dashes of paint suggest detail.","i":"The bridge is the Pont Louis-Philippe in Paris, linking the Île Saint-Louis with the Right Bank, and the subject is a working riverside of barges, quays and platforms rather than a monument. Guillaumin was a friend of Pissarro and Cézanne and exhibited with the Impressionists from their first show in 1874. The flags suggest a festive day, and modern life painted from the quayside is typical of the group.","c":"Guillaumin's thick, visible strokes and broken dashes already depart from polished Salon finish, and the barges and bridge give a firm horizontal structure to a bustling view. His color can be heavier and less luminous than Monet's or Pissarro's, but there is a sturdy honesty in the way he records working Paris. Less famous than his friends, he deserves attention for pictures like this."},"met:834308":{"d":"The Roman Charity, a famous tale of filial devotion in which a daughter secretly feeds her imprisoned father with her own milk to keep him from starving. The oil on canvas, about 148 by 138 centimeters, is a nearly square picture dated 1622 by Hendrick ter Brugghen, who worked in Utrecht after studying in Rome. Pictures of the subject usually set the pair in a prison with a guard looking in; the museum's data record only the title.","i":"The story, told by the Roman writer Valerius Maximus, concerns Pero, who saved her father Cimon, condemned to die of hunger in prison, by nursing him secretly; when a jailer discovered them, the authorities were so moved that Cimon was freed. The subject was a favorite example of filial piety in Renaissance and Baroque art. It also gave painters an emotionally charged, faintly scandalous scene that could be justified as a lesson in virtue.","c":"Ter Brugghen was among the gentlest of the Utrecht followers of Caravaggio: he typically favored cool, silvery tones, lemon-yellow and salmon accents, and human tenderness over theatrical violence. The Roman Charity, a subject that can tip into sensation or sentiment, suits his restraint. It is a fine example of Utrecht Caravaggism turned toward compassion."},"nga:144298":{"d":"Shown from the lap up, a pale-skinned man sits in profile, facing right, playing a bagpipe. Light from the left leaves his face in shadow as he looks off to the right. He wears a floppy chocolate-brown beret, a voluminous cream-white shirt falling off one shoulder, and a fawn-brown robe gathered in his lap. He blows into a long mouthpiece and squeezes the tawny bag between his forearms; two long pipes lie over his shoulder, and his fingers cover the holes of the chanter. The background is pale brown, and the signature HTBrugghen fecit 1624 sits beside his face.","i":"Ter Brugghen was the leading Utrecht painter to bring home the lessons of Caravaggio's followers, whom he had seen in Rome, with half-length figures lit from one side. Musicians were a favorite subject of this circle. The bagpipe was a rustic instrument, linked in the Netherlands with peasants, shepherds and country festivals and also, in a bawdy joking tradition, with folly and lust; a viewer of 1624 would probably have enjoyed the player as a vivid type with a touch of comic coarseness.","c":"Ter Brugghen's cool, chalky color, here in pale browns and fawns, and the broad, simplified planes of the shirt give the picture a sculptural calm. The profile and the strong side light are handled with confidence, and the squeeze of the bag is convincing. He is less theatrical than Honthorst, more tender and poetic; this is not the loudest of Utrecht Caravaggism but one of its subtlest."},"met:435817":{"d":"Christ hangs on a dark cross against a flat olive-brown sky pricked with tiny stars, his emaciated body lit by a cold light. His arms are stretched wide, blood runs in thin red threads down from the nailed hands, and his head, crowned with thorns, hangs to one side; a long white cloth wraps his hips and a scroll marked INRI is fixed above. At the left the Virgin, in a pale head-cloth and a grey-green and mauve mantle, clasps her hands and looks down. At the right Saint John, in a red cloak over green, gazes up with hands pressed together. A skull and bones lie at the foot of the cross. Oil on canvas, about 155 by 102 centimetres.","i":"Though painted in the 1620s, this Crucifixion deliberately looks back to earlier religious art. The Met notes that Christ's contorted body and the blood streaming from his wounds refer to the work of early-sixteenth-century German artists, then sought by collectors, and that the picture was meant to inspire the private prayers of a Catholic viewer; it was probably made for one of the hidden Catholic churches of Utrecht. The Virgin and John, set symmetrically at the sides, stand in for the viewer's own agonised watching. Ter Brugghen, who had lived in Italy and absorbed Caravaggio's lighting, was one of the Utrecht Caravaggisti, but here he chooses harshness over polish.","c":"The picture is brutal in the right way. The sinewy, twisting Christ, with his bloodied hands, is among the most painful in Dutch art, and the cold light and flat, star-spotted sky strip away every comfort. The solemn Virgin and John, in their rich draperies, offer the only warmth. The conception is stronger than the execution: the mourners are somewhat doll-like, their gestures stiff, and the symmetry can feel schematic. Beside Rembrandt's humane drama, ter Brugghen's is stark and almost medieval."},"cma:149074":{"d":"A weeping figure with a book and a skull, in oil on canvas, about 125 by 102 centimeters, painted about 1621 by Hendrick ter Brugghen. The museum long took the subject to be the Greek philosopher Heraclitus because of the tears, but the globe he usually weeps over is missing, and the book and skull point to Saint Jerome, the scholar who translated the Bible into Latin. The tears are probably meant to intensify his penitence.","i":"Jerome, who died in 420, retired to the desert as a penitent while continuing his scholarship, and artists show him with a book for learning and a skull as a reminder of death. The mistaken identity shows how close the pictorial types of the weeping philosopher and the penitent saint could be. In Utrecht, where a large Catholic minority likely supported such imagery, a Jerome weeping over his sins offered a sober meditation on repentance.","c":"The picture gains from its ambiguity: by pruning the attributes to a book and a skull, Ter Brugghen makes feeling, not setting, the subject, and a viewer can see why the figure was taken for a philosopher. His soft, silvery modeling avoids Caravaggio's harsh shadows and gives the tears a gentle, human weight. It is a thoughtful, quiet picture rather than a dramatic one."},"cma:157158":{"d":"A small, wide panel, about 43 by 66 centimetres, shows the climax of the story of Tobias. Tobias, the young man at the right, applies the gall of a fish to his father Tobit's blind eyes to cure them, and at the same moment the angel Raphael, who has travelled with him as a relative, reveals that he is an angel. The picture is painted in oil on wood and dated to about 1625.","i":"The Book of Tobit tells how Tobias journeys to recover family money, protected by Raphael, and on the way catches a huge fish whose organs prove useful: the heart and liver to drive out a demon, and the gall to cure his father's blindness. The story, which Catholic painters loved, is about family devotion and trust in providence, and Raphael, whose name means God heals, is the guardian angel of travellers and the young.","c":"Strozzi paints with a loose, fluent brush and warm colour, and this small panel has the freshness one expects of an oil sketch: the gesture of the healing and the reveal of the angel are told with vivid economy. The group is busy and the space compressed, so some details are hard to read. But the human warmth of the scene and the painterly vitality are typical of the artist at his best."},"nga:118846":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 116 by 86 centimeters, showing Saint Francis of Assisi at prayer, painted between about 1620 and 1630 by the Genoese artist Bernardo Strozzi. Strozzi, who had himself been a Capuchin friar, painted the saint repeatedly. In such pictures Francis is usually shown in his coarse brown habit, absorbed in devotion before a crucifix or a skull, in a rugged setting that recalls his life as a hermit.","i":"Francis (1181 or 1182 to 1226), founder of the Franciscans, was enormously popular in the Counter-Reformation as a model of humility, poverty and intense devotion, and the Capuchins, a reformed branch of his order, honored him especially. Prayer pictures like this were made for churches and private devotion to encourage viewers to share the saint's absorption. A skull and crucifix, when present, point to meditation on death and Christ's Passion.","c":"In his Genoese years Strozzi blended Caravaggio's earthiness with the rich color and fluent brushwork he knew from Rubens and Van Dyck, both active in Genoa. Pictures of this period are usually admired for physical presence and sensuous paint more than for subtlety of feeling, and a saint is rendered as a living man. It is a strong example of the type rather than a surprise."},"nga:46140":{"d":"A vertical portrait, about 147 by 95 centimeters, of Bishop Alvise Grimani, in oil on canvas, dated 1633 or after. At this scale a three-quarter-length format would be normal for the type, showing the sitter's vestments and bearing as well as his face. It is by Bernardo Strozzi, the Genoese painter who settled in Venice around 1630 and became one of its leading portraitists.","i":"A portrait of a bishop from a leading patrician family joined two kinds of authority: church office and family rank. His name marks him as a Grimani, a house that gave Venice doges and cardinals, and younger sons of such families were often placed in senior church posts. Vestments, gestures and setting in such portraits were chosen to display dignity, learning and piety, and to hand the likeness down as a statement of the family's standing.","c":"Strozzi's Venetian portraits are usually praised for solid presence and a rich, loaded brush, and a prelate's robes give a painter of his appetite for texture ample room. He shows the sitter as a man of weight without idealizing him. As an official portrait it is conventional in format, but the paint handling and the sense of personality are what distinguish it from routine commissions."},"met:437763":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 146 by 224 centimetres, shows the climax of the Book of Tobit. The young Tobias treats the eyes of his blind father Tobit with the gall of a fish while the angel Raphael, who has travelled with him in disguise, stands by. The Met's keywords also record a woman, the fish and a dog: Tobit's wife Anna, the fish from the river, and the family dog that, in the book, went with Tobias on his journey.","i":"In Tobit 11, Tobias applies the fish's gall to his father's eyes, as the angel had advised, and white films fall away so that Tobit can see again; in Tobit 12 Raphael reveals himself as one of the seven angels who stand before God. The story is a lesson in family devotion and divine providence, and it was especially favoured in Catholic Italy, where Raphael was honoured as protector of travellers and the young.","c":"A broad, generous painting in Strozzi's idiom: energetic brushwork, warm colour and robust, earthy figures, with the old man observed, as Strozzi's old men characteristically are, with sympathy. The dog and fish add lively, homely detail. The risk is looseness: the design can seem diffuse, and he is less poised than Reni and less thrilling than Rubens, whose influence he absorbed. Even so, the bold painterly warmth gives the picture an immediacy."},"cma:110566":{"d":"A woman, identified by recent scholarship as Minerva, the Roman goddess of war, gazes upward as if seeking inspiration, having put aside her armour for more contemplative pursuits. The canvas is large, about 146 by 100 centimetres, and was painted around 1636, when Strozzi was working in Venice. It shows the goddess in the guise of a thoughtful female figure rather than a warrior. A preparatory drawing for it is also in the Cleveland Museum of Art.","i":"In the 1630s Strozzi painted a number of female figures representing intellectual and artistic pursuits, reflecting the appeal of such allegories among learned patrons in northern Italy. Minerva, goddess of wisdom as well as war, was an apt figure for the arts and for eloquence, and her upward gaze signals inspiration. The picture presents an ideal: war laid aside for the life of the mind. For years it was known only as an allegorical figure, and the identification as Minerva is recent.","c":"Strozzi is at his best in such half-portrait, half-allegory figures: warm flesh tones, rich fabrics and a lively, confident brush give the goddess a living presence. The upward glance is a standard but effective sign of inspiration. The weakness is the generic quality of allegory, and Minerva's attributes are subdued enough that the subject took time to identify. But the painting's vigour and colour make it enjoyable regardless of its meaning."},"nga:33255":{"d":"A boy or young man is shown from the chest up in a vertical panel, his body angled slightly to the viewer's right and his hazel eyes glancing at the viewer from their corners. He wears a soft black cap, slightly askew, over long chestnut hair that flows past his shoulders, and a collarless crimson jacket with dark brown trim, with pleated ivory-white shirt material puffing out at the shoulders and neck and a brown band with black knot-like designs. Light falls from the right and above, and the background is teal blue.","i":"The sitter's name is not known. The portrait comes from the Milan of Ludovico Sforza in the 1490s, where Leonardo da Vinci's example of three-quarter views and soft shading shaped local painters. Boltraffio, the most gifted of Leonardo's Milanese followers, applied it to portraits of young Milanese nobles. The crimson jacket, black cap and embroidered shirt mark a fashionable and prosperous youth, and the cap worn at an angle gives him a hint of youthful swagger.","c":"Boltraffio at his best: the modelling is delicate and smoky, the glance sideways gives the youth a private, thoughtful air, and the crimson against the teal ground makes a bold colour contrast. It lacks Leonardo's depth, and the face has something of a doll's smoothness, but within the Leonardesque tradition it is one of the most attractive portraits of its day, and shows how Leonardo's new manner passed into the work of his Milanese circle."},"met:852668":{"d":"A village sits beside its river in this oil on canvas of about 73 by 92 cm, dated 1899. Picquigny is a town on the Somme in northern France, and the title names the place. Frits Thaulow, a Norwegian painter who lived in France in the 1890s, specialized in rivers, village streets and reflections, and here he treats the quiet water and the buildings along it as the subject of the picture.","i":"Picquigny is known in history for the treaty of 1475, in which Edward IV of England and Louis XI of France met on a bridge over the Somme and agreed to end the war. For Thaulow it was a picturesque river town, one among many in Picardy and Normandy that he painted. Rivers and villages offered him the changing play of light on water, and a calm place like this suited the contemplative Nordic Impressionism of his generation.","c":"Thaulow is the painter of water, and his best pictures hold reflections and the glitter of light on a surface with real skill, combining the tonal tradition of Barbizon with the Impressionists' color. The weakness is monotony: village after village rendered in the same formula, so that places blur together. This canvas is typical in its virtues, a confident, atmospheric view, and its limits, a pleasantly familiar mood more than a fresh discovery."},"nga:204":{"d":"In a room with avocado-green walls, three people cluster around and support a pale young woman who leans back on a pillow, while an older man looks on. Her eyes are barely open, one hand rests on her breast, and her pink gown opens over a white chemise. A man behind looks down at her face, a blond man in a yellow robe braces her, and a woman in teal satin holds a glass vial. At left a round gaming table lies toppled, with coins spilled from a red purse and cards scattered on the floor.","i":"Longhi was the chronicler of Venetian daily life, and here a swoon, a fashionable display of feeling, is treated as a small social drama. The overturned table, scattered cards and spilled coins suggest that gambling lies behind the collapse, though Longhi leaves the cause to the viewer. Venice was famous for its passion for play, and the picture can be read as gentle satire on a leisured society that took its amusements seriously.","c":"Longhi's small pictures succeed through observation of manners rather than painterly bravura: a cool, sweet palette of pink, teal, lemon and green, tidy doll-like figures, and a comic crisis played out on a shallow stage. The humor is gentler than Hogarth's and carries no moral thunder. A critic may find the painting thin, but the wit and the careful costume detail make it a sympathetic record of a vanished world."},"nga:205":{"d":"Five people gather in an indoor setting. At the center a man in a light gold embroidered coat stands with a woman in an ivory gown trimmed with pink lace, who holds a fan and wears a pink flower in her powdered hair; another woman stands behind her. At right a blindfolded man in a brown coat with silver embroidery holds a wooden stick, and at lower left a figure in a dark green robe sits on a chair. A small round object lies on the floor, and a table with wine bottles and pastries stands behind.","i":"The Game of the Cooking Pot, or gioco della pignatta, was a traditional carnival amusement in which a blindfolded player tried to smash a pot with a stick. Longhi shows it transplanted into a genteel Venetian interior, where well-dressed company plays at a country game. The picture belongs to his chronicle of Venetian leisure, made as small cabinet pictures for the same kind of patrons who appear in them.","c":"The humor is quiet and the observation sharp: the blindfolded man with his stick, the amused onlookers, the dainty costumes. Longhi's characteristic shallow space lines figures up as on a stage, and the drama is small. A critic could say that his pictures are social records more than great painting, but their intimate scale, light palette and wit make them sympathetic and quietly rewarding."},"met:436903":{"d":"An interior scene with a woman and a man in a room, with a dog among the objects the museum lists; the title suggests a social call. It is oil on canvas, about 61 by 49.5 centimeters, dated 1746, by Pietro Longhi. The vertical cabinet picture of Venetian domestic life is one of four of the same size and date by the artist in the museum, with The Letter, The Meeting and The Temptation.","i":"Longhi specialized in small scenes of Venetian domestic life: polite visits, card games, fittings and flirtations among the leisured classes. A visit was a basic social ritual, in which a gentleman called on a lady in her rooms, and the semi-private encounter allowed conversation, gossip and flirtation under a veneer of politeness. A dog in such a scene is often a symbol of fidelity, though it may be simply a household pet.","c":"Longhi's pictures are modest in scale and ambition, and their appeal lies in observation of manners and costume rather than painterly bravura. The figures can seem doll-like and the space shallow, yet the pale, sweet colors and quiet wit make him the portraitist of his era. As a small social comedy it is neat and agreeable, and its charm depends on the viewer's pleasure in detail."},"met:436904":{"d":"A household scene in oil on canvas, about 61 by 49.5 centimeters, dated 1746. The museum's keywords list a woman, a man, a girl, a child, a doll, laundry, sleep and the reading of a letter, so the picture joins a message with a home in the middle of daily life. It is one of four pictures by Pietro Longhi of the same size and date in the museum, with The Visit, The Meeting and The Temptation.","i":"Letters carried news and secrets in the eighteenth century, and a letter in a genre scene invites the viewer to speculate about its sender and its effect. Longhi places the message inside an ordinary household, among laundry, a doll and a sleeper, so that a private matter is set against the ordinary round of domestic life. Pictures of this kind appealed to Venetian patrons who enjoyed seeing their own world observed with gentle humor.","c":"The interest is in the small contrasts: a letter's secret against the bustle of a household, a child's doll against adult feeling. Longhi's gentle, observant manner suits the theme, though his figures remain doll-like and his space shallow. It does not strive for psychological depth, and a viewer expecting the suspense of Dutch letter pictures will find a lighter, more social comedy."},"met:436905":{"d":"A woman and a man meet in a scene the museum's keywords describe through costume: lace, a shawl, a stomacher, a glove, a cap and a shoe, with masks among its themes. Painted in 1746 by Pietro Longhi in oil on canvas, about 61 by 49.5 centimeters, it is a vertical cabinet picture and one of four of the same size and date in the museum, with The Visit, The Letter and The Temptation.","i":"In eighteenth-century Venice masks were worn publicly for months of the year, letting people of different ranks mingle with a degree of anonymity, so a masked meeting was a classic setting for flirtation and intrigue. The long list of costume details in the museum's keywords is a reminder that clothing signaled rank and fashion, and that Longhi's pictures are among the best visual records of what Venetians wore.","c":"Longhi's subject here is as much costume as drama: the lace, shawl, stomacher and glove are painted with the careful affection of a chronicler. The result is charming rather than gripping, and the meeting is a tableau, not a story. Its value is documentary and decorative, and it shows a painter who found his niche by looking closely at the city around him."},"met:436906":{"d":"A scene in an interior with a man and women, whose figures the museum's keywords identify as including a prostitute and a domestic worker. The title promises a moral drama set within a household. It is oil on canvas, about 61 by 49.5 centimeters, dated 1746, by Pietro Longhi, and it is one of four of the same size and date in the museum, with The Visit, The Letter and The Meeting.","i":"Moralizing scenes of temptation, the maid lured away or the young man corrupted by bad company, were stock subjects in genre painting across Europe, here transposed to Venice. Longhi's tone is wry rather than preachy: he observes and leaves the moral to the viewer. For Venetian audiences, such a picture touched the real moral anxieties of a city famous for its pleasures, and for the vulnerability of those who served in its households.","c":"Longhi treats a charged subject with his usual lightness, and the restraint is both his strength and his limit. A sterner moralist, such as Hogarth, would press the lesson; Longhi lets the situation speak, in a tidy interior with cool colors and neat detail. The picture is more a social document than a work of high drama, and it is most rewarding as part of the set of four."},"met:13132":{"d":"A man works in a workshop interior, as the title and the museum's keywords tell: a coppersmith at his craft. The canvas is about 61 by 53 centimetres, and the record gives no further description, so his pose, tools, the metalwork around him and the lighting cannot be described with confidence. Pictures of craftsmen in their workshops were a staple of late-nineteenth-century genre painting, valued for the glow of metal and the dignity of skilled labour.","i":"Artisans at work interested painters in the 1890s as figures of skill and tradition at a time of rapid industrialisation, and many American artists chose such subjects for their combination of picturesque detail and moral worth. Edgar Melville Ward, an American painter, painted this one about 1898, and the Met received it in 1900 as a gift from a group of donors.","c":"A craft picture of the type that sold well to museums and collectors of the day. Without the details to judge, one can say that such works depend on the observed truth of the worker's gesture and the sparkle of the copper, and that their main danger is a sentimental picturesqueness. Their appeal is the sense of work as something dignified and skilled, and this one was thought worth giving to a national museum."},"met:436814":{"d":"A garden with cypress trees in Italy is the subject of this oil on canvas, about 49 by 65 cm, dated 1894. Kaulbach is best known as a Munich portraitist of elegant women and children, so a landscape is a rarity and perhaps a relaxation from his society work. The title names the place as Italian, and the dark, upright cypresses are the element that gives the picture its structure.","i":"Since Goethe's time, German artists had gone to Italy for light, gardens and the classical past, and the cypress, an evergreen of Mediterranean villas and cemeteries alike, became the emblem of that southern world, associated both with formal gardens and with remembrance. An Italian garden was a place of retreat, with its walks, walls and shade. For a German painter in the 1890s, such a picture was a souvenir of a journey and a statement of cultivated taste.","c":"A garden study from an artist whose reputation rested elsewhere often has a spontaneity that society portraits lack, and the dark vertical cypresses give a strong compositional rhythm. What it lacks, perhaps, is the polish and the assurance of his portraits, and it does not claim the intensity of the Italian landscapes of Böcklin. It is a pleasant, honest, relaxed picture, valuable as a glimpse of a portraitist at leisure."},"met:10485":{"d":"The artist's wife, Maria Pease, is portrayed in a miniature in watercolor on ivory, only 5.7 by 4.6 centimeters, painted in the late 1830s. The tiny scale makes it an object for the hand, a pocket or a case. Alvan Clark painted the portrait of the woman he married in 1826 at a time when miniatures were still the standard likeness, though the daguerreotype was about to challenge them.","i":"Alvan Clark is better known as a maker of telescopes. He worked as a portrait painter in Massachusetts and turned to optics in the 1840s, and he and his sons became the leading American makers of refracting telescopes, with lenses used in observatories across the world. Miniatures were his trade before photography undercut it, and the patient, precise hand that painted them also ground his lenses.","c":"Portraits of a spouse by a professional are often the most direct of the maker's work, and this miniature is plausibly no exception. Clark's miniatures are honest rather than flattering, the work of a man who looked closely at the physical world. As an artist he is minor; as a human document, a husband's picture of his wife, it is particularly appealing."},"met:10484":{"d":"A woman, unnamed in the title, is portrayed in a miniature of watercolor on ivory, about 9 by 6.5 centimeters, painted about 1835. At this scale the picture is mostly a face and a little costume, and the thin ivory support gives the colors a delicate glow. Held in the hand or kept in a case, it was made to be looked at closely and privately.","i":"Miniatures on ivory were the main portrait medium for middle-class Americans in the 1830s. Cheaper than oil and easy to carry, they were given at engagements and weddings, worn as lockets and sent to distant relatives. A Portrait of a Lady with no recorded name shows how such objects drifted from their families, leaving the beauty of the work but not the identity of the person.","c":"Clark worked in the fine, stippled manner of the miniaturist's craft, building a face from tiny touches over the glow of the ivory. The result is a gentle and reserved likeness, with little attempt at flattery or drama. The anonymity of the sitter limits the interest, and the genre allows little invention, but within its tiny frame the picture shows a careful, honest hand."},"met:12887":{"d":"A woman, Mrs. Albert Bridges, born Eliza Kimball, is portrayed in a miniature of watercolor on ivory, about 6.4 by 5.3 centimeters, painted about 1839. The title gives both her married and her maiden name, a common form in museum records. The tiny, intimate scale made the picture a keepsake, to be worn or kept in a case, and gives most of its small space to her face.","i":"Naming a married woman as Mrs. followed by her husband's name, with her maiden name in parentheses, is the convention of the day and of the record. Such miniatures marked a marriage or were given between family members, and they often passed down through families until donated to museums. Clark painted many of them in Boston before he turned to making telescopes in the 1840s.","c":"A competent miniature in which the care is in the face. Clark does not idealize his sitters, which gives the portraits a plain truthfulness, though the pose and treatment follow the conventions of the genre. It is a pleasing object, small enough to hold, and will reward anyone willing to look closely at its tiny touches."},"met:10483":{"d":"Dr. Samuel A. Bemis is the sitter of this miniature in watercolor on ivory, about 7.6 by 6.4 centimeters, painted about 1840. The title gives his professional rank, which mattered in the social world of 1840, and the picture follows the usual form of the American miniature: a small, bust-sized likeness in which the face is painted with the finest care. Held in the hand, it would have been looked at closely and privately, as a keepsake for family.","i":"A miniature of a professional man was a mark of respectability, given to family or kept as a record of success. Alvan Clark painted such portraits in Boston in the late 1830s and 1840s, just as the daguerreotype was arriving to offer a quicker and cheaper alternative. The use of Dr. in the title reminds a modern viewer how important a professional title was in the social world of the time.","c":"A small, careful likeness of a man in the public eye. Clark's strength is plain observation, and this portrait is typical of his output: sound drawing, restrained color and an unembellished face. It does not show a flash of personality, and the miniature's tiny scale limits what any painter could do, but the record of a physical presence is clear and convincing."},"met:15023":{"d":"John Pickering is the sitter of this miniature in watercolor on ivory, painted about 1840; its size is not recorded, but miniatures of this kind are small enough to hold in the hand. If, as seems likely, he is John Pickering (1777-1846), the Massachusetts lawyer and scholar of languages, the sitter was in his sixties, a distinguished citizen of Boston. It is a private likeness, made for family and friends rather than for public display.","i":"John Pickering compiled a vocabulary of Americanisms in 1816 and a Greek-English lexicon in 1826, and studied Native American languages. A miniature by Alvan Clark, who painted portraits in Boston before turning to telescope-making, gave a prominent townsman a small private likeness for his family. Clark's later fame as a maker of great telescope lenses reflects the same precise, patient hand.","c":"Portraits of scholars invite attention to the face, and Clark's miniature, a modest and truthful likeness, does what the genre asks. It will not astonish, but with a sitter known to later history the miniature gains interest beyond its craft, and here is the face of an early collector of Americanisms. As with Clark's other miniatures, the pleasure lies in the precision."},"nga:37630":{"d":"A seated woman is shown in side profile against a plain, warm brown ground. Her brown hair is neatly arranged beneath a lace bonnet, and she wears formal dress with a high lace collar, a large oval brooch at the chest and a ring on her left hand. The features are soft and gently modeled, and with no furniture or landscape to distract, the eye goes to the face and to the fine lace and jewelry. Oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1845.","i":"The title gives the sitter by her husband's name, as was customary for a married woman, and her lace bonnet, collar and brooch mark her as a respectable matron. Portraits like this were commissioned to keep a likeness within the family, even as the daguerreotype began to compete for such work. The painter, Alvan Clark, turned within a few years from portraits to grinding telescope lenses, the craft for which he is best remembered.","c":"A plain, dignified portrait in which the lace and brooch get more careful attention than the empty brown setting. The profile pose gives a clean silhouette and a calm presence but little psychological probing. Clark was a capable portraitist, not an original one, and history has remembered his lenses rather than his paintings. As a record of a sitter, though, the picture is unfussy and honest."},"met:678013":{"d":"A tiger rests in this small oil on canvas, about 27 by 36 centimeters, painted between about 1850 and 1865 by the French sculptor Antoine-Louis Barye. The museum describes it as animal art. Barye's best-known works are bronzes of big cats and other wild animals in combat, and this small painting translates his study of the tiger's powerful, supple body from sculpture to paint, in a quieter mood than the fights of his bronzes.","i":"Barye studied living animals and skeletons at the Jardin des Plantes in Paris and made the big cats a symbol of Romantic energy; his bronzes of tigers and lions attacking prey were sensations at the Salons of the 1830s. The tiger in repose shows a different side: the beast's power held in reserve. Barye painted in oil largely for himself in his later years, and such small pictures were seldom shown at the time.","c":"Barye the painter is looser and more atmospheric than Barye the sculptor: the oil sketch treats the cat as a mass of color and shadow, with quick brushwork closer to Delacroix, his friend, than to his own bronzes. The size is small and the finish that of a study. It shows what a sculptor sees: weight, muscle, the way a big cat's body folds when it rests."},"nga:65827":{"d":"A table laid with food and vessels and centered on a ham, as the title says, painted in oil on a tall panel about 98 by 83 centimeters and dated 1650. Gerret Willemsz. Heda belonged to the Haarlem school of breakfast and banquet pieces developed by his father, Willem Claesz. Heda, and by Pieter Claesz., in which a few costly objects are set on a cloth-covered table and typically lit from one side.","i":"Still lifes of food and drink displayed prosperity: pewter, silver and glass were costly, ham and lemon were luxuries, and the order of the arrangement spoke of a well-run household. Many historians also see a quiet moral note, since half-eaten food, a tilted vessel or a peeled lemon recalls how quickly pleasure passes; how far a particular picture intends that message is debated. Such panels hung in the homes of prosperous burghers.","c":"The Heda manner is about texture and restraint: the sheen of metal, the clarity of glass and the matte surface of bread and meat, each separated from the next by tonal subtlety. Gerret inherited the discipline from his father, and a large panel like this shows the family's command of it on an ambitious scale. The effect is cool and composed rather than appetizing; the craft is secure, if less inventive than the best of Willem Claesz."},"met:437903":{"d":"A young woman in a white muslin dress sits turned on a chair at a tall window, a large portfolio resting on her lap and a pen held lightly in her other hand, and looks round at the viewer as if interrupted. Her light brown hair is gathered up in loose curls, and a pink sash is tied under her breast. A rose-red cloth drapes the chair, and a red slipper shows beneath her skirt. The room is dark, and daylight from the window falls across her face and dress. Through the glass appears a pale building with a terrace on which two tiny figures stand close together under an evening sky.","i":"For much of the twentieth century this picture was catalogued as the work of the far more famous Jacques-Louis David and admired as one of his finest portraits; in the 1990s it was reassigned to Marie Denise Villers (1774–1821), a Parisian painter. The Met identifies the sitter as Charlotte du Val d'Ognes and dates the picture to 1801. The subject, a woman shown as an artist at work, belongs to the years after the Revolution, when the Salon had been opened to all artists and women painters were more visible than before. The couple at the window add a note of romance whose meaning is uncertain.","c":"A strikingly modern picture. The play of window light on white muslin is handled with a clarity that David's followers rarely reached, and the young woman's direct, slightly amused glance gives the portrait immediacy. The tiny couple on the terrace makes the street outside as vivid as the room. The composition is awkwardly tilted, with the portfolio hiding the body and the foreshortened foot oddly placed, but those oddities are part of its charm. That a canvas of this quality was taken for David for so long says as much about assumptions as about the painter's accomplishment."},"met:904366":{"d":"A very small painting, about 26 by 18.5 centimeters, in oil on canvas, from about 1801, showing a woman seated on a balustrade. The title records only the pose and the setting, not a named sitter, so it may be a portrait, a study or a small figure picture. The vertical format and tiny scale suggest a work for private enjoyment rather than for public exhibition.","i":"Women painters found new opportunities in post-Revolutionary Paris, when the Salon opened to all artists, and Marie Denise Villers, who trained in the circle of Marguerite Gérard and Girodet, was among those who exhibited there around 1800. A seated figure at a balustrade places the sitter at the edge between interior and open air, a setting often used in portraits of the period to suggest light, leisure and reverie.","c":"Villers is remembered for a handful of portraits of unusual clarity and intelligence in the handling of light, and a small picture like this is best seen as a glimpse of the same eye at work on a modest scale. With no documented sitter or purpose it offers pleasure rather than argument. The pose against architecture is the point to enjoy, and the brevity of the means is part of the appeal."},"met:15095":{"d":"A miniature of about 7 by 5.6 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1839, depicts a man. The title does not name him. It dates from the years when Emanuel Leutze, a young painter raised in Philadelphia, supported himself with portraits before going to study in Düsseldorf in 1841. At this size the portrait could be held in the palm, and as is usual for the form, the face is the focus while the dress and background are kept simple.","i":"Emanuel Leutze is best known for history painting, above all Washington Crossing the Delaware of 1851, and this small miniature comes from the beginning of his career, before he turned to large historical subjects. Miniatures like this one were affordable, portable likenesses that painters without a reputation could sell to local sitters. The sitter's name is not recorded in the title.","c":"A modest early work, valuable as a reminder that painters of national subjects often began as providers of small likenesses. As a miniature it is competent but not remarkable, and the scale itself cannot show the dramatic ambition Leutze would bring to historical subjects. Its interest is mostly biographical."},"met:11417":{"d":"A crowded rowboat battles across a river choked with ice floes under a stormy dawn sky. George Washington stands upright in the bow in a dark cloak and cocked hat, gazing ahead, while a standard-bearer beside him struggles to hold up a flag that snaps in the wind. Around them oarsmen of every kind push at the ice with poles and oars: a rifleman in the bow, farmers in broad hats, a man in a Scots bonnet, a Black oarsman facing backward. A band of golden light on the horizon catches Washington's face, and in the right background more boats and horses make for the shore. The canvas is huge, nearly 3.8 by 6.5 meters, and dark in tone.","i":"The subject is the night of December 25–26, 1776, when Washington led the Continental Army across the Delaware River for a surprise attack on Hessian forces at Trenton. Leutze, German-born and raised in America, painted it in Düsseldorf during the revolutions of 1848, hoping the American example would encourage European liberals. Much is idealized: the Stars and Stripes did not exist until 1777, the boat is smaller than the real cargo boats, and the river is said to be modeled on the Rhine. The first version, in Bremen, was destroyed in a bombing raid in 1942; this second, full-size version was shown in New York in 1851 and given to the Met in 1897.","c":"This is a painting of the idea of Washington rather than of an event, and it works on those terms. Leutze controls a vast crowd with clarity: the diagonals of oars and poles, the plates of ice, and the single lit face make the picture legible from across a gallery. Historians point out that a man standing in such a boat would risk capsizing it. The Düsseldorf polish is hard and stagey, and the figures carry more symbol than character. But its simplicity has made it one of the most durable and most parodied images in American history."},"met:11418":{"d":"A man is the subject of this large portrait, about 129 by 103 centimeters, oil on canvas, dated 1856. The sitter is named in the title as Worthington Whittredge, a fellow American painter. The generous scale gives a colleague the treatment of an important sitter, and the single figure fills the canvas. Leutze, then working in Düsseldorf, painted it, and the title is the surest guide to its subject.","i":"Whittredge (1820-1910), a Hudson River School landscape painter, worked in Düsseldorf in the early 1850s, where he knew Leutze well and posed for the figures of Washington and the steersman in Washington Crossing the Delaware. Portraits of friends exchanged between artists recorded the community of Americans abroad. Whittredge later became one of the leading landscape painters in the United States.","c":"A confident portrait of a friend by a painter more used to pageants than portraits. The scale and the personal familiarity give the sitter presence without strain. It lacks the explosive drama of Leutze's history paintings, but a quieter, human subject lets his skill as a painter of faces speak."},"met:11620":{"d":"A portrait of Gideon Tucker, painted in 1830 by the young Long Island artist William Sidney Mount, in oil on canvas, about 87 by 69 centimeters. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait. It is the companion of a portrait of Mrs. Gideon Tucker of the same year and size, so the two were evidently painted as a pair for the same household.","i":"In 1830 Mount was twenty-three and building his career on portrait commissions among Long Island and New York families, work that paid his way while he developed the gift for scenes of everyday life that would make him one of the first major American genre painters. Pendant portraits of husband and wife were the staple of such commissions, and they recorded respectability and a plain, dignified presence rather than display.","c":"A young painter's competent commission, made in a plain, direct manner. The candor and attention to character that mark Mount's later genre pictures are visible here in embryo, though the portrait lacks the charm and invention of his scenes of rural life. It is best valued as a stage on the way to his best work and as an honest record of provincial respectability."},"met:11624":{"d":"A portrait of Martin Euclid Thompson, painted in 1830 by William Sidney Mount, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 62 centimeters. The museum's keywords record only the sitter's name and the genre. Thompson (1786 to 1877) was a New York architect, and the picture, somewhat smaller than the Tucker portraits Mount painted in the same year, belongs to the portrait practice that supported the young artist.","i":"Mount, newly established in New York, was building a clientele among professional and artistic circles in 1830, and the portrait of an architect belongs to that world. Thompson's buildings were part of the new commercial city then taking shape in New York, and a portrait ties the painter to the patrons who were making it. Portraits like this also served as calling cards for a young painter seeking further commissions.","c":"A direct, unadorned likeness: at this stage Mount avoids both flattery and ornament, and the strength of the picture is the candor of the face. It is not the equal of his genre scenes, in which his feeling for character comes alive in narrative, but it is a sound example of the honest, workmanlike portrait practice that supported him in his early years."},"met:11626":{"d":"A portrait of Mrs. Gideon Tucker, painted in 1830 by William Sidney Mount, in oil on canvas, about 87 by 69 centimeters, the same size as the portrait of her husband. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, and her own first name is not given in the title, a reminder of the period's convention of naming a wife by her husband's name.","i":"A wife's pendant portrait recorded her place in a family and community as much as her looks: quiet dress and a plain setting suggested respectability, modesty and good household management, qualities valued in a society where such families formed the backbone of local life. For Mount, then at the start of his career on Long Island and in New York, commissions like this were a dependable source of income.","c":"As a companion portrait it is best judged beside its pair, and its interest lies in the same honest directness one expects from the young Mount. It is competent and unshowy rather than lively; his real strengths were still to appear in the genre scenes he was beginning to exhibit around this time. A modest picture, and a useful document of his early practice."},"met:11627":{"d":"Men gather around a raffle for a goose, the prize named in the title, in a scene the museum's keywords reduce to men and food. Mount painted it in oil on a mahogany panel, about 43 by 59 centimeters, in 1837, the same year as The Tough Story. It belongs to his small, humorous pictures of rural male company, in which a shared pastime draws a group of characters together.","i":"Raffling a bird was a familiar country pastime, part of the sociable gambling of taverns and village gatherings in nineteenth-century America. Mount found in such scenes a way to show ordinary Americans with humor and without condescension, at a moment when audiences were learning to value pictures of their own world. The goose, a plump holiday prize, stands for the plain abundance of rural life.","c":"Mount's gift is to give each member of a group a distinct temperament, and a raffle is an ideal occasion, since hope, doubt and rivalry all show on faces. The risk is a drift toward stock comic types, yet the small, smooth panel suits his precise, economical handling. It is slighter than his best tavern pictures, but an engaging example of American genre painting in its early days."},"nga:166464":{"d":"Three men sit and stand around a rusty stove in a room with wide plank floors. At left an injured man, head and knee bandaged and a crutch against his leg, leans forward and points at a pipe-smoking man opposite, a glass in his hand. A third man behind, in a fur-trimmed cloak and fur hat, smiles with his head tipped down. A top hat spills playing cards, bottles fill a cabinet, and a Long Island Railroad notice hangs on the wall. Oil on wood, about 43 by 56 centimeters, signed and dated 1837.","i":"A tough story was a tall tale, and the picture catches the moment when the teller's listeners decide how far to believe him. The smoker looks past the injured man and the third man hides a smile, so the audience seems skeptical. The Long Island Railroad notice, a new enterprise in 1837, may hint at the subject of the tale, and the tavern setting places the scene in the rural male society Mount knew from his Long Island home.","c":"A model of Mount's skill at storytelling through expression alone: the injured man's pointing finger, the smoker's sidelong gaze and the hidden smile of the third man tell the whole story. The stove anchors the group like the center of a stage, and details such as the spilled hat and the bottles give anecdote without clutter. The humor is affectionate rather than mocking, which is why the picture has outlived its period."},"met:11619":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 69 by 87 centimeters, painted in 1840 to 1841, showing the making of cider in a farmyard. The museum's keywords list men, a woman, children, a dog, a pig, a chicken and barrels, and the theme of work, so the picture is a busy rural scene in which labor, family life and animals share the same space. It is larger and more ambitious than Mount's small tavern scenes.","i":"Hard cider was a staple drink of rural America, pressed from apples each autumn, and cider making was a seasonal community task. Mount painted Long Island life with warmth and without condescension, in years when American patrons were beginning to want pictures of their own country's ordinary people. A scene of cheerful rural labor also carried a message of abundance and contentment that suited the young republic's self-image.","c":"At this scale Mount can build a whole community into one picture, and his strength is the way each figure keeps an individual manner within a unified scene. The risk is sentiment, since the labor looks idealized and the rural world untroubled. Even so, the observation of animals, tools and gestures is convincing, and the picture remains a valuable document of nineteenth-century farm life."},"cma:155783":{"d":"The view looks into a barn through an open door. Inside, in the shadow, a young man in a vest and cap sits on a stool and plays the fiddle. Beside him an older man in a straw hat and green coat listens, and a taller man in a pale coat and wide hat leans in the doorway with his hands in his pockets. Outside, in the light at the right, a young Black man in a tan coat, patched blue overalls and a yellow neckerchief leans on the door, holding a hat and smiling as he listens. A pitchfork leans on the wall, and a jug and an axe set in a block sit among the straw and corn husks. It is a small, finely worked oil on canvas.","i":"Cleveland places the scene in rural Long Island before the Civil War. It shows an African American laborer listening intently to a fiddle tune enjoyed by white men. Music links the figures in a bond of shared humanity, but they occupy separate spaces, one inside and one outside, divided by the barn door, which the museum reads as a symbol of the pronounced divisions in America at the time. Mount, a Long Island native, was an accomplished fiddle player and even patented a new design for the violin. Modern writers have debated how far the picture shows sympathy and how far it merely reflects the social distance of its day.","c":"The composition does its work gracefully. The door acts as a frame within the picture, the warm light picks out the listener, and the shadowed musicians recede just as they should. Mount shows real respect for his Black subject, giving him dignity and individual features. Yet the picture still places him outside, looking in, and it is hard to tell whether the painter sees this as a problem or simply as a fact. The ambiguity makes the picture richer to think about, if slightly evasive."},"met:11621":{"d":"An old man, identified as the Reverend Zachariah Greene, tells a story to a boy and a girl; the long title calls it Great-Grand-Father's Tale of the Revolution. The oil on canvas, about 61 by 51 centimeters, is dated 1852. It combines portrait and genre scene, showing a real person in the act of passing on memory to the children who listen.","i":"By 1852 the soldiers of the Revolution had almost all died, and Americans were beginning to treat their stories with reverence. A picture of a great-grandfather telling the tale to children turns memory into inherited duty. Mount portrays a real person, the Reverend Zachariah Greene, in the role of storyteller, uniting a portrait with a genre scene and answering the public's growing appetite for national history.","c":"A tender picture in which Mount's usual humor gives way to reverence. The children's rapt attention turns a portrait into an image of memory being handed on, and the old man's presence supplies the weight. It leans toward sentiment, as many pictures of the period did, but its sincerity is evident, and it shows how genre painting could carry a nation's memory."},"met:11623":{"d":"A horizontal landscape, about 56 by 76 centimeters, painted in 1862 to 1863 in oil on canvas, showing farmhouses among trees on Long Island, the countryside where Mount was born and lived. The museum's keywords list trees, houses and a farm, so the picture is a plain view of working farmland rather than a grand prospect. It is a late work, from the last years of Mount's career.","i":"Mount spent his life on Long Island, and its farms, fields and people supplied nearly all his subjects. A picture of farmhouses and trees, painted in the early years of the Civil War, can be read as a quiet image of rural continuity at a moment of national upheaval. The plain, unglamorous view belongs to the strand of American landscape that valued ordinary scenery over sublime peaks.","c":"A late, unforced picture: Mount, who made his name with figures, here turns the same patient observation to buildings, trees and light. It lacks the animation of his genre scenes, and the absence of figures leaves it quiet, almost reticent. As a document of Long Island's vernacular architecture and as a reminder of his range it is a pleasing, modest work."},"met:436637":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 135 by 185 centimeters, showing a sumptuous table. The museum's keywords list a lemon, a lobster, fruit, grapes and even a chair, all part of a banqueting scene painted about 1640 to 1641 by Jan Davidsz. de Heem. At this scale the picture is a showpiece, meant to be seen from across a room, with costly foods gathered to display abundance.","i":"De Heem, born in Utrecht and active in Leiden and Antwerp, was a master of the pronk, or sumptuous, still life, in which lobsters, lemons, fruit and glass declared worldly wealth. The abundance also carried a moral warning, since food spoils and pleasures pass, a message that coexisted with open delight in luxury. Large canvases like this hung in the homes of rich merchants and wealthy collectors.","c":"De Heem blended Dutch precision with Flemish richness, and the table glitters: the lobster's shell, the lemon's peel and the bloom on the grapes are tests of illusion. At this size display tips into theater, and a viewer may prefer the intimacy of his small panels, but this is the kind of picture that made his reputation across the northern and southern Netherlands."},"met:436636":{"d":"A very small panel, about 25 by 19 centimeters, painted about 1640. On a table covered with a cloth, the museum's keywords list oysters, a lemon wedge, grapes and other fruit, a leaf and a glass. The tiny vertical format, the few objects and the close viewpoint make it an intimate picture, meant to be examined at short range for its finish.","i":"Oysters and lemon were a luxurious pairing of the Dutch table, and oysters also carried associations with pleasure and the senses. The fragile glass, the cut lemon and the fruit are staples of the small banquet still lifes that De Heem painted around 1640: a display of skill that also hints, as such pictures often do, at the brevity of enjoyment.","c":"A jewel of a picture: the miniature scale rewards the closest looking, and the gleam of oysters, the glow of a lemon and the transparency of a glass are exactly the effects such panels exist to show. De Heem is generally at his most refined in his small works, and one senses an artist enjoying the discipline of a few elements. It lacks the grandeur of his banquets but shows the same skill in concentrated form."},"nga:46097":{"d":"A vase of flowers, in oil on canvas, about 70 by 57 centimeters, painted about 1660 by Jan Davidsz. de Heem. The title and the vertical format are those of the Netherlandish flower piece, in which a bouquet of many species is set in a vase against a dark or plain ground, often with insects or a few fallen blossoms to add life and variety to the arrangement.","i":"Flower paintings were prized for their rarity and cost: tulips, roses and other species that bloom in different seasons were combined from separate studies into a bouquet that could never have existed at one moment. Collectors enjoyed this as a celebration of creation and of the painter's skill, and many viewers also read the fading blooms as a reminder of transience.","c":"De Heem's flower pieces of about 1660 are known for dense, jewel-like detail and a luminous contrast between bright blooms and dark ground. A picture of this kind demands patience from the viewer, since the pleasure is in discovering species and textures one by one. It is a refined late example, though the genre's formula leaves little room for surprise."},"nga:117959":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 48 by 82.5 centimeters, painted about 1645 to 1646 by Philips Wouwerman. As the title says, it is a battle scene of the kind Wouwerman made in numbers: a skirmish of mounted soldiers in an open landscape, with small figures and horses set in a broad space, rather than a record of a named engagement. Such cabinet pictures were made for collectors who enjoyed lively action and expert drawing of horses.","i":"Wouwerman, of Haarlem, became one of the most popular painters in the seventeenth-century Netherlands with pictures of horses, hunts and cavalry skirmishes. The Dutch Republic had been at war with Spain for most of his lifetime, and the war ended with the Peace of Münster in 1648, shortly after this picture was painted, but pictures of this kind were entertainment and an exercise in soldierly spirit rather than history.","c":"Wouwerman's early cavalry fights are admired for quick, silvery handling, a gift for putting many small figures into clear action and, above all, the drawing of horses. The subject can feel generic, and the clash is more choreography than drama. Still, as an early example of a painter who would perfect the type, it displays his vivacity and his command of space."},"met:437953":{"d":"A small panel, about 31 by 41 centimeters, painted about 1653 to 1654 by Philips Wouwerman. The museum's keywords list a man and a woman on horseback, the woman riding sidesaddle, in a landscape with trees, a boat and bathers. It is a leisurely picture of an outing, not a hunt or a battle, and the small format suits a cabinet picture for close viewing.","i":"Riding for pleasure was a pastime of the well-off, and the sidesaddle marks the woman as a lady, as it was the conventional way for women to ride in the period. The boat and the bathers in the background add notes of leisure, and the picture presents an idealized world of ease in a landscape. Wouwerman's pictures of horsemen and riding parties were popular with collectors.","c":"Wouwerman's horses are the real subject: well drawn, and set with confidence into a luminous landscape. The small scale suits his precise touch. The scene is pleasant rather than profound, and some viewers may find the formula repetitive, but for those who enjoy the poise of a rider and mount it is a fine specimen of his mid-career manner."},"nga:218605":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 52 by 67 centimeters, painted about 1665 to 1668 by Philips Wouwerman, showing a hunting party about to set out. Wouwerman's hunting pictures usually gather elegantly dressed riders, horses, dogs and attendants in a sunlit landscape, often with a pale horse among them. The title promises the moment before the chase, a time of anticipation and display.","i":"Hunting was an aristocratic pastime and a favorite subject for Wouwerman's patrons; pictures of the departure for the hunt gave them the pleasure of ceremony, dress and horses without the violence of the kill. The setting suggests a country estate, rank and leisure, and Wouwerman's paintings were collected widely across Europe, so the type became a standard image of a gentleman's outdoor life.","c":"In his later years Wouwerman's pictures often grew more elegant and softly lit, and this late hunt shows his mastery of the type. A crowd of riders and dogs must be arranged so that the eye keeps moving, and that is where his skill lies. The charm is in finesse and horse-drawing rather than depth, and it is a polished product of a famously productive workshop."},"nga:41661":{"d":"A young barefoot child in a dark blue gown sits on a low ledge between thin white columns with gold detailing, one hand outstretched and the other near his chest, surrounded by older men in turbans, hats, hoods and colorful robes, some holding books or scrolls and talking. Behind him a man in pink and a woman in blue, her hands crossed on her chest, look on; a pale pink castle rises beyond, and small gold figures fill niches under blue vaults. Oil on panel, about 54 by 33 centimeters, c. 1513.","i":"In Luke's Gospel, twelve-year-old Jesus stays behind in Jerusalem after the Passover; his parents find him after three days in the Temple among the teachers, listening and asking questions, and all who hear him are amazed at his understanding. The scene foreshadows his ministry, and Mary and Joseph's distress at losing him counts among the Seven Sorrows of the Virgin. Painters often set it within a grand architectural frame, here an ornate columned portico.","c":"Orley, a Brussels painter who would become court painter to Margaret of Austria in 1518, was still young here, and the panel crams rich detail into a small space: gilded ornament, varied turbans and robes, a pale castle in the distance. The architecture, mingling Gothic and early Renaissance ornament, is the most inventive part. One admires the richness of the setting more than any emotional force, in a promising early work."},"nga:41662":{"d":"The back of a small panel, about 54 by 33 centimeters, painted about 1513 by Bernard van Orley in oil. It shows a putto, a plump, childlike figure, with the coat of arms of Jacques Coëne, and the museum lists its theme only as a symbol. Heraldic decoration of this kind was painted on the outer side of a panel, where it could identify the owner without competing with the devotional scene on the front.","i":"Heraldry on a devotional panel identified the patron or owner and tied a family to the picture's devotion. A putto presenting a coat of arms was a fashionable Renaissance motif, borrowed from classical and Italian art, and its appearance on a Netherlandish panel of about 1513 shows how quickly such motifs had spread north. Coëne is presumably the donor or first owner, though little about him is documented here.","c":"Modest but revealing: the reverse of a panel is rarely seen, yet it carries the evidence of who paid for the work and what he wanted it to say. As an image it is less than the scene on the front, but Orley handles the playful putto with ease. It is mainly of interest as a clue to ownership and as an example of how thoroughly early panels were designed."},"nga:41663":{"d":"At the center a woman in a long blue dress, a golden crown over her long blonde hair, joins hands with a man in a red-pink robe, while people in a mixture of robes stand on either side and look at the couple. Behind them rises an ornate building with arches, columns and intricate stonework under a bright blue sky. The oil on panel is a small vertical picture, about 54 by 33 centimeters, painted about 1513 by Bernard van Orley.","i":"The Marriage of the Virgin shows Mary's betrothal to Joseph, a scene from her life told in the Golden Legend and the apocryphal gospels. In the usual account Joseph is chosen from among Mary's suitors when his staff miraculously blossoms. Such scenes decorated altarpieces and prayer books and affirmed that Christ's birth took place within a lawful, blessed marriage.","c":"A small panel of lively invention: the architecture, with its arches, columns and stonework, is as much the subject as the couple, and Orley uses it to show an up-to-date taste for classical ornament grafted onto a Gothic tradition. The faces and gestures look conventional beside the setting. As an early work it is interesting more for ambition than for emotional force."},"met:438467":{"d":"An interior scene about 64 by 76 centimetres, painted in oil on wood, shows the Birth and Naming of Saint John the Baptist: the infant John, his mother Elizabeth in her bed, his father Zechariah and other men and women in a domestic room, with a peacock among the motifs the Met lists. The panel is painted on both sides. On the back is a trompe-l'oeil, an illusion of a real object, with a painting of the Man of Sorrows, the suffering Christ.","i":"The story, told in Luke 1, shows the aged Elizabeth bringing John into the world and Zechariah, struck dumb, confirming the name by writing it down. Netherlandish painters set such scenes in the bedrooms and parlours of their own day, making the sacred story familiar. A panel painted on both faces was meant to be seen turned or opened, perhaps as part of a larger altarpiece. The Met's Martyrdom of Saint John the Baptist, of the same date and size, is probably a companion.","c":"Van Orley, a young painter at this date, shows a gift for rich Netherlandish detail and lively storytelling: textiles, furnishings and the busy room are painted with relish. The trompe-l'oeil on the reverse is a clever demonstration of illusionism and adds wit. Compared with the cooler clarity of earlier Flemish masters it is more crowded and mannered. It is a lively, ingenious picture rather than a profound one."},"met:440209":{"d":"A panel about 65 by 74 centimetres, painted in oil on wood, shows the martyrdom of Saint John the Baptist, who is beheaded at the order of Herod Antipas, with Salome, who asked for his death, among the figures. The Met's keywords record men, women and Salome, so the scene seems to include the household of Herod as well as the executioner. It is close in date, size and subject to Van Orley's Birth and Naming of Saint John the Baptist in the same museum.","i":"According to Mark 6 and Matthew 14, Herod had imprisoned John for condemning his marriage to Herodias, and at his birthday feast he promised Salome, Herodias's daughter, whatever she wished after she danced; prompted by her mother, she asked for John's head on a platter. John's birth and death, shown in two companion panels, framed the Baptist's story. The subject allowed painters to combine piety with the drama of a court crime.","c":"A vivid and decorous treatment of a gruesome subject. Van Orley gives the scene movement and rich costume, and the horror is handled with a measure of restraint rather than gore. The crowded staging can seem stagey, and the figures are typed rather than deeply felt, but the panel shows a young painter with a gift for narrative. It forms a good pair with the Birth and Naming of the same saint."},"met:437229":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child are attended by angels in a panel about 85 by 70 centimetres, painted in oil on wood about 1518. The Met's keywords add a fountain, music and peafowl, so the scene is a garden-like or courtyard setting where angels make music and peacocks stroll. It combines the Netherlandish love of detail with architectural and decorative ideas drawn from Italy, which Van Orley knew through designs and prints reaching Brussels, especially those of Raphael.","i":"The fountain is a traditional symbol of the Virgin and of the source of life, recalling the fountain sealed of the Song of Songs and the idea of Christ as the spring of grace. The angels' music is heavenly praise, and peafowl, whose flesh was thought in antiquity to be incorruptible, stand for immortality. Together the motifs turn a devotional picture into a visual hymn. Van Orley was soon to become court painter to Margaret of Austria in Brussels.","c":"A rich, ambitious picture. Van Orley piles up fountain, music, angels and birds so that the Madonna becomes the centre of a small paradise, and the Italian-style architecture gives a clear stage. The result is more decorative than devout, and Netherlandish detail and Italian forms do not always sit easily together; Mary herself can be overwhelmed by the setting. But the invention, colour and symbolic wit make it an appealing Madonna."},"nga:368":{"d":"A young saint stands facing the viewer on a gray ledge against a rich gold, arched ground, his right leg bent and resting on the ledge. He holds a long red sword in one hand and a red flower with green leaves in the other. His curly, shoulder-length brown hair, arched brows, cleft chin and slight smile give him a courtly look, and he wears a dark blue-green tunic with a high white collar under a long red cloak, burgundy tights and tan boots. A halo-like design is incised around his head. Tempera on poplar, about 79 by 55 centimeters, about 1473 to 1474.","i":"Saint Florian was a Roman officer martyred in what is now Austria in the early fourth century; he is the patron saint of firefighters and is often shown in armor with a banner, a bucket or a sword. Here he is dressed as an elegant young nobleman with a sword and a flower. The panel is generally thought to belong to a polyptych made for the Griffoni chapel in San Petronio, Bologna, which was later broken up.","c":"The panel shows Cossa's art at its Ferrarese best: figures of hard, sculptural clarity, an almost enameled finish, and an air of aristocratic poise. The saint's courtly stance, the leg propped on the ledge and the glossy red cloak give him a swagger more worldly than devout, and the flat gold ground sets off the sharp outlines. It is a polished, slightly cool beauty, typical of the accomplished Ferrara-Bologna school."},"nga:369":{"d":"Seen from the knees up behind a gray ledge, a woman stands against a gold ground in an arched panel, holding a palm frond in one hand and, in the other, a pair of disembodied eyes attached to a stem like a flower. Her body faces the viewer, but her head turns to the left and her hooded gray eyes look down at the eyes she holds, one eyebrow lifted. Blond hair is pulled up under a sheer white head covering. She wears a ruby-red dress under a forest-green robe, and an incised halo radiates behind her head. Tempera on poplar, about 77 by 56 centimeters.","i":"Saint Lucy of Syracuse, a Christian martyr of the early fourth century, is the patron saint of the blind and of eyesight; according to legend her eyes were put out, and in the best-known version they were miraculously restored. Artists show her carrying her eyes on a dish, or as here on a stem, together with the martyr's palm. The unusual flower-like eyes turn a gruesome attribute into a gentle emblem.","c":"Cossa's Lucy is a bold, almost surreal image: the eyes on a stem are treated with complete composure, and the saint's knowing, sidelong look at them gives the panel a wry intelligence. The enamel-hard finish and the crisp textiles are typical of Ferrarese and Bolognese painting at its most refined. It is among the more memorable fifteenth-century saints because it is both devout and strange."},"nga:41615":{"d":"Christ hangs by his outstretched hands from a wooden cross in a round panel, against a shiny gold ground, flanked by two mourners on a gray stone platform. His eyes are closed and his head tips down; blood trickles from the crown of thorns, hands, feet and side, and runs onto a skull at the base of the cross. At left an older woman in a wimple and purple cloak grips her cloak; at right a long-haired man in a garnet cloak looks up, his mouth turned down. An INRI plaque tops the cross. Tempera on poplar, about 60 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The mourners are the Virgin Mary and Saint John the Evangelist, who stood beside the cross according to John's Gospel. The skull at the foot of the cross refers to Golgotha, the Place of the Skull, and to Adam, who in medieval legend was buried there, so that Christ's blood falls on the first man's remains as a sign of redemption. INRI abbreviates the Latin inscription Jesus of Nazareth, King of the Jews. The panel may have crowned a polyptych.","c":"Cossa's stern, taut Crucifixion is notable for its restraint: the figures are few, the gestures compressed, and the grief of the mourners is shown with contained dignity in the closed eyes and pressed lips. The gold ground and the network of fine cracks give the panel a precious, battered presence. The angular, hard-edged Ferrarese style makes Christ's body almost carved, which heightens the painfulness of the subject."},"met:11711":{"d":"A woman in a necklace, a neck ribbon and lace is the subject of this tiny miniature, painted around 1770 in watercolor on ivory and measuring only about 4.6 by 3.1 centimeters, smaller than a matchbox. The record lists these details and names the sitter as Mrs. Michael Taney, born Monica Brooke, but the museum gives no description of her hair, expression or the background. It entered the collection through the Harris Brisbane Dick Fund in 1938.","i":"Monica Brooke Taney of Calvert County, Maryland, was the mother of Roger Brooke Taney, who became Chief Justice of the United States and wrote the Dred Scott decision of 1857. Charles Willson Peale, who studied in London with Benjamin West from 1767 to 1769, returned to Maryland as a fashionable portraitist, painting both oils and miniatures. This miniature belongs to the Maryland years before the Revolution.","c":"Peale's early miniatures have a bright, precise charm: clean drawing, fresh color and details such as lace carefully noted, though the faces can be a little hard. At this tiny size the ivory glows through the thin color, and the likeness has to compress character into a face scarcely larger than a thumbnail. A pleasing early example of American miniature painting, enhanced by the interest of its sitter's son."},"nga:62968":{"d":"A middle-aged man stands facing us in a landscape, leaning his left elbow on a tall, podium-shaped rock where an open book rests, and pointing with his other index finger. He wears a tawny-brown coat and vest, brown knee breeches and white stockings. Behind him stands a gray stone statue of a woman with scales and a tall staff, beside a tree with peach-colored fruit; a torn sheet lies at his feet, and in the distance a man in a red coat leads a mule. Oil on canvas, about 201 by 147 centimeters.","i":"The picture is a legal manifesto. The open book carries the Latin words Nolumus leges Angliae mutari, we do not wish the laws of England to be changed, a medieval baronial cry for the common law, and the torn sheet reads Imperial Civil Law, summary proceeding, a rejection of trial without a jury. Painted in 1770, amid colonial resistance to British measures, it presents Bordley, a Maryland lawyer and agricultural writer who had helped support Peale's training in London, as a defender of English liberties.","c":"An unusually ambitious early work, two meters tall, in which Peale, just back from study under Benjamin West in London, attempts a full-length portrait loaded with allegory. The painting is meticulous, clear in color and a little stiff in pose, and its most impressive feature is the confidence of the argument: the sitter is a thinking man engaged in the debates of his day. It asks to be read as much as looked at."},"met:11710":{"d":"A woman in lace, ruffles, ribbon, a pearl necklace, a headband and an elaborate hairstyle is shown in this oil on canvas of about 79 by 62 centimeters, painted around 1771. The record lists these costume details and names the sitter as Margaret Strachan, who became Mrs. Thomas Harwood. Her pose, expression and the background are not described, and the portrait came to the museum through the Morris K. Jesup Fund in 1933.","i":"Margaret Strachan was painted by Charles Willson Peale in Maryland in the years when he was the leading portraitist of the Chesapeake gentry and had just returned from his studies with Benjamin West in London. The fine attention to lace, ribbon and pearls, as in the record's list, is typical of the period's portraits, which celebrated the fashion and wealth of the sitter. The double title records both her maiden and married names.","c":"Peale's Maryland portraits of the early 1770s have a freshness and clarity that distinguish them from the stiffer provincial work of their day: the lace and pearls are painted with real pleasure, and the face is clear and attentive. They also show the limits of a painter newly back from London: the figures can be a little flat and the space shallow. An appealing early work by a major American painter."},"nga:1103":{"d":"A formal portrait of John Philip de Haas, painted in 1772 by Charles Willson Peale in oil on canvas, about 127 by 102 centimeters. That converts to 50 by 40 inches, a standard size in English portrait practice. The museum's keywords record only the sitter's name and that he is male, so the picture is best described by its type: a commissioned likeness meant to hang in a well-placed man's house.","i":"Peale had returned from study under Benjamin West in London in 1769 and was building a practice among colonial gentlemen, adapting the formats and poses of British portraiture for American patrons. De Haas (1735 to 1786) later served as an officer in the Continental Army, and the portrait belongs to the years before the Revolution, when such likenesses recorded the confidence of an emerging colonial elite.","c":"Peale at this date is a young painter eager to meet British standards, careful with faces and costume and sometimes stiff in anatomy, with a clear, firm light that makes his early portraits easy to read. The generous size gives the sitter presence, and the picture should be judged as a solid, representative work of his first years of practice rather than a surprising one."},"met:11709":{"d":"A tiny miniature of General Nathanael Greene, painted in 1778 in watercolor on ivory and measuring only about 4.5 by 3.5 centimeters, smaller than a matchbox. The record lists the sitter, a man and a portrait; the museum gives no description of his uniform, expression or the background. It came to the museum in 1924 with the bequest of Charles Allen Munn.","i":"Greene (1742–1786), a Rhode Island Quaker who became one of Washington's ablest generals, was in 1778 serving as the Continental Army's quartermaster general. Peale, who served in the Pennsylvania militia, painted many officers' miniatures during the war. The tiny scale made miniatures portable keepsakes for officers and their families in a time of war. Greene later commanded the Southern campaign.","c":"A miniature of a general is a small monument, and Peale, an ardent patriot who knew his subjects as comrades, gives the form a directness appropriate to a republican soldier. The scale demands clarity over drama, and the portrait offers a face firmly seen; it is not the grand image of a hero but the portable likeness of a man known to his friends. Historically eloquent, artistically modest."},"met:11713":{"d":"A small portrait of a man, listed in the record only as 'man' and 'portrait', painted around 1775 in oil on canvas and measuring about 25 by 20 centimeters. His clothing, pose and expression are not described, and the sitter is not named. The same size, date, medium and fund as Portrait of a Woman suggest that the two were pendants, a husband and wife painted as a pair.","i":"Charles Willson Peale painted oil portraits of this small size, scarcely larger than a book, for sitters who wanted a likeness at modest cost. In 1775 the American colonies were on the brink of war, and Peale, a patriot, would soon serve as a soldier as well as paint. The sitters are anonymous, and the pair's identification as man and wife is an inference from their matching size.","c":"Peale's small portraits often distill a person into a few clear marks: attentive eyes, a neat contour, a patch of white linen. If this pair follows that pattern, they have an economy that larger portraits sometimes lose. With the sitters unknown, their interest is mainly as a sample of Peale's modest, direct style on the eve of the Revolution."},"met:11716":{"d":"A small portrait of a woman wearing a cap, lace, a necklace and a ribbon, according to the record, painted around 1775 in oil on canvas and measuring about 25 by 20 centimeters. Her pose and expression are not described, and the sitter is not named. Its size, date, medium and fund match those of Portrait of a Gentleman, so it was probably painted as the companion to that picture.","i":"A cap with lace and a ribbon at the neck were the staple of respectable women's dress in colonial America, and a necklace marked some means. Peale, who began his working life as a saddler and also repaired watches before teaching himself to paint, made small oils like this one for the middling sort of colonial town, who could not afford his large, framed portraits.","c":"The attraction is the sitter's dress: cap, lace and ribbon are painted with careful pleasure, the clear economy of a painter who began as a craftsman and respects well-made things. Peale's women of this period can look slightly stiff, and the face in a small oil lacks the delicacy of his miniatures, but the clarity is engaging. A modest pendant, whose companion adds to its meaning."},"met:15127":{"d":"A miniature of a man named Ennion Williams, painted in 1776 in watercolor on ivory and measuring just 3.8 by 3 centimeters, hardly larger than a postage stamp. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait' and says no more of his face, clothing or the background. The year is that of the Declaration of Independence, and the miniature was acquired by the Metropolitan Museum through the Dale T. Johnson Fund in 2006.","i":"In 1776 Peale moved to Philadelphia and was soon in uniform with the militia while still painting. Miniatures this small were tucked in lockets and snuffboxes or set in bracelets, and the vogue for them peaked just when families were separated by war. Ennion Williams appears to be an otherwise little-recorded sitter.","c":"A smaller, tighter picture than most, and everything depends on the face: Peale's miniatures are valued for a plain, honest likeness, and the tiny scale concentrates it. The risk is a small-scale hardness; the glory is intimacy. Treated as a piece of jewelry and of history, it is moving in its modesty."},"met:15128":{"d":"A man in a tiny portrait miniature, painted in 1776 in watercolor on ivory and measuring only about 3.4 by 2.8 centimeters, roughly the size of a large postage stamp. The record lists a man and a portrait, so his face, dress and expression are not described. He is evidently the husband of the sitter of Mrs. Joseph Donaldson (Frances Johnston), also dated 1776 and given by the same donor, Gloria Manney, in 2006.","i":"Pairs of miniatures of husband and wife were a common way to mark a marriage or a separation, and in 1776, the year of the Declaration of Independence, many families faced the second. Peale was soon to put on a militia uniform himself. These ivories, small enough for a pocket or a locket, could travel where larger portraits could not, and carried a face through the uncertainties of war.","c":"Every miniature by Peale is an exercise in compression: the face must carry the likeness, and the ivory's glow does the rest. His miniatures of men tend to be plain and direct, without the lace and pearl decoration of the women's portraits, and this one is likely to follow suit. Its value is as half of a pair, whose companion gives it its meaning."},"met:15129":{"d":"A woman wearing a pearl necklace and lace is the subject of this miniature, painted in 1776 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 3.8 by 3.1 centimeters. The record names her as Mrs. Joseph Donaldson, born Frances Johnston, but does not describe her hair, expression or the background. Her husband's miniature, Joseph Donaldson, is dated the same year and was given by the same donor.","i":"She is identified by her married name and her maiden name, a double identification that museums use for sitters known from family tradition. Pearls and lace were the standard marks of a lady's dress in 1776. The date places the portrait in the first year of independence, when Charles Willson Peale was working in Philadelphia and about to join the militia.","c":"A tiny, tidy portrait in which the white of the ivory, the lace and the pearls do much of the work; Peale's precise touch and pale, clean coloring are well suited to a woman of fashion. The scale leaves no room for psychology, and the portrait remains an elegant record. Pleasant, slight and of historical interest."},"met:11706":{"d":"A miniature of George Washington, painted around 1777 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 3.8 by 3.5 centimeters, hardly larger than a postage stamp. The record lists the sitter, a man and a portrait, and says nothing of Washington's uniform, expression or the background. The miniature was given to the museum in 1883 by William H. Huntington. It is a private, hand-held image of the commander rather than a state portrait.","i":"Peale painted Washington from life seven times between 1772 and 1795, and he knew the general as a friend and a comrade in arms. In 1777 Washington was commanding an army that had just won at Trenton and Princeton, and his image was in demand. A miniature served as a gift to relatives and friends, and as an emblem of loyalty to the cause.","c":"A tiny Washington cannot offer the monumental presence of the full-length portraits, but it carries something different: an observant likeness by a painter who had sat with his subject, in a form meant to be held. Peale's directness, the clear contour and plain coloring suit the sitter's reserve, and the picture avoids the heroic gloss of later images. Modest, authentic and precious."},"met:11714":{"d":"A miniature of an unnamed man, painted around 1777 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 3.8 by 3.2 centimeters. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait' and says nothing more of his face, dress or expression. It was acquired in 1938 through the Fletcher Fund. As the title says, the sitter is a gentleman whose name has not come down with the picture.","i":"Watercolor on ivory was a delicate technique: the ivory was cut into thin slices, smoothed and degreased, and the painter built flesh tones in tiny dots and hatched strokes, letting the white of the ivory glow through. Peale learned miniature painting during his studies in London from 1767 to 1769, and in the 1770s and 1780s he painted many such portraits of soldiers and civilians.","c":"The test of a miniature is how much character survives at a few centimeters, and Peale's best examples manage a lively, unposed look with very little means. Without a name to attach to the face, the picture rests on that quality alone, and it succeeds as a fine, clear small likeness. Its anonymity is a loss to history but not to art."},"met:11705":{"d":"A miniature of General Henry Knox, painted in 1778 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 7 by 5 centimeters, noticeably larger than most of Peale's miniatures of these years. The record lists the sitter, a man, a portrait and 'general', but his uniform, pose and expression are not described. It was a gift of J. William Middendorf II in 1968.","i":"Knox (1750–1806) was a Boston bookseller who taught himself artillery from his books and became Washington's chief of artillery; in the winter of 1775–76 he brought the cannon captured at Fort Ticonderoga to Boston across the snow, forcing the British to evacuate the city. After the war he served as the first Secretary of War. In 1778 he was a brigadier general.","c":"The larger format gives the face room to breathe, and Knox, a big, genial man, would have filled it. The miniature's strengths are the direct gaze and clear modeling that Peale could achieve when he had a little more space; its limits are those of the form, the lack of drama and background. A sympathetic and straightforward portrait of a soldier-bookseller."},"met:11704":{"d":"A miniature of Arthur St. Clair, painted in 1780 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 4.4 by 3.5 centimeters, smaller than a matchbox. The record names the sitter, a major general of the Continental Army, but does not describe his uniform, expression or the background. The small likeness of a Revolutionary officer came to the museum in 1932 through the Morris K. Jesup Fund.","i":"St. Clair (1737–1818), a Scottish-born officer, was a major general in the Continental Army and was court-martialed and acquitted after abandoning Fort Ticonderoga in 1777. He later presided over the Congress of the Confederation and governed the Northwest Territory, and his name is attached to the disastrous defeat of 1791 by a confederacy of Native nations, in which much of his army was destroyed. Peale painted him before that defeat.","c":"A miniature of a man whose fame later turned to notoriety carries a poignancy the painter could not foresee. Peale gives him a clear, unflattering face, as he did most of his soldiers, and the format permits little else. It is an honest, sober small portrait, more interesting for its sitter's history than for any display of technique."},"met:11712":{"d":"An older woman and a young girl are shown together in a double portrait, in oil on canvas, about 127 by 102 centimeters, painted between 1777 and 1780. The record lists a girl, a woman and a portrait, and the title identifies them as Mrs. Samuel Mifflin and her granddaughter Rebecca Mifflin Francis. The museum gives no description of their dress, poses or setting, so only the sitters, the date and the size can be stated.","i":"A grandmother with a granddaughter is a less common subject than a mother with a child, and it speaks of a family's sense of continuity across generations. The Mifflins were a prominent Philadelphia family of Quaker background, and Peale painted the grandmother together with a companion portrait of her husband, Samuel Mifflin. The picture dates from the Revolution, when Philadelphia was occupied and then restored to American hands.","c":"Peale's double portrait pairs the aged and the young with some tenderness, and the larger format lets him describe costume and gesture with more freedom than in his miniatures. The result is more formal than affecting: the pose is conventional, and the old woman's face is probably the strongest passage. A solid piece of Philadelphia portraiture of the Revolutionary years."},"met:11718":{"d":"A man with a book, identified as Samuel Mifflin, sits for a portrait in oil on canvas, about 126 by 101 centimeters, painted between 1777 and 1780. The record lists a man and a book, with the keywords Books, Men and Portraits; his pose, dress and the setting are not described. The canvas matches the size of the portrait of his wife with their granddaughter, so the two were evidently painted as a pair for the same house.","i":"A book in a portrait signifies learning, piety or the habit of reading, and for a Philadelphian it suggests the sober, educated character that the city's Quaker elite prized. Peale, who had returned to Philadelphia in 1776, was meanwhile serving in the militia and painting officers and officials, but his civilian portraits of this time show a calmer side of Revolutionary Philadelphia. The Egleston Fund bought both pictures for the museum in 1922.","c":"Peale's best civilian portraits of these years have a plain candor: a firm head, a steady look, little ornament. The book is a useful prop, and the pendant format gives the picture a domestic dignity. It lacks the spirit of Peale's more personal likenesses, but its modest assurance suits an elderly gentleman of the Revolutionary era."},"met:11707":{"d":"A full-length portrait of George Washington in oil on canvas, about 241 by 157 centimeters, nearly two and a half meters tall, painted around 1779 to 1781. The record lists Washington, the Battle of Trenton, a man and a cannon, so the general is shown in a military setting with artillery and the battle of 1776 in the background. The museum gives no further description of his pose, uniform or the landscape.","i":"At Trenton, on the night of December 25 to 26, 1776, Washington led his army across the icy Delaware and surprised the Hessian garrison, giving the American cause a victory when it needed one most. Peale, who fought in the Pennsylvania militia in the Trenton and Princeton campaign, painted Washington in this heroic mode for patrons who wanted a statement of republican virtue. The railroad magnate Collis P. Huntington gave it to the museum in 1897.","c":"A full-length state portrait of the new republic's hero, and its ambition is evident: scale, pose and the battle behind. Peale knew Washington's face better than most painters, and the head has an unidealized firmness. The body is less certain, with the stiffness of a figure planted in the foreground and a landscape that serves as a backdrop. Impressive for its history, if not always for its painting."},"met:15130":{"d":"A miniature of an unnamed woman, painted around 1778 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 3.9 by 3 centimeters. The record lists only 'woman' and 'portrait', so her hair, dress and expression are not described, and the sitter's name is not given. It is one of several Peale miniatures that came to the museum in 2006 as a gift of Gloria Manney.","i":"Women's miniatures of the late 1770s typically show powdered or elaborately dressed hair, a ribbon and a lace-trimmed bodice, and they were often made as gifts between husband and wife or as keepsakes for children. In 1778, as the British left Philadelphia and normal life resumed, Peale's practice revived, and he painted many such small likenesses. The anonymity of this one makes it a type more than a person.","c":"An anonymous lady in a few centimeters of ivory: the sort of picture that tests a painter's economy. Peale's coloring, clean and slightly cool, and his firm outline are well suited to the scale, and the likeness reads clearly. The loss of the sitter's name does not harm the picture as an image, though it cuts it off from the life that gave it meaning."},"met:15131":{"d":"A miniature of Esther Bowes, wife of John Cox, painted around 1778 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 4.4 by 3.5 centimeters. The record lists a woman, a portrait and the sitter's name; the museum gives no description of her dress, hair or expression. Like the Portrait of a Lady of the same date, it was given to the museum by Gloria Manney in 2006.","i":"A sitter identified by both her married and her maiden names is one whose family history is recorded, and Esther Bowes belonged to a family in the Philadelphia region; her husband may be the Philadelphia merchant and Revolutionary quartermaster John Cox, though the museum does not say so. Peale painted such portraits for the circle of patriot families who were his friends and patrons.","c":"Peale's women are rarely glamorous but often vivid, and the miniature scale suits his habit of noting every detail of dress and jewelry with clarity. The inscription of a name gives this one an advantage over its anonymous neighbors, and the image itself is pleasing, spare and exact. A good example of Peale's wartime miniatures, whose quality lies in simplicity."},"met:11702":{"d":"A miniature of a man named Anthony Butler, painted in 1782 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 4 by 3.2 centimeters. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait'; his face, clothing and the background are left unrecorded. It was given to the museum in 1974 by James Parker in memory of General and Mrs. Cortlandt Parker, so it presumably passed down through a family.","i":"The date, 1782, places the miniature in the final phase of the War of Independence, when the surrender at Yorktown had effectively decided the conflict and officers and soldiers began to think of home. The sitter's name appears without a title, so whether he was a soldier or a civilian is not known. Miniatures like this were exchanged as keepsakes at a time of anxious waiting for peace.","c":"The small scale concentrates attention on the eyes and the contour, and Peale's economy of means gives even an unidentified sitter a clear presence. This one stays within the conventions of the type, with plain handling and no ornament, but the technical accuracy of the stippled flesh and the precise edge give it a quiet appeal. A modest object whose family history is better recorded than its subject's."},"met:11719":{"d":"A portrait of the Philadelphia merchant and banker Thomas Willing, painted in 1782 in oil on canvas and measuring about 126 by 101 centimeters. The record lists a man and a portrait with the sitter's name; his pose, dress and setting are not described. The painting entered the museum in 1966 as a gift of Dr. Ernest G. Stillman, by exchange.","i":"Thomas Willing (1731–1821) was one of the richest men in Philadelphia, a partner of Robert Morris, mayor of the city in 1763, and first president of the Bank of North America, founded in 1781. He was a member of the Continental Congress who voted against independence in 1776, yet he remained in the city and supported the financing of the war. Peale painted him a year after the bank's founding.","c":"A banker's portrait demands authority without display, and Peale's plain, firm manner suits it: the head is solid and the likeness evidently direct. The picture is a document of a man at the pivot of American finance, and its modest pose and sober dress match the sitter's reputation for caution. It is not Peale's liveliest work, but it is one of his most historically weighty."},"cma:98268":{"d":"Washington stands in the quiet aftermath of the Battle of Princeton, accompanied by his horse and a groomsman. In the background at the left, a line of captured British soldiers in red coats winds across the field. The canvas, about 131 by 122 centimeters, was painted around 1779 by Charles Willson Peale, who had served under Washington. Some scholars identify the groomsman as William Lee, a mixed-race enslaved man whom Washington forced to work as his personal assistant.","i":"Peale served under Washington and befriended him in the Revolutionary War, which made him an ideal painter to commemorate Washington's early victory at Princeton in January 1777. He produced several versions of the composition amid a surge of enthusiasm for the American cause; some were commissioned by American colonists, as in this case, others by Washington's admirers in France, Spain, Holland, Cuba and even England. Peale was also a naturalist who directed the first mastodon excavation in the United States.","c":"A well-judged commemoration: Washington in repose, the horse beside him, the defeated enemy as a pageant in the distance. The composition is stately and clear, though the figure's stiffness and the neat staging make the battle feel remote. The inclusion of the groomsman, who in most celebrations of the general would be left out, now gives it an added historical interest."},"met:15132":{"d":"A miniature of General Richard Montgomery, painted around 1784 to 1786 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 4.4 by 3.4 centimeters. The record lists a man, a portrait and a profile, so he is shown from the side rather than full face. Montgomery had been dead since 1775, so the miniature must be posthumous, presumably based on an earlier portrait or on memory. It was acquired in 2006 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"Richard Montgomery (1738–1775), an Irish-born officer who had served in the British army, joined the American cause and died leading the failed assault on Quebec on December 31, 1775, becoming one of the first martyrs of the Revolution. Peale, who in the 1780s was assembling a gallery of portraits of Revolutionary heroes for his museum in Philadelphia, made images of such figures for a public that revered them.","c":"A profile is a stern form, flattering to the nose and chin and hostile to expression, which suits a hero commemorated after his death. Peale gives Montgomery a crisp outline, in the manner of a coin or medal, and the effect is more emblem than likeness. A well-made memorial miniature of a martyr of the cause, with the cool distance of a medal."},"met:11708":{"d":"A miniature of James Peale, the painter's younger brother, painted around 1788 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 4 by 3 centimeters. The record lists a man and the sitter's name; his dress, pose and expression are not described. It was a gift of J. William Middendorf II in 1968. The picture shows a painter portraying another painter, a fellow miniaturist, in the medium in which both were skilled.","i":"James Peale (1749–1831) learned painting from his older brother and became the family's leading miniaturist; he served as an officer in the Revolution and later painted still lifes. Brothers sat for one another readily, and a miniature among family members was a private object, the kind exchanged to mark a marriage, a birth or a separation. Charles painted him in the late 1780s, when both were established in Philadelphia.","c":"A brother is the most forgiving sitter and the most demanding critic, and the miniature has the relaxed, affectionate directness one expects. At this scale Charles's technique is at its most confident, and the portrait gains from the shared craft of the two men. Intimate and unpretentious; like many family likenesses, its charm is private."},"nga:195347":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated, her body turned slightly to our left while her head faces us. She has delicate features, a fair complexion with soft pink cheeks and light blue eyes, and long brown hair in voluminous waves, pulled halfway back and adorned with pearls. Her pale yellow gown has a low, lace-edged neckline and a fabric ornament at the bust, with blue ribbons and red jewels at the sleeves. The ground is a muted brown. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 48 centimeters, 1788.","i":"The sitter is Rebecca Bryan White, titled by her husband's name, Mrs. John White, in the period's fashion. Peale painted many such small half-lengths in Philadelphia, where a prosperous post-Revolutionary society wanted portraits that combined contemporary fashion, signs of refinement such as pearls and lace, and a plain background that kept attention on the face. The pearls and lace suggest the taste of the city's elite.","c":"Peale takes pains with the face and costume: a luminous complexion and fine detail on the lace and ribbons, set against a muted brown ground that keeps attention on the sitter. The delicate, rosy features lean toward the idealization fashionable in the 1780s, and the pose is quiet. It is a refined, gently flattering example of his late portraits, charming rather than searching."},"nga:50684":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a man and a woman sit side by side on a grassy rise. He leans toward her, propped on one elbow, a long wooden telescope in one hand and a green parrot beside the other; she sits upright, a hand on his forearm, pearls woven through her hair, in a white dress with an ocean-blue sash, holding peaches and a spray of purple clover. A tree rises behind, and a meadow leads to a distant bay with tiny boats and buildings. Oil on canvas, about 106 by 153 centimeters, dated 1788.","i":"Peale's double portrait takes its relaxed, outdoor format from British conversation pieces such as Gainsborough's, but replaces their formal reserve with unusual intimacy: the husband leans toward his wife, and her hand rests on his arm. The telescope, parrot, peaches and clover may allude to the couple's interests, hopes or abundance, though any exact meaning is conjectural. The picture's chief subject is the affection of the pair.","c":"A warm, appealing double portrait in which Peale loosens his usual precision: gentle smiles, a tender touch and delicately painted details, pearls, peaches, parrot, give the picture an affectionate air. The drawing is firmer in faces and accessories than in the lounging figure, whose pose is awkward but memorable. It stands among the most informal American portraits of its decade, and its charm outweighs its stiffness."},"met:11703":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Elie Williams, painted in 1789 in oil on canvas and measuring about 92 by 69 centimeters, a half-length format. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait' and does not describe his pose, dress or setting. The painting was given to the museum in 1967. The date places it in the year of Washington's inauguration as president.","i":"In 1789, when this portrait was painted, Peale had been established for several years in Philadelphia, running his museum of natural history and portraits in addition to painting, and the new Constitution was taking effect. Elie Williams is known here only by name, and the museum does not record his occupation. A portrait of this size was a significant commission, within reach of a prosperous man.","c":"By 1789 Peale's oil technique had matured: the modeling is firmer and the light softer than in his Maryland portraits of the early 1770s, and his sitters tend to look out with a steady, intelligent gaze. If this portrait follows the pattern, it is a competent, unpretentious likeness whose merits are plainness and honesty, which were also the republican virtues of the day."},"met:15126":{"d":"A mother with an infant, shown in a miniature painted in 1790 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 6.5 by 5.2 centimeters. The record lists a mother, a woman, an infant and the sitter, Rachel Brewer Peale, and the title names the baby as Eleanor. The museum gives no description of their poses, dress or setting. Charles Willson Peale painted this portrait of his own wife and child, and it was given by Gloria Manney in 2006.","i":"Rachel Brewer married Peale in 1762 and bore him many children, several of whom he named for great painters, among them Raphaelle, Rembrandt and Rubens. She died in 1790, the year of this miniature. The portrait of a wife and baby, painted by the husband and father, is among the most personal works in Peale's output, which was dominated by public figures.","c":"Intimate and unforced: the mother and child group is among the tenderest of Peale's miniatures, and the larger format of the ivory lets him describe both faces with care. The scale is still tiny and the paint thin, but the emotional charge of a husband painting his wife in the year of her death is unmistakable. It gains from what is known of its circumstances and loses nothing by it."},"met:11717":{"d":"A miniature of Rachel Brewer Peale, the painter's first wife, painted around 1790 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 5.7 by 4.6 centimeters. The record lists a woman, a fichu, a pearl, a miniature and a portrait, so she wears the light scarf called a fichu and a pearl, and the mention of a miniature may mean that she wears or holds one. The museum gives no further description of her face or the background.","i":"A fichu, the light triangular scarf crossed over the neck and chest, and a pearl were signs of modest elegance, appropriate to the wife of a successful artist and museum keeper. Peale painted his family often and treated them as seriously as his official sitters. The miniature was given to the museum in 1968 by J. William Middendorf II, and it shares its subject with the portrait of Rachel and the baby Eleanor.","c":"A quietly affectionate portrait: a pearl, a scarf and a steady look are all the picture needs. Peale, who tends to idealize little, shows his wife with a plain candor that does honor to both. The miniature is minute but fully realized, and with the portrait of Rachel and Eleanor it forms a small record of a family."},"met:16894":{"d":"A miniature of Nathaniel Ramsay, painted around 1793 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 4.4 by 3.5 centimeters, and still preserved in its gold case and red leather case. The record gives the sitter's name and the cases but no description of his face, dress or background. The gift of the estate of Elizabeth C. W. O'Connor reached the museum only in 2025.","i":"Nathaniel Ramsay (1741–1817) was a Maryland lawyer who commanded a regiment in the Continental Army, was wounded and captured at the Battle of Monmouth in 1778, and was appointed United States marshal for Maryland in 1790 by Washington. The survival of the original gold and leather cases is unusual: it shows how miniatures were worn, carried and kept, protecting the fragile ivory.","c":"The miniature is of a man who combined a soldier's and a lawyer's careers, and Peale's clear, uncomplicated style suits a sitter of that practical stamp. The pleasure of the object is as much in its preserved cases as in the likeness; together they show how a small portrait was valued as a precious thing. A well-kept relic of the early republic."},"met:15017":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man named Joseph Stallings, painted about 1829 by George Catlin in watercolor on ivory. It measures only about 5.6 by 4.5 centimeters, smaller than a playing card. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait; the tiny picture was meant to be held close, worn or kept in a case, and every detail of the face had to be conveyed in a few delicate touches.","i":"Before George Catlin became known for his paintings of Native Americans, he made a living as a portraitist, including miniatures on ivory, which families prized for their intimacy and durability and which cost far less than a full-size oil. Photography did not yet exist, so a miniature was the most personal way to keep the likeness of someone absent or lost.","c":"A miniature is judged by economy: a few delicate washes and fine touches to model a face on a surface smaller than a playing card. Catlin's miniatures are competent, polite and modest, and not what he is remembered for. Their interest lies in seeing a famous artist at an early stage of his career, and in the intimacy of the object itself."},"met:10427":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed man, painted in 1827 by George Catlin in watercolor on ivory, about 6 by 4.8 centimeters. The title preserves the sitter's anonymity, as often happens when a miniature is separated from its case or family. It was made to be held in the hand or kept in a locket or case, and its small size concentrates attention on the face.","i":"Ivory was the favorite support for miniatures because its pale, slightly translucent surface lets light pass through thin watercolor, giving flesh a glow that opaque paint cannot match. Catlin practiced law before turning to painting around 1820, and miniatures were a dependable source of income for a young artist working in several eastern cities before he took up the work for which he is remembered.","c":"The success of a miniature lies in the likeness and the finish, and a plain, unnamed gentleman gives the painter nothing else to work with. Catlin's miniatures are tidy and unpretentious; this is a well-judged small portrait whose luminous ivory ground carries the head forward. Without a sitter's name its interest is chiefly as an example of the craft."},"met:10426":{"d":"A miniature portrait, only about 5 by 4.4 centimeters, in watercolor on ivory, painted about 1830 by George Catlin of Clara Bartlett Gregory, the artist's wife. The title gives her married name, so sitter and painter were bound by marriage, and the picture belongs to the most personal kind of miniature: a portrait of a spouse, small enough to be carried or worn.","i":"In the late 1820s and early 1830s Catlin worked as a portraitist, including miniatures, before devoting himself to the journeys west that produced his paintings of Native peoples. A likeness of his wife is a reminder of the household behind the traveling artist. Miniatures like this were exchanged as tokens of affection and kept in cases or lockets, which suits a portrait of a spouse.","c":"Because the sitter is the artist's wife, a viewer is invited to read affection into the miniature's careful, gentle finish, but the picture is better judged as a skilled small likeness, modest in ambition. It lacks the force of the Native American portraits that made Catlin's name, and its appeal is intimacy rather than power, which suits an object made to be held close."},"nga:50656":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a Shawano woman with medium-brown skin sits angled to the right and looks off in that direction, in a vertical portrait about 72 by 59 centimeters. She has broad, flushed cheeks, light brown eyes, a cleft chin and closed lips. Her black hair is parted down the middle, with the part painted red, and a ponytail falls over one shoulder. She wears an earring of long silver tubes, at least nine strands of blue-toned beads, a lace-edged chemise of pink striped fabric and a thick white fur wrap. The background is mottled mauve and turquoise.","i":"Shawano is Catlin's spelling of Shawnee, a people who had been driven from the Ohio Valley and by 1830 were settled west of the Mississippi. Catlin painted the portrait in 1830, on his first journey to the frontier, and recorded the sitter's name in translation, as he did for many. Her dress combines trade goods, silver ornaments, glass beads and printed cloth with local fur, showing how deeply Native communities were involved in exchange with Europeans.","c":"Catlin's women are rarer than his warriors, and this portrait gives its sitter calm dignity: the sidelong glance, the careful depiction of beads and silver, and the soft mottled ground that keeps attention on face and ornament. His drawing is sometimes stiff and his flesh tones generalized, but here the plain directness works. As a document it is invaluable; as a picture it is quietly effective, if less polished than the portraits of professional European painters."},"met:15018":{"d":"A miniature portrait of an unnamed man, painted about 1835 by George Catlin in watercolor on ivory, about 6.5 by 5.4 centimeters, and set in a gilded copper locket. The back of the locket holds a hair reserve, a compartment for a lock of hair. Such lockets were worn or carried as keepsakes, joining a painted likeness with a physical relic of the person it recorded.","i":"A lock of hair behind a portrait made the locket both image and relic, a convention of nineteenth-century sentiment that linked absent or deceased loved ones with a trace of their bodies. By the mid-1830s Catlin's career had shifted toward the western journeys that made his reputation, so the approximate date places this miniature at the edge of his portrait-painting years.","c":"In a locket miniature the case matters as much as the picture: the gilded copper setting and the hair compartment make it an object of sentiment first and a work of art second. The portrait itself is a neat, undemonstrative likeness, agreeable as an example of craft, but its significance lies in the way it embodies a vanished custom of memory."},"nga:50657":{"d":"The head, shoulders and chest of a young man in Ojibbeway attire face the viewer in this vertical portrait. The right side of his face, on the viewer's left, is painted crimson, and horizontal stripes of red and white line the other cheek. A silvery ring hangs from his nose; a brown fur headdress has a cardinal-red patch, and pearl-white feathers with red tips hang beside his face. His tan coat has sleeves streaked with sky blue, red and lime green; at the neck are fur, a string of white claws and silvery beads, and a high beaded collar. The background is mottled sage, tan and cream.","i":"Ojibbeway is Catlin's spelling of Ojibwe, the people of the upper Great Lakes also called Chippewa. The date, 1843, falls in the years when Catlin was exhibiting in England, and groups of Ojibwe performers toured Britain and France at the time, so the portrait may be of a visitor, though the record does not say. The title gives the sitter's rank in translation, and dress and face paint are carefully recorded as signs of status.","c":"Catlin puts great stress on face and costume, and the frontal pose and unblinking gaze give the boy chief a stern, formal presence. The red and white paint, the white feathers and the cardinal patch are bold, almost decorative, and the plain mottled ground makes them pop. The modeling is simple, the anatomy slightly flat, and the picture is more an emblem than a character study. It succeeds as a record of a striking appearance."},"nga:50654":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a man in Iowa attire looks just off to the left with heavy-lidded eyes. The sides of his square face, his bald head and his neck are painted bright orange-red, and his ears, lips and eyelids are red; deep lines run across his forehead and beside his nose. A spiky headdress fans like a fountain from a small cap, and beaded earrings hang from his ears. More than a dozen necklaces hang beneath two shorter chokers, one with a wide oval shell. Green stripes make a shallow V on his chest, a red handprint marks his arm, and red cloth wraps one shoulder.","i":"Medicine, in Catlin's usage, means spiritual power or mystery, and a medicine man was a healer and ritual leader. The Iowa, who gave their name to the state, then lived in the Missouri valley. The red paint that covers his head, the handprint and the necklaces are marks of his role and standing. The date, 1844 to 1845, coincides with the visit of Iowa performers to England and France, which may be the occasion of the portrait, though the record is silent.","c":"A striking, almost abstract portrait: the head painted brilliant orange-red against a pale turquoise ground is a bold stroke of color, and the lined face under the spiky headdress has real presence. Catlin's drawing of features is competent, and the quiet, heavy-lidded gaze, as if looking inward, gives dignity. It is among the more memorable portraits in his later series, because the sitter appears neither exotic nor sentimentalized but simply there."},"nga:50655":{"d":"The head, shoulders and chest of an Iowa man, his face mostly painted red and green, fill this vertical portrait. He is angled to the right and looks into the distance with dark eyes. Crimson covers his forehead, cheeks and neck; four green lines angle up one cheek like fingers, a green line on the other cheek could be the thumb, and a green mark on his chin may be the palm. His spiky headdress has two feathers and a fur band; he wears earrings, a necklace of bear claws, beads and shells, and a garment of white fur and tawny hide, against tan clouds and ice-blue sky.","i":"White Cloud was a name borne by Iowa chiefs, and Catlin painted a leader of that name in the early 1830s, when he visited the Iowa on the Missouri frontier; this later version repeats the subject. The painted hand on the face is a striking sign, whose meaning for the sitter is not recorded here. The bear-claw necklace, a mark of prowess, and the fur band show high status. The Iowa were pushed from their lands in the 1830s, and Catlin hoped to record what he feared would be lost.","c":"The painted hand is the most arresting element: the green fingers and palm print across the cheek and chin turn the face into a kind of emblem, and the red forehead lights it. Catlin's craftsmanship is plain, the modeling smooth and slightly flat, and the sky almost incidental. As a portrait it is more a record of paint and ornament than of mind, but the sitter's distant gaze lends a moving gravity."},"nga:50624":{"d":"Seen from above, a shoreline with people in colorful clothing on a sandy beach faces small and larger vessels on the water, the larger with dark sails and people on board. The horizon blends into a sky that shades from pink to blue, with clouds, and behind the shore are structures and greenery. The palette of green, ochre and blue-green is applied in smooth, detailed strokes on a canvas about 38 by 51 centimeters, dated 1847 or 1848. It is the first in a long series of scenes from the voyages of the French explorer La Salle.","i":"Fort Frontenac, on Lake Ontario at what is now Kingston, Ontario, was the base of René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle, who left it on 18 November 1678 with the friar Louis Hennepin, the officer Henri de Tonti and a small party to build a ship above Niagara Falls and to push westward for trade and empire. Catlin, who painted the series in the late 1840s, never saw these events and worked from the narratives.","c":"The series opens like a pageant: bright costumes, a gentle pink sky and a high, clear view give the departure the air of a festival rather than a risky venture. Catlin's strengths are the calm sweep of shore and water and the lively little figures; his weakness here is a lack of specific detail, since the scene is imagined. It is an attractive, orderly beginning to an ambitious history cycle, though less compelling than his portraits."},"nga:50625":{"d":"A winter scene of an icy river with activity along its shore. At right a rocky cliff, partly snow-covered, carries evergreen trees; at left the river runs toward a distant waterfall, and a low horizon stresses the cliffs and the falls. People on the shore are busy among boats and tents. The palette is cool blues and whites for the ice and earth tones for the cliffs and figures; water and sky are smooth, rocks and trees more textured. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"In the winter of 1678 and 1679 La Salle's party camped on the Niagara River, building a ship above the falls. Hennepin, the friar in the party, gave the first European description of the great cataract, and an engraving made for his book was the first published picture of it. Catlin shows the camp beneath the falls, with the icy river and tents recalling the hardship of a Canadian winter, and combines natural wonder and human enterprise in a single view.","c":"The cool blues and whites make this one of the most atmospheric of the series: the glassy ice, the textured snowy cliff and the distant ghost of the falls create a convincing winter mood. The tiny figures, tents and boats give scale, and the low horizon makes the cliffs loom. The painter could never have seen the 1679 river and relies on the Niagara he knew from the 1840s, but the effect is persuasive."},"nga:50626":{"d":"A grand waterfall seen from an elevated viewpoint, surrounded by landscape filled with people and canoes. The horizon is high, marked by a line of trees and the pouring water; mist rises from the base and blends with soft clouds. Delicate, expressive strokes capture the motion of water, trees and figures, in vivid, cool blues for sky and water and earth tones for people and canoes, with touches of white suggesting snow on the ground and plants. The canvas measures about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"A portage is the carrying of boats and goods overland around an obstacle, and Niagara Falls was the great barrier on the water route between Lake Ontario and Lake Erie. Catlin's title places the scene at Table Rock, the ledge beside the Horseshoe Falls that became the best-known viewpoint of the nineteenth century. He shows the 1679 expedition at work in a place his own audience knew as a tourist sight, linking history and spectacle.","c":"The falls are the star: the mist at the base, the glittering blues and whites and the crowd of tiny canoes and people give a sense of scale and grandeur. Catlin's handling of water is fluent, and the high horizon makes the cataract fill the picture. The painting is more pageant than record, and the Niagara of 1847, a tourist spectacle, shapes the vision of a wilderness of 1679. Still, it is one of the series' most dramatic views."},"nga:50627":{"d":"A shoreline with people engaged in various activities, the horizon in the upper third where water meets sky. Groups of people, some standing and others at work, line the shore, and trees carry a hint of snow. The palette is earth tones, pale blues and splashes of red, and the brushwork is detailed yet loose, giving the scene a lively atmosphere. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title names the moment as the driving of the first bolt in the keel of La Salle's ship, the Griffon.","i":"The Griffon, named for the griffins on the coat of arms of Count Frontenac, La Salle's patron, was to be the first sailing ship on the upper Great Lakes. She was built in the winter of 1678 and 1679 on the Niagara River above the falls, and on 26 January 1679, by Hennepin's account, La Salle drove the first bolt into her keel. The ceremony marks the start of the enterprise that was to carry the fur trade and French claims into the interior.","c":"Driving a bolt is a hard moment to make dramatic, and Catlin wisely makes the whole shore the subject: workers, bystanders and splashes of red coats give a lively rhythm against the snow. The painting is a record of beginnings, more modest than the spectacle of the falls, and its small figures lack individuality. It is a pleasant, orderly scene of work in the open air, and good at conveying the cold."},"nga:50628":{"d":"A snow-covered landscape seen from an elevated viewpoint, with a high horizon where snow-laden trees blend into a pastel sky. The ground is covered in snow with streaks of green and brown, and snowy trees run from the foreground to the horizon. Two figures with dogs on a sled can be seen in the distance. The brushstrokes are loose, capturing the texture of snow, the movement of the figures and the foliage, in a cool, earthy palette of soft greens, whites and browns with muted sky tones. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"In the winter of 1679 La Salle's enterprise depended on long journeys across frozen country for supplies, and dog sleds, a method of travel borrowed from Native peoples, were the standard way to move over snow. The title places the journey back to Fort Frontenac, probably to bring supplies and settle business matters. Hardship, distance and debt defined the venture, and the picture makes the cold and the emptiness part of the story.","c":"An unusually spare picture: snow, bare trees and a tiny sled in the distance carry the whole subject. The loose brushwork captures the softness of snow and the effect of cold pastel light, and the emptiness conveys distance and effort better than a crowded scene would. There is no drama here, but it is one of the series' most poetic moments."},"nga:50629":{"d":"A gathering of Native people along a waterway as a large boat is maneuvered ashore, or into the water. People on land and in canoes raise their arms toward the boat. In the background a crowd lines the shore, with a forest and a soft pink and gray sky. The painting is detailed in its brushstrokes, capturing textures and motion in a vibrant natural palette, on a canvas about 38 by 56 centimeters. The title names the boat as the Griffon, La Salle's ship, and the date as July 1679.","i":"To the Native people who watched, a ship with masts and sails, built by strangers on their river, must have seemed a wonder or a threat. The Seneca, who controlled the Niagara region, are reported to have distrusted La Salle's building projects, and the ship was a symbol of French ambition to control the fur trade. Catlin, who painted Native crowds with sympathy throughout his career, makes the onlookers the real subject.","c":"The raised arms and the gathered crowd turn a technical event into a human one, and the pink and gray sky gives a soft light to the shore. Catlin's flair for the reactions of onlookers is evident, though the figures are generalized and the ship itself is not much more than an object of attention. It is a vivid image of cultural encounter, if more imagined than documented."},"nga:50630":{"d":"A coastal scene of maritime and shore activity under a high horizon and a sky of golden and orange hues. In the foreground are small boats with figures and, further out, a larger ship. In the distance figures gather along the shore, with teepees visible further back. The brushwork is detailed and conveys movement; turquoise waters, warm sky tones, green foliage and earthy land make up the palette. The canvas is about 38 by 57 centimeters, and the title dates the scene to 7 August 1679, the Griffon's first sailing.","i":"The Griffon sailed on 7 August 1679 to explore the lakes, passing through Lake Erie and Lake Huron to Green Bay, where La Salle loaded a cargo of furs and sent her back. On the return voyage she disappeared, with her crew and cargo, and her fate remains a mystery; the wreck has never been conclusively identified. Catlin shows the hopeful beginning, in a golden light that gives no hint of disaster.","c":"The golden sky and the turquoise water are the picture's pleasures, and the contrast of tiny canoes with the larger ship gives scale. If the conical shelters in the distance are teepees, they come from Catlin's experience of the Plains rather than from the Great Lakes, where bark and mat lodges were the rule: a reminder that the series is reconstruction. As a sunlit view it is cheerful, if not accurate."},"nga:50631":{"d":"A broad vista of water meets a sky with scattered clouds, with numerous figures and activities in careful brushwork. People gather on a sandy shoreline in the foreground, with more people and canoes in the distance, and elevated, wooded land beyond carries tents or teepees against a pastel sky. The palette combines blues, beiges and greens with vibrant reds for the figures. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title names the Griffon's arrival at Mackinac on 27 August 1679.","i":"Mackinac, the strait between Lake Huron and Lake Michigan, was the crossroads of the Great Lakes fur trade, where Huron, Odawa and French traders met. The Griffon, the first sailing ship to reach it, arrived in late August 1679, an astonishing sight for the people on the shore. From here La Salle's ship went on to Green Bay, the farthest point she was ever to reach.","c":"A bright, busy harbor view: the pastel sky and blue water set off the vivid red figures on the beach, and the groups of people at several distances give a sense of a lively gathering place. Catlin's handling of space, with elevated land behind the shore, is clear and easy. The picture seems to care more for the people on the shore than for the ship that brings them together."},"nga:50632":{"d":"A riverside village scene with villagers and conical lodges on both banks, canoes full of people on the water and some drawn up on a sandy shore. Green hills with trees line the horizon, and the sky shades from peach to light blue; the brushstrokes suggest movement and bustle. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title names the arrival of La Salle's party at an Illinois village on 1 January 1680.","i":"The Illinois, a confederacy of related peoples who lived along the Illinois River, were important allies and trading partners of the French, and their great village had hundreds of mat-covered lodges. La Salle's party reached it in the middle of winter, short of food, so the welcome was a matter of survival as well as diplomacy. From this region La Salle built Fort Crèvecœur and tried to launch the next stage of his enterprise.","c":"If the conical dwellings are teepees, as the museum's description has it, they come from Catlin's Plains experience; the Illinois lived in long, mat-covered lodges. Within that limit the picture is lively: canoes and figures crowd the river, the green hills and peach sky are agreeable, and the brushwork conveys movement. Painted on card, not canvas, it has a sketch-like freshness that the formal pictures of the series lack."},"nga:50633":{"d":"A gathering of people in a circle, surrounded by trees with green foliage. The figures wear earthy-toned garments, and shelters stand in the background. The palette is browns, beiges, earthy reds, blues and greens. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 42 by 60 centimeters, and the title identifies the circle as a feast given by the Illinois for La Salle's party on 2 January 1680.","i":"Among the Illinois, as among many Native peoples of the Great Lakes and the Mississippi valley, hospitality to visitors was formal: guests were seated in a circle, offered food, and welcomed with the calumet, the ceremonial pipe whose smoking sealed friendship. French accounts record being fed corn, meat and other dishes. For La Salle's cold and hungry party the feast was a welcome relief, and for the Illinois a way of binding powerful visitors into an alliance.","c":"The circular arrangement, a natural device for a feast, gives order and focus to the picture, and the earthy colors against the green of the trees are well judged. The drawing is generalized, so the gathering reads more as a social pattern than as a portrait of individuals. It is a quiet, civil image that treats the Illinois as hosts, not as exotic spectacle, which is to Catlin's credit."},"nga:50634":{"d":"A scene in an Iroquois village, in which Henri de Tonti, La Salle's lieutenant, pleads for peace, as the title says. The museum's record gives only a tree among the things shown, and no description, so the figures, the dwellings, the council and the colors are left unstated. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 38 by 56 centimeters, painted in 1847 or 1848 as part of Catlin's series on La Salle.","i":"Tonti, an Italian-born officer who had lost a hand in war and wore an iron replacement, was La Salle's most loyal lieutenant and often his envoy to Native communities. Peace was essential to the French, because the Iroquois confederacy, armed by Dutch and English traders, threatened their Illinois allies and the whole western fur trade. Most accounts place Tonti's best-known attempt to mediate in the autumn of 1680, when the Iroquois invaded the Illinois country.","c":"Without a description of the scene the picture can only be judged by its place in the series, as one of the few to center on diplomacy rather than travel. A council scene asks the painter for gestures and faces rather than landscape, and Catlin, a practiced observer of Native councils, should be at home with the subject. The theme of a European pleading for peace in a Native village is a thoughtful reversal of the usual story of conquest."},"nga:50635":{"d":"A river curves around lush green hills in a view from slightly above. The brushstrokes are smooth and controlled, giving the water a reflective quality, and the hillsides show many varieties of green. Greens and blues dominate, with touches of brown and red in the canoes, and the hills stretch back into the distance in a serene scene. The canvas, about 42 by 60 centimeters, is a little larger than most of the series, and the title names the capture of Father Hennepin and two companions by the Sioux in April 1680.","i":"In April 1680 Hennepin and his two companions, paddling up the Mississippi on La Salle's orders, were seized by a Dakota war party and taken north to their villages, where they lived for some months before being found by the French trader Daniel Greysolon, Sieur du Lhut. The captivity gave Hennepin the material for the books that made him famous, though his later claims were exaggerated and are doubted. Catlin, who knew Sioux country, shows the capture as a silent moment in a vast landscape.","c":"The title promises a drama, but the picture offers calm: a river bending among green hills and a few small canoes, with the captive party all but lost in the scene. This restraint is both a strength and a weakness: the landscape is persuasive and the color harmonious, but the tension of capture is absent. Catlin is more at home with the open country than with the narrative, and the picture works best as scenery."},"nga:50636":{"d":"A river flows past high cliffs in a wide view from slightly above, with canoes carrying people lined up in a horizontal band across the water. The cliffs and river dominate the composition, under a sky full of light clouds. The brushstrokes are smooth and precise, in rich greens for the cliffs, pale blues and grays for river and sky, and warm earthy tones for the canoes. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"Lover's Leap is the name given to a high rock on Lake Pepin, on the upper Mississippi, from which, in a legend popular in the nineteenth century, a Dakota woman named Winona leapt rather than marry a man she did not love. Hennepin and his two companions, among the first Europeans on this stretch of the river, passed it in April 1680, shortly before they were captured by the Dakota. The legend was added to the story by Catlin's day, in the age of Romantic travel writing.","c":"A broad, calm river view where the cliffs, painted in rich greens, and the row of canoes make a strong horizontal rhythm; the precise brushwork gives a convincing sense of geology. The legend adds a Romantic touch but not drama in the picture itself. Catlin's feeling for the vast scale of the Mississippi valley, which he knew well from the 1830s, is the real strength here, and the historical figures are lost in the landscape."},"nga:50637":{"d":"A landscape with waterfalls, a river, trees and hills in the distance. In the foreground, a group of people stand near a canoe on a grassy patch. Distinct brushstrokes show texture and movement, in warm tones with soft greens and earth colors under a muted peach sky. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title names the Falls of St. Anthony on the upper Mississippi and dates the scene to 1 May 1680.","i":"Hennepin named the falls for his patron saint, Anthony of Padua, in 1680, during his captivity among the Dakota, and his description made them famous in Europe. The falls, on the Mississippi at what is now Minneapolis, were sacred to the Dakota, who knew them as Owamniyomni, and later powered the mills that built the city. The only major waterfall on the river, they were largely destroyed as a natural feature by dams and development in the nineteenth century.","c":"A pleasing, gentle picture in which the falls are modest in scale, a ribbon of water between trees rather than a thundering cataract, and the figures near their canoe are small and calm. The warm greens and peach sky are agreeable. The real falls, now a concrete apron beside a modern city, make the painting a rare vision of a vanished place, though Catlin's version is idealized."},"nga:50638":{"d":"A river flows from left to right under a blue sky streaked with pink and gray clouds. On the right, green hills with lush vegetation rise, and small canoes with figures paddle along the water. The brushwork is distinct, capturing the landscape in various shades of green and earth tones, and the palette is serene, with greens and blues dominating and the hills' vibrant greens contrasting with the sky's soft hues. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"Father Louis Hennepin was a Recollect friar from the Spanish Netherlands who accompanied La Salle and later wrote vivid accounts of the expedition. His 1683 book was a success, and a later edition, published in 1697, claimed that he had descended the Mississippi to the sea before La Salle, a claim that most historians reject. Catlin's title shows the friar in motion, and the picture illustrates the kind of travel that made his tales so popular.","c":"The picture is mostly landscape: green hills, a moving river and a sky of pink and gray clouds, with tiny canoes giving the sense of travel. Catlin paints it with fluent, distinct touches and a pleasing harmony of green and blue. As history it is vague, but as a picture of a river valley in spring it is attractive and calm."},"nga:50639":{"d":"A snow-covered landscape with a high horizon where a line of evergreen trees meets a sky of blue and grayish clouds. The sky and the treetops are textured with lively brushstrokes. Figures in winter clothing pull wooden canoes across the snow, with more people engaged in the same task further back, giving the scene depth and movement. The palette is whites, blues and earth tones, and the scene conveys a chilly, determined atmosphere. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"In December 1681 La Salle and his men set out for the Illinois country at the start of the second expedition, hauling their canoes and baggage over the frozen shore of Lake Michigan on sledges, a method of winter travel borrowed from Native peoples. It was an exercise in endurance: the party pressed on in bitter cold to reach the Illinois River and then the Mississippi. The picture records the grit that his enterprise demanded.","c":"A good winter picture: the pale blues and whites, the textured evergreens and the line of tiny figures dragging canoes give a strong sense of effort and cold. The high horizon pushes the viewer into the ice, and the figures' diagonal movement adds rhythm. It lacks the grandeur of the falls, but the human labor is convincing and the mood exactly right."},"nga:50640":{"d":"A river flows through a landscape of rolling hills, sparse trees and shrubs. The sky is slightly overcast, with clouds in varied grays and patches of blue. Canoes filled with figures paddle along the river, and tall trees with detailed branches stand on the left. The palette is warm greens and browns against blues and grays. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title dates the scene to 6 February 1682.","i":"On 6 February 1682 La Salle's company, some twenty-odd Frenchmen with a larger party of Native men, women and children, reached the Mississippi from the Illinois River and began the descent to the sea. It was the great moment of his career: the river that Marquette and Jolliet had reached in 1673 would now be followed to its mouth. The mixed company of French and Native travelers was typical of the expeditions of the time.","c":"A quiet, understated image of a great beginning: the overcast sky, the sparse trees and the string of canoes convey modest scale, and the muted palette suits the sense of entering an unknown river. Catlin handles the tall trees with care, and the sky is the most expressive part. It is more atmosphere than event, and the thin drama reflects the painter's gift for place over incident."},"nga:50641":{"d":"A gathering beside a river, with tall trees and grass where several groups of people have assembled. Canoes are visible on the water and drawn up along the shore. The landscape includes a wide river, greenery and dense forest in the background, under a light blue sky with a few clouds. People are engaged in various activities along the water's edge and among the trees. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title places it at the mouth of the Arkansas River in March 1682.","i":"At the Quapaw villages near the mouth of the Arkansas River in March 1682 La Salle's party was received with hospitality and carried out the first of the ceremonies of possession by which the French claimed the Mississippi valley: planting a cross and a column bearing the arms of France, singing hymns and firing guns. The Quapaw, who lived there and were not consulted, took part as hosts and witnesses.","c":"The picture is a harmonious social scene: tall trees, grass, canoes and clusters of people create a balanced composition and an easy, hospitable mood. The subject, a formal act of claiming land, is deliberately downplayed, and the viewer must read the title to know the occasion. This lowers the drama but also lets the human gathering take the lead. As a record of a cross-cultural meeting, it is gentle and generous."},"nga:50642":{"d":"An open settlement with thatched buildings at left and center, smoke rising from the roofs, and more houses and trees on the horizon below pale green hills. In the foreground people gather in groups, some standing and others seated on the ground. A group left of center walks under an awning on a carpet laid out for them toward another group at the right. The palette is earthy browns, greens and reddish browns with light blue and gray in the sky, and a pale green border surrounds the painting.","i":"The Taensa, a people of the lower Mississippi related in language and culture to the Natchez, lived in thatched houses around a temple and a chief's residence, and their rulers were treated with great ceremony. French accounts of La Salle's visit describe the courtly welcome, with mats and carpets laid for the guests and the chief receiving them in state. The Taensa's culture was later disrupted by European disease and by the pressure of other peoples.","c":"A courtly picture: the carpet and awning, the formal approach of one group to another and the smoke rising from the thatched roofs give the scene a ceremonial calm. The pale green border gives it the air of a plate in a book. Catlin's gift for crowds of small figures is well used, and the village's neat order is persuasive. It is one of the more successful scenes of reception in the series, though necessarily imagined."},"nga:50643":{"d":"A group of people gather in a clearing next to a riverbank, with a horizon of lush trees and a pale blue sky with wispy clouds. In the foreground Native people interact with men in European-style clothing near a tall cross, and a cluster of rounded huts nestles among tall trees. The brushstrokes are fine, with a detailed crowd and subtle texture in the vegetation, in earthy browns, greens and soft pastels. The canvas is about 37 by 57 centimeters, and the museum's keywords note the Natchez.","i":"The Natchez, who lived in large villages around temple mounds on the lower Mississippi and were ruled by a sacred leader called the Great Sun, received La Salle's party in March 1682. The French raised a cross and claimed the land, as they did at every stop. The Natchez were nearly destroyed in wars with the French in 1729 to 1731, and the survivors were scattered, so the encounter in the picture marks the start of a short, tragic relationship.","c":"A calm, well-balanced scene: the cross sets a vertical accent among the trees and huts, and the crowd of Native people and Frenchmen is arranged with a good sense of interaction. The fine brushwork gives the vegetation texture, and the pastel sky is attractive. As with the rest of the series, the figures are small and summary, so the picture works best as a landscape of a meeting. Its quiet tone belies the consequences."},"nga:50644":{"d":"About twenty French men and eighteen Native people gather at a water's edge, most attending to a man who stands holding a rifle across his body and gesturing with the other hand. He wears a brown suit, dark hat and red knee-length cloak, with a sword at his hip, and points toward a rough cross set upright in marshy grass beside a white flag on a pole. Native people, some with red face paint, form an outer circle; eight canoes line the shore under copper-brown clouds. A painted frame carries the number 22, and the signature reads G. Catlin P.","i":"On 9 April 1682, near the mouth of the Mississippi, La Salle took formal possession of the whole river basin in the name of Louis XIV, naming it Louisiana in the king's honor, with a column bearing the royal arms, a cross, hymns and volleys. The claim, which covered the homelands of dozens of Native nations who were not asked, led to a French colony and, in 1803, to the Louisiana Purchase by the United States.","c":"The most formal picture in the series is also the most staged: La Salle, the center of the gathering, gestures to the cross while rings of men and Native onlookers form concentric circles around him, a clear, static design. The varied attention of the spectators gives a sense of the occasion's strangeness, and Catlin's sympathy for the onlookers is evident. The scene is competent but stiff, and the painted frame and number make it feel like a book illustration."},"nga:50645":{"d":"A coastal scene with a view of beach and ocean, a high horizon, and ships visible in the distance at left. Figures stand near a broken ship hull on the shore, while others work along the beach and near a structure with a flag. Inland, the landscape becomes more densely vegetated, under light clouds in a warm, pinkish sky. The brushstrokes are distinctive in the water, clouds and vegetation, and the palette is pastel blues, sand and touches of green. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"In 1684 La Salle sailed from France with four ships and some three hundred colonists to found a colony at the mouth of the Mississippi. He missed the river and landed on the Texas coast at Matagorda Bay, and in early 1685 the supply ship Aimable ran aground at the entrance, with much of the cargo lost. The wreck was the first of a chain of disasters that ended the colony within a few years, and Catlin's calm view belies its significance.","c":"A pastel shipwreck: the pink sky and pale sand turn a disaster into a gentle view. Catlin picks out the broken hull and tiny salvors with care, but the color softens the loss, and the picture seems to be more about the beach and the water than about the catastrophe. It shows the painter's liking for open coastal space and his difficulty with drama; as a document of a historical turning point it is oddly serene."},"nga:50646":{"d":"Small figures in the distance engage in various activities under a low horizon that gives the sky a large place. A group of soldiers in uniform line up on the horizon against green hills. In the foreground at left a group of figures, some holding what appear to be weapons or sticks, are gathered, while two individuals stand apart. The brushstrokes are smooth with fine detail, and the palette is warm yellows and greens under a brown and orange-tinged sky. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters.","i":"Encounters between La Salle's men and the peoples of the Texas prairies could turn quickly to friendship or violence, depending on gifts, gestures and past experience, and the French knew the pipe ceremony and exchanged presents. A war party might be on its way to or from a raid on an enemy, and the line of French soldiers on the horizon suggests the tension of such a meeting. The scene is the moment before the outcome is known, with both sides wary.","c":"The low horizon gives the sky a leading role, and the long line of soldiers against green hills is an effective graphic device that sets up a tension of distance. The foreground group is a little vague, and the scene's drama is held in suspense; this is a picture about waiting and watching. It shows Catlin using composition, not gesture, to tell a story, with moderate success."},"nga:50647":{"d":"A landscape with people at a campsite in the foreground, tents under trees, a plain leading to a hill in the background and more hills further away. The colors are earthy with hints of orange and blue, depicting a calm day, and the sky shifts from beige to blue. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title places the scene on a Texas prairie in April 1686.","i":"In the spring of 1686, after the colony at Fort Saint Louis had begun to fail, La Salle set out overland with about twenty men to find the Mississippi, which he had missed by hundreds of miles from the sea. The expedition crossed the prairies of eastern Texas, which were then the hunting grounds and homelands of many Native peoples. The camp in the picture is the kind of halt made on that long trek.","c":"A restful interlude in a story of hardship: the camp is small, the prairie wide, and the hills recede in layers of warm earth and pale blue. Catlin, who knew prairie landscapes from his journeys, paints the space with confidence, and the tents give a human measure. There is little incident to see, but the calm and openness are persuasive, and the picture is one of the series' quietest successes."},"nga:50648":{"d":"Seen from eye level, a group of people and various structures stand under a cloudy sky tinged with warm pink, with rolling hills on the horizon. Some people are on horseback, and others are engaged in activities; buildings with conical shapes emit smoke at the left. Fine brushstrokes create detailed foliage and hills, in a color scheme of earth tones with red and white accents. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title places the reception in a Cenis village in May 1686.","i":"The Cenis, a French rendering of Hasinai, were a Caddo confederacy of eastern Texas who farmed corn and lived in large, grass-thatched, beehive-shaped houses; they were known for their elaborate hospitality, and the name Texas derives from a Caddo word meaning friend or ally. La Salle's party, ragged after months of travel, was received and obtained horses and food from them.","c":"A lively, colorful picture in which horsemen, smoking conical houses and a warm pink sky create a crowded, convivial village scene. Catlin's attention to the architecture, the beehive-like houses, is a plus, and his small, generalized people add movement. The horses are a point of accuracy, since French accounts mention that the Cenis kept them. It is a good example of the series' ethnographic ambition."},"nga:50649":{"d":"A landscape with a group of figures in the foreground engaged in an activity, with lush greenery around them. A body of water stretches horizontally across the picture, with rolling hills and a sky with birds in flight beyond. The brushwork is soft and fluid, and the palette is dominated by calming greens, blues, pastel pink and warm yellow. The canvas is about 38 by 56 centimeters, and the title gives the subject as the death of La Salle at the hands of Duhaut.","i":"La Salle was killed by his own men in Texas in 1687, by the usual dating, shot by Pierre Duhaut, one of a group of conspirators who resented his harshness and blamed him for the colony's failures. His death ended the French attempt to found a colony at the mouth of the Mississippi, and Duhaut was in turn killed by his accomplices. The survivors scattered, and Fort Saint Louis was destroyed by Native attack in the following years.","c":"Catlin treats the murder with surprising calm. The pink sky, the soft green hills and the birds in flight make the scene idyllic, which can undercut the drama of betrayal, though it may also suggest the indifference of the land to human tragedy. As the last in the series it closes with a low-key note, one that suits the elegiac theme of ambition defeated by distance."},"nga:50309":{"d":"Three individuals stand side by side, full length, against a plain background of earth tones that emphasizes their figures and attire. They have long dark hair with feather decorations and wear traditional robes with fringes and patterns. Each holds a specific object: a fur, a quiver with arrows, and a feather fan. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 61 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"After the loss of his first Indian Gallery to creditors, Catlin re-created his collection in Belgium in the 1860s from his sketches, notes and memory, producing hundreds of pictures on card, known as the Cartoon Collection. Group portraits like this present three men as types of the Sioux warrior: the fur, quiver and eagle-feather fan are signs of status and skill. The Sioux, a large group of related Dakota, Lakota and Nakota peoples, dominated the northern Plains.","c":"Catlin's later cartoon pictures are simplified, flat and a little stiff next to his 1830s originals, and these three men stand in a tidy row like specimens. But the plain ground and bold accessories emphasize costume and bearing, and the fan, quiver and fur are drawn with enough care to be informative. As art they are modest; as documents of regalia they are useful, with the proviso that they reflect Catlin's idealizing eye."},"nga:50313":{"d":"A group stands outside in a circle, with long dark hair decorated with red, white and black feathers. Some wear tan trousers with long fringe, others black and white feathers hanging from colorful belts. Many have arms, legs and chests painted with red lines, swirls, dots or handprints, and they wear silver arm and wrist bands, earrings and long strands of beads. They hold long bows, round shields, tall spears and boomerang-shaped clubs; those at the edge dance, bent over or with arms outstretched. In the center, dancers raise tall sticks with red ovals hung with black strands.","i":"The scalp dance was a victory ceremony of many Plains peoples, held after a successful war party, in which the returning warriors and their families danced around trophies taken from enemies, displayed on poles. Catlin saw such dances in the 1830s and described them with a mixture of fascination and horror; today they are better understood as public celebrations of victory and of the safe return of the warriors. The tall sticks with red hoops carry the scalps.","c":"Catlin's dance pictures are patterns of figures more than portraits: the circle, the variety of costume details and the ring of tall sticks at the center make a lively design. The drawing is flat and the movement stiff, and the figures are schematic compared with the 1830s originals. But the paintings record dress, weapons and ritual not preserved in many other sources, if through an outsider's eyes. As art they interest more as pattern than as painting."},"nga:50314":{"d":"A group of men dance in a tightly knit, curved line, their poses dynamic and suggestive of movement and rhythm. They have dark hair styled with feathers, high cheekbones and strong profiles, and wear skirts of natural materials, carrying bows, arrows and sticks. Some sit on the ground playing drums and rattles. The background is a gradient of sky-blue and sandy brown. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 62 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"Catlin visited the Teton Sioux in 1832 and later described dog feasts and dances as ceremonies of honor and fellowship, in which the dog, a valued companion, was offered to honor a guest or to celebrate a warrior's deed. The musicians seated on the ground, with drum and rattles, are essential to such dances, which unite the dancers with the community. His account, like his picture, reflects the viewpoint of an outsider fascinated by what he saw.","c":"A good example of how Catlin's cartoon style handles movement: the curved line of dancers, bent knees and swinging arms give the rhythm of a processional, and the seated musicians anchor the composition. The faces are generalized and the space shallow, but the pictorial pattern is readable and the energy comes through. It is among the more successful of the dance scenes in the series."},"nga:50355":{"d":"Three people stand on a grassy area against a light background with a hint of blue. At left, one holds a bow and a feathered shield, wearing a fringed garment and tall moccasins. In the center, a person with long dark hair wears a richly decorated robe. At right, a person with a child on their back wears a fringed dress and holds a feathered object. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 46 by 61 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"The Comanche, masters of the horse on the southern Plains, controlled an enormous territory and were feared by Spaniards, Mexicans and Americans alike. Catlin met them in 1834, when he traveled with a US Dragoon expedition into their country, and wrote that they were awkward on foot but superb on horseback. This group portrait presents a chief, his wife with a child on her back, and a warrior, a small social unit of the Comanche world.","c":"Catlin's Comanche pictures are among his most striking because he remembered their dress and bearing well: the shield, the fringed garments, the child cradled on the woman's back. The trio is posed like a family group, with the center figure most imposing, and the pastel ground is plain. The drawing is stiff, but the attention to family and costume gives the picture warmth. It is a worthwhile record of an extraordinary people."},"nga:50365":{"d":"Three people stand side by side outdoors on an open plain of grass under a neutral sky. The one on the left wears a feather headdress and a fur wrap with colorful patterns, and holds a bow and arrows. The one in the middle wears a feather headdress, a woolen cloak, patterned leggings and moccasins. The one on the right wears feathers in the hair, a fur garment and moccasins. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 46 by 60 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"The Shoshone, whose homeland lay in the Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin, were hunters and horsemen, and the title records the name as Catlin spelled it. Catlin's travels did not take him deep into Shoshone country, so the picture presumably draws on other sources or on visitors he met. The costume details, fur wrap, woolen cloak and patterned leggings, show the mixture of native and trade materials that marked Plains dress by the nineteenth century.","c":"A formulaic group portrait: three men in a row, each given a distinct headdress and garment so that a viewer can compare. Catlin's pleasure in costume carries the picture, but the faces are generic and the stance stiff, perhaps the result of working from secondhand information. It is more catalog than character study, and of interest chiefly for the dress it records."},"nga:50385":{"d":"Three full-length figures stand together against a soft pastel sky with hints of blue and yellow. At left a woman in a decorated dress holds a feather. In the center a man with long, ornamented hair and a beaded robe holds a fur piece and a shield. At right a warrior in a dynamic stance holds a bow. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 46 by 60 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"Minatarree is Catlin's spelling of Hidatsa, a farming people of the upper Missouri who lived in earth-lodge villages beside the Mandan, whom Catlin visited in 1832. Both peoples were devastated by the smallpox epidemic of 1837, which killed most of them, and Catlin's record of 1832 became a precious memorial. This later picture is a re-creation of the sort he made in the 1860s from his sketches and memory.","c":"The variety of the trio, a woman, a man in ceremonial dress and a warrior in action, gives the picture more life than the plain row of men in other cartoon groups, and the soft sky sets off the beaded robe. The warrior's dynamic stance is the best part. The drawing is simplified and the faces generalized, but the group has an easy, natural air that Catlin's formulas often lack."},"nga:50389":{"d":"Several figures in traditional attire form a circle and perform a rhythmic dance around a central structure of long, thin poles. The figures wear feathered headdresses and have elaborately painted bodies, and the sky is softly rendered in shades of blue. The title names it the Green Corn Dance of the Minatarrees, Catlin's spelling of the Hidatsa. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 45 by 60 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"Corn was the staple crop of the Hidatsa and the Mandan, who farmed the Missouri bottomlands, and ceremonies of planting and harvest were central to their year. A green corn dance celebrates the ripening of the first crop, an event of thanksgiving and renewal. Catlin, who recorded ceremonies he witnessed with ethnographic zeal, usually noted the dancers' paint and costume with care, even if the dances' inner meaning escaped him.","c":"A pattern picture: the ring of dancers, the central poles and the soft blue sky arrange themselves into a clean, almost diagrammatic design. Catlin's focus on painted bodies and headdresses gives the figures individuality of costume, if not of face. The painting records an important ceremonial form, though it is less lively than the best of his dance scenes."},"nga:50390":{"d":"A group of figures dance in a circle, adorned with intricate attire and each holding a long spear. The background has swirling hues of blue, orange and yellow. The title names it the Buffalo Dance of the Mandan, a ceremony meant to bring the herds near. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 45 by 61 centimeters, painted in 1861, a re-creation of a subject Catlin first painted in the 1830s.","i":"In 1832 Catlin stayed at a Mandan village on the upper Missouri, painted portraits of its leaders and witnessed its ceremonies, including the Buffalo Dance, danced to bring the herds near when hunting was poor. The Mandan lived in earth lodges and farmed corn; in 1837 smallpox killed all but a small fraction of them, leaving Catlin's paintings and writings as one of the best records of their life.","c":"Catlin's Mandan pictures are his most precious, and this one holds some of the original's vigor in the swirling, almost fiery sky behind the circle of dancers. The figures are compact and symmetrical, and their spears create a lively fence of verticals. It lacks the first-hand authority of the 1832 paintings, which he had to leave behind, but it is a fitting memorial to a people who were nearly annihilated."},"nga:50398":{"d":"Three people stand side by side under a cloudy sky on an open, grassy ground. The one on the left wears a robe and has long dark hair with feathers. The one in the middle, also with dark hair and feathers, holds a staff and a shield. The one on the right, with long dark hair, wears a fur-lined robe over a decorated dress. Their clothes are bright with vibrant patterns and colors. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 62 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"Riccarree is Catlin's spelling of Arikara, also called the Ree, farmers of the Missouri valley who lived in earth-lodge villages and were frequent traders and, at times, fierce opponents of American fur traders: in 1823 the US Army and its allies attacked their towns in the first military campaign against a Plains nation. Catlin visited them in 1832.","c":"A trio in bright, patterned dress against a cloudy sky; Catlin gives each a different pose and accessory, and the central figure's staff and shield provide a vertical axis. The faces are generic, and the colors, though cheerful, are too uniform to suggest the differences of rank he intended. A modest, neatly arranged picture that works as a costume plate."},"nga:50413":{"d":"Three people, one standing and two seated, are shown on open grass under a light blue sky, within a green frame. The central figure stands with feathers in long hair and a decorated cloak, and holds a small object. The two seated figures have long dark hair and wear cloaks with intricate patterns. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 62 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"Puncah is Catlin's spelling of Ponca, a small Siouan-speaking people of the Missouri valley, whom he visited in 1832. The Ponca were later forced from their homeland in Nebraska to Indian Territory, and in 1879 their chief Standing Bear won a landmark court case in Omaha establishing that a Native American is a person under the law. The people in the picture belong to an earlier time, but the history that followed gives them added weight.","c":"The pyramid arrangement, with a standing figure between two seated ones, gives a simple, stable composition, and the intricate patterns on the cloaks are the picture's main interest. Catlin's work in this series is a record rather than an investigation, and the faces do not individualize; still the poised triad and the green frame give the picture a neat, finished air. A useful, quiet piece."},"nga:50435":{"d":"Three ball players, one each from the Choctaw, the Sioux and the Ojibwe, are the subject, in an oil on card mounted on paperboard about 45 by 61 centimeters, painted in 1861. The museum's record gives no description, so their poses, costumes, sticks and the setting are left unstated. Ball games played with netted or hooked sticks were widespread among the peoples of eastern North America and the Plains, and are the ancestors of modern lacrosse.","i":"Catlin was fascinated by Native ball play, which he saw as a combination of athletic contest, ritual and social drama; the Choctaw game, played by teams of hundreds with two sticks, was said to be rough but governed by rules. The players were stars, celebrated in their communities, and the word celebrated in the title puts them on a par with the chiefs and warriors in his gallery. The title brings three nations together in a single frame.","c":"Without a description of the picture's surface, it can only be judged as a variant of Catlin's group-portrait type, one that places the sportsmen of three nations side by side to show their different dress and equipment. His 1830s ball-play paintings are among his liveliest, so a calm lineup of three players is a more restrained image. As a record of athletic costume and equipment it is likely informative."},"nga:50439":{"d":"A group of people dance or perform a ritual near tipis, in traditional clothing and headdresses, dancing energetically in a circle on a grassy field. In the background tipis stand in a line, decorated with geometric patterns and supported by long wooden poles. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 61 centimeters, painted in 1861, and the title names it the Slaves' Dance of the Saukie, Catlin's spelling of Sauk.","i":"The Sauk, or Sac, and their allies the Fox lived in the upper Mississippi valley until the Black Hawk War of 1832 broke their hold on their lands in Illinois; Catlin painted the war leader Black Hawk that year, when he was a prisoner. The title names a Slaves' Dance, a name whose meaning the museum does not explain and that may reflect Catlin's own interpretation of the ceremony he saw.","c":"The dance ring, the long-poled lodges and the green grass compose a clear and lively pattern, and the dancers' headdresses give the scene color. But the figures are small and repetitive, and the drawing is summary. Like most of the cartoon dances it is better as ethnographic pattern than as painting, though the plain energy of the circle carries it."},"nga:50442":{"d":"A group of men gather closely in a dance formation, full length and facing slightly right. Knees are bent and arms posed in movement, and their faces show a range of expressions, with body paint and feathers. Their long dark hair is styled with feathers, and they wear natural-colored, fringed garments decorated with feathers and painted designs. Soft earth tones blend upward to a light blue sky, with green grass underfoot. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 61 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"K'nisteneux is Catlin's spelling of Kristineaux, a name for the Cree, a people of the northern forests and plains of Canada. Bear dances were widespread among northern peoples, who honored the bear as a powerful animal and sought its spirit's goodwill before and after the hunt. Catlin recorded bear dances among several peoples; in such rituals the dancers are not merely entertainers but intermediaries between people and the animal world.","c":"The tight formation of men, each in a slightly different pose, gives the picture a sense of collective rhythm, and the range of expressions, unusual in these dance scenes, lends individuality. The earth tones and fringes are well matched to the grass and sky. The anatomy is simplified, but the picture catches a lively moment better than many in the series."},"nga:50443":{"d":"A group of figures is arranged in a circular formation, their heads painted bright red, carrying spears or sticks. The background is light tan with hints of blue and green, and the style of the figures and attire implies a cultural or historical narrative. The title names it the Discovery Dance of the Saukie, Catlin's spelling of Sauk. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 44 by 60 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"Catlin described several war dances among the Sauk and Fox, in which participants reenacted the stages of a war party; a Discovery Dance, by its name, presents the scouts' discovery of the enemy, a vital stage in raiding. Such dances rehearsed tactics and honored the warriors' courage and cunning. The red-painted heads may signal war, since red was a color of war and vitality among many Plains and Woodland peoples.","c":"The red heads in a ring against a pale ground make a very graphic image: the effect is almost like a frieze, and the repeated spears add rhythm. The figures are nearly identical and the picture lacks the detail of the larger dance scenes, but it is striking for its limited, bold color. Of modest finish, it works as a diagram of a ritual."},"nga:50457":{"d":"Four people stand at the edge of a body of water, two close and two farther away, all facing left toward pale blue water framed by large gray rocks. In the near pair, one person stands with a large fish in both hands, wearing earrings, a fringed skirt and a yellow-and-red belt, with a child in a swaddled cradle on their back; behind stands an adult in a light blanket with a red stripe. A basket of gray fish lies on the grass. In the distance one person fires an arrow into the water and another sits with a basket of fish.","i":"The Salish people known as the Flathead lived in the mountain valleys of what is now Montana and Idaho, and fished and hunted bison. The name Flathead is a misnomer, since they did not flatten their heads; it may have come from sign language. The picture shows a daily activity, fishing, with a child cradled on a parent's back, a reminder that Catlin's gallery aimed to record family life as well as warriors and ceremonies.","c":"An unusually domestic subject for Catlin: the fisher holding up a big catch, the swaddled baby on the back and the basket of fish at the feet make a lively little genre scene. The gray rocks and pale water give a cool setting that differs from his sunny prairies. The figures in the distance are sketchy, but the foreground pair is drawn with care and humor. It is a fresh, appealing departure from the standard lineup."},"nga:50621":{"d":"Three individuals stand in a line on grass in a bordered frame with the number 21 at the top. The one at left, with long dark hair, wears a light-colored fringed garment with beadwork and holds a feathered fan. The middle figure wears an elaborate feathered headdress and a beaded, fringed garment, and holds a feather-adorned staff or spear. The one at right, with long dark hair, wears a fringed, beaded garment, holds a wooden pipe and carries a fringed, beaded bag. It is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 45 by 60 centimeters, painted in 1861.","i":"The Omaha, who farmed and hunted bison in the Missouri valley in what is now Nebraska, were visited by Catlin in 1832. Chief, wife and warrior form a small society in miniature: leadership, family and defense. The number 21 at the top is probably a catalog number, a sign that this is one of a numbered series, the Cartoon Collection that Catlin created in the 1860s as a re-creation of his Indian Gallery.","c":"Catlin's formula is plain: three people, three props, an even line. What lifts the picture is the careful painting of beadwork and fringe, and the feather fan, staff and pipe that distinguish each figure. The plain grass and bordered frame give it the air of a plate in a catalog. The composition is static and the faces are blank, but as a record of Omaha dress it is informative."},"nga:50461":{"d":"A conical lodge stands in the foreground beside a river, its covering stirring gently, with a canoe on the water at right under a soft pastel sky. Lush trees frame the background, and a rack near the lodge displays hanging items; the grass is bright with rustic color. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 63 centimeters, dated 1862 with a question mark, and the title names the Nayas Indians.","i":"The name Nayas, as Catlin spelled it, is not easy to match with a modern nation, and the museum's description of a lodge, a canoe and a drying rack gives few clues. Such scenes, without portraits, show the settings and tools of Native life: fishing and travel by water, the processing of fish or meat on racks. They reflect Catlin's aim of recording not only people but their material world.","c":"A peaceful, almost pastoral view in which lodge, canoe and drying rack stand for a way of life; the trees frame it neatly and the pastel sky is gentle. With no figures to speak of, the picture asks the viewer to read the objects, and it does so with a quiet charm. It is somewhat generic and sentimental, and the unclear nation of the title limits its documentary value."},"nga:50310":{"d":"Three full-length figures stand in a row. The one at the left, arms close to the body, is wrapped in a garment with a bird and a fur collar and has straight hair past the shoulders. The center figure wears a large feather headdress and a detailed cape with a sun-like emblem, with matching leggings, and the face is visible under the headdress. The one at the right stands in profile holding a spear and shield, in a fringed garment and a feathered headband, against a neutral background. The card is about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The sun-like emblem on the chief's cape is typical of the symbols Plains artists painted on robes to record deeds or visions, and it gives the chief an air of cosmic authority. The family group, a chief, his daughter and a warrior, expresses Catlin's wish to show the social order of the Sioux. The date range, 1861 to 1869, covers the years of the Cartoon Collection, painted in Belgium while the Sioux were fighting the US Army.","c":"The chief's headdress and cape are the visual center, and the sun emblem glows against the plain ground; the daughter and warrior are quieter foils. Catlin remains an illustrator rather than a painter in these cartoons, but there is a certain stately simplicity in the three standing figures. The picture invites the viewer to imagine what the chief's authority felt like, though its flatness limits the effect."},"nga:50311":{"d":"Four adults and a child sit on the ground within an oval frame. At the far left a child lies with a bow and arrow. Next to the child a woman in a fringed garment with intricate designs sits cross-legged. At her right a man with long hair, feathers and facial markings holds a pipe, and beside him sits another woman in a similar garment. Seated at the far right is a fourth adult with long hair, feathers and a beaded necklace, also holding a pipe. The soft landscape behind is nondescript, in blues and greens.","i":"A pipe held in a council or among visitors was a sign of peace and good faith: smoking together sealed agreements and welcomed guests. Catlin, who was often offered the pipe on his travels, took it as a gesture of friendship. The seated group, with women, a child and two men sharing pipes, conveys a domestic council, with the child's bow a hint of the next generation of hunters. The date range, 1861 to 1869, reflects the long period of the Cartoon Collection.","c":"The oval frame and seated arrangement give the picture the intimacy of a miniature, and the pipes create a quiet link among the sitters. The scene is sweet, perhaps over-sweet: the child with the bow is picturesque, and the colors are soft. Catlin gets the sense of gathered family, but the faces are blank and the composition rather static. It stays in the memory more as a gentle idea than as a vivid image."},"nga:50312":{"d":"A Plains encampment seen from slightly above, with a high horizon and a sky of soft clouds in light blue, pink and white. In the center stand tipis decorated with geometric patterns in beige, brown and red, and figures, mostly Native people, are scattered around the camp. In the distance a line of figures on horseback moves diagonally to the right. The brushwork is detailed, with attention to the patterns on the tipis and the texture of the land, in soft greens and light browns. The card is about 48 by 64 centimeters.","i":"Tipis, the portable homes of the Plains peoples, were covered with bison hides sewn together and often painted, and a family could take one down and move it in an hour or two; the work of making and raising them belonged to women. A village scene like this records the social world that supported the warriors and chiefs of Catlin's portraits. The date range, 1861 to 1869, places it among the cartoon re-creations.","c":"A gentle, panoramic image where small, well-observed incidents spread across a quiet landscape: the patterned tipis make a pleasing rhythm, and the line of riders gives the picture a sense of movement and distance. Catlin's strength in such scenes is the sense of an ordered community living on a vast plain. The painting is soft and rather flat, but its clarity and pleasant color make it one of the more appealing village views."},"nga:50315":{"d":"A large gathering of people in an open landscape: a wide grassy area bordered by hills under a blue sky with scattered clouds. Numerous figures are engaged in various activities, some seated, others standing or involved in a dynamic assembly, with the composition suggesting motion and engagement around a central activity. The palette is warm and earthy in the foreground, with cooler greens and blues framing the background. The title names it the Ball-Play of the Women of the Sioux, and the card is about 47 by 64 centimeters.","i":"Women's ball games were played by many Plains peoples, often with hooked or netted sticks and goalposts, and were athletic contests that drew large crowds and bets. Catlin, who was fascinated by games of skill and wrote vivid accounts of men's ball play, also recorded this women's game among the Sioux. The picture challenges the notion that Native women's roles were limited to the household, though it still reflects Catlin's outsider viewpoint.","c":"The panorama of a field full of figures is a good vehicle for Catlin's gift for crowds: the open grass, the framing hills and the scattered groups give a sense of festival. The brushwork is small and summary, so the game itself is hard to read, and the faces are generic. As an image of communal recreation it is cheerful, and a relief from the ritual solemnity of most of the series."},"nga:50316":{"d":"A dog feast, a ceremonial meal of the Sioux, is the subject of this oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 64 centimeters, dated between 1861 and 1869. The museum's record gives no description, so the number of people, the dwellings, the setting and the colors are left unstated. Catlin attended a dog feast during his 1832 visit to the Sioux and described it in his writings, and the picture is part of his effort to record their customs.","i":"Among the Sioux, as among other Plains peoples, the dog feast was an honor: the dog, a valued companion, was sacrificed and its meat served to guests or to men being honored for a deed, an act of hospitality that carried respect rather than contempt. Catlin, who attended such a feast, wrote of it with the curiosity of an outsider; his picture is part of his effort to record the full range of Native customs, including those that startled his audience.","c":"With no description to go by, the picture can only be placed in the ethnographic series, as a record of a ceremony that Catlin found interesting and unusual. The subject asks for a circle of figures and a central pot, and a painter of his skill could make that readable; the interest is documentary more than pictorial. The viewer should look for the arrangement of the participants and the details of dress."},"nga:50317":{"d":"This is a picture of an object, not a scene: a flat, frontal view of a hide, symmetrical and without figures or landscape. A central rectangle carries bold geometric ornament, with rows of red and tan lines running across it, and an irregularly shaped border, probably the outline of the hide itself, surrounds the field. A swirling pattern on the left side breaks the symmetry. The colors are earthy, with muted greens. Painted in oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters, it works like a specimen drawing in paint.","i":"'Facsimile' means a faithful copy, and the panel belongs to Catlin's wider effort to record Native material culture alongside faces and villages. Plains robes were made from buffalo hides, and the porcupine quills named in the title were dyed, flattened and sewn or woven into geometric bands, a skilled craft usually practiced by women. Designs of this kind often carried meaning for their makers, but a copy made decades after Catlin's travels cannot say what this one meant.","c":"Beside Catlin's portraits and villages this is a modest thing, and an honest one: he does not dress the object up, and the measured symmetry suggests careful looking. But a facsimile is only as good as its source, and flat paint cannot convey what makes quillwork remarkable, its fine, raised, glinting surface. As art it asks very little of the viewer; as evidence it is most useful to someone who can compare it with surviving robes."},"nga:50318":{"d":"An elevated viewpoint shows a Native rider on horseback chasing a buffalo, with more buffalo and riders spread across the grasslands behind, under a blue sky with white clouds. The palette is dominated by earthy tones. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 47 by 63 centimeters, dated between 1861 and 1869, and the title places the hunt among the Sioux of the upper Missouri.","i":"The mounted buffalo hunt, with bow or lance, was the center of Plains life: bison supplied meat, hides for shelter and clothing, and bone and sinew for tools. Catlin painted many chases and wrote with passion about the herds, warning in the 1830s that they were being destroyed and proposing a national park to preserve them along with the peoples who depended on them. The picture records the world that he feared was ending.","c":"An energetic subject that suits the cartoon style well: the rider and buffalo form a clear diagonal, and the spread of figures across the plain gives a sense of scale. The elevated view allows the whole chase to be read at once, though it flattens the drama. The earthy palette is appropriate. Catlin's buffalo chases are among his most popular images, and this later version keeps their dash."},"nga:50319":{"d":"The aftermath of a buffalo hunt is the subject of this oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 48 by 64 centimeters, dated between 1861 and 1869. The museum's record gives no description, so the figures, the carcasses, the camp and the colors are left unstated. After a successful chase on the Plains, the work of butchering the animals, drying the meat and preparing the hides, much of it done by women, could fill days.","i":"The buffalo hunt did not end with the kill. Women and men worked together to skin and butcher the animals, to cut the meat into strips for drying and to prepare the hides, and nearly every part of the animal was used. The title implies a companion to Catlin's pictures of the chase itself, and shows his interest in the economic life that surrounded the hunt, in an effort to show Plains life as a whole system.","c":"Without a description of the surface the picture can only be judged by its place in the series, as a quieter counterpart to the energetic chase scenes. Aftermath pictures need to convey labor and abundance, and Catlin's habit of small, clear figures should serve this well. As an image of the cycle of Plains life it is likely informative and, in its calm, a worthwhile pendant."},"nga:50320":{"d":"A wide view from a slightly elevated perspective shows a scene of daily life: a high horizon where green hills meet a sky painted in soft strokes of blue, pink and orange. On the grassy plains, in front and behind, figures, tipis, people and horses engage in various activities. An oval frame contains the composition. The palette is earthy greens and browns with pastel hues in the sky. The picture is an oil on card mounted on paperboard, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Catlin's village scenes were meant to counter the picture of Native Americans as wandering savages; he showed them as members of organized communities with homes, families and daily routines. The oval frame, which recalls a print or a miniature, gives the picture a finished, almost decorative air. Painted in the 1860s, when the Plains wars were beginning, it looks back to a time that Catlin knew was ending.","c":"A calm, slightly sentimental portrait of Plains life: the soft sky, the neat arrangement of lodges, people and horses and the oval frame all contribute to a pleasing, orderly effect. The activities are summarized rather than observed, and the sense of place is general. The picture is attractive and informative, but Catlin's idealization smooths out the harder realities of Plains life and politics."},"nga:50321":{"d":"A peaceful landscape viewed from an elevated perspective, with a horizon dividing sky from land and distant blue hills. The sky has soft blues and gray clouds, and the brushwork is smooth, creating a serene atmosphere. A group of people on horseback carrying goods moves along a path toward the rolling hills, suggesting movement and continuity. The palette is earthy greens, browns and distant cooler tones. The title names Halsey's Bluff, and the card is about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Plains peoples such as the Sioux moved their camps seasonally, following the buffalo, and households packed their tipis and goods on horses and on travois, frames dragged behind horses or dogs. A column on the march was a sight that Catlin described with admiration. The picture, like many in the series, shows the community in motion rather than at war or in ceremony, with Halsey's Bluff as the landmark named in the title.","c":"The long perspective and quiet sky make the line of horsemen small and dignified within the landscape, and the smooth brushwork lends calm to the scene. It lacks the drama of the buffalo chase but gains a gentle epic quality, as a people moves through a country that is their own. The riders are generalized, and the color is subdued, but the picture has a restful, dignified sweep."},"nga:50322":{"d":"A wide, quiet landscape opens beneath a sky that shades from light yellow through orange to pink, with a few wisps of cloud. A line of trees and rolling hills lies in the middle distance. Tipis stand clustered in the foreground with people gathered around them, and figures in canoes move on a river or lake. The ground is painted in warm yellows, greens and browns with smooth, meticulous strokes. Painted small, on card, it is closer to a view of a place than to a portrait of a people.","i":"Lac du Cygne is French for Swan Lake, one of many French names that fur traders left on the lakes and rivers of the upper Mississippi country. Catlin's title calls the place a Sioux village, and the canoes are a reminder that Sioux communities living by lakes and rivers traveled by water as well as on horseback. Views like this were meant to show how people lived, with their homes, boats and surroundings, and not only how they looked.","c":"The sky is the best thing here, graded from pale yellow to rose with a lightness that suits the quiet title. The village is more diagrammatic: tipis and figures sit in tidy clusters under even light, so that nothing is hidden and little is felt. Catlin's village views often work this way, as reporting delivered in the manner of a tasteful landscape, and this one is serene, legible and a little bland."},"nga:50323":{"d":"A low horizon runs across a wide landscape of green and brown ground, with small clusters of trees and several tent-like structures. In the distance, water reflects the sky, and a canoe is visible. The sky is the picture's main event: warm oranges and yellows give way to darker clouds above. Whatever figures the title implies are small beside the land and the weather.","i":"War parties on the Plains were usually small groups of volunteers who followed a man with a reputation for success, and they often traveled light and on foot to raid horses or avenge a death. Catlin's title does not say what this party intends. Set against a sunset, it takes on a quiet, almost elegiac tone, which suits how Catlin liked to present his subjects: dignified, picturesque and, in his view, in danger of disappearing.","c":"The landscape is competent and atmospheric, with a low horizon that hands most of the panel to the sky, and there is a real hush in the warm light on the water. What is missing is the drama the title promises. A war party the viewer must search for is a weak subject, and the picture works better as a study of weather and terrain than as a record of Sioux life."},"nga:50324":{"d":"Seen from a slightly raised viewpoint, a dense forest of tall trees and heavy foliage closes off the horizon. A dark body of water lies to the left, and on the right a warm glow burns among the trunks with figures gathered around it, so that the light becomes the focus of a picture otherwise built from deep blues and greens. The hour seems to be night or twilight. The brushwork is fine and exact, especially in the leaves.","i":"A bivouac is a temporary camp in the open, a word from military usage, and Catlin's title treats a Sioux war party like soldiers on campaign. The subject is the pause between exertions, men resting at a fire rather than fighting. Catlin knew Sioux communities from his travels of 1832 and 1835 to 1836, but this panel was painted in the 1860s, so it is best read as a composed, remembered scene and not as a note made on the spot.","c":"Most of Catlin's later panels are bright, evenly lit afternoons, so this dim forest with its single point of firelight comes as a welcome change. The glow gathers the figures and gives the dark greens a purpose, and the idea recalls the European tradition of night scenes lit by one flame. The people are tiny and summary, however, and the trees and water are handled with more assurance than the warriors they shelter."},"nga:50325":{"d":"A ring of dancers turns on light green grass beneath a gentle blue sky with a few clouds. The figures wear feathers and body paint, and some carry objects that look like instruments or ceremonial items. The whole scene is set inside a painted oval border on the card, which gives it the look of a keepsake miniature or cameo. The circle of dancers is the organizing idea of the picture.","i":"Dance was central to Sioux social and religious life, and Catlin painted many kinds, from solemn rites to social gatherings. The word 'amusing' in his title points to a lighter occasion, though without his written account the particular dance and its meaning cannot be named. The circle was a natural form for many Plains dances, and the feathers, paint and carried objects mark the dancers as dressed for an occasion and not merely at play.","c":"A dance gave Catlin a rare excuse for rhythm, and the repeating figures around the ring hold the picture together better than many of his standing groups do. The limits are familiar: the dancers are faceless and weightless, and the pretty oval border turns movement into something rehearsed for display. It is lively in plan and polite in execution, an arrangement more than an experience."},"nga:50326":{"d":"A wide view from above takes in figures and animals in the foreground and a horizon of hills and trees beyond. Overhead the sky is full of swirling clouds, painted with a softness that suggests movement, and the earth tones of brown, green and gold give a feeling of late afternoon or early light. The figures and animals blend into their surroundings rather than standing out from them. The handling is soft but still detailed.","i":"Catlin painted at least two bivouacs of Sioux war parties, one apparently at night and this one at sunrise. A sunrise camp is a moment of decision: the party has rested and must now break camp and move on, and the title leaves open whether toward a raid or toward home. Catlin lingered on the hour of the day because light was his chief means of giving a scene mood, and here it softens a subject that might otherwise be grim.","c":"The clouds are the liveliest thing on the card, and they show that Catlin could handle air and light with some freedom when a subject allowed it. The figures, by contrast, melt into the ground so completely that the war party becomes hard to follow, and the soft brushwork, gentle where it might be crisp, flattens the narrative. It is a pleasant sunrise with people in it, not yet a picture about them."},"nga:50327":{"d":"Against a soft and muted background, three full-length figures stand side by side. At left a man leans on one leg, in fringed clothing and a feathered headdress, with a bow and a shield. In the center a figure in a decorated garment with animal motifs holds a staff hung with feathers. At right a figure in a wrapped garment stands with folded arms and holds a fan-like object.","i":"The Crow, who call themselves Apsáalooke, were known to traders and travelers as superb horsemen and dressers, and Catlin paid close attention to their clothing. Feathered staffs and animal designs on garments were often marks of status or achievement among Plains peoples, though the exact meanings here cannot be read from the picture. Catlin painted Crow people on his 1832 journey up the Missouri, and later groupings like this one repeat that early observation in a tidier form.","c":"This is Catlin the cataloger: three figures presented almost as specimens, each in a different pose to show a different set of dress and arms. The costume is described with care, and the pale ground lets it stand out. What the panel lacks is life, since the faces are close to interchangeable and the bodies stand without weight. Set beside the single portraits of the 1830s, the later grouping feels formulaic, informative rather than moving."},"nga:50328":{"d":"Seven people stand close together, filling the oval almost to its edges. At far left a child, seen from behind, holds a thin bow and arrow; at far right a shorter figure, perhaps a woman, cradles a small dog. The five between them wear tan robes with uneven edges, fur and long tan leggings, their garments painted with geometric shapes and with figures of people and animals, some on horseback. Faces and bodies are striped red and black, and necklaces, earrings and bracelets abound. A large bird, apparently an eagle, perches behind the second figure from the right.","i":"The painted robes are the key to this panel. Plains men often decorated garments with pictures of their deeds in war, and the horsemen on the most elaborate robe probably record achievements of the wearer, though that reading is a guess. A bird perched on a person's back may refer to a spirit helper or to a tamed eagle kept for its feathers. Catlin's title says only 'distinguished' and names no individual.","c":"This is the most densely packed of the Crow groups, and the density is its virtue: nearly every inch is information, from the robe pictographs to the stripes of paint. It is also its problem, because seven people pressed into an oval leave little room for composition, and the eye wanders over details without finding a center. As documentation it is richer than most; as a picture it is crowded and static."},"nga:50329":{"d":"Three figures stand in a row against a soft, cloudy sky, with faint mountains and grassy ground behind them. At left a man holds a spear upright; his dark hair carries feathers and his garment is decorated. The central figure wears a feathered headdress, a fringed tunic with circular decorations and moccasins, and holds a bow and arrows. At right a figure turns slightly aside, hands folded, in a long garment with a patterned shawl and hair tied back. An oval within a larger green frame encloses the scene.","i":"Catlin often arranged Native sitters by role, as chief, warrior and wife, and the arrangement tells the viewer what a Crow community was made of: leaders, fighters and the women whose work produced clothing, lodges and food. Circles on a tunic or a patterned shawl were sources of pride, since Crow dress was famous among traders. Whether the figures are portraits of particular people or types assembled from several is not known.","c":"The cloudy sky and faint mountains give this group more air than most cards in the series, and the central figure's pose, upright with bow and arrows, has a certain dignity. Even so, the figures are lined up like a frieze, each facing front, and the faces carry little individual character. It is a decorous, attractive card that says more about costume than about people."},"nga:50330":{"d":"A tipi stands in a grassy field with Native figures nearby going about daily activities, while horses graze in the background beneath a serene sky of wispy clouds. The colors are earthy tones and gentle blues. The title points to a chief at his toilette, meaning his grooming and dressing, as the subject. The scene is laid out as a quiet piece of camp life on a card some 47 by 63 centimeters across.","i":"A toilette, in the old sense, is the ritual of washing, dressing and arranging one's appearance. Crow men were remarked on by visitors for the care they gave to their long hair and finery, and a picture of a chief preparing himself treats Crow dress as a deliberate art and not as mere costume. How closely the scene follows anything Catlin witnessed is unknown, since the panel was painted decades after his travels.","c":"The tipi, figures and grazing horses make an agreeable, evenly lit setting, and the wispy clouds are touched in lightly. For a subject as private as a chief at his dressing, the card seems to keep a public distance, wide in view and small in figures, and the intimacy the title promises is mostly left to the imagination. It is an unusual idea that the execution treats as routine."},"nga:50331":{"d":"Within a rounded frame, a calm lake lies in the foreground, bordered by a sandy shore where people sit or recline on the bank and others stand in the water. Sparse greenery rises behind them toward distant tipis, and mountains and clouds fill the sky. The strokes are smooth, and the palette is earthy browns with soft greens and blues. It is a quiet, leisurely subject for an artist better known for dress and arms.","i":"Bathing in a lake or river was an ordinary part of camp life in warm weather, and here the usual display of feathers and weapons gives way to leisure. The title's 'warriors' says who the bathers are, not what they are doing. Pictures like this show that Catlin's gallery also records ordinary days, which the combative reputation of the Plains peoples tended to hide from his audiences.","c":"A quiet idea, well suited to the small format: the water gives the card a broad horizontal calm, and the distant tipis place the scene without insistence. But the figures are too small and too lightly drawn to carry much feeling, and the shoreline is handled with the same even softness as everything else. It is pleasant and restful, and rather easy to forget."},"nga:50332":{"d":"Tipis spread across an open plain in the light of a sunset. In the foreground a cluster of large tipis is surrounded by people busy with various tasks, and more tents are scattered toward the distance. Low hills lie before the horizon, and the sky blends sunset colors into cooler tones above. A few trees stand in the scene. It has the character of a panoramic encampment view rather than a portrait.","i":"The tipi was the portable home of the buffalo-hunting Plains peoples: a cone of hides on a frame of poles that the women of a household could take down and carry to a new camp in a short time. A village on the move was a highly organized thing, and Crow camps, with their large herds of horses, had a reputation for grandeur among traders.","c":"Catlin keeps his viewpoint high and his light even, so that the encampment reads like a map made pleasant: every lodge is visible and every activity separate. The sunset sky is the one touch of poetry, and its blended warm and cool bands are done with a deft, light hand. Otherwise the card is more inventory than drama, and it is hard to tell apart from other village views in the series."},"nga:50333":{"d":"A settlement lies in the near distance, ringed by lush greenery, with a row of traditional structures and small figures close to the viewer. Behind them dense foliage rises into hills, and beyond the hills stand mountains, their upper parts partly veiled in cloud. The technique is smooth and gentle, and the palette stays within greens, browns and blues. The layered recession, from lodges through woods and foothills to peaks, is the picture's main device.","i":"The Salmon River rises in the mountains of present-day Idaho, far west of the Missouri country that Catlin traveled in 1832, and the title places a Crow village there. Crow bands ranged widely across the northern Plains and the Rockies in search of buffalo and grass for their horses. The museum dates the panel only to between 1855 and 1869, so it was made long after Catlin's first western journeys, and its scene was worked up at a distance.","c":"As a landscape this is among the more ambitious cards in the series. The recession from lodges to foothills to cloud-wrapped peaks is clearly staged, and the soft handling gives it a gentle, almost pastoral mood. The cost is that a Crow village becomes scenery: the figures are small and incidental, and the settlement sits like a hamlet in a European valley. It is attractive and quiet, and says more about Catlin's taste for picturesque views than about Crow life."},"nga:50334":{"d":"Blue-green mountains rise above a landscape of grassland, where a path follows a riverbank and a few tipis stand among small human figures. The sky is pastel blue and pink, and the palette is dominated by greens, blues and earth tones. The brushstrokes are smooth and deliberate. As the title says, this is a modest settlement and not a great encampment, one small incident in a large and peaceful country.","i":"Village pictures like this support Catlin's chief claim for his gallery: that he was recording a way of life, not just faces. Crow families typically lived in small groups through much of the year, spreading out to find grass for their horses, and gathered into large camps in summer for hunts and ceremonies. The panel was probably worked up from earlier sketches and memory.","c":"Small is the right word. Catlin works at a reduced scale here, with figures like punctuation marks, and the cool blue-green mountains give the landscape unusual depth for the series. The deliberate, smooth brushwork keeps everything legible, but also keeps everything the same: grass, water and hills are handled with identical care, so no single passage draws the eye. A tidy, pleasant view, closer to a travel illustration than to a painting with a point of view."},"nga:50335":{"d":"Three figures stand on grassy ground against a plain sky of beige, blue and green. At left a person with dark hair wears a pink garment trimmed in white and green, arms crossed. In the center a figure in a detailed outfit with a circular pattern on the chest and feathered headwear holds a long pole. At right a figure with a bow wears feathered headwear and a decorated garment. The background is a simple wash of sky and grass.","i":"The Blackfoot confederacy, the Siksika with their allies the Kainai and Piikani, dominated the northwestern Plains from what is now Montana into Alberta, and was feared and respected as a power in war and trade. Catlin painted Blackfoot sitters at Fort Union in 1832. The circular pattern on the central figure's chest may be a sun or shield motif, but the picture does not explain it, and the title says only warriors and a woman.","c":"This is a pale, quiet card. The pink garment and the central figure's patterned chest are the only strong notes, and the plain sky leaves the figures nothing to react against. Catlin's drawing is competent but not alive: arms cross and poles are held, yet no gesture feels like a decision. It succeeds as a record of the way these figures dressed and does little to suggest what they thought."},"nga:50336":{"d":"Four figures in traditional clothing stand and sit within an oval on a beige card. Two adults sit at the center, one adult stands at left holding a spear and shield, and a child stands at right holding a bow and quiver. Behind them is an open landscape with clouds. The group is balanced and calm, with the seated pair giving weight to the middle. The card itself is mounted on paperboard.","i":"To Catlin a medicine man was a figure of spiritual power and healing, and he explained that 'medicine' among the peoples he visited meant mystery or sacred power, not drugs. In Plains societies leadership often drew on both war honors and spiritual standing, which may be why a chief, his wife and a medicine man are grouped. The child with a bow is a reminder that boys were trained in hunting skills from an early age.","c":"A neat, symmetrical card, anchored by the seated pair. The child at the edge, with a miniature bow and quiver, brings in a change of scale and a hint of the future, and the oval on its beige mount adds a note of old-fashioned portrait miniature. But the group is stiff and the faces are similar, and the picture is better as a family tableau than as a character study."},"nga:50337":{"d":"Three men in traditional dress occupy a grassy field with trees behind them and a blue sky streaked with light clouds. One sits on the ground facing front with his knees drawn up, while the other two stand side by side, facing each other. All wear feathers in medium-length dark hair and finely decorated cloaks draped over their shoulders in soft earth tones, one marked with a sun design in vivid color. A hide hangs over the shoulder of one standing man.","i":"Unlike the formal rows of many cards, this trio is arranged as if in conversation, two standing men facing each other across a seated third. Painted robes were a Blackfoot specialty: men wore hide cloaks with designs recording war honors or visions, and a sun motif would suit a people for whom the Sun was a central figure in belief. The great summer sun ceremonies were among the largest gatherings of the northern Plains.","c":"The grouping is better than most: the seated man breaks the row, the two standing figures hint at an exchange, and the trees and sky behind them are lightly done. The cloaks are the real subject, and their colors, especially the sun design, are the high points. But the poses remain stock, the faces show little expression, and the feeling of conversation is more a matter of arrangement than of observed life."},"nga:50338":{"d":"Three figures stand in an oval vignette. At left a man stands upright with a rifle in one hand, wearing a feathered headdress, a necklace, arm bands and a fringed garment around his waist. The middle figure turns away, seen from behind, wrapped in fur and apparently wearing a feathered headdress. At right a woman with long dark hair, in a pink shawl over a patterned light dress, carries an infant on her back in a woven basket. Behind them are grass, a light blue sky and clouds.","i":"The Arapaho lived on the central Plains, closely allied with the Cheyenne, and followed the buffalo on horseback. The rifle shows that trade goods had reached them, while the woven carrier with its infant records the daily work of Arapaho women. A figure seen from behind, as here, shows the back of a robe, often decorated as richly as the front. Whether the three are individual portraits or a type is not stated.","c":"Turning one figure away is the card's best idea. After the usual row of frontal poses, the fur-wrapped back breaks the symmetry and gives the group some rhythm, and the mother with her child is a human note that the weapons cannot supply. The faces are generic and the landscape only a hint, so the effect is of a good arrangement without much individuality, an agreeable page from a pictorial survey of dress."},"nga:50339":{"d":"Three figures from head to toe stand in a plain, softly brushed setting. At left a figure stands upright, arms crossed and head slightly bowed, with long dark hair tied back and a knee-length garment. The central figure sits on what looks like a rock, holding a spear upright, face partly hidden. At right a figure with feathers in long dark hair stands holding a spear, torso bare, a garment covering the waist and thighs. Subtle earth tones and light vegetation at their feet are all the setting offers.","i":"The Arapaho were buffalo hunters of the central Plains, and spears served both in the hunt and in close fighting. Catlin gave no names, and the picture leaves the figures' ranks and relationships unstated. The inclusion of a woman reflects his habit of pairing men with women in such groups, to represent a household or community and not only a fighting force. The date of the panel, between 1861 and 1869, places it in his later gallery.","c":"This is one of the plainest of Catlin's group portraits: a bare ground, long upright spears and a bowed head. That restraint is not unattractive, and the verticals of the spears give the group a rhythm. But the figures are so lightly sketched, with the central face half hidden, that the card feels unfinished next to richer examples, and there is little here to reward a second look."},"nga:50340":{"d":"A family group stands in a row within an oval frame on a neutral-toned card. The adult male at the center wears traditional clothing, with feathers in his long black hair, and holds a fur piece. Beside him a woman holds a child's hand, the child wearing little, and two young people in fringed tunics stand face to face on the other side. Behind them are a light blue sky, green foliage and brown ground.","i":"The Assiniboine, who call themselves Nakoda, ranged over the northern Plains and parklands north of the Missouri as hunters and traders, long allied with the Cree. Catlin met Assiniboine people at Fort Union in 1832, where they came to trade. A warrior shown with wife and children fits his aim of presenting Native people as members of families and communities, though the identities of these sitters are not known.","c":"A domestic scene in the oval format, and gently affecting for being so unassuming: the child's hand in the woman's is the one gesture of contact in a row of straight-ahead poses. Otherwise this is Catlin's usual procedure, figures stood in a line against a pale landscape, and the oval frame lends it the stiffness of a nineteenth-century family keepsake. The warrior at the center is hardly more than a support for his regalia."},"nga:50341":{"d":"A horizontal oval is split down the middle to show the same man twice. On the left he stands in profile in Assiniboine dress: a feathered headdress, a tan leather tunic with decorated medallions, beaded shoes, a pelt trailing behind, a long pipe in hand, and a domed white building in the distance. On the right he stands with his back half turned in a dark military-style coat with gold epaulets, a top hat and a sword at his hip, with bottles in his pockets, holding a fan and an umbrella. Tents appear far off.","i":"The pairing appears to repeat Catlin's well-known picture of Wi-jún-jon, called The Light or Pigeon's Egg Head, an Assiniboine who traveled to Washington in the early 1830s and came home in an army uniform. In Catlin's telling, his tales of the white man's world were not believed and he was killed. The fan, umbrella and bottles are Catlin's satire on what 'civilization' had done to him, a view colored by his dislike of the whiskey trade.","c":"The painting stands out because it has an argument. The left half is dignified, with its quiet profile and long pipe; the right half is a caricature, from the epaulets to the pocket bottles. That contrast gives it more bite than most cards in the series, though the moral is muddled: Catlin sympathizes with the man yet mocks the clothes he was given, and the joke is at the sitter's expense."},"nga:50342":{"d":"Outdoors, three figures stand in a row. At left a figure faces forward holding a shield, with long dark hair and feathers and a fringed outfit; at center another faces forward in a similar fringed outfit with a feathered headpiece. At right a boy stands with arms under a blanket marked with a circular emblem, wrapped warmly. Behind them the sky is a gradient from blue to yellow and green over an open landscape of light grass.","i":"The Nez Perce lived on the Columbia Plateau, in what is now Idaho, Oregon and Washington, and were famed as horse breeders; they also traveled east to hunt buffalo on the Plains. In 1831 a small Nez Perce and Flathead party reached St. Louis, where Catlin painted two of its members, and the visit became linked with the missionary movement in the Northwest. This panel is a later work, dated between 1855 and 1869.","c":"The best passage is the sky, a gradient from cool blue through yellow into green that brings dawn or dusk light into a card that otherwise offers little. The figures are the weak part: the two upright men repeat each other's pose, and the boy swaddled in his blanket is more bundle than person. As an image of Nez Perce dress it is serviceable; as a composition it repeats the formula of the series without adding to it."},"nga:50343":{"d":"On open, grassy terrain, a hunter takes aim with a rifle at a herd of animals, antelope by the title, that look ready to flee. Overhead, soft brushstrokes blend blue and gray in the sky. At the back, trees stand in several greens with touches of red. The palette is harmonious: greens, browns, blues and grays. Unlike most of the series, this one is painted in oil on canvas, about 45 by 63 centimeters.","i":"Pronghorn antelope were among the fastest animals on the Plains, and hunters approached them with patience, sometimes using decoys, because the animal's keen eyesight made it hard to come within range. The rifle shows the period: guns reached the northern Plains through the fur trade, and by the 1830s the Assiniboine used them alongside bows. Catlin painted hunting scenes to show the Plains peoples' skills as well as their dress.","c":"Catlin chooses the right moment, the pause just before the herd bolts, and the sky of soft blue and gray is the most sensitive passage on the canvas. The hunter, though, is a small and stiff presence, and the animals form a pattern more than an observed group. It is a competent hunting scene that lacks the speed and danger of Catlin's best pursuit pictures."},"nga:50344":{"d":"A grassy landscape under a pastel sky holds three figures in a row. The central figure, in a garment with intricate patterns, holds a stick; long hair carries feathers. At left a figure wrapped in a patterned robe has feathers in the hair. At right a figure in a fringed dress with circular patterns has long, flowing hair and a steady gaze. All three wear wrist bands or bracelets. Earth tones fill the ground, and soft hues the sky.","i":"Catlin grouped a chief, a wife and a medicine man as a kind of social portrait of the nation, with political, domestic and spiritual standing side by side. Among the Cheyenne, a central council of forty-four chiefs was a body apart from the warrior societies and the spiritual leaders. The details of dress here are more reliable than any identification, since the sitters are not named.","c":"Soft pastels and long patterned robes give this card a gentler look than the weapon-heavy groups in the series, and the central figure's stick is a useful vertical. As portraiture, though, it is thin: the stares are steady and similar, the bodies lack weight, and the setting is a few brushed greens and grays. The decoration does the work that the drawing should."},"nga:50345":{"d":"Three men stand full length, facing forward, against an earthy ground and a sky turning from blue to light cloud. Two stand in neutral poses while the man at the center has a more dynamic stance. All have long dark hair with feathers, strong cheekbones and body paint, and wear little clothing but necklaces. They carry bows, shields and quivers with arrows.","i":"The Cheyenne were mounted buffalo hunters and fighters of the central Plains, and the arms shown here, the bow, the quiver and the round hide shield, were a warrior's equipment. Shields were made of thick buffalo hide and were believed to carry protective power from the owner's vision, so their painted designs were personal. Catlin's title says only warriors and names no individuals.","c":"The central man's stance, weight shifted and body angled, gives this group a little life, and the arrangement of bows, shields and quivers makes a tidy rhythm of curves across the card. Even so, the card is essentially an arms inventory: the faces are alike, the bodies lightly modeled and the ground empty. Catlin's gift for the telling individual head, often admired in his first portraits, is hardly in play."},"nga:50346":{"d":"A camp of conical tipis fills an open, grassy area, with trees behind and a blue sky lightly clouded. The tipis are made of hide on wooden poles and carry painted symbols and designs. A group of people in front of them is engaged in various activities, and a horizontal pole runs across the foreground before the lodges. The colors are earth tones, and the style is detailed and illustrative.","i":"Among Plains peoples, painted tipi designs were often tied to a dream or vision and belonged to the family that owned them, so a decorated lodge was a mark of distinction. Cheyenne camps were arranged in a great circle at the summer gatherings, with each band in a fixed place, though a view like this shows an ordinary camp. Catlin's attention to the lodge decoration reflects his interest in ethnographic detail.","c":"Catlin's village scenes are valuable mainly for particulars, and the painted lodges here are the particular worth noticing: they are given enough care to look copied from life. The setting is bland, the figures small, and the horizontal pole in front reads as a mere cross-line. The card is more descriptive than evocative, and it rewards a close look more than a long one."},"nga:50347":{"d":"A man stands beside a black horse on open, flat ground under a pale blue sky, with light foliage behind. He has long dark hair with feathers and wears skin trousers and moccasins, with a quiver and bow slung across his back, a necklace and a bracelet. A blanket lies on the horse, which is decorated with colorful cloth and feathers. The pairing is the point; the setting is hardly more than a pale ground.","i":"The horse was the center of Plains warfare and prestige, and a decorated horse was a warrior's pride, with feathers and cloth marking honors and special occasions. Warriors often led their best war horses on the march to keep them fresh, riding lesser animals until a fight was near. Catlin's title treats the scene as a pause, not an action, and the Cheyenne rider is shown with his mount as a partner.","c":"A black horse against a pale sky is a strong simple idea, and it gives the card a weight that many cards in the series lack. The decoration of cloth and feathers is itemized with care, and the quiet of the moment suits the title. What is missing is any sense of a bond between rider and animal; the two stand side by side like exhibits, and the open ground around them is left almost empty."},"nga:50348":{"d":"A textured, hide-like surface within a border carries rows of small painted scenes. At upper left two tipis stand with small figures nearby, some on horseback; to the right, mounted figures talk with a group on foot. Below, figures seem to struggle, some mounted and some not. In the middle two rows of figures march in procession, and at the bottom groups dance. The muted, earthy palette gives the whole an aged look. In effect it is a painted copy of a decorated robe, made on card.","i":"Plains men painted their robes with scenes of their deeds, a form of pictorial autobiography in which horses, weapons and enemies are drawn in a flat, narrative shorthand. The figures are small and schematic by design, since the point was to record an event and not to model a body. That tradition later moved onto paper as ledger drawing. Catlin copied the robe rather than interpreting it, and whose deeds it records is not known.","c":"The interest here is that the original pictures were made by Native artists in a visual language of their own, and Catlin acts as a careful transcriber. The rows of riders, processions and dancers are clear and lively, and the muted color suits the material. But a copy by an outsider cannot carry the quality of the original hide; the panel is best valued as a record pointing toward an art Catlin did not make."},"nga:50349":{"d":"A landscape in an oval frame shows a Native encampment, with conical tipis scattered across a grassy field. A hill slopes gently up toward the horizon, and distant hills fade beneath a tranquil sky that moves from light blue to soft peach. The brushwork is smooth, and the vegetation and sky are realistic but softly idealized. The palette is warm greens and browns on the land. A muted border completes the oval.","i":"Small camps like this were the ordinary unit of Cheyenne life outside the great summer gatherings: a few related families moving with the seasons and the herds. Catlin's title stresses smallness, as if to contrast it with the large camps that dazzled travelers. The word village, like many he used, borrows from European life; Plains peoples lived in mobile camps that could be struck and moved in hours.","c":"The softening of the vegetation and sky gives the oval a mild, pastoral charm, and the gently sloping hill makes a comfortable backdrop for the tipis. It is the kind of picture that sells a place by flattering it, and the peach sky flatters most of all. Nothing in it is wrong and nothing is surprising; its idealization is a veil laid over the observation."},"nga:50350":{"d":"In the foreground a tipi stands with figures in traditional dress gathered around a horse. More figures and animals are scattered across the middle ground, and beyond them trees, hills and mountains rise under a partly cloudy sky. The brushwork is smooth and the details are careful, in tones of green, brown, blue and white. The title tells the viewer that a hunting party is about to leave camp.","i":"The fall hunt was a major event of the Plains year, when men rode out to lay in meat and hides before winter, and its success depended on organization and timing as well as skill. The title gives the figures a family relationship, brothers, and a purpose, which turns an ordinary scene of camp life into a small story. The companion panel, 'Returning from Their Fall Hunt,' completes the narrative.","c":"Departure is a better subject than it looks, because anticipation can give a picture tension, and the large sky and distant mountains do suggest a journey about to begin. What the picture lacks is the buildup: the figures at the tipi are about as animated as figures in any other camp scene, so the story must be supplied by the title. Pleasant and orderly, it is a landscape with a plot attached."},"nga:50351":{"d":"Broken tree trunks and poles lie in the foreground, like the remains of a structure, and beyond them hills carry scattered trees under a cloudy sky. Birds fly among the trees. Several riders move through the grass, gesturing and pointing at something out of view. The brushwork is smooth, the colors earthy, and the soft blue sky balances them. The title says the hunters are coming home, and the picture shows them still in motion.","i":"Hunters returning in autumn brought the meat and hides on which the winter depended, and a successful party was a cause for celebration in camp. The pointing riders suggest a sighting or a signal, of the camp, of game or of strangers, though the picture does not say. The ruined poles in the foreground, perhaps a drying rack or an abandoned lodge, add a note of time passed that fits a return after absence.","c":"The foreground wreckage is the picture's most interesting choice. It breaks the usual clean grass and gives the scene a mood of time passed that Catlin's tidier camp scenes lack, and the pointing figures bring some action to a series that is often static. The execution is still smooth and generalized, though, and the signs of ruin are never made to mean anything. A good idea half developed."},"nga:50352":{"d":"A group portrait of four Kiowa people, one of the small oils on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters and mounted on paperboard, that Catlin painted in the 1860s for his second gallery of Native portraits. In this format he usually stood full-length figures in a shallow space against a pale landscape and set down their costume and equipment with care, so the card presumably offers four standing sitters, with dress and arms as the chief matter.","i":"The Kiowa were mounted hunters and raiders of the southern Plains, living in what is now Oklahoma and Texas, and Catlin met them in 1834 when he accompanied a U.S. Dragoon expedition sent to open talks with the Comanche and their neighbors. The Kiowa kept pictorial calendars, marking each summer and winter with a small picture on hide, so they were themselves makers of images. Beyond the title, the panel names no one.","c":"Catlin's group cards of this kind rely on dress and equipment more than on faces, and they are better used as records of how a people presented itself to an outsider than as paintings in their own right. Taken as pictures they are carefully competent, rarely more, and the shallow space and quiet palette keep them in a low register that rewards study more than first impressions."},"nga:50353":{"d":"A neutral ground with hints of green backs a row of four people. At far left a figure wears a knee-length skirt and necklaces. Next comes a figure with long dark hair and arm and neck bands, holding a weapon. The third has feathers in the hair, a tasseled and beaded outfit and a club. The fourth, with long dark hair and a feather, carries a bow and arrow and a shield. All are barefoot and wear fringed garments.","i":"Catlin's title, a chief with his wife and two warriors, sets out a small household of standing, and the club, bow, shield and beaded tassels are visible marks of a Kiowa man's status. In the 1830s the Kiowa were at the height of their power on the southern Plains, allied with the Comanche after a long migration south from the northern Plains. The sitters are not named, so the picture describes a type of leadership and not a particular family.","c":"The row of four reads clearly, and the differing weapons give each figure a distinct silhouette, which is the picture's chief strength. The neutral ground lets the beadwork and fringes carry what color there is. But the sameness of stance and the lack of any shared action or glance make the group a lineup rather than a family, and the best that can be said is that it is orderly."},"nga:50354":{"d":"From a raised viewpoint, a sunlit pastoral landscape holds people at various tasks. Tall trees, built from textured brushstrokes, stand at the center, and a line of fainter trees leads the eye toward the horizon, where a calm, clear sky of soft blues and tinted clouds begins. The palette is warm and earthy: greens, browns and yellows. It is a gentle subject of gathering and not of war.","i":"Wild grapes grew along the creeks and timbered bottoms of the southern Plains, and gathering them in late summer was part of the seasonal round of work that supplemented the hunt, usually done by women and children. The title makes the gatherers Kiowa, a people Catlin met in 1834, and the subject shows a side of Plains life far removed from the war and ceremony that his audiences expected.","c":"The tall central trees give the picture a firm vertical and a lively surface, and the receding line of paler trees makes a convincing distance. As a landscape it is handsome. As an account of grape gathering it is less satisfying, since the activity is small and the people are lost in the scenery. Catlin is more at ease with trees than with the tasks going on beneath them."},"nga:50356":{"d":"Four men are shown full length on a grassy plain beneath a light blue sky. Three sit side by side on animal hides or garments, while a fourth stands slightly to the left holding a bow. All have dark hair adorned with feathers, patterned and fringed clothing, and body paint; one wears a sash across his chest. Two white horses stand behind them, and the group has a calm, formal presence.","i":"By the 1830s the Comanche were the dominant power of the southern Plains, with a reputation as the finest horsemen in North America, and horses were the measure of wealth and status. The white horses behind the group are a sign of this. Catlin met the Comanche in 1834 on the Dragoon expedition from Fort Gibson and wrote of them with admiration for their horsemanship.","c":"The two white horses are the unexpected asset: they stand pale against the pale sky and give the seated men a calm backdrop, a rare compositional touch in a series fond of empty grounds. The arrangement of three seated and one standing is also more varied than the usual line. Facial detail remains slight, however, and the picture documents its subjects' finery more than it reveals them."},"nga:50357":{"d":"Five figures stand in front of a decorated tipi in a grassy area under a sky of soft clouds. An adult woman is at the center, flanked by two young boys, with another child partly hidden behind her. At right a small child interacts with a dog. The picture is a family scene more than a portrait row, and the tipi, with its painted decoration, fills the background.","i":"The chief's family is set before the family lodge, which Catlin calls a wigwam, a word from the eastern woodlands that was often applied loosely to Plains tipis in his day. The woman is probably the children's mother, though the title does not say. Dogs were household animals and, before the horse arrived, the Plains peoples' principal beasts of burden, pulling loads on a travois.","c":"The small child and the dog are the freshest notes: a gesture of play that Catlin does not set up so much as allow, and it lifts the picture out of the catalog mode. Elsewhere the card is conventional, with the central woman and two boys posed for the viewer and the tipi doing duty as a backdrop. It is warmer than most of the series, and the warmth comes from the margin."},"nga:50358":{"d":"A group of riders moves in a lively scene across a grassy plain. In the distance a line of trees meets the sky, and tipis nestle among the trees. The brushwork is detailed, with fine lines defining the horses and riders, and the sky is blue and gray with clouds. The ground is green and yellow. The movement of the horsemen is the subject.","i":"A sham fight was a mock battle, a display of skill and a drill for real combat, and Catlin saw Comanche riders demonstrate such feats in 1834. He wrote that a rider could drop to the side of a galloping horse and shoot from beneath its neck, using the animal as a shield, and he thought the Comanche among the finest horsemen he had seen. The picture shows riders in motion, not the details of any one trick.","c":"Horsemen in motion are always a test, and Catlin passes it only modestly. The fine drawing of the horses and riders sets a rhythm across the plain, and the line of trees with tipis gives the eye a place to rest. But the figures are small and evenly spaced, so the sense of speed is described more than felt, and the sky, usually the best passage in these cards, is a standard blue and gray. A tidy mock battle."},"nga:50359":{"d":"Seen from above in a panoramic view, a group of riders crosses a landscape with hills in the distance. The horizon sits a third of the way down from the top, dividing land from sky. Detailed brushstrokes capture the motion of horses and riders, and the palette is earthy with bright accents. The scene conveys movement and interaction between the riders and their surroundings.","i":"Comanche riders were famous in their time for tricks: hanging from one side of a galloping horse by a heel and a hand to shoot arrows from beneath its neck, or picking objects from the ground at speed. Such skills were not only performance but training for war, where a rider using his mount as cover could approach an enemy and loose arrows with little exposure. Catlin's title names the skill and leaves the details to the imagination.","c":"The raised viewpoint is the right choice, since it lets the whole field of action open out, and the horizon at one-third gives the sky a balanced share. The riders themselves are too small to show the skill the title promises: the astonishing tricks Catlin described in words are only suggested in paint. It is an adequate picture of a spectacular subject, and the gap between text and image is instructive."},"nga:50360":{"d":"A figure lies on the grass reaching out toward an object, while others on horses gallop across a grassy expanse. Behind them are trees and a sky in soft colors. The riders wear cloaks of various colors, and the palette is natural: greens, browns and the bright patches of the cloaks. It is a small oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters, and shows a skirmish of mounted men.","i":"The Jicarilla Apache, from the mountains and plains of northern New Mexico and southern Colorado, and the Comanche, who dominated the plains to the east and south, were long-time rivals in a region of raiding and shifting alliances. There is no sign that Catlin saw such a fight himself, and his source for the scene is unknown. The fallen figure reaching out is the narrative center, but what he reaches for is unclear.","c":"The fallen man, reaching toward something the viewer cannot name, is a small mystery that gives the card more narrative interest than most of the series, and the colored cloaks of the riders enliven the grass. The gallop itself is unconvincing, a procession of similar poses across the field, and the drawing of the fight is too tentative to carry the violence the title promises. Battle is not what Catlin does best."},"nga:50361":{"d":"A wide view over a grassy plain with a high horizon shows groups of figures and horses in the distance. Close at hand, numerous people, most on horseback, are busy in what looks like coordinated action. They wear traditional clothing, and their purposeful movement could be read as a hunt, though the title gives it as a camp on the move. The brushwork is fine and detailed, in greens, browns, reds and earth tones, under soft blues and grays.","i":"A Comanche band changed camp often, following the buffalo and the grass, and a move involved the whole community: lodges struck, poles and goods lashed to horses or dragged on travois, children and the old carried along. The sight of a whole band on the march was one of the spectacles of the Plains, and it offered a painter a way to show a people's wealth in horses and equipment at a glance.","c":"The high horizon and wide plain give the scene a real sense of space, and the clustered riders in the foreground have an energy the series seldom manages. What is lost is clarity: the activity is hard to read, the figures blend into the grass, and it takes the title to tell the viewer that this is a camp on the move. As spectacle it works; as record it is vague."},"nga:50362":{"d":"An elevated view overlooks a vast landscape, with the horizon in the upper third and trees fading into the distance. In the foreground a figure on horseback gallops, while other riders are scattered through the meadow. The brushwork is smooth and soft, the colors earthy greens, browns and muted reds. The sky shifts from blues to pinkish tones. A defile, in the military sense, is a column moving in file, and the picture spreads such a column across a wide meadow.","i":"'Defile' is a military word for troops marching in a narrow column, and Catlin applies it to a Comanche war party to suggest an orderly advance. Comanche war parties from the southern Plains ranged far into Texas and Mexico for horses, captives and plunder, and were feared across a wide region; the scene's quiet pastoral light gives no hint of this.","c":"There is a pleasing breadth to the view, and the foreground rider gives it a sense of scale and speed. But the high horizon and scattered riders turn the war party into a dispersed pattern in a meadow, with little sense of the purposeful column the title describes. The soft handling makes it a landscape with riders, and a rather calm one, for a subject that people of the time found alarming."},"nga:50363":{"d":"Viewed from slightly above, the scene places the horizon in the upper third, where a band of trees meets the sky, with tipis nestled at its base. The sky blends blue and peach with delicate strokes. In the foreground, figures stand near brown horses on open grassland, with greens for the grass and trees and touches of red in the grass. The colors are warm throughout.","i":"Tawahquena is usually taken to be Catlin's spelling of Tawakoni, a Wichita-speaking people of what is now Texas and Oklahoma. Wichita-speaking peoples were farmers as well as buffalo hunters, living in settled villages, which set them apart from the wholly nomadic Comanche and Kiowa who surrounded them. Catlin met Wichita groups in 1834, during the Dragoon expedition, and this view was painted long afterward.","c":"The peach and blue sky, delicately brushed, is the best part of the picture, and the high horizon allows the tipis and horses to be arranged in comfortable bands across the field. The touches of red in the grass are a pleasing, slightly unexpected accent. As often with Catlin's village scenes, though, the people are scarcely individuals, and the place is more a set of well-spaced elements than a settlement lived in."},"nga:50364":{"d":"Four people stand side by side, upright and facing forward, within a tan frame. The two figures on the left have long dark hair; the two on the right have shorter hair adorned with feathers. They wear garments of animal hide with fur trim, some with decorative patterns, and all four wear necklaces, one also with armbands. One figure holds a long decorated object that resembles a pipe. The background is a blend of soft earth tones. The card, about 42 by 57 centimeters, is smaller than most in the series.","i":"Pawnee Pict was a name used in Catlin's day for the Wichita, a people of the southern Plains who lived in villages and farmed. Catlin visited their country in 1834 during the Dragoon expedition. The long decorated object that resembles a pipe would suit a chief, since smoking a pipe sealed agreements and welcomes. Daughters in a chief's portrait present his household as well as his rank.","c":"The pipe is the card's most telling prop, and the family grouping, a father with his daughters and a warrior, has an easy formality. At this smaller size the figures feel compact, and the fur trim and patterns of the garments are neatly rendered. Yet the faces remain generalized, the background nearly absent and the sitters stiff. It is a restrained, documentary little panel."},"nga:50366":{"d":"Three men stand in a natural setting of trees and plants in soft earth tones, framed in an oval against a greenish-gray rectangle. At left a man draws back an arrow in his bow, aiming; he has short hair with feathers and wears a light blue loincloth with a sash and bracelets. The central figure stands with legs apart, holding a spear upright and looking to his left. The man on the right, relaxed, holds what looks like a whip or rope. Both wear feathers, necklaces and reddish-brown loincloths.","i":"The Shoshone lived west of the continental divide and on the western Plains, in what is now Wyoming, Idaho, Utah and Nevada; some bands became mounted buffalo hunters while others remained on foot in the Great Basin. The whip or rope held by the man on the right may point to the horse, which transformed Shoshone life in the 1700s. How Catlin came to paint Shoshone sitters is unknown.","c":"The most dynamic figure here is the archer at left: drawing an arrow gives the card a gesture of intention that most standing groups in the series lack. The oval frame and the greenish-gray mount make it a neat, small package. But the three figures do not interact, the central pose is a stock stance, and the soft earth-toned scenery is a placeholder."},"nga:50367":{"d":"Two figures stand upright on grass beneath a clear sky with scattered clouds. The person on the left has reddish hair styled in a mohawk, facial markings, a necklace and earrings, a light-colored cloak and fringed pants, and holds an axe with a decorative handle. The person on the right has dark hair with feathers, facial markings, a large necklace and a detailed garment, and holds a staff. The pairing is plain and symmetrical, and the setting is kept simple.","i":"The title records that neither the nation nor the names of the sitters are known. The left figure's red crest of hair is probably a roach, a headdress of dyed deer hair worn by warriors of several prairie and woodland nations, among them the Pawnee and Osage, and the decorated axe is both a weapon and an object of display. These details narrow the possibilities but do not settle them.","c":"Anonymity is an odd fit for Catlin, whose project depended on names, and the picture shows what is lost when the label drops away: the pair becomes a study in costume and attitude. The roach and the decorated axe make strong shapes, and the paired poses, one with a weapon and one with a staff, are neatly balanced. As portraiture, though, it has little to offer, since no personality is on view."},"nga:50368":{"d":"Six people face the viewer in an oval vignette. At left a woman sits on the ground with a child behind her and a younger one in her lap, in a decorated carrier with a square handle. At right three men sit or stand with bows, arrows and an axe. Their heads carry crests of long, thin red fibers or feathers radiating from the back, and all wear layered necklaces, silver bracelets and earrings. Garments are painted with horsemen or geometric patterns. The seated man has red handprints on his arms, a bull's head outlined on his chest and face, and a ring in his nose.","i":"The red crests are almost certainly roaches, the dyed deer-hair headdresses that marked Pawnee men, who shaved their heads except for a scalp lock. Painted robes with horsemen and the red handprint were signs of war honors in Plains thought, a handprint often meaning hand-to-hand combat, though meanings varied by nation and man. The Pawnee lived in earth-lodge villages along the Platte and Loup rivers, farming corn and hunting buffalo each season.","c":"This card is rich in detail: tattoos, handprints, the bull's head, rings, silver and painted robes with riders, all described with a patience that makes it a valuable record. As a picture it is overstuffed and a little blank, with everyone facing front and no one looking at anyone. The woman with her child in the carrier brings the one note of tenderness. It rewards the eye more than the heart."},"nga:50369":{"d":"Inside a narrow pale green border, three men stand full length on a grassy patch against a light ground. The man on the left holds a staff and wears a feathered headdress and a robe with intricate designs. The central figure, with a red crest of hair, wears a robe fastened with a fur shawl and also holds a staff. The man on the right carries a shield and a cylindrical object on his back. The sheet is a little taller than most in the series, about 50 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The central figure's red crest is a roach, the dyed deer-hair headdress worn by Pawnee men over a shaved head, and the staffs may be signs of authority or ceremonial wands. The cylindrical object on the right-hand figure's back could be a quiver or a case. The Pawnee were long-standing enemies of the Sioux and Cheyenne, but Catlin's title offers no names and no occasion.","c":"The picture rests on three nicely varied silhouettes: the staffs, the shield and the roach each add a different vertical or curve. The robe designs are described with care, though the faces carry little character and the pale ground leaves the figures floating. It is dutiful, balanced and a little dry, an inventory of dress that suggests the formal presence of Pawnee leaders without conveying it."},"nga:50370":{"d":"A stretched hide fills the card, its edges curling and uneven, painted with stylized figures and scenes. The figures wear traditional attire and ride adorned horses, a bear appears among them, and geometric designs fill the gaps. The background is neutral, which sets off the detail of the paint. The effect is of an old hide treated as an object, with its own wear. It is a painted copy of the robe, about 47 by 61 centimeters.","i":"The title's wording, 'Fantastic Professional Designs,' is Catlin's: 'professional' refers to the doctor's calling, and 'fantastic' shows how strange such imagery looked to an outsider. Plains robes of this kind could record visions as well as deeds, and the bear is associated with healing power in many Plains traditions. The flat, stylized figures belong to the pictographic art that later flowered in ledger drawing.","c":"The curling edges give this copy a physical presence that flatter facsimiles lack: the hide has been painted as an object with a history. The pictures on it, stylized horses and a bear in bright flat color, are arguably more vivid than much of the figure drawing in Catlin's own manner. It is a quiet acknowledgement that Native artists had a visual language of their own."},"nga:50371":{"d":"A leather-like backdrop within a border carries painted scenes of horses and buffalo. The central area holds intricate drawings of mounted warriors and animals; riders on decorated horses suggest a narrative. Below, wolves and a buffalo stand near a plant or bush. The surface resembles an animal hide, and the style recalls traditional Native painting, with its flat figures and its emphasis on the relation of people, animals and their surroundings. The card is a painted copy of a decorated robe.","i":"'Doctor' was Catlin's word for a medicine man, a healer and keeper of spiritual power. A doctor's robe with horses, wolves and buffalo may link its wearer to the animals from whom he drew strength, and Pawnee doctors belonged to societies that held ritual knowledge. Such pictures were not simply war honors. Catlin copied the robe, and the meaning of its figures is not explained.","c":"As a document it is of interest chiefly because it preserves an image of Pawnee picture-writing on its native material, with a texture and curl of hide that the painter has reproduced convincingly. The animals are lively and the arrangement clear. What Catlin cannot supply is the meaning of the signs or the presence of the maker; the copy is careful and mute."},"nga:50372":{"d":"An oval painting, separated from the plain card around it, shows a natural landscape at a level view, with a low horizon where grassy plains meet a line of trees. Distant buffalo graze to the left, and two rest in the grass near the center; a figure half hidden by foliage stands at the right. The sky shades from yellow to blue, the colors are earthy, and the strokes are smooth and blended, giving a serene effect.","i":"Approaching buffalo on foot, with the animals unaware, was a technique of the hunt: hunters crept forward under cover, sometimes in hide or wolf skins, and used the wind to get close enough for a bow. The Pawnee, who lived in permanent earth-lodge villages, left each summer and winter on organized hunts under strict leadership. Catlin's title uses the present participle to hold the viewer at the moment before the chase.","c":"The quiet is the point, and it works: low horizon, resting buffalo, a hunter barely seen. The stillness gives the viewer a share in the hunter's patience, and the gradient sky is gracefully done. Nothing happens, which suits the title, but the figure is so concealed that the human interest is nearly nil, and the oval vignette lends the whole a decorative, almost wallpaper gentleness."},"nga:50373":{"d":"On a hill, tipis stand against a sky glowing with the colors of sunset, and in the foreground a group of people sit around a campfire while animals graze nearby. A few trees stand in the scene. The palette combines earth tones with the warm hues of the sky, giving the scene a peaceful, rural feel. It is a view of an encampment as the day ends.","i":"The Pawnee lived in permanent earth-lodge villages, planting corn, beans and squash, and left them for months each year to hunt buffalo, living in portable tipis. A camp at sunset would be on one of these hunts. The campfire gathering was the social heart of such a camp, where news was exchanged and stories told. Catlin's title chooses an hour that suited the picturesque.","c":"The glowing sky and the campfire make a convincing evening scene, and the small group around the fire is a believable human center. The treatment is still generic, with tipis on a hill as tidy as a toy village and the sky doing most of the expressive work. It is atmospheric and gentle, and it is not a close observation of Pawnee life."},"nga:50374":{"d":"A wide expanse of land stretches under a light blue sky with occasional white clouds, its horizon marked by rolling hills or small mountains and sparse trees. In the foreground a man in traditional dress pulls on a rope tied to a resisting horse. Several horses gallop in the distance, and another stands alone at the right. The brushwork is detailed, with attention to textures, and the colors are earthy.","i":"Wild horses, descended from Spanish stock, ranged the southern Plains in large herds, and catching them was a skilled and risky business: a mounted man might rope a horse with a lariat, or herds were driven into enclosures. Catlin wrote with interest about the wild horses of the southern prairies and the methods used to catch them. The Pawnee also obtained horses by raiding and trade.","c":"The rope is the picture's drama: a taut line between man and animal gives a diagonal that the otherwise placid composition badly needs. The galloping herd in the distance supplies scale, and the clouds are lightly handled. But the struggle is described rather than rendered, with the pulling man and balky horse in static poses, and it lacks the force a more practiced painter of horses would bring to the subject."},"nga:50375":{"d":"A warrior in traditional attire holds a spear and shield and faces a rearing horse. The horse is shown with detailed muscular features and an ornate saddle blanket, and the background is kept minimal so that the pairing stands out. The composition stresses movement and the traditional elements of dress and equipment. It is a small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The title names a custom, the offering of a prized horse to the spirits, and it must be taken as Catlin's account of a Pawnee practice, not as an eyewitness record. The giving up of a valued horse figures in the religion of several Plains peoples, at funerals, in vows and in thanks, since sacrifice meant parting with something dear. The rearing horse and the raised spear show the moment before the act.","c":"The rearing horse gives the card a surge of energy, and the plain ground concentrates attention on the two figures, which suits a subject of such weight. The horse's muscles are described carefully, though in the stylized way that Catlin's animals tend to have, and the plain treatment leaves the emotional pitch low for a moment that must have been charged. It is an effective composition that under-plays its drama."},"nga:50376":{"d":"Three men stand full length outdoors against a pale ground of sky and grass, within an oval framed by a parchment border. The man on the left wears a fringed and beaded robe and holds a long spear decorated with feathers; his headpiece has upright feathers and a quiver hangs from his shoulder. The central figure wears a long beaded tunic and a bold necklace and holds an axe. The man on the right, in a red shirt with intricate design and fringed pants, holds another axe. Both wear feathered headpieces.","i":"The Osage, who call themselves Wahzhazhe, were a Siouan people of the Missouri and Arkansas river country who had been pushed west into what is now Kansas and Oklahoma. Catlin remarked on the height and bearing of Osage men, whom he met in 1834 near Fort Gibson, and he painted several. The feathered headpieces here are probably roaches, worn above a shaved head with a scalp lock.","c":"This is a handsome card, thanks to the bold red shirt and the beaded tunic against a pale ground, and the sitters' confident bearing. The echo of the two axes is a neat device. The weakness is the one shared by the whole series: the three heads are interchangeable beneath their feathers, and the parchment border seems to promise a sophistication of portraiture that the picture does not deliver."},"nga:50377":{"d":"A family-like group sits and stands in a row in front of a faintly painted landscape within an oval frame. At left a woman sits on a tree stump with an unclothed child in her lap, her long hair loose and her wrap patterned with flowers; the baby reaches for her necklaces. Three men follow, one seated cross-legged and two standing, with shaved heads, feathered headdresses, collars and necklaces, and red marks painted on head, chest and arms. One holds a spear, one a bow and arrows, and the seated man cradles a long wooden object.","i":"Osage men shaved their heads except for a roach or scalp lock and painted head and body, and necklaces and collars signaled wealth and status. The group's family cast, a woman and baby beside three men, presents the Osage as a community and not only as warriors. The baby's grasp at the necklaces is a rare unposed gesture in a series of formal poses.","c":"The baby grasping at the necklaces is the card's best invention, a moment of apparent spontaneity amid composed poses, and the pale green and watery sky give the whole a pleasant freshness. The sitters' poses are otherwise the usual row, and the oval frame on its shell-pink card has the air of a keepsake. It is one of Catlin's more domestic groups, and the domestic is where it does best."},"nga:50378":{"d":"The card shows a pictographic composition on a tanned-hide ground, bordered in soft greenish gray. The upper half is filled with small horizontal red lines in rows. Below them are symbols and geometric patterns, including rows of triangles and concentric circles. In the lower half stand two ornate tipis with long poles crossing at their tops, in bright orange and red designs, and a large buffalo stands between and behind them. It is a painted copy, on card, of a robe.","i":"The Omaha were a Siouan-speaking people of the Missouri River valley in what is now Nebraska, farmers in earth-lodge villages who hunted buffalo on the plains. The two painted tipis and the buffalo are the clearest elements of the design; the rows of red lines and geometric motifs may be tallies or symbols, but their meaning is not given and cannot be read from a copy. Catlin took an interest in such robes as records of individual lives.","c":"The composition is clear and elegantly balanced, the dense rows of red lines above setting off the open, more figurative lower half where the tipis and buffalo stand. As a design it is more satisfying than many of Catlin's own compositions, which says something about the strength of the model. His part is faithful copying, and the picture's value lies there."},"nga:50379":{"d":"Two riders move from left to right across an open landscape. The horizon divides grassy plains from distant hills, and the sky is a gentle blue streaked with cloud. The brushwork is detailed and conveys motion, and the palette sets warm earth tones for the riders, their horses and the nearby ground against softer greens and yellows farther off. The pursuit is the entire subject.","i":"The Osage and the Comanche were frequent enemies, and the Dragoon expedition of 1834, which Catlin joined, aimed in part to bring the nations of the southern Plains to peace with one another and with the United States. The painter therefore knew how recently these peoples had fought. The picture shows a chase and gives no hint of its outcome or of who the riders are.","c":"Two riders in profile make a clean, readable frieze, and the sense of forward movement is helped by the left-to-right direction and the open plain. The sky is lightly done and the colors well chosen. What the picture cannot do is create threat: pursuer and pursued are the same size, at the same speed and equally calm, so the chase is more diagram than action."},"nga:50380":{"d":"Three figures stand against a background of subtle blues and earth tones. In the center a man in a ceremonial headdress and a decorated tunic holds a tall spear. To his right a woman holds a large piece of hide, and at left a younger person crouches, adjusting gear. The standing man dominates the group. It is a small card, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"'Favorite wife' is Catlin's phrase, reflecting a society in which prominent men might have more than one wife. The Mandan lived in earth-lodge villages on the upper Missouri and were farmers and traders, and Catlin spent weeks among them in 1832. In 1837 smallpox killed most of the nation, and his Mandan paintings became important records of a people nearly lost. The hide the woman holds recalls the women's labor of dressing buffalo robes.","c":"The picture's best quality is historical pathos, which no amount of competent drawing could add: the Mandan were soon devastated, and Catlin's record stands as one of the few of their appearance. As a card it is quiet and a little stiff, the tall spear making a firm vertical but the faces remaining generic. Its subtle blues and earth tones are tasteful, and the woman with the hide gives a note of work amid the display."},"nga:50381":{"d":"Three men stand outdoors on grass beneath an overcast sky with hints of clouds. The man on the left holds a spear and a round shield, and has painted markings on his face. The middle man holds a shield in his left hand and a bow in his right, with an arrow ready to be fired. The man on the right aims his bow and arrow. All three have long dark hair with feathers, fringed garments, leggings and moccasins, and the shields have patterns and tassels. A green outer border frames the scene.","i":"The Mandan were farmers and traders as well as warriors, and their villages on the Missouri were protected by log palisades and a ditch. The round shields shown here were made from the thick hide of a buffalo's neck, and a warrior's painted designs were usually believed to give protection. The title, 'Armed for War,' is Catlin's and stresses equipment over any particular event.","c":"Two of the three men are drawing or aiming bows, which gives this card a rare directed energy: the arrows point the same way, and the eye follows. The patterned shields add pleasing circles to a composition otherwise made of verticals, and the overcast sky keeps the tone serious. Yet the faces are blank and the archers' poses mannered, more a drill diagram than a moment in a fight."},"nga:50382":{"d":"A group stands together outdoors on a grassy landscape beneath a blue sky. Three adults stand and a fourth sits on the ground; a child at left faces the group holding a bow and arrows. All have long hair, the adults' adorned with feathers. One standing man wears a decorative fringed tunic and holds a shield painted with designs; another is draped in a fur cape; the seated figure wears fringed pants and has body paint on the arms.","i":"The Mandan, whose earth-lodge villages stood on the upper Missouri, were known to Catlin as hospitable, ceremonial and dignified, and he spent several weeks among them in 1832, a stay that produced his best-known Mandan paintings and, much later, his book on the O-kee-pa ceremony. Painted shields and fur capes were signs of a warrior's standing; the child holding a bow reflects the early training of boys.","c":"The mixture of heights, from standing men to a seated adult to a child at the edge, gives this group more variety than most, and the painted shield, fringed tunic and fur cape make a satisfying contrast of surfaces. As in so many of Catlin's group cards, however, the figures do not engage each other and the faces lack distinction. It is a cataloger's picture with a few good touches."},"nga:50383":{"d":"Two adults and an infant stand in a softly shaded oval set within a rectangular border. The man at the right faces forward in an elaborate feathered headdress, his long dark hair falling over his shoulders and his decorated outfit fringed to the ground; in his left hand he holds a long, feather-trimmed implement. At left stands a woman, turned slightly to the right, wrapped in a patterned blanket and holding a small infant whose face is barely detailed. Behind them are grass, a slightly cloudy sky and a domed lodge with an entrance.","i":"A civil chief governed in peacetime, settling disputes and speaking for the village, as distinct from the war chief who led in battle, and Mandan towns recognized both roles. The domed earth lodge behind the family is the Mandan home: a timber frame covered in earth, entered through a covered passage. Catlin's title sets the chief within a household, a reminder that he painted families as well as warriors.","c":"The domed lodge is a welcome detail, since it places the family in a particular culture and not just a pale landscape, and the headdress and fringed outfit are drawn with care. The infant's blank face and the woman's sketchy features are typical weaknesses, however, and the oval vignette softens everything toward sentiment. The picture shows the Mandan as Catlin wished to remember them, dignified and domestic."},"nga:50384":{"d":"Four people sit or recline on the ground in a pastel, cloudy setting with sandy terrain, a shield and a spear decorated with feathers and fur standing upright in the earth behind them. At left an older man leans back, his face patterned with markings and his long hair feathered; he wears a fur-trimmed shawl and leggings decorated with quillwork, and holds a long feathered pipe. At the center a younger man sits with a child on his lap, necklaces around his neck. At right a woman reclines on one arm in a simple garment.","i":"Minatarree is Catlin's spelling of Hidatsa, a people who lived in earth-lodge villages beside the Mandan on the Knife River in what is now North Dakota, farming corn and hunting buffalo. The family arrangement, an old man, a younger man with a child, and a woman, suggests generations at rest, with the old man's pipe as a sign of his place in council. The shield and spear planted behind them evoke a warrior's past.","c":"Catlin lets his figures sit, and the relaxed, reclining arrangement is a welcome change from the standing lineup; the group has a believable ease, and the pastel cloud sky is delicately done. The quillwork leggings and the planted weapons give the foreground interest. The faces are still more suggested than drawn, and the child on the lap is almost a prop, but as a portrait of old age at rest it is gentler than most."},"nga:50386":{"d":"A man and a woman stand side by side, full length, in front of a row of dome-shaped dwellings with faint figures or bundles before them. The man at left has long hair, a feathered headdress, a mustache and a stern expression; he wears clothing decorated with colorful beadwork and fringes, and holds a pipe and a large ceremonial shield trimmed with feathers. The woman, with long dark hair, wears a beaded dress fringed at the hem and several strands of necklaces, her hands clasped. The sky is light blue with a few clouds.","i":"Riccarree is Catlin's spelling of Arikara, called Ree by traders, a Caddoan-speaking people who farmed and lived in earth-lodge villages on the Missouri River in what is now South Dakota. The dome-shaped dwellings behind the pair are probably those earth lodges. Catlin met the Arikara during his 1832 voyage up the Missouri; the pipe and the feathered shield mark the man as a leader and a warrior.","c":"The dome-shaped lodges behind them, with their ghostly figures, are the picture's best idea: the setting is specific, and the muted sky lets the couple's beadwork stand out. Of the two sitters, the man has more presence, since the stern face and mustache suggest an individual and not a type. The woman is more a pose than a person. It is a decent portrait pair, undone only by thin handling of the dress."},"nga:50387":{"d":"A wide river fills the picture, seen from a slightly raised spot on a riverbank whose edge is sharply eroded. The horizon lies about a third of the way down, where the water meets the land far off and a faint outline of a structure stands on the far side. Closer in, people fish or sit among patches of grass on the bank. The brushwork is smooth, the clouds detailed and the river flowing. Muted greens, earth tones and delicate blues give a calm, serene air.","i":"The Mandan built their earth-lodge villages on high banks of the Missouri, and Catlin painted the village of Mih-tutta-hankush more than once during his stay in 1832. A view across the river at a distance shows the setting as a river traveler would have met it, with the Missouri as the highway of trade and the village as a landmark. Catlin's steamboat voyage of that year carried him to this stretch of the river.","c":"The horizon at one-third and the wide, flowing river give the picture a spacious calm, and the delicate clouds and eroded bank are observed with some feeling. The village, though, is the subject of the title, and it is nearly lost in the distance; the people who fish are small and anonymous. It is a handsome river view that keeps its subject at arm's length."},"nga:50388":{"d":"Six people sit inside a conical lodge. At the center a figure in a feathered headdress and traditional dress presides, with others seated around, dressed more modestly. A fire burns with a cooking pot over it, wooden posts hold up the roof, animal skins cover the ground, and more people sit against the wall. Light filters in from an opening in the roof, falling into the dim interior. Interiors are rarer in Catlin's work than open-air scenes.","i":"In his Letters Catlin tells of being feasted by Mandan leaders during his stay in 1832, with a meal served in the host's lodge and the ritual of the pipe. Hospitality was a formal matter among the Mandan, and a guest's acceptance was a form of alliance. The title puts the painter himself among the figures, an unusual self-inclusion, and the chief is not named.","c":"A rare interior, and the best part is the light: a shaft from the smoke hole falling into the dim lodge gives the card a real sense of enclosure and a modest drama. The figures around the fire are more convincingly grouped than usual, as people sharing a meal and not a lineup. The handling is thin and the faces blank, but the idea of the painter writing himself into the scene, a guest among hosts, is disarming."},"nga:50391":{"d":"A game scene of the Mandan, built around bow and arrows, with trees in the setting. Catlin painted such contests as lively companions to his portraits and village views, usually with small figures spread across an open space and a few onlookers at the edges. The card is a small oil on card, about 48 by 64 centimeters, and its subject is a competition of archery skill carried out in the open air.","i":"Games were an important part of Mandan life, and Catlin described several, among them contests in which men tested their skill with the bow. The Mandan lived by farming and hunting, and archery was a practical skill raised into sport, with stakes of reputation and sometimes of goods. The title gives only the name of the game, and its rules are not known.","c":"Catlin's game scenes tend to be livelier than his standing groups, since a contest gives him figures in motion in place of a lined-up pose. They are still drawn at a distance, in his smooth, small-figure manner, and seldom match the vividness of the games he described in words. The subject is valuable for its ordinariness, a reminder that Plains life had leisure as well as war."},"nga:50392":{"d":"A circle of Native men sits gathered around a central point, probably a small fire. Behind them rolling hills and trees recede under a sky with clouds. The brushstrokes are smooth and the colors earthy, touched with blue. Shields and spears are placed around the group, standing or lying near the men. The scene is a council and not an action, and the ring of figures sets its own quiet composition.","i":"A foot war party set out on foot, without horses, a mode of raiding used by the Mandan and many of their neighbors on the upper Missouri, and before leaving the leader called a council to smoke, plan and seek the favor of the spirits. The shields and spears set around the circle are the party's equipment, and their placement suggests an orderly meeting and not a hasty one.","c":"The circle is a simple, effective device, and it gives the card a sense of shared purpose that the lineups lack. The figures are small and alike, though, and the weapons set around the ring tell more of the story than the faces do. It is a quiet card whose value lies in the scene it records, a council of equals before a risk."},"nga:50393":{"d":"A figure in a feathered headdress and patterned garments stands in the foreground, holding ceremonial objects, as if addressing or leading an assembly. Around him people sit and stand in varied postures on a grassy field, listening or watching attentively. Hills encircle the scene beneath a sky with scattered clouds, and in the background is a structure that resembles an earthen or wooden lodge. The colors are muted, the mood peaceful, and the picture is set within an oval border.","i":"To Catlin 'medicine' meant mystery, and a medicine man was a person with access to it: healer, ritual leader and counselor. Among the Mandan, men with such powers led ceremonies, including those for calling the buffalo and for curing the sick. The crowd here seems attentive and calm, and the picture suggests authority exercised by speech and not by spectacle. The lodge behind them is a reminder that this was a settled village society.","c":"The composition has a clear center, the standing figure, and the ring of listeners makes a natural frame, which is more than many of the cards manage. Because the audience reacts, the picture suggests relationships and not just costume. But the faces are small and the gestures slight, and the oval's softness mutes whatever force the occasion had. It is a gentle sermon in paint."},"nga:50394":{"d":"The title identifies the picture as a facsimile, an exact painted copy, of the robe of Mah-to-toh-pa, a Mandan chief. In Catlin's other robe facsimiles the hide is shown flat and frontal, its surface covered with small painted scenes of horsemen and combat, and this one presumably follows that pattern. It is a small painting on card, about 45 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Mah-to-toh-pa, whose name means Four Bears, was a Mandan second chief and a celebrated warrior, and Catlin painted his portrait in 1832. Four Bears died in the smallpox epidemic of 1837, which killed most of his people. A painted copy of his robe preserves the kind of pictorial record of battle honors that Mandan men wore, a particular man's deeds in his own visual language, from a world that was ending.","c":"A copy of a robe is a modest sort of picture, but this one has a human face behind it, and Catlin was right to want the record: Four Bears, whom Catlin and Karl Bodmer both painted in the early 1830s, was dead within a few years. The painter's task is transcription, and his skill lies in fidelity, not invention. As art it rests on the original; as testimony it counts."},"nga:50395":{"d":"A Mandan ceremony, named in the title with a phrase that probably echoes the Mandan story of a great flood whose waters sank again. Catlin's pictures of ceremonies usually set the participants in a ring or in ranks within a wide landscape, seen from a raised viewpoint and painted with small, smoothly handled figures, and this small oil on card, about 47 by 64 centimeters, probably follows that pattern.","i":"The O-kee-pa was the great annual Mandan ceremony, and Catlin, who watched it in 1832, published a full account in 1867, with illustrations. He tied it to the Mandan memory of a great flood, which the ritual commemorated, and he reported that the ceremony included severe tests of endurance by young men. His account was contested in his day but later supported by other observers. The picture's title probably alludes to the flood story, though that is a reading and not a certainty.","c":"Catlin's ceremonial scenes follow a method: the ritual laid out in a ring or in ranks, within a broad landscape, rendered with small figures in smooth paint. They are valuable as records and generous in coverage, but they do not stand beside his best field work for freshness, and the later cards in particular can feel more diagram than experience. The interest of this one lies in its title, which points toward the deepest layer of Mandan belief."},"nga:50396":{"d":"On plain beige, an oval frame holds three full-length figures standing side by side. The one on the left, with short black hair and a red feather, wears fringed pants and an upper garment and holds a curved object in the right hand. The middle figure wears a headband of red and white feathers, long black hair and a fur wrap over one shoulder, and holds a long spear. The one on the right, in fringed leggings and a feathered headband, holds a long staff. Abstract blue and pink hues suggest sky, with a hint of grass.","i":"The Iowa, or Ioway, were a Siouan-speaking people of the Missouri and Iowa river country who lived partly in villages and partly by the hunt. Catlin painted a number of Iowa sitters, among them members of a party who toured England and France in the 1840s. Whether these three were among them is not stated, and the title names no individuals.","c":"The oval and the beige mount make this a tidy keepsake, and the blue and pink wash behind the figures is attractive in an abstract way, as if the background were an afterthought of pure color. The three men are carefully dressed and posed but carry little expression, and the objects they hold, a curved implement, a spear and a staff, are the main interest. It is typical of the series: handsome, orderly, impersonal."},"nga:50397":{"d":"A group portrait of three Iroquois, one of the small oils on card, about 46 by 63 centimeters and mounted on paperboard, that Catlin painted in the 1860s. In this format he generally stood full-length figures in a shallow space against a light landscape and recorded their dress and equipment with care, so the picture presumably shows three standing sitters in a pale setting, with costume and bearing as its chief matter.","i":"The Iroquois, or Haudenosaunee, 'people of the longhouse,' were a league of nations, among them the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga, Seneca and Tuscarora, centered in what is now New York State. By Catlin's day they had lived for two centuries beside European settlers and had adopted many of their goods. His native Pennsylvania lay at the edge of their historic sphere of influence.","c":"Catlin's eastern groups are in some ways the most poignant of the series, because the nations they show were already living close to settlers, their old dress partly given up, and a portrait is as much a memory of costume as a record of it. As painting they are among his plainest cards, a link in a long chain more than a statement. This one deserves attention mainly as evidence of how widely he cast his net."},"nga:50399":{"d":"Four people sit on the ground in a semicircle before a natural background of green trees in the distance. The one at far left is wrapped in light-colored cloth and holds a staff. The second has long hair with a feather and decorative clothing, the third a feathered headdress and long hair, and the fourth a headdress with white and red elements. All wear fringed clothing with decorative details. The seated arrangement lends the group a conversational air, as if gathered for council.","i":"The Ojibwe, or Chippewa, lived around the upper Great Lakes, in forests and on lake shores, hunting, fishing and harvesting wild rice and maple sugar, and were among the largest Native nations north of Mexico. Catlin met Ojibwe people in the 1830s on the upper Mississippi, and in the 1840s he painted a party of Ojibwe visiting London. The aged chief's wrapped figure and staff mark age and counsel.","c":"The seated semicircle is a good pictorial choice, since it lets the figures overlap slightly and read as a group, and the wrapped elder with his staff gives the card a quiet anchor. The headdresses and fringes are described with care, but the faces are summary and the pastel landscape behind is perfunctory. It is a sober, well-ordered card that does not try for more."},"nga:50400":{"d":"A softly colored ground, shading from earth tones to a faint sky, backs three full-length figures. At left a man stands relaxed, a long pipe in his left hand and a small fur in his right; he has long brown hair with feathers, a skin garment and patterned markings on his limbs. In the center another man holds a spear upright, in a feathered headdress, patterned skin garments, fur and a sash over one shoulder. At right a woman holds a wrapped infant in a cradleboard, wearing a dress and shawl with several necklaces.","i":"The pipe, the spear and the cradleboard divide the picture into peace, war and the home. A cradleboard, a wooden frame in which an infant was bound and carried, was among the most characteristic possessions of Ojibwe and other Native mothers, and it left the mother's hands free for work. Catlin's title mentions two warriors and a woman; the baby, like the woman's work, is a quiet part of the household.","c":"The cradleboard is the best touch; it gives the woman a task and the group a point of tenderness. The men's relaxed postures, pipe in one hand and spear upright in the other, are less stiff than usual, and the soft gradient from earth to sky behind is simple and pleasing. What the card lacks is a distinctive face: all three look calm and nothing more, and the sitters remain types."},"nga:50401":{"d":"A group fills the foreground in front of a large decorated tipi on grassy ground, under a sky of soft blue tones. On the left two adults stand and one sits at the center, all with long dark hair adorned with feathers and intricate clothing. To the right three young males are in motion: one aims a bow and arrow, and the other two hold bows and arrows. The youths' movement gives the card a liveliness that the adults' poses lack.","i":"Archery practice was part of a boy's education: young males learned to hunt and to fight by shooting at targets and small game from an early age. The adults at the left, seated and standing, represent the elders who oversaw such training. The tipi is more common among Plains-living Ojibwe bands than among the woodland people who lived in bark-covered wigwams, so the setting may be more generic than local.","c":"The shooting youths bring a welcome burst of movement to the right side, and the shift from the stately adults to the active boys makes a small story. The balance of the whole is less sure: the painted tipi looms behind with more detail than the faces, and the adults at the left are stiff. Still, this is among the more animated group cards, and the contrast of age and youth works."},"nga:50402":{"d":"Three figures stand in an oval with a border, on grass under a blue sky. In the center a person with facial paint, long dark hair, a feather and a headpiece adorned with feathers holds a shield and a spear. To the left another, with short hair and a feathered headpiece, in a long garment, holds a stick. On the right a third stands with hands clasped, in a patterned dress, with long dark hair. The oval encloses the group like a locket.","i":"The Sauk, or Sac, and their close allies the Meskwaki (Fox) came from the Great Lakes to the Mississippi valley of Illinois, Iowa and Wisconsin, living in farming villages in summer and hunting on the prairies in winter. Catlin painted Black Hawk and other Sauk leaders in 1832, after the war that bears Black Hawk's name. A warrior with family and a boy shows the Sauk community that the war disrupted.","c":"The shield and spear in the central hand are the card's strongest note, and the painted face adds a flicker of individuality. As with so many of the cards the handling is thin and quick, and the poses are unconnected: no glance, no touch. It succeeds as an accurate-looking summary of costume and as a pleasantly composed oval, but it leaves the family relation of the title unexpressed."},"nga:50403":{"d":"A group portrait of Black Hawk, the Sauk leader, with five other Sauk men held as prisoners. It is probably arranged like Catlin's other group cards, with figures set in a shallow landscape, and it is a small oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters. Captives are the subject, though the format is likely the polite one of a portrait group, and the dress and bearing of the men are the matter of the picture.","i":"Black Hawk (1767 to 1838) led a band of Sauk, Meskwaki and Kickapoo who crossed the Mississippi into Illinois in 1832 to reclaim lands they had been made to give up; the conflict, called the Black Hawk War, ended at the Bad Axe River in August. Black Hawk and his companions were held at Jefferson Barracks near St. Louis, where Catlin painted them that year. His portraits of the captives are sympathetic.","c":"The subject has more weight than the format can usually bear: captives posed in the same polite manner as any other group. Catlin's sympathy for the defeated is evident in his choosing the subject at all, and the 1832 sittings were a rare chance to paint a famous man while his fate was undecided. This later version, made decades afterward, is probably a quieter echo of that encounter."},"nga:50404":{"d":"The number 15 is written at the top of this card, where an oval holds three full-length figures. On the left a figure with folded arms wears patterned attire. In the center another, in a feathered headdress, holds a staff and an axe. On the right a third, in a headdress and fringed clothing, holds a decorated shield. The background is a softly colored sky with a greenish tone, in the usual pastel manner of the series.","i":"The Sauk, with their Meskwaki allies, were governed by civil chiefs and by war leaders, and standing depended on generosity, oratory and war honors. Catlin painted Sauk and Fox leaders in the 1830s on the upper Mississippi, among them Keokuk, an able speaker who counseled accommodation with the United States, and the war leader Black Hawk, who did not. The three props here, staff, axe and shield, are objects of rank rather than of any particular event.","c":"The folded arms of the left-hand figure are a quiet departure from the usual poses, and the staff, axe and shield give the other two a fine mixture of silhouettes. The oval and pastel ground are the series' standard dress, though, and the figures are rigid and without expression. The numeral at the top, presumably a catalog number, is a reminder that the card was meant for a long, orderly display."},"nga:50405":{"d":"A rider sits upright on a dark horse in mid-stride across an open grassy plain. He has high cheekbones and long dark hair adorned with a feather, and wears a fringed hide garment decorated with patterns, leggings and moccasins; he holds a long stick or staff. The horse's tack is adorned with colorful designs. Behind them is a vast sky of subtle blue and pink, suggesting dawn or dusk, and nothing else breaks the horizon. An oval and a rectangular mat frame the picture.","i":"The Running Fox appears to be Catlin's English rendering of the name of Keokuk, the Sauk leader, whom he painted on horseback on the upper Mississippi in the mid-1830s. Keokuk was famous as an orator and a favorite of the United States government, and Catlin noted his pride and his gift for speech. The equestrian portrait, borrowed from European tradition for rulers and generals, here honors a Native leader in the same terms.","c":"An equestrian portrait is the grandest format in European art, and Catlin uses it to give this rider a dignity that his standing groups give no one. The empty plain and the pink and blue sky concentrate attention on rider and horse, and the decorated tack lends color. The sky is lightly handled and the plain is little more than a ground; the picture's strength is its simplicity, and it is one of the more memorable in the series."},"nga:50406":{"d":"Three full-length figures stand in an oval within a rectangular border, in front of a softly painted landscape of grass and pastel sky. At left a figure wears a patterned, fringed garment and feathers on the headdress, with long black hair. The central figure wears a tunic, a loincloth and a feathered headdress. At right a figure with long, straight black hair is wrapped in a pink embroidered shawl. The card is narrower than most in the series, about 47 by 53 centimeters.","i":"The Menominee, whose name comes from their word for wild rice, lived around Green Bay and the Menominee River in what is now Wisconsin and Michigan, fishing, hunting and harvesting wild rice. Catlin painted Menominee sitters in the mid-1830s. A chief with his wife and son is the conventional form for presenting a people as a continuing community, but the individuals are not named.","c":"The narrower card makes the group look compressed, with the three figures standing close like a family photograph, and the pink shawl is a rare strong color in a pastel scheme. The costume is described with care, but the faces are sketched and the landscape is a few strokes. An agreeable, minor card, notable chiefly for its shape and for the shawl."},"nga:50407":{"d":"Three men are set against a natural, grassy background under a distant sky. On the left an old man sits on the ground wrapped in beige cloth, with long dark hair and a solemn expression, holding a long pipe. On the right two young men stand full length. One wears two long braids; the other a feathered headdress, and holds a shield adorned with feathers. Both wear red leggings, beadwork, fringed garments, necklaces and jewelry, and one has a pinkish-white cloth draped over his shoulder.","i":"'Beaux' was an eighteenth-century word for dandies, and Catlin's choice of it suggests his amusement at the young men's careful finery, set beside an old man in plain wrapped cloth. Young Menominee men took pains over their looks, with braids, jewelry and beadwork, as young men everywhere do. The pairing of age and youth shows two generations of a community, one in repose and the other on display.","c":"Catlin's title is a joke, and the picture half shares it: the contrast between the seated elder wrapped in beige and the two dandies in red leggings and beadwork has a gentle humor that most of the series lacks. The painting is otherwise ordinary, with stock poses and generic faces. The humor makes it more human than it deserves to be, and the old man, solemn with his long pipe, gives it its dignity."},"nga:50408":{"d":"Three figures stand outdoors against soft blue and green tones. The one on the left wears a feathered headdress and a fur-like garment with beadwork and holds a long object with tassels and feathers. The second wears a white garment marked with black and red designs and a feathered headdress. The third, in a light pink shawl with decorative elements, carries a child on the back and holds a similar decorated long object.","i":"The Oto, or Otoe, were a Siouan-speaking people of the lower Platte River country in Nebraska, closely allied with the Missouria and living in earth-lodge villages. By the 1830s they were reduced in numbers by disease and war, and they treated with the United States. Catlin's title names two chiefs and a woman; the decorated long objects they hold are probably staffs or pipe stems, signs of office.","c":"The tasseled and feathered staffs held by two of the figures give the card an unusual repeating motif, and the white garment with red and black designs makes a strong center. A mother carrying a child on her back is a familiar domestic touch. As in many of the series, the faces are blank and the drawing perfunctory. The motif of the staffs is the most distinctive thing about it."},"nga:50409":{"d":"Four people stand outdoors against a painted sky and grassy ground, in an oval with a brown and green border. At left a woman, wrapped in a patterned blanket, with long black hair, stands with her child, who is simply dressed. In the center stands a person with long dark hair adorned with feathers and a painted face, wearing a necklace and an ornate wrap. At right a person with long hair tied back, upper body bare and a blanket around the waist, holds a decorated spear.","i":"Weeco is usually identified with Waco, one of the Wichita-speaking peoples of Texas and Oklahoma who lived in grass-house villages and farmed, and with whom the 1834 Dragoon expedition made contact. The title lists a chief, a wife and a warrior, but the picture also shows a child. The decorated spear and the painted face are signs of a fighting man's equipment.","c":"The child at the left, held close, softens the group, and the painted face and spear at the right give it a measure of strength. Costume gets more attention than anatomy, and the adults are arranged with the usual courteous distance between them. The oval frame and green border make it a miniature gallery, charming in its way, but still a catalog of attributes."},"nga:50410":{"d":"Three full-length figures stand next to each other against a soft gradient from pale sky to grassy ground, inside an oval frame. The central figure wears a pink shawl with fringe, beige leggings and moccasins, and holds a ceremonial staff. On the left, a person in a beige dress with decorative detailing and layered necklaces holds a basket. On the right a figure stands with arms folded under a white blanket with red trim, a feather in the hair.","i":"The Piankeshaw were one of the Miami peoples of the Wabash River valley in Indiana and Illinois, and after a series of land cessions they were moved west of the Mississippi in the early nineteenth century. The basket in the hand of the figure at left is a reminder of the domestic and farming economy, and the staff in the center is a sign of ceremonial standing. Catlin's title gives no names.","c":"A quiet, balanced trio: the pink fringed shawl in the center is the picture's colorful heart, and the folded arms under the white blanket at right make a simple, strong shape. The soft gradient behind lets the figures read clearly. But the faces are generalized, and the group is arranged without any relation among its members. A well-composed minor card."},"nga:50411":{"d":"Three people stand on grass under an open sky within an oval frame with a brown border. At far left a woman with long dark hair wears a tan dress and necklaces. In the center a man wears a dark coat, a white shirt and boots, and holds by the hand a young boy dressed in very little, with feathers in his hair and a bow and arrow. The contrast between the man's European-style clothes and the boy's costume is the picture's main subject.","i":"The Kaskaskia were one of the Illinois Confederacy, who had lived along the Mississippi and Illinois rivers and were Catholic converts by the eighteenth century. By the 1830s a small remnant had left their homeland for lands west of the Mississippi. The chief in a coat and boots shows how far American styles had entered; the boy with bow and feathers is dressed as a hunter, as though to represent the old ways.","c":"This is a picture of transition: the man's dark coat and boots set against the boy's feathers and bow make an immediate point about change without comment. The point is sharper than the painting, since the faces are barely modeled and the oval frame gives the whole a decorous, studio air. Catlin sets the generations side by side with a light touch, and lets the viewer draw the conclusion."},"nga:50412":{"d":"An oval framed by a gray-beige mat holds three people standing side by side and facing forward. The one on the left has long dark hair with feathers and beads and wears an orange cloak. The middle figure wears a feathered headdress and a white fringed garment with red accents. The one on the right has long hair with feather adornments, a light garment with patterns and a sash, and holds a curved pipe and two arrows. Behind them are lush greenery, what may be a waterfall or river, and a cloudy blue sky.","i":"Red Jacket, or Sagoyewatha (about 1750 to 1830), was the Seneca leader best known as an orator and a defender of Seneca land and tradition against missionaries and land companies; he received a silver peace medal from George Washington. He died in 1830, about the time Catlin began his western travels, so a picture made in the 1860s must rest on an earlier portrait or on other sources. The title does not say which figure he is.","c":"A famous name attached to a modest card: Red Jacket was a figure of real stature, but the picture gives him no more emphasis than his companions, and the polite lineup, with its pleasant greenery, does nothing to suggest the orator's force. The orange cloak and the hint of water in the background are the freshest touches. Historical interest outruns the painting."},"nga:50414":{"d":"A person stands upright in an animal-skin robe with intricate patterns, one arm resting on another figure's shoulder and the other slightly raised; the face has high cheekbones and wide-set eyes, and long dark hair falls over the shoulders. At the left another figure stands beside a small dog, holding a stick with feathers. Behind them several tipis stand in a semicircle on a grassy area, with other people and a couple of animals in the camp, under a softly painted sky nearing dusk.","i":"Puncah is Catlin's spelling of Ponca, a small Siouan-speaking people closely related to the Omaha, who lived on the Missouri near the mouth of the Niobrara River in what is now Nebraska. Catlin visited the Ponca on his voyage up the Missouri in 1832 and wrote of them as a small nation. The tipis arranged in a semicircle behind the chief indicate a camp, and the family around him show him as the head of a household.","c":"The sitter's gesture, an arm laid on another's shoulder, is a real expression of family feeling, rare in a series of separated poses, and the semicircle of tipis and the dusk sky place the group in a believable camp. The dog and the other small figures add life at the edges. The large robe with its patterns is described carefully, but the faces are generalized and the setting is a pleasant backdrop."},"nga:50415":{"d":"Within a larger neutral-toned mat, three figures stand side by side beneath a soft pastel sky, with greenery at their feet. The central figure has short black hair with feathers, wears a garment around the waist and holds a staff adorned with feathers and tassels. The figure at left has long black hair and is wrapped in a light blanket-like garment. The one on the right is also wrapped in a blanket, one arm exposed, with long dark hair.","i":"The Ottawa, or Odawa, were Algonquian-speaking traders and fishermen of the Great Lakes, whose name is said to mean 'to trade,' and they traveled widely by canoe. Catlin's gallery includes several Ottawa pictures, among them a torchlit fishing scene. The blankets wrapping two of the figures were a common trade good by his day, replacing hides and furs in everyday dress.","c":"The staff with feathers and tassels is the one lively object in a quiet card, and the pastel sky and neutral mat keep the tone restrained. The two blanket-wrapped figures are almost interchangeable, wrapped like parcels, and the chief at the center is hardly more individualized. The blankets themselves are the most interesting detail, since they record the effect of trade on dress."},"nga:50416":{"d":"Two men are shown in an oval within a rectangle, in a grassy landscape with rocks and a blue sky with clouds. On the left stands an upright figure with a stern expression, a feathered headdress, a knee-length garment with a sash and decorative trim, holding a staff. On the right sits a man on a rock in a dark suit, white shirt, red sash and boots, with short dark hair and sharp eyes, holding a folded piece of paper.","i":"The pairing stages an encounter between two worlds: the chief in feathered dress, the missionary in a plain dark suit holding a paper that may stand for scripture or a letter. The Mohican, an Algonquian people of the Hudson valley, were among the earliest Native nations to meet Protestant missionaries, and by the nineteenth century the survivors had moved west. The title leaves the viewer to read both figures.","c":"The two figures face their own ways, one posed in the usual heroic standing manner and the other casually seated on a rock like a traveler stopping to read, and that small difference in pose says a good deal about the picture's meaning. It is a thoughtful pairing, if lightly painted. The piece of paper in the missionary's hand is a pointed prop, and the chief's stern look is the only comment the picture allows itself."},"nga:50417":{"d":"A group portrait of three Peoria Indians, one of Catlin's small oils on card, about 47 by 64 centimeters, from the 1860s. In this series he usually set full-length figures in a shallow space against a pale landscape and recorded their costume and equipment with care, so the card promises three standing sitters against a pale ground, with their dress and arms as the chief matter.","i":"The Peoria were one of the Illinois Confederacy, Algonquian-speaking peoples who once lived along the Illinois River, where the city of Peoria takes its name from them. By the 1830s, after land cessions, they had been moved west of the Mississippi to Missouri and then Kansas, and their numbers were small. Catlin's interest in such displaced eastern nations reflects his claim that he was recording peoples before they vanished.","c":"A small eastern nation reduced and moved on is the kind of subject that gives Catlin's series its elegiac edge, and the title alone carries some of that weight. As a painting the card is probably in his usual orderly manner: clear, even-handed and slightly flat. Its value is in the roll call, the sense that he meant to leave nobody out, more than in the individual picture."},"nga:50418":{"d":"Nine people stand closely together, full length, with varied faces, hairstyles and ornaments. Some have long hair with feathers and headdresses; they wear elaborate clothing with patterns, fringes, necklaces and beadwork, and hold a feathered staff and arrows. The background is a muted blend of earth tones, and an oval border encloses the group. The crowd of figures fills the vignette almost to its edges.","i":"In 1843 a party of Ojibwe from Canada reached London, where they were exhibited in dances and demonstrations and met the public and the press, and Catlin, who was showing his own Indian Gallery in the city, painted them. Such displays were popular and sometimes demeaning: they showed Native people as spectacles, even as the visitors observed the city with their own interest. The picture records the troupe as a group, with no individual named in the title.","c":"Nine figures in an oval is a squeeze, and the group has the compressed, shoulder-to-shoulder look of a theatrical tableau. That suits the subject, a troupe presenting itself to a public, and Catlin lets the costumes carry it: the variety of patterns, fringes and beadwork makes an interesting surface. But the faces are small, the poses repeat, and the result is crowd rather than portrait."},"nga:50419":{"d":"A group portrait of Iowa visitors to Europe, one of the small oils on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters, that Catlin completed in the 1860s. Unlike most of that group it commemorates a journey and not a homeland, a visit to the capitals of Europe in the 1840s, and it belongs to the unusual pictures in which Native travelers are shown away from home. Its subject is the travelers themselves, not the sights they saw.","i":"In 1844 and 1845 a party of Iowa (Ioway) people from the Missouri country toured England and France, giving performances of dances and mock combats for paying audiences and, reportedly, before royalty, including the French king. Catlin knew the touring Iowa and made portraits of them. Such tours gave Europeans a living view of the people in his Indian Gallery, and gave the visitors a view of European capitals.","c":"This is a document of contact in reverse: the people looked at are visitors who also looked, and Catlin was one of the few painters who knew both worlds. The pictures of Native people in Europe form the most unusual stretch of his later work, and their interest is historical first and pictorial second."},"nga:50420":{"d":"A group painting of Ojibwe people in Paris, one of the small oils on card, about 46 by 63 centimeters, that Catlin made late in his career. It belongs to the unusual pictures that show Native travelers in a European capital, at a time in the 1840s when parties of Ojibwe and other nations toured Britain and France, performing dances for paying audiences.","i":"Parties of Ojibwe traveled to Europe in the 1840s, and a group performed in Paris, where Catlin was exhibiting his gallery. Their dances were staged for audiences who paid to see them, while the visitors saw a city of boulevards, theaters and palaces. The pictures Catlin made of such parties record the encounter from both sides: each was curious about the other.","c":"As a record it is a rarity, showing Native travelers as sightseers and performers in a European capital, not as scenery in their own country. As a picture it is probably in Catlin's usual manner of the 1860s, orderly, descriptive and a little dry. What matters is the idea that his subjects were also observers, and that the gallery could turn the tables."},"nga:50421":{"d":"In a softly rendered landscape of clouds and hints of greenery, three people stand in a row. The figure at far left is wrapped in a light-colored cloak with embroidered edges, with long dark hair. The middle figure wears a similar embroidered cloak and a feathered headdress, and holds a tomahawk. The figure on the right wears a dark long coat tied at the waist with a red sash, light trousers and moccasin-like footwear, with short, dark, styled hair. Each is adorned in clothing with intricate patterns.","i":"The Oneida, one of the six nations of the Iroquois league, lost much of their New York land after the Revolution, and many moved to Wisconsin in the 1820s and 1830s, where Christian missionaries worked among them. The figure in a coat and sash is evidently the missionary, set beside two Oneida in their ceremonial dress; the contrast shows a people holding to old forms of display while living within a Christian community.","c":"The tomahawk in the middle figure's hand against the missionary's dark coat at the end of the row is a pointed pairing, and the embroidered cloaks give the left side a rich surface. The three stand in a line and do not look at one another, so the encounter remains an idea and never becomes an event. It is more thoughtful in concept than lively in execution."},"nga:50422":{"d":"Three people stand upright, facing forward, against an earth-toned landscape with a blue sky and light clouds, grass and patches of ground, in a green and beige frame. The one on the left wears a light patterned garment, likely a shawl or blanket, with dark hair showing underneath. The central figure wears a fur-like outfit with fringes and patterns and holds a pipe. The one on the right is dressed in fur-like attire, also holding a pipe, with dark hair and a feather ornament.","i":"The Delaware, who call themselves Lenape, were once the people of the Delaware River valley in Pennsylvania, New Jersey and New York. Over two centuries of pressure from colonists and treaties they were moved west to Ohio, Indiana, Missouri and then Kansas by the 1830s. The two pipes in the picture are a reminder that the pipe was a nearly universal sign of diplomacy and welcome.","c":"Two pipes in a row of three give the group a gentle rhythm of repeated shapes, and the fur-trimmed fringe of the central outfit is described with some relish. The sitters are otherwise generalized, the pose frontal and the landscape cursory, so the effect is dignified but rather impersonal. Against the Delaware's long history of displacement, the calm of the picture is a kind of silence."},"nga:50423":{"d":"Under a blue sky, with distant hills behind, three people stand outdoors side by side. The one on the left has long dark hair tied back and wears a pink dress and moccasins. The one in the center has dark hair with a headdress, wearing a light brown garment with red sashes. The one on the right has short dark hair and wears a dark coat with a red sash, holding a hat. The mixture of Native and European-style dress is the picture's point.","i":"The Creek, or Muscogee, lived in towns across Georgia and Alabama and were forced west to Indian Territory in the 1830s; Catlin met Creek people there in 1834. By then many Creek men wore a mixture of European coats, hats and sashes with traditional ornaments, and Catlin recorded that mixture and not the older dress alone. The man holding his hat on the right is one of many such portraits of Southeastern nations in transition.","c":"The pink dress, the red sashes and the hat in the right-hand figure's hand give the group a mix of colors and styles that is livelier than most. Catlin's honesty in recording the dark coat is to his credit: he does not pretend that these people dress as their ancestors did. The drawing is thin and the poses are the usual ones, but the card has a documentary candor that outweighs its stiffness."},"nga:50424":{"d":"Two figures sit in an open landscape of green plains, rocks and a cloudy sky, shown from the knees up within an oval frame. Each sits with legs bent and feet on the ground. The one on the left has dark hair with feathers and wears a long garment patterned across the chest and shoulders, reaching the knees, with moccasin-style footwear, and holds what appears to be a feather fan. The one on the right has dark hair with feathers and wears a long-sleeved top with a decorative sash, knee-length leggings and moccasins.","i":"The Choctaw were forced from Mississippi to Indian Territory in the early 1830s, one of the first of the removals, and by 1834 Catlin found them settling in what is now Oklahoma. He is best remembered among his Choctaw pictures for the ball play, the team game played with two sticks that he watched there. The seated, relaxed pose here is closer to a portrait of rest than of ceremony.","c":"Seated figures, cropped at the knees and framed by an oval, are an unusual and welcome variation on the standing lineup, and the feather fan gives one of them a small gesture. The rocks and cloud behind add a little weight to a landscape usually left blank. But the two are hardly in conversation, and the faces are summary. A mild, intimate card."},"nga:50425":{"d":"Three individuals stand in a landscape with light clouds above an earthen ground, inside an oval with a border. Each has distinct features and feathered hair. The one on the left wears a long brown tunic and holds a bow and arrow. The one in the middle wears a feathered headdress, a poncho and fur on one shoulder and holds a pipe and a pouch. The one on the right wears a light tunic with a red robe draped over the shoulders and a feathered headband.","i":"Weeah is usually identified with the Wea, a small Miami-speaking people from the Wabash country of Indiana, once centered at Ouiatenon, who by the 1830s had been moved to Missouri and then Kansas. The pipe and pouch suggest a leader's role, the bow and arrow a hunter's. The title lists two warriors and a woman, though the picture does not say which figure is which.","c":"The poncho and red robe vary the silhouettes within the row, and the pipe and pouch in the center give it a slight air of ceremony. For the rest, the group has the formal reserve of the series: faces that individuate only slightly, and a landscape reduced to a border. It is a competent card that tells little about the people in it beyond the way they dress."},"nga:50426":{"d":"Eight people sit and stand on grass in an oval with a beige mat: five adults and three children. Four are seated, with four standing behind. On the left are four men, three holding long rifles and one a long, thin pipe, in colorful headbands or headdresses with long feathers; two seated men wear bright yellow sashes with red and green designs. A seated woman holds an infant in a large basket with a handle, while another child touches her hair and an older child stands at the far right. The sky is a pastel mix of pink and blue.","i":"After the Second Seminole War began in 1835, groups of Seminole were captured and some were held at Charleston's Fort Moultrie in 1837 and 1838, awaiting removal west; Catlin visited and painted them there. The rifles in the men's hands are the weapons of the war, and the women and children are a reminder that it was fought against whole communities. The title says prisoners, but the picture shows composure.","c":"The family details give the card its human interest: the infant in its basket, the child reaching toward the woman's hair, the older child turned away at the end. Those small gestures make the prisoners of the title people in a way the standing row cannot. The colors, yellow sashes against a pink and blue sky, are cheerful in a way the subject is not, which gives the whole a curious, almost innocent tone."},"nga:50427":{"d":"Five people are shown in an oval matte within a rectangular border, before a softly painted outdoor scene with a pale sky. In the center a person stands holding a gun, with elaborate facial paint, feathers in the hair, a fringed tunic and moccasins. To one side another sits in a cloak and feathered headdress. A woman at the left holds a baby, and two figures sit on the right. The standing gun-bearer is plainly the focus of the group.","i":"Osceola, or Asi-yahola (about 1804 to 1838), was a Seminole leader of the Second Seminole War against removal from Florida. He was captured in October 1837 while under a flag of truce, held at Fort Moultrie near Charleston, and died there in January 1838; Catlin painted him in his last days. The picture presents him with a gun and war paint, among fellow Seminoles, as the image of resistance.","c":"Osceola's fame gives the card an interest that its drawing hardly earns: the gun and the facial paint make him the obvious center, and the group around him, with a mother and baby on one side, is arranged to point to him. But the figures are separate, the faces generic, and the oval keeps the history at a polite distance. This larger group is a commemoration; the single portrait from life is the better record."},"nga:50428":{"d":"Two figures stand side by side on flat green grass beneath a blue-gray sky, framed in a black oval with tan and pale-blue borders. The one on the left wears a long, quilted coat and trousers, with short dark hair, and holds a feathered staff. The one on the right holds a long rifle and wears a fringed tunic, belt, leggings and moccasins. Both have dark hair and feathered headbands. The two costumes, one mostly European in cut and the other in the older frontier mode, are the picture's contrast.","i":"The Cherokee of the southeastern mountains had adopted farming, a written language devised by Sequoyah in the 1820s, a constitution and newspapers, and were still removed to Indian Territory: some by treaty in the 1820s and 1830s, and most by force in 1838 and 1839 on the Trail of Tears. Catlin painted Cherokee sitters in the Indian Territory in 1834. The two costumes here show a nation in two styles at once.","c":"The two costumes give the pairing a tension of old and new that Catlin handles with restraint: the quilted coat and the fringed tunic stand side by side without comment. The black oval and double border make a formal, almost portrait-miniature setting, and the flat green ground keeps the figures clear. The faces are stock, but the idea of showing a nation in two kinds of dress is good."},"nga:50429":{"d":"A scene of Kickapoo preaching and prayer, with trees in the setting: presumably a group of Kickapoo men and women in the open air, with a speaker at the center. It belongs to Catlin's scenes of customs and ceremonies and is a small oil on card, about 47 by 64 centimeters. Such pictures usually show the gathering from a modest distance, with small figures grouped in a clearing.","i":"In the 1830s the Kickapoo religious leader Kennekuk, called the Kickapoo Prophet, preached a faith that blended Christian ideas with Kickapoo practice, urging sobriety, peace and prayer, and his followers used inscribed prayer sticks. Catlin met him and painted his portrait; a scene of Kickapoo preaching and praying probably refers to services of this kind. The title records the practice and does not name the preacher.","c":"A preaching scene gave Catlin one of his more unusual subjects, a Native religious movement rooted partly in Christianity, and it carries a different charge than the dance and war pictures. The restrained title is typical of him, and so presumably is the painting: orderly, small in figure and quiet in key. The subject deserves more attention than the picture is likely to repay."},"nga:50430":{"d":"Three figures stand on grassy ground under a cloudy sky of yellow and green hues. The one on the left is a woman in a dress holding a cylindrical object. The central figure, a man with one hand on his hip and the other raised to his chest, wears a fringed cloak. The man on the right stands with arms folded, in a robe and traditional moccasins. The central man's easy pose is the one relaxed gesture in an otherwise formal arrangement.","i":"The Potawatomi, one of the Three Fires of the Great Lakes with the Ojibwe and Ottawa, lived around Lake Michigan, farming corn, fishing and hunting. Under a series of treaties in the 1830s they were pushed west to Iowa, Kansas and beyond, and a picture like this belongs to Catlin's effort to record peoples whose old life was ending. The cylindrical object the woman holds is not identified.","c":"The center man's hand on the hip is a rare relaxed gesture, and it gives the group a slightly swaggering note that offsets the folded arms of his neighbor. The yellow and green cloud tints make an interesting sky for a group portrait. The woman is a shadowy presence and the faces remain stock. It is a decent small card with a good touch of attitude at the center."},"nga:50431":{"d":"Five people stand together on grass under a soft blue and pink sky, in an oval frame on beige paper. On the left a man with a rifle stands beside a woman; on the right two men flank two small unclothed boys. The three men wear feathered headdresses, layered necklaces, hooped earrings and silver arm bands. The central man, his bare chest covered in thin black horizontal lines, holds a long object with red fibers. The man at the far right has stretched earlobes through which three arrows are thrust, and holds a strand of beads and a thin branch with a small flame at its tip.","i":"Shawano is Catlin's spelling of Shawnee, an Algonquian people once of the Ohio valley who were moved west to Kansas. The man at the far right, with arrows through his earlobes, a strand of beads and a flaming wand, closely matches Catlin's portrait of Tenskwatawa, the Shawnee Prophet and brother of Tecumseh, who preached religious revival and Native unity in the early 1800s. He died in 1836, and the identification here is a likelihood, not a fact stated by the title.","c":"If the identification holds, this is a historically loaded group, and the details Catlin keeps, the arrows through the ears, the beads, the flame, make the right-hand figure the one the eye finds first. The rest is the usual gathering of attributes: a rifle, silver arm bands, painted lines. The picture rewards the viewer who knows whom to look for, and gives little to the viewer who does not."},"nga:50432":{"d":"Three figures stand outdoors on a grassy plain beneath an open, lightly clouded sky, with two tipis and seated figures in the distance. In the center a man stands upright holding a long, decorated staff, with long dark hair adorned with colorful feathers and beads, a richly decorated fringed garment and moccasins. At his right a young boy carries a small bow and arrows, his long hair unadorned. At his left a woman in a long beaded dress stands with hands lightly at her sides.","i":"K'nisteneux is Catlin's spelling of Knisteneaux, the old fur-trade name for the Cree, who ranged from the northern woodlands onto the plains and were among the most important partners of the fur trade. The boy with a miniature bow is learning the hunter's skills, and the woman's beaded dress shows the work of Cree women. The two tipis in the distance place the family on the plains side of Cree territory.","c":"The family arrangement, with a boy at the warrior's side and a woman at his other, gives the group a settled stability, and the beadwork on the dress is among the best passages of decorative detail in the series. The two distant tipis add depth. The man's staff and rich outfit make him the focus, and the two others recede; it is a patriarch's portrait with a family attached."},"nga:50433":{"d":"Three full-length figures are set in an open landscape under a light blue sky with clouds, in an oval within a larger rectangular frame. At left a woman, her hair parted in the middle, stands in a light shawl, long turquoise dress and red shoes, with a small child on her back. At the center a man in a feathered headdress, earrings and several necklaces, dressed in dark green with decorative trim and red accents, holds a long rifle; red paint circles his eyes. At right another man sits cross-legged in a similar headdress and gray clothing, holding a tomahawk.","i":"The Mi'kmaq, called Micmac in Catlin's day, are an Algonquian people of Canada's Maritime provinces and the Gaspe, among the first Native nations in the northeast to meet Europeans, in the sixteenth century. They became close trading partners of the French and were known for their birch-bark canoes and for adopting European dress and trade goods early. Whether Catlin saw Micmac people in their own country or elsewhere is unknown.","c":"The color is the surprise: a turquoise dress, red shoes and a dark green coat give this card a livelier palette than most. The seated figure with a tomahawk breaks the standing line, and the mother with her child on her back adds a human note. The faces are generalized and the oval conventional, but as a study of dress it is one of the brighter cards."},"nga:50434":{"d":"A vast open landscape of blue and beige tones stretches behind three figures standing side by side. The one on the left wears a cape featuring human-like figures and fringes, with long dark hair and feathers. The middle figure wears a fur outfit with a feathered headpiece and bracelets, and holds a bow and arrows. The one on the right wears a light shawl with a pink border and jewelry, bare arms exposed. The figures on the cape are the most unusual detail.","i":"Yntah is Catlin's spelling of Ute, the people who gave their name to Utah, long-time inhabitants of the Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin and among the first Native people to use horses. The museum dates the panel only to between 1855 and 1869, so it comes from a later stage of Catlin's work than his journeys of the 1830s. A medicine man's cape with painted human figures may record visions or deeds.","c":"The cape with its tiny painted figures is the picture's most interesting passage: it shows Catlin looking at a native picture-making tradition as well as at a person. The expanse of blue and beige behind is wide and empty, which suits the Ute country but leaves the figures isolated. The faces are summary and the pose stock; the interest is in the clothes."},"nga:50436":{"d":"A Native camp is seen from an elevated viewpoint, with tipis marked by symbols and people engaged in various activities. Distant hills rise under a cloudy sky, with soft transitions between land and sky. The brushstrokes are delicate and precise, in a palette of earthy tones: greens for the hills, browns for the tipis and ground, and grays and blues for the sky. Figures on horseback in the distance add movement. An oval frame encloses the whole.","i":"The title calls the dwellings skin tents, the common nineteenth-century name for tipis, and places them among the Ojibwe, whose Plains-living bands, known as Saulteaux or Bungi, hunted buffalo and used hide tents in place of the bark-covered wigwams of the forest. The horsemen in the distance fit this plains way of life. The symbols painted on the tents are probably owners' marks or designs received in visions.","c":"The raised viewpoint, the soft transitions and the tiny horsemen make a competent village view, and the hills fade pleasantly. The painted symbols on the tipis are the freshest detail. Beyond that it is a typical entry: a tidy, evenly lit encampment in which each element is correct and none is felt. For a viewer who has seen several of Catlin's villages, the pleasure is in recognizing the type."},"nga:50437":{"d":"A snow-covered landscape holds a herd of dark bison scattered across it, and two figures in fur garments crouch near one of the animals. The terrain consists of soft, rolling hills with snow-dusted bushes under a pale blue sky. The palette is mostly white and blue with hints of dark brown, and the brushwork is detailed in the snow and the fur textures. The dark animals stand out sharply from the white ground, and the winter quiet is almost complete.","i":"In deep snow a buffalo floundered, and a hunter on snowshoes could overtake it on foot and kill it with a lance or arrow, a method Catlin painted more than once. Winter hunts supplied meat and robes when the herds were slow, and the Ojibwe bands on the northern edge of the plains hunted buffalo in this way. The crouching figures here are probably hunters closing in on the animal.","c":"The white and blue palette is the picture's great virtue: it is a cold, silent scene, and the dark bison against the snow make a strong pattern. Catlin handles the snow and the fur textures with unusual care. The human drama is minimal, since the two crouching hunters are small and ambiguous, so this is a landscape with animals and men in it; still, it is a welcome departure from his usual green and tan."},"nga:50438":{"d":"Eight men in a line stand and walk together against a light, pastel ground inside a gray-green mat, with subtle changes of stance and direction that suggest movement. Their dark hair is adorned with red and black feathers and styled into crests or pulled back. They wear fringed skirts, beaded necklaces and bracelets, and carry spears, bows and arrows, a circular patterned shield and a small fan-like object. The line of figures runs across the card like a procession in a frieze.","i":"Catlin painted several dances of the Sauk and Fox, and the word amusing marks a social dance rather than a ceremony; the performers dress in full finery, with roaches, feathers and weapons, because dances were occasions for display as well as enjoyment. Catlin saw Sauk and Fox dances in the 1830s on the upper Mississippi. His title does not name the dance.","c":"The line of dancers, with its small changes of stance, is a clever solution to the problem of showing movement in a still picture: the figures read as individuals in a rhythm, a procession and not a pile. The decorative details, from the fan to the circular shield, are well observed. The pastel ground is bland and the faces uniform, but the card has momentum."},"nga:50440":{"d":"A group of people forms a circle surrounded by tipis, with trees behind and a sky of blue and pink hues. Individuals sit and stand in a communal setting, engaged in what seems to be a ceremonial performance or gathering. The figures are drawn with attention to clothing and posture, so the circle reads as a community and not a crowd. The picture is a small genre scene of dance in oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The eagle was honored across Native North America as a messenger to the sky, and its feathers were worn by those who had earned them. The Choctaw, like several Southeastern nations, performed an Eagle Dance, and Catlin saw Choctaw dances in Indian Territory in 1834, after the removal that brought the Choctaw from Mississippi. The title records the dance and the nation and does not describe the steps.","c":"A ring of figures under a pink and blue sky gives the card a communal feeling, and the attention to posture within the circle is better than usual. The ring is more a pattern than a performance, though: nobody leaps, no feather flies, and the dance becomes decorous and still. Catlin gives the setting better than the movement."},"nga:50441":{"d":"A group of people cluster together, facing different directions, many with bent knees, on light green grass under a blue sky in an oval frame. Almost all are bare-chested, their skin marked with horizontal lines of bright red paint, and wear orange and yellow belts with long white-and-black feathers at the waist. They have long necklaces, turquoise earrings, and thin braids with feathers and red crests. Some hold spears, bows and instruments. At the right one figure in a plain brown dress turns a face back over the shoulder. In front is a semicircle of gray stones holding three large vessels.","i":"Berdache was the old European term for a person living in a gender role different from that expected at birth, a role recognized in many Native nations and now often discussed under the modern term Two-Spirit. Catlin saw a Sauk and Fox dance honoring such a person in the 1830s and painted it, and his written account, like most outside accounts of the time, reflects the surprise of an outsider and should be read critically. The figure in the plain dress is probably the person honored.","c":"The composition is lively and unusual: the crowd of dancers with bent knees, the red body paint and the stones and vessels in front give it a ceremonial seriousness quite unlike the series' lineups. The single figure in plain dress at the right, glancing back over a shoulder, is the quiet center. As a picture it is matter-of-fact, giving the ceremony its space and its detail."},"nga:50444":{"d":"A scene of the Ojibwe snowshoe dance, a winter dance held, according to Catlin, at the first snowfall of the season to give thanks for the snowshoes that made winter travel and hunting possible. Like Catlin's other dance pictures it is probably a small genre scene, in oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters, with the dancers grouped in a ring or in file in an open landscape.","i":"Snowshoes were essential equipment in the northern woodlands and prairies, letting hunters travel and overtake deer, moose and buffalo in deep snow, and the Ojibwe made them with frames of ash and webbing of hide. A dance of thanks for the first snow joins practical need and religion, as many Native ceremonies did. Catlin's gallery includes several Ojibwe dances from his travels in the mid-1830s.","c":"The idea is one of Catlin's most charming: a dance of thanks for a season's weather, showing how closely ceremony followed the practical calendar. His even, descriptive manner is probably better suited to recording such a custom than to making the dancers move, so the interest lies in the idea more than in the handling."},"nga:50445":{"d":"A ceremonial gathering is seen from an elevated viewpoint, with a high horizon, green hills, trees and a vast cloudy sky. Groups of people are gathered, some on horseback or performing rituals. The brushwork is detailed, concentrating on figures in ceremonial attire, and the palette is earthy greens and browns. An oval frame encloses the scene. The wide view lets the viewer take in the whole crowd at once.","i":"The Choctaw ball game, played with two sticks and a small ball between large teams, was a serious affair, sometimes called the little brother of war, and the night before it the players and women danced in a ritual of preparation. Catlin saw the game in 1834 and painted several scenes of it. The Ball-Play Dance records the ceremony and not the match.","c":"The wide, high view is the right choice for a crowd, and the green hills and cloud-filled sky give the ritual a generous setting. Catlin's strength is in recording the scale of such gatherings, and the oval keeps the eye in. But the figures are small and the ritual details, however carefully drawn, are hard to read at this scale, so the whole becomes a pattern of dots in a field. It is an inclusive picture, not an intimate one."},"nga:50446":{"d":"Seen from above, Native people paddle canoes through a marshy field of rice, with a high horizon just below the point where the sky meets gentle hills and a line of trees. Rich vegetation frames the scene on the sides, and an expansive calm sky stretches above. The brushwork is fine, giving texture and depth to the reeds and water, and the palette is muted: greens, browns and blues with hints of gray and white.","i":"Wild rice, a tall aquatic grass, is harvested in late summer from canoes: one person poles through the stand while another bends the stalks over the boat and knocks the ripe grain loose with sticks. The Ho-Chunk, called Winnebago in Catlin's day, and their Ojibwe and Menominee neighbors in the upper Mississippi and Great Lakes country depended on the harvest, which was both food and a social occasion. Catlin traveled that country in the mid-1830s.","c":"The raised viewpoint and the marsh, with canoes threading through, are unusually well chosen: the picture shows a process, not a pose, and the vegetation frames it like natural wallpaper. The subtle greens and browns are well managed. The figures are tiny and the canoes few, so the harvest feels more suggested than seen. Even so, it is one of the more purposeful landscapes in the series, because its subject is work."},"nga:50447":{"d":"A village of tipis spreads across a plain, with rolling hills where the land meets the sky. The colors are muted earth tones with soft blues and grays, and figures and horses are seen on the hills. The scene is an encampment on open ground, quiet and broad, in a small oil on card of about 48 by 64 centimeters. The view is a place portrait, though the title names a trading post.","i":"Fort Pierre, on the west bank of the Missouri in what is now South Dakota, was the main trading post of the American Fur Company for the Teton Sioux (Lakota), who brought buffalo robes to trade for cloth, guns and metal goods. Catlin visited in 1832 on the steamboat Yellow Stone and painted Lakota portraits there. The tipis in the picture are probably the Lakota encampment around the post, the usual sight in trading season.","c":"The plain and the hills are painted with an easy breadth, and the muted palette suits the open country. The tipis seem to be the real subject, which shifts the emphasis from the trade to the people who made it possible. As a view it is unassertive and a little vague, a record of the place's character more than of its buildings."},"nga:50448":{"d":"A stretched, parchment-like ground carries stylized animals and symbols. The animals, a buffalo, fox, deer, owl, dog, heron and others, are drawn in a flat, simplified manner, and among them are the sun, the moon and various tools or weapons. The whole is arranged like a chart on a hide, with each motif given its own space. It is a painted copy, on card, of an Ojibwe robe, in the manner of other robe facsimiles in this series.","i":"Animal figures like these may stand for the clan totems of the Ojibwe, who inherited a clan from the father, such as the crane, loon, bear and marten, or for personal spirit helpers. The sun and moon, tools and weapons suggest a record of beliefs and possessions rather than of battles. The meaning of the particular arrangement is not explained.","c":"The clarity is the thing: each animal is set apart like a specimen, and the little vocabulary of symbols reads almost as an alphabet. As a design it is attractive and well spaced. Catlin's contribution, as with other facsimiles, is fidelity, and the panel is a respectful transcription of an art in which animals and signs carry meaning that the outsider's copy cannot unlock."},"nga:50449":{"d":"A nighttime landscape shows a river extending toward the horizon, with trees and foliage in the foreground and scattered fires casting a reddish glow across the scene. The palette is deep blue and black, warmed by the red of the fires, and the sky and water are painted with smooth, flowing brushwork. The darkness occupies most of the card, so that the lit patches are the only guide to where the action is.","i":"Spearing fish at night by firelight, from canoes or from the shore, was a widespread practice around the Great Lakes: the light draws fish to the surface and lets the spearman see his target. Catlin's title assigns the method to the Ottawa, an Algonquian people of the Great Lakes who were expert canoeists and fishermen. The scene is a work of subsistence that is also a spectacle.","c":"This is one of the series' best nocturnes: the red fires on blue-black water give the card a depth and atmosphere that Catlin's daytime cards seldom reach, and the smooth, flowing brushwork suits the subject. The fishermen are almost invisible, which is a loss for a picture titled for an activity, but the mood is strong. It shows what he could do when a subject let him paint light and not costume."},"nga:50450":{"d":"A forest scene with a thick canopy of tall trees. A group of stylized figures gathers around an area framed by trees and partly hidden structures. The brushwork is fine and detailed, especially in the trees and foliage, and the palette is earthy greens and browns, with subtle variations that capture filtered light. The gathering is a small focus in a large natural setting, with figures dressed in ceremonial attire.","i":"Black Hawk died on 3 October 1838 and was buried on the Des Moines River in what is now Iowa. Catlin painted Black Hawk in 1832 and is not known to have attended the funeral, so this scene must rest on report or imagination. The forest setting, with the mourners small among tall trees, gives the death of a famous leader a quiet, elegiac frame.","c":"A funeral in a forest is a good subject for an artist who painted trees so carefully, and the dense canopy and filtered light give the card a solemn mood that most of the series lacks. The mourners are too small to move, and the stylized figures are less affecting than the greenery around them. It works as an atmospheric landscape with a death in it, but its main power is in the idea."},"nga:50451":{"d":"A gathering is seen from an elevated viewpoint, the horizon just above the middle of the picture, with tipis, small buildings and trees in the background. A circle of people in red headdresses dances or performs a ritual, and a horseback rider is present, possibly leading the event. In the distance figures stand near a small cabin-like structure, with others gathered on its roof. The brushstrokes are precise and the colors earthy, with red headdresses and a blue sky with clouds.","i":"War dances were performed by Plains and prairie nations before a war party set out and after its return, and also for visitors; the dancers acted out their deeds and displayed their weapons and finery. The Sauk and Fox gave such dances for white spectators on the upper Mississippi in the 1830s, and the figures on the roof of the cabin may be onlookers of that kind, though the picture does not say.","c":"The high viewpoint and the circle of red headdresses give the picture a strong, simple pattern: the red against green and blue is the most vivid accent among the dance scenes. The mounted leader and the onlookers on the cabin roof add a small narrative about spectatorship. The dance itself remains a ring of dots, and the precise brushwork flattens the energy. It is an effective arrangement of color, if a cool account of a hot subject."},"nga:50452":{"d":"Rolling hills and a cloudy sky in soft pastels form the setting for three standing people. The one on the left, with long dark hair, wears a garment with a sun-like pattern along the hem. The other two have bare torsos, jewelry, markings on the legs and long dark hair. The central one wears a feather headdress and crosses the arms; the one on the right holds a bow and arrows. The picture is a small group portrait, in oil on card, of a people Catlin painted late in his career.","i":"The Navajo, who call themselves Diné, lived in the high deserts and canyons of what is now Arizona, New Mexico and Utah as herders, farmers and raiders, and were famous for their weaving. The sun-like pattern on the left figure's garment may be a woven design, since Navajo textiles were already prized across the Southwest. Catlin did not visit Navajo country in the 1830s, and how he came to paint these sitters is unknown.","c":"Folded arms and a feathered headdress give the central figure the card's one memorable pose, and the sun-patterned hem is a good detail. Otherwise the picture suffers from vagueness: partial clothing and a pastel countryside might belong to any of Catlin's peoples, and little here specifies Navajo life. It is a minor card, with something of the air of a picture worked up from description."},"nga:50453":{"d":"On grass and against a neutral background, four people stand in a line. Three face one another, and one is turned slightly aside. The central figure wears a wrapped garment with a decorative belt and fringes; the others wear lower-body coverings and have bare upper bodies. All have body paint, feathers in long dark hair and detailed faces. One holds a feathered spear, another has a bow and a quiver of arrows on the back, and the remaining two hold staffs or spears.","i":"In a title like this, warriors means men who raided and fought, and among the Apache a leader's standing rested on skill in raiding and war, on generosity and on the support of kin. Apache groups were dispersed bands led by local headmen, not tribal chiefs, a fact that a title such as 'Apachee Chief' tends to hide. The spears, bows and painted bodies are Catlin's way of showing readiness.","c":"The faces are given more detail than usual in the series, and the facing-in poses of three of the figures make a small circle of attention, which is a modest but real gain over the plain lineup. The painted bodies and feathers are summarized, and the neutral ground gives the figures nothing to stand in. The card is carried by the interest of its sitters more than by its manner."},"nga:50454":{"d":"Four people stand on grass against a lightly painted background of blue and green. Two men at the left wear traditional clothing and feathered headdresses, one in a red-bordered blanket and the other in leggings and a sash, with spears or staffs. Another man in the center wears leggings and holds a bow. At the far right a woman in a skirt and shawl holds a baby in a cradleboard. The group is a family of fighting men and a mother, arranged in a row with the cradleboard as its human note.","i":"The Apache are a group of related Athabaskan-speaking peoples of the Southwest and the southern Plains, among them the Jicarilla, Mescalero, Chiricahua and Western Apache, who were mobile, skilled in trade and raiding, and long resisted Spanish, Mexican and American control. 'Apachee' is Catlin's spelling. The cradleboard places the family at the center, though no individual is named.","c":"A woman with a baby in a cradleboard is the surest observation here, and it gives the group a human anchor beside the spears and staffs. The red-bordered blanket is a good color accent. But the group is arranged in the usual row and the soft blue-green background is generic; nothing in the picture says Southwest. Catlin's later groups show his formula at its most habitual."},"nga:50455":{"d":"Three people in full length stand within an oval frame, before a soft landscape of blurred green foliage and a cloudy sky. The one on the left, turned to the side with the head toward the center, holds a spear and wears a wrapped skirt, with feathers in long dark hair. The middle figure faces left in a rich brown fur cape over a colorful, patterned pink garment. The one at the right wears a long-sleeved reddish-brown top and a blue-green skirt, with intricately braided hair. All wear beaded necklaces.","i":"The woman at the right, in a long-sleeved top and skirt with braided hair, shows the dress of Apache women, who made clothing from hide and cloth and decorated it with beads. The turned heads of the two other figures link the group and suggest conversation. The pink patterned garment under the fur cape may be woven or trade cloth, a reminder that the Apache were traders as well as raiders.","c":"The two turned heads give this card something rare in the series, a sense that the figures know each other, and the fur cape over a pink patterned garment is the most striking costume detail. The oval, the blurred foliage and the neutral border follow the standard scheme, and the faces are not much more than types. It is a small but real improvement on the lineup."},"nga:50456":{"d":"Four men are shown full length. The one on the left stands with his back to the viewer, a foot propped on a rock or mound, leaning slightly forward on a spear, a fur garment around his waist. The second faces the viewer with a spear in his left hand. The third stands facing front, body turned slightly, holding a ceremonial staff with a hand on his chest. The fourth turns to the right, arm extended, a round shield in the other hand. All are shirtless, with long dark hair and feathers, against a plain wash of earth tones.","i":"The Navajo had acquired horses and sheep from the Spanish by the seventeenth century, and by the 1850s they were herders and formidable warriors in conflict with Mexican and American forces. In the years when this panel was painted they were being defeated and, in 1864, marched to Bosque Redondo in what is called the Long Walk, a fact the picture does not hint at. The title's warriors carry spears and a shield, though Navajo fighters of the period also used bows and firearms.","c":"This is among the more varied groups in the series: one man turns his back to the viewer and props a foot on a rock, another gestures with an extended arm, a third presses a hand to his chest. That variety, rare in Catlin's rows, gives the picture some theatrical life, and the muscular torsos are given more attention than usual. The setting is an empty wash, though, so the energy has nowhere to go."},"nga:50458":{"d":"Four people occupy a natural landscape of grass and open sky with soft clouds, all barefoot. The central figure, a woman, stands upright wrapped in a draped cloth, her long hair decorated with two feathers, and a young child stands beside her, visible from the waist up. At right a man holds a spear in one hand with a bow slung over his shoulder, his long hair styled back and a cloth around his waist. At left another figure sits holding a wrapped bundle, hair long, eyes on the ground.","i":"The Flathead, whose own name is Salish, or Selish, lived in the Bitterroot Valley and the mountains of western Montana, hunting buffalo on the plains each year and fishing and gathering roots at home. Despite the name, they did not flatten their heads, a custom of Pacific coastal peoples such as the Chinook; the origin of the name is uncertain. The seated figure with the bundle is not explained.","c":"A seated, bowed figure holding a wrapped bundle is the unexpected center of feeling in a group otherwise standing at attention, and the child at the woman's side adds a gentle change of scale. The cloth-draped woman and the barefoot figures are plainly dressed, and the plainness gives the picture a restraint that suits it. The faces and poses remain generic, but the downcast figure suggests mourning or care."},"nga:50459":{"d":"A group of people stands and sits outdoors near a tent-like structure in a forest of tall trees. One person at the center wears a shawl, a necklace and a feathered headpiece; others sit or stand nearby, and a child holds a small stick. In front of them lie fish, and in the background more fish are drying on a line near the tent. The scene is a family at its daily work, set among the trunks rather than on an open plain.","i":"Fishing and drying fish were part of the Salish seasonal round, alongside the gathering of roots and berries and the buffalo hunt on the plains, and the fish laid out and hung in the picture make the family's livelihood visible. A picture that shows fishing and not hunting reminds the viewer that the Flathead lived by many means. Catlin's title makes the central figure a chief but gives no name.","c":"A domestic scene with food in the foreground is a gentle departure from the portrait row, and the fish on the ground and drying on a line give it an unusually specific, almost still-life interest. The forest setting gives a feeling of enclosure. The figures are small and generalized and the tent-like structure is vague, but the subject is fresh in the series."},"nga:50460":{"d":"Three adults and a baby are set in an oval on a rectangular card with neutral borders, against an open landscape of grass and cloudy blue sky. Two men stand side by side in animal-hide leggings, with long dark hair, calm expressions and feathers in the hair; one holds a wooden staff, and one wears circular earrings and a necklace. The woman at the right wears a white shawl over a blue skirt and a large nose ornament, and carries a swaddled baby on her back.","i":"Nayas is Catlin's name for a people whose modern identity is uncertain, and the museum dates the panel only to between 1855 and 1869, a period in which Catlin's accounts of his travels are less well documented than those of the 1830s. The woman's large nose ornament is the distinguishing detail: nose ornaments were worn by some peoples of the Pacific coast and elsewhere. The baby on her back is carried as in many Native cultures.","c":"The nose ornament is the card's single strong ethnographic note, and the swaddled baby on the woman's back adds a human one. Beyond those, the picture is an unspecific arrangement: two men with calm expressions, a staff, an oval of grass and sky. The vagueness of the title's people matches the vagueness of the picture, and the card is more a gesture toward completeness than a record."},"nga:50462":{"d":"A family group portrait: an old man, his granddaughter and a boy, in the format of Catlin's small group portraits in oil on card, about 48 by 64 centimeters. A grandfather with children at his side suggests a domestic grouping rather than a display of arms, a gentler subject than most of the group. As in the others, the sitters are probably full length and frontal, set against a pale landscape.","i":"An old man with a granddaughter and a boy at his side is a subject that stresses continuity between the generations, and Catlin liked to present Native peoples as families and communities. Grandparents were keepers of stories and tradition in most Native societies, and the picture gives age its due. The Nayas of the title cannot now be matched with certainty to a modern people, and no names are given.","c":"The title promises something unusual: an old man and children, not warriors and weapons. A family with an elder at its head is a gentler subject than most of the series offers. Beyond the title little can be said; as an idea the card is attractive, and as a picture it is probably in the series' usual orderly, descriptive manner."},"nga:50463":{"d":"Two men stand in full view, one with his back to the viewer holding a paddle, the other in profile with one leg bent and resting on a small rock, also holding a paddle. Both have long dark hair adorned with feathers, high cheekbones and strong noses, and wear loincloths; they seem to be looking at something in the distance. Behind them, other figures sit in a canoe on the water, and the background is soft foliage with trees and a misty horizon.","i":"The Haida, whose homeland is Haida Gwaii, the Queen Charlotte Islands off the coast of British Columbia, were the great canoe builders of the Northwest Coast, carving seagoing canoes from single cedar logs and traveling, trading and raiding along hundreds of miles of coast. The paddles and the canoe are the right emblems, though the scant dress probably owes more to Catlin's habits than to Haida custom, since in a cool, wet climate cedar-bark capes and blankets were everyday wear.","c":"The paddles and the canoe give the picture a reason to exist, and the back-turned figure and the profile with a raised leg make a graceful contrasting pair. The misty foliage and horizon are among the better atmospheric passages of the later series. The scant dress, however, is a convention that tells more about the painter's idea of Native people than about the Haida, and the faces are types."},"nga:50464":{"d":"Three men sit on the ground in a simple landscape of grass and distant trees under a pale sky. The man on the left sits on a log with one leg over the other, holding a bow and a shield, his long dark hair adorned with red and white feathers. The central man holds a feathered fan and arrows and wears a similar headdress. The man on the right sits on a rock leaning forward, a stick in both hands, a headband of red and white feathers and beads around the neck. All wear little clothing.","i":"The Chinook lived at the mouth of the Columbia River and were among the Pacific coast's great traders, whose trade jargon, Chinook Jargon, spread through the Northwest. Chinook people practiced head flattening, binding an infant's skull to produce a sloped forehead that marked free birth and status. Lewis and Clark met the Chinook in 1805, and by the 1850s the lower Columbia peoples had been reduced by disease.","c":"Three seated men are a welcome change from the standing row, and the red and white feathers repeated on each head tie the group together with a quiet logic. The shield, fan, bow and stick are a pleasing sequence of silhouettes. The three look the same age and the same type, and the landscape is a few strokes, so the picture reads as a decorative arrangement."},"nga:50465":{"d":"Three figures are set against a pastel gradient with a green border, which carries a label marked 95. The central figure stands upright, facing forward and holding a staff, with high cheekbones, dark eyes and straight black hair with feathers, in a loincloth and necklace. At left another figure stands in profile holding a paddle-like object, with a similar face, feathers and a necklace. The figure at the right sits on a rock, looking forward, with multiple necklaces.","i":"Klahoquaht is usually identified with Clayoquot, one of the Nuu-chah-nulth (Nootka) peoples of the west coast of Vancouver Island, who hunted whales and sea mammals from canoes and were among the first on the Pacific coast to trade with European ships in the eighteenth century. The paddle-like object suggests the sea. How directly Catlin knew these people is uncertain.","c":"The pastel gradient behind the figures is a cool, lovely backdrop, and the paddle in the profile figure gives the group a clear theme. But the loincloths and necklaces are generic, the poses stiff, and nothing here places the sitters in the wet, forested world of the coast. It is a card in which the geography of the title outruns the evidence of the picture."},"nga:50466":{"d":"A line of five people stands on flat ground against a soft, neutral background. From the left, a man with short dark hair holds a spear; another man with feathers in his hair holds a paddle or club; a third wears a feathered headdress and a red band across his forehead; a woman in a skirt with beaded accessories holds arrows; and a small child stands beside her. The line has the look of a procession halted for the portrait.","i":"The Clatsop, a Chinookan people of the south bank of the Columbia near its mouth, hosted Lewis and Clark through the winter of 1805 to 1806 at Fort Clatsop, and by the 1850s they had been devastated by introduced diseases, which killed most of the people of the lower Columbia in the 1830s. Catlin's picture belongs to his effort to record peoples before they faded, but the details are sparse.","c":"A line of five with a child at the end is a long row of faces, orderly but inert, and the red forehead band is the one strong accent. The objects, a spear, a paddle or club and arrows, are well distributed along the row. It is an inventory of types and not of lives, and the neutral ground gives the sitters nothing to stand in."},"nga:50467":{"d":"On off-white paper with a pale-blue border, three people stand side by side on a grassy plain beneath a light blue sky. The one at left, shirtless in a fur-like loincloth over fringed trousers, holds a long bow and an arrow. The one in the middle wears a cream-colored shawl over similar trousers. The one at right, also shirtless in a fur-like skirt, balances a pot or vessel on the head. All have necklaces and earrings, and the two at left have long feathers in the hair and red markings on the face.","i":"The Walla Walla, a Sahaptian-speaking people of the Columbia Plateau around the junction of the Walla Walla and Columbia rivers, were horsemen and fishermen who traded along the river. The pot or vessel on the right-hand figure's head belongs to the daily work of carrying water or goods, though the title does not say what it holds. In 1855 the Walla Walla, with the Cayuse and Umatilla, ceded most of their land to the United States in a treaty.","c":"The vessel balanced on the right-hand figure's head is the picture's oddity and its best detail, a small observation of everyday life that the rows of weapons usually crowd out. The fur and fringed trousers are described with care, and the off-white paper with its pale-blue border gives a tidy presentation. The faces are generic and the grass a flat backdrop."},"nga:50468":{"d":"Rows of stick-figure scenes cover a textured surface that resembles animal hide, arranged in horizontal registers. In the top register, figures on horseback are in combat. Below, groups of figures interact, and the lower registers continue with figures in dynamic poses, possibly rituals or ceremonies. A horizontal bar adorned with feathers or tassels crosses the central area, and a small conical shape like a lodge appears at the left of the middle section. A greenish-brown border frames the whole, which is a painted copy of a robe.","i":"The robe is a pictographic record of the kind Lakota and Dakota men painted to show their deeds in war and the events of their lives, drawn in a flat, abbreviated manner in which each figure is a legible sign: a horse, a rider, a lance, an enemy. The registers are read as a sequence of episodes. Whose life this robe recorded is not stated, and Catlin's copy preserves the pictures without their story.","c":"As a document it is faithful and neat; the horizontal registers and the bar with tassels give it a rhythm, and the hide texture is convincingly rendered. The originals' makers are not credited and their intentions can only be guessed. The painting is best taken as a window on a Native art tradition and not as an example of Catlin's own. Its flatness is not a fault but the style of the model."},"nga:50469":{"d":"On open grass, under a softly painted sky with hints of cloud and distant greenery, three people stand together. The central figure faces forward in a beige fringed garment, a necklace and a feathered headdress, holding a decorated object. At left a slightly smaller figure stands in a similar outfit with necklaces, a red cloth belt and a feathered headdress. At right another turns sideways, wrapped in beige cloth with long dark hair tied back. All wear moccasin-style footwear and colorful striped leggings.","i":"Stone is usually identified with the Stoney, or Stoney Nakoda, a division of the Assiniboine who live in the Rocky Mountain foothills of what is now Alberta and hunted bison and mountain game. The old name Stone refers to the Assiniboine custom of cooking with heated stones. The woman and boy of the title complete a family group, but their roles are not distinguished in the picture.","c":"The slightly smaller figure at the left, in a matching outfit, gives the group an echo that is more interesting than the usual three in a row, and the striped leggings are a bright detail. The figure turned sideways breaks the frontal rhythm. As with many of the series' groups, though, the faces are faint and the landscape is a few strokes, so the card relies on costume."},"nga:50470":{"d":"A man, a woman and a boy stand side by side in an oval with a green outer frame, against a clear sky and a plain with some greenery. In the center a man wears a long hide garment with red accents, a necklace and a colorful feathered headdress, and holds a shield and a long object. At the right a woman in a red shawl carries a child resting on her back. At the left a young boy wears a tasseled garment and a headpiece with a feather.","i":"Copper is Catlin's name for the Copper Indians, the Yellowknife Dene of the far northwest of Canada, who took their English name from the native copper they worked into knives and tools, a metal found in the Coppermine River region. A chief with wife and children is typical of Catlin's practice. The headdress and shield, however, look more Plains than subarctic, and may reflect his habit of dressing subjects in a general costume.","c":"The oval frame and green outer border give this card a neat finish, and the red of the woman's shawl and the man's garment accents are the colors that hold the eye. The child asleep on the mother's back is the picture's quiet center. The man's headdress and shield look more like the Plains than the far northwest, so the card is better as a family tableau than as a record of a particular people."},"nga:50471":{"d":"A neutral gradient, like an open space, sets off a row of four people. The person on the left holds a child; the two adults on either side stand confidently with neutral expressions, and the central figure holds a staff. Each has long dark hair adorned with feathers, and clothing made from natural materials; some wear beaded necklaces. One holds a shield and another carries a quiver of arrows. The card is a little narrower than most, about 47 by 61 centimeters.","i":"The Spokane, a Salish-speaking people of eastern Washington whose name is often translated as Sun People, lived by fishing the Spokane River for salmon, by hunting and by gathering, and traded across the Columbia Plateau. Spokane and neighboring Plateau peoples had acquired horses by the eighteenth century. Catlin's title lists a chief, two warriors and a boy.","c":"A neutral gradient and a row of four give the picture a minimalist look, and the child held at the left end is a tender touch. The shield and quiver add differentiating objects. The uniform expressions and stances are the series' habitual weakness, and nothing in the picture is specific to the Plateau. It is a modest, orderly card."},"nga:50472":{"d":"Facing the viewer in full length, three people stand within an oval with a softly painted horizon. On the left a woman with dark hair wears a brown fringed dress and red leggings. The middle figure, likely a man, wears a beaded garment and a headpiece with feathers and holds a stick or staff. The figure on the right, also likely a man, wears a feathered headdress, a beaded necklace and an arm band, bare-chested with a blue cloth over the lower body and gray leggings, holding a painted shield and a ceremonial club.","i":"Athapaskan, spelled Athapasca by Catlin, names a large family of languages spoken from Alaska and northwestern Canada to the American Southwest, the Apache and Navajo among them. In a title like this it refers to the Dene peoples of the subarctic, hunters of caribou and moose and trappers in the fur trade, whose clothing was tanned hide decorated with quillwork and beads. The painted shield and club look generalized.","c":"A woman in a fringed hide dress and red leggings makes a convincing figure at the left, and the beaded decorations add texture. The two men to her right are less certain, dressed in a vaguely Plains kit of feathers, shield and club. The soft horizon in the oval is plain. The picture is a good example of how Catlin's formula could smooth very different peoples into one pleasant arrangement."},"nga:50473":{"d":"Beneath a cloudy sky, with a vast landscape behind them, four people stand in a row. The first on the left has long dark hair and a round face, and wears a fringed garment with a draped shawl. The second also has long hair and wears a feathered headdress, a fringed tunic and moccasins. The third has long hair with feathers, a tunic with geometric patterns and a long stick. The fourth, a child on the far right, wears a dress and beaded necklaces, with beads in the long hair.","i":"The Dogrib, now called the Tlicho, are a Dene people of the Great Slave and Great Bear lake country of Canada's Northwest Territories, caribou hunters who lived by seasonal movement and the fur trade. Catlin's title simply counts four people. The geometric patterns on the third figure's tunic may be quillwork or beadwork, crafts in which northern Dene women excelled.","c":"The child at the far right in a dress and beaded necklaces ends the row with a touch of scale, and the geometric patterns on the third figure's tunic give a decorative center. The vast landscape and cloudy sky behind are vague, but the picture has a modest charm. As with many cards in this late group, the title does more to place the subjects than the picture does."},"nga:50474":{"d":"In a painted outdoor setting of soft, natural colors, three people stand shoulder to shoulder. The one on the left wears a dark fur garment with feather adornments in the hair, white bead necklaces and a gentle expression. The central one wears a light fringed garment and feathers in the hair, and holds a red pipe with feathers. The one on the right wears a light dress with fringe and bead decorations, her long hair adorned with beads.","i":"Selish, or Salish, is the name of the people called Flathead by outsiders, and the term also names a large language family of the Pacific Northwest and the interior. The Selish of western Montana were hunters, gatherers and traders, and pipe smoking and ceremonial gift-giving were part of their diplomacy. The red pipe may be of the red pipestone traded widely across the continent, a stone that was named catlinite after Catlin himself.","c":"The red pipe with feathers is the card's most vivid object, and the central figure's calm hold on it gives the group a center. The left-hand figure's gentle expression is a rare nuance. The dress and beadwork are pleasantly described, but the poses are formulaic, and the painted outdoor setting is light and noncommittal."},"nga:50475":{"d":"Three people are shown from head to toe on grassy ground under a clear blue sky with some clouds, in an oval with a greenish-gray border. On the left a man stands confidently with a hand on his hip, in fringed leggings, a beaded necklace and a red headband with feathers. In the center another man is wrapped in a white cloak with a sun-like design and wears a feathered headband. At right a woman in a blue dress with detailed beadwork at the neckline and hem, hair in two long braids, wears multiple beaded necklaces.","i":"The Chipewyan, who call themselves Dene, were hunters of caribou in the subarctic forests and barrens of northern Canada and trading partners of the Hudson's Bay Company. Women's beadwork on dresses, such as the blue dress here with beaded neckline and hem, was an important craft of the northern Dene in the nineteenth century. The sun-like design on the white cloak may be painted or embroidered.","c":"The man with his hand on his hip has a swagger that gives the group some energy, and the woman's blue dress with its beaded edges is the best color in the card. The sun-like design on the cloak is a bright secondary note. The group is otherwise typical: oval, grass, blue sky, calm faces. A modestly attractive card that depends on its few details."},"nga:50476":{"d":"An open, snowy landscape under a gradient sky, with a patch of clouds on the horizon, holds three people standing side by side, in an oval matte. The one on the left is in profile with folded arms. The central figure faces front, holding a long, pointed spear, in a cap with a tassel, a coat and pants. The one on the right is wrapped in a shawl or large garment, with long hair, and carries a child on the back. All are dressed warmly.","i":"Esquimaux is the old spelling of Eskimo, a word now replaced by Inuit for the people of the Canadian Arctic and Greenland, and by Inuit and Yupik for those of Alaska. The warm garments and the child carried on the back fit Arctic dress, and the spear and snow suggest hunting on land and ice. Where Catlin met Arctic sitters is not known.","c":"The warm clothing and the snow are the picture's novelty, a change from the series' green grass, and the child carried on the back gives the group a quiet focus. The pale gradient sky is simple and pleasant. But the figures are as stiff as any, and the Arctic is represented by a few strokes of white. It adds range to Catlin's survey without adding depth."},"nga:50477":{"d":"A subtle pastoral landscape of grass and open sky frames three people shown head to toe. The central figure stands upright, holding a spear decorated with feathers; the figure on the left sits, holding a bow; the one on the right stands in a relaxed pose with a bent leg. Each has distinct facial features and black hair adorned with feathers, and wears traditional attire with decorative elements of feathers, animal hide or fur, and necklaces.","i":"The Aleut, who call themselves Unangan, lived on the Aleutian Islands and the Alaska Peninsula as skilled sea-mammal hunters in skin boats, and were devastated in the eighteenth century by Russian fur traders. Their traditional clothing was waterproof parkas of bird skin or sea-mammal gut, so the feathered headdresses and grassy setting here seem to owe more to Catlin's general costume than to the islands. How he came to paint Aleut sitters is unknown.","c":"This is an example of a title promising more than a picture can deliver: an Aleut chief on grass with feathered hair reads as a plains figure under a different label. The poses, a seated bowman, an upright spear bearer and a relaxed third with a bent leg, are well varied, and the card is graceful enough as an arrangement. As ethnography it should be treated with caution."},"nga:50478":{"d":"On a gray and brown mount, an oval shows three people in full view before a soft landscape of rolling hills and a muted sky. The one on the left has long dark hair, a headpiece of natural materials and a blue waist garment, and stands with one hand on the hip. The central figure holds a spear, wears feathers and a cloth wrap on the head, and a red and orange poncho over a tunic with tall boots. The one on the right wears a turquoise skirt, red leggings and a decorative necklace, hands by the sides.","i":"The Cochimi were the Indigenous people of the central Baja California peninsula, hunter-gatherers whom Jesuit missionaries encountered from the late seventeenth century and whose numbers collapsed from disease and mission life in the eighteenth. By the 1850s they were much reduced, so a picture made then must rest on very little observation or on earlier accounts. The poncho and boots may reflect Spanish and Mexican influence.","c":"The mixture of dress, a poncho, tall boots, a cloth head-wrap and a turquoise skirt, makes this one of the more visually interesting cards, and it hints at a culture formed by contact. But the picture's authority is thin: how Catlin could have known the Cochimi, so reduced by his time, is unclear, and the oval and rolling hills are the series' standard. It is a pleasing arrangement of costumes with a question mark behind it."},"nga:50479":{"d":"An oval on a sepia-toned ground shows three people in a grassy landscape of green hills under a light blue sky. On the left a man in a feathered headdress, with paint on his face, long hair and a fringed outfit, holds arrows. In the center another man, wrapped in cloth, in a feathered headdress and necklaces, holds a ceremonial pipe. On the right a woman with braided hair wears a skirt, necklaces and bracelets.","i":"The Mohave lived along the lower Colorado River in what is now Arizona, California and Nevada, farming the river's floodplain, and were known as tall, vigorous people and fierce fighters. The grassy hills in the picture do not look like the desert river valley they lived in. Mohave women wore skirts of bark fiber and men often went with little clothing, so Catlin's feathered headdresses and fringed outfits seem borrowed from the Plains.","c":"As a title it points to the Colorado River; as a picture it points to the Plains. The ceremonial pipe in the center and the arrows on the left give the group a useful variety of objects, and the sepia mount frames the oval nicely, but the green hills and fringed costumes belong to Catlin's habitual repertoire. It is a reminder that the later cards sometimes decorate a place rather than record it."},"nga:50480":{"d":"Three people stand against a natural backdrop of green grass, rolling hills and soft blue mountains under a clear sky, in an oval within a rectangular frame. The woman on the left has long dark hair in two braids and wears a light dress with fringes and beaded necklaces, barefoot. The central man has feathers in his long hair, jewelry at the neck and a cloak with a red sunburst design. The man on the right holds a spear and a shield and wears fringed animal-skin leggings and a belt, barefoot.","i":"The Yuma, or Quechan, live at the junction of the Colorado and Gila rivers on the present border of Arizona and California, a river people who farmed the floodplain and were known for their fighting. Their location made the Yuma Crossing the key ford of the southern overland route to California, and the Gold Rush of 1849 brought thousands of emigrants through their country. The red sunburst on the chief's cloak is a striking motif, though its meaning is not given.","c":"The red sunburst on the central figure's cloak is the best idea in the card, a bold geometric note against the soft blue mountains and green hills, and it makes the chief the unmistakable center. The rest is the standard repertoire: spear, shield and a daughter with braids. The distant blue mountains give an unusual depth for a group portrait. It is a handsome arrangement more than a record of Yuma life."},"nga:50481":{"d":"Within an oval border and a landscape of soft pastel tones, three people stand side by side. The one on the left holds a bow and wears a white garment with accessories. The one in the middle stands with hands touching in front, in a similar white garment with orange fringe. The one on the right holds a spear and wears a red-brown cloth with accessories. The group is quiet, pale and orderly, with the orange fringe as its brightest color.","i":"Yumaya is probably Catlin's variant of Yuma, the Quechan people of the lower Colorado River, though the title is not explained. The repeated bow and spear mark the figures as fighting men, while the white garments with orange fringe are the picture's main distinctive costume. Where Catlin gathered his Yuma material is not known.","c":"A white-garbed trio in pastel is a restrained card: the orange fringe is the only lively color, and the clasped hands of the central figure give a quiet, almost courtly gesture. The bow and spear are minor accents. For the rest it is a vague arrangement, since the title does not fully name the people and the picture does not help."},"nga:50482":{"d":"Five people, four standing and one seated, pose in a circular frame within a rectangular mat. At far left a man with shoulder-length hair wears little more than a waist covering, red paint on the body and several necklaces. Next to him stands a man with longer hair, a wide-brimmed straw hat, a necklace and a bow. A woman beside them holds a basket and an arrow, in a skirt and necklaces. Behind are a seated woman and a standing child, among trees and foliage under a misty sky.","i":"The Maya, descendants of the builders of the great stone cities of Yucatan, Guatemala and Belize, were living by the nineteenth century as farmers in villages, speaking languages that survive today. The wide-brimmed straw hats are a sign of cultural mixture, since the style came with the Spanish. Catlin's journeys in Latin America in the 1850s are known mainly from his own later writing, and this picture is dated only to between 1855 and 1869.","c":"The hats and the basket and arrow in the woman's hands show a people in daily clothes and not a display of regalia, and the seated figure and child in the background give the circle depth. The circular format is unusual and pretty. But a circle holding five figures in a landscape is a squeeze, and the faces are hardly Maya in any way a viewer could test. The card extends the gallery southward."},"nga:50483":{"d":"A bison is pursued by a rider on a grassy prairie under a cloudy sky. The bison has detailed brown fur and an expressive eye, and seems to move swiftly. A galloping horse runs beside it, with attention to the mane and the motion that suggest speed. The composition is enclosed in an oval with a muted border, so that the chase is cropped to a single pair of animals and a rider.","i":"Running buffalo, riding alongside the herd at a gallop and shooting an arrow or driving a lance behind the ribs, was the most prestigious hunting method on the Plains, and it demanded a trained buffalo horse and a steady hand. Catlin described the chase with enthusiasm and painted it many times, and for his European audiences it came to stand for Plains life. The riders in his pictures are rarely named.","c":"Catlin's buffalo chases are among his best-known subjects, and this one has the right ingredients: the bull's wide eye, the sweep of the horse's mane and a clear diagonal of pursuit. The oval format squeezes it, and a herd and a crowd of hunters are reduced to a pair, so the chase lacks the dust and tumult of his fuller versions. It is a good summary of a subject he painted better at greater scale."},"nga:50484":{"d":"From a slightly raised viewpoint the plain opens in a broad prospect with a low horizon and a sky of blue clouds. A hunter on horseback engages a group of buffalo in the foreground, and a vast herd extends into the distance across the grassland. The brushwork captures textures and details realistically, with earthy tones for the animals and land set against the vivid sky. The title promises mishaps in the chase as well as its triumph.","i":"Catlin wrote that the buffalo chase was dangerous as well as thrilling: a horse could stumble in a prairie-dog hole at full gallop and throw its rider, or a wounded bull could turn and gore the horse. The accidents of the title are those hazards. Native hunters accepted the risks as part of the most prestigious form of hunting, and a picture that shows them gives the chase a more honest face than the simple triumph of the hunter.","c":"The title is more interesting than the execution, which gives the plain, the herd and the hunter a calm, even treatment. The vast herd stretching away is the picture's chief asset, a real sense of numbers and distance that makes the lone hunter small. If the accidents of the title are in the picture, they do not disturb the smooth surface; the speed and the danger Catlin described in words are what is missing."},"nga:50485":{"d":"A vast grassy plain stretches away with a herd of bison in the distance, under a sky of subtle bands of cloud. Two figures hunt near a fallen bison in the foreground, and the colors are predominantly earth tones. Catlin's title names the painter himself as one of the hunters, so the picture is partly a self-portrait in action, though it is not clear which of the two he is.","i":"Catlin was himself a keen hunter, and in his writings he describes taking part in buffalo hunts with Native companions, sometimes on horseback and sometimes on foot. By including himself in the picture he affirms that he was an eyewitness, a point he cared about since many in his audience doubted what he reported. The Native companion is not named.","c":"Putting himself in the picture is a bold claim of presence, and the fallen bison in the foreground gives the scene a clear focus. The pair is small against the plain, and the herd in the distance is a gentle brown mass. It is more a statement of authority than a hunting picture: the painter was there. As a narrative it is calm, even slight."},"nga:50486":{"d":"A herd of bison covers a slope under a high horizon that divides the grassy field from the sky. The bison are scattered, those nearer rendered in detail and those farther less defined. The brushstrokes are smooth, depicting fur and grass texture realistically. The palette is serene: earthy browns of the bison against the greens of the grass, under a soft blue and gray sky. A wallow, the dust bowl in which bison roll, is the subject named by the title.","i":"A wallow is a shallow bowl in the prairie where bison roll in dust or mud, to shed their winter coats, ease the bite of insects and cool off, and over many years the repeated rolling wore wallows into the ground that stayed visible for generations. Catlin described them in his Letters as a common feature of the plains. The picture is a study of the animals' daily habits.","c":"Without a hunter the picture is calmer and in some ways truer than the chases: the animals are simply at their ease, and the contrast of near detail and far haze makes a convincing depth. The sky and grass are pleasant, if unadventurous. Catlin's affection for the buffalo is real, and shows in the patient treatment of the fur. Still, it is a quiet picture of little incident."},"nga:50487":{"d":"Rolling green hills stretch into the distance beneath a high horizon and a sky of wispy clouds. In the center, two riders on horseback engage with a herd of buffalo. The palette is earthy greens and browns with vibrant blues, and more hills in the distance add depth. The riders are small in the landscape, and the herd forms a dark mass in the middle ground.","i":"The title names the animals' behavior: buffalo bulls in a herd would close ranks around cows and calves when pursued, a defensive habit that Catlin described and painted. Hunters generally preferred to kill cows, whose meat and robes were better, so a hunter's approach to a herd was an act of judgment as well as skill. The image shows a moment when the herd is gathered and not yet in flight.","c":"Two riders and a herd in a rolling green country: the high horizon and the long hills give the picture a reassuring breadth, and the vibrant blue gives an unusual freshness. Catlin's observation of animal behavior is the point, but the riders are tiny and the bulls' protectiveness is hard to read at this size. A pleasant landscape with buffalo, short on the tension its title describes."},"nga:50488":{"d":"A large herd of bison is spread across a plain, seen from a raised viewpoint. In the foreground two bison fight; the rest of the herd disperses over the grassy expanse toward a high horizon, with distant animals and a few trees. The sky is soft tan and brown with hints of blue, possibly dawn or dusk. The brushstrokes are smooth, conveying movement among the bison. Earthy tones dominate: deep browns of the animals against the lighter greens and yellows of the grass.","i":"Bison bulls fought in the late-summer rut, charging and locking heads for dominance, and Catlin, who admired the animal greatly, painted such combats more than once. He saw the buffalo as the Native peoples' companion in a way of life that was disappearing, and in 1832 he proposed a national park for the Native peoples and the bison. The picture contains no hunters; the animals themselves are its subject.","c":"Catlin was one of the first painters to treat the buffalo as a subject worth looking at in its own right, and this card shows why: the heavy brown masses of the fighting bulls against the lighter grass, the dispersed herd behind. It is a rare place where his careful, smooth manner and a sense of weight and movement meet. The dawn or dusk sky is quiet and fine. The landscape is a little formulaic, but the animals are persuasive."},"nga:50489":{"d":"Horsemen confront bears in a grassy landscape with scattered trees and an open sky. Several riders are involved in the encounter, their horses in motion, some rearing, which suggests the intensity of the moment. The composition is dynamic, drawn around the interaction of riders, horses and bears, and the figures' dress and ornaments and the postures of the animals are visible in detail. The title names the riders as Cree and the animals as grizzlies.","i":"Grizzly bears were the most feared animals of the northern plains and mountains, and Plains peoples hunted them rarely and with ceremony; a necklace of grizzly claws marked a man who had faced one. K'nisteneux is Catlin's spelling for the Cree, who ranged across the northern woodlands and plains. Catlin's account of an attack on two bears may rest on what traders and Native hunters told him.","c":"Rearing horses and bears at close quarters make this one of the few cards with real danger in it, and the dynamic composition is a relief after the standing rows. The dress details are given care. The execution still tends to smooth the fear away, and the scattered trees and open sky are the series' habitual pleasant backdrop. It is an action picture painted in the voice of a gentle observer."},"nga:50490":{"d":"A gathering of Native people stands among tipis in a grassy open area, with green foliage and trees behind. A group encircles a central activity near several conical tents, the participants adorned in headdresses and traditional attire. The atmosphere is calm and communal. The title names a pipe dance, but the picture's central action is not described in detail, and the ring of figures is what the eye finds first.","i":"Pipe ceremonies ran through the political and spiritual life of the Plains peoples: smoking sealed treaties and friendships, opened councils and carried prayers upward with the smoke. Catlin's Assiniboine pictures come from his 1832 stay at Fort Union, where he saw the pipe ritual repeatedly. A dance built around the pipe would be an occasion of ritual and diplomacy as well as display.","c":"The ring of figures among tipis gives the card a calm community feeling, and the central focus is clear. But the dance is again a ring of dots, and the green foliage behind is generic, so the ceremony is described as a scene rather than felt as an event. Catlin's field studies of the 1830s were quicker and fresher; this later one is a quiet summary."},"nga:50491":{"d":"A large crowd gathers in a flat landscape, some on horseback and others standing in a circle, with more riders spread across the distance. The horizon is flat and uninterrupted, which balances the composition, and the sky above is brushed in soft horizontal strokes with gentle clouds. The palette is warm and earthy, dominated by the green of the grass and the light blue of the sky, with natural tones in the clothing.","i":"Horse racing was a favorite sport among the Plains peoples, among them the Hidatsa, whom Catlin called Minatarrees, and large wagers of robes, horses and weapons were placed on the outcome. The circle of spectators and the riders in the distance show a race with its course. A horse race was a social event as much as a contest, and Catlin met the Hidatsa in 1832 on the upper Missouri.","c":"The flat horizon is a bold choice, balancing the crowd and giving the sky a calm, regular band, and the gathering in a ring has a plausible spectator energy. Catlin is good at crowds seen from a distance, and this is a clear example. The riders in the distance are the weak spot: tiny and evenly spaced, they give no sense of speed. A well-organized scene that does not quite race."},"nga:50492":{"d":"In the center of a large crowd, a pale-skinned painter in a brown tunic, white collar and brown hat stands at a canvas with palette and brushes, painting a tall man who faces him at left. The sitter wears a long painted tan garment with a red stripe down the front, fringed trousers with teal, yellow and red patches, a feather headdress of white and black that trails to the ground, thin red horns and a feathered staff. Onlookers kneel, sit, stand or ride, with small dogs among them, before tall trees, a blue-gray sky and a river at right.","i":"Catlin told how the Mandan reacted to his portraits in 1832: they were amazed that a painted face could look back, and some feared it would take the sitter's life. According to his account, Mah-to-toh-pa, Four Bears, a leading chief, sat first, and others followed. Catlin put himself in the picture as the second figure, a rare self-portrait at work, and the circle of onlookers records the community's curiosity. Four Bears died of smallpox in 1837.","c":"A painter painting is a charming subject and a shrewd advertisement, and the crowd of onlookers gives the card more life than any lineup: every face has a reaction, and the dogs add a note of domestic comedy. The pale painter among the reddish-brown crowd is a bold device, honest about who is looking. The composition is a little scattered, and the sitter's headdress to the ground steals the show; but the invention is genuine, and so is the warmth."},"nga:50493":{"d":"A vast plain of greenish-yellow grass is dotted with small mounds of earth under a high horizon, with light blue mountains far off and a sky of pale blue and soft gray clouds. A hunter and two riders on horseback appear among the mounds. The brushwork is meticulous and fine, bringing out the texture of the grass, and the palette is earthy greens, yellows and browns. The mounds are the burrow entrances of the colony the title names.","i":"Prairie dogs lived in colonies that early travelers called villages or towns, each burrow marked by a mound of earth and watched by a sentry that barked an alarm, and some towns covered many acres. Catlin described them in his Letters, including the popular belief that burrowing owls and rattlesnakes shared the holes. In the picture the mounds are the visible sign of the village.","c":"The mounds make an unusual pattern, small repeated bumps in a field of yellow-green, and the distant blue mountains give the plain a measure. The subject is a good one for Catlin's patient, detailed manner, and the tiny riders add scale. The painting is a naturalist's record more than a landscape: the village is the thing, and there is little mood beyond it. A mild but charming card."},"nga:50494":{"d":"From an elevated viewpoint, a wide grassy plain carries scattered tipis and small figures. Green rolling hills lead into the plain, and a line of trees stretches toward the horizon, where distant mountain ridges rise. The brushwork is smooth, and the palette runs through shades of green, earthy browns and the red of the tipis, under a sky with light gray and white clouds and hints of blue. The overall atmosphere is tranquil and vast.","i":"Fort Union, built in 1828 near the mouth of the Yellowstone River, was the American Fur Company's chief post on the upper Missouri, where Assiniboine, Crow, Cree and Blackfoot traders came with buffalo robes. Catlin spent several weeks there in 1832 and painted many of the visitors. The tipis in the picture are probably the camps of the Native peoples who gathered about the post to trade.","c":"A broad view from a height, with the tipis scattered like small red notes in the green, is one of Catlin's better uses of the format: the plain, the tree line and the distant mountains make a convincing recession. The fort that gives the picture its title is not what the eye finds; it is a landscape of the people who came to it. Calm, spacious and rather empty of drama, it makes a good document of a setting."},"nga:50495":{"d":"At eye level, the scene shows a shelter and tipis with a high horizon and a backdrop of lush trees. People sit under the shelter while items hang there to dry. Two tipis stand in the distance with figures and a dog nearby. The brushstrokes are smooth and controlled, and the palette is earthy, with browns, greens and hints of blue sky. Greenery and soft blues give a serene setting to an act of manufacture.","i":"Flint arrowheads were made by flaking: the maker struck chips from a stone with a hammerstone, then pressed off the finer flakes with a pointed tool of antler or bone until a sharp point emerged. It was a skilled craft, and the title treats it as a typical occupation of the Apache. Catlin liked to show crafts at work.","c":"A shelter full of seated workers is a pleasant change from the standing rows, and the craft gives the picture a purpose and a quiet industry. The hanging items add a note of domestic realism. The subject is better than its treatment: the figures are small, and the process of knapping, a close-up of hands, can hardly be seen at this distance. A clear idea, rendered at too great a remove."},"nga:50496":{"d":"A tan hide with irregular, wavy edges, as natural skin has, fills the card, its surface covered in detailed, vibrant scenes in rows. Stylized figures and horses appear in dynamic poses, adorned figures that may be warriors in scenes of hunting or combat. At the bottom a fringe-like detail may be tassels of leather or sinew. The hide is set against a muted gray-brown ground within a green painted edge, as if displayed like a specimen.","i":"Mandan men painted their robes with pictures of their war deeds, and a chief's robe would list his battles in rows of horsemen and enemies. The title names Four Men, a Mandan chief; Catlin knew several Mandan leaders by their English-translated names. A facsimile preserves the images but not the leather, and the original's owner is not further identified.","c":"The rows of horsemen and the wavy hide edge make this copy lively as an arrangement, and the green edge frames it like a treasure. Catlin's faithful transcription gives these pictures a second life. But it remains a copy, and the scenes' meaning belongs to Four Men and not to the painter. As art it is borrowed; as evidence it is useful."},"nga:50497":{"d":"An oval painting captures a wide landscape. The horizon lies in the upper third, with rolling hills against a sky of warm hues; fluid brushstrokes move from golds and oranges near the horizon to darker browns and grays above. In the foreground are tipis and small figures, suggesting a settlement, and animals graze in the middle ground with trees in the background. The palette is earthy browns and greens with the warm tones of the sky, and the whole has the calm of evening.","i":"The title gives no nation: it is a type picture of an encampment, the kind of general view Catlin made to show Plains life as a whole. Sunset camps, with horses brought in and fires lit, belonged to the daily rhythm of Plains life, and the grazing animals in the middle ground are probably horses being watched. For his European audiences such a picture was an emblem of a peaceful, picturesque way of life.","c":"The sky is the performance: the gold and orange band at the horizon dissolving into brown and gray above is painted with a freedom the figures never receive. As a type picture it is competent, and as a sunset it is pretty; the encampment below it is generic and could be any of a dozen others. It confirms how far, by the 1860s, Catlin's villages had become formulas."},"nga:50498":{"d":"A river curves through the scene, with hills meeting the sky and clouds in the distance. Trees, plants and grassy hills fill the composition and lead the eye toward the horizon. The brushstrokes are smooth, and the palette is earthy greens, browns and blues, calm and serene. Painted on canvas, and smaller and wider than most cards in the series, about 38 by 56 centimeters, it is a pure landscape, without the figures that usually anchor Catlin's views.","i":"The St. Peter's is the old name of the Minnesota River, which joins the Mississippi near Fort Snelling, and Catlin traveled up it in the mid-1830s on his way to the pipestone quarry on the Coteau des Prairies. The curious bluffs are grass-covered hills whose smooth forms Catlin found remarkable. Landscapes like this record a country that settlers were about to claim.","c":"Without figures or incident, this canvas shows how good Catlin could be when he simply looked at a country: the river's curve and the layered hills lead the eye naturally, and the calm greens and blues are well balanced. It lacks the hooks of his Native subjects, and some may find it empty. For a viewer interested in the Upper Mississippi before the plow, it is among the most direct records of the series."},"nga:50499":{"d":"A river flows diagonally from the lower left to the upper right, with a canoe carrying three figures on the water. Rolling hills in the distance catch the sunlight, and the palette is earthy tones with lush green along the banks and a pale blue sky with clouds. The diagonal of the river draws the eye into the picture, and the tiny canoe gives the scale of the landscape.","i":"The Grand Detour is the Big Bend of the Missouri River in what is now South Dakota, where the river makes a long loop and returns to within a short distance of itself, so that a walker could cross the neck in a fraction of the time a steamboat took to go round. Catlin passed it on the Yellow Stone in 1832. The scene shows the Missouri as the highway on which his journey, and the fur trade, depended.","c":"The diagonal river is a simple and effective structure, and the sunlit hills give the far side a bright, receding edge. The tiny canoe is a nice human touch. Catlin's Missouri views are among the most trustworthy parts of the later series, since the country was fresh in his memory and plainly loved. They are not dramatic, but they are honest."},"nga:50500":{"d":"A broad landscape is seen from a raised viewpoint, with a high horizon and distant mountains against a light blue sky with scattered clouds. The brushstrokes are smooth and blended, creating depth and atmosphere. Earthy greens and browns mark the land, with cooler blues and grays in the mountains and sky. Close to the viewer a group of horses stands near trees, and figures stand by a river, with a waterfall in the background.","i":"The Snake River, the largest tributary of the Columbia, rises in Wyoming and crosses southern Idaho in a great arc, falling over cataracts such as Shoshone Falls on its way, and its valley was the route of fur trappers and emigrants along the Oregon Trail. Catlin's title does not say which falls he means. The museum dates the panel between 1855 and 1869, so it belongs to the later phase of his work.","c":"The waterfall in the background gives the landscape a focal point, and the horses and figures in the foreground bring the sense of a trail; the distant mountains in cool blue and gray give atmospheric depth. It is a respectable picture of a famous kind of western scenery, but the falls are modest in the frame, and the surface is as smooth as everything else in the series. Beautiful country, politely painted."},"nga:50501":{"d":"A river winds through elevated terrain with lush greenery, and small figures can be seen on the hills. The brushstrokes are smooth, and the colors range through greens, grays, blues, browns, tans, pinks and muted oranges. The hills are high, rounded, grass-covered bluffs rising from the valley, as the title says, and the figures on them give the scale of the land. It is a river-country view with the human presence kept small.","i":"The upper Missouri below the mountains runs through a valley bounded by smooth, high bluffs of grass and clay that Catlin found beautiful, and he looked at them for weeks from the deck of the steamboat in 1832. They were the backdrop to Native hunting, travel and trade for generations. Painted decades later, they are partly remembered scenery, with the gentle hues of recollection.","c":"The pinks and muted oranges in the clay of the bluffs are the picture's special reward, a subtle palette that suits the half-bare slopes, and the small figures on the hills give the sense of a huge empty country. The painting is not dramatic, and the bluffs are smoothed into a sweet pastoral. But the sheer range of delicate colors makes it one of the more beautiful of the pure landscapes in the series."},"nga:50502":{"d":"A fire on the prairie, a subject Catlin painted more than once for its spectacle, usually as a line of flame and smoke sweeping over the grass with animals and riders fleeing before it. This card is a small landscape in oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters, and the title promises the same pattern: burning meadows, a lit sky and the speed of the flames.","i":"Prairie fires were set deliberately by Native peoples to renew grass, drive game and clear ground, and were also started by lightning or carelessness, and they could race across the plains faster than a horse. Catlin wrote vividly about them in his Letters, and a fire title promises a strong effect of light and speed. The title says burning meadows and does not say whose fire it was.","c":"Fire was a subject that could carry Catlin beyond his usual calm, and his prairie-fire pictures are known for their theatrical sky and glow. In the small card format the effect is likely to be reduced, as it is for all his large subjects: the rectangle holds the blaze like a sample, and the viewer must imagine the roar. The subject promises more heat than most of the series delivers."},"nga:50503":{"d":"A river flows from near to far down the center of the picture, between hills, with a large conical hill rising in the distance. The brushstrokes are smooth, and the colors are green for the vegetation, muted earth tones for the hill and soft tones for the sky. A small canoe with figures floats near the river in the middle distance. The card is smaller and wider than most in the series, about 38 by 59 centimeters.","i":"The title names a landmark, Pike's Tent, presumably for the conical hill in the distance, and presumably after Zebulon Pike, the army officer who explored the upper Mississippi in 1805 and 1806. Rivers were the highways of the fur trade and of travel in the old Northwest, and a canoe on the water shows their use. Catlin's river landscapes of the 1830s record a country soon to be settled.","c":"The conical hill is a strong, simple form, and the river's perspective leading toward it is orderly and calm. The tiny canoe is a nice scale marker. This is a landscape without drama, in the same quiet dialect as Catlin's other river scenes, and it will please a viewer who enjoys clear recession and green hills more than incident."},"nga:50504":{"d":"A wide river winds into the distance, seen from an elevated viewpoint with a low horizon, so that the sky takes up the upper half of the picture in a blend of blue with hints of pink and gray clouds. Distant trees frame the river at the horizon. On the left, dense clusters of tall trees stand close to the viewer; on the right, a line of trees marks the opposite bank. Browns and grays of the riverbanks and driftwood give the picture an earthy base.","i":"The Lower Mississippi, below the mouth of the Ohio, was a broad, slow, muddy river bordered by forest and swamp, crowded with steamboats, flatboats and driftwood. Catlin traveled on it during his western journeys of the 1830s, and a river view like this recalls the highway of his travels. The title names no Native subjects, and the picture appears to be a pure river view.","c":"The varied brushwork, soft in sky and river and sharper in the trees, gives the picture an impressionistic quality unusual in the series, and the sky that fills the upper half is a pleasing atmospheric expanse. The tall trees at left frame the view well. It is a quiet, wide, unpeopled river; its virtue is breadth, and its weakness is that nothing in it asks to be remembered."},"nga:50505":{"d":"Three men are in the foreground of a landscape: one aims a rifle, and the others are among the grass. In the background a herd of bison grazes in a sunlit meadow, with hills and trees on the horizon and a clear sky of light brushstrokes. The palette is dominated by greens and browns, with trees at the right. The painter's title includes himself among the three, as an active hunter.","i":"Catlin hunted on his western journeys and wrote of buffalo hunting as a pleasure and a necessity, since fresh meat fed the travelers. By showing himself with a rifle he places himself among the hunters and not only as an observer, and the two companions, whom the title does not name, may be fellow travelers or guides. Catlin himself proposed a park to protect the buffalo, which gives the scene an uneasy edge.","c":"The quiet of the moment is the picture's merit: the herd grazes, the rifle is leveled, nothing has happened yet, and the sunlit meadow gives the hunt an almost pastoral calm. The three men are tiny and sketchy, so the idea of Catlin the hunter has to be taken from the title. As a self-portrait it is modest to the point of invisibility."},"nga:50506":{"d":"A Native settlement stands beside the sea, seen from a slightly raised viewpoint, with the horizon in the upper third where the sea meets the sky in a warm golden light. Tents and other structures hold people engaged in various activities, and more figures can be seen in the distance on the water. The brushwork is smooth in sky and water, and the palette is earthy browns and greens, soft blues and gold.","i":"The Nayas of the title are not matched with certainty to a modern people, and the title places their village by the sea, with bathing as the activity. Coastal peoples lived by fishing, shellfish gathering and sea travel, and their villages sat on shores where canoes could be drawn up.","c":"The golden horizon over the sea is the warm heart of the card, and the smooth sky and water are among the best of its passages. The bathers are too small to matter, and the tents and structures are a vague cluster; the geography is hinted at rather than established. It is a pleasant coastal view with an honest title and an elusive subject."},"nga:50507":{"d":"Three riders on horseback cross a prairie of grass and rocks under a sky blending blue and peach. The central figure raises his hands, the rider on a pale horse seems unbalanced, and the third carries a bow. The scene is framed within an oval with a green border. The title describes a reversal, a man who had taken a scalp being scalped himself, and the raised hands and the unbalanced rider suggest a moment of crisis.","i":"Scalping was practiced by many Native nations as a way of taking a trophy of victory, and its spread was also encouraged by bounties that European colonies and governments paid for scalps. Pawnee and Cheyenne were long-standing enemies on the central plains. The title is Catlin's, and its moral is retributive: the man who took a scalp has lost his own. The specifics of the incident are unknown.","c":"A rare moment of crisis for the series: the raised hands of the central rider and the lurching pale horse give the picture an instability that is exactly right for the title. The oval, however, locks the action in a decorous frame, and the peach and blue sky is serene when the picture is not. It has the ingredients of drama without quite delivering the shock."},"nga:50508":{"d":"Numerous conical tents are arranged as a village on open ground, with a line of trees at the horizon and low hills and a body of water beyond. The sky shades from golden yellow to soft blue, casting a soft light over the scene. Figures and tents are scattered across the landscape, indicating human activity. The palette is mainly warm and earthy. It is a small landscape in oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The conical tents may be tipis, used by the Plains-going Apache bands, since the Apache of the desert Southwest more often lived in brush-covered wickiups. The title's village borrows a word from European settlement for what were mobile camps. The Jicarilla, who lived in the mountains and plains of northern New Mexico, hunted buffalo and traded with the Pueblo town of Taos.","c":"The golden sky is the warm center of an otherwise standard encampment view, and the body of water behind adds a welcome distance. The scattered tents and figures read as a settled place, but they are evenly lit and evenly drawn, with none of the singularity of a real village. The picture is serene and polite, and could serve as a village of almost any nation."},"nga:50509":{"d":"Rolling hills and mountain peaks extend into the distance, painted in smooth, somewhat blended strokes. The palette is muted earth tones: greens, browns and the white of snow on the peaks. The sky runs from light blue to dark gray, and clusters of trees stand close to the viewer. The mountains are the subject, and the picture is a rare pure mountain view in Catlin's work.","i":"The Salmon River Mountains of central Idaho are among the most rugged ranges in the lower forty-eight states, cut by the Salmon River, which Lewis and Clark found impassable in 1805 and turned back from. A pure mountain landscape was a rare thing in Catlin's work, which centered on people.","c":"The mountains rise in believable layers, the snow gives them a clean cap, and the darkening sky adds weather to a series that mostly has none. The foreground trees are a bit stiff and the blending a bit soft, but the picture shows what Catlin could do with a high country. It is not great mountain painting by the standards of Bierstadt or Church, but it is sincere and unaffected."},"nga:50510":{"d":"A group stands outdoors on grass under a pale blue sky, framed by white paper and a pale-green frame. At the center a person with intricate red-and-white body paint, otherwise unclothed, faces the viewer, with shoes, a shield-like object, a quiver and a curved object lying around the feet. A line of people behind, in tan shawls and tunics, some with red, white and black feathers in dark hair, wear necklaces and earrings; a child at far right balances a pot on the head, and a person at left carries a spear and a fan of feathers.","i":"The title is a quoted request, and Catlin's writings describe sitters who competed to be painted first as well as those who feared the portrait would take their life. The central figure's body paint and the objects set around the feet, shield, quiver and weapon, suggest a warrior displaying himself in full array to be recorded. The title does not name the speaker.","c":"The central painted figure with his possessions arranged at his feet is the card's most original conceit, a portrait within the portrait that makes the sitter the author of his own image. The crowd behind, with the child's pot and the spear and feather fan, adds a lively supporting cast. The idea outstrips the painting, which is smooth and generalized, but the picture has the rare quality of being about the act of looking."},"nga:50511":{"d":"Green hills adorned with trees and bushes rise in the background of a landscape, their soft contours made by smoothly blended brushstrokes. The dominant colors are green and blue, with hints of pink in the sky. A group of people is seated in the foreground, small beneath the bluffs. The picture is a river-country view, a place portrait with a human note.","i":"The Nishnabotna is a river of western Iowa and northwest Missouri that enters the Missouri River from the east, and its mouth is marked by the high wooded bluffs of the Missouri valley, built of wind-deposited loess. Catlin passed it on his voyage upriver in 1832. The seated group in the foreground is not identified.","c":"A gentle picture whose green and blue hills are tidily composed and whose seated group adds a note of rest. Its virtue is calm; its weakness is that calm is all it has. There is little to distinguish these bluffs from other grassy hills in the series except the title, and the picture trusts the viewer to supply the river and the journey."},"nga:50512":{"d":"A bison stands in a grassy landscape with three horse-mounted hunters flanking it from different angles. The horses are in motion, their harnesses suggesting speed, and the bison is detailed, with textured fur and a powerful presence. The sky above is in lighter tones. The three-way composition fixes the animal at the center and turns the picture into a study of the moment when the hunters converge.","i":"The Comanche hunted buffalo from horseback with the lance and the bow, riding close beside the animal to strike behind the ribs, and a good buffalo horse would hold its pace within a few feet of the horns. Catlin saw Comanche hunters in 1834 and wrote with admiration of their skill. The three-way approach in the picture shows the hunters isolating a single bull.","c":"Three riders converging on one animal is a strong, clear design, and the bull's shaggy bulk is among the best-observed things in the later series. The horses' motion is competent if stiff. The sense of danger is slighter than in Catlin's chase pictures of the 1830s, but the focus, the triangle of hunters about a single beast, makes this one of the most coherent buffalo pictures in the group."},"nga:50513":{"d":"A wounded bison stands in a prairie landscape, with other bison in the distance. The style concentrates on detail and texture in the fur and grass. The palette is earthy, with the dark browns of the animal and the muted greens and yellows of the grassland under a sky that moves from turquoise to gray. The scene conveys a sense of openness and solitude, the animal alone in the foreground with the herd far behind.","i":"A wounded bull at bay is a subject Catlin returned to, describing the old bull's stoic dignity and the danger it posed to hunters. The scene is a hunting moment seen from the animal's side, and it carries a sympathy that matches Catlin's admiration of the buffalo as a noble creature. The herds, which numbered in the millions in his time, were nearly exterminated by the 1880s.","c":"Catlin lets the animal be the subject, and the result is one of the few pictures in which a beast has personality: the bull's low head and dark bulk make a sorrowful, upright presence against the open grass. The turquoise-to-gray sky is lovely and quiet. The emotion is carried by the stillness and not by action, and it is stronger for being understated."},"nga:50514":{"d":"A lone bison, dying according to the title, stands or lies on a grassy plain, seen from a viewpoint slightly above it, with the horizon set high in the frame. Above the horizon the sky is painted with soft brushstrokes in gray, blue and faint pink. The animal's fur and the grass are in earthy browns and greens, set against the cooler tones of the sky. Nothing else appears: no herd, no hunter, only the animal and the wide land.","i":"Catlin deplored the waste of the buffalo hunt carried on by whites, who killed for tongues and robes and left the carcasses on the prairie, and he often wrote of the animal as the companion of the Native peoples. A solitary dying bull is therefore as much a statement as a scene. By the 1880s the great herds had been nearly wiped out, and this picture, made in the 1860s, looks forward to that loss.","c":"A single animal on an open plain is a bold simplification, and the soft gray, blue and pink sky gives the picture a hushed, elegiac tone that suits its title. The painting is plain: the bull is described more than felt, and the pale sky is the more expressive of the two halves. But the idea is strong and the quiet is real. Here Catlin's sympathy for the animal does the work of drama."},"nga:50515":{"d":"A sandy shore holds people and conical tents, with a river curving toward the horizon, where a strip of blue shows sea or sky. Along the river stand structures resembling houses. The brushstrokes are fine and deliberate, capturing detail, and the palette is earthy greens and browns against blue sky and water. The picture sets a Native camp beside a young town, so that natural and human elements coexist in a single view.","i":"Chicago in 1837 was a raw town of a few thousand people at the mouth of the Chicago River on Lake Michigan, incorporated as a city that year, built on land that Native peoples, chiefly the Potawatomi, had held until the Treaty of Chicago in 1833 forced their removal. The tents on the sand are a reminder of those people in a landscape that was being transformed. Catlin's title records the year as a marker of how fast the change was.","c":"The coexistence is the interest: tents on the sand, houses by the river, the horizon opening onto the lake. Catlin sets them side by side without comment, and the juxtaposition is more telling than any sermon. Pictorially the view is modest, fine and slightly dry, with the buildings reading as small blocks. It has the value of a witness to a place about to become unrecognizable."},"nga:50516":{"d":"A view of the Platte River prairies, titled as pasturage: an open plain on which, as the title and the themes suggest, mammals graze, probably buffalo, with a distant range and perhaps a few people. The Platte country of Nebraska was the heart of the buffalo range. It is a small oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters, in Catlin's wide, calm manner for prairies.","i":"The Platte, a broad and shallow river rising in the Rockies and crossing Nebraska, was the highway of the westward emigrants of the 1840s and 1850s and the grazing ground of enormous buffalo herds. Catlin's phrase American Pasturage suggests his view of the plains as a natural pasture, a landscape that could support animals and peoples without the plow, and he feared that settlement would end it. The title is his.","c":"Catlin's prairie views are at their best when the land itself is the subject: calm, wide and quietly colored. The title here makes an argument, that the open grass is a natural pasture which the plow would waste. The idea gives the picture a purpose beyond its beauty, and it is one of the few in the series with a point that looks forward as well as back."},"nga:50517":{"d":"A dense forest of tall trees forms a canopy over the scene. Human figures walk along a ridge, and below, at the foot of a cliff, a canoe with figures paddles along a river. The palette is green and brown. The trees close in on the view, leaving the river and the ridge as the only ways through, and the people are small against the great trunks.","i":"The Lower Mississippi flows through a country of high bluffs, cypress swamp and forest, and for the Native peoples of the South, the Natchez, Choctaw, Chickasaw and others, it was a highway. By Catlin's time the Southeastern nations were being removed from their homelands to Indian Territory, and a picture of Native figures and a canoe on the river recalls what was being lost. The title names no nation.","c":"The canopy of tall trees is a convincing, enclosed space, and the tiny walkers on the ridge and the paddlers in the canoe give a sense of scale. The picture lacks a strong center, and the greens and browns are of a single note. It is a competent forest scene, calm and unemphatic; its pleasure is the quiet of the river under the bluff."},"nga:50518":{"d":"A grassy landscape is seen from a slightly raised viewpoint, with the horizon near the upper third of the frame and a vast sky with light clouds. In the distance a river winds through the land, bordered by trees. Bison are scattered across the grassy hill in the foreground, and more move toward the horizon. The brushstrokes are smooth, creating a realistic, detailed depiction, and the palette is lush greens, browns and a vibrant blue sky.","i":"Cedar Bluffs is a common place name along the Missouri for hills dotted with red cedar, a dark evergreen against the grass, and the title does not say which of them is meant. The bison on the hill were the animals on which the Plains peoples depended. Catlin passed through such country on his voyage up the Missouri in 1832, and the herd is the natural inhabitant of the scene.","c":"The scattering of bison on the hill, with more streaming toward the horizon, is the right solution to a herd: a sense of numbers without crowding. The river's distant winding and the vibrant sky give the picture clarity and breadth. It is among the more harmonious of Catlin's landscapes with animals, though the harmony comes at the cost of drama: nothing disturbs the herd or the viewer."},"nga:50519":{"d":"A scene of Caddo people gathering wild strawberries, with trees in the setting, on a card wider than most of Catlin's, about 47 by 73 centimeters, which suggests a broad, panoramic composition. The subject is seasonal work in woods or prairie, a gentle theme of harvest and not of war, probably painted with small figures in a wide landscape, in oil on card.","i":"The Caddo were farmers of the Red River and east Texas, builders of mounds and large grass-thatched houses, and wild strawberries ripened in spring in woods and meadows, an early fruit in the seasonal round. Gathering fruit was commonly women's and children's work. Caddo potters made some of the finest engraved vessels of the southern woodlands.","c":"Gathering fruit is a gentler subject than the hunt, and the unusually wide card suggests that Catlin gave it room to breathe, which is the right instinct for a landscape with small figures. His quiet, orderly manner suits harvest scenes better than combat scenes, and this is a welcome change of pace in a series crowded with weapons."},"nga:50520":{"d":"A rolling green hill dominates the scene under a high horizon, with a line of marching figures extending into the distance and creating depth. In the foreground a line of figures carrying shields or circular objects marches in unison across the grass. The sky is a mix of blue and gray with scattered clouds. The brushwork is smooth and meticulous, delineating the forms with precision, and the palette is earthy greens and browns, the figures in reddish-brown attire.","i":"Indian file is the old term for single file, the way parties of Native men were said to travel when crossing country, each stepping in the tracks of the one before so that the size of the party could not be told. The Iowa, a Siouan-speaking people of the Missouri and Mississippi country, appear in the title. A column of shield bearers suggests a war party or a ceremonial march; the picture does not say which.","c":"A long file of figures winding into the distance is an effective way to show a procession: the diminishing line gives depth and a sense of purpose, and the shields make a repeated pattern of circles. The rolling hill is the picture's quiet strength. The figures near and far are alike, and a single anecdote would have raised the interest. As design it is clean; as an event it is blank."},"nga:50521":{"d":"A vast landscape is divided by a horizon line between sky and prairie. Large herds of buffalo are scattered across the plains as dark silhouettes, and the ground is covered with green vegetation and trees. In the foreground, wolves gather around a fallen buffalo, depicted in detail. The brushstrokes suggest movement, and the earthy greens and browns of the ground contrast with the pastel blues and pinks of the sky.","i":"Buffalo sometimes became mired in mud, quicksand or bog when crossing rivers and wet ground, and wolves, which followed the herds, gathered at such a victim. Catlin described the wolves of the prairie, white and gray, as constant companions of the herds, feeding on the sick and the fallen. The picture is a small natural history of the plains, with predators and prey and no human being.","c":"The wolves around the fallen animal are the card's best-observed passage, a small, grim cluster in detail while the herds beyond remain silhouettes. The pastel sky is calm and indifferent, which is the right tone for the subject. It is one of the more natural-historical pictures in the series, and the quietness of the dying animal and the waiting wolves feels truthful."},"nga:50522":{"d":"A beach is crowded with numerous small figures at work on the sand, and the sea stretches to the horizon, with clouds and a sailboat. The sky grades from deep orange near the horizon to softer blues and yellows above, and the water is cooler in tone. The palette is harmonious: warm earth tones in the sand, vibrant blues and oranges in sky and sea. The title explains the activity: people gather at a whale stranded on the shore.","i":"The Klahoquaht, usually identified with Clayoquot, were one of the Nuu-chah-nulth peoples of Vancouver Island who hunted whales from canoes, a great feat of skill and ritual. A whale stranded on the beach was a windfall: the whole community gathered to cut up the blubber and meat, which was shared among households. The sailboat on the horizon is a reminder of the European traders who already visited the coast.","c":"The sunset sky over the sea is the picture's glory, an orange band opening into soft blue and yellow, and the small figures on the sand give it a lively human industry. The sailboat on the horizon is a nice touch, a hint of the wider world. The figures are tiny, and the event of the title must be taken on trust: the picture reads first as a beach at evening."},"nga:50523":{"d":"A large wooden canoe takes shape in a dense forest, seen from a slightly raised viewpoint under tall trees that hide the horizon. Several figures carve and hollow out a log lying on the ground, while others do different tasks around them. The brushwork is smooth, with careful attention to leaves and branches, and the palette is rich and earthy: greens, browns and soft reds and tans. The forest background is lush, its greens and browns blending into one another.","i":"Large dugout canoes, hollowed from a single log, were the chief means of travel for the peoples of the Pacific Northwest coast and of many river peoples elsewhere; shaping a big hull took weeks, with adzes and, on the Northwest Coast, steam to spread the sides. Which people Catlin meant by Nayas is uncertain. The picture records skilled communal work and the forest that supplied its materials.","c":"The canoe makers at work are a good subject for a painter of process, and the forest, with its leaves and branches carefully drawn, is among the better settings in the late series. The figures are small among the trees and the pace of the work is hard to feel, but the picture has a purposeful, orderly quality that suits its subject: the hull in the making is the center of the composition."},"nga:50524":{"d":"In a forest clearing near water, a group of people carries a large wooden canoe. The horizon is low, with lush green vegetation and tall trees behind. The palette is earthy greens and browns with the blue of the water, and some figures wear red garments. The red clothes pick out the carriers against the green, and the long hull of the canoe is the clear subject of the picture.","i":"A new canoe was a major investment of labor, and its first launch could be an occasion of celebration; on the Northwest Coast the largest canoes carried dozens of people and were owned by chiefs or kin groups. Carrying a hull from the forest to the water was a communal task, as the group of figures here shows. The title says only Nayas Indians.","c":"The red garments against the green are the picture's best device, tying the carriers into a visible chain, and the water at the edge of the clearing gives a destination to the action. It is a lively subject, but the figures are small, and the sense of weight, the hull's heavy burden, is not communicated. A pleasing idea, a little too tidy."},"nga:50525":{"d":"A semicircle of figures in feathered headdresses and blankets sits around a central figure. Low vegetation and clouds appear on the horizon, with soft, blended brushstrokes throughout. The palette is earthy for the figures and a tree, and lighter blues and whites for the sky and clouds. The arrangement is a ring that opens toward the viewer, so that the council is seen as one sees a stage.","i":"A council was the Sioux way of deciding: leaders and men of standing sat in a circle, smoked the pipe, and spoke in turn, with decisions reached by agreement and not by vote. The central figure may be the speaker, or the chief who has called the meeting. Catlin witnessed councils on several of his journeys in the 1830s, and the title names no occasion.","c":"The semicircle is a satisfying form, and its opening toward the viewer invites a seat in the circle. The blankets and feathers give a rich mix of color, and the cloud-filled sky gives dignity. The figures, as usual, are small and alike, and the central figure is hardly distinguished; the council is more an arrangement than a debate."},"nga:50526":{"d":"A group of bison moves across the scene, some in a trench and others on the grassy landscape, seen from a slightly raised viewpoint that gives a wide view. The horizon is high, with hills, trees and a vast sky beyond. The brushstrokes give detailed texture to the bison and the landscape, and earthy tones of brown, green and soft blue dominate. The scene conveys movement and the vastness of the open plains, and the title promises bears among the herd.","i":"Catlin reported that grizzly bears would attack buffalo on the upper Missouri, singling out a bull and bringing it down by weight and strength. The grizzly was the dominant predator of the northern plains and hills, and Plains peoples respected and feared it. The incident is likely drawn from reports as much as from his own sight, and the trench in the ground may be a buffalo trail or a worn gully.","c":"The pursuit and the swirl of bison in motion give the scene a sense of commotion that Catlin's quieter pictures lack, and the high horizon makes a convincing stage. The bears, if they are small in the frame, do not dominate the picture as the title suggests, and the trench draws the eye more than the attack does. It is an energetic landscape of animals with an incident folded in."},"nga:50527":{"d":"A landscape from a slight height looks over a settlement of small wooden structures surrounded by lush trees. Tree trunks lie arranged to resemble canoes, with people and structures nearby, and figures move about. In the background a wide expanse of water leads toward distant landmasses, with the horizon in the upper third. The sky, painted in expressive strokes of yellow and soft green, suggests dawn or dusk, and the foreground is earthy greens and browns.","i":"Villages of wooden houses beside a sheltered sea, with canoes drawn up on the shore, are typical of the Pacific Northwest coast, where cedar forests supplied planks, posts and canoes and the water supplied food and travel. Nayas is a name Catlin used for a people not matched with certainty to a modern one. The title chooses an hour of rich light that suited his taste.","c":"The sky of yellow and soft green, painted with expressive strokes, is the best part, and the water leading to distant islands gives a real feeling of open space. The wooden houses and canoes make a more particular setting than most of Catlin's villages, and the trees close them in. The figures are as small as ever. It is one of the more atmospheric village views of the late series."},"nga:50528":{"d":"A circular frame holds a nighttime landscape seen at a slight downward angle. A group of figures gathers around a small campfire, with silhouettes of trees rising on the left and blending into the night sky. In the lower part of the frame a vast expanse of water, a lake or the sea, meets the sky and partly reflects the moonlight through clouds. The palette is deep blues and blacks with subtle crimson from the campfire, and the brushstrokes are smooth.","i":"A night scene lit by a single small fire is a challenge to a painter, and Catlin made several nocturnes, including torchlight fishing and ceremonies. Fires on a shore served for warmth, cooking and light, and for the peoples of the coast the evening was a time for stories and gathering. The title names the Nayas and nothing more about the people or the place.","c":"The deep blue and black, with the red of the campfire a small warm center, give this card a calm, hushed stillness that is a pleasure after brighter daytime views, and the moonlit water adds distance. The circular frame suits the lamplight effect. The group around the fire is a few small shapes, and the picture's success is entirely in the mood. As a nocturne it is among the best of its group."},"nga:50529":{"d":"A coastal scene is seen from a raised viewpoint, the horizon dividing sky and sea, with a sailboat on the horizon. On the left tall trees with thin trunks and lush foliage rise, and below them figures stand on a cliff overlooking more figures on the shoreline. The palette runs from greens and browns to the yellows and oranges of a sky with scattered clouds. The cliff and the people below suggest the use of a ladder, as the title says.","i":"A notched log serving as a ladder was among the simplest and most widespread of Native tools, used to climb cliffs, to enter houses through roof openings and to reach storage and lookouts. The title makes the ladder the subject, an ordinary device raised into a picture. Nayas is Catlin's name for a people whose modern identity is uncertain, and the ladder's place is not named.","c":"The vantage over cliff, shore and sea is generous, and the sailboat on the horizon, with the orange sky, gives the card a rewarding depth. The ladder itself is a quiet hero: a plain thing that makes the descent plausible. The tall trees at the left frame the view neatly. It is a pleasant coastal picture whose subject is practical and modest, and whose execution is the same."},"nga:50530":{"d":"Five people stand in varied positions against a light, neutral backdrop with a hint of blue and sparse vegetation. They have distinctive faces, long black hair styled with feathers and minimal clothing made from animal skins, and some have painted patterns or tattoos on their bodies. They hold objects such as spears or staffs adorned with feathers. The picture is a group portrait in Catlin's South American series, about 47 by 62 centimeters, in oil on card.","i":"Carib is the name of a large family of peoples of northern South America, from the Guianas and the Orinoco, and of the island Caribs of the Lesser Antilles, who gave their name to the Caribbean Sea. Catlin's South American pictures belong to journeys of the 1850s that are known mainly from his own later writing, and the museum dates this panel from 1854 to 1869. The title does not say where he met these sitters.","c":"Five figures in varied positions give the group more movement than the usual row, and the painted patterns and feather ornaments are the interest. Catlin's South American cards carry over the formula of the North American ones: the pale ground, the staffs, the careful costume. What they lose is the specificity of place; the sparse vegetation and neutral backdrop might be anywhere, and the title does the work of locating the picture."},"nga:50531":{"d":"Three people are shown full length. The one at far left leans against a rocky surface holding a bow, with long dark hair adorned with feathers and red fabric around the waist. The central figure stands holding a bow, wearing a fringed garment and feathers in the hair. The one at right poses with one knee raised, holding a spear, with feathered dark hair, jewelry and light-colored fabric around the waist. The background is a painted scene of soft brown and green tones, with foliage on the left side.","i":"Woyaway appears in nineteenth-century accounts of the Guiana interior, where the name was used for peoples of the upper Essequibo country; its modern equivalent is uncertain. The three bows and spear are the equipment of the hunter in the forest. The rock at left and the raised knee give the group more varied poses than most of the South American cards.","c":"The leaning figure and the raised knee give these men a casual ease, and the bows, the fringes and the feathers make a nice repeated vocabulary. The foliage at the left suggests a forest edge. The picture is neat and pleasant, in the manner of the whole group, but it remains an inventory of equipment more than of individuals."},"nga:50532":{"d":"Side by side and full length, three people face the viewer. The two on the left are men with long dark hair, body adornments and colorful headdresses, one holding a bow and arrows; the one on the right is a woman with long braided hair, necklaces and a patterned shawl. Behind them stands a structure resembling a traditional dwelling, with figures beside it. The ground is grassy and the sky cloudy, on a brown and green ground. The card is slightly narrower than most, about 46 by 60 centimeters.","i":"The Taruma lived in the forests and savannas of northern Brazil and Guyana, in the region of the Rio Negro and the Essequibo, and by the nineteenth century were a small people. The dwelling in the background places the group at home, and the shawl is perhaps European cloth, a sign of trade.","c":"The dwelling in the background is the most informative thing in the card, since it gives the sitters a home, and the braided hair and patterned shawl of the woman make a good contrast with the men's headdresses. The faces are generalized and the landscape a few strokes. It is a modest, orderly group whose backdrop adds more than most."},"nga:50533":{"d":"Four people stand against a neutral background of subtle earth tones and light foliage, in a simple green border. Two adults at left stand with a child, one holding a spear with feathers; the fourth, an adult, sits or crouches on a rock at the right. All have dark shoulder-length hair with red and white feathers and strong features, and wear short wraps or cloths and necklaces, one with arm ornaments.","i":"Goo-a-give is Catlin's spelling of a name that cannot now be matched with certainty to a known people, and the same name appears on another card in the series, a dance. His South American pictures of 1854 to 1869 rest on journeys known mainly from his own later writing. The red and white feathers are the main shared decoration, which suggests a common style of dress for the group.","c":"The red and white feathers repeated on every head give the group a strong unity, and the seated figure on the rock breaks the line at the right. The faces are strong, as Catlin's often are, and the minimal clothing allows the body ornaments to stand out. But the plain ground and the uncertain identity limit the picture to a pleasing arrangement."},"nga:50534":{"d":"Four people, three adults and a child, stand with their full bodies visible against a gradient sky and a grassy landscape. The child stands at left holding a stick, and an adult male beside him holds a spear. Another male in the center holds a staff and a shield, and a woman sits at the right. The seated figure and the child at either end give the row a change of height, and the staff and shield make its center.","i":"The Arawak, or Lokono, lived along the coasts and rivers of the Guianas, farming cassava and fishing, and belong to the great Arawakan language family that stretched from the Caribbean to the Amazon; the Taino whom Columbus met were related. By the nineteenth century the Guiana Arawaks were trading with Europeans and influenced by missions. Catlin's title spells the name Arowak.","c":"A child at one end of the row and a seated woman at the other give the group some variety of height, and the staff and shield in the center make a firm axis. The gradient sky is gentle. Beyond that it repeats the series' habits: frontal figures, minimal landscape and no particular action. It is a decent summary card."},"nga:50535":{"d":"A ring of people stands on grass, with a forest behind and a sky of soft clouds. Most wear distinctive headwear of colorful feathers. The central figure stands slightly apart from the group, with a more elaborate headdress and additional body adornments. The forest is lush, and the effect of the picture is communal: a ring of people around a leader, set in a serene and harmonious backdrop.","i":"Zurumati is Catlin's name for a people that has not been matched with certainty to a modern one, and it appears in several of the South American cards. A ring of figures around a central leader in a more elaborate headdress suggests a chief or ritual figure addressing the group, though the title does not say. Catlin's own later writing is the main source for his South American travels.","c":"A ring around a central figure is a more social composition than the lineups of the series, and the dense forest behind, with its soft clouds, gives the group a natural enclosure. The feathers are the color. The faces and the leader's elaborate dress are treated only in a general way, and the identity of the people is a puzzle: it is a picture in which the title does as much as the paint."},"nga:50536":{"d":"A simple background of soft blue and brown tones, grassy ground and a muted green border frame three people standing side by side. The one on the left holds a wooden object and a long bow, with shoulder-length dark hair and colorful feathers, a necklace and minimal clothing. The central figure holds a large ceremonial staff, with feathers, a beaded necklace and a loincloth. The one on the right holds a long spear and wears a necklace and loincloth. All are barefoot.","i":"Zurumati is Catlin's name for a people not matched with certainty to a modern one. The staff held by the central figure, large and ceremonial, marks him as a leader or ritual specialist, while the bow and spear are the arms of hunting and war. The barefoot figures and loincloths are typical of Catlin's depiction of the forest peoples of lowland South America.","c":"A trio of bow, staff and spear is a neat set of verticals, and the soft blue and brown ground makes a pleasing, quiet setting. The central staff is the focus. But the figures are stock and identical in dress, and the picture could be exchanged with others in the series without much loss. It is orderly and decorative."},"nga:50537":{"d":"Four people stand next to each other against a background of blue-gray cloud-like forms fading into light tan with a greenish tint around the edges. The first has short dark hair and raised arms, the second short dark hair and arms akimbo. The third holds a shield and spear and wears a feathered headdress, a small warrior in costume. The fourth has arms resting in front. The poses are varied and lively for this series.","i":"A group of children is unusual in Catlin's gallery, which is mostly adult. The poses, raised arms, hands on hips, and a shield and spear, suggest a lively moment of play or display. Zurumati is Catlin's name for a people not matched with certainty to a modern one. Nothing more is known about the sitters.","c":"Children are the freshest subject in the South American group: the raised arms and akimbo pose give a spontaneity the adult rows never reach, and the small warrior with shield and spear is charming. The faint cloud-like ground is an unusual abstract backdrop. The faces are summary, but the animation is real. It is a small, lively picture."},"nga:50538":{"d":"Four people, shown head to toe, stand side by side against a gradient of earthy tones with a hint of grass, framed by a thick gray-green border. They have long dark hair adorned with colorful feathers and prominent cheekbones, and wear loincloths with various decorative adornments, necklaces and some arm and leg bands. The two on the left hold bows, one also an axe; the man second from the left holds a bow and an arrow in his left hand.","i":"The Macushi are a Carib-speaking people of the savannas on the borders of Guyana and Brazil, around the Rupununi and Rio Branco, who farmed cassava and traded; they were among the peoples whom explorers such as Robert Schomburgk met in the 1830s and 1840s. Catlin's title spells the name Macouchi. The bows and axe are the arms of the hunter and the forest clearer.","c":"The prominent cheekbones and the colorful feathers give the four faces more character than most in the late series, and the repeated bows make a strong rhythm. The border is thick and the ground is plain, so the figures stand like exhibits in a case. It is a decent, dutiful card, with a good eye for faces."},"nga:50539":{"d":"Six people stand in a row against a plain background framed by a muted teal border. The central figure is a man with dark hair adorned with feathers and shells, a necklace and a fringed skirt. Two women with long dark hair flank him, one with a pot on her head. Two young children stand between them in similar attire, and on the far right a young boy with feathers in his hair holds a bow.","i":"The pot balanced on one woman's head belongs to the daily work of carrying water, and the Conibo, a Panoan-speaking people of the Ucayali River, were known for fine pottery decorated with geometric designs. A household of six, with a boy holding a bow at the end, presents the family as a working unit. Catlin's title does not name the members or their relationships.","c":"Six figures in a row are a long line, but the children placed between the adults give it rhythm, and the pot on a woman's head is a welcome detail of work. The muted teal border is handsome. As in the other cards of the group, the ground is plain and the poses frontal, and the card's merit is in its details."},"nga:50540":{"d":"Two people ride a dark horse, both upright and facing forward. The one in front has defined features, dark eyes and mouth, feathers in the hair, minimal clothing and a lance. The one behind has straight dark hair and similar minimal clothing. The surroundings are tall grass with various small flowers, against a softly painted background of blue sky shading into muted earth tones, framed by a light gray-green border. The pair fill the card like a small equestrian monument.","i":"The Conibo, or Konibo, are a Panoan-speaking people of the Ucayali River in Peru, river travelers who lived by fishing, farming and trade in canoes, and are known today, with the Shipibo, for their geometric designs on cloth and pottery. A horse is an unexpected companion for a forest river people, and the title's bride and groom suggest a wedding procession; the source of the scene is unknown.","c":"A couple on one horse is a charming, affectionate arrangement, and the small flowers in the grass and the sky shading into earth give it a fresh pastoral note. The riders are stiff and the horse is more prop than animal, and the question of how a river people came by a horse hangs over the whole. It is a sweet, slightly puzzling card."},"nga:50541":{"d":"Three people stand next to each other against a softly painted, abstract outdoor landscape of green foliage and a neutral sky. The central figure has medium-length dark hair, a tall feathered headdress, painted chest designs, a waist covering, necklaces, bracelets and an arm band. At left a young figure with dark hair and facial markings wears minimal clothing with painted chest designs and bracelets. At right another, with feathered earrings, wears an elaborately beaded top, arm and ankle decorations and a patterned waist covering.","i":"Chetibo, or Setebo, are a Panoan-speaking people of the Ucayali River in eastern Peru, relatives of the Shipibo and Conibo, who lived by fishing, farming and hunting and were noted for their body painting and beadwork. The tall feathered headdress and painted designs are the dress of display. The title makes them a family, though the figures' relationships are not specified.","c":"The painted chest designs, the beaded top and the feathered earrings make this one of the most decorated cards of the group, and the central tall headdress gives the arrangement a clear peak. The abstract green and neutral setting is soft but featureless. As a record of adornment it is rich, and it is more convincing as dress than as family."},"nga:50542":{"d":"Four people in traditional clothing stand in a line against a soft green landscape. A child stands on the far left, and the adults wear decorative hair accessories. They are adorned with jewelry, and the woman carries a baby on her back. The picture is a family group in the familiar format of the series: a shallow row of full-length figures on a pale ground, in oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters, with the detail concentrated on dress and ornament.","i":"The Shipibo, whose name Catlin spells Sepibo, are a Panoan-speaking people of the Ucayali River in Peru, known for the geometric designs they paint on cloth, pottery and the body. Their patterned textiles are admired today as an important craft tradition. The woman carrying a baby on her back shows a way of carrying children that continues in the Amazon.","c":"A family in a row with a baby on the mother's back is the card's human center, and the soft green landscape is a gentle backdrop. It is modest and sympathetic, if generic: the geometric patterning for which the Shipibo are best known is the one thing a viewer might look for and not be sure to find."},"nga:50543":{"d":"Five people stand closely together against a soft blur of earthy and pastel shades. In the center a man wears a tall feather headdress, facial paint and necklaces. To his left stands a shorter man in a similar headdress and facial paint. Beside them a young boy wears a necklace and decorated bands. At the far left a woman holds a paddle-like object, and at the far right a young child crouches, holding a bow with an arrow poised to shoot.","i":"The Iquito are a Zaparoan-speaking people of the northeastern Peruvian Amazon, along the Nanay River, and the city of Iquitos takes its name from them. The paddle in the woman's hand is the tool of river life, and the child with a bow and arrow is learning the hunter's art. Catlin's South American pictures are dated 1854 to 1869.","c":"The crouching child with the arrow poised is the card's best moment, a small drama of intention at the edge of a placid group, and the tall feather headdresses give the center a rich vertical. The woman with the paddle adds an emblem of river life. The soft blur behind the figures makes the group look compressed, but crowded and pleasing."},"nga:50544":{"d":"A light sky with subtle clouds near the bottom backs a row of three people on grass. Each has shoulder-length dark hair with colorful feathers, minimal clothing and jewelry. One holds a feathered spear, while the others have decorative adornments. The group is simple and symmetrical, a quiet trio without a landscape to speak of, set against pale sky and a strip of grass.","i":"The Omagua lived along the upper Amazon in large river villages and were noted for flattening the foreheads of infants; their name comes from a Quechua word for flat head. Visited by Orellana's expedition in 1542, they were reduced in the following centuries by disease, slave raids and missions to a small remnant. Catlin's picture, made when the Omagua were few, cannot show the large towns the first Europeans described.","c":"Three men in a symmetrical row against a pale sky make a calm, simple picture, and the feathered spear gives it a point. The hair and the feathers are the main decoration. There is little to distinguish the men or to evoke the great river towns that made the Omagua notable, so the card is the thinnest kind of document, a type without a history."},"nga:50545":{"d":"A row of four people fills the card. The two adult men on the left hold spears; they have long black hair with feathers and wear grass skirts, the first with a shield, the second with a bow and quiver. Beside the second man stands a woman with a bare torso wearing necklaces, bracelets and a short skirt, her hair decorated. A young child stands next to her, unclothed, with short hair. The background is a serene sky with clouds in blue tones, and a green border frames the card.","i":"The Xingu, a great tributary of the Amazon in central Brazil, flows through a region of many peoples, including Carib-, Tupi-, Arawak- and Ge-speaking groups, and the title names the river and not a single nation. The grass skirts and shields are the equipment of forest dwellers, and the river was their road. Catlin's South American pictures rest on travels described in his own later writing.","c":"The grass skirts give this card a slightly more specific costume than most in the group, and the child beside the woman adds a small, tender note. The blue cloud background is soft and uninflected. As a record it is weakened by vagueness: a river is not a people. It is a pleasant group in the familiar frame."},"nga:50546":{"d":"In soft earthy colors, four people stand in a row on grass and greenery under a neutral sky. The first, at left, holds a spear with his back to the viewer. The second faces forward holding a paddle-like instrument, the third faces forward with hands at the sides, and the fourth faces forward with arms crossed. All wear necklaces, arm bands and leg bands and minimal clothing.","i":"Angustura is Catlin's spelling of Angostura, the Orinoco river town in Venezuela, now Ciudad Bolivar, and the title probably refers to the Indians of that region and not to a single people. The paddle marks the river-going life of the Orinoco peoples, who traveled and traded by canoe across a vast basin. The sources for Catlin's journeys along the Orinoco are chiefly his own writings.","c":"The back-turned figure with the spear at the left is the best idea, a device Catlin uses to break the row, and the crossed arms of the last figure end the line with a pause. Otherwise the picture is a standard lineup in earthy colors. The title names a town and not a people, and the picture reflects that vagueness."},"nga:50547":{"d":"Four people stand against an abstract, soft-hued background, with grassy ground beneath. At left a woman with long dark hair, in a shawl, holds a small unclothed child. In the center a man stands with a raised arm holding a spear. The adult on the right stands in a relaxed posture holding a bow. The raised spear arm of the central man is the one gesture of action in an otherwise quiet group.","i":"The Mura lived along the Madeira River and the lower Amazon in Brazil and had a fierce reputation among the Portuguese as river raiders, who attacked travelers in canoes. They resisted colonial rule for much of the eighteenth century and were reduced in that century and the next by disease and warfare. The family group here, a mother and child with two men, shows them domestic and calm.","c":"The raised spear of the central man gives the card a rare gesture of action, and the mother holding her unclothed child gives it a rare note of tenderness; the contrast between the warrior's pose and the domestic group is the picture's most interesting feature. The abstract background is soft and unspecific. It is a quiet card, undistinguished but not unattractive."},"nga:50548":{"d":"Several people are gathered in an outdoor setting by water, under an open sky, with grassy land. Two seated men at the center have long dark hair, one wearing a feathered headpiece; they have facial markings or tattoos, loincloths and beaded necklaces. A woman and a child stand to the left, and in the background two other figures, including a child, sit in a canoe on the water.","i":"Marahua appears in nineteenth-century accounts as a Panoan-speaking people of the upper Amazon, and the people's modern identity is uncertain. Marked faces, as in the picture's facial markings, were a sign of belonging for many Amazon peoples. The canoe in the background reflects their river life. Panoan peoples, among them the Shipibo and Conibo, are known for their body painting and bead ornaments.","c":"Seated men at the center, a woman and a child at the side and a canoe in the background make an unusually layered group, with a sense of place by the water that most of the South American cards lack. The facial markings and the beads are carefully noted. The execution remains soft and descriptive, but the composition is a step toward a scene and away from a lineup."},"nga:50549":{"d":"Five people stand in a natural setting: two adults and three children, in full-body view. In the center an adult man, bare-chested, with long black hair, colorful feathers and facial markings, wears a cloth at the waist and beaded jewelry, holding a long wooden paddle or staff. To his left a child with similar features holds a bow, feathers in the hair. At the right an adult woman with tied-back hair, a necklace and a draped cloth holds the hand of an unclothed child and carries another on her back. A gradient of light colors and a green border complete the card.","i":"Orejona is Spanish for big-eared, and early Spanish chroniclers applied the word, Orejones, to peoples of the Amazon who stretched their earlobes with large plugs; it was a nickname and not a self-name. The chief with a paddle or staff stands for leadership on the river. The picture does not say which people Catlin meant, and the name's modern equivalent is uncertain.","c":"A chief with three children and a mother is a full family tableau, and the child with a bow and the one carried on the mother's back give it a nice range of ages. The tall central figure with feathers is the clear anchor. The painted gradient behind is delicate and the green border neat. The picture works as a family portrait; as ethnography it is anonymous."},"nga:50550":{"d":"A group of people gathers in front of a simple shelter made from natural materials, against lush greenery. The treatment is realistic, with careful attention to the setting and the people, and the group seems relaxed and communal, an image of family or social bonds. The group is rendered in a general way, with attention to the whole and not to individual figures. The card is about 46 by 61 centimeters, in oil on card.","i":"The shelter is probably a thatch-roofed, open-sided house of the kind used across the lowland forests of South America, built from poles and palm leaves. Orejona, meaning big-eared in Spanish, was a name given by outsiders to peoples who wore large ear ornaments. The title does not identify a nation.","c":"A shelter with a group before it is a more natural arrangement than the lineups, and the lush greenery gives a context that most of the South American cards lack. With no visible focus, the picture rests on its sense of place, and the sense is a gentle one. It is a modest, plausible scene of lowland life."},"nga:50551":{"d":"Three men stand side by side, facing each other, against a warm gradient. The man on the left holds a spear and has short dark hair, high cheekbones and a determined expression; he wears a fringed garment, loose shorts and decorations on the ankles and biceps. The central man stands with hands on hips, in a feathered headdress and a fringed garment with a belt, a decorated shoulder strap and a quiver of arrows on his back. The man on the right holds a bow, with a shield on his back and bands on arms and neck.","i":"The Chayma, or Chaima, were a Carib-speaking people of the mountains and valleys of eastern Venezuela, around Cumana, who were gathered into Spanish missions in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Humboldt visited their missions in 1799 and wrote about them. By Catlin's day they were largely mission Indians, and the fringes and feathers shown here are probably Catlin's usual costume more than the dress of the Chayma.","c":"The central man's hands on his hips give him a swagger, and the facing arrangement of the three makes a conversation of the kind the series seldom stages. The warm gradient ground is attractive and suits the heat of the subject. The costumes, though, look like Catlin's standard repertoire, and the title promises a specificity that the dress does not provide."},"nga:50552":{"d":"A painterly, softly blurred background of greenery sets off a row of three people. The person on the left has tousled hair and holds a bow and arrow, with broad features and a strong jaw. The central figure has straight shoulder-length hair, a neutral expression and a bow. The figure on the right has hair parted in the middle in a bob style and stands with hands at the sides. All wear traditional clothing made from natural materials.","i":"The Gran Chaco is a vast lowland of dry forest and grass in Paraguay, Argentina and Bolivia, home to many peoples, among them the Toba, Mataco and Lengua, who hunted, fished and gathered and later raised horses. Catlin's title names the region and not a nation. Chaco groups had a reputation among Europeans as unconquered, and the picture does not say which people is meant.","c":"The tousled hair and strong jaw of the left figure and the parted, bobbed hair of the right one make more varied heads than usual, and the painterly green blur behind lends a soft atmosphere. The three are dressed plainly, so the card relies on faces and hair. It is a restrained portrait of a region more than of a people, and its restraint is attractive."},"nga:50553":{"d":"Five people stand side by side against a simple background in a light, natural tone. At left are a young child and an adult male, both in minimal clothing; in the center are two adorned adult males, and at right a woman holds an infant. The group is arranged in a straight row, with the adornment of the central men as the focus, and the woman and infant close the line at the right.","i":"The Payagua lived along the Paraguay River and were famed as canoemen who controlled the river, trading, fishing and raiding for centuries against the Spanish; by the nineteenth century they were reduced to a few hundred, and they were gone as a distinct people by the twentieth. The title says only members of the tribe. Catlin's group preserves a record of a people on the edge of disappearance.","c":"A straight row in plain light is Catlin at his most schematic, and the picture draws its interest from the history of its subject: the Payagua were masters of a river and are now almost unknown. The adorned men at the center give some richness, and the woman with an infant gives a human note. As a painting it is slight; as a record it is a good deal weightier than it looks."},"nga:50554":{"d":"A group of five individuals and a baby stands in a row against an earth-toned ground with a green border. In the center a man holds a stick and a bow; he has long dark hair with red and white feathers, distinct cheekbones and jewelry. A woman beside him holds a baby, and three other children stand to the left. All have long dark hair and wear beaded jewelry, and one child wears a feather.","i":"The Lengua, who call themselves Enxet, are a people of the Paraguayan Chaco, hunters, gatherers and fishers who lived in small bands across the dry forest and grassland. Leaders among the Lengua, as elsewhere in the Chaco, led by influence and example. The title records a chief with two wives and four children, and Catlin presents the large family as an ordinary household.","c":"A large family in a single row is a lot to handle, and Catlin handles it by simple arrangement: the man at the center, women and children flanking, and the red and white feathers repeating through the group. The jewelry gives some richness and the green border a neat finish. It is a clear, unfussy family record, and its effect depends on the numbers."},"nga:50555":{"d":"A group portrait of a Lengua medicine man with two warriors, with trees in the background, using the same grouping Catlin used for the Plains peoples. It is a small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters, and in this format Catlin usually stood the three figures full length, in a shallow space, with the medicine man's regalia and the warriors' weapons carefully noted.","i":"A medicine man, in Catlin's usage, is a healer and keeper of spiritual power, and among the peoples of the Chaco such shamans cured the sick, spoke with spirits and foretold events, with an authority that could rival the chief's. The Lengua lived in the Paraguayan Chaco. The two warriors are not named.","c":"A medicine man flanked by warriors is Catlin's formula for a people's leadership, and its use in South America shows how readily he carried the pattern across the continent. The pattern promises the usual orderly trio, with the shaman's regalia as its chief interest. It is more a statement of method than a picture to look at closely."},"nga:50556":{"d":"Against an open landscape of soft colors, four people stand in various poses. The one on the left has a hand on the hip, the second holds a spear, the third turns his back to the viewer, and the fourth also holds a spear. All have dark hair and wear traditional clothing with adornments. The varied poses, with one figure turned away, make the row livelier than usual.","i":"The Botocudo, as the Portuguese called them, were hunter-gatherers of the Atlantic forests of eastern Brazil who moved in small bands, and who resisted settlers into the nineteenth century, when many were killed or driven out. The back-turned figure and the spears give the group the look of men in the field; the title names only the people.","c":"The figure turned away from the viewer and the hand on the hip break the lineup into something closer to a conversation, and the spears give the group a rhythm of verticals. The open landscape is soft and vague. The idea of variety is welcome, though the cards of this group use it often; a pleasant, brisk card in a familiar manner."},"nga:50557":{"d":"In a softly painted setting of light blues and beiges, three people are shown in full. On the left a person with long dark hair, in a red garment and red leg coverings, holds a paddle and a basket. In the center another with dark hair and large circular earrings stands with a spear, draped in an orange-red textile. On the right a person with dark hair, in a shorter garment with red accents, holds a stick-like object.","i":"Botocudo is the Portuguese name, from botoque, a wooden plug, for the peoples of eastern Brazil who wore large wooden discs in their lips and ears; the Krenak and other Ge-speaking groups are their modern descendants. The large circular earrings here may be a hint of the ear discs. The Botocudo resisted Portuguese settlement fiercely and were reduced by war and disease in the nineteenth century.","c":"The orange-red textile draped on the central figure is a strong color accent, and the paddle and basket at the left give two of the figures a useful job. The large circular earrings are the one ethnographic hint in a card otherwise rather generic. The soft light blue and beige ground is attractive, and the warm reds against cool tones make a pleasing arrangement."},"nga:50558":{"d":"Three people stand together, the two on the left facing forward and the one on the right turned slightly to the side. Each has distinct facial features and medium-length dark hair decorated with feathers, a neutral expression and a relaxed posture, with the lower body minimally clothed and ornaments such as necklaces. The one on the far left crosses the arms, the middle one holds a bow and arrow, and the one on the right leans on a decorated spear. The background is a simple green landscape with soft skies.","i":"Auca, from a Quechua word meaning enemy, was applied by Spanish speakers to peoples who resisted conquest, notably the Mapuche of Chile and Argentina, and later the Huaorani of Ecuador. The title does not say which people Catlin meant. The three children with bow and spear are shown in the stances of adults, a version of the warrior's ideal learned early.","c":"Children posed as adults is an arresting idea, with the bow, the leaning on a spear and the folded arms giving them the stances of their elders, and the effect is half comic and half touching. The execution is as faint and generic as the series always is. It gives the group a charm that the rows of adults do not have."},"nga:50559":{"d":"Three men stand together against a neutral ground of soft colors. The one on the left holds a bow and arrow, the central figure a spear, and the one on the right a shield and a club. All three have feathers in long, dark hair and wear minimal clothing, with distinct faces. The three weapons are distributed like items in a case, one to each man.","i":"The Puelche, people of the east in the Mapuche language, were hunters of the Argentine pampas and northern Patagonia, who gained horses in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries and became skilled riders, hunting rhea and guanaco and raiding settlements. By the nineteenth century they had merged with other peoples of the pampas. The bow, spear and club are the traditional arms; the title names no individual.","c":"Three weapons, three men: bow, spear and shield-and-club make an orderly display. The neutral background and the plain composition give it a dignity that would suit an arms catalog, and the three faces have some individuality. But it is a card without place: nothing says pampas, and the three could stand on any of the continent's grounds. It is decent and uninspired."},"nga:50560":{"d":"Facing forward in a straight line, four people stand against a painted setting of muted earth tones, faded greens and a subtle blue sky. From left to right are a young girl in a dress and beaded necklaces, a man with long hair and a feather, an unclothed child, and a man with long dark hair holding a stick. The line is plain and frontal, with the girl's dress the one garment of European cut.","i":"Patagon was the old name for the people of Patagonia, the windy steppes at the southern tip of South America, now generally known as Tehuelche, tall hunters who followed guanaco on foot and, after the eighteenth century, on horseback. The name goes back to the Magellan expedition of 1520, whose chronicler described them as giants. The chief, his brother and the daughter make a family group, and the museum dates the panel 1856 to 1869.","c":"The long row of four, with its girl in a dress and necklaces and its unclothed child, gives some human variety, and the faded greens and subtle blue sky are a restrained, rather tender scheme. The picture has no sense of the huge wind-swept steppe that is Patagonia, and the figures are the same stock types as elsewhere. It is a polite, thin card."},"nga:50561":{"d":"Three men are shown in full-body portraits against a light, neutral field with hints of green, framed with a green border. The central figure stands with one leg crossed over the other, holding a spear adorned with feathers, with long black hair with feathers, a beaded necklace and minimal clothing. At left another stands holding a pipe, with feathers in his long black hair and anklets of feathers. At right the third holds a bow and arrow, in similar attire with a necklace.","i":"The Toba, who call themselves Qom, are a people of the Gran Chaco of Argentina, Paraguay and Bolivia, hunters, fishers and gatherers who adopted the horse in the colonial period and were known as formidable fighters. Catlin's title spells the name Tobos. The pipe, spear and bow make a kit of peace, war and the hunt, divided among the three men.","c":"The crossed-leg pose of the central figure is the card's only relaxed gesture, and the feathered anklets are a good detail. The three share dress and manner, and the green border and neutral field leave them unplaced. It is decorative and tidy; the young men of the title are given youth in neither faces nor action."},"nga:50562":{"d":"Four people stand side by side on a beach, with the ocean at the left, a boat behind them and green pointed trees beyond, under a soft, hazy sky. Three men have long, slightly wavy dark hair; the woman at the right wears her hair in two braids, a woven skirt, a simple top and several necklaces, and holds up a net on posts. The men wear necklaces and waist coverings and hold a paddle, a staff and a stick.","i":"Fuegian is the old collective name for the peoples of Tierra del Fuego, the islands at the southern tip of South America. The canoe peoples among them, the Yaghan and Kawesqar, traveled the channels in bark canoes, hunting seals and gathering shellfish. Darwin met Fuegians on the Beagle voyage of 1832 to 1834, and disease and settlement almost destroyed them in the nineteenth century. The net and boat show the sea as their living.","c":"The beach, boat and net give this card a setting and an occupation that the lineup cards lack, and the soft hazy sky suits the maritime edge of the world. The men's hair and the woven skirt are given some character. The figures are as stiff as ever and the pointed trees a stock backdrop, but the picture comes close to a sense of place: a coast, a livelihood and a boat."},"nga:50563":{"d":"A small clearing opens in a dense forest. A person lies in a hammock while another cooks over a fire, and an anteater walks nearby. The painting shows detailed textures in the leaves and bark, and the palette is dominated by deep greens and browns. The anteater is the title's subject, walking near the camp, and the people are set small among the trees.","i":"The giant anteater, up to two meters long with its tail, is a toothless animal of the South American savannas and forests that tears into ant and termite nests with long claws and licks up the insects with a very long tongue. It is harmless unless cornered. The hammock and the cooking fire show a forest camp of travelers or villagers, and Catlin took a naturalist's interest in such animals.","c":"The detail in the leaves and bark is the picture's chief virtue, a dense, close-up forest quite different from the open plains, and the three elements, the hammock, the fire and the anteater, give a calm domestic tableau. It is a gently comic subject, an odd animal at the edge of a camp. The painting is competent and a little static, and the humor is in the title."},"nga:50564":{"d":"Three figures stand in the foreground with long dark hair styled up with feathers, necklaces and minimal attire with feather elements around the waist and legs. They hold long sticks adorned with feathers. Behind them are a natural landscape of grass, a distant mountain range and a cloudy sky. The dance is reduced to three performers posed in a landscape, with the feathered sticks as their emblems.","i":"Goo-a-give is Catlin's spelling of a name that cannot now be matched with certainty to a known people. A dance called handsome in the title is a display, and the feather-decked sticks the dancers carry, with the feather ornaments at waist and leg, are the means: feathers were prized across the Amazon and the Chaco for dancing and ceremonial dress.","c":"Three dancers are a thin way to render a dance, and the card is more a stately costume parade than an event. The feather sticks and ornaments are well observed and give a repeated motif, and the distant mountains with the cloudy sky provide a calm backdrop. It is a handsome subject, handsomely but quietly treated."},"nga:50565":{"d":"A rider on a galloping horse aims a firearm at birds in flight across rolling plains, with sloping hills beyond. The brushwork is detailed and captures movement. The palette is earth tones and muted colors, with green, brown and soft yellow, and the sky is full of swirling pink and orange clouds. Lush vegetation with small colorful flowers fills the foreground, and more riders in the distance are engaged in similar pursuits.","i":"The title calls the quarry ostriches, the common colonial name for the rhea, a large running bird of the South American plains that the pampas peoples hunted on horseback, traditionally with bolas, weighted cords thrown to entangle the legs; a firearm is an adaptation. The title places the scene near Buenos Aires and names the hunters Auca, a Spanish word for the unconquered peoples of the pampas.","c":"The swirling pink and orange clouds are the picture's drama, and the horse's gallop and the rider's aim give a clear diagonal of action; the small flowers in the foreground are a pretty touch. The picture is more movement than detail, and the distant riders are summarily done. For a series that is often static, it has genuine verve."},"nga:50566":{"d":"A serene river landscape is seen from a slightly raised angle. The horizon lies near the upper third, with a dense strip of forest along the far bank. Two animals scavenge for food on a sandy bank at the right, and at left a small canoe with two figures paddles quietly nearby. The brushstrokes are smooth and detailed, capturing lush foliage and gentle ripples, and the palette is natural: the rich greens of the vegetation against the soft blues and earth tones of the river and sky.","i":"The title joins two things, a palm bridge (pont de palmiers, French for bridge of palms) and jaguar shooting; tiger was the colonial name for the jaguar, the great cat of the American tropics, which Europeans in the Amazon hunted for its skin and its threat to livestock. The picture's river view, with a canoe and two animals on a sandbank, may be the setting for either. Catlin's French title is unexplained.","c":"The river view is the picture's strength: the dense far bank, the gentle ripples and the tiny canoe give a believable, quiet tropical scene. What the title promises, a bridge and a hunt, is not what the eye finds first, and the two animals on the sandbank may or may not be the jaguars of the title. It is a pleasing river picture with an elusive title."},"nga:50567":{"d":"Several figures move through dense foliage with large leaves and tall trees in a forest at night, lit by torches under a dark sky. The painting contrasts the warm light of the torches with the deep shadows around them, and the figures are caught in motion, as if exploring or searching. It is painted on canvas, about 46 by 61 centimeters, not card, and the torchlight is the subject as much as the figures.","i":"In the Amazon and Orinoco basins, river turtles come ashore on sandbanks at night to lay eggs, and people hunted them, and gathered the eggs, by torchlight; turtle meat and oil were an important food and trade item. The scene in the dark with torches takes advantage of that behavior. The hunters are not identified, and how Catlin saw such a hunt is unknown.","c":"The torches against the dense dark are the whole interest: the oranges and yellows of firelight on leaves and the deep shadows around make this one of Catlin's most theatrical pictures, and the canvas gives it more weight than the cards. The jungle is, however, a stage of leaves, and the figures are small. For a viewer used to the bright plains, the contrast is startling."},"nga:50568":{"d":"A forest scene of dense greenery and trees shows wild peccaries moving across a glade. The painting gives realistic details of leaves and tree trunks, in greens, earth tones and soft browns and reds. Two figures in different attire observe the scene from the side, while another figure stands on a fallen trunk holding a spear. It is painted in oil on canvas, about 45 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Peccaries are the wild pigs of the American tropics, and herds of white-lipped peccaries are dangerous: they clack their tusks, charge together and can kill a dog or a person, so hunters traditionally climbed a tree or log out of reach and speared them from there. The figure on the fallen trunk is following that practice. The title makes the hunters Carib.","c":"The man on the fallen trunk with his spear is a clear, purposeful figure, and the herd moving across the glade gives the picture a rare mixture of danger and watchfulness. The leaves and trunks are carefully painted. It is a good example of Catlin's use of canvas for his more ambitious late subjects. The excitement is held in check and the green is monotonous, but the situation is vivid."},"nga:50569":{"d":"A dense jungle is lit along an illuminated path, with a faint horizon formed by trees and foliage in the distance. The greenery is painted with intricate brushstrokes that enhance texture, in rich greens and browns, with highlights of orange and yellow where the light strikes the foreground. In the distance, cooler blues and grays add depth. At the right a group of figures is partly visible among the foliage and shadows. The picture is oil on canvas, about 46 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Ignis fatuus is Latin for foolish fire, the old name of the will-o'-the-wisp, the flickering light that appears over marshes at night and that travelers followed, according to folklore, to their harm. A South American forest at night offered many such lights, from fireflies and luminous fungi to marsh gases. The title attaches the phenomenon to the Zurumati, a people not matched with certainty to a modern one.","c":"A path of light through a dark jungle is one of Catlin's most imaginative subjects, and the canvas gives it body: the oranges and yellows of the glow against the deep greens and the cool blue and gray distance make a convincing atmosphere of mystery. The figures at the right are lost in shadows, which suits the title. It is among the few pictures in which Catlin lets the unseen matter."},"nga:50570":{"d":"A vast expanse is seen from an elevated perspective, with a high horizon revealing distant mountain peaks partly covered in mist. Smooth brushstrokes depict lush scenery in rich earth tones and green hills and trees, and the sky is blue with soft pink and white clouds. Figures gather on a hill slope with sparse vegetation around them, and clusters of trees spread toward the misty mountains. The scene conveys tranquility and space.","i":"The Pampa del Sacramento is a large grassland in the Peruvian Amazon, between the Huallaga and Ucayali rivers east of the Andes, long known to Franciscan missionaries and explorers as a gateway to the forest. Catlin's title places the picture there; the misty peaks in the distance would be the outer ranges of the Andes.","c":"A big view with misty mountains and a soft sky is a good subject for Catlin's patient atmospheric manner, and the figures on the slope give a human measure. The picture is among the most spacious of the South American cards, and it shows that his landscapes travel better than his portraits. It is a calm, tidy view that adds a continent to his work without adding passion."},"nga:50571":{"d":"A serene landscape has a body of water in the distance, lush rainforest on the right and a small encampment with people closer to the viewer. The scene is detailed in subtle brushstrokes, with a palette dominated by greens and earthy tones. The river's wide shore and the forest at the right frame a quiet camp, the people small beside the water and the great trees.","i":"The Essequibo, the largest river of Guyana, rises in the Guiana highlands near the Brazilian border and flows north to the Atlantic, and it was the route to the interior for Dutch and British traders, missionaries and explorers. The camp on its shore is probably a temporary river camp of travelers or villagers. Catlin's time in Guiana is known from his own later writing.","c":"The river and the rainforest are painted with gentle, even care, and the small encampment gives the shore a plausible human use. It is a calm, unemphatic picture, in the register of Catlin's best quiet landscapes, but there is no striking passage and no event to remember. It adds a place to the gallery rather than a picture."},"nga:50572":{"d":"A lush jungle of dense foliage blocks the horizon. In the distance a small body of water can be seen with boats carrying figures. The brushstrokes create textures that depict the tropical vegetation, and the palette is dominated by green shades with some brown and black accents and splashes of red and blue. The forest fills the frame, and the water is a slit of light at the far edge, with the small boats the only sign of people.","i":"The Amazon flows through the largest tropical forest on earth, whose tall trees and tangled vines, as Catlin and other travelers reported, shut out the sky and the view. The title's luxuriant is the nineteenth-century word for rich tropical growth, a note of wonder common in travelers' accounts. The boats in the distance show the river as the only road.","c":"Dense foliage with a single window of water is a good formal idea: the forest closes the picture, and the boats and the blue and red notes are the only relief. The textures of the leaves are handled with real patience. The monotony of the greens is hard to avoid, and no figure or event rescues it; as a statement of the forest's size it works, as a picture to linger over it is a little dutiful."},"nga:50573":{"d":"A tropical setting of lush foliage, trees and hut-like structures is seen from a slightly raised viewpoint that gives a comprehensive view. The painting has detailed textures and fine brushstrokes, primarily in green tones with hints of brown. In the distance structures and figures suggest a small community, and a body of water runs parallel to the viewer, bordered by more vegetation.","i":"Dutch Guiana, now Suriname, was a Dutch colony on the northeast coast of South America with plantations of sugar, coffee and cacao worked by enslaved Africans, while Carib and Arawak peoples lived in villages in the interior and along the rivers. A Carib village in the colony was therefore a near neighbor of plantations. Caribbe is Catlin's spelling.","c":"The huts, trees and water are laid out in an orderly way, with the water a gentle band in front, and the detail of foliage is patient and varied. The village is a cluster of structures and small figures, which recalls Catlin's plains villages with the tipis changed to huts and the grass to jungle; the formula travels. It is a pleasing, slightly generic view."},"nga:50574":{"d":"A mountain range fills the middle of this small oval view, its ridges stacked toward a high horizon and dissolving into hazy hills at the right. Below, a broad carpet of trees descends to the foreground, where a few figures sit around a campfire in the lower right corner. The sky moves from pale blue into muted gray with a touch of pink. Greens and browns dominate, lifted by faint pink and blue highlights. The vignette is painted in oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters, and set inside a rectangular sheet.","i":"The title places the scene in the Crystal Mountains, and a companion picture, View of the Crystal Mountains, Brazil, locates the range in Brazil. As in many of Catlin's South American views, tiny figures give the scale and show that the land is inhabited, not empty. Such pictures grew out of his travels in the 1850s, after the loss of his first Indian Gallery to creditors, and were painted up on card in the following years from sketches and memory.","c":"The pale, hazy ranges recede convincingly, and the sky, with its single touch of pink, is the most carefully observed passage. The foreground is weaker: the trees are a green mass without much variety, and the campfire group is too small to hold any interest. Beside Catlin's Plains portraits, which depend on sharp individual likeness, this is a competent but generic piece of scenery."},"nga:50575":{"d":"Seen from ground level through an oval opening, the view crosses a clearing of tall plants and small trees toward a village of pitched-roof houses lined along the horizon. Behind and above them the jungle rises in dense foliage. The brushwork is delicate and precise, softening everything into a dreamy haze, and the palette stays within earthy greens and browns under a pale, apparently overcast sky. A muted border frames the oval. It is a small oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Arowak is an older spelling of Arawak, a name given to Arawakan-speaking peoples of northern South America and the Guianas. Catlin presents the village as a settled, orderly community, not a curiosity, which fits his lifelong aim of recording Native peoples as they lived. How closely this scene follows a particular place is not known, and the title is the only evidence for the people.","c":"The soft, misty handling suits the humid air and gives the village a calm that more emphatic painters might have missed. But the delicacy is also a limitation: the houses are summarized, not observed, and nothing in the picture distinguishes this village from the others in the series beyond the title. As evidence about the Arawak it is slight; as an atmospheric vignette it is pleasant."},"nga:50576":{"d":"From a high vantage point the eye drops into a deep, rugged canyon, with rock formations and patches of green on the far horizon and a river winding through the terrain below. At the right, small figures stand at the cliff edge, busy at some task. Rocks and sky are handled in fluid, subtle strokes, and browns and greens in the stone and vegetation play against a bright, cloudy sky. The oval vignette sits in an almost square sheet of card, about 55 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The title is unusual and the museum record does not explain it, so the place cannot be identified from the picture alone. What is clear is the type: a prospect from a height over a wild gorge, the kind of grand natural spectacle that nineteenth-century travelers and their readers prized. The tiny figures at the cliff edge give the scale and make the viewer, perched above them, feel the drop.","c":"This is among the more adventurous compositions in Catlin's South American series: the plunge into the gorge gives it a depth that his level riverbank scenes lack, and sky and rock are painted with a lightness that avoids heaviness. The weakness is again the figures, scarcely more than marks, so that scale is asserted rather than felt."},"nga:50577":{"d":"Greenery closes off the whole view: palm fronds, trailing vines and bright flowers rise from the water's edge and hide the horizon. In the foreground a narrow, canoe-like boat carries one standing figure, while two more work at the bank. Catlin lingers over the leaves, building a dense tapestry of greens broken by small bursts of blossom, and surrounds the scene with an earth-toned border. The picture is a small oil on card, roughly 47 by 62 centimeters, seen from only a little above the level of the water.","i":"The Trombetas is a northern tributary of the Amazon in Brazil. Catlin went up the great river in the 1850s, and pictures like this one record the experience of traveling by water through country whose banks were a wall of vegetation. The small figures working at the edge suggest that people lived and labored there, but the title puts the shore, not the people, first.","c":"The leaves are handled with real appetite, and the range of greens avoids monotony better than in many of Catlin's jungle scenes. What the picture lacks is a place for the eye to rest: there is no distance, no sky, and the three figures are too small to organize the crowded surface. It works as an impression of overwhelming vegetation; as a composition it is thin."},"nga:50578":{"d":"The museum record gives no description of this picture beyond its title and the single recorded motif of trees, so its details cannot be reported with confidence. The title promises a village of Indigenous people on the bank of the Amazon, a subject Catlin painted many times in this series, usually as a modest settlement among trees at the water's edge with small figures for scale. It is a small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters, with the same format as its neighbors.","i":"Catlin traveled in South America in the 1850s, and the Amazon and its tributaries supplied most of the subjects in this series of small oils. In nineteenth-century usage 'Indian' covered all Indigenous peoples of the Americas, and Catlin applied the word to Amazonian peoples just as he had to the Plains nations, extending his project of documenting Native life to a second continent.","c":"Judged by its title and the company it keeps, this is typical of Catlin's Amazon series: a quick, sympathetic record of a riverside settlement, rich in green and thin in particulars. His gift for humid light and masses of foliage is balanced by little interest in the architecture of villages or the faces of their people. These pictures reward being seen as a run, not singly."},"nga:50579":{"d":"A thick forest interior fills the view, its canopy so continuous that no horizon or sky shows. Catlin paints tall trunks and heavy leaves with energetic strokes, and the greens deepen into dark shadow to give the dimness of the undergrowth. Small figures in red are scattered across the scene, apparently moving through the trees, and tiny flowers punctuate the greens. The red of the clothing is the only strong contrast in an otherwise green picture, painted in oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"Zurumati, named in the title, is presumably the locality or the people Catlin met there; the museum record does not identify it further. The interior of the forest was a favorite subject for nineteenth-century travelers and painters, who found in its density a sign of nature's abundance and of the difficulty of passage. The red figures, set against so much green, can be read as people moving through the forest as if at home in it.","c":"The red-clad figures are a shrewd stroke: they punctuate the green and give the eye a path through a picture that otherwise has no structure. The foliage, however, is painted at one pitch of intensity, so the forest feels dense but not deep, and nothing distinguishes tree from undergrowth in the lower register. It is more convincing as an impression of overwhelming green than as a view of a place."},"nga:50580":{"d":"The museum record gives no description of this picture beyond its title and the Indigenous theme, so its details cannot be stated with confidence. The title indicates a view toward a forested shore from the water, the position of a traveler in a boat, which is how Catlin saw much of the Amazon. Like the others in the series it is a small oil on card, about 46 by 63 centimeters, and it was probably worked up in the studio from field sketches.","i":"'Looking ashore' is a traveler's phrase, and it fits Catlin's journeys by river steamer and canoe, when the bank was the only land in sight. The Amazon's forest edge, tall, continuous and seemingly endless, is a constant in travelers' accounts of the period. Catlin offered such views to audiences at home who knew the river chiefly from books.","c":"The title and the series suggest a view without incident, a forest wall seen across water. That can make a handsome picture when the light is right, and Catlin's best Amazon scenes do catch the heavy, humid glow of the river. As a type, though, the form is repetitive, and the series would have gained from more variety in vantage point and weather."},"nga:50581":{"d":"A green landscape of rolling hills, scattered trees and a path winding through them opens under a blue sky streaked with thin clouds. Small figures appear along the way. Catlin blends his greens and earth tones with soft strokes, so the hills melt into one another without hard edges. It is an unassuming, pastoral view, painted in oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters, and it has the calm of a place passed through rather than a place lived in.","i":"The title names a mountain after its flowering shrubs, though the museum's description picks out no blossoms and the location is not stated. The path and the little travelers make the hill a place for passing through, a reminder that Catlin saw this country as a traveler. Pictures like this belong to the nineteenth-century habit of collecting named views of distant lands.","c":"Of all the pictures in this series, this one comes closest to a conventional landscape, with a path to enter by and a graded sky above. Catlin's smooth blending produces an agreeable harmony, but also a sameness; the hills lack the structure that would make them feel like mountains, and the figures stay anonymous. It is pleasant work, forgettable next to the Plains pictures that made his name."},"nga:50582":{"d":"Gentle, rounded hills roll back toward a high horizon topped by softly painted clouds. The brushwork is smooth, the greens and browns of the land are quiet, and the sky takes on pastel tones. In the near ground, partly hidden by the lie of the land, stand structures that the museum's description reads as possibly tipis, though in a Brazilian setting they may equally be huts. The mood is tranquil and the scale open. A small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Catlin placed these mountains in Brazil, but nothing in the picture pins down a locality; what it offers is a general sense of the interior as open, rolling country. The structures in the foreground connect the view to the Indigenous peoples who were his real subject. Quiet uplands like this are a change from the river and jungle scenes that make up most of the series.","c":"Quiet and unforced, this is one of the calmer pictures in the series. The hills are drawn with a soft, steady hand, and the pastel sky avoids the heaviness of Catlin's jungle interiors. The price of the calm is blandness: the terrain has no distinctive feature, and the half-hidden structures are too vague to anchor the scene. It reads as a pleasant landscape, not a record of a particular place."},"nga:50583":{"d":"Figures crowd the foreground of this scene, standing in varied poses, with dark hair set with feathers, little clothing, and ornaments. Some carry paddles or spears; others balance baskets or pots on their heads. Behind them stands a wooden structure with a thatched roof, and green foliage rises beyond, with more figures glimpsed in the distance. Unlike most of Catlin's Amazon views, people rather than scenery are the subject. The oil on card measures about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Conibo (Catlin writes Connibo) is the name of a people of the Ucayali River in Peru, related to the Shipibo and now often grouped with them. River turtles and their eggs were an important food on the Amazon and its tributaries, so a turtle hunt was a natural subject for a scene of daily work. The title puts the viewer at the moment of return, with the party coming home carrying its gear.","c":"Here the figures are large enough to be looked at, and the picture benefits: the varied poses and the carried baskets give a rhythm that the landscapes in the series lack. The drawing is stiff, the figures seem posed more than caught at the end of a hunt, and the faces are generic. Still, it comes closer than most to the ethnographic record Catlin wanted."},"nga:50584":{"d":"From a raised vantage the plains spread out to a low horizon where a range of hills closes the view. Cattle graze across the open land, the animals and the ground painted in detailed, realistic strokes in earthy tones. Above, a blue sky carries clouds touched with pink and gray, giving the scene a serene feeling. The picture is a small oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters, and the animals are the nearest thing it has to a subject.","i":"The Pampa del Sacramento is a savanna in eastern Peru, in the Amazon basin. The cattle there were not native animals but descended from European stock, so the 'wild' herds were feral. For Catlin, whose earlier fame rested on buffalo and the Plains, a grassland with roaming herds offered a southern counterpart to the American West, and he also painted horsemen driving such cattle.","c":"The elevation gives the plain a breadth the thicker jungle pictures cannot, and the animals are observed with more care than Catlin's tiny figures usually get. The low horizon and the pink-and-gray sky are well judged. What is missing is incident: the cattle are scattered evenly, nothing interrupts the sweep of land, and the eye wanders. A restful, competent view, closer to documentation than to invention."},"nga:50585":{"d":"A canoe carrying Indigenous fishers slides across dark, reflective water, hemmed in by tall trees and thick foliage. The brushwork is smooth and the palette is built from deep blues and greens, suited to a night scene lit by the moon. The still surface doubles the trees and the boat. The title names the activity, spearing, which on these rivers usually meant fishing. The picture is a small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The Chaco is the great lowland region of Paraguay, Bolivia and Argentina, so the title points far from the Amazon; Catlin gathered his South American scenes under a single theme of Indigenous life by water. Night spearing, by torchlight or moonlight, was a widespread fishing method on tropical rivers, and Catlin painted it more than once. The moonlight lets him treat a work scene as a quiet nocturne.","c":"The nocturnal blues and the doubled reflections give this a calm that most of the daytime scenes in the series cannot match, and the smooth handling suits it. It is also nearly empty: the figures are a dark cluster, and the drama of the hunt is suggested only by the title. A competent nocturne that shows how little it took to lift a conventional river view."},"nga:50586":{"d":"Riders on horseback fan out across a wide grassy plain under a soft, pale sky. The horizon sits high, giving more room to the ground, where fluid brushstrokes make the grass and the sky seem to move. Greens, rust and ochre predominate, and the figures are spread across the field in a loose scatter rather than a tight group. The picture is a small oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The title joins two subjects: the Conibo of the Ucayali and the feral cattle of the pampas, a legacy of European livestock. Roundups on horseback belong to the cattle-country world of South America, and Catlin, a veteran painter of mounted Plains hunters, found in them an echo of his earlier subjects. The title names the riders as Conibo without further explanation.","c":"A lively pattern of riders over a long stretch of ground, and the wind-blown feel of the grass is neatly caught. Catlin evidently enjoyed the subject, which let him return to the mounted hunters he had painted in North America. But the figures are too small and too alike to convey a driving action; the sense of motion lives in the brushwork more than in the drawing."},"nga:50587":{"d":"A river runs through dense jungle, and a boat with a thatched roof lies close to a sloping bank. People dressed in red gather both on the boat and ashore, and makeshift structures stand along the slope. The foliage is rendered in deliberate, naturalistic strokes, while the figures are more stylized; the earthy palette is enlivened by the red. The oil on card is about 46 by 62 centimeters, and the red figures are the first thing the eye finds.","i":"Orejona echoes the Spanish orejón, 'big ear', a label colonial writers gave to Amazonian peoples who wore large ear ornaments; it is probably Catlin's rendering of a name he heard locally. The roofed boat is a reminder that the river was both highway and home. The figures' red may represent cloth or the annatto body paint widely used in the Amazon.","c":"The red figures against deep green make a strong, simple chord, and the roofed boat gives the composition an anchor that many of its neighbors lack. The stylization of the people is the weak point: they read as small red marks, not individuals, and the bank is crowded without being organized. A neat, memorable color scheme carries a slight picture."},"nga:50588":{"d":"A canoe rests on the water beside a riverbank where small figures gather and go about various tasks. Forest forms the backdrop, painted in smooth strokes and natural earthy tones. The view is enclosed in an oval frame, which concentrates attention on the bank and its little crowd, and the whole has the finish of a miniature. It is a small oil on card, about 48 by 63 centimeters, and its many tiny activities are the picture's main interest.","i":"The Omagua were an Amazonian people whom sixteenth-century Spanish chroniclers described living in populous riverside villages; by Catlin's day their numbers had fallen sharply. The subtitle 'Boat Sketch' suggests that the composition derives from a drawing made from the water, the viewpoint most open to a traveler on the Amazon.","c":"The oval and the riverbank tableau give the picture a tidy, almost miniature finish, and the many small figures animate the bank. As elsewhere, Catlin sacrifices individuality: the figures are shorthand, and the subtitle 'Boat Sketch' is more convincing than the polish, which suggests a field jotting enlarged into a studio picture without much new observation."},"nga:50589":{"d":"Seen from a slightly raised angle, a riverbank camp occupies the lower part of the picture: a cluster of tent-like shelters stands at the left, with people moving around them, while a canoe with figures floats on the river at the right. Lush rainforest takes up the whole upper part of the view. Catlin handles it in smooth, detailed strokes, with rich greens set against touches of earth color. The oil on card is about 48 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The Mura lived along the rivers of the central Amazon basin, notably the Madeira, and were known to Portuguese colonists as formidable river people who traveled by canoe. 'Encampment' and the tent-like shelters suggest a temporary camp rather than a permanent village, which would suit a mobile, river-based people. 'Boat Sketch' again hints at a sketch made while afloat.","c":"The elevated angle helps: it opens the camp to view instead of burying it, and the canoe at the right balances the shelters. The foliage, however, takes most of the sheet and is painted with little change in tone, so the eye slides over it. Neat and legible, the picture records a scene more than it interprets one."},"nga:50590":{"d":"A long boat lies at the water's edge with people aboard and smoke curling up from it, while other figures stand together on the grass nearby, perhaps gathered for some event. Farther back, huts sit among greenery below a line of tall trees and a blue sky. The brushwork is detailed enough to make the figures legible, and the greens, browns and blue hold together calmly. This is a notably larger sheet than most in the series, about 55 by 78 centimeters.","i":"Mayoruna is an outsiders' name for Panoan-speaking peoples of the Peru and Brazil border region, among them the Matsés, who were known for facial ornaments and for resisting contact. The smoke from the boat may be a cooking fire, but the picture does not say. As a scene of a gathering, it shows Catlin trying to catch community life and not only the setting.","c":"The larger sheet gives Catlin room, and he uses it to better effect than usual: the group on the grass and the smoking boat make two centers of interest, tied together by the river's edge, and the tall trees close the space with a light touch. The figures remain too summary to read as individuals, but the picture hangs together better than the smaller ones."},"nga:50591":{"d":"A river leads the eye to a grassy clearing where huts stand, and a boat with people on board lies on the water. Individuals dressed in red stand on the bank, bright against the green, while towering palms rise from dense jungle behind. The palette is simple: greens, the red garments and a blue sky. It is a small oil on card, about 48 by 64 centimeters, and the color scheme is clear even at a distance.","i":"The Yahua are the Yagua, an Amazonian people of northeastern Peru, often noted for palm-fiber skirts and for red pigment from annatto used on the body. Catlin's red figures may therefore echo a real practice, though the museum describes them as wearing red attire. The village stands for an Indigenous way of life along the rivers that Catlin believed was disappearing.","c":"The three-part scheme of red, green and blue is bold and legible at a glance, and the palms give the background a fine vertical rhythm. The limits are the usual ones: the huts are summary, the figures are repeated types, and the village is built from familiar elements of this series rather than from observed particulars. Effective as a color design, less so as ethnography."},"nga:50592":{"d":"Water fills most of the view, with a dense line of green foliage forming the far shore. Two boats float on it, one near and large, the other smaller and farther off. The sky is lightly clouded, and the brushwork is smooth and realistic across water, land and sky. Blues and greens dominate, with earth tones and a few muted reds. The picture is a small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"The wide river with a low green shore is the characteristic view from a boat on the Amazon, where the stream can be many miles across and the banks appear as a thin line. The subtitle 'Boat Sketch' and the two boats point to the traveler's position, and they show how much of Catlin's South America was seen from the water.","c":"A clean, restful composition: the large boat anchors the lower foreground and the small one measures the distance. Water and sky are handled convincingly. Beyond that the picture has little to hold attention, and the green fringe of the far shore is a single unvaried band. It conveys the monotony of the great river honestly, perhaps too honestly for a picture meant to please."},"nga:50593":{"d":"Tall trees and large-leaved plants make up the dense green view, and two thatched huts sit tucked among them. In the foreground a group of people gathers, painted in dark tones that stand out against the surrounding green. Catlin blends his strokes smoothly, which gives the foliage a soft realism and some depth. Shades of green dominate, with accents of earthy brown. The oil on card is about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The Cocama were a Tupi-speaking people of the upper Amazon and the Ucayali region of Peru, closely related to the Omagua, and their name appears in many spellings in nineteenth-century travel accounts. 'Looking Ashore' repeats the traveler's viewpoint of a boat approaching land: the huts and the group on the bank are what a river party would see first.","c":"The dark group against the green is an effective accent, and the blended handling suits the damp air. But the thatched huts are lost among the vegetation, and the composition has no clear structure beyond the contrast of figures and leaves. Like many pictures in the series, it is agreeable at a glance and hard to remember afterward."},"nga:50594":{"d":"In a clearing of green grass, figures dance in the foreground, each caught in a different expressive pose. Others gather near a tent, and dense trees fill the background, opening to reveal a calm stretch of water edged with greenery. Unlike most of Catlin's quiet riverbank scenes, this one is organized around a group in motion. It is a small oil on card, about 46 by 61 centimeters, and the dancers are the clear center of attention.","i":"Tapuya (Tapuia) was a Tupi word for peoples who did not speak Tupi languages, and Portuguese colonists used it as a catch-all label for 'wild' interior groups, so the title names a category more than a single nation. Dance scenes were among Catlin's constant interests; his Plains pictures of Mandan and Sioux ceremonies are better known. Here he applies the same format to a South American camp.","c":"Dance gives Catlin's stiff figure drawing something to do, and the ring of expressive poses lifts the picture above the sleepy riverbank scenes around it. It cannot match the specificity of his Plains ceremonial paintings, where the costume and ritual were recorded at first hand; here the dancers are mostly gesture, and the 'Tapuya' label says little about who they are."},"nga:50595":{"d":"A dense, verdant forest fills the picture, its trees hiding most of the horizon, with a patch of water visible at the left. Huts stand among the trees, and people go about various activities around them. The brushwork is meticulous in both foliage and figures, and the palette is rich green with earth tones in the huts and the skin. A thick border frames the scene. The oil on card measures about 48 by 63 centimeters.","i":"Mauhees is probably Catlin's spelling of the name usually written Maué or Mawé (now Sateré-Mawé), a people of the lower Amazon between the Madeira and Tapajós rivers, known as the first cultivators of guaraná. The word 'encampment' implies a temporary settlement, perhaps one Catlin passed while traveling, and the museum record does not say where along the river it stood.","c":"Among the encampments in the series this is one of the more carefully finished; the thick border sets it off like a miniature, and the details of the huts and figures reward a close look. The problem is the same overload of green that afflicts the others, with the camp half-buried in leaves. Precision of touch cannot make up for the lack of a clear focus."},"nga:50596":{"d":"The foreground opens onto still water ringed by greenery and flowers, with large-leaved plants standing out against softer vegetation behind. Gentle brushwork builds a humid, hothouse atmosphere, and paler greens in the distance push the background back. Deep green dominates, but flecks of red, blue and yellow light up the flowers. Unlike most of the series, this is painted in oil on canvas rather than card, and it measures about 47 by 60 centimeters.","i":"A lagoon here means a still backwater or oxbow lake separated from the main river, a typical feature of the Amazon floodplain, where the water level rises and falls with the seasons. Such quiet pools full of flowering plants were a staple of travelers' descriptions, from Humboldt onward, and an invitation to the armchair viewer to imagine the tropics.","c":"Free of figures, this is among the purest of Catlin's tropical views, and the quiet water and the flecks of color have an appeal that the busier village scenes lack. The canvas ground also lets the paint sit more softly than on card. Yet the vegetation is generalized: nothing is identifiable as a particular plant, and no naturalist's eye is at work."},"nga:50597":{"d":"A green wall of foliage rises almost to the top of the picture, built up in layered leaves and branches, with huts seen at a distance in the clearing beyond. Closer to the viewer, red-toned figures are busy with various tasks. The high horizon and the compressed space make the forest feel enclosing, and the palette stays almost wholly within shades of green, with the figures as the only strong contrasting note. Oil on card, about 48 by 63 centimeters.","i":"No people is named in the title, which calls the scene simply an Indian camp, so it stands for the type more than for a particular community. Catlin believed he was recording Native life before it vanished, and the forest camp was a South American counterpart to the Plains encampments of his North American pictures. The red figures draw the eye first, as the human presence amid the vegetation.","c":"In its restrained palette this is one of the most coherent of the forest pictures: the layered greens have real density, and the red figures give the eye a place to start. Its weakness is flatness, because the high horizon shuts out any sense of recession, and the huts at the back are barely articulated. It is closer to a decorative screen than to a window."},"nga:50598":{"d":"The view looks across a river or channel to a settlement in a lush tropical landscape, framed in a circle. Tall palms and dense foliage rise along the horizon in the upper third of the picture, and small thatched huts stand on the bank where a group of people in red has gathered. In the foreground a canoe with a thatched cover is paddled by a figure in red. Soft, blended strokes and a palette of greens and blues, with a pink-tinged light sky, set the mood.","i":"The title announces a portrait sitting: Catlin had made his reputation by painting chiefs, and he evidently meant to do the same in South America. 'Tobos' is perhaps his spelling of Toba, a people of the Gran Chaco, though the scenery here is riverine. The museum's description mentions only the settlement and the boat, so the sitting itself, if it is shown, is not what the eye finds first.","c":"The circular format is neatly managed, with the canoe giving a diagonal into the village and the palms providing a lively skyline. The title promises the encounter between painter and chief that was the heart of Catlin's career, but the picture delivers a landscape, and the people are reduced to red marks. A charming vignette that sidesteps its own subject."},"nga:50599":{"d":"From a raised viewpoint, a row of leaning palms marks the horizon just above the middle of the picture. In the grass below, a number of people and animals have gathered, and a simple thatched shelter stands far back. More palms sway against a pale blue sky. Soft strokes and a restrained palette of gentle greens and earth tones keep the scene light and airy. The oil on card is about 46 by 63 centimeters.","i":"As in Painting the Tobos Chief, the title names its people Tobos, perhaps Catlin's form of Toba. 'A small village' is a repeated phrase in his titles, apparently meant to convey the modest scale of the settlements he saw: clusters of dwellings, not towns. The animals among the people are not identified in the museum's description.","c":"The leaning palms against the sky are the best idea here: they give the horizon a swaying rhythm, and the pale blue air is delicate. The people and animals on the grass are arranged in an even scatter, as if for counting, and the thatched shelter is too distant to anchor the composition. A pleasing and slightly empty picture."},"nga:50600":{"d":"Night has closed over a thick forest. A faintly lit path runs across the picture, and dark silhouettes of trees and leaves press in from all sides. Where the trees part, a strip of warm light breaks through, in muted oranges and yellows against the deep greens and blacks. Blended strokes soften the line between light and shade, so forms dissolve and the depth seems greater than the small scale of the oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters, would suggest.","i":"Ignis fatuus, Latin for 'foolish fire', is the old name for the will-o'-the-wisp, the flickering light seen at night over marshes, traditionally said to lead travelers astray and often explained as burning marsh gas. Catlin's title turns an ordinary forest night into a ghostly one and sets it on the Uruguay River. The warm glow is the picture's only sign of the phenomenon.","c":"Atmosphere is what the painting is after, and it achieves it better than most of the series: the darkness has real density, and the single warm slit of light is placed with theatrical sense. The cost is legibility. The forms are vague to the point of muddiness in the lower half, and the title does more of the work than the paint does."},"nga:50601":{"d":"The museum offers no written description of this picture, but its record lists an alligator, a nest and trees among the subjects. The title therefore points to a natural-history scene, a reptile's nest in the vegetation of South America, rather than a view of a human settlement. The alligators of South America are in fact caimans, so the animal is probably one of them. A small oil on card, about 46 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Natural-history subjects are unusual in Catlin's work, and this one shows how far his South American trip widened his range beyond the Indigenous portraits of his earlier career. In writing about his journeys he described the creatures, plants and landscapes he met as well as the peoples. Alligators and caimans build their nests by heaping vegetation into a mound, a habit that could catch a curious traveler's eye.","c":"Animal anatomy was never Catlin's strength; his buffalo are lively but loosely drawn, and a reptile's nest in the tropics asks for more patient observation than his rapid manner usually allowed. The subject is interesting for what it says about the breadth of his curiosity, and less for what the picture can be expected to deliver."},"nga:50602":{"d":"Standing on the riverbank, the viewer looks out over water to a low horizon where the stream meets a wall of greenery. Catlin renders the tropical vegetation in intricate brushwork, almost entirely in greens. A canoe with two figures rides on the water, and foliage layered behind gives the entrance its depth. Painted in oil on card, the view measures about 48 by 63 centimeters.","i":"An 'entrance to a lagoon' is a narrow channel leading from the main river into a quiet backwater, a common feature of the Amazon floodplain. Such gaps in the bank could lead to fishing grounds and to settlements off the main stream. The two figures in the canoe are poised at the threshold, which gives the title a small human narrative.","c":"The threshold idea gives the picture a clearer organization than most of its neighbors: the eye moves from the bank through the channel to the green beyond. The foliage is rendered with energy, though largely in one register, and the two paddlers are too small to carry feeling. A decent, workmanlike river view."},"nga:50603":{"d":"The viewer stands in a shady spot beneath tall trees hung with trailing vines, looking out through the leaves toward open country. Beyond the trees the horizon is low and soft, a blend of greens and muted pastels with patches of pale blue sky. Earth-colored structures stand at the right. Catlin paints the foliage with soft, sweeping strokes, so the picture has a lush, textured surface. The oil on card is about 46 by 60 centimeters.","i":"The Conibo are a people of the Ucayali River in Peru, today often grouped with the Shipibo, and are known for fine pottery and woven cloth. The shaded viewpoint under the trees, looking out to the village, turns the viewer into a traveler arriving at the edge of a settlement. As often in this series, the structures are secondary to the natural frame around them.","c":"Framing the village through trees is a sound idea and gives the picture a genuine sense of space, with a bright distance seen from a cool foreground. The vines are painted with more freedom than the usual leaf-by-leaf detail. What disappoints is the village itself, reduced to a few earthy shapes at the right, hardly worth the attention the title asks for."},"nga:50604":{"d":"A wide grassland runs back to a low horizon, and the sky takes up much of the picture, with gray and pink clouds across a blue ground. At the lower left corner, horsemen in red set off across the grass. Small patches of red and blue appear farther away. Earthy greens and blues are the main colors, and the sparse, open composition emphasizes distance. It is a small oil on card, about 47 by 64 centimeters.","i":"The title says the riders are starting out in pursuit of wild horses, the feral descendants of Spanish stock that roamed the grasslands of South America. Catlin had painted wild-horse hunts on the North American prairies, and the pampas version repeats the format, with riders in the corner and the whole open country before them. 'Connibos' follows his habit of naming the group in the title.","c":"The sky, large and mobile, is the part that works; it gives the open landscape its air. Pushing the riders into a corner is an interesting choice that dramatizes the emptiness they will cross. But the red horsemen are tiny and stiff, and the swell of the plain is not shaped enough to suggest the distance the title implies. A modest picture with one good idea."},"nga:50605":{"d":"A vast grassy plain stretches toward a low horizon and a group of distant hills. Several riders on horseback occupy the foreground. Above, the sky glows in oranges, yellows and pinks with only a hint of blue, and the smooth surface of the paint lets the colors melt into one another. The palette passes from earthy greens at the bottom to warm tones above. The oil on card is about 44 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Lavoir is French for a washing place, and 'grand lavoir' presumably names a large pool or flooded stretch of the plain where people and animals came to drink and bathe, though the museum does not say. The Pampa del Sacramento is a savanna in eastern Peru. A glowing sky like this lets Catlin pair open grassland with a dramatic backdrop, as in his earlier Plains scenes.","c":"The sky is the real subject, and it is handled with more ambition than the pale skies of the jungle pictures; the warm light reaching down onto the grass gives the whole sheet a unity. The riders are scattered tokens, and the plain beneath them is so even that the picture has little structure apart from the horizon. Sky effects of this kind were popular, and Catlin's use is competent, not inventive."},"nga:50606":{"d":"A peaceful village stands near a body of water, with trees and thatched buildings along the horizon. Small figures are scattered across the landscape: some by a canoe at the water's edge, others gathered on open ground. The colors are earthy browns and greens beneath soft sky tones, and the mood is tranquil. The title speaks of a portrait sitting, but the museum's description notes only the village and its surroundings. Oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Lengua is the Spanish name for peoples of the Paraguayan Chaco, now often called Enxet or Enenlhet. Painting a chief's portrait was the basis of Catlin's Indian Gallery, and the title shows him repeating the practice in South America, where the sitter would be recorded as a leader of his people. In these small vignettes the sitting becomes a detail within a landscape.","c":"As a village scene it is calm and well balanced, with the water, the canoe and the huts arranged at comfortable intervals. As an illustration of its title it fails, since the painter and the chief, if they are there at all, are lost in the crowd of small figures. Catlin's reputation rests on the faces he painted, and in this South American version the faces have gone missing."},"nga:50607":{"d":"The scene looks out over a calm river to a far shore of dense foliage. A narrow wooden boat with two figures rests on the water, and the bank is thick with vegetation reaching into the background. The brushwork is smooth, even slightly impressionistic, and the palette is built from many shades of green with dashes of earth brown and pale blue-gray. The oil on card is about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The title records an act typical of a traveling artist: stopping to sketch the view. Whether one of the two figures in the boat represents Catlin is not stated in the museum's description, but the title invites the thought. The Uruguay River, which forms much of the border between Argentina and Uruguay, belongs to the southern part of South America that Catlin's journeys covered.","c":"The loose, almost impressionistic touch is the most interesting thing here; the water and trees are put down with a freedom that the finished jungle pictures usually avoid, and the pale gray-blue keeps the greens from cloying. The picture is slight, and the humor of an artist sketching within a sketch is not developed. A modest, honest view with an appealing surface."},"nga:50608":{"d":"Within an oval boundary, a narrow canoe carries figures up a river, while on the bank more figures haul on a rope, apparently towing the boat against the current. Tall trees and thick vegetation hide the horizon behind them. Catlin uses soft strokes, and his palette combines greens and browns with deep reds. This is one of the few pictures in the series about physical effort, painted in oil on card, about 48 by 63 centimeters.","i":"Poling and towing canoes up rapids was the ordinary way to travel upriver in South America, and travelers of the period often described the labor involved. The title attaches the scene to the Lengua, who are usually placed in the Paraguayan Chaco, far from the Uruguay, a discrepancy the museum record does not explain. Catlin was drawn to action scenes in North America too, where he painted hunts and ceremonies.","c":"The diagonal of rope and canoe gives real tension, and the oval format concentrates the effort. It is among the more successful attempts in the series to show people doing something rather than posing in front of scenery. The figures remain small and the forest behind is a stock green screen, so the drama is more indicated than felt."},"nga:50609":{"d":"Seen from above, a river winds beneath a high horizon where warm sky tones and a softly shining sun blend together. Groups of people gather on a hill near the water, interacting, and a canoe with people aboard rests on the bank. Palms and other trees dot the area. The palette is pastel, with blues, pinks and light greens, and its earth tones melt into the water. A small oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters.","i":"'Small village' and 'Lengua' are labels Catlin applied to several pictures in the series, and the village here is less a place than a group of people on a hill. Rivers supplied such settlements with food and transport. The mood is idyllic, in keeping with the nineteenth-century image of Indigenous life as simple and harmonious, an image Catlin helped shape and one a modern viewer should treat with caution.","c":"The pastel palette and soft sun create a mood quite different from the dense-green majority of the series, and the high viewpoint gives a clear overview of the village. But the sweetness comes close to prettiness, and the figures on the hill are assembled, not seen. As a decorative landscape it succeeds; as a record it is thin."},"nga:50610":{"d":"Tall trees and lush greenery fill the landscape, rising to a high horizon with just a glimpse of sky. Water in the foreground carries people in canoes, and farther back figures stand near huts on a hill. The water, the hill and the trees form three clear bands, a layered arrangement common in the series. Soft brushstrokes and earthy greens and browns keep the atmosphere tranquil. Oil on card, about 46 by 63 centimeters.","i":"This is a second 'small Lengua village', with 'Uruguay' added to the title. That one label serves for two pictures may suggest how Catlin worked: he painted variations on a theme from a stock of sketches and memories, not portraits of single, documented places. The canoes in the foreground again point to the river as the approach to the village and its lifeline.","c":"The three-band structure of water, hill and trees is clear and restful, and the soft handling suits it. But set beside its twin, A Small Lengua Village, this picture shows how similar the compositions of the series could be: the same village on the same kind of rise, the same canoes. It is pleasant, and easy to confuse with its neighbors."},"nga:50611":{"d":"In an oval frame, a river scene opens with people and canoes at the water, some paddling, others fishing. Grassy banks lead up to a settlement of simple structures where groups of people gather. Tall trees form a canopy above the houses and rise into the sky behind. The brushwork is detailed, building texture into the foliage and the ground, and the palette runs through earthy greens, yellows, browns and blues. Oil on card, about 48 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The Payaguá were canoe people of the Paraguay River, renowned for their skill on the water and, to the Spanish, for river raiding in the colonial period. A scene of fishing and paddling therefore suits them. The title uses the plural 'Payaguas Indians' in the style of the period. By Catlin's day the group had been much reduced, and sources on their life are scattered.","c":"The oval format and the many small activities give this picture a pleasing density, and the canoes lead the eye toward the settlement more elegantly than in many of the series. The treatment is conventional: a green bank, a cluster of roofs, a canopy of trees. The attraction lies in the arrangement, which has a gentle logic, and not in the observation."},"nga:50612":{"d":"A pastoral view, taken from a slightly raised position, shows a field of small plants and flowers in the foreground, painted with fine, intricate strokes. Beyond, simple thatched buildings sit among greenery, and a line of trees, with tall palms silhouetted against a pastel sky, marks the horizon in the upper third. The brushwork is delicate, the colors soft greens, warm earth tones and gentle pastels, and a surrounding frame echoes the scene's tones. Oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The Remos, or Remo, were a people of the Ucayali region of Peru, recorded in missionary and traveler accounts of the Amazon. Catlin's picture is less concerned with them than with the pastoral mood, created by the flowering field and the pastel sky. Such an arrangement set Native life within a peaceful landscape of fields, a convention familiar from European painting, and appealed to viewers who wished to see harmony.","c":"A real change of mood from the green walls elsewhere: the flowering foreground, the open sky and the silhouetted palms make an airy, garden-like picture, and the delicacy of the touch is attractive. It is also the least ethnographic of the villages, because people are not described and the field takes center stage. Charming and slightly sentimental."},"nga:50613":{"d":"From a raised viewpoint, huts nestle in a lush, wooded setting beneath a low horizon. A group of figures gathers in the foreground, and trees rise behind them, with dense vegetation framing the sides of the scene. The brushstrokes are smooth, giving the trees a dense, even look, and the palette is warm and earthy, with greens, browns and soft sky blue. Oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"Sepibo is Catlin's spelling of Shipibo, the Panoan-speaking people of the Ucayali River in Peru, known today above all for intricate geometric designs on cloth and pottery, a tradition said to be centuries old. Nothing in the picture shows that art, but the name is a reminder of how much Catlin's small scenes leave out about the cultures they name.","c":"The elevated view lets the huts be seen clearly, and the foreground group gives the picture a human center that is easy to read. The finish is smooth and slightly lifeless; the forest is a tidy frame rather than a living mass. A competent village portrait whose main virtue is clarity."},"nga:50614":{"d":"Open water spreads across the view, with a boat in the foreground and a belt of lush green on the far bank. The sky is painted in shades of gray and blue with soft clouds, and the whole picture is held to a cool scheme of blues and greens warmed with browns. It is a small oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters, and its subject is a river junction, where wide water gives the sky more room than most of the series allows.","i":"The Rio Purus is a large southern tributary of the Amazon, entering the main river in Brazil upstream of Manaus. A river mouth is a natural subject for a traveler by boat, since it marks a point where one waterway gives onto another and the horizon opens. For Catlin, journeying through the Amazon system, it offered a rare moment of breadth in a landscape where forest usually blocked the view.","c":"With the sky given room and the foliage reduced to a band, this is one of the more open and relaxed pictures in the series, and the cool palette is a welcome relief from the heat of the jungle interiors. It does not go further: the boat is a placeholder and the clouds are generic. Quiet, honest and unremarkable."},"nga:50615":{"d":"An oval opening shows a stretch of open land from a raised point of view. Several figures are engaged in some activity there, with a few animals among them, in front of a high wall of palms and other trees. The sky above is soft blue and gray, and the greens vary widely, with occasional touches of bright red among the leaves. Fine brushstrokes detail the plants and people for a realistic, contained effect. Oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The title describes the travelers' pause: the party has stopped so that the painter can draw. The fact that figures and animals share the clearing makes it a small expedition scene, in which Catlin shows himself, at least by implication, among his companions. His books about his journeys likewise put the traveler at the center, and the oval view gives the halt the look of a keepsake.","c":"The oval vignette and the clearing in front of the green wall give a neat, self-contained composition, and the touches of red in the leaves are a good surprise. The weakness is that no act of sketching is clearly visible: what the title promises is not clearly seen. As a quiet travel keepsake it works well enough."},"nga:50616":{"d":"A scene of everyday life unfolds in the foreground, where a group of people is busy with some task, some of them near a modest, sloped-roof shelter at the left. Dense green foliage stretches across the middle of the picture beneath a horizon set just above center. Soft brushwork gives sky, trees and leaves a smooth texture, and the colors are muted: greens and browns, with soft blues and grays. Oil on card, about 48 by 63 centimeters.","i":"'Wigwam' is a word from the Algonquian languages of North America, which Catlin and his contemporaries used loosely for almost any Indigenous dwelling. Its use for a Conibo house on the Ucayali shows how he carried the vocabulary of his Plains years into South America. The activity of the group is not specified in the museum description, so the picture is best read as a general record of household life.","c":"Observation of daily life is Catlin's declared ambition, and the muted tones and the unforced grouping suit it here. Yet the picture never specifies what the people are doing, the faces are blank, and the word 'wigwam' says more about Catlin's vocabulary than about Conibo building. An amiable scene that sketches a way of life without examining it."},"nga:50617":{"d":"Thatched huts stand on a broad yellow plain under a sky tinted with soft blues and pinks. A line of greenery and trees marks the horizon, and a lone figure can be made out near the buildings in the distance. The brushstrokes are soft, and the colors combine earthy greens and yellows with pale sky tones. With almost no clutter in the foreground, the picture is notably open. Oil on card, about 46 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The name Pacapacurus is not explained in the museum record, and its modern equivalent is uncertain; it is probably Catlin's rendering of a name heard on his travels. The yellow plain suggests dry grassland, unlike the wet forest of most of the series, and the lone figure underscores the settlement's isolation.","c":"The emptiness is the picture's strength. With few elements, huts, a field of yellow, a thin line of trees and one figure, Catlin achieves a sense of distance and quiet that his denser pictures miss. It risks blandness, and the huts are barely characterized, but the restraint is welcome, and the pale pinks of the sky give the scene a gentle warmth."},"nga:50618":{"d":"Night covers a river. From the bank, the view runs across dark water to a line of trees fading into blackness in the upper third of the picture. A red-orange glow at the water's edge lights the nearby foliage, and a canoe with figures moves across the surface near it, heading for the dim shores. Dark clouds hang above. Deep blues and blacks dominate, and the loose, almost impressionistic brushwork gives movement to leaves and water. Oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Fishing at night with a torch to attract fish and a spear to take them was practiced on many rivers of the Americas, and Catlin painted the night-spearing scene more than once. The torch makes a small hearth of light in a vast darkness, a favorite effect of Romantic painters, and lets a labor scene turn into a quiet drama of fire and shadow.","c":"The glow against the darkness is a theatrical but effective device, and the looser, broader touch allowed by a night subject frees Catlin from the leaf-by-leaf detail that clogs his daylight jungle pictures. This is one of the stronger paintings of its type: it is simple, it takes a risk with value, and the red fire does real work. The figures and the canoe are cursory, but the mood is convincing."},"nga:50619":{"d":"Five people pose at full length against a pale, textured background: four stand and one woman sits on the ground with a child beside her. Two of the standing figures hold long pipes, and another seems to hold a bow and arrow. The men wear plumed headdresses, cloaks, patterned and beaded garments and moccasins; the seated woman wears a patterned dress, arm jewelry and bead necklaces. Facial features are carefully drawn. The sheet, about 45 by 60 centimeters, has a worn green border with the number 37 at the top.","i":"Konza is a form of Kansa (Kaw), the Siouan-speaking people who gave their name to the state of Kansas. The group is a composed portrait in the manner of Catlin's gallery, arranging a chief and members of his community, men and women with a child, to stand for the nation. Painted in the 1860s, it is probably adapted from earlier portraits and sketches of the 1830s.","c":"As a group portrait this is stiffer than Catlin's best single likenesses, with the figures set side by side like a display. The costume detail is where he takes care, and the seated woman and child add a note of warmth. Painted in the 1860s from earlier material, it carries the formality of a studio recollection and little of the encounter that gave the portraits of the 1830s their immediacy."},"nga:50620":{"d":"Lush trees and undergrowth enclose a clearing where several figures go about everyday tasks while horses graze close by. The brushstrokes are detailed, and the palette is held to natural tones, with hints of red. The moment is unspecific: nothing in the record singles out an event, so this is a scene of ordinary life in a forest camp, painted in oil on card, about 47 by 62 centimeters, with the animals and the people given about equal weight.","i":"The horses are the notable detail. They arrived in the Americas with the Spanish and spread widely, transforming Indigenous societies, above all on the plains, where Catlin first painted mounted hunters. A forest camp with grazing horses links the two halves of his career, though the title names no people and no place, so the picture stands for a general idea of Native life with horses.","c":"Unassuming and a little muddled: the figures and horses are present but not arranged, and the forest does not make a convincing setting for animals that, in Catlin's North American work, belong on open prairie. The charm lies in the casualness, in the sense of a camp glimpsed in passing. As a composition it never quite comes into focus."},"nga:50622":{"d":"Five figures are shown at full length. Two stand at the left, one with arms crossed and one holding a staff. At the center a seated man holds a feathered staff and looks toward a companion who gestures with an outstretched hand, and at the far right another stands with a shawl over one shoulder. All five wear orange and black feathered hair extensions, fringed garments with vivid patterns, and beads or necklaces. The background is a softly colored sky over earth tones, with no landmarks. Oil on card, about 46 by 62 centimeters.","i":"Black Hawk (1767–1838) was the Sauk leader who fought the United States in 1832, in the war that carries his name; the Prophet, probably Wabokieshiek, was his adviser. Catlin painted Black Hawk in 1832 after his defeat, when the captive leaders were held at Jefferson Barracks near St. Louis. Which figures are meant here is not stated in the museum record.","c":"Group portraits of this kind turn history into a tableau. Catlin gives the poses an almost theatrical clarity, with gesture and staff doing the talking, and the feathered hairstyles and fringed garments are painted with evident interest. But the faces are less alive than in his on-the-spot portraits of 1832, and the empty background leaves the figures floating. A formal, studio-made memorial to men Catlin had drawn from life decades earlier."},"nga:50623":{"d":"A large crowd gathers around an open space seen from a slightly raised viewpoint. People move in circular formations across the central ground, which they dominate, while tipis and thatched structures stand at the left. Beyond, a high horizon holds low land, distant trees and a few buildings under a soft sky. The brushwork is detailed and precise, and the palette combines earthy browns and greens with muted blues. Oil on card, about 46 by 61 centimeters.","i":"Catlin wrote at length about Native dances and ceremonies and painted many of them, especially among the Mandan, Sioux and other peoples of the plains. 'Apachees' is the old plural spelling of Apache, the Southwestern people. Apache dwellings varied from brush-covered wickiups to the hide tipis of the Plains Apache, so the mix of tipis and thatch is not necessarily a confusion.","c":"A scene of mass movement is hard to paint, and Catlin copes by showing it from above in neat circles: the rhythm of the dance is legible, though the figures lose individuality and the whole becomes a pattern. The precise handling gives it a documentary seriousness. Compared with his well-known Mandan ceremonial pictures, it feels like a studio recollection rather than an eyewitness account."},"nga:50650":{"d":"A large conical tipi, with circular patterns painted near its entrance, stands in the foreground, and two figures interact beside it. More tipis and domed structures are arranged farther back, among trees and low brush. The horizon is high, so the soft pastel sky takes up much of the picture. Smooth strokes and a palette of earthy greens, browns and ochres give a calm, daylight effect. Oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The Minatarree, or Hidatsa, lived with the Mandan in earth-lodge villages on the upper Missouri, which Catlin visited in 1832. A vapor bath was a practice of purification and health among many Plains peoples: steam was made by pouring water on heated stones inside a closed lodge, and bathers often plunged into cold water afterward. The tipi in the picture may serve as that bath lodge.","c":"The subject is rare and interesting, but the picture treats it as little more than a lodge in a landscape: the steam, the heat and the plunge, which Catlin describes with relish in his writings, are absent. The pastel sky and smooth handling give the village a charming calm, while the painted circles on the tipi are the liveliest detail. The picture illustrates its title without exploring it."},"nga:50651":{"d":"Five figures stand on grass in an oval vignette: four adults and a child. A woman at the left holds the child; two men and the woman face slightly left, while a man at the right, holding a bow, turns toward them. He wears a feathered headdress and a long fringed shirt and carries a circular, feather-decorated object; the man beside him is wrapped in a thick robe. All have long dark hair. A blue and pink sky and a brown-toned border complete the picture. Oil on card, about 44 by 61 centimeters.","i":"The title names its figures by role: two chiefs, a medicine man and a woman with a child. A medicine man was a spiritual and healing figure, and the circular feathered object may be a shield or a medicine object, which fits a leader's regalia. The group stands for a whole community, in line with Catlin's aim of recording a nation, not just its notables.","c":"Among the 1860s group portraits, this is a relatively humane one: the mother and child soften the row of notables, and the oval format and grassy ground make a compact, balanced group. The individual faces, though, are generic and the poses repeat; the emphasis falls on costume. It shows what Catlin wished to preserve more than what the sitters were like."},"nga:50652":{"d":"This is not a scene but a copy of an object: the painted decoration of a Mandan robe. A set of concentric circles forms the center, filled with intricate geometric patterns and spiky triangular accents and crossed by a horizontal line. Stylized animal shapes and human-like figures surround the central motif, and the whole is enclosed within an irregular, scalloped edge that suggests the outline of the original hide. Oil on card, about 46 by 60 centimeters.","i":"Mandan robes were buffalo hides painted with designs: some record the deeds of a warrior, others carry geometric or sun-like motifs of spiritual meaning. Catlin visited the Mandan villages in 1832, five years before the smallpox epidemic of 1837 nearly destroyed the people, and recorded their ceremonies and objects. A facsimile preserves a design that the original owner would have understood and that the museum record does not explain.","c":"Pattern and symbol replace the usual scenery, and the result is more arresting than many of the landscapes. The flat, graphic design suits the card, and Catlin's role as copyist is respectful. It is not an artwork of invention but a record, whose value is documentary; as a picture it is spare and slightly dry."},"nga:50653":{"d":"On a snowy plain, a wounded buffalo lies in the foreground with blood showing on its coat. A second buffalo is down farther off, and two hunters on snowshoes approach. Rolling hills and groups of buffalo fill the background, and snow-covered pines stand at the right beneath a bright blue sky touched with pink and gray. Catlin paints the animals in precise detail and the snow and sky in smoother passages. This one is oil on canvas mounted on paperboard, about 46 by 64 centimeters.","i":"Hunting buffalo in deep snow was an effective winter method: the animals floundered in drifts, and hunters on snowshoes could approach and kill them with lances at close quarters. Catlin painted winter hunts of this kind in his Plains work. The scene is not a battle but a working hunt, important for food and hides, and it shows the buffalo's central place in Plains life.","c":"One of the most vivid of Catlin's small oils on card: the bleeding animal in the foreground has real weight, the pale blue sky is bright and cold, and the contrast of detailed animals with softer snow is well judged. Catlin's buffalo are always livelier than his people, which suggests why the Plains remained his strongest subject. The hunters, though, are small and generic, the cost of working on this scale."},"nga:50658":{"d":"From a raised viewpoint the eye looks over a camp of tipis scattered across open ground, with figures busy among them. Low hills close the background, and the sky glows softly, as at sunrise or sunset, in golden tones. Green vegetation spreads into the distance, and a river, partly hidden, runs along the right. The palette is of earthy browns and greens with gold in the sky. Oil on card, about 47 by 63 centimeters.","i":"The Crow, who call themselves Apsáalooke, were a horse-rich Plains people of the Yellowstone and Bighorn valleys, and travelers of the period praised their large, well-made skin lodges. The Salmon River named in the title flows through Idaho, outside the Crow's main homeland, a discrepancy the museum record does not explain. Catlin's first date of 1855 falls long after his Plains journeys of the 1830s.","c":"A well-judged composition: the raised view lets the tipis be counted and placed, and the golden light behind low hills gives the camp a settled, peaceful glow. This is Catlin on familiar ground, and the confidence shows, since the tipis are drawn with more conviction than the huts of his Amazon pictures. It remains a distant, tidy view and not an intimate one."},"nga:50659":{"d":"A single figure stands in a small boat in a dim jungle, holding a long fishing spear while a torch lights the scene. Dense vegetation climbs upward and merges with the dark sky, and fluid brushstrokes give the leaves a sense of movement. Dark greens dominate, and orange and red light from the flame is reflected on the water. The canopy hides the background and gives a feeling of depth and seclusion. This is a slightly smaller sheet than most, about 41 by 57 centimeters.","i":"Torch-fishing works because fish attracted to the light can be speared from a boat held motionless on the surface. The method was practiced across the Americas, and Catlin used it as a subject several times in this series. Compared with the daytime river scenes, the dark forest and the flame make the Amazon appear mysterious, in line with the image of the tropical interior as a world apart that travelers' books promoted.","c":"Among Catlin's night scenes this one is the most concentrated: a single figure, one flame and the surrounding dark. The reflected orange on the water is a well-observed touch, and the mood is stronger than in the busier daytime scenes. The foliage is somewhat repetitive, but the drama of light is genuine."},"nga:12126":{"d":"A vertical panel, about 81 by 62 centimetres, painted in oil, shows the Holy Family attended by angels: the Virgin Mary with the infant Christ, Joseph, and heavenly attendants who gather around them. Domenico Beccafumi, the leading Sienese painter of his day, was known for twisting, sculptural figures, unusually vivid colours and a strange, glowing light, and a late work such as this one, from the 1540s, is likely to show these traits.","i":"The Holy Family, Mary, Joseph and the Christ Child, was a favourite subject of private devotion, presenting domestic life as a model of piety and order. Angels in attendance show that the family is also part of the divine plan: they may adore the Child, make music or hold his attributes. In Beccafumi's Siena, pictures of this kind were made for private chapels and homes. The group is usually read as a quiet celebration of the Incarnation.","c":"Beccafumi was among the most inventive of the Mannerists: he used acid, shifting colours, twisting poses and a glowing, smoky light unlike anything in Florence or Rome. A Holy Family gives him room for these gifts. The cost is a certain strangeness, as figures can seem elongated or over-twisted, and the devotion is cooler than in earlier Sienese painting. For admirers the strangeness is the attraction; for others it is mannered. Either way, it is unmistakably his own."},"met:437913":{"d":"A saint, shown reading, fills a small panel of about 47 by 38 centimetres, painted in tempera against a gold ground. The Met tentatively names him Saint Mark the Evangelist. With its gold background and single figure, it has the look of a section from a polyptych, the multi-panel altarpiece favoured in Venice, in which individual saints stood side by side in separate frames.","i":"Mark, the author of the second Gospel, is patron saint of Venice and is usually shown with a book or scroll and his symbol, the winged lion. A saint shown absorbed in reading presents the evangelist as writer and teacher and invites the viewer to contemplate the word. The query in the title reflects the absence of a clear attribute; other readers, such as the Church Fathers, were also shown reading. Vivarini's workshop on Murano supplied such panels to churches in Venice and beyond.","c":"Vivarini's style is hard and linear, with the sculptural drapery and stern gaze that he learned partly from Mantegna's example. The reading pose is a quiet change from the usual blessing or staring figure, and the gold ground gives the panel a jewel-like sharpness. But the manner was beginning to look conservative in the 1470s beside the soft light of Giovanni Bellini, and the figure is somewhat severe. It is a fine fragment rather than a complete statement."},"nga:259":{"d":"Shown from the waist up behind a gray stone ledge, with a landscape of trees, cliffs and winding paths behind, Mary holds the chubby Christ Child in the crook of one elbow. Both have flushed cheeks, fair hair and gold-edged halos. Her marine-blue mantle, edged in gold with a plum lining, covers a veil and an apple-red dress; one hand gestures toward the child, who sits on a white pillow on the ledge in a pine-green tunic. A purple banner hangs behind her. Tempera on poplar panel, about 53 by 41.5 centimeters, c. 1475.","i":"Half-length Madonnas behind a ledge were the staple devotional image of fifteenth-century Venice, made for private chapels and bedrooms. Bartolomeo Vivarini, who came from a family workshop on Murano, kept to a more linear, sculptural manner shaped partly by Paduan art, while Giovanni Bellini was making the type softer and more atmospheric. The hanging banner is a cloth of honor that marks Mary as Queen of Heaven, and the ledge sets the sacred figures just beyond the viewer's reach.","c":"A calm, finely finished devotional picture whose clear line, crisp folds and enamel-like color belong to a tradition that Bellini's softer Madonnas were overtaking. Vivarini's version is cooler and more formal, but the mother's lowered gaze and the child's sturdy form give the group quiet dignity, and the landscape's winding paths make a pleasing counterpoint. It is conservative but accomplished, a good example of the high craft of a Venetian workshop."},"met:437912":{"d":"An altarpiece-scale panel, about 190 by 150 centimetres, painted in tempera on wood in 1484. It shows the Death of the Virgin, the Dormition, in which Mary dies surrounded by the apostles. The Met's keywords record Mary, Christ and angels; in the traditional composition Christ appears beside the bier to receive her soul, which he holds in the form of a small child, while angels attend from above.","i":"The Death of the Virgin comes from apocryphal accounts of the apostles gathering miraculously at Mary's deathbed, and from the Golden Legend. Christ receives her soul, shown as a swaddled infant, and carries it to heaven; her body follows in the Assumption. In Venice, a city especially devoted to the Virgin, the subject was a comfort to the faithful, who saw in Mary's peaceful death a promise of their own salvation.","c":"Vivarini was a master of the Venetian polyptych, and the monumental scale shows his gift for solemn, firmly drawn figures with deep-cut drapery. But in 1484 the style had begun to look severe next to the soft atmospheric manner of Giovanni Bellini, and stiff gestures and sharp outlines can hold emotion at a distance. It is an imposing, archaic work: more monument than drama, and the more impressive for its size."},"met:436114":{"d":"A group of monkeys in human dress are the experts of the title, and they appear as connoisseurs appraising a painting. A top hat, a walking stick, a book, a parasol, a palette and a chair are among the props, so the animals are dressed and equipped as critics and artists in a studio. The oil on canvas, about 46 by 64 centimeters, was painted in 1837 in the French genre known as the singerie.","i":"A singerie shows monkeys behaving like people, a French tradition from the early eighteenth century used both for decoration and for satire; the monkey, which imitates without understanding, was a standing emblem of the mere copyist. Chardin's Monkey Painter of about 1740 had already used the animal to mock painters, and Decamps turned it against judges and critics. The picture has been read as a jibe at the Salon jury.","c":"Decamps handles the joke with a straight face and a sure touch. It is a sharper and funnier picture than most of the period's serious genre, and the satire lands because the monkeys' human roles are so well chosen. The weakness, as with all satire, is topicality: some of the target, a Parisian jury, has lost its sting. But the wit travels well, and this is among his most enjoyable pictures."},"met:436115":{"d":"In the parable of the Good Samaritan, a traveler beaten and robbed on the road is helped by a passing stranger. A horse, a man, a woman and a town appear, so the scene includes the road, the helper's mount and a place of shelter. The oil on canvas, about 93 by 74 centimeters, is dated by the Met to before 1853, and the vertical format gives the figures weight.","i":"The parable (Luke 10:25-37) answers the question of who counts as a neighbor: a priest and a Levite pass by a man left half-dead on the road from Jerusalem to Jericho, while a Samaritan, a member of a despised group, binds his wounds, brings him to an inn and pays for his care. Nineteenth-century painters, among them Delacroix, took it up as an image of compassion.","c":"Decamps brought to biblical subjects the heat, dust and rough surface of his Eastern pictures, treating the parable as a scene in a real country. The humane message gains force from this plain presentation, yet the broad, thick handling can seem murky, and the emotion is less direct than in Delacroix's treatment of the same text. It is a serious, characteristic picture rather than a popular one."},"met:436116":{"d":"A military patrol, armed men on horseback among them, is the subject of this Orientalist canvas, about 74 by 92 centimeters, dated to about 1855 to 1856. Men, weapons and horses carry the military theme. Decamps had traveled to Greece and Asia Minor in 1827 and 1828 and made his name with pictures of what he saw and imagined there, in strong light and thick paint.","i":"Orientalist pictures of patrols, guards and caravans were popular with French collectors from the 1830s, combining adventure with picturesque costume and an image of an exotic state in which armed authority was always near. Decamps, one of the first French painters to work on such subjects after personal travel, set a pattern that later painters such as Gérôme polished. Scholars now note how selective the vision was.","c":"Decamps's Orient is a place of heat, shadow and heavy paint, and by the mid-1850s he had been repeating the theme for twenty years. The picture reads as a polished reprise rather than a fresh discovery, the surface thick and worked, the color earthy rather than bright. Beside the cooler, more finished Orientalism of Gérôme it is rougher and more personal, and more convincing for it."},"met:435651":{"d":"Christ's dead body is mourned by his mother, Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene in this tall devotional picture, about 91 by 56 centimeters, painted around 1520 to 1525. Sadness and suffering are the subject: the lamentation over the body after the Crucifixion. The paint layer has been transferred from its original wooden panel to canvas, a restoration treatment that moves the painting to a new support.","i":"The Lamentation is the scene of grief after Christ is taken down from the Cross and before the Entombment. It is not described in detail in the Gospels, and medieval devotion elaborated it as an image to meditate on Mary's sorrow and Christ's sacrifice. John was the disciple who stood by the Cross; Mary Magdalene, who anointed Christ's feet, is typically shown with her jar of ointment. Benson was Italian-born and worked in Bruges.","c":"Benson's work belongs to the gentle Bruges tradition of Gerard David, with a touch of Italianate sweetness, and the grief here is controlled and tender rather than violent. The tall format concentrates the figures and presses their sorrow on the viewer. The transfer to canvas has probably flattened some of the surface, so the picture affects more as design than as painting. It is a good example of the sober, intimate devotion of Bruges around 1520."},"nga:94743":{"d":"A bouquet of flowers in a glass vase, painted in oil on copper, about 32 by 22 centimetres, in 1621, the year of the painter's death. Bosschaert, the founder of the Dutch flower piece, usually arranged his bouquets symmetrically, with each bloom clearly visible and lit, often on a ledge with a shell, an insect or a butterfly. The tiny scale and the polished copper support give the picture a jewel-like, enamel finish.","i":"Bosschaert's bouquets combine flowers that bloom in different seasons, such as tulips, roses, irises and lilies, so that no real bouquet could look like this; in the early Dutch Republic, with its new botanical gardens and passion for rare bulbs, a picture of every precious flower together was a luxury. The glass vase, the insects and the flowers' brief beauty could also remind viewers that life is short. Bosschaert, born in Antwerp, worked in Middelburg and other Dutch towns.","c":"Bosschaert set the pattern for flower painting: each bloom is observed with scientific clarity and given its own space, the colours are clear, and the vase and ledge provide a serene order. The stiffness is the price of this clarity, and the arrangements lack the lushness of later Baroque bouquets. But the picture has the calm and jewel-like finish that made his work the standard. It is small, precise and quietly beautiful."},"met:442849":{"d":"Women sew at a table in this small oil on canvas, about 26 by 38 centimeters, from the mid-1890s. Eugène Carrière usually painted in a nearly monochrome range of browns and grays, and his figures seem to emerge from a warm mist; the subject, women at quiet domestic work, was typical of his intimate scenes. The picture is a cabinet-sized study rather than a large exhibition canvas.","i":"Carrière's favorite subject was the family: mothers with children, sleeping infants, women working at home. These were meant as secular images of tenderness, free of anecdote, in which the absence of detail encouraged symbolic readings. The Symbolists admired him, and he counted artists such as Rodin among his friends. Sewing, a task of patience and care, suits the quiet, devotional tone of his imagery.","c":"Carrière's smoky manner dissolves forms into atmosphere, which suits a quiet scene like this one but risks monotony: the brown fog makes distinctions of texture and space hard to read. At his best he creates an unusual hush, and at his weakest the mist becomes a formula. This small canvas belongs to his mature mode and shows the method clearly."},"met:435856":{"d":"The painter's own face is the subject of this self-portrait, an oil on canvas of about 41 by 33 centimeters, dated around 1893. Eugène Carrière, a French Symbolist, typically let his heads emerge from a dark, misty ground, so that features appear to condense out of shadow. The picture is a close, intimate study rather than a formal portrait, and its small, modest format keeps attention on the head alone.","i":"Self-portraits by Symbolist painters are less about outward likeness than about inner life, and Carrière's smoky blending of face into ground turns his own features into a meditation on presence and disappearance. He was in his mid-forties then, and he had become known for his tender pictures of family life and for portraits of writers such as Verlaine.","c":"Carrière's blur is both his signature and his limit. Here it concentrates attention on the face and eyes in a way that is more intimate than a sharp likeness would be, and the result is sober, even melancholy. It is a reticent self-portrait, without bravura, and its quiet insistence is its main virtue."},"nga:177785":{"d":"A baby is shown from head to toe, upright, possibly seated or reclined, with soft, rounded features, rosy cheeks, small eyes and a slight smile. The hair is sparse and light, and the baby wears a light garment with red accents on the feet, likely booties or socks. One hand holds a silver object on a ribbon, possibly a rattle. The background is dark with red tones, which sets off the pale clothing. The painting is about 56 by 46 centimeters, dated 1899 or 1900, and signed Eugène Carrière at the upper left.","i":"Carrière, a French Symbolist, painted his wife, children and friends in a smoky, nearly monochrome manner, in which figures emerge from brown-gray shadow as if seen through mist. He made maternal and family subjects the center of his art, believing that the simplest human bond carried a universal meaning. The sitter shares the artist's surname, which suggests a child of his circle or family. The picture is a tender portrait of infancy.","c":"Carrière's blurred, shadowy style can feel mannered, as if every subject were seen in a dream. Here the pale garment and the slight smile against the dark ground produce a gentle, almost devotional effect, and the shimmer of the rattle gives a note of life. It is less ambitious than his large maternal compositions, but the tenderness is convincing. A small, quiet picture that matches the intimacy of his vision."},"nga:52146":{"d":"Dozens of men and women, mostly dressed in black and charcoal gray, walk or ride in carriages along a busy Paris street in this narrow vertical canvas. In the foreground a woman in a midnight-blue jacket and black hat carries two round hat boxes and holds up her skirt to show a white petticoat. A streetlamp with several lanterns stands at the left, posters cover a curving storefront whose sign reads GRANDS MAGASINS DU PRINTEMPS, and scaffolding hangs above. Rows of oatmeal-brown buildings recede to the left under a pale blue sky.","i":"The street is the rue du Havre beside the Gare Saint-Lazare, where the Printemps department store stood; department stores were the new temples of consumption in the 1880s, drawing crowds of shoppers, and the woman with hat boxes is one of them. The dark clothing marks bourgeois Paris. Béraud, a chronicler of Belle Époque life, painted such scenes with the keen eye of a reporter, and the scaffolding may belong to the rebuilding of the store after its fire in 1881.","c":"Béraud's talent is for compressing the energy of a busy street into a small panel: the crowd in black is varied by gestures, the woman in the foreground lifts her skirt with a flash of white, and the lamp, posters and signs give a vivid sense of place. The color is restrained and the handling brisk. It lacks the poetry of Monet's or Caillebotte's Paris, but as a witty chronicle of the city it delights."},"nga:195475":{"d":"A large sailing frigate flying the American flag at lower right takes part in a sea battle: it has fully extended sails, a gray hull with a wide white stripe and cannon ports, with tiny sailors climbing the rigging and sails dotted, perhaps by shot holes. At left a more distant ship fires its guns, bursts of yellow flame and smoke drifting over smooth green water; faint silhouettes of other ships fill the background. The sky grades from pale yellow at the horizon to blue above. Oil on canvas, about 71 by 107 centimeters, 1850.","i":"The picture shows the last action of the frigate President, which in January 1815, under Stephen Decatur, was caught off New York by a British squadron and surrendered after a running fight. The battle took place weeks after the peace treaty was signed at Ghent, but before news reached America. Lane painted it in 1850, long after the war, when naval history was a popular subject for patriotic pictures.","c":"Lane is best known for calm, light-filled harbor scenes, and here he applies his clean drawing of ships and his smooth, glassy sea to an action subject. The result is orderly rather than ferocious: the sky is serene, the water smooth, and the gunfire reads as a note in the composition rather than its point. As history it is dutiful and precise; as drama it is cooler than most battle pictures."},"nga:84783":{"d":"Large sailing ships and smaller vessels crowd a harbor in this horizontal view. Right of center, a big ship with a black hull, a long bowsprit and many billowing white sails carries tiny figures across its deck; another large vessel stands at far left, and three smaller boats sail left of center and at far right. All fly flags, including red-and-white stripes with a blue corner. A small barrel floats near the bottom of the gray sea, and a distant shore with more sails, smokestacks and dark buildings lies under a pale blue sky and pink clouds. Oil on canvas, about 60 by 89 centimeters, 1852.","i":"By 1852 New York was the busiest port in the United States, and the smokestacks on the distant shore hint at the industry growing around it. Lane, a native of Gloucester who had trained as a lithographer in Boston, made harbor pictures with the exact drawing of rigging that shipowners and merchants valued. A view like this honors sailing ships at the moment steam power and industry were beginning to change the harbor.","c":"Lane's New York Harbor is an exercise in order: the black-hulled ship placed off center, the smaller craft carefully spaced, the pearly sea and sky unified in a gentle luminous calm. The little floating barrel at the front gives scale and a hint of anecdote. It is a bustling subject treated with his usual composure, and it shows his gift for making commerce look serene."},"met:11395":{"d":"The Golden State enters New York Harbor in this wide canvas of 1854, about 66 by 122 centimeters. Ships and boats are the picture's subject, and the harbor's traffic fills the water around the principal vessel. It is probably a ship portrait of the kind owners and captains commissioned to record their vessels, set within the life of the port, and it is painted with the clear light that marks Fitz Henry Lane.","i":"The 1850s were the golden age of American shipping, when the California gold rush and the China trade brought a boom in fast vessels, and New York was the busiest port in the nation. Ship portraits showed a vessel at its best, under sail or steam, with accurate rigging and flags. A picture like this one was a source of pride for its owners and a record of a vessel's arrival.","c":"Lane combines the precision of a ship portraitist with a gift for atmosphere that lifts the picture beyond the genre. The calm water, the crisp reflections and the luminous sky are typical of his mature style, now called luminism. The harbor traffic is arranged clearly, if a little formally, and the effect is serene, with not a hint of the bustle that must have filled the port."},"nga:76213":{"d":"Two ships and two sailboats, all under full sail, float on gently rippling water before a pale pastel sky. A rounded rock rises near the center in the hazy distance, and the low sun behind the first ship's sails makes a golden glow around it. At left a man in a red shirt rows a boat with two traps toward a two-masted ship with three figures on deck; at right three vessels cluster, including a three-masted boat with four men, seven barrels and two dinghies trailing. Oil on canvas, about 70 by 120 centimeters, signed and dated 1860.","i":"Halfway Rock is a small ledge in Casco Bay off the coast of Maine, a landmark for coastal traffic, and Lane returned to Maine's waters repeatedly. Becalmed vessels wait for wind, a condition that let him paint still water and hazy light, the qualities that make him a leading figure of American Luminism, the style that prized quiet atmosphere, hidden brushwork and a glowing horizon.","c":"A fine example of Lane's mature Luminist manner, in which almost invisible brushwork, a low sun and a hazy pastel sky turn a minor coastal incident into a hush. The rigging, delicately outlined against the sky, and the tiny working figures keep the picture rooted in observation. Calm is hard to paint without dullness; Lane succeeds by tuning color and the spacing of the vessels."},"met:11396":{"d":"A calm harbor stretches across this wide canvas of 1862, about 97 by 152 centimeters, with ships, boats, trees and small human figures. Stage Fort, the headland that gives the picture its title, lies across the water of Gloucester Harbor. The scene is in Fitz Henry Lane's native town on the coast of Massachusetts, and he paints it with his usual clarity of light and precise detail of vessels and shore.","i":"Stage Fort, probably named for the stages or scaffolds on which early settlers dried fish, was the site of an English fishing settlement on Cape Ann in the 1620s, so the view carries historical associations. Lane painted it in 1862, in the middle of the Civil War, and the serenity of the harbor may be read as a counterpoint to the national conflict, though the picture makes no such statement.","c":"This is Lane at his best: the water is a luminous mirror, the light is exact, and the composition is built from quiet horizontals that give an effect of timeless calm. The human figures are small and the incident minimal, which only increases the sense of stillness. It is one of the clearest statements of luminist taste in American painting."},"nga:57611":{"d":"A masted wooden vessel floats near the center against a vivid sunset that fades from lilac purple to carnation pink along a low horizon. It is angled away from us, one sail tied up near the top of a tall mast, while four people stand on its lumber-filled deck tying up other sails. A second boat in the distance is silhouetted against the pink sky; the water is deep blue at the bottom and lightens toward hills at the horizon, under wisps of slate-gray cloud. Oil on canvas, about 63 by 97 centimeters, 1863.","i":"Penobscot Bay on the Maine coast was a center of the lumber trade, and schooners carried timber from the state's forests to growing cities, so the picture records both a working vessel and an evening mood. Lane returned to Maine repeatedly. Painted in 1863, with the Civil War raging elsewhere, its stillness can be read as a note of calm, though any such reading is the viewer's.","c":"One of Lane's most celebrated effects: a sky shifting from lilac to carnation pink above deep blue water, with the ship in silhouette. The composition is simple, a single vessel at the center and a second in the distance, and the reduction creates the stillness. The formula risks repetition across his work, but here the color is exactly judged, and the sunset feels observed rather than decorated."},"nga:127202":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 25 by 38 centimeters, painted about 1864, showing Brace's Rock at Eastern Point, Gloucester, on the coast of Cape Ann in Massachusetts. The museum's keywords call it simply a landscape, and the small, horizontal format is that of a modest coastal view made for a collector or friend rather than for exhibition. Lane knew this stretch of shore intimately.","i":"Lane was born in Gloucester, worked in Boston for years and returned to the harbor in 1848, where he lived until his death in 1865; Eastern Point's rocks, ledges and light were daily sights. Cape Ann's rugged coast appealed to Luminist painters, for whom rock, still water and low Atlantic light offered ideal subjects. This picture dates from the year or so before his death.","c":"A late, quiet picture in which Lane's formula is pared to essentials: a plain rock, the sea, and a clear, even light. In a small work the scale reveals the habit of looking, with sharply defined edges and a calm, observed atmosphere. It is best seen as a distillation of subjects Lane had painted for years, modest but characteristic of the artist."},"nga:228356":{"d":"A man sits with one knee bent, his body turned right and his face turned slightly back toward us, hands clasped on his bent knee, the other leg hanging. He has brown skin, high cheekbones, short curly black hair and beard, and a neutral expression as he gazes into the distance. He wears a cream short-sleeved shirt, red and white striped shorts and a yellow sash. The background is a solid light blue with visible brushstrokes, and he sits on a red object with blue fabric. Oil on canvas mounted on board, about 92 by 74 centimeters, 1879 to 1880.","i":"The title names the sitter in full, which gives the study the standing of a portrait. Karin Bergöö, a young Swedish painter in training at this date, later married the painter Carl Larsson and became known both for her own work and for the home they created at Sundborn. A figure study like this, painted from a posed sitter, belongs to the exercises through which art students of the period built their skills, here carried to a finished canvas.","c":"A frank, unfussy study: the flat blue ground, strong red and yellow accents and the sitter's calm, distant gaze give it presence beyond its modest apparatus. The pose is natural, with clasped hands and one leg hanging, and the treatment of the features is dignified rather than picturesque. The visible brushwork is disciplined, and it shows a gifted young painter's grasp of bold, simple color."},"nga:92995":{"d":"A boy of perhaps ten, shown from the chest up in a vertical portrait, turns his body to the left but looks back at the viewer from the corners of large hazel-brown eyes under thin arched brows. His face is round, with an upturned nose and a coral-red mouth slightly open, and his short dark brown hair falls just below the ears. He wears a loose black jacket tied at the neck with a black bow, and a tall, oyster-white collar that is the brightest note against the brownish-olive green background. It is a small family portrait of modest size.","i":"René was the youngest of Degas's brothers, and the portrait dates from the mid-1850s, when Degas was a young art student whose first sitters were his own relatives. Family portraits let him experiment without the demands of a patron. The oblique pose, with the body turned one way and the head looking back, gives the child a watchful, slightly guarded air, a quality Degas would later cultivate in portraits of adults. The plain ground and clear contours recall the Ingres-inspired training he had received.","c":"A modest but telling early work. The sideways glance, the open mouth and the large pale collar give the boy a startled, living look that many more finished portraits of children lack, and the colour is held to a few close tones. The handling, however, is still careful and a little tentative: the quick insight is present, the confident economy of later Degas is not. It is chiefly of interest as early evidence of his gift for the sidelong look."},"met:438857":{"d":"A wide, small canvas, about 35 by 62 centimetres, holds a nude man, as the title and the museum's keywords say. The record gives no further description, so his pose, the ground and the lighting cannot be reported with confidence. The horizontal format suggests a reclining or extended figure, and at this date, 1856, when Degas was twenty-two and training in the academic tradition, a life study of the nude was a basic exercise.","i":"In the mid-1850s Degas was studying at the École des Beaux-Arts and copying old masters in the Louvre, before leaving for Italy in 1856, and drawing and painting from the nude model was the foundation of an academic training. Such studies were not meant for exhibition; they trained the eye in anatomy, light and the weight of the body, and could later feed into history paintings. This one reached the Metropolitan Museum as a gift in 2007.","c":"A student's work, and an honest one. What interests in Degas's early studies is the seriousness of the training, the same that later made his drawing of dancers and bathers so firm. Without the details to hand it cannot be said how far the paint is convincing; the picture is chiefly valuable as evidence of how much groundwork lay beneath the later Impressionist freedom."},"met:436120":{"d":"An elderly woman, identified only as Italian, is the subject of this portrait on canvas of about 75 by 61 centimetres. The museum lists a woman and a portrait but gives no further description, so her pose, dress and expression cannot be described with confidence. Degas painted it in 1857, during the three years he spent in Italy, where he studied the old masters and made portrait studies of people he met, including relatives and local models.","i":"Degas travelled to Italy in 1856 and stayed until 1859, visiting Naples, where he had relatives, Rome and Florence. Pictures of old women, in black and with headcloths, were a stock subject for artists of the time, who found in them dignity and character; Degas follows that tradition with unusual sobriety. The title records only the type, an old Italian woman, and not her name; the picture came to the Met by bequest in 1966.","c":"A grave, early portrait in which the young Degas shows respect for the old masters and a gift for character without flattery; the sitter's age is treated with dignity, not sentiment. Without the details to judge, it is fair to say that it belongs to his apprentice years, before the sparkle of invention that marks his mature work, and is valuable as evidence of how seriously he learned to see."},"nga:46598":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a young man in a black cadet's uniform and cap leans against the back of a chair and looks at the viewer with pale blue eyes; his closed mouth turns slightly down. Gold dominates the costume: braided ropes loop from the shoulder, buttons line the open jacket and the vest, a sky-blue and gold striped belt carries a medallion, and a gold band circles the cap. His right hand rests against the coffee-brown chair back, while the other hangs at his side, possibly on the hilt of a dagger or sword. The wall behind him is rust red.","i":"The sitter is Achille, one of Degas's younger brothers, painted when Degas was in his early twenties and still studying and copying in museums. A portrait in uniform said as much about a family's hopes as about the young man himself: it records a career just beginning and the discipline that came with it. Degas follows the conventions of formal portraiture, the casual lean and the display of rank, but the serious, slightly doubtful face keeps the picture from being merely a record of a uniform.","c":"Degas takes the formal portrait seriously here. The pose, the gold detail and the red wall are handled with a care that shows how closely he had looked at Ingres and at older portraiture. The braid and buttons are the liveliest part; the face, though honest, is somewhat flatter, and the stance owes more to convention than to observation. Accomplished rather than daring, a solid early piece in which the originality is still waiting."},"nga:92994":{"d":"A young man's face fills this small vertical oil sketch, only about 20 by 15 centimetres, painted on paper laid on canvas. He is lit from the left but turned slightly to the right, so most of the face lies in shadow, and he looks out from the corners of large dark eyes with his lips set in a line. He has a moustache and a trimmed beard, and black hair parted to one side curls over a high forehead. Dark lapels meet over a white scarf or undergarment at the neck. A chestnut-brown wash fills the background, and brushstrokes are visible throughout.","i":"Degas drew and painted himself repeatedly in his twenties, using his own face as a model that never tired and never objected. The shadowed face and warm brown ground recall the self-portraits of Rembrandt, an artist he studied and copied. The glance from the corner of the eye is one he used again and again for other sitters. Such small self-portraits were private exercises, not public statements, and the expression is best read as concentration rather than as any stated mood.","c":"Degas is a candid observer of himself: no flattery, no heroic pose, only a face in shadow and a brush that moves quickly. The loosely washed ground and rapidly drawn collar suit the small scale. It lacks the psychological bite of the best self-portraits of the period, and the beard under the chin is lost in the wash, but it is interesting for the early evidence of his habit of treating the head as a problem of light and tone."},"met:438156":{"d":"A young woman is shown with an ibis, a long-billed wading bird, in a vertical canvas of about 100 by 75 centimetres. The museum's record lists the woman and the bird and links the picture with Rome, but gives no further description, so her pose, dress, setting and the colours cannot be described with confidence. The picture is unusual in Degas's early work, combining a portrait-like figure with an exotic animal; it was begun in 1857–58 and reworked in 1860–62.","i":"Degas began the picture in Italy and returned to it after going back to Paris, reworking it in 1860–62, so it records two stages of his early development. The ibis, sacred in ancient Egypt as the bird of the god Thoth, adds a note of the exotic and perhaps of allegory, though its exact meaning here is not clear. Pictures of women with birds or flowers were familiar in nineteenth-century painting, and Degas seems to be testing an unusual variation.","c":"An odd, intriguing early picture, more ambitious than finished: the pairing of a young woman and an exotic bird promises a meaning that the picture does not quite deliver, and the reworking over several years suggests that Degas was not wholly satisfied. Without details one cannot judge the handling; its interest is as evidence of a young painter searching for a subject beyond the academic repertoire. It entered the Met only in 2008."},"nga:101776":{"d":"Two groups face each other in front of a hilly river landscape in this loosely painted vertical scene. At the left, a young man in a white tunic stands with feet planted wide and one fist clenched, reaching with the other hand to stroke a reddish-brown horse at his shoulder; behind him a man holds up a fluttering pale blue cape. At the right, under a tree, a balding older man, a boy in a white tunic and a woman in a marigold-orange shirt stare back. Beyond them horses stand near a green bank, with white buildings on the far side of the water.","i":"The story comes from Plutarch. Philip II of Macedon was about to send away a magnificent but unmanageable horse, Bucephalus, when his young son Alexander asked to try him. Alexander had noticed that the horse was frightened by its own shadow, turned it towards the sun, calmed it, and rode it. The cloak held up behind him probably alludes to Plutarch's detail that Alexander let his cloak fall before mounting. Degas took up several ancient subjects in the early 1860s, as a young artist testing his command of history painting.","c":"A curious, unresolved picture. Degas gives the story none of the usual heroics: no rearing horse, no triumph, only two groups regarding one another across a quiet clearing. That is its thoughtful, modern side, and the loose handling suits it. But the figures are stiffly arranged, and the pair of raised arms with no head suggests a canvas dropped rather than finished. It says more about where Degas was heading than about where he arrived."},"met:436121":{"d":"A huge bouquet fills most of the canvas: dahlias, asters and gaillardias in white, blue, pink, orange and red, crowded into a large vase on a table with a red patterned cloth. At the far right edge, partly cut off by the frame, a woman leans on her elbow with her hand against her cheek, a ring on her finger. She wears a pale cap with a dark ribbon, a dark bow at her neck and a tawny jacket with frills, and she looks away to the right at something outside the picture. At the lower left stand a glass carafe and a crumpled napkin. Behind are green patterned wallpaper and the edge of a window, and the paint is thick and varied, with dabs of pure colour for the petals.","i":"The sitter is probably the wife of Paul Valpinçon, a schoolfriend of Degas, and the setting probably the Valpinçon country house at Ménil-Hubert in Normandy, which Degas immensely enjoyed visiting; the late-summer flowers suggest the season of a stay. A pencil drawing of the woman, also dated 1865, is in the Fogg Museum. What matters most is the arrangement. In a conventional portrait the sitter would be centred and the flowers a prop; here she is pushed to the edge and the bouquet takes over. The Met sees this as an example of Degas's aim of capturing people in seemingly casual, slice-of-life views, a habit that grew into the cropped, off-centre compositions of his later work.","c":"The picture is bolder in design than in feeling. The bouquet is painted with real relish, each bloom a dab of a different note, and the woman's cheek and ringed hand are quietly observed. But the sitter seems oddly detached, her face less alive than the flowers, and the split between the two halves is uneasy. That unease is partly the point: this is an early, confident break from the centred portrait, well ahead of its time in 1865. It is better as a prophecy of the mature Degas than as an achieved picture in its own right."},"nga:46600":{"d":"A man and a woman sit on opposite sides of a burgundy-red, low-backed sofa in a room with striped, floral wallpaper. At the left the woman faces the viewer in a pinkish-tan dress with a full skirt and a crimson shawl pulled over white puffed sleeves; her dark hair is smoothed back and her lips are closed. At the right the bearded man perches on the back of the sofa, one elbow jutting out, and turns to look sidelong at the viewer. Through a doorway beyond him a ghostly grey figure can be made out. Passages of clothing are thinly painted or scraped away.","i":"The sitters are Degas's sister Thérèse and her husband and cousin, Edmondo Morbilli. The double portrait is of an unusual kind: the pair share a sofa but neither a pose nor a glance, and the husband's casual perch gives the scene an informal, almost candid note. Degas used family portraits in the 1860s to explore how couples occupy a room and how much a portrait can say through placement alone, without any plot or emblem.","c":"More interesting for its design than for its people. The curved arms of the sofa, the busy wallpaper and the open doorway are organised with real invention, and the husband's awkward perch is a bold, unflattering stroke. The faces, though, are smooth and reserved, and large areas are thinly worked or scraped away, so the picture feels like an experiment abandoned in the middle. It anticipates the offbeat group portraits of the 1870s without yet matching them."},"met:436122":{"d":"A bearded man in a black hat and coat sits hunched forward on a stool, with a portfolio of prints open at his feet. With one hand he lifts out a sheet showing a flower, while his eyes turn up and aside from under the hat brim; the face is half in shadow. Behind him, on a table, lie open albums and loose sheets of flower prints, and on the wall at upper right a framed board is pinned with colorful Japanese fabric samples. At upper left a glass cabinet holds a small white horse figure. The colors are brown, ochre and black, with pale sheets standing out. The painting is small, with a rough, quick finish.","i":"The Metropolitan Museum identifies the prints as cheap, outdated colored lithographs by the flower painter Pierre-Joseph Redouté, set beside a fashionable Tang dynasty horse and Japanese fabrics, a juxtaposition that suggests an old-fashioned collector piling up objects without discrimination. The sitter's name is not recorded. In the 1860s Degas developed portraits in which people are interrupted at their tasks among settings that reveal their character, and the Japanese fabrics show the new taste for Japanese art in Paris. The painting is dated 1866 and was bequeathed by Mrs. H. O. Havemeyer in 1929.","c":"Degas's wit is in the arrangement: the stoop, the sideways glance and the clutter around the man tell the viewer more about a type than the half-hidden face does. It is a sharp early example of the interrupted portrait he would refine for decades. As a finished painting it is slight. The face is partly lost under the hat and the prints on the table are dashed in. Its pleasures are those of an observant sketch: dry humor and a feel for collectors' obsessions."},"nga:46492":{"d":"A pale-skinned young woman in a voluminous rust-orange dress stands facing the viewer, shown from the knees up in a vertical portrait. Her dark brown eyes look slightly downward; she has a wide face, rounded cheeks, a broad nose and a dimpled chin, and her chestnut hair is pulled up and back. The dress has a charcoal-grey band across the shoulders and flaring cuffs, with wide pleats falling over the chest, and the puffed white sleeves of her shirt show beyond the dress sleeves. She holds an indistinct, light object in one hand and seems to stand in a corner, the walls washed in muted brown and green.","i":"The title records no name, and the picture is best read as a study of a contemporary figure rather than a society likeness. Degas painted such figures in the 1860s, when he was trying to join portraiture with modern-life subjects: a woman in current dress, in an ordinary corner of a room, neither idealised nor posed as a fashionable sitter. The large, simplified mass of the dress is the main pictorial event, and the object in her hand tells no story.","c":"The large, calm shape of the dress, held by the grey band at the shoulders, is the strongest part of the picture, and the face is drawn without softening or flattery. The surrounding corner, thinly washed and mostly empty, gives the figure little to do, and the object in her hand never resolves into anything. It is an honest, slightly awkward picture, and in its lack of polish it points away from the Salon portrait towards Degas's later, odder compositions."},"met:436144":{"d":"A man is shown in an artist's studio, identified by the title as James Tissot, with the signs of his profession around him: the museum lists a painting, an easel, a frame and a walking stick. The canvas is large, about 151 by 112 centimetres, which gives the friend a near life-size presence. The record does not describe his pose, clothes or the colours, so those cannot be reported with confidence. The setting shows the sitter in his own element, a painter among his work.","i":"Tissot and Degas were friends and fellow painters in Paris in the 1860s; Tissot was a fashionable painter of costume pictures and society scenes who moved to London in 1871 and became very successful there. Degas, who rarely accepted portrait commissions, painted friends, and this is one of his earliest large pictures of an artist at work. Setting the sitter with canvas, easel and cane presents him as a gentleman painter, a role Tissot cultivated.","c":"A crisp, intelligent double statement, a portrait of a man and of a milieu, in which the elegant sitter and the apparatus of his craft are set together with Degas's sharp eye for how a person occupies space. Without the specifics to hand, it is fair to say that it is among the more substantial of Degas's early portraits of friends, and also a witty tribute to a rival's dandyism. The sitter's smart air is part of the point."},"met:436145":{"d":"A small portrait, about 27 by 22 centimetres, shows the musician Joseph-Henri Altès in profile. The museum lists only the sitter, a man, a profile and a portrait, so his dress, expression and background cannot be described with confidence. A profile view is a severe, sculptural format that recalls Renaissance portrait medals and Italian portraits of the fifteenth century, which Degas had studied during his years in Italy.","i":"Altès (1826–1895) was a flautist and later conductor at the Paris Opéra, part of the world of orchestral musicians whom Degas knew through his friend, the bassoonist Désiré Dihau; he later painted a celebrated group of Opéra musicians. A small, quickly painted portrait of a colleague, like this, was made for friendship rather than as a commission, and reflects the close ties between Degas and Parisian musicians.","c":"A crisp little portrait whose profile format gives the head an almost medallic firmness, in the manner of the Italian Renaissance portraits Degas had admired. At this scale economy is everything, and a format that suppresses expression makes the likeness depend on the contour. Without detail to examine, it is fair to say that it is a restrained, assured work, more a tribute to a friend than a display of virtuosity."},"met:436150":{"d":"A woman is shown in profile with a handbag, in a small horizontal canvas of about 22 by 27 centimetres. The museum's keywords list the profile and the purse, and the title names the sitter as Marie Dihau, but the rest, her pose, dress and setting, cannot be described with confidence. A profile with an everyday accessory like a purse suggests an informal, observed moment rather than a formal sitting, a hallmark of Degas's portraits of friends.","i":"Marie Dihau (1843–1935) was a singer, pianist and teacher, and the sister of Désiré Dihau, the Opéra bassoonist whom Degas knew well; he became a friend of the family and painted her again in a larger portrait at the piano. This small portrait dates from 1867–68, when Degas was moving from history subjects towards modern life, and portraits of musicians and other friends were an important part of that turn.","c":"A small portrait of notable freshness. The profile, the handbag and the casual quality of the observation make Marie Dihau seem caught as a friend would see her, and the scale lets Degas concentrate on silhouette and contour. Without the details to examine, the fair verdict is that it is a delicate, personal and modest work, valuable as part of the circle of musicians whom Degas painted with real affection."},"met:436149":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in an interior on a horizontal canvas of about 55 by 65 centimetres. The title names her as Madame Théodore Gobillard, born Yves Morisot (1838–1893); the museum's keywords record only the woman, the interior and the portrait, so her pose, dress and the room cannot be described with confidence. A wide format for a portrait gives the sitter her surroundings, and Degas liked to place friends in their own rooms.","i":"Yves Morisot was the elder sister of the painter Berthe Morisot, and married Théodore Gobillard in 1867; Degas, a friend of the Morisot family, painted her in 1869. The portrait comes from the years when Degas was making informal portraits of friends and relatives, in unusual poses and with a careful eye for domestic interiors, and was beginning to move in the circle of the future Impressionists. It came to the Met in 1929 with the Havemeyer bequest.","c":"A relaxed, personal portrait of a family friend. Without the details to hand, its strengths can only be inferred from Degas's habits: an unconventional view, a sitter caught off guard and the lived-in quality of a domestic room. His portraits of women of this period can be coolly observant, even unflattering, and he did not aim to please sitters. Its distinguished history in the Havemeyer collection is part of its appeal."},"met:436168":{"d":"A small panel of about 27 by 21 centimetres shows two men, as the title says, though the museum's record gives no description of the figures, their clothes or their setting. At this size, and on wood, the picture is likely to be a quick sketch or a study rather than a finished work, of the kind Degas made for himself in the later 1860s, when he was trying out ideas for compositions of figures in modern interiors.","i":"In the 1860s Degas moved from history painting towards scenes of modern Paris, and pictures of pairs of men in conversation, in cafés, at the stock exchange or in a studio, belong to that turn. Small oil sketches on wood were made for pleasure and to record ideas, and many stayed in his studio until his death in 1917. The date is given only as between about 1865 and 1869, and the identity of the two men is not recorded.","c":"A sketch like this is best judged by its economy rather than its finish, and Degas had a rare gift for suggesting character in a few strokes. Without being able to examine the picture closely, one can only say that the small panel is a typical by-product of his working life and should be enjoyed for its glimpse of a quick, assured hand. It would be unfair to ask it to carry the weight of a major work."},"met:436141":{"d":"In a bare rehearsal room with a pale wooden floor, a ballet class pauses. At the centre a dancer in a white tutu with pink ribbons steps forward in pink slippers, her arms loose at her sides. Behind her another dancer rests a hand on the barre, and at the far right a third, in white, stands in an open doorway with her back turned. At the left, a violinist seen from behind sits in a black coat beside a dark piano, with a group of girls in white clustered close to him. A tall mirror reflects more dancers. In the foreground a top hat, a violin case and a small green watering can lie on the floor. Degas's red signature is at lower right, on a panel the size of a book.","i":"One of Degas's earliest rehearsal-room scenes, it was made around 1870, the date given by the Metropolitan Museum; other writers place it a year or two later. It shows a class at the Paris Opéra, and the dancer in the centre has been identified as Joséphine Gaujelin. Degas worked from preparatory studies rather than on the spot, and the picture introduces the barre, the mirror and the bare floor that return in his later ballet series. It also includes a male musician, which his later dance pictures mostly omit. The picture was shown in 1874; critics were divided, some finding it erotic and others disjointed.","c":"At this scale and early date Degas is still learning the subject. The dancers have a posed, slightly stiff look and their faces are summary, but the structure is already assured: the long floor, the tilted mirror and the empty foreground with its top hat and violin case lead the eye with remarkable control. The casual objects give the picture the feel of an overheard moment. It is a prototype rather than the finished achievement of his later class pictures, but it already sets out his method: an oblique view, a cropped foreground and an unposed moment."},"met:436152":{"d":"A woman dressed in grey is the subject of this portrait on a vertical canvas of about 91 by 72 centimetres, painted around 1865. The museum's record lists only a woman and a portrait, so her pose, expression, setting and the shades of grey cannot be described with confidence. The title makes the colour of her dress the picture's keynote, and a portrait organised around a single muted colour suits Degas's restrained approach in the mid-1860s.","i":"Portraits of the mid-1860s by Degas belong to his years of transition from academic training to modern life subjects, and show a sitter treated with a cool, unflattering attentiveness. The sitter is not named, though the picture may record a friend or relative, as many of his early portraits did. The picture was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1957 by Mr. and Mrs. Edwin C. Vogel.","c":"With few specifics available, the fair verdict rests on type: a mid-sized, grey-toned portrait of a woman of the 1860s is likely to be subtle in its tonal gradations and restrained in gesture, qualities Degas shared with Manet and Whistler at the time. The risk of such a scheme is a certain quietness that can read as reserve. It is best seen as a good, unshowy portrait from the years before Impressionism."},"met:436162":{"d":"A man and a woman share a room in a small canvas of about 32 by 46 centimetres, their silence the subject. The museum lists a woman, a man, an interior and reading among the elements, and identifies the models as Emma Dobigny, one of the artist's models, and Edmond Duranty, the critic. The record gives no further description, so the poses, furnishings and colours cannot be reported with confidence. The title, Sulking, points to a quarrel or mood between them.","i":"Degas arranged scenes of tension between men and women in modern interiors around 1869–72, notably this one and the picture called Interior; like a stage set, they present a moment of silence in a bourgeois household and leave the story to the viewer. Duranty championed such subjects in his writing, urging artists to paint modern life with the detail of a novelist. The models were people Degas knew, so the quarrel is staged, and its cause is left unstated.","c":"A small psychological drama told without words: the silent pair in a room, the blank spaces between them and the sulking title make a precise, unresolved tension, and Degas shows already the cool novelistic eye that would make him the poet of modern manners. The narrative is deliberately open, so some find it elusive; the treatment is careful rather than flamboyant. Among his early genre pictures it is a very good small example."},"nga:46596":{"d":"Dominated by red and fiery orange, this nearly square picture shows a woman seated at the left in a high-backed chair, leaning forward in profile towards the right, her face in shadow. A round, flat, leaf-shaped fan rests on its edge in her lap, almost at the centre of the canvas. Her long ruby-red dress spreads towards the bottom right corner, against a coral-red wall that runs the full width of the picture. At the right edge, cropped by the frame, are a black statuette of a figure with raised arms, a footstool and a white triangular form, perhaps a lamp shade.","i":"Madame Camus was a friend of Degas, who painted her more than once around this time. Here he is less interested in her likeness than in the arrangement of colour and shape: a red room, a round fan, a profile partly lost in shadow, and objects sliced by the frame. The cropping and the flattened, patterned effect are often linked to the influence of Japanese prints and of photography, both of which fascinated Degas and his circle in the late 1860s.","c":"Colour is the subject, and Degas handles it with nerve: an almost monochrome field of red held together by small notes of black and white, with the flat disc of the fan acting as a bullseye. The face, left in shadow, withholds the likeness a sitter might expect, and the cropped objects at the right feel arbitrary rather than purposeful. Among the more daring portraits of the 1860s, it succeeds more as design than as character study."},"met:436123":{"d":"A scene from the ballet in Meyerbeer's opera Robert le Diable, in which ghostly nuns rise from their tombs, as performed at the Paris Opéra, fills a canvas of about 66 by 54 centimetres. The museum's keywords list ballet, performance, dance and a man, and the composition is generally described as seen from the orchestra pit, with the heads of the musicians cutting across the foreground and the dancers on the stage beyond. The record gives no further description, so the colours cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"Meyerbeer's grand opera of 1831 was one of the great spectacles of the Paris Opéra, and its ballet of the nuns a celebrated attraction. Degas, an opera-goer and friend of musicians, painted it in 1871 and returned to it in a later version of 1876 now in London. By placing the viewer among the musicians' heads, he made the picture a view of the theatre as a social world, not only of the stage, an invention he often reused.","c":"A bold and unusual vantage point: the dark mass of the orchestra in front and the pale, spectral stage beyond make a powerful contrast, and the scene remains a kind of theatrical memory rather than a record. Painted when Degas was still working out how to place the viewer in a modern spectacle, it is more experimental than his later ballet pictures. Its pleasure is in the daring of the point of view."},"nga:91222":{"d":"Two horses, one dark brown and one cream-white, stand together on the right along the ridge of a grassy hill. The brown horse, in profile near the viewer, rests its head across the back of the white horse, which stands with its tail turned towards the front. Below them the ground dips to a river or canal that crosses the whole canvas, lined with buildings with red or brown roofs and smoking chimneys; long, low boats lie near the far shore. Beyond, sage-green slopes rise under an ivory sky touched with pink, and the background is deliberately indistinct.","i":"Horses were one of Degas's lifelong subjects, from the racing pictures of the 1860s onward. This small canvas of 1871 stands apart from them: two animals at rest in a French landscape, with no jockey, rider or event. The brown horse leaning its head on its companion, a habit horses at pasture do have, is the whole subject. The signature and date show that Degas regarded it as complete, though it reads like a quick, affectionate note from nature.","c":"A small, relaxed picture, closer to a sketch than to a statement. The soft greens, the ivory sky and the hazy distance are pleasing, and the pair of horses is observed with warmth. But the buildings and boats are only suggested, and the composition has no strong centre beyond the animals, so the picture charms rather than convinces. Among Degas's horse paintings it is a rare moment of rural calm."},"nga:1157":{"d":"Six jockeys in coloured silks sit on brown and black horses on a grassy field, five clustered at the lower left and the sixth, in white, approaching from the right. The silks are black with white sleeves, pink with black sleeves, black with sky-blue sleeves, emerald green and canary yellow. Two more riders in top hats sit farther back, and two horses run on the track behind. The horizon lies about a third of the way up; along it stand three smoking chimneys, clustered houses and a distant church with spires, under a watery blue sky with lilac-tinged clouds. The paint is loose and blended.","i":"Horse racing was a fashionable pastime in Second Empire Paris, and Degas, who knew the courses well, returned to it again and again. He is less interested in the drama of the finish than in the moments before the start, when jockeys wait and jostle. The factory chimneys on the horizon belong to the modern landscape around the course, and the bright silks supply small accents of colour. Painted on a wooden panel barely 35 centimetres wide, the scene is closer to a jewel than to a public picture.","c":"A tiny panel that gets a great deal into a small space. The silks sparkle against the soft green of the turf, and the low horizon leaves most of the picture to a wide sky. The grouping at the lower left is nicely off-centre and conveys the horses' restless waiting, and the smoke from the chimneys is a witty modern touch. The riders themselves are summary, though; it is a pleasing impression rather than a deep exploration."},"met:436174":{"d":"A woman bends to her work with an iron, as the title says; the museum's keywords list the woman, ironing, work and a clothes iron. The canvas is small, about 54 by 39 centimetres, and dated 1873, and the record gives no more detail, so her pose, dress, the room and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. Degas painted women at this trade several times in the early 1870s, drawn to the repeated, physical gestures of the laundress's work.","i":"Laundresses and women ironing became one of Degas's characteristic subjects around 1869–73, and offered a modern urban subject that did not rely on society, myth or religion: the work of poor women in a great city. They also gave him strong poses, such as leaning on the iron or stretching over a table, which he studied in drawings. He painted them without sentimentality; the writer Edmond de Goncourt left an admiring account of his laundresses in his journal.","c":"A modest, small picture of a large theme. Degas's laundresses are among his most truthful images, free of both sentimentality and condescension, and here, as elsewhere, the gesture of work, not the face, carries the feeling. Without the details to hand it cannot be said how finished it is, but the small scale and the plain subject suit each other, and the picture remains an intimate record of labour."},"nga:46599":{"d":"A woman in a full silvery-white dress dotted with grey sits on a low-backed white sofa against a pale peach wall, her body angled to the left and her gaze directed that way with pale green eyes. Her chestnut hair is parted in the middle and pulled back, her lips are closed, and her hands rest in her lap with the wrists crossed. The dress darkens from eggshell white on the chest and sleeves to nickel grey over the skirt and is tied with a black sash. Blended brushwork gives the whole picture a soft, almost hazy look.","i":"The sitter is Estelle Musson, who married Degas's brother René and was also Degas's cousin. He painted her during his stay in New Orleans in 1872–73, where her family lived; she is known to have suffered from failing eyesight, which may bear on the unfocused gaze. Nearly the whole picture is built from whites, greys and peach, so that the quiet sitter seems to dissolve into the room.","c":"One of the most delicate of Degas's family portraits. The white dress, white sofa and peach wall are tuned within so narrow a range that the picture seems almost without edges, and the sitter's quiet, inward look is unforced. It is not a flattering portrait, since the complexion has a yellowish cast, but it is a sympathetic one. The blurred handling keeps the picture subdued, even a little evasive, yet the restraint is the point."},"met:438817":{"d":"A tall rehearsal room with pale green walls and a worn plank floor is crowded with ballet students in white tutus, many with coloured sashes. In the foreground a girl with a red flower in her hair bends forward with her face turned away, while a music stand with an open score and the scroll of a stringed instrument cut across the lower left. Further back, dancers stretch, wait, or lean against a mirror that reflects the windows. At the right, a white-haired ballet master in a loose grey suit rests on a tall cane. A group of girls watches from a raised platform at the far end. The tulle is painted in thin, pearly touches against the green.","i":"The class is held in a rehearsal room of the Paris Opéra under the ballet master Jules Perrot, a celebrated dancer and choreographer, who stands at the right with his cane. The opera house on the rue Le Peletier burned down in October 1873, so Degas, who painted this in 1874, must have worked from earlier drawings and from memory. The baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure, a leading collector, commissioned it, and the poster for Rossini's William Tell on the wall alludes to him. A companion version is in the Musée d'Orsay. Ballet suited Degas as a subject of modern working life: he shows the waiting, stretching and fatigue behind the performance.","c":"Degas arranges apparent chaos with a surgeon's care. A steep diagonal floor, figures cropped by the edge and a mirror that opens the room sideways suggest a glimpsed moment, yet every group is carefully placed. The whites, built in thin cool layers against the green, are beautifully varied. The ballet master is a little stiff beside the girls, and the faces are kept deliberately blank, so the interest lies in the pattern of bodies rather than in character. Together with its Orsay companion, it set the pattern for his later rehearsal scenes."},"met:436139":{"d":"Two young dancers practise at a barre in a rehearsal room, one standing and the other bent over, with a green watering can in the foreground, as the composition is usually described. The picture is made in mixed media on canvas, about 76 by 81 centimetres, with oil thinned with turpentine combined with pastel and other media, which gives the surface a dry, matt look. The museum's record gives no more detail, so the exact poses and colours cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"Degas's rehearsal rooms show the work behind the spectacle: the barre, the watering can that dampened the floor, and the tired postures are the ordinary routine of the Opéra's young dancers. The mixed technique, in which oil thinned with turpentine dries matt like pastel, was one he used in the 1870s for rapid, light effects. The picture entered the Met with the Havemeyer bequest of 1929; Louisine Havemeyer, a friend of Mary Cassatt, was one of Degas's greatest American collectors.","c":"A compelling picture: the strong diagonal of the barre, the watering can bright as a still life, and the careful placement of two figures in a nearly empty room give the composition an unusual, almost abstract strength. The dancers' faces matter little; their postures tell everything. The dry, powdery surface, neither oil nor pastel, suits the dusty, bare rehearsal room. A fine demonstration of Degas's gift for turning a rehearsal corner into a design."},"nga:46493":{"d":"Shown from the lap up, a young woman with rosy cheeks and upswept chestnut hair sits in an upholstered chair angled to the left, looking in that direction. Light falls directly on her oval face, with its pointed chin and arched dark brows, and a sliver of silvery blue shows around the iris of the eye nearer the viewer. She wears a long black dress, a brown shawl, perhaps fur, and pale gloves, one hand tucked into a fold of her skirt. Canary-yellow and white flowers spill around her head from a vase behind, against a stone-blue ground with terracotta red above.","i":"The title names the sitter only as Mademoiselle Malot, and the picture does not say who she was or what she did. Painted about 1877, when Degas was exhibiting with the Impressionists, it shows his taste for portraits of women in everyday interiors, with a lively, cropped composition and flowers placed near the head as a counter-accent. Rather than the formal likeness of a patron, it is an informal study of a person caught in a quiet moment.","c":"The face is the success: lit from the front and rendered with a restraint that makes the thin blue ring of the iris and the parted lips unusually vivid. The flowers and background are lively, but the black dress and fur dissolve into murk, and the hidden hand leaves the pose a little awkward. A clever, unshowy portrait that rewards close looking more than it commands the eye."},"nga:165300":{"d":"A dimly lit ballet studio holds about two dozen young dancers tying their shoes, stretching and practising on pointe. All wear knee-length tutus with tight bodices and belts of canary yellow, rose pink or royal blue. A spiral staircase rises along the left edge, where two dancers are seen from the waist down; a knot of girls stands left of centre, and at the right, nearest the viewer, four cluster around a mahogany bench with rose-pink slippers piled beside it. Through a wide opening at upper right more dancers practise in a brighter room with parchment-yellow walls. Faces and costume details are loosely painted.","i":"From the early 1870s Degas turned from portraits and racecourses to the ballet, concentrating not on performance but on the rehearsal rooms and wings. Many of the girls came from poor families and trained for years for a small chance of a stage career. Degas shows fatigue and routine as much as grace: one girl stoops to tie a shoe, another turns to look back. Like most of his ballet pictures, it was probably composed in the studio from drawings.","c":"The strength is in the arrangement: the dark foreground group, the bright room beyond, and the spiral stair cutting off the left edge, a device that makes the space feel real and uncontrived. The faces are too summary to give individual dancers much life, and the bright belts make the general effect somewhat decorative. Beside the larger, more fully worked Dance Classes of the mid-1870s, this is a lighter, looser rehearsal piece."},"nga:93045":{"d":"A long, narrow canvas is spread with the floor of a rehearsal room, scuffed and tan, lit from the left and from two windows at the back. In the lower left a dancer in a red jacket and pale blue tutu sits slumped, her head propped in her hand, with a cello lying on the floor behind her. At the centre, one girl sits on a wooden chair, her tutu draped over its back, and gazes at another who stands with her back to the viewer, bowing her head as she adjusts the green sash at her waist. Further off, more dancers in blue-white tutus practise or wait. A small mirror hangs on the mint-green wall, and the figures are placed off-centre and cropped by the frame.","i":"Degas made about forty pictures in this long, frieze-like horizontal format, and The Dance Lesson, of about 1879, is the first. Such narrow canvases show the influence of Japanese prints, which he knew and admired, and the unexpected cropping and tilted floor belong to the same taste. Like his other rehearsal scenes, it shows the dancers off duty, waiting, tired, adjusting costume: the unglamorous labour behind the performances of the Paris Opéra. The setting is a rehearsal room rather than the stage, and the picture is as much about space and light as about the girls.","c":"The format does much of the work. By stretching the room into a frieze and tilting the floor upward, Degas makes the viewer a casual visitor at the edge of the scene, and the cropping and off-centre figures keep the eye moving. The slumped dancer at the left, whose face is never seen, is the emotional point, a note of weariness that most ballet pictures avoid. The paint is thin, the colours tonal rather than brilliant, and the dancers at the right almost a blur. It is a small, sharp experiment rather than a major statement."},"met:782304":{"d":"A young woman covers her mouth with her hand, as the title says, in a small vertical portrait of about 41 by 33 centimetres. The museum's record gives only the woman and the portrait, so her features, dress and setting cannot be described with confidence. It is painted in oil colours freely mixed with turpentine, a thinned, matt, quick-drying technique that Degas used in the 1870s for sketches that approach the look of pastel.","i":"The gesture of a hand over the mouth could signal a yawn, a stifled laugh or a thought left unspoken, and Degas was fascinated by such unposed, transitory gestures, which broke with the formal conventions of portraiture. A sitter whose hand hides part of the face challenges the idea that a portrait must show features clearly. The model is not identified, the date, about 1875, is approximate, and the Eugene V. and Clare E. Thaw Charitable Trust gave the work to the Met in 2018.","c":"A sketch with a bold idea: a hand over the mouth withholds the very feature a portrait normally displays, and the freely mixed paint gives the head a nervous immediacy. Without the details to hand one cannot judge how fully the face is resolved, and the picture may be better as an idea than as a finished image. Even so, it shows Degas thinking about the instant, not the pose."},"met:437993":{"d":"A woman and the hats of a milliner's shop are the subject of this pastel on a square support of about 69 centimetres. The museum records that the support is made from five pieces of wove paper, backed with paper and laid on canvas, and lists only a woman and hats among the subjects, so her pose, the shop interior and the colours cannot be described with confidence. Pastel, built up in layers of dry colour, gave Degas the matt, velvety surfaces he liked for hats, feathers and fabrics.","i":"Milliners' shops became one of Degas's favourite subjects in the 1880s, and he returned to them again and again in pastel and oil. They offered a world of women's work and women's vanity, in which customers tried on hats in front of the mirror, and he treated the scenes with a mix of fascination and cool detachment. The pictures have also been read as reflections on appearance, trade and the way women were looked at; Degas himself offered no commentary.","c":"Even without the details, the choices are characteristic: the square sheet pieced from several papers shows Degas altering his support as he worked, and pastel lets him render hats and ribbons with a dry, shimmering richness while keeping the woman's gesture exact. The subject, a woman and her appearance, is chosen for observation, not glamour. Among his pictures of milliners it is an early, assured example."},"nga:94902":{"d":"A band of galloping horses crosses the middle of this tall canvas, their reddish-brown bodies stretched to the left with legs fully extended. Beneath the flying hooves a fallen jockey lies on his back, eyes closed and legs splayed, in a pink jersey with black sleeves, white breeches and black-topped boots, his helmet on the ground behind his head. Two of the foremost horses have empty saddles; riders behind wear smudged orange, lavender and green. The horizon is high, above the horses' backs, with blue sky and pink and yellow clouds. The handling is loose and brushy, with black sketched lines defining bodies and harness.","i":"This is generally identified with the steeplechase scene Degas sent to the Salon in 1866, when racing was a fashionable subject and a rider's fall an obvious source of drama. Unlike a conventional sporting print it offers neither a winner nor a finish, only the moment when the field races on past the fallen man. Degas kept the canvas for years, reworking it in 1880–81 and again around 1897, so the surface carries more than one period of his thinking.","c":"An ambitious, uneven picture. The idea is striking: a spectacle of speed treated with the seriousness of a history painting, the fallen man pushed to the bottom of the frame and the field sweeping past him. Yet the horses, with legs extended in the old convention, read more as a pattern than as animals in motion, and the reworked paint gives the surface a patched look. More a statement of Degas's modern subjects than a fully achieved painting."},"nga:52169":{"d":"Looking slightly down, the viewer sees three ballerinas in rose-pink costumes and one man in a black suit and top hat against a backdrop of dabbed and stroked greens. The closest dancer, shown from the knees up in profile, appears to have her eyes closed; a coral flower sits in her large bun, a black choker circles her neck, and she grips her elbows at her waist. Behind her shoulder, cut off by the right edge, the man stands in shadow looking on. Another dancer appears between them, and a third stands with her back turned. The picture is small, loosely painted and edged with a narrow border.","i":"Degas often showed the wings of the Paris Opéra, where male subscribers could come and go and meet the dancers. The black-suited man at the edge is usually read as one of them, a presence whose watching hints at the dancers' dependence on wealthy admirers. Degas gives no story, only juxtapositions: pale costume against dark suit, a dancer withdrawn into herself, a man observing from the margin. The museum dates the picture to between 1876 and 1883.","c":"A small picture with a surprisingly large idea behind it. The green backdrop, built from dabs and strokes, makes the pink costumes glow, and the cropped man at the margin adds a note of unease that the dancers' composure does not remove. The handling is quick, sometimes almost abstract, and the faces are summary except for the nearest dancer's. Slight in size, it is typical of Degas's way of making modern life feel both close and slightly unsettling."},"nga:57514":{"d":"On a wooden panel barely 23 centimetres wide, three women gradually emerge from a haze of colour. At the left, the back of one woman's head and shoulder lean towards the edge of a low balcony wall; she wears a dark blue, off-the-shoulder dress. Beside her a second woman in a pink dress with a ruffled neckline, a black necklace and a small white earring looks left in profile, a black fan near her face. Beyond them a dancer in a green dress stands with her arms above her head against soft green and orange. A red curtain frames the left side and a black one the right.","i":"The loge is a theatre box, and Degas, a regular at the Opéra, painted the audience as often as the stage. Here watchers and watched share the frame: two women in a box look out at a dancer, and the viewer looks at all three. Spectators in boxes were a staple of modern-life painting in Paris, treated by Renoir and Mary Cassatt as well as Degas, and the cropped, partial view puts the viewer in a seat among them.","c":"A tiny, hazy picture whose subject has to be coaxed out, which is part of its charm: the soft green and orange of the stage, the black fan and the red curtain are set against one another with relish. But it is hardly more than a note, with vague faces and too little substance to hold the eye for long. As an experiment in blurred, partial vision it works; as a picture of the theatre it only suggests."},"met:436138":{"d":"A long, narrow canvas, about 39 by 90 centimetres, shows dancers in a rehearsal room, with a double bass among the objects recorded, an instrument that would accompany their practice. The museum's keywords list women, dancers and a musical instrument, so the exact poses, the arrangement of figures and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The panoramic, frieze-like format, more than twice as wide as it is high, is one that Degas favoured in the 1880s for scenes of the Opéra's rehearsal rooms.","i":"Rehearsal rooms were where Degas found his dancers: not on the stage but in the bare, practical rooms where they trained, stretched and waited, with musicians supplying the music. By the 1880s he was working in pastel and in thin oil, and he often chose very wide formats that let him arrange figures in a strip, almost like a frieze, with unexpected objects, such as an instrument, cutting into the picture. The date, about 1882 to 1885, suggests that he worked on it over a period.","c":"The very wide format and the pairing of dancers with a large instrument promise a composition without a centre, in which the eye travels along the room as along a frieze. Such arrangements were Degas's way of making an unglamorous space beautiful through placement and light, and the double bass is a witty, unexpected presence. Without more detail one can only say that it belongs to his most inventive vein of the 1880s."},"nga:164915":{"d":"Six mounted jockeys line up on a grassy field in this loosely painted horizontal scene. Most of the horses are chestnut, with two dark brown ones at the middle; one, second from the right, rears on its hind legs, and another at the left nuzzles its neighbour. The riders wear caps or helmets, white breeches and tall black boots, their caps and shirts in goldenrod yellow, aquamarine, white, red or peach. A black silhouette at the right suggests a man in a top hat, and swipes of paint across the field suggest a crowd. Green fields run back to trees and low hills under an ice-blue sky.","i":"The moment is the one Degas preferred to the race itself: the parade before the start, with horses jittery and riders waiting. The rearing horse breaks the line and hints at how hard the animals were to control. By the mid-1880s Degas had been painting racecourse scenes for some twenty years, and the thin, almost sketch-like paint, with the weave of the canvas showing through, reflects his taste for rapid, open handling in these later years.","c":"The rearing horse enlivens an otherwise even line, and the thin, transparent paint gives the field an airy look. Yet the jockeys and the crowd are so summary that the picture borders on an unfinished sketch, and the repeated horses make the middle rhythmic rather than dramatic. Compared with the sharply observed small racing panels of the 1860s and 1870s, the mood is looser and more atmospheric: impressive for its freedom, less so for its detail."},"nga:66409":{"d":"Shown from about the hips up, a woman stands with her back to the viewer, her pale face deep in shadow, in a black and grey dress and a black hat set on the back of her head. Her elbows are bent, perhaps to clasp her hands, and her head is turned just enough to show the line of forehead, nose and chin in near profile. Beyond her a far wall is brushed with sketchy strokes of marigold orange, lemon-lime green, brown, black and pale yellow that suggest paintings in gold frames. The canvas is nearly square and signed in dark red at the lower right.","i":"The subtitle, Visit to a Museum, gives the clue: the woman is looking at pictures, and the loosely brushed wall stands for a gallery hung with gilt frames. Degas found the Louvre a fascinating modern-life setting, and around 1880 he made prints and drawings of fashionable visitors there, in which his friend Mary Cassatt served as model. Turning the figure's back to the viewer makes the act of looking the real subject.","c":"The rear view is a bold choice: the face is almost unseen, and attention goes to the contour of the dress and the tilt of the hat. The warm oranges and yellows of the background give a lively sense of crowded frames without any detail. But the figure's dark mass sits heavily in the frame, and the hazy wall cannot quite balance it. An interesting experiment in anonymity and modern pose, more suggestive than complete."},"nga:53532":{"d":"A woman stands in profile at the lower right, bending over a white table to iron a pale blue shirt, seen from slightly above. Her chestnut hair is in a bun, her sleeves are rolled to the elbow, and she wears a denim-blue dress speckled with white dots and a dusty rose-pink apron. One hand presses the iron while the other straightens the collar. A folded white shirt and a small empty bowl lie on the table. In the background a row of hanging garments in pink, gold, teal and lavender softens the light from three tall, curtained windows. The brushstrokes are feathery, giving a hazy quality.","i":"Laundresses and ironers were a subject of working-class Paris that Degas returned to from about 1870, drawn by the repeated, physical gestures of the trade as much as by any social comment. The museum records that he began this canvas about 1876 and finished it about 1887, a long interval during which his handling grew softer and his colour lighter. The woman is observed with sympathy but no sentiment, labour presented as a pattern of pose and light.","c":"The pale hanging shirts and soft window light are beautifully managed, and the broad white table gives the figure weight and the whole picture a calm, tilted geometry. What the long gestation leaves is a certain blurring: the woman's face and the details of her work have less definition than the surroundings. The result is more tender than the harder-edged laundresses of the 1870s, but also somewhat less pointed."},"met:436153":{"d":"A small portrait of a young woman, about 27 by 22 centimetres, in oil on canvas, dated around 1885. The museum's record lists only a woman and a portrait, so her features, dress, pose and setting cannot be described with confidence. The size is that of a private, informal picture, and Degas often painted such small portraits of friends and models, quickly and without commission.","i":"The sitter is not named. In the 1880s Degas was concentrating on pastel, dancers and bathers, and his portraits of these years were often small and private. The picture came to the Metropolitan Museum with the bequest of Louisine Havemeyer in 1929, part of the collection she and her husband built with the advice of Mary Cassatt, and it is typical of the modest works of Degas that Americans prized.","c":"Without detail to examine, the fair verdict is on scale and intention: a small, private portrait by a painter who looked hard at faces and had no wish to flatter, and who by this date could capture a likeness in a few strokes. Pictures of this kind rarely aim at grandeur; they show an artist's hand in unguarded mood. Their interest lies in the economy of the notation and in what they reveal of his habit of looking."},"nga:1158":{"d":"In a dance studio, two ballerinas in knee-length tutus and ballet slippers sit on a bench and stretch towards the right, while four more rehearse at the back of this long, horizontal picture. Every costume is robin's-egg blue, with a low-cut, sleeveless bodice and full skirt. The nearest dancer, at the lower right, leans down to reach her foot, her face in shadow; behind her another pulls on pale pink tights. In the upper left four dancers stand in a line with right legs raised, arms gently bent, and French doors light the back corner. The soft brushwork gives a blurred, ethereal look.","i":"By the 1890s the ballet was Degas's principal subject, and he treated it with less concern for particular rooms or individuals than for patterns of movement and colour. The two stretching dancers in the foreground and the four with raised legs at the back echo one another, a rhythm he often tried out in drawings and pastels. His failing eyesight in these years may have encouraged the looser handling, but the wide format lets the two groups balance each other with real care.","c":"The long format and the blue costumes tie the picture together, and the foreground dancer's stooping pose, her face hidden, has the offhand truth Degas valued over the glamour of the ballet. But the paint is so soft that the four dancers at the back dissolve into one rhythmic blur, and the electric blue and yellow arch behind the bench is hard to read. Atmospheric and graceful, though without the crisp invention of his 1870s rehearsal scenes."},"met:436140":{"d":"Dancers in pink and green costumes are grouped together in a nearly square canvas of about 82 by 76 centimetres, with colour as the declared subject of the title. The museum's keywords list women, dancers and ballet, so the number of figures, their poses and the setting cannot be described with confidence. By around 1890 Degas's ballet pictures had become increasingly broad and brilliant in colour, built up in layered strokes that dissolve costume and space into fields of pink, green and other hues.","i":"Degas painted dancers at the Opéra for some thirty years, and by 1890 the subject had become a vehicle for colour and design: tutus, scenery and stage light merge into a pattern. His failing eyesight is sometimes cited as a reason for the looser handling of his later work, though the broad colour was also a deliberate artistic choice. The painting entered the Metropolitan Museum with the Havemeyer bequest of 1929.","c":"Colour is the force here: the title sets out the scheme in four words, and the pink and green, played against each other, give the picture a nearly abstract vibrancy that few ballet pictures of the period approach. The risk of such late works is loss of figure, as the dancers dissolve into colour fields, and the intimacy of the earlier ballet scenes gives way to a decorative effect. Even so, it is an arresting picture."},"nga:46597":{"d":"Four ballerinas with russet-orange hair tied in buns crowd together on the left half of this large horizontal canvas, adjusting the straps of their bodices. Three stand in a row receding from the lower left corner, in rust-orange bodices over wide, knee-length tutus flecked with celery green and buttercup yellow; two have their backs to the viewer, and one looks over her shoulder. A fourth, in a brighter carrot orange, stands apart at the far left with one arm raised. Behind them a grove of dark green trees gives way to a sunset meadow under a coral-pink and golden sky streaked with lavender-grey cloud. Black lines outline the bodies.","i":"Painted around 1899, this belongs to the last phase of Degas's career, when he returned again and again to a single motif and repeated it in different media. Dancers adjusting their straps in the wings recur in his drawings, pastels and oils of the 1890s. The glowing oranges and greens have little to do with ordinary stage lighting; they are Degas's own, and the trees and sunset behind probably stand for painted scenery. The gestures are ordinary and rhythmic rather than dramatic.","c":"Late Degas at full strength: blocked-in orange and green, outlined in black, turn four everyday gestures into a rhythm, and the sunset gives the canvas a heat that the earlier, quieter ballet scenes lack. Some of the drawing is coarse, and the faces are all but absent, as if the bodies were the only concern. Yet the scale and colour make it one of the most forceful of his last paintings."},"nga:135512":{"d":"A young woman sits and a girl stands at an open, vine-covered gate in front of a park. The woman faces us from the center of the opening, head tipped slightly under an ivory hat with a dark feather, in a pale shell-pink shawl and a full, tiered skirt, with long-stemmed red, blue and yellow flowers in her lap. The girl, in profile, rests her hands on the trellised gate; she wears a slate-blue pinstriped jumper, white stockings and black boots. An open umbrella lies upside down nearby, and dappled sunlight falls through the trees. Oil on canvas, about 65 by 81 centimeters.","i":"Eva Gonzalès was Manet's only formal pupil, and this picture of a nanny and her charge belongs to the modern-life subjects favored by his circle: parks, gates, children and the women who looked after them. The artist signed as if her name were written on the bottom rail of the gate, a witty touch. Painted late in a short career, it shows a young woman painter making an independent contribution to Impressionist-era Paris.","c":"Gonzalès combines Manet's lessons, loosely painted features, strong blacks used as accents and a direct frontal gaze, with a lighter, brighter touch of her own. The dappled sunlight and the daring placement of the figures at a gate give it a modern feel. The looseness is the point, though it leaves the faces less individual than in Manet's own portraits; the picture is a fresh, confident work by an artist who died too young."},"met:439120":{"d":"Saint Basil of Caesarea celebrates Mass while the emperor Valens, who came to the church, is shown among the figures; the museum's keywords list Basil, Valens, a woman, a man and putti. The oil on canvas, about 137 by 79 centimeters, is dated 1746 and is by Pierre Subleyras, a French painter who worked in Rome. The tall, narrow format suits a composition designed for an altar, or a related study for one.","i":"According to tradition, Valens, an emperor who favored Arian doctrine, which denied the full divinity of Christ, pressed Basil to compromise; when he attended the bishop's Mass he is said to have been so overcome by the ceremony that he staggered, and Basil accepted his offering. For the Catholic Church the scene displayed the authority of the Church over secular power, and the putti signal heavenly approval.","c":"Subleyras was among the finest French painters working in Rome, and his strengths, clear groupings, silvery color and grace of gesture, suit a ceremonial subject. The risk of such scenes is stiffness, and cool elegance is his remedy: the drama of an emperor humbled before a bishop is staged with clarity. It is a polished, intelligent piece of mid-century Roman religious painting."},"met:440464":{"d":"A portrait of Pope Benedict XIV, born Prospero Lambertini, painted in 1746 by Pierre Subleyras in oil on canvas, about 64 by 49 centimeters. The museum's keywords record only the sitter, a pope, a man and a portrait. At this modest size it is a portrait of a type that could serve as a reduced version of a larger formal likeness, a gift or a study. The date is the same as that of Subleyras's Mass of Saint Basil in the same museum.","i":"Benedict XIV, pope from 1740 to 1758, was a learned canon lawyer known for wit, moderation and patronage of the arts and sciences. A papal portrait had a double purpose, recording the person and presenting the office, and copies circulated among cardinals, nuncios and friends. Subleyras's gift for giving ceremonial sitters a look of intelligence suited a pope renowned for his learning.","c":"A skillful, intelligent likeness in which Subleyras's usual qualities, clear drawing, silvery color and quiet dignity, serve a sitter famous for his wit. Papal portraits tend to freeze into formula, and this small version seems to let personality through. Without the larger formal likeness for comparison it is best taken as an assured example of Roman portraiture at mid-century."},"met:11744":{"d":"A man at work with jewelry and tools is the subject of this upright canvas, about 117 by 90 centimeters, painted around 1882. The record lists a man, jewelry, a physical tool and work, with the keywords Jewelry, Men, Working and Tools. The museum gives no further description of the workshop, the costume or the colors, so the setting is described only by its subject, an Arab craftsman at his trade.","i":"Charles Sprague Pearce, born in Boston, lived in France from 1873 and studied with Léon Bonnat, a teacher of many American painters who had himself traveled in Egypt and the Near East. Pearce took up Orientalist subjects of craftsmen, peasants and street scenes, and such pictures were prized by Americans abroad for their precise ethnographic detail. The painting was given to the museum by Edward D. Adams in 1922.","c":"A craftsman at his bench suits Pearce's academic training: firm drawing, a taste for meticulous detail in tools and metalwork, and a certain dignity given to labor. Orientalist pictures are open to the charge of exoticism, and the jeweler may be more type than individual, but such honest attention to work is more engaging than the harem fantasies of the genre. An accomplished, serious picture."},"met:435821":{"d":"A woman is shown in profile in a miniature painted on a thin plate of ivory in 1804. A pendant, perhaps a piece of jewelry worn by the sitter, is among the details, and the title's 'said to be' marks the identification with Emma, Lady Hamilton as traditional rather than proven. Miniatures of this type are usually only a few centimeters across, made to be held in the hand, worn or kept in a case.","i":"If the identification is right, the sitter is Emma Hamilton, born Emma Lyon, who rose from poverty to become a model for George Romney, wife of the British envoy at Naples and companion of Admiral Nelson. Her face was among the most reproduced in Britain, so her name has been attached to many likenesses. A profile view suits the neoclassical taste of 1804, which admired cameo-like heads inspired by antiquity.","c":"Adam Buck was one of the more elegant miniaturists in London in the early 1800s, and his portraits favor clear outline, delicate color and a neoclassical grace that owes something to Greek vase painting. A profile on ivory of this date shows him at his most characteristic. As a likeness of Emma it must be taken on trust, but as an example of Georgian refinement on a small scale it is entirely reliable."},"nga:65898":{"d":"Three sailing ships pitch wildly in crashing waves beyond craggy boulders at the lower left, beneath towering clouds. The central ship tips sharply with billowing ivory sails and two red, white and blue flags whipping in the wind, while white spray kicks up against its side. The second ship's sails are furled except for one that crashes onto the deck as tiny sailors scurry; the third floats farther off. At the lower right a broken mast, a barrel and two bundles bob in emerald-green waves, and a patch of golden light breaks through at upper left. The artist signed and dated it 1667.","i":"Storm scenes with ships driven toward rocks were a favorite of Dutch collectors, whose prosperity depended on the sea and who knew its dangers; the red, white and blue flags are the Dutch colors. Such pictures also carried moral overtones of life's perils and of divine providence, and the golden break in the clouds may suggest hope. Backhuysen, a leading Amsterdam marine painter, was known for his stormy seas.","c":"Backhuysen specialized in stormy seas, and this one shows why: the rolling waves with white spray, the ships heeling over and the sky torn by a gap of golden light are handled with convincing energy. The tiny figures give a human scale to the elemental drama, and the wreckage at the lower right makes the danger concrete. It lacks the calm clarity of Willem van de Velde's ships, but it excels in drama."},"met:10072":{"d":"A large oil study on paperboard, about 92 by 68 centimeters, dated about 1815, that the museum titles as two works in one: A Study from Life and a Study for Angel Releasing Saint Peter from Prison. Its keywords list a woman in profile. As a working study rather than a finished exhibition picture, it shows an artist's preparation: a head observed, apparently from a model, for use in a figure composition.","i":"In the Acts of the Apostles an angel frees Peter from prison, striking off his chains and leading him past the guards; painters usually show the angel as a radiant, youthful figure. Allston, a Romantic who painted visionary religious subjects, used life studies to give such figures physical truth before idealizing them. The pairing in the title suggests that the woman's profile helped him think about the angel's features, though the study can be enjoyed for its own sake.","c":"A study invites a different judgment: it should be admired for directness and for what it shows about method, not for finish. One expects the painterly handling that earned Allston the name of the American Titian, and a profile study has a clarity that finished pictures sometimes lose. Its interest is the glimpse of a perfectionist at work, preparing an ideal figure from a real face."},"nga:177332":{"d":"Three cows drink in a placid body of water in front of a landscape dim with golden sunset light, in a horizontal painting. White eddies form around rocks as the water crosses the foreground. One cow is white, another dark brown and the third tawny, and a rider in a red vest and brown hat on a white horse approaches along a path, with a pale gray dog running ahead. Trees fill the right side, and the sky shades from buttery yellow to pale blue, with clouds lit mauve pink.","i":"Allston, who studied in London with Benjamin West, admired the Venetian painters' glowing color and was nicknamed the American Titian. This small landscape descends from Claude Lorrain's golden pastoral scenes and from the cattle-by-the-water pictures of the Dutch and of Aelbert Cuyp. Painted about 1819, just after he returned to Boston from seven years in England, it brings European Romantic landscape to American soil before the Hudson River School.","c":"A small, glowing picture in which Allston turns an ordinary cow pasture into poetry through color alone. The sky, shaded from yellow to ultramarine, is the star, and its fine gradations show the painter's debt to the Venetians. It is a landscape of mood: there is little incident and no grandeur, and it lacks the force of his visionary works. But for quiet harmony of light and color it is among the most accomplished early American landscapes."},"cma:141935":{"d":"Allston's London banker and business agent, Samuel Williams, looks off absently while holding a book, in an invented setting of columns that opens onto an Italianate landscape. The museum suggests he may be inspired by the pictures in the book. The oil on canvas is large, about 142 by 112 centimeters, and was painted about 1817. The sitter's attention is turned inward rather than toward us, so the portrait is as much about a state of mind as about a face.","i":"Allston, an American painter living in London, portrayed the man who managed his money as a daydreamer. The portrait explores reverie, the Romantic experience of being lost in imagination, and invites viewers to join Williams in dreaming of faraway places. Because the setting is invented, with architecture opening onto distant landscape, it works like a poem rather than a record. The museum adds that Allston experimented with painting recipes, even trying to mix pigments with skimmed milk.","c":"A portrait that aims higher than likeness: it is closer to Allston's imaginative pictures than to a business sitter's commission, and it wins by atmosphere, the absent gaze, the soft light, the invented distance. The cost is some vagueness about the sitter's character, since we learn his mood but not his habits. It is a rare and quietly successful English-period portrait by an American Romantic."},"met:10070":{"d":"A single female figure absorbed in reverie, painted in 1831 by Washington Allston in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters. The title identifies her as Spanish, and the museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, so the picture is best described as an imaginary figure of a kind Allston painted late in his career: a beautiful woman, lost in thought, rather than a likeness of a named sitter.","i":"Reverie, the state of inward daydreaming, was a favorite theme of Allston's, and it also animates his portrait of Samuel Williams. Imaginary pictures of beautiful women, often in exotic dress, were popular with American collectors of the 1830s, who enjoyed the romantic sentiment and the associations of Spain. Allston, the leading American Romantic painter, gave such subjects a dreamlike, poetic quality that raised them above mere pretty pictures.","c":"Allston's late pictures of dreaming women are about mood: soft transitions, rich, low-keyed color and a deliberate vagueness. They sometimes tip toward the sentimental, and the title carries more narrative than the painting does. But the tonal richness for which he was called the American Titian is the reason to look, since color here carries feeling rather than description."},"met:438824":{"d":"A tall vertical canvas, about 173 by 121 centimeters, of Saint John the Baptist preaching, painted about 1650 by Mattia Preti, the Calabrian painter known as the Cavaliere Calabrese. The museum's keywords list the saint and sheep, so he is set in the wilderness with a flock rather than before a crowd. Pictures of the subject usually show him as a gaunt, rugged figure in camel hair, with a reed cross, pointing or addressing listeners.","i":"John, forerunner of Christ, preached repentance in the wilderness along the Jordan and baptized those who came to him. The sheep in the picture can be read as the flock of the faithful he gathers, and as a reminder of his words on seeing Jesus, Behold the Lamb of God. Such images combined a biblical scene with a call to penitence, and the saint's austere life made him a favorite of Baroque preachers and painters.","c":"Preti combines Caravaggio's strong contrasts with the sweeping drapery and muscular figures of the Roman Baroque, and the dark ground and strong light give a picture of this type its force. His figures can be heavy and the effects theatrical, but the Baptist as a rugged, earthy man rather than an ideal saint is typical of his manner and effective. It is a powerful, somewhat blunt picture of a painter in his vigorous mid-career."},"met:437333":{"d":"Pilate washes his hands, the gesture by which he tries to disown responsibility for the condemnation of Christ; the museum's keywords list Pilate, the act of washing, a man and Christ. The oil on canvas, about 206 by 185 centimeters, is a large, nearly square picture dated 1663, painted when Mattia Preti had settled on Malta. At this scale the figures are close to life size, which draws the viewer into the scene.","i":"In the Gospel of Matthew, Pilate sees that he cannot prevail, takes water and washes his hands before the crowd, declaring himself innocent of the blood of this just person. The gesture has become proverbial for evading blame. Seventeenth-century viewers saw in it an image of cowardice and of the corruption of justice, and a prompt to meditate on the Passion and on one's own part in it.","c":"The Caravaggesque method, with figures pressed close to the viewer, dramatic light and an unglamorous psychological realism, suits a moral scene about evasion of responsibility, and Preti's Maltese manner tends to be darker and heavier than his earlier work, which gives the subject appropriate gravity. The size and the darkness can be oppressive, but the oppression serves the story."},"nga:226639":{"d":"A clean-shaven man sits cross-legged on the floor, bent forward, cutting a block of dried tobacco leaves with a knife on a wooden board; a bowl beside him collects the flakes. His head is unevenly shaved, short at the sides and bushier down the middle, and he is barefoot. He wears a yellow vest over a white shirt with sleeves rolled up, a scarlet overskirt and slate-gray knee-length pants. A scale and two loaves of bread rest on a white cloth before him, and a hand holding a glowing pipe emerges from the shadows. Oil on canvas, about 103 by 91 centimeters, 1660s.","i":"Tobacco, imported from the Americas, was a costly commodity in seventeenth-century Europe, and cutting it was manual work. The museum lists labor and slavery among the picture's themes; the partly shaved head and bare feet suggest an enslaved man, and Preti worked for decades on Malta, where the Knights of Saint John kept enslaved captives. The picture is a rare Baroque image of a worker whose dependence is part of its subject.","c":"A remarkably direct picture: the man is the whole subject, not a servant in the corner of a grander scene, and the dark ground, side light and heavy forms are Caravaggesque strengths that give him weight and dignity. The painter neither prettifies nor caricatures him. It is a rare, unsettling image whose power comes from its concentration, and its realism is matched by the question it leaves about the man's situation."},"nga:112629":{"d":"Four men and a woman gather around the dead Saint Gennaro, shown from the knees up in a honey-gold cape and pointed bishop's hat, his stone-gray face resting on a stump, hands bound with rope, red seeping from his neck. At right a woman bends over his face holding a silver chalice, squeezing a rag or sponge over it. A man behind cradles the saint's head, another spreads his arms over the saint's back, and at left a bald man holds a knife or sword with a bird-shaped hilt. Light falls from the right.","i":"Gennaro, or Januarius, bishop of Benevento, was beheaded around 305 during the persecution under Diocletian and became patron saint of Naples; his dried blood, kept in vials, is said to liquefy on feast days, a miracle the Neapolitans watch as a sign of the city's safety. The woman collecting the blood recalls the tradition that a pious woman gathered it. Preti worked in Naples in the 1650s, when Gennaro's cult was central to civic life.","c":"Late Preti is heavy, dark and intense; the cramped group pressed around the body, the light striking from the right and the greenish flesh give the picture a funereal, tactile force. It is more about grief and relic than about heroic martyrdom: the violence is over, and the tenderness of the attendants is the theme. The darkness may repel some viewers, but it concentrates feeling."},"cma:146148":{"d":"A basket of plums, its details emerging from deep shadow, takes the central place in this still life of 38 by 46 centimeters, painted in 1769. Beside the fruit are cakes in their crisp papers and a glass of water with sparkling highlights and reflections, with growing moss adding a touch of immediacy. The colors are vivid reds and violets. The picture is oil on canvas by Anne Vallayer-Coster.","i":"Fruit still lifes were extremely popular in later eighteenth-century France, and a basket of plums was a common motif, going back to Chardin, whose tabletop pictures set the standard. Vallayer-Coster was admitted to the Royal Academy in 1770, a year after this picture. The glass of water and the cakes suggest a refreshment table, a scene of modest pleasure. The museum notes a companion picture, a plate of peaches and a branch of cherries.","c":"The strength is the contrast of deep shadow and bright color, a Chardin-like discipline in which a few objects are given exact weight. The glass of water, with its sparkle and reflections, is a virtuoso touch that lifts the picture above the routine plum piece. It is small and quiet, without the decorative lushness of the Rococo, and it already shows the exactitude that would win her a place in the Academy the next year."},"met:437864":{"d":"A vase of flowers stands beside a large conch shell in this still life of about 50 by 38 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1780 by Anne Vallayer-Coster. The recorded motifs, a vase, flowering plants, a conch and a seashell, bring together two kinds of natural beauty, the botanical and the marine, in one arrangement. The upright format and modest size make it a picture for a private room or salon rather than a public hall.","i":"Shells were fashionable collectors' objects in eighteenth-century France, displayed in cabinets and studied as part of the new natural history, and the conch, with its pearly interior, was among the most admired. Placing one beside a vase of flowers pairs the beauty of the garden with that of the sea, and may invite a comparison of fleeting blossoms with the lasting shell. By 1780 Vallayer-Coster was one of the best-known still-life painters in France.","c":"Vallayer-Coster's still lifes are praised for precise observation and a cool, balanced elegance that separates her from the florid Rococo. The pairing of flowers and shell lets her contrast soft petal with hard, glossy shell, the kind of problem she solves with unfussy skill. Next to Chardin she is less poetic, but for finish and decorative poise she is among the best of her generation."},"nga:227490":{"d":"A large ornate vase overflows with roses, peonies, poppies and other flowers in red, pink, blue, purple, white and green. The vase, adorned with gold and a small carved figure on its front, stands on a marble surface that also holds a pineapple and peaches at right and leaves and clusters of green grapes at left. Light comes from the upper left, lighting the blooms and throwing dark shadows against a dark gray ground. Oil on canvas, about 108 by 90 centimeters, dated 1783.","i":"Anne Vallayer-Coster was admitted to the Académie royale de peinture et de sculpture in 1770, in her twenties, a rare honor for a woman, and became one of France's most successful still-life painters. A pineapple, a hothouse fruit, and a vase of costly alabaster signal luxury, so the picture displays wealth and taste as well as flowers. Such large decorative still lifes suited the elegant interiors of late eighteenth-century Paris.","c":"Vallayer-Coster's still lifes are praised for velvety textures and a refined balance of rich color against a dark ground. The large format lets her stage a showy vase, flowers, pineapple, peaches and grapes as a display of abundance. Compared with Chardin, with whom she is often compared, she is more decorative and less meditative, but her technical assurance is considerable: marble, alabaster, petals and fruit bloom each stay distinct."},"nga:46183":{"d":"A man's body turns to the right while he looks directly at us. He has fair skin and dark brown hair with two curls falling onto his forehead, well-defined eyebrows, brown eyes, a straight nose, a cleft chin and light red lips in a neutral expression. He wears a dark coat with a high collar edged in a yellow and green pattern, and a white cravat tied in a voluminous knot. The background is a plain brown. Oil on canvas, about 55 by 46 centimeters, dated 1808.","i":"Pierre Paul Prud'hon (1758 to 1823) was a favorite of the Empire's official circles, and 1808 was a year of success for him, when his large allegory Justice and Divine Vengeance Pursuing Crime was shown at the Salon. His portraits combine Neoclassical clarity with soft modeling and shadow drawn from Leonardo and Correggio. The plain ground and simple dress here keep attention on the sitter's face and direct gaze.","c":"A direct, unembellished likeness whose strengths are the soft modeling of flesh and the quiet intensity of the gaze. Prud'hon, better known for allegory and romantic compositions, brings to portraiture the gentle chiaroscuro derived from Correggio, and the plain brown ground allows nothing to compete with the face. It is a sober, intimate picture with little display, and it rewards close looking rather than a quick glance."},"met:437340":{"d":"A large vertical portrait, about 216 by 142 centimeters, of Charles Maurice de Talleyrand-Périgord, Prince de Talleyrand, painted in 1817 in oil on canvas. The size is that of a ceremonial portrait, about seven feet tall, in which the sitter's rank can be displayed in costume and bearing. The museum's keywords record only the sitter and the genre, so the picture is described here by its type: a state portrait of a great statesman.","i":"Talleyrand (1754 to 1838), a bishop turned diplomat, served the Old Regime, the Revolution, Napoleon and the restored Bourbons, and represented France at the Congress of Vienna, a career that made his name a byword for political survival. A portrait of this scale presented him as a pillar of the restored monarchy, while for later viewers it records a man famous for reserve, wit and flexibility.","c":"Prud'hon's gift is for tenderness and shadowed atmosphere, not ceremony, so a state portrait on this scale asks him to work outside his usual register. The result is likely to be more dignified than dramatic, and interest lies in seeing a master of reserve portrayed by a painter more at home with dreamy allegories. Whatever its restraint, it is a major late portrait in his output."},"met:437339":{"d":"The figures are Andromache, the widow of Hector, her young son Astyanax and a man, with the Trojan War as the setting, according to the museum's keywords. The oil on canvas, about 132 by 171 centimeters, carries an uncertain date, either about 1813 to 1817 or 1823 to 1824, which suggests a long period of work by Pierre Paul Prud'hon. It is a large horizontal history picture on a theme of loss, rather than a portrait.","i":"After Troy fell, the Greeks feared that Hector's son would one day avenge the city and resolved to kill the boy. In Euripides' Trojan Women Astyanax is hurled from the walls, and in Seneca's version Andromache hides him in Hector's tomb until Ulysses forces her to surrender him. The subject of a mother protecting her child gave painters a chance to show maternal love and helpless suffering, themes that suited Prud'hon's temperament.","c":"A tragic subject suits Prud'hon's gift for pathos and shadowy, Correggio-like drama, and the museum's double date suggests a long period of work on the picture. Its power rests on feeling rather than on classical clarity, which sets it apart from the cooler Neoclassicism of David. Some viewers find the effect overwrought; others find the tenderness the real subject."},"nga:46100":{"d":"Saint Sebastian leans against a tall white column with his arms tied behind his back, his face tilted upward and his eyes raised. Arrows pierce his arm, torso, knee and foot. He is bare-chested, with a draped light purple cloth tied around his waist and shoulder-length brown hair, and the background is solid black. The top of the column is broken off, and at its foot a detailed white stone relief with figures in varied poses lies among fragments of stone. The oil on panel measures about 115 by 66 centimeters.","i":"Sebastian, a Roman soldier condemned under Diocletian for his Christian faith, was tied up and shot with arrows, survived and was nursed back to health, then was clubbed to death. Because the arrows resembled the sudden strikes of plague, he became the saint invoked against epidemics, and Renaissance artists welcomed him as a subject that allowed the nude male body to be shown with religious justification. The broken column and relief evoke the ruins of pagan Rome and the painter's passion for antique sculpture.","c":"Aspertini was the eccentric of Bolognese painting, and this panel shows it: the black ground makes the pale body glow, the raised eyes and arrows are painted with unusual intensity and the antique relief at the base is invented with archaeological relish. The drawing of the body lacks the polish of Raphael's, and the whole is a little strange, but its oddness is its strength. Sebastian is neither idealized nor stoic."},"met:437941":{"d":"A small oil view, about 43 by 54 centimeters, of Lake Nemi and the town of Genzano seen from the terrace of the Capuchin monastery, in the Alban Hills southeast of Rome. The museum's keywords list a lake, trees and a landscape. It was painted about 1756 to 1757 by Richard Wilson, a Welsh-born painter, in or just after his Italian years, and shows a famous beauty spot as it appeared to a visitor on the monastery terrace.","i":"In antiquity Lake Nemi was called Diana's Mirror, after the sanctuary of the goddess on its shore, and its classical associations and soft scenery made the Alban Hills a favorite stop for Grand Tour travelers. Wilson, who began as a portrait painter and turned to landscape in Italy, sold such views to English visitors who wanted a poetic souvenir of their travels.","c":"A small, early and probably freshly observed Italian view: Wilson's strength is a golden, atmospheric light that unifies hills, water and sky, and the blur between observation and ideal is pleasing. Compared with his large composed landscapes this is simpler, and its quiet calm shows why English collectors loved his Italian scenes. It is a modest picture, but a telling one."},"nga:61394":{"d":"A large horizontal landscape, about 122 by 170 centimeters, dated 1762, showing Lake Albano in the Alban Hills south of Rome. The title is the only description, so the picture belongs to the tradition of the Italianate landscape: a view of a classical site, built from a lake as the central expanse, framing trees and distant hills, arranged in receding stages from foreground to horizon. Painted by Richard Wilson in London from his Italian experience.","i":"Lake Albano fills a volcanic crater near Castel Gandolfo and was a Grand Tour stop rich in historical memories: Alba Longa, the legendary mother city of Rome, was said to have stood on its shore. Wilson left Italy in the 1750s, so a view dated 1762 was necessarily painted in England from sketches and memory for patrons who wanted to recall their Italian travels.","c":"In the early 1760s Wilson was at the height of his reputation, and his Italian landscapes unify a scene with golden light and gentle classical structure, treating nature as poetry rather than topography. A large canvas gives him room for a measured, spacious composition. The price is a certain idealizing generality, and the calm can seem remote, but it shows why he is called the first major British landscape painter."},"nga:61395":{"d":"A very large landscape, about 142 by 210 centimeters, dated about 1762 to 1770, whose title, Solitude, sets a mood rather than naming a place. Wilson's Italianate landscapes of these years typically show a quiet stretch of country with water, trees and distant hills, often with a few small figures or a ruin to give scale. The great size makes it a picture for a grand room, not a cabinet.","i":"Solitude, being alone in nature, was a favorite theme of the eighteenth century, associated with contemplation, melancholy and poetry. Wilson aimed to make landscape a vehicle for ideas and emotions, and a title like this turns a view into a state of mind. His taste for such pictures was increasingly out of step with patrons as the century advanced, and he ended his life in comparative obscurity in Wales.","c":"Wilson's gift is for atmosphere and large, calm structure, and a title like Solitude suits his habit of leaving much of the canvas to light, air and distance. The size is grand and the mood contemplative; the risk is emptiness, a poetic idea carried by tone alone. Where it succeeds the picture earns its title: one feels distance and quiet more than anecdote."},"cma:154962":{"d":"In the distance rises the peak of Cader Idris, the mountain in northwest Wales, while riders in the foreground descend toward the river Mawddach. The oil on canvas, about 90 by 106 centimeters, dates from about 1774, and the museum notes that another version is in the Walker Art Gallery in Liverpool. The mountain, the river and the small riders together give a view of a landscape being traveled as well as admired.","i":"Wilson's earlier Welsh pictures usually suggested links to the country's heroic past; here, the museum observes, the emphasis falls on the pleasing beauty of the land. He was the first major British artist to specialize in landscape, and after his Italian years of 1750 to 1756 he treated English and Welsh scenery as he had the Roman countryside: as a vehicle for ideas and emotions.","c":"A late Wilson in which the lessons of Claude and Italy are applied to Welsh mountain scenery: golden, atmospheric light and a balanced composition turn a real mountain into a poetic prospect. The result is gentler and more domestic than the grand historical landscapes of his earlier years, and some viewers will prefer the older Wilson's drama. It shows how a painter of Italy could make Wales look like Arcadia."},"met:436305":{"d":"A small panel, about 27 by 34 centimeters, painted probably about 1625 to 1630 by Georg Flegel. The museum's keywords list a lemon, a pitcher, bread and a bird, so the picture is a modest tabletop still life of food and a vessel, painted on wood with the care of a miniature. Its small size invites close viewing, and its few objects are set out with the clarity of a display in a cabinet.","i":"Flegel worked in Frankfurt, one of the first German painters to specialize in still life, drawing on Netherlandish models; Frankfurt's merchant community, which included many émigrés from the southern Netherlands, collected such pictures. Bread, a lemon and a bird were everyday luxuries and markers of a well-provided table, and as in many still lifes they may also have been meant to recall the pleasures and brevity of life.","c":"Flegel's small panels are admired for sharp, patient observation: the crumb of bread, the glint of a pitcher and the skin of a lemon rendered with a clarity close to portraiture. Compared with Dutch contemporaries' tonal softness, his drawing is crisper and his effect more austere, closer to an inventory than a composition. It rewards looking more than it dazzles."},"nga:76207":{"d":"A basket piled with fruit, propped by two blue-and-white porcelain plates, nearly fills this small horizontal panel. Green, red and purple grapes, grape leaves, apples, plums, apricots, an orange and a quince lie piled between the plates, which tilt like cupped hands, and more fruit, including cherries and medlars, is scattered along the smoke-gray ledge. The background is purple-tinged gray, the light comes from the left, and the artist signed the panel at lower right. The oil on panel measures only about 18 by 23 centimeters.","i":"Still lifes of fruit and flowers were collectors' items in the early Dutch Republic, where wealth from trade allowed burghers to enjoy rarities such as imported oranges and Chinese porcelain. The blue-and-white plates are probably Wanli-period export porcelain, then a prized luxury. The quince, medlars and cherries ripen at different times, so the picture gathers an impossible abundance. Such pictures delighted in plenty, though viewers might also recall the fragility of ripeness.","c":"At scarcely the size of a book, the panel is a miniature feast: the fruit is observed with the clarity of a botanist, the porcelain glints with blue and white, and the clean light from the left gives every piece a sculpted shadow. The composition is stable, almost architectural, and the colors are sparing. It shows the precision Van der Ast learned from his teacher Bosschaert and a calm confidence of his own."},"nga:76208":{"d":"A low straw basket overflows with flowers on a slate-gray table: white and pink roses, irises, a striped carnation, flame-orange poppies and at least three tulips, one scarlet, one white veined with red and one red streaked with yellow. Four seashells lie at the left, including a spiky murex and a large triton shell occupied by a hermit crab, and at the right grapes, cherries, a plum and other fruit. A sprig of red berries sits at the center front, and a dragonfly, nearly lost in shadow, hovers at upper right. The panel measures about 18 by 24 centimeters.","i":"Flowers from different seasons crowd the bouquet, so the picture is an invention, not a record of one garden: it unites tulips of spring, roses of summer and fruit of autumn. Tulips, the passion of Dutch collectors in the 1620s, are the stars, and the streaked varieties were prized, though later shown to be caused by a virus. Shells added the wonders of the sea, and the dragonfly and the flowers hint at a theme of transience that Dutch viewers knew: beauty is brief.","c":"Van der Ast's delight in the unusual is the picture's engine: the tulips' striations, the spiky murex and the hermit crab make a collection of rarities rather than a mere bouquet. The small, dark ground makes every color glow, the composition is cleanly arranged, and the dragonfly is a witty touch. It is more of a catalog than an experience, a flaw typical of this precise school, but a delightful catalog."},"nga:158587":{"d":"A broad seascape at sunset, the horizon in the lower third where the sky meets a calm sea. Several sailing ships float in the distance beneath the setting sun, which casts pale yellow and orange light over sky and water, while dark purple clouds cross the pale blue sky and cooler blues and gray shadows lie in the water. Oil on canvas, about 96 by 127 centimeters, painted in 1894 by Hendrik Willem Mesdag.","i":"Scheveningen, a fishing village on the North Sea near The Hague, was the home subject of Hendrik Willem Mesdag, the leading marine painter of the Hague School. A banker's son who took up painting seriously in his mid-thirties, he painted the fishing fleet and the beach for decades, and his seas, with their gray, silvery tonal harmonies, defined the Dutch seascape for his generation.","c":"Mesdag's late seas are about atmosphere and tone: the distant ships are minor accents against a vast, quiet sky, and the color is held to harmonies of warm light and cool shadow. The subject lacks narrative and risks monotony, but it conveys the breadth and stillness of the North Sea shore. It is a restful, accomplished picture rather than a dramatic one."},"met:10782":{"d":"A tiny miniature portrait of a man named William Gale, painted in 1776 by Joseph Dunkerley in watercolor on ivory. It measures only about 3.5 by 2.9 centimeters, about the size of a large coin, and was meant to be held in the palm or set in a case, brooch or locket. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, and describe the genre as a miniature.","i":"Miniatures on ivory were the intimate portraits of the eighteenth century: exchanged between family and friends, carried by travelers and soldiers, and kept close to the body. The year 1776, which saw the American Declaration of Independence, was a time of separations in which small portraits mattered, though the circumstances of this commission are not documented. Dunkerley is recorded as active between 1776 and 1806.","c":"At this scale a painter has little room for error: the face must register at once and the finish must be even. Dunkerley's miniature is a small, workmanlike likeness whose appeal lies in its precision and the intimacy of the form, and the sitter is otherwise little documented. As an example of the miniaturist's craft it is neat and sincere, a quiet reminder of how people once kept one another near."},"met:437917":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted in oil on a wood panel about 125 by 97 centimeters, a format large enough for a three-quarter-length figure. The year given is 1651, the year the Antwerp portraitist Cornelis de Vos died. Her name is unknown; De Vos's women sitters are often shown in dark silk with a white collar and cuffs. The size and support suggest a formal commission for a household of some standing.","i":"De Vos was the portraitist of Antwerp's prosperous merchants and their families, and his sitters sought sober dignity, not flattery. Portraits of women of this size were often made as pendants to the husband's portrait, so this panel may once have hung with a companion, though that is only a possibility. The sitter's anonymity means her rank and family cannot be stated; the scale suggests a woman of means.","c":"De Vos's strength was an honest, unglamorous likeness: steady drawing, a clear sense of volume and a fine eye for white linen and black silk, qualities that kept him busy among Antwerp's burghers even as Van Dyck set the city's fashion for elegance. Portraits of this late date can feel plain next to Van Dyck's, but their plainness is also their appeal. Whatever its particular strengths, it belongs to the painter's sober tradition."},"met:437918":{"d":"A portrait of a young woman in oil on canvas, about 118 by 95 centimeters, with the year given as 1651. She holds a hand fan, a fashionable accessory, and her name is unknown. By the middle of the century canvas had become the usual support for a large portrait of this kind. The painter is Cornelis de Vos of Antwerp, who died in 1651, which would make this one of his last works.","i":"Fans were fashionable accessories for women in the Netherlands and Flanders in the seventeenth century, signifying elegance, leisure and a place in polite society, and they appear in portraits as part of a woman's presentation of herself. A young sitter might have been painted on the occasion of an engagement or marriage, but nothing says so. The picture is a record of how well-off Antwerp women wished to appear in mid-century.","c":"De Vos's quiet, truthful portraits stand somewhat apart from the glamour of Van Dyck, and a young woman with a fan gives him a chance to show his skill with fabric and a plain, unflattering gaze. Within the painter's output it is probably a late and typical work, honest, polished and unassuming."},"met:437824":{"d":"A half-length Madonna sits turned to the right with her head bowed, looking down at the naked Child on her lap. She wears a rose-red dress with a pleated bodice, a blue mantle with a green lining and a white veil over russet hair. The haloed Child turns toward the viewer, his hands raised. At the left hangs a green cloth, and at the right two tree trunks rise beside a landscape of a bay and pale hills under a blue sky. A stone ledge runs along the bottom. The panel is crossed by fine cracks and is worn in places.","i":"This is an early work from about 1508, when Titian was a young man in Venice, still close to Giovanni Bellini and Giorgione. The Metropolitan Museum notes that he first placed the figures in a more traditional upright pose, then changed the composition so that the Virgin leans toward the Child, producing a moment of shared affection instead of two divine icons. The pair of tree trunks echoes their bond. The picture was owned by Jean de Jullienne in Paris, sold in 1767 as a Titian, and later hung at Burghley House; it entered the Museum with the Jules Bache Collection in 1949.","c":"The idea is the best part: Titian takes a Bellini-style Madonna and loosens it, with the Virgin's lowered gaze and the Child's round body suggesting real tenderness instead of ceremony. The landscape is soft and Giorgionesque, and the paired trunks are neatly chosen. The weakness is the picture's condition. Cracking and wear make the faces and drapery hard to judge, and the Child's pose is a little awkward. It is hard to rank as a result: a promising, humane early work rather than a decisive one."},"met:437825":{"d":"A young man with shoulder-length auburn hair and a short reddish beard turns his head sharply to one side, his gaze slipping out of the picture beyond the viewer. His body is turned away, in a black tunic with deep folds and a narrow band of white shirt at the collar. At the lower right his hand holds a grey-green object, probably a glove, which just crosses the edge of the picture. The background is a plain blue-grey, and light falls from the left onto his forehead, cheek and the white linen. The face is carefully modelled in soft transitions, while the black sleeve is brushed in broad, loose strokes that merge with the dark ground.","i":"The sitter is unknown. The Metropolitan Museum dates the portrait about 1515, when Titian was in his twenties and still absorbing the example of Giorgione, who died in 1510. The pose, a half-length figure turned in space against a dark ground, with a sideways glance and a single prop, comes from Giorgione's portraits. The picture was once given to Giorgione and to Palma Vecchio, and later specialists, among them Roberto Longhi, agreed on Titian. It was bequeathed to the Museum by Benjamin Altman in 1913. The date is approximate, and other writers place it a few years earlier.","c":"The strength here is restraint. The turn of the head, the soft modelling of the face and the lack of ornament give the sitter a quiet presence, and the white collar sets it off. The costume is less convincing: the black sleeve nearly vanishes into the background, and the hand is an afterthought. Compared with Titian's later portraits, the structure is simple and the sitter's character is held back; he is an elegant presence rather than a personality. Still, the glance and the loose hair show Titian already treating a portrait as an encounter, not an inventory."},"nga:354":{"d":"A small, nearly square canvas, about 66 by 55 centimeters, painted about 1515 to 1520 by Titian, showing an allegorical subject: Cupid, the winged boy-god of love, with the wheel of the title, an emblem of time and fortune. Allegories of this kind, painted in Venice in Titian's early years, were made for private owners who enjoyed learned conceits and ambiguous poetic images.","i":"The wheel is an old emblem of the changes of fortune, and pairing it with Cupid suggests a reflection on love and time: love may turn with the wheel, or may be the force that drives it. The precise program is not documented, so the subject remains open to interpretation, as is common with Venetian allegories of the early sixteenth century, which were made for educated patrons who liked such puzzles.","c":"Early Titian is about paint: color laid in rich, soft transitions, flesh modeled by light rather than line. A small allegory like this is a showcase for that sensuousness more than for a clear program, and its appeal is to the eye. The obscure meaning may frustrate, but the handling rewards close looking, and it shows the young painter already stepping out of the shadow of Giorgione."},"nga:41638":{"d":"A grey-bearded old man in the red of a cardinal fills the canvas from the waist up, set against a plain brown ground. His shoulders are angled to one side, and his head turns the other way so that he looks off beyond the frame with deep-set, watchful eyes under heavy brows. A red four-cornered cap sits over his ear-length dark hair; a short, buttoned red cape, painted in broad, silky strokes, drapes from his shoulders over a white garment with a red cuff. One hand, palm up, lies loosely at his waist. Light comes from the left, catching his high forehead, long hooked nose and the soft, blended strokes of the beard.","i":"Pietro Bembo (1470–1547), a Venetian scholar and poet, wrote the Prose della volgar lingua, which helped make Tuscan the model for literary Italian, and served as secretary to Pope Leo X. He was made a cardinal in March 1539. The portrait is believed to have been commissioned by Bembo himself, probably to mark that honour, and is probably the picture he mentions in a letter of May 1540. Titian had already painted him at least once. Bembo is shown not in ceremony but as a thoughtful man glancing aside, which suited the ideal of the Renaissance humanist who became a churchman. The picture came to the National Gallery from the Kress collection.","c":"A study in economy. The red cape fills the lower half in broad, silky strokes, and the face, lit against a plain ground, carries the whole drama. The averted gaze makes Bembo look like an intelligent man interrupted, not a figure posed for posterity. The hand is more summary than the head and its gesture is not clearly explained, a loose end that a stricter portraitist would have tied. Even so, Titian's mature handling is already in place, with texture built from quick, open brushwork. It is quieter than his portraits of popes but no less alert."},"nga:41593":{"d":"A portrait of a boy, Ranuccio Farnese, painted in 1541 to 1542 by Titian in oil on canvas, about 90 by 74 centimeters. The sitter was about eleven years old, so the picture is a child's portrait, but of a child with an important place: a grandson of Pope Paul III, already holding a high office in a religious order. The size suits a half-length portrait, and gives the boy a dignity beyond his age.","i":"The Farnese family owed its rise to papal power: Pope Paul III, who reigned from 1534 to 1549, enriched his grandchildren with titles, and Ranuccio, destined for the Church from boyhood, became a cardinal at fifteen. A portrait of a boy in such a position presented a future prince of the Church and a family asset. Titian, the leading painter of Venice, was a natural choice for the Farnese, who later brought him to Rome.","c":"Titian paints childhood with little sentimentality: the boy is presented with the gravity his position required, and the surface is alive with soft, warm color. Child portraits of the period often make the sitter a miniature adult, and one looks for the mixture of formality and youth that Titian could achieve. It is a strong, restrained example of his portraiture of the early 1540s."},"nga:435":{"d":"A woman, shown with an apple in her hand, painted about 1550 by Titian in oil on canvas, about 98 by 74 centimeters. The title records the sitter's attribute rather than her name, since she is unidentified. In Renaissance Venice a woman holding fruit could be a bride, an allegory of love or a poetic image of beauty, so the picture sits between portrait and allegory.","i":"The apple has many meanings in Renaissance art: the fruit Paris awarded to Venus, the tempting fruit of Eve, a love gift, a symbol of fertility and marriage. In Venice painters such as Titian produced half-length images of beautiful women that blur portrait and fantasy, often for male collectors. Whether this sitter was a real woman or an idealized type remains unknown, and the picture's appeal lay partly in that uncertainty.","c":"Titian's half-length women of about 1550 are made of warm light and flesh that seems to breathe, and his handling is looser than in his earlier years, with broken, suggestive brushwork that gives the surface life. The ambiguity of sitter and apple prevents a single reading, but the pleasure is in the paint. It is a strong example of the late Titian's way with a female sitter, though her character remains elusive."},"nga:46144":{"d":"A formal portrait of Andrea Gritti, Doge of Venice, painted about 1546 to 1550 by Titian in oil on canvas, about 134 by 103 centimeters. Gritti led the Republic from 1523 to 1538, so the picture was made some years after his death and is necessarily based on earlier likenesses. A doge's portrait normally shows the ducal cap and robes of office, a ceremonial image rather than a private one.","i":"Gritti was a merchant and soldier-diplomat who had lived in Constantinople before leading the Venetian recovery of Padua and, later, becoming doge, and he was remembered as a strong, forceful ruler. The Republic honored its doges with portraits in the Doge's Palace and in family collections. A posthumous likeness kept the memory of a leader whose personality had become part of Venice's civic myth.","c":"Titian was the great portraitist of power in the sixteenth century, and a likeness of a long-dead doge shows what he could do with a type: a massive, controlled presence, color built from warm reds and golds, and a face that carries the weight of office. The posthumous origin may explain a certain ceremonial distance compared with his portraits from life. Still, it is an impressive image of authority."},"nga:41":{"d":"A half-naked woman sits at the left, her body angled to the left while she looks across it toward a mirror held up by a winged child, one hand pressed to her chest. A heavy crimson velvet robe, lined with fur and edged with gold embroidery, falls around her hips. Her blond hair is coiled in rows of pearls, a teardrop pearl hangs from her ear, and gold bracelets and rings glint on her arms and fingers. A second child, half hidden behind the mirror, reaches over to hold a ring of laurel above her head. A dark green curtain hangs at the upper left. The brushwork is loose and the colors warm, with the pale flesh set against deep reds and browns, and the mirror gives back a glimpse of her face.","i":"The goddess of love admires herself, attended by two cupids, one with the mirror and one with a wreath of victory. The pose recalls ancient statues of Venus, and the picture has been read both as a celebration of ideal beauty and as a warning about vanity; it may well mean both. Titian made several versions. This one, from about 1555, is believed to be the earliest and the only one entirely by his own hand. X-rays show that he painted it over an abandoned double portrait, reusing a red cloak. It stayed in his house until his death in 1576, and Rubens later copied another version. The Soviet government sold it from the Hermitage in 1931, and it passed to Andrew Mellon.","c":"Late Titian at his most sensuous and least tidy. The brushwork is loose enough to melt the edges of the flesh into the velvet, and the fur, pearls, and gold are suggested more than described, yet every material reads. It is a demonstration that finish can be left behind without losing physical truth. The weaker passages are the cupids, who seem slightly clumsy and stuck on, and the general warmth, which can make the picture feel heavy. As a meditation on beauty and its reflection, however, it is among the most complete of his later works."},"met:437823":{"d":"A portrait of Filippo Archinto, Archbishop of Milan, painted in the mid-1550s by Titian in oil on canvas, about 118 by 94 centimeters. The museum's keywords record only the sitter and the genre. At this size it is a formal portrait of a high churchman, in which robe, hands and gaze convey rank and temperament, and it belongs to the long run of prelate portraits Titian painted for Italian and papal patrons.","i":"Archinto (about 1500 to 1558) was a Milanese churchman who served the papacy as a diplomat and administrator before being named archbishop of his native Milan in 1556. His tenure was reportedly clouded by political opposition from Milan's Spanish rulers, and he died in 1558. Titian, who had painted popes, emperors and cardinals, portrayed him in his late years, when the artist's own manner had grown looser and more concentrated.","c":"Late Titian portraits strip away display and rely on a few dark, rich tones, flesh built from warm, loosely brushed strokes and an intensity of attention. A cleric in dark robes is ideal for that economy: the head and hands carry the picture. The result is dignified and psychologically alert, and compared with the sitter's high rank it is notably restrained."},"nga:43725":{"d":"A very large canvas, nearly square at about 238 by 263 centimeters, showing Saint John the Evangelist on Patmos, the Aegean island where, by tradition, he was exiled and saw the visions set down in the Book of Revelation. Pictures of this subject usually pair the saint with his symbol, the eagle, and with a book or writing materials, set in a wild, open landscape. The museum dates the work to about 1553 to 1555, when Titian was in his sixties. A picture of this size was most likely made for a church or a confraternity rather than a private room.","i":"Tradition identifies John with the author of the fourth Gospel and of Revelation, and places the writing of the latter in exile on Patmos. Painters therefore showed him there as writer and visionary at once. His emblem, the eagle, comes from the four living creatures of Ezekiel and Revelation, which early Christian writers matched to the four Evangelists; John received the soaring eagle because his Gospel opens on the highest theological ground. Renaissance viewers read the lonely island as a setting for revelation.","c":"Titian was in his sixties here, and the late manner is the interest: contours loosen into broad, visible strokes, and warm color and shifting light carry the feeling that line carried in his earlier work. A solitary visionary in an open landscape suits that handling, since it lets figure and sky merge. Within his output the picture belongs with the large religious canvases of the 1550s, in which drama comes from atmosphere and paint rather than from crisp outline."},"met:437826":{"d":"Venus, nude and seen from behind, perches on a red velvet-covered seat at the left and throws her arms around Adonis, who sits at the right and half turns away from her. Her fair hair is in curls and a thin chain hangs at her neck. Adonis, in a sheer pink tunic open at the chest and laced sandals, grips a spear with one hand and the leashes of two hounds with the other; one hound looks up, the other crouches and stares out at the viewer. At the left a small winged Cupid hides his face beneath a tree where a bow and quiver hang. Behind, a shaft of dawn light breaks through the clouds. Oil on canvas, about 107 by 133 centimetres.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses. Venus, goddess of love, has fallen for the mortal huntsman Adonis and tries to stop him leaving for the hunt, fearing, rightly, that he will be killed; the frightened Cupid hides at the left. Titian called such mythological pictures “poesie,” poems in paint, and devised this composition in the early 1550s for Philip II of Spain. He explained to Philip that he showed Venus from behind to vary the view after a Danaë shown from the front. The Met notes that Titian's workshop made many versions, but that this one is of exceptional quality and was painted by Titian himself.","c":"Titian is at his most tender and most worldly here. The back of Venus, glowing against the shaded foliage, is one of his great nudes, and Adonis's gentle, resigned glance gives the mythology a human ache. The loose, late handling of the dogs and landscape does not let every passage hold together, and Cupid and the foliage are murky. But the main group, locked in a clinging X of limbs, has a physical truth often missing from later treatments of the subject."},"nga:43722":{"d":"A vertical portrait, cut off at the thighs, of an older man with receding white hair and a long white beard that falls to his chest. He wears a full suit of dark pewter armor, picked out with cream-white highlights, under a burgundy-red cloak tied over one shoulder. His body angles to the viewer's right, but his dark eyes look sideways out of the picture. One hand braces a baton against his hip while the other gathers the cloak. His helmet rests on a gray stone ledge behind him, against a charcoal-gray ground.","i":"The sitter was a Venetian naval commander, and the picture is built from the signs of that office: the armor, the baton of command, the helmet set aside on a ledge, the heavy cloak. Venetian patricians were usually painted in dark robes and a sober manner, so the armored format marks him as a man of action in the Republic's service. Titian used the same convention for princes and generals, and the sideways glance adds a note of watchfulness to a standard formula.","c":"Titian gets a great deal from restraint. The charcoal ground, the darkly gleaming armor and the single burgundy mass of the cloak leave the weathered face and the hands to carry the picture, and the oblique glance supplies tension without any gesture. The pose is a stock commander's formula, so invention is limited, but the surface handling and the sense of a living, wary old man lift it well above routine."},"nga:1223":{"d":"A mythological canvas of about 107 by 136 centimeters, a cabinet-scale picture made for private pleasure rather than for a church. It treats the story of Venus and Adonis from Ovid's Metamorphoses in the manner of Titian's well-known compositions of the 1550s, in which the goddess tries to hold back the young hunter as he leaves. The museum's date, a span from the 1540s to the early 1560s, suggests a picture that was begun, and then reworked or completed, over a long period in the Venetian studio.","i":"Ovid tells how Venus, wounded by her own son's arrow, fell in love with the mortal Adonis and warned him against dangerous game. He ignored her, and a boar killed him; from his blood she made the anemone. Titian chose the moment of parting, so a viewer who knows the ending reads every gesture as doomed. He called his mythologies poesie, poems in paint, and they were made for educated patrons who enjoyed both the story and its sensuous display.","c":"The attraction of the subject for Titian is the clash of bodies: the goddess's pale, yielding flesh against the hunter's impatient stride, all built in warm, broken color. The composition, repeated for several patrons, became one of the most influential of the century. Because the studio repeated the design, the quality of individual versions varies, and the wide date range here hints at a complicated making. The idea is stronger than any single repetition of it."},"met:437827":{"d":"A nude Venus, a young lute player and Cupid, shown as a winged child, share a scene in which a curtain forms part of the setting. The canvas, about 165 by 210 centimeters, is large for a private picture. The Met catalogues it as by Titian and his workshop, and it dates from about 1565 to 1570, in the painter's old age. It belongs to a group of related compositions in which Titian paired the goddess of love with a musician.","i":"The pairing of a nude goddess with a musician is usually read as a meditation on the pleasures of sight and hearing, the two senses Renaissance writers ranked highest. The lutenist's attention to the goddess stands for the admirer who looks at beauty and cannot possess it, and the lute was the instrument of love songs. Cupid completes the allegory. Such pictures hung in private rooms, where viewers enjoyed both the erotic charge and the learned conceit.","c":"The late Titian is felt in the paint: flesh built from broad, warm, loosely blended strokes, with forms kept soft rather than sharply drawn. The composition is a well-tried formula and, as the Met's attribution to Titian and workshop suggests, not every passage has the same authority. Even so, the sensuous mood and the tension between music and gaze hold the picture together, and it shows how profitable the design proved."},"cma:134620":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 135 by 217 centimeters, showing the kings arriving at the stable with their retinue. The Cleveland Museum of Art points to the naturalistic touches: riders struggling to keep restless horses and mules still, a bowing white horse with its groom in shadow, the cradled Christ child, and a small dog urinating on a beam. The museum notes that overcleaning has damaged the picture, though many fine passages survive. It is attributed to Titian and his large workshop, with the extent of his own hand still under study.","i":"The Magi, in Matthew's Gospel wise men who followed a star from the east, were the first Gentiles to honor Christ with gold, frankincense and myrrh, and their arrival is celebrated at Epiphany. Renaissance painters turned the story into a pageant of rank and travel, with horses, grooms and exotic dress. Titian's workshop repeated successful designs for different buyers, which is why versions in Milan and Madrid exist and why the question of how much is by Titian himself remains open.","c":"The Cleveland picture lives in its details rather than in a unified drama: the bowing white horse, the shadowed groom, the tender cradled infant and the comic dog bring the sacred scene down to ordinary life with real observation. Overcleaning has flattened the surface, and the museum itself is cautious about how much is by Titian. Even so, the passages that survive well show a painter of the first rank, or a studio working close beside him."},"met:437320":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Elizabeth Bushby, is portrayed in a miniature painted in 1804 on a thin slab of ivory by the English artist Andrew Plimer. Plimer was known for fresh, soft-focus portraits of women, with loosely curling hair and misty, cloud-like backgrounds, and the recorded motifs here are simply a woman and a portrait. Miniatures of this kind were small enough to be worn as jewelry or held in the hand.","i":"Andrew Plimer and his brother Nathaniel were among the most fashionable London miniaturists of the years around 1800, and Andrew trained under the leading miniaturist of the age, Richard Cosway. Portrait miniatures were tokens of affection exchanged between lovers, family members and friends, often set in lockets and bracelets. Nothing in the title identifies the sitter's rank or role beyond her name, so the picture preserves a private relationship rather than a public status.","c":"Plimer at his best achieves a breathable softness: hair that seems to float, skin with a porcelain glow, a cloudy sky stippled in behind. These virtues are also limits, since the formula flatters more than it probes, and sitters tend to resemble one another. In 1804 he was at the height of his fashion, and his miniatures show the same taste for sensibility that governed contemporary novels and fashion plates."},"met:11698":{"d":"A woman in a lace-trimmed ruffle is portrayed in a miniature only about 5 by 4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1818 by Anna Claypoole Peale. The title names her as Mrs. Thomas Larcombe, born Anna Smith. At this scale a sitter's face is only a few centimeters across, and the artist builds it from tiny strokes and dots so that the warmth of the ivory glows through the flesh tones.","i":"Anna Claypoole Peale belonged to Philadelphia's Peale dynasty of artists, the daughter of the painter James Peale and niece of Charles Willson Peale, and she made a professional career in the portrait miniature, an art open to women. Pictures like this were exchanged and worn as keepsakes. Naming the sitter by her married and maiden names records both her identity and her family connections.","c":"At five centimeters tall, the picture shows what the best miniaturists did: fix a character with a few exact touches. The lace and ruffle give the sitter a crisp, fashionable outline, and the controlled stippling keeps the flesh luminous. Peale's miniatures are admired for clarity and a restrained finish rather than for flourish, and this early example shows both qualities."},"met:17450":{"d":"A girl named Sarah Ann Beck is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 7.6 by 6.4 centimeters, painted around 1818 by Anna Claypoole Peale. The recorded motifs are simply a girl and a portrait. The size is larger than some of the artist's tiniest miniatures, which leaves room to show the child's face and the upper part of her dress. Girls' miniatures of the period often show a simple, high-waisted dress and loosely dressed hair.","i":"Portraits of children were precious keepsakes in an age when many did not survive childhood, and a miniature could be carried by a parent or kept in a family case. Children were difficult sitters, and miniaturists often worked from quick sketches made in short sittings, finishing details later. Sarah Ann's name is the only fact the title gives, so her family and circumstances are lost, and the picture preserves a face and little else.","c":"A child's portrait tests a miniaturist's tact: the face must be fresh and unforced, without the hard lines that suit adults. Peale's cool, clear manner is well adapted to youth, and the softness of ivory flatters a child's skin. Where adult sitters can look formal, a portrait of this kind can have an unguarded directness that suits the genre well."},"met:15305":{"d":"An unnamed woman is the subject of this miniature of about 6.7 by 5.4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1822 by Anna Claypoole Peale. The title, Portrait of a Lady, shows that her name was lost or never recorded, and the museum lists only a woman and a portrait. Women's miniatures of the early 1820s typically show a bust-length sitter in a high-waisted dress, with softly dressed hair.","i":"Miniatures were often commissioned as gifts, at engagement or marriage or before a long separation, and were set in lockets, bracelets or small cases. Unlike a large oil portrait, a miniature was a private object, shown to friends and kept close. The loss of this sitter's name is a reminder of that privacy: a keepsake that her family recognized at a glance has become anonymous.","c":"Peale's strengths are consistency and polish: the faces are modeled in fine, even strokes, the ivory glows through the flesh, and the composition is plain and balanced. The consistency is also the limit, for her sitters of these years can seem alike in their composure. Within those limits it is a pleasing, well-made object, and the unnamed lady keeps her dignity."},"met:14312":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. John A. Brown (Grace Brown) is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 7.3 by 6.2 centimeters, painted in 1827 by Anna Claypoole Peale. The recorded details include a braid in the sitter's hair, a style in keeping with the later 1820s, when braids and loops of hair were piled high on the head as a mark of fashion.","i":"In 1827 Peale was in mid-career, and her miniatures were being shown at exhibitions such as those of the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, where miniatures hung alongside oils. The braided hair marks the period's elaborate styles, in which a woman's hair was an object of display. Naming the sitter as Mrs. John A. Brown identifies her in the conventional way, by her husband's name.","c":"The generous size of this miniature gives Peale room to show her skill in the hair and the face, and the braid adds a textured, three-dimensional note that relieves the smoothness of the flesh. The pose follows the type and is slightly stiff, as is common. It is a clean, accomplished record of a fashionable woman of her moment."},"met:11699":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 6.7 by 5.2 centimeters, dated 1832, by Anna Claypoole Peale. The title, Portrait of a Gentleman, is all the museum records of his identity. Men's portraits of the early 1830s usually show the sitter in a dark coat with a high collar and a stock or cravat, against a plain or lightly clouded background, with the face the main field of attention.","i":"A gentleman's miniature of 1832 might mark a marriage, a business success or a departure, and it would be kept in a case among family treasures. By this date miniatures were made for a broader middle-class clientele than the aristocratic buyers of the 1780s, and a portrait declared respectability. Anna Peale continued the family tradition of portraiture, in which a man's likeness was a sign of his place in the young republic.","c":"Male sitters give a miniaturist less to play with than women: the clothing is dark and the hair short, so the burden falls on the face. Peale's even, careful modeling suits that task, and the plain ground keeps attention on the head. The result is dignified rather than lively, and it shows the sober masculine taste of Jacksonian America."},"met:15123":{"d":"A man named George Weaver is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 5.8 by 4.8 centimeters, dated 1833, by Anna Claypoole Peale. The recorded motifs are a man and a portrait. At this size the sitter's head is only a few centimeters high, so everything depends on the artist's control of tiny strokes and on the translucent glow of the ivory beneath. Such a likeness was presumably made for the sitter's family or a close friend.","i":"The ivory support, a thin sheet cut from an elephant's tusk, gives miniatures their luminous flesh tones: the painter works in transparent watercolor with touches of opaque white, and the pale surface reflects light back through the paint. Weaver's name is all the title records, but the craft is plain to see: in 1833 Peale was a seasoned professional in her early forties, with a long career behind her.","c":"A small, sober portrait like this shows the miniaturist's craft without distraction: the stippled cheek, the clean edge of the collar, the quiet ground. The danger of such precision is dryness, and the test is whether a few warm touches in the flesh keep the face alive. A named sitter gives the picture a personal edge that anonymous examples lack."},"met:15124":{"d":"A woman named Mrs. Samuel Vaughan is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 5.9 by 4.6 centimeters, dated 1838, by Anna Claypoole Peale. The recorded motifs are a woman and a portrait, and the title gives her only by her married name. The date places it a year before the announcement of the daguerreotype in 1839, when photography began to challenge the miniature as the usual way of keeping a small likeness.","i":"Within a few years of 1839 the portrait miniature began its decline: a daguerreotype was cheaper, faster and, to many buyers, more accurate. Painters like Peale held out by emphasizing color, softness and the artist's touch, but the trade shrank. Mrs. Vaughan's portrait belongs to the last years in which a miniature was the usual choice for a prosperous family wanting a small likeness.","c":"As a late work in the genre, the miniature shows a craft at its maturity just before the camera overtook it. Peale's clarity and restraint are qualities the new medium could not quite match, along with colored flesh and the glow of ivory. The risk of the period was a drift toward smooth blandness as buyers asked for flattery, and one looks for the points where the likeness resists it."},"met:15122":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 7 by 5.7 centimeters by Anna Claypoole Peale. The museum gives no date, and the title records no name, so the portrait stands as an example of the miniaturist's craft rather than a document of a particular man. The recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait, and the format, a head-and-shoulders likeness on a thin plaque of ivory, was meant to be held, worn or kept in a case.","i":"Miniatures traveled in pockets, lockets and letters, which is one reason so many lose their sitters' names: a portrait separated from its case or its inscription becomes anonymous within a generation. What remains is the likeness itself and the evidence of a trained hand, in an art that Anna Claypoole Peale practiced for decades as a professional in the Peale family tradition.","c":"With no sitter or date to lean on, the miniature must be judged on its painting alone, and that is the right test for this artist. Peale's hallmark is an even, restrained modeling, with soft transitions in the flesh and a clear contour; what a critic looks for is whether the eyes carry the sitter's life. It is an unusually pure test, with nothing but craft to assess."},"met:15125":{"d":"George Washington is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 7.7 by 6.2 centimeters by Anna Claypoole Peale. The museum gives no date. Washington sat to several members of the Peale family, among them her father James and her uncle Charles Willson Peale, and a miniature of him by Anna is probably a copy of one of the family's portraits, made long after the president's death in 1799.","i":"After his death in 1799 Washington's image became a national relic, and copies of the portraits made from life by the Peale family were in constant demand for homes, schools and public buildings. A miniature of the first president, kept in a case, could serve as a patriotic keepsake and a mark of civic virtue. The Peales, who had known and painted Washington, had a particular authority in reproducing his features.","c":"Copying a famous face is a test of discipline, not invention: the task is to keep a known likeness crisp at pocket scale, and Peale's even, restrained touch is well suited to that. What is missing, inevitably, is the direct observation that makes the family's life portraits compelling, so this is better judged as a loyal transcription than as a fresh portrait. Its value lies in craft and in the connection to Washington."},"met:10756":{"d":"A man identified as J. W. Gale is portrayed in a miniature of about 7.5 by 5.7 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1807 by Anson Dickinson. The recorded motifs are a man and a portrait. Dickinson, a Connecticut-born miniaturist, was by this decade among the busiest American practitioners of the art, traveling between cities to find sitters. Miniatures of this size were set in cases or lockets.","i":"In the early republic portrait miniatures were the most affordable form of likeness for the rising professional and merchant class, made by artists who traveled between cities and exhibited at academies. A sitter like J. W. Gale, known now only by initials and surname, was probably an ordinary citizen, not a celebrity, and the survival of his likeness shows the wide spread of portraiture in the young country.","c":"Dickinson's miniatures of these years are often direct and plain, with firm drawing, slight stylization and a clear, light palette; they lack the airy elegance of the English school but gain in candor. The reticent sitter in dark coat and light ground is typical, and the face carries the picture. It is an honest, workmanlike portrait that shows what American patrons wanted: likeness, respectability and an economical price."},"met:10754":{"d":"A woman known as Mrs. Robert Watts, born Matilda Ridley, is portrayed in a miniature of about 7.3 by 5.7 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1810 by Anson Dickinson. The title records her both by her husband's name and her maiden name, and the recorded motifs name only the sitter. The size is a common one for Dickinson's miniatures of these years.","i":"Women of the merchant and professional families of the early republic had miniatures made at engagement or marriage, to be given to a husband or kept as heirlooms. The title names the sitter twice, by husband and by birth family, which is how contemporaries understood a woman's place in a web of kinship and how a museum can trace such an object through families.","c":"Dickinson's women's portraits are sound: even, careful modeling, a soft setting and attention to the fine details of dress. He lacks the refinement of his famous contemporary Edward Greene Malbone, and the pose follows the standard. Even so, the picture shows the dignified simplicity of American Federal taste, in which a clear likeness and a restrained palette were enough."},"met:15034":{"d":"William Ward Burrows II, as the museum's data identify the sitter, is portrayed in a miniature of about 7.3 by 5.7 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory between about 1807 and 1813 by Anson Dickinson. The recorded motifs are a man and a portrait. The date range ends in 1813, the year of Burrows's death, so the miniature may show him in the last years of his short life.","i":"Burrows (1785-1813) was a U.S. Navy officer and the son of the first Commandant of the Marine Corps. As a lieutenant in command of the brig Enterprise, he was mortally wounded in September 1813 when his ship defeated HMS Boxer off the coast of Maine, a victory that made him a naval hero of the War of 1812. A miniature of such a man would have become a precious memorial after his death.","c":"Dickinson fixes the likeness in a few clean strokes, and the date range makes the object poignant. The recorded motifs show no ship or battle, only the face, and that plainness suits the miniature and leaves the sitter's history to the title. It is a competent, serious portrait whose interest is as much historical as artistic."},"met:14308":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 7 by 5.7 centimeters, painted around 1814 by Anson Dickinson. The title, Portrait of a Gentleman, is the museum's way of recording that the sitter's name is lost. The recorded motifs name only a man and a portrait, so the picture's interest lies in the face and the economy of its design.","i":"1814 was the third year of war with Britain, a time of anxiety and mobilization, yet the demand for private likenesses continued. Dickinson's patrons were men of business and the professions who wanted a keepsake in a case or locket, often for a wife or a mother. The loss of the sitter's name is common among miniatures, which were held privately and rarely carried inscriptions.","c":"Dickinson's strength in these years is directness: a sturdy outline, clean color and a face modeled in small, sure touches. What his miniatures lack is the glamour of the English school; what they have is a sober truthfulness that suits American sitters of the Federal period. This unnamed gentleman offers the viewer little but his face, and the portrait asks to be judged on that alone."},"met:10750":{"d":"Dr. Valentine Mott (1785-1865), the New York surgeon, is portrayed in a miniature of about 7.3 by 6.4 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1820 by Anson Dickinson. The recorded motifs name only the sitter, so the portrait relies on the face and its simple presentation. Mott was then about thirty-five and already among the most skilled surgeons in the United States, a professional man whose likeness a miniature could keep in a family case.","i":"Mott became famous in 1818 for the first successful ligation of the innominate artery, a daring operation, and went on to a long career as a teacher and operator, with students and patients across the country. A miniature from about 1820 therefore portrays a rising medical celebrity, and miniatures of professional men served both as family keepsakes and as images of respectable achievement. The sitter's name and profession give it a documentary value beyond its quality.","c":"A portrait of a distinguished surgeon invites a crisp, observant treatment, and Dickinson's manner usually supplies one: a firm face, simply modeled, with the economy typical of his work. It lacks the poetry of the best English miniatures but has the directness suited to a man of action. As a record of a famous medical figure at the start of his career, it is both a likeness and a document."},"met:10751":{"d":"Edward Livingston, the lawyer and statesman, is portrayed in a large miniature of about 10.5 by 8.3 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1827 by Anson Dickinson. The recorded motifs include a quill, the pen of a writer and legislator, beside the sitter. The size, larger than many miniatures of the time, suggests an important commission, and the quill identifies the sitter's calling at a glance: a man who made his career with words and laws.","i":"Livingston (1764-1836), a New Yorker who moved to Louisiana, served as mayor of New York, congressman and senator, and later as Andrew Jackson's Secretary of State. He is best remembered as a legal reformer: the criminal and civil codes he drafted for Louisiana were widely admired by reformers in America and Europe. The quill therefore reads as a precise attribute: the pen of the lawgiver, a mark of the thinker rather than the soldier.","c":"A miniature of a public man is always a tension between private likeness and public image. Dickinson chooses the plain approach: a steady face, simple dress and the quill as the sole sign of the sitter's calling. The generous ivory allows more care in the features than his smaller works, and the result is a dignified portrait that fits the reformer's reputation for seriousness."},"met:15035":{"d":"A woman named Mary Fullerton, known as Mrs. George Burroughs, is portrayed in a miniature of about 9 by 6 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory between 1825 and 1830 by Anson Dickinson. The recorded details include an elaborate headdress, a fashion of the later 1820s, when hair and headgear grew tall and ornamented. The size is large for a miniature, and the upright format gives room for the head and its decoration.","i":"In the later 1820s fashionable women dressed for display: large sleeves, wide necklines and headdresses of ribbon, lace or feathers that added height. A miniature of this kind records not just a face but a woman's participation in fashion and her family's means. The title again names the sitter by her husband's name and her maiden name, a double identification that museums use to trace such objects through families.","c":"Fashionable headgear is a gift to a miniaturist, who can show texture and pattern, and the size of the ivory leaves room for it to read. Dickinson's plain, firm style avoids prettiness, which keeps the sitter from dissolving into her costume. It is a good, clear record of late-1820s fashion."},"met:15036":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 7.7 by 6.4 centimeters, dated 1838, by Anson Dickinson. The title records no name, and the museum lists only a man and a portrait. The date places the work at the end of the golden age of the American miniature, just before the daguerreotype began to replace painted keepsakes. At this size the likeness was meant to be held in the hand or set in a case.","i":"By 1838 Dickinson, born in 1779, was nearing sixty and had spent more than three decades painting miniatures, a span that took him through the Federal period and the age of Jackson. The miniature, an art of small private likenesses, was about to be overtaken by the camera, which could record a face in minutes for a fraction of the cost. Portraits like this one, with an anonymous sitter, show how the art served ordinary clients as well as celebrities.","c":"A late work by an experienced hand shows the confidence of long practice: economical drawing, an easy handling of the ivory and no fuss. Next to the more elaborate Victorian miniatures that followed, it keeps the plainness of the Federal tradition. Because the sitter is anonymous, it stands only on the quality of its painting, and there it is solid rather than inspired."},"nga:231772":{"d":"A very small portrait on copper, only about 17 by 13 centimeters, of a figure seen from the waist up and turned slightly to the right, one hand raised to the chest. The face is pale with rosy cheeks, a thin mustache and long curly brown hair. A loose blue garment hangs over a billowing white shirt. Behind is a dark interior with an arched window on the right, through which a landscape of trees and a distant spire appears under a blue sky.","i":"The title suggests an artist playing the part of a lover, probably in a self-portrait. The hand on the heart is the old gesture of sincere devotion, and the loose, open dress looks more like costume than everyday wear. In the Dutch Republic, small pictures of amorous young men appealed to buyers who enjoyed wit and fine finish. The arched window recalls the framing devices of the Leiden fine painters, whose tiny, polished pictures were prized by collectors.","c":"On a copper plate this small the paint can be handled with enamel-like smoothness, and the little view of landscape and spire in the window shows the care that Leiden painters lavished on distance. Ary de Vois does not match the finish of Gerrit Dou or the sparkle of Frans van Mieris, whose manner he echoes, and the pose is a stock one for its day. As a polished, charming cabinet picture it succeeds on its own modest terms."},"met:436657":{"d":"A portrait of a young man, painted in oil and gold on a sheet of paper that has since been laid down on a wood panel, about 52 by 38 centimeters. The title gives the sitter's dates as about 1495 to 1522, and the picture is dated 1517, so he was in his early twenties and Holbein, born about 1497, was barely younger. The sitter belonged to the Hertenstein family of Lucerne. Paper is a support more often used for studies than for finished portraits.","i":"Holbein was making his name in Basel and Lucerne when he painted this, working for patrician families. A portrait of a young man in 1517 recorded rank and a moment of life, and the sitter's early death in 1522, at about twenty-seven, gives the image a poignancy he could not have foreseen. The Hertenstein family was prominent in Lucerne, which probably explains why the young painter was asked to portray one of its sons.","c":"Holbein's early portraits show a young painter already committed to exact observation rather than flattery, and this one belongs to that group. The technique, oil with gold on paper, is experimental, and the design is simpler and less assured than the portraits he later made in England. What it shows is how early his instinct for a convincing head was formed, which is the quality that eventually made him one of the great portraitists of the northern Renaissance."},"met:436658":{"d":"A young man in a black cap and a black silk gown with a fur collar sits half turned, looking steadily at the viewer, his auburn hair cut in a straight fringe. A small ruffled collar shows at his neck, and his clasped hands rest on a green table covering, holding a pair of gloves, a gold signet ring on one finger. At the lower left lie a small leather-bound book with gilt clasps and a folded paper bearing a line of handwriting. The background is a deep, flat blue, and gold lettering on either side reads ANNO 1532 and AETATIS SVAE 29, giving the year and the sitter's age. It is a small, finely finished portrait in oil and gold on oak, about 42 by 32 centimetres.","i":"Holbein made this portrait in 1532, the year he returned to England after several years in Basel. Among his first London sitters were German merchants of the Steelyard, the Hanseatic League's trading base on the Thames, and the sitter, from a merchant family of Cologne, is usually placed in that circle. The picture follows a formula Holbein used for them: a plain coloured ground, a few objects that hint at the sitter's life, and an inscription fixing year and age. The inscription puts the man's birth about 1503; he died in 1560. Within a few years Holbein was painting for Henry VIII and had become the leading portraitist in England.","c":"Holbein's clarity is the whole point. The face is built from soft, exact modelling, without flattery; the black gown is full of shifting satin highlights; the book, ring and paper are rendered with the same unblinking care as the skin. What is missing is any hint of what the man is thinking: the gaze is level, the mouth closed, the character contained. That reticence is part of Holbein's genius, but it makes the picture cooler than the great Steelyard portrait of Georg Gisze, where the objects around the sitter do much of the talking."},"nga:72":{"d":"A small portrait on panel, about 49 by 38.5 centimeters, of Sir Brian Tuke, an administrator in the service of Henry VIII. Holbein painted it in England, and the museum offers two possible dates: about 1527 to 1528, during his first stay, or about 1532 to 1534, early in his second. The double date shows that the evidence does not settle the matter. The small scale points to a private portrait rather than a state commission.","i":"Brian Tuke rose through the royal service and held posts that kept Henry VIII's government running, among them Master of the Posts, in charge of the royal couriers, and Treasurer of the King's Chamber. Holbein portrayed many such officials, men whose careers depended on competence and the king's trust. Such portraits recorded a sitter's standing and gave family and friends a lasting likeness, and they show how Tudor administrators wished to appear: sober, capable and loyal.","c":"Holbein's English portraits succeed by refusing to flatter: a precise drawing of the head, restrained color and close attention to age and character. A panel of this size concentrates all of that on the face of a senior civil servant rather than a great noble, which is more convincing than ornament would have been. The uncertainty over its date is a reminder that even major Holbeins are not fully documented."},"met:436660":{"d":"An unidentified man in royal livery, painted in oil and gold on parchment that has been laid down on a linden panel, and dated 1532 to 1535. He is known only by his dress. Livery was the uniform that a lord or the Crown gave to its servants, marked with badges and colors, so the sitter was presumably a member of Henry VIII's household. Parchment is a surface associated with miniatures and manuscripts, which suggests a small, close-up object rather than a wall picture.","i":"Livery made allegiance visible, and a servant of the king, such as a guardsman or household officer, would have worn it with pride. A portrait that defines its subject by his uniform rather than his name suggests either that his identity has been lost or that service itself was the point. Holbein, who was to become the king's painter in 1536, depicted nobles and humbler household members alike, and this picture is a reminder of that range.","c":"Holbein gives a servant in uniform the same exact attention he gave courtiers, and the picture shows how little he relied on rank for interest. Without the sitter's name it lacks the historical weight of his portraits of Cromwell or More, but it has the same direct, unflattering observation, and the gold in the medium marks it as a work of some luxury."},"met:436659":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Derick Berck of Cologne, dated 1536. The panel, about 53 by 42.5 centimeters, has at some time been transferred to canvas, a treatment usually carried out to save a failing wood support and one that can alter a surface. Berck is usually identified as one of the German merchants who traded in London through the Hanseatic Steelyard, a community that Holbein served with a series of portraits in the 1530s.","i":"The Steelyard was the London base of the Hanseatic League, the north German trading association, and its merchants were prosperous foreigners who could afford Holbein's portraits. They appear as men with the self-possession of traders working in a foreign capital. A portrait like this declared a merchant's standing in a business that rested on reputation, and it could be sent home to family or kept as a memorial of years abroad.","c":"The strength of Holbein's German merchant portraits is the clarity of the head and the unfussy truth of the sitter's presence. Transfer from panel to canvas can flatten the finest touches, so the surface may not show all of the original delicacy. Even so, as a record of a living face in 1536 it is exact, businesslike and convincing, and it shows how Holbein could make a plain commercial sitter as interesting as a courtier."},"met:436661":{"d":"A portrait miniature of William Roper, who lived from 1493 or 1494 to 1578, painted by Holbein in 1535 or 1536 on vellum laid on card. The format is intimate, made to be held in the hand rather than hung on a wall. Roper was a lawyer who married Margaret More, daughter of Sir Thomas More, and the Met also owns Holbein's miniature of her, dated to the same years.","i":"Roper married More's eldest daughter Margaret in 1521, lived in More's household, and later wrote one of the earliest lives of his father-in-law, a source for much that is known of More's character and last years. This miniature falls in the months around More's execution in July 1535. Miniatures were private tokens, kept among family and friends and valued as likenesses.","c":"Like Holbein's other miniatures, this one works by exactness of drawing: at such a scale there is no room for anything beyond the head and a few essential details, so the likeness must carry the picture. His small portraits have the clarity of his chalk drawings. Its chief interest lies in the sitter's tie to More, but the craft is first-rate."},"met:436662":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Margaret Roper, born Margaret More, who lived from 1505 to 1544, painted by Holbein in 1535 or 1536 on vellum laid on a playing card, a common backing for miniatures of the period. The format is intimate, meant to be held in the hand. The Met also owns Holbein's miniature of her husband, William Roper, dated to the same years.","i":"Margaret was Thomas More's eldest daughter and the most learned of his children, schooled in Latin and Greek under his direction and admired by humanists such as Erasmus, whose work she translated into English. The date places the miniature just after More's execution in 1535, and a likeness of the daughter closest to him would have mattered to the family. Women of her learning were rare, which gives the portrait unusual historical interest.","c":"A miniature of a woman of learning lets Holbein do what he often does in small portraits: reduce the sitter to head, headdress and a few lines of dress, and make the face do the work. The painting's value lies partly in its subject, a member of More's family, and partly in the economy of the likeness. As art it is accomplished and discreet rather than dazzling, a fine example of Holbein's small-scale precision."},"cma:145960":{"d":"A small, square panel, about 22 centimeters on each side, painted in oil on wood about 1532; tree-ring analysis of the wood helped fix the date. It shows Terminus, the Roman god of boundaries, merged with the features of Erasmus of Rotterdam (about 1466 to 1536), the celebrated Dutch humanist. Erasmus adopted Terminus as a personal emblem together with the motto concedo nulli, meaning that he yields to no one, and Holbein turns that emblem into a picture.","i":"In Roman legend Terminus refused to give up his place on the Capitoline Hill even when Jupiter claimed it, and so became the god of firm boundaries. Erasmus took him as a symbol of devotion and steadfastness, a fitting emblem for a scholar trying to hold his ground in an age of religious conflict. The device shows how Renaissance humanists borrowed pagan myth for moral and personal ends, and Holbein's fusion of face and god brings emblem and portrait together.","c":"The picture is a witty piece of humanist play rather than a conventional portrait, and Holbein, who knew Erasmus and portrayed him more than once, handles the conceit with economy. The small size gives it the character of an emblem, to be read as much as looked at. It sits oddly in his catalogue, perhaps nearer to the design work of his Basel years than to his English portraits, yet it displays his skill at fusing likeness and symbol."},"nga:71":{"d":"A half-length portrait of the infant Prince Edward, son of Henry VIII, painted in oil on panel about 57 by 44 centimeters and dated probably 1538. The child, born in October 1537, holds a golden rattle, a nursery object turned into something like a scepter, so the baby appears as a miniature ruler rather than a plaything of the nursery. A Latin verse beneath addresses the child and urges him to emulate his father.","i":"Edward's birth in 1537 gave the Tudor dynasty the male heir Henry had long sought. Holbein's portrait is a statement of that hope: the rattle stands in for a scepter and the inscription sets a challenge to equal his father. Holbein is recorded giving Henry VIII a portrait of the prince as a New Year's gift in 1539, and this picture is usually identified with it. Such an image asked the court and the king to see an heir already in command.","c":"Holbein faces a hard problem, how to make a baby look royal, and solves it with formal discipline: a stiff, ceremonial pose, symbols of rule and an inscription that shifts the picture from nursery to monarchy. The face, as in any infant portrait, is hardly individual, and the stiffness is deliberate. It is less a likeness than a dynastic announcement, and as that it is both elegant and faintly comic."},"met:436663":{"d":"A portrait miniature on vellum laid on card, dated about 1535, of Thomas Wriothesley, who lived from 1505 to 1550 and later became the First Earl of Southampton. When it was made he would have been about thirty and already an official in royal service. The small size makes it a private object, a likeness to be kept and shown to chosen viewers, in a format that Holbein practiced only occasionally during his years in England.","i":"Wriothesley began his career under Thomas Cromwell and rose to be one of Henry VIII's principal secretaries, then Lord Chancellor in 1544, and was created Earl of Southampton in 1547. He was a major figure in the political and religious conflicts of the king's last years and one of the executors of Henry's will. A likeness from about 1535 shows him before the peak of that career, when he was still making his way.","c":"As a miniature it works by precise drawing and a controlled, quiet surface, qualities that make Holbein's small portraits as convincing as his large ones. Knowing Wriothesley's later reputation, many viewers look for hardness in the face, but the picture records a man of about thirty and tells no such story. Its virtue is its exactness, and its interest is the contrast between this early likeness and the powerful figure he became."},"met:435752":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 142 by 103 centimeters, in which a young woman in the costume of the Ciociaria, a region southeast of Rome, looks at her child in an interior. The subject of mother and infant is treated with Bouguereau's usual finish: smooth, blended flesh, careful modeling and an idealized sweetness. The picture, dated 1871, belongs to a long series of Italian and Breton peasant figures made for private collectors, many of them American.","i":"Bouguereau studied in Rome in the early 1850s, and Italian peasant models in regional dress, especially from the Ciociaria, became staples of French Salon painting. A mother and child recalls the Madonna and Child, the dominant image of motherhood in European art, but transfers it to a contemporary country household, so that the sentiment of the sacred image is available without its theology. The pictures appealed to buyers who wanted a calm, idealized view of family life.","c":"Bouguereau's technique is faultless in its own terms: the skin is smooth as porcelain and the modeling exact. The result is also airless and sentimental, with the peasant's hardships polished out of existence, which is why critics of his own day and since have treated him as the Academy's most polished and least challenging painter. The competence is undeniable; the feeling is rehearsed rather than observed."},"met:435754":{"d":"A canvas of about 129 by 89 centimeters showing two young children, a boy and a girl, whom the title identifies as Breton brother and sister. Bouguereau painted it in 1871, when peasant children in regional costume were among his steadiest subjects. The treatment is typical: a smooth, finished surface, carefully modeled faces and an atmosphere of calm. The children are presented as individuals but probably also as types of a picturesque province.","i":"Brittany, in the far northwest of France, had a distinct language and dress that attracted painters from the middle of the century, and its peasants stood for an older and supposedly more devout France. Bouguereau, who spent his career within the Academy and was a favorite of Salon buyers, supplied an idealized version of that world. Pictures of children sold well to families who wanted tender subjects, and this one belongs to a large group of similar works.","c":"Bouguereau's strengths, an exquisitely smooth surface and secure drawing, are all present, and the careful modeling of the heads makes the children convincing as solid forms. Against that stands the sweetness: nothing suggests that Breton children lived in hardship, and the idealization drains the picture of the force a more observed image might have had. Admirers of technique will find much to enjoy; admirers of truth, less."},"met:435753":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 164 by 112 centimeters, dated 1872, showing a woman and a man with a cat and a spinning wheel among the details. The title and the props point to a rustic courtship in which a young man declares himself to a woman beside her spinning wheel. Bouguereau paints the figures with his usual smooth surface and idealized, healthy faces, and the picture is large enough to dominate a room.","i":"The spinning wheel traditionally stands for a woman's domestic industry and virtue, marking her as a suitable bride; the cat, a household animal, adds some ambiguity, since in older art it could signify domestic comfort or, less kindly, a lover's wiles. Courtship scenes in a humble country setting offered urban buyers a sentimental, safe vision of rural life, and the title leaves viewers to imagine whether the answer will be yes.","c":"Bouguereau's courtship scenes are among his most readable pictures, with every prop doing a clear job, and the Met's canvas is a large, assured example. The accomplished modeling cannot hide the staged quality: the pair are models in peasant dress rather than people observed, and the emotion is signaled rather than felt. As a document of Salon taste in the 1870s it tells more than it does as a record of country life."},"met:435630":{"d":"A small oil portrait, about 30 by 25 centimeters, of Edward Miles (1752 to 1828). The picture is undated, and the museum's keywords list writing among its subjects, so the sitter probably appears with a pen or paper. The scale is that of a sketch or private likeness rather than a formal commission. Beechey (1753 to 1839) was a year younger than his sitter, and the two belonged to the same generation of London artists.","i":"Edward Miles was himself a painter, a miniaturist who exhibited at the Royal Academy and later worked in Russia and the United States. A portrait of a fellow artist differs from a commission, since it records a colleague or friend and is not governed by the demands of a client. Beechey, who became portrait painter to Queen Charlotte in 1793 and was knighted in 1798, spent most of his career on society sitters.","c":"A small portrait of a fellow artist is a different task from a society commission, and sketch-sized pictures by Beechey can be more direct than his formal ones. Without a date or an obvious setting there is little to place it in his career, and it has none of the ceremony of his royal portraits. It is minor, pleasant and unforced, interesting mainly for its sitter."},"met:435632":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified woman on a canvas of about 127 by 102 centimeters, the standard English three-quarter size of fifty by forty inches, painted around 1805. The museum's keywords list a parasol, a shawl and a headband, accessories typical of fashionable dress in the early years of the century. It is the kind of picture painted for a private house, to hang in a family's reception rooms and record how the sitter wished to be seen.","i":"Beechey, portrait painter to Queen Charlotte from 1793 and a knight from 1798, supplied the English gentry with portraits in the tradition of Reynolds. A parasol and shawl are fashion accessories, signs of leisure and taste, and a headband followed the new, simpler styles of the years after the French Revolution. The sitter's name has been lost, so what remains is the type: a woman of means, shown as she wished to be seen.","c":"Beechey's portraits of women are competent, agreeable and rarely more. This one belongs to the English tradition of the smooth, dignified likeness, with the fashion carefully recorded, but it lacks Lawrence's dash or Reynolds's intelligence, and the lost identity leaves the viewer with only the dress. Its interest is as a representative piece of English portraiture at the turn of the century, sound rather than daring."},"nga:46092":{"d":"A military portrait of a British general on a canvas of about 77 by 64 centimetres, a size suited to a bust-length or half-length likeness. The title gives the sitter's rank and knighthood, Lieutenant-General Sir Thomas Picton, and the date of 1815 to 1817 places the work around or after his death at Waterloo. Beechey, a leading portraitist of the British establishment, painted official figures in a warm, straightforward style, and a picture of this kind concentrates on the sitter's face and uniform.","i":"Picton, a Welshman from Pembrokeshire, rose to fame in the Peninsular War and was killed at Waterloo on 18 June 1815 while commanding a division. His earlier governorship of Trinidad had led to a controversial trial, but in death he was celebrated as a national hero, and memorials to him were raised in Britain. If the portrait was painted after his death, as the date suggests, it was made from earlier likenesses to satisfy that public interest.","c":"Beechey was a dependable portraitist with a gift for natural, unaffected likenesses, but he lacked the dash of Lawrence, the leading painter of the heroes of the Napoleonic wars. A picture of this kind succeeds by conveying firmness and honesty, and a plain presentation suits a soldier who died in action. The result is a sound record rather than an exciting portrait, and if it is posthumous it is bound to be a little static."},"met:435631":{"d":"A portrait of the future George IV, painted while he was still Prince of Wales, which places it before he came to the throne in 1820. The canvas, about 143 by 113 centimeters, matches the traditional British format called a bishop's half-length, 56 by 44 inches, a size used for formal likenesses of people of rank. The museum gives no date, and its keywords describe only a prince in a portrait, so the picture is best understood as a representative image of a royal heir by a painter of the royal household.","i":"George, born in 1762, was heir to George III for fifty-eight years, became Regent in 1811 and king in 1820. As Prince of Wales he was a celebrated patron and collector, a leader of fashion and a figure of political and moral controversy. Portraits of the heir were often made in several versions for family, ministers and regiments, and they presented him as a dignified, cultivated successor. Beechey, portrait painter to Queen Charlotte from 1793, was one of the painters who supplied them.","c":"Beechey was a sound portraitist whose royal pictures are dignified and conscientious, but he never became the Prince's favorite: that role went to Thomas Lawrence, whose dash and flattery suited an image-conscious heir better. Beside Lawrence's portraits of the same sitter, a Beechey is steady rather than sparkling. Without a date or documentary background, this one stands as an honest example of the type: competent, a little dry, and interesting chiefly for its subject."},"met:436079":{"d":"A family group on a wide canvas, about 102 by 159 centimetres, shows Uvedale Tomkyns Price with a woman and other members of his family, accompanied by a dog and a swan. It is a conversation piece, the English type of group portrait in which relations and friends are shown talking, playing or posing at ease in a house or garden, rather than in stiff ceremonial poses. The date is given as possibly the early 1730s.","i":"Conversation pieces became fashionable in Britain in the 1720s and 1730s among landed gentry and prosperous townspeople who wanted portraits that showed families as agreeable, affectionate and well provided for. The dog and the swan add liveliness and suggest the pleasures of a country household. Price, who lived from 1685 to 1764, is named as the head of the family, so the picture is above all a record of his household's standing and harmony.","c":"Dandridge was a capable, not an inspired, painter, and beside Hogarth, whose conversation pieces of the same years are livelier, his groups can seem stiff, with figures placed side by side rather than bound together by action. The charm of a picture like this lies in the particular details of faces, clothes and animals, and in the sense of a real family. It is a good document of its class and moment, and a modest work of art."},"met:436834":{"d":"A tiny panel, only about 18 by 14 centimeters, painted on both sides: a portrait of a young man on the front and, on the reverse, a girl making a garland, with a cat among the subjects the museum lists. It is oil on poplar, dated about 1508, by Hans Süss von Kulmbach, who trained and worked in Nuremberg in the circle of Dürer. The small size and the painted back suggest an object made to be handled and turned over rather than hung on a wall.","i":"Double-sided portraits often paired a likeness with an image that commented on the sitter's life, and a girl making a garland suggests courtship, since garlands and wreaths were common love tokens in German Renaissance art. The cat, a creature that could stand for home comforts or for desire, adds an ambiguity. The panel may have been made for a betrothal or an admirer, but the sitter is unnamed and the circumstances of the commission are unclear.","c":"Kulmbach was among Dürer's most gifted followers, gentler and more decorative than his master, and a panel this size suits his strengths: clear contour, fresh color and quiet charm. The painted reverse is the surprise, a small figure subject of a kind that was rare in German art at this date. The portrait side has less of Dürer's probing, but as an inventive little object the double-sided panel is memorable."},"met:436835":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 62 by 38 centimeters, painted in oil on fir in 1513. It shows the Ascension, when Christ rose to heaven forty days after Easter, watched by Mary and the apostles, the figures the museum lists. The upright format, which emphasizes the rise from earth toward heaven, suggests that it may have belonged to a larger altarpiece or a set of devotional scenes. Hans Süss von Kulmbach probably painted it in Nuremberg, where he had worked with Dürer.","i":"The Acts of the Apostles says that Christ was lifted up before the eyes of his disciples and hidden by a cloud, while two angels asked why they stood gazing at the sky. Northern European painters usually gathered the apostles and the Virgin on the Mount of Olives with their faces turned upward, so that the viewer follows their gaze toward heaven. The Ascension completed Christ's earthly mission and stood for the believer's own hope of rising.","c":"A tall, narrow panel is a good shape for an ascent, and Kulmbach, formed in Dürer's workshop, brings to the scene Nuremberg's clarity of outline and fresh color. He is less intense than Dürer, and his figures are more graceful than moved. It is a pleasing, well-made image of a subject that tests a painter's invention, rather than a daring one."},"nga:195800":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 102 by 178 centimeters, painted in 1867, showing emigrants halting for the evening on their way west, with a covered wagon among the subjects the museum lists. Although the title names the year 1840, the picture was made more than a quarter of a century later, so it is a retrospective view of the overland migration rather than an eyewitness record. The horizontal format is that of a landscape, in which the travelers are set within a wide expanse of country.","i":"By 1867, with the Civil War over and the transcontinental railroad under construction, the covered-wagon migration was already becoming a national legend. Paintings of emigrants at rest at day's end gave viewers a picture of pioneer courage and domestic order, with the wagon as a home on wheels. Such scenes shaped the popular memory of westward expansion, and they seldom dwell on its costs, above all for the native peoples whose lands lay along the route.","c":"Reinhart is a less celebrated painter than Albert Bierstadt, whose Emigrants Crossing the Plains dates from the same year, and he probably offers a more modest, anecdotal view. The appeal of such a picture is narrative and nostalgic rather than scenic: it asks to be read for what the travelers are doing. As a record of how Americans of the 1860s imagined their pioneers it is useful, though it does not aim at the grandeur of the leading western landscapists."},"met:436878":{"d":"A miniature copy on vellum, not an oil painting, made in 1721 by the English miniaturist Bernard Lens after Rubens's portrait of his wife Helena Fourment and their young son Frans. The record gives no dimensions, but vellum copies of this kind are small enough to be held in the hand. The Met's keywords for the subject list a woman, a boy, flowers and a parrot, so the miniature reproduces a rich domestic group in a reduced and portable form.","i":"Bernard Lens, who taught drawing to members of the royal family, was one of the busiest copyists of his day, and in early eighteenth-century England such watercolour copies gave collectors the look of famous paintings that they could never afford to own. Helena Fourment, who married Rubens in 1630 when she was sixteen, appears in many of his pictures. The copy is a record of how Rubens was admired and spread by reproduction.","c":"As a copy, the miniature must be judged as a translation. Lens turns Rubens's broad, fleshy brushwork into fine, controlled touches, and the miniature gains clarity and loses force. Such watercolour copies were prized for their delicacy and portability, and Lens was skilled at them, but nothing of Rubens's vitality can survive the reduction. It is of interest chiefly as an early record of how the original was valued and known."},"nga:1212":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, sitting or leaning with her head turned to the right. Her light brown or perhaps gray hair falls in loose waves around her face, and she has red lips and dark eyebrows. She wears a yellow dress with a deep, lace-trimmed neckline and a brown belt, holds a small bunch of red-petaled flowers close to her neck, and has a red ribbon at her wrist. The background is dark and abstract with hints of foliage. The canvas is about 76 by 64 cm.","i":"The title, The Hon. Mrs. Gray, uses the courtesy title of a peer's daughter or of the wife of a peer's younger son. Daniel Gardner, a portraitist from Kendal who trained with Romney and worked in London, made a specialty of intimate, sentimental likenesses of women in the 1780s. Flowers were a standard emblem of femininity and charm, and the red ribbon and yellow dress are accents of fashion, suited to the age's taste for ease and sensibility.","c":"Gardner's delicate manner resembles Romney's but with a lighter touch and a taste for translucent color, and the red of the flowers and ribbon sings against the yellow dress. The strengths are lightness and an engaging informality; the weaknesses are a certain generalization of the face and a pretty formula. He lacks the audacity of Gainsborough or Reynolds, but he is a charming specialist in intimate sentiment, and this is an agreeable example of his best vein."},"met:437593":{"d":"A very large canvas, almost two and a half meters tall, joins a portrait to a myth. The singer Marcantonio Pasqualini, named in the title, is crowned by Apollo, god of music. The recorded motifs add a harpsichord and a satyr identified as Marsyas, the mythical musician who challenged Apollo, so that the sitter appears among the instruments and figures of a contest between art and presumption. Andrea Sacchi painted it in 1641.","i":"Apollo crowning a musician is a classical emblem of victory in art, and Pasqualini (1614-1691) was a celebrated singer in Rome, associated with the circle of the Barberini family, who were also Sacchi's patrons. Marsyas, who challenged Apollo to a musical contest and was flayed for his presumption, may allude to rivals the singer had outshone. The picture honors a living performer in the language of myth, equating his voice with the god's gift.","c":"Sacchi championed restraint in an age of Baroque exuberance, arguing in 1636 for compositions of few figures against the crowded designs of Pietro da Cortona, and this canvas applies that discipline to an allegorical portrait. A living sitter beside a god and a satyr risks incongruity, but the clear, calm design keeps the whole dignified. The scale is grand, the drama muted, and the approach is that of a classicist more than a showman."},"met:437638":{"d":"Nude and partly nude figures gather for the wedding of Cupid, the winged god of love, and Psyche, the mortal princess he loved, on a wide panel of about 131 by 157 centimeters painted around 1540. The recorded motifs include nude women and men, and the scene belongs to the festive, decorative mythologies that Venetian painters produced for private rooms. The wooden panel has since been transferred to a Masonite support in a modern conservation.","i":"Apuleius told the story in his second-century novel The Golden Ass: Psyche, loved by Cupid, must pass impossible trials set by his mother Venus before she is made immortal and the pair are wed at a banquet of the gods. Renaissance readers took it as an allegory of the soul (Psyche means soul) united with love. The tale was popular for marriage furnishings and palace decoration in sixteenth-century Italy, so a panel like this may have belonged to a bridal chamber.","c":"Schiavone's loose, sketchy handling and his elongated, elegant figures, learned from Parmigianino's prints and Venetian color, make mythologies like this feel improvised and lively, and his touch anticipates Tintoretto's. The shorthand can look careless, and the transfer to Masonite has probably dulled the surface. Among Venetian mid-century mythologies it is quirky and fresh, if not among the strongest."},"nga:46139":{"d":"Seven mourners crowd around the dead Christ, whose body lies across the lap of the Virgin on a grassy slope. Saint John supports him under the arms and looks out at the viewer; a woman with long blond hair, probably Mary Magdalene, kneels to cradle one hand; another woman lifts a foot; two men hold the pincers, nails and hammer. Nearly every face is red-eyed and tearful. Behind, a steep hill carries the crosses of Calvary, and a river, an arched bridge and hazy blue mountains recede to a high horizon. Oil on panel, about 169 by 152 centimeters, dated about 1505 to 1507.","i":"The Lamentation mourns Christ's body after it was taken from the cross. The pincers, nails and hammer name the work of the deposition, and the men holding them are probably Nicodemus and Joseph of Arimathea. In the background, the empty central cross and the two thieves still hanging show that the Crucifixion has only just ended. Solario worked in Milan, where Leonardo's soft modeling set the standard, and his tearful faces and detailed landscape probably owe something to Netherlandish painting.","c":"Solario absorbed Leonardo's soft modeling and added Netherlandish detail, and this panel shows both: pale, tender flesh, reddened eyes on nearly every face, and a deep landscape of river, bridge and distant mountains. The tight grouping risks looking arranged rather than moved, and the colors, pink, green, amber and lilac, are sweeter than the subject strictly needs. Still, the open grief and the clarity of the design give it real feeling."},"met:437699":{"d":"Salome presents the severed head of Saint John the Baptist in this compact oil on wood, about 57 by 47 centimeters, painted around 1507-9. The recorded motifs are the woman, the head and the Baptist. Solario, a Milanese painter, favored half-length figures close to the picture plane, and the format brings the viewer face to face with the gruesome trophy and the young woman who carries it.","i":"In the Gospels of Matthew and Mark the daughter of Herodias, named Salome only by later tradition, dances for King Herod and, at her mother's prompting, asks for the head of John the Baptist, who had condemned the marriage. Leonardo's followers in Milan made the episode a quiet, contemplative image, in which the beauty and calm of the young woman heighten the horror of the head. Such pictures joined martyrdom to a moral lesson about vanity and vice.","c":"Solario translated Leonardo's soft modeling and subtle expression into a cooler, more finished surface, and the contrast between a serene face and the macabre head is the picture's pleasure. The weakness is a certain smoothness and lack of inner drama: he is a gifted follower, not an equal, and the horror is suggested rather than felt. As one of the Milanese Salomes, it is polished and effective."},"met:437698":{"d":"Christ appears as the Salvator Mundi, Savior of the World, raising his hand in blessing, on a tall panel nearly two meters high and 131 centimeters wide, painted around 1524. In pictures of this type he faces the viewer and usually holds a globe topped with a cross in the other hand. The large scale suggests an image for a chapel or church, not a small devotional picture.","i":"The Salvator Mundi shows Christ as ruler of the world, blessing the believer with one hand and holding the orb of sovereignty, topped by a cross, in the other. The type was popular in northern Italy and the Netherlands around 1500, including in Leonardo's circle, where Solario worked. The painter died in 1524, so this may be among his last works. Its stately, frontal format suits a picture meant to be seen from some distance.","c":"A large, frontal Christ asks for a presence that Solario's smooth, Leonardesque manner can only partly supply: soft modeling and a calm face give gravity, but the stillness of the type can slide into stiffness. The picture shows an artist at the end of his career holding to the formulas of his master's generation. It is dignified rather than moving."},"nga:167923":{"d":"A square panel, about 47 by 49 centimeters, in tempera on a gold ground, with three men hanging on crosses above a crowd. The haloed Christ on the middle cross wears a crown of thorns and bleeds from hands, feet and side; INRI is lettered in gold above his head, and four small angels painted entirely in dark blue fly in pairs beside him. On one side a gold angel lifts the soul of a thief from his mouth, while on the other two black winged creatures hover. At lower left four women comfort the fallen Virgin; at lower right three men draw straws for a blue tunic.","i":"All four Gospels record that two thieves were crucified with Christ, and Luke says one repented and was promised paradise. Medieval painters showed their fates directly: an angel receives the soul of the repentant thief while devils claim the other. The soldiers casting lots for Christ's garment fulfill a verse of Psalm 22 cited in John's Gospel. Panels like this were meant to be read at close range, scene by scene, as a prompt for prayer.","c":"Vanni's panel is a lesson in Sienese narrative economy: a crowded scene, readable at once, with the rhythm of the crosses set against flat gold. The color is bright and carefully chosen, and the small angels and devils show lively invention. Naturalism is not the aim: scale is symbolic, space shallow, and the greenish flesh a familiar effect of the traditional green underpaint. It is a conservative, devout work in the tradition of Simone Martini, delighting in careful detail."},"nga:198423":{"d":"A tall panel ending in a pointed arch, about 47 by 24 centimeters, on a gold ground, in which Jesus appears twice. Above, on a rocky ledge, he kneels with arms folded and looks up at a small angel who holds out a golden chalice. Below, he stands in profile and clasps the hand of Saint Peter, who kneels before him, while one disciple sleeps sitting up and another lies stretched on his stomach. A rose hedge and small flowering trees mark the garden. In the distance, soldiers with lances and shields come from a castle behind a man with a dark halo.","i":"On the night before his death Jesus prayed in the garden of Gethsemane that the cup of suffering might pass from him, and Luke says an angel strengthened him. The disciples he had asked to keep watch fell asleep. The chalice in the angel's hand turns his prayer into an object, and the dark halo probably marks Judas, who leads the soldiers to arrest him. Painters often showed two moments together in one panel, so that the story could be read at a glance.","c":"The panel packs a sequence of events into a narrow space, and Vanni manages the shifts of scale and scene with a clarity that suits a portable devotional object. The sleeping apostles, the cheerful rose hedge and the thin line of soldiers on the ridge are small touches of observation within an unrealistic gold world. The figures are stylized and the garden is more emblem than place, but the story reads easily."},"nga:198424":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 47 by 25 centimeters, in a gold frame with bands of lapis blue. Christ strides from under an archway across a wooden door lying flat on the ground, which pins and crushes a black beast. He holds a tall staff with a white banner marked with a red cross and clasps the hand of an old man with a long white beard. Behind the old man a crowd of men and women kneels at the mouth of a craggy cave. Two figures hold scrolls with Latin inscriptions, one proclaiming the Lamb of God and the other the defeat of the devil.","i":"The Descent into Limbo, or Harrowing of Hell, shows Christ between his death and Resurrection going down to free the righteous who died before him, led by the old man, probably Adam. It comes not from the canonical Gospels but from the apocryphal Gospel of Nicodemus. The door crushing the devil is the broken gate of hell, and the banner is the sign of victory. In Byzantine art the scene was the usual image of the Resurrection.","c":"The gold relief arch above the scene, the flat gold ground and the bright banner make a compact, brilliantly decorative image, and the door flattening the demon is a lively, almost comic stroke. The kneeling crowd is packed in with little individuality, and the space is a stage set rather than a place, but the story is told with confidence. As the right wing of a triptych, it balances the Agony at the other side."},"nga:206072":{"d":"A small folding triptych in tempera and gold, about 57 by 116 centimeters when open, with three hinged panels in gold frames inset with lapis blue. The square center shows the Crucifixion, flanked by two tall panels: Jesus at prayer in the garden on the left, and Christ freeing the souls of the righteous from Limbo on the right. Latin scrolls appear in the right panel, and the central blue band carries a Latin signature in which the painter names himself as Andrea Vanni of Siena. It dates from the 1380s.","i":"Triptychs of this kind were probably made for private prayer, opened on a table or small altar so that the owner could meditate on the Passion in sequence: the night of prayer, the Crucifixion, and the victory over death in the Descent into Limbo. The order runs from suffering to triumph. The signature shows a Sienese painter's pride in his workmanship, a point worth noting because signatures were still comparatively rare in the fourteenth century.","c":"Seen whole, the triptych has a satisfying logic: a crowded, emotional center framed by a quiet garden at the left and a triumphant rescue at the right. Vanni's style is the late Sienese tradition, bright, linear and rich in goldwork, indebted to Simone Martini and the Lorenzetti and little touched by the new naturalism of Florence. It lacks their depth, but for a devout owner the clarity and luxury of the object were the point."},"met:437465":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified man, painted in oil on canvas, about 83 by 65 centimeters, and dated 1693, when Rigaud was in his early thirties and establishing himself in Paris. No name, rank or profession has come down with the picture. In portraits of this period Rigaud often keeps the setting simple, so that attention stays on the sitter's face and the careful details of dress.","i":"Portraits of this kind recorded a man's status through the details of dress and bearing. By 1693 Rigaud was gaining a reputation among Parisian professionals and the nobility, a clientele that later brought him royal commissions, including his 1701 portrait of Louis XIV. A picture of an unnamed sitter is a reminder of how many such likenesses survive without the identities that once gave them meaning.","c":"Rigaud's gift was the ease with which he joined an exact likeness to an air of consequence, and the best of his portraits of the 1690s already show it. With no named sitter, this picture is valued mainly as a sample of his early manner, balancing individual character against the formal poise that clients wanted. It is a competent, confident portrait from before he found his full grandeur."},"met:437463":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified general officer, painted in oil on canvas about 1710 and measuring about 137 by 105 centimeters. Rigaud's portraits of French commanders typically show the signs of command, such as armor or a rich coat, a baton, and sometimes a hint of a battlefield or tent, around a head painted with sharp individuality. The museum's keywords add only that the sitter is a man in military dress, so the exact rank and campaign are unknown.","i":"The War of the Spanish Succession, which ran from 1701 to 1714, multiplied the number of French officers who wanted portraits as marks of rank and service. A portrait of a general under Louis XIV combined the individuality of a likeness with the conventions of the baroque state portrait, in which pose, drapery and a few military props declared authority. Rigaud, the court's leading portraitist, supplied the formula for many of them.","c":"Rigaud's military portraits are a formula carried out with exceptional crispness: heads that are individual and alert, with armor, sash and drapery rendered in sumptuous finish. The risk of the formula is stiffness and repetition, and with an unnamed sitter the picture has little to say beyond its type. At its best the type gives a sense of confident power; here it is competent, grand and rather impersonal."},"cma:143206":{"d":"A portrait of Cardinal Guillaume Dubois, painted in oil on canvas in 1723 and measuring about 147 by 114 centimeters. The cardinal is shown in luxurious, flamboyant dress: a voluminous wrap of scarlet silk topped by a short fur cape. He holds a letter inscribed Au Roy, to the king. The museum notes that the clothing alone shows a man of consequence, and the picture was painted in the same year the young Louis XV began to rule in his own right.","i":"Dubois won the favor of Louis XIV as tutor to the king's nephew, the future regent, the Duke of Orléans, and later served as chief minister under the regent and Louis XV. He became a cardinal in 1721, an ambition the museum ties partly to politics, since the title gave him the power to remove adversaries with impunity. The letter addressed to the king presents him as the crown's loyal servant at the moment the young king began to rule.","c":"Rigaud's specialty, the display of rank in silk and fur, is at full stretch here, and the letter marked Au Roy turns a flamboyant display into a statement of loyalty. The result is theatrical but convincing, a showpiece of the late portrait manner by the painter who defined it, and a record of a minister who died that same year."},"met:435635":{"d":"A small upright panel, about 56 by 38 centimetres, painted in oil on spruce in 1527. It is a portrait of a single man, Leonhard von Eck, chancellor of Bavaria, then in his late forties. As in most German portraits of the period, the image concentrates on the face and the sitter's standing, in a size suited to a private room, and it was painted by a young artist who had entered the service of the Bavarian dukes at Munich about this time.","i":"Leonhard von Eck was the dominant minister of the Bavarian dukes for decades, a lawyer and diplomat who led the Wittelsbach policy of resisting the Reformation. A portrait of such a man presents authority more than personality, and it is usually read as a statement of office. Barthel Beham, a Nuremberg artist expelled from his native city in 1525 for radical religious opinions, had found a place at the Catholic court of Munich, and his portraits for it made his reputation.","c":"Beham trained as a printmaker, and his portraits keep a printmaker's firm outline and exact observation of the face. That gives them directness, and sometimes a certain dryness. Beside Holbein or Cranach he is less inventive, but within a modest scale he can convey a sitter's presence, and this portrait is a worthy record of a powerful official at a turning point in German history."},"met:712539":{"d":"A half-length portrait of Saint Philip Neri, painted in oil on canvas about 45 by 36 centimeters in 1645 or 1646. The museum's keywords record a man, a portrait, a saint and Philip Neri, so his pose, vestments, expression and the background are left unstated. Dolci, the Florentine painter, was known for refined devotional pictures with a smooth, enamel-like finish, and this small canvas portrays a saint who had died only about fifty years earlier.","i":"Philip Neri (1515 to 1595), the Florentine-born apostle of Rome, founded the Congregation of the Oratory, a community of priests that held informal prayer, music and discussion for laypeople, and he was known for his humor and humility. He was canonized in 1622, and portraits of him were made to encourage devotion. Because he had been dead only half a century, his image could be drawn from earlier likenesses, so a picture like this is part portrait and part holy image.","c":"Dolci's reputation has swung from the heights of nineteenth-century admiration to the depths of twentieth-century scorn as too sweet, and it is now being reassessed. A saint's portrait in a small format suits his talents: the careful finish, the soft emotion and the devotional focus. As a work of the 1640s, when Dolci was a young master, it is probably more direct and less cloying than his later saints."},"cma:143784":{"d":"The Adoration of the Shepherds, the visit of the shepherds to the newborn Christ, is painted in oil on canvas, about 89 by 71 centimeters, about 1670. The museum's note concerns the painter's style and his strict use of traditional Christian iconography, and does not describe the picture itself, so the number of shepherds, the arrangement of the Holy Family and the colors are left unstated. Dolci was known for a smooth, polished surface, delicate emotion and a quiet, devout mood.","i":"The story comes from the Gospel of Luke: angels announce Christ's birth to shepherds keeping watch by night, and they hurry to the stable to find the child in a manger. The shepherds stand for humble witnesses chosen before kings and scholars. The Cleveland museum notes that Dolci, a devout follower of a brotherhood devoted to Saint Benedict, followed strict iconography, and a Nativity of this kind would have served private devotion or a patron who wanted a clear, sober image.","c":"Dolci has long divided critics. Nineteenth-century admirers loved his purity; later scholars found him sugary and conventional, and the museum's own note says that his traditionalist compositions have diminished his historical significance. A Nativity from the 1670s, when he was in his fifties and already established, is likely polished, tender and unsurprising. Its virtue is serenity and craft; its limit is that it will not startle."},"met:437163":{"d":"A portrait of Lucrezia Agliardi Vertova, dated 1557, painted in oil on canvas about 91 by 69 centimeters. The title gives her dates as 1490, with a question mark, to 1558, so she was in her late sixties and close to the end of her life. The museum's keyword for the picture is abbess, which suggests that she held a religious office or is shown in the dress of one. Moroni, who worked in Bergamo, painted such portraits of local sitters with plain, direct observation.","i":"Moroni's patrons were largely the nobles and professionals of Bergamo, then part of the Venetian Republic, and they wanted likenesses that recorded rank, age and character without display. A portrait of an elderly woman from a local family served as a record for her relatives and descendants. A portrait painted about a year before her death, when she was around sixty-seven, was likely to be kept by the family as a lasting memorial.","c":"Moroni's strength was the plain truthfulness of his portraits, and that is the right approach for an elderly sitter, where idealizing would have been out of place. His training under Moretto da Brescia gave him a sober palette and an interest in character over display. The picture has little of the glamour of Venetian portraiture, but it carries the weight of a real, specific person, which is the quality for which he is most admired."},"met:437164":{"d":"A portrait of Bartolomeo Bonghi, who died in 1584, painted shortly after 1553 in oil on canvas, about 102 by 82 centimeters. Moroni, who worked chiefly in Bergamo, was known for sober, unidealized likenesses, and a canvas of this size would typically show the sitter at half-length or a little more. The museum's keywords record only a man in a portrait, so the picture is best taken as an example of Moroni's plain, dignified manner.","i":"Bonghi lived more than thirty years beyond the date of this portrait, so the picture shows him as a younger man. Moroni's sitters were largely the nobles and professional men of Bergamo, who wanted likenesses for family collections that recorded status without grandeur. Dignity, sobriety and capability were the qualities a sitter in the Venetian mainland territories wished to project, and Moroni excelled at conveying them.","c":"Moroni's gift was a patient realism: heads modeled honestly, color restrained, expression steady and direct. A portrait of this kind shows why later critics have valued him as one of the most truthful portraitists of the sixteenth century, if not the grandest. The limits of the formula are its quietness and its small range of poses, but the best of his sitters, and this is a representative one, seem to look back with real presence."},"nga:255":{"d":"A nearly square canvas, about 60 by 65 centimeters. At the lower right a man in a black jacket with crimson sleeves is shown from the chest up in profile, hands pressed together in prayer. Facing him, the Virgin, in a cherry-red dress with a cream mantle over her head and blue drapery at her waist, holds the Christ child, who leans on her shoulder, holds an apple in one hand and grasps her finger with the other. A gold line marks her halo. The setting is a plain room with pale walls. Moroni painted it about 1560.","i":"Combining a portrait with a devotional image let a patron place himself beside the Madonna, an old convention for the donor. Here the man is a worshiper in the same room as the sacred figures rather than kneeling in a separate space. The apple in the child's hand is usually read as the fruit of the Fall, which Christ will redeem. Such small pictures served private prayer in a household, and the donor is unnamed in the title.","c":"Moroni was a portraitist first, and the picture shows it: the donor is the vivid presence, with a careful trace of the patterned collar and cuff, while the Madonna and child follow a quiet, conventional type. The juxtaposition of an ordinary man in contemporary dress and a sacred pair in the same plain room is unusually direct. It is a modest, sincere picture, closer to portraiture than to altarpiece painting."},"nga:46051":{"d":"A full-length portrait, about 202 by 117 centimeters, of a standing man beside a small dog. He has dark hair and a russet beard and wears a knee-length taupe tunic under a floor-length gray coat with puffed sleeves and a broad black fur lining. His body angles to the viewer's right, but he turns his head to look straight out. One hand grips the fur with an extended forefinger; the other is held open at his waist. The dog wears a red collar with silver bells. A pale blue wall, marble molding and a red curtain complete the room.","i":"The long fur-lined coat, the dog and the full-length format mark the sitter as a nobleman. Gian Federico Madruzzo belonged to the Madruzzo family of Trent, which produced prince-bishops and cardinals and served the Habsburgs. Full-length portraits were still a mark of high status in Italy at mid-century, a format made famous by Titian's portraits of rulers. The small dog is a conventional sign of fidelity and household ease, and the open, relaxed gestures suggest assurance.","c":"Moroni's strength is the unforced presence of his sitters, and this full-length portrait shows it at scale: the figure stands naturally, without a heroic pose, and the careful records of fur, tunic and dog make the man seem a person in a room rather than a symbol. The dog and the small gestures add informality. It is less grand than Titian's state portraits, and more believable, which was Moroni's gift."},"nga:1184":{"d":"A man seen from the knees up leans back in a wooden chair, wearing a loose black garment with a white collar and a velvety black three-pointed cap. His body faces the viewer's left, but he turns his head and looks out from the corners of light brown eyes, one brow slightly cocked. His cheeks are hollow and lined, his silvery beard is trimmed close. Lit from the right, he rests one arm along the chair, with a gold ring on his little finger, and keeps a place in a closed book with the other hand. Oil on canvas, about 97 by 74 centimeters, about 1575.","i":"The title is a traditional nickname, not a documented identification, so the sitter's own name appears to be unknown. The closed book with a finger marking the page signals a man of learning or teaching, and the ring a certain standing. Moroni's portraits of professional men reflect the standing that educated citizens outside the nobility had won in sixteenth-century Italy. The sidelong glance and the unidealized face present a man at ease with his own age and intelligence.","c":"One of Moroni's most admired portraits, it succeeds because it refuses display. The dark garment and the dark ground reduce the picture to the face, the long arm and the book-holding hand, and the side lighting makes every wrinkle count, with a sidelong glance of dry intelligence. The restraint of this late work, the lack of ornament, the plain cap, is the mature Moroni at his best. Few Renaissance portraits make a plain old man so interesting."},"cma:137135":{"d":"A modest-sized portrait of Vincenzo Guarignoni, painted in oil on canvas about 1572 and measuring about 63 by 51 centimeters. A Latin inscription gives the sitter's name, his age of 45 and the date, which makes the picture more precisely documented than many of its period. Guarignoni came from a prominent family of Bergamo. The museum notes his direct, thoughtful gaze, which enhances the lifelike quality, while the solemn detachment suggests dignity rather than a particular personality.","i":"In Bergamo, then part of the Venetian Republic, noble and professional families commissioned portraits as records of lineage and standing. The inscription makes this picture a kind of document, fixing a person at 45. The museum observes that Renaissance portraits often convey the dignity of a station rather than the minute traits of character, and the Latin text and poised gaze belong to that tradition.","c":"Moroni makes his sitter present through a plain, direct gaze and a careful, unfussy handling, and at this modest size the portrait has an intimacy that larger display portraits lack. As the museum says, it offers dignity and clarity rather than a search of character. It is typical of the Bergamask manner of the time, and quietly authoritative, though less probing than his most individual portraits."},"met:437165":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified man, painted in oil on canvas, undated, about 122 by 103 centimeters. The museum's keywords include a helmet, so he is probably presented as a soldier or gentleman-at-arms, with the helmet near him as a sign of his role. The size is large enough for a figure with some of the setting. Moroni, the leading portraitist of Bergamo, was known for plain, honest likenesses.","i":"A helmet in a portrait tells the viewer the sitter's role. In the sixteenth century, men of the Bergamo gentry often held commissions in the Venetian militia, so a sitter shown with armor would have wanted to be remembered as a soldier as well as a citizen. Without a date or a name, the picture is best read as a type, a man of the Venetian mainland dressed to show his service.","c":"Moroni's unflattering honesty is his hallmark: he portrays what is in front of him with little rhetoric, and a helmet used as a quiet emblem rather than a showpiece suits that plain manner. Lacking a name or date, the picture has less of the specific character of his best-known sitters, and its interest lies chiefly in how a restrained painter handled a military subject."},"nga:434":{"d":"A bearded man in a cranberry-red robe edged with gold over a topaz-blue textured shirt stands before a gold ground in a tall panel with a pointed top. He looks off to the viewer's right and points that way with one arm crossed over his chest, while the other hand holds a scroll lettered ECCE AGNUS DEI, Behold the Lamb of God. His beard falls in long ropes to his chest, and a patterned halo is punched into the gold. Tempera on panel, about 89 by 42 centimeters, probably about 1325.","i":"The figure is Saint John the Baptist, who, on seeing Jesus, cried out the words on his scroll. His pointing arm is his usual sign, directing the viewer to Christ, and the shape of the panel suggests it once stood beside a central image in a larger altarpiece, where he would have pointed toward it. The camel-hair shirt and the red mantle are his traditional dress. Such gold-ground panels were made to glow in the candlelit dimness of churches.","c":"Lippo Memmi is often overshadowed by his brother-in-law Simone Martini, and this panel shows why he is remembered as an excellent follower: a refined line, flowing contour and narrowed, elegant eyes, all within the Sienese taste for gold and fine tooling. Where Simone is intense, Memmi is gentle, and the Baptist's gesture is more courteous than commanding. It is a graceful example of Sienese craft rather than a work of striking invention."},"nga:13":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel with a pointed top, about 51 by 24 centimeters, showing the Virgin from the hips up against a richly patterned gold ground. In a burgundy gown and a long midnight-blue mantle edged with gold, she holds the Christ child, who clutches her scarf with one hand and touches her fingers with the other; both have punched halos. Far smaller than the Virgin, a donor kneels at the lower left in a dove-gray hooded cloak, his hair cut in a ring above the ears, one hand raised as he looks up at her. A border of flowers and dots frames the panel. Tempera, 1325 to 1330.","i":"A donor portrait puts the person who paid for a picture inside it, in the presence of the sacred figures. Medieval scale was symbolic rather than optical: the donor is tiny because he is humble, while the Virgin looms as the great intercessor. His haircut and hooded cloak suggest that he may be a cleric or member of a religious order. The child's grasp at his mother's scarf is a human touch that Sienese painters liked to add to a formal image.","c":"Lippo Memmi sets the formality of gold, punched halos and border against delicate drawing, and the human exchange between mother and child keeps the image from stiffness. The tiny donor is a convention, a mark of status and piety rather than a likeness. It is a refined small devotional panel in the elegant manner of Simone Martini's circle, charming rather than inventive, with some of the red under-layer now showing through the worn gold."},"met:437064":{"d":"A small panel, about 39 by 19 centimeters, tempera on a gold ground, showing Saint Clare of Assisi as a woman holding a book, an attribute the museum lists among its themes. It is by Lippo Memmi and dated about 1330. The narrow, upright shape and small size suggest that it was one element of a larger altarpiece with several saints, perhaps in an upper tier, rather than an independent picture.","i":"Clare of Assisi, who lived from 1194 to 1253, was the first follower of Saint Francis and founded the Poor Clares, a women's order devoted to poverty. She was canonized in 1255, and images of her soon appeared in Franciscan churches across Italy. The book may allude to the Rule she wrote, which was the first monastic rule known to have been written by a woman. A single saint in a gold frame served as a focus for prayer and a reminder of Franciscan ideals.","c":"At this scale Memmi's gifts are concentrated: a smooth, simple contour, a quiet face and the shimmer of tooled gold. A single standing saint leaves little room for narrative, so the effect depends on refinement of line rather than invention. As a fragment of a lost whole it has the poignancy of a piece taken from its context, though it inevitably lacks the force that the full altarpiece would have given it."},"met:437065":{"d":"A tall panel, about 89 by 42 centimeters, in tempera on a gold ground, showing Saint Paul, identified by the sword the museum lists among its subjects. It is by Lippo Memmi and dated about 1330. The narrow, upright shape suggests a side panel from a polyptych, an altarpiece made of several framed panels in which saints stood in separate frames beside a central image.","i":"Paul, the apostle to the Gentiles, persecuted Christians until his conversion and then wrote the letters that make up much of the New Testament. His usual attributes are a sword, the instrument of his beheading in Rome, and a book or scroll for his epistles. In altarpieces he commonly stands for the teaching of the Church, often beside Peter, who holds the keys. In a Sienese polyptych he gave doctrinal weight to the figures around the Virgin.","c":"Memmi gives the apostle a dignified, gentle presence, and the narrow panel concentrates attention on the figure and the glowing gold. The face and drapery have the refinement of the Martini workshop, but the effect is quieter than Simone's: the saint is an icon of authority rather than a drama. It is a graceful and typical piece of Sienese craft, less memorable on its own than as part of the ensemble it came from."},"met:437063":{"d":"A small panel, about 50 by 26 centimeters, in tempera on a gold ground, dated about 1350, in which the museum lists the Virgin and the Christ Child, Saint Francis, Saint John the Baptist and an angel. It is by Lippo Memmi. The presence of Saint Francis beside the Baptist suggests a Franciscan connection, and the small scale a private or small-chapel devotion. It was made in the years just after the Black Death of 1348 devastated Siena.","i":"The Virgin and Child with a saint on each side was the standard format for devotional panels. John the Baptist points toward Christ as the Lamb of God, and Francis, who received the stigmata, represents the humility of the Franciscan order, which was strong in Siena. Angels, as courtiers of the heavenly queen, add to her honor. A small panel like this was made for prayer before an image, bringing together the saints important to its owner.","c":"A small panel shows Sienese refinement: delicate drawing, rich gold and a harmony of colors, with charm depending on line and tooled gold more than on psychological depth. Dated late in Memmi's career and soon after the plague, it shows how the Sienese tradition of Simone Martini continued toward mid-century. It is conservative, and a little formulaic, but the formula was perfected and the craft is first-rate."},"met:436929":{"d":"A canvas of about 88 by 70 centimeters, dated 1653, illustrating the moment in Genesis when Abraham sends away Hagar and her son Ishmael. The museum lists Abraham, a woman, a child and a boy. Nicolaes Maes was about nineteen and had probably recently left Rembrandt's workshop for his native Dordrecht, and the choice of a biblical scene of family conflict with a few large figures is characteristic of Rembrandt's school.","i":"At Sarah's demand, Abraham sent away his concubine Hagar and their son Ishmael after Isaac was born, giving them bread and a skin of water. It is a scene of painful choice: Abraham obeys his wife and God's word, yet the departure of his son costs him. Dutch Protestants valued such stories as lessons in obedience, trust in God's providence and compassion for the outcast, and Rembrandt's circle returned to the subject repeatedly.","c":"Maes's early works show the influence of Rembrandt's teaching in warm light and expressive faces, and this picture is a skilled exercise in that manner. The drama is carried by gesture and light rather than by invention, and Maes handles both capably. It lacks Rembrandt's depth of feeling, but it shows a gifted pupil, still very young, mastering the master's language before he turned to other kinds of painting."},"nga:70":{"d":"An old woman dozing over a book, as the title says, painted in oil on canvas, about 82 by 67 centimeters, about 1655. The subject and date place it among the pictures of old women reading, praying and sleeping that Nicolaes Maes painted in Dordrecht in the mid-1650s, a type that usually presents one figure in a plain domestic setting with the face modeled by a single, warm light.","i":"In Dutch art a sleeping reader could carry more than one meaning: an image of tired, pious old age after long devotion to the Bible, or a mild warning against sloth and neglected duty. Rembrandt, Maes's teacher, had painted old people reading the Bible, and Maes continued the theme in a simpler, homelier tone. Viewers enjoyed the observed truth of the sleeper, and perhaps a smile at her expense, as much as the moral.","c":"Maes's pictures of old women are among his most attractive, pairing the tonal lessons of Rembrandt with sharp observation of everyday life. The small drama of a head nodding over a page is easy to read and kindly in spirit. It is a modest picture, closer to a character study than a story, and it shows how readily a gifted pupil turned Rembrandt's vocabulary to genre."},"met:438740":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified woman dated 1657, painted in oil on copper, a hard, smooth support that suits small, finely finished portraits. The museum's keywords record only a woman in a portrait, so the picture belongs to the type of Dutch burgher portrait of the 1650s, usually modest in dress and plain in background, with the head lit to stand out clearly. Nicolaes Maes was then working in Dordrecht.","i":"A portrait on copper was a costly, intimate kind of likeness, suited to a family keepsake or to a small cabinet, where its smooth surface could be admired up close. By the later 1650s Maes had begun to take portrait commissions alongside his genre scenes, and portraiture soon became his main source of income. A woman's portrait of this type was often made as one of a pair with her husband's.","c":"On a copper support, a painter can work with great delicacy, and the type rewards close looking: the smoothness of the ground lets a clean, fine line show. Maes at this date is still near the Rembrandt-inspired restraint of his early work, before his later, more courtly manner. Without a named sitter, the portrait is best valued as an example of his early portrait style, which is careful and decent, if modest."},"met:436934":{"d":"A young woman in an apron works at a table covered with a cloth, peeling apples with a knife over a bucket, with a basket nearby, the objects the museum lists. The oil on wood panel, about 55 by 46 centimeters, was painted about 1655 by Nicolaes Maes of Dordrecht. The subject is a plain domestic task, treated as a small interior, and the keywords add servants and working among its themes.","i":"Dutch domestic pictures of the 1650s often praised household industry while hinting at its dangers. An apple is both food and the fruit of the Fall, and women at housework could signify virtue and thrift, or a quiet invitation. Maes painted a number of servant and housewife scenes in the middle of the decade, treating the kitchen as a place where small acts carry meaning. Interpretations vary, and the picture can also be enjoyed as the image of a task done with attention.","c":"Maes makes a modest chore interesting by the care of his observation: apples, cloth, bucket and apron are all things with weight and texture, and the woman's concentration sets the mood. The warm light shows what he learned from Rembrandt about making a humble scene glow. It is a quiet, small picture without much narrative, and its appeal lies in its steady, unforced truthfulness."},"met:436932":{"d":"A small canvas, about 45 by 53 centimeters, showing a woman making bobbin lace at a lace pillow, with a child in a high chair beside her. The museum's keywords list scissors, a diadem cap and a baby bumper, the padded cap worn to protect the head of a child learning to walk. The interior scene was painted about 1656 by Nicolaes Maes of Dordrecht.","i":"Lacemaking was a skilled household craft, and Dutch artists used it to praise diligence and domestic order; Vermeer's Lacemaker of about 1669 is the best-known example. Here the woman's work and her care for the child appear together, a double image of the housewife as worker and mother. Moralists such as Jacob Cats held up household industry as a woman's chief virtue, and a picture like this would have confirmed its owners' ideals.","c":"The subject lets Maes combine two kinds of attention, to work and to child, and the lace pillow, bobbins and scissors are observed with enjoyment. Compared with Vermeer's later lacemaker the effect is more anecdotal and less still, but it has a warmth that Vermeer avoided. It is a small, successful domestic picture from the years when Maes was at his best as a genre painter."},"met:436933":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified woman, painted about 1665 to 1670 in oil on canvas, about 112 by 89 centimeters. By this date Maes had become a busy portrait painter, working in a lighter, more elegant manner than the dark tones of his Rembrandt-inspired youth. The museum's keywords record only a woman in a portrait, and her name has not come down with the picture.","i":"Portraits like this were commissions from the Dutch regent and merchant classes, made to record status and fashion, with dress, pose and expression arranged to show prosperity and modesty together. Many of Maes's portraits were made as pairs of husband and wife, which hung together in the home. In the later 1660s Maes was developing the more elegant, courtly manner that would carry him to success in Amsterdam.","c":"Maes's later portraits succeed through tact: a graceful pose, a soft, flattering light and a quiet competence in fabric and flesh. They lack the penetration of his early Rembrandtesque studies and verge on formula, with attractive sitters who can look alike. Even so, this is a polished example of a type that Dutch buyers wanted, and a reminder that portraiture, not genre, is what earned Maes his living."},"met:436930":{"d":"A small oil portrait on canvas, about 44 by 33 centimeters, of Ingena Rotterdam, dated 1676. The title identifies her as the betrothed of Admiral Jacob Binkes, and the museum also owns his portrait, of the same size, evidently made as a pair. She died in 1704. Nicolaes Maes was by then established in Amsterdam as a fashionable portrait painter, having moved there from Dordrecht in 1673.","i":"Betrothal portraits were made to mark an engagement and to hang together in the home, so that the two likenesses, shown side by side, anticipated the union. Binkes, a naval commander of the Dutch Republic in its wars with France, died in 1677, a year after this portrait, so the pair records a short engagement, and the portrait of Ingena outlived him by almost three decades.","c":"Small portraits like this are among Maes's most attractive late works: tactful, softly lit and quick in execution, without the bulk of a state portrait. They trade the Rembrandt-inspired depth of his youth for polish and a graceful likeness. As half of a documented pair, tied to a particular story of engagement and loss, the picture holds more interest than a typical portrait of the period."},"nga:121137":{"d":"A portrait of a lady dated 1676, painted in oil on canvas, about 116 by 91 centimeters. The title indicates a woman of some standing, and the date places it in the years after Nicolaes Maes had moved from Dordrecht to Amsterdam in 1673, where he became a leading portraitist. The sitter's name is not given, so the picture is best understood as an example of the fashionable Dutch portrait of the 1670s.","i":"Portraits of well-to-do women in the 1670s showed rank through silks, jewelry and a relaxed, courtly pose, in a style influenced by the French and Flemish portrait manner then in vogue. The Dutch elite of Amsterdam wanted likenesses that were elegant without being showy. A portrait of this size would hang in the family house, often with a matching portrait of a husband, and display taste along with status.","c":"Maes in Amsterdam is a smooth, accomplished portraitist, with a light touch and a gift for flattering likenesses, but also a repetitive one, since sitters in this period are posed and dressed to a pattern. This portrait fits that description: competent and elegant, if somewhat generic, and mostly valuable now for what it shows of 1670s fashion and of Maes's later manner, far from the Rembrandtesque depth of his early works."},"met:436931":{"d":"A small oil portrait of Admiral Jacob Binkes, about 44 by 33 centimeters, painted on canvas by Nicolaes Maes. It is undated, but it matches the size of the portrait of his fiancée Ingena Rotterdam, dated 1676, so the two were probably painted together. The title gives his dates as about 1640 and 1677, so he died in his late thirties.","i":"Binkes was a naval officer of the Dutch Republic, which in the 1670s fought France and England at sea and in colonial waters. An admiral's portrait displayed rank and service for family and friends, and the small format suggests a personal likeness rather than a state commemoration. He died in 1677, a year after his betrothal portrait was painted, which gives the pair a note of loss.","c":"A compact portrait that relies on a convincing, alert head: Maes keeps the setting plain and the handling quick and clean. Beside grander naval portraits, with ships and flags, this is human-sized and modest. It is not a showpiece, but it is a sound record, and because it is half of a pair its value lies partly in its companion and in the short story it records."},"cma:151377":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 183 by 108 centimeters, of the death of Simon Magus, the sorcerer who claimed to be the son of God and tried to prove it by flying over Rome. Through the prayers of Saint Peter, the demons holding him up abandoned him, and he fell to his death. The Cleveland Museum of Art thinks this is probably a studio copy after a lost oil sketch by Batoni for a huge altarpiece commissioned in 1746 for Saint Peter's Basilica. It dates from about 1745 to 1750.","i":"The story comes from early apocryphal accounts of the apostles and was popular because it dramatized the victory of true faith over sorcery, fitting for a basilica built over Peter's tomb. The altarpiece was to be translated into mosaic because the damp air of Saint Peter's quickly ruined oil paintings, a practice that replaced many of the basilica's altarpieces. Batoni's mosaic was never made, for reasons that remain unclear.","c":"Batoni was one of Rome's leading painters at mid-century, and his large religious compositions combined Baroque movement with clear, classical drawing. A studio copy of a lost sketch cannot show his hand fully: the elegance of the composition is evident, but the surface will be less assured than the original's. As a record of a failed commission it is informative, and it gives a taste of his skill at dramatic action."},"cma:166780":{"d":"A small oil sketch, only about 24 by 31 centimeters, made about 1746 as a study for a larger picture. Its subject is the story the Cleveland museum summarizes: Antiochus lies in bed near death from a mysterious illness, and his grieving father summons a doctor, who notices that the sight of the young woman Stratonice quickens the youth's pulse and so discovers that passion is the cause. The father then selflessly offers her to his son.","i":"The tale, told by Plutarch and other ancient writers, concerns the Syrian king Seleucus, his son Antiochus, who fell sick for love of his stepmother Stratonice, and the physician who deduced the cause. It entered painting as a story of love's power and of a father's generosity, with the doctor's cleverness as a bonus. As the museum notes, it remained popular well into the 1800s, and later artists such as David and Ingres painted it.","c":"An oil sketch is where a painter's habits show, with brushwork typically quick and fluent and the grouping of the actors cleanly worked out. Batoni's finished pictures are smoother and more classical, but sketches like this one often have a vitality that the final canvas loses. The subject, both medical drama and love story, suits his mixed Baroque and classical taste, and the small scale makes it an attractive, intimate object."},"met:435622":{"d":"A mythological canvas, about 125 by 173 centimeters, dated 1761, in which the Met's keywords list Diana, Cupid, a bow and arrow, and dogs. Wide and horizontal, it is a decorative picture for a collector's room, of a kind Batoni supplied to noble patrons, and it sets two opposed powers, the huntress goddess and the god of love, in a single encounter.","i":"Diana, the virgin goddess of the hunt, was the sworn enemy of love, and Cupid, whose arrows made gods and mortals fall in love, was her classic foil; whichever of them handles the other's weapons gives the picture its comic or moral turn. Eighteenth-century painters liked the subject because it allowed elegance and wit. Batoni's foreign patrons in Rome valued such pictures as smart ornaments, pleasures of antique learning rather than serious allegories.","c":"Batoni is better known for portraits of Grand Tourists than for mythologies, but his mythological pictures show the same clear drawing, smooth finish and idealized types, in a manner descended from Raphael and the Carracci. The cool, polished surface is more admirable than warm, and the comedy is gentle rather than witty. It is a well-made decorative canvas, and a reminder that he was a history painter before he was a portraitist."},"nga:129282":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man on a canvas of about 75 by 63 centimetres, painted around 1762 by Pompeo Batoni. At this size the picture is a half-length or bust-length likeness that concentrates on the sitter's face and dress. Batoni, one of the most sought-after portraitists in Rome, was known for a smooth, glossy finish, clear light and poses of easy confidence, and his sitters were often foreign visitors to the city.","i":"Batoni's clients were largely young men from Britain, Ireland and northern Europe who came to Rome on the Grand Tour to complete their education, and who wanted a portrait as a souvenir and as proof of their cultivation. If this man was such a visitor, as is likely but not certain from the painter and date, the portrait would have gone home to hang among family pictures. The sitter's name is not recorded, so he is read as a type: an educated gentleman of the Enlightenment.","c":"Batoni's gift was for polished surface and agreeable presence: his portraits render silk, skin and lace with a cool brilliance that pleased his patrons. The weakness is a certain formula, a ready-made poise that can make his sitters resemble one another, and he seldom probes character as Reynolds sometimes does. Even so, within the limits of flattery he is a very skilful portraitist, and this picture is a good example of his clean, bright manner."},"met:435623":{"d":"A very large full-length portrait of an unidentified young man, about 247 by 176 centimeters, painted about 1760 to 1765, with a book and a dog among the subjects the museum lists. The scale and the manner are those of Batoni's portraits of wealthy visitors to Rome on the Grand Tour. Close to life size and over eight feet tall, it is a picture designed to hang in a grand room and to command it.","i":"The Grand Tour brought young British and other northern European aristocrats to Rome, where they bought antiquities and commissioned portraits from Batoni to record the journey. The book signals education and taste, the dog loyalty and the pleasures of country life, and the full-length format their rank. Such portraits were shipped home to hang in country houses as proof of cultivation and of a gentleman's finished education.","c":"Batoni made himself the portraitist of choice for Grand Tourists by giving them what they wanted: a convincing face, a fashionably relaxed pose, and silks and stone rendered with enameled polish. The strengths of the type are polish, ease and legibility; its weakness is sameness, since sitters can look like mannequins in grand costume. The best examples have a freshness, and the scale and finish of this canvas show how well the formula worked."},"met:437295":{"d":"A wide history painting, about 135 by 196 centimeters, showing Thusnelda, wife of the Germanic leader Arminius, led as a captive in the triumph of the Roman general Germanicus; the museum's keywords list a woman, a child, a man and a crowd, with Rome as the setting. The subject was the basis of a monumental canvas of 1873 in Munich, and this much smaller picture, dated about 1875, is probably a later, reduced version.","i":"In 17 AD Germanicus celebrated a triumph in Rome over the Germanic tribes, and Roman writers record that the captive Thusnelda, with her young son, was displayed in the procession. Nineteenth-century painters turned her into a figure of dignified suffering, a proud, defeated enemy facing a curious crowd. In Germany, where Arminius had become a symbol of unity after 1871, the picture carried a patriotic meaning that the Roman sources did not intend.","c":"Piloty belonged to a school that paired meticulous archaeological detail with theatrical staging, and this is a good example: crowd, costume and architecture are arranged with a stage's clarity around the captive. The effect is more theatrical than moving, and the finish can look like a tableau. But the skill with which a large cast is organized is impressive, and the picture is a reliable sample of ambitious history painting of the 1870s."},"met:12797":{"d":"The record lists the motifs of this portrait as a woman with a shawl and a collar, a tree, grass, an urn, a funerary structure and a shoe. The mention of the shoe suggests that she is shown at full length, and the canvas is tall, about 122 by 80 centimeters. The sitter is the painter's wife, Olivia Leonard. Her pose, expression and costume are not otherwise described, and the date is given only as about 1810 to 1820.","i":"An urn and a funerary monument were standard symbols of mourning in American art of the early nineteenth century, often paired with a weeping willow, and a portrait that includes them may commemorate a death in the family or simply reflect the taste for sentimental memorial imagery. The museum record does not say which. Painted by her husband, it is a picture in which the artist's closeness to the sitter may be part of the content.","c":"Funerary props such as the urn and monument lend a portrait solemnity, but they also stiffen it, and a painter working in provincial America had few other models for a formal full-length. The strength of such portraits is usually directness, not elegance. As a record of a family, and of the mourning conventions of its time, this portrait is more valuable than it is polished."},"nga:46128":{"d":"A small panel, about 41 by 25 centimeters, in tempera on a shiny gold ground. A nearly naked Christ hangs by his hands from a T-shaped cross, head tipped to his shoulder and eyes closed, blood trickling from the crown of thorns, hands, feet and side. Five tiny angels in royal-blue garments catch the blood in golden chalices. At the cross's foot a balding, white-bearded man in a gold brocade tunic kneels at left, the Virgin in lapis blue stands behind him with tears on her cheeks, and a haloed figure with long blond hair prays at the right edge.","i":"The angels catching Christ's blood in chalices connect the Crucifixion with the Eucharist, in which wine becomes his blood, and would have recalled the sacrament to a viewer at Mass. The richly dressed kneeling man at the left may be the person who commissioned the panel, but he is not named. Small panels like this served private prayer, and in Cologne the tender, flowing manner of the Master of Saint Veronica suited that intimate devotion.","c":"The Master of Saint Veronica is a leading name in the Cologne soft style of about 1400: gentle faces, flowing drapery and sorrow expressed as grace rather than violence. This small Crucifixion is in that mode, with restrained grief, luminous color and tender angels. Fine cracks and worn gold dull the effect somewhat, but the beauty of the design and of its small, careful details comes through."},"nga:127587":{"d":"A woman kneels at the lower right, leaning toward a wafer offered by a balding, bearded monk in a patched brown robe, while three angels surround them. Saint Mary of Egypt is gaunt and gray-tinged, with a tear on her cheek and chestnut hair falling down her back; she is naked to the waist except for a white cloth, and her lower body is wrapped in cobalt-blue fabric. One angel kneels holding a burning torch, another hovers behind her with wide white wings, a third looks on. Light falls from the upper left. Oil on canvas, about 209 by 142 centimeters, about 1695.","i":"According to her legend, Mary of Egypt lived as a prostitute in Alexandria, then after a conversion spent decades as a hermit in the desert beyond the Jordan. The monk Zosimas, who found her, brought her communion before her death, which is the moment shown. She was a model of repentance, and in the Catholic Church of the 1600s such images stressed penitence and grace; the angels and torch stress divine presence at her passing. The large size suggests an altarpiece.","c":"The drama is theatrical in the best Baroque sense: a spotlight from the upper left, an angel whose white wings span the canvas, and a gaunt saint set against the monk's rough habit. Ricci would later lighten his palette in the Veronese-inspired style that made his fame, but this earlier phase of strong light and dark has an intensity that the later work often relaxes. It is a forceful, well-staged picture of devotion."},"cma:150117":{"d":"An oil sketch on canvas, about 79 by 63 centimeters, for a decoration in the church of San Bernardino dei Morti in Milan. The museum describes it as the apotheosis, the ascent into heaven, of a saint shown in military costume. Its record gives no fuller account of the composition, so the angels, clouds and attendants that such subjects normally involve are not described here. Sketches of this kind let a painter lay out masses of light and color so that patrons could judge a design before the large work was begun.","i":"The saint's identity is unknown: documents describing the commission have not been found, and the military dress does not by itself identify him, since several soldier-saints exist. The church, San Bernardino alle Ossa, is known as the church of the dead because of its ossuary, decorated with human bones, and a soldier rising to heaven would offer a counterweight of hope. Ricci, a Venetian, was working in Milan in the mid-1690s, and the sketch dates from about 1695.","c":"Ricci's sketches typically show him at his freest, with the brush moving quickly and the design built from masses of light, and the upward thrust of an apotheosis is a subject that suits him. The limits are those of any sketch: without the finished work, the drama is incomplete, and the saint's anonymity leaves form without story. It is nonetheless a good guide to his gift for flowing, airy design."},"met:437458":{"d":"A Holy Family scene in oil on canvas, about 127 by 116 centimeters, nearly square. The museum's keywords list Christ, the Virgin Mary, Saint Joseph, angels and reading, so the group is a domestic one, with a book among them and angels attending the Child. No fuller description of the arrangement, the colors or the setting is on record, and none is claimed here. The picture dates from about 1700, when Ricci, an itinerant painter working in several Italian cities, was developing the lighter, more colorful manner that made him a leader of Venetian painting.","i":"Holy Family pictures were made for private devotion, to hang in a bedroom or a small chapel, and the mood is usually tender and domestic. The reading is a clue: Mary is often shown with a book at the Annunciation, a sign of her wisdom and of the Scriptures that foretold the Child, and a book in the Holy Family makes the household a school of faith. Angels attend as a reminder that the Child is God.","c":"Ricci's religious canvases of around 1700 turned away from the dark, heavy manner of much Roman and Bolognese painting toward lighter colors and a freer touch learned from Veronese, and a Holy Family was an ideal subject for it: warm, informal and unthreatening. The risk is sweetness, since angels and sentiment can slide into prettiness. A picture of this kind should be enjoyed for its color and ease rather than for depth of feeling."},"met:437457":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 66 by 102 centimeters, shows the Baptism of Christ. The museum's keywords list Jesus, John the Baptist, an angel, a man and a woman, so onlookers share the bank of the Jordan with the central pair. No fuller description of the composition or the setting is given, and none is claimed here. The size and the date, about 1713 to 1714, are close to those of the National Gallery's Last Supper by Ricci, painted when he was in England.","i":"The Baptism of Jesus by John in the Jordan is told in the first three Gospels, and it is the moment at which the Spirit descends in the form of a dove and a voice declares him the Son of God, a revelation of the Trinity that artists marked with a dove and rays of light. The man and woman among the onlookers reflect the tradition of crowding the riverbank with witnesses. Ricci's pictures of these years are probably sketches for larger decorations.","c":"Ricci is at his best in water and light, and the Baptism gives him both. His Venetian palette of silvery blues, pinks and creamy whites makes a riverbank sparkle, and the grouping gives a loose, breathing rhythm. The picture is a sketch more than a finished work, with the weaknesses of one, and it asks the viewer to imagine the larger design. As a sample of his English years it is lively and bright."},"nga:12130":{"d":"Seen through a wide stone arch, seventeen people and a dog gather around a long banquet table laid with a white cloth in a darkened hall lined with shallow columns. Christ, at the center of the far side, has light radiating from his head and points a finger upward; the apostles converse in pairs and trios in robes of sage green, tan, brown and slate blue. A gold candelabrum hangs overhead. In the foreground an old man twists to lift a jug from a basin, another turns his back beside an overturned seat, and a spotted dog gnaws a bone. Golden statues flank the arch.","i":"Ricci set the Last Supper in the manner of Veronese, whose huge sixteenth-century banquet pictures, with their architecture, dogs and servants, were the model for Venetian painters of Ricci's generation. The date, 1713-14, falls when Ricci was working in England, and the sketchy handling noted by the museum is typical of a design for a larger work. The overturned seat and gnawing dog bring everyday life to the sacred meal, as Veronese had done.","c":"Ricci's debt to Veronese is plain, and his invention is lively: the spiraling gestures, the turned backs and the dog bring a crowd to life, and the golden statues give the architecture a theatrical sparkle. Close up, the paint is loose and the figures summary, so it works better as a spectacle than as a meditation on the sacrament. It shows how a Venetian could give the Last Supper the sparkle of a feast."},"nga:46157":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, about 222 by 159 centimeters, painted in 1725 as a memorial to Admiral Sir Clowdisley Shovell, the British naval commander who died in 1707. The museum's record gives no description of the image. A memorial of this kind is not a portrait of the living man but an imagined monument, in which allegorical figures such as Fame, Victory and mourning sea gods usually honor the dead hero around a sarcophagus or portrait, set against grand architecture.","i":"Shovell (1650-1707) was one of the most celebrated English admirals of his day and died when his flagship and other ships of the fleet were wrecked on the rocks off the Isles of Scilly, with the loss of well over a thousand men. A Venetian painting of 1725 commemorating him is thought to belong to a set of imaginary tombs of British worthies that the agent Owen McSwiny commissioned from Venetian painters for English patrons in the 1720s.","c":"The allegorical tomb, with its rhetorical gestures, is a limited form, and mourning was not Ricci's natural subject. Yet the format suits his fluid, theatrical design, and a Venetian painter's color gives the solemn occasion a lightness that the marble monuments of the day lacked. As a record of how Britain's heroes were remembered by foreign painters it is of historical interest, and its large scale is imposing."},"nga:212":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 85 by 35 centimeters, painted in 1733. The museum's title names the subject as a miracle of Saint Francis of Paola, and the record gives no description of the scene, so the miracle, the figures and the setting are not described here. The proportions, more than twice as high as wide, are those of a design for a narrow field in a church, and the picture seems to have been planned as a companion to a canvas of the same size, The Exaltation of the True Cross.","i":"Francis of Paola (1416-1507), a Calabrian hermit who founded the Order of Minims and was canonized in 1519, was famed as a miracle-worker; his emblem is the word 'Charitas', love, in a glory of rays. He ended his life in France, where Louis XI had summoned him in 1482 to pray for the king's health. In the 1730s Ricci, in his seventies and back in Venice, painted a number of religious pieces, and a miracle scene was a standard theme for a church.","c":"Ricci's late sketches have a swift, airy touch that kept him fashionable into his seventies, and a narrow format, which demands a vertical design, suits his habit of piling figures upward on clouds. Without the finished version the scene is hard to judge, and its chief interest is as evidence of the old painter's undiminished energy. Together with its companion it makes a lively pair, charming rather than weighty."},"nga:213":{"d":"A large cross stands at the center of a narrow canvas, about 84 by 35 centimeters. Angels fly above in clouds, some holding instruments, garlands or tall staffs, while below a group in colorful dress gathers at the foot of the cross before a classical cityscape of arches and stone buildings. At lower left a man in a white waist cloth kneels with raised hands; at right two women in flowing gold and seafoam-green robes look at the cross, one resting a hand on it. A pale yellow arch closes the top of the picture.","i":"The subject is the Exaltation, or raising up, of the True Cross, the relic said to have been found in Jerusalem by Helena, the mother of the emperor Constantine, whom the museum's data name among those shown. Her discovery of the cross in the fourth century, and its triumphant display, was a favorite subject of Baroque churches, where it demonstrated the power of faith and the victory over death. The classical architecture sets the story in the ancient world.","c":"A lively sketch for a tall decorative field: the cross as a strong central vertical, the angels rising in a spiral, and the colorful crowd below giving the pattern its liveliness. The rapid strokes and bright gold and green are typical of Ricci in his seventies, though the scene is crowded, and it reads more as ceremony than as drama. A decorative success rather than a profound image."},"cma:97146":{"d":"A small canvas, about 35 by 44 centimeters, showing the moment from the Gospel of John when Christ, confronted by Pharisees who have brought a woman accused of adultery, tells them that whoever is without sin should throw the first stone. The Cleveland Museum identifies the picture as a copy of a painting by Ricci in the Minneapolis Institute of Arts, perhaps made by a student and certainly not by Ricci himself. Its record does not describe the figures or the setting, so the composition is not detailed here.","i":"The story, from John 7:53 to 8:11, ends with the Pharisees drifting away one by one and Christ sending the woman off with a warning not to sin again. The museum notes that the subject was popular in eighteenth-century Venice, partly because magnanimity was a celebrated virtue in painting, music and literature, and partly because the showy, exotic scene let painters present an openly sexualized woman. Copies show how widely Ricci's compositions circulated.","c":"As a copy it can show only what the original did, and a derivative picture rarely rises above its source. The subject suits Ricci's theatrical manner, with its crowd of reacting figures, and a good copy keeps the clarity of that design, but a pupil's hand usually loses the brio of the master's brushwork, so the paint looks careful where Ricci's would be quick. Its interest is as a record of a popular composition and of his influence."},"met:816618":{"d":"The shepherd Paris must choose which of three goddesses is the most beautiful, and this canvas, about 97 by 135 centimeters and painted around 1640, shows the contest with three nudes. Juno, Minerva and Venus stand before him, the messenger god Mercury has brought them, and a dog joins the group. The wide format lays out the moment of judgment as a clear, theatrical encounter.","i":"At the wedding of Peleus and Thetis a golden apple marked 'for the fairest' was thrown among the guests, and Juno, Minerva and Venus each claimed it. Zeus sent them to Paris, who judged. Juno offered power, Minerva wisdom and victory, and Venus the love of the most beautiful woman, Helen. Paris chose Venus, and the Trojan War followed. Painters enjoyed the story as a pretext for the nude and as a moral about choosing pleasure over wisdom.","c":"Turchi was a refined painter with a cool, polished surface, trained in Verona and active in Rome, closer to the Carracci and to Guido Reni's idealism than to Caravaggio's drama. Such a classical manner suits a pretext for idealized nudes. The result is elegant and finished rather than passionate: the goddesses are posed more than moved by the story, and the picture will please viewers who prefer polish to emotional force."},"met:11206":{"d":"A portrait miniature in watercolor on ivory, only about 8 by 8 centimeters, made around 1825 and showing a man named in the title as Benjamin Moore McVickar. The record lists no features beyond 'man' and 'portrait', so his pose and dress are not described. Objects of this kind were designed to be held in the hand or worn, and the small scale would have allowed delicate modeling of the face.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the intimate counterpart of oil portraits, exchanged between family members and lovers as keepsakes. The McVickars were a well-known New York family, and Ingham, who emigrated from Ireland to New York in 1816, was one of the city's fashionable portraitists and a founder of the National Academy of Design in 1825. The miniature dates from about that year.","c":"Ivory suits Ingham's reputation for a smooth, porcelain-like finish, and the miniature format asks for restraint and a keen likeness; his glossy touch translates well to a surface the size of a palm. The trade-off is a certain blandness: the sitter reads as a type of refined New York gentleman more than as a character. A polished keepsake, accomplished rather than searching."},"met:11205":{"d":"A full-length portrait of a child in a landscape, large for its subject at about 172 by 135 centimeters, painted around 1830. The record lists a girl, a basket, flowers and a tree, so the sitter, Amelia Palmer, is shown outdoors with her flowers. The museum gives no description of her pose or dress, so the picture is best understood as a formal commission of a type common among prosperous families of the period.","i":"Children were painted in their best clothes and surrounded by flowers and gardens to signal innocence and the family's prosperity. In the 1830s such large portraits of daughters hung in the drawing rooms of wealthy New Yorkers. Ingham, a leading portraitist for that society, was known for the polished, enamel-like finish of his flesh and fabrics.","c":"Ingham's smooth finish suits a child's skin and a muslin dress, and the large format gives this portrait a presence that most American child portraits of the period lack. The risk is sweetness: flowers, basket and garden belong to a formula, and the polish can feel like a veneer. As a record of a prosperous New York childhood around 1830 it is vivid; as an image of one particular girl, less sure."},"met:11208":{"d":"A portrait of Frances Wilkes Colden, wife of David Cadwallader Colden, painted in 1830 on a canvas of about 73 by 60 centimeters, a moderate size for a society portrait. The museum describes no further detail of her pose, costume or setting, and the keywords give only 'Portraits' and 'Women'. The picture came to the museum by bequest from Grace Wilkes, presumably a member of her family.","i":"The Coldens were a distinguished New York family: David was the son of Cadwallader D. Colden, a lawyer, mayor of New York City and congressman. A portrait of a married woman by Ingham around 1830 served as a record of family and status, and Ingham, who painted much of the city's elite, was known for flattering likenesses with a smooth finish.","c":"In Ingham's society portraits the sitter is polished into a smooth, porcelain-like surface, with fabrics and jewelry rendered more vividly than character. If this portrait follows his habit, its pleasures are craftsmanship and elegance and its limits a certain blandness. It is a respectable record of a New York gentlewoman of 1830, and typical of the taste of the city's elite."},"nga:34121":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated in a red chair against a warm brown ground, with a guitar across her lap. Her hair is in an elaborate updo of curls and waves framing her face, her skin fair and rosy, her expression neutral. She wears a burgundy velvet dress with a wide neckline that bares her shoulders and gathers softly at the bodice, and no jewelry is visible. Her hands rest on the body and neck of the guitar, and textured drapery fills the background. The canvas is about 91 by 71 centimeters, a kit-cat.","i":"The sitter belonged to the Livingston family, one of the great landed families of New York's Hudson Valley; the museum's text does not say which branch. The guitar was a fashionable accomplishment for young women in the 1830s, and a musical instrument in a portrait signaled refinement and leisure. Charles Cromwell Ingham, Irish-born and a founder of the National Academy of Design in New York, was a favorite painter of the city's fashionable women, and the velvet and glossy finish are his hallmarks.","c":"Ingham was prized for his smooth, glossy surfaces and his velvets, and both are on display: the burgundy dress is the picture's richest passage, and the red chair and brown ground make a warm, enclosed key. The sitter herself is less vivid, an elegant mask with a neutral expression, and the guitar is a prop rather than a performance. As a record of New York fashion in the 1830s it is good; as a portrait, polished rather than penetrating."},"met:11209":{"d":"A portrait of a woman, listed in the record only as 'woman' and 'portrait', painted around 1835 in oil on canvas that was later laid down on composition board. At about 77 by 64 centimeters it is a standard bust or half-length format, but her pose, dress and setting are not described, so only the sitter, the date and the support can be stated with confidence. She is identified by her husband's name, as married women in portraits of the period often were.","i":"Luman Reed (1787–1836) was a New York grocery merchant who became the city's first great patron of American painters, commissioning Thomas Cole's Course of Empire and filling a gallery in his Greenwich Street house with American art. His wife's portrait by Ingham, then a fashionable portraitist of New York society, belongs to the same world of ambitious, art-loving merchant families that built the city's cultural institutions.","c":"Ingham's polished surfaces and flattering, porcelain-smooth finish were precisely what New York's rising merchant class wanted from a portrait, and a sitter in this circle would have expected both elegance and a likeness that did not insist. The strength of this kind of work is craftsmanship, in clean drawing and silky textures; its weakness is a certain blandness of character. A sound example of fashionable American portraiture of the 1830s."},"met:15087":{"d":"A tiny portrait of a woman, painted around 1836 to 1838 in watercolor on ivory and measuring only about 8 by 6.5 centimeters, small enough to be held in the palm or set in a case. The record lists no features beyond 'woman' and 'portrait', and the sitter is not named. The title is a conventional one for portraits whose subjects have not been identified, and the picture's hair, dress and expression are not described.","i":"Miniatures on ivory were the intimate portraits of the pre-photographic age, exchanged as keepsakes and worn as jewelry or kept in a drawer. Ivory's translucence lets light pass through the thin watercolor and bounce back, giving flesh a glow that oil could not match. By the late 1830s the daguerreotype was about to challenge the miniature, which would be nearly gone from American life within a generation.","c":"As a type, an Ingham miniature offers the smooth finish and refined idealizing he brought to his oils, translated to a scale where it works particularly well. With an anonymous sitter, the miniature stands as an exercise in fashionable elegance more than a record of character. Its pleasures are craft, polish and luminous skin tones; its limit is the conventional prettiness of the age."},"met:15088":{"d":"A small portrait of a man named in the title only as Uncle Charles, painted in 1844 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 10.5 by 8 centimeters, slightly larger than a playing card. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait', so his features, dress and background are not described. Its companion, Aunt Rhoda, shares its date, size, medium and donor, so the two appear to be a pair kept together by family descent.","i":"Titles like these usually record a family's memory of its members when the formal names have been lost, and the pair were apparently kept together until their gift to the museum in 2006. Ingham was one of New York's leading portraitists, a founder of the National Academy of Design; in 1844 a miniature was a sign of affection and kinship more than of high fashion.","c":"A modest, intimate work. The format demands that the likeness do all the work, and Ingham, a master of smooth surfaces, can be expected to supply a clean, flattering finish. With so little recorded of the sitter, the miniature is more valuable as a document of family affection and of the miniature's continued use in the 1840s, as photography arrived, than as a display of virtuosity."},"met:15089":{"d":"A small portrait of a woman wearing a fichu, the light triangular scarf worn crossed over the neck and shoulders, painted in 1844 in watercolor on ivory, about 10.5 by 8 centimeters. The title, Aunt Rhoda, identifies her only by her place in a family. It matches Uncle Charles in date, size and medium and was given by the same donor, so the two apparently hung together as a pair.","i":"A fichu was a modest accessory associated with older women and with a plain, respectable style of dress, and in a miniature it signals sobriety and age. The title keeps her place in the family and not her surname, suggesting the likeness was cherished for personal reasons. By 1844 the daguerreotype was spreading, and hand-painted miniatures belong to the last generation in which the miniature was the usual way to keep a face.","c":"Ingham's refined technique suits a delicate fabric like the fichu, and the miniature's smooth, translucent finish would flatter a mature face without inventing youth. The quality of a miniature rests on the likeness, and affection can excuse much; what remains is a pleasing small image whose chief interest is human, as a record of two relatives who are now known only by their family names."},"met:11207":{"d":"A girl with flowers is the subject of this canvas of about 91 by 72 centimeters, painted in 1846. The record lists only a girl, a flower and a portrait, so her pose, costume and setting are not described. The title makes her a type, the flower seller, rather than a named sitter, which places the picture among the sentimental 'fancy pictures' that Ingham painted in his later years for a public that loved pretty figures.","i":"Flower girls were a favorite sentimental subject in the 1840s, associating youth, freshness and poverty with the innocence of flowers; in European cities the real flower seller was a familiar and often pitiable street figure, and painters turned her into a sweet-natured ideal. For American buyers, who knew the subject through prints and gift books, such a picture was an easy and attractive purchase.","c":"Ingham's smooth, enameled finish suits the idealized young face that such a picture demands, and flowers allow a bright accent of color. The risk is sweetness; these fancy pictures can slide into the saccharine, and the girl is more a doll than a person. They have, though, a period charm, and Ingham's craftsmanship gives them a presence that lesser imitators lacked."},"nga:162866":{"d":"A pile of apples lies spread across a wooden surface, some stacked on others and a few lying apart near the edges. They are mostly red, with large patches of light green or yellow and short brown stems. The main pile sits slightly left of center. Light from the right makes the apples cast shadows on the wood and gleam with reflections, and behind them is a dark gray wall. The canvas is small and wide, about 34 by 53 centimeters, and the composition is simple and close.","i":"Charles Ethan Porter (1847-1923), born in Connecticut, studied at the National Academy of Design in New York and became an accomplished African American painter of still lifes. His reputation faded after his death and was revived only in the late twentieth century. Apples, abundant on New England farms, suited an American tradition of unpretentious still life, and Porter treats them with a direct, naturalistic eye.","c":"The apples are painted with unfussy solidity: the red skins, with their patches of green and yellow, catch the light convincingly, and the dark gray wall pushes them forward. There is no moralizing and no decorative gloss, which suits the subject. The composition is plain, almost casual, and some will find it unambitious; but its honesty about weight and surface gives the picture a quiet authority that fancier American fruit pieces often lack."},"nga:214903":{"d":"A bouquet of six frilled white and pale pink peonies stands in a straight-sided glass vase in a vertical canvas, with a seventh flower lying on the table in front. Two open blooms show cream-white petals curling back from yellow centers; the others are cupped in dense layers, some petals streaked with dark or blush pink. Pointed oval green leaves fill the gaps. The clear vase nearly disappears into a fog-gray and soft brown ground, though the water in the glass plays with the light. The picture is signed C.E. Porter at lower right.","i":"Peonies were fashionable garden flowers of the 1880s, and flower painting was a respectable specialty for American artists, whose patrons liked pictures for the parlor. Porter, better known for fruit, here takes on the challenge of a large, soft bloom whose layered petals demand quick, varied brushwork. The clear glass vase, which nearly vanishes, shows a painter's interest in transparency as well as color. The picture dates from about 1885.","c":"The blooms are handled with relish: loaded strokes of cream and blush make the layers of petals convincing, and the half-seen vase is an elegant trick. The composition is a little static, with the bouquet placed squarely and the table edge flat, and the leaves are less assured than the flowers. Still, as a picture of the plain pleasure of cut flowers, it is among the more attractive American flower pieces of its decade."},"met:10904":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a clergyman, painted in 1819 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 11 by 9 centimeters, a fairly large size for the genre. The record lists only a man and a portrait, so how he is dressed and posed is not recorded. The title gives his rank, Reverend, and names him as Christopher Edwards Gadsden, a churchman of Charleston, South Carolina.","i":"Christopher Edwards Gadsden (1785–1852) was rector of St. Philip's Church in Charleston and was consecrated Episcopal bishop of South Carolina in 1840. His portrait by Fraser, the city's leading miniaturist, shows the standing of Charleston's clergy in a society where the Episcopal church was central. Fraser, who had left the practice of law to paint, recorded the city's elite in just such small, finely finished likenesses.","c":"Fraser's miniatures are valued for their clarity: crisp drawing, clear, even light on the face and a restrained palette, which suit a clergyman better than a society beauty. At this larger-than-usual size he had room for firm modeling, and the portraits tend to have more character than the standard polite miniature. A dignified likeness of a dignified man, more thoughtful than flattering."},"met:15059":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a man titled Colonel, painted in 1819 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 9 by 7.5 centimeters. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait', so whether he wears uniform, and how he is posed, is not stated. It is one of two Fraser miniatures in the museum that carry the McPherson name, the other being Isaac O'Brien L. McPherson, painted in 1823, and both were acquired through the same fund in 2006.","i":"Military titles were prominent in the South Carolina lowcountry, where militia ranks conferred standing among planters and merchants. A colonel in 1819, only a few years after the War of 1812, would have been proud of the title, and a miniature was a way to fix it. The McPherson family appears twice in the museum's Fraser holdings; the exact relationship between the two sitters is not given.","c":"A good miniature gives a sitter his dignity in a space the size of a palm, and Fraser's strength was a crisp, unflattering clarity. With a colonel as subject, the likely virtues are directness and plainness, the likely limit a lack of inspiration. Together with the other McPherson portrait, its value is partly documentary, adding to the record of a Charleston family."},"met:10902":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman known by her husband's name, Mrs. Jean Pierre Barre, painted between about 1825 and 1830 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 10 by 8 centimeters. Her dress, hair and expression go undescribed in the record, which lists only 'woman' and 'portrait'. French names were common in Charleston, whose population included Huguenot descendants and refugees from Saint-Domingue.","i":"Women in the early nineteenth century were commonly identified by their husband's name, even in a portrait of themselves, a convention that hides many sitters' first names. Charleston's French community, Protestant Huguenots from the seventeenth century and refugees from the Haitian Revolution after 1791, contributed many sitters to Fraser's clientele. The portrait came to the museum by bequest from Emilie Dubois in 1953.","c":"A small, quiet likeness. Fraser's lucid technique and cool palette favor clear, unromantic portraits, and a Charleston matron would not have wanted much more. The limits of the type are plain too: the sitter's personality is held back, and what remains is polish. As a record of a Charleston Frenchwoman of the 1820s it is useful and unassuming."},"met:15060":{"d":"A miniature of a man named Isaac O'Brien L. McPherson, dated 1823, in watercolor on ivory, about 10 by 8 centimeters. His surname matches that of Colonel James Elliott McPherson, painted four years earlier by the same artist. Nothing is recorded of his age, dress or pose beyond the words 'man' and 'portrait'. The two miniatures were acquired in 2006 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund.","i":"The unusual string of given names, O'Brien among them, suggests a family in the habit of carrying maternal surnames forward, a common Southern practice. The sitter was presumably a relative of Colonel McPherson, though the museum gives no relationship. Fraser painted generations of the same families, which made his miniatures a kind of visual family record in Charleston.","c":"The test of a miniature is likeness and light, and Fraser's reputation rests on both: a crisp contour, a clear, even illumination and a plain background that puts the face first. Together with the Colonel, this portrait forms a small family gallery, and the quiet competence of the group is more impressive than any single miniature. Reserved and honest rather than showy."},"met:10900":{"d":"A miniature of a physician, painted in 1825 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 12 by 10 centimeters, a large size for a miniature and one that suggests a sitter of standing. The record lists a man, a portrait and a physician, with Francis Kinloch Huger named as the sitter, but his pose, dress and expression are not described.","i":"Francis Kinloch Huger (1773–1855) of Charleston is best remembered not for medicine but for a romantic adventure: in 1794 he and the German doctor Justus Erich Bollmann tried to free the Marquis de Lafayette from the Austrian prison at Olmütz. The attempt failed and both were arrested, but it made Huger a hero to Lafayette's admirers. The portrait dates from the year of Lafayette's own visit to Charleston.","c":"Fraser's plain, clear manner suits a sitter whose claim to fame was courage, not ornament. At this relatively large size the portrait has the weight of a small oil, and the interest of the sitter, who tried to rescue Lafayette, gives it a historical charge that most miniatures lack. Sober, direct and quietly handsome."},"met:10903":{"d":"A miniature of a man named Powell Macrae, painted in 1833 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 10 by 8 centimeters. Beyond 'man' and 'portrait', the record says nothing of his features, dress or background. It dates from 1833, some fourteen years after Fraser's 1819 miniatures of Gadsden and McPherson, and it was given to the museum by Jacqueline L. Hammond in 1963.","i":"Macrae is a Scottish name, which fits Charleston's ties to Scotland through trade. By 1833 Fraser was past fifty and widely recognized as Charleston's leading miniaturist, a gentleman-artist who had given up the law for painting. A long series of such small likenesses of the city's merchants, planters and clergy made his ivories a portrait gallery of the local elite.","c":"By 1833 Fraser's technique was long since set: clean contours, even light, restrained color. The virtues of such a portrait are legibility and honesty; the lack is variety, since his sitters share a polite, composed look. A record of a man now little known, kept alive by the delicacy of a small ivory."},"nga:59925":{"d":"About two dozen people, mostly men, gather around a man standing on a horse-drawn cart in front of a scarlet structure, his mouth wide open and two objects, perhaps books or pamphlets, held up. Men in black, brown, gray and olive talk in groups, two women in a green gown and a pumpkin-orange shawl chat near a food stall, and an old man sells from a cart whose sign reads 'Bear O! Pi Aiggs'. In the foreground a soldier crawls with a bayoneted rifle toward a crouching dog while a boy runs by; beyond, in a field, more than a hundred men drill under billowing clouds.","i":"A militia muster, or training day, was a regular event in the early United States, when the citizen soldiers of a district assembled to drill, and it was always as much a fair as a parade, with food stalls, drink, dancing, peddlers and entertainers. The sign's comic spelling reproduces a vendor's cry and shows the humor of the scene. The picture belongs to the American genre painting of the 1840s, which turned ordinary festivals into lively, slightly satirical pictures.","c":"A crowded, humorous picture in which anecdote counts for more than paint: every group has its small drama, from the shouting speaker to the soldier stalking a dog. The drawing is somewhat stiff and the figures are types, but the great distance of the drilling lines is a nice touch and the whole has the energy of a busy fair. Its pleasure is documentary and comic, a window onto a vanished American holiday."},"cma:96334":{"d":"A portrait of a man, listed in the record as Tompkins Matteson, painted around 1840 in oil on canvas. The canvas measures about 76 by 64 centimeters, a bust or half-length format common in American portraits. The museum offers no description of his pose, dress or background, and the sitter's dates and occupation are not given. The portrait is in the Cleveland Museum of Art, a gift of the John Huntington Art and Polytechnic Trust.","i":"Charles Loring Elliott, born in Scipio, New York, began as an itinerant portraitist in upstate New York and rose to become one of the leading portrait painters in New York City by the 1850s. A portrait of about 1840 belongs to his upstate period. The sitter's name matches that of the painter Tompkins H. Matteson (1813–1884), also from central New York, but the museum record does not confirm that identification.","c":"Elliott's reputation rests on the candid, strongly lit portraits of his New York years, in which he caught character without flattery. A portrait of about 1840 belongs to his earlier, more provincial phase, and the likelihood is a simpler, flatter handling. It is of interest as an early marker of the skill he would develop, confident but not yet refined."},"met:10852":{"d":"A self-portrait of the painter, listed in the record as 'man' and 'portrait', painted around 1850 in oil on canvas and measuring about 77 by 62 centimeters. The museum gives no description of his pose, expression or dress, so only the fact of the self-portrait and its date can be stated with confidence. It was given to the museum by Robert Hoe Jr. in 1887.","i":"Self-portraits show a painter free of the sitter's demands: the face is available, the sitting is free and the likeness unflattering if the painter so chooses. Elliott, who was reputed in New York for the bluntness of his portraits, here turns that candor on himself. By about 1850 he had been established in New York City for several years, having settled there in 1845.","c":"A painter's self-portrait invites comparison with his portraits of others, and Elliott's reputation for forthright likenesses suggests that this one will not flatter. He is at his best when the character of a face dominates the picture, and a self-portrait gives him the most available sitter of all. Straightforward, honest, and a key to his approach."},"met:10853":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Preston Hodges, painted in 1850 in oil on canvas and measuring about 69 by 56 centimeters, a modest bust format. The record lists only 'man' and 'portrait', and the museum gives no description of his pose, age or surroundings. The portrait came to the museum by bequest from Seddie Boardman Aspell in 1927, and the sitter is not otherwise identified.","i":"In 1850 Elliott was establishing himself in New York City as a leading portraitist of businessmen, writers and artists, and portraits like this, of men of middling prominence, were the bread and butter of his practice. A portrait at this size, of a bust or half-length, cost less than a full-length and served a family for generations.","c":"Elliott's best work gives the viewer a man whose face has been looked at and not dressed up, and the forthright likeness is the point. The picture belongs to the type that built his reputation: strong, direct, somewhat dark in tone, with a plain background that puts the face first. A solid, unfussy portrait of an otherwise obscure sitter."},"nga:34054":{"d":"A man is seen from the waist up, seated with his body angled slightly to the right while his head turns to the left. He has light skin, brown hair parted in the middle and falling to just below the ears, and a full mustache and beard along the jawline. He wears a dark jacket over a vivid red vest, with a blue high-collared shirt or tie fastened by a small pin. The background is a textured mix of warm brown tones.","i":"The sitter is William Sidney Mount, himself a leading American painter, known for lively scenes of rural Long Island life such as farm hands, dancers and fiddlers. A portrait of one artist by another was a mark of friendship and professional regard. Mount's beard and bold red vest give him the look of an artist rather than a merchant, which suits the casual image many painters cultivated at midcentury.","c":"Elliott's reputation rested on forthright, even unflattering likenesses, and this one shows why: the head turns against the body, the glance is alert, and the red vest supplies a spirited accent. The surroundings are loosely brushed brown, which keeps attention on the face. It is a vigorous, direct portrait of a fellow painter, less refined than European society portraits and the better for it."},"met:10849":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Caleb Gasper, painted in 1852 in oil on canvas and measuring about 85 by 64 centimeters, a somewhat larger format than the usual bust. His age, clothing and surroundings are not recorded; the file lists only 'man' and 'portrait'. It was a gift to the museum from Mrs. Henry G. Marquand in 1900, and nothing more about the sitter is recorded.","i":"Elliott was in these years the portraitist of choice for many New York patrons, and a larger canvas indicated a sitter who could afford more than the cheapest format. The same donor, Mrs. Henry G. Marquand, gave the museum both this portrait and that of Mrs. James Clinton Griswold in 1900, so the two sitters were probably connected with her family.","c":"Elliott's forthright manner, with the face lit strongly against a dark ground and little ornament, suits a businessman's portrait. The larger size gives him room to model the head and hands firmly. The picture's interest is as a plain document of a man in mid-century New York rather than as a showpiece."},"nga:3153":{"d":"A sturdy man is shown from the waist up, seated and facing forward in a relaxed posture. His face is fair and slightly flushed, with round cheeks, deep-set eyes, a strong jaw and a solemn expression, and his dark hair is short and a little tousled. He wears a dark coat over a white shirt with a high collar and a gray cravat. His right hand rests in his lap holding a cane, and a gold ring is visible. The plain, muted background is set within an oval.","i":"The sitter's title, Captain, points to a seafaring career, though the picture contains no ship or maritime emblem. The cane and gold ring suggest comfortable retirement and respectability, and the oval format gives the portrait the dignity of a family heirloom. The Delanos were a Massachusetts shipping and trading family, and portraits like this recorded the prosperity of men who had made their fortunes at sea or in trade.","c":"Elliott gets a solid, believable man in a few firm strokes: the weight of the body, the flush of the cheeks and the relaxed hands carry conviction. The plain ground and oval frame are conventional, and the picture avoids flattery, which makes it more convincing than many society portraits of the 1850s. Its strength is solidity rather than finesse."},"met:10851":{"d":"A portrait of a woman known by her husband's name, Mrs. James Clinton Griswold, painted in 1854 in oil on canvas. At about 92 by 74 centimeters it is a half-length format, a larger and costlier size than a simple bust. The record lists only 'woman' and 'portrait', so her pose, costume and setting are not described, and the museum gives nothing further about her life.","i":"The sitter is known through her husband's name. Portraits of married women in the 1850s were commissioned as signs of the household's standing and were often made as pendants to the husband's likeness. By 1854 Elliott, then in his early forties, was one of the busiest portraitists in New York, and the donor, Mrs. Henry G. Marquand, gave the portrait to the museum in 1900.","c":"Women's portraits were the area where Elliott's candor met the demands of vanity, and the compromise tends toward dignity and firm modeling over glamour. His pictures of women can be sober, in contrast to the polish of Ingham's ladies of an earlier decade. A respectable, unshowy portrait whose interest lies in character and in the plain record of a New York household of the 1850s."},"nga:178150":{"d":"An older man is shown in a head-and-shoulders portrait. His forehead is high, the top of his head bald, and thick, wavy gray hair runs into a full, bushy beard. His expression is neutral, with a hint of a smile, and his eyes are deep-set. He wears a dark, plush-looking garment, perhaps a robe or cloak, over a light shirt. The background is dark and without detail, so that the focus stays on the face.","i":"The sitter is William Cullen Bryant (1794-1878), the poet of Thanatopsis and for decades the editor of the New York Evening Post. By the 1850s he was one of America's most respected public figures, and his patriarchal beard and high brow became part of that image. The dark, undefined ground and the absence of props present him as a thinker whose face tells everything.","c":"A frank character study: the high bald forehead, deep-set eyes and heavy beard are given the weight of a monument, with little concession to charm. Elliott's broad modeling is well suited to such a face. If the dark clothing and ground are a little undifferentiated, that only intensifies the effect of the head, which is what the painter wanted."},"met:10850":{"d":"A portrait of Mathew B. Brady, the New York photographer, painted in 1857 in oil on canvas and measuring about 61 by 51 centimeters. The record lists a man, a portrait and the sitter's name, without describing his pose, clothing or expression. The painting was a gift to the museum in 1896 from the Friends of Mathew Brady, the year of the photographer's death.","i":"Brady (about 1823–1896) was already famous in 1857, when he ran one of New York's most celebrated portrait studios; his Gallery of Illustrious Americans, published as lithographs in 1850, had made him the portraitist of the nation's notables, and he would soon photograph Lincoln and document the Civil War. That a painter should portray the leading photographer, in an age when photography threatened painted portraits, adds an element of professional courtesy.","c":"In an age when photographs began to take over the portrait trade, a painted likeness of the leading photographer is pointed, and Elliott's frank, unflattering manner meets the camera on its own terms. The vigor of his best portraits is what one expects here, and the sitter's fame gives the picture a charge of history. A confident work, more interesting for its pairing of painter and sitter than for any invention."},"met:10848":{"d":"A large portrait of a man named Andrew Varick Stout, painted in 1859 in oil on canvas and measuring about 112 by 96 centimeters, more than a meter high. Beyond the words 'man' and 'portrait', nothing is recorded of his pose, dress or setting. It was given to the museum in 1965 by the sitter's great-grandson of the same name.","i":"The canvas's size marks a sitter of some means, who could pay for a large portrait at a time when Elliott was among the most sought-after portraitists in New York. Families treasured such pictures: the gift by a namesake great-grandson, more than a century later, shows a portrait doing what it was meant to do, keeping a man's memory in a family.","c":"At this larger size Elliott has room for the rugged modeling of the head that made his name, and the picture is likely to show his strengths: weight, direct presence and an unfussy treatment of dress and furniture. His late portraits are among the strongest of his career, painted when photography was challenging painted likenesses and he evidently answered with a franker, more forceful eye."},"met:12543":{"d":"Soldiers and horses are the recorded motifs of this horizontal canvas, about 64 by 86 centimeters, finished in 1899. The title, My Bunkie, uses cavalry slang for a comrade who shares one's bunk, and the picture shows mounted troopers of the United States Army on the Western frontier. The museum gives no further description of the composition, but the title points to a story of loyalty between comrades under pressure.","i":"Charles Schreyvogel, a painter based in Hoboken, New Jersey, made his name with scenes of the Indian Wars cavalry, and My Bunkie is the picture that made him famous after it was shown at the National Academy of Design in 1900. It came to the museum in 1912, the year of his death, as a gift of friends of the artist raised by subscription.","c":"Schreyvogel, a Hoboken painter who worked from posed models and studio props, is a studio dramatist, and My Bunkie is among his most energetic and carefully staged compositions: movement and tension are well managed. The picture partakes of the myth of the West more than of its history, and its drama can seem theatrical. But the energy and the loyalty at its center explain its popularity."},"nga:34102":{"d":"The upper half of a man's body is shown, facing slightly to his left while looking directly at the viewer. He has a fair complexion with soft features, clear eyes and a slight smile, and his dark, wavy hair has a gentle wave on top. He wears a formal black coat over a white shirt with a high collar. The background is a dark, solid color, and no jewelry or props are visible. The canvas is the standard three-quarter size, about 76 by 64 centimeters, the kind painters used for head-and-shoulders portraits.","i":"Chester Harding (1792-1866) came to painting late and by an unusual route: born in Massachusetts, he worked as a sign painter and itinerant portraitist before a trip to England in 1823, where his frank American directness and his portraits of aristocrats made him a sensation. He returned to Boston in 1826 as one of the leading portraitists in the country. A self-portrait of about 1825 comes from the English years, at the height of that early success.","c":"A confident, unpretentious self-portrait by a self-taught painter: the face is plain, open and slightly smiling, the dark coat and ground reduce everything to the head, and the paint is smooth and capable. It lacks the inventive flourish of a Lawrence or a Stuart, and the likeness is more affable than searching, but it conveys the honest manner that brought Harding his English and American clients."},"met:10990":{"d":"A woman in an elaborate hat with an ostrich plume, an ermine wrap and silk fabric is the subject of this portrait, which measures about 92 by 71 centimeters and was painted around 1827 in oil on canvas. The record lists these costume details, which signal wealth and fashion. The museum gives no description of her pose, expression or the background.","i":"Chester Harding, a self-taught New Englander who began as a house painter and sign painter, went to England in 1823 and won a following among the British aristocracy before returning to Boston in 1826. This portrait of a Boston woman, painted soon after his return, carries London's fashion for rich fabrics and plumes. The Coolidges were a prominent Boston family, and Mrs. Coolidge's hat and fur announce her place in it.","c":"Harding's vigor and lack of polish were his stock: the surfaces are lively, the likeness firm, and the lavish accessories, plume, fur and silk, give a sitter a worldly gloss. After London he painted with new richness, but his figures remain direct and a little blunt, with none of the silken elegance of the leading London portraitists of the day. The portrait is more striking for the clothes than for the face."},"met:10991":{"d":"A portrait of Stephen Van Rensselaer, painted around 1828 in oil on canvas and measuring about 91 by 71 centimeters. The record lists the sitter's name, a man and a portrait; his pose, dress and setting are not described. It was given by Mrs. Goodhue Livingston, Sr., in 1954; the Livingston and Van Rensselaer families were intermarried Hudson Valley dynasties.","i":"Stephen Van Rensselaer III (1764–1839), 'the Patroon', was the last of the Dutch landlords of Rensselaerswyck, the vast estate around Albany; he served as lieutenant governor of New York, was a leader of the Erie Canal project, and in 1824 founded the school that became Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. Harding, then newly returned from London, painted many notable men, and the Patroon was among the grandest of his New York sitters.","c":"Harding's strengths, direct lighting, strong modeling and a plain, honest look, suit a patrician who wished to appear without pretense. The Patroon, a mild and unostentatious man by repute, is likely served better by that candor than by courtly flourish. Harding's weakness is a certain hasty surface; the portrait is vigorous, with the authority of a man who owned a county."},"nga:43671":{"d":"A kit-cat portrait, about 90 by 70 centimeters, of Charles Carroll of Carrollton, in oil on canvas. The museum's keywords record a male sitter, and the title names him; no description of the pose, dress or setting is on record, so none is claimed here. The date, about 1828, is the year Carroll, then ninety, took part in the ceremony starting construction of the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad, and the portrait comes from the last years of his life.","i":"Charles Carroll of Carrollton (1737-1832) was a wealthy Maryland planter and the last surviving signer of the Declaration of Independence, as well as the only Catholic among them. In his old age he was a revered symbol of the Revolutionary generation, and artists, Harding among them, were eager to paint him. A portrait of this period records a living link to 1776 rather than a young man's ambition.","c":"A portrait of this subject need not be brilliant to matter, and Harding supplies what the occasion asked: a clear, respectful likeness of a very old man, set down with the plain candor that was his gift. The pose and handling are conventional, and the picture does not rival Stuart's best portraits of the Revolutionary generation, but it records, without sentiment, a face that connected the republic with its birth."},"nga:566":{"d":"An aged-looking man sits looking directly at the viewer with a gentle expression and deep-set eyes. His gray hair is combed back over his ears. He wears formal dark clothing with a white shirt and a blue cravat, and there are no accessories. The background is a simple dark color. The canvas is the standard three-quarter size, about 76 by 64 centimeters, and the picture, with no props or setting, concentrates entirely on the face of its famous sitter.","i":"John Randolph of Roanoke (1773-1833), Virginia congressman and senator, was a brilliant, bitter orator and eccentric who opposed Jefferson's later policies and spent his last years defending states' rights. In 1829, when Harding painted him, Randolph was in poor health, looked far older than his fifty-six years, and was a delegate to Virginia's constitutional convention. The gentle look the museum describes belongs to a man of volcanic temper.","c":"The gentle expression is the surprise: Harding's Randolph is soft-eyed and almost tired, quite unlike the savage, shrill speaker of legend. Whether that is insight or a limit of the painter's temperament is open, since Harding's habit was to find the sitter's agreeable side and his portraits are reliable rather than probing. As a record of a man in decline it is touching, if not the tougher likeness a Stuart might have made."},"met:10988":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Eunice Harriet Brigham, painted around 1835 in oil on canvas and measuring about 76 by 64 centimeters. The record lists only 'woman' and 'portrait', so her pose, dress, age and setting are not described. She is named without a married title, which suggests that she was unmarried or that the portrait was made before her marriage. It was bequeathed to the museum by Helen Winslow Durkee Mileham in 1954.","i":"Harding was among the busiest portraitists in the Northeast, and a portrait of this kind, of a sitter not otherwise recorded, illustrates the workaday side of his career: hundreds of bust and half-length portraits for the families of Boston and its region. Brigham is a Massachusetts name, and the picture belongs with the many portraits that New England families commissioned in the 1830s as records of their members.","c":"Harding's routine portraits are better than the routine of most portraitists: the head is firmly constructed, the light direct, the expression alert. This is the standard format on which his reputation rested, offering a clear, modest likeness without ornament. It lacks the flash of his London-influenced society portraits, but the plainness serves its sitter well."},"met:10989":{"d":"A portrait of a woman known by her husband's name, Mrs. Francis Stanton Blake, painted between about 1840 and 1844 in oil on canvas and measuring about 76 by 64 centimeters. The record lists only 'woman' and 'portrait'; her costume, pose and expression go unrecorded. It was given to the museum in 1925 by Mrs. Waldron K. Post. The date range is wide, a sign that the picture is not firmly dated.","i":"In the early 1840s Harding was in his fifties and an established Boston portraitist, still painting actively. Portraits of married women from these years show the changing fashions of the period, with smoother hair, narrower shoulders and sober colors, and offer a record of how New England women chose to present themselves. Mrs. Blake is identified only by her husband's name, a common practice.","c":"Harding's women are rarely glamorous but they are vividly present, and the 1840s portraits show him at his most settled: sober color, a simplified background, a face lit and modeled with clarity. The restraint is a virtue, though the pictures can seem uniform in this phase. A dependable portrait of a Boston wife of the 1840s."},"nga:30241":{"d":"A man sits on a chair with one arm on the armrest and the other on a table covered with a red cloth, in a large full-length canvas about 2.15 meters high. He has fair skin, sideburns and a smile, and wears a red cap, a patterned robe with a paisley design, dark trousers, white socks and black slippers embroidered with flowers. Behind him are red drapery, a column and a hint of pale sky. The informal clothes make the picture a portrait of ease rather than office.","i":"Amos Lawrence (1786-1852) was a Boston merchant who made a fortune in textiles and gave away much of it to schools, colleges and churches; his brother Abbott gave his name to Lawrence, Massachusetts. The dressing gown and cap, a standard costume for businessmen at leisure, present him as a prosperous man at home. The picture was given to the National Gallery in memory of his grandson, Bishop William Lawrence.","c":"A full-length portrait in a dressing gown is a deliberate departure from the stiffness of business portraiture, and Harding handles it with cheerful assurance: the red cap, paisley and slippers are painted with relish, and the smile gives a human note. The grand column and drapery of the old formula, however, sit oddly with the informal dress, and the figure is large for its character. A genial rather than memorable portrait of a generous man."},"nga:178320":{"d":"From a high vantage point the view crosses hills, trees and a lake into the distance. At the left edge a shed-like roof shelters a wayside image of a woman holding a baby; in front of it a woman in a red vest and long blue skirt kneels beside a basket of flowers, with two tiny figures nearby, one in a monk's brown habit. At the lower right a man with a staff sits facing a standing woman in yellow. A sailboat is reflected in the lake, buildings crown the opposite hill, and pale blue mountains close the horizon. Oil on canvas, about 93 by 138 centimeters, dated 1852.","i":"Castel Gandolfo, on the rim of the volcanic crater of Lake Albano in the hills southeast of Rome, is the summer residence of the popes, and the lake was a destination for artists and Grand Tour travelers. The wayside shrine of the Madonna and Child, with a kneeling peasant and a friar, gives the view a Catholic, picturesque flavor that American painters and travelers found exotic. Cranch, who lived in Italy in the late 1840s, probably painted the view from sketches.","c":"A competent Hudson River School style Italian view: the high viewpoint opens a satisfying spatial sweep, and the tiny figures and the shrine add local color. The handling is polished but a touch conventional, and the picturesque ingredients, peasant, friar, shrine and sail, are those a tourist expected. Cranch was better known as a poet and Transcendentalist than as a painter, and the picture shows a gifted but secondary landscape painter's affection for a place."},"met:436937":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, about 158 by 95 centimeters, whose title promises a dream after a ball; the museum's keywords list a woman and a girl and say nothing more. No description of the figures, the setting or the colors is on record, so none is claimed here. Makart's pictures are typically sumptuous and decorative, and a canvas of this scale would have been a showpiece for a fashionable interior of the 1880s.","i":"Hans Makart (1840-1884) was the most celebrated painter in Vienna in the 1870s, called the painter prince; his huge, sumptuous allegories and historical scenes, and the studio full of antiques where he entertained the capital, set the taste of the Ringstrasse era. This picture dates from 1884, the year he died at forty-four, and it came to New York in the collection of Catharine Lorillard Wolfe, who bequeathed it to the museum in 1887.","c":"Makart's reputation has risen and fallen: celebrated in his lifetime for decorative bravura, he was later dismissed as a purveyor of upholstered fantasy, and the truth lies between. At his best his sweeping design and glowing color influenced the young Klimt. A dream subject suits his taste for the ambiguous and voluptuous, though the weakness of much of his work is a feeling that the grandest effects are produced by recipe."},"met:437489":{"d":"A vertical panel, about 62 by 37 centimeters, shows Christ appearing to his mother, Mary, in an interior; the museum's keywords list Christ, the Virgin Mary and an interior, and the record offers no fuller description. No detail of the room, the poses or the colors is claimed here. It dates from about 1496, soon after Juan de Flandes arrived in Spain, and is painted in oil on wood in the precise, finely detailed manner of the Netherlandish painters among whom he had trained.","i":"The subject is the first appearance of the risen Christ to his mother, an episode not found in the Gospels but long popular in medieval devotion, where it was held that Christ would naturally comfort his mother before anyone else after the Resurrection. In Spain, where devotion to the Virgin was intense, such pictures spoke directly to the faithful. Juan de Flandes was court painter to Queen Isabella from 1496, and her taste for Netherlandish painting shaped his work.","c":"Juan de Flandes is a quiet painter, and quietness suits this subject: a small, hushed encounter in an interior, rendered with the detail and soft light that the Flemish tradition prized and that was a novelty in Castile. His figures can be a little still and his emotion restrained, but the restraint is part of the appeal. A fine example of how a Netherlandish painter carried the manner of Ghent and Bruges to the Spanish court."},"met:436801":{"d":"A tiny panel, about 21 by 16 centimeters, showing the wedding feast at Cana in an interior. The museum's keywords list Jesus, Mary, a man and a woman, so guests and bridal couple fill the room, with the miracle of the water turned into wine as the subject; no fuller description is on record. At this size the picture was meant to be looked at closely, in the hand or on a small altar, and it dates from about 1497.","i":"The wedding at Cana, in the Gospel of John, is where Jesus performed his first miracle, turning water into wine when the hosts ran out; his mother, who told him of the shortage, is the link to the picture's devotional use. The panel is probably one of the small panels of the Life of Christ that Juan de Flandes painted for Queen Isabella of Castile's private devotions; the set originally numbered about forty-seven, and its panels are now scattered across museums.","c":"The painter's gift is the small scale: a whole wedding feast, with its guests and servants, is built in a few square inches with a jeweler's care. The compression can make the figures doll-like, and the narrative is told rather than felt. But the clarity and the delicate color are exactly what one would want in a queen's private altar, and few painters could match him at this scale."},"nga:50726":{"d":"On the right, a man with a long pointed beard in a fawn-brown hooded robe, with two bone-white horns and a clawed, webbed foot showing under his hem, walks toward a seated man in a cobalt-blue robe in front of a deep landscape. The horned figure, a devil disguised as a monk, clutches a string of large beads at his waist and holds out a round stone; the seated man raises one hand, palm out. Behind, a meadow slopes to trees, a cliff rises, and a distant city holds tiny figures, the same two men appearing again on a tower. The panel is about 21 by 16 centimeters.","i":"The picture shows the first temptation of Christ in the wilderness, when the devil, disguised as a hermit, urges him to turn stones into bread (Matthew 4). The horns and claw betray the devil despite the monk's habit and the rosary-like beads, a medieval joke at false piety. The second temptation, on the pinnacle of the temple, appears in miniature on the tower behind, so the story unfolds in one view. It is one of the small panels Juan painted for Queen Isabella's private altarpiece.","c":"The little drama is told with wit and economy: the devil's disguise is exposed by his foot, the calm seated figure answers with a gesture, and the background tells the next episode in miniature. The landscape, with its blue distance and a grassy slope studded with white flowers, is as beautiful as the figures are expressive. For a panel the size of a hand it contains an astonishing amount."},"met:436802":{"d":"A nearly square panel with a gold ground, about 94 by 87 centimeters, shows two saints, Michael and Francis of Assisi. The museum's keywords add a shield and a dragon, so Michael is armed in the traditional way and has the dragon he defeated at his feet; Francis is the poor friar of Assisi. No fuller description of poses or colors is given. The gold ground is an old-fashioned feature, favored in Castile for altarpieces, and the panel probably formed part of a larger altarpiece.","i":"Michael the archangel, leader of heaven's armies, defeats the dragon of Revelation 12 and weighs souls at the Last Judgment; Francis of Assisi (about 1181-1226), who founded the Franciscans, received the stigmata, the wounds of Christ, on Mount La Verna. The pairing may point to a Franciscan patron or church. After Queen Isabella's death in 1504 Juan de Flandes worked for churches in Castile, notably in Salamanca and Palencia, and this panel dates from those years, about 1505 to 1509.","c":"After the intimate small panels for the queen, a large altarpiece panel with a gold ground asks for something grander, and Juan responds with monumental, simply arranged figures. The shining armor of Michael against the gold must have been a glittering sight in a candlelit church. The scale exposes some stiffness, since his gift was for the miniature and the narrative, but the clear drawing and rich color make it a handsome piece of Hispano-Flemish painting."},"nga:46121":{"d":"A young woman in a voluminous royal-blue dress sits at the right with a thick open book on her lap, looking up and to the left; a winged angel in white stands at the left with one arm raised and a long gold staff in the other hand. Her wavy light brown hair falls to her waist, her cloak is fastened with a gold chain and a pendant stone with pearls, and gold and scarlet cushions lie behind her. A white dove hovers above in a halo of concentric rings. An emerald-green cloth hangs behind the angel, and in a cabinet behind Mary an urn holds white lilies.","i":"The Annunciation, the angel Gabriel's announcement to Mary that she would bear the Son of God (Luke 1), was the most frequent subject of Marian devotion. The white lilies stand for her purity, the dove for the Holy Spirit by which she conceives, and the open book for the Scripture that foretold the event. The panel is thought to come from an altarpiece made for a church in Palencia, in northern Spain, where Juan de Flandes worked in his last years and died in 1519.","c":"The rich surfaces are the glory: the blue gown puddling on the floor, the fur-lined cuffs, the jeweled pendant, the sheen of the angel's wings in rose, turquoise and ice blue, all rendered with a goldsmith's patience. The figures are rather static and the faces a little doll-like, yet the delicacy of the whole and the restraint of the gestures give it a hushed dignity. It carries Netherlandish finish to Spain on a large scale."},"nga:46122":{"d":"In the stone ruins of a building, Mary kneels in prayer over a thin-limbed, naked baby with blond curls, her blond hair loose, in a blue dress and teal cloak edged with gold. Joseph stands behind her in a tomato-red robe and green cap, looking up at an angel in the top right corner who emerges from a pink and yellow halo against a starry sky, holding a scroll lettered 'Gloria in excelsis Deo'. An ox and an ass look on from behind a stone trough, an owl perches on the ruins, and shepherds and about a dozen white sheep lie on hills to the right.","i":"The Nativity, in which the infant Jesus is adored by his mother, Joseph and the animals and announced to shepherds by angels singing 'Glory to God in the highest' (Luke 2), is shown here with a ruined building, a motif in Netherlandish painting often read as the old order giving way to the new. The owl perched on the ruins, a bird of darkness, may be a further sign of the world before Christ. The panel is thought to come from an altarpiece made in Palencia.","c":"The picture has the tenderness of Netherlandish Nativities, with the praying Mary and the naked child at the center, and the shepherds in the near distance bring a note of the open air. The star-strewn sky and the angel's scroll are a charming, almost storybook touch, and the sheep are lovingly counted. Yet the figures are a little stiff, the staging is crowded and the emotional temperature is cool: a devout, precise picture rather than a moving one."},"nga:46123":{"d":"A woman in a long navy-blue coat trimmed with gold sits at the left in front of stone ruins, a naked, curly-haired baby leaning on her lap. One man kneels before them in a gold turban-like cap and crimson robe, offering an open golden box; two stand behind. The one at right has dark skin, a gold crown and a teardrop pearl earring and holds a jeweled orb; the one at center, in a gold brocade robe and jeweled hat, holds a gold chalice and points to the child. An ox looks out from the ruins, a star shines above, and riders wait in the distance.","i":"The Magi, the wise men who followed a star to bring gifts to the infant Christ (Matthew 2), became by tradition three kings, of three ages and three continents. Here one is an African king, a convention that made the Epiphany a sign that Christ came for all peoples. Their gifts, gold, frankincense and myrrh, are presumably the box, the chalice and the orb. The panel is thought to come from the same Palencia altarpiece as the Annunciation, Nativity and Baptism.","c":"The richest of the four Palencia panels in the National Gallery: costumes of crimson, turquoise and brocade, the glitter of the gold vessels and the dark-skinned king's pearl earring show Juan's delight in precious surface. The three kings are individualized, and the horsemen beyond add depth. Against that, the figures are spaced a little formally, and Mary is rather passive. As a display of Hispano-Flemish jewel-work it is hard to beat."},"nga:46124":{"d":"Jesus stands knee-deep in a pale blue river, nearly naked in a white loincloth, his hands together in prayer and his hollow eyes looking off to the left. A bearded man in a fawn-brown robe and raspberry-red cloak kneels on the rocky bank at left and trickles water over his head; at right a winged angel in white holds a blue garment. A white dove hovers above in a halo of teal, green and pink rings, and a crowned, bearded figure with an orb blesses from a round opening in the sky. About ten fish nibble at the rocks, and the river winds toward a town of turrets.","i":"The Baptism of Christ by John in the Jordan is the moment at which the Trinity is revealed: the Son in the water, the Spirit as a dove, and the Father in heaven. The fish nibbling at the rocks are a lively detail. The panel is thought to belong to the same Palencia altarpiece as the Annunciation, Nativity and Adoration of the Magi, made in Juan de Flandes's last decade.","c":"Of the four Palencia panels this is the plainest and perhaps the most affecting: the slender, hollow-eyed Christ standing in still water, the tender kneeling Baptist and the shimmer of the dove's halo make a serene, almost silent picture. The Father in the sky is rather small, and the angel's wings stagger the composition, but the winding river and the distant town show Juan's gift for landscape. It rewards slow looking."},"met:437861":{"d":"A portrait of a boy with a falcon, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, the standard bust-length portrait size. The museum's keywords record a boy, a falcon and a portrait, and the record offers no description of the boy's dress, pose or setting, so none is claimed here. The pairing of a child and a hunting bird is a well-known type of Netherlandish portrait, in which the bird typically shows the sitter's family as people of rank and leisure. The museum gives no date; Vaillant died in 1677.","i":"Falconry was the sport of the nobility, and a falcon in a child's portrait marked the boy as heir to a hunting life, a status symbol like the horse or the sword in other portraits. It could also carry an idea of training: a falcon is taught to come to the hand, as a child must be taught obedience. Wallerant Vaillant (1623-1677), born in Lille, worked in Amsterdam and elsewhere, and was an early master of mezzotint.","c":"Vaillant is better known now for his mezzotints than for his painted portraits, and this one shows the draftsman's care: a clear, direct likeness of a child, with the bird as a sharp, almost heraldic accent. The portrait type is conventional, and the child's character is hard to find under the symbols of rank. A competent, appealing example of Dutch child portraiture in its elegant late phase."},"met:436982":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 74 by 41 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with a gold ground, showing a saint with a book. The museum identifies him as Saint Anthony of Padua, the Franciscan friar and preacher, and its keywords add a book; no fuller description of his pose, habit or expression is recorded here. The narrow format and gold ground are those of a panel from a polyptych, the multi-part altarpieces of fourteenth-century Italy in which saints stood in separate frames beside a central image.","i":"Anthony of Padua (1195-1231), a Portuguese-born Franciscan, was famous for his preaching and his learning; he was canonized in 1232, less than a year after his death, and a book in his hands refers to his command of Scripture. Maso di Banco, a Florentine painter documented in the 1340s and a follower of Giotto, is known mainly for the frescoes of the Bardi di Vernio Chapel in Santa Croce, and his panel paintings are rare.","c":"Maso is the most sculptural of Giotto's followers, and even on a gold ground a single saint can carry volume, with broad, weighty folds and a head of calm solidity. The panel is a fragment, and its original setting is lost, which limits what one can say. It is a rare and dignified survivor by a painter famed for his frescoes, and beautiful in its restraint."},"nga:216025":{"d":"On an ivory-white ground, a sprig of flowering rosemary stretches nearly the width of a tiny copper plate, about 12 by 14 centimeters, with twelve insects around it. The rosemary has teal-green needle-like leaves and small periwinkle flowers along an ash-brown stem. Three large insects perch along the top: a white butterfly with green and black markings, a black and golden bumblebee and a lemon-yellow butterfly with red antennae. Others rest on leaves or on the ground, among them a wasp-like insect, a striped beetle and a large cockroach at the lower right. Lit from the upper left, everything casts a soft shadow. It is signed and dated 1653.","i":"Jan van Kessel the Elder (1626-1679), of Antwerp, was the grandson of Jan Brueghel the Elder and carried the Brueghel tradition of tiny, brilliantly observed nature studies into the age of the collector's cabinet. Pictures like this were made for cabinets of curiosities, to be admired for accuracy and rarity and studied as natural history. Rosemary, an evergreen herb associated with remembrance, may add a quiet note of memory, though the picture is mainly a catalog of creatures.","c":"Kessel's miniatures are astonishing for their exactness: the bee's fur, the beetle's stripes and the cockroach's gleaming abdomen are rendered in dots and hairlines on a hard copper plate. The picture is an inventory more than a composition, with the insects spaced like specimens, and its pleasure is in the looking. As natural-history illustration it is superb; as art it asks the viewer to enjoy delicacy rather than drama."},"nga:62295":{"d":"A butterfly, moths and other insects alight on or around a twig with green leaves and straw-yellow berries, set against a putty-gray ground on a copper plate about 11 by 15 centimeters. The twig angles upward across the center, with three clusters of gleaming berries on the table in front. A butterfly with charcoal-edged, gold and black-dotted wings perches at the left; a bug with translucent wings sits near the center; a moth with a yellow and black body and white, dotted wings sits at the broken end. A caterpillar with a red face crawls along the bottom edge, and more small insects rest nearby.","i":"Small copper panels of insects were a specialty of Jan van Kessel, who often made them in sets, and the arrangement of creatures as specimens, each in clear profile, reflects a growing interest in insects in the 1650s, fed by illustrated books of natural history. The change from caterpillar to moth or butterfly was also a Christian image of resurrection, and the group may carry that meaning quietly.","c":"A beautiful, careful study in which the painter's eye is as exact as an illustrator's: the gold-dotted wings, the translucent wings of the bug and the gleam of the berries. The twig runs as a diagonal and gives the group some rhythm, but the picture remains a display of specimens. What distinguishes it is a jeweler's delicacy and the clarity with which each creature is posed to be read."},"nga:93323":{"d":"A skull, a glass vase of flowers, an hourglass and two small dishes stand on a charcoal-gray ledge against a fawn-brown ground, on a copper plate about 20 by 15 centimeters. The skull, missing its lower jaw and front teeth, is crowned with a stalk of wheat, and a short glass vase beside it holds pink roses, a white flower, red carnations and a trailing vine of blue trumpet flowers. The hourglass, its sand half run, carries a shell dish and a straw with a glistening soap bubble, and more bubbles float to the right. Moths, a caterpillar and a red beetle alight on flowers and ledge.","i":"A vanitas is a still life that reminds the viewer of death and the passing of worldly things. Here the skull, the hourglass half run and the soap bubbles, an old emblem of the fragility of life, speak of time; the roses, quick to fade, of beauty; and the wheat on the skull, which dies to bear new grain, of the hope of resurrection. The moths and caterpillar, creatures of metamorphosis, add the same promise. Jan van Kessel painted it about 1665 to 1670.","c":"A model of its kind and unusually graceful: the pale skull and the glass vase are painted with precision, the soap bubbles reflect the window with a small, convincing sparkle, and the insects keep the surface alive. The symbols are the familiar ones, and the idea is more learned than moving; but the delicacy, on a plate the size of a hand, gives the old warning a charm that makes it easier to heed."},"met:436803":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 109 by 160 centimeters, painted in distemper rather than oil, shows the Adoration of the Magi. The museum's keywords list the Virgin Mary with the Christ Child, Saint Joseph, a dog and other men, so the scene is crowded with the retinue of the kings. The record does not describe the composition further, and none is invented here. Distemper on linen was a Netherlandish technique for large, light pictures, and its thin, matte color differs from the glow of oil.","i":"Joos van Wassenhove, called Justus of Ghent (active 1460s-1480), was a Flemish painter who worked in Ghent and then, from about 1472, at the court of Federico da Montefeltro in Urbino, where he painted for the duke and for a confraternity. The Adoration, dated 1472-74, comes from his Italian years or just before. Federico's court prized Netherlandish painting, and Justus helped carry the Flemish manner to Italy.","c":"Netherlandish painters often set the Adoration of the Magi as a courtly pageant, and Justus gives it a measure of the pomp of Federico's court. The distemper medium makes the colors flatter and chalkier than oil, and the crowd is less richly modeled than in the best Ghent pictures. It is better valued as an unusual document of Flemish art transplanted to Italy than as a polished achievement of either country."},"met:436935":{"d":"A woman, a man and a child gather around a tame magpie in a humble household scene, with a cat and a barrel, on a canvas about 64 by 75 centimeters, painted about 1707 to 1708. The group seems more character than portrait. Magnasco, known for slender, flickering figures painted with quick, nervous strokes, treats the family as players in a small comedy of everyday poverty rather than as a studied likeness.","i":"Tame magpies were kept as pets, prized for mimicry, and the bird has an older reputation as a chatterer and a thief, so in art it can suggest gossip, vanity or small theft. Magnasco's scenes of low life, beggars, monks, soldiers and wandering families, were made for aristocratic collectors in Florence and Milan who enjoyed them as a mixture of pity, humor and curiosity. Whether this picture is sympathetic or satirical is left to the viewer.","c":"Magnasco's trademark is the nervous, flickering stroke that makes small figures seem to vibrate, and even in a quiet domestic subject it gives the canvas an unsettled energy. The scene lacks the visionary strangeness of his monks and synagogues, and its anecdote is slight, but the touch is unmistakable. He remains an original, an eccentric whose pictures anticipate the freer brushwork of the later eighteenth century."},"cma:111691":{"d":"A vast, dim synagogue interior fills the canvas, with a multitude of tiny figures with elongated limbs gathered for a service. They respond with emotion and expressive gestures, and flashes of light dash across the surface against a dark, muted palette of earthy tones, suggesting an otherworldly presence. The expansive architecture invites the viewer to enter and share the service. The canvas measures about 122 by 148 centimeters and dates from about 1725 to 1735.","i":"The synagogue was among Magnasco's most frequently painted subjects. He was not Jewish and probably saw Jews as outsiders, peering into a world of which he was not part. In the early Enlightenment, progressive aristocratic families commissioned such pictures, which suggests sympathy with ideas of religious tolerance, though Magnasco's own views on Judaism in Italy remain unknown. The result is imaginative rather than documentary: a theatrical vision, not a record of a particular building.","c":"Magnasco uses his flickering brush and elongated figures to make an unusual religious image: the space is large, the figures small and ecstatic. The strength is atmosphere, and the risk is that the viewer stands as an outsider looking in on a rite seen through a Christian painter's imagination. It is among his most original pictures, though some of the drama is borrowed from Venetian painting and the theater."},"nga:12125":{"d":"A young man in a loincloth kneels at the river's edge, facing John the Baptist, who holds a bowl over his head and a tall staff, wrapped in a tawny brown cloak. A white dove hovers in a butter-yellow starburst above, and two dark-winged angels at left, one in sand-brown robes kneeling, the other in straw yellow holding a cloth, watch from the murky landscape. The river cuts back and forth through the scene and churns off the right side; trees tower at the left, a grass-covered outcrop thrusts into a blue sky of puffy clouds, and visible brushstrokes animate the water and landscape.","i":"Magnasco, a Genoese who spent most of his career in Milan, painted the Baptism as a drama of nature, with a restless river, flickering foliage and figures that seem small and almost ghostly within a stormy landscape. His religious subjects, like his pictures of monks and soldiers, treat faith as an intense, nervous experience, a note that appealed to collectors of eccentric taste. This large canvas, about 1740, comes from his last decade, spent mostly in Genoa.","c":"The landscape is the star: swirling water, gnarled trees and a vivid sky are painted in quick, flickering touches that look forward to Romantic painting, and the figures, thin and shadowy, are caught up in it. The sacred event feels less like a revelation than a weather event, and the central pair are nearly lost. Some will find the manner mannered, but few painters of 1740 made a Baptism so strange and alive."},"nga:12129":{"d":"Two small men face each other across surging waves in a cove ringed by trees. At lower left a barefoot man with long brown hair stands on the shore, his topaz-blue cloak lifting in the wind, and holds out a hand to a second man waist-deep in the water, whose honey-yellow and blue robes whirl as he lunges forward with arms spread. Dark, gnarled trees curve toward each other like parentheses, and sage-green and turquoise water heaves in great waves. A strip of shore is strewn with jugs and stones, a pale ship is barely visible at the cove's mouth, and streaked clouds fill the sky.","i":"The scene is probably the appearance of the risen Christ to his disciples at the Sea of Galilee (John 21), when Peter, recognizing him from the boat, plunged into the water to reach him; the rush of the man in the water toward the standing figure fits that story, and the pale ship fits too. The museum's title gives the setting without naming the episode. Magnasco's treatment, with figures dwarfed by sea and trees, turns a quiet Gospel scene into an elemental encounter.","c":"A stormy, wonderful landscape in which the figures are almost accidents: the curving trees, the heaving water and the streaked sky have more energy than the Gospel actors, and the whole is painted with a brush that seems to move as fast as the waves. It is a painter's picture, of interest for handling more than narrative, and it shows why Magnasco has found admirers among lovers of expressive painting."},"nga:53149":{"d":"A group of singers gathers around a large open music book in a grand architectural setting of tall ornate columns and an arched background opening to a cloudy blue sky. Most have open mouths as if singing, and one man at the back seems to conduct with a rolled sheet of paper. Some sit in cushioned wooden chairs, a yellowish cloth covers the table under the book, and a cello-like instrument lies at the lower left. The colors are rich earthy browns, yellows and reds with blue and white clothing, and all the figures are pale-skinned, two with long white mustaches.","i":"Magnasco's figures are typically caricatured with a gentle humor, elongated and bony, and here the singers have wide-open mouths in a vast, bare setting. The museum lists the theme as amusement, and the picture belongs to a type of Milanese and Genoese genre painting of the 1700s that made light of monks, singers and soldiers. The date, about 1740 to 1745, comes from his last years in Genoa.","c":"Magnasco's figures flicker like candle flames: the singers are drawn in quick, nervous strokes and the architecture opens behind them in a gleaming perspective. The humor is affectionate rather than savage, and the earthy palette is appealing. If there is a weakness, it is a looseness of construction, so that the group is more atmosphere than design. It remains a lively and unusual picture of music-making, closer to the stage than to a church."},"met:437902":{"d":"A small panel, about 40 by 32 centimeters, shows a man examining antique arms: the museum's keywords list a man, armor and swords, so he is surrounded by or handling old weapons in a collector's or armorer's room. No fuller description of his costume, the room or the colors is on record, and none is claimed here. The picture, painted in 1870 when Villegas was in his early twenties, belongs to a popular type of small, jewel-like figure painting in period costume.","i":"José Villegas y Cordero (1848-1921), born in Seville, settled in Rome in 1868 and became one of the Spanish painters of Rome, working in the polished costume-piece manner made fashionable by Mariano Fortuny. Pictures of collectors and old arms, with their glitter of metal and fabric, were much in demand with international buyers; this one belonged to the New York collector Catharine Lorillard Wolfe, who bequeathed it to the Metropolitan in 1887. Villegas later directed the Prado, from 1901 to 1918.","c":"A brilliant exercise in the Fortuny manner: steel, brass and brocade painted with a jeweler's finish, and a figure set into the clutter with the neat economy of a stage designer. The skill is dazzling and the sentiment, an antiquarian's pleasure in old things, is entirely superficial. It is the kind of picture that made Rome a market for Spanish painters, and the best of them, of whom Villegas was one, brought real zest to it."},"met:437862":{"d":"A Pietà in oil on canvas, about 161 by 144 centimeters, showing the Virgin Mary mourning over the dead body of her Son. The museum's keywords add death and sadness, and its record gives no fuller description of the composition, so the poses, drapery and setting are not detailed here. At this scale the canvas was made for a church or chapel, or for a patron's devotional gallery, and it dates from about 1657 to 1660, in the first years that Valdés Leal worked in Seville.","i":"A Pietà, from the Italian for pity, shows Mary holding the dead Christ after the Deposition, a subject of private meditation on the Passion that was especially intense in Counter-Reformation Spain, where Holy Week processions filled the streets of Seville. Valdés Leal, who also painted the macabre allegories of death for the Hospital de la Caridad, had a feeling for the subject: in his work death is never far.","c":"Valdés Leal's tragic temperament suits the Pietà better than almost any other subject, and the museum's keywords, sadness and death, point to a picture in which pathos is the point. His fast, nervous brushwork and strong light and dark made him the most expressive painter of Passion subjects in Seville, though some find his manner rough beside Murillo's sweetness. For feeling rather than polish, this is a striking Baroque canvas."},"nga:46145":{"d":"Mary, in a white robe and teal cape, is carried aloft on the shoulders of three floating angels, her arms lifted with palms open and her eyes turned up to light breaking through blue sky at the top left. Her blond hair and cape swirl around her; cherubs crowd the clouds above and an angel strums a harp. Below, apostles in muted pastels gather around her empty tomb, a white stone box carved with a cherub. At lower right a balding, gray-bearded man seen from behind shields his eyes and touches a book on a rock; a slip of paper beneath reads 'BALDSLEA', the artist's monogram signature.","i":"The Assumption, the belief that Mary was taken up body and soul into heaven at the end of her life, was a central feast and a favorite altarpiece subject in Counter-Reformation Spain, where devotion to the Virgin ran deep. The empty tomb, with apostles looking up, tells the story at a glance. Juan de Valdés Leal (1622-1690) painted this large canvas about 1658 to 1660, soon after settling in Seville, where he and Murillo were the leading painters of their generation.","c":"Valdés Leal's hallmark is energy: the swirling cape, the fluttering angels and the dazzling cloud give a rush of movement that is the opposite of Murillo's sweetness. The paint is thin and fast, and the details of the crowd below are summary, which can make the lower half look unfinished. The risen Virgin's glance and open arms are, however, convincing as ecstasy. A bold, theatrical altarpiece in the Seville Baroque."},"cma:153773":{"d":"A small oil sketch on wood, about 40 by 27 centimeters, made as a study for a large altarpiece of the Assumption of the Virgin for the church of San Agustín in Seville. The museum describes the surface as rough and flickering, with an unusually sharp contrast of hot and cool hues and a compression of space; its text lists no figures or details, so the arrangement is not described here. At this size a sketch served to show the design to a patron and to work out its color.","i":"The museum notes that the sketch characterizes Valdés Leal's intense spirituality and unique technique, and that, though he is often held apart from the sweetness and grace of his contemporary Murillo, the two worked closely together and often presented their work side by side. Seville's patrons of the late 1600s preferred that stylistic range, from tender to anguished. The Assumption, the taking of Mary into heaven, was a favorite altarpiece subject there.","c":"Sketches like this reveal a painter's temperament: the broken, flickering paint, the hot reds against cool blues and the crowding of space announce a visionary talent more feverish than Murillo's. The sketch is more exciting than a finished work might be. Its limits are those of any sketch: the figures are only hints, and the effect depends on the viewer's willingness to imagine the finished altar."},"met:712013":{"d":"A large still life on a horizontal canvas, about 102 by 157 centimetres, in which grapevines, other fruit and three birds fill the scene. In the Caravaggesque still life of early seventeenth-century Rome, natural forms are normally set close to the viewer, lit strongly and observed with unidealised care, so that each leaf, grape and feather has weight and texture. The picture is large for its type, which suggests it was made for a decorative scheme in a grand house.","i":"Independent still lifes were still a new kind of picture in Rome around 1600, when Caravaggio's Basket of Fruit showed that fruit alone could be a serious subject, and collectors soon sought them. Grapes could stand for the vintage, abundance and, in Christian art, the wine of the Eucharist, while birds add life and movement. No single message can be assigned to a picture like this; it is usually read as a celebration of nature's richness and of the painter's skill at imitating it.","c":"Cavarozzi is known chiefly as a figure painter, and a still life by him is a rarity that interests specialists as much as collectors. The genre rewards close, truthful observation, which his Caravaggesque training would supply: grapes with a convincing bloom, leaves with weight, birds with life. The risk in so large a canvas is a decorative sprawl, with parts that do not add up to a design."},"met:816624":{"d":"A small horizontal still life, about 50 by 68 centimetres, showing a basket of fruit that includes apples, grapes and a pomegranate. The type goes back to Caravaggio's famous fruit basket of about 1600, and Roman painters in his wake usually showed such fruit at close range under a strong light, so that skins, stems and leaves are crisp and each piece has weight. Cavarozzi, a follower of Caravaggio, painted it around 1620.","i":"Apples, grapes and pomegranates were everyday fruit and also familiar symbols: the apple recalls the Fall, the grape the wine of the Eucharist, and the pomegranate, with its many seeds, fertility and, in Christian art, the Resurrection. Whether Cavarozzi intended any of this is uncertain. Collectors in the early seventeenth century valued still lifes as demonstrations of how well painting could imitate nature, and as pleasing objects in their own right, so the picture is best read first as a display of observation.","c":"A modest picture whose strength lies in restraint. A basket of fruit at this small scale is intimate, and the Caravaggesque habit of firm light and exact detail gives the apples and grapes weight. It lacks the daring of Caravaggio's own fruit basket and the lavish variety of later Neapolitan still life. As a rare still life by Cavarozzi, a figure painter by training, it is a quiet success."},"met:435695":{"d":"A portrait in enamel of Henry Hope (1735/36-1811), made in 1802 by Henry Bone after a painting by an artist named Jones. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, and no size or description of the sitter's pose or dress is on record, so none is claimed here. Enamel is a difficult medium: powdered colored glass is painted onto a metal plate, usually copper or gold, and fused in a kiln, so the finished surface is hard, glossy and permanent, and each firing risks cracks or dull colors.","i":"Henry Hope belonged to the Dutch-British banking family of Hope and Company of Amsterdam, one of the great financial houses of Europe; he took refuge in England in 1794, when French armies approached, and died there in 1811. The enamel copies a painting by a Jones whom the museum does not further identify. Copying portraits in enamel was Bone's specialty, since the medium could hold a likeness without fading for centuries.","c":"Bone's enamels are technical feats, with tiny, controlled touches and faces glowing with a fired luster that oil cannot match. The problem is that enamel imposes a smooth, glassy finish, so a copy tends to lose the brushwork and character of the original painting, and the sitter becomes a polished image. As a record of a banker's likeness and an example of a demanding art it is admirable, though cool."},"met:435696":{"d":"A portrait in enamel of Thomas Howard (1585-1646), Earl of Arundel, made in 1808 by Henry Bone after a painting by Peter Paul Rubens. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait; no size or description of the sitter's pose or dress is on record, so none is claimed. The enamel copies an oil painting by a very different painter, in a medium that fuses powdered glass onto a metal plate, so the original's loose, rapid brushwork has to be translated into smooth, fired color.","i":"Arundel, Earl Marshal of England, was the first great English collector of antique sculpture and Old Master drawings and a patron of Rubens, Van Dyck and the engraver Wenceslaus Hollar; Rubens painted him about 1629-30, during his diplomatic mission to England. In 1808 Bone, then at the height of his fame as an enamel painter, copied the portrait for collectors who wanted famous British worthies in permanent form.","c":"A good test of Bone's skill, since the warm, fluid strokes of a Rubens portrait are hard to translate into enamel. Bone keeps the pose and the likeness, and the colors are brilliant, but the paint's movement, the heart of Rubens, is replaced by gloss. A valuable record of a great collector and a monument to a Regency taste for Old Master portraits; for Rubens himself one must go to the original."},"met:435697":{"d":"A portrait in enamel of George IV (1762-1830) as Prince Regent, made in 1816 by Henry Bone after a painting by Sir Thomas Lawrence. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait; no size or description of the pose, dress or setting is on record, so none is claimed here. Enamels after Lawrence were royal and aristocratic commissions, valued because the fired colors would not fade, and Bone was enamel painter to the royal family.","i":"George, Prince of Wales, ruled as Prince Regent from 1811, when his father George III was judged unfit to reign, until he became king in 1820. Lawrence, the leading portraitist of the Regency, painted him repeatedly, and Bone copied such images for presentation and sale. Royal portraits of this date typically show the Regent in uniform or state dress with orders and decorations, advertising the monarchy's prestige in a period of war and unrest.","c":"Lawrence's Regent is all silk and glitter, and enamel suits it: the fired colors can match the shine of satin, medals and braid. The cost is that enamel cannot take Lawrence's quick, nervous touch, so the copy has the sheen without the life. As a royal portrait it is as polished as one could ask, and as cool as a medal."},"met:435698":{"d":"A portrait in enamel of the physician Matthew Baillie (1761-1823), made in 1817 by Henry Bone after a painting by John Hoppner. The museum's keywords record a man, Matthew Baillie and a portrait, and no size or description of the sitter's dress or pose is on record, so none is claimed here. The title adds the letters F.R.S., Fellow of the Royal Society, as a mark of Baillie's scientific distinction.","i":"Matthew Baillie, a Scottish-born doctor and the nephew of the anatomists William and John Hunter, became one of the most eminent physicians in London, attended George III and published a pioneering book on pathological anatomy, The Morbid Anatomy of Some of the Most Important Parts of the Human Body (1793). Hoppner's portrait of him was copied by Bone in 1817, preserving the likeness of a famous medical man.","c":"A scientist's portrait suits the exact art of enamel, which makes few concessions to flourish, and Bone's version is probably as faithful to Hoppner as the medium allows. The result is a precise, slightly cold likeness, and it shows why enamel copies were prized by those who wanted a permanent record of eminent men. As a work of imagination it is limited; as a work of craft it is admirable."},"met:435700":{"d":"A portrait in enamel of Algernon Percy (1602-1668), tenth Earl of Northumberland, made in 1827 by Henry Bone after a painting by Anthony van Dyck. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, and no size or description of his dress or pose is on record, so none is claimed here. The date falls late in Bone's career; he was seventy-two and had been copying famous portraits in enamel for decades.","i":"Percy, a leading nobleman of Charles I's reign, was Lord High Admiral under Charles I, a patron of Van Dyck, and later sided with Parliament in the Civil War. Van Dyck, court painter to Charles I, painted him, and the portraits at Petworth House, the Percy seat in Sussex, were well known to collectors. Bone's enamel copy of 1827 shows the English taste of the 1820s for copies of the great Van Dycks.","c":"Van Dyck's glamour is hard to translate: the silk and the elegant hands that made his sitters the model of Cavalier grace depend on a light, fluent brush, and enamel fixes them in a hard sheen. Bone gets the pose, the rank and the color, and loses the air. The little portrait is nevertheless an exquisite object, the work of a veteran craftsman at seventy-two."},"met:435699":{"d":"A portrait in enamel of Charles X (1757-1836), King of France, made in 1829 by Henry Bone after a painting by François Gérard. The museum's keywords record a king, a crown, a man and a portrait; no size or description of the pose or robes is on record, so none is claimed here. The crown among the keywords shows that the king is portrayed as a monarch, in the formal manner of a state portrait.","i":"Charles X, brother of Louis XVI and Louis XVIII, reigned from 1824 until the July Revolution of 1830 drove him from the throne; he died in exile in 1836. Gérard, the leading portraitist of Napoleon's court and of the restored Bourbons, painted him in coronation dress in 1825. Bone's enamel of 1829, made the year before the revolution, records the king at the height of his reign and was a fashionable object for English collectors of royal portraits.","c":"A king's portrait in enamel is well matched: gold, velvet and jewels are what fired color renders best, and the shine of the medium suits the splendor of a state portrait. The cost is a human face; Charles X is more a robe with a head than a personality, as in the tradition of such portraits. An impressive technical display, and a record of the Bourbon restoration's last glory."},"met:769297":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of Pope Clement X, in oil on canvas, about 78 by 62 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a man, a portrait and a pope; no description of the sitter's pose, vestments or setting is on record, so none is claimed here. The date, about 1670 to 1671, falls at the start of the pontificate, since Clement, born Emilio Altieri in 1590, was elected in April 1670 at the age of seventy-nine.","i":"Papal portraits were made to be copied and sent to courts and churches, and a new pope was painted within months. Gaulli, called Baciccio, a Genoese in Rome and a protégé of Bernini, was among the leading portraitists of the papal court and would soon paint the vast ceiling of the Gesù (1672-79). Clement X, who ruled until 1676, left much of the government to his adopted nephew, Cardinal Paluzzo Altieri.","c":"Baciccio's papal portraits combine Bernini's sense of presence with a rich, Rubens-like warmth, and an aged pope gives a painter room for frank observation. The purpose of such a picture, an image of authority, limits the psychological freedom, yet a sitter of nearly eighty gives much to work with. A vigorous example of Roman Baroque portraiture, acquired by the Metropolitan in 2017."},"met:643540":{"d":"A bust-length portrait of a woman, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 59 centimeters. The sitter is not identified, and the museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, so her dress, pose and setting are not described here. It dates from the 1670s, when Gaulli, then in his thirties, was working in Rome on large decorative commissions for the Jesuits and painting portraits for the city's patrician and clerical families.","i":"Portraits of unnamed women are common in museum collections because the identification was usually carried by a label or frame and is easily lost. A woman's portrait by Baciccio of the 1670s would show a Roman or Genoese lady in the dress of her rank, with the soft, warm flesh painting that he had absorbed from Rubens and Van Dyck. The Metropolitan received this picture in 2014 as a gift in honor of Keith Christiansen, its curator of European paintings.","c":"Gaulli is one of the more painterly Roman portraitists of his day, and a woman's portrait gives him room for soft, melting flesh and fluid draperies. The anonymity limits the picture's interest, and the conventions of rank, the composed gaze and decorous costume, keep it from being intimate. As an example of Roman Baroque portraiture at its warmest it is attractive; as a character study it is reserved."},"nga:230998":{"d":"A valley opens beneath purple and brown mountains around a still, reflective lake, with a small rowboat floating on the water. Close at hand are gray rocks, brown tree trunks and branches, and lush green vegetation at the water's edge. The sky glows in warm yellow, peach and orange, shifting to pale greenish blue at the top, and the lake repeats these colors with those of the mountains and trees. The canvas is large and horizontal, about 76 by 127 centimeters.","i":"Elizabeth Gilbert Jerome (1824-1910) painted in the manner of the Hudson River School, the generation of American landscape painters who treated mountains, lakes and glowing skies as emblems of national character. A tiny rowboat on a still lake adds human scale and the poetry of rest typical of such pictures. Landscape painting was largely a male profession in 1872, and the National Gallery's acquisition of a landscape by a woman painter, little known today, fills out the story of American art.","c":"The sky is the triumph: warm yellows and peaches melting into blue-green, with the lake picking up the glow so that the whole valley seems to hold its breath. The foreground rocks and trees are painted with care, and the small rowboat gives scale. The composition follows the Hudson River formula closely and has little that is surprising, but its mood of stillness and its handling of reflected light are both accomplished."},"met:10191":{"d":"A night scene of rural labor, in oil on canvas, about 61 by 85 centimeters, painted about 1863 to 1864 by David Gilmour Blythe. A house, men, a farm, maize and night make up the subject, so the picture shows men husking corn after dark near a farmhouse. A corn husking was a communal harvest task, often done in the evening and treated as a social occasion.","i":"A husking bee brought neighbors together to strip the husks from the harvested ears of maize, and it was a staple of American rural life and of genre painting in the nineteenth century, a symbol of community and abundance. Blythe, who worked in Pittsburgh and made his name with satirical scenes of courtrooms, street life and the Civil War, tended to give such subjects a sharp, sometimes comic edge, though how he treated this one is not certain.","c":"Blythe is among the strangest American genre painters of his time: rough, humorous and sometimes grotesque, he painted the people of the upper Ohio valley with an anti-idealizing bite. A night scene of corn husking adds the challenge of artificial light, which can make ordinary faces seem theatrical. A Blythe is rarely bland, so the scene is likely to have an edge. This picture comes from the last years of his short career."},"met:436901":{"d":"A portrait of a man drawn in plumbago, an old term for graphite, on vellum, dated 1680, by David Loggan. The sitter is a man whose name is unknown. Such drawings were typically bust-length and finished with great care. The work is a drawing, not a painting, and fine graphite on smooth vellum was Loggan's specialty, producing small, polished likenesses with a delicate gray tonality.","i":"Loggan, born in Danzig and active in England from the 1650s, was one of the leading portraitists in black lead, a medium that suited the fine detail of wigs, lace and faces and was probably cheaper than a painted miniature. He is also remembered for his engraved views of the Oxford and Cambridge colleges. Portraits like this one were collected by sitters and friends as keepsakes. The man is unnamed.","c":"A plumbago portrait lives by its precision: the soft modeling of the face, the crisp hair and lace, all in fine, controlled lines on the creamy vellum. Loggan was a master of the medium, and even a routine sitter gains dignity in his hands. The medium and the master suggest a polished, impersonal elegance typical of the later Stuart court."},"met:435864":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified woman with a dog, in oil on canvas, about 97 by 72 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a woman, a dog and a portrait, and the record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so none is claimed here. The canvas dates from the 1740s, when Giacomo Ceruti, a Lombard painter, painted both the elegant portraits of the local gentry and the unsparing pictures of the poor for which he is now most admired.","i":"Ceruti is called Il Pitocchetto, the little beggar painter, because of his unsentimental pictures of beggars and laborers, but he painted portraits of the gentry as well, and in both he treats his sitters with unusual frankness and dignity. A woman with a dog was a stock type of portrait, but the museum calls the sitter unidentified, and nothing is known of her rank. The dog may stand for fidelity or affection, as in many portraits of the period.","c":"Ceruti's finest pictures give the humble a weight and realism that anticipate nineteenth-century painters like Courbet, and even his conventional portraits have a sober truthfulness that sets them apart from the Rococo prettiness of his day. The meaning of this picture stays open, since the sitter's identity is lost, but the directness of the treatment is the point: this is a person, not an ornament. A serious portrait by a painter who deserves to be better known."},"met:828497":{"d":"A small portrait of an old man with a dog, in oil on canvas, about 65 by 45 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a man, a dog and a portrait, and no description of his face, clothing or the dog's breed is on record, so none is claimed here. The format is smaller than most of Ceruti's portraits, and the subject, a man of advanced age with a faithful animal, belongs to the kind of unsentimental figure picture for which the painter is celebrated.","i":"Ceruti's pictures of old men and the poor, painted in the 1730s and 1740s, show his sitters as individuals, with wrinkles, patched clothes and dignity, at a time when most painters turned such people into picturesque types. The dog, the old companion of the poor and aged, may carry a note of loyalty and solitude. The Metropolitan acquired the picture in 2019, a gift in honor of Keith Christiansen and in celebration of the museum's 150th anniversary.","c":"Ceruti was among the first painters in Italy to look at old age and poverty without condescension, and this small picture belongs to that body of work, where the observation of a face counts for more than flattery. Its compact size gives it concentration. Beside his larger, more celebrated beggar pictures it is modest, and its virtue is the seriousness with which it treats an ordinary man and his dog."},"met:11181":{"d":"A small landscape painted in oil on pressboard mounted on wood, about 32 by 50 centimeters, with a man and a dog among the elements the museum records. No fuller description of the terrain, light or colors is on record, so none is claimed here. The scale and the support suggest an outdoor sketch or a study kept for the artist's own use, and the date, about 1852 to 1853, places it around the time Hunt joined Jean-François Millet at Barbizon.","i":"William Morris Hunt (1824-1879), born in Vermont, studied in Düsseldorf and Paris before settling in Barbizon, the village near the forest of Fontainebleau where Millet and the landscape painters of the Barbizon school worked. He returned to America in 1855, became Millet's great champion in Boston and influenced a generation of New England collectors. A figure and dog in a landscape fits Millet's rural subjects.","c":"Hunt's landscape sketches are among his freshest work, painted with a directness that he picked up in the villages of France and that was rare in American painting of the 1850s. Small and unpretentious, the picture is better as a record of his seeing than as a composition. It anticipates the loose, atmospheric manner of his late landscapes and shows him already thinking like a Barbizon painter."},"met:11177":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 117 by 90 centimeters, showing a girl at a fountain; the museum's keywords record only a girl and a fountain, and the entry does not describe her costume, her pose or the setting, so none is claimed here. The scale and the subject belong to the figure pictures that Hunt painted in France in the early 1850s, when he was a pupil of Thomas Couture and then of Millet.","i":"A girl at a fountain is an old and gentle subject, with echoes of Greuze and of the biblical well, and in mid-nineteenth-century French painting it was a way to show a peasant or a model in a simple, picturesque setting. Hunt, trained in Couture's studio, where large figure compositions were the rule, and then in Millet's village, was moving between two ideals: the academic figure and the observed rustic. The date, 1852 to 1854, spans that change.","c":"A large early canvas by an American still finding his manner. The subject is conventional, and the picture's strength is probably in the handling of the figure in the broad, sculptural manner of Couture; what it lacks is the personal voice that Hunt developed later in Boston. It is more interesting as evidence of his training than as a finished vision, but it shows a painter with real power over paint."},"met:11179":{"d":"A small vertical canvas, about 36 by 22 centimeters, showing a boy in a hat. The museum's keywords record a boy and a hat, and the entry does not describe his clothing, pose or setting, so none is claimed here. The title makes him Italian, and the date, 1866 with a question mark, carries the museum's own doubt. Hunt painted a number of such costume studies of Italian models in the 1860s, probably during a stay in Italy.","i":"Italian peasant boys and girls in regional costume were popular models for foreign painters in Rome and Florence, and a taste for Italian types reached the United States through travelers and artists like Hunt. These small pictures are less portraits than character studies, meant to catch a type and a bit of costume. The picture came to the Metropolitan in 1908 in a bequest from Martha T. Fiske Collord, together with Italian Girl.","c":"A small, quick study: Hunt's gift is in the broad, sculptural handling of a head and a hat, and strong light and dark make the boy a solid presence in a tiny space. The type is picturesque and the sentiment light, and one should not ask for the depth of his best portraits. It is a charming sketch that shows how Couture's lessons had settled into a sure hand."},"met:11180":{"d":"A narrow vertical canvas, about 41 by 21 centimeters, showing a young woman in profile, wearing the Ciociaro costume of the Roman countryside. The museum's keywords say nothing about her expression, her pose or the colors, so none is claimed here. The costume, named for the ciocie, the rawhide sandals tied with cords, was a favorite of foreign artists in Rome, who hired models from the countryside to pose in it.","i":"A profile is a format that emphasizes line and shape, and Hunt, who admired both Millet and the sculptural treatment of figures, uses it here for a costume study rather than a portrait; the model is nameless. The Ciociara costume was a picturesque signature of Italy in the 1860s and appears in many foreign paintings. The picture is dated 1867, and like Italian Boy it came to the Metropolitan in 1908 in the Fiske Collord bequest.","c":"In profile and in a narrow format, the figure has the economy of a medal or a relief, and Hunt's broad handling gives the head sculptural weight. The costume, however, remains a costume, and the picture belongs to a kind of Italianate sentiment that was a market staple. It is quick and attractive rather than deep, a sketchbook idea given the dignity of a canvas."},"met:11175":{"d":"A horizontal portrait of Charles Sumner in profile, in oil on canvas, about 56 by 69 centimeters. The museum's keywords record a man, a portrait and a profile, and the entry gives no description of Sumner's pose, clothes or setting, so none is claimed here. The date, 1875, comes the year after Sumner's death in March 1874, so the picture was presumably made from memory, earlier sketches or other sources rather than from a living sitter.","i":"Charles Sumner (1811-1874), senator from Massachusetts, was one of the most uncompromising opponents of slavery in the United States, nearly killed in 1856 when Representative Preston Brooks of South Carolina beat him with a cane on the Senate floor. After the Civil War he championed civil rights for freed people. Hunt made his career in Boston, and a profile suited a monumental, medal-like image of a public man remembered in death.","c":"Hunt's late portraits are broad and quick, with a monumental simplicity, and a profile of a heroic senator, the head set against a plain ground, suits that manner. The posthumous date means the picture works more as memorial than as a likeness drawn from the sitter's presence, and a profile, though emphatic, gives little of the senator's famous gravity. It remains a vigorous tribute by a painter who could make a head look like a bust."},"met:11174":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 97 by 64 centimeters, of bathers; the museum's keywords record male nudes and bathing, and the entry gives no description of the figures' number, poses or setting, so none is claimed here. The date, 1877, places it among Hunt's last works, when he painted in a loose, atmospheric manner, with broad, blended strokes, that moved away from the polished finish of the academy.","i":"Bathing figures offered painters a classical excuse for the nude, from Michelangelo's soldiers surprised in the river to Cézanne's later bathers, and in America nude male bathers would soon appear in the work of Thomas Eakins and others. Hunt's The Bathers, painted at the end of his life, treats the subject in the poetic, hazy spirit of the Barbizon painters rather than the muscular clarity of the academy.","c":"Hunt's late manner dissolves figures into light and air, and a bathing scene suits it: the nude bodies need only the lightest suggestion, and the surrounding atmosphere carries the mood. The weakness is an unresolved structure, since quick, blended strokes can leave the anatomy vague. With its dreamy tone it is closer to Puvis de Chavannes or the Barbizon painters than to the muscular American nudes that followed."},"met:11182":{"d":"A broad horizontal landscape, about 61 by 107 centimeters, whose title names a sand bank with willows at Magnolia. The museum's keywords record a landscape and a tree, and the entry gives no fuller description of the light, colors or composition, so none is claimed here. Magnolia, a seaside village on the Massachusetts coast of Cape Ann, was where Hunt painted in the late 1870s, in a loose, atmospheric manner.","i":"After the Boston fire of 1872 destroyed many of his works and his studio, Hunt turned increasingly to landscape, painting outdoors on the New England coast, and the sand dunes, willows and pasture of Cape Ann were among his subjects. These late landscapes are less finished than his earlier figure work and draw on the hazy, luminous example of the French Barbizon painters whom he had known and championed.","c":"Hunt's late landscapes are painted at speed, and this one has the fresh breath of the coast: the sand bank and willows are blocks of tone, and the whole holds together by the unity of light. The looseness that some find casual is the point, since he was after atmosphere, not description. A modest, honest picture, strongest for its direct sensation, and a good example of why his late work appealed to Boston collectors."},"cma:109974":{"d":"A vertical portrait, in oil on canvas, about 61 by 38 centimeters, titled His First Model, Miss Russell. The title names the sitter as Miss Russell and calls her someone's first model, a phrase the museum's record does not explain; the entry gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so none is claimed here. The date, about 1873, falls in Hunt's Boston years, when he painted portraits and taught a school for women students.","i":"Hunt's Boston class of the 1870s, taught largely to women, gave young artists their first experience of the model, and the title may refer to that experience: the sitter who first posed for an artist. Whether it commemorates a pupil's first model or Hunt's own is not recorded here. A title of this kind makes the picture a small piece of studio history, an anecdote about the beginnings of an artist's training.","c":"A picture of this kind lives on the freshness of a first sitting, and Hunt's quick, broad manner would suit it, giving the model presence with few strokes. The title offers an anecdote, but the painting must stand as a portrait of a woman, and it does so as a pleasant, informal piece from the master's Boston studio. It is typical of his late-period economy and, as a document of teaching, more interesting than its modest scale suggests."},"cma:145298":{"d":"A nearly life-size figure of Winged Fortune, on a canvas about 2.5 meters high, painted in 1878 as a study for the mural The Discoverer. In the mural she stands behind Christopher Columbus in his boat, her left hand on the rudder and her right hand spreading the sail. The Cleveland Museum's record gives no further description of the study's colors or handling, so the figure's pose, drapery and wings are not detailed here.","i":"The Discoverer was one of two huge murals that Hunt painted in 1878 for the Assembly Chamber of the New York State Capitol in Albany, the other being The Flight of Night. Fortune, who guides Columbus's boat, is an allegory of the luck or providence that accompanies discovery. The museum notes that the murals deteriorated badly and a new ceiling was eventually added to the chamber, concealing them entirely. Many of Hunt's other works were lost in the Great Boston Fire of 1872.","c":"A large, ambitious study, and a poignant one because the finished mural is lost to view. Hunt painted the Albany murals in great haste, on a wall that proved unsound, and the allegories were already looking battered within a few years. The study shows what the mural intended: a monumental, windblown allegory in a sweeping design, translated into American patriotism. It survives as the best evidence of an ambition that technique defeated."},"met:11173":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 94 by 65 centimeters, showing a goddess, Anahita, as a study for the mural The Flight of Night. The museum's keywords record only a goddess, and the entry gives no further description of her pose, drapery or setting, so none is claimed here. It dates from 1878, the year Hunt painted his two great murals for the Assembly Chamber of the New York State Capitol in Albany.","i":"The study's title names the figure Anahita, after an ancient Persian goddess of waters and fertility; the mural itself, The Flight of Night, is usually described as showing Night in a chariot fleeing before the dawn. The mural, painted in great haste in 1878, deteriorated and was concealed behind a new ceiling, so studies like this one are the best evidence of its design. Allegory of this grand kind was fashionable in American public buildings of the 1870s.","c":"A grand, fast-moving study in which Hunt tries for the sweep of a ceiling painter, and the draperies and surge of the figure show his ambition for a monumental American allegory. Close up the handling is broad and sketchy, and the pose is more theatrical than convincing; but the energy is real, and the study survives where the mural does not. For scale and daring it deserves attention, if not for refinement."},"met:11176":{"d":"A vertical study in oil on paper, about 94 by 64 centimeters, for The Discoverer, one of Hunt's two murals in the Assembly Chamber of the New York State Capitol. The museum's keywords record an allegory and a man, and its entry gives no description of the figures, so their poses and costumes are not detailed here. Oil on paper is a quick, portable support, suited to a rapid first design, and the study is dated 1878, the year of the murals.","i":"The Discoverer shows Columbus guided by Fortune, the allegorical figure who holds the rudder and spreads the sail; the keywords list a man as well as an allegory, presumably Columbus. A goddess of Fortune steering a discoverer to a new world combined the Renaissance personification of fortune with American pride in its first European navigator, a theme that fit a legislative chamber in 1878. The mural was lost to view behind a later ceiling.","c":"On paper, in oil, Hunt can work fast, and this is a design in the grand allegorical style he had absorbed from Couture, with a swagger that suits a mural. As a sketch it is lively and has the interest of recording a lost work; as a painting its figures are more diagram than drama, and one has to imagine the mural's scale. A document of a failed ambition, and a vigorous one."},"nga:96655":{"d":"A tall bouquet fills a vertical panel against a dark ground. A barely visible terracotta vase on a stone ledge holds carnations, tulips, peonies, roses, tuberoses, hops and other blooms, which swirl in circular patterns; a stalk of wheat and a few thin stems add bright, sinuous lines. Peaches and green and purple grapes are heaped on the ledge at the right, with an orange butterfly below. Small butterflies hover and tiny insects crawl over petals and fruit, and drops of water glint on leaves and tulips. Strong light crosses the face of the arrangement and leaves deep shadow at the sides and back.","i":"Flower pieces like this were luxury objects, made for wealthy collectors. Blooms of different seasons are gathered into one bouquet that could never exist in a real garden, since the painter worked from studies made over many months, so the picture is a feat of invention as much as of observation. Fruit, insects and dew add to the sense of abundance. Viewers of the period could also read such pictures as reminders that beauty fades, but delight in rarity and skill was usually the main point.","c":"Huysum was among the most sought-after flower painters of his time, and the panel shows why: a swirling rhythm that carries the eye around the bouquet, a smooth, enamel-like finish, and fine observation of insects and dew. The dark ground makes the colors burn. The price of such polish is a certain coolness, and the bouquet is packed so tightly that individual flowers compete. Few painters equaled his control of small detail."},"nga:56255":{"d":"A tall arrangement of blue irises, cream-white carnations, violets, peonies and tulips rises from a terracotta urn carved with child-like putti. Warm light from the upper left falls on the bouquet, which sits near the corner of a stone ledge veined in caramel brown and golden yellow. Some blooms face the viewer, others droop or turn away. A brown and orange butterfly rests on a scarlet poppy at the top, another sits on the ledge, and a bird’s nest woven of twigs and moss, holding four blue eggs, lies beside the urn. The signature reads “Jan Van Huysum fecit.”","i":"The nest with its eggs, the butterflies and the flowers of spring and summer gathered into a single bouquet turn the picture into a celebration of nature’s fertility. Nests and butterflies in Dutch still lifes can also hint at fragility and change, though how strongly depends on the viewer. The Latin signature, fecit, meaning “made it,” is the kind of learned flourish that raised the status of a painter of flowers, then ranked below history painters.","c":"The cool blues and whites against the warm, streaked ground give this panel a clearer design than many of Huysum’s bouquets, and the tulips rising high keep the spiral from clogging. The finish is polished almost to a fault, and the urn and ledge are handled with less interest than the flowers. Even so, the nest, the butterflies and the dew are drawn with a precision that explains his reputation among collectors."},"met:435775":{"d":"A small, wide canvas, about 18 by 36 cm, showing the beach at Kynance Cove on the Cornish coast; the record lists a beach as its subject and gives no further description. Brett’s late coastal pictures are known for crisp detail and bright, high-keyed light, and the long, low format suits the horizontal sweep of a shoreline. Beyond that, the title and format are all the record supports, so the scene itself is best left for the viewer to read from the picture.","i":"Kynance Cove, on the Lizard peninsula, was a favorite destination for Victorian visitors, and its rocks of red and green serpentine made it one of Cornwall’s best-known beauty spots. Brett had begun as a Pre-Raphaelite-influenced painter of exact natural detail, and from the 1870s he concentrated on coastal views of Britain. A small picture like this was probably a study or a modest work for a private buyer, not a major exhibition piece.","c":"Brett’s Val d’Aosta of 1858 set a high mark for exact observation, and his later coastal pictures rarely match its intensity. They are competent, bright and well informed about rock and weather, but more routine. A small panoramic strip such as this one asks to be judged by its truthfulness to a place, not by ambition, and on those terms it is a pleasant, if minor, late example."},"nga:41640":{"d":"A woman struggles against a man who grips her shoulder to push her back onto a canopied bed, in a dark room lit from the left. The man, in profile, wears a silver breastplate, a thin gold crown and a golden-yellow cape slipping from his shoulders; he raises a finger to his lips as he strides at her. She twists away, one hand braced on the bed and the other grasping his hair and coronet, her face turned up and her blue gown torn at the neckline. A partly unsheathed sword and a torn white flower lie on the floor. Oil on canvas, about 195 by 172 centimeters.","i":"The story comes from the Roman historian Livy: Sextus Tarquinius, son of the last king of Rome, forced himself on Lucretia, the virtuous wife of Collatinus, threatening to kill her; she told her husband and father and then took her own life, and the outrage led to the overthrow of the monarchy and the founding of the Roman Republic. Painters treated the subject as a tale of tyranny and chastity; the finger to the lips marks his demand for silence.","c":"Crespi's tenebrism here is theatrical and unsparing: the spotlight on the bed, the black ground and the twisting bodies give the scene a raw, physical immediacy that goes beyond the polite classicism of many Lucretia pictures. The painter, a Bolognese master known for sharp light and genre scenes, makes the violence human rather than allegorical. The result is disturbing by design, and it is among his more daring early pictures."},"nga:203":{"d":"Under a dark, blue-green sky, women sleep on stony ground on a sloping, tree-filled hillside, draped in fabrics of red, yellow, blue and green. Around them nude, winged cherubs rifle through golden chests or poke at the sleepers' faces with small objects, many glancing at one another with fingers to their lips. A few dogs look up at the cherubs as if just woken, a small deer lies at lower left, and a low stone wall runs across the lower right corner. Silhouetted hills and dark trees close the low horizon. Oil on copper, about 52 by 76 centimeters, about 1690 to 1705.","i":"The subject is the mischief of Cupids among the nymphs of the huntress Diana, who had sworn chastity: in the usual telling, while the nymphs sleep, the little gods of love steal their bows and arrows, disarming them and preparing them for love. The cherubs' fingers to their lips are a request for silence, so as not to wake them. Such playful classical fables were popular with Italian collectors of the Baroque, who liked small, polished pictures on copper.","c":"Crespi is better known for earthy genre scenes and religious pictures, and this night fantasy is an unexpected, witty departure: the dusky sky, the sleeping women in rich drapery and the pack of mischievous putti make a tale told with gentle motion. The small copper support gives the colors a jewel-like clarity. It is lighthearted rather than profound, and its charm lies in the comedy of the cherubs' stealth."},"met:436875":{"d":"A woman painter at work in her studio. The Met’s subject terms list an easel, a palette, a painting and a sketch, a vase with a bouquet and a cap, so the picture shows both the equipment of the craft and a woman’s place within it. At about 116 by 89 cm, it is a substantial canvas, painted in 1789 on the eve of the French Revolution. Studio interiors were an established subject in European painting, but a woman as its protagonist is much rarer.","i":"In the 1780s the Académie Royale in Paris limited its women members to four at a time and kept women out of its life classes. Studio scenes by women, such as Adélaïde Labille-Guiard’s self-portrait with two pupils of 1785, argued for a place in the profession. Lemoine’s picture belongs to that conversation. Whether the painter at the easel is meant as Lemoine herself is not stated in the available record.","c":"The picture’s force lies in its subject: a woman’s workplace shown as a serious one, with its tools set out plainly. The arrangement is clear and the accessories are carefully done, though the handling is more modest than that of her better-known contemporaries Labille-Guiard and Vigée Le Brun. It is valuable as evidence of how women artists wished to be seen in the years before 1789, and attractive as a picture."},"met:679844":{"d":"A fish market is laid out like a feast. At the left a bearded fishmonger with a heavy cleaver cuts slices from a salmon on a wooden counter, with a great grey fish and other catch in front of him. At the right a woman in a red jacket rests her hands on a brass basin that holds a carp, glancing to the side beside a tray of small fish and a wooden barrel. Behind the counter a maid with a wooden bucket looks out at the viewer. Through a stone archway beyond, townspeople walk down a street, with a tower in the distance. The fish are painted with glistening accuracy against warm browns and red. The date 1568 is at the lower left. Oil on Baltic oak, about 129 by 175 centimetres.","i":"Beuckelaer and his teacher and uncle, Pieter Aertsen, pioneered in Antwerp this new kind of scene of everyday life. The Met sees it emerging at a time of religious conflict, when artists were turning from purely religious themes to secular subjects, and notes that the picture celebrates the flourishing fish industry and the sea's great bounty. It probably also carried a moralising subtext, warning against the excesses of food and sexual pleasures, a double meaning that scholars find in many such market and kitchen scenes. The date is 1568, a few years before Beuckelaer's early death.","c":"Beuckelaer's strength is abundance. The scales and wet gleam of the fish are done with a near-photographic eye, the market is arranged as a diagonal of stalls, and the two main figures, the grim fishmonger and the watchful seller, have more character than most of his stock types. The weaknesses are a piled-up quality, the blank expressions, and a stiffness of pose that suggests studio arrangement rather than observed life. The moral subtext is invisible to a modern viewer, so the picture reads as a display of goods. As a record of sixteenth-century trade, it is vivid."},"met:436843":{"d":"Apollo, the sun god, drives his horse-drawn chariot across the sky, with Aurora, goddess of the dawn, among the figures. The Met’s subject terms list the chariot, horses, Apollo, Aurora and women. The canvas is large and nearly square, about 205 by 193 cm, painted in the cool, orderly, classicizing manner that Lairesse brought to Dutch painting. Its size suggests a work made for a patron’s interior, such as a salon, rather than for a church, though the record here names no commission.","i":"In Greek and Roman myth Aurora, the dawn, opens the gates of the east and scatters roses before the chariot of the sun, which Apollo drives across the sky. The best-known version, Guido Reni’s ceiling in Rome of 1614, shows the chariot led by Aurora. Dutch patrons knew the story from Ovid and from prints, and it could flatter a house as a place of light, learning and prosperity as well as simply celebrate the morning.","c":"Lairesse composes for legibility, and the large scale gives the scene a pleasing grandeur. The drapery and anatomy are more studied than felt, however, and the effect is closer to a well-ordered stage set than to the warm observation of his Dutch contemporaries. It is a learned, capable early work from his first years in Amsterdam, and more admirable than moving."},"cma:156420":{"d":"Calypso, the nymph who kept Odysseus captive for seven years, reclines on her bed while Hermes, the messenger of the gods, swoops down to order his release. The museum stresses her charms and the luxurious surroundings, and its caption adds that she looks out at the viewer with the same charming smile she once turned on Odysseus. The canvas, about 91 by 114 cm, is a cabinet-sized history painting in the classicizing style that earned Lairesse the nickname “the Dutch Poussin.”","i":"In the fifth book of the Odyssey, Zeus sends Hermes to the island of Ogygia with a command that Calypso must let Odysseus go. She protests that the gods begrudge goddesses their mortal lovers, but she obeys, and Odysseus builds a raft. Painters chose the messenger’s arrival because it holds a clash of desire and duty, and it let them show a luxurious interior and a reclining female figure at the same time.","c":"Lairesse arranges the story with theatrical clarity: the swooping god and the reclining nymph tell the tale at a glance. The elegance is real, if cool, and the poses owe more to French academic habit than to observation. The picture’s charm lies in its human note, Calypso’s knowing smile, which gives the divine drama a touch of comedy and keeps it from becoming merely correct."},"met:10934":{"d":"A wide landscape of open farmland, as the title Broad Acres suggests, is painted on a large canvas about 122 by 181 centimetres. The museum records flowers, a tree and a landscape but no more, so the fields, the sky, the buildings and the colours cannot be described with confidence. The generous horizontal format and size are those of an exhibition picture, intended to impress with a sense of breadth and abundance.","i":"Edward Gay (1837–1928) was born in Ireland, came to America as a child and, after training in Germany, became a painter of the American countryside in a quiet, descriptive manner. Pictures of the wide, cultivated acres of the northeastern United States spoke of the country's agricultural plenty in the years after the Civil War, when farmland was still the basis of the national economy. The Met received this one in 1887, the year it was painted, as a gift from a group of donors.","c":"A calm, expansive landscape in the tradition of the Hudson River School's later years, in which the pleasure lies in the breadth of the view and the quiet detail of fields and flowers. Without the picture before the eye, its weaknesses can be guessed from the type, since such views can slip into a picturesque sameness. Gay's pictures were admired for their honest, unforced truth to the open land."},"nga:134165":{"d":"A cluster of grapes hangs from a vine with green leaves and thin tendrils that loop around a gray nail at the top of the canvas. The grapes are mostly deep red and purple, with some pale yellow, and they and the leaves cast gray shadows on a plain cream-colored wall. The effect is of an object hung up against a wall and seen at arm's length. Oil on canvas, about 61 by 41 centimeters, dated 1883, by Edwin Deakin.","i":"A hanging bunch of fruit before a plain wall belongs to the trompe l'oeil tradition, which aims to make the viewer mistake the painted object for the real one, and which flourished in nineteenth-century America in the work of painters such as William Harnett and John Peto. Deakin, an English-born painter who worked in California, specialized in grapes, perhaps reflecting the state's growing wine and fruit industries. The nail and the tendril are the key to the illusion.","c":"A quietly effective still life whose power is in its restraint: the dark bunch, the pale wall and the soft shadows are enough, and the nail and tendril add a witty touch of illusion. Deakin's handling is precise without being dry, and the near-monochrome setting allows the colors of the fruit to tell. It is a small triumph of observation and design."},"nga:12143":{"d":"The Virgin sits squarely on a blocky throne, her head tilted slightly toward the Child on her knee as she looks out at the viewer. Behind the pair a patterned cloth of honour in grey, brick red and gold is held up by five angels, two on each side and one at the top. Mary wears a white veil, a slate-blue dress and a teal-green robe lined with pale green, its folds picked out in dense gold lines. The Child reaches toward her veil while his other hand rests on a small creature in his lap. Lion finials top the throne arms, against a leafy gold ground.","i":"Mary enthroned beneath a cloth of honour is shown as Queen of Heaven, and the lions on the throne recall the throne of King Solomon, whose wisdom the Child is understood to fulfil. Such images were meant to inspire devotion and to show the Virgin's dignity as well as her tenderness. The gold lines in the drapery follow a Byzantine habit that Italian painters kept long after 1300. The small creature in the Child's lap is unusual, and its meaning is uncertain.","c":"A formal and handsome image whose strengths are plain: a confident silhouette, a harmony of teal, slate and coral against gold, and careful pattern in the cloth and wings. The Child's hand on his mother's veil is a charming touch. Elsewhere the faces are generic, the angels repeat each other and the space is shallow. It is a solid example of Riminese taste, more decorative than moving."},"nga:272":{"d":"Christ stands hip-deep in a stylised river, hands pressed together in prayer, his head tipped toward John the Baptist, who steps from a rocky bank on the left in a fur-like garment under a white robe, one hand on Christ's head and the other holding a staff. Two winged angels at the right, in indigo and brick red, stand with hands joined, one bearing a patterned cloth. White curving lines mark the slate-blue water. Above, a smaller figure in a half-circle of heaven raises his hand and holds a red book. All of this is set against a gold ground within a gold border.","i":"The scene follows the Gospel accounts of Christ's baptism in the Jordan (Matthew 3, Mark 1), where the heavens open and a voice declares him God's Son. The figure above is usually read as God the Father, and the angels hold the cloth with which Christ will be dressed, a tradition inherited from Byzantine icons. The Baptism marks the start of Christ's ministry and offered the model for the Christian sacrament. Baronzio worked in Rimini, where painters had learned from Giotto's visit earlier in the century.","c":"The panel is at its best as decoration. The gold ground, the graded colours of the angels' wings and the regular white curls that stand for flowing water give it a jewel-like surface, and the arrangement is clear and symmetrical. Space and weight are less convincing: the figures are slender and flat, and the emotion is held back. Baronzio took less from Giotto than from the Riminese taste for line and colour."},"nga:41683":{"d":"About a dozen figures gather in a bedchamber on the left and a covered space on the right. On the left a woman in a white veil and gold halo lies under a bright red coverlet while two women tend a swaddled, haloed baby. In front, a grey-bearded man sits on a bench, brow furrowed, writing on a scroll that would read NOMEN EST IOHANNES, while a standing man beside him gestures toward him with both hands. At the right a man in green holds the nude baby as a man in white vestments performs the circumcision. Gold punched ground and a gold border frame the scene.","i":"The scenes illustrate Luke 1:57–66. Elizabeth gives birth to John in old age; on the eighth day relatives gather to circumcise the child and name him, and Zechariah, struck dumb for doubting the angel who foretold the birth, writes 'His name is John' and recovers his speech. Two moments share one panel, with a figure appearing twice, a common medieval way of telling a story over time. John was the forerunner of Christ, and his life was a popular subject for altarpieces.","c":"Baronzio is a lively storyteller here. The reading across the panel is clear, from bed to writing bench to circumcision, and details such as the writer's furrowed brow, the gesturing hands and the patterned wall keep it varied. The architecture, however, is a stage set of flat planes with only approximate perspective, and the faces are close to a type. It has real charm but little of the spatial command of Giotto's Florentine followers."},"met:435607":{"d":"A small gold-ground panel, about 67 by 38 cm, in which the story of Christ is told in several scenes instead of one. The Met’s subject terms name Christ, the Virgin Mary, saints, the Coronation of the Virgin and the Last Judgement, so the narrative runs from earthly life to the end of time. Men and women appear against flat gold. The medium notes that the paint layer has been transferred from its original wood panel to canvas, a restoration measure.","i":"Giovanni Baronzio worked in Rimini in the 1340s, in a local school shaped by Giotto’s earlier visit to the town. Small narrative panels like this were probably made for private prayer, where a viewer could follow the story of salvation scene by scene. The Coronation of the Virgin and the Last Judgement are not Gospel events but traditional closing episodes, and the Judgement in particular reminded viewers of their own final accountability.","c":"The gold ground and condensed storytelling give the panel a jewel-like appeal, and the Riminese taste for expressive feeling makes it more intense than many Italian panels of its date. Space is shallow and the figures follow set types, so it lacks the spatial invention of the leading Florentine and Sienese painters. A minor master, but a useful witness to how far Giotto’s influence spread across northern Italy."},"met:15065":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in watercolor on ivory, a miniature of only about 8 by 6.5 centimeters, dated 1829. She wears a shawl fastened with a brooch, details that suggest a fashionable woman of her day. As with most miniatures, the face carries the portrait and the surroundings are kept simple. The title does not name her, so she is known only as a lady.","i":"In the late 1820s a miniature like this would have been a personal object, kept in a case, worn as a pendant, or exchanged between family members and friends. The shawl and brooch point to a woman who followed fashion and could afford accessories, although the portrait does not tell her rank. Women miniaturists such as Goodridge found a steady market for such small likenesses in their own communities.","c":"Miniature portraits live on delicacy: skin built from tiny strokes of watercolor over the glowing white ivory, with a few accessories added crisply. Goodridge works within a conventional formula, flattering and quiet, and the picture does not try to be more. What stands out is the care taken over a tiny surface, and that care is the point."},"met:15067":{"d":"A miniature of a man in watercolor on ivory, only about 5.8 by 4.7 centimeters, dates from about 1825. It is smaller than a playing card. As is usual for the form, the face is the focus, and the title does not name the sitter. Pictures of this size were made to be held in the hand or kept in a small case, close to the owner.","i":"Men's miniatures were commissioned for engagements and marriages, before voyages and journeys, or to remember the dead, and they served as tokens of affection as much as records of appearance. With no name attached, this gentleman's identity is lost, a common fate for miniatures separated from their cases and inscriptions. What remains is a face from the 1820s, recorded by a woman painter working in a thriving trade.","c":"Goodridge's gentlemen tend toward clear, steady likenesses without drama. At this tiny scale a few touches decide everything, and the success of a miniature depends on the eyes and mouth, which the painter must set down in a handful of strokes. The result is competent and tidy rather than inspired, and its interest is craft."},"met:15064":{"d":"A gentleman is portrayed in watercolor on ivory in this miniature of about 7.8 by 6 centimeters, painted around 1829 by Eliza Goodridge. It is larger than her miniature of about 1825 with the same title. The face is the focus, and the title does not name the sitter. The small format means that the whole portrait is built from tiny, deliberate touches.","i":"Goodridge, the younger sister of the Boston miniaturist Sarah Goodridge, worked at a time and in a place with a steady demand for small likenesses. Such portraits were exchanged as gifts, worn or kept as keepsakes, and often outlived the memory of the sitter, as this one has. The generic title is a reminder that many miniatures have lost their names, leaving only the person's face and the painter's skill.","c":"A miniature's success lies in subtle transitions of tone, and watercolor on ivory allows a glow that oil cannot match. Goodridge handles the form in a clear, conventional manner and avoids sentimentality. The picture does not break new ground, but it shows reliable professional skill at a time when such portraits were a mainstay of the trade."},"met:15066":{"d":"A boy is the subject of this miniature of about 5.9 by 4.8 centimeters, watercolor on ivory, from around 1829. A flower is among the details, a small touch of nature in a portrait that otherwise concentrates on the child's face. At this size the image could be held in the palm or sit in a small case, and the title does not name the sitter.","i":"Flowers in children's portraits commonly stand for youth, freshness and the brevity of life, though a flower may also simply be a pretty accessory. Parents ordered miniatures of children to keep a record of a brief stage of life, and in an era of high child mortality such likenesses were also tokens of remembrance. The boy's name has not come down with the picture.","c":"Children's miniatures reward a light touch, and Goodridge's approach, a clear face and one simple accessory, is appropriate. The flower brightens the composition and humanizes it. As in the rest of her work, the aim is a tender, conventional likeness rather than invention. The charm is in the modest scale."},"met:10957":{"d":"A named sitter, Alice Goudry, appears in this miniature of about 8.9 by 7.3 centimeters, watercolor on ivory, from the early 1830s. The details recorded are a lace collar, an earring and a brooch, the accessories of a fashionable woman of the time. The lace in particular is the kind of detail where a miniature painter shows skill, since every thread must be suggested with the tip of a fine brush.","i":"Naming the sitter is a bonus for a miniature, since many lose their identification. Alice Goudry's lace collar, earring and brooch speak of a woman with some means and an interest in dress, and such portraits were often given to a husband, fiancé or relative. The 1830s were the last decade in which miniatures dominated small-scale portraiture, before the daguerreotype arrived in 1839.","c":"The lace collar and jewelry give this miniature more to look at than the plain male portraits, and Goodridge handles them with a finesse that suits the form. The face keeps the soft, idealized look expected of the genre. Within her output it is a pleasant, well-made example, even if it does not break new ground."},"met:15916":{"d":"A watercolor on ivory miniature of about 6.4 by 5.1 centimeters, dated 1817, shows a man named in the title as James Fowle Baldwin. A named sitter is more than can be said of many miniatures of this period. As is usual for the form, the image concentrates on the face and keeps the surroundings simple, so that the whole portrait is held in a few carefully placed touches of color.","i":"Elkanah Tisdale was an American miniaturist who also worked as an engraver, and he is often credited with the 1812 caricature that gave gerrymandering its name. A miniature of 1817 would have been made for family or friends, to be kept in a case or worn as a keepsake. Portrait miniatures were the intimate, portable portraits of the era before photography, and the named sitter makes this one easier to place than most.","c":"A small, clear likeness by a painter who was also an engraver, and an engraver's training would encourage firm drawing of the features. Miniatures of this date are valued for precision and reliability, and this one is decently both. It is a competent record, more notable for the sitter's name than for any pictorial surprise."},"met:436199":{"d":"Christ heals the sick in a scene whose recorded motifs are Christ, illness, a man, a woman and a dog. The canvas, about 89 by 105 centimeters, is dated 1742 and was given to the museum by William H. Webb in 1885. The museum offers no further description of the crowd, the setting or the lighting, but the subject is that of Rembrandt's celebrated etching, the Hundred Guilder Print, whose manner Dietrich often imitated.","i":"Matthew's Gospel tells how great crowds followed Christ and he healed their sick. Rembrandt's etching of about 1648, called the Hundred Guilder Print because of the price it was said to fetch, made this one of the best-known religious compositions in northern Europe, and German painters of the eighteenth century turned to it as a source. Dietrich, court painter in Dresden, was famed for his ability to imitate the manners of earlier masters, above all Rembrandt.","c":"A skilled pastiche: the drama of light, the crowd of afflicted figures and the central radiating Christ come from Rembrandt, and Dietrich handles them with fluent competence. What he cannot borrow is Rembrandt's depth of feeling, and the picture has the lively surface and external drama of an imitation. A clever, enjoyable exercise that shows how thoroughly Rembrandt had become a model for eighteenth-century painters."},"met:436200":{"d":"The Holy Family receives its first visitors in a scene whose recorded motifs are Mary, Saint Joseph, the Christ Child, a man, a woman, a donkey and a dog. The horizontal canvas measures about 55 by 73 centimeters and is dated to the 1760s. The museum gives no description of the light, the stable or the number of shepherds, though the Nativity night scene was a favorite of Rembrandt's school, which Dietrich often imitated.","i":"The shepherds who, in Luke's Gospel, were told of Christ's birth by angels and hurried to see him made a favorite subject for painters who liked humble figures and a night-time glow. The donkey and the dog add a note of everyday life. The picture was among the first paintings the Metropolitan Museum bought, in 1871, shortly after its founding, when it was assembling a collection of European old masters.","c":"Dietrich adapts the older Dutch manner without strain: the glow, the grouped figures and the humble details are pleasantly handled, though the emotion is gentler and prettier than Rembrandt's. The picture is skilled and agreeable, with the air of a cabinet piece for a collector of old-master styles. It is not an original vision, but a polished compliment to the tradition it follows."},"met:436201":{"d":"A couple is caught in a compromising moment, the title says: Surprised, or Infidelity Found Out. The record lists a woman, a man, a couple and Cupid, and the near-square canvas measures about 73 by 73 centimeters. No date is recorded, and the museum gives no description of the setting, the poses or the expressions, so the arrangement of the figures and the role of Cupid, the god of love, are not detailed here.","i":"Pictures of lovers interrupted were popular in the eighteenth century, mixing erotic suggestion with moralizing, and a Cupid in the scene lets the painter play with the conventions of allegory: love is blind, or fickle, or mischievous. Both this picture and Dietrich's Adoration of the Shepherds were bought by the museum in 1871 in its first acquisitions, which shows the range of subjects in the founding collection.","c":"Dietrich was a chameleon, and here he turns his hand to Rococo gallantry with the same fluent ease as to Rembrandt. The moral is delivered with a smile, the colors are bright and the figures are neat, but the narrative is conventional and the sentiment shallow. It is a witty rather than a profound picture, and shows how widely German painters of the mid-eighteenth century roamed through other men's styles."},"nga:46568":{"d":"A large, pale fish lies near the bottom right corner with smaller fish, in front of a polished brass pot, a dark brown bottle and a copper jug with two handles. The palette is rich browns, bronzes, blacks and cream whites, and light glints off the metal vessels, while most of the background is dark so that the pale fish stand out. It is an oil on canvas, about 75 by 101 centimeters, dated 1882.","i":"Carlsen, born in Denmark and active in the United States, was a still-life specialist who admired Chardin, and he took from him a taste for humble kitchen objects arranged with great care. Fish and copper and brass vessels are a traditional kitchen subject going back to Dutch painting of the seventeenth century, and they allow a painter to show the contrast of soft flesh and hard, gleaming metal.","c":"Carlsen's early still lifes are quiet exercises in tone, and this one delivers: the silvery fish against the dark ground, the warm glow of copper and the cool shine of brass are held in a deep, rich harmony. The composition is simple, the objects ordinary, and the interest lies in the handling of reflected light. It has the dignity that earned him the nickname of the American Chardin, though it is darker and more studied than his later work."},"met:437608":{"d":"The head of the Virgin Mary alone is the subject of this small oil on wood panel, about 38 by 29 cm, painted around 1509. A close view of a single head is unusual for a devotional picture and is often a fragment of a larger composition, or a study, though the title alone does not settle which. Franciabigio was a Florentine painter who shared a workshop with Andrea del Sarto around 1508 and drew on Fra Bartolomeo and Raphael.","i":"In pictures of Mary, the face carries the meaning. Lowered eyes suggest humility, an upward gaze devotion, a tilted head tenderness or grief, and the idealized beauty of the features expresses her purity. Without the rest of the composition, the head cannot be tied to a particular moment, whether the Annunciation, the Nativity or the Crucifixion, but it offers the viewer what such images were chiefly for: a face to pray to.","c":"Franciabigio is overshadowed by Andrea del Sarto, but his strength is firm drawing and clean, sculptural modeling, and a head gives him nothing to hide behind. The success of the panel rests on the structure of the face and the handling of the oil. The risk, for the idealized type, is an impersonal prettiness. As a fragment of Florentine High Renaissance feeling it is attractive without being searching."},"met:11758":{"d":"A man and a horse are the figures in this horizontal oil on canvas, about 56.5 by 74 centimeters, dated 1872. The title promises a conversation, though its subject is left to the viewer. Enoch Wood Perry, an American genre painter who had studied in Europe, specialized in unforced scenes of everyday life, and this picture belongs to the American genre painting that flourished in the years after the Civil War.","i":"After the Civil War, American buyers favored pictures of everyday life that were gentle, humorous and reassuring. A conversation involving a man and a horse belonged to a long line of rural genre scenes in which small incidents stood for community, work and character. The exact situation behind the title is left open, so the viewer supplies the story.","c":"Perry was a pleasant, competent storyteller whose genre pictures are modest in ambition. Here the title does the narrative work and the painting supplies the setting; the strength lies in the quiet understatement of the moment. Beside the great genre painters of the period, such as Winslow Homer, it lacks bite, but it belongs to the gentle, anecdotal strain of American painting that audiences loved."},"met:11759":{"d":"A man reads a newspaper in front of a house in this small oil on canvas, about 30 by 41 centimeters, painted around 1874. The man and his paper are the whole drama: the title makes his reading the mark of a type, the true American. The format is a small cabinet picture, meant for a private owner rather than for public exhibition.","i":"In the 1870s the newspaper was a symbol of citizenship: a literate, informed public was expected to follow politics and business daily, and the country had a thriving press. The title can be read affectionately or ironically, as a joke about the man whose patriotism consists of reading the news. The house behind him places him at home, a householder and a citizen at once.","c":"Light genre painting depends on a quick read, and Perry gives it: a clear silhouette, a plain setting and an unmistakable occupation. The gentle humor works because it does not push. The picture is minor, but its subject, a citizen absorbed in his newspaper, has aged into a document of how Americans saw themselves in the 1870s."},"nga:46135":{"d":"The Holy Family pauses on its journey into Egypt. The record supplies no description, so what can be said rests on the title, the horizontal panel of about 58 by 75 cm, and Heemskerck’s manner around 1530: figures set in a landscape, painted in the fine oil technique of the early Netherlandish tradition and with growing interest in Italian ideas passed on by his teacher Jan van Scorel. It is a cabinet picture, probably meant for private devotion.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew says only that an angel warned Joseph to take Mary and the child to Egypt. Later legend added incidents, such as a palm tree bowing to offer its fruit, and artists used the pause to show the family in a quiet, domestic moment. Such scenes were popular in private devotional pictures. Viewers saw in the Holy Family’s humble endurance a model for their own lives, and in the exile an early sign of Christ’s later suffering.","c":"The picture comes before Heemskerck’s years in Rome (1532 to 1536) and the muscular, heavily classicizing manner for which he later became known, and it is interesting for that reason. Without a closer description the most that can be said is that it shows a gifted painter in his early thirties working within a familiar formula, softer in feeling than his later, more crowded and more learned compositions."},"met:436638":{"d":"A small upright portrait on wood, about 52 by 35 cm, of an elderly man. The title names the sitter as Jacob Willemsz van Veen and gives his dates as 1456 to 1535, so he was about seventy-five when his son painted him in 1532. The Met’s subject terms list only a man and a portrait, and the record lists no costume, setting or inscription, so the picture is best described as an intimate, private-scale likeness, made for the family and not for public display.","i":"Maarten van Heemskerck took his name from the village of Heemskerk, where he was born, and the title shows that he painted his own father. A portrait of this kind combines record and tribute: it fixes the features of an old man for his family, and it also shows that the son had become a painter of standing in Haarlem. In the Netherlands of the 1530s, honest likenesses of the old were prized, and a frank portrayal of age was no insult.","c":"Heemskerck’s portraits of the early 1530s are generally sober and direct, and a likeness of his father is the kind of subject that invites plain truth over flattery. It belongs to the part of his career that precedes the crowded, learned, Italian-inspired narratives for which he is better known, and it is arguably more convincing than many of them: modest in scale, attentive to the face, and free of rhetoric."},"met:436672":{"d":"The boy Jesus sits in the Temple at Jerusalem, answering the questions of learned men gathered around him, while a woman, probably his mother, has come in search of him. The scene also includes reading, with books in play, and a dog that has found its way in. Painted in 1668 on a small wood panel, 38 by 50 centimeters, it is a cabinet picture made for close viewing.","i":"The story is told in Luke 2:41-52. At twelve, Jesus stays behind in Jerusalem after the Passover, and his parents find him after three days in the Temple among the teachers, listening to them and asking questions; all who hear him are astonished at his understanding. Painters used the subject to show the wisdom of Christ, and for Protestant viewers, who prized Bible reading, a scene of learned debate over books was easy to appreciate.","c":"Hondius is remembered as a painter of hunts and animals, and a scriptural scene is not his strongest suit. The interest lies in how he sets a familiar story in a believable crowd and lets a dog humanize the space. As a small history piece it is competent and legible, though less probing than the biblical scenes of Rembrandt and his pupils."},"cma:149770":{"d":"A monkey and a cat sit beside a fire in a picture that tells a fable. The monkey flatters the cat into pulling scalding chestnuts out of the flames; the cat does the painful work, only to find that the monkey has already eaten nearly all of them. The cabinet canvas, 62 by 74 centimeters, turns the story into a small drama of expression and gesture among animals. It was probably painted in the 1670s.","i":"The story is attributed to Aesop, and the museum suggests that Hondius followed the retelling by Jean de La Fontaine, whose fables were widely known by then. Its warning is plain: beware flattery, and beware doing another's dirty work. The English phrase 'cat's paw', for someone used as a tool by another, comes from this tale. Animals acting out human folly were a familiar vehicle for moral lessons, and viewers would have taken the point at once.","c":"Hondius was best known for hunts and animals, so a fable of this kind lay naturally in his field. Such a picture depends on animal faces and postures that can carry a joke, and its chief virtue is probably gentle humor along with observation of fur and gesture. It is an amusing, lightly moral cabinet piece rather than a profound picture, and its modest ambitions are well matched to the painter's gifts."},"met:816629":{"d":"The Holy Family is shown fleeing into Egypt, with an angel, in this oil on copper about 60 by 49 centimeters, painted about 1664. The museum's keywords record the angel, Mary, the Christ Child, Saint Joseph and the Flight into Egypt, so the donkey, the landscape, the light and the colors are left unstated. Copper, a smooth and costly support, was chosen for small, finely finished pictures made for collectors, and Maratti, a leading painter in Rome, was known for clear, graceful compositions.","i":"The story is told in Matthew 2: warned in a dream that Herod meant to kill the newborn king, Joseph took Mary and the child and left by night for Egypt. Painters embroidered the tale with legends, among them a palm tree that bowed to give them dates and Egyptian idols that toppled as the child passed. The angel who guides or protects the family recalls the angel who warned Joseph, and the subject was popular as a picture of divine protection on a journey.","c":"Maratti, the heir of Raphael and the Carracci in the eyes of his Roman contemporaries, aimed at balance, nobility and clarity, and this small copper shows his restraint: figures grouped with classical calm, gestures kept gentle, and a polished surface. The effect is graceful rather than moving, and the 1660s found it admirable, though later taste has found him cool. As a cabinet picture it is refined and accomplished."},"cma:307326":{"d":"A woman holds a drawing that shows Venus forging the love-darts of her adolescent son Cupid, a picture within the picture. The sitter is Francesca Gommi, the painter's wife, in a vertical portrait about 99 by 75 centimeters, painted about 1701. The museum's note does not describe her pose, dress or setting, so those are left unstated. The portrait was presumably painted shortly after the marriage, as an homage from the artist to his new wife.","i":"Maratti's first wife died in 1700, which allowed him to marry Francesca Gommi, his longtime mistress, who had modeled for him since the 1670s and was the mother of his only child, Faustina. The drawing she holds adds an allegory to the portrait: Venus making Cupid's weapons suggests that love will conquer all, a compliment to the marriage as well as to the sitter. The picture is both a likeness and a declaration of affection.","c":"Maratti was the leading painter in Rome of the late seventeenth century, known for altarpieces and for portraits of popes and cardinals, and a personal portrait of his wife shows another, warmer side. The painting-within-a-painting device is witty and well integrated, and the likeness carries real tenderness. The polished, idealizing finish of the Roman academy remains, but affection gives it life. It is one of the more appealing portraits of the period."},"nga:215580":{"d":"Rugged gray rocks, rolling green hills and tall trees in dark green and rusty orange fill this landscape of about 97 by 119 centimeters. At the left, red and gray wooden structures sit on rocky ground, with small white goats walking on the rocks around them. A gray river runs from near the buildings down to a waterfall at the center, and the sky is pale blue with white and gray clouds. The painting was made around 1665 to 1670.","i":"Allart van Everdingen traveled to Scandinavia in the 1640s, and his drawings of waterfalls, pine forests and log cabins gave Dutch painters their picture of the north. Back in Holland he turned the sketches into paintings for buyers who had never seen such scenery, and his waterfalls inspired Jacob van Ruisdael's famous versions. The log cabins and goats add a rustic human note to the wild landscape.","c":"Everdingen's northern landscapes brought a new roughness and grandeur to Dutch painting, and this canvas has the energy of the type: strong diagonals, a central cascade, and weighty rock. He is less poetic than Ruisdael, who borrowed his motifs and refined them, and the paint handling can seem plain. Still, as an invention it was influential, and the picture shows why."},"nga:208068":{"d":"The viewer looks down across a river valley toward mountains. The middle and far distance, with its mountains, trees and buildings, is painted in tones of ice and cobalt blue, and the horizon sits about halfway up. A gold glow from the left lights the clouds, with flat aquamarine sky at the top center. Near the viewer, dark brown and green ground is backlit by low sun, and a rocky outcrop rises along the left edge. A dozen or more tiny figures and a few animals gather on paths below it, and others work around boats on a river winding away at the right.","i":"Herman Saftleven worked in Utrecht and was known for imaginary views of the Rhine valley. Landscapes like this gather steep rocks, river traffic and village life into an ideal composition instead of a portrait of a particular place, and collectors prized them as travel in miniature. The cool blues in the distance are the painter’s way of suggesting vast space, a device called atmospheric perspective, and the small figures give the cliffs their scale.","c":"A tiny panel, about 17 by 23 cm, with a large sense of space. The cold blue distance against the warm, backlit foreground and the gold sky is handled with real atmosphere. The formula of a rocky repoussoir at one side, a winding river and a hazy far horizon is conventional, and the figures are mere accents, but at this scale the finish is delicate and convincing. A fine late work by an artist then in his early sixties."},"nga:56394":{"d":"A woman faces to the right, shown at three-quarter length, in a loose gold satin gown with draped white sleeves and a white undergarment at the neckline. Her fair, curly hair is gathered in two bunches with ringlets on her shoulders, and she wears a single strand of pearls and a drop earring. One hand holds blue fabric; the other holds a small oyster-shell dish catching a thin stream of water from a dark rock at the upper right. Dark foliage and a cloudy blue and gray sky fill the background. The canvas is about 127 by 103 cm.","i":"The shell, the stream, the loose satin gown and the landscape background probably evoke a water nymph or similar pastoral figure, a flattering conceit popular in Restoration England and associated with Sir Peter Lely’s portraits of court beauties. The picture is therefore less a record of everyday dress than a compliment, presenting the sitter as graceful and natural. The record gives only the name “Lady Borlase,” so no more can be said of her here.","c":"Gerard Soest was a Dutch-born portraitist who settled in England, and his work lacks Lely’s glamour but is more direct. The face here is quietly and honestly observed, and the satin catches light with some conviction, yet the nymph conceit sits a little stiffly on a sitter whose neutral expression gives nothing of the role away. A sound, rather plain Restoration portrait, more interesting for its fancy dress than for its psychology."},"met:438949":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 32 by 25 centimeters, showing columns of the Greek temple traditionally called the Temple of Neptune at Paestum, in southern Italy. The ruined temple and its columns are the whole subject, and the picture belongs to architectural painting. It is dated 1838, during Constantin Hansen's years in Italy, and its small upright format suggests a study made on the spot rather than a finished studio picture.","i":"Paestum, founded as the Greek colony of Poseidonia, has three well-preserved Doric temples, and the one called the Temple of Neptune is now generally thought to have been dedicated to Hera. Its heavy columns of golden travertine drew architects and painters from the eighteenth century on, as one of the best-preserved Greek temples anywhere. For a Danish artist in Italy, the visit was something of a pilgrimage to the sources of classical form.","c":"A modest study that reflects the Danish Golden Age training in direct observation, in which exact looking at light and form mattered more than grandeur. Hansen turns a famous site into a close, quiet view of stone, avoiding the usual picturesque rhetoric. It is small and slight, and it does not show the figure and portrait painter that he mostly was, but it records a firsthand encounter with an ancient monument."},"nga:32678":{"d":"A large portrait of a man named in the title as Lodovico Widmann, on a canvas about 206 by 138 cm, the scale of a formal, full-length or near-full-length likeness. The museum gives no description of his pose, costume or setting, so only the type of picture can be stated: a Venetian state portrait of the 1630s in oil on canvas, made to proclaim a sitter’s standing in his family and city. The date is given as probably 1637.","i":"Tiberio Tinelli was among the leading portraitists in Venice in the 1630s and painted senators and merchants in a dignified, restrained manner that descends from Titian and Tintoretto. A portrait this large told visitors to a house who held it. The Widmann name belongs to a family of wealthy merchants in Venice who later bought patrician rank, though the record here does not say how Lodovico fits into that history.","c":"Tinelli’s strength was calm command: a clear, unflattering likeness and a stately, unfussy presentation. Beside Van Dyck’s elegance, which was changing portraiture across Europe in these years, his formula can look old-fashioned, resting on the Venetian tradition of Tintoretto’s senators, but it carries conviction. Few portraitists of the moment gave a sitter so much weight with so little show, though the result is more dignified than lively."},"met:437955":{"d":"Nude men and women, cattle, sheep, dogs, cats and birds share a wooded landscape on a small sheet of copper, about 22 by 31 cm. The Met’s subject terms list trees, birds, male and female nudes, cats, cows, dogs and sheep, and the title names the Golden Age. The copper support gives the paint a smooth, hard, enamel-like gloss, typical of the small, jewel-like cabinet pictures Wtewael made for collectors.","i":"The Golden Age is the first of the ages of the world in Ovid’s Metamorphoses: a time of peace without laws, labor or war, when the earth gave freely and people and animals lived together without fear. Nudity here expresses innocence. In 1605 the Dutch Republic was still at war with Spain, so such a vision of harmony may have carried extra appeal, and learned buyers could enjoy the Ovid as well as the pretty bodies.","c":"Wtewael combined Mannerist elegance with a love of detail. The small scale and copper support suit his fine, polished touch, and the animals are drawn with obvious pleasure. The risk is blandness, since the nudes pose more than they act. Beside his wittier, more erotic cabinet pictures of gods and lovers, this idyll is gentler and more decorative, but its delicacy and color remain attractive."},"nga:53142":{"d":"In a wooded landscape, people and animals gather in a ring around a shallow pool. The shadowed foreground is a band of figures and objects: a horse, a reclining woman and child, a goat tended by a nude child, a dog sniffing at crockery and pans, a cat lapping from a pewter dish, and a young man drinking from a shell. Beyond, a gray-haired, bearded man in turquoise, pink and blue robes taps a rock with a rod, and water gushes into the pool, where men, women and children fill jugs. Light glints on skin, fur and, above all, the metalware.","i":"The story is told in Exodus 17: the Israelites, thirsty in the desert, complained to Moses, and at God’s command he struck the rock at Horeb and water flowed out. Christian readers saw the rock as a figure of Christ and the water as the grace of baptism, following Saint Paul (1 Corinthians 10). Wtewael’s emphasis falls on the relief of the crowd: people drinking, washing and tending children.","c":"A virtuoso, crowded little panel in Wtewael’s late manner, with pastel colors, a polished surface and a foreground still life of pewter and brass that is as lovingly painted as the faces. The cost is that the miracle itself is small and tucked to one side, and the large cast, however pleasing, dilutes its drama. It is best enjoyed as a delight of detail, and it shows that Wtewael, in his late fifties, remained sharp."},"nga:108661":{"d":"Two heads fill a vertical panel, shown from the shoulders up in profile and facing left against a shadowy dark green curtain. The man, closer to the viewer, overlaps the woman. He wears a black cap on silver, shoulder-length curls, a thin mustache and narrow chin beard, a broad flat white collar and a velvety black coat; his nearer hand is lifted before his chest, fingers curled gently inward. The woman has soft jowls, closed, downturned lips, and her hair drawn back under a black covering. Her black dress has a sheen that suggests satin.","i":"The sitters are the artist's own parents: Salomon de Bray, himself a painter and architect in Haarlem, and his wife, Anna Westerbaen. The doubled profile, unusual in Dutch portraits of the time, recalls antique coins and medals and gives the pair the dignity of a founding couple. The sober black clothing and white collar are typical of respectable Dutch dress, plain apparel that proclaimed seriousness rather than display. Painted in 1664, it is a son's record of his family.","c":"The profile format is a daring choice, and the artist handles it with restraint. The two heads are modeled with firm, sympathetic observation, from the old man's soft jowls to the woman's set mouth, and the dark ground concentrates attention on faces and the one raised hand. There is no flattery, and the sitters' age is left plainly visible. As an image of filial feeling it is moving, even if the dark palette is austere."},"nga:46476":{"d":"A small portrait of a man, about 41 by 33 centimetres, painted in oil on canvas in 1875. The title names the sitter as Simon Hayem. At this modest size the picture is more a private likeness than a showpiece, and Bastien-Lepage, then still in his twenties, was known for portraits that concentrate on the face, with the plain, even light and exact observation of the French naturalist painters.","i":"Bastien-Lepage trained in Paris under Alexandre Cabanel and rose to fame in the mid-1870s. He became known for combining academic finish with direct observation of ordinary people and light, a naturalism that later inspired young painters in Britain and America. He died young, in 1884, aged thirty-six. Portraits of friends and patrons such as this one helped support his career and show the sober, truthful side of his art.","c":"Bastien-Lepage's strength was a sober, truthful likeness without flattery, and this small portrait is best seen in that light. It lacks the ambition of his big rural subjects, and its quiet competence will not startle anyone. But the clear light and the exact modelling of the head show the training and the eye that made him so influential. The picture is a good, modest example of his work as a portraitist."},"met:435621":{"d":"A young peasant woman stands in a kitchen garden beside a leafy tree, in a gray bodice with white chemise sleeves and a long, worn brown skirt. Her head is raised and her wide eyes stare past the viewer; one arm reaches out and her fingers clutch at leaves, while the other hangs at her side. Cabbages, a wicker frame and a weathered farmhouse fill the dappled green setting, painted with careful open-air detail. At upper left, against the foliage, three faint, translucent figures appear, one in golden armor and the others veiled. They are the saints she hears. The canvas is huge, about 2.5 by 2.8 meters.","i":"Joan, born in Domrémy in Lorraine, said that she first heard the voices of Saints Michael, Catherine and Margaret at about thirteen, telling her to drive the English from France. After France lost Alsace and part of Lorraine to Germany in 1871, she became a patriotic emblem, and Bastien-Lepage, who came from Damvillers in Lorraine, gave her a deeply personal treatment. He painted her as a country girl, not a warrior. At the Salon of 1880 critics praised her expression but felt the ghostly saints clashed with his naturalism. The American collector Erwin Davis bought it and gave it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1889.","c":"The girl is the success. She is not an armored heroine but a farm girl in a real garden, and her stiff, rapt posture is more convincing than most history painting of the time. The flaw, which critics noticed in 1880, is the saints: thin, pasted-looking apparitions that belong to a different kind of picture and undercut the honesty of the garden. It is one of the most ambitious works of Bastien-Lepage's short career, and it shows both his strength, plain observation, and his limit when asked to paint the unseen."},"nga:159836":{"d":"A stone bridge with turrets at each end spans a placid river and takes up the right half of this horizontal landscape; low sunlight angles through its four large arches and glows along the right side of the sky, which deepens to blue at left. Vegetation hangs from the towers. In the shadowed foreground two men on horseback gesture toward the bridge, another points to a man standing with arms spread in a boat, and a cowherd drives three cows through the water. Beyond, sunlit hills carry two low-towered buildings, and hazy mountains close the horizon.","i":"The bridge is the Ponte Molle, or Milvian Bridge, the ancient crossing of the Tiber just north of Rome, where Constantine defeated Maxentius in 312, and a favorite site for travelers entering the city. Dutch painters who had worked in Rome, like Asselijn, brought home the warm light and rustic scenes of the Roman countryside, which northern collectors bought as visions of Italy. The figures are generic types of campagna life, and the picture is more a mood of golden evening than a record.","c":"Asselijn handles the effect that matters, the low sun striking through the arches with a silhouetted bridge and a sky shading from gold to blue, with sure economy, and the shadowed bank makes the glow richer. The figures are lively but secondary. Compared with Claude Lorrain's Roman evenings, from which this tradition descends, the small canvas is more modest in ambition but crisper and more homely."},"met:15055":{"d":"A man is portrayed in this tiny miniature, only about 3.2 by 2.3 centimeters, painted in watercolor on ivory around 1785 to 1790. At roughly the size of a postage stamp, it is among the smallest portraits one is likely to see. Ezra Ames, who later became the leading portrait painter of Albany, New York, was still in his late teens or early twenties when he made it.","i":"Miniatures of this date were the most intimate form of portraiture: tiny, portable and often worn as jewelry or kept in a case. The title does not name the sitter, so he is known only as a gentleman, a reminder of how many such portraits lost their identification. Ames was largely self-taught, and early works like this one show a young painter practicing the exacting craft of the miniature.","c":"At this size the whole portrait depends on a few well-placed touches for the eyes, nose and mouth, and the success of the face is a matter of fractions of an inch. The execution is naive but careful, as befits a young self-taught painter. Compared with Ames's later oil portraits it is an apprentice piece, and its charm is in its minuteness."},"met:10075":{"d":"A man is the subject of this portrait of about 1810, an oil on canvas of about 91 by 71 centimeters, painted by Ezra Ames. The title names the sitter as Philip Van Cortlandt, a Revolutionary War colonel from the Hudson Valley who later served in Congress. The portrait follows the plain, direct manner of the Albany painter, who typically favored clear outlines and strong light on the face, and it presents the sitter as a dignified elder of a leading family.","i":"By 1810 Philip Van Cortlandt was a veteran of the Revolution and a respected elder statesman of a leading New York family, and a portrait recorded his place in the new republic. Ames, who settled in Albany in 1793 and painted its notables for four decades, supplied the leading men of the Hudson Valley with straightforward likenesses that stressed dignity and character.","c":"Ames was a self-taught painter, and his portraits have the strengths and limits of that training: firm drawing, honest observation and a certain flatness of modeling. This picture presents the colonel without flattery and without flourish, and its value is as a clear record of a man who helped shape the early republic. It is a good example of American provincial portraiture at its most direct."},"cma:157243":{"d":"A man named John Scoville is the subject of this portrait of about 1810, an oil on canvas of about 76 by 60 centimeters, by the American painter Ezra Ames. The size is that of a standard bust or half-length portrait for a private house. As in much of Ames's work, the sitter is shown plainly, in the manner of a leading citizen who wanted a clear and truthful likeness.","i":"Ames was the leading portraitist of Albany, New York, in the early republic, and his clients included merchants, lawyers and officials who wanted a dignified record of themselves. A portrait like this one was a family heirloom, passed down with the house. The sitter's name has survived, which gives the picture an identity that many portraits of the period have lost.","c":"Ames's strengths are directness and honesty, and a portrait like this relies on firm drawing and clear light rather than on bravura. He lacks the polish of Philadelphia or Boston portraitists, and that very plainness is what gives his sitters their solidity. This is a sound, unpretentious example from an artist who deserves more notice than he generally gets."},"nga:34068":{"d":"A woman sits in a red chair, shown from the waist up, with her right elbow on the chair arm and her head leaning on her hand; her left hand holds white fabric in her lap. She has blue eyes, a large nose and pink lips set in a slight smile, and her curly brown hair is pinned up with a headband bearing a crescent-shaped ornament. She wears a dark dress with a lace-trimmed neckline and short puffed sleeves, a gold brooch and gold earrings. The background is warm brown, with reddish fabric draped on the right.","i":"The sitter was Maria Gansevoort, from an old Dutch family of Albany, New York, and the wife of the merchant Allan Melvill; she was later the mother of the novelist Herman Melville. Her father, General Peter Gansevoort, had defended Fort Stanwix in 1777. The head-on-hand pose belonged to portraits of sensibility, suggesting thoughtfulness, and the gold jewelry and lace announce a family of standing.","c":"Ezra Ames was Albany's leading portrait painter of his day, and this picture shows his strengths: a plain, direct likeness, a clear pose and honest attention to lace, brooch and earrings. The modeling is flat, and the large nose is recorded without flattery. It lacks the polish of the Philadelphia and Boston portraitists but gains in frankness. Knowing she was the mother of Melville adds interest to the quiet, slightly amused face."},"nga:52267":{"d":"A little girl, two years and nine months old according to the inscription, stands facing the viewer before a landscape of forested foothills and rocky mountains under an ice-blue sky with pale pink clouds. She wears a sky-blue bodice trimmed with lace, a full black skirt and a white bonnet with a royal-blue ribbon and pink rose. A long white lace mantilla drapes over her bonnet, shoulders and wrist, and one hand rests on her hip. A small shaggy white dog sits at her feet, and a low stone wall angles away behind them. Her wide gray eyes look straight out.","i":"The inscription names her as the daughter of the Infante Don Luis. Her father, a brother of King Charles III, had married a woman of lower birth, so his children were excluded from the royal succession and the family lived away from court, at Arenas de San Pedro. Goya painted the family around 1783 and 1784. The girl later became Countess of Chinchón and the wife of Manuel Godoy, and Goya painted her again in 1800.","c":"Goya, then in his late thirties, does here what he did best with children: no sentimentality and no coy pose, only a direct, curious gaze and a small body that seems barely to hold still. The costume is a lovely passage of blue, black and lace, and the clear sky gives a freshness his formal portraits of adults often lack. Landscape and dog are still conventional; the child is the achievement."},"nga:92":{"d":"A young woman in a silvery-grey gown stands full-length in a park, looking out with calm dark eyes. A wide-brimmed straw hat trimmed with a white ribbon sits on a cloud of powdered grey hair, with tendrils falling over her shoulders. The low-necked bodice is edged with lace and a garland of pink roses, the waist is cinched with a pink ribbon, and the skirt is looped up in puffs at the sides, each caught with roses and ribbons, before falling in fine pleats to her pointed shoes. In one hand she holds a pink carnation, in the other a closed fan. At her feet a small pug in a belled collar lifts a paw. Behind are simplified trees and a pale blue sky.","i":"María Ana de Pontejos y Sandoval (1762–1834) was about twenty-four when Goya painted her, around 1786, shortly after she married the brother of the Count of Floridablanca, chief minister of Charles III. The pink carnation, a flower of love often held by brides, and the pastoral costume, following the fashion set by Marie Antoinette, suggest the portrait marks the wedding. Goya was then rising at the Spanish court and in 1786 was named a painter to the king. Her straight posture and distant gaze recall Velázquez's royal portraits, which Goya studied closely, and the simplified landscape is an English-style garden setting.","c":"This is the courtly Goya of the 1780s at his most delicate. The silver gown, the pale roses and the airy trees are painted in light, nearly abstract touches, and the little pug in its belled collar relieves the formality. The face is the weak point: smooth and doll-like beside the penetrating likenesses he would soon make, it gives the sense that the sitter is wearing the costume rather than inhabiting it. As an essay in elegance it is lovely, but the satirist and psychologist of his later portraits is still to come."},"met:436545":{"d":"A small boy in a bright red suit stands full length in a shadowy gray-green room, holding a string tied to a magpie at his feet. The bird, a white card in its beak, hops forward. Behind the boy, three cats crouch in the gloom at the left, their eyes fixed on the magpie, and a green wire cage full of finches stands at the right. He wears a silver-gray satin sash, a lace-edged white collar and cuffs, and white shoes with bows. His pale face, brown hair, and wide eyes catch the light, while the background is dark and almost empty. A line of lettering along the bottom edge names him.","i":"The boy was the youngest son of the Count and Countess of Altamira, patrons of Goya, and the count commissioned the portrait in 1787–88, along with several others of the family. Manuel was about three or four, born in 1784, and died at eight, in 1792. The pets invite interpretation: the caged birds may stand for innocence and the cats for threat, though nothing records Goya's intention. The magpie holds a card bearing the painter's name, a playful signature. The portrait hung in the Altamira palace in Madrid until it was sold in Paris in 1878. Jules Bache bought it in 1926, and it reached the Met in 1949.","c":"One of the most appealing portraits of a child in Western art, and it earns that standing by pairing tenderness with unease. The red suit and silvery sash are brushed with effortless sparkle, and Goya refuses sentimental prettiness: the boy is small, grave, a little stiff, while the cats' eyes gleam from the dark. The details are charming rather than profound, and attempts to read doom into the cats may say more about hindsight than about the 1780s. But the quiet strangeness is real, and it hints at the darker Goya to come."},"met:436541":{"d":"A man in a wig, jacket, necktie, breeches and stockings is shown with a letter, and the Met’s subject terms list reading. The canvas is a portrait of modest size, about 93 by 68 cm. No description is supplied in the record, so the details of pose, color and setting are best left to the picture itself. The sitter’s dates, 1747 to 1800, show that he was about forty-five when Goya painted him in 1792.","i":"Sebastián Martínez was a wealthy merchant of Cádiz and a notable collector of paintings and prints. Goya was his guest in Cádiz around 1792 and 1793, when the illness that left him deaf struck, and the portrait is generally read as a tribute to a friend and patron. Collectors like Martínez belonged to the circle of cultivated, Enlightened Spaniards who admired Goya and supported his work outside the court.","c":"Few portraits of the early 1790s show so well how Goya was leaving the conventions of court portraiture behind. A man of business and taste is presented as a cultivated, thinking individual, not a figure of rank, and the likeness gains in warmth from the artist’s friendship with the sitter. Some of the surrounding detail is conventional, but the face carries the picture, and it stands among Goya’s best portraits from before his deafness."},"nga:46173":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, a heavy-set man sits at the corner of a table against a dark brown ground and looks at the viewer from the corners of his brown eyes, brows drawn together and lips set in a line. His gray hair is brushed back and curls over each ear. His black coat is densely trimmed with loosely painted gold and has scarlet cuffs, a froth of white lace at the throat and a gold starburst medal on a white and light blue ribbon. One hand holds a folded paper bearing his name and title, resting on more papers on a green cloth.","i":"The folded paper names him as Treasurer General, a senior post in the royal finances, and Goya’s signature and the date, 1801, are written on it as part of the picture. The medal and gold-laced coat advertise rank; the pale blue and white ribbon recalls that of the Order of Charles III, though the record does not identify the medal. Goya had been First Court Painter since 1799, so a portrait of a high official fell naturally to him.","c":"A forceful, unflattering portrait. Goya makes no effort to soften the heavy face, the double chin or the sidelong stare, and the gold braid is handled with such freedom that it reads as glitter and not as pattern. The man’s pride and wariness come through together. It stands among the best of Goya’s male portraits from the turn of the century, when he could paint the powerful without flattering them."},"met:436542":{"d":"A portrait of an officer of the Spanish army’s engineers, named in the title as Ignacio Garcini y Queralt, Brigadier of Engineers, who lived from 1752 to 1825 and so was about fifty-two when he sat. The canvas measures about 104 by 83 cm and is dated 1804. The record gives no description of pose, dress or setting, so only the type can be stated: a formal commission from Goya, made to hang with its pendant, the portrait of the sitter’s wife, Josefa.","i":"Brigadier was a senior rank, and the engineers were an educated technical corps responsible for fortifications, roads, canals and maps, a profession that Enlightenment Spain esteemed. A pair of portraits of husband and wife, as with the Garcinis, was a standard commission among the professional and landed classes, made to hang together in a house. In these years Goya was in great demand, and sitters of this standing came to him readily.","c":"A sound, workmanlike likeness from Goya’s busiest period, when his formula for sitters of middle rank was well established: a strongly lit head, a dark ground and few props. The result is more sober than his portraits of fashionable women of the same years, and the engineer remains somewhat reserved, but the head is convincingly alive. It gains from being read beside its pendant, where the contrast of the couple becomes part of the picture."},"met:436543":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Josefa de Castilla Portugal y van Asbrock de Garcini, who lived from 1775 to about 1850 and so was about twenty-nine when Goya painted her in 1804. The canvas measures about 104 by 82 cm and was made as a pair with the portrait of her husband, Ignacio Garcini. The record gives no description, so pose, dress and setting are not detailed here; the picture is a formal likeness of a married lady of the Madrid professional class.","i":"Pendant portraits hung together in a house set out a husband’s profession and a wife’s place at home. The wife’s picture was usually where fashionable dress, jewelry, a fan or a mantilla could be displayed, along with the household’s taste. Goya in 1804 was among the most expensive and sought-after portraitists in Madrid, so the commission itself signaled the couple’s means as well as their wish to be shown as a matched pair.","c":"Goya’s women of 1800 to 1805 are often more vivid than the men painted beside them, with attentive faces and a thoughtful use of color that avoids the prettiness of earlier court portraiture. Costume and accessories are generally handled with a few quick strokes while the face carries the picture, and a portrait of this date can be expected to follow that pattern. Read beside her husband’s, it adds a livelier note to a sober pair."},"nga:985":{"d":"Seen from the knees up, a clean-shaven young man leans on his right elbow against a ledge and holds a black top hat so that its crimson lining shows. He faces front but looks just past the viewer, with dark eyes, closed lips, a cleft chin and a hint of stubble; long brown bangs fall over his forehead. A white knotted neckcloth, a faintly blue-striped white vest and a gray jacket with wide lapels and silver buttons complete the dress, lit from the left against a dark ground streaked brick and coffee brown.","i":"Bartolomé Sureda is recorded as director of the royal porcelain factory at the Buen Retiro in Madrid, and this portrait was probably made as a pair with that of his wife. The gray coat, the top hat and the natural, unpowdered hair follow English fashion, which in Spain around 1800 marked a modern, progressive man. The casual lean on the ledge, with a glance just off to one side, fits that image of easy, up-to-date confidence.","c":"The Sureda portraits share a striking modern simplicity: a dark ground, light from one side and a few well-judged accents, the silver buttons catching the light and the crimson hat lining flashing like a signal. The pose feels casual, even slightly impatient. The face is less probing than in Goya’s best likenesses of these years, but the fresh, unfussy handling is persuasive and looks ahead to his later portraits."},"nga:991":{"d":"Seen from the knees up, a pale-skinned woman in a muted sapphire-blue gown sits ramrod straight in a chair that faces the viewer’s left in profile, though she turns her round face and large light brown eyes toward the viewer. One forearm rests on the wooden arm of the chair and her other hand lies across it. The high-collared dress, painted loosely in slate and royal blue shaded with mauve pink, has a hint of sheen, with a white collar open at the throat. The chair is upholstered in gold fabric with a suggested brocade pattern, against a dark, nearly black ground.","i":"She is the wife of Bartolomé Sureda, and the two portraits share size, date and dark ground, so they were evidently hung together. The upright, slightly turned pose was a conventional way to show a woman of her class as composed and decorous, while the plain dark ground and sober dress follow the simpler fashions of the years around 1800. Pendants of this kind recorded a marriage as much as two individuals.","c":"A restrained, almost severe portrait whose beauty is in the paint. The dress, built from slate and royal blue with mauve shadows, and the loosely brushed gold chair glow against the dark ground. The woman herself is observed with candor and without prettiness, and her stiff posture and level gaze give her dignity. It is one of Goya’s most striking portraits of women of this period, with color doing the work that lace and ribbons did in his earlier pictures."},"met:436551":{"d":"A portrait of Ferdinand, heir to the Spanish throne, as a youth. The Met’s subject terms list only a man and a portrait. The canvas is modest in size, about 83 by 67 cm, and the date is given as about 1800, when the prince, born in 1784, was fifteen or sixteen. The record gives no description of dress, orders or setting, so those are not detailed here; the format is that of an official likeness of the heir, made for the court.","i":"Ferdinand was Prince of Asturias from 1789, heir to Charles IV, and portraits of the heir were a matter of state, showing the dynasty’s continuity. Goya, First Court Painter from 1799, painted the royal family often. The boy later reigned as Ferdinand VII, first in 1808 and again from 1814 to 1833, a ruler remembered for restoring absolute monarchy, and Goya painted him as king as well. Early portraits show him before that history.","c":"Painting an heir at a delicate age, Goya had to balance dynastic dignity against evident youth, and the record suggests a competent, workmanlike royal likeness more than a psychological one. The later portraits of Ferdinand as king are far more searching about his character. Read in sequence, they give this earlier picture its interest: it shows what a court painter was expected to do, and what he later chose to add."},"nga:46469":{"d":"Shown from the knees up against a dark gray ground, a young woman with peachy skin stands angled to the viewer’s left and looks out with brown eyes. Her dark curly hair is covered by an ivory-white lace mantilla touched with pale gold and russet, whose long ends she gathers in one hand; they fall over a long black dress with a shimmering white bodice. Tight off-white gloves reach her short puffed sleeves, with a bow at one arm, and she holds a closed silvery fan. Her brownish-coral necklace is a squiggle of paint, and Goya signed faintly at lower left.","i":"The mantilla and basquina, a lace scarf and a black, long-skirted dress, were the formal outdoor dress of Spanish women, worn for church and the promenade. Around 1800 they were also treated as an emblem of Spanish national taste at a time when French fashion was widespread. The title does not name the sitter. A portrait of an unidentified woman in this dress may be partly a study of the national type as much as a likeness.","c":"Goya’s loose, flickering paint turns lace and silk into light: the mantilla and bodice shimmer while the face stays calm and individual, and the necklace dissolves into a squiggle of coral. The long black and white shape is bold and nearly abstract, and the dark gray ground gives it presence. It is among the freest of Goya’s portraits of women from these years, with an economy that looks forward to later painting."},"met:436547":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Narcisa Barañano Gayoso de Goicoechea, dated about 1805, on a canvas of about 112 by 78 cm. The Met’s subject terms list a lace mantilla, a rose, a hand fan and a ring, so she is shown in the Spanish lady’s formal dress of lace and flowers, with a fan in hand. The record gives no more description, so pose, color and setting are not detailed here. One catalogue record lists the painting as attributed to Goya.","i":"The mantilla, fan and flower were the standard equipment of a Spanish lady’s formal dress around 1800, and the portrait presents the sitter as elegant and well bred. The ring may point to marriage, or may simply be part of the display of wealth. A long family name of this kind marks a sitter of established standing. The “attributed to” in one record is a reminder that the authorship of some portraits in Goya’s orbit has been debated.","c":"If the attribution to Goya is accepted, the picture belongs to his portraits of Spanish ladies in mantillas from about 1800 to 1805, where lace and silk are rendered in light, quick strokes and the face carries the interest. Because one record qualifies the attribution, the handling deserves close looking, and a viewer should keep an open mind about how much of the paint is his own. The type, at least, is one he made his own."},"nga:43625":{"d":"A boy with pale skin and slate-blue eyes stands in a dark brown room holding an open book, his body angled slightly to the viewer’s right and his lips barely parted. Short blond hair tousles across his forehead, and a tall white collar flares in a V at his neck. He wears a black hip-length jacket and long, high-waisted trousers, both densely trimmed in gold along the front, hem, cuffs and legs, over a white shirt, with black shoes and pale stockings. Light from the viewer’s left throws his shadow to the right.","i":"Victor Guye was the son of General Nicolas Guye, a French officer serving in Spain, whom Goya also painted. The date, 1810, falls within the Napoleonic occupation, when Joseph Bonaparte was on the Spanish throne and Goya, who remained in Madrid, painted officers and officials of the French-backed government. The open book signals education and the gold-trimmed suit the family’s rank; together they present the child as heir to a successful career.","c":"The child is observed with the directness Goya reserved for children: a solemn, slightly uncertain boy in a costume that seems to wear him as much as he wears it. The gold braid is handled with the loose brilliance of the late portraits, a few strokes standing for a whole pattern, while the plain brown room keeps the figure monumental. A tender picture that never turns sentimental, and a fine example of Goya’s portraits of children."},"nga:95":{"d":"Shown from the waist up in front of a black background, a young woman with pale skin and curly brown hair looks directly out at the viewer over her shoulder, her body angled to the viewer’s left. Her dark brown eyes are steady and her pink lips closed. Curls fall across her forehead, and the rest of her hair is pulled up under a white lace mantilla tucked beneath a wide orange and black shawl. Her clasped hands and forearms are covered by long white gloves that disappear under the shawl. The canvas is a compact 71 by 58 cm.","i":"The title gives the sitter’s name and her title of address, Señora, and little else. The black ground, shawl and mantilla are typical of Spanish dress of the period. The glance over the shoulder breaks the formality of a conventional pose and gives the impression of a moment caught. Portraits like this, with bare backgrounds and no props, concentrate on the sitter as a person, and they reflect Goya’s mature approach in the years after about 1800.","c":"A compact, intense picture. The black ground, orange and black shawl and white lace set up a bold pattern, and the direct, slightly challenging glance gives the sitter presence. Goya paints the lace with a few quick touches and keeps the face smooth and quiet, so that the eye goes to her expression. It is one of his most arresting small portraits of women, and its economy is typical of his mature manner."},"met:436546":{"d":"A portrait of a boy called Pepito, shown with a drum and a horse: the Met’s subject terms list boys, drums, horses and toys, so the playthings mark him as a child at play. The canvas measures about 105 by 85 cm and is dated about 1810. The record gives no description of the boy’s clothing, pose or setting, so only the toys can be named. The title records the sitter as José Costa y Bonells, who died in 1870; Pepito is the familiar form of José.","i":"Children in Spanish portraits of this era often carry toys that point to their later lives, and a drum and a horse may foreshadow a military career for a boy of a family that hoped for one, though the record does not say so. Goya’s earlier portrait of Manuel Osorio, also at the Met, likewise surrounds a child with pets that are widely read as symbolic. Such pictures were commissioned by families to record a child’s early years.","c":"Goya’s portraits of children are marked by candor: he treats the child as a person with his own presence, not as a decorative miniature adult. The toy drum and horse add anecdotal charm, but the more lasting interest is the child’s face, which in Goya’s hands is never merely sweet. This picture, made about 1810 during the upheavals of the Peninsular War, ranks among his notable portraits of children."},"met:436544":{"d":"A portrait of the architect Tiburcio Pérez y Cuervo, who lived from 1785 or 1786 to 1841, painted in 1820 on a canvas of about 102 by 81 cm. The Met’s subject terms list a man, a portrait and an architect, and the record gives no further description of his pose, dress or props, so those are not detailed here. Goya was seventy-three or seventy-four, deaf, and recovering from the grave illness of late 1819.","i":"In 1820 Goya was at work on the Black Paintings that covered the walls of his own house, yet he continued to paint portraits of friends and professional men, and in the same year he portrayed the doctor who had saved his life. A title that names the sitter’s profession shows how he was meant to be known. Such likenesses could be exchanged between friends as well as commissioned, and the sitter’s link to Goya is not stated here.","c":"Goya’s late formal portraits are usually pared down: dark tones, a lit head, a man looking out at the viewer, with little display. Whatever fantasies the Black Paintings were exploring, the portraits of these years remain steady, humane and unadorned. The record on this picture is slim, so the safest judgment is that it follows that pattern, and that it shows the old Goya’s gift for taking a likeness down to weight and tone."},"nga:214255":{"d":"A small panel, about 20 by 30 centimeters, holds a close study of a wildflower: a cluster of large green leaves and delicate pink-and-white blossoms set against brown earth and dry, fallen foliage. Leaves and flowers are scattered through the middle of the picture, with denser clusters near the center, so that the eye travels across the ground as if crouching beside the plant. The palette is restrained to deep greens, earthy browns and soft pinks and whites. It is a cabinet picture of nature at close range, painted in oil on wood in 1863.","i":"Trailing arbutus is a creeping North American woodland plant, one of the first flowers of spring, and in New England it is known as the mayflower. In the 1860s American painters influenced by the English critic John Ruskin studied plants with a naturalist's fidelity, arguing that truth to nature was a moral as well as an artistic duty. Bridges, who trained with William Trost Richards, belonged to that current. Such small panels were made for quiet domestic display.","c":"Its merit is intimacy. A tight format, a plain ground of earth and dead leaves, and a restrained palette of greens, browns and soft pinks give the plant dignity without a vase or a bouquet's formality, and the blossoms are kept soft rather than diagrammatic. It lacks the decorative finish of Bridges's later watercolors of birds and flowers, but gains in freshness, and it shows Ruskinian precision applied with real tenderness."},"nga:50302":{"d":"A long, horizontal view looks across a grassy field toward a row of farm buildings on a low rise under a blue sky. Mounds of cut hay line the bottom edge, and men in white shirts and tall black hats work with rakes and scythes. One man leads a child by the hand, a dog trots beside a man with a basket and bottle, and a wagon loaded with the green harvest waits with two brown oxen. A long white house stands at the center of the skyline.","i":"This is a farm portrait. Prosperous American farmers in the early republic commissioned painters to record their land, buildings and the work of the season, much as English gentry commissioned estate views. The haymaking, the neat row of buildings and the orderly field express the owner's industry and the settled prosperity of the countryside. Alexander was a young, largely self-taught painter in the early 1820s, and such pictures mix folk directness with sharp observation.","c":"This early work, by a painter who later became Boston's fashionable portraitist, has charm in its clear, orderly layout, the pleasing rhythm of buildings along the horizon and the small, lively figures. The perspective is naive and the figures are stiff, but crispness and a vivid sense of place are its strengths. It is a valuable record of American farming and a welcome contrast to the grander landscapes of the Hudson River painters."},"nga:32581":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, his body turned slightly to his right and his head toward the viewer. He has defined cheekbones, small bright eyes and short, neatly combed gray or white hair, and wears a dark coat over a white shirt with a high collar against a muted, dark background. There are no jewels or objects to distract. The panel measures about 65 by 54 centimeters and was painted around 1835.","i":"Plain portraits like this one served the prosperous middle class of Jacksonian America: merchants, professionals and farmers wanted a record of their faces and their respectability, and they got it from painters who worked quickly and charged modestly. The dark coat and high white collar are the conventional dress of the respectable man, and the absence of props lets the face carry the portrait. The sitter's name, Aaron Baldwin, is all the title offers.","c":"Alexander was one of Boston's most sought-after portraitists in the 1830s, and this quiet picture shows why: it is direct and unfussy, with a convincing head and no distracting detail. It lacks the glamour of Gilbert Stuart or the polish of Thomas Sully, and the plain background can make it seem austere. But the honesty of the face is its strength, and it makes a good comparison with the more romantic portrait of Sarah Blake Sturgis."},"nga:34066":{"d":"A young woman faces forward, her head angled slightly to her left. She has fair skin, rosy cheeks, dark eyes, red lips and brown curly hair, and she wears a teal dress with a lace scarf and a teal headband, set against a dark, solid background. The canvas is about 61 by 46 centimeters and dated 1835, a bust-length portrait whose single color and plain ground concentrate attention on the sitter.","i":"The sitter, Sarah Blake Sturgis, was about twenty when this was painted. She married the Boston merchant and abolitionist Francis George Shaw and became the mother of Robert Gould Shaw, who led the 54th Massachusetts, one of the first Black regiments in the Civil War, and of the reformer Josephine Shaw Lowell. A portrait of 1835 shows the fashionable curls and lace of a young woman of a wealthy Boston family.","c":"A fresh, pleasant portrait of a young woman, painted with clear drawing and clean color, a little sweeter than the sober male portraits Alexander also painted. The dark ground and single strong color give it bold simplicity. Alexander flatters moderately, and the likeness avoids sentimentality, while the lace and curls add decorative interest. It is a good example of fashionable Boston portraiture of the 1830s."},"nga:166461":{"d":"Two men, a dog and a horse gather at the wide front of a barn. A blond man in a tall straw hat and olive-green jacket leans on the right side of the opening, ankles crossed and hands in pockets, a slender greyhound at his leg. At the left a dark-haired man in a loose red shirt bends to work on a horse’s hoof, a tool box at his feet. A shadowed woman stands behind the horse, and a fire flickers in a hearth. Autumn leaves, a broken plow and a handbill reading “STOP THEIF!!!,” misspelled, fill out the corners.","i":"The title sets idleness against work, and the picture does so through its two men: one dressed for a stroll and leaning back, the other with sleeves rolled up and bent to his task. It is a rural genre scene of the kind that appealed to American audiences in the 1850s, when farm life was becoming a subject for nostalgia, humor and mild social comment. The misspelled handbill may be a joke that the viewer is left to place.","c":"Mayer tells his story with clarity: the contrast of the two men is plain at once, the horse’s head is silhouetted against the window, and sunlight dapples the barn wall. The picture is precise rather than inspired, and its moral is a little pat, but the detail of autumn leaves, tools and the misspelled poster rewards looking. It shows American genre painting of the 1850s at its most carefully observed."},"met:11524":{"d":"A street in the old part of Annapolis, Maryland, is the subject, with trees, houses and a cart in the scene. The oil on canvas, about 40 by 52 cm, is dated 1876. The title names an actual street, which makes the picture a topographical record of a town the painter knew; Francis Blackwell Mayer was a Marylander trained in Paris, and he is closely associated with Annapolis.","i":"The year 1876 was the centennial of American independence, and it produced a wave of interest in colonial buildings and towns. Annapolis, the colonial capital of Maryland, was rich in eighteenth-century brick houses, and a picture of a quiet old street reads as a tribute to a vanishing past, set against the new industrial country. The cart and the trees give the scene a settled, everyday air rather than a monumental one.","c":"A small, direct picture of a modest street, interesting more for its subject than for its brilliance. Mayer's French training should have given him a sure hand, and the unglamorous choice of an ordinary old street is itself a modern gesture, anticipating the later American taste for colonial towns. It is quiet, affectionate and minor, a record in the way a good local view always is."},"met:437468":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 205 by 123 cm, showing the portico of a country house, with human figures, columns, a fountain and ruined masonry; those are the Met’s subject terms. The record gives no fuller description, but the upright format and large size suggest a decorative panel designed to be set into the wall of a salon, as a window onto a garden or park. It is dated 1773 and is one of several large Hubert Robert canvases at the Met.","i":"Hubert Robert, nicknamed Robert of the Ruins, made architecture the theme of his career: classical porticos, fountains and ruins softened by figures going about everyday business. Such scenes joined two Enlightenment tastes, admiration for Roman antiquity and affection for the picturesque garden, with the decay of old stone reminding viewers of time passing. Reviewing the Salon of 1767, the critic Diderot wrote that Robert’s ruins stirred grand thoughts of the passing of all things.","c":"Robert is at his best in light and air: warm stone against clear sky, and little figures that give scale and life. His large decorative canvases can be formulaic, the same elements recombined, and the figures sometimes seem like stage extras. This is a handsome example of that production, admired more for its atmosphere than for any single passage, a pleasure to look at though not a deep one."},"cma:148860":{"d":"Two small canvases made as companions, each about 43 by 33 cm: a view inside the colonnade of Saint Peter’s in Rome during the conclave, and a view into the Grotto of Posillipo near Naples. The museum notes that the colonnade picture is based on a drawing of 1758, the year Clement XIII was elected, and that Robert aged its columns and added a soldier in ancient armor. The grotto, a Roman tunnel over 2,000 feet long, is shown grander than it ever was, with coffered vaults and engaged columns.","i":"Paired views of two famous Italian sights suited collectors who had made, or dreamed of, the Grand Tour, and the contrast is built in: a public colonnade open to the sky against a dark passage cut through rock. In both, Robert alters reality to give the architecture an ancient grandeur. He had studied in Italy from 1754 to 1765, and classical architectural subjects dominated his work after his return to Paris.","c":"As a pair the two pictures work well: open, columned light against enclosed tunnel dark, one a public space and one a passage through rock. Robert’s changes to the real places are the point, and they show his inventive streak, while the small format keeps the effects crisp. Neither has the ambition of his large decorative canvases, but together they are a lively demonstration of how he turned topography into imagination."},"cma:148861":{"d":"A view inside the colonnade of Saint Peter’s in Rome, on a small canvas of about 43 by 33 cm. The museum says it is based on a drawing dated 1758, the year Pope Clement XIII Rezzonico was elected. Robert aged the columns and added a soldier in ancient Roman armor, so that Bernini’s Baroque colonnade reads like an ancient monument. The title ties the scene to the conclave, the gathering of cardinals that elects a new pope.","i":"Robert studied in Italy from 1754 to 1765 and returned to Roman subjects for the rest of his career. Adding a soldier in ancient armor to a Baroque colonnade is a deliberate fiction, one that makes Rome a city where antiquity and the present share the same space. The date of the drawing, 1758, matches the conclave that elected Clement XIII, so the subject is tied to a real event even though the image is partly invented.","c":"A small, deft picture. Bernini’s colonnade becomes a long perspective of columns with deep shadow and bright openings, and the clash between modern event and ancient costume is witty. Robert’s invention outweighs his accuracy: he trades topography for mood. It is a charming cabinet piece, less grand than his big decorative canvases but more exact in drawing, and its companion, the Grotto of Posillipo, plays the same trick in a darker key."},"cma:148862":{"d":"A view into the Grotto of Posillipo, the tunnel that connects Naples with Pozzuoli, on a small canvas of about 43 by 33 cm. The museum notes that the Roman tunnel, over 2,000 feet long and carved from volcanic rock, was improved under Charles III of Naples but was never the grand passage Robert shows, with coffered vaults and engaged columns. The picture is therefore an invention, a fantasy of ancient engineering built on a real place.","i":"The Grotto was a famous stop on the Grand Tour, and the nearby tomb traditionally thought to be Virgil’s added a literary reason to visit. Robert turns the rough tunnel into a monumental ancient hall, in line with the eighteenth-century taste for imagined antiquity. It pairs with the colonnade picture, where he also ages the architecture. Together they suggest that for Robert the past was something to be imagined and improved, not simply recorded.","c":"The contrast of dark vault and bright mouth gives the picture its drama, and the columns set up a rhythm that leads the eye toward the light. It is an effective small fantasy rather than a record, and its deliberate falsification is part of the charm. The effect is atmospheric more than detailed, and it shows Robert’s talent for making architecture feel inhabited and mysterious with a few strokes."},"met:437479":{"d":"A tall decorative canvas, about 205 by 122 cm, of cattle returning to a farm, with a boy, a man and a woman: the Met’s subject terms are cattle, a boy, a man, a woman and a farm, and no fuller description is recorded. The upright format and scale match those of The Portico of a Country Mansion, of nearly the same date, so the two were probably made as companion panels for one room. The farm scene gives the pair a rustic counterpart to the portico’s classical grandeur.","i":"In the 1770s fashionable Parisians romanticized the countryside, and farm scenes with cattle and children suited the taste for rustic simplicity. The picture is not a record of farm labor but a pleasant fiction, country life as an urban patron might wish to see it from the salon. Pictures of this type were valued for the calm they brought to a room, and for the contrast with the city outside the windows.","c":"Robert is on less familiar ground with cattle and farmhands than with ruins and fountains, and the animals and people are less convincing than his usual small figures. The charm of the picture lies in its warm light and its easy, unforced composition, and as part of a decorative ensemble it would have worked well. Taken alone it is agreeable rather than memorable, a competent variation on a formula that suited him better in Italy."},"nga:41665":{"d":"A view of the Ponte Salario, the bridge by which the Via Salaria crosses the Aniene River just north of Rome, on a canvas of about 91 by 121 cm. The museum record gives no description, so what can be said rests on the title, the horizontal format and Robert’s habits: a picturesque Roman monument, probably with small figures busy in the foreground under a luminous sky. Robert left Italy in 1765, so a picture of about 1775 would have been made from drawings and memory.","i":"The Ponte Salario is an ancient bridge, rebuilt several times, on the road out of Rome to the north, and it was a favorite subject for painters of the city’s surroundings. For Robert and his French patrons, such a bridge combined Roman history, picturesque age and present-day life. Painted about a decade after he left Rome, the view is probably a recollection and, like many of his pictures, may blend record with invention.","c":"Robert’s Roman views are fluent and decorative, and at their best they make old stone seem warmed by the sun. The record cannot confirm how well this canvas achieves that, but the subject suits his talents, and its large format gives room for figures that supply scale. The formula, a monument framed by foliage with figures at the water’s edge, repeats across his career; its pleasant atmosphere, not its originality, is what recommends it."},"met:437473":{"d":"A large canvas, about 175 by 124 cm, showing a garden with a temple, a fountain and women bathing, the subject terms the Met records. It is dated 1777 to 1779 and belongs to a group of large decorative canvases by Hubert Robert at the Met, including The Dance, The Fountain, The Swing and Wandering Minstrels. Similar in size and date, they appear to have been designed as an ensemble. The record gives no more description, so details of figures and light are left to the picture.","i":"A bathing pool in a garden with a temple turns the park into an Arcadia where women bathe unobserved beneath classical architecture. Pictures like this belong to the late eighteenth-century fashion for garden fantasies, when owners of Parisian houses decorated salons with idealized landscapes, and Robert himself designed real gardens. Bathers in a landscape recalled both antique nymphs and the Rococo pleasures of Boucher and Fragonard.","c":"The sparkle of water, the pale temple and the airy trees make an attractive picture, and Robert’s high-keyed palette and quick touch suggest figures instead of portraying them. The women are generic and the design relaxed rather than rigorous, but as decoration it succeeds: it makes a room feel opened to a garden. A well-judged ornamental work in which the setting does more than the people."},"met:437476":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 173 by 85 cm, in which men and women dance beside a waterfall; those are the subject terms the Met gives. It was painted in 1777 to 1779 and is one of the group of large decorative canvases by Robert at the Met. The slender upright shape suggests a panel designed to fit between windows or doors; the record gives no more description of the dancers or the setting.","i":"Open-air dances and waterfalls belong to the pastoral fantasy of the late eighteenth century, when fashionable gardens were laid out with picturesque cascades and artificial rocks, as Robert himself designed. A dance in this setting expresses a gentle idea of rustic happiness, with gaiety and nature in harmony. No story is recorded for the picture; the subject is atmosphere, and the viewer’s task was simply to enjoy it, as one might a stroll in a garden.","c":"The tall format forces Robert to stack his scene, with figures below and cascade above, and he does it with ease, turning the vertical into a virtue. The dancers are drawn as light silhouettes, and the pleasure is in the whole composition rather than in individual faces. Not a profound picture, but a graceful and well-managed example of decorative painting, and a fair measure of what French patrons wanted from him."},"met:437477":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 173 by 80 cm, of women washing at a fountain beneath a column and a monument. Those are the subject terms the Met records, and the picture belongs to the same group of large decorative canvases painted by Hubert Robert in 1777 to 1779. The record gives no further description, so the treatment of the figures and light is left to the picture; the combination of monument, fountain and everyday work is Robert’s familiar mix.","i":"Washerwomen at a fountain among antique monuments were Robert’s way of joining ancient grandeur and everyday life, a mix he knew from the Rome of his student years, where fountains and old ruins served ordinary people. The contrast between permanent stone and passing human business is the picture’s underlying idea. Eighteenth-century viewers were invited to reflect that monuments outlast the people who use them, while daily life simply goes on.","c":"A pleasant example of Robert’s decorative formula: a monument anchoring the composition, women at their work giving it life, and light to join them. It is more ornamental than searching, the figures are accents more than characters, and the column and fountain do most of the work. Yet the soft light and the easy balance of stone and figures show the skill that made him a favorite of French patrons."},"met:437480":{"d":"A swing hangs by ropes from a tree in a garden with a statue, a balustrade, a tree stump and a dog; the subject terms also list women, men and children playing. The tall canvas, about 173 by 88 cm, was painted in 1777 to 1779 as one of the large decorative canvases by Hubert Robert at the Met. The record gives no further description, so the exact grouping and costumes are left to the picture.","i":"The swing is the best-known emblem of eighteenth-century French leisure, thanks to Fragonard’s The Swing of 1767 to 1768, which treats the motif in a playful, amorous key. Robert’s garden version looks more innocent, with children and families and a statue presiding. Here the swing is a sign of carefree pleasure in a park, and the statue and balustrade add a note of classical order to the fun.","c":"Beside Fragonard’s flirtatious swing, Robert’s is a family affair, more wholesome and less daring, with a statue and garden architecture to keep the tone decorous. The painting is relaxed in handling and attractive in its airy light, but it lacks Fragonard’s wit and sensuous charge. As one panel in a set it supplies variety of mood; taken alone it is a gentle, graceful picture."},"met:437481":{"d":"Musicians perform near a fountain and an obelisk, the subject terms the Met gives. The canvas, about 175 by 123 cm and dated 1777 to 1779, is one of the museum’s group of large decorative panels by Hubert Robert. It is wider than its companions The Dance and The Swing, and the record gives no further description of the minstrels’ number or costume, so those are left to the picture. The setting suggests an imagined Roman piazza or park.","i":"Wandering musicians in a park or a Roman piazza were a standard picturesque subject, adding sound and humanity to the architecture. The obelisk and fountain place the scene in a Rome of the imagination, and the minstrels, as itinerant entertainers, belonged to the lower orders that the picturesque style liked to observe. For patrons they offered a pleasant, harmless image of popular life.","c":"The obelisk and the fountain supply the grandeur and the minstrels supply the charm. Robert’s gift for fitting small human incidents into large architecture is evident, though the performers are more decorative than characterized. The composition is airy and balanced, and the paint is fluent. It is, like its companions, a polished piece of decoration more than a deeply felt picture."},"met:437470":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 175 by 79 cm, with a man and a woman at the mouth of a cave; the Met’s subject terms are a cave, a man and a woman. It is dated 1784, some years after the 1777 to 1779 group, though similar in height and shape to The Dance and The Fountain. The record gives no further description of the rocks, the light or the figures, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"Caves and grottoes were fixtures of the picturesque garden, and Robert designed such rockwork himself, notably at the Bains d’Apollon at Versailles. A cave mouth is a threshold between daylight and darkness, a motif that both the picturesque and the sublime loved: it offers shelter, mystery and a contrast of light and shade. The man and woman give human scale, and they may be travelers or herders at rest, though the record does not say so.","c":"Robert’s caves are as much about light as about rock: a dark interior, a bright rim, and small figures as lit accents. The tall format is used with confidence. As often with his decorative panels, the figures are stock types and the effect is atmospheric more than dramatic, but the contrast of dark and light is bold and well controlled, and it shows he could do more than ruins and fountains."},"met:437471":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 82 by 138 cm, showing a ruined aqueduct with a man, cows, dogs and sheep; those are the subject terms the Met gives. The record offers no date or further description. Roman aqueducts, with their long arcades striding across the countryside, were a favorite subject for Robert, and the herd animals suggest that the ruin has become part of a working pastoral landscape.","i":"An aqueduct was a symbol of Roman engineering power, and its ruin in a field where cows graze sets ancient empire against humble present life. Robert’s Italy was full of such sights, and French viewers read them as meditations on time and the fate of civilizations. A wide landscape panel of this kind could also work as a decorator’s tool, opening a wall onto distance.","c":"The long arcade gives the picture a strong horizontal rhythm, and the cows and sheep tell the story of survival at a glance. Robert handles the stonework with a convincing sense of weight, while the figures are only small accents. It is a well-made, somewhat formulaic variation on his ruin theme, calm and even a little sleepy, which was probably what a decorative commission asked for."},"met:437472":{"d":"A long, low canvas, about 59 by 156 cm, showing ruined arches with a child, a man and a woman; the Met’s terms are ruins, arch, child, man and woman. The record gives no date and no further description. The wide, shallow shape suggests a panel to hang over a door or a mirror, and its size nearly matches that of A Colonnade in Ruins, which suggests the two were made as a pair for the decoration of a room.","i":"Ruined arches were Robert’s signature subject: ancient Roman architecture overgrown and half collapsed, with ordinary people living at its feet. In Rome he absorbed the example of Panini’s views and Piranesi’s etchings of antiquity, and he returned to the theme for the rest of his career. The mood is reflective more than melancholy, since ruins invited viewers to think about time, empire and the endurance of everyday life.","c":"The wide, low format gives the ruins a processional rhythm, and the arches repeat across the canvas like a musical phrase. The figures are tiny, and the real subject is the architecture, its weight, texture and decay. As a decorative panel it is effective rather than searching, but the repetition of arches is well judged and the broken masonry has convincing weight."},"met:437474":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 81 by 138 cm, with a bridge over a waterfall; the Met’s terms list a bridge, a horse, cows and a waterfall. The record gives no date or further description. The size is almost identical to that of Aqueduct in Ruins, so the two were probably companions, one showing a ruin of the Roman world and the other a bridge over a cascade, and both suited to hang in the same room.","i":"Waterfalls and bridges belong to the picturesque landscape that Robert favored, with rushing water, rock and a human crossing setting wild nature against human making. Cascades like this were also staples of fashionable gardens, which Robert helped to design. With cows and horses crossing, the scene is a calm, rural version of the sublime, nature’s power softened for the salon.","c":"The cascade gives movement where Robert’s ruins give stillness, and the bridge’s arch lends the picture a firm structure. Cows and horses are stock figures, and the foam and spray are handled more as decorative pattern than as observation, but the effect is lively and fresh. A good partner to Aqueduct in Ruins, with the same competence and the same limits."},"met:437475":{"d":"A long, low canvas, about 58 by 155 cm, of a ruined colonnade with men, women and dogs among the columns; the Met’s terms are columns, ruins, men, women and dogs. The record gives no date or further description. Its format and size nearly match those of Arches in Ruins, so the two were probably made as companions, perhaps to hang over doors or mirrors in the same room.","i":"A colonnade, a row of columns carrying a roof or beam, stands for classical architecture at its most orderly, and in ruin it becomes a symbol of time’s work. The figures wandering through it are ordinary people using a vanished grandeur as a street or a shelter, a favorite idea of Robert’s. Classical ruins also made a flattering setting for a patron who wished to be seen as a person of taste, even in a modern drawing room.","c":"The row of columns marches across the canvas with a rhythm that suits the wide format, and the ruined sections break it up convincingly. The people and dogs add life. It is a competent, pleasing decorative panel that adds little to what Robert does better elsewhere, but its steadiness and atmosphere are reliable, and the composition holds together well."},"nga:61393":{"d":"Three people sit and stand together in a garden against layers of green foliage. At the left an older woman with gray hair in a lace-trimmed updo, in a light green gown and white ruffled shawl, looks toward a young woman at the center, dressed in pink, with white feathers and small yellow flowers in her curls and a lute-like instrument in her lap. Behind them at the right a smiling man in gray, holding a black hat and cane and with his legs crossed, looks out at the viewer. The canvas is about 92 by 71 cm.","i":"Informal groups in the open air, called conversation pieces, were an English specialty of the 1700s. They showed a family or friends at ease in a garden or parlor, performing the polite accomplishments of genteel life: music, courtesy and affection. The instrument is a sign of a young woman’s education and charm. The record does not name the sitters, so who they are, and whether the man is husband or father to the others, remains open.","c":"Wheatley’s small-scale figure style is graceful, neat and colorful, and his sitters are shown with an easy, courteous affability. The picture lacks the psychological bite of the best English portraiture, and the figures are somewhat stiffly arranged, but the delicate pinks and greens and the polished surface make an attractive whole. A charming period record, less searching than Gainsborough’s family groups."},"met:440568":{"d":"A family group appears in an interior: a man, a woman and a child, with girls among the sitters, in fashionable dress with ruffles, bows and a feathered hat, on a carpet. The oil on canvas, about 98 by 127 cm, dates from about 1785. It is a conversation piece, a family portrait that shows the sitters at ease in their own surroundings, a type popular in eighteenth-century England.","i":"The conversation piece, developed by Hogarth and carried on by Zoffany, showed families as a unit, with their taste and possessions on view. By the 1780s it also carried the sentimental ideal of the affectionate household, with parents and children bound in domestic harmony. Whatever the Saithwaites' particular history, the picture's message is the familiar one of the age: prosperity, refinement and family feeling, offered to guests and to descendants alike.","c":"Wheatley is remembered chiefly for his Cries of London, but his conversation pieces share the same lightness of touch and good-natured observation. The pleasures are elegance and charm, and the delicate rendering of ruffles, bows and feathers. He lacks the psychological bite of Zoffany, and his poses can be stiff. Typical of its time, it is a pleasant, well-bred record of a family at ease."},"met:436863":{"d":"A wide landscape of about 87 by 138 cm, dated 1865, records the island home of General Giuseppe Garibaldi on Caprera, off the north coast of Sardinia. Garibaldi, the hero of Italian unification, settled on the island in the 1850s and lived there as a farmer between campaigns. Frederick Richard Lee, a Royal Academician best known for rural English landscapes, turned his skill to a topical subject.","i":"Garibaldi was an international celebrity in the 1860s, and his visit to England in 1864 was received with enormous crowds. British admirers saw in his simple island farm an image of the soldier who returns to the plow, a modern Cincinnatus. A picture of his residence served hero-worship through topography, letting a viewer picture the general at home among his crops and animals, far from the courts and politics.","c":"Lee's strength is the long, calm English landscape tradition; applying it to a rocky Mediterranean island is a stretch, and the picture's interest is topical, almost a pilgrim's image for Garibaldi's admirers. As painting it is competent and orderly rather than inspired. It documents a taste for political celebrity as much as a taste for landscape, which is what makes it more interesting than its modest artistry would suggest."},"met:437935":{"d":"An old bridge over a stream, with a man and a dog nearby, is the subject of this oil on canvas of about 55 by 83 cm, painted about 1828. Hendon was then a village in Middlesex, north of London, and is now part of Greater London. Frederick Waters Watts was a landscape painter who followed the example of John Constable, painting the English countryside from observation rather than from classical formula.","i":"In the 1820s English landscape painters turned toward unromanticized scenes of the home countryside, a movement Constable led. An old bridge was both picturesque and a reminder of the working landscape, with a path for farm traffic over the stream. The village was later absorbed by London's growth, which gives the scene a nostalgia that the painter did not intend.","c":"Watts follows Constable's example in the freshness of green and the broad handling of sky and water, but lacks his master's depth of feeling and his gift for the telling touch. The picture's pleasures are modest: a good example of the naturalistic landscape of the 1820s, honest and agreeable. It is a minor artist in the shadow of a great one, and the shadow is both his teacher and his limit."},"met:436266":{"d":"The Met’s subject terms list the elements of the story: an aged Isaac in bed, his sons Jacob and Esau, their mother Rebecca, a table and Esau’s quiver, all in a bedroom interior. Painted in 1642 on a canvas of about 101 by 128 cm, the picture gathers the family in one dim chamber, with the drama carried by gesture and light. The record gives no further description, so the exact poses and colors are left to the picture itself.","i":"The subject comes from Genesis 27. Isaac, old and blind, asks his elder son Esau to hunt game and prepare a meal before receiving his blessing. Rebecca, who favors Jacob, has him disguise himself in goatskins and bring the food, so that the blessing goes to him instead. The story was popular in Rembrandt’s circle in Amsterdam because it joins deceit and warm family feeling, and it let painters show touch, glance and light inside a dim chamber.","c":"Eeckhout, a pupil of Rembrandt in the 1630s, handles the story in his master’s idiom: a warmly lit interior, shadows pressing in, and drama carried by gesture and glance. He brings a young painter’s earnestness to it, in his early twenties. Govert Flinck’s version of the subject, in Amsterdam, is better known, but Eeckhout’s shows the same school at work, and the shared approach explains why the Rembrandt circle returned to the theme."},"nga:75164":{"d":"Four people, a dog and a donkey gather beside a copse of trees before a run-down town. At the left a barefoot, grey-bearded man in a fur-trimmed cap holds a tool in one hand and extends the other towards the rest, while a dog sniffs his bare foot. At the lower right a man in a yellow robe, red cape and feathered turban sits on a rock, head in one hand, the other raised; beside him a woman in a teal cloak holds a tiny grey dog that bares its teeth at the old man. Behind them stand a casket and two donkeys, under a sunset sky.","i":"The story is from the Book of Judges, chapter 19, one of the darkest in the Bible. A Levite travelling with his concubine and servant reaches Gibeah at nightfall and finds no one willing to take them in, until an old man coming from the fields offers his house; that night the townsmen attack it, the concubine dies, and the Levite's call for vengeance leads to civil war among the tribes of Israel. Eeckhout paints the moment of welcome, the dog and ruined town hinting at what follows.","c":"A learned, atmospheric early picture by a young follower of Rembrandt: the glowing evening sky, the exotic dress and the hints of menace, in the little dog's bared teeth and the ruined town, give a story of hospitality a shadow. The figures are somewhat stiff, the group static, and the story obscure enough to need its title; Eeckhout lacks Rembrandt's psychological depth. Yet as an unusual subject treated with sympathy and a rich palette, it rewards attention."},"met:436267":{"d":"Men and women make music, with a dog nearby; the Met’s terms are men, women, music and dogs. The canvas is small, about 51 by 62 cm, and dated to the early 1650s. The record gives no further description of the instruments, the number of figures or the setting, so those are left to the picture itself. It is a cabinet picture of the kind a Dutch collector would hang in a private room.","i":"Musical parties were a favorite Dutch subject. They show leisure, courtship and harmony, and in the hands of some painters they carry a moral note about vanity or lust. The dog is often a sign of fidelity, though in other contexts it can mean appetite. Eeckhout, better known for biblical and history pictures, painted such company scenes in the 1650s, when the type was in vogue in Amsterdam.","c":"Eeckhout had a talent for easy, fluent storytelling, and a musical party let him exercise it without the burden of a heavy theme. The type is well worn, and he does not aim at the finish of Ter Borch or the subtlety of Vermeer, but there is an appealing naturalness and a Rembrandtesque warmth in the light. A pleasant, minor work by a painter whose range was wider than his fame."},"nga:46125":{"d":"An older man, a young man and a dog stand on a verandah that opens onto a tree-filled landscape. The older man at the left, with long silver-gray hair, a thin white mustache and a lead-gray jacket, turns his head away, holds gathered black velvet in one hand and rests the other on the young man’s shoulder. The young man has thick chestnut curls, an orange ribbon at his neck and a russet jacket over an embroidered gold and blue vest; he looks out at the viewer and points toward the dog. The signature is painted as if carved on the chipped balustrade: “N. De Largillierre F. 1685.”","i":"The title calls the older man a tutor, and the picture presents a familiar relationship: a mentor in sober dress beside a youth in fashionable color, the heir to a household. The record does not name the pair. Such double portraits told viewers that the young man’s education was supervised and an investment of his family. The signature rendered as if cut in stone is a playful, learned device, since it claims for painting the permanence of sculpture.","c":"An early large portrait by Largillierre, painted a few years after his time in England and ahead of his grand Parisian manner. The glossy brown fur of the dog, the silvery gray jacket, the bright ribbon and the glowing velvet are done with obvious relish. The setting, with columns and balustrade, is a little stage-managed, but the two faces are alive, and the contrast of ages gives the picture its warmth."},"met:436846":{"d":"A woman and a young boy share the canvas with a parrot and a dog, in a portrait of about 140 by 107 cm dated 1696. The museum’s title identifies the boy as an enslaved servant, and its subject terms list a woman, a boy, a parrot, a dog and a servant. The record gives no description of dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture. The woman is the sitter and has no name in the title; the child is a subordinate figure placed beside her.","i":"Portraits of wealthy European women with a young servant, a parrot and a dog were a convention of the late 1600s, and the child, probably of African descent by the usual pattern of such pictures, like the exotic bird, advertised the sitter’s wealth and her reach into the colonial trade. Such children were enslaved or held in servitude, and the museum’s present title names that condition plainly. Reading the picture means seeing both the sitter’s status and the system that supported it.","c":"Largillierre’s skill with fabrics, flesh and animals is evident in the type, and the grouping of woman, child, parrot and dog makes a lively arrangement. But the picture is hard to enjoy without noticing how the child is used: as a foil, a prop and a sign of the sitter’s rank. A critic can admire the craft and still see the composition’s conventions as part of a history that the museum’s title now states openly."},"nga:134427":{"d":"A man sits in a red cushioned chair, seen from the waist up with his body turned to the left and his face turned to the viewer. He wears a white striped head covering that resembles a beret, a reddish-brown coat with white ruffled sleeves, a white collar and a light pink ribbon. In his hands are drawing tools, a pencil or pen and a stack of papers. Behind him are small tan and white sculptures, including a nude male figure, a cherub and a woman’s head, with paint brushes and what could be a palette, against gray walls. The canvas is about 93 by 73 cm.","i":"Largillierre presents himself as a working artist in the studio, with the drawing tools and the small statues that stand for the study of sculpture and the antique. A soft cap in place of a formal wig was the usual sign of an artist at work. In 1707 he was about fifty-one, successful, and long a member of the Académie royale, which he had joined in 1686, and a self-portrait was a way to assert the dignity of his craft.","c":"A straightforward, even unflattering self-image: the cheeks sag slightly, the pose is relaxed and the props are quietly arranged. There is none of the glamour that Largillierre supplied for his sitters. The reddish coat and red chair give the picture warmth, and the sculptures and brushes explain his craft without fuss. It is a modest, attractive record of a society painter at home, more candid than most self-portraits of the period."},"met:436847":{"d":"A formal portrait of a man named in the title as André François Alloys de Theys d’Herculais, who lived from 1692 to 1779 and was therefore about thirty-five when Largillierre painted him in 1727. The canvas measures about 138 by 105 cm. The record gives no description of pose, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture itself; the format is that of a three-quarter-length commission for a gentleman of standing in the France of Louis XV.","i":"Largillierre was the leading portraitist of the Parisian professional and financial classes as well as the court, and his portraits of the 1720s combine a firm likeness with an elegant, slightly relaxed pose and richly painted fabrics. The long name, with a place name attached, suggests a family with a landed estate, though the record does not say what office or career the sitter had, so nothing more can be asserted here.","c":"Largillierre is more convincing with a face than with a character: the likeness is brisk and plausible, while the clothes, in the manner of the time, do much of the talking. By 1727 the formula was fully formed, and the pleasure of a portrait like this lies in its fluent execution and in the ease it gives the sitter. It is a good example of the polished, rather impersonal portraiture that Parisians of rank expected."},"nga:50254":{"d":"Shown from about the lap up, a light-skinned woman sits facing the viewer in a voluminous garment that covers her head and forehead beneath a black veil that falls past her shoulders. Light from the left falls on her smooth skin and flushed cheeks, and her gray eyes look out from under gently arched brows. The garment, a wimple, is pleated across chest and sleeves, and the veil rises in a stiff peak above her head. Her right hand rests in her lap with a finger marking her place in a small book. A gold inscription runs across the top of the peanut-brown ground.","i":"The inscription calls her the daughter of Sir Robert Throckmorton, Baronet. The Throckmortons were a prominent English Catholic family, and since English law did not allow Catholic convents, daughters who wished to take vows often went abroad. The Dames Augustines Anglaises, an English Augustinian community in Paris, received them. The book with its marked place shows a nun at prayer or study, and the portrait may have been made for her family.","c":"A quiet, dignified portrait that avoids the display of Largillierre’s secular sitters. The folds of the habit and the black veil make strong shapes, and the face, lit from the left, is rendered with gentle realism, with a hint of a smile at the corners of the lips. It is a convincing study of an inward character, and the austere palette suits the subject. A small, moving departure from his Parisian society manner."},"met:436309":{"d":"A bearded man in a velvet cap, shown on a nearly square panel of about 60 by 52 cm. The Met’s subject terms list a hat, a man and a portrait, and the record gives no further description of his features, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture. The title gives no name, so he is an unidentified sitter; the format is that of a small, intimate portrait or character study, painted in 1645.","i":"Govert Flinck trained in Rembrandt’s workshop in the 1630s, and a bearded man in a soft velvet cap is a Rembrandt-school type, one that blurs the line between portrait and character study. The cap and beard give an air of age and learning. By 1645 Flinck was becoming a leading portraitist and history painter in Amsterdam, and sober heads like this show his mastery of his master’s lessons while he moved toward a smoother, lighter style.","c":"By 1645 Flinck had moved away from Rembrandt’s shadows toward a more open, polished manner, and a head like this shows both influences: the soft, warm light of the master with a cleaner, more finished surface. It lacks Rembrandt’s depth of feeling, and the type is a familiar one, but it is a capable, attractive panel by a painter who would win major Amsterdam town-hall commissions in the 1650s."},"met:437405":{"d":"A young woman dressed as a shepherdess, painted on a panel that has since been transferred to canvas, about 67 by 51 cm. The record links the figure with Flora, goddess of flowers, and with Saskia van Uylenburgh, Rembrandt’s wife, and classes the picture as a tronie, a Dutch character study in costume. The record gives no date or further description of her dress, attributes or setting, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"In Rembrandt’s circle in the 1630s, painted costume pieces of young women as shepherdesses or as Flora were a fashion. They let the painter show off exotic dress, flowers and soft light, and they let the buyer enjoy an Arcadian fantasy. Rembrandt painted Saskia in such roles, and his pupils followed. Whether the model here is Saskia, as the link suggests, cannot be settled from the record, and the picture is better read as a type than as a likeness.","c":"Flinck follows Rembrandt closely in subject and mood, and the picture is graceful, with soft light, delicate coloring and an inviting face. It is more polite than Rembrandt’s own treatment: the young woman is pretty rather than particular, and the shepherdess costume is a pleasant pretext. Valuable as evidence of the master’s influence on his pupil and as a pleasing period piece, but a slightly bland picture beside the work that inspired it."},"met:436710":{"d":"A woman with a book, in a miniature painted on ivory in 1790. The Met’s subject terms list a book, a woman, a neck ribbon, a shawl and lace, and call the work a miniature portrait. The size is not recorded, but miniatures of this kind are small enough to be held in the palm. The record gives no further description of her pose, her surroundings or the colors, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"Miniatures on ivory were the private portraits of the late eighteenth century: keepsakes exchanged between lovers and family members and kept in cases, lockets or boxes. A woman reading was a fashionable image of sensibility in the years before the Revolution, when reading was a sign of feeling and cultivation. Isabey, then in his early twenties, was at the start of a career that would carry him to the court of Napoleon.","c":"Isabey’s early miniatures are marked by soft, delicate touches and gentle color, and a subject like a woman reading allows him to play with fabrics, lace and the light on a face. The pose is conventional and the sentiment mild, but the finish is exquisitely controlled. It is a charming product of the last years of the old regime, valued for craft more than for psychology."},"met:436711":{"d":"A miniature portrait of a woman on ivory, dated about 1795. The Met’s subject terms list a bow, a ribbon, a fichu, a neckerchief and a stripe, so her dress is marked by a soft fichu or neckerchief, a ribbon and bow, and a stripe in the fabric. The sitter is unnamed, and the size is not recorded. The record gives no further description of her face, hair or background, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"The fichu, a light triangle of fabric worn around the neck and crossed over the bodice, and the simple bow and ribbon, are marks of the plainer fashion that followed the Revolution, when the elaborate silks of the old court gave way to lighter, simpler dress. An unnamed sitter in a small ivory portrait was probably painted for her family, a husband or a lover. Isabey worked in Paris through these years, and his clients were the new society that emerged.","c":"In a tiny space Isabey gives the face an easy, natural air and handles the soft fichu with a light touch, and the plain styling matches the simple tastes of the years after the Terror. As craft it is delightful, though the type is conventional and one pretty sitter resembles another. Its merit lies in refinement more than in character, and Isabey’s later court portraits are grander without always being more alive."},"met:436713":{"d":"A miniature portrait on ivory of the painter’s wife, shown sewing, dated about 1796 to 1800. The title names her as Jeanne Laurice de Salienne and gives her death as 1829, and the Met’s subject terms list sewing and a woman. The size is not recorded, and the record gives no further description of her costume, setting or the colors, so those are left to the picture itself. Records spell her name in more than one way.","i":"A portrait of a wife by her husband is a private picture, made for the family and not for display or sale, and often more relaxed than a commission. Showing a woman at needlework was a standard sign of domestic virtue, but here it also gives the sitter something to do, so that the miniature records a moment of ordinary life instead of a formal pose. Isabey painted many miniatures of the Napoleonic elite; this one is of his own household.","c":"Intimate and unforced. Without needing to flatter a client, Isabey can paint his wife attentively, and the occupation of sewing gives the picture a natural downward gaze and an unposed look. The technique is skilled, but the main quality is sympathy. Among his miniatures, which are often polished to the point of blandness, a work like this stands out as personal and warm."},"met:436714":{"d":"A miniature portrait of Napoleon I on ivory, dated 1810. The Met’s subject terms list Napoleon, a man and a portrait, and the size is not recorded. The record gives no description of his uniform, pose or background, so those are left to the picture itself. In 1810 Napoleon was forty or forty-one, newly remarried to Marie-Louise of Austria, and at the height of his power as emperor of the French.","i":"Isabey was Napoleon’s favorite miniaturist and a designer of his ceremonies, and he drew and painted the emperor and his family repeatedly. Miniature portraits of Napoleon were also official gifts, set in snuffboxes and frames and given to loyal servants, allies and diplomats, so a small ivory likeness served the regime as image-making. The format made the emperor’s face portable and personal, a counterpart to the great state portraits.","c":"Isabey’s Napoleons are usually flattering: the features are refined, the gaze steady, the imperial calm assured. That is part of their purpose and part of their limit, since the miniature gives an image of the emperor as he wished to be seen, with little of the man’s restlessness. Technically the work is very fine; historically it is valuable as an official face, not a candid view."},"met:436715":{"d":"Another ivory miniature of Napoleon I, dated 1812, two years after the Met’s earlier one. The subject terms list Napoleon, a man and a portrait; the record gives no description of dress, pose or background and no size, so those are left to the picture itself. In 1812 Napoleon was forty-two or forty-three, and the year brought the invasion of Russia, in which the Grande Armée was shattered.","i":"A portrait of 1812 was made at the peak and the turning point of the empire, since the year began with the empire at its widest and ended with the retreat from Moscow. Miniatures such as this continued the stream of imperial likenesses that Isabey supplied, and viewers today, knowing what followed, may read the image against the history. The picture itself was made as an image of authority and permanence.","c":"A highly finished official image: Isabey’s emperor is composed and in control. The craft is of the highest order, with precise, soft modeling in a tiny space, and its value for a historian is as propaganda just as it is for an art lover as miniature craft. As a portrait of a man it tells less than a candid sketch would, but as a measure of the regime’s self-image it is exact."},"met:436718":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed woman painted on a thin plate of ivory, about 1815. The museum's record gives no size, sitter or description of dress, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait miniature, the small, finely worked likeness made to be held in the hand or set in a case, locket or frame and examined at close range. Jean-Baptiste Isabey, the French painter credited here, was the leading miniaturist of the Napoleonic era, and the picture belongs to the kind of ivory portrait for which he was famous.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the intimate portraits of the age: given to relatives and lovers, worn as jewelry, carried abroad and kept as remembrances. Isabey painted for Napoleon, for both of his empresses and then for the restored Bourbon kings, so his name stood for the height of fashion in Paris. Because this sitter is not named, her rank and story are unknown; the picture is best read as a keepsake likeness meant to be treasured by someone who knew her.","c":"Isabey's reputation rests on delicacy: soft modeling, a fluid watercolor touch that lets the ivory glow through the flesh, and a grace that flattered sitters under every regime. His charm can tip into sameness, with faces smoothed toward one polished type, and his best portraits are those in which the sitter's own features push back. With no named sitter and no description in the record, this one is best judged as a representative example of his refined, fashionable manner."},"met:436717":{"d":"A portrait of a young woman, identified in the title as Augusta Temple Palmer (1807 to 1840), who became Mrs. Rufus Prime, dated 1828. The museum lists the support as card rather than ivory and gives no size or description of costume, pose or setting, so only the kind of picture can be said: a delicately finished likeness by the leading French painter of portrait miniatures, presumably made for her family or her husband. At the date she was about twenty-one.","i":"Isabey's sitters came from the courts and the wealthy circles of Paris, and by 1828 a likeness by him was a mark of means and taste. The title records her as a wife, Mrs. Rufus Prime, the formal way a married woman was named, and gives her dates, 1807 to 1840, which show a life of only about thirty-three years. A portrait of this kind may well have been kept by her family as a personal memorial as well as a likeness.","c":"Isabey's later portraits are admired for their ease and clarity: a clean line, a light touch with flesh and a gentle gloss that suited Restoration taste. The weakness of the manner is predictability, since sitters are smoothed toward one pleasing ideal. The record describes nothing of this sheet, so a fair verdict can only be general: a polished product of the best-known miniaturist of his day, valued as much for its documented sitter as for invention."},"met:437170":{"d":"A drawing room given over to music. The museum's keywords list a woman, a man, a piano, a dog and an interior, and the oil panel (about 89 by 117 cm, dated 1878) places them in a comfortably furnished room. The record offers no further description, so poses, costumes and the identity of the figures are left to the viewer's eye. It is a picture of modern leisure rather than a portrait commission, with the figures set among the furnishings of a prosperous household.","i":"Making music was the central ritual of the nineteenth-century salon: the piano signaled education, leisure and the daughters' prospects, and an evening of music was a social occasion as much as a pleasure. Mihály Munkácsy, a Hungarian who made his career in Paris, painted such scenes of elegant society in the late 1870s, after earlier pictures of peasants and the poor. His marriage in 1874 to a wealthy widow, Cécile Papier, gave him entry to the world he shows.","c":"Munkácsy was a fluent, dramatic painter whose dark, rich tonality and confident drawing made him a Salon success, and interiors like this show how well he handled space, furniture and light. The risk of the manner is heaviness: the glossy finish and theatrical grouping can feel staged beside the lighter, quicker observation of the Impressionists working in the same city. Still, the picture records an affluent world's pleasures with evident ease and assurance."},"nga:41617":{"d":"The pale, bearded head of Christ, crowned with thorns, floats against a white cloth hung over a wooden rod, in this vertical panel of about 81 by 66 centimeters. His reddish-brown hair is wavy and chin-length, his heavy-lidded brown eyes look down, his lips are parted, and blood drips down his forehead. A brown spot on his cheek might be a bruise. The cloth is frayed along its front edge and painted in coral pink, icy blue, silvery white and dove gray against a black background.","i":"According to the medieval legend of Veronica, a woman wiped the face of Christ on the road to Calvary and his features remained on her cloth, a true image, in Latin vera icon, which gave her name. The veil became one of the most revered relics in Rome, and painters showed it as a head on a cloth. A picture like this invited the believer to contemplate Christ's suffering directly and intimately.","c":"Fetti paints the cloth with the virtuosity of a still-life painter, in loose, glowing touches of pale color, and the dark ground intensifies the effect of the head. The face is gentle and human rather than ecstatic, which gives the picture a quiet pathos. Fetti's warm, Venetian-influenced technique and a fluid handling that recalls Rubens are evident; it is moving without exaggeration."},"met:438169":{"d":"Christ appears as Salvator Mundi, the Saviour of the World, among clouds with putti, in a small oil on wood panel of about 60 by 44 centimetres. In this long-established type the figure faces the viewer, with a blessing hand and often a globe in the other. The museum's record gives the elements, Christ, putti and clouds, but not the details of pose or colour, so those cannot be reported with confidence. Fetti, working in Venice in 1622 or 1623, painted in a loose, flickering manner learned from Venetian masters.","i":"Salvator Mundi is a devotional type that spread across Europe in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries: a frontal Christ who blesses the viewer and holds an orb standing for the world he governs. Putti and clouds lift the picture towards the heavenly, placing Christ in glory. Small panels like this were made for private devotion, to be kept close and prayed before.","c":"Fetti was at his best in small, informal pictures, and his loose, sketchy handling is a pleasing counter to the polished Salvator Mundi of earlier generations. The risk with such quick paint is a loss of the solemnity the subject usually demands, and the putti can tip the picture towards the decorative. It dates from the last months of a short career, which gives it a poignancy beyond its modest scale."},"met:436301":{"d":"Two men act out a New Testament parable on a small oil panel, about 61 by 44 centimetres. The museum lists the subject as the mote and the beam, Christ's image of the man who fusses over a speck of dust in his brother's eye while a great timber sticks out of his own. Fetti usually told his parables as down-to-earth scenes among ordinary people in contemporary dress, with a freedom of touch that makes each look like a sketch from life.","i":"The parable comes from the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 7:3–5; also Luke 6:41–42), where Christ warns against judging others while blind to one's own greater faults. Fetti painted a series of parables in Mantua, where he was court painter to the Gonzaga; the small format and everyday realism made the moral teaching vivid and accessible. The absurdity of the beam in the eye allows a rare chance for humour in a religious picture.","c":"Fetti's parables are among the most original small religious pictures of the Baroque: Gospel teaching brought down to ordinary people, painted with a loose, flickering brush. The Mote and the Beam is a harder subject than most, since a speck and a beam are not easy to make legible in a small panel, and the viewer may depend on the title. Even so, the sketch-like freshness is delightful."},"nga:105":{"d":"A portrait of the young Prince of Wales, the future George IV, in oil on canvas, about 76 by 63 centimetres and dated 1781. The museum record gives only the sitter's name and title and notes a male sitter, so his dress, pose and background cannot be described with confidence. At the date of the picture the prince was about nineteen, and his image was already in demand as that of the heir to the throne and a young leader of fashion.","i":"George, Prince of Wales, eldest son of George III, became Prince Regent in 1811 and King George IV in 1820. He was one of the great patrons of the arts in Britain, and his likeness was sought from his teens. Gainsborough Dupont was the nephew and pupil of Thomas Gainsborough and carried on the family's fashionable portrait practice after his uncle died in 1788.","c":"Dupont is usually seen as a gifted follower of his uncle, with the Gainsborough feathery touch and fluent drawing, but he never reached the master's subtlety of colour. A portrait of the Prince by him is a record of an important sitter more than an exciting painting; beside Thomas Gainsborough's own portraits it is competent but lacks vivacity. Without close detail to examine, it is best valued as a document of its royal sitter."},"nga:52263":{"d":"A woman is shown full length, her body turned to the right and her head turned back towards the viewer. She has curly grey-white hair, arched eyebrows and a small mouth with a neutral expression, and wears an elegant light-coloured dress with a large bow at the back and a big dark feathered hat. A flower is tucked in her arms, which gather up some of her skirts. Tall trees rise behind her, with the edge of a wall in the upper right, and the whole picture has an olive-green tint. The canvas is small, about 59 by 40 centimetres.","i":"Georgiana Cavendish, Duchess of Devonshire (1757–1806), was one of the most famous women in Britain: a leader of fashion, a political hostess for the Whigs and a celebrated beauty whose huge feathered hats were widely imitated. Thomas Gainsborough painted her several times, and the large hat here recalls his portraits. This is a small version by his nephew Dupont, who painted copies and variants of his uncle's popular portraits in the years around and after Gainsborough's death in 1788.","c":"The costume, the plumed hat and the pose speak of the Duchess's celebrated style, and the soft green setting borrows the feathery landscape Gainsborough made his own. Dupont's version reduces the original to a pleasing souvenir; the figure is a little lifeless, the face expressionless and the olive-green tone monotonous. It is attractive as a record of fashion but cannot hold the eye as Gainsborough's own portraits do."},"nga:52264":{"d":"A very small oil on board, about 15 by 12 centimetres, shows the upper half of a man facing forward with his head slightly turned to one side, looking directly at the viewer. He has a fair complexion, a rounded face and closed lips, and his hair is dressed in the eighteenth-century manner with curls around the ears. He wears a dark coat with red lapels and brass buttons over a white shirt with ruffled details. No jewellery or objects are visible, and the background is a textured, earth-toned surface. The title names the sitter as William Pitt.","i":"William Pitt the Younger (1759–1806) became prime minister in 1783 at the age of twenty-four and held the office, with a break, until his death. Gainsborough Dupont painted portraits of public figures and carried on the practice of his uncle, Thomas Gainsborough, who died in 1788. A small panel like this may be a preparatory study or a reduced record of a larger portrait; the title does not say.","c":"At this scale the interest is the rapid, summary likeness: the red-lapelled coat and ruffled shirt are simply stated, and the face has a candid, slightly boyish look. It is a small, serviceable record and does not convey the intellect of a statesman; judged beside full portraits of Pitt, it is slight. Its charm is its modesty."},"nga:52266":{"d":"A woman sits outdoors and looks directly at the viewer with a serene expression, her features soft, her lips full and her eyes expressive. Her voluminous hair curls around her face and shoulders. She wears a light-coloured gown with sheer sleeves and a fitted waist. Behind her are trees and foliage, with a distant horizon, and the scene conveys a peaceful moment in nature. The canvas is small, about 59 by 40 centimetres, and the composition appears to follow the full-length portrait of the same sitter by Dupont's uncle, Thomas Gainsborough.","i":"The sitter is Elizabeth Linley, the celebrated soprano who married the playwright Richard Brinsley Sheridan in 1773. Her loose hair and woodland setting, rather than an interior, belong to the informal portraits in landscape that Gainsborough made a speciality and that suggested sensibility and natural grace. Elizabeth died in 1792, and portraits of her took on a poignant air. Gainsborough Dupont, trained by his uncle, often painted in his manner.","c":"Beside Thomas Gainsborough's famous full-length of the same sitter, which this appears to follow, the comparison is not kind: Dupont's version has the sweetness of the original but little of its feathery, shimmering touch, and the face is blander. As a small, pretty souvenir of a celebrated beauty, it succeeds modestly. Its value is as evidence of how Gainsborough's compositions spread through his workshop, not as a rival to the master."},"cma:122344":{"d":"Mary Anne Jolliffe is portrayed in a canvas of about 76 by 64 cm, painted after 1788 by Gainsborough Dupont, the only assistant his famous uncle Thomas Gainsborough ever had. Dupont captures her beauty with soft, flickering brushstrokes, detailing her frilly collar and her impressive hairstyle, curled and powdered in the current style. The portrait originally hung alongside that of her husband Thomas in the drawing room of their home, Ammerdown House in Somerset.","i":"After his uncle's death in 1788, Dupont continued the workshop and finished works, and the late works of the two artists are often hard to tell apart. A pair of portraits of husband and wife, hung together in the drawing room, was a standard way of declaring a marriage and a family's standing, and the elaborate hair and lace show the fashion of the day. Thomas's portrait still hangs at Ammerdown, while hers came to the museum.","c":"Dupont's manner is the late Gainsborough's, which makes his best work look like the master's: the flicker and softness are a tribute to the uncle, and this is counted among Dupont's best portraits. The risk of discipleship is imitation, and he lacks the audacity and invention of Thomas. Still, the portrait captures the beauty of its sitter with charm and a pleasing lightness, and it is an attractive example of the way a style outlived its inventor."},"met:436239":{"d":"A young woman, Anne Elizabeth Cholmley (1769–1788), later Lady Mulgrave, is shown with lace, a shawl and a fichu, and a ring among the details, in a tiny oil on wood panel of about 18 by 15 cm. Gainsborough Dupont, the nephew and assistant of Thomas Gainsborough, painted it before her death, and the small scale makes it an intimate likeness, closer to a miniature than to a family portrait for a drawing room.","i":"The sitter died young, at about nineteen, and a small portrait of a young woman who became Lady Mulgrave serves also as a memorial. The fichu and lace belong to the fashion of the 1780s, and the ring may allude to an engagement or marriage, though that is only a possibility. A panel of this size was meant to be kept close, perhaps as a gift to a fiancé or a family keepsake, and its meaning is chiefly personal.","c":"The challenge for Dupont is to keep his uncle's looseness at a tiny scale, and the soft, flickering strokes that work on a larger canvas become delicate touches here. The poignancy of the sitter's early death gives the picture a charge that the painting alone might not carry. As art it is a modest, charming piece by a skilled follower, less inventive than his uncle's but nimble, and as a keepsake it is affecting."},"nga:65567":{"d":"A bearded man stands with his body and head turned slightly to the right, shown on an oil panel about 64 by 47 cm and dated about 1539 to 1540. He wears a black flat-topped cap and a dark robe with puffed sleeves and lacy white cuffs and collar, and a gold ring with a green stone on his left forefinger. Beside him a wide window with a yellow sill opens on a landscape: dark trees, green fields, a rustic house and a faint figure beside a horse, under a blue sky.","i":"The title names no individual, only a member of the Fröschl family, so the portrait speaks for a household as much as for one man. The gold ring, the lace-edged linen and the fine dark robe signal means, and a window with a country view was a common way to give a portrait depth and a hint of the sitter's world. Hans Mielich worked in Munich and later served the Bavarian ducal court, painting portraits and miniatures for wealthy patrons.","c":"A clear, frank portrait of the generation after Dürer and Holbein: the lace, the beard and the glint of the ring are described with care, and the sitter is allowed a plain, serious presence. It does not reach the psychological depth of the great German portraitists, and the landscape glimpsed through the window feels more like a convention than an observed place. Mielich's strength lay in such crisp, dependable description, which made him a favored painter of Munich patrons."},"nga:61174":{"d":"Two roosters, a rabbit and three small birds hang from the top of this tall canvas against a nearly black wall, their heads resting on a stone ledge about a third of the way up. The tawny rabbit's nose points at the viewer from an azure-blue purse lined with silver, with a pair of scissors tucked behind its strap. A black fly sits on a rooster's red comb. Two white-and-scarlet falcon's hoods hang nearby, and a carved relief of nearly nude figures on the ledge below lies mostly in shadow.","i":"Hunting still lifes were pictures of privilege: in the Dutch Republic, hunting and hawking were largely reserved for the landed elite, so a hanging hare or bird, a falcon's hood or a game bag signaled a gentleman's pursuits, even when the buyer was a wealthy townsman. They were also displays of skill with textures. The fly on the comb plays on illusion, inviting the viewer to wave it away, and dead creatures could also remind viewers of life's brevity.","c":"Van Aelst is the most aristocratic of Dutch still-life painters, and this canvas shows why: the surfaces are cool, exact and polished, and each texture, from fur to silk to feather, gets its own touch. After years at the Medici court in Florence he brought a courtly finish that sets him apart from the looser Amsterdam school. The risk is coldness, since the display can feel more cunning than moving, though the dark wall adds drama."},"nga:199285":{"d":"Tall white cliffs rise on the left and right and fall away toward a vivid blue sea dotted with small ships. In the foreground two people with their backs to the viewer look out across the drop, one in a dress with a shawl over the head, the other in a wide-brimmed yellow hat and apparently holding a book or paper. Gray rocks with light green grass and a cluster of trees surround them. The oil on canvas, mounted on board, measures about 50 by 68 cm and is dated 1846.","i":"Møn, an island in southeastern Denmark, ends in a long line of chalk cliffs, and Sommerspiret is a named point on them. In the 1840s the cliffs drew Danish painters and visitors, who prized a dramatic landscape in a low and gentle country. The two onlookers seen from behind are a Romantic device that lets viewers share their gaze and feel the scale of the height, and the tiny ships far below make the point plain.","c":"The freshness of the color is the picture's chief virtue: chalk white against deep blue sea in clear light, with the small ships giving the drop real depth. Libert was in his mid-twenties, and the work belongs to the Danish Golden Age habit of honest, close observation rather than grand invention. The two stock figures are a tidy convention, but they place the viewer at the cliff's edge, and the view is faithful and agreeable rather than daring."},"met:437384":{"d":"A young woman identified as Salome is seated at the center of a tall canvas (about 160 by 103 cm, dated 1870) in a room crowded with rich things: the museum's keywords list a carpet, fur, a chest with marquetry inlay and drapery. The record gives no further description, so her costume, pose and expression are not described here. The picture is a single figure in an Orientalist interior, in which the setting counts for as much as the story.","i":"In Mark and Matthew the daughter of Herodias dances for Herod and, at her mother's prompting, asks for the head of John the Baptist; the historian Josephus supplies her name. In the nineteenth century she became a figure of exotic danger in French art and writing, set among imagined Eastern splendor. Henri Regnault, a Prix de Rome winner who had traveled to Spain and Morocco, painted her just before the Franco-Prussian War, and he was killed in battle in January 1871, aged twenty-seven.","c":"Regnault's gift was for color and for the sheer feel of materials, and the fur, inlay and fabric are painted with evident relish. The picture invites more admiration for its textures than for its drama, and the setting does much of the work that the story might have done. His death at twenty-seven leaves it as evidence of a talent that never matured: bright and assured, but not yet profound."},"nga:41646":{"d":"A woman is shown from the hips up in a sapphire-blue satin gown, her body turned in profile to the left while she looks over her shoulder at the viewer from large, hooded grey eyes. Wisps of brown hair fall across her forehead, soft curls tied with a royal-blue bow frame her face, and a pearl choker circles her neck. With one hand she holds the transparent lining of her low-cut bodice, and with the other a wreath of orange, blue, pink and white flowers at her waist. The background is dark, with a patterned curtain at the left.","i":"Ebba Sparre (1626–1662) was a Swedish countess, famed for her beauty and a close friend of Queen Christina, at whose court the French painter Sébastien Bourdon worked from 1652. The over-the-shoulder glance and the wreath of flowers recall the portraits of Anthony van Dyck, which set the fashion for elegant, informal court portraiture, and the flowers may allude to her youth or her marriage. The portrait belongs to Bourdon's two-year stay in Sweden, which ended after Christina abdicated in 1654.","c":"A charming, fluent portrait: the satin gown, pearls and flowers are painted with delicate touches, and the over-the-shoulder glance gives the sitter a lively, flirtatious presence. Bourdon is less incisive than the greatest portraitists of his day, and the face is a little generalised in its sweetness, but the elegance of the pose and colour is considerable, and it conveys well the grace of the Swedish court's most admired beauty."},"met:435755":{"d":"A vertical canvas (about 152 by 118 cm, dated about 1650) shows the baptism of Jesus. John the Baptist and Christ are the two protagonists, and an angel attends the scene, as the museum's keywords record. No further description is given, so the landscape, the dove and the pose of each figure are not described here. By type it is a classical history painting of mid-century France, with a few large figures arranged for clarity and calm.","i":"The Baptism (Matthew 3, Mark 1, Luke 3) is the moment when Christ's ministry is revealed: the Spirit descends like a dove and a voice from heaven names him the beloved Son. John is usually shown as a rugged prophet, often with a reed cross, and angels are often shown holding Christ's garments. Sébastien Bourdon, a Protestant by upbringing who worked in Rome and Paris, painted many Catholic subjects for church and private patrons.","c":"Bourdon was a quick, versatile artist who borrowed from Poussin's classicism, Roman genre scenes and Venetian color, and his religious pictures are typically clear, balanced and dignified. What they sometimes lack is the concentrated intensity of Poussin himself, so that emotion is stated more than felt. This canvas, from the years just before he left for Sweden, is a sound example of French mid-century classicism rather than one of its high points."},"nga:46164":{"d":"A procession of nine women and girls leads toward a smiling blond infant in a basket held by a bare-chested, bearded man beside a river, in a wide canvas about 120 by 173 centimetres. The tallest woman, near the centre, wears a gold diadem and a golden-yellow gown over blue and reaches out her arm toward the baby; behind her three women and three girls follow in bright wraps, one girl offering a tiny bouquet. Two women stoop to the basket; across the river, men look on beneath a ruined abutment, with a pale stone city in the distance.","i":"The scene is from Exodus 2: to save the Hebrew baby Moses from Pharaoh's order to kill all newborn boys, his mother set him afloat in a basket among the reeds of the Nile, where Pharaoh's daughter found him and adopted him. His sister, who watched, offered to find a nurse, who was his own mother. The story of a child saved by providence was a favourite of seventeenth-century painters, and Bourdon sets it in the stately classical manner of Poussin.","c":"Bourdon was an eclectic and versatile painter, and here he adopts Poussin's classical language: the frieze-like procession, the clear light, the ordered landscape and the Egyptian city in the distance are handled with polish. The maidens' graceful variety is a delight. But the picture lacks Poussin's intellectual rigour and depth of feeling, and the smiling baby is sweet rather than moving. It is a pleasing, ordered composition of calm elegance."},"met:435756":{"d":"A horizontal landscape, about 70 by 92 cm, dated probably to the 1660s, in which trees, a horse and human figures are the elements the museum lists. No more is recorded, so the buildings, water and sky are not described here. By its title and type it is an ideal landscape in the manner of Claude Lorrain and Poussin: a composed view rather than a portrait of a place, with small figures giving scale and a quiet note of life.","i":"Classical landscapes imagined an ancient or pastoral world, often recalling the Roman countryside and the poetry of Virgil, and French collectors prized them for their order and calm. The figures and horse in such pictures give a human measure and a hint of a story or a day's labor. Bourdon had come to know the manner during his years in Rome in the 1630s, and landscapes remained part of his output beside the religious and mythological pictures that built his reputation.","c":"Bourdon painted landscapes with an easy, poetic touch, and a canvas of this size shows his gift for balancing trees, light and figures. The idiom is borrowed, though, and a critic would set it below Claude's glowing atmosphere or Poussin's structure: the result is graceful and orderly rather than original. Since the record names only trees, a horse and figures, judgment must stay general, but the type suits his skill as an intelligent adapter of established formulas."},"nga:127554":{"d":"A large ceramic vase, white with a blue pattern, holds white peonies, blue and white irises, pink roses and red fuchsias among dark green leaves. It stands on a pale green cloth with gold patterns, and a few pink petals have fallen on the cloth around its base. The wall behind is dark brown. The oil on canvas, about 61 by 51 cm and dated 1875, is a domestic flower piece of the kind that found a ready market among American buyers of the 1870s.","i":"In nineteenth-century America a bouquet on a table was a graceful emblem of home, taste and the seasons, and Victorian viewers also knew a language of flowers in which each bloom carried a meaning. The scattered petals add a quiet reminder that cut flowers fade. George Cochran Lambdin, a Philadelphia painter, had turned from figures and landscapes to flower pieces by the 1870s and became especially known for his roses.","c":"The picture's best quality is contrast: bright blossoms and a white-and-blue vase glow against the dark wall, and the petals on the cloth give the arrangement a touch of life. It is a pleasing and well-managed bouquet, if somewhat sweet and unadventurous beside the Dutch flower painters or the quietly radiant flower pictures Fantin-Latour was painting in Paris in the same years. Its appeal is gentle rather than powerful."},"met:11392":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel (about 61 by 30 cm, oil on wood, dated 1878) holds roses and their leaves, the only subjects the museum records. Nothing is said of a vase, the background or the color of the blooms, so those are not described here. The narrow, upright format is unusual for a flower piece, and the picture belongs to the rose pictures that Lambdin made in quantity for American buyers during the 1870s.","i":"Roses were the flower of Victorian sentiment, standing for love and beauty and, because their season is short, for the passing of time. Lambdin is remembered chiefly for painting them, and pictures like this one met a steady demand for refined decoration, bringing a garden into the house through the winter. The date places it in the best-known phase of his career, when he had turned from earlier figure subjects to flowers.","c":"In his rose pictures Lambdin's touch is soft and fluent, with forms melting into shadow rather than being counted petal by petal. That delicacy is also the limit: such roses rarely ask for a second look beyond their pleasant color, and next to a Dutch flower piece or Fantin-Latour the observation is less searching. Within his work this is a representative example of the pictures that made his reputation."},"met:11393":{"d":"A large upright canvas (about 102 by 79 cm, oil, dated 1870 to 1880 with a question mark) takes flowers and, as the title says, the side of a greenhouse as its subject. The museum's keywords add nothing beyond flower, so the species, the arrangement and the state of the glass are not described here. It is a garden subject rather than a bouquet in a vase, which makes it a departure from the cut-flower pieces for which Lambdin is best known.","i":"Greenhouses were emblems of the Victorian passion for gardening, where exotic plants and early blooms were raised for show, and Philadelphia, where Lambdin worked, was one of America's centers of horticulture. A picture of the working side of such a building turns attention from the finished bouquet to the places where flowers are grown. Whether it records a particular garden is not stated in the museum's record.","c":"Flowers in a setting are harder than a bouquet in a vase, since light, space and the building must all be organized, and a critic would value the attempt to look beyond the usual rose piece. At over a meter tall it has the presence of a decorative panel, which suits a subject drawn from the garden. How well the handling matches his polished bouquets is for the eye to judge; the record is silent."},"nga:45876":{"d":"An elevated view of a pastoral landscape in which a town of pale buildings nestles among rolling hills beneath a vast light blue sky, the horizon just above the middle of the canvas. Lush trees and foliage in many greens frame the foreground, and a small brown path leads down from the top of a hill toward the distance. At the center an ornate white building with large windows stands in a sunny patch of land, while other structures are scattered across the hills and valleys. The palette is muted greens, soft yellows, earthy browns and cool blues. Oil on canvas, about 66 by 92 centimeters, c. 1821 to 1854.","i":"Easby Abbey is a ruined medieval abbey on the River Swale just outside Richmond in North Yorkshire, and the picturesque ruins attracted painters and tourists from the late eighteenth century. George Cuitt the Younger was a local artist who lived in Richmond and devoted much of his career to its ruins and countryside, in paintings and etchings. The title links the view to that familiar setting, and the ornate building with large windows is presumably the abbey.","c":"A mild, well-ordered view in the English picturesque tradition: framing trees, a path leading the eye, and a broad valley opening beyond. Cuitt's handling is neat and clear rather than dramatic, and the distant view does not make the abbey as imposing as the great watercolorists of the Yorkshire abbeys did. Its charm is as a local record, a modest painting loved for the place it shows."},"met:10923":{"d":"A boy's head, painted in oil on a canvas about 69 by 56 cm and dated between about 1867 and 1873. The museum's keywords record only a boy and the portrait genre, so costume, expression and background are not described here. The title calls it an ideal head, meaning a generalized type of youth rather than a strict likeness, though the name in parentheses, George Spencer Fuller, shares the artist's surname and points to a family model who posed for it.","i":"Ideal heads, in which a young model stood for innocence, reverie or the promise of youth, were a popular category in mid-nineteenth-century American painting: they asked for mood rather than narrative. George Fuller worked largely as a farmer in Deerfield, Massachusetts, between about 1860 and 1875 and painted more sparingly in those years, so the date range falls within a quiet period before he returned to full-time art and a Boston reputation in 1876.","c":"Fuller's late manner is built on soft edges, warm browns and a veil of shadow, and a young face suits it: the forms emerge from the dusk with a quiet inwardness. Its limit is a family likeness among his pictures, in which murkiness can stand in for precision. As a head study this is gentle and sympathetic rather than searching, a good small example of the tonal poetry that Boston admired in him."},"nga:42852":{"d":"A head-and-shoulders portrait shows a woman seated and facing slightly left, with a fair complexion, soft features, gentle eyes and a prominent nose. Her short, curly light brown hair is partly covered by a modest cap of soft fabric tied at the back, matching the cream scarf at her neck, and she wears a dark coat over a lighter garment. The background is plain and dark. The sitter bears the names of the painter's wife and was probably her mother. The canvas is dated 1876.","i":"George Fuller spent years farming in Massachusetts before returning to full-time painting in 1876, and portraits of family members belong to that private world, painted for love rather than the market. The dark ground and soft edges show his aim to evoke mood and feeling, not to describe each feature, in a manner that anticipated American Tonalism. A cap and scarf, with no jewelry, speak of plain dress and a modest, domestic character.","c":"Fuller's strength is atmosphere: soft transitions, warm glow and a dark ground that lets a face emerge. The portrait carries the humility of a family likeness, plain and intimate, without display. The cost of his method is a certain vagueness of structure, in which features dissolve into shadow, and some sitters lose definition. It is not a showy picture, but it is sincere and quietly moving, an honest tribute to a person he loved."},"nga:34193":{"d":"The upper half of a woman is shown seated in profile, facing left, with fair skin, closed lips, light-colored eyes and light brown hair styled up. She wears a high-collared dress with lace detailing and a dark shawl over her shoulders, and a brooch with a dangling accessory hangs at her neck. The background is dark with subtle hints of color. The portrait of the painter's wife, Agnes, about 69 by 56 cm, dates from about 1877.","i":"Agnes Higginson married George Fuller in 1860, and they lived for years on a farm in Massachusetts until he returned to painting in 1876. A profile view is rare in American portraiture of the period and recalls cameos and Renaissance portrait medals, giving the sitter a quiet, classical dignity. The portrait is private, painted for the family and kept by it, and the small brooch and lace are the only ornaments of a plain, affectionate image.","c":"A tender, quiet portrait: the profile gives the face a clear, sculptural outline, and the dark ground leaves lace and shawl to glimmer softly. Fuller's affection is evident, and the restraint suits it. The limits of his method are a certain haze and an absence of strong character; this is a likeness that speaks of love more than of personality. At its best it has the poetry that made him admired in Boston for mood in painting."},"met:10925":{"d":"A young woman is shown in a field with a basket and wearing a necklace; the museum's keywords record her as an agricultural worker and class the canvas as a portrait. The oil on canvas, about 128 by 103 cm and dated 1880, is in Fuller's late manner, in which figure and landscape tend to melt into a hazy atmosphere. The title is a nineteenth-century racial term, now regarded as offensive, for a person with one African grandparent.","i":"The title is a period racial label. In the decades after the Civil War, white Northern painters and writers often pictured mixed-race women as beautiful, wistful figures, a sentimental type that reduced individuals to a category. The museum gives no sitter's name, so the picture is best read as evidence of how such women were imagined and idealized by white audiences, within Fuller's wider taste for dreamlike, poetic figures.","c":"Fuller's veiled, tonal handling gives the figure dignity and gravity, and the hazy field makes her seem part of a quiet, enclosed world; it is far from the caricatures common in the period's popular imagery. Yet the title's racial label and the picturesque setting limit its frankness, and the softness that elsewhere reads as poetry can here look evasive. It remains one of his larger late figure pictures and an important document of its time's attitudes."},"met:10924":{"d":"A single woman is the subject of a tall canvas about 127 by 82 cm, dated 1882, and the museum's keywords record nothing more, so her pose, dress and setting are not described here. The name Nydia most likely refers to the blind flower girl of Edward Bulwer-Lytton's novel The Last Days of Pompeii (1834), a character that painters and sculptors loved. By type it is a figure picture of Fuller's last years, painted in his soft, shadowy manner.","i":"In the novel Nydia, a blind slave from Thessaly, sells flowers in the streets of Pompeii, loves the Athenian Glaucus and guides him and his beloved through the darkness of Vesuvius's eruption. The American sculptor Randolph Rogers made her famous in marble in the 1850s, and a picture of this name belongs to the same popular taste for the sentimental heroine, although the museum's record does not confirm the source.","c":"In Fuller's late manner the haze that blurs edges and merges figure with shadow suits a blind heroine and a world lit only by memory. The cost is definition: the drawing can be hard to read, and the softness can seem more like a habit than a choice. Late nineteenth-century Boston admired such pictures as poetry in paint, and this one shows why, though a modern viewer may want firmer structure."},"nga:42851":{"d":"A young girl with fair skin and light brown, textured hair is shown in a textured brown garment with a white collar against a dark background. The painting is signed Violet and dated Sept 1882 in the upper right corner, which suggests that Violet is the child's name. The canvas is about 69 by 56 cm. Fuller painted it in the last two years of his life, in the soft, shadowy manner of his late figure pictures.","i":"Fuller's late pictures of women and girls are painted in a hazy, glowing manner, closer to reverie than to portraiture, and some are of unnamed models. Here the signature, with a child's name and a month, gives the picture a personal frame, a likeness set down in a particular season. A child's portrait was a family keepsake, and the plain dress and dark ground concentrate attention on the face, giving the girl a thoughtful seriousness.","c":"A child's portrait in Fuller's style has soft edges, warm browns and a shadowed ground, and the effect is gentle, poetic and slightly melancholy. The textured paint of hair and dress gives the surface life. The risk of the method is vagueness, a face that dissolves into mood, and the sitter is half-hidden by it. But the picture anticipates the Tonalism of Dewing and Thayer, and its dreamlike quality has lasting appeal."},"met:10922":{"d":"A horizontal canvas about 76 by 102 cm places a woman in a landscape with trees, the elements the museum's keywords list. The title, And She Was a Witch, reads like a line from a story or ballad, though no source is given in the record. The date, 1877 to 1884, runs to the year of Fuller's death and suggests that the picture was worked on over several seasons. Colors, pose and the hour of day are not described here.","i":"Witchcraft had a deep, uneasy place in New England memory, from the Salem trials of 1692 onward, and nineteenth-century writers such as Hawthorne turned that history into romance. Fuller was a New Englander, and a witch in a twilight landscape suits his taste for dreamlike, half-legendary subjects. The picture is probably meant to evoke folklore and atmosphere rather than the horror of persecution, but the museum offers no explanation, and the title is the only clue.","c":"Here the landscape matters as much as the figure, and Fuller's twilight tones, smoky edges and golden-brown light give the subject a convincing air of folk tale. The gain is atmosphere; the loss is clarity, for woman and trees can merge in a way that leaves the story unspoken. As one of his last works, it shows the poetic late style at full strength and also the vagueness that his critics noted."},"cma:108157":{"d":"A boy's head painted in oil on a canvas about 61 by 51 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art records only the title and a date of before 1885, the year after Fuller's death, and offers no description, so expression, costume and background are not described here. It is a small head study of a young model, a type that recurs in the artist's late work, and it is not recorded whether it was meant as a portrait of an individual child or as a generalized type.","i":"Heads of children and young people were a favorite subject for Fuller and his Boston circle, valued for innocence and mood rather than for a story. The museum can date the picture only as before 1885, the year after Fuller's death, so its place in his career is left open. Whether the boy was a particular child or an imagined type is not recorded; either way the picture belongs to a long tradition of the picturesque child, popular with American collectors of the 1870s and 1880s.","c":"A restrained, quiet picture whose success depends on one thing: how convincingly a young face can emerge from warm shadow. Fuller was good at that gentle emergence, and such heads are among the least pretentious and most approachable of his late works. They lack the narrative ambition of his larger figure pictures, and, without more information about its date, this one is best taken as a modest sample of his style, not a key to it."},"met:10805":{"d":"A winter scene in oil, about 66 by 92 cm and dated 1858. Snow, a school and a wagon are the things the museum's keywords list, and the title gives the schoolhouse its color: red. The record does not describe the sky, the figures or the time of day, so these are left to the viewer's eye. By type it is a rural genre landscape, a view of a familiar village building rather than a topographical portrait of one particular spot.","i":"The little red schoolhouse became a symbol of rural American community, learning and neighborliness, and snowy village scenes gave nostalgic comfort to a country that was rapidly urbanizing. George Henry Durrie, who worked in New Haven, Connecticut, made New England winters his specialty, and after his early death in 1863 the printmakers Currier and Ives published lithographs after his paintings, spreading the type across the country.","c":"Durrie's winters are valued for crisp observation of cold light and for an affectionate, orderly storytelling that has made them icons of American nostalgia. The red building gives a bold note against the white of the snow, and the composition is easy to read. The drawback is sweetness: the scene is tidier and kinder than rural life was, and later prints made the formula more so. Within his work it is a representative, well-made example."},"nga:89675":{"d":"A man and a dog trudge through ankle-deep snow towards a barn and farmhouse under a heavy, lead-grey sky. The man, in a brown hat and suit, carries a sack on one shoulder and a basket on his forearm and heads for a gap in a stone wall. Six cows stand or nose through the snow near a grey, snow-topped barn; the butter-yellow house has a terracotta-red wing, laundry on a line and a second figure in brown. Snow on the roofs is white in the low sun and mauve-pink in shadow, and slate-grey hills close the horizon beneath a strip of warm yellow light.","i":"George Durrie, of New Haven, Connecticut, specialised in snowy farm scenes of rural New England, and his winter landscapes were widely known through prints published by Currier & Ives. Such pictures presented the farm as a place of work and shelter in a harsh season, with the wash on the line, the cows and the walking figure combining labour and homely comfort. They appealed to Americans who were leaving the countryside for towns and remembered farm life with nostalgia.","c":"Durrie's winter farm picture is a hymn to a vanishing way of life, and its quiet details persuade: the heavy sky, the pink shadows on the snow, the laundry on the line. The design is orderly to the point of tidiness and the figures are small and generalised, but the cool grey and warm yellow of the light are fine. It is a good example of the type that Currier & Ives made popular."},"nga:109732":{"d":"A snowy rural scene shows a large barn, a farmhouse and bare trees dusted with snow under an overcast sky of dark grey clouds. At the left, brown cows are fenced in by the barn, and a person walking along a snowy path brings them a bundle of golden hay. The palette is made of earth tones, with greys and browns playing against the white snow. At about 67 by 92 centimetres it is a larger version of the theme than the artist's other Winter in the Country in the same museum.","i":"The farm in winter was Durrie's speciality: hay for the cattle, a barn roof under snow and a path trodden to the shelter stand for the care and thrift on which a New England farm depended. The picture belongs to an American taste for nostalgic rural subjects in the years before the Civil War, a taste that Currier & Ives prints after Durrie spread widely.","c":"A larger and plainer cousin of Durrie's other winter farm. The golden hay is the single warm note in a palette of greys and browns, and the figure's errand gives the scene its quiet purpose. The barn and the cows are a little heavy and the sky leaden, so the picture can feel stolid beside the subtler light of the smaller version. But it is a respectable, sincere record of rural routine."},"met:12605":{"d":"A small horizontal landscape in oil, about 38 by 60 cm and dated 1883, shows meadows at East Hampton on Long Island, New York, with trees, the only features the museum's keywords record. The sky, the light and any buildings or figures are not described here. The scale and the plain title suggest a modest, observed view of open grassland and trees rather than a grand composition.","i":"East Hampton's flat farmland, wide skies and old farmhouses drew artists to the South Fork of Long Island in the 1870s and 1880s, and the village became a favorite summer haunt of painters. Pictures like this belong to the American habit of loving, local landscape that followed the Hudson River School's grander wilderness views, and they gave city buyers a calm memory of the countryside. George Henry Smillie, trained in the Hudson River tradition, exhibited regularly at the National Academy of Design.","c":"The modest size suits a quiet subject, and Smillie, a second-generation Hudson River painter, handles meadow and trees with the competence of long habit. The picture is pleasant and truthful but not adventurous: it stands apart from the broad, bright brushwork the Impressionists were developing in France at the same moment and keeps to the tonal, descriptive naturalism of the preceding generation. Its pleasure is calm, not surprise."},"met:10604":{"d":"A self-portrait of the painter George Hewitt Cushman, a man shown in watercolor on ivory and dated 1839. It is tiny: only 3.2 by 2.5 cm, just over an inch high, the size of a keepsake meant for a locket or small case. The museum's record gives no description of pose, dress or expression, so only the type and scale can be described: a portrait miniature, made to be held close to the eye.","i":"Miniature painters often made self-portraits as advertisements, showing prospective sitters what they could do in a few square centimeters. The year matters: the daguerreotype was announced in 1839, and within a decade the photograph began to replace the painted miniature for ordinary portraits, so Cushman's craft stood on the edge of a sudden change. The museum's keywords record a man and a self-portrait, but nothing of costume or setting.","c":"At this scale the achievement is control: a face, a head of hair and a likeness resolved on a plate not much larger than a postage stamp is a test of eyesight and patience, and the ivory's glow helps. Cushman is a lesser name beside the leading American miniaturists such as Malbone, and the record allows no verdict beyond competence. As a document of a craftsman portraying himself on the eve of photography it has real historical charm."},"met:10605":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Rebecca Wetherill, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1849. At 10.8 by 8.3 cm it is large for a miniature, roughly the size of a hand, and was probably framed and displayed like a small picture rather than worn as a locket. The museum records no detail of her dress, pose or setting, so only the name, medium and scale can be given with confidence.","i":"By the late 1840s the portrait miniature was competing with the daguerreotype, and miniature painters answered with larger, more finished ivories that offered color and a luxurious surface. A named sitter in a work of this size suggests a commission for a family to keep. The museum does not say who Rebecca Wetherill was or tell us more than her name, so her rank and story cannot be read from the picture.","c":"The larger ivory gives Cushman room to show what the medium does best: translucent color, a luminous flesh tone and the fine hatching that builds a face. He is a capable rather than an inspired miniaturist, and the record gives no sign of the individuality that lifts a likeness above a competent one. Its value is that of a carefully made, well-documented likeness from the last great years of the art."},"met:10602":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory of a girl, 3.2 by 2.5 cm, dated 1856. The title names her Alice Cushman, which is the painter's own surname, so she was probably a relative, perhaps a daughter, though the record does not say. Nothing is recorded about her dress, pose or expression; at barely an inch high the picture is a keepsake made to be held close, perhaps worn in a locket or kept in a small case.","i":"By 1856 photography had taken over most everyday portraiture, so a miniature of a child bearing the painter's own surname was probably a family keepsake rather than a commission for a fee. In an age when childhood illness was common, such likenesses kept a young face in the family's hands. The record does not say whether she was a daughter, niece or other relation.","c":"As a feat of control the little portrait deserves respect: a face and a character fitted into a space about as wide as a large coin, with the ivory's glow doing some of the work. Its sentiment is genuine but its ambition is modest, and a viewer should not expect the individuality of the major miniaturists. In a collection it reads best as an intimate record of a craft near its end."},"met:10603":{"d":"A portrait of a girl named in the title as Ida Cushman, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1856. At 11.1 by 8.3 cm it is a large miniature, framed as a small picture rather than set in a locket. The title gives the painter's own surname, so she was probably a relative, though the record does not say so, and it describes nothing of her dress, pose or setting.","i":"A larger ivory like this could stand on a table or hang on a wall, giving the painter room for soft flesh tints and fine hatching. If, as the shared surname suggests, the girl belonged to the artist's family, it was a keepsake made with affection, at a time when photographs were rapidly replacing hand-painted likenesses of ordinary sitters.","c":"In the larger format both the strengths and the limits of the miniature show: a smooth, polished finish and a gentle, clear likeness, but, as far as the record shows, little of the surprise or insight that makes a child feel individual. Cushman's skill is real, though not that of an artist of the first rank. Its value is as an affectionate, carefully made family likeness from the last years of the miniature's popularity."},"nga:133512":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 23 by 18 centimeters, shows a boy of the Arapaho people, as the title states. It is painted in grisaille, that is, in shades of a single neutral tone without full color. The catalog describes no further details of pose, dress or setting, so these are left unstated. The tonal restraint gives the picture the look of a study, concerned with form, light and the character of the face, such as an artist might make in preparing a larger picture or an illustration to be printed in black and white.","i":"George de Forest Brush trained in Paris under Jean-Léon Gérôme and, after returning to the United States, spent time in the West in the early 1880s, where he made studies of Native American life. His approach was sympathetic and observational, though his Native subjects were also shaped by academic habits and by an Eastern audience for pictures of a people widely, and wrongly, thought to be disappearing. This small study belongs to that body of work.","c":"Studies in monochrome strip a picture to essentials, and the best of Brush's have a calm, sculptural dignity that owes much to his Paris training. Reduced to tones, this one asks to be judged on drawing and modeling, not color, and the small format favors that kind of concentration. It is a modest piece, but a thoughtful one, and a useful key to the quieter side of an artist better known for his later pictures of mothers and children."},"nga:133518":{"d":"Seen from behind, a figure sits cross-legged on a blue-green mat with yellow details, carving a large slab of white, gray-marbled stone with a small chisel and hammer. The sculptor has light brown skin, dark straight hair, a gold armband and a leopard-print cloth wrapped at the waist over orange trousers visible at the knees. Intricate designs run along the right and top edges of the slab, including the outline of a standing figure in a feathered headdress, vines, animals and geometric patterns. The floor is white marble and the wall behind is gray. The panel is horizontal, about 32 by 59 centimeters.","i":"Brush, who studied in Paris with Jean-Léon Gérôme, here imagines the craft of the pre-Columbian Americas with the detailed, archaeological finish of his teacher's pictures of antiquity. The subject reflects nineteenth-century American curiosity about ancient Mexico, fed by popular histories such as William H. Prescott's Conquest of Mexico, and the scene is a Western reconstruction. The figure seen from behind makes the act of making the subject, a kind of image of the artist at work.","c":"Brush fuses Gérôme's polished academic finish with an archaeologist's curiosity, and the result has an odd authority: the intricate carving on the slab is rendered with the care of a miniaturist, and the back view, which denies the sculptor a face, makes the labor itself the subject. The reconstruction is imaginary, a Victorian vision of pre-Columbian life, and its glamour is that of a stage set. As painting it is an assured small panel."},"nga:204092":{"d":"An older woman is seen from the chest up, looking toward the viewer. Her forehead is wrinkled, her brown eyes deep-set and her lips thin and pink, and she wears a white cap and a black garment with a white ruffled collar. The background is a mottled, muted brown, and small areas of text appear in the upper left and right corners. The oil on panel is small, about 37 by 31 cm, and dated 1569: a plain, direct portrait with nothing to distract from the face.","i":"The title gives the sitter's age, sixty-eight, so she was born about 1500, and the lettering in the corners presumably records that age and the year, as many Netherlandish portraits do. The black dress, white cap and ruff are the sober costume of a respectable, well-off woman of the Low Countries. Her name is not recorded here. Such portraits often preserved the likeness of an elderly family member for her descendants.","c":"De Vos is remembered for large religious and allegorical compositions marked by his Italian training, so this plain portrait shows him in a more sober register. It is honest and unflattering: wrinkles, deep-set eyes and thin lips are recorded without softening, and the small panel depends on the face alone. The result is less showy than his history pictures and more convincing, a good example of Netherlandish directness."},"nga:61248":{"d":"A rocky shore in warm tones of orange, brown, red and purple fills the foreground, with two boats and a simple structure near the center and another boat by the water at the bottom edge. The horizon sits almost at the top, where a range of mountains is faintly visible against a strip of pale-purple sky beyond a broad, light blue sea that stretches from the rocky shore on the right to the distant mountains. The whole is built from small dot-like brushstrokes of reds, oranges, blues and purples. Oil on canvas, about 65 by 92 centimeters, 1891 to 1892.","i":"Cross adopted the divisionist technique of Seurat and Signac in 1891, the year he settled on the Mediterranean coast in the south of France, and this is one of his first large pictures in the method. A calanque is a small, steep-sided inlet. Neo-Impressionists applied separate dots of pure color to be mixed by the eye, aiming for greater luminosity and order than the Impressionists' brushwork, and the south's strong light suited the idea.","c":"A radiant early divisionist landscape: the warm rocks, which could be too hot, are balanced by the large expanse of cool blue sea, and the dotted touch makes the light vibrate. The high horizon flattens the space into a decorative pattern. Cross is less rigorous than Seurat, and his application is somewhat freer, but his sense of Mediterranean color is a pleasure and anticipates the Fauves' use of color."},"nga:177052":{"d":"A green field of cotton plants with white blossoms is the setting of this small painting of about 34 by 46 cm, dated about 1890. Three figures harvest the cotton; they appear to wear skirts and bonnets and have dark skin, though their faces are not detailed. Buildings stand at left, green trees rise against a pale blue sky with touches of gray, and the loose, visible brushstrokes and warm natural colors suggest an open-air study.","i":"By 1890, a quarter-century after the end of slavery, most Black Southerners who worked the cotton fields did so as sharecroppers or tenants, in an economy that kept many of them in debt. A picture of cotton picking might be read as a pastoral record, but it shows the labor that supported the Southern economy and the lives of those who did it. William Gilbert Gaul, who painted Civil War and Southern scenes, observed the region as an outsider.","c":"Gaul's strength is lively handling and warm natural color, and this small board has the freshness of an open-air note. The workers are anonymous, with faces left undetailed, as was common in white painters' views of Black labor, so the picture records the activity but little of the people. It is incomplete as a record of conditions in the cotton fields, and its value lies in its direct, unsentimental observation of the work."},"nga:43724":{"d":"A panel about 120 by 92 cm, painted in oil before 1530, shows Charity, one of the Christian virtues, personified as a woman. The museum record gives no description, so the pose, dress and number of figures are not stated here; the usual form of the subject, which del Sarto painted more than once, is a young mother with small children, their closeness standing for generous love. By type it is an allegorical figure picture rather than a story.","i":"Charity is the greatest of the three theological virtues (1 Corinthians 13), and from the late Middle Ages she was commonly shown as a mother with children, nursing or sheltering them, to express love that gives without return. Florentine painters of del Sarto's generation made the figure monumental, in the manner of a Madonna. The picture reads as a statement of the virtue; the museum does not say for whom it was painted.","c":"Andrea del Sarto was called the faultless painter, and the label fits his soft, warm color, smoky modeling and graceful drawing, though Vasari thought him too timid to be truly great. If the panel follows his usual manner, as its date and attribution suggest, it shows how well he could turn a stock allegory into a believable human group. His polish can feel cool, with serenity taking the place of passion, but few Florentines drew so surely."},"nga:41598":{"d":"The Madonna and Child on the front of a double-sided oil panel, about 52 by 42 centimeters, painted about 1496 to 1499 by Albrecht Dürer, then in his mid to late twenties. The reverse of the panel carries a different subject, Lot and His Daughters, so the two sides were evidently conceived as parts of one object but meant to be seen separately.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the most common sacred image of the period, and Dürer, who had returned from his first trip to Italy in 1495, was combining German draftsmanship with the lessons of Venetian painting. The Virgin was both Mother and Queen of Heaven, and an image like this served private prayer. Dürer treated the subject in many forms, from half-length Madonnas to enthroned groups and scenes in a landscape.","c":"Dürer's Madonnas of the late 1490s combine Gothic precision with a new, Italian-inspired monumentality, and even his early paintings display an unmatched control of line and surface. The double-sided format makes the panel a rare survival and a document of his experimental early work as a painter, a medium in which he is far less common than in print."},"nga:41599":{"d":"A man and two women walk along a dirt path through a green landscape, carrying packs, walking sticks and provisions, while in the distance fiery explosions send flames and smoke skyward. The old man, bearded and wrinkled, wears a fur-lined blue coat and a turban under a split purple and green crown, and carries a basket of shiny silver disks. The women follow, their blond hair bound up; one carries a distaff, a small orange chest and keys, the other balances a blue pack on her head. Oil on panel, about 52 by 42 centimeters, with Dürer's AD monogram on a rock.","i":"The picture shows the flight of Lot and his two daughters from the burning of Sodom and Gomorrah, in Genesis 19, after angels warned him to leave; his wife, who looked back, was turned into a pillar of salt. The keys, chest, distaff and silver are household goods and attributes that Dürer gives the fugitives. The subject was often read as a warning against sin and a model of salvation by obedience.","c":"An ambitious early picture in which Dürer sets a catastrophe within a landscape: the bright fires and smoke at the horizon contrast with the quiet greens and the small, grave procession, and the costumes and props are described with jewel-like precision. The figures are somewhat stiff, and the fire against so small a group leaves the drama oddly muted, but as an early northern landscape of apocalypse it is unusual and inventive."},"met:436243":{"d":"Christ is shown as Salvator Mundi, Savior of the World, in a frontal, half-length image on a linden panel about 58 by 47 centimeters, painted around 1505. The panel appears to be unfinished: the face and hair are worked with Dürer's exacting care, while parts of the robe and hands are left in a more provisional state, which gives a rare look at how he built up a painting from drawing to color.","i":"Salvator Mundi shows Christ as ruler and blesser of the world, usually with a raised right hand and a globe in the left, symbols of his dominion and mercy. The type was popular in northern Europe for private devotion, and its plain, frontal pose invited the believer to meet Christ's gaze directly. Dürer made it around the time of his second journey to Venice, when Italian ideas of the ideal human type were much on his mind.","c":"The frontal, symmetrical pose recalls Dürer's Munich self-portrait of 1500, which also gives its face Christlike authority, and the comparison is rewarding. The unfinished state is a gift to the modern viewer, because the underlying drawing and the logic of the paint layers are visible, but it means the panel cannot be judged as a completed devotional image. It remains a serious, austere picture, impressive in the intensity of its head."},"met:436245":{"d":"Mary holds the Christ Child in a small devotional panel, about 28 by 22 centimeters, dated 1516 and painted in oil on spruce. Dürer's Madonnas typically focus on the tenderness of mother and child, and the intimate scale suggests a picture meant to be kept close, in a private room or an oratory, rather than displayed in a church. Panels by Dürer are rare, since by 1516 he worked chiefly on prints.","i":"By 1516 Dürer was famous across Europe as a printmaker and worked for the Emperor Maximilian I, and he painted few panels; a small Madonna of this date was probably made for a private patron or as a gift. The Virgin and Child is the central image of Catholic devotion, and the intimate half-length format, developed in the Netherlands, allowed the believer to meditate on Christ's humanity as well as his divinity.","c":"Dürer's gifts of detail, line and firm modeling are never absent, but in his Madonnas the Italian ideal of ease and grace sits beside northern precision, a tension that gives them a slightly austere quality. Beside his prints of the Virgin, which are inventive and various, the paintings can seem restrained. Still, a dated Dürer panel is a rare thing, and it shows the clarity of his eye at small scale."},"nga:41600":{"d":"A portrait of a clergyman, oil on parchment mounted on fabric, about 42 by 33 centimeters, dated 1516, by Albrecht Dürer. The title adds the name Johann Dorsch with a question mark, so the identification of the sitter is uncertain. Oil on parchment is an unusual support for a painting of this size, and the thin, finely textured surface contrasts with the wood panels most painters used.","i":"Dürer made portraits of clergy, councillors and merchants in Nuremberg and on his travels, and a portrait of a churchman recorded learning and office, in the years just before the Reformation shook the Church. The sitter's name is a suggestion, not a certainty. Dürer's portraits of the 1510s are known for their psychological intensity and the sharpness of their drawing.","c":"Dürer's portraits are distinguished by their uncompromising attention to the face: the shape of the skull, the lines of age, the direction of the gaze. It comes from the period of his great portrait drawings and engravings. The parchment support suits his taste for exact, linear description."},"met:436244":{"d":"Three heads are pressed close together against a deep green ground. At the left Saint Anne, her face framed by a tightly drawn pale veil that covers her chin, looks out at the viewer with wide, steady eyes and rests a hand on Mary's shoulder. At the right Mary, with reddish-gold hair and a translucent rose-pink blouse with a high collar, lowers her eyes toward her child, her hands joined in prayer. The Christ Child, a plump infant with curly hair, sleeps in the lower left, wrapped in red cloth and pillowed in Anne's arms. Faces are modelled in delicate flesh tones, the veil in cool grey creases. A small monogram and date sit at the right edge.","i":"Dürer signed and dated this picture 1519, though the date may have been added later; the Metropolitan Museum says probably 1519. Saint Anne, the Virgin's mother, was a popular subject in German art around 1500. A drawing in the Albertina in Vienna is thought to be a study for her head, and she is said to have been modelled on Dürer's wife, Agnes. The panel belonged to the Tucher family of Nuremberg, patrons of the artist, and was acquired in 1630 by the Bavarian elector Maximilian. Later damage and repainting led to its being sold off as a copy, until restoration showed it to be Dürer's own. It came to the Museum in Benjamin Altman's bequest of 1913.","c":"An intimate and unusually close picture. The cropping brings the three heads together, and the contrast between Anne's frank stare and Mary's lowered eyes gives the group a charge that most Northern Madonnas lack. The sleeping Child, painted with the same precision as the pleats of the veil, anchors the whole. Its condition is a limit: the history of damage and repainting shows in places, and some of the modelling has gone flat. As an invention it is quieter than Dürer's prints, but few of his devotional paintings are so direct."},"met:435851":{"d":"In a rocky landscape, the dead Christ sits upright on a broken stone throne, eyes closed, his head dropping toward one shoulder, wearing only a white loincloth. At the left an old man with a long beard, in a pale blue tunic, sits holding a hand to his chest; at the right a nearly naked old man rests his head on his hand, a skull and bones at his feet. A bird flies upward from the ruined throne, and wild animals move among the rocks at the upper left. The back of the throne and the block under the right-hand figure are covered with lettering in imitation Hebrew. In the distance on the right are a walled town, turbaned figures and blue hills. Oil and tempera on wood, about 70 by 87 centimetres.","i":"The Met calls the picture a meditation on death and resurrection: Christ's dead body is displayed on a broken throne inscribed in pseudo-Hebrew. With him are Job, whose faith in a living Redeemer Christians read as foretelling the Resurrection, and Saint Jerome, who wrote a commentary on the book of Job. The landscape, barren on one side and lush on the other, alludes to death and life, as do the animals, and the bird flying upward is a symbol of the soul. The turbaned figures in the background would have been familiar to Venetians through their trade with Egypt and the Middle East. Carpaccio painted it about 1490, an early work.","c":"This is among Carpaccio's strangest pictures: a devotional image with no story, only three men and a landscape, and it works through contrast, the pale lit body of Christ against dark rock and blue distance. Few Venetian pictures of the 1490s are so odd, with a ruined throne, a dead tree, stage-set animals and a made-up script. The figures are drier than the setting, and the symbols pile up, so that the panel can read as a puzzle rather than a prayer. Yet the stillness is real: the three men share a silence that stays in the mind."},"nga:498":{"d":"Shown from the hips up against a landscape, a woman in a burgundy dress with marigold-orange sleeves and a sheer veil sits in profile facing left and reads a small red book, one finger marking her place. A faint gold ring around her white-headdressed head is her halo. She sits on a low white stone ledge, with powder-blue fabric draped near one knee; at left, a sliver of another figure's shoulder, elbow and toes, wrapped in midnight blue and leaning on a maroon pillow, shows that the panel was cut from a larger picture. Beyond lie bare and leafy trees, a mustard-yellow meadow and ice-blue water.","i":"Mary is often shown reading, because the Annunciation traditionally finds her at her book, and the picture presents her as a model of devout learning, absorbed and calm. The halo is a thin gold ring, in the Venetian way. Because the panel has been cut and transferred from wood to canvas, the other figure, partly visible at the left, is incomplete, and who it was is not known. The landscape beyond recalls the lagoon and hills near Venice.","c":"Carpaccio is usually admired for large narrative cycles teeming with detail; this fragment shows another side, a hushed and tender contemplation. The profile is beautifully drawn, the burgundy and marigold are chosen with a colorist's eye, and the landscape's bare and leafy trees give a quiet suggestion of time. The cutting is a loss, and the composition is incomplete, yet the picture's intimacy may be heightened by the very truncation."},"nga:46107":{"d":"Shown from the shins up, a woman with a heart-shaped face and heavy-lidded eyes holds a seated child on her lap, below two winged baby heads that float in pale clouds. Her ruby-red dress is tied with a dark cord, and a lapis-blue robe with a coppery gold lining drapes over her shoulders and lap, with a white cloth over her head. She holds a green-covered book open in one hand; the child, in a translucent white garment, raises two fingers and a thumb in blessing. A small bird perches on the near ledge, a white rabbit crouches in the distance, and towns on hills lead to ice-blue mountains.","i":"The book in Mary's hand and the child's blessing gesture show Christ as the Word and teacher. The small bird, with a red, black and white head, resembles a goldfinch, a bird that traditionally foretells his Passion because it eats thistles and carries a red mark like a drop of blood. The white rabbit is often read as a sign of purity and fertility, though in some contexts it can stand for lust overcome. The winged heads are cherubim.","c":"Carpaccio's Madonna is less majestic than Bellini's, but she has a sweet, slightly melancholy gravity, and the landscape of towns, water and distant mountains is drawn with a storyteller's relish for small incident. The picture is a jewel-like devotional image, charming in its details, if less original in conception than his narrative cycles. It is a good example of how a Venetian painter of the generation after Bellini kept the old devotional types alive."},"nga:32":{"d":"Close to the viewer, a bearded, balding man in a royal-blue tunic and ruby-red robe leads a donkey, leaning on a walking stick and looking back at it. On the donkey sits a woman in a blue and gold brocade cloak, holding an infant close; the baby, in white, reaches for her face while turning to look at the viewer. All three have halos. A grassy path lined with carefully drawn plants runs beside a river, and green trees, blue mountains and a few buildings fill a horizon two-thirds up under a peach-streaked azure sky. It is a horizontal oil on panel, about 72 by 111 centimeters.","i":"The Flight into Egypt (Matthew 2:13 to 15) shows Joseph leading Mary and the child away from Herod's massacre of the Innocents. Carpaccio, whose Venetian narrative cycles are full of lively detail, turns the story into a pastoral journey: the plants along the path are carefully observed, and the river and hills suggest a real countryside. The baby's backward glance toward the viewer draws the beholder into the family's progress. The painting was probably made for private devotion in a Venetian home.","c":"Carpaccio's gift for storytelling is clear: Joseph's tired but watchful backward glance, the mother's absorption in her child and the infant's playful turn make a tender, human scene, and the foreground plants are painted with botanical affection. The composition is simple, the color jewel-like and the landscape spacious. It lacks the monumental grandeur of Bellini or Titian, but the intimacy and charm are real. A good example of Carpaccio's gentler devotional mood."},"nga:61371":{"d":"Strips of flower beds, each a single color, run back from the viewer like the panels of a patchwork quilt: pale pink, butter yellow, white, and light blue, divided by narrow paths. A small figure in a blue jacket and brown trousers walks across the beds at the right. The horizon sits about halfway up the canvas, lined with low, dark-brown farm buildings under steep thatched roofs, and two bare trees twist up against a sky almost filled with cream-white cloud. The brushwork is loose, short, and parallel, giving the scene a soft, hazy look. The palette is muted, with the sky gray, and the wide horizontal format spreads the fields into a calm view.","i":"Van Gogh painted this in 1883 at The Hague, in his second year there, when he was still working in the shadow of the Hague School and had not yet seen Impressionism. The beds are hyacinths grown for the bulb trade; when the picture was shown in 1902 it was titled Tulpenland, or 'tulip country,' although the flowers are not tulips. The low viewpoint makes the beds recede in steep perspective, which interested him at this time. The picture apparently stayed among family belongings and was sold off cheaply in 1902. Paul Mellon bought it in 1955 and gave it to the National Gallery of Art in 1983.","c":"A modest early picture, valuable because it shows what van Gogh did before color took over. The idea is clear: strips of color pulling the eye to the horizon. But the paint is thin and the sky heavy, the cottages are dull, and the figure is small and flat. There are glimmers of what follows in the way the beds are treated as fields of pure color. It is not yet a Van Gogh in the sense people mean, but it shows an eye already thinking in color."},"met:436531":{"d":"A woman cooks at an open fireplace, the museum's keywords adding a pancake and the fire itself. The oil on canvas is small, about 44 by 38 cm, and dated 1885, from the two years Van Gogh spent in his father's parish at Nuenen in Brabant, where he painted weavers, peasants and their cottages. Her pose and the room are not described in the record, so only the subject and its humble, working setting can be given.","i":"In his Dutch period Van Gogh believed that peasants were the truest subject for a modern painter, an idea he took from Millet and from writers he admired, and he wrote that a picture of peasant life should smell of bacon, smoke and steaming potatoes. Cooking by a fire, a daily act of survival and warmth, belongs with the potatoes and coffee of The Potato Eaters, the large painting he finished at Nuenen in 1885.","c":"This is early Van Gogh, and it looks it: as in most of his Nuenen work the palette is dark and earthy, the figure heavy and the drawing awkward, since figure construction was his persistent difficulty in these years. What carries it is conviction. The firelight, the weight of the paint and the refusal to prettify a poor woman at work give a sense of lived reality that the polished genre pictures of the period lack."},"met:438722":{"d":"A woman peels potatoes: the museum's keywords list a woman, a peeler, potatoes and food preparation. The oil on canvas, about 41 by 32 cm and dated 1885, was painted at Nuenen in Brabant, where Van Gogh was studying peasant faces and hands in the months around The Potato Eaters. The record does not describe her pose or setting. On the back of the canvas Van Gogh later painted a self-portrait in a straw hat.","i":"Potatoes were the staple of the Brabant peasants and the heart of Van Gogh's ambitions in 1885; he made dozens of studies of heads and hands for The Potato Eaters, wanting to show that the people eating potatoes had dug the earth with the same hands they put in the dish. Reusing a canvas, as he did here, was a sign of poverty: paint was costly and Theo's allowance had to stretch.","c":"Heavy, quick and unglamorous, the picture is a study in labor: the figure is built with thick, dark strokes and no attempt at charm. Van Gogh's drawing is still clumsy at this stage and the figure can read as a block, but the single, repeated action of peeling gives it an honest rhythm. It is minor beside his Arles pictures but shows the seriousness that came first, before color changed everything."},"met:436524":{"d":"Sunflowers fill a horizontal canvas of about 43 by 61 cm, painted in oil in 1887. The museum's keywords give only the flower and the still-life genre, so the number of blooms, the surface they rest on and the background color are not described here. The date places the picture in Paris, where Van Gogh lived with his brother Theo and was brightening his palette under the influence of the Impressionists and Japanese prints, a year before the better-known sunflower pictures painted at Arles.","i":"In Paris Van Gogh made a small group of sunflower still lifes, studying yellow against contrasting grounds and testing the complementary colors he had read about in Delacroix and absorbed from the circle of Seurat and Signac. Sunflowers were a cheap, striking motif that needed no model. Only later, in Arles in 1888, did they become the large, famous pictures meant to decorate Gauguin's room and to stand as a kind of personal emblem.","c":"The Paris sunflowers are experimental, and this one shows the artist thinking in color: pure yellows and oranges against their opposites, laid on in quick, separate strokes. It lacks the monumental confidence of the Arles series, and the arrangement can feel casual. But the looseness is part of its charm: it is a private study from a year of rapid change rather than a finished statement, and it already has the vitality of his mature manner."},"met:436532":{"d":"A bust-length self-portrait, small and bright. The artist wears a broad yellow straw hat whose brim is built from short, parallel strokes of lemon yellow and green, above a face with a ginger beard and moustache, pale blue-green eyes and flushed cheeks. He is turned slightly away, his gaze directed past the viewer. His blue jacket and white shirt collar are painted in the same short, hatched strokes, and the background, in pinks, oranges and blues, is made of small dabs and dashes. At the edges bare canvas shows. It is a small oil painting, about 41 by 32 centimetres, painted on the back of an earlier peasant study.","i":"Van Gogh painted more than twenty self-portraits in Paris, where he lived with his brother Theo from 1886 until early 1888. In a letter he explained that he had bought a good mirror to work from himself, for want of a model, because if he could paint the colouring of his own head he could go on to paint other people. In 1887 he was absorbing Neo-Impressionism, the dotted colour method of Seurat and Signac, and the Met notes his awareness of its technique and colour theory; here it turns into his own rhythm of short, directional strokes. The canvas was reused: on its other side is The Potato Peeler, a peasant study from 1885.","c":"It is small, direct and bright: the hat is a blaze of yellow, the cool blue of the jacket sets it off, and the stabbing strokes give the head a nervous, almost electric energy. The face is searching rather than flattering. The technique is not yet entirely his own, since the Neo-Impressionist stroke feels borrowed and the patchwork background is busy where he would later simplify, but the intensity is already unmistakable. Among the Paris self-portraits it is one of the most open in colour and one of the most forceful."},"met:436527":{"d":"A flowering fruit tree stands in an orchard on a vertical canvas about 72 by 53 cm, painted in oil in 1888. The museum's keywords record only trees, so the number of trees, the colors of blossom and sky and any fence or figures are not described here. The date points to spring at Arles, when Van Gogh, newly arrived in Provence in February, painted a series of orchards in bloom in a few weeks.","i":"Blossom is the oldest image of renewal, and Van Gogh, newly arrived in the south after a hard winter in Paris, greeted it with a burst of work. His orchards owe something to Japanese woodblock prints, which he admired for their flat color, bold cropping and respect for nature, and he spoke of Provence as his own Japan. The tall format may reflect that influence.","c":"The blossoming orchards are among the happiest and most open of his pictures, free of the strain that later marks his work, and this one shows how quickly he translated Provence's light into pure, bright strokes. A critic may find the handling more cautious than in the great summer pictures, still half under the Impressionists' influence; the drawing is clear and the color clean and hopeful. It is a fresh beginning more than a summit."},"met:436530":{"d":"A vase of oleanders and a book make up this still life, a horizontal canvas about 60 by 74 cm, painted in oil in 1888. The museum's keywords list the flowers, the book and the genre, so the table, the colors of the blossoms and the book's cover are not described here. The date places it in Arles, where Van Gogh was working through the summer on flowers, fields and portraits in the strong light of Provence.","i":"The oleander is a Mediterranean shrub, beautiful and poisonous, and it grows everywhere in southern France. The book beside the vase matters: Van Gogh read voraciously, in Dutch, French and English, loved the novels of Zola, Maupassant and Dickens, and treated reading as part of life, so books appear in several of his still lifes. Whether this book carries a particular meaning is not recorded in the museum's keywords.","c":"The long, pointed leaves invite sweeping strokes, and the painter responds with a lively, calligraphic rhythm that keeps the bouquet from looking staged. The inclusion of a book gives the table a personal note, as if it were a corner of the artist's life rather than a studio arrangement. The picture is less intense than his sunflowers or portraits, but it shows his gift for making an ordinary corner of a room feel inhabited."},"met:436533":{"d":"Shoes are the sole subject of this still life, a horizontal canvas about 46 by 55 cm, painted in oil in 1888. As the plural title says, there are at least two, and the museum's keywords add nothing about their condition, the surface they stand on or the background, so these are not described here. By type it belongs to the group of shoe pictures Van Gogh painted in Paris and Arles, where an everyday object is treated as a subject worthy of full attention.","i":"Worn shoes were an old motif for Van Gogh, painted at Nuenen, Paris and Arles. Later thinkers, including the philosopher Martin Heidegger, have read them as stand-ins for a laboring life, for the artist himself, or for the walk of a pilgrim. The museum offers no such interpretation; the picture first shows what it sets down: shoes that have been walked in, given the same care as a portrait.","c":"The humble subject is treated with full seriousness: the weight of the paint and the plain design give the shoes a presence close to portraiture. The achievement is empathy rather than description, and the simplicity makes it stronger than a more elaborate arrangement would have been. Beside the bright flower pieces it is austere, but it shows the same insistence on the dignity of ordinary things."},"nga:46506":{"d":"The head and shoulders of a chubby baby fill a vertical canvas about 35 by 24 cm, painted in 1888 in shades of mint, laurel and eucalyptus green. The brushstrokes are long and visible, some thick enough to cast shadows. The skin is cool light green with peach highlights, the eyes large, vivid blue and gazing down and to the viewer's right, the lips full and the pudgy fingers touching. A white cap and garment are outlined in blue, and a gold bracelet and a gold ring with a red stone appear on one hand against a darker green ground.","i":"The baby is Marcelle Roulin, born in July 1888 to Joseph Roulin, the postal worker in Arles who became Van Gogh's close friend, and his wife Augustine. Van Gogh painted every member of the family, some more than once, and the pictures grew out of friendship. A baby was an unusual subject for him; he treats her seriously, with the attention he gave adults, so that her stare becomes the center of the picture.","c":"In a conventional portrait green flesh would be an error; here it gives the baby a cool, strange luminosity, and the heavy strokes make the cheeks and fingers feel solid. The color is invented rather than observed, which is the point, and the wide, intent gaze supplies the life. It is a small, concentrated example of Arles-period color used for feeling, and a tender one."},"nga:46626":{"d":"A girl sits in a curved cane armchair, one hand lying along its arm, the other holding a sprig of pink oleander in her lap. Seen from the knees up, she faces the viewer with a calm, direct gaze, her dark hair parted in the middle and tied at the nape with a red ribbon. Her jacket is striped brick-red and blue with a row of round gold buttons; her wide skirt is royal blue spotted with orange. Behind her is a flat field of pale aqua. The paint is thick and the brush strokes show, with green tints in the pale skin, and dark outlines emphasize the chair and the shapes of the clothes.","i":"Van Gogh painted this in Arles in July 1888. 'Mousmé' is a word from Pierre Loti's novel Madame Chrysanthème, meaning a young Japanese woman; he told his brother Theo that his was a Provençal girl of twelve to fourteen. The title joins two ideas: the Japan he believed he could find in the south of France, inspired by the Japanese prints he collected, and a local sitter. The flat color, bold outline, and flowers echo those prints. He wrote that the portrait cost him a week of effort. It came to the National Gallery of Art with the Chester Dale collection.","c":"The color is the glory: blue and orange dots against an aqua wall give a vibration the Impressionists, for all their color, seldom matched. The drawing is less certain. The left arm is oddly stretched, the hands are summary, and the face sits stiffly above the lively clothing. These roughnesses are part of the charm, since van Gogh builds the portrait out of the intensity of his looking, and the girl's mild gaze is the quiet center of the picture. It is among the freshest of his Arles portraits."},"nga:52178":{"d":"A white road and a lilac-gray stone wall curve through golden fields under a bright turquoise sky streaked with white. A man in a blue shirt and pinkish trousers walks away from the viewer through the tall grass at the left. The wall, painted in dashes of lilac, salmon pink, and blue, snakes forward from the background to a gateway with two stone pillars, beyond which stand three haystacks and a farmhouse with an orange roof. A few round green trees and one blue tree rise near the house, and red-flowered oleanders spill over the wall in the foreground. A band of blue along the horizon suggests distant mountains. The paint is thick and the strokes are long and visible.","i":"Van Gogh painted this near Arles in the summer of 1888, when the wheat harvest filled the plain with golden stacks. He had moved to Provence in February, seeking strong light and brilliant color, and he built the picture on paired opposites: blue against orange, red against green, pink clouds against turquoise sky. He admired the heavy, jewel-like paint of the Marseille painter Monticelli, whose example helped draw him south. His sister-in-law, Johanna van Gogh-Bonger, sold the picture in 1890, after his death, and Ailsa Mellon Bruce bequeathed it to the National Gallery of Art in 1970.","c":"A picture bursting with the pleasure of being in the south. The curving wall leads the eye cleverly into depth, the complementary colors vibrate, and the flowering oleander in the foreground gives the scene a sense of fullness. The faults are minor: the walking figure is almost an afterthought, and the farm buildings are summary beside the grandeur of the wall. It lacks the nervous energy of his later Saint-Rémy canvases, but it is among the most open and happy of his landscapes, and a fine example of his Arles palette."},"met:436529":{"d":"A woman with black hair gathered in a bun and tied with a dark blue ribbon sits at a green table, her head tilted and resting on one hand, her gaze drifting off to the left. She wears a dark blue dress with a pale green-white fichu, embroidered with a pink flower motif, crossed over her chest. A saturated yellow ground fills the background, flecked with small dabs of lighter yellow, and the orange back of her chair shows at the right. In the foreground lie several books, one open, with pale blue-green and orange covers. The paint is laid on in short, thick, directional strokes, and the figure is outlined in dark blue.","i":"The sitter is Marie Ginoux (1848–1911), who with her husband Joseph-Michel ran the Café de la Gare in Arles, close to the Yellow House where Van Gogh lived in 1888. She wears the traditional dress of an Arlésienne, the Provençal costume Van Gogh admired. Gauguin was staying with him that autumn and also portrayed her. Van Gogh returned to the subject in 1890, working from a drawing by Gauguin, so several versions exist; the Met dates this canvas 1888–89. The books on the table suggest that she was a reader, a quality Van Gogh prized in the people he painted.","c":"One of Van Gogh's most economical portraits. A few broad areas, the yellow ground, the green table and the dark dress, together with the blue contour around the figure, do nearly all the work, and the tilted head and drooping gaze give the sitter a quiet inwardness amid brilliant colour. It is calmer than his self-portraits of the same months. The table and books are summarily treated, and the hands are awkward. But the yellow carries the picture, setting a warm, slightly melancholy mood that a more detailed background would have spoiled."},"met:436535":{"d":"A golden wheat field fills the lower half, its stalks drawn in thick, curving strokes that look like waves of wind. At the right two dark cypresses rise like tongues of flame, the taller one reaching the top edge. Beyond them a low band of blue-grey mountains crosses the distance. Silvery-green olive trees and bushes crowd the left side, and a green bank with a few red and pink flowers edges the lower right. Above, the sky is a churning mass of white, turquoise and blue paint, with curls of cloud that echo the hills. The paint is laid on so thickly that it stands up from the canvas in ridges. Oil on canvas, about 73 by 93 centimetres.","i":"Van Gogh painted this in 1889 at Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, where he had entered an asylum that May. The Met says he began developing the composition in late June, making an initial study from the landscape, and made studio versions that September, one at the same scale (now in the National Gallery, London) and a smaller replica. In his letters he compared the cypress to an Egyptian obelisk in line and proportion and called it a splash of black in a sunny landscape. He told his brother he wanted to do something with cypresses like his canvases of sunflowers, since nobody had yet painted them as he saw them.","c":"This is among the best-known of Van Gogh's Saint-Rémy landscapes, and it earns the fame. Sky and wheat vibrate together, the cypresses anchor the right edge, and the colour, from lemon yellow to cool turquoise, is pitched with total confidence. The risk in such pictures is that energy hardens into mannerism, and in the sky the repeating curls verge on pattern. Still, the handling follows the forms closely, the wheat leans as the wind would push it, and one powerful rhythm holds the whole together. It is a landscape that moves."},"met:436536":{"d":"Women pick olives in a grove, with a stepladder against the trees, in a horizontal canvas of about 73 by 91 cm dated 1889. The museum's keywords list women, a stepladder and ladder, and the olive grove, but do not give the number of figures, their dress or the colors of tree, ground and sky, so these are not described here. By type it is a harvest scene set in a landscape, painted in the south of France while Van Gogh was living at the asylum of Saint-Rémy.","i":"The picture shows women at the olive harvest, work that Van Gogh could set beside the sowers and gleaners of Millet, whose images of rural labor he revered. It was painted during his stay in the asylum, and it shows how he kept finding subjects of ordinary work even while ill, turning to the trees and fields just outside the walls and to the people who tended them.","c":"The ladder gives a vertical accent to a horizontal field of swirling silver-green foliage, and the women, small and schematic, mainly give scale and rhythm; the trees are the real subject. The story is secondary to the surface, where the strokes twist and repeat like woven cloth. It is a convincing statement of his late landscape style, though less intimate than his portraits of the same year."},"met:437980":{"d":"A single huge cypress rises like a flame from the lower left, filling almost the full height of the canvas. Its dark green mass is built from thick, curling strokes of green, blue, and ochre that seem to twist upward. At the upper right a pale crescent moon hangs in a turquoise sky filled with swirling cream-and-pink clouds. To the right, blue-violet mountains roll away, and at the foot of the tree the foreground is a tangle of yellow-green grass with small white flowers. The paint is heavily textured. No figure or building appears, and the viewer is brought close to the base of the tree, looking up.","i":"Van Gogh painted cypresses at Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, where he was a voluntary patient in the asylum of Saint-Paul-de-Mausole from May 1889. He was fascinated by them. He told his brother Theo that they were always on his mind, as beautiful in line and proportion as an Egyptian obelisk, and that it surprised him no one had painted them as he saw them. Often planted in southern cemeteries, the cypress carries associations of mourning, yet in his letters he praises its line and color. The Met acquired this canvas in 1949.","c":"Here van Gogh makes the paint itself the subject. The cypress is less a tree than a rising wave of strokes, and the sky answers it with its own spirals, so the whole canvas moves. It is tremendously energetic and confident, and the color, with its cool greens against turquoise, is more refined than the legend of frantic painting suggests. The foreground is thinly resolved, and the all-over swirling can verge on mannerism, but at its best the picture conveys what he said he wanted: the beauty of the thing as he saw it."},"met:437984":{"d":"Augustine Roulin, wife of the Arles postal worker Joseph Roulin, sits facing the viewer and holds the cord that rocks a cradle outside the picture; the title, La Berceuse, means the woman who rocks the cradle and sings the lullaby. The museum's keywords add flowers. The vertical oil canvas, about 93 by 74 cm and dated 1889, is one of five versions Van Gogh painted of the subject.","i":"Van Gogh imagined the picture as a comfort for lonely people: he wrote that sailors and fishermen far from home might feel in it the rocking of a cradle and the sound of a lullaby, and he planned to hang it between two sunflower canvases like the central panel of a triptych. The Roulin family had stood by him after his breakdown, and the portrait is also a tribute to their kindness.","c":"The figure is simplified into large, flat shapes and set against a busy, decorative ground, so the result is closer to an icon than to a portrait of a particular woman. That is its strength and its limit: the calm, mask-like face gives the image monumentality and peace, but the personality of the sitter is largely submerged. Five versions show Van Gogh testing how color and pattern could soothe."},"nga:106382":{"d":"Seen from the chest up, a man with short orange hair and a full rust-colored beard turns his shoulders to the left and looks out sideways with pale blue eyes, his lips closed. His skin is tinted with green and ochre where the light strikes it. He wears a painter's smock in layers of cobalt, azure and lapis blue, and the background is painted in the same blues in long, nearly parallel strokes that curve around his head and shoulders like an aura. In the lower left corner he holds a palette dabbed with yellow, green and pink, together with a bundle of brushes.","i":"Van Gogh painted this in 1889 at the asylum of Saint-Paul-de-Mausole in Saint-Rémy, while recovering from a severe breakdown; the National Gallery counts it among his last self-portraits. Unlike the better-known version in the Musée d'Orsay, he holds a palette and brushes, presenting himself as a working painter rather than a patient. The orange hair and beard against blue use the contrast of complementary colors he had studied for years. He painted himself often, partly because he could not afford models. Reading the swirling background as a sign of mental turmoil is common but is only one interpretation; the strokes also follow the contours of the head and give the portrait its structure.","c":"This is steadier than the better-known Orsay self-portrait from the same year. The face is built from broad, exact planes and the sideways glance is controlled, so the swirl of blue frames the head instead of overwhelming it. The hot orange head against cold blue is the whole design, and it works. The weaker area is the bottom left, where palette, hand and sleeve cluster awkwardly at the edge. It lacks the nervous energy of the Orsay version, which some prefer, but gains in poise."},"nga:164923":{"d":"Nine oranges and lemons sit in a shallow basket, with two dark green cypress branches behind it and a pair of cobalt-blue gloves in front, on a horizontal canvas of about 48 by 62 cm. The muted orange and golden-yellow fruit and the woven basket are outlined in blue, and the long, loose strokes remain visible throughout. The top glove has three brown stripes. The ground is sand brown below and pale turquoise above, and the artist signed it Vincent Arles 89 at lower right.","i":"Signed in Arles in 1889, the picture was painted in the months after the breakdown of December 1888, while Van Gogh was out of hospital and still living in the town before he entered the asylum at Saint-Rémy in May. It is built on the contrast of orange and blue, a pair of complementary colors that fascinated him, and the cypress branches add a Provençal touch.","c":"Blue outline around orange fruit gives the picture the look of cloisonné enamel, a debt to Japanese prints and to the Pont-Aven painters, and it turns a simple arrangement into a lively play of complementary color. The loose, long strokes keep it fresh, and the gloves add an odd, personal note. It lacks the drama of the larger Arles canvases, but as a demonstration that color alone can build a picture it is persuasive."},"nga:46627":{"d":"Three women pick olives in an orchard, on or beside a ladder, in a horizontal landscape of about 73 by 92 cm dated 1889. One stands on the ladder facing us, another leans against it with her hair covered by a cloth and her back turned, and the third stands at its foot, turned away; all wear long skirts and reach for olives or into baskets. Long, parallel, curving strokes in sage green, tan, brown and spruce blue build the trees, ground and pale sky, and bare canvas shows through in places.","i":"The olive harvest comes in late autumn, and Van Gogh painted several versions of the subject at Saint-Rémy, where he was recovering in an asylum in 1889. The women at work recall Millet's peasants, whom he revered, while the gnarled trees were a motif he felt could answer the old tradition of the Garden of Olives. He declined to paint a biblical Gethsemane from imagination, as his friends Gauguin and Bernard had done, and chose the real trees and the real harvest.","c":"The surface is the achievement: trees, ground and sky are made of the same swirling, parallel strokes, so that the whole picture moves like a single fabric. The figures are schematic, outlined in dark brown and a little stiff, and the unfilled canvas gives the picture an unfinished air. But the rhythm, the subtle palette and the sense of labor as part of the landscape make it a fine example of his Saint-Rémy style."},"met:436525":{"d":"A bouquet of flowers in a vase is the single subject of this still life, a vertical canvas about 65 by 54 cm, painted in oil in 1890. The museum's keywords record only the bouquet, the vase and the flowers, so the kinds of blossom, their colors and the table or wall behind them are not described here. The date puts it in the final year of Van Gogh's life, before his death in July 1890.","i":"Flowers occupied Van Gogh through his last year, from the almond blossom and irises of Saint-Rémy to the garden flowers of Auvers, and he treated them as exercises in color contrast as well as pleasures in themselves. A bouquet was also a subject within his means: it needed no model and cost little. Whether he painted this one from a particular garden is not recorded.","c":"As flower painting it is more nervous than serene: in his last year the strokes are typically quick, the contours drawn in paint, and the bouquet becomes a field of marks. That energy gives it life, but it also leaves the arrangement looser than the carefully constructed flower pictures he made at Arles. A critic would value it as a lively late study rather than a major statement."},"met:436526":{"d":"A man, a woman and a child are shown in a garden, as the museum's keywords record, and the title gives the moment: a child's first steps. The horizontal canvas, about 72 by 91 cm and dated 1890, is a copy in oil of a composition by Jean-François Millet, which Van Gogh knew from a black-and-white reproduction, so its colors are his own invention. The record does not describe the poses.","i":"At Saint-Rémy in 1889 and 1890 Van Gogh made a series of what he called translations of other artists' work into the language of color, and he compared himself to a musician playing another composer's score in his own way. Millet's tender scene of parents coaxing a toddler forward would have had a personal meaning in early 1890, when his brother Theo's son, named Vincent after him, was born.","c":"A copy can only be as good as its translation, and Van Gogh's is a lively one: the glowing, unnatural color and the strong outlines turn Millet's sober scene into something warmer and more decorative. Some find the color a little crude beside Millet's quiet tonality, but the energy is unmistakable, and the picture shows how Van Gogh learned from artists he loved. As an independent work it is less personal than his own portraits and landscapes."},"met:436528":{"d":"A tall white pitcher stands on a bright green table, holding a spray of irises in blue and violet that fan across the canvas. Their sword-shaped leaves, in bluish green, sweep outward, and a single bloom bends at the upper left while another droops at the right in a dull purple. The wall behind is pale cream, scored with short, thick strokes of white paint. The edge of the table is a deep green band across the foot of the picture. The flowers are painted with thick curved dabs of cobalt blue and violet, outlined in darker blue. It is an oil painting on canvas, about 74 by 92 centimetres, with a strongly cropped, decorative design.","i":"Van Gogh painted irises more than once: the Getty's version was made at Saint-Rémy in 1889, soon after he entered the asylum, and the Met dates this canvas to 1890, the last year of his life. He left the asylum that May for Auvers-sur-Oise and died there that July. The bold outlines, flat ground and cropped sprays of flowers owe something to the Japanese prints he collected, and the colour is built on contrast: blue-violet blooms against a pale wall and a green table. The wall is worked in short, thick horizontal strokes, so even the empty ground has texture and movement.","c":"This is a vivid, assertive picture: the blue-violet flowers against the pale ground make a simple, strong chord, the sword-like leaves create rhythm, and the thick paint gives the petals a tactile presence. It succeeds as decoration and energy more than as botany. The great spray feels slightly cramped, the cropping is bold, and the white wall is rather chalky. Yet the repeated strokes and the cool-warm balance of blue and cream are sure, and the whole has a vitality that makes tamer flower pieces look dull."},"met:436534":{"d":"A vase of roses is the subject of this tall canvas, about 93 by 74 cm, painted in oil in 1890. The museum's keywords give roses and the still-life genre only, so the number of blooms, the vase and the colors of the flowers and ground are not described here. The date places it in the last year of Van Gogh's life, when he painted flowers at Saint-Rémy and Auvers on a larger scale than before.","i":"Roses had long carried associations of love, beauty and brevity, but Van Gogh's flowers are chiefly studies of color and rhythm. A practical point for viewers: some of the red and pink pigments he used were unstable and have faded in several of his pictures, so roses of this date can look paler today than when they were painted. The museum's keywords do not record the original color of these blooms.","c":"A large flower picture asks the painter to organize a crowd of forms, and Van Gogh does it with a swarm of strokes that give leaves and blooms a restless life. The result is more energetic than graceful, and the scale gives it a decorative presence quite unlike the intimate bouquets of earlier years. Whether the color has shifted with time is a question any viewer of his late flower pieces must keep in mind."},"met:437998":{"d":"An olive grove fills this horizontal canvas of about 73 by 92 cm, painted in oil in 1889. The museum's keywords record the grove, the landscape genre and the tree species, so the sky, the ground and the number of trees are not described here. The date places it in the months Van Gogh spent at the asylum at Saint-Rémy in Provence, where the olive groves at the foot of the nearby Alpilles hills were among his most persistent subjects.","i":"Van Gogh returned to the olive groves of Saint-Rémy again and again in 1889, writing to his brother of their silvery foliage and the way ground and sky changed color around them, and of how hard it was to catch. The trees, ancient, gnarled and long cultivated, linked him to the oldest landscape of the Mediterranean, and he painted them as living things rather than as scenery.","c":"The twisting trunks and swirling foliage are treated almost as living creatures, and the repeated, rhythmic strokes tie ground, trees and sky into one moving surface; at its best the effect is hypnotic. The danger of the method is repetition, since the same rhythm can run across the whole canvas without relief. The record does not detail this version, but olive groves are among the most consistent successes of his Saint-Rémy months."},"nga:163323":{"d":"Green fields roll back in undulating waves beside a road in a horizontal landscape of about 72 by 91 cm, painted in 1890. The fields, on the left and center, are built from thick, curling strokes of emerald, pea and celery green with touches of corn yellow; the pale green road runs up the right edge, layered with butter yellow, spring green and faint blue. The horizon sits about halfway up, and an aquamarine sky swirls with white and periwinkle clouds.","i":"Van Gogh settled at Auvers-sur-Oise, north of Paris, in May 1890 and died there in July, so the picture comes from the last weeks of his life, when he painted with extraordinary speed. Green wheat is the young crop of early summer, before it ripens to gold. Of the wheat fields of that summer he wrote to his brother that they expressed sadness and loneliness, yet he also found the countryside healthy and restoring.","c":"The strength of the picture is rhythm: the fields heave like a green sea, the road leads the eye up and back, and the swirling sky answers the ground. It is calmer and clearer than some of his last, more agitated wheat-field pictures, and the fresh palette of green and yellow keeps it buoyant. The weakness, if any, is a certain evenness of treatment, with every part of the surface carrying the same energy."},"nga:46505":{"d":"A woman in a long white dress and a floppy canary-yellow hat stands against a field of pale green wheat, shown from the knees up and turned slightly to the left. She looks off into the distance with green eyes and closed lips; her black hair is pulled back under the hat, which has a sky-blue ribbon. Her high-collared dress is covered in thick, pale-pink daubs, and a dark outline traces her figure against the background. The field is painted in long and short curved dashes of celery green, with orange poppies dotting the grass and angular blue shapes at either side. The paint is thick and the strokes are visible throughout.","i":"Van Gogh painted this in the last weeks of his life at Auvers-sur-Oise, north of Paris, where he arrived in May 1890 and died in July. He painted the same sitter in two pictures that summer and described her in a letter as a peasant woman in a big yellow hat with a knot of sky-blue ribbons; her name is not known. In these final months he worked at a great pace, producing about seventy paintings, and often used flat fields of pattern as backgrounds. The title suggests a girl, but the sitter looks like an adult woman. The picture came to the National Gallery of Art with the Chester Dale collection.","c":"A lovely, odd, quiet picture. The pale dress, thick with pink flecks, gives a tactile surface that is almost embroidered, and the wheat, a flat tapestry of dashes, makes the figure float in an airy field. The head, however, is stiff and the hands summary, and the face has an expression more weary than the cheerful palette suggests; some may find the contrast jarring. It lacks the force of his best Auvers portraits, but it shows his late method clearly: pattern, outline, and color doing the work that modeling once did."},"nga:72328":{"d":"A mass of white roses bursts from the neck of a tan jug and fills almost the whole canvas. The round blooms, tinged with blue, mauve and yellow in their shadows, overflow in every direction, set among emerald and olive leaves, many outlined in dark blue. A few blossoms lie loose at the lower left and at the foot of the jug, which stands on a sea-green table. The wall behind is mint green, scored with wavy diagonal streaks of cream that give the whole surface a sense of movement. The paint is laid on thickly in short, curling strokes, so that the bouquet seems to turn and breathe.","i":"Van Gogh painted the bouquet at Saint-Rémy-de-Provence in May 1890, in his last days at the asylum there, before he left on 16 May for Auvers-sur-Oise by way of Paris. He told his sister that he had worked like a madman on great bouquets of irises and roses. The roses were originally pink, a colour that has faded, so the pink-and-green contrast he intended has been lost. The National Gallery notes that he saw blossoming plants as signs of renewal. Because the paint was so thick, the picture was left to dry and reached him in Auvers in June. It came to the gallery in 1991 as a gift from Pamela Harriman.","c":"Even drained of its pink, the picture works. The spiralling strokes make the blossoms turn and the diagonal hatching in the wall adds a kind of wind, so the bouquet feels alive. What has been lost is the complementary contrast that held it together: the faded flowers now read as a pale mass against a pale wall, which flattens the effect and exaggerates the dark outlines. As one of the last still lifes of his life, it also shows a confidence and cheerfulness that sit awkwardly beside the popular myth of the tormented painter."},"met:435581":{"d":"A young man is the subject of this small panel, about 27 by 21 centimeters, painted around 1470 by Antonello da Messina in oil on wood. The recorded motifs name only the man and the portrait, but Antonello's portraits of this kind characteristically show a bust-length sitter in three-quarter view, lit from one side against a dark ground, so that the face stands out with almost sculptural clarity. The small scale suggests a private likeness.","i":"Antonello was the Italian painter who most thoroughly absorbed the oil technique of the Netherlands, and his portraits brought a new directness to Italian painting: the sitter turned toward the viewer, the face lit and modeled with fine gradations, the expression alive. They influenced Venetian portraiture directly after his visit to Venice in 1475-76. The sitter's name is lost, and the picture gives his youth and presence without his biography.","c":"Few Italian portraits of the 1470s combine quiet and presence so well: the face is modeled with the softness of oil, the gaze meets the viewer's, and the lack of ornament gives it a modern directness. It is small, almost austere. Compared with the profile portraits still fashionable in Florence it feels startlingly alive, and compared with Flemish models it is simplified and monumental."},"nga:37":{"d":"A young woman in an azure-blue mantle embraces her baby in this vertical panel. Shown from the waist up, Mary looks down through lowered lids, loose copper-colored tendrils showing beneath the mantle, over a brick-red robe patterned in gold. Her elbow rests on a sage-green ledge. The child, wrapped in a red cloak and seated on a green cushion, gazes off to the right, one hand at her neckline and the other arm around her neck. Behind them, sloping fields and trees recede to ice-blue hills under a nearly white sky.","i":"The half-length Madonna and Child, set close behind a ledge, was a devotional format in both Netherlandish and Italian painting, and Antonello learned from northern models how oil paint could render soft light and fine detail. The child reaching into Mary's neckline recalls the nursing Madonna, a symbol of Christ's humanity. Mary's lowered eyes suggest quiet contemplation and, perhaps, a foreboding of the Passion. The ledge separates the sacred figures from the viewer while bringing them close.","c":"Antonello fused Netherlandish oil technique with Italian monumentality, and this panel shows the blend: clear, smooth modeling, rounded faces and a luminous landscape drawn into hazy distance. It is gentle and plain, without the sweetness Bellini would develop from Antonello's example, and calm to the point of severity, which suits the quiet moment of mother and child. The transfer of the paint layer to a new support is a condition to bear in mind."},"met:435580":{"d":"Christ wearing the crown of thorns is shown in a small, close-up devotional panel, about 43 by 31 centimeters, painted in oil, possibly over tempera, by Antonello da Messina. The recorded motifs are the figure of Jesus and the crown of thorns. The museum gives no precise date, though Antonello's career ended in 1479. His small devotional images of the suffering Christ typically show the figure bust-length against a dark ground, so that the viewer meets him face to face.","i":"The crown of thorns recalls the mockery of Christ by Roman soldiers before the Crucifixion, and an image of the suffering Savior in close view was designed for private meditation, inviting compassion by confronting the viewer with the victim's face. Antonello's northern oil technique allowed him to render tears and bruises with painful realism. The type, known as an Ecce Homo, Behold the Man, after Pilate's words, was popular in late fifteenth-century Italy.","c":"Antonello's small devotional Christs are among the most affecting images of their time: the intimacy of the scale, the directness of the gaze, the realistic treatment of the suffering face and the control of light give them a force that larger altarpieces rarely match. A modern viewer may find the emotion exaggerated, but the precision of the paint keeps it from melodrama. It is a compact image that lingers."},"met:679686":{"d":"A woman reads beside a flowering plant in this oil on canvas of 1884, about 48 by 60 centimeters, whose title, Hortensia, is the French word for the hydrangea. The Belgian painter Fernand Khnopff, then in his mid-twenties, makes the woman and the flower the two principal elements of a quiet interior scene. The picture combines a figure, a book and a plant in the manner of a still life with a person.","i":"Khnopff belonged to the Belgian avant-garde group Les XX and admired the English Pre-Raphaelites and the Symbolist Gustave Moreau. A woman absorbed in reading, set beside a luxuriant flower, suggests silence, introspection and a withdrawn private world, themes that the Symbolists loved. The hydrangea, with its large, pale, almost artificial blossoms, suited the taste of the time for the exquisite and the cultivated.","c":"This is an early Khnopff, still more realist than the cool, enigmatic images that made his name, but already concerned with silence and the self-contained female figure. Its restraint and tonal control are admirable. It lacks the eerie power of his later Symbolist work, and some of its charm lies in how far it stays from them."},"nga:1196":{"d":"A small devotional panel painted in oil around 1505 by the young Raphael, about 59 by 44 cm, showing the Virgin Mary with the Christ Child. The museum's record gives no description, so the pose, the setting and the colors are not stated here; the picture is described by type. It is a private Madonna of the kind Raphael painted in numbers in Florence: an intimate image of mother and son, calm in mood and arranged with balanced, rounded forms.","i":"Raphael came to Florence around 1504 and studied Leonardo's pyramid-shaped groups and soft modeling, which he turned into gentle Madonnas for private patrons. In such pictures Mary's tenderness invites the viewer to feel the closeness of mother and child and to pray. The Small Cowper Madonna takes its name from the Earls Cowper, English owners of the picture, and the word small distinguishes it from a larger panel with the same family name painted a few years later.","c":"These early Florentine Madonnas are admired for their calm clarity: a simple, harmonious design, soft modeling and a gentleness that seems effortless. About 1505 Raphael was still close to his Umbrian master Perugino, whose sweetness he gradually enriched with Leonardo's lessons. Next to his later altarpieces such a panel is modest in ambition, but it shows the quality that made him famous: unforced grace. The record has no description, so this judgment rests on the type and date."},"nga:28":{"d":"A knight in plate armor on a rearing white horse drives a long lance down into a dragon that writhes beneath the horse's raised hooves. His helmet is pewter-gray, with a thin gold halo above it, and a blue-green cloak billows out behind him. The horse turns its head to look out of the picture; a gold strap on its neck bears the name RAPHELLO. The tawny, dog-headed dragon claws at the earth and twists its neck to stare up at the saint. At the right a woman in a ruby-red dress kneels in prayer in a meadow, and beyond her hills, pines, and two small towers fade into a pale blue sky. A rocky cave mouth fills the left edge. The panel is tiny, about 28 by 21 centimeters.","i":"On the saint's left leg is the blue garter of the English Order of the Garter, with the first word of its motto, HONI, written on it. In 1504 Henry VII of England made Guidobaldo da Montefeltro, Duke of Urbino, a knight of the Garter, and the picture was presumably painted for the duke, as a gift for the English envoy who brought the insignia or for Henry himself. Raphael was in his early twenties. The panel was in England by 1627, entered the royal collection of Charles I, was sold after the Civil War, and in 1772 reached the Hermitage in Russia. The Soviet government sold it in 1931 to Andrew Mellon.","c":"A small picture whose charm lies in its composure. Raphael takes a chivalric subject, gives the horse a spirited twist, and sets the whole in a calm Umbrian landscape, so that the violence is elegant rather than frightening. The dragon is the weak point, a rather domestic reptile who offers little threat, and the princess watching from afar is a stock figure. But the rearing horse and the billowing cloak make a splendid silhouette, and the long diagonal of the lance is placed with a young painter's complete assurance."},"nga:27":{"d":"A devotional panel, painted in oil in 1508 and measuring about 81 by 58 cm, showing the Virgin and the Christ Child. The museum record carries no description, so the pose, the setting and the colors are not given here; the picture is described by type. It is one of the larger Madonnas of Raphael's last years in Florence, with the figures designed as a single, softly modeled group and an air of settled calm.","i":"The painting takes its double name from two owners, the Niccolini family of Florence and the Earls Cowper of England. A Madonna and Child in a Renaissance home was a focus for prayer, the pairing of mother and son standing for the Incarnation. In Florence Raphael learned from Leonardo to arrange sacred figures as a compact group, and by 1508 he was carrying that idea further, with firmer forms and a clearer sense of weight.","c":"By 1508 Raphael had absorbed Leonardo's softness and was adding a new monumentality, and a picture like this shows the synthesis: a balanced group, rounded forms and a gentle mood. The weakness, for some modern viewers, is a sweetness so well balanced that it can look like a formula repeated with small variations. Its importance lies in how much later Madonna painting learned from it. The record has no description, so this rests on type and date."},"met:437371":{"d":"A very small panel, about 24 by 29 cm, painted in oil about 1504, shows the Agony in the Garden. The museum's keywords record a garden, sleeping men, an angel and other figures, which fits the Gospel scene of Christ praying on the night before his arrest while his disciples sleep. Pose, landscape and light are not described in the record. Panels of this size belong to a predella, the band of small scenes beneath an altarpiece, and this one is usually linked to Raphael's Colonna Altarpiece for Perugia.","i":"In the Gospels (Matthew 26, Luke 22) Christ prays in the garden that the cup of suffering pass from him; an angel comes to strengthen him, while Peter, James and John, asked to keep watch, fall asleep. The scene stresses his human fear and his acceptance of the Passion. In a predella it opened the story of the Passion that the altarpiece above would crown. Raphael was about twenty-one.","c":"In a panel not much bigger than a book Raphael shows his Umbrian training under Perugino: a clear, orderly space, delicate figures and a calm that keeps even this anguished subject gentle. The drama is deliberately understated, which some viewers will take for reticence; what impresses is how much he fits into a tiny space and how sure the design is for an artist barely out of his teens."},"met:437372":{"d":"A symmetrical altarpiece in an ornate gilded frame. In the main panel the Virgin sits enthroned on a stepped dais beneath a canopy, holding the Christ Child on her lap; he leans forward to bless the infant John the Baptist, who stands at the foot of the throne looking up. Four saints stand around them in a landscape of blue hills: Peter in a yellow cloak, with a book, at the left, and Catherine of Alexandria behind him; at the right a female saint, Lucy, and Paul in a red cloak, holding a book. In the semicircular lunette above, God the Father, bearded, blesses and holds a globe between two kneeling angels against a blue ground.","i":"Painted around 1504, when Raphael was about twenty-one, for the Franciscan convent of Sant'Antonio da Padova in Perugia, this is the only Raphael altarpiece in the United States. The nuns may have asked for the Christ Child and John to be fully clothed. The composition follows the calm, symmetrical manner of Raphael's teacher Perugino. The altarpiece was later split up: the predella panels were sold to Queen Christina of Sweden and are now scattered among several museums, while the main panels passed to the Colonna family of Rome, whose name it bears. J. Pierpont Morgan bought it, and the Met received it as a gift in 1916.","c":"A beautiful, calm picture, and a clear demonstration of how far a pupil can go while remaining inside his master's idiom. The triangular grouping, symmetrical saints, and sweet landscape are Perugino's formula carried out with greater grace and a fresher feeling for the figures' weight; the Child reaching toward the infant Baptist is a lively touch. But the formula is still a formula: the saints are static, and the picture lacks the dynamism and psychological richness of Raphael's later Florentine and Roman work. It is the work of a brilliant student, not yet of the master he became."},"nga:26":{"d":"A round picture, a tondo, painted in oil about 1510 on a panel that was later transferred to canvas. The museum record gives no description or size, but the composition is well known: the Virgin sits in an open landscape with the Christ Child and the infant John the Baptist, who kneels and offers a small cross to Jesus. The three figures form a pyramid within the circle, set against hills and sky.","i":"The cross is the point: the infant Baptist, who in the Gospels proclaims Christ as the Lamb of God, offers it to the Child, who takes it, accepting his future Passion, while the Virgin's calm watchfulness shows both love and understanding. The tondo shape, favored in Florentine homes, suited the unified group. The painting takes its name from the Spanish Dukes of Alba, who once owned it.","c":"Raphael's tondo is the summit of his Madonnas in the round: the group is monumental yet gentle, the Virgin's turning pose recalls Michelangelo's Doni Tondo, and the landscape adds breathing room. The transfer from panel to canvas, made long ago, has inevitably altered the surface, so some of the delicacy of the original handling is lost. Even so, the design carries: circle, pyramid and gaze are perfectly tuned."},"nga:12131":{"d":"A half-length portrait in oil on panel, about 60 by 44 cm and dated about 1515, of the young Florentine banker Bindo Altoviti, who was then in his mid-twenties and living in Rome. The museum's record gives no description of costume, background or pose beyond the title, so these are not detailed here. It is a portrait of a private patron of the arts, not a commission from a ruler.","i":"Bindo Altoviti was a banker from a Florentine family settled in Rome and a patron who collected and commissioned works by leading artists of his day, among them Raphael, Cellini and Vasari. A portrait by Raphael declared taste and connections as much as appearance: the sitter presents himself as a young man of culture, at ease and in fashion. In Rome, banking and art patronage went hand in hand at the papal court.","c":"The portrait is admired for its ease: a relaxed, youthful grace takes the place of the stiff dignity of earlier portraits, and it shows Raphael's rare gift for flattering a sitter without erasing him. Some find the idealization too smooth to reveal character, yet few Renaissance portraits seem so modern in their informality. What lingers is charm rather than psychology."},"cma:122348":{"d":"A young man shown in oil on a wood panel, about 58 by 44 cm, dated about 1629 or 1632. The museum calls it a tronie, a Dutch word for a head or facial expression, in which strong contrast of light and shade shapes the contours of the face. A striped scarf and a glittering pendant on a jeweled chain add an exotic touch. The picture bears Rembrandt's monogram, but its authorship is not certain.","i":"Tronies were not meant as identifiable portraits but as studies of facial appearance and fanciful costume, and around 1630 Rembrandt used the popular format to explore lighting effects and expressions. Earlier writers took the sitter to be Rembrandt himself; the museum now considers him an anonymous study of human character. Scarves and chains were the kind of props a studio kept for such pictures. Some scholars give the panel to Isaac de Joudreville, a pupil and associate who painted similar tronies.","c":"The lighting is the point: strong contrasts model the face and make the jewels sparkle, exactly what Rembrandt and his Leiden circle were exploring. The smooth, elegant finish is what the museum associates with Isaac de Joudreville rather than the more varied handling of Rembrandt's secure works, so the picture is best taken as a product of his circle, interesting for how widely his lighting experiments spread."},"met:437385":{"d":"A man in a turban and eastern-style dress is the subject of a large canvas, about 153 by 124 cm, dated 1632; the museum's keywords record a man, a portrait and Orientalist painting. Pose, costume details and setting are not described, so the picture is described by type. By its scale it is a showpiece in the exotic manner the young Rembrandt cultivated in Amsterdam: a character figure in rich costume, with an unnamed sitter, rather than a commissioned portrait of a known person.","i":"Dutch artists and collectors of the 1630s were fascinated by the East, with which Dutch trade was growing, and Rembrandt owned turbans, robes and weapons that he used for character heads and for figures in biblical scenes, where eastern dress signaled an ancient, faraway world. Whether this sitter is a real person posing in costume or an invented type is not recorded. The size and finish suggest an ambitious picture for a collector.","c":"Painted in his first Amsterdam year, the picture shows Rembrandt confident enough to take on a large, showy canvas, with rich textures, deep shadow and a figure that fills the space. To a modern eye the theatricality is the weakness: fancy dress can feel like a pretext rather than character. The strength is the sure, bold painting of costume and light, which would later become more inward."},"met:437386":{"d":"A man's portrait on a canvas about 112 by 89 cm, painted in 1632. The museum's keywords give only a man and the portrait genre, and the title says that the sitter is probably a member of the Van Beresteyn family, so costume, pose and setting are not described here. A portrait of this size was a commission for a prosperous household, and this one was evidently painted as a pair with a portrait of a woman of the same size, now in the same museum.","i":"The man's and woman's portraits of 1632 share a size and a title, which shows they were made as pendants: husband and wife facing each other across the space between them, the usual Dutch format for a marriage pair. Rembrandt painted The Anatomy Lesson of Dr. Nicolaes Tulp the same year, as his Amsterdam career took off. The word probably in the title admits that the sitter's identity remains uncertain.","c":"Early Amsterdam portraits by Rembrandt succeeded because they gave rigid conventions a lively presence: a direct gaze, a clear light on the face and often the sense that the sitter has just turned toward the viewer. Rivals such as Thomas de Keyser were more formal. Without a detailed description the verdict has to stay with the type: crisp, polished and confident, but bound by the demands of costume and rank."},"met:437387":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man, painted in oil on a wood panel about 76 by 52 cm and dated 1632. The museum's keywords give only man and portrait, so clothing, pose and background are not described here. The size suits a half-length likeness, and the date points to the first period of Rembrandt's success in Amsterdam, where he had just settled to work as a portraitist.","i":"When Rembrandt moved to Amsterdam, around 1631 to 1632, he joined the studio of the art dealer Hendrick Uylenburgh and quickly became fashionable as a portraitist among merchants, regents and ministers. Sitters wanted an honest, dignified likeness that also showed standing and means. The sitter here is not identified, so the picture stands for a type: the successful Amsterdam man of the early 1630s.","c":"What made Rembrandt's early portraits stand out was the handling of light and flesh, where soft, directed illumination gives faces volume and life. The limits are the conventions of the commission: costume and pose follow fashion and the sitter's wishes. The record offers no description of this panel, so the verdict is general: a good example of the portrait business that built his fame, with its strengths and constraints."},"met:437388":{"d":"A woman's portrait on a canvas about 112 by 89 cm, painted in 1632. The museum's keywords give only a woman and the portrait genre, and the title says the sitter is probably a member of the Van Beresteyn family, so costume, pose and setting are not described here. The identical size and title show that it was painted as the pendant of the man's portrait, and a woman in a pair like this normally appears in dark, costly clothing with white linen.","i":"In a Dutch marriage pair the two portraits hang side by side, the husband on the viewer's left and the wife on the right, so that the figures seem to face each other and the union reads as one household. Women were shown with decorum: dark silk or wool, white collar and cap, hands and face lit, and jewelry as the sign of means. The word probably in the title admits that the sitter's identity is not certain.","c":"Seen as a pair, the pictures show Rembrandt giving formal commission portraits a lively, convincing presence without breaking decorum. Women's portraits were more constrained than men's, and the risk is a certain stiffness of costume and pose; the compensation is the care he gave to faces and hands. As the record is silent about details, the verdict stays general: an accomplished, representative piece of his early Amsterdam work."},"met:437389":{"d":"A woman in armor is shown as Bellona, the Roman goddess of war, on a canvas about 127 by 97 cm, dated 1633. The museum's keywords record the goddess, the woman and the armor, and classify the picture as a tronie, a character study in costume rather than a story, so her pose, the colors and any shield or helmet are not described here. It is an imaginary figure probably painted from a model, with mythology as its pretext.","i":"Bellona was the Roman goddess of war, usually shown in helmet and armor with a spear or shield and associated with the fury of battle rather than with strategy, which belonged to Minerva. Armed female figures were a familiar subject in the Dutch Republic, then at war with Spain, and could stand for martial courage or the defense of the state. Rembrandt probably used such imaginary figures to explore light on metal.","c":"The gleam of armor is the picture's showpiece: in his mid-twenties Rembrandt builds the metal from reflected light and shadow and lets the dark ground push it forward. As a goddess she may seem less convincing, more a calm studio model than a figure of fury, and the interest is pictorial rather than narrative. It is a bravura exercise rather than one of his deeper characterizations."},"met:437390":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed woman, painted in oil on a wood panel about 68 by 50 cm and dated 1633. The museum's keywords say only that it shows a woman, so her dress, pose, expression and the background are not described here. In size and format it is a half-length or bust portrait of the kind Rembrandt produced in large numbers for Dutch families in the early 1630s.","i":"Rembrandt's portraits of 1633 date from the busiest, most fashionable phase of his early career, when sitters from merchant and ministerial families wanted a likeness that was dignified and true. A woman's portrait would show sober dress, white linen and perhaps a modest piece of jewelry as the sign of means. Because no sitter is named, the picture speaks for its type more than for an individual.","c":"A small panel like this depends on the face, and Rembrandt's strength is the soft, observant modeling of skin and the economy of the design. The record gives no sign of anything beyond a skilled, well-judged commission, however, and such pictures can look conventional beside his history paintings and self-portraits. Their value is as evidence of the volume and quality of his portrait business in the years that made his name."},"met:437391":{"d":"An unidentified young woman is the subject of this large canvas, about 126 by 101 cm and dated 1633, and the title gives her an accessory, a fan. The museum's keywords add nothing about dress, pose or setting, so these are not described here. By type it is a commissioned portrait of a prosperous young woman of the Dutch Republic, painted in the first years of Rembrandt's success in Amsterdam, with the fan serving as both a fashionable accessory and something for the hands to do.","i":"Fans were a fashionable accessory and a mark of leisure and means in the 1630s, and portraits of young women often gave them a fan, a glove or a flower to hold. The sitter is not named, so her status can be read only in general terms: she is well-off, and the size of the canvas shows that her family paid for a full-scale portrait. Portraits of this size were often made in pairs.","c":"The scale gives the sitter real presence, and Rembrandt's light, falling softly on face and hands, avoids the flat stiffness of many commissions. Still, a critic would note that fashionable costume and accessory dominate the design, and that the portrait reveals little of the sitter's character. In his rapid early success Rembrandt could make such a picture efficiently; it is capable and attractive more than searching."},"nga:177795":{"d":"A bearded man, shown from the waist up, holds up a rolled sheet of paper marked with musical notation, in a vertical panel about 66 by 48 cm. He wears a black wide-brimmed hat, a black jacket and a broad, layered white collar, with a gold ring on his little finger, and turns his face to look out with dark gray eyes under heavy lids. His lips are slightly parted, a mole marks his cheek, and light from the upper left throws his shadow on a brown wall. The panel is signed Rembrandt f 1633.","i":"The sheet of music and the parted lips invite a reading of the sitter as a singer or music lover, although the museum records no name or profession. In seventeenth-century portraits musical notation could also signal harmony, refinement and a cultivated life. The signature, Rembrandt f 1633, uses the Latin fecit, made it, and dates the panel to his first years in Amsterdam, when he was building a reputation as a portraitist.","c":"A direct, unflattering portrait: bags under the eyes, a mole, jowls and a bump on the nose are all there, and the dark coat gives the lit face and hand a strong stage. The greenish cast of the hand and the slack, open mouth are bolder than a standard commission requires, and they give the man a believable, momentary presence. It shows Rembrandt's early skill in catching a living expression."},"cma:124089":{"d":"A woman wearing a lace collar and gold jewelry is the subject of this portrait, painted in oil on a wood panel about 78 by 65 cm and dated 1635 or earlier. The museum notes that smooth, fluid strokes render her soft skin, while thicker, more textured strokes bring out the collar and jewelry. The panel has been cut down from its original rectangular shape, and it was probably painted as a pair with a portrait of a man, now in a museum in Japan.","i":"At twenty-five Rembrandt moved from Leiden to Amsterdam, and within a few years he was one of the city's most successful portraitists. The museum notes that the lavish lace and gold would have pleased a wealthy client, and that such was the demand that he probably used assistants to help finish many portraits, this one included. The likely pendant, a man's portrait, is now in the Kawamura Memorial Museum of Art in Japan.","c":"The contrast between smooth flesh and thickly worked lace and gold is an effective device: attention goes first to the living face and then to the sitter's wealth, exactly the order a client would want. Where assistants helped, as the museum suggests, quality can vary from passage to passage. It is a polished, persuasive commission rather than a daring one, and a fair measure of the standard of Rembrandt's workshop in the mid-1630s."},"nga:572":{"d":"A man in oriental dress, the title says, is shown on a canvas of linen about 99 by 75 cm, painted in oil about 1635. The museum's record gives no description, so his pose, headgear, fabrics and setting are not given here; the picture is described by type. It belongs to the group of exotic character figures in fancy costume that Rembrandt painted in the 1630s, closer to a tronie than to a commissioned portrait of a named person.","i":"Costumes drawn from the East carried a double meaning in the Dutch Republic: they evoked the real trade routes to the Ottoman and Persian worlds and also served painters as a shorthand for biblical and ancient settings. Rembrandt kept a studio stock of robes, turbans and weapons and posed models in them for character heads and for figures in his Old Testament scenes. Whether the sitter was a model, a friend or an imagined character is not recorded.","c":"Studio showpieces of this kind give Rembrandt the chance to enjoy heavy cloth, glinting ornament and a face lit from one side, and he handles them with assurance. Their weakness is the lack of a purpose beyond display: the sitter has no story, and the exotic dress risks becoming costume. The lack of detail in the record prevents closer judgment, but the type is a lively expression of the painter's early love of texture and theater."},"nga:80":{"d":"An elderly woman sits in a wooden chair, seen from the knees up, in a black dress with white cuffs, a wide starched and pleated collar, a brown stole and a close black cap. She looks off to the left with brown eyes, her face lined and slightly jowled, and holds a pair of pince-nez spectacles in one hand, which rests on a thick, clasped book in her lap. The vertical oil canvas, about 110 by 92 cm and dated 1637, is almost all dark brown and black, relieved by the white of collar, cuffs and face.","i":"Spectacles and a closed book are the attributes of a woman who reads, most likely a Bible or devotional work, and in Dutch Protestant culture such an image suggests piety and learning as well as age. Her name is not recorded. Rembrandt painted several portraits of elderly women in the 1630s and 1640s, and they are often counted among his most sympathetic: age is shown with respect, not flattery.","c":"This is a portrait of age without flattery or fuss: the lined face, the heavy lids and the pause with the glasses in her hand create a living presence out of a mostly dark composition. The black dress merges with the ground so that the light is gathered on face, hands and book. It is a restrained, humane picture and, for its date, a strong example of how Rembrandt could dignify an ordinary sitter."},"nga:85":{"d":"A moustached man with heavy jowls looks out from a plain, cracked grey wall, his body turned to one side and his face turned back toward the viewer. He wears a tall dark fur hat banded with a gold chain and a gold medallion, a thick fur-trimmed coat over a rust-red sleeve, and a single pearl in his ear. A gold chain lies across his chest, and a pendant ornament with a black tassel hangs at his shoulder. In his right hand he grips an upright gold-tipped staff. Light from the upper left falls on the furrowed forehead, the nose and the hand, while the fur is brushed thinly and darkly. The signature and the date 1637 are in the upper right.","i":"Nobody knows who the sitter was. The title refers only to the costume, a fur hat, long coat and chains that have long been read as Polish or eastern European; Polish names have been suggested over the years, but none is secure. Rembrandt, who collected exotic dress and props, may have painted a real man in his own clothes, or a model in costume for a character study of the kind the Dutch called a tronie, and the picture may sit somewhere between the two. Dated 1637, when Rembrandt was thirty-one and a leading portrait painter in Amsterdam, it hung in the Hermitage in St Petersburg in the eighteenth century and reached Washington as part of the Mellon collection.","c":"The face is the success: slightly tired, the brows drawn together, the mouth just open, as if the sitter had been spoken to. The fur, gold and chain are built in rough, thick strokes that read as texture at arm's length, and the hat gives the head a strong, dark shape. The costume, however, tells more than the man does. Beside Rembrandt's portraits of identified sitters from the same decade the character feels unspecific, more type than individual. It is an accomplished painting of dress and a good, if guarded, painting of a person."},"met:437392":{"d":"A portrait of Herman Doomer, named in the title with approximate life dates of about 1595 to 1650, painted in oil on a wood panel about 75 by 55 cm and dated 1640. The museum's keywords give only a man and a portrait, so his clothing, pose and expression are not described here. The sitter was a craftsman rather than a merchant or magistrate: an ebony worker who made frames, and a man whom Rembrandt knew through his trade.","i":"Herman Doomer was a German-born ebony worker in Amsterdam who made frames, and the two men knew each other professionally; Doomer's son Lambert later trained in Rembrandt's studio. A portrait of a craftsman by a famous painter records a friendship in the art world and the dignity of skilled work, and it reminds viewers that Rembrandt's sitters were not only wealthy patricians but the people around his own trade.","c":"By 1640 Rembrandt's portraits had become broader and more atmospheric than the crisp commissions of 1632, and this one is admired for the way it gives an older, working man gravity and presence without fuss. As often with Rembrandt, the light picks out the face and leaves the rest in a warm half-dark. The risk of such restraint is austerity, but the picture rewards slow looking."},"nga:1210":{"d":"A young woman's face emerges from a deeply shadowed background, shown from the waist up with her body angled to the viewer's right and her face turned toward us. She has gray eyes, a rounded nose and slightly parted pink lips, and a golden veil covers her long strawberry-blond hair and falls over the shoulders of her dark dress, which has a high white lace collar and a gold chain. The panel, about 62 by 49 cm, is painted mostly in deep browns and earth tones, with light that gives her face and hair an aura-like effect.","i":"The sitter is Saskia van Uylenburgh (1612 to 1642), the daughter of a Frisian burgomaster, whom Rembrandt married in 1634 and who died after eight years of marriage. The museum dates the panel in two stages, begun about 1634 to 1635 and completed about 1638 to 1640, so it was probably reworked later; the fanciful veil and chain place it between a portrait and a tronie, the artist's imaginative costume piece.","c":"The face rising from the dark, lit by a soft glow on skin and veil, is one of Rembrandt's tenderest early images, and the limited palette of browns and gold gives it a hushed mood. The hybrid character, part likeness and part fancy dress, makes it less revealing as a record of Saskia than a drawing or a more direct portrait would be. Even so, its intimacy is easy to feel."},"met:437393":{"d":"Bathsheba is shown seated and being washed, with a group of figures around her and a fountain nearby, as the museum's keywords record. The small oil panel, about 57 by 76 cm and dated 1643, is a horizontal composition of a biblical subject treated as a nude, one of the genres the museum lists alongside religious art. The record does not describe the poses, the architecture or the light, so they are left to the viewer's eye.","i":"The story comes from the Second Book of Samuel (chapter 11): King David sees Bathsheba bathing from his roof, summons her, and, after she conceives, arranges the death of her husband Uriah in battle; the prophet Nathan then condemns him. A picture of the bath gave artists a chance to show a female nude within a respectable biblical frame, and it carries a warning about desire and abuse of power. Rembrandt returned to the subject in 1654 in a larger, more famous picture, now in the Louvre.","c":"Rembrandt's nudes are generally admired for refusing classical idealization, and in this small panel the figure's presence is meant to rest on warm color and a softly lit body rather than on ideal proportion. The scene is less psychologically charged than his later Bathsheba in the Louvre, where the sitter's thoughts become the subject, and the small size keeps it intimate. It is a charming and skillful picture rather than a profound one."},"met:437401":{"d":"A portrait of a man holding a pair of gloves, painted in oil on a wood panel and dated 1648. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's dress, pose or setting, so only the kind of picture can be stated: a single-figure commission of about 81 by 67 centimeters, in which the gloves are the one object named in the title and in the subject keywords. The sitter is not identified in the title, and he is shown as an individual rather than as a character type.","i":"Gloves were part of a gentleman's equipment in seventeenth-century Dutch portraits, held or worn to signal rank, manners and leisure, and many sitters of Rembrandt's generation were painted with them. The accessory suggests a man of standing, probably a merchant or professional of Amsterdam, though the record names no one. Because the sitter is anonymous, the picture is best read as a likeness made for his family or business circle, meant to show him dignified and composed.","c":"By 1648 Rembrandt had moved away from the showy portraits of his early Amsterdam years toward quieter, more concentrated likenesses, with the face as the center of light and the costume left to sink into shadow. The record offers no further detail of this panel, so a fair verdict can only be general: a sober commission from a painter who made more of a sitter's presence than of his props, with the gloves as the expected mark of status."},"nga:1201":{"d":"A windmill stands on a round, stone-walled bastion that fills the left half of the picture, dark brown against a sky heavy with storm cloud on the left and open blue and white light on the right. Its sails catch the sun. Below it a path runs to the water's edge, where a woman kneels over a basket of washing, a ragged man leans on the wall, and another woman leads a child down the slope. A man in a red jacket rows a small boat across the silvery water at the lower right, and trees on the far bank are reflected in it. The foreground is built from thin, warm browns, the sky from freer, wetter strokes.","i":"The site is probably imagined or composite. The National Gallery's catalogue mentions bastions with mills near Amsterdam and in Leiden, where Rembrandt's father was a miller, as possible sources. Landscape was a small part of his painted work, and this, dated 1645 to 1648, is the best known of the few examples. Doubts about its authorship were raised in the twentieth century, but a broad consensus now accepts it as Rembrandt's. Its sale from an English collection in 1911 to the American collector Peter A. B. Widener for £100,000 caused controversy in Britain and was raised in the House of Commons. It entered the National Gallery of Art with the Widener bequest in 1942 and was cleaned in 1977–79.","c":"The Mill is admired more for atmosphere than for incident. The bastion is almost an abstract mass of brown, so the sails and the washerwoman's pale cloth carry the light, and the storm sky is brushed with a freedom that anticipates later landscape painting. The weakness is the foreground, where the figures are summary and the drama belongs to the sky. Still, it is one of the few places where Rembrandt's handling of shadow and glow is applied to open air instead of to a face, and the result is moodier than most Dutch landscapes of his day."},"met:437394":{"d":"Out of a dark brown ground, an elderly man with a full beard and a wide black hat looks down at a pale bust. He stands in three-quarter view, one hand laid on the bust's head, the other on his hip, wearing a black sleeveless tunic over full white sleeves and a heavy gold chain that falls across his chest. Light gathers on the hat band, the sleeves, the chain, and the bust, leaving his eyes in shadow. At the left a stack of books is just visible. The paint is thick and rough where the gold and silk catch the light and thinly glazed in the darks, and the whole picture glows warmly. The canvas is nearly square.","i":"Rembrandt painted this in 1653 for Antonio Ruffo, a Sicilian nobleman who asked for a picture without prescribing the subject. The scholar Julius Held argued in 1969 that the figure is Aristotle, wearing the chain that Alexander the Great, his pupil, gave him, and contemplating Homer. Readings differ: it may show a philosopher weighing wealth and fame against poetry, or simply thought itself, and the picture does not settle the question. Ruffo later commissioned more pictures from Rembrandt, including a Homer. The Metropolitan Museum bought this one in 1961 for $2.3 million, at the time the highest price paid for any picture.","c":"Few pictures of thought are as convincing with so little action. The costume of velvet and gold is set against the pale poet, and the contrast between worldly reward and imaginative power is carried by the touch of a hand alone. The tone is rich, the handling free, and the shimmer of the sleeve is among Rembrandt's most daring passages. The weak point is the unresolved subject: nothing in the picture says what Aristotle feels, and viewers may supply melancholy too readily. But that ambiguity, a hand laid on a head, is what keeps the picture alive."},"met:437395":{"d":"A three-quarter-length portrait on canvas, about 140 by 115 centimeters, dated 1654. The title identifies the sitter as probably Floris Soop (1604 to 1657) and as a standard bearer, the officer who carried a unit's flag. The museum's record gives no further description, so the picture can be described only by its type: a large single-figure portrait in which the sitter's role is named in the title itself, painted at the point in the 1650s when Rembrandt's brushwork had become broad and heavily loaded.","i":"A standard bearer held an honored post, since the flag was the rallying point and emblem of a company, and portraits of such officers were a respected genre in the Dutch Republic. The title expresses doubt about the sitter, saying \"probably\" Floris Soop, so the identification should be treated as likely rather than certain. The picture is usually read as a portrait of a man in a role of public trust, not only a record of his features.","c":"Rembrandt's portraits of the 1650s are among his boldest, with thick, assertive paint and a willingness to let costume and flag become areas of pure painterly invention. The record gives no particulars of this canvas, so the verdict rests on the artist's manner in the period: a commission that turns a conventional military portrait into an image of presence, at the cost of the polish that more fashionable portraitists offered their clients."},"met:437396":{"d":"A portrait on canvas, about 78 by 69 centimeters, of Hendrickje Stoffels (1626 to 1663), dated to the mid-1650s. The museum's record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type. It is a likeness of a woman in Rembrandt's own household rather than a commissioned portrait of a client, and it belongs to the years when his handling of paint had become broad, warm and thickly layered.","i":"Hendrickje Stoffels came into Rembrandt's household as a servant after the death of his wife Saskia, and became his companion and the mother of his daughter Cornelia, born in 1654. She is thought to have posed for several of his pictures of these years, including Bathsheba in the Louvre. When Rembrandt's finances collapsed in the 1650s she helped keep his affairs running, which makes a portrait of her a record of personal loyalty as well as of a face.","c":"Portraits of people Rembrandt knew intimately are often his most direct, since there was no client's vanity to manage. Without a description in the record, the judgment is necessarily general, but this is the kind of picture in which his late style reveals itself: modest in pose, generous in its warm tones and tender in its regard for the sitter. It tends to be admired more for feeling than for display."},"nga:82":{"d":"A woman seen from the lap up against a dark background, dressed in black with a crisp white collar, cap and cuffs. Soft light from the left picks out her oval face, flushed cheeks and slightly parted lips. One arm rests on a table covered with a rose-red patterned carpet, beside two pieces of fruit, perhaps apples, and a clasped book. Her other hand holds a deep pink carnation on the arm of a wooden chair. She wears a ring on the third finger of each hand. The signature, \"Rembrandt f. 1656\", is painted near the upper right.","i":"The carnation, called a pink in Dutch and English, was often given to a bride or held by a sitter in marriage portraits, where it is usually read as a sign of love and fidelity. The rings may support that reading, though the museum does not name the sitter or say that the portrait marked a wedding. The clasped book suggests piety or learning, and the fruit and rich carpet show household comfort. Together the objects describe a respectable, prosperous woman.","c":"A quiet, unflattering and convincing portrait. Rembrandt gives the face warm, softly modeled flesh and sets the white linen in crisp, bright strokes, while the carpet's red answers the carnation as the only strong color. The pose is a standard seated formula, so the interest lies in the sitter's steady gaze and in the way light shapes her features. It lacks the drama of his late biblical figures but shows his authority with ordinary sitters."},"nga:1198":{"d":"An older, bearded man sits at a desk in a shadowy room, shown from about the knees up, with one elbow on the arm of a heavy wooden chair and his hand pressed to his forehead. His other hand holds a quill and brushes his knee. He wears a voluminous brown robe, loosely brushed with golden highlights, over a rust-red garment. Papers lie on the desk, and the oversized hilt of a long sword leans against the wall behind it. White highlights on the collar, the quill and the sword are the most freely painted areas.","i":"The sword is the traditional attribute of Saint Paul, who according to tradition was beheaded in Rome, and the quill and papers recall the letters he wrote to early Christian communities. The hand pressed to the brow is a long-standing gesture of reflection. Together they show Paul as a writer pausing to think, not as a preacher in action. The setting is usually read as a study, or possibly a prison cell, where he composed some of his letters.","c":"One of the most persuasive of Rembrandt's late images of thought. The figure is not heroic: he is an old man tired and absorbed, and the paint, rubbed and loaded in earth tones, carries that weight without any rhetoric. The glint on the sword is the single sharp accent, and the shadow absorbs the room so completely that the saint seems to occupy a space of his own. It is a rare case where a saint reads as a person."},"nga:1204":{"d":"Three men and a woman sit around a wooden table in a dim room, painted almost entirely in warm bronze, golden yellow and dark brown. On the right, Mercury is backlit so that his face is nearly a silhouette; one palm lies on the table by a glass of amber liquid. At the other end an old woman with a dark head cloth holds a gray fowl in her lap, beside a gray-bearded man who clutches a rust-red cap. Facing the viewer, a long-bearded figure in a gleaming copper and silver robe raises one hand over the table. A hearth glows at the left. Signed and dated 1658.","i":"The subject comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses. Jupiter and Mercury, traveling disguised as mortals, were refused shelter everywhere except at the poor cottage of the aged couple Philemon and Baucis, who shared all they had and were about to sacrifice their only goose. The gods then revealed themselves, destroyed the inhospitable village, and rewarded the pair. The picture shows the moment of recognition, which Rembrandt treats as a quiet realization rather than a thunderous reveal.","c":"Mythological subjects are rare in Rembrandt's late work, and this is a small, intense example. The golden gloom, the dim hearth and the old couple's hesitant faces make the story a human one, while the god's raised hand and glowing robe are enough to signal divinity. A critic could say that the drama is understated almost to the point of obscurity, but the warmth of the color and the feeling for hospitality are entirely characteristic of him."},"nga:1206":{"d":"A man seen from the hips up in a black suit and tall black hat, set against a dark brown background. His body is turned almost in profile to the right, but he looks at the viewer from the corners of his eyes under lifted brows, and the wide hat brim casts a shadow across them. Shoulder-length curly hair falls on a wide lace-edged white collar, and broad white cuffs encircle his wrists. A black velvet cape hangs from one shoulder. One arm points to the right while the other hand holds fawn-brown gloves, cut off by the bottom edge.","i":"The black suit with white collar and cuffs was the sober dress of prosperous Amsterdam citizens in the 1650s, a fashion that signaled wealth through fine cloth and linen instead of color. The tall hat and gloves mark a gentleman, and the pointing arm gives the portrait a sense of address, as though the sitter had turned to speak. The title leaves him unnamed, and the picture is thought to be one of a pair of portraits.","c":"A bold design built from a few strong masses: the black figure, the bright collar and cuffs, and the face half-hidden under the hat's shadow, which gives it a guarded, watchful air. The black coat takes light with a convincing sheen. The pointing arm is a little stagey, a stock gesture of the genre, but the sidelong glance and the sense of a particular, thinking man place it well above routine commissions of the period."},"nga:1207":{"d":"A woman seen from the hips up against a dark background, dressed in a black dress with wide white cuffs. She holds a full, billowing white ostrich feather, which arches down from one hand and is steadied at her waist by the other. Light falls from the left, shadowing one cheekbone and her neck. A transparent, lace-edged white wrap, fastened with a bow, covers her shoulders. She wears a thick, possibly jeweled bracelet on each wrist, two gold rings, one set with a clear gem, dangling gold earrings and a round pendant necklace.","i":"An ostrich-feather fan was a costly accessory, imported and mounted on a handle, and it signals fashion and wealth in the same way as the gold jewelry and the fine lace. The portrait closely matches the Gentleman with a Tall Hat and Gloves in the same collection in size and date, and the two seem to have been conceived as a pair, as husband and wife portraits often were. The museum title does not name her.","c":"The white feather is the showpiece, a loose, airy form painted with quick, confident strokes against the density of the black dress. The glints of gold and the transparent wrap are touched in with great economy, so that the wealth is suggested without being itemized. As a face, she is less searching than some of Rembrandt's late sitters, and the pose follows a fixed formula, but the surface is lively and beautifully judged."},"met:437398":{"d":"A young woman in a wide black hat turns her head to the left, in profile, her face softly lit against a dark brown ground. The hat is crowned with leaves and small pink blossoms; she wears a pearl necklace and a single pearl drop earring. A loose white blouse with gathered, ruffled sleeves fills the centre of the canvas, and a yellow apron or skirt scattered with tiny pink flowers falls below it. At the left she holds out a few flowers in one hand, while her other hand gathers a heavy brown cloth. The paint on the blouse and sleeves is thick and quick, and the colours are warm and low-keyed: cream, ochre and brown, with accents of red.","i":"Flora, the Roman goddess of spring and flowers, was a favourite subject in Dutch art, and Rembrandt had painted her earlier, in 1634, with his wife Saskia as the model. This picture, of about 1654, is a fancy-dress personification in the manner of Venetian half-lengths such as Titian's. The Met says the face recalls Saskia, who died in 1642, and to a lesser degree Hendrickje Stoffels, but that no portrait of either woman was intended; the often-repeated identification with Hendrickje is therefore a guess. Costume pieces of this kind were popular with Dutch collectors, and the picture shows Rembrandt in the 1650s drawn to Venetian warmth and broader handling.","c":"The Flora is a more careful picture than its rough surface suggests. The glowing face and the pearls are handled delicately, while the blouse and sleeves are built from thick, rapid strokes that look forward to the freest passages of Rembrandt's later work. The weakness is the arrangement: the pose is a little stiff, the gesture with the flowers is not resolved, and the flowers themselves are barely painted, so the allegory feels thin. Still, few pictures show better how he turned a costume piece into a study of light on fabric and skin."},"nga:79":{"d":"An older man is shown from the waist up, his body turned to the left and his face turned back toward the viewer. His brow is furrowed, and his deep-set gray eyes are tired but steady; the nose is bulbous and faintly pink, the cheeks sunken and shaded, and he wears a wispy gray mustache and goatee. A dark brown beret with a thin gold band sits over gray curls scratched into the wet paint, and his coat has a high collar turned up. Light falls from the upper left onto the face, while the body, the clasped hands in the lower corner and the background sink into brown darkness. The signature and date, Rembrandt f. 1659, are at left.","i":"Rembrandt painted this in his early fifties, three years after the insolvency that forced the sale of his house and his large collection. He painted himself throughout his career, and in his later years many of these portraits become more direct and less theatrical. The provenance is documented from Montagu House by 1770, then through the Duke of Buccleuch's collection; Andrew Mellon bought it in 1929 and it passed to the National Gallery in 1937. Whether the weary expression reflects his troubles is something viewers read in, since he never said so; the unsparing record of an aging face is clear.","c":"This is late Rembrandt in condensed form: a face built from thick, warm strokes that emerge from near-black, with everything else left deliberately unresolved. There is no costume or gesture to hide behind, and the searching look is one of the most direct in portraiture. The weak points are minor. The hands are dark and barely readable, and the brown coat melts into the background, leaving the lower half of the canvas almost empty. That emptiness forces all attention onto the eyes: there is observation here, not performance."},"cma:127573":{"d":"A portrait of a bearded man wearing a hat, in oil on canvas, about 84 by 69 centimeters and dated about 1655 to 1660. The museum notes that the cloak and hat are painted broadly and thinly, and that the figure's thoughtfulness is comparatively superficial. The picture belongs to the group of unnamed bearded-man portraits and character studies associated with Rembrandt's late period, and the museum regards it as probably a workshop product rather than an autograph work by the master.","i":"Because Amsterdam was religiously tolerant, it drew Jewish settlers of both Sephardic and Ashkenazic origin, and Rembrandt lived among them. For that reason many unnamed late portraits were once thought to show Jewish sitters, and this one was formerly said to portray the philosopher Baruch Spinoza or a Jewish student. The museum has reconsidered those identifications for lack of evidence, so the man should be regarded as anonymous.","c":"The picture borrows the vocabulary of Rembrandt's late manner, with the dark ground, the introspective bearded man and the weight of a heavy hat. But the broad, thin handling of cloak and hat gives it less depth than his autograph portraits, and the thoughtfulness feels more like a pose than a state of mind. It is best valued as evidence of how widely the master's manner was imitated in his studio."},"met:437397":{"d":"A heavy-set man of fifty-four looks straight out of a dim brown space, his body angled and his head turned to face the viewer. He wears a large black beret over grey, curling hair, a thick brown coat with a high collar, and a flash of red at the chest. His brow is knit, his eyes are moist and steady, and his mouth is firm beneath a thin moustache. Light falls from the upper left onto forehead, cheek and bulbous nose, where the paint is built up thickly. The coat, by contrast, is brushed loosely in warm browns, and the hands are out of sight. A signature and the date 1660 appear faintly at the right.","i":"Rembrandt painted himself throughout his career, and by 1660 the portraits had become a record of ageing as much as of identity. The date falls shortly after his bankruptcy of 1656 and the sale of his house and collection. In 1660 he moved to the Rozengracht, where Hendrickje Stoffels and his son Titus ran an art-dealing business that employed him. The usual reading of this picture as a portrait of defeat is a modern projection: the sitter is richly dressed, holds himself firmly and presents himself as a working painter. Seventeenth-century collectors prized self-portraits by famous artists, so the image also had market value.","c":"Among Rembrandt's late self-portraits this one is notable for restraint. There is no fancy costume and no theatrical lighting, only a face painted with extraordinary economy: loaded strokes on forehead and cheek build the flesh while the shadows stay thin and warm. The coat is almost abstract, and the lower half of the canvas is dull by comparison, but that is part of the design, which sends everything to the eyes. Other self-portraits of the 1650s and 1660s are more dramatic, yet few are more convincing about what age looks like in oil paint."},"met:437400":{"d":"A man of middle age looks out from a dim olive-brown background, his head slightly tilted. He wears a broad-brimmed black hat, a black cloak and a wide white falling collar fastened with cords that end in small tassels. Shoulder-length hair frames a lean face with a thin moustache and a steady, guarded expression. Light comes from the upper left, leaving the far side of the face and most of the hat in shadow, so that the pale collar and the face stand out from the dark clothes. One hand, with a cuff of white lace, appears at the lower edge. The brushwork is thin and soft in the face and loose and dark in the cloak.","i":"The sitter is not identified, so the picture reads as a type: a well-off Dutch burgher of the 1650s in sober black with a fashionable falling collar. Rembrandt painted many such portraits for the middle-class patrons who bought his work, and made them with unusual indifference to display; the face is the only bright passage and the clothes dissolve into shadow. The Met dates the picture to about 1655–60, reports it as generally considered to be by Rembrandt, and notes that the hands, body and hat are much worn. It hung in the Lansdowne collection in London before Henry G. Marquand bought it and gave it to the museum in 1890.","c":"Even in its worn state the face is the best thing here: a thin, searching likeness, built from soft transitions and a few touches of warm red at lip and cheek. The rest has suffered. The abraded hands, body and hat leave the black cloak flat, and the picture is less commanding than the great portraits of the 1630s or the late group portraits. It is a quiet, intelligent portrait rather than a showpiece, and its chief interest is how little Rembrandt needed to make an anonymous sitter seem a particular person."},"nga:84":{"d":"A young man in black sits with his body angled to the right while turning to look at the viewer, shown from the hips up. He has green eyes under hooded lids, a wispy mustache and auburn curls falling to his shoulders. Light from the upper left casts the shadow of his nose across a cheekbone. A tall flat white collar with two silvery tassels contrasts with the velvety black of his jacket. One hand rests on his knee, the other is curled against his hip near a patch of crimson, perhaps a cushion. A conical hat lies beyond him, in front of a mottled gray and brown wall.","i":"The title ventures only a possible identification of the sitter as Govaert Flinck, a pupil of Rembrandt in the 1630s who became a successful Amsterdam painter, and the question remains open. The sitter's dress is fashionable but restrained, the tall collar and tassels marking a young man of means or ambition. The pose, turning toward the viewer, was a standard way to give a sitter a lively, attentive air.","c":"A restrained and psychologically alert portrait. The face is carefully modeled in warm light, the black costume is handled in broad, simplified masses, and the crimson patch gives the only note of heat. Rembrandt's late portraits of young men often seem pensive rather than flattering, and this one has that quality in the hooded gaze. The pose and setting are stock, but the sitter's presence is persuasive."},"nga:1199":{"d":"In a space almost entirely shrouded in shadow, light falls on a bearded man kneeling before a woman who cradles an infant, while about a dozen people to the left look on. The group in the center is small, reaching less than halfway up the picture. The kneeling man wears a pale yellow robe and holds an instrument like a stylus near the child, who is wrapped in white cloth. At the left a turbaned man writes in a book slung over his arm. A ladder and a draped canopy rise behind the woman. The paint is applied in soft dabs of brown, yellow, ivory and gray.","i":"The subject is the circumcision of Jesus on the eighth day after his birth, when, according to the Gospel of Luke, he was also given the name Jesus. Christian artists read it as the first shedding of Christ's blood, a foreshadowing of the Passion. The turbans and robes place the scene in an imagined Jewish Temple, and the man recording in a book may allude to the naming, though that is an interpretation rather than a fact.","c":"Rembrandt makes the rite small and almost hidden, so that the viewer has to look through darkness to find it. The loose, dabbed handling dissolves most details, which blurs the story at a distance but rewards close attention, and the golden light has real pathos. It is a late picture of intimate scale and little display, one that depends on atmosphere more than on clear narrative."},"met:437399":{"d":"A portrait of a man holding a magnifying glass, painted in oil on canvas, about 91 by 74 centimeters, in the early 1660s. The museum's record gives no further description of the sitter's clothing, pose or setting, so only the type of picture can be stated. It is the companion, or pendant, of Woman with a Pink in the same museum: the two canvases match closely in size and date, and were evidently painted to hang together as a pair.","i":"A magnifying glass in a portrait is usually read as the mark of a man who looked closely at things, such as a collector, an amateur of art, a physician or a scholar, though the record names no profession. Pendant portraits were commissioned by couples to hang as a pair in the home, and their shared scale and date make that likely here. The sitter is not named in the title, so his identity must remain a matter for the catalogue.","c":"Rembrandt's portraits of the early 1660s show his late, bold manner at its most settled: thickly handled, warm in tone and uninterested in flattery. The magnifying glass is an unusual prop that suggests an individual's interests rather than a type, and that is the kind of detail that distinguishes his portraits from the routine commissions of the day. The record provides no description of the paint, so the judgment is general rather than particular."},"met:437402":{"d":"A portrait of a woman holding a pink, the garden carnation, painted in oil on canvas, about 92 by 75 centimeters, in the early 1660s. The museum's record gives no description of her clothes, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by type. It is the pendant of Man with a Magnifying Glass in the same museum, and the two canvases match closely in size and date, which suggests that they were painted to hang together as a pair.","i":"A pink held in the hand is common in Dutch portraits of women, where it is often read as a flower of love and of marriage, and sometimes of betrothal, though the meaning varies with the picture. Its pairing with a male portrait suggests a married couple, which was the usual reason for pendant portraits. The title does not name the sitter, so her identity and rank cannot be recovered from the record.","c":"As with the companion portrait, the judgment must rest on Rembrandt's manner in the early 1660s, when he painted ordinary sitters with great directness, warm color and thick, unhurried paint. The flower is the sort of modest attribute he handled well, as a note of freshness against the darker tones. The pairing lets the portrait be judged as part of a domestic set, and as a late example of his portraiture of ordinary citizens."},"nga:1208":{"d":"A man seen from about the knees up, wearing a voluminous black cloak and a tall, brimmed hat, looks straight at the viewer with brown eyes. His body angles to the left, and he has a wispy mustache, closed lips, a slight double chin and reddish-brown hair to his shoulders. A wide flat white collar with two tassels breaks the black. One hand grips the wooden end of a chair arm. Behind him the wall is brick red, with a coffee-brown rectangle to the left. Brushwork is visible in the face, and a network of cracks crosses the canvas.","i":"The tall black hat, black cloak and plain white collar are the standard dress of a prosperous Dutch burgher around 1660, when sober black cloth and fine linen carried the message of wealth and respectability. The man looks out directly, and the hand on the chair arm gives him an air of settled authority. The museum title leaves him unnamed, so his profession and rank can be judged only from the clothes.","c":"A late portrait of great economy: the black mass of the cloak, the bright collar and the warm face are all the picture needs. The red wall gives a rich ground without distracting from the man, and the visible brushwork in the face makes the flesh look alive. The pose is conventional, and the sitter is not a dramatic personality, but the steady gaze and the weight of the figure give it real authority."},"nga:83":{"d":"A woman, shown to the thighs against a dark earth-brown ground, looks down and to her left with her head tilted and her lips slightly parted. In her right hand, at the left of the picture, she holds a dagger by its hilt and turns the point toward her chest, while her left hand is held open at the side, palm out. She wears a tawny gold gown with gauzy sleeves, a laced bodice over a cream chemise with a deep V neckline, a gold belt, a pearl necklace and a gold pendant with a teardrop pearl; a large pearl hangs at her ear. Warm light from the upper left falls on her face and chest. Thick, rough paint makes the gold embroidery glitter, and the face is softly blended.","i":"Lucretia's story comes from the Roman historian Livy: a noblewoman raped by Sextus Tarquinius, son of Rome's last king, tells her husband and father, makes them swear to avenge her, and stabs herself. Her death sparked the revolt that ended the monarchy and founded the Roman Republic, so she stood for chastity and civic virtue. Rembrandt painted her twice, in 1664 (this picture) and in 1666 (now in Minneapolis, which shows the wound). It is signed and dated 1664, five years before his death. The costume is contemporary fancy dress rather than Roman, and suggestions that a member of his household posed for her are unproved.","c":"A late work that gains power from restraint. The thickly painted gold embroidery glitters as pure paint, and the head is modelled with great softness, so the face seems to exist in a different, tenderer kind of light than the dress. The spread arms are theatrical, and the left hand's open gesture is not clearly explained, which makes the moment feel held rather than lived. It is quieter than the 1666 version, where the wound is shown, and some prefer it for its dignity. Few pictures of the subject make the decision itself so visible in a face."},"met:437417":{"d":"A small portrait in oil on an oak panel, about 36 by 29 centimeters, of Lieven Willemsz van Coppenol, who was born about 1599 and died in 1671 or later. The museum's record gives no date and no description of the sitter's dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type. It is a bust-scale likeness on a panel of the kind that was often made as a keepsake or as a study rather than as a grand commission.","i":"Coppenol was an Amsterdam writing master, a calligrapher celebrated for the virtuosity of his penmanship. Rembrandt also portrayed him in etching and in a larger painted portrait now in Kassel, which shows that the two men knew each other and that the writer's reputation was considerable. A small panel such as this probably served a more personal purpose than a formal commission, though the record does not say what that was.","c":"A small panel by Rembrandt invites close looking, since the late style depends on the way thick paint models a face. The record gives no description of the surface, so the judgment cannot be specific. Even so, the sitter is an unusually interesting one, a master of the pen drawn by a master of the brush, and the picture is best valued as a rare painted record of a figure better known in Rembrandt's prints."},"cma:143195":{"d":"An elderly man sits with his eyes nearly closed and his hands clasped in prayer over a large book. The figure appears still and introspective, lit dramatically against a dark palette. The museum describes the garment as painted with broad, flat brushwork and notes that the hands' shadows and the uniform treatment of the hair are weak. It is oil on canvas, about 87 by 72 centimeters, dated to the 1660s or later, and is no longer given to Rembrandt but to a follower or a later eighteenth-century imitator.","i":"Late in his life Rembrandt explored religious themes from an intimate perspective, including a possible series of apostles, and his picture of an old man at prayer was widely imitated. The clasped hands over a large book, probably a Bible, suggest personal Protestant piety, with devotion centered on Scripture rather than on saints or altars. The picture appeals to that tradition of private devotion, though the museum does not identify the sitter or the subject.","c":"The museum's reattribution is persuasive, since the telltale weaknesses are the flat handling of the cloth, the unconvincing shadows on the hands and the even treatment of the hair. The picture has the right mood but not the understanding behind it: the design copies the late Rembrandt's stillness without the variety of touch that gives the real thing its depth. It remains a revealing document of his long influence."},"met:437530":{"d":"A very small portrait of a man, painted in oil on a copper plate only about 22 by 15 centimeters and dated 1597, when the artist was about twenty and three years before he left for Italy. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's clothing, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type. The title guesses that the sitter may have been an architect or a geographer, but it does not name him.","i":"Painting on copper gave small portraits a smooth, enamel-like surface and was favored for fine detail. The suggestion that the sitter was an architect or a geographer implies a man of learning and practical skill, the sort of person who moved in the educated circles of Antwerp, though the museum says only \"possibly\". Rubens was then still a young painter in the studio of Otto van Veen, and early portraits of this kind give a glimpse of his beginnings.","c":"An early work by a painter who would become a giant of the Baroque, and not yet recognizably his: the scale is small, the manner is careful and the surface smooth, in the Flemish tradition of precise portraiture. With no description in the record the verdict must be modest. Its value is as evidence of Rubens's training before the Italian years changed his style, rather than as a statement of his mature gifts."},"nga:46159":{"d":"A young woman stands before a heavy red curtain, shown from the knees up, in a white satin gown embroidered with gold knots and a huge lace ruff fanned out like a plate around her head. Her curled brown hair is crowned with an elaborate headdress of pearls, gold and feathers, and a pearl earring rests against the ruff. Her body is angled to the left, but she turns her head to look at the viewer, her cheeks flushed and her red lips in a faint smile. A closed black fan hangs from her hand; her other arm falls straight down. Behind her, at the left, are a brown pilaster and cornice, and at the right dark green marble columns. The satin is painted in long sheets of cream, silver and gold.","i":"Rubens painted her in Genoa in 1606, during his years in Italy as court painter to the Duke of Mantua. According to an inscription on the back of the canvas, she was twenty-two years old and a newlywed from one of Genoa's leading noble families. The picture is formal and ambitious, with a grand architectural backdrop and the dress treated as a display of rank. It is often cited as a model for the portraits Anthony van Dyck painted in Genoa in the 1620s. The inscription also records that the canvas was trimmed at some time, so the original composition was probably wider than it is now.","c":"A showpiece of costume and rank. Rubens handles the satin with a fluid, silvery touch, and the flushed face with its sideways look gives the stiff dress a person to wear it. The great ruff and the sweep of fabric overwhelm the figure, and her arms hang oddly, almost detached from the body, so it is easier to admire the display than to feel the sitter. It lacks the warmth of Rubens's later portraits of his own family, but it set a pattern of elegant, outward-looking portraiture that Van Dyck would turn into a formula."},"nga:71349":{"d":"From a point high in the sky, a young man plunges head first, arms flung up, from a golden chariot as it overturns, wearing only a red sash. Around him four horses, three white and one grey, rear and tumble through the clouds along with eleven women, some nude and some in billowing robes, several with butterfly wings. A shaft of golden light from the upper right crosses the whole scene, and orange flames lick up from the earth in the lower right. At the upper left a faint, pale ring arches through the sky. The colours are smoky greys against warm flesh, red reins and flashes of orange, and the manes and hair are brushed in quick, loose strokes. Some reins have been painted over and show as dark lines.","i":"The subject is from Ovid's Metamorphoses: Phaeton, son of the sun god, begged to drive his father's chariot for a day but could not hold the horses, and Jupiter struck him down with a thunderbolt to save the earth from burning. The National Gallery dates the picture about 1604–05, with probable reworking about 1606–08, in Rubens's Italian years, when he was in his late twenties. The ring across the sky is the zodiac, and the winged women are usually read as the Hours, who in Ovid yoke the horses. Painters of Rubens's day often took the story as a warning against rash ambition, but how he meant it is a matter of interpretation.","c":"A young man's painting in the best sense: crowded with ideas and not yet in control of them. The twisting bodies, the rearing horses and the beam of light make a whirl that never settles. The weaknesses are those of a picture still being worked out: the women are hard to count, the head-first Phaeton is partly lost in the tangle, and the overpainted reins show a design adjusted rather than conceived in one sweep. It reads best as a preview of the energy of the Antwerp altarpieces he would paint a few years later."},"met:437529":{"d":"A small devotional panel in oil on oak, about 66 by 51 centimeters, painted about 1608 to 1609. It shows the Holy Family with Saint Elizabeth and the infant Saint John the Baptist, with a dove. The museum's record gives no further description of the poses or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a domestic-scale religious image intended for private contemplation in a home or chapel rather than for display in a church, painted soon after Rubens returned from Italy to Antwerp.","i":"Elizabeth was the cousin of the Virgin Mary and the mother of John the Baptist, who in the Gospel tradition recognized Christ as the Lamb of God. The meeting of the two children, with their mothers, was a favorite theme of Italian Renaissance painters, and Rubens, who had spent eight years in Italy, knew those models well. The dove traditionally stands for the Holy Spirit, though it can also allude to an offering of doves at the Presentation.","c":"Rubens's early Antwerp devotional pictures combine what he learned from Italian art, the sculptural bodies of Raphael and Michelangelo and the warm color of Titian, with Flemish flesh that is warm and full of blood. The record does not describe this panel, so the verdict is general. The grouping of mother, child and cousin was a familiar formula, and the picture's interest lies in the vitality that Rubens gave to it."},"met:437533":{"d":"Two heads painted on one wood panel, about 70 by 52 centimeters, dated to about 1609. The museum's record gives no description of the faces, their age, expression or direction, so the picture can be described only by its type: a study of heads, as the title says, rather than a finished portrait or narrative scene. The keywords list a man. A study of this kind is a working picture, the sort a painter keeps in the workshop for reference.","i":"Rubens's studio used oil studies of heads from live models as raw material for larger figure compositions, since a good head could be reused in altarpieces and history paintings. The practice shows how carefully he prepared his large commissions and how much he valued the individual face. The record does not say whether these heads were used in a particular finished picture, so any link to a known composition is a matter of speculation.","c":"Studies like this show a painter at his most spontaneous: no subject to serve, only the pleasure of observing a face and setting it down with warm, rapid paint. The record gives no description of the surface, so the judgment must be general. Heads of this kind often have more life than the finished pictures that use them, and are the part of Rubens's work where his powers of observation are plainest."},"nga:46473":{"d":"A man and a woman face left in profile against a dark background, shown from the shoulders up, the woman closer to the viewer and overlapping the man. Her reddish-gold hair is braided across the crown and bound with pearls and a red jewel, with gossamer white fabric at her neck. He has a tanned complexion, a long bumped nose and a burgundy robe. They stand behind a stone ledge, lit from the front so that a halo-like glow shows against the midnight-blue ground. The number 19 is painted at the lower left.","i":"The title names Agrippina the Elder and her husband Germanicus, Roman figures of the early first century. Germanicus was a popular general and the adopted son of the emperor Tiberius, and Agrippina, granddaughter of Augustus, was admired for her loyalty and courage. They were seen as models of Roman marital virtue. The profile pair, the ledge and the dark ground recall the format of an ancient cameo or relief, a kind of antique object that Rubens studied.","c":"A clever, scholarly invention: the stone-like profiles turn a double portrait into a painted imitation of a classical relief, and the light on the dark ground gives the figures a sculptural presence. The paint is smooth and enamel-like, and the faces are idealized rather than individual, so the picture is more a display of learning than an expression of character. For Rubens it is a small, polished exercise of antiquarian taste."},"nga:43721":{"d":"A bearded man with copper-blond hair stands on a stone pedestal and gestures to five men gathered before him. This is Decius Mus, with his muscular right arm raised, in a knee-length toga, form-fitting armor and a scarlet cape. Spears, battle axes, a shield covered with gold lightning bolts and a crested helmet are piled at the pedestal, and an eagle hovers in the upper left. The five men listening carry golden staffs with different symbols, and one holds a flag of scarlet and ginger. The landscape and costumes are loosely painted under a powder-blue sky.","i":"Decius Mus was a Roman consul who, according to Livy, gave his own life in battle against the Latins in 340 BC, vowing himself to the gods of the underworld to secure victory for Rome. The scene shows him addressing his army, and the eagle, a bird of Jupiter, is read as a sign of divine approval. The subject was a classic example of civic virtue, and it was probably made as a design for a tapestry series.","c":"The loose, rapid handling is a pleasure: costumes and background are barely stated, and yet the figures have bulk and urgency. The muscular Roman types owe much to the antique sculpture that Rubens studied in Italy. As an oil sketch it is more lively than a finished tapestry could be, and the rhetoric of Decius's raised arm is typical of his heroic manner. Its weakness, if any, is the stagey clarity of a design meant for weavers."},"nga:50298":{"d":"A very large canvas, about 224 by 330 centimeters, dated about 1614 to 1616, showing the prophet Daniel in the den of lions. The museum's record gives no further description, so the picture can be described only by subject and kind: a monumental Old Testament scene in a wide format, with a human figure in the midst of powerful wild animals. It is a picture made to be seen at a distance in a large room, not held close like a devotional panel.","i":"In the Book of Daniel the prophet was thrown to the lions for praying to his God against the king's decree, and was found unharmed in the morning because, as the text says, he trusted in God. Christians read the story as a type of the Resurrection, and as a model of faith under persecution. A letter of 1618 in which Rubens describes a Daniel painted from life among many lions is generally taken to refer to this canvas.","c":"Rubens was the great painter of animals in motion, and the lions here are the reason to look: their weight, muscle and individual temperaments make the danger physical. Daniel himself is a more classical, less startling figure, so the animals can steal the scene. It is a vigorous and ambitious work of his Antwerp years, strongest as a study of nature, with the religious meaning carried more by the story than by feeling."},"nga:12207":{"d":"A bearded man in profile, seen from the chest up and facing left with downcast eyes. He has chestnut hair and beard, thick brows and a slightly bumped nose, with pink touching the nose and cheeks. His robe is vibrant rose over the shoulders and scarlet over the arms, divided by a band of gold fringe. In one hand he holds a lidded vessel with bosses around the rim, and the other hand tips the lid, revealing rounded dark brown objects inside. Light comes from the upper left against a mottled olive green and ginger background.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew speaks of wise men from the East who brought gifts to the infant Christ, and tradition made them three kings, named Caspar, Melchior and Balthasar, who offered gold, frankincense and myrrh. The vessel here is one of those gifts. Rubens painted the subject many times, and a head study of this sort was probably made as preparation for a larger Adoration of the Magi. The title records the king's name, Melchior, and calls him the Assyrian.","c":"A brilliant piece of observation, in which the vivid rose and scarlet of the robe, the warm flush of the skin and the gleam of the vessel are rendered with a few fluent strokes. The pose is quiet but the paint is not, and the face has a convincing gravity. It is an example of Rubens's habit of preparing grand compositions from living models, and the study is often more spontaneous than the altarpiece for which it served."},"cma:135661":{"d":"Diana, the Roman goddess of the hunt, strides forward at the head of a group of full-bodied nymphs, a crescent on her forehead marking her as the moon goddess. At the left a nymph fends off a lustful satyr. Two dogs wear collars marked with the moon and stars, showing they are Diana's companions. Rubens brings the large figures right to the front of the picture, and Diana steps forward so that the space between viewer and subject seems active. It is an oil on canvas, about 216 by 179 centimeters, dated about 1615.","i":"Diana lived apart from men with her nymphs, and often stood for unattainable beauty and chastity. The satyr's attack on the nymph refers to a struggle between vice and virtue, so the picture combines a sensual display of female bodies with a moral undertone. The museum notes that the nymph at the right has the features of Isabella Brant, Rubens's wife, and can be compared with his portrait of her in the same collection.","c":"Rubens makes chastity look luxuriant. The nymphs' ample, warm-toned flesh, the strong forward movement of the goddess and the struggle at the left make an animated, crowded surface, and the moral lesson sits easily beside the pleasure of looking. Not every figure is equally refined, and the satyr is conventional, but the picture has the vitality and confident arrangement of his best Antwerp mythologies."},"met:437525":{"d":"A banquet from classical mythology, painted in oil on wood, about 108 by 164 centimeters, around 1615. The museum's record gives no description of the figures, but its keywords list the hero Theseus, male and female nudes, food and dining, and the setting is a dining room. The picture can be described only by its type: a crowded, convivial mythological table scene in a wide format, in which nude bodies and a feast are the main interest.","i":"The subject comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses. On his way home Theseus and his companions were halted by the flooded river Achelous, whose god invited them to dine in his cave and entertained them with stories of transformation. The meal brings together the heroes' travel, the river god's hospitality and the abundance of nature. Learned patrons enjoyed such pictures because they allowed a scholarly story to be told with sensuous, lively figures.","c":"A subject that suited Rubens perfectly, since it let him combine learned storytelling with the pleasures of food and flesh. The record offers no description of the composition, so a fuller verdict is not possible. Pictures of this kind show his skill at organizing a crowded group of bodies into a single rhythmic movement, and are among his most genial inventions, though they are less ambitious than his great altarpieces."},"met:437523":{"d":"A mythological hunting scene in oil on wood, about 133 by 107 centimeters, dated about 1616. Its keywords list a wild boar, a dog, a man and a woman, who are named in the title as Meleager and Atalanta. The museum's record gives no further description of their poses or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a pair of heroic figures with a slain or hunted boar, a form of mythological picture that allowed Rubens to paint athletic bodies, animals and landscape together.","i":"According to Ovid, the Calydonian boar hunt brought together heroes from across Greece. Atalanta, the huntress, drew first blood, and Meleager, who killed the boar, gave her the trophy because he loved her. That gift led to a quarrel with his uncles and ultimately to his death, so the story combines hunting glory with the dangers of passion. The couple's moment is usually read as the high point of that story.","c":"The theme gave Rubens a chance to show virile and female athletic bodies side by side, and the animals are the kind of subject at which he excelled. Without a description in the record the judgment must be general. Pictures of this kind combine hunting energy with erotic charge, and their weakness is occasional theatricality, but the powerful forms and warm color are typical of his mature mythological manner."},"met:437536":{"d":"A very large hunting picture in oil on canvas, about 245 by 376 centimeters, dated about 1616. The museum's keywords list men and women on horseback, hounds, a gray wolf and a fox, and the subject is the hunt for wolves and foxes. The record gives no further description of the composition, so the picture can be described only by its type: an animal and hunting scene on a grand scale, intended for a princely hall rather than for a small room.","i":"Hunting was the favorite pastime of European courts, and large hunt pictures proclaimed the patron's wealth and his command of land and animals. Rubens painted several such scenes in these years, and they gave him the chance to show galloping horses, struggling beasts and dramatic bodies together. The presence of women among the riders reflects the fact that courtly hunts were social events, and hunting pictures could also serve as portraits of the participants.","c":"A hunt is a gift for a painter of energy, and Rubens uses it to full effect: the swirl of horses, hounds and prey makes a rhythmic, almost musical design. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is based on the type, and on the artist's reputation for animal painting. At this scale the picture's strength is its movement, while its subject is conventional and its characters stay types rather than individuals."},"cma:125247":{"d":"A woman looks out at the viewer with a faint, wry smile and a slightly raised eyebrow, her head tilted a little. Her reddish-brown hair is pulled up and held by a jewelled band, and a pearl drop hangs from her ear. She wears a black mantle over a dark dress edged with black lace and a fine white collar, and gold chains cross her chest. One hand rests lightly on her breast. The background is a warm mottled brown, brushed in loosely. The face is modelled in pink, cream and soft grey with fluid, confident strokes, while the hand and clothing are painted more broadly. It is a small oil painting on wood.","i":"Isabella Brant was Rubens's first wife; they married in 1609, and she died in 1626 at the age of 35, according to Cleveland. The museum says he painted her many times in many different ways, and that she modelled for a nymph in another Rubens in the museum. This intimate portrait, painted at least five years before her death, suggests their closeness through its warm palette, luscious brushwork and her lively expression. Cleveland adds that Rubens painted the face first and added the hands later. After her death he wrote of having lost an excellent companion.","c":"The attraction is candor. Rubens does not flatter the nose or the slightly crooked smile, and the result is a person rather than a type, with a lightness unusual in seventeenth-century portraits of wives. The face is painted with great fluency, and the loose background lets it glow. The hand, added later, is more routine and less convincing. But a portrait this small and private was never meant for show, and its sketch-like freedom is part of its honesty. It says more about the marriage than a formal likeness would."},"nga:45640":{"d":"A tapestry is held up by winged putti against an architectural frame of gray and gold columns, and it shows two ranks of men, women and children gathered around two central figures meeting at its center. At the left Abraham, in armor and a crimson cape, leans toward the white-bearded Melchizedek, who wears a wreath over a red cap and a heavy golden cloak with a white fur collar. Bread, large urns and a brown horse appear around them. A low green hill and pale sky show between the groups.","i":"In Genesis, Melchizedek, priest and king of Salem, met Abraham after his victory and brought out bread and wine and blessed him. Christian writers read the bread and wine as a prefiguration of the Eucharist, so the scene belongs to the group of Old Testament subjects that foretell the sacrament. The picture of a tapestry within a picture is probably a design for a woven hanging in Rubens's Triumph of the Eucharist series.","c":"Ingenious and lively, the sketch condenses a large procession into a compact picture, with the putti and the curling tapestry edge adding a playful illusion of depth. The color is rich, the figures muscular and the rhythm strong, though the dense crowd of similar types can feel crammed. Rubens designed such cartoons with extraordinary speed, and the sketch is more spontaneous than any woven version could be."},"met:437531":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted in oil on wood, about 77 by 60 centimeters, dated about 1625 to 1627. The title gives the sitter as probably Susanna Lunden, born Susanna Fourment (1599 to 1628), so she would have been in her late twenties. The museum's record gives no description of her costume, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length or bust portrait of a woman of means by the leading painter of Antwerp.","i":"Susanna Fourment belonged to an Antwerp merchant family, and her younger sister Helena became Rubens's second wife in 1630. Rubens painted members of the family more than once, and the London portrait known as the Straw Hat, long identified as Susanna, is among his most admired likenesses of women. The word \"probably\" in the title shows that the identification of the sitter is not certain, although the family connection makes a close tie between artist and sitter likely.","c":"Rubens's portraits of women in his own circle are among his most informal and warm: the flesh is full of blood, the expressions are relaxed and there is little of the stiff formality of a court portrait. The record provides no description of this panel, so the verdict is based on that general character. The pleasure of such pictures lies in the sitter's presence, and the weakness, if any, is a certain sameness of type."},"nga:99280":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 45 by 66 centimeters, painted about 1630 in oil on wood. The title names the Old Testament meeting of David and Abigail, but the museum's record gives no description of the composition. The picture can be described only by subject and format: a narrative scene in a wide, compact panel, of a kind Rubens used for preparatory sketches or for small finished pictures of biblical episodes that could be hung in a private room.","i":"In the First Book of Samuel, David set out with armed men to punish the rich Nabal, who had refused him supplies. Nabal's wife Abigail rode out to meet him with gifts of food and pleaded for her husband, and David relented. She is remembered as a figure of wisdom and peacemaking, and the scene is usually read as the triumph of reason over wrath. David later married her after Nabal's death.","c":"The record gives no description, and the verdict must be modest. The subject suited Rubens: a procession of armed men, laden animals and a woman who kneels to plead gave him the chance to arrange a rhythmic group in a landscape. A panel of this size usually has the freshness of a sketch, and even if it is a lesser work it shows how readily he found drama in biblical narrative."},"met:437527":{"d":"A small vertical panel in oil on wood, about 71 by 48 centimeters, painted about 1630 to 1632. The museum's record gives no description of the arrangement, but its keywords name Christ, an angel, Saint Paul, Melchizedek and Elijah, together with a skeleton and a snake. The picture can be described only by its type: a crowded theological allegory on a small support, the scale and format of an oil sketch made to plan a larger work in which saints, prophets and the figures of death and sin appear together.","i":"The figures named in the keywords are the usual witnesses of Eucharistic theology. Melchizedek offered bread and wine, Elijah was fed by an angel in the desert, and Paul taught the meaning of the Lord's Supper. The skeleton and snake are the usual emblems of death and sin, and the sacrament is the power that overcomes them. This is how the picture is usually read, as a Counter-Reformation statement of faith in the sacrament.","c":"Rubens was one of the great inventors of allegory, able to combine many learned references in a single energetic design. The record gives no description, so the verdict has to be general: a panel of this kind is more interesting for its quick, vivid organization of a complex idea than for finish. Such sketches show the engineer at work, and they are often livelier than the large pictures made from them."},"met:437524":{"d":"A small vertical panel in oil on wood, about 50 by 41 centimeters, painted about 1632 to 1633, showing the Coronation of the Virgin. The museum's keywords list Christ, the Virgin Mary, God the Father and a man, but the record gives no description of the arrangement. The picture can be described only by its type: its small size and wood support are typical of the oil sketches Rubens made to plan altarpieces and other large commissions, before the final composition was fixed.","i":"In Catholic tradition the Virgin is taken up to heaven and crowned Queen of Heaven by her Son, often with God the Father and the Holy Spirit present. The subject was popular in the Counter-Reformation, which stressed the honor due to Mary and her role as intercessor. The record does not say which patron or church the design was made for, so no further context can be given with confidence.","c":"The record offers no description of the handling, and the verdict can only be general. The theme was a staple of Baroque art, and the best treatments combine heavenly splendor with a feeling of warm human tenderness. Rubens, with his gift for large floating groups of figures and a ready sense of light, was well suited to it, and oil sketches of this kind usually have more vitality than the formal altarpieces they prepare."},"met:437534":{"d":"A small horizontal panel in oil on wood, about 50 by 84 centimeters, painted about 1630, showing the triumph of King Henry IV of France. The museum's keywords list a king, men and women, horses and chariots, but the record gives no further description. The picture can be described only by its type: a triumphal procession in the manner of ancient Rome, with a royal figure in a chariot, and probably an oil sketch for a larger painting rather than a finished cabinet picture.","i":"Rubens had painted the great cycle glorifying Marie de' Medici for the Luxembourg Palace, and a second cycle celebrating her late husband Henry IV was planned afterward. The Triumph probably belongs to that unfinished project, in which the murdered king was shown as a Roman conqueror. Such images used classical imagery to turn a recent monarch into a heroic figure and to support his dynasty.","c":"Rubens was the master of the triumphal procession, and the surging movement of horses and figures suits an oil sketch, where the paint can be fast and the design can be tested. The record gives no description, so the judgment is of the type. Sketches for projects that were never completed have a particular interest, because they show the ideas before diplomacy, politics or money changed them."},"met:437528":{"d":"A large wide canvas, about 177 by 210 centimeters, painted in the early or mid-1630s. The museum's keywords list the Holy Family, Saint Anne, Saint Francis, the infant Saint John the Baptist and sheep. The record gives no description of the poses, so the picture can be described only by its type: a devotional group in which saints from different times gather around the Christ Child in a single domestic scene, with the sheep alluding to the lamb of sacrifice and to the Baptist.","i":"Saint Anne was the mother of the Virgin, and the infant John the Baptist, who would later proclaim Christ as the Lamb of God, is regularly shown with a lamb. Saint Francis, who lived over a thousand years later, appears by the logic of devotion rather than history, which is typical of the Baroque. Together they make a gathering across time, bringing the saints into the family circle to invite the viewer to share it.","c":"In his last decade Rubens painted tender, easy family groups with a looser touch and warmer, more luminous flesh than in his early hard-edged pictures. The record gives no description, so the judgment is general. The risk of such pictures is sweetness, with a crowd of saints and a lamb straying into sentiment, but the best of them keep a robust, human warmth, and the large scale gives the group a calm authority."},"met:437535":{"d":"A large canvas in oil, about 198 by 243 centimeters, probably painted in the mid-1630s. The museum's keywords list Venus, Adonis, Cupid, a nude figure and a dog. The record gives no further description, so the picture can be described only by its type: a mythological love scene on a grand scale, with the goddess of love and a young hunter, a little god of love and a hound, painted for a patron who wished to see an Ovidian story in a sensuous, lifelike form.","i":"In Ovid's Metamorphoses the goddess Venus fell in love with the mortal Adonis, and warned him against the dangers of the hunt. He ignored her and was killed by a boar, and the goddess made the anemone grow from his blood. The picture's theme is usually the parting, in which love foresees loss. Rubens knew Titian's Venus and Adonis, which shaped later treatments of the subject, and he studied Titian's work in Spain.","c":"The late Rubens made the female nude warmer and more tender, and the theme of a foreboding farewell gave him a subject that was lyrical as well as erotic. The record offers no description of the picture, so the judgment must be general. His rosy modeling, soft touch and sense of tragic charm make such pictures among the most appealing of his last years, though the poses are less athletic than those of his earlier mythologies."},"cma:95264":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 74 by 106 centimeters, which the museum regards as likely a copy after Rubens's oil sketch for the Triumph of the Eucharist tapestry series. The museum's record gives no description of the composition, so the picture can be described only by its title and type: an allegorical triumph in which the Church appears in procession, painted in oil on wood and dated after 1628. The original sketch is in the Prado in Madrid.","i":"Isabella, daughter of King Philip II of Spain, commissioned the tapestry series for a royal monastery in Madrid, where the finished tapestries still hang. The Triumph of the Eucharist series celebrated the sacrament in the language of a Roman triumph, a Counter-Reformation statement that the Church and the Eucharist had overcome heresy and error. Oil sketches like this were the means by which such designs were approved before being woven.","c":"The museum's candid label is the main point: this is a copy, not the sketch, and a copy cannot match the speed and sureness of Rubens's own hand. It is still useful as a record of a composition that exists in its full glory only in the tapestries and in the Prado sketch. Its weaknesses are the usual ones of a copyist, with a certain flatness where the original has bounce."},"met:437526":{"d":"A forest at dawn with a deer hunt, painted in oil on wood, about 62 by 90 centimeters, around 1635. The museum's keywords name dawn, forest, hunting and deer, but the record gives no further description of the composition. The picture can be described only by its type: a landscape in a wide format in which the early light and a hunting party in the woods are the subject, rather than a portrait of a particular estate or a view of a famous place.","i":"Hunting was a courtly pastime and a favorite subject for Rubens, and a deer hunt in a forest invites a poetic reading of the hour and the season. Landscape was a late interest of his: most of his independent landscapes belong to the 1630s, when he had bought a country house and spent long periods away from Antwerp. Such pictures could serve as pleasures of the eye for a patron or, as here, a meditation on nature at dawn.","c":"Rubens's late landscapes are among the freest of his works, with fluid paint, luminous skies and a joyful sense of space. The record gives no description of this picture, so the verdict must be general. A forest at dawn is a theme that rewards his gift for atmosphere and for the movement of figures and animals, and such pictures are generally valued for the poetry that the old master allowed himself away from his official commissions."},"met:437532":{"d":"A family stands in a garden. Rubens, in a broad black hat, black doublet and violet cloak, stands behind them with one hand on the base of a marble statue and a leather strap across his chest. His wife Helena, in a black velvet gown with a lace collar and a hanging fan of coloured feathers, leans toward him, their hands joined. Between them their small son Frans, in a cream gown with a blue sash and cap, looks up and reaches for his father; a leading string held by Helena keeps him close. Behind rise roses on a trellis and a stone fountain on which a scarlet macaw perches. Oil on wood, about 204 by 158 centimetres, a very large panel.","i":"The Met dates the picture about 1635 and describes the setting as an idealised version of the garden of the mansion Rubens built in his native Antwerp. Rubens was then about fifty-eight, Helena about twenty-one and Frans about two. The strap across his chest alludes to his right, as a nobleman, to carry a sword, and the boy's formal clothes mirror his father's. The Met points to the contrast between her plump, pearly young hand and his ruddy, weathered one. It is a rare image of the painter with his family. The Nazis seized it from Baron Édouard de Rothschild in Paris, and it was restituted to him in 1946.","c":"Rubens gives himself a modest supporting role and lets the picture be about warmth. The sweep of Helena's black velvet, the clasp of their hands and the toddler's eager reach make a believable family moment, painted with the fluid, feathery brushwork of his last years. The weaknesses are the garden props, which look assembled rather than seen, and an idealising that smooths Helena into the pearly type of his late nudes. Beside the formal grandeur of his state portraits this is intimate and loose, and among the most human pictures he painted."},"cma:151517":{"d":"A small horizontal oil sketch on wood, about 26 by 54 centimeters, from about 1639. A bear attacks a hunter, and a companion comes to his aid, while the other men work to keep off a second angry bear. The museum notes that Rubens suggests the energy and drama of a ferocious hunt with a few quick brushstrokes. It is one of seven surviving studies out of an original eighteen made for a series of paintings for the halls of the Alcázar Palace in Madrid.","i":"In June 1639 Philip IV of Spain received a letter from his younger brother Ferdinand explaining that Rubens had completed all the sketches for the series. The sketch was made in the last year of the painter's life, and the paintings were never completed. Hunting scenes suited a royal palace, and combined the pleasures of royal sport with the drama of danger, so the series would have celebrated the Spanish court's favorite pastime.","c":"The sketch shows Rubens's unfailing power to turn a chaotic struggle into a clear design, with writhing bodies and animals compacted in a small panel and the paint reduced to bare essentials. Few finished hunt pictures could be so alive. Its great interest is as one of the last works of the painter, whose ambitions at the end of his life were still large and his hand was still quick."},"nga:52457":{"d":"A seated woman holds an infant on her lap while three children crowd at her knees, on a terrace overlooking green hills and water. She wears a frilly silvery cap and shawl and a fern-green skirt patterned with coral flowers, and gazes down at the children. The boy looks up at her, while the two girls look directly out. The chair is upholstered in scarlet, with a large slate-blue bird on its back, and gold-edged red drapery hangs between stone columns. At the right a column is formed of two creatures with pink female heads and scaly, snake-like legs.","i":"The title identifies the sitter as Deborah Kip, wife of Sir Balthasar Gerbier, the Dutch-born courtier, painter and diplomat in English service. The date of 1629 to 1630 coincides with Rubens's diplomatic mission to England, where Gerbier served at court. The columns, drapery and terrace are borrowed from aristocratic portraiture and lend an ambitious family a grand setting, while the children show the household's prosperity and continuity. The picture was reworked, probably in the mid-1640s.","c":"A warm and lively family group, one of Rubens's most successful portraits of a household that was not his own. The children's individual expressions, the mother's tender glance and the glowing greens and reds are all handled with confidence, and the grand setting does not overwhelm the family. The later reworking, reportedly involving Jacob Jordaens, may account for some uneven passages, but the whole retains a convincing feeling of life."},"met:11864":{"d":"A man, a woman and a boat on a river under trees appear in this oil on canvas, about 51 by 72 cm, dated 1889. The long title is a line from Longfellow's poem The Song of Hiawatha, and the picture was apparently painted as one of a series of illustrations for an 1890 edition of the poem. Remington, best known for his pictures of the American West, applied his skill with figures and wilderness to a literary subject.","i":"Longfellow's 1855 poem, a verse epic loosely based on Ojibwe legends, was enormously popular in the nineteenth century and was often illustrated. Such editions presented Native American subjects to white readers in a romantic light, as noble figures belonging to a vanishing wilderness, and the pictures reflect the period's ideas more than Native life. The long title is a line of the verse itself, so the picture was meant to be read alongside the text.","c":"Remington's gifts are movement and a sense of wilderness, and an illustration gives him a chance to use both. This picture is less typical than his cavalry and cowboy scenes, and it carries the staginess of Longfellow's romanticism. The strength is its fluency; the weakness is that the Hiawatha world is invented by a poet and imagined by a painter from the outside. As an episode in Remington's career it is interesting, though not among his best."},"nga:35084":{"d":"A large portrait in oil on canvas, about 167 by 133 centimeters, dated 1788 to 1789 and altered at a later, unknown date. The title gives the sitter's name and military rank, Captain Patrick Miller, but the museum's record gives no description of his costume, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type. It is a ceremonious single-figure portrait from the first years of Raeburn's independent practice in Edinburgh, after his return from Italy.","i":"A rank in the title shows how a sitter wished to be known, since a captain's commission was a mark of public standing as well as a profession, and portraits often recorded it. The date places the picture in the years when Raeburn was establishing himself as the portraitist of the Edinburgh professional and landed classes. The later alteration noted by the museum means that the picture as it now stands may differ from the one first delivered.","c":"Raeburn painted directly on the canvas without preliminary drawing, a method that gave his best portraits a bold, fresh authority. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict has to be general. A work of 1788 to 1789 belongs to his early career, when he was assimilating what he had seen in Rome and London, and it is usually valued for its directness rather than for the polish of his later pictures."},"nga:1195":{"d":"A large single-figure portrait of David Anderson, painted in oil on canvas, about 153 by 108 centimeters, and dated 1790. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's age, costume, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a single-figure commission of the kind that Raeburn painted for the professional and landed families of Edinburgh at the start of his independent career, giving the sitter room and dignity on a canvas of considerable size.","i":"Few details of the sitter are given in the record, which names him but supplies no profession or rank. Portraits of this scale were made to hang in a family house and to declare the sitter's standing in the society of Edinburgh in the later eighteenth century, when the city's lawyers, merchants, doctors and landowners formed a confident and prosperous class. Raeburn became their favorite painter.","c":"Raeburn's style of the 1790s is direct and unfussy: a strong light on the face, broad strokes of the brush and no attempt to prettify. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the judgment must be general. It is the kind of portrait that persuades by the sitter's plain presence rather than by elaborate accessories, and its qualities are those that made Raeburn a more candid painter than most of his London contemporaries."},"nga:108":{"d":"A young woman in a white dress sits facing the viewer, shown from the hips up against a golden landscape. Her body angles to the left, and she looks steadily from the corners of large slate-blue eyes with touches of hazel. Her ash-brown hair is pulled loosely back and falls in curls around a heart-shaped face. A translucent white ruffle flares at her neckline, and the bodice wraps to a bow at the back. The landscape is loosely painted in gold and mustard, with smoky blue and purple mountains and bands of plum-purple cloud above.","i":"The plain white muslin dress and loosely dressed hair were the fashion of the 1790s, when simplicity was admired as the mark of natural sensibility. The landscape background, with its sunset-like glow, belongs to the same taste, which wished to place a young woman in nature rather than in a formal interior. The title calls her Miss Eleanor Urquhart, which marks her as unmarried, and the picture is usually read as a portrait of youth.","c":"A fresh and direct portrait. The white dress is painted in a few unfussy strokes, the face is steady and unflattering in the best sense, and the glowing sky behind makes a handsome foil. The landscape is almost abstract in its broad washes of color, and it shows Raeburn's confident economy at its best. If there is a weakness it is the conventional pose, but the sitter's gaze keeps it alive."},"nga:32580":{"d":"A group portrait in oil on canvas, about 102 by 120 centimeters, painted between about 1790 and 1795. Its title names three sitters, John Johnstone, Betty Johnstone and Miss Wedderburn, and the picture is classed as a family portrait. The museum's record gives no description of their poses, costume or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a wide, three-figure arrangement of the kind that required the painter to give each sitter a place, a pose and a share of attention.","i":"Group portraits were more expensive and complicated than single ones, and a family or a circle of friends usually commissioned one to record their bonds. The record does not say how the three sitters were related, so their connection should not be assumed. A picture of this kind was intended to hang in a family house and to fix a particular moment in the shared life of the group, as a memorial to affection as well as a likeness.","c":"The group portrait was a severe test of a painter's ability to balance several people and to keep each of them alive, and Raeburn, who generally preferred single figures, met it by letting the sitters be informal and absorbed in one another. The record gives no description, so the judgment is general. At this scale and with this naturalness the picture is among the more attractive of his group portraits."},"met:437361":{"d":"A portrait of a woman painted in oil on canvas, about 123 by 104 centimeters, dated about 1794. The title names her as Mrs. Richard Alexander Oswald, born Louisa Johnston, with a possible birth about 1760 and a death in 1797. The museum's record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a three-quarter-length portrait of a married woman of landed society, painted by the leading portraitist in Scotland.","i":"The title records her by her husband's name, which was the usual way of naming a married woman at the time, and gives her death in 1797, only a few years after the portrait was painted. She would have been in her early thirties when she sat, and the picture may have been kept by her family as a likeness and a memorial. The record gives no information about the commission.","c":"Raeburn's portraits of women in the 1790s are less polished than those of his London rivals, but they are more candid and have a sturdy truthfulness, with strong light, a firm structure and fluent brushwork. The record offers no description of this canvas, so the verdict must be general. It is a good example of his skill with an ordinary sitter, who is presented without idealization and with a sense of character."},"nga:110":{"d":"An older man sits in a rustic wooden chair and dangles a pocket watch for a small child who stands at his knees. Light from the upper left leaves his face partly in shadow while it pours onto the child's upturned face. He looks out at the viewer under heavy brows, with gray hair tied back, in a black coat with a high collar. The boy, in a white garment with a squared neckline, reaches for the watch's fob. The background is loosely painted in streaks of orange, gold and olive green, suggesting trees against a yellow sky.","i":"The title names John Tait and his grandson, so the picture is a record of two generations. The watch, a valuable and fashionable object, is the point of contact between the old man and the child, and it is also a quiet reminder of the passing of time. The dates given by the museum, about 1793 with additions about 1800, show that the picture was worked on over several years, though the record does not say what was added.","c":"One of Raeburn's most engaging pictures. The contrast of the old man's shadowed, lined face and the child's bright, rounded one is the whole drama, and it is told without sentiment or fuss. The loose handling of the chair, clothing and background gives the group freshness, while the watch supplies a simple story. If it risks sweetness, the old man's guarded, watchful gaze keeps the picture honest."},"nga:52274":{"d":"A portrait of a married woman, in oil on canvas, about 97 by 77 centimeters, dated about 1790 to 1800. The title names her only as Mrs. George Hill, and the museum's record gives no description of her dress, pose, setting or background. The picture can be described only by its type: a half-length or three-quarter-length portrait of a woman of the Edinburgh professional or landed classes, painted in the years when Raeburn had become the leading portrait painter in Scotland.","i":"In the title the sitter has no first name, since a married woman was habitually identified by her husband's, and the record gives no further information about who George Hill was. Such portraits were made for the sitter's family and were often paired with a likeness of the husband. The wide dating of the picture, a full decade, suggests that the museum has not been able to fix it more closely.","c":"Raeburn's women's portraits of this decade combine broad, confident handling with a plain and honest observation of the face, and are usually more successful than his formal costume pieces. With no description in the record, any judgment of this particular canvas has to be general. It stands as a representative example of his Edinburgh portrait practice, without a particular claim to be remembered among his best."},"met:437363":{"d":"A portrait of William Forsyth (1749 to 1814), painted in oil on canvas, about 76 by 63 centimeters, about 1800. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-length or half-length portrait on a modest canvas, a format for sitters who wanted a good likeness without the expense of a large canvas.","i":"The record gives the sitter's name and dates but no profession, rank or history, so nothing more can be said with confidence about who he was. Portraits of this size were usually commissioned by the sitter or his family, to hang in the house or to be given to relatives, and were valued above all as a faithful likeness of a particular man.","c":"Raeburn's smaller portraits of men of this date are among his most direct, with the face strongly lit and the dark coat reduced to broad masses. The record offers no description of this canvas, so the judgment must be general. A portrait of this kind is more valuable for its candor than for any display, and it is a fair example of the unflattering, vigorous manner in which he painted the professional men of Scotland."},"met:437365":{"d":"A portrait of William Robertson (1753 to 1835), Lord Robertson, painted in oil on canvas, about 126 by 100 centimeters, and dated 1805. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a three-quarter-length portrait of a senior figure in the law, made for a sitter whose judicial title is part of his name. It is a dated example of Raeburn's work for the legal profession of Edinburgh.","i":"The title Lord Robertson is the style of a judge of the Court of Session, the highest civil court in Scotland, whose members took such titles on appointment. A portrait of this kind recorded a sitter's rank and authority and was commissioned to be hung in a family house or in a place of public honor. The date of 1805 may mark his elevation, though the record does not say that.","c":"The Edinburgh judges were among Raeburn's best subjects, since their strong, experienced faces suited his method of painting directly with a broad, fully loaded brush. The record gives no description of this portrait, so the verdict must be general. The gravity, plain structure and fine head that mark his best portraits of the legal profession are what one can reasonably expect here, and the picture is a fair representative of his mature style."},"met:437354":{"d":"A tall group portrait in oil on canvas, about 239 by 153 centimeters, painted about 1808 to 1809. Its title and keywords name three Drummond children, Margaret, Henry Dundas and George, and a horse also appears. The museum's record gives no further description of their poses, dress or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a full-length portrait of young children from a prosperous family, with a horse as a mark of country life and privilege. It is a companion in date and size to the portrait of their father in the same museum.","i":"Full-length children's portraits with animals were a favorite form in British art around 1800, showing the family's wealth, the children's health and the pleasures of country life. The horse and the large scale were meant to impress, and the picture was probably commissioned by the father as a record of the family's heirs. The companion portrait of George Harley Drummond, dated the same years, shows that Raeburn painted the family as a group of commissions.","c":"Raeburn's mature portraits of children are among his freshest, combining the large format and horse of the grand manner with his own bold, loaded brushwork and a candid sense of character. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict must be general. At this size the picture is a major statement of his maturity in Edinburgh, where his reputation stood highest."},"met:437355":{"d":"A full-length portrait of a man with a horse, painted in oil on canvas and dated about 1808 to 1809. Its height of about 239 centimeters and width of about 147 centimeters match the standard whole-length canvas of British portraiture. The title names the sitter as George Harley Drummond (1783 to 1855), who would have been about twenty-five. The museum's record gives no further description of his dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type.","i":"The whole-length portrait with a horse showed a gentleman as a landowner and horseman, and this was the most expensive and ambitious form of portrait a family could order, a statement of wealth and standing. The picture is a companion in date and size to Raeburn's portrait of the Drummond children in the same museum, which suggests that a single patron ordered a set of family portraits at one time.","c":"Raeburn took the English tradition of the gentleman with his horse, which Gainsborough, Reynolds and Lawrence had perfected, and made it plainer and more direct, with the broad handling that was his own. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. A whole-length of this size was a real test for a painter based in Edinburgh, and it shows that he could meet the demands of a grand commission."},"nga:109":{"d":"A portrait of Colonel Francis James Scott, painted in oil on canvas, about 128 by 102 centimeters. The museum gives the dates 1796 and 1811, which probably means that the picture was begun in the 1790s and finished or reworked years later. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's uniform, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a military portrait of a colonel, on a canvas of three-quarter-length size, painted by the leading portraitist in Scotland.","i":"In the years of war with France many Scottish gentlemen held commissions in volunteer and fencible regiments raised against invasion, and a colonel's portrait recorded both the sitter's rank and his public spirit. The long gap in the dating may reflect a change in the sitter's circumstances, though the record does not say so. Without a description, the sitter's costume and attributes cannot be read, and the portrait should be taken as a record of a named officer.","c":"Raeburn's military portraits succeed when he lets a strong light and a plain, upright figure do the work, and fail when uniform gets in the way of character. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict has to be general. The long span of dates suggests a picture of unusual history, and it is best valued as a document of a Scottish officer painted by the artist who recorded the whole of his society."},"nga:995":{"d":"Two young boys sit side by side on boulders, with a cloudy sky and dark tree branches above them. They have round, flushed cheeks, short light brown hair and brown eyes, and wear matching russet-brown suits with rows of gold buttons, white ruffled collars, white stockings and black laced shoes. The boy on the right looks out with his hand on his brother's shoulder. The other boy looks up at him, one arm across his lap, holding a plum-purple and blue hat and a stick with a rope. Another hat and a few fallen leaves lie at their feet.","i":"A double portrait of brothers was a way to show the family's continuity, and the hand on the shoulder and the shared gaze emphasize their bond. The matching suits and the sticks and hats suggest a country childhood of outdoor play, while the fallen leaves give an autumn setting. The picture is dated probably about 1811, and the title identifies the sitters only as the Binning children, so nothing more about them is recorded here.","c":"A genuinely charming double portrait, and more naturalistic than most children's portraits of its time. The pose, with one boy leaning on the other, makes a single compact shape, and the loosely painted trees and sky in yellow, green and slate blue give a soft setting. The two faces are so alike that the picture's interest lies in the bond rather than in two distinct characters, but the warmth and freshness are convincing."},"met:437356":{"d":"A portrait of Alexander Maconochie (1777 to 1861) of Meadowbank, painted in oil on canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters. The museum's record gives no date and no description of the sitter's dress, pose or setting, though since Raeburn died in 1823 it must have been painted before then. The picture can be described only by its type: a half-length or bust portrait on a modest canvas, of a man whose family held the title of Lord Meadowbank in the Scottish courts.","i":"The name Meadowbank was the judicial title of a Scottish judge of the Court of Session, a title carried by members of the Maconochie family, and reference data identify the sitter as Lord Meadowbank. In Scotland such a title came with appointment to the bench, so the portrait would have recorded a man of legal distinction. The record gives no further information, so the circumstances of the commission are unknown.","c":"Raeburn was the natural painter of the Edinburgh legal world, and his direct light and broad brushwork suited its strong, experienced faces. The record offers no description of this canvas, so the verdict must be general. A bust-length portrait of this size has little room for accessories, which means that everything depends on the head, and heads were the part of a picture where Raeburn was most reliable."},"met:437357":{"d":"A portrait of James Johnston of Straiton (died 1841), painted in oil on canvas, about 90 by 69 centimeters. The museum's record gives no date and no description of the sitter's age, clothing, pose or setting, though since Raeburn died in 1823 the picture must have been painted before then. It can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a Scottish landowner, the form of canvas generally chosen when a sitter wanted a good likeness with room for the hands.","i":"In Scottish usage a name followed by \"of\" and a place name designates the owner of an estate, so the title marks Johnston as a laird of Straiton. A portrait of a landowner recorded his standing in the county as well as his face, and was usually intended to hang in the family house. The record gives no further details of his life, so any further reading of the picture's meaning has to remain general.","c":"Raeburn painted Scottish lairds with great sympathy: rugged, weathered faces under a strong light, with no pretense of elegance. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is of the type rather than the particular. Such portraits were the staple of his practice, and the best of them rise above the routine through the frank, unposed character that he found in the faces of landed men."},"met:437358":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Janet Law, painted in oil on canvas, about 90 by 69 centimeters. The museum's record gives no date and no description of her dress, pose or setting, and the title gives only her name, without any style such as Miss or Mrs. Since Raeburn died in 1823 the picture must have been painted before then. It can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a woman, made on a canvas that leaves room for the hands.","i":"The title does not say whether she was married, and the record gives no information about her family, rank or the circumstances of the commission. A portrait of a woman at this scale was ordinarily made for her family or her husband, to hang in the house, and in Edinburgh at this period such commissions came from lawyers, merchants, doctors and landowners. Beyond this, the picture's context must be treated as unknown.","c":"Raeburn's women are rarely idealized: he paints a plain, intelligent face under a strong light, in simple dress, with a sureness that flatters nobody and convinces everybody. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict has to be general. Among his many portraits of ordinary sitters, it represents the unpretentious side of his art, valued for candor rather than for display."},"met:437359":{"d":"A portrait of John Gray (1731 to 1811) of Newholm, painted in oil on canvas, about 125 by 102 centimeters. The museum's record gives no date and no description of the sitter's dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a three-quarter-length portrait of an older man, since the sitter's dates mean that he was between about fifty-six and eighty when Raeburn was working. It is a large canvas of the kind commissioned by a family to preserve the likeness of its head.","i":"The \"of Newholm\" in the title marks Gray as the owner of an estate, which in Scotland was the usual way of naming a landowner. The picture was probably commissioned by the family, and portraits of elderly landowners often served as records of the senior member of a household, to be kept as a family heirloom. Beyond the name and dates, the record gives no information about his life or the commission.","c":"Raeburn was particularly good with older sitters, since their lined and weathered faces suited his strong light and direct, unfussy handling. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict must be general. A three-quarter-length portrait of an elderly man gave him a good chance to be honest about age, and portraits of this kind are among the most sympathetic in his large output."},"met:437360":{"d":"A portrait of Lady Maitland, named in the title as Catherine Connor, who died in 1865, painted in oil on canvas, about 126 by 101 centimeters. The museum's record gives no date and no description of her dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a three-quarter-length portrait of a titled woman. Since Raeburn died in 1823 the picture must have been painted before then, when the sitter was a good deal younger than at her death.","i":"The style of Lady Maitland usually shows that a woman was the wife of a knight or baronet, or the daughter of an earl, and the record names her by both her title and her maiden name. A portrait of this size and form would have been made for her family, as a record of her new standing and as an ornament for the house. The record gives no more information about the commission.","c":"Raeburn's portraits of women in this format are among his most appealing, plain in design and tender in their handling of the face, in which the light seems to arrive from a single window. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is of the type. A picture of this scale is a respectable, well-made example of his practice, not one of his most daring."},"nga:5":{"d":"The Virgin Mary sits low, on the ground or on a cushion, with the Christ Child, in a small panel painted in tempera around 1430 by Fra Angelico. It measures about 61 by 45 centimeters, a size for private prayer rather than for an altar. Pictures of this type usually set Mary in a flowering garden or before a hanging cloth, with her Son in her lap, and Angelico paints them with clear, luminous color and a gentle calm.","i":"In the Madonna of Humility, Mary sits on the ground instead of a throne, an image of her humility (the word comes from humus, earth) that thrived in the devotional culture of mendicant orders such as the Dominicans, Angelico's own order. A garden setting often hints at the enclosed garden of the Song of Songs, a symbol of Mary's virginity, and the closeness of mother and child invites the viewer's tenderness.","c":"In works of this period Angelico joins the radiant color and graceful line of the Gothic tradition to the new Florentine understanding of volume and space, and a small panel of about 1430 shows the balance well: figures with quiet sculptural weight, luminous color and a sweetness kept in check by dignity. It is intimate and gentle, if less ambitious than his later frescoes at San Marco."},"nga:41613":{"d":"Two physician saints, the twin brothers Cosmas and Damian, heal a sick woman named Palladia in this small panel of about 36 by 46 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil around 1438-40 by Fra Angelico. A horizontal format of this size is typical of a predella, the row of narrative scenes beneath an altarpiece, and the panel is generally linked with the predella of the San Marco altarpiece in Florence, which told the lives of the same two saints.","i":"Cosmas and Damian were physicians who treated the poor without payment and were martyred under Diocletian; they became patrons of doctors and, in Florence, of the Medici family, who paid for the San Marco altarpiece (Cosimo de' Medici's name saint is Cosmas). The Golden Legend says that Palladia, who had spent her fortune on doctors in vain, was cured through their prayers, and that a gift she pressed on Damian caused a quarrel with his brother.","c":"Predella scenes show Angelico at his most narrative: small figures, clear gestures and a gentle humanity that makes the miracle credible. The format asks for economy, and he supplies it with luminous color and an unforced composition. These panels are less monumental than his frescoes at San Marco but have an intimate, precious quality, and a story of healing told without fuss is one of the things he does best."},"nga:41581":{"d":"The Magi, or three wise men, come to worship the newborn Christ in this tempera panel on poplar by Fra Angelico, dated by the museum between about 1440 and 1460. In pictures of this subject the kings, richly dressed and often followed by a train of attendants, kneel before the Virgin and Child and offer gifts of gold, frankincense and myrrh, while a star recalls their journey from the East.","i":"The story in Matthew 2 of wise men following a star to Bethlehem was celebrated at Epiphany, and Florence made it a civic festival: the Confraternity of the Magi, based at San Marco, staged a pageant of the kings, and the Medici favored the subject for its image of princes bowing to a greater king. The three gifts name Christ as king, God and man. The date range extends past Angelico's death in 1455, which suggests uncertainty about the date or another hand's share.","c":"The Adoration suited Angelico's strengths: a courtly procession, luminous color and a quiet piety. If, as the dating suggests, other hands share in the panel, the finest passages are probably those in his clear, tender manner. It offers pageantry more than psychological depth, and in that respect it stands apart from the sterner innovations of Florentines like Donatello and Masaccio."},"met:436941":{"d":"A small portrait of Sebastian Andorfer (1469 to 1537), painted in oil on a panel of Swiss stone pine, about 43 by 36 centimeters, and dated 1517, when the sitter was about forty-eight. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-length portrait of the kind made in southern Germany in the early sixteenth century. The wood is characteristic of the Alpine region, and the painter, Hans Maler, worked in the Tyrol.","i":"Hans Maler was active in Schwaz, a Tyrolean town enriched by silver and copper mining, and the wealthy families of the region and the Habsburg court were his clients. A portrait of this kind recorded a man's appearance and age and, in German Renaissance practice, was often accompanied by an inscription or a coat of arms. The record does not describe the sitter's occupation, so his standing must be inferred from the commission itself.","c":"German portraiture of the 1510s is an art of exact observation, and Maler belongs to its sober tradition: a firmly drawn head, a clear silhouette and a plain ground, with every fold and wrinkle noted. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. His portraits lack the psychological depth of Holbein or Dürer, but they have a blunt candor and are valuable evidence of the mining aristocracy of the Tyrol."},"met:436942":{"d":"A small portrait of Ulrich Fugger the Younger (1490 to 1525), painted in oil on a linden panel, about 40 by 32 centimeters, and dated 1525, the year of the sitter's death. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-length portrait of a young man from a family of great wealth. Linden was a common wood for panel paintings in southern Germany, and the painter, Hans Maler, worked in the Tyrol.","i":"The Fuggers of Augsburg were the greatest merchant bankers of the age, lenders to the Habsburg emperors and owners of mines in the Tyrol, which may help explain a connection with Hans Maler, who worked at Schwaz, a mining town. A portrait such as this recorded a member of the dynasty in a fashionable form for his family and friends. The sitter was about thirty-five at the date of the picture.","c":"Maler's Fugger portraits are among the best records of the south German banking world, and he gives his sitters a firm, unflattering clarity without Holbein's depth of character. The record gives no description of this panel, so the judgment is general. A small portrait of a young man at the end of his short life, it has a plain, direct presence, and its value is as much historical as artistic."},"nga:23":{"d":"A Madonna and Child in tempera on a panel about 75 by 55 centimetres, painted around 1470, when Botticelli was in his mid-twenties. His early Madonnas, learned from his teacher Fra Filippo Lippi, usually set the half-length figures close to the viewer in a domestic, intimate manner and draw them with a fine, flowing outline, with Mary bowing her head in a gentle, melancholy gaze toward her Child.","i":"Botticelli trained in Lippi's workshop, where he learned the idea of a Madonna who is a gentle young Florentine mother. The melancholy often seen in his Madonnas is read as her foreknowledge of the Passion, her love tempered by sorrow. Such panels were made for domestic devotion in the homes of Florentine merchants and bankers, and the young painter was soon to become one of the city's leading artists, working for the Medici circle.","c":"Even in an early work Botticelli's line is alive: the contours flow and curve with a musical rhythm, and the sadness in Mary's face is subtle. The young painter does not yet have the confidence or invention of his mature work, and the Child's pose can look awkward. But the grace is already there, and the picture shows him turning Lippi's sweetness into something more intense."},"nga:41671":{"d":"A portrait of Giuliano de' Medici, about 76 by 53 centimetres, painted in tempera on a panel around 1478 to 1480. Giuliano, the younger brother of Lorenzo the Magnificent, was murdered in 1478, so this is probably a memorial portrait, painted after his death. Several versions of the image survive, from Botticelli and his workshop, which suggests that it was copied for friends and supporters of the Medici.","i":"Giuliano (1453–1478) was killed in the Pazzi conspiracy, an attempt by rival Florentine families to overthrow the Medici; he was stabbed in the cathedral during Easter Mass on 26 April 1478, while his brother Lorenzo escaped wounded. After the conspiracy failed, the Medici made Giuliano a martyr for the family's cause, and portraits of him helped keep his memory alive. Botticelli, who worked for the Medici circle, was well placed to paint him.","c":"Botticelli gives the young man a grave, inward dignity that suits a memorial: the clear outline, firm modelling and restrained colour make him an image of calm. The likeness is probably idealised, and the portrait gives away little of Giuliano's personality, but the combination of clarity and melancholy is characteristic of Botticelli. The existence of other versions testifies to the image's power for his contemporaries."},"nga:24":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 68 by 102 centimetres, painted in tempera and oil, shows the Adoration of the Magi. In Botticelli's Adorations the Holy Family usually sits under a ruined shelter while the three kings kneel with their gifts and a crowd of attendants gathers behind them. The picture dates to about 1478 to 1482, in the years when he was working in the circle of the Medici.","i":"Matthew's Gospel tells of the wise men who followed a star to Bethlehem. In Florence the subject had a special meaning, because the Medici family belonged to the Compagnia dei Magi, a confraternity that staged a pageant of the Epiphany, and Botticelli's most famous Adoration includes portraits of the family. The ruins often seen in such scenes are read as the old, pagan world giving way to Christ's new order.","c":"Botticelli handles the pageant with a rhythmic, flowing line, and the kneeling kings, the gentle Virgin and the crowd of figures are arranged with elegance and nervous grace. Space is less convincing than line, and the crowd can seem like a decorative frieze rather than a believable throng. It is a delightful, lyrical Adoration rather than a monumental one, and shows how Botticelli favoured poetry of line over realism of space."},"nga:21":{"d":"A portrait of a young man, in an almost square panel about 44 by 46 centimetres, painted in tempera around 1482 to 1485. The sitter is unidentified. Botticelli's portraits of young Florentines are among the most characterful of the period: they show the head and shoulders close to the viewer, in three-quarter or profile view, with firm outline and an intense, slightly aloof gaze.","i":"In the 1480s Botticelli, who had painted frescoes in the Sistine Chapel in Rome in 1481–82, was one of the most sought-after painters in Florence, and portraits like this recorded the educated young elite of the city of Lorenzo de' Medici, Ficino and Poliziano. Such panels were kept at home or exchanged between friends. The sitter's name is lost, so he is read as a type: a thoughtful young Florentine of the late Quattrocento.","c":"A concise, quietly intense portrait. Botticelli's sharp line and firm modelling give the head a sculptural clarity, and the sitter's reserve suggests an inner life. The sitter's anonymity limits its interest, and the likeness is tidy rather than dramatic, but few portraits of the 1480s convey so well the alert, self-possessed youth of Medici Florence."},"nga:41583":{"d":"The Virgin kneels in adoration of the infant Christ in a poplar panel painted in tempera between 1480 and 1490; its dimensions are not recorded. In this type of picture, Mary, hands joined, gazes down at the newborn Child lying before her, in a quiet scene often set before a rocky landscape or a ruined shelter. The title gives the essence of the subject: a mother worshipping her son.","i":"The adoring Virgin follows the vision of Saint Bridget of Sweden, who described Mary kneeling in prayer before the newborn Child, and it became a favourite image of Italian devotion. The viewer is invited to adore with her, and the Child's vulnerability, lying on the ground, hints at his future sacrifice. Botticelli returned to the theme several times, and the repetition shows the demand for quiet devotional pictures in Florentine homes.","c":"Botticelli at his gentlest: the Virgin's bowed head and delicate hands are drawn with exquisite line, and the sadness mixed with joy is typical of him. The danger of such pictures is sweetness, and the workshop repeated the types, so quality varies from panel to panel. At its best it has a quiet, devotional intensity and a hushed grace that few Florentines could match."},"met:435728":{"d":"A small panel touched with gold shows a bare chapel with a steep wooden roof and brick walls. The elderly Saint Jerome, in a white robe, kneels at right on a linen sheet, held up by two monks in brown habits, and opens his mouth to receive the host from a priest in a pink chasuble who bends toward him from the left. Two young acolytes in white surplices stand at the far left holding candles. Behind the altar a palm rises against the wall, and a red cardinal's hat hangs above it, a traditional attribute of Jerome. Small windows at either side show patches of blue sky.","i":"Jerome, the fourth-century scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, spent his last years in a monastery near Bethlehem. This scene follows a popular medieval letter attributed to Eusebius, which describes the dying saint receiving his last communion among his brethren. The Metropolitan Museum states that the panel was commissioned by Francesco del Pugliese, a Florentine wool merchant and follower of the preacher Savonarola, and that it matches an entry in a 1502 inventory. Jerome was never a cardinal in the later sense, but later art routinely gave him a red hat. The frame comes from the workshop of Giuliano da Maiano.","c":"A late Botticelli, stripped of the grace of his mythologies. Line and gesture carry everything: the priest's careful offering, the saint's open mouth, the bent heads of the supporting monks. The steep, boxlike chapel is a rudimentary space and the acolytes are stiff, but this is a devotional object, small enough to hold close. Beside the Venus pictures of the 1480s it feels austere, even anxious, which suits the Savonarolan years in which it was made."},"met:435729":{"d":"A long panel, about 67 by 150 centimetres, painted in tempera around 1500, shows three miracles of Saint Zenobius, bishop of Florence, in one continuous setting. The Met's keywords record a miracle, a resurrection, men, a horse and the saint, so at least one scene shows him restoring a dead person to life. The shape and size suggest that it was one of a series of panels from his legend.","i":"Zenobius, an early bishop of Florence, is one of the city's patron saints, and the cathedral kept his relics, so his legendary miracles were a matter of civic pride. Botticelli painted this panel around 1500, in the years after the fall and execution of the preacher Savonarola in 1498, when his art took a more austere, intense turn. It probably belonged to a series of scenes from the saint's life, which would have been displayed together.","c":"Late Botticelli is a surprise to those who know the Primavera: the languid grace gives way, in his last works, to angular drama and abrupt gesture. The panel's strengths are its narrative speed and clarity; its colours are hard and its figures stiff, and some will find it a retreat from his earlier beauty. Others see a daring, expressive new style. Either way it shows an artist rethinking his art in a time of crisis."},"met:436966":{"d":"Under a ruined timber shed, the Virgin kneels in a dark blue mantle, hands pressed together, and looks down at the naked Child lying on the edge of her cloak on the bare ground. Small red-orange cherubs cluster around them and form a glowing ring about her head. At the left Joseph, in a yellow robe, sits asleep with his head on his hand, beside a fruit tree and a wicker fence, and an ox stands in the shadow behind. From the right two shepherds approach, bent forward, one with his hat in his hand. Behind, a road winds through a rocky, hilly landscape to a pale blue sky. A bare tree stands in the middle distance, and small figures stand on a ledge at the far right. The forms are hard-edged, as if cut from stone.","i":"This is an early work, dated shortly after 1450, when Mantegna was about twenty and was working on the Ovetari Chapel frescoes in Padua. It joins a Nativity to the arrival of the shepherds, and the Child lies directly on the ground. The bare tree with one living branch has been read as a sign of renewal. The sleek surfaces and the clear, detailed landscape are thought to show Netherlandish influence, and the picture has been linked to Mantegna's stay at the Este court in Ferrara in 1450–51, though a patron is not certain. The panel was later transferred to canvas, and a small strip at the right was lost.","c":"Even at this early date the hard, sculptural drawing is unmistakable, and some passages are already bold: the sleeping Joseph sunk in his yellow cloak is a fine invention. But the picture is not yet fully coordinated. The figures sit on separate planes, the Virgin's pose is stiff, and the landscape recedes in layers like stage flats. That hardness is also its force, giving the Nativity a bleakness that softer Florentine Adorations avoid. It lacks the command of space of the Camera degli Sposi, painted two decades later, but it shows clearly where Mantegna started."},"met:436968":{"d":"A small arched panel shows the Virgin in half-length, her head tilted so that her cheek rests against that of the sleeping Child she holds. She wears a white veil within a gold halo and a dark green robe with red cuffs, fastened with a small brooch at the throat. The Child, naked except for a dark cloth, droops heavily in her arms, his eyes closed and his plump hands limp. Behind them the background is packed with the winged heads of angels, deep red on the left and paler golden-brown on the right, each modelled like a small relief carving. A marbled stone ledge runs along the bottom edge. The forms are sharply drawn and firmly modelled.","i":"Mantegna was about twenty-three when he made this picture, around 1454, in Padua, where he had already begun the frescoes of the Ovetari Chapel in the Eremitani church. In 1453 he had married Nicolosia, daughter of the Venetian painter Jacopo Bellini and sister of Giovanni and Gentile Bellini, which tied him to the leading Venetian workshop. The red heads are conventionally seraphim, the highest rank of angels, set against gold. The Child's limp, sleeping pose is often read as a premonition of Christ's death, an interpretation rather than a certainty. Giovanni Bellini's early Madonnas show how readily the younger painter absorbed this sculptural, tender type.","c":"The Child is the picture's great invention. The limp limbs, swollen cheek and tilted head are observed with a hardness that makes sweetness impossible, and the rhyme of the two heads, cheek to cheek, is genuinely moving. The red mass of seraphim sets the pale flesh off dramatically, almost like carved relief. Less successful is the Virgin, whose face is severe and rather sour, and whose large hands are stiff. The picture is austere rather than lovely, and more convincing for it; its sculptural clarity taught the Venetians who followed, Giovanni Bellini above all."},"nga:41682":{"d":"A young child stands almost filling a tall, narrow canvas, with a delicate silver scepter topped with a cross tucked into one elbow, while the other hand is raised to his chest with the first two fingers extended in blessing. Short golden rays form a halo around his head. He has short blond hair, a high forehead, chubby cheeks and deeply shaded eyes, and looks off to the right. He wears a white tunic and a burgundy cape edged with gold, fastened with a jeweled clasp, and stands barefoot. Swirling patches of brick red, yellow, green and white behind him could be a tapestry.","i":"The raised fingers show that the child is the infant Christ giving a blessing, and the cross-topped scepter marks him as ruler of the world, so he appears as Savior and king rather than as a helpless baby. Such images made a theological point: from the start Christ is both child and sovereign. The picture was probably made for private devotion. The date of about 1460 coincides roughly with Mantegna's move to the court of Mantua.","c":"Mantegna turns a small child into a figure of weight and authority. The sharply cut folds of the cape and the hard, clear contours make the child seem almost carved, a quality that owes much to his study of ancient sculpture, and the thin tempera on canvas gives a matte, chalky surface. The gaze is solemn and not at all sweet, which is the point."},"nga:41584":{"d":"A small bust portrait in strict profile shows a man facing left against a mottled blue-grey ground. He has a high, smooth forehead, a straight, strong nose, a firm closed mouth and a rounded chin; his close-cropped brown hair is almost entirely hidden by a plain black cap. Over a thin edge of white shirt he wears a dull red garment with a high grey collar, its shoulder and chest modelled in streaks of darker red. The face is lit evenly from the front, and the flesh is built up from fine strokes of ochre and pink. The blue background is textured and patchy, and rather rough beside the delicate face.","i":"The sitter is unknown, and no document names him. The National Gallery catalogues the panel as by Andrea Mantegna, about 1470, though it has also been given to Giovanni Bellini or to a pupil of Mantegna. Strict profile was the north Italian court fashion, descended from Roman coins and from medals, and it emphasised outline and rank more than personality. The painting is traced only from 1906, when it was in a private collection in Hungary. Its paint layer has been transferred from the original panel to canvas and then to hardboard, which probably cost it some of its surface and complicates judgement of its condition.","c":"Small and austere, the portrait gets its power from drawing. The profile is cut with a hard, sculptural precision typical of Mantegna, who studied antique sculpture, and the cap and sloping shoulder lock the head into a compact shape. But the sitter has little personality beyond a stern gaze, the red garment is flat, and the transfers have left the background coarse. If by Mantegna himself, it is a minor example of his skill; if not, a very good picture by someone close to him. The best of it is the forehead and eye."},"met:436967":{"d":"Four figures crowd a small canvas, cut off at the waist. At the center the Virgin, in a yellow headcloth, green mantle and dull-red gown, holds the naked Christ Child, who stands on a white cushion on her lap and leans toward her, one hand on her chest. Bearded Joseph at left wears an orange-red robe. At right a woman in an orange-red mantle and white veil, identified as Mary Magdalen, turns her head in profile toward the group. All have gold halos. Behind them is a dark hedge hung with orange fruit. The pigment is matte and the forms look carved, with heavy, serious faces.","i":"Mantegna was court painter to the Gonzaga family at Mantua, and the Metropolitan Museum says he made this for the Gonzaga court. He drew on Roman funerary reliefs, in which the relatives of the dead are pressed together in grave, emotional unity. He used distemper, a water-based paint that stays soluble, which let him blend layers and model forms strongly; the same medium has also made the picture vulnerable, and past conservation and varnish have dulled its original brilliance. The female saint in red is identified as Mary Magdalen, which makes this an unusual Holy Family. The date of about 1495–1500 places it late in his career.","c":"The close-up crop is the picture's boldest idea, and it works: the faces have the weight of carved stone and no emotion is softened. It is a grave, unflattering image of a family, closer to a Roman relief than to the sweet Madonnas of the Venetians. Its drawbacks are the surface, now dulled so that the figures read as flat, and the awkward, statue-like Child. Magdalen's presence is unexplained and makes the group feel assembled from separate heads. Among Mantegna's late works it is austere, even forbidding, and more admirable than inviting."},"nga:1181":{"d":"A small upright panel, about thirty centimetres high, shows Judith standing in the open front of a tent. Its rose-pink flaps, edged with gold bands of script-like ornament, are drawn back and tied at either side. Her red-gold hair is curled, her white dress slips from one shoulder, and a heavy ultramarine mantle wraps around her. In one hand she holds a straight blade at her hip; with the other she lowers the head of Holofernes, face turned away, into a sack held open by her maid, who wears a white head cloth and a yellow tunic with a red cape. In the dark behind, a bare foot protrudes from a gold bed. The floor is broken flagstones, drawn in careful perspective.","i":"The story comes from the Book of Judith: a widow enters the camp of the Assyrian general Holofernes, who is besieging her city, and cuts off his head while he lies drunk. In Renaissance Italy she stood for courage, chastity and the defeat of a tyrant by the weak. This coloured panel repeats a composition that also appears in grisaille panels in Dublin and Montreal and in a drawing in the Uffizi. The attribution is uncertain: the National Gallery of Art catalogues it as by Mantegna or a follower, possibly Giulio Campagnola, and dates it about 1495–1500 by comparison with Mantegna's late grisailles. It may once have belonged to the Gonzaga of Mantua, but that is not proved.","c":"What counts here is surface: the silk of the tent, the blue mantle with its velvet bloom, the careful floor. Judith stands like a statue, her face blank and her gesture polite, and the horror of the story is kept out of sight; the severed head is turned away and nothing is bloody. That restraint can read as elegance or as coolness. If the panel is Mantegna's own, it is a small work of finish rather than force; if it is a follower's, he has caught the master's firm contours. Either way, it is more jewel than drama."},"met:436049":{"d":"King Philip IV (1605–1665) is shown in parade armor, the decorated armor made for ceremony and display rather than battle, in a vertical canvas of about 183 by 118 cm, painted about 1628. He was then about twenty-three. Gaspar de Crayer, a Flemish court painter in Brussels and a contemporary of Rubens and Van Dyck, portrayed the king for the court of the Spanish Netherlands, where such images stood in for the absent sovereign.","i":"Philip IV ruled Spain, the Spanish Netherlands, Naples, Sicily and, until 1640, Portugal, and he was a great patron of Velázquez and Rubens. The Netherlands were governed in his name from Brussels by his aunt Isabella, so portraits of the king reminded subjects of their sovereign. Parade armor asserted military authority even for a monarch who rarely campaigned, with the king presented as the head of a martial dynasty.","c":"Crayer is a capable, dignified court portraitist working in the shadow of Rubens and Van Dyck, and the armor gives him an occasion to show his skill with metal and light. Beside Velázquez's coolly alive Philip, his king is more of an emblem, and the stiffness of the pose reflects the formality of the commission. The picture is a competent record of the young monarch, valued for its subject and its place in the Flemish court tradition."},"met:436048":{"d":"Alexander the Great and the philosopher Diogenes meet in this very large canvas, about 225 by 324 cm, which is more than three meters wide, with a horse and a boy among the figures. The size is that of a history painting made for a hall or palace, a dramatic stage for a famous anecdote. Gaspar de Crayer, a leading Flemish painter in Brussels, was an heir to the manner of Rubens and Van Dyck, and the painting was among the Met's earliest purchases, in 1871.","i":"In the story from Plutarch and Diogenes Laertius, the young conqueror visited the philosopher, who lived in a tub and lay in the sun, and asked what he could do for him; Diogenes answered that he should stand out of his light. Alexander is said to have remarked that, if he were not Alexander, he would wish to be Diogenes. The anecdote set royal power against philosophical independence and flattered a prince who could admire wisdom.","c":"Crayer's large history paintings show the Flemish Baroque's debt to Rubens: ample figures, warm color and theatrical grouping, and the picture's idea, the contrast between the elegant conqueror and the half-naked sage, is clear. The scale demands command of a crowd, and he provides it competently. What he lacks is Rubens's fire, and the picture's energy is lower than its subject deserves. It is respectable and well made, and a reminder of the many gifted painters in Rubens's shadow."},"met:15588":{"d":"A tiny portrait of an unnamed man, painted in watercolor on a thin sheet of ivory, about 3 by 5 centimeters, around 1770. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait miniature, a likeness made to be held in the hand or set into a locket, bracelet or case and seen at close range. Benbridge, born in Philadelphia, painted it at about the time he returned to America from his training in Italy.","i":"Miniatures were personal objects, given between relatives, lovers and friends and kept as a remembrance rather than hung on a wall. Ivory gave the watercolor a luminous, flesh-like glow, and the small size meant that a likeness could travel. Because the sitter is not named, his rank and story are lost, and the picture is best read as a private keepsake of a man in the colonial society of the 1770s.","c":"Benbridge's reputation rests on his oil portraits, where his Roman training, partly under Pompeo Batoni, shows in clear modeling and a polished surface. A miniature lets that discipline work on a tiny scale. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general: a competent, delicately made likeness, valued more as an example of colonial American miniature painting than for any individuality in the sitter."},"met:10138":{"d":"A three-quarter-length portrait of an unnamed gentleman, painted in oil on canvas, about 125 by 100 centimeters, in the early 1770s. The museum's record gives no description of his clothing, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a commissioned portrait of a man of means, on the standard canvas size for a figure shown to about the knees, made soon after Benbridge came back to America from Italy and about the time he settled in Charleston, South Carolina.","i":"In the British colonies a portrait was the principal way a prosperous man recorded his standing, and planters and merchants ordered them from the few trained painters at hand. Benbridge's Italian training gave him a standard of finish that few colonial rivals could match. The sitter is not named, so his occupation and rank are unknown. The picture is best read as a statement of respectability meant to hang in a family house.","c":"Benbridge was among the best-trained painters in the colonies, with clear drawing, smooth, enamel-like paint and a European sense of pose. The record offers no description of this canvas, so the judgment is general. His portraits can be less animated than Copley's, but they show a command of design and finish that placed him well above most provincial painters of the time."},"met:10137":{"d":"A tiny portrait of a woman named only as Mrs. Clark, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4 by 3 centimeters, in the early 1770s. The keywords record lace, a cap and a ribbon, details of a woman's headdress and dress. The museum's record gives no further description of her features or costume. It is a portrait miniature, a likeness meant to be held in the hand or worn, and it is attributed to Henry Benbridge.","i":"The title identifies her only by her husband's surname, the usual way of naming a married woman in the eighteenth century, so her own first name and family are not recorded here. A cap of lace and ribbon was the everyday mark of a respectable matron, and such details in a miniature let the viewer judge her taste and means. Such keepsakes were often exchanged at marriage or kept as memorials.","c":"Lace and ribbon give a miniaturist the chance to show fine, light touches of white against the warm ivory, and a painter of Benbridge's training could do that well. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general. Its modest scale suits the intimate purpose, though in pictures of this kind the details of dress often speak more clearly than the personality of the sitter."},"met:10136":{"d":"A portrait of a woman known as Mrs. Benjamin Simons, painted in oil on canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, between about 1771 and 1776. The museum's record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a married woman, painted in the years when Benbridge was working in Charleston, South Carolina, for the planters and merchants of the region.","i":"The sitter is named only by her husband's name, in the usual way, and Simons is a long-established South Carolina name, so the portrait was probably commissioned in Charleston. Portraits of wives were often ordered alongside those of husbands to furnish a town house or plantation house. The record gives no further details of the commission, so her own story must be sought elsewhere.","c":"Benbridge's portraits of women from his Charleston years combine a Roman training in composition with the more reserved taste of the colonies. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the judgment is general. At this size and date it shows him at his steadiest: a clearly organized, carefully finished likeness, without the daring of the best Copleys, but also without the awkwardness of lesser provincial work."},"met:10139":{"d":"A very small portrait of an unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4 by 2.5 centimeters, around 1780. The museum's record gives no description of his clothing, hair or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait miniature, whose narrow format suggests that it was made to fit a small case, locket or piece of jewelry. It was painted during the Revolutionary War by Henry Benbridge.","i":"Miniatures were especially valuable in wartime, when family members were separated by service or exile and a pocket likeness was the easiest to carry. The sitter is unnamed, so his rank and story are lost. Reading the picture as a keepsake, made for a wife, sweetheart or child, is the most natural interpretation, though the record does not say for whom it was made.","c":"Benbridge's miniatures show the discipline of an oil painter working small, with a clean contour and a restrained palette. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general. A miniature as small as this leaves little room for individuality, and the likeness depends on a few touches around the eyes and mouth, which at this size separate a keepsake from a mere type."},"met:10135":{"d":"A tiny portrait of a woman named Elizabeth Ann Timothy, painted in watercolor and gouache on ivory, about 3.7 by 3 centimeters, between about 1775 and 1785. The museum's record gives no description of her features, hair or dress. It is a portrait miniature, made to be held in the hand or worn, in which opaque gouache is used, as was usual, to bring up highlights and fine details over the translucent watercolor.","i":"The record names her in full but gives no dates, family or profession, so little more can be said about who she was. Miniatures of women were often painted for a betrothal or a marriage, or as gifts to relatives, and they were kept as personal tokens rather than displayed. The record does not say what occasion this one served, so any reading of its purpose has to remain a guess.","c":"The addition of gouache to watercolor lets the miniaturist model with more body than pure wash allows, a technical refinement that marks the work of a trained painter. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general. As a keepsake it succeeds by being small, clear and carefully finished, though the quality of the likeness itself cannot be judged from the record."},"nga:106339":{"d":"A small panel, about 18 by 27 centimetres, shows still water stretching toward the horizon as the sun sets. Dark windmills and sailboats stand in silhouette against a cloudy sky, with reflections on the yellow water, and the setting sun at the centre casts a pale yellow glow across the middle of the scene while the edges lie in shadow. In the foreground are grass, a wide dirt path and two rowboats by the water. The palette is muted browns, greys and yellows.","i":"In the 1640s Dutch landscape painters, among them Jan van Goyen and Salomon van Ruysdael, worked in a restrained, tonal manner, using a limited range of browns, greys and yellows to capture the damp, hazy light of the Netherlands. Windmills and sailboats were emblems of the country's reclaimed land and its trade. Anthonie van Borssom, an Amsterdam artist often linked with Rembrandt's circle, shows in this small evening view a gift for atmosphere.","c":"A quiet, tonal little picture. The glow of the setting sun, the dark silhouettes and the reflections on still water are managed with a minimum of means, and the muted palette gives a mood of calm melancholy. Its scale is modest and the composition conventional, and it lacks the sparkle of Van Goyen at his best. But the simple atmosphere is persuasive and shows a young painter alive to the Dutch light."},"met:435723":{"d":"A milkmaid, cows, a pig and sheep share a farmyard with a barn, in a canvas of about 51 by 69 centimeters painted around 1650-55 by the Dutch artist Anthonie van Borssom. The recorded motifs are cows, a pig, a milkmaid, a barn, a woman, cattle and sheep, so the scene is a plain account of daily life on a Dutch farm rather than an event, in the tradition of Dutch barnyard and cattle pieces.","i":"Cattle pieces and farmyard scenes were popular in the Dutch Republic of the mid-seventeenth century, where dairy farming was a source of national wealth and cows were a symbol of the land's prosperity. Painters like Paulus Potter, Adriaen van de Velde and Aelbert Cuyp made animal painting a specialty. Such pictures offered city buyers a sense of the countryside as an orderly, productive place, and a milkmaid, a familiar figure of rustic work, adds a human note.","c":"Van Borssom is perhaps better known for his drawings than his paintings, and his farmyard is modest next to Potter's, whose cattle have a sculptural presence. But the plain, unforced arrangement has a documentary honesty. Without the finish of the great specialists, it relies on observation and atmosphere, and the animals, a cow, a pig, a flock of sheep, are drawn with evident affection."},"met:11746":{"d":"A small portrait of a man named Stephen Hooper, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.6 by 3.7 centimeters, and dated 1773. The museum's record gives no description of his features, clothing or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait miniature, made to be held in the hand or worn, by a painter then working in Boston. Henry Pelham, the artist, was the half-brother of John Singleton Copley.","i":"Pelham was the boy who posed for Copley's Boy with a Squirrel. A miniature by him from 1773 comes from the busy Boston society of the years before the Revolution, when merchants, officials and their families sought likenesses. The record gives no profession or family for Stephen Hooper, so he must be taken as one of the sitters of that circle whose life is otherwise unrecorded.","c":"Pelham was far less celebrated than Copley, and his miniatures are modest in invention, with firm outlines and simple modeling. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. He was better known in his own day as a printmaker and mapmaker, and later as a land agent in Ireland, and the miniatures are best seen as the competent work of a talented member of a gifted family."},"met:15151":{"d":"A small portrait of an unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.2 by 3.5 centimeters, and dated 1779. The museum's record gives no description of his clothing, hair or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait miniature made for the hand or for a case. By 1779 the artist, Henry Pelham, had left Boston for Britain, so the picture belongs to the years of the Revolutionary War, and was painted far from the city where his reputation began.","i":"Pelham had left Boston in 1776, so this miniature belongs to his years in Britain, away from the colonial society in which his name was made. The sitter is unnamed, and his rank and circumstances are not recorded. Portrait miniatures were personal objects, exchanged between family and friends, worn or kept in a case, and valued for being a faithful reminder of a face. The picture is best read as a private likeness of that kind.","c":"A competent, small likeness; the record does not describe it, so the verdict has to be general. Pelham's miniatures lack the brilliance of the best by his contemporaries in London, who had the example of Richard Cosway and George Engleheart behind them, but they have the clarity of a painter trained in Copley's circle. At this size the sitter's face is everything, and the picture is valued chiefly as a record."},"met:436077":{"d":"A tall portrait of an unnamed woman, painted in oil on canvas, about 196 by 131 centimeters, large enough to show the sitter at or near full length. The museum's record gives no date and no description of her dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a formal commission in the English baroque manner, painted by the Swedish-born artist who settled in London in 1689 and became the chief rival of Sir Godfrey Kneller.","i":"Formal portraits of this size were made for aristocratic and gentry families in the decades around 1700, to be hung in the great rooms of a country house, where height and scale proclaimed rank. The sitter is not named, so her family and standing must be inferred from the commission, and the picture can be read as a statement of status in the polished, courtly language of the period. The record gives no further information.","c":"Dahl's portraits are generally softer and more restrained than Kneller's, with an elegant surface and a calm, courtly polish. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His weakness is a certain sameness in poses and in porcelain-like faces, but at his best he gives his sitters grace without strain, and he stands as the most accomplished alternative to Kneller in the London of his day."},"cma:143272":{"d":"A seascape in oil on canvas, about 46 by 65 centimeters, dated 1892. The museum's record gives no description, so the picture can be described only by its title and type: a sea view in a wide format, of a modest size for a work that would hang in a home rather than in a gallery. Henry Ward Ranger was an American painter of the Tonalist school, who preferred low-keyed, hazy effects of light and atmosphere to precise detail.","i":"Tonalist painters wished to evoke a mood rather than record a view, building a landscape from close harmonies of brown, green and gray, and the sea, with its open horizon and changing light, was a natural subject. Ranger was influenced by Dutch landscape painting and by the French Barbizon painters, whose moody, atmospheric landscapes shaped the American Tonalist taste. The record does not say where the scene is, so it should be read as a general mood piece.","c":"Ranger's landscapes of the 1890s are quiet, tonal and carefully made, and admirers prized their poetry while critics found them a little monotonous in color. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. A small seascape of this type is an economical piece of mood-making, more convincing as atmosphere than as observation, and it shows why his work was popular with American collectors of the period."},"cma:121343":{"d":"A landscape in oil on canvas, about 72 by 93 centimeters, dated 1895. The museum's record gives no description of the composition, and the title points to a single, watchful presence in the scene without saying what it is, so the picture can be described only by its type: a large, moody Tonalist landscape by Henry Ward Ranger, who in 1899 established an important artist colony in Old Lyme, Connecticut.","i":"A title like The Lone Sentinel invites the viewer to read the landscape as a symbol, a single enduring thing standing guard over an empty scene, and such solitary motifs suited the Tonalist taste for solitude, silence and reverie. The record does not say what the sentinel is, so no firm reading can be given. The mood is probably melancholy and contemplative rather than narrative, as the title suggests.","c":"Ranger's best pictures achieve a quiet, enveloping mood through close tones and soft edges, and a lone motif against a broad sky is a classic way to do it. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His work can be repetitive, and the emphasis on mood sometimes outruns observation, but a canvas of 1895 at this size belongs to the middle of his career, when his manner was fully formed."},"met:11853":{"d":"A woodland scene in oil on canvas, about 72 by 91 centimeters, painted between about 1895 and 1900. The keywords list trees, a forest and a landscape, and the title gives the season as spring, but the record gives no further description of the light, colors or any figures. The picture can be described only by its type: a forest picture in the low-keyed, atmospheric manner of the American Tonalists, painted by Henry Ward Ranger.","i":"The forest in spring, with its fresh growth and soft light, was a favorite theme for painters in the Barbizon tradition, which Ranger admired, and a natural subject for the Tonalists, who took that pastoral mood and softened it further, preferring a harmony of greens and browns to sharp detail. The record does not say where the woods are, so no particular place can be named.","c":"Ranger's woodland pictures are among his most successful, since the dense, enclosed subject suited his softly blended tones and his preference for mood over detail. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. A forest scene of this size and period would be typical of his work: quiet, atmospheric and a little sentimental, valued for its harmony rather than for any dramatic invention."},"nga:53376":{"d":"A bearded man holds and looks down at an open book while a winged angel holds a feather pen and an inkwell, both half-length against a peanut-brown ground. The man is balding, with a rim of gray curls and a full grayish-white beard; his forehead is deeply wrinkled and his cheeks hollow. A marine-blue cloth drapes over his brick-red garment, and he braces the book with one hand and lifts a few pages with the other. The angel, seen from behind, has blond braids wound around the head and tied with a red bow, and snowy white wings.","i":"The saint is Matthew the Evangelist, author of the first Gospel, who is traditionally accompanied by an angel, his emblem among the four evangelist symbols. Showing the angel as a helpful attendant who holds the inkwell turns divine inspiration into a quiet collaboration. Cantarini, from Pesaro, was a pupil of Guido Reni, and the naturalism of the old man's face and the idealized angel reflect two sides of Bolognese painting of the 1640s: observation and grace.","c":"The contrast is the picture's strength: the saint's deeply lined forehead and hollow cheeks are observed with stark honesty, while the angel's braids and white sleeve are almost decorative. Turning the angel's back to the viewer is an inventive touch that makes the saint, not the messenger, the focus. The color is restrained but well judged, with blue cloth and red garment set against plain brown. It is one of Cantarini's more attractive half-length saints."},"met:912940":{"d":"A view of the interior of the Sint-Bavokerk, the great church of Haarlem, painted in oil on an oak panel, about 39 by 31 centimeters, around 1676. The museum's record gives no further description, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small, upright architectural view of a Gothic church interior, of the sort painted in Haarlem for collectors who admired accurate perspective and the effects of light on stone. The painter, Job Berckheyde, was a native of the city.","i":"After the Reformation the Sint-Bavokerk, also called the Grote Kerk, became a Protestant church, and its walls were whitewashed and its altars removed, leaving the Gothic structure bare. Painters such as Pieter Saenredam and Berckheyde made a specialty of such interiors, which celebrated both the city's pride in its church and the Protestant taste for plain, light-filled space. The date of about 1676 places this picture in the later phase of the genre.","c":"Berckheyde is better known for his views of Haarlem's squares and churches than for any great innovation, and his work is prized for its clarity and quiet order. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. His church interiors are less austere than Saenredam's, often with more figures and incident, and they have a gentle, domestic warmth that suits their small scale."},"met:438510":{"d":"A tiny landscape on a wooden panel, about 12 by 22 centimetres, shows the outskirts of a village, with trees and village buildings recorded among its subjects. The museum's description gives no further detail, so the colours, the weather and the arrangement of the houses cannot be reported with confidence. At this size and date, around 1880, it reads as an open-air sketch, the kind of notation painters made on the spot to catch a view and a light effect.","i":"Aman-Jean is best known as a Symbolist, a painter of dreamy, soft-edged portraits of women, and he was a close friend of Georges Seurat, with whom he shared a studio in the early 1880s. This small panel comes from his youth, when he was a student in Paris and made landscape sketches in the tradition of Corot and the Barbizon painters. Such panels, painted quickly outdoors, were part of every young painter's training.","c":"A modest panel, to be taken for what it is: a small notation of a village edge, probably painted outdoors, with all the freshness and the incompleteness that implies. It tells little of the Symbolist painter Aman-Jean would become, whose softly modelled women are the heart of his work, but it shows the foundation of direct observation beneath that later dreaminess. Without the details to hand it is best valued as a record of a young painter's training."},"met:437034":{"d":"A Netherlandish panel painted in oil on wood, showing Joseph interpreting the dreams of two fellow prisoners. The museum's record gives no date or size, but its keywords list Joseph, prisoners, men, food, a bedroom and a cat. The picture can be described only by its type and these keywords: a biblical story set, as Netherlandish painters often did, in a recognizable domestic interior with a bed rather than in a bare prison, and given homely details such as a cat.","i":"In Genesis, Joseph, imprisoned in Egypt, interpreted the dreams of Pharaoh's chief cupbearer and chief baker. The cupbearer's dream of a vine foretold his restoration to favor, while the baker's dream of baskets of bread carried on his head foretold his execution, and both came true. The episode begins the chain of events that leads to Joseph's rise to power. The food in the keywords perhaps refers to the cupbearer's cup and the baker's baskets.","c":"The Master of the Story of Joseph is an anonymous painter, named after a group of Joseph panels, and the name is a reminder of how much Netherlandish painting of the Renaissance survives without a signature. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is general. Such narrative panels are valued for their crisp, jewel-like detail and their domestic settings, and the cat and the bed suggest an observant, humorous touch."},"met:437168":{"d":"A small upright panel in oil on wood, about 30 by 22 centimeters, painted between 1510 and 1515. It shows the scene known as Ecce Homo, in which Pilate presents the scourged Christ to the crowd. The keywords list Pontius Pilate, Christ, Mary and another man, but the record gives no further description of the arrangement. The picture can be described only by its type: a compact, finely detailed devotional panel, made for close and private contemplation.","i":"In the Gospel of John, Pilate brought Jesus out wearing the crown of thorns and the purple robe and said, Behold the man, in Latin Ecce Homo, hoping to satisfy the crowd without condemning him. Late medieval devotion dwelt on Christ's suffering, and such images invited the viewer to feel compassion. The presence of Mary, listed in the keywords, adds the mother's grief to the scene.","c":"Jan Mostaert worked in Haarlem and later served at the court of Margaret of Austria, and his small devotional panels combine the careful finish of early Netherlandish painting with a livelier, sometimes eccentric sense of figure and costume. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. As a compact Passion image it is typical of the northern taste for intense detail and emotional directness."},"met:437323":{"d":"A wide view of the Brazilian countryside, painted in 1650 in oil on wood, about 61 by 91 cm, with a man and a woman among the figures. Frans Post went to Brazil in 1637 in the retinue of Johan Maurits of Nassau, governor of Dutch Brazil, and returned to the Netherlands in 1644; this picture, like most of his Brazilian landscapes, was probably painted at home from sketches and memory.","i":"The Dutch West India Company held parts of northeastern Brazil from 1630 to 1654, a colony built on sugar grown by enslaved labor. Post's landscapes presented it as orderly and picturesque: a wide horizon, fertile land, tropical plants and small figures. They offered Dutch buyers an image of distant possessions, and they tend to leave out the violence and the labor on which the colony depended.","c":"Post made a Dutch landscape out of Brazilian elements: the low horizon and broad sky are Haarlem habits, the palms and exotic plants the novelty. The strengths are calm structure and luminous distance. The weaknesses are formula, since the same arrangement recurs across scores of paintings, and generic figures. Historically it is valuable as one of the earliest European landscapes of the Americas, and it remains pleasing to look at."},"nga:151105":{"d":"Shown from the chest up, a clean-shaven young man looks down and to the left under gathered brows, with his shoulders angled to the right. He has a prominent nose, a pointed chin, parted lips and dark blond hair parted in the middle and falling to both shoulders. One arm crosses his chest, and that hand holds a staff along the edge of the picture. He wears a tawny-brown coat buttoned down the front over a high white collar, against a fawn and brown background. The painter signed and dated it faintly on the left edge: P. Soutman F.A. 1640.","i":"The title gives no name, rank or occupation, so the picture may be a commissioned portrait or a character study. A staff can be a gentleman's walking stick, a pilgrim's or shepherd's staff, or a sign of office, so its meaning is open. The half-length format, with a turned head and a downcast, thoughtful look, belongs to the Dutch taste for both portraits and expressive single figures of the 1630s and 1640s.","c":"Soutman had worked in Rubens's circle before settling in Haarlem, and this plain panel shows him in a sober Dutch mood: a clearly lit head, a simple coat and a restrained palette of browns. The pose, with its crossed arm and sidelong glance, is a little stiff, and the paint lacks the dash of his early master. It is best valued for the quiet candor of the face, which has the look of an observed person."},"nga:104729":{"d":"A man seen from the hips up props a flagpole with a voluminous topaz-blue flag against his shoulder. He turns his face to the viewer from a profile stance, one elbow jutting out and a gloved hand on his hip. He has shoulder-length brown hair, a mustache and a narrow goatee, and wears a wide black hat with pale blue, turquoise, yellow and orange plumes, and a rose-pink satin suit trimmed with gray lace, with a white lace collar, a baby-blue sash and a sword. A coat of arms hangs on the wall behind him. It is dated 1640 at the lower left.","i":"The title names the sitter as Andries Stilte and gives his role as a standard bearer, the officer who carried the flag of a company, probably in a civic guard, an honor given to men of good family. In Dutch cities these guards were social clubs as well as militias, and officers had themselves painted in splendid dress. The coat of arms on the wall probably alludes to his family, and the satin, lace and plumes announce wealth and fashion more than military duty.","c":"Verspronck was the most polished of the Haarlem portraitists, and this canvas shows why: the satin is painted in broad patches of white light that make the pink glow, the lace is crisp and the pose has an easy swagger. The costume threatens to upstage the man, and the face is less searching than Frans Hals's, but the picture is a superb piece of surface description and a lively example of the officer in parade dress."},"met:437893":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man, painted in oil on canvas, about 79 by 64 centimeters, and dated 1645. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a Dutch burgher by Johannes Verspronck, a Haarlem portraitist who was a contemporary and rival of Frans Hals and who painted the city's prosperous citizens.","i":"Portraits of men in Haarlem in the 1640s were made for merchants, brewers, officials and professionals who wished to record their standing in sober black with white collars. The sitter's identity and rank are not recorded here, so the picture is best read as a statement of respectability and self-possession, made for his family, and as a document of the clothing and manners of the city's prosperous middle class.","c":"Verspronck's manner is smoother and cooler than Hals's, with thin, even paint, careful modeling and a quiet, attentive sitter. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His portraits seldom surprise, but they are reliably well made, and the best of them have a clarity of observation that gives plain sitters a convincing, steady presence."},"met:16893":{"d":"A tiny portrait of Archibald McCall, painted in watercolor on ivory with lead white and gold leaf, about 3.8 by 3.2 centimeters, between about 1755 and 1760. The museum's record gives no description of his features or dress, though the gold leaf was probably used for a detail of costume such as buttons or braid. It is a portrait miniature, made to be held in the hand or worn, by John Hesselius, who worked in Maryland and Virginia.","i":"The title gives only the sitter's name, and the record adds no profession or family. A miniature with gold leaf is a more costly object than a plain one, so the commission was probably an important or festive one, perhaps for a wedding or a gift, though the record does not say. In the mid-eighteenth-century Chesapeake region, miniatures were relatively rare, and a painter who could make one was a useful member of a small artistic community.","c":"Hesselius was primarily an oil painter, and his miniature is a small, precise piece of work in which the gold leaf adds a touch of luxury. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general. Rarity and interest are its main claims: relatively few miniatures survive from the middle of the century in the colonies, and this is a documented example from the hand of a leading Chesapeake portraitist."},"met:11079":{"d":"A portrait of a woman identified as Mrs. Richard Galloway, painted in oil on canvas, about 93 by 76 centimeters, and dated 1764. The keywords record the details of her dress: a cap, a fichu, a chemise and a ruffle. The museum's record gives no further description of her pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a colonial woman of means, probably painted in Maryland, where the artist, John Hesselius, was based.","i":"The fichu, a light kerchief worn around the shoulders and neckline, and the cap with its ruffles were the standard dress of a respectable woman in the mid-eighteenth century, and the plain linen suggests modesty rather than display. She is also recorded as Sophia Galloway, though the title gives only her husband's name, which was the usual way of naming a wife in colonial portraiture.","c":"Hesselius was one of the leading portraitists in Maryland, and his women's portraits have a firm, painterly handling of fabric and a clear, rather flat modeling of the face, which is where his provincial limits show. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His work lacks the polish of Copley's Boston pictures, but it has honesty and a pleasing plainness, especially in the treatment of linen and lace."},"nga:9":{"d":"A woman holding a nude baby boy sits on a blue patterned pillow on the floor before a brick-red curtain held up by two blue-robed angels. She wears a blue cloak with a white hood over a shell-pink dress, and looks slightly off to the right while supporting the child. The baby, whose face has adult proportions, reaches up to her neck. Both have flat gold halos, and a haloed dove floats at the top of an arch of gold ground. A Latin inscription runs along the bottom edge: AVE MARIA GRATIA PLENA DO.","i":"The inscription is the angel Gabriel's greeting to Mary at the Annunciation, Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with you, and the dove is the Holy Spirit. Mary sits low on the floor, which gives the type its name, Madonna of Humility: the Queen of Heaven shown humbly, close to the earth, yet honored with a cloth of honor held up by angels. The curtain and the arch of gold give her a royal setting.","c":"Masaccio died at twenty-six, and this early panel already shows what set him apart: the Virgin is heavy and solid, the child has real physical weight, and the drapery falls in simple, believable folds rather than in Gothic curves. The greenish cast of the flesh and the stiff gold halos still belong to an older world, and the adult-like face of the baby is awkward. It is a quiet, serious work that points toward his frescoes in the Brancacci Chapel."},"nga:206127":{"d":"A triptych of the Crucifixion, painted in oil on panel and later transferred to canvas, about 1482 to 1485. As the title says, the central panel shows Christ on the cross with the Virgin and Saint John, and the wings carry Saint Jerome and Saint Mary Magdalene. The museum's record gives no description of the figures, poses or landscape, so the work can be described only by its structure: three tall panels, a central scene and two flanking saints, designed to be seen together as a single devotional image.","i":"The three parts form a devotional sequence. In the center are Christ and the two mourners at the foot of the cross, and on the wings stand two penitent saints: Jerome, who retreated to the desert to do penance, and Mary Magdalene, the repentant follower of Christ who stood at the cross. Together they invite the viewer to consider sin, repentance and redemption through Christ's sacrifice. The record gives no information about the commission.","c":"The triptych shows the system that would make Perugino one of the most sought-after painters in Italy: a calm, clear arrangement, soft light and graceful, slightly sentimental figures. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. The transfer from panel to canvas, a drastic conservation method of the past, may have cost some of the original subtlety, and the work is best appreciated for its design."},"nga:29":{"d":"The left wing of a triptych of the Crucifixion, a tall narrow panel about 95 by 30 centimeters, painted in oil on panel and later transferred to canvas, about 1482 to 1485. It carries one of the two penitent saints named in the title, Jerome or Mary Magdalene. The museum's record gives no description of the figure, pose or landscape, so the wing can be described only by its place in the whole: a flanking saint beside the central Crucifixion, balancing the wing on the other side.","i":"Saint Jerome, who lived as a hermit in the desert and translated the Bible into Latin, is shown with a stone with which he beat his breast, with a lion or with a cardinal's hat. Mary Magdalene, who stood at the cross and was the first to see the risen Christ, is shown with her jar of ointment. Both saints were models of penitence, and the pairing suggests that the triptych was meant to inspire repentance and gratitude for Christ's sacrifice.","c":"Perugino's side figures are among the most graceful parts of his early altarpieces: the contour is fluid, the gesture restrained and the head tilted in his characteristic way. The record gives no description of this wing, so the verdict is general. The risk is monotony, since the same gentle manner serves every saint, but in a narrow panel the elegance of line is a real virtue."},"nga:30":{"d":"The central panel of a triptych, about 102 by 57 centimeters, painted in oil on panel and later transferred to canvas, about 1482 to 1485. It shows the Crucifixion with the Virgin and Saint John, as the title says. The museum's record gives no description of the composition, so the panel can be described only by subject and type: Christ on the cross at the center, with his mother and the beloved disciple as the two mourning witnesses at its foot, and in Perugino's usual way probably an open landscape beyond.","i":"At the foot of the cross the Virgin and Saint John, the two figures named in the Gospel of John as standing beside it, embody the grief of the faithful, and the viewer is meant to join their compassion. The wings of the triptych carry two penitent saints, Jerome and Mary Magdalene, so the whole ensemble was a meditation on sin, repentance and Christ's sacrifice. Triptychs of this kind were made as devotional altarpieces or for private chapels.","c":"Perugino's Crucifixions are remembered for their calm symmetry, luminous skies and the delicate, sorrowing grace of their figures, and this early example already has the clarity of design that made him famous. The record gives no description of the panel, so the verdict is general. His restraint can slip into formula, with the same tilted heads and gentle emotion in picture after picture, but at this date the manner was still fresh and carefully felt."},"nga:31":{"d":"The right wing of a triptych of the Crucifixion, a tall narrow panel about 95 by 30 centimeters, painted in oil on panel and later transferred to canvas, about 1482 to 1485. It carries one of the two penitent saints named in the title, Jerome or Mary Magdalene. The museum's record gives no description of the figure, pose or landscape, so the wing can be described only by its place in the whole: a flanking saint beside the central Crucifixion.","i":"Wings in a triptych were often hinged so that they could be closed over the central scene, and the saints on them stood as companions and witnesses to the event at the center. Jerome and Mary Magdalene are easily told apart: Jerome, the desert hermit and translator of the Bible, is shown with a stone, a lion or a cardinal's hat, and Mary Magdalene with a jar of ointment. Both were models of repentance.","c":"A narrow wing is a severe test of design, since a single figure must hold a tall, thin field and still relate to the picture beside it. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. Perugino was adept at such elegant, upright figures, with gentle contour and a calm gesture, and the wing is best judged as part of the harmony of the whole triptych."},"nga:1177":{"d":"A small portrait of the painter Lorenzo di Credi, painted in oil on panel in 1488 and later transferred to canvas, about 46 by 32 centimeters. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's features, dress or background, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-sized likeness of a fellow artist by Perugino, who is better known for altarpieces, and a rare example of his work as a portraitist.","i":"Lorenzo di Credi, born in the 1450s and died in 1537, was a Florentine painter trained in the workshop of Verrocchio, which Perugino is also said to have attended. A portrait of one artist by another could mark friendship or professional respect, and may have been a gift or an exchange between colleagues. The record does not say what the picture's original purpose was, so this reading is only a possibility.","c":"Portraits by Perugino are rare, and this one is valued for what it shows of his observation when the subject was a colleague rather than a type: a clear, gentle likeness with the soft modeling common in Florentine workshops of the 1480s. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. It is a small picture but important evidence of the culture of the workshop."},"nga:356":{"d":"A devotional picture of the Virgin and Child, painted in oil on a poplar panel, about 70 by 50 centimeters, around 1500. The museum's record gives no description of the poses, costume or landscape, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length Madonna and Child of the kind Perugino and his large workshop produced in quantity for churches and private houses, with the mother and son placed close to the viewer on a panel made for prayer.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the commonest image of Renaissance devotion, placed in bedrooms and chapels as a focus for prayer and a pattern of motherhood. Perugino's version of the subject, with its gentle expression and tilted head, was widely imitated, and the young Raphael learned much from it. The record gives no information about the original owner or setting, so its devotional use is a matter of type.","c":"Perugino's Madonnas set a standard of sweetness for Umbria and Florence, with serene faces, soft light and an airy spaciousness. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. Critics have long noted that the formula, repeated for a large market, can turn bland, and that Raphael gave the same type more life. Around 1500, however, the manner was at its height, and this is a representative example."},"met:437272":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 27 by 46 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood and undated in the record. The keywords list Christ, soldiers and men, and the subject is the Resurrection. The picture can be described only by its type and format: a compact narrative scene of the kind that formed part of a predella, the row of small scenes beneath the main panel of an altarpiece, in which the risen Christ is usually shown rising from the tomb among the guards.","i":"In the Gospels, Christ rose from the tomb on the third day, while the soldiers set to guard it were overcome with fear. Renaissance painters typically showed him stepping out of the sarcophagus with a banner of victory and the guards asleep or thrown to the ground. A predella scene allowed an altarpiece to tell a sequence of events, and the Resurrection, as the central event of Christian faith, often appeared among them.","c":"Perugino's Resurrection altarpiece in the Vatican is the best-known version of the subject, and a small panel of this kind shows the same lucid arrangement at a reduced scale. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. Predella scenes are often more spontaneous than the main panels, and tempera gives a crisp, jewel-like surface, though the compact format limits what the figures can express."},"met:437273":{"d":"A tall panel, about 160 by 67 centimeters, painted in oil, with a query, on wood and undated in the record. The title names two saints, John the Baptist and Saint Lucy, and the keywords list a man and a woman. The record gives no description of the figures or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: probably the tall, narrow format of a side panel in a larger altarpiece or polyptych, in which a single standing saint was shown on each wing.","i":"John the Baptist is usually shown in a rough camel-hair garment, often with a reed cross, a lamb or a scroll that proclaims Christ as the Lamb of God. Lucy, a martyr of Syracuse, is usually shown holding a palm and a dish with her eyes, an allusion to the legend that her eyes were torn out and that she was a protector of sight. Altarpieces often paired such saints as patrons of the church or of the donor and his family.","c":"Single standing saints give a painter of Perugino's type the chance to show what he did best: graceful contour, quiet gesture and a calm, spacious setting. The record gives no description of this panel, and notes the oil medium only with a query, so the verdict is general. Figures of this height can look elegant but static, and the success of a side panel depends on the harmony of the whole altarpiece, which cannot be judged from one part."},"met:496203":{"d":"A tall, very narrow panel, about 85 by 30 centimeters, painted in oil about 1895. Its title and keywords give a woman standing beside a balustrade, with a poodle, and the record gives no further description of her dress, pose or setting. The picture can be described only by its subject and format: a decorative figure panel of a kind Paul Ranson made in the 1890s, when he belonged to the Nabis, the Paris group of painters devoted to flat color, bold pattern and decoration.","i":"The narrow vertical shape echoes the format of Japanese hanging scrolls, which the Nabis admired, and shows their wish to make paintings part of the decoration of a room, to hang as panels like tapestries or screens. The name Nabis comes from the Hebrew word for prophets. They favored subjects of quiet domestic and symbolic mood. The record does not describe the woman or the poodle, so no further meaning can be stated.","c":"Among the Nabis, Ranson is the one closest to Art Nouveau, with a taste for sinuous line and rhythmic pattern, and a panel of this proportion suits that gift. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is general. His work is less famous than that of Bonnard or Vuillard, and sometimes more mannered, but the best of his decorative panels have a lively linear grace and a modern elegance."},"met:436876":{"d":"Marion Lenbach (1892–1947), the painter's daughter, is shown in a large portrait of about 150 by 105 cm, dated 1900, when she was about eight. Franz von Lenbach was the most sought-after portraitist in Germany, painter of Bismarck, emperors and popes, and here he turns the Old Master manner he cultivated to a child, in a canvas large enough to give her a presence beside his famous sitters.","i":"A father's portrait of his child is both private and public. Lenbach was a celebrity of Munich society, and the villa he built there, now the Lenbachhaus, was a showcase of his art and his family. Pictures of his daughters show a tenderness unusual in his official portraits of statesmen. The warm, brown tonality he borrowed from Titian, Rubens and Velázquez dignifies the child as a worthy subject for the grand tradition.","c":"Lenbach was a master of the flattering Old Master pastiche: warm brown tones and a face glowing out of shadow. In a portrait of a child, the formula yields something tender, and the large size gives it some pomp. His weaknesses, widely noted, are a slickness and a reliance on photographic aids, and much of his later work repeats itself. Family portraits like this are among his more intimate and persuasive pictures."},"met:437885":{"d":"A Roman triumphal procession stretches across this very wide canvas, about 130 by 438 centimeters, dated 1789. The museum's keywords record horses, soldiers, armor, a king, a crowd and a chariot, and name among the figures the victorious Roman general Lucius Aemilius Paullus Macedonicus and his captive, King Perseus of Macedon, so the arrangement of the cortege and the colors are left unstated. The long format suits the subject, a procession seen like an ancient frieze.","i":"In 167 BC the Roman general Aemilius Paulus celebrated a triumph after defeating Perseus, the last king of Macedon, at Pydna; according to Plutarch, the procession lasted three days and displayed captured statues, paintings and weapons, with the defeated king walking before the chariot. Vernet, son of the marine painter Joseph Vernet, was known for horses, and the subject let him combine antique erudition with his specialty. Roman subjects were fashionable in France just before the Revolution.","c":"Vernet's frieze is neoclassical in subject and clear in arrangement, with horses drawn with the sureness one expects from the best horse painter of France. The parade moves, the crowd is varied, and the length of the canvas is handled with rhythm. It is more an exercise in antiquarian display than a drama; the passion of David's Roman subjects is missing. As a showcase of skill and of the taste of 1789, it is lively."},"met:438779":{"d":"A peasant family is the subject of this small oil on copper, about 38 by 30 centimeters, painted between about 1640 and 1648 by Antoine Le Nain. The museum's keywords list a man, a boy, a girl and a cat: a household gathered in the quiet manner of the Le Nain brothers, who painted rural people with unusual seriousness. The copper support gives a smooth, hard surface that suits the fine detail of a cabinet picture.","i":"The Le Nain brothers of Laon painted French peasants in the 1640s with a dignity unlike the comic or rowdy peasants of Flemish and Dutch art: their figures gaze out silently, in plain rooms, in muted browns and grays. Whether the pictures express sympathetic observation, Counter-Reformation piety or Parisian collectors' taste for rustic simplicity is debated. The painting presents a household as a group of individuals.","c":"Antoine Le Nain's small coppers have a hushed gravity: figures set in a close group, restrained color, a quiet light and a sense of shared silence. The pleasure is in the lack of anecdote and the concentrated attention each face receives. There is no moralizing and no sentiment, which is why the Le Nains were rediscovered in the nineteenth century by Realists such as Champfleury and Courbet."},"nga:75030":{"d":"A mound of creamy yellow butter sits left of center on a wooden table against an olive-green background. A white cloth, presumably the wrapper, is pushed down around its base, and the top is marked with gouges and furrows. A long narrow wooden paddle stands upright in the right side of the mound, sunk almost to its handle. Two white eggs rest on the table in front of the cloth. Loose brushstrokes are visible throughout, so the swipes of paint seem almost to become the butter. It is signed in black at the lower left: A. Vollon.","i":"Butter, eggs and the plain tools of the dairy are among the humblest of subjects, and French painters from Chardin onward gave them dignity by looking closely and painting with love. Vollon belonged to the tradition of the French kitchen still life, admired by collectors for its robust, physical paint. The picture's real subject is the likeness between butter and oil paint, both soft, creamy substances that take the mark of a tool.","c":"A deliberately ordinary subject treated with relish. The butter is built from thick, juicy strokes, the cloth from cooler whites, and the olive ground sets off the yellow with great economy. Vollon's bravura can verge on showing off, and his still lifes lack Chardin's stillness, but this picture earns its place by its honest delight in the stuff of paint. Few painters have made a lump of butter so persuasive."},"met:437916":{"d":"Cheese is the subject of this large still life, about 85 by 90 centimeters, painted probably in the late 1870s by Antoine Vollon. The recorded motifs are the cheese and the still life itself. Vollon was a master of the kitchen and market still life, known for thick, buttery paint that seems to match the textures it describes, and the almost square canvas gives the cheese the dignity of a full composition.","i":"Kitchen and market still lifes were hugely popular in France under the Second Empire and the Third Republic, appealing to bourgeois buyers who liked pictures of food and the good life and echoing Chardin's eighteenth-century tradition. Vollon, a native of Lyon, a city proud of its cuisine, was among the most successful practitioners and was widely collected by Americans. Cheese, a staple of the French table with strong regional character, suits a picture of sensuous appetite.","c":"Vollon's handling is the point: he lays on paint as thick and creamy as the subject and makes a mundane object into a study of texture and light. The risk of such bravura is that technique outweighs thought, and his still lifes can seem like exhibitions of dexterity. At his best, the density of his paint recalls Chardin and its exuberance recalls Courbet. This is a confident, appetizing picture."},"met:440888":{"d":"A window is the sole subject of this small oil study, about 37 by 25 centimeters, painted on paper in 1856 by the German artist Anton Dieffenbach and later laid down on canvas. Pictures of windows, with light entering and a view of the outside, were a favorite motif of German Romantic and Biedermeier artists, who used the window as a threshold between the inner and the outer world.","i":"From Caspar David Friedrich to Adolph Menzel, German painters found in the window a motif that joined interior and exterior, domestic quiet and longing for distance. Small studies on paper like this were often made as exercises in light and perspective, not for sale; they were the training ground of the realist tendency of the 1850s, interested in the plain facts of seeing. The choice of so modest a subject shows confidence in the interest of ordinary things.","c":"As an exercise it is sensible and fresh: the plain subject lets the painter concentrate on the fall of light and the geometry of the frame. In 1856 it belongs to the realist moment in German art, when oil sketches by Menzel and others claimed independence. If it lacks the poetry of Friedrich's windows, it has the honesty of a study made to see how light works."},"nga:50255":{"d":"Four figures meet on a wide landscape. At the left a man in a red cloak, yellow tunic and a helmet with a blue plume holds a long wooden rod and looks to the right. Beside him another, in white and blue with a blue helmet, carries a large round silver shield with a gold rim. Beyond a boulder and two small trees, two nearly nude women with long braided hair stand and sit by a blue pool, with a bench of fruit and a pitcher near them. A small black dog stands between the groups, and a white castle with a golden dome crowns a hill at the right.","i":"The subject comes from Tasso's epic Jerusalem Delivered. The knights Carlo and Ubaldo, sent to bring the hero Rinaldo back from the enchantress Armida's island, must pass the temptations of her nymphs, who bathe and offer fruit. The wand and the shield are the magical aids given to the knights, whose steady advance shows duty overcoming pleasure. At more than four meters wide, the canvas was probably made for a wall in a large room.","c":"A large, light and elegant decorative canvas in the Venetian rococo, with the silvery color and fluent, slightly mannered drawing of Gian Antonio Guardi, who was more at home with graceful figures than with grand drama. The nymphs are charming, the knights somewhat stiff, and the landscape is airy and delightfully unreal. As the work of the elder Guardi, possibly with his brother Francesco, it is more decoration than epic, but a delightful one."},"nga:206777":{"d":"Two figures sit outdoors on either side of a gray rock. At the left a woman with long golden hair and draped clothes in lilac and teal rests her arm on the rock and holds a tall, thin cross. At the right an angel with white wings, short curly brown hair and a red and gold garment over a long white robe leans toward her. A small chalice and a closed book lie on the ground between them. Dark trees rise at the left, and green and blue hills roll away under a bright blue sky. The panel is dated 1626.","i":"The woman is Mary Magdalene, shown in the wilderness as a penitent, with a cross to meditate on. According to a medieval legend she spent her last years as a hermit in Provence, where angels brought her comfort. The chalice and closed book may suggest the Eucharist and the Scriptures, the sources of her consolation. The subject allowed painters to combine a graceful female figure with a message of repentance, which Counter-Reformation taste valued.","c":"Vignon was a restless, colorful painter who absorbed Caravaggio's naturalism and Elsheimer's small-scale landscapes in Rome before returning to France. Here the bright colors, the quick brushwork of the foliage and the intimate bond between woman and angel are all appealing, and the scale of the panel suits the subject. The drawing of the figures is a little mannered, and the emotion is calmer than the subject demands, but the picture is vivid and unusual."},"met:437040":{"d":"A woman and cattle share a wide landscape in this canvas of about 61 by 101 centimeters, painted around 1880 by the Dutch artist Anton Mauve. The title refers to the farm routine of moving livestock to fresh pasture, and the recorded motifs are the woman, the cattle and the landscape. The horizontal format gives space to the open Dutch skies and fields that Hague School painters loved.","i":"Mauve belonged to the Hague School, a group of Dutch painters who turned from grand subjects to the modest scenery and workers of their own country, painting in soft gray and silvery tones. Vincent van Gogh, Mauve's cousin by marriage, took painting lessons from him in 1881 and 1882. The theme of cattle moved to fresh grazing is an image of the seasonal rhythm of rural work, treated without sentiment or drama.","c":"Mauve's landscapes are admired for their tonal subtlety: the silvery light, the soft grays and greens, and the way figures and animals sit within the atmosphere instead of standing against it. The tenderness for the quiet life of the land is genuine, even if it can seem sentimental to modern taste. This canvas shows his mature manner, spacious, quiet and unemphatic, which Van Gogh admired and later outgrew."},"met:437041":{"d":"A man works with a horse and a cart gathering wood in this canvas of about 42 by 33 centimeters by Anton Mauve. The recorded motifs, a horse, a man, a cart and work, describe a scene of rural labor set in a landscape. The museum gives no date. Wood gathering was an everyday task of country life, and Mauve, like his Hague School colleagues, found in such unremarkable work a subject worthy of a painting.","i":"Gathering firewood and brushwood was a seasonal task in the heath and woodland villages of the Dutch countryside, and Mauve spent his last years at Laren in the Gooi, where an artists' colony took root and such scenes surrounded him. The subject reflects the Hague School's respect for rural labor, shown without hardship or sentiment. A man, horse and cart in a landscape also allowed the painter to study the harmony of figure, animal and place that was central to his art.","c":"The vertical format is less usual for Mauve, whose wide pastoral panoramas are better known, and it gives the cart and horse a close, concentrated presence. As so often with him the interest is in tone: soft browns and grays that bind figure and ground. The subject is workaday and the treatment quiet, without the heightened feeling Millet gave to peasant work, which is both its modesty and its limit."},"met:437042":{"d":"A shepherd with a dog brings his sheep back to the fold in this canvas of about 50 by 86 centimeters by Anton Mauve. The recorded motifs are the sheep, the shepherd, a man, a dog and a landscape, and the title suggests the end of the day, when the flock is gathered in. The museum gives no date. Mauve painted sheep and shepherds throughout his career, and they are the subject for which he is best known.","i":"The shepherd and his flock are among the oldest images of pastoral life, carrying echoes of the Good Shepherd of the Gospels, but Mauve stripped the theme of sentimental or religious framing and showed it as daily labor in a wide, damp northern landscape. The Hague School admired Barbizon painters like Millet and Jacque, who painted shepherds and sheep, and Mauve's pictures were widely collected in the Netherlands and abroad, especially in the United States.","c":"Mauve's flocks, a soft mass of grays and creams set against a pale sky, are among the best-loved images of Dutch nineteenth-century painting, and the reason is tone, the unity of sky, ground and animals in a single light. The shepherd and dog are small, secondary notes, and the flock carries the picture. The sweetness of the subject can verge on sentiment, but his restraint keeps it within bounds."},"met:437043":{"d":"A shepherdess watches her flock in this canvas of about 45 by 64 centimeters by Anton Mauve. The recorded motifs are a woman, sheep, a dog and a shepherd, so the scene shows the daily vigil of a flock-keeper with her dog in the open country. The museum gives no date. The subject recalls Jean-François Millet's famous shepherdesses, which Mauve and other painters of the Hague School admired.","i":"The shepherdess is an old pastoral figure, once an elegant fantasy of Rococo court painters; by the mid-nineteenth century Millet and the Barbizon painters had made her a working peasant, patient and solitary, in the open air. Mauve, who learned from the Barbizon painters, kept that realism: she is a laborer minding a flock, not an ornament. The soft, silvery light is his own and carries the Hague School's feeling for the damp northern atmosphere.","c":"The color is characteristically muted, gray-greens, creamy fleece and a pale sky, and the unity of tone is the picture's strength. Mauve's weakness is repetition: he painted sheep so often that individual canvases can blur, and the sweetness can verge on formula. Still, the softness of his touch and the consistency of his light give pictures like this a quiet, reliable pleasure."},"met:437044":{"d":"A shepherd and sheep stand near a tree in the fading light of this upright canvas, about 66 by 45 centimeters, by Anton Mauve. The title names the hour, twilight, and the recorded motifs are a tree, sheep and a shepherd. The museum gives no date. Mauve painted many evening and dusk effects, in which the light is low, the colors gather into grays and golds and the shapes soften.","i":"Twilight, the hour between day and night, was a favorite theme for Romantic and Barbizon painters, and for the Hague School it offered subtle gradations of gray and gold in the big sky of a flat country. A shepherd bringing in the flock at the end of day adds a note of rest and the completion of labor. Mauve, who died at forty-nine, had a special feeling for such quiet endings.","c":"Dusk is where tonal painters show their skill, and Mauve is among the best at it: the lights are subtle, the darks transparent, and the sheep and shepherd are given to the dimming light without losing their forms. The mood is elegiac, and the risk is a certain monotony of gray. But the best of his twilight pictures have a gravity that makes them more than pretty pastoral."},"nga:52245":{"d":"A group of six children hold hands and play in a grassy area, forming a line in which one child ducks under the joined arms of two others. Their faces show joy, with wide eyes and open, upturned mouths. Three wear pastel dresses in pink, green and blue, and the other three wear jackets and trousers. The background is a lush green landscape with dappled light falling from above. The picture is small, about 33 by 41 centimeters, and was painted around 1860.","i":"Pictures of children at play were popular in nineteenth-century France, where a growing middle class valued childhood as a time of innocence and wanted charming scenes for the home. The game of passing under joined arms is a familiar one, and the picture records a moment of play that viewers recognized from their own youth. The record does not say where the park is or who the children are.","c":"A charming, lightly sentimental sketch of childhood, painted with fresh color and a pleasing flicker of sunlight through leaves. Merle was a skilled academic painter, better known for sweeter, more finished mother-and-child pictures, and this small canvas has more spontaneity than most of them. Its limits are those of the genre: the children are types, with cheerful, similar faces, and the delight is more charming than deep."},"met:437069":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 175 by 110 centimeters, dated 1872, in which an allegory of autumn is built, according to the museum's keywords, from falling leaves, a child and a female nude. The record gives no further description of the poses, setting or colors, so the picture can be described only by its type: an academic allegorical subject of the kind exhibited at the Paris Salon, in which a season is embodied in human figures rather than in landscape.","i":"Allegories of the seasons were a standard theme of academic art, and autumn is usually the season of harvest and decline, a reminder that life, like the year, turns toward its end. Falling leaves are an obvious image of mortality and change, and the presence of a child alongside a nude figure suggests the cycle of generations. The record does not say how the artist intended this combination, so this is only a likely reading.","c":"Merle was a polished academic painter, and the figures here are smoothly modeled and gracefully arranged. The weakness is the weakness of the school: the allegory is more decorative than felt, the sentiment is sweet rather than poignant, and the idealized nude has little of the fresh observation that Courbet and Manet were demanding in the same years. As a document of official taste in 1872 it is clear and competent."},"cma:146724":{"d":"A small horizontal panel, about 28 by 45 centimeters, painted about 1624 to 1628. It shows the moment after the Last Supper when the apostle Peter, at the left, denies knowing Jesus to protect himself. The museum notes the dramatic light, the twisting bodies, the startlingly low viewpoint and the tight detailing, which combine two styles: the light of the Caravaggesque painters, then in fashion in the Netherlands, and a more refined, courtly manner popular earlier in the century.","i":"In the Gospels, Peter had promised to stay with Jesus to the death, but when challenged in the courtyard of the high priest he denied three times that he knew him, and wept when a cock crowed, as Christ had foretold. The subject, popular with Utrecht painters in the 1620s, shows ordinary human weakness and the possibility of forgiveness. The painter, Peter Wtewael, was the son of the Utrecht Mannerist Joachim Wtewael.","c":"Few works by Peter Wtewael are known, and this small panel is a fascinating hybrid: the heightened lighting of the Caravaggisti sits on a father's tight, elegant Mannerist drawing. The mixture is not always comfortable, since the twisting poses fight the dramatic light, but the low viewpoint and nocturnal intensity give the scene real urgency. It is best seen as an interesting experiment by a little-known artist at a moment of transition."},"met:437956":{"d":"A large wide canvas, about 114 by 160 centimeters, painted in the 1620s. The museum's keywords list a woman and a man, a kitchen, meat, food, eggs and a domestic worker, but the record gives no description of the poses, colors or any background scene. The picture can be described only by its type: a kitchen piece, a Netherlandish genre showing an abundance of food and the people who prepare it, painted by Peter Wtewael, son of the Utrecht painter Joachim Wtewael.","i":"Kitchen scenes with heaps of meat, fish, vegetables and eggs were a Netherlandish specialty from the sixteenth century, when Pieter Aertsen and Joachim Beuckelaer made them an art form. They celebrated the abundance of the Low Countries, and could carry moral overtones about appetite and excess, with eggs and meat sometimes read as hints of sensuality. The record does not say whether this one has such a message.","c":"The kitchen scene gave a painter the chance to show off still-life skill and the physical presence of food, and Wtewael inherits that tradition. The record gives no description of the canvas, so the verdict is general. His work is rare and uneven, with a taste for sharp detail from his father's Mannerism, and this large canvas is of interest chiefly as a late echo of a sixteenth-century genre in the age of Dutch realism."},"nga:34641":{"d":"A dark cathedral interior with grand Gothic architecture: high vaulted ceilings, arches and columns, and a checkered floor leading to a distant altar near the center. Light enters through arched windows on the left and picks out patches of wall, floor and ceiling. Several people walk, sit or kneel by smaller altars, some in colorful and intricate clothing, others in plain dark dress. It is painted in oil on a copper plate, about 47 by 59 centimeters, around 1625 to 1630.","i":"Peeter Neeffs worked in Antwerp, where the great church interiors were a source of civic and religious pride, and his pictures were made for collectors who admired the grandeur of Gothic space and the skill of perspective. The small figures give scale and show the church in use, with worshippers at side altars, which in Catholic Flanders were still active centers of devotion. The record does not name the church, and Neeffs often combined real and invented features.","c":"Neeffs was a specialist, and his strengths are plain here: convincing recession of arches toward the altar, delicate gradations of light on stone, and tiny figures placed with a sure touch. The copper support gives a smooth, enamel-like finish that suits fine detail. The coloring is limited and the formula was repeated through his career, but the effect of silence and height is well managed, and the picture rewards close looking."},"met:437187":{"d":"A tiny view of the interior of a Gothic church by day, painted in oil on a copper plate only about 13 by 16.5 centimeters, probably between 1635 and 1640. The keywords list a church building and a human figure, but the museum's record gives no further description. The picture can be described only by its type: a miniature-scale church interior, meant to be viewed closely, which forms a pair with a companion showing the same kind of interior at night.","i":"Pairs of day and night views of church interiors were a Flemish specialty, since the contrast showed off the painter's range: by day, light enters through the windows and defines the architecture, while by night candles and lanterns create pools of warm light in the darkness. The pair was probably made as a cabinet picture to be examined from close range, and appealed to collectors who liked both architecture and effects of illumination.","c":"At this size the architectural drawing has to be extremely precise, and Neeffs was a specialist at exactly this kind of exactness. The record gives no description of the figures, which at this scale are mere touches of color. The small day view is neat rather than dramatic, and its main charm is the pairing with the night scene, where the painter's invention is likely to be more striking."},"met:437188":{"d":"A tiny view of the interior of a Gothic church at night, painted in oil on a copper plate only about 13 by 16.5 centimeters, probably between 1635 and 1640. The keywords list the church building, an interior, a man, a woman and the night, but the record gives no further description. The picture can be described only by its type: a miniature-scale nocturnal interior, in which the light must come from candles, lamps or lanterns, and which forms a pair with a companion showing the church by day.","i":"A church at night may suggest a vigil, a procession or a private visit, with the figures carrying a light through the dark nave. Painters of nocturnal church interiors enjoyed the problem of how artificial light picks out columns and arches while the vaults sink into shadow, and collectors admired the technical bravura. The man and the woman in the keywords give the picture a human story, which the record does not describe.","c":"The night view is likely to be the more striking of the pair, since the problem of lighting a dark space with small flames suits Neeffs's delicate control of tone. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. The effect, in a miniature on copper, is of a glowing jewel, and the contrast with its daylight companion shows how much expressive range a specialist could find in one subject."},"nga:46053":{"d":"The interior of a Gothic cathedral, with tall cream-colored arches and vaulted, ribbed ceilings. Framed pictures hang above wooden pulpits with lit candles, and dark gray statues stand high on the walls. A pale blue sky shows through tall windows, and the black and white checkered floor is broken here and there by rectangles of gray and white. Men, women and children in vibrant clothing walk across the floor or kneel by altars. Painted on copper, it measures only about 15 by 23 centimeters.","i":"The cathedral of Our Lady in Antwerp was the greatest church of the Spanish Netherlands and a favorite subject for Antwerp painters. It held altarpieces by Rubens, and in Catholic Flanders its altars were still places of daily devotion, shown here with kneeling worshippers. Pictures like this appealed to citizens and visitors as a souvenir of civic and religious pride, and their small scale and copper support made them suitable for a collector's cabinet.","c":"A precisely drawn little picture, in which the cream-colored stone, the ribbed vaults and the checkered floor lead the eye in a convincing perspective. The sparkle of tiny candle flames and brightly dressed figures adds life to the pale architecture. Neeffs repeated the type often, and his Antwerp views can be dry, but the control of space at this tiny scale is impressive. At about 15 by 23 centimeters it is a model of jewel-like exactness."},"nga:43450":{"d":"A man is seated and faces forward, his body angled slightly right, in a half-length portrait of about 75 by 60 centimeters. He has light skin, blue eyes and light brown, upward-styled hair, and he looks directly at the viewer with a faintly serious expression. He wears a dark coat over a high-collared white shirt with horizontal pleats and a cravat-like neckcloth. In his left hand he holds a small vial of red liquid, and a wedding band shows on his right hand. His chair is red with a scrolled armrest, and the dark brown background lightens around the figure.","i":"The vial is the clue to the sitter's identity, since men were often shown with the tools of their trade; it suggests a physician, druggist or dealer in remedies, though the title says only that he holds a vial. The wedding ring and the companion portrait of his wife show a married couple recorded as a pair. Erastus Salisbury Field, only in his early twenties, was traveling the Connecticut River valley and painting such pairs for local families.","c":"Even in this early portrait Field is sure of his main device: the figure placed squarely, lit from the front, against a dark ground that lightens behind the head like a halo. The face is drawn with care though flatly, and the pleats of the white shirt are rendered with charming precision. The red vial is a bright spot that animates the composition. It is plain, direct and effective, as good folk portraiture usually is."},"nga:43451":{"d":"A woman sits in a red chair, facing the viewer, in a half-length portrait of about 75 by 60 centimeters. Her fair face is framed by dark curls and a white bonnet with a green and orange ribbon. She wears a dark, patterned dress with long sleeves and a white lace collar, with pearls around her neck and a circular brooch at the center of her collar. The background grades from dark to light behind her head.","i":"She is the companion of the man with the vial, and the pair, painted about 1827, would have hung together in their home. The white indoor bonnet, lace collar, pearls and brooch show a woman of means who also observed the modesty expected of a married woman. Such portraits recorded a couple's place in a small town, and they were among the few images of ordinary people available before photography.","c":"Field's strength here is in color and pattern: the touch of orange and green in the ribbon, the white of the lace and the black of the dress. The face is broad and solid, with the simplified drawing of a self-taught painter, and its calm directness is its best quality. As half of a pair, it completes the set well, though it is a quieter picture than its companion."},"nga:52949":{"d":"A man sits and rests his right arm on the arm of a chair in this half-length portrait of 1827, about 76 by 59 centimeters. He has fair skin and dark curly hair, and his expression is composed. He wears a white shirt, a vest striped in red, white and green or blue, and a dark jacket. The background is a blend of browns. The unusual name in the title is the only information given about the sitter.","i":"Field was about twenty-two when he painted this, and the portrait comes from the early years when he traveled the towns of western Massachusetts offering likenesses to farmers and tradesmen. The striped vest was fashionable, and the casual pose, with an arm resting on the chair, gave the sitter an air of ease. Such portraits were a source of family pride, hung in a parlor and passed down.","c":"The vest is the star: the stripes of red, white and green or blue give the picture a lively pattern that the plain brown ground sets off. The face is solid and the pose conventional, and the sitter's composure is convincing. Folk painters often succeed more with textiles than with anatomy, and this portrait is an example of that gift."},"nga:59900":{"d":"A woman is portrayed from the waist up, seated upright on a wooden chair and holding a book in her left hand. She has blue eyes and dark brown hair styled in rolls with a braided crown, and she wears a lace collar, a blue-gray garment and gold earrings. The background is plain. The oil on canvas measures about 76 by 61 centimeters and dates from around 1835.","i":"A book in a woman's hand marks literacy and piety, qualities that parents and husbands wanted a portrait to record. The upswept hair with rolls and a braided crown is the fashionable style of the 1830s, and the lace collar and earrings show that she dressed with care. No name accompanies the picture, so the woman is known by what she holds.","c":"Field's women are often more striking than his men, and here the blue eyes and the clear, simplified face stand out against the plain ground. The blue-gray dress is handled in broad, flat tones, and the book anchors the pose. It is a modest and pleasing portrait whose strength is its calm and its clarity."},"nga:50300":{"d":"A man sits with his hands clasped in front of him in this half-length portrait of about 90 by 74 centimeters, painted around 1837. He has a slightly stern expression, deep-set eyes, light bushy eyebrows and short, neatly combed white hair. He wears a black coat, a buttoned vest, a high-collared white shirt and an intricate cravat. The background is dark and uniform, and a bit of a red chair shows behind him.","i":"Clasped hands are a pose of composure and self-possession, and with the sober black clothing they present the sitter as a man of settled position. The title gives only his surname, but the companion portrait names Mrs. Harlow A. Pease, so he is presumably Harlow A. Pease, painted with his wife as a pair. Field made many such pairs in the 1830s for the families of the Connecticut River valley.","c":"The white hair and bushy brows are painted with care and give the face a vivid character, and the black coat forms a strong mass against the dark ground. The stiffness of the clasped hands is typical of Field's honest, direct approach. It is a plain but impressive portrait of old age, with no flattery."},"nga:50301":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, facing forward, with her hands resting on a book held upright. Her expression is composed, her skin light, her lips defined and her cheekbones prominent. Her hair is in a neat updo under a lace-trimmed bonnet, and she wears a dark, full-sleeved dress with a lace collar and a pin at the center. The background is a muted brown. The canvas, painted around 1837, measures about 90 by 74 centimeters.","i":"Mrs. Pease is the companion of the portrait titled Mr. Pease, and the two were designed to hang together. The upright book is a sign of piety and education, and the dark dress with its lace collar and pin is the respectable costume of a mature married woman. The title uses her husband's full name, as was the custom, and so identifies her through him.","c":"The dark dress against the brown ground is a rather somber harmony, relieved by the lace and bonnet, and Field concentrates on the strong, simplified face. The book acts as a visual anchor. It is not a flattering portrait, but it is a dignified one, and next to her husband's it makes a quietly impressive pair."},"nga:52950":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, facing slightly to the right, with his left arm draped over the back of a red chair and his right hand resting on his lap. He is fair, clean-shaven, with prominent cheekbones and deep-set eyes, and his short light brown hair is slightly tousled. He wears a black coat, a black waistcoat, a white high-collared shirt and a black cravat against a muted dark brown ground. The canvas measures about 86 by 73 centimeters.","i":"The arm draped over the chair back is a pose of easy confidence borrowed from fashionable portraiture, and it relaxes the formality of the black clothing. This is the companion of the portrait of Mrs. Paul Smith Palmer and her twins, painted between 1835 and 1838, and together they record a young family. The black suit was standard dress for a man of business or the professions.","c":"Field gives the sitter a convincing presence: the angled body, the casual arm and the direct face make a vivid, unforced image. The black clothing merges with the dark ground, leaving the face and the red chair as the main color accents. It is a straightforward, likeable portrait, and a good example of how Field made the formal pose seem natural."},"nga:52951":{"d":"A mother and her twin children are the subject of this portrait of 1835 to 1838, an oil on canvas of about 98 by 86 centimeters. The title names the mother as Mrs. Paul Smith Palmer, and the twins are not named. Adding two children to a parent's portrait makes the picture larger and more complex than a single-sitter likeness, and the size suggests a family of some means.","i":"Portraits of a mother with her children were pictures of motherhood and family continuity, and in an age when many children died young they were a precious record. Twin births were noteworthy and perhaps especially worth recording. The companion portrait of her husband, Paul Smith Palmer, was painted at the same time, so the pair would have hung together in the family's house.","c":"Group portraits are the hardest test for a self-taught painter, since they demand a convincing arrangement of several bodies. Field simplifies, uses clear contours and bright color, and finds an order that is pleasing if flat. The picture is more affecting for its theme than for its drawing, and in the best folk tradition it is moving because it is so plainly meant."},"nga:56733":{"d":"A man sits in a portrait pose holding an open book of sheet music. He wears a dark suit with a white shirt and a black bow tie, his hair is neatly styled, and his complexion is fair, with an attentive expression. Behind him are a red curtain and a landscape with a river or lake, rocks and greenery. The oil on canvas, about 89 by 74 centimeters, dates from around 1838. The title gives the sitter's name only as possibly Mr. Cook.","i":"A tune book was the tool of a singing master or a church singer. In early nineteenth-century New England, singing schools taught congregations to read music and sing hymns in parts, and the book identified a man active in that musical life. The red curtain and the landscape are conventions borrowed from grander portraits, which a modest sitter could afford in a village painter's version.","c":"The sheet music gives the picture a clear subject and the sitter an air of attentive pleasure, which sets it above many portraits of its type. The curtain and the landscape view are painted with a charming naive formality. It is not a profound likeness, but it conveys the community pride of a local musician, and the pleasing detail makes it memorable."},"met:10880":{"d":"A girl holds or stands beside a book in this tall portrait, about 149 by 77 centimeters, painted around 1848. The very upright canvas suggests a standing child shown at a large scale. Erastus Salisbury Field painted her in his typical manner, with firm outlines and bright, flat areas of color. The sitter belonged to the Bangs-Phelps family, but her individual name is not given in the title.","i":"Field worked as an itinerant portraitist in Massachusetts and neighboring states. After a short period of study with Samuel F. B. Morse in New York in the 1820s, he returned to the Connecticut River valley to paint the faces of farmers, merchants and their children. The book signals literacy and moral education, qualities parents wished to record in a daughter's portrait. By 1848 the daguerreotype was already competing with painters.","c":"Field's portraits have a clean, decorative power: the strong silhouette, the clear color and the careful face make a girl a figure of dignity. The modeling is flat by academic standards, and that flatness is exactly what gives the picture its folk-art charm. The scale is generous for a child, and it works."},"met:10878":{"d":"An interior with a woman and a man gathered around a death fills this canvas of about 89 by 117 centimeters, painted between 1865 and 1880. The title recalls the last of the ten plagues of Egypt in the Book of Exodus, when the firstborn child of every Egyptian household died. Erastus Salisbury Field, known earlier as a portraitist, painted the biblical scene late in life with simple forms and intense feeling.","i":"The tenth plague, in Exodus 11 and 12, finally persuaded Pharaoh to let the Israelites leave Egypt, and it gave Passover its name, since the angel of death passed over the Israelite houses. Nineteenth-century American Protestants knew the story from illustrated family Bibles, and Field's picture shows how widely such images were adapted by self-taught painters. The viewer is meant to feel the parents' grief.","c":"The flattened space and the plain figures concentrate the emotion, and Field's simplification lends the story an almost liturgical stillness. Some passages feel stiff, and the scene lacks the drama of European history painting. It is the work of a self-taught painter with a big subject and a quiet voice, and the quiet is more affecting than a louder treatment might have been."},"met:10879":{"d":"A woman, a man and a girl are the figures in this canvas of about 89 by 117 centimeters, painted between 1865 and 1880. The title is tentative, since the subject is given as an Egyptian scene that might be Moses and Zipporah. It matches The Death of the First Born in size, date and donor, and it belongs to the group of biblical subjects with which Erastus Salisbury Field ended his career.","i":"Zipporah was the daughter of the Midianite priest Jethro and became the wife of Moses after he fled Egypt, as told in the Book of Exodus. Because the title is only a suggestion, the picture can also be read as a family group in an imagined ancient setting. Field built such scenes from illustrated Bibles and his own invention, since he had no first-hand knowledge of the Near East, and the result is antiquity as a New England household might have imagined it.","c":"Field's late biblical pictures are admirable for their directness and lack of pomp: figures are lined up with clear contours and the space is shallow, like a stage set. The weaknesses are his difficulty with perspective and anatomy and the vagueness of the story. The strength is the sincerity with which a village painter approaches the ancient world."},"nga:43631":{"d":"The Ark of the Covenant, the sacred chest of the Israelites, is the subject of this canvas of about 51 by 61 centimeters, painted between about 1865 and 1880. In the Book of Exodus the Ark is a gold-covered chest topped by two cherubim, made to hold the tablets of the Law. Erastus Salisbury Field, a Massachusetts painter of portraits who turned late in life to biblical scenes, imagined it in his own simple style.","i":"The Ark was the holiest object in ancient Israel, carried through the wilderness and later kept in the Temple's innermost room, and its story includes the fall of the walls of Jericho and the Ark's capture by the Philistines. Nineteenth-century Americans, steeped in the Bible, knew these stories from sermons and illustrated Bibles, and Field worked from such pictures and from his imagination.","c":"Field's late biblical pictures rely on clear shapes and bright color rather than on correct perspective or archaeology, and their sincerity is their appeal. A holy object is well suited to this approach, because simple outlines can give it solemnity. The picture's limits are those of a self-taught painter, but its directness is more moving than polish would be."},"nga:50260":{"d":"Dozens of men and women gather among clusters of columns and sandstone-colored buildings along a crimson river that winds from the horizon in this wide canvas of about 77 by 103 centimeters. At the center, a crowned bearded man in an emerald-green robe and scarlet cloak stands among the columns and gestures toward the river, while a crowned woman in blue throws up her hands as someone pours red liquid from a bucket. A single white lightning bolt zigzags from a cloud in a navy sky.","i":"The title quotes the Psalms and recalls the first of the ten plagues of Egypt in Exodus, when the Nile turned to blood because Pharaoh would not free the Israelites. Field's Egyptians are costumed from imagination with the help of illustrated Bibles, and the lightning bolt, which is not part of that story, signals divine anger. Such subjects reflect the deep familiarity of nineteenth-century Americans with the Old Testament.","c":"Field's architecture of stacked columns and flat rose-pink blocks has a naive grandeur, and the crimson river winding into the distance gives the picture a striking spine. The crowd is made of small, stiff gestures and the perspective is approximate, but the strangeness is part of its force. The red river is the kind of image that stays in memory."},"nga:56730":{"d":"The Taj Mahal, the white marble mausoleum built in the seventeenth century at Agra in India, is the subject of this canvas of about 89 by 117 centimeters, painted between about 1860 and 1880. Erastus Salisbury Field worked in western Massachusetts and presumably relied on prints and travel books for the view. The exterior of the building is the central motif, and it is shown in the clear outlines and bright color of Field's late manner.","i":"Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died in 1631, and it was long a symbol of the wealth and romance of the East in Western imagination. Field, who painted Egyptian and biblical subjects late in life, took the Taj as another exotic wonder, known to Americans from engravings and the illustrated books that brought distant places into the parlor.","c":"The strength of the picture is the confidence with which a New England painter reduces a famous monument to simple, strong shapes, and the dome and minarets have a striking, almost toy-like clarity. The perspective and detail owe more to imagination than observation, and the result looks like a dream of India. As an example of nineteenth-century exotic fantasy by a self-taught artist, it is a delight."},"nga:56731":{"d":"A marching army is the subject of this canvas of about 89 by 117 centimeters, painted between about 1865 and 1880. The title ties it to the Old Testament, presumably to the Egyptian army pursuing the Israelites in the Book of Exodus before the crossing of the Red Sea. It belongs to a group of biblical pictures that Erastus Salisbury Field made late in life, long after he had given up traveling as a portrait painter.","i":"The pursuit of the Israelites by Pharaoh's chariots and soldiers, told in Exodus 14, ends with the parting of the Red Sea and the drowning of the Egyptians, and it was a favorite subject of nineteenth-century preachers as a story of divine deliverance. Field's pictures of such episodes reflect a rural American world where the Bible was the main source of history and the imagination.","c":"Armies marching in file suit the self-taught painter's love of repeated shapes and strong pattern, and the subject allows a procession of figures with a rhythm that can be hypnotic. The details are summary and the space flat, but the effect is of a powerful vision. Field's biblical series may be naive, yet it is also sincere and original."},"nga:56732":{"d":"A pale blue pond lies at the center of this landscape with gentle waves rippling its surface. Pale purple mountains rise faintly on the horizon under a pastel blue and lavender sky with wispy clouds, and in the foreground trees and hills in gold, green and brown lead down toward the water. The canvas measures about 56 by 69 centimeters and dates from between about 1860 and 1880.","i":"Leverett, Massachusetts, was Erastus Salisbury Field's birthplace, and the title identifies the pond as a local scene, which makes the picture unusual in his output of portraits and imagined biblical subjects. In old age he returned to the hills of the Connecticut River valley, and the landscape has a sense of memory and affection for a home place. It belongs to the New England tradition of the pastoral view.","c":"The pastel sky and the soft purple mountains make this one of Field's gentlest pictures, and the pale blue water gives the composition a calm center. Compared with his bold figures and architecture, the handling is hushed, and the foreground trees in gold and brown are charmingly simplified. It is a modest landscape, but it feels sincere and personally observed."},"met:437760":{"d":"A landscape with cattle, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 80 by 107 centimeters, probably around 1800. The keywords list cattle and a landscape, but the museum's record gives no further description of the animals, herders, light or setting. The picture can be described only by its type: a pastoral scene in the Dutch tradition, painted by Jacob van Strij of Dordrecht, in which a herd stands in a quiet countryside.","i":"Dutch painters of cattle landscapes looked back to the seventeenth century, above all to Aelbert Cuyp, who worked in Strij's home town of Dordrecht and made golden light on grazing cows into a national theme. Around 1800 such pictures appealed to collectors who admired the Golden Age and to a public that valued rural calm. The record gives no information on where the scene is set, so it is best read as a general pastoral.","c":"Van Strij is a late and capable follower of Cuyp, with a clear, warm light and well-observed animals. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. His pictures are pleasant and skillful but seldom surprising, since they repeat a formula already perfected a century and a half earlier, and their real interest is as evidence of how long the Golden Age tradition lasted in Dutch taste."},"met:10159":{"d":"A long dugout canoe glides across the glassy water of a wide river at dawn, heaped with bundles of furs. At the right a man in a pink shirt and a tall red-and-white cap sits at the stern, steering with a long pole; in the middle a younger man in a blue coat reclines against the cargo and looks out with a relaxed half-smile. At the bow a black cat sits upright, apparently tethered. Behind them a low island of trees rises from pale mist, and the sky is a luminous cream streaked with pink. The boat and figures are mirrored in the still water in warm reds and dark reflections.","i":"George Caleb Bingham, a self-taught painter from Missouri, painted this picture in 1845. By then the fur trade that had once made the Missouri famous was in decline, and the picture has often been read as an elegy for a vanishing way of life. The older man is usually taken to be a French-descended trader and the younger his son, of mixed descent; the black animal in the bow is catalogued as a cat. Bingham's still, luminous river and balanced, almost classical design set his frontier genre painting beside the best American landscape of the 1840s.","c":"The picture's calm is its triumph. The composition is a pyramid of figures and cargo, balanced between the cat at the left and the steersman at the right, and the reflections double everything in the glassy water, giving the scene a dreamlike stillness. The mist and low sun are handled with real delicacy. Against that, the figures are stiff and their faces summary, and the cat is more emblem than animal. But few pictures have so memorably made the American frontier a place of reverie rather than conquest."},"nga:166430":{"d":"Three people gather at the door of a small thatched cottage in a sunlit yard, in an almost square canvas of about 65 by 76 centimetres. A woman in a white cap and blue garment leans her forearms on the lower half of a Dutch door, while a man in a straw hat, white shirt and red vest stands looking at her in profile and another man in a brown hat and green coat sits beside the door. Picket fences, a half-dead tree, a field and a lake with two cows lie beyond, and birds wheel above the roof.","i":"Bingham, a self-taught painter from Missouri who had worked mainly as a portraitist, was in the mid-1840s turning to scenes of everyday life, and this picture is an unusual early one: the thatched cottage looks more English than American, probably borrowed from European prints or paintings. The scene is usually read as a rustic flirtation, with the standing man courting the woman at the door, and as a gentle picture of country contentment.","c":"A charming early genre piece. The small figures are arranged around the door in a clear triangle, the sunlit yard and delicate distance are pleasing, and the details of birds and cows are nicely observed. It is not Bingham's best: the figures are stiff and the picturesque cottage is borrowed from European models, so it lacks the authority of his later river scenes. It remains interesting evidence of his move toward genre painting."},"nga:75206":{"d":"A flatboat carrying eight men floats toward the viewer down a wide river, spanning most of the width of the canvas. At the centre a man in light blue trousers and a pink shirt dances with one foot and both arms raised; at the right a seated musician plays a fiddle, and at the left a smiling man strikes the bottom of a metal pot with his fingers. The other men sit or recline around them, some looking at the dancer and two at the viewer, and bedrolls and skins are stored in the low cabin.","i":"Flatboats carried farm produce down the Mississippi and Missouri to New Orleans, and by 1846 steamboats were beginning to replace them, so the picture records a way of life that was fading. Bingham, who lived in Missouri, gave it a genial, idealised treatment, with the men at ease while the river carries them. It was bought by the American Art-Union and circulated in engravings, which helped make its image of the cheerful frontier boatman familiar to a wide public.","c":"Bingham arranges the men in a stable triangle, with the dancer as its apex, and the calm river, haze and low horizon give a luminous, spacious setting that suits their ease. The figures are distinct individuals, and the humour is genial. The poses are a little staged, as though the men were holding them for the painter, but the clarity and warmth make it his most engaging early picture and a defining image of American river life."},"nga:126127":{"d":"A bearded old man with a wrinkled, ruddy face sits on a wooden box and smokes a long pipe, peering at the viewer under bushy white brows. He wears a cardinal-red cap over shaggy grey hair, a loose yellow shirt, patched blue trousers and scuffed boots, with his legs crossed and one elbow propped on a stack of three barrels. Behind him a wooden houseboat floats on a river lined with trees and mountains, and the sunset turns the hills and trees red and gold. The painter signed and dated it 1850.","i":"The picture is a character study, not a portrait of a named man: the river boatman, with his pipe and patched clothes, stands for the free, rough life of the Mississippi frontier. Bingham, who lived in Missouri and knew the river, painted such figures in the 1840s and 1850s for an urban audience that enjoyed its picturesque types. The sunset and the houseboat give the figure a setting, and the old man's easy pose suggests contentment as well as hardship.","c":"A vivid character study: the weathered face, the pipe and the red cap are painted with sympathy and fine detail, and the sunset glow warms the whole scene. The pyramid of barrels and the sturdy figure make a strong composition. The pose is a little staged and the patched rags a little picturesque, but the picture has a directness and a warmth that make it one of the most appealing American images of its type."},"met:692597":{"d":"Saint Francis of Assisi stands in this tall, narrow panel, about 160 by 60 centimeters, painted in tempera and gold around 1480-81 by Antoniazzo Romano. The recorded motifs are a man and the saint. The proportions suggest that it formed one wing of a larger altarpiece, in which saints stood in separate panels beside a central image. The paint has been transferred to a new wooden support.","i":"Francis (1181/82-1226), founder of the Franciscan order, received the stigmata, the wounds of Christ, on Mount La Verna in 1224, and he is usually shown in a brown habit tied with a corded belt, displaying the marks in his hands and feet. In Rome in the 1480s, when Sixtus IV, himself a Franciscan, sat on the papal throne, the saint's image stood on altars in churches and chapels. The gold ground and standing figure belong to an older altarpiece tradition.","c":"Antoniazzo Romano was a leading Roman painter of the later fifteenth century, combining Umbrian and Tuscan lessons with an older Roman taste for gold and solemnity. The long, still figure here has dignity, but the conservatism of his art is evident next to the Florentines who painted the Sistine Chapel walls at the same moment. The picture's virtues are clarity, color and gravity."},"met:435584":{"d":"The Nativity is shown in a wide, shallow panel, about 29 by 67 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood in the 1480s by Antoniazzo Romano. The recorded motifs include Mary, the Christ Child, an angel, a donkey, sheep and other animals in a landscape. The wide, low format is characteristic of a predella, the row of small scenes beneath an altarpiece, where narrative could be told more freely than in the main panel.","i":"The Nativity is told in Luke 2, with the shepherds and their flocks, and the ox and donkey were added by tradition from a verse of Isaiah. In late medieval and Renaissance pictures Mary kneels in adoration of the Child lying on the ground, a form influenced by the visions of Saint Bridget of Sweden, and angels add praise. The landscape setting gives the sacred event a place in the world of daily rural life.","c":"Predella panels let painters be more informal than in the main altarpiece, and Antoniazzo takes advantage with animals and landscape. The wide frame is filled with small, clear incidents; the colors are bright and the forms simple. He is no innovator, and his figures tend toward a formal gravity, but in this humble subject that gravity becomes tenderness. It is a charming minor panel."},"met:435583":{"d":"The Virgin nurses the Christ Child in this small panel, about 38 by 27 centimeters, painted in tempera on a gold ground by Antoniazzo Romano; the museum gives no date, though the artist died in 1512. The recorded motifs are Mary, the Child, breastfeeding, a cushion and halos, and the gold ground lifts the group out of the everyday world. A panel of this size would have served a private household or a small chapel.","i":"The nursing Madonna, a devotional type that stresses Christ's humanity, was immensely popular in Rome and central Italy, where Antoniazzo's workshop produced many small Madonnas for private devotion, often following venerated local icons. The gold ground and halos keep an older, Byzantine-influenced convention alive in an age when Florentine painters had already abandoned them, a conservatism that Roman patrons seem to have valued.","c":"The picture is a work of devotion more than invention: a calm, symmetrical group, a mother's tenderness reduced to a few lines, and a gold ground that shimmers behind. Antoniazzo's weakness is a certain sameness of faces; his strength is a sincerity that suits small sacred images. Compared with the sophisticated Florentine Madonnas of the same decades it is plain and rather archaic, yet that plainness is moving."},"met:436836":{"d":"A woman personifying Music is shown among her attributes, which the museum's keywords list as a lute, a violin, a book and a bird. The record gives no further description of her pose, dress or setting, so the picture can be described only by its subject and type: an allegorical figure picture, in oil on canvas, about 106 by 144 centimeters, dated 1649, painted by the Parisian classicist Laurent de La Hyre.","i":"Music was one of the liberal arts, the group of studies thought worthy of a free person, and painters personified such arts as women with the instruments or tools of their craft. The lute and violin stand for the instrumental music of the day, the book for the written score, and a singing bird for natural song. Such pictures were made for the cultured patrons of Paris, who liked to see the arts honored in classical form.","c":"La Hyre painted with the clear, cool restraint of French classicism, and his allegories are known for their delicate colors, calm poses and carefully arranged still-life details. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. He lacks Poussin's intellectual depth and Vouet's decorative energy, but the best of his pictures have a gentle poetry and an elegance of design that make them among the most appealing of the French Baroque."},"cma:145473":{"d":"Two allegorical women embrace in a quiet landscape. Peace burns the weapons of war, and Justice holds a sword and scales. A Latin inscription beneath an antique urn reads Justice and Peace kissed, a quotation from Psalm 85:10. The museum adds that the peaceful, pastoral landscape around them is described in Psalm 85:12. The picture is oil on canvas, about 55 by 76 centimeters, and dated 1654.","i":"The Psalm says that mercy and truth have met together and that righteousness and peace have kissed each other, a promise of harmony between law and mercy. The date of 1654 coincides with the end of the Fronde, the French civil wars in which the parlements and the nobility tried and failed to limit the king's power, so the subject may have carried political meaning: a hope that justice and peace had been restored. The museum raises this as a possibility.","c":"A small, clear and finely judged picture. La Hyre arranges two figures in a calm landscape with a classical economy, using soft, harmonious color, a clean line and a few telling objects such as the burning arms and the urn. The embrace is formal rather than passionate, and the scale is modest, but the allegory is legible, and the pastoral setting lends it gentleness. It is a typical late work of a painter who preferred quiet to display."},"met:436211":{"d":"A portrait of a man, about 87 by 72 centimeters, dated either 1653 or 1655, in oil on canvas. The title raises the possibility that it is a self-portrait of the painter, Willem Drost, who was then about twenty, and the keywords list a letter. The museum's record gives no further description of the man's dress, pose or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a young man, in the style of the Amsterdam painters around Rembrandt.","i":"Drost was a pupil of Rembrandt in Amsterdam in the early 1650s and went to Italy not long afterward, dying in Venice in 1659 at about the age of twenty-six. A letter in a portrait can mark a sitter as a correspondent, a merchant or a scholar, or serve as a prop in an artist's self-portrait. The question mark in the title is the museum's honest sign that the sitter's identity remains open.","c":"Drost absorbed Rembrandt's lessons well: broad, confident strokes, a warm glow and a thoughtful, unposed sitter. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. If it is a self-portrait, it is a remarkably assured one for a man in his early twenties, and even if not, it shows why some of his pictures have been mistaken for his master's. He is closer to Rembrandt in surface than in depth of feeling."},"met:437415":{"d":"A tronie, a character study rather than a portrait of a named person, showing a woman as a sibyl with a book. The museum's keywords give only the woman, the book and the sibyl, and the record gives no further description of her costume, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type. It is painted in oil on canvas, about 98 by 78 centimeters, around 1654, in the circle of Rembrandt in Amsterdam, where Willem Drost was a pupil.","i":"The sibyls were prophetic women of antiquity whom Christian tradition made witnesses to the coming of Christ, and they are traditionally shown with books, since their prophecies were collected as the Sibylline Books. Rembrandt's pupils painted such costume pieces as exercises in expression and exotic dress, for a market that liked picturesque figures. The sitter was probably a studio model, and the sibyl is a role, not a person.","c":"A handsome exercise in the Rembrandt manner: the broad, warm brushwork, the dramatic light and the contemplative expression all follow the master's lead. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. Drost's pictures of this kind are among the more accomplished works of Rembrandt's circle, though they are more decorative than searching, and the sibyl's role leaves her individual character veiled."},"met:906341":{"d":"A man is portrayed with a tablet inscribed in Hebrew in this canvas of about 100 by 82 centimeters, painted around 1575 by Antonio Campi of Cremona. The title is the museum's own, and the recorded themes name only men and portraits. The format is a half-length portrait on canvas, typical of the Lombard tradition of sober, individual likenesses, in which the sitter's face and one telling attribute carry the picture.","i":"A Hebrew inscription in a sixteenth-century Italian portrait is rare and must have been meaningful to the sitter. He may have been a Christian scholar of Hebrew, a humanist interested in the original language of the Old Testament, a convert, or a member of the Jewish community; the picture itself does not say. Cremona, where Campi worked, had a notable Hebrew press in the 1550s and 1560s.","c":"Campi's portraits are among the more sober of the late Renaissance in Lombardy: attentive to the character of the head, restrained in color and unflattering in the best sense. This picture gains extra interest from its unusual attribute, which makes the sitter's story a puzzle the paint cannot solve. As a work of art it is a serious, competent portrait; as a document it is intriguing."},"met:436815":{"d":"A large history painting in oil on canvas, about 157 by 189 centimeters, undated in the record. The keywords list men on horseback and a figure of Christ, but the record gives no further description of the composition. The picture can be described only by its subject and type: the arrival of a Crusader army before the walls of Jerusalem, most probably the First Crusade of 1099, painted in the monumental German manner of Wilhelm von Kaulbach, who worked in Munich.","i":"The Crusades were a favorite subject of nineteenth-century Romantic and national painters, who saw in them a union of faith, chivalry and heroic action. Kaulbach, a pupil of Peter von Cornelius, painted vast, learned history pictures for the Bavarian court and the museums of Munich and Berlin, and wished to present history as a moral drama. The figure of Christ in the keywords suggests a vision or a symbol of divine guidance, though the record does not describe it.","c":"Kaulbach was one of the most famous German painters of his day, admired for the learning and scale of his compositions, and later criticized for their theatrical, crowded effect. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. A crusader subject offers horses, armor and spectacle, but history painting of this kind has aged badly, and is valued today more as a record of nineteenth-century taste than for any personal vision."},"met:437459":{"d":"A portrait miniature on vellum, a fine animal skin, of John Churchill, first Duke of Marlborough, whose dates the title gives as 1650 to 1722. The record lists the sitter and a man, and notes that the medium is vellum; no date or dimensions are recorded, and the museum gives no description of his dress, wig or expression. The painter, Christian Richter, was a Swedish miniaturist who worked in London.","i":"Marlborough, the greatest English general of his age, won the victories of Blenheim in 1704 and Ramillies, Oudenarde and Malplaquet in the War of the Spanish Succession, and was rewarded with the dukedom and with Blenheim Palace. His likeness was reproduced in many forms, and a small portrait on vellum would have been a cherished memento for a supporter. Richter worked in London in the early eighteenth century, mostly in enamel and watercolor miniatures, sometimes copying other painters' portraits.","c":"A miniature of a national hero is a small act of homage, and Richter, a competent specialist, gives the Duke the polished, dignified look the public expected. The medium of vellum gives a soft, matte surface unlike enamel's gloss. The likeness is perhaps more a symbol than a study; it records the Duke as England chose to remember him, handsome, calm and victorious."},"nga:42414":{"d":"A small oil on wood panel, about 23 by 18 centimeters, shows the head and shoulders of a man in strict profile, facing left. His brown hair is slightly tousled and curls at the ends, his nose is prominent, and his eyes look straight ahead. He wears a dark jacket over a white shirt with a high collar. No jewelry or props distract from the face, and the background is a muted, almost monochrome tone. The title names the sitter only as Mr. Kline.","i":"Around 1800 to 1810, profile portraits were a familiar fashion in the United States, helped by the popularity of silhouettes and of machines that traced a sitter's outline. A painted profile gave the same clean contour with color added, and a small panel like this was probably affordable for families of modest means. The plain dark coat and the absence of props suggest a sober sitter whose portrait records a face rather than displays rank. The painter, Jacob Eichholtz, was a Pennsylvania coppersmith turned portraitist.","c":"Next to Eichholtz's later, larger portraits, this panel is plain, and its limits are the limits of the format: with no props and a flat ground, the sitter has to carry the picture on outline alone. What it does offer is a firm contour of brow, nose and chin, and an unfussy honesty about unruly hair. It is competent provincial portraiture, interesting mainly as a record of how the artist began."},"nga:42415":{"d":"A man is shown in profile on a small oil on wood panel of about 23 by 17.5 centimeters, with his right side turned toward the viewer. His short brown hair recedes a little at the forehead, his nose is prominent, and his face is clean-shaven and fair. He wears a dark coat with metal buttons, a high-collared white shirt and a black cravat. The background is a plain, dark oval. The question mark in the title shows that the sitter's first name is not certain.","i":"Several small panels of about 1808 in the National Gallery of Art carry the Leman name, which suggests a family group painted at much the same time. Whether this man is a Jacob, as the title tentatively says, is not settled. His dress is the conventional uniform of a respectable man of the period: dark coat, stiff white collar and black neckcloth. Such plain presentation fits the sober, practical tone of early American portraiture.","c":"This is the plainest of the Leman group, with no bird, jewelry or accessory to enliven it, so the picture lives or dies on the contour of the face. The receding hair and strong nose are recorded without flattery, which is to the painter's credit. The result is a sound likeness but not a lively one, closer to a document than to a characterization."},"nga:42416":{"d":"A young boy is shown from the chest up, in profile, on a small oil on wood panel of about 23 by 17 centimeters. In his right hand he holds a small bird with a red head. His hair is short and light blond, his profile delicate, with a slightly upturned nose. He wears a dark outfit with small white buttons and a wide white collar painted in fine detail. Behind him is a dark oval, surrounded by a wood-textured border that frames it like a cameo.","i":"Children in early American portraits are often given a small companion or emblem. A bird in a child's hand is usually read as a sign of innocence, tenderness or the fragility of young life, though it may simply record a pet. The wide white collar and buttoned dark suit reflect the clothing of well-to-do boys at the time. Like the other Leman panels of about 1808, it was probably painted for the family to keep as a record of a child.","c":"The bird is the picture's best idea: a small red accent on a restrained palette, it gives the panel a story that the adult profiles lack. The profile still keeps the boy at a distance, since there is no meeting of eyes, and the oval and wooden border lend the panel a pleasing, cameo-like neatness. It is a charming minor work, more engaging than searching."},"nga:42417":{"d":"A young woman's upper body is shown in profile on a small oil on wood panel of about 21 by 17 centimeters. She stands sideways, facing left, with her gaze directed forward. Her complexion is fair, her nose prominent, and curly bangs frame her face while the rest of her hair is pinned up. She wears a pink dress with short sleeves and a white lace collar, a black necklace and gold hoop earrings. The ground is deep brown to black, fading toward the edges, and the surface shows wear.","i":"Her costume tells the viewer what the profile cannot. A pink gown, lace collar, black necklace and gold earrings mark a young woman of a comfortable family who followed fashion. The curled fringe and upswept hair recall the neoclassical styles of the early 1800s, which borrowed from the looks of ancient Greece and Rome. Portraits of daughters were kept by families as records of youth, and this one is named only by her surname and her unmarried title.","c":"This is the liveliest of the small Leman panels because color is allowed to play: the pink dress against a dark ground, with small accents of gold and black, is well judged. The profile limits expression, so charm comes from costume and crisp outline. Wear to the surface limits what can be judged of its subtler modeling, but it still shows the clear shapes and direct eye of a largely self-taught painter."},"nga:45883":{"d":"Two girls in white stand side by side on a stone terrace in this tall double portrait. Each wears a floor-length white dress with a ruffled collar and cuffs. The girl on the left wears a bonnet trimmed with flowers and pink ribbons and holds a small tawny book in her lowered hand, while her other arm circles her sister's waist. The second girl rests an arm across her shoulders and carries her matching bonnet by its ribbons. Behind them are a stone balustrade, pillars and a red curtain, and dark clouds crowd the evening sky.","i":"Matching white dresses, linked arms and nearly identical faces present the sisters as a pair united by affection, a theme parents valued in portraits of daughters. The book hints at education and piety, and the flowers on the bonnet at youth. The balustrade, curtain and stormy sky are devices from English grand-manner portraiture, which a Pennsylvania painter could borrow to give a family a measure of polish and standing.","c":"This is an ambitious work for a painter of Eichholtz's background, and it succeeds where it counts: the sisters' embrace is tender, and the white dresses glow against the olive terrace and red drape. The faces are almost interchangeable, and the stormy backdrop feels like a borrowed prop rather than part of the scene. Still, the picture conveys family affection with a directness that more polished portraits of the day often lack."},"nga:33961":{"d":"An elderly woman sits facing the viewer, shown from the waist up, with her hands clasped in front of her. Her calm face is lined with age. She wears a white frilled bonnet tied under her chin and a dark shawl over a white high-collared blouse. She sits on a red chair trimmed with gold, and a red book and a handkerchief rest on the surface beside her. The background moves from deep red to brown. The canvas has been transferred to a wood panel.","i":"This portrait is the companion of the likeness of her husband, Robert Coleman, an Irish-born ironmaster who became one of the wealthiest men in Pennsylvania. Pairs of husband and wife were a standard commission and were hung together. Where his props suggest business, hers suggest domestic virtue: the red book is probably a devotional work, and the handkerchief a sign of a settled, modest life. Her plain bonnet and shawl state respectability rather than display.","c":"Eichholtz is at his best in the frank, unflattering record of an old face: the lines, the steady look and the clasped hands. The red ground behind her gives the white bonnet and dark shawl a strong silhouette, though the chair and its gold trim add color more than structure. The pose is conventional, but the calm is persuasive, and it makes the more restful half of a good pair."},"nga:33962":{"d":"A gray-haired man sits facing the viewer with his right hand resting on a table. His hair is neatly combed, and he wears a dark, high-collared coat over a white shirt, with a yellow vest showing at the front. On the table lie a quill, an inkwell and papers. The background is a dark, neutral tone. The portrait, about 92 by 71 centimeters, is oil on canvas that has been transferred to a wood panel, and it was painted around 1820.","i":"The quill, inkwell and papers announce the sitter's trade. Robert Coleman, an Irish-born ironmaster, built a fortune in Pennsylvania through furnaces and forges, and the portrait presents him as a literate man of business rather than a gentleman of leisure. The setting is deliberately plain. Portraits of this kind told neighbors and descendants what the sitter valued: industry, accounts kept, and a name that could be trusted.","c":"The firm, simply lit head is the best passage, and the bright yellow vest is a bold note in an otherwise dark composition. The props are laid out a little too neatly, like a label, but they do their narrative work. It is direct, unflattering American portraiture, closer to an honest ledger than to the glamorous English manner some of Eichholtz's contemporaries preferred."},"nga:34158":{"d":"A woman is seated, her left arm resting on a stone ledge and her right hand holding that arm. Her skin is smooth, her cheeks rosy, her eyebrows dark, and her dark curly hair is styled around the face and gathered at the back. She wears an off-the-shoulder white dress with puffed sleeves and holds a vivid red shawl with a floral pattern. Behind her are a stone column at the right and a dusky sky in soft blue, orange and pink, with greenery in the distance.","i":"The column, ledge and evening sky are settings borrowed from grand-manner portraiture, here scaled down for a private sitter. The red patterned shawl is the most telling object: imported shawls, perhaps in the Kashmir style, were costly, fashionable accessories in the early 1800s and signaled wealth and taste. The off-shoulder white dress follows the high fashion of the day, and the whole picture presents the sitter as elegant and well connected.","c":"Here Eichholtz reaches for the polish of Philadelphia portraiture, probably under the influence of Thomas Sully, whom he had consulted years earlier. The diagonal of the arm along the ledge and the red shawl against a pale dress and a sunset sky give the picture a pleasing rhythm. The face is smooth and a little generalized, so the result is more graceful than searching."},"met:10845":{"d":"A portrait of a man named Samuel Humes, painted in oil on canvas, about 74 by 61 centimeters, about 1825. The museum's record gives no description of his age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a sitter of the American middle class, by Jacob Eichholtz, who worked in Lancaster, Pennsylvania.","i":"Eichholtz, who began as a coppersmith, became the leading portrait painter of Lancaster and its region, painting merchants, ironmasters, lawyers and clergymen in a plain, honest style. A portrait of a man like Humes was a mark of respectability in the young republic, when a prosperous citizen wished to be remembered by his descendants. The record gives no profession for the sitter, so his place in that society is not stated.","c":"Eichholtz's portraits are firm and direct, with a strong sense of the sitter's character, though his debt to Thomas Sully can be seen in the pose and the soft modeling. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. He is not a painter of subtlety, but his plainness suits his sitters, and his best work has an honest, slightly craggy presence more convincing than the refinement of more fashionable artists."},"nga:34051":{"d":"A man faces forward with his head turned slightly to his right. His expression is stern, his complexion fair, and his short dark hair recedes from the forehead. He wears a white shirt with a high collar and a cravat under a dark coat with a broad collar and buttons. The background is a plain dark backdrop that isolates the head and shoulders. The canvas, about 76 by 63.5 centimeters, dates from around 1830.","i":"The sitter wears the sober uniform of the respectable American man of the 1830s: dark broadcloth coat, stiff white collar and cravat. With no props and no setting, the portrait offers a character instead of a career, and the stern look was an accepted sign of seriousness and reliability. Pictures of this type were commissioned by men in business and the professions to hang at home and be handed down to descendants.","c":"A competent portrait whose strength is the head: the turned face, the slightly sideways glance and the clear, uncluttered lighting give a flicker of personality to an otherwise formulaic type. The dark coat is a broad mass with little articulation, and nothing else competes for attention. It shows how steady demand for plain, truthful likenesses shaped provincial portraiture, even if it does not rise above it."},"nga:34093":{"d":"A woman with fair skin, light blue eyes and dark brown hair gathered in an updo is the sole subject of this portrait. She wears a dark gown with long sleeves and a white collar against a simple background. The canvas, painted in oil around 1830, measures about 69 by 57 centimeters. Few objects or accessories are included, so the picture is built from only a few elements: the lit face, the white collar and the dark mass of the dress.","i":"Named in the title as Mrs. Clarkson Freeman, the sitter is identified, as married women of the period usually were, by her husband's name. The dark gown with a plain white collar conveys modesty and respectability, and the simple background keeps attention on her face. Portraits of husband and wife were often painted as pairs, so a companion likeness of Clarkson Freeman may exist or may once have existed.","c":"The light touches carry the portrait: blue eyes and the white collar stand out against dark dress and ground, and the face is modeled with care and without flattery. Restraint is the point, and it gives the sitter an unaffected, direct presence. But little else sets the picture apart from many portraits of its type, and the dress dissolves into the background."},"nga:34052":{"d":"A man sits turned slightly to the left and looks directly at the viewer. He has fair skin, light-colored eyes and dark brown wavy hair with sideburns reaching the jawline, and he wears a black coat with a high collar over a white shirt. Beside him is an easel holding a sketch, and in his left hand he holds a palette and brushes. Draped fabric in red or brown shades fills the background. The canvas measures about 75 by 63 centimeters and dates from around 1835.","i":"The easel, palette and brushes identify the sitter's pursuit, whether as a professional painter or as an amateur proud of the accomplishment. Such attributes turned a portrait into a statement of identity, showing how a man wished to be known. The draped curtain is a studio convention borrowed from European portraiture, and it signals dignity. Portraits that show the sitter's own craft were a small but established category.","c":"Props give this portrait more narrative interest than most of Eichholtz's sober likenesses: the sitter's direct gaze, slight turn and the palette in hand add life. The dark coat and shadowed curtain are handled as broad masses, and the sketch on the easel plays a supporting role. It is a likeable, inventive late portrait, though less polished than the best portraits of his Philadelphia contemporaries."},"nga:42918":{"d":"A man's upper body is shown in a classic portrait pose, facing forward and looking at the viewer. His complexion is fair, his face smooth, his eyes warm brown, and his hair is styled with a slight wave and a side part. He wears formal dress: a dark jacket, white shirt, black waistcoat and black bow tie. The background is plain and muted, and there are no objects or jewelry. The canvas measures about 76.5 by 64 centimeters and dates from around 1835.","i":"The sitter's middle name suggests a family link to the painter, and the surname recalls the Leman panels Eichholtz painted about 1808, so he may belong to a younger generation of the same circle. The black coat, waistcoat and bow tie were the standard dress of the professional or business man in the 1830s. The frontal pose and plain ground give a calm, straightforward image of a man who wished to be seen as sober and capable.","c":"Set beside the small profile panels of about 1808, this canvas shows how far Eichholtz had come: the head is modeled with confidence, the gaze meets the viewer, and the black clothing is handled as a sober mass. Nothing here is showy, and there is little to remember beyond the steady eyes. As a quiet, secure likeness from a mature provincial painter, it does its job well."},"nga:119295":{"d":"A half-peeled pomegranate, a few hazelnuts, a bunch of purple grapes and a glass of pale yellow wine sit on the corner of a chipped gray stone ledge against a dark background. The wineglass has a flaring cup of flat panels, scalloped along the upper edge, on a stem of twisted ropes of clear and black glass. The pomegranate's scarlet seeds glisten against its rust-orange rind, a broad green vine leaf arches over the fruit, and tiny water droplets sparkle on the ledge. It is signed and dated 1675 on the front of the ledge.","i":"Dutch still lifes of the later seventeenth century were prized for their illusion of reality and for their skill with reflections, textures and light. A half-peeled pomegranate, with its dazzling seeds, was a favorite of painters of fruit and could suggest abundance and, in Christian tradition, resurrection, while a fragile glass and a chipped ledge might be read as reminders of how fleeting such pleasures are. These readings are possibilities, and the picture can be enjoyed as pure display.","c":"A superb exercise in surface: the shine of the glass, the dewy bloom of the grapes and the wet sparkle of the pomegranate seeds are all handled with a miniaturist's patience. The dark ground and the strong light from the left concentrate the effect, and the corner of the ledge pushes the objects toward the viewer. Walscapelle is a lesser name than the great fruit painters, but this small panel has their polish and exactness."},"nga:42405":{"d":"A portrait of a named steamboat, painted in oil on a wide canvas about 73 by 122 centimetres. James Bard specialised in such pictures, and he typically showed the vessel close to profile on calm water, with hull, decks and fittings drawn with a draughtsman's care and the surroundings kept simple. The title gives the steamer's name, so the picture is above all a likeness of one particular ship, made as a record rather than as a landscape or a story.","i":"In the mid-nineteenth century the steamboat stood for speed, trade and national progress, and owners, builders, captains and passengers wanted accurate likenesses of their vessels, much as other patrons ordered portraits of themselves. Bard, a New York painter, made a career of supplying them. Such pictures are usually read as records of a particular ship at a particular moment, and exactness was the point: an owner or crew member could check every funnel and deck against the real vessel.","c":"Bard's strength is exactness. The ship's lines and fittings are set down with a draughtsman's clarity, and plain, even water keeps the eye on the vessel. The weakness lies in the setting: sky, shore and any figures tend to be generic and a little stiff, so the picture reads as a document more than a view. Its appeal today comes from that mix of precision and plainness, which makes the steamer look like a proud possession."},"nga:52947":{"d":"A steam towboat named John Birkbeck moves from left to right across calm water, smoke drifting from its smokestack, flags flying, one of them bearing the boat's name. Small figures can be made out on deck. Low hills separate the water from the sky, and the colour runs through blues, greens, pastels and greys, warming toward the horizon. The brushwork is smooth and detailed, and both the water and the distant hills are treated in a calm, idealised way, so the whole picture is orderly and clear.","i":"A towboat was a working steam vessel built to haul barges and canal boats, not to carry passengers, so a portrait of one records the freight trade that kept American rivers and canals busy in the 1850s. Pictures of this kind were probably commissioned by an owner or builder. The flag carrying the boat's name works like a signature, identifying the vessel at a glance. Bard painted many steam vessels, and they now serve as a record of working life on the water.","c":"A tidy, confident ship portrait. Bard keeps the vessel crisp and legible and lets the warm glow near the horizon soften the hills, so the boat stands out against the light. The smooth finish and the clearly lettered flag are the picture's real accomplishments. What it lacks is any sense of work or weather: the water is glassy, the figures tiny, the mood idealised calm. As documentary art it is effective; as drama it is slight."},"cma:99444":{"d":"A portrait of Samuel Barber Clark, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 64 by 52 centimeters, about 1810. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait by James Frothingham of Massachusetts, who modeled his style on Gilbert Stuart, the leading portraitist of the young United States.","i":"The record gives the sitter's full name but no age, profession or family, so the circumstances of the portrait are unknown. In the early republic a likeness in the style of Gilbert Stuart was a sign of success, and such portraits were ordered to record a marriage, a coming of age or an achievement. A panel rather than a canvas is a less common support for an American portrait of this date, which the record does not explain.","c":"Frothingham was one of the best of Stuart's followers, and his portraits show the master's lessons: a warm, firmly modeled face, soft transitions and a few bright accents of white. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. He rarely matched Stuart's feeling for character, and his pictures can be flat, but at his best the clean, assured handling gives a likeness of real presence."},"met:10919":{"d":"A portrait of Christopher Colles, painted in oil on canvas, about 77 by 65 centimeters, between about 1812 and 1816. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait by James Frothingham. The name is that of an Irish-born engineer and canal promoter who died in 1816, and if he is the sitter, as seems likely, the picture was painted near the end of his life.","i":"Christopher Colles emigrated to America in the 1770s, lectured on science, built an early public water system for Manhattan, published a book of road maps and promoted canals, which makes him one of the early American advocates of internal improvements. If the portrait shows him, it records a man of ideas in his seventies, near the end of a career of ambitious projects that brought him more fame than profit.","c":"Frothingham painted in the direct manner he took from Gilbert Stuart, and a sitter with a record of public usefulness gave him an interesting subject. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. It is a straightforward portrait with real conviction, valued more for its likeness of a notable early American engineer than for any virtuosity of handling."},"met:14513":{"d":"A portrait of Benjamin West, painted in oil on a tall, narrow canvas, about 112 by 47 centimeters, and dated 1818. The museum's record gives no description of the pose, dress or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a likeness of the American-born president of the Royal Academy, who was then about eighty. The unusual narrow format may indicate a sketch or a design made for another purpose, but the record does not say.","i":"West, born in Pennsylvania in 1738, became history painter to George III and, from 1792, the second president of the Royal Academy after Reynolds, holding the post, with a short break, until his death in 1820. Green's portrait records him in the last years of a long career, when younger painters made likenesses of the elder statesman of British art, and the format may serve that purpose.","c":"James Green was a competent portraitist, and the portrait of West is a respectful record of a venerable public figure. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is general. Its interest is chiefly historical: it preserves the face of the artist who taught a generation of American painters, from Charles Willson Peale to Samuel Morse, and it is a straightforward likeness rather than an ambitious design."},"nga:43598":{"d":"A man in eighteenth-century dress stands against a tree. He has fair skin and powdered white hair, and wears a green coat, blue waistcoat, white cravat, dark breeches, blue stockings and black shoes with gold buckles. He holds a three-cornered hat, rests one arm on a branch and crosses his legs at the ankle, with a calm expression and a slight smile. Behind him are the tree, a serene landscape with a reflective pool, distant hills and a cloudy sky. The canvas is about 92 by 72 centimeters, dated about 1777 to 1780.","i":"The sitter is Lord Algernon Percy, a younger son of the first Duke of Northumberland, later created Earl of Beverley. The relaxed pose against a tree, with the hat in hand, was the fashionable way to show a gentleman at ease in the English landscape, a manner popularized by Gainsborough and Reynolds. It signals leisure and the ownership of land without any display of arms or office. The portrait is the pendant of one of his wife in the same museum.","c":"An agreeable, elegant portrait in the English manner of the 1770s, with a well-judged contrast between the green coat and the airy landscape, and a pleasant ease in the pose. The treatment of the face is gentle but generic, and the landscape is a conventional backdrop, so the picture owes more to the formulas of Gainsborough than to Millar's own invention. It is a good example of how lesser painters spread the fashionable portrait type."},"nga:43599":{"d":"A portrait of Lady Algernon Percy, painted in oil on canvas, about 92 by 72 centimeters, between about 1777 and 1780. The museum's record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait of an aristocratic woman by James Millar. It matches the portrait of Lord Algernon Percy in the same museum in size and date, and the two clearly form a pair.","i":"The style Lady Algernon Percy was that of the wife of Lord Algernon Percy, a younger son of the first Duke of Northumberland, one of the greatest families in England, who was later created Earl of Beverley. A pair of portraits like this was probably made for the couple's own household. The record names her only by her title, so her own name and background must be sought elsewhere.","c":"James Millar was a British portraitist little known today, who borrowed the fashionable types of Gainsborough and Reynolds, and the paired portraits of Lord and Lady Algernon Percy show that influence in their airy, informal manner. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. It is a pleasant, competent work whose main interest lies in its sitters and in the way the couple was designed to be seen together."},"nga:234272":{"d":"A small devotional picture on a copper plate, about 60 by 47 centimeters, painted between about 1730 and 1739. The subject is the Lamentation over the dead Christ, the mourning of the body after it was taken down from the cross. The museum's record gives no description of the figures or setting, so the picture can be described only by its subject and type: an intimate image for prayer, by the Guatemalan painter Tomás de Merlo, of the kind kept in a private house or chapel.","i":"In the Lamentation the Virgin, Saint John and the holy women mourn over the body of Christ, an image meant to arouse compassion and to help the viewer meditate on the Passion. Copper was widely used in the Spanish colonies for small devotional pictures, since its smooth, durable surface took fine detail and kept its color. The record does not identify the figures, so the usual cast of mourners is assumed rather than described.","c":"Merlo worked in colonial Guatemala in the early eighteenth century, when painters often took their compositions from European prints and adapted them to local taste, with strong emotion and rich color. The record gives no description of this picture, so the verdict is general. Devotional images of this kind are valued for their intensity and craftsmanship, and for showing how a European subject was made at home in the Americas."},"met:10946":{"d":"A deep wooded gorge in the Catskill Mountains, painted in oil on a tall canvas, about 122 by 101 centimeters, and dated 1862. The museum's keywords list the sky, clouds, mountains, trees, a canyon and the sun, and the title names the place as Kauterskill Clove, but the record gives no further description of the light, colors or any figures. The picture can be described only by its type: a grand, upright landscape of a famous American scenic site, by Sanford Robinson Gifford.","i":"Kaaterskill Clove was one of the most celebrated views in America, painted by Thomas Cole and many later artists of the Hudson River School, and visited by tourists staying at the mountain hotels of the Catskills. Such pictures made the wilderness of the new nation into a source of national pride and spiritual feeling. Gifford was famous for suffusing his landscapes with a hazy golden light, so the sun in the keywords is likely to be the picture's main actor.","c":"Gifford was the great poet of light among the Hudson River painters, and a gorge with the sun in it gave him a subject in which his gifts were at their best: atmosphere, depth and glowing haze. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His landscapes can lack the narrative drama of Church's or the detail of Bierstadt's, but their quiet radiance makes them among the most modern in feeling of the school."},"nga:121618":{"d":"A tiny man sits on a sheer rocky outcropping high above a sunlit expanse of land that stretches to meet a sun-dappled sea. He wears a blue shirt, tan trousers and a soft narrow-brimmed hat, appears to have red hair and a beard, and holds a white sheet, presumably paper or a notebook. Behind him are an open box of paints and brushes, a camp stool and a closed white parasol. Gray crags catch bright light from the upper right, and a forested hill descends to a mauve plain and a hazy blue sea. The signature, S R Gifford 1865, is cut into the rock as if carved.","i":"Mount Desert Island on the coast of Maine was a favorite sketching ground for Hudson River painters from the 1850s, and the title says the artist is at work there. A tiny figure dwarfed by a vast prospect was a standard way of conveying the sublime, and here it also becomes a playful touch, because the signature appears carved into the rock like a tourist's initials. The figure is probably Gifford himself, though the title says only the artist.","c":"A tiny, brilliant picture. The scale of the man, a speck on the cliff, makes the land and sea seem immense, and the luminous haze, soft mauve and golden light show Gifford's real strength. The humor of the carved signature adds charm. At about 28 by 48 centimeters it is an oil sketch in spirit, with less finish than his large exhibition pictures, which is exactly its appeal: a modest, direct record of the pleasure of looking."},"cma:144968":{"d":"A view of Mount Hayes in New Hampshire shows human intrusion into a remote landscape. On the left riverbank a log cabin stands amid a recently cleared patch of land with several tree stumps, while figures in its doorway greet a man who has arrived with a canoe of supplies. The wide canvas, about 77 by 136 centimeters, was painted in 1866, and the museum notes that Gifford considered it one of his finest creations.","i":"In the 1860s, after the Civil War, Americans saw their wilderness being cleared and settled, and painters of the Hudson River School recorded the change with both pride and regret. The cabin, the stumps and the canoe tell of the pioneer's labor, and the small scale of the settlers against the mountain and river keeps nature in command. The picture can be read as a celebration of settlement or as a quiet elegy for the wild, and probably as both.","c":"A calm, well-balanced picture in which the broad river and the distant mountain are drawn together by a soft atmosphere, and the little cabin gives a human anchor. The narrative is slight, and the figures are small, but that is the point: Gifford's art is about space and light, and the story is only an excuse for them. That he ranked it among his finest works is credible, since it is among his most complete."},"nga:123386":{"d":"Two large pears sit on a white tablecloth. The pinkish one on the left rests directly on the cloth, and the yellow one on the right sits on a white plate beside a small knife. Behind them stand two clear glasses of yellowish liquid and a dark wine bottle, and a letter with two stamps lies at the lower left corner of the table. The background is dark brown. It is a small canvas, about 29 by 30 centimeters, painted around 1866.","i":"A tabletop with fruit, wine, a knife and a letter is an informal, almost private still life, the kind of arrangement an artist might make on his own table. The letter suggests a personal note, though the record does not say whose. Still lifes are rare among Gifford's known works, which are mainly landscapes, so this small picture shows a side of his work that was seldom exhibited and probably meant for himself or his friends.","c":"A modest, unpretentious picture in which a landscape painter tests his eye on near things. The pale cloth, the pears and the glass give his feeling for tone something to do at close range. It lacks the grandeur of Gifford's landscapes and the polish of the professional still-life painters of his time, but it is an honest and charming study of ordinary objects, and its small scale suits the subject."},"cma:169614":{"d":"The picture shows Haverstraw Bay on the Hudson River, painted in oil on a small, wide canvas, about 24 by 51 centimeters, and dated 1868. Haverstraw Bay lies about forty miles north of New York City and is the widest point of the river. In the 1860s it was a prime area for shad fishing, and the museum notes that Gifford's picture records the fishing taking place amid a delicately luminous morning haze.","i":"Shad fishing was a major activity on the Hudson, and its boats and nets were a familiar part of the river scene. Gifford, who grew up in the river town of Hudson, New York, painted the river and the Catskills throughout his life, and his pictures often unite a working landscape with a poetic atmosphere. The museum adds that fishing for the now endangered American shad is currently prohibited on the Hudson, which makes the picture a record of a vanished practice.","c":"A small panel of great delicacy: the haze unites the scene in a single luminous tone, and the long horizontal format suits the wide, quiet bay. It is a more intimate picture than Gifford's large exhibition canvases, and it shows the luminist side of the Hudson River School at its subtlest. Its modest scale hides how much control of tone it needs, and the working subject keeps it from becoming merely pretty."},"met:10947":{"d":"A wide landscape of Tivoli, the hill town east of Rome, painted in oil on canvas, about 67 by 128 centimeters, and dated 1870. The museum's keywords list a sky, a cliff, trees, a river and the sun, but the record gives no further description of the light, colors or any figures. The picture can be described only by its subject and type: a panoramic Italian view with a river below a cliff, in which the sun is one of the listed subjects.","i":"Tivoli had drawn painters since the Renaissance for its waterfalls, its cliff-top ruins and its views over the Roman Campagna, and it was a fixture of the Grand Tour. Gifford, a leading painter of the Hudson River School, traveled in Europe and the Near East in 1868 and 1869, and this picture of 1870 probably draws on that journey. The sun in the keywords points to his usual interest in the effect of light and haze on a large view.","c":"Gifford's mature style, in which the whole picture is held together by a single glowing atmosphere, suits a place like Tivoli, where light and distance are the real subject. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His Italian views are less dramatic than his American ones, and their picturesque stock motifs can seem familiar, but the unified haze is his own and gives them a quiet distinction."},"met:10945":{"d":"A view of Isola Bella on Lake Maggiore in northern Italy, painted in oil on canvas, about 51 by 91 centimeters, and dated 1871. The keywords list the lake, a boat, the sky and clouds, a cliff, a hill and a sunset, but the record gives no further description of the island, the colors or the people. The picture can be described only by its subject and type: a wide lake view in the evening, by the American Hudson River painter Sanford Robinson Gifford.","i":"Isola Bella is one of the Borromean Islands, shaped in the seventeenth century by the Borromeo family into a terraced palace and garden that rises from the water like a ship, and it was a stop on the tourist route through the Italian lakes. American painters visiting Italy found in such sites the beauty and the history that their own landscape lacked. The sunset in the keywords suggests the glowing, atmospheric effect that was Gifford's specialty.","c":"Gifford was drawn to broad lakes and evening light, and a subject like this gave him all he needed: water, mountains, boats and a sky. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is general. His later paintings sometimes grow soft to the point of vagueness, but the best of them give the Italian lakes a pearly calm that no guidebook illustration could match."},"nga:107525":{"d":"A dirt path winds through a field of gold-green grass toward a town on low hills, in a densely hazy view. Tiny figures walk the path and one rides a horse; others gather by a white dome in the distance. At the right, six people sit or stand near a conical white shelter, with two horses and about two dozen small animals grazing close by. The town's low flat-topped buildings, a domed building with two slender minarets and palm trees are painted in muted mauve pink and peach. The sun is a hot blaze low over rocky mountains. It is signed and dated 1874.","i":"Siout, now spelled Asyut, was one of the chief towns of Upper Egypt, on the Nile, with the desert hills behind it. Gifford visited Egypt on his journey of 1868 and 1869 and painted pictures from his sketches for years afterward, among them this one of 1874. Americans in the years after the Civil War took a lively interest in Egypt, which the recently opened Suez Canal made more accessible to tourists.","c":"The picture is a study in light as much as in place: the small figures and the faint town are almost dissolved by the hot haze, and the sun seems to burn through the picture. Gifford's Egyptian views succeed because he trusts atmosphere over detail, and the pale colors are a convincing equivalent of desert glare. The long, wide format emphasizes space, and the tiny people measure it. The foreground is somewhat empty, but the emptiness is the effect."},"cma:172806":{"d":"A forest interior in full autumn color, painted in oil on a small upright canvas, about 38 by 32 centimeters, and dated 1876. A dense network of overarching trees casts shadows on the rugged ground, so that sunlight reaches the path only in scattered patches. A solitary hiker is visible in the distance. The museum notes that Gifford painted several such forest interiors, of which this is an intimately scaled example.","i":"Forest interiors were a favorite subject of Hudson River painters, who studied sunlight filtered through leaves, and the autumn foliage of New England and New York was a national point of pride. The tiny walker gives scale and a sense of solitary communion with nature. The museum adds that Gifford Pinchot, who was named after the artist, became the first chief of the United States Forest Service, a reminder of how the age's love of wild woods grew into a conservation movement.","c":"A small, concentrated picture in which the light, rather than the trees, is the subject: the patches of sun on the ground and the blaze of autumn color make a rich but controlled effect. The scale suits it; Gifford's large pictures can feel vast and vague, while this one is close and attentive. The tiny hiker keeps the woods from feeling empty. It is one of the most appealing of his late interiors."},"nga:121547":{"d":"A wide field scattered with broken stones lies between two crumbling temples, lit by sunlight with a soft pink glow that warms the cream-white marble. On the left a row of columns, several broken, faces right, with fragments of pediment and wheel-like column drums tumbling from its steps. Two tiny men in the field inspect the fragments, one in an orange suit and bowler hat sketching, the other in a white tunic and red cap. At the right a brick-red tower stands beside a temple with columns carved as six robed women. Beyond, a peach plain meets blue water and distant mountains under a pink haze.","i":"The Parthenon, the great temple of Athena on the Acropolis, was a symbol of classical civilization, and it was ruined in 1687 when a Venetian shell exploded gunpowder stored inside it. The six robed women are the caryatids of the neighboring Erechtheion. Gifford traveled in the Mediterranean and the Near East in 1868 and 1869 and painted this view in 1880, the year of his death. The tiny sketching tourist among fallen marble makes the point: modern people look at antiquity across a gulf of time.","c":"A late work of great calm, in which the pink haze, the pale marble and the minute figures turn a famous ruin into a meditation on time. Gifford avoids the picturesque cliché of a single heroic temple, preferring a field of fragments and a wide, hazy space. The drawing of the architecture is more schematic than loving, and the pink can be sweet, but the scale of the men against the stones is touching and quite original."},"met:437082":{"d":"A scene of a baker's cart, painted in oil on canvas, about 98 by 125 centimeters, and dated 1656. The keywords list a baker, a woman, a man, a boy, bread and the cart, but the museum's record gives no description of the setting, poses or colors. The picture can be described only by its subject and type: a scene of ordinary working life in France, in which a baker delivers bread from a cart to customers, painted by Jean Michelin, who followed the Le Nain brothers in painting humble people with dignity.","i":"Bread was the main food of the French poor, and in the years after the Fronde, when war and bad harvests had caused hunger, a baker's cart was a matter of daily importance. Pictures of the humble were bought by collectors who admired their dignity and truth, and Michelin was among the few who made them a specialty. The record does not say whether the scene has a moral or charitable meaning, so its message is a matter for the viewer.","c":"Michelin shares the Le Nains' respect for the poor, who are shown without comedy or caricature, and the subject of bread gives the painting an almost sacramental plainness. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. If his work is less subtle than the best Le Nain, it is among the rare French pictures of everyday working life of its time, and is valued for that."},"met:437076":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman named Hannah Mahady, painted on ivory about 1760, the record giving neither a size nor a description of her features, dress or setting. The picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness meant to be held in the hand or set in a case, locket or frame, of the kind made by Jeremiah Meyer, the German-born London miniaturist who became painter in miniature to Queen Charlotte. The medium is given only as ivory, so the exact technique is not stated.","i":"Miniatures of the 1760s were fashionable tokens of friendship and affection in London, exchanged between family members, lovers and friends, and worn as jewelry or kept in a case. The record gives no information about Hannah Mahady beyond her name, so her rank and story are unknown. The date of about 1760 comes before Meyer's appointment to the queen, so the picture belongs to the period in which he was building his reputation.","c":"Meyer was among the best English miniaturists of his generation, admired for a refined, delicate touch and a quiet grace in the sitter's pose. The record gives no description of this miniature, so the verdict is general. His portraits can look cool and polite, lacking the warmth of Cosway or the crisp tone of Smart, but they are exquisitely finished, and they set a standard of elegance for the genre in the 1760s."},"met:438947":{"d":"A small oil study on paper laid down on cardboard, about 29 by 21 centimeters, shows an overgrown mineshaft. The museum's keywords record leaves, a doorway and ruins, so the masonry, the vegetation, the light and the colors are left unstated. It is a close-up study of the kind made outdoors, painted about 1824 by Carl Gustav Carus, who was a physician and naturalist as well as a landscape painter.","i":"Carus, a friend and follower of Caspar David Friedrich, wrote Nine Letters on Landscape Painting, in which he asked the painter to show the hidden life of the earth, and he was well versed in geology. An abandoned shaft overgrown with leaves is a Romantic emblem of nature reclaiming human work and of the passage of time, and a doorway into darkness also suggests a threshold between the visible and the hidden.","c":"Studies like this show what Romantic landscape could be before it was arranged into a finished picture: an intense, close look at a tangle of leaves around a dark opening. Carus's scientific training shows in the careful attention, while the dark doorway lends a poetic charge. It makes no grand statement, but its hush and precision are compelling, close in spirit to Friedrich's contemplative silence."},"met:439122":{"d":"A study of Gothic windows in the ruins of the monastery at Oybin, in an upright oil on canvas about 43 by 34 centimeters, painted about 1828. The museum's keywords record only windows, so the masonry, the vegetation, the sky and the light are left unstated. Oybin is a rocky hill in the Zittau Mountains of Saxony, crowned by a medieval castle and the shell of a monastery, a site that German Romantic painters visited and painted.","i":"Oybin, a rocky hill with a medieval castle and monastery long fallen into ruin, was a favorite of German Romantic painters; Caspar David Friedrich, Carus's friend and model, is among those who drew it. Empty Gothic windows were a powerful Romantic motif: they stood for the passage of time, for lost faith and for a medieval German past that people were rediscovering after the Napoleonic wars. Carus brought to such subjects his interest in the hidden life of the earth.","c":"A tight, almost architectural study in which the windows themselves are the subject. Carus brings a scientist's care to the stonework and a Romantic's feeling to the light, and the picture is quieter and more intimate than the grand ruins of Friedrich. The narrow upright format stresses the verticals of the Gothic openings; its merit lies in stillness more than drama. A fine example of Romantic ruin painting at close range."},"met:788905":{"d":"A house in a garden at night, lit by the moon among clouds, with trees, in an upright oil on canvas about 29 by 22 centimeters, painted about 1833 to 1835. The title names the house as Schloss Milkel, a manor in Upper Lusatia in Saxony. The museum's keywords record a garden, a house, night, trees, the moon and clouds, so the house's form, the color of the sky and the placement of the moon are left unstated.","i":"Moonlight was a favorite subject of German Romantic painters, who found in the night a mood of longing, silence and communion with nature. Carus, a friend of Caspar David Friedrich and a theorist of landscape, wrote of painting as a way of revealing the life of the earth, and night scenes allowed him to dwell on atmosphere over detail. The picture is small and intimate, and a country house seen by moonlight is more a mood than a portrait of a building.","c":"A small night piece that succeeds through economy: the pale light, the dark mass of garden trees and the faint silhouette of the house create a hush. Carus is less eerie than Friedrich, and less atmospheric than the Norwegian Johan Christian Dahl, but his scientific eye for cloud and light gives the scene credibility. It is a gentle, attractive picture in which feeling matters more than description."},"nga:127219":{"d":"A patchwork of cultivated fields slopes down to the right and back toward an ice-blue lake in this nearly square landscape. Rectangular fields in fern green, caramel brown and golden tan fill the lower half. Sage-colored trees and bushes with dark trunks separate the grassy strip nearest the viewer from the fields beyond and tuft the whole landscape. A rocky slate-gray mountain brushed with lime-green trees rises along the left edge and curves around a wedge of arctic-blue water. A few wispy clouds line a horizon halfway up the picture. It is an oil on paper laid on canvas, about 40 by 52 centimeters.","i":"Calame, a Swiss painter based in Geneva, became the leading Swiss landscapist of his generation, and his large Alpine views of cliffs, torrents and storms were sold across Europe to buyers who saw in Switzerland the image of sublime nature. This small picture is of a quieter kind: a farmed valley with a lake, painted with an eye to the structure of land and light. Oil sketches on paper were the usual way to gather material outdoors, to be used later in studio compositions.","c":"Free of the drama of Calame's large exhibition pictures, this small view has a clarity and a fresh geometry that make it more attractive than many of his finished canvases. The patchwork of fields reads as planes of flat color, and the cool blue lake gives depth and air. It is closer to the outdoor sketches of Corot and Valenciennes than to the Alpine sublime he sold. A modest, honest study that wears its immediacy well."},"nga:102195":{"d":"A dense forest scene with several fallen trees, seen from a slightly raised viewpoint that looks down into a chaotic tangle of trunks and branches. The bark is white and brown, patched with green moss or lichen, and the shredded interior of the broken wood shows light brown with reddish-brown in the shadows. The ground below is a light green. There is no sky and no distant view: the picture is a close study, painted in oil on paper laid on canvas, about 25 by 41 centimeters.","i":"Studies like this were made outdoors, as raw material for studio paintings. Calame, who made his reputation with Alpine views, drew and painted rocks, trunks and foliage in detail so that his large compositions would be convincing in every part. Fallen and splintered trees could also carry a Romantic meaning: they were evidence of storm, avalanche or lightning, the destructive forces of the mountains, and made fitting foreground elements in pictures of the sublime.","c":"Out of context a tangle of logs might be dull, but close observation makes it compelling. The pale bark and raw wood against green moss produce a strong, almost abstract pattern, and the viewpoint lets the eye move through the confusion. These studies show a Calame more direct than the polished finish of his exhibition pictures, and they sit comfortably beside the nature studies of his contemporaries in France and Germany."},"met:435686":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unidentified woman, dated 1773, painted on ivory by the British miniaturist John Bogle. The title gives only the initials MCC, presumably recorded on the miniature or its case, and the museum's record gives no size and no description of her features, hair or dress. The picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness made to be held in the hand, worn, or kept in a locket or case.","i":"Initials were often placed on the reverse of a miniature, sometimes worked in hair or gold wire, to identify the sitter to the person who owned it, and this practice explains titles like this one. Without a name the woman's rank and story are lost, and the picture survives as a keepsake whose private meaning has gone. The date of 1773 places it in the great age of the English portrait miniature.","c":"Bogle is a lesser-known name than Cosway or Smart, but his miniatures show the polish of the London school, and a woman's portrait gave him room for the fine detail of hair, lace and complexion. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. As an anonymous sitter she can be judged only as a type, but the craft is that of a skilled practitioner at a high point of the art."},"met:435685":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unidentified man, dated 1774, painted on ivory by John Bogle. The title gives only the initials AC, and the museum's record gives no size and no description of his features, hair or coat. The picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness made for the hand, a locket or a case, painted in the transparent watercolor technique that gave ivory miniatures their glow. It is a companion in style, though not necessarily a pendant, to Bogle's miniature of a woman dated the year before.","i":"A miniature of a man in 1774 might be a gift to a wife, fiancée or friend, or a likeness carried by a soldier, a traveler or an official, and such objects were valued as portable presences. The initials suggest that the owner of the object knew the sitter's name and that the record has lost it. The picture belongs to London's heyday for miniatures, when the demand for small likenesses was at its height.","c":"At about this date miniaturists were refining a smooth, luminous technique, and a male likeness tests a painter mainly through the face. The record gives no description, so the verdict is general. Bogle's competence is not in doubt, but without a named sitter the picture has to stand on craft alone, and it does so quietly."},"met:436769":{"d":"A small portrait of a young man, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 28 by 21 centimeters, in the 1480s. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress, pose or background, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-length Renaissance portrait by Jacometto, a rare Venetian artist known for small, jewel-like portraits and miniatures, of a size that could be held in the hand or placed on a table.","i":"Jacometto, called Veneziano, worked in Venice in the later fifteenth century and is known from a handful of tiny portraits and miniatures whose fine finish shows his debt to Netherlandish painting and his place among the Venetian artists who took up oil. A portrait of a young man in these years often marked a coming of age, a betrothal or a gift of friendship. The sitter is not named here, so his rank and occasion are unknown.","c":"Jacometto's few surviving portraits are among the most delicate of the Venetian fifteenth century, with a smooth surface, minute detail and a soft, clear light that owes much to Netherlandish models. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. His work can seem small in ambition beside the great Bellinis, but its precision and tenderness give it a lasting place, and it shows how widely Netherlandish technique spread in Venice."},"met:10895":{"d":"A still life of fruit, painted in oil on canvas, about 64 by 76 centimeters, around 1857. The museum's keywords list only fruit, and the record gives no description of the kinds of fruit, the table, the dishes or the background. The picture can be described only by its type: a mid-century American tabletop still life of the sort made by John F. Francis, who worked in Pennsylvania and specialized in abundant dessert tables of fruit, nuts and wine.","i":"American still life painting grew out of the Peale family of Philadelphia and became popular in the 1850s, when prosperous middle-class households wanted pictures of abundance for the dining room. Fruit signaled good fortune, hospitality and the bounty of the new country, and a still life hung where food was eaten. The record does not name the fruits, so any symbolic reading remains general.","c":"Francis has a plain, honest handling of fruit and a gift for glassware, with a solid, sometimes stiff arrangement that lacks the poetry of Peale's best. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. His still lifes are more admired today for their fresh, unpretentious sense of plenty than for sophistication, and they have an American directness that sets them apart from European models."},"nga:214118":{"d":"In an oval composition, a large chunk of watermelon with seeds sits at left and a cluster of grapes, still attached to a brown vine, at right, both resting on a table with a white tablecloth. Loose seeds and a smaller piece of fruit lie nearby. Reds, pinks, purples, greens and browns glow against a dark background. The painting is small, about 28 by 36 cm, oil on wood, dated 1863.","i":"Fruit still life became an American specialty in the nineteenth century, with Philadelphia painters such as the Peale family setting an early example. These pictures celebrated abundance and domestic comfort rather than carrying a hidden allegory, and a cut watermelon, a summer fruit and a rural pleasure, suited that mood. Hung in a middle-class parlor, such a panel offered a pleasure of the senses and showed the painter's skill with texture.","c":"Francis was a specialist, and the oval format, the dark ground and the saturated reds and purples give the fruit a jewel-like glow. The contrast between juicy flesh and the bloom of grape skin is the challenge, and the palette of reds, pinks and purples suggests that he understood it. The weakness is formula: the arrangement is plain, and variety comes from the fruit rather than from invention. It is a small, honest pleasure."},"nga:178114":{"d":"A tall, dark glass bottle labeled Old Cherry stands behind a decorative silver basket filled with confections, including a slice of yellow cake with white frosting and several yellow biscuits, all arranged on a table. Beside the bottle are two clear glasses of an amber liquid, with a silver knife and more yellow pastries scattered on the white tablecloth below. Greenery shows through a window at the far right, and the left side lies in shadow. The oil on canvas dates from 1866.","i":"A table of cake, pastry and cordial reflects the hospitality of a comfortable household: silver, glass and sweets were signs of means and of a mistress's care. The picture dates from the year after the end of the Civil War, and its plenty may be read as domestic peace restored. Dessert still lifes were popular with middle-class American buyers, who enjoyed seeing familiar luxuries rendered with such exactness.","c":"This is a showcase of surfaces: glass, silver, frosting, cloth, each demanding a different handling of light. The reflections in the silver and the transparency of the glasses are the real test, and the window with its glimpse of green gives the dark interior some air. It is more elaborate than Francis's fruit pieces and perhaps a little cluttered, but the technical assurance is considerable and the effect is appetizing."},"nga:214117":{"d":"A wicker basket filled with yellow pears and a few small red fruits, which the title calls crabapples, rests on a table. More fruit lies scattered in front of it, casting shadows on a white tablecloth. A white cloth with red trim and frayed edges hangs over the handle of the basket against a dark brown background. The picture is undated, small, about 30 by 32 cm, and painted in oil on board.","i":"Pears and crabapples are fruits of late summer and autumn, the sweet set against the tart, and the frayed cloth gives the basket a homely, country air. Such pictures were made for ordinary households rather than grand patrons: they stood for plenty and the pleasures of the farm, without any allegory beyond that. The modest scale and the plain brown ground keep the attention on the fruit.","c":"A modest picture that depends on a few means: the glow of the pears against the dark ground and the cast shadows on the white cloth. Making simple things look desirable is the whole task, and Francis does it with clarity of arrangement. The weakness is a sameness of formula, with the dark backdrop and tabletop recurring from picture to picture. It is a minor variation, but an honest and pleasant one."},"nga:46091":{"d":"A woman seen from about the waist up sits turned slightly to her left, her hands delicately holding a string of pearls. She has fair skin, rosy cheeks, dark eyes and tight dark curls partly pinned up with a green ornament, and her expression is composed. Her deep blue dress has a low neckline with white ruffled trim and matching ruffled cuffs, with a pink drape around her shoulders. Behind her a painted landscape shows a tree branching across the top left and, at the right, a distant sky turning to mountains and sparse foliage. It is dated 1749.","i":"A string of pearls was a favorite attribute in eighteenth-century portraits of women, a sign of wealth and purity, and the drape and the painted landscape were borrowed from the fashionable English portrait types that colonial painters knew from imported mezzotint engravings. John Greenwood, born in Boston in 1727, was about twenty-two when he made this portrait, probably there. The sitter's name is given in full, but the record says no more about her.","c":"A remarkable picture for a young painter in colonial Boston: the blues and pinks are clear and fresh, the hands are carefully drawn and the face has a calm, direct charm. The pose and landscape are borrowed, and the modeling is a little flat, which shows the limits of a provincial training, but there is a freshness and sincerity that more polished works lack. Greenwood left America in 1752 and spent most of his life in Europe."},"met:15080":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named Samuel Goodwin, Sr., painted in watercolor on ivory, about 7 by 5.7 centimeters, and undated in the record. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a miniature larger than most, with room for the head and shoulders. It is by John Hazlitt, the English painter, brother of the essayist William Hazlitt, who worked for a time in America.","i":"The suffix Sr. in the title shows that the sitter had a son of the same name, so he belonged to an established family. A miniature of this relatively generous size would have been displayed in a case or frame on a desk or wall rather than worn. The record gives no date, profession or place for the sitter, so the circumstances of the commission are unknown.","c":"Hazlitt was a competent portraitist of the second rank, and a miniature of this size lets the painter model the face broadly instead of dotting it in. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. A plain, unflattering manner suits an American sitter of this date, and the picture is best valued for its directness and for the family connection of its maker."},"met:436427":{"d":"A still life of game birds, painted in oil on a small upright canvas, about 46 by 36 centimeters, in the 1650s. The title and keywords give a partridge and small birds, but the record gives no description of how they are arranged, whether they are alive or dead, or what surrounds them. The picture can be described only by its type: a hunting still life by Jan Fyt of Antwerp, in which feathered game is the entire subject.","i":"Game birds were the prey of the nobility, who largely held the right to hunt them in the Spanish Netherlands, so a still life of partridges and small birds suggested the privileges and pleasures of the hunting class. It could also be read as a reminder of the brevity of life, since the birds' lovely plumage is a short-lived beauty. The record does not say for whom the picture was painted, so it should be seen as a type of collector's picture.","c":"Fyt was the most brilliant painter of game after Snyders, and a small vertical panel like this lets him concentrate on what he did best: the soft, layered texture of feathers and the sheen of plumage against a dark ground. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is general. He sometimes repeats compositions, but the fluent brushwork and clean, well-judged arrangement make even a modest picture lively."},"met:436424":{"d":"A still life with a basket and birds, painted in oil on canvas, about 60 by 77 centimeters, and undated in the record. The title and keywords name only a basket and birds, and the record gives no description of whether the birds are alive or dead, what the basket holds or what surrounds them. The picture can be described only by its type: a wide still life by Jan Fyt, of the kind produced in Antwerp for collectors who admired abundance and the rendering of textures.","i":"A basket was a common prop in still lifes of the game and the kitchen, serving to hold fruit, eggs or small birds and to add a contrasting texture of woven twigs to feathers and fur. Such pictures advertised plenty and the pleasures of the country estate, and the Antwerp market was known for them. The record does not say what the basket holds or whether the birds are alive, so no narrative or symbolic reading can be given.","c":"Fyt was a master of the contrast between soft feathers and hard woven twigs, and a basket gives him a good second texture to play against plumage. The record gives no description, so the verdict is general. His still lifes sometimes lack the monumental drama of Snyders's, but they are quicker, lighter in touch and more delicate in color, and a wide, quiet picture like this shows that gift without strain."},"met:436425":{"d":"A still life of a hare and birds, painted in oil on canvas, about 61 by 79 centimeters, and undated in the record. The keywords list birds and a rabbit, which in the museum's title is a hare, but the record gives no further description of the arrangement, the setting or the colors. The picture can be described only by its type: a game piece by Jan Fyt, of the kind made for patrons who prized the hunt and the table.","i":"The hare was a prize of the hunt and a favorite subject for painters of game, for the softness of its fur and the pathos of the subject, which could serve as a reminder that life is brief. With birds, it formed a typical display of the hunter's bag, a theme that appealed to aristocratic patrons and to prosperous citizens who aspired to their pleasures. The record does not describe the composition.","c":"A hare and some birds give Fyt the chance to set fur against feather, and his rapid, well-controlled brush excels at both. The record gives no description of the canvas, so the verdict is general. The subject is conventional and the arrangement presumably the pyramidal one that he and Snyders favored, but his touch is lively and his color is warm, and he stands as one of the finest painters of game of the seventeenth century."},"met:436426":{"d":"A large still life of a hare, partridges and fruit, with a dog, painted in oil on canvas, about 95 by 110 centimeters, and undated in the record. The keywords list fruit, birds, a hare and a dog, but the record gives no description of the poses, the setting or the colors. The picture can be described only by its type: a grand game piece by Jan Fyt, combining the hunter's bag with the fruits of the garden and the presence of a hunting dog.","i":"The combination of game and fruit set the produce of the forest beside the produce of the garden, a celebration of the abundance of the land, while the dog adds a living presence and recalls the hunter who is absent. Such large canvases were made for the dining rooms and galleries of rich Antwerp houses and palaces, and Fyt supplied a European market. The record does not describe the dog's role, so its meaning, whether watchful or playful, must remain open.","c":"At this scale Fyt's gifts are fully displayed: the sweep of the composition, the textures of fur, feather and fruit, and the lively inclusion of the dog. The record gives no description, so the verdict is general. Compositions of this kind can be a little crowded, with every inch of surface made to show skill, but the painter's confident, fluid brushwork and the sheer abundance of the subject carry it along."},"met:11845":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man titled Major Greene, B.A., painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5.1 by 3.8 centimeters, about 1777. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose. The rank in the title suggests an officer, and the date places the picture in the Revolutionary War years, when John Ramage, an Irish-born painter, was probably working in New York, then occupied by the British army. It is a small likeness made for the hand, a locket or a case.","i":"The letters B.A. after the name probably indicate a degree or an office, but the record does not explain them, and the sitter's regiment and story are unknown. A miniature of an officer was a standard keepsake for a wife or family left behind, or a gift to a friend, and in wartime such objects were especially precious. The picture is best read as a personal memento of a soldier of the war.","c":"Ramage, who had trained as a goldsmith, brought a goldsmith's precision to the miniature, with a clean outline and a finely worked surface. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. His miniatures are more careful than inspired, but they have a clarity and firmness that explain his success with sitters in a divided, wartime city."},"met:11841":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Gilbert Van Cortlandt, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.5 by 3.5 centimeters, about 1785. The museum's record gives no description of his features, hair or clothing, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, who worked in New York after the Revolutionary War. Van Cortlandt is the name of one of the old Dutch families of New York, so the picture belongs to Ramage's clientele among the city's established families.","i":"The Van Cortlandts were a leading New York dynasty of landowners and merchants, with a manor on the Hudson, and a member of the family would have been an obvious patron for a fashionable miniaturist in the city in the 1780s. The record does not say which Gilbert Van Cortlandt this was or what his profession was. The miniature probably marks a personal occasion, such as a marriage, and was meant to be kept in a case or locket.","c":"Ramage's New York sitters of the mid-1780s are among his most characteristic, painted with a firm outline, bright, slightly hard color and an unflattering directness. The record gives no description of this miniature, so the verdict is general. He is not as subtle as the best English miniaturists, but he is one of the principal chroniclers of New York's post-war society, and his work is valuable for that."},"met:11842":{"d":"A portrait miniature of George Clinton, governor of New York, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.4 by 3.5 centimeters, about 1785. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. Clinton was governor of the state from 1777 to 1795, so he held office when the miniature was painted.","i":"George Clinton was the first governor of New York after independence, a military leader who served as a brigadier general in the war and later became vice president of the United States under Jefferson and Madison. A miniature of a statesman was often made for his family or for a political friend, and sometimes copied for admirers. Clinton opposed the ratification of the Constitution in 1788, which gave him a prominent place in the political debate of the decade in which this likeness was made.","c":"A likeness of a public man at the height of his power, painted in the plain, direct style that suited New York politics. The record gives no description of the miniature, so the verdict is general. Ramage's firm outlines and clear color make his portraits easy to read, and a sitter as famous as Clinton shows how far his reputation reached."},"met:15157":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 3.1 centimeters, about 1780. The museum's record gives no description of his features, hair or clothing, so the picture can be described only by its type: a very small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. It dates from the years of the Revolutionary War, when Ramage was working in New York, then held by the British army.","i":"In wartime New York, soldiers, officials, merchants and refugees all sought likenesses to send or to leave behind, and a miniature was the most portable form. The sitter is not named, so whether he was a British officer, a loyalist or an American is unknown. Ramage, who had trained as a goldsmith, was well placed to make such keepsakes, since he understood how to fit a small picture into a locket or a case.","c":"At 3.8 centimeters high, the face must be established in a few precise touches, and Ramage's firm outline and clear tones do this competently. Nothing in the record describes the likeness, so any verdict must stay general. The miniature is unpretentious, with none of the elaborate costume or setting that would distinguish it from dozens of similar portraits, and its value is that of a faithful keepsake."},"met:15158":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Catherine Nicholson, named in the title as Mrs. William Few, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 3.1 centimeters, and dated 1787. The museum's record gives no description of her features, hair or dress, so the picture can be described only by its type: a very small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. It matches in date a miniature of William Few in the same museum.","i":"William Few of Georgia signed the Constitution in 1787 and became one of Georgia's first senators, so the pair of miniatures records a couple connected with the political life of the new republic, in the city that was then the seat of Congress. Miniatures of husband and wife were often made as a pair, to be kept together or exchanged. The record gives no details of the marriage or of the commission.","c":"The miniature has the clarity of Ramage's best work: a firm, even contour, bright but controlled color and a face drawn with a goldsmith's care. The record gives no description of this likeness, so the verdict is general. Alongside her husband's portrait it gains documentary interest, and it is a good example of the small-scale portraiture of the early republic."},"met:17540":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named James Bleecker, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 1788. The museum's record gives no size and no description of his features, hair or clothing, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. Two other Ramage miniatures in the museum, dated about 1788 and about 1795, show women titled Mrs. James Bleecker.","i":"Bleecker was an old New York name, borne by a family of merchants and landowners, and the fact that the Met holds several miniatures connected with it suggests that Ramage found regular patrons in the city's established circles. The record does not say which James Bleecker this was, so his profession and circumstances are not given. A man's miniature of this date was often made as a companion to his wife's, to be kept in the family.","c":"Ramage's male portraits of the late 1780s have a crisp, upright quality, with a firmly drawn head and a clear tone. The record gives no description of this miniature, so the verdict is general. He is an honest recorder rather than a flatterer, and in a family group like this his plainness helps to give the set a sober authority."},"met:11843":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named Gulian Ludlow, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.9 by 3.2 centimeters, about 1790. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small, upright likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. A miniature of his wife, Maria Ludlow, of about the same date is in the same museum.","i":"Gulian is a Dutch given name used in old New York families, and Ludlow was a well-known surname in the city's merchant circles, so the sitter probably belonged to that world. A husband and wife often had miniatures made together, to hang in a house or to be given to children, and the Ludlow pair seems to be such a set. The record gives no more information about his life.","c":"A competent, well-finished miniature in which the face is carefully drawn and the colors are clear and a little hard, as is typical of Ramage. The record gives no description of the sitter's expression, so the verdict is general. His portraits of New York merchants have a no-nonsense honesty that suits the subject, and the pair with Maria Ludlow gives the picture its place in a documented set."},"met:11846":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Maria Ludlow, called Mrs. Gulian Ludlow in the title, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 3 centimeters, about 1790. The museum's record gives no description of her features, hair or dress, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. It is the companion of a miniature of her husband in the same museum.","i":"Women's miniatures of the 1790s show the fashion of the new republic: dressed hair, a soft kerchief or lace at the neck and a simple gown. The record gives no description of this one, so these features are only the usual ones of the period. The title gives her husband's name first and then her own, a reminder that married women were habitually identified through their husbands.","c":"As a pair with her husband's portrait, the miniature gains meaning, and Ramage's firm, clean method gives both a family resemblance. The record gives no description of her likeness, so the verdict is general. A woman's miniature of this period often depends on delicate modeling of soft skin and lace, and Ramage, trained as a goldsmith, handled such detail with precision rather than with tenderness."},"met:11848":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.4 by 3.4 centimeters, about 1790. The museum's record gives no description of his features, hair or clothing, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage. By this date New York was ending its years as the capital of the United States, and Ramage was one of the best-known miniaturists in the city.","i":"Around 1790 New York was the seat of the new federal government, and the city's lawyers, merchants, officials and visitors made a ready market for small portraits. The sitter is not named, so he cannot be placed among them, and the picture survives as an anonymous record of the period's taste for the miniature as a personal, portable likeness. Ramage later left for Montreal, which gives his New York career a clear end.","c":"A competent anonymous portrait in Ramage's clear, firm manner. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general. When the sitter is unnamed, a miniature can be judged only on craft, and by that measure Ramage's work holds up: the outline is crisp, the color clean and the finish careful, though the likeness may lack the animation of the best English work."},"met:13385":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Elijah Boardman, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5.2 by 3.5 centimeters, about 1790. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. Boardman was a merchant of New Milford, Connecticut, and the Met also holds a large portrait of him by Ralph Earl.","i":"Boardman kept a store in New Milford and later served as a United States senator from Connecticut. The miniature, made about the same time as Earl's large picture, shows him in a smaller, more private form. The record does not say which was ordered first or where the miniature was painted, though Ramage's patrons were mostly in New York. Having himself portrayed twice suggests a man alert to the value of an image.","c":"Beside Earl's large portrait of the same man, which shows him as a merchant, the miniature is a quiet, intimate counterpart: a face and little else. The record gives no description of the likeness, so the verdict is general. Ramage gives the small face a clear outline and a steady expression, and the miniature adds a personal touch to the public image of a rising Connecticut businessman."},"met:14314":{"d":"A portrait miniature of William Few, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5.6 by 4.6 centimeters, about 1787. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a larger-than-usual miniature by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made to be kept in a case or frame. A miniature of Catherine Nicholson, titled Mrs. William Few, of the same date is in the same museum.","i":"William Few, a delegate from Georgia, signed the Constitution in 1787 and became one of Georgia's first United States senators. In 1787 the Congress sat in New York, where Ramage worked, so the miniature was probably painted there during a political season of great importance. The record gives no details of the commission, so it remains uncertain who ordered it.","c":"A larger miniature gives Ramage room to model the face more fully than in his smallest works, and the result is a sober, believable likeness of a politician in the middle of a historic year. The record gives no description of the picture, so the verdict is general. It lacks flattery and display, which suits a statesman of the Georgia backcountry, and its principal value is as a document of a signer of the Constitution."},"met:14357":{"d":"A portrait miniature of John Maunsell, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.1 by 3.2 centimeters, about 1785. The museum's keywords describe the sitter as a general, but the record gives no description of his features, uniform or pose, and does not give his regiment or service. The picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case, of an officer of senior rank.","i":"A general's miniature was both a keepsake and a record of rank, and officers often had small portraits made to give to wives and family before or after campaigns. A small portrait was far easier to carry on campaign than a canvas. The record does not say where the miniature was painted or which army the sitter served, so his story is not given here.","c":"Ramage's goldsmith's training gave his miniatures an exactness of outline and a firm, jewel-like finish, which suit a military sitter. The record gives no description of this likeness, so the verdict is general. Without uniform details to judge, the picture rests on the plain, clear head, and it is valuable chiefly as a documented portrait of a man of rank by one of the principal miniaturists in the New World at the time."},"met:17541":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Theophylact Bache, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 1790. The museum's record gives no size and no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. The Met holds two Ramage miniatures of women born Bache, titled Mrs. James Bleecker, which suggests that the three belong to one family.","i":"Theophylact Bache was an English-born merchant of New York, and a brother of Richard Bache, who married Benjamin Franklin's daughter, so his family had ties to both the city's commerce and the circle of the founders. A merchant's miniature of about 1790 was often made as a gift to a spouse or child. The record gives no more detail about the commission.","c":"A typical Ramage likeness of a New York merchant: firm, clear and well finished, with no attempt to flatter. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. The family group of miniatures, spread across several works in the same museum, adds to its interest, since it lets a viewer see how one painter served an extended New York family over several years."},"met:15159":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named Thomas Witter, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.3 by 3.5 centimeters, about 1786 to 1787. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. The date places it in the years when New York was the seat of Congress and Ramage was active in the city.","i":"The record gives only the sitter's name, with no profession, age or family, so little more can be said about who he was. In the later 1780s New York was crowded with merchants, lawyers and officials, and the demand for small portraits came from all of them. A miniature of this kind marked a marriage, a departure or a friendship, and was valued as a personal object rather than as a public portrait.","c":"A competent Ramage with a crisp contour and clean color, and no more can be said for it without a description in the record. The verdict is therefore general: the miniature is neither remarkable nor weak, and it stands as one of many sound portraits that made up his steady practice. Its value is in the name that it preserves."},"met:17515":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Elizabeth Garland Bache, titled Mrs. James Bleecker, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 4.8 by 4.1 centimeters, about 1788. The museum's record gives no description of her features, hair or dress, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. A miniature of James Bleecker of about the same date is in the same museum.","i":"The title gives her husband's name first and her birth name in brackets, showing that she was born a Bache, a New York merchant family also represented in the museum by a miniature of Theophylact Bache. In the 1780s a married woman's portrait was commonly made for her husband or her family as a keepsake, and a pair with the husband's own miniature was a normal arrangement. The record does not state the family relationships.","c":"A woman's miniature of this period tests a painter's skill with soft detail, and Ramage handles it with a clear, even touch rather than with the charm of the English school. The record gives no description of this likeness, so the verdict is general. Grouped with the other Bleecker and Bache miniatures, it contributes to a sober, well-documented family record of New York at the turn of the republic."},"met:11840":{"d":"A portrait miniature of George Washington, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5.2 by 3.5 centimeters, about 1789. The museum's record gives no description of his features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. The date is that of Washington's inauguration as the first president of the United States, in New York.","i":"Washington took the oath of office in New York on April 30, 1789, and portraits of him were in constant demand from admirers and from his family and friends. A miniature could be easily copied and given, and it carried the presence of a great man into a private room. The record does not say whether Washington sat for Ramage or whether the portrait draws on other likenesses, so its status as a life study is a matter for scholars.","c":"A sober, small likeness rather than a heroic icon: the face matters more than any display. Ramage's honest, goldsmith-precise style suits the subject, though it lacks the grandeur of the large portraits by Stuart or Peale. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. As one of the small portraits made of Washington in New York, it has the interest of contact with its subject, and is valued for that more than for artistic brilliance."},"met:11847":{"d":"A memorial miniature, painted in watercolor on ivory and set in a gold locket of marquise shape, about 3.1 by 1.9 centimeters, and dated 1794. The title and the keywords, a woman and sadness, indicate a mourning picture for a woman named Mary Long, but the record gives no description of the scene. Mourning miniatures of the period usually show a grieving figure beside an urn or tomb under a willow, often with a lock of hair; this describes the type, not this picture.","i":"Memorial miniatures were made after a death to console the living, and the sentimental culture of the 1790s prized open expressions of grief. The title does not say whether Mary Long is the person mourned or the one who mourned, though Memorial to suggests the former. Such pieces were worn as jewelry, so private grief became a visible sign of loyalty to the dead. The record gives no more information about her.","c":"A tiny object, only about 3 centimeters tall, and as a piece of goldsmith's and miniaturist's work it is a precise little jewel, which suits Ramage's training as a goldsmith. The record gives no description of the image, so the verdict is general. Such memorials depend on conventional symbols and are seldom individual, and their appeal is emotional and technical; this one has the added interest of a documented date and a surviving locket."},"met:17514":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Sarah Bache, titled Mrs. James Bleecker, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 5.4 by 3.5 centimeters, about 1795. The museum's record gives no description of her features, hair or dress, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness by the Irish-born painter John Ramage, made for the hand, a locket or a case. The date falls around the time that Ramage moved to Montreal, so it may be one of his last American miniatures.","i":"Two women born Bache are titled Mrs. James Bleecker in the museum's records, one dated about 1788 and this one about 1795, and the record does not explain the relationship; they may be successive wives, relatives, or both. The title puts her husband's name first and her own in brackets, which was the habit of the period. The miniature was presumably made for her family, as a keepsake and a record of her place in the Bache and Bleecker circle.","c":"By the mid-1790s Ramage's manner was settled: firm outline, clear color, a smooth finish and a plain sense of the sitter. The record gives no description of this likeness, so the verdict is general. It is a sound, unpretentious picture, valued as part of a family group of miniatures, and it does not compete with the more brilliant work of the English miniaturists of the same decade."},"met:15156":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed man in a red coat, painted in watercolor on ivory and set in a gold locket, about 3.2 by 3.5 centimeters, and undated in the record. The title identifies the sitter as an officer, but the record gives no description of his features, regiment or facings. The picture can be described only by its type: a small military keepsake, of the sort an officer's family or friends would wear or keep.","i":"The red coat was the uniform of the British army, which occupied New York during the Revolutionary War, and a Ramage miniature of an officer probably belongs to those years or to the garrisons that followed. Wearing a likeness in a locket was a private act of affection, and an officer's portrait could be sent home to a family or left with a sweetheart. The record gives no name, so the man's regiment and story are lost.","c":"A scarlet coat gives a miniaturist a strong, simple color to set against pale flesh and ivory, and Ramage, with his goldsmith's training, makes the locket as much a part of the object as the picture. The record gives no description of the likeness, so the verdict is general. The type depends on bold color and formal pose more than on character, and the picture is best valued as a vivid little souvenir of the war years."},"met:437579":{"d":"A portrait of William Man Godschall (1720 to 1802), drawn in pastel on paper laid down on canvas, about 60 by 45 centimeters, and dated 1791, when the sitter was about seventy. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-sized pastel portrait by John Russell, the leading English pastellist of the day, made as a pendant to the portrait of his wife, Sarah Godschall, of the same size and date.","i":"Pastel, a dry medium of powdered pigment pressed into sticks, was the fashionable alternative to oil in eighteenth-century portraiture, prized for its soft, velvety surface and its quick execution. Russell, who was appointed painter in crayons to the king, produced portraits of the gentry in large numbers. A pair of pastels of an older couple, in uniform size, was made to hang together in the house as a family record.","c":"Russell's pastels are famous for their smooth, glowing surface and their exactness of likeness, and a sitter of seventy gave him scope for honest observation of age. The record gives no description of this portrait, so the verdict is general. His portraits can be a little glossy and tight, and the flesh tones sometimes harden, but at his best he combines fidelity with a gentle, domestic charm that oil rarely allowed."},"met:437583":{"d":"A portrait of Sarah Godschall (1730 to 1795), wife of William Man Godschall, drawn in pastel on paper laid down on canvas, about 60 by 45 centimeters, and dated 1791, when she was about sixty-one. The museum's keywords list lace, a shawl, a cap, a bow, a ribbon and a bouquet, details of her dress and of the flowers in the picture, but the record gives no further description of her pose or colors. It is the pendant to her husband's pastel by John Russell.","i":"A cap of lace with ribbons and a shawl were the usual dress of a respectable woman of mature age in the early 1790s, and a bouquet is a conventional sign of grace, affection and the passing beauty of flowers. As the pendant to her husband's portrait of the same year, the picture records an older couple at home. She died in 1795, four years after the portrait was made.","c":"The lace, ribbon and flowers are the kind of detail on which a pastellist's reputation rests, since the soft chalks render gauze and bloom with an ease oil cannot match. The record gives no description of this portrait, so the verdict is general. Russell's women can look a little polished, but his treatment of faces that have aged is truthful and kind, and the portrait has a gentle domestic authority."},"met:437580":{"d":"A double portrait of a mother and her daughter, drawn in pastel on paper laid down on canvas, about 61 by 45 centimeters, and dated 1801. The title names the mother as Mrs. Robert Shurlock, born Henrietta Ann Jane Russell (1775 to 1849), and the child as Ann. The keywords list a turban, a cap, lace and a shawl, but the record gives no further description of the poses or colors. The picture can be described only by its type: a mother-and-child pastel by John Russell.","i":"The mother's maiden name was Russell, the same as the artist's, and she was probably his daughter, so the picture is likely a family portrait made by a father of his daughter and granddaughter, an unusually personal commission. Turbans were fashionable head-dresses of the years around 1800, reflecting the Oriental taste of the period. The record does not state the relationship, so it should be treated as probable rather than certain.","c":"A mother and child offer Russell the chance for a warmth that formal commissions deny him, and the keywords of turban, lace and shawl promise his usual delicate skill with fabric. The record gives no description of the surface, so the verdict is general. A portrait of 1801 shows the late style of a man who had perfected pastel but not renewed it, though the family tie gives it feeling that routine commissions lack."},"met:437581":{"d":"A portrait of Ann Manwaring, titled Mrs. Robert Shurlock Sr., drawn in pastel on paper laid down on canvas, about 61 by 45 centimeters, and dated 1801. The keywords list a shawl, a bow, a cap, lace and a snuffbox, details of dress and accessories, but the record gives no further description of her pose, age or colors. The picture can be described only by its type: a bust-sized pastel by John Russell of a woman of the older generation of the Shurlock family.","i":"Snuff-taking was a common habit of both sexes in the eighteenth century, and a snuffbox in a woman's portrait is an uncommon, individual detail, a sign of personality rather than of formal display. Together with the cap, lace and shawl, it marks a woman of comfortable middle-class standing. She is presumably the mother of Robert Shurlock, whose portrait of the same year is in the same museum.","c":"The snuffbox gives this portrait a human note beyond the usual lace and ribbons, and Russell's smooth, exact pastel handling serves the details well. The record gives no description of the face, so the verdict is general. As part of a set of family portraits made in the same year, it has more interest as a collective record than as an individual great work."},"met:437582":{"d":"A portrait of Robert Shurlock (1772 to 1847), drawn in pastel on paper laid down on canvas, about 60 by 44 centimeters, and dated 1801, when the sitter was about twenty-nine. The museum's record gives no description of his dress, pose or expression, so the picture can be described only by its type: a bust-sized pastel by John Russell of a young man in the prime of life, one of three Shurlock family pastels of the same year in the same museum.","i":"Robert Shurlock was probably the husband of Henrietta Ann Jane Russell, who appears with her daughter in another pastel in the same museum, and the son of Ann Manwaring, whose own portrait of the same year is also there. That makes this picture part of a family set in which the painter was himself a member by marriage. The record gives no profession for the sitter.","c":"A direct, uncluttered portrait of a young man: Russell's pastel gives the skin a luminous finish and the eyes a clear focus. The record gives no description of the likeness, so the verdict is general. Within the family group the picture is probably the most conventional, since a young man's dress leaves little scope for the lace and fabric in which Russell excelled, and its interest lies in the sitter's connection with the artist."},"met:437691":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Mrs. Comyns, drawn in pencil with some watercolor on paper, about 1760. The museum's record gives no size and no description of her features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a delicate portrait drawing, tinted in places with watercolor, by John Smart, who was then about nineteen and later became one of the leading English miniaturists.","i":"In the 1750s and 1760s Smart studied at William Shipley's drawing school in London and won prizes from the Society of Arts, and drawings like this belong to his early years, when he was making portraits in pencil and wash before he turned to ivory miniatures. A woman identified only by her married name can be placed only as one of the sitters of that early period. The record does not say who Mrs. Comyns was.","c":"Early Smart drawings show a precocious refinement: a light, exact touch with the pencil, clear contours and a modest tint of watercolor. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. Without the full color and polish of his later ivories, a drawing like this has the charm of an early, direct statement, and it shows that his skills were already established in the 1760s."},"met:437692":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Mrs. Caroline Deas, drawn in pencil and watercolor on paper, about 1760. The museum's record gives no size and no description of her features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small portrait drawing in the early manner of John Smart, who later became one of the leading English miniaturists. The watercolor is added to the pencil drawing to give color to the face and dress.","i":"The title gives the sitter a married name and a first name, Caroline, but the record offers no further information. Deas is a surname known in colonial South Carolina, which may or may not be relevant, since the record does not establish a connection. The picture is best read as a private portrait drawing of a woman of standing in the 1760s.","c":"A clear, unpretentious drawing in which line does most of the work and color is limited to a light wash. The record gives no description, so the verdict is general. As an early product of Smart's training, it is an attractive and careful record, and it differs from his later miniatures mainly in the freer, more open handling that paper allows."},"met:437693":{"d":"A portrait of Sir George Armytage, fourth baronet (1761 to 1836), drawn in pencil and watercolor on paper, possibly about 1763, when he was a small child of about two. The keywords list a boy, but the museum's record gives no size and no description of his dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a delicate portrait drawing of a very young child by John Smart, who was then in his early twenties.","i":"Portraits of young children were commissioned by parents who wished to record a child's appearance, and in families of landed rank like the Armytages, who held a baronetcy in Yorkshire, they preserved the heir's early years. The title gives the sitter his later rank rather than his rank at the time, a reminder that museum titles describe the man the child became. The record's word possibly signals that the date is uncertain.","c":"A child's portrait is among the hardest tests of a miniaturist, since a small face has few features to fix a likeness, and the painter must catch charm without sentimentality. The record gives no description of the drawing, so the verdict is general. Smart's early skill, evident in the fine, clear contour of such works, makes this an attractive document of an early English child portrait, though dating and condition cannot be judged from the record."},"met:437689":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Sir William Hood, painted on ivory about 1766 by John Smart. The museum's record gives no size and no description of his features, dress or pose, and the medium is stated only as ivory, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small likeness made for the hand, a locket or a case. The title gives the sitter's rank, Sir, but does not say whether he was a knight or a baronet.","i":"By 1766 Smart had moved from pencil drawings to miniatures on ivory, and the commissions he received came from the gentry and the professional classes of London. A sitter with a knighthood or baronetcy was typical of a successful young miniaturist's clientele. The record gives no further information about him, so no more can be safely said about his life or the occasion of the portrait.","c":"In the mid-1760s Smart was in his mid-twenties and already admired for the neatness and precision of his stipple and the clarity of his faces. The record gives no description of the miniature, so the verdict is general. An early Smart on ivory shows the promise of the artist who would become one of the two leading English miniaturists of his generation, and its crispness makes a good contrast with his freer drawings."},"met:437694":{"d":"A portrait of an unmarried woman titled Miss Ramus, drawn in pencil and watercolor on paper, about 1770. The museum's record gives no size and no description of her features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait drawing in the manner of John Smart's early work, with the pencil lines visible and the watercolor added lightly. The title does not give her first name.","i":"The sitter's family name, Ramus, is given without a first name, so her identity within the family is not stated. A drawn portrait of an unmarried woman around 1770 would typically record a particular moment, for example an engagement or a coming of age, and be kept in a family album or a case. The record gives no information about the commission, so these are only the usual purposes of such pictures.","c":"Smart's drawings of the 1770s show a refined, fluent line, with watercolor used sparingly to give the face and dress a gentle bloom. The record gives no description of this one, so the verdict is general. The charm of such drawings is in their transparency: nothing is hidden, and each stroke of the pencil can be read, which was a quality his more finished miniatures on ivory could not display."},"met:437690":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Mrs. Charlotte Lennox, drawn in pencil and watercolor on card, and dated 1777. The museum's record gives no size and no description of her features, dress or pose, so the picture can be described only by its type: a small portrait in the manner of a miniature, by John Smart, who usually signed and dated his work. The medium is card, not the more usual ivory.","i":"The name Charlotte Lennox is best known from the novelist and poet of that name (about 1730 to 1804), author of The Female Quixote and a friend of Samuel Johnson, but the record does not say that she is the sitter, and Lennox was not a rare surname. If it is she, the portrait shows a woman writer in the year 1777, a period when women of letters became public figures. As it stands, the identification is only a possibility.","c":"Smart's work in 1777 is at its height: crisp, luminous and carefully dated, and the choice of card over ivory may signal a more informal commission. The record gives no description of the portrait, so the verdict is general. Whoever the sitter, a Smart of this date is a safe example of English miniature painting at its best, with a lightness of touch that few of his rivals matched."},"met:437695":{"d":"A portrait of a woman who is said to be Lady Dering, drawn in pencil and watercolor on paper, and undated in the record. The title uses the cautious phrase said to be, so the identification of the sitter rests on tradition and not on documents. The museum's record gives no size and no description of her features or dress, so the picture can be described only by its type: a portrait drawing in the manner of John Smart's early work.","i":"Lady Dering would be the wife of a baronet or knight named Dering, probably one of the baronets of Surrenden Dering in Kent, but the record does not say which. The phrase said to be is a warning that such identifications are inherited from owners and dealers and cannot always be tested. The picture is best read as a portrait of a woman of rank whose name remains uncertain.","c":"A typical example of Smart's pencil and watercolor portraits: precise, delicate and economical. The record gives no description, and the uncertain identity limits what can be said about the sitter, so the verdict is general. As a drawing it has the freshness of an immediate record, and its value does not depend on the name attached to it."},"nga:138852":{"d":"Three tall three-masted ships under full sail float in calm Arctic water among icebergs and ice floes, spaced across the picture with the leftmost, nearest ship the largest. Rowboats bring men alongside, and others haul massive slabs of whale blubber up a ship's side or walk on a nearby floe. Closer to the viewer a trio of seals sits on a floe and a polar bear stands nose to nose with a cub, while narwhals, a whale's tail and two walruses break the surface, and birds fly low across the water. Clouds with ivory tops and lavender undersides curve over the left half of the sky.","i":"Hull was one of Britain's chief whaling ports in the early nineteenth century, and its ships sailed each spring to the Greenland and Davis Strait grounds to hunt whales for oil, which lit lamps and greased the machines of the age. The title names two ships, presumably Hull whalers, and a picture like this was probably made for an owner or a captain to commemorate them. Ward packs the polar animals into the foreground as a natural history spectacle.","c":"John Ward was a ship portraitist of Hull, and this small, crowded canvas serves description: the ships are drawn with a shipwright's care, the clouds are well observed, and the polar animals add curiosity. The animals are cheerfully arranged for the viewer's benefit, like a natural history chart, and the grim work of stripping blubber is kept low-key, so it is a document more than a drama. It is a fine record of a vanished industry."},"met:12641":{"d":"A portrait of Jonathan Dwight, painted in oil on canvas, about 75 by 67 centimeters, about 1790, and attributed to Joseph Steward. The museum's record gives no description of the sitter's age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a New Englander by a painter who was a Congregational minister as well as an artist in Connecticut and who later opened a museum in Hartford.","i":"The Dwights were a large and prominent New England family, which produced ministers, merchants and judges, but the record does not establish the sitter's place within it. A portrait of about 1790 was made for the family, often as a record of a man at a point of achievement, and the attribution to Steward reflects the museum's caution about the artist. The record gives no further information on the commission.","c":"Steward was largely self-taught, and his portraits are sincere and a little stiff, with flat modeling and a plain, clear design of the sort found among untrained New England portraitists. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. The attraction of such pictures is in their direct, unpretentious observation, a quality that more polished painters of the period sometimes lose."},"nga:195701":{"d":"A portrait of Elizabeth Stevens Carle, painted in oil on canvas, about 97 by 80 centimeters, about 1783 to 1784. The museum's record gives no description of her age, dress, pose or setting, so the picture can be described only by its type: a half-length portrait of a woman, by the American painter Joseph Wright, painted in the years just after his return from England and France.","i":"Wright learned his craft in London, where his mother, the wax modeler Patience Wright, took him as a boy, and he studied with Benjamin West before returning to America in 1783. A portrait of 1783 to 1784 thus belongs to the first months of the new republic, when a painter trained in London brought a polished style to American sitters. He died young, in 1793, of yellow fever in Philadelphia. The record gives no further information about the sitter.","c":"Wright's portraits combine London polish with a firm, observant handling, and at their best have a directness that lifts them above provincial work. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. He died at about thirty-seven, and because his surviving work is small, a portrait of 1783 to 1784 is notable as a document of the transition from colonial to republican portraiture."},"met:436889":{"d":"A scene of peasants dancing, painted in oil on canvas, about 67 by 75 centimeters, and dated 1651. The keywords list dancing, a man, a woman and a dog, but the record gives no description of the setting, costumes or musicians. The picture can be described only by its type: a lively peasant dance in the open air, by Johannes Lingelbach, a German-born painter who worked in Amsterdam after spending several years in Rome and Italy.","i":"Peasant dances were a favorite subject of Flemish and Dutch painters, who showed rustic festivity as a lively spectacle and sometimes as a warning about drunkenness and lust. Lingelbach, who had returned from Italy in 1650, belonged to the group of northern painters who took the open-air genre scenes of Rome's Italianate painters back to the Netherlands. The record does not say whether the setting is Italian or Dutch, so the mood is described here only in general terms.","c":"Lingelbach's figures are lively, quick and painted with a light, sparkling touch, and he was often asked to supply the people in other artists' landscapes. The record gives no description of this canvas, so the verdict is general. He is a skilled painter of the second rank, whose peasants lack the earthy weight of Brouwer's or Ostade's, but whose sense of movement and warm Italian light give a dance scene real charm."},"met:436890":{"d":"A large battle scene, painted in oil on canvas, about 113 by 161 centimeters, and dated 1671. The keywords list soldiers, men, a battle and horsemen, but the record gives no description of the opposing forces, the landscape or the moment of the fight. The picture can be described only by its type: a cavalry battle of the kind painted in the Netherlands in the seventeenth century, by Johannes Lingelbach, who is better known for Italianate market, harbor and peasant scenes.","i":"Battle pictures of the Dutch Golden Age, popularized by Philips Wouwerman, showed clashes of cavalry with galloping horses, smoking guns and swirling clouds, and were bought by collectors who enjoyed the spectacle without much concern for a particular historical event. The picture was made a year before the French invasion of the Dutch Republic in 1672, though no link to the war is recorded. The record does not identify the battle.","c":"A battle scene gave Lingelbach, who was good at figures in motion, a chance to show horses, riders and smoke in quick, small strokes. The record gives no description, so the verdict is general. The type is energetic but impersonal, with little of the psychological interest of history painting, and Lingelbach's lighter, decorative touch suits it better than a grim account would. The best of such pictures are admired for their movement."},"nga:46177":{"d":"Christ stands almost nude in a shallow river, in a white loincloth, hands raised to his chest, while John the Baptist, in a camel-skin robe, kneels on the bank and pours water over his head. Angels flank the pair, one holding Christ's cloak, and two small angels play a lute and a violin. Above them fifteen figures, among them clergy and saints with attributes such as a sword, a bishop's crook and a basket of wafers, and a man in armor kneeling on a dragon, form an arch against a gold ground. At the top God the Father sits crowned, with a scroll beneath him.","i":"The Latin scroll quotes the voice from heaven at the Baptism, declaring Jesus the beloved Son with whom God is well pleased, so the picture shows the first public revelation of the Trinity, with Christ in the water and the Father above. The saints floating in an arch belong to the heavenly court, and the man with a dragon is probably Saint George, though the record does not name him. The gold ground and the vision-like arch were older conventions that Cologne painters kept alive.","c":"A rich, crowded and unusual composition, in which the naturalistic river scene below is joined to a golden vision of saints above. The Master of the Saint Bartholomew Altar, a Cologne painter, combines Netherlandish observation, seen in the faces, the landscape and the beautifully rendered robes, with an older taste for gold and ornament. The picture can feel overfull, with the heavenly arch crowding the central drama, but the color is brilliant and the angels' music gives it charm."},"met:435998":{"d":"Harry Paulet (1719/20–1794), an admiral and the sixth Duke of Bolton, is portrayed on a canvas of about 127 by 102 cm, which at 50 by 40 inches is the standard English size for a half-length portrait. The title gives both his naval rank and his noble title, the two sides of his public identity. Francis Cotes was the main London rival of Reynolds in the 1760s and a founding member of the Royal Academy.","i":"Portraits of naval officers often carry signs of service, and portraits of peers carry signs of rank; this sitter was both, a career sailor who also held a dukedom. A portrait like this told the world, and the family's descendants, that its subject served the nation as well as owning land. Such pictures hung in country houses as part of a gallery of ancestors, a family's visual argument for its standing.","c":"Cotes had a gift for fresh, fluent likenesses, especially in pastel, and in oil his handling is smooth and cool, with a taste for fine fabrics and fashionable color. Beside Reynolds he is less ambitious in invention, and beside Gainsborough less spontaneous. A half-length of a naval duke is a solid commission, competent and polished. Cotes died in 1770 at forty-four, so portraits like this are the legacy of a career cut short."},"nga:46093":{"d":"A woman is shown in side profile, facing left, within an oval border against a dark green ground. Her hair is elegantly dressed and threaded with pearls, her complexion is fair, her nose delicate and her lips softly closed. She wears a light pink gown with gathered fabric at the bodice, covered by a black lace shawl, and a necklace. The dress is that of the mid-eighteenth century, and the painted oval recalls a cameo. Oil on canvas, about 78 by 63 centimeters, painted about 1768 to 1770 by the English portraitist Francis Cotes.","i":"A profile view was rare in English portraiture of the 1760s, when sitters were normally shown in three-quarter view, and it recalls the antique cameo and medal, a taste fed by the Neoclassical interest in Greek and Roman art. The sitter is named by her husband's name, Mrs. Thomas Horne, and the pearls and lace mark a refined, fashionable woman. Cotes made his reputation with pastel portraits, and this oil carries some of their soft, silvery delicacy.","c":"A refined, quiet picture whose profile and oval frame keep the sitter at an elegant distance. The pink gown, black lace and pearls are handled with the lightness Cotes learned in pastel, and the dark green ground sets off the fair complexion. It is less penetrating than the best of Reynolds or Gainsborough, and the pose gives little inner life, but it shows the polished style that made Cotes one of the most fashionable portraitists of the late 1760s."},"met:437282":{"d":"A portrait of a man with a celestial globe, painted in oil on a wood panel, about 105 by 76 centimeters, and dated 1624. The museum's keywords list the man, the portrait and the globe, but the record gives no description of his age, dress, pose or setting, and does not name him. The picture can be described only by its type: an upright portrait of a learned or professional Amsterdam man, with the globe as the single object chosen to describe his interests, by Nicolaes Eliasz Pickenoy.","i":"A celestial globe, showing the constellations, marked an astronomer, navigator, mathematician or teacher, or a man who wished to be seen as learned and worldly. In Amsterdam in the 1620s the city's merchants and shipowners depended on navigation, and leading globe makers were based there. Because the sitter is not named, his profession remains a question, and the globe may be as much a statement of intellectual standing as of occupation.","c":"Pickenoy was one of Amsterdam's leading portraitists before Rembrandt arrived, and his work is sober, exact and dignified, with a clear, even light and careful rendering of face and costume. The record gives no description of this panel, so the verdict is general. His portraits can look stiff beside those of Rembrandt or Hals, but their honesty and finish are admirable, and a portrait with an object like the globe has more individuality than his routine commissions."},"met:816515":{"d":"A large allegorical picture, painted in oil on canvas, about 150 by 194 centimeters, between about 1665 and 1675. The keywords list a man, a lute, a musical instrument and Cupid, together with the allegory of the title, but the record gives no description of the figures, their number, poses or colors. The picture can be described only by its subject and type: a Baroque allegory of two kinds of love, by Michele Desubleo, a Flemish-born painter who worked in Italy.","i":"Sacred and profane love was a theme from Renaissance philosophy, which distinguished a heavenly love of the spirit from an earthly love of the senses and made them two aspects of Venus. Titian's famous painting on the subject, in Rome, set the standard, and Baroque painters presented the theme as a choice or a contrast, often with Cupid as a mediator. A lute is a standard sign of earthly love and music, but the record does not describe how the picture uses it.","c":"Desubleo was a follower of Guido Reni, and his best figures have Reni's clear contour, pale, smooth flesh and sweet expression, which suits an allegory. The record gives no description of the composition, so the verdict is general. His later manner tends toward a smooth elegance, more graceful than intense, but the subject allows a sensuous, decorative treatment, and the picture is a good example of the Bolognese tradition carried beyond Bologna."},"nga:177155":{"d":"Four boys sit or lean against boxes, a wooden platform and a stone wall on a quay. At the left one sits on the platform, leaning back with his legs crossed, in a blue shirt, brown trousers and a wide-brimmed hat. Beside him two others stand or sit on stacked boxes, one in light trousers, a buttoned vest and a light jacket, the other behind in a red shirt, dark vest, jacket and brown hat. The fourth leans on the low wall at the right in dark-blue trousers, a brown jacket and a straw hat. Behind them is a busy harbor of docked sailing ships under a pale gray sky.","i":"The title uses a period word for the boys that is now considered outdated and offensive, and it is kept here only as the museum's record of the work. Nineteenth-century harbor scenes with Black children and workers were often painted by white artists as picturesque genre subjects, and the viewpoint is that of an outside observer. The record does not say where the quay is, who the boys were or what the artist's relationship to them was.","c":"The four boys are clearly differentiated by their poses and clothes, grouped in a loose, relaxed arrangement that gives the picture a natural, observed look, and the busy harbor behind them gives context. The record does not describe their faces, so the judgment cannot go further. The picture's interest lies in making children the subject of a harbor scene, but the vagueness of its documentation, and the outside viewpoint of its painter, limit what can be known from it."},"met:436788":{"d":"A portrait of a man in oil on canvas, about 104 by 80 centimeters, dated 1648, by Cornelis Jonson van Ceulen the Elder, the London-born portraitist who by then was working in the Dutch Republic. His name is unknown. The size and date match those of the companion Portrait of a Woman, so the two were presumably painted as a pair.","i":"Pendant portraits of a husband and wife were a standard commission in the Dutch Republic, hung facing each other to represent a marriage and a household. The man here is unnamed, so his rank and profession are unknown, and the pairing with the woman's portrait is an inference from the matching size, date and artist. Jonson's Dutch sitters in the 1640s came largely from the prosperous burgher class, who wanted a refined, sober likeness.","c":"Jonson's Dutch portraits tend to be cooler and more delicate than his earlier English ones: soft modeling, a silvery tonality and restrained poses. As one half of a pair it presumably carries the polite, understated manner for which he was valued. Its interest is chiefly historical, as a document of mid-century Dutch taste in marriage portraits."},"met:436789":{"d":"A portrait of a woman in oil on canvas, about 104 by 80 centimeters, dated 1648, by Cornelis Jonson van Ceulen the Elder. Her name is unknown, so the picture speaks for a type, the prosperous Dutch wife, more than for an individual. Its size and date match the companion Portrait of a Man, and the two presumably hung together as a pair, a husband and wife being the most usual explanation for such pendants.","i":"In Dutch marriage portraits the wife's picture usually answered her husband's in pose and scale, and her dress and jewelry stated the family's prosperity. The woman here is unnamed, so her rank can only be guessed. Jonson, who had made his career among the English court and gentry before moving to the Netherlands in the early 1640s, adapted his courtly polish for a Dutch clientele.","c":"A portrait of this kind depends on the quiet authority of the face and the careful rendering of costume, and Jonson's smooth, precise manner was well suited to both. The sober dignity usual in his Dutch portraits of women is likely to be present. It is best seen alongside its pendant, since the pair's meaning lies in the relationship between them."},"nga:123073":{"d":"A woman is seen from the waist up behind an oval opening, painted entirely in tones of sepia and golden brown. She looks steadily out with large dark eyes, her face turned to the left, with dark hair pulled back and curls on her shoulders. The satin or silk of her bodice glints, a sheer cloth covers her chest, and one long-fingered hand touches a ribbon at her chest while the other holds a closed book. A curtain is pulled aside to show a church tower. Carved babies on the oval hold artists' tools, and the upper corners show a drawing, drafting tools, a globe, a lute and a caduceus.","i":"Anna Maria van Schurman (1607 to 1678) of Utrecht was one of the most learned women of her age: a scholar of languages, including Hebrew, who attended lectures at the University of Utrecht from behind a screen, and a painter, engraver and sculptor who corresponded with scholars across Europe. The tools of the arts in the frame celebrate her accomplishments, and the book in her hand her learning. The monochrome format suggests a model for an engraving, though the record does not say so.","c":"A subtle, intelligent portrait. The sepia monochrome gives the face a quiet, sculptural clarity, and the allusive frame of putti, tools and instruments turns the picture into an emblem of a learned life. Cornelis Jonson van Ceulen, an English-born painter long active in the Netherlands, was a fine, exact portraitist, and this small panel shows him at his most refined. The attributes are a little crowded, but the sitter's steady gaze holds the picture together."},"met:437759":{"d":"A small portrait of a woman on a linden panel, about 38 by 27 centimetres, painted in oil around 1510 to 1515. The Met's keywords record a coif, a necklace, a ring, embroidery and a window, so the sitter is shown in her best dress and jewellery, with an opening behind her to the world outside. Bernhard Strigel painted such portraits in Memmingen, in southern Germany.","i":"In south German towns around 1500, portraits of women showed wealth and respectability through jewellery, embroidery and a well-pinned coif. The ring may mark a marriage or engagement, and the window opens the picture to the world outside. Strigel was also court painter to Emperor Maximilian I, for whom he portrayed members of the imperial family. The sitter here is not named, so she is read as a type: a woman of means in the early German Renaissance.","c":"Strigel's portraits are direct and firmly drawn, with exact details of cap, embroidery and jewels, and this one has the sober clarity that the German taste of the time admired. The limits are plain: the colour is restrained, the sitter's expression guarded and the manner conservative beside Dürer's. The portrait is less searching than Dürer's or Holbein's but is an honest, handsome record of a woman and her costume."},"nga:33753":{"d":"The sitter, named in the title as Hans Roth, stands before a window and turns slightly to the left. He has fair skin, light blue eyes and reddish-brown hair that falls just above his ears, and his lips are set in a neutral expression. He wears a flat black hat with a wide brim and a brown fur mantle over a black garment with a tall white ruffled collar. In his left hand he holds a round orange fruit, and a small gold ring sits on his pointer finger. The window at the left opens on a rocky mountain, a blue river and a road with a distant rider.","i":"The panel is dated 1527, and the NGA also holds a panel of Margarethe Vöhlin of the same year, so the two were presumably made as a pair, probably for a married couple, though the museum text does not say so. A ring and a fruit often hint at betrothal, marriage or wealth in German portraits of the period; what the fruit means here is not recorded. The window view of river, road and rider sets the sitter in a wider world.","c":"Strigel, then in his mid-sixties and a year from his death, keeps to a clear, unfussy manner: a firm silhouette of black hat and brown fur against a pale wall, with a small landscape window for depth. The face is composed, even impassive, and lacks the probing of Dürer or Holbein, but the sharp drawing and the contrast of black, brown and green hold it together. A sound South German portrait by a painter better known for altarpieces."},"nga:33754":{"d":"The painted back of a portrait panel of Hans Roth, dated 1527, decorated not with a portrait but with a coat of arms. A central shield is divided into black and gold sections, with decorative elements above it and a figure resembling a unicorn rising from the crest, all against a deep red ground. The panel is displayed in a wooden frame with crossbars. The record gives the height as about 43 centimeters and the width as only 3, which presumably measures the panel's thickness rather than its face.","i":"Coats of arms were the emblems of a family's identity, and in the Renaissance they were often painted on the backs or covers of portraits, so that when a pair of portraits was closed, the owner's arms were visible. The heraldic unicorn crest is a conventional decorative figure. The painter, Bernhard Strigel of Memmingen, made both portraits and heraldic work. The related portrait of Margarethe Vöhlin, dated the same year, is in the same museum.","c":"A heraldic panel is a work of design more than of observation: the strong black and gold shield, the deep red ground and the fanciful crest are arranged with the crisp clarity of a good emblem. The verdict is of the type, not the particular. It is rare to see such a reverse, normally hidden, and it shows how a Renaissance portrait was made and used. As a painting it is decorative, not expressive."},"nga:33755":{"d":"A woman is seen from the waist up, seated and angled slightly to the right. She has blue eyes, faint brows and a long sloping nose, and her blond hair is pulled back under a gold cap-like headdress and a black wide-brimmed hat. She wears a high-necked white, black and gold dress, a gold chain falling to her waist and a black belt with a gold clasp, and two gem-set gold rings on her folded hand. Behind her a scarlet wallcovering with pink flowers fills two-thirds of the space; at the right a landscape opens with a tree, a leaping dog, a bridge, a town and mountains.","i":"The title names her as Margarethe Vöhlin, and Vöhlin was the name of a wealthy merchant family of Memmingen and Augsburg, which may place her in the circle of the south German trading elite. Strigel lived in Memmingen and also painted the Habsburg court. The gold chain, rings and elaborate dress proclaim wealth, and the folded hands were the conventional pose of a respectable wife. The museum also holds a painted coat of arms from the reverse of a portrait of Hans Roth, dated the same year.","c":"A fresh, finely observed portrait of the German Renaissance. Strigel's strengths are the clear, frontal design, the bold scarlet wallcovering that makes the pale face glow, and the jewel-like detail of the gold, while the small landscape at the right opens the space. The face is less individualized than Holbein's would be, and the pose a little stiff, but the honest rendering of the sitter's features, with the slightly bulbous nose and the double chin, shows a refusal to flatter."},"nga:33756":{"d":"The back of a portrait panel, about 43 by 30 cm, carries no figure but a heraldic design. A painted lattice of vertical and horizontal wooden bars frames a central rectangular panel, where an emblem of swirling shapes stands on a red ground. The museum’s description reads the whole as a coat of arms, presumably that of the sitter’s family, though the text does not say whose. Painted in oil on the reverse of the panel in 1527, it belongs with the portrait of Margarethe Vöhlin on the front.","i":"Portraits in sixteenth-century southern Germany were often painted on both sides of the panel, with the likeness on the front and the sitter’s coat of arms on the back. Arms identified a family and its standing, and a panel could be hinged, hung or kept so that either side could be shown. The Vöhlin name is that of a prominent merchant family of Memmingen, the town where Strigel worked, which would suit a sitter who wished to display lineage.","c":"Judged as painting, the reverse is plainly decorative: a strong red ground, a firmly drawn emblem and a trompe-l’oeil frame of wooden bars give it a crisp, graphic punch. It shows how much of a painter’s work in Strigel’s world lay in heraldry and craft design alongside portraiture. The real interest lies in the pairing, since the blunt, bold back sets off the likeness on the front, and the two sides were meant to be read together."},"nga:46187":{"d":"Seven figures gather before a gilded ground, three adults and four children. At the left a woman in a blue dress, green cloak and white headscarf sits on a brown bench with a nude child on her lap while two other children stand at her knees and play with him. Two men stand beside her at the right, a younger bearded man in a red robe and tall red hat and an older man in a fur-lined jacket and yellow cap. In front a fourth child in white crouches with a long T-shaped staff that a small brown dog bites. The woman and children have golden halos.","i":"Mary Cleophas belongs to the Holy Kinship, the late medieval image of the extended family of Christ. According to the legend, Saint Anne married three times and had a daughter named Mary by each husband: the Virgin, Mary Cleophas and Mary Salome. Mary Cleophas’s four sons are usually named as James the Less, Joseph the Just, Simon and Jude, which fits the four children here. Popular in German lands around 1500, the subject let families see their own domestic life in holy company.","c":"A late work by an aging painter: the gold ground, firm outlines and neat, bright colors are conservative for the 1520s, when Dürer and Holbein had moved far beyond them. What saves the panel is its domestic naturalism. A toddler on the lap, a child at play with a staff and a dog worrying that staff make the holy family feel like a real household. The poses are stiff and the space shallow, but the humane touches give it charm."},"nga:46188":{"d":"Five figures sit or kneel before a wall of textured gold, beside a large brown bird. At the center a woman in a red gown, white head-covering and dark green shawl holds an open book and looks down. A dark-bearded man in a red tunic and wide red hat sits at her left, and a bearded man in a green fur-lined cloak looks toward her, hand on his chest. A child in white puts a hand into her book. At the lower left a short-haired figure in a pink robe sits holding another open book beside the bird, wings raised. The woman, child, reader and bird wear golden halos.","i":"Mary Salome is one of the three Marys of the Holy Kinship legend and the mother of the apostles James the Greater and John the Evangelist. The haloed bird is very probably John’s eagle, the symbol of his Gospel, so the seated reader beside it is probably John himself. The book in the mother’s lap, with a child reading it, can be read as an image of Christian instruction passed from one generation to the next. The panel is the companion of Saint Mary Cleophas and Her Family.","c":"Calmer than its companion, this wing has a quiet center in the open book, and the red, green and pink robes stand out against the gold with a clarity suited to an altarpiece seen from across a church. The poses are stiff and the space shallow, yet small human touches, the child’s hand in the book and the man’s hand on his chest, keep the group warm. A sturdy, conservative South German wing, more devout than inventive."},"met:11057":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as Moses Pond, painted in 1832 when Healy was eighteen or nineteen. The canvas measures about 76 by 64 cm, which is 30 by 25 inches, a size widely used for American bust-length portraits of the time. The Met’s terms list a man and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his features, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"George Peter Alexander Healy, born in Boston in 1813, began painting portraits in his teens and was largely self-taught before he went to Paris in 1834. A plain, truthful likeness of a respectable citizen, in dark clothing against a dark ground, was just what American sitters wanted in the 1830s, since it suggested honesty and sound character. The record does not say who Pond was or why he sat for the young painter.","c":"As the work of a teenager, the portrait is accomplished, though it relies on the firm outline and simply lit head that self-taught portraitists often use. Healy’s later fluency, shaped by his Paris training under Baron Gros, is not yet in view. Its value is as a first stage: honest, direct and unglamorous, from a painter who would go on to portray presidents and kings."},"met:11054":{"d":"A portrait of Alexander Van Rensselaer, dated 1837, on a canvas of about 92 by 72 cm. The Met’s terms list the sitter and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his pose, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. Healy, then in his early twenties, had left for Europe in 1834 to study. The Met also holds a portrait of Euphemia White Van Rensselaer, painted five years later.","i":"The Van Rensselaers were among the oldest and largest landholding families of New York, descended from the Dutch patroons of the Hudson Valley around Albany. A portrait by a painter newly trained in Paris gave a young member of such a family a polished, European-flavored likeness in the late 1830s, when sitters wanted to look both established and up to date. The record does not give his career, so nothing more is asserted about him here.","c":"Healy’s portraits of the late 1830s combine the clean drawing and smooth finish learned in France with a plain American directness: warm flesh, dark clothing, an attentive face. They are more polished than the work of most provincial American portraitists and less daring than the best European portraits of the day. Reliable, dignified and a little reserved, this is a representative piece by one of the busiest portraitists of his generation."},"met:11055":{"d":"A portrait of Euphemia White Van Rensselaer, dated 1842, on a canvas of about 115 by 89 cm, noticeably larger than the portrait of Alexander Van Rensselaer that Healy had painted five years earlier. The Met’s terms list a woman and a portrait, and the record gives no description of her pose, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture. Her married name in the title presumably links her to the same family as Alexander; she is also recorded under the later name Cruger.","i":"Portraits of well-to-do American women around 1840 emphasized grace, refinement and family standing, through dress, lace, jewelry and a gentle, composed expression. The larger canvas suggests a more formal commission than a bust portrait. Healy, who by 1842 had painted Louis-Philippe of France, was a fashionable choice for East Coast society, and a portrait by him told visitors to a house that the family moved in cosmopolitan circles.","c":"Healy’s portraits of women in the 1840s tend toward soft modeling and flattering idealization, which can blunt individual character. This one, larger and more formal than the portrait of Alexander Van Rensselaer, shows the same smooth finish, and it is more interesting as a record of how an affluent woman wished to be seen than as a psychological study. A polished, well-mannered picture."},"met:11058":{"d":"A self-portrait by George P. A. Healy, painted in 1851 when he was in his late thirties, on a canvas of about 62 by 52 cm. The Met’s terms list a man and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. Its modest size suggests a private likeness more than one of the large public portraits on which Healy’s reputation rested.","i":"Self-portraits by successful portraitists serve as advertisement as much as introspection: they show the painter as a gentleman and not as a craftsman. By 1851 Healy had painted King Louis-Philippe and, in 1845, the ailing Andrew Jackson at the Hermitage, and he was dividing his time between Paris and the United States. A self-portrait at this stage records a confident, widely traveled man.","c":"Healy’s reputation rests on volume and reliability: he is rarely a great painter but is nearly always a convincing recorder of faces, with a sober palette and a gift for putting sitters at ease. A self-portrait removes the client, and with the client the flattery that clients invite. The result is probably his most candid kind of picture, and a useful measure of the plain, honest manner behind his more public work."},"nga:166453":{"d":"The head and shoulders of a clean-shaven man with black hair and a lined face appear against a dark ground. His body is angled to the viewer’s right and he looks in that direction with blue-gray eyes under black brows. His hair is combed loosely back, with one lock falling onto his forehead and curling around his ear. Lightly flushed cheekbones rise over hollow cheeks, and wrinkles mark his forehead, the corners of his eyes and his mouth. He wears a black jacket with wide rounded lapels, a white shirt and a black bow tie. Healy signed and dated the picture in red paint: “G.P.A. Healy 1860.”","i":"Painted in 1860, the year Lincoln was nominated and elected president, the portrait shows him before the beard he wore in office. Still unfamiliar to many voters, he needed images, and painters, photographers and printmakers rushed to supply them. Healy, who had settled in Chicago in 1855, returned to Lincoln later, painting him in The Peacemakers (1868), a scene of the president with Grant, Sherman and Admiral Porter in 1865.","c":"A sober, searching portrait. The tousled hair, hollow cheeks and lined face make a plain, unglamorized man, and the dark ground focuses attention on the face. Healy does not flatter, and the lack of a beard gives a Lincoln different from the one everyone knows. Photographs of 1860 are sharper, but the canvas offers color and an unhurried, thoughtful gaze, and it records a candidate before he became a monument."},"nga:52386":{"d":"A woman is shown in side profile, with delicate features, fair skin and rosy cheeks. Her dark, wavy hair is styled away from her face, and she wears a high-collared black dress with white ruffled trim. Two roses, one yellow and one pink, are pinned at her chest. The background is a muted green, with part of a red chair visible behind her. Healy signed the picture and dated it 1876 in the upper left. The canvas is about 77 by 64 cm.","i":"The profile pose gives the portrait the cool formality of a classical cameo, and the black dress with white trim shows a woman in fashionable but sober dress. The two roses add a note of color and of personal taste, though what they signify here is not recorded. The sitter’s life is not described in the record, so nothing more is asserted about her. By 1876 Healy was in his early sixties and had painted portraits for more than forty years.","c":"An elegant, restrained late portrait. The profile gives the face a clean, sculptural outline, and the two roses are the only touches of color against the black dress and muted green ground. The painter’s old-fashioned skill is in evidence, though it lacks the vigor of his best likenesses of statesmen, and the effect is affectionate rather than probing. A refined, quietly decorative picture, more interesting for its design than for its psychology."},"nga:177789":{"d":"A seated man is seen from the waist up, his torso turned slightly to the left and his head turned to look at the viewer. He has pale skin, high cheekbones, straight eyebrows and thin lips set in a neutral expression. His gray hair is tied in a low ponytail with some curls around his ears, and he wears a dark coat over a white shirt with a high collar. He sits in a dark red chair against a blurred brown background. The museum records the work as Healy’s copy after Gilbert Stuart and dates it only to between 1848 and 1879.","i":"Gilbert Stuart painted Jefferson from life in 1805, and that likeness became the standard image of the third president. Copies by later artists such as Healy kept it in circulation at a time when Americans were assembling series of portraits of their founders for public buildings and private galleries. Jefferson (1743 to 1826) was the chief author of the Declaration of Independence and president from 1801 to 1809.","c":"A copy is a different task from an original and should be judged as one. Healy keeps Stuart’s poise and soft modeling, and his own tidy finish shows in the smooth flesh and quiet ground. It has less of Stuart’s feathery brilliance and more of the evenness of the mid-nineteenth century, but it is a competent act of homage and a useful record of how later generations learned to see the founders."},"met:438462":{"d":"A large canvas of about 117 by 134 centimeters shows the Massacre of the Innocents, the episode in the Gospel of Matthew in which King Herod, afraid of a rival king, orders the killing of all boys under two in Bethlehem. It was painted in 1824 by François Joseph Navez, a Belgian pupil of Jacques-Louis David who had known the older master in his Brussels exile. The picture is a large religious composition from the Belgian Neoclassical school.","i":"The story of the Holy Innocents, whose feast falls on 28 December, was told by artists from the Middle Ages onward as a warning about tyranny and a tribute to the first martyrs. In 1824 images of civilians cut down by soldiers had contemporary resonance, since Delacroix showed his Massacre at Chios at the Paris Salon of that year, in response to the Greek War of Independence. Painting the biblical subject also allowed a display of emotion, as mothers resist the soldiers.","c":"Navez keeps David's clarity of organization and sculptural figures while allowing a Romantic intensity of feeling, and the discipline of classical composition holds the horror in check. The cost may be a certain frozen quality, since the gestures are rhetorical rather than spontaneous. Still, the picture is an impressive attempt to unite the lessons of David with the emotional appeal of the new generation."},"met:438953":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 25 by 30 centimeters, shows a cemetery at Pronoia, near Nauplia, the Greek town now called Nafplio. The museum's keywords record only a cemetery, so the graves, the trees, the mountains, the figures and the light are left unstated. It was painted between about 1841 and 1847 by Carl Rottmann, the Munich landscape painter who had traveled in Greece to collect motifs.","i":"King Ludwig I of Bavaria sent Rottmann to Greece in 1834 and 1835 and commissioned a cycle of Greek landscapes for Munich; Ludwig's son Otto had been installed as king of the newly independent Greece, and Nauplia was his first capital. Rottmann's Greek pictures combine accurate topography with a grand, heroic mood, and a cemetery adds a note of memento mori in a land strewn with antiquity. This small canvas may be related to that project.","c":"Rottmann is better known for his monumental, dramatic Greek views, and a small canvas like this shows a quieter side: a sober, observed motif without heroic staging. The cemetery, with its note of mortality, suits his taste for the elegiac in a landscape where history is everywhere. The record gives no description of the surface, but small oil studies of this kind often have a freshness that the finished cycle, painted in the studio, lacks."},"cma:159309":{"d":"A young woman in ancient dress sits in an antique interior, her head resting on her hand in a pose of melancholy. The museum points to the chair, the brazier and the table, which Vien rendered with archaeological care, and its caption invites viewers to find three strands of pearls. The handling is delicate and the palette graceful, in the manner of eighteenth-century French painting. The canvas is about 68 by 55 cm and dates from 1756.","i":"Vien lived in Rome in the 1740s, when excavations at Herculaneum and Pompeii were reviving interest in ancient furniture and dress, and this picture dresses a familiar subject in that new learning. The museum notes that the head-on-hand gesture goes back to the Renaissance, and that images of women in interiors contemplating a letter with longing or sadness come from Dutch daily-life painting, here transformed into an ancient setting. The mood is wistful rather than tragic.","c":"A charming, lightweight mood piece that points toward Neoclassicism without yet leaving the Rococo behind: the archaeological furniture is crisp, but the soft color and tender feeling belong to the age of Boucher. It is gentler and less severe than the work of Vien’s pupil David, and its antique costume is a polite fiction more than a reconstruction. Slight, but fresh, pleasing and historically telling."},"met:189656":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper, about 32 by 22 cm, dated between 1775 and 1781, with a woman and putti, the plump winged children of classical decoration. The title names it as an allegory of the sense of smell. The record gives no further description, so details of pose, flowers and setting are left to the picture itself. An oil sketch on paper is usually a design or study and not a finished picture, and this one may have been made as a design for a larger decorative scheme.","i":"The five senses were a traditional allegorical set in European art, usually personified as women with attributes, and smell was commonly signaled by flowers, perfume or incense. Putti often carry the attributes. The dates 1775 to 1781 coincide with Vien’s years as director of the French Academy in Rome, so the sketch may belong to that period, though the record does not say where it was made.","c":"Oil sketches on paper show a painter’s hand more freely than finished canvases, and this one presumably has the pale, airy color and elegant, rounded drawing that make Vien’s decorative work agreeable. The allegory itself is a stock one, and its small size limits its ambition, but it offers a pleasant glimpse of a painter best known for severe academic pictures in a lighter, more playful mood."},"nga:1162":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Lucrezia Sommaria, in oil on a panel of about 63 by 46 cm, dated about 1510. The museum record gives no description of her pose, dress or setting, so only the type can be stated: a bust or half-length Florentine portrait of the early sixteenth century, made for a family to record a woman’s place in it, perhaps on her marriage.","i":"Ridolfo Ghirlandaio, son of the great fresco painter Domenico, ran the family workshop in Florence. Florentine portraits of women around 1510 drew on Leonardo’s Mona Lisa and on the portraits Raphael painted in the city, which favored a half-length figure, a turned pose and a calm, self-possessed look. The record does not identify the sitter beyond her name, so her family and circumstances are left open.","c":"Ridolfo is a gentle, competent painter and never an innovator. His best portraits borrow Leonardo’s and Raphael’s poise and add a precise, slightly dry finish. With a record that offers no more than the name, the safe verdict is that this is a quiet, well-bred likeness typical of Florentine portraiture of the day, elegant in outline and restrained in feeling, and more interesting as a period document than as a bold statement."},"met:436494":{"d":"A very narrow upright panel, about 36 by 10 cm, shows the Nativity with saints. The Met’s terms list the Madonna and Child, the Christ Child, an angel and the saints Peter, Paul and John the Evangelist. The record gives no date or further description. The tall, slender shape suggests that it was part of something larger, such as a frame, a predella or a small shrine, and not a picture meant to hang alone.","i":"The saints named here were not at the Nativity, so the panel is a devotional image and not a narrative one. It joins the birth of Christ to the apostles Peter and Paul, the pillars of the early Church, and to John the Evangelist, author of the fourth Gospel, so that the viewer prays in their company. Such combinations, common in Florentine devotional art, were perhaps chosen for the owner’s patron saints or for the intercession of the saints shown.","c":"Ridolfo’s small devotional works carry the tidy, sweet-tempered idiom of Florentine painting after the High Renaissance: clear forms, gentle gestures and a calm monumentality learned from Fra Bartolommeo. Compressing a Nativity and several saints into a sliver demands economy more than invention. Without a date or a known context, the panel is chiefly of interest as a fragment, a pleasing piece of craft by a Florentine workshop painter of the generation after his father Domenico."},"met:11012":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 58 by 104 cm, shows a seashore in the morning, with boats and human figures; those are the subject terms the Met gives, along with seas and seascape. The record gives no further description, so the light, coastline and figures are left to the picture itself. The long horizontal format suits the sweep of a shoreline and the calm of an early hour, and the picture is dated 1866.","i":"William Hart, born in Scotland and raised in Albany, New York, made his name with Hudson River School landscapes of trees and cattle, but he also painted the coast. Calm, luminous shore scenes were popular in America in the 1850s and 1860s, and a view of morning, with the day just beginning, offered city buyers a quiet, restorative image. The record does not say which coast is shown.","c":"Hart is at his best with soft light and settled composition, and a morning shore gives him both. The picture is competent, well-mannered and more relaxed than his large panoramic landscapes, but it lacks the intensity of the luminist painters Fitz Henry Lane and Martin Johnson Heade, who made stillness a subject in itself. Agreeable rather than memorable, it shows the sound middle ground of American painting after the Civil War."},"cma:173181":{"d":"A small upright canvas, about 32 by 27 cm, dated 1877 and titled as an autumn scene in the Adirondack Mountains of northern New York. The museum record gives no description, so the exact view is left to the picture, but the title promises the fall foliage that painters of the Hudson River School loved. The museum’s caption adds that the artist, William Hart, was the grandfather of the author E. B. White.","i":"The Adirondacks became a fashionable destination in the late 1860s and 1870s, helped by W. H. H. Murray’s popular book Adventures in the Wilderness (1869), and painters followed the tourists, sketching in the woods and finishing small pictures in the studio. Autumn color gave city buyers a taste of wilderness at its showiest, in a form that could hang in a parlor.","c":"By 1877 the grand panoramas of Cole and Church had given way to more intimate, more literal views, and Hart was a dependable practitioner of that later phase. Small autumn landscapes of this kind rely on color and mood, and a small format keeps them intimate. Without more detail it can be judged only as a modest example, pleasant rather than ambitious."},"met:11011":{"d":"A landscape of about 60 by 85 cm with trees and cattle, dated 1883. The title places the scene at Napanoch, a community in the Rondout Valley of Ulster County, New York, west of the Hudson. The Met’s terms list a tree, cattle, cows and a landscape, and the record gives no further description, so the light, layout and number of animals are left to the picture itself.","i":"Cattle at pasture under trees were Hart’s trademark, especially from the 1870s on, and the pastoral landscape offered urban buyers a comforting image of the countryside as the United States industrialized. Hart, born in Scotland and raised in Albany, was a leading member of the later Hudson River School, when the dramatic wilderness of its founders had given way to gentler, lived-in scenery.","c":"Hart’s late pastorals are among the most agreeable pictures of the later Hudson River School: balanced, softly lit and convincingly drawn in their cattle. They lack the grand ambition of the school’s founders, and their appeal is familiarity, a peaceful scene, settled and unthreatening. Scene at Napanoch fits the pattern, a skilled and likable piece of work that rarely surprises."},"nga:72077":{"d":"Five people surround the dead Christ, who is seated and supported by them. He has long dark hair and a dark beard, his eyes are closed, and he is nude except for a white cloth at his waist and two more over his shoulders. Blood runs from his forehead, hands, feet and a wound in his side, and a halo of yellow light glows behind his head. Two mourners seem to be women with covered heads, two are young men with long hair, and an old gray-bearded man stands behind. A circle of thorny branches lies at his feet against a dark ground.","i":"The Lamentation, the mourning over Christ’s body after it is taken from the cross, is not described in detail in the Gospels but grew from devotional tradition. The mourners probably include the Virgin, Mary Magdalene, John and Joseph of Arimathea or Nicodemus, though the museum text does not name them. The crown of thorns at Christ’s feet recalls the Passion. In Counter-Reformation Venice, such pictures were meant to move the viewer to compassion.","c":"Late Palma, painted when he was in his early seventies: dark, compact and emotional, with the figures pressed close around the pale body against the darkness. The warm robes and yellow light give the picture a certain glow, but the composition is a workshop formula that Palma, a prolific painter, used many times. It is moving without being original, and it lacks the intensity of Tintoretto, the master in whose shadow Venetian painters worked."},"met:437242":{"d":"A large upright canvas, about 216 by 135 cm, shows the Crucifixion, with Christ on the cross, the Virgin Mary, soldiers and horses; those are the Met’s subject terms. The record gives no date and no further description. The size and format are those of an altarpiece or a major devotional picture for a church or chapel, though the record does not say where it hung.","i":"Venetian painters made the Crucifixion a crowded, dramatic scene, as in Tintoretto’s huge version at the Scuola di San Rocco (1565), with soldiers and horsemen below the cross. The soldiers and horses here suggest the Roman guard, which in the Gospels includes the centurion who recognized Christ as the Son of God, and the Virgin’s grief gives the scene its emotional center. Palma, a younger Venetian who knew Tintoretto’s work well, painted in this vein.","c":"Palma worked fast and for many patrons, and his church pictures vary widely in quality. A large Crucifixion with soldiers and horses played to his strengths in arranging crowds and in dusky Venetian color, even if the emotion can be more theatrical than felt. At this scale the test is whether the drama holds across the whole surface, and Palma, a practiced organizer of groups, usually manages it."},"met:436823":{"d":"A portrait of Charles Beauclerk, Duke of St. Albans (1670 to 1726), on a canvas of about 127 by 103 cm, painted about 1690 to 1695, when he was in his twenties. The Met’s terms list only a man and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. The size is that of a formal three-quarter-length commission for a nobleman.","i":"Beauclerk was a son of Charles II and the actress Nell Gwyn, and he was made Duke of St. Albans in 1684, at the age of thirteen. Born outside marriage but acknowledged by the king, he belonged to the line of Stuart illegitimate dukes, and a portrait by the leading painter in England told the world that he was a person of rank. Kneller, born in Lübeck, was the dominant portraitist in England in these years.","c":"Kneller’s formula, an ample, quickly handled likeness with some swagger and a rather generalized face, could be turned out in large numbers, and his studio assistants painted much of the drapery. The best of his portraits, from his own hand, have genuine vitality. For the early 1690s the type is conventional, but it carries conviction, and a sitter of this rank, young and confident, suited it well."},"met:436824":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named in the title as Lady Mary Berkeley, on a canvas of about 74 by 64 cm, dated about 1700. The Met’s terms list only a woman and a portrait, and the record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. “Lady Mary” is the style of the daughter of an earl or a higher peer, which suggests a member of the family of the Earls of Berkeley, though the record does not say so.","i":"Kneller’s women of the 1690s and 1700s followed a formula set by his Hampton Court Beauties, a series of court ladies painted for Queen Mary II: a loose, flowing “undress” gown, a pearly complexion and a gentle, slightly sweet expression. A portrait of this kind recorded a young woman’s beauty and family standing, often for a marriage, and was valued as much for its flattery as for its likeness.","c":"Kneller’s output was large and his quality uneven, but women’s portraits like this often show him at his best, with a fresh, quick touch and a face that is flattering and still believable. The format is modest and the formula familiar, so the picture asks to be enjoyed for its ease and charm, not for any depth of character."},"met:437090":{"d":"A near-full-length portrait of Charles, King of England (1600 to 1649), in oil on canvas, about 200 by 141 centimeters, painted in 1629 by the Dutch court painter Daniël Mijtens. The king was in his late twenties, four years into his reign. At this size and date it is a state portrait of the kind produced for royal residences and for the king's courtiers and diplomats.","i":"Charles came to the throne in 1625, and in 1629 he dissolved Parliament and began eleven years of rule without it, a time when the court's image of order and splendor mattered greatly. Official portraits such as this, copied in studios, carried that image to palaces, courtiers and foreign courts. Mijtens had served James's court and, in the first years of Charles's reign, was the leading court painter before Van Dyck arrived in 1632.","c":"Mijtens paints the king in the sober Anglo-Dutch manner that preceded Van Dyck: firm, clearly lit and attentive to costume, but stiffer in pose and cooler in characterization. Beside Van Dyck's later portraits, which gave Charles a languid, aristocratic grace, Mijtens's king is likely to look more like a monarch on parade. The picture is valuable as a record of the image of Charles before Van Dyck remade it, honest rather than inspired."},"met:10806":{"d":"A woman in a hat holds a fan in this portrait, oil on canvas, about 109 by 82 cm, dated 1873. The title gives no name, so she may have been a friend, a paying sitter or a model. Duveneck, an American from Kentucky, had trained in Munich and brought home the bravura manner of the Munich school: broad, loaded strokes, dark warm grounds and strong contrasts of light and shade.","i":"A fan, in portraits since the Renaissance, is both an accessory of fashion and a prop for the hand, and handled with a gesture it can convey poise, flirtation or reserve. Portraits of women by American painters of Duveneck's generation, trained in Munich or Paris, blended European technique with the American market's demand for gentility. The sitter's identity matters less here than the display of painting.","c":"Duveneck in his mid-twenties already had the Munich bravura in hand, learned from Hals and Courbet: the loaded brush, the dark ground, the figure struck out of shadow with a few confident planes. The method makes his portraits vivid at a distance, but at times heavy and tonally narrow. Beside the cooler elegance of Whistler or the later polish of Sargent he is rawer and more direct, which is his charm."},"nga:1097":{"d":"A man with a full beard and moustache, dressed in black with a white ruffled collar, looks directly at the viewer. He is placed to the left of the canvas against a dark background. The museum record gives few other details, so his expression, the lighting and the handling of paint cannot be described with confidence. The canvas is about 56 by 46 centimetres, a modest size for a portrait, suited to a private sitter.","i":"Duveneck, born in Covington, Kentucky, trained in Munich under Wilhelm von Diez and became the star of the Munich School of bravura portraiture, with rapid, fluid brushwork, dark backgrounds and strong contrasts of light. A portrait with a dark ground and a white ruffled collar belongs to that manner. The sitter, Leslie Pease Barnum, is not described further in the record, and the date of 1876 falls at the moment when Duveneck's reputation in the United States was growing.","c":"A bold young painter's portrait: whatever the detail, Duveneck's Munich training would give the head a strong, direct handling, and the sitter's frank gaze against a dark ground fits the manner. The black clothing and dark background leave little to look at but the face and the white collar, which is the point. As an early work of his bravura period it is notable for energy more than subtlety; later, in Venice and Florence, his colour grew lighter."},"nga:1098":{"d":"A man sits facing the viewer, shown from the waist up, with round, dark eyes under curved brows, a straight nose, and a reddish-brown beard and moustache that match his hair. He wears a dark suit with a white collar at the neck beneath a charcoal-grey overcoat. In his right hand, resting on his lap, he holds a cigar, with a gold ring on the hand and a chain across his torso. The background is dark brown, nearly black in places. At about 78 by 66 centimetres, it is a compact, informal portrait.","i":"The sitter was William Gedney Bunce, a fellow American painter best known for his luminous views of Venice. Painters often portrayed one another, as a mark of friendship and as practice, and Duveneck's Munich-trained, broadly brushed style, with figures emerging from a dark ground, was admired as a sign of modern, confident painting. The cigar and the ring give the sitter an easy, informal air.","c":"Strong and simple: the dark suit and background throw the head and the hand with its cigar into relief, and the direct gaze has a frank, unposed quality. Duveneck's loaded brush makes the head solid, if somewhat heavy. The dark coat is almost swallowed by the dark background, and the picture's tonal range is narrow. Still, it is a vigorous example of the Munich manner, and it records one American painter by another with impressive economy."},"cma:103997":{"d":"A young woman, likely an artist's model, holds a shallow brass bowl containing lemons and grapes in this oil on canvas of about 87 by 63 cm, painted about 1880. Frank Duveneck loved Venice and spent several months there during the early decades of his career. Trained in Munich, he painted in a broad, fluid manner, and the bowl and fruit offer a ready contrast of metal, fruit and flesh.","i":"Venetian girls with shawls and beads, posing with fruit and brass, were a favorite subject for foreign painters in the city, and models of this kind sat for many artists. Venice gave visiting Americans a ready-made picturesque. The lemons and grapes add Mediterranean color and a touch of abundance. Such a picture is a type, not a portrait: it tells nothing of the sitter's life, and was made for the buyers who wanted a souvenir of Italy.","c":"The Venetian Girl suits Duveneck: a model with a bowl of fruit gives him textures of metal, fruit and flesh to model with a fluent, loaded brush. The danger of the genre is anonymity, a type instead of a person, and a Venetian costume piece risks a touristic sweetness. His firm painterly attack keeps it from prettiness, and the picture has the vigor of a teacher demonstrating how it is done."},"nga:177161":{"d":"A small oil sketch on wood, about 39 by 45 centimeters, made about 1887 by Pascal-Adolphe-Jean Dagnan-Bouveret as preparation for a larger painting of Breton women at a pardon, the religious pilgrimage festival of Brittany. The setting is outdoors, and the subject is worship by many figures. Such oil sketches on small wooden panels were made quickly, to fix the grouping of the figures before work began on the large canvas.","i":"A pardon is a Breton religious festival, usually held on a saint's day, when villagers in traditional dress gather at a chapel or churchyard for Mass and procession, and sometimes to ask pardon for their sins. French painters of the 1880s, including Dagnan-Bouveret, visited Brittany for its piety and costume, which seemed to preserve an older, simpler France. A study records the poses and grouping before the artist composed the finished picture.","c":"A preliminary study reveals the working method of the academic naturalists of the 1880s: careful drawing of the figure, controlled tonal color and a quiet, unsentimental respect for the sitters. Dagnan-Bouveret was admired for painstaking observation, and studies like this show the research behind the finished work. Its value is as a document of process."},"nga:46507":{"d":"A young girl with long blond hair and pale skin is shown from the chest up in profile, facing the viewer’s left. She has large brown eyes under faint brows, a small nose and closed coral-pink lips. A scarlet headband holds her hair, which falls below her shoulders, with short bangs on her forehead. A black hat with an upturned brim sits at the back of her head, ribbons streaming down her back, and a loosely painted gold earring dangles from one ear. Her black cape has a pewter-gray collar. The background is streaked teal over pale yellow.","i":"Henner was born in Alsace, and after France lost most of the province to Germany in 1871 he painted Alsatian girls in local costume, whose black hats and ribbons became a symbol of loyalty and loss. A small panel like this suited private patriotic feeling more than public declamation. Dated 1873, it belongs to the first years of that mood, when many French viewers read such images as quiet emblems of the lost provinces.","c":"A small, soft, polished picture. The profile, the pale skin and the streaming ribbons against a streaked teal and yellow ground make a gentle, almost icon-like image, and the loosely brushed earring keeps it fresh. It risks sentimentality, as much of Henner’s work does, but the cool color and the plain pose hold it back, and as a patriotic emblem it is understated. It shows his gift for soft modeling at a small scale."},"met:436644":{"d":"A female nude is the subject of this canvas of about 97 by 71 cm, dated 1881; the Met’s terms list only female nudes, and the record gives no description of the pose or setting. Henner’s nudes typically show pale, luminous flesh against a dark, hazy ground. The double title casts the bather as Echo, the nymph of Greek myth who could only repeat the last words spoken to her.","i":"The double title shows a habit of the time: a nude bather given a mythological name to lend her dignity at the Salon. In Ovid’s Metamorphoses the nymph Echo loved Narcissus. Punished by Juno so that she could only repeat what others said, she was rejected by him and wasted away until only her voice remained. The name lets a dreamy, secluded nude carry a story of loss.","c":"Henner treats flesh as a source of glowing light against shadow, in a soft manner owed to Correggio and Leonardo’s sfumato. The risk is monotony, since the formula of pale body, dark ground and, often, red hair recurs across hundreds of canvases. Echo of 1881 is typical: its virtues are the soft transitions and dreamy mood, its weakness a lack of specificity, since the myth is more a label than a story."},"nga:46508":{"d":"A small panel, about 27 by 19 cm, dated 1890, shows the upper half of a woman in profile, facing left. She has fair skin, a prominent nose and closed lips, and her hair is covered by a dark headpiece. She wears dark, cloak-like clothing against a muted greenish-slate ground. There is no visible jewelry and she holds no objects, so the picture rests entirely on the profile and the tones.","i":"Henner made many small portrait heads on wood, and the plain presentation here, with no jewelry, props or setting, gives the picture the concentration of a medal or a cameo. The title gives only the sitter’s married name. The record does not say who she was or how she knew the painter, and small portraits of this kind were often made for friends and family rather than for exhibition.","c":"Henner’s late profile heads are spare: a clean silhouette, a limited palette, no attributes. The dark clothes against a slate-green ground make an almost monochrome harmony, and the face carries the picture. At this small scale the result is intimate and a little austere. It lacks the glowing flesh of his nudes but has a quieter truthfulness, and the prominent nose and set mouth show that he could look without sentiment when he chose."},"met:436207":{"d":"An interior with men and a woman: the title names Don Quixote, Sancho Panza and their hosts, Basil and Quiteria. The Met’s terms list an interior, men, women and Don Quixote. The canvas is about 92 by 73 cm and carries a queried date of 1863. The record gives no further description, so the arrangement of the figures, the light and the furnishings are left to the picture itself.","i":"The scene comes from the second part of Cervantes’s Don Quixote. Basil, a poor young man, stages a fake suicide at the wedding of the woman he loves, Quiteria, who is to marry the rich Camacho. She agrees to marry the dying Basil, and when he recovers the trick stands. The newlyweds then entertain the knight and his squire. The date 1863 matches the year of Doré’s illustrated edition of the novel, which made his image of the story famous.","c":"Doré was a master of theatrical effect, and his engravings for Don Quixote are among the best-known images of the novel. As a painter he was less admired in his own day, and critics often found his canvases looser and less incisive than his prints. The painted version translates the novel’s comedy into color and domestic warmth, but it has never displaced the illustrations in the public mind."},"met:11397":{"d":"A rural scene of about 69 by 87 cm, dated 1853, with a woman, a girl and a tree. The title names the occupation of basket making, which presumably belongs to one of the figures. The Met’s terms list a tree, a woman and a girl, and the record gives no further description, so the setting, the light and the exact action are left to the picture. The wide format leaves room for the figures and for a landscape around them.","i":"German-born Louis Lang came to the United States in 1838 and worked in Philadelphia and New York, and The Basket Maker belongs to the mid-century American taste for scenes of honest labor and domestic virtue. A woman working beside a child promised viewers a quiet moral, that craft and family go together, and such pictures reached buyers through exhibitions and prints.","c":"A pleasant, well-composed, mildly sentimental picture in the manner of its day: the figures are idealized and the labor is a moral emblem more than a record. Lang had European training and it shows in the polish. The picture has charm and competence, nothing more, and its main interest lies in what it says about the American appetite for gentle, reassuring genre in the early 1850s."},"met:16580":{"d":"An interior with women art students at their work, painted about 1871 on a canvas of about 68 by 59 cm. The Met’s terms list a woman, a painting, an interior and a student, and the record gives no further description, so the number of figures, the studio setting and the work on the easels are left to the picture. The subject was a modern one: the education of women as artists.","i":"In the decades around 1870 American women entered art schools in growing numbers. Cooper Union opened a School of Design for Women in New York in 1859, and the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts gradually opened classes to women, though life classes with nude models long remained a point of dispute. A picture of art students was thus a picture of a social change, and a sympathetic one.","c":"An engaging subject handled in the polished genre manner of the period, with neatly arranged figures, even light and mild sentiment. Lang was no innovator, and the scene is more genteel than searching, but it is a sympathetic record of women taking up the profession, painted at a moment when that was still a matter of debate. Its interest is historical as much as artistic."},"nga:219555":{"d":"The upper body of a young person is shown in profile, facing left, against a ground of dark purple and blue with light orange highlights and hot pink at the lower left. The sitter has pale skin, a long nose, thin pink lips and green-brown eyes with pale purple lids. Long wavy hair curls around the shoulders in brown, yellow, green and dark blue, with purple and rusty red shadows, and a dark green garment has a white and light blue collar. The paint is laid on in small overlapping oval dots, and two vertical tan and blue lines behind the head suggest paneling.","i":"Théo van Rysselberghe, a Belgian, was the leading Neo-Impressionist of his country, taking up Seurat’s divided brushwork after seeing A Sunday on La Grande Jatte in Brussels in 1887. The method builds a picture from small dots of color that blend in the eye, and the unnatural hair colors here, green, blue and yellow, are not meant literally but as optical play. The profile recalls Renaissance portrait medals, and the flat bands behind the head may reflect Japanese prints.","c":"A small, jewel-like work in which the dotted technique is applied lightly, avoiding the stiff, mechanical look of some Neo-Impressionism. The hair becomes a shimmering mass of green, yellow and blue while the face stays calm, and the hot pink and orange accents at the edges are bold. A highly accomplished, decorative portrait; if there is a limit, it is that the technique’s polish keeps the sitter at a distance."},"met:437697":{"d":"Soldiers camp in a landscape with trees, men and horses; those are the Met’s subject terms. The panel is about 73 by 105 cm and undated, and the record gives no further description. A bivouac picture of this kind typically shows army life at rest, with small figures set in a wide, atmospheric landscape, and not a battle. The number of soldiers, their activities and the time of day are left to the picture itself.","i":"Pieter Snayers, a Flemish painter of battles and military scenes, worked in Brussels for the Habsburg rulers of the Spanish Netherlands and painted generals’ victories and sieges of the Thirty Years’ War. Bivouac pictures, showing soldiers resting, tending horses and sharing food, were popular with collectors, who could enjoy the military world at a safe distance and sometimes find a moral note in the camp’s idle violence.","c":"Snayers is a chronicler more than an inventor: his horses, costumes and rolling topography are given with clear, confident detail, and the wide space is well managed. His figures can be small and rather stiff beside those of more inventive battle painters, and the incidents of camp life are told without much wit. Skilled and informative, if seldom inspired, and best enjoyed for its small incidents."},"nga:73":{"d":"A chest-up portrait of a man, his body and head turned slightly to the left. He has pale skin, blue eyes, high cheekbones, slightly furrowed brows, a straight nose and pink lips in a neutral expression. His wavy brown hair reaches his shoulders, and he wears a dark green jacket with a white-and-yellow fur collar and a floppy black hat whose brim curves around his forehead. The background is plain black. A monogram appears at the upper left and the date 1507 at the upper right. The panel is about 40 by 32 cm.","i":"Hans Schäufelein trained in Dürer’s workshop in Nuremberg, and a bust-length portrait against a plain dark ground follows the type that Dürer and his pupils used. The monogram and date set in the upper corners declare authorship in the manner of Dürer’s own signed works, a sign of the painter’s growing confidence in his status. The sitter’s name is not recorded, but the fur collar and fashionable hat mark a man of means.","c":"A young painter’s accomplished exercise in the Dürer manner. The black ground isolates the head, the face is firmly modeled and the wavy hair and fur are carefully rendered. It lacks Dürer’s penetration, since the gaze does not quite connect, but the clarity and simplicity are impressive in a painter better known for woodcut illustration and altarpieces. The picture suggests what a good pupil took from a master of portraiture."},"met:441672":{"d":"A large double-sided panel, about 140 by 135 cm, painted in oil and gold on fir. The front shows the Dormition of the Virgin, the death of Mary among the apostles; the Met’s terms mention apostles and reading. On the back, Christ carries the cross. The record gives no further description, so the figures, colors and setting are left to the picture itself. The size and the double-sided painting suggest a wing of a large altarpiece.","i":"The Dormition, or falling asleep, of the Virgin comes from apocryphal tradition: the apostles are miraculously gathered at Mary’s bedside to witness her death, after which Christ receives her soul. In German art around 1500 the scene often shows the apostles holding candles and reading prayers. On the back, Christ carrying the cross begins the Passion. A wing painted on both sides would have been seen at different times of the church year.","c":"Schäufelein learned from Dürer how to organize a crowded scene with clarity, and he adds the strong, slightly harsh color and angular drapery of South German painting. As a woodcut designer he told stories briskly, and his figure types are vigorous if coarser than Dürer’s. A large panel in oil and gold is a substantial work for a painter better known for prints, and it shows his gifts as a painter and not only an illustrator."},"met:436190":{"d":"Louis-Félix Amiel (1802 to 1864), named in the title with his dates, is the sole subject of this portrait of 1837, an oil on canvas of about 61 by 50 centimeters. The picture is a bust-sized likeness in the Romantic manner, and the artist, Eugène Devéria, was a painter who had won early fame as a colorist. The sitter is shown as an individual rather than a type.","i":"Portraits of friends and colleagues were common among the Romantic generation of French artists and writers, who liked to record their circle. Devéria had become famous at twenty-two with a vast, brilliantly colored picture of the Birth of Henri IV, shown in 1827, and he moved in the liveliest literary and artistic company. A portrait of 1837 reflects the more personal, sober side of that world.","c":"Devéria's reputation was made by a showy history painting, but a portrait like this one shows a quieter ability to record character. The handling is competent and unfussy, and it suits the Romantic interest in the individual face. It is a modest, honest picture that now interests mainly for the sitter and the moment, though it lacks the force of the era's best portraits."},"met:437867":{"d":"A bouquet of flowers in a crystal vase, painted in 1662 on a canvas of about 50 by 40 cm, when Veerendael was about twenty-two. The Met’s terms list flowers, a vase and still life, and the record gives no description of the blooms, the ground or any insects. Flemish flower pieces of this period typically set a mixed bouquet against a dark ground, and a glass vase gives the painter a chance to show reflected light and the water inside.","i":"Flower still lifes from the Southern Netherlands celebrated God’s creation as well as collectors’ love of rare blooms, and the fragility of cut flowers and glass could also remind viewers that beauty is short-lived. Veerendael was a pupil of Daniel Seghers, the Jesuit lay brother of Antwerp who painted flower garlands around devotional images, and he worked in the same tradition of careful, jewel-like flower painting.","c":"Veerendael works in the Seghers tradition, and a bouquet like this is clear, careful and rather cool: each bloom is placed with precision, and the glass vase is a delicate challenge. Beside the more flamboyant flower painters who came later it is reserved and static, and the flowers can seem arranged rather than growing. A good minor example of the Antwerp school, whose appeal is the freshness of individual blooms, not invention."},"met:19426":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, only 3.8 by 2.5 cm, showing a boy named in the title as Williams Middleton. The Met’s terms list a boy and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his features or dress. At this size a miniature can show little more than the head and shoulders, and it was meant to be held in the hand or kept in a case, a locket or a small frame. It is one of five Middleton miniatures by Mary Roberts at the Met, dated about 1752 to 1758.","i":"The Middletons were a leading planter family of colonial South Carolina, wealthy from rice and indigo grown on plantations near Charleston, and the five miniatures presumably show siblings. A miniature of a child was a keepsake of a household’s heir, sent to relatives or kept close at a time when portraits of any kind marked prosperity. The name Williams, used here as a given name, may recall a family surname, since colonial families often gave mothers’ maiden names to sons.","c":"Roberts’s miniatures are simple and direct, and as far as the format allows, the faces are drawn plainly, the color is clear and the finish neat. At about an inch and a half high they cannot match the polish of the best London miniaturists, but they have a freshness and a sober honesty that suit their young sitters. Women painters were rare in the colonies, and Roberts is among the earliest known, which adds to their interest."},"met:19427":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 2.5 cm, of a boy named in the title as William Middleton. The Met’s terms mention lace, a bow, a ribbon and a waistcoat, so the child is dressed like a small gentleman. It was painted about 1752 to 1758 by Mary Roberts, and at this size little more than the head, collar and shoulders can be shown.","i":"Children of wealthy families in the 1750s were dressed as miniature adults, in waistcoats, lace and ribbons, and the clothing in a portrait advertised the family’s means as much as the child’s looks. In Charleston, textiles and lace came by ship from London, and a boy so dressed told viewers that his family belonged to the colony’s elite.","c":"In so small a space the costume does much of the work, and the lace, bow and waistcoat give this miniature its air of formality. Miniatures of this modest kind do not aim at the subtle modeling of the great European miniaturists, and their value is the directness with which a family’s children were recorded. Neat, bright and engaging, it is best enjoyed with the others in the set."},"met:19428":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 2.5 cm, showing a small boy named in the title as Thomas Middleton, identified elsewhere as Thomas Middleton of Crowfield, a family plantation near Charleston. The Met’s terms list a boy, a child and a portrait; the record gives no description of his features or dress. It was painted about 1752 to 1758 by Mary Roberts.","i":"In the eighteenth century a miniature could be the only lasting likeness of a child, since childhood mortality was high in the colonial South and many children did not live to adulthood. A portrait of a very young boy, as this appears to be, was therefore also an act of remembrance and hope, and the name Crowfield links him to the Middleton plantation world that supported such commissions.","c":"An unpretentious little picture, best understood as a family record and not an exercise in refinement. Roberts does not sentimentalize, as far as such a small scale allows, and the miniature gains from being seen alongside its siblings. Its limits are those of the form in colonial America, where a woman painter could offer neat, honest portraiture but little of the polish that London patrons expected."},"met:19429":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 2.5 cm, showing a girl named in the title as Hester Middleton. The Met’s terms list a girl and a portrait, and the record gives no description of her hair, dress or expression. Painted between about 1752 and 1758 by Mary Roberts, it is a companion to the miniatures of the other Middleton children at the Met, and like them is small enough to be held in the palm.","i":"Portraits of daughters in colonial families often anticipated their roles as future brides: a miniature could be exchanged between families, kept as a likeness for relatives overseas, or worn as a keepsake. In Charleston’s planter society a family’s daughters were part of its alliances, and a portrait of a child signaled that her family could afford to commission one. The record does not describe Hester’s later life.","c":"A child’s portrait of this kind depends on a few strong signs, the eyes, the cheeks, the hair, and a painter of modest training can supply them effectively. These miniatures do not attempt the soft modeling of the great European masters of the form, and their charm lies in their plainness. Seen beside the other Middleton portraits, the picture shows how a simple formula could still give each child an individual presence."},"met:19430":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, about 3.8 by 2.5 cm, showing a girl named in the title as Henrietta Middleton, who as an adult became Henrietta Middleton Rutledge. The Met’s terms list a girl and a portrait, and the record gives no description of her hair, dress or expression. Mary Roberts painted it about 1752 to 1758, and it is small enough to be held in the palm.","i":"Henrietta Middleton later married Edward Rutledge, a South Carolina signer of the Declaration of Independence, so this little girl grew into the leading families of the Revolution. A miniature made in childhood is a rare early likeness of someone who would later belong to the political elite of the new nation, though nothing in the picture itself foretells that. It records a child first and a future role only in hindsight.","c":"Seen as a group, Roberts’s portraits of the Middleton children have the same plain candor, and this one adds the interest of its sitter’s later life. The miniature is a modest thing, bright and simple, and it does not repay comparison with the subtle ivory miniatures of London. Its value is as an early document of colonial American portraiture by a woman painter, and as a small, appealing likeness of a child who grew into history."},"met:10246":{"d":"A woman with a dog, painted in 1786 on a canvas of about 126 by 100 cm. The Met’s terms list a fichu, a ribbon, lace and a shawl, so her dress is soft and informal, with a light scarf at the neckline and trimmings of lace and ribbon. The record gives no description of her pose, the dog or the setting, and the title does not name the sitter.","i":"A lapdog in a woman’s portrait of the 1780s is a sign of affection and fidelity as well as of leisured domestic life, and the soft fichu, lace and ribbon follow the loose, natural fashion that Gainsborough and Reynolds had made popular in English portraiture. Mather Brown, a Boston-born painter in London and a student of Benjamin West, was trying in the 1780s to make a career in a crowded market.","c":"Brown absorbs the English portrait style of Reynolds and Romney, with fluent brushwork and warm color, and the picture is graceful. It is also derivative: the type is borrowed from more famous painters, and the face is more generic than personal. The dog adds an element of informality, and the picture is a fair measure of what an ambitious American could achieve in London in the 1780s."},"nga:560":{"d":"A half-length portrait shows a young man in a black coat with a high collar and a white cravat. He faces forward while his head turns slightly aside; his features are sharp, and his light hair is dressed in a classic manner of the day. A red drapery fills the background behind him. The canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, is a modest portrait format. It was painted in 1787 by Mather Brown, an American working in London, and shows William Vans Murray, a Marylander then in his twenties.","i":"Murray (1760–1803) was a Maryland lawyer who studied in London in the 1780s and later served in Congress and as United States minister to the Netherlands, where he helped open the negotiations that ended the undeclared naval conflict with France. The plain dark coat suits a serious young professional, while the red drapery, borrowed from grand portrait tradition, lends dignity. Brown's London clientele included many Americans abroad.","c":"Brown learned from Benjamin West and from the grand English portraitists, and this early portrait shows a young painter handling a conventional formula with competence: the face is crisply drawn and the drapery gives vigor to an otherwise sober composition. It lacks the fluid brushwork of Reynolds or Lawrence, and the characterization is guarded. Still, it is a clear, honest likeness of a significant early American diplomat."},"nga:34076":{"d":"A man in a dark suit and white cravat sits turned slightly to his right, lit against a red drapery. He has light brown curly hair, fair skin, light blue eyes, a straight nose and a faint smile. Behind the drapery's edge, books are stacked horizontally, only partly hidden. The canvas, about 75 by 63 centimeters, is a half-length portrait painted by the American Mather Brown in London about 1788, with the figure brightly lit against the saturated red behind.","i":"The sitter is Thomas Dawson (1725–1813), an Irish landowner and politician raised to the peerage as Viscount Cremorne in 1785. The books behind him are the traditional sign of a cultivated, learned gentleman, while the red curtain is a standard marker of rank, and the plain dark suit shows a man comfortable enough in his position to avoid display. The mild smile, less common in formal portraiture of the time, softens the formality.","c":"Brown's portraits of the late 1780s are pleasantly fluent without being profound, and this one fits that description. The red backdrop and the sitter's gentle smile give life to a conventional design, and the books are neatly integrated rather than piled on as props. The brushwork is smooth and somewhat cautious. A good, unforced likeness, it shows why an American trained by West could find sitters among the British and Irish gentry."},"met:18113":{"d":"A portrait of General George Eliott on a very large canvas, about 249 by 164 cm, large enough for a full-length figure, dated 1790. The Met’s terms list a general, a sword and a battle, so the sitter is shown with a sword and with a battle scene, presumably the Great Siege of Gibraltar of 1779 to 1783, for which Eliott was famous. The record gives no further description of his uniform, pose or the battle.","i":"George Augustus Eliott (1717 to 1790) was governor of Gibraltar and commanded its garrison through the Great Siege against France and Spain, a defense that made him a national hero. He was raised to the peerage as Baron Heathfield in 1787 and died in 1790. Sir Joshua Reynolds painted him in 1787 holding the key to the fortress, and John Singleton Copley painted the siege itself. Brown’s picture belongs to the same celebration.","c":"A large, ambitious, patriotic canvas. Brown follows the conventions of British heroic portraiture, a standing commander before a battle, but without the force of Reynolds’s portrait of Heathfield or Copley’s siege pictures, whose fame eclipsed it. It shows the aspirations of a young American in London to be a history painter, with bold scale and a charged subject. The drama is more stagey than convincing, but the ambition is evident."},"met:10247":{"d":"A young woman with a piano, painted in 1801 on a canvas of about 127 by 102 cm. The Met’s terms list a piano, a girl and women, and call the picture a portrait. The record gives no description of her pose, her dress or the placement of the instrument, and the sitter is not named. The size suggests a formal portrait of a young lady of a well-off household.","i":"By 1801 the piano was the fashionable instrument for accomplished young women, and a portrait with a keyboard instrument signaled education, refinement and, often, readiness for marriage. Such pictures presented music as one of the polite accomplishments a family could cultivate in its daughters. Mather Brown, still working in London, was by this time past the peak of the success he had enjoyed in the 1780s.","c":"Brown’s later portraits are quieter than his showy pictures of the 1780s, with the simpler lines and plain grounds of early-nineteenth-century taste. They are competent without being inspired, and a portrait with a piano invites the pleasant business of fabrics, hands and a polished instrument. A decent, unassuming picture that shows an American in London working steadily in the middle of the market."},"nga:1100":{"d":"The upper half of a man is shown in a formal, seated pose, angled slightly to his right with his head turned toward the viewer. He has short dark brown hair, a smooth complexion, well-defined eyebrows and a serious expression. He wears a dark jacket with gold buttons over a high-collared white shirt with a ruffled front, and the background is a dark, neutral color. The panel measures about 71 by 54 cm and is dated about 1820.","i":"Matthew Harris Jouett was the leading portraitist of Kentucky, and he had studied briefly with Gilbert Stuart in Boston in 1816. His Kentucky sitters, frontier gentry and professional men, wanted to look like the gentlemen of the East Coast and of England, and a dark coat, white cravat and plain ground gave them that polish. The record does not say who Augustus Fielding Hawkins was, so nothing more is asserted about him.","c":"Jouett took from Stuart a feeling for flesh color and a soft, lifelike modeling, and the face here has the quiet directness of that school. His work is more modest than his teacher’s, sometimes a little stiff in pose, and this is a plain, serious likeness without display. Its strength is the quality of attention: a young man sitting for an honest portrait by a leading portraitist of his region."},"met:11284":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as John Grimes, painted about 1824 in oil on a wood panel of about 72 by 55 cm. The Met’s terms list only a man and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. Jouett, who died in 1827 at thirty-nine, painted it in the last years of his short career.","i":"By the mid-1820s Jouett was the established portraitist of Lexington and its region, painting lawyers, politicians, merchants and their families. Several of his portraits, like this one, are painted on wood panels, a feature that gives his small-format likenesses a smooth, enamel-like finish. The record does not say who Grimes was, so his profession and his connection to the painter are left open.","c":"A candid, plain likeness from the last years of Jouett’s career, in the method learned from Stuart, which models flesh softly and keeps backgrounds plain. He lacks his teacher’s subtlety, but he supplies what his sitters wanted, a clear, dignified record, and the best of his portraits have a freshness that survives their provincial limits. Without a closer description, this one is best placed as representative rather than exceptional."},"cma:97267":{"d":"A portrait of a man named in the title as James G. McKinney, in oil on a canvas of about 65 by 52 cm and dated only as the early 1800s. Because Jouett painted from about 1815 until his death in 1827, the portrait probably belongs to the 1810s or 1820s. The record gives no description of the sitter’s age, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"Portraits of local men, merchants, lawyers and landowners, were the staple of the American frontier painter, and a likeness by the region’s best-known portraitist was a mark of standing in a growing town. The record does not say who McKinney was, and nothing in it ties the portrait to a particular commission, so his place in Kentucky society is left open.","c":"A modest, workmanlike likeness, and the record does not allow a close judgment. Jouett’s best heads are fresh and direct, and the weaker ones are stiff, and how this one falls is for the viewer to decide. It is at least representative of the plain, honest portraiture of the early republic, made by a painter of limited training who learned quickly from Gilbert Stuart."},"cma:120877":{"d":"A portrait of Senator Thomas Hart Benton, in oil on a wood panel of about 71 by 60 cm, dated to the 1820s. The record gives no description of his pose, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. Benton (1782 to 1858) was by then a leading figure in Washington, a senator from Missouri, and a likeness of such a man was a mark of public standing.","i":"Thomas Hart Benton was one of the first two United States senators from Missouri, elected when the state entered the Union in 1821, and he served until 1851. Nicknamed Old Bullion for his defense of gold and silver coin over paper money, he was a champion of westward expansion. The twentieth-century painter of the same name, Thomas Hart Benton, was his great-nephew.","c":"A competent, historically interesting likeness of a prominent public man by a painter whose strength was plain, clear portraiture, taught by Stuart and tailored to American sitters. It carries less rhetoric than the Washington portraits of grander painters, which suits the straightforward character of its maker. It is not a revelation, but it is a credible record of a public face in the 1820s."},"met:11797":{"d":"A group portrait of artists in a studio, presumably Benjamin West’s in London: the Met’s terms identify West and Matthew Pratt, the painter himself, among the figures, with an easel, a painting support and a palette in view. The canvas is about 91 by 128 cm and dated 1765. The record gives no further description, so the grouping of the figures and the work on the easel are left to the picture itself.","i":"Benjamin West, born in Pennsylvania, settled in London in 1763 and became history painter to George III and later president of the Royal Academy. His studio taught a long line of young Americans, among them Pratt, who studied there in the mid-1760s, and later Charles Willson Peale, Gilbert Stuart and John Trumbull. The title can be read as naming both a studio and the beginnings of an American school of painting.","c":"Pratt’s best-known work and a rare, informal record of a studio. Its charm lies in its documentary interest and its modest, friendly arrangement; as painting, the figures are somewhat stiff and the space cramped, and the handling is competent but not brilliant, showing Pratt’s debt to West and to English conversation pieces. The interest is historical more than aesthetic, but it is a lively image of how American painters trained in the 1760s."},"nga:30744":{"d":"A devotional picture of the Virgin and Child with Saint Jerome, painted between 1764 and 1766 on a canvas of about 78 by 60 cm. The record gives no description, but the title is that of Correggio’s celebrated altarpiece in Parma, and a picture of this name by a student in London in the mid-1760s is most likely a copy or free reworking of it, though the record does not say so. In Correggio’s altarpiece the Virgin and Child are attended by Jerome and Mary Magdalene.","i":"Copying was how young American painters, with no old masters at home, learned: London offered copies and prints of Italian paintings, and copying Correggio let a student study color and composition. Correggio, with his soft modeling and tender expression, was admired in the eighteenth century as a master of grace. Saint Jerome, the translator of the Latin Bible, is the saint of scholars and is usually shown with a lion and a book.","c":"If, as seems likely, this is a copy, it shows how much Pratt had to learn: copies lose the fluency and melting transitions of an original, and a small canvas cannot carry Correggio’s elaborate composition. The interest lies in the exercise more than the result, a young American learning from an Italian master through a London studio, and a useful reminder that early American painting was an apprenticeship to Europe."},"met:19815":{"d":"A man seen in profile, painted about 1769 on a canvas of about 76 by 64 cm. The Met’s terms list a man, a portrait and a profile, and the record gives no further description of his dress, expression or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. A profile was a less common choice in colonial American portraiture than the three-quarter view, and it gives the picture the severity of a medal. The Met also holds the portrait of Christiana Stille Keen, presumably his wife.","i":"Profile portraits recall Roman coins and medals, which Enlightenment viewers admired as images of civic virtue, and a profile gives a sitter a calm, impersonal dignity. Pratt painted this picture and its companion in profile soon after returning to Philadelphia from his training with Benjamin West in London. The record does not say who Reynold Keen was, so nothing more is asserted about him.","c":"The profile format suits Pratt’s plain, clean manner, learned in West’s studio: a firm contour, a clear light, no fuss. It lacks the glowing surfaces and psychological richness of Copley’s colonial portraits, but its restraint is distinctive, and the pair has a dignity that grows from simplicity. A characteristic example of Philadelphia portraiture in the late 1760s."},"met:19816":{"d":"A woman seen in profile with a flower, painted about 1769 on a canvas of about 76 by 64 cm. The Met’s terms list a woman, a profile and a flower, and the record gives no further description of her dress, hair or expression, so those are left to the picture itself. It is presumably the pendant to the portrait of Reynold Keen, which has the same size and date and is also in profile.","i":"A flower in a woman’s portrait signals beauty and feminine virtue, and in marriage portraits it can also stand for love or fidelity, though the record does not say what this one means. As a pendant to her presumed husband’s portrait, it recorded a marriage as a matched pair. The profile gives the sitter a calm, classical dignity, like a head on an ancient coin.","c":"The pair shows Pratt’s plain, disciplined manner, learned from West: clean contours, clear light and an unfussy arrangement. A profile asks little of a painter’s modeling and much of his drawing, and his training in West’s studio should have given him a firm line. The portrait lacks the richness of fabric and surface that Copley gave his Boston ladies, but its restraint gives it a quiet character of its own."},"met:11799":{"d":"A portrait of Elizabeth De Lancey, titled Mrs. Peter De Lancey, painted about 1771 on a canvas of about 76 by 64 cm. The Met’s terms list a woman and a portrait, and the record gives no description of her dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. Pratt painted a second De Lancey family portrait about a year later, Cadwallader Colden and His Grandson Warren De Lancey, which is also at the Met.","i":"The De Lanceys were among the wealthiest and most powerful families of colonial New York, one that would largely side with the Crown in the Revolution. A portrait by a painter who had trained with Benjamin West in London gave a sitter the polish of the English school at a time when New Yorkers wanted English fashion. The record does not describe Mrs. De Lancey’s life, so nothing more is asserted about her.","c":"Pratt’s New York portraits are neat, a little dry, and more attentive to likeness than to luxury of costume; beside Copley’s brilliant rendering of satin and lace they look plain. They have an honest, unflattering directness that is their strength, since the sitter is looked at, not dressed up. The record offers few details, but the type is clear, sober and reliable."},"nga:3159":{"d":"A portrait of William Henry Cavendish Bentinck, 3rd Duke of Portland (1738 to 1809), painted about 1774 on a canvas of about 76 by 63 cm, a standard bust-length size. The record gives no description of his pose, dress or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. A portrait of an English duke by an American painter in 1774 is unusual, and the record does not say where Pratt painted it or whether it follows another artist’s portrait.","i":"The 3rd Duke of Portland was one of the richest English aristocrats and a leading Whig. He served twice as prime minister, briefly in 1783 and again from 1807 to 1809, and was chancellor of the University of Oxford from 1792. In 1774 he was thirty-six. A portrait of a man of his rank, even by an American painter, was an unusual commission, and it links the story of colonial painting to the English elite.","c":"Pratt’s plain, clear style is not the grand manner that an English duke usually received from Reynolds or Gainsborough, and the result is modest beside the sitter’s rank. That honesty has its own interest: it is an American painter’s English portrait, trained in West’s studio but lacking the glamour of the London portraitists. As a painting it is sound rather than splendid."},"met:11798":{"d":"An old man and a boy share a canvas of about 127 by 102 cm, painted about 1772. The title names them as Cadwallader Colden and his grandson Warren De Lancey, and the Met’s terms list a boy and a man. The record gives no further description of their dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. It is a larger and more ambitious canvas than Pratt’s bust-length portraits.","i":"Cadwallader Colden (1688 to 1776) was a physician, scientist and colonial official who served as lieutenant governor of New York and acted as its governor several times, and he remained a firm servant of the Crown as the Revolution approached. A grandfather portrayed with a grandson is a dynastic image, showing a name and an office passing to a younger generation, and the De Lancey grandson ties two leading New York families together.","c":"A more complex task than a single head, and Pratt handles it with modest success: the old and the young face are set in contrast, and the arrangement is clear and dignified. The figures remain a little stiff and the surroundings plain, but the picture’s human interest, an elderly statesman beside a child, gives it a warmth. A good example of Pratt’s work on a family theme."},"nga:147":{"d":"A woman in a carnation-pink dress, gold crown and gold halo climbs a ladder with a sledgehammer over one shoulder, toward a life-sized white statue of a nude male on a tall base decorated with eagles and garlands. The statue holds a gold staff and what appears to be a branch. The scene is a narrow town square, with about a dozen onlookers on the ground and on a balcony, and two women brace the foot of the ladder. A building at the right bears the carved name EVSEBIV. The panel is vertical and arched.","i":"Saint Apollonia was a Christian martyr of Alexandria in the third century, remembered above all for having her teeth broken in torture, which made her the patron saint of toothache and of dentists. Here she is shown in an episode from her legend, destroying a pagan idol, an act of zeal that stands for Christianity’s triumph over the old gods. The carved name EVSEBIV may refer to Eusebius, the early church historian who preserves the account of her martyrdom, though the museum text does not explain it.","c":"A delightful narrative picture by Giovanni d’Alemagna, a German-born painter who worked in Venice with Antonio Vivarini. The saint’s determined climb, hammer on shoulder, is vivid and a little comic, and the neat square with its watching crowd has the feel of civic theater. The color is clear, the figures slender and elegant in the late Gothic manner, and the antique base adds an early Renaissance note. The space is not fully convincing, but the charm outweighs the stiffness."},"nga:50662":{"d":"A very small panel, about 14 by 10.5 cm, about the size of a postcard, showing Saint George and the dragon, painted in oil about 1432 to 1435. The record gives no description of the picture. By tradition the subject shows George, a Roman soldier-saint in armor, killing the dragon that threatened a city, with the princess he saved nearby; how Rogier arranged these elements is left to the picture itself. At this scale the work was meant to be held or kept close, probably for private devotion.","i":"According to the Golden Legend, a dragon terrorized a city in Libya and demanded victims. When the king’s daughter was chosen, George rode in, subdued the dragon with his lance and led it into the city, whose people then converted. The story became the pattern of Christian chivalry, and George was patron of knights and of England, whose Order of the Garter is under his protection. It is usually read as the triumph of faith over evil.","c":"Rogier, trained in the tradition of Campin and Van Eyck, is best known for large, emotionally intense altarpieces such as the Deposition (Prado, about 1435). This tiny George is another thing: a jewel-like narrative in which precision of detail and clarity of color replace his usual pathos. It dates from the early years of his independent career, and like all miniature-scale panels it asks to be looked at close."},"met:435837":{"d":"A small panel, about 32 by 23 cm, showing a man in prayer, painted in oil about 1440. The Met’s terms list a man and a portrait, and the record gives no description of his features, dress or hands. A man in prayer in a portrait of this size and date is probably the donor wing of a devotional diptych, turned toward a companion panel with the Virgin and Child, though the record does not say so.","i":"In the fifteenth-century Netherlands, wealthy patrons ordered small diptychs for private prayer: on one wing a sacred figure, usually the Virgin and Child, and on the other the owner, shown in prayer. The donor’s portrait put an individual presence before the holy figure and invited the viewer to join the prayer. Which companion panel this man faced, and who he was, are not recorded.","c":"The portrait type is one Rogier and his contemporaries perfected: a head modeled in clear light, a dark ground to isolate the devout face and a sense of inward attention. One record gives a different attribution, to the workshop of Robert Campin, Rogier’s early master, so the quality of the actual paint matters; the best Rogiers have a sharp, linear grace that few workshop panels match. As an example of the type, it remains valuable."},"nga:51":{"d":"A small, bust-length oil portrait of an unnamed woman, about 34 by 25.5 cm, painted about 1460 in Rogier van der Weyden’s late manner. The museum record gives no description, so the details of her dress and expression are best looked for in the picture itself. Rogier’s portraits of this kind typically isolate a single sitter against a plain ground, so that attention falls on the face, the headdress and the hands.","i":"Portraits of women in the fifteenth-century Netherlands, usually made on marriage or for a family, advertised status through costume: fine linen, a fashionable headdress, a dark expensive gown. The sitter of this one is not securely identified, a common situation for early Netherlandish portraits of women, and the picture is best read as a likeness of a woman of means in the Burgundian Netherlands, not as a record of a known life.","c":"A highly refined example of Rogier’s late portrait style, in which the drawing is exact and cool and the sitter seems at a delicate remove from the viewer. The restraint is the picture’s power: an economy of line, a stillness, a tension between delicate features and the austerity of the design. Those who prefer Van Eyck’s texture may find Rogier more spiritual and less tactile, but both painters made portraits of lasting force."},"met:437487":{"d":"A small bust-length portrait of a young man set against a flat, pale ivory ground that has cracked with age. His brown hair is cut in a straight fringe and falls over his ears; his eyes are pale and heavy-lidded, his nose long and thin, and his gaze passes to the left beyond the viewer. He wears a black gown with a narrow white edge at the neck, a red collar and a thin gold chain. His long, bony hands cross the lower edge of the panel; the one in front holds a small hammer, and a gold ring is on the finger beside it. The panel is only about thirty centimetres high.","i":"The sitter was long thought to be Lionello d'Este, Marquis of Ferrara and a patron of Rogier, until Ernst Kantorowicz argued in 1939–40 that he was Lionello's illegitimate son Francesco, who was raised at the Burgundian court and served Burgundy as an officer and ambassador. The d'Este arms appear on the back. The panel is dated about 1460. The hammer and ring have never been explained: they may refer to court ritual or tournaments, and the ring was painted out until a cleaning in 1934 revealed it. At one time the picture was even titled Portrait of a Goldsmith. It came to the Museum in the Friedsam bequest of 1931.","c":"Rogier's late portraits tend toward severity, and this is among the clearest. The flat pale ground removes all setting, leaving the head, the dark gown and the hands, and the long fingers with their small objects are the most vivid things in the picture. The head is small against the broad shoulders and the expression is guarded, so the panel declares rank more than it reveals character. As an image of court manner, cool, upright and reserved, it is hard to match."},"met:437865":{"d":"A hunting party has halted for a meal outdoors, with men, women, food, drink and a dog in an oil on canvas about 59 by 50 centimeters, painted about 1737. The museum's keywords record a man, a woman, food, a picnic, a dog and drinking, so the number of figures, their costumes, the landscape and the colors are left unstated. The subject, a fashionable picnic during the hunt, belongs to French Rococo painting of the court of Louis XV.","i":"The halt of the hunt, with ladies and gentlemen eating and flirting in a woodland clearing, was a favorite subject of French Rococo painters, since it combined the royal pastime of the chase with the charm of the fête galante. Carle Van Loo, who later became first painter to the king, painted a large Halt of the Hunt for the French court in the late 1730s, and this smaller canvas may be related to that commission.","c":"Van Loo handles the scene with cheerful fluency: the playful grouping, the sparkle of silks, and the dog and the food as lively still-life accents. It is a typical Rococo confection, with less wit than Watteau's fêtes and less sensuality than Boucher's pastorals, but with more narrative clarity than either. The picture works as a pleasing emblem of aristocratic leisure; it asks to be enjoyed, not interrogated."},"cma:139532":{"d":"This loosely painted sketch is the preliminary design for an altarpiece in the church of Saint Ulrich, Vienna. Mary and Joseph have brought the infant Jesus to the temple; the aged Simeon cradles the child in his arms, turning his gaze to heaven as he realizes he is holding the Son of God. Strong contrasts of light and shadow underscore the drama. The canvas is about 70 by 52 cm, and the florid frame is a mid-eighteenth-century Austrian addition.","i":"The Presentation follows Luke's Gospel: forty days after the birth, the parents bring Jesus to the Temple to fulfill the law, and Simeon, who had been promised that he would see the Messiah, recognizes him and gives thanks. He also warns Mary that a sword will pierce her heart, a hint of the Passion. An altarpiece on this subject presents the child as offering and revelation at once, for a congregation that was meant to share Simeon's wonder.","c":"Maulbertsch's sketches are among the most spirited things in Austrian Rococo, and this one shows why: flickering strokes, a shadowed ground and a few bright accents give the moment its drama without detail. A sketch gets to the point more quickly than a finished altarpiece, and it has the freshness of a decision being made. The florid frame suits it. The price is the roughness that a patron would have wanted resolved."},"met:439117":{"d":"Saints surrounded by clouds are the subject of this oil on canvas, about 70 by 51 cm, dated about 1772 to 1773. The title names the Royal Hungarian Saints, the holy kings and princes of Hungary, who were usually Stephen, his son Emeric and Ladislaus. The small size and the subject suggest a design for a larger project such as a ceiling or altarpiece, in the vaporous, shimmering manner of the Austrian Rococo.","i":"Maria Theresa, who ruled Hungary as queen as well as Austria as archduchess, promoted the cult of Saint Stephen and founded the Order of Saint Stephen in 1764. A glorification, or apotheosis, shows the saints received into heaven among clouds and light, a form that lent the dynasty sacred ancestors and linked the crown to heaven. The image served a political message as well as a religious one: the Hungarian holy kings blessing the Habsburg monarchy.","c":"Maulbertsch's sketches are the high point of the Austrian Rococo's flicker and shimmer, and a heavenly glorification is the right subject for them: saints floating in cloud, light breaking through. Critics have often praised the vibrating touch and the luminous color, sometimes found acid. The danger is restlessness, a vortex without a still center, but in a sketch that energy is a virtue, and the picture is more alive than a finished version might be."},"met:435818":{"d":"A small portrait of an unnamed man, painted in oil on oak in 1533, only about 30 by 22 centimetres. Barthel Bruyn the Elder, the leading portraitist of Cologne, often shows his sitters close up and half-length, with the face, the dress and the hands doing the work and little else. The small oak panel was probably painted for a private house, where it hung as a record of the sitter's person and standing.","i":"Cologne in the 1530s was a rich free imperial city, and its merchants, councillors and officials had themselves portrayed as signs of standing and family continuity. Small panels like this one were often painted in pairs for a husband and wife, and this man's companion may be the Portrait of a Woman of the same size and date in the same collection. The sitter is unidentified, so the picture is read as a type: a respectable Cologne citizen, recorded plainly and without flourish.","c":"Bruyn is at his best when he stays plain: a clear light on the face, firm contour, accurate detail and no attempt to flatter. This portrait belongs to that sober tradition. It lacks the psychological subtlety of Holbein's contemporary portraits, and Bruyn's sitters tend to be reserved, but the honesty is attractive, and in its small format the picture has more presence than its size suggests."},"met:435819":{"d":"A small portrait of an unnamed woman, painted in oil on oak in 1533, about 30 by 22 centimetres, the same size and date as the Portrait of a Man by the same artist in the same collection. The two panels may be companions. Bruyn the Elder's women sitters are usually shown half-length, in the headdress and costume of a well-to-do Cologne household, with fabric and jewellery serving as signs of status.","i":"A wife's portrait in the sixteenth century served the family as much as the sitter. Dress, headwear and jewellery told visitors about the household's wealth, and a modest, composed expression told them about her virtue. If this panel was made as a companion to the Portrait of a Man of the same year, as the identical size and date suggest, the pair would have shown a married couple. Her name is not recorded, so she is read as a type.","c":"The portrait has the virtues of Bruyn's plain style: a careful likeness, clean modelling and a refusal to prettify. It is a less adventurous picture than the Italianate portraits then appearing elsewhere in Europe, and the stiffness that Bruyn's sitters can show reads as sober dignity. If seen with its companion, it gives a believable picture of the Cologne middle class on the eve of the religious conflicts of the later sixteenth century."},"nga:1178":{"d":"In a deeply shadowed room, lit sharply from the left, a man, a woman holding a swaddled baby and a satyr, with a man’s torso and goat’s legs, sit and stand around a table. The bare-chested satyr, crowned with leaves and with a horn almost lost in shadow, leans toward the table and looks up at the woman, one palm open. The peasant at the right, his back mostly turned, looks over his shoulder with one brow raised, cheeks puffed as he blows on a spoonful of soup. The woman smiles at the satyr from the corners of her eyes, and the baby twists to stare too.","i":"The scene illustrates Aesop’s fable of the satyr and the traveler, or peasant. Taken in on a cold day, the satyr watches his host blow on his fingers to warm them and then on his soup to cool it, and flees, unable to trust a man who blows hot and cold with the same breath. A tale of country wit and the double-dealer, it was popular with painters in Antwerp, such as Jordaens, in the 1620s.","c":"A bold, large, theatrical picture. The raking light from the left, the deep shadow with a few details emerging, and the comic faces, the cocked brow, the puffed cheeks, the baby’s wondering look, make an amusing, human scene that is easy to read. The handling combines northern low-life humor with Italian chiaroscuro and shows why Liss influenced Venetian painters. The dark ground swallows some secondary detail, but the brilliance of the central group more than redeems it."},"met:828241":{"d":"The Magdalen is shown at a moment of choice between worldly pleasure and repentance. The Met’s terms list the saint, an angel and a skull, and the canvas is about 99 by 126 cm, painted about 1626. The record gives no description of the setting, her dress or the other figures beyond those terms, so the detail of the composition is left to the picture itself. The wide format leaves room for the interplay between the angel’s call and the saint’s hesitation.","i":"Mary Magdalene, the sinner forgiven by Christ, was a favorite of Counter-Reformation art, since her story of repentance showed the Church’s forgiveness and the value of penance. Scenes of her temptation or conversion set earthly vanity against the heavenly call, with an angel urging her upward. The skull is a memento mori, a reminder of death and of the emptiness of worldly show, and a standard sign of the penitent saint.","c":"Liss’s late Venetian manner is all bravura: rich color, shimmering fabrics, dramatic light and a restless, sensuous brush. Subjects like this one let him have it both ways, since the picture can condemn vanity while savoring its pleasures, and the temptation is often more vivid than the virtue. The record is thin on detail, but the date, only about five years before his early death, places it among his most accomplished years."},"cma:145452":{"d":"Cupid, the god of love, looks directly out of a canvas of about 88 by 66 cm, painted about 1630. The museum notes rich colors, fluid brushwork, deep contrasts of light and shadow and the concentrated energy of the pose, which together create a strong connection with the viewer. The record does not describe his attributes, such as bow, arrows or wings, so those are left to the picture itself.","i":"Cupid, also called Amor, was the Roman god of love and the son of Venus. In the early seventeenth century a single-figure Cupid was a popular subject, sensuous, witty and easily read as a sign of love’s power or a warning of its pain. Caravaggio’s Victorious Love of about 1601 to 1602 set the model for an impudent Cupid who stares at the viewer, and the museum notes Liss’s debt to Caravaggio.","c":"Liss’s Cupid draws its force from handling: warm flesh tones, loaded strokes and a dark ground. It lacks Caravaggio’s startling realism but replaces it with a Venetian love of color and a lively painterly surface, and the direct gaze keeps it from sweetness. A good example of why Liss, who died young, in his early thirties, was a bridge between northern realism and Venetian Baroque."},"met:439118":{"d":"A portrait of the Reverend Philip Cocks (1735 to 1797), painted in the late 1760s, when he was in his thirties, on a canvas of about 90 by 69 cm. The Met’s terms list only a man, and the record gives no description of his dress, pose or setting, so those are left to the picture itself. The title gives him as a clergyman; whether he is shown in clerical dress is not recorded.","i":"Johan Zoffany, German-born, came to England in 1760 and rose through theatrical portraits of David Garrick and the patronage of the royal family, becoming a founding member of the Royal Academy in 1768. Alongside his conversation pieces he painted single portraits for the gentry and professional classes, and a clergyman’s likeness is typical of that market. The record does not say where Cocks served.","c":"Zoffany’s strength was lively, precise observation: clear light on the face, an unflattering truthfulness about expression and crisp detail in the surroundings. A single-figure portrait gives him less scope than his conversation pieces, but the sitter’s personality can still be read in the face, and the style is plainer than Reynolds’s grand manner. A pleasing, direct likeness, and a reminder that his range included quiet portraits as well as theatrical set pieces."},"nga:61398":{"d":"Seven children and a dark brown dog gather in a grassy clearing beside a shadowy grove. A plank laid across a sawn trunk makes a seesaw: a boy in fawn brown sits low at the left, the eldest girl steadies the youngest on the raised end, and a boy in moss green stands balanced at the middle, lifting his cap. At the right, a girl in a blue sash touches the hand of the oldest boy, who holds a small silver fish, while another girl sits in front with the dog. A pool glints in the lower right corner, and a valley opens to distant hills.","i":"Johann Zoffany, a German-born painter who settled in London, made his name with conversation pieces: group portraits showing a family at ease in a believable setting. A commission like this told parents their children were lively and well turned out, and told visitors the family could afford to have them painted. The seesaw, fish and dog are probably props of a day's outing, not a hidden story. Such pictures reflect a growing eighteenth-century interest in childhood as a playful stage of life.","c":"Zoffany's gift was for the individual face and the crisp, almost enamel-like surfaces of silk, ribbon and buckle, and both are on show. The seesaw's diagonal keeps a seven-figure group from freezing into a frieze, and the dark wood at the right makes the pale dresses glow. Against that, the poses stay a little staged, the children read as small adults, and the landscape works as a backdrop. A charming, skilled record rather than a searching one."},"nga:46138":{"d":"About twenty colorfully dressed people fill a long, shallow room behind an elderly man and a young woman who stand before a bishop in a pearl-white robe and tall pointed hat. The couple, both with traces of gold halos, face each other, and the bishop joins their outstretched hands. Men gather at the left and women at the right, glancing in every direction. One man plays a lute, others break sticks, and more broken sticks lie on the floor. At the far right an old woman in black shuts her eyes and raises a palm. The walls are pale lavender.","i":"The scene follows the legend, told in apocryphal gospels and retold in the Golden Legend, that Mary's suitors each brought a rod to the Temple and that only Joseph's burst into flower, marking him as her husband. The rejected suitors, snapping their rods in frustration, give the story its comic edge, and the bishop stands for the Temple's high priest. Episodes from Mary's life were common subjects for the small predella panels beneath altarpieces, and this long, narrow format suggests that was probably its original place.","c":"Early Signorelli, and small. It lacks the muscular drama of the Orvieto frescoes he would paint a decade later, and the figures are slight. What succeeds is the staging: a crowd set in a clear, shallow space, the central gesture legible at a glance, lively reactions from the onlookers, and an unusual range of raspberry, teal and gold against plum walls. Charming rather than moving, and a good example of how he managed a crowd."},"met:437673":{"d":"A tall, upright panel of altarpiece scale, about 171 by 131 centimeters, painted in oil with gold. Its subject is the Assumption of the Virgin, the moment when Mary is taken up bodily into heaven, with the archangel Michael and Saint Benedict as companion figures. Angels and a devil are also recorded among its figures. The format is that of a picture meant to be read from a distance above an altar, with each figure shown clearly and given a distinct role in the devotion it supported.","i":"Mary's Assumption, celebrated on 15 August, was one of the most popular dedications for Italian churches. Michael, leader of the heavenly army, is usually shown in armor overcoming the devil, and the devil's presence here fits that pattern. Benedict, author of the Rule that organized Western monastic life, points to a Benedictine setting, though that is an inference from his presence. Together the saints pair heaven's reward for Mary with the defeat of evil and the discipline of the cloister.","c":"Signorelli's strength in these years was sculptural drawing: figures with weight and clear contours, and a taste for strong action over sweetness. A critic would look here for structure and firmness rather than charm. His altarpieces of the 1490s tend to be ordered and severe, and the real fireworks come a few years later in the Orvieto frescoes, where the same anatomical power has full scope. This panel is a sturdy, serious work from the run-up to that peak."},"nga:41690":{"d":"In this horizontal panel of tempera, oil and gilding, more than two dozen men and women press together at the foot of three crosses. The crucified bodies sag, tied and nailed, with blood running from hands and feet and from a gash in the central figure's side, where a mounted soldier on a white horse drives in a spear. Spears bristle in verticals and diagonals. At lower left a woman in a navy robe has fainted, held by two kneeling women, while another woman and a clean-shaven man look on with hands clasped low. A spindly tree rises at right, with a distant city and blue clouds behind.","i":"The mounted soldier is traditionally named Longinus, the Roman who pierced Christ's side, and the other two crosses carry the thieves crucified with him. The fainting woman is the Virgin Mary, supported by the holy women, and the man with clasped hands is probably Saint John the Evangelist; the Gospel of John places both at the cross. A crowded Calvary of this kind turned the event into a public spectacle that the devout viewer could enter, feeling both the cruelty and the grief.","c":"Signorelli, famous for muscular bodies, turned here to organizing a crowd, and the bristling spears give the surface a strong rhythm of verticals and diagonals. The fainting Virgin supplies the emotional center. But so many figures share the grief that its force thins, faces repeat, and the bright colors keep the mood closer to pageant than tragedy. A solid mature work in which design counts for more than pathos, and less daring than the Orvieto frescoes that preceded it."},"met:437674":{"d":"A small, almost square panel, about 51 by 48 centimeters, painted in oil with gold. Its only subjects are the Virgin Mary and the Christ Child, the plain pairing of a picture made for private prayer rather than for a church altar. Signorelli painted it around 1505 to 1507, soon after finishing his great fresco cycle in Orvieto Cathedral. Pictures of this type invite the viewer to look at the intimate bond of mother and child, with the larger meaning of the Incarnation understood but not narrated.","i":"The Madonna and Child is the most repeated subject of Italian Renaissance religious art. In a small panel the subject is reduced to its essentials: Mary as the mother who intercedes with her Son, and the Child as Savior, whose later suffering is sometimes hinted by a gesture or a held object. Families kept such panels in bedrooms or small chapels to focus daily prayer, and workshops made them in numbers, so the quality of execution, more than novelty of subject, set one apart from another.","c":"For a painter known for large, crowded, muscular fresco cycles, a small Madonna is a quieter and less original kind of work. What a critic can fairly credit is the firm drawing and sculptural solidity that Signorelli brought even to modest commissions, and the careful finish that suited a private picture. What is missing is the invention and energy of the great cycles. This belongs to the steady devotional output around them: competent, devout and not surprising."},"nga:46186":{"d":"Mary sits on a white stone pedestal on a broad step, her veiled head tilted as she looks down at the nude infant in her arms, who reaches toward her face. She wears a raspberry-pink dress under a midnight-blue cloak. Four men flank her: at upper left a winged man in gold helmet and breastplate raises a sword and holds scales in which a tiny figure kneels in prayer; at right a white-bearded man in a split, pointed hat reads an open book; below, two kneeling men hold books, one writing. Four small angels hover behind her, two holding white flowers. Blue mountains and pale sky lie beyond.","i":"The picture is a sacra conversazione, or holy conversation, in which the Virgin and saints from different centuries share one calm space without any narrated event. The winged figure with sword and scales is the archangel Michael, who weighs souls at the Last Judgment; the tiny figure in the pan, praying to Mary, presumably shows a soul seeking her intercession. The white flowers are lilies, a sign of Mary's purity. The two writers are probably learned saints, such as Church Fathers or evangelists, and the pointed hat marks a bishop.","c":"By the mid-1510s Signorelli was past seventy, and the fierce anatomical energy of his Orvieto years has settled into calm. The color is rich, the striped wings and lapis and raspberry robes vivid, and the saints' faces individual, especially the furrowed kneeler. But the arrangement is stacked and symmetrical, the Child is stiff, and beside Raphael's fluid groupings of the same years it looks old-fashioned. A dignified late work from an artist outside the main current."},"met:687513":{"d":"A vertical panel, about 89 by 62.5 centimeters, painted in oil on walnut and showing the Lamentation over the dead Christ. The Virgin Mary, Mary Magdalene and Saint John the Evangelist are the recorded mourners. Luis de Morales, a Spanish painter active mainly in Extremadura, specialized in compact devotional pictures of this kind, finished with great care and meant to be studied at close range, where the tenderness of a gesture or the pallor of a face could be read slowly in private prayer.","i":"The Lamentation shows the grief over Christ's body after it is taken down from the cross, a moment the Gospels do not describe but that medieval devotion and Netherlandish painting developed to invite viewers to grieve alongside the mourners. In sixteenth-century Spain, where the Counter-Reformation stressed images that move the faithful to compassion, such pictures were prized. Mary Magdalene, who anointed Christ, and John, the beloved disciple, are the standard companions of the sorrowing Virgin. Morales earned the nickname El Divino largely through such subjects.","c":"Morales is remembered for small, intense devotional pictures: pale, ivory-smooth flesh, long tapering fingers and a tenderness bordering on the morbid, a Spanish blend of Flemish detail and Leonardesque softness. At his best the formula moves the viewer; at its weaker, repeated types and poses make the work seem produced to a pattern. A critic would value this panel for its concentration of feeling rather than invention: a fine example of his manner, not a departure from it."},"met:436787":{"d":"A view of the Pont Neuf, the Seine bridge at the western tip of the Île de la Cité in Paris, with the river and boats below it. It is an oil on canvas about 55 by 82 centimeters, dated 1849 to 1850, from the years when the Dutch-born Jongkind was working in the French capital. The recorded subjects are the bridge, the river and boats, so the picture belongs with the views of working Paris and its river that occupied him throughout his career.","i":"The Pont Neuf, begun in 1578 and opened in 1607, is despite its name the oldest standing bridge across the Seine in Paris. In the 1840s the river was still a working waterway of barges and laundry boats, and Baron Haussmann's rebuilding of the city, which would transform the quays, had not yet begun. Jongkind's river views record that older Paris, but they do so with the attention of a painter watching weather and light rather than the nostalgia of an antiquarian.","c":"Jongkind trained in The Hague, and these early Paris views rest on a Dutch training in sound construction and tonal control of sky and water. They do not yet have the dazzling, abbreviated touch that later made younger painters take notice. A critic would call this a promising and reliable early view: well built, quietly observed, and more orderly than inspired. Its interest is partly historical, as a record of a bridge and river that every Parisian knew."},"met:438381":{"d":"A broad view along the Seine in central Paris, painted in 1854 on a small canvas of about 44 by 66 centimeters, later mounted on wood. The river carries a barge, or péniche, and a crane stands on the bank. Beyond, the landmarks recorded in the picture are the Tuileries Garden, the Louvre, the Palais de la Légion d'Honneur and, far off, Notre-Dame, all under a blue sky. It is a topographical view in which famous monuments and the working river share the scene.","i":"The title gives the viewpoint, the Left Bank quay opposite the Tuileries. The picture records Paris just before Haussmann's rebuilding changed the city, and some buildings seen along this stretch did not survive the century: the Palais de la Légion d'Honneur was burned during the Commune of 1871 and rebuilt. Jongkind painted many river views like this, and they work both as portraits of a place and as studies of changing weather over water.","c":"Jongkind's skill here is compression: a wide panorama of landmarks and a working river fitted onto a canvas the size of a small window, under a sky that takes real notice of air and light. Monuments and working equipment share the view without either dominating, which gives it character. It is more topographical than the Impressionist pictures it prepared, and the care over detail keeps the touch a little tight, but it is a lucid, truthful piece of observation."},"nga:136018":{"d":"The horizon sits in the lower third, so a light blue sky filled with fluffy clouds takes up most of the canvas. At the left, tall trees line a canal; in the center a dirt path runs along the far bank, where a rider on a donkey looks back over a shoulder toward the viewer. White buildings stand in the distance at the right beyond a grassy field. The canal water repeats the sky and trees. The paint is thick and plainly brushed, especially in the clouds. This is a small canvas, about 34 by 47 centimeters, dated 1864.","i":"A towpath is the track beside a canal along which horses or donkeys hauled barges, so the title names the working life of the waterway, even though the only traveler seen is a single rider. Flat country, still water and large skies belong to the Dutch tradition in which Jongkind was trained, and he kept returning to them during his long career in France. His subject is ordinary on purpose: the interest lies in weather, reflection and light on a plain day.","c":"Small, quick and unfussy. The low horizon and heavy paint in the sky show where Jongkind's interest lay, and the reflections tie sky and ground together while the white buildings give a single bright accent. It makes no grand claim and has little structure to spare, but the freshness of observation is exactly what younger painters such as Monet admired in him. A slight picture whose value is its immediacy."},"met:436785":{"d":"A view of Honfleur, the old port on the south bank of the Seine estuary in Normandy, painted in 1865 in oil on canvas, about 52 by 82 centimeters. The recorded subject is ships, which places the picture among Jongkind's many harbor scenes, where hulls, masts and quays are set beneath a broad, changeable sky. The wide horizontal format suits an open view across water, the kind of scene that rewards a painter who works fast and watches the weather.","i":"Honfleur drew landscape painters through the middle of the nineteenth century. In the 1860s Jongkind worked there alongside Eugène Boudin and the young Claude Monet, who later said that Jongkind completed the education of his eye. The attraction was the estuary's light, the shifting weather and a harbor where sailing vessels were still at work. Seen this way, the picture is less a portrait of a town than a record of the conditions in which Impressionism would take shape.","c":"By the mid-1860s Jongkind had settled on a rapid, nervous notation of weather, and harbor scenes were its natural subject. The market valued him for atmosphere rather than monumental structure, which is both a strength and a limit, since his compositions are modest and rarely build to a climax. As a document of the Honfleur circle, though, a picture like this matters more than its size suggests."},"met:761570":{"d":"A man of about thirty, Carlo Rimbotti, is shown in a small portrait on a wood panel, about 53 by 42 cm, dated 1548. His name and lifespan (1518 to 1591) are given in the picture's title. Salviati, a Florentine who worked in Rome, Florence, Venice and briefly France, painted relatively few portraits, and a small panel like this was the kind of picture made for the sitter's own house, a record of a man in his prime.","i":"The mid-sixteenth-century portrait of the Florentine milieu, shaped by Bronzino and his circle, set the model: cool self-possession, elegant clothes, a controlled expression that conceals as much as it reveals. A sitter aged thirty in 1548 is placed within that tradition. Why Salviati, a friend of Vasari and a painter of great patrons, portrayed him is uncertain; such portraits cemented friendships and recorded social standing.","c":"Salviati is better known as a decorator of palace walls, and his easel portraits are few, which makes each one worth attention. They share the polish and reserve of Florentine Mannerism: a cool finish, a controlled outline, a sitter who withholds himself. The limitation of the type is that it flatters rank at the expense of personality, and judged by that standard this small panel is a refined, rather than a revealing, document."},"met:822083":{"d":"A portrait of the banker Bindo Altoviti, aged about fifty-four, painted around 1545 in oil on a slab of marble, a hard, smooth support that is rare in portraiture. The picture measures about 88 by 73 cm. Altoviti was a Florentine in Rome and a patron to many artists, and Salviati, a Florentine painter who spent much of his career in Rome, moved in the same world.","i":"Bindo Altoviti (1491–1557) was one of the leading bankers in papal Rome, and he was a patron of Raphael, who painted him as a young man, of Cellini, who cast his bronze bust, and of Vasari. A banker who has himself portrayed on marble makes a statement about permanence: oil on stone was fashionable in Rome, promoted by Sebastiano del Piombo, as a way of making painting last longer.","c":"A hard stone support invites a smooth, polished surface and discourages the soft blending possible on canvas or wood, and that suits the Mannerist taste for finish and Salviati's own controlled manner. The sitter was a cultivated man, and the portrait aims to match him with elegance and reserve. The price of the technique is a certain coolness: the picture is more admirable than warm."},"nga:41726":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a young woman with pale, faintly yellow-tinged skin and flushed cheeks looks out from the corners of her brown eyes, her body turned to the viewer's right. Short dark curls lie on her forehead, and the rest of her hair is covered by a gold net headdress fringed with pearls; a pearl-drop earring and a necklace of small black gems complete her jewelry. A brown garment lined with black-dotted white fur frames a raspberry-pink dress trimmed with gold. A thick gold chain with a jeweled medallion hangs between her fingers. Light falls from the right against a dark ground.","i":"The sitter's name is not known. Her pearls, gold chain, jeweled pendant and fur lining place her among the wealthy elite of mid-sixteenth-century Italy, and a portrait like this recorded rank, wealth and beauty at once. It may have been made to mark a betrothal or marriage, but nothing in the picture confirms that. The sidelong glance and closed lips follow the courtly ideal of reserve, in which a sitter shows composure rather than emotion.","c":"Salviati is better known for large decorative cycles in Rome, Florence and elsewhere, and this shows him as a refined portraitist in the cool, polished manner of Florentine court painting. The surface is the achievement: pearls, gold chain and black-flecked fur are rendered with relish and set against dark brown to push the pale face forward. What it lacks is life behind the face. The sitter is poised but remote, a beautiful mask more than a person."},"met:437598":{"d":"An unnamed man is the subject of this large portrait, oil on canvas, about 123 by 93 cm. The size allows the sitter to be shown well beyond head and shoulders, and dress, pose and setting are the means by which a man of the sixteenth-century Italian elite announced his standing. Salviati, a Florentine who worked in Rome, Florence and Venice, painted relatively few portraits beside his frescoes and altarpieces.","i":"Because the title records no name, the portrait speaks mainly through convention: clothes, bearing and pose signal rank, education and self-command, in the cool Florentine fashion set by Bronzino and others. Portraits like this were made to commemorate a marriage, an appointment or a friendship, and to pass the sitter's image to his family. The sitter may yet be identified; what remains is a type of Mannerist self-presentation.","c":"Salviati's portraits have the polish of Florentine Mannerism and the reserve to match: a controlled outline, a smooth finish, a sitter who keeps his own counsel. At this size the demands on the painter rise, since a large canvas needs a figure with presence and not only elegance. The risk is a distance that never quite becomes personality. It is a capable, cool image, more admirable than intimate."},"met:436867":{"d":"A small, close portrait, only about 25 by 23 centimeters, of a young peasant girl wearing a white headcloth that frames her face. Painted in oil on canvas in 1885, it is the kind of head study Wilhelm Leibl made of the rural people among whom he lived in Upper Bavaria, at a scale that suits close looking. The format leaves room for little beyond the face and the headcloth, so the picture rests entirely on the sitter's presence and on the painter's patient observation of her.","i":"Leibl admired Gustave Courbet, whom he met in 1869, and the exacting portraits of Hans Holbein, and in the 1870s he left Munich's studios to live among villagers in rural Bavaria. His peasants are not the cheerful or pitiable types of popular genre painting; they are individuals, observed without sentiment. A white headcloth was part of regional working dress, and it also gives the painter a clean, bright shape around the face.","c":"Leibl's gift was fidelity: economical, firm modeling of a plain face and a refusal of anecdote. What lifts such a head above a mere study is that the girl is not asked to smile or to illustrate anything. The small size concentrates attention. Beside his French contemporaries the palette can look somber and the handling dry, but few German painters of the 1880s looked so honestly at working people."},"cma:139728":{"d":"A cabinet-sized landscape, about 50 by 61 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1849. The title names a wood chopper, so a figure at work with an ax is part of the scene, set within a stretch of country. Inness painted it in his early twenties, in the manner of the Hudson River painters who shaped his early work: a finished surface, a recognizable piece of countryside, and a human figure to give scale and a sense of lived-in land.","i":"In American landscape painting of the 1840s and 1850s, woodcutters and tree stumps carried two meanings at once. They stood for honest labor settling a new country and, quietly, for the loss of the forest that painters from Thomas Cole onward had warned about. The wood chopper is therefore a more loaded figure than he looks, though a small picture like this likely treats the theme gently, offering a pleasant rural scene with a note of work in it.","c":"Early Inness pictures such as this are accomplished and careful but still tied to the Hudson River idiom he would later outgrow; the personal handling of light and air that defines his mature work has yet to appear. The pleasure lies in craft and in the plain human scale of the subject. It is of interest chiefly as a starting point, one that makes the distance to the moody, tonal landscapes of his last decades easy to measure."},"nga:195785":{"d":"A modest landscape, about 46 by 66 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas around 1852. It is cataloged simply as Landscape, with American scenery as its theme, and names no particular site or incident. The long horizontal format and the plain title suggest an exercise in arranging land, trees and sky rather than the record of a famous place. It dates from the years when Inness's European travels were widening his sense of what a landscape could be.","i":"Without a site or story to decode, the picture is best read as a statement of landscape taste at midcentury. Inness's training was in the precise, detailed manner of the Hudson River School, but in Europe he looked hard at old masters such as Claude Lorrain, whose framed, glowing space receding to a bright horizon was a model of ideal landscape. Which of the two voices dominates here is best judged at the picture itself.","c":"A critic has to judge this picture on arrangement alone, and in these years Inness's arrangements were still largely borrowed: the Hudson River School's precision on one side, the old masters' framing on the other. It is competent and agreeable, and it matters mostly for what came later, when Barbizon painting and his own spiritual convictions turned him toward mood and tone."},"nga:30776":{"d":"A viewer looks down across a valley. Jagged tree stumps stand closest, spaced across the width of the painting. A man in a yellow broad-brimmed hat, red vest and gray trousers reclines on one elbow beside a tall, slender tree with golden leaves. Beyond a green meadow dotted with stumps, a long steam locomotive trailing white smoke crosses a bridge over a wooded ravine, leaving a cluster of brick-red buildings dominated by a domed roundhouse. Chimneys smoke, a steeple suggests a town, and hazy mountains close the horizon beneath a sky that deepens from shell pink to pale blue.","i":"The painting was made for the Delaware, Lackawanna and Western Railroad and shows its roundhouse at Scranton, Pennsylvania. The stumps are the leftovers of land cleared for track, buildings and fuel, while the reclining man, a standard figure of pastoral calm, watches the new machine without alarm. The picture has been read both as a celebration of progress and as a quiet record of its cost, and it probably holds both: the locomotive seems to belong to the landscape rather than invade it.","c":"An early landmark that marries the old pastoral recipe, a reclining figure, a framing tree and a diagonal into depth, to a new industrial subject, and does it without fuss. The ravine's diagonal, the smoke and the graded sky are handled with real skill, and the stumps add bite. The foreground figure and tree are conventional, and the whole is more literal than the hazy, spiritual landscapes of Inness's later years. Still, few pictures show the railroad arriving so calmly."},"cma:109238":{"d":"A view of Durham, a small town in central Connecticut, painted in 1858 in oil on canvas, about 39 by 67 centimeters. The place name as title and the wide horizontal format point to a topographical landscape, a portrait of a particular stretch of country rather than an imagined scene. Inness painted it in the later 1850s, when he was moving from the crisp detail of the Hudson River School toward a softer, more unified treatment of light and air.","i":"Landscapes named for towns reflect the 1850s taste for pictures of specific, familiar places. Collectors bought American scenery not only for sublimity but for recognition and local pride. By then Inness had seen Barbizon painting in France, and its example, simpler masses and a scene held together by tone, had begun to alter his work, though a view like this still keeps a strong sense of the actual place.","c":"A critic would prize a picture like this less for drama than for restraint: its horizontal sweep lets land and sky do the work, and it shows Inness learning to simplify. It also marks the limits of his early-middle period: pleasant, well judged, and not yet the individual voice of the 1880s. Not a landmark, but a sound and unpretentious one, and a useful step in his development."},"met:11229":{"d":"A large landscape, about 91 by 128 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1861. It shows the Delaware River where it cuts through the ridge known as the Delaware Water Gap, on the Pennsylvania and New Jersey border; a rainbow, a river and cattle are among the recorded elements. The scale is that of an exhibition picture, and the combination of famous scenery, cattle and a rainbow gathers three of the favorite ingredients of American landscape painting into one view.","i":"By midcentury the Delaware Water Gap was a major tourist destination, reached by rail from New York and Philadelphia, and a standard subject for painters. Rainbows in the Hudson River tradition usually signified divine promise, the sign given after the Flood, and cattle suggested a settled, fruitful land. Painted in the year the Civil War began, the picture may have held added meaning for its first viewers as an image of peace, though that reading is a suggestion rather than a documented fact.","c":"By 1861 Inness could organize a big scenic view with confidence, and the luminous sky and the way the rainbow sets the mood show how well he understood the stage-management of landscape. The effect is slightly theatrical, a Hudson River spectacle with Barbizon softness, and the rainbow is the kind of device he would leave behind as his work turned toward subtler moods. A handsome, ambitious picture from a painter still choosing his path."},"met:11228":{"d":"A landscape of the Delaware River valley, painted in oil on canvas around 1863, about 56 by 77 centimeters. Hills are its recorded subject. At a little over half a meter high it is a cabinet picture for a home or a collector's wall, smaller than the exhibition canvases Inness made of the same region, and it belongs to the quieter, more domestic side of his work rather than to the scenic landmark views.","i":"The Delaware valley, within easy reach of New York and Philadelphia, had been a favorite sketching ground for American landscape painters since the 1840s. From about this time Inness was also absorbing the mystical writings of Emanuel Swedenborg, whose idea that the visible world mirrors a spiritual one would deepen his later landscapes. Whether this picture already carries such overtones is not documented, so it is safest read as a quiet topographical view.","c":"Compared with the showier Delaware Water Gap of a couple of years earlier, a picture whose recorded subject is simply hills points toward the quieter choice that marks Inness's best work: let land and light carry the mood. It is modest, and its modesty is part of its interest. Like many of his pictures of these years, it sits between the careful topography of his youth and the poetry of his maturity."},"cma:118116":{"d":"A landscape painted in 1864, oil on canvas, whose title names the season of the grain and hay harvest. Harvest was one of Inness's recurring subjects in the 1860s, and the picture belongs with the settled, farmed countryside he painted in those years, as opposed to the wild scenery of earlier American landscape painting. At about 56 by 77 centimeters it is a cabinet picture, made for a domestic setting rather than a public gallery wall.","i":"Harvest is an old image of the turning year and of labor rewarded, and in 1860s America it could speak of national plenty as well as of the farm. Painted in the last full year of the Civil War, such a picture may have been read as a sign of the abundance of the Northern states; Inness's Peace and Plenty of 1865, painted as the war ended, makes that link explicit. This earlier work is quieter but belongs to the same line of thought.","c":"Inness's harvest pictures mark the shift from the crisp Hudson River manner toward softer, more unified light, and they are among the most approachable of his pictures of this decade. At this stage the formulas of the pastoral are still visible, and the dissolved forms of the 1880s and 1890s are far off. Within those limits it is warm and well balanced, and a good sign of where he was heading."},"met:11232":{"d":"A very large landscape, about 2 by 2.85 meters, painted in oil on canvas in 1865. Trees are recorded among its elements, and the title names its theme: a countryside at peace and rich in produce. At this scale the picture was made for an exhibition hall, to be taken in from across a room before it is read in detail. It is an ambitious picture of Inness's middle career, with a message as well as a view.","i":"Painted in the year the Civil War ended, the title turned a rural scene into a national hope: after four years of fighting, a land at ease and fruitful. Northern viewers could read in it the promise of a restored Union without any soldier or battlefield being shown. Landscape painters of the period often used harvest and light to speak of providence and reconciliation. The picture also reflects Inness's growing interest in Swedenborg and his belief that landscape could carry moral and spiritual meaning.","c":"A large public statement, and Inness handles the scale with confidence. But allegory tends to flatten a picture: the message is general, the means familiar, and it lacks the surprise of his later, smaller and moodier landscapes. Its importance is as a document of the moment the war ended and as proof that Inness could meet the Hudson River standard of the ambitious exhibition picture whenever he chose."},"cma:109239":{"d":"A winter landscape built on the contrast between a glowing sunset and a harsh, bleak land. Warm pinks and yellows fill the sky, while the frozen ground below is cool blue. A pond is part of the scene. Inness first placed a figure standing at the pond's left edge but later painted it out, so the finished picture holds no human presence and leaves weather and light as its subject. It is an oil on canvas of 1866, about 56 by 77.5 centimeters.","i":"The picture is about effect, not incident. A warm sky over cold ground was a classic way of stirring feeling, and by removing the figure Inness took away the one narrative hook the scene had. His growing interest in the mystic Emanuel Swedenborg encouraged him to treat landscape as a mirror of inner states; a winter sunset can be read as austere and consoling at once, though the painting itself tells no story.","c":"The color contrast is the achievement: pink and yellow sky playing off cold blue ground, a sunset that glows without sentiment. Painting out the figure was a good gamble, since the picture is left with little to explain and nothing to distract from the light. It is smaller and plainer than Inness's big exhibition pieces, but its focus on atmosphere over anecdote looks forward to his best late work."},"cma:117783":{"d":"A small upright picture, about 30 by 22 centimeters, painted in oil on board in 1866. The title puts the viewer inside woodland, a subject Inness returned to throughout his career. A board support and modest scale are typical of quick, direct work from nature, though a small finished picture for a private buyer is also possible. Either way, the format leaves room for only a few broad masses of trunk, foliage and light, so the subject is likely the feel of being among trees.","i":"The woodland interior was a favorite theme of American landscape painters after Asher B. Durand, who urged artists in the 1850s to study trees directly from nature, trunk by trunk and leaf by leaf. For Inness forest light remained a central concern to the end of his life, and in 1891 he painted a far larger Sunset in the Woods. A small panel like this is best read as one step in that long engagement with the inside of the forest.","c":"The small woodland oil usually shows a painter's touch more freely than his exhibition pictures do, and Inness, whose handling was loosening in the 1860s, is well suited to it. This is a minor picture, and its size limits its ambition, but it gives a candid glimpse of how he thought about light and mass. Its charm is its directness."},"nga:125748":{"d":"A vast countryside is seen from an elevated point of view. In the foreground a wide brown and green field, apparently of wheat or a similar crop, is worked by small figures gathering it into piles. Brown and white cows graze on a green area behind the field, and a stand of tall dark trees rises in the middle distance. Rolling green hills with scattered white buildings lead to a tall, light blue mountain. Gray and white clouds fill most of the upper half, and a ray of light between them falls on the field below.","i":"The picture presents the harvest as an emblem of a settled, fruitful America, painted two years after the Civil War. The tiny laborers show work on a very large land, and the shaft of light breaking through the clouds is usually read in this tradition as a sign of providence. The Delaware valley, close to New York and Philadelphia, was a favorite painting ground, and Inness had known it for years.","c":"The strength is spatial: field, trees, cattle, hills and mountain are layered with real clarity, and half the canvas given to a lively sky keeps the scene from feeling like a map. The ray of light gives the picture its focus. The weakness is a degree of tidiness; the workers are small and the farm pleasant, and the mood stays on the comfortable side of grand. Inness would soon trade this clarity for softer, more private atmosphere."},"met:11226":{"d":"A horizontal landscape, about 76 by 116 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1869. Its title names the season and the open grassland, and trees and a river are recorded among its elements. The size and format are those of a substantial picture for a parlor or a collector's gallery, and the subject, a quiet stretch of ordinary country at the turning of the year, is typical of Inness's choices in the late 1860s, when he preferred modest views to grand scenery.","i":"Autumn had been claimed for American painting since Thomas Cole argued in the 1830s that the brilliance of the national autumn foliage gave the country's scenery a glory that Europe lacked. By Inness's time the season also carried a gentler meaning of ripeness and approaching rest, and a meadow beside a river suits that mood. The picture asks to be read less as a national statement than as a study in late-year calm.","c":"Autumn pictures tempt a painter toward loud color, and Inness's instinct in these years was to hold the palette in a related family of warm tones, so that the season glows rather than shouts. The picture belongs to the years when his touch moved from careful detail toward breadth of effect. It is a moderate, well-judged work, and it shows the road to the great tonal landscapes still unfinished."},"cma:109249":{"d":"A landscape painted in 1871 in oil on canvas, about 74 by 113 centimeters, whose title gives both place and weather: a storm moving in over the Alban Hills, the volcanic highland southeast of Rome. It dates from Inness's stay in Italy in the early 1870s, when Rome and the hill towns around it were the base for his work. A storm subject usually puts the sky at the center of the picture, and the long horizontal format suits the sweep of weather over open country.","i":"The Alban Hills, with their crater lakes, villas and ruins, had been staple landscape ground for visiting painters since Claude Lorrain and Poussin in the seventeenth century, and a nineteenth-century American working there joined a long line. A threatening sky over an Italian view also fits the Romantic taste for weather as drama. Inness's Italian years, from about 1870 to 1874, are often seen as the point where he blended the American landscape tradition with a more European sense of atmosphere.","c":"A storm picture lives or dies by its sky, and the subject suited Inness's growing gift for atmosphere beyond his early precision. It offers the chance to set dark masses against a lit patch of land, and the Alban Hills supplied a ready stage. The risk is melodrama. Among his Italian pictures this is one of the more theatrical, and many viewers will find his calmer evenings more convincing."},"nga:46426":{"d":"From an elevated viewpoint the eye travels over a green landscape toward a distant lake whose light blue water meets a pale yellow sky. The sun casts a golden glow across the land. In front, a shore covered in overlapping greenery and several round trees leads to a small hill at the right, topped by a small gray structure. At the bottom, two indistinct brown animals graze on a dirt mound, and another gray building is tucked behind bushes and trees. Golden clouds drift across the sky.","i":"Lake Albano fills a volcanic crater in the Alban Hills southeast of Rome, near Castel Gandolfo, and was a favorite subject for painters from Claude's day onward. Inness worked in the area during his Italian years, in the early 1870s. The grazing animals and modest buildings are the traditional furniture of the pastoral, signaling a timeless, tranquil country, while the title's sunset is the real subject: golden light that dissolves detail and unifies the view.","c":"A good example of how Inness's handling softened in Italy: the pale water melts into the sky and a single golden glow binds the view together, so that it reads as atmosphere rather than description. The weaker side is sweetness. Golden clouds, grazing animals and a hilltop building come close to a formula inherited from Claude Lorrain, and the picture keeps just inside the line of the picturesque. The unity of light is its real success."},"nga:53813":{"d":"The picture looks out from a hill over a valley, with a pale river winding through a hazy distance of light blue, pale pink, yellow and green. The almost cloudless sky is light blue with faint pink near the horizon. On the hill at the right stand large green trees and fields in earthy greens and browns, and two small figures walk through them, one on horseback in a red dress and white shawl, the other behind the horse in darker clothes. A large canvas, about 98 by 161.5 centimeters.","i":"Perugia stands on a hilltop in Umbria, and the Tiber runs through the valley below it, so the title places the view in the heart of central Italy, a region of hill towns that drew foreign painters in the nineteenth century. The tiny riders and walkers supply scale and a note of rural life. The picture's real subject is the soft, hazy light of the valley, painted during Inness's years in Italy.","c":"This is Inness learning to paint air. The distant blue, pink, yellow and green melt without hard edges, and an almost cloudless sky is a bold choice for a large canvas, since there is no weather to lean on. The foreground, with its trees and tiny figures, is more conventional, a nod to the picturesque, but the distance is a convincing, quietly adventurous piece of observation."},"met:11230":{"d":"A large horizontal landscape, about 96 by 160 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1875. The title fixes a time of day and a Massachusetts town; trees and cattle are recorded among its elements, the usual furniture of a pastoral evening. At this size and format it is an exhibition-scale picture, painted soon after Inness came home from four years in Italy, and it turns his attention from Italian hill country to the farmland of New England.","i":"Medfield, a town southwest of Boston, was a place Inness knew from the early 1860s, when he lived there, so the picture is partly a return to familiar ground. Evening, with cattle coming home, is a time-honored image of rest at the end of work; for a painter drawn to Swedenborg's view of nature as spiritual correspondence, such a quiet hour held more than scenery. The title keeps the claim modest.","c":"By 1875 Inness had absorbed the soft focus and glowing evening light he met in Italy, and a New England pasture at dusk is a fitting place to use them. The subject is a stock pastoral theme, and a critic could call it comfortable; but it shows him bringing European atmosphere home without losing his sense of American place, and it points toward the tonal landscapes of his last decade."},"met:11233":{"d":"A monumental landscape, about 2 by 3 meters, painted in oil on canvas in 1876. Trees are the recorded subject, and the title names a pine grove at the Villa Barberini in the Alban Hills near Rome, where tall pines gave Inness a subject on an almost architectural scale. The huge format announces a painting meant to hang in a major exhibition and to be admired for its ambition as well as its beauty.","i":"Pine groves on Roman villa estates were a classic subject for foreign painters, and the umbrella pine became a kind of emblem of Italy. A picture dated 1876 comes a couple of years after Inness left Italy, which suggests it was developed from earlier studies and memory, as large canvases often were. The grove also gave him what he valued most in a landscape: large, simple masses and light moving through air.","c":"At this scale the risk is emptiness, and the achievement of a grove picture lies in making trunks, shade and light hold a big canvas together. The subject, tall trees against sky, suited Inness's gift for large simple masses and glowing air. It is among his grandest Italian pictures, though perhaps more impressive in size than moving in feeling, and the later, smaller tonal works distill the same lessons with more intimacy."},"nga:178145":{"d":"A shepherdess, seen from behind, tends a flock of sheep on a tree-lined dirt path. She wears a blue skirt, a white shirt and a white bonnet covering her hair. To the right a green, grassy hillside with trees and grazing cattle rises, and in the background on the left, green fields and white cliffs lie beneath a cloudy sky. It is a rural scene of modest size, about 65 by 97.5 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1876.","i":"Étretat, on the Normandy coast, is famous for its white chalk cliffs, painted by Courbet and later by Monet. Here the cliffs that made the place famous are kept to the background, and the pastoral hillside takes the foreground. A shepherdess with her flock is an old rural motif, popular in French painting in the generation of Millet, and it signals traditional country life rather than a seaside resort.","c":"A painter who comes to a famous coast and turns his back on its famous view has made a choice, and Inness's choice was a shepherdess and a hillside. That is both the charm and the limit of the picture: the figure is well placed along the path, and the soft greens and whites are fresh, but the subject belongs to the French rural genre of Millet and Charles Jacque more than to Inness's own gift for broad atmosphere."},"cma:149422":{"d":"A landscape of a wheat field, painted in oil on canvas about 1875 to 1877, about 51 by 76 centimeters. The title names the crop, not any event, so the field itself, its color and the sky above it, is the subject. Grain fields recur in Inness's work from the 1860s onward, and the subject allows a broad band of warm color to anchor a quiet composition without any story to tell.","i":"Wheat is an old image of the year's turning and of work rewarded, and Inness had used grain fields as national symbols in the 1860s. By the later 1870s the symbolism had grown quieter: a field of grain was a way of painting light, color and air at the plainest possible scale. The change reflects his conviction that a landscape's meaning lay in feeling more than in story.","c":"Without a story, a wheat field depends on two things: the weight of its color and the air above it. Inness's eye for both was dependable by the late 1870s. The risk is monotony, one crop under one sky; the reward is the calm of an unforced view. The picture shows how far he had moved from the encyclopedic detail of the 1850s toward the broad, felt effects that made his reputation."},"met:11227":{"d":"A landscape of oak trees in autumn, painted in oil on canvas about 1878, about 54 by 76.5 centimeters. Cattle are recorded among its figures, so the scene is probably a pasture with its livestock, and the oaks and their autumn color give the picture its title. The size is a comfortable cabinet format for a private home, and the subject is the kind of settled farm country that Inness painted repeatedly in these years.","i":"Oaks tend to turn russet and brown, and to hold their leaves late into the season, a color range that suited Inness's tonal approach. The oak has long stood for strength and endurance in European and American art, and cattle beneath it add the pastoral note of peace and plenty. A viewer in the 1870s would have taken such a picture as a calm, reassuring image of the American countryside.","c":"Cattle under big trees can be a stock arrangement, and a picture like this depends on how well mass and light are managed. Inness's instinct in the late 1870s was for strong simple forms softened by atmosphere. It falls squarely within his mature manner: attractive, reliable and unforced, though more comfortable than daring. It is the kind of picture that made his reputation with collectors."},"cma:122644":{"d":"A small, wide picture, about 25 by 51 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1881. The title makes the sky the subject: fleecy clouds, the soft rounded cumulus that drift in fair weather. A format exactly twice as wide as it is high gives a sweep of land and sky, and the small scale encourages quick, direct handling, which suits a painter chasing the look of moving clouds.","i":"Constable's cloud studies of the 1820s had made the sky a subject in its own right, and the Barbizon painters Inness admired followed that lead. By 1881 Inness was ready to make a picture mostly about air and light, in line with his own view, published in 1878, that a painter should convey the impression a scene makes rather than list its parts. A sky is the purest test of that aim.","c":"A small sky picture is a test of touch: the soft edges and quick modulation of a cloud cannot be faked. Inness, by 1881 at ease with feathery handling, is on firm ground, and the two-to-one format sets a calm horizontal. As a minor, personal work it lacks the weight of his large exhibition pictures, but its freshness is part of what later painters admired in him."},"cma:133298":{"d":"A small landscape, about 33 by 47 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas around 1878 and named for Montclair, the New Jersey town where Inness made his home from the late 1870s. A picture of this size, named for the painter's own neighborhood, is likely a personal view of familiar ground rather than a commission or an exhibition piece, and it gives the viewer a glimpse of the countryside that the artist saw each day.","i":"Inness's move to Montclair began the last and most celebrated phase of his career. Painting the country around his own house, not distant or famous sites, suited his belief that a landscape's value lay in the impression it made on the mind. The date places the picture at the start of that phase, before the full soft, glowing manner of the 1880s had developed.","c":"Small views of a painter's own surroundings let him work without the demands of exhibition, and they are often the freshest part of an output. A picture like this should be judged less as a statement than as a record of a place and a moment of looking. Inness's best-known work came in the decade that followed, so this is a useful early marker rather than an arrival."},"met:11235":{"d":"A landscape of 1887, oil on canvas, about 76 by 115 centimeters, whose title names the first light of day. Trees are recorded among its elements. By this date Inness was in his sixties and living in Montclair, New Jersey, and the picture comes from the period of the soft, glowing tonal landscapes for which he is best known: scenes in which color and form are held in a haze of light, and the time of day is the true subject.","i":"Dawn and dusk were the hours Inness preferred, when light dissolves detail and one tone seems to spread over the whole scene. He held that a landscape's purpose was to convey an impression, and he followed Swedenborg's idea that nature mirrors spiritual states; a sunrise, a daily renewal, is a natural subject for that belief. His landscapes do not spell such meaning out. They invite the viewer to feel as much as to look.","c":"This is the Inness whom the twentieth century admired most: color melted into a soft, even glow, forms suggested rather than stated, and a time of day treated as a state of mind. The risk of the manner is murk, and some late canvases do turn muddy; a sunrise is a subject that rewards keeping the light luminous. The date places it in the artist's most characteristic decade."},"cma:110952":{"d":"A landscape of 1888, oil on canvas, about 56 by 70 centimeters, cataloged under the plain title Landscape and naming no site. The date places it in the last decade of Inness's life, the period of his soft, atmospheric late manner, and the lack of a place name fits it: the picture is offered as a mood rather than a map reference. The compact horizontal format is a quiet, domestic one, suited to a private room.","i":"A title as plain as Landscape asks the viewer to supply the place. For Inness in the late 1880s that was a feature: he argued that a painter should render the impression a scene makes on the mind, not an exact survey, and a generic title lets the mood stand alone. Seen in that light, the picture is less about a locality than about air, tone and the feeling of a quiet hour.","c":"Late Inness is where the painter found his truest voice, and a plain Landscape of 1888 belongs to that period. The aims are soft transitions, a unified tone and a mood of reverie, and the danger is vagueness; the viewer should look for the structure under the haze, the placing of masses and the direction of light. It stands among the artist's quieter late works, characteristic and unassuming."},"met:11231":{"d":"A very small landscape, about 23 by 31 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas. The museum gives its date as either 1884 or 1889, so it can be placed only in the later part of Inness's career, when his work was at its most atmospheric. Beyond the plain title, no site or incident is named, which leaves a modest, unassuming picture whose subject is simply a piece of country seen in a particular light.","i":"Small works like this show that Inness's method depended on mood, not size: a single tonal idea, a handful of broad masses and a unifying light could be enough. In the 1880s his reputation was at its height, and collectors prized even his modest canvases for exactly that quality. A picture without a title to steer the viewer asks to be taken as pure effect.","c":"Small canvases test a painter's economy, and a landscape this size lives on a few decisions: where the horizon sits, how the light falls, how softly the edges are kept. Inness, with his late gift for suggesting much with little, is at home here. The picture is slight in ambition, and it is best enjoyed as one enjoys a good sketch: for the confidence of its touch and its freedom from convention."},"met:11234":{"d":"A spring landscape of about 1891, about 74 by 115 centimeters, named for Montclair, New Jersey, where Inness lived. Blossoming trees are its subject, as the title and the recorded subjects indicate. The medium line is unusual: oil on canvas with crayon or charcoal, so that drawn marks as well as brushwork contribute to the surface. The wide format gives the trees room to spread across the picture in the soft, luminous manner of Inness's last years.","i":"Flowering trees are an old image of renewal, and for Inness, who read nature as a language of spiritual life after Swedenborg, spring carried more than seasonal charm. Blossoming orchards were also a favorite subject of the French Impressionists, but Inness's late version of such a scene trades bright, broken touches for a soft, unified veil of tone, so that the renewal is felt as a mood rather than described as a fact.","c":"Blossom tempts a painter toward sweetness, and Inness's late manner, which dissolves detail into a soft, even light, is well suited to avoid fussiness. The drawn marks in the medium suggest he was still experimenting in his sixties. It is a representative late work: delicate in mood, soft in its edges and a little hazy in structure, pleasant rather than profound."},"nga:166496":{"d":"A shaft of warm light from the left pierces a dark wood of tall trees. It falls on a stout white trunk, patched with olive green and brown, and on a stretch of moss-green forest floor near the center. The other trees carry high canopies of burnt-orange leaves that run off the top and side edges, and the ground is carpeted in warm browns with touches of scarlet and pine green. Beyond the trunk a gray and brown boulder, with a slash of rust orange, lies among the trees, and a lighter emerald glow shows in the distance.","i":"The picture dates from Inness's last years, when his late style had reached a rich, glowing depth in which saturated color is held by soft edges. A shaft of light on a pale trunk in a dark interior suggests an almost sacred quiet, and for a follower of Swedenborg light in darkness pointed toward the divine. Inness rarely spelled out such meanings, but his belief that nature mirrors spiritual order makes the reading plausible.","c":"One of Inness's most appealing late woodland interiors: the burnt-orange canopy and the lone shaft of light on the white trunk give it drama without melodrama, and a unified warm tone holds the saturated colors together. The scale, about 1.2 by 1.8 meters, gives the forest an enveloping presence. If there is a weakness, it is the insistently rich palette, which can tip toward the decorative, but it shows how late Inness could make a handful of colors feel large."},"nga:178273":{"d":"A dark green field stretches into the distance under a light blue sky partly covered by gray and beige clouds. Near the center, a line of trees and a few small, indistinct buildings stand along the horizon; one on the left sends up a plume of smoke from a small chimney, and in front of it a figure in a dress is barely distinguishable. The colors are earthy, deep green shading into soft browns and blues. At the center a yellow full moon sits just above the horizon, glowing warmly. Oil on canvas, 1891, about 76 by 113 centimeters.","i":"The harvest moon is the full moon nearest the autumn equinox, bright enough to let farmers work on into the evening, so the title joins season, light and labor in one phrase. The smoking chimney and the lone figure add a human note of home and rest to a vast, quiet field. Painted three years before Inness's death, the picture belongs to the twilight scenes in which he found a natural image of peace.","c":"In late Inness the moon is not a spectacle but a source of unifying warmth. The yellow disk just above the horizon ties the deep green field, the earthy distance and the cloudy sky into one tone, and the indistinct buildings and figure have the vagueness he valued. The picture reads as a moment of feeling rather than a description. It is almost too hushed, and the dark field can feel heavy, but it is eloquent in its restraint."},"cma:134072":{"d":"A landscape of 1892 in oil on canvas, about 81 by 106 centimeters, painted two years before Inness's death. Forms are blurred and the effects come from translucent colors, so that the physical landscape seems to turn into something close to an apparition. The title promises a sunny autumn day, but the picture offers atmosphere rather than detail, and the soft, glowing treatment is its whole point.","i":"Inness was highly prolific, making more than a thousand works in a career of about fifty years, and the pictures of his last years are his most openly spiritual. Following Swedenborg, he believed that the visible world corresponds to an unseen spiritual one, and he sought to make landscape hint at that presence. The Cleveland Museum records that he died in 1894 watching a sunset, exclaiming how beautiful it was, a story that suits the pictures.","c":"The Cleveland Museum treats this as typical of the late style, and it is a good place to meet both its qualities and its risks. The glow comes from translucent color rather than surface sparkle, which gives the light an inner source. The danger is vagueness, and a critic would ask whether enough structure survives the haze to hold the picture together. For many viewers this blurred, devotional Inness is the one who matters most."},"met:438654":{"d":"Monks gather in a crypt around a corpse in this oil on canvas, about 73 by 91 centimeters. A crypt, monks and a dead body are the elements, and the title announces drama: this is a Gothic-Romantic subject in which a vaulted tomb and figures in religious habits are chosen for their atmosphere of mystery and death. No date is given for the picture, though the artist died in 1850.","i":"Scenes of monks, crypts and tombs belonged to the Gothic taste of the early nineteenth century, fed by novels like Ann Radcliffe's and Matthew Lewis's The Monk and by the French Troubadour painters, who staged medieval and monastic scenes. Alexandre-Évariste Fragonard, son of the famous Jean-Honoré, painted costume dramas from history. The subject says less about faith than about the thrill of mortality.","c":"A theatrical little drama in which light and darkness carry the effect, and Fragonard's skill is in the staging. Such pictures are easy to dismiss as melodrama, and the narrative is not clear from the title alone, but this one is typical of the Romantic era's relish for death and ruin. It lacks the verve of his father's brush, though it shows that the son inherited an instinct for performance."},"nga:42930":{"d":"A dim street where several people in long dark skirts and aprons carry large sacks filled with white cloth. They are bent over under the weight as they cross a cobblestone road in front of a building whose lit interior shows blue wallpaper. At the right, another street with more buildings leads to a carriage, where people appear to pass their sacks up to a person on top. Oil on canvas, about 83 by 68 centimeters, dated 1899.","i":"Steinlen, Swiss by birth, lived in Montmartre in Paris and recorded the working poor in drawings, prints and paintings, with sympathy and without sentimentality. Laundresses, who carried heavy bundles of washing between customers and the wash-houses, were a staple of Parisian realist art from Daumier to Degas. Here the bent bodies stress a trade of hard physical labor, and the lit window may set the comfort of the households they serve against the effort that supplies it.","c":"Steinlen's strength is the telling silhouette: the bent backs and swollen sacks tell the story at once, and the dim palette keeps pathos from turning into spectacle. He is better known as a draftsman, illustrator and poster artist, and the canvas has less bite than his prints, but it is a sober, sympathetic picture of labor that avoids both caricature and sermon."},"nga:372":{"d":"A vertical canvas, about 150 by 103 centimeters, painted in oil around the 1580s. The title names Susanna, heroine of the story in the Book of Daniel in which a virtuous young wife is watched at her bath by two elders. Domenico Tintoretto, son and chief assistant of the great Jacopo Tintoretto, painted it in a manner shaped by his father's workshop. Venetian painters usually showed Susanna as a nude or half-dressed bather in a garden, which made the subject popular with private collectors as well as devout patrons.","i":"The story comes from the Greek additions to the Book of Daniel. Two elders who desire Susanna threaten to accuse her of adultery unless she yields; she refuses and is condemned, until the young Daniel cross-examines the elders and exposes their lie. For painters it offered both a moral example of chastity under pressure and an acceptable reason to paint a female nude. Many buyers likely valued the picture for its beauty as much as for its lesson.","c":"Domenico Tintoretto worked in the long shadow of his father, and his pictures tend to repeat Jacopo's inventions with a smoother, tamer finish. This Susanna invites comparison with Jacopo's celebrated versions, and it loses: the drive of the brushwork and the shimmering, nervous color are missing. It is a capable piece of Venetian workshop production around the 1580s, competent in drawing and pleasing in effect, but derivative rather than inventive."},"met:438543":{"d":"A still life of the late 1620s, oil on canvas, about 47 by 59 centimeters, in which seashells and a box made of thin strips of wood, known as a chip-wood box, are arranged for display. The modest size suits a picture made for a collector's room. Such a picture sets natural forms, with their spirals and ridges, against a plain man-made object, and invites close, unhurried looking at shape and texture rather than any story.","i":"In the seventeenth century shells were prized as curiosities of nature and as signs of far-flung trade, and they filled the cabinets of collectors and the pictures of still-life painters in Germany, France and the Netherlands. A shell can also carry a moral note, since it is a beautiful but empty house, a reminder of life's brevity. Stoskopff, born in Strasbourg and active in Paris and Germany, was among the most original still-life painters of his generation.","c":"Stoskopff is admired for clear-eyed, cool observation that sets him apart from the lush abundance of Dutch and Flemish still life. His arrangements are carefully weighed and quietly luminous. A small shell piece like this shows those qualities in miniature: it relies on shape and placement, not spectacle. It may lack the drama of his glassware and vanitas pictures, but it has stillness and dignity, and it rewards the patience it asks."},"met:437322":{"d":"A portrait of a woman in strict profile, painted in tempera on a wooden panel about 49 by 35 centimeters, around 1480. The pure side view, with the head turned fully to the side, was the standard format for portraits of Florentine women in the second half of the fifteenth century, borrowed from ancient coins and medals. The sitter's name is not known. The painter, Piero del Pollaiuolo, belonged to a Florentine family workshop famed for goldsmith's work, sculpture and painting.","i":"In Florence, profile portraits of young women often marked a marriage or betrothal, and the costume and jewels on display proclaimed the family's wealth as much as the bride's beauty. The profile suited that purpose, since it presents the sitter as an ideal of civic femininity: a clear contour of brow, nose and neck, with her gaze turned away from the viewer. The Pollaiuolo workshop's skill in goldsmithing and anatomy explains the precise, jewel-like line such portraits needed.","c":"In a profile, expression is carried almost wholly by line, and the Pollaiuolo shop was famed for line. A critic would praise the clear contour and cool, finished surface, and note that the format leaves little room for psychology: the sitter is presented as an ideal of Florentine womanhood rather than as a personality. Piero is usually ranked below his brother Antonio in energy; this is an elegant, slightly distant picture, admirable more than warm."},"nga:111627":{"d":"On a wooden ledge against a dark brown ground, a shallow lavender-gray dish with a scalloped edge holds about two dozen figs, some deep purple, some pale green. Behind them to the left sits a round, golden-brown loaf with a split top, and a knife handle juts toward the viewer from the lower left. A wide-bellied black bottle, corked and glossy with window reflections, stands beside a small wooden barrel, and at the back right the neck of a copper vessel rises from pale shavings of ice in a keg-like bucket.","i":"Luis Meléndez worked in the Spanish bodegón tradition, which gives humble food the dignity of a major subject. Bread, figs and a bottle make a plain meal, and the ice that chills the copper vessel is a refreshment in a hot climate, the kind of detail that makes the scene feel lived in. The knife handle thrust toward the viewer is an old device for breaking the barrier between picture and room. Meléndez rarely adds a moral; the pleasure is in the things themselves.","c":"Meléndez is regarded as the finest Spanish still-life painter of the eighteenth century, and this picture shows why. Textures are distinguished with great exactness, from the crust of the loaf to the gloss of the bottle, where the window reflections show real optical observation, and the dark ground gives every object presence. The view is cool and unsentimental and can seem remote. Few still lifes of the period give such weight to ordinary things."},"met:437053":{"d":"A picnic is set out on a stone ledge in open country, with a tree, blue hills and a cloudy sky glimpsed at the left. At the centre, bunches of white and purple-black grapes spill over a basket. In front lie a melon cut open to show its seeds, a wedge of melon, three crusty bread rolls and a few dried figs. On the left are apricots on a white plate, quinces and pears, a bowl under a pewter lid, and a copper pitcher with a lid. At the right a wicker basket spills a crumpled cloth and plates, with a wooden tub behind. Warm light picks out the textures against dark foliage. The canvas is large, about 1 by 1.5 metres.","i":"Meléndez was the leading Spanish still-life painter of the eighteenth century, and this canvas belongs to the largest commission of his career: forty-four still lifes for the royal palace at Aranjuez, outside Madrid. Many of his still lifes show fruit and kitchen vessels against plain backgrounds. This one is exceptional for its grand size and its landscape, which turns a kitchen subject into a picnic, the merienda, of the kind courtiers enjoyed on excursions away from Madrid's summer heat. Local fruit, bread and humble copper and earthenware are treated with the care usually given to nobler subjects, which the Met reads as a deliberate ennobling of Spanish culture.","c":"Meléndez has no rival for sheer tactile truth: the dusty bloom on the grapes, the bread's cracked crust and the grain of the melon rind are each distinct. The composition is less sure. The objects are packed shoulder to shoulder, the eye is led nowhere in particular, and the landscape at the left feels pasted on, a stage backdrop. Compared with the austere, spotlit still lifes of Sánchez Cotán, this is more generous but less intense. Its pleasure is abundance, not drama."},"cma:151555":{"d":"A small horizontal still life, about 34 by 48 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas around 1772. The title lists its ingredients: fish, bread and a kettle, the raw materials and tools of a simple Spanish kitchen. Meléndez usually grouped such objects close together in front of a dark ground, so that each surface, whether scale, crust or metal, catches the light on its own terms. The picture belongs to the Spanish bodegón tradition of humble food treated with seriousness.","i":"Meléndez began with portraits and portrait miniatures in his father's studio and from the 1760s specialized in still life. Active mainly in Madrid, he is regarded as the finest Spanish still-life painter of his century, but he spent much of his life in poverty, as the Cleveland Museum notes. His simple kitchens, with humble ingredients and no human figures, found little reward in his lifetime but have since become some of the most admired images of eighteenth-century Spain.","c":"Meléndez's reputation rests on plain, truthful rendering of ordinary things, and fish, bread and a kettle are exactly the materials to show it: each surface is given its own weight and sheen. The method is the opposite of showy, and his pictures can feel austere; their charm is honesty. A critic would rate this a characteristic and persuasive example, modest in scale but sure in its effects, rather than one of his most elaborate pantry compositions."},"met:437640":{"d":"A large portrait, about 1.6 meters high and 1.4 wide, of the Prussian naturalist and explorer Alexander von Humboldt, painted in oil on canvas in 1859, the year he died at nearly ninety. The title gives his rank and dates, and the scale and format are those of a formal commemorative portrait of a national figure rather than a casual likeness. The painter, Julius Schrader, was a German portraitist.","i":"Humboldt was the most famous scientist of his age. His five-year expedition to Spanish America, from 1799 to 1804, and his multivolume Cosmos, which sought to describe the unity of nature, made him a hero to scientists, artists and the public, including American landscape painters such as Frederic Church, who followed his routes in South America. A portrait of 1859 records him at the end of his life, as a world figure and a sage of the age.","c":"Schrader is best remembered for his likeness of Humboldt, and the portrait explains why: it is a dignified, carefully observed image of a great old man, in the sober academic manner of mid-century Germany. What it offers is authority and honest observation of age. What it does not offer is surprise or probing psychology; it is a competent, official portrait whose interest lies in its sitter rather than its handling."},"nga:154233":{"d":"A bare-chested young woman, Europa, sits sideways on a large white bull as it plunges into a blue sea. A winged, smiling putto wades ahead, pulling the bull by a garland of leaves and flowers, and flower rings circle the bull's horns. Europa clutches a horn and gestures toward three women at the lower right, who sit and stand near a tree, mouths open, reaching out toward her. Rocky shadow fills the foreground, trees rise at the right, and puffy clouds tower in a topaz sky above a pale sun on the horizon.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses. Jupiter, in love with the Phoenician princess Europa, took the form of a gentle white bull, joined her as she played with her companions on the shore, and carried her across the sea to Crete; the continent is named after her. The garland-pulling putto, a servant of love, helps to make the abduction look like a gallant game, which was how eighteenth-century French taste preferred to see such stories.","c":"De Troy, then in his thirties, tells the story clearly and decoratively: the colors, bronze orange, royal blue, lavender gray and mint, are chosen to please, and the figures are placed with an eye for graceful gesture. What is missing is danger. The smiling putto and the flowers turn a kidnapping into an outing, and the women's alarm reads as stage business. It is a charming decoration, skilled and slightly empty."},"cma:147479":{"d":"A mythological canvas, about 1.06 by 1.39 meters, painted in oil in 1720, of the god Pan and the nymph Syrinx, a story told in Ovid's Metamorphoses. In the poem Syrinx flees the god's advances, is trapped at the edge of a river, and begs for rescue, whereupon the gods transform her into reeds, which Pan later gathers to make the pipes forever associated with him. The Cleveland Museum singles out the painter's rich colors and voluptuous figure types, suited to the love-themed subjects favored by elite patrons.","i":"Ovid's tale is about desire and loss: the pursued nymph escapes only by ceasing to be human, and the god's pursuit ends in music, since Pan makes the reeds into the pan pipes. For painters of the early eighteenth century such stories were ideal pretexts for pursuit, graceful nudes and pastoral settings. Collectors enjoyed them as erotic yet learned, with the classical source lending respectability.","c":"The Cleveland Museum names rich colors and voluptuous figure types as de Troy's gifts, and a critic would agree: he was a fluent decorator of love stories for the aristocratic salon. What the type rarely supplies is the pathos Ovid builds into the tale, in which transformation means loss, and such pictures can read as entertainment more than myth. It is polished, pleasing and light."},"met:438126":{"d":"An elegant interior with a man and a woman, painted in oil on canvas in 1724, about 65 by 54 centimeters. The title points to a garter as the focus of their encounter, a flirtatious gesture of intimacy in the manner of fashionable society scenes of the day. The figures wear the clothes of Parisian high society, and the room is arranged as a stage for the exchange between them. The small, upright format suits a cabinet picture for a private collector's room.","i":"De Troy was one of the leading painters in 1720s Paris of the tableau de mode, a scene of fashionable society that mixes light erotic suggestion with exact costume and furnishing. The garter was a traditional token of intimacy, and Dutch genre painters such as Gerard ter Borch and Gabriel Metsu had already shown how a modest domestic incident could carry a suggestive undercurrent. Parisian collectors enjoyed that playful risk, and enjoyed seeing their own manners and rooms on the canvas.","c":"Light, polished and knowing. De Troy's tableaux are more sophisticated in costume and setting than in psychology, and his figures play at feeling more than they feel. Where Watteau lets melancholy show beneath the elegance, de Troy offers wit, glossy fabric and gentle flirtation. A critic would value it as a lively record of manners, accomplished in its textures, and would put it below the best of Watteau or Chardin for depth."},"met:438127":{"d":"A fashionable couple in an interior with a dog, painted in oil on canvas about 1724, about 65 by 53 centimeters. The title names the moment as a declaration of love, a theatrical situation in which a man presses his suit and a woman must respond. The dog is recorded among the figures, adding a domestic note. Nearly identical in size and date to The Garter, the picture appears to be a companion to it, as if two scenes from a single flirtation were hung side by side.","i":"A declaration of love was the classic opening move of a gallant courtship, and de Troy treats it with the poise of the stage, where conversation was an art. The dog is a double-edged emblem: in Western art it often stands for fidelity, but it was also a fashionable lap pet, so it may simply mark the comfort of the room. If the two pictures were conceived as a pair, they suggest the stages of a courtship.","c":"De Troy handles the declaration as theater: crisp narrative, tidy props and body language doing the work. He is at his best in the rustle of satins and the sheen of wood and lacquer, and at his weakest in feeling, since the lovers seem to be performing love rather than feeling it. The result is delightful but shallow, and a representative sample of the Rococo salon's taste for amorous comedy."},"met:437845":{"d":"A wide, horizontal picture, about 86 by 150 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas about 1736. It shows the biblical Triumph of Mordecai, in which Mordecai, honored by the Persian king, is paraded on the royal horse through the city of Susa while his enemy, the proud minister Haman, is forced to lead the horse. Mordecai, Haman, a horse and Susa are recorded in the picture. The processional format suits a story of public humiliation turned into honor, with onlookers.","i":"The story is told in chapter 6 of the Book of Esther. Haman, who has plotted to destroy the Jews of Persia, is ordered by the king to honor the very man he hates, a reversal that turns the tale and is celebrated at the Jewish feast of Purim. Christian tradition sometimes read Esther as a type of the Virgin as intercessor, and the humbling of Haman as the downfall of pride.","c":"De Troy's biblical and historical scenes rely on the rich colors and orchestrated crowds of the French academic tradition, and he is more convincing as a storyteller of public ceremony than as a painter of spiritual feeling. The wide format gives the procession rhythmic movement, and Haman's forced service is the dramatic hinge. The style is lively, elegant and a little decorative: Rococo history painting as theater."},"met:437178":{"d":"A Dutch domestic scene of 1675, oil on canvas, about 64 by 80 centimeters. The recorded subjects are a woman, a child, an infant and a bedroom, which with the title points to a lying-in room, where a mother recovers after childbirth and the newborn is the center of attention. The picture belongs to the Dutch tradition of intimate genre, in which a private interior is painted with fine detail and a clear but quiet story.","i":"In the Dutch Republic a birth was a household event with its own customs: the mother kept to her bed for days, helped by a midwife and by women relatives and neighbors who visited with gifts and food. Pictures of the lying-in room celebrate family and domestic virtue, and they also recall that childbirth and infancy were dangerous. Matthijs Naiveu trained in Leiden in the circle of Gerrit Dou, whose fine, polished manner prized such close-up domestic subjects.","c":"The Leiden fine painters prized polish and finish, and Naiveu, working a generation after Dou, keeps that care in a more genial, less intense key. Subjects of this tender, observed kind are his strong suit. What he lacks is Dou's daring play of light and the concentrated power of the best Dutch domestic pictures, so the work feels accomplished but derivative. It is a pleasing survival of the Leiden tradition in its late phase."},"met:782305":{"d":"A room portrait of about 1845, oil on canvas, about 63 by 52 centimeters. The Met records only a bedroom and a mirror among its subjects, so the room itself is the sitter, painted for its furnishings and light rather than for any figure. Bernstorff Palace is an eighteenth-century country house north of Copenhagen, and the picture belongs to a mid-century taste, particularly strong in Denmark and Germany, for exact, quiet views of domestic interiors.","i":"Interior views were a specialty of Golden Age Danish painting and of northern Europe in general: rooms painted as portraits of a domestic world, valued as records of taste and comfort and for the play of daylight across fabric and wood. A mirror lets the painter show more of the room and play with reflection. Mirrors have an old iconography as emblems of vanity, but in nineteenth-century interiors they mostly add light and depth.","c":"With no figure to draw the eye, the picture rests on the room: the play of light, the sheen of wood and cloth, the depth the mirror opens. Danish interiors of this period have a quiet, undemonstrative honesty, and a good one makes ordinary space feel inhabited. A critic would praise the restraint and the sharp-eyed objectivity, while noting that the genre is small in ambition and the result more a document than a drama."},"met:441374":{"d":"An oil sketch, about 64 by 110 centimeters, for a large ceremonial picture of the reception of Napoleon III and Empress Eugénie by Kabyle leaders at Algiers on 18 September 1860. The wide format holds a crowd, with the emperor, women and a ceremony recorded among the subjects, so the sketch is working out how to arrange a great public occasion into one unified composition. Painted about 1862 to 1867, it belongs to the Orientalist painting of the Second Empire, which brought colonial subjects to the Salon.","i":"The imperial visit to Algeria in 1860 was a display of power and, in the emperor's plans, of reconciliation with the colonized population. Kabyle leaders came from the mountains east of Algiers, a region the French had conquered only a few years earlier. A painting of their reception helped the regime present conquest as consent, and Pils, known for official military and ceremonial pictures, was well placed to supply it. Today it is best read as imperial propaganda in the form of history painting.","c":"As a sketch it is freer and fresher than the finished academic canvases of its type: the quick placing of groups, the notes of costume color and the planning of the crowd are what to look at. Pils was an official painter of the Second Empire whose reputation rests on large, orderly scenes with little personal fire, so the looseness is the main attraction. A revealing record of how imperial images were made, more document than inspiration."},"nga:133837":{"d":"A small upright panel, about 37 by 24 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in 1616. The title names two characters from the Book of Esther: the Jewish queen of Persia and her cousin and guardian Mordecai. The painter, Hendrick van Steenwijck the Younger, was best known for architectural views and interiors populated with small figures, so the setting around the pair probably counts for as much as the story. The vertical format and very small scale suggest a work for a collector's cabinet.","i":"Esther, a Jewish orphan raised by her cousin Mordecai, became queen of Persia and, at his urging, risked her life to plead with the king to save her people from Haman's plot. Mordecai's counsel is the hinge of the story. Reformation-era viewers, Catholic and Protestant alike, valued Esther as a model of courage and prudent intercession, and her story was a popular subject for small cabinet pictures.","c":"Steenwijck's real talent is architecture: perspective, light on stone and the sense of depth in a small space, with figures as accents. In a picture this size the precision is the draw, but the narrative is thin, and the story needs the title to be understood. It is a skilled cabinet piece by a specialist, not an ambitious history picture, and it is best enjoyed for craft."},"met:437750":{"d":"A small architectural picture of about 1615, painted in oil on a copper plate. A portico in the Renaissance manner, with columns and arches receding into depth, is the principal subject, and the elegant figures of the title stand or stroll within it in fashionable dress. Copper gives a smooth, hard surface that suits the fine detail of such pictures, and plates of this kind were usually small, made for a collector's cabinet.","i":"Architectural painting was a Flemish and Dutch specialty around 1600. Painters used the new science of linear perspective to build imaginary palaces and churches from the pattern books of Hans Vredeman de Vries, whose designs spread the classical language of columns and arches across northern Europe. Hendrick van Steenwijck the Elder learned from Vredeman, and his son carried the tradition on. The elegant figures put the architecture to use as a stage for polite society and show its scale.","c":"A specialist's showpiece: the interest is in perspective, crisp detail and the glint of light on stone, and the copper support lets the touch be fine. The architecture is imagined rather than recorded, assembled from the vocabulary of Vredeman's prints, and the figures are accents more than people. It is virtuosic, cool and exact, a picture to admire for its craft rather than to feel."},"met:438490":{"d":"A vertical panel of about 48 by 35 centimeters looks into the interior of the Oude Kerk, or Old Church, in Delft, with columns, human figures and a dog. The Reformation had stripped Dutch churches of altars and images, leaving whitewashed spaces open to light and public use. Emanuel de Witte, who lived in Delft until the early 1650s, painted this view probably in 1650, in oil on wood.","i":"After 1572 Dutch Protestants took over the old Catholic churches, whitewashed their walls and removed altars and statues. The bare architecture that remained was both a place of worship and a public gathering place where people and even dogs wandered. Painters like De Witte found in these interiors a subject made of geometry, light and space, and buyers liked pictures that celebrated local landmarks.","c":"De Witte's gift was to treat architecture as a stage for light and silence. In this Delft view the columns and the space matter more than the little figures and dog, which give scale and a touch of daily life. It is a refined early church interior in which the cool, empty space is the real subject."},"nga:130808":{"d":"Looking down the central nave of a deeply shadowed church toward the altar, sunlight from the right bathes the white pillars and a wooden family pew at the left, over which a child's eyes peek. Gold chandeliers hang below the dark vaults, and about a dozen men in black enter in a line. At the right two men talk beside a dog, one pointing to a stone slab lifted from the floor to show earth beneath, with shovels, a broom and a skull nearby. In the lower right a woman nurses a swaddled baby.","i":"The Oude Kerk, Amsterdam's oldest church, became Protestant in 1578, and its walls and pillars were whitewashed and stripped of images. It was also a public space, used for worship, meetings and burials beneath the floor, and the open grave, shovels and skull here belong to that daily life. Set beside the nursing mother and the child, the skull is a memento mori, a reminder of mortality, and the dogs add a note of comic realism.","c":"De Witte is the great poet of the whitewashed church: he records less architecture than light-filled space, with sunlight raking the pillars and darkness gathering in the vault. This is one of his most human interiors, thanks to the open grave, the nursing mother and the dogs. Where Saenredam's churches are exact and silent, de Witte's are inhabited. The staging is a little theatrical, but the grave beside the nursing mother gives it a quiet moral edge."},"cma:145450":{"d":"A spacious church interior, largely imaginary, is painted in oil on canvas of about 62 by 54 centimeters, around 1680. De Witte's precise description of architectural forms convinces the viewer that it is an actual place, and he included some specific features of the Oude Kerk in Amsterdam to create a sense of familiarity. Whitewashed walls and columns make a play of light and shade across the plain surfaces.","i":"In the late 1500s militant Protestants occupied Catholic churches in the Netherlands and stripped them of rich decoration and idolatrous images. The resulting spare, whitewashed interiors became a favorite subject of Dutch artists, who were intrigued by the interplay of light and shadow over unadorned walls and by the challenge of depicting perspective. Churches also served as public gathering places in the 1600s, and even dogs were allowed.","c":"In his Amsterdam years De Witte increasingly composed imaginary interiors, and this canvas shows the gain and the loss: the architecture is orchestrated for beauty and mood, but it is less tied to a visible truth. Light moves across plain surfaces with calm authority. This is a late, assured performance rather than an inventive one."},"met:437335":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 115 by 56.5 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with a gold ground, about 1442. Its subject is the Virgin and Child with saints, the standard sacred image of an Italian church. The gold background, a sign of heavenly light, lifts the figures out of any earthly setting and turns space into a luminous surface, in the manner of Sienese painters of the period. The upright shape suggests that the panel was once part of a larger altarpiece or served as a small devotional altar.","i":"Saints beside the Madonna act as patrons and intercessors, each recognizable by a conventional attribute, and the arrangement presents the sacred figures as timeless presences rather than characters in a story. A picture like this was made to be prayed before, in a church or chapel. Sienese painters of the fifteenth century kept the gold ground, refined line and rich ornament of the previous century longer than their Florentine rivals, who were turning to perspective and sculptural form.","c":"Priamo della Quercia, brother of the great sculptor Jacopo della Quercia, is a minor master, loyal to the courtly, linear Sienese tradition. A critic would judge the panel on delicacy of line and richness of gold rather than on spatial invention, where Florence led. It is graceful, devout and a little backward-looking, an honest example of how Siena preferred beauty of surface to the new science of space."},"met:435655":{"d":"A small upright panel, about 43 by 34 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in 1644. The title and the recorded subjects, a man, a woman, cattle and a dog in a landscape, point to a pastoral pause: herders resting with their animals in open country. The panel support and small scale are typical of cabinet pictures made for Dutch collectors, who liked rural scenes that could hang in a private room and be studied at close range.","i":"Pastoral scenes of herders and cattle were very popular with Dutch buyers, who liked an idealized countryside peopled by relaxed peasants and well-fed animals. Berchem, born in Haarlem in 1620, became one of the most successful painters of such scenes, later adding the warm light of Italianate landscape. A picture of 1644, made when he was in his early twenties, belongs to his formative years.","c":"Even in his early twenties Berchem was a fluent painter of animals and figures, and a small panel like this shows his early, tighter manner before the golden light and decorative ease of his mature style. The charm lies in the unforced grouping and in the animals, which he always drew well. The risk is conventionality: the subject is a formula he would repeat for decades. As a young man's work it is accomplished and promising."},"cma:143813":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 102 by 137 centimeters, painted in oil in 1657. The title names its subject as rush gatherers, country people cutting and collecting rushes, the tall marsh plants used for matting, baskets and thatch, so the scene is set at the edge of water. In Berchem's pictures of these years such rural work is typically set in an Italianate landscape of warm light, hills and animals, with the workers treated as picturesque rather than burdened.","i":"Dutch collectors liked a blend of Dutch and Italian features, a countryside more picturesque than their own flat land, in which labor looks close to leisure. Berchem, born in Haarlem, was one of the most successful painters of this type of landscape in the Republic, and the 1650s were a peak of his productivity. A subject such as rush gathering gave him a quiet pretext for water, reflection and a low, calm view.","c":"Berchem was prolific and sure-handed, and his pastoral landscapes sold widely: warm light, graceful figures, a decorative ease. The formula is pleasing and seldom profound. A canvas of this size shows him at his most ambitious, and a viewer can enjoy the craft of the grouping and the atmosphere of the sky without expecting surprise. It is the decorative end of Dutch landscape, charming but not searching."},"nga:71554":{"d":"A hawking party and a herd share a rocky shore beneath a towering cliff. At the lower left a man and woman on horseback talk to a man with a lance; he wears a plumed hat with a horn at his side, and she wears a yellow dress with a falcon on her gloved hand. Dogs, goats, sheep and cows fill the foreground, with men in turbans and robes nearby and two men coaxing a goat into a rowboat already loaded with cows. Beyond lie ships, a distant church tower and a sky of piled white clouds.","i":"Berchem, born in Haarlem, was one of the leading Dutch painters of Italianate landscape, scenes of Italian harbours and countryside with travellers, herdsmen and animals, made for patrons who often never saw Italy. The turbaned figures and the ships point to the Mediterranean trade that enriched the Dutch Republic, and the falcon marks a pastime of the nobility. The title is generic, and the port is probably an invention assembled from sketches and memories rather than a recorded place.","c":"A lively, elegantly composed little scene. Berchem's strengths are the grace of his figures and animals, the variety of types and textures, and the clear light; everything is arranged with a decorator's tact. The charm is real, but the picture is more pretty than profound, with a stock of horsemen, goats and ships. Beside Claude Lorrain's luminous harbours it lacks atmosphere; within the Dutch Italianate manner it is a polished example."},"nga:41639":{"d":"A young woman sits facing the viewer on a stone bench, her hands steepled in prayer over a baby who sleeps propped between her knees, his chin on crossed forearms. She wears a cherry-red dress under a teal-blue cloak that pools around her feet, and a translucent white veil; both have shiny gold halos like plates. Behind her swirl crimson forms, perhaps blossoms, between dark green bushes with round green fruit. Carved gold vines frame the panel and form two roundels in the upper corners, one with an angel, one with a seated woman at a desk, against midnight blue.","i":"The roundels hold the Annunciation: the angel Gabriel at the left and, at the right, Mary with her book, so the panel joins the beginning of Christ's life to an image of the Madonna watching over her sleeping Son. The enclosed garden is a traditional setting for Mary, linked to the garden of the Song of Songs and to her purity. A sleeping Child is often read as a foreshadowing of the Passion, the sleep of death.","c":"Tura is one of the strangest of the Ferrarese painters, and this small panel shows why: a hard, jewel-like finish, brittle folds of cloth, elongated fingers, and a feeling that is tender and slightly uneasy at once. The scrolling gold frame and plate-like halos keep a goldsmith's richness. Those who prefer Bellini's warm, human Madonnas may find it cold, but it is unmistakably original, and a good introduction to the court style of Ferrara."},"met:437849":{"d":"A small, nearly square panel, about 40 by 38 centimeters, in tempera on wood, showing the Flight into Egypt. Mary, the Christ Child, Saint Joseph and a donkey are the figures, so the Holy Family is presumably on the road with their animal, as in the traditional version of the episode. It was painted in the 1470s by Cosmè Tura, court painter at Ferrara, whose hard, linear and intense manner sets him apart in Italian art. The small square format suggests part of a larger altarpiece, perhaps a predella.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew tells how an angel warned Joseph in a dream to take Mary and the child to Egypt to escape Herod. Painters made the journey a quiet pastoral scene, with Joseph leading the donkey and Mary carrying the child, and the episode was a favorite for the predellas of altarpieces, where small narrative scenes accompanied the main saints above. Tura's hallmark angularity may be expected in the travelers.","c":"Tura is one of the most original painters of the early Renaissance: his forms look metalworked, his lines are taut, his colors cool and sharp, and his figures twist with nervous energy. A small narrative panel like this is likely to show those qualities in concentrated form. It lacks the grandeur of his large altarpieces, but it is the kind of piece that makes clear how far Ferrarese painting stood from Florentine calm."},"met:437851":{"d":"A small portrait of a young man in profile, in tempera on a wood panel only about 28 by 20 centimeters, painted in the 1470s by Cosmè Tura. His name is unknown. The profile view, in which the head is seen in pure side view like a figure on a medal, was the standard format for court portraits in northern Italy at this time.","i":"Profile portraits imitated Roman coins and the medals of artists such as Pisanello, which circulated at the court of Ferrara, and they emphasized outline, rank and character over inner life. Tura worked for the Este family, so the sitter may belong to the court world, but his name is unknown. Portraits are a small part of Tura's surviving work, which makes any sitter of this kind a rarity.","c":"The profile format suits Tura: its emphasis on contour lets his taut, almost metallic line dominate, and the young man's features would be defined by the sharp edge of the face against the ground. The format and the artist make a hard, intense likeness more likely than a soft one. It shows a different side of a painter known for religious panels."},"nga:206071":{"d":"Four narrow panels, each about 31 by 12 centimeters, stand side by side as one horizontal picture, with a single landscape of rocky ground, low hills and blue sky running across them. From left to right stand Saint Francis, with the wounds of the stigmata and a gold cross; the angel Gabriel, in profile with a white lily; the Virgin, in blue over pink, with a book; and Saint Louis of Toulouse, in a white miter and carrying a staff. Each figure has its own frame, painted as an arch in darker tones of the landscape.","i":"The Annunciation is split across the two middle panels, so that angel and Virgin address each other over a gap, while two Franciscan saints stand guard at the ends. It is uncertain whether four boards were joined or one panel was painted to look like four, the latter seeming more likely. A set like this was probably made for a Franciscan church or chapel, and may have been part of a larger altarpiece, but its original setting is not known.","c":"Seen together, the panels show Tura's invention: a continuous rocky landscape makes the four figures share one space, and the arch-like frames, darkened toward the tops, give them a shadowed, chapel-like depth. The figures keep his hard linear manner, and the quiet spacing gives the group a stately rhythm. If the arrangement is a little rigid and the scale very small, the set has the concentrated beauty of an enamel."},"nga:41585":{"d":"A full-length standing figure faces the front with head tilted down and a solemn expression. He has short dark hair with a tonsure and wears a brown robe with long sleeves, tied with a rope at the waist. In one hand he holds a black book with a white spine, and a faint halo surrounds his head. In the combined view of the set, red wounds show on his hands, feet and right side, and he braces a thin gold cross. The background is a textured blue with rocky forms. Tempera and distemper, about 31 by 12 centimeters.","i":"Francis of Assisi, founder of the Franciscan order, is identified by the brown habit with its cord, the tonsure and the stigmata, the wounds of Christ that he is said to have received on Mount La Verna in 1224. The cross and book stand for the Passion he imitated and the Rule he gave his friars. Placed with Louis of Toulouse, another Franciscan saint, he suggests that the set was probably made for a Franciscan setting.","c":"Tura's Francis has none of the gentle, bird-preaching sweetness that Italian art often gives him. The heavy-lidded, deeply lined face and the sharp, angular drapery make the saint look gaunt and austere, an ascetic marked by suffering. It is a compelling characterization in a very small space, and typical of Tura's love of the hard, strained line. The limit is the format: a single figure on a narrow strip allows little more than a vivid emblem."},"nga:41586":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 31 by 12 centimeters, showing the archangel Gabriel in profile, facing right. His curly blond hair is lifted off the back of his neck, and he wears a deep pink cloak over a forest-green robe. Dark green, almost black wings spring from between his shoulder blades. One hand is raised with the first two fingers extended; the other holds the stem of a white lily. He stands on rocky ground against a textured blue ground. Tempera and distemper on panel, about 1470 to 1480.","i":"Gabriel brings Mary the news that she will bear the Son of God, the event told in the Gospel of Luke. The raised fingers are a gesture of speech or blessing, and the white lily is Mary's traditional emblem of purity, carried here by the messenger. His profile and lifted hair suggest arrival in motion. He is paired with the Virgin Annunciate in the neighboring panel, so that the two panels tell the Annunciation across a small gap of space.","c":"Tura gives the archangel an edgy, un-sweet beauty: the tight profile, sharply cut drapery and near-black wings make him look cast in metal rather than flesh. This is Ferrarese art at its most distinctive, and the dark wings are a daring touch that most painters would have avoided. On the other hand the small scale and single pose leave little room for the drama of Tura's larger altarpieces. Still, a small jewel."},"nga:41587":{"d":"A full-length woman stands upright, her head slightly tilted. Her features are delicately painted, with a straight nose and lips at rest, framed by wavy, light golden hair that falls around her face and neck. She wears a red robe under a blue drape that cascades in intricate folds to the floor, and a thin band at her neck. One hand holds a slender closed book against her body; the other is raised with the palm upturned. The background is a deep, textured blue, with earthy tones near the bottom. Tempera and distemper, about 31 by 12 centimeters.","i":"In the Annunciation the Virgin Mary is visited by the angel Gabriel, who tells her she will bear the Son of God; Annunciate names her as the one addressed. The book recalls the tradition that she was reading the prophecies of Isaiah when the angel appeared, and her raised hand shows surprise or acceptance. Her red and blue clothing are the colors usually given to Mary. Gabriel stands in the neighboring panel.","c":"The Virgin is a Tura type: long oval face, small mouth, tall slim body wrapped in drapery that falls in sharp, almost metallic folds. The effect is more elegant and remote than tender, and a critic would value the poise and the drawing of hands and robe over any emotional warmth. In this narrow format her separation from the angel is a weakness; as part of the set, she works."},"nga:41588":{"d":"A man in a bishop's dress stands upright, holding a staff in his left hand and making a gesture with his right. He has a large nose and brow and a somber expression. A tall, pointed hat covers his head, and he wears a red robe with gold and brown accents and a golden medallion at his neck. The head of the staff has intricate detail, with what looks like a small animal figure near the top. The textured background combines blue and earthy tones that suggest cloud or fog above brown ground.","i":"Louis of Toulouse, son of a king of Naples, gave up his claim to the throne to become a Franciscan friar and was made bishop of Toulouse at twenty-three; he died young in 1297 and was canonized in 1317. The bishop's hat and staff mark his office, and his place beside Saint Francis points to a Franciscan setting. His rank and his humility are meant to be seen together, a prince who chose poverty.","c":"The saint's strong, almost homely face, with its big nose, heavy brow and somber look, is individual rather than idealized, and typical of Tura: character through angularity. The rich vestments give a goldsmith's pleasure. In the narrow strip the figure is wedge-shaped and a little rigid, so the panel works best as one note in the chord of the four."},"met:437850":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 72 by 40 centimeters, showing Saint Louis of Toulouse against a gold ground, in tempera. The paint layer has been transferred from its original wood to canvas and then stretched over a new wooden support, a drastic repair used on damaged panels. Louis was a young Franciscan bishop, and the tall, upright shape suggests a side panel of a polyptych. The painter is Cosmè Tura of Ferrara, and the date is given as 1484, with a question mark.","i":"Louis of Toulouse (1274 to 1297), son of the king of Naples, gave up his claim to the throne to become a Franciscan friar and was made bishop of Toulouse before dying at twenty-three. He is normally shown in the habit of a friar with a bishop's cope, miter and crozier, sometimes with a crown at his feet. Franciscan communities and the Angevin dynasty promoted his cult, and his image appears in altarpieces for Franciscan churches.","c":"Even in a damaged and transferred state, a saint by Tura commands attention for the hard, brittle drawing of the drapery and the intensity of the pose. The gold ground flattens the figure, and transfers often cost a surface some of its original subtlety, so the painting has to be judged as a survivor. It remains an authentic fragment of Ferrarese painting at its most original."},"nga:178128":{"d":"A small seascape, about 23.5 by 37.5 centimeters, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 1830. The plain title Marine, the French term for a sea picture, names no particular place or event. A panel this small suits a quick, sketch-like study of water and sky, the kind of picture painters of the Romantic generation made at the shore, and the horizontal format fits an open view across the water.","i":"Eugène Isabey was one of the leading French marine painters of the Romantic generation. Trained by his father, the miniaturist Jean-Baptiste Isabey, and close to the English painter Richard Parkes Bonington, he was named an official painter to the French navy around 1830. Marine pictures of the time mixed observation of coastal light with a taste for drama and for the life of ports. Isabey later taught the young Johan Barthold Jongkind.","c":"Isabey's small panels are generally looser and more truthful than his theatrical exhibition pieces, and a sketch-sized marine of about 1830 belongs to that fresher side of his work. Among French sea painters it sits in the line that runs from Bonington through Isabey to Boudin and Jongkind. A modest picture, but a good sign of the observation on which the later Impressionist view of the sea was built."},"met:438950":{"d":"A stormy sea breaks along the Normandy coast in this oil sketch on paper laid down on canvas, about 33 by 51 centimeters, probably painted in the 1850s. The subject is wind and waves, with no ships or figures noted among the main elements. Eugène Isabey, a French marine painter, used oil on paper to catch the quick effects of weather in the open air before working them up in the studio.","i":"Storms and shipwrecks were favorite Romantic subjects, because they showed nature's power over human plans, and the Normandy coast offered Isabey a ready supply. As the son of a famous court miniaturist, he moved from fashionable costume scenes to the sea, where he became one of the leading French marine painters of his generation. His open-air sketches influenced younger painters such as Jongkind, who studied with him.","c":"As a sketch, the picture has an immediacy that finished marine paintings of the period often lack: the sea is built from rapid, loaded strokes, and the sense of weather is convincing. It makes no attempt at a story, and that is a strength. It shows why Isabey's studies were admired by painters who would soon dispense with finish altogether."},"nga:177682":{"d":"In a lavish room with red drapery, wood paneling and a tiled floor, a table is set with glassware and a golden bowl. In the background, elegantly dressed figures, apparently women in elaborate gowns, stand as if taking part in a musical or theatrical entertainment, with instruments visible among them. Soft, warm light picks out the textures of the costumes. The scene is a costume piece, painted in oil on canvas in 1874, about 64 by 53 centimeters.","i":"By the 1870s Isabey, who had made his name painting the sea, was producing costume pieces: scenes of elegant company in the dress of past centuries. The mood recalls the gallantry of Watteau, whose fêtes galantes were enjoying a revival in French taste, and the lavish interior and glittering table suit a buyer's nostalgia for the refinement of the old regime. The title keeps the occasion vague, a party rather than a story.","c":"An accomplished pastiche: warm light, rich fabric and a lively sense of costume, all handled with Isabey's fluent brush. But like many such costume pieces it borrows its charm more than it creates it, and where Watteau's gallantry carries an undertone of melancholy, this offers decoration. Isabey's best work remains his sea pictures and harbor scenes; this late costume piece is pleasant and slightly empty."},"nga:195131":{"d":"A busy tavern kitchen with a high-beamed ceiling and stone floor. A large table at the center is laden with food, and several figures talk and dine around it. To the left a group descends a wooden staircase, and in the foreground dogs and a cat roam. A big fireplace at the right is stacked with cooking utensils and pots, and food hangs from the ceiling, all in a warm, golden-brown palette. Oil on canvas, about 75 by 107 centimeters, painted in 1875.","i":"The subject and its warm, crowded manner recall seventeenth-century Dutch painters of tavern and kitchen life, above all Jan Steen, whose disorderly households became proverbial. Nineteenth-century French collectors admired and imitated such pictures. In 1875 Isabey was in his early seventies, a celebrated marine painter, and this tavern scene shows him indulging a taste for old-master genre. The animals, hanging food and pots are the usual still-life vocabulary of the Dutch kitchen.","c":"An enjoyable homage: the warm brown tonality, crowded space, animals and still-life details are convincingly Dutch in flavor, and the brushwork is lively. But it lacks the sharp human observation and moral wit of Steen, and the figures are types more than people. The result is no rival to the old masters, and it says more about nineteenth-century taste for them than about country taverns."},"met:437179":{"d":"A landscape of 1828, oil on a wooden panel, about 70 by 92 centimeters, showing the countryside near Penshurst, a village in Kent best known for Penshurst Place, home of the Sidney family. Trees are recorded among its elements, and a title that names a place rather than an event points to a view of the real, wooded Kentish scene. The wooden panel follows the practice of seventeenth-century Dutch landscape painters, whom Patrick Nasmyth admired and imitated.","i":"Nasmyth, born in Edinburgh and working in London, was nicknamed the English Hobbema for his debt to the tree-filled country scenes of the seventeenth-century Dutch painter Meindert Hobbema. Penshurst already had a literary reputation: Ben Jonson's poem To Penshurst, published in 1616, is regarded as the first English country-house poem. Collectors in the 1820s bought pictures of rural England as a comfortable, picturesque image of the nation.","c":"Nasmyth's foliage is carefully observed and his light gentle and moist. The debt to Hobbema is plain, and the picture offers the pleasures of a well-made, orderly woodland scene rather than the daring of his contemporary Constable. He was an able follower rather than an innovator, and he died young in 1831, so his output is small. A pleasant, accomplished picture in a conservative idiom."},"nga:12199":{"d":"In a room crowded with easels, white plaster heads and statuettes, a box of paints, books and papers, a young woman in a white gown tied with a sky-blue ribbon stands looking at a sketch while a girl in a gold dress sits in an olive-green velvet chair beside her, a portfolio on her lap and a stylus or brush raised to her chin. A table at the right holds plaster casts of faces and a hand, and a shelf behind holds busts of a man, child and woman. The scene is lit from the upper left.","i":"Plaster casts after antique sculpture were the basic teaching aids of academic art training, so their presence in the room signals serious study. Around 1800, in the years after the Revolution, drawing was a fashionable accomplishment for young women of the Parisian middle class, and Louis-Léopold Boilly, a specialist in small, polished scenes of Paris life, painted several studio scenes and interiors. The picture presents the studio as an elegant, domestic, feminine space rather than a workshop.","c":"A charming, meticulous painting whose real subject is the clutter: the casts, bottles, cloths and portfolios are rendered with a trompe-l'oeil precision, and the soft side light holds them together. The two figures are delicate but conventional, in the smooth profiles of the neoclassical taste, and the story is slight. Boilly's polish recalls the Dutch cabinet picture adapted to Parisian taste, and the pleasure lies in looking at the objects as much as at the people."},"nga:111638":{"d":"On a town boulevard, fourteen men, women and children watch a man in a plumed bicorn hat and long dark coat fan out a deck of cards, while a young woman in a pale green dress and bonnet reaches to take one. The performer has long grey hair knotted at the back, a gold earring and gold glints, possibly bells, on his hat and coat. At the left more people stroll, and a ragged group clusters around a table with a cup and glass balls, with dogs underfoot and a shop labelled Patissier. It is a tiny panel, about 24 by 33 centimetres.","i":"Street entertainers and charlatans were part of the daily show of the Paris boulevards, and Boilly, a keen observer of the crowd, painted the mixture of classes that gathered around them: well-dressed women, ragged onlookers and children. The card trick draws the young woman, and the cup and balls on the table suggest a second game, so the picture is also a quiet warning about gullibility. It dates from 1806, when such scenes of everyday life were popular with collectors of small cabinet pictures.","c":"A brilliantly observed little picture. Boilly packs a whole social range into a few square centimetres, from elegant lady to ragged onlooker, and gives each face a distinct reaction; the tiny dog and the pastry cook's sign add wry detail. The finish is smooth, almost enamelled, and the colours are bright and clear. The cool polish keeps the scene at a distance and the humour gentle, but the picture is a delight."},"met:438099":{"d":"A crowded gallery scene of 1810, oil on canvas, about 62 by 83 centimeters, showing visitors to the Louvre looking at Jacques-Louis David's enormous Coronation of Napoleon. Men, women and children are among the public; the subject is less David's picture than its audience. The small, horizontal format and the fine detail of the figures place it among Boilly's cabinet pictures of Parisian life, painted for collectors who enjoyed seeing their own city and manners observed with wit.","i":"David's Coronation, painted for the emperor between 1805 and 1807, was one of the largest and most discussed pictures of its day, and the Louvre, opened to the public during the Revolution, was by 1810 a place where Parisians of every rank mixed to look at art. Boilly's picture records that new habit: art as public spectacle, seen by a mixed audience, and also the regime's use of painting to broadcast its glory.","c":"Boilly was a sharp observer of Parisian types, and here he offers something rare: a picture about looking at pictures, crowded with individual reactions. The strength is the variety of the crowd and the wit of the observation; the weakness may be a certain busyness, with so many figures that each is a type more than a person. As social history it is invaluable; as painting, deft and entertaining rather than profound."},"met:817504":{"d":"A portrait of a boy painted in oil on canvas about 1805, about 72 by 59 centimeters. The recorded subjects, besides the boy himself, are a doll, a cake, an apple and a stool, so the child is shown among playthings and treats, a way of presenting childhood as a world of its own. The size, nearly three-quarters of a meter high, is that of a serious portrait commission for a family's home.","i":"Child portraits of the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries show a new sentiment, partly shaped by Rousseau's writing on education: the child as a child, with toys and sweets, not as a miniature adult. Boilly, a Parisian painter of domestic life, was skilled at it. Apples and cakes in such pictures are most likely simply treats, though apples in older art could suggest innocence; the doll belongs to the same world of play.","c":"Boilly's gifts, a lively face, a glossy, precise surface and a keen eye for detail, suit children well, and the toys add charm and interest. A viewer may find the accessories sweet and a little staged. Between Chardin's quiet children and the polished officialdom of Empire portraiture, this shows a skilled commercial artist at ease with his sitter and his market."},"nga:46477":{"d":"A girl is shown from the waist up in front of a dark background, looking over her shoulder at the viewer. She has wide-set hazel eyes under curved brows, flushed cheeks and a pointed chin, and her brown hair falls in bangs and is otherwise pulled back. She wears a white dress with a low neckline, high waist and short ruched sleeves, with white fabric tucked into the neckline. The brown background glows slightly brighter around her head. It is a very small oil, about 22 by 17 centimetres.","i":"The title gives the sitter's name, Caroline Mortier de Trévise, which suggests that she belonged to the family of Édouard Mortier, Marshal of the Empire and Duke of Treviso. A companion portrait of Malvina Mortier de Trévise, of the same size and date, probably shows a sister. Boilly, a Paris painter famed for genre scenes, also made small, finely finished portraits of Parisian families, prized as keepsakes. The picture belongs to the years of the First Empire, when the new nobility wanted likenesses of its children.","c":"A delicate, sympathetic child's portrait. The over-the-shoulder glance gives the girl life, and the soft glow behind her head sets the pale dress against the dark ground. The execution is as fine as enamel; Boilly renders skin and muslin with a smoothness that approaches miniature painting. The limits are those of the manner: a certain porcelain polish and little psychological depth. But within its small format the portrait is charming and direct."},"nga:46478":{"d":"A young girl is shown from the waist up, facing the viewer. She has a round face with soft features, light brown eyes and a small mouth, and her brown hair is parted in the middle and falls smoothly to the sides. She wears a white garment with a high collar and puffed sleeves ending just above the elbows, made of a light, textured fabric. The background is a plain brownish tone, and no jewellery or objects distract from her. The panel is small, about 22 by 17 centimetres, and was painted around 1810 to 1812.","i":"The sitter's name identifies her as a member of the Mortier de Trévise family, and the portrait of Caroline Mortier de Trévise, of the same size and date, is evidently its companion. The frontal pose and plain ground make this portrait calmer and more formal than that of her sister. In the Empire period, simple white muslin dresses were the fashion for girls and young women, and a small portrait like this recorded a child at a particular age for her family.","c":"Malvina's portrait is quieter than her sister's: the head-on view, the smooth parting, the plain brown ground and the unadorned white dress produce a calm, direct image, painted with the soft precision typical of Boilly. The very plainness can seem expressionless, and the likeness is tidy more than animated. But the soft modelling of the round face and the clear gaze give it a childlike dignity."},"met:435687":{"d":"A very small portrait of a man, about 22 by 17.5 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas; the Met gives no date. Its size is identical to that of the Portrait of a Woman by the same painter, which suggests that the two were made as a pair to hang side by side, perhaps as husband and wife. At this scale the likeness is probably head-and-shoulders, a portrait to be hung or held in a private room rather than shown in public.","i":"Pendant portraits of husband and wife were the usual way for a middle-class couple to record themselves, and in the Revolutionary and Napoleonic years new patrons, merchants, officials and professionals, were Boilly's main clients. A small portrait of this kind was cheaper than a full-size canvas and easy to display at home. Boilly began as a painter of trompe-l'oeil, a training that shows in the miniature-like finish of his faces.","c":"Boilly's small portraits give a lively impression of individual character: a firm, detailed face, a smooth finish and little flattery. A critic would admire the directness and technical polish while noting that so small a format leaves scant room for invention. It is a fair example of the unpretentious bourgeois portraiture that made his living."},"met:435688":{"d":"A very small portrait of a woman, about 22 by 17.5 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas, with no date given. It matches the Portrait of a Man by the same artist in size, so it was probably made as its companion, the two hanging together as a couple. A portrait of this size was meant for a home and could be looked at from close range, which suits Boilly's fine, miniature-like handling.","i":"In early nineteenth-century France a woman's portrait recorded her place in the household and her taste: dress, cap and hairstyle announced fashion and respectability. If she and the man in the companion portrait are a couple, the pair makes the marriage itself the subject, a bond between two families. Boilly's faces, painted with the clarity of a miniature, made him a popular choice for such commissions.","c":"The lively, finely finished head is again the point. Boilly avoided both flattery and caricature, and the result, a plain, sympathetic likeness, has more life than many grander portraits of the day. Neither small picture is ambitious, but they show why his clientele stayed loyal through the Empire and the Restoration."},"met:436567":{"d":"A long, low panel, about 78 by 151 cm, gathers several episodes from Saint John the Baptist's early life into one picture, painted in tempera and oil with gold. A bedroom with a bed is a main setting, with women attending an infant; Mary and Elizabeth are among the figures, and the young Baptist also appears again as a child. The long shape is typical of narrative panels that Florentines set into the paneling or furniture of a private room, and this panel was one of a series.","i":"John the Baptist is the patron saint of Florence, so episodes from his life had a civic as well as a religious appeal. The bedroom belongs to the infancy story in the Gospel of Luke: Elizabeth, long childless, bore John, and her cousin Mary, who stayed with her, is often shown attending the birth. Showing the saint again as a child recalls the tradition that he and the Christ Child met in boyhood. The interior probably follows a Florentine bedchamber.","c":"This is lively narrative in the Florentine manner Granacci learned in Ghirlandaio's workshop: clear figures, a believable domestic interior and a decorative use of gold. The series is usually discussed for the problem of who painted what, since several hands were involved. As storytelling it is charming and fluent rather than profound, closer to illustration than to the heroic style Michelangelo, Granacci's friend, was inventing in these very years."},"met:438445":{"d":"A very tall, very narrow panel, about 48 cm high and only 15 cm wide, shows the Crucifixion in tempera and gold on wood. Christ hangs on the cross, and the Virgin Mary and soldiers also appear. The proportions suggest that the panel belonged to a larger ensemble, possibly with a companion showing the Resurrection, rather than standing alone as a picture; it may have been a shutter, a pilaster panel or part of a small devotional piece.","i":"The Virgin and the soldiers are standard figures of the Passion: Mary is traditionally shown grieving at the foot of the cross, while the soldiers are those who carried out the execution. If this panel was paired with a Resurrection, the two together would set Christ's death beside his victory over it, the sequence at the heart of Easter. Narrow panels like this were probably meant for private prayer, where a small, precious image could be held within a larger frame or case.","c":"Granacci, pupil of Ghirlandaio and friend of Michelangelo, was a clear, versatile Florentine draftsman, and a narrow strip is an exacting test: the composition must climb rather than spread. The gold gives a tiny scene a jewel-like quality. As a surviving part of a larger whole it is hard to judge fairly, but his habitual clarity and decorative instinct, rather than intense feeling, are what such a panel is likely to offer."},"met:438432":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child fill this devotional panel of about 87 by 65 cm, painted in oil on wood around 1520. The subject was among the most repeated in Florentine painting, and the size suits a domestic chapel or a bedroom wall rather than a church altar. By this date Granacci was working in the shadow of a new generation, Fra Bartolomeo, Andrea del Sarto and Raphael, whose monumental, rounded Madonnas set the standard in Florence.","i":"As in every Madonna and Child, Mary is both a tender mother and the Mother of God, and the Child is both an infant and the Savior. Florentine families commissioned such panels for prayer at home, often in connection with marriage and the birth of children. Without an identified patron or documented setting, the picture is best read as a type: an image of motherhood that invites prayer and offers the household a model of devotion.","c":"Granacci in his later decades is usually judged a capable but secondary figure, adapting the clarity he learned from Ghirlandaio to the grander manner of his juniors. A Madonna in oil like this is best weighed in that light: sound drawing and an agreeable calm are to be expected, together with a monumentality that likely owes more to Fra Bartolomeo than to his own invention. It is an accomplished picture by a minor master."},"nga:30214":{"d":"A woman stands in the center of the picture, seen from the knees up, partly in front of an older man behind her at the right. She has pale skin, dark eyebrows and a thin red mouth in a slight smile, her dark hair is pinned up with a pink flower, and she wears a seafoam-green dress with thin straps and a puffed skirt. The man wears a black suit, bow tie and top hat and has gray hair and a pointed gray beard. The background is split: dark brown behind him, a colorful abstract garden scene behind her.","i":"Jean-Louis Forain made his name painting the Paris world of the opera and theater, in the circle of Degas, and this picture probably shows the backstage encounter between a dancer or actress and a wealthy gentleman, a familiar subject. The painted garden at the left looks like a stage set. Forain, who was also a caricaturist, had a sharp eye for the transactions of the demimonde, and the meaning of the pair is rather cynical.","c":"Forain's compositional trick, the vertical division between the real dark side and the painted fantasy, makes a witty visual statement about illusion and reality. The figures are drawn with a caricaturist's economy, and the woman's averted, thoughtful look keeps her human. The handling is quick and the color somewhat acid, but the observation is shrewd. It is a small, pointed social comedy rather than a grand painting."},"nga:3238":{"d":"A woman's head and neck appear twice in two overlapping sketches on this pastel, one upside down like a reflection. In the upper sketch she faces the viewer, with pale white-blue skin and dark brown hair in a bun; her features are outlined in pale gray and hard to see against her skin. The upside-down sketch shows her head in profile with her hair down over one shoulder, and hints of fingers. Lines of white, green and gray cross the pale-yellow ground. The pastel on canvas measures about 61 by 50 centimeters.","i":"Forain, a friend of Degas and a gifted draftsman, made many quick studies of women, and this sheet appears to have been drawn with the canvas turned around so that a second sketch overlaps the first. Such studies were kept in the studio as private exercises and as material for his finished scenes of the theater and society. The sitter is unnamed and probably a model.","c":"A free, improvised sheet whose interest lies in its line, with pale gray contours and light colored hatching that seem to float on the yellow ground. The double image gives an accidental, almost cinematic quality. It is a draftsman's note rather than a finished picture, and as such it shows Forain's skill with a few quick strokes."},"nga:164921":{"d":"A crowd of spectators stands and sits on light brown chairs in the foreground of this picture of about 74 by 93 centimeters, most with their backs turned and facing a horse race. The women wear long, colorful dresses and hold parasols, and the men wear gray suit jackets and black top hats. Behind them a long strip of green grass runs toward the center, where riders on horseback race to the left, seen over the heads of the crowd. A second crowd and a few cream-colored tents stand on the far side, with trees beyond.","i":"Longchamp, in the Bois de Boulogne on the western edge of Paris, opened as a racecourse in 1857 and became the setting for the fashionable social events of the season, including the Grand Prix. Forain follows the example of Manet and Degas, who also painted the race: placing the viewer among the spectators, and showing their backs and parasols as much as the race itself.","c":"The viewpoint from behind the crowd is the picture's best idea: it makes the race a distant event and the people the real subject. The parasols and dresses add color to a sky-and-grass palette. Forain handles the crowd with energy, though the horses are only touches. It is a lively social panorama rather than a sporting picture, in line with the Impressionist interest in modern life."},"nga:66416":{"d":"A wide, grassy green field with distant green hills lies under a blue sky with fluffy white clouds. To the right of center, two men and one woman seem to be engaged in conversation: one man wears a dark suit and top hat, the other a red shirt, tan breeches, tall black boots and a round hat, and the woman wears a long pink dress and a large pink hat. Behind them four riders head toward the back left, and more spectators watch at the far left, one holding a white parasol. The gouache on canvas is about 81 by 115 centimeters.","i":"Racecourses were a favorite subject of Degas, Manet and their circle, because they brought together fashion, sport, the outdoors and modern city crowds in one picture. Forain, who followed Degas, painted the social side of racing: the owners, trainers, jockeys and fashionable women who conversed at the edge of the track. The loosely drawn faces turn the people into types, in the style of a caricaturist.","c":"Forain's gouache has a hazy, vibrating surface made of short, overlapping strokes, and the green field gives the composition a luminous openness. The figures are indistinct, but the social comedy of their groupings comes through. It is a lively and atmospheric picture, though the breadth of the field and the vagueness of the faces keep it lighter than his best work."},"nga:66417":{"d":"Two women stand near still blue water among tall green grass and white flowers. The woman in a bright pink dress holds out a long rod whose string ends in a small red lure floating on the water, while behind her a woman in a brown dress and white apron holds a baby in white clothes and a bonnet. A brown shape like a small dog runs toward them. Their reflections lie in the water, and the sky moves from light blue to warm pink at the horizon, crossed by purple clouds and a flock of small birds. The brushstrokes are light and feathery.","i":"The title names the angler as the artist's wife, Jeanne Bosc, whom Jean-Louis Forain married in 1891. In the 1890s Forain, known for caricatures of Parisian high and low life, turned to gentler subjects drawn from his own family, and this picture belongs to that domestic vein. Fishing in a pastoral landscape was a pleasant leisure of the middle class, and the flickering touch suggests the influence of the Impressionists he had exhibited with.","c":"The feathery touch and the pink dawn sky make this one of Forain's most Impressionist pictures, and the reflection of the women in still water is nicely handled. The subject is slight and the figures are somewhat generic, but the mood of relaxed domestic leisure comes through. It is a pleasant surprise from a painter usually remembered for biting satire."},"met:655744":{"d":"A young man, William Edward Dighton (1822–1853), is shown in Middle Eastern dress in an oil on canvas of about 53 by 39 cm, painted about 1852 to 1853. Frederick Goodall, who was born in the same year as his sitter, later became known for scenes of Egypt and the Bible, but he did not travel to Egypt until 1858, so the costume here is probably studio dress.","i":"In nineteenth-century Britain, portraits in Eastern costume were a Romantic fashion, signaling an adventurous spirit and an imagination drawn to a distant East. The practice also reveals the period's fantasy of the Orient and the imbalance of empire, since the dress is worn as a costume rather than lived reality. The sitter died in 1853, at about thirty-one, so the portrait carries the poignancy of a short life as well as the fashion of its time.","c":"Goodall was a painter of the picturesque East, and a portrait of a friend in costume lets him show his skill with fabric and headgear more than psychological depth. The danger is that the dress overpowers the man. Friendship adds directness, though, and a portrait made before Goodall's Egyptian journeys shows the Orientalist fashion already formed in London studios. It is charming, somewhat theatrical, and valuable as evidence of taste."},"cma:122158":{"d":"A tiny portrait of a woman, only about 16 by 15 centimeters, painted in oil on wood about 1540. The background is smooth and enamel-like, a quality restored by recent conservation, and the details are rendered with meticulous care. Gold leaf was applied to the sitter's headdress and to the embroidery of her dress, so these parts glint with real metal against the paint. The painter is Corneille de Lyon, whose small likenesses resemble portrait miniatures.","i":"Corneille de Lyon worked both for the French court and for wealthy merchants, and his northern manner, close to portrait miniatures and manuscript painting, contrasted with the Italian taste then fashionable in France. The gold on the headdress and embroidery signals the sitter's wealth, since such details were costly and time-consuming to apply. The sitter is unnamed, but the careful display of costume suggests a woman of means who wished her status recorded.","c":"Miniature-like in its detail, this is a picture to look at from a short distance. The enamel-like background and the glints of gold lift the head and costume into a jewel-like unity, and the reserve of the sitter suits the scale. Corneille is sometimes called limited because his formula varied little, but within it few matched him for finish and quiet observation."},"met:435944":{"d":"A small portrait of a man, about 21 by 17 centimeters, painted in oil on wood about 1535 by Corneille de Lyon. As the title records, the sitter is shown with gloves, a standard accessory in portraits of gentlemen. His name is unknown. The size and the wooden support place it among the small, intimate likenesses on which the painter's reputation rests.","i":"Gloves in Renaissance portraits were signs of rank, leisure and good manners, since only gentlemen and officials wore fine ones, and holding or wearing them in a portrait was a way of presenting oneself as a person of standing. The sitter here is unnamed, so his rank cannot be established. Corneille's clientele included courtiers and members of the educated and mercantile classes, so the sitter may have belonged to either circle.","c":"Corneille's small portraits are prized for their calm objectivity, and a man with gloves places this one within the established type: the sitter presented simply, without drama, in a format intended to be handled closely. Within the type, the formula is dependable, the finish high and the personality understated. The painter's real achievement is the consistency of such portraits."},"nga:50293":{"d":"A small portrait on a walnut panel, only about 16 by 14 centimeters. A man seen from the waist up faces forward and gazes out; he has fair skin, light brown hair, a thin mustache and beard and light green eyes, and his expression is mostly neutral, with a faint upturn at the corners of his lips. He wears a black garment with a high collar and a hood, and a black flat cap. The plain blue background deepens to black at the edges. Oil, about 1536 to 1540, by Corneille de Lyon.","i":"Corneille de Lyon, a painter of Netherlandish origin who worked in Lyon and for the French court, specialized in small, intimate portraits of courtiers and well-to-do townspeople against a plain, bright ground. Pictures of this size were handled like miniatures, kept in a cabinet or exchanged as tokens. The sitter here, dressed soberly in black, is unnamed, which is typical of many of Corneille's portraits.","c":"Corneille's small portraits are among the most refined of their kind in sixteenth-century France: the face is modeled with great delicacy, the flat blue ground isolates the head, and the effect is of a person seen close at hand. This man is rendered with typical reserve, a quiet, slightly guarded expression and minimal gesture. The format limits drama but concentrates attention, and Corneille's calm objectivity made him a master of the small likeness."},"met:442491":{"d":"Two apostles, Saints Peter and Paul, are presented together on a horizontal canvas about 56 by 72 centimetres. Bartolomeo Manfredi, one of the most influential followers of Caravaggio in Rome, usually painted half-length figures close to the picture plane, strongly lit from one side against a dark ground, with the models treated as ordinary working men. A picture of this size would have suited a private chapel or a collector's room.","i":"Peter and Paul were the twin pillars of the Roman Church, remembered together on 29 June. Peter, the fisherman who received the keys, is usually shown grey-haired and bearded, with keys; Paul, the learned apostle to the Gentiles, is bald and bearded, with a sword and a book. The pairing was popular in Counter-Reformation Rome as a statement of the Church's unity and authority. Caravaggio's followers gave such saints the faces of everyday models, which brought them close to ordinary viewers.","c":"Manfredi turned Caravaggio's lessons into a method that others could follow, and the half-length figures, dark ground and single raking light are the essence of it. The result is forceful and clear, though it lacks the spiritual depth of Caravaggio's own religious pictures: the saints are solid presences, not visions. As a record of the Caravaggesque idiom in Rome during the early 1620s it is eloquent, but it should be judged as a thoughtful variation, not an original statement."},"met:435682":{"d":"A small Madonna and Child, about 52 by 37 centimetres, painted in oil on wood between 1506 and 1518. The Met's keywords record a bird in the picture, so the Child probably holds or watches a small bird, perhaps a goldfinch, a symbol of the Passion. Boccaccino, from Cremona, painted gentle half-length Madonnas under the influence of Venetian painters such as Giovanni Bellini and Giorgione, with soft light and warm colour.","i":"The goldfinch, according to legend, took a thorn from Christ's crown and was marked with a drop of his blood, so a bird in the Child's hands is a quiet foreshadowing of the Passion in an image of tenderness. Small Madonnas of this kind were made for private devotion in the homes of north Italian merchants and nobles. Boccaccino was the leading painter of Cremona in the early sixteenth century and brought Venetian ideas to the city's art.","c":"Boccaccino's Madonnas have a gentle, glowing sweetness that owes much to Venice: soft light, warm colour and a calm, tender expression. The drawing can be soft, even weak, and the types are repeated from picture to picture. But within its small, devotional frame this is an attractive example of Lombard-Venetian painting of the years around 1510, and shows why he was esteemed in Cremona."},"nga:42941":{"d":"Two crowds of dozens of men clash on the dirt floor of an outdoor arena. The paint is loose, and most details are indistinct. Many men wear black caps. The group at the left surges near a flagpole; the group at the right eddies around a dark brown bull that plants its front feet. Swirls of buttercup yellow, scarlet, powder blue and white enliven both groups, and a man in white crouches on the ground in front of the bull. More spectators, painted in dabs of pearl white, rust red and ochre, fill the far side of the arena and beyond its walls.","i":"Goya's bullfight pictures and his Tauromaquia prints of 1816 gave Spanish painters a model for the loose, dramatic treatment of bull and crowd. Eugenio Lucas Velázquez was one of Goya's most devoted followers, and his son, Eugenio Lucas Villamil, carried that manner into the 1890s. The bullfight was both a national spectacle and a favorite subject for collectors who wanted something Spanish, and this rough, crowded arena seems closer to a popular fiesta than to a formal corrida.","c":"The handling is bold and loose, and flecks of yellow, red, blue and white keep a crowd alive, which is the Goya tradition at its most spirited. But it is an echo, a late performance of a manner already decades old, made when Spanish painting was moving in other directions, and the dissolving of detail gives energy but also vagueness. A lively but second-hand picture."},"nga:90155":{"d":"Fine black lines of paint and ink on a white ground make this seascape look at first glance like a print. The horizon lies about a quarter of the way up. The nearest ship angles into the distance right of center, its masts reaching three-quarters of the way up the panel, with another nearly alongside and a third near the left edge. Cannon poke from open ports, most sails are furled, and over a dozen smaller ships and rowboats recede into the distance, many flying striped flags. At the right, horse-drawn carriages wade between the beach and rowboats.","i":"This is a pen painting, a Dutch specialty in which the artist draws in ink on a prepared white panel with the detail of an engraving. Willem van de Velde the Elder was the best-known master of such pictures of ships, and collectors prized them as records of the fleet in the years of the Republic's naval power. The flags, gun ports and rigging are drawn with the accuracy that shipowners and naval officers wanted, and the signature floats on a board in the water.","c":"A virtuoso of line: each rope and spar is drawn, and dozens of vessels recede convincingly, giving the black-and-white image a crisp clarity that few oil painters could match. The price is color and atmosphere: compared with Dutch marine painters in oil, the effect is dry and diagrammatic. It is a feat of patience in an unusual medium, of more interest as documentation and technique than as mood."},"met:11006":{"d":"A small upright oil study on paper, about 31 by 24 centimeters, dated 1852. The title names Godesberg, the Rhineland town near Bonn known for the ruined castle on the hill above it. Trees are recorded among its elements. Oil on paper and a small size suit a sketch made on the spot, the kind of study painters gathered as raw material for larger pictures in the studio, and a modest, direct record of a place seen on a student's travels.","i":"James McDougal Hart, a Scottish-born American painter, studied in Düsseldorf in the early 1850s, where the academy trained a generation of American landscape painters in the careful study of nature and of the Rhine's romantic scenery. A sketch of 1852 comes from that stay. The Rhine, with its castle ruins and legends, was among the most visited landscapes in Europe, and for Americans it stood for old-world picturesqueness.","c":"A small study like this is judged as a sketch: its value lies in freshness and the honest recording of light and foliage. The Düsseldorf training gave Hart a firm sense of structure, which his later and larger landscapes, particularly his tranquil pictures of cattle and meadows, built on. It is a modest, charming record of a student's year abroad, and a window on how American painters learned their craft."},"met:11007":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper, about 24 by 33 centimeters, dated broadly to the first half of the 1850s. Mountains and trees are the recorded subjects, and the plain title names no place, so the picture reads as a study of landforms and atmosphere rather than a portrait of a named site. Oil on wove paper is a common support for a painter working outdoors, since it is light, cheap and quick to carry.","i":"American landscape painters of the mid-century were taught, by Asher B. Durand among others, to study mountain forms, rock and foliage directly from nature, and oil sketches on paper were the usual means. A study like this would feed larger compositions made later in the studio. Mountain scenery, from the Catskills and White Mountains to the Alps, was one of the great subjects of the age, and it gave American painters a national theme.","c":"Small studies of this kind often show a painter's eye more freely than his finished pictures, and a sketch of the early 1850s is a useful sign of the training that gave James McDougal Hart his later assurance with trees, hills and light. It is not a major work and makes no claim to be, but it has the honest freshness of looking. Its value is documentary rather than dramatic."},"met:11008":{"d":"A horizontal landscape of 1876, oil on canvas, about 51 by 86 centimeters, in which trees and cattle are the recorded elements. A pasture is the whole subject: grazing animals, shade and open grass, painted at the width of a long window. Cattle were the specialty for which James McDougal Hart became best known, and this canvas shows the type at its simplest.","i":"Hart came to specialize in tranquil pastures with cattle, a subject with roots in seventeenth-century Dutch painting and in the pastoral scenes of the French Barbizon school. In an America growing more urban and industrial, such scenes offered a comforting image of farm and field, and in 1876, the year of the Centennial, they could also stand for an older, rural idea of the nation. Cattle at rest were a ready emblem of peace and plenty.","c":"Hart is a sound, unshowy painter, and his best pictures have a calm sureness in the drawing of cattle and a pleasant softness in foliage. He is less poetic than Inness and less dramatic than Church, and a viewer may find his work comfortable and familiar. But that comfort is part of the appeal. A quiet, competent example of the later phase of the Hudson River School."},"met:13454":{"d":"A large landscape of 1887, oil on canvas, about 91 by 138 centimeters. The recorded elements are sky and clouds, trees, cattle and an artificial pond, a pastoral scene on a generous scale. The title points to changing light, shade that shifts as clouds pass or as the day moves on, and the wide format gives the cattle, water and sky room to share the view.","i":"A farm pond is a sign of managed, productive land, and cattle at water an old emblem of pastoral peace. By 1887 the American countryside was an object of nostalgia for an increasingly urban public, and pictures like this answered the feeling. Hart was by then one of the best-known American painters of cattle, and a canvas of this size was made for exhibition rather than for a corner of a parlor.","c":"By 1887 the taste for tonal landscape, Inness's kind, and for French Impressionism was changing American painting, and Hart's clear, detailed pastoral was beginning to look old-fashioned. As such it is a late, assured example of the Hudson River manner: calm, skilled and comforting, if not searching. Its large size and clear light are its strengths; the sentiment is its limit."},"met:436191":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 118 by 70 centimeters, painted in oil in 1849, showing Diana, the Roman goddess of the hunt, as a nude woman with a dog. The Met records a nude woman and a dog, so the picture is a single-figure subject in which the body and the hound carry the story. The vertical format gives the goddess the height of the canvas, and her setting was probably woodland, the natural home of the huntress and of Diaz's art.","i":"Diana, who bathed in forest pools and punished the hunter Actaeon for seeing her naked, is identified by her bow, crescent moon and hounds, and the dog here is the key to her identity. Painters used her as a pretext for a nude in a landscape, and mythological nudes of this kind were a staple of the Salon in the mid-nineteenth century, offering sensuous flesh under the cover of antiquity.","c":"Diaz is far better known for forests than for figures, and in his nudes he is at his best when the body melts into glowing color and foliage, as in Correggio, whom he admired. The picture should be judged on the softness of flesh and the richness of color; its weakness is a certain looseness of drawing, and the myth is little more than a pretext. A sensuous work, but secondary to his landscapes."},"met:436192":{"d":"A forest scene of autumn, painted in oil on canvas in 1867, about 50 by 66 centimeters. The title names a woodland pond, and the Met records autumn, forest and landscape among its subjects. A pond under tall trees is a place where water mirrors foliage and sky, so autumn color can glow against dark shade. The cabinet size and horizontal format suit a collector's wall.","i":"Diaz began his career decorating porcelain, and his forest pictures keep a jewel-like intensity of color that came from that training. The Forest of Fontainebleau, near Paris, was his chief subject, and its woodland pools were a favorite motif of the Barbizon painters, for whom a pond in autumn was a poetic image of nature's cycle, solitude and decay. The picture of 1867 comes late in a successful career.","c":"Diaz's forests glitter: where Rousseau is somber and Corot silvery, he sets flecks of warm color in dark shade, and the effect is decorative, sometimes at the expense of structure. A woodland pond in autumn gives him just the contrasts he liked, and a viewer can enjoy the sparkle without expecting depth of mood. Attractive, richly colored and slightly mannered."},"nga:111573":{"d":"A woodland scene fills the canvas, with dense trees massed on the right, their white and brown trunks twisting upward and their leaves in dark green, yellow ochre and rusty orange. At the lower left a small reflective pool lies behind dark stones, with a low green meadow of tall grass beyond. Farther off are more tall trees and a few white buildings with red roofs. The horizon appears at the left, under a blue sky with bright white clouds near the horizon and dark grey cloud in the upper left corner. At about 98 by 125 centimetres, it is a large landscape in the manner of the Barbizon school.","i":"Diaz was one of the Barbizon painters who made the forest of Fontainebleau, near Paris, a subject in its own right rather than a backdrop for stories. The title names the edge of the forest at Les Monts-Girard, so the picture records a place where woodland meets open ground and distant roofs, and a passing change of weather. By 1868 Diaz had long been successful, with a following among collectors for his richly coloured forest scenes.","c":"Diaz's usual glowing forest interior is here opened up to the sky: the twisting trunks on the right, with their rust and ochre foliage, are handled with the sureness of long habit, and the dark cloud at the upper left adds a touch of drama. The pool and stones at the lower left are somewhat perfunctory, and the picture lacks the focus of his best, jewel-toned woodland pictures. Solid and handsome, it reaffirms rather than extends his manner."},"met:436193":{"d":"A small forest scene on wood, about 38 by 47 centimeters, painted in 1872. The title and the recorded subjects, trees and forest, put the viewer at the margin where woodland meets open ground, a favorite position because it lets deep shade be set against brighter light. A panel support gives a smooth surface suited to the fine, quick touches of Diaz's late style.","i":"Forest edges, clearings and pools were the stage on which the Barbizon painters explored light, with shade within and sunshine beyond. By the 1870s Diaz had been painting around Fontainebleau for some forty years and produced very many small panels of this kind, which found a ready market in France and among American collectors. They were sold as poetic, portable pieces of countryside for the walls of a bourgeois home.","c":"Small panels are where Diaz is at his best: the touch is quick, the color jeweled, and the spontaneity can feel more convincing than in his larger exhibition canvases. The late panels can also be formulaic, the same sparkling foliage and shadowy glade turned out in quantity. A late panel such as this typically offers charm and ease, but little surprise."},"met:436195":{"d":"A small wooded view, painted in oil on a wooden panel in 1873, about 32 by 44 centimeters. The title promises a vista through trees at Fontainebleau, so the eye is led between trunks toward a more distant view, a framing device that gives even a small panel a sense of depth. The Met records trees and forest as its subjects.","i":"Framing a distance with foreground trees was a device used by landscape painters since Claude Lorrain, and the Barbizon painters adapted it to the rocky, wooded terrain of Fontainebleau, with its old oaks and sandy clearings. The forest, once a royal hunting ground, drew painters from the 1820s onward; in 1861 part of it was set aside as an artistic reserve to protect the trees they painted.","c":"A modest panel that shows the economy of Diaz's late manner: a few well-placed trunks, a lit opening, a spray of warm color, and the space is made. The risk is routine, and sparkle can substitute for observation. But a vista gives a picture a pull that plainer forest scenes sometimes lack, and the small scale is right for the effect. A pleasing, skilled trifle."},"met:436196":{"d":"A forest landscape painted in oil on wood in 1874, about 47 by 60 centimeters, two years before Diaz's death. The title names the great royal forest near Paris, and the Met records it as a forest landscape. The cabinet size makes it an easel picture for a collector's wall, and the subject is a stretch of woodland as a whole, in the dense, glittering manner Diaz had developed over four decades.","i":"Fontainebleau, about sixty kilometers southeast of Paris, was a royal hunting ground and, from the 1820s, the open-air studio of landscape painters who lodged in the nearby village of Barbizon, which gave the school its name. Diaz first worked there in the 1830s alongside Théodore Rousseau. For urban buyers a forest picture was a window onto an unspoiled nature that the railway had made an easy day trip.","c":"A late work, painted when Diaz could produce Fontainebleau at will, and it shows the typical strengths and limits of that fluency: richness of color and authority in the handling of foliage, against a certain repetition. At its best the manner conveys the enclosed, filtered light of the woods. A representative, rich and decorative picture, more reliable than surprising."},"nga:195825":{"d":"A path enclosed almost entirely by trees runs into a dense, sun-dappled forest in this horizontal landscape. The trunks, leaves, undergrowth and the near stretch of path are painted in shadowy ash brown, brick red and muted green, while sunlight from the left burnishes the middle-distance canopies to a muted gold and a patch of azure sky shows above them. Deep in shadow at the left, a woman in a dark garment and white cap sits huddled at the foot of a tree. The path darkens again as it recedes. The panel is signed and dated 1874.","i":"The huddled woman, perhaps a gatherer of wood or a traveller resting, is the only human note, and her smallness measures the forest. Such figures were a stock device of the Barbizon painters, who set peasants and wanderers in the woods to suggest that nature was larger and older than human affairs. The scene is probably not a topographical view; Diaz most likely built his forest pictures in the studio from memory and from sketches made on walks around Fontainebleau.","c":"The best part is the contrast between the dark, brooding foreground and the golden glow in the distance, which gives the large panel depth and a sense of the hour. The figure is so submerged in shadow that she hardly registers, and the brown foreground is heavy. Compared with Diaz's sparkling small panels, this larger forest interior is slower and more sombre, but it has the weight of a picture meant to hang."},"nga:35088":{"d":"A small panel, only about 32 by 44 centimetres, looks along a grassy path into a forest. Tall trees close overhead, and dense foliage and branches hide the sky. Rocks and shrubs dot the forest floor, and the path draws the eye back into the shade. The trunks are dark brown, with patches of white suggesting dappled sunlight falling through the branches. Leaves and ground are mostly dark green, with touches of brown, lighter green and yellow.","i":"Forest interiors like this were Diaz's trademark. He knew the forest of Fontainebleau intimately and made it his lifelong subject, and collectors valued the small panels as jewel-like pieces of nature. The path leading in, the dappled light and the dark mass of foliage are staples of the Barbizon school's picture of the forest as a place of retreat and quiet. Painted in 1874, two years before his death, it comes from the last stretch of his career.","c":"A little panel that does what Diaz did best: a few dark greens and a handful of white and yellow flecks suggest moving light under trees. The paint is quick and the effect convincing, if somewhat formulaic, since the same scheme recurs in dozens of his panels. It lacks the figure or incident that gives his larger woodland pictures a focus, but its economy is attractive, and it shows the spontaneous painting that encouraged the young Renoir."},"nga:195858":{"d":"Tall trees surround a small, still pond in this woodland scene. Olive green, rusty orange, yellow ochre and dark brown dominate the foliage and grassy ground, and some trunks glow white or pale grey where sunlight dapples them. The pond reflects the green leaves and the blue sky glimpsed through a break in the trees at the centre, under soft white clouds. On close inspection, a small indistinct shape that may be a person stands near the centre beside a tall tree, suggested by splashes of dark red, white and blue. The panel measures about 79 by 104 centimetres.","i":"The title names a modest woodland pool that Diaz, like other Barbizon painters, found as worthy a subject as any grand view. The tiny figure, barely more than three dabs of colour, supplies scale and a hint of life without a story. Painted in 1876, the year of the artist's death, the picture is among his last, and it carries his lifelong habit of turning a corner of the Fontainebleau forest into a place of jewel-like colour.","c":"A late summing-up of what Diaz did best: the pool gathers light in the middle of the dark woods, the reflected blue is a small jolt of freshness, and the colour is rich without turning garish. The figure is a scribble that adds scale but little else, and the foliage in places turns formulaic. Assured rather than inventive, it is affecting chiefly as a quiet close to a long career."},"met:436187":{"d":"A cavalry soldier on his horse is the subject of this narrow little panel, about 24 by 14 centimetres. The museum lists a horse, a soldier and equestrianism but gives no further detail, so the uniform, the horse's colour, the setting and the soldier's pose cannot be reported with confidence. Detaille was famous for the accuracy of his military costume and equipment, and a dragoon, a mounted soldier of the French line cavalry, would have been rendered down to buttons and straps.","i":"Édouard Detaille was the pupil of Ernest Meissonier, the master of tiny, exact military pictures, and as a young man he served in the Franco-Prussian War of 1870–71. After the French defeat, the army became a subject of national feeling, and Detaille's accurate paintings of soldiers, with their uniforms and drill, were popular with the public and with American collectors; this one reached the Metropolitan Museum from the collection of Martha Fiske Collord in 1908.","c":"A precise miniature of a cavalryman, in the Meissonier mould: Detaille's strengths are accuracy, finish and a dry, objective clarity, and his weaknesses are a certain coldness and a lack of imaginative reach. On a panel this small the detail can be dazzling, but the sentiment is polite rather than moving. His later large battle scenes show the same virtues and limits on a bigger stage."},"met:436188":{"d":"A large battle canvas, about 122 by 215 centimetres, shows French troops in the defence of Champigny, with the museum recording the battle of Villiers, soldiers, a cannon and a general among its subjects. The title and keywords place the scene in the fighting of late 1870 outside besieged Paris, but no further description is given, so the arrangement of the troops, the terrain and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The panoramic format is the standard one for a battle picture meant for public display.","i":"The battle of Villiers-Champigny, fought on the eastern edge of Paris from 30 November to 3 December 1870, was a French attempt to break the German siege of the capital; it failed after heavy losses, though the defenders held part of their ground. Detaille, who served as a soldier in the war and made his career on its memory, painted this in 1879, when memories of it were a subject of strong national feeling. Henry Hilton gave it to the Metropolitan Museum in 1887.","c":"A large, accurate and well-organised battle picture: Detaille was a master of uniform, equipment and formation, and a canvas of this size gives him room to show a whole army engaged. What is missing is the emotional force of the great history painters: the figures are many and carefully drawn, but the drama can feel staged, and the patriotism a little dutiful. Its value is as an image of how the French chose to remember a defeat."},"met:436189":{"d":"Mounted gendarmes on duty as orderlies are the subject of this canvas, about 56 by 42 centimetres, dated 1894. The museum lists a horse, a man, a dog and equestrianism, so the picture shows at least a mounted man and a dog, but no further detail is given, and the exact number of figures, the setting and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The title is a military term for gendarmes serving as orderlies, who carried messages and escorted officers.","i":"By 1894 Detaille was one of the most famous painters in France and the great illustrator of the army, producing pictures of manoeuvres and parades that fed a public taste for national pride in the years of revanche, the longing to avenge the defeat of 1870. A picture of gendarmes on horseback, with their splendid uniforms, belonged to that popular vein of military genre. It came to the Met in 1948 as a gift from the estate of George Albert Draper.","c":"A polished example of the military genre in which Detaille excelled: the horses, harness and uniforms are likely to be drawn with absolute fidelity, and the small canvas lets the detail be dense without looking busy. The weakness is the lack of anything beyond accuracy: the subject has no story and no inner life, and the effect is of a very good uniform plate. Excellent as a record of French army dress, cautious as a work of imagination."},"met:770595":{"d":"An upright panel, about 86 by 52 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood with a gold ground, about 1400. Christ hangs on the cross, with the grieving figures the Gospels place nearby: the Virgin Mary, Saint John the Evangelist and Mary Magdalene, and an angel is also recorded among the figures. The gold background, standard in painting of this date, turns Calvary into a timeless, sacred space instead of a particular hillside.","i":"John's Gospel places the Virgin and the beloved disciple at the foot of the cross, and the Gospels name Mary Magdalene among the women who watched. Angels hovering around the crucified Christ, often collecting his blood in chalices, were a late-medieval way of linking the sacrifice to the Eucharist. A panel of this shape and size was probably made for private devotion or a small chapel, to focus prayer on Christ's suffering and the sorrow of those who loved him.","c":"Stefano da Verona belongs to the International Gothic: courtly elegance, delicate line, rich pattern and a fondness for tender detail. The gold and refinement soften the drama of a painful subject, and a critic might find the effect more graceful than harrowing. He ranks below Gentile da Fabriano and Pisanello, but he is a distinctive painter of exquisite surfaces, and a Crucifixion like this shows the courtly taste of northern Italy around 1400."},"nga:202761":{"d":"At the open door of a black-and-white marble entryway, a richly dressed child in plum-purple sleeves and a green-lined cape drops a silver coin into the hat of a ragged boy, while holding the hand of a nurse in a scarlet bodice and white collar. Beside the door a tanned woman nurses a baby, and two small children look down at coins on the step. A small white dog looks up. In the room beyond, a man and woman look on beneath a landscape painting and a tall mantle. Light pours through the door. Signed and dated 1663.","i":"The picture is a scene of charity. Giving alms to the poor was a Christian duty stressed in Dutch society, and children were taught it early. Ochtervelt sets the lesson in a very Dutch way, with the contrast of rich and poor across a threshold, marble floor against bare step, silks against rags. The man and woman in the room beyond, perhaps the child's parents, watch approvingly, so the gesture is also a display of the household's virtue.","c":"Ochtervelt stands comparison with Pieter de Hooch here: the receding marble floor, the doorway and the light flooding through it make a persuasive space, and the child's silks and the nurse's scarlet bodice are painted with relish. The social contrast is handled without sermonizing, which lifts the picture above a moral tale. The weakness is a slightly sugary child and a staged air: the poor look picturesque. Still, a well-organized and humane picture."},"cma:155941":{"d":"A small interior scene, painted in oil on canvas about 1668, about 59 by 49 centimeters. A company of figures makes music, with a recorder and a violin played without scores, and a row of female portraits hangs on the background wall. The figures are calm and elegantly arranged, so at first glance it is a refined domestic concert, but the Cleveland Museum reads it as probably a brothel interior.","i":"The clues are the portraits and the music. A row of female portraits is rarely found in other pictures of the period. The recorder and violin, played without scores, were thought cruder than the elegant, complicated music-making in the polite households of Pieter de Hooch, and were associated with prostitution. Dutch genre painters often hid such meanings under polite surfaces and expected viewers to read the signs, so a calm surface does not rule out a double meaning.","c":"The surface of politeness over a hint of vice is the subject, and Ochtervelt handles it with tact: the calm demeanor and silky finish make the double meaning more insinuating than an openly bawdy scene would be. It succeeds as a clever puzzle and as polished genre painting in the vein of Metsu or Ter Borch. Its weakness is that it needs explaining; a modern viewer sees elegance first, and the meaning only afterward."},"met:437227":{"d":"A woman in a dressing gown and cap, a hairdresser, a letter, a dog, a patterned tablecloth and a shoe are all recorded in this picture, painted in oil on canvas in the early 1670s, about 91 by 64 centimeters. Together they point to a lady's toilette interrupted by a letter, an intimate moment in a well-furnished room. The tall, upright format is that of a substantial picture for a wealthy household.","i":"In Dutch genre painting a letter signaled love, and a woman reading one while being dressed is a familiar scene of courtship and its small anxieties. The dog can stand for fidelity, the slipper or shoe often carried an erotic hint, and a dressing gown implied intimacy. Painters including Vermeer, Metsu and Ter Borch made letter pictures popular in the 1660s and 1670s, and viewers were expected to read the clues and guess the story.","c":"In his later years in Amsterdam Ochtervelt's manner grew smoother and more fashionable, and satin, lace and elegant furnishings take over from the plainer interiors of his early work. The polish is delightful but cool, and the story remains a puzzle of signs rather than a felt emotion. It does not match the psychological depth of Vermeer or Ter Borch, but as sophisticated society genre it is a pleasure."},"nga:42921":{"d":"This is an oil portrait of a single man on a vertical canvas about 92 by 72 centimeters, close to the English 'kit-cat' size of 36 by 28 inches, a half-length format that normally shows the head, torso and a hand. The title names the sitter as Captain Robert Calder, an officer of the Royal Navy, and the picture dates from about 1787 to 1790, when Calder held that rank. It is typical work for Lemuel Francis Abbott, a London portraitist who painted many naval men, Nelson among them, in plain, direct likenesses.","i":"Captains were the working elite of the Georgian navy, and many sat for portraits that recorded rank and marked a stage in a career. Calder is remembered chiefly for the Battle of Cape Finisterre in 1805, after which, by then an admiral, he was reprimanded at a court martial for not renewing the fight. A portrait made some fifteen years earlier knows nothing of that: it belongs to the ordinary, confident business of naval portraiture, in which uniform stood for service and standing.","c":"Abbott was valued for candid, rather literal likenesses, not for rhetoric. Set beside the swagger of Reynolds or the polish of Lawrence, his portraits are plainer fare, and the best of them, such as his portrait of Nelson, have a directness that more fashionable work lacks. A picture of this kind rewards attention to the face and little else, and it will please viewers who prefer honest record to flattery."},"nga:118598":{"d":"Twelve people sit and stand along a curving stone bench as a thirteenth, kneeling, washes the foot of a seated man at the center of the group, in this horizontal panel of about 36 by 52 cm. Jesus, in profile, with shoulder-length blond ringlets and a pointed beard, wears a red gold-trimmed robe, and the white-bearded seated man looks down at him with a hand flat on his chest. The robes are ruby red, green, sky blue, lilac and orange, and all but one man have faint gold halos.","i":"The scene is from the Gospel of John, in which Jesus washes the disciples' feet at the Last Supper as a lesson in humble service, and Peter, the white-bearded man, protests before accepting. The man without a halo, sitting at lower right and pointing toward Jesus, with a pouch at his belt, is Judas, the betrayer, who carried the common purse. The scene was the basis of the Maundy Thursday rite in which rulers and bishops washed the feet of the poor.","c":"Garofalo has absorbed Raphael's lessons of calm, frieze-like grouping, and the figures along the bench are clear and unhurried, with bright robes giving the little panel an enamel-like charm. The drama is gentle: the gestures of Peter and Judas are the only strong notes. The Ferrarese taste for bright color and precise finish remains, and the picture may lack the depth of the Roman masters, but it is a graceful, clear piece of narrative."},"met:436443":{"d":"In a bedroom, the Augustinian friar Saint Nicholas of Tolentino is the central figure in a long, low panel of about 33 by 66 cm, painted about 1530 and later transferred from wood to canvas. The subjects include birds, a bed and a monk, and the picture illustrates the saint's miracle of the birds. A pair at the Met has the same size and date, and both were probably predella panels of an altarpiece for an Augustinian church.","i":"Nicholas of Tolentino (about 1245–1305) was an Augustinian friar famed for his charity and miracles. In the legend, when he was ill, he was served roast birds, and after he blessed them they came back to life and flew away, a story of austerity and holy power. Small narrative panels along the base of an altarpiece, the predella, told such miracles in anecdote form for the faithful who stood close.","c":"Garofalo works as a storyteller in a small format, with a Raphaelesque clarity learned in Rome and a Ferrarese love of bright color, and a bedroom scene with a saint and birds is warmly domestic. The transfer from wood to canvas has probably cost some of the original surface's subtlety, so the picture is better enjoyed for its design than for its finish. It is a charming, clear and slightly plain narrative panel."},"met:436444":{"d":"A woman, a man and the Augustinian saint Nicholas of Tolentino appear in this long, low panel of about 33 by 65 cm, painted about 1530 and later transferred from wood to canvas. The subjects include an infant, and the title says that the saint revives a child. It is a pendant of the Met's panel of the miracle of the birds, with the same size and date, and both were probably predella scenes from an altarpiece.","i":"Nicholas of Tolentino (about 1245–1305) was credited with many miracles, among them the raising of the dead, and the inquiries for his canonization recorded many such cases involving children. A parent who brought a dead child to a saint was acting on faith, and the revival was proof of a saint's holiness. Predella scenes like this showed the saint's power at close range to worshippers who stood at the altar, in a style easy to follow.","c":"As a pendant, the panel balances its companion, but a scene of a mother and a revived child asks for more feeling than a miracle of birds, and Garofalo gives clarity and tact more than anguish. The Raphaelesque grouping is orderly and the color bright, though the transfer to canvas has probably softened the surface. It is a graceful, rather placid narrative, a modest but well-made piece of Ferrarese devotional storytelling."},"nga:42411":{"d":"A family group fills a wide canvas of about 119 by 161 centimetres, dated 1804. The museum's listing gives only the type, a family portrait, so the number of sitters, their poses, clothes and setting cannot be described with confidence. A horizontal format of this size was well suited to a group of parents and children gathered in a parlour or garden, as American painters of the early republic often arranged them.","i":"The artist, Ralph E. W. Earl, was the son of the portraitist Ralph Earl and later became best known as the painter and companion of President Andrew Jackson. His early work shows the plain, crisp style of the New England portrait tradition his father had practised. Family groups recorded a household for posterity, and the sitters here are not named. If the dates are right, the painter was a teenager in 1804, which makes the work an early performance.","c":"Without detail to examine, it is best to judge by the tradition it belongs to: the American family group of the early republic is typically formal, flat and sincere, more concerned with documenting a household than with psychology. If this is indeed the work of a teenager, its competence is notable, though it can hardly show the sophistication the younger Earl later gained in London. The charm of such pictures is their candour."},"met:10836":{"d":"A portrait of a woman named Mrs. Williams, painted in oil on canvas in 1837, about 95 by 77 centimeters. The title gives only her married name. The size is that of a substantial family portrait, painted for display in a parlor. The painter, Ralph E. W. Earl, was the son of the portraitist Ralph Earl and lived for years at Andrew Jackson's Hermitage near Nashville, where he served as the president's painter.","i":"In 1837 a painted portrait was still the only way to keep a likeness; the daguerreotype was announced in 1839. Portrait painters like Earl served a growing class of families who wanted a record of their members, and such pictures were hung at home as memorials of face, dress and standing. The sitter's story is not told in the title, so her dress, hair and bearing are the clues to how she wished to be seen.","c":"Earl's portraits are direct, sober and a little stiff, in the plain, firm manner of provincial American portraiture; they lack the polish of Thomas Sully or the authority of Gilbert Stuart. Their value lies in honest observation, and in showing how respectable Americans of the 1830s wished to appear: dignified, plain and unadorned. A modest, serious piece by a painter chiefly remembered for his portraits of Jackson."},"met:12552":{"d":"A woman and a girl absorbed in reading, painted in oil on canvas in 1895, about 87 by 114 centimeters. The title, given in English and French, evokes a book of animal stories, perhaps Rudyard Kipling's recent Jungle Book, so the subject is a quiet scene of reading aloud, with the book at its center rather than any event. The picture sits between portrait and genre scene, a moment of shared reading recorded for its tenderness.","i":"James Jebusa Shannon, born in the United States and settled in London, became a fashionable portraitist of the 1890s, painting society beauties and their children with a soft, silky touch recalling Gainsborough and Van Dyck. A picture of reading to a child answers to the Victorian cult of domesticity and of childhood, and to the boom in illustrated children's books. The exotic jungle of the title is set against the safety of home.","c":"Shannon's strengths are fluency, silky texture and agreeable color, and the picture charms. It is also sweet and safe, a society painter's idea of childhood, and it lacks the bite of Sargent's portraits. As a document of fashionable taste around 1895 and the Victorian love of stories read aloud, it is persuasive; as painting it is easy rather than searching."},"met:437259":{"d":"A camp scene, painted in oil on canvas about 1725, about 54 by 65 centimeters, the same size and date as Troops on the March. The recorded subjects are soldiers, men and women, a tent, a tree, horses and a donkey, so the picture shows an army at halt, with the rough comforts of camp: shelter, animals and company. Pater paints such scenes as small-scale pastorals in which soldiers rest instead of fight.","i":"Rest after the march is an old theme, and in Watteau's tradition a halt in camp lets soldiers, women and animals mingle in an easy, open-air setting. The donkey and tent add homely touches. The picture draws on the armies of the War of the Spanish Succession, which ended in 1714 and which many buyers still remembered a decade later; the war had become a source of anecdote rather than fear.","c":"As a pendant to the march, the camp scene is the calmer and more pictorial of the two: it lets Pater do what he does best, a loose grouping of figures under trees. The soldiers are costume figures more than portraits of men at war, and a critic would call the result charming and light, a little thin. The pair shows how quickly Watteau's inventions became fashionable decoration."},"met:437260":{"d":"A scene of soldiers on the move, painted in oil on canvas about 1725, about 54 by 65 centimeters. The Met records a woman, a man, a soldier and horsemanship among its subjects, so riders and foot soldiers share the picture with women, who often appear in such scenes as wives and camp followers. It is a cabinet-sized view of army life, to be studied closely rather than seen from across a room.","i":"Military subjects of this sort go back to Watteau, who painted small scenes of troops on the march and at rest around 1710, drawn from the War of the Spanish Succession. Pater, his pupil, repeated and prettified them. Troops on the March and Troops at Rest form a pair, two moments in the life of an army. The pictures show soldiering as picturesque, with no battle and no suffering, a gentle version of war that suited collectors of the Régence.","c":"Pater turns Watteau's observant soldiers into an agreeable decoration: the lines of figures make a pleasing movement, and the colors are light and well chosen. What is lost is the edge of Watteau, who let weariness and uncertainty show on his soldiers' faces. A critic would value the grace and the economy, and note that the pair works better as decoration than as a statement about war."},"nga:32697":{"d":"Six women, four men, two children and a dog sit or stand in a loose line in a misty forest, painted in hazy tones of mossy green and earthy brown. A copse fills the left two-thirds, with a fountain sculpture twice human height, where a mostly nude woman and child sit on a curving basin. In front, couples flirt: a man crowns a seated woman with flowers, another pair embrace, a woman half-opens a fan above a kneeling man, and a smiling woman looks out from the lower left. Two children play with a dog, and more people stand in the distance.","i":"This is a fête champêtre, the country-party variant of the fête galante, the genre Watteau created, in which elegantly dressed men and women flirt and play at leisure in a park. The statue of a mother and child, probably Venus and Cupid, presides over the scene as a hint of love's patronage. The flower crown, the embrace and the half-opened fan are conventional signals of courtship, and the whole was a dream of aristocratic leisure for patrons of the Régence and early Louis XV years.","c":"Pater was Watteau's pupil and follower, and this is the genre in the hands of a lighter, more decorative talent: the misty greens and browns, the blended distances and the rhythm of couples are charming, and the colors are carefully harmonized. What is missing is Watteau's undercurrent of melancholy and the subtlety of his figures; Pater's party is pretty but little else. He died at forty; this is a good example of his easy, graceful gift."},"nga:43320":{"d":"An outdoor gathering in a pastoral landscape. A group in soft, flowing garments, many in bright dresses and shawls, congregates under tall leafy trees, many seated on the ground. A pastel-blue sky and a serene green landscape stretch to a distant horizon. At the far left appears the edge of a staircase or building, where a woman leans with her back to the viewer. Oil on canvas, about 72 by 100 centimeters, from about 1730 to 1735.","i":"A terrace is the boundary between the formal house and the garden, a platform for conversation and flirtation, and a natural setting for the fête galante, the genre Watteau created, in which elegantly dressed men and women spend a day at leisure in a park. The picture shows an idle party spread on the grass, in dress that mixes contemporary fashion with the loose, theatrical costume of the genre. For patrons of the Régence and early reign of Louis XV, such pictures were a dream of aristocratic leisure.","c":"A late work by Pater, who died in 1736, with the loose, feathery handling and bright costume color that make him an easy painter to like. The group has a gentle rhythm, and the pastel sky and green distance are pleasing. But the composition is slack and the whole is more decorative than substantial, lacking the poetic ache Watteau gave to such scenes. A pleasant, soft, easy picture."},"met:437258":{"d":"A tiny panel, about 16 by 23 centimeters, painted in oil on wood. Children are at play: a boy and a girl, a dog and a toy are recorded among its subjects, set beneath trees. The title evokes the legendary age of innocence and ease described by ancient poets, so the picture presents children's games as a small paradise. A panel of this size was meant to be held or hung close, in a private cabinet.","i":"In classical myth, as told by Hesiod and Ovid, the Golden Age was a time of peace and plenty when people lived without labor or strife. In eighteenth-century French art, children at play became an image of that innocence, which let adult buyers smile at a time before cares. Pater painted pictures on this scale for cabinet collectors, little visions of pastoral ease that match the taste of the Régence for charm and lightness.","c":"Pater's best small panels have a feathery sparkle and a dainty color sense, and this scale suits his gifts. The risk is sweetness; the picture is slight, charming and quickly seen. A critic would call it a pleasant trifle, not a statement, though it shows why Régence taste took to him: grace, ease and no demands on the viewer."},"met:437257":{"d":"A large outdoor scene, about 107 by 142 centimeters, painted in oil about 1733, set at the fair of Bezons, a village on the Seine near Paris. Dancing is the center of attention: the recorded subjects include dancers, a crowd of men and women, trees and a dog. At this scale it is one of Pater's more ambitious canvases, a festival in which different ranks of society could mingle in the open air.","i":"Pater, Watteau's pupil, turns the courtly fête galante toward the popular fair, a setting where different ranks of society danced and flirted. Parisians went out to fairs on the city's outskirts for a day's pleasure, and the subject has a long ancestry in the Flemish kermesse of Rubens and Teniers. The picture belongs to a taste of the 1730s for rustic entertainments seen with a courtly eye, in which the country is a stage for polite pleasure.","c":"A busy, accomplished and decorative picture. Pater's color is pleasing and his figures slim and lively, and the large format gives him room for a crowd with a gentle rhythm of dance. But the crowd is an arrangement of charming types more than a study of people, and the effect is lightness rather than depth. As a vision of French leisure it delights, if it stops short of Watteau's best."},"met:437256":{"d":"A musical party in the open air, painted in oil on canvas about 1734, about 52 by 68 centimeters. The Met records a woman, a man, a child and musicians with their instruments, set in a landscape, so the picture shows a small company gathered for a country concert, with music as the occasion for conversation and courtship. The cabinet size and horizontal format suit a private room, where the soft costume colors and the park setting could be enjoyed at close range.","i":"Music is the classic companion of love in the fête galante, the genre created by Watteau and carried on by Pater. The title Concert champêtre echoes the Louvre's famous Pastoral Concert, attributed to Giorgione or Titian, which the French knew well and which had inspired earlier painters of outdoor music. A concert in a park lets idle, well-dressed figures listen, flirt and dream, and a child among the company gives the party the air of an outing.","c":"Pater's gift is for light, gracious groupings and a feathery touch, and a musical subject is well matched to it: figures and instruments can make a gentle rhythm across the canvas. But he never reaches Watteau's depth. His lovers are charming costume figures, and the mood is pleasant rather than moving. A late work, since he died in 1736, in which skill outruns feeling."},"met:436058":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman, painted on a thin plate of ivory, possibly around 1780. The sitter is not named. Miniatures of this kind were usually no larger than the palm of a hand, made to be worn as jewelry, held in a case or kept as a keepsake, and the ivory gives flesh tones a soft, translucent glow that oil on canvas cannot match. The picture is a private image, meant to be looked at close up and alone.","i":"Portrait miniatures were the intimate counterpart of the grand oil portrait: exchanged between lovers and family members, worn as a locket or bracelet, and treasured after the sitter was gone. In Britain in the later eighteenth century the art flourished, with Richard Cosway and others making miniature painting a fashionable profession. Richard Crosse, who was deaf and mute from childhood, was one of the successful practitioners of the day.","c":"Crosse's miniatures are admired for clarity and delicate finish rather than flamboyance. Where Cosway turned his sitters into elegant, airy dreams, Crosse stays closer to a plain likeness, with careful handling of the ivory that gives faces a gentle glow. A miniature of this kind lives by the face and the details of costume; with an unnamed sitter, it asks to be admired for its presentation, not her story."},"met:437497":{"d":"A large canvas, about 180 by 121 centimeters, painted on both sides in distemper and perhaps oil: the Flagellation on the front and the Madonna of Mercy on the reverse. A double-sided picture of this shape and material is typical of a processional banner, carried through the streets by a religious confraternity. The Flagellation shows Christ bound and beaten before his Crucifixion; on the reverse, the Madonna of Mercy shelters believers beneath her outspread cloak.","i":"Confraternities, lay brotherhoods devoted to prayer and charity, carried banners in processions on feast days and in times of plague or hardship. The Flagellation recalls Christ's suffering, which brotherhoods devoted to penance took as a model, while the Madonna of Mercy, who spreads her cloak over her protégés, offered the members hope of protection and intercession. Romanino was active in Brescia, where confraternities were important patrons, and the two faces served different moments of the procession.","c":"Romanino was a Brescian painter whose earthy, energetic manner, with warm color and expressive, sometimes rough figures, set him apart from the more polished Venetians. A processional banner demands bold, legible figures that read at a distance, and it is a rare survival, since such objects were carried, folded and worn. Whatever its condition, it shows the physical drama he brought to popular religious art."},"met:14305":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1840 and only about 9 by 7 centimeters, small enough to sit in the palm of a hand. The title gives her married name, with her maiden name, Frances Sargeant, in parentheses. It matches in size the miniature of her husband, Christopher Grant Perry, by the same painter, and the two were probably made as a pair. The slight translucence of the ivory gives the flesh a soft glow.","i":"A miniature like this was a family treasure: a likeness small enough to carry or wear, made for a husband, a child or a keepsake box. Around 1840 the portrait miniature was at the peak of its popularity in America, just before the daguerreotype, announced in 1839, began to replace it. The pair of Mr. and Mrs. Perry shows how a married couple chose to be remembered together, in matching format, as a unit of the family.","c":"Richard Morrell Staigg was among the best American miniaturists of his day, known for a smooth, delicate finish and a sweet, graceful way with women's faces. The risk of that sweetness is idealization: sitters become gentle types, and individuality can fade. At this size, however, grace and finish are exactly what is wanted. A well-made, charming example of the art at its last high point before photography."},"met:14310":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1840, about 9 by 7 centimeters. The title gives only his name, Christopher Grant Perry, and its size matches the miniature of Mrs. Perry by the same painter, so the two form a married pair. Watercolor is applied in fine strokes and stipples over the white ivory plate, which shows through the thin color and lends the face a luminous quality.","i":"In the 1840s a man's miniature was commissioned for a few reasons: to mark an engagement or marriage, to give to a parent or child, or to serve as a keepsake for a family member who traveled. Unlike a full-size portrait, a miniature was private, held in the hand or kept in a case with a clasp, for looking at alone and up close. The painter, Richard Morrell Staigg, was born in England and built his career in New England.","c":"Men's fashion of the 1840s offered a dark coat and white linen, leaving the face to do the work, and the success of a male miniature is in the modeling and expression. Staigg's fine touch and tidy finish suit the job. The result is more dignified than lively, as formal portrait miniatures of the period tend to be. A solid, careful likeness of the kind that built his reputation."},"met:12634":{"d":"A portrait miniature of the painter Washington Allston, in watercolor on ivory, dated 1841, about 9 by 7 centimeters. Allston, then in his early sixties, was the most admired American Romantic painter of his generation, and the younger painter who portrayed him knew him. The picture records the senior artist two years before his death, at close range and at the size of a pocket keepsake.","i":"Washington Allston (1779-1843) was the leading Romantic painter of the early United States, known for dreamy landscapes, biblical and literary subjects, and a long, unfinished masterwork, Belshazzar's Feast. In Boston and Cambridge he was treated almost as a sage by younger artists and writers. A portrait of him by a younger artist is a kind of tribute, and the choice to record him in miniature gives it the intimacy of a personal relic.","c":"Portraits of famous men tend to be judged by whether they catch the man behind the reputation, and a miniature leaves little room for it. What Staigg can do here is deliver a firm, delicate likeness and some sense of the sitter's thoughtful presence, and the picture is valuable as an image of Allston in his later years. It is a respectful record more than a searching characterization, and it shows Staigg at his most serious."},"met:12632":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1849, about 11 by 9 centimeters, noticeably larger than the earlier Perry miniatures. The title gives her married name and, in parentheses, her maiden name, Amalia Heredia. It matches the miniature of her husband, George Henry Loring, by the same painter in date and near size, so the two were made as a pair.","i":"By 1849 the daguerreotype was widespread and cheap, and miniature painters had to offer what a photograph could not: color, flattery and craftsmanship. A larger miniature carried more detail, with room for lace, jewelry and the texture of a dress. Staigg, who by then had a strong reputation, would later turn largely to oil painting, so a miniature of this date comes from the last great decade of the art.","c":"The larger format gives Staigg more room, and the finish is correspondingly smooth. A woman's miniature of the late 1840s asks for refinement and charm, and Staigg supplied both with a deft touch. The danger is blandness: the faces of his women tend toward a similar, soft prettiness. Even so, the technical control, the fine, even strokes that build flesh tones on ivory, is a pleasure to look at."},"met:12633":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1849, about 12 by 9 centimeters. It is the companion of the miniature of his wife, Mrs. George Henry Loring, by the same painter, and the two are similar in size and date. At roughly 11 to 12 centimeters high, a miniature of this size could hold a bust-length figure with some detail, though it was still meant to be held in the hand or kept in a case.","i":"A pair of husband and wife kept as a unit makes sense of the format: each portrait is incomplete without its partner, and together they record a marriage. In the 1840s a pair of miniatures was a typical commission for a wedding or an anniversary, and families kept them in cases or framed on a parlor table. As photography grew, the market for such pictures shrank, so a pair of 1849 belongs to the last great age of the American miniature.","c":"As a male portrait it concentrates on the face: modeling by fine strokes, with the translucent ivory showing through the thin color. Staigg is a skilled but conservative practitioner, and his men are dignified and a little uniform. The picture works as a courteous, finished likeness. Like its companion, it belongs to the last great decade of the art, and its value is as a record of craft and of a sitter."},"met:15190":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman named Ann King, painted in watercolor on ivory, about 11 by 9 centimeters. No date is recorded, so it cannot be placed precisely within Staigg's career. The title gives her name alone, and the ivory plate, thin and slightly translucent, gives the face a soft inner light that the painter exploits by keeping the color thin and the strokes fine.","i":"With only a name to go on, Ann King is known as a face and a costume, as are most sitters of miniatures today: the stories that once made such portraits precious, whom they were made for, when and why, have been lost. Miniatures survive because families cherished them, and many were later given to museums with their names intact but their histories forgotten. That loss is part of the poignancy of the form.","c":"Without a date or a story, the miniature is judged on its craft: the evenness of the flesh tones, the delicacy of the features, the economy of the costume. Staigg's strengths, grace and finish, are the right ones for the form. His women sometimes verge on a prettiness that smooths over individuality, and this may be an example. A pleasant, accomplished piece, and a reminder that most miniatures survive as faces without histories."},"met:10881":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Sharp Delany, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1799, only about 7 by 6 centimeters, a size that fits in a closed hand. The title gives his name and nothing else. Painted in the last year of the eighteenth century, it belongs to the Federal period of American portraiture, when miniatures were exchanged among families of the young republic, and its tiny scale means that the face and a little of the costume must carry everything.","i":"Robert Field was born in England and came to the United States in the 1790s, when a new republic of merchants, officials and lawyers wanted portraits to record its leading families. Miniatures were the affordable, portable form: private tokens for spouse, child or friend. Field's clear, crisp style suited the sober taste of the American Federal era, and he became one of the best-known miniaturists in the young country.","c":"Field's miniatures are clear, linear and plain, closer to a sharply drawn likeness than to the dreamy elegance of the English school from which he came; sitters look directly out, with no attempt to idealize. That frankness is the charm. The finish is skillful, and at this tiny size the economy of means is impressive. The limitation is a certain stiffness, which suited Federal taste."},"met:10882":{"d":"A portrait miniature of George Washington, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1800, about 8 by 6 centimeters. The date falls just after Washington's death in December 1799, when demand for his likeness was immense. A miniature like this was probably made from an existing portrait rather than from life, since the standard image of the first president circulated through paintings and prints, above all those by Gilbert Stuart.","i":"After Washington died in December 1799, Americans mourned him with processions, orations and an outpouring of memorial objects: engravings, medals, mourning jewelry and portraits. Miniatures of the hero were worn or displayed as patriotic keepsakes, and artists such as Field supplied them, often following Stuart's famous image of the president. A small likeness of Washington was a private relic of the nation's founder.","c":"A miniature of Washington is judged less on invention than on faithfulness to the national icon, and Field delivers a clear, sober version in keeping with his plain style. It lacks the soft modeling of Stuart's original, which no small watercolor could match, but it has the virtue of directness. As patriotic memorabilia it is of more interest for what it meant to its owner than for what it adds to Washington's iconography."},"met:14402":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named Charles Goldsborough, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1802, about 7 by 6 centimeters. The title names the sitter and nothing more. Miniatures of this kind usually show the head and shoulders, with the face at the center, and the careful modeling of a face a few centimeters high depends on very fine, short strokes of the brush laid over the white of the ivory.","i":"Portrait miniatures in the early republic served families of means and rising officials; such a likeness might be commissioned at a marriage, on a departure for a distant post, or to console a bereaved family. Field worked in the cities of the Mid-Atlantic states, where he found sitters among merchants and officeholders. The date places the portrait in the early years of Jefferson's presidency.","c":"Field's faces are firmly drawn and honest; he does not flatter or soften, and that candor gives his sitters a gravity that fits the republic's self-image. The weakness is a certain monotony: his miniatures can look alike in pose and treatment, and the costumes are plain. A reliable, unfussy work from a competent craftsman."},"met:15058":{"d":"A portrait miniature of an unnamed woman, painted in watercolor on ivory about 1800, about 8 by 7 centimeters. Her dress is recorded in some detail: a lace fichu, a light scarf draped around the neck and shoulders, and a ribbon at her neck, which together suggest the soft, simple fashion of the years around 1800. The ivory plate and the fine strokes of watercolor give the face and the white lace a delicate, translucent quality.","i":"Fashion around 1800 favored the soft, high-waisted look inspired by classical antiquity, with light muslin and lace at the neckline instead of the heavy silks and powdered hair of earlier decades. A fichu and a neck ribbon were everyday elements of a woman's best dress, and in a portrait they show that she cared to be seen as modest, tasteful and up to date. Such a miniature might be exchanged at an engagement or kept after a marriage.","c":"The finest passage in a miniature of this type is usually the white lace, which shows how the ivory itself can serve as the brightest highlight, and Field's crisp, linear touch is well suited to it. The face is serene and plain, in his frank manner, with little attempt at the dreamy elegance of English miniatures. Charming, well observed and modest: a good example of the American Federal miniature at its simplest."},"met:10884":{"d":"A portrait miniature of Robert Gilmor Sr., painted in watercolor on ivory in 1804, about 9 by 7 centimeters. The title adds Sr. to distinguish him from his son of the same name, the Baltimore collector Robert Gilmor Jr., who became one of the first American patrons of landscape painting. The miniature shows the father in the form that suited a merchant family: small, private and portable.","i":"The Gilmors were a merchant family of Baltimore, and the son used the family's wealth to collect art, a sign of how early American patronage grew out of commerce. A miniature of the father in 1804 records a successful businessman in the years of Federal-era trade. The family's attitude to pictures, as records of status and as sources of pleasure, marks the early American art market.","c":"A businessman's miniature rests on an honest, firm likeness, and Field gives that: the head is drawn with care and the treatment is plain and without flourish, which suits the sitter's trade. As art it is modest. As a record of a patron's father, it is a pleasant footnote to the history of American collecting, and a reminder of how much of that history began with small, private commissions."},"met:10885":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a man named Robert Stuart, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1804, about 7 by 6 centimeters, smaller than a playing card. The title gives his name and nothing else, so he comes down to the viewer as a face and a costume. At this size every stroke counts: the artist works with a brush of a few hairs, building the face from tiny touches over the bare white of the ivory.","i":"Ivory for miniatures is cut into thin, flat sheets and bleached, and the painter uses transparent watercolor so that the white beneath glows through the flesh tones; mistakes are hard to correct, which gave a skilled miniaturist a reputation for sure hand. Looking closely at such a picture, a viewer can see the stipple and fine hatching that shape the cheek and brow. The craft is the point as much as the sitter.","c":"A critic looking at the work of a skilled but not inspired craftsman would find clarity and consistency here, and a modest charm. The portrait does not try to flatter, which may be why it has survived with its honesty intact. Compared with the lushness of English miniatures it is spare, and it is best valued for its precision and for its small, steady sense of a real person."},"met:10886":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman named Sarah Louisa Jenkins, painted in watercolor on ivory in 1804, about 8 by 7 centimeters. The title gives her full name, which is a rarity among miniatures, since so many survive anonymously. A portrait this size, in a gilt case or locket, would have been kept close, perhaps to mark a betrothal, a marriage or a family gift, though the picture itself records no occasion.","i":"Young women's miniatures in the early republic often marked a courtship or marriage, a way to give a loved one a face to hold while apart. A full name on the museum record means that she can still be traced in the history of the family, even if her story is not told in the title. The portrait preserves her as she wished to be seen in 1804: neat, composed and presentable.","c":"Field's portraits of women are clear and unaffected: a plain face drawn with sympathy, in costume that does not try to impress. The simplicity can look severe beside the soft, romantic women of English miniatures, but it matches American taste, which prized sincerity over elegance. A small, honest and sympathetic work, and a good counterpart to his portraits of the men of the republic."},"met:11654":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 25 by 36 centimeters, recorded as existing by 1894. A woman and a child are the figures, and fortune-telling is the subject: the woman is presumably telling the child's fortune, perhaps by reading the palm. Newman painted such small, imaginary scenes with glowing, jewel-like color, and the picture is a cabinet piece that asks to be examined at close range.","i":"Fortune tellers, usually Gypsies, were a favorite theme of European painting from Caravaggio onward, and of Romantic painting in the nineteenth century, when they stood for mystery, fate and a free life outside society. Newman's version, with a child as client, gives the theme a gentler, more wistful turn: what does the future hold for the very young? He is often grouped with American visionary painters such as Albert Pinkham Ryder, who also painted imagined scenes in glowing color.","c":"Newman's pictures stand apart from the landscape and realism that dominated American painting of his day: small, dark and richly colored, with a dreamlike glow recalling Delacroix and the Venetians. This one is typical in having a slight, mysterious subject and a charm that lies in the color. The risk is murk, and his figures are often indistinct. Its virtue is conviction: it is the work of someone who painted for private vision rather than the market."},"met:11655":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 36 by 46 centimeters, recorded as existing by 1894, showing the banishment of Hagar and her son Ishmael. The subject comes from the Book of Genesis: Sarah, jealous of Hagar's child, demands that the servant and her boy be sent away, and Abraham reluctantly sends them into the wilderness with bread and water. In a small format the story becomes a private, intimate image rather than a public history picture.","i":"Genesis 21 tells how Hagar, the Egyptian servant who bore Abraham's son, was sent away with the boy at Sarah's insistence; in the desert, when the water ran out, an angel showed Hagar a well and promised that Ishmael would father a great nation. Painters were drawn to the story for its pathos: an abandoned mother and child, and a rescue that turns despair to hope.","c":"A small picture like this lives by feeling and color, not by narrative detail, and Newman's glowing, shadowy manner suits the desert pathos better than a literal treatment would. The danger is vagueness; the reward, an emotional charge from a handful of dark, rich tones. Compared with the large public history pictures of the day it is private and understated, and that is its appeal."},"met:11656":{"d":"A small oil on canvas, about 26 by 31 centimeters, recorded as existing by 1894, of the Virgin Mary with the infant Christ and the young Saint John the Baptist. The Met lists the Madonna, the Christ Child and the child saint among its subjects, a grouping that goes back to Renaissance Italy, where painters such as Leonardo and Raphael made it a favorite. At this size it works as a private devotional image or a cabinet picture.","i":"In Renaissance devotion the infant John, the future forerunner of Christ, meets the infant Jesus in the company of Mary, so that the future roles of the two boys are foreshadowed in play. The subject was beloved by painters because it combined the sacred with the tenderness of domestic life. A nineteenth-century American painter choosing it was looking back to that Italian tradition, aware of Raphael and Leonardo and of the Venetian color he admired.","c":"Newman, who studied in Paris and admired the old masters, brings to the subject a glowing, shadowed color and a hushed devotional mood rather than Raphael's clarity. The effect is atmospheric and a little murky, and the drawing is secondary to feeling. As an American painter's late echo of the Renaissance Madonna, it is personal and sincere. It will charm those who like the visionary in American art, and puzzle those who want clear form."},"cma:117873":{"d":"A tiny oil on canvas, about 16 by 21 centimeters, dated only to the nineteenth century. The title names a bather, a figure usually shown undressed or partly dressed beside or in water, a subject that painters from Rembrandt to Courbet and Renoir used as a way to paint the human body in natural light. At so small a scale the picture can only be a brief, intimate vision, hardly larger than a book, to be held close.","i":"The bather bridges mythology and everyday life: with a pool, a stream or the sea, the scene could stand for a goddess's nymphs, Susanna's bath or simply a modern woman at the water's edge. Painters of Newman's generation used it to show the body without a story, though a nineteenth-century viewer might still have supplied a classical association. Newman's small, imagined scenes were made on a scale for private looking.","c":"At this scale the success of a bather lies in its color and in a few strokes that set the flesh and the water glowing. Newman's visionary habit of letting form dissolve into colored light is well suited to the subject, and the picture's smallness makes it a jewel more than a statement. The risk is slightness, and the lack of a precise date makes it hard to place within his career. A fragment of private reverie."},"met:437700":{"d":"The birth of the Virgin Mary is staged as a household scene on a tall canvas of about 205 by 171 cm, of altarpiece scale. Women attend the newborn infant in a domestic interior, and a man is also present, presumably Joachim, Mary's father. Solimena, the leading painter of Naples, painted it about 1690, in the early phase of his career, combining dramatic light and shade with the warm color and swirling movement of the Neapolitan Baroque.","i":"The story comes not from the Gospels but from the apocryphal Protoevangelium of James: Anne and Joachim, long childless, were promised a daughter by an angel, and Mary was born in answer to their prayers. The feast of the Nativity of the Virgin is kept on 8 September. Counter-Reformation art liked to present sacred events in homely detail, with attendants and household objects, so that the faithful could imagine them as real.","c":"Solimena was still a young man in 1690, learning from Luca Giordano's lightness and Mattia Preti's shadow, and the picture shows an ambitious talent finding a style. The strengths are the warm light and the lively crowding of figures; the risks are a certain rhetoric and weight. Later he would clarify and cool his manner, but this earlier energy has an appeal that the polished altarpieces sometimes lack."},"cma:146167":{"d":"The risen Christ appears to his mother in a tall canvas of about 223 by 170 cm, painted about 1708. Strong contrasts of light and dark recall Caravaggio's long aftermath in Naples, yet the forms are elegant, the colors rich and the body of Christ perfected, in the manner of the idealizing strand of Italian art. Solimena sought to improve on nature through the study of antiquity, aiming at grandeur and nobility that would inspire reverence and devotion.","i":"The Gospels name Mary Magdalene as the first witness of the Resurrection, but a long devotional tradition held that Christ appeared first to his mother, and Ignatius of Loyola's Spiritual Exercises includes this apparition among the scenes for meditation. The image joins the two moments of Mary's life as a mother, grief at the cross and joy at the Resurrection. Devotion to the Virgin was intense in Counter-Reformation Naples, which makes the subject a natural one for a church or a devout patron.","c":"The picture combines two currents, and the combination is apt: the Caravaggesque shadow supplies drama while the idealizing polish supplies nobility. It can feel schooled, a grand manner worked out rather than felt, and the perfected body of Christ may seem more statue than flesh. But as an example of Neapolitan late Baroque classicism at its most ambitious, with Solimena at the peak of his reputation, it makes its case."},"met:437701":{"d":"A Neapolitan nobleman, Diego Pignatelli d'Aragona (1687–1750), is shown with an enslaved servant in a small oil on canvas, about 59 by 46 cm, probably painted in 1731 or 1732. The nobleman's rank and the servant's presence are both part of what the picture is meant to display. Solimena, then in his seventies, was the most celebrated painter in Naples, and portraits were a minor part of his output.","i":"Enslaved servants were part of many aristocratic households in eighteenth-century Italy, and portraits often included them as signs of wealth and worldliness. Such images treated the servant as an accessory, and modern museum titles, like this one, name the enslavement plainly to restore the person to the story. The Pignatelli were one of the great noble houses of Naples, and a portrait of one of its members stood for the house as well as for the man.","c":"The portrait lives on a tension. Solimena, the master of grand altarpieces, adapts his courtly manner to a small format, and the nobleman's likeness is the expected business. Yet the second figure draws the modern eye, and a critic must note that the conventions of display which flatter the master also reduce the servant to an emblem. Its quality lies in the finish; its interest now lies in what it reveals about the society that commissioned it."},"nga:32689":{"d":"An old woman in a white bonnet sits on a stool at the left edge, in profile, hands in her lap, while three children stand on a low rise to the right: a girl in blue, a boy playing a thin pipe, and an older boy holding an instrument in the crook of his arm. A fourth boy walks away toward the horizon with a pole on his shoulder. Behind them olive-green fields roll back to slate-gray hills under a steel-gray sky broken by ice-blue patches, with sheep, cows, a rider, a house and a church spire in the distance. The nearly square canvas has its horizon about halfway up.","i":"Louis Le Nain and his brothers Antoine and Mathieu made their name in Paris with peasant subjects, unusual in French painting and calmer than the brawling villagers of Dutch art. Here the piper's torn elbows hint at poverty, yet nobody jokes or quarrels; the music and the long look across the fields suggest pastoral calm. Historians still debate whether such pictures record rural hardship, offer a gentle moral about patience, or satisfy city buyers' taste for country life. The painter's intention is not documented.","c":"Le Nain's peasants do not perform. They stand and sit with a gravity that Dutch painters of rustic life rarely allowed, and the cool gray-green landscape under a heavy sky suits that silence. The cost is stiffness: the children are lined up like a small choir, each absorbed in a private world. Yet the plain honesty of the group is hard to forget, and few French painters of the 1640s looked at the poor so levelly."},"nga:41603":{"d":"Three figures sit around a barrel covered with a white cloth that serves as a table: an old man in profile at the left, a staff between his knees and a hat in his lap; a woman in the center, a spindle tucked under her arm, gazing out with unfocused eyes; and a boy at the right holding a jug and a tall glass of red liquid. Their clothes are ripped and frayed. At the back a girl stands at a high hearth, her face warmed by the fire. A cold, bright light from the left bleaches the sand-brown and white tones of the room.","i":"Le Nain painted the rural poor with a seriousness that was unusual in French art of the 1640s. The scene has no story: an old man, a woman and a boy, a spindle, a girl at the fire. Torn clothes and a bare room point to poverty, yet the figures sit upright, and their quiet dignity is sometimes linked to Catholic ideas of charity toward the poor; others see a pastoral fantasy for city buyers. Why such pictures were made, and for whom, is still debated.","c":"The light is the picture's best invention. A cold, bright glow from the left drains color from the sand-brown clothes and leaves the boy's skin almost translucent, making the group feel more real than any gesture could. The woman who stares out of the picture is a portrait in all but name. What is missing is any exchange between the figures: they sit side by side like sitters, which gives the scene its stillness and also a certain stiffness."},"nga:55845":{"d":"A high viewpoint looks down across sunlit white stucco buildings with terracotta tile roofs, plowed fields and a line of trees. In the lower left a grassy outcropping edged with gray boulders, perhaps a retaining wall, lies in the shadow of trees behind the viewer. A few white birds fly above the roofs. The horizon sits three-quarters of the way up, under a thin sliver of opal-blue sky, and a peach-colored full moon rises low at the left. Dabs of paint stand in for stucco, foliage and furrows. The canvas is horizontal and signed at lower left.","i":"The title names an hour, not a place: twilight in midsummer, when the last sun and the rising moon share the sky. Metcalf, who trained in Paris in the 1880s, belonged to the American painters who adopted the Impressionists' broken brushwork and high-keyed color. A view from a height with a high horizon was a device often used to turn land into pattern. Nothing in the title identifies the site, so the picture is best read as a study of light and atmosphere.","c":"The raised viewpoint and the warm roofs against cool tree shadow give the picture a settled, glowing order, and the thick dabs are used intelligently: they read as stucco in one place, leaves in another. Metcalf's handling is more cautious than Monet's or Pissarro's, though, and the moon, a small peach disc low in a pale sky, feels more like a label for the title than the source of the light. A pleasing early canvas, before Metcalf's New England years."},"nga:84377":{"d":"An elegantly dressed man in a camel-brown jacket lined with blue and trimmed with silver lace sits on a rock, turns his torso toward the viewer and holds up a dead partridge by one leg. At his side a white dog with black spots crouches, eyes on the bird, and a second dead bird lies on the rock; behind them are a sack and a powder horn. In the distance two women talk by a low wall, and a drive of trees leads to a three-story manor house under gray clouds. The vertical canvas is signed and dated 1722.","i":"Hunting was a noble privilege in France, and a gentleman shown with his dog and his bag of game announced his rank as plainly as a title. Here the setting helps: the manor at the end of the drive stands for the estate, and the women by the wall show the leisure that went with it. The Beringhen family held high court offices under the French kings. Oudry, a pupil of the portraitist Largillierre, was within about a decade painting hunts for Louis XV.","c":"Oudry paints the dog and the partridge well, and the dog's fixed gaze on the bird is the liveliest thing in the picture. The sitter himself is not flattered: rounded, flushed cheeks and a trace of double chin are recorded without fuss, which gives the face a believable humanity. The pose is a stock formula of hunting portraiture and the landscape is a stage set, but the canvas shows why Oudry soon turned to animals, where his talent lay."},"nga:89604":{"d":"Two dogs stand before the base of a pair of columns in a landscape. At the left a short-haired cream greyhound with tawny patches is caught in mid-stride, front paws off the ground, turning to look behind it; at the right a long-haired black dog with a few white marks stands planted on all four feet, watching it. Both tails are raised as if in play. The column base is chipped, and beyond it lie muted green trees and a pale blue sky. Shiny gold capitals name the dogs, MISSE and LVTTINE, and the signature and the date 1729 appear on the stone.","i":"Giving each dog a name in gold capitals treats them as sitters, and the columns behind them borrow the stage furniture of formal human portraits, where a column and a landscape signaled rank. Dogs were prized in French hunting culture, and a favorite pair could be painted with a sitter's care. The picture does not say who owned Misse and Luttine; Oudry painted hounds for noble patrons and, in time, for the king. The playful pose suits the lively, intimate taste of Louis XV's France.","c":"This is dog portraiture at its most winning. Oudry sets the thin-framed greyhound against the dense black dog and lets their contrast do the work: one all air, one all weight. The observation is exact without being fussy, and the cracked column base mocks the formal tradition gently. The background is plain and the black dog static beside its partner's leap, but the pair holds the eye better than some human portraits of the period."},"cma:144743":{"d":"A hare and a leg of lamb, game and butcher's meat, are the whole subject of this tall oil painting, roughly 98 by 74 centimeters. Cleveland's curators describe the composition as starkly simple, with a sterile background that leaves the eye nothing to study but the surfaces. Everything rests on the painter's skill at rendering textures, which the title's two contrasting items put on show. Such paintings were generally displayed in hunting lodges or dining rooms, where they glorified the hunt and the bounty it brings.","i":"Cleveland links the picture's exactitude to the Enlightenment, whose faith in scientific reasoning encouraged painters to render fur, feather and flesh as accurately as they could. Game pieces of this kind celebrated the hunt and its bounty, and a dining room was a natural home for them. The museum also notes a sobering fact: the canvas dates from 1742, a year after a devastating famine in France, a contrast that modern viewers, at least, can hardly ignore when they look at so lavish a display.","c":"The severity is the point, and the risk. With no setting to lean on, Oudry must win by touch alone, and he, a master of such effects, has the means. The result is a display piece, impressive for its truthfulness and a little cool, closer to demonstration than to feeling. Among his still lifes it shows how far he would go for plain virtuosity, at some cost to atmosphere and warmth."},"met:437234":{"d":"A dog stands guard over dead game in this horizontal oil painting, about 65 by 81 centimeters. The museum lists a dog, a bird and a fox among its subjects, so the picture belongs to the hunting still life, a type that sets the spoils of the chase beside a living hound. Oudry's game pieces typically give fur and feather close, tactile attention and place the group in a shallow space with a hint of landscape or a plain wall behind it. Such a canvas was made to hang in a dining room or a hunting lodge.","i":"In the France of Louis XV the right to hunt was a mark of rank, and a picture of the kill celebrated that privilege. The dog adds a second theme, loyalty and vigilance: its owner's faithful guardian. Oudry painted such subjects throughout his career, and this canvas, dated 1753, falls two years before his death in 1755. Its size and date match those of 'Ducks Resting in Sunshine' in the same museum, which suggests, though it does not prove, that the two were made as a pair.","c":"Oudry's best game pieces succeed by observation: the weight of a limp bird, the way light settles on fur, the alertness of a hound. A living dog gives a still life a story and adds drama to what could be an inventory. These are elegant, well-crafted decorations that rarely ask more than admiration; his large hunts for Louis XV show him at fuller stretch. Among his late game pictures, it is likely to be admired more than loved."},"met:437235":{"d":"Ducks rest in sunshine in this horizontal oil painting, about 65 by 81 centimeters, which the museum's keywords tag as Ducks and Landscapes. The subject is living birds, not game: ducks at ease in a place bathed in light, as the title says. The picture belongs to the tradition of poultry and waterfowl painting that Oudry pursued alongside his hunting scenes, and which let him study plumage and the effect of sunlight on feathers. Its size and date, 1753, match those of 'Dog Guarding Dead Game' in the same collection.","i":"Oudry's animal paintings were prized for their fidelity, and collectors hung ducks, hounds and game in country houses alongside his more formal hunts. The title stresses the light, and Oudry was interested in how light alters color, a subject he lectured on at the Academy. If this canvas was the companion of 'Dog Guarding Dead Game', as its size and date suggest, the pair would set living birds at rest against dead quarry: the peaceful and the hunted sides of the countryside.","c":"Oudry's ducks are a pleasure of a quiet kind: the subject allows no drama, so the painter's skill lies in the plain business of feather, light and settled pose. Such pictures are decorative by design, easier to enjoy than to argue about, and they rank below his royal hunts for ambition. What they show is an artist, within two years of his death, still looking closely at ordinary creatures and finding in them enough to paint."},"nga:46102":{"d":"A small panel, about 53 by 31 centimetres, shows the Virgin and the Christ Child enthroned in the company of Saints Peter and Paul. The two apostles are usually recognised by Peter's keys and Paul's sword and book. The museum's record of a tempera medium is marked as uncertain. Domenico di Bartolo, a Sienese painter, was working around 1430 under the influence of Florentine painting, with its solid, weighty figures, while keeping Siena's love of elegant line and rich colour.","i":"Peter and Paul, the princes of the apostles, were the founders of the Church in Rome and are often paired on altarpieces: Peter holding the keys given to him by Christ, Paul with the sword of his martyrdom and a book for his Epistles. Set beside the Virgin, they present the Church's authority to the worshipper. Panels of this size were made for private devotion or small chapels, and the choice of saints may reflect the name saints of the person who commissioned it.","c":"A telling moment in Siena's turn towards the Renaissance. Domenico di Bartolo is remembered above all for the lively, human scenes he painted in the hospital of Santa Maria della Scala in the 1440s, and in this earlier panel the sober forms and Florentine solidity are only beginning to appear. As devotional painting it is graceful and competent, though less inventive than his later work: a painter around thirty, between two traditions."},"nga:103172":{"d":"A bank of red, blush-pink and white poppies fills the bottom third of this nearly square canvas, painted so loosely that the blossoms are dabs of color on long sage-green stems flicked with navy. Beyond lies powder-blue water with low, rounded rocks tinted white, pink, blue and green, edged with rust-orange touches. A small boat with white sails drifts past, a pale spit of land marks the far horizon, a lone bird crosses a milk-white sky, and bare canvas shows at the bottom edge. The painter signed and dated it at lower left, 1891.","i":"Hassam was one of the American Impressionists, and the Isles of Shoals, a group of rocky islands off the coast of New Hampshire and Maine, drew him back summer after summer. The poet Celia Thaxter kept a famous flower garden there, and the islands attracted a circle of painters, writers and musicians. Hassam had studied in Paris in the late 1880s and brought home the Impressionists' high-keyed color and broken touch; here he applies them to an American place, with poppies as a bright screen before the sea.","c":"The color is the success: reds and pinks that vibrate against the cool blue of the water, set down with a freedom that leaves some canvas bare. The stems are still drawn rather than merely suggested, which shows a young American keeping his footing while absorbing Impressionism. The weaker part is the distance, where boat, bird and far shore are small, tidy notes that give the eye less to do than the foreground. A fresh, likable picture."},"met:437262":{"d":"A large vertical canvas, about two meters high, shows Prince Henry, eldest son of James I, with his companion Sir John Harington in the hunting field. The museum's keywords add a horse, hounds and deer to the two named figures. It is a formal court portrait of 1603, the year James VI of Scotland became James I of England, by Robert Peake the Elder, then a leading London portrait painter and later in the prince's service. Two boys, about nine and eleven, appear in adult courtly dress, as an image of princely education.","i":"Hunting was the sport of kings and the schooling of princes, and James I's heir is shown as a huntsman in training beside a boy of his own station. Henry's admirers, many of them committed Protestants, hoped for a warrior prince. He died of illness in 1612, aged eighteen, and Harington died two years later. At the time the picture said something simpler: here is the heir, healthy, skilled and royal, and the court has a future.","c":"Peake's manner is linear and decorative: costume, pattern and rank matter more to him than space or modeling, and this portrait is a precise record of dress and bearing, not a study of character. That limit is also its charm. Two boys with the poise of adults, painted just before English portraiture was transformed by Continental painters, give it the strangeness and appeal of a transitional work, stiffer than the Netherlandish portraits that later displaced it."},"met:437263":{"d":"A tall, narrow canvas, about 154 by 79 centimeters, holds a formal portrait of Princess Elizabeth, daughter of James I, painted around 1606, when she was about ten. The museum's keywords list the elements of her costume and accessories: a tiara, a pearl necklace, a collar, lace and embroidery, and a book. As a type it is a court portrait of the new Stuart dynasty, in which dress and jewels state rank before they describe a child.","i":"The museum's title points to what came later: in 1613 Elizabeth married Frederick V, Elector Palatine, and in 1619 the couple accepted the crown of Bohemia, which they lost the next year. She spent most of her long widowhood in exile at The Hague. Through her youngest daughter Sophia she is the ancestor of the Hanoverian kings of Britain. The book, as in many portraits of royal girls, probably signals education and piety.","c":"A princess of ten gave Peake a great deal to record, and the lace, embroidery and pearls are the real subject. The price is individuality. As in most Jacobean court portraits, costume speaks louder than character, and the child is turned into a small adult icon of monarchy. It matters as a dynastic image, not as a study of a girl, and it looks stiff beside the royal children Van Dyck painted a generation later."},"met:437774":{"d":"A long, low panel of tempera on wood, about 24 by 186 centimeters, carries Christ and the twelve apostles against a gold ground. It is more than seven times as wide as it is tall, proportions typical of a predella, the band of small pictures set beneath the main panels of an altarpiece, and they suggest a single row of thirteen figures. The gold ground was the standard setting of Italian devotional painting around 1400, placing the holy figures in a space outside daily life.","i":"Christ among the twelve presents the foundation of the Church: the men he chose to carry his teaching. Painters usually told them apart by attributes, Peter by his keys, Andrew by an X-shaped cross, John as the beardless youth. A predella sat just above the altar table, in plain view of priest and congregation, so a row of apostles probably served as a reminder that the Mass joined the worshippers to Christ's first followers.","c":"Taddeo di Bartolo was a busy, widely traveled Sienese who worked quickly and well, and his best pictures keep the Sienese tradition alive into the 1400s through graceful line and bright color on gold. A row of thirteen similar figures, however, invites repetition, and the predella was where a workshop most readily economized. His narrative gifts have more room in the frescoes he painted for the Palazzo Pubblico in Siena."},"met:436939":{"d":"A small oil on wood panel, about 25 by 15 centimeters, shows a woman who sells goods in the street; the museum's keywords name her a peddler and street vendor. Painted in 1880, it is a cabinet picture in the manner of the Russian genre painters of the day, who took ordinary city people as their subject and told a story about them at a glance. The narrow upright format suits a single figure seen close.","i":"Vladimir Makovsky belonged to the Wanderers, the Russian realists whose association, founded in 1870, sent traveling exhibitions through the provinces and claimed the daily life of the people as a worthy subject. Peddlers were familiar figures in Russian streets, and painters of the day used them to suggest poverty, patience and small humor. Makovsky's tone is generally sympathetic and anecdotal rather than accusing.","c":"Makovsky's strengths are warmth, quick characterization and a clear story told in a small space. His limit is a gentle, sentimental surface: he is less sharp than Perov, whose street scenes cut deeper, and far less probing than Repin. In an unassuming panel like this one, look for the economy of the telling and the likeness of a type, not for ambition."},"nga:119005":{"d":"Under a heavy blanket of clouds, eleven figures work and rest in a barren landscape. At the left a man raises an ax over his shoulder, a sandaled foot on a fallen trunk, while others squat over a log or carry a rope-bound bundle of wood. At the center two women, a man and a baby huddle around a fire, a big-bellied pot hanging from a frame of logs. At the right a bearded man sips wine as a woman and two children shelter under a rocky overhang. Snow-covered huts lie far behind.","i":"This canvas matches 'Autumn' in size and date, so the two evidently belong to a series of the four seasons. Italian decorative painting of the eighteenth century showed each season through its characteristic labors and pleasures. Giaquinto dresses his figures in classical tunics, which turns the scene into an allegory, not a record of peasant life. Winter's business is wood and warmth, and the wine, bread and fire by the rocks supply the comfort that makes the cold bearable.","c":"Giaquinto handles the season with Rococo lightness: golden yellows, rose pinks, lapis and crimson glow against a cold sky, and the group around the fire is composed with a nice sense of rhythm. But no one looks cold. The tunics and sandals belong to a pastoral dream, and winter is felt only in the clouds and the distant snow-covered huts. It is a decorative pleasure, not a harsh report."},"nga:119006":{"d":"Nine adults and three plump children gather in small groups under a copse of trees, with rolling hills and hazy mountains opening to the right. Women hold trays of figs and grapes, a child reaches for a fig, and a muscular man sits beside a large urn. At the right a bare-chested man crowned with vine leaves leans limply on a supporter, his feet on a heap of fruit, while a bearded man in a spotted animal skin presses grapes into his cup. Light pours from the left, and the sky glows peach before deepening to twilight blue.","i":"Autumn means the vintage, and Giaquinto fills the picture with fruit and wine. The bare-chested man in the crown of vine leaves reads as Bacchus, god of wine, and the bearded man in an animal skin who fills his cup as one of his followers; the god's limp pose recalls ancient images of the drunken Bacchus being helped along. The canvas shares its size and date with 'Winter', and the two evidently belong to a series of the seasons.","c":"The sunset sky, graded from peach to twilight blue, is the best passage, pouring warm light over a crowd of rosy, blond figures grouped with an easy, curving rhythm. The faces repeat and the poses are pretty rather than felt, and the vintage has no weight of labor behind it. It is accomplished Rococo decoration, pleasant to look at, with less force than the ceilings and altarpieces on which Giaquinto's reputation rests."},"met:438850":{"d":"Mary Magdalene, shown as a penitent, appears with an angel and a skull, two of the usual companions in images of her penance. The painting is an upright oil on canvas, about 160 by 118 centimeters, dated about 1750, by the Italian Rococo painter Corrado Giaquinto. At this size it would suit a private chapel or a patron's gallery as much as a church.","i":"In Catholic tradition Mary Magdalene, a follower of Christ long identified in the Western Church with the repentant sinner, withdrew to a life of penance after the Resurrection. The skull is a memento mori and a sign of her meditation on death, and the angel recalls the legend that angels carried her up daily to hear heavenly music. The subject was a favorite of the Counter-Reformation and the Rococo alike, joining a beautiful figure to a lesson in repentance.","c":"Giaquinto brought a light, silvery, flowing Rococo touch to religious subjects, and the penitent saint was a theme well suited to his sweetness of color and sinuous drawing. At the same time, the type invites sentimental ecstasy, and a lesser example can seem sugary. It stands in the lively tradition of Italian devotional painting at mid-century that Giaquinto carried to the court of Madrid."},"met:441234":{"d":"A mythological scene in which the sorceress Medea restores the youth of Aeson, the father of her husband Jason. The principal figures are the enchantress and the old man she restores. It is an upright oil on canvas, about 74 by 55 centimeters, dated about 1760, by the Italian Rococo painter Corrado Giaquinto. At this modest size it may be a sketch or a cabinet picture.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses: when Jason returned from the quest for the Golden Fleece, his aged father Aeson was near death, and Medea, using herbs gathered on a moonlit night, drained his old blood and replaced it with a magic brew, restoring his youth. Rococo painters liked such tales of magic and transformation, and the subject let Giaquinto show a dramatic ritual with a beautiful sorceress.","c":"Rococo mythology suits Giaquinto's quick, luminous touch, and a story of rejuvenation lends itself to rising figures and airy light. At this scale the picture is likely closer to a sketch than to a finished work, and sketches show his flair better than his more routine altarpieces. The subject and the painter are a good match."},"met:437485":{"d":"A girl is the single subject of this portrait miniature, painted around 1790 on a thin plate of ivory, the usual support for such pictures. The form itself tells much: a likeness small enough to hold in the palm, made to be kept in a case, worn as a jewel or set into a frame. The pale, slightly translucent ivory lets flesh tones glow through thin, transparent color, which is why miniaturists chose it for faces.","i":"Miniatures of children were made to be treasured: a parent's keepsake, a gift to grandparents, a record of a child's appearance at a given age in the years before photography. The girl is not named in the title, which is common for miniatures separated from their first owners. Around 1790 the portrait miniature was at the height of its popularity in Britain and Ireland, where painters served a wide clientele as well as the aristocracy.","c":"At this scale the miniaturist must catch a likeness and a mood in a few square centimeters, and the best, such as Cosway or Engleheart, do so with startling economy. Roch was a sound practitioner rather than a star, and a portrait of a girl in this idiom will please by freshness of face and delicacy of finish more than by invention. Its interest is human: a child survives in an object meant to be held close."},"nga:284":{"d":"Gold fills the background of this small, nearly square panel, about 31 by 38 centimeters. Christ hangs at the center, thin and pale, his head tipped to the viewer's left. Three small angels in cobalt blue catch blood from his hands and side in gold bowls while a fourth prays. A woman with long copper hair kneels at the foot of the cross, clasping his feet. At the left a woman in lapis blue swoons into the arms of two others; at the right a man in blue and rose prays, beside armored soldiers, one of them pointing up.","i":"The scene combines Gospel and legend. The swooning woman in blue is the Virgin, held by the holy women; the kneeling woman is usually read as Mary Magdalene; the praying man is John; and the pointing soldier is the centurion who recognized Christ as the Son of God. The skull below is usually read as Adam's, in the tradition that Christ's blood redeemed the first man, and the angels' bowls recall the Eucharist. A panel this size was probably made for private devotion.","c":"Paolo Veneziano, the leading Venetian painter of his day, keeps the Byzantine gold and jewel-like blues, yet the gestures carry Gothic feeling: the Virgin's swoon, the Magdalen clutching the feet, the soldier's pointing hand. The figures are slight and the space a shallow stage, so the pathos is courtly and restrained, far from the weighty tragedy of Giotto. Within its limits it is a precious, delicately emotional object."},"met:435920":{"d":"A man is the sole subject of this undated portrait miniature on ivory, a format in which British painters made likenesses small enough to hold in the hand. Such miniatures are typically head-and-shoulders portraits painted in transparent color on a thin ivory plate, which lets the pale ground glow through the flesh tones, and they were made to be kept in a case or worn as a jewel. The sitter's name has not been preserved in the museum's title.","i":"The portrait miniature was portraiture's private form: a keepsake for relatives, a love token, a souvenir for a traveler or soldier, sometimes a memorial. This sitter's name has been lost, so what the picture meant to its first owner cannot now be recovered. Its survival, as a small memory of a face, is its chief interest, and a reminder of how many such likenesses once passed from hand to hand.","c":"Miniatures live on delicate handling and the glow of ivory under thin color, and the best rival full-size portraits for character at a fraction of the scale. Collins belongs to the large body of competent British miniaturists whose names are now little known, and a work of this kind should be judged for its fidelity to a face and the neatness of its finish, qualities a viewer can test only by looking closely at the sitter."},"met:435999":{"d":"A woman is the sole subject of this portrait miniature, painted in 1767 on a thin plate of ivory. It belongs to the world of the mid-Georgian miniature, a small, personal likeness made to be worn or kept in a case. The ivory support lets flesh tones glow beneath thin color, which is why painters favored it for faces. The sitter is not named in the museum's title, and the painter, Samuel Cotes, was the brother of the portrait painter Francis Cotes.","i":"The year 1767 falls within the great age of the English miniature, when polished likenesses on ivory were exchanged as keepsakes and love tokens and worn as pendants. A woman's miniature might mark a betrothal, a marriage or a memory. Francis Cotes, Samuel's brother, was a leading portraitist in pastel and oil and a founder member of the Royal Academy in 1768, so the family trade was fashionable likeness-making. The sitter's identity has not come down with the picture.","c":"Cotes is today less famous than Richard Cosway, whose looser, more fluid manner came to dominate fashionable miniature painting in London from the 1770s. Work of this kind succeeds by careful finish and restraint, and its risk is a certain sameness, a polite likeness that flatters everyone alike. Its best use is as a clear, dated example of the English miniature before Cosway's elegance changed the fashion."},"met:437663":{"d":"A woman appears in this portrait miniature on ivory, dated 1801 and titled The Hours. The title is poetic or allegorical, recalling the Horae of classical myth, the young goddesses who watched over the seasons and the order of the day, and the title leaves open whether she is an allegory or a sitter with a poetic name. The format is a small hand-held picture in transparent color on a thin plate of ivory, made to be kept in a case or frame.","i":"In Greek myth the Horae were daughters of Zeus and Themis, goddesses of the seasons and of orderly time. Late Georgian artists took such themes from poetry and the antique, and Samuel Shelley, who painted imaginative subjects as well as portraits, was one of the miniaturists who carried them onto ivory. A miniature with this title is therefore both a keepsake-sized object and a small poem about the passing of time.","c":"Shelley was a versatile miniaturist and a founder, in 1804, of the Society of Painters in Water Colours, so the transparent, light-filled touch of watercolor was his natural language. A title like The Hours promises ambition beyond routine likeness-taking, and the idea is the most interesting thing about the picture. The risk of such classical-flavored miniatures is prettiness; at their best they give a woman a poetic dignity that plain portraiture did not."},"met:437664":{"d":"A man is the sole subject of this portrait miniature on ivory, which the museum dates 'probably' to about 1800. In Britain at that date such likenesses were often oval and only a few centimeters high, painted in transparent watercolor so that the white of the ivory shows through the flesh tones like a light behind the face. They were mounted under glass in a case, pendant or frame, and were made to be carried or kept close, not hung on a wall.","i":"A miniature of this date was a personal object: a keepsake for a wife or family, a token between engaged couples, a memento for those parting, and in the war years around 1800 many men left home to serve. The sitter's name is not recorded in the title, so he stands for a type, the ordinary educated Briton who could pay a miniaturist for a likeness, rather than for a known life.","c":"A miniaturist of 1800 had to flatter a little and be exact a lot, and Shelley, a regular exhibitor at the Royal Academy, was a competent hand at both. Male dress of the period was sober, which leaves the face to carry the picture, so clear modeling and a luminous ivory tone are the qualities to look for. They matter more than invention in so small a field, and they are the measure of this work."},"cma:145124":{"d":"Hunters move through a rocky landscape in this canvas of about 127 by 175 cm, painted about 1635. Dughet, who is also known as Gaspard Poussin, spent his whole career in Rome. At fifteen he entered the studio of Nicolas Poussin, who married his sister, and he concentrated exclusively on landscape, preferring a rugged type of scenery. The painting, made when he was about twenty, shows the young landscapist already at work on rocks, trees and the open country around the city.","i":"Dughet's landscapes belong to the classical, or ideal, tradition shaped by Annibale Carracci, Claude and Poussin: a composed nature of rocks, trees and distances, with small figures giving a human measure. Hunters are a fitting subject, since hunting was the sport of nobles. His rugged scenery was widely imitated well into the eighteenth century, and British Grand Tourists prized his work, which shaped the taste for the picturesque and the design of English landscape gardens.","c":"Even at twenty, Dughet has the structural clarity that distinguishes him: solid rocks, dark masses of trees and a convincing recession into air. The figures are small and routine, and the early work lacks the atmospheric subtlety of his maturity, but the formula of the rugged landscape was so well made that it outlasted him by generations. It is a confident, sober picture, an impressive beginning more than a summation."},"met:436229":{"d":"A wooded landscape with men and forest is the subject of this wide canvas, about 96 by 154 cm, painted by Gaspard Dughet, the Roman landscapist who worked in the circle of Poussin. The title calls it imaginary, which means a composed landscape invented from elements studied in nature, not a view of a real place. Trees dominate, and the small figures give a human measure to a scene of shade and quiet.","i":"The imaginary or ideal landscape, a composed nature rather than a topographical view, was the central achievement of Roman landscape painting in the seventeenth century, and Dughet drew on sketches made in the countryside around Rome and Tivoli to recombine real motifs. A forest suggested solitude, mystery and refuge, and the men in it may be hunters, travelers or shepherds. The picture invites reverie more than it narrates, and its value lay in the mood.","c":"Dughet's trees are the stars: massive, dark and carefully built, with luminous openings that give the forest depth and air. The formula, a screen of foliage balanced against a distance, is repeated through his work, and the figures are not individualized, but the formula is beautifully constructed. It is a persuasive example of the grandeur he gave to wooded scenery, a landscape made for a quiet room and a contemplative eye."},"met:13384":{"d":"Martha Goldthwaite, who appears under her married name as Mrs. George Ingersoll, is the subject of this miniature, painted around 1820 in watercolor on ivory. It measures only about 8 by 6 centimeters, a size made to be held in the palm or set in a case, locket or frame. The artist, Sarah Goodridge, supported herself by painting such likenesses, and the title's form, a husband's name with the maiden name in brackets, is how museums record a married woman sitter.","i":"Sarah Goodridge, born in Templeton, Massachusetts, in 1788, taught herself to paint miniatures and by about 1820 was working in Boston as a successful portraitist on ivory. A married woman then took her husband's name in society, and the title keeps her own name in brackets so that she can be traced. For a family, a wife's miniature was both a likeness and a record of marriage, to be handed down through later generations.","c":"Goodridge's miniatures are generally admired for directness: a clear likeness, clean color and no rhetoric. Beside the dash of English miniaturists such as Cosway, the American manner of her day is plainer and more literal, which can look timid but gives these pictures their candor. This portrait, a quiet record of one of her many sitters, is best valued as a sound example of the steady, honest work on which she built a long career."},"met:15068":{"d":"A woman is the sole subject of this miniature, painted around 1820 in watercolor on ivory, a plate only about 8 by 6 centimeters. The museum's keywords note a collar, lace and a locket among her dress and ornaments, small items that a miniaturist could render with a fine brush on so small a surface. Her name is not given in the title, so she is known only as a lady. It is a keepsake-sized likeness, made for a family and kept in a case.","i":"Lace collars and lockets were visible signs of a woman's taste and sentiment in the 1820s. A locket often held a lock of hair or a second likeness, so a miniature of a woman wearing one hints at a web of private ties. The picture, probably made for a husband or family, was meant to be seen close at hand, in the palm, not hung for display.","c":"Lace and a locket give a miniaturist the chance to show fine brushwork, which suited Goodridge's careful technique, but the real test remains the face, where a good likeness balances candor against the polite smoothing that sitters wanted. Without the sitter's name there is nobody to compare it with, so its interest lies mostly in its clean, competent example of American miniature painting around 1820."},"met:10956":{"d":"The sitter is the painter Gilbert Stuart (1755-1828), shown in a watercolor-on-ivory miniature of about 9 by 7 centimeters made around 1825, when he was about seventy. Stuart was the best-known American portraitist of his generation, famous above all for his portraits of George Washington, and he lived in Boston, where the miniaturist, Sarah Goodridge, sought him out for advice. A portrait of a painter by a painter is both a likeness and a tribute.","i":"Goodridge taught herself and then won the friendship of Stuart, whose advice she valued, and this small portrait of him is a tribute from one painter to another. Stuart died in 1828, only a few years after the likeness was made, and the miniature preserves the face of the artist whose Washington portraits still define the first president's image. It is a document of friendship as much as a commission.","c":"A miniature of a famous painter by his admirer is bound to be read against the sitter's own work, and the comparison is instructive: where Stuart's oils are fluid, broad and suggestive, Goodridge's ivory is smaller, tighter and more careful. What she may have learned from him is plain-spoken likeness. As a record of a face the portrait has obvious value, and as a work of art it shows Goodridge at her most personal."},"met:13494":{"d":"A boy, named in the title as Edward Blake Parkman, is the subject of this miniature, painted around 1825 in watercolor on ivory and measuring about 10 by 7 centimeters. Children's miniatures were often made as family keepsakes and as gifts to relatives. Goodridge, known for a clear, direct style, would have needed a patient sitter, since even a short sitting asked a child to stay still for long stretches.","i":"A boy's portrait at this date might mark a stage, a first long jacket or the start of schooling, or simply a parent's wish to fix a likeness against time and the risks of childhood, when many children did not reach adulthood. That the museum can name him suggests, though it does not prove, that the picture stayed with a family that kept its identity, and a boy's miniature in a case outlived the childhood it shows.","c":"Children are hard to paint at this scale: the features are soft, the expression changes quickly, and the sitter will not hold still. A sound miniature of a boy usually succeeds by simplicity and by keeping the face fresh rather than fussy, and Goodridge's plainness suits the task. The appeal lies in unforced candor, and the limits are those of the form: a small, tidy likeness with little room for invention."},"met:15069":{"d":"A man is the single subject of this miniature, painted around 1825 in watercolor on ivory, about 8 by 6 centimeters. The title records only that he was a gentleman, a label that gives his standing but not his name. The size is that of a keepsake carried in a pocket or set in a case, and the clear, direct handling for which Goodridge is known would have made it a ready likeness for family and friends.","i":"Men's portraits of the 1820s were often commissioned to mark a marriage, a business success or a departure, and in the growing American republic a miniature was an affordable way for merchant and professional families to have a likeness. The sitter is anonymous now, so the picture is mostly evidence of how ordinary prosperous Americans wished to look: composed, neat and dignified.","c":"Male dress of the period was dark and sober, which leaves the face to carry the picture, and a miniaturist's skill shows in how well the modeling holds within a few square centimeters. Goodridge's method was direct, and in such a picture the result is likely to be honest rather than flattering. It lacks the dash of the best English miniatures but has a dignity that belongs to its American setting."},"met:14521":{"d":"A tiny watercolor on ivory, only 6.7 by 8 centimeters, shows a woman's bare chest from the collarbone to just below the breasts, with no face or arms in view. The skin is pale peach, the nipples soft pink, and a small beauty mark sits near the center. A swirl of white gauze folds above and below, and a dark ground shows at the top and bottom edges. Fine stippling and gradations model the flesh so that it seems to glow, with the ivory showing through. The miniature is now mounted in a red leather case with a white padded border.","i":"Sarah Goodridge, a Boston miniaturist who had learned from Gilbert Stuart, painted her own body in 1828 and sent it to Daniel Webster, the Massachusetts senator and a frequent sitter, in the year his wife died. 'Beauty' was a polite word for breasts, and by leaving out the face she kept herself unidentifiable if the package was opened by anyone else. Miniatures were treated as intimate tokens, to be held and even kissed. Whether this was a proposal, a love token, or a daring joke is not known. Webster married someone else in 1829, but his family kept the miniature until the 1980s, and the Metropolitan Museum acquired it in 2006.","c":"A virtuoso little picture whose force depends on context. As a nude it is technically assured, the pearly flesh and gauze handled with a miniaturist's patience, and it is unusual in early American art for being a woman's picture of her own body rather than a man's fantasy of one. As an object its power is smallness: it has to be held to be seen. It may be less a portrait than a message, but the delicate observation of skin, down to the beauty mark, gives it an individuality no allegorical nude of the period could match."},"met:10958":{"d":"A woman is the sole subject of this miniature, dated 1830 and painted in watercolor on ivory, about 10 by 7 centimeters. The year places it in the middle of Goodridge's long career, two years after Stuart's death, when she was working at full strength. The sitter's name is not recorded, and the picture is known only by its type: a small, framed or cased likeness of the kind that families kept as a keepsake of a wife, daughter or friend.","i":"By 1830 the miniature was the standard private portrait in America. Photography was still some years away, and the daguerreotype that later replaced it was announced only in 1839. A portrait on ivory cost far less than one in oil and could be made in a few sittings. Women's miniatures were often gifts to husbands, parents or children, and Goodridge supported herself for decades by such work.","c":"Goodridge in 1830 is a confident professional: fresh color, a clear likeness, and enough finish to please a sitter without losing the face. The limitation is the form's own: a few square centimeters leave little room for invention. Within them her work stands comparison with the English miniatures that set the standard, and its American plainness is part of the charm."},"met:436971":{"d":"Cattle stand in a landscape in this canvas of about 62 by 83 centimeters. The title points to a flood gate, a water-control structure, as the landmark of the scene. Émile van Marcke, a French painter who specialized in cattle in the tradition of his teacher Constant Troyon, painted it in the later nineteenth century. The picture is undated, and it is a rural subject of the kind that suited the French taste for cattle pictures in those decades.","i":"Cattle in a landscape were the subject of countless French pictures after the Barbizon painters brought the countryside into fashion, and they appealed to city buyers who saw in them an image of unspoiled rural calm. Van Marcke's cows descend from Troyon and, further back, from seventeenth-century Dutch painters such as Paulus Potter and Aelbert Cuyp. The flood gate is a reminder of the managed, working landscape behind the idyll.","c":"Van Marcke was a skilled, popular animal painter, and the picture offers what his market wanted: sturdy cattle, a believable pasture and a gentle light. It is competent and unpretentious, but it follows a formula established by Troyon and does not extend it. The pleasure lies in the observation of animals rather than in any ambition for the landscape."},"met:436203":{"d":"An allegory of Dawn, with putti heralding the day, fills this large canvas of about 198 by 240 cm. The scale is that of a decorative painting for a ceiling or wall in a Venetian palace, and it may belong to a set representing the times of the day. Gaspare Diziani, a Venetian Rococo painter who followed Sebastiano Ricci, was known for airy decorative schemes and also worked as a designer of stage sets.","i":"Dawn, personified in Greek myth as Eos, or Aurora in Roman, rose each morning to announce the sun's chariot, and her retinue of putti scattering light and flowers became a favorite subject of decorative painting. In sets of the Four Times of Day, Dawn begins the sequence. Such pictures turned a palace room into a sky, and flattered the owner as the patron of a bright world, in keeping with the cheerful mythology of the Rococo.","c":"A decorative performance in pale, airy color, with figures that float and a flickering touch that reflects Diziani's theater training. He works in Ricci's lineage but with less force than Tiepolo, the unquestioned master of such skies. The result is charming, though the sweetness and the lack of individual character are the risks of the manner. As decoration it succeeds, and the large scale gives it presence."},"met:435705":{"d":"A long, low canvas, about 27 by 102 centimetres, shows the legend of the infant Servius Tullius, a baby asleep in his cradle with a woman and a man beside him, in a scene from early Roman legend. The Met's keywords record a woman, a man, an infant and a cradle, and the shape suggests a panel from the front of a chest or a decorative frieze set into a room.","i":"The story, told by the Roman historian Livy, says that while the child Servius slept in the house of King Tarquinius Priscus, a flame appeared around his head; Queen Tanaquil, who understood omens, forbade anyone to wake him until it died away, and she raised him as her own. He became the sixth king of Rome. In Renaissance Venice, tales from Roman history decorated chests and rooms as examples of virtue and destiny, and the republic saw itself as heir to Rome's civic tradition.","c":"A charming narrative fragment. Bonifacio paints the quiet drama of the sleeping child and the awed onlookers with Venetian warmth, and the long format lets him spread the incident out gracefully. The scene is small and the figures conventional, and the colour is more interesting than the storytelling. As a rare example of a classical subject in a career devoted mostly to religious pictures, it is of interest, though it is a decorative piece rather than a major work."},"met:435706":{"d":"The Virgin and Christ Child are accompanied by several saints in a wide panel, about 83 by 127 centimetres, painted in oil. The Met's keywords record Saint Joseph, Saint John the Baptist, a woman, a man and another saint, so the Holy Family appears with the infant Baptist and companions. In Venetian paintings of this type the figures usually sit together in an open, sunlit landscape, with rich colour, rather than before a formal throne.","i":"Venetian sacre conversazioni of the 1500s relaxed the formal rows of earlier altarpieces into friendly groups in which saints share the Virgin's space and seem to converse. The inclusion of Joseph and the young John suggests the Holy Family, and such wide panels were made for private devotion in Venetian houses. Bonifacio de' Pitati, a native of Verona who worked in Venice, ran a large workshop and produced many of them. The date is not recorded.","c":"Bonifacio is a pleasant, prolific painter who follows Palma Vecchio and Titian, with rich colour and cosy grouping, and the warmth of this picture is attractive. The figures, however, are rather generalised and the composition tends to the formulaic. Compared with Titian's mastery it lacks intensity, but it is a good example of the Venetian studio production that supplied devotional pictures to the city's well-to-do homes."},"nga:204932":{"d":"Seen whole, the small altarpiece, about 76 by 66 centimeters overall, opens like a pair of shutters on the Virgin and Child. In the pointed central panel she holds the baby, who hooks a hand in her neckline. In each wing a saint stands on a floor of orange, gold and blue diamonds against punched gold: Peter with a book and a key as long as his forearm, John with a book and a quill. Above, in a clover-shaped peak, Christ as the Man of Sorrows crosses his arms, a wound bleeding in his hand.","i":"A folding triptych like this was probably made for private prayer: the owner opened the wings to face the Virgin and Child, with the saints turned toward them as intercessors. The Man of Sorrows in the peak shows the Passion that awaits the Child, and it remains visible when the wings are shut. The words on the base, AVE GRATIA PLENA DO, are the start of the angel's greeting to Mary, so the whole object is framed as a prayer to her.","c":"Nardo's reputation rests on large frescoes in Santa Maria Novella, and here, in a small portable altar, he works as a refined craftsman: symmetrical, jewel-like, the weight of his figures tempered by gold and pattern. The scheme is conventional and the faces are generic. But the Child's tug at his mother's dress, the sturdy saints and the fine punched gold make a coherent, tender object."},"nga:402":{"d":"The left wing of a small folding triptych shows Saint Peter, a standing figure that fills a narrow panel about 45 by 12.5 centimeters. He is an older man with short steel-gray hair and a trimmed beard, his forehead lined and his brow furrowed. He wears a topaz-blue garment under a honey-orange robe lined ruby-red at the wrist and shoulder, holds a dark-covered book, and carries a large key. He stands on a floor of orange, gold and blue diamonds against a punched gold ground, turned toward the central panel.","i":"Peter carries the key of the kingdom of heaven, given to him by Christ in the Gospel of Matthew, and the book stands for his teaching. The saints in the wings, angled toward the center, are usually read as intercessors who present the viewer's prayers to the Virgin and Child. The inscription on the base of the triptych, AVE GRATIA PLENA DO, gives the opening of the angel's greeting to Mary, which fixes the central theme.","c":"Nardo's wing figures have the weight of sculpture: Peter fills his strip of gold with a solid, dignified body, and the colors, honey-orange against ruby-red and topaz-blue, are chosen with a decorator's relish. The face is stern and a little generic, and the pairing with John across the way is neat to the point of formula. Still, it shows the solid, solemn Florentine style of the mid-fourteenth century, after Giotto."},"nga:403":{"d":"The pointed central panel, about 45 by 26 centimeters, shows the Virgin half-length in a deep blue, gold-trimmed mantle over a rose-pink dress, a sheer veil on her wavy blond hair and a small red ornament above her forehead. She looks aside while the Child on her arm gazes at her, reaches around her neck and hooks a hand into her neckline. His body is a baby's, but his features are scaled like an adult's. Above, in a three-lobed frame, Christ as the Man of Sorrows crosses his arms, a wound bleeding in his hand.","i":"Two moments are joined: the infant embracing his mother and, above, the adult Christ displaying his Passion wounds as the Man of Sorrows. Together they say that the Child's birth leads to his death, a thought a private worshipper could carry into prayer. The lettering on the base, AVE GRATIA PLENA DO, quotes the angel's greeting to Mary at the Annunciation. Because the Man of Sorrows sits in the peak, it would stay visible even when the wings were closed.","c":"The tender detail, the Child's hand tugging at the neckline, gives life to a symmetrical, formal scheme, and the blue, rose and gold of the central panel are handled with jewel-like care. The Virgin's calm face is conventional, and the adult-featured baby is an old convention that Giotto's followers had not yet shed. But as a small devotional object, its pointed top crowned by the Man of Sorrows, it is skillfully made and cleverly conceived."},"nga:404":{"d":"The right wing of the triptych, about 45 by 12.4 centimeters, shows Saint John the Evangelist as a balding old man with a fringe of long gray hair and a full beard, his forehead creased and his brow furrowed. His rose-pink robe, lined dark olive-green and turned back at neck and wrists, covers a topaz-blue garment. He holds a dark-covered book at his side and raises a feather quill. He stands on a floor of orange, gold and blue diamonds against a punched gold ground, angled toward the central panel.","i":"John's quill and book mark him as the author of a Gospel, three letters and the Book of Revelation, as tradition holds. He is the disciple to whom the dying Christ entrusted Mary (John 19), which makes him a natural companion of the Virgin in a triptych. Painters showed him either as a beardless youth or, as here, as an old man, the aged writer; with Peter he forms a pair of saints flanking the Virgin.","c":"The wing is nearly a mirror of Peter's, with the same furrowed brow and the same solid, patient bearing, so that key and quill become the only real difference. That is economical but a little mechanical. Yet the pink robe against its dark olive lining is the finest color passage in the wings, and the raised quill gives the figure a rare moment of action. A competent, handsome wing, subordinate to the central panel as it should be."},"met:436230":{"d":"A horizontal oil painting, about 79 by 98 centimeters, shows a fashionable interior in which a man and a woman, among others, are absorbed in a game of cards. The museum's keywords also list a dog and a domestic servant, the quiet witnesses of a drawing-room evening. Painted around 1756 to 1759, it is a French conversation piece, a type of picture that records polite society at leisure, with its furnishings, costumes and manners, in the setting of a private house.","i":"Cards were the great pastime of eighteenth-century French society, played for money as well as pleasure, and the card table was where conversation, flirtation and rank were all on display. Moralists also condemned gambling, so such a picture can carry a gentle warning as well as a compliment, though a given canvas may intend nothing of the kind. The servant and the dog place the game inside a well-run household.","c":"Dumesnil is a minor master of a minor genre, and he lacks the intimacy of Chardin and the brilliance of Boucher. His interest is documentary: dress, furniture and the choreography of a card party, set down with an even, polite finish. If the figures are stiff and the mood is cool, the coolness may be the point, since the polite surface was the style of the world he painted. It remains a clear window on Parisian leisure in the 1750s."},"met:15119":{"d":"A man named in the title as Cyrus Mansfield is the subject of this tiny portrait, only about 7 by 5 centimeters, painted around 1808 in watercolor on ivory. Its maker was then about seventeen, a student at Yale who reportedly helped to pay his way by painting small likenesses. The size is that of a keepsake to be held in the hand or worn, and the thin, pale ivory lets transparent color glow over the face.","i":"Samuel F. B. Morse is known today as the inventor of the telegraph and of the code that bears his name, but he began as a painter and wanted to be a great one. At Yale he painted miniatures for classmates and townspeople, and after college his talent led him to study with Washington Allston in Boston and London. This miniature belongs to those student years, when a likeness on ivory earned pocket money and built a reputation.","c":"A miniature by a teenager is likely to show care before confidence, and that is what to look for: neat modeling, a fixed pose, a face handled with caution. What matters is the habit of close observation and drawing from life, which would serve Morse in his later portraits and in the large, ambitious pictures of the 1820s. As a work of art it is modest; as a document of a famous man's beginnings it is more rewarding."},"met:11604":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Mrs. Daniel DeSaussure Bacot, is the subject of this vertical portrait of about 76 by 63 centimeters, a standard portrait size of roughly 30 by 25 inches, painted around 1820. The museum's keywords note lace, a necklace and a shawl, the fashionable details of a well-to-do woman's dress. It is a commissioned society portrait, made in the years when Morse spent seasons painting portraits in Charleston, South Carolina.","i":"In the early republic portraiture was the one branch of painting that paid, and Morse, who dreamed of grand history subjects, took commissions from prosperous families. The names DeSaussure and Bacot are associated with Charleston, where Morse worked in these years. A married woman's portrait recorded her standing as well as her face, and the lace, necklace and shawl, a fashionable accessory of the period, show the family's means and taste.","c":"Morse's portraits of about 1820 are careful, sober and a little cool. He had absorbed from Allston and his London years a taste for rich, warm color, but sitters in a commercial practice wanted a clear likeness. Lace, a necklace and a shawl give a trained painter the chance to display skill with textures. As a portrait it is a sound record rather than an inspired one, and it shows why Morse longed to escape portraiture for history painting."},"nga:166463":{"d":"Looking across a cavernous, half-domed chamber, the viewer sees more than a hundred men in black suits and white high-pointed collars at curving rows of desks, standing in groups, or leaning on the barrier wall. A chandelier has been lowered, and a silhouetted man on a ladder reaches for a light on its top tier. Speckled columns with white capitals and crimson curtains ring the room, and gold-bordered panels fill the dome. In a balcony at the right a man in Pawnee dress and a tall headdress looks out beside a clock reading 6:14. The huge canvas is signed and dated 1822.","i":"Morse chose the old House chamber, now Statuary Hall in the Capitol, for a grand picture without a hero: the hero is the institution. Lighting the chandelier is often read as an emblem of enlightened government. Morse reportedly meant to show the huge canvas to paying crowds and sell prints, a plan that brought him little profit. The man in Pawnee dress is a reminder that Native delegations were visiting Washington in these years.","c":"Morse sets himself a hard problem, a crowd of men in near-identical black coats, and solves it with perspective: the curve of desks, columns and curtains leads the eye to the chandelier, where one small act, a man on a ladder, becomes the picture's drama. The cost is that the figures are tiny and alike, so the whole is more survey than story. It is a patient, architectural, curiously modern record, stronger than its modest fortunes suggest."},"nga:176300":{"d":"A man, named in the title as Joseph Gales, is shown in a tiny oil panel, about 14 by 9 centimeters, as head and upper torso in three-quarter view. He faces slightly to the right while his head turns toward the viewer, with a subtle, partial smile and dark, styled hair. He wears a dark coat over a white collar or scarf. The background is dark and shadowy, with no visible jewelry or objects, so the face is the whole subject.","i":"The sitter is probably the Washington newspaper editor Joseph Gales, whose National Intelligencer reported the debates of Congress. The date, 1821 to 1822, falls in the months when Morse was preparing his large picture of the House of Representatives, and small panels like this one may have served as portrait studies for it, though that is a probability and not a record. Such a study captures a face quickly, before it is placed in a crowd.","c":"As a small oil study it escapes the stiffness of finished portraits: the partial smile and the turn of the head suggest a man caught mid-conversation, and the plain dark ground and coat concentrate everything in the face. The handling is economical and direct. Its limits are scale and finish, since it is a sketch and not a statement, but it shows the quick, observant eye that gave Morse's best likenesses their life."},"nga:60790":{"d":"A man named in the title as Eliphalet Terry is the subject of this vertical oil portrait of about 76 by 63 centimeters, painted around 1824, a standard size of roughly 30 by 25 inches. It matches the portrait of his wife, Lydia Coit Terry, in the same museum, so the two were evidently made as pendants, a husband and wife hung together. As a type it is a commissioned likeness of an American gentleman of the 1820s.","i":"Pendant portraits of husband and wife were a standard commission for American families of the 1820s, hung in a parlor to declare a household's standing and its sense of marriage as a partnership. Morse, who had trained for history painting under Allston and in London, found that portraits paid, and in these years he made his living by them. The title gives only the sitter's name, so his story must be sought outside the picture.","c":"Morse's portraits of these years are typically plain, careful and sober, with a firm, candid statement of the sitter. They lack the feathery flesh painting of Stuart and the gloss of Sully, and they gain a certain honesty by it. A likeness of this kind is best judged beside its pendant, where the pair's shared scale and plain design give the household a quiet dignity."},"nga:60791":{"d":"A seated woman is shown from the waist up, angled slightly to the side but facing forward, with fair skin, rosy cheeks and dark brown curly hair. She wears a white lace cap and a lace collar over a dark garment, with a red fabric draped around her shoulders. The background is plain, with an indistinct vertical line at one side. The vertical canvas, about 76 by 63 centimeters, was painted around 1824 as a pendant to her husband's portrait.","i":"The title gives Lydia Coit Terry both her own name and her married one, as museums do for married women of the period. The lace cap, which married women wore indoors in the 1820s, and the collar mark a woman of settled age and respectable standing, while the red drapery adds a note of warmth. Hung beside her husband's portrait, the picture records a household's standing as well as a face.","c":"The strength is the simple design: the plain ground, the white cap and collar and the single bar of red make a clear, memorable pattern, and the sitter looks out with an unfussy directness. Morse is no flatterer here. What is missing is the gloss of the more fashionable portraitists, since the handling is dry and careful, and the result is a good example of the sober American portrait tradition."},"met:11602":{"d":"De Witt Clinton, governor of New York and the great promoter of the Erie Canal, is the subject of this vertical oil portrait of 1826, about 76 by 64 centimeters, close to the standard portrait size of 30 by 25 inches. It is a likeness of a public man at the height of his fame, painted by an artist who was then helping to found the National Academy of Design in New York.","i":"Clinton was governor of New York when the Erie Canal opened in 1825, and he had backed the project through years of ridicule that called it Clinton's Ditch. A portrait of 1826 therefore honors a civic achievement as much as a man, and he died only two years later. For Morse, who became the first president of the National Academy of Design, painting such a figure was both a commission and a mark of his standing in New York.","c":"Morse's portraits of public men tend toward dignity and clarity rather than drama, and they rarely flatter. A likeness of this kind succeeds when the face carries authority without pose, and Morse's candor and solid modeling suited such subjects better than the dash of Sully or the grandeur of Lawrence. Its limits are those of the commission: formal, serious, restrained, a record of a public man more than a portrait of a personality."},"met:11605":{"d":"A large canvas, about 187 by 146 centimeters, shows Morse's daughter Susan seated on a chaise longue in a dress trimmed with lace and a ribbon, with a sketchbook and a sketch, a footstool and a shoe in the scene. The museum's themes are Drawing and Contemplation, and the title adds The Muse, so the picture turns a family portrait into a quiet allegory of the artist's inspiration. It was painted around 1836 to 1837, when Susan was about seventeen.","i":"Morse painted this portrait at a turning point: within a few years the telegraph would claim most of his attention. Calling his daughter the Muse can be read as a double tribute, to the young woman as his own inspiration and to drawing as a calling. A father's portrait of his daughter on the edge of adulthood also carries an affectionate, private note beyond the public conventions of a commissioned portrait.","c":"This is among the most ambitious of Morse's portraits, and its easy, reclining pose gives it a relaxed intimacy that his formal commissions lack. The handling of lace and ribbon is likely to be the high point, and the affection is evident. The muse conceit is polite rather than inspired, and the setting is somewhat conventional, but as a portrait of a daughter by a painter-father it has a warmth that most of his society likenesses do not."},"met:436615":{"d":"Men and women gather in a dining room in this small panel, about 41 by 66 centimetres. The museum's listing records the interior and the diners but nothing more specific, so the exact poses and dishes cannot be described. The type is the Dutch merry company: a group of fashionably dressed figures, drawn on a small scale, eating, drinking and flirting in a well-appointed room. Dirck Hals, the younger brother of the portraitist Frans Hals, was one of the Haarlem painters who made it popular in the 1620s.","i":"Seventeenth-century viewers could read merry-company pictures in two ways: as lively portraits of sociable, well-to-do life, and as gentle warnings about food, drink and love that were thought to lead to excess. Their small scale and polished finish suited the private rooms of Haarlem and Amsterdam collectors. The date of 1628 falls in the type's first great period of popularity.","c":"Dirck Hals is a more genteel and less spontaneous painter than his brother: his small, elegant figures are set in a tidy tableau, with dress and furniture handled in a smooth, descriptive way. What he lacks in the headlong brushwork of Frans he supplies in a witty sense of social theatre. Without more detail, this banquet can fairly be judged a competent, attractive example, lively and well organised but seldom deeper than polite entertainment."},"nga:118611":{"d":"A man tunes a lute, one hand over the strings and the other turning the pegs, his round face tipped toward the neck so that he looks out from the corners of his eyes. He has a curled dark blond mustache, a faint goatee, curly light brown hair and a soft-brimmed gray hat, and wears a wide white collar and a billowing fawn-brown cloak with red ribbons at the knees. Beside him a table under an aquamarine cloth is crowded with a clay pipe in a bowl of embers, a ewer, a glass, a plate with a roll and knife, and a small violin.","i":"The museum identifies the sitter as the painter himself. A musician surrounded by tobacco, drink and food belongs to a Dutch tradition of pictures about the senses, which can be read as a celebration of pleasure or a gentle warning about it; tuning an instrument is also a familiar figure for seeking harmony. Molenaer worked in Haarlem and was married to the painter Judith Leyster, and here he casts himself as a good-humored entertainer, not a dignified master.","c":"Molenaer is no Hals, and the handling is brisk rather than brilliant. But the sideways glance, the comfortable sprawl of the pose and the crowded table give the picture an immediacy that many formal self-portraits lack. The still life of pipe, ewer and plate is painted with real relish, and the whole is amusing without being coarse, a likeable record of a painter's self-image."},"met:435842":{"d":"Calm water, a state yacht and other craft fill this horizontal panel, about 70 by 92 centimeters, painted around 1660. The title and the museum's keywords name only ships and boats, so the picture belongs to the Dutch marine tradition in which still water, slack sails and a broad sky are the real subject. State yachts were the ornate vessels of the Dutch government and admiralties, used for ceremony and official travel, and painters liked to show their carved sterns and flags.","i":"The Dutch Republic lived by the sea, and pictures of calm water with ships said something about the confidence of a trading nation: order, prosperity and command of the waters. Jan van de Cappelle was a wealthy Amsterdam dyer who painted alongside his business and collected pictures, among them many Rembrandt drawings. His taste for still air and silvery light made him one of the leading Dutch painters of calms.","c":"Van de Cappelle's calms are among the most poised sea pictures of the Dutch Golden Age: the sky takes the lion's share of the panel, the ships sit lightly on glassy water, and silvery tones keep everything in a single quiet key. What he gives up is drama; his ships do little and his seas never rise. He pleases by serenity and by exact observation of light on water, not by incident."},"nga:50724":{"d":"A young woman, shown head and shoulders, stands before a dense juniper bush whose dark, spiky leaves frame her pale face like a halo. Her hair falls in tight ringlets, her eyes meet the viewer's without warmth, and her mouth is closed. Behind her a misty landscape of dark trees, still water and far hills dissolves into a pale sky. The oil painting is about 38 by 37 centimeters and has been cut down at the bottom, losing the lower part of the figure.","i":"The juniper is the sitter's emblem: ginepro, in Italian, puns on Ginevra. Ginevra de' Benci, from a wealthy Florentine banking family, was probably painted about the time of her marriage in 1474, though some scholars link the picture with the Venetian ambassador Bernardo Bembo, whose emblem appears on the back. The painter was in his twenties and had trained in Verrocchio's workshop, which makes this one of the earliest surviving portraits by Leonardo.","c":"The face is a calculated riddle: cool, pale and unsmiling against the glittering dark juniper and a landscape dissolving in blue haze. Oil paint lets Leonardo model skin and curls with a softness that tempera could not, and the air behind her announces a lifelong study of atmosphere. The expression is almost blank and the pose stiff beside his later portraits, and the cut bottom leaves the composition lopsided, but the promise is unmistakable."},"nga:50725":{"d":"On the back of the portrait panel, about 38 by 37 centimeters like the front, a second picture shows an emblem instead of a face: a wreath of laurel and palm, with juniper, encircles a scroll lettered VIRTUTEM FORMA DECORAT, Latin for 'beauty adorns virtue'. The ground behind it is painted to look like porphyry, the speckled purple-red stone prized since antiquity. The design is compact and graphic, in strong contrast with the soft modeling of the portrait on the other side.","i":"Laurel and palm are signs of victory and virtue, and juniper is Ginevra's own plant. The motto is generally linked to Bernardo Bembo, the Venetian ambassador in Florence, whose emblem used laurel and palm, so the reverse is usually read as his tribute, joining Ginevra to his device. Beauty and virtue together were a favorite theme of Renaissance love poetry, and porphyry, a stone associated with rank and permanence, gives the claim a lasting look.","c":"The back of a panel is a modest place, and Leonardo treats it as a design problem: a crisp wreath and scroll, a fake stone that shows off the painter's skill at imitation, and a motto that answers the front. It lacks the mystery of the portrait, and its pleasures are those of wit and craft rather than painterly depth. But it shows how the panel was conceived, as a double-sided object in which image and word complete each other."},"cma:97139":{"d":"A small oil painting on wood, about 41 by 30 centimeters, shows the Virgin and the Christ Child, the most common devotional subject of the Renaissance, catalogued under Leonardo's name and dated about 1500. The upright format and modest size suit a picture for private prayer or a domestic room, seen at close range. Leonardo's Madonnas typically draw mother and child into a compact group, model them in soft light and shade, and give them an interplay of glances and gestures.","i":"The Madonna and Child expresses Mary's motherhood and Christ's humanity, with a note of foreknowledge of the Passion. Leonardo often gives the Child a playful object that foretells it: a carnation in the Munich Madonna, a yarn-winder shaped like a cross in the Madonna of the Yarnwinder. Around 1500 he was returning to Florence after years in Milan, and his Madonnas of these years turn the formal icon into a lively family moment.","c":"Leonardo gave the Madonna a new naturalism: a mother who is a living woman, a child who twists and plays, a group built by soft light instead of outline. A small panel dated about 1500 and attached to his name invites comparison with those achievements, and the questions to ask are those his own hand answers best: the unity of the group, the softness of the faces, the depth of shadow. Followers borrowed the formula without the depth."},"met:436520":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Marie Adélaïde of Savoy, Duchess of Burgundy, is the subject of this vertical portrait, about 73 by 59 centimeters, painted in 1710. It is a court likeness in the French manner of the last years of Louis XIV, the kind of picture that recorded a member of the royal family for the king and his circle. The painter, Pierre Gobert, was one of the specialists in portraits of that world, and the size suits a half-length or bust-length image.","i":"Marie Adélaïde came from Savoy as a child of ten to marry the grandson of Louis XIV, and her high spirits made her a favorite of the aging king. In 1710, the year of this portrait, she gave birth to the boy who would become Louis XV. Two years later she died of measles, six days before her husband, and the surviving infant became the heir. The picture records a princess at the height of her position.","c":"Gobert's portraits aim to please: smooth surfaces, tasteful color, features made a little more regular than life. They are polished and dignified, and they rarely probe character. Set beside the grand portraits of Rigaud or the abundance of Largillierre, Gobert looks reserved. Yet this was the manner a court wanted for its royal ladies, and as an image of a short-lived, much-loved princess the picture has more interest than refinement alone would give."},"met:437975":{"d":"Boats ride a sea settling after a storm along a Sicilian coast at evening, on a canvas of about 83 by 107 centimeters dated 1853. The recorded motifs are boats, the sea and the evening light, and the Met classes it as landscape painting. Andreas Achenbach, a leading Düsseldorf painter, was famous for sea pieces and storm scenes in which the weather itself is the subject and the figures are small.","i":"The theme of calm after tempest is a Romantic favorite, setting nature's violence beside the survival of the boats and the people in them, and a glowing sunset gives it a note of reassurance. Sicily drew northern painters seeking strong light and dramatic coasts. Düsseldorf paintings of this kind were widely collected abroad, and American collectors in the 1850s knew the school well through exhibitions in New York.","c":"Achenbach was among the German painters who first treated the sea in violent weather as it really looks, and a post-storm sunset lets him contrast luminous sky with heavy cloud. His handling is fluent and his command of atmosphere convincing, though the mood can tip toward the theatrical. He lacks Turner's radical daring but surpasses many Düsseldorf contemporaries in sheer observation."},"nga:43439":{"d":"A small boy stands full-length in a landscape, both hands in front of him, holding a thin stick in one and a wide-brimmed straw hat with a blue bow in the other. He has a full face, large round eyes and short, curly fair hair, and wears a dress striped in blue and orange with small flowers, over white trousers and black shoes. At his side a small black dog sniffs a leafy branch. Soft green hills, scattered trees and a light sky lie behind. The tall canvas, about 115 by 72 centimeters, was painted in 1845.","i":"In the 1840s small boys often wore dresses over long white trousers until they were breeched, that is, put into men's clothes, and this portrait shows Henry at that stage of childhood. Portraits of children with pets, hats and toys were popular with middle-class American families, who often bought them from regional painters of the kind now called folk or naive. The dog, a standard emblem of loyalty and companionship, gives a child's day a little story.","c":"Waters works in the plain, linear style often called folk or naive, and the picture's appeal lies there: crisp contour, bright flat color, a striped dress that sets up a lively pattern against the green, and big round eyes that give the boy an unnerving directness. The space is shallow and the proportions a little odd, but such departures give the portrait its character. It is more memorable than many correct academic portraits of children from the same decade."},"nga:43636":{"d":"Two boys sit side by side on the grass in front of a landscape of hills, scattered forest and a cloudy sky. The boy on the left has short brown hair, holds a book and has a black cat on his lap, and a straw hat lies near his feet. The boy on the right has light blond hair and holds an apple and a black hat. Both are dressed in formal outfits. The canvas, about 112 by 89 centimeters, was painted around 1845.","i":"Double portraits of siblings celebrated the family and the new regard for childhood in antebellum America. The props probably speak for each boy's character: a book and a cat on the left, an apple and a hat on the right. They are best read as emblems of a happy, well-ordered home, not as records of fact. Such pictures were made for parents to hang in a parlor and to keep as a record while the children grew.","c":"Waters arranges the two boys with a naive symmetry, the dark cat on one lap and the dark hat in the other boy's hand balancing across the center, and the result has the stiff charm of the type. The faces are simple, the bodies stiffly joined and the space shallow, yet crisp contours, bright accents and an open sky give it a pleasant clarity. Good of its kind, and a record of an 1840s childhood."},"met:437171":{"d":"A castle stands in a landscape with trees, clouds, a man, a woman and a dog, on a small panel of about 40 by 56 centimeters. The picture is undated, and the artist, a Dutch painter of the 1600s, is known for landscapes and village scenes with buildings. The small figures and the dog give scale to the structure and a hint of daily life in its shadow.","i":"Old castles and ruins were favorite subjects in seventeenth-century Dutch landscape because they carried a pleasant melancholy: a reminder of the passing of time and of fallen powers. The small figures and dog show that life goes on beneath the old building. Dutch buyers collected such pictures for display at home, which explains their modest scale.","c":"An unpretentious picture in which the old building and the clouded sky do most of the work. The small figures give scale but remain secondary, and the composition is orderly rather than daring. Murant is a minor master beside the great Dutch landscape painters, and this picture shows him as a careful, reliable craftsman with an eye for old buildings."},"met:436278":{"d":"A horizontal oil painting, about 71 by 88 centimeters, mounted on a wood panel, shows female nudes together with a tree and fruit, according to the museum's keywords. The title, Allegory, says that the figures stand for an idea and are not portraits or characters from a story. Etty built his career on such pictures, painting the nude in warm, luminous flesh tones with a loose, painterly touch learned from Titian and Rubens. The undated panel is typical of the genre he made his own.","i":"Fruit and nude women are an old language of abundance, fertility and the seasons, and in a title as open as Allegory the likeliest meaning is plenty, the bounty of nature, though the idea is not spelled out. Etty made the nude his lifelong subject, and myth and allegory gave a British painter respectable reasons to paint it. Even so, his nudes drew complaints of indecency alongside the admiration of his colleagues.","c":"Etty's gift is warm, luminous flesh painting, learned from Titian and Rubens, and the nude is where he is most himself. Less convincing is the allegorical machinery, which reads as a pretext, and his compositions can be loose, with anatomy that gives way to the pleasure of paint. A small allegory of fruit and flesh is typical: sensuous, a little sketchy, more persuasive as paint than as idea."},"met:436279":{"d":"Three nude women, the Graces or Charites of Greek myth, are the subject of this small vertical oil painting on millboard, about 57 by 48 centimeters. The traditional formula, handed down from ancient sculpture and fresco, shows the three sisters close together, linked by their arms, two facing the viewer and one turned away. Millboard, a stiff, inexpensive support, was often used for quick oil studies, which suggests that this is a sketch rather than a finished exhibition picture.","i":"The Graces, daughters of Zeus, were goddesses of charm, beauty and joy, companions of Venus, and the giving, receiving and returning of favors was a standard reading of their linked dance. They offered artists a respectable excuse to paint three nudes together, which is why they appear from Botticelli and Raphael to Canova, whose marble group was famous in Britain in Etty's day.","c":"Etty's best passages are in the flesh, painted with the warmth and glow he learned from the Venetians, and three bodies give him curves and shifts of light to enjoy. A study on millboard suits him, since the quickness keeps the figures alive. But his anatomy can be soft and loose, and these Graces lack the sculptural clarity of Canova or the exuberance of Rubens. The charm lies in the touch, in a painter delighting in his material."},"met:437068":{"d":"Saint Anthony of Padua receives a vision of Christ in this miniature painted on ivory by Anton Raphael Mengs; the museum gives no date or size. The recorded motifs include the saint, a skull and Jesus. In the standard version of the subject, the Christ Child appears to the Franciscan friar as he prays or reads, and the skull reminds the viewer of mortality. Ivory miniatures served private devotion as well as portraiture, small enough to hold in the hand.","i":"Anthony of Padua (1195-1231), a Portuguese-born Franciscan, was celebrated as a preacher, and later tradition said that the Christ Child appeared to him, standing on the book he was reading or in his arms, a sign of his closeness to God. The skull, a memento mori, is a common companion of saints in prayer. Mengs was the son and pupil of a miniaturist and began his career making small works, so this devotional piece could belong to his early years.","c":"As a miniature the picture rewards close looking: fine drawing and delicate color on the glow of ivory. Mengs, later the leading Neoclassical theorist, was in youth a prodigy of technical finish, and a devotional miniature like this shows that early dexterity. Its sweetness is typical of religious miniatures of the mid-century, and it is likely to be polite rather than ecstatic, as befits a painter who would become a champion of restraint."},"met:438780":{"d":"A boy with flowers appears to personify Pleasure in this pastel on paper laid down on canvas, about 62 by 49 centimeters, made around 1754 by Anton Raphael Mengs. The title and the museum's keywords, boys and flowers, point to an allegory in the form of a youthful figure, a conceit of Rococo taste. The pastel medium gives the soft, powdery surface that eighteenth-century viewers prized in such pictures.","i":"Allegorical heads of children and youths, named for virtues, seasons or pleasures, were a staple of mid-eighteenth-century taste, and pastel, popularized by Rosalba Carriera, was their ideal medium. Mengs, trained from childhood by his father in pastel and miniature, was about twenty-six, and he would soon turn from the Rococo to the severe Neoclassical manner he would champion. This picture comes from the Rococo side of his work.","c":"Pastel suits the softness of the subject and the glow of youth, and Mengs handles the medium with skill. But the picture is a charming trifle, and its sweetness belongs to Rococo conventions that he later set aside in his own theory. For that very reason it is interesting: the champion of Neoclassical severity shows here a gift for prettiness."},"nga:195461":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 264 by 152 centimeters, shows the Adoration of the Shepherds, the Gospel episode in which shepherds come to see the newborn Christ. Pictures of the subject usually gather the Virgin, Joseph, the Child in his manger and the shepherds in a humble stable, often with an ox and ass and with light that seems to come from the infant. The scale and upright shape are those of an altarpiece or other large devotional picture, painted around 1764 to 1765, when Mengs was working at the Spanish court in Madrid.","i":"The shepherds, poor men keeping watch by night, were the first to be told of Christ's birth, which made their adoration a favorite image of humility rewarded. Mengs, admired in his day as the leading painter of the new classical taste, probably looked to Correggio's famous Holy Night, in which the Child himself lights the scene, and to Raphael for clear drawing, so that a Nativity of his combines the glow of the one with the order of the other.","c":"Later critics often judged Mengs a better theorist than painter: his blend of Raphael's drawing, Correggio's light and the antique's nobility yields pictures that are correct, smooth and cool. The risk is an academic chill, a sense of ingredients assembled by rule, while a contemporary such as Tiepolo is full of invention. Still, clarity of design and quiet devotion in such an altarpiece show why Europe's courts admired him."},"met:441115":{"d":"The artist Anton Raphael Mengs portrays himself in an oil painting of about 90 by 66 centimeters dated 1776, three years before his death. The recorded motifs name only the man, the portrait and the self-portrait. An artist's self-portrait is a deliberate statement of how he wished to be seen, and by 1776 Mengs was among the most celebrated painters in Europe, first painter to the Spanish king.","i":"Mengs was born in Bohemia, trained by a miniaturist father, became court painter in Dresden as a teenager and settled in Rome, where his friendship with the archaeologist Johann Joachim Winckelmann helped make him the leading painter of the early Neoclassical movement. He also served Charles III of Spain as first painter. His self-portrait, from the last years of his life, is an image of the professional at the peak of his reputation.","c":"Mengs's self-portrait is cool and self-possessed, as one would expect: his Neoclassical training makes it more composed than expressive, and it would be unfair to ask it for the psychological drama of a Rembrandt. It has the clarity, sober color and firm drawing that he praised in his theory. As a document of an artist-theorist at forty-eight, it is dignified and slightly stiff."},"cma:142311":{"d":"Infante Don Luis de Borbón, a son of King Philip V of Spain, is portrayed on a canvas of about 153 by 100 centimeters, painted around 1776 by Anton Raphael Mengs. The museum describes the encrustations of medals and the cacophonous fabrics that crowd the figure, a deliberate experiment in representing status, privilege and royal honors. The portrait is oil on canvas, in the three-quarter-length format probably favored for princes.","i":"Don Luis (1727-1785), a younger brother of Charles III, was made an archbishop as a boy because he was a younger son, but his philandering cost him his Church titles and brought banishment from the court. In his exile he became an avant-garde patron of the arts, especially of the young Goya. Mengs painted the infante when Enlightenment skepticism was beginning to question the claims of inherited power, which the overloaded display of honors seems to address.","c":"Mengs, famous for classical purity, is almost baroque here in his accumulation of fabrics and decorations: the picture pushes his experiment in representing status to the edge of excess, and that is its interest. The face is calmer than the clothes, and the sitter's complicated life is not hinted at. As a document of Spanish court taste and of a prince on the margins of power, it is more fascinating than beautiful."},"met:437067":{"d":"Johann Joachim Winckelmann (1717-1768), the German scholar of ancient art, is portrayed in an oil painting of about 64 by 49 centimeters, dated around 1777 and attributed to his friend Anton Raphael Mengs. Because Winckelmann died in 1768, a portrait dated about 1777 must be a later version, probably based on an earlier likeness. The recorded themes name only men and portraits, and the modest scale suits a study or a friend's wall.","i":"In his 1755 Thoughts on the Imitation of Greek Works, Winckelmann described Greek art as marked by noble simplicity and quiet grandeur, an idea that shaped the Neoclassical movement. Mengs, his close friend in Rome, painted the Parnassus ceiling of the Villa Albani under his influence as a manifesto of the new taste. After Winckelmann's murder in Trieste in 1768, his likeness became an image of the founder of art history, and replicas would naturally be sought by friends and admirers.","c":"A posthumous variant lacks the freshness of a sitting, and it is probably better as memorial than as portrait. Mengs's polish, careful but a little dry, matches the austerity of the sitter's own ideals. It is an honorable image, a picture of a mind more than of a personality, and in that it suits the man who taught his age to see antiquity as a standard."},"nga:195586":{"d":"A woman sits in this small canvas, about 46 by 37 centimeters, painted between 1880 and 1885. The museum's keyword is only that she is seated at full length, so the picture is best described by type: a figure study of a woman at rest, the kind of quiet single figure that Ribot painted in his later years. His habitual manner built figures out of a dark ground, with strong highlights and a dense, forceful handling of paint.","i":"Ribot's best-known pictures are dark-toned scenes of cooks and kitchen boys that reworked the chiaroscuro of Ribera and Zurbarán, and they won him the admiration of Courbet's and Manet's generation of realists. By the 1880s he was a respected elder who changed little, and quiet figures like this one distill his lifelong interest in form emerging from shadow, with gravity and without anecdote.","c":"Ribot's reputation rests on the dark, forceful tenebrism of the 1860s and 1870s, and his later pictures, however handsome, tend to repeat the formula: black ground, a pool of light on face and hands, thick paint. At its best the manner gives a quiet figure weight and gravity with few means. If a late work is less daring than his best, it keeps his integrity, and it shows how much the realists of his time owed to the Spanish masters."},"met:437456":{"d":"A family group is the subject: the museum's keywords list a man, a woman, a boy, a girl and the family, in a vertical oil of about 55 by 46 centimeters, dated only 'possibly' to about 1880 to 1885. It records the working people of the Breton coast, where fishing families lived by the sea. The title is plural, fishermen and their families, so the picture is evidently a group and not a single household, and it blends portrait with a scene of daily life.","i":"From the 1860s many painters traveled to Brittany, where fisherfolk with their distinctive dress and customs seemed a survival of older France in a modernizing country. Such pictures could be picturesque or serious; Ribot, in the last decade of his life, belongs to the serious side, giving working people the dignity of his dark, grave manner. Family groups of the poor also appealed to collectors who saw in them honesty and endurance.","c":"Ribot's grave manner suits working people, to whom he gives dignity without sentiment, and a group of fisherfolk gives him more to organize than a single figure. The danger of such a group is that the figures sit side by side without relating, and his usual remedy is to let one pool of light gather them. Where that works, the picture has the sober humanity that earned Ribot the admiration of younger realists."},"nga:71503":{"d":"From a slight height above a dirt road, the view passes a grove of trees to a broad plain and a narrow teal-blue lake at the foot of towering, hazy mountains. Snow lies in the peaks and mist drifts among them, and the meadows form a patchwork of green and sand-brown fields crossed by an ice-blue stream. Tiny buildings line the lake, with a church on a low hill. In the shadows of the foreground trees people camp beside a covered wagon, a donkey and a glowing fire. The canvas is huge, about 1.8 by 3 metres.","i":"Albert Bierstadt, born in Germany and raised in Massachusetts, trained in Düsseldorf, where landscape painters were taught to combine meticulous detail with heroic composition, and made sketching trips in Switzerland before returning to America in 1857. This large canvas uses Alpine scenery as a stage for the sublime, with tiny human figures dwarfed by monumental nature, an idea that would define his later paintings of the American West.","c":"A feat of technique from a young painter: the vast distances, the atmospheric haze and the precisely painted details of fields, buildings and figures are impressive. But the drama is theatrical, and the foreground screen of trees and the tiny campers give the scene a staged quality. The sublime here is a formula rather than a personal vision. It is best seen as an overture to his Western canvases, which have more energy."},"met:10154":{"d":"A huge panorama nearly three metres wide opens from a green meadow to snow-covered peaks. In the middle distance a waterfall drops from cliffs into a still lake that mirrors the rock. In the foreground a Native American encampment sits among tall trees: tepees stand in groups at the left and at the right beside the water, horses graze or are led across the grass, and figures gather around fires and by the shore. Warm light from the left touches the meadow, the cliffs and the dark pines, while the central mountain glows white in a pale haze beneath a sky of drifting cloud. The surface is smooth and detailed.","i":"Bierstadt joined Frederick W. Lander's survey expedition in 1859 and made many sketches, from which he worked up this picture in New York in 1863. The peak was named for Lander after his death in the Civil War, and the scene is an idealised composite, not a view. The people in the foreground are Shoshone; a reviewer in Harper's Weekly called the picture a historic landscape, since it showed a way of life believed to be vanishing. It was promoted with a single-picture exhibition and engravings, shown opposite Church's Heart of the Andes at the 1864 Metropolitan Fair, and sold in 1865 for 25,000 dollars, then a record for an American painting. The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 1907.","c":"As spectacle it succeeds: the lake and waterfall lead the eye from the camp to the glowing peak, and the scale and clean light made it a sensation in the 1860s. As landscape it is arranged more than observed, and contemporary critics complained that the rocks lacked geological solidity. The Shoshone camp is painted as scenery, small and generalised, which says more about how the picture views the West than about the people in it. It remains the defining statement of western landscape painting of the Civil War era."},"met:10156":{"d":"A single tree is the whole subject, painted in oil on paper mounted on board, a sheet only 24 by 20 centimeters. It is a study, not a finished picture: the kind of quick, close observation Bierstadt made outdoors to consult later in the studio when composing his large landscapes. Dated about 1864, it is raw material of the sort he gathered on his travels for his big canvases.","i":"Bierstadt's grand paintings of the American West were assembled in the studio from many small studies like this one: rocks, clouds and trees sketched from nature, then combined into a composition. The study shows the working method behind the spectacle, and his training in Düsseldorf, where landscape painters learned to base their pictures on careful study from nature. Bierstadt was born in Germany, raised in Massachusetts and studied in Düsseldorf in the 1850s.","c":"Small studies like this often have more life than the grand canvases they fed. Oil sketches of this kind tend to be quick, the structure of the tree grasped directly, with no pressure toward sublimity. They show that Bierstadt could observe with freshness, a quality sometimes lost in the large pictures, whose theatrical lighting and vast scale invited charges of showmanship. The study is slight, but it is honest."},"cma:172815":{"d":"A distant granite peak, Mount Starr King, rises over Yosemite Valley in this wide canvas of about 97 by 142 centimeters, dated 1866. Two Native Americans with a packhorse appear in the composition, small figures that give the landscape scale and recall the people who had lived in or visited the valley for thousands of years. Bierstadt painted it in his New York studio from the sketches he made in Yosemite in the summer of 1863.","i":"In 1864 President Lincoln signed a bill setting aside Yosemite as public land, and it became a national park in 1890. Pictures like this thrilled East Coast audiences and helped build support for protecting natural wonders. The two Native figures acknowledge that Indigenous peoples had lived in or seasonally visited the region for millennia; later government and military agencies dispossessed them, and descendants of the seven nations with ties to Yosemite now live in neighboring areas.","c":"Bierstadt's genius is for scale and atmosphere, and this picture is built to deliver both: a tiny foreground opens onto vast space, with the peak held in the distance. The formula won him fame and a mansion called Malkasten, German for paintbox, and critics have since faulted it for theatricality and idealization. Yet the idealized view helped build support for preservation, and the Native figures are given dignity even if their main job is to supply scale."},"met:10150":{"d":"The Merced River winds through Yosemite Valley beneath towering mountains, with trees, clouds and a canoe on the water. Painted in 1866 on a canvas about 91 by 127 centimeters, it shows the valley as Bierstadt's audiences wished to see it: broad, calm and magnificent. He had first visited Yosemite in 1863 and worked up such pictures in his New York studio from the sketches he made there.","i":"Yosemite was little known to most Americans when Bierstadt first went there in 1863. His paintings, with the photographs of Carleton Watkins, made it famous in the East, and in 1864 Congress and President Lincoln set the valley aside as a public trust, a step toward the national park system. The canoe is a reminder that the valley had long been home to Indigenous peoples, whose presence is easy to overlook in so grand a view.","c":"Bierstadt's Yosemite is a place made grander than life: proportions are enlarged and the light is gilded. Purists in his time and since have complained that it is stage scenery. But the best of his views are persuasive spectacle, and a painting like this, with its slow river and deep distance, conveys the scale of the Sierra in a way the black-and-white photographs of the day could not. It is a persuasive piece of national myth-making."},"cma:127224":{"d":"Lava and ash pour from Mount Vesuvius against the night sky in this small canvas of about 43 by 61 centimeters, dated 1868. The museum notes that Bierstadt, then living in London, rushed to Italy when he heard that the volcano was active again, though it is not certain that he saw an eruption; the image of spewing lava and ash is nevertheless convincing. The picture is a smaller version of a much larger canvas that is now lost.","i":"Vesuvius had drawn artists and tourists for centuries, since it buried Pompeii and Herculaneum in AD 79. Eruption pictures belong to the tradition of the Sublime, which prized natural violence viewed from safety, and painters from Joseph Wright of Derby to Turner had exploited it, with the volcano at night as a favorite. Bierstadt, best known for American mountains, shows that the same appetite for spectacle could be turned to the Old World.","c":"A reduced version of a lost large picture is an unreliable guide to the original, but the effect works: the dark sky, the glow and the flying sparks are staged with theatrical skill. Bierstadt's showman's instinct suits a volcano, and the smaller scale concentrates the drama. He lacks Wright's human witness and Turner's dissolving fire, so the result is spectacle rather than vision, but as spectacle it holds attention."},"nga:166427":{"d":"Three-quarters of this horizontal canvas is filled with roiling, deeply shadowed clouds that tower over a line of buffalo crossing a grassy meadow. The small animals form a diagonal line receding to the right; sunlight flashes on the stream where the front buffalo crosses, while the others are nearly backlit. Trees at the left whip in the wind, the mountains at the right are lost in darkness, and small patches of blue sky and a sunlit cliff face glint beyond. The canvas is about 75 by 126 centimetres.","i":"Bierstadt's Plains and Rocky Mountain scenes of the 1860s presented the American West as a place of grandeur and wildness, with the buffalo as its emblem. The impending storm adds foreboding, and in hindsight the picture can be read as an elegy: after the transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869, the great herds were destroyed within a few years. Whether Bierstadt intended any such warning is uncertain; the picture is first of all a display of weather and animal life.","c":"The sky is the real subject, and Bierstadt paints it with theatrical power: the dark, towering clouds, the shafts of light and the wind-bent trees create a convincing sense of oncoming weather. The buffalo are small but well placed, and the diagonal line gives depth. The effects are staged, and his animal drawing is less sure than his handling of mountains and light, but the picture has a mood of real foreboding."},"met:10152":{"d":"Nevada Fall, one of Yosemite's great waterfalls, plunges down a granite cliff in a vertical canvas of about 99 by 76 centimeters, painted in 1872 or 1873. Trees frame the scene, and a man and a woman appear as small figures that measure the scale of the cliffs. By then Bierstadt was an established star of western landscape, and the upright format emphasizes the sheer height of the fall.","i":"Nevada Fall drops nearly 600 feet on the Merced River above Yosemite Valley. By the 1870s Yosemite was an established destination, and a picture of its falls was both a souvenir and a statement about the nation's natural wonders. The man and woman may stand for visitors, small and awed, in the Romantic tradition that set human figures against the vastness of nature to evoke the Sublime.","c":"Bierstadt's reputation had begun to slip by the 1870s as taste turned toward quieter, French-influenced landscape, and critics found his pictures overscaled and theatrical. A vertical Nevada Fall is still dramatic, and working at the scale of a single cascade lets him concentrate on one motif rather than a panorama. It lacks the epic breadth of his earlier Yosemite views but keeps some of their wonder."},"cma:150968":{"d":"Fir trees stand against storm clouds in a small oil sketch on paper, mounted on canvas, about 35 by 47 centimeters, dated around 1870. The title names only the trees and the sky, and the working method is plain: Bierstadt made quick studies from nature to store up effects of weather and form for use in his large studio pictures. The subject is the drama of changing weather above evergreens.","i":"Oil sketches on paper were the working notes of a landscape painter: fast, direct records of light, cloud and trees, painted outdoors. Bierstadt made such sketches on his journeys in the American West and Europe and used them to build his large exhibition pieces. Clouds and conifers, repeated motifs in his big canvases, were recorded in this way, and today sketches like this are often prized for their freshness.","c":"Freed from the need to impress, Bierstadt paints with more immediacy, and a sketch like this gets weather and form down in a few sure strokes. It may be the most natural thing in his output, and for many modern viewers it is more convincing than the grand works for which he was famous. It is slight, and nobody should expect a composition, but it gives a clear glimpse of how he saw."},"nga:166428":{"d":"Across a gleaming forest-green lake rise tall, jagged, snow-covered mountains. A steep hill at the left, striped with rows of upright boulders and slender dark trees, carries a narrow waterfall into the lake, and strong sunlight falls from the upper left on its centre and the curving shoreline. At the right the ground is carpeted in rust-red, sage-green and tan growth studded with boulders. In the foreground dead tree trunks jut from the water, and beyond them a small black bear wanders at the water's edge. The canvas is about 154 by 244 centimetres.","i":"Mount Corcoran is a peak of the Sierra Nevada in California, presumably named for William Wilson Corcoran, the Washington banker and collector who founded the Corcoran Gallery of Art; the gallery bought this picture in 1878. Bierstadt, who first went West in 1859, painted the wilderness as a place of untouched grandeur, with a small bear as its only inhabitant. By the later 1870s taste was turning from his grand, highly finished manner toward quieter, more tonal landscapes of French inspiration.","c":"A powerful piece of stage-craft: the lake as a mirror, the jagged peaks cut against vivid sky and the cascade of light across the left hill give the scene grandeur and depth. The details are exact, almost hard, and the formula of dark foreground, lit middle ground and misty peaks is familiar from his earlier work. The small bear is a nice touch. It works as an emblem of untouched wilderness, if less fresh than his earlier Western pictures."},"met:12849":{"d":"A river and trees appear in a landscape after a storm, on a wide oil canvas of about 68 by 98 centimeters. The Met's record lists the picture as formerly attributed to Albert Bierstadt and gives only a tentative date of about 1870 to 1880, so both the author and the date remain open. The title names the moment, the calm after bad weather, a favorite subject of nineteenth-century landscape painters who liked the drama of clearing skies.","i":"Storm-and-clearing pictures belonged to the Romantic landscape tradition, in which weather stood for emotion and divine power, and the clearing of a storm suggested renewal. Pictures in the manner of famous painters were common in the nineteenth century, because Bierstadt's success bred followers and imitators. A work described as formerly attributed to him has usually lost the name through comparison with securely documented pictures.","c":"Without a secure author, the picture must stand on its own: a competent, pleasant American landscape in the grand manner, with a calm river and a clearing sky. It has the polish that Bierstadt's admirers wanted, but the downgrade suggests that specialists missed the individual boldness of his documented work. It is a fair reminder that skill alone is not the same as a personal style."},"met:10158":{"d":"The Matterhorn's peak catches the first light of sunrise in this tall canvas, about 149 by 108 centimeters, with a tree in the foreground. The upright format stresses the height of the mountain, and the dawn is as much the subject as the peak. Bierstadt, better known for the American West, also painted the Alps, and the Met dates this picture to after 1875.","i":"The Matterhorn's first ascent in 1865 ended in disaster when four of the seven climbers fell to their deaths on the descent, and the mountain's fame since has been partly tragic and partly scenic. For nineteenth-century viewers the peak was a symbol of the Sublime: remote, dangerous and beautiful. Dawn light on a snow summit suggested purity and renewal, which suited a painter who liked to present nature as grand and uplifting.","c":"Bierstadt's love of drama finds an ideal motif in the Matterhorn: a single pyramid of rock, lit at dawn, requires little invention to be theatrical. The vertical canvas, high color and Romantic staging make it an Alpine counterpart to his Yosemite pictures. Critics find such work more manufactured than felt, but it is efficient picture-making, and the glow on the summit remains a spectacle."},"met:10149":{"d":"Lake Louise lies among snow-covered Rocky Mountains, with trees and dead wood in the foreground. Painted in oil on paper mounted to board, about 38 by 53 centimeters, it is an oil study of the kind Bierstadt made on or near the spot rather than a finished exhibition canvas. The wide format suits a broad view of lake and mountains, and the Met dates the study to about 1889.","i":"Lake Louise became famous as a tourist spot after the Canadian Pacific Railway reached the Rockies in the mid-1880s, and the lake was named for Princess Louise, a daughter of Queen Victoria. Bierstadt made his name on the American frontier in the 1860s, and by 1889 his reputation had faded. A new accessible wilderness reached by train offered fresh subjects, and studies like this were the raw material for larger pictures.","c":"As a study this has what the big pictures lost: spontaneity. Oil on paper lets him lay in cold mountain air, snow and dark trees directly, without the theatrics of studio composition. It is slight, but it shows a veteran still capable of looking hard. Beside the polished canvases of his prime, the sketch is the more convincing record of an actual place."},"nga:124525":{"d":"A herd of bison spreads across a wide plain lit by golden light, with a river winding toward distant blue mountains. In the foreground dead and dying animals lie among bleached skulls and bones, and a prairie dog peeks out of a hole at lower left. At the center a hunter seen from behind, in a feather headdress and orange loincloth, rides a rearing white horse and raises a feathered spear over a charging bison. Seven more riders gallop in from the right edge, while a fox, two deer and the nearest animals move away to the left. Hundreds of bison dot the riverbanks beneath a pale sky with a few wisps of cloud.","i":"Bierstadt made two versions of this subject in 1888, this larger one and a smaller canvas now in Cody, Wyoming. By then the great herds had been destroyed by commercial hide hunting, so the spear hunt shown here is a romantic image rather than a record. The picture is an elegy for the bison and for the Plains peoples who depended on them. An undated newspaper clipping records that the American selection committee rejected his work for the 1889 Paris Exposition as old-fashioned, and that he showed it independently as a Chevalier of the Legion of Honor. His widow gave this version to the Corcoran Gallery in 1909; it passed to the National Gallery in 2014.","c":"As spectacle it succeeds. The warm light, the huge sky and the rearing horse make an effective stage, and the skulls and carcasses placed close to the viewer give the elegy a hard edge. As description it is theatrical: the feathered hunter is a type, not an observed man. By 1889 Bierstadt's grand manner was also out of fashion with critics. Even so, it is a sober late statement, and details such as the prairie dog and the scattered bones show him still looking closely."},"met:10151":{"d":"A mountain rises in a landscape study painted in oil on paper, about 38 by 53 centimeters, dated to the 1880s. The title records nothing but the mountain, which is typical of the working sketches Bierstadt made for himself: quick studies of the forms and light of a peak, kept as reference for finished pictures in the studio. It is a private note rather than a public statement.","i":"Bierstadt traveled widely, to the Rockies, the Sierra Nevada, the Alps, Canada and Alaska, and made oil sketches as he went. They were the raw material of the large paintings that made his name, and their quick, direct handling often seems fresher than the exhibition pieces. By the 1880s the mountains were also subjects of national pride and tourism, with railways bringing the public to places he had first reached on horseback.","c":"As a sketch it works better than as a statement. A mountain on paper cannot carry the spectacle of his big canvases, and it does not try to. What it offers instead is the painter's quick intelligence about rock, snow and cloud. It is a modest work, best seen as evidence for the method behind his famous pictures, and a reminder that the painter of grand scenery began with small observation."},"met:10155":{"d":"A sea cove with a beach and trees is painted on a small wood panel, about 36 by 48 centimeters, dated to about 1880 to 1890. Only trees and a beach are shown, and the title names no place, so the picture reads as a quiet coastal view rather than a topographical record. It is a minor work in the output of a painter famous for mountains, and a reminder that he also looked at shore and water.","i":"Bierstadt's coastal scenes are a small part of his output beside the Rockies and the Sierra, but he painted shorelines from time to time, usually in a modest format like this one. A cove of trees and sand offered a different kind of nature from the Sublime: a quiet, private scene for a parlor wall, a picture of leisure and coastal retreat as much as of wilderness.","c":"A small panel where Bierstadt relaxes: nothing grand, a cove and some trees. The pleasure is the freshness of a nearly sketch-like handling, but the lack of drama exposes his dependence on spectacle, and without a mountain to enlarge he is an agreeable rather than a gripping painter. It is best enjoyed as a break in the sequence of his heroic landscapes."},"met:435603":{"d":"A servant dozes in this small oil on wood, about 35 by 26 centimeters, painted in 1871. The record lists only a man, a domestic worker and sleep, with the keywords Sleeping, Men and Servants. The title makes him a footman, the liveried servant who attended a household's door and carriage, and the scene is a moment of private rest in a working day. Details of his pose and setting are not recorded.","i":"A sleeping servant was a gentle comic subject in nineteenth-century genre painting: the footman, whose job is to be alert and on show, is caught off duty. Charles Bargue, a Parisian painter in the circle of Jean-Léon Gérôme, is better known today for the drawing course he published with Gérôme in 1866–71, from which generations of art students, among them Vincent van Gogh, worked.","c":"A small, accomplished genre piece. Bargue's academic training shows in the sureness of figure drawing for which his teaching course is famous, and the humor lies in the contrast of livery and slumber. It is also a minor painting: the idea is slight, and the polish of the Gérôme school can feel cool next to the warmth of humbler precedents. A neat anecdote rather than a statement."},"met:435604":{"d":"The record lists a man with a pipe in an Orientalist subject, and the title names him as a bashi-bazouk, an irregular soldier of the Ottoman army. The canvas is upright, about 46 by 33 centimeters, and was painted in 1875. Beyond the pipe, his costume, pose and setting are not described, so the picture is best understood as a character study of a type popular with Parisian painters and their buyers.","i":"Bashi-bazouks, from a Turkish word meaning unruly or disorderly, were irregular troops of the Ottoman army, notorious for indiscipline and atrocities, and French Orientalist painters such as Gérôme loved them for their colorful weapons and costumes. The type became a staple of Salon painting. A pipe-smoking portrait shows the dangerous warrior at rest, which gave the exotic a human, almost genial face.","c":"Orientalist character studies of this kind are judged on costume, texture and attitude, and Bargue, a close associate of Gérôme, shared his master's taste for precise, sharply lit detail. The type is rather a cliché of the period's exoticism, a swaggering stranger observed from outside, and today such a picture reveals more about French fantasy than about Ottoman life. Skillful, but of its time."},"met:437046":{"d":"A girl, named in the title as the Infanta María Teresa of Spain, appears in a vertical portrait of 1646, about 148 by 103 centimeters, with a dog and a chair listed among its elements. At nearly five feet high it is a large court portrait, of a size to be seen at a distance in a royal palace. The Infanta was then about eight years old, the daughter of Philip IV, and the painter, Mazo, worked in the circle of Velázquez at the Spanish court.","i":"Mazo was Velázquez's son-in-law and assistant, and portraits of royal children at the Spanish court followed a formula: a richly dressed child, a chair or table as a mark of rank, a pet to soften the formality. Her brother, the heir Baltasar Carlos, died in 1646, leaving her heiress for a time. She married Louis XIV in 1660, a union that sealed the Peace of the Pyrenees between France and Spain.","c":"Mazo is the most faithful follower of Velázquez, and portraits like this show both his debt and his limits. The formula is sound and the child is presented with calm authority, but the light is less atmospheric and the brushwork less free than in Velázquez's own infantas, and the sitter's presence less intense. It is a dignified, competent portrait that makes its master's achievement clearer by comparison."},"met:437868":{"d":"A man, named in the title as Gaspar de Guzmán, Count-Duke of Olivares, is shown in an equestrian portrait, about 128 by 104 centimeters, dated 'about 1636 or later'. The museum's keywords list a man and horses, so the picture belongs to the ceremonial type in which a ruler or commander appears on horseback. The size is modest for the type, and the composition probably derives from the large equestrian portrait that Velázquez painted of Olivares around 1636, which Mazo, as his assistant, knew well.","i":"Olivares was the chief minister of Philip IV from 1621 until his fall in 1643, the man who tried to modernize Spain and hold its empire together through costly wars. An equestrian portrait was a format normally reserved for kings and generals, and its use for a minister expresses the unusual power he held. He died in 1645, two years after his dismissal, and the image recalls the confidence of his years in office.","c":"A reduced version of a famous original loses scale and the sweep of the large design, and a smaller canvas cannot match the drama of Velázquez's space. Mazo is a careful, intelligent copyist, and the picture is valuable for keeping the master's invention alive in a smaller form. As an independent work it cannot compete; as a record of how a master's design traveled through his workshop, it is informative."},"met:437754":{"d":"A woman is the lone subject of this vertical canvas, about 96 by 69 centimeters, dated 1874. The title places her after a ball, a moment between public glitter and private rest, a favorite theme for painters of modern Parisian life. Stevens specialized in elegant women in luxurious interiors, and the picture would have been valued for its fashionable dress, its fine finish and the small story it suggests.","i":"Alfred Stevens, a Belgian who settled in Paris, was the painter of the fashionable woman of the Second Empire and early Third Republic, her gowns, interiors and private moods. After the Ball implies a narrative the viewer must supply: fatigue, memory, perhaps disappointment or a love affair. His modern-life scenes sold well to collectors in France and especially America.","c":"Stevens is a painter of surfaces: silks and satins are his subject as much as the women who wear them. The finish is exact, the composition elegant and the mood agreeable. Beside his friends Manet and Degas he seems less probing, his women types of fashion more than individuals. For the pure enjoyment of cloth and atmosphere, he is a connoisseur's painter."},"nga:46540":{"d":"A young woman dressed in white holds a bouquet in this small panel, only about 29 by 22 centimeters, painted between about 1865 and 1875. The title tells what is shown, and the painter's known manner fills in the type: Alfred Stevens, a Belgian working in Paris, specialized in elegant Parisian women and rendered silk, satin and lace with great finish. Oil on wood suits such a small, polished cabinet picture.","i":"Stevens was the painter of the modern Parisienne, the fashionable woman of the Second Empire and after, whom he showed absorbed in letters, flowers, mirrors and Japanese objects. A woman in white holding flowers can suggest purity and an occasion, a bride, a ball, a visit, but the title gives no story, so the image is best read as a study of grace and fashion for their own sake. Such pictures sold well to the new bourgeois collectors.","c":"Stevens's strengths are elegant design and a brilliant touch with fabrics, and admirers prized his command of whites. His weakness is a polite emptiness: the women are beautifully dressed and rarely seem to be thinking. Beside Manet, his friend, he lacks daring, and beside Whistler, tonal subtlety; but within its limits a small panel by Stevens is a perfectly judged object."},"met:437756":{"d":"A woman wears a Japanese robe, with a mirror and fans among the objects, in a vertical canvas of about 93 by 64 centimeters painted about 1872. The picture belongs to the Parisian craze for things Japanese, which since the 1860s had made kimonos, fans, screens and lacquer fashionable accessories of the modern interior, and which Stevens, a painter of fashionable women, was quick to exploit.","i":"After Japan opened to the West in the 1850s, Japanese prints, fabrics and decorative objects reached Paris, and by the 1860s and 1870s artists such as Manet, Whistler, Monet and Stevens were filling their studios with them. Dressing a Parisienne in a robe and surrounding her with fans and a mirror displays this fashionable taste and links the woman to ideas of exotic leisure. The mirror can also hint at vanity.","c":"Stevens plays the Japanese theme as fashion rather than scholarship: the robe is a splendid piece of textile and a pretext for color and pattern. The result is elegant and a little superficial, a Parisian parlor's view of Japan, but done with technical flair. Monet's La Japonaise is more playful and Whistler's Princess more refined; Stevens is the careful decorator, and a very skilled one."},"met:437755":{"d":"A woman and a painting are the central elements of this studio interior, a large canvas of about 107 by 136 centimeters, dated 1888. The picture combines a room with a picture within a picture, and an artist is part of the scene, so the theme is looking at art. Stevens often painted fashionable women in his own surroundings, and the studio gave him a setting both elegant and professional.","i":"The studio was a modern subject, a social space where artists, models, patrons and visitors met, and paintings of ateliers by Courbet, Bazille and Manet had made it a theme of modern art. For Stevens, who lived by selling to Parisian and American collectors, the studio also staged the pleasure of looking at art as a refined social activity. Pictures of women looking at pictures flatter the viewer by implying shared taste.","c":"At large scale in 1888 Stevens still does what he always did well: the poised woman, the rich accessory, the soft light. The subject, a picture within a picture, invites more complexity than he gives it, and he stays on the surface of a well-bred scene. The picture is easy to admire and hard to remember, and shows how far his refined manner had drifted from the daring of Manet and Degas."},"nga:195487":{"d":"A black slate in a wooden frame hangs from a nail on a dark gray-brown wooden wall, and from the same nail hang a white pipe and two boxes. Printed on the frame are the words CHEW, TIGER and BRIGHT SWEET, with a star between the last two. In white chalk the slate carries a tic-tac-toe game, a simple tiger labeled A TIGER, two stick figures, three multiplication sums and a line of childish spelling, DEZE IZ DE KIND ME DAD UZEZ DERE BULLY. One box is dark blue and marked Cut Plug; the other is white with teal designs and is tied to long matches.","i":"Trompe l'oeil, French for fooling the eye, tries to make painted objects pass for real ones, and the form flourished in late nineteenth-century America in the work of William Harnett and John Peto. The printed words suggest a tobacco advertisement, with Tiger presumably a brand of chewing tobacco, and the misspelled chalk line reads as a child's boast that his father buys it, bully being period slang for excellent: a joke on advertising and on schooling.","c":"McCallion's slate is a small comedy of deception: the plain wall, the dull slate and the dangling objects are painted so flatly and plainly that the viewer must look twice, and then the chalk jokes reward the second look. It lacks the dense textures and elegiac mood of Harnett and Peto, whose still lifes carry more feeling for age and wear, and it sits closer to a clever advertisement. As a piece of wit and illusion it succeeds."},"nga:142008":{"d":"A tree trunk, branches, plants, fruit and flowers are arranged to form the head, neck and chest of an old person, like a portrait, turned to the left against black. The ash-brown trunk is the head, with stumps for the bulbous nose, protruding chin and ear, and sunken, shadowed eyes and mouth; moss or fungus makes short hair, and branches rise like antlers. Grapes sit among the branches on the left, apples and pears on the right, ivy climbs behind, and cherries dangle from the ear. Flowers form a garment, with wheat hanging from the neck.","i":"Arcimboldo, painter to the Habsburg emperors, made a specialty of composite heads built from plants, fruit, animals and objects, including series of the four seasons and the four elements. Here all four seasons are combined in one aged head: spring's flowers, summer's wheat, autumn's grapes and fruit, and winter's gnarled, barren bark and ivy. The old man stands for the full cycle of the year and of a human life, and the court prized such wit, learning and wonders of nature.","c":"The conceit is ingenious and not merely a trick: the head is convincingly an old man, with warts and a bulbous nose in bark, and the details are painted with the precision of a botanical study. The tension between the ridiculous and the learned is the point. The single idea is repeated without development, so the effect is more clever than moving, but no one made the visual pun so persuasively, and the Surrealists later claimed him."},"met:641257":{"d":"The head of Christ is the sole subject of this oil on poplar panel of about 42 by 31 centimeters, painted around 1506. The Spanish painter Fernando Yáñez de la Almedina probably made it soon after returning from Italy, where he had worked in Florence in the circle of Leonardo da Vinci. Poplar was the standard panel wood in Italy, a clue to the painter's training, and the image presents the face for personal devotion.","i":"Yáñez was one of the first Spanish artists to bring the Italian High Renaissance home, and his work in Valencia and Cuenca shows the lessons of Leonardo: soft light, gentle modeling and calm idealized faces. A head of Christ shown alone was meant for private prayer, inviting the believer to meet his gaze and meditate on his suffering or his mercy.","c":"The Leonardesque influence gives the face a melting softness and a thoughtful gravity unusual in Spanish painting of the time. The picture lacks the mysterious atmosphere of Leonardo himself, but its seriousness and refinement are persuasive. As a document of how Italian ideas arrived in Spain, it is fascinating, and as a devotional image it is sober and effective."},"nga:41696":{"d":"Christ, nude except for a blue cloth, stands bound to a freestanding column in a courtyard, facing the viewer with his head turned to the right, a halo with red spokes above him. Two muscular men with raised whips lean away from him in mirror-image poses, one facing the viewer in a royal-blue plumed cap and pink waist cloth, the other seen from behind in a parrot-green turban. A patterned floor, three arches and a deep landscape of rolling hills and hazy blue mountains lie behind. Vertical cracks in the panel are visible.","i":"The flagellation, told in the Gospels, is the beating that Christ suffered at Pilate's order before the Crucifixion, and the column to which he was bound became one of the relics of the Passion, venerated in Rome. Renaissance painters used the scene to set contrasting poses against each other: the calm, upright Christ in the center and the tormentors at the sides. The picture is small and was probably meant for private devotion, though the elegant courtyard turns it into a stage.","c":"Bachiacca makes the cruelty decorative: the mirror-image tormentors in their plumed caps and exaggerated poses look more like dancers than torturers, and the courtyard and landscape are pretty. That tension is typical of early Florentine Mannerism, and its strangeness is part of the interest. Christ's calm figure at the center is the picture's strongest note, but the whole lacks the emotional force a more serious painter would bring to the subject."},"met:435595":{"d":"The Virgin and the Christ Child are shown in this panel of about 87 by 67 centimeters, painted in oil with gold, possibly in the early 1520s, by the Florentine painter Bachiacca. The recorded motifs are Mary, the Child and the Madonna and Child group. The use of gold, at a date when Florentine painters had largely abandoned gold grounds, suggests decorative touches, such as haloes or borders, that give the picture a jeweled richness.","i":"In the early 1520s Florence was dominated by the Madonnas of Raphael and Andrea del Sarto, with their calm pyramidal groups and warm intimacy, and Bachiacca, a younger painter working in their orbit, adapted them to smaller, more decorative pictures for private devotion. The Virgin and Child in a close embrace was among the commonest images in Florentine homes, where it stood as a protective presence and as a model of motherhood.","c":"Bachiacca is at his best as a decorator: his Madonna panels have a jeweled color and a touch of quirky invention that set them apart from the more monumental work of del Sarto. Here, as in his other devotional pictures, the sweetness of the figures is balanced by a taste for ornament. The result is charming rather than deep, and comparison with Raphael shows that it lacks his grandeur and psychological ease."},"met:435593":{"d":"Eve, nude, appears with her two infant sons, Cain and Abel, in this small upright panel of about 40 by 24 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil in the 1520s by Bachiacca. The recorded motifs are the three figures, infants and a female nude. The format suggests one panel of a pair or series, perhaps a pendant to a panel of Adam, and the subject gives the painter a chance to combine a nude with domestic tenderness.","i":"After the expulsion from Eden, Eve bore Cain and Abel, the first children, and their story, in which Cain kills Abel from jealousy, makes the first family a tragedy. Painters showed Eve as the mother of the human race between innocence and labor, often with a spindle, as in the saying about Adam delving and Eve spinning. A picture of Eve with her infant sons looks forward to the murder and sets her as the first mother, the counterpart of the Virgin Mary as the new Eve.","c":"The subject lets Bachiacca blend two of his enthusiasms, the nude and the intimate domestic motif, and the small format emphasizes the figure's soft outline. The contrast between the intimacy of mother and infants and the knowledge of Cain's crime gives the image a chill. His tempera-and-oil technique gives a smooth, enamel-like surface suited to this scale. It is a quirky, refined work, not a monumental one."},"nga:41614":{"d":"About fifty men, women and children, along with a giraffe, an ox and other animals, gather in groups across a hilly landscape in this vertical panel. The people wear robes and dresses in pink, orange, blue, red, green and brown. At the lower left, eight men and women crowd behind a man who points right with a stick. Others sit, bend to the ground or stand at barrel-sized urns, while a porcupine, white rabbit, black bear, cow and cheetah-like animals are spaced along the foreground. A path leads to buildings on a jagged coastline, and craggy blue mountains rise under a cloudy sky.","i":"In Exodus 16 God feeds the starving Israelites in the desert with manna, a fine flaky bread that appears on the ground each morning; the man pointing with a stick is presumably Moses, who instructs the people to gather it. Christians saw it as a prefiguration of the Eucharist. Bachiacca fills the landscape with an exotic menagerie, a taste Florence knew from the Medici's collection of rare animals, including a giraffe in the 1480s.","c":"Bachiacca turns a sacred story into a catalog of curiosities: the giraffe, porcupine and bear are as prominent as the miracle itself, and the many small groups invite the eye to wander. The color is bright and varied, and the landscape stretches into a jagged, fantastic coast. The result is delightful if scattered: as narrative it lacks a focus, but as a display of a Mannerist's inventive taste for the strange it succeeds."},"met:435594":{"d":"Leda, nude, appears with the swan, infants and eggs in this small oil on wood of about 43 by 32 centimeters by Bachiacca. The museum gives no date, though the artist died in 1557. In the myth, Jupiter takes the form of a swan to seduce Leda, and she bears eggs from which her children hatch, which the picture shows as infants and eggs near her.","i":"The myth of Leda was among the most popular erotic subjects of the Renaissance after Leonardo da Vinci's lost version and Michelangelo's lost painting of 1530, much copied, which set the pose. Leda's children, by Jupiter and by her husband Tyndareus, include Helen, whose abduction began the Trojan War, and the twins Castor and Pollux, so the eggs hold the seeds of epic. In a Florentine bedroom or study the picture was a refined exercise in the nude under the cover of myth.","c":"The great Ledas of the sixteenth century were Leonardo's and Michelangelo's, both lost, and Bachiacca's small panel belongs to the large family of copies and variations. His Mannerist elegance and precise finish give the nude a porcelain polish, and the swan and babies bring a note of fairy tale. It lacks the sensual power of the great models and is more cabinet curiosity than revelation, but it has a skilled minor master's charm."},"met:435614":{"d":"The Virgin sits holding the nude Christ Child, who is joined by the young Saint John the Baptist, in this small devotional panel of about 58 by 44 centimeters, painted in oil with gold around 1497. Mary wears a veil, and the figures have halos touched with gold. The informal grouping of mother and two playmates, one the future Savior and one his cousin, gives a domestic warmth to the sacred subject.","i":"John the Baptist, the patron saint of Florence, was often shown as a child with the Christ Child, since according to the Gospel of Luke their mothers, Mary and Elizabeth, were cousins. The boy foreshadows his role as the prophet who will point to Christ. The picture dates from about 1497, when the painter, then known as Baccio della Porta, was a follower of the preacher Savonarola, before he entered the Dominican order in 1500 and became Fra Bartolomeo.","c":"The softly rounded modeling already shows the influence of Leonardo, and the faces are sweet and serene. The picture lacks the monumental simplicity of Fra Bartolomeo's later altarpieces, and its grace can verge on prettiness. It is a charming early devotional picture and a good guide to the Florentine taste of the 1490s."},"met:435615":{"d":"A man is the sole subject of this small portrait, an oil on wood panel of about 40 by 31 centimeters, painted shortly after 1497. His name has not come down with the picture. Portraits are rare in the work of Fra Bartolomeo, who spent most of his career painting altarpieces and devotional images for churches and convents in Florence, and this panel shows how he treated a lay sitter.","i":"In Florence in the 1490s the portrait was changing under the influence of Netherlandish painting, in which oil allowed smooth modeling of the face and a convincing sense of presence. A small portrait like this would have recorded a friend, patron or fellow believer, perhaps from the circle around Savonarola in which the painter moved. The plain, serious look suits the sober mood of the city after 1494.","c":"A direct, unflattering portrait in which the head is modeled with soft transitions of tone, and the sitter's concentrated gaze carries the picture. It lacks the grandeur of the artist's religious paintings, and its modest scale limits its ambitions. As a rare example of the portraitist in him, it is a valuable and appealing piece of Florentine realism."},"met:11287":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 91 by 142 centimeters, shows a group of men, women and children traveling together toward freedom, as its title says; the museum's keywords name a woman, a child, a man and slavery. Painted in 1867, two years after the end of the Civil War, it is a history picture about enslaved people escaping, most probably toward the Union lines. Its large format and emphatic title give it the weight of a public statement.","i":"Kaufmann, born in Germany and an emigrant after the failed revolution of 1848, sided with the Union and made the war and slavery his subjects. In the 1860s most American paintings of slavery were either sentimental or avoided the subject, so a canvas that makes the flight to freedom its title is unusual. The words On to Liberty may echo the Union slogan On to Richmond, as if the fugitives were marching in their own cause.","c":"The picture's moral seriousness and its choice of subject are its strengths, and Kaufmann gathers men, women and children into one forward-moving group. Its weakness, as with much academic history painting of the day, is a certain staging, with emotion declared rather than felt. It cannot claim the unforced dignity of Winslow Homer's later pictures of Black life. Its importance is historical and considerable: a large, public, sympathetic image of freedom-seekers when few artists painted one."},"nga:34116":{"d":"A seated man is shown from the waist up, his body turned slightly while he looks off to the side. He has a long white beard, closely shaven hair, deep-set eyes and a slightly furrowed brow. He wears a black jacket over a gray waistcoat, with a gold chain tucked into the waistcoat and a ring on one hand, and he holds a black top hat. The background is plain and dark, so the light clothing and beard stand out. The canvas is small, about 46 by 36 centimeters.","i":"The title names William Morris Hunt, the Boston painter and teacher (1824 to 1879) who persuaded New England collectors to buy Millet and the Barbizon painters and trained a generation of artists. The date, about 1879, is the year of his death, so the portrait may be a tribute. The top hat and gold chain are signs of respectability, and the serious, inward look suits a man who argued for feeling over polish.","c":"Lawson was a competent portraitist, and this is a quiet, sober likeness: the dark ground, the careful face, the gold chain and the top hat give the man dignity and no drama. The restraint is ironic for a sitter who preached loose, bold brushwork, and one wishes for a little of Hunt's own daring. As a record of a face and a reputation it is honest and attractive, a modest picture of an important teacher."},"nga:126137":{"d":"A view of Mount Tom, painted in 1865 in oil on canvas about 41 by 62 centimeters, takes the mountain of the title as its subject. The name is probably that of the ridge in the Connecticut River valley of Massachusetts, a popular destination for the landscape painters and tourists of the period. The picture is a small, horizontal landscape by Thomas Charles Farrer, a British-born painter who had moved to the United States in 1862.","i":"Farrer belonged to the American Pre-Raphaelites, a group of painters inspired by the English critic John Ruskin, who urged artists to go to nature and record it with scrupulous truth, leaf by leaf and rock by rock. Their landscapes are often bright in color and intense in detail, in contrast to the broader, atmospheric Hudson River School. A view of a recognizable local mountain suited that program of exact observation.","c":"Pre-Raphaelite landscape stands or falls on whether its labor of detail still adds up to a whole, and the best of Farrer's work does: clear light, bright color and a sense of nature seen at close range. The risk is a hardness that flattens space. Small canvases like this one favor coherence, and the picture shows a painter taking Ruskin's advice seriously."},"nga:41611":{"d":"Christ steps from the tomb onto a ledge, draped in a voluminous ivory-white cloth, holding a red staff with a white banner marked with a red cross and raising his other hand to show the wound in his palm. A gash shows on his right side, his curly brown hair falls to his shoulders, his beard is forked, and he looks upward. Radiating lines and a golden halo surround his body, and blue fills the corners. The ledge is painted as yellow marble with a black medallion at its centre.","i":"The Risen Christ shows his wounds as proof of the Resurrection and as the source of redemption. The banner with a red cross on white is the sign of victory over death, and the radiance expresses glory. Tall, narrow panels of this type often formed the top or centre of an altarpiece, or served for private meditation on the Passion; the painted marble ledge, treated like a real piece of architecture, makes the figure seem to step toward the viewer. Bergognone worked mainly in Lombardy.","c":"Bergognone is a quiet painter, and his Christ has a gentle, unforced dignity: the draperies fall in large, soft folds, the face is calm and the colour pale and clear. He lacks the intellectual drama of his contemporary Leonardo in Milan, and the figure may seem a little doll-like in its stillness. But the painted marble and the solemn radiance give the panel a serene presence that suits a devotional image."},"met:435656":{"d":"A tall panel, about 2.4 metres high and 1.1 metres wide, painted in oil and gold on wood, shows the Assumption of the Virgin: Mary rises toward heaven borne by angels. The format, taller than a person, indicates a major altarpiece panel, perhaps the centre of a larger ensemble. Bergognone, a Lombard painter who worked for the Certosa of Pavia, painted it around 1510.","i":"According to tradition, the Virgin did not undergo bodily decay but was taken up into heaven at the end of her earthly life; the feast of the Assumption is on 15 August. In altarpieces she normally rises on clouds, borne by angels, while the apostles wonder below at the empty tomb. The image is a promise that the faithful too will rise, and it was especially favoured in churches dedicated to Mary. Gold in the paint suggests the heavenly light.","c":"Bergognone's late style keeps the calm, even light and soft modelling he had developed in the 1490s, while the younger Milanese painters around Leonardo were turning to more dynamic forms. The gentle, ordered composition and clear colours give the panel a tranquil, devout effect, but it can seem a little static, and a certain provincial plainness remains. It is a dignified, unhurried altarpiece rather than a stirring one."},"met:436635":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Agnes Sewell is the subject of this portrait miniature on ivory, which the museum dates only 'possibly' to about 1800. Miniatures of this kind were small likenesses, typically a few centimeters high, painted in transparent color so that the white ivory glows through the flesh tones, and kept in a case, locket or frame. The title preserves the sitter's name, which is more than survives for many miniatures.","i":"In British portraiture around 1800 the miniature served a wide public: a wife's likeness for a husband at sea or at war, a mother's for a child, a keepsake exchanged between family and friends. The name Agnes Sewell has stayed with the picture, but her story is not told in it, and the date is uncertain, so the miniature is a small human trace more than a documented history. Hazlehurst was one of the many professionals who met the demand.","c":"A miniaturist's craft shows in small things: the clarity of the eyes, the softness of the flesh tones against the ivory, the neatness of hair and dress. Hazlehurst worked alongside the leaders of the form, such as Cosway, and a piece like this is best judged for fidelity and finish, not invention. Its pleasure is the quiet one of a face preserved at hand-held scale, with a name attached."},"met:11409":{"d":"A boy named in the title as Amory Sibley Carhart is the subject of this large vertical portrait, about 162 by 111 centimeters, painted around 1860 to 1865. The size is large enough to show a standing child at or near full length, with the dress and setting that parents expected from an expensive commission. It is a formal child's portrait of the Civil War years by Thomas Le Clear, a portraitist from upstate New York who had settled in New York City.","i":"Children's portraits grew popular in mid-nineteenth-century America as prosperous families embraced childhood as a special, innocent stage and wanted it recorded at a particular moment. A large canvas of this kind announced the family's means as much as the child's charm, and the boy's name stayed with the picture, so he can be traced. Painted during the Civil War, it belongs to a world of private comfort alongside public crisis.","c":"Le Clear's portraits are plain, solid and honest, with a firm likeness and little rhetoric, and a child suits him better than a statesman because there is less pose to defend. The weakness of his work is a certain dryness: the paint is neat and the characterization polite. Beside the warmer, more painterly treatments of childhood by Eastman Johnson, his portraits read as documents more than poems, though good ones."},"met:11411":{"d":"William Cullen Bryant, the poet and newspaper editor, is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 70 by 57 centimeters, dated 1876 with a question mark. Bryant was then in his early eighties, the grand old man of American letters. The bust or half-length format, with the figure close to the picture plane, is the standard one for a distinguished public sitter, and it concentrates attention on the head of a man whose face the public knew well.","i":"Bryant made his name as a poet with Thanatopsis, a meditation on death written in his youth, and as editor of the New York Evening Post for almost half a century; he also argued early for the creation of Central Park. By the 1870s his long white beard and furrowed face were the public image of an American sage, and portraits of him were wanted by clubs, libraries and admirers.","c":"Le Clear gives Bryant what the public wanted: a patriarchal head, with the beard and weathered features recorded in plain, steady strokes. The likeness is honest and the handling unfussy, but it has little of the penetration that a great portraitist would find in so rich a face, and the interest is the sitter's more than the painter's. It is a dependable late portrait of a national figure."},"met:11957":{"d":"A huge horizontal canvas, about 221 by 372 centimeters, shows George Washington and the Marquis de Lafayette together at Mount Vernon in 1784, with a woman and a child among the figures, the house, trees and a broad, cloudy sky. It is a history painting of 1859 on a theatrical scale, and its long full title names the scene and its message: the home of Washington after the war. The figures are by Rossiter, and the landscape setting is usually credited to Louis Rémy Mignot.","i":"Lafayette returned to America in 1784, and his visit to Mount Vernon joined two comrades of the Revolution at the moment when Washington, having given up his command, farmed his estate as a private citizen. In 1859 the house was being rescued as a national shrine, and with the country dividing, the pair stood for unity and for the friendship of two republics. The woman and child make the political legend a family scene.","c":"The picture is a well-staged piece of civic piety, grand in scale and careful in its setting, and it shows how the 1850s imagined the founders: dignified, courteous, a little stiff. The figures are small beside the landscape, the poses polite rather than alive and the emotion dutiful, so it works better as a cultural document than as painting. The pairing of a figure painter with a landscape painter was typical of the era's large history pictures."},"met:15579":{"d":"A man named in the title as Frederick Gore King is the subject of this miniature, about 8 by 5 centimeters, painted around 1827 in watercolor on ivory. The size is that of a keepsake to be held in the hand, worn or set in a case, and the ivory support lets transparent color glow over the face. Cummings, its painter, was an English-born New Yorker who had recently helped to found the National Academy of Design.","i":"Cummings was among the founders of the National Academy of Design in 1826 and later its treasurer, and he made his living, like most American miniaturists, from portraits for families and friends. The title keeps Frederick Gore King's full name, which allows the miniature to be tied to a particular man; a likeness at this date might mark a marriage, a departure or a family gathering.","c":"Cummings's miniatures are admired for clear likenesses and firm drawing, qualities he owed in part to his teacher Henry Inman, and he preferred a plain American directness to the elegance of English miniaturists. In a small portrait like this the test is the face, and the usual rewards are candor and a steady, careful finish. It is a competent early example by one of New York's leading miniaturists of his day."},"met:16859":{"d":"The painter's own face is the subject of this tiny self-portrait, about 8 by 6 centimeters, painted around 1825 in watercolor on ivory, when Cummings was about twenty-one. A miniaturist's self-portrait is a calling card as well as a likeness: it shows what he can do to a face at the smallest scale, and in a small case it could be given to family or friends. The museum's keywords record a man and a self-portrait.","i":"Self-portraits by miniaturists were often made at the start of a career, as an advertisement and a keepsake. Cummings, born in England in 1804 and brought to New York as a child, was then training with Henry Inman and soon to become a founder of the National Academy of Design. The picture therefore shows a young man at a hopeful moment, setting himself up as a professional.","c":"A self-portrait on this scale shows a painter at his most careful: there is no client to satisfy, only his own face to get right. Whatever its finish, the interest lies in seeing a young artist take his own measure, and Cummings's later reputation for candid likeness gives the picture a context. It cannot show the strengths of his mature work, but it records the beginning of a long career."},"met:15032":{"d":"A man is the subject of this miniature, about 8 by 7 centimeters, dated only to the years 1825 to 1830 and painted in watercolor on ivory. The title records only that he was a gentleman. Men's dress in these years, with high collars, stocks and dark coats, left a miniaturist with little to display but the face, and Cummings, trained by Henry Inman, was valued for exactly that clarity of likeness.","i":"In America in the later 1820s a miniature cost far less than an oil portrait and could be finished in a few sittings, so it suited the rising merchant and professional families who wanted likenesses without a painter's full fee. The sitter's name has been lost, and with it his story; what remains is a face and a range of dates, a reminder of how many portraits were made and how few sitters are remembered.","c":"Cummings's reputation rests on clear, firm likenesses without ornament, and an anonymous portrait of this kind shows that virtue in plain form. The limit is also plain: without a name or a story, the picture offers a convincing face and a competent finish, and little more. It is a solid, quiet example of the American miniature's best habits: candor, economy and care."},"met:10591":{"d":"A man named in the title as Benson John Lossing is the subject of this miniature, about 10 by 7 centimeters, painted around 1835 in watercolor on ivory. It is slightly larger than many miniatures and shows a sitter then in his early twenties, before the books that made him famous. The ivory lets thin, transparent color glow over the face, and the format is the keepsake size that families wore or kept in a case.","i":"Lossing became one of the most widely read popular historians of nineteenth-century America, best known for his Pictorial Field-Book of the Revolution, which combined narrative with his own engraved views of battlefields and houses. This miniature shows him earlier, at the start of his career in the Hudson Valley, as a record of a man before the fame that later put his name on many title pages.","c":"A miniature of a young man still unknown to history is valued first as a likeness, and Cummings's likenesses are plain, firm and clear, which is exactly what such a sitter would want. Much of the interest is extrinsic: the pleasure of putting a face to a name that later filled many volumes. The craft is secure, and invention was never the point."},"met:10592":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this unusually large miniature, about 17 by 14 centimeters, painted in 1835 in watercolor on ivory and called The Bracelet. At twice the usual size it was probably made to be exhibited and hung, not worn, and the title points to a particular accessory, perhaps a token of affection, since bracelets were common love gifts and could hold a likeness or a lock of hair. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait.","i":"By the 1830s American miniaturists such as Cummings were producing larger, more ambitious ivories for exhibition at the National Academy of Design, where they hung beside oil portraits and competed for attention. A title like The Bracelet adds a sentimental note, in keeping with the taste of the time for fancy pictures in which a portrait doubled as a small story of love or longing. The woman remains anonymous, which possibly marks it as a showpiece more than a commission.","c":"A large miniature is an ambitious thing to attempt: more surface means more chances for the paint to go wrong, and the delicate transparency that makes a small ivory glow is harder to sustain across a bigger plate. If the picture succeeds it will be through Cummings's secure drawing and clean color, and its risk is the sweetness that the title invites. It shows the miniature stretching toward the scale of oil portraiture, shortly before photography ended the art."},"met:10596":{"d":"A man named in the title as Gustavus Adolphus Rollins is the subject of this small miniature, about 7 by 6 centimeters, painted around 1835 in watercolor on ivory. His given names recall Gustavus Adolphus, the seventeenth-century Swedish king and Protestant hero. The ivory support, thin and pale, lets transparent color glow over the face, and the size suits a case or locket for private looking.","i":"Miniatures of the 1830s were still a household object: gifts at betrothal or marriage, mementos for a journey, or likenesses of the dead. The title preserves Rollins's full name, which is more than survives for many sitters, though his story is not told in the picture. The painter, Cummings, was then among the busiest miniaturists in New York.","c":"A miniature that survives with a full name and a painter's name is a small victory of documentation, and Cummings's competence makes it a good one: firm drawing, clean color, a sober likeness that does not flatter. As a work of art it is quiet, and its great virtue is exactitude. In the context of his 1830s production it is a representative piece."},"met:10598":{"d":"A man is the subject of this very small miniature, about 6.5 by 5 centimeters, painted around 1835 in watercolor on ivory. The title records only that he was a gentleman. It is among the smaller of Cummings's miniatures, a size that fits a pocket-case or locket, so it was made for private looking and not for display, and the pale ivory shows through the thin color to give the flesh its glow.","i":"In the mid-1830s the miniature was at its American height: portable, affordable and prized as a keepsake. The sitter's name has not been preserved, which is the usual fate of miniatures separated from their cases and families. What remains is a face, a date and a painter, enough to place the picture within one of the best-documented kinds of American portraiture.","c":"At this size a miniaturist cannot hide: every line of the face must be right, and the best of Cummings's work meets the test with firm, clear, unfussy drawing. An anonymous gentleman gives little to say, but the economy of means is the point. Such a picture is best enjoyed in the hand and at close range, and judged by the likeness it keeps."},"met:10600":{"d":"Only about 3 by 3 centimeters, this is a miniature in the strictest sense, a watercolor on ivory about the size of a half-dollar coin, small enough to set in a locket or brooch. The museum's keywords list a man, a woman, a boy, a girl, a family and a necklace, so it appears to hold a whole family in a space smaller than the palm of a hand. The title, A Mother's Pearls, suggests that the children are the pearls. It is dated 1841.","i":"The idea that children are a mother's jewels goes back to Cornelia of Rome, who is said to have presented her sons as her treasures, and it was a staple of nineteenth-century sentimental writing. A title like this gave a family portrait its moral: that affection is wealth. At such a size the picture was meant to be worn or carried, a private emblem of home, and 1841 falls in the heyday of the sentimental keepsake.","c":"To paint several heads on ivory 3 centimeters wide takes a very steady hand and good eyes, and the achievement here is first of all technical: control, clarity, no wasted touch. The risk is that likenesses shrink to types and that sentiment outruns observation, and a title this sweet invites both. Even so, as a feat of miniaturizing and as an object perfectly judged for the hand, it is a remarkable small thing."},"met:16860":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this miniature, about 9 by 7 centimeters, painted around 1835 in watercolor on ivory. The title does not name her, so she is known only as a lady. Women's fashions of the mid-1830s typically combined wide sleeves with elaborate hair, giving a miniaturist plenty to record around the face, and the ivory support lets transparent color glow through the flesh tones. The size is generous for a miniature, a case-sized likeness easy to hold and examine at leisure.","i":"A lady's miniature of the 1830s was most likely a gift: from a husband to a wife, from a daughter to a mother, or from a woman to a fiancé. The unknown name means the original story is lost, but the type remains a clear image of how American women of means typically wished to be remembered: composed, tasteful and a little formal. Cummings worked at the center of that market in New York.","c":"Cummings's women are usually drawn with the same firm, clean line he gave his men, and the likenesses avoid sugar. In a portrait of this size the face has room to breathe, and the test is whether the modeling keeps the glow of ivory without hardening. An anonymous sitter and a competent hand give a picture that earns respect more than affection, which is typical of Cummings at his steadiest."},"met:17072":{"d":"A boy and a girl are the subjects of this large miniature, about 19 by 15 centimeters, painted in 1842 in watercolor on ivory. The title identifies them only as the children of Homer Ramsdell, Esq., the usual way to name young sitters by their father's standing. At more than twice the usual miniature height, it is a showpiece, with room for two figures, and it was probably meant to be exhibited or displayed in a frame rather than worn.","i":"Naming the children by their father puts the family first: Ramsdell was probably the patron, and the picture records his household. Double portraits of siblings celebrated the idea of childhood innocence and the family as a moral unit, favorite themes of the 1840s. Cummings's scale and ambition here reflect the rivalry between miniatures and oil portraits in the years before photography made both seem slow.","c":"A double portrait of children on ivory poses real difficulties: two faces to match, two sets of clothing, and little sitters who will not hold still. A large plate lets Cummings give them more presence than a pocket miniature allows, and his clear line is good at the unfussy truthfulness that children's faces repay. The risk of the larger size is some loss of jewel-like concentration, but the ambition is welcome."},"met:15028":{"d":"A man named in the title as Mr. McKinley is the subject of this miniature, about 7 by 6 centimeters, painted around 1840 in watercolor on ivory. The museum's portrait of Mrs. McKinley is the same size and date, and the two were probably made as a pair, a husband and wife kept together. As a type it is a plain domestic likeness: a face on a pale ivory plate, painted in transparent color.","i":"A pair of miniatures marks a marriage's partnership as pendant oil portraits do, but at an intimate scale, to be held and compared. The couple's first names are not given in the titles, so they are known only by their married surname, in the old form Mr. and Mrs. Their story is the common one of sitters whose pictures outlived their records. Cummings painted many such couples in New York.","c":"As a pair these portraits work better than either alone: matching scale, similar handling and a shared date give them a quiet dignity as a household record. Cummings's firm drawing keeps the male likeness plain and candid, without the flattering gloss of English miniature fashion, and its limits are those of a sound, unambitious commission."},"met:15029":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. McKinley is the subject of this miniature, about 7 by 6 centimeters, painted around 1840 in watercolor on ivory. It matches the portrait of Mr. McKinley in size and date, and the consecutive museum numbers support the idea that the two belong together. Women's dress in the early 1840s, with smooth hair parted in the middle and a modest neckline, left the painter's attention on the face.","i":"Women's miniatures like this one were often gifts at marriage or keepsakes for children, and a wife's portrait paired with her husband's was a convenient way to preserve a household in a few square centimeters. Cummings's patrons in these years included many such middle-class and professional families, whose names, like McKinley, are now only partly traced.","c":"Cummings gives his sitters no theatrical air. The woman of such a pair is presented with the same candor as the man, and that quiet frankness is the picture's best quality. What it lacks is any special spark: the likeness is the object and the painter's invention stays in the background. For a pair of household portraits that is entirely appropriate."},"met:10593":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Thomas Streatfield Clarkson, born Elizabeth Van Horne, is the subject of this miniature, about 10 by 8 centimeters, painted around 1844 in watercolor on ivory. The full name gives both her married and her maiden name, which lets museums and families trace her descent; Van Horne recalls the old Dutch families of New York. The relatively large size suits a formal likeness meant to be seen at length.","i":"This portrait and that of Levinus Clarkson of 1845 seem to belong to one family, and such sets show how a household kept its members' likenesses together. A miniature of a married woman of this standing was both a record and a possession, part of the apparatus of family memory that also included silhouettes, oil portraits and, soon, photographs. Cummings, by the mid-1840s, was a senior figure of the New York art world.","c":"At about 10 centimeters tall this is a generous miniature, and Cummings uses the space for a steady, dignified likeness without fuss. The 1840s were the last full decade of the miniature before the daguerreotype took hold, and the best of his work shows an art at its most confident and least ornamental. The sitter's name and lineage add documentary value to a competent, careful work."},"met:10597":{"d":"A man named in the title as Levinus Clarkson is the subject of this small miniature, about 6 by 5 centimeters, dated 1845 and painted in watercolor on ivory. The shared name suggests a family connection with the portrait of Mrs. Thomas Streatfield Clarkson in the same collection. It is a plain, small likeness of the kind that fits a pocket case, with transparent color over the pale ivory giving the flesh its glow.","i":"Dated exactly to 1845, it belongs to the last years in which the portrait miniature was the usual private likeness. The daguerreotype, introduced to America in 1839, was already winning customers by being cheaper and quicker, and Cummings's art would soon be squeezed. Levinus was a family name among the Clarksons, so the sitter carried a heritage as well as a face.","c":"The year 1845 gives this small portrait a poignancy the picture itself does not state: a mature craft facing a technology that would replace it. Cummings handles the likeness with the plain firmness for which he was known, and in a face of this size that is enough. It is not a work to stir passion; it is a model of careful professional skill."},"met:12700":{"d":"A man is the subject of this miniature, about 10 by 8 centimeters, painted around 1840 in watercolor on ivory. The title records only that he was a gentleman. The size is generous, large enough to give the face real presence, and well above the small locket-sized pieces common in the same decade. Transparent color over the ivory gives the flesh its characteristic glow.","i":"Portraits of unnamed gentlemen were the bread and butter of a miniaturist like Cummings: merchants, lawyers and clerks who wanted a likeness for a wife or family. Their anonymity today is the price of the form's portability, since small objects separated from their cases lose their names. Still, the portrait speaks of mid-century America's rising professional classes.","c":"This is a competent portrait whose merit is clarity. A man of about 1840 is drawn with a steady line and plain color, and the absence of ornament gives the face full attention. Without a name or a story the picture cannot ask for more than respect, but respect it earns. Cummings, who also wrote the Historic Annals of the National Academy of Design, knew the standards of his craft."},"met:15031":{"d":"A man is the subject of this very small miniature, about 5.6 by 4.2 centimeters, dated to the years 1840 to 1845 and painted in watercolor on ivory. It is one of the smaller of Cummings's portraits in this group, the size of a pendant locket or a coat-pocket case. The title records only that he was a gentleman, and transparent color over the pale ivory gives his flesh its glow.","i":"The smallest miniatures were the most portable, and so the most intimate: carried by soldiers, travelers, lovers and widows, they were held in the palm and looked at in private. A man's miniature of the 1840s often marked a farewell, an engagement or a death. The sitter's name has not survived, but the object's intimacy still does.","c":"At this size every decision shows: the shape of an eye, the line of a collar, the tone of a cheek. Cummings's firm drawing is an advantage where a softer hand would blur. The result is likely to be a sober, exact little likeness, more an object to treasure than a painting to analyze, which is just what a pocket miniature was meant to be."},"met:15027":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Oswald John Cammann, born Catherine Navarre Macomb, is the subject of this miniature, about 10.5 by 8.5 centimeters, painted around 1843 in watercolor on ivory. Giving both her married and maiden names follows the museum's practice for married sitters, and Macomb is a name known in early American history. The size, among the largest of Cummings's single portraits in this group, gave room for a formal, dignified likeness.","i":"A married woman's miniature of the early 1840s recorded a marriage between named families as well as a face, and it would be kept in the house or passed to her children. Cummings was by then among the most sought-after miniaturists in New York, and a sitter of this standing may well have chosen him for his reputation for firm, faithful likenesses.","c":"The formal size and a sitter with a full name make this one of Cummings's more documentary works, and his firm style suits a lady painted with dignity and without flattery. It is not a picture that surprises, and the face is the whole interest. Competent, clear and well documented, it stands as a good example of his professional output in the early 1840s."},"met:15030":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Maria Margaretta Oswald is the subject of this very small miniature, about 5.6 by 4.2 centimeters, dated broadly to the years 1840 to 1850 and painted in watercolor on ivory. The wide date range reflects how hard it can be to fix a miniature by style alone. It is the size of a locket or pocket case, a likeness made to be held and looked at in private.","i":"A woman's name preserved in full, with a middle name, is a gift to later researchers, since most miniatures have lost theirs. The 1840s were a decade of great demand for such small portraits, as the growing American middle class could afford likenesses, and the fashion for keepsakes extended from engagement tokens to mourning pieces. Cummings, by now a senior New York painter, was a reliable supplier of exactly this kind of work.","c":"At this scale a portrait lives or dies on the eyes and the set of the mouth, and Cummings's firm drawing was well suited to the demand. A broad decade as a date is a reminder that his manner changed little over the years: steady, clear and unfussy. The picture is a modest, well-made likeness, valued as much for the name it keeps as for any special brilliance."},"met:13345":{"d":"A man is the single figure of this vertical oil painting, about 72 by 51 centimeters, dated 1865, and the museum's keywords add firearms and the American Civil War. The title uses the wartime term contraband, which Union commanders applied to enslaved people who escaped to their lines. It is the first of three panels of identical size called A Bit of War History, which seem to follow one man from fugitive to recruit to veteran, and it presents him with the seriousness of a portrait.","i":"In 1861 General Benjamin Butler refused to return fugitives to their enslavers by calling them contraband of war, and the word spread through the Union army and the press for the thousands who crossed the lines. Wood's picture gives one such man a face and dignity at the end of the war, when images of Black Americans were still too often caricatures. With the Recruit and the Veteran, it argues in paint for the part Black men played in the Union's victory.","c":"The three-panel idea is the most interesting thing about the series, and the first panel sets it up with simple, serious means. Wood's handling is careful, academic and unsentimental, and he gives the sitter dignity; the limits are a certain stiffness and the tidy finish of illustration. The pictures matter chiefly as rare, sympathetic images of formerly enslaved men, made soon after the war by a white northern painter."},"met:13344":{"d":"A soldier is the single figure of this vertical oil painting, about 72 by 51 centimeters, dated 1866; the museum's keywords name a soldier and the Civil War. It is the last of three panels of identical size that tell the story of one man from escape to enlistment to survival, and the title promises a figure who has come through the war. The matching size and format of its companions make the three a deliberate set, to be read in sequence.","i":"The Veteran completes the sequence in which the sitter passes from refugee to soldier to survivor. By 1866 Black veterans were pressing for citizenship and the vote, and the title makes the claim that service earns standing. Wood's three canvases were probably shown together, and their quiet argument seems aimed at the white northern public who controlled the nation's institutions and might be persuaded by a sober, orderly case.","c":"As the finale, the picture has to carry the weight of the series, and Wood's solution is restraint: no battle, no rhetoric, only a man and his title. That restraint is both honorable and limiting; the painting is dignified rather than moving, and the argument would gain from more individual feeling. It stands nevertheless among the notable American pictures of the war's aftermath."},"met:13346":{"d":"A soldier stands as the single figure of this vertical oil painting, about 72 by 51 centimeters, dated 1866; the museum's keywords name a soldier, firearms, the Civil War and a portrait. The title marks the middle chapter of the three-panel series called A Bit of War History: a man who has enlisted and now bears arms in the Union cause. It is sized like a formal portrait and treats the figure with a portrait's gravity.","i":"About 180,000 Black men served in the Union army, most after the Emancipation Proclamation of 1863 allowed their enlistment, and their service changed public opinion about citizenship. Wood's Recruit is a visual witness to that change: a man who was property becomes a soldier with a weapon in his hand. The sequence argues that enlistment was the hinge between slavery and citizenship.","c":"The Recruit is the most direct of the three: uniform, rifle, upright stance. A soldier picture runs the risk of stiffness or mere costume, and Wood avoids both by treating the figure as a portrait, with sober attention to character. Still, the tidy brushwork and clean finish blunt the force of the subject, and Winslow Homer's rougher war pictures strike harder. It is the honorable work of a sympathetic painter."},"met:435768":{"d":"Mary and the Christ Child are the whole subject of this small panel, about 34 by 29 centimeters, painted before 1508, and an apple figures among the things shown. The medium is tempera on wood, the old egg-based paint that gives a clear, matte surface and crisp contours, and the format is that of a devotional image meant to be looked at closely. The style is that of Bramantino, the Milanese painter who favored severe, sculptural forms and cool, pale color.","i":"The apple is the key to the picture's meaning. As the fruit of the Fall, it is usually read as a sign of the sin of Adam and Eve, and when it is placed near the Christ Child it presents him as the new Adam who redeems that sin. Images of the Virgin and Child of this size were made for private prayer at home, to help the owner dwell on the Incarnation and on Mary's role as intercessor.","c":"Bramantino's reputation rests on large, strange altarpieces and frescoes, so a small devotional panel like this shows him in a quieter register. The qualities to look for are the ones that set him apart from the softer Leonardesque painters of Milan: firm, sculptural form, clear outline and a cool, austere restraint. His very name, 'little Bramante', acknowledges the architect who shaped his taste. It is a modest work, but one that repays slow looking."},"nga:531":{"d":"A Holy Family in oil on a wooden panel, about 101 by 79 centimeters, painted by the Florentine artist Bronzino about 1527 to 1528. The title identifies the subject as the family group of Mary, the infant Christ and Joseph; the catalog gives no more description, so the exact poses, the setting and any additional figures, such as the young Saint John the Baptist, are left unstated. It is a domestic devotional painting of a kind made for the private rooms of a Florentine household.","i":"The Holy Family, popular in Florence since the fifteenth century, presents Joseph as an attentive guardian and the household as a model of Christian family life. About 1527 to 1528 the city was in political upheaval, after the Sack of Rome and the expulsion of the Medici, and such pictures offered a stable, intimate subject for private devotion. The Child often anticipates the Passion through a gesture or an object, a shadow of sorrow in a tender scene.","c":"In the late 1520s Bronzino was still the pupil and collaborator of Pontormo, and his early religious pictures usually show it in twisting poses, close-cropped compositions and a cool, polished surface. Judged as an early statement of the elegant Mannerist idiom he would later perfect in portraiture, the panel interests more for its promise and its place in his development than for the warmth of feeling that Raphael or Andrea del Sarto could bring to the subject."},"nga:1143":{"d":"A young mother and her small son are shown together on a vertical panel about 100 by 76 centimeters, painted in oil about 1540. The title is all the catalog supplies, so the details of their poses, clothes and setting are left unstated. A double portrait of this kind allowed the sitter to be presented both as an elegant woman and as the mother of a son. Bronzino's manner in such pictures is polished and precise, with fabrics and jewelry rendered in cool, hard-edged detail.","i":"In sixteenth-century Florentine families a son was the guarantee of the family's future, and a portrait showing a mother with her boy proclaimed lineage as much as affection. Neither sitter is named, and the picture remains a portrait of a type: wealth, dynastic hope and the good fortune of motherhood, expressed through rich dress and a restrained pose. Such images were designed to display social ideals as much as to record faces.","c":"Few portraitists matched Bronzino in making cloth, jewels and skin look equally hard and cool, and the same polish risks making human feeling secondary. In a mother-and-child picture that tension is especially interesting: the formal calm of Florentine court portraiture meets the natural intimacy of a child's presence. As an example of his court style at its most controlled, it is a fair test of whether his reserve repels or fascinates."},"nga:46106":{"d":"A portrait of the Duchess Eleonora di Toledo, wife of Cosimo I de' Medici, painted in oil on a panel about 86 by 65 centimeters, about 1560. The catalog gives no description beyond the sitter's name, so her pose, dress and setting are left unstated. In Bronzino's portraits of the duchess she is generally shown as a remote, impassive figure whose rank is expressed through the lavish fabric and jewels of her dress rather than through expression.","i":"Eleonora (1522–1562) was a Spanish noblewoman who married Cosimo I in 1539, bore him many children and bought the Pitti Palace in 1549. Portraits of a ruling duchess served the state: they displayed legitimacy, wealth and the continuity of the line. Official images such as this were often repeated in several versions for relatives and ambassadors, which is why more than one Eleonora portrait by Bronzino and his workshop exists.","c":"Bronzino's portraits of the Medici court create an image of power through stillness: nothing moves, nothing is spontaneous, and the person is subordinated to rank. That cold perfection is both the glory and the limitation of his style. A portrait of about 1560, painted late in the duchess's life, may be less brilliant than the famous earlier portrait with her son, and studio assistance on such state portraits was common, but the type keeps its authority."},"met:435918":{"d":"A table is piled with objects that stand for the pursuits of life, with a skull among them: the museum records books, jewellery, a violin and music, a globe and a skull. The panel is large for a still life, about 94 by 112 centimetres, and the record gives no further description, so the arrangement, the table and background and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. It is a vanitas, a Dutch seventeenth-century type of still life that reminds viewers of the shortness of life.","i":"Vanitas still lifes take their name from Ecclesiastes, vanity of vanities, and they set the signs of learning, art, wealth and pleasure beside a skull as a memento mori: the books and globe for knowledge, the violin and music for the arts and sensual pleasure, the jewellery for riches. This one, painted in 1662 by Edwaert Collier, offers its moral in a rich display. It was bought by the Metropolitan Museum in 1871.","c":"A learned, handsome picture that makes its sermon through beautiful things: the objects are chosen for a nice contrast between the glitter of jewellery and the cold, plain skull. The vanitas formula is by now familiar, and the picture is more systematic than original, each emblem placed to be read. Collier was among the specialists of the type, and the pleasure of the surfaces, however much the moral says otherwise, is its quiet irony."},"nga:397":{"d":"A woman holds a baby in the crook of her arm against a gold ground in this arched, vertical panel, shown from the waist up and angled slightly to the viewer's right. Her dark blue mantle, bordered in gold and lined in celery green, covers her head and shoulders; a gold star marks one shoulder and she holds a stylized rose. The nude Child, wrapped at the waist in a transparent cloth, grips her finger and reaches for the rose. The punched gold makes halos and decorative bands.","i":"The star on Mary's shoulder is usually read as a sign of her virginity or of her role as star of the sea, a guide to the faithful, and the rose can be read as a flower of her purity and of love, with a hint of the Passion that the Child will suffer. The arched top suggests this was the central panel of a polyptych, a multi-panel altarpiece. Giotto, working in Florence in the early 1300s, is the painter credited with restoring weight and space to Italian painting.","c":"Giotto makes the Virgin a solid body under her mantle, with real weight, and the exchange between mother and child, the finger gripped and the rose reached for, gives the formal scheme a living gesture. The Child's grown-up features and the flat gold keep one foot in the Byzantine tradition. At devotional scale Giotto's grandeur is more intimate than in his Padua frescoes, but it is plainly the same mind, and the painting has real authority."},"met:436504":{"d":"A small gold-ground panel combines the Nativity and the visit of the Magi. Mary lies wrapped in a dark teal mantle on a bed inside a wooden shed with a planked roof. In front of the manger an old king in a red robe kneels with his back to the viewer and reaches for the swaddled Christ Child; his crown lies on the ground. Beside him two crowned kings stand in yellow-green and rose, one holding a gold cup. At the left, haloed Joseph in a blue tunic and ochre cloak stands apart, with two shepherds in brown cloaks behind him, one playing a pipe. A goat and a sheep graze below, the ox and ass look on, and angels hover in the gilded sky. Tempera on wood, about 45 by 44 centimetres.","i":"The Met places this panel in a series of seven narrative scenes from the Life of Christ and attributes it to Giotto, the Florentine often called the founder of Italian painting, with a date of possibly about 1320. It praises the sophisticated view of the stable from slightly below and the columnar solidity of the figures, and notes the impetuous action of the kneeling king, who picks up the Child, and Mary's expression of concern. The Adoration of the Magi, told in the Gospel of Matthew, celebrates Christ's revelation to the wider world and is marked at Epiphany; the ox and ass behind the manger come from tradition, not the Gospels. Both the date and the degree of Giotto's own hand are open to question.","c":"Even at this size the gravity of Giotto shows. The figures have weight and are spaced to read at a glance, the stable is built as a real structure, and the king's rush toward the Child gives a human jolt to a ceremony older painters made stately. The panel is harder to judge as evidence: the gold ground is crazed with cracks, and scholars have debated how much of the series is by Giotto himself rather than his busy workshop. As a distillation of his style it is excellent; as proof of his hand, less certain."},"met:435852":{"d":"Two children lean over a table against a dark brown ground, their faces lit by a warm light. A boy with brown hair, in an ochre jacket with a white lace collar, dangles a crayfish before the nose of a ginger-and-white cat that crouches on the table, its ears laid back and its eyes wary. A girl with curly fair hair and a coral-red dress grips his shoulder and leans in, grinning with delight. A second crayfish lies on the table edge at the bottom. The paint is quick and fluid, especially in the lace and the cat's fur.","i":"Annibale Carracci, born in Bologna, was a founder, with his brother Agostino and cousin Lodovico, of a reform of painting based on close observation of life. This early canvas, dated to the late 1580s, was once attributed to Agostino until Roberto Longhi established it as Annibale's. The motif of a child bitten by a crayfish appears in a drawing by Sofonisba Anguissola, and later in Caravaggio's Boy Bitten by a Lizard. Annibale freezes the moment just before the cry. Scholars have suggested a mild moral warning about teasing, in the tradition of Flemish proverb pictures, but the painting does not insist on it. The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 1994.","c":"The idea is a good one: stop the clock just before the squeal. The cat's flattened ears, the boy's cool concentration and the girl's gleeful face tell the story at a glance, and the fur and the shiny orange crayfish are painted with relish. The weaknesses are the empty dark background, which gives the children nowhere to be, and the tangle of arms and hands. The handling is quick, close to sketch. It also asks viewers to find cruelty charming. Even so, it is the liveliest evidence of Annibale's early naturalism."},"cma:157501":{"d":"A boy raises a glass to drink in this small canvas, about 56 by 44 centimeters, painted in 1582-83 when Annibale Carracci was in his early twenties. The museum points to the coarse surface of the canvas, the unrefined subject and the striking distortion of forms seen through glass. A jug in the picture throws reflected light onto the boy's shirt, an observation that heightens the sense of reality.","i":"The painting belongs to the moment when the Carracci of Bologna rejected the artificial elegance of Mannerism and turned back to nature, founding an academy in the early 1580s to train painters in drawing from life. A boy drinking is a subject from daily life, of the kind Annibale also explored in his early pictures of a butcher's shop and a bean eater. Such low subjects were thought unworthy of the grand manner, so the picture is a deliberate statement of independence.","c":"The boldness lies in the subject and the surface: an unglamorous boy, a rough canvas, a glass that bends forms as glass really does and a jug that throws light on the shirt. For a young painter this attention to optical fact is remarkable, and it anticipates Caravaggio and the Dutch genre painters. It is closer to a study than a composed picture, with a vitality his later, grander works trade for balance."},"nga:41673":{"d":"A river landscape in oil on a wide canvas, about 88 by 148 centimeters, painted about 1590. The museum's record gives only the title, so the river's course, the boats or figures, the trees and the light are left unstated. Landscape of this ambition and size, a view in its own right rather than a background for a sacred story, was still a novelty in Italian art around 1590, and Annibale Carracci, a Bolognese painter, was among its pioneers.","i":"Annibale Carracci, with his brother Agostino and his cousin Lodovico, led the reform of painting in Bologna in the 1580s, calling for a return to the study of nature and of the great masters. His early landscapes are often cited as a step toward the ideal landscape of Claude and Poussin, in which a calm, ordered countryside suggests a golden age. A river, with its winding recession, is a natural subject for such an idea.","c":"Annibale's landscape is airy and rational: the ordered recession from foreground to distance, the balanced masses of trees and hills and the soft light lay the foundations for Claude and Poussin. The figures, whatever their number, are subordinate and probably rather generic. The picture has less poetry than Claude's, but its clarity of structure and fresh observation of nature are significant. It may look modest beside his grand frescoes, yet it shows him as an innovator in a new genre."},"met:438338":{"d":"The burial of Christ is the subject: his body is laid to rest by his followers while mourners, among them Mary Magdalene, grieve. The picture is oil on copper, about 44 by 35 centimeters, painted in 1595 by Annibale Carracci. Copper gives a smooth, hard surface, favored for small pictures of fine finish, and this is a cabinet devotional picture, made to be examined at close range.","i":"The entombment, told in all four Gospels, is the quiet interval between the Crucifixion and the Resurrection: Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus take the body down and lay it in a new tomb. Mary Magdalene, who watched the burial and returned on Easter morning, is a natural witness. Counter-Reformation artists sought pathos that would move the viewer directly, and a small copper like this invited private contemplation of the Passion.","c":"Annibale combined Correggio's tenderness and Venetian color with firm drawing and clear staging, and on copper those qualities condense: plain groupings, grief held in gesture and glance, a smooth glowing surface. The restraint keeps the pathos from melodrama. Next to the Caravaggesque drama emerging in Rome in these years, it is quieter and more lyrical."},"nga:46108":{"d":"In the left half of this horizontal painting, a bare-chested Venus sits holding up a black-framed mirror, tended by three nude women and several winged putti. Two women dress her braided blond hair while the third gazes at a teardrop pearl; one putto ties her sandal, another steadies the mirror, a third draws strings of pearls from a gold box. A sky-blue cloth covers her lap, and a rose-pink curtain hangs behind. On the right, a terrace holds a gray statue of a nude man with grapes, and two distant figures, one in golden armor, sit by a balustrade.","i":"The toilet of Venus, with the three Graces as her attendants, is a classical motif of beauty and love, drawn from ancient poetry and Renaissance imitations of it. The statue of a nude man with grapes is probably the wine god Bacchus, linking love to wine and pleasure, and the armored figure in the distance is probably Mars, the war god who was Venus's lover. The mirror also makes the picture a reflection on vanity and beauty.","c":"Annibale reconciles Venetian sensuality with Bolognese clarity: the soft, golden flesh and the luxurious drapery owe much to Titian, while the drawing is firm and the composition clear. It is a picture of unabashed pleasure, with a witty cast of putti handling pearls and combs. The distant landscape is typical of Carracci. It is an early statement of the sensuous classicism that would shape Baroque painting."},"met:438813":{"d":"John the Baptist bears witness to Christ in an open landscape, in an oil on copper of about 54 by 44 centimeters painted around 1600 by Annibale Carracci. The recorded motifs list John, a landscape and Jesus, so the saint's testimony to Christ is set within an ordered, spacious natural scene. In Annibale's late work the landscape is more than a backdrop: it is a stage on which the sacred story is placed with calm order. Copper's smooth surface suits the fine detail of foliage and distance.","i":"The Gospel of John (1:29-34) tells how John the Baptist saw Jesus approaching and declared him the Lamb of God, bearing witness that he was the Son of God. The scene is the moment when the Baptist's mission is fulfilled. Annibale's landscape setting reflects his role in creating the ideal landscape of the seventeenth century, in which nature is arranged in clear, harmonious planes to frame a sacred story.","c":"Annibale makes the landscape an equal of the figures: the ordered space, framed by trees and receding to a luminous distance, lets the sacred encounter breathe, and his firm drawing keeps the saint solid and the story clear. This is the blend of nature and narrative that Poussin and Claude later developed. On copper the colors have an enamel-like depth. It is quietly influential, a small picture pointing toward the future of landscape."},"met:435853":{"d":"In a golden heaven, the youthful Christ at the left and God the Father, an older bearded man with a sceptre, at the right, together lower a crown of stars over the head of the Virgin, who sits between them in a blue mantle and mauve bodice with her hands open. Both are draped in pale grey-white robes that leave their chests bare. The white dove of the Holy Spirit hovers above. On banks of grey cloud around them, angels play a violin, a lute and a flute, while at the bottom a group of cherubs and an angel in green rise out of a glowing mist. Tiers of blessed souls fill the sky at the top corners. Oil on canvas, about 118 by 141 centimetres.","i":"Annibale Carracci, with his brother Agostino and cousin Ludovico, reformed painting in Bologna in the 1580s, turning from Mannerist artifice toward direct observation and clear storytelling. He moved to Rome in 1595 to work for the Farnese family, and the Met dates this picture to after 1595. The Coronation of the Virgin, with the Trinity crowning Mary as Queen of Heaven, was a favourite subject of Counter-Reformation art. The loose handling suggests a sketch for a larger scheme, though the project it served is uncertain. Christ and the Father are shown as classical, athletic figures, an echo of Annibale's study of antique sculpture and of Michelangelo.","c":"The sketch has the fluent strength of Annibale's Roman manner: the muscular Christ and Father, painted with Michelangelesque weight, make a rhythmic triangle around the gentle Virgin, and the warm golden light gives the heavens real atmosphere. The angel musicians are less convincing, conventional types who play without conviction, and the crowd of souls at the bottom is vague. The drama is limited. Not every Counter-Reformation vision was meant to be moving, and this one seems more interested in order and clarity than in rapture, which is characteristic of Annibale."},"met:12703":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. William Wortley Worsley, born Rebecca Smith, is the subject of this very narrow miniature, about 6 by 3 centimeters, dated 1805 and painted in watercolor on ivory. The long, slender format is unusual even for a miniature and suggests a particular mount or setting, such as a bracelet clasp or a long locket. Sully painted it when he was about twenty-two, early in a career he began in miniatures.","i":"Thomas Sully, born in England in 1783 and raised in Charleston, South Carolina, began as a miniature painter and moved on to oil portraits, becoming one of the busiest portraitists in the United States. His miniatures of the early 1800s are the work of a young man making his living and learning his trade. The title gives the sitter's married and maiden names, which lets her be traced.","c":"Early Sully miniatures show a young painter finding his hand: the drawing is neat, the color clean, and the likeness unfussy, and the glow that would make his oils so fluent is not yet there. The unusual narrow format makes demands on composition, and what survives is a tidy record of one woman's face. It is worth looking at less for itself than as a first step toward the polished Sully of the 1820s and 1830s."},"met:12706":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Thomas Sully, born Sarah Annis, is the subject of this small square miniature, about 5 by 5 centimeters, painted around 1802 in watercolor on ivory. The sitter became the painter's wife, and the title gives her married name as well as her maiden name. The square shape is less common than the usual oval, and the scale is that of a keepsake kept in a case.","i":"A painter's portrait of someone close to him is a private object more than a public one: not a commission but a record of affection, kept in the family. Sully, then about nineteen, was making his living as a miniaturist and learning his craft among the portraitists of the Southern cities. The picture shows what he could do when the sitter was someone he knew well.","c":"Portraits of loved ones are often the most sincere of an artist's work, and a small miniature of this kind can have more tenderness than his commissions. Sully's early manner is simple and clean, and such a likeness may keep the candor that a paid sitter might not allow. It does not show the confident, glossy Sully of later years, and it is the more appealing for that."},"nga:32591":{"d":"A man is seated and shown from the waist up, slightly turned to the side with his face toward the viewer. He has a fair complexion, short curly light gray hair and a composed expression. He wears a dark coat over a white shirt and cravat, holds a pair of spectacles, and wears two large gold rings set with polished stones. The background is a plain, softly shaded gradient. The canvas, about 92 by 74 centimeters, is dated 1810.","i":"Spectacles in a sitter's hand suggest an older, literate man, and the gold rings suggest prosperity: modest attributes that tell the viewer a good deal without an elaborate setting. In 1810 Sully had just spent time in London, where he had studied with Benjamin West and Thomas Lawrence, and the plain, softly lit composition shows his debt to English portraiture. The title gives only the name of the sitter, Joseph Dugan.","c":"A solid, quiet portrait: the face is carefully modeled, the hands with their spectacles and rings give the sitter his character, and the soft, empty ground keeps the eye on him. It lacks the dash that Sully would bring to the portraits of the 1820s, but the restraint is honest and the likeness believable. It is an early, sober example of the polished style that made him the leading portraitist of Philadelphia."},"nga:33748":{"d":"A naval officer stands facing the viewer in a black coat with a wide collar, gold lace, gold buttons and gold epaulets, over a high white cravat and long white trousers. He has thin blond hair, deep-set eyes and a slight smile. On the left his hand rests on a rolled white document on a table covered in red cloth, with books and a black-and-gold hat; on the right he holds a long curved sword by his side. Dark red drapery fills the wall at the left. The canvas is nearly 240 centimeters high.","i":"Captain Charles Stewart was a United States Navy officer who later commanded the Constitution in the War of 1812 and became one of the service's most celebrated figures. The scroll and sword mark command, the table and drapery borrow the furniture of English portraits of statesmen and generals, and the whole is a grand-manner image made for a native hero. Sully painted it in 1811 to 1812, just after his return from London.","c":"This is one of the most ambitious pictures of Sully's early career. He borrows the grand manner of Lawrence, the standing officer, the red drapery, the carefully arranged props, and applies it to an American. The composition is sturdy and the face lively, but the pose is stiff and the props sit as if placed by a stage manager. Still, it shows clearly how the young republic dressed its heroes in the language of English aristocratic portraiture."},"cma:96262":{"d":"A married couple, Jean Terford David and Mary Sicard David, are recorded in this pair of portraits, each about 90 by 70 centimeters, painted in 1813 as pendants, a husband and wife hung side by side. Cleveland's museum notes that David was a French-born American officer who served as a paymaster in the War of 1812 and had recently married. The matched size and date point to a commission for the new household, and such pendants usually have the sitters turned slightly toward each other.","i":"Pendant portraits of a newly married couple marked a household's beginning: they were hung together in a parlor and handed down as a pair. David's uniform records his service in a war then in progress, and his wife's likeness completes the picture of a young family, one of the many Sully painted in these years. Their matching format shows how a successful portraitist turned out commissions in pairs.","c":"Taken together the pair shows Sully's method: warm color, easy poses and a courteous eye that smooths faults. Husband and wife are treated in the same polished way, which gives the pair a pleasing balance, and the military detail in the husband's portrait is a small triumph of design. What is missing is any edge of observation: the pair flatters, as he intended, and that is both its charm and its limit."},"cma:96263":{"d":"A man in uniform, named in the title as Jean Terford David, is shown in this vertical portrait, about 90 by 71 centimeters, painted in 1813. His body is turned at an angle, so that the single fringed epaulet on his left shoulder stands out, and there is none on the right. The pose is a small piece of design: it makes the badge of rank prominent and hides the awkwardness of a uniform that is not symmetrical.","i":"Cleveland's museum notes that David was a French-born American officer who served as a paymaster during the War of 1812 and had recently married when he sat for this portrait. The single epaulet signals his rank. It also records that Sully, who by his own inventory painted more than 2,600 works, treated the portrait as a problem to solve gracefully, and that his working motto was that nothing is so sure of success as flattering your portraits.","c":"Sully's gift was ease and polish: a fluent handling learned from English portraiture, and an instinct for composition that turns a practical difficulty, here a one-epaulet uniform, into a strength. The flattery in his motto is also his limit, since his sitters tend toward a pleasing sameness, bright and smooth. This portrait is typical of him: elegant, skillful, a little glossy, and admirably tactful in its solution."},"cma:96264":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mary Sicard David is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 90 by 70 centimeters, painted in 1813. It is the companion of the portrait of her husband, Jean Terford David, with the same size and date. The format is close to the kit-cat size of 36 by 28 inches, a half-length image that normally shows the head, torso and a hand, and it is the usual size for a fashionable portrait of the 1810s.","i":"Sully's working motto, as Cleveland records it, was that nothing is so sure of success as flattering your portraits. That practical rule shaped how he painted women in particular, softening features, brightening color and giving the sitter a graceful pose. A portrait of a young wife was meant to please her husband and family as much as to record her, and as the companion of her husband's portrait it also recorded their marriage.","c":"A good Sully portrait of a woman is a study in graceful ease. If this one follows his usual lines it will be soft in modeling, pleasant in color and a touch idealized, qualities that sold well and wear less well. Its interest is increased by its companion: the two make a matched pair that shows a clever painter turning the same formula to two different sitters."},"met:12698":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Katherine Matthews is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 70 by 58 centimeters, painted in 1812 to 1813. The size is modest, a bust or half-length image rather than a grand full-length, and suited to a parlor wall. Sully, a few years after his return from study in London, was establishing himself as Philadelphia's leading portraitist, and sitters came to him for a likeness that was polished and flattering.","i":"A married woman's portrait of 1812 to 1813 recorded status, taste and family, and the title's form, a first name with her husband's surname, follows the convention of the period. Nothing in the title identifies her husband or her circle. Painted while the War of 1812 was being fought, the portrait belongs to a time when private life in Philadelphia went on, with portrait sittings among its pleasures.","c":"Sully's women of these years have a soft, pearly finish and a courteous grace learned from English portraiture, with Lawrence the obvious model. The risk is sameness, and the pleasures are those of polish. A picture of this kind is judged chiefly on the likeness and the quality of the surface, in which Sully was rarely less than skillful, and rarely more than pleasing."},"nga:34059":{"d":"A man named in the title as Robert Walsh is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1814. The museum's keywords record only a male sitter, so the picture is described by its type: a half-length portrait in the polished manner Sully brought home from London, of a size that would hang comfortably in a study or parlor.","i":"In 1814 Philadelphia was a center of publishing, medicine and the arts, and Sully was making his name there among the merchants, editors and professional men of the city. A portrait of such a man was both a likeness and a credential. The title gives only the sitter's name, and the details of his life must be sought elsewhere.","c":"Sully's male portraits of the 1810s are sober, well modeled and quietly flattering: a steady head, a calm expression, a coat painted with a few broad touches. They lack the brilliance of Lawrence, whom he admired, but they keep a human frankness that the grander English manner can lose. A work of this type is a sound, unshowy likeness."},"nga:1129":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, facing the viewer, with pale skin, thin lips, rosy cheeks, light green eyes and gray hair curled around his ears. His expression is neutral. He wears a dark coat with gold buttons over a pale yellow shirt and a white cravat, against a dark, indistinct brown background. The vertical canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, is dated 1815, and the title names the sitter as Abraham Kintzing.","i":"Sully's portraits of older men in these years are largely about the face, with the clothes and background kept simple so that the head carries the picture. The title gives only the sitter's name. At this date Sully was Philadelphia's busiest portraitist, and the sitter's age and respectable dress suggest a man of settled position, the gold buttons the one flash of display.","c":"The face is the interest: the light eyes, the rosy cheeks and the curled gray hair are recorded with an easy attention that avoids caricature. Beyond it the picture is deliberately plain, a dark coat and brown ground that make no demands. Sully was capable of far more theatrical effects, but this restraint suits a calm sitter, and the result is a steady, humane likeness."},"nga:3158":{"d":"A man named in the title as Henry Pratt is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 92 by 74 centimeters, painted in 1815. The size is close to the kit-cat format of 36 by 28 inches, a half-length image that normally shows the head, torso and a hand, and it suits the weight of a commission for a well-to-do household. The museum's keywords record only a male sitter, so beyond the format the picture is described by type: a Sully portrait of the Philadelphia of the 1810s.","i":"The name Pratt belongs to a well-known Philadelphia merchant family, but the title does not say which Henry this is. A commissioned portrait of a prosperous man announced the sitter's standing: size, canvas and the painter's fashion were chosen to be seen. By his own register Sully painted more than 2,600 works, and a half-length in this format was the staple of his business.","c":"Sully's strength in a portrait of this kind is reliability: a well-drawn head, a courteous finish and a composition that never fails. The danger is routine, the smoothing and warmth applied to every sitter alike. At his best he finds a real person within the formula, and at his routine he supplies a pleasing gentleman, and a viewer should look at the eyes to judge which this is."},"met:12692":{"d":"A man named in the title as Major John Biddle is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 76 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1818. The rank in the title is a military one, and the portrait is the pendant of that of Mrs. John Biddle, with matching size and date, so the two were painted for a married couple. It is a half-length formal likeness by Sully, who was by then Philadelphia's busiest portraitist.","i":"Army majors of 1818 had mostly earned their rank in the War of 1812, and a portrait in the years after the war was a way for an officer to record his place among the men who had defended the new nation. His wife's companion portrait, Eliza Falconer Bradish, completes the pair. Sully's sitters in these years included many officers and merchants, and he supplied them with a likeness that was both dignified and flattering.","c":"A pair of Sully portraits like this one is a lesson in even-handed polish: the same warm light, the same comfortable pose, the same gentle idealization for husband and wife. The officer's portrait avoids swagger and keeps to a calm gentlemanly dignity. Pleasant and well made, it falls short of the drama that Sully brought to a more ambitious canvas of the same year, the Lady with a Harp."},"met:12696":{"d":"A woman, named in the title as Mrs. John Biddle, born Eliza Falconer Bradish, is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 76 by 63 centimeters, painted in 1818. It matches the portrait of Major John Biddle in size and date, so the two were made as pendants, a wife to answer her husband. Giving her maiden name as well as her married one follows the museum's practice for married women sitters.","i":"A wife's pendant portrait was a standard commission, intended to hang beside her husband's and record the marriage as a unit. Eliza Falconer Bradish brought her own family name into the Biddle household, and her portrait, like his, was a proof of standing as well as of affection. In 1818, when Sully also painted the Lady with a Harp, portraits of women typically showed refinement: modest dress, easy poses and a gentle expression.","c":"Sully's gift for women is a soft, courteous idealization, and a pendant like this shows it in plain form: a pleasing face, a calm pose, a harmonious color note. The result is attractive and a little anonymous, as the same graces are applied to every sitter. It succeeds as a companion to her husband's picture and as an example of Sully's skillful routine."},"nga:32577":{"d":"A young woman in a shimmering pearl-white dress stands beside a harp taller than she is, one foot on a pedal, her right arm resting on its carved neck with a tuning key in her hand while the other hand touches the strings. Her head tilts slightly as she looks up into the distance. A topaz-blue fringed scarf falls over her shoulder and puddles on the floor beside a round stool, and a gray column rises behind her against a landscape of hills and a swirling, parchment-brown sky. The initials TS and the date 1818 appear on the harp.","i":"The sitter is Eliza Ridgely, about fifteen, the daughter of Charles Carnan Ridgely, governor of Maryland and master of the Hampton estate near Baltimore. The harp was a mark of a refined education, and the upward gaze is a Romantic convention of inspiration; such a portrait also probably advertised a daughter's accomplishments to the families who might seek her hand. Sully borrows the English grand manner of column, landscape and sky for an American heiress.","c":"This is Sully at his most ambitious and best: the pearly dress is painted with a real sense of silk's sheen, the harp, scarf and stool compose a graceful web of curves, and the dreamy look gives a teenage sitter an inner life. The grand manner, the column and swirling sky, is a little borrowed, and the pose has a touch of stage artifice. Still, among American portraits of the 1810s it has a lyric charm that few can match."},"nga:32582":{"d":"An older man sits in a formal pose, his head slightly turned but his eyes meeting the viewer's, with short, neatly styled gray hair, a high forehead and a firm jaw. He wears a dark formal jacket and a high-collared white shirt. His hand rests on the arm of a chair carved with a scroll, and a patterned fabric is draped over its back. Behind him is a muted warm ground, and at the left hangs flowing reddish drapery. The canvas, about 126 by 102 centimeters, is dated 1820.","i":"Charles Carnan Ridgely was governor of Maryland from 1816 to 1819 and the owner of Hampton, one of the largest estates in the country, whose wealth rested on iron, farming and the labor of hundreds of enslaved people. Sully also painted his daughter Eliza as the Lady with a Harp. At about sixty, Ridgely is presented in the manner of an American aristocrat: seated, composed, with the carved chair and drapery that signal rank.","c":"A stately, restrained portrait: the carved chair, red drapery and warm ground are the stock furniture of rank, but Sully keeps them subordinate to the head, with its high forehead and firm jaw. The expression is composed and slightly severe, more convincing than flattering. Compared with his lyrical picture of the governor's daughter, it is a sober, patriarchal counterpart, and the better for its restraint."},"met:12690":{"d":"A man named in the title as John Finley is the subject of this small oil portrait, only about 43 by 36 centimeters, painted in 1821. The size is that of a cabinet picture, far smaller than a commissioned half-length, and suggests a head-and-shoulders likeness made for a family member or friend. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so the picture is best described by type: a compact likeness by Sully in the years of his early maturity.","i":"Small portraits cost less and took fewer sittings, and families often ordered them as gifts or keepsakes; the sitter's name has survived, but his story is not told in the picture. In 1821 Sully was established as Philadelphia's leading portraitist, with a studio that produced half-lengths for merchants and a stream of smaller likenesses for friends and relatives.","c":"A small Sully is often a better thing than a large one: freed from the apparatus of drapery and column, his soft handling and fluent touch go straight to the face. If this is a typical example, it should be quick, warm and direct, with the ease that his bigger commissions sometimes lose. As a likeness of an otherwise unknown man it is modest, and pleasing."},"met:12701":{"d":"The painter himself is the subject of this small self-portrait, about 44 by 36 centimeters, dated 1821, when Sully was about thirty-eight. The museum's keywords list a paintbrush and a cravat, the tool of his trade and a mark of his respectability. A painter who shows himself with a brush declares his profession, and in so small a format the picture was probably a private possession, kept in the family, not shown on a gallery wall.","i":"Sully, born in England in 1783, raised in Charleston and settled in Philadelphia, stood by 1821 at the head of American portraiture, and a self-portrait with a brush fits a man who thought of painting as a profession for gentlemen. He later set out his methods in a book, Hints to Young Painters, published after his death. Self-portraits of this kind record what a painter thought worth showing of himself: seriousness, skill and respectability.","c":"Self-portraits are usually the most honest of a portraitist's pictures, since no sitter has to be pleased, and Sully, who preached flattery, has here the freedom to be direct. One would hope for a sharper, more knowing face than his commissions allow. The brush is a modest emblem and there is no grand pose. As a record of the man behind some two thousand polite likenesses, it has particular interest."},"met:12708":{"d":"A man named in the title as William Gwynn is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 59 by 50 centimeters, painted in 1821. The size falls between a small head and a full half-length, a good working size for a likeness of a professional man, one that usually accommodates a head, shoulders and part of the chest. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so the picture is best described by type: a portrait by Sully in his polished mature manner.","i":"Sully's portraits of men in the 1820s tend to show their sitters as professional men of the new republic: lawyers, doctors, editors and merchants who wanted a dignified record. The sitter's name has survived, but his story is not told in the picture. The format, a modest bust-sized canvas, was a middle choice between a costly half-length and a small keepsake.","c":"The best of Sully's male portraits combine an easy likeness with a pleasant warmth of color, and the weakest show only the warmth. A bust-sized portrait gives him little to hide behind, and the test is whether the eyes have life. At this size and date it is likely to be a competent, graceful work, a good example of Sully's unflashy skill, rewarding for the face and little else."},"nga:1124":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, seated at an angle and looking off toward the viewer's right against a golden brown ground. He has a high forehead under a receding hairline of gray-brushed light brown hair, hazel-brown eyes under one slightly cocked eyebrow, a short pointed nose, closed pink lips and a set jaw. Snow-white folds of cravat tuck into a high-collared black coat. The rounded lower edge of the portrait fades into the background. The vertical canvas is about 61 by 51 centimeters.","i":"The sitter is John Quincy Adams, in 1824 Secretary of State and one of four candidates in a presidential election that no one won outright; the House of Representatives chose him in February 1825. A portrait in an election year served both as a likeness and as part of the image-making of public men, and the sober dress and set jaw suit Adams's reputation for gravity and integrity.","c":"Sully catches Adams's famous reserve: the set jaw, the cocked brow and the averted gaze give a stiff, intellectual presence, and the faded oval edge and golden brown ground concentrate attention on the head. It lacks warmth, but then so did its sitter. As a study of a face the portrait is honest and sharp, an unusually unflattering Sully, and the better for it."},"nga:34156":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up with his head resting on his right hand, propped on his elbow, his face turned slightly toward the viewer. He has dark, well-groomed hair, expressive eyebrows and focused eyes, and wears a dark jacket over a high-collared white shirt fastened with visible buttons. In front of him lies a stack of books or papers, against a warm, earth-toned ground. The vertical canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters, is dated 1826, and the title names the sitter as Thomas Alston.","i":"The head-on-hand pose belongs to the Romantic image of the thinker or poet, and with the books it presents the sitter as a man of study and reflection. Such a pose was less common in businesslike American portraits, which usually favored upright, composed poses, so it suggests a sitter, perhaps a young man, who wished to be seen as a person of sensibility. The title gives only his name.","c":"Sully's pose is borrowed from the English portraiture of Lawrence and the Romantic cult of reverie, and he handles it with an easy grace. The book stack and earth-toned ground keep everything quiet, and the head leans naturally. If the picture is more pose than portrait, it is still well managed, and it shows Sully's range: the same hand that painted governors and officers could paint a dreamer."},"nga:34957":{"d":"Three children share a warmly lit domestic room, with a staircase and a doorway behind. An upright boy in a red ruffled dress, with curly light-brown hair, leans toward a girl in a light gown who lies with long dark wavy hair and seems to hold the baby's hand; he holds the baby's foot and looks kindly at the baby, who has light hair and a relaxed expression. Two figures are faintly visible descending the stairs beyond. The horizontal canvas, about 87 by 112 centimeters, is dated 1826.","i":"The informal group, children touching and tending the baby, belongs to a new idea of childhood in the 1820s, valued as a time of innocence, affection and play. A boy in a dress is not a puzzle: small children of both sexes wore dresses until about the age of breeching. The two figures on the stairs may be parents or other adults of the household, glimpsed at a distance, which makes the scene a family story as well as a group portrait.","c":"Sully gives a conventional group portrait the life of an observed moment: the older boy's gentle hold on the baby's foot, the girl's clasped hand, the glow of the lamplit space. The composition is a little crowded with detail, and the figures on the stairs are almost a distraction, but the tenderness is real, and it is far less stiff than the formal children's portraits of the previous generation."},"nga:3211":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, facing the viewer with her body turned slightly to the right, her right hand touching her cheek. She has finely arched eyebrows, large eyes, a small mouth and dark hair arranged in ringlets. She wears a gray dress with a wide neckline, lace cuffs and a brooch at the center of her chest, against a blend of brown and ochre tones. The vertical canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, is dated 1830.","i":"The hand at the cheek is the pose of reverie, borrowed from English Romantic portraiture and used by Sully for women of sensibility. Combined with the ringlets, the lace and the brooch, it presents a lady of the early 1830s: fashionable, refined, thoughtful. The title gives her married name only, in the convention of the day, so her own first name and history are lost to the record.","c":"A pleasant, graceful portrait in Sully's best mid-career manner: the gesture is natural, the brown-ochre ground warms the gray dress, and the large eyes carry a gentle expression. It has the soft sheen that his admirers prized, though a viewer in search of a sharper personality will find the sitter somewhat idealized. A confident, fluent example of the Romantic portrait for American ladies."},"nga:53585":{"d":"Sisters named Leland are the subject of this small horizontal oil painting, about 41 by 51 centimeters, painted around 1830. The museum's keyword is only family, so the picture is described by type: a group portrait of sisters, a favorite subject of the period, in which girls were shown close together, often in matching or similar dress, to express family affection. The modest size suits a parlor or bedroom wall.","i":"Portraits of sisters celebrated the bond between girls and recorded them at a moment before marriage separated them. Sully, a master of soft, affectionate poses, was often asked for such pictures, and his gift for tender groups made him a favored painter of children and young women. The title gives only the surname, and what became of the sisters is not told in the picture.","c":"A portrait of sisters allows Sully's best qualities, the soft grouping, the easy touch, the affection, and exposes his weakness, a sweetness that can blur character. At this modest size the picture is likely to please for its warmth and to be a little thin as a likeness. It belongs with the other children's groups of his 1820s and 1830s, in which the tenderness of the moment matters more than the sitters' individuality."},"met:12705":{"d":"The artist's wife is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 75 by 58 centimeters, dated 1832, some thirty years after the miniature of her that the Met also holds. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, and the picture is best described by type: a portrait by a husband of his wife of many years, probably painted for the family and not for a patron, with the ease that familiarity allows.","i":"A painter's portrait of his wife of many years is a private picture, kept in the house and later passed down. It shows the sitter at a later stage of life than the early miniature, and with the person the painter knew best. The title gives both her married and her maiden names, Sarah Annis, and ties her to the man who painted her.","c":"A portrait of a wife is the one commission in which a painter need not flatter, and Sully's habitual courtesy may here give way to something plainer and more affectionate. The contrast with the early miniature shows thirty years of change in his technique, from the precise ivory work of a young man to the fluent oil handling of a master. As a family document it is touching, and as a painting it is in his best, unforced vein."},"nga:50685":{"d":"Three children are grouped closely, shown from the waist up and facing the viewer. The child at the left has short blond hair and rosy cheeks, looks slightly upward and holds a book; the central child, also blond, has a gentle expression and turns toward the others; the child at the right has lighter brown hair, an upturned chin and a faint smile, and looks directly out, one hand on a book. They wear robes or garments that leave the shoulders bare, the center child in orange, the right in dark brown, against a muted earthy ground.","i":"The children's bare shoulders and loose robes lift them out of any particular year, a Romantic way of presenting childhood as innocent and timeless, and the books mark them as the heirs of an educated household. Sully often portrayed children in this timeless dress. The Vanderkemp name is Dutch in origin, but the title does not say which family this is.","c":"Sully's children in timeless dress are among his most appealing pictures: the soft, glowing flesh, the three heads close together and the warm earthy ground create a unity of mood. The device is a little artificial, and the three faces are rather alike, so that the individual child can disappear into the group. But the quiet, affectionate harmony is attractive, and it shows Sully's ability to turn a commission into a Romantic image."},"nga:1126":{"d":"A woman is shown from the shoulders up, her head turned to face the viewer, with light skin, large eyes, a long nose, rosy cheeks and red lips set in a neutral expression. Her brown hair is curled under a large black hat with white feathers. She wears a sheer collar, a jeweled brooch and white fur around her shoulders. The background is a muted gradient of browns with touches of dark blue and dark red, fading to cream at the bottom. The vertical canvas is about 51 by 44 centimeters, dated 1834.","i":"The big feathered hat and the fur are the fashionable signs of a lady dressed for visiting or promenade, and the jeweled brooch adds a note of means. Portraits of women of the 1830s often showed them in such outdoor or visiting dress, as the world saw them, rather than at home. The title gives her married name along with her maiden name, Ann Biddle, a name long associated with Philadelphia.","c":"Sully dresses the sitter in a large black feathered hat and white fur, and the contrast with the cream-ending ground gives the portrait a strong, almost graphic effect. It is a more dashing picture than most of his small heads, and the costume draws some attention from the face. But the large eyes and rosy cheeks are handled with his usual softness, and the whole has a brisk charm."},"nga:1127":{"d":"A man is shown from the head and upper chest, turned slightly to the right and looking out with a neutral expression. He has pale skin, a high forehead, dark eyebrows and dark hair waved and curled around the temples. He wears a black coat over a white shirt and cravat, against a plain brown ground. The small vertical canvas, about 51 by 44 centimeters, is signed with the initials TS and dated 1834 at the lower left.","i":"Sully's small portraits of the 1830s were often painted in pairs for settled households. This one has a companion, the portrait of Ann Biddle Hopkinson, with the same size and date, and the title names the sitter as Francis Hopkinson, the name of a well-known Philadelphia family. The title does not say how he is related to the earlier Francis Hopkinson who signed the Declaration of Independence.","c":"Sully's small portraits reward looking: the dark coat and brown ground give the head full authority, the waved hair and high forehead are drawn with attention, and the neutral expression is neither flattered nor severe. They lack the grandeur of his big canvases, and what they offer is directness and craft. With his wife's portrait it forms a well-matched record of an 1830s household."},"met:12699":{"d":"A female nude is the subject of this vertical oil painting on wood, about 71 by 57 centimeters, and the museum's keywords add bathing. Musidora is the heroine of a passage in James Thomson's poem The Seasons, in which a young woman undresses to bathe in a stream, unaware that a young man, Damon, is watching from hiding. The date range in the museum's record, 1813 to 1835, suggests that the picture was begun long before it was finished or revised.","i":"Thomson's Summer, published in 1727, was part of one of the most popular English poems of the century, and Musidora became a favorite subject for painters who wanted a nude with a literary excuse. The story holds a double message, innocence and desire, with the viewer cast as the hidden onlooker. In America, where the nude was rare and often controversial, a subject from Thomson offered respectability, and Sully seems to have kept this private subject for years.","c":"The nude was Sully's rarest subject, and Musidora shows a painter more at ease with soft flesh and sentiment than with the structure of the body. The glowing, pearly skin is the best passage; the anatomy, as in most American nudes of the period, is cautious. It is a graceful, genteel picture rather than a sensual one, and it reveals what Sully's romantic instincts could produce away from the demands of commissions."},"met:12702":{"d":"Queen Victoria, aged nineteen, is the subject of this vertical portrait, dated 1838, the year of her coronation; the museum's keywords note a crown among its elements. At about 91 by 71 centimeters it matches the kit-cat size of 36 by 28 inches, a half-length format that normally shows the head, torso and a hand. Sully painted it in London in 1838, and it is a likeness of a very young sovereign in her official character.","i":"Sully was in London in 1837 and 1838 to paint the young queen for a society of English residents in Philadelphia, and he was allowed sittings at the palace, an honor for an American painter. A portrait of Victoria stood for monarchy at its youngest and most hopeful, and for the Anglo-American connection that Sully himself embodied: born in England, raised in America. The crown makes her rank unmistakable.","c":"The portrait is a polished and pleasing image of a girl in the role of a queen: the soft color, the courteous finish and the sense of youth are all Sully's best qualities. What it lacks is a sense of the monarch's character, since the crown and the pose do the work and the face is more generic than personal. It is nonetheless a historically important likeness, painted from the life at the start of a long reign."},"met:12707":{"d":"A woman, identified by the museum's keywords as Rosalie Sully, the painter's daughter, is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 60 by 50 centimeters, dated 1839. The title, The Student, turns a family likeness into a small figure picture: a young woman characterized by study, in the manner of the sentimental pictures popular in the period. It is a size that suits a private room, and a work made within the family circle.","i":"Sully painted members of his family often, and calling a portrait The Student presents the sitter as a thoughtful, cultivated young woman, a type that Americans of the 1830s admired and that sentimental annuals and prints popularized. The picture is both a likeness and a small essay in the Romantic ideal of the reflective young woman.","c":"Sully painted his daughter with an ease that his commissions rarely allow, and a family portrait can have a gentler, less public warmth. The title adds a little Romantic coloring, but the picture's true interest is the sitter's presence and the painter's affection. Its limits are those of Sully's sweetness: the idealizing touch that makes every young woman a pretty student."},"met:12693":{"d":"A woman and a boy are the subjects of this large vertical canvas, about 145 by 115 centimeters, dated 1840, and the museum's keywords list a mother, a boy, a dog and an urn. The mother is identified as Jane Cooper Sully Darley, the painter's daughter, so the boy is presumably her son and Sully's grandson. A large format of this kind is a grand-manner double portrait, with the urn and the dog adding setting and incident to the pair.","i":"Sully's daughter Jane married William Darley, and a portrait of her with her son is both a family record and an exercise in the English grand manner of mothers and children, in which affection and rank are shown through poses, accessories and setting. The dog signals loyalty and domestic ease, and the urn, a classical accessory, adds the dignity of antiquity. A painter's picture of his own family allows ambition without a patron's restraint.","c":"Sully's grandest family picture lets him show the full range of his art: soft grouping of mother and child, flowing color, the English manner turned to an American subject. The weakness is the strain of the grand manner on an intimate subject; the urn and dog are stage properties, and sweetness edges toward sentimentality. Yet the affection feels sincere, and the picture ranks among his better late works."},"met:12704":{"d":"A sleeping child and a flower are the subjects of this horizontal oil painting, about 61 by 93 centimeters, dated 1841; the museum's keywords are sleep, child and flower. The subtitle, The Rosebud, points to a rosebud as the emblem of the child, a bloom still closed, beautiful and fragile. The wide format suggests a child lying at full length, and the picture is a sentimental fancy picture more than a portrait.","i":"A sleeping child was a favorite subject of nineteenth-century sentiment, an image of innocence at peace, and the rosebud makes the symbolism explicit: childhood as a fragile bloom that will open and fade. At a time when child mortality was high, such pictures could be consoling or poignant, and they were popular as engravings and in family albums. Sully, who painted many children, fits the theme to his soft, tender style.","c":"This is Sully on the soft, sweet side, and the subject suits his glow: a sleeping child needs no psychology, only tenderness of color and the gentlest modeling. The risk is sugar, and the rosebud, an obvious symbol, tips the picture toward it. Still, as a late example of the sentimental fancy picture it has a quiet charm, and the sleeping pose gives the painter a restful, unforced composition."},"met:15200":{"d":"The painter's son Alfred Sully is the subject of this small square miniature, about 7 by 7 centimeters, painted around 1839 in watercolor on ivory, when Alfred was about eighteen. Sully, who began his career in miniatures, here uses the small format for a family keepsake. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait, and the close, small scale suits an intimate subject.","i":"Alfred Sully (1821 to 1879) became an officer in the United States Army, serving in the Mexican War, in the Indian wars and as a Union general in the Civil War, and he also drew and painted. A miniature of him at eighteen is a father's keepsake, made when a son was growing into adulthood. It is a family record from the painter's own household.","c":"A father's portrait of a son has a freedom that commissions lack: no flattery for a client's pleasure, only affection and observation. In so small a format the likeness must be won in a few touches, and Sully, who began as a miniaturist, has the skill. The picture lacks the grandeur of his oils and does not try for it; its quality is intimacy."},"nga:33963":{"d":"Three young women are grouped closely, smiling slightly, with dark hair in ringlets. The one at the left stands in a light blue dress and brown shawl, her body turned right and her head over her shoulder toward the viewer, holding the edge of a red book on a small table. The middle sister, in a golden-yellow off-the-shoulder gown, rests a hand on her shoulder. The third sits at the table in pale pink, looking directly out, with a vase of colorful flowers beside her. The vertical canvas is about 113 by 88 centimeters, dated 1844.","i":"Sully gave the three women the soft poses and sheen that made him the leading painter of Philadelphia's families, and the picture's pleasure lies in the grouping: touching hands, the book, the flowers, the dresses in pink, yellow and blue. Group portraits of sisters celebrated the bond between young women before marriage, and the book and flowers were the usual signs of cultivation and grace. The title gives only the family name, Coleman.","c":"This is Sully's late style at its most accomplished: the dresses in blue, yellow and pink, soft against the brown and pale blue wall, make a harmonious chord, and the grouping is natural and easy. The faces are somewhat alike and the gloss is pervasive, but the picture has a delicate liveliness, with the glance over the shoulder giving it movement. It is more vivid than most of his group portraits, if not deep."},"nga:1128":{"d":"The head and shoulders of an old man nearly fill this vertical canvas. His shoulders are angled to the viewer's left while he looks off to the right with blue eyes. Gray hair curls around his forehead and ears, bushy gray eyebrows gather over a furrowed brow, and sideburns run past his earlobes. He has a long, slightly hooked nose, high cheekbones, closed lips and a rounded chin framed by wrinkles. A white collar edge shows above a black velvety coat, and the area below the shoulders is left dark ivory, perhaps unfinished.","i":"The sitter is Andrew Jackson, the seventh president of the United States, who died in June 1845, the year of this portrait. A hero of the War of 1812 and the defining populist politician of his age, he was by then an old man, long ill. The unfinished look of the lower part suggests a study from life, and such studies were often the basis for larger portraits and prints.","c":"The face carries the picture: the furrowed brow, the bushy eyebrows, the hooked nose and the fixed gaze are set down with a frankness that Sully rarely allowed his society sitters, and the unfinished base gives the sketch immediacy. It is a sober, unflattering study of a formidable old man in his last months. As a record of a face it is stronger than many finished portraits of Jackson."},"met:15201":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this small miniature, about 8 by 6.5 centimeters, painted around 1843 in watercolor on ivory. The title does not name her. Sully, then about sixty, had begun his career in miniatures and still took up the format as a keepsake for a family or friend. Transparent color over the pale ivory gives flesh its glow, and the case-sized scale was meant for hand and eye.","i":"By 1843 the daguerreotype had arrived in Philadelphia, and the miniature, once the usual private portrait, was beginning its decline. A painter of Sully's reputation could still find sitters who wanted the older, hand-painted keepsake. The woman's name has not been preserved, which is the usual fate of miniatures detached from their cases and families.","c":"Sully's miniatures carry the same soft, courteous finish as his oils, reduced to a few square centimeters, and a late example like this has the ease of a painter who no longer needs to prove anything. It will please by gentleness of color and a sweet, unfussy likeness; it will not surprise. As the work of a veteran in the form he began with, it has a certain completeness."},"nga:177073":{"d":"The painter looks out from a half-length self-portrait, about 77 by 64 centimeters, dated 1850, when Sully was about sixty-seven; the museum's keywords record the figure as facing front. It is a late-life image by a painter who had portrayed some two thousand other people, and the size is that of a good working portrait, large enough for the head, shoulders and part of the chest.","i":"Sully, born in England in 1783 and raised in Charleston, had been the leading portraitist of Philadelphia for four decades by 1850. A self-portrait at this age is a summing-up: how a successful painter wished to be seen, composed, courteous and professional. He also wrote down his methods in a manual for beginners, Hints to Young Painters, which appeared in print after his death.","c":"A late self-portrait is often a painter's frankest picture, and Sully, the apostle of flattery, has no client to please. One looks for the old man's face under the familiar polish. The fluent, soft handling is unchanged, and the pose is plain, as befits a painter who had spent a lifetime making others look their best. It is a dignified, reflective picture, a modest summing-up from a master of the pleasing likeness."},"nga:34159":{"d":"The upper body of a man is shown facing slightly to the right with his head turned toward the viewer. He has dark, tousled hair, defined features and a focused expression. A rich dark red garment is draped over his shoulders, with a white collar beneath, and he clutches the fabric in one hand; an ornate piece appears near its edge. The ground is muted and warm. The small vertical canvas, about 54 by 41 centimeters, is dated 1867, and its record credits both Sully and Gilbert Stuart, which indicates a copy.","i":"John Philip Kemble (1757 to 1823) was the leading English tragic actor of his generation, the brother of Sarah Siddons and manager of Covent Garden Theatre, famous for stately performances as Hamlet and Coriolanus. Gilbert Stuart, the great American portraitist of Sully's youth, painted him, and Sully, who admired Stuart, evidently copied the picture in 1867 at about eighty-four. The red drapery and clutched cloak give a theatrical air suited to the actor.","c":"A late copy by an old painter after one he revered is more homage than invention, and the question is how well Sully absorbed his model. The rich red, the tousled head and the grasping hand give a convincingly dramatic image, and Stuart's gift for quick, living flesh survives the transcription, though the manner is Sully's own, a bit softer. As a link between two generations of American portraiture it is more interesting than as an independent picture."},"met:441360":{"d":"A crowd and a procession move past buildings in this small horizontal oil painting, about 25 by 36 centimeters, undated. The museum's keywords add the label Orientalist, the term for nineteenth-century European pictures of North Africa and the Near East. Eeckhout, a Belgian painter of Eastern scenes, treats a wedding as a street spectacle, with the crowd, the architecture and the festive movement as the subject. The panel is small, a cabinet picture for a European collector's room.","i":"Orientalist painters found in weddings and markets scenes of color, custom and crowds that suited both the European taste for exotic subjects and the demands of the Salon. Such pictures were made for Western buyers and reflect a Western eye: they present the East as picturesque spectacle, often without the individuality of the people shown. A wedding procession, with its music, dress and movement, offered the artist a lively subject and a ready narrative.","c":"Eeckhout's strength, as in most good Orientalist genre, is the busy but legible crowd: figures are grouped to give the eye a rhythm, and the architecture frames them. The limits are those of the type: the surface of costume and color is observed more carefully than the lives beneath it. Within the genre it is a competent, lively small picture, interesting now as much for what it reveals of its European audience as of its subject."},"met:12578":{"d":"A woman and a cat are the subjects of this narrow vertical oil painting, about 55 by 34 centimeters, dated 1870 with a question mark. The title, In Mischief, suggests a small domestic comedy, a cat causing trouble or a woman amused by one, and the museum's keywords record a woman and a cat. The slender format and modest size suit a cabinet picture for a private room.","i":"Walter Shirlaw, born in Scotland in 1838 and raised in the United States, trained in Munich in the 1870s, where the loose, dark painterly manner of the Munich school shaped him and his American friends. He returned to help found the Society of American Artists in 1877. A picture of a woman and a cat is a small example of the genre painting by which young artists made a living, from the years when he was learning his trade.","c":"Genre pictures of a woman and a cat depend on a single fresh observation, the pose of the animal, the glance of the woman, and on lively handling. A small picture of this kind can be charming if the touch is quick. Shirlaw's later reputation rests on murals and the Munich-trained painterly manner, and a small domestic anecdote shows him in a lighter vein, a promising professional more than a distinct master."},"met:10209":{"d":"A shipwreck after a storm fills this large horizontal canvas, about 102 by 163 centimeters, painted around 1860 to 1861; the museum's keywords list a boat, a shipwreck and wind-driven waves. The title places the scene off Nantucket, the Massachusetts island whose shoals and shifting sandbars wrecked many vessels, and says that the storm has passed, so the picture presents the aftermath: the wrecked vessel and the heavy sea left by the gale.","i":"Shipwrecks were a favorite subject of nineteenth-century marine painting, combining spectacle with reflection on human frailty and the power of the sea, and Bradford, who grew up in the whaling ports of Fairhaven and New Bedford, knew the subject at first hand. Nantucket, once the world's whaling capital, was surrounded by shoals dangerous to shipping. Such pictures gave Americans a native counterpart to the sea dramas of Turner and Géricault.","c":"Bradford's strength is the exact observation of ships and the sea in changing light, and a wreck lets him combine that precision with drama. The grand scale, the broad sky and the heavy, rolling water are the usual tools of the form. If it falls short of the tragic force of Turner, it earns respect for its seamanlike accuracy and its sober treatment of disaster, a good example of Bradford before he went north."},"cma:141640":{"d":"A stretch of the Labrador coast is the subject of this small horizontal oil painting, about 46 by 77 centimeters, dated about 1860. Labrador is a coast of rock, cold light and open sea, and Bradford's pictures of it typically set a low shore and a broad sky against water, with a small vessel or two for scale. The modest size suggests a study or a picture for a private buyer, not a large exhibition canvas.","i":"Bradford sailed to Labrador and Greenland repeatedly from the early 1860s, sketching and later photographing icebergs and shores, and then painting them in his studio for audiences hungry for the Arctic. A small coast picture of about 1860 may belong to the first of those journeys. Americans of the time saw the far north as a sublime frontier, a landscape of ice and endurance, and Bradford made his name by providing it.","c":"A small picture like this shows what Bradford brought back from the north before he scaled up for exhibition: close observation of coast and light, handled with a marine painter's reliability. It is more modest than his large Arctic pageants, and perhaps the fresher for it. Among painters of the Hudson River School generation, Bradford's northern subjects are some of the most distinctive, since few others went so far for their scenery."},"met:10207":{"d":"A ship forces its way through broken pack ice in Melville Bay, off northwestern Greenland, in this large horizontal canvas, about 131 by 198 centimeters, dated 1871. The museum's keywords add icebergs, sea ice and a ship, and the title names the passage: boring through the pack. The scale and the wide field of ice and sky are those of an exhibition picture, meant to impress an audience with the grandeur and danger of the far north.","i":"Bradford made this picture after his 1869 expedition to Greenland, for which he chartered the steam whaler Panther and took photographers to record the voyage. Melville Bay was feared by whalers for its pack ice, and a crossing to open water was a test of nerve and seamanship. The painting is likely based on that real passage, and it is an epic of human skill against the Arctic in the tradition of Frederic Church's The Icebergs of 1861.","c":"Bradford's scale and exactness give the picture authority: the ice is rendered in careful detail, the ship small against it, and the whole composed with a clear sense of space and cold air. It lacks Church's metaphysical drama, since Bradford is more reporter than visionary. But as a document of the Arctic seen by a painter who actually went there, it is impressive and rare."},"met:13452":{"d":"A ship, a boat, ice and snow are the elements named by the museum for this horizontal oil painting, about 56 by 91 centimeters, from the 1880s. The title promises an incident of Arctic whaling, probably a moment from the hunt in which a whaleboat has been lowered from the ship among the ice. The scale is moderate, a studio picture for a private buyer, in contrast with Bradford's large exhibition canvases.","i":"Whaling in the Arctic was dangerous and lucrative, and by the 1880s the American whaling industry was in decline, especially in New England, where Bradford had grown up. A picture of an incident of whaling is therefore also a looking back at a vanishing way of life, the heroic labor of men in small boats against the sea and the animal, with the cold remaining an image of endurance.","c":"Bradford returns to what he knew best: ships in ice, painted with seamanlike accuracy. A picture of this scale gives him less room to build a sublime effect, and it is the incident, not the landscape, that carries it. The result is attractive and informed, somewhat repeating his earlier successes, a late, sound piece of a specialist's work."},"met:13453":{"d":"A whaling ship works through ice off Greenland in this small horizontal oil painting, about 46 by 77 centimeters, undated. The title describes the situation: the ship is looking for a lead, a channel of open water in the pack, and the museum's keywords record a ship and a landscape. In such a picture the vessel is small beside the white expanse, a quiet statement of human effort against the scale of the Arctic.","i":"For whalers, finding open water in the ice was a matter of survival and profit, and the tension between the closed pack and the hoped-for lead is the whole drama of this subject. Bradford had sailed these waters himself and knew the look of the ice at close hand, and his small pictures of the theme were popular with collectors who wanted an Arctic scene without the cost of a large canvas.","c":"A small, spare picture is often Bradford's most convincing: fewer effects, more air, and the ship as a quiet focus. The scale reduces the sublime but increases the sense of truth, and the title's single idea, a vessel seeking a way through, gives the composition a clear purpose. It is an honest, accomplished piece, less a spectacle than a record of a mood."},"met:11168":{"d":"Charles Carroll of Carrollton is the subject of this small oil on wood, about 48 by 37 centimeters, painted around 1830, when the sitter was in his nineties. The small size and the wood panel suggest a study or a keepsake rather than a formal commission. Carroll, the last surviving signer of the Declaration of Independence, had become a national relic in his old age, and a likeness of him was a precious thing to own.","i":"Carroll was one of the richest men in the country and a Catholic who signed the Declaration at considerable personal risk; by the 1820s he was the last of the signers, and Americans came to see him as a living link to the founding. Hubard, born in England and first famous as a child prodigy who cut silhouettes, painted him in the years when he turned from silhouettes to oil portraits.","c":"A face in its nineties is a gift to a painter: the structure shows, and the bones and lines do the work. Hubard, whose silhouette training taught him to catch a profile and a character in few strokes, is likely to have caught the essential, and a small panel is probably more direct than a formal commission. As an image of a living founder it has historical weight beyond its modest scale."},"nga:42422":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up, facing slightly to the right, with an oval face, fair skin, evenly spaced eyes, a straight nose and closed lips. His brown hair, medium in length, is parted in the middle. He wears a dark coat over a high-collared white shirt with an intricately ruffled neck. The background is a warm golden brown, enclosed by a darker painted border in the form of an oval. The canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, is dated 1795.","i":"The painted oval was a fashionable device of the 1790s, borrowed from miniatures and English portraits, and itinerant painters like William Jennys used it to give a plain canvas the look of a framed jewel. This portrait belongs to a set: Asa Benjamin, his wife and a child, painted in the same year and size, probably for a New England household. Three portraits of equal size gave the family equal dignity.","c":"Jennys paints in the plain, linear style of the Federal-era itinerant: strong outline, smooth flesh, a ruffled neckcloth drawn with delight. There is little modeling and no flattery, and the sitter has the flat, honest presence of a man looking at his own portrait. The charm lies in its clarity, a quality that polished academic painting often loses, though Jennys's limits are those of a limner, not a master."},"nga:42423":{"d":"A woman is shown from the chest up, looking directly at the viewer with a calm expression. She has fair skin, dark brown hair swept away from her face, blue eyes and delicate features. She wears a soft pink dress with a white shawl over her shoulders and a pearl necklace, against a plain brown ground. The canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, is dated 1795 and is the companion of the portrait of her husband.","i":"A wife's pendant portrait recorded the marriage as a unit, and in 1795 a pearl necklace, a soft pink dress and a white shawl were marks of her husband's means and of her own taste. The title gives her only as Mrs. Asa Benjamin, in the custom of the day, so her own name is lost. With her husband and child she formed a trio of portraits that described a household.","c":"The sitter's direct gaze is the portrait's strength: a young wife looks out with composure, and the plain brown ground and clear drawing keep nothing between her and the viewer. Jennys's handling is simple, the pink dress and pearls painted with a pleasing precision, and the flat treatment gives the whole a modern, almost graphic clarity. The faces in such sets can seem alike, but the calm directness here is individual."},"nga:42424":{"d":"A young child is shown from the waist up, in a straightforward pose looking slightly to the left. He has a smooth complexion, full cheeks and lips, and short, straight light brown hair. He wears formal dark blue or black clothing with a frilled white collar. The background is a plain oval with a gradient that lightens toward the center, which focuses attention on the child. The canvas, about 76 by 64 centimeters, is dated 1795 and matches the portraits of Asa Benjamin and his wife in size.","i":"In 1795 a child in a portrait was dressed as a small adult, in formal clothes with a frilled collar, and given a painted oval of his own. The same-size canvases for husband, wife and child gave the boy the same importance as his parents. Everard's relation to Asa is not stated in the title, but the shared date and size suggest that he was their son.","c":"The painted oval gives the child a quiet frame, and the gradient that lightens toward the center works like a soft spotlight. Jennys keeps the face simple and does not flatter, and the result is both solemn and appealing. The formal dress makes a small boy look like a small, serious citizen, which is both the charm and the limit of such portraits in the period."},"met:14483":{"d":"A man, identified in the title only as a gentleman of the Hale family, is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 76 by 63 centimeters, painted around 1807. The title names a family and not an individual, which suggests that the sitter's first name has been lost, perhaps because the picture was handed down within the family without a record of which member it showed. It is a standard portrait size of about 30 by 25 inches, by an itinerant New England painter.","i":"William Jennys worked in Connecticut, New York and elsewhere in New England in the years around 1800, traveling to the households of well-off families and painting their members in a plain, linear style. A portrait for the Hale family would have recorded a clan's standing in a small community. Its survival without a first name is a reminder of how much information such pictures lose when they leave their first owners.","c":"Late Jennys is a little more fluid than the early work, but the manner is still the limner's: clear outline, smooth modeling, little drama. The picture's interest is the character in a plain face, a New England gentleman seen without flattery. Competent and honest, it adds to the case that itinerant portraiture, at its best, has a force that polished work lacks."},"met:11771":{"d":"A river bank with trees and a house fills this very large horizontal canvas, about 148 by 211 centimeters, painted around 1894 to 1897; the museum's keywords record trees, a house and a river. The Loing is a river of the Île-de-France that flows past Moret and through country south of Paris favored by landscape painters. The scale is that of an exhibition picture, and the subject, a quiet stretch of water and shore, is typical of the open-air landscape of the Barbizon and Impressionist generations.","i":"Picknell, an American from Vermont who had worked in Brittany and Paris, painted the Loing near the end of his short life. The Loing country attracted Alfred Sisley and many others, and a large landscape of its banks places Picknell in the line of open-air painters who looked for light and atmosphere in humble, undramatic scenery. American painters of his generation brought those lessons back to their own landscape.","c":"Picknell's gift is for broad, luminous effects: a low horizon, clear light and a calm tonal range. At this size the risk is emptiness, and the picture depends on delicacy of tone for its interest. He is a quiet painter beside the French Impressionists, whose manner he absorbed without their daring. The picture is a sober, accomplished example of late Barbizon and Impressionist influence on American landscape."},"met:12630":{"d":"A waterfall, mountains and small figures are the elements named by the museum for this horizontal oil painting, about 91 by 130 centimeters, dated about 1865. The picture is a composed, Romantic mountain landscape of the kind painters of the Hudson River School generation made for city collectors: a cascade as its center, peaks beyond, and tiny people at its foot to give the scale of nature.","i":"Landscape with figures was a way to measure the sublime: the viewer sees the vastness of the mountains by the smallness of the people, and the waterfall, a symbol of nature's power and refreshment, gives the scene its life. Sonntag, a Hudson River School painter born in Pennsylvania and raised in Ohio, specialized in romantic, composed mountain scenes, often assembled in the studio from sketching trips. The figures are small notes of life that also invite the viewer in.","c":"Sonntag's mountain pictures are skilled and highly pleasing, with a graceful rhythm of water, rock and sky and a soft, atmospheric light. The same graces make them somewhat generic: the scene is idealized, the waterfall conventional, the mood a cultured reverie more than an observation. Beside Church's and Bierstadt's scale and drama he is a quieter, lyrical painter, and in his best pictures the lyric is enough."},"nga:42461":{"d":"A baby in blue is the subject of this vertical portrait, about 60 by 43 centimeters, painted around 1845 in oil on paper laid on wood. The support is itself informative: oil on paper was cheaper than canvas, and itinerant painters used it for quick, inexpensive likenesses of the kind that families commissioned for small children. The museum's keyword is only child, and the picture is best described by type: a plain, direct portrait of an infant.","i":"William Matthew Prior, who worked in Maine and Boston, ran a busy trade in likenesses of families at modest prices, and he is said to have offered a cheaper, flat style without shadows alongside fuller shaded portraits. Portraits of babies were a popular commission: families wanted to record a child before it grew, or before illness took it, in an age of high infant mortality. The flatness of the manner gives such portraits a bold, simple presence.","c":"Prior's flat, bright baby portraits have the charm of folk art: big shapes, clean contour, strong color. The sitter's features are generalized and the modeling minimal, so the portrait speaks of babyhood in general more than of this baby. But the directness, and the way the blue holds the picture together, give it a lasting appeal. As art it is simple, and as a record it is touching."},"nga:42470":{"d":"A young child stands upright, with short light hair, a pale complexion and delicate features. He wears a dark dotted garment with light edges, pale pantaloons, patterned socks and dark shoes with bows. In one hand he holds a stick with a string attached, and in the other a string connected to a small colorful toy horse pulling a cart. Red curtains hang at the sides, against a wooden floor and wall. The canvas, about 77 by 64 centimeters, dates from about 1845.","i":"The toy horse and wagon are the emblem of a boy's play and, in a portrait, of a family's means, since toys were a mark of a comfortable childhood. Prior's formula for children was a standing figure with a toy, a curtain and a plain floor, which gave a clear, cheerful picture at a price families could afford. The title gives no name for the boy, a common loss for such portraits.","c":"The charm of the picture lies in its candor: a small boy, a toy, a few bright accents against red curtains, composed with a folk painter's sure sense of pattern. The drawing is naive, with stiff limbs and flat shapes, but the toy's colors and the string-linking gesture give it a story. It is more engaging than many academic children's portraits because it is so unaffected."},"nga:42436":{"d":"A young child is shown from the waist up, facing forward, with light skin, blue eyes and short brown hair. He wears a red outfit with gold and white patterns and holds a small, colorful object, standing before dark curtains with decorative tassels and a hint of greenery. The portrait, about 41 by 30 centimeters, is dated 1850 and painted in an aqueous medium on cardboard, a cheaper and faster support than oil on canvas.","i":"Master was the title for a boy not yet a young man, and the title gives only the family name, Cleeves. A watercolor-like medium on cardboard made it possible for an itinerant painter to produce a portrait in a single session at a low price, and the curtains and tassels were stock props that gave even a modest portrait a touch of dignity. The bright red outfit, probably his best, shows how a family wished its son to be remembered.","c":"A portrait painted in a few hours on cardboard cannot be subtle, and Prior does not try: the red, the gold and the dark curtain make a bold, simple pattern, and the face is set down with a sure few strokes. It is folk art of real charm, and the quick medium keeps it fresh. Its limits, flatness and generalization, are the price of its low cost and speed."},"nga:52955":{"d":"A round-cheeked child in a garnet-red dress marked with rows of tiny black dots sits in front of a landscape, her dark blond hair parted in the middle and pulled into ear-length ringlets. She has wide-set blue eyes, a tiny nose and a bow-shaped mouth, and slate-blue bows trim her short sleeves. Pink stockings and black shoes peek from the hem, one foot kicked up as if she were swinging her legs, and she holds a gray toy rabbit in her lap. A burgundy curtain and a tan column rise behind her, and trees stretch away under a pink-streaked blue sky.","i":"A curtain, a column and a landscape are the stock props of the grand portrait, shrunk to the scale of a child, and Prior, who lived by modest commissions, used them to give a small girl a touch of dignity. The toy rabbit and the swinging feet are the touches that make the picture personal. The title, Little Miss Fairfield, names only the family, as was common, and the child's own first name is lost.","c":"Prior's flat, bright handling works well with a child: the red dress with its rows of dots, the ringlets and the pink stockings make a pattern as pleasing as a quilt, and the kicking foot gives the stiff composition a moment of life. The face is simple, almost doll-like, and the space is flattened, but the picture has the vivid directness that the best folk portraiture offers."},"nga:59911":{"d":"Two young girls are shown together against a simple dark ground. The girl at the left sits with a lapful of colorful flowers; she wears a coral pink dress, jewelry and a bow in her curly hair. The girl at the right stands holding a toy lamb on wheels, in a yellow dress with black polka dots and red ribbon accents. Both have soft features and blushing cheeks. A woven basket full of flowers stands between them on the ground. The canvas, about 90 by 102 centimeters, is dated 1854.","i":"Flowers and a toy lamb are the language of childhood innocence in the 1850s: flowers can suggest freshness and brevity, and a lamb gentleness and, in a Christian reading, the Lamb of God. Such props told viewers that the girls were sweet, well-behaved and loved. The title gives only the surname, Burnish, and nothing in the picture says how old the sisters were.","c":"Prior's color is the pleasure: coral pink against yellow with black dots, a basket of flowers between, all set against a dark ground that makes the colors ring. The two figures are stiffly posed and the sisters' faces nearly identical, but the formality is part of the charm, and the double portrait has a clear, bold pattern. It is a typical, attractive example of mid-century American folk portraiture."},"nga:56736":{"d":"A young boy stands in a white dress trimmed in blue and tied boots, with rosy cheeks and short blond curls. In one hand he holds a book and in the other a straw hat; a red bead necklace circles his neck, and a cap and a walking stick with tassels lie beside him. A red curtain hangs at the top, and trees and a building appear at the left. The canvas, about 77 by 61 centimeters, is dated broadly to about 1846 to 1873.","i":"The book, the hat and the stick are props that tell of schooling, of outdoors and of walking out, and a red bead necklace, a common keepsake, adds a note of ornament. The date range, 1846 to 1873, nearly the whole of Prior's later career, shows how hard it is to date the work of itinerant painters, who repeated their formulas for decades. Such a portrait was a family record of a child in his best dress.","c":"Prior's formula is at its most complete here: curtain, landscape, props and a child posed full-length, all handled in a bright, flat manner. The attraction is clarity and a pleasing pattern of whites, blues and reds; the cost is that the child becomes a type and the setting a stage set. That is just what such pictures were meant to be, and their directness still appeals."},"met:12646":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mary Cadwalader Rawle is the subject of this small oil portrait, about 31 by 27 centimeters, painted in 1868. The size is modest, a head-and-shoulders likeness suited to a private room, and the museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait. The names Rawle and Cadwalader are both associated with Philadelphia's old families, so the sitter probably belonged to that world.","i":"By the late 1860s the carte-de-visite photograph had cheapened the likeness, so a painted portrait, even a small one, was a mark of means or affection. William Oliver Stone was an American portraitist of the 1860s and 1870s working in the polished, fashionable manner of the period, and a small likeness of this kind would have been made for a family or a close friend.","c":"A small portrait of this kind depends on a good likeness and a refined touch, and Stone's work belongs to the polite, competent tradition of mid-century American portraiture that the camera was beginning to overtake. Without a vivid trait or an unusual pose to point to, the picture is best valued as a record of a named sitter, handled with taste but little risk."},"met:437239":{"d":"Children of the Heathcote family are shown in this large horizontal group portrait, about 140 by 172 centimeters; the museum's keywords record a boy, a girl and a portrait, and the title names them as the grandchildren of Sir William Heathcote, third baronet. The date is not recorded. A canvas of this size was made for a country house, where a group of the heirs of a baronet's family would hang in a principal room.","i":"William Owen, a Royal Academician who became portrait painter to the Prince Regent, was a favorite of the English gentry, whose children he painted in the easy, affectionate poses made fashionable by Reynolds and Lawrence. A group of grandchildren, rather than children, honors the head of the family, the baronet at the center of the lineage. For a family of landed rank, such a picture said that the line was secure and the next generations flourishing.","c":"Owen was a skilled follower of Reynolds and Hoppner, with a fluent, glowing manner, and a group of children gave him scope for charm. The danger of the type is sweetness and a sameness of faces, and Owen, never as inventive as Lawrence, sometimes falls into it. At its best, in the grouping and in the fresh complexions, the picture is a pleasing record of Regency childhood."},"met:11674":{"d":"A girl, the painter's daughter, perhaps named Anne, is the subject of this small oil portrait, about 36 by 30 centimeters, painted around 1837 to 1838. The size is that of a cabinet picture, and the likeness is a private one, made in the painter's own household. The museum's keywords record a girl and a portrait. A child's portrait by a father allows an informality that commissioned work often lacks.","i":"William Page, born in Albany and a pupil of Samuel F. B. Morse, became a leading American portraitist of the 1840s and later a restless experimenter with the color methods of Titian and the Venetians. His family pictures date from the early years of his career, before the theories and the controversies of his later years. The identification of the child is tentative, and the museum's possibly signals that the evidence is not conclusive.","c":"Page's early family pictures are fresh and plainly direct, free of the heavy, glowing flesh of his later Titian-inspired manner. A small portrait of a child needs a quick eye and a light hand, and a father painting his own daughter has both advantages and tenderness. The picture's modest size and private nature are its charm; its limit is that it shows Page before he became the painter he wished to be."},"cma:136610":{"d":"A man named in the title as Colonel William Leete Stone is the subject of this vertical oil portrait, about 91 by 74 centimeters, painted in 1839. The size is close to the kit-cat format of 36 by 28 inches, a half-length image that normally shows the head, torso and a hand, and it is the usual size of a formal portrait for a public man. The title's Colonel is a rank, probably militia, of a kind common among civic leaders in the early republic.","i":"The name is that of a New York newspaper editor and writer, the probable sitter, who edited the Commercial Advertiser; in 1836 he investigated Maria Monk's sensational Awful Disclosures about a Montreal convent and exposed it as a fraud. In 1839 William Page was a young portraitist rising in New York, and portraits of prominent men like this were part of his rise.","c":"Page in 1839 was still working within the plain, sturdy portrait manner of his teacher Morse, and a half-length of a public man is a straightforward test of it: firm drawing, serious color, and a face recorded without much flattery. The later, Titian-inspired Page, with glowing flesh, is not yet here. It is a competent, earnest portrait, and its interest is partly that of a painter's apprenticeship."},"met:11675":{"d":"A girl, the painter's daughter, probably named Mary, is shown in this small oil portrait, about 36 by 30 centimeters, painted around 1840. The museum's keywords record a girl, a portrait and a profile, so she is seen from the side. A profile view is unusual in American portraits of the period and recalls the profile portraits of Renaissance Italy, an art that Page, who later lived in Italy, came to admire.","i":"Page's small portraits of his daughters were made in the family's household, probably as studies or keepsakes, and the profile pose, a view that reduces a face to a contour, suggests a painter testing how much form and character he could convey with the fewest means. The museum's tentative identification as Mary reflects the limits of the family record.","c":"A profile of a child is a bold thing to try: the contour must be right, and the single eye and cheek carry the whole likeness. Page's pictures of his daughters show a painter more experimental and less formal than in his commissions, and the unusual view gives the work a quiet strangeness. It remains small and tentative, a promising sketch of an approach that his later work would develop."},"nga:166467":{"d":"Two hunters in a marshy landscape look at a wide-eyed dog that holds a dead duck in its mouth. One man kneels in profile, a hand held out toward the dog, in a beige jacket trimmed with red and knee-high brown boots, his rifle resting by its barrel on the ground; four dead ducks lie by his foot. The second man, clean-shaven in a red brimless cap, stands behind, loading or cleaning his rifle with a rod. Tall grasses and cattails surround them, water stretches to a high horizon, and the sky is streaked pink and gray.","i":"Duck shooting in the marshes around New York and New Jersey was a popular sport of the 1850s, and Ranney, who lived in New Jersey, made a specialty of hunting scenes, a subject popular in prints too. The title names the key moment: the dog retrieving the bird. Sporting pictures like this told an urban audience of an outdoor world of skill, companionship and manly leisure, with the faithful dog as an emblem of loyalty.","c":"Ranney's best pictures have a lively, observed truth: the dog's rapt stare, the hunters' casual attitudes, the glint on the hobnails and the damp air of the marsh are given with an eye for detail that no studio could invent. The composition is a touch crowded and the figures a little stagey, but the human and canine drama is clear and engaging. It stands among the better American sporting pictures of its decade."},"met:10167":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this tiny enamel miniature, only about 4 by 3.5 centimeters, dated 1785. The museum's keywords note a cap, a veil, lace and a fichu, the soft head-covering and scarf of an English woman of the 1780s. Enamel, colored glass fused onto a copper plate in a kiln, is a harder and more durable medium than watercolor on ivory, and its colors do not fade. The sitter shares the painter's surname, which suggests a family connection.","i":"William Russell Birch, born in England in 1755, was a leading enameller of his time before he emigrated to Philadelphia in 1794, where he became better known for his engraved views of the city and of country houses. An enamel miniature was a luxury keepsake meant to last, and the fired color keeps its brilliance for centuries. A portrait of a relative, made at the height of his English career, shows him applying his full skill to a private subject.","c":"At four centimeters across, an enamel portrait demands extraordinary control, since the colors are applied and fired in stages and any fault is hard to correct, so a convincing face in cap and veil is a technical achievement. Birch's enamels are admired for their precision and smooth surface, and the price of that polish is a certain coolness. As a small jewel of technique and a family document it is excellent."},"met:15004":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. William Bingham, born Anne Willing, is the subject of this enamel miniature, about 11 by 8.5 centimeters, dated 1795. That is large for an enamel, and the medium, colored glass fused onto copper in a kiln, makes it a luxury object whose colors will not fade. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait, so the picture is best described by its type: a formal likeness of a leading lady of Philadelphia society.","i":"Anne Willing Bingham was the wife of the Pennsylvania merchant and senator William Bingham and the hostess of the most admired salon in the capital in the 1790s, when Philadelphia was the seat of the federal government. A portrait of her in enamel, by an English artist who had arrived in the city only a year before, linked a newcomer's skill to the most fashionable sitter in town.","c":"Enamel gives Anne Bingham's likeness a hard, jewel-like clarity, and Birch's control of the medium shows in the smooth flesh and the precision of detail. The cost is a certain stiffness, as the fired surface leaves little room for softness or spontaneity, and the sitter's celebrated charm is better caught in the painted portraits by Stuart. As a luxury object and a technical performance it is impressive."},"met:15005":{"d":"A man is the subject of this tiny enamel miniature, about 3.7 by 3.2 centimeters, painted around 1795. The title records only that he was a gentleman. Enamel, colored glass fired onto a copper plate, gives the work a glassy brilliance and a permanence that watercolor on ivory cannot match, and at this size it was made to be held, worn or set in a case. The sitter's name has not been preserved.","i":"Birch had worked in London as an enameller before emigrating, and in Philadelphia in the mid-1790s he found a market among the merchants and officials of the capital who wanted a likeness that would last. An enamel was costlier than a miniature on ivory, which marked the sitter's means as well as his face. The portrait's anonymity is a reminder of how many such small pieces have lost their names.","c":"The technical challenge of enamel is part of the interest, since a convincing man's face at less than four centimeters demands great control, and Birch had it. The result is likely to be precise, bright and slightly cool, a face seen through a glassy surface. Without a name to give the picture a story, it is judged as craft, and as craft it is excellent."},"met:15007":{"d":"A tree stump in a landscape is the subject of this small enamel on copper, about 7.6 by 6.4 centimeters, dated 1815; the museum's keywords are tree and landscape painting. The title says it is after Ruisdael, so it is a copy of a Dutch landscape by Jacob van Ruisdael, translated into colored glass fused onto the copper in a kiln. As a type it is a collector's reduction: a famous composition shrunk into a durable, jewel-like object.","i":"Jacob van Ruisdael, the great Dutch landscape painter of the seventeenth century, was admired by collectors of the early nineteenth, and few of his paintings were then in America, so copies, prints and enamels spread knowledge of his work. Birch, who was well known for copying paintings in enamel, found a market among American collectors who wanted taste in a small form. Copying a stump was also a way to show off his technical skill.","c":"A copy is judged by what it keeps of the original, and an enamel, being fired and unforgiving, has to simplify the subtle tones of the Dutch painting. Birch's version keeps the plain, rugged subject, and the glassy surface gives the dark tones a strange luster. It is a skilled but secondhand piece, interesting chiefly as evidence of how a taste for old masters reached early America."},"met:10165":{"d":"A boy named in the title as Brock Livingston Delaplaine is the subject of this small enamel miniature, about 7.6 by 6.4 centimeters, dated 1817. The museum's keywords record a boy and a portrait. Enamel, colored glass fused onto copper, gave a child's likeness a permanence that a watercolor could not, and at this size it was made as a keepsake to be set in a frame or case.","i":"Birch, then about sixty-two and living near Philadelphia, was by 1817 better known for his city views than his portraits, but he still took commissions in enamel from friends and from families who valued permanence. The boy's given names, Brock and Livingston, look like family names, the kind a parent chose to record a lineage. A child's enamel was a lasting memorial, kept long after the boy had grown.","c":"A child's face in enamel is a test: the glassy medium prefers firm outlines, while children's faces are soft. Birch is more at home with precise, adult features, and a boy's portrait tends to be neat rather than tender. It is a polished, durable memorial, and the date and the full name make it a good small document, if not a very warm picture."},"met:10164":{"d":"Bishop William White is the subject of this very small enamel portrait, only about 3.3 by 2.6 centimeters, painted around 1830, when the sitter was in his early eighties. The tiny size is that of a jewel or a keepsake in a locket, and the medium, colored glass fused onto copper, preserves its color indefinitely. A likeness of a clergyman of his age is a portrait of dignity and reverence.","i":"William White (1748 to 1836) was the first Episcopal bishop of Pennsylvania and a major figure in organizing the American Episcopal Church after the Revolution; he also served as chaplain to the Continental Congress and later to the Senate. Philadelphians revered him as a link to the founding generation, and a small enamel of him, probably after a painted portrait, was a devotional and civic keepsake.","c":"In so small a space the old bishop's face must be reduced to its essentials, and Birch, in his seventies, manages a dignified, legible likeness with the exactness his craft demands. The small scale concentrates the picture's meaning on the head, and the fired surface lends the image a calm that suits a clergyman. It is a miniature icon of a revered man, more a tribute than a probing portrait."},"met:10166":{"d":"Daniel Webster, the senator and orator, is the subject of this very small enamel portrait, only about 3.3 by 2.7 centimeters, dated after 1833, which suggests that Birch made it from an existing likeness of that year or later, not from a sitting. The museum's keywords record Webster and a portrait. At this size it is a collector's keepsake, a statesman's face on a fired glass surface.","i":"Webster was famous in the 1830s as a defender of the Union and the Constitution, the great speaker of Massachusetts in the Senate, and Americans collected his likeness the way later generations would collect photographs of celebrities. Small enamel versions made his image portable and durable, and by turning out such likenesses an aged Birch could supply a market for national heroes.","c":"A portrait made from another portrait is at one remove from life, and Birch's Webster has the neat, generalized finish of a copy. Webster's craggy brow and dark eyes, which make his portraits memorable, are hard to translate at this scale, and enamel cannot suggest his famous presence. As a tiny relic of a national reputation it is interesting, but it is more souvenir than portrait."},"met:15006":{"d":"A waterfall landscape with trees and a bird is the subject of this small enamel on copper, about 5 by 5 centimeters, undated. The title names Principa Falls, and the museum's keywords add a tree, a landscape and a bird. Enamel, colored glass fused onto copper in a kiln, gives the little scene a glossy, jewel-like brilliance, and the picture was made as a collector's cabinet piece or a decorative keepsake.","i":"Birch is remembered for his engraved views of Philadelphia and for American country seats, so a waterfall landscape in enamel is a reminder that he also copied and composed landscapes for private buyers. Enamel landscapes were collectors' curiosities in the early nineteenth century, prized as much for the difficulty of the technique as for the scene, and they often reproduced famous paintings or prints of picturesque places.","c":"Landscape suits enamel less well than the face: the glass fires with bright, saturated colors that can make foliage look hard and brilliant. A waterfall gives Birch a chance to show how clear whites and blues survive the kiln. The result is a small, bright, curious object, skilled and pretty, but a long way from the feeling for light that Ruisdael or Cole would give the same subject."},"met:14306":{"d":"Daniel Webster is the subject of this minute enamel portrait, only about 2.7 by 2 centimeters, painted around 1833. It is one of the smallest pieces in the group, a size that fits a ring or a brooch, and the medium, colored glass fused onto copper, gives it a bright, permanent surface. The museum's keywords record Webster and a portrait.","i":"In 1833 Webster was at the height of his fame, a leading senator who had defended the Union in the nullification debates, and his likeness was in demand. A tiny enamel portrait of this size would be worn or kept by an admirer, an emblem of loyalty to the statesman as much as a record of his face. Birch, at nearly eighty, found in such small commissions a way to keep his old skill working.","c":"The portrait is an exercise in compression: the face reduced to a tiny oval, the brow and eyes carried by a few touches. The result is more emblem than likeness, and the fired glass gives it a luminous calm. As technical wit it is impressive; as a portrait it can only suggest the great head and the deep voice of its sitter."},"met:15481":{"d":"A man is the subject of this tiny miniature, only about 3.7 by 2.5 centimeters, painted around 1785 in watercolor on ivory. The title records only that he was a gentleman. In America in the 1780s a small likeness of this kind, set in a gold case or locket, was a prized possession, and Verstille, a painter of the Revolutionary generation, was among the few who could make one.","i":"Verstille belongs to the first generation of American miniaturists, working in the years just after independence, when officers and officials wanted likenesses to give to families. His small ivories, typically delicate in brushwork, show the English style adapted to a new republic. The sitter's name is lost, but his dress, hair and bearing place him in the world of the 1780s.","c":"Verstille's miniatures have a small, firm precision, and in early examples like this the faces are drawn plainly and softly colored, with a naive sweetness rather than the brilliance of the later English school. The limits are those of the American provincial tradition, but the small scale and the plain honesty of the faces give the pictures their appeal. It is valued for what it keeps of a vanished world."},"met:11839":{"d":"A man named in the title as George Henry Remsen is the subject of this small miniature, about 4.8 by 3.7 centimeters, painted around 1790 in watercolor on ivory. The title gives his full name, which allows the portrait to be traced to a family. Painted in the first decade after independence, it is a keepsake in the small, portable format fashionable in the new republic.","i":"Remsen is a Dutch-American name long associated with New York, and portraits of this kind recorded the merchants and professional men of the city after the Revolution. Verstille was working in the years when the new nation's middle class took up the habit of having likenesses made. The picture stands as one of a small body of American miniatures of the 1790s.","c":"At this scale a miniaturist gets only a few strokes to find a face, and Verstille's strength is a clear, quiet drawing; his faces are a little simplified, his colors pale, and the effect is sober rather than brilliant. Beside the polished English miniatures of Cosway and Smart, the picture is plain, but plainness is its appeal: a New Yorker of 1790 seen without ornament."},"met:13076":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Ethan Stone, born Abigail Maria Storrs, is the subject of this small miniature, about 5.6 by 4.4 centimeters, painted around 1795 in watercolor on ivory. The museum's keywords record only a woman and a portrait. The title gives her married and her maiden names, and the Storrs name links her to the portrait of Clarissa Storrs in the same collection, so these ivories seem to belong to one family circle.","i":"A family's set of miniatures was a way to keep the household together in the days before photography, and several of Verstille's sitters share the names Storrs and Stone, which suggests that he worked for a group of related families in the 1790s. A miniature of a married woman marked a marriage as well as a face and could be exchanged between sisters, mothers and daughters. The sitter's story outside these names is not told in the picture.","c":"Verstille's women are drawn with quiet care, and a miniature like this one has the modest, close-up attraction of a family keepsake: a clear face, soft color and simple shapes. It lacks the glamour of the best English ivories, and it does not try for it. The interest lies in the sense of a real person in a real household, seen by a painter who knew how to be plain."},"met:13077":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Clarissa Storrs is the subject of this small miniature, about 5.7 by 4.3 centimeters, painted around 1795 in watercolor on ivory. The title gives only her own name, with no married name, so she was probably unmarried at the time. It matches the portrait of Mrs. Ethan Stone, born Abigail Maria Storrs, in size and date, and the two seem to be sisters or relatives painted in the same period.","i":"Unmarried women's miniatures were commonly made as love tokens or gifts, so Clarissa's portrait could have been a gift for a suitor or a family keepsake made before a marriage. Verstille, who painted in the years after the Revolution, offered such families an affordable, portable likeness. The Storrs name recurs across the museum's group of his work, which shows a close circle of sitters.","c":"Plain and graceful, the miniature of Clarissa shows the strengths and limits of Verstille's manner: delicate drawing, pale, harmonious color and a calm, direct presentation. It makes no attempt to dazzle. Beside her relative's portrait it gives a pleasing impression of two women painted with equal care, and as a small document of an early American family it is more engaging than its modest scale suggests."},"met:15220":{"d":"A man named in the title as Frederick Kuhl is the subject of this very small miniature, about 4 by 3 centimeters, painted around 1795 in watercolor on ivory. The title gives his full name, and the surname suggests a family of German descent, one of the communities that settled in Pennsylvania, New York and the mid-Atlantic. The size is that of a locket or pendant case.","i":"Verstille's sitters of the 1790s include merchants, officers and neighbors from many towns, and a name like Kuhl records how varied the new republic's population was. A man's miniature of this date might mark a marriage, a long absence or a death, and it would have been kept in a case, not displayed. The sitter's story, other than his name, is not told in the picture.","c":"The best of Verstille's small male portraits are direct and unfussy, with the fine, delicate finish of the period, and a sitter such as Kuhl benefits from that candor. At 4 centimeters the picture reduces everything to the face, which is drawn simply and without flattery. It is a modest, honest piece of the first generation of American miniature painting."},"met:15221":{"d":"A man is the subject of this tiny miniature, about 4 by 3 centimeters, painted around 1795 in watercolor on ivory. The title records only that he was a gentleman. It has the same size and date as the miniature of Frederick Kuhl and comes from the same group of Verstille's work, though the title does not say that they are related. The pale ivory glows through the thin color to give the flesh its warmth.","i":"A gentleman in the 1790s was a man of independent means or a profession, and a miniature gave him a portable likeness to leave with family or friends. His name is lost, and so are the circumstances: a marriage, a journey, a public office. What remains is evidence of a society in which the middling and upper ranks were beginning to take likenesses as a normal part of life.","c":"In small pictures like this one, Verstille's quiet drawing and pale color are enough, and attention goes straight to the eyes. The sitter has dignity without drama. It is not an exciting picture, but it is a respectable one, typical of the provincial excellence of American miniature painting around 1795."},"met:15222":{"d":"A man named in the title as Lieutenant Colonel Elias Parker is the subject of this very small miniature, about 3.6 by 3 centimeters, painted around 1795 in watercolor on ivory. The rank in the title is a military one, and for a painter of the Revolutionary generation, an officer of that era was a natural sitter. The tiny size is that of a pocket case or locket.","i":"Lieutenant colonels of the 1790s were mostly veterans of the Revolution or officers of the state militias, and a miniature was a favorite way for them to leave a likeness with family. The rank was a mark of civic standing in the early republic, and it often stayed with a man for life. The title does not say whether Parker's rank was Continental or militia.","c":"A soldier's miniature has a different pressure from a lady's: the face must hold authority in a space the size of a coin. Verstille is more at home with plain candor than with swagger, and the result is likely to be a sober, sturdy likeness with the officer's rank carried by his dress rather than his bearing. As a small relic of the post-Revolution army, it has an appeal beyond its scale."},"met:15223":{"d":"A man is the subject of this small miniature, about 5.8 by 4.6 centimeters, painted around 1795 in watercolor on ivory. The title records only that he was a gentleman. It is among the larger of Verstille's miniatures in this group, big enough to give the head and shoulders a little more room, and the thin, transparent color lets the ivory's glow show through the flesh.","i":"In the 1790s a miniature of this size was a considered purchase: more costly than a pocket-size piece, it might be framed for a wall or desk, a token of the sitter's standing. The gentleman's name has been lost, but the picture's date places him among the citizens of the young republic who took up the portrait miniature as a mark of status and sentiment.","c":"At this size Verstille has room to model the head, and a viewer can see the quiet draftsmanship that is his chief gift. The color is pale and the style plain, with the soft charm of provincial work. It makes no great claim, and its sincerity is the point: a man of the 1790s, looking out with the unforced gravity of a person who did not often sit for his portrait."},"met:15225":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Joseph White, born Elizabeth Stone, is the subject of this small miniature, about 5.6 by 4.2 centimeters, painted around 1802 in watercolor on ivory. The title gives her married and her maiden names, the Stone name linking her to the other Stone and Storrs portraits in the same collection. Verstille painted it near the end of his life, since he died in 1803.","i":"A married woman's miniature near 1800 marked a new household, and the repeated Stone and Storrs names in Verstille's work suggest that he served a circle of intermarried families. The painter's own life was nearly over, so such a late work records both a sitter and the last phase of an artist's career.","c":"Late Verstille is still the plain, careful painter of the early work: soft, pale color, simple drawing and a quiet calm, with little change in manner across twenty years. A woman painted in this way has a gentle, unadorned dignity. It is not a showy miniature, but it has the modesty of the best of the American provincial tradition."},"met:15224":{"d":"A woman named in the title as Mrs. Anstis Stone is the subject of this small miniature, about 4.8 by 4 centimeters, painted around 1802 in watercolor on ivory; the museum's keywords note a cap, a fichu and lace. The cap and the fichu, a light scarf worn around the neck and shoulders, are the dress of a respectable married woman of the period, and the lace trim shows care for fine detail.","i":"A cap and a fichu mark a matron of the early republic, dressed modestly and tastefully. The Stone name connects her with others in the same group, and her portrait was probably made for her family, as a record of the woman at the center of a household. Anstis is an unusual given name or family name, a trace of the family's own history.","c":"Lace, cap and fichu give a miniaturist the pleasure of fine detail, and Verstille's careful touch shows in the soft folds and the pale glow of the face. The expression is calm, and the face is set with quiet clarity. It is a gentle, competent portrait of a matron, valuable as a record of provincial dress and taste."},"nga:50258":{"d":"A man is seated at full length, his body turned slightly right but his gaze fixed on the viewer, one hand on his knee and the other holding a sword upright. He has a serious expression, a square jaw, high cheekbones and neatly styled dark hair. He wears a blue coat with gold buttons, a yellow waistcoat with frilled white cuffs, beige breeches, white stockings and black shoes with rectangular buckles. A black tricorn hat with gold trim rests on a table, and in the background a window or picture shows lines of soldiers, horses and trees under a dark blue sky.","i":"The title gives the sitter his rank, captain, probably in the local militia, and the sword, the tricorn and the scene of soldiers behind him make the point. Around 1780, in the middle of the American Revolution, military standing was a source of pride in New England communities. The portrait is a pendant to that of his wife, Mrs. Samuel Chandler.","c":"Chandler's flat, brightly lit manner is on full view: the face is a solid mask, but the coat, waistcoat and buckles are described with fond precision. The pose, hand on knee and sword upright, is a stiff formula, yet that stiffness is exactly what gives the portrait its provincial authority. Charming and forceful rather than refined, and a good example of what a talented self-taught painter could do."},"nga:50259":{"d":"The museum offers no written description of this portrait beyond the sitter's identity, given as Anna Paine Chandler Whitney, presumably her name after a later marriage. The canvas, about 139 by 122 centimeters, matches in size the portrait of her husband, Captain Samuel Chandler, and both were painted around 1780 by Winthrop Chandler, so the two were evidently conceived as a pair. Her pose, dress and setting are not recorded.","i":"Pendant portraits of husband and wife were a standard commission in provincial New England, hung together in the best room of the house as a statement of family standing. Winthrop Chandler, a largely self-taught painter from Woodstock, Connecticut, worked in the small towns of Connecticut and Massachusetts, and this pair dates from the middle of the Revolutionary War.","c":"Chandler is one of the most distinctive of American provincial portraitists: hard-edged, flatly lit, fascinated by pattern and costume, with an almost folk-art intensity. If this pendant follows the manner of its mate, expect a firm, rather mask-like face and loving detail in the clothes. The result lacks the polish of Copley but has a frankness and decorative force that make these portraits memorable."},"nga:12139":{"d":"A boy is shown from the shoulders up in profile facing left, against a lapis-blue ground. He stares steadily forward with brown eyes, rosy cheeks and closed pink lips, his chin pulled slightly down and back, his brown hair cut in a straight diagonal fringe across the forehead to the nape. He wears a brimless black cap with a high rounded crown and a high-necked black garment embellished with gold, black and pearls, the collar edged in white. The small tempera panel measures about 27 by 21 centimeters.","i":"The boy is Francesco II Gonzaga (1466-1519), who became the fourth marquis of Mantua in 1484 and later married Isabella d'Este, the great collector and patron, in 1490; he was a soldier who commanded Venetian forces at the Battle of Fornovo in 1495. The profile view, derived from ancient coins and Renaissance medals, was the standard princely portrait in northern Italy, stressing dynastic dignity. Painted when he was a child, it records the heir to Mantua before he took up his role.","c":"The profile is a hard format to make live, since the face must express character through contour alone, and Baldassare d'Este manages it with a firm, clear line, the boy's steady gaze and the sharply designed cap. The rich blue ground sets off the head. The picture has the crisp elegance of Ferrarese court art, and the child's seriousness already suggests the future ruler. It is charming and exact, if less searching than Flemish portraits."},"met:436826":{"d":"A small winter scene painted in oil on a wooden panel, only about 36 by 43 centimetres. Snow covers the countryside and the water has frozen hard enough for people to skate, so a wide, wintry landscape is combined with figures at leisure on the ice. The panel support and modest size make this a cabinet picture, meant to be looked at closely, in the fine-finished manner that Dutch painters had cultivated since the seventeenth century.","i":"Barend Cornelis Koekkoek belonged to the Dutch Romantic generation, who looked back to the seventeenth-century landscapists and treated their winter scenes with skaters as part of a national heritage. Skating on frozen canals and lakes was already a familiar image of Dutch life. The picture is usually read both as a charming scene of leisure and as a deliberate echo of the Golden Age. Koekkoek moved to Cleves, in Germany, in 1834, the year after this picture.","c":"Koekkoek's gift was for finish, and on a panel this small, precise detail and carefully placed figures reward close looking. The limit is that the picture is an elegant revival: it adds polish and a little sentiment to an old Dutch type without the sharper observation of the seventeenth-century originals it recalls. Among his own work it is typical rather than surprising, but it is a well-made example."},"met:436827":{"d":"A river landscape painted at the end of the day, with the Rhine as its subject and the light of sunset as its theme. At about 82 by 108 centimetres it is a medium-sized canvas. It belongs to the Romantic tradition of the Rhine view, in which a famous river, evening light and a sense of distance combine to give a mood as much as a place, and where the sky and its reflected glow carry much of the effect.","i":"From the early nineteenth century the Rhine was a favourite Romantic subject, linked with legend, ruined castles and the growing travel trade, and artists in the Rhineland supplied views to collectors. Koekkoek settled at Cleves, near the lower Rhine, in 1834. A sunset adds a further layer, usually read as the end of day and an elegiac, reflective mood. Catharine Lorillard Wolfe of New York, who collected contemporary European art, bequeathed the picture to the Metropolitan Museum in 1887.","c":"A sunset asks a painter to build glow out of carefully graded tones, and it tests as much as it flatters. Koekkoek was strongest at crisp observation and tidy construction, so his Rhine views are better admired for polish than for daring colour. The mood is sincere but follows the Romantic script, and the picture comes across as well made rather than personal. It shows the discipline of his winter scenes in a more expansive form."},"met:435770":{"d":"John the Baptist preaches to a crowd of men and women in an open landscape, on a wide panel about 55 by 75 centimetres. Breenbergh, who had spent about ten years in Rome, usually set biblical scenes among Italianate hills, trees and ruins, with small figures in broad scenery and warm, even light across the whole. The preacher, the crowd and the landscape share the space, so the picture is as much a landscape with a story as a figure subject.","i":"The subject comes from the Gospels (Matthew 3, Mark 1, Luke 3): John preached repentance in the wilderness and baptised those who came to him, announcing the one who would follow. For Dutch Protestants an open-air sermon to a mixed crowd may also have had a modern ring, recalling the field preaching of the Reformed church in the previous century. Rembrandt painted the subject at about the same date, and the theme clearly interested Amsterdam painters of the 1630s.","c":"Breenbergh's gifts are clear light, brisk figure drawing and a convincing sense of open air, and a panel of this kind shows them to advantage. The crowd is varied and neatly grouped, though its members are stock types, and the preacher lacks the intensity that Rembrandt gave the same subject. Breenbergh remains a poet of the sunlit Italianate landscape rather than a painter of drama, and the pleasure here is in the setting."},"met:435820":{"d":"A portrait of a woman on an oak panel, about 45 by 36 centimetres, painted in 1557. A coat of arms in the picture identifies her as a member of the Slosgin family of Cologne, so the image presents both a person and a lineage. It was painted by Barthel Bruyn the Younger, son and pupil of the city's leading portraitist, who carried on the family workshop and its sober, truthful style.","i":"Coats of arms in Cologne portraits declare descent and civic standing, and the arms here tell later viewers whose family the sitter belonged to, even though no personal name is recorded for her. Sixteenth-century portraits of women stressed dress, headwear and composure as signs of virtue and wealth. The panel is usually read as a record kept within the family as a statement of its place in the city.","c":"Bruyn the Younger continues his father's plain method, and the result is an honest, restrained likeness. It lacks the sharper characterisation of the best German portraiture of the period, and it shows less of the Renaissance polish that portraitists like Antonis Mor were offering in the Netherlands in the 1550s, but the heraldic display gives it documentary value. It is a respectable workshop product rather than an individual statement."},"nga:12118":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, Saint Catherine of Alexandria rests a book and a palm frond against a spiked wheel, all against a gold ground in an arched panel whose top has been cut across above her halo. Her garnet-red robe, patterned with delicate diamond shapes, is fastened by a round gold brooch lettered with her name, over a sky-blue garment with a marine-blue collar. Her wavy blond hair is parted in the middle, and a gold crown and halo merge into the shimmering background, which is cracked in places. She glances up and aside, lips closed.","i":"Catherine, a legendary princess of Alexandria, is identified by her attributes: the spiked wheel on which she was sentenced to die, which, in the story, shattered at her touch; the palm of martyrdom; the book of the learning with which she is said to have defeated pagan philosophers; and the crown of her royal birth. As a single saint on a gold ground, the panel probably formed part of a larger altarpiece, with other saints flanking a central Madonna.","c":"Bulgarini works within the Sienese tradition descending from Duccio, and this half-length shows its strengths: elegant outline, a delicately patterned robe, and a gentle, sidelong glance that lends the saint human warmth against the unearthly gold. He is less original than Simone Martini or the Lorenzetti brothers, and the forms are rather flat, but the quiet, refined charm and the rich surface are appealing."},"nga:170":{"d":"A small panel, about 56 by 46 centimeters, in oil on poplar, shows the Virgin with the Christ Child, the museum's keywords naming Mary, the Child and the subject. In north Italian devotional pictures of about 1490 the Virgin is usually shown at half-length, often behind a parapet or against a landscape, with the Child held close in a quiet, intimate group. The size suits a picture for private prayer, to be seen at close range in a house or chapel.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the commonest picture in a Renaissance household, a focus for prayer and a sign of the family's piety, and its meaning rests on the Virgin's tenderness and on the Child as the Savior, often shown blessing or playing. Bartolomeo Montagna, who settled in Vicenza and absorbed the example of Venice and Giovanni Bellini, made such pictures a specialty, giving them Bellini's dignity and a sturdier, more sculptural presence.","c":"Montagna is the sturdy, more severe cousin of Bellini: his figures are firmly modeled, with clear contour and a serious mood, and he lacks Bellini's lyric glow but has his own gravity. In a Madonna of about 1490 he is at his most agreeable: sober, solid and a little cool, with the Virgin's grave face and the compact group more memorable than any decorative flourish. It is north Italian devotion at its most restrained."},"met:437145":{"d":"A single saint on a small panel, about 50 by 38 centimetres, painted in oil on wood in the 1490s. The subject is Justina of Padua, a young martyr honoured as one of that city's patrons, presented as a devotional image rather than a narrative scene. Montagna, who worked mainly in Vicenza, painted such figures with a firm, clear drawing learned from Mantegna and the Paduan school. The panel probably formed part of an altarpiece or served as a private devotional image.","i":"According to tradition, Justina was a young woman of Padua martyred by the sword around the year 300 under the emperor Maximian, and her usual attributes are the martyr's palm and a sword or dagger. Her relics rest in the great church of Santa Giustina in Padua, and she was later also revered in Venice, where her feast day, 7 October, coincided with the victory at Lepanto in 1571. A saint like this was both a model of purity and constancy and a local protector.","c":"Montagna paints saints with a sculptural firmness, a cool clarity and a restrained sentiment that set him apart from the softer Bellini tradition. A single figure of a young saint suits that temperament, since it asks for dignity and a clean outline rather than drama. The risk is severity: his holy women can seem remote. As a type it is a dignified, finely made devotional image of the provincial Renaissance in the Veneto."},"met:437144":{"d":"The Virgin Mary is shown adoring her infant son, in an upright panel about 63 by 52 centimetres, painted on wood. The composition belongs to a type common in the Veneto around 1500, in which Mary kneels or leans over the Child and the viewer is invited to share her contemplation. Bartolomeo Montagna, who was based mainly in Vicenza, gave such devotional pictures a firm outline and a cool clarity, and a picture of this size was meant for close, private viewing.","i":"Madonnas adoring the Child show Mary contemplating the infant Christ with prayerful reverence, a pose that recognises him as God incarnate as well as her son. The type grew out of Nativity imagery and was a favourite of Venetian devotion in the decades around 1500. The presence of the Child at close quarters often carries a quiet allusion to the Passion, since the infant's body prefigures the sacrifice that will follow.","c":"Montagna blends Bellini's gentle Venetian devotion with a harder, more sculptural finish. His Madonnas are grave and dignified rather than tender, and a picture of this type is likely to share the dry elegance and cool colour for which he is known. He lacks Bellini's poetry of light, but the best of his Madonnas have a sober intensity and a craftsman's accuracy that reward attention."},"cma:119701":{"d":"A small portrait, about 39 by 29 centimetres, of a young man who wears a plaquette, a small relief medallion, on his hat as a badge of rank. The relief shows an allegory of fortune and virtue in the form of a winged woman and a child, and it reproduces an actual work by the Paduan sculptor Andrea Riccio. The museum suggests that the sitter may belong to the household of the Sforza, the ruling family of Milan, although his identity is not certain.","i":"Badges on hats were personal emblems in the early sixteenth century, chosen to announce a man's character, loyalties and cultivation. A plaquette after Riccio would have shown that the sitter shared the taste for small bronzes and moral allegory; fortune and virtue was a favourite theme of Renaissance thought, asking how far virtue can master chance. The picture presents him as a young nobleman of learning, and the badge works as a private motto displayed in public.","c":"Bartolomeo Veneto is a painter of precise costume and cool, exact likenesses, and the badge is a good example: he copies a tiny work of sculpture as carefully as a face. That attention gives the portrait documentary interest. His portraits often let the dress and accessories speak more than the face, and the manner has a slightly hard Lombard-Venetian dryness. It is a revealing, well-observed portrait rather than a deeply felt one."},"nga:398":{"d":"A man is shown from the chest up in this vertical portrait, turned to the viewer's left and looking down with brown eyes. He wears a soft black cap with a gold badge over the temple, shoulder-length dark hair, a white shirt with a wide square neckline and a striped black-and-gold doublet. A voluminous black coat lined with tawny fur and edged in gold is held shut by his ringed right hand. Behind him hangs a scarlet curtain, and at the right a small window or picture shows a landscape with a horseman riding away from a man with a lance.","i":"The fur, gold and fine cloth proclaim wealth and rank, and the curtain and ledge are devices borrowed from Venetian portraiture to give a sitter dignity. The small paper sealed with red wax may be a letter, a conventional sign of a man of business or learning, though it does not name him, and his identity is unknown. The tiny landscape scene of a rider fleeing a lancer is unexplained; it might be an allegory or a personal reference, but nothing in the record settles it.","c":"The picture's chief pleasure is the costume: fur, velvet, gold and linen are distinct in texture, and the red curtain sets off the dark coat. The man himself is quiet, even passive, with eyes lowered, so the portrait reads as an impression of wealth more than of character. The transfer from panel to canvas may have cost some of the original surface. It is handsome, if less penetrating than portraits by Titian or Lotto."},"met:439272":{"d":"A study of a boy in a blue jacket, drawn in pastel and chalk on blue paper, about 41 by 33 centimetres, and made in 1717. The blue paper supplies a cool middle tone against which warm flesh colours and light highlights stand out, and soft, powdery pastel lets skin and hair be blended without hard outlines. The sitter is unnamed, and the word study suggests a drawing made from a living model rather than a commissioned portrait.","i":"Benedetto Luti, a Florentine who worked mainly in Rome, was admired in his day for such pastels, which were collected as independent works of art. Heads of children and young people were a favourite subject, valued for their freshness and charm rather than for the sitter's identity. The Study of a Girl in Red, of the same year and similar size, is probably a companion, and the two are best seen as a pair of lively, informal character studies.","c":"Pastel is a medium made for the bloom of childhood, and Luti uses its softness to model the face with delicate gradations while the blue paper keeps the whole cool and quiet. The danger in such subjects is sweetness, and the best of Luti's heads avoid it by keeping to plain, unforced expressions. The limit is modesty: it is a charming head, not a deeply individual portrait."},"met:439273":{"d":"A study of a girl in a red garment, drawn in pastel and chalk on blue paper, about 42 by 34 centimetres, and made in 1717, the same year as the boy in blue. Red and blue make a natural contrast, with the blue paper giving a cool ground, and the soft medium lets the face and hair blend gradually from light to shadow. As with its companion, the sitter is unnamed, and the word study implies work done from a model.","i":"Studies of children in pastel were a fashionable kind of picture in early eighteenth-century Italy, bought by collectors who prized the charm of youthful faces. This girl and the boy in blue may have been made as a pair: the colours complement each other and the sizes are close. Luti trained in Florence and worked in Rome, where he also painted altarpieces and taught, but he was known for his pastel heads as well.","c":"A graceful head in which the cool paper, the red garment and the warm flesh give a clear colour scheme and the pastel's softness gives a gentle, lifelike surface. Such studies are at their best when they avoid coyness, and the informality of this kind of picture suits that. As a pendant to the boy, it completes a charming pair, and together they show the care that collectors lavished on small, intimate things."},"met:702752":{"d":"A small devotional scene painted in oil on copper, about 38 by 31 centimetres, showing Christ and the Woman of Samaria, the episode from the Gospel of John in which Jesus, resting at Jacob's well, asks a Samaritan woman for a drink. Copper gave painters a hard, smooth surface that takes fine detail and keeps its colour, and small pictures on it were made for private rooms and cabinets. The subject is a two-figure encounter.","i":"In John 4, Jesus, a Jew, speaks to a Samaritan woman, a stranger and an outsider, and offers her 'living water'; she recognises him as a prophet and goes to tell her town. The scene is read as a story of grace offered across social and religious boundaries, and its image of water relates to baptism. Late Baroque Rome, where Luti worked, favoured pictures of this kind, graceful and clear in their teaching, for devotion in the home.","c":"Luti belongs to the graceful late Baroque classicism of Rome, and his oils on copper have a polished, easy elegance. The subject, a quiet conversation, suits his gentle manner, and the small size and hard support reward fine finish. He does not match the dramatic power of earlier Baroque masters, and the result may be more pleasing than moving, but it is a refined cabinet picture."},"nga:12":{"d":"A long procession winds from a gateway in a distant walled city to a shed at the front, where the three Magi kneel before the seated Virgin and Child. Mary, in a lapis-blue mantle over a pink dress, holds a gold vessel while the blond Child raises two fingers in blessing; Joseph sits nearby, head in hand. The oldest Magus kisses the Child's feet, the second lifts his jewelled crown, and the youngest, with a dagger at his waist, holds a gold vessel. A donkey and a bull feed in the shed, with a gold sunburst above.","i":"The Gospel of Matthew (2:1–12) tells how wise men followed a star to Bethlehem and offered gold, frankincense and myrrh. Artists made the three Magi represent three ages of man, and sometimes the known continents, so that all humanity pays homage to Christ. The long cavalcade is typical of Sienese painting's love of pageant. Joseph's pensive pose is a human touch, and the shaped top of the panel suggests that it was the central part of an altarpiece.","c":"A richly decorative picture, in the Sienese tradition of rare colour and gold. The winding cavalcade, the walled city and the Magi's brocades are delightful, and the three ages of the kings are well differentiated. In the front, though, the figures are stiff and the space is shallow; Benvenuto lacks the Florentines' command of perspective and anatomy and follows the older Sienese ideal of grace. It is a beautiful object more than a convincing scene."},"nga:1140":{"d":"The Virgin and Child are accompanied by Saint Jerome and Saint Bernardino of Siena in a panel about 70 by 50 centimetres, painted in tempera. The two saints are presumably identified by their usual attributes, Jerome's cardinal's hat or book and Bernardino's plaque with the monogram of the name of Jesus. A picture of this size suited a private chapel or bedchamber, and it brings the saints into direct company with the Virgin so that a viewer who shared their names or devotions could pray in their company.","i":"A group of the Virgin with saints is called a sacra conversazione, a holy conversation, in which saints from different centuries share one space. Bernardino, a preacher who worked in Siena and died in 1444, was a recent hero there, and Jerome the scholar saint was a favourite of learned devotion. The choice of saints often reflects the patron's name or the order or confraternity for which the picture was made. Mary and the Child at the centre are the focus of prayer.","c":"A gracious, small Sienese devotional picture. Benvenuto's soft modelling and gold-tinged colour give it a sweet calm, and the arrangement of saints on either side is balanced. But the figures have little individuality, and the group lacks the depth and unity of space that Florentine and Venetian sacre conversazioni of the same years were developing. It is a quiet, pleasing work of the late Sienese school, more devout than daring."},"nga:41667":{"d":"Christ, in a rose-pink robe with blood running from the crown of thorns, carries a tall cross at the centre of a grassy landscape. Two gaunt men in grey jab and jeer at him, while about seven onlookers, one in gold armour, watch behind his shoulder. At the right the Virgin in a lapis-blue cloak, holding out her hands, stands with two other haloed mourners and a crowd, and two boys watch from the branches of trees. Horsemen gather at the left, and a town of towers rises on the high horizon.","i":"Christ's walk to Calvary is told in the Gospels, and John 19:17 says that he carried the cross himself. Late fifteenth-century devotion encouraged believers to follow Christ's steps in imagination, and pictures like this gather the cruelty of the soldiers and the grief of the mourners, presumably the Virgin, Saint John and Mary Magdalene, into one scene. The jeering men and the weeping women set violence and sorrow side by side.","c":"The picture divides into three clear groups, and the contrast between the jeering tormentors and the mourning group on the right is its best idea. The grimacing faces are expressive, and the colours are bright and legible. But the cross seems to weigh little on Christ's shoulder, and the pleasant grassy landscape and tidy figures soften the brutality. It is a story told with clarity rather than force."},"nga:41668":{"d":"Christ hangs on the cross against a deep landscape, head sunk on his shoulder, blood running from his hands, side and feet, with INRI on a red sign above. At the left a mounted man with a spear, presumably Longinus, stands near a group of women around the swooning Virgin, who is supported by Saint John and another woman, while three children watch from a hill. At the right three armoured men draw straws, with more soldiers on horseback behind. A green meadow, a lake and pale blue mountains lie beyond.","i":"John 19 describes the soldiers casting lots for Christ's robe, the sign Pilate had written and the spear that pierced his side. The Virgin's swoon, which is not in the Gospels, expresses her compassion, her suffering with her son, a theme dear to late medieval devotion. The children watching from a hill and the tears on every face of the mourners add a human, narrative note typical of Sienese painting.","c":"Benvenuto composes the scene with a high degree of clarity: the vertical of the cross against the sky divides two well-organised groups of mourners and soldiers. The fainting Virgin is the emotional centre, and the tears on every face are insistent. The result is moving, though the sweetness of colour and landscape keeps pathos within decorative bounds, and the mourners' poses are conventional. It is a beautifully legible small Crucifixion."},"nga:41669":{"d":"Christ, seen from behind, haloed and barefoot, carries a white banner with a red cross and steps onto the fallen door of Hell, which has crushed a wild-haired red demon. Inside a box-like opening in the rock, more than two dozen men and women look up at him. He takes the wrist of a long-bearded kneeling man in a fur garment with a hoe across his knee, presumably Adam, while a second kneeling man in a yellow robe grips his other hand. Cliffs and a sapphire sky surround the opening.","i":"After his death, according to the creed and the apocryphal Gospel of Nicodemus, Christ descended into the realm of the dead to free the righteous of the Old Testament. The scene is called the Harrowing of Hell. The white banner with a red cross is the sign of his victory over death, the demon crushed by the gate shows Satan's defeat, and the kneeling bearded man with a hoe is presumably Adam, the first to be rescued. The image gave comfort by promising release from death.","c":"The most unusual of the group: the crowd of souls crammed into a box-like opening in a boulder looks like a stage trap-door, and the crushed demon is almost comic. That oddity is part of its charm, and the upturned faces and Christ's outstretched hands give a clear, urgent focus. The geometry is awkward and the figures are repetitive, but the inventive setting makes it memorable among the Passion scenes."},"nga:41670":{"d":"Christ stands on a stone tomb before a cave opening in the rock, a lavender cloth over one shoulder and hips, his long reddish-blond hair and red beard framed by a gold halo. He raises two fingers in blessing and holds a staff with a white banner marked with a red cross. Six soldiers in yellow, pink, blue, violet and orange armour lie sprawled around a white stone slab, one propped on an elbow looking up. Rivers wind through rocky country to a distant town under a sky that deepens from yellow to blue.","i":"The Gospels do not describe the moment of the Resurrection itself, but Italian painters developed an image of Christ stepping from the tomb with the banner of victory, while the guards sleep or recoil. Matthew 28 tells of the soldiers placed at the tomb. The picture is the culmination of the Passion cycle and proclaims the central belief of Christianity. The soldier who looks up shows a first dawning recognition, and the sprawling guards contrast with the calm, upright Christ.","c":"The calm of the standing Christ against the sprawl of the sleeping soldiers is a neat piece of design, and the bright armour colours are delightful. The landscape of winding rivers and a distant town gives a poetic sense of dawn. The figure of Christ is a little stiff and the soldiers' foreshortening only approximate, but the picture works as a clear, joyful finale to the Passion sequence."},"nga:460":{"d":"Christ kneels in prayer in a gloomy rocky landscape, looking sharply up at a small winged angel in the upper right who holds out a gold chalice. He wears a terracotta-red cloak over a blue robe, and his halo is marked with a red cross. Behind him, two groups of men sleep huddled on the ground in robes of pink, black, scarlet, steel blue and brown. Distant hills fill the high horizon under a band of charcoal clouds in a slate-blue sky. The panel is almost square, about 42 by 47 centimetres.","i":"The scene, from Matthew 26 and Luke 22, shows Christ praying on the Mount of Olives the night before his death, asking that the cup of suffering pass from him. The angel brings the chalice, which stands for the Passion and recalls the cup of the Eucharist, while the sleeping disciples have failed to keep watch. The panel is probably one of five Passion scenes of the same size and date in the National Gallery, which together would have formed a predella.","c":"Benvenuto lets the setting carry the mood: the bleak rocks, the low heavy cloud and the sleeping men make a convincing night of dread, and Christ's sharp upward look gives the scene its single point of tension. The sleeping groups are tidy rather than slack, and the angel is small. The panel's strength is its economy and clear storytelling; its limit is a certain gentleness that softens the agony the title promises."},"cma:173656":{"d":"The title, Maternal Solicitude, points to a mother animal watching over her young, the kind of sentimental wildlife subject that Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait painted for a wide American public. The picture is a small oil on wood, about 50 by 61 centimeters, dated 1873. Tait's wildlife pictures usually place animals in a detailed, naturalistic woodland or mountain setting, and the title promises a tender moment within such a scene.","i":"Tait, born in England and settled in New York, won fame in the 1850s and 1860s with Currier and Ives prints of hunting and camp scenes, and in his own paintings turned to the animals of the Adirondacks, which he visited often. Pictures of mothers and their young were favorites of Victorian audiences, who saw in wild creatures a model of domestic virtue, an idea that Tait's patrons found as congenial as the picturesque setting.","c":"Tait's strength is sharp observation of animals and the ability to place them in a believable landscape; his weakness is sentiment, and a title like this invites it, making nature endorse the Victorian household's values. Technically his pictures are crisp, and the small panels often have the best concentration. Next to Landseer, the English master of sentimental animal painting, Tait is less theatrical and more naturalistic."},"met:12770":{"d":"A hunting dog stands on point, frozen in the pose that signals game ahead, in a small oil on wood, about 25 by 34 centimeters, dated 1874 and painted by Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait. The museum's keywords are hunting, a bird and a dog. A bird, perhaps the quarry, shares the scene, and the picture catches the tense moment before the hunter's gun is raised.","i":"Pointers and setters were bred to freeze and indicate the hidden position of game birds such as quail or grouse, and the moment of the point, when the dog's whole body is a signpost and the hunter prepares his shot, was a favorite subject of American sporting painters and print publishers. Upland bird shooting was a fashionable gentleman's pastime after the Civil War, and sporting pictures decorated clubs, dens and hunting lodges.","c":"The subject is chosen for its tension, and Tait, a keen student of animals and the outdoors, understands it: the dog's anatomy and stillness are the focus, and a small panel suits the concentration. The painting is a precise, literal rendering of a sporting moment, with no ambition beyond that. It is the kind of picture that pleases specialists in dogs and birds, and its charm is in the exactness."},"met:12768":{"d":"A deer is the subject of this small oil sketch, about 29 by 36 centimeters, painted around 1882 by Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait. The title says it is a sketch from nature, so it records a deer as observed in the woods rather than composed in the studio, and the recorded motif is simply the deer. Sketches like this were raw material for a painter of animals: studies of anatomy, pose and light that could be used again in finished pictures.","i":"Tait spent time in the Adirondack woods studying deer, a favorite subject he painted repeatedly. The nature sketch was part of the American landscape tradition, in which painters were encouraged to study directly from the outdoors. Deer represented grace, shyness and the wild beauty of the northern forests, and they were also a prized quarry for hunters, so Tait's deer pictures had a double appeal, to nature lovers and to sportsmen.","c":"A sketch can be judged only as a sketch: the question is whether the deer's grace and alertness are caught in quick, certain strokes, and the best of Tait's studies manage it, with a freshness often lost in finished, polished animal pictures. The small size and informal handling give an immediacy that the more famous sporting subjects lack. It is a modest but truthful piece of animal observation."},"met:12769":{"d":"A doe and two fawns are the subject of this small oil on academy board, about 25 by 36 centimeters, painted in 1882 by Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait. Academy board, a stiff, primed cardboard, was a favored support for quick oil studies and small cabinet pictures. The recorded motif is deer, and the grouping of mother and young is a tender variation on the artist's frequent theme of Adirondack wildlife.","i":"A mother deer with fawns was a staple of Victorian animal painting, an image of tenderness and vigilance in the wilderness. The audience for such pictures was also the audience for the new interest in protecting the wilderness: New York created the Adirondack Forest Preserve in 1885, and the region's deer had long been hunted hard. A gentle image of a doe and her fawns suited a time that was learning to value wild places.","c":"The tender grouping of mother and two fawns is made for sentiment, and Tait's honest observation of animal bodies saves it from sweetness. At this small size on a board he works economically, in a few touches that suggest fur, ears and alert stance. The picture is minor, but it shows the naturalism that made him a leading American animal painter of his day."},"met:12772":{"d":"Rabbits sit on a log, with a flower among the recorded details, in this small canvas of about 25 by 31 centimeters, painted in 1897 by Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait, then in his late seventies. Small animals in a woodland setting, observed with sympathy and a touch of whimsy, were a gentler subject than the hunting and camp scenes that had made his name.","i":"Rabbits were popular in Victorian art for their softness and timidity, and a pair or group on a mossy log with a woodland flower made a gentle decorative picture for a home. In his late seventies Tait seems to have moved from the exciting hunting scenes of his youth to quieter animal subjects, which sold to the same urban public that wanted nature at a comfortable remove.","c":"The small scale and soft subject allow Tait to display his skill with fur and foliage, and the log gives a plain stage on which the rabbits' rounded forms are arranged. The risk is cuteness, and the picture sits on that edge. Compared with his best Adirondack scenes it is slighter, but it shows a lifelong painter's undimmed attention to animals."},"met:437653":{"d":"A portrait miniature of a woman, painted on ivory in 1808 by the German miniaturist Carl August Senff. The museum's keywords record a woman and a portrait; the catalog gives no dimensions or description, so her features, dress and background are left unstated. Miniatures of this date, in the neoclassical taste of the Napoleonic era, typically show the head and shoulders against a plain or softly clouded ground, in the high-waisted fashion of the day, painted in fine stippling on a thin plaque of ivory.","i":"In the first decade of the nineteenth century the miniature remained the favorite private portrait in Europe: it was exchanged between lovers, given to relatives, and carried by travelers and soldiers in wartime. The sitter is unnamed in the title, so the picture speaks about its type: a likeness kept close and intimate, small enough for a locket or a case. Senff worked in the clear, refined manner favored in those years.","c":"The finest miniatures of the time combine a likeness with a pearly translucency that oil cannot match. Without a view of the surface, this one should be judged by the standards of the type: softness of blending, delicacy of line and economy of background. It is a representative piece of the golden age of the portrait miniature, a trade that lasted until photography took over in the 1840s."},"met:435835":{"d":"A three-quarter-length portrait, about 141 by 97 centimetres, of an unnamed woman accompanied by a dog, painted in oil on canvas in the late 1560s. Bernardino Campi painted noblewomen of Lombardy in rich, formal costume, typically standing or seated against a plain or curtained ground, and the dog, a familiar accessory in such pictures, adds a note of domestic intimacy to an otherwise ceremonial image.","i":"Dogs in portraits of women stood for fidelity and companionship, qualities valued in a wife, and they also showed that the sitter belonged to a household that kept pets. The sitter's name is lost, but the scale and costume mark her as a woman of rank in Lombardy, then under Spanish rule, where court fashion favoured stiff, formal dress. Campi, from Cremona, also taught Sofonisba Anguissola, who became court painter to Philip II of Spain.","c":"Campi was a skilled, polished portraitist, and a canvas of this size gives him room to display his control of fabric, jewellery and composition. The dignity and grace of the figure are convincing. What is lacking is the psychological depth of Titian or Moroni; the face is composed to the point of reserve, and the style is more courtly than searching. As a record of Lombard fashion it is valuable, and as a portrait it is competent and handsome."},"nga:112373":{"d":"A nearly nude, pale-skinned woman rides an oversized seashell drawn by dolphins across dark green water. She sits on a seat of twisted red coral, her body turned to the right and her face lifted toward the sky, and she steers the dolphins with red cords looped through their mouths. A blue cloth and a sheer white veil flutter around her. Four muscular men surround the shell, blowing into shells or playing a flute. Strong light from the left singles her out, and the rest sinks into shadow, with rocky mountains and gray clouds behind.","i":"The woman is Galatea, the sea nymph of Greek and Roman myth who loved the shepherd Acis and was pursued by the Cyclops Polyphemus, as told by Ovid. Her triumph at sea, riding a shell among musical sea-gods, was made famous by Raphael's fresco of about 1512 and became a standard motif of Baroque painting. The four men are presumably tritons or similar sea-gods; no Cyclops appears in the museum's description.","c":"Cavallino often worked on a modest scale, and this large canvas shows his gentler side applied to a grand myth. The best passage is Galatea herself, a glowing, languid figure caught by the raking light against the darkened sea; the musicians, by contrast, are heavy and rather generic. The theatrical lighting looks back to Caravaggio, while the sweetness of her features is Cavallino's own. An engaging picture rather than a commanding one."},"nga:89687":{"d":"A man on a horse rides in profile to the left, seated upright on a tan saddle, in a blue uniform with red accents and a black-and-red hat. He has a black mustache and gray hair, and a long wooden lance rises behind him, taller than his head. In the background at the left another horse and rider in the same uniform appear. The sky is cloudy blue, the ground blue with a strip of brown and dark green below the central horse, and the brushstrokes are loose and visible. The canvas is tiny, about 21 by 27 centimeters.","i":"Toulouse-Lautrec painted this at about thirteen or fourteen, long before the Paris cabarets that made him famous. Cavalry, horses and hunting were the interests of his aristocratic family, especially of his father, and his first lessons in painting came from the horse painter René Princeteau, a family friend. The hussars belong to a youthful phase of observation, when a boy filled sketchbooks with horses and riders.","c":"Even at this age the horse, the lance and the flick of a uniform are caught with the quick, nervous line that later marks his work: bold profile, strong outline, economy of means. The color is simple and the painting slight, the loose brushstrokes those of a gifted boy rather than a master, but the sureness of the gesture and the eye for movement are already evident. It is a charming juvenile work, valued for what it foretells."},"nga:89686":{"d":"A horse-drawn cart seen from behind moves to the right in the center of this tiny panel, about 16 by 22 centimeters. A person in dark blue clothes and a dark hat sits in it beside a white-and-pink bundle that could be another person, and a gray-blue horse pulls it while another person in gray and tall black boots stands in front of the animal. Swirls of brown and several greens suggest dense foliage, painted in thick, visible strokes. The date is 1880.","i":"Lautrec was about fifteen when he painted this small oil, a schoolboy whose gift for drawing horses and carriages had already been noticed. In 1880 he was recovering from the fractures that stunted his growth and would shape his life, and he drew and painted constantly, mostly the horses, dogs and country scenes of his family's estates. The title, The Trap, uses the English word for a light carriage.","c":"This is a sketch, not a finished picture, and its interest is in the handling: the thick, quick strokes that turn foliage into brown and green swirls, and the carriage and horse reduced to a few shapes. The young painter is learning the Impressionist trick of letting touch stand for texture. It is a lively, modest work, and the sureness with which the horse is set into the foliage shows the talent that later made his line famous."},"nga:52228":{"d":"A white dog spotted with caramel brown and with long, floppy ears sits in a garden beside a green park bench, leaning slightly on one paw and looking off to the right with relaxed ears. A blue bird crouches on the ground in front of its feet. A bush and tree trunk, painted in peanut brown and earthy green, fill the background at the right. The panel is tiny, about 24 by 14 centimeters, and the artist wrote his monogram and the dog's name, LA FLECHE, at the lower right.","i":"A dog's portrait with its name inscribed treats the animal as a sitter, and for a boy of sixteen or seventeen, whose family kept hunting dogs and horses, it was natural to paint a favorite. Lautrec's early oils are full of such animal studies, made at his family's country estates, and they reveal the close observation and love of animals that later appear in his portraits of Parisian entertainers.","c":"The loose, rapid brushwork is already a statement of freedom: the dog's relaxed ears and the cocked paw are caught with just enough strokes, and the blue bird adds a surprise of color. The setting is merely suggested. As a very early work it shows more promise than finish, and its charm comes from the painter's evident affection for the animal."},"nga:52229":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, her body facing the viewer while she looks over her shoulder to the right, her face in profile. Her carnation-pink and tangerine hair is pulled into a bun, with fluffy bangs that hide her eyes, and her snub nose is reddened and her lips pursed. She wears a tight black blouse with bright orange trim at a shallow V neckline, her hands resting in her lap. Light falls from the right, against a honey-orange and plum background. The panel is about 24 by 15 centimeters.","i":"Carmen Gaudin was a red-haired model who posed for Lautrec repeatedly in the mid-1880s. Such sitters, working-class women of Montmartre, were his chosen subjects in this decade: not society ladies but the people he met in the streets, workrooms and cafés of his neighborhood. Her hair, painted in pink and orange, is the picture's chief note of color and character.","c":"Lautrec gives the profile a nervous, almost sculpted force: the bun, the cocked head, the exaggerated pink-orange hair against the plum ground. The loose strokes, the scraped coffee-brown edges and the strict economy make the portrait feel quick and alive. Color is used for character more than likeness, and the result is vivid, slightly unflattering and wholly unsentimental, a mark of his early maturity."},"met:437836":{"d":"A man named in the title as Albert (René) Grenier, born in 1858, is the subject of this small oil portrait on wood, about 34 by 25 centimeters, painted in 1887. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so beyond the small size and the wooden panel the picture is described by type: an informal portrait of a friend by Lautrec in the years when he was establishing himself in Montmartre.","i":"Lautrec was a social being as well as a solitary observer, and in the late 1880s he made informal portraits of friends from the Montmartre circle. Albert Grenier, six years older than the painter, belonged to that world, and a portrait of a friend was often made for pleasure, not for a fee. The title gives his full name and dates, which shows the museum's interest in the sitter's identity.","c":"Lautrec's small portraits of friends are among his most direct pictures: free of the demands of a client, they look hard at the face and let the thin paint and the visible board do the rest. If this one follows his habits it will be spare, quick and unflattering, a record of character more than of rank. Such pictures lack grandeur and gain in truth."},"nga:66437":{"d":"A woman sits at a round table, her short brown hair with bangs across her forehead, her cheekbones sharp, her brows thick and her pale lips lifted on one side in a half smile. She wears a high-collared black dress with a pale yellow bow at the neck and a white apron over her lap, and holds a small, translucent glass resting on the table. The background is a blurred blend of brown, gray and dark green, like a wall, and the paint is thick and visible. The canvas is about 73 by 50 centimeters.","i":"The title places the scene at the Bastille, then a working-class district of eastern Paris, and names the sitter as Jeanne Wenz. The glass, the table and the apron show an ordinary café woman, not a society sitter; Lautrec, whose own world was Montmartre, was drawn to such unidealized figures and painted them without sentiment. The half smile gives the portrait its mood: wary, amused and a little weary.","c":"The best thing here is the face: the sharp cheekbones, the thick brows and the lopsided half smile give an immediate sense of a person with a past. Lautrec lets the background dissolve in thick, scrubby strokes so that the figure holds the frame, and the white apron and yellow bow supply the only bright accents. It is blunt, sympathetic and observant, without the cruelty his critics sometimes found in him."},"met:437838":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this oil painting, about 56 by 46 centimeters, dated 1889 to 1891; the museum's keywords record only a woman. The title places her in the garden of Monsieur Forest, a Montmartre garden where Lautrec set up an outdoor studio and painted models in natural light. Pictures of this kind are less theatrical than his famous Moulin Rouge scenes: they are quiet studies of a figure in open air, with the loose, thin paint and visible strokes of his garden series.","i":"In the garden of a friend, Lautrec worked from models outdoors in the Impressionist way, and he used the series to explore daylight, which he mostly set aside for the artificial light of cafés and music halls. The women who posed appear without the glamour of stage and poster. The title gives the setting but not the sitter, so the picture is about a type and a light, not a person.","c":"Outdoors Lautrec is a quieter, more tender painter than in the music halls, and the garden pictures show his command of colored light and his interest in the sitter's inner life. The loose handling can leave the figure sketchy, and the garden is only a few strokes. Still, the series is among his most underrated: it shows an artist who is more than a chronicler of nightlife, and who can paint an ordinary afternoon with delicacy."},"met:438016":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this oil painting on cardboard, about 65 by 53 centimeters, dated about 1890 to 1891; the museum's keywords record a woman and a portrait. The title, The Streetwalker, names her trade, a prostitute who solicited on the street. Painted on cardboard in the thin, quick manner Lautrec often used, the picture belongs to the subject he returned to throughout his life: the women of the Paris demimonde, treated frankly and without moralizing.","i":"Lautrec lived among the dancers, singers and prostitutes of Montmartre, and he treated them as individuals, often with sympathy, at a time when the middle-class public saw them as types or as scandal. A picture titled The Streetwalker could shock a bourgeois viewer, but his tone is neither salacious nor moralizing: the woman is looked at and recorded. His pictures of such women became one of the most honest records of the city's night life.","c":"The strength is the absence of judgment. Lautrec records posture, dress and expression with a few strokes and leaves the viewer to supply the rest, which is more powerful than moralizing. The risk of the cardboard technique is that the figure can seem hurried or incomplete, and some viewers find his candor cold. At his best, as in this subject, he gives a marginal person real presence."},"nga:53140":{"d":"A woman sits upright in a yellow-and-tan reclining chair, her body turned to the right and her face toward the viewer, with pale skin shaded in green and curling light-purple hair in an updo. She wears a white and lilac polka-dot blouse with puffy sleeves and a long striped skirt, and cradles a small black, tan and light-green dog in her lap. Behind her are a grassy area, a wooden fence painted in green, teal and dark red, a bent tree trunk and a sky of crisscrossing strokes. The brown cardboard shows through in places.","i":"Lautrec's garden portraits of about 1890, made outdoors in a Montmartre garden, show models and friends posed in natural light, and this one probably belongs to that group. A lady holding a pet is an old formula of portraiture, and here it gives the sitter something to do with her hands and the picture a touch of intimacy. The strange colors, purple hair and green shadows, come from the Impressionists' lesson that outdoor light is colored.","c":"The picture's boldness is in its color: purple hair, green shadows on pale skin and a sky of crisscrossed strokes, all set on bare brown cardboard that Lautrec lets show through like a drawing's paper. The sitter's face is calm and slightly blank, and the dog nearly steals the picture, but the vivid paint handling makes the whole feel modern, raw and alive. It shows how he could turn a quiet garden sitting into a daring color study."},"met:437835":{"d":"A tall, narrow picture crops three figures close together at the Moulin Rouge. At the right a man in a dark pinstriped suit and black top hat sits with a gold-topped cane across his knees; his face is ruddy, his moustache pointed and his ear flushed red. At the left two women lean toward him: one in profile with ginger hair, a plumed hat and a black ribbon at her neck, and a second behind her shoulder with a chalk-white face and sharply arched brows, wrapped in a frilled pale shawl. The background is a band of yellow and green. The paint is thin and dry, with the tan cardboard showing through, and the signature appears at the lower left. Oil on cardboard, about 86 by 66 centimetres.","i":"The man is William Tom Warrener, an English painter and friend of Lautrec's, and the picture shows him doing what the Moulin Rouge existed for: being talked into something. The Met points to the women's suggestive attitudes and Warrener's ear, reddened in embarrassment, as signs of the risqué conversation. The painting served as a preparatory study for a colour lithograph of 1892, made the year after Lautrec's poster for the Moulin Rouge had made him known across Paris. The women are not identified; with Warrener they make up a typical Lautrec scene of Montmartre night life, seen from the inside by an artist who spent his evenings there.","c":"Lautrec packs more character into this cardboard sketch than many painters manage in a finished portrait. The ruddy face, the stiff top hat and the blush of embarrassment are caught in a few economical strokes, and the cropping, with the women pressed against the edge, gives the closeness of a crowded dance hall. The woman at the far left, however, dissolves into loose shapes, and the lower half of the picture is thin. As a design it already looks like a print, with flat colours and strong outline, which is exactly what it was made to become."},"nga:46542":{"d":"Seven people crowd together at and between tables in a cafe interior, in a vertical composition dominated by blues and pale greens. In the right foreground a woman in a teal-blue jacket and a frilled cap with a green bow sits opposite a man in an olive-green bowler and cinnamon coat at a butter-yellow table, both in profile. At the center a woman in a long black coat stands beyond the table, her head nearly touching the top edge. Beyond them two men sit at another table, one with flame-red hair, while two women stand at the left with their backs turned. Charcoal lines outline each figure.","i":"The Moulin de la Galette was a famous dance hall under a windmill on Montmartre hill, immortalized by Renoir in 1876 as a sunny, dancing crowd. Lautrec's view, painted sixteen years later, is cooler and more crowded: it looks at the tables, not the dance floor, and crops figures at the edge as Degas and Japanese prints did. His Montmartre was a place of ordinary customers, working-class and bohemian, seen with a reporter's frankness.","c":"The composition is bold: the stacked, overlapping figures and the cropped heads give a sense of a crowded room glimpsed in passing, and the blue-green key is unusual, cool and slightly sour. The charcoal outlines and thin paint on cardboard keep the whole sketchy and alive. There is no center; the effect is of a snapshot. It is more a brilliant notation than a finished picture, but it captures the mood of Montmartre pleasure with startling economy."},"nga:46696":{"d":"A man and a woman stand with their backs to the viewer, facing a room where others pose or dance, in this vertical oil on cardboard painted so loosely that it looks like a pastel. At the left the woman wears a brown coat outlined in blue, a fur stole and a flowered blue hat; the man is cut off by the edge. Across from them a woman in a teal dress stands with feet planted wide and hands on her hips, one eyebrow cocked and her lip curled in a sneer, gathering her skirts to show white petticoats. A bandstand rises at the upper right.","i":"The quadrille at the Moulin Rouge, which opened in 1889, was the cancan as danced by professional performers, and it made the club famous. Lautrec, a regular, painted the dancers and their audience again and again, and his posters of 1891 had made him known. The picture catches the moment before the dance: the bold stance and the sneer are part of the performance, an invitation and a challenge to the watching crowd.","c":"Everything depends on the figure with her hands on her hips: the planted feet, the cocked eyebrow and the lopsided sneer give a whole character in a few strokes, and the cropped foreground couple set her at a distance as on a stage. The loose, pastel-like strokes on brown cardboard make the scene feel improvised and electric. Lautrec's wit and unforgiving eye for faces are at full strength, and the picture is among the liveliest records of Paris nightlife."},"nga:46544":{"d":"Two women stand in profile, seen from the knees up, each holding her skirt around her waist so that thighs and buttocks are bare above dark green, thigh-high stockings. They step forward together with faint smiles and heavy-lidded eyes. One has blond hair gathered at the nape and a lilac garment; the other has flame-red hair and a mint-green wrap hooked over her shoulders. A third woman, in spruce green, turns her back behind them. The paint is thin and streaky, drawn as much as brushed, and the brown cardboard shows through as the ground of the walls, which are marked with crimson lines.","i":"The title points to the brothel on the rue des Moulins in Paris, where Lautrec lived for long stretches in the mid-1890s and painted its women. The raised skirts probably refer to the routine medical inspection required of registered prostitutes, a subject he treated in other versions. The women are shown without drama or comment: tired, practiced, a little amused. That detached sympathy, neither salacious nor moralizing, sets his brothel pictures apart from the sentimental or titillating treatment common in popular art of the day.","c":"This is closer to a drawing in oil than a finished canvas, and its force comes from that economy: a few strokes of lilac, mint and pink over bare cardboard give the bodies weight and the room its tired air. Lautrec's candor has a precedent in Degas's brothel monotypes, but he adds color and wry warmth. The composition is slight, the third figure barely stated; as observation it is unsparing."},"nga:46695":{"d":"A man in a dark bowler, seen from behind with a cigarette hanging from his lips and his hands in his pockets, looks down at a bar running along the left edge. Squeezed beside him, a red-haired woman in a teal feather boa and a turquoise hat leans on one elbow and glances back at him from the corners of her eyes. At right a woman in a tie, jacket and blue feathered cap tips her head back. Paint is spread thin, and the tan cardboard shows through as jacket and skin, with cobalt and violet lines marking the forms.","i":"The inscription reads, in French, 'for Méténier, after his Alfred la Guigne,' so the picture is dedicated to a writer, probably Oscar Méténier, and takes its subject from a character or work of his. La guigne is French slang for bad luck, and the name suggests a down-at-heel figure of the Paris streets. Méténier wrote about the city's low life, and Lautrec's own subjects were the bars, cafés and brothels of Montmartre. The figures are therefore best read as types of that world rather than portraits.","c":"Lautrec's economy is the point: cobalt and violet outlines, a few patches of flat color, and bare cardboard standing in for flesh and cloth. A cropped sign at the right edge and the central man's turned back give the scene the abruptness of a snapshot, a lesson from Degas and Japanese prints. It is a sketch rather than a resolved picture, and the figure at right is thinly stated, but the glances exchanged at the bar are caught with wit."},"met:437837":{"d":"Two women share a sofa, shown close together and off duty, in the kind of room Lautrec visited in Paris brothels in the mid-1890s. The catalog keywords describe the sitters as prostitutes and the pair as a couple. The support is thin cardboard, and the oil is applied in the fluid, drawing-like manner of his small interiors, which gives the scene the immediacy of an observed moment rather than a pose. The picture is horizontal, about 63 by 81 centimeters, and takes its title from the piece of furniture on which the women sit.","i":"Lautrec lodged in Paris brothels for periods between 1892 and 1895 and painted the women in their idle hours between clients: waiting, resting, eating, dressing. Pairs on divans recur in this work and are probably meant as acts of companionship rather than spectacle. Earlier painters and poets, from Courbet's Sleepers to Baudelaire's verse, had treated intimacy between women as a male fantasy; Lautrec's version is read as more matter-of-fact and more sympathetic, the divan being simply where the women spent their days.","c":"The restraint impresses: no leer, no moral, only two bodies leaning together with the loose fall of people who are not performing. Thin paint keeps the picture close to drawing, and the sofa and room are summarily stated, which keeps attention on the pair. Beside the larger, more crowded Salon at the Rue des Moulins this is a smaller, more private picture, and the more sympathetic for it, though too sketchy to count as fully resolved."},"nga:46694":{"d":"A man in an ash-brown suit and top hat sits on a wooden ladderback chair in the lower right, turned mostly away so that only the tip of his nose, a cheek and a double chin show. A silver-topped cane rests in his lap, and a glass stands on the round table beside him. In the upper left three women are grouped in the room beyond: one in an indigo mask, pink top and close-fitting trousers, a hand on her hip, another with her back turned in a hooded pink robe. Mostly vertical strokes outlined in cobalt blue give the man's skin a pale turquoise cast.","i":"The title names the sitter, Maxime Dethomas, a painter and stage designer who was a friend of Lautrec. Yet Dethomas offers almost no face; he is defined by hat, cane, bulk and the direction of his gaze toward the women. The masks and hooded costume suggest a masked ball, probably of the kind held at the Paris Opéra. The portrait records a man in his habitat, watching, and tells the viewer what sort of man he was rather than what he looked like.","c":"Turning the sitter's back to the viewer is a daring, almost journalistic choice, and it works: the heavy figure in the corner, cut off by the frame, sets off the bright, flickering group of masked women as a spectator is set off from a stage. Thin, cobalt-outlined handling on bare cardboard, with its turquoise flesh, conveys artificial light with great economy. It is sketch-like, and some faces are barely stated, but the idea is original and well carried."},"nga:72012":{"d":"Seen from slightly above, a woman dances at the center of a square canvas, her pale face shadowed in slate blue and flushed pink at the cheekbones, her lips crimson. Two flaring pink flowers, each almost as large as her face, are pinned in flame-red hair. She wears a black bodice with puffed sleeves and a low square neckline, and her lime-green skirt flares to show layers of pink beneath as she points a black-stockinged foot. Thirteen courtiers in blue, green and black stand behind her, among them a dark-haired man in a brick-red bolero suit who watches from her right.","i":"The dancer is the actress and singer Marcelle Lender, shown in the bolero from Hervé's comic operetta Chilpéric, revived at the Théâtre des Variétés in Paris in 1895. The courtiers behind her belong to the show's burlesque royal court. Lender was a star of the boulevard stage, and Lautrec, a devoted theatergoer, treated her as he did Yvette Guilbert and Jane Avril: as a public figure whose stage persona, rather than her private face, was the likeness worth catching.","c":"One of Lautrec's largest easel paintings, and unusually ambitious for a stage subject. The plunging viewpoint and acid colors, pink flowers against lime skirt, turn theatrical lighting into pure pigment, and the dancer's blue-shadowed, blushing face looks properly artificial. The crowd behind her is more sketchily brushed, which suits a picture about one performer. Its brilliance is chiefly one of color and nerve; as a likeness of a person it says less than his best portraits."},"met:438018":{"d":"A nude woman is shown with a mirror, in a room that includes a bed, so that her reflection gives a second view of her within the same small picture. Lautrec paints in thin oil on cardboard, so the tan of the support shows through and the figure is built from loose, drawn strokes rather than smooth modeling. The format is a modest upright, about 62 by 47 centimeters, the scale of a picture made to be hung in a private room. The date, 1897, places it among his late brothel subjects.","i":"A woman at her toilette is an old theme: Titian and Velázquez painted Venus with a mirror, and Degas drew bathers in the 1880s. Lautrec removes the goddess. The catalog keywords class the woman among his prostitutes, so the mirror reads as part of a working routine, preparation rather than vanity. In earlier painting a mirror often stood for vanity or self-knowledge; here it perhaps simply doubles the view, as his Elles lithographs of 1896 watch women in their private moments.","c":"Painted two years before his breakdown of 1899, this keeps the old freedom of touch: a few supple strokes establish the body and the room. Nothing is idealized; the nude is simply a person in the middle of her day, the opposite of the polished Salon nude. The mirror is used modestly, less as a compositional puzzle than as a way of making the picture feel looked at. Small and sketchy, it remains a study rather than a major statement."},"met:437834":{"d":"A small portrait of a woman identified only as Mademoiselle Nys, painted in oil on a thin wooden panel just 27 by 22 centimeters. Lautrec left the wood unprimed, so its warm tone and grain show through and serve as part of the flesh, the shadows and the background. The scale is intimate, closer to a quickly made likeness of an acquaintance than to a formal commission, and the handling follows his usual thin, fluid, drawing-like manner.","i":"Lautrec painted and drew the people around him: friends, models, entertainers, café acquaintances. The title gives only a surname and Mademoiselle, so the sitter's identity and the occasion are not spelled out by the picture itself. Raw wood and cardboard were cheap supports that he exploited rather than concealed, letting the ground act as a color in the picture. The year 1899 was a hard one for him: after a breakdown he spent the early months in a clinic at Neuilly, and he went on drawing and painting.","c":"The unprimed wood does much of the work: warm brown for flesh and background, with a few quick darks and accents to make a face. It is a sketch, but a confident one, showing how little Lautrec needed to find a likeness. Comparison with his large public pictures of the early 1890s would be unfair; this is a private, small-scale piece, valuable as evidence of his late economy and of his willingness to let the support speak."},"met:437839":{"d":"A small oil painting on a wooden panel, about 41 by 32 centimeters, showing a woman, a man and a horse, with riding as the subject. The title gives only a first name, Émilie, and the museum dates the panel loosely to the late 1890s. Lautrec's small panels of this kind are painted fast, in thin paint with the wood often left showing, building a scene from a few strokes of drawing in color. The scene is best described as a small genre picture rather than a formal equestrian portrait.","i":"Horses and riders run through Lautrec's work from the drawings he made as a boy and from the lessons of his first teacher, René Princeteau, a painter of horses and sporting scenes. In the 1890s he returned to the subject at racecourses and the circus. The title, a first name only, suggests a person known to him, and the combination of figures and horse points to an observed scene or a friendly pictorial joke rather than a commission.","c":"Small panels like this show Lautrec's draftsmanship at its quickest: line and a little color doing the work of a full painting. His feeling for animals in motion is among his most reliable gifts, and it tends to be least labored at this scale. The work is modest, to be enjoyed for its speed and ease; it adds to the record of his lifelong interest in horses without making a large statement."},"met:436328":{"d":"A young boy is shown in a tiny portrait miniature painted on ivory, wearing a cap trimmed with a feather and a collar. The format is the pocket portrait: a small likeness meant to be held in the hand or set in a case, with fine touches of the brush building up the face. The title does not name the sitter. Miniatures of this kind were usually painted in watercolor and gouache on thin sheets of ivory, whose pale surface glows through the transparent color.","i":"In eighteenth-century France portrait miniatures were intimate keepsakes: likenesses of children, spouses and lovers carried in a locket, a snuffbox or a bracelet. The cap with its feather and the broad collar suggest a child of a comfortable family, though the sitter is unidentified. The painter, Marie Anne Gérard, was the wife of Jean-Honoré Fragonard and a miniaturist in her own right; the format was often considered suitable for women painters because it could be worked at home, on a small scale.","c":"The format rewards patience rather than boldness, and the name Madame Fragonard is a reminder of how much skilled work by women painters has stood in the shadow of their celebrated husbands. As a likeness it is conventional, a charming child's portrait of its day, with none of the brio of Fragonard's own oil sketches, but the delicate, luminous finish proper to ivory suits a young sitter well. A small, polished, modest piece."},"cma:130163":{"d":"A young woman's head, wrapped in a turban, is painted in oil on a small canvas about 55 by 46 centimeters. It is a tête d'expression, an expressive head: an academic exercise in which a painter conveys a single emotion through the face alone. The type strips away story, setting and companion figures, leaving the head to carry everything. David painted it around 1780, and the museum records that its quality was so well known that his students asked permission to copy it.","i":"From 1760 such head studies became standard training at the École des Beaux-Arts in Paris. They taught future history painters to read and render the passions, following a tradition codified by Charles Le Brun in the seventeenth century, which held that the face is the instrument of the soul. The aim was moral as much as aesthetic, since beauty and virtue were meant to be linked. The turban is probably a studio device, lending exotic, timeless dress that keeps attention on the face.","c":"In his early thirties and at the end of his Roman training, David shows here the discipline that underlies the famous clarity of the Oath of the Horatii a few years later. Expressive heads are often dry exercises; the interest is the firm, sculptural handling and the restraint of feeling. That his own pupils sought to copy it is a fair measure of its authority. It remains, at heart, a school piece, admired for its excellence rather than its adventure."},"met:436105":{"d":"A bare, vaulted prison cell, lit from the left. Socrates, white-bearded and stripped to the waist, sits upright on a low bed with one forefinger raised, still arguing, while his other hand reaches for the cup of poison that a young attendant holds out, his face buried in his arm. Around him his friends give way to grief: Crito, in red, grips the philosopher's thigh; an old man in gray, Plato, slumps at the foot of the bed with eyes lowered; a crowd in blue, ochre, and crimson presses in at the right. Chains and scrolls lie on the floor at the lower left, and in the arched doorway beyond, a woman climbs the stairs and glances back. The figures are arranged like a relief across a shallow stage.","i":"The subject comes from Plato's Phaedo, which tells how Socrates, condemned in 399 BC for impiety and for corrupting the young, spent his last hours discussing the immortality of the soul before drinking hemlock. David painted it in 1787 for a private patron, the young Charles-Michel Trudaine de la Sablière, and took advice from the Hellenist Jean Adry, who urged him to show Plato as still and Crito as more moved. According to the dialogue Plato was not present, and he was a young man at the time, yet David shows him as an elder, perhaps as the author imagining the scene. At the Salon of 1787 the picture was a triumph; its calm self-command suited Enlightenment ideals.","c":"This is Neoclassicism at its most disciplined: David clears the stage, flattens the space into a relief, and lets gesture carry the drama. The achievement is the contrast, one lucid man among men who cannot hold themselves together. It is cooler than his Oath of the Horatii of 1784, and some find it too schematic, with poses that read as diagrams and surfaces polished like enamel. Yet the raised finger beside the attendant's averted face is a stroke of real psychological economy, and the picture set the pattern for moral history painting in France."},"met:436106":{"d":"A man in a black suit sits at a table covered with scarlet velvet, pen in hand, and turns his head to look up at the woman who leans on his shoulder. She stands beside him in a white silk gown with a blue sash, her powdered hair piled high, and faces the viewer with a slight smile. On the table lie papers, an inkwell, and tall glass apparatus for experiments with gases. A large round-bottomed flask and brass instruments lie on the floor at the lower right, and a folder and a dark cloak rest on a chair at the left. Behind them, gray painted-marble pilasters stand in a bare, cool room. The figures are lit clearly and set close to the picture plane. The canvas is about 2.6 meters tall.","i":"Antoine Lavoisier, the chemist who named oxygen and helped found modern chemistry, and his wife, Marie-Anne Paulze, who assisted him in the laboratory, translated scientific papers, and drew his experiments, sat for David in 1788. She had studied drawing with David and commissioned the picture. Technical examination at the Met has shown that David first painted a richer, more fashionable couple, with a hat for her and a gilded table, and then replaced them with sober clothes and instruments. The portrait was kept from the Salon, reportedly for fear that a picture of this wealthy servant of the old regime would provoke viewers. He was guillotined in 1794. The Met bought the picture in 1977.","c":"A remarkable double portrait because it treats a wife as a partner: Madame Lavoisier stands above her husband, her gaze meets the viewer while his turns to her, and the apparatus is painted with a lucidity that makes science look elegant. David's cool, hard light and the stone pilasters give the couple the stature of Roman citizens. Its weakness is a certain frozen quality. The figures are lit like statues, and their relationship, for all the gestures, remains posed. The scarlet velvet is the best passage."},"nga:46114":{"d":"Napoleon stands full-length in his study at the Tuileries Palace, in the uniform of a colonel of the Imperial Guard, with one hand tucked into his waistcoat and a sword on a chair beside him. On the desk lie papers and the civil code known by his name, the candles have burned low, and a clock shows that dawn is near, so the emperor appears to have worked through the night. Rich draperies and furnishings of the imperial style surround him. Oil on canvas, about 204 by 125 centimeters, dated 1812, by Jacques-Louis David.","i":"The picture presents Napoleon as the legislator and tireless worker rather than as a conqueror: the Civil Code, the guttering candles and the dawn clock tell the viewer that he has labored through the night for his people, while the sword resting nearby recalls his military power. It was painted for a Scottish admirer, the Marquess of Douglas, and delivered in 1812, the year of the disastrous Russian campaign, as a calculated image of the emperor's virtues.","c":"David combines portrait and political statement with great control: the clear drawing, the cold lamplight and the details of desk and clock make the emperor the very image of the conscientious ruler, and his solid, unheroic bulk gives him a human presence. The theatrical props can feel staged, and the picture has the stiffness of an official image, but as propaganda it is sharply specific. It is among the defining images of Napoleon."},"nga:46113":{"d":"A portrait of Madame David, in oil on canvas, about 73 by 59 centimeters, dated 1813, by Jacques-Louis David. The title gives her only as Madame David, and the sitter is generally identified as the painter's wife. The canvas is of modest size, the kind he reserved for family and friends rather than for official commissions. David painted it late in his career, when his portraits of family and friends had become plain and concentrated.","i":"Jacques-Louis David married Marguerite-Charlotte Pécoul in 1782; they divorced in 1794, amid the political upheavals of the Revolution, and remarried in 1796. A portrait of 1813 by the aging painter of his wife records a relationship of long standing, and the plain approach David took with family portraits, without the grandeur of his state pictures, makes them among his most direct. The title's formality hides a private history.","c":"David's late portraits of relatives and friends are among his most affecting: shorn of ceremonial rhetoric, they show the sitters with a candid, almost severe regard. A portrait of his wife, painted when she was nearly fifty, would be expected to share that plain honesty. Few painters of the period looked at a sitter's face with such concentrated steadiness."},"met:436107":{"d":"A general of Napoleon's army is shown on a large upright canvas nearly two meters high, in a commissioned portrait of a professional soldier. As in David's other late portraits, the interest centers on the sitter's face and bearing, with the finish concentrated on the head. The picture is dated 1816, the year after Waterloo and the year David himself left France for exile in Brussels. The sitter, Étienne-Maurice Gérard, fought in Napoleon's last campaign and was later a marshal of France.","i":"Gérard owed his rank to Napoleon's campaigns, and David, once the Emperor's First Painter, painted him after the fall of the Empire, in the year David left France as a regicide who had voted for the death of Louis XVI and then served Napoleon. A portrait made among former servants of the Empire carries loyalty on both sides. Gérard later became a marshal under Louis-Philippe and briefly prime minister, so the sitter's story did not end with defeat.","c":"David's late portraits drop the glamour of his Empire work and look hard at the face, and this one is typical: the head is modeled with a sculptor's firmness, and the sitter's will is evident without rhetoric. It does not have the invention of David's double portrait of the Lavoisiers, also in the Met, but the directness is impressive, and the picture is a sober, convincing record of a soldier's character."},"nga:118800":{"d":"A bouquet of many flowers bursts from a glass vase on a light grey table against a black wall, in a tiny picture on copper, about 24 by 19 centimetres. Two large red and cream tulips crown the arrangement, with smaller red, blue, white, pale yellow and pale purple flowers below. Some blooms droop over the rim, and green leaves and pink buds have fallen onto the table, where a red and black beetle sits at the left and a small blue snail with a white shell crawls along the far edge at the right.","i":"In 1623 tulips were rare, costly flowers, treasured by collectors, and the red and cream striping of the two at the top is typical of the broken tulips that were most prized, their streaks caused by a virus that growers did not then understand. A painted bouquet let a patron own every flower in one picture, regardless of season. The fallen buds, drooping blooms and creeping snail are usually read as reminders that beauty passes. The picture appears to be a companion to the artist's Still Life with Iris.","c":"A tiny, polished picture in which the tulips are the stars: their cream and red patterns stand out against the black wall, and the copper support gives the colour a glassy brilliance. The arrangement is dense and slightly crowded, and the small creatures are charming touches of observation. It lacks the grandeur of Ambrosius Bosschaert's more ordered bouquets, but it shows the same loving attention to petals, glass and insects in a very small compass."},"nga:118801":{"d":"A bouquet of many colourful flowers bursts from a glass vase on a light grey table against a black wall, in a tiny picture on copper about 24 by 19 centimetres. A large bright orange lily is at the centre, surrounded by red, white, blue, yellow and pink blooms, with a big blue iris at the top right. Some flowers droop over the rim and a few small ones have fallen onto the table, where a brown beetle sits at the left and a caterpillar at the right.","i":"Flower pieces like this belonged to a new kind of picture that spread across Northern Europe around 1600. The bouquet combines flowers that bloom at different seasons, so it could not exist in nature; the painter has assembled a collector's garden on one panel. The drooping petals, fallen blossoms and insects are usually read as reminders that beauty is short-lived, a vanitas theme, though they also show the painter's skill. Peter Binoit, a German painter, worked in this Flemish-derived tradition.","c":"A small jewel. The copper gives the paint an enamel-like clarity, and the black ground makes the colours blaze. The beetle and caterpillar are charming, and the loosely bursting arrangement is livelier than the stiff symmetry of some earlier bouquets. The crowding is a little confused and the flowers jostle, but the picture's energy and finish are impressive for a work of 1623, an early example of German flower painting."},"nga:40":{"d":"A horizontal altarpiece panel, about 102 by 144 centimeters, painted around 1492 to 1495 in oil on poplar. The title names the figures: the Madonna and Child, with Saint Jerome and Saint John the Baptist, in the format called a sacra conversazione, in which saints share a space with the Virgin. The museum's record gives no further description of the poses, setting or colors, so those cannot be reported here.","i":"Jerome, the scholar and translator of the Bible, and John the Baptist, the forerunner of Christ, were both hermit saints who withdrew to the wilderness, and each has recognizable attributes: Jerome a lion and sometimes a cardinal's red robe, John a camel-hair garment and a cross-staff. In late fifteenth-century Venice the sacra conversazione was the favored altarpiece type, showing the saints not as mere visitors but as a calm company around the Virgin.","c":"Cima, a native of Conegliano who learned from Giovanni Bellini, is a painter of calm, luminous harmony, and in a sacred conversation he is at home: the figures are balanced, the color clear and the landscape a gentle backdrop of his native Veneto hills. He lacks Bellini's depth of feeling and Titian's fire. Pleasing and serene, a little cool, and typical of Venetian altarpieces of the 1490s."},"nga:46111":{"d":"A single saint, Helena, in a small oil on panel of about 40 by 32 centimeters, painted around 1495. The record gives only the title and medium, so how she is posed, dressed and set is not described here. In Venetian painting Helena, the mother of the emperor Constantine, is typically shown crowned and holding the cross she found in Jerusalem, and the small format suggests a panel for devotion or a detached fragment.","i":"Helena (about 250–330) was the mother of Constantine, the first Christian emperor. Tradition holds that she traveled to Jerusalem in her old age and found the True Cross, the cross on which Christ was crucified, and built churches on the holy sites. She is a patron of discoverers and of finders of lost things, and Venetian churches kept her relics.","c":"Cima painted Helena several times, and she suits his gift for dignified, serene female figures: a calm face, a clear contour and a luminous color, all softened by Bellini's lesson. A single saint offers little opportunity for drama, and the interest lies in poise and tone. If the small panel was once part of a larger ensemble, its charm today lies in the self-contained gravity of the figure."},"nga:309":{"d":"A balding, bare-chested old man with a long white beard kneels before a rocky cave opening, a stone in his hand, and looks up at a small crucifix opposite. He is Saint Jerome, gaunt and hollow-cheeked, in a powder-blue robe slipped off one shoulder; a lion lies beneath the cave. The crucified Christ is about half Jerome's size, and a black snake emerges from a crevice at the cross's base. Beyond are a vivid blue river, a grassy plain, a walled town and pale blue mountains. The artist signed on a painted scroll propped before the saint's knee.","i":"Jerome, the fourth-century scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, spent years as a hermit in the Syrian desert, beating his breast with a stone to mortify his flesh and resist temptation. The lion recalls the legend that he removed a thorn from a lion's paw, and the snake can stand for the Devil's temptations. This image of the penitent saint in the wilderness was widely painted in Renaissance Venice.","c":"Cima's Jerome is a fine example of the small devotional picture: the gaunt body, the pale landscape and the luminous distance, a view of the Veneto hills, make a perfect harmony of austere figure and open air. The scale is intimate and the detail fine, down to the signature on its painted scroll. Restraint is the picture's strength; a more theatrical painter would have overplayed the saint's agony."},"met:435901":{"d":"A small panel, about 20 by 27 centimeters, painted around 1510 in oil on wood, showing the Madonna and Child with two saints. The record lists Mary, the Christ Child, Francis of Assisi and another saint, whom the title names as Clare. The museum gives no description of the arrangement, the landscape or the colors. The scale is that of a picture for private devotion, to be kept in a room or a small chapel.","i":"Francis of Assisi, who received the stigmata, and Clare, who founded the order of the Poor Clares with his encouragement, were the two great figures of the Franciscan movement. A panel pairing them with the Virgin and Child suggests a Franciscan patron, perhaps a member of the order or a convent, though the museum does not say. Small sacre conversazioni like this were sold to devout buyers across the Veneto.","c":"Cima was a master of the small, serene devotional panel, and this is a typical example: harmonious grouping, soft light and the clear, gentle faces that his patrons liked. It is a quiet, slightly formulaic work, unadventurous beside the large altarpieces, but it has the perfection of a well-made object. Pleasing without being profound."},"met:435902":{"d":"Three saints stand together in this nearly square canvas, about 128 by 122 centimeters, painted around 1513 and transferred from its original wood panel to canvas. The record lists Saint Roch, Saint Anthony Abbot, Saint Lucy and a wild boar. The museum gives no description of the setting, poses or colors, but the grouping suggests one section of a larger altarpiece or a picture for a church that honored saints who protect against plague, disease and blindness.","i":"Roch, a pilgrim shown with a plague sore on his leg and a dog, was invoked against the plague, which struck Venice repeatedly; Anthony Abbot, an Egyptian hermit, is shown with a pig or boar and a bell and was invoked against skin diseases; and Lucy, a martyr of Syracuse whose name means light, holds her eyes on a dish and protects against blindness. Together they form a trio of protectors of the body and its ailments.","c":"A grave, handsome trio: Cima gives each saint a calm, upright dignity, and the clear light and clean colors of his best work keep them from stiffness. The transfer to canvas may have dulled the surface, but the composition holds together by the rhythm of the three figures. It is a dignified, not dramatic, picture, and its restraint is in keeping with Cima's whole career."},"nga:224":{"d":"On a small panel, the Virgin sits on the lap of her mother, Saint Anne, who is enthroned in a shadowed niche hung with a garland of fruit and leaves; two winged infants behind her hold up a golden halo. The Christ child, seated on Mary's knee, reaches for the finger of Saint Catherine, who kneels at the left in a gold wrap over green, with a red cloth trailing behind her. On either side stand a man in a green hooded habit, hand to his chest, and a man in a white robe and black cape holding a stem of lilies. At the foot of the steps lie a crown, a sword and a piece of a broken wheel. The colours are warm and the forms soft.","i":"Catherine of Alexandria, a princess of the early fourth century known from legend, was said to have had a vision in which the infant Christ took her as his bride; the crown, sword and broken wheel at her feet are the signs of her royal birth and martyrdom. The man with a faint wound on his hand is Saint Francis, showing the stigmata, and the man with lilies in black and white is Saint Dominic. The National Gallery dates the panel to 1510–1515, early in Correggio's career, when he was in his twenties. Its recorded history begins in Ferrara, in the collection of Count Costabili, who died in 1841.","c":"A small, youthful picture that already hints at the soft, enclosing warmth of Correggio's mature style. The tender exchange between child and Catherine is the heart of it, and the gentle modelling of faces and hands points toward his later work. But the arrangement is crowded, the saints at the sides are stiff attendants, and the pyramid of Anne, Mary and child is awkward in its details; surface damage to the faces of Mary and Anne also limits what can be judged. His later versions of the subject, with their dissolving shadows and easy movement, are far richer."},"nga:46167":{"d":"A half-length figure of Christ against a black background, with a bright green curtain at the right. The figure has long, curly light brown hair, fair skin, rosy cheeks, thin arched brows, a long straight nose and a small, slightly open mouth, with a gentle, almost androgynous look. A peach-pink garment with gold trim is worn under a blue cloth draped over the left shoulder. One hand holds an open book, and the other points to a page. Oil on panel, about 43 by 33 centimeters, painted about 1515.","i":"A Salvator Mundi, Savior of the World, normally shows Christ blessing and holding a globe, so the open book here is unusual; a youthful figure pointing at a page could also recall the boy Jesus among the doctors, so the subject may be read either way. The book points to Christ as the Word and as teacher. Such small panels were made for private devotion, to be looked at closely and at length.","c":"The soft modeling of the face and the glow of the flesh against the black ground show Correggio's early debt to Leonardo's sfumato, and the tender, slightly dreamy expression is characteristic of the painter. The picture is small and rather sweet, and the ambiguity of the figure makes it harder to read than his larger devotional works. It is valuable as a glimpse of the artist in the 1510s, before his great Parma commissions."},"met:435992":{"d":"Four full-length saints stand shoulder to shoulder in a dark wood, lit by a soft glow. At left Peter, bald and bearded, in a blue tunic and yellow mantle, looks down, with keys in one hand and a book in the other. Beside him a woman in a blue-green veil and dark gown, hands crossed on her chest, has a small green dragon bound at her feet by a cord; she is Martha. Next, Mary Magdalen in yellow and red holds an ointment jar and glances toward the viewer with a faint smile. At right Leonard, bearded, in a brown monk's habit, gazes upward and holds a pair of shackles. Flowers and grass edge the rocky ground.","i":"The picture was commissioned by a local patron, Melchior Fassi, for his chapel in the hospital church of Santa Maria della Misericordia in Correggio, the painter's home town near Parma, and it stayed there until 1690. Fassi chose the saints, each identified by an attribute: Peter's keys, Martha's tamed dragon, Magdalen's ointment jar and, for Leonard, patron of prisoners, fetters. Correggio was in his twenties, and the soft light and veiled, ambiguous expressions show how closely he had studied the methods of Leonardo da Vinci. The Metropolitan Museum dates it to about 1515.","c":"An early Correggio that already shows his gift for atmosphere. Light seems to come from inside the figures, and Magdalen's faint smile hints at the sweetness of his later work. As a composition it is stiff: the four saints are lined up like statues, no one looks at anyone, and the dark forest flattens space instead of deepening it. Beside the swirling domes he painted in Parma in the 1520s, this is a quiet, introspective picture, interesting mainly as a first sign of his talent."},"nga:41590":{"d":"The painting shows the Holy Family: the Virgin Mary, the Christ Child and Saint Joseph. It is a small, horizontal picture, about 37 by 46 centimeters, the scale of a devotional image for a private room rather than an altar. Its paint layer has been moved from the original wooden panel to a hardboard support, a conservation treatment used on decayed panels. The museum gives it to Giorgione, probably around 1500, a cautious attribution that reflects how few paintings are securely his.","i":"The Holy Family shows Christ's earthly household as a model of domestic virtue, with Joseph as protector and Mary as mother. In Venice around 1500, Giovanni Bellini and the painters of his circle set such groups in half-length or in open landscape for patrons who wanted a devotional image that was also a work of art. Giorgione's contribution to the Venetian manner was a softened light and a mood of reverie, in which landscape counts for as much as the figures.","c":"The attribution is the real question. Giorgione died at about thirty-three, and the group of paintings every scholar accepts is small, so a picture like this sits on a fault line of connoisseurship. Transfer of the paint layer to a new support commonly leaves a surface worn or reconstructed, which makes judging touch harder still. Whoever painted it, the intimate scale and gentle mood make a quiet, appealing devotional piece rather than a commanding one."},"nga:432":{"d":"The picture shows the Adoration of the Shepherds, the Gospel scene in which shepherds, told of Christ's birth by angels, come to worship the newborn child. It is a horizontal panel about 91 by 110 centimeters, large enough for a small chapel or a patron's private room, and the museum dates it to 1505 to 1510. It is often known as the Allendale Nativity. Painters of the Venetian school treated this subject as an outdoor pastoral, with figures and landscape sharing the stage, and this panel follows that habit.","i":"The Adoration of the Shepherds comes from the Gospel of Luke: an angel announces the birth to shepherds in the fields, and they hurry to find the child. For Venetian patrons around 1500 the subject joined devotion to a pastoral idyll, with Christ born among humble people and animals and the first witnesses simple men. It was a theme in which a painter could make landscape and light carry feeling, which is why it suited Giorgione, in the last years of his short life.","c":"Few paintings are firmly Giorgione's, so each attribution invites debate, and scholars have argued over how much of this panel is his and how much belongs to the young Titian, particularly in the landscape. What is not in doubt is the lyrical, quiet mood, which makes the sacred event intimate rather than ceremonial. That poetic quality, more than any dramatic incident, is why the picture is prized and why it is hard to pin to one hand."},"nga:41691":{"d":"Set in a landscape, Saint George lunges over the neck of his brown horse, thrusting the broken stump of a red-and-white striped lance down at a forest-green dragon curled beneath its hooves, blood dripping from the dragon's jaws. He wears royal-purple armor, a plumed helmet and a flying scarlet cape, and his brow is knotted. At left a crowned young woman kneels on a rock in a cranberry gown and gold cloak, hands raised. A severed foot, an arm and two heads lie at lower right; a hilltop city, tiny onlookers and an angel in a yellow glow fill the distance.","i":"The legend, retold in the medieval Golden Legend, says a dragon outside the city of Silene demanded victims until the lot fell on the king's daughter. George, a soldier-saint, wounded the monster with his lance, and the princess led it into the city; the people then accepted baptism. He was the model Christian knight overcoming evil, and the severed remains of earlier victims make the threat concrete. The angel above signals divine help for his act.","c":"Sodoma gives the story real energy: the knight's lunge, the rearing horse with bared teeth and the fluttering cape drive the picture, and the kneeling princess adds a courtly tenderness. The Leonardo-inspired softness of his earlier career has given way to something more theatrical, with precious colors, purple armor against a celery-green skirt, and some decorative stiffness. It is an engaging, slightly operatic Sienese treatment, more lively than profound."},"met:437978":{"d":"A nearly square canvas, about 60 by 59 centimeters, centers on Christ at the moment known as Ecce Homo, 'Behold the man.' After the scourging and mocking, Pilate presents him to the crowd, crowned with thorns and robed in mock purple. The museum dates the picture to Sodoma's last decade, about 1540 to 1549. The compact size and tight focus suggest a devotional image meant for close, private contemplation rather than a large narrative of Pilate and the crowd.","i":"The words come from the Gospel of John (19:5), when Pilate shows the scourged Jesus to the people. From the fifteenth century, the half-length Ecce Homo became a favorite image of Italian devotion, inviting the viewer to feel pity and a share of responsibility for Christ's suffering. Such pictures hung in private rooms and chapels, to be used in prayer.","c":"The Ecce Homo asks for restraint, and Sodoma, whose gift was for sweetness and soft modeling rather than anguish, is at his best when he holds back. By the 1540s his manner had become quieter and more conventional than in his early Roman and Sienese frescoes. A small late devotional canvas like this is more a document of his sustained workshop habits than a fresh invention, though its focus and small scale make it direct and affecting."},"cma:97147":{"d":"A small upright canvas, about 59 by 47 centimeters, showing the Crucifixion of Christ, the central event of Christian devotion. The museum gives only a sixteenth-century date, and Sodoma, active in Siena and Rome, painted the subject repeatedly. Pictures of this size and format were made for private chapels and domestic devotion, to be placed where a person could pray in front of them. Sodoma's figures are usually slender and softly modeled, with a lingering Leonardesque gentleness.","i":"The Crucifixion represents the culmination of the Passion and the Christian doctrine of redemption, with Christ dying for human sin. Painted versions often add the grieving Virgin and Saint John beside the cross, and Mary Magdalene kneeling at its foot, so that the viewer is invited into the circle of mourners. A small canvas encourages quiet meditation rather than public display, and is made to be looked at close up, in prayer.","c":"Sodoma tends toward elegance rather than ferocity: slender bodies, soft light and a grace that sits oddly with the violence of the subject. Where his large fresco cycles show invention and energy, a small devotional canvas like this is more conventional, rewarding for finish and tenderness rather than novelty. As with many sixteenth-century Crucifixions, its success depends on restraint, and a sober, quiet treatment suits the subject better than rhetoric."},"nga:41680":{"d":"On a panel only 16.5 centimetres high, the Virgin stands half-length behind a stone sill, her head tilted as she looks down at her son. The naked Child stands on a blue cloth spread across the ledge, reaches up toward her, and holds out a few pomegranate seeds near her face. She wears a rose-red dress fastened at the neck with a dark jewel and a blue cloak lined in green, and in her hand she holds the opened fruit, half lost against the dress. A thin gold halo hangs behind each head. Two windows on either side open onto pale green meadows, small trees and blue hills, under a sky that fades from blue to yellow. The surface is smooth and polished.","i":"The pomegranate, a fruit with many seeds inside one skin, is a traditional emblem of the Church and, with its red juice and its bursting rind, of Christ's Passion and Resurrection. Here the Child offers its seeds to his mother and, in effect, to the viewer. The size suggests a picture for private devotion, and the sill and window views follow Netherlandish models, as does the oil medium, which Florentine workshops were then adopting from the north. The panel is often called the Dreyfus Madonna, after a former owner. It has been given to Leonardo da Vinci, to Verrocchio's workshop and to Lorenzo di Credi, who trained there alongside Leonardo; the National Gallery of Art catalogues it as Credi's, but specialists have not settled the question.","c":"The picture's appeal is its finish. Credi was known for smooth, enamel-like surfaces, and the modelled flesh, the glassy landscape and the cool blues are carefully done. What it lacks, if it is not Leonardo's, is any hint of unease or invention: the gesture is sweet, the faces are quiet, and the composition sits neatly inside its frame. It is most valuable as evidence of how fast Florentine painters took up oil and the Flemish window view, and as a measure of the difference between a polished pupil and a restless master."},"met:436909":{"d":"The Virgin adores the Christ Child, who is attended by the infant Saint John the Baptist and an angel, four figures in a quiet scene of devotion. It is painted in tempera on a wooden panel and dated to the early 1490s. The subject is a Madonna adorante, in which Mary kneels in worship before her newborn son instead of holding him on her lap. A panel of this kind was made for domestic devotion, a picture to pray before in a Florentine household.","i":"The Madonna adoring the Child recalls the Nativity, with Mary worshiping her son as God as well as her child. Saint John the Baptist, Christ's cousin, is a favorite companion because he is the patron saint of Florence and the forerunner who will announce Christ. The angel adds a heavenly witness. Together the figures turn a family group into an act of worship, one the owner was meant to join.","c":"Lorenzo di Credi trained in Verrocchio's workshop beside Leonardo, and took from Leonardo the soft modeling but little of his daring. His pictures are famous for polish, with smooth, enamel-like surfaces and gentle, pious faces. This panel is a typical product: refined, careful, pleasing to the eye and the devotions of a quiet household, though without the emotional force or invention that a more adventurous painter might have brought to the subject."},"met:436910":{"d":"A portrait of a young woman, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 59 by 40 centimeters. The upright format is typical of Florentine bust-length portraits of the 1490s. The title does not name the sitter, so she is known only by her youth and the likeness the artist gave her. Oil allowed Lorenzo di Credi the fine, smooth finish for which he is known, and the panel's modest size suggests a picture for a private room rather than a public display.","i":"Portraits of young Florentine women around 1500 were often made to mark a betrothal or marriage, recording beauty, dress and hair as signs of a family's standing. The sitter here is not identified, so such a purpose is probable rather than documented. Around 1490 Florentine portraiture, under Leonardo's influence, was moving from the old profile format toward softer modeling and a more natural pose, and a painter from Verrocchio's workshop would have known Leonardo's Ginevra de' Benci.","c":"Credi trained beside Leonardo under Verrocchio, and the influence shows in the soft modeling of the face. His own contribution was an even, polished surface, which gives his portraits a cool clarity. The likeness is composed and graceful, but it lacks the animation that Leonardo gave his women; the sitter seems presented rather than caught. A refined, quietly appealing picture that is more admirable for finish than for psychological depth."},"nga:293":{"d":"Shown from the lap up, a young woman sits facing the viewer, cradling a plump baby against an inky black ground. A sea-blue cloth covers her head and shoulders, over a scarlet gown belted high and a cobalt mantle lined with orange. A thin gold line makes her halo, and she lowers her eyes toward the child. He wears a gray tunic with rolled sleeves and gold rays, and turns to pluck a carnation from a cream-white vase with fern-green scallops. Soft light falls from the upper left across their pale, peachy skin.","i":"The carnation is often read in Christian art as a flower of the Passion: its red suggests blood, and its spiky petals and clove-like scent recalled the nails of the Crucifixion. As the child reaches for it, the picture quietly foretells his sacrifice, and the mother's lowered gaze takes on a note of contemplative sadness. Small panels like this were made for private devotion, where such symbols could be read slowly.","c":"Luini, a Lombard painter, absorbed Leonardo's soft modeling and sweetened it, and this small panel is typical: graceful, gentle, easy to love. The dark ground concentrates the light on flesh and drapery, and the blues and scarlet are handled with real skill. It lacks Leonardo's anatomical intelligence and psychological tension, and the faces are somewhat generic, but the turning child and the glowing colors have the charm that made Luini's Madonnas so popular."},"nga:12150":{"d":"A tall fresco, about 229 by 140 centimeters, painted by Luini around 1520 to 1522. The title places it in the story of Cephalus and Procris, with Procris praying to Diana, goddess of the hunt and of chastity. Fresco is pigment laid on wet plaster, and this panel has been detached from its original wall. It is one of a group of related frescoes, probably made to decorate a private house, where such classical stories were a mark of the owner's learning.","i":"In Ovid's Metamorphoses Procris is the faithful, jealous wife of the hunter Cephalus. Diana is her protector: in the story she gives Procris a hound and an unerring javelin, the very weapon that will later kill her. A prayer to Diana is therefore a moment of trust that a viewer who knows the ending reads with irony. Cycles like this gave educated owners tales of love, hunting and fate from classical literature.","c":"Luini is at his best in gentle narrative, and the fresco medium, with its matte, chalky surface, suits the soft palette he favored. What the series lacks is the urgency of the story: his figures are graceful rather than driven, and a tale of jealousy and accidental death becomes a pleasing pastoral. Charming and unpretentious, it is a decorative achievement rather than a dramatic one."},"nga:12151":{"d":"Seen from a slightly raised viewpoint, a hilly landscape with trees and buildings holds five figures, some in gold robes. In the left foreground a kneeling figure with golden hair, in a golden robe over white, raises a small tool beside a tree, busy at the ground, while others walk across the hills. The colors are muted and earthy, soft greens, browns and yellows. The title suggests the kneeling figure is Cephalus hiding the jewels. The fresco measures about 222 by 150 centimeters.","i":"Jewels belong to the testing episode of the Cephalus and Procris myth, in which the hunter, disguised, tempts his own wife with gifts to see whether she will stay faithful. The title points to a moment from that sequence of suspicion and trial, which in Ovid ends with the couple's estrangement and, finally, Procris's death. The lesson Renaissance readers took was that doubt and testing destroy trust.","c":"Here landscape and figures share the picture, and the effect is of a pastoral idyll more than a drama. Luini's broad, softly lit countryside shows his affection for the Lombard landscape, and the low-keyed palette is pleasing. The weakness is narrative: without the title, what the kneeling figure is doing is hard to read, and the fresco's calm tone does little to suggest the moral stakes of the episode."},"nga:12152":{"d":"An elegant temple, pale orange with white columns, a triangular pediment and large windows, stands behind a green garden of red flowers and a stream edged with reeds. A red doorway at the top of a few steps opens on a brown interior, and a few people sit or stand on the steps looking in. At left two figures walk toward the door: one with a bare chest and a goat's legs, plainly Pan, the other in a pink tunic and white boots with blond hair. The fresco is about 226 by 104 centimeters.","i":"Pan, the half-goat god of shepherds and wild places, belongs to the world of the Cephalus and Procris story, a world of hunting, woodland and rustic gods. Diana's temple stands for the goddess of the chase and of chastity, who protects Procris in the myth. The title does not spell out the exact episode, but a visit to the sanctuary is probably meant as a moment of vow, consultation or refuge.","c":"The architecture is the star: a pale temple with classical columns and windows that look more Renaissance than ancient, set in a garden that is a pleasure ground rather than a wilderness. Luini's palette is clear and warm, the little figures delicate, and the effect is nearer to a stage set than to drama. It gives a good idea of how mythological frescoes might have decorated a Lombard house in the 1520s."},"nga:12153":{"d":"A hunt in a wooded landscape: in the foreground a pack of hounds worries a fallen animal on a grassy patch ringed with vegetation, and above them a figure in purple raises an arm. The ground rises in a spiral toward a dense backdrop of tall trees, with light filtering through the branches under a cloudy sky. Natural, earthy colors give depth and distance. It is a tall, narrow fresco, about 211 by 110 centimeters, from the same mythological cycle as the other Cephalus and Procris scenes.","i":"Hunting is the heart of the Cephalus and Procris myth: Cephalus is a hunter, his dawn departures feed his wife's jealousy, and it is a hunt that ends in her death. The title, which says he is punished, suggests the point at which he pays for his part in the tragedy. The hounds pulling down their quarry set a hard tone, as an image of a chase that ends by turning on the hunter.","c":"Luini's feeling for woodland atmosphere is the best thing here: light through branches, graded distance and a convincing tangle of undergrowth show a painter who looks at nature with affection, and the hounds add welcome energy. Less certain is the story, since the small figures are hard to read without the title. As landscape and animal painting it is attractive; as myth it is muted."},"nga:12154":{"d":"The title names the climax of the myth: the hunter Cephalus hurls his javelin at a movement in the undergrowth and strikes his wife Procris. The catalog lists a spear, trees, mountains and a landscape among the things shown, so the death takes place outdoors, in the wild country where the story is set. The fresco, about 144 by 123 centimeters, is squarer than the other panels from the same cycle and was painted around 1520 to 1522.","i":"In Ovid's telling, Cephalus calls on the breeze, Aura, to cool him after the hunt. Procris, told he is calling to a rival, hides in a thicket to spy. He mistakes the rustle for a beast and throws his javelin, a weapon that never misses and that Procris had herself given him. She dies forgiving him. Renaissance readers saw a warning about jealousy and about acting before looking.","c":"The death of Procris is hard to paint in fresco: it is quick and violent, and it needs a figure drama that Luini's gentle temperament does not naturally supply. He leans toward grace over horror, so the scene is closer to an elegy than a shock, and the landscape does more to set the mood than the action does. It is touching and decorous, and fully in character for the artist."},"nga:12155":{"d":"A figure with curly blond hair, in a purple robe and laced sandals, stands at the lower left holding a long staff, looking toward the viewer and gesturing toward two people and three small dogs curled asleep on the grass at right, dressed in yellow, red and white. Rolling green hills and tall trees fill the right side, and a gray castle on a mountain stands far off at left under a pale gray-blue sky with soft clouds. The title suggests the standing figure is Cephalus and the sleepers are nymphs.","i":"Nymphs are spirits of woods and streams, and in Cephalus's story they belong to the retinue of Diana, goddess of the hunt. The sleeping group and their dogs suggest the pause of a hunting party in the open air. The hunter Cephalus lives among such company, in a world where the goddess of dawn desires him and Diana's followers are sworn to chastity, so even a quiet scene carries a hint of the story's tension between desire and fidelity.","c":"A gently told pastoral: the sleeping group and the distant castle give the scene a dreamy hush that suits Luini's soft temperament. The standing figure who turns to the viewer and points to the sleepers is an effective narrative device, drawing the eye into the picture. The composition is simple and the figures somewhat generic, but the broad landscape of hills and trees does most of the work. Pleasant and quiet, it asks for little and delivers that."},"nga:12156":{"d":"A fresco about 182 by 118 centimeters, painted by Luini around 1520 to 1522 as one of a group of mythological scenes from the story of Cephalus and Procris. The title names its subject as the despair of Cephalus, most plausibly the hunter's grief after the death of his wife Procris, whom he killed by mistake with his own javelin. Detached from its original wall, the painting keeps the soft, chalky surface of the medium. It was made to be seen as one episode in a sequence.","i":"In Ovid's telling, the hunter Cephalus recounts his wife's death in tears, left with the knowledge that his own hand, throwing the javelin she had given him, killed her. Despair is the story's closing note, turning a tale of hunting and jealousy into a tragedy of mistaken identity. Renaissance viewers read such episodes as moral examples about jealousy, suspicion and acting in haste, set in the learned, classical mood that owners of villas liked to display.","c":"A subject of pure emotion tests a painter's range, and Luini, whose gift was sweetness and grace, is more at home with tenderness than anguish. In Luini's frescoes soft light and pale, even color usually turn grief into elegy, with beauty tempering the pain. The fresco survives as a fragment of a larger decorative program, so it is best judged as one note in a sequence rather than a self-sufficient picture. Restrained, pleasing, a little too gentle for its theme."},"nga:12157":{"d":"A fresco about 176 by 107 centimeters, painted around 1520 to 1522 and one of the Cephalus and Procris scenes by Luini. The title speaks of the misfortunes of Cephalus, in the plural, which suggests that the scene gathers or sums up reversals in the hunter's story rather than a single incident. Fresco is pigment applied to wet plaster, and this picture has been detached from its original wall. It belongs to a decorative cycle probably made for a private house.","i":"The legend of Cephalus is a chain of misfortunes: the pursuit by the goddess of dawn, the testing of his wife's faith, her flight and return, and finally the hunting trip on which his javelin kills her. In Ovid, Cephalus himself tells the story as one of sorrow. A cycle of such episodes would have been read in order as a moral tale of love tested by suspicion, and as an elegant display of classical learning.","c":"Luini is a painter of grace rather than catastrophe, and a title that promises misfortune is a hard test of his temperament. In the soft, even light and pale colors of his frescoes the usual result is sadness without violence, pleasing to look at but short of the weight such a subject requires. Seen as part of a decorative series, it does its job; seen alone, it is more charming than moving."},"nga:12158":{"d":"A tall, narrow fresco, about 229 by 108 centimeters. At the left a figure in a red garment, only partly in the frame, stretches an arm toward a white unicorn that kneels on its front legs with its back legs extended, so that its long horn almost touches her outstretched hand. They stand on a light green field with red flowers and small ferns, with thin trees before distant, rocky mountains and a beige sky blending into the earthy tones of the land. The title identifies the woman as Procris.","i":"The unicorn was a medieval and Renaissance emblem of chastity, said to be tamed only by a virgin, so its bow is a tribute to the woman's purity. In the Cephalus and Procris story, Procris is the faithful wife whose loyalty is tested, and in Renaissance illustrations of Petrarch's Triumph of Chastity a unicorn often accompanies the virtuous. The image would have been read as praise of constancy, which makes the later tragedy of the myth sharper.","c":"The unicorn is drawn with delicate grace, its folded legs and lowered head a courteous gesture, and the tall format gives the image the quality of a tapestry or an heraldic emblem. Because the woman is only partly in the frame, the picture reads as a fragment of a larger composition. It succeeds in what Luini does best, a gentle, fairy-tale poetry, but the human figure is secondary, and the effect is decorative rather than dramatic."},"nga:44":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a young woman faces the viewer directly, with an oval face, dark brown eyes and a mouth whose corners turn up faintly. Her hair is parted in the middle under a wide gold headdress that makes a halo-like ring, and large teardrop pearls hang at her ears. A black dress with puffed sleeves frames a sheer butter-yellow panel across her chest. A gold Maltese cross hangs from a thick gold chain, which she touches with one hand while holding the body of a small furry brown animal with bared teeth. Dark green curtains flank a black center.","i":"The portrait advertises rank through costume: pearls, gold, fine sheer fabric and puffed sleeves were the marks of a wealthy Lombard woman in the 1520s. The sitter is not identified. The small animal may be a fashionable fur accessory or an emblem, since the ermine, for instance, stood for purity, but the meaning is uncertain. The Maltese cross may point to piety or a family link to the Order of St. John, though that too is only a possibility.","c":"Portraits are relatively rare in Luini's output, and this one shows his strengths: a clear, serene face, carefully rendered pearls, gold and sheer fabric, and a dark ground that concentrates the light. The face is more idealized than individual, a Luini type with soft Leonardesque shading at eyes and mouth, and the sitter's character stays elusive. As a record of dress and status it is excellent; as an insight into a person, less so."},"nga:46155":{"d":"Shown from the waist up, a woman with smooth, pale skin looks out from the corners of her wide-set hazel eyes against a nearly black ground, her body angled to the left. Wavy auburn hair cascades down her back, tied in a bow over a thin pink ribbon, with tendrils around her face. Her moss-green gown has a golden-brown panel across the chest and puffed sleeves over a white undergarment, tied with a pink sash. With one hand she holds the foot of an oyster-white lidded vessel like a small urn, and with the other she tips up its conical lid.","i":"The covered jar is the attribute of Mary Magdalene, who by long tradition anointed Christ's feet with ointment and brought spices to his tomb. Her loose, flowing hair recalls both her penitence and, by the same tradition, her earlier life as a sinner. In Lombardy in the 1520s she was a popular subject for private devotion, often painted as a half-length beauty in fashionable dress, so that the picture could be admired as an image of a saint and as a lovely woman at once.","c":"Luini's Magdalen is nearer an elegant lady than a penitent: the sidelong glance, the faint smile and the fashionable gown are those of a Leonardesque beauty, and the saint is identified chiefly by her jar. The shading at the corners of the eyes and lips is an inheritance from Leonardo's sfumato, handled with skill, if with less depth. It is a polished, appealing piece of Milanese devotional glamour, easy to admire and not very demanding."},"nga:261":{"d":"A nearly nude woman reclines on golden-yellow grass, her legs extended to the left and her body turned toward the viewer, her left elbow propped on a swell of ground. She looks off to the left with hazel-brown eyes, her copper-brown hair wound around her head, a teardrop pearl at her ear. With both hands she holds the ends of a translucent cloth wrapped about her hips and back. Red and white fabric and a gold and pearl ornament lie nearby. Behind her are trees, an aquamarine river with an arched bridge, rocky cliffs with towns and indigo mountains under jeweled blue clouds.","i":"The reclining nude Venus is a type created in Venice with Giorgione's Sleeping Venus of about 1510 and developed by Titian. This Lombard variation is awake and looks away, and her pearl and veil are fitting attributes, since Venus was born from the sea and pearls were associated with her. Such pictures were probably made for private rooms, for patrons who enjoyed classical subjects as a pretext for beauty, and who read the goddess as an image of love.","c":"The landscape is the delight: the bridge, the cliffs and the towns glimmer with a jeweled, fantastical color, and the translucent veil is skillfully handled. The Venus herself is demure rather than erotic, her pose a little self-conscious and her anatomy somewhat generalized. Beside the Venetian reclining nudes that inspired it, it is cooler and more decorous, but that restraint is its character: a polished, graceful and slightly detached Lombard poem to beauty."},"met:435668":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 31 by 57 centimetres, painted in tempera and gold on wood, shows Saint Nicholas providing dowries. The Met's keywords record a bedroom, women and the saint, so the scene shows the household in the night when, in the legend, Nicholas threw bags of gold through a window to save three poor sisters from ruin. It matches a companion panel of the saint's story in the same museum in size, date and manner.","i":"In the best-known legend of Nicholas, a poor man with three daughters could not afford their dowries and faced having to send them into prostitution; Nicholas secretly threw a bag of gold into the house on three nights, so that each could marry. The story made him the protector of unmarried girls and underlies later traditions of gift-giving. In Florence, where the city's dowry fund had been set up in 1425, a tale of generous dowries had a topical resonance.","c":"The story's drama, a secret gift at night, gives the painter a chance for a quiet, intimate scene, and Bicci di Lorenzo handles it clearly: the bedroom setting, the saint's gesture and the bright colour create a pleasing narrative. The treatment is gentle and unemphatic, and the space shallow. It is a modest, charming panel typical of Florentine devotional storytelling of the 1430s."},"met:435669":{"d":"A horizontal panel, about 31 by 57 centimetres, painted in tempera and gold on wood, shows Saint Nicholas bringing three youths back to life, in an interior. The Met's keywords record the saint, men and women. In the legend's most familiar form, three boys were murdered by an innkeeper and hidden in a tub, and Nicholas, passing through, prayed and restored them. It matches a companion panel of the saint's story in the same museum.","i":"Nicholas, a fourth-century bishop of Myra in what is now Turkey, became one of the most popular saints of the Middle Ages, protector of children, sailors, merchants and unmarried girls. This miracle, the raising of the three youths, is one reason he is the patron of children. The panels probably formed part of a predella beneath an altarpiece, where the faithful could read his deeds in sequence. Bicci di Lorenzo painted such narratives for Florentine churches in the 1430s.","c":"Bicci di Lorenzo was a conservative Florentine painter who kept the gold, bright colour and graceful figures of late Gothic art while younger artists such as Fra Angelico and Uccello were changing painting. The panel is neat and attractive, with a clear narrative, but the space is shallow, the figures repeat types, and the drama is subdued. It is a pleasant, old-fashioned storytelling piece rather than an innovation."},"met:437180":{"d":"After the Fall, Adam and Eve are confronted by God in a garden, according to the title and the record's motifs: a tree, cattle, a goat, a deity, an angel, and the pair themselves. The picture is an oil on copper, an upright panel of about 68 by 50 centimeters, dated 1740. The museum's record gives no further description of the poses, the colors or how the animals are placed.","i":"Genesis tells that after Adam and Eve ate the forbidden fruit, they heard God walking in the garden and hid, and God called to Adam and questioned him; Adam blamed Eve, and Eve the serpent. The scene of rebuke, in which the shame of the first couple is shown, was less often painted than the Temptation or the Expulsion, and it let painters show a moment of judgment before punishment.","c":"Natoire is a graceful French Rococo history painter, and oil on copper suits him: a smooth, enameled finish and delicate, silvery coloring that would give the Garden a gentle sensuousness. It also shows the limits of Rococo religion: Natoire's God is more courteous than awe-inspiring. A refined cabinet picture rather than a deeply felt one."},"met:437374":{"d":"A portrait of an unidentified woman, painted in oil on a wooden panel about 68 by 58 centimeters and dated 1635. It follows the format of the Dutch bourgeois portrait of its time: a bust or half-length figure against a plain ground, normally in dark clothing relieved by white linen, and often painted as the pendant to a husband's portrait. Ravesteyn was an established portraitist in The Hague, and the clear light and sober presentation are typical of his work for the city's well-to-do families.","i":"In the Dutch Republic of the 1630s prosperous families commissioned portraits as proof of respectability, standing and lineage, to hang in the house and to pass down. Dark dress and white linen signaled sobriety as well as wealth, in a culture wary of show. The sitter's name has not come down with the picture, so it speaks for its type, a respectable woman of the Dutch well-to-do, rather than for an individual life.","c":"Ravesteyn was in his sixties when he painted this, and his strengths were a solid, objective likeness and careful attention to costume rather than drama or psychology. He lacks the dash of Frans Hals and the depth of Rembrandt, and his sitters can seem composed to the point of stiffness. As an example of the careful, sober Hague style that so many families wanted, it is reliable and dignified, if rarely surprising."},"met:437087":{"d":"A woman is portrayed in oil on a wooden panel about 75 by 60 centimeters, and the title singles out her lace collar as the picture's richest detail. The format is the standard one for Mierevelt: a half-length figure against a plain ground, the face modeled in clear, even light, the dress probably dark, as was usual for Dutch sitters of the 1630s. The title does not name her, so she is known by her costume and by the artist's hand.","i":"Fine lace was costly in the 1630s and a clear sign of wealth, and a broad lace collar was a fashionable replacement for the older stiff ruff. Dutch moralists warned against luxury in dress, so the portrait balances the two: a modest dark gown and sober pose set off by one conspicuous marker of status. A portrait like this was meant to hang at home as a record of the family's respectability for those who came after.","c":"Mierevelt's portraits are efficient and polished, and the lace and fabrics are the places where the brush shows most delight. The faces tend toward smooth, slightly masklike finish, which reflects a busy workshop. The result is skilled but formulaic, a faithful record of a person's rank and dress rather than a penetrating study of character, and it explains why Hals and Rembrandt seemed so fresh beside such portraits."},"nga:46095":{"d":"Shown from the waist up in three-quarter view, a woman faces left. She has fair skin, a long nose and narrow pink lips, and her dark brown hair is drawn into a large, smooth bun set with a golden brooch or pin and a strand of lace. Around her neck is a very large lace collar, with several layers of long lace above three strands of pearls. Her dark gown has intricately patterned sleeves, a silver bodice and lace-trimmed cuffs. The background is plain gray.","i":"A broad ruff of lace and a triple strand of pearls were costly and conspicuous marks of status. By 1638 the ruff had been dropped by most men and by fashionable younger women, but older and more conservative women often kept it as a sign of dignity, so it may point to a sitter of the older generation. The title gives no name, so the portrait speaks as a type: a prosperous Dutch matron presented as modest, dignified and rich.","c":"Mierevelt records every texture with care, the layered lace, the pearls and the patterned sleeves, while the face is handled in a smooth, rather generic manner. The plain gray ground leaves nothing to distract from the costume. It is a competent, formal likeness that gives a clear picture of status and propriety; set beside Hals or Rembrandt of the same years, it lacks life, but it was exactly what many sitters wanted."},"met:437085":{"d":"A portrait of a named sitter, Margaretha van Clootwijk, who was about fifty-eight when it was painted in 1639; she was born about 1580 or 1581 and died in 1662. It is oil on a wooden panel about 70 by 58 centimeters, in the format Mierevelt used for most of his sitters: a bust or half-length figure against a plain ground, in the sober dress usual for Dutch sitters. The title records her name and her dates, so she is not anonymous.","i":"A portrait of an older sitter in the Dutch Republic was about dignity and continuity: a record of a respected family member to keep in the house and pass to descendants. Mierevelt was by 1639 about seventy-two and the leading portraitist of Delft, and many of his sitters were families who wanted just such a plain, truthful record. A named sitter with known dates reminds the viewer that these panels were once personal, not only exercises in dress.","c":"Mierevelt's late portraits keep the clear, even light and exact finish of his earlier ones, with faces smoothly modeled and white linen carefully drawn. The result is dependable rather than inspired, and it can seem impersonal beside the lively portraits that Frans Hals was painting in Haarlem. Portraits of older sitters in this tradition record age frankly, which is the Dutch school's great virtue, but Mierevelt's version does not reach for more than clear statement."},"met:437086":{"d":"A portrait of a man, Jacob van Dalen (1570–1644), who was about seventy when it was painted in 1640. It is oil on a wooden panel about 70 by 58 centimeters, and the catalog notes a coat of arms among the things shown, which marks the sitter's family and standing. Mierevelt typically set male sitters against a plain ground, dressed in black relieved by a white collar, with the face modeled in clear light. The sitter is called Vallensis, the Latin form of his name.","i":"A coat of arms in a portrait asserts lineage and standing, and displaying one was a mark of social ambition or established rank in the Dutch Republic. The Latin name Vallensis, meaning of the valley, suggests a learned or professional man, since scholars and physicians often Latinized their names. The portrait presents him at about seventy as a respected elder, and was probably made to hang at home as a record of his family's place.","c":"A stiff, exact document more than a drama: Mierevelt delivers a likeness with clear light, careful finish and a face modeled with great clarity, and the heraldry adds a flourish of status. The strengths are accuracy and sobriety; the weaknesses are a certain dryness and sameness, since his male sitters often seem cast from one mold. Next to the animated portraits of Frans Hals, it feels formal and reserved, but it was the Dutch establishment's preferred style."},"met:437915":{"d":"An upright view into the Oude Kerk, the Old Church of Delft, painted in oil on canvas, about 83 by 66 centimeters, and dated 1660. The catalog keywords name the church interior and a column, which suits the oblique views favored by Delft church painters, with a massive pillar near the viewer and the nave receding beyond. It is a cabinet picture of a public building, made for collectors who prized convincing perspective and the quiet light of whitewashed walls.","i":"After the Reformation, Dutch Reformed churches were stripped of altars and images and whitewashed, and the great medieval buildings became civic monuments as well as places of worship. The Oude Kerk in Delft held the tombs of naval heroes, and Vermeer was buried there in 1675. Paintings of church interiors were not devotional pictures but celebrations of architecture, perspective and civic pride, bought on the open market by townspeople.","c":"Van Vliet belongs to the Delft group of church painters with Houckgeest and Emanuel de Witte, and is generally ranked below de Witte for atmosphere. His strengths are firm architecture, a convincing sense of space and a cool, clear light; his surfaces lack the glowing warmth of de Witte's best. A sober, careful, handsome record of a building, in which the perspective rewards the eye more than any incident."},"nga:58":{"d":"Shown from the hips up, a boy with pale skin and copper-brown hair stands before a slate-gray rocky outcrop, with a narrow view of landscape at the left. His body faces left, but he turns his dark-eyed gaze to the viewer, his hair in waves to a flat white collar. He wears a golden-yellow brocaded doublet, split sleeves showing a white shirt with wide transparent cuffs, and over it a pewter breastplate and a topaz-blue sash. One hand rests on a staff, the other on the gold hilt of a rapier. Pale yellow clouds drift in an ice-blue sky.","i":"The sitter is Henry Stuart, Duke of Gloucester (1640 to 1660), youngest son of Charles I, painted about 1653, when he was twelve or thirteen and living with his exiled family on the Continent after his father's execution. The armor and sword present him as a soldier-prince despite his age, a statement of dynastic pride and hope of restoration. The formula descends from Van Dyck's court portraits, and Hanneman had worked in London in the 1630s. Henry died of smallpox in 1660.","c":"Hanneman adapts Van Dyck's aristocratic formula with competence and some relish for textures: the gleaming breastplate, the shimmering brocade and the filmy cuffs are all handled with pleasure. The boy's face is sweet but guarded, and the pose is correct rather than easy. As a portrait it is polished rather than searching; the Van Dyck recipe is followed faithfully, but without the original's ease and sparkle."},"met:436630":{"d":"A woman is the sole sitter of this oil portrait, painted about 1653 on a canvas about 80 by 64 centimeters, a size suited to a bust or half-length likeness. The title gives no name. In the 1650s Hanneman was one of the fashionable portraitists of The Hague, and his sitters were courtiers and prosperous citizens painted with the courtly elegance he had learned from Van Dyck in England.","i":"Hanneman had worked in England in the 1620s and 1630s, absorbing Van Dyck's manner, and when he returned to The Hague he became the portraitist favored by the Orange court and by the Stuarts in exile. An unnamed woman of the 1650s may be a court lady or the wife of a prosperous Hague citizen; the style offers refinement of dress and bearing as a sign of rank. Her identity, like that of many sitters, has been lost.","c":"Hanneman is the best Dutch interpreter of Van Dyck's elegance, though he lacks his model's nervous finesse, and his sitters can look composed rather than alive. This portrait, from his prime, belongs to the courtly Hague style: refined, orderly and rather unrevealing. Beside the vitality of Frans Hals he seems cool; beside the plain burgher portraits of the time, he is glamorous. Polish is his chief gift."},"met:437352":{"d":"A small cabinet picture, about 37 by 50 centimeters, painted on wood in the later 1630s, showing a party in an interior. The catalog keywords list men and women, drinking and a party, so the subject is revelry among ordinary people, not a portrait or a story from scripture. Quast made a specialty of such lively low-life scenes in small formats, with quick brushwork and slightly caricatured figures meant to amuse a buyer who knew the type.","i":"Merry companies were a staple of Dutch art in the 1630s, and they worked on two levels. They offered the pleasure of a lively, comic scene, and they also carried a warning: drinking and revelry were treated as folly, since pleasures are brief and the cost is paid in health and money. The viewer was invited to laugh at the figures and perhaps to see something of himself, with the moral left unspoken.","c":"Quast's panels are brisk and humorous rather than refined, relying on gesture and expression more than on color or atmosphere. Beside the more polished merry companies of Dirck Hals or Willem Buytewech they look rough, but energy and sly observation are their virtue. A modest, lively period piece, interesting for its lack of pretension; it is not a picture to linger over for subtlety."},"met:437193":{"d":"A woman reads in this small picture of about 38 by 28 centimetres, which the museum classes as a portrait. The record lists only a woman, a portrait and reading, so her dress, pose and the room cannot be described with confidence. The title and the small, upright format are those of the refined cabinet pictures of elegant women at their pastimes, in the manner of the Dutch fine painters, with a polished surface made for the close inspection of collectors.","i":"Eglon van der Neer, son of the landscape painter Aert van der Neer, worked in the Netherlands and later for the Elector Palatine in Düsseldorf, where he died in 1703, the year given for this picture. Women reading, writing or playing music were a staple of Dutch genre painting, a sign of private refinement, and by the end of the century they had become fashionable ornaments in the interiors of the rich. The Met owns the picture through the bequest of Michael Friedsam in 1931.","c":"A polished small picture, typical of the fine painters of Dutch genre in their late phase: smooth, detailed, a little cool, and more interested in the elegance of the woman and her setting than in what she reads. The attraction is the finish; the limitation is a certain porcelain sameness and lack of feeling, for which late Dutch genre has been criticised beside the pictures of Vermeer's day. Pleasant and craftsmanlike, rather than memorable."},"nga:131947":{"d":"Seen from the lap up, a woman in a wide-brimmed hat with a drooping pink feather sits in profile before a low stone wall, looking left. She wears a coral-red bodice patterned in gold, an apricot satin skirt and a translucent white apron, and holds a hoop the size of a head, weaving colorful flowers into it, with more blossoms in her lap. The wall is carved with a sunflower and roses and carries a fountain topped by a stylized fish. Through a gap in the dark trees behind her, a seated couple embrace in a clearing, beneath a distant dome and towers.","i":"A garland of flowers was a traditional emblem of spring, courtship and love, and the embracing couple in the clearing makes the amorous theme plain: the woman weaves her crown either for a lover or in thought of love. The sunflower, a flower that turns toward the sun, was read as a sign of devotion, and the fountain suggests the pleasures of a garden. Flowers also warned of the brevity of beauty, so the picture may mix delight with a quiet caution.","c":"Schalcken, a pupil of Samuel van Hoogstraten and Gerard Dou, shows the Leiden finish at its most polished: the satin skirt, the gauzy apron and the carved wall are rendered with jewel-like care, and the signature trompe l'oeil, as if cut into the stone, is a witty touch. The woman is an idealized type and the symbolism slight, so the picture is pretty rather than profound. Next to Dou's rigor, it favors charm over substance."},"met:437633":{"d":"A canvas about 65 by 80 centimeters showing the mythological lovers Cephalus and Procris, painted probably in the 1680s. The catalog keywords list only a man and a woman, so the picture concentrates on the pair, and the moment most often painted from their story is the death of Procris, struck by the javelin her husband threw by mistake. Schalcken was known for small, smooth, highly finished pictures and candlelit effects, and the format here is that of a cabinet picture for a collector.","i":"The story comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses: Procris, jealous of the rumored mistress Aura, hides in a thicket to spy on her husband Cephalus, who mistakes the rustle for a beast and kills her with the unerring javelin she had given him. In seventeenth-century Holland classical myths of love and loss were favorite subjects for educated collectors, who read them as warnings against jealousy and haste, and enjoyed the chance to see beautiful figures in dramatic poses.","c":"Schalcken's strength is surface finish and effects of light, and a myth of tragic passion needs more emotional weight than his smooth, elegant manner readily supplies. His mythological figures tend to resemble fashionable models in costume, so pathos can come across as polish. A skilled and handsome cabinet picture, but one that offers elegance in place of tragedy, and that rewards the eye more than the heart."},"met:438118":{"d":"A small panel, about 25 by 20 centimeters, showing a huntsman and a peasant woman beside the Isar River with a view of Munich beyond. The catalog keywords add a horse, a boy and a dog, and list riding as a subject, so the huntsman is probably mounted. It is a landscape with figures in the manner of Dutch seventeenth-century cabinet pictures, painted in oil on wood in 1823, with the city on the horizon to anchor the scene in a real place.","i":"Wilhelm von Kobell, court painter in Munich, specialized in small, precise landscapes with horses, hunters and peasants, a Bavarian descendant of the Dutch landscape tradition of Wouwerman and Potter. The Isar runs through Munich, and the view of the city places the encounter in a recognizable home setting. Such pictures expressed local pride and a quiet, orderly view of country life, in the calm Biedermeier years after the Napoleonic wars.","c":"Kobell's cabinet-size pictures are precise and delicate, with an even light and a carefully drawn horse, and the small encounter between rider and woman has a light humor. He is far less ambitious than his Romantic contemporaries such as Caspar David Friedrich, and the tidy, orderly detail can seem a little dry. A charming, slight picture, a good example of a small, well-made, regional landscape."},"met:438629":{"d":"A family scene shows a woman, a man and an infant with vegetables in the room, in an oil on canvas of about 48 by 37 cm, painted about 1820 to 1825. The title names a milestone of early childhood, the first steps. Franz Ludwig Catel, a German who settled in Rome in 1811, specialized in pleasing pictures of Italian everyday life and landscape for northern collectors.","i":"A baby's first steps is one of the stock tender subjects of family life, and early nineteenth-century painters were drawn to such scenes as the Romantic generation made a cult of simple domestic feeling. For German and other northern buyers, Italian settings added a further charm: the poor but contented family under a southern sky. Such pictures sold an ideal of uncomplicated happiness, and they say as much about the buyers' hopes as about Italy.","c":"Catel's gifts are clear composition and a smooth, bright finish, and a family around a child suits them. The vegetables give him a small still life within the genre scene, a chance for crisp detail. The weakness is sweetness: the picture offers happiness as a formula, and the Italian peasant is a type, not a person. It is a pleasing, minor work, expertly arranged and politely sentimental."},"met:438848":{"d":"A narrow street of old Berlin is shown in this upright cityscape of about 41 by 28 centimetres, with the Nikolaikirche, one of the city's oldest churches, among the buildings. The museum's keywords list a road, cobblestones, buildings, a man and a dog, so the street is paved with cobbles and has a person and an animal on it. The record gives no further description, so the light, the façades and the colours cannot be reported with confidence.","i":"Eduard Gaertner was Berlin's leading painter of architectural views, trained in Paris and known for views of the city with exact perspective and fine detail. In 1831 the Parochialstrasse, in the medieval heart of Berlin, was a quiet corner, far from the grand new buildings of Schinkel. Gaertner's streets give Berlin a human scale, where a man and a dog on the cobbles suggest the daily life of the city. The Metropolitan Museum acquired it in 2006.","c":"Gaertner's cityscapes are among the clearest of their time: lines are exact, the perspective is rigorous, and the architecture is rendered with a careful, objective affection. The risk is a certain dryness, since the figures are small and the poetry of old buildings in sunlight is a matter of description rather than feeling. In a vertical street view like this, though, the narrow format makes the perspective itself the drama."},"met:439065":{"d":"A woman and a child are at a table in a conservatory, with flowers, a chair, toys and tea things recorded by the museum, in a small upright interior of only about 24 by 20 centimetres. The record gives no more detail, so the number of family members, their dress and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The glazed sunroom, filled with plants and light, was a fashionable feature of well-off German homes in the 1830s, a place for family tea and quiet pleasure.","i":"The picture belongs to the Biedermeier period, the era of bourgeois domesticity in German-speaking lands between 1815 and 1848, when middle-class families valued home, comfort and the cultivation of flowers. Gaertner, better known for his architectural views of Berlin, here paints a private family world in a small conversation piece, perhaps commissioned by the family. The title names the family of Mr. Westphal, whose members are not identified individually in the record.","c":"A delightful small interior in which Gaertner's precision, usually spent on façades, is turned on a family's private world: the perspective of the conservatory, the plants and the light are likely to be handled with crystalline accuracy, and the intimate scale suits the subject. The figures are probably secondary to the setting, as often with a painter of architecture. It is a gentle, charming Biedermeier document rather than a major picture."},"nga:53595":{"d":"A large pink brick building with grand architecture fills this wide canvas of about 51 by 80 cm, under a sky of white and light blue with fluffy clouds. The details are intricate, with tall windows and arched doorways, and behind the building on the right rises a large clock tower, with smaller buildings along the street in the distance. Crowds of small people gather around it, some dressed as soldiers, and horse-drawn carriages appear at the far left and right. It is dated 1848.","i":"Thorn is the German name of Toruń, then a Prussian provincial city and now in Poland, a Hanseatic town of brick Gothic and the birthplace of Copernicus. Brick Gothic town halls were symbols of the civic pride of such trading cities, and Gaertner, who painted the monuments of Berlin and other places, treated them as portraits of buildings. The soldiers among the crowd reflect the garrison life of a Prussian town in the year of revolutions.","c":"Gaertner's gift is exact architecture rendered in a dry, clear light, and he lets small figures animate the foreground without disturbing the building. The picture is an architectural portrait whose strengths are precision, calm and rich detail. The weakness is a reportorial coolness: nothing in it trembles. His clarity makes this a valuable document of the Toruń of 1848, and a good example of the mid-century taste for the accurate and orderly view."},"nga:166469":{"d":"A tall bouquet of celestial-blue, cream-white and maroon flowers fills a rust-red urn beside a pile of fruit on a brown stone table. Two white tulips veined in wine red top the bouquet, with two ocean-blue irises at the right, bluebells at the back, a white anemone and a white rose below. A second rose and a rosebud lie on the table, with a nest of three speckled eggs behind them, and a red-winged butterfly has landed on a fallen leaf. At the right are pale green grapes, peaches, an orange, a pear and plums, with a cut citrus fruit at the center. Light falls from the left.","i":"Flower pieces of this kind celebrated nature's abundance and also its brevity: the nest with eggs suggests new life, the butterfly transformation, the fallen blossoms mortality. In the tradition Roesen inherited from the Dutch, a bouquet combines flowers that never bloom together, so the picture displays what only art can unite. The date, 1848, is about the time he emigrated from Germany to the United States, where such abundant still lifes found eager buyers.","c":"Roesen, who began as a painter on porcelain, brings that craft's precision to the delicate veining of the tulips and the gleam of the grapes. The arrangement is crowded and a little static, lacking the compositional daring of the great Dutch flower painters, but the freshness of the colors and the sheer profusion are persuasive. A bright, confident example of his early manner, polished and somewhat decorative."},"met:11938":{"d":"A large still life, about 102 by 128 centimeters, combining fruit and flowers. The catalog keywords list a pineapple, a vase, irises, a lily, a bird's nest with eggs and other fruit, the elements Roesen liked to combine in a single crowded display. It is dated 1850 to 1855 and is a showpiece for a parlor or dining room, with its profusion intended to dazzle by its detail.","i":"The pineapple was a luxury fruit, long a symbol of hospitality and wealth, and in an American picture of the 1850s it signaled refinement as much as plenty. The bird's nest and eggs, a recurring motif in Roesen's work, suggest new life and a safe home, while the blossoms hint at beauty that fades. Together the objects say that the good life is abundant, domestic and rather fragile.","c":"This is Roesen at his most lavish, balancing exotic and native fruit with flowers and a nest in a single crowded display. The finish is smooth and the colors bright, and the contrast of a spiky pineapple with soft petals gives his skill with texture room to work. As in much of his work, the density makes the picture busy and the eye finds few places to rest. Handsome, abundant and a little overwhelming."},"met:11939":{"d":"A wide still life, about 91 by 127 centimeters, in which the catalog keywords list grapes, peaches, strawberries and a lemon heaped together. The format is that of a fruit piece in Roesen's usual manner: a table or ledge laden with ripe fruit, built up in a pyramid or a spreading mass and painted with a smooth, glistening finish. It is dated 1855, in the years after he had settled in the United States, and was meant as a handsome decoration for a well-furnished room.","i":"A picture of abundant fruit was an emblem of prosperity, and Americans in the 1850s, enjoying a growing economy and plentiful food, liked such displays as signs of a thriving household. Fruit pieces also carried an older moral note, since ripeness leads to decay, though Roesen's pictures give far more weight to delight than to warning. Lemons and other imported fruit also signaled trade and wealth.","c":"Roesen painted many such compositions, and their repetition can make them seem formulaic: the same lavish heaps, the same smooth, bright surface. Yet within the formula the texture is expertly rendered, the bloom on grapes and the velvet of peaches being his specialties, and the large format gives the abundance real presence. A showpiece of nineteenth-century American decorative taste that is better admired for its craft than its invention."},"met:18819":{"d":"A fruit still life with grapes, other fruit and a glass of wine, painted in oil on board and recorded with its gold leaf frame as part of the object. The dating, 1865 to 1870, places it late in Roesen's career. Pictures of this type show a table or ledge set with ripe fruit and a drinking glass, painted with a smooth, bright finish. The gilded frame was made to match the richness of the contents and to give the picture the look of a luxury object.","i":"Grapes and wine are an old pairing in still life, recalling feasting, the pleasures of the senses and, in a Christian reading, the wine of communion. In the 1860s such a picture, in its gold frame, belonged to the self-image of a prosperous household that could enjoy fine fruit and wine. Whether any moral or religious overtone was meant here is hard to say, but the subject carried those associations for educated viewers.","c":"Late Roesen can repeat itself, and a smaller panel like this may seem a variation on a formula. What it shows is how a skilled craftsman can make a restricted subject glitter, with the gleam of glass and the bloom on grapes as his tools. The gold frame is part of the effect, announcing the picture as a treasure. Competent and decorative rather than adventurous."},"met:18820":{"d":"A small still life, about 41 by 51 centimeters, in which strawberries are heaped in a compote, a footed dish, with grapes, peaches and leaves around it. It is painted in oil on board and recorded with its gold leaf frame, and dated 1865 to 1870. Compared with Roesen's large showpieces, this is an intimate picture, with a few objects rendered in the same smooth, bright, detailed manner that made his reputation.","i":"Strawberries were prized for their sweetness and short season, which made them, in earlier Dutch and Flemish still life, an emblem often read as fleeting pleasure. In a nineteenth-century American parlor the dish suggested refinement as well as plenty, a compote being the kind of tableware found in comfortable homes. The gold frame, part of the object, presented the picture as a treasure meant to be kept and admired.","c":"The smaller scale suits Roesen better than the big banquet pieces, because the grouping is less crowded and the details, the red of the berries and the soft bloom on the grapes can be enjoyed one by one. It does not break new ground, and the arrangement is conventional, but it is graceful and well made. A pleasing late work, quiet in ambition and sure in craft."},"nga:214133":{"d":"Ripe peaches in orange, pink and yellow are stacked closely in the center, with clusters of dark purple and light green grapes on top of and around them. A thin stick with green leaves rests across the peaches. The table is a dark red-and-gray marbled surface, with a white cloth edged in gold draped over the right side, and the wall behind is a gradient from brown at left to pale orange at right. The panel is small, about 35 by 46 centimeters, and undated.","i":"Fruit stacked on a marble ledge is an old still-life arrangement, linking luxury and the senses, and Roesen turned it into a staple of American parlor taste in the mid-nineteenth century. The white cloth edged in gold and the marble table suggest a prosperous house, the fruit an abundant harvest. As with most still lifes of ripe fruit, a quiet note of time and decay lies under the surface, though the picture gives more weight to pleasure.","c":"The small panel concentrates Roesen's gifts: the soft blushing skin of the peaches, the bloom on the grapes and the stack's tight, convincing mass are all handled with practiced ease, and the plain gradient wall keeps attention on the fruit. The treatment is conventional and the composition modest, but a small panel suits him and the result is bright, direct and agreeable. A confident, if not original, piece."},"met:10966":{"d":"A man and a woman, a pair of lovers, are shown with a musical instrument in an idealized classical setting; the title makes them Greek. The canvas is about 102 by 131 centimeters and dated 1846. No specific myth is named, so the picture is a romantic fantasy of antique courtship rather than an illustration of a text, and the instrument, in the catalog keywords, makes music the language of their feeling.","i":"Henry Peters Gray belonged to the generation of American artists who studied the Old Masters in Italy and returned with a taste for allegory and idealized beauty. Anonymous Greek lovers let a painter treat love in a classical setting with refinement and without the awkwardness of a real story, and Greek subjects were fashionable in the 1840s. Music, long associated with courtship, completes the picture of an idyllic antiquity.","c":"Gray's skill lies in smooth, warm color and soft, generalized forms, and he is known to have admired Venetian color, but his forms lack its weight and force. The picture is gentle and agreeable and a little sugary, and the lovers are types rather than people. Today it reads as an artifact of 1840s idealism, better as a document of American taste than as a lasting statement about love."},"met:10968":{"d":"A large horizontal canvas, about 123 by 194 centimeters, painted in 1848. The catalog lists a soldier, a man, a woman and a child among the figures, so the picture is a narrative group, not a portrait. The title is a moral statement: wages are what is paid, and the likely payers are ordinary families. Painted as the Mexican-American War was ending, it would have had topical force for American viewers.","i":"The title recalls the biblical phrase “the wages of sin,” so war is presented as a wrong whose payment is suffering. Moral pictures of this kind appealed to American audiences of the 1840s, who expected art to improve as well as please. The presence of a woman, a child and an older man suggests that the cost falls on the family at home, and the soldier names the cause.","c":"Gray's moral allegories tend to be earnest and theatrical, with gesture and sentiment standing in for subtlety, and a large canvas leaves little room to hide that. It is a serious attempt at an unusual subject, an anti-war picture in a country at war, and the ambition deserves notice. It does not have the savage candor of Goya's Disasters of War, and relies on the era's didactic idiom."},"met:10967":{"d":"A small sentimental picture of country life, about 77 by 64 centimeters, painted in 1858 to 1859. The title names the pride of a village, and the catalog keywords list a man, a woman and a dog, so the subject is an anecdote from rural life, not a portrait or a history. Gray, who had studied in Italy, brings a smooth, European idealizing finish to an American rustic subject, with soft modeling and warm color.","i":"Village scenes of gentle sentiment were popular in mid-nineteenth-century America, a country that was urbanizing quickly and liked to picture a simple, virtuous countryside. The picture belongs to the years just before the Civil War, when such images offered comfort and a shared ideal of home. The dog adds a note of loyalty and warmth, the stock companion of rural virtue in this kind of subject.","c":"A modest, polished exercise in sentiment. Where contemporaries such as William Sidney Mount and George Caleb Bingham looked hard at American types, Gray tends to borrow European conventions of idealized rusticity, so the figures seem posed rather than observed. The finish is accomplished and the mood pleasant, but the picture offers little that is vivid or surprising."},"met:437939":{"d":"A small interior, about 61 by 91 centimeters, painted in oil on wood in 1824, showing a Highland family at home. The catalog keywords list a woman, a man, an infant and a dog in a cottage room, so the picture is a genre scene, an observed everyday moment with a family at its center, rather than a portrait. Wilkie's early manner, learned from Dutch cabinet pictures, shows in the careful detail and the clear arrangement of figures.","i":"Wilkie made his name with small scenes of Scottish rural life, and after Sir Walter Scott's novels and George IV's visit to Edinburgh in 1822 made the Highlands fashionable, Scottish subjects found eager buyers in England. A picture like this presented a poor household with dignity and affection, appealing to urban viewers who looked back with nostalgia to a simpler life, and who enjoyed the story told by each figure.","c":"Wilkie's genre pictures are masterly in narrative clarity and humane in feeling, giving each figure character and a rich Dutch-inspired finish. This is a good example of his careful observation, though the sentiment sometimes drifts toward the picturesque. Within a year he left for a long tour of the Continent that would change his style, so the picture belongs to the end of his early, tightly finished manner."},"nga:46066":{"d":"Three figures sit closely together, all looking at an open book: two adults in flowing, light-colored garments and head coverings, with serene expressions, and a child leaning forward with his back to the viewer. The backdrop is a textured sky, golden yellow at the top, darkening to earthy tones below. The panel is small, about 26 by 19 centimeters, oil on canvas laid on wood, and dated 1841. The title names the group as the Holy Family with Saint Elizabeth and the infant Saint John the Baptist.","i":"Elizabeth, the Virgin's older cousin, and her son John, later called the Baptist, were often painted with the infant Christ, linking the two families and the two missions. A book at the center of the group suggests scripture, and the picture of a mother or kinswoman teaching a child to read was a favorite devotional image. The date, 1841, is the year Wilkie died on the voyage home from the Near East, where he had gone to study biblical settings.","c":"A tiny, quiet, almost sketch-like picture whose golden sky gives the group a glow, and whose head-bent trio around a book is both domestic and devotional. Wilkie's late religious works were controversial, critics often finding the broad handling coarse, and this small panel is better judged as a study than a finished statement. It succeeds in tenderness and intimacy, less in refinement of detail."},"met:441227":{"d":"The Holy Family, Mary, Joseph and the Christ Child, is joined by the infant Saint John the Baptist in an upright oil painting on wood about 88 by 65 centimeters. The catalog keywords list a bird among the elements, perhaps the goldfinch that Renaissance painters often placed in the hands of the two children. Perino del Vaga painted it in the mid-1520s, in the sculptural, graceful manner he had learned as a young assistant of Raphael.","i":"John, Christ's infant cousin and the patron saint of Florence, often joins the Holy Family in Florentine and Roman painting. A bird in the hands of the children, if a goldfinch, usually foretells the Passion, because legend says the bird fed on thistles and took a thorn from Christ's crown. The play of the children is tender but ominous, and Mary and Joseph look on with protective care.","c":"Perino takes Raphael's idiom, elegant figures, flowing drapery and affectionate relationships, and stretches it toward early Mannerism with more sinuous poses and brighter color. As a devotional picture it is polished and sophisticated, though perhaps overly refined, with elegant gesture in place of deep feeling. A fine example of the generation that inherited Raphael's workshop and made something more mannered of it."},"nga:46130":{"d":"Five men and two women gather around the naked infant Christ, who lies on a white cloth on grass at the foot of the picture. Kneeling around him are a man in a rose-pink robe with crossed arms and a staff topped by a cross, a crowned woman in green and pink holding a palm frond, and Mary in apple-red with a billowing green and silver cloak. Four men stand behind, one pierced by arrows and raising his fingers over the child. Above, God the Father blesses from a cloud among child angels; a man carrying a lamb climbs steps at right.","i":"The central event is the Nativity, but the group is joined by saints: the arrow-pierced figure is Saint Sebastian, the plague saint, and the man with the cross-topped staff is probably John the Baptist. This mixture of the Nativity with a gathering of saints was common in sixteenth-century altarpieces. The man with the lamb recalls the shepherds and Christ as the Lamb of God, and the Father above gives divine approval. The signature, in the Latin imperfect tense, says Perino the Florentine was making this work.","c":"A virtuoso, crowded, richly colored altarpiece, combining Raphael's grace with the sinuous, elongated elegance of early Mannerism. The color is bright and the gestures are intricate, with figures turning and pointing in a way that pleases the eye. The effect is accomplished but somewhat artificial, and the infant is almost lost among the showy supporting cast. It dazzles more than it moves, which is a fair verdict on much Mannerist painting."},"nga:285":{"d":"Mary stands over her sleeping infant in a small upright panel, only about 21 by 17 centimetres. Her head tilts down, her fingertips meet in prayer, and she wears a moss-green robe lined with dark blue over a crimson tunic with a gold collar. The nude Child lies on a blue cloth over a ledge, propped on cushions, eyes closed. Behind them a landscape of green hills and pale mountains includes a shepherd leaning on a staff beside his sheep and a path winding through tall trees. The painter signed the ledge.","i":"Mary's prayer over the sleeping Child shows her recognising her son as God incarnate. The Child's sleep is often read as a foretaste of his death, and the ledge on which he lies can recall a tomb or altar. The shepherd in the distance echoes the shepherds of the Nativity, and perhaps Christ as the Good Shepherd. Small panels like this were made for private devotion, and Venetian painters of the early sixteenth century, Basaiti among them, followed Giovanni Bellini's model of sweet half-length Madonnas against open landscape.","c":"A tender small devotional picture whose appeal lies in its landscape and its quiet. The calm of the hills and the shepherd lend the figure group a natural, outdoor air, and the greens, crimson and blue are pleasing. Basaiti follows Bellini's formulas faithfully but without his depth of feeling, and the faces are conventional. The cracks across the sky and surface also reduce the effect today. It is a pleasant, minor work by a competent follower."},"met:437968":{"d":"Peasants gather at a fountain in a pastoral landscape, with women among the figures. The oil on canvas, about 79 by 121 cm, is a horizontal picture of a size suited to a drawing room, library or dining room. Francesco Zuccarelli, born in Tuscany and based mainly in Venice, with long stays in England, built his reputation on such cheerful, idealized country scenes, in which figures, trees and water are arranged for charm rather than topographic truth.","i":"Pastoral landscapes of this kind present an Arcadian countryside where laborers are content and nature is gentle. The fountain, a natural meeting place, gives an excuse for groups of figures to chat, drink and rest. English collectors, who knew the Italian landscape tradition from the Grand Tour and from Claude, were among Zuccarelli's best customers, and such a picture brought home a sunlit, ideal Italy. It tells no story; it offers a mood.","c":"Zuccarelli's landscapes are easily dismissed as decorative, and they were meant to be; what keeps them alive is the lightness of touch, the silvery tonality and the graceful rhythm of trees and figures. The weakness is formula: groups of peasants and trees repeat from picture to picture, and the feeling is more pleasant than deep. At its best it is a gentle, well-bred pastoral, the Rococo taste for the countryside as a garden."},"met:438951":{"d":"A small oil study, about 23 by 30 centimeters, of an evening by Lake Arresø in northern Zealand, Denmark's largest lake. The catalog keywords list a man, a dog and the lake, so a figure and his dog stand in a quiet, open scene. The date, about 1837, makes it an early work by Lundbye, then about nineteen. It is probably the kind of small oil sketch Danish painters of the time made directly from the motif, with attention to the light of a summer evening.","i":"Lundbye belonged to the Danish Golden Age and sought a native landscape: heath, lakes and farmland near Copenhagen, painted as an expression of national feeling. After the loss of Norway in 1814 and the state bankruptcy of 1813, Danish painters turned to the humble countryside of their own small nation as a source of identity. Arresø, a wide, shallow lake, offered just the kind of quiet, unassuming scenery that suited his temperament.","c":"A modest, tender sketch whose strength is truth to a mood: the low horizon, the still water and the figure and dog as human scale. As with several Danish Golden Age painters, Lundbye's small studies are often more convincing than his finished pictures, because the direct observation has not been tidied. It shows promise cut short, since he died in 1848, aged twenty-nine."},"met:435900":{"d":"A monumental altarpiece, more than three meters tall, showing the Adoration of the Shepherds with Saint Catherine of Alexandria. The record lists Mary, Saint Joseph, the Christ Child, sheep, an angel and Catherine, so the saint appears among the holy figures at the manger, an anachronism typical of devotional altarpieces. The canvas is dated 1599, and the museum gives no further description of the poses, light or colors.","i":"The adoration of the shepherds follows Luke's account: shepherds watching their flocks by night were told by angels of Christ's birth and went to see him. Catherine, the learned virgin martyr of fourth-century Alexandria, usually carries a wheel and a palm, and her presence suggests that the altarpiece was made for a church, chapel or confraternity dedicated to her. Cigoli, a Florentine and a friend of Galileo, was a leading painter of the generation that reformed Florentine art around 1600.","c":"Cigoli replaced the cool, twisting Mannerism of his predecessors with a warmer, softer naturalism, learned from Correggio and Barocci, and this ambitious altarpiece shows his aims: a clear, sacred drama with human tenderness. The scale of the picture allows a grand design, though a large canvas can be sweeter than it is strong. It stands for a transitional moment between late Mannerism and the Baroque."},"met:437649":{"d":"A half-length image of Christ giving a blessing and holding a book, painted in tempera on a small wood panel, about 39 by 24 centimeters, against a burnished gold ground. The catalog keywords call it the Savior of the World, a type that shows Christ facing the viewer as teacher and judge. Painted about 1311 in Siena, it was probably made as part of a larger altarpiece or as a small devotional image, to be seen by candlelight in a church or a private chapel.","i":"Christ blessing and holding the book of the Gospels derives from Byzantine icons, which Sienese painters such as Duccio adapted with a softer, more human touch. The book marks him as teacher and judge, the raised hand as the Latin Church's gesture of blessing, and the gold ground as heavenly light, setting him outside earthly space. For a worshiper the image was not a portrait but a presence, addressing the viewer directly.","c":"Segna di Buonaventura was a close follower of Duccio, the founder of the Sienese school, and his work shows the master's soft elegance in a somewhat more provincial version. This panel is a sound, reverent devotional image, delicately drawn and effective through gold, line and subdued color. It is less inventive than Duccio's own work, but its fine detail and quiet intensity reward a close look."},"met:437650":{"d":"A tall, narrow gold-ground panel, about 153 by 67 centimeters, showing the Virgin with the Christ Child, painted in tempera about 1320 in Siena. The catalog keywords also name Saint Peter and Saint Paul, who probably appear in smaller images above or beside the Virgin, as in a polyptych, a multipart altarpiece. Panels of this kind stood on altars as images of worship, and the gold ground, burnished to glow in candlelight, set the figures apart from ordinary space.","i":"The Madonna and Child was the central image of Sienese devotion, since the city regarded the Virgin as its protector and consecrated itself to her. Duccio's Maestà of 1311 on the cathedral altar had set the model, and painters like Segna kept its types alive. Saints Peter and Paul, the apostles on whom the Church was founded, would have stood beside her as intercessors, joining the viewer's prayers to the holy company.","c":"Segna repeated Duccio's formulas with fine gold work and tender gestures, but his Madonna is less subtle in modeling and drawing than his master's. The pleasure here lies in the delicate line, the Child's affectionate pose and the glow of the gold. It is a good example of how long the prestige of Duccio's style lasted, a decade after the Maestà, and of how a workshop could keep it going."},"met:437651":{"d":"A single saint, John the Evangelist, stands on a gold ground, painted in tempera on a panel about 69 by 42 centimeters, about 1320. The catalog lists only the saint, so the panel is probably one of the side saints of a larger altarpiece, a polyptych, in which each saint occupies a separate compartment. John is usually shown youthful and beardless, holding the book of his Gospel, and the burnished gold behind him makes him a timeless presence rather than a figure in a place.","i":"John the Evangelist, the apostle whom Jesus loved, was traditionally the author of the fourth Gospel and of the Book of Revelation. His emblem is the eagle, the bird that soars highest, like his theology. In an altarpiece, saints stand beside the central Virgin or Christ as witnesses and intercessors, so the image presents him as a guarantor of the faith, close to the devotions of the person praying before the altar.","c":"A good example of how Sienese painters turned a single standing saint into a calm, dignified presence: line, gold and soft color do the work, and the modeling is limited. The gestures are conventional, and the effect comes from graceful design rather than psychological depth. A solid, modest example of Segna's craft, rewarding for its delicacy and for the survival of its fine gold surface."},"met:442749":{"d":"A tall panel, about 125 by 53 centimeters, on a gold ground, showing Saint Silvester Gozzolini in tempera, dated to the 1320s. The catalog keywords list a man and angels and link the saint to the Benedictine tradition, so he is shown as a monk. The format, a single standing figure on a narrow panel, suggests that it was once a compartment of a larger altarpiece. Segna, a Sienese painter, worked in the Trecento manner of gold, line and soft color.","i":"Silvester Gozzolini (1177 to 1267) founded the Silvestrine Benedictine order near Fabriano in 1231. This panel was painted more than fifty years after his death, an early image of a holy man still within the memory of his order. He was venerated locally long before his formal canonization, which came only in 1890, so the picture shows how religious orders promoted the cult of a founder, probably for a church of his congregation.","c":"Segna's saints are quiet, well-drawn figures, and here the subject's rarity gives the panel interest beyond its style. It is an early image of the order's founder, though probably not a true likeness, distinguished by a direct, hieratic presence. As painting it is conventional Sienese Trecento work, with limited modeling and set gestures, but as a document of a local founder's cult it is valuable."},"met:442750":{"d":"Saint Benedict stands on a gold ground, painted in tempera on a tall panel about 125 by 53 centimeters and dated to the 1320s. The catalog keywords list a book and angels as well as the saint, so he probably holds the volume of his Rule, as monk saints usually do. The panel matches the Met's Saint Silvester Gozzolini by the same painter in size and date, and the two probably come from one altarpiece, where each saint filled his own narrow compartment.","i":"Benedict of Nursia (about 480 to 547) wrote the Rule that organized Western monastic life around prayer, work and obedience. The Silvestrine order, founded by Silvester Gozzolini, followed his Rule, so the two saints make a natural pair. A book is Benedict's usual attribute and stands for the Rule itself. Placed beside Silvester in an altarpiece, he would show the younger order's founder as heir to the patriarch of Western monks.","c":"A calm, conventional image in which gold ground, long vertical lines of the habit and a gentle face do the work, in the restrained Sienese manner of the early fourteenth century. Segna's figures have little weight, but their sober dignity suits a monastic saint. The panel's interest lies partly in its role within a larger altarpiece; taken alone it is quietly competent rather than memorable."},"met:437276":{"d":"A tiny gold-ground panel, about 22 by 20 centimeters, shows the Virgin and Child with six saints. The catalog names Saint George, Saint Jerome, Saint Anthony the Great and Saint Catherine among the men and women gathered around the Virgin. Painted in tempera in the late 1440s, it is small enough to be held in the hand, a devotional picture for a private room or perhaps a fragment of a larger work.","i":"The Virgin and Child with saints around them is a sacra conversazione in miniature, in which holy figures from different centuries share one space and one quiet moment. Each saint is known by his attributes: George the soldier who killed the dragon, Jerome the scholar and penitent, Anthony the desert hermit, Catherine the learned princess and martyr. In a private panel the saints were often the owner's patrons, chosen for personal protection.","c":"Pesellino, grandson of a painter and a close associate of Fra Filippo Lippi, specialized in small, finely painted pictures, and this gold-ground miniature is a jewel in that spirit: delicate, clear and charming in its crowd of tiny figures. What it lacks is monumentality, which hardly matters at this size. It is best enjoyed up close, as the private object it was meant to be."},"nga:250":{"d":"Christ hangs on a cross, crowned with thorns, his head bowed, blood running from his hands, side and feet. A scroll above reads INRI, and above it a white pelican pecks its own breast in a green nest. Two barefoot, bald-headed men kneel at the foot of the cross, one in an ivory tunic holding beads and a stone, with a red mark on his chest, the other in a brown hooded cloak; a skull and two bones lie between them. Behind rise a barren pink mountain and a walled city with a gold dome, under a flaming red sun at one side and a black disk at the other.","i":"The pelican, said in legend to feed its young with its own blood, was a medieval symbol of Christ's sacrifice. The kneeling saints are Jerome, who struck his chest with a stone in penance in the desert, and Francis, in the brown habit of his order. The skull at the foot of the cross is Adam's, since legend says Christ was crucified over Adam's grave and redeemed him. The darkened sun and moon mark the darkness that fell at the Crucifixion.","c":"Pesellino's gift was for small, precise, jewel-like pictures, and this panel shows it: the clear colors, the vivid blood and the neatly drawn city are set out with a miniaturist's care. The greenish flesh, probably a green underpaint showing through, is typical of Florentine tempera. The Crucifixion is treated with elegance more than agony, and the symmetrical design is somewhat static, but as an image for private devotion it is effective and exquisitely finished."},"nga:166499":{"d":"A crowd of players in vibrant brown, orange, yellow, pink and green clothing, decorated with feathers and silver jewellery, lunge towards a small ball on the ground. The figures have light brown skin and long dark hair, many with feathers in it, and those at the middle of the picture hold sticks with small nets at the end. Behind them rises a dramatic landscape of tall purple mountains and green hills under large pink-and-white clouds sweeping across a blue sky. The canvas is about 71 by 103 centimetres, and the museum dates it to 1851.","i":"Seth Eastman was a US Army officer who served at Fort Snelling in Minnesota, near the Dakota, or Sioux, and made hundreds of drawings and paintings of Native American life, including illustrations for Henry Schoolcraft's government survey of the tribes. The ball game shown is a stick-and-ball sport related to lacrosse, important in the social and ceremonial life of many Native nations. His pictures offered white audiences an ethnographic record, though from an outsider's view.","c":"The energy of the scrimmage is well caught: the converging bodies, flung arms and sticks, and bright costumes give the game real life, and the mountain backdrop adds a Romantic grandeur. The mountains suggest a composed rather than observed landscape, and the swirl of figures can verge on decorative pattern. As an ethnographic painting it is valuable; as an artwork it is lively but formulaic."},"nga:46142":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 153 by 236 centimeters, painted about 1544, when Tintoretto was in his mid-twenties. The subject is the conversion of Saul on the road to Damascus. Telling the story requires a fallen rider, a startled escort and a vision from the sky, and Tintoretto's early manner favored just such drama, with strong diagonals, foreshortened poses and sharp contrasts of light. The horizontal format gives room for a sweeping, theatrical scene.","i":"In the Acts of the Apostles Saul, a persecutor of Christians, is struck down on the road to Damascus by a blinding light and hears Christ's voice asking why he is persecuted. He is baptized, becomes Paul and carries the Gospel to the Gentiles. The horse from which he is traditionally shown falling is not in the text, but later artists made it standard. The sudden, undeserved conversion was a model of divine grace.","c":"A young painter's ambitious picture, in which Tintoretto absorbs Michelangelo's muscular foreshortening and Titian's color in a bid for attention. The energy is more convincing than the detail, and the composition is not yet as controlled as in his mature work. It nevertheless shows the appetite for movement, scale and sudden light that would define him, and is more interesting as an announcement than as a finished statement."},"nga:46189":{"d":"A wide horizontal canvas, about 106 by 193 centimeters, painted about 1546 to 1548. The title names the season, so the subject is an allegory of Summer in the Renaissance tradition of the Four Seasons, usually a personified figure or a scene of harvest. The wide format suggests a decorative picture, perhaps for a wall or ceiling in a Venetian house, the kind of secular commission by which the young Tintoretto earned part of his living.","i":"Personifications of the seasons were popular for decorating houses in the sixteenth century, linking the cycle of the year to labor, abundance and the stages of life. Summer is the season of harvest and ripeness, often tied to the goddess Ceres and shown with ears of grain. For wealthy Venetians with land on the mainland, such images flattered a culture of villas and estates, and offered classical learning in a pleasing form.","c":"Early Tintoretto, in which strong, rapid drawing and a taste for Michelangelesque bodies already show through the decorative task. As an allegory it lacks the urgency of his religious pictures, and its original setting is lost, so the whole must be imagined. It is lively decoration, more interesting for what it shows of a young painter finding his way than for its subject, and best seen as a sketch of ideas to come."},"met:437821":{"d":"A very wide canvas, about 155 by 408 centimeters, showing the miracle in which Christ feeds a multitude with five loaves and two fishes. The catalog lists Christ, bread, fish, men, women, a dog and a landscape, and landscape is evidently a major part of the picture, a broad setting through which a crowd is spread. The format, more than four meters long, suggests a decorative picture for a large room, and the date, about 1545 to 1550, places it among Tintoretto's early works.","i":"The story, told in all four Gospels, has Jesus bless five loaves and two fishes and feed some five thousand people, with twelve baskets of scraps left over. The miracle foreshadows the Eucharist, the bread that sustains the faithful. The dog and the families in the crowd bring everyday life into the scene, reminding the viewer that Christ's first audience was an ordinary hungry crowd, not an assembly of the learned.","c":"The unusual shape gives Tintoretto room for a landscape setting more spread out than the tightly packed groups of his later religious work. A loose crowd and broad sky are interesting, if a little diffuse, and the picture reads more as an experiment than a resolved statement. It shows his early debt to Venetian landscape and to the new freedom of handling, and its scale is its most striking feature."},"nga:12208":{"d":"Shown from the knees up, a bearded man in black turns slightly to the left yet looks at the viewer from the corners of his hazel eyes. He wears a voluminous velvet cloak trimmed with sable-brown fur, rests one arm on a stone ledge carved with leaves and holds a brown glove in the other hand. Green drapery fills the right half of the background; at left a window opens on a deep landscape in tan, gold and bronze, with hills, a fence, steps, perhaps aqueducts and a castle-like complex beneath a pale sky.","i":"The sitter is unknown. A black velvet robe edged with fur marks a Venetian of rank, probably a patrician or senior official, and the glove and carved ledge are conventional signs of status and refinement. The landscape seen through a window was a formula Venetian portraitists used to place a sitter in the wider world, and might refer to estates on the mainland or simply add elegance. Portraits like this hung in the family house.","c":"Tintoretto's portraits, painted alongside his great narrative cycles, are direct and restrained: the sidelong glance, the strong modeling of the face against the dark cloak and the warm distant landscape give the sitter a quiet authority. Compared with Titian's more flattering portraits they are plainer and more severe. This one shows his gift for character with a minimum of display, and the landscape window adds air without distracting from the face."},"met:437822":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed man, painted in the 1550s on a canvas about 113 by 89 centimeters, a size usually used for three-quarter-length portraits. Tintoretto painted many Venetian officials and gentlemen in this period, often in dark clothing against a plain or draped ground, with the face the focus of the picture. The title gives no name, so the sitter is known only through the likeness.","i":"Venetian office and rank were visible in costume: the black robes of the patrician class marked belonging to the governing elite, so dark dress did not mean modesty but status. Portraits of this kind hung in family palaces or were kept by relatives as records of the sitter's standing. The identity of this man is not recorded, but the format, the clothes and the grave bearing tell of a person of weight in the city.","c":"Tintoretto's portraits depend on a few strong decisions: lighting the face with directness, letting the dark clothing recede, and leaving a human glance. They are less flattering than Titian's and less decorative than Veronese's, and the best of them convey a plain, unadorned presence. Without a name to hang on it, this one stands or falls on that directness, which is its main virtue, and its likeness seems built to last."},"met:437820":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 78 by 134 centimeters, probably of the 1560s, showing the Finding of Moses. Pharaoh's daughter and her attendants discover the infant Moses in a basket among the reeds of the Nile. The catalog lists women and Moses, and Tintoretto, as often, would have set the scene in a Venetian-looking landscape, with figures in lively, twisting poses. The size suits a room in a private house rather than a church.","i":"In the book of Exodus, when Pharaoh ordered Hebrew baby boys drowned, Moses's mother hid him in a basket of reeds on the river. Pharaoh's daughter found him, took pity and raised him as her own, so the future liberator of Israel grew up in the house of the oppressor, saved by a woman's compassion. Christians read his rescue from the water as a foreshadowing of baptism, and Venetian painters liked the chance to show women, water and landscape together.","c":"Tintoretto's smaller canvases are brisk, with a quick, flickering brush that suits the story's mix of tenderness and bustle, and the subject fits his gift for women's groups and light on water and foliage. Some passages are merely indicated, and the picture has less drive than his large religious cycles. A lively, fairly minor work, valuable as an example of his domestic scale rather than his highest ambitions."},"nga:46143":{"d":"A very large canvas, about 216 by 416 centimeters, painted about 1575, showing Doge Alvise Mocenigo with members of his family kneeling before the Madonna and Child. It is a votive picture, in which patrons present themselves to the Virgin in a vision, and Venetian state portraiture of this kind joins likeness and devotion. The sheer size and horizontal format suggest it was made for a major interior, where the whole group could be read at once.","i":"Alvise I Mocenigo was doge from 1570 to 1577, the years of the Battle of Lepanto in 1571 and of the great plague of 1575 to 1577, which killed a large part of Venice's population. Votive pictures asked for divine protection, and also advertised the family's piety and dynastic standing by placing the doge's relatives with him in the Virgin's presence. Mary was Venice's great intercessor, and images of her appeared in homes, offices and churches.","c":"Tintoretto's portraits of Venetian rulers are direct and sympathetic, while his heavenly figures are swirled with drama, and a large group like this puts both manners side by side. The best passages are likely the individual heads and the ordered grouping. Such commissions required fidelity to the patron's wishes and probably workshop help, so the whole is more dutiful than inspired, but it is a fine record of state piety."},"met:437819":{"d":"The aged Doge Alvise Mocenigo (1507 to 1577) is presented to Christ as the Redeemer by Saint Mark the Evangelist, whose winged lion and an angel also appear, according to the catalog keywords. The canvas, about 97 by 198 centimeters, is dated probably 1577, the doge's last year. It is a votive image, in which the head of the Republic is shown in humble devotion under the protection of the city's patron saint, in a wide format that suits a ceremonial room.","i":"Mocenigo was doge from 1570, through the Battle of Lepanto and the plague of 1575 to 1577. In 1576 the Senate vowed to build the church of the Redentore, the Redeemer, in thanksgiving for the end of the plague, which made devotion to Christ under that title especially Venetian in these years. Saint Mark, the city's patron, presents the doge as he does in many state pictures, and the lion and angel complete the heavenly court.","c":"Tintoretto's votive pictures for the state combine a sober portrait of the ruler with dynamic, theatrical heavenly figures. The doge's head is likely the most carefully observed passage, while angel and lion supply movement, and the broad handling reflects the speed of his late manner. It succeeds as civic piety, a public statement of a state's humility before God, though it moves less as private feeling."},"nga:41694":{"d":"A portrait of an unnamed Venetian, shown as a procurator of Saint Mark's, painted about 1575 to 1585 on a canvas about 139 by 101 centimeters. The procurators were among the most powerful officials of the Republic, holding office for life and managing the basilica and its funds. He would be shown in the formal robes of the office, with the head as the focus of the picture.","i":"Procurators of Saint Mark were chosen from the patrician class, often after distinguished service, and served for life; they were second only to the doge in prestige, and many doges had once been procurators. Portraits showed their formal robes and were kept in family palaces as statements of lineage and rank. Because the title gives no name, the man is known by his office rather than as an individual.","c":"Late Tintoretto portraits are more sketchily painted than the polished work of his earlier years, with rapid, broken brushwork, but they keep a keen eye for character. The format here is conventional, and the interest lies in the economy with which a grave head and a dark, heavy robe are made to carry authority. It rests on likeness and bearing rather than on invention, and its quiet gravity is the point."},"nga:33757":{"d":"A devotional picture of the Virgin, about 93 by 73 centimeters, painted about 1575 to 1585. As the title says, the Madonna is associated with stars, which in Christian imagery surround or crown the Virgin. The size and upright format are those of a picture for a private room or small chapel. Tintoretto's late Madonnas are brushed freely, with strong contrasts of light and dark, and the subject suits his taste for glowing, mobile light.","i":"Stars around the Virgin come from the Book of Revelation, where a woman clothed with the sun wears a crown of twelve stars, and from her title Stella Maris, Star of the Sea, which was especially dear to Venice as a maritime city. The Virgin was the Republic's great protector, and images of her appeared in homes, offices and churches alike, to be addressed in prayer.","c":"At this scale the dramatic Tintoretto of the large cycles is reduced to a few quick, strong strokes, and the result is warm, loosely painted and a little sketchy. It shows his speed and his ability to turn a simple devotional theme into a play of light. Those who prefer polish may find it rough, but the freedom of handling is the pleasure, and it was a successful formula."},"met:435638":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 95 by 24 centimeters, from the Cellier Altarpiece, painted in oil on wood in 1511 to 1512 by Jean Bellegambe of Douai. The catalog keywords list men, women, angels, bishops, monks, saints and a Madonna and Child, so the altarpiece brought a crowd of holy figures together. The narrow shape shows it was one section of a larger, multipart ensemble designed to be seen as a whole on an altar.","i":"Bellegambe worked at Douai in northern France for abbeys and churches, producing altarpieces rich in saints, monks and donors in a style indebted to Netherlandish painting. His pictures present the Church as a gathering of the holy: bishops, monks and martyrs around the Virgin, with angels above. A single narrow panel can only show part of that vision, but it keeps the sense of a heavenly company arranged for the worshiper.","c":"Bellegambe's glowing color, delicate detail and ornate architecture show Netherlandish training, but his composition looks back to the fifteenth century, and next to Antwerp contemporaries he can seem old-fashioned. His craft is excellent, and the brilliance of color gives his pictures real charm. A narrow fragment like this can only hint at the whole, and so is best appreciated for its refinement of detail."},"met:435637":{"d":"A narrow, upright portrait panel, about 68 by 29 centimeters, of Charles de Saint-Radegonde, called Charles Coguin, abbot of the Benedictine abbey of Anchin near Douai, painted in oil about 1511 to 1520. The tall, narrow shape suggests it was a wing or section of an altarpiece, on which a donor of high rank would be shown, presumably in the dark habit of his order, in devotion before the sacred scene.","i":"Abbots commissioned altarpieces for their abbeys and had themselves portrayed as donors, kneeling in prayer. The act expressed piety, asserted authority and secured a place in the community's prayers after death. Bellegambe painted for Anchin and other abbeys near Douai, and this panel is a rare, individual likeness within a religious ensemble. The sitter is named by name and office, which makes it a record of how an abbot wished to be seen.","c":"As a portrait this is probably direct for its date, with the Netherlandish habit of exact observation applied to an ecclesiastic's face. The tall, narrow format limited what Bellegambe could do, leaving no room for the elaborate detail of his altarpieces, so the face is the main event. Honest, careful and somewhat stiff, it is a good example of the donor portrait as a document of status."},"met:438377":{"d":"A very small portrait of a man, only about 24 by 17 centimeters, painted in oil on wood about 1625 to 1626. The catalog keywords list a ruff and a hat, and a seashell as the object he is shown with. The size is that of a cabinet picture, to be held in the hand or hung close to the eye. Thomas de Keyser worked in Amsterdam and painted portraits at many scales, from heads and busts like this to small full-length figures.","i":"A shell in a portrait is an attribute with several possible meanings. It may mark a collector, since exotic shells were prized in the Dutch Republic in the 1620s and displayed like jewels; it may allude to wealth from overseas trade; or it may carry a vanitas message about the fragility of beauty. The picture is probably the companion of the Met's Portrait of a Woman with a Balance, close in size and date, so the two sitters may be husband and wife.","c":"De Keyser's cabinet portraits rely on crisp drawing, a cool tonality and exact finish, and the tiny scale demands precision. The shell adds personality but leaves the picture's meaning undeclared, which is charming and slightly maddening. He lacks the psychological depth of Rembrandt, who arrived in Amsterdam a few years later and soon overshadowed him, but at his best he is a careful and appealing observer of the city's merchant class."},"met:438378":{"d":"A very small portrait of a woman, about 23 by 18 centimeters, painted in oil on wood about 1625 to 1626, with a balance, a pair of weighing scales, as her attribute. It matches the Portrait of a Man with a Shell in size and date, and the two probably form a married pair. The balance sets it apart from the standard formula of a bust against a plain ground and gives the likeness a story, though the story is not told in the title.","i":"A balance in a woman's portrait may refer to weighing coins or goods, perhaps her trade or her husband's; to honest dealing and justice; or to the weighing of the soul and moral self-judgment. Dutch portraits of the period often used such emblems to tell viewers what the sitter valued while leaving the exact meaning to be guessed. Since the sitters are not named, the symbol's precise sense remains uncertain.","c":"The balance is the picture's most interesting feature, turning a conventional likeness into a small puzzle. De Keyser's cool, exact finish suits such a puzzle, since the emblem is stated crisply but not explained. The head itself is modest in expression, and the painter does not push for psychological depth. As a pendant to the man with the shell, it forms a pleasing, thoughtful pair, charming rather than profound."},"nga:158097":{"d":"An octagonal portrait on copper, about 28 by 22 centimeters. A bearded man, shown from the chest up, wears a chestnut-brown jacket with gold buttons and a thick, lace-trimmed, pleated cream collar that rises to his ear. His body angles to the right while his light brown eyes cut back to the viewer, with wrinkles at their corners. His full beard almost hides his red lips, and his dark hair is combed back from a receding hairline. Light from the left leaves the far side of his face in shadow against a dark brown ground.","i":"The fancy ruff is the point: a large, starched, lace-edged collar was costly and demanded constant laundering and care, so its size announced wealth and leisure. By 1627 many men were switching to flat falling collars, so a big ruff can mark an older or more conservative taste. The sitter is unnamed. Copper is a smooth, hard support favored for fine small portraits, and the octagonal shape suggests a keepsake or a framed set.","c":"On copper de Keyser can render fine detail: the pleats and lace of the ruff, the gold buttons, the wrinkles at the eyes. The lighting is simple and the sidelong glance gives life to a conventional pose. It is an accomplished miniature in oil, a record of a man's status and a good example of the Amsterdam portrait at its most exact, though it goes little deeper than the skin."},"met:436818":{"d":"A double portrait on wood, about 75 by 53 centimeters, dated 1629, showing a musician and his young daughter. The catalog keywords list a man, a girl and a lute, so the father is identified by his instrument, with the child close beside him. De Keyser, who worked in Amsterdam, was a leading portraitist of the 1620s, known for clear, bright likenesses of merchants and their families.","i":"A lute stands for harmony and for a cultivated household: music-making was an accomplishment of the well-to-do and a sign of concord within the family. A father with a child also suggests lineage and affection. The title does not say whether he was a professional musician or a gifted amateur, and the sitters are not named, so the picture speaks first of the pleasures and values of a Dutch home in the 1620s.","c":"A double portrait requires the painter to relate two figures, and de Keyser's strengths, clear bright color and precise observation, suit the task. The interplay between father and child is the interest, and the lute gives the picture a purposeful center. He lacks Rembrandt's drama and Frans Hals's brio, but he is a careful, appealing reporter of the middle-class family. A modest but attractive picture."},"nga:63055":{"d":"A young woman with long blond hair kneels at the bottom center of this tall panel, head bowed and fingertips pressed together. A crowd of about two dozen, mostly men, surrounds her: a bare-chested executioner twists toward her and unsheathes a sword, an armored man rests a hand on a white and gold shield pinning a dragon, and others hold halberds and spears. On a cloud bank above sit the Virgin and Child with two bearded saints, while an angel brings a crown and a palm and putti scatter flowers.","i":"The kneeling woman is Saint Margaret of Antioch, who by her legend refused a pagan suitor, survived torture and a dragon that could not hold her, and was beheaded. The dragon under the shield recalls that episode. The two saints in the clouds, one with a key and one with a sword, are Peter and Paul, and the angel's crown and palm announce the martyr's reward. The picture pairs the moment before the blow with her welcome in heaven.","c":"Cesari, called the Cavaliere d'Arpino, was the most honored painter in Rome around 1600, and the panel shows why: the design is clear, the heavenly and earthly tiers are neatly linked, and the shifting silk of Margaret's cloak is painted with real brilliance. It also shows why his reputation faded. The crowd is a stock of poses and the emotion is polite; beside Caravaggio, who once worked in his shop, the whole looks like a pageant."},"met:10560":{"d":"A horizontal landscape, about 41 by 76 centimeters, painted in oil in 1883. The title says no more than Landscape, and the catalog gives no place or figures, so the subject is simply a stretch of open country. Kenyon Cox is known chiefly for figure painting, portraits and murals, so a pure landscape is an unusual subject for him. The wide, low format suits an open view in which sky, horizon and ground are the main elements.","i":"Cox trained in Paris under Carolus-Duran and Gérôme from 1877 to 1882 and returned to New York committed to an academic, classical ideal of art. In the early 1880s American painters were absorbing the lessons of the French Barbizon school and of painting outdoors, and a small landscape from that moment is probably an informal study, a relaxed exercise for a figure painter rather than a considered exhibition piece.","c":"A quiet, unassuming picture. It lacks the precision and monumentality of Cox's figure work, but it is unaffected, a straightforward response to light and space. Because landscape was not his main business, it shows him looking without a program, which is its charm. A modest study, interesting chiefly for what it reveals of an academic painter's private habits of observation."},"nga:195765":{"d":"A landscape of rolling green hills seen from an elevated viewpoint. The nearest hills, at left, are light green with yellowish grass, and a dense forest of dark green trees fills the slopes at right. Toward the high horizon the hills flatten, leading to a small white building with a red roof. The sky is light blue with soft white clouds, and the brushstrokes are soft. The title, Flying Shadows, suggests the shadows of clouds crossing the hills. Oil on canvas, about 76 by 92 centimeters, dated 1883.","i":"Kenyon Cox, who had studied in Paris with Carolus-Duran and Gérôme and returned to New York in 1882, was best known as a figure painter and muralist, and landscapes are a smaller part of his work. In the 1880s American painters were absorbing the French open-air taste for light and broad landscape views; this picture belongs to that moment, with its soft touch and high vantage. The title suggests a poetic interest in passing light.","c":"A pleasant, competent landscape in which the soft brushwork and high horizon give a broad, calm view. It does not have the originality of the American Impressionists or the force of Cox's own later murals, and the forms are somewhat generalized, but it shows a thoughtful academic painter trying a subject of pure light. Its interest is partly as a rare landscape by a figure painter."},"met:10559":{"d":"A woman is shown playing a harp, in an upright canvas of about 76 by 45 centimeters, painted in 1888. The subtitle, A Solo, makes the performance the subject: a single musician absorbed in her playing. The catalog lists a woman, a harp and a musician, so the picture is a figure study of an instrument and its player, not an identified portrait. The tall format suits the instrument's own height.","i":"Music was a favorite theme for late nineteenth-century painters, who saw in it an analogy for painting's own harmonies of line and color, and women at instruments were common emblems of refinement. The harp, with its associations with antiquity, the lyre and angels, lent itself to idealized pictures. Kenyon Cox, trained in Paris, shared the era's belief that the beautiful figure was the highest subject for art.","c":"Cox is a skilled draftsman, and the figure is solidly constructed; the problem of making a harp and a player compose into a tall, narrow shape is neatly solved. The mood is hushed rather than animated, and his academic polish can feel cool, so the picture is more decorative than expressive. As an exercise in grace and structure it succeeds; as an evocation of music it is controlled, not passionate."},"met:437325":{"d":"Margherita Gonzaga (1591–1632), a princess of Mantua and later Duchess of Lorraine, is shown in an oil on canvas of about 93 by 69 cm. She wears a pearl necklace, a brooch, a tiara and lace, the jewels and linen of princely rank. Pourbus served the Gonzaga court at Mantua in the early 1600s, where he portrayed the ducal family, and the sitter would then have been a girl or young woman.","i":"A princess's portrait was a diplomatic object. Dynastic marriage was the chief tool of princely courts, and likenesses traveled ahead of brides to show prospective husbands what they were getting. Margherita married Henri II, Duke of Lorraine, in 1606, binding Mantua to a duchy close to France. Pearls, a tiara and fine lace marked rank and wealth, and pearls were also read as signs of purity, a fitting emblem for a bride.","c":"Pourbus brought the Flemish habit of exact description to the Italian court, and the lace, pearls and tiara are the sort of detail he rendered with patience. The challenge of a princess's portrait is to let a young person show through the formality of her costume; the risk is a mannequin of rank. Within the genre it is a careful, courtly image, valued less for psychological depth than for its authority and finish."},"met:10994":{"d":"A tiny tabletop still life, about 20 by 30 centimeters, painted in 1877. The catalog lists a book, a feather, a coin, a postage stamp, an inkwell and an envelope, the writing materials and small valuables of an office desk. The objects are laid on a flat surface and rendered with the exactness of a trompe l'oeil painter, the style of illusion meant to make a viewer believe the things are really there.","i":"Harnett was a master of trompe l'oeil, the fool-the-eye tradition, and he found many buyers in the 1870s among businessmen who liked pictures that flattered the world of commerce. A banker's table with coin, stamp, ink and pen reflects the business culture of the Gilded Age. Harnett had trained as an engraver of silver in Philadelphia, and that training gave him an eye for fine detail and crisp edges.","c":"At only 20 by 30 centimeters the picture packs in convincing, worn textures, from the paper's edge to the sheen of the coin, and the arrangement is simple and clear. Harnett's early pictures are more modest than his later door pieces, but they already show his instinct for objects that look handled and used. The fun is the trick, and the picture is a sly, tidy miniature."},"cma:141641":{"d":"A skull, a spent hourglass, an extinguished candle and a tattered book make up a memento mori, and on the book's inside cover is inscribed a quotation from Shakespeare's Hamlet. The canvas is about 61 by 82 centimeters and dated 1879. The title, To This Favour, comes from the quotation. The objects are painted with the precision of trompe l'oeil, so that the worn pages and the bone seem within reach.","i":"The Latin phrase memento mori, remember that you must die, names a tradition in which objects remind viewers of mortality. The quotation comes from the graveyard scene, where Hamlet, holding a skull, jokes that however thickly a lady paints her face, to this favor she must come. The word paint also winks at the painter's own artifice, so a solemn warning doubles as a joke about illusion.","c":"The pairing of grave symbols with a sly pun is the picture's cleverness: the inscription turns the painted surface into a joke about painting itself, the paint that cannot save a face from death. Skull, hourglass and candle are the standard kit of vanitas, but the realism of the tattered book and the wit lift it above routine. A learned, somber and slightly playful performance."},"met:10993":{"d":"A trompe l'oeil picture, about 76 by 63 centimeters, dated 1879, in the form of a letter rack, a type in which a board, crossed by tapes, holds letters, cards and papers flat against a wall, painted so they look real. The title says the rack belongs to the artist, which suggests a personal selection of mail and small keepsakes, though the catalog gives no list of its contents.","i":"Letter racks were common in nineteenth-century offices and homes, and as a subject they let painters display a range of textures and printed matter while hiding names, dates and jokes in plain sight. The picture offers the pleasure of seeming to look into another man's mail and the pleasure of being fooled. Harnett and John F. Peto both made a specialty of such racks.","c":"The charm of a letter rack lies in its shallow, flat arrangement: the picture persuades by how convincingly paper curls, edges fray and shadows fall, and Harnett's skill with such textures is high. The format also confines him to a flat grid, so the interest is in details rather than in composition. A good example of a type, more clever than moving."},"nga:81871":{"d":"A plucked chicken hangs upside down by one yellow leg from a string against a brown wooden panel. Its bare skin is pale yellow with patches of pale pink; the tail and head are still intact, with white plumage and a red crest. A few small white feathers cling to the skin, and others drift downward in the air. Painted in 1882, during Harnett's years in Munich, the canvas is about 87 by 52 centimeters.","i":"The picture belongs to the tradition of the dead-game still life, from Dutch kitchen pieces to the American hunting pictures of the 1880s, but it dispenses with the table and the luxuries: one humble bird, stripped and hanging. The title is also a pun, since to be plucked was slang for being fleeced, and clean evokes complete loss. The trompe l'oeil precision makes the bird an object lesson in mortality.","c":"The bare skin and the drifting feathers show Harnett's ability to turn an unpromising subject into a convincing presence. The tall format and plain panel concentrate attention on the bird, and the humor is dry. It lacks the sumptuousness of his other pieces but gains in directness. A bold, slightly macabre choice for a still life, and one that rewards a long look."},"nga:79531":{"d":"A violin and bow hang with two sheets of curling music from a nail on a dark green wooden door, the violin filling the top two-thirds of the canvas, with white rosin dust on its strings. The door is worn: pins, small holes and vertical cracks, a metal ring, rusty hinges with a bolt missing and part of the lower one broken. A scrap of newspaper is pasted below, and a pale blue envelope tucked in at the lower left bears a stamp and is addressed to W. M. Harnett, New York, postmarked Paris, 27 April 1886.","i":"Trompe l'oeil, the tradition of painting objects so convincingly that the eye is fooled, goes back to the Dutch and to early American painters of illusion. Harnett turned it to pictures of used things: a worn violin, nails, a battered door, all signs of age and use. The envelope addressed to the artist gives the picture a date, a place and a name, making the artist's identity part of the illusion. Music is also an old emblem of transience.","c":"A showpiece of American illusionism. The cracks, the stained wood and the dust on the strings are so persuasive that one wants to touch, and the restrained palette of greens and browns and the vertical stack of violin, bow and music give the composition dignity. The risk of such painting is that the trick is all, but here the elegiac tone, the sense of a worn, beloved object, lifts it above mere virtuosity."},"met:10995":{"d":"A very small picture, about 14 by 19 centimeters, painted on wood in 1888, showing a newspaper and a pipe. The catalog lists a pipe, a newspaper and a still life, and the title names the paper, the New York Daily News. The printed columns and the pipe are rendered with the exactness of a trompe l'oeil painter, and the small scale suits an intimate subject, the quiet moment with tobacco and the day's news.","i":"Newspapers appear often in Harnett's still lifes as the most everyday of printed objects, and the choice of a real, named paper tied his pictures to the world of 1888 New York, its commerce and politics. A pipe and newspaper evoke a male leisure ritual of the period. Painted newsprint is also a challenge, since the artist must suggest columns of print without writing them out, and viewers enjoy testing how much they can read.","c":"A tiny cabinet picture that shows Harnett's mastery of paper and wood in a few square inches. The trick of a newspaper is to look flat, crisp and folded, and it is done here with deft economy. At this size it is almost a sketch, a casual exercise that shows his ease rather than his ambition. Pleasant, slight and a good example of the small works that sold readily to collectors."},"met:10996":{"d":"A small still life, about 36 by 44 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas in 1888, with books, sheet music and a musical instrument. The catalog gives no title beyond Still Life, and the grouping is close to that of My Gems, painted the same year, in which instruments, scores and well-worn books stand for an educated man's leisure. The objects are painted with the sharp, tactile finish of a trompe l'oeil specialist.","i":"Musical instruments and books were common in seventeenth-century vanitas pictures, where they stood for the transience of pleasure. In Harnett's hands the worn objects are more affectionate than moralizing, a nostalgia for well-used things. Music also invites the viewer to imagine sound, and curled pages of sheet music add a sense of time spent. The picture belongs to the last years of his short life; he died in 1892, aged forty-four.","c":"A compact example of Harnett's late manner: an even light, a dark ground and carefully built textures of wood, paper and metal. It lacks the surprises of his best pieces, and the arrangement is conventional, but it is a sound demonstration of why he was the leading American trompe l'oeil painter of his day. Careful, quiet and modest rather than showy."},"met:10997":{"d":"A violin hangs from a nail on a weathered grey-green door, with a sheet of printed music pinned behind its body. A bow hangs at the left beside a small brass matchbox, and a rusted horseshoe, a padlock and a slotted wooden tab are fixed to the planks at the right. A black flute with pale bands lies across the lower corner, and a small white card with a few written words rests near it. Iron strap hinges cross the door, and the date 1888 is painted on the lower edge. Every object casts a sharp shadow, and the paint is so smooth that the surface looks like real wood and metal rather than a picture.","i":"The Met calls Harnett the leading still-life painter in America in the centennial years, celebrated for pushing trompe l'oeil, French for 'fool the eye', to its limits. The hinged door is slightly ajar and the objects hang on prominent nails, which gives the flat-looking arrangement real depth. These playfully illusionistic pictures were especially popular with middle-class collectors who admired skilled realism. The things chosen, a violin, music, a horseshoe for luck and a flute, suggest an amateur's musical pleasures. Harnett, who was born in Ireland in 1848 and died in New York at forty-four, painted this in 1888. It entered the museum in 1963.","c":"The pleasure is mostly in the trick, and it is a good one: the curl of the paper, the shadow under each nail and the rust on the hinges are painted with such patience that the eye looks twice. The composition is better than a pile of objects would be, with the upright violin set against the diagonal of the flute. The limit is that once the deception is understood, little else is offered; the picture asks for admiration of skill, not feeling. Harnett's fame faded after his early death and revived only in the mid-twentieth century."},"nga:43680":{"d":"A smoking pipe, a piccolo, books, sheet music, an oil lamp, a white and blue pitcher and a few matches are arranged on a wooden table against a dark ground. The black piccolo rests on a stack of books at the center, and dogeared, stained sheet music drapes over the table's front, one title reading Hélas quelle douleur. A tall silver lamp stands behind, and book spines read Shakespeare and Dante. The panel is about 46 by 35 centimeters, signed and dated 1888.","i":"The title, My Gems, suggests that these are the artist's treasures: books, music and a pipe in place of jewels. The names Shakespeare and Dante announce a cultured owner, and the piccolo, sheet music and pipe suggest leisure hours. Everything shows wear, with frayed paper and stained cloth, and so stands for time and use, an affectionate, nostalgic attachment to well-handled things that appealed to American collectors of the 1880s.","c":"Harnett's rendering of textures, worn leather, tattered paper, glazed ceramic and burnished metal, is superb, and the dark ground pushes the objects forward with theatrical force. The composition is balanced and calm. The cultural names are displayed a little too eagerly, as a set of credentials, but the painting's warm, elegiac mood outweighs any calculation. A confident late work that shows why his illusions were admired."},"met:437909":{"d":"Saint Peter Martyr, the Dominican saint of Verona, heals a young man's leg in this narrow panel of about 53 by 33 centimeters, painted in tempera and gold around 1450 by Antonio Vivarini of Venice. The recorded motifs are Peter, a man and a woman: the young man and, presumably, a female witness. The gold ground and the small scale suggest one scene in a series on the saint's miracles.","i":"Peter of Verona (about 1205-1252), a Dominican preacher and inquisitor, was murdered by assassins on the road to Milan and canonized within a year; his images show him with a blade in his skull and a wound in his chest. His miracle cycle includes the story of a young man who, in remorse for having kicked his mother, cut off his own foot, which the saint restored. If this is the episode, it taught obedience to parents and the power of penance.","c":"Antonio Vivarini works in the elegant, gold-rich Gothic idiom of the Venetian workshops, with decorative color and linear grace, while Padua and Florence had already moved on to sculptural form and perspective. The small scene is told with clarity and a certain naive charm, the surface still glowing with gold. It is conservative, but conservative in a way that gives pleasure: jeweled color and fluent line."},"met:435917":{"d":"A tiny devotional panel, about 21 by 14 centimeters, painted about 1550, showing the Adoration of the Shepherds. The catalog keywords list the Christ Child, Mary, a saint, an angel, cattle and a lamb, so the picture brings the holy family, the animals of the stable and the shepherds' offering together in a small space. Coffermans worked in Antwerp, where small devotional pictures were made in quantity for the market and for export.","i":"In the Gospel of Luke, shepherds in the fields are told by an angel of Christ's birth and go to find the child in a manger. The lamb is the shepherds' gift and an emblem of Christ as the Lamb of God, while the ox and ass at the manger come from Isaiah and later legend. A small panel like this invited close, private devotion, to be held or set where a family could pray before it.","c":"Coffermans is known for adapting earlier Netherlandish compositions, and what he offers is polish and charm in a form that was easy to repeat. At this size the picture can only hint at the grandeur of the artists he followed, and it is pleasing rather than original. Its value is as a typical example of how Netherlandish devotional painting survived, in a more commercial form, after the great fifteenth-century masters."},"nga:129865":{"d":"Tiny armies march and fight among huge rock formations and massive buildings under a stormy sky. Rain falls from flint-gray clouds at the left, while at the right a brilliant white sun breaks through and opens patches of blue. A walled city of silvery buildings, with domes and a colosseum, crowns the cliffs on the horizon. Below, columns of warriors on foot and horseback pass Joshua, who stands on a rocky height in a plumed helmet, breastplate and scarlet toga with his right hand raised. Priests in white turbans gesture skyward, and a lightning bolt strikes a distant city.","i":"In the book of Joshua, during the battle of Gibeon against five Amorite kings, Joshua prays for the sun to stand still so that Israel can finish the fight, and the sun halts in the middle of the sky while hailstones fall on the enemy. The miracle shows God's power answering a leader's prayer. The city's domes and amphitheater are imaginative, not archaeological, and a picture of a victory shown in 1816, just after Waterloo, would have resonated with British viewers.","c":"Martin's gift is scale: the vast space, the colossal ramp of rock, the lightning and the theatrical light produce an awe that his tiny figures cannot, and that was precisely his appeal. He was loved by the public and scorned by some critics as a showman, and the figures are indeed small and stiff. But the audacity of the vision and the command of perspective make the picture memorable, an early statement of a manner he would exploit for decades."},"nga:46134":{"d":"Close to the viewer, a gray-haired, bearded man sits against a tall, narrow rock before a deep landscape, his elbow propped on a ledge and his head resting in his hand. He turns to look up at a raven perched on the stone that holds a roll in its beak. He wears an aquamarine-blue robe under a rose-pink cloth lined with fleece, and his bare feet rest on the ground. In the distance a river runs through grassy land, where small figures point up at a man kneeling in a chariot that rises into a peach-toned cloud.","i":"In the first book of Kings, Elijah announces a drought and hides by a brook, where ravens bring him bread and meat morning and evening: a sign that God provides for the faithful. The tiny scene in the distance jumps to the end of his life, when he is taken up to heaven in a chariot of fire, so two moments are shown in one image. Elijah was a model of solitary prayer, especially for hermits and the Carmelite order.","c":"Savoldo's early landscape painting is poetic, and the contrast between the heavy, brooding prophet in the foreground and the small miracle in the distance is striking. The figure is placed close, strongly modeled and given real weight by the bold blue of the robe, and the quiet attention to the raven's offering gives the picture an unforced gravity. The influence of Giorgione's soft landscape seems evident, and the mood is more contemplative than dramatic."},"nga:41689":{"d":"A man is shown from the knees up, seated and turned slightly to the right, with short, curly dark hair, a thin moustache and a goatee. His expression is neutral as he gazes at the viewer. He wears a dark red garment with voluminous sleeves under a shining gray breastplate, a light green cloak over his shoulder and silvery leg armor. In one hand he holds a long wooden staff; his other hand is raised, fingers curled. A stone column stands at the left, and at the right a distant landscape with blue fields and structures lies under a blue sky.","i":"Armor in a portrait could mark a real soldier or a nobleman who wished to be seen as one, and the staff suggests command. In the 1520s northern Italy was a battlefield of the Italian Wars, so military men formed a major class of patrons. The sitter is unnamed, and knight is a cataloguer's description rather than a known title. Savoldo had a particular interest in the play of light on metal, and the polished breastplate gives him the chance to show it.","c":"The picture is calm and solid. A rich dark red, cool steel and a pale green cloak form a distinctive color scheme, and Savoldo's quiet, seemingly unposed handling keeps the sitter from seeming theatrical. The sitter's neutral expression conveys little inner life, and the formal pose holds him at a distance. A restrained, handsome portrait that is less dramatic than its subject of arms and armor might suggest."},"nga:46185":{"d":"Four people, shown from the waist up, surround a nude infant in a nighttime scene. The baby lies on a white cloth, holding the end of a translucent yellow cloth. Mary, the largest figure, stands just right of center in a lapis-blue robe over a rose-pink dress, her fingertips together over the child. Two gray-bearded men stand to the left, one in profile with palms raised, the other with a hand to his chest, half lost in shadow. At right a clean-shaven man in an olive jacket looks up in prayer. Far off, gold and coral light breaks through dark clouds, where small figures see an angel.","i":"The scene follows the Gospel of Luke: shepherds in the fields at night are told by an angel of Christ's birth and go to find him. The glowing distance shows the announcement while the foreground shows the arrival, so two moments appear in one picture. The half-length group, the dark stone building behind and the hushed gestures bring the viewer close, to share in a private act of reverence.","c":"Savoldo was known for nocturnal scenes and for light that glows rather than floods, and here the effect depends on a few lit passages in a dark ground. Mary's fingertips and the men's gestures create a hushed, reverent mood, and the half-shadowed faces draw the viewer in. The darkness hides some detail, but the silent, contemplative focus is the achievement, and the picture influenced later painters of night scenes."},"met:437630":{"d":"A bearded man in a lustrous pink mantle sits at a table covered with green cloth, a quill in his right hand, writing on a sheet of paper by the light of a small oil lamp. His left hand rests on a blue inkpot. He looks up toward the angel who stands behind him in a deep violet robe with blue-grey wings and leans toward him. The room is otherwise dark. At the left a window opens on a moonlit sky and glimpses of buildings; at the right a second opening shows tiny figures gathered around a fire. The satin of the saint's cloak catches the light in sharp, bright folds.","i":"Matthew the Evangelist, according to tradition, wrote the first Gospel, and artists often showed an angel, his symbol, dictating to him. Savoldo, from Brescia and active in Venice, specialised in night scenes with several light sources: here a lamp, a fire in the distance and the moon. The lamp lights the saint and his cloak while the angel stays half in shadow, which makes the visitation seem secret and physical. The picture is undated but is generally placed in the 1530s, and the Met bought it with the Marquand Fund in 1912. Its nocturnal effects are sometimes said to anticipate Caravaggio, though any direct influence is unproven.","c":"Savoldo's nocturne is among the most original religious pictures of its time. The pink satin glows like a lit shade against the dark, the angel's near-silhouette is dramatic, and the domestic scale of the scene, a tired man at a desk, listening, is moving. The optical effects of satin, lamp, fire and moon are clearly the painter's real subject, and the figures themselves are rather stiff: Matthew's shoulder and arm are awkward, and the angel's face is almost blank. Even so, as a demonstration of what light can do in a religious picture, it is rare and haunting."},"met:437607":{"d":"A vision of Paradise on a small copper panel, about 54 by 48 centimeters, painted about 1598. The museum's keywords record Christ, Mary, John the Baptist, Saint George and the Fall of Man, so how the figures are arranged in heaven, where Adam and Eve appear and the colors are left unstated. Copper, a smooth support that suits fine detail, was favored for small, precious pictures, and Carlo Saraceni was then a very young painter newly arrived in Rome.","i":"A picture of Paradise gathers the saved around Christ and Mary, with John the Baptist, the forerunner, and Saint George among the intercessors. The inclusion of the Fall of Man, the story of Adam and Eve, recalls why salvation was needed and links the beginning and end of the Christian story in one image. The format, a crowded celestial court painted small, recalls the tradition of the Last Judgment but at the intimate scale of a private devotional picture.","c":"An early work by a painter who would become one of Caravaggio's more independent followers, it shows a young artist's ambition in cramming a vast theme onto a small copper. The smooth ground lets minute figures be rendered with clarity, though the crowded design can feel busy, and the maturity of his later altarpieces is not yet present. It is a jewel-like curiosity, more interesting for the promise than the performance."},"met:438690":{"d":"A very large altarpiece, about 305 by 231 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas about 1612, shows the Dormition of the Virgin, her death among the apostles. The museum's keywords record men and women, the Virgin Mary and the Death of the Virgin, so the number of figures, the arrangement of the bed, the light and the colors are left unstated. At more than three meters tall, it was made for a church, where it would have been seen from some distance above an altar.","i":"The Dormition, the falling asleep of the Virgin, is an old subject: the apostles gather around Mary's deathbed, and, in the traditional version, Christ appears to receive her soul. In Rome around 1600 the subject was delicate, because Caravaggio's version, with its swollen, earthly Mary, had been rejected by the Carmelites of Santa Maria della Scala in 1606. Saraceni, one of Caravaggio's followers, painted the subject more than once, and his treatment is gentler and more orderly.","c":"Saraceni takes Caravaggio's lessons, close figures, strong light and humble faces, and softens them with a more decorous mood. On such a large canvas the cast of mourners must be organized with care, and clarity of grouping is his strength. The effect is solemn but not tragic, a more consoling vision than Caravaggio's. It is a valuable example of how Caravaggism spread and was tamed for church use."},"met:436499":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 114 by 59 centimeters, showing the goddess Diana as a nude huntress. The catalog lists a bow and arrow, a deer and nudity as the elements, so the goddess of the hunt is shown unclothed, with her attributes and an animal beside her. Giampietrino, a Milanese follower of Leonardo da Vinci, painted nude and half-nude female figures in smooth, softly lit modeling derived from his master. No date is recorded for it.","i":"Diana, the Roman goddess of the hunt, wild animals and chastity, is traditionally shown with a bow and a deer, and her chastity was famous: the hunter Actaeon was punished for seeing her bathing. A nude Diana let a Renaissance painter treat the ideal female nude with classical dignity, the goddess's nakedness licensed by mythology. Such pictures were made for the private rooms of educated patrons, who enjoyed classical subjects and beauty together.","c":"Giampietrino takes Leonardo's soft modeling and elegant types but lacks his anatomical knowledge, so the bodies tend to be smooth and rather generic and the faces idealized. The painting succeeds as polished, decorative sensuality but goes no deeper. It is useful as evidence of how Leonardo's manner spread through Milan by way of his followers, and its tall format has a decorative elegance."},"nga:45890":{"d":"Christ, in a red robe, rises on a black cloud under an arched gold top, raising two fingers in blessing and holding a cross-topped staff with a red pennant. His wounds are visible on his hands and side. Four bearded men on clouds to each side look up, holding a palm, a book, stone tablets, a harp and a sword. Below, twelve men and a woman kneel in two groups on either side of a green rock marked with two footprints; the woman, in blue with a gold halo, is Mary, with a young man in green beside her. Rays are incised into the gold.","i":"In the Acts of the Apostles, forty days after the Resurrection, Jesus is taken up into a cloud as his followers watch. In northern European art the footprints left on the Mount of Olives became a favorite detail, as here. The eight figures on the clouds are probably Old Testament patriarchs and prophets, Moses with his tablets and David with his harp among them, witnesses to the fulfillment of prophecy. The arched top shows that this was a panel from a larger altarpiece.","c":"A courtly, late Gothic panel in the Westphalian tradition, with some Netherlandish influence: crisp, angular draperies, bright colors on a tooled gold ground and a symbol-rich design in which placement conveys rank. Its strengths are clarity and richness. Its weakness is the lack of depth and anatomical conviction beside contemporaries such as Rogier van der Weyden, though that was not the aim; this is a devotional image meant to dazzle and instruct."},"met:436329":{"d":"A man gathers olives beneath olive trees, with a waterfall in the background, in this tall canvas of 1868, about 213 by 131 centimeters. Tivoli, outside Rome, was famous for its waterfalls on the Aniene, its ancient ruins and its olive groves, and it drew landscape painters for centuries. The size and vertical format place the picture among the large landscapes made for exhibition, by François-Louis Français, a French painter.","i":"The olive harvest links the picture to the age-old rhythm of the Italian countryside, an image of timelessness that appealed to French viewers. Français traveled in Italy and painted its landscapes in the tradition of the French classical landscape, which treats a view as a composed whole. A modern viewer might note that the peasants are small, unnamed figures and the scene is idealized.","c":"Français combines careful observation with a classical sense of composition: the gnarled olives, the cascade and the harvest are arranged with balance. The effect is polished rather than spontaneous, a Salon landscape for an audience that liked its Italy tidy. It lacks the freshness of Corot's Italian sketches but offers a scale and finish that impressed visitors in 1868."},"met:437890":{"d":"A large upright portrait of a boy with a greyhound, painted in oil on canvas about 174 by 102 centimeters, possibly in the 1570s. The catalog lists a boy, a sighthound and a portrait, so the child is shown beside his hunting dog, in the grand, upright format used for children of noble or wealthy Venetian families. The sitter is not named, and the picture is an unusual example of Veronese turning from religious feasts to the likeness of a child.","i":"A greyhound was an emblem of nobility, hunting and fidelity, and in a child's portrait it suggested the aristocratic upbringing the boy would receive. Venetian patricians had villas on the mainland, where hunting was a chief pastime, so the boy is presented as a future gentleman. Portraits of children were often commissioned to record heirs, and the dog gives the child something to do and the picture a narrative.","c":"Veronese's portraits are rarer than his religious feasts, and this one shows his clear, silvery color and ease with silk. The dog's elegant lines echo the boy's pose, and the likeness is more affectionate than formal, a relaxed elegance unlike the stiff princely portraits of the day. A charming picture whose strength lies less in psychological depth than in grace of design and the light sheen of costume."},"met:437891":{"d":"Venus, nude but for a pearl necklace, a small coronet and a blue cloth at her thigh, stands at the left and presses one hand to her breast while the other rests on the shoulder of Mars. He bows his bearded head to her, wearing gilded armour and a mauve satin cloak. A winged Cupid at the lower left binds her ankle to Mars with a cord; a second Cupid at the right holds the bridle of a dappled grey horse. Mars's helmet lies at his feet on a stone base. Behind them a broken classical ruin with a sculpted head, a cloth hung on a tree and green trees stand against a blue sky with white cloud. The cool, silvery satin sets off the warm flesh.","i":"Veronese took a story from Greek myth, the love of the war god Mars and Venus, and made it an image of harmony. The Metropolitan Museum describes milk flowing from Venus's breast and Mars disarmed, an image of love's civilising effect on war; Cupids tie the pair together and hold back the war-horse. The Museum notes that the picture was possibly commissioned by Emperor Maximilian II and was in the imperial collection in Prague by 1621, with other mythological pictures by Veronese. It probably dates from the 1570s, the height of his Venetian career. The exact programme and patron remain uncertain, though the idea of concord born of opposites was a popular Renaissance theme.","c":"Veronese's gift for setting silk, armour and skin side by side is fully used. The mauve satin is the best passage, shot with light, and Venus's flesh is warm and plain-spoken rather than idealised. The allegory is agreeable rather than moving: the lovers' glances do not meet, Cupid's knot and the milk are emblems more than drama, and the horse watches the viewer rather than the lovers. As a sample of how Venetian painting could be sensual and yet decorous, it is clear and handsome; as a statement about love and war it is thin."},"nga:46146":{"d":"A canvas of about 108 by 84 centimeters showing Saint Jerome in the wilderness, painted about 1575 to 1585. Jerome is shown as a penitent hermit, with the objects that identify him in Renaissance painting: a rock for striking his chest, a crucifix, a skull and sometimes a lion. Veronese's version is a devotional picture for a private room or chapel, in which the saint's austere life in the desert is set against a landscape.","i":"Jerome (about 342 to 420) was the scholar who translated the Bible into Latin, the Vulgate, and who retired to the desert near Bethlehem. Renaissance artists show him as a penitent, beating his breast with a stone before a crucifix, with the lion from a legend that he removed a thorn from its paw. His red cardinal's hat, anachronistic, marks his authority as a Doctor of the Church. In Counter-Reformation Venice, the penitent saint was a popular model of repentance.","c":"The subject asks for rugged austerity, and Veronese is by temperament a painter of elegance, so his hermit tends to be more graceful than gaunt. What he supplies is glowing color, fluent flesh and a landscape that opens the picture to light. The emotional intensity of Titian's or Tintoretto's hermits is absent, and the treatment is lighter than the subject deserves, but the beauty of the handling is real."},"nga:45":{"d":"A small upright canvas, about 58 by 44 centimeters, painted about 1581 to 1582, showing the Finding of Moses. Pharaoh's daughter and her attendants discover the infant in his basket by the Nile. Veronese painted the subject several times, and liked it for the chance to show richly dressed women in a river landscape. At this size it may have been made for a private collector, a cabinet picture whose jewel-like color rewards close looking.","i":"In the book of Exodus, Moses's mother hides her baby in a basket among the reeds to save him from Pharaoh's order, and Pharaoh's daughter finds him, takes pity and raises him. Veronese typically dresses the princess and her ladies in contemporary Venetian silks, so a biblical story becomes a courtly scene of the present, and Venetian patrons could enjoy seeing their own world inside sacred history. The moral is that providence protects the chosen.","c":"In small format Veronese's color becomes jewel-like, with silks and glittering landscape in lively balance, and the composition is elegant. The spiritual weight is slight, since the story's pathos is overlaid by pageantry, and the picture is a little more decorative than moving. It is a good example of how he turned a moment of danger into a scene of courtly grace, and the brushwork is fluent and light."},"met:437889":{"d":"A portrait of the sculptor Alessandro Vittoria (1525 to 1608), painted by Veronese about 1580 on a canvas about 111 by 82 centimeters. The catalog lists a man and an artist, so Vittoria is shown as a professional man, the leading sculptor of Venice, by a colleague. The size is that of a three-quarter-length portrait, and the picture is a record of friendship and respect between two of the city's most prominent artists.","i":"Portraits of artists by artists record friendship and professional pride. Vittoria, trained under Jacopo Sansovino, was the leading sculptor of late sixteenth-century Venice, and he and Veronese moved in the same circles; both are credited with decoration at the Villa Barbaro at Maser. A portrait by a painter of a sculptor may also carry a quiet argument in the old debate over whether sculpture or painting was the nobler art.","c":"Veronese is not usually thought of as a portraitist, and his portraits are restrained beside his festive religious pictures: sober dark clothes, a direct look, and a face from which the sitter's character emerges. The picture is a tribute rather than a display, and its quiet authority is its strength. It lacks the glitter of his large canvases but offers something more reflective and personal, an artist's gaze meeting an artist's face."},"nga:63264":{"d":"A horizontal canvas, about 140 by 173 centimeters, painted about 1585 to 1586, near the end of Veronese's life. The title joins two moments in the legend of Saint Lucy, her martyrdom and her last communion, so the picture shows the saint receiving the sacrament and meeting her death. The size suggests an altarpiece or a large devotional picture for a church or chapel, where the faithful could contemplate the saint's courage and the comfort of the Eucharist.","i":"Lucy was a young woman of Syracuse in Sicily who vowed her virginity and was martyred, about 304, under the emperor Diocletian. She is invoked for the eyes, since her name suggests light, and in the legend she received communion before she died. A last communion of the martyr, in the Counter-Reformation years, emphasized the Eucharist as a source of strength, and Venetian Catholics of the 1580s would have appreciated that message.","c":"The late Veronese turns from the festive brightness of his banquet scenes to a graver tone, and the subject suits that change. Martyrdom is violent, but he handles it with decorum, so the pathos is formal rather than fierce, and elegant gesture replaces raw suffering. This will disappoint those who want drama, but it gives the picture a dignified, dusky beauty typical of his last years, and a sincerity beyond mere pageant."},"nga:41697":{"d":"A wide canvas, about 146 by 283 centimeters, painted about 1582 to 1588, showing Rebecca at the well. The story calls for a young woman at a well, a servant and his camels and attendants, and Veronese typically shows biblical figures in sumptuous contemporary Venetian costume, set in a pastoral landscape with classical architecture. The wide horizontal format suggests a decorative picture for a wall or ceiling, one of a series of Old Testament scenes such as Venetian patrons liked.","i":"In the book of Genesis, Abraham sends his servant to find a wife for Isaac among his own kin. The servant prays that the girl who offers him water and waters his camels will be the one, and Rebecca does exactly that, proving her courtesy and generosity. Christian readers saw in her a type of the Church as the bride of Christ. For Venetian painters the story was also a pretext for beautiful costume and animals.","c":"Veronese was the master of the festive, light-filled biblical scene, and this subject is made for him: a well, a girl, camels and silks. The wide composition, with figures strung out almost like a frieze, turns an Old Testament story into a pageant, and the narrative's moral is secondary to its visual delight. The spiritual content is less intense than in Tintoretto, but the pleasure it gives is generous."},"nga:45891":{"d":"An upright canvas, about 98 by 75 centimeters, painted about 1583 to 1584, showing the Annunciation, the moment when the angel Gabriel tells the Virgin Mary she will bear the Son of God. Veronese's Annunciations usually set the scene in an elegant Venetian interior with Gabriel arriving in a rush of drapery, bearing a lily, and the dove of the Holy Spirit above. The size suits a private devotional picture or a small altarpiece.","i":"In the Gospel of Luke, Gabriel greets Mary and tells her she has found favor with God, and she answers that she is the handmaid of the Lord. The scene marks the Incarnation, which the church celebrates on 25 March. In Venice that date was doubly important, since the city's founding was traditionally placed on 25 March 421, the feast of the Annunciation.","c":"Veronese's Annunciations are graceful and lightly colored, with the angel's rushing entrance set against Mary's startled modesty. They are more decorative than mystical, and a viewer wanting the intensity of Tintoretto will find them gentler. For a devotional picture of modest size, though, it is very satisfying: silvery color, balanced composition and a sweetness of expression that matched what Venetians expected of a sacred image."},"met:438134":{"d":"Saint Catherine of Alexandria sits in her prison cell, with a dove and the palm of martyrdom, according to the catalog keywords. The canvas is about 116 by 84 centimeters and was painted about 1585. In Catherine's legend, a dove brought her food while she was imprisoned, so the picture chooses an episode of divine care, not her martyrdom on the wheel.","i":"Catherine, a learned princess of Alexandria, debated fifty pagan philosophers and converted them, and the emperor Maxentius imprisoned her. In the Golden Legend a dove fed her in prison and angels tended her wounds. She was condemned to be broken on a wheel, which shattered, and was finally beheaded. The palm is the sign of the martyr, and the dove the messenger of heavenly comfort.","c":"A prison scene tempts a painter to heighten the darkness, but Veronese keeps his color rich and the light generous. The saint's elegance and dignity suit his taste, and the dove adds a note of tenderness. Compared with Tintoretto's drama, the picture offers consolation more than anguish, and its grace over gloom is typical of his late religious pictures, a calm, luminous image of faith under duress."},"nga:46147":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 181 by 115 centimeters, showing Saint Lucy with a donor, the person who commissioned the picture, who appears in the saint's presence. Lucy is usually shown with a palm and with her eyes on a dish, her attribute. The museum's date range, about 1585 to 1595, runs past Veronese's death in 1588, so studio help is possible. Votive pictures of this kind presented the donor under the saint's protection.","i":"Lucy of Syracuse, a virgin martyr of about 304, is invoked against blindness, since her name suggests light and legend says her eyes were put out. A donor shown with her announced his devotion and asked for her protection, and in Venice many such votive paintings were placed in churches, where they secured prayers for the donor. The pairing of saint and donor in one space expresses trust that heaven listens.","c":"The pairing of a saint's elegance with the donor's plain, direct portrait is the picture's interest, for the sitter is usually given more individuality than the idealized saint. Veronese's graceful design and silvery color are present, but the late date and possible studio involvement mean some passages may feel routine. A dignified votive picture, more convincing as a likeness of a patron than as an image of sanctity."},"met:437078":{"d":"A waterfall tumbles through the rocky, wooded country of Mont-Dore, in the volcanic Massif Central of France. Trees, a goat and a man appear too, small figures that give scale to wild nature. Painted in 1818, when Michallon was in his early twenties, the canvas measures about 41 by 56 centimeters, a modest wide format that suits an intimate view of a real place rather than a grand imagined scene.","i":"Mont-Dore, in the Auvergne, was a spa town, and its waterfalls and volcanic scenery drew artists in the early nineteenth century as a nearer French alternative to the Alps. Michallon was trained in the tradition of historical landscape, in which nature was composed on classical lines, while his generation also began to paint outdoors from observation. The picture sits between those two ways of seeing: a recorded place, carefully composed.","c":"Michallon died at twenty-five, so his achievement is only a few years' work. This waterfall shows a gift for clear structure and for the weight of rock and foliage, with a freshness learned from outdoor study; it is promising rather than fully formed. It anticipates the early landscapes of Corot, who studied briefly with him, but lacks the silvery depth that Corot later found."},"nga:221599":{"d":"A small oil sketch on paper, about 16 by 22 centimeters, showing the ruins of the Forum at Pompeii. Reddish-brown stone walls, partly dilapidated, stand behind lines of tan and gray columns and free-standing arches, and pale stones litter the brown ground. Beyond are rolling gray hills and distant mountains under a blue sky with fluffy white clouds. Dated 1819, it is probably a study made on the spot, the kind of sketch an artist kept in the studio for later use.","i":"Pompeii, buried by the eruption of Vesuvius in AD 79, had been excavated since the mid-eighteenth century, and by 1819 it was a magnet for artists and travelers. The Forum was the city's civic center, with temples and a basilica. For French students in Rome, sketches like this recorded the ancient world as it appeared and supplied material for historical landscapes peopled with heroes. This one shows ruins as ruins, without figures or story.","c":"The charm of such sketches lies in their freshness: a few quick strokes catch the clarity of southern light, the weight of stone and the sweep of the hills. Free from the conventions of the finished classical landscape, they can look surprisingly modern. At this size it is slight, but the sureness is impressive for a young painter who would die at twenty-five, and it points toward Corot, who learned from him."},"nga:364":{"d":"A small panel, about 40 by 46 centimeters, painted in tempera about 1435, joins two scenes: the Annunciation, in which the angel Gabriel tells Mary she will bear Christ, and the Expulsion from Paradise, in which Adam and Eve are driven from the Garden of Eden. The pairing is deliberate, since the Annunciation begins the story of redemption that answers the Fall. A panel of this size and shape may have formed part of the predella, the band of small scenes beneath an altarpiece.","i":"Medieval theology saw Mary as the new Eve and Christ as the new Adam: Eve's disobedience brought death, and Mary's obedience at the Annunciation brought redemption. Putting the two scenes together teaches that parallel at a glance. The feast of the Annunciation on 25 March was also linked by tradition with the Creation and the Fall. In Siena, a city dedicated to the Virgin, the message of Mary's role in salvation had special weight.","c":"Giovanni di Paolo's art is one of linear fantasy, with a taste for strong outline, bright color and expressive, slightly strange faces. This panel shows his gift for narrative economy: one pictorial space for two scenes, linked by theology rather than by logic. The figures are less naturalistic than those of Florentine contemporaries such as Fra Angelico, but that nervy intensity is the pleasure. A small, vivid, inventive panel that rewards attention."},"met:436513":{"d":"Mary, Joseph and the infant Jesus appear in a temple with a man and a woman, according to the catalog: this is the Presentation of Christ in the Temple, where the child is brought to be offered to God. The panel, about 39 by 46 centimeters, is painted in tempera and gold about 1435. It matches the National Gallery's Annunciation and Expulsion from Paradise in size and date, so the two probably come from the same altarpiece.","i":"In the Gospel of Luke, forty days after the birth Mary and Joseph bring the child to the Temple in Jerusalem for the rite of purification. The old man Simeon, who had been promised he would not die before seeing the Messiah, takes the child in his arms and prophesies Mary's sorrow, and the prophetess Anna praises God. The scene is also an image of Candlemas, the feast of the candles, and an early sign that the child's destiny includes suffering.","c":"Giovanni di Paolo turns a ceremonial scene lively, with angular gestures and quick, expressive faces, and the gold and bright color set it apart from the sober Florentine approach to the same subject. As a predella panel it was meant to be read quickly and enjoyed in miniature, and it succeeds. It does not have the monumentality of a main altarpiece, but its charm is the point."},"met:436515":{"d":"Two standing saints on a gold ground: Matthew the apostle and evangelist, and Francis of Assisi. The panel, about 139 by 88 centimeters, was painted in tempera about 1435. Its size and the placement of two saints side by side suggest a compartment of a polyptych, in which saints flanked a central Madonna or other subject. Matthew is usually shown with a book, his Gospel, and Francis with the brown habit and the wounds of the stigmata.","i":"Matthew, a tax collector called by Christ, wrote the first Gospel, and his symbol is an angel. Francis (1181 or 1182 to 1226) founded the Franciscan order and received the stigmata, the wounds of Christ, in 1224. Saints beside each other in an altarpiece often reflect the church's patrons or the donor's name saints, so the pairing may point to a Franciscan setting or to someone named Matteo or Francesco.","c":"Standing saints on gold were standard business for a Sienese workshop, and Giovanni di Paolo gives them more tension than the average, with strongly outlined figures and intense, slightly unsettled expression. Without the rest of the polyptych the panel lacks context, but the individual saints have a monumental presence that holds attention. It is a sound, serious piece that shows how much character he could bring to a repeated type."},"met:436512":{"d":"A flowery garden in which angels greet the blessed: the catalog lists a garden, angels, saints, men, women and rabbits. Painted about 1445 in tempera and gold, the panel is small, about 45 by 38 centimeters, and has been transferred from its original wood to canvas, a conservation treatment. The picture is a vision of heaven as an enclosed meadow, a paradise in the old sense of a walled garden, rather than a court of stern judges.","i":"Medieval and Renaissance viewers imagined heaven as a flowering garden where the saved embrace the angels who welcome them, an image close to Dante's Paradiso. Giovanni di Paolo illustrated a manuscript of Dante's Divine Comedy in these same years, and his heavenly gardens show the same taste for the visible and the tender. The rabbits among the flowers may suggest innocence and gentleness, though their meaning in this picture is not certain.","c":"This is among Giovanni di Paolo's most delightful inventions: the flowering meadow of the blessed is imagined with gentle joy, and line, color and decorative pattern make a paradise that looks like a woven garden. It is less naturalistic than Florentine painting, but more tender and playful than their majesty. Small and exquisite, it is a good introduction to the expressive, linear Sienese imagination."},"met:436510":{"d":"A tall panel, about 145 by 81 centimeters, painted in tempera on a gold ground about 1445. The Virgin nurses the Christ Child, with two angels in attendance and a donor, the man who commissioned the picture, shown in the presence of the sacred figures. The gold ground is partly checkered with modern red glazes, a later restoration that changes the original effect. The size suits an altar or a chapel wall.","i":"The nursing Madonna, known as Maria lactans, is one of the most tender types of Marian imagery, stressing Christ's humanity and the Virgin's motherhood, and it was popular from the Middle Ages onward. In Siena the Virgin was the city's protector. A donor shown before her acknowledges the patron's piety and asks her intercession; the catalog gives no name or station for him.","c":"The type is conventional, but Giovanni di Paolo animates it with his linear grace, flowing, angular drapery and a certain unforced tenderness in the group. The later red glazes on the gold are a restorer's addition and distract from the original effect. It remains an appealing, intimate devotional image, more inward than grand."},"nga:15":{"d":"A small, wide panel, about 27 by 46 centimeters, painted about 1450 in tempera, showing the Adoration of the Magi: the three kings arrive with their retinue to worship the child and offer gifts. The wide proportions suit a predella, the band of small scenes beneath an altarpiece, and Giovanni di Paolo often painted such narratives on a small, lively scale. The subject gave painters a chance for a long procession of kings, horses and attendants.","i":"In the Gospel of Matthew, wise men from the East follow a star to Bethlehem and bring gifts of gold, frankincense and myrrh, symbols of kingship, divinity and suffering. In later tradition they became three kings, representing three ages of man and the known continents. The procession of horses and costly clothes let painters show the pageantry of courts, and the grand cavalcade recalled the pageants of Renaissance courts and cities.","c":"On a predella scale, Giovanni di Paolo's fantasy and linear energy find an ideal outlet: processions, bright colors and gold-touched costume are what the format asks for, and he supplies them with zest. As a miniature the scene works; it cannot carry the weight of a major altarpiece, but it delights in details and in the speed with which a story is told."},"met:436508":{"d":"A very tall, narrow panel, about 180 by 43 centimeters, dated 1454, with a gold ground. The catalog lists the Madonna and Child, Saint John the Baptist, other saints and musical instruments. The proportions show it was one vertical section of a larger altarpiece, in which the Virgin and saints were arranged in separate tall compartments. Musical instruments in such pictures are often held by angels, a sign of the music of heaven.","i":"In a polyptych the Madonna and Child usually occupied the center, with saints on both sides. John the Baptist, the forerunner who announced Christ, was among the most venerated of saints, and his presence marks the picture as a devotional programme rather than a single story. The date of 1454 places it in Giovanni di Paolo's mature years, when he was one of the leading painters of Siena.","c":"The panel is a fragment: its narrow, tall proportions require a vertical design, and Giovanni di Paolo's gift for pattern, flowing line and gold works well within those limits. Yet without the context of the whole altarpiece the effect is incomplete, and the figures can seem isolated. It is better admired as a piece of decorative drawing than as a self-sufficient picture."},"met:436509":{"d":"A small, nearly square panel, about 27 by 23 centimeters, painted in tempera and gold about 1460, showing the Adoration of the Magi. The catalog lists the Virgin, Christ, Saint Joseph, a cow, a donkey and horses, so the stable animals and the kings' horses share the picture with the holy family. It is a devotional picture for a private room, or a predella panel, in which the whole story of the visit is compressed into a few square inches.","i":"The ox and ass at the manger come from a verse of Isaiah and from later legend, and in art became fixtures of the Nativity. The horses recall the Magi's long journey, and the gifts of gold, frankincense and myrrh point to Christ's kingship, divinity and death. Giovanni di Paolo painted the subject several times, varying the composition each time, so the picture belongs to a series of inventive returns to a favorite theme.","c":"The compact scale suits him: the animals and figures are arranged in tight, clear groups with real delicacy. By 1460 his figures had become more elongated and mannered, with sharper, more angular draperies, and this panel shows that late style, which is more expressive than naturalistic. It is a miniature, to be enjoyed up close, and nothing in it is wasted."},"met:436514":{"d":"A very narrow panel, about 48 by 15 centimeters, on a gold ground, painted about 1470 and showing four female martyr saints: Catherine of Alexandria, Barbara, Agatha and Margaret. The shape suggests a pilaster or side element of an altarpiece, where small standing saints in a row flanked the main panels. Each saint is usually identified by an attribute: Catherine's wheel, Barbara's tower, Agatha's dish and Margaret's dragon.","i":"These four women were among the most popular virgin martyrs of the late Middle Ages. Catherine was condemned to the wheel, Barbara was shut in a tower by her father, Agatha was tortured, and Margaret was swallowed by a dragon and emerged unharmed. All chose faith and death over marriage or pagan worship, and viewers prayed to them for protection. A row of such saints in an altarpiece formed a heavenly escort for the central scene.","c":"Small and narrow, the panel leaves little room for Giovanni di Paolo's invention, but the figures' elegance and the decorative play of gold and color are pleasing. It is a late work, and the elongated, linear style is at its most mannered. As a fragment its interest is mainly decorative, a reminder of the many small elements of a large altarpiece that have survived separately."},"met:436511":{"d":"A small panel, about 29 by 22 centimeters, in tempera and gold, showing the miraculous communion of Saint Catherine of Siena. The catalog lists angels and Christ, so the picture shows the saint in her vision receiving the sacrament from heaven. The scale is intimate, that of a devotional picture or one scene in a series about her life. The date is not recorded, though Giovanni di Paolo's career ended in 1482.","i":"Catherine of Siena (1347 to 1380) was a Dominican laywoman, a mystic and an adviser of popes who urged Gregory XI to return from Avignon to Rome. In her legend she lived on little more than the Eucharist, and her biographers told of communion received miraculously from Christ or from angels. She was canonized in 1461 by Pius II, a Sienese pope, which explains why her images were in demand in the city.","c":"Small devotional narratives suit Giovanni di Paolo, who tells the story with clarity, bright color and an almost childlike directness. The angels and the Christ lend the scene a sweet solemnity, and the gold ground gives it the glow of an icon. It does not offer much psychological depth, but that is not the aim: it is a clear, tender image of a local heroine, made to be loved."},"met:438021":{"d":"A small square panel, about 30 by 30 centimeters, painted in tempera and gold, showing the mystic marriage of Saint Catherine of Siena to Christ. The catalog also lists Saint Peter and Saint Paul, so the vision is shown as a meeting of the saint with the Savior and the apostles. The scene is probably one of a set of small narrative panels, perhaps from a predella, devoted to her life. The date is not recorded.","i":"In her vision, Christ placed a ring on Catherine's finger as a sign of her union with him, and she alone could see it. The Sienese Catherine's marriage follows the older tradition of her namesake, Catherine of Alexandria, and she is distinguished by the black and white habit of the Dominican tertiary. She resisted human marriage and was the city's most celebrated daughter, so the scene had civic as well as devotional meaning.","c":"A small devotional narrative in which line and color do the work: the interplay of the figures, the bright draperies and the gold ground produce a clear, lyrical image. It is static, since the ritual hardly allows movement, and the faces are more conventional than individual. But Giovanni di Paolo's delicacy and his feeling for his city's saint give it a quiet charm."},"met:438022":{"d":"A small panel, about 30 by 24 centimeters, in tempera and gold, showing Saint Catherine of Siena exchanging her heart with Christ. The catalog lists a church among the elements, so the vision takes place within a sacred building. The scene is intimate in scale and probably one of a series devoted to Catherine's life, painted by a Sienese artist. The date is not recorded.","i":"According to her biographer Raymond of Capua, Christ appeared to Catherine in a vision, opened her side, took out her heart and gave her his own in its place. The exchange of hearts expressed the union of her will with Christ's and became one of her best-known miracles. It was a vision for contemplation rather than narrative, and the painted version offers the faithful a pattern for their own devotion.","c":"The exchange of hearts is a difficult subject, since a literal image can be gruesome, and Giovanni di Paolo handles it with the delicacy of a miniaturist, keeping the miracle calm and the gestures gentle. The small scale and the gold ground turn it into a jewel of devotion. It lacks the grandeur of major altarpieces, but it is touching and well judged, and it gives a clear account of the legend."},"nga:32682":{"d":"A woman seated in an upholstered chair by a vanity table touches the hair of a little girl leaning against her lap, while a man leans on the chair back and looks down at the child. All three have powdered hair, flushed cheeks and slight smiles, and their satins and silks glint. The woman, in gold satin and a lilac-bowed bodice, tucks blue flowers in the girl's hair; the girl, in forget-me-not blue, hugs a bundle of pink and red flowers. The man, in a jacket patterned with gold pagodas, holds a folded letter. An open box on the floor bears the signature and the date 1756.","i":"Drouais was a fashionable Paris portraitist who became a favourite of the court, and this nearly life-size family group shows the intimate, sentimental style of domestic portraiture that Rococo taste admired. The folded letter and the open box with ribbon and pearls suggest a gift or happy occasion, and the vanity, clock and fine fabrics advertise the family's comfort. The title does not name the sitters.","c":"Drouais is at his most charming here. The shimmering satin, the lace, the pagoda-patterned jacket and the gleaming vanity are painted with a relish that makes the picture a catalogue of Rococo luxury, and the mother's touch on the child's hair is natural and unforced. The faces are interchangeable in their sweetness, however, and the huge canvas is more a display of fabrics than a study of three individuals. A delightful period piece rather than a deep one."},"met:436213":{"d":"A woman with a musical instrument is the subject of this portrait of 1757, an oil on canvas of about 80 by 65 centimeters. The title says that she is said to be Madame Favart (Marie Justine Benoîte Duronceray, 1727 to 1772), a star of the Parisian comic opera, which means that the identification rests on tradition rather than proof. The music and instrument allude to her profession as a performer.","i":"Madame Favart was the most famous singer and actress of the Opéra-Comique in the 1750s, celebrated for her roles as a country girl in pastoral comedies, and the wife of the playwright Charles-Simon Favart. Portraits of performers allowed the public to see their idols, and Drouais, a fashionable society painter, could make a performer look as elegant as a lady of the court. The instrument may refer to a particular role in a rustic piece.","c":"Drouais's portraits are known for their smooth, flattering finish, and this one shows his gift for turning a performer into a vision of polite elegance. The face is pretty and generic, the fabrics glossy and the pose graceful; the instrument supplies a note of narrative. It is a polished, pleasant likeness that tells much about the taste of the age and less about the individual."},"cma:122341":{"d":"A woman in a magnificent gown is the sole subject of this portrait of 1759, an oil on canvas of about 101 by 86 centimeters, painted by François-Hubert Drouais, who like many French portraitists of the ancien régime was skilled at veiling his sitters' imperfections. Her face has been idealized, smoothed and perfected, while the main interest lies in the gown of gold and white fabric strewn with meticulously detailed flowers and lace, a particularly fine rendering of eighteenth-century printed cloth.","i":"Printed and woven silks were among the most expensive commodities of the eighteenth century, and a portrait that records every flower on a gown also records the wealth that bought it. The sitter's rank as a marquise is conveyed by the splendor of her dress rather than by her face or by symbols of office. Drouais was one of the fashionable painters for aristocratic women of the 1750s and 1760s.","c":"The focus is the gown: the printed cloth is rendered with astonishing patience. The face is sweet and bland, as the flattering formula required, and the sitter's personality is hard to read. As a portrait of a person it is conventional; as a portrait of a dress it is superb."},"met:436214":{"d":"A woman with ribbons, lace, a bow, a feather, pearls, a crucifix and a sheet of music is the subject of this portrait of 1761, an oil on canvas of about 116 by 88 centimeters. The title identifies her as Marie Rinteau, known as Mademoiselle de Verrières, an actress and singer. Her costume and accessories, rich in small details, show the skill of François-Hubert Drouais at painting fashionable dress.","i":"Marie Rinteau became the companion of Marshal Maurice de Saxe and the mother of his daughter Aurore, who was the grandmother of the novelist George Sand. The musical notation points to her career, while the crucifix, perhaps worn as a pendant, might allude to piety or simply to fashion. A portrait of a performer of her standing was a mark of success in the Paris of Louis XV.","c":"Drouais gives Rinteau a smooth, glowing finish: the lace, ribbons and pearls are described with relish, and the face is pretty, alert and slightly theatrical. The numerous accessories risk fussiness, but they tell her story of music, devotion and elegance. It is a vivid piece of social portraiture, and its interest is helped by the sitter's fascinating family history."},"met:436215":{"d":"A young princess of the French royal family is portrayed in this small oil on canvas of 1762, about 65 by 53 centimeters. She wears lace, a ribbon and a necklace of flowers, and a muff is among the details of her dress. The title gives her dates, 1734 to 1782. She was a daughter of Louis XV, and the portrait presents her apparently in a relaxed, fashionable mode rather than in the full robes of state.","i":"Madame Sophie was one of the unmarried royal daughters known as Mesdames de France, who lived at Versailles; she was twenty-eight in 1762. Royal portraits were circulated to courts and families, and a painter like Drouais, who portrayed the royal family and the king's favorite Madame de Pompadour, worked in a courtly style that made even a princess look soft and approachable.","c":"Drouais is at his best with the glossy surfaces of a courtly portrait: the lace, the ribbon and the muff are painted with skill, and the face is gentle and refined. The result is graceful but impersonal. If the princess's character does not emerge, the picture succeeds in its main business, which was to present a daughter of the king as a lovely and amiable person."},"met:436216":{"d":"A young woman in the guise of a Vestal Virgin is shown with a veil, a bouquet, a belt and a fringed striped garment in this oil on canvas of 1767, about 80 by 64 centimeters. Her flowers and veil identify the role. The sitter's name is not given. Drouais, a fashionable French portraitist, dressed her in classical costume, as many sitters of the 1760s were, in a taste for the antique stirred by the excavations at Herculaneum and Pompeii.","i":"Vestal Virgins were priestesses of Vesta in ancient Rome who tended the sacred fire and swore chastity; to be portrayed as one was a compliment to a woman's purity and devotion. Portraits in costume, called portraits historiés, flattered sitters by linking them with ideal figures, and by the 1760s the fashion for antiquity gave the Vestal a particular charm. The bouquet may be an offering.","c":"The classical disguise is more fashion than archaeology: the veil and flowers soften and flatter, and the young woman's modern prettiness shows through the antique dress. Drouais manages the combination with grace. It is a bit of a masquerade, but it is carried out with skill, and the picture catches a moment when Paris dressed as Rome."},"met:436217":{"d":"A boy and a black spaniel are the subjects of this portrait of about 1767, an oil on canvas of about 65 by 53 centimeters. The boy, whose name is not recorded in the title, is shown with his pet as a companion. Drouais was celebrated for his portraits of children, which presented them as charming and natural, in keeping with the new interest in childhood.","i":"In the second half of the eighteenth century thinkers such as Rousseau urged parents to treat childhood as a distinct, natural stage, and portraits of children became more informal and affectionate. A pet dog was a standard companion, suggesting loyalty, tenderness and the boy's gentleness. The portrait would have been commissioned by the family, and it was designed to charm.","c":"Drouais's children are among his best work, because their freshness and lack of vanity escape the formula of his adult sitters. The black dog sets off the boy's pale face, and the spaniel's fur gives scope for texture. Sentiment is a risk, but here it is held in check by observation. It is a charming, lively picture of childhood."},"nga:46033":{"d":"An oval portrait shows a woman with fair skin, rosy cheeks and elaborately dressed, powdered hair, a curl falling over her right shoulder. She wears a light dress with a low neckline and puffed sleeves, a string of pearls lying on one shoulder, and a garland of pink, yellow and light blue flowers hangs across her chest. The background is a plain, muted green. The canvas measures about 71 by 59 centimetres, and the title identifies the sitter as Jeanne Bécu, Comtesse du Barry.","i":"Jeanne Bécu, the illegitimate daughter of a seamstress, rose through Paris society to become the last official mistress of Louis XV in 1769, and a marriage of convenience gave her the title of comtesse du Barry so that she could be presented at court. Drouais, who had painted Madame de Pompadour, portrayed her in the pastoral fashion of the 1770s, with garland and pearls. After the king's death in 1774 she was removed from court; she was guillotined during the Revolution in 1793.","c":"The flowers and pearls are neat, the colouring soft, and the face has the bright prettiness that Drouais could deliver on demand, which is precisely what a royal favourite wanted. What it lacks is a sense of the woman beneath the fashion: the expression is bland, the pose conventional, and the garland more decorative than characterful. A competent, handsome court image that does its job."},"nga:1192":{"d":"Shown from the waist up in profile facing left, a young woman fills the height of the panel against an inky black ground, brightly lit from the left. Her auburn hair is pulled back under a golden net cap set with pearls and garnets, and a long ponytail wrapped with black and white pearls falls past her waist. A jeweled brooch on a black band at her brow carries the motto MERITO ET TEMPORE. She wears a patterned honey-gold dress with a low square neckline, a long pearl necklace with a ruby-and-black pendant, a jeweled belt and a red carnation.","i":"The sitter is Bianca Maria Sforza (1472 to 1510), niece of Ludovico Sforza, ruler of Milan, painted probably in 1493, the year she was married by proxy to Maximilian of Habsburg, later Holy Roman Emperor. Such a portrait served diplomacy: it showed a prospective bride's beauty and, through her jewels, her family's wealth. The carnation often signals betrothal, and the motto may be translated as by merit and in time.","c":"A fine example of the Milanese court profile, in which the contour of the face and the jewels are defined with a clear line and the skin is modeled with soft light, an echo of Leonardo's influence on his associate Predis. The profile format, descended from medals, flattens the sitter and turns her into an emblem of rank; the long neck and elaborate jewelry are striking. It is a dignified emblem, but the individual's character remains concealed."},"met:11980":{"d":"A ship and a horse appear in a long, narrow panel painted on gilt leather, just 26 by 71 centimeters. The setting is marine, and the shape suggests a frieze or a decorative panel more than a window onto space. Ryder painted this unusual support around 1880, in the dusky, simplified manner for which he became known. The title gives the setting as a smugglers' cove, a romantic hideout for illicit trade, which Ryder reduces to a few simple masses, more mood than story.","i":"Smugglers were a Romantic subject of the nineteenth century, associated with moonlit coves, secret landings and daring outlaws. Ryder drew his subjects from poetry, opera, the Bible and the sea, and tended to turn an adventure into a mood. Gilt leather, a decorative material more often used for wall coverings, gives the surface a dull glow behind the paint, an experiment typical of an artist who treated the physical stuff of painting as part of its expression.","c":"Ryder reduces a story to a few massed shapes and a hush of tone. His strength is mood: forms simplified toward abstraction, with a poetic darkness. His weakness is physical, since his habit of layering paint and varnish has caused many pictures to darken and crack, so what remains may be more somber than he intended. Even so, small works like this show why later American painters, Marsden Hartley among them, admired him."},"met:11976":{"d":"A small landscape, only 19 by 25 centimeters, is titled for the curfew hour, the time of evening when a bell once signaled that fires be banked and doors closed. The subject is simply a landscape, so the picture is best understood as a mood of dusk in the simplified, shadowy manner Ryder was developing by 1882, the date by which the picture was known.","i":"The title probably recalls the opening of Thomas Gray's Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard, 'The curfew tolls the knell of parting day', a poem every educated American knew and a standard text of evening melancholy. Ryder liked poems and used them for mood rather than illustration. Tonalist painters of his era valued dusk and moonlight as states of mind, and a title like this tells the viewer what feeling to bring.","c":"Ryder was a miniaturist of feeling, and on a panel this small he packs a whole evening. The simplified forms and low tone avoid detail; the picture is about hush. Some will find it vague, but the vagueness is the point. Like many of his pictures it has probably changed with age, and it is hard to know exactly how it first looked, which is a hazard of all his work."},"met:11975":{"d":"A bridge and trees stand in a landscape on a long, narrow panel of gilt leather, about 25 by 68 centimeters. It is nearly the same size and support as the Met's other Ryder on gilt leather, The Smugglers' Cove, painted around 1880. The unusual shape and shining ground suggest a decorative purpose, perhaps a panel to hang above a door or along a wall, rather than a conventional easel picture.","i":"A bridge in a quiet landscape can stand simply for a place or for crossing and passing, a symbol Romantic and Symbolist painters liked. Ryder's pictures often avoid explicit story, letting simple shapes suggest a mood; no source for this one is documented, and the viewer is invited to supply the meaning. The gilt leather adds a warm, slightly antique glow that suits the sense of a half-remembered place.","c":"A long thin panel suits Ryder: the bridge becomes an arc across the format and the trees a mass. Even at small scale the design is bold, with flat shapes and low tone. But the support and layered paint have aged, and some subtlety has probably been lost. It is a modest, curious picture, and its chief value is as evidence of how freely Ryder experimented with materials."},"met:11981":{"d":"A small boat rides the sea in a nearly square panel, about 29 by 31 centimeters. The title borrows from Victor Hugo's novel Les Travailleurs de la mer, published in 1866, a story of labor and struggle against the ocean. Ryder's marines of the 1880s reduce such themes to a few simple shapes in a dusky tone, and this one dates from about 1880 to 1885.","i":"Hugo's novel Toilers of the Sea tells of a Channel Islands seaman, Gilliatt, who risks his life salvaging an engine from a wrecked ship, battling storm, tide and an octopus. Ryder gives no story, but the title sets the image of a lone boat in the context of heroic labor against the power of nature. His marines combine Romantic reverence for the sea with a personal reduction: the boat is small, and the sea and sky are large.","c":"Few American paintings hold so much mystery in so little space. Ryder simplifies drastically, using big shapes and restrained tone, to make a tiny boat feel alone before eternity, and the small panel adds to the effect. If a fault is wanted, it is that age and his reworking have probably dulled the surface, but the design remains strong. It is a key picture for understanding why Ryder is thought of as an American visionary."},"met:11979":{"d":"A boat and clouds are set on a moonlit sea in a small square panel of about 29 by 31 centimeters. The Met gives the medium as oil and possibly wax, one of Ryder's experiments with the paint itself, which he built up in layers. The date is broad, between 1870 and 1890, because Ryder rarely dated his pictures and often reworked them over years, so that a single panel can record a long sequence of changes.","i":"Moonlit marines were Ryder's signature, an American counterpart to the Romantic seascapes of Europe, in which the sea at night stood for solitude, danger and the infinite. He aimed to paint not the look of the sea but its feeling, simplifying forms toward the essential. The small boat under a large sky is a familiar image of human fragility, and it carries the quiet weight of a private meditation.","c":"Ryder reduces sea, sky and boat to a few interlocking shapes and lets the light of the moon do the rest. The surface, made of oil and possibly wax, gives a glow that is partly accident, since his methods have left many pictures darker and more cracked than he intended. For viewers who can accept some damage, the result remains poetic, and it is easy to see why later artists learned from his economy."},"nga:32624":{"d":"Three nude women bathing in a river by moonlight scramble away, startled by a man on horseback in a winged gold helmet and armor who rides beside a massive tree. The tree's branches sweep up and to the right from a thick central trunk to span the top of the canvas. Golden clouds veil the glowing full moon in the upper left, and the river reflects bright clouds and a muted blue sky. The palette is almost entirely warm white, dark moss green, smoke gray and touches of teal blue; cracks run across the surface.","i":"The subject comes from Wagner's Ring cycle: the Rhine maidens, guardians of the river's gold, confront the hero Siegfried, who possesses the ring made from it. The scene prefigures the hero's doom, since the maidens warn him and he refuses to return the ring. Ryder was captivated by Wagner's operas, and the myth of greed, fate and lost innocence suited his taste for symbolic, inward subjects that could not be tied to a literal setting.","c":"Ryder is among the most original American painters of his era. His moonlit pictures, with simplified, rhythmic masses and glowing, thickly worked surfaces, are more concerned with mood than with description. The swelling tree dominates and the figures are small and indistinct, so the drama is carried by the pattern of light and dark. His experimental technique left cracks that mar the surface, but the vision holds: dreamlike, poetic, more music than illustration."},"met:11978":{"d":"A man and cattle appear in a small landscape, about 24 by 36 centimeters, which the Met dates tentatively to 1897 or 1898. It is a pastoral subject of herdsman and herd. The uncertain date is typical of Ryder, who worked on pictures for years and rarely dated them, and so the picture can be placed only roughly in his career. Its small size suits a private room, where such a quiet scene could be studied at close range.","i":"Pastoral landscapes with herdsmen and cattle had been favorites of American collectors since the 1850s, when Barbizon painters like Millet and Troyon became fashionable in the United States. Ryder borrows the motif but treats it through mood rather than anecdote, reducing forms to broad masses. Such a picture says less about farm life than about a feeling of quiet at the end of a day.","c":"Ryder's landscapes without water are rarer and less known than his marines, and this one shows him turning the same reduction to the land. The date is uncertain and the surface probably altered, so judgment must be provisional. At this size it is a quiet, personal note, interesting for what it shows of his range rather than as one of his strongest statements."},"nga:39760":{"d":"A white horse stands at the center of a muted brown-and-yellow landscape, hitched to a cart with one large wheel and a load of yellow-brown material. It waits on a faint path that runs toward the lower left. At the right a figure in jacket and hat, seen from behind and painted almost entirely in dark browns and black, looks down at the harness. A flat brown field fills the left, tall dark trees rise on the right, and a pale tan sky closes the scene. The small canvas reduces rural labor to a few broad masses.","i":"A farm horse and a figure at a humble task belong to the long tradition of rural genre painting, and in the 1870s Ryder's low-toned landscapes stood close to the French Barbizon painters whom American collectors then admired. The title names a small chore, yet the picture is usually read less as anecdote than as mood: patience, work and a hushed, unhurried air. Ryder was already more interested in the effect of dark and light masses than in telling a story.","c":"The near-monochrome palette is the picture's gamble, and it largely pays off: the white horse becomes a single bright note in a field of browns, while the dark figure and trees anchor the sides. The forms are broad and simplified, with little detail to hold interest at close range. It is a modest early work, but it already shows the reduction to a few weighty masses that Ryder would push much further in his later, visionary pictures."},"met:11974":{"d":"A boat lies at sea beneath a cloud, according to the title, on a canvas about 51 by 61 centimeters dated to about 1900. It belongs to Ryder's late marines, the pictures in which a single small vessel sits in a large space of water and sky. By 1900 he had reduced the elements of such scenes to the essentials. Ryder, then in his early fifties and living as a recluse in New York, worked slowly on a few pictures at a time.","i":"In Ryder's late marines the lone sailboat becomes an emblem of isolation, a human venture against the elements, and the cloud of the title may add a note of foreboding. He painted slowly, glazing and reworking for years, and his pictures became increasingly personal and symbolic. The title leaves open whether the cloud is weather or mood, and Ryder, an unusually private man, would not have told.","c":"Late Ryder is the most abstract and the most uneven. When the design holds, as a dark mass against a lighter sky, it has a grandeur out of all proportion to the size of the canvas. When the surface has been overworked, as his habit invited, the picture can become heavy and murky. A canvas of about 1900 should be seen with allowance for age and for his endless reworking."},"met:437757":{"d":"An elderly woman is shown at prayer with a rosary, in a compact canvas about 78 centimeters high. It belongs to the small-scale, single-figure devotional studies that Matthias Stom, a Netherlandish painter working in Italy, produced in the wake of Caravaggio. His pictures of this kind usually set a figure in strong, directed light against deep shadow, so that the face and the hands carry the feeling. The sitter is not named, and the image is best understood as a type of devotion rather than a portrait.","i":"The rosary, a cycle of prayers counted on beads, was promoted strongly by the Catholic Church after the Council of Trent, and an old woman telling her beads was a ready emblem of humble, steady devotion. Whether she is an anonymous worshipper, a type of the pious widow or a particular saint is not settled by the title; the first reading is the most likely. Such figures gave viewers in a Catholic setting an example for their own private prayer.","c":"Single-figure studies suit Stom's manner, which depends on light and sharp observation more than on grand composition. The Caravaggesque formula, which he probably learned from the Utrecht followers of Caravaggio and refined in Italy, is a dependable route to intensity: a raking light on age and a few well-judged accessories. Its risk is routine, since the effect of piety can be produced almost by recipe."},"nga:34137":{"d":"Shown from the waist up in a vertical portrait, a white-haired man in a dark blue military coat turns to the left and looks in that direction. His hair is brushed back from a high forehead and tied at the nape; he has brown eyes, a pointed nose, pursed lips and a double chin. The coat has brass buttons, wide buff facings and a gold epaulette bearing two silver stars, over a ruffled cravat, and a blue-and-yellow medal with a gold eagle is pinned to the lapel. The background glows golden brown at the right and darkens to coffee brown at the left.","i":"William Smallwood commanded Maryland troops in the Revolutionary War and rose to major general, a rank consistent with the two stars on the epaulette. The eagle badge is probably that of the Society of the Cincinnati, the officers' fraternity founded in 1783. The portrait, dated 1785 to 1788, falls within his years as governor of Maryland and presents him as a soldier-statesman of the new republic. Pine, born in London, had settled in Philadelphia in 1784, where he painted leading figures of the Revolution.","c":"Pine, a London-born portraitist, gives Smallwood a plain, frank likeness: the face is firmly modeled, the double chin is not disguised, and the gold lace and facings are handled with convincing sheen. The sober gradient background keeps attention on the sitter. It lacks the dash of the best British portraiture of the day and the pose is stock, but as a record of a Revolutionary officer by an experienced hand it is direct and credible."},"met:441230":{"d":"A female martyr saint is shown half-length in this oil on a wooden panel, about 51 by 42 centimeters, painted about 1650. The museum's keywords record a woman, a portrait and a saint, and give no name, so her attributes, such as the palm of martyrdom, a wheel, a sword or a crown, her gaze and the colors are left unstated. Nuvolone, a Milanese painter, was known for graceful devotional pictures with soft, idealized faces.","i":"Female martyr saints, among them Catherine, Agatha, Lucy, Cecilia and Barbara, were a favorite subject in Counter-Reformation Italy, and are usually identified by an attribute that recalls the manner of their death. They were presented with sweet, resigned faces and a hint of ecstasy, to move viewers to devotion and to admiration of their courage. Nuvolone worked in Milan, a center of Counter-Reformation piety under the Borromeo cardinals, and met a steady demand for such images.","c":"Nuvolone's saints are admired for their graceful, Reni-like softness and clear, luminous color, and criticized for sweetness. Without a named subject or a description of the surface, the picture is best judged on the quality of its type: the refinement of expression, the polish of the finish and the harmony of its color. As a Lombard devotional painting it is a characteristic example of the artist's gentler side."},"met:437368":{"d":"The Virgin, the Christ Child, Saint Joseph and an angel appear together in a small devotional painting in tempera, about 56 by 38 centimeters. The work has been transferred from its original wooden panel to canvas, a drastic conservation treatment that can thin or flatten a paint surface. Raffaellino del Garbo, a Florentine pupil of Filippino Lippi, drew his figures in the soft, flowing line of late fifteenth-century Florence, and pictures of this size were usually made to hang in a private room rather than a church.","i":"The Holy Family, which adds Joseph to the Virgin and Child, grew popular in Italy in the later fifteenth century, partly as devotion to Joseph as the Child's patient protector spread. The angel marks the scene as sacred rather than ordinary home life. A small panel like this would have hung in a bedroom or private chapel, inviting the household to model its own family life on this one and to pray before it.","c":"Raffaellino worked in the orbit of Filippino Lippi and, behind him, Botticelli, and his figures share their sinuous line and wistful expressions without matching their inventive force. At this size the borrowed grace is enough to please, and the subject asks for tenderness rather than drama. The transfer to canvas makes the original surface hard to judge, so what survives reads mainly as drawing: attractive, competent and a little sweet."},"met:437157":{"d":"Christ appears in a wild, wooded landscape surrounded by animals, among them a bear, a lion, a deer, a fox and a bird, with an angel in attendance. The oil on canvas is small, about 46 by 55 centimeters, and wider than it is tall, a format suited to a landscape crowded with creatures. It is an early work by the Brescian painter, made when he was in his late teens or early twenties.","i":"The scene refers to the forty days Christ spent fasting in the desert after his baptism. The Gospel of Mark says he was \"with the wild beasts\" and that angels attended him; the animals are usually read as a sign of the peace between humanity and creation lost in Eden and restored in Christ, the new Adam. Small canvases like this offered a devotional subject that also allowed the pleasures of landscape and animal painting.","c":"The theme of Christ among the beasts is unusual, and it gives Moretto room for the Venetian taste for landscape and color that reached Brescia in his youth. The picture is fresh rather than commanding, and the young painter's hand is not yet the grave, silvery one of his mature altarpieces. As a small, inventive episode it is charming, and it shows a side of Moretto that his later church commissions seldom allowed."},"met:437158":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed on a nearly square canvas, about 87 by 81 centimeters, with an hourglass among the objects included in the picture. Moretto painted it in the early 1520s, in the years when Titian's polyptych for Santi Nazaro e Celso (1522) was making Venetian color known in Brescia, while the city's painters kept a sober, direct way of looking at the sitter. The hourglass makes this more than a plain likeness: it brings the idea of passing time into the picture.","i":"The hourglass is a familiar emblem of the brevity of life. In a portrait it can mean a reminder of mortality, a sign of the sitter's sober outlook, or a pointer to the careful use of time. Because the sitter's name is lost, the picture is best read as a statement of character rather than rank: a thoughtful man, probably a local gentleman, who wished to be remembered together with a prompt to reflection.","c":"Moretto is at his best as a portraitist when he holds back: a cool, silvery tonality, no flattery, and a sitter allowed to speak for himself give his portraits a quiet authority that sets them apart from the grander display of Venetian examples. This early portrait already shows that restraint, with the hourglass as the only explicit comment on the sitter. Its limit is the anonymity of the man, whose reserve can read as reticence."},"nga:41592":{"d":"Three mourners support the gray, lifeless body of Christ on a stone ledge. The Virgin, in a white veil and navy cloak, presses her face to his, her mouth open in grief; a curly-haired man in emerald green and vivid red holds his arm and weeps; a woman with loose auburn hair clasps his crossed ankles. Blood trickles from the wound in his side and the holes in his feet. A white ointment jar stands on the ground, a rocky tomb opens at the right, and hills, buildings and clouds recede beyond.","i":"The scene lies between the Lamentation and the Entombment. By the usual conventions the weeping man in green and red is Saint John, the woman at Christ's feet is Mary Magdalene, identified by her loosened hair and the ointment jar, and the veiled woman is the Virgin. The upright panel keeps the group close to the viewer, inviting shared mourning, which suited the emotional emphasis of sixteenth-century Catholic devotion. The ash-pale body is the still center of the grief.","c":"The picture's strength is its emotional directness. The Virgin's open-mouthed grief and Magdalene's hair-veiled face are rendered without prettification, and the ash-pale body stands out against saturated green and red. The compact pyramid of figures is easy to read. Its force is unusually raw for Moretto, whose mature manner is generally calmer, and the ledge and tomb push the figures toward the front in a slightly staged way, but the result is affecting."},"nga:371":{"d":"Shown to the thighs, a woman in a cream-white satin gown trimmed with gold bows looks out from the corners of her brown eyes while turning her head to the viewer's left. A gold and pearl headdress holds back chestnut hair, and a ruffled white collar lies over two heavy gold necklaces. A white chemise patterned in black is pulled through slits in the sleeves, and three gold rings glint on her hands. She holds white gloves and a pink carnation, her hand resting on a red-covered table, and a dark green feather fan. A green velvet curtain parts to reveal a stone pillar.","i":"The costume announces wealth, and the accessories hint at a purpose. The pink carnation is a familiar Renaissance token of love and betrothal, and gloves were customary gifts at a marriage, so the portrait may commemorate an engagement or wedding. The sitter is unnamed. The curtain and pillar are stock signs of dignity borrowed from grander portraits; a picture like this would hang in the family palace, recording her person and the standing of her house.","c":"White on white is a hard test, and Moretto passes it with a cool, silvery key that separates the satin, the ruffled collar and the embroidered chemise without losing the face. The sidelong glance gives the sitter presence, and the accessories are placed with discretion rather than display. If anything the costume overshadows the woman, whose features are less individually characterized than her clothes, but the sober elegance is characteristic of Moretto at his most polished."},"met:437156":{"d":"The Entombment of Christ is shown on a large upright canvas about 2.4 meters high, with the Virgin Mary and Mary Magdalene among the mourners; grief is the subject as much as the event. The scale is that of an altarpiece. The date, 1554, is the year of the painter's death, so this belongs to Moretto's last phase, usually described as more austere than his early, Venetian-influenced work.","i":"The Gospels describe Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus laying the body in a rock tomb while the women who had followed Jesus watched. For an altarpiece of the 1550s, shaped by the reforms of the Council of Trent, the aim was to move the viewer through a clear, sober account of the Passion rather than through ornament. Mary Magdalene, who came to anoint the body, and the Virgin offer two models of grief for the beholder to share.","c":"Moretto's late altarpieces trade the Venetian glow of his youth for cooler color and a more austere devotional tone, and this one appears to follow suit. Its scale demands a firm composition, and it earns respect for restraint rather than excitement: the grief is dignified, not theatrical. Next to the high drama of Tintoretto or the warmth of Titian it can seem quiet, but quiet was a considered aim in post-Tridentine Brescia."},"met:440725":{"d":"A man and a woman, lifted out of the great crowd of Frith's Derby Day, fill a small upright canvas about 46 by 32 centimeters. The picture is dated 1860, two years after the large painting was shown at the Royal Academy, so it is probably a later, reduced version of the central group rather than a preparatory sketch. Frith's manner is crisp and highly finished, with bright color and close attention to dress and expression.","i":"Derby Day, which Frith painted in 1856–58, was the sensation of the 1858 Royal Academy, so popular that a rail had to be placed in front of it. It showed Victorian society mixed together on Epsom Downs, from carriage gentry to gamblers, acrobats and pickpockets, and viewers read it like a modern Hogarth, full of small morals about luck, class and temptation. Isolating the central pair turns that survey into something closer to a pair of character portraits.","c":"Frith was a superb reporter of Victorian manners, but his gift depends on accumulation: the crowd gives meaning to every face. Cut down to two figures, the picture keeps the crisp drawing and well-observed costume yet loses much of the narrative momentum that made the original famous. It is a pleasant, competent keepsake beside the great canvas, useful mainly as a way of looking closely at what the vast original can only offer in passing."},"nga:137183":{"d":"Three men in black garments and long white aprons stand behind a table on which a naked corpse lies on a white sheet, its head to the right and its face falling toward the viewer. The sunken eyes and the sharply protruding ribs and hips are the most precise details; the men are broadly sketched, their faces indistinct. The gray-haired man at the left touches the corpse's pelvis, the man in the middle may be taking notes, and the third leans over the table. A thick arch with two deep windows rises behind them, and two unlit lamps hang above.","i":"Gervex, in his early twenties, chose a subject from modern science: the dissection of the dead at the Hôtel-Dieu, the old Paris hospital beside Notre-Dame. The theme descends from anatomy pictures such as Rembrandt's Anatomy Lesson of Dr. Tulp, but without ceremony or portrait groups: the men are anonymous workers and the corpse is a nameless body. Naturalist painters of the 1870s prized such unidealized subjects, and Gervex later returned to the hospital theater with a picture of the surgeon Péan.","c":"As a sketch it is more convincing than many finished academic pictures of the time. The pallor of the body, the dim vault and the faceless gowned men create a sober, clinical mood, and the loose handling suggests the influence of Manet and the Impressionist circle. The weakness is human content: with faces barely stated, interest rests on the choice of subject rather than on any individual. It is a bold, unsentimental piece of modern-life painting."},"nga:46440":{"d":"A woman is shown in profile, her head turned to the left, in a head-and-shoulders portrait on a plain, light ground. Her dark hair is styled back, her collar is high with ruffled edges, and her features are drawn firmly: a sharp nose, defined cheekbones, a firm chin. The artist signed it \"A. Legros\" and dated it 1875 in the upper right. The pose and the bare background leave nothing to look at but the shape of the head, and the sitter is not named.","i":"The strict profile recalls Italian Renaissance portraits and medals. Legros, a French-born artist who settled in London in 1863, admired the old masters, and the format may reflect that taste. A profile avoids flattering expression and reads as shape and character. The sitter's name is not given here, so the picture is best taken as a study in severity of form, the austere kind of likeness favored by artists who wanted to return portraiture to fundamentals.","c":"Legros' portraits favor sobriety over charm, and this one is accordingly spare: a strong contour, a restrained palette and a bare ground, with the force carried by the line of nose, cheek and chin. It works best as drawing in paint. The lack of atmosphere or personal detail keeps it an exercise in austerity, but it shows the old-master discipline that made him a respected teacher."},"met:436060":{"d":"Bacchus, god of wine, appears in a landscape with nymphs and a putto, a small cherubic child. The panel measures about 58 by 72 centimeters, and the title puts the landscape on a par with the figures: this is a mythological picture in which the setting matters as much as the story. Painted probably in the 1640s, it is the kind of small, decorative cabinet picture that Dutch collectors hung in a private room.","i":"Bacchus, the Roman god of wine, is usually shown young and wreathed in vine leaves, with grapes, a cup and a following of nymphs and satyrs. Such scenes let painters present a classical idyll of ease and plenty, with the nude and the pastoral as respectable subjects. In Holland they could be read lightly, as pleasure, or by moralizing viewers as a warning against excess. Which reading applies here cannot be said from the title alone.","c":"Cuylenborch, who died in his late thirties, is a minor name, and this panel is best taken as a pleasing example of a widespread fashion rather than a personal statement. Small mythologies in sunny landscapes were produced in quantity for Dutch collectors; their appeal lies in grace of pose and soft setting, not depth of invention. The balance between figures and landscape is the thing to judge."},"nga:59883":{"d":"Five figures sit and stand in a row across a wide canvas. At the left a woman in a gray dress with lace at the neckline holds a folded paper and draws a girl to her side; two girls in white dresses hold a pink flower and a small bud; a seated man in a dark jacket, yellow vest, white cravat and green trousers holds a small book; and a boy in a green coat with gold buttons stands partly behind him. A black semicircle fills the space behind them, against a brown ground that darkens toward the top.","i":"Johnson worked in Baltimore, where he is generally regarded as the earliest documented professional African American portraitist; his clients were largely prosperous white families such as this one, which is unnamed. The paper in the mother's lap and the book in the father's hand point to literacy and learning, qualities families of the early republic liked to display. The flower and bud carried by the girls are usually read as emblems of youth and promise.","c":"Johnson arranges the five figures almost as a frieze, with clothes painted as clean, flat areas of color picked out with fine lace. The result is stylized rather than naturalistic, and his sitters tend to be more alike than individual. The black semicircle behind them is an unusual, bold device that gathers the group and sets off the pale dresses. It lacks depth, but the controlled color and rhythmic spacing give it a quiet authority."},"met:11269":{"d":"A boy and a girl, named in the title as Edward and Sarah Rutter, are portrayed together on a nearly square canvas about 91 by 81 centimeters, with a bird and strawberries among the details that enliven the picture. Johnson's children are typically drawn as simplified, clearly outlined figures in bright, carefully finished color. The portrait dates from about 1805, when he was working in Baltimore for local families.","i":"Strawberries and birds appear regularly in portraits of children from this period. The fruit is generally read as a sign of innocence and the sweetness of youth, and a small bird as a pet or as an emblem of the soul and of freedom, though in a children's portrait either may simply record a favorite treat or companion. Portraying siblings together reflects the early republic's idealization of family affection and childhood.","c":"Children suited Johnson's gifts, since his smooth, simplified faces and clear, flat color convey youth and freshness without strain. The bird and the fruit enliven what might otherwise be a stiff double portrait, giving the sitters something to hold and the viewer something to enjoy. The pose is formal and the modeling slight, but the crisp outline and cheerful color make the picture memorable."},"nga:52953":{"d":"A woman sits in a dark reddish-brown chair with a dark green cushion, shown from the knees up, her body turned slightly left while she faces the viewer. Her dark curly hair is held by a white headband, and her dark red dress has lace at the neckline and at the cuffs of its short sleeves. One hand holds a small open book and the other a twig with round green leaves. At the left a white window opens on leafy trees and a sky that shades from gray to pale blue and pink; a dark gray wall fills the right.","i":"The book and the twig are the sitter's attributes. A book in a woman's hand usually signals literacy, refinement and piety, and a sprig of green leaves is a common emblem of life, constancy or growth in American portraits of the period, although nothing here fixes its meaning. The window view, a familiar device, links the quiet interior to the world beyond. The portrait is a plain record of a woman who wished to be seen as educated and respectable.","c":"The picture's best passage is the window: a small, clear view of trees and graded sky that adds depth to an otherwise flat arrangement. The dark chair, dark wall and dark red dress make a restrained harmony, and the white headband and lace give crisp accents. The figure remains somewhat stiff, as Johnson's sitters often are, but the economy of means and the clean silhouette give it presence."},"nga:56735":{"d":"A man identified only as Mr. Baylor is portrayed on a small canvas about 61 by 50 centimeters. The sitter has red hair. Johnson's portraits of this period typically show the sitter bust-length or to the waist, in the fashionable dress of the early republic, with little modeling, a clearly outlined silhouette and an even, steady light. Johnson was working in Baltimore at this date, painting local families.","i":"Johnson's Baltimore clients were typically merchants, professionals and their families, who commissioned likenesses as signs of standing in a young, commercial city. The title gives only a surname, so the sitter's rank and occupation are not recorded in the picture itself. It is best read as a straightforward declaration of respectability. Pictures of this size were modest commissions, hung at home as records of the household.","c":"Portraits of this type depend largely on the face and the clothes, and Johnson's flattened, linear style serves the task well: a clear silhouette, even light and a plain statement of the sitter. The effect is dignified but impersonal, and the red hair is the strongest individual note. It is a dependable, rather than inspired, example of Johnson's work, useful for showing how his repertoire of types was adapted to each client."},"nga:166457":{"d":"A mother and her two daughters wear nearly matching white dresses of light, gauzy fabric gathered below the bust, and all three look out at the viewer. The woman sits at the left on a dark sofa with nail-head trim against an elephant-gray wall, a white band in her curling brown hair and a sprig of strawberries in her lap. Her arm wraps around the younger child, a blond girl in a coral necklace who stands on the sofa at the center. At the right the older girl, black-haired and wearing a gold necklace, holds a basket of strawberries and rests her other hand on a parasol.","i":"The white, high-waisted dresses are the neoclassical fashion of the Federal period, and the sofa and its nail-head trim point to a comfortably furnished household. The mother's embrace presents her as an affectionate, attentive parent, in line with the ideal sometimes called republican motherhood. Strawberries, carried by both mother and daughter, are usually read as emblems of sweetness and fruitfulness; the parasol marks the older girl's genteel upbringing.","c":"This is among Johnson's most ambitious designs: three figures locked together by the mother's embracing arm, the white dresses forming a single field against the gray wall, and the sofa's curve closing the group at the left. The faces follow Johnson's habit of simplification, so individuality is limited, but the pattern is strong and the repeated strawberries tie the figures together. It shows Johnson at his most decorative and assured."},"nga:45955":{"d":"Three children in matching pine-green suits stand in a row across a wide canvas, facing the viewer with round, rosy faces. The tallest, at the right, has shoulder-length cinnamon hair, rests an arm on the middle child's shoulder and holds a basket of pink roses; the two smaller blond children hold hands, one with a branch of dark red cherries, the other with a pink rose. Ruffled collars and black boots complete the costumes. A black dog with a bird in its mouth stands at the lower right, and a window alcove opens onto a slender tree, pale mountains and blue sky.","i":"Johnson's children's portraits are formal, but the props are chosen with care. Roses and cherries are usually read as emblems of youth and the sweetness of childhood, while the dog carrying a bird adds a note of country life and perhaps a reminder that early pleasures are fragile. The matching suits and linked arms and hands present the children as a united family group, and the window view places them in a comfortable, well-ordered home.","c":"This is one of Johnson's best-known pictures, and deservedly. The row of identical green suits sets up a rhythm, the linked arms and clasped hands turn the row into a unit, and the dog in the corner adds welcome animation, while the window opens depth behind the flat figures. The drawing is simple and the modeling slight, with the faces nearly interchangeable, yet the clear color and stately arrangement have a charm that more sophisticated portraiture often lacks."},"nga:59884":{"d":"A young woman sits on a wooden chair, seen from the knees up, her body angled left while her head turns toward the viewer. She has short, curly blond hair and a round, fair face, and wears a white gown with a wide neckline and blue bows on the shoulders, with a brown necklace and round gold pendant. One hand holds a small brown book with green edges; the other rests on a brown table and holds a pink rose, beside a sheet of paper with lines of writing. The background is a plain dark gray.","i":"The book, the rose and the written paper are the attributes of an educated, gracefully accomplished young woman: reading and writing were signs of a good upbringing, and the rose speaks of beauty and youth. The lines on the paper are not legible, so they work as a sign of literacy rather than a message; they might stand for a letter or verse. The portrait presents the sitter as the cultivated young woman prized in the early republic.","c":"The plain dark ground and the white dress make a bold, simple contrast, and Johnson's linear, flattened manner suits this spare design. The blue bows and pink rose are the only strong accents, and the book and paper are rendered with care. The face, as often with him, is round and evenly lit, with little psychological probing, but the picture's quiet clarity and crisp silhouette give it an appealing directness."},"met:436241":{"d":"A herd of cattle wades across a ford in a river, with a horse and a dog among the animals, on a small, wide canvas about 36 by 62 centimeters. The picture is a rural landscape in which the beasts and the water, rather than any story, supply the interest. Dated 1836, it comes from the years after Dupré's visit to England, when he was learning to paint with a loaded brush and dark, rich tones.","i":"Cattle fording a stream is an old subject in landscape painting, best known from Dutch seventeenth-century artists such as Cuyp and Potter, whom the Barbizon painters admired. For Dupré and his colleagues, who worked in the countryside outside Paris, such a motif offered an unidealized view of rural life and weather: working animals, wet ground and moving water. The picture is probably best read as a study of place and mood rather than as an anecdote.","c":"Dupré's reputation rests on thick, dark, richly worked paint and a feeling for weather. If this canvas follows his usual manner, the effect depends on the interplay of water, sky and the animals' dark bulk, with the drawing of the beasts secondary to the mood. The heavy tonality can seem leaden, yet the unaffected observation made this kind of landscape important for the younger French painters who followed."},"nga:35089":{"d":"A group of large trees stands at the centre of a field of grass and rocks beneath a dramatic cloudy sky. Below the trees a small shape that could be a person looks up, and farther back a few small triangular forms might be houses. The brushstrokes are expressive and the smaller shapes carry little detail. Grass and foliage are painted in earthy browns, oranges and greens, and the blue sky is piled with thick grey and white clouds. The canvas measures about 32 by 42 centimetres.","i":"Dupré, one of the Barbizon painters, loved oaks and stormy skies, and gave them a weight and emotion unusual in French landscape of his day. The old oak was a favourite subject of the Barbizon generation, who found in a single great tree a symbol of endurance and the forest's age. The tiny figure beneath the trees provides scale and a human response to the grandeur of nature.","c":"A small canvas with a big temperament. The thick, brisk brushwork and the heavy sky give the landscape an almost theatrical drama, and the trees are massed with real authority. It can also feel overheated: the earthy palette is dark, the details are indistinct, and the tiny figure does little. Still, as a quick, forceful statement of the old oak theme, it shows the painter's talent for turning ordinary country into weather."},"met:438030":{"d":"The broad valley of the Loire is shown on a small wood panel about 27 by 49 centimeters, with the river running through the landscape. The panel is undated, and its modest size and support are typical of the oil sketches and small cabinet pieces that Dupré and his Barbizon colleagues made from the countryside. It is a landscape with no story to tell: place, light and atmosphere are its whole subject.","i":"The Loire, France's longest river, runs through the traditional heart of the French countryside, and its valley was a popular motif for landscape painters. For the Barbizon generation, a landscape did not need a story or a classical pretext: weather, light and the mood of a place were subject enough, and a modest panel like this could express them in the most direct way, without the staging of an exhibition picture.","c":"Small panels were where Dupré could work most freely, and the best of them have a spontaneity that his larger exhibition pieces can lack. Without a date or a documented context this one is hard to place precisely, but as a record of a place and a mood it is typical: dark, rich and unforced. Its modest ambition limits it, though the honesty of the approach is attractive."},"nga:131415":{"d":"In a dim room lit by a window at the left, an old woman in profile sits in a wooden chair cutting a slice into the top of a loaf in her lap, while a boy stands at her knee with his head bowed over clasped hands. Her wrinkled face, white lace-edged scarf and pleated ruff are brightly lit, as is a nearby spinning wheel with a tuft of unspun wool on its distaff. A plate with a yellow substance, perhaps butter, rests on a three-legged stool. The boy's brown jacket is frayed at the cuffs.","i":"Saying grace before a meal was a mark of Dutch Protestant piety, and the picture is a quiet sermon on thankfulness and humility: the child prays while the old woman holds the bread, and the spinning wheel adds the virtue of industry. The woman may be a grandmother or a nurse. The scene continues the Leiden tradition of Gerrit Dou, Mieris's teacher, whose small, finely finished pictures of everyday moral subjects were prized by collectors.","c":"Mieris was in his teens or only just out of them when he painted this, and it shows Dou's lessons clearly: a dim interior, a bright pool of light, patient attention to the texture of cloth, wood and skin. The old woman's face is observed without flattery, and the glint on the spinning wheel is a small triumph. The composition is simple and a little static, and the mood earnest rather than silky, but the sincerity is appealing."},"nga:205573":{"d":"A bearded soldier sits at a table before an arched window, smoking a long white clay pipe and looking steadily at the viewer. Light from the window picks out his copper-red beard, lace collar and steel gorget, his plum-purple jacket, white sleeves banded in black and gold, and the crimson linings of his slouching boots, while the room behind falls into shadow. On the table are playing cards, more pipes, a tall glass of beer and a jug; armor, a horn and a fringed banner lie heaped in the corner, and a seven of hearts lies on the floor.","i":"Pictures of soldiers at ease belong to the Dutch guardroom tradition, which showed troops between campaigns with their pipes, cards, drink and discarded gear. Viewers probably enjoyed the display of material and color, but the clutter could also carry a moral: tobacco, cards and beer were common emblems of idleness and fleeting pleasure, and the heap of abandoned armor suggests that fighting has given way to indulgence. Whether Mieris meant a warning or simply a cheerful scene is open.","c":"Mieris, a pupil of Gerrit Dou, already shows here the virtuosity that made him famous: the sheen on the plum jacket, the glint of the gorget and the pools of window light are painted with great precision. The sitter's steady gaze adds some wit. The accumulation of props can feel like a catalog, and the picture is more about surface than psychology, but as a demonstration of fine painting it is hard to fault."},"met:437088":{"d":"A man with a musical instrument and a woman are the figures in this tiny panel, only about 15 by 11 cm, painted in oil on wood about 1678 to 1680. The title names the courtship song, a serenade. Frans van Mieris was a leading member of the Leiden fijnschilders, the fine painters, who built small, highly finished pictures with minute brushwork for collectors who admired virtuosity.","i":"Serenades belong to the world of Dutch courtship pictures, where a lute or guitar is the instrument of love and a song under a window is an offer of attention. Such scenes are usually read with a smile: the pleasure of music and the pleasure of flirtation, with a hint of folly that moralists did not miss. Pictures this small were meant to be examined at arm's length by a collector and shown to guests as a marvel of craft.","c":"Van Mieris is the proof that smaller is harder: satin, hair and the instrument must be built with tiny brushes, and the lacquered surface hides the work. He is a virtuoso, though less moving than Vermeer or Ter Borch, and his late works, like this one, are often judged more mannered than his best. The charm is in a perfectly judged, slight anecdote; the limit is that the smoothness can feel airless."},"nga:41595":{"d":"Mary stands on a golden crescent moon at the center of a tall panel, hands joined in prayer and eyes downcast, in a ruby-red dress and navy cloak, with a jeweled band in her strawberry-blond hair. Twenty winged angels lift her, sing from sheet music and play instruments, among them a lute, a harp and a dulcimer. Above, in a ring of dark clouds, two haloed men on a throne hold a crown over which a white dove hovers. Below, fields, a winding river and castle-like buildings recede into the distance, with tiny figures along the shore.","i":"The picture merges two themes: the Assumption of the Virgin, borne up by angels, and her crowning as Queen of Heaven by the Trinity. The two men and the dove above are usually read as God the Father, Christ and the Holy Spirit. The moon beneath her feet echoes the woman of Revelation, long identified with Mary. The music names the antiphon Ave regina caelorum, \"Hail, Queen of Heaven,\" so the angels sing her praise. A panel this large was probably made for a church or chapel.","c":"The Master of the Saint Lucy Legend, an anonymous Bruges painter, works here on a monumental scale in the delicate, courtly manner of late fifteenth-century Bruges, with jewel-bright robes and careful detail. The angel choir is the invention that lifts the picture: each figure has its own color and role, and the legible music gives a rare glimpse of the period's sound. The landscape below is charming, while Mary herself is serene but somewhat generic."},"met:441965":{"d":"The dead Christ is held up by angels in this canvas of about 131 by 97 cm, painted about 1710. It is a devotional image rather than a narrative scene: the body is shown for contemplation, with angels as mourners and supporters. Trevisani, born in Capodistria on the Adriatic and trained in Venice, spent most of his career in Rome, where he became a favorite of cardinals and popes and a leading painter in the refined, classicizing style of the early eighteenth century.","i":"Christ shown dead and supported by angels belongs to the type of the image of pity, made to prompt meditation on the Passion. Christ's body offered to view is usually read as a reminder of the Eucharist, the sacrifice renewed at the altar, and such compositions were hung above altars or in private chapels. Angels as mourners turn the scene from a historical event into a vision, which was the heart of its devotional appeal.","c":"Trevisani gives suffering a classical polish, learned from Maratta and Reni: smooth modeling, clear contours, idealized grief. The result is moving in a controlled way. Critics have often found his religious pictures too sweet or too composed, and a dead body that looks serene can lose the shock of death. Yet the discipline is real, and in early eighteenth-century Rome this is a fine example of tender, intellectual piety."},"cma:147696":{"d":"The Holy Family is joined by Christ's maternal grandparents, Saints Anne and Joachim, and by the baby John the Baptist, his cousin, in a canvas of about 158 by 107 cm. Anne and Joachim hold books, probably representing the Old Testament. This is a presentation sketch, a highly finished model shown to a patron for approval before the full-size altarpiece was painted, here for the church of Santa Maria in Via Lata in Rome, and it dates from about 1729.","i":"In the early 1700s Roman artists returned to basic Christian subjects, especially those centered on the Christ Child. Adding the grandparents and the infant Baptist turns the Holy Family into an extended family, stressing relationship and descent. The books held by Anne and Joachim link Christianity to the older Hebrew tradition, while the Baptist foreshadows Christ's mission. Santa Maria in Via Lata stands on the Corso, the ancient Via Lata.","c":"Trevisani's tender visual language looks back explicitly to Guido Reni, as the museum notes, and the sketch shows why that example mattered: graceful grouping, soft modeling, a family drawn into a close group. A sketch has the advantage of freshness over a finished altarpiece, and this one is revealing about the painter's planning. It is sweet to the edge of sugary, the price of the Reni tradition, but the composition is clear and the feeling sincere."},"met:438659":{"d":"Monks appear in a cloister beside the church of Gesù e Maria in Rome in this small canvas of 1808, about 50 by 39 centimeters. The architecture of the cloister takes up much of the picture, and the monks are small figures within it. François Marius Granet, who had lived in Rome since 1802, specialized in such views of convent buildings with strong contrasts of light and shadow.","i":"Granet came to Rome from Aix-en-Provence and stayed for more than twenty years, painting the interiors of churches, crypts and cloisters in a dramatic play of light that suited the Romantic taste for the monastic and the medieval. In the years after the French Revolution and the closing of many French convents, his images of monastic life may also have carried a note of nostalgia. The church of Gesù e Maria stands on Rome's Via del Corso.","c":"Granet's early interiors are among his strongest: the austere architecture, the light slicing across stone and the tiny monks give a sense of solitude. This small canvas has the quiet concentration of the type, and the effect is more atmospheric than anecdotal. The figures are summary, but their smallness is the point. It is a modest, effective picture that points toward the Romantic cult of silent places."},"met:436569":{"d":"Monks gather at vespers in the choir of a Capuchin church in Rome in this large canvas of about 197 by 148 centimeters, painted in 1814 to 1815. A window admits light into the church interior, and a man and a boy are among the figures. François Marius Granet, who lived in Rome for more than twenty years, made a specialty of such austere monastic interiors, and he painted this subject more than once.","i":"Granet's view of the Capuchin choir made his reputation: it shows the friars at the evening office, in an interior of austere simplicity and strong chiaroscuro. The Capuchins were a reformed Franciscan order devoted to poverty. In the years after the French Revolution, the monastic world of Rome offered painters and viewers a picturesque image of faith and ritual unmarked by modern upheavals.","c":"The vast picture shows Granet's strengths: the control of light as it falls through a window onto the monks' habits and architecture, and the sense of silence and ritual. The architecture has grandeur. The drawing of the faces is generic, and the large scale slightly dilutes the intimacy of his small interiors, but the effect is of calm, devotional reverie."},"met:438948":{"d":"A study of an arch is the subject of this small oil on paper, about 27 by 22 centimeters, painted around 1805 to 1810 and later laid down on canvas. The title places it in the so-called stables of the Villa of Maecenas at Tivoli, vaulted ancient Roman structures near the river Aniene. The picture is an on-the-spot study of masonry, light and shadow by François Marius Granet.","i":"Tivoli was a favorite training ground for landscape painters in the early nineteenth century, who came to sketch its waterfalls, temples and ruins. Oil sketches on paper, painted outdoors, were a means of studying effects of light directly, and artists kept them as personal references, as Granet did. The ancient arch is not a motif of grandeur but of texture: old masonry, shadow and the depth of a vault.","c":"The sketch shows the simplicity of Granet at his best: a single arch, a few tones and a strong sense of light. Without anecdote, the picture is a pure study of mass and space that anticipates the intense simplicity of later nineteenth-century painting. It is small, quick and truthful, and its modesty is its strength."},"met:437973":{"d":"A bridge and waterfalls at Tivoli are the subjects of this small, tall canvas of about 38 by 28 centimeters, painted around 1810 to 1820. Tivoli, east of Rome, offered painters a picturesque combination of cascades, ruins and bridges, and the river Aniene drops there in a dramatic series of falls. The picture is a vertical view by François Marius Granet, which suits the plunge of water.","i":"Ponte San Rocco, a bridge at Tivoli, was one of the standard motifs of visitors to the town, and artists from Claude onward recorded it. For Granet and his circle of friends in Rome, including Ingres, the area offered open-air subjects and a picturesque counterpart to the interior views for which he was best known. The waterfalls were a Romantic spectacle.","c":"Granet's feeling for architecture and light gives the bridge a convincing solidity, and the falls provide movement. The picture is more careful than his oil sketches but retains their freshness of vision. As a Roman view it is conventional in subject, though the economy and the clear tonal structure show an artist who knew exactly what he wanted."},"met:437866":{"d":"The Virgin and Child sit enthroned between Saints Peter and Paul, with angels in attendance, on a tiny panel of tempera and gold, only about 30 by 19 centimeters. The gold ground, which stands for the light of heaven, fills the space behind the figures, and the subject follows the layout of a large altarpiece reduced to miniature scale. No date is recorded here, but Lippo Vanni was active in Siena in the middle of the fourteenth century.","i":"Peter and Paul, the two chief apostles and joint patrons of Rome, are frequent companions of the Virgin in Italian altarpieces; Peter is normally identified by his keys and Paul by his sword and book. The enthroned Virgin shows Mary as Queen of Heaven, and the angels attend her like a court. A panel this small was probably meant for private devotion, or formed part of a larger portable altarpiece.","c":"Lippo Vanni belongs to the Sienese tradition of Simone Martini and the Lorenzetti brothers, and a gold-ground panel of this kind displays its core values: graceful line, jewel-like color and a sense of ceremony. He is a secondary figure beside those masters, a refined craftsman more than an innovator, and a miniature format leaves little room for the weight and space they achieved. What remains is the charm of exact finish and glowing gold."},"met:11597":{"d":"A seascape on a nearly square, upright canvas, about 76 by 64 centimetres, includes a boat and a man among the elements the museum records. No more description is given, so the weather, the sea, the type of vessel and the colours cannot be reported with confidence. The title, Marine, is the traditional term for a picture of the sea, and Moran was one of America's most successful marine painters in the 1870s.","i":"Edward Moran was born in England and emigrated to the United States as a boy, joining his brothers, including the landscape painter Thomas Moran, in the Philadelphia art world; he made his name painting ships and shores and later became known for a series of pictures of New York harbour and the Statue of Liberty. The date, about 1871, falls when he was establishing himself as a marine specialist, in a tradition influenced by Turner. The picture came to the Met in 1963 by the bequest of Emily Buch.","c":"Without the picture before the eye, the honest verdict is on the type: Moran's seascapes are skilled, dramatic and highly marketable, with strong light effects and well-drawn vessels, and sometimes more theatrical than observed. A vertical format is unusual for a marine and suggests a concentrated, perhaps stormy effect. As a specimen of American marine painting of the 1870s, popular with collectors, it is competent, though a modest example."},"met:441353":{"d":"A small interior scene, about 31 by 39 centimetres, painted in oil in 1876, with three figures: a man, a clothed woman and a nude figure, with a shield among the furnishings. The setting is Orientalist, an imagined Eastern or Ottoman household of the kind French painters made popular in the nineteenth century. The French subtitle, Un Envoi de Serbie, means a consignment or dispatch from Serbia, which probably points to a captive or a gift sent to a master.","i":"French artists of the 1860s and 1870s painted many pictures of imagined harems, slave markets and Eastern palaces, sold to a European public that wanted exotic and erotic subjects. Benjamin-Constant had travelled to Morocco in 1872 and drew on that experience for such scenes. Recent scholarship, especially since Edward Said's Orientalism of 1978, has read these pictures as fantasies that eroticised the East and made the subjection of women a spectacle, and the picture is now usually viewed critically.","c":"Benjamin-Constant was known as a brilliant technician of rich fabrics, glowing surfaces and flesh, and a small canvas like this shows that polish. The subject, however, is a stock one built to excite a buyer, and skill cannot hide the voyeurism at its centre. For all its clever handling it is shallow: an exercise in the exotic rather than an observation of any real place. Its interest today is largely historical."},"nga:75181":{"d":"On a daylit, covered terrace two women lounge along a ledge while a turbaned musician bends over a lute-like instrument. Close to the viewer, one woman reclines on a large gold pillow in an orange-red dress with gold embroidery and a transparent blouse, her red hair cascading, her eyes half-closed and one arm thrown behind her head. The other, in gold and cream with a coin-link headband, props her head on her hand and gazes out. Dark green drapery is drawn back to reveal a bright sea with three sailboats approaching a port. Oil on canvas, about 142 by 221 centimeters, about 1879.","i":"Jean-Joseph Benjamin-Constant was one of the leading Orientalist painters of the French Salons, and he had traveled in Morocco in the early 1870s. Harem and terrace scenes like this fed a Western fantasy of the East in which women lie at leisure for a male viewer; they were assembled in the studio from costume, props and sketches and reflect French colonial attitudes more than observed reality. The title implies a ruler's private world.","c":"A well-lit, technically accomplished set piece. The painter handles satin, gold embroidery and the half-shadowed musician with the bravura typical of the academic Salon, and the open view to the sea gives a convincing blaze of daylight. The content is a stock Orientalist fantasy, glamorous and condescending, and it is hard now to look at without seeing its fiction. As painting it is skillful; as an account of any real place it tells nothing."},"met:435649":{"d":"A tall canvas, about 121 by 80 centimetres, shows the Old Testament heroine Judith with the sword she used to kill the Assyrian general Holofernes. Benjamin-Constant, a painter of Orientalist and Byzantine subjects, usually staged such scenes in sumptuous costume and richly decorated settings, and the picture is dated possibly to about 1886. The Met's keywords name Judith, a sword and the story of Judith and Holofernes.","i":"In the apocryphal Book of Judith, the widow Judith enters the camp of the Assyrian general Holofernes, who is besieging her city of Bethulia, wins his trust and beheads him with his own sword once he is drunk, saving her people. Renaissance and Baroque artists saw her as a model of courage and virtue; by the late nineteenth century painters increasingly treated her as a dangerous seductress, a femme fatale, as Klimt would in 1901. The subject also suited the Orientalist taste for exotic splendour and violence.","c":"Benjamin-Constant could make costume and decor glitter, and a heroine with a sword offers him every chance. But set beside the Judiths of Caravaggio or Artemisia Gentileschi, which show physical effort and moral resolve, the limit appears: the late nineteenth-century treatment tends to turn danger into decoration. The picture is accomplished and theatrical, and it shows the Salon's taste for spectacle rather than psychological depth."},"met:441356":{"d":"A market street in Cairo, a bazaar, is the subject of this vertical oil panel, only 25 by 18 centimeters, dated 1839. Pictures of this kind typically pack stalls, awnings and shadowed arcades into a narrow view. Dauzats had been in Egypt in 1830 and 1831, so the picture was painted years after his trip, presumably from drawings made on the spot. It is small enough to hang in a study, and was probably bought as a souvenir of the East.","i":"Cairo's bazaars, with their covered streets and trades grouped by quarter, fascinated French visitors after Napoleon's Egyptian campaign opened the country to European study. Orientalist painters presented them as picturesque and timeless; scholars today point out that this timelessness was a European fiction, and that such scenes selected and simplified a living city. Dauzats's slant is topographical rather than sensational, interested in architecture and atmosphere.","c":"A small picture whose attractions are those of modest, observant Orientalism: architecture, shade and a sense of crowd. Dauzats lacks the fierce color of Delacroix and the theatrical detail of Gérôme, and there is something dutiful in his handling. But for the look of Cairo before its nineteenth-century rebuilding, he is a reliable witness, and a panel this size is an easy one to enjoy."},"met:441357":{"d":"The Great Pyramid at Giza is the subject of this vertical oil canvas, 43 by 33 centimeters, which belongs to the Orientalist taste and is dated 1830 or later. Dauzats visited Egypt in 1830 and 1831, so the view probably draws on what he saw and sketched there, worked up afterward in the studio into a compact picture of the best-known monument in the country.","i":"The Great Pyramid, built for the pharaoh Khufu about 4,500 years ago, was the most visited monument in Egypt for European travelers, who arrived in numbers after Napoleon's expedition of 1798. Dauzats traveled in Egypt and Syria in 1830 and 1831 with the patron and writer Baron Taylor, and his views fed the French public's appetite for the Orient. Such pictures blend documentation with the romance of ancient and distant places.","c":"Dauzats was a precise, well-informed topographical painter, more reporter than poet, and the Great Pyramid asks for exactly that: mass, scale and a convincing desert light. He is at his best when he lets stonework and air do the work. The picture will not shake anyone's idea of the Orientalist school, but his sober approach avoids the theatrical posing that spoiled much of the genre."},"met:12978":{"d":"An unnamed man is portrayed on a canvas about 66 by 49 centimeters, painted around 1850. The picture was formerly attributed to the American portraitist Thomas Hicks, and the identity of its painter is now uncertain. Its modest, upright format is the standard one for the middle-class portrait of mid-century America, a size made to hang in a parlor and to record a face for the family.","i":"Portraits like this were the staple of American painters around 1850, when a likeness in oil was the usual way for a family to mark its standing and keep a record of a relation. The daguerreotype, introduced in 1839, had begun to compete with painters at the cheaper end of the trade, so the oil portrait stressed the advantages of color, scale and permanence. With no name or biography given for the sitter, the picture is best read as an example of the type.","c":"As a type, this is a plain, respectable likeness of the kind that countless working portraitists produced. The loss of the Hicks attribution is a reminder of how much mid-century American portraiture is anonymous: with neither painter nor sitter secure, the picture cannot be set against any known body of work and has to stand on the presence of the face alone, which is the real test of any portrait of this kind."},"met:11082":{"d":"The painter's wife is shown in profile on a tall canvas, about 117 by 74 centimeters, painted around 1884, six years before Hicks's death. A profile reduces a sitter to a clean outline and withholds the direct gaze, so the picture depends on the drawing of the head, the carriage of the figure and the costume. Hicks was a successful New York portraitist, and this is presumably a private picture of someone he knew well rather than a paid commission.","i":"A portrait of one's own wife was both a record of affection and a demonstration of the painter's skill, free from the demands of a paying client. The profile pose recalls Renaissance portrait panels and medals; by the 1880s, with growing American interest in the Italian Renaissance, such a pose could suggest refinement and classical restraint. As with many such pictures, it shows how the artist chose to see her.","c":"Hicks's portraits are polished but conventional, and beside the bolder portraiture of Sargent and Whistler in the 1880s his approach looks cautious: sound drawing, tidy finish and an unforced pose. The profile gives the design clarity, and the tall format a dignified, upright shape. It is an affectionate portrait, valued as a document of the artist's household as much as for its style."},"nga:399":{"d":"Seen from the chest up behind a stone ledge, a light-skinned man with a mustache, a beard and shoulder-length brown hair tilts his head back and looks out from the corners of his eyes. He wears a black jacket with full sleeves over a white shirt, and his right fist rests on a closed moss-green book with a metal clasp, clutching a wad of gray cloth. A square window cut into the gray stone wall behind him shows a city under a pale blue sky, and the letters VVO appear carved into the front of the ledge.","i":"The sitter's name is unknown, but his book, his black dress and the window with a city view present a man of education and means, in the Venetian tradition of portraits behind a parapet that Giorgione and Titian had made fashionable. The head thrown back and the sidelong glance give the portrait an air of haughty wariness, and the clenched cloth hints at tension. The meaning of the letters VVO is not recorded.","c":"Cariani, born near Bergamo and active in Venice, is remembered for pictures with a certain edge, and this is among his most striking. The tilted head, the sidelong glance, the clenched fist and the cool architectural frame make a portrait with real tension, and the black coat against pale stone is handled with Venetian tonal richness. Compared with Titian's flawless poise it is rougher, but more intriguing for that."},"nga:37636":{"d":"Seen from the waist up behind a ledge, a man with a pale olive complexion rests his arm beside a small, shaggy white dog. His dark eyes look out from the corners, a mustache and trimmed beard frame lips that curve slightly up, and his brown hair merges into a dark olive-green curtain. A black robe is linked by a gold chain over a white shirt, and a sword hilt shows at his waist. A pink book stands on a box inscribed VICO LO OPUS AETA; an open window shows a robed figure on a zigzag path and ice-blue mountains.","i":"The dog is a traditional sign of fidelity, and with the sword and the book it frames the sitter as a man of both arms and learning. The distant window, with a small figure on a winding path toward the mountains, may allude to the journey of life, though that reading is uncertain. The Latin words on the box, opus (work) and aeta (probably short for aetatis, of the age), suggest a note of authorship or of the sitter's age, but the full inscription is not clear.","c":"Cariani's gift is for rich, warm color, and this portrait glows: the honeyed light, olive curtain, gold chain and white fur are built into a harmony that recalls Giorgione and the young Titian. The small dog tucked against the man's arm adds a note of charm, and the distant mountain path lends depth. The characterization is somewhat stock, but the picture's warmth is hard to resist."},"nga:90793":{"d":"Three half-length figures crowd behind a forest-green ledge. A bearded, ruddy man in a red hat with a pink band and a fur-lapelled orange coat strums a lute, glancing up and off to the left with parted lips. At left a dark-haired man in a black fur-collared cloak looks down at him, holding a red book; at right a delicate-featured young person in a red cloak with a fur lapel turns to look out at the viewer. A crumpled white cloth and a small box with a tilted lid lie on the green surface, against a gray background.","i":"Concerts were a Venetian specialty: Giorgione's and Titian's pictures had made music-making a way to show refinement, and a lute, a symbol of harmony and love, set among three friends suggests conviviality and perhaps courtship. The red book may be a songbook, and the figure who looks out draws the viewer into the group. The museum's keyword is amusement, and the picture is best read as a celebration of pleasure and companionship rather than a story.","c":"The burnt-orange fur, the reds and the green ledge against a neutral gray are the colorist's pleasure here, and the faces, each turned differently, make a simple concert a small drama of glances. The grouping is tight and slightly stiff, and the poetic haze of Giorgione is missing, but the intimacy and the sensuous color are persuasive. It is a minor master on a good day."},"nga:109784":{"d":"Two windmills stand on the far side of a gray stretch of water, one at the center and one in the distance at the right, under a pale gray sky with patches of light blue. The water reflects the green and brown of the banks, and a blue boat lies below the main mill. A bridge with white railings crosses at the right, where two dark silhouettes, an adult and a child, walk toward the mill. Leafless trees and green fields recede at the left, and low reddish-brown buildings gather around the mills.","i":"The painting records a corner of The Hague where the city's edge met the polders. Jacob Maris belonged to the Hague School, painters who in the later nineteenth century turned from picturesque views to the plain Dutch landscape of canals, mills, wet fields and wide skies, admiring the seventeenth-century landscapists and the Barbizon painters whom Maris had seen while living in Paris. The two small figures give scale and a quiet human note.","c":"Maris's gift is atmosphere: here, a pale gray key of water and sky with small touches of blue, held together by broad, assured handling. The wide format gives the scene a calm breadth, and the two silhouettes are placed with just the right restraint. Some will find the gray palette cool and the subject modest, but the tonal control is sure and typifies the Hague School at its best."},"met:436974":{"d":"A very small oil sketch, only about 14 by 18 centimeters, shows a canal with a boat and a horse beside it. The pairing suggests the horse-drawn barge traffic of Dutch waterways. The date is not recorded. Maris, a leading painter of the Hague School, also worked on this small scale, where the subject is a place and a mood rather than a composed picture for exhibition.","i":"Canals carried much of the Netherlands' traffic until the railways, and horse-drawn boats were an everyday sight that Hague School painters recorded as part of the national landscape. For Maris and his circle, such ordinary scenes, painted in a subdued, silvery key, were a way of finding poetry in the plain surroundings of the country. A sketch of this size was probably made for the artist's own use, or as a small picture for a private buyer.","c":"At this scale the question is economy: a few touches must suggest water, a boat and an animal, and the Hague School's gift for atmosphere shows best when it is least forced. The appeal of such a sketch is its directness and freshness; its limit is that, small and undated, it says little about the course of Maris's development. It is better read as a private note than as a statement."},"met:11761":{"d":"A board about 62 by 50 centimeters is hung with papers and small objects, painted so exactly that the viewer might reach out and pull them from their places; among the items is the image of a man. It is a trompe l'oeil still life of the kind known as a rack picture, which was fashionable in the 1880s, and Peto paints it in a soft, dusty light that makes the flat surface and its clutter seem to hold real depth.","i":"An office board was a working object, a place to tuck letters, bills, cards and cuttings where they were to hand. Painted, it becomes a portrait of its owner by way of his possessions, a device popular in American still life of the 1880s. The image of a man among the papers may be a photograph or print of a friend, hero or relative; without a clearer identification, its meaning is open, and the picture works mainly as an exercise in making flat things look real.","c":"The strength of such pictures lies in studied disorder: edges, overlaps and small shadows that convince the eye. Peto handles them with a lighter, less brittle touch than Harnett, and the flatness suits his interest in surface over depth. What is lost is narrative: as a record of a stranger's possessions it asks to be admired more than read, and some viewers will find it a virtuoso exercise rather than an experience."},"nga:126162":{"d":"This is a trompe l'oeil still life, in oil on canvas about 51 by 77 centimeters and wider than it is tall. Such a picture is designed to be mistaken for real objects, flat things laid or hung on a surface so exactly that the viewer might reach out to touch them. Peto's trompe l'oeil works of the 1880s typically show the humble belongings of everyday life, worn and a little shabby, in soft, dusty tones. The title addresses the viewer directly, as though inviting a pick.","i":"Trompe l'oeil painting has a long history, from the ancient story of Zeuxis and Parrhasius and the painted grapes that fooled the birds, through the Dutch seventeenth century, to American painters such as Raphaelle Peale and William Harnett, with whom Peto is always compared. Its pleasure is a moment of deception followed by admiration for the skill. A title like this one plays on the game: the viewer is asked to choose, yet nothing in the picture can be picked up.","c":"Peto and Harnett shared a taste for worn objects, but Peto's touch is softer and more atmospheric, and his pictures prefer wistfulness to Harnett's crisp polish. The best draw the viewer in through the accumulation of tiny details; the risk is that, as a game, the picture can seem a feat rather than a feeling. Peto's work was long confused with Harnett's, whose signature was sometimes added to it, and his reputation has grown steadily since."},"met:11762":{"d":"A violin is the central subject of this small, upright canvas, about 41 by 31 centimeters, painted between about 1887 and 1890. Peto painted the instrument many times, and his violin pictures usually hang it against a door or board, together with a bow and a scrap of music, as if it could be lifted down from its nail. The title refers to the old violins of Cremona, the Italian town of the greatest makers.","i":"Cremona was the home of Stradivari, Guarneri and Amati, so an \"old Cremona\" was the dream of every violinist and collector. Whether the instrument in the picture is truly Italian hardly matters: the title gives it a pedigree and a history. A violin is also a traditional image of music's fleeting nature, and a worn one hung on a door like discarded goods adds the thought that beauty and skill can outlast their uses.","c":"A violin gives a painter curved, glossy forms that catch the light against a flat, worn ground, and Peto exploited the contrast repeatedly. This small version concentrates attention on the instrument, though the scale gives less room than the larger door pictures for the accumulation of detail on which he thrives. Its nostalgia is handled with restraint, and it shows why the subject became a signature for him."},"nga:164947":{"d":"On a white tablecloth stand a large slice of yellow cake and a halved lemon at the center, a white plate of red berries at the left and a glass of pale yellow liquid with a lemon slice and a straw at the right. The wall behind is teal blue on the right and fades to black toward the left edge. The canvas is small, about 26 by 35 centimeters, and the few objects are set in a simple row.","i":"Fruit, cake and a cool drink make a small celebration of everyday pleasure, a theme with deep roots in still-life painting. The cut lemon, whose peel and juicy flesh tested painters' skill in seventeenth-century Dutch pictures, echoes that tradition, and the cake and glass, a straw already in place, suggest a treat about to be enjoyed. In the Victorian United States such tabletop pictures were popular decorations for the dining room.","c":"This is a modest picture, and its virtue is restraint: a few clearly separated forms, a cool dark wall that sets off the yellows and whites, and no attempt at grandeur. Peto's gift is for the look of things, and the lemon, cake and glass each draw a different kind of attention. Beside his melancholy door pictures it is lighter and sunnier, though it lacks their depth of feeling, and it will charm more than it moves."},"nga:54191":{"d":"A violin, a bow and a tattered page of sheet music hang from a dark forest-green wooden door in a tall vertical picture. The violin hangs by a string from a nail, its neck angled slightly to the right, with the bow almost upright behind it; white powder coats its body under the strings, one of which is broken. The music is headed \"VIOLIN,\" its notation legible and its edges chipped. Pins, holes and cracks mar the door, a scrolling hinge is broken, a key hangs by a brass-plated keyhole, and the initials JFP seem to be carved above the violin.","i":"Peto and William Harnett took ordinary, worn objects hung on a door or board and painted them so exactly that a viewer might reach out to touch them. The violin was a favorite motif: it suggests music, but this one is dusty and broken-stringed, so it becomes an image of time passing and pleasures gone quiet. The carved initials act as a signature that seems to belong to the door itself, a small game played on the viewer's eye.","c":"The picture's success lies in the accumulation of small, exact observations: the dust on the violin's belly, the chipped edges of the paper, the cracked wood and worn paint, all gathered on a plain, strongly vertical ground that lends them quiet gravity. Peto's touch is softer and more elegiac than Harnett's, and the melancholy of the broken string is earned by the care with which it is painted, though a hint of nostalgia verges on sentiment."},"nga:164948":{"d":"A mug, a book, biscuits and a match, the four things named in the title, are painted on a wood panel only about 10 by 12 centimeters, about the size of a hand. Peto painted other small still lifes in the 1890s, and this belongs with them: a handful of commonplace objects treated with the seriousness that larger pictures reserve for grander subjects. The title records only the objects, which is all the picture claims to offer.","i":"The objects suggest the remains of a private moment: a book put down, something to drink, a snack and a match that may have lit a pipe or lamp. Humble tabletop subjects of this kind descend from Dutch breakfast pieces, where food and vessels showed both the pleasure of living and its brevity, and a match, a small, quickly spent thing, is an apt emblem of brevity. Peto's later still lifes are often read as quiet, elegiac records of ordinary life.","c":"On a panel this small the picture succeeds through restraint: a few things, no props to explain them, and a scale that draws the viewer close. Texture and tone must do all the work, and Peto is at home in that. It has an intimacy that the larger door pictures lack, but its slightness means that it can seem a fragment, a note rather than a statement."},"nga:105627":{"d":"A red jockey's cap hangs by a loop from a nail on a worn forest-green door that fills the tall canvas. Behind it angles a long riding crop, above it hangs an upside-down horseshoe, and at the lower right a spur and leather straps hang from another nail. Scraps of paper are tacked behind them, among them a torn poster headed \"OAKLEY,\" \"Race Track,\" \"Six Great Races,\" and numbered tickets. A keyhole in a gold-colored plate and a broken hinge lie to the side, and the wood is cracked and studded with rusty nails.","i":"Horse racing was one of the great popular pastimes of late nineteenth-century America, and the picture reads like the corner of a jockey's or a gambler's locker: the cap and crop, a horseshoe for luck, a spur, and posters and tickets from a day at the races. The tattered state of everything, with torn posters and a broken hinge, gives the display a gentle melancholy, as though the excitement has passed and these are what remain.","c":"A single crimson accent, the cap, against a field of worn green gives the picture an immediate focus, and the layers of torn paper are handled with conviction. Peto's touch is softer and more atmospheric than Harnett's, and the melancholy comes from the wear rather than from any story. It is one of the larger and more fully realized of his door pictures, in which the accumulation of detail is balanced by a firm design."},"nga:110275":{"d":"Two round, crusty rolls and a cream-colored cup on a saucer stand on a light blue counter against a dark red wall, on a tiny panel about 15 by 22 centimeters. The rolls, at the lower left, are dark brown on top and golden or white on their sides; the cup, a little right of center, carries a floral design in blue and yellow, and its saucer has a gold rim. A light brown object, perhaps a utensil handle, lies in front at the right. Black shadows fall on the wall.","i":"A roll and a cup stand for the simplest meal, and the picture belongs to the long tradition of the breakfast piece, which since the Dutch seventeenth century has found dignity in humble food and everyday crockery. The decorated cup and the gold-rimmed saucer imply a household with pleasant tableware, but nothing in the picture says whose. The tone is domestic rather than symbolic, and a small picture like this suited a private room.","c":"The dark red wall and the light blue counter set off the warm browns of the rolls, and the colors are chosen with care for so small a surface. Crust and glazed china are the kind of textures Peto enjoyed, and he gives each a different kind of attention. It is a sturdy, unpretentious picture, and its pleasure lies in how little it asks. The scale and the casual arrangement keep it a note rather than a statement."},"nga:110276":{"d":"A dark brown teapot and a fish sit on a white cloth against a textured beige wall, on a panel only about 15 by 22 centimeters. The teapot stands at the left with its spout turned toward the viewer; the fish, mostly greenish gray with some white and pale red scales, is propped up on something, perhaps a plate or another fish, its head facing right. The cloth has a few small folds. It is a small, quiet arrangement of two household things.","i":"The title names a meal rather than a symbol: tea and fish, a pairing that belonged to the English breakfast table of the Victorian era. Peto painted many small tabletop pictures in his later years, and like the Dutch breakfast pieces of the seventeenth century they find dignity in plain food and ordinary crockery. The fish and the pot are unremarkable on purpose; what the picture offers is the pleasure of looking closely at familiar things.","c":"The restricted palette of brown, gray, white and beige is a deliberate test: the fish's greenish scales and the teapot's dark glaze must carry the picture, and the folds of the white cloth supply what drama there is. Peto's affection for the unglamorous is clear, but the scale leaves little room for ambition, and the arrangement, with the fish propped at an angle, can feel casual rather than composed. It charms by its modesty."},"nga:110277":{"d":"A dented copper kettle rests on a forest-green tablecloth, filling the left two-thirds of a tiny horizontal panel about 13 by 20 centimeters. The finial of its loose lid and its tall spout, angled to the right, nearly touch the top edge, and the far side of the kettle falls into shadow. A thin yellow matchstick with a dark blue head lies on the cloth below the spout. Behind it, a tornado-shaped gouge is dug out of the elephant-gray wall.","i":"A battered kitchen kettle is an object that has been used for years, and the word \"old\" in the title lends the picture a note of nostalgia: its dents are a record of service. Copper vessels were a favorite test of skill for still-life painters from the Dutch seventeenth century onward, since their gleam and reflections are hard to render. The matchstick and the gouged wall are small human traces, giving a lived-in air without telling a story.","c":"Copper is a rewarding subject, and Peto, working on a panel scarcely bigger than a book, gives the kettle volume and weight with simple means: one big, dented form, a shadowed side, a lighter side. The gouge in the wall is a typical touch of accident and wear. The composition has no ambition beyond the kettle itself, but the off-center placement and the stray match keep it from being static, and its modesty is persuasive."},"nga:110278":{"d":"A yellow inkpot, a white pen or quill and an opened blue envelope lie on top of a large green book, on a panel about 11 by 17 centimeters. A drop of black ink runs down the side of the pot. The envelope, torn open along one side, carries a pink stamp in one corner, and the book's spine is striped in black and red within gold borders. The book rests on a black surface against a tan wall that falls into shadow at the left.","i":"Pens, ink and letters were favorite subjects for nineteenth-century still-life painters, since they suggest a human presence that has just left. A torn envelope with its stamp implies that a letter has arrived and been read, or is about to be answered, and the drop of ink on the pot is a tiny sign of recent use. Letters carry the ideas of news, absence and friendship, so the picture can be read as a small, private reminder of correspondence.","c":"The picture's hallmark is the small, exact incident: the drop of ink on the pot, the ripped paper edge, the stamp. Peto sets these against a restrained ground, and the blue of the envelope is the one strong note of color. The result is intimate rather than imposing, with the charm of a glimpse onto a desk, though its narrative is thin and its scale leaves little room for more."},"nga:110279":{"d":"Three oranges stand in a row before a black wall, a peeled one in the center with a whole one on either side. Behind them, partly hidden by the peeled orange, stands a glass goblet filled with a dark orange liquid, with small orange rinds lying at the bottom of the glass. The objects rest on a light brown surface, and the panel is small, about 16 by 23 centimeters.","i":"Oranges, a peeled fruit and a glass of wine are a classic combination in still life. Since the seventeenth century, Dutch and Flemish painters have used a peeled fruit to show off texture and to suggest the pleasures of the senses, and a glass of wine adds an idea of conviviality. Together, fruit and wine are conventional emblems of enjoyment that may not last, though here the message is light, and the picture can simply be enjoyed for its color.","c":"A black wall behind and a warm, light surface in front make for a dramatic setting in a small space, and the saturated orange of the fruit stands out strongly. The picture is a straightforward homage to older still-life habits rather than an innovation, and its charm lies in the economy of three oranges and a glass. Beside the elegiac door pictures it is cheerful and direct, if not very searching."},"cma:147520":{"d":"A card rack, a kind of bulletin board used to gather clippings, scraps of paper and other small items, is painted so convincingly that its contents seem to exist in real space. A jack of hearts is among the cards, and a cigarette butt perched at the bottom looks ready to be plucked from the frame. The papers are tattered and frayed at the edges. The canvas is about 76 by 64 centimeters, an upright format that suits a hanging board.","i":"Card racks were everyday objects in American homes and offices of the 1890s, and painting one turns it into a portrait of memory: the things collected on it show what a person valued enough to keep. The Cleveland museum notes that it is unclear whether the items carry symbolic meaning. Peto lived in a New Jersey resort town and sold some of his paintings to tourists, so pictures like this were made for ordinary buyers rather than grand patrons.","c":"Peto's racks are quieter than Harnett's: the objects are not crisply new but frayed, creased and dim, and they speak of wear and memory. The cigarette butt at the bottom is a witty touch, since it asks to be plucked. The composition balances disorder and design, and the jack of hearts gives a bright accent. The pleasure of the picture is the deception; its feeling is mild and nostalgic."},"nga:57102":{"d":"A forested landscape is seen from an elevated angle, with a high horizon, a blue sky and fluffy white clouds. A large, gnarled tree trunk stands in the center, with taller trees behind it in dark green and rusty red leaves. At the lower left a group of travelers gathers on the dark brown ground, in shadow, in red, brown, black and white clothes; three wear wide-brimmed hats and one seems to be a woman with her hair in a bun. More people are visible at the right, and a light brown path curves up a hill into the woods.","i":"Wooded landscapes with travelers were popular in the Dutch Republic, where buyers valued views of nature that were not specific places but invited the imagination. The gnarled tree and winding path draw the viewer into the picture, while the small, shadowed travelers are staffage, figures that give scale and life. The picture belongs to a seventeenth-century taste for the wooded landscape that artists such as Jacob van Ruisdael and Meindert Hobbema took to its height.","c":"The high horizon and central trunk give an orderly, almost stage-like design, and the rusty-red leaves add a warm note against the dark green and the pale clouds. The travelers are little more than shadowy accents, so the drama rests with the trees. Pynacker later became known for luminous, warm-toned landscapes, and this earlier picture, with its darker greens and shadowed figures, is more restrained, though not without charm."},"met:435769":{"d":"The biblical story of the Judgment of Solomon is shown on a wide wooden panel about 79 by 103 centimeters, with the king, the women and the infant at the center of the action. Bramer, a Delft painter, was known for dramatic scenes in dark interiors with flickering light and nervous, quick brushwork, and this picture follows that manner. It dates from the 1640s, some years after his return from a long stay in Italy.","i":"The story (1 Kings 3) tells of two women, each claiming the same living child. Solomon orders the child to be cut in two, and the true mother's cry to spare it reveals her. The subject was a standard image of wisdom and just rule, often painted for town halls and courts in the Dutch Republic. A panel of this size could also hang in a private house, where the story's emotion would be the draw. The moment shown is probably the king's command or the mother's plea.","c":"Bramer's strengths are invention, theatrical light and a quick, sparkling touch, and the story gives him an ideal contrast of emotion: the true mother's anguish against the king's calm judgment. Beside Rembrandt's deeper psychology, his figures are more typed and his color more brittle, and a crowded design can feel mannered. Even so, few Dutch painters of his generation handled a crowded scene with such energy."},"met:438952":{"d":"A moonlit night scene seen from the ramparts of the Citadel in Copenhagen, on a small upright canvas about 29 by 24 centimeters. A soldier stands by a sentry box, another man is present, and sailing ships ride on the waters of the Øresund, the strait beside the city, under a night sky with clouds. The date is 1839, when Rørbye was among the leading painters of the Danish Golden Age, trained in the exact observation taught by Eckersberg.","i":"Moonlit views were a Romantic specialty of the early nineteenth century, prized for stillness and solitude, but Rørbye gives his night a specific place: the Kastellet, the star-shaped fortress guarding Copenhagen's harbor, and the busy waterway of the Sound beyond. The sentry is a classic figure of vigilance, and his lonely watch adds to the hush. The picture is usually read as a mood of quiet and distance, set firmly in a real, known setting.","c":"Night views test a painter's control of tone, and Rørbye's training under Eckersberg stands him in good stead: the scene is exact in its place and quiet in its mood. The small size and the human detail of the sentry give it a modest, intimate scale. It is less dramatic than German or Norwegian Romantic moonscapes and more concerned with a particular site; as a refined, small-scale example of Golden Age painting it is persuasive."},"met:437084":{"d":"Two rams do battle in a broad landscape, watched by men and women, with a horse and rider among the figures. At about 1.7 by 2.5 meters this is a large, wall-sized canvas, a size that suggests a decorative scheme for a villa or palace room rather than a cabinet picture. The subject is a rustic spectacle, the kind of lively episode of country life that Jan Miel, a Fleming working in Rome, specialized in.","i":"Jan Miel was associated in Rome with the Bamboccianti, artists who painted the everyday life of the city and countryside: peasants, travelers, carnival revelers and rustic games. A fight between rams was a popular country entertainment, and for patrons such a scene offered energetic, good-humored detail seen with affection from above. On this scale the low-life subject has been given the status of decoration, which makes it unusual within the Bamboccianti's usually smaller output.","c":"Miel's talent was for lively groups and sparkling local color, and he was at his best on a small scale. At this size the landscape becomes a stage for the fight, and the manner, so well suited to a cabinet, has to stretch to fill the wall. The enlargement is an interesting experiment, but the picture succeeds mainly through its humor and the amusement of its onlookers."},"met:437324":{"d":"A young woman, unnamed, is portrayed on a small wooden panel about 39 by 32 centimeters. Pieter Pourbus worked in Bruges, where he painted the city's merchants, bankers and nobles with a calm, precise touch, and his portraits of women usually give careful attention to costume, headwear and jewelry, with the sitter set before a plain ground. No date is recorded here.","i":"Portraits of young women in the sixteenth-century Netherlands were often made to mark a betrothal or marriage, or to record a family member, and the sitter's costume and jewels communicated rank and wealth. No identification survives here, so the sitter can only be placed in general terms as a respectable young woman of the urban elite. Pourbus's Bruges clientele ranged from local burghers to foreign merchants and bankers, who wanted careful, sober likenesses.","c":"Pourbus's portraits are reliable rather than exciting: clear drawing, even light and honest rendering of dress, in the idiom of Netherlandish portraiture that avoids flattery. He lacks the penetrating psychology of a contemporary such as Antonis Mor, but his best likenesses have a calm presence. With neither a name nor a date for the sitter, this one is best admired as a poised likeness in the idiom of its day."},"nga:117150":{"d":"Warm light from the left illuminates four men standing in pairs beside a dirt road that winds between rocky, tree-studded hills and a misty river, in a large, almost square canvas about 139 by 173 centimetres. Gnarled trees with high green and gold canopies divide the scene. Two men with tall staffs rest among boulders at the right beside a bearded goat, a man on a donkey at the left turns to the man behind him, and a cattle driver approaches along the road. The river flows toward a tall mountain in a butter-yellow haze under a pale sky with pink clouds.","i":"Jan Both was one of the Dutch Italianates, painters from Utrecht who went to Rome, loved the golden light of the Roman countryside and brought it home to the Netherlands, where collectors prized it. His warm, hazy light and framing trees owe much to Claude Lorrain. The scene shows no particular place, but an ideal south of herdsmen and travellers at ease. Both died young, in 1652, and this is one of his late, ambitious works.","c":"Both is the most atmospheric of the Dutch Italianates, and this large canvas shows his gifts: the gold light that unifies the scene, the framing trees that lead the eye into misty distance, and the balance of rock, water and sky. The figures are small and stock, and the composition follows Claude's formula without his poetic depth. But as a serene evening view it is persuasive, and its scale is impressive."},"nga:50852":{"d":"A tranquil lake scene has a low horizon, with a pale green hill in the background under a pale, cloudy sky. In the foreground at the right stands a large, textured rock surrounded by greenery and red flowers. In the distance a wooden dock extends into the lake from the left, and a person in red appears to sit or stand there with their back to the viewer. The canvas is small, about 25 by 40 centimeters.","i":"Sabbath Day Point is a promontory on Lake George, in northeastern New York, a destination for American travelers in the 1850s and a place whose name is traditionally linked to a Sunday encampment of British troops in the French and Indian War. The picture shows a landing place rather than a battlefield, and the mood is peaceful. Kensett was a leading Hudson River School painter, and Lake George was one of his recurring subjects.","c":"Kensett is best known for the hushed, light-filled calm of luminism, and this small canvas shows him on the way there, still attentive to the textures of rock, leaves and flowers while keeping the horizon low and the sky pale. The result is gentle rather than powerful, and the tiny figure in red serves as an accent more than as a story. The quiet is the point, and it is well sustained."},"cma:143477":{"d":"A wide view looks across the Saco River to Mount Chocorua, one of the most distinctive peaks of New Hampshire's White Mountains, on a canvas about 80 by 124 centimeters. The museum describes it as a distant rendering, so the mountain is a far landmark rather than a close-up, and the title sets the scene in October. It is a broad, orderly landscape in the Hudson River School manner, made for a public eager to see the newly accessible mountain country.","i":"After railroad service to the region improved in the early 1850s, the White Mountains became a favored destination for tourists and artists who wanted to commune with nature. Chocorua, with its sharp, unmistakable summit, was among the most painted peaks. Pictures like this fed a market among city dwellers for scenery they had seen or hoped to see, and they were read as proof that unspoiled nature offered spiritual refreshment close to home.","c":"Kensett, a New Yorker, was among the calmer spirits of the Hudson River School, a temperament that matches his reputation for charity and for helping fellow artists. This view shows his preference for breadth and balance over drama: a long distance, a wide format, no stormy effects. The result is serene and a little polite, and a bolder painter would have pressed the peak harder, but the control of space and tone is secure."},"cma:160938":{"d":"This small canvas, about 36 by 51 centimeters, shows Niagara Falls and dates from about 1851 to 1852. No further description is recorded here, so only the type of picture can be stated: a scenic view of the best-known natural wonder of North America, made on a scale suited to a private home. Kensett's landscapes of this period generally favor a calm, balanced composition and a clear, even light over theatrical effect.","i":"Niagara was both a tourist attraction and a national symbol, the New World's answer to the great sights of Europe, and by the early 1850s railroads had made it easy to reach. Painters approached it in very different ways, from theatrical and crowded views to the vast, immersive panorama that Frederic Church exhibited in 1857. A small canvas like this may have been a study made on the spot or a modest picture for a private buyer; which it was is not recorded.","c":"Niagara tests a painter of Kensett's temperament, since the subject is made for drama and he was known for serenity. If the picture follows his usual approach, it will have chosen composure over thunder, and it cannot rival Church's panorama for impact. It belongs to the early maturity of an artist who found his greatest strength in quiet, and its modest scale is part of the honesty of the attempt."},"met:11310":{"d":"A river and mountains are the subjects of this wide landscape, about 81 by 122 centimeters. The title names the Hudson but no particular site, so the picture should be read as a view of the river valley in general. It was painted in 1857, when Kensett was one of the leading members of the Hudson River School, the group of American landscape painters named for this very river.","i":"From the 1820s, when Thomas Cole made the Hudson Valley and the nearby Catskills famous, the river was the heartland of American landscape painting, a place where wilderness, picturesque scenery and growing commerce met. For buyers in 1857, mostly New York merchants and collectors, such a view stood for the beauty and promise of their country. Because the title is general, it was probably meant as an ideal image of the river rather than as a record of one view.","c":"Kensett's Hudson pictures lack the storms and chasms of Cole's. Instead of theatrical effects he offers composure, and, as in much of his work, this canvas is likely to be balanced and carefully finished. It is also a little mild, a river-and-mountain formula that many painters of the school produced. The best of Kensett lies in the later coastal pictures, where the same composure becomes something rarer."},"nga:178026":{"d":"A wide river flows through green hills at the right of this broad landscape, under a vast pale blue sky full of white clouds. On a hill at the left a path runs along the top, where a small figure seen from behind walks after a group of brown and white cattle. Tall, dark green trees separate the path from the slope down to the river. The brushwork is smooth, and the palette is mostly greens, browns, blues and yellows.","i":"Mount Morris lies in the Genesee Valley of western New York, a rich farming region, and the picture presents it as a pastoral scene of settled land: the herd and its drover are small in the wide view but give it a human use. Mid-nineteenth-century Americans liked to see farmland and wilderness in harmony, as proof that settlement could be gentle. The broad river and sky suggest abundance rather than conquest.","c":"The smooth handling and long, clear distances are typical of Kensett, who avoided the theatrical weather of Church and Cole. The design is balanced, with trees at the left, the river at the right and the herd leading the eye into the picture. It may seem tidy, almost too well-mannered, and the sky is more a backdrop than a drama, yet the harmony of green, brown and blue is secure, and the calm is convincing."},"nga:42389":{"d":"A dark aquamarine sea stretches before a bulky, stony outcropping at the right of a wide landscape, with the horizon a third of the way up and a pale blue sky that lightens almost to white. Gentle waves break over rocks and boulders along the beach. The tan and caramel face of the outcropping rises vertically from the water, with dark green plants in its crevices, and at its foot a tiny fisherman in a red shirt casts from a rock. White sails dot the distance, and a green spit of land extends at the left.","i":"Beacon Rock stands in Newport harbor, in the Rhode Island resort that became a fashionable summer retreat in the 1850s. The tiny fisherman beneath the cliff is a traditional device of Romantic landscape, setting the scale of nature against a single human life, though the mood here is calm rather than awed. Kensett's coastal pictures of the later 1850s anticipate the quiet, light-filled seascapes of his last years.","c":"The vertical face of the rock against the horizontal sea gives the composition a firm structure, and cool aquamarine water against tan stone is a fresh color scheme. The fisherman's red shirt is the one warm accent and fixes the scale. The rock is described in more detail than the soft sea and sky, which makes the picture slightly lopsided, but it shows Kensett's talent for making an uneventful view feel monumental."},"cma:124771":{"d":"A small, wide canvas, about 32 by 56 centimeters, shows a view near Newport, Rhode Island, painted in the 1860s. No further description is recorded here, but Kensett's Newport pictures generally concentrate on the meeting of rocks, shore and a pale, expansive sky, treated in an economical, quiet manner. It is the kind of modest coastal picture that he made in numbers during his later career.","i":"By the 1860s Newport was the premier summer resort of America's wealthy, and its coast, with its rocks, beaches and open horizons, was a favorite subject for artists. Painters were drawn to its light as much as to its scenery. A view of this size would have appealed to buyers who wanted a quiet souvenir of the shore in their own homes, and it would hang easily among other small pictures.","c":"On this small scale Kensett gains in directness what he loses in grandeur: a low shore, a stretch of water and a wide sky are enough to set a mood. He was by now moving toward the manner of his late work, now often called luminism, with its calm and its reduction of the scene to essentials. As only the title is known of its subject, it is hard to judge more, but the format suits his gifts."},"met:11311":{"d":"A wide, quiet lake lies under a milky sky, its surface almost a mirror in tones of gold, grey and olive. Mountains step back from the shore on both sides; the tallest, a pointed peak at the centre right, rises behind lower ridges, and a small wooded island and a tiny islet float near the left. A band of mossy rocks and reeds runs across the lower right, and a tuft of grass edges the lower left. The light is soft and diffuse, as if the sun were behind thin cloud at the upper right, and the air is hazy. The paint is thin and smooth, with almost no visible brushwork. The artist's initials and the date are at the lower right. Oil on canvas, about 112 by 169 centimetres.","i":"Kensett painted the view from Homer Point on the west shore of Lake George, looking northeast toward the Narrows, with Black Mountain as the dominant peak and Clay, Recluse and Little Recluse islands in the water. A 2013 study in the Met's journal corrected an older guess that the viewpoint was Crown Island, and showed that Kensett kept the topography largely faithful while shortening the lake and raising the mountains by 30 to 50 percent. It also noted a figure in a canoe, in place of the pleasure boats he usually painted, suggesting the depth of historical time as well as of space. The picture dates from three years before his death in 1872.","c":"This is a picture of almost nothing happening, and that is its claim. The haze and the glassy water hold a mood of reverie, and the balance of dark island, pale water and rising peak is calm and sure. A harsher view would say it is almost too tasteful: the mountains are built up by formula, the foreground is thin, and nothing disturbs the surface, so it asks to be admired rather than felt. Beside Frederic Church's grand theatres of nature, Kensett offers quiet; beside Heade, little menace."},"met:11323":{"d":"A lake, a boat and trees make up this wide landscape, about 61 by 92 centimeters, dated 1870. The title places it on Conesus Lake, one of the smaller Finger Lakes of western New York, on a summer day. It comes from the last years of Kensett's career, when he reduced his compositions to calm, broad areas of water, land and sky.","i":"Conesus Lake lies in the Finger Lakes region of western New York, close to the Genesee valley that Kensett had painted in 1857. By 1870 such lakes were summer resorts and refuges from the city, and a day on the water, with a boat at rest, expressed the leisure that prosperous Americans now sought in nature. The scene belongs among Kensett's late works, in which calm and light are the real subject.","c":"In his last decade Kensett pared his compositions to broad horizontal bands under a hushed, even light, and a lake is the ideal subject for that approach. The boat and trees give just enough incident, and the picture is a model of calm. Its risk is blandness, since nothing happens, but the unforced harmony of tone is exactly what he was after."},"met:11307":{"d":"A long, narrow canvas, twice as wide as it is tall at about 46 by 91 centimeters, shows a stretch of beach at Eaton's Neck, the peninsula on the north shore of Long Island. It is dated 1872, the year of Kensett's death, when he painted a group of small, spare coastal pictures. The wide format turns the view into a band of sand, sea and sky.","i":"Long Island Sound was the water along which Kensett spent his summers, and it became the subject of many of his last pictures. A panorama like this, close to a frieze of land, water and sky, was his chosen way of making calm itself the subject. Kensett was also one of the founding trustees of the Metropolitan Museum, and these late works, painted in his final year, are among the freest he made.","c":"With a format this wide and a subject this plain, almost everything depends on the proportion of sand, sea and sky, and Kensett's judgment of those intervals is exact. The effect is of lucid stillness, a meditation more than a view, and it anticipates later painters' interest in near-abstract bands of color. Some will find it too bare; others will think it the clearest statement of what he was after."},"met:11308":{"d":"The title makes weather the subject: a foggy sky over a landscape, on a wide canvas about 78 by 116 centimeters, dated 1872. Kensett's late pictures generally pare the landscape down to essentials and let light and air do the work, and the title suggests this is a picture of that kind. No particular site or incident is recorded, which is part of its point.","i":"Fog interested nineteenth-century landscape painters because it veils detail, softens color and makes the viewer aware of the air itself, a concern that J. M. W. Turner took far and that the American luminists explored in their own, calmer way. A picture of a foggy sky is both a record of weather and a statement of what the painter thought important. Dated in Kensett's final year, it shows him still testing how little a landscape needs.","c":"At its best Kensett's late manner comes close to abstraction: pale, smoky tones, no incident, almost no edges. A fog picture presses that reduction further and is a delicate test of tonal control. If it succeeds it does so by subtlety, and if it fails it can seem blank; the cumulative effect is of a painter after essentials, and it points toward the atmospheric painters of the next generation."},"met:11309":{"d":"An approaching storm over Long Island Sound is the subject of this upright canvas, about 88 by 69 centimeters, dated 1872. A tree is among the elements shown. The upright format is unusual for Kensett, who generally preferred wide horizontals for coastal subjects, and the title promises a rare note of weather and tension in a body of work known for calm.","i":"Storm and calm were old opposites in marine painting, and a gathering storm picks the moment before rather than during: tension and anticipation rather than catastrophe. For a painter famous for serenity, a storm is a change of mood, and viewers may have read it as a reminder of nature's other side, which a summer resident of the Sound knew well. It is a late picture, painted in the year of the artist's death.","c":"A painter known for calm shows here that he could handle a moody sky. The tall format gives the weather more weight than his usual wide bands allow. Storms suited Church or Bierstadt better than Kensett's temperament, and if his version follows habit it will convey the threat through tone rather than theatrical lightning. That restraint is the picture's interest and its limit: persuasive in mood, but not the force of nature it announces."},"met:11312":{"d":"A small canvas, about 26 by 34 centimeters, shows Lake George with a tree and hills, dated 1872. Lake George, in the foothills of the Adirondacks in northeastern New York, was one of Kensett's most persistent subjects, and he returned to it over some twenty years. At this size the picture is intimate, closer to a private note on a favorite place than to a showpiece for exhibition.","i":"Lake George became a tourist destination in the mid-nineteenth century, with steamboats, grand hotels and a reputation for scenery that drew painters and writers. Kensett painted it from the early 1850s on, and the lake gave him what he most liked: a quiet sheet of water, soft hills and a changing but gentle light. The picture is dated in the year of his death, so it belongs to the last body of work he made.","c":"Small pictures like this show Kensett's gift most clearly, since there is no room for rhetoric: a tree, a lake and some hills are enough to make a mood. Its slightness is a limit, and the subject is one he had painted many times, but a view he knew so well could be done with an unforced ease, and the restraint is the charm."},"met:11313":{"d":"A tree, a lake and hills make up this small, wide canvas, about 28 by 45 centimeters, painted in 1872. The title, \"A Reminiscence,\" signals that the picture shows Lake George recalled rather than recorded on the spot, a memory of a place Kensett had painted since the early 1850s. No figures or incidents are listed, so the lake and its hills carry the whole mood.","i":"A \"reminiscence\" title was a way for nineteenth-century painters to say that a picture came from memory and feeling rather than from a record of one view, and it suited the Romantic idea that landscape should convey the emotions a place evokes. For Kensett, who had painted Lake George for two decades, the lake was a familiar friend, and the gentle mood of the picture fits the idea of a fond recollection.","c":"Memory simplifies, and Kensett's late landscapes were already distilled, so a reminiscence of the lake is less a change of method than a confirmation of it. The result has an unforced, tender calm. The danger of a picture called a recollection is sentiment, but the restraint of the design keeps it in check, and the small scale suits a mood of private retrospect."},"met:11314":{"d":"A small canvas, about 25 by 36 centimeters, shows Lake George, its water and a wide sky above. The word \"free\" in the title marks it as an informal study, loosely handled and not carried to the finish of an exhibition picture. It dates from 1872, the year of Kensett's death, and belongs to the group of late pictures that reached the Metropolitan Museum in 1874.","i":"Landscape painters of Kensett's generation made oil studies from nature as the raw material for larger studio pictures, and a \"free study\" suggests a sketch in which the brush was allowed to move quickly and detail was left aside. Lake George was a subject Kensett had returned to for some twenty years. Here the lake and the sky are reduced to essentials, which shows how his finished pictures were built from direct observation of light and water.","c":"Because it is a study, the usual standard of finish does not apply, and the pleasure lies in directness: water and sky are read as a few fluid, simplified areas. The small scale gives it intimacy. It is not a showpiece and should not be judged as one, but as evidence of how Kensett saw, and how little he needed to say, it is among the most unguarded things he left."},"met:11315":{"d":"A lake with mountains beyond fills this wide canvas, about 57 by 93 centimeters, dated 1872. Its formal title, naming both the lake and the state, fits a considered picture rather than a quick note, and it is among the larger Lake George canvases of Kensett's last year. The subject is the broad sheet of water and the hills that enclose it, a scene he had painted many times since the early 1850s.","i":"Lake George lies in the southeastern Adirondacks, ringed by mountains, and by the mid-nineteenth century it was one of the best-known scenic lakes in America, served by steamboats and grand hotels. History and literature added to its glamour: the forts and battles of the French and Indian War, and James Fenimore Cooper's novel The Last of the Mohicans, set in the region. Painters valued it for its serenity.","c":"Kensett's gift for composing a large view with minimal means is the point here: a level lake, mountains in layered distance and a spacious sky, with no strong incident to disturb the hush. Some will find it too placid, and a bolder painter would have used the mountains for drama. But the exactness of tone and proportion is the real subject, and it is carried off with the confidence of long familiarity."},"met:11316":{"d":"A large, wide canvas, about 79 by 122 centimeters, shows the rocky shore at Newport, Rhode Island, in 1872. No incident is recorded: the rocks themselves are the subject, set where the coast meets the sea and sky. It is one of the largest pictures in the group of late works that reached the Metropolitan Museum in 1874, and it looks back to the Newport coast that Kensett had painted since the 1850s.","i":"Newport's rocky coast had drawn Kensett since the 1850s, and he returned to it in his last year. Painters of the Hudson River School, schooled by geology and by the English critic John Ruskin, often treated rocks as records of deep time, and the coastal rocks gave a painter solid forms to set against the softness of sea and sky. The picture joins the permanent and the passing: stone, and the changing light that falls on it.","c":"Rocks were a test of solidity for Kensett, and Newport's weathered surfaces give him a chance to make stone hold light without drama. The large size makes this one of the more ambitious pictures of his last year, yet it remains calm, with no crashing surf to compete with the stillness. It is less theatrical than the cliff pictures of some of his contemporaries and more convincing as an image of coastal quiet."},"met:11317":{"d":"A marsh landscape with trees in autumn, about 47 by 78 centimeters, dated 1872. The title and the museum's keywords point to the colors of October, when marsh grasses and foliage change. Beyond trees and the low, level ground of the marsh, no features are recorded, so the picture seems to rest on the quiet drama of season and light rather than on any particular landmark or incident.","i":"Autumn was a favorite theme of American landscape painters from the 1850s, when artists such as Jasper Cropsey made the brilliant fall foliage of the Northeast an emblem of national pride. Kensett's October is quieter. A marsh is a modest, level place, and his interest is in the muted tones of the season rather than in blazing display. The salt marshes of Long Island Sound, near his summer retreat at Darien, Connecticut, offered such subjects.","c":"The difficulty of a marsh is that it offers nothing to look at: no peaks, no cliffs, only a flat expanse and a sky. Kensett turns that poverty into a virtue, building the picture from delicate shifts of tone and a firmly held horizon. The effect is plain, even austere, and not to every taste, but the observation is exact and the mood of the fading year is unforced."},"met:11318":{"d":"A single old pine is the subject of this upright canvas, about 87 by 69 centimeters, dated 1872 and placed by its title at Darien, Connecticut, on Long Island Sound, where Kensett spent his summers. The tall format follows the form of the tree, and the picture is in effect a portrait of a familiar local landmark, set within a landscape. The museum's keywords list only the tree and the landscape, with no figures.","i":"American landscape painters, following the English critic John Ruskin, treated individual trees as characters to be studied with care, and old trees, with their weathered shapes, stood for endurance and the passage of time. Kensett's summers at Contentment Island, near Darien, gave him a neighborhood of familiar subjects, and a veteran pine on that shore is the kind of local landmark an artist grows fond of.","c":"Kensett is usually a painter of horizontal calm, and an upright picture of a single tree is a departure. Its success must rest on the tree's silhouette against the light, and his habitual restraint probably keeps the sentiment of the old pine in check. It is less well known than his coastal views, but the departure from his formula is part of its interest."},"met:11319":{"d":"A storm is clearing over the sea, with a boat on the water, on a small, wide canvas about 29 by 62 centimeters, dated 1872. The title describes a storm moving away, so the picture catches the moment when the weather is breaking and calm returns, rather than the height of the tempest. The museum's keywords list the boat, the storm and the sea.","i":"Storm and calm were the great opposites of marine painting, and the moment after the storm, when the clouds break and the sea settles, had a long place in Romantic art as an emblem of relief and renewal. A boat on the water adds the human stake: it has come through, or is now safe. For Kensett, whose temperament favored calm, the passing of a storm suited him better than its height.","c":"Kensett was never a painter of tempests, and here the subject plays to his strength: the drama is already over, and what remains is atmosphere, the light returning through breaking cloud. At small scale the picture is quick and direct, with the boat as a single accent. It is not a major work, but it is a sensitive one, and the subject is well matched to the painter."},"met:11320":{"d":"A salt meadow, the low, tidal grassland of the Long Island Sound shore, is seen in October on a wide canvas about 46 by 76 centimeters, dated 1872. Beyond the title no features are recorded: the subject is a plain expanse of grass under autumn light, with nothing to claim the eye but the land and the sky above it. The picture is built almost entirely from the proportion of earth to air.","i":"Salt meadows were once working landscapes, the hay fields of the Connecticut and Long Island coasts, cut by farmers for salt hay. Kensett found in these unglamorous lowlands a landscape that the tourist did not seek. The October of the title, the quiet season after the summer visitors have gone, fits his late taste for subdued tones and plain subjects, in which the observation matters more than the motif.","c":"What can be made of a flat meadow? Kensett's answer is that nearly everything depends on the proportion of land and sky and on the subtlety of the grasses' tone. The picture's plainness is its subject, and it has the rare quality of being content to be quiet. It risks monotony, but the best of his late pictures are exactly such risks taken."},"met:11321":{"d":"A seascape with no named place, titled simply The Sea, about 39 by 78 centimeters, twice as wide as it is tall, and dated 1872. The title makes the open water itself the subject: the museum records no ship, headland or shore among its features, only the sea. The wide, low format suits a view of a level horizon and a broad stretch of water.","i":"In the nineteenth century the open sea was a standard image of the infinite and of nature's power. Kensett gives it in the quietest register: no storm, no wreck, simply water meeting sky. The plain title and the wide format place the viewer at the edge of a limitless expanse, a mood of contemplation that later critics associated with the American style now called luminism.","c":"This is Kensett's art reduced almost to its elements: a horizon, a band of water, a sky. The challenge is to make the empty interesting, and the proportions and the gradations of color and light must do it. Few painters of his time risked so little subject. Whether it succeeds depends on the viewer's patience, and it rewards that patience."},"met:11322":{"d":"An upright study, about 38 by 26 centimeters, of beech trees, dated 1872. Beeches, with their smooth gray bark and spreading, rounded foliage, were favorite subjects for landscape painters. As a study it is presumably a record of observation made from nature rather than a composed picture, and it differs from Kensett's usual wide horizons in its tall, narrow focus on trunks and leaves.","i":"Oil studies of trees were basic training for the Hudson River painters, who were urged by John Ruskin to paint nature faithfully, leaf by leaf and branch by branch. A study of beeches is a working document: a record of how a trunk divides and how light falls through leaves, kept for use in later compositions. Such pictures show the groundwork beneath the calm of the finished landscapes.","c":"Studies show a painter without his Sunday clothes, and Kensett's are the better for it: quick, fresh and less polished than his exhibition canvases. A tree study offers none of the horizontal serenity for which he is known, but it demonstrates the observation that underlies it. Its limits are those of any study, a fragment rather than a statement, yet it is a useful and lively fragment."},"met:11324":{"d":"A sunset over the sea, about 46 by 76 centimeters, dated 1872. The museum's keywords record the sun, the sky and the sea, so the picture is built from those three elements: the low sun and the glow around it, the sky above and the water below. It is one of several sunset pictures among the late works that reached the Metropolitan Museum in 1874.","i":"Sunsets were a staple of nineteenth-century landscape painting, valued for their color and for the religious feeling many viewers read into them, with the sky as a site of divine presence, as in Frederic Church's Twilight in the Wilderness of 1860. Kensett's sunsets over Long Island Sound are more intimate and calm. They record the ordinary end of an ordinary day, and make that enough.","c":"A sunset invites excess and Kensett resists it: the color is held within a narrow range and the horizon keeps everything steady. The risk of the genre, sentiment, is well avoided, though a viewer who craves drama will not find it. It is a modest, well-judged example of what he could do with the simplest possible subject."},"met:11325":{"d":"A larger sunset, about 71 by 105 centimeters, dated 1872, in which the sun, the evening sky and the sea are the subject. Its title, with \"on the Sea\" added to the plain word \"Sunset,\" places the viewer out over open water at the end of the day, and the museum's keywords list the sun, the evening and the sea. The larger size gives the sky room to glow.","i":"A sunset painted in the year of the artist's death is tempting to read as a farewell, but that is hindsight: Kensett could not know it was his last season, and these pictures belong to his habit of painting the evenings of the Sound. In Hudson River School culture the sunset also stood for the end of the day as a type of reflection on time, so the picture invites contemplation whatever the circumstances.","c":"On this larger scale the broad expanse of glowing sky is given room to work, and the evenness of the horizon steadies it. Kensett's late sunsets are the opposite of showy, and this one's restraint is persuasive. If it has a fault, it is that it repeats a formula, sky above and sea below, that he had by now mastered; but the mastery is evident and the effect is calm."},"met:11326":{"d":"The sky is the subject of this canvas, about 51 by 81 centimeters, dated 1872. The museum records only the sky among its features, so the picture is a study of sunset color and cloud rather than of a particular place. The plain title names what is shown, a sky at sunset, and the wide format gives the heavens the whole stage, with no land to share it.","i":"Cloud and sky studies were a central practice for nineteenth-century landscape painters. John Constable made his famous cloud studies in the 1820s, and Ruskin's Modern Painters taught that the sky was the key to a landscape's mood and truth. A picture devoted to sky alone is rarer as a finished work, and it shows how far this attention to atmosphere could go: the changing light itself becomes the subject, with nothing earthly to compete.","c":"To paint only sky is to give up everything that normally gives a landscape its structure, and Kensett's late work shows the willingness to try. Without a horizon to steady it, success depends on the control of color and light, on the gradation of tones and the shaping of clouds. It is a daring subject for a painter sometimes thought mild, and as a record of his eye it is among his most direct."},"met:11328":{"d":"A forest of cedars at twilight is the subject of this large canvas, about 72 by 104 centimeters, dated 1872 and set at Darien, Connecticut. The museum's keywords record trees and a forest. It is one of the more substantial pictures of Kensett's last year and, unlike most of his late work, it turns from the open shore to a stand of trees.","i":"The cedars are probably the native red cedars that grow along the Connecticut coast, dark, slender evergreens that make a natural curtain against the evening sky. A wooded interior at dusk recalls the forest scenes of the older Hudson River painters such as Asher Durand, who treated trees as characters, and the dim light gives the scene a note of mystery and quiet. It is a late return to a subject on which Kensett's generation had been formed.","c":"A wooded twilight takes Kensett away from the open horizons he usually paints and into the territory of his elders, and the size suggests ambition. The dim light calls for subtle control of tone, and the picture succeeds if it keeps the dark masses of the trees from turning heavy. It is a quiet, serious late work that shows he had more than one manner at his disposal."},"met:11329":{"d":"Twilight settles over Long Island Sound at Darien, Connecticut, with a boat on the water, on a small, wide canvas about 29 by 62 centimeters, dated 1872. The place was Kensett's summer retreat, and he knew its water in all lights. The museum records only the boat and the landscape, so the picture is carried by the evening light and the stillness of the Sound.","i":"Twilight, the interval between sunset and dark, was a favorite time for American landscape painters because of its hush and its soft, merging light. Kensett's summer home at Contentment Island, on the Darien shore, looked out on the Sound, and he painted its evenings repeatedly in his last year. The boat is a quiet human note rather than a story, a sign that the water is a place of daily use.","c":"Twilight is a hard subject because the values come close together and the painter must keep a scene legible while it dissolves. At this small scale Kensett's method is to simplify, and the result is delicate rather than showy, with the boat giving focus and scale. It is the kind of picture that gains from being seen in quiet, and some of its quality is easily missed in a quick glance."},"nga:56574":{"d":"A pink-toned sandy beach curves like a letter C toward a terracotta-orange rocky outcrop and boulder at the left, topped with muted green trees and blanketed in moss or grass. Pale blue water laps at the shore beneath a hazy, milky sky, and a few sailboats sit near the horizon. A white rowboat is pulled up on the sand, with one person inside and a red cloth or net hanging over the side, while a figure in a straw hat carries a basket away from the viewer.","i":"Beverly lies on the North Shore of Massachusetts, a coast of rocks and small beaches where Kensett painted in the later 1860s. The figures, with their boat, net and basket, appear to be ordinary shore folk, so the scene shows a lived-in shore rather than a resort display. Such pictures gave viewers in crowded cities a restful image of the sea; they are among the finest examples of the American style now called luminism.","c":"This is Kensett at his most distinctive: a very simple design of curving shore, level horizon and luminous, milky sky, where the whole effect depends on tone and light rather than incident. The pinks, pale blues and warm terracotta are close in value, which gives the picture its hush, and the small figures supply scale and quiet human interest. It can seem almost empty, but the quiet is deliberate and well controlled."},"met:437745":{"d":"Judith appears with a sword and the severed head of Holofernes, the Assyrian general she has just killed, on a tall canvas about 2 by 1.5 meters, painted around 1640 and not far short of life size. The museum's keywords list the sword, the head and women among its subjects. Stanzione, a leading painter in Naples, typically gave such subjects monumental, classicizing figures, and this canvas has the scale for that.","i":"The Book of Judith tells how a Jewish widow entered the camp of Holofernes, whose army was besieging her city of Bethulia, gained his trust and beheaded him with his own sword while he lay drunk, so that the Assyrians fled. In Catholic Europe after the Reformation, Judith was a favored heroine, a figure of faith, chastity and the triumph of the weak over a tyrant, and a subject beloved of Baroque painters from Caravaggio to Artemisia Gentileschi.","c":"Stanzione's Judith is typically more statuesque than ferocious. Where Caravaggio and Artemisia seize on the physical violence, Stanzione, shaped by the classicism of Guido Reni and Domenichino, prefers composure and idealized beauty. The result is handsome and controlled, with fluent drapery and strong light, but less shocking and less psychologically charged than the most famous treatments of the theme. Its appeal is polish rather than terror."},"met:435733":{"d":"A huge altarpiece panel of about 281 by 175 centimeters shows the Virgin and Child enthroned with saints and angels, painted in tempera around 1493 to 1495. Mary sits at the center as Queen of Heaven with the Christ Child on her lap, and the saints and angels stand or kneel around her in a calm gathering. The scale and the format of an altarpiece suggest that the painting was made for a church altar, probably in Florence.","i":"Such a group is called a sacra conversazione, or holy conversation, in which saints of different centuries share a single space with the Virgin as if in quiet discussion. The saints chosen would have been patrons of the church or its donors, and angels add splendor. Botticini, trained in the Florentine workshop tradition and influenced by Botticelli, painted many such devotional pictures for Florentine churches.","c":"Botticini is a talented but secondary master, whose Botticelli-like types and clear compositions are attractive but rarely surprising. The altarpiece has the dignity of its size and the sweetness of its types, but its figures are static and the arrangement formulaic. It is a good example of the Florentine altarpiece in the years of Savonarola, valuable for what it shows of the period's taste more than for novelty."},"met:435734":{"d":"Saint Sebastian is shown with an angel in this tall, narrow panel of about 137 by 58 centimeters, painted in tempera and oil. The shape suggests an altar wing or a panel from a larger polyptych. The saint, a Roman soldier martyred under the emperor Diocletian, was shot with arrows for his Christian faith and is usually shown bound and pierced by them.","i":"Sebastian survived the arrows, according to legend, was nursed by a Christian widow and later clubbed to death, and he became the patron saint invoked against plague, since arrows were an old image for pestilence. Florence suffered repeated outbreaks in the later fifteenth century, which made him one of the most popular saints of the time. The angel may bring the martyr's palm or crown.","c":"Sebastian gave painters an excuse to show a beautiful, nearly nude male body, and Florentine artists took full advantage. Botticini's version is elegant and restrained, with the youthful body set against a calm background. The sentiment is more of sweetness than agony, which may disappoint viewers looking for drama. It shows how the saint of plague became an image of ideal beauty."},"nga:153202":{"d":"Far below the viewer, a steel-gray river winds through a valley lined with hazy mountains. Ridges march down from the upper left toward the center, growing paler and mistier, their craggy faces warmed by pale sunlight from the upper right. Meadows of moss green and rust brown, scattered with trees, run down to the water, with specks of livestock and a few buildings in the middle distance. At the lower left, on a bluff, a tiny man in white sits on a stool beside an easel and open paint box, looking toward a grove.","i":"The Juniata River flows through central Pennsylvania, and Moran, a young Philadelphia painter in his mid-twenties, painted its valley with an eye trained by Turner, whose work he had studied in England. The tiny painter on the bluff is a familiar device of Romantic landscape: he sets the scale of the view against a human figure and makes the picture a quiet comment on looking and painting, since he has turned from his canvas to the scene itself.","c":"An ambitious early canvas: the winding river, the layering of ridges into mist and the warm, raking light give breadth and depth in the Hudson River School manner, with an atmosphere that owes something to Turner. The peach, taupe and olive of the mountains are lovely. The foreground is somewhat schematic, but this is a skillful, even tender picture by a young painter who would soon turn to the grand scale of the West."},"nga:82648":{"d":"Sheer cliffs, lit cream white and rust orange by the low sun, tower over a long line of mounted riders winding into the distance on a wide, horizontal canvas. The glowing cliffs fill the upper right, shading to flame orange down their steep sides and to a lavender haze in the distance; a tall promontory looms over lower, rounded buttes. More than two dozen horses and riders in feathered headdresses cross a shallow valley toward a river and a row of tiny tepees on the horizon.","i":"Moran made his reputation with the American West, joining the 1871 Hayden survey of Yellowstone, and his paintings of its colored rock helped win public support for protecting such landscapes. The riders here are Native Americans presented as part of the picturesque scenery, an idealized image: by 1881 the Native peoples of Wyoming were largely confined to reservations, and the picture belongs to the nostalgia of the time for a West that was passing.","c":"Moran's color is the picture's glory: the cliffs glow with an almost theatrical intensity against the lavender haze, and the long horizontal format gives the scene breadth. The riders are loosely painted and generic, serving as staffage that adds scale and romance, and the effect is stage-managed and more heightened than any real evening. As a document of the West it is a romantic invention; as a painting it shows an assured handling of light and distance."},"nga:50729":{"d":"Roiling, churning waves break against a patch of sandy beach and fill most of this wide, horizontal canvas. The water deepens from aquamarine to teal, slate and midnight blue amid clouds of ivory-white crests and spray, while a glassy sheen coats the beach where it drops sharply to the surf. A battered wooden cage is pounded by the waves near the lower center, and a few shells are suggested with touches of orange and gray. A strip of sky across the top lightens from sable brown at the left to tan at the right.","i":"Moran, best known for the canyons and geysers of the West, also painted the Atlantic, and in 1884 he built a house and studio at East Hampton, Long Island, where the sea became a regular subject. The title uses the language of epic poetry, in which the sea is often called resounding. The battered cage, perhaps a fishing trap, is the only trace of human work, and its pounding by the waves sets the sea's power against the smallness of human effort.","c":"The wide panorama and the churning water, painted in swirling teal and slate with white crests, show Moran's Turnerian gift for energy, and the mirror sheen on the wet beach is a fine observation. The sky is a mere strip, so the picture is all water, and the broken cage is the one small story. It is lively and fresh, if less grandly composed than his western views, and a reminder of how well he could handle motion."},"met:435858":{"d":"A mounted gentleman and a shepherd with his flock share this canvas of about 65 by 81 cm, a pastoral encounter in open country rather than a battle or a portrait. The title and the subjects (a rider, a horse, sheep, a shepherd) point to a landscape with figures, the kind of cabinet picture made to hang in a salon or private room. Casanova, a London-born painter who worked in Paris and Vienna, was best known for battle scenes and lively landscapes with riders.","i":"Pictures that set a traveler on horseback beside a herdsman descend from seventeenth-century Dutch and Italian landscape painting. The pairing contrasts worldly rank with rural simplicity and invites the viewer to imagine a short exchange on the road: directions asked, news traded. The word cavalier suggests a gentleman or an officer rather than a nameless wayfarer, but no particular story or sitter is known for this picture, so it is best read as a general pastoral scene.","c":"Casanova made his name with battle pictures full of movement, so a quiet exchange between rider and herdsman is a change of key. The subject gives little room for his strongest talent, and the interest here probably rests on the horse and the contrast of the two figures rather than on any new idea. Dated around 1802, near the end of his life, it reads as competent late work in an inherited formula, pleasant rather than inventive."},"met:437031":{"d":"The Virgin and Child sit enthroned between Saints James the Less and Lucy, on a small panel in tempera and gold, about 31 by 44 centimeters and wider than it is tall. The gold ground stands for the light of heaven. The painter's name, Marco di Paolo, links him to the tradition of Paolo Veneziano, the founder of the Venetian school, and the panel's gold and delicate decoration belong to that tradition. No date is recorded.","i":"Lucy of Syracuse, a virgin martyr of the early fourth century, is usually shown with her eyes on a dish, a reference to the legend of her torture, and her name, from the Latin for light, made her the saint of sight. Her relics were brought to Venice in 1204, so she was a major Venetian saint. A panel of this size, pairing her with James the Less, was probably made for private devotion, perhaps for a patron named for these saints.","c":"Fourteenth-century Venetian painting joined the formality of Byzantine models with a growing Gothic elegance, and a gold-ground panel like this shows both in miniature: glowing color, patterned cloth and careful, linear drawing. This is a work of craftsmanship more than of invention, and a small devotional piece does not demand more. The finish and the gold give it a jewel-like charm, and the thoughtful pairing of saints gives it a personal air."},"cma:109354":{"d":"Little is recorded about this picture beyond its title, date and size: an upright oil on canvas, about 102 by 67 centimeters, painted in 1882 by the American artist R. Way Smith. The title promises a scene of summer leisure, the sort of quiet, light-filled subject popular with American painters and buyers in the 1880s, but no description of its contents is on record here, so only the type of picture can be stated.","i":"Pictures of summer afternoons belong to a late nineteenth-century taste for leisure subjects, as a growing middle class in the United States took vacations, rowed and read outdoors, and wanted images of ease for its own walls. The French Impressionists had made the light of a summer day a subject in its own right, and American painters of the 1880s were beginning to absorb the lesson. Without more information the picture is best read as a mood piece rather than a narrative.","c":"A title like this sets a clear test: the painter must make light, warmth and idleness visible, and a picture of this kind lives or dies by its handling of sunlight and shade. The large upright canvas suggests ambition, and the date places it at a moment when American painting was moving toward a brighter palette. With so little on record about the artist, any firmer verdict would be guesswork; the canvas itself must decide."},"met:875634":{"d":"A woman identified by the title as Rachel Ruysch appears with a painter's palette, flowers and butterflies on a canvas about 76 by 64 centimeters, dated 1692. The museum lists the painter and the sitter under the same name, so the picture presents itself as a self-portrait. The attributes are those of her art: the palette marks her profession, and the flowers and butterflies recall the subjects of her celebrated still lifes.","i":"Ruysch (1664–1750), the daughter of the Amsterdam anatomist Frederik Ruysch, trained with the flower painter Willem van Aelst and became the most successful woman painter of the Dutch Golden Age, working into her eighties. A palette in a woman's hand was a bold claim of professional status at a time when few women could make a living by art. Flowers and butterflies point to her specialty, and also to the passing beauty and transience that flower pictures often evoked.","c":"If it is by Ruysch herself, it is a rare departure from the flower pieces on which her fame rests, and its interest lies chiefly in what it records: a woman claiming a place as a professional painter and choosing the attributes of her art. The pairing of a human face with the blossoms and insects she painted is a neat statement of identity. Whether the portrait matches her flower pictures is for the canvas itself to show."},"cma:166507":{"d":"A fisherman is the human figure in this small estuary scene, painted on a panel only about 15 by 24 centimeters between 1625 and 1650. Estuary views, where a river widens toward the sea, were a staple of Dutch landscape painting in those years, typically with a low horizon, a wide sky and soft, silvery color. No further description is recorded here, so the picture is best understood as a miniature example of that type.","i":"Water shaped the life of the Dutch Republic, and its painters made rivers, estuaries and coasts a subject of constant interest, in the work of Jan van Goyen, Salomon van Ruysdael and others. The fisherman, a small figure, gives scale and shows the water at work, a source of food as well as trade. Small panels like this were bought by middle-class householders to decorate their rooms, with no need of grand subject matter.","c":"At this size a painter must work economically, and the best Dutch tonal landscapes of these years, with their thin, fluid paint and hazy skies, achieve atmosphere with a few touches. If this panel follows the pattern, its value lies in the sky and the water more than in the figure. It is a modest, unpretentious picture, enjoyable for its calm, and it shows how widely the new landscape taste had spread."},"cma:146872":{"d":"A Native American camp is the subject of this canvas, about 41 by 61 centimeters, painted around 1890. No description of its contents is recorded here, but Blakelock's camps typically show tepees gathered at dusk or by moonlight beside woods or water, in dark, glowing tones built up from many layers of paint. The picture is a poetic evocation of the West rather than a documentary record of any real place.","i":"Blakelock's camps are not records of particular peoples: they are nostalgic images of a way of life that Americans of the 1890s saw as vanishing, even as the real Native nations of the West were being confined to reservations. He is said to have drawn on journeys in the West in his youth, but the mood came from the Barbizon painters and the Tonalist taste for dusk, silence and glowing dark color.","c":"At his best Blakelock achieves a glow that few American painters match: dark, jewel-like surfaces, worked and reworked, in which a few warm touches of fire or sky light the whole scene. The formula, a camp under trees at dusk, is one he repeated many times, and the risk is routine. Whether this picture reaches the intensity of his finest depends on the canvas, but the type shows why his best work is prized."},"nga:214131":{"d":"A glass of amber liquid stands at the center of a marble tabletop, surrounded by green and purple grapes, peaches, a green plum and two walnuts, one partly opened. A stem of purple grapes twists up toward the upper right, while on the left the green grapes are still attached to a thin, leafy branch. A bee is perched on one of the peaches, with a drop of juice dripping down beside it. The background is dark brown, and the board is small, about 31 by 41 centimeters.","i":"A table of fruit on a marble ledge, a glass of wine and a bee make up the vocabulary of seventeenth-century Dutch and Flemish still life, handed on to American painters through traditions such as that of the Peale family in Philadelphia, where Ramsey was born. The bee and the dripping juice show ripeness at its height and, by the old conventions of vanitas, hint that sweetness is brief. The picture can also simply be enjoyed as a display of abundance and craft.","c":"In his early twenties Ramsey already shows technical assurance: the sheen of the grapes, the translucency of the glass, the sticky drop of juice, and the bee as a witty accent of life. The composition is a little by the book, with the glass at the center and the fruit disposed around it, and the dark ground is conventional. It is promising rather than profound, but the observation is acute and the surface convincing."},"nga:168756":{"d":"Shown from the shoulders up and from a low viewpoint, a man with dark brown skin and black hair looks up and off to the viewer's left on a small vertical canvas. His shoulders are angled to the right while his head turns; his lower lip is slightly forward, and a mustache runs from the corners of his mouth to a square jaw. His hair forms a halo around his face, a crimson cloth wraps his shoulders, and the background shimmers from golden to vivid yellow. The paint is loose, especially in the cloth and background.","i":"The title calls the sitter a chief of Abyssinia, the old European name for Ethiopia, but whether he was a real dignitary or a model posed in costume is not recorded here. Regnault, who was killed in the Franco-Prussian War in January 1871 at the age of twenty-seven, had traveled in Spain and North Africa, and his work belongs to French Orientalism, which fed a taste for exotic types. Such heads are better read as studies of color and character than as documentary portraits.","c":"Regnault's flair for color is the attraction: the crimson cloth against the shimmering yellow, a low viewpoint that makes the head monumental, and a loose, confident touch that suggests Spanish painting and Manet. The limits are those of its type, since Orientalist pictures reduce the sitter to a striking motif and say little about a person. As a display of brilliance in a short career cut off by war, it is memorable, if not deeply probing."},"cma:618083":{"d":"Lions and a fox are the animals of this small panel, about 43 by 53 centimeters, set in a rugged landscape of rock and crag, in the manner of the Flemish painter Roelant Savery. Savery typically crowded his landscapes with animals, native and exotic, among craggy settings and twisted trees, and the title suggests the same formula: big cats and a cunning fox in wild country. No further description is recorded here.","i":"Savery worked for Emperor Rudolf II in Prague, where the imperial menagerie gave him exotic animals to draw, and later settled in Utrecht. His landscapes crowd animals of many kinds into a dramatic setting, a display of the variety of creation that appealed to collectors of curiosities. The pairing of lions and a fox also recalls animal fables, in which the lion stands for strength and royalty and the fox for cunning.","c":"Savery's reputation rests on animal-filled landscapes in which close observation meets Mannerist fantasy: craggy rocks, twisted trees and vividly drawn beasts. The animals tend to be arranged for pose and outline rather than for behavior, which gives his pictures their decorative, collector's-cabinet charm and also their stiffness. A small panel such as this concentrates the formula; it is more diverting than profound, and the diversion is the point."},"met:437600":{"d":"A woman is portrayed with a dog among the details on a canvas about 98 by 72 centimeters, a size suited to a half- or three-quarter-length figure. The picture belongs to the tradition of the Spanish court portrait of the later sixteenth century, which favored a dignified, immobile pose, rich costume and a restrained dark background. No date is recorded, and the sitter is unnamed.","i":"The portrait is catalogued under Rolán de Moys, a Netherlandish painter linked with Spain, though another record names Alonso Sánchez Coello, the Spanish court portraitist, a reminder of how closely the two traditions overlapped. Small dogs in court portraits were fashionable companions and emblems of fidelity. Costume, jewels and pose, rather than character, were meant to announce the sitter's rank and piety.","c":"Court portraits of this kind are about status rather than psychology, and their virtues are exact rendering of costume and a poised, composed likeness; their risk is stiffness. The conflicting attributions show how close the Netherlandish and Spanish traditions were, shaped by Antonis Mor's model of the Habsburg portrait. It is a conscientious, cautious likeness, more valuable as a record of manners than as an exploration of character."},"nga:46058":{"d":"The Crucifixion of Christ is the subject of this small Russian icon of the nineteenth century, about 47 by 36 centimeters, painted in oil on a wooden panel with a brass cross set into it. In the standard Orthodox composition Christ hangs on the cross with the Virgin and Saint John beside him, though the record here lists only Christ. The use of oil rather than the traditional egg tempera, and the added metal cross, mark a devotional object of the later Russian tradition.","i":"In Orthodox practice an icon is not simply a picture but a means of prayer, and the Crucifixion is usually shown with the calm dignity of Christ's sacrifice rather than the agony stressed in much Western art. The brass cross let into the panel adds a sacred object to the image, which a worshipper could touch or kiss as the faithful traditionally venerated the cross. Icons of this size were made for the home, hung in the main room's corner of honor.","c":"An icon of this kind is judged less on originality than on fidelity to a sacred type and the quality of its craft. The combination of an oil painting with an inset metal cross gives it an unusual, hybrid character that points to the persistence of the tradition in an age of modern materials. With the record so spare, any closer verdict is hard, but the object's value lies in that continuity of devotion."},"met:437578":{"d":"Christ is shown in glory, holding a book, on a large panel of tempera on wood, about 107 by 78 centimeters, attributed with a query to a painter of Novgorod in the late fifteenth century. The book is the Gospel, the sign of Christ as teacher and judge. In Russian icons of this type the figure is typically frontal and solemn, and the colors are clear and strong against a luminous ground.","i":"Christ in glory shows the risen and ruling Lord, the image of the Second Coming and the Last Judgment, and the Gospel book he carries is the Word by which the world is judged. Novgorod, a wealthy merchant republic until Moscow absorbed it in 1478, was one of the great centers of Russian icon painting, and its icons are prized for bold color and clear design.","c":"Novgorod icons are admired for bold color, clear drawing and a monumental simplicity, and a large panel of this kind gives those qualities room. Icon painting did not aim at individual invention but at the faithful transmission of a sacred type, so the interest lies in the authority of the design and the intensity with which the old formula is realized. The Novgorod attribution is tentative."},"met:437552":{"d":"The Protection of the Mother of God is the subject of this small icon, about 31 by 27 centimeters, in tempera on wood. In the usual composition the Virgin appears in the air above a congregation of saints and onlookers and spreads her veil over them as a sign of her protection; the museum's keywords list men, women, saints and the Virgin Mary among the figures. It is a work of the sixteenth century by an anonymous Russian painter.","i":"The feast commemorates a vision said to have been seen in the tenth century in the Church of Blachernae in Constantinople by Saint Andrew the Fool, who saw the Virgin spread her veil over the faithful. The subject became especially dear in Russia, where the feast of the Protecting Veil is kept in October, and Ivan the Terrible's great Cathedral of the Intercession on Red Square, better known as Saint Basil's, was dedicated to it.","c":"The subject challenges the painter to make a crowd legible and the Virgin's veil a clear sign of shelter, and at this small scale the task calls for exceptional neatness. Sixteenth-century icons of the type typically combine bright mineral colors with fine gold highlights, and a successful one reads at a glance. Its charm lies in a sense of tenderness and community: a congregation under a mother's cover, painted with the economy the format demands."},"met:437554":{"d":"The archangel Gabriel announces to the Virgin Mary that she will bear Christ, on a small panel, about 33 by 26 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood in the second half of the sixteenth century. In the usual Russian composition Gabriel approaches with a gesture of blessing and Mary receives the message, often before a building, with the figures drawn in tall, graceful proportions. The museum's keywords list the angel, Gabriel and Mary.","i":"The Annunciation, recorded in the Gospel of Luke, is the moment of the Incarnation, when God becomes man, and its feast on March 25 is among the twelve great feasts of the Orthodox year. Mary's quiet acceptance of the message makes her the model of humility and obedience. Small icons like this were made for home devotion, or formed part of a set of feast icons, a series for the church year.","c":"The Annunciation depends on gesture and rhythm: two figures, one message, a few lines of drawing. Muscovite icon painters of the sixteenth century, working in the long shadow of Dionisy, favored elongated, graceful figures and a light, refined palette, and a small panel like this distills that taste. It is a modest example, but the clarity and calm of the design are exactly what the subject requires."},"met:437557":{"d":"A small panel, about 18 by 16 centimeters, painted in tempera on linen and decorated on both sides, belongs to a saints' calendar, an illustrated guide to the saints commemorated through the church year. The museum's keywords list saints and men among the figures. The painting on both sides and the small size suggest an object meant to be handled, perhaps one leaf of a folding or bound set. It is dated possibly to the sixteenth century.","i":"In Orthodox practice nearly every day of the year commemorates a saint, and calendar icons gather these saints, in rows of small figures, so that a worshipper can find the saint of the day. Such icons served as both devotional images and practical reference, joining the church's liturgical year to the daily life of a household. The use of linen, a lighter support than wood, points to portability.","c":"As a work of art, a calendar panel is closer to a chart than to a picture: its figures are formulaic, and its beauty lies in the rhythm of repeated forms and clear color. The charm is real, if modest, and the small scale demands a precision that is easy to underrate. It is more a document of religious life than a showpiece, but a vivid and unusually direct one."},"met:437558":{"d":"Saint George, in armor on horseback, spears the dragon on a panel of tempera on wood about 69 by 50 centimeters, perhaps of the sixteenth century. The museum's keywords list the dragon and the horse. In Russian icons of this type the saint is a youthful, victorious horseman, usually on a white horse, and the dragon, a coiled serpent, writhes under the hooves; the princess whom he saves sometimes watches from a tower.","i":"Saint George, a soldier martyred around 303, became in legend a dragon-slayer who rescued a princess, and the dragon stands for the devil and the defeat of paganism. In Russia he was beloved as the patron of soldiers and farmers, and as the patron of Moscow, whose coat of arms shows him spearing a serpent. His icons were made for churches, for homes and as protective images.","c":"The icon type turns on a single dynamic diagonal, rider and spear, and a small number of clear shapes, so success depends on how firmly the rhythm of horse and lance is drawn. Russian versions are more decorative and less naturalistic than Western ones, with the drama carried by line and color. If this one follows the usual pattern, it is a vigorous, readable image of triumph, in which the symbolism matters more than any realism."},"met:437555":{"d":"The Mother of God, with Christ, is shown among saints and men in this small icon, about 36 by 31 centimeters, in tempera on wood, from the late sixteenth or early seventeenth century. The museum's keywords list Christ, Mary, saints and men. The title translates the name of the feast usually called the Synaxis of the Mother of God, which honors the Virgin herself on the day after Christmas.","i":"In the Orthodox calendar, December 26, the day after the Nativity, is given to the Synaxis of the Theotokos, a gathering of the faithful to honor Mary for her part in the Incarnation. The icon gathers her with Christ and a company of holy figures, making visible the idea of the Church as a community of saints around the Mother and Son. Its message is communal rather than narrative: it shows fellowship, not an event.","c":"Gathering-type compositions test a painter's ability to organize many figures without confusion, and the best of them make a calm rhythm from repeated heads, haloes and robes. In a small panel the problem is sharpened, and success depends on tightly controlled drawing. Whether this icon fully succeeds is a matter for close looking, but the format shows the sophistication of Russian workshops at the turn of the seventeenth century."},"met:437556":{"d":"The prophet Elijah, known in Russian as Elias, is carried up to heaven in a chariot drawn by horses, on a panel of tempera on wood about 69 by 61 centimeters, from the late sixteenth or early seventeenth century. The museum's keywords list horses, angels, saints and chariots. In the usual Russian version the chariot is often red, a fiery whirlwind lifts the prophet, and his follower Elisha waits below to catch the mantle that falls to him.","i":"The story is told in 2 Kings 2: Elijah is taken up by a whirlwind in a chariot of fire, and his successor Elisha takes up the mantle he lets fall. In Russia, Saint Elias also took over some of the role of the old thunder god Perun in popular belief, so that his feast, in July, was linked with storms, rain and the harvest.","c":"The scene gives the icon painter a rare chance for movement in a tradition of calm: a diagonal ascent, plunging horses, flaring fire. The red-and-gold palette suits the subject, and the panel's size gives the design room. As in most icons the drama is controlled by formal clarity, so the effect is of solemn wonder rather than spectacle, which is how the tradition wanted it."},"met:437560":{"d":"Saints Boris and Gleb, shown with a horse, appear in this small icon, about 32 by 27 centimeters, in tempera on wood, from the seventeenth century. A cover of silver and silver-gilt protects the panel; such covers usually leave only the faces and hands of the painted figures visible and turn the icon into a precious object. In one common type the two princely brothers ride side by side or stand together, holding the crosses of martyrdom.","i":"Boris and Gleb, sons of Prince Vladimir of Kyiv, were murdered in 1015 by their brother Svyatopolk during the struggle for succession. They accepted death without resistance and were honored as the first native saints of the Russian Church, passion-bearers who model brotherly love and meekness. Princes and soldiers took them as protectors, and a metal cover, an expensive gesture, shows the value its owner placed on the icon.","c":"The silver cover changes how the icon is seen: the painting is concentrated in the faces and hands, and the precious metal does the work of ornament. That makes the object more a treasure than a picture, though the painted faces must still carry the devotion. The format reflects a seventeenth-century Russian taste for richly adorned icons, and its restrained, glowing effect is typical of that taste."},"met:437564":{"d":"The Resurrection of Christ is shown in the Orthodox way, as the Harrowing of Hell, on a small panel about 31 by 25 centimeters, in tempera on wood, possibly of the seventeenth century. In the usual composition Christ, in radiant robes, stands over the broken gates of hell and pulls Adam, and with him Eve, from their tombs, while kings and prophets look on. The museum's keywords list Christ, hell, saints and men among the figures.","i":"Eastern Christian art does not usually show Christ stepping from the tomb as in the West. It shows the Descent into Hades, in which Christ breaks the gates of death, binds Satan and frees the righteous dead from Adam onward. This is the central image of Easter in the Orthodox Church, and the Easter hymn proclaims Christ trampling down death by death. The panel is a picture of victory over death rather than of a single event.","c":"The Resurrection icon offers a crowded, vertical drama, and its success depends on clear grouping: the commanding Christ, the dark pit, the figures lifted from their tombs. Seventeenth-century Russian painters were skilled at this clarity, and a small panel concentrates it. The intensity of the image, an act of rescue, is the point, and the contrast between the radiant Savior and the dark below is the core of its design."},"met:437566":{"d":"Christ's Entry into Jerusalem is shown on a small icon, about 31 by 26 centimeters, in tempera on wood, probably of the seventeenth century. The museum's keywords list Christ and men. In the usual composition Christ rides a donkey toward the city gate, blessing, while the crowd spreads garments and branches before him; the city is shown as a cluster of towers and walls, and a group of disciples follows behind.","i":"The scene, told in all four Gospels, marks the beginning of Holy Week and the feast of Palm Sunday, one of the twelve great feasts of the Orthodox year. Russia had no palms, so willow branches took their place, and the day is known as Willow Sunday. The people's welcome to the humble king on a donkey, fulfilling the prophecy of Zechariah, is also a foreshadowing of the Passion to come.","c":"The challenge of an Entry is to give a crowd scene a clear direction, and the usual solution, a strong lateral movement carried by the donkey, serves well. In so small a panel the painter must reduce buildings and branches to essentials, and the best examples have a pleasing, almost musical rhythm. The icon is more notable for the clarity of tradition than for personality, which is what such pictures are designed to be."},"met:437559":{"d":"A tiny icon, only about 11 by 10 centimeters, shows the Christ Child with Saints Boris and Gleb, painted in tempera and gold on wood and set in a mount of gold and enamel studded with jewels. The museum dates it broadly between the sixteenth and eighteenth centuries. The small painted panel and its precious setting together make a luxurious object, probably meant to be held or worn rather than hung on a wall.","i":"Boris and Gleb, the murdered princes who became Russia's first native saints, were patrons of rulers and soldiers, and a small icon with the Christ Child at their side presents them under the protection of Christ. Jeweled gold mounts of this kind were the work of skilled goldsmiths, and such objects were prized as personal treasures and could serve as gifts between nobles or offerings to churches.","c":"In a miniature the painted part is dwarfed by its setting, and the real interest lies in the contrast between the humble egg-tempera panel and the glitter of gold, enamel and gems. The painting must hold its own at a tiny scale, and the sacred message survives the luxury. It is better thought of as the jeweler's art than as painting, and is best admired for its preciousness."},"met:437567":{"d":"A calendar of saints and festivals is laid out on a small panel, about 31 by 26 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood against a gold ground in the seventeenth or eighteenth century. The museum's keywords list the calendar system and saints. In such icons, tiny figures and scenes are arranged in rows, each commemorating a saint or festival of the church year in order, so that the panel works like a picture-book calendar.","i":"In the Orthodox Church every day has its saint or feast, and the great feasts, from Christmas and Easter to the Annunciation, structure the year. Calendar icons gather this cycle into a single image, so that a household could read the church year at a glance and use it as a guide for fasting, feasting and prayer. The gold ground sets the whole round of days in heavenly light.","c":"Calendar icons are among the most demanding of small-scale works, since dozens of figures must be legible in a tiny space, and the best of them turn the compression into a pattern, a woven tapestry of small scenes. The chief pleasure is the variety of tiny, bright vignettes within a unified design. It is less a picture than a document, but a beautiful one."},"met:437568":{"d":"Three female saints appear in this small icon, about 31 by 26 centimeters, painted in tempera on wood in the eighteenth century and covered with a silver-gilt and enamel mount that usually leaves only faces and hands visible. The record does not name them, but family icons often grouped the patron saints of a household's women. The metal cover, with its enamel decoration, makes the icon a rich, jewel-like object.","i":"Russian families commonly commissioned icons of the saints whose names they bore, the patron saints of each member of the household, and a group of three women may be such a family set, honoring a mother and daughters, or a woman's named patrons. Saints such as Catherine, Barbara and Paraskeva were especially popular with women. A metal cover was an expensive enhancement, a sign of devotion and means.","c":"The enamel and silver-gilt cover dominates the effect, and the painted portions, faces and hands, must carry the personal and devotional meaning. A group of three standing figures lends itself to a calm, rhythmic arrangement, but the tradition rarely allows individuality. As an object it shines; as painting its virtue is restraint, and its appeal is the richness of the whole rather than any single face."},"met:437569":{"d":"The Resurrection of Christ is shown as the Harrowing of Hell in this small icon, about 31 by 27 centimeters, in tempera on wood, dated to the eighteenth or nineteenth century. Christ breaks open the gates of hell and raises the righteous dead; the museum's keywords add an angel to the figures, which in the usual composition may be one of those who bind Satan in the pit. It is a late example of an ancient image.","i":"Icons of this size were produced in great numbers by village workshops in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, for homes and chapels across Russia, and the Easter image was among the most loved. Some late icons keep the old composition but show Western influence in softer modeling, so they record the persistence of tradition in changed conditions. The angel suggests an elaborated version of the composition.","c":"A late icon of an old subject is judged by how it handles the inherited design: whether it keeps the clarity of the older versions or grows fussy. The subject gives it authority, and the sturdy composition carries it, but a later date often implies more mechanical repetition of formulas than earlier icons show, and the difference between a lively and a merely competent version can be fine. Its value is chiefly as a witness to a living tradition."},"cma:95195":{"d":"An elderly mariner is portrayed on a canvas about 73 by 59 centimeters, painted in the nineteenth century by the American artist Samuel Isham. The quotation marks around \"Old Sea Captain\" in the title suggest a character study of a type rather than a formal commission for a named sitter. No further description is recorded here, so the picture is best understood as a portrait study of an old seafarer.","i":"The old sea captain was a stock figure of late nineteenth-century American art and literature, a symbol of rugged independence and of the fading age of sail, and such character heads were popular with artists and buyers alike. Isham, trained in Paris, was also an art historian, the author of The History of American Painting (1905), which gives his own practice an informed context. Without a name, the sitter stands as a type.","c":"A character study of this kind lives on the face, and a French academic training typically gave a painter solid, tonal modeling of the head, the part that must carry the picture. Pictures of old seamen risk sentimentality; the quotation marks in the title suggest some awareness of that. With so little on record any firmer judgment would be guesswork, but the subject is one of the more appealing in American art of the period."},"cma:144966":{"d":"The Virgin and the Christ Child are shown with the young Saint John the Baptist in a devotional painting in tempera and oil on wood, dated about 1490. No dimensions or further description are recorded here, so only the type can be stated: the infant Jesus and his cousin John meet as children under Mary's care, a subject that Botticelli and his workshop painted many times for Florentine homes. The sinuous line and sweet, melancholy types are typical of the painter in the 1490s.","i":"John the Baptist was the patron saint of Florence, so images of him as a child beside the Christ Child had a special appeal there. The young John usually looks toward Jesus, sometimes holding a cross of reeds or a scroll, as a sign of his future role in proclaiming him as the Lamb of God. A picture of this kind served family devotion, offering a sacred model for the children of the household.","c":"Botticelli's late Madonnas keep the flowing line and wistful expression of his earlier work but grow more severe, in the years when Savonarola's preaching was changing Florence. Many surviving pictures of the type were shared with his large workshop, and scholars differ on the degree of his own hand. Judged as a type, it shows the elegance of contour and the melancholy sweetness that made the painter famous, with the reservation that quality can vary."},"met:10505":{"d":"A triptych, a three-part hinged object about 80 by 55 centimeters, combines oil painting on canvas with an ebony-stained pine frame, gold leaf and velvet. The museum's keywords list a column, which suggests the temple architecture of Egypt named in the title. The date is given as 1874 or later, and the maker is recorded as the American painter Sarah Freeman Clarke, though other records have attributed it to Samuel Colman.","i":"After the opening of the Suez Canal in 1869 and the premiere of Verdi's Aida in 1871, Egypt became a fashionable theme in Western decoration, and Egyptian motifs entered American interiors of the 1870s. The triptych form, borrowed from Christian altarpieces, is adapted here to a secular, decorative object, a kind of parlor shrine to travel. Clarke, a Boston painter who studied with Washington Allston, belonged to the circle of American artists who worked abroad.","c":"An unusual hybrid of painting and furniture, it makes the best sense as a decorative ensemble: ebonized wood, gold leaf and velvet frame the painted subject in a rich, theatrical way, typical of the taste of the 1870s. Its painted portion is only one part of the effect, and as a document of how the Egyptian Revival entered American homes it is more telling than as a picture. Authorship is unsettled, which adds to its interest."},"nga:494":{"d":"A long, low panel tells the story of Apollo and Marsyas in three episodes within one rocky landscape. At the center Apollo, in a pale yellow toga and a laurel wreath, plays a violin-like instrument and looks out at the viewer. At the right Marsyas sits on a rock playing bagpipes, while behind him a woman in pink, her foot on a shield, plays a bagpipe and gazes at her reflection in a pool. At the left Marsyas, nude and tied to a tree, is threatened by Apollo, who holds a knife to his arm.","i":"Classical writers tell how the goddess Minerva, or Athena, threw away a pipe when she saw in the water how playing it disfigured her face, how the satyr Marsyas found it and challenged Apollo to a contest, and how Apollo won and flayed him for his presumption. The woman at the pool is Minerva. The tale was usually read as a lesson about pride and the triumph of refined harmony over wild passion. A long panel like this was probably made for a chest or wall paneling.","c":"The storytelling is the picture's pleasure: three moments unfold across one landscape with clear gestures and bright color, in the tradition of Sienese narrative painting. The figures are elegant and a little stiff, and the cruelty of the flaying is handled with decorum, in keeping with a decorative panel. The unnamed painter is competent rather than inspired, but the continuous narrative is lively and easy to follow."},"cma:150840":{"d":"A simple glass flask holds a large rose, a red anemone and a white narcissus tinged with pink, with a scattering of smaller blossoms around it, on a small panel about 26 by 23 centimeters. A window is reflected in the glass, though not drawn in precise detail, and a few chips in the stone ledge make the material seem tangible. The composition is sparing: a few flowers arranged with great balance and sophistication.","i":"Flower pieces were prized in the Dutch Republic and beyond for their beauty and their meaning: the rose traditionally speaks of love and also of thorns, the anemone of fragility, the narcissus of vanity and spring, and the whole bouquet of the brevity of life. Verelst moved early in his career from Holland to London, where he worked for the royal court and was extravagantly praised for the realism of his flower paintings.","c":"With just a few flowers Verelst achieves poise: the large rose, the red anemone and the pink-tinged narcissus are balanced against the smaller blooms, and the reflected window and chipped stone add convincing touches of reality. Beside the lavish bouquets of Rachel Ruysch or Jan van Huysum it is restrained, but its restraint is its charm. The English admiration for his realism is easy to understand."},"nga:42458":{"d":"A man is shown seated and facing forward in a half-length portrait, with his torso and hands visible and his left arm resting on a surface. He has angular features, light-colored eyes and brown hair styled to the side, and wears formal attire. In his left hand he holds a book. Behind him a textured gradient serves as a background, with a pillar at the right and abstract greenery at the left.","i":"John Stone is presented as a respectable, literate man: formal dress, a book in hand, and a pillar and greenery borrowed, in simplified form, from the grand portraits of earlier generations. Around 1845 such portraits were commissioned by a rising American middle class. The portrait of Eliza Welch Stone, of the same date and size, suggests that this was one of a pair, a husband's portrait hung beside his wife's.","c":"The directness of the picture is its appeal: angular features, light eyes and a frank frontal pose give the sitter more individuality than the stock props around him. The anatomy and space are simplified, as in much popular American portraiture of the 1840s, and the pillar and greenery are decorative more than convincing. As a likeness it is plain and forthright, and as period evidence it is valuable."},"nga:42459":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated and facing the viewer directly, her right arm resting on a surface as she holds a white vase of pink and orange flowers. She has large, light-colored eyes, a straight nose, a closed mouth and tightly curled brown hair falling over her shoulders. Her dark dress has a white lace collar and a brooch, and a ring is on her right hand; a colorful patterned fabric lies in her lap. Behind her are a draped red curtain and a wall with a detailed blue pattern.","i":"The flowers, the patterned textile, the curtain and the jewelry make an inventory of respectable domestic taste: a woman of the house presented among beautiful things. Flowers in a woman's portrait of the 1840s commonly signal gentleness and domestic virtue, and the ring and brooch mark marriage and means. The portrait is probably the companion to that of John Stone, which is of the same date and size.","c":"This is a busy picture: curtain, patterned wall, flowers and fabric compete for attention, and the figure at the center is flatter than any of them. The decorative richness is the portrait's strength, with its reds, blues, pinks and yellows bright against the dark dress, while the large eyes give the face a wide-eyed directness. It is a charming, slightly naive picture whose accessories speak louder than the sitter."},"nga:50823":{"d":"A man is shown in a portrait of the upper body, facing forward, with his hands resting on a red book. He has a prominent nose, deep-set eyes, thick eyebrows and dark hair with some gray, and he wears a black coat, a white shirt and a black bow tie. The background is a warm gradient with no other detail, so the figure, the book and the dark clothing make up almost the whole picture.","i":"A man in a plain black coat with a book under his hands is the picture of mid-century respectability: sober dress, literacy and piety in a single image. The red book, which may be a Bible, is the one strong color accent. The sitter's name is not recorded. The matching size and date suggest that it may be the pair of the Portrait of a Woman by the same hand, perhaps as husband and wife.","c":"Austerity is the point: black coat, white collar, a red book and a plain ground leave the face to do the work, and the heavy brows, deep-set eyes and graying hair give it a believable character. The painter does not flatter, and the severity has dignity. The limits are those of the type, with flat modeling and stiff hands, but it is a strong, unfussy likeness within a narrow range."},"nga:50824":{"d":"A woman is shown from the waist up, seated upright and turned slightly to the left, with sharp features, pronounced eyebrows, blue eyes and a serious expression. A white headdress with ribbon-like ties frames the face above a dark, high-collared garment. In her left hand is a red book, and her right hand rests on a white cloth. On the brown wall behind her hangs a framed picture of a green tree against a muted landscape, and the overall color scheme is warm and earthy.","i":"The white cap, dark high-collared dress and red book give the sitter a sober, devout air, and the book may be a Bible or devotional work, a common prop for women of the period. The framed landscape on the wall is a quiet sign of the household's taste and cultivation, a picture within the picture. The sitter is unnamed, and the portrait may have been made as the companion to a portrait of a man of the same size and date.","c":"The sharp features and serious gaze give this sitter a presence that the plain, warm palette does nothing to soften. The landscape in its frame is a charming touch of invention, and the white cap against the brown wall makes a strong shape. The modeling is flat and the hands are simplified, as in much popular portraiture of the time, but the directness and economy of the picture are its strength."},"nga:229378":{"d":"A young girl stands beside a large dog on a tall canvas, about 2 by 1.4 meters, and looks directly at the viewer. She has fair skin and straight brown hair to her shoulders, and wears a dark burgundy dress with a white lace collar, black stockings and shoes. The light gray dog at the left reaches almost to her shoulder, its mouth slightly open to show a pink tongue and white teeth. The walls behind are plain gray, with deep red and gold drapery with teal designs at the right.","i":"The title gives the names of the girl and the dog, Isabelita and Thor, which suggests a portrait of a child and her companion; the sitter's family is not recorded here. A child with a large dog is a theme of grand portraiture that goes back to Velázquez, whom Sorolla studied and admired. The dog's size gives the child a protective companion and sets off her smallness, and the full-length format dresses an everyday friendship in the dignity of a state portrait.","c":"At about thirty Sorolla shows a young painter's command of a grand format: a dark dress and gray dog against gray walls, with red-and-gold drapery for color. The child's direct gaze is the heart of the picture, and the dog, painted with evident affection, nearly steals it. The palette is sober, far from the sunlit beaches of his later fame, and the plain ground asks much of the two figures; it is a strong early portrait."},"met:438761":{"d":"The archangel Gabriel greets the Virgin Mary in her bedroom on a tiny panel, only about 16 by 10 centimeters, painted in oil with gold on linden wood. The museum's keywords list the bedroom, Gabriel, Mary and the Annunciation. The domestic setting follows the Northern tradition of placing the Annunciation in an ordinary room of the day, and the use of oil, still recent in German lands, shows the influence of Netherlandish painting. It dates from 1440 to 1450.","i":"The Annunciation, from the Gospel of Luke, is the moment when Gabriel tells Mary she will bear the Son of God. Northern painters set it in a bedroom or chamber of their own day, so that the miracle takes place in familiar surroundings, and the bed, a prominent object in such rooms, may quietly allude to the conception. A panel this small was made for private devotion, perhaps as one wing of a small altarpiece or a diptych.","c":"In so small a panel the painter must reduce the scene to angel, Virgin and a few furnishings, and the appeal lies in intimacy and fine detail. South German painters of these years were absorbing Netherlandish realism without always mastering it, and a modest, probably provincial, panel like this shows that process at work. Its charm is that of a private devotional object more than of a showpiece."},"met:436476":{"d":"The Annunciation is shown on a nearly square panel, about 99 by 94 centimeters, painted in oil on wood with a gold ground, around 1460. The museum's keywords list the archangel Gabriel, the Virgin Mary and a bed, which places the scene in Mary's bedchamber in the familiar Northern manner. The combination of the oil technique with a gold ground gives the panel a glowing, precious look.","i":"The Annunciation, from the Gospel of Luke, is the moment when Gabriel tells Mary she will bear the Son of God. Northern painters set it in a bedchamber of their own day, so that the miracle takes place in familiar surroundings, and the bed in the room, a sign of privacy and of the marriage chamber, is also read as a quiet reference to the Incarnation. A panel of this size was probably part of an altarpiece.","c":"With a gold ground and a domestic interior, the panel joins two worlds: the heavenly gold of medieval tradition and the closely observed room of the new Netherlandish realism. The best Annunciations make the encounter almost silent, and quiet is what the subject needs. Whether this painter achieves it depends on the figures' expressions, but the format, the scale and the bed give a convincing sense of a real chamber."},"met:436477":{"d":"The Nativity is shown on a nearly square panel, about 94 by 92 centimeters, painted in oil on wood with a gold ground, around 1460. The museum's keywords list the Virgin Mary, Joseph, the Christ Child and other men and women, presumably shepherds or attendants. Gold backgrounds were increasingly rare in the Netherlands by 1460, and the combination of the new oil technique with a gold ground gives the panel a precious, devotional look.","i":"Many fifteenth-century Nativities follow the vision of Saint Bridget of Sweden, who saw Mary kneel in adoration before the newborn Child lying on the ground, radiant with light, while Joseph looked on. The scene is both a record of Christ's birth and a model of devotion: the Virgin and Joseph worship the Child, and the viewer is invited to do the same. The panel may have formed part of a series with an Annunciation of similar size and date in the same collection.","c":"The great painters of the day, Rogier van der Weyden above all, set the standard for emotional clarity in such scenes, and a follower working in their shadow tends to repeat their types without their intensity. The large size suggests an important commission, and the gold ground gives the panel a glowing richness. If it is typical of the Netherlandish workshops, it is best valued for its type and its richness more than for individuality."},"met:437743":{"d":"The Trinity receives the adoration of the saints on a tall, narrow panel, about 1.7 meters high and 56 centimeters wide, painted in tempera and gold on wood around 1400. The museum's keywords list God the Father, Christ, the Virgin Mary, the archangel Gabriel and saints among the figures. The tall format, the gold and the heavenly subject suggest that it was part of a large altarpiece, in which narrow vertical panels were stacked in tiers.","i":"The adoration of the Trinity by all the saints is the image of the Church triumphant in heaven, a gathering of angels, martyrs, confessors and holy women before God, drawing on the Book of Revelation. In the International Gothic art of Spain around 1400, such a heavenly court was rendered with gold, patterned robes and rich color, so that the viewer could glimpse paradise. The panel probably comes from a retable, a large altarpiece.","c":"The tall format forces the painter to stack figures in tiers, and the challenge is to keep a crowded heaven clear and rhythmic. Spanish International Gothic painters loved ornament, with gold tooling, brocades and bright, flat color, and space and anatomy count for less than pattern and devotion. Alone, if it was once part of a larger altarpiece, it loses some of its logic, but as a vision of heaven it is lavish and orderly."},"met:437742":{"d":"The archangel Michael, in armor and with wings, fights the dragon on a large, almost square panel, about 105 by 104 centimeters, painted in tempera on a gold ground around 1405. The museum's keywords list the sword, the armor, the wings, the shield and the dragon, and give as the subject the War in Heaven. The picture is catalogued, with a query, as the work of a Valencian painter active in Italy.","i":"The Book of Revelation describes a war in heaven in which Michael and his angels fight the dragon, who is Satan, and cast him down. Michael became the leader of the heavenly host, the patron of soldiers and protector of the Church, and the weigher of souls at the Last Judgment. A painter trained in Valencia and working in Italy would have known both traditions of the subject, and the panel reflects the exchange of styles between Mediterranean courts.","c":"International Gothic painters made Michael as elegant as he is fierce: a shining knight, a graceful sweep of wings and a dragon more decorative than terrifying. The near-square format and the gold ground make a handsome, emblematic design. Pattern matters more than physical force, but ornament is part of the appeal. The mixture of Spanish and Italian training makes the picture a good example of how styles traveled around 1400."},"met:437736":{"d":"The twelve-year-old Christ is shown in the Temple among the teachers, with the learned men reading, and with Mary and Joseph who have come to find him, on a large panel about 112 by 76 centimeters in tempera and gold on wood. The museum's keywords list Christ, Mary, Joseph, men and women and the act of reading. It is a work of the early fifteenth century by an anonymous Catalan painter, and its size and gold suggest a panel from an altarpiece.","i":"Luke's Gospel tells how Mary and Joseph, returning from Jerusalem, found the boy Jesus after three days, sitting in the Temple listening to the teachers and asking questions, and astonishing them with his understanding. The scene shows Christ's divine wisdom and is also an image of a mother's anxiety, since Mary asks why he has treated them so; in Catholic devotion the loss of the child in the Temple is counted among the Seven Sorrows of Mary.","c":"Catalan altarpiece painting of the early fifteenth century is marked by lively narrative, bright color and rich patterned surfaces, and a scene of animated discussion, with its many heads and books, suits it well. The painter can interest the viewer in the rhythm of gestures and glances, if not in subtle emotion. The gold ground flattens the setting into pattern, and the result is closer to a jeweled tapestry than to a window onto the world."},"met:437026":{"d":"The Virgin is shown alone on a small panel, about 48 by 40 centimeters, in tempera and oil on wood with a gold ground, made in the second quarter of the fifteenth century. The museum records only Mary among its subjects. A single figure of this kind is often a fragment of a larger altarpiece or a half-length devotional image, and the record does not say which. The anonymous painter is Catalan.","i":"In Catalan altarpieces of the period the Virgin appears as Mother of God, as Queen of Heaven and as the grieving mother at the Crucifixion, and a single figure may have belonged to any of these scenes. Devotion to Mary was intense in late medieval Spain, and an image of her alone invites personal prayer. The gold ground is the traditional sign of heaven, which makes her figure present as well as remote.","c":"Without its original context the panel has to stand on the figure alone, and the quality of the face and the drapery must do the work. Catalan painters of the 1420s to 1440s joined International Gothic grace with growing attention to Netherlandish realism, and the mixture of tempera and oil suggests such a transition. It is an appealing fragment, if one can only guess at what it lost."},"met:437724":{"d":"The Virgin sits enthroned with the Christ Child, flanked by two saints, in this small gilded panel, about 52 by 35 centimeters, painted in tempera, oil and gold on wood. The museum names the saints as Catherine of Alexandria and Jerome: Catherine, the royal martyr usually shown with her wheel, and Jerome, the scholar of the Latin Bible, often shown in a cardinal's red with a lion. The mixture of tempera and oil marks a transitional moment in Spanish painting, when Netherlandish oil technique was reaching the peninsula.","i":"A Virgin enthroned between saints presents a heavenly court, with the Child as its king; the saints are intercessors chosen by the patron or the church. Catherine, one of the most popular saints of the late Middle Ages, was the patron of scholars and students, and Jerome, translator of the Bible, a patron of learning, so the pairing suggests a devotion to wisdom and study. The small size points to a private altarpiece or the central panel of a modest retable.","c":"Spanish painters of the mid-fifteenth century took from Flanders the new oil medium and the love of rich textiles and jewels, and combined them with the gold and strong color of their own tradition; a small panel like this is an attractive example of that mixture. The poses are stiffer than a Flemish original and the space shallow, but the glitter of gold and the brightness of color give it a charm that cooler Northern pictures lack."},"met:437738":{"d":"A nearly square panel, about 86 by 86 centimeters, in tempera and gold on wood, shows the beheading of Saint John the Baptist. The museum's keywords list the saint, a soldier and a woman, the executioner and Salome, the figures the Gospel story requires. The gold used in the paint and the ground marks an important commission for a church, and the large field gave the painter room for a narrative with several actors.","i":"John the Baptist, the prophet who baptized Christ, was imprisoned by Herod Antipas for condemning his marriage to Herodias and was beheaded at the demand of her daughter, whom tradition names Salome. The scene belongs to a cycle on the Baptist's life and death that probably filled a Catalan altarpiece; companion panels in the same museum show Salome dancing and presenting the head. For a medieval viewer John was the last of the prophets and a model martyr.","c":"Catalan painters of the mid-fifteenth century liked a clear, dramatic narrative, bold color and gilded detail, and this large, nearly square field has room for them. The violence is handled with the restraint of the period: the actions are clear and the formality of the figures keeps horror at a distance. The anonymous painter is capable rather than inspired, and the pleasure lies in the vivid storytelling and the glow of the gold."},"met:437739":{"d":"A nearly square panel, about 87 by 86 centimeters, in tempera and gold on wood, shows Salome dancing before Herod; the museum's keywords list Salome, a man and a woman, and add dancing, a dog and dining, so the scene is Herod's birthday banquet. It is the first episode in the tale that ends with the Baptist's beheading, and companion panels in the same museum show the sequel. The gilded surfaces and the large scale mark a commission for a church.","i":"The Gospels do not describe the dance, and medieval painters imagined it: a graceful, perhaps even acrobatic performance that so pleased Herod that he swore to give the dancer anything she asked. The danger of that oath is the story's turning point. Pictures of the subject warned viewers about lust, rash promises and the manipulation of a girl by her mother, Herodias, who wanted John silenced. The dog adds the everyday setting of the feast.","c":"Banquet scenes let painters show off costume, tableware and architecture, and Catalan masters of the time took the chance with relish. The gestures are usually stiffer than the story needs, and a dancer is shown as a formal pose rather than a movement, but the decorative richness is attractive. It shows Catalan painting at its most courtly: narrative, bright and a little formal, with a taste for color and detail that outweighs its drama."},"met:437740":{"d":"A nearly square canvas, about 88 by 88 centimeters, shows Salome presenting the head of Saint John the Baptist on a tray at a banquet; the museum's keywords add a man, a dog and a dining room. Its paint layer, originally tempera and gold on wood, has been transferred to canvas, a drastic conservation treatment of the past that saved a failing panel. It is one of three Catalan panels of the mid-fifteenth century that evidently belong to a cycle on the Baptist's life.","i":"In the Gospels Herod's birthday feast ends with the tray and its grim trophy, and the story was told to show the cost of tyranny and lust and to honor the Baptist's courage in speaking truth to power. The dog, a common detail of banquet scenes, adds a touch of everyday life to the horror. Medieval viewers knew the story from sermons and the liturgy, and the picture would have been read as both history and warning.","c":"The picture is at its best in the contrast of the gruesome with the courtly: a dining room, a tray, polite figures and a severed head, set down with the matter-of-fact clarity of the Catalan tradition. The transfer to canvas has probably cost some of the original surface, which limits what can be said about its finish. But the narrative is clear and the gold gives it dignity, and it survives as a vivid fragment of a lost altarpiece."},"met:437722":{"d":"A small panel, about 72 by 56 centimeters, in oil and gold on wood, shows the Mass of Saint Gregory, with a man, a saint, an altar and Christ among the museum's keywords. In the traditional composition, Pope Gregory the Great kneels or stands at an altar as Christ appears above it, showing his wounds, often with the instruments of the Passion around him and attendants watching. The oil medium and gold place it in the Hispano-Flemish style of the late fifteenth century, painted about 1490 to 1500.","i":"The legend says that during Mass a doubter asked for a sign of the Real Presence of Christ in the Eucharist, and Christ appeared on the altar as the Man of Sorrows. The subject reached its height of popularity in the late fifteenth century, when images of the Mass of Saint Gregory were also associated with indulgences, remission of punishment in purgatory, for those who prayed before them. The picture therefore teaches a doctrine and offers a promise.","c":"The subject is a test of devotional painting: the divine appearance must be both astonishing and orderly. A Spanish version of about 1495 would typically bring Flemish detail to the altar furnishings and vestments and set the solemn event within a compact, well-ordered space. The stiffness of the figures and the formulaic arrangement limit the drama, but the intensity of the Man of Sorrows gives it a gravity that suits its message."},"met:436416":{"d":"Christ rises from the tomb in this panel, about 79 by 63 centimeters, in oil and silver on wood with a gold ground; the museum's keywords list soldiers and a shield alongside Christ. The soldiers are the guards set at the tomb, who in pictures of the Resurrection are shown sleeping or startled, with their shields and arms at hand. The silver mentioned in the medium was probably used for armor and weapons, and the gold ground gives the picture the glow of a precious object.","i":"The Resurrection is the central event of the Christian faith, and in a retable it would crown or close a cycle of the Passion. The sleeping guards, drawn from the Gospel of Matthew, set the sacred event against human weakness: the soldiers who should watch miss the miracle. Aragonese painters of the mid-fifteenth century, working under Flemish influence, often added vivid details of armor and landscape to such subjects.","c":"Aragonese painting of this date takes Netherlandish realism and applies it to a gilded ground, an odd and appealing mixture: the soldiers and their armor are likely to be treated with Flemish attention, while the sacred figure remains more formal. The result is vivid, decorative and slightly naive, and the silver details give a metallic sparkle that few Northern pictures attempt. It is a lively, anonymous work with a strong sense of surface."},"met:437733":{"d":"A tall, narrow panel, about 125 by 62 centimeters, in tempera and gold on wood, combines the Virgin and Child, a Pietà and a group of saints. The museum's keywords list angels, Christ, Mary Magdalene, John the Baptist, John the Evangelist, Saint Peter and a dragon, which suggests several tiers of figures stacked one above another. The shape is typical of a vertical strip of a Castilian altarpiece, where devotional and narrative scenes were arranged in columns.","i":"The Pietà, the Virgin mourning the dead Christ, and the Virgin with the Child present the beginning and the end of Christ's earthly life, a pairing meant to move the worshipper to compassion. The saints, Peter, the two Johns and Mary Magdalene, are the figures most closely connected with Christ's ministry and Passion, and the dragon probably marks a dragon-slaying saint, such as Saint George, Saint Margaret or Saint Michael.","c":"Castilian altarpieces of the late fifteenth century stack their figures with a frank, decorative piety, and this panel offers a dense, glittering compression of images. The drawing is likely to be angular, the faces rather alike and the spaces tight, but the gold and bold colors have the force of a devotional diagram. As a fragment of a vanished whole it lacks context, yet one can still feel how the original must have glowed in a dim church."},"met:437741":{"d":"A long horizontal panel, about 89 by 236 centimeters, in tempera on wood with a gold ground, shows six apostles side by side. The museum names Peter, Paul, Andrew, Bartholomew and John the Evangelist, with a sixth apostle among them, and such figures are normally told apart by attributes: Peter's keys, Paul's sword and book, Andrew's X-shaped cross, Bartholomew's knife. The proportions suggest a row from the lower register of an altarpiece, with a companion panel carrying the other six.","i":"A row of apostles at the base of an altarpiece presented the foundation of the Church, the witnesses who carried Christ's word, directly above the altar where the Mass was celebrated. The attribution to an Oña painter links the panel to northern Castile, around the Benedictine monastery of San Salvador de Oña, and an altarpiece for such a house would show the apostles as the forebears of the monastic life.","c":"The apostles' row has the grave rhythm of a procession, and the gold ground gives each figure the isolating dignity of an icon. The drawing is likely to be firm and the faces individualized by beards and hair, but the poses are repeated and the draperies somewhat generic, as a workshop's output on a big commission tends to be. It is impressive for its width and gravity, a quiet, monumental fragment of a lost altarpiece."},"met:437725":{"d":"A noblewoman, named in the title as Doña Marianna Stampa Parravicina, Countess of Segrate, is the subject of this tall full-length portrait, about 205 by 117 centimeters, painted in oil on canvas by an unknown Spanish painter of the early seventeenth century. The title gives her birth year, 1612, so the portrait presumably dates from her youth. A full-length of this size is a state portrait, meant to show rank through dress, stance and setting at nearly the scale of life.","i":"Segrate is a town near Milan, and the Stampa family belonged to the Milanese nobility, which lived under Spanish rule: the Duchy of Milan was a possession of the Spanish crown from the sixteenth century to 1706. A portrait of a Lombard countess by a Spanish painter, in the stiff, rich fashion of the Spanish court, shows how far Spanish taste and dress had spread through the Habsburg dominions. The mixed title, Doña and Condesa di Segrate, captures that world.","c":"A good Spanish court portrait is formal, upright and costly in its details, and the interest of this one lies in what it tells of rank and of fashion rather than of the person. The anonymous painter is competent, but a full-length demands a sustained command of design that this type of picture, with its rigid stance and set expression, seldom shows. The attraction is the display: dress, jewels and presence made into a statement of lineage."},"met:437734":{"d":"The head of a man, shown alone, fills this small oil on canvas, about 36 by 27 centimeters, by an anonymous Castilian painter of the mid-seventeenth century or later. The museum's keywords record a man and a portrait. A head-only canvas of this size is a study or a fragment, and Spanish painters of the seventeenth century made such heads from life as preparation for larger pictures or as exercises in character.","i":"Heads of old men, beggars, philosophers and saints were a favored type in seventeenth-century Spain, where painters following Ribera and Velázquez prized close observation of weathered faces. A head of this kind could be a sitter's portrait, a model's study for an apostle or a prophet, or a fragment cut from a larger picture. Without a name or a context it is best read as evidence of the painter's eye.","c":"A head study depends wholly on observation, and the best Spanish examples have a candor that makes them the equal of finished portraits. Without being able to name the painter, one can still value the economy of such a picture: a face, a ground and a few strokes of light. The anonymity limits its claim on art history, but the human presence in a plain head can speak more directly than a polished portrait."},"met:437728":{"d":"A man is the subject of this oil portrait on canvas, about 64 by 52 centimeters, by an anonymous Andalusian painter of the late seventeenth century. The museum's keywords record only a man and a portrait, so the picture is best described by type: a bust or half-length likeness of a sitter in the formal manner of Spanish Baroque portraiture, in which dark dress and a quiet pose were preferred to display. The size suits a private room or a family collection, and the sitter's name is lost.","i":"In the late seventeenth century, under Charles II, Andalusian painters in Seville and Cordoba worked in the shadow of Murillo, who had died in 1682, and portraiture was a steady part of their living, for churchmen, officials and merchants. A sober likeness of this kind recorded a man's face for his family or for a corporation in which he served. The loss of his name leaves only the evidence of the face and of the painter's competence.","c":"Spanish portraits of this date tend to be sober and direct, with strong light on the face against a dark ground, and the best of them have a candor that polished court portraits rarely match. Without a name or a recorded commission, this one is best valued for the plain human presence of the face; the painter is competent but not inventive. It is a quiet example of the realist habit that Spanish painting never lost."},"met:236111":{"d":"The Virgin and Child appear with an angel in this large oil painting, about 127 by 108 centimeters, from the eighteenth century; the canvas has been relined, that is, backed with a second canvas to strengthen it. The title names the Valencian devotion of Nuestra Señora de los Desamparados, Our Lady of the Forsaken, whose image shows the Virgin inclining toward the Child, often with angels overhead and sometimes with suffering figures at her feet. The museum's keywords record only an angel and the Madonna and Child.","i":"Our Lady of the Forsaken is the patron of Valencia. The devotion began in the early fifteenth century, when a preacher, Father Jofré, moved Valencians to found a refuge for the mentally ill and the abandoned, and the Virgin became their protector and later the city's beloved patron. Paintings of the image multiplied in the eighteenth century, in churches and homes, and they were meant to make the devotion present to the worshipper.","c":"A devotional picture of this kind should be judged less as invention than as service: it must make a revered image clear, rich and moving to its viewers. Eighteenth-century Spanish painters did so with warm color, sweetness of expression and the floating grace of angels, and an anonymous canvas like this one shows a competent workshop doing its job. It lacks the mystical intensity of the golden age, but it keeps the lively fervor of popular devotion."},"nga:224921":{"d":"The Virgin holds the Christ Child in this vertical painting, about 98 by 74 centimeters, in oil on canvas, dated between 1635 and 1640. The museum's only keyword is Madonna and Child, so the picture is best described by the manner of its painter: Stanzione, one of the leading painters in Naples in those years, who combined Caravaggio's strong light with the graceful forms of the classicism of Guido Reni. Such a canvas was made for a private chapel or a collector's room, and its subject invites quiet devotion.","i":"Stanzione, trained in the Caravaggesque circle of Naples, made his reputation with sweet, graceful religious pictures that served the city's many churches and monasteries. His Madonnas present the Virgin as an approachable mother: tender, beautiful and dignified, in keeping with the Counter-Reformation's wish for images that moved the heart as well as instructed the mind.","c":"Stanzione is at his best in the soft modeling of flesh and the elegance of drapery, and a Madonna of the mid-1630s shows him balancing the dramatic light of Naples with a refined, classical sweetness. The danger is prettiness: his figures can be so graceful that they lose weight. Beside the harsher Ribera he seems courtly, even gentle, and the best of his Madonnas have a quiet conviction."},"met:437770":{"d":"A tall panel, about 138 by 78 centimeters, painted in oil, gold and white metal on wood, carries two scenes from the legend of Saint Agapitus of Praeneste, one on each face: the saint in the arena, with a lion and a bear among the museum's keywords, and, on the other face, his beheading. The panel is evidently a wing of an altarpiece, painted on both sides so that it showed one scene when closed and another when open. It dates from about 1500 to 1505.","i":"Agapitus was a boy martyr of the third century at Praeneste, now Palestrina, near Rome; according to the legend the wild beasts in the arena refused to harm him, and he was finally beheaded. The beasts that spared him are a sign of divine protection, and the story presented a young saint's constancy to worshippers. Altarpieces with painted wings were opened on feast days and closed for ordinary days, so martyrdom scenes were part of the church's liturgical rhythm.","c":"Swiss altarpiece painting of about 1500 mixes late Gothic narrative with the new interest in oil and detail, and these wings have both: bright color, gold and silvery metal for armor and ornament, and lively action. The two sides of the panel, one calm and one violent, show good planning. The painter is not a great one, but he knows how to tell a story, and the surviving surfaces give a clear sense of what a provincial altar looked like."},"met:437771":{"d":"A tall panel, about 138 by 78 centimeters, painted in oil, gold and white metal on wood, carries two miracles of Saint Remigius, one on each face: the saint replenishing a barrel of wine, with a man, a woman, a girl and wine among the museum's keywords, and, on the other face, the burning wheat. Like the panel of Saint Agapitus of the same size and date, it was evidently a wing of an altarpiece painted on both sides. It dates from about 1500 to 1505.","i":"Remigius, bishop of Reims in the late fifth and early sixth century, baptized Clovis, king of the Franks, and was remembered for his miracles. In the legend he blessed an empty barrel of wine, which filled, and he stopped a fire from burning a field of wheat. Such stories of plenty and protection spoke to a community that depended on the harvest, and a pair of wings with his miracles would have made the saint's help feel close.","c":"Both faces show an appealing, homespun narrative: a saint at ease among ordinary people, miracles told with domestic detail. The wine barrel, the wheat field and the household figures are likely to be painted with a naive directness that has more charm than a polished court style would. The space is shallow and the figures simple, and the metal and gold provide glitter, but the storytelling is clear. It is a modest, likeable, anonymous piece of Swiss late Gothic painting."},"met:437773":{"d":"A woman is the subject of this small enamel portrait by an anonymous Swiss painter of about 1780; the museum gives no dimensions, and the keywords record only a woman and a portrait. Enamel, colored glass fused onto a metal plate in a kiln, was a specialty of Geneva, whose painters made portrait enamels, watch cases and jewelry for the courts and merchants of Europe. At this date such a portrait would be a keepsake to be set in a locket, bracelet or snuffbox.","i":"The enamel portrait offered a permanence that watercolor could not, since the colors are fired glass, and by the later eighteenth century Geneva's enamellers supplied portraits of women, sentimental subjects and miniature copies of famous paintings to an international clientele. The sitter probably belonged to the middle or upper ranks that could afford the luxury, but her name and circumstances are not recorded.","c":"Enamel gives a woman's face a smooth, luminous finish that no other miniature medium quite matches, and the Genevan makers were masters of it. The cost is a certain coolness, as the fired glass resists the soft spontaneity of watercolor on ivory. An anonymous example like this cannot be judged by the painter's invention, only by its refinement, and refined it is likely to be, as the medium demands."},"met:15917":{"d":"Three boys are the subjects of this large watercolor miniature on ivory, about 12.5 by 9.2 centimeters, painted in the 1830s by Thomas Badger. The museum's keywords record a leaf and a butterfly as well as the boys, so the group is accompanied by natural emblems. At more than twice the size of a pocket miniature it is a showpiece meant to be framed and displayed, not worn.","i":"The butterfly, a traditional emblem of the soul and of fleeting life, and the leaf, a sign of growth and of seasons passing, give a group portrait of boys a quiet moral: that childhood is brief. Parents of the 1830s, in an age that prized sentiment and the innocence of the young, welcomed such touches. The title does not name the boys, and the picture is best seen as a family keepsake whose family has been lost.","c":"A group of three on an ivory plate is hard to do: heads must be differentiated, poses arranged and the whole kept clear in a small field. Badger's strength, as in the best American miniatures of the decade, is a firm, neat likeness, and the butterfly and leaf give the group a note of charm. The danger is sweetness and the sameness of the three faces, but within its scale it is an ambitious and pleasing picture."},"cma:136682":{"d":"A tall, slender woman in a white muslin gown stands on a terrace and leans lightly against a stone pedestal, holding a pink rose. Her pale hair is covered by a loose white cap and veil, long grey gloves reach up her forearms, and a blue sash circles her waist. A peacock perched on a stone urn at the right turns its head and spreads its long, jewel-coloured tail down the steps. Behind her the ground falls away to a lake and distant blue mountains under a heavy, clearing sky, and red flowers grow at the lower left. The picture is more than two and a half metres high, and the white dress sweeps almost to the bottom edge.","i":"The Cleveland museum identifies the sitter as the Irish poet Lady Manners, shown as the goddess Juno, whose bird is the peacock. She rejected the portrait as unflattering. Lawrence exhibited it at the Royal Academy in 1794 with a label saying it was for sale, and it was still in his collection when he died in 1830. Catherine, the museum adds, wrote poetry about longing to escape the fashionable world. Portraits that dressed a sitter as a classical deity were a long English tradition, but Lawrence, then in his mid-twenties and already the king's painter, made the allegory part of a modern, fashionable picture.","c":"Lawrence's gift for bravura is clear. The white dress, painted in loose, wet strokes against cool greys, shimmers more than it describes, and the colours of the peacock and the stormy sky add a rich contrast. The sitter's displeasure is understandable. The pose is languid and the face a little generic, and the pasted-on allegory of Juno leaves little room for the personality of a poet. The picture shows why Lawrence became Britain's most fashionable portrait painter: it dazzles first and describes second."},"cma:173631":{"d":"A view of Fort George Island, at the mouth of the St. Johns River in northeastern Florida, is the subject of this vertical oil painting, about 65 by 55 centimeters, dated 1878. The Cleveland museum notes that Moran visited the area in February and March of 1877, made many sketches around St. Augustine and Jacksonville, and then worked them up into more than a dozen oil paintings in his Newark, New Jersey, studio. The picture is a studio painting made from field studies, not painted on the spot.","i":"In the 1870s the northeast coast of Florida was becoming a tourist destination, reached by steamboat and railroad, and Moran, better known for his Western landscapes of Yellowstone and the Grand Canyon, found there a softer subject. The museum records that Cleveland industrialist Hinman B. Hurlbut bought this picture directly from the artist for $225, a reminder of how the new wealth of the Gilded Age filled its houses with landscapes.","c":"Moran's Florida pictures are lyrical and unspectacular, a pleasant change from the grandeur of the West: he uses glowing light, soft mist and fine tonal gradations to give a flat coast a sense of poetry, in a manner learned from Turner. The cost is a certain vagueness, and the picture is less memorable than his great mountain views. It is an attractive and relaxed example of a painter's gentler side."},"cma:370119":{"d":"The infant Jesus places a ring on the finger of Saint Catherine of Alexandria in this tall panel, about 148 by 103 centimeters, painted in oil about 1560, as the Holy Family looks on. Cleveland's museum notes that the faces of the women and children are elegant and otherworldly, while the elderly Saint Joseph is more naturalistic and may be a likeness of someone the painter knew. The saint's usual attribute, the spiked wheel, is barely visible below her hand.","i":"The mystic marriage, a purely symbolic wedding in which Catherine, who had dedicated herself to God, is joined to Christ by his ring, was a favorite subject of the Renaissance. Maso da San Friano, a Florentine who worked for the Medici, painted it in the Mannerist style of the mid-sixteenth century, which prized elegance, complex poses and a cool beauty, with a naturalistic Joseph as a counterpoint to the idealized women and children.","c":"Maso's elegant refinement is the picture's first pleasure: the women's faces are cool, ivory-like and calm, the poses elaborate and the surface polished. Saint Joseph, by contrast, is a living old man, with the weathered individuality of a portrait, and the contrast is the picture's most interesting idea. The coolness and artifice limit its warmth, but it is a fine, sophisticated example of Florentine Mannerism by an artist who died young."},"nga:41675":{"d":"A woman and child sit on a throne against a shiny gold ground in this small vertical panel, about 36 by 26 centimeters. The Virgin, in a marine-blue mantle over a pink robe, tilts her head and looks off to the side, with a narrow nose and a thin-lipped mouth. The Child sits upright in a red robe, points with one hand and holds a scroll in the other. Two small angels in the upper corners hold up a patterned cloth, and two small men look up from the lower corners: one with a long beard and a scroll reading ECE AGNU DEI, the other with gold keys.","i":"The scroll, Behold the Lamb of God, carries the words John the Baptist spoke on seeing Christ, and the keys identify Saint Peter, so the men below are John and Peter, standing as witnesses at the Virgin's throne. The angels hold a cloth of honor, a sign of royalty, behind the pair. A small devotional panel like this one belongs to the world of Cimabue and his contemporaries, who gave the Byzantine formulas of the Virgin a new gravity in Florence.","c":"The panel is small and precious, and its design is a lesson in the severe elegance of the Duecento: the strict symmetry, the gold ground, the Virgin's angular, slightly distorted face and the red border make an icon of great clarity. It lacks the scale and depth of the great Florentine altarpieces, and the faces are formulaic, but the luminous color and the graceful tilt of the Virgin's head show the style already moving toward the human."},"nga:41686":{"d":"A man is shown from the waist up, standing in the corner of a room beside a window on the right that opens onto green mountains and trees under a blue-and-white sky. He has a pale face with a large nose, prominent chin, deep-set eyes and a slight frown, and reddish-blond shoulder-length hair under a vivid red hat. He wears a dark robe patterned with green flowers over a red garment, and two rings, one with a blue stone and one with a red. The wall behind is brick-patterned, with a blue hanging and wooden beams overhead.","i":"A window opening on a landscape was a Netherlandish device for placing a sitter in the world, and by about 1500 it had traveled to the Alpine regions, where trade routes and mining wealth linked Tyrol with Italy, Germany and the Low Countries. The red hat, robe and two rings show a man of means, perhaps a merchant, official or mining investor, though nothing names him. Portraits of private men were still relatively rare in this part of Europe.","c":"The portrait's attraction is its unidealized, candid face: the large nose, heavy chin and faint frown are recorded with an honesty that suggests a real person rather than a type. The room is less successful: the man is wedged into a corner and the space is cramped, and the landscape through the window is more a pattern than a depth. A provincial work, it still has an immediacy that polished Italian portraits sometimes lose."},"nga:53528":{"d":"The saint stands upright in bishop's dress against a dark ground: a jeweled mitre topped with a cross, a red robe with gold trim over a white vestment, curly brown hair and a serene face. In one hand he holds a crosier, and in the other he cradles a mitred head, a sign of martyrdom. A golden patterned panel stands behind him, and the name SANCTUS ALBANUS is lettered at the base. The tall narrow panel is about 156 by 52 centimeters, painted in oil about 1500 to 1525.","i":"Alban of Mainz, a martyr of the fifth century venerated in the German lands, is shown with a severed head, the usual sign of a saint who, in the legend, picked up his own head after his beheading and carried it to the place of his burial. Such cephalophore saints were popular in the late Middle Ages, and the miracle was a sign of the power of faith beyond death. Panels of standing saints like this one, probably from an altarpiece, offered a church a company of protectors.","c":"These tall panels are decorative and ceremonial, and the saint's jeweled mitre, red and gold robes and the golden panel behind him are painted with real care for richness. The figure is stiff and the face a type, and the grisly head is likely handled with a gentleness that avoids horror. As one of a set of four, the panel's effect is of a procession of protectors, and the glitter of surface is the point."},"nga:53529":{"d":"The saint stands facing forward, slightly to the side, with a solemn face and light hair, in a red robe with wide sleeves over a white vestment and an elaborately patterned mitre. In one hand he holds a bishop's crosier and in the other a model of a church, and a large medallion hangs at his neck. The background is rich golden ornament against deep red. The tall narrow panel is about 156 by 52 centimeters, painted in oil about 1500 to 1525.","i":"Wolfgang, a bishop of Regensburg in the tenth century, is one of the most popular saints of Bavaria, Austria and the Tyrol, a patron of woodcutters, carpenters and shepherds. The church model is his usual sign, referring to the church he founded at the lake now named for him, and in some pictures an axe is added. As one of a set of four standing saints, he stands among the protectors whom a late medieval Tyrolean church would call on.","c":"The panel is a study in surface: red and gold patterns on the vestments, the ornate mitre and medallion, and the dark red ground rival each other for attention. The saint himself is quiet and stiff, with a solemn face that does not engage the viewer, as befits an icon-like figure in a church. It is decorative piety at a high level of finish, and the church model, a clear and charming attribute, is its one touch of narrative."},"nga:53530":{"d":"A bishop stands slightly turned and reads from a book, in a mitre adorned with crosses and a deep red and gold robe with white at collar and hem, a large brooch on his chest. A tall gold-colored crosier stands beside him, and a dark tapestry-like ground fills the background. At the bottom a small kneeling figure in black with a ruffled collar holds a red rosary, and another small figure is partly visible at the saint's feet. The tall panel is about 156 by 52 centimeters, painted in oil about 1500 to 1525.","i":"The saint is Valentine, here a bishop: probably Valentine of Passau, the early missionary bishop of Raetia whose cult was strong in Tyrol, where he is buried near Merano, and who was invoked against epilepsy. The small kneeling figure in black is probably the donor who paid for the panel, shown at the saint's feet in the usual way with his rosary. Donor figures, tiny beside the saints, recorded the patron's piety and sought the saint's protection.","c":"The donor is the surprise: a small, plainly dressed worshipper beside the splendid saint, painted with an unflattering directness that makes him more human than the saint. The bishop himself, deep in his book, is a fine, composed figure, though one of a type. The result is typical of Tyrolean church painting around 1500: gold, red and attention to costume, with a streak of realistic observation in the human details."},"nga:53531":{"d":"A saint named in the title as Alcuin stands in this tall narrow panel, about 155 by 52 centimeters, painted in oil about 1500 to 1525; the museum's keywords record a book and a sword as his attributes. It matches the panels of Saints Alban, Wolfgang and Valentine in size, date and format, so it is likely another standing figure in rich dress against a patterned ground. As one of a set of four, it was probably part of an altarpiece or a group of church panels.","i":"Alcuin of York, the great scholar of Charlemagne's court and abbot of Tours, was a teacher of Europe, and a book suits a saint of learning. The sword, which usually marks a martyr, is harder to read in that light, and the iconography of provincial altarpieces is often lost in this way. The panel's meaning rests on its place in the set: a company of holy figures standing guard over the altar.","c":"A set of four tall saints lives by repetition with variation, and this panel, like the others, offers rich costume and quiet frontality. Where its companions have bold attributes, the model church and the carried head, this saint is quieter. The set's merit is collective: four protectors standing in a row, with gold and red as the family colors. Taken singly, the picture is an agreeable, competent provincial work, interesting for what it adds to the group."},"nga:46048":{"d":"A woman's head is the subject of this small oil painting on a poplar panel, about 40 by 29 centimeters, dated about 1510 to 1520. The museum's keyword is only female, so the picture is best described by type: a head study in the Umbrian manner of the time, in which the face is a gentle oval, the features delicate and the expression sweet and withdrawn, following the example of Perugino and the young Raphael. It may be a fragment of a larger religious picture or an independent ideal head.","i":"Umbrian painting around 1500 was admired for gentleness and grace: Perugino's Madonnas and saints gave Italy a calm, spacious ideal of beauty that Raphael absorbed and transformed. A head like this one, whether a portrait of a woman or an ideal head, shows the manner's common currency, the oval face, the lowered gaze, the soft modeling. The painter's name is lost, which is common for the many followers of Perugino who supplied churches and homes.","c":"Umbrian painters of this date could be charming and could be formulaic, and a head of this kind lives by delicacy of modeling and a quiet expression. If the face is somewhat generic, it has the sweetness that the school prized, and the small, precise scale suits a private picture. It lacks the individuality of a true portrait and the grandeur of Raphael, but it is a pleasant example of the Perugino tradition at one remove."},"nga:219341":{"d":"At the front corner of a wooden table, against a deep sage-green ground, stand an ivory-white lidded stoneware jug, a long white clay pipe, an open paper packet of tobacco and a burning ember in a clay dish. The jug has a ridged neck, a metal lid with a thumbpiece and an oval on its body enclosing a coat of arms with three stacked crosses and the date 1650. The pipe lies along the table's edge, a knot of ash fallen from its bowl, with scattered tobacco and broken pipe ends like sticks of chalk. Light falls from the left.","i":"A pipe, tobacco and a glowing ember make a smoker's still life, a favorite Dutch subject of the mid-seventeenth century. The pleasures of tobacco could be read as a moral lesson: smoke and burning embers were old emblems of the brevity of life, and some viewers took smoking as a vice. The jug, a German Westerwald stoneware, bears the three crosses of Amsterdam's arms. The painter's signature, Jan van de Velde fecit, is written on the table edge as if carved there.","c":"A great deal is done with very little: a corner of table, a handful of humble objects, and a cool green ground against which the ivory jug and white pipe glow. The textures are exact, the stoneware's glaze, the crumpled paper, the dry tobacco, and the diagonal of the pipe gives the composition tension. It is a smaller, plainer picture than the silver-and-glass banquets of Heda or Kalf, but it has a quiet, decisive economy."},"nga:46025":{"d":"A man is the subject of this small oil portrait on panel, about 27 by 19 centimeters, by an unknown Venetian painter of the late fifteenth century. The museum's keywords record only a man, so the picture is best described by type: a bust-length portrait on a small panel, in the Venetian manner that developed after Antonello da Messina brought the northern oil technique and the three-quarter pose to the city in the 1470s. The size suits a private possession, kept in a room or a family collection.","i":"Venetian portraits of the later fifteenth century recorded senators, merchants, humanists and clergymen in a city that valued civic dignity and personal reputation, and they were usually made for the sitter's family. The pose, a dark ground and plain dress typically signaled gravity rather than display. The sitter's name is lost, and the portrait is one of many anonymous Venetian faces from the generation of Giovanni Bellini.","c":"Venetian portraits of this date typically have a calm, even candor: a sober face, strong light and a dark ground, with little ornament. The best, by Bellini and Antonello, are among the great portraits of the Renaissance; the anonymous ones, like this, show the high standard of the average Venetian workshop. It is a modest picture with a sure sense of character."},"nga:1235":{"d":"A young man is shown from the chest up, facing slightly left and looking at the viewer out of the corner of his eye. He has light blond, shoulder-length wavy hair under a black cap, a fair complexion, light green eyes, a strong jaw and thin lips in a neutral expression. He wears a brown mantle lined with gray fur over a white garment, against a dark gray ground. The small panel is about 28 by 23 centimeters, painted about 1505.","i":"Around 1505 Venetian portraiture took on a new mood under the influence of Giorgione and the young Titian: sitters became introspective, with glances turned aside and an air of private thought. The fur-lined mantle and black cap mark a young man of means, possibly a patrician, and the sidelong glance gives him a hint of reverie. The sitter's name is lost, but his type is the educated young Venetian of the early sixteenth century.","c":"The sidelong glance is the picture's charm: the face seems to stop for a moment in thought, and the dark ground and fur mantle isolate it. The handling is soft and the likeness plausible, but the painter does not reach the depth of Giorgione; the work is a good, attractive example of a fashionable type. It lacks a name and a documented context, but as a mood it has real appeal."},"nga:1139":{"d":"In a wooded glade, two pairs of figures sit among birds: at the left a nude woman on a boulder rests a hand on the shoulder of a white-bearded satyr, who holds up what may be a shell; farther back a second nude woman, seen from behind, raises a long stick toward a gray cat on the far bank of a thin river. At the right a young man in a pink robe over a blue tunic, barefoot, draws a bow across a violin-like instrument tucked under his chin. The long, low panel is about 40 by 81 centimeters, painted about 1515 and transferred to canvas.","i":"The musician is Orpheus, the Thracian poet whose music charmed animals, trees and even the powers of the underworld; his instrument, a bowed lira da braccio, is the Renaissance equivalent of the ancient lyre. The nymphs, the satyr and the wild creatures around him belong to the pastoral world of Venetian painting in the years of Giorgione and Titian, in which music, love and nature are joined. A long panel like this was probably set into furniture or wall paneling in a Venetian house.","c":"The picture is a poem of idleness: the glade, the birds, the lazy poses and the music make an Arcadia without plot, and the thin river and bronze-green trees give the setting a dreamy depth. The figures are small and not strongly characterized, and the story is hard to read, but the mood, soft, warm and a little mysterious, is just what the Venetian pastoral aimed at. The transfer to canvas may have dulled the surface, but the charm survives."},"nga:216037":{"d":"A large pig carcass hangs upside down from a wooden frame at the center of this small panel, about 40 by 52 centimeters, dated 1653, cut open to show white ribs and red muscle while several people tend it. At the left a boy by a barrel and basket holds a handful of small knives; at the right a man in a red cap and white apron watches with a drink in his hand, as an older man in a furred cap holds one edge of the carcass. Behind it another boy blows up what is probably the animal's bladder, and two women look out of a wooden building.","i":"Pig-killing was a yearly event of the Dutch countryside in late autumn, when meat was salted and smoked for winter, and pictures of it belong to a tradition of slaughtered-animal scenes that Rembrandt would famously take up in his Slaughtered Ox of 1655. Victors, a pupil of Rembrandt, treats the work with the matter-of-fact humor of the peasant genre: the blown-up bladder is a children's toy, and the onlookers enjoy the occasion. For some viewers the carcass could also recall the brevity of life.","c":"Victors is not Rembrandt, but he has a fine eye for the human side of a rough job: the boy with the knives, the man with the drink and the child with the bladder give the scene liveliness and humor. The carcass itself is painted with an unflinching, almost tender realism, in the pinks, reds and purples that a Dutch painter loved. The composition is a little crowded and the figures awkward, but its frankness and warmth engage."},"nga:157388":{"d":"Viewed from above across a low horizon, a fleet of large ships sails on a rough dark-blue sea tipped with white surf. The biggest ship, at the center, has four sails and many flags, including a red, white and blue striped flag and a large golden one with a red lion, and tiny sailors swarm over its deck and rigging. Smaller sailboats and a rowboat lie behind, and the faint blue outline of a far coast rises on the horizon beneath a dark gray, stormy sky. The canvas is about 57 by 97 centimeters and dated 1614.","i":"Hendrick Cornelisz. Vroom of Haarlem is called the father of Dutch marine painting, and pictures like this one show why: he gave the Netherlands, a nation whose wealth and survival depended on ships, a seascape art of its own. The golden flag with a red lion is the banner of Holland, and the striped flag shows the Dutch colors, which mark the fleet as Dutch. Fleet pictures served as patriotic images in the years when the young republic was winning its place on the sea.","c":"Vroom's strengths are accuracy and clarity: the ships are drawn with a shipbuilder's detail, the rigging is crisp and the figures on deck are as numerous as the realism demands. His seas are more schematic than the atmospheric waters of later Dutch painters such as Van de Velde and Van de Cappelle, and the tonal unity of the 1650s has not yet arrived. But the stormy sky and the dark, white-crested waves give the early work a sturdy drama."},"cma:173242":{"d":"A single figure, a philosopher, is the subject of this small oil painting on panel, about 38 by 27 centimeters, dated 1882. The title suggests a scholar absorbed in thought or study, the sort of old man with books in a quiet room that French academic painters of the period favored. Panel and small scale were the usual choices for such cabinet pictures, prized by collectors for fine finish and a hint of the old masters.","i":"Walter Gay, a Massachusetts-born painter who settled in Paris in 1876 and trained with Léon Bonnat, began with small, polished figure pictures of this kind, in the taste of the Salon and of collectors who admired Meissonier. The philosopher was an old theme, from Rembrandt's meditating scholars to the Salon's learned old men, and it let a young painter show skill in character, costume and light.","c":"At twenty-six Gay is learning the Salon's polite skills: he can paint a figure with finish, and he knows how to put a quiet old man into an atmosphere of study. Pictures of this kind rarely surprise, and the strengths, neat drawing, controlled tone and tasteful color, are those of the training. It is a promising apprentice work by a painter who would find his true subject, the quiet room, years later."},"cma:105224":{"d":"A group of villagers sit in a Dutch interior listening with rapt attention to a spooky tale told by a woman at a spinning wheel, in this large horizontal canvas, about 121 by 192 centimeters, painted in 1887. A young girl clutches her doll in fascination. The women wear the traditional dress of Volendam, a fishing village in North Holland, so the picture is a genre scene of a particular place, and its size gives it the weight of an exhibition piece.","i":"In the 1880s the Netherlands became a magnet for American and other foreign artists, who found in its villages, costumes and cottage interiors a picturesque, unspoiled world, and MacEwen, from Chicago, was one of them. The ghost story is a timeless evening pleasure, and the picture turns a village gathering into a study of attention, with faces held in suspense. Such pictures appealed to Salon audiences who prized storytelling and sentiment.","c":"The picture's skill is in its management of a crowd's attention: faces turn, eyes widen, the girl hugs her doll, and the storyteller at her wheel is the still center. The Dutch interior and its light give it a mood that recalls Rembrandt, learned as much from the Hague painters as from the old masters. The sentiment is a little neat and the types somewhat quaint, but it is narrative painting at its best: one idea, clearly told."},"nga:214135":{"d":"Two clusters of grapes hang from the top edge of this small vertical canvas, about 45 by 31 centimeters. The cluster at the left is reddish-purple and the one at the right green, both on vines that loop around thin, leafy branches. Light falls on the right-hand grapes, leaving the left sides of both clusters in shadow. Wide leaves in green and orange-brown fill the top, against a brown ground, lighter on the right and darker on the left. The painting is undated.","i":"Hanging grapes are an old still-life motif, traced back to the ancient story of the painter Zeuxis, whose painted grapes were said to fool the birds, and the subject let a painter show skill at translucent skin, bloom and the roundness of fruit. In nineteenth-century America grapes were a symbol of abundance and cultivated taste, and artists such as Way supplied pictures of fruit for dining rooms and parlors.","c":"The pleasure here is simple: the red and green clusters side by side, the light on one and the shadow on the other, the dark leaves and the brown ground that make the fruit glow. Way paints the grapes with convincing roundness and bloom, though the composition is plain and the leaves somewhat stiff. It is a modest, attractive work from a specialist in fruit."},"nga:214136":{"d":"A single bunch of grapes hangs in this vertical canvas, about 55 by 45 centimeters, dated 1867; the museum's keyword is fruit. The motif is an old one in still life, and a hanging bunch lets the painter show the fruit's roundness, bloom and translucent skin against a plain ground. The painter, Sarah Wilhelmina Wenzler, is an American little known today, and her picture is best described by its type: a careful, domestic study of fruit.","i":"Still life was one of the genres open to women painters in the nineteenth century, since it could be studied and painted at home and sold for parlors and dining rooms. Grapes, with their associations of abundance and the vine, were a favorite subject. An 1867 date places the picture in the years after the Civil War, when American collectors increasingly bought native still lifes of fruit and flowers.","c":"Hanging grapes are a test of observation: the bloom on the skin, the transparency where light passes through, the weight of the cluster on its stem. A modest, little-known painter like Wenzler reaches for no grand effect, and the picture's value lies in its quiet attention to the fruit. It does not claim more, and for that reason it is attractive in its simplicity."},"cma:151796":{"d":"A view of the Pointe de Cabellou, a headland at Concarneau on the coast of Brittany, is the subject of this wide horizontal oil painting, about 60 by 92 centimeters, dated 1881. Picknell painted in Brittany in these years, working outdoors in the clear, bright light of the coast, and the scene is likely to combine low land, sea and a broad sky, the elements he favored. It is a plein-air landscape of the kind American artists brought home from the French coast.","i":"Concarneau and Pont-Aven in Brittany drew American painters in the late 1870s and 1880s, who found there cheap lodging, strong light and picturesque fisherfolk, and who formed a colony in which Picknell was a leading figure. His Road to Concarneau of 1880, with its stark, bright road under a high sky, was admired, and this coast picture of the next year continues the theme. Such pictures were part of the American encounter with French open-air painting.","c":"Picknell's strength is a clear, luminous honesty before nature: low horizons, a sense of wind and light, and a tonal control learned from Corot and Pissarro. The danger in such a landscape is emptiness, and the composition depends on the delicacy of its grays and greens. It lacks the exuberance of the Impressionists, whom he approached with caution, but it has a sober integrity that wears well."},"cma:144496":{"d":"A cantaloupe, peaches, raspberries and chestnuts are the fruits of this small still life, about 36 by 46 centimeters, painted about 1880; Cleveland's museum notes that each peach, raspberry and chestnut is painted from a different angle. The objects are rendered in meticulous detail, capturing a range of textures from the soft fuzz of peach skin to the coarse webbing of the melon's rind.","i":"William Mason Brown began as a portrait and landscape painter and turned to still life in the 1860s, which brought him success and renown. His close study of fruit is in the spirit of the American taste for truth to nature associated with Ruskin and the American Pre-Raphaelites, in which every texture and detail was a matter of pride. Pictures of fruit were sold to families for dining rooms and parlors, where they stood for abundance and good living.","c":"The painter's triumph is texture: peach fuzz, raspberry bumps, the netted skin of the melon and the sheen of chestnuts are all convincing, and the different angle of each fruit gives the group a lively rhythm. The risk of such precision is a certain coolness; the fruit is observed rather than enjoyed, and the arrangement is more inventory than composition. Still, the fidelity and the quiet are impressive, and the picture is a very good example of its type."},"nga:214137":{"d":"Large green lily pads and blooming lotus flowers fill this small vertical painting, about 31 by 24 centimeters. The blossoms, white and pink, rest on the ends of long stems, and pads and stems dominate the upper and middle of the picture, while the bottom third is dark green water reflecting the leaves, with a few pink petals floating on it. The background is a light green. The painting is dated 1894 and painted in oil on board.","i":"The lotus is sacred in Buddhism: it rises clean from muddy water and stands for purity and enlightenment, which is why the title calls it Buddha's flowers. Theodore Wores, a San Francisco painter, visited Japan in the 1880s and again in the 1890s and made a specialty of Japanese subjects for American audiences hungry for the picturesque East. The lotus ponds of Tokyo, such as the one at Ueno, were a famous summer sight.","c":"The picture is bold in its cropping: the pads and stems press up against the top edge, and a band of dark water below gives the flowers a pale stage. The color is cool and fresh, and the pink-white blossoms stand out nicely against the greens. Wores's debt to Japanese prints shows in the flat pattern and the cropped view. It is a quiet, handsome small picture of a famous sight."}};
export const BIOS = {"q259538":"Edwin Austin Abbey was born in Philadelphia in 1852, studied at the Pennsylvania Academy and in 1871 began work in New York as an illustrator for Harper's, where his drawings of old English life and of Shakespeare and the poets won fame. In 1878 he went to England and lived there for the rest of his life, in the Cotswolds and later in London. He turned to oil painting, with scenes from Shakespeare and medieval legend such as King Lear, Act I, Scene I, and was elected to the Royal Academy in 1898, the year he showed this picture. He also painted large murals: the Quest of the Holy Grail for the Boston Public Library (1890 to 1901) and a series for the Pennsylvania State Capitol at Harrisburg, and he was commissioned to paint the coronation of Edward VII. He died in London in 1911.","q725410":"Lemuel Francis Abbott was born in Leicestershire around 1760, the son of a clergyman, and at fourteen became a pupil of the painter Francis Hayman in London. After Hayman's death in 1776 he worked on his own, and by about 1780 he had settled in Bloomsbury as a portraitist, exhibiting at the Royal Academy though never becoming a member. His sitters were mostly naval officers, scientists and writers, among them Admiral Sir Robert Calder, the astronomer William Herschel and the poet William Cowper. His portrait of Horatio Nelson, begun in 1797 while both men lodged in Bond Street, became the standard likeness, and Lady Nelson wrote that the resemblance was great. Overwork and an unhappy home life are said to have broken his mind. He was declared insane in 1798 and died a few years later.","q306759":"Abbott H. Thayer was born in Boston in 1849 and grew up near Keene, New Hampshire, in the shadow of Mount Monadnock, where he trapped and sketched birds and took his first commissions painting animal portraits. He studied in New York and then in Paris under Jean-Léon Gérôme in the later 1870s. Back in America he became known for serene, softly lit figures of women and girls, often posed by his own children as angels or classical heroines, and he also painted society portraits such as Mrs. William F. Milton. Collectors, above all the Detroit industrialist Charles Lang Freer, bought his work steadily. Thayer settled in Dublin, New Hampshire, where he taught pupils, among them Rockwell Kent. His painting went out of fashion after his death in 1921.","abbott_handerson_thayer":"Abbott Handerson Thayer was an American painter, naturalist and teacher, born in Boston in 1849. As a boy he hunted, trapped and drew birds in the New Hampshire countryside, and he never lost his love of natural history. After training in New York and under Gérôme in Paris, he became a painter of idealised figures, among them a Hebe in a pale classical robe and angels modelled on his own children. In his last decades he worked with his son Gerald on a book, Concealing-Coloration in the Animal Kingdom, published in 1909, which argued that the shading of animals hides them from predators. His idea of countershading proved sound, though his claim that nearly all animal colour is protective was mocked by Theodore Roosevelt. Thayer died in New Hampshire in 1921.","q329811":"Abraham Bloemaert was a Catholic painter in the Protestant Dutch Republic. Born in Gorinchem in 1566 and raised in Utrecht, where he worked all his life, he supplied Catholic churches and patrons with altarpieces, and Utrecht painters, whatever their faith, with a training in drawing. His style moved from Mannerist twisting figures to a more natural manner with lighter colour and humble subjects in his later years, and he painted shepherds, peasants and old men's heads as well as biblical and mythological scenes. His pupils included Hendrick ter Brugghen and Gerrit van Honthorst, and his four sons, among them Hendrick, were also painters. He made drawings and prints throughout his career. Head of an Old Man, of 1625 to 1628, is one of these studies of character. He died in Utrecht in 1651.","q1824525":"Abraham Brueghel was baptised in Antwerp in 1631, the son of Jan Brueghel the Younger and great-grandson of Pieter Bruegel the Elder. He trained in the family workshop and sold his first flower piece at fifteen. At eighteen he went to Italy to carry out a commission for Prince Antonio Ruffo in Sicily, a collector who also owned Rembrandt's Aristotle. He moved to Rome in 1659, joined the Bentvueghels, the society of Dutch and Flemish artists, under the nickname Rijngraaf, and was admitted to the Accademia di San Luca in 1670. By the mid-1670s he had settled in Naples, where he died around 1690. There he brought the rich, decorative Flemish-Roman still life of fruit and flowers to a city that had not known it, painted in warm light and glowing colour.","q2821872":"Abraham de Vries was a Dutch portrait painter who led a restless life. He is now believed to have been born in The Hague around 1590, not in Rotterdam as once thought, and he is recorded in Rotterdam in 1617. In the 1620s he travelled in France, where at Aix-en-Provence he taught the Antwerp painter Jan Cossiers and met the scholar Peiresc, a friend of Rubens, and he later worked in Paris and Antwerp, joining the Antwerp guild in 1634. The Cardinal-Infante Ferdinand, governor of the Spanish Netherlands, invited him to Brussels, where his portraits were reportedly judged superior to those of Van Dyck. By 1644 he was settled in The Hague, where he made his will in 1648 and died in 1649 or 1650. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man, dated 1643, belongs to this last period.","q538840":"Abraham Hondius was born in Rotterdam around 1630, the son of a city stonemason, and trained there with Pieter de Bloot and Cornelis Saftleven. He moved to Amsterdam in 1659 and to London in 1666, where he spent the rest of his life. More than two thirds of his output consists of hunting scenes, animal fights and animal studies, painted in a vigorous manner that borrowed from many painters without settling into a style wholly his own. He also painted landscapes, genre and religious subjects, such as the Christ among the Doctors in the Metropolitan Museum, and views of London, among them a Frozen Thames beside Old London Bridge. The Monkey and the Cat, in Cleveland, shows his taste for comic animal subjects. His last known work is dated 1690, and he died in London in the 1690s.","q330139":"Abraham Janssens, who added the name van Nuyssen to his signature, was born in Antwerp, probably in 1575, and died there in 1632. He studied under Jan Snellinck, then lived mainly in Rome from 1597 to 1602, where Caravaggio's strong light and heavy shadow left a lasting mark on his style. After returning he became a master of the Antwerp guild and in 1607 its dean. Until Rubens came home from Italy in 1608, Janssens was the leading history painter in the Low Countries, and his large allegory Scaldis and Antverpia, commissioned by the city magistrates, remains his masterpiece. Rubens's return did not immediately eclipse him, but over time the younger man took most important commissions. The Dead Christ in the Tomb with Two Angels dates from about 1610.","q330848":"Abraham van Beyeren was born in The Hague around 1620, the son of a painter, and began training in 1636 with Tyman Cracht. He lived in Leiden and then moved repeatedly, through Delft, The Hague, Amsterdam, Alkmaar and Gouda, settling at last in Overschie, near Rotterdam, where he died in 1690. His moves suggest he never grew rich. He began with seascapes and fish still lifes, influenced by Jan van Goyen, but in the 1650s and 1660s he turned to the lavish banquet pieces for which he is known, with silver jugs, ham, lobster, glass and fruit painted in a free, glowing touch. He signed with the monogram AVB and never dated a picture, so his chronology is hard to fix. Little noticed in his own day, he is now ranked among the finest Dutch painters of sumptuous still life.","q957766":"Abraham van Cuylenborch was born in Utrecht around 1620 and died there in 1658. Little is recorded of his life beyond his work as a painter of small landscapes in the manner of Cornelis van Poelenburch, the leading Italianate painter in Utrecht. Like Poelenburch, he set tiny, smoothly painted figures, often nymphs, bathers or other mythological company, in warm, hazy scenes with grottoes and ruins that recall the countryside near Rome. Pictures of this kind were made as cabinet pieces for private collectors and were meant to be looked at closely. The Bacchus and Nymphs in a Landscape, probably of the 1640s, is an example. He died in his late thirties, and his name is now known mainly to specialists in Dutch Italianate painting.","q330854":"Abraham van Diepenbeeck was baptised at 's-Hertogenbosch in 1596 and began as a glass painter. He worked mainly in Antwerp, where he died in 1675. Though not a formal pupil of Rubens, he kept close to his workshop, collaborated on projects under his direction, and absorbed the influence of Rubens and Van Dyck, especially in the large paintings he began making in the 1630s. Much of his output consisted of designs: windows for Antwerp churches and convents, tapestry patterns, and drawings for engravers working for printers such as the Plantin Press. He is best remembered for the plates in the Duke of Newcastle's treatise on horsemanship, published in Antwerp in 1658, where the exiled English royalist William Cavendish lived after the Civil War. His Saint Cecilia belongs to the Rubensian tradition he served.","q944962":"Achille-Etna Michallon was born in Paris in 1796, the son of the sculptor Claude Michallon, and studied under Jacques-Louis David and the landscape theorist Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes. In 1817 he won the first Prix de Rome ever awarded for historical landscape and spent more than two years in Italy, painting views in and around Rome that combine classical order with fresh observation from nature. He is remembered above all as an early teacher of Camille Corot, who worked with him before Michallon died of pneumonia in 1822, aged twenty-five. Waterfall at Mont-Dore, dated 1818, records a visit to the volcanic Auvergne. His career was very short, but he is regarded as an important link between neoclassical landscape and the open-air painting that came after.","q235647":"Adélaïde Labille-Guiard was born in Paris in 1749, the daughter of a haberdasher. She learned miniature painting from François-Élie Vincent, then took up pastel with the help of Maurice Quentin de La Tour, and oil painting under Vincent's son François-André, whom she married in 1800. In 1783 she was admitted to the Académie royale on the same day as her rival Élisabeth Vigée Le Brun. Portraits of the king's aunts and of his sister, Madame Élisabeth, brought her royal patronage, and her Self-Portrait with Two Pupils of 1785 shows her at the easel in a silk gown, a confident statement of a woman's place in the profession. She ran a studio for women pupils and argued for women's admission to the Academy. She died in Paris in 1803.","q1797689":"Adam August Müller was born in 1811 and studied at the Royal Danish Academy, where he became one of the favourite pupils of C. W. Eckersberg, the master of the Danish Golden Age. He aimed at history painting, with religious and historical subjects, but his health was poor, and he died in 1844 at the age of thirty-two. The Hall of Antiquities at Charlottenborg Palace, painted in 1830 when he was only nineteen, shows a room in the Copenhagen palace that housed the Academy, and reflects his teacher's insistence on precise perspective, clear daylight and careful drawing. Müller's career was brief, but his work is counted as an important part of Danish art in the years between Eckersberg's generation and the next.","q4678798":"Adam Buck was born in Cork in 1759 and became a skilled miniature painter in Ireland in the 1780s, working in Cork and Dublin before moving permanently to London in 1795. He is a classic portraitist of Regency taste: his sitters, often women and children, wear light Greek-revival gowns and are posed against plain backgrounds among urns and furniture copied from ancient Greek vases, which he collected. His patrons included the singer Angelica Catalani, the actor John Philip Kemble, the collector Thomas Hope, the Duke of York and George IV. Between 1795 and 1833 he sent more than 170 miniatures and small full-lengths to the Royal Academy. He also taught, designed fashion plates, and illustrated Sterne's Sentimental Journey. He died in London in 1833. The Met's portrait of a woman said to be Emma, Lady Hamilton is dated 1804.","q349377":"Adam Frans van der Meulen was born in Brussels in 1632 and trained there under the battle painter Pieter Snayers. In 1664 Colbert, Louis XIV's minister, and the painter Charles Le Brun brought him to Paris, where he became the king's chief painter of campaigns. He accompanied the royal army into Flanders and elsewhere, sketching sieges, marches and cavalry actions on the spot and working them up into large canvases and tapestry designs for the Gobelins. He was received into the Académie royale in 1673 and died in Paris in 1690. His crisp, panoramic scenes, in which the king and his court appear as small figures in a broad landscape, made royal conquests look orderly and splendid. The Cavalry Engagement at the Metropolitan Museum shows the energy he gave to smaller subjects.","q22001979":"Adam Ludwig d'Argent was born in Stuttgart in 1748 and worked as an enamel painter and engraver, first in his native city and later in Paris and elsewhere in Germany. He died in 1829, and little else is recorded of his life. The enamel in the Metropolitan Museum, a likeness of the Emperor Joseph II of about 1780, measures barely an inch and a quarter high. Enamel portraits were made by painting with coloured glass powders on a metal plate and firing it, a slow, exacting craft that gives jewel-like colour and a surface that does not fade, and such miniatures served as court gifts and keepsakes. D'Argent's signature on this one was hidden on the back until the miniature was taken out of its frame for the museum's catalogue of European miniatures, published in 1996.","q364184":"Adolf Schreyer was born in Frankfurt in 1828 and studied at the Städel Institute there, then in Stuttgart and Munich. Travel shaped his art: he accompanied Prince Maximilian of Thurn and Taxis through Hungary, Wallachia, Russia and Turkey, followed the Austrian army into Wallachia in 1854, and visited Egypt and Syria in 1856 and Algiers in 1861. He settled in Paris in 1862, returned to Germany in 1870, and lived at Kronberg near Frankfurt until his death in 1899. He specialised in galloping horses, Arab riders and battle incidents, painted with strong draughtsmanship and dramatic light. French and American collectors, including the Vanderbilts and the Astors, bought him eagerly, and the Metropolitan Museum owns several of his Arab scenes, among them Arabs on the March. His reputation faded with the taste for Orientalism.","q164961":"Adolph Menzel was born in Breslau in 1815 and moved with his family to Berlin, where he lived for the rest of his long life. The son of a lithographer, he was largely self-taught. He first became famous for about four hundred drawings illustrating Franz Kugler's history of Frederick the Great, then painted the king's court in pictures such as The Flute Concert at Sanssouci. Alongside these official successes he made small, unposed pictures of his own rooms, balconies and Berlin streets, painted freely and often unfinished, as in the Met's view of his sitting room in the Ritterstrasse. His Iron Rolling Mill, of 1872 to 1875, was among the first great paintings of industrial labour. He was ennobled in 1898, and when he died in 1905 he was given a state funeral.","q116418":"Adolf Ulrik Wertmüller was born in Stockholm in 1751 and went to Paris in 1772, where he studied with his cousin Alexander Roslin and with Joseph-Marie Vien. Elected to the Royal Academy in 1784, he painted a portrait of Marie Antoinette for Gustav III of Sweden, and in 1787 produced his best-known picture, Danaë Receiving Jupiter in a Shower of Gold, a nude that later caused a stir when shown in Philadelphia. He emigrated to the United States in 1794 and painted George Washington from life in 1795, the portrait now in the Metropolitan Museum. After a period back in Sweden he returned to Philadelphia in 1800, married in 1801, and retired to a farm on the Delaware, where he died in 1811. He was an elegant portraitist in the French manner.","q1152965":"Adolphe Monticelli was born in Marseille in 1824 and studied there before moving to Paris, where he attended the École des Beaux-Arts under Paul Delaroche, copied Watteau and other old masters in the Louvre, and admired Delacroix. In 1855 he met Narcisse Diaz, and the two often painted together in the forest of Fontainebleau. He made festive scenes of elegantly costumed figures in dreamlike landscapes, and bouquets of flowers, putting on paint in thick, jewelled layers that glitter like enamel. After 1870 he returned to Marseille, where he lived in poverty, selling for small sums, and died in 1886. Cézanne befriended him and painted landscapes with him around Aix. Vincent van Gogh admired his colour and felt he was continuing Monticelli's work, and collectors in Scotland and America later paid high prices.","q364139":"Adriaen Brouwer, born at Oudenaarde in 1605 or 1606, was one of the great painters of low life. He worked in Haarlem, Amsterdam and, from 1631 or 1632, Antwerp, where he joined the guild, and he died there in 1638 when he was about thirty-two. His small panels show peasants and soldiers smoking, drinking, brawling and sleeping in taverns, and he specialised in studies of faces twisted by drink, pain or disgust, painted in quick, thin strokes with subtle colour. The Youth Making a Face, of about 1632 to 1635, is a head of this kind. Rubens and Rembrandt collected his work. His low-life scenes were imitated by Adriaen van Ostade and David Teniers the Younger, and he is now regarded as one of the most gifted Flemish painters of his generation.","adriaen_coorte":"Adriaen Coorte was a still-life painter who worked in Middelburg in Zeeland. He is known only from his pictures, signed and dated between 1683 and 1707, and from a few records, among them a fine imposed in 1695 for selling a painting before joining the local Guild of St Luke. He is believed to have studied around 1680 with the bird painter Melchior d'Hondecoeter in Amsterdam, and his birth and death dates are unknown. Instead of the lavish bouquets and banquets of his time, Coorte painted small pictures of a few objects, such as gooseberries, strawberries, asparagus or shells, set on a stone ledge against a dark ground and lit with quiet precision. His work attracted little notice for centuries and is now much admired. Gooseberries on a Table is dated 1701.","q864279":"Adriaen Hanneman was born in The Hague in 1603 and trained there before travelling to England about 1623, where he stayed for some fifteen years and came under the spell of Anthony van Dyck, whose elegant, courtly portraits shaped his style for life. Returning to The Hague in 1638, he became the leading portraitist at the court of the Princes of Orange and of the exiled Stuart family. The Washington portrait of Henry, Duke of Gloucester, about 1653, shows the youngest son of Charles I, who lived in exile in the Netherlands, in the silk, lace and long ringlets of a young prince. Hanneman remained in The Hague until his death in 1671. His polished, flattering likenesses were much in demand among the court circle, and his work is a link between English and Dutch portraiture.","q367858":"Adriaen Isenbrant was a painter of Bruges, where he became a master in the guild in 1510; he died in 1551. He is said to have worked in the style of Gerard David, the leading painter of Bruges, and after David's death in 1523 he became one of the most successful painters in the city, producing devotional panels with Madonnas, the Nativity, the Adoration of the Shepherds and the Magi, and portraits. His pictures are gentle in feeling and light in colour, with landscape backgrounds that show a debt to Joachim Patinir and the Antwerp painters, and they were sold abroad as well as in Bruges. The Washington Adoration of the Shepherds, probably of 1520 to 1540, is a typical product of his busy workshop.","adriaen_van_de_velde":"Adriaen van de Velde was born in Amsterdam in 1636, the son of the marine painter Willem van de Velde the Elder and younger brother of Willem the Younger. He studied in Haarlem with the landscape painter Jan Wijnants. He painted sunny pastoral landscapes with cattle, sheep, shepherds and travellers, beach scenes at Scheveningen, and some biblical subjects, in a gentle silvery light, and he also made etchings. His skill in drawing animals and small figures was so valued that other landscape painters, among them Jacob van Ruisdael, Meindert Hobbema and Jan van der Heyden, asked him to supply the figures in their pictures. He lived in Amsterdam and died there in January 1672, aged thirty-five. Sleeping Shepherds, dated 1663, shows his clear, quiet manner at its best.","q970806":"Adrien Dauzats was born in Bordeaux in 1804, the son of a theatre scene painter, and studied at the local drawing school under Lacour before moving to Paris. About 1829 he went with Baron Taylor, a dramatist and antiquarian, to Egypt, Syria, Sinai and Palestine to make drawings for Taylor's illustrated travel books; later he travelled with him in Spain, and in 1839 he joined a French military and diplomatic expedition to Algeria. He painted architecture, church interiors and Oriental subjects, among them the Great Pyramid and a Cairo bazaar, and he was among the first artists to show the East with scrupulous exactitude. With Alexandre Dumas he made a book about a fortnight at Sinai. He died in Paris in 1868.","q380743":"Aelbrecht Bouts, the younger son of the Leuven master Dirk Bouts, was born in the early 1450s and died in Leuven in 1549. When his elder brother Dirk inherited their father's workshop in 1475, Aelbrecht set up on his own in the same city. While his brother kept to their father's style, Aelbrecht developed a manner of his own, with strong colours, rich surfaces and fine detail. His workshop turned out devotional pictures for private buyers, including pairs of panels showing Christ crowned with thorns and the sorrowing Virgin, half-length Madonnas, and heads of saints, many produced in several versions. Examples are now in museums from Brussels and Cambridge to Cleveland. The Head of Saint John the Baptist on a Charger in the Metropolitan Museum dates from about 1500.","q313194":"Aelbert Cuyp was born at Dordrecht in 1620, the son of the painter Jacob Gerritsz. Cuyp, and spent his whole life in that river port. He learned from his father and was influenced by the Dutch Italianate landscapists, whose golden light he made his own, although he is not known to have travelled to Italy. He painted the river Maas at Dordrecht, with ships and distant towers, and herdsmen, cattle and riders in sunlit meadows, as in Horsemen and Herdsmen with Cattle and The Maas at Dordrecht. In 1658 he married a wealthy widow and became a deacon of the Reformed church, and he painted little afterwards. He died in 1691. Cuyp was only modestly prized in Holland, but his pictures were treasured in eighteenth-century England, and many hung in English country houses before reaching American museums.","q553273":"Willem van Aelst was born in Delft in 1627 into a family of magistrates and learned painting from his uncle, the still-life painter Evert van Aelst. He became a master of the Delft guild in 1643, lived in France from 1645 to 1649, and then went to Florence as court painter to Grand Duke Ferdinando II de' Medici, who presented him with a gold chain and medal. In Italy he was known as Guillielmo d'Olanda. Returning to the Netherlands in the 1650s, he settled in Amsterdam and became one of its leading still-life painters, with polished, cool-toned compositions of flowers, fruit and hunting trophies with dead game, which suited wealthy patrons. The painter Rachel Ruysch was among his pupils. He died in 1683, and the still life with dead game, dated 1661, shows his refined manner.","q381801":"Aert van der Neer was born at Gorinchem about 1603 and, according to Houbraken, worked as a steward to the lords of Arkel before turning to painting, probably after meeting the Amsterdam painters Rafael and Joachim Camphuysen, whose sister Lysbeth he married in 1629. He lived in Amsterdam for the rest of his life and specialised in small landscapes of two kinds: moonlit scenes along canals and rivers, and frozen winter views full of skaters and players of kolf, painted with silvery, transparent skies and glowing reflections. His pictures sold poorly, so in 1659 he opened a wine tavern to add to his income; two years later he went bankrupt, and he died in poverty in 1677. His son Eglon became a portrait painter. Collectors now prize his night scenes, but he was little valued in his own time.","agnolo_bronzino":"Agnolo Bronzino, born in Florence in 1503, was the pupil and close companion of Jacopo Pontormo, whose restless emotional manner he gradually replaced with a cool, polished elegance. From 1539 he was court painter to Cosimo I de' Medici, painting the Duke, his wife Eleonora of Toledo and their children in portraits of icy composure, with fabrics rendered in minute detail, so that the sitters seem armoured in silk. He also made altarpieces, tapestry designs and frescoes in the Palazzo Vecchio, and he wrote poetry. He remained in Florence all his life and died in 1572. His portraits set the standard for court portraiture across Europe for decades. The Cleveland Portrait of a Woman, of about 1550, is typical of this controlled, aristocratic style, in which feeling is held strictly in check.","q363296":"Agnolo Gaddi was born in Florence about 1350, the son of Taddeo Gaddi, the pupil of Giotto, and carried on the family workshop with his brother Giovanni. He painted in Florence and Prato, and his masterpiece is the fresco cycle of the Legend of the True Cross in the choir of Santa Croce in Florence, painted in the late 1380s and 1390s, a great narrative of the finding of the cross, in a clear, graceful, rather decorative manner that follows Giotto's tradition while adding fashionable colour and costume. He also painted the frescoes of the Chapel of the Holy Girdle in Prato Cathedral. His pupil Cennino Cennini set the Giottesque method down in his Book of the Art. Gaddi died in Florence in 1396. The National Gallery of Art owns a triptych of about 1387 and a Coronation of the Virgin.","agust_n_esteve_y_marques":"Agustín Esteve y Marqués was born in Valencia in 1753, probably the son of a sculptor of the same name, and studied at the Academy of San Carlos there before moving to Madrid, where he won a drawing prize at the Academy of San Fernando in 1772. He worked as a portraitist in the circle of Francisco Goya, whose style strongly influenced him, serving as an assistant and making many copies of Goya's portraits. In 1800 the king named him Painter to the Court, though at a salary far below Goya's. Among his better portraits is that of the Countess of San Andrés, painted around 1800. The Portrait of Juan María Osorio, of about 1786, dates from the years when he was establishing himself among Madrid's aristocratic sitters. He is remembered mainly as Goya's follower, though his own portraits have charm.","q77132":"Albert Bierstadt was born in Solingen in 1830 and raised in New Bedford, Massachusetts. In the 1850s he studied in Düsseldorf, where he also met American friends, and travelled in Switzerland and Italy; Lake Lucerne, of 1858, reflects this period. After joining Frederick Lander's expedition in 1859, he painted the Rocky Mountains on a large scale, with theatrical light and mist, and The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak made him famous in 1863. He visited California in the 1860s and went on to paint the Sierra Nevada, as in Mount Corcoran, of about 1876 to 1877, and the Plains, where Buffalo Trail: The Impending Storm, of 1869, shows a herd threatened by a storm. His late picture The Last of the Buffalo, of 1888 to 1889, laments the passing of the herds. He died in 1902.","q948598":"Albert Pinkham Ryder was born in New Bedford, Massachusetts, in 1847, and moved with his family to New York about 1867. Turned down at first by the National Academy of Design, he studied informally and later took part in the Society of American Artists. He made small, moody pictures of moonlit seas, lonely boats, and scenes from poetry and opera, working slowly, building up thick layers of paint, oil and varnish and scraping them back until the forms were reduced to a few glowing shapes. His methods were unsound, and many of his works have darkened and cracked. Ryder lived for decades in cluttered rooms in Manhattan and was known as a hermit, though he had many friends among artists. Admired by younger painters and shown at the Armory Show of 1913, he died in 1917.","q163420":"Albert van Ouwater was one of the earliest painters of the northern Netherlands, active in Haarlem in the middle of the fifteenth century; he is thought to have been born around 1410 to 1415 and to have died in 1475. Almost nothing about his life is documented. His reputation rests on a passage in Karel van Mander's Schilder-boeck of 1604, which names him as the teacher of Geertgen tot Sint Jans, and on a single large panel attributed to him, the Raising of Lazarus, now in Berlin. That picture shows a church interior with a convincing perspective and brings the Flemish manner of Jan van Eyck to Holland. A small Head of a Donor in the Metropolitan Museum, dated about 1465 to 1470, is also given to him, though so little survives that his art is hard to judge.","q20926463":"Albertis del Orient Browere was born in Tarrytown, New York, in 1814, the son of the sculptor John Henri Isaac Browere, famous for his life masks of Jefferson and other Founding Fathers. In the mid-1830s he settled in Catskill, a centre of Hudson River painting, and exhibited at the National Academy of Design and the American Art-Union. He painted Catskill landscapes, still lifes and, above all, genre scenes of country life, among them his best-known work, Mrs. Cormick's General Store, of 1844. His Rip Van Winkle, painted in 1833, pictures the Catskill legend in Washington Irving's story. The Gold Rush drew him to California in the early 1850s, but he returned to Catskill and went on painting there until his death in 1887.","q2831817":"Alberto Pasini was born at Busseto in the duchy of Parma in 1826 and studied at the Parma academy. Encouraged by Paolo Toschi, he went to Paris in the early 1850s, worked with the Cicéri family of scene painters and lithographers, and had a lithograph accepted at the Salon of 1853. In 1855 he took the place of Théodore Chassériau in the embassy of the French minister Prosper Bourée to Persia, a journey that gave him the subject of his life: the architecture, bazaars and caravans of the East. He later travelled to Egypt, Arabia and Istanbul, and in 1876 painted four pictures for the Ottoman sultan Abdülaziz. Settled near Turin after 1870, he sold his detailed, sunlit views to collectors in Europe and America. He died at Cavoretto in 1899.","q5580":"Albrecht Dürer was born in 1471 at Nuremberg, the son of a goldsmith, and trained first as a goldsmith before being apprenticed to the painter Michael Wolgemut. After his journeyman years along the Rhine, he married Agnes Frey in 1494 and visited Venice in 1494 to 1495 and again in 1505 to 1507, absorbing Italian ideas of proportion and the nude. His woodcut series of the Apocalypse (1498) made him famous across Europe, and his engravings, such as Melencolia I, set standards of precision. He painted for Frederick the Wise, elector of Saxony, and became court artist to Emperor Maximilian I. In 1520 to 1521 he journeyed to the Netherlands and kept a diary. He sympathised with the Reformation, wrote on perspective and human proportion, and died in Nuremberg in 1528.","q516941":"Alessandro Magnasco was born in Genoa in 1667 and trained in Milan, where he settled and worked until 1735, spending several years in Florence in the service of Gian Gastone de' Medici. He painted dark, restless pictures of monks, gypsies, beggars, soldiers and scenes of torture, with long, jittery figures and quick, flickering brush strokes that anticipate later Romantic painters. Landscape and architecture were sometimes supplied by specialist collaborators. The Choristers, Christ at the Sea of Galilee and The Baptism of Christ, all about 1740, show his strange, nervous religious manner. He returned to Genoa in 1735 and died there in 1749, and his work was rediscovered by twentieth-century critics.","q507279":"Alessandro Turchi was born in Verona in 1578 and trained there under Felice Brusasorci, whose unfinished canvases he completed with Pasquale Ottino after Brusasorci's death in 1605. He worked for a time in Venice, then by 1616 was in Rome, where he painted for Cardinal Scipione Borghese, took part in decorating the Quirinal Palace, and competed with Andrea Sacchi and Pietro da Cortona for altarpieces. He stayed there for the rest of his life and died in 1649. Known as L'Orbetto, a nickname said to come from a boyhood spent leading a blind man, he painted soft, glowing, slightly Caravaggesque religious and mythological pictures, many of them small cabinet pieces on black marble. The Judgment of Paris in the Metropolitan Museum dates from about 1640.","q5479973":"Francis Alexander was born in Windham County, Connecticut, in 1800 and grew up on a farm, teaching himself to paint. In 1820 he went to New York, where he worked with Alexander Robertson, and he spent the winters of 1831 and 1832 in Rome. By the 1830s he was settled in Boston, then the busiest portrait market in America, where he painted such sitters as Mrs. Fletcher Webster, daughter-in-law of Daniel Webster, and in 1842 Charles Dickens. His clean, direct likenesses, among them Aaron Baldwin and Sarah Blake Sturgis, suited New England patrons. In the 1850s he moved to Florence, where he died in 1880. His daughter Francesca Alexander became known there for her illustrations and her collection of Tuscan folk songs, which won the admiration of John Ruskin.","q2833132":"Alexander Helwig Wyant was born in Port Washington, Ohio, in 1836 and worked as a sign painter before seeing paintings by George Inness at an exhibition in Cincinnati in 1857. He went to New York to meet Inness, won the backing of the Cincinnati patron Nicholas Longworth, and studied abroad in 1860, seeing the work of Corot and Dupré, and in 1865 with the Norwegian landscape painter Hans Gude in Karlsruhe. Back in New York he was elected to the National Academy of Design in 1869 and helped to found the American Watercolor Society in 1878. His early work followed the Hudson River School, but he moved toward the dim, hushed landscapes of Tonalism. After a stroke in 1873 paralysed his right arm, he learned to paint with his left. He died in 1892.","q310437":"Alexandre Cabanel was born in Montpellier in 1823, the son of a carpenter, and went to Paris as a teenager to study with François-Édouard Picot at the École des Beaux-Arts. He won the Prix de Rome in 1845 and spent five years at the Villa Medici. Smooth, polished and gracefully idealised, his mythological and religious pictures suited the taste of the Second Empire: his Birth of Venus, shown at the Salon of 1863, was bought by Napoleon III, and he became, with Gérôme and Meissonier, one of the three most successful artists of the age. He taught for decades at the École des Beaux-Arts and was a sought-after portraitist, painting the New York collector Catharine Lorillard Wolfe in 1876. The Met's Birth of Venus is a later version of 1875. His reputation fell with the rise of modern art.","q704938":"Alexandre-Évariste Fragonard was born in Grasse in 1780, the son of Jean-Honoré Fragonard, and died in Paris in 1850. He learned the lively touch and light subjects of his father, then trained further under Jacques-Louis David, whose discipline tempered his charm. He painted and sculpted in the troubadour style, a fashion of the early nineteenth century for small, polished scenes taken from medieval and Renaissance history, with careful period costume and a taste for the picturesque and the sentimental. Dramatic Scene with Monks in a Crypt is a theatrical subject of this kind. He was a regular exhibitor at the Paris Salon and also made drawings and sculpture. His reputation has always been overshadowed by that of his famous father, though the troubadour taste he served has found new interest among historians of French Romanticism.","q1359864":"Alexandre-François Desportes was born in 1661 at Champigneulles in Champagne and trained in Paris with Nicasius Bernaerts, a Flemish painter of animals and hunting scenes. In 1695 he went to Poland, where he painted hunts for King John III Sobieski, and on his return he became Louis XIV's painter of the royal hunt and kennel, portraying the king's hounds and the game they killed. His oil sketches from nature of plants, animals and landscape, made on paper, are among the most freshly observed of their time. He also designed tapestries for the Gobelins, and painted still lifes with game, fruit and silver, such as the one of 1734 in the National Gallery, in a Rococo fashion. He died in Paris in 1743.","q458288":"Alexandre-Gabriel Decamps was born in Paris in 1803. After training in Paris and travelling in his youth in Greece, Asia Minor and Turkey, he made his reputation at the Salon of 1831 with scenes of everyday life in the East, painted with thick, strongly lit, richly worked paint that puzzled conventional critics but impressed Delacroix and Théophile Gautier. He soon ranked with Delacroix and Ingres among the leaders of French painting and was called the founding father of French Orientalism. He also painted biblical and historical subjects, hunting scenes, dogs, and comic pictures of monkeys, including The Experts, in which apes judge paintings like connoisseurs. He received a council medal at the Exposition of 1855. He died in 1860 after being thrown from his horse while hunting at Fontainebleau.","q2833377":"Alexandre-Louis Leloir was born in Paris in 1843 into a family of artists: his father Auguste was a painter, his mother a fashion illustrator, his grandfather Alexandre Colin a pupil of Girodet, and his younger brother Maurice also became a painter and illustrator. He entered the École des Beaux-Arts in 1860 and competed repeatedly for the Prix de Rome, twice winning second prize. At the Salon from 1863 he first showed biblical and classical subjects, but from 1868 he turned to genre scenes drawn from medieval life, seventeenth-century interiors in the Dutch manner, and the Orient. He illustrated editions of Molière and other authors, and Choosing the Dinner, of 1872, is typical of his lively period interiors. He took a medal at the Exposition of 1878 and died in 1884, aged forty.","q353008":"Alexei Savrasov was born in Moscow in 1830 into a merchant family and studied at the Moscow School of Painting, Sculpture and Architecture under Karl Rabus, graduating in 1850 and specialising in landscape. From 1857 he taught at the school, where his pupils included Isaac Levitan and Konstantin Korovin, who remembered him with gratitude. One of the founders of the Wanderers, the group of realist painters who from 1870 took exhibitions out of the capitals, he painted quiet, lyrical views of the Russian countryside in place of grand scenery. His best-known picture, The Rooks Have Returned, of 1871, shows a village church in early spring and became a national favourite. Night Scene on the Volga is dated to the same year. Drink and family troubles ended his teaching career, and he died in poverty in 1897.","q21994014":"Alfred Copestick is known from a small group of views of New York harbour signed and dated 1857 and 1858, and from life dates given by the Metropolitan Museum as about 1837 to 1859, which suggest that he died at about twenty-two. Little else is recorded of him. His New York from the Harbor Showing the Battery and Castle Garden, of 1858, looks across the water toward the southern tip of Manhattan, with the Battery park and Castle Garden, the old round fort that served as the city's landing depot for immigrants from 1855. The small canvas measures about fourteen by twenty-one inches. It came to the museum in 1954 with the bequest of Edward W. C. Arnold, who collected prints, maps and pictures of New York.","q355550":"Alfred de Dreux, as he signed himself, was born in Paris in 1810 into a family of architects and artists. The painter Théodore Géricault, a friend of the family, drew him as a child and from 1823 taught him, and he went on to study with Léon Cogniet. Horses were his passion. His first Salon success came in 1831, and from 1840 he painted portraits of horses in the stables of the Duke of Orléans, a series that made him the favourite horse painter of the French court. After the revolution of 1848 he followed the royal family to England, where he found many aristocratic patrons, and later he painted Napoleon III and his family. He died in Paris in 1860 of a liver abscess. Seated Arab Man with Horse is possibly a work of the 1850s.","q21503300":"Alfred Q. Collins was an American portrait painter whose dates are given as 1855 to 1903. Little is recorded of his life beyond these dates and a few pictures. The Metropolitan Museum holds his portrait of Alexander Stewart Wetherill, painted between about 1890 and 1900. His dates place him among the generation of American painters who came of age after the Civil War, many of whom finished their training in Paris, and portraiture was then one of the steadier ways of earning a living. Portraits like this one were hung in homes, offices and clubs as records of a person's standing, and many are now known chiefly by the sitter's name. Collins died in 1903, at about forty-eight, so his working life was short.","q22669580":"Alfred S. Waugh was an American portrait painter whose dates are given by the Metropolitan Museum as about 1810 to 1856. Little is recorded of his life. He is represented there by a single Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1841, a likeness of a man whose name has not survived. Pictures of this kind, bust or half-length portraits of ordinary citizens, were the staple of American portrait painting before photography became cheap in the 1850s, and many were painted quickly and for modest sums. Such works are now valued as a record of ordinary American faces in the early nineteenth century. Without a documented career, it is not possible to say where Waugh trained or worked, and his name is known today mainly from a handful of dated portraits.","q175130":"Alfred Sisley was born in Paris in 1839 to wealthy British parents and was sent to London at eighteen to prepare for the family import business, though he showed no aptitude for it. Back in Paris from 1861 he studied in Charles Gleyre's studio, where he befriended Monet, Renoir and Bazille. He painted almost nothing but landscape, working outdoors along the Seine at Louveciennes, Marly-le-Roi and, later, Moret-sur-Loing, and in 1874 he painted twenty views of the Thames near Hampton Court. His father's trading business was ruined by the Franco-Prussian War, and Sisley's money problems never ended; he depended on dealers and patrons. He never became a French citizen, and he died at Moret in 1899, just before the market for his pictures began to rise.","q773855":"Alfred Stevens was born in Brussels in 1823 into a family of artists and art lovers: his brother Joseph painted animals, his brother Arthur became a dealer and critic, and his father collected Delacroix. He studied at the Brussels academy and from 1843 in Paris, at the École des Beaux-Arts, and first drew attention with a social picture of poor vagrants. He then found the subject that made his name: elegantly dressed modern women in fashionable Parisian interiors, painted with a Dutch seventeenth-century finish and a sharp eye for silk and lace. Manet was a friend. His Japanese Robe of about 1872 reflects the Paris craze for things Japanese. He won medals at the Paris exhibitions and sold well in Europe and America. He died in Paris in 1906.","q3373427":"Alfred Thomas Agate was born in 1812 and lived in New York from 1831, where he studied with his older brother, the portrait and history painter Frederick Styles Agate, and with Thomas Seir Cummings, and exhibited at the National Academy of Design. In 1838 he sailed as portrait and botanical artist with the United States Exploring Expedition under Charles Wilkes, a four-year voyage through the Pacific, Antarctic waters and the Pacific coast of North America. He drew landscapes, plants and people, among them the tattoos and dress of Pacific islanders and the first known picture of Mount Shasta, and he supplied more than half of the 342 pictures reproduced as lithographs in the expedition's reports. His health was broken by the voyage, and he died of consumption in Washington in 1846, aged thirty-three.","q2646046":"Alfred Thompson Bricher was born in Portsmouth, New Hampshire, in 1837 and was educated at an academy in Newburyport, Massachusetts. After a start as a Boston businessman, he took up painting full-time in 1858, studying nature on his own and, according to some accounts, with Albert Bierstadt and William Morris Hunt. He moved to New York in 1868, joined the American Watercolor Society in 1873 and was elected to the National Academy of Design in 1879. He is known for calm marine and coastal views, in the clear, still light of luminism, many painted at Grand Manan Island, where he spent his summers. A Quiet Day near Manchester dates from 1873, when he was painting mainly maritime subjects. His reputation sank as tastes changed, but his pictures have been admired again in recent decades. He died in 1908.","q2644198":"Alfred Wahlberg was born in Stockholm in 1834. His father was a painter, but he first trained as a musician at the Royal Swedish Academy of Music before turning to landscape and selling a picture to the Swedish art society in 1856. He studied in Düsseldorf from 1857, briefly under the Norwegian landscape painter Hans Gude, and after returning to Stockholm in 1862 he made his name with his Swedish lake landscape from Kolmården. He moved to Paris in 1866, where he left the Düsseldorf manner for the freer, more atmospheric handling of French landscape, and won Salon medals in 1870 and 1872 and a first-class medal at the Paris World's Fair of 1878. He is credited with bringing modern French landscape methods to Sweden. A Day in October, near Waxholm is dated 1873. He died in 1906.","q21994015":"Alfred Wordsworth Thompson was born in Baltimore in 1840 and was meant for the law, but he opened an art studio instead and drew illustrations for Harper's Weekly, including a sketch of John Brown in prison. After a short spell as a war correspondent he went to Paris, staying until 1868, and studied at the École des Beaux-Arts under Charles Gleyre, Alberto Pasini and Émile Lambinet, with a course in horse anatomy from the sculptor Barye. In New York he became a National Academy member in 1875 and sold more than a hundred paintings there, about forty of them scenes from the Revolutionary War. Old Bruton Church, Williamsburg, of 1893, is a colonial subject of the kind he painted in his last years. He died at Summit, New Jersey, in 1896.","q21487059":"Luther Allen was an American portrait painter who lived from 1780 to 1821. Little is recorded of his life beyond these dates and a few pictures. The National Gallery of Art has his portrait of Lucia Leonard, painted in 1801, when he was about twenty-one. In those years the United States was full of painters with little academic training, who learned their craft as sign, coach or house painters and travelled to find sitters. Likenesses of this kind were usually made quickly, for modest fees, and kept by the family as a record of a daughter, a wife or a parent. Allen died young, at about forty-one, and the portrait of Lucia Leonard remains the best evidence of what he could do. Nothing more can safely be said of his training or career.","q15429866":"Allen Butler Talcott was born in Hartford, Connecticut, in 1867 and studied there under Dwight Tryon, then at the Art Students League in New York and for three years at the Académie Julian in Paris, under Jean-Paul Laurens and Benjamin-Constant. He showed at the Paris Salons of 1893 and 1894. A landscape painter, he worked outdoors on small wooden panels in a Barbizon-influenced, Tonalist manner that later took in some Impressionist touches, and he became known for his trees. From 1901 until his death he spent summers at the Old Lyme art colony in Connecticut, a gathering of painters at Florence Griswold's boarding house, and wintered in New York. He won a silver medal at the St. Louis Exposition in 1904 and died at Old Lyme in 1908, aged forty-one. His Return of the Redwing is dated about 1900.","allen_smith_jr":"Allen Smith Jr. was born in Rhode Island in 1810 and grew up in Massachusetts. He studied in New York with the miniaturist William Daniel Parisen and in the antique classes of the American Academy of the Fine Arts and the National Academy of Design, where he exhibited from 1832 and was an associate member until 1860. After spells in Detroit and Cincinnati he settled in Cleveland about 1842 and became a popular portrait painter. With eight children to support, he also coloured and retouched photographs for local studios, and after the Panic of 1857 he took a permanent job producing oil-painted photographs. He retired to Lake County, Ohio, in 1882 and devoted himself to landscapes of the Big Creek valley, as in Landscape near Painesville. He died in Cleveland around 1890.","q468272":"Washington Allston was born in 1779 on a rice plantation in South Carolina and graduated from Harvard in 1800. In 1801 he sailed to London and entered the Royal Academy Schools, then under the presidency of Benjamin West, and from 1803 he studied in Paris and Italy, where he met Washington Irving and Samuel Taylor Coleridge, who became a lifelong friend. After a second stay in England he settled in Boston in 1818 and later in Cambridgeport, Massachusetts. A poet as well as a painter, he pioneered American Romantic landscape, with dreamy moonlit and sunset scenes, rich Venetian colour that earned him the title of the American Titian, and visionary biblical subjects. The huge Belshazzar's Feast occupied him for decades and was unfinished when he died in 1843. Samuel F. B. Morse was among his pupils.","q240526":"Lawrence Alma-Tadema was born at Dronrijp in Friesland in 1836 and trained at the Antwerp academy and with the Belgian history painter Baron Henri Leys. He first made his name with scenes of ancient Egypt and Merovingian history, then found his lifelong subject in daily life in ancient Rome and Greece, painted with archaeological detail, gleaming marble and brilliant light. The dealer Ernest Gambart bought his work and brought him to the English market, and in 1870 he moved to London, becoming a British subject in 1873. He was knighted in 1899 and received the Order of Merit in 1905, and his studio in St John's Wood was famous for its decoration. After his death in 1912 his reputation fell with the rise of modernism, and it has been rebuilt since the 1960s. A Difficult Line from Horace is dated 1881.","q2048640":"Alonzo Chappel was born in New York City in 1828, of French Huguenot descent, and worked there as a historical painter and illustrator, dying at Middle Island, Long Island, in 1887. He is remembered for numerous small paintings of Revolutionary War figures and episodes of the early republic, many made as designs for engravers working for publishers who wanted portraits and scenes for popular histories of the United States. Several appear as plates in J. A. Spencer's History of the United States of 1858. Larger oils include Enlisting Foreign Officers, of 1857, now in the Museum of the American Revolution. The Metropolitan Museum's George Washington: Design for an Engraving, of about 1860, is one of these working pictures for the printmaker. Reproduced as prints, his images reached a wide public.","q1494309":"Alphonse de Neuville was born at Saint-Omer in 1835. He took a degree in letters and entered the naval school at Lorient before turning to art, working briefly in Picot's studio and receiving advice from Delacroix. From the early 1860s he sent pictures to the Salon and became the leading French painter of military subjects, recording the Crimean War, the Italian campaign and the Mexican expedition. After the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 to 1871 he reached the peak of his career with intensely patriotic pictures such as The Last Cartridges and Le Bourget, which spoke to French feelings about defeat and made him very popular. He also illustrated books, among them Jules Verne's Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas. The Dispatch-Bearer, of 1880, shows his sympathy for the lone soldier. He died in Paris in 1885.","q448267":"Alvan Clark was born in 1804 at Ashfield, Massachusetts, and began as a portrait painter and miniaturist, working in Boston and Cambridgeport and also engraving. Portraits such as Lovice Corbett Whittemore, of 1845, show a competent, straightforward likeness. His real fame came from a second career: in the 1840s he took up telescope making, and with his sons he founded Alvan Clark and Sons, which made some of the finest refractor lenses in the world. The firm made the 26-inch lens of the Naval Observatory in Washington, through which Asaph Hall found the moons of Mars in 1877, and, after Clark's death, the 36-inch Lick and 40-inch Yerkes lenses. Clark discovered double stars himself. He died in 1887, remembered chiefly as an optician and not as a painter.","alvan_fisher":"Alvan Fisher was born in Needham, Massachusetts, in 1792 and grew up in Dedham, where he worked as a clerk in his brother's store. At eighteen he decided to paint and was apprenticed to John Ritto Penniman in Boston, learning portraiture while helping to decorate carriages and paint signs. In 1815 he opened his own studio in Boston and travelled widely, painting landscapes, rural scenes, animals and portraits. He is often described as the first American painter to specialise in genre subjects, scenes of everyday country life, for a wide audience. A Calm Watering Place, with cattle, dates from 1816, the second year of his professional career. He always called Dedham home, and he died there in 1863.","q10395841":"Álvaro Pires de Évora, called Alvaro di Piero in Italy, was a Portuguese painter, presumed to have been born at Évora, who worked in Tuscany in the early fifteenth century. He is documented from before 1411 until after 1434. Vasari mentions him in his life of Taddeo Bartoli, saying that he lived at the same time and painted in nearly the same manner, with brighter colour and shorter figures, and that he made many panels at Volterra, one in Sant'Antonio at Pisa, and others elsewhere. The religious works that survive date from about 1411 to 1450 and are in a late Gothic style close to that of the Sienese Taddeo Bartoli. The Presentation in the Temple, in the Metropolitan Museum, is dated about 1405 to 1415. He is a rare early Portuguese painter whose name survives.","q1525705":"Giovanni Ambrogio Bevilacqua, called il Liberale, was a Milanese painter, apparently the son of a carpenter named Pietro, and a pupil of Vincenzo Foppa. He is recorded from 1481 to at least 1512 and worked in Lombardy in a style that still belonged to the late Gothic and early Renaissance. In 1485 he signed a fresco of Saints Roch, Sebastian and Christopher, and he perhaps also completed a Madonna and Saints with Donors for the parish church of Landriano. His surviving works are mostly devotional: Madonnas, saints and donors for churches and private patrons around Milan and Pavia, now in the Brera and the Sforza Castle in Milan, the Accademia Carrara in Bergamo, and in New York, where the Metropolitan Museum owns a panel of God the Father. His name is little known outside specialist circles.","q211562":"Ambrogio Lorenzetti was a Sienese painter, the younger brother of Pietro Lorenzetti, documented between 1319 and 1348 and probably killed, like Pietro, by the Black Death in 1348. He worked in Siena and Florence and brought to Sienese painting a new interest in space, solid figures and the observation of everyday things. His most famous work is the fresco cycle of Good and Bad Government, painted in 1338 and 1339 for the Sala dei Nove in the Palazzo Pubblico in Siena, which shows the city and its countryside in remarkable detail, with working people at peace under just rule. He also painted altarpieces, among them the Maestà at Massa Marittima. The Metropolitan Museum's Madonna and Child is a devotional panel in his tender, graceful manner.","q21994022":"Ambrose Andrews was an American portrait painter who lived from 1801 to 1877. Little of his life is securely recorded, and he is known mainly from a handful of signed or documented pictures. The best known of these, The Children of Nathan Starr, painted in 1835 and now in the Metropolitan Museum, is a group portrait, a kind of picture commissioned by families who wanted a lasting record of their household. Such portraits were made before photography could do the job, and they are valued today for what they show of dress, furnishings and family life in America in the 1830s. Andrews belongs to the large number of portraitists of the early republic whose careers have to be pieced together from a few surviving canvases.","q459626":"Ambrosius Benson was born in Lombardy, in Ferrara or Milan, about 1495 to 1500, and moved to Bruges around 1515, where he apprenticed with Gerard David and became a naturalised citizen. He was made a master of the painters' guild in 1519 and later held office in it. Though Italian by birth, scholars regard him as a painter of the Flemish school. His large workshop produced religious pictures, portraits and several popular half-length types, including the Magdalen and the Sibyl, which were copied and sold abroad; he was especially popular in Spain. He tended not to sign his pictures, and little is known of him from the records. He died in Bruges in 1550. The Lamentation in the Metropolitan Museum dates from the 1520s. In the nineteenth century his pictures were widely imitated.","ambrosius_bosschaert":"Ambrosius Bosschaert was born in Antwerp in 1573 and moved with his family to Middelburg in Zeeland in 1587, because of the threat of religious persecution. He joined the Middelburg painters' guild in his early twenties, later became its dean, and also worked as an art dealer. He is recognised as one of the first painters to treat flowers as an independent subject, making small, exact, symmetrical bouquets, normally on copper, with tulips and roses, shells and insects. He later worked in Amsterdam, Bergen op Zoom, Utrecht and Breda, and died in The Hague in 1621 while at work on a commission. His three sons, Ambrosius, Johannes and Abraham, and his brother-in-law and assistant Balthasar van der Ast carried his manner on, making Middelburg the chief centre of flower painting in the Dutch Republic. Flowers in a Glass is dated 1606.","ambrosius_bosschaert_the_elder":"Ambrosius Bosschaert the Elder, so called to distinguish him from his son Ambrosius the Younger, is regarded as one of the founders of Dutch flower painting. Born in Antwerp in 1573, he spent most of his career in Middelburg, where his family had settled in 1587. His bouquets are symmetrical, bright and exactly painted, with tulips, roses and irises set in a glass or vase on a stone ledge, often with a shell, a butterfly or a drop of dew for extra detail. The flowers in them bloomed in different seasons, so the pictures cannot have been painted from a single bouquet. Bouquet of Flowers in a Roemer, of about 1620, uses the roemer, a type of wine glass, as the vase. He died in 1621, and his sons and his brother-in-law Balthasar van der Ast continued his workshop.","american_18th_century":"American 18th Century is not one painter but a heading used for pictures whose makers are unknown. The National Gallery's list includes portraits such as Elisha Doane and Jane Cutler Doane, of about 1783, and The Cheney Family, of about 1795; a view of Mount Vernon; a battle scene, the Attack on Bunker's Hill, with the Burning of Charles Town; and religious and landscape pictures from earlier in the century, among them Christ and the Woman of Samaria. Pictures labelled this way were mostly made by self-taught or itinerant portraitists and decorative painters, who often borrowed poses and subjects from English prints. Many of the gallery's anonymous American pictures were given by Edgar William and Bernice Chrysler Garbisch. They are valued as records of colonial and early republican taste.","american_19th_century":"American 19th Century is a heading used for pictures whose makers are unknown, not the name of one painter. The National Gallery's group covers the whole century and shows how varied unsigned American painting was: plain portraits of unnamed men and women, an early profile likeness, a mother and child of about 1810, and a portrait of the painter Charles Loring Elliott, of about 1865. Others show well-known figures, such as the English-born actor Junius Brutus Booth, of about 1830, and Abraham Lincoln, dated about 1864 or after. Most were made by itinerant or self-taught portraitists, who worked quickly and cheaply for ordinary families, and by painters who copied from photographs and prints. Such pictures are valued today as records of faces and fashions that formal portraits often left out.","q5423324":"Ezra Ames was born at Framingham, Massachusetts, in 1768, and settled in Albany, New York, about 1793, where he worked for forty years as the city's leading portrait painter. He began as an ornamental painter, making coach panels, signs and decorations, and learned portraiture partly by copying the work of Gilbert Stuart. His likenesses of Albany's merchants, lawyers and public men are plain, firm and honest, with strong modelling and a sober palette, such as Philip Van Cortlandt (about 1810) and John Scoville. He also painted miniatures, and the Metropolitan Museum has an early Portrait of a Gentleman on ivory, of about 1785 to 1790. He died in Albany in 1836. His portraits give a good picture of the professional and mercantile class of a prosperous New York town in the early republic.","q2843640":"Ammi Phillips was born in Colebrook, Connecticut, in 1788 and worked as an itinerant portrait painter from the mid-1810s into the early 1860s, travelling through western Connecticut, Massachusetts and the Hudson valley of New York. About eight hundred pictures are attributed to him, though only eleven are signed. For years his work hung unidentified under the names of the Border Limner and the Kent Limner, until collectors and scholars, with the art historian Mary Black, showed that these were all by Phillips. His style changed with the years, and he tailored each portrait to the sitter with personal details. He is most famous for his portraits of children in red, above all Girl in Red Dress with Cat and Dog, which in 1985 became the first American folk painting to sell for a million dollars. He died in 1865.","q206820":"Anders Zorn was born in 1860 at Mora in Dalarna, Sweden, and raised by his maternal grandparents. After studying at the Royal Swedish Academy in Stockholm he left for Paris, London and Spain, and first made his name in the 1880s as a master of watercolour, handling the medium with a boldness that had rarely been seen. In the 1890s he took up oil painting and etching, painting nudes in sunlight and water and, above all, portraits of the wealthy. He visited the United States many times, painted the presidents Cleveland, Roosevelt and Taft, and found loyal American patrons, among them Isabella Stewart Gardner of Boston. He won a gold medal at the Paris Exposition of 1889 and the Legion of Honour. He settled again at Mora in 1896 and died there in 1920. The Met's portraits of Mrs. Bacon and her husband date from 1897.","andrea_appiani":"Andrea Appiani was born in Milan in 1754. Intended for a career in medicine, he entered instead the private academy of Carlo Maria Giudici, studied fresco with Antonio de' Giorgi and oil painting with Martin Knoller, and in 1776 joined the courses at the Brera academy. His early work was church frescoes, and in the 1780s he worked for a time as a stage designer in Florence. Under French rule he became the leading neoclassical painter in Lombardy, painting frescoes for Napoleon's royal residences in Milan and portraits of Napoleon and his circle, and Napoleon named him his official painter in Italy. A stroke in 1813 paralysed his right hand and ended his career, and he died in 1817. The Cleveland Portrait of a Musician dates from about 1800.","q240737":"Andrea del Castagno was born at Castagno in the Mugello, near Florence, around 1419 and died in Florence in 1457. He was one of the most forceful Florentine painters of the generation after Masaccio, with a hard, sculptural style, strong outline and a keen interest in perspective and anatomy. He worked in Venice in 1442, then in Florence, where his best-known works are the Last Supper in the refectory of Sant'Apollonia, a cycle of famous men and women painted for the Villa Carducci, and the monument on the cathedral wall showing the mercenary captain Niccolò da Tolentino on horseback, finished in 1456. Vasari told a story that Castagno murdered his fellow painter Domenico Veneziano out of jealousy, but documents show Domenico outlived him by several years. The National Gallery's David is painted on a ceremonial shield.","andrea_del_sarto":"Andrea del Sarto, the son of a tailor, was born in Florence in 1486 and died there in 1530. He trained with Piero di Cosimo and worked closely with Franciabigio, producing frescoes for the church of the Santissima Annunziata, among them the Birth of the Virgin of 1514, and for the Chiostro dello Scalzo. His gentle, harmonious figures, warm colour and fine drawing brought him the nickname of the faultless painter. In 1518 he went to France to work for Francis I but returned to Florence within the year. He painted altarpieces, many portraits, and Holy Family pictures such as the Met's, of about 1528, and Vasari, who studied under him, left a warm account of his life. The poet Robert Browning made him the subject of a famous poem in 1855. He died during the plague of 1530.","q390098":"Andrea di Bartolo was a Sienese painter, stained-glass designer and illuminator, the son and pupil of Bartolo di Fredi, one of the most prominent painters in Siena. He is first documented in 1389, working with his father and Luca di Tommè on an altarpiece, now lost, for the shoemakers' guild altar in Siena Cathedral, and about 1390 he set up his own workshop. It was very successful: he received commissions from the Franciscans of Montalcino and the Dominicans of Siena and, like Simone Martini before him, travelled to work in the Marches, the Veneto and Treviso. He painted altarpieces and devotional panels such as the Madonna of Humility in a delicate, conservative Sienese style, and he died in Siena in 1428. The National Gallery of Art has several of his panels, and the Metropolitan Museum a Crucifixion.","q2846442":"Andrea di Lione, also written Andrea di Leone, was born in Naples in 1610 and worked there all his life, dying in 1685. He began by helping Belisario Corenzio paint battle scenes in the Viceroy's palace and then trained with Aniello Falcone, whose studio gave him a rigorous education, and in the late 1630s he contributed to the decoration of the Buen Retiro Palace in Madrid. He was deeply influenced by Giovanni Benedetto Castiglione, whose Voyage of Jacob inspired his own romantic Old Testament scenes, set in warm, soft landscapes full of animals, still-life details and incident. He painted battles, biblical and classical stories and pastoral subjects for patrons in Naples and Spain. The Tobit Burying the Dead in the Metropolitan Museum belongs to the 1640s.","q5681":"Andrea Mantegna was born about 1431 near Padua and trained there in the workshop of Francesco Squarcione, though he learned most from the sculptures of Donatello and from antique remains. As a teenager he began painting frescoes in the Ovetari Chapel in Padua, and in 1453 he married Nicolosia, daughter of Jacopo Bellini and sister of Giovanni Bellini. From 1460 he served the Gonzaga family at Mantua as court painter, where he painted the Camera degli Sposi, a room whose ceiling opens in an illusionistic oculus on to the sky, and the nine Triumphs of Caesar, now at Hampton Court. His hard, sculptural figures, steep perspective and learning in Roman archaeology influenced a whole generation. He also made engravings. Judith with the Head of Holofernes, about 1495 to 1500, is a grisaille. He died in Mantua in 1506.","q495008":"Andrea Sacchi was born near Rome in 1599, the son of a painter, and trained under Francesco Albani, who had learned in Bologna the classical manner of the Carracci. He worked in Rome for the Barberini family, and for Cardinal Francesco Barberini he painted the ceiling of the Palazzo Barberini with Divine Wisdom, a calm, clear composition of a few large figures. It was soon rivalled by Pietro da Cortona's crowded Triumph of Divine Providence in the same palace, and in 1636 the two painters' supporters argued in the Accademia di San Luca over whether a picture should have a few figures or many, a debate that became a landmark of Baroque theory. Sacchi took the side of restraint. Marcantonio Pasqualini Crowned by Apollo, of 1641, portrays a singer much admired by the Barberini. He died in 1661.","q495060":"Andrea Schiavone, whose real name was Andrea Meldolla, was born at Zara in Dalmatia, then part of the Venetian Republic, around 1510 to 1515, to parents from Meldola in Romagna; Schiavone means the Slav. He worked mainly in Venice, where he brought the elongated, graceful figures of Parmigianino, whose drawings he copied in etching, into a city dominated by Titian's colour. His brush was loose and sketchy, and the freedom of his handling influenced Titian, Tintoretto and Jacopo Bassano. By 1540 Vasari had commissioned a large battle picture from him. Few of his paintings are documented, perhaps because, as Vasari says, he mostly worked for private clients. His etchings, with their dense webs of fine hatching, were admired by later etchers, including Rembrandt. He died in Venice in 1563.","q495110":"Andrea Solario was born in Milan around 1465, the younger brother of the sculptor Cristoforo Solari, who gave him his first training while working on Milan Cathedral and the Certosa di Pavia. In 1490 he went with Cristoforo to Venice, where the portraits of Antonello da Messina and other Venetian painters left a mark on him, and he returned to Milan in 1493. His earliest dated work is a Holy Family of 1495. He became one of the most gifted followers of Leonardo da Vinci in Lombardy, using Leonardo's soft shading in Madonnas and portraits. About 1507 he went to Normandy to paint for the French cardinal Georges d'Amboise at the Château de Gaillon. His Madonna with the Green Cushion is in the Louvre, and the Metropolitan Museum owns his Salome with the Head of Saint John the Baptist. He died in 1524.","q76416":"Andreas Achenbach was born in Kassel in 1815 and grew up in Düsseldorf, where he studied at the academy under Johann Wilhelm Schirmer and became one of the leading German landscape and marine painters of the century. Sea storms, harbours, mills, and Dutch, Norwegian and Italian scenes were his subjects, and travels to Holland and Scandinavia in the 1830s and to Italy, including Sicily, in the 1840s gave him new motifs. He painted with great energy, strong contrasts of light and dark, and a taste for drama, and his work was bought by collectors across Europe and in the United States. In 1848 he helped found the Düsseldorf artists' society Malkasten. He lived to the age of ninety-four, dying in 1910 as one of the last of his generation. Sunset after a Storm on the Coast of Sicily is dated 1853.","q20119940":"Andrew Dunn was an Irish painter whose dates are given as 1800 to 1820, probably the years in which he was active. He is known here from a single picture, a Portrait of an Officer in the Metropolitan Museum, and nothing certain can be said of his training or career. The years around 1800 to 1820 were the years of the Napoleonic wars, when officers of the army and navy commonly had their portraits painted, whether before departing for service or after a promotion or a decoration, and the uniform, with its colour, lace and buttons, told a viewer the regiment and rank at a glance. Portraits of this kind recorded a man's place in the service, and many are now identified only by their uniform, as this one is, since the title gives no name for the sitter.","q4758284":"Andrew Plimer was baptised in 1763 at Wellington in Shropshire, the younger son of a clockmaker. He and his elder brother Nathaniel, also a future miniaturist, trained as clockmakers but ran away from home and spent more than two years travelling in Wales and the west of England with a band of Gypsies. About 1781 they settled in London, where Andrew went to work as a manservant for the fashionable miniaturist Richard Cosway, who then trained him in portrait painting. He became one of the best-known miniature painters of his day and exhibited at the Royal Academy. His most famous picture shows the three daughters of Sir John Rushout. He married in 1801 and died at Brighton in 1837. His portrait of Elizabeth Bushby in the Metropolitan Museum is dated 1804.","q18783557":"Andrew Robertson was born in Aberdeen in 1777, one of three brothers who became painters; Alexander and Archibald went to America, while Andrew made his career in Britain. He moved to London in 1801 and became a miniature painter in demand among the gentry and the military. Dissatisfied with the prettiness of Richard Cosway's work, which he described as pretty things but not pictures, he created a new kind of miniature: larger and rectangular, with more gum in the paint to give it depth and lustre, aiming at the richness of a full-size oil. This style dominated the art by the middle of the nineteenth century, and the Victoria and Albert Museum holds several of his works. His self-portrait is in the National Portrait Gallery in London. He died in 1845.","q378182":"Giuseppe Angeli was born in Venice in the early eighteenth century and died there in 1798. He trained in the studio of Giambattista Piazzetta, from whom he took the strong light and shadow and warm colour of his first pictures, and by 1741 he was a member of the painters' guild. He became a teacher at the Venetian Academy of Fine Arts when it opened in 1756 and was its president in 1772. He painted genre and religious subjects, altarpieces for Venetian churches such as San Stae, and frescoes at the Villa Widmann-Foscari at Mira and in palaces in the city, and a portrait of the Patriarch of Venice is in Madrid. His Elijah Taken Up in a Chariot of Fire in the National Gallery of Art dates from about 1740 to 1755. He is now known mainly to specialists in Venetian painting.","q123098":"Angelica Kauffman was born in Chur, Switzerland, in 1741 and trained as a child under her father, a painter who took her through northern Italy and on to Rome, Florence and Venice. In London from 1766 she was a favourite of Joshua Reynolds, and in 1768 she became one of the founding members of the Royal Academy, one of only two women among them. She painted portraits and classical and literary scenes in a graceful neoclassical style, and contributed paintings to interiors by Robert Adam. In 1767 she was tricked into marriage by an impostor posing as a Swedish count, and in 1781 she married the Venetian painter Antonio Zucchi. She settled in Rome in 1782, where Goethe and other visitors called on her, and died there in 1807.","q5664":"Fra Angelico, born Guido di Pietro near Fiesole about 1395, entered the Dominican order, took the name Fra Giovanni, and was called Angelico, the angelic, after his death, which came in Rome in 1455. He trained as an illuminator and painter, probably in Florence, and developed a luminous style, with pure colour, graceful figures and a new command of perspective learned from the example of Masaccio and Brunelleschi. His most celebrated work is the cycle of frescoes made from about 1438 for the Dominican convent of San Marco in Florence, financed by Cosimo de' Medici, where each monk's cell has its own devotional scene. In 1445 Pope Eugenius IV called him to Rome, where he painted the chapel of Nicholas V in the Vatican. Pope John Paul II beatified him in 1982, and he is regarded as the patron of Catholic artists.","q4766924":"Anna Claypoole Peale was born in Philadelphia in 1791, the daughter of the miniaturist James Peale and niece of Charles Willson Peale. She served a five-year apprenticeship in her father's studio and at fourteen sold two copies of French landscapes at auction. She first showed at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts in 1811, with a still life, and from 1814 exhibited portrait miniatures on ivory, taking the commissions her father had decided to decline. She worked in Philadelphia, Baltimore, New York and Washington. In 1824 she and her sister Sarah Miriam were the first women elected academicians of the Pennsylvania Academy. She married a Baptist minister, William Staughton, in 1829, and after his death that year she resumed painting, then retired shortly after marrying General William Duncan in 1841. She died in 1878.","anna_dorothea_therbusch":"Anna Dorothea Therbusch was born in Berlin in 1721, the daughter of the Polish-descended portrait painter Georg Lisiewski, who taught her and her sister Anna Rosina; both were hailed as child prodigies. Her early work copied the fêtes galantes of Antoine Pesne and the manner of Watteau, Lancret and Pater, which Frederick the Great admired. After marrying a Berlin innkeeper in 1742 she gave up painting for years, returning to it in 1760 at the court of the Duke of Württemberg in Stuttgart. In 1765 she went to Paris, where Diderot befriended her and she was elected to the Académie royale in 1767. Back in Berlin from 1768, she became portrait painter to Frederick the Great, decorated rooms at Sanssouci with mythological scenes, and painted royal portraits for Catherine the Great. She died in Berlin in 1782.","q16858002":"Anne Foldsone, later Anne Mee, was the eldest child of John Foldsone and was educated at a school in Queen Square, Bloomsbury. She began to paint at twelve, with lessons from George Romney, and after her father's death in 1784 she painted to support her family. As Miss Foldsone she gained royal and aristocratic patrons, and Horace Walpole noted in a letter of 1790 that she was at Windsor painting the princesses. The Prince Regent later employed her to paint fashionable beauties, many of whom went to Windsor, and her portraits were engraved for fashionable magazines. In 1812 she started a serial, Gallery of Beauties of the Court of George III, but only one number appeared. She married the Irish barrister Joseph Mee in 1793, had six children, and died at Hammersmith in 1851.","q17423465":"Anne Hall was an American painter of portrait miniatures, who lived from 1792 to 1863. The Metropolitan Museum holds several of her works, among them miniatures of Charles Henry Hall and Eliza Hall, made between about 1810 and 1820, a portrait of Anne Catherine Ward and John Ward of about 1840, and a subject picture, The Magdalen, of about 1830. That she painted a religious figure as well as portraits suggests an ambition beyond the usual likeness. Miniatures of this period were generally painted in watercolour on thin slices of ivory, small enough to be held in the hand, worn as jewellery or kept in a case, and they were the common form of personal portrait before photography. Little is firmly recorded of her training or career.","q433434":"Anne-Louis Girodet was born at Montargis in 1767 and took the name Trioson from his guardian, a physician who adopted him. He trained in the studio of Jacques-Louis David, and the Coriolanus in Washington, dated 1786, is an early work from those years, painted when he was about nineteen. In 1789 he won the Prix de Rome and spent 1790 to 1795 in Italy, where he painted the Sleep of Endymion, a moonlit nude that broke with his master's severe manner. Back in Paris he worked for Napoleon, among other things painting Ossian Receiving the Spirits of French Generals for Malmaison and the Revolt at Cairo; his Burial of Atala of 1808 was one of the great successes of the Salon. His poetic, sometimes fantastic spirit makes him a link between Neoclassicism and Romanticism. He died in Paris in 1824.","q22669600":"Anne Teakes is recorded as active between 1812 and 1827, and little else is known of her. She is represented here by a single picture, Mrs. Teakes, in the Metropolitan Museum, which carries two dates, 1812 and 1827. The two dates may mean that the portrait was altered or inscribed at a later time, but the record does not say. Who Mrs. Teakes was, how she came to be painted, and how the painter and the sitter were related are not stated. Many portraits of the early nineteenth century survive from painters whose names are known only from a few pictures, and this is such a case. Nothing more can safely be said of her training or career.","q468772":"Anne Vallayer was born in Paris in 1744, the daughter of a goldsmith who worked at the Gobelins, and she became a painter of still life at a time when that genre ranked low. She was admitted to the Académie royale in 1770, at twenty-six, and her gift for flowers, fruit, shells and musical instruments soon won notice from collectors and artists. Marie Antoinette took a particular interest in her work, and in 1781 Vallayer was given lodgings in the Louvre, the same year she married the lawyer and parliamentarian Jean-Pierre Silvestre Coster. She also painted portraits, though her success with figures was limited by the restrictions placed on women. She survived the Terror, but the fall of the monarchy, her chief patron, hurt her reputation. She died in Paris in 1818. Basket of Plums is dated 1769.","q21857382":"Annibal Christian Loutherbourg is known here only through a single picture, a portrait of a man said to be James Madison, dated 1795. The Metropolitan Museum calls him French, and nothing else about him is securely recorded. The title is cautious about the sitter, too: it says only that the portrait is said to show Madison. If it is Madison, he was in 1795 a Virginia congressman in his mid-forties, who had helped to write the Constitution and had married the widow Dolley Payne Todd the year before. He later served as secretary of state and, from 1809 to 1817, as the fourth President of the United States. A portrait of him at this date would be an early likeness of a statesman who had not yet reached the presidency.","q7824":"Annibale Carracci was born in Bologna in 1560 and trained with his cousin Ludovico and his elder brother Agostino. In the early 1580s the three founded an academy, later called the Incamminati, the Progressing, and set out to reform painting by returning to the study of nature and of the great Italian masters. His early pictures, such as Boy Drinking, show fresh, vigorous observation of everyday life. In 1595 he went to Rome at the invitation of Cardinal Odoardo Farnese, and in the gallery of the Palazzo Farnese he painted, between 1597 and 1608, the great ceiling of mythological love scenes, a masterpiece of the early Baroque. He was reportedly poorly paid for it, and illness and low spirits darkened his last years. He died in Rome in 1609 and was buried near Raphael in the Pantheon.","q4233718":"Anonymous Artist is not a painter but a label for a picture whose maker is unknown. The National Gallery of Art uses it for a portrait of Barbara Villiers, Duchess of Cleveland, painted about 1661 to 1665. Barbara Villiers, born in 1640, married Roger Palmer in 1659 and became the most notorious mistress of Charles II, who acknowledged five of her children and made her Countess of Castlemaine and later Duchess of Cleveland. She was painted many times, above all by the court painter Sir Peter Lely, and portraits of famous beauties were often repeated in studios or copied by other hands for admirers. This anonymous portrait belongs to that world of Restoration court portraiture. She died in 1709.","q4770718":"Anson Dickinson was born in 1779 at Milton, in Litchfield, Connecticut, the eldest of ten children of a master carpenter. He was apprenticed to a silversmith, did enamel work, made frames and painted signs before becoming a miniature painter, and he probably learned from Elkanah Tisdale. In 1802 he advertised portrait miniatures in a New Haven newspaper, and in 1804 the leading American miniaturist, Edward Greene Malbone, painted his portrait. From 1805 he travelled constantly, painting prominent local people and students of the Litchfield Law School, and he met Washington Irving in Albany in 1810. He was among the first members of the American Academy of the Fine Arts in 1816. He produced a very large number of works and was famous in his lifetime, but he was later largely forgotten. He died in 1852.","q572610":"Anthonie van Borssom was born in Amsterdam in 1631 and was buried there in the Westerkerk in 1677. According to the Netherlands Institute for Art History he was an Italianate landscape painter who copied or adapted the work of the popular landscape painters of his day, among them Jacob van Ruisdael, Paulus Potter, Aelbert Cuyp, Nicolaes Berchem, Philips Koninck, Jan Wijnants and Aert van der Neer, and he is probably to be counted among Rembrandt's pupils in the late 1640s. He also made drawings. A journey along the Rhine between 1650 and 1655 took him as far as Kleve. His River Scene with Windmill and Boats, Evening, dated about 1645, shows the Dutch love of evening light on water and a flat country, with sails and a mill against a pale sky.","q176694":"Anthonis Mor, called Antonio Moro in Spain, was born at Utrecht around 1517 and trained under Jan van Scorel. His earliest known portrait is dated 1538. In 1547 he joined the painters' guild in Antwerp, and about 1548 he won as a steady patron Cardinal Antoine Perrenot de Granvelle, minister of Charles V, which opened the way to the Habsburg courts. He developed a formal court portrait based on Titian, in which the sitter appears grand and self-possessed, and he painted Mary Tudor in England in 1554, when she was about to marry Philip II. He also worked in Portugal and Spain. His portraits of men have special psychological force, and his style was very influential in the Iberian peninsula, where it led toward Velázquez. He died in Antwerp in 1577.","q22575836":"Anthony Meucci was a portrait painter active between 1818 and 1837. Little is recorded of his life beyond these dates. The Metropolitan Museum has two of his portraits of women: Mrs. Daniel Parkman, who was born Mary George McDonough, painted about 1825, and a Portrait of a Lady. The record gives no birthplace or teacher for him, and nothing can safely be said about where he trained. Portraits like that of Mrs. Parkman, which names both her married and maiden names, were commissioned by families who wanted a likeness of a wife or daughter in her best clothes, and they were generally painted in a few sittings and kept as a record of a woman of standing. His working years fall in the age before photography.","q150679":"Anthony van Dyck was born in Antwerp in 1599, the son of a cloth merchant, and was a prodigy, a master in the Antwerp guild by 1618 and the chief assistant of Rubens about the same time. Between 1621 and 1627 he lived in Italy, and in Genoa he painted a series of grand portraits of the nobility, such as Giovanni Vincenzo Imperiale (1626) and Marchesa Balbi, in which the sitters have a new elegance. He also painted Isabella Brant, Rubens's wife, in 1621. After a few years in Antwerp he settled in England in 1632 as principal painter to Charles I, who knighted him, and he created the image of the Stuart court in portraits such as Philip, Lord Wharton (1632) that set the pattern of English portraiture for a century. He died in London in 1641, aged forty-two.","antoine_alphonse_montfort":"Antoine-Alphonse Montfort was born in Paris in 1802 and at fourteen became a pupil of Horace Vernet; later he worked in the studio of Baron Gros and received advice from Géricault. In 1827 and 1828 he sailed as ship's painter on the frigate La Victorieuse, visiting Corsica, Malta, the Greek islands, Istanbul, Syria and Egypt. In 1837 and 1838 he took part in an expedition to Syria, Lebanon and Palestine, where he dressed as a local, travelled with caravans, lived in a tent, studied Arabic and kept a detailed diary. The Cedars of Lebanon of 1837 belongs to this journey. He exhibited at the Salon from 1835 to 1881 and taught at the École des Beaux-Arts. His work is known for its precise ethnographic detail. He died in Paris in 1884.","q586606":"Antoine Le Nain, the eldest of the three Le Nain brothers, was born in Laon in northern France around 1600. By 1630 he and his brothers Louis and Mathieu were all living in Paris, sharing a studio that Antoine founded; his admission to the painters' guild allowed his brothers to train there without paying fees. In 1632 he painted a group portrait of the aldermen of Paris. The brothers painted religious pictures, portraits and, from about 1640, scenes of peasant life treated with a dignity rare for the time, often in silvery grey and brown tones. They signed only the family name and many works may be collaborations, so scholars still argue over who painted what. All three brothers entered the Académie royale in 1648, and Antoine died in Paris that year, within days of Louis.","q451489":"Antoine-Louis Barye was born in Paris in 1795 and began in metalwork, as an apprentice to a maker of military equipment and in the goldsmith's trade, before studying with the sculptor François-Joseph Bosio and the painter Baron Gros. He is best known as a sculptor of wild animals in bronze, which he modelled after studying living animals and dissecting dead ones at the Jardin des Plantes. His Lion Crushing a Serpent, shown at the Salon of 1833, made his name, and from 1854 he taught at the Museum of Natural History. He also painted, chiefly watercolours and small oil sketches of animals and the landscape of Fontainebleau, alongside his sculpture. The Tiger in Repose in the Metropolitan Museum is one of these. He died in Paris in 1875.","q2389104":"Antoine Vestier was born at Avallon in Burgundy in 1740 and trained in Paris in the studio of Jean-Baptiste Pierre. A portraitist and miniaturist, he first showed at the Salon de la Correspondance and was admitted to the Académie royale in 1785, when his portrait of the painter Gabriel François Doyen served as his reception piece. His sitters included the royal cabinet-maker Jean Henri Riesener, the painter Nicolas-Guy Brenet and Jean Thurel, an old soldier who wears three medals for seventy-two years of service, in a frank portrait of 1788. His portrait of Eugène Joseph Stanislas Foullon d'Ecotier is dated 1785. His daughter Marie-Nicole was also a portraitist, and she married the miniaturist François Dumont. Vestier outlived the Revolution by many years and died in Paris in 1824.","q587078":"Antoine Vollon was born in Lyon in 1833, the son of an ornamental craftsman. He trained as a metal engraver and at the École des Beaux-Arts in Lyon from 1850 to 1853, and then decorated enamelled pans and stoves. In 1859 he moved to Paris, where he studied with Théodule Ribot and learned from the Dutch still-life painters of the seventeenth century, and he became friendly with Daumier, Daubigny and Carpeaux. His still lifes of copper pots, fish, game, fruit, cheese and flowers, painted with a loaded brush and warm colour, brought him medals and a following of collectors. In 1876 his picture of a woman of the Pollet quarter of Dieppe with a basket on her back won a prize at the Salon. He was called a painter's painter, and his reputation has never been fully reassessed. He died in 1900.","q183221":"Antoine Watteau was born at Valenciennes in 1684 and moved to Paris around 1702, where he worked for a scene painter and then with Claude Gillot, who introduced him to the theatre and the commedia dell'arte, and with Claude Audran III, the keeper of the Luxembourg Palace, where he saw Rubens's Marie de' Medici cycle. In 1717 he was received into the Academy with the Embarkation for Cythera, a picture that created a new type, the fête galante, showing elegantly dressed couples in parkland. His other subjects include actors, such as Mezzetin, and the shop sign he painted in 1720 for the dealer Gersaint. In 1719 he went to London to consult Dr. Richard Mead. A frail man, he died of tuberculosis at Nogent-sur-Marne in 1721, aged thirty-six, and was soon imitated all over Europe.","q588840":"Anton Dieffenbach was born in Wiesbaden in 1831 and grew up in Strasbourg, where he took lessons from Charles Duhamel and, on his recommendation, went to Paris to study with the sculptor James Pradier. After Pradier's death in 1852 he returned to Germany to devote himself to painting, and in 1856 and 1857 he studied intermittently at the Düsseldorf academy, taking private lessons and joining the artists' association Malkasten. A landscape and genre painter noted for his portrayal of children, he chose subjects influenced by Ludwig Knaus. One picture, A Day Before the Wedding, was bought for the King of Württemberg, and a copy was sold in the United States in 1868. He lived in Paris from 1863 until the Franco-Prussian War, later in Berlin, and went back to Strasbourg in 1897, dying in the early twentieth century.","q591907":"Anton Mauve was born in Zaandam in 1838, the son of a Mennonite minister, and grew up in Haarlem. He was apprenticed to Pieter Frederik van Os and then Wouter Verschuur, and later worked outdoors with Paul Gabriël at Oosterbeek, the Dutch Barbizon. Encouraged by his friends Jozef Israëls and Willem Maris, he gave up his early, highly finished manner for a freer one, in a tender harmony of delicate grey, green and light blue. A leading painter of the Hague School, he is best known for peasants at work and flocks of sheep, which were so popular with American patrons that dealers distinguished between pictures of sheep coming and sheep going. He was an early influence on his cousin-in-law Vincent van Gogh, who studied with him in The Hague in 1881 and 1882. Mauve died in 1888.","q76718":"Anton Raphael Mengs was born at Ústí nad Labem in Bohemia in 1728, the son of the Danish-born court painter Ismael Mengs, who raised him in Dresden and from 1741 in Rome, setting him to copy Raphael and the antique. In 1749 he was appointed first painter to the Elector of Saxony, though he spent most of his time in Rome. There he became a friend of the scholar Johann Joachim Winckelmann, whose portrait he painted, and his ceiling fresco Parnassus at the Villa Albani, finished about 1761, was hailed as a manifesto of neoclassicism. He worked for Charles III of Spain in Madrid in the 1760s and 1770s. His influence was great in his lifetime, but his cool, correct manner later seemed dry. He died in Rome in 1779.","q5594":"Antonello da Messina, born in Messina in Sicily around 1430, is the great link between Italian and Netherlandish painting of the fifteenth century. He is thought to have trained in Naples, where Flemish pictures were known, and he brought to Italian art the close observation, luminous colour and smooth finish of oil painting, although Vasari's claim that he introduced the oil technique to Italy is no longer accepted. He worked in Sicily and Calabria, and in 1475 and 1476 in Venice, where his San Cassiano altarpiece impressed Giovanni Bellini and later Venetian painters. His small portraits, with the head turned three-quarters and lit from the side, are among his best-known works. The Met's Portrait of a Young Man dates from about 1470. He died in Messina in 1479.","q598649":"Antoniazzo Romano, whose real name was Antonio di Benedetto Aquilio, was born in Rome about 1430, the son of a painter, and became the leading painter of the Roman school in the later fifteenth century. As a young man he was fined for brawling in the streets. His first recorded work, of 1461, was a replica of the miraculous Virgin and Child of Santa Maria Maggiore, made for Alessandro Sforza of Pesaro; from 1464 he worked for the papal court, and in 1467 he finished the funerary chapel of Cardinal Bessarion in Santi Apostoli. He was influenced by Benozzo Gozzoli and Fra Angelico, and specialised in repainting or reinterpreting older images and in making new devotional pictures with an archaic, gold-ground look. He died about 1510. The Met's Saint Francis is dated 1480 to 1481.","q601175":"Antonio Campi was born in Cremona about 1522, a half-brother of the painter Giulio Campi and full brother of Vincenzo, in a family in which painting was the trade. He trained with Giulio in a style tending toward Mannerism, and his first signed work, of 1546, shows the influence of Camillo Boccaccino and, through him, of Parmigianino. Around 1547 he made a series of chiaroscuro prints, and from 1549 he worked with Giulio on eight canvases of Stories of Justice for the Palazzo della Loggia in Brescia, as well as on frescoes in Cremona churches. He was also an architect, and in 1585 he published an illustrated history of Cremona. His Portrait of a Man with a Hebrew Tablet in the Metropolitan Museum dates from about 1575. He died in January 1587.","antonio_del_ceraiolo":"Antonio del Ceraiolo, also known as Antonio di Arcangelo, was a Florentine painter active in his native city between 1518 and 1538. His nickname comes from his father's trade, ceraiolo, or candle maker. According to Vasari, he was a pupil first of Lorenzo di Credi and then of Ridolfo Ghirlandaio, and Vasari praised his gifts as a portraitist. Two of his altarpieces, a Crucifixion with Saints Francis and Mary Magdalen and a Saint Michael, survive in the museum at San Salvi in Florence. Most of his other surviving paintings are half-length Madonnas with the Christ Child, usually with the young Saint John the Baptist, the patron saint of Florence. The Madonna and Child in a Niche, of the 1520s, in Cleveland is a work of this type.","antonio_guardi":"Antonio Guardi, usually called Gian Antonio, was born in Vienna in 1699 into a noble family from Trentino. With his brothers Nicolò and Francesco he inherited the family workshop after his father Domenico died in 1716, and by 1730 he had a workshop in Venice. One of his first important clients was the collector Marshal Johann Matthias von der Schulenburg, for whom he painted many pictures on Turkish and Oriental themes, and he also made copies after other artists. He trained his younger brother Francesco, who worked with him as a figure painter before turning to views of Venice in the late 1750s. His sister married Giambattista Tiepolo. He was a founder member of the Venetian Academy in 1756, and died in 1760. The Head of a Woman in Cleveland is dated about 1739.","q2404265":"Antonio Joli was born in Modena in 1700 and trained in Modena, Bologna and Rome, where he studied with the view painter Giovanni Paolo Panini. He worked first as a designer of theatre scenery, in Rome, Venice, Dresden, Warsaw, London and Madrid, and in London in the 1740s he worked for the Haymarket opera house. In Venice, where he spent several years in the 1740s, he painted views of the city and its festivals in the manner of Canaletto, such as the Washington pictures of a procession in the courtyard of the Ducal Palace and of a procession of gondolas in the Bacino of San Marco, both dated 1742 or after. From 1762 he lived in Naples, working for the royal court, and he died there in 1777.","q935534":"Antonio Vivarini, called Antonio of Murano, came from a family of glassworkers and was the earliest of a dynasty of Venetian painters that included his younger brother Bartolomeo and his son Alvise. He trained with Andrea da Murano and was influenced by Gentile da Fabriano; his earliest dated altarpiece is of 1440 and the latest of 1464, and he was alive in 1470. Until 1447 he collaborated with his brother-in-law Giovanni d'Alemagna, and the two produced large polyptychs in a rich, late Gothic manner, full of gold and bright colour. Afterwards he worked alone or with Bartolomeo, and in Padua he painted at the Ovetari Chapel, where Mantegna also worked. It is sometimes hard to say which Vivarini painted a given picture. The Met's Saint Peter Martyr Healing the Leg of a Young Man is dated about 1450.","antwerp_16th_century":"Antwerp 16th Century is not one painter but a heading used for pictures made in Antwerp in the 1500s by unnamed hands. The National Gallery's group includes a Martyrdom of Saint Catherine of about 1540 and a set of linked portraits of 1542 and 1543: a member of the de Hondecoeter family, with a painted coat of arms on the back of the panel, his wife, and an almoner of Antwerp. Sixteenth-century Antwerp was the richest port in northern Europe and a great centre of painting, where many masters supplied altarpieces, portraits and pictures for the open market, so panels by painters who cannot be named are common. Portraits like these record the merchants and officials of a prosperous city, and they are valued for the dress, the setting and the confident faces.","q22276332":"Aramenta Dianthe Vail was an American painter of miniatures, born in 1820. She lived in Newark, New Jersey, in 1837 and 1838, and then in New York City from 1839 to 1863, where the city directories list her as a miniature painter until 1858 and afterward as a seller of fancy goods. She showed work at the National Academy of Design in 1838, 1841 and 1847, at the Apollo Association and the American Institute, and at the Brooklyn Art Institute in 1863. She seems never to have married. Her style has been called idiosyncratic, her figures unusually constructed, and she liked a matte finish, unlike most painters of her generation. The Metropolitan Museum holds two of her portraits of children. She died in 1888.","q17747277":"Archibald Robertson was born near Aberdeen in 1765, the eldest of three artistic brothers, and studied at Marischal College, in Edinburgh, and from 1786 in London, with Joshua Reynolds and Benjamin West and at the Royal Academy. After running an art school in Aberdeen he came to New York in 1791, at the invitation of wealthy people who wanted an art teacher, and was soon asked to paint George and Martha Washington. With his brother Alexander he ran the Columbian Academy of Painting in New York. He made miniature portraits, watercolour landscapes and engravings, and he wrote Elements of the Graphic Arts, published in 1802. His miniature of Sir Joshua Reynolds, of 1786 to 1791, dates from his London years. He died in 1835.","archibald_willard":"Archibald Willard was born in Bedford, Ohio, in 1836, the son of a Baptist pastor, and worked as a wagon painter in Wellington, Ohio, for E. S. Tripp, learning the elaborate decoration that was then fashionable on wagons. He served in the 86th Ohio Infantry in 1863 and painted scenes of army life. After the war a pair of humorous pictures of children, Pluck and Pluck No. 2, brought him to the notice of the Cleveland photographer and chromolithographer James F. Ryder, who saw their commercial promise. About 1875 he painted The Spirit of '76, first called Yankee Doodle, a scene of three marching figures, with his father as the model for the central one; shown at the Centennial Exposition of 1876, it became one of America's best-known pictures, thanks largely to Ryder's prints. He died in 1918.","q7751":"Giuseppe Arcimboldo was born in Milan in 1526 into a family of artists. He began by designing stained glass and tapestries for Milan Cathedral and for Monza, and in 1562 went to Vienna to work for the Habsburgs, serving Ferdinand I, Maximilian II and Rudolf II at Vienna and Prague for about twenty-five years as court portraitist and designer of festivals. He is famous for composite heads built of fruit, vegetables, flowers, fish, books or animals, such as the Four Seasons, which he painted in several versions from 1563, and the Four Elements. His portrait of Rudolf II as Vertumnus, god of the seasons, was painted about 1590. These witty pictures amused learned courts that loved curiosities. He returned to Milan in 1587 and died there in 1593. The Surrealists later admired him.","q123071":"Arnold Böcklin, born in Basel in 1827, trained in Düsseldorf and then in Rome, where he lived from 1850 and married in 1853. He built his career between Switzerland, Germany and Italy: professor at the Weimar academy for two years after 1858, then in Rome, Basel and Munich, in Florence from 1876 to 1885, and then in Zurich and near Florence, where he died in 1901. He painted mythological scenes set in Italian-looking landscapes, with nymphs, satyrs, centaurs and sea creatures, in rich and sometimes violent colour. Admired in his own lifetime in Germany and loved by the wider public for the Island of the Dead, he was a forerunner of the Symbolists and later influenced Giorgio de Chirico and the Surrealists. The Sanctuary of Hercules, of 1884, belongs to his Florentine years.","q212657":"Artemisia Gentileschi was born in Rome in 1593, the daughter of the painter Orazio Gentileschi, from whom she learned to paint, and she became the most successful woman painter of her century. At seventeen she painted Susanna and the Elders. A trial in Rome in 1612, in which she testified against the painter Agostino Tassi, marked her early life. She married and moved to Florence, where in 1616 she became the first woman admitted to the Accademia del Disegno, enjoyed the protection of the Medici and was friendly with Galileo. She later worked in Rome, Venice and Naples, and about 1638 to 1640 joined her father at the court of Charles I in London. Her Biblical heroines, such as Judith and Esther, are painted with force and clarity. She died in Naples, probably in 1656.","q8205641":"Arthur Fitzwilliam Tait was born near Liverpool in 1819 and, after his father's bankruptcy, was sent to live with relatives in Lancaster, where he grew fond of animals. In Manchester he joined the art dealers Agnew and Zanetti, learned painting on his own, and made reproduction lithographs, including railway views in the 1840s. After seeing George Catlin's exhibition in Paris, he emigrated to the United States in 1850 and set up a small painting camp in the Adirondacks to work there in summer. From 1852 Currier and Ives made lithographs of his pictures, which spread his name, and in 1858 he became a full member of the National Academy of Design. He painted deer, hunters, barnyard fowl and wild birds, and small panels of chickens that were widely reproduced. He died in 1905.","asher_b_durand":"Asher B. Durand was born at Jefferson Village, now Maplewood, New Jersey, in 1796 and trained as an engraver. His engraving of John Trumbull's Declaration of Independence, finished in 1823, made his name, and for years he was the leading American engraver, producing portraits and bank notes. Encouraged by Thomas Cole and by the patron Luman Reed, he turned to painting, first portraits and then landscape, and became the chief painter of the Hudson River School after Cole's death in 1848. His Kindred Spirits of 1849 shows Cole and the poet William Cullen Bryant in a Catskill ravine. He was president of the National Academy of Design from 1845 to 1861, and his Letters on Landscape Painting, published in 1855, urged students to paint directly from nature. He died in 1886.","q391608":"Asher Brown Durand was born in 1796 in New Jersey, the son of a watchmaker and silversmith, and at sixteen was apprenticed to the engraver Peter Maverick in Newark. He rose to the top of his trade, and his engraving of Trumbull's Declaration of Independence made him famous in 1823. The New York merchant and collector Luman Reed bought his portraits and landscapes and encouraged him to paint, and Durand later portrayed both Reed and the collector Jonathan Sturges. After Thomas Cole's death in 1848 he became the leading landscape painter of the Hudson River School, making oil studies outdoors in the Catskills, the Adirondacks and elsewhere and working them up in the studio. His woodland pictures, such as The Beeches and In the Woods, show a close study of trees. He lived to ninety and died at Maplewood in 1886.","q470846":"Amico Aspertini was born in Bologna around 1474 into a family of painters. He studied under Lorenzo Costa and Francesco Francia, went to Rome with his father in 1496 and again between 1500 and 1503, and filled sketchbooks with drawings after antique sculpture. Back in Bologna he joined Francia and Costa in painting the frescoes of the Oratory of Santa Cecilia in 1504, a commission from Giovanni II Bentivoglio. After the fall of the Bentivoglio he spent 1508 and 1509 in exile in Lucca, where he painted the splendid frescoes in the Chapel of the Cross in San Frediano. His eccentric, eclectic style, with crowded figures, odd gestures and bright, unnatural colour, anticipates Mannerism, and he is a leading figure of the Bolognese school. He died in 1552. The Saint Sebastian is dated about 1505.","q541976":"Jan Asselijn was born at Dieppe about 1610, of a French Huguenot family, as Jean Asselin, and studied with the Dutch landscape painter Esaias van de Velde. He travelled in France and Italy, where he took up the manner of Pieter van Laer, called Bamboccio, and joined the society of northern artists in Rome, the Bentvueghels, under the nickname Krabbetje, little claw, because of a withered hand. In 1645 he married at Lyon, a marriage that brought him back to the Netherlands, where he settled in Amsterdam. He was a friend of Rembrandt, who etched his portrait, and he helped to bring a fresh, clear Italianate landscape inspired by Claude Lorrain to Dutch painting. His best-known picture is The Threatened Swan. Frederick de Moucheron was his pupil. He died in 1652.","q472522":"Balthasar van der Ast was born at Middelburg in 1593 or 1594. His sister married Ambrosius Bosschaert the Elder, with whom he trained, lived and worked and whom he accompanied on his travels. He joined the Utrecht guild in 1619, and when Bosschaert died in 1621 he took over his workshop and pupils. He later worked in Delft, where he died in 1657. He painted flowers and fruit with shells, insects and lizards, first in Bosschaert's symmetrical manner, and later in looser arrangements, with bowls of fruit and baskets spread across a table, and his shells and insects are especially fine. He had a fine sense of colour and texture, and shells became something of a speciality of his. The Basket of Fruits and the Basket of Flowers are dated about 1622.","q1402629":"August Heinrich was a German painter who lived from 1794 to 1822 and died at about twenty-eight. Little is recorded of his life. The Metropolitan Museum's At the Edge of the Forest, of about 1820, belongs to the years when German painters, many of them young, were turning from classical landscape to the close, affectionate study of woods, fields and light that marks the Romantic movement. The forest, with its associations of folk tale, poetry and national history, was a favourite subject of German Romantic painters, who often showed it as a place of quiet and mystery. His short life leaves few signs of his training or career, and his name is known mostly to specialists in German art of the early nineteenth century.","q203076":"August von Pettenkofen was born in Vienna in 1822, the son of a merchant and landowner, grew up partly on his father's estate in Galicia, and from 1834 studied at the Vienna Academy with Leopold Kupelwieser and Franz Eybl, painting genre scenes in the Biedermeier manner. During the revolution of 1848 he served as a military painter, and a stay at Szolnok in Hungary made a deep impression on him, giving his style a more picturesque, truthful turn. In 1852 he went to Paris and came under the influence of the Barbizon painters. He is known above all for honest, unsentimental pictures of Hungarian peasants, gypsies and markets, without theatrical pathos. He joined the Vienna Academy in 1866, was knighted in 1876, and made annual trips to Venice in the 1870s. He died in Vienna in 1889.","q2871069":"Auguste Bonheur was born in Bordeaux in 1824, the brother of the more famous Rosa Bonheur and of the sculptor Isidore, and he learned to paint in a family of artists. Like Rosa he painted animals, especially cattle, in bucolic landscapes, with a good grasp of horizon, atmosphere and space. His work shows the influence of seventeenth-century Dutch cattle painters such as Aelbert Cuyp and Paulus Potter. In his lifetime he was compared with his successful sister, which is thought to have harmed his career, though his pictures were popular with British collectors, and in the Netherlands the art dealer Vincent van Gogh, uncle of the painter, owned one. The Met's Environs of Fontainebleau: Woodland and Cattle is a typical subject. He died at Bellevue in 1884.","q39931":"Auguste Renoir was born in Limoges in 1841, the son of a tailor, and began as a painter on porcelain in Paris. He studied in the studio of Charles Gleyre, where he met Monet, Sisley and Bazille, and painted outdoors with Monet at La Grenouillère in 1869. He took part in the Impressionist exhibitions of 1874, 1876, 1877 and 1882. Portraits for the publisher Georges Charpentier and his wife made him fashionable, and the banker Paul Bérard, at Wargemont in Normandy, became a loyal patron and friend; Renoir painted the Bérard children, including Margot. After a journey to Italy in 1881 he turned to a firmer, more linear style, then to a warm, glowing manner. Arthritis crippled his hands from the 1890s, but he kept painting at Cagnes, in the south of France, until his death in 1919.","q3570722":"Auguste-Xavier Leprince was born in Paris in 1799, the son of a painter and lithographer about whom little is known. He was essentially self-taught, working, as one patron said, almost without any guide except nature. Between 1816 and 1819 he painted livestock and their keepers in rural settings, in the manner of Dutch seventeenth-century animal painters, and the teenager became famous, with dealers asking very high prices for his first pictures. He made his Salon debut at nineteen, in 1819, with six landscapes and rural scenes, and won a medal. His patrons included the Duchesse de Berry, Charles X and the antiquary Alexandre du Sommerard. In his twenties he opened an atelier in Paris and taught his younger brothers Robert-Léopold and Pierre-Gustave, among others. He died at twenty-seven, in 1826, his brilliant promise cut short.","augustin_de_saint_aubin":"Augustin de Saint-Aubin was born in Paris in 1736 into a family of artists and designers: his father was the king's embroiderer, and his brothers Charles-Germain and Gabriel-Jacques were artists too. He was trained by his brother and by the engravers Étienne Fessard, Nicolas-Henry Tardieu and Laurent Cars, and he sent an etching to the Salon in 1752. He took many commercial commissions, such as bookplates, frontispieces, invitations and tradesmen's cards, and illustrated books such as Boccaccio's Decameron. In 1776 he was appointed official engraver at the Royal Library. He is best remembered for portraits, especially his engravings of Charles-Nicolas Cochin's portrait drawings of notable people. In his lifetime he was considered the most successful of the siblings. He died in 1807. Laban Searching for His Stolen Gods is dated 1753.","q21994029":"Augustin Dubourg was a French painter whose dates are given as 1758 to 1800, so that he died at about forty-two. Little is recorded of his life. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Woman, undated, is the picture by which he is represented here. His working life coincided with the end of the old regime, the Revolution and the Directory, a period in which the clientele for portraits widened from the court and the aristocracy to officials, professional people and the new middle class, and in which French portraiture moved toward plainer settings and a simpler, more direct manner. Nothing certain can be said of his training or of the identity of his sitter, whose name has not been recorded in the title.","q20825602":"Augustus Fuller was an American portrait painter who lived from 1812 to 1873. Little is recorded of his life in a form that can be relied on, and he is known chiefly from a few portraits in the Metropolitan Museum, among them Mr. Pierce, dated 1843, and a Portrait of a Boy. In the 1830s and 1840s a great many American portraits were made by painters working outside the academies, who travelled from town to town or set up in one place and charged modest fees for a likeness painted in a few sittings. Their pictures were kept by families as records, in the years before photography became cheap. Without a documented career, nothing can safely be said about where Fuller trained or worked.","augustus_leopold_egg":"Augustus Leopold Egg was born in London in 1816, the son of a gunsmith whose family had come from Alsace, and entered the Royal Academy Schools in 1836. He was a member of the Clique, a group of young artists founded about 1837 by Richard Dadd, and a follower of Hogarth in his taste for moral stories, as in his pair of pictures on the life and death of the Duke of Buckingham. He was a close friend of Charles Dickens, with whom he founded the Guild of Literature and Art, and he acted the lead in Bulwer-Lytton's play Not So Bad As We Seem to raise money for it. His best-known work is the triptych Past and Present, of 1858, which shows the break-up of a respectable Victorian family. Ill health took him abroad, and he died at Algiers in 1863.","q2281880":"Aurelio Lomi was born in Pisa in 1556 and worked mainly in his native city. He may have been trained first by his father, Giovanni Battista Lomi, but he soon went to Florence, where in the 1580s he worked under Alessandro Allori and Lodovico Cigoli. In Pisa he painted a Saint Jerome for the cathedral in 1595 and frescoes for several churches. He also worked in Genoa, where he painted altarpieces for churches including Santa Maria Assunta in Carignano, and in Rome, where he frescoed the Pinelli chapel in the Chiesa Nuova with scenes of the life of the Virgin. His half-brother Orazio Gentileschi, later the father of Artemisia, was one of his pupils, and so were Orazio Riminaldi and Domenico Fiasella. He died in 1622. The Gathering of Manna is one of his biblical scenes.","q212593":"Hendrick Avercamp was born in Amsterdam in 1585 and was deaf and mute, which gave him the nickname the Mute of Kampen. He is thought to have studied with the Danish-born portrait painter Pieter Isaacsz, and he was influenced by the landscape painters of the Amsterdam school. About 1614 he settled in Kampen, a town on the IJssel, where he lived for the rest of his life and died in 1634. He specialised in winter scenes: crowded panoramas of skaters, sledges, ice fishermen, gossiping couples and people falling over, spread across a frozen river or canal beneath a pale sky, with tiny figures observed with humour and affection. He also painted watercolours of the same subjects, and he is considered the founder of the Dutch winter landscape. A Scene on the Ice is dated about 1625.","q461907":"Jean Béraud was born in Saint Petersburg in 1849, the son of a French sculptor who was probably working on Saint Isaac's Cathedral; after his father's death the family moved to Paris. He was studying law until the Franco-Prussian War of 1870, then became a student of Léon Bonnat and showed at the Salon from 1872. He made his name in 1876 with a picture of a funeral party returning from a cemetery, and spent his career painting the Paris of the Belle Époque with sharp detail and a touch of mockery: boulevards, cafés, omnibuses, Montmartre and the Champs-Élysées, in a style between the academic Salon and Impressionism. He also set religious subjects in modern Paris. He received the Legion of Honour in 1894 and died in 1935. Paris, rue du Havre dates from about 1882.","q18508740":"William Perkins Babcock was an American painter, born in 1826, who spent most of his career in France and is remembered as one of the Americans who lived at Barbizon in the circle of Jean-François Millet. The National Gallery's Flowers in a Cut Glass Vase is dated to the 1850s, with the last figure of the date uncertain, the years when he was beginning his career abroad. Babcock belongs to the first generation of American artists who went to France to learn their trade and stayed in touch with the French painters of peasant life and the forest. He died in 1899.","q7770":"Francesco d'Ubertino Verdi, called Bachiacca, was born in Florence in 1494 into a family of goldsmiths, painters and embroiderers, and died there in 1557. He was apprenticed in Perugino's Florentine studio and by 1515 was working with Andrea del Sarto, Pontormo and Granacci on painted chests and wall panels for the bedroom of Pierfrancesco Borgherini. He made his reputation with predellas and small cabinet pictures, later also painting large altarpieces. In 1540 he became an artist at the court of Duke Cosimo I de' Medici and Eleonora of Toledo, and his first commission there was to decorate the Duke's private study with plants, animals and a landscape, which shows Cosimo's interest in botany. His pictures combine figures and exotic costumes borrowed from Italian, German and Netherlandish prints into new compositions.","q468214":"Ludolf Backhuysen, also written Bakhuizen, was born in Emden in East Frisia in 1630 and came to Amsterdam around 1650, where he worked as a merchant's clerk. His talent for drawing and calligraphy led him to give up business for art in the late 1650s, and he studied under Allart van Everdingen and Hendrik Dubbels. He joined the Amsterdam painters' guild in 1663 and, after the van de Veldes left for England in 1672, became the leading Dutch painter of the sea. He took pains to study storms at first hand, sailing out in an open boat, and his rough seas and ships in distress, such as the picture of 1667 in the National Gallery, are painted with intense realism. Peter the Great took lessons from him in 1697. He was also a calligrapher and etcher, and he died in 1708.","q3184555":"Joseph Badger was born in Charlestown, Massachusetts, in 1708 and died in Boston in 1765. He worked as a house painter and glazier and was largely self-taught as a portraitist. By the 1740s he was one of Boston's leading portrait painters, recording clergymen, merchants and their families in a plain, linear style with sharply defined faces; his sitters included the theologian Jonathan Edwards. The Cleveland portrait of Jeremiah Belknap, about 1758, and the Metropolitan's James Badger of 1760, a child believed to be his grandson, show his manner late in life. The arrival of Joseph Blackburn in 1754 and the rise of John Singleton Copley overshadowed him, and he is now valued as an early native-born portraitist of colonial New England.","q804745":"Baldassare Estense, known in English as Baldassare d'Este, was born at Reggio about 1443 and is supposed to have been an illegitimate member of the ruling house of Este, since no father's name is recorded, while he was called Estensis and received unusual rewards from the Dukes of Ferrara. He was a pupil of Cosimo Tura and was also a medallist. In 1469 he painted a likeness of Borso d'Este and was sent to present it to the Duke of Milan. From 1471 to 1504 he was a salaried officer at the court of Ferrara, living in the Castel Nuovo and later as governor of Castel Tedaldo. His portrait of Tito Strozzi of 1483 survives in Ferrara. The National Gallery's portrait of the young Francesco II Gonzaga, of about 1474 to 1480, belongs to the same court tradition. His will is dated 1500.","q177213":"Baldassare Peruzzi was born in 1481 in a small town near Siena and became one of the leading architects and painters of his day, working in Rome for many years alongside Bramante, Raphael and Antonio da Sangallo on the new Saint Peter's. For the banker Agostino Chigi he decorated the Villa Farnesina in Rome with painted perspectives, a fresco cycle that fooled the eye. After the Sack of Rome in 1527 he went back to Siena, where he served the Republic as an architect, designing fortifications, and painted frescoes in the Chapel of Saint John the Baptist in the cathedral. By 1535 he was back in Rome for good, and he died there in 1536 and was buried in the Pantheon near Raphael. The Met has frescoes from the Villa Stati-Mattei.","q164696":"Hans Baldung, called Grien, was born in 1484 or 1485 into a family of educated men in Swabia and trained, probably in Strasbourg, before joining Albrecht Dürer's workshop in Nuremberg about 1503 to 1507. The nickname Grien, green, may refer to his fondness for green clothes, or serve to distinguish him from other men called Hans Baldung. He left Dürer's workshop to establish himself in Strasbourg, where he spent most of his career, and painted the high altarpiece of Freiburg Minster in 1512 to 1516, a Coronation of the Virgin among saints. He is best known for his extraordinary woodcuts and drawings of witches, horses and Death seizing the maiden, subjects that mingle desire and fear. He died in Strasbourg in 1545. The New York Saint John on Patmos, about 1511, belongs to his early Strasbourg years.","bannister_edward_mitchell":"Edward Mitchell Bannister was born in 1828 at Saint Andrews, New Brunswick, and moved to Boston in the late 1840s. Barred by prejudice from art schools and apprenticeships, he was largely self-taught, working as a barber, photographer and portrait painter before turning to landscape. With his wife Christiana Carteaux he was prominent in Boston's Black community and its abolition movement. He moved to Providence, Rhode Island, in 1869 and was a founding member of the Providence Art Club. His pastoral landscapes, influenced by Jean-François Millet and the Barbizon painters, and his seascapes of the Rhode Island coast, show a fine control of colour and atmosphere. In 1876 he won the first prize in painting at the Philadelphia Centennial Exhibition. His style fell out of fashion, and he was largely forgotten after his death in 1901 until the 1960s.","q6129357":"James Bard was born in New York City in 1815, with a twin brother, John, and the two began painting ships together as boys: their first known picture, of the steamboat Belona, was made in 1827 when they were twelve. Their joint works were signed J. and J. Bard, Picture Painters, and are not easily divided between them. After John's death in 1856 James carried on alone for many more years. He painted portraits of steamboats and towboats, such as the Steamer St. Lawrence of 1850 and the Towboat John Birkbeck of 1854, in watercolour and later in oil, with the hulls, engines and flags rendered flat and precise, so that they are sometimes called naive. His wife's brother, a steamboat captain, commissioned several pictures. He died poor and almost forgotten in 1897, and his work has since become valuable.","q808081":"Barend Cornelis Koekkoek was born in Middelburg in 1803, the eldest son of the marine painter Johannes Hermanus Koekkoek, who gave him his first lessons. He studied at the Middelburg drawing academy and, from 1822, for four years at the academy in Amsterdam. In 1834 he moved to Cleves, in Germany, where he spent the rest of his life and found his ideal subject in the wooded landscapes of the lower Rhine, with great oaks, winding paths and wide views painted with minute detail and a romantic mood. In 1841 he published a book of advice to young painters, urging them to stay true to nature, and founded a drawing academy at Cleves that drew students from the Netherlands and Germany. Among his clients were Frederick William IV of Prussia, Tsar Alexander II and Willem II of the Netherlands. He died in 1862.","q261496":"Barent Fabritius was baptised in 1624 at Middenbeemster, North Holland, the son of Pieter Carelsz. Fabritius and younger brother of the more famous Carel Fabritius. He studied with his brothers Johannes and Carel and probably with Rembrandt as well. He painted biblical subjects, among them the Three Angels before Abraham and the Presentation in the Temple, as well as mythological and historical scenes and expressive portraits, and his warm, brownish colour and strong contrasts of light show the influence of Rembrandt's circle. He worked in Amsterdam, where he was buried in 1673. Abraham Dismissing Hagar and Ishmael is dated 1658. His career has been overshadowed by that of Carel, who died young in the Delft gunpowder explosion of 1654, but he is now better appreciated.","q321465":"Thomas Barker, known as Barker of Bath, was born in 1769 at Trosnant near Pontypool in Monmouthshire, the son of a Japanware decorator who also painted horses. He never took a lesson in drawing or painting. When he was sixteen his family moved to Bath, where a rich coach-builder, Charles Spackman, became his patron, set him to copy the Dutch and Flemish masters, and at twenty-one sent him to Rome with ample funds, though Barker painted very little there. He exhibited at the Royal Academy and British Institution for almost fifty years, and painted landscapes, rural scenes and a wide range of subjects. His life-sized Woodman, of which he painted two versions, sold for 500 guineas each. Shepherd Boys and Dog Sheltering from a Storm is an early work of about 1789 to 1790. He died in 1847.","q316731":"Federico Barocci was born at Urbino in 1535 and remained there for most of his life. He learned from his father, a sculptor and goldsmith, and from the Venetian painter Battista Franco, and worked in Rome in his twenties, notably on a pavilion in the Vatican gardens. About 1563 he fell ill, and his later years were troubled by a weak digestion that allowed him to work only a few hours a day. His patrons were the Della Rovere dukes of Urbino, and churches and confraternities across central Italy. He painted warm, tender altarpieces in soft, glowing colour and prepared them with many chalk and pastel studies, a practice that influenced the Carracci, Rubens and Bernini. He died at Urbino in 1612. The Metropolitan Museum holds his Saint Francis, of about 1600 to 1604.","q216999":"Antoine-Jean Gros was born in Paris in 1771 and trained from 1785 in the studio of Jacques-Louis David. In 1793 he left France for Italy, and in 1796 he met Josephine Bonaparte, who introduced him to her husband; he painted Bonaparte at the Bridge of Arcole and soon followed the army as a kind of official painter, later being made a baron. His Bonaparte Visiting the Plague Victims at Jaffa of 1804 and the Battle of Eylau of 1808 turned the Napoleonic wars into vast, colourful, emotional theatre, with a vigour that later inspired Géricault and Delacroix. In 1816, when David went into exile, Gros took over his studio, but his late history paintings were criticised as untrue to David's severity. He was found drowned in the Seine in 1835, an apparent suicide. The Washington portrait of Dr. Vignardonne dates from 1827.","q163543":"François Gérard was born in Rome in 1770, to a French father and an Italian mother, and was brought up in France. He studied with the sculptor Augustin Pajou and the painter Nicolas-Guy Brenet and, from 1786, with Jacques-Louis David. His Cupid and Psyche, shown at the Salon of 1798, was a great success, and under Napoleon and the Restoration he became the most sought-after portraitist in France, painting Madame Récamier, Napoleon in his coronation robes and the Prince de Talleyrand; the Met's portraits of Talleyrand and his wife date from about 1804 and 1808. He also painted large history pictures such as the Battle of Austerlitz and the Entry of Henri IV into Paris. He became first painter to Louis XVIII and a baron in 1819, and he held a famous salon in Paris. He was called the painter of kings. He died in 1837.","q535607":"Giovanni Baronzio belongs to the Rimini school, the group of painters who worked on the Adriatic coast of Italy in the fourteenth century and answered Giotto's innovations, Giotto having himself worked in Rimini, with a more linear, elegant and sometimes sharply emotional style. Very little is recorded of his life; he is named as from Rimini, and a handful of signed works, among them a polyptych dated 1345, fix the years in which he was active. The New York panel, with scenes from the life of Christ crowded into small compartments against a gold ground, is dated to the mid-1340s. Pictures of this kind were made for the private devotion of wealthy patrons or for small chapels, and Baronzio's name and career rest on very few surviving pieces.","barrois":"Barrois is a painter known only by a single surname, and the only picture here under that name is a Portrait of a Man dated about 1790, in the Metropolitan Museum. The museum gives no first name, no birth or death dates and no place of work. The surname suggests a French painter, but the record does not say. The date falls in the years just after the French Revolution began, when portraits were a busy trade in Paris and the provincial cities, supplying nobles, officials and merchants with likenesses in oil, pastel and miniature. Nothing certain can be said about where Barrois was born or trained, or about the sitter, whose name has not been recorded. The picture is valued as a record of dress and manner in about 1790.","q499934":"Barthel Beham was born in Nuremberg in 1502 into a family of artists and learned his art from his elder brother Hans Sebald Beham and from Albrecht Dürer. He was particularly active as an engraver in the 1520s, making tiny prints of fine detail that place him among the Little Masters. In 1525 he was banished from Lutheran Nuremberg, with his brother and Georg Pencz, the so-called godless painters, for denying baptism, Christ and transubstantiation. Later pardoned, he moved to Catholic Munich to work for the Bavarian dukes William IV and Ludwig X, and there became one of the leading German portrait painters, favoured by patrons including Emperor Charles V. The portrait of Chancellor Leonhard von Eck, dated 1527, is an early example. He died in Italy, in 1540, on a journey made under the patronage of Duke William.","q698115":"Barthel Bruyn the Elder was born in Wesel or Cologne in 1493 and trained on the Lower Rhine. His earliest documented altarpiece, a Coronation of the Virgin of 1515 to 1516, was commissioned by a professor of the University of Cologne, and his altarpieces show the influence of his relative Jan Joest and later of Joos van Cleve. In the 1530s he developed a more Italianate style, drawing on Raphael and Michelangelo as he knew them from engravings. He is best known for his portraits, which show Cologne's merchants, officials and their wives half-length against plain backgrounds, with crisp detail. He was the first important portrait painter in Cologne and founded a prolific workshop, carried on by his sons Arnt and Barthel the Younger. The Met has a Portrait of a Man and a Portrait of a Woman, both dated 1533. He died in 1555.","q809344":"Barthel Bruyn the Younger was born in Cologne about 1530 and trained in the workshop of his father, Barthel Bruyn the Elder. From about 1547 he worked with his father and his older brother Arnt on a series of fifty-seven New Testament scenes for the cloisters of the Carmelite convent in Cologne. His only signed painting, a diptych of Christ Carrying the Cross and Vanitas of 1560, now in Bonn, is the touchstone by which scholars have identified his other works. His portraits are similar to his father's but a little simpler, with half-length sitters against a flat background and attention to costume and hands, in a limited palette of black, white, grey and brown. After his father died in 1555 he inherited the workshop and served the same clients. He sat on the Cologne City Council, and failing eyesight ended his painting before his death between 1607 and 1610.","bartholomaeus_spranger":"Bartholomaeus Spranger was born in Antwerp in 1546, the third son of a trader, and was apprenticed in turn to Jan Mandijn, Frans Mostaert and Cornelis van Dalem, all mainly landscape painters. He then travelled to Paris, Milan, Parma and Rome, where he worked for Cardinal Alessandro Farnese and Pope Pius V. In 1575 he went to Vienna to serve the Emperor Maximilian II, and in 1581 settled in Prague as court painter to Rudolf II, who ennobled him in 1588. Spranger developed a Northern Mannerism of smoothly modelled, elongated figures in elegant poses, often with a nude woman seen from behind, a style that spread through prints and through artists such as Karel van Mander. He died in Prague in 1611. Ceres and Bacchus is one of his mythological pictures.","q809318":"Bartholomeus Breenbergh was born at Deventer before November 1598 and, after his father died in 1607, was probably brought up at Hoorn. His registered teachers were Pieter Lastman and Jacob Pynas in Amsterdam, and he is recorded there as a painter in 1619. He then went to Rome, where he lived from 1619 to 1630 and painted ruins, villas and countryside in warm Italian light, often with small figures and sometimes with a biblical or mythological story. Back in Amsterdam from 1630 until his death in 1657, he continued to paint Italianate landscapes and religious scenes in a clear, bright manner, and he also made etchings. His work influenced Dutch Italianate painters. The Preaching of John the Baptist, of 1634, belongs to his Amsterdam period.","q380704":"Bartholomeus van der Helst was born in Haarlem in 1613, the son of an innkeeper, and by 1636 had moved to Amsterdam, where he married the same year. His first dated work is a group portrait of the regents of the Walloon Orphanage of 1637, and his first major commission was a large civic guard group, finished in 1642. From the 1640s he became the favourite portraitist of Amsterdam's wealthy families and of the stadholder's circle, in elegant portraits with fine silks and polished surfaces, and he overtook Rembrandt in popularity. His Banquet of the Civic Guard of 1648, celebrating the Peace of Münster, was long counted among the finest group portraits of the Dutch Golden Age. He also painted a few genre scenes and biblical subjects. He died in 1670. The Musician is dated 1662.","q4865240":"Bartholomew Dandridge was born in London in 1691, the son of a house painter, according to Horace Walpole. He studied at Sir Godfrey Kneller's academy and later at the St Martin's Lane Academy, and for more than forty years he had a career as a fashionable portrait painter in London, working in a style close to that of John Vanderbank. In 1732 Lord Barrington commissioned him to paint Frederick, Prince of Wales, on horseback, and in 1733 he moved to Great Queen Street, to a house that had been part of Kneller's. He played a part in the development of the conversation piece, a small group portrait in an informal setting, making groups of model figures to study light and shade. The Met's portrait of Uvedale Tomkyns Price and his family is a picture of this type. He died in 1755.","q192062":"Bartolomé Esteban Murillo was baptised at Seville in 1618 and orphaned as a child, and was brought up by his sister and brother-in-law. He studied in Seville with Juan del Castillo, his uncle and godfather. His first major commission, eleven canvases for the Franciscan convent of San Francisco in Seville, painted from 1645 to 1648, made his name, and after a second stay in Madrid in 1658 to 1660 he returned to Seville and in 1660 helped found its academy of painting. In the 1660s and 1670s he painted the Virgin and Child, the Immaculate Conception and scenes of beggar children with warmth and gentle, silvery colour, and also portraits such as Don Andrés de Andrade y la Cal. He died in Seville in 1682, a few months after falling from a scaffold at the Capuchin church in Cádiz.","bartolom_esteban_murillo":"Bartolomé Esteban Murillo, born in Seville in 1617 or 1618, was, before the nineteenth century, more widely known abroad than any other Spanish painter. His religious pictures were soft, tender and easy to love, and none was more popular than his many versions of the Immaculate Conception, the Virgin standing on a crescent moon among cherubs. The prolific imitation of his work by pupils and followers spread his fame across Europe. He was also a painter of charming scenes of street children and flower girls, and the Cleveland Museum's Laban Searching for His Stolen Household Gods belongs to a series of Old Testament scenes from the story of Jacob. He worked in Seville for his whole career, apart from visits to Madrid, and died there in 1682.","bartolom_p_rez":"Bartolomé Pérez de la Dehesa was born in Madrid in 1634 and became the son-in-law and pupil of the painter Juan de Arellano, the leading flower painter of Madrid. He is known as a painter of flowers and still lifes, the kind of picture called a bodegón in Spain. He also painted scenery for performances at the theatre of the Buen Retiro, for which he was named painter to the king, without a salary, in January 1689. He painted religious pictures too, such as the Our Lady of Good Counsel of about 1680 in Cleveland. He died in 1693 after falling from a scaffold while painting a ceiling in the Monteleón palace in Madrid, and was buried in the church of San Ildefonso. He is little known outside Spain, but he was a skilled painter in the Spanish Baroque.","q519379":"Bartolomeo Cavarozzi was born at Viterbo in 1587 and arrived in Rome as a child, where he trained with Tarquinio Ligustri and then Giovanni Battista Crescenzi, a painter of still lifes and an influential connoisseur. His earliest known work, of 1608, is an altarpiece in Rome. He became one of the more distinct and original followers of Caravaggio, painting Biblical subjects and still lifes in strong light, with tender, rounded figures. About 1617 he travelled to Spain with Crescenzi, where he gained some renown and helped spread Caravaggio's manner, before returning to Italy. His fruit and grape pictures, such as the Basket of Fruit of about 1620 in the Metropolitan Museum, show a sensitive eye for texture. He died in Viterbo in 1625, and his reputation grew markedly in the late twentieth century.","q2886107":"Bartolomeo degli Erri was a painter of Modena, active in the third quarter of the fifteenth century, and a brother of the painters Agnolo and Bartolomeo Bonascia, who together are considered key figures of the Quattrocento in Modena. He probably contributed to the large Coronation of the Virgin triptych in the Galleria Estense, dated between 1462 and 1466, which is mainly attributed to his brother Agnolo. A series of small panels showing scenes from the lives of saints comes from three dismembered altarpieces made between 1466 and 1474 for the church of San Domenico in Modena, and the Metropolitan Museum's Saint Dominic Resuscitating Napoleone Orsini and Saint Thomas Aquinas Aided by Saints Peter and Paul belong with these. He seems to have died young, around 1482, and his style reflects the art of nearby Ferrara.","q2013867":"Bartolomeo di Giovanni, who died in 1501, was a Florentine painter whose works were first grouped by Bernard Berenson under the name Alunno di Domenico, the pupil of Domenico, because the painter had executed the predella of Domenico Ghirlandaio's Adoration of the Magi of 1488 in the Ospedale degli Innocenti. Archival research later gave his real name. He also collaborated with Botticelli. He specialised in narrative panels: small predella scenes, and the panels for wedding chests and wainscoting that were then in demand in Florence for the homes of the wealthy, painting mythological and biblical stories in bright, clear colours with many small figures. He also painted altarpieces, such as the Madonna and Child with Four Saints of 1498, now at Mount Holyoke College. The Met's Trinity is a devotional work.","q2886111":"Bartolomeo di Tommaso, also called Bartolomeo da Foligno, was born in Foligno about 1400 and died in 1453 or 1454. He was a painter of the Umbro-Sienese school and was active by 1425. From 1425 to 1442 he worked in Ancona, then returned to Foligno with his wife Onofria, the sister of the painter Pierantonio Mezzastris. In 1442 he is documented at Norcia, working in the choir of Sant'Agostino with a team of painters, among them Andrea de Litio, and the administration of Pope Nicholas V asked him to work in Rome. A Virgin and Saints of 1430 was painted for San Salvatore in Foligno. The Betrayal of Christ in the Metropolitan Museum, of about 1445 to 1450, is a narrative panel in the late Gothic tradition of central Italy.","q721166":"Bartolomeo Manfredi was born at Ostiano, near Cremona, in 1582 and worked mainly in Rome, where he died in 1622. He was one of the closest followers of Caravaggio and is sometimes said to have devised a new type of picture based on his master's: scenes of everyday life, such as soldiers, gamblers, musicians and fortune tellers, painted with strong light and shadow and half-length figures, which were popular with collectors. The German writer Joachim von Sandrart, who visited Rome in the 1630s, called this the Manfredi method, and it spread to northern painters in Rome, including the Utrecht Caravaggisti. His religious pictures, such as the Met's Saints Peter and Paul, have the same strong shadows and sturdy figures. His career was short, and few works are securely dated.","q809507":"Bartolomeo Montagna was born about 1450 as Bartolomeo Cincani and later took the name Montagna; he was the leading painter of Vicenza in his time, also working in Venice, Verona and Padua. He is famous for his many Madonnas, with soft figures and eccentric architecture of marble, and he was strongly influenced by Giovanni Bellini, in whose workshop he may have worked about 1470. Vasari called him a pupil of Mantegna, but this is widely doubted. He died in 1523. His son and pupil Benedetto Montagna was a productive engraver. The Metropolitan Museum has his Madonna Adoring the Child and a Saint Justina of Padua of the 1490s, a graceful standing figure of a saint. His clear colour and calm figures made him popular with the churches of the Veneto, and he painted many altarpieces in and around Vicenza.","q809520":"Bartolomeo Veneto was active from 1502 to 1531 and worked in Venice, the Venetian mainland and Lombardy. He studied under Gentile Bellini. Little is known of his life beyond his signatures, dates and inscriptions; his earliest dated work, a Virgin and Child of 1502, is signed half Venetian and half Cremonese, which suggests that he was connected to both places. He began with small devotional pictures, but as portraiture grew in fashion he turned to it, and his portraits became quite popular. Of about forty paintings accepted as his, only nine are inscribed with his name, and all are on wood. He seems to have received no public commissions, and later in life he gained many in northern Italy. His later work, especially that done on commission in Milan, shows the influence of Leonardo da Vinci.","q456971":"Bartolomeo Vivarini was born in Murano about 1432, the younger brother of Antonio Vivarini and the uncle of Alvise Vivarini, and he is known to have worked from 1450 to about 1499. He collaborated with Antonio in the 1450s, and then ran his own workshop in Venice, producing polyptychs and altarpieces for churches in Venice, the Veneto and the Marches and as far as Naples and Bari. He is said to have learned oil painting from Antonello da Messina, and to have produced the first oil painting made in Venice, in 1473. Most of his works, however, are in tempera, with a hard outline, good colour and figures of dignified and devout expression. As vivarino means goldfinch, he sometimes drew a goldfinch beside his signature. The Death of the Virgin is dated 1484.","q918859":"Marco Basaiti was born about 1470 and worked mainly in Venice, where he was a contemporary of Giovanni Bellini and Cima da Conegliano. His name appears in several spellings, and Vasari took Marco Basarini and Marco Basaiti for two painters, though they are the same man. There is little documentation about him besides his signatures and a guild ledger of 1530, which records him as a painter of figures, and he is thought to have died about then. His works are mainly portraits and religious subjects. Trained in the quattrocento manner, he began his career just as the new style of the sixteenth century appeared, and he had to adapt his style to keep up. His Madonna Adoring the Child dates from about 1520.","q346502":"Jacopo Bassano was born about 1510 in Bassano del Grappa, in the Veneto, and trained with his father, Francesco the Elder, and in Venice, where he saw the work of Titian, Bonifacio de' Pitati and the Mannerist prints of Parmigianino. He spent nearly all his life in his native town and ran a family workshop with his sons Francesco, Leandro and Gerolamo. His pictures show Biblical stories as rustic scenes, with shepherds, animals, tools and farm buildings painted with great detail, and he was one of the first painters to treat night scenes with torchlight and glowing colour. His Miraculous Draught of Fishes is dated 1545 and the Annunciation to the Shepherds about 1555 to 1560. His loose, brilliant brushwork was admired by Venetian painters. He died in Bassano in 1592.","q541859":"Jules Bastien-Lepage was born in 1848 in Damvillers, in Lorraine, and trained in Paris under Alexandre Cabanel, though his true schooling was the countryside of his youth. He made his name at the Salon of 1874 with a portrait of his grandfather, and went on to paint peasants out of doors with an unflinching, closely observed realism, as in Haymaking of 1877. Joan of Arc, exhibited at the Salon of 1880, shows a Lorraine peasant girl in a farm garden, hearing her saints in the air above her. It was bought by the American collector Erwin Davis and given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1889. Bastien-Lepage was widely imitated in Britain and America before his early death in Paris in 1884.","batcheller_frederick_stone":"Frederick Stone Batcheller was an American painter who lived from 1837 to 1889 and died at about fifty-two. Little is recorded of his life. He is represented here by a flower piece, Myosotis and Roses in a Vase, undated, in the National Gallery of Art. Myosotis is the botanical name for forget-me-nots, small blue flowers that were popular in nineteenth-century America as symbols of remembrance, and a vase of them with roses was a favourite subject for parlour pictures. Flower painting was one of the steadier lines of work for American painters of his generation, who sold to middle-class buyers. Without a documented career, nothing can safely be said of his training or of where he worked.","q505613":"Pompeo Batoni was born in Lucca in 1708, the son of a goldsmith, and moved to Rome in 1727, where he studied the antique and Raphael and learned from Sebastiano Conca. He worked at first as a painter of religious and mythological subjects, but from the 1740s he became the outstanding portraitist for the foreign visitors on the Grand Tour, painting more than two hundred, mostly British noblemen and gentlemen, standing in front of Roman ruins and antique statues, in clear, polished colour. Popes, emperors and princes also sat to him, including Benedict XIV, Joseph II and the Grand Duke Leopold of Tuscany. He died in Rome in 1787. The National Gallery's Portrait of a Gentleman, of about 1762, belongs to the years of his greatest fame, when Rome was at the centre of European travel.","battista_dossi":"Battista Dossi, born about 1490 and dead in 1548, was the younger brother of the more celebrated painter Dosso Dossi, and the two worked together as court painters of the Este family in Ferrara. Their partnership was so close that their hands are not always easy to tell apart, and Battista's own share is often seen as the more decorative: landscape settings, tapestry designs, and the ornamental frameworks that surrounded his brother's figures. They worked for Duke Alfonso I d'Este and for his son Ercole II, and also for the Duke of Urbino at the Villa Imperiale at Pesaro. After Dosso's death in 1542, Battista continued to work at court. The Holy Family with a Shepherd, of the early 1520s, now in Cleveland, is attributed to him.","q21497538":"Leila T. Bauman was an American painter, active from about 1855 to 1870, and almost nothing is known of her life. She is known to have come from Verona, New Jersey, near Newark, and may at some point have worked for the print publishers Currier and Ives. Only two oil paintings by her are known, both river landscapes in the Garbisch Collection at the National Gallery of Art: Geese in Flight and U.S. Mail Boat. Internal evidence dates them to the 1850s and suggests that they were inspired by views around New York City. Geese in Flight was shown in the inaugural exhibition of the National Museum of Women in the Arts in 1987. She is an example of the many nineteenth-century women painters whose names survive only through one or two works.","q207298":"Frédéric Bazille was born in Montpellier in 1841 into a wealthy Protestant family and went to Paris in 1862 to study painting, though he had begun to study medicine. In the studio of Charles Gleyre he met Monet, Renoir and Sisley, and, being comfortably off, he shared lodgings with them and helped them with money and with a place to work; he bought Monet's Women in the Garden. He painted figures in sunlight out of doors, such as Family Reunion, as well as still lifes and landscapes of his native Languedoc, like the Met's Porte de la Reine at Aigues-Mortes of 1867. He enlisted in the Zouaves at the outbreak of the Franco-Prussian War and was killed in action in November 1870, aged twenty-eight.","q21497617":"The Beardsley Limner is the name given by scholars to an unidentified American portrait painter who was active in the 1790s and is named, as limners often are, for a family whose portraits he painted. A limner, from an old word for an illuminator, was a portrait painter without formal training, usually self-taught and often travelling, who worked in the years before academies and photography. The National Gallery's two portraits, Girl in a Pink Dress and Charles Adams Wheeler, both of about 1790, show the typical qualities of the limner: a flat, clear outline, simplified faces, and careful attention to costume and detail. His real name has not been found, and his pictures are valued as vivid records of children and adults in the early republic.","q455712":"Domenico di Pace, called Beccafumi after his patron, was born near Siena in 1486; according to Vasari, the son of a farm worker, he was noticed drawing by the landowner Lorenzo Beccafumi, who took him up and gave him his name. He worked mainly in Siena, with a stay in Rome about 1510 to 1512, and became the city's leading painter of the sixteenth century and one of the last undiluted representatives of the Sienese school. He combined Mannerist distortion, glowing and often acid colour and strong light effects in altarpieces and frescoes. His most famous project is the marble pavement of Siena Cathedral, for which he drew many of the large scenes in black and white inlay from about 1519, and he also modelled bronze angels for the cathedral. He died in 1551. The National Gallery's Holy Family with Angels dates from the 1540s.","q21403137":"Francis A. Beckett was an American painter who lived from 1833 to 1913, and he is represented here by one picture, Blacksmith Shop, of about 1880, in the National Gallery of Art. Little is recorded of his life. The blacksmith's forge had long been a favourite subject in American art and popular literature, since Longfellow's poem The Village Blacksmith of 1840 made the smith a symbol of honest labour, and a picture of the shop, with its fire, anvil and workers, offered a painter a scene of warm light and a familiar everyday trade, at a time when factories were beginning to replace the old craftsman. Beyond the picture itself, nothing certain can be said of Beckett's training, patrons or career.","q48566":"Sir William Beechey was born at Burford, Oxfordshire, in 1753, was orphaned young, and was meant by his uncle for the law, but entered the Royal Academy Schools in 1772, where he is thought to have studied under Johan Zoffany. He worked in Norwich from 1782 to 1787, then in London, where a portrait of John Douglas, Bishop of Carlisle, shown in 1789, made his name. His adept, restrained portraits in the tradition of Reynolds suited the royal family: in 1793 he was commissioned to paint Queen Charlotte and became her portrait painter, and in 1798 he was knighted and elected to the Royal Academy after painting George III reviewing his troops. He painted Nelson and many officers. The National Gallery's portrait of Lieutenant-General Sir Thomas Picton, killed at Waterloo, dates from 1815 to 1817. He died in 1839.","q1857977":"Osias Beert was born around 1580, in Antwerp or possibly Kortrijk, and studied in Antwerp under the little-known Andries van Baesrode. He joined the city's guild of Saint Luke in 1602 and married in 1606. He is counted among the earliest and most influential still-life painters of Flanders, developing both the tabletop banquet piece with festive delicacies and the lavish bouquet in a Wan Li porcelain vase. His pictures show oysters, cheese, fruit, wine and sweets laid out on a table in careful rows. He also traded as a cork merchant, which suggests that painting alone did not bring a high income, and he lived in the modest shippers' quarter of Antwerp. He died about 1623, and his widow had to sell furniture and paintings to settle a debt. The Dishes with Oysters, Fruit, and Wine dates from about 1620 to 1624.","q421775":"Cornelis Bega was born in Haarlem in 1631 or 1632, the son of a sculptor and goldsmith, Pieter Begijn, and the illegitimate daughter of the painter Cornelis van Haarlem, and he adopted the name Bega when he began working professionally. He was a student of Adriaen van Ostade and painted similar genre scenes, usually small groups of peasants in a tavern or other interior, along with fanciful figures such as alchemists and astrologers, as in The Alchemist of 1663. In 1653 and 1654 he travelled through Germany, Switzerland and France with fellow painters Dirk Helmbreker, Vincent van der Vinne and Guillam Dubois, a journey recorded in van der Vinne's diary. In 1654 he joined the Haarlem Guild of Saint Luke. He died in Haarlem in 1664, reportedly of the plague, and was buried in his grandfather's grave.","q164688":"Bernardo Bellotto was the nephew and pupil of Canaletto, born in Venice in 1721 or 1722, and at the beginning of his career he painted crisp, bright views of Venice much like his uncle's, such as the Piazza San Marco of about 1740, now in Cleveland. He also travelled in northern Italy, painting country views such as Vaprio d'Adda of 1744. In 1747 he left for Dresden, where he became court painter to the King of Poland and Elector of Saxony, and he then worked in Vienna and Munich before settling in Warsaw in 1767. He used the name Canaletto in Germany and Poland. His views of Dresden, Pirna and Warsaw have a colder, sharper light than Canaletto's and are valued as historical records. He died in Warsaw in 1780.","q3080910":"Francesco Benaglio, whose original name was Francesco di Pietro della Biada, was born about 1432, the son of a grain merchant from Bergamo, and his family moved to Verona, where he is documented from 1456 until his death in 1492. He took the name Benaglio from a noble family of Bergamo. His first known work, a triptych in the church of San Bernardino in Verona of 1462 to 1463, was a prestigious commission, which suggests that he was already well known. In 1475 he and another painter were jailed for four months for painting, one night, obscene or defamatory frescoes on the facade of a palace, apparently at the instigation of the owners' enemies. His surviving Madonnas show striking perspective foreshortening, rigid geometric drawing and monumental figures against stylised landscapes. The Madonna and Child dates from the late 1460s.","q816847":"Benedetto Luti was born in Florence in 1666 and trained under Anton Domenico Gabbiani before moving to Rome in 1690. There he gained the patronage of Grand Duke Cosimo III de' Medici, who admired his pastel portraits, and Luti was among the first to treat pastel as a medium for finished pictures rather than preparatory studies. Roman families such as the Colonna, Pallavicini, Barberini and Odescalchi employed him, and he also painted in oil and fresco, including for the Lateran basilica. He joined the Accademia di San Luca in 1694, was knighted in 1720 and elected its head. He also traded as an art dealer and ran a school of drawing, where his pupils included Panini, William Kent and the van Loo brothers. The Met's Study of a Boy in a Blue Jacket and Study of a Girl in Red are dated 1717. He died in 1724.","q968357":"Jean-Joseph Benjamin-Constant was born in Paris in 1845, grew up in Toulouse, and trained there and then in Paris under Alexandre Cabanel. In 1872 he travelled to Morocco with a diplomatic mission, and the colours, costumes and architecture he saw there fed his Orientalist paintings for years: harem scenes, palace interiors and processions in sumptuous, jewel-like colour. The Favorite of the Emir, of about 1879, is an example, a woman reclining in an interior; it belongs to the United States Naval Academy Museum, which lent it to the Gallery. He later became a successful portraitist and decorative painter, with portraits of Queen Victoria and Pope Leo XIII among his commissions. He died in Paris in 1902. His taste fell from favour for much of the twentieth century.","q20852879":"Benjamin Trott was an American miniature painter who lived from about 1770 to 1843. The Metropolitan Museum has a dozen of his portraits on ivory, painted between about 1798 and 1825, and among the sitters are Philadelphians such as Mrs. Nathaniel Chapman, born Rebekah Biddle, and Benjamin Chew Wilcocks, besides a number of men and women whose names have not been recorded. Miniatures of this kind were painted in watercolour on thin slices of ivory, usually from life, and were worn as jewellery, kept in cases, or exchanged between families and lovers. Little is firmly recorded of his training and career, and he is known today mainly through these surviving works.","q313498":"Benjamin West was born near Springfield, Pennsylvania, in 1738, to a Quaker family, and was painting portraits in Philadelphia as a young man. In 1760 patrons helped him to travel to Italy, and in 1763 he settled in London. There he painted The Death of General Wolfe in 1770, showing a modern military subject in contemporary dress, which was a sensation. He was a founding member of the Royal Academy in 1768, became history painter to George III from 1772, and succeeded Reynolds as president of the Academy in 1792. His studio in London was the training ground for many American painters, among them Charles Willson Peale, Gilbert Stuart, John Trumbull, Mather Brown, Washington Allston and Samuel F. B. Morse. His reputation faded after he died in London in 1820, but he is now valued again as a pioneer of history painting. Hagar and Ishmael was begun in 1776.","q248615":"Benozzo Gozzoli was born in Florence in 1421 and trained as an assistant to Fra Angelico, with whom he worked in Rome and at Orvieto; he is also recorded as an assistant to the sculptor Lorenzo Ghiberti on the doors of the Baptistery. His fame rests on the frescoes of the Procession of the Magi in the chapel of the Medici palace in Florence, painted for Piero de' Medici about 1459 to 1461, a glittering pageant in which members of the Medici circle ride in the Magi's train through a landscape of castles and hunting scenes. He later painted a cycle of the life of Saint Augustine at San Gimignano and a long series of Old Testament scenes for the Camposanto in Pisa, on which he worked from 1468 until 1484. He died in Pistoia in 1497. His charming, narrative manner is much loved today.","q818396":"Benvenuto di Giovanni was born in Siena in 1436 and was first recorded as a painter in 1453; he worked chiefly in Siena until his death about 1518. Besides panel paintings he made choral miniatures, designs for the cathedral pavement and frescoes. His style changed markedly in the 1480s, and he was influenced by several artists of his time. His son Girolamo di Benvenuto also became a painter, and the work of father and son is sometimes confused. Dated and signed works of his survive across a period of forty-three years. The National Gallery of Art has several of his panels, among them an Adoration of the Magi of about 1470 to 1475 and a group of scenes from the Passion that are probably dated 1491. He died about 1518, at about eighty.","q711737":"Nicolaes Berchem was born in Haarlem in 1620 and became one of the most esteemed and prolific Dutch painters of pastoral landscapes. The son of the still-life painter Pieter Claesz, he belonged to the second generation of Dutch Italianate landscape painters, who travelled to Italy or aspired to, and brought home sketchbooks of ruins and country scenes. His idyllic Italian views, with hills, cliffs and trees in a golden light and with peasants, herdsmen and cattle in the foreground, were in great demand, and he is credited with about 850 paintings, along with some 80 etchings and 500 drawings. He also painted figures for other landscape painters, among them Jacob van Ruisdael, Meindert Hobbema and Jan Hackaert. He died in Amsterdam in 1683. The View of an Italian Port dates from the early 1660s.","q7789":"Ambrogio Bergognone, also called Ambrogio da Fossano, was born about 1453, apparently at Fossano in Piedmont, and worked in and near Milan. Though nearly a contemporary of Leonardo da Vinci, he painted in a manner closer to the pre-Renaissance Lombard art of Vincenzo Foppa and Bernardino Zenale, with soft, calm figures and clear colour. His nickname is attributed to his affinity with the Burgundian school. He is best known for his work at the Certosa di Pavia, the Carthusian monastery, where he worked for eight years, partly with his brother Bernardino, on designs for choir stalls and on altarpieces. In 1494 he returned to Milan and worked at San Satiro. He died about 1523. Christ Risen from the Tomb is in the National Gallery of Art, and the Metropolitan Museum has an Assumption of the Virgin.","q15795":"Berlinghiero was an Italian painter of Lucca who is documented from 1228 and died between 1236 and 1242. He painted in the Italo-Byzantine manner of the early thirteenth century, with gold backgrounds and solemn, flat figures, and he was the father of the painters Barone, Bonaventura and Marco Berlinghieri. His actual name is not certain: he is known from the inscription Berlingerius me pinxit on a crucifix, which is the basis for attributing other works to him. He is also mentioned in a parchment of March 1228 among the residents of Lucca who swore to keep the peace with Pisa after a five-year war. The Madonna and Child in the Metropolitan Museum, possibly of the 1230s, is attributed to him. He is among the earliest named painters of Tuscany.","bernard_gaillot":"Bernard Gaillot was a French painter of historical and religious subjects, born at Versailles in 1780, who died in Paris in 1847. He was a pupil of Jacques-Louis David. His chief pictures, produced between 1817 and 1831, were mainly religious: Cornelia, Saint Martin for the Val-de-Grâce, the Conversion of Saint Augustine of 1819 and the Dream of Saint Monica of 1822 for Notre-Dame-des-Victoires, Saint Louis Visiting the Holy Sepulchre for the sacristy of Saint-Denis, and altarpieces for churches at Sens, Lille and Eu. They show the clear drawing and smooth finish of David's school applied to the church commissions that followed the Restoration. The Woman Sleeping in a Landscape with a Letter, of about 1800, in Cleveland, is a work from his youth.","q4893365":"Bernard Lens was born in London in 1682, the third of the name, and became the best-known of a family of artists. He was an English painter of portrait miniatures, serving as the miniature painter at the courts of George I and George II, and as instructor in miniature painting, then called limning, to Prince William and the princesses Mary and Louisa. He also acted as a consultant in fine arts to upper-class families. He copied old master paintings, and also pictures by contemporary artists, in miniature, so that collectors could possess small versions of famous works; the Met's copy after Rubens's portrait of Helena Fourment and her son Frans is of this kind. He died in London in 1740.","q454678":"Bernard van Orley was born in Brussels between 1487 and 1491 and died there in 1541. He never visited Italy, but he belonged to the Romanists, the Flemish painters influenced by Italian Renaissance painting, in his case especially by Raphael. A versatile artist, he served as a kind of commissioner of the arts for the Brussels town council and as court artist of the Habsburg rulers, painting portraits and religious scenes and designing tapestries and, late in his life, stained glass. He concentrated on the design and left the execution of his works largely to others, as Raphael's workshop did, so that the quality of the surviving works varies. His tapestries, such as the Hunts of Maximilian, are among the finest of the age. The Met's Virgin and Child with Angels dates from about 1518.","q822891":"Bernardino Campi, born at Cremona in 1522, belonged to the extended Campi family, which owned the main artistic studios of the city, though he was only a distant relative of Giulio and Antonio Campi. His influences included Cremonese painters such as Camillo Boccaccino and artists from neighbouring regions such as Correggio, Parmigianino and Giulio Romano. He worked in Cremona, Milan and Reggio Emilia. He is known as one of the teachers of Sofonisba Anguissola, whom he taught in Cremona, and of Giovanni Battista Trotti. He made several sets of copies of Titian's Eleven Caesars and decorated the Palazzo del Giardino at Sabbioneta for Vespasiano Gonzaga. He died in 1591. The Met's Portrait of a Woman dates from the late 1560s.","q21852646":"Bernardino da Genoa is known here only through one panel, a Madonna and Child with Angels, dated 1515, in the Metropolitan Museum. The museum gives him no dates, and the name suggests that he came from Genoa or worked there, but nothing else about his training or career is certain. In the early sixteenth century Genoa was a rich seaport, with painters from Lombardy and the Netherlands working there and the city's merchants buying altarpieces and devotional pictures for churches and for their houses. A Madonna and Child with Angels was one of the commonest subjects for a private chapel or a bedchamber, and many are by artists whose names survive only in a signature or an old inventory. Nothing more can safely be said.","q2405424":"Bernardino de' Conti was an Italian painter, apparently born at Castelseprio, in Lombardy, in 1465, who is recorded in Milan between 1496 and 1522 and died around 1525. He is reported to have been the son of an obscure painter named Baldassarre, and he worked in the circle of Leonardo da Vinci in Milan. He is best known for his oil portraits, which show Milanese noblemen, churchmen and ladies in half-length against dark backgrounds, with soft modelling, and to a lesser extent for devotional pictures such as the Madonna and Child. The critic Bernard Berenson argued that La Belle Ferronnière, long given to Leonardo, was really by Bernardino de' Conti. Little is known of his life after 1522. The Madonna and Child in the Metropolitan Museum is a late example.","bernardino_detti":"Bernardino Detti was an Italian painter who lived from 1498 to 1571, and his Portrait of a Young Man is in the Metropolitan Museum. Little is firmly recorded of his training and career. His long life spanned the High Renaissance and the age of Mannerism, and portraits of young men with a plain background and the sitter looking directly out were a popular type in sixteenth-century Italy, kept as records of family and friendship. The Met's Portrait of a Young Man is undated. Nothing certain can be said of the sitter's name or of the circumstances in which the picture was painted.","q338410":"Bernardino Fungai was a Sienese painter, born in 1460, who died about 1516, and his work marks the transition from late Gothic painting to the early Renaissance in the Sienese school. He kept a fairly archaic style in his pictures, which are mainly devotional: Madonnas, saints, and altarpieces for churches in Siena and its territory, with clear outlines, bright colour and gold. The city was proud of its painting tradition, and artists like Fungai answered to the conservative taste of its churches and confraternities. The Nativity in the Metropolitan Museum, probably painted after 1500, is a work of this kind. He worked in Siena throughout his career and was active into the sixteenth century.","bernardino_luini":"Bernardino Luini was born around 1480 at Dumenza on Lake Maggiore, near Luino, from which his name is taken, and worked in Milan and Lombardy. He painted in the circle of Leonardo da Vinci and took from Leonardo as much as his native roots allowed; many of his pictures were once attributed to Leonardo. He is known especially for graceful women with elongated eyes, a type the writer Vladimir Nabokov called Luinesque. He painted a great deal in fresco, in the churches of San Maurizio in Milan, the sanctuary at Saronno and Santa Maria degli Angeli in Lugano, and in private houses, and he also made panel paintings such as Salome with the Head of Saint John the Baptist and the Virgin and Child. He died in 1532.","bernardo_cavallino":"Bernardo Cavallino was born in Naples in 1616 and died in 1656, probably in the plague that devastated the city. He is regarded as one of the most original painters in Naples in the first half of the seventeenth century. It is said that he trained with Massimo Stanzione, was a friend of Andrea Vaccaro and was influenced by Van Dyck, and his pictures lie between Caravaggio and Murillo, with shadow touched with theatrical sweetness and a posed ecstasy. Little is known of his background and training. Of eighty paintings attributed to him, fewer than ten are signed, and he worked through private dealers and collectors whose records have not survived. His small, silvery pictures of biblical and classical stories, such as the Adoration of the Shepherds, appeal for their refined colour. He is now much admired.","q366562":"Bernardo Daddi was the leading Florentine painter of the generation after Giotto, active in Florence from about 1312 until his death in 1348, probably in the plague of that year. He ran a busy workshop that produced altarpieces and small devotional triptychs for private chapels and bedchambers, painted on gilded panels with graceful, softly modelled figures and clear colour in the tradition of Giotto. He also painted frescoes in the Pulci and Berardi chapel in Santa Croce, and the tabernacle in Orsanmichele, finished in 1347. The National Gallery's Crucifixion (about 1320 to 1325), Saint Paul and a Group of Worshippers (1333) and Madonna and Child with Saints and Angels (about 1345) show his work from early to late. His small devotional pictures were widely copied.","q437724":"Bernardo Strozzi was born in Genoa about 1581 and trained with minor local painters and then with the Sienese Pietro Sorri, who led him toward naturalism. In 1598 he joined the Capuchins and was called il Cappuccino, the Capuchin; he later received permission to leave his habit for that of a secular priest and was known as il Prete Genovese, the Genoese priest. He left the monastery to support his mother and, after a dispute with the Capuchins that led to his imprisonment, in 1630 or 1631 he moved to Venice, where he worked until he died in 1644. He became a main founder of Baroque painting in Venice, with rich glowing colour and broad, energetic brushstrokes, painting religious scenes, portraits, still life and genre. The Healing of Tobit is a work of about 1625.","q259393":"Bernhard Keil was born at Helsingør, Denmark, in 1624 and studied first with the Danish painter Morten Steenwinkel. In 1642 he went to Amsterdam, where he was a pupil of Rembrandt until 1644, then worked with the dealer Hendrick Uylenburgh and ran a workshop of his own from 1649 to 1651. In 1656 he travelled to Rome, where he is registered until his death in 1687, and he also worked in Bergamo and Venice. Italians called him Monsù Bernardo. He painted genre scenes, portraits and religious pictures in a Rembrandtesque manner, with warm shadows and low-life figures, and he was influenced by Jan Lievens. In turn he influenced painters in Lombardy and Venice, among them Pietro Bellotti and Antonio Cifrondi. The Lacemaker is dated to about 1665.","q637420":"Bernhard Strigel was born at Memmingen about 1461, the most important of a family of artists established in that Swabian town, and was probably a pupil of Zeitblom at Ulm. He stood in high favour with the Emperor Maximilian I, in whose service he repeatedly went to Augsburg, Innsbruck and Vienna, and he painted portraits of the emperor and his family, among them a group portrait of Maximilian with his family. His religious paintings include altar wings with scenes from the Life of the Virgin, now in Berlin, and ten pictures of the Genealogy of Christ in Nuremberg, but his portraits, which are detailed, skilful and luminous in colour, are more admired. Examples include a portrait of Conrad Rehlinger of 1517 and the Met's Portrait of a Woman. He died in 1528.","q105320":"Berthe Morisot was born in Bourges in 1841 and grew up in a cultivated Parisian family. With her sister Edma she studied landscape under Corot, and she first showed at the Salon in 1864. She became a close friend of Édouard Manet, whose brother Eugène she married in 1874, and she was one of the founders of the Impressionist group, exhibiting in seven of its eight exhibitions and missing only that of 1879, when her daughter Julie was born. Her subjects were mostly women and children in gardens and domestic interiors, painted in light, feathery brushwork with a silvery palette, such as Young Woman Knitting and The Pink Dress. She sold her work to a small circle of admirers. She died in Paris in 1895, and Mallarmé helped organise a memorial exhibition in 1896.","q331884":"Jean-Victor Bertin was born in Paris in 1767, the son of a master wigmaker, and was the pupil of the landscape painter Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes and of Gabriel-François Doyen. A painter of historical landscape, inspired by Italy and known for the minute detail of his classical style, he exhibited regularly at the Paris Salon from 1793, won a gold medal in 1808, and received works commissioned for the palaces of Trianon and Fontainebleau between 1811 and 1817. Prestigious amateurs such as the Duke of Berry and the banker Jacques Laffitte collected his work. He was a teacher whose pupils included Michallon, Cogniet and Camille Corot. He was granted the Legion of Honour in 1822, and in old age had money troubles. He died in 1842.","q2311437":"Biagio d'Antonio Tucci was born in Florence in 1446 and was active in Florence, Faenza and Rome. It is not known with whom he trained, but his early style reflects Filippo Lippi, Verrocchio and Domenico Ghirlandaio. By 1472 he was in partnership with Jacopo del Sellaio, with whom he painted the Morelli-Nerli wedding chests, and he continued to produce painted chests throughout his career, like Sellaio. By 1476 he was running workshops in both Florence and Faenza, and the Ragnoli Altarpiece for Faenza was his first work there. In 1481 and 1482 he assisted Cosimo Rosselli on the frescoes of the Sistine Chapel in Rome. His paintings show Florentine innovations and, in their decoration, an influence from early Netherlandish painting. He died in 1516. The Triumph of Camillus is a cassone panel of about 1470 to 1475.","q856749":"Bicci di Lorenzo was born in Florence in 1373, the son of the painter Lorenzo di Bicci, and joined his father's workshop; he married in 1418 and was registered in the painters' guild in 1424. His son Neri di Bicci later took over the workshop. He received many important commissions, mostly altarpieces and frescoes, among them apostles for the cathedral workshop and works for Santa Maria Nuova. According to Vasari he painted for the Medici. His panels with scenes from the life of Saint Nicholas, of 1433 to 1435, are in the Met. In 1447 or so he began painting the chancel of San Francesco in Arezzo, but at his death in 1452 he had completed only the four Evangelists in the vault and the arch with the Last Judgement, and Piero della Francesca was called in to finish the cycle, which became the Legend of the True Cross.","q3166488":"Joseph Bidauld, properly Jean-Joseph-Xavier Bidauld, was born in Carpentras in 1758 and died near Paris in 1846. He learned first from his elder brother Jean-Pierre-Xavier, a painter of landscape and still life, then worked in Paris and from about 1785 to 1790 in Italy, where he sketched the Roman countryside, Naples and the mountains of the Abruzzi. Lake Fucino, dated about 1789, comes from that journey. Back in France he became a leading painter of calm, carefully composed landscapes in the classical tradition of Claude, shown at the Salon and bought by official and private patrons. He was elected to the Académie des Beaux-Arts and long respected, though the Romantic landscapists who followed made his clear, orderly manner seem old-fashioned.","q598030":"George Caleb Bingham was born in Virginia in 1811 and grew up in Missouri, where he was apprenticed to a cabinetmaker and taught himself to paint portraits. He studied briefly at the Pennsylvania Academy in 1838 and is famous for his pictures of life on the Missouri and Mississippi rivers: fur traders, flatboatmen, raftsmen and card players, painted with a clear, calm, almost frieze-like order and a light that seems to hang in the air. The American Art-Union bought and engraved his pictures, making them widely known. He also painted election scenes, such as The County Election (1852), and was himself a Missouri politician, state treasurer and adjutant general. He died at Kansas City in 1879. Fur Traders Descending the Missouri, of 1845, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q84106":"Peter Binoit was a German still-life painter who worked in Frankfurt am Main and Cologne in the early Baroque period. He was born about 1590 and died on 14 May 1632. The National Gallery's Still Life with Iris and Still Life with Tulips are both dated 1623 and are small flower pieces in the manner of the Flemish and Dutch painters who had taught Frankfurt its taste for such pictures: a vase or glass on a ledge, with flowers painted one by one with precise detail, sometimes with insects. Frankfurt's merchants, many of them immigrants from the Netherlands, were enthusiastic buyers of these pictures. He is among the earliest German painters of the genre. Beyond a few works and these dates, little is recorded of his life.","q175370":"Thomas Birch was born in Warwickshire, England, in 1779 and came to the United States in 1794. He assisted his father, William Birch, in preparing the book Birch's Views of Philadelphia, a set of twenty-nine engravings published in 1800, with subscribers that included President John Adams and Vice-President Thomas Jefferson. Thomas's first major painting was a view of Philadelphia from the Treaty Elm in Kensington, engraved in 1804. He painted portraits until about 1807, then took up marine painting, which became his chief subject: views of ships, harbours, bridges and lighthouses around New York and Philadelphia, and naval battles of the War of 1812. He was America's first notable ship portraitist, and was widely copied. He died in 1851. View of the Delaware near Philadelphia is dated 1831.","bisschop_cornelis":"Cornelis Bisschop was born in Dordrecht in 1630 and about 1650 studied with Ferdinand Bol in Amsterdam. By 1653 he was back in Dordrecht, where he married. According to Houbraken he was the first to paint trompe-l'oeil carved wooden panels so skilfully that they became popular. He painted historical allegories, portraits, still lifes and genre scenes. He was invited to paint for the Danish court, but died unexpectedly in 1674, leaving a wife and eleven children. Two of his sons, Abraham and Jacobus, and three of his daughters became painters, and they were his students, along with Margaretha van Godewijk, who wrote an emblem about his self-portrait with a curtain, which illustrates the story of Zeuxis. Woman with a Pearl Necklace is of the 1650s.","q3184614":"Joseph Blackburn was an English portrait painter whose origins and training are not recorded. He is first documented in Bermuda in 1752 and was in New England by 1754, working in Newport and Boston, then in Portsmouth, New Hampshire, from about 1760 to 1763, before returning to England in 1764. He brought a fashionable rococo manner to the colonies: pale, smooth faces, easy poses, and silks and satins painted with a bright, glossy touch. His sitters, among them Hannah Wentworth Atkinson and Samuel Cutts and his wife, were merchants, officials and their families. His elegance gave local painters, above all the young John Singleton Copley, a model to learn from. His later years are poorly documented, and his best-known work remains the American portraits.","q41513":"William Blake was born in London in 1757 and was apprenticed at fourteen to the engraver James Basire, then studied at the Royal Academy Schools. A poet, painter and printmaker, he developed his own method of relief etching, with which he printed and coloured his illuminated books, among them Songs of Innocence and of Experience, The Marriage of Heaven and Hell and Jerusalem. He also made watercolours and tempera paintings of biblical, Miltonic and visionary subjects, many for his steady patron Thomas Butts, who bought The Last Supper of 1799 and Job and His Daughters of 1799 and 1800. Late in life the young painter John Linnell commissioned Blake's Dante drawings and Job engravings. Blake was little known in his lifetime. He died in London in 1827, and Alexander Gilchrist's biography of 1863 began his rise to fame.","q1353482":"Ralph Albert Blakelock was born in New York City in 1847, the son of an English-born physician. He began medical studies at the Free Academy but gave them up in 1864, and between 1869 and 1872 he travelled alone through the American West, spending time among Native Americans. Largely self-taught, he painted landscapes and scenes of Indian life from his notebooks and memories, and his most characteristic pictures are moonlit landscapes with glowing skies and thick, heavily worked, glazed surfaces. He exhibited at the National Academy of Design. He married Cora Bailey in 1877, and with nine children he found it hard to make a living, selling paintings at very low prices. He was committed to a hospital in 1899 and stayed there for most of the next twenty years, while his pictures grew in value. He died in 1919.","q721349":"Boccaccio Boccaccino was born in Ferrara about 1467 and probably studied there under Domenico Panetti. Few facts of his life are known. He worked in Venice, Ferrara and especially Cremona, where he founded a school and was the master of Garofalo. His most celebrated work is the series of frescoes in the nave of Cremona Cathedral, painted from 1506 to 1519, which show the Birth of the Virgin and other scenes of her life, and which Altobello Melone later carried on. His remaining works, among them the Marriage of Saint Catherine in the Accademia in Venice, show rich drapery, varied colour and a harmony of landscape. Several works once given to Perugino, Pinturicchio and Garofalo have been reassigned to him. His son and pupil Camillo Boccaccino also painted in Cremona. He died there about 1525.","bohemian_painter":"Bohemian Painter is not a single named artist but a label for a painter active in Prague in the middle of the fourteenth century, whose name is unknown. The Metropolitan Museum's Virgin and Child Enthroned, of about 1345 to 1350, dates from the years when Prague, the capital of the Kingdom of Bohemia, was becoming one of the great artistic centres of Europe under the Luxembourg dynasty, and Charles IV, king of Bohemia from 1346 and soon Holy Roman Emperor, was gathering painters, goldsmiths and builders at his court. Panels of this period show the Virgin enthroned with the Child in gold and rich colour, in a courtly Gothic style that combines Italian and French influences. Nothing more can be said of the painter's name, training or birthplace.","q715909":"Louis-Léopold Boilly was born in 1761 at La Bassée, near Lille, and trained in Douai and Arras before moving to Paris in 1785, where he first made a name with gallant, sometimes risqué scenes of lovers and domestic intrigue. In 1794, during the Terror, he painted the Triumph of Marat to answer a denunciation for immorality, and he survived. In the following decades he became the most observant painter of Parisian life under the Directory and the Empire, with crowd scenes, trompe l'oeil, small genre pictures and a great many small portraits. The Public Viewing David's Coronation at the Louvre, of 1810, is a lively record of fashionable Parisians looking at the huge picture. He died in Paris in 1845.","q374039":"Ferdinand Bol was baptised at Dordrecht in 1616 and went to Amsterdam to study with Rembrandt, in whose workshop he worked in the later 1630s. By the early 1640s he was painting on his own, and for the next two decades he was among the most successful painters in the city, making portraits, regent group portraits and history paintings in a manner that grew lighter and more elegant than Rembrandt's. He painted large classical scenes for the Amsterdam town hall, now the Royal Palace. In 1653 he married a wealthy woman, and after a second rich marriage in 1669 he seems to have given up painting. He died in Amsterdam in 1680. The Metropolitan Museum holds a Portrait of a Woman of 1642 and Petronella Elias with a Basket of Fruit, of 1657, a portrait of a child.","q442528":"Giovanni Antonio Boltraffio was born in Milan in 1466 or 1467 and, according to Vasari, came from an aristocratic family. He worked in the studio of Leonardo da Vinci in Milan, and together with Bernardino Luini he is regarded as one of the strongest artistic personalities to come out of it. His portraits and Madonnas combine Leonardo's soft shading and gently turned heads with a cool, polished finish, and several were once given to Leonardo himself. His signed altarpiece for the poet Girolamo Casio, painted about 1500 for a church in Bologna and now in the Louvre, is his most important surviving work. The Portrait of a Youth in the National Gallery of Art was painted about 1495 to 1498. He died in 1516.","q21497718":"Charles V. Bond was an American painter who lived from 1825 to 1866 and died at about forty-one. Little is recorded of his life. He is represented here by Still Life: Fruit, Bird, and Dwarf Pear Tree, dated 1856, in the National Gallery of Art. Fruit pieces were among the most popular pictures in mid-nineteenth-century America, and they hung in parlours and dining rooms as symbols of abundance. The dwarf pear, a small tree grafted onto quince roots, was then popular in American gardens, so a picture of one with ripe fruit and a bird was a subject that would have appealed to buyers who were also amateur gardeners. Nothing more can safely be said of his training, patrons or other work.","q30131758":"Horace Bonham was born in 1835 and spent most of his life in York, Pennsylvania, apart from a grand tour of Europe. The well-educated son of a judge, he passed the bar but never practised law, working instead as a newspaper editor and, during the Civil War, as a revenue agent, before taking up the study of oil painting. His most famous work is Nearing the Issue at the Cockpit, of 1879, a crowded genre scene of men watching a cockfight, which entered the Corcoran Gallery in Washington. Most of his other work was never sold commercially and is held at a historic house museum in York, with a few pieces in private collections. He remains little known. He died in 1892.","q744747":"Bonifazio Veronese, born Bonifazio de' Pitati, was a Venetian Renaissance painter, born in Verona in 1487, who died in Venice in 1553. His work was an important influence on the younger generation of Venetian painters, in particular Andrea Schiavone and Jacopo Tintoretto. He ran a large workshop that produced altarpieces and large decorative canvases for churches, confraternities and private houses, in a style that blended Titian's colour with a taste for crowded, picturesque, sometimes rustic scenes, as in the Legend of the Infant Servius Tullius, in the Metropolitan Museum. The Met's Madonna and Child with Saints is of the kind called a sacra conversazione, in which saints share one space with the Virgin. He lived in Titian's shadow, but his rich colour and lively narrative held their own.","q380395":"Richard Parkes Bonington was born at Arnold, near Nottingham, in 1802. His family moved to Calais, then to Paris, when he was fourteen, and he trained in the Louvre and in the studio of Baron Gros. He made his name with coastal scenes of the Channel, painted in oil and watercolour with a low horizon, a wide sky and a brilliant handling of light and air, and later with small historical scenes in a free, troubadour manner. He was a friend of Delacroix, with whom he shared a studio, and he visited Venice in 1826, painting views of the Grand Canal. His light, quick technique influenced both French and British painters. He died of tuberculosis in London in 1828, aged only twenty-five. The Grand Canal is dated 1826 to 1827.","q170259":"Léon Bonnat was born in Bayonne in 1833 and spent his boyhood in Madrid, where he studied the Spanish masters at the Prado; Ribera and Velázquez left a lasting mark on his dark, solid style. He later trained in Paris under Léon Cogniet and in Rome, and in 1868 he travelled to Egypt and the Holy Land with Jean-Léon Gérôme, an experience that produced pictures such as An Egyptian Peasant Woman and Her Child. He became the most sought-after portraitist of the Third Republic, painting Victor Hugo, Adolphe Thiers and, in 1880, the New York collector John Taylor Johnston, and a celebrated teacher whose pupils included Thomas Eakins and Toulouse-Lautrec. He directed the École des Beaux-Arts from 1905 and died in 1922; his collection forms the museum in Bayonne.","q20899718":"William Bonnell was an American painter who lived from 1804 to 1865. Little is recorded of his life. The National Gallery of Art has his portrait Clement Bonnell, painted about 1825, when he was about twenty-one. The sitter shares the painter's surname, which suggests a family portrait, but the relationship is not recorded. In the 1820s many American portraits were painted by young men with little academic training, working in their home towns or travelling to find sitters, for modest sums, and the pictures were kept by the family as records of a husband, a father or a child. Such pictures are now valued for their direct, unselfconscious look. Beyond this, nothing certain can be said of Bonnell's training or career.","q346808":"Gerard ter Borch the Younger, as the Metropolitan Museum calls him to tell him from his father of the same name, was born at Zwolle in 1617 and settled at Deventer, where he died in 1681. He painted portraits and small, polished scenes of well-to-do domestic life: women writing letters, at their toilet, or playing the theorbo with a gentleman caller, in quiet rooms with calm light and silk and satin gowns rendered with extraordinary skill. His Paternal Admonition so impressed Goethe that a living picture of it appears in his novel Elective Affinities. The Museum owns five pictures, among them Curiosity, of about 1660 to 1662, the Van Moerkerken Family, of about 1653 to 1654, and A Young Woman at Her Toilet with a Maid, of about 1650 to 1651.","q366081":"Paris Bordone was born in Treviso in 1500 and had moved to Venice by late adolescence. According to Vasari he was apprenticed briefly, and unhappily, to Titian, and he kept a streak of Mannerist complexity and provincial vigour of his own. In the 1520s he painted Holy Families and altarpieces for churches in Lombardy, among them one for San Agostino in Crema. His masterpiece, of 1534 to 1535, is the large canvas of the Fisherman Presenting the Ring to the Doge, painted for the Scuola di San Marco and now in the Accademia in Venice, which shows a Venetian legend in a grand palace setting with steep perspective and bright colour. He also painted smaller cabinet pieces of half-length figures from mythology and religion. He died in Venice in 1571. The Baptism of Christ dates from about 1535 to 1540.","q130531":"Hieronymus Bosch, whose real name was Jheronimus van Aken, was born in 's-Hertogenbosch, in the Duchy of Brabant, about 1450, into a family of painters, and he spent all his life there, taking his professional name from the town. He joined the Brotherhood of Our Lady, a religious confraternity, in 1486, and he married a woman of property, which gave him a comfortable standing. His paintings of hell, sin and folly, among them the Garden of Earthly Delights and the Haywain, are crowded with monsters, hybrids and strange symbols that have fascinated viewers ever since. Philip the Fair commissioned a Last Judgment from him in 1504, and Philip II of Spain later collected his work with passion, which is why so much of it is in Madrid. He died in 1516. The New York Adoration of the Magi is dated about 1475.","q459665":"Ambrosius Bosschaert was born in Antwerp in 1573 and spent most of his career in Middelburg, where his family had moved because of the threat of religious persecution. He signed his flower pieces with the monogram AB, the B set inside the A, and painted symmetrical bouquets of tulips and roses, with scientific accuracy, in small dimensions, normally on copper. His brother-in-law Balthasar van der Ast lived and worked in his workshop and accompanied him on his travels, which took him to Amsterdam, Bergen op Zoom, Utrecht and Breda. At the time of his death in 1621 in The Hague he was at work on an important commission there for a flower piece, which is now in Stockholm. The Bouquet of Flowers in a Glass Vase in the National Gallery of Art is dated 1621, the year of his death.","q981631":"Jan Both was born in Utrecht between 1610 and 1618, the son of a glass painter, and studied with Abraham Bloemaert. He and his brother Andries travelled together to Rome via France, and by 1638 both were there, Andries painting low-life scenes in the manner of Pieter van Laer and Jan landscapes in the manner of Claude Lorrain. In 1639 Jan took part with Herman van Swanevelt and Claude in a project for the Buen Retiro Palace in Madrid. According to Houbraken, Andries drowned in a canal in Venice. By 1646 Jan was back in Utrecht, where he painted wide, imaginary landscapes drenched in Mediterranean golden light, with travellers, bandits and peasants, and sometimes figures by other painters such as Cornelis van Poelenburch. He died in 1652. His Italianate Evening Landscape dates from about 1650.","q5669":"Sandro Botticelli was born in Florence in 1445 and trained with Fra Filippo Lippi before opening a workshop of his own. He worked for the Medici family and their circle, and his best-known pictures, the Primavera and the Birth of Venus, are mythologies painted in the late 1470s and 1480s for Medici relatives. In 1481 and 1482 he painted frescoes in the Sistine Chapel in Rome. His portrait of Giuliano de' Medici, who was murdered in the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478, is one of several made after the assassination. In his later years he was influenced by the preacher Savonarola, and his style became more severe. Famous in his lifetime, he was largely forgotten until the Pre-Raphaelites and the critics Ruskin and Pater revived him in the nineteenth century. He died in Florence in 1510.","q180932":"François Boucher was born in Paris in 1703, the son of a designer, and trained with François Lemoyne and the engraver Jean-François Cars. He won the Prix de Rome in 1723 and spent 1727 to 1731 in Italy at his own expense. Back in Paris he rose rapidly, was received into the Academy in 1734, and became the favourite painter of Madame de Pompadour, who sat for him and took lessons from him. He painted mythologies, pastorals and nudes in pink, blue and cream, and designed tapestries for Beauvais and the Gobelins and models for the porcelain works at Sèvres. In 1765 he became first painter to the king. Diderot attacked his artificiality, and Neoclassical taste turned from him; he died in 1770. The Cleveland Museum of Art and the Metropolitan Museum hold his work, including The Toilette of Venus.","q212600":"Eugène Boudin was born at Honfleur in 1824, the son of a ship's steward, and grew up in Le Havre, where he worked in a stationer's and frame-maker's shop and began to show his sketches in the window. Encouraged by Millet and other painters who bought them, he received a grant from the town to study in Paris. He devoted himself to painting the sea and sky of the Normandy coast, and from the 1860s the fashionable crowds at Trouville and Deauville, in small panels like On the Beach at Trouville (1863) and Princess Pauline Metternich on the Beach. In 1858 he persuaded the young Claude Monet to paint outdoors, and the poet Baudelaire praised his skies; Corot called him the king of skies. He showed in the first Impressionist exhibition in 1874, and died at Deauville in 1898.","q553795":"Sébastien Bourdon was born in Montpellier in 1616, the son of a Protestant glass painter, and was apprenticed in Paris. Despite his poverty, he went to Rome in 1636, where he studied Poussin, Claude Lorrain and Caravaggio, and was forced to flee in 1638, fearing prosecution for his Protestant faith. In 1648 he was a founder of the Académie royale de peinture et de sculpture. In 1652 he went to Sweden, where Queen Christina made him her first court painter, and the National Gallery's portrait of her friend Countess Ebba Sparre, of 1652 to 1653, dates from this stay. He was skilful at many styles, from dashing Rubens-like portraits to plain, sympathetic bust portraits, landscapes and mythologies. His chief work is the Crucifixion of Saint Peter for Notre-Dame. He died in Paris in 1671.","q313561":"Dieric Bouts was born at Haarlem about 1415 and settled in Leuven by about 1457, where he became the city's official painter in 1468. His most famous works are the Last Supper altarpiece for the church of Saint Peter, Leuven, completed in 1468, and the pair of Justice of Emperor Otto panels for the town hall. His figures are tall, slender and quiet, set in carefully built landscapes and interiors, with a calm that suits devotional images such as the Metropolitan Museum's Virgin and Child and The Mourning Virgin; Christ Crowned with Thorns. He also painted fine portraits, as in Portrait of a Man (about 1470). His sons Dieric the Younger and Albrecht continued the workshop. He died in Leuven in 1475, and is now counted among the principal Netherlandish painters of the century.","bracquemond_marie":"Marie Bracquemond was born Marie Quivoron in 1840 in Brittany and began showing at the Paris Salon while still an adolescent. She never had formal art training but received limited lessons from Ingres and advice from Paul Gauguin. She married Félix Bracquemond, the printmaker who helped to popularise Japanese art in France, and together they made ceramics for the Haviland company. She exhibited in three of the eight Impressionist exhibitions, in 1879, 1880 and 1886, painting women in sunlit gardens in pale, bright colour. Her husband disliked Impressionism, and according to their son he was jealous of her work and belittled her ambition, and she gave up painting about 1890. She died in 1916, and only a fraction of her output has been found. Marguerite is dated about 1874.","q16197540":"John Bradley is recorded as an American painter active between 1832 and 1847, and he is known here from a portrait of Emma Homan, painted about 1844. Portraits by painters of his generation, many of them travelling from town to town, are valued today for their plain design, bright colour and the careful record they give of dress and family furnishings. Nothing certain about Bradley's birthplace, training or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His Emma Homan, which carries the sitter's name and a date, is a documented likeness rather than an anonymous one, and it shows the kind of work that American families could commission locally in the 1840s.","q21497964":"J. W. Bradshaw was an American painter about whom almost nothing is recorded, and the dates sometimes given for him are not reliable. The National Gallery of Art has his picture Plains Indian, dated to the last quarter of the nineteenth century. In those years, the time of the Plains wars, the near destruction of the buffalo herds and the confinement of the Plains peoples on reservations, painters, photographers and illustrators travelled west or worked from studies and photographs to record the dress and faces of Native people, and eastern buyers wanted such pictures. A single head or half-length figure was among the commonest forms. Whether Bradshaw saw his sitter in person is not stated, and nothing more can safely be said of his training or career.","q7799":"Bartolomeo Suardi, called Bramantino, was born in Milan about 1456, the son of Alberto Suardi. He was trained by Donato Bramante, whose name he adopted in a diminutive form, and he took from Bramante's school a style of still, solemn realism. He also assimilated elements of Leonardo da Vinci, who arrived in Milan in the 1480s, but he stayed faithful to his central Italian training, and his later pictures show strange, quiet, geometric compositions, influenced by Piero della Francesca, with large, immobile figures and unnatural light. He is documented in late 1508 in Rome, helping to decorate the Vatican Stanze, though nothing of his work survives there, and by 1509 he was back in Milan. He designed the Trivulzio tapestries and worked as an architect on Milan Cathedral. He died about 1530. The Madonna and Child dates from before 1508.","q541789":"Jan de Bray was born in Haarlem about 1627 and was the best pupil of his father, the painter, architect and poet Salomon de Bray, whom he followed closely; he was also influenced by Frans Hals and Bartholomeus van der Helst. He worked in Haarlem until he was sixty, when he went bankrupt and moved to Amsterdam. He painted group portraits and history pictures, and specialised in the portrait historié, in which his family appear as figures from history, as in his two versions of the Banquet of Cleopatra, the second of 1669 with great pathos, because most of the people he shows had died in the plague of 1663 to 1664. His Portrait of the Artist's Parents, Salomon de Bray and Anna Westerbaen, of 1664, belongs to the same time. Houbraken called him the pearl in Haarlem's crown. He died in 1697.","q2938914":"Carl Fredrik von Breda was born in Stockholm in 1759 into a family of painters of Dutch origin and entered the Royal Swedish Academy at nineteen. He went to Britain, where he was a pupil of Sir Joshua Reynolds, and he specialised in portraits, in the manner of Reynolds, and acquiring a reputation as the Van Dyck of Sweden. Mrs. William Hartigan, painted between 1787 and 1796, comes from his British years. He returned to Sweden in 1796, became a professor at the Academy of Arts, a popular portraitist and a court painter, and painted the leading figures of Swedish society and culture. His son Johan Fredrik was also a painter and studied with him. He died in Stockholm in 1818.","q1911509":"Adam van Breen was born in Amsterdam in 1585 and specialised in winter landscapes with skaters. He became a member of the Hague Guild of Saint Luke in 1612, and besides The Hague he is known to have worked in Amsterdam and in Oslo, then called Christiania. He died in Norway in 1642. His Skating on the Frozen Amstel River, of 1611, shows skaters on the frozen Amstel, in the lively, small-figured style of Hendrick Avercamp and the Amsterdam school of winter painters. The scene is a record of an everyday winter pleasure in the early Dutch Republic. Few details of his life are recorded beyond these places and dates, and he is now known to specialists mainly through his winter pictures.","q282043":"Jules Breton was born in 1827 at Courrières, in the Pas-de-Calais, and studied in Ghent and Antwerp before going to Paris, where he first exhibited at the Salon in 1849. He devoted his career to the farm labourers of his native Artois, and his Gleaners of 1854 and Recall of the Gleaners of 1859 made his name. The Weeders, painted in 1868, comes from his mature period. Breton painted peasants as dignified, almost classical figures in golden light, mixing careful observation with idealisation, and was admired in France and especially in the United States, where his Song of the Lark of 1884 lent its title to Willa Cather's novel. He also published poems and memoirs. He died in Paris in 1906.","q5446751":"Fidelia Bridges was born in Salem, Massachusetts, in 1834, the daughter of a sea captain, and was orphaned at fifteen when both parents died within months of each other. She became an exceptional painter of flowers, plants and birds, working at first in oil, as in Trailing Arbutus of 1863, and later mainly in watercolour, with delicate, close observation and, in her compositions, a debt to Japanese prints. She was the only woman among seven artists in the early years of the American Watercolor Society. From the 1870s she designed Christmas cards and illustrated books for Louis Prang's firm and for Scribner's Monthly, making a successful business of what many then saw as an amateur's pastime, and Mark Twain collected her work. She died in 1923.","british_18th_century":"British 18th Century is not one painter but a heading for pictures whose makers are unknown. The National Gallery's group includes a Portrait of a Lady of about 1770 to 1775 and a portrait of the Hon. Sir Francis Burton Conyngham, of about 1790 to 1795. Portrait painting was a large trade in Georgian Britain, and while the best-known painters, such as Reynolds, Gainsborough and Romney, had many pupils, assistants and imitators, many more portraits were made by provincial or little-known artists, often based on the poses and costumes of the leading portraitists. Pictures like these are valued as records of dress, hairstyles and manners, even when the painter's name is lost.","british_19th_century":"British 19th Century is not one painter but a heading for pictures whose makers are unknown. The National Gallery's group includes a Portrait of a Man of about 1830, a portrait of Mr. Tucker of Yeovil painted between about 1800 and 1820, and Portrait of an Unknown Family with a Terrier, of about 1825 to 1835. In the early nineteenth century, before photography became widespread, portraits were made in great numbers by painters in provincial towns, who charged moderate prices and often travelled to the sitter's house. Family groups with a pet dog, like the one here, record middle-class domestic life and the affection for animals that was a mark of the period. The sitters' names are often lost, as in two of these pictures.","british_or_irish_painter":"British or Irish Painter is not an artist's name but a label for a picture by an unknown hand, and the Metropolitan Museum uses it for one portrait of the late eighteenth century, William Paulet Carey. Carey, who lived from 1759 to 1839, was an Irish art critic, engraver and dealer, born in Dublin as the son of a baker and newspaper owner. He studied drawing at the Royal Dublin Society's school and began as a painter and engraver, but an accident to his eyes ended his career as an artist. In England he spent fifty years promoting British art, writing and distributing criticism. The label shows that scholars cannot yet say whether the portrait was painted in England or in Ireland, or by whom, but the sitter's career as a critic and promoter of art gives the picture added interest.","british_painter":"British Painter is not one artist but a label for works by unknown British hands, and the group here is a varied one: portraits of men and a woman, many apparently from the late sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries, among them Sir John Shurley of Isfield (1565 to 1632), a Portrait of a Woman of about 1600 and a man with the initials RH, as well as a portrait of an officer, a view of Tottenham Church, and an eye miniature. The eye miniature, a tiny portrait of a single eye, was a love token fashionable in Britain from the late eighteenth century. Early English portraits were often painted by local or itinerant painters, who followed formulas for dress and pose. Their names are lost, but their pictures record the faces and clothes of English families.","q2470305":"Briton Rivière was born in London in 1840, of Huguenot descent, the son of William Rivière, a drawing master at Cheltenham College and later at Oxford, who was his chief teacher. He was educated at Cheltenham and at Oxford, where he took his degree in 1867. He first exhibited at the Royal Academy in 1857, and from 1863 was a regular exhibitor, at first with historical and literary subjects such as The Eve of the Spanish Armada. In 1865 he began, with Sleeping Deerhound, a long series of animal pictures, and he became one of the most popular animal painters of Victorian England, often giving animals a human, sympathetic expression. Pallas Athena and the Herdsman's Dogs, of 1876, is a classical subject with dogs. He became a Royal Academician in 1881 and died in 1920.","q18508624":"Richard Norris Brooke was born in Warrenton, Virginia, in 1847, and lived there during the Civil War, when it was held by the Union army. He studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts from 1865 and graduated in 1871, taught in Philadelphia, and was briefly head of fine arts at the Virginia Military Institute. In 1873 he became United States Consul at La Rochelle, and while in France he studied with Léon Bonnat, later working with Carolus-Duran and Benjamin-Constant. He settled in Washington in 1880 and was active in its art life, helping to found the Art Students League of Washington and serving as vice principal of the Corcoran School from 1902 to 1917. He is known especially for genre scenes showing African Americans, such as A Pastoral Visit, of 1881. He died in 1920.","q21317354":"Samuel Marsden Brookes was born near London in 1816 to a family of Dutch origin and emigrated with his parents to the United States in 1833, settling near the new city of Chicago, where his father, a botanist, tried to establish a nursery. His father disapproved of art as a profession, but Brookes copied the work of travelling portrait painters, painted miniatures, and in 1841 became an itinerant portrait painter. In 1845 he went back to England for a year to copy old masters in the London galleries. He moved to San Francisco in 1862 and became known for still lifes of fish and game, painted with fine detail, and also painted some landscapes. A member of the Bohemian Club, he died in San Francisco in 1892. His Still Life with Fan and Pendant is dated about 1865 to 1875.","q209050":"Jan Brueghel the Elder was born in Brussels in 1568, the younger son of Pieter Bruegel the Elder, and was nicknamed Velvet Brueghel for his delicate, glossy finish. He trained in Antwerp, spent the years from about 1589 to 1596 in Italy, where he enjoyed the patronage of Cardinal Federico Borromeo in Milan, and became court painter to the Archdukes Albert and Isabella in Brussels. His small, brilliantly detailed landscapes, flower pieces and paradise scenes were prized by collectors across Europe. He was close to Rubens, with whom he painted several joint works, among them the Garden of Eden with the Fall of Man, in which Rubens painted the figures and Brueghel the animals and landscape. He died of cholera in Antwerp in 1625.","q437233":"Hendrick ter Brugghen was born in 1588, probably at Deventer, and grew up in Utrecht, where he studied with Abraham Bloemaert. He spent about ten years in Italy, from about 1604 to 1614, and there absorbed the lessons of Caravaggio, whose dramatic naturalism he brought back to the Netherlands. In Utrecht from 1614, he became a leading member, with Gerrit van Honthorst and Dirck van Baburen, of the Utrecht Caravaggisti. His own approach is quieter and more poetic than theirs: musicians, saints and soldiers in half-length, in silvery greys, pinks and yellows, with softly modelled faces and an unusual feeling for atmosphere. He died in Utrecht in 1629, aged forty-one, and was almost forgotten until the twentieth century. The Cleveland Saint Jerome and the Metropolitan's Roman Charity and Crucifixion are among his finest works.","q5546688":"George de Forest Brush was born in Shelbyville, Tennessee, in 1855, of New England parents, and grew up in Danbury, Connecticut. He studied at the National Academy of Design in New York and in Paris with Jean-Léon Gérôme, where his fellow student was Abbott Thayer. After returning in 1880 he went west and lived for some months among Arapaho, Crow and Shoshone people in Wyoming and Montana, and he later developed a series of paintings of Native American life from his drawings, shown in the 1880s and including An Arapahoe Boy. In the 1890s he turned to Madonna-like pictures of his wife and children, in a calm, Renaissance-inspired style. With Thayer, he helped develop ideas of camouflage. He lived in Dublin, New Hampshire, and died in 1941.","q1918666":"Budapest Master is a name of convenience: scholars use it for an unidentified painter whose pictures they group on grounds of style, and the Metropolitan Museum describes him as Spanish. Nothing is known of his real name, his dates or his birthplace. The Annunciation in the Metropolitan Museum, the picture here, shows the archangel Gabriel announcing to the Virgin that she is to bear a child, one of the most frequently painted subjects in medieval and Renaissance Spanish altarpieces, often as part of a series of scenes of the Virgin's life. Without a documented career, nothing more can safely be said of his training, workshop or patrons.","q21498022":"Jonathan Budington was an American portrait painter who lived from 1779 to 1823. Little is recorded of his life in a form that can be relied on, and he is known chiefly from a few portraits, among them Father and Son, dated 1800, in the National Gallery of Art, which he painted when he was about twenty-one. A double portrait of a father and his boy was a way for a family to record both the head of the household and the heir, usually with a plain background and a simple, direct pose. Such pictures were made in the early republic by painters with little academic training who travelled or worked in their home towns. Beyond these facts, nothing certain can be said of his training or career.","q18607993":"Jan Jansz van Buesem was a Dutch painter of tavern scenes, whose dates are given as 1600 to 1649. The National Gallery's Tavern Scene with Standing Couple is dated 1653 or later, which cannot be reconciled with those dates, so his dates are uncertain. Little is recorded of his life. Tavern and peasant scenes were among the most popular subjects for Dutch painters of the Golden Age, who painted drinkers, musicians and lovers in rustic interiors, for buyers in towns, and they were often followed by many lesser masters. Buesem is one of those lesser-known painters, and the one picture here is the best evidence of his skill. Nothing certain can be said of his teachers or career.","q2578459":"Giuliano Bugiardini was born in Florence in 1475 and died there in 1554. According to Vasari, who wrote his Life, he trained in the workshop of Domenico Ghirlandaio, where he met the young Michelangelo, and then worked with Mariotto Albertinelli. He became a friend of Michelangelo, and his portrait of the sculptor, painted about 1522 and now in the Casa Buonarroti, is one of the best-known likenesses of him. Vasari also tells that Michelangelo supplied him with drawings for an altarpiece of the Martyrdom of Saint Catherine in Santa Maria Novella. His pictures, mostly Madonnas, altarpieces and portraits for Florentine families, are careful, soft and rather conservative, and he never rose to the first rank. Vasari, who liked him, presents him as a kindly, slow and good-natured man, a Florentine of the Michelangelo circle.","q809491":"Bartolomeo Bulgarini was a Sienese painter of the fourteenth century, born between about 1300 and 1310 and dead in 1378, who worked both before and after the Black Death of 1348. His name was known from documents for centuries, but only in recent decades have art historians linked it to a group of works, which Bernard Berenson in 1917 had grouped under the invented name Ugolino Lorenzetti. His style is related to Ugolino di Nerio and Pietro Lorenzetti. In 2021 his name reached the news when Botticelli's Portrait of a Young Man Holding a Roundel sold for about ninety-two million dollars, since the roundel contains a small painting of a bearded saint attributed to Bulgarini. The National Gallery's Saint Catherine of Alexandria dates from about 1335 to 1340.","q21403116":"Horace Bundy was born in Hardwick, Vermont, in 1814, and in 1836 is listed in Lowell, Massachusetts, as a carriage maker, so he probably learned to paint by decorating sleighs, buckets and signs. He became an itinerant portrait painter in Vermont and New Hampshire without formal instruction, and, unlike most itinerant painters, signed and dated all his pictures and often added the place where they were painted, which gives a record of his travels. He was a Millerite, and after the Great Disappointment of 1844 joined the new Adventist movement, spending almost as much time preaching as painting. In 1863 he became pastor of an Adventist church at Lakeport, New Hampshire. His Vermont Lawyer, of 1841, is his best-known work. He died in Concord, New Hampshire, in 1883.","q325568":"Dennis Miller Bunker was born in New York in 1861 and studied at the National Academy of Design and in Paris under Jean-Léon Gérôme. Returning to the United States in 1885, he taught at the Cowles Art School in Boston and became a close friend of John Singer Sargent and of the collector Isabella Stewart Gardner, who supported him. He painted portraits and, in his last years, bright landscapes at Medfield, Massachusetts, in an Impressionist manner. In 1890 he married Eleanor Hardy, and the Metropolitan Museum's portrait of her was painted that year. He died in December 1890 of meningitis, aged twenty-nine, and his early death cut short one of the most promising careers of his generation. The portrait was given to the Museum by several gentlemen in 1893.","byzantine_13th_century":"Byzantine 13th Century is a heading for works by unnamed painters of the Byzantine world, not a single artist. The National Gallery's two pictures, a Madonna and Child on a Curved Throne of about 1260 to 1280 and an Enthroned Madonna and Child of about 1250 to 1275, are panels in the Byzantine style, the Virgin shown full-face and solemn, holding the Child, against a gold ground. In the thirteenth century such icons were made in Constantinople, which the Byzantines recovered from the Crusaders in 1261, and by Greek painters working in other places, and their forms strongly shaped Italian painting, where the tradition was called the maniera greca. The painters' names are lost, because icon painters rarely signed their work.","byzantine_painter":"Byzantine Painter is not one artist but a label for an unknown painter who worked in the Byzantine tradition. The Metropolitan Museum's The Presentation in the Temple belongs to the fifteenth century, the last century of the Byzantine Empire, which fell to the Ottoman Turks in 1453. After that date the tradition of icon painting continued in Venetian Crete, where the Cretan school flourished and exported icons to Italy and across the Mediterranean, and in the Greek world. The Presentation, the scene of the infant Christ brought to the Temple in Jerusalem, is one of the feasts of the Church year and was a standard subject of Byzantine icons. The painter's name, training and birthplace are not known.","c_l_woodhouse":"Almost nothing is recorded of C. L. Woodhouse beyond a single painting. Hunter and Indian Guide is an oil on canvas about two feet by two and a half, and its inscription at the lower right is the artist's only biography: it reads, in the museum's transcription, Designed and Executed by C. L. Woodhouse, Artist, April 21, 1869, Lansing, Mich. That places him in Michigan's capital in the year of the picture. Hunting scenes with a guide were a favourite subject of American painters and print makers in the later nineteenth century, and this canvas belongs to that taste. His first name, dates, training, teachers, patrons and other works are not documented, and the Cleveland Museum of Art catalogues him simply as an American artist.","q295144":"Gustave Caillebotte was born in Paris in 1848 into a rich family whose fortune came from textiles and property. After studying law and serving in the Franco-Prussian War he turned to painting, trained under the academic painter Léon Bonnat, and from 1876 exhibited with the Impressionists. His picture of workmen scraping a floor and Paris Street; Rainy Day, with its steep perspectives, remain his best-known works. His private income made him the group's most generous patron: he paid for exhibitions, bought pictures by Monet, Renoir, Degas and Cézanne, and on his death in 1894 left his collection to the French state, causing a famous dispute over its acceptance. From the 1880s he lived at Petit-Gennevilliers on the Seine, where he gardened and sailed; the Chrysanthemums of 1893 come from his own garden. He was little known as a painter until the later twentieth century.","q621994":"Alexandre Calame was born near Vevey in 1810, the son of a marble worker whose business failed, and from the age of fifteen he worked in a bank to support his mother while drawing in his spare time. In 1829 a banker named Diodati, who had noticed his talent, made it possible for him to study with the landscape painter François Diday. From 1835 he exhibited Alpine pictures in Paris and Berlin: glaciers, torrents, storm-bent pines, Monte Rosa and the Jungfrau, painted on a large scale with Romantic drama. He became the best-known Swiss landscape painter of his generation, with a strong following among German collectors, and his lithographed studies of mountain scenery were widely used as drawing models. He visited Rome and Naples in 1844, taught in Geneva, and died at Menton in 1864.","q21497951":"Nothing is recorded of H. Call except what the canvas itself says. Prize Bull is signed at the lower right, H. CALL, and dated 1876, and the National Gallery of Art catalogues the artist only as American and active in that year, adding that the picture was recorded as coming from Massachusetts. It shows a large brown-and-white bull in profile on a grassy plain, small-headed and heavy-bodied, with low trees on the horizon and a pink and purple sky. Portraits of champion livestock were commissioned by farmers and breeders in nineteenth-century America to celebrate an animal that had won a prize, and many were painted by local or travelling artists. Whether Call was one of them is not known, and no first name, dates, training, patrons or other works have been identified.","q148475":"Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot, known to his friends as Father Corot, was born in Paris in 1796 and did not become a professional painter until he was in his late twenties. His years in Italy from 1825 to 1828 gave him clear, solid open-air studies, and for decades afterward he divided his year between the Paris studio, the family house at Ville-d'Avray and sketching trips to Normandy, Burgundy and the forest of Fontainebleau. His landscapes grew softer and more silvery from the 1850s, and The Forest of Coubron (1872) and River View show the late manner. He also painted figures in the studio, among them the Gallery's Corot's Studio and Gypsy Woman with Mandolin, models in costume with musical instruments. A generous man, he helped other painters. He died in Paris in 1875, so widely imitated that forged Corots became proverbial.","q134741":"Camille Pissarro was born in 1830 on St Thomas in the Danish West Indies, was sent to school near Paris, and returned home to work in his father's store before leaving for Venezuela in 1852 with the Danish painter Fritz Melbye. He settled in Paris in 1855, where Corot, Courbet and Daubigny shaped his early landscapes. At Pontoise and Louveciennes he developed the broken, light-filled handling of Impressionism, and in London during the Franco-Prussian War he met the dealer Paul Durand-Ruel, who became his chief supporter. He was the only painter to show in all eight Impressionist exhibitions, from 1874 to 1886, and a patient teacher to Cézanne, Gauguin and Mary Cassatt. With failing eyesight he painted city views from high windows, among them Rouen and the Boulevard Montmartre. He died in Paris in 1903.","q182664":"Giovanni Antonio Canal, called Canaletto, was born in Venice in 1697, the son of a theatrical scene painter, and began by designing opera sets in Venice and Rome. By the early 1720s he had turned to painting views of the city, and from the late 1720s his clear, sunlit, carefully drawn scenes of the Grand Canal and the great squares sold well to visitors on the Grand Tour. His agent and chief patron was the British consul in Venice, Joseph Smith, who also arranged engravings of his work. When the War of the Austrian Succession cut off English travellers, he went to England, where he worked from 1746 to about 1755, painting the Thames, country houses and Warwick Castle. After a period of lower esteem, he is again ranked as the leading master of the Venetian view.","q573430":"Simone Cantarini was born at Pesaro in 1612 and is often called il Pesarese after his native city. He is said to have trained under Claudio Ridolfi, who introduced him to Venetian colour and the soft manner of Federico Barocci, and about 1634 he entered Guido Reni's studio in Bologna, where he learned etching. He was a difficult pupil, and early accounts tell of a quarrel in which he hurled a canvas against the wall after Reni criticised it; he left Bologna and returned only after Reni's death in 1642. He painted altarpieces, portraits and tender devotional pictures, combining Reni's classical grace with a more natural warmth, and made thirty-seven etchings admired for their delicacy. Called to Mantua in 1647, he was dismissed for taking too long over a portrait, fell ill, and died at Verona in 1648, aged thirty-six.","caravaggio":"Michelangelo Merisi, called Caravaggio after the Lombard town of his family, was born in Milan in 1571 and trained there under Simone Peterzano before reaching Rome about 1592. He worked briefly for the Cavaliere d'Arpino, then sold pictures through dealers until Cardinal Francesco Maria del Monte took him into his household; The Musicians, of about 1597, dates from this time. The Contarelli Chapel pictures in San Luigi dei Francesi, unveiled in 1600, made him famous: ordinary models, hard shadow, and sacred scenes in everyday dress. In 1606 he killed a man in a brawl and fled Rome, working in Naples, Malta and Sicily; The Crucifixion of Saint Andrew and The Denial of Saint Peter belong to these last years. He died at Porto Ercole in 1610. Admired and condemned in his day, he was half forgotten until the twentieth century.","q976391":"Giovanni Busi, called Cariani, was born between about 1485 and 1490, probably in the Bergamo area, where his father's family came from the village of Fuipiano al Brembo, and he is recorded in Venice by 1509. He may have trained with Giovanni Bellini or Giorgione, and he was strongly influenced by Palma Vecchio, with echoes of Titian and of Lorenzo Lotto, whom he probably met in Bergamo, where he worked from 1517 to 1523. His religious scenes show a provincial love of scenery, and his portraits of Venetian gentlemen and his music party A Concert are solidly drawn and warm in colour. He moved between Venice and the mainland and died, probably in Bergamo, in 1547. Little documentation survives, and he is regarded as an able, individual painter of the Venetian school rather than a leader of it.","q821116":"Karl August Senff was born in 1770 at Kreypau near Merseburg in Saxony and trained in Leipzig under Adam Friedrich Oeser and in Dresden under the portraitist Anton Graff and Christian Leberecht Vogel. In 1803 he went to the university at Dorpat, now Tartu in Estonia, as drawing master, and stayed there until he died in 1838, heading its drawing school from 1818. He is remembered chiefly as a teacher, who set out a new method of teaching practical drawing in a textbook with etchings and watercolour plates, and as the etcher of portraits of officers of the Russian army. His Portrait of a Woman of 1808, an oval miniature on ivory less than two and a half inches high, shows him at work on the small scale. The sitter is not identified.","q21854836":"Carl Friedrich Thienpondt was born in Berlin in 1730 and died in Warsaw in 1796. According to the Metropolitan Museum he studied from 1745 under Antoine Pesne, the French-born court painter in Berlin, took up miniature painting in 1751, and in 1753 went to Dresden to learn from the miniaturist Ismael Mengs. He then settled in Warsaw, where he was much sought after for miniatures painted in enamel, a hard, glassy medium that keeps its colour almost unchanged for centuries. The museum holds two small enamels of about 1760, A Sea Nymph and Diana, each only a few inches across, which came with the bequest of Mary Clark Thompson in 1923. Both take mythological subjects. Little more is recorded of his career in the sources consulted, and nothing is known here of his patrons.","q61987":"Carl Gustav Carus was born in Leipzig in 1789 and was a physician and scientist before he was a painter. He studied medicine, became professor of obstetrics in Dresden in his twenties, and later served as personal physician to the King of Saxony. Painting was an avocation, but a serious one: from about 1817 he was a close friend and follower of Caspar David Friedrich. His Nine Letters on Landscape Painting, written between 1815 and 1824 and published in 1831, became a key text of German Romanticism, urging the painter to read the life of the earth in nature. His moonlit Schloss Milkel, the overgrown mineshaft and the Gothic windows of the ruined monastery at Oybin share Friedrich's mood of ruin and silence. He also wrote on psychology and the unconscious, and died in Dresden in 1869.","q647805":"Carl Rottmann was born near Heidelberg in 1797, studied with his father, a drawing master, and moved to Munich in 1821, where he made his name with landscapes of Italy. King Ludwig I of Bavaria commissioned a series of Italian views in fresco for the arcades of the Hofgarten in the early 1830s, and in 1834 sent him to Greece to record its scenery. From that journey came a second series of large views for the Neue Pinakothek, painted between 1838 and 1850, the principal work of his last years. His Greek pictures show bare, light-struck hills, ruins and graveyards under dramatic skies, and The Cemetery at Pronoia near Nauplia, near the first capital of modern Greece, belongs among these subjects. He died in Munich in 1850 and is now counted among the leading German landscape painters of his generation.","q20859495":"Carl Weinedel (1795 to 1845) is listed as a portrait miniaturist working in New York, and the Metropolitan Museum, which catalogues him as American, holds three of his small portraits. They are painted in watercolour on ivory, the usual support for miniatures in the early nineteenth century: Mrs. E. Hunt of 1829 and two works titled Portrait of a Gentleman, of 1836 and 1837. The 1836 portrait measures only about two and a third inches high. Miniatures of this kind were made for sitters to give to family and friends, to wear or to keep in a case. Beyond his dates, his New York address and these dated works, little is recorded of his training, teachers, clients or reputation, and apart from Mrs. Hunt his sitters remain unnamed. The museum acquired the 1836 portrait in 2006.","q686597":"Carle Vanloo was born at Nice in 1705 into a family of painters of Dutch origin. He learned first from his elder brother Jean-Baptiste, went with him to Rome, and studied there under Benedetto Luti and the sculptor Pierre Legros. He won the Prix de Rome in 1724, returned to Paris in 1734, and joined the Academy the next year. He painted everything the court wanted: mythologies, altarpieces, portraits, Turkish scenes and hunting subjects, with Madame de Pompadour among his patrons. The Metropolitan Museum's Halt of the Hunt is an oil sketch for the large painting installed in the panelling of Louis XV's dining room at Fontainebleau in 1737. In 1762 he became first painter to the king, and he died in Paris in 1765. Celebrated in his lifetime, he lost ground when Rococo taste fell from favour.","q585852":"Carle Vernet was born at Bordeaux in 1758, the youngest son of the marine painter Claude-Joseph Vernet, and learned from his father and from Nicolas-Bernard Lépicié. He won the Prix de Rome in 1782. His entry for admission to the Royal Academy, The Triumph of Aemilius Paulus (1789), a long frieze of more than one hundred figures and a dozen horses, was shown at the Salons of 1789 and 1791 and is now in the Metropolitan Museum. After the Revolution, in which his sister was guillotined, he turned to battle pictures, hunts, races and above all horses. Napoleon gave him the Legion of Honour for scenes of the Italian campaign and the Battle of Marengo. His son Horace became a still more successful painter. A fine horseman himself, Carle died in Paris in 1836.","q239980":"Carlo Cignani was born in Bologna in 1628 and trained first under Battista Cairo and then under Francesco Albani, whose graceful classical manner he followed and whose most famous pupil he became. He also drew on Correggio's softness of light. He painted frescoes, altarpieces and gentle cabinet pictures of rounded figures, among them Charity. His principal work, the Assumption of the Virgin in the dome of the Madonna del Fuoco chapel in Forlì Cathedral, occupied him for about twenty years, and he spent his later life in Forlì, dying there in 1719 aged ninety-one. When the Accademia Clementina was founded at Bologna in 1706 he was named its principe for life, though he did not live in the city. His pupils included Marcantonio Franceschini and Giuseppe Maria Crespi. In his day he ranked among the finest painters in Italy.","q348024":"Carlo Crivelli was a Venetian who made his career far from Venice. After a conviction for adultery in 1457, which cost him six months in prison, he is recorded at Zara on the Dalmatian coast in 1465 and by 1468 had settled in Ascoli Piceno in the Marches, where he painted altarpieces for churches and confraternities in the surrounding hill towns until his death in about 1495. His panels are marked by hard, sharp outline, gilded relief ornament, richly patterned brocades and swags of painted fruit, and he proudly signed himself Venetus, Venetian. Madonna and Child Enthroned with Donor (1470) and the Madonna and Child (about 1490) in the National Gallery of Art belong to the Marches period. Knighted in 1490, he was long overlooked and is now ranked among the most distinctive painters of the Italian Renaissance.","q503322":"Carlo Dolci was born in Florence in 1616 and spent nearly all his life there. Apprenticed as a boy to the painter Jacopo Vignali, he became a painter of devotional pictures for private patrons, among them members of the Medici family, whose jewel-like finish suited a pious and courtly taste. His Saint Philip Neri and Adoration of the Shepherds show the smooth, enamel-like surface and tender expression for which he was known. According to his biographer Filippo Baldinucci, he could take weeks over a single foot, which made large frescoes impossible and led him to repeat popular subjects in several versions. His daughter Agnese also became a painter. The story goes that in 1682 the sight of Luca Giordano painting at astonishing speed left him in despair. He was buried at Santissima Annunziata in Florence.","q951849":"Carlo Francesco Nuvolone was born in Milan about 1609 and died there in the early 1660s. His father, Panfilo Nuvolone, was a painter of frescoes and altarpieces, and his younger brother Giuseppe also painted. He studied at the Accademia Ambrosiana under Giovanni Battista Crespi, called Il Cerano. His soft light, sweet faces and clear colour were so close to the manner of Guido Reni that he was nicknamed the Guido of Lombardy. He painted religious subjects, altarpieces and portraits, and worked in fresco at the Certosa di Pavia and at the Sacri Monti of Varese and Orta in the 1650s. By the middle of the century he was the leading painter in Lombardy. The Metropolitan Museum's Female Martyr Saint belongs to the devotional pictures of his mature period.","q1041995":"Carlo Innocenzo Carloni was born in 1686 at Scaria, in the Val d'Intelvi north of Como, a valley that sent stuccoists and painters across Europe; his elder brother Diego Francesco was a stuccoist. He trained under Giulio Quaglio and, from 1706, in Rome under Francesco Trevisani. Between 1712 and the early 1750s he worked chiefly north of the Alps, painting ceiling frescoes in Vienna for Prince Eugene of Savoy, in Prague's Clam-Gallas palace, at Ludwigsburg for the Duke of Württemberg (1730 to 1733), at Ansbach, and at Brühl. Returning to Italy he worked at Monza, Bergamo and Brescia, finishing at Asti Cathedral in 1773. His light, lively decoration with airy perspectives carried late Baroque into Rococo. He died at Scaria in 1775, aged eighty-nine, better known in German-speaking lands than at home.","q538998":"Carlo Maratti, also spelled Maratta, was born in 1625 at Camerano near Ancona and entered the Roman studio of Andrea Sacchi in 1637, remaining until Sacchi's death in 1661. In the later seventeenth century he was the leading painter in Rome, producing altarpieces, portraits of popes and cardinals, and gentle Madonnas for several popes and for foreign courts; Louis XIV commissioned his Apollo and Daphne in 1681. Clement XI knighted him in 1704 and had him restore Raphael's frescoes in the Vatican. He married Francesca Gommi in 1700, and his portrait of her, now in Cleveland, dates from about 1701. His Flight into Egypt shows his smooth, graceful manner. He died in Rome in 1713 and left many pupils. His fame faded in the nineteenth century, when critics found him cold.","q938607":"Carlo Saraceni was born in Venice about 1579 and went to Rome in 1598, where he lived for most of the next twenty years and joined the Accademia di San Luca in 1607. He admired Caravaggio's strong light and the small, forest-fringed landscapes of the German painter Adam Elsheimer, then living in Rome, and joined the two in a personal manner, which made him one of the first Caravaggisti. In 1606 he painted the altarpiece for Santa Maria della Scala that replaced Caravaggio's rejected Death of the Virgin. Later commissions included frescoes in the Quirinal Palace in 1616 and 1617 and two paintings for Santa Maria dell'Anima in 1618. Saraceni returned to Venice in 1620 and died there the same year. He is now valued as one of the most original of Caravaggio's early followers.","q5371205":"Emil Carlsen was born in Copenhagen, studied architecture at the Royal Danish Academy, and emigrated to the United States in the early 1870s, settling in Chicago. He went to Paris in the 1870s and came to admire the still lifes of Chardin, whose quiet arrangements of kitchen objects shaped his own. After years of struggle, a still life accepted by the Pennsylvania Academy in 1883 began his rise; Still Life with Fish dates from 1882. He directed the California School of Design in San Francisco from 1887 to 1889 and later taught at the National Academy of Design and the Art Students League. His son Dines also became a still-life painter. Carlsen won a gold medal at the St. Louis exposition of 1904, later turned to tonal landscapes and seascapes, and died in New York in 1932.","q2245140":"Fra Carnevale, whose name was Bartolomeo di Giovanni Corradini, was born at Urbino about 1420 to 1425 and died there in 1484. The title Fra shows that he was a friar. He is thought to have trained in Florence with Fra Filippo Lippi, and he worked in Urbino under the Montefeltro dukes, who made their small city a centre of the Renaissance. For centuries he was unknown, and his best paintings were grouped under the name Master of the Barberini Panels until the identification was made in the twentieth century. The Birth of the Virgin, dated 1467 and in the Metropolitan Museum, belongs with related scenes from the life of Mary now in Boston and Washington. It sets the story in a lofty interior built in careful perspective, with precise Renaissance architecture.","caroline_l_ransom":"Caroline Louisa Ormes Ransom was born in Newark, Ohio, in 1838 and became a portrait painter. She studied in New York with Asher B. Durand, Thomas Hicks and Daniel Huntington, and then for two years in Munich with Wilhelm von Kaulbach. Returning to America in 1860, she set up a studio in Cleveland, where she painted portraits for local sitters; the Cleveland Museum of Art owns her portrait Mrs. Goss (The Woman in Fur). In 1867 Congress paid $1,000 for her portrait of the antislavery congressman Joshua R. Giddings, which hung in the Capitol, and a second major commission followed in 1875. In 1876 the Army of the Cumberland made her an honorary member, the only woman so honoured. She never married, moved to Washington, D.C., in 1884, and painted there until her death in 1910.","q274901":"Charles Auguste Émile Durand, who took the name Carolus-Duran, was born in Lille in 1837 and studied there and in Paris. In the 1860s he travelled in Italy and Spain, where the direct handling of Velázquez confirmed his taste for rich blacks and fluent brushwork. Success came at the Salon with La Dame au gant (1869), a portrait of his wife, the painter Pauline Croizette. By the 1870s he was among the most fashionable portraitists in Paris, painting actresses, aristocrats and wealthy Americans, such as Mrs. William Astor in 1890. In the early 1870s he opened an atelier that drew many Americans, among them John Singer Sargent, who studied there from 1874 and painted his teacher's portrait in 1879. He directed the French Academy in Rome from 1905 to 1913 and died in Paris in 1917.","q5581":"Vittore Carpaccio, born in Venice about 1465, probably trained with Lazzaro Bastiani and was influenced by Gentile and Giovanni Bellini and by the manner of Antonello da Messina. He is above all a storyteller. His great cycles of canvases for Venetian confraternities, the Legend of Saint Ursula (1490s, now in the Accademia) and the scenes of Saints George, Jerome and Tryphon in the Scuola di San Giorgio degli Schiavoni (about 1502 to 1507), which remain in their original room, fill Venetian streets, ships, costumes and animals with sharp, cheerful detail. He also painted altarpieces and Madonnas, such as the Virgin Reading in Washington. He died about 1525 or 1526. The Venetian painters of the next generation overshadowed him, and he was largely forgotten until the enthusiasm of John Ruskin, who wrote about Saint Ursula in the 1870s, brought him back.","q1441399":"Francis Bicknell Carpenter was born in 1830 at Homer, New York, studied with the local painter Sanford Thayer, and moved to New York City in 1851, where he built a portrait practice among prominent sitters. He is remembered chiefly for one picture, First Reading of the Emancipation Proclamation of President Lincoln (1864). With the President's permission he lived at the White House for about six months in 1864, sketching the Cabinet members and having Lincoln pose; the huge canvas was bought by a patron, Elizabeth Thompson, who gave it to Congress, and it hangs in the Capitol. Carpenter published an account of his stay, Six Months at the White House with Abraham Lincoln (1866). His portrait of Lucy Tappan Bowen, in the National Gallery, dates from 1859. He died in New York in 1900.","q461464":"Eugène Carrière was born at Gournay-sur-Marne in 1849 and trained in Strasbourg and Paris under Alexandre Cabanel. He became the best known of a group of artists who painted in a vague, dreamlike manner, in tones of brown and grey with figures that seem to melt into the surrounding mist, an approach linked to the Symbolists. His great theme was family life, and he painted his wife and children again and again, as in Women Sewing at a Table (about 1894 to 1896). The Metropolitan Museum also owns his Self-Portrait of about 1893. A friend of Rodin and Clemenceau, he was respected for his generosity as well as his art, and taught pupils in a studio in Paris. He died of cancer of the throat in 1906, and his work fell out of fashion, though it is again studied.","q2700128":"John William Casilear was born in New York City in 1811 and trained as an engraver, working for a time as an engraver of banknotes. He became a close friend of the painter Asher B. Durand, and turned to landscape painting in the 1830s and 1840s. In 1840 he travelled to Europe with Durand, Kensett and Thomas Rossiter, and the trip gave him a taste for luminous, tranquil lake and mountain views. A member of the Hudson River School, he painted places such as Lake George and the lakes of the Adirondacks, in a soft, silvery manner close to Kensett's. His late pictures, like the Met's Mountain Lake Scene of 1883, show how long this style persisted. He died in 1893.","q104884":"Caspar David Friedrich was born at Greifswald, then in Swedish Pomerania, in 1774 and studied at the academy in Copenhagen before settling in Dresden in 1798, where he lived for the rest of his life. He painted lonely, silent landscapes, with monks, ruins, cemeteries and travellers seen from behind gazing into mist or sea, in which nature stands for the soul and for God. His Cross in the Mountains, of 1808, used as an altarpiece, caused a controversy, but Monk by the Sea was bought by the king of Prussia in 1810. A stroke in 1835 left him weak, and he died in 1840, nearly forgotten. His rediscovery came about 1900, and he is now regarded as the great painter of German Romanticism. The National Gallery of Art owns Northern Landscape, Spring, of about 1825.","q217715":"Caspar Netscher was born about 1639 in Heidelberg or possibly Prague; his father, a sculptor, died while he was small, and his mother took the children to Arnhem, where a physician adopted the boy. He trained with the Arnhem painter Hendrick Coster and then, about 1654, with Gerard ter Borch at Deventer, who became his model for glossy satin and small, quiet interiors. In 1658 he set out for Italy but married at Bordeaux in 1659 and stayed there painting cabinet pictures. By 1662 he was in The Hague, where he found success as a portraitist, was patronised by William III, and sold pictures to Cosimo III de' Medici. The Card Party, of about 1665, is typical of his polished genre scenes. His sons Theodorus and Constantijn also painted. He died in The Hague in 1684.","q173223":"Mary Cassatt was born in 1844 at Allegheny City, Pennsylvania, into a wealthy family, and studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts from 1861 before going to Paris in 1866, where, because women were barred from the École des Beaux-Arts, she studied privately and copied in the Louvre. In 1877 Edgar Degas, whose work she admired, invited her to exhibit with the Impressionists; she showed with them in 1879, 1880, 1881 and 1886, and the two became lifelong friends. She painted women and children at home, at the theatre and in boats, with warm tenderness and without sentimentality, and from 1890, inspired by Japanese woodcuts, made colour prints of great refinement. She advised wealthy American friends, above all Louisine Havemeyer, on buying pictures. Her sight failed after 1914, and she died in France in 1926.","q1968947":"Vincenzo di Biagio, called Catena, worked in Venice and died there in 1531; his birth is put at about 1480, though older books say 1470. He began in the manner of Giovanni Bellini and took up something of Giorgione's softer, atmospheric style only after Giorgione's death in 1510. An inscription of 1506 on the back of Giorgione's portrait of Laura, in Vienna, names Catena as that master's colleague, an early record of his career. The only picture he dated, the Martyrdom of Saint Christina in Santa Maria Mater Domini, Venice, bears the year 1520. He painted Madonnas, altarpieces and, perhaps best, portraits, including one of Doge Andrea Gritti. His wills show a prosperous man with friends in Venetian humanist circles. Overshadowed by Titian, he is valued today for quiet, dignified portraits like the Metropolitan's Venetian Senator.","q455133":"George Catlin was born at Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania, in 1796 and trained as a lawyer before taking up portrait painting in Philadelphia and New York, working in oil and in miniature. After seeing a delegation of Native Americans in Philadelphia, he decided to record their peoples before their way of life vanished, and from 1830 to 1836 travelled in the West, painting hundreds of portraits and scenes of the Plains tribes. He toured the United States and Europe with his Indian Gallery and published Letters and Notes on the Manners, Customs, and Condition of the North American Indians in 1841, and he proposed a national park to protect the Plains and its peoples. His debts forced the sale of the collection, now mostly at the Smithsonian. He died at Jersey City in 1872. The Metropolitan Museum owns four of his early miniatures.","q822982":"Bernardo Cavallino was born in Naples in 1616 and died there about 1656, probably in the great plague, though some give an earlier date. The Neapolitan biographer De' Dominici said that his only master was Massimo Stanzione, but this is not entirely convincing, and Ribera or Aniello Falcone may also have taught him. He began with strong light and shadow, but from the late 1630s his small cabinet pictures of biblical and mythological stories grew more refined and courtly, with lighter colour and softer effects. The Triumph of Galatea shows this mature taste. He worked through private dealers and collectors, and few records survive. De' Dominici counted him among the best Neapolitan painters of his half-century, but his reputation declined, and he was not rediscovered until the early twentieth.","q2778951":"Jean-Charles Cazin was born in 1841 at Samer in the Pas-de-Calais and trained under Horace Lecoq de Boisbaudran, whose method taught students to draw from memory after close observation. In 1868 he became director of the school of fine arts at Tours. He spent time in England from about 1874 to 1876, where the Pre-Raphaelites impressed him. He painted quiet landscapes of the northern French coast and countryside, with small figure groups, as in Weary Wayfarers and The Great Windmill and the Rainbow, in low tones and poetic stillness, often at twilight or moonrise. His wife Marie, his pupil, became a painter and sculptor. He was made an officer of the Legion of Honour in 1889 and died in 1901 at Le Lavandou. His fame in Britain and America was high in his lifetime; today he is less known.","cecco_del_caravaggio":"Cecco del Caravaggio is the name of a follower of Caravaggio who was active in Rome from about 1610 to the mid-1620s. Cecco is a diminutive of Francesco, and Giulio Mancini named him as a notable follower about 1620. In 2001 the scholar Gianni Papi identified him as the Lombard painter Francesco Buoneri, a view now widely, but not universally, accepted. He may have been Caravaggio's servant or pupil and may have posed for him. No work is signed or dated. His large, solid figures, strong contrasts of light and dark and heavy folds of drapery are known from the Resurrection in the Art Institute of Chicago and Christ Driving the Money Changers from the Temple in Berlin. The Metropolitan Museum's Christ on the Cross, of about 1615 to 1620, is given to him on the same basis.","q2260399":"Cenni di Francesco di Ser Cenni was a Florentine painter recorded in the city from 1369 or 1370 until 1415; he may have died about 1425. His only signed work is the fresco cycle of the Legend of the True Cross in the Cappella della Croce di Giorno in San Francesco at Volterra, dated 1410, a large and lively narrative. Other documented works include a Madonna fresco of 1393, a triptych of 1400, a polyptych of 1408 and a Saint Jerome of 1411, and a couple of dozen more have been attributed to him by comparison with Volterra, among them a polyptych of about 1390 now in the Getty Museum. His manner belongs to late Gothic Florence, with bright colour and an elegant, narrative line. The Metropolitan Museum's Saint Catherine Disputing, with two donors, is dated possibly about 1380.","q21095000":"Cephas Giovanni Thompson was born in 1809 at Middleborough, Massachusetts, the second son of the portrait painter Cephas Thompson, from whom he received his first instruction. He became a fashionable portrait painter, and his best-known work is his portrait of Nathaniel Hawthorne, painted in 1851. In 1852 he took his family to Rome, where he lived until 1859 and was much admired by the American writers and artists there, among them Hawthorne, whose daughter Una played with his children. An unfinished family picture from his Roman years shows his skill at intimate group portraits free of religious or classical allusion. The Metropolitan Museum's Spring dates from 1838. After his return he worked in New York, where he died in 1888 at the age of seventy-nine. Little more is recorded of his patrons.","q5063492":"Cephas Thompson was born in 1775 at Middleborough, Massachusetts, and lived there most of his life. No formal training is recorded: from about 1788 to 1805 he cut more than fifty slate gravestones near his home, and he taught himself to paint portraits. From about 1800 to 1825 he made seasonal painting trips along the Atlantic seaboard, from Baltimore and Norfolk to Charleston and New Orleans, and he returned five times to Bristol, Rhode Island, where he painted over a hundred portraits, many for the DeWolf family, his most important early patrons. His sitters included Chief Justice John Marshall and Commodore Stephen Decatur, and George Washington Parke Custis, Washington's step-grandson, studied with him. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of his wife, Olivia Leonard, shows his plain, direct style. His son Cephas Giovanni also became a painter. He died in 1856.","q1982670":"Cesare Dandini was born in Florence in 1596 and died there in 1657. He studied in turn with Francesco Curradi, Cristofano Allori and Domenico Passignano, and joined the Accademia del Disegno in 1621. His brother Vincenzo, his nephew Pietro and Pietro's sons were painters too, which made the Dandini a Florentine family of artists. Cesare painted religious subjects, allegories and half-length figures of saints and young people with clear drawing, bold colour contrasts and a polished finish, comparable to that of his contemporary Carlo Dolci. Charity, of about 1630, is one of several allegorical figures among his works. His pupils included the young Stefano della Bella, who later became famous as an etcher. A Holy Family with the Infant Saint John rediscovered in 2020 has been linked to pictures in the Hermitage and the Metropolitan Museum.","q933026":"Giuseppe Cesari, called the Cavaliere d'Arpino after his birthplace in the hills south of Rome, was born in 1568 and came to Rome as a boy, entering the team of painters at the Vatican. By the 1590s he was one of the most favoured painters in the city, and Pope Clement VIII made him a knight. He ran a large studio and about 1593 to 1594 employed the young Caravaggio, who painted flowers and fruit for him. Cesari frescoed the vault of the Contarelli Chapel in San Luigi dei Francesi, which Caravaggio's canvases later completed, and he began a long cycle of frescoes in the Capitoline Palace. He also designed the mosaics of the dome of Saint Peter's. His Martyrdom of Saint Margaret dates from about 1608 to 1611. He died in Rome in 1640, his late Mannerist style long out of favour.","q35548":"Paul Cézanne was born in 1839 at Aix-en-Provence, the son of a banker, and studied law at his father's wish while taking drawing lessons; his school friend Émile Zola urged him to Paris in 1861. There he met Camille Pissarro, who taught him to paint outdoors and lighten his palette, and they worked together at Pontoise and Auvers, where he painted the House of Père Lacroix in 1873. He showed with the Impressionists in 1874 and 1877 but met with ridicule, and from the 1880s he worked mostly in Provence, building pictures from small, parallel strokes of colour. His repeated subjects were still lifes, portraits, bathers and Mont Sainte-Victoire. His first solo exhibition, in 1895, began his fame, and Picasso and Matisse took him as a founder of modern art. He died in 1906 after being caught in a storm while painting.","q18508680":"Thomas Chambers was born in 1808 at Whitby, Yorkshire, and emigrated to the United States in 1832. He worked in New York City, then in Boston and Albany, and had returned to New York by about 1857. He painted views of New York Harbor, the Hudson, the Connecticut Valley and storms at sea, often basing his compositions on engravings by William Henry Bartlett and others, which he adapted in a bold, flat, decorative style. His pictures sold cheaply, and he is thought to have gone back to England about 1866; later sources place his death in 1869. He was forgotten until 1942, when the discovery of a signed painting led collectors to link it with unattributed pictures, and he was given a first exhibition at the Macbeth Gallery in New York. More than sixty-five works have since been identified.","q207447":"Jean-Siméon Chardin was born in Paris in 1699, the son of a cabinetmaker, and trained with the history painters Pierre-Jacques Cazes and Noël-Nicolas Coypel. He joined the Academy in 1728 as a painter of animals and fruit with The Ray, and later became its treasurer. For decades he painted still lifes of kitchen utensils, bread, fish and fruit, and quiet scenes of servants and children, such as the Met's Soap Bubbles, building up thick, soft layers of colour with a dignity that the critic Diderot hailed as true to nature. Louis XV and collectors across Europe bought his pictures, and in old age, with failing eyes, he turned to pastel portraits. He died in 1779.","q113536":"Charles Antoine Coypel was born in Paris in 1694 into the leading French family of court painters; his father Antoine Coypel was first painter to the king, and he trained under him. In 1747 he succeeded to the same post and became director of the Academy. He painted religious and mythological subjects and portraits, designed a celebrated series of tapestry cartoons of Don Quixote for the Gobelins, and wrote plays and lectures on art. The Metropolitan Museum's double portrait of François de Jullienne and his wife Marie Elisabeth, dated 1743, is a pastel, a medium well suited to the lighter taste of the Rococo; the Julliennes were a wealthy Paris family of dyers who were patrons of Watteau. Coypel died in Paris in 1752.","q771320":"Charles Bargue was born in Paris in 1825 or 1826 and died there in 1883. He was a painter and lithographer who worked closely with the academic master Jean-Léon Gérôme, and with him made the Cours de dessin, a drawing course of lithographed plates after casts, old master drawings and the live model, published by the dealer Goupil in the later 1860s and early 1870s. Generations of art students learned to draw from it, and Vincent van Gogh copied its plates as a beginner. As a painter, Bargue worked in Gérôme's polished manner, mostly on Orientalist and genre subjects, such as A Bashi-Bazouk, an Ottoman irregular soldier, and A Footman Sleeping, both in the Metropolitan Museum. Little else is recorded of his life here.","q21408533":"Charles C. Hofmann, born in Germany in 1820 and dead in 1882, taught art to deaf pupils in Philadelphia before mental illness brought him, in the late 1870s, to the almshouse of Berks County, Pennsylvania, where he is recorded as an inmate in his last years. He painted a remarkable series of views of the almshouse and of farms in the neighbourhood, among them the Berks County Almshouse of 1878 and the View of Benjamin Reber's Farm of 1872, in a manner that combines the precise detail of a surveyor's plan with the bold outlines and bright colour of folk art. Hofmann has become one of the best-known American folk painters, and his views of rural Pennsylvania are valued as historical records as well as pictures.","q21497714":"Charles Caleb Ward, who lived from about 1831 to 1896, is catalogued by the Metropolitan Museum as an American painter. His Coming Events Cast Their Shadows Before, painted in oil on a wooden panel in 1871, is a genre scene of boys, girls and a dog, with horses and advertisements in view. The title is a line from Thomas Campbell's poem Lochiel's Warning of 1802, here turned to humorous use. Pictures of children and their everyday amusements were popular with American buyers after the Civil War. The painting came to the Museum in 1967 with the bequest of Susan Vanderpoel Clark. Little else is recorded here about Ward's training, patrons or other works, and nothing certain about where he lived or worked.","q5076001":"Charles Cromwell Ingham was born in Dublin in 1796 and trained at the Dublin Society's school before emigrating to New York in 1816. He became the most fashionable painter of women in the city, noted for the smooth, glossy, almost enamelled finish of his portraits, with sitters in rich silks and satins. In 1825 and 1826 he was one of the founders of the National Academy of Design, and he exhibited there for many years. He died in New York in 1863. His Washington portrait of Cora Livingston, about 1833, shows a young woman of one of the leading families of New York, the Livingstons. His style marks the transition from the plainer portrait of the early republic to the more sentimental and decorative taste of the Victorian age.","q2958219":"Charles de Coubertin was born in 1822 and died in 1908. A painter of this name and these dates was the father of Pierre de Coubertin, the founder of the modern Olympic Games, and the two are probably the same man. The Metropolitan Museum's Halt of Caravans at the Wells of Saba (Beersheba) in the Desert South of Hebron, painted in 1850, is an Orientalist scene of travellers and camels at a well in the Holy Land. Such subjects, which joined biblical associations to the colour of Near Eastern travel, were popular with French audiences in the years when painters began journeying to Egypt, Syria and Palestine. The painting was given to the Museum in 2017. Beyond this, little is recorded here of his training, teachers or career.","q2744998":"Charles Dominique Joseph Eisen was born at Valenciennes in 1720 and died at Brussels in 1778, but his career was made in Paris, where he became one of the most sought-after designers of book illustrations of his day. His vignettes and plates for the Fermiers Généraux edition of La Fontaine's Contes (1762) are the best known, engraved by the leading craftsmen of the time from his drawings. He also painted, though paintings by him are rare. The Metropolitan Museum owns two small oil panels, each titled Putti with a Medallion, given by J. Pierpont Morgan in 1906. Playful children of this kind were a favourite motif of Rococo decoration. Other details of his training and patrons are not recorded here.","q2958342":"Charles-Edouard de Beaumont was born at Lannion in Brittany in 1821 and died in Paris in 1888. He worked as a lithographer, illustrator and painter. The Metropolitan Museum's In the Sun, painted in 1875, is a scene of contemporary life with sleeping figures, men and women, parasols and a cemetery in view, the kind of lightly told modern subject that found a market among Parisian and American buyers in the 1870s. It came to the Museum in 1946 from the estate of Marie L. Russell. His training, teachers and patrons are not recorded here, and little else of his career is documented in the sources consulted.","q21985309":"Charles F. Berger is recorded as active from 1841 into the 1890s, a long career for a portrait painter in the age of photography; the Metropolitan Museum catalogues him as American. The one picture by him in the museum is a Portrait of a Gentleman of 1843, a watercolour on ivory, the usual medium of the portrait miniature. In America the miniature flourished until the 1840s, when the daguerreotype, introduced in 1839, offered cheaper likenesses and drove many miniaturists to other work or to photography itself. The portrait was bought for the museum in 2006. His birthplace, training, teachers and patrons and the name of the sitter are not recorded here, and nothing further can be said with certainty.","q252357":"Charles-François Daubigny was born in Paris in 1817 and began as an illustrator and etcher, drawing for publishers while he studied painting. He travelled in Italy in 1836 and returned to a career in landscape, painting the rivers of France from the 1850s. From 1857 he worked from a studio boat, the Botin, and recorded his river trips in an album of etchings, the Voyage en bateau (1862). His house at Auvers-sur-Oise became a meeting place for painters, and in July 1890 Van Gogh painted Daubigny's garden there as a tribute. The National Gallery's Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois, Auvers (Washerwomen) and Sunset on the River show his river subjects; The Farm (1855) is earlier. He died in 1878, admired by Monet and the Impressionists.","q16538493":"Charles-François Marchal was born in Paris in 1825 and died there in 1877. The Metropolitan Museum's Penelope, painted about 1868, shows the wife of Odysseus busy with needlework, an allusion to the shroud she wove by day and unpicked by night to put off her suitors while her husband was away. Classical heroines of this kind were popular subjects at the Paris Salon in the years of the Second Empire. The picture was given to the Museum in 1917 by Mrs. Adolph Obrig in memory of her husband. His teachers, patrons and the rest of his career are not recorded in the sources consulted, and little more can be said of him with certainty.","q5077811":"Charles Fraser was born in Charleston, South Carolina, in 1782 and died there in 1860. He studied law and practised it for some years, but painting was his real interest, and he became the most important miniature painter in Charleston. Influenced by Edward Greene Malbone, who worked in the city, he painted small portraits in watercolour on ivory, such as Dr. Francis Kinloch Huger and Colonel James Elliott McPherson, for the families of the Carolina low country. The Metropolitan Museum owns six, dated between 1819 and 1833. In 1857 the city of Charleston honoured him with a retrospective exhibition of more than three hundred of his works, a rare tribute for an American artist of his time.","q2959122":"Charles Frederick Ulrich was born in New York in 1858 and trained at the National Academy of Design before going to Munich, where he studied at the Royal Academy and learned the careful, detailed manner of German genre painting. He lived for years in Venice and later in Germany, and died in Berlin in 1908. His subjects were craftsmen and street life in Venice and, in America, the arrival of immigrants, in In the Land of Promise, Castle Garden (1884). Glass Blowers of Murano (1886) shows men and women at work in a glassmaker's interior, painted with close attention to light and gesture. It was given to the Metropolitan Museum in the year it was painted by several gentlemen, a sign of the reputation it had won.","q125409":"Charles Jacque was born in Paris in 1813 and began as an engraver and etcher before devoting himself to painting. About 1849 he settled at Barbizon, where he joined Millet and Théodore Rousseau and became the group's specialist in farm animals: sheep, pigs and poultry in barnyards and on the edge of woods. The Sheepfold (1857) is a typical subject. He was a prolific etcher whose hundreds of plates helped to revive the art in France, and he kept poultry himself and wrote on poultry-keeping. He died in Paris in 1894. Barbizon pictures of country life were eagerly bought by American collectors in the later nineteenth century, and the Metropolitan Museum's two panels came from such buyers.","q860154":"Charles Joseph Natoire was born at Nîmes in 1700 and trained in Paris with François Lemoyne, winning the Prix de Rome in 1721 and studying in Rome for several years. Back in Paris, he became a leading painter of the Rococo, decorating the Hôtel de Soubise with scenes from the story of Psyche and designing the cartoons for Beauvais tapestries of Don Quixote. In 1751 he became director of the French Academy in Rome, where he taught young pensioners such as Fragonard and Hubert Robert and led them on drawing trips into the countryside. He stayed in Rome until 1775 and died nearby, at Castel Gandolfo, in 1777. His small copper of The Rebuke of Adam and Eve is dated 1740.","q271676":"Charles Le Brun was born in Paris in 1619, the son of a sculptor, and trained under Simon Vouet before going to Rome with Poussin in 1642. Back in Paris he worked for Chancellor Séguier and the finance minister Fouquet, whose château at Vaux-le-Vicomte he helped decorate. When Fouquet fell, Louis XIV and Colbert took Le Brun into the crown's service: he directed the Gobelins factory, shaped the Academy's teaching, and in 1664 became first painter to the king. He led the vast decoration of Versailles, including the Hall of Mirrors. His lecture on the expression of the passions guided academic teaching for generations. The Jabach Family (about 1660) shows a Cologne banker and collector with his family. After Colbert's death in 1683 his influence waned, and he died in Paris in 1690.","q5080338":"Charles Loring Elliott (1812 to 1868) was an American portrait painter from upstate New York who settled in New York City in 1845 and became, by the 1850s, one of the most sought-after portraitists in the country. The National Gallery owns three of his portraits of well-known men: Captain Warren Delano (about 1852), the painter William Sidney Mount (about 1850) and the poet and newspaper editor William Cullen Bryant (about 1854). His portraits are direct and vigorous, with strong modelling and a plain, unflattering truth that contemporaries found refreshing; his sitters were merchants, writers and artists. Mount, a painter of Long Island scenes, died in 1868, the same year as Elliott himself. The Delano portrait was a gift of Frederic A. Delano, a member of the sitter's family.","q21994041":"Charles Noel Flagg was born in 1848 and died in 1916. He worked as a portrait painter in Hartford, Connecticut, where Mark Twain lived with his family in the 1870s and 1880s. His portrait of the author, painted in 1890 and now in the Metropolitan Museum, shows the writer at about the height of his fame. It was given to the Museum in 1917 by Ellen Earle Flagg, the year after the artist's death. Beyond these facts, little is recorded here about his training, teachers or clients, and he is known today chiefly for this portrait of one of the most famous Americans of his day.","q21853239":"Charles Paul Jérôme de Bréa was born about 1739 and died in 1820; the Metropolitan Museum records him as French and holds one work by him, a miniature on ivory of Benjamin Franklin, dated 1777. It is a copy after the portrait by Jean-Baptiste Greuze painted in that year, when Franklin was in Paris as the envoy of the new United States and a figure of great celebrity, whose likeness was in demand in every format, from paintings and medals to prints and miniatures. Copies by lesser hands like this one carried his image through France and beyond. The miniature came to the Museum in 1968 as a gift of J. William Middendorf II. Nothing certain is recorded here of de Bréa's training, patrons or other works.","q2959962":"Charles Peale Polk was born in Maryland in 1767, the nephew of Charles Willson Peale, and learned to paint in his uncle's circle in Philadelphia in the 1780s. He worked as a portrait painter in Pennsylvania, Maryland and Virginia until his death in 1822. He is best known for his portraits of George Washington, dozens of them copied from his uncle's life portraits and sold to a public eager for the President's likeness; the Metropolitan Museum's example dates from about 1790. His flat, linear style is plainer than Peale's. The Museum also owns his portrait of Joseph Howell Jr. of about 1787, an early work.","q18600457":"Charles Philips was born in London in 1703 and died there in 1747. He belonged to the generation of English painters who made small group portraits, called conversation pieces, fashionable in the 1730s, working in the same field as William Hogarth and Joseph Highmore. His pictures show families and friends at home in carefully furnished rooms. The Strong Family, dated 1732, is a family group in an interior with playing cards, and it came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1944 as a gift of Robert Lehman. Such pictures were painted for gentry and prosperous middle-class families who wanted themselves, their children and their houses recorded together. Little else is recorded here about his training or patrons, and he is overshadowed now by Hogarth.","q3384376":"Charles Pierre Cior was a French miniaturist, born in 1769 and recorded after 1838; the Metropolitan Museum's entry gives no places for his birth or death. The one work in the museum, Portrait of an Officer, is a round miniature on ivory three inches across, showing a man in uniform. Miniatures on ivory were the standard small portraits of the age, kept in cases or worn as jewellery, and they were popular with officers of the Revolution and the Empire and their families. The miniature was given to the Museum in 1925 in memory of Louis V. Bell. Nothing certain is recorded here about Cior's training, patrons or career.","charles_po_rson":"Charles Poërson was born in 1609 in Lorraine and trained in Paris under Simon Vouet, the leading painter of Louis XIII's court, whose busy workshop produced altarpieces, decorations and tapestry designs on a large scale. Poërson became one of Vouet's collaborators and followers and painted for Paris churches. He died in 1667. The Cleveland Museum of Art's Rest on the Flight into Egypt, about 1640, an oak panel of about 14 by 18 inches, was long given to Vouet himself and has only recently been attributed to Poërson. It was made as a small devotional picture of the Holy Family at rest, with an angel bringing fruit to the infant Christ. His son Charles-François later directed the French Academy in Rome.","q3164608":"Charles Rémond was born in Paris in 1795 and died there in 1875. He trained as a landscape painter in the tradition of historical landscape, the composed classical view with ruins and figures from the Bible or mythology, and spent the early 1820s in Rome, where French landscapists studied the ruins and the Campagna. There, working outdoors, he made the oil study now in the Metropolitan Museum, a View of the Colosseum and the Arch of Constantine from the Palatine, painted about 1822 to 1824 on paper and later mounted on canvas. Small, quickly painted studies from nature like this were private records and not for sale. Little else is recorded here about his career.","q325633":"Charles Robert Leslie was born in London in 1794 to American parents and grew up in Philadelphia, where he trained as an artist, before sailing to England in 1811, where he joined the American painters Washington Allston and Samuel F. B. Morse. He studied at the Royal Academy, became a member in 1826, and made his name with small, humorous and sentimental scenes from Shakespeare, Cervantes, Molière and Sterne, which were often engraved. Queen Victoria commissioned him to paint her coronation (1838) and the christening of her eldest child. In 1833 he spent some months as drawing master at West Point. He wrote a well-regarded life of his friend John Constable (1843), became professor of painting at the Academy in 1848, and died in London in 1859.","q20119904":"Charles Robertson was an Irish miniature painter who lived from about 1760 to 1821. Ireland had a lively school of miniaturists in the later eighteenth century, centred on Dublin, and the Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man, dated 1810, is a small portrait on ivory of the kind such painters produced for gentry families, officers and merchants. Miniatures of this period were kept in cases or worn as jewellery, and painters built up a likeness in tiny strokes of watercolour. The sitter of the Museum's miniature is not named. The portrait came to the Museum in 1962 in a bequest from Millie Bruhl Fredrick. Beyond his dates, nothing certain is recorded here of Robertson's training, teachers or clients.","q5082397":"Charles Schreyvogel was born in New York in 1861 to German parents, worked as a lithographer's apprentice, and studied painting in Munich. Back in New York he took up Western subjects, but he worked at home in Hoboken, New Jersey, where he posed models in cavalry uniforms in his backyard and painted them with close attention to detail. My Bunkie, finished in 1899, shows a trooper swooping down to rescue a dismounted comrade under fire. It won the National Academy of Design's Clarke Prize in 1900 and made his name, and he became, with Frederic Remington, one of the best-known painters of Indian-war cavalry scenes. He died in 1912, aged fifty-one, and friends gave My Bunkie to the Metropolitan Museum by subscription that year.","q466921":"Charles Sprague Pearce was born in Boston in 1851 and went to Paris in 1873, where he studied under Léon Bonnat, the portraitist known for his sober realism. Travels in Egypt and Algeria in the 1870s gave him the subjects of his early Orientalist pictures, such as The Arab Jeweler (about 1882), a craftsman at work among his tools. From 1885 he lived at Auvers-sur-Oise, north of Paris, and turned to scenes of French peasant life, painted with sober colour and a classical sense of composition. He exhibited at the Paris Salon and won medals there. He remained in France for the rest of his life and died at Auvers in 1914. The Metropolitan Museum received The Arab Jeweler as a gift in 1922.","q323928":"Charles-Théodore Frère was born in Paris in 1814 and became one of the leading French painters of the Near East. He travelled in Algeria, Egypt, Syria and Palestine and exhibited his scenes of desert caravans, tents and bazaars at the Paris Salon for decades. His brother Pierre-Édouard Frère was also a painter, of cottage interiors and children. Jerusalem from the Mount of Olives, dated by 1880, shows the Holy City from the traditional viewpoint of pilgrims and artists, with camels, tents and men in the foreground. He died in Paris in 1888. Orientalist painting of this kind was popular with collectors in Britain and America, and the Metropolitan Museum's example came with the Wolfe bequest of 1887.","q18508068":"Charles Wesley Jarvis was born in 1812 and died in 1868. He was a son of the New York portrait painter John Wesley Jarvis, one of the busiest portraitists of the early republic, and he worked as a portrait painter in the same tradition. The Metropolitan Museum's Mrs. Adrian Baucker Holmes, painted about 1850, is an oil portrait of a woman, given to the museum in 1913 by Mrs. J. H. Blauvelt. Portraits of this kind were commissioned by families in the years before photography spread, and many are known today only from museum holdings. Beyond his dates, little is recorded here about Jarvis's training, patrons or career.","q454945":"Charles Willson Peale was born in 1741 in Maryland and began as a saddler in Annapolis before teaching himself portraiture. Maryland patrons paid for him to study in London from 1767 to 1769 with Benjamin West. Settling in Philadelphia in 1776, he served as a militia officer in the Revolution and painted George Washington from life seven times, beginning in 1772; George Washington at Princeton shows the general with his horse and a groom. Peale also painted Henry Knox and Arthur St. Clair, in miniature. In 1786 he opened a museum of natural history and art in Philadelphia, where he later showed the mastodon skeleton he had excavated in 1801. He helped found the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts and named his sons Raphaelle, Rembrandt, Rubens and Titian. He died in 1827.","charlotte_eustache_sophie_de_fuligny_damas_marqu":"Charlotte Eustache Sophie de Fuligny Damas was born in Paris in 1741 into the old aristocracy, became the marquise de Grollier, and died at Épinay-sur-Seine in 1828. She is known as an amateur painter of flowers and fruit, a pursuit open to women of her rank when professional careers were not. Her Still Life with a Vase of Flowers, Melon, Peaches, and Grapes is dated 1780 and brings together a bouquet and ripe fruit in the manner of the Dutch and Flemish still lifes then fashionable with French collectors. The Metropolitan Museum acquired it in 2022. Little else is recorded here about her training, teachers or how many pictures she painted.","q265820":"William Merritt Chase was born in 1849 in Williamsburg, Indiana, studied in Indianapolis and New York, and in 1872 went to Munich, where he trained at the Royal Academy and absorbed the dark, fluent brushwork of the Munich school. He returned to New York in 1878 and set up in the Tenth Street Studio Building, where his large studio, filled with curios and visited by patrons, made him a leading figure of the city's art world. He taught for decades at the Art Students League and founded his own school, which became the Parsons School of Design, and from 1891 to 1902 he ran a summer school at Shinnecock Hills on Long Island. His later work grew lighter and more Impressionist, as in A Friendly Call (1895) and Gathering Autumn Flowers, painted at Shinnecock. He died in 1916.","childe_hassam":"Childe Hassam was born in 1859 in Dorchester, Massachusetts, and trained as a wood engraver and illustrator in Boston before turning to painting. He studied in Paris at the Académie Julian from 1886 to 1889, where he took up Impressionism, and on his return settled in New York. His sunlit scenes of city streets, Boston Common, Gloucester and the Isles of Shoals off New Hampshire made him the most prominent American Impressionist, and he joined the group called the Ten American Painters in 1898. In his Flag series, from 1916 to 1919, he painted the avenues of New York hung with Allied flags. Sweeping Snow, from the 1890s, is a small winter city scene, only about nine inches high. He was prolific and successful, and he died in 1935.","chinese_painter":"Chinese Painter is the label the Metropolitan Museum gives to unnamed Chinese artists who made pictures for foreign customers, above all Americans. Until 1842 foreign merchants were allowed to trade only at Canton, where they lived in a row of warehouses called the Thirteen Factories, and a local industry of painters grew up to supply them with ship portraits, views of the factories, portraits of merchants and captains, and pictures on glass. The Museum's four examples are Liberty (about 1800), painted in oil on glass for the American market; a portrait of William Conklin (about 1800); a portrait of the ship John W. Brewer (about 1845); and a small oil on copper of the burning of the Factories in Canton, which happened in December 1856. The painters' names are lost.","chinese_qing_dynasty":"Archery Contest and Procession by a Lake are two nineteenth-century paintings in the National Gallery of Art, catalogued under the name of the Qing dynasty, which ruled China from 1644 to 1911. Their painters are not named. Both are in oil on cloth, cotton in the case of Archery Contest, a Western medium that in nineteenth-century China was used chiefly in the ports where foreigners traded, for sale to visitors, rather than in the traditional art of ink and colour on silk or paper. Archery held a special place under the Qing, since the ruling Manchus prized mounted archery as a martial skill. Both pictures came to the Gallery as gifts of Dr. Catherine Lilly Bacon. Nothing more is recorded about their makers.","q21497724":"Almost nothing is known of the painter called Chipman. The National Gallery of Art lists him only by a surname, as American, and gives his dates as 1770 to 1910, a span so wide that it records uncertainty and not a lifetime. His one picture there, Melons and Grapes, dates from the middle of the nineteenth century and is catalogued as a naive still life in oil on canvas. It came to the Gallery with the gift of Edgar William and Bernice Chrysler Garbisch, who gathered American folk painting. Fruit pieces were among the most popular subjects for self-taught and amateur painters of the time. Nothing is recorded of Chipman's first name, teachers or patrons.","q381458":"Christen Købke was born in Copenhagen in 1810, the son of a baker whose family lived at the citadel, Kastellet, and he remained attached to Copenhagen all his life. He studied at the Royal Academy from 1822 and, from about 1828, under Christoffer Wilhelm Eckersberg, who taught the young Golden Age painters to look at nature closely. Købke painted the citadel, Frederiksborg Castle, the Copenhagen lakes and portraits of family and friends, among them his sister Sophie and his brother Valdemar, with light-filled, calmly observant realism. He travelled to Italy in 1838 to 1840. Patrons were few, and he died of pneumonia in 1848, aged thirty-seven. Neglected for decades, he is now regarded as one of the finest painters of the Danish Golden Age.","q1028749":"Christian Friedrich Zincke was born in Dresden about 1683 to 1685 and came to London in 1704, where he trained with the enamel painter Charles Boit. He became the leading enamel miniaturist in England, and in 1732 he was appointed cabinet painter to Frederick, Prince of Wales. Enamel miniatures, painted on small plates of copper coated in white enamel and fired, kept their colour better than watercolour on ivory, and Zincke's were prized for their fine finish. He retired in 1746 and died in 1767. The Metropolitan Museum has four, including Richard Abell (1724) and Mrs. Vanderbank (about 1730), both small portraits showing the smooth, polished surface that made his name.","q334200":"Christian Krohg was born at Vestre Aker, near Oslo, in 1852. He first studied law, then turned to art, training in Berlin and Karlsruhe, and spent years in Paris and at the artists' colony of Skagen in Denmark. The leading Norwegian naturalist, he painted the poor, fishermen and working women with unsparing honesty, in The Net Mender (1879) and the large Struggle for Existence (1889). His novel Albertine (1886), about a seamstress driven into prostitution, was confiscated by the police and caused a scandal, and he later painted its heroine in the police doctor's waiting room. With his wife Oda he was at the heart of the Kristiania Bohemians. He taught a generation of Norwegian painters, became the first head of the State Academy of Art in 1909, and died in 1925.","q6071148":"Christian Richter was a Swedish painter of portrait miniatures, born in 1678 and dead in 1732. The Metropolitan Museum owns one example, a portrait of John Churchill, first Duke of Marlborough, painted on vellum, a thin sheet of prepared calfskin that was a usual support for miniatures before ivory became common in the early eighteenth century. Marlborough (1650 to 1722) was the commander who won the battles of Blenheim, Ramillies and Oudenarde in the War of the Spanish Succession, and his likeness was in demand in many forms. The miniature came with the bequest of Mary Clark Thompson in 1923. Little more is recorded here about Richter's training, patrons or career, and the date of the portrait is not known.","q536581":"Christian Wilhelm Ernst Dietrich, called Dietricy, was born in Weimar in 1712, the son of a court painter, and trained in Dresden, where he became court painter to Augustus III, elector of Saxony and king of Poland, and later a professor at the Dresden Academy and director of the painting school of the Meissen porcelain factory. He worked in many manners: landscapes after Italian and Dutch models, religious scenes in the manner of Rembrandt such as Christ Healing the Sick (1742), peasant interiors after Ostade, and mythologies. He was also a prolific etcher whose pastiches of old masters were so skilful that they were sometimes taken for originals. He died in Dresden in 1774, among the most celebrated German painters of his time.","q2287607":"Christoffel van den Berghe was born in Antwerp about 1590 and worked in Middelburg in Zeeland, where he is recorded in 1628 or later. Many painters from the southern Netherlands settled in the northern cities after the fall of Antwerp in 1585, and Middelburg had a large community of such immigrants. His A Winter Landscape with Ice Skaters and an Imaginary Castle, painted on panel about 1615 to 1620, belongs to the winter-scene tradition of the early Dutch Republic, in which skaters, sledges and villagers enjoy the frozen canals and fields beneath a fantastic castle. The Metropolitan Museum received it from the Markus bequest in 2005. Little else is recorded here about his training, teachers or patrons.","q363823":"Christoffer Wilhelm Eckersberg, called the father of Danish painting, was born in 1783 in southern Jutland and trained at the Royal Academy in Copenhagen before going to Paris in 1810 to study in the circle of Jacques-Louis David. In Rome from 1813 to 1816 he painted outdoors, in precise, cool, brightly lit views, and made friends with the sculptor Thorvaldsen. View of the Cloaca Maxima, Rome (1814) shows the arched outlet of the ancient sewer near the church of San Giorgio in Velabro. As professor at the Copenhagen Academy from 1818, he taught perspective and painting from nature to a generation that includes Købke and Constantin Hansen, and wrote a treatise on perspective. He also painted ships and portraits. He died in 1853.","q312616":"Petrus Christus was born at Baarle, in Brabant, in the early fifteenth century, and in 1444 was admitted as a citizen of Bruges, where he spent the rest of his career. He probably took over the workshop of Jan van Eyck after Van Eyck's death in 1441, and he carried on the older master's smooth oil technique and love of detail while giving his figures a simplified, rounded solidity. His works include the Lamentation, the Portrait of a Young Girl in Berlin and the dated Portrait of a Carthusian of 1446. The Nativity, about 1450, and the paired portraits of a male and a female donor, about 1455, are in the National Gallery of Art. He died in Bruges in 1475 or 1476.","q366212":"Frederic Edwin Church was born at Hartford, Connecticut, in 1826 and studied with Thomas Cole at Catskill, New York, from 1844, becoming the leading landscape painter of the next generation. Inspired by the writings of Alexander von Humboldt, he went to South America in 1853 and 1857 and painted the Andes in huge, detailed canvases. Heart of the Andes, shown alone in a New York gallery in 1859 with an admission charge, drew crowds and sold for ten thousand dollars, a record for a living American painter. Twilight in the Wilderness (1860) is in Cleveland. Church later travelled to Europe and the Near East and built a Persian-style house, Olana, above the Hudson. Rheumatism crippled his painting hand, and taste moved away from his work, but it has recovered. He died in New York in 1900.","q928350":"Ludovico Cardi, called Cigoli after his native village near Florence, was born in 1559 and trained in Florence with Alessandro Allori and Santi di Tito. Absorbing Tuscan drawing and the softer colour of Correggio and Barocci, he became one of the leading Florentine painters of his generation, and also worked as an architect and wrote on perspective. He was a friend of Galileo, and in the dome of the Pauline Chapel in Santa Maria Maggiore in Rome, painted for Pope Paul V, he showed the Virgin standing on a moon pitted with craters, as Galileo had seen it through the telescope. The Adoration of the Shepherds with Saint Catherine of Alexandria is dated 1599. In 1613 he was made a Knight of Malta, and he died in Rome the same year.","q8453":"Giovanni Battista Cima, called Cima da Conegliano after his native town in the hills north of Venice, was born about 1459 and settled in Venice in the 1480s. He learned from the example of Giovanni Bellini and became one of the leading altarpiece painters in the city, known for calm, luminous Madonnas and saints standing in clear light against hills like those of his homeland, sometimes with a castle on a rise. His surfaces are clean and almost enamel-like, and his colour is cool and silvery. He painted for churches in Venice, Parma and the Veneto and was at work until about 1517. The Metropolitan Museum's Three Saints: Roch, Anthony Abbot, and Lucy, of about 1513, and the Washington Saint Jerome in the Wilderness show his devotion to saints in landscape.","q3559930":"Vincenzo Civerchio was a painter and architect of the Lombard school, born at Crema, then under Venetian rule, in about 1460 to 1470, and died in 1544. The National Gallery of Art owns one panel by him, Christ Instructing Peter and John to Prepare for the Passover, painted in tempera in 1504. It illustrates the passage in the Gospel of Luke in which Christ sends two disciples ahead to make ready the meal that became the Last Supper. Panels of this kind were often part of a larger altarpiece or a cycle of the Passion, but the picture's original setting is not recorded here. Little else is known here of his teachers, patrons or reputation, which has remained largely local to northern Italy.","q354832":"Pieter Claesz was born about 1597 at Berchem near Antwerp and settled in Haarlem, where he is recorded by 1617 and joined the painters' guild in 1620. With Willem Claesz. Heda he led the Haarlem school of breakfast pieces: tables with a roll of bread, a ham, a pewter plate, a glass of wine, half a lemon and a knife, painted in subtle greys, greens and golds and set in a low, even light. He also made vanitas pictures with skulls, hourglasses and snuffed candles, reminders that earthly things pass. Still Life with Peacock Pie (1627) is a more lavish banquet piece, with a pie crowned by the bird's own feathers. His son Nicolaes Berchem became a landscape painter. Claesz died in Haarlem in 1660, and is now counted a master of Dutch still life.","q265398":"Clara Peeters is one of the few women known to have painted still lifes in the Netherlands in the early seventeenth century, and very little is documented of her life. The Metropolitan Museum gives her birth as about 1587, probably at Mechelen; her paintings are dated from 1607 to the early 1620s, and she is thought to have worked in Antwerp. She painted laid tables with cheese, fish, bread, game and fine glass and silver, and early flower pieces such as A Bouquet of Flowers, of about 1612. In many pictures she placed a tiny reflection of herself at her easel in the polished surface of a goblet or pitcher, like a hidden signature. Her work reached collectors in Spain and elsewhere, and she is now recognised as an important pioneer of the genre.","q355563":"Claude Déruet was born at Nancy about 1588 and died there in 1660. He spent his career at the court of the dukes of Lorraine, for whom he worked as court painter, and his style belongs to late Mannerism, with elongated, elegant figures, bright colour and a taste for pageantry. The Metropolitan Museum's Triumph of the Amazons and Departure of the Amazons, both of the 1620s, show armoured women warriors, plumed and on horseback, in landscape settings. The pair came as part of the Harry G. Sperling bequest of 1971. Little else is recorded here about his training, teachers and patrons, although Lorraine in his day also produced the etcher Jacques Callot and the painter Georges de La Tour.","q1118436":"Claude Guy Hallé was born in Paris in 1652 and died there in 1736. He came from a family of painters, being the son of Daniel Hallé and the father of Noël Hallé, and he trained in the Parisian academic tradition and became a member of the Royal Academy. He painted religious and historical subjects for churches and the court. The Metropolitan Museum's The Restoration of the Catholic Religion in Strasbourg, about 1681, belongs to the years when Louis XIV annexed the city and returned its cathedral to Catholic worship; it shows angels, a cross, and men and women in an allegorical celebration of that event. The Museum acquired it in the Harry G. Sperling bequest of 1971. Little else is recorded here of his patrons or reputation.","claude_joseph_vernet":"Claude-Joseph Vernet was born at Avignon in 1714, the son of a decorative painter, and went to Rome in 1734, where he lived for some twenty years painting views and sea pieces. The Waterfalls at Tivoli, of 1737, is one of the first large pictures from his Roman years. His harbours, storms, shipwrecks and moonlit coasts won him patrons among English Grand Tourists and the Roman nobility, and he returned to Paris in 1753 at the request of Louis XV, who commissioned from him a series of views of the ports of France; he completed fifteen of a planned twenty-four. Diderot wrote of his Salon pictures with delight. His son Carle and his grandson Horace were also painters. He died in Paris in 1789, the leading marine painter of his age.","q153472":"Joos van Cleve was born around 1485, probably in the Cleves region, and died in Antwerp in 1540. He is recorded as a master of the Antwerp painters' guild in 1511 and later served as its dean, running a busy workshop; his son Cornelis also became a painter. For many years he was known only as the Master of the Death of the Virgin, after an altarpiece, until scholars matched that name to Joos. He painted altarpieces, such as the Metropolitan's Crucifixion and Last Judgment, popular half-length Virgins whose soft modelling shows the influence of Leonardo, and incisive portraits. About 1530 to 1531 he worked in France and portrayed Francis I and Queen Eleanor. Today he is valued as one of the leading Antwerp painters of his generation.","q336747":"François Clouet was born about 1515 to 1520, probably at Tours, the son of the court painter Jean Clouet, and succeeded his father in the royal service in 1540 or 1541, becoming painter and valet de chambre to Francis I and to his successors Henry II, Francis II and Charles IX. He is known for portraits, many of them drawings in coloured chalks of members of the court, which were kept as records and copied by other painters, and for a handful of fine panels, among them A Lady in Her Bath, of about 1571, in Washington, long taken to portray Diane de Poitiers, though the sitter is uncertain. He died in Paris in 1572. His cool, exact manner set the standard for French court portraiture.","q20054825":"Niccolò Codazzi was born in 1642 and died in 1693; he was the son of Viviano Codazzi, a Lombard-born painter of architectural views and ruins who worked in Rome and Naples, and he followed his father's specialty. Painters of this kind, called prospettisti, made pictures of buildings, ruins and imaginary architecture, usually with small figures added by a colleague. The National Gallery's The Basilica of Constantine with a Doric Colonnade, of 1685 to 1690, shows the huge vaulted ruin in the Roman Forum, a favourite subject for such views, framed here by an imagined colonnade. The picture comes from the McCrindle collection. Little else is recorded here about his career, patrons or reputation.","q21408232":"Elias V. Coe (1794 to 1843) was an American portrait painter of whom little is recorded. The National Gallery of Art owns two of his portraits of 1837, Mrs. Phebe Houston and Henry W. Houston, a husband and wife painted as a pair, in a plain, flat, naive style that relies on firm outline and careful rendering of faces, clothes and ornaments rather than on depth or shadow. Pairs of portraits like these were typical of the work of local and travelling portrait painters in the American countryside before photography, who were paid a modest fee and finished a likeness within days. The pictures come from the Garbisch collection of American folk painting. Coe's training, birthplace and patrons are not recorded here.","q347455":"Jules Coignet was a French landscape painter, born in Paris in 1798 and died in 1860. Few details of his teachers or patrons are given here. The Metropolitan's View of Beirut, dated 1844, belongs to the French vogue for the Levant, when painters and travellers brought home sweeping views of Eastern cities and harbours for a Parisian public hungry for Oriental subjects. Beirut was then a small Ottoman port, and such pictures are now valued as records of a place before its great growth. Coignet exhibited at the Paris Salon, and he is now a minor figure, known mainly to specialists in French landscape and Orientalist painting.","q334001":"Thomas Cole was born in 1801 at Bolton-le-Moors in Lancashire and emigrated with his family to the United States in 1818, working as an engraver before turning to painting. In 1825 his Hudson River views, spotted in a New York shop window by the painters John Trumbull, Asher Durand and William Dunlap, brought him sudden fame, and he is regarded as the founder of the Hudson River School. He settled at Catskill, New York, travelled in Europe in 1829 to 1832 and again in 1841 to 1842, and painted wild White Mountain and Catskill scenery as well as allegorical series: The Course of Empire (1833 to 1836) and The Voyage of Life, of which the National Gallery's four canvases of 1842 are the second version. He also entered the Ohio Capitol competition. He died in 1848.","q21498216":"The Conant Limner is the name given to an unknown painter who made the portrait Sophia Burpee Conant, about 1813, in the National Gallery of Art. A limner is an old word for a portrait painter, and in American art history such names are given to anonymous artists known only from their works. The name is a modern convenience, evidently taken from the family of the sitter, and the dates of 1733 to 1873 in the catalogue are not those of a life but a wide guess. The picture is an oil on canvas of about twenty-two by seventeen inches in the naive style, and it comes from the Garbisch collection of American folk painting. Nothing is recorded of the painter's real name, training or patrons.","q1126773":"Conrad Faber von Creuznach was born at Kreuznach on the Nahe and is recorded in Frankfurt am Main from 1524, where he spent the rest of his career, dying there in 1552 or 1553. He was a portrait painter of the city's patricians and merchants, working in a clear, firm manner in the German Renaissance tradition, with careful attention to faces, costume and jewellery. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man with a Moor's Head on His Signet Ring is painted in oil, gold and white metal on a linden panel; the Moor's head on the ring was presumably the sitter's device, and the metal was used to give the ring and other accessories a glint. It was bought in 1912 with the John Stewart Kennedy Fund. Little else is recorded here about his training and sitters.","q159297":"John Constable was born in East Bergholt, Suffolk, in 1776, the son of a prosperous miller, and entered the Royal Academy Schools in 1799. He painted the farmland and rivers of the Stour valley, which he called his own country, and made his name with large canvases such as The Hay Wain of 1821, which won a gold medal at the Paris Salon of 1824 and influenced Delacroix and the Barbizon painters. He painted skies with extraordinary directness in oil sketches, especially at Hampstead, and made many studies of Salisbury Cathedral for his friend Bishop John Fisher, including the Met's view from the Bishop's grounds, about 1825. Slow to win acceptance at home, he was elected a Royal Academician only in 1829. He died in 1837.","q219403":"Constant Troyon was born at Sèvres in 1810, the son of a porcelain painter, and began as a decorator at the Sèvres porcelain factory before turning to landscape. In the 1830s and 1840s he painted in the forest of Fontainebleau and at Barbizon with Rousseau and Dupré, and a journey to Holland in 1847 turned him to the animal painting of Paulus Potter and Aelbert Cuyp. He then specialised in cattle, sheep and dogs in sunny pastures, as in Going to Market (1860), and his work sold in large numbers, above all to American collectors. Road in the Woods, of the mid-1840s, belongs to his earlier landscape period. He won medals at the Salon and the Legion of Honour, and died in Paris in 1865, after losing his reason in his last years.","q539474":"Constantin Hansen was born in Rome in 1804, where his Danish father was working as a portrait painter, and grew up in Copenhagen. He studied at the Academy under Christoffer Wilhelm Eckersberg and belonged to the circle of friends who became the painters of the Danish Golden Age. He lived in Italy from 1835 to 1844, and the Temple of Neptune at Paestum, which he painted in 1838, shows the Greek columns glowing in warm Italian light. Back in Denmark he painted frescoes and decorations, among them for the University of Copenhagen, and group portraits, of which A Group of Danish Artists in Rome (1837) is the best known. He was also a noted draftsman. He died at Frederiksberg in 1880, one of the leading figures of Danish painting.","continental_painter":"Continental Painter is the label the Metropolitan Museum uses for a group of unnamed European miniaturists, whose small portraits are catalogued by date and medium and not by author. The twelve examples span nearly three centuries, from about 1550 to about 1780, with one later chalk drawing, and show how the portrait miniature changed: oil on card in the sixteenth century, oil on copper and metal in the 1630s, enamel on gold, painted in colours fused by firing, in the middle and late seventeenth century, and ivory in the eighteenth. Most sitters are unidentified, though one lady of about 1780 is said to be Princess Apraxine. The miniatures came to the Museum as bequests and gifts, notably from Millie Bruhl Fredrick in 1962. Their makers' names, places and careers are lost.","q3101498":"George Cooke (1793 to 1849) was an American painter of portraits and landscapes, and the National Gallery of Art has one work by him, the portrait Samuel McKenney, painted in 1837. It shows the sitter at waist length facing the viewer and is catalogued in a Realist style. The picture was given to the Corcoran Gallery of Art in Washington by the sitter's great-great-granddaughter, Ann MacCarteney Coulthurst, and passed to the National Gallery when the Corcoran collection was transferred there in 2014. Portraits of this kind were commissioned by the sitter or the family and kept as heirlooms. Little more is recorded here about Cooke's training, patrons or other works, and nothing certain can be said of his reputation.","q367798":"Adriaen Coorte painted small still lifes in the Dutch Republic in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries, and almost nothing is known of his life. The dates 1683 to 1707 given for him are those of his signed and dated pictures, not of his birth and death. He worked probably at Middelburg in Zeeland. His still lifes are small, often painted on paper or panel, with a few asparagus, gooseberries, strawberries, grapes or shells set on a dark ledge in a pool of light, and they have an unusual calm and intimacy. The National Gallery's Still Life with Asparagus and Red Currants (1696) and Still Life with a Hanging Bunch of Grapes, Two Medlars, and a Butterfly (1687) are typical. Overlooked in his own day, he was rediscovered in the twentieth century.","q316016":"John Singleton Copley was born in Boston in 1738 and was brought up by his mother and his stepfather, the engraver Peter Pelham, who died when John was a boy. With almost no teachers, he taught himself to paint and by his early twenties was the finest portraitist in the American colonies, painting Boston merchants, ministers, craftsmen and their wives in strong, tactile likenesses with a vivid sense of fabrics and surroundings. In 1765 he sent his Boy with a Flying Squirrel to London, where it was praised by Reynolds and Benjamin West. In 1774, as Boston turned to revolution, he left for Europe and settled in London, where he painted dramatic history pictures such as Watson and the Shark, of 1778. He died in 1815.","q720941":"Corneille de Lyon was a Netherlandish painter, born at The Hague around 1500 to 1510, who was working in Lyon by 1533 and spent the rest of his life there, dying about 1574 or 1575. He became a painter to the French court, and ran a workshop that produced small bust portraits on wood panels, usually with a bright green or blue background, of courtiers, scholars and merchants. His smooth, enamel-like finish and fine detail connect his work to manuscript painting and miniatures rather than to the Italian taste then reaching France, and gold leaf was used to pick out jewellery and embroidery. The portraits in Washington, Cleveland and the Metropolitan Museum all show this manner. He was famous in his day, and modern scholars have since worked to separate his own hand from the many workshop copies.","q2520084":"Cornelis Bisschop was born at Dordrecht in 1630 and died there in 1674. He is said to have trained in Amsterdam with Ferdinand Bol, a pupil of Rembrandt, and then returned to his home city, where he painted portraits and genre scenes for the regents and merchants of Dordrecht. His small domestic scenes are close to those of his fellow townsman Nicolaes Maes, with quiet figures in carefully described interiors. The Metropolitan Museum's A Young Woman and a Cavalier, of the early 1660s, is a small scene of courtship with a pitcher and candlesticks, in the manner of Dutch genre painting of the 1650s and 1660s. It came to the Museum in 1982 with the Jack and Belle Linsky collection. Little else is recorded here of his patrons and reputation.","cornelis_de_heem":"Cornelis de Heem was born in 1631 and died in 1695, the son of the still-life painter Jan Davidsz. de Heem, from whom he learned to paint fruit, flowers and laid tables in the lavish Flemish manner. He worked in the style of his father, painting fruit, flowers, oysters, lobsters and silver and glass vessels on tables spread with rich cloths. The Cleveland Museum of Art's Still-Life with Crayfish, Oysters, and Fruit, of the late seventeenth or early eighteenth century, is catalogued as the work of a follower of Cornelis, not by him. Its porcelain bowl most likely came from China; political turmoil in China had stopped ceramic exports to the West by the 1640s, so the bowl was as valuable as the gold vessel on the table. Little else is recorded here about his career and patrons.","q474934":"Cornelis de Vos was born at Hulst in Flanders about 1584 or 1585 and worked in Antwerp, where he became a master in the Guild of Saint Luke in 1608 and later served as its dean. Apart from the great Rubens and Van Dyck, he was Antwerp's leading portraitist, painting merchants, magistrates and their families with a sober, solid realism and a gift for children, in group portraits that are warm and natural. His brother Paul de Vos and his brother-in-law Frans Snyders were also painters. He died in Antwerp in 1651. The Metropolitan Museum has two portraits of women by him, one with a fan, from the Marquand gift of 1889 and an 1871 purchase. His quiet, truthful portraits are admired more now than they were in his own day.","cornelis_jonson":"Cornelis Jonson, also called Jonson van Ceulen or Johnson, was born in London in 1593 to a family of Flemish refugees and trained, the Cleveland Museum says, in Amsterdam. He was a leading portrait painter in England from about 1618 until 1643, painting courtiers, gentry and London merchants in small, careful heads and half-lengths, often within painted oval frames, in the manner of Dutch portraiture. His portrait of a woman, possibly Elizabeth Boothby, dated 1619, shows a merchant's daughter in silk, silver-thread lace and jewels in her hair, signs of wealth and status, within a faux marble oval. He left England for the Dutch Republic when the Civil War began, and spent the last twenty years of his career there. He died in Utrecht in 1661.","q636113":"Cornelis Jonson van Ceulen was born in London in 1593 to Flemish parents and became a leading portrait painter at the court of Charles I in the 1620s and 1630s, painting courtiers and country gentry in small, clear, finely finished portraits. In 1643, as civil war approached, he left England and settled in the Netherlands, working in Middelburg, Amsterdam, The Hague and finally Utrecht, where he died in 1661. His portrait of the scholar Anna Maria van Schurman, painted in 1657, belongs to this later Dutch period. Van Schurman, the first woman to attend a Dutch university, was a celebrated linguist and engraver and one of the most admired women of her time.","q16324885":"Cornelis Jonson van Ceulen the Younger was born in London in 1634, the son of the portrait painter of the same name, and was taken as a boy to the Netherlands when his father left England at the start of the Civil War. He learned to paint from his father and worked as a portraitist in Dutch cities, ending in Utrecht, where he died in 1715. His portraits follow the sober, fine-grained style of his father's and are sometimes confused with them. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man with a Watch, dated 1657, shows a prosperous sitter displaying a watch, then a costly luxury and a sign of status, and also a reminder of the passing of time. The painting was given in memory of Felix M. Warburg by his wife and children in 1941.","q740934":"Corrado Giaquinto was born at Molfetta, in Apulia, in 1703 and trained in Naples with Francesco Solimena, and then in Rome, where he worked from about 1727 to 1753, painting altarpieces and frescoes in a light, airy, silvery style that carried the Rococo into Roman churches. In 1753 he went to Madrid as court painter to Ferdinand VI, painting ceilings and decorations in the new Royal Palace, and he became director of the Royal Academy of San Fernando. He returned to Naples in 1762 and died there in 1766. He was among the most admired Italian painters in Europe in the middle of the century. The National Gallery of Art owns two canvases, Winter and Autumn, of about 1740 to 1750, from a set of the Seasons.","correa_juan":"Juan Correa was born in Mexico City in 1646 and died there in 1716. He was the leading painter of a large workshop in the viceroyalty of New Spain, where he produced altarpieces, devotional paintings and portraits for the churches and religious orders of the capital. He worked with Cristóbal de Villalpando on the sacristy of the Mexico City cathedral in the 1680s, and he made large series, such as an Apocalypse. Like most colonial painters he took many of his compositions from European prints, and he and his shop repeated popular images in numerous versions. Christ Gathering His Garments after the Flagellation, of the 1670s, is a devotional scene of the kind that sold to private clients. His work is now seen as a high point of Mexican painting of the viceregal period.","q8457":"Antonio Allegri, called Correggio after his native town near Reggio Emilia, was born in 1489 and died there in 1534. After early work in his home district, he painted mainly in Parma, where he decorated the dome of San Giovanni Evangelista with a vision of Christ among the apostles and, between 1526 and 1530, the cathedral dome with a vast Assumption of the Virgin, in which figures seem to rise through clouds into the sky. For Federico Gonzaga of Mantua he painted a famous series of mythological loves, including Io and Danaë. He softened forms with warm light and painted flesh and gesture with sensuous tenderness, as in The Mystic Marriage of Saint Catherine. Greatly admired in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, he influenced Baroque ceiling painting and the grace of the Rococo.","q29447":"Cosimo Rosselli was born in Florence in 1439 or 1440 and died there in 1507. He trained in the workshop of Neri di Bicci, ran a busy workshop in Florence producing altarpieces and devotional panels, and taught Piero di Cosimo, who took his name from him, and Fra Bartolomeo. In 1481 he was one of the Florentine painters summoned by Pope Sixtus IV to decorate the walls of the Sistine Chapel in Rome, alongside Botticelli, Ghirlandaio and Perugino; his contributions were the Last Supper, the Sermon on the Mount and the Destruction of Pharaoh's army. The Metropolitan Museum's Madonna and Child with Two Angels, of the early 1480s, and Portrait of a Man, of about 1481 to 1482, belong to these years. He is remembered mostly for the Sistine commission and for his pupils.","q7129":"Cosmè Tura was born at Ferrara about 1430 to 1433 and became court painter to the Este family, first to Borso d'Este from 1458 and later to Ercole I. His style is unmistakable: tense, sharply drawn figures with spiky draperies of metallic hardness, strange landscapes, and enamel-like colour. He worked on the frescoes of the Palazzo Schifanoia and painted altarpieces and portraits for the court; The Flight into Egypt and the profile Portrait of a Young Man, both of the 1470s, are in the Metropolitan Museum, along with a Saint Louis of Toulouse. Younger painters of Ferrara overtook him in his last years, and his reputation faded in later centuries before twentieth-century historians restored him as the most original painter of the Ferrarese school. He died in Ferrara in 1495.","q454892":"Francesco del Cossa was born at Ferrara about 1436 and worked for the Este court there, beside Cosmè Tura and the young Ercole de' Roberti. His greatest work is the fresco of the months of March, April and May in the Palazzo Schifanoia (1469 to 1470), full of courtly life and lit with clear, cool light. In 1470 he wrote to Duke Borso d'Este to protest that he was being paid at the same low rate as lesser painters, and when the Duke refused to raise it he left Ferrara for Bologna, where he painted the Griffoni polyptych for San Petronio, about 1473 to 1474; the National Gallery's Saint Florian, Saint Lucy and Crucifixion come from it. He died in Bologna, probably of plague, in 1477 or 1478.","q632772":"Francis Cotes was born in London in 1726, the son of an apothecary, and trained with the portrait painter George Knapton, who also taught him to work in pastel. He became the best English pastellist of his day, making crayon portraits of fashionable sitters in fresh, delicate colour, and from about 1760 painted with great success in oils, rivalling Reynolds and Gainsborough in the market for society portraits. He was a founder member of the Royal Academy in 1768, painted Queen Charlotte and her children, and taught the pastellist John Russell. He died in 1770, at the height of his fame. The Metropolitan Museum holds his portrait of Admiral Harry Paulet, sixth Duke of Bolton.","q34618":"Gustave Courbet was born at Ornans, in the Franche-Comté, in 1819 and moved to Paris in 1839, where he taught himself largely by copying in the Louvre. He called himself a Realist and refused to idealise: his Burial at Ornans of 1849 to 1850 showed a village funeral on the scale of a history painting, and when the 1855 Exposition Universelle rejected two of his canvases he built his own Pavilion of Realism nearby. He painted peasants, nudes, hunting scenes, forests and seas, often with a palette knife; the New York Woman with a Parrot, of 1866, is among his best-known nudes. A convinced republican, he took part in the Paris Commune of 1871 and was imprisoned over the fall of the Vendôme Column. He fled to Switzerland and died in exile at La Tour-de-Peilz in 1877. His influence on the Impressionists was large.","q933739":"Kenyon Cox was born in Warren, Ohio, in 1856. After study in Cincinnati and at the Pennsylvania Academy, he went to Paris in 1877, working under Carolus-Duran and Jean-Léon Gérôme, and returned to New York in the early 1880s. He taught for many years at the Art Students League, wrote widely on art, and painted murals for the Library of Congress and several state capitols, as well as figure pieces such as The Harp Player of 1888. A devoted classicist who believed in drawing, tradition and the example of the old masters, he led the American attack on the modern works of the Armory Show of 1913, writing of them in scathing terms. His murals are admired for their clarity, though his reputation as a critic has fared less well. He died in 1919.","q191748":"Lucas Cranach the Elder and Workshop is how museums describe pictures made in the Wittenberg studio that he ran for nearly fifty years, with his sons Hans and Lucas the Younger and a team of assistants. The workshop turned out portraits, altarpieces and woodcut designs with such efficiency that many compositions exist in repeated versions, and its output included official images of the Saxon electors, who were Cranach's patrons and who protected Luther. The portraits of Frederick the Wise, who died in 1525, and of his brother John the Constant, who died in 1532, are dated 1532 to 1533, and were made as a dynastic series for the electors who followed. Saint Maurice, about 1520 to 1525, shows that the workshop served Catholic as well as Protestant patrons.","q5113040":"Christopher Pearse Cranch was born in 1813 in Alexandria, Virginia, and grew up in Washington and Quincy, Massachusetts. He trained as a Unitarian minister at Harvard Divinity School and preached for some years, but he was drawn to the Transcendentalist circle of Emerson and Margaret Fuller and to the arts, and left the pulpit for painting. He was also a poet and a gifted caricaturist, and his drawings illustrating Emerson's phrases, such as the giant eyeball, are famous. As a landscape painter he followed the Hudson River School, painting scenes in New England and, after he moved to Italy in 1846, in the Roman countryside, as in Castle Gandolfo, Lake Albano (1852). He lived in Paris in the 1850s, returned to the United States in 1863, and died in Cambridge, Massachusetts, in 1892.","q528620":"Giuseppe Maria Crespi was born in Bologna in 1665 and was nicknamed Lo Spagnolo, the Spaniard, reportedly for the fashionable dress he affected. He trained with several Bolognese masters, among them Domenico Maria Canuti and Carlo Cignani, and studied the work of the Carracci, Correggio and the Venetians. His style is more individual than his teachers': warm, dark, with thick brushwork, and he painted both religious pictures and scenes of everyday life with unusual sympathy, as in the Seven Sacraments he made for Cardinal Ottoboni. Prince Ferdinando de' Medici was an admirer. The National Gallery's Cupids Disarming Sleeping Nymphs is on copper, and Tarquin and Lucretia is a mythological tragedy. His pupils included Giovanni Battista Piazzetta, and he died in Bologna in 1747.","q2340302":"Donato Creti was born at Cremona in 1671 and grew up in Bologna, where he trained with Lorenzo Pasinelli and became one of the leading Bolognese painters of the early eighteenth century. His elegant, cool style, with sleek, elongated figures and a smooth surface, anticipates the Rococo, and he painted mythological and historical subjects, easel pictures for private collectors and frescoes. His best-known work is a set of eight small paintings of the planets and the Moon as seen through the telescope, made about 1711 for Count Luigi Ferdinando Marsili and now in the Vatican, with tiny figures in landscapes looking at the sky. Alexander the Great Threatened by His Father, of about 1700 to 1705, is an earlier work in the National Gallery. He died in Bologna in 1749.","q3503915":"John Crome was born in Norwich in 1768, from a poor family, and worked as a house painter and sign painter before taking up landscape under the patronage of the wealthy collector Thomas Harvey, who let him study his Dutch paintings. He spent his career in Norwich as a drawing master, and in 1803 he helped found the Norwich Society of Artists, the basis of the Norwich School of landscape painting. Inspired by Hobbema and Ruisdael, he painted woods, heaths, rivers and skies of Norfolk with broad, robust handling, as in Hautbois Common, Norfolk, probably about 1810. He was nicknamed Old Crome to distinguish him from his son John Berney Crome, and he died in 1821.","q1451318":"Jasper Francis Cropsey was born on Staten Island in 1823 and began as an architect before turning to landscape. In 1856 he went to London and stayed seven years, and in 1860 his vast Autumn on the Hudson River caused a stir in England, where audiences doubted that the bright colours of the American fall could be real. Returning home, he painted broad views of the Hudson Valley, New Jersey and Pennsylvania, among them the Met's Valley of Wyoming and Pompton Plains, New Jersey, and in 1869 built a great house, Aladdin, in Warwick, New York. His reputation faded after the 1870s as taste turned to French-influenced painting, and it revived only in the twentieth century. He died in 1900.","q555224":"Henri-Edmond Cross was born in 1856 at Douai in northern France as Henri-Edmond Delacroix and changed his surname to Cross to avoid confusion with the great Eugène Delacroix. He trained at the academy in Lille and then in Paris, and began as a painter of dark, naturalistic scenes. After meeting Georges Seurat and Paul Signac in the 1880s, he took up the dotted technique of Neo-Impressionism about 1891 and settled in the south of France, at Saint-Clair near Le Lavandou on the Mediterranean coast. Calanque des Antibois, of 1891 to 1892, uses bright, separate touches of colour to give the effect of heat and light, and his later work grew into broader mosaics of pure colour. He helped found the Société des Artistes Indépendants and died in 1910. His colour influenced Matisse and the Fauves.","q5538263":"George Cuitt the Younger was born in 1779 and died in 1854. He came from Richmond in Yorkshire, the son of a painter and drawing master of the same name, and learned his trade from him. He was a painter and etcher of landscapes and architecture, interested in ruins and old buildings, and lived for some years in Chester, where he published etchings of the city's ruins and old buildings. Easby Abbey, near Richmond, was a ruined abbey on the River Swale and a favourite subject of Romantic landscape painters; the National Gallery's picture, dated about 1821 to 1854, was given by Miss Harriet Winslow. Cuitt was a modest provincial artist without a wide reputation, and little is recorded here about his patrons.","q22094660":"D. B. is the name under which an unnamed French miniature painter appears in the Metropolitan Museum; the museum dates the work to 1812 and records only the nationality. The single work, Portrait of an Officer, is a miniature on ivory, the standard support for such small portraits in the Napoleonic period, when miniatures were popular with officers of the Empire and their families. The year 1812 was that of the Russian campaign, in which many men then in uniform did not return. The miniature came to the Museum in 1923 with the bequest of Mary Clark Thompson. The sitter is not identified, and nothing further about the painter, his training or his career is recorded here.","q21497829":"François van Daellen is an obscure Dutch painter known from a few works, and the dates 1636 to 1651 attached to his name in the catalogue are probably the years in which he is recorded rather than the span of his life. The National Gallery of Art's Vanitas Still Life, of about 1650, is painted in oil on paper laid down on a wooden panel, and it assembles a skull, a bone, books and a candle. These are the usual signs of the vanitas, a still life meant to remind the viewer that life is short and worldly things pass away: the candle burns down, the books record knowledge that cannot save, and the skull stands for death. The picture was bought with the Nell and Robert Weidenhammer Fund. Nothing is recorded here of van Daellen's birthplace, teachers or patrons.","q663837":"Pascal-Adolphe-Jean Dagnan-Bouveret was born in Paris in 1852 and studied at the École des Beaux-Arts under Jean-Léon Gérôme. He became one of the leading painters of the French academic naturalism of the 1880s, a friend of Jules Bastien-Lepage, with smooth, carefully observed scenes of peasants and religious devotion, painted from posed models. He spent summers in Brittany, where he found the subject of Breton Women at a Pardon, a religious procession and pilgrimage, for which the Gallery's small study of about 1887 was made. He won medals at the Salon and the Legion of Honour, and his work was admired and collected in the United States. He died in 1929, long after his polished style had been displaced by modern art, and he is now remembered chiefly for his Breton pictures.","q164735":"Johan Christian Dahl was born in Bergen, Norway, in 1788 and studied at the Academy in Copenhagen before moving in 1818 to Dresden, where he lived for the rest of his life and became a professor at the Academy. In Dresden he shared a house with Caspar David Friedrich and became his friend, though his own style was more naturalistic. After a stay in Italy in 1820 and 1821, where he sketched an eruption of Vesuvius, he painted moonlit harbours, shipwrecks, mountain waterfalls and the fjords and birch trees of his native Norway, which he revisited in later years. He is regarded as the father of Norwegian landscape painting and as a pioneer of painting clouds from direct study. He died in Dresden in 1857.","q3308769":"Michel-François Dandré-Bardon was born at Aix-en-Provence in 1700 and trained with the painter Jean-Baptiste van Loo before studying in Rome. He became a history painter and, above all, a teacher and writer: he held a professorship at the Royal Academy in Paris and wrote a treatise on painting (1765) and a book on the costumes of ancient peoples, which artists used as a reference. The National Gallery's The Adoration of the Skulls, painted about 1733 to 1734, is catalogued as a Baroque picture on the theme of worship, and it shows him early in his career. Although his own pictures are now less known than his books, he was a respected figure in the French art world of his day. He died in Paris in 1783.","q655535":"Daniël Mijtens was born at Delft about 1590 and trained in the Netherlands before moving to England about 1618, where he became painter to James I and then to Charles I, who confirmed him in the royal service in 1625. He painted the king, Queen Henrietta Maria and the court nobility in formal, full-length portraits, in a precise, rather stiff style with close attention to costume, lace and jewels. The Metropolitan Museum's Charles I of 1629 is a characteristic example. In 1632 the arrival of Anthony van Dyck, whose elegant portraits set a new fashion, ended his position at court, and Mijtens left England about 1635 for The Hague, where he died in 1647 or 1648. His portraits remain valuable records of the court of Charles I.","q20879492":"Daniel Dickinson (1795 to 1877) is catalogued by the Metropolitan Museum as an American miniature painter, with five watercolours on ivory dating from about 1820 to 1830. They include Charles Leland (1822), Mr. Hagner (about 1825) and a portrait of the Philadelphia painter John Neagle (1830), and several are in their original cases of gilded copper or red leatherette. Portrait miniatures like these were made to be kept in a pocket or worn, and in the 1820s were the most affordable and portable form of likeness; photography took over their trade after 1840. Most came to the Museum in 2006 through the Dale T. Johnson Fund and the gift of Gloria Manney. Dickinson's training, home and patrons are not recorded here.","q21752065":"Daniel F. Ames (1803 to 1886) is catalogued by the Metropolitan Museum as an American portrait miniaturist. The museum owns two of his Portraits of a Lady, one dated 1840 to 1850 and the other about 1845, both painted in watercolour on ivory, the usual medium for miniatures. Both belong to the years just after the daguerreotype reached America in 1839, when photographic portraits began to take away the miniaturist's trade, and many painters of his generation turned to photography or to larger oil portraits. The miniatures came to the Museum as gifts of Jacqueline Loewe Fowler in 2005 and Gloria Manney in 2006. The sitters are not named, and nothing is recorded here of Ames's training, home or patrons.","q323987":"Daniel Huntington was born in New York in 1816 and studied with Samuel F. B. Morse and then with Henry Inman, before going to Rome in 1839 to 1840 and again later. Elegant and sociable, he became one of the most successful American painters of the century, with a large practice in portraits of bankers, ministers, scientists and writers, as well as religious and literary pictures such as Mercy's Dream, of 1850, taken from Bunyan's Pilgrim's Progress and widely engraved. He served as president of the National Academy of Design from 1862 to 1870 and again from 1877 to 1890, and he was a founder of the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He died in New York in 1906, aged ninety. The Washington portraits include the writer and critic Henry Theodore Tuckerman.","q187506":"Honoré Daumier was born at Marseille in 1808 and grew up in Paris, where he learned lithography and, from 1830, drew political caricatures for the journals La Caricature and Le Charivari. His cartoon of King Louis-Philippe as the giant Gargantua, swallowing the people's money, earned him six months in prison in 1832. Over the following decades he produced some four thousand lithographs, mocking lawyers, politicians and the middle class, and sympathising with the poor. Painting, which he took up seriously in the 1850s, was a private passion: broad, dark, sculptural pictures of washerwomen, railway passengers, drinkers and Don Quixote, rarely exhibited in his lifetime. Nearly blind in old age, he was given a cottage at Valmondois by his friend Corot, and he died there in 1879. The Third-Class Carriage in New York is one of his best-known pictures.","q18516482":"David Baudringhien was a Dutch painter born about 1581 and dead in 1650. The Metropolitan Museum owns two of his small portraits, a Portrait of a Man dated 1627 and a Portrait of a Woman, both painted in oil on copper and catalogued as miniatures. Oil on copper gave a smooth, hard surface well suited to fine detail, and was favoured in the Netherlands for small portraits and cabinet pictures. The pair came to the Museum with the collection of Giovanni P. Morosini, presented by his daughter Giulia in 1932. The sitters are not named, and nothing is recorded here about Baudringhien's training, where he worked or who his patrons were, so little more can be said of him with certainty.","q21481695":"David Boudon was a miniature painter who lived from 1748 to about 1816. The Metropolitan Museum has one work by him, a Portrait of a Lady painted in watercolour on ivory, which belongs to its American Wing though its maker is classed as French. The sitter is not named. Ivory miniatures were fashionable throughout Europe and America in the later eighteenth century, when ivory replaced vellum and enamel as the usual support, and painters built up the likeness in fine strokes of watercolour. The portrait was given to the Museum in 2006 by Gloria Manney. Beyond his dates, nothing is recorded here about Boudon's training, his place of work or his patrons.","q5234146":"David Gilmour Blythe was born in 1815 at East Liverpool, Ohio, and worked as an apprentice woodcarver, a sailor and an itinerant portrait and panorama painter before settling in Pittsburgh in 1856. There he became known for sharp, sometimes bitter genre scenes of city life, courtrooms and street children, and of the Civil War, in a dark, detailed manner. Corn Husking, of about 1863 to 1864, shows men at work at night on a farm. He was a lonely man given to drink, and he wrote verses. He died in Pittsburgh in 1865, aged fifty. Largely forgotten after his death, he was revived by twentieth-century historians as a vivid and original American satirist.","q3695975":"David Johnson was born in New York in 1827 and died at Walden, New York, in 1908. Largely self-taught, he became a Hudson River School painter of quiet, precise views of rivers, lakes and hills in the northeastern states, with a close observation of rocks, trees and water that reflects the call of the day to paint nature exactly. Study, North Conway, New Hampshire, of 1851, in Cleveland, is a close study of a tumbled riverbed of boulders, moss and broken branches, in a village that was a popular base for tourists in the White Mountains. The Metropolitan Museum owns three, including Near Squam Lake, New Hampshire (1856), and On the Unadilla, New York (1884), which show his later, warmer and broader handling. He was a respected, if never famous, landscape painter.","q5236742":"David Loggan was born at Danzig in 1634 and trained there before working in the Netherlands, where he learned engraving. He came to England about 1653 and settled in London and Oxford, becoming public engraver to the University of Oxford in 1669. He is best known for his bird's-eye views of the Oxford and Cambridge colleges, published as Oxonia Illustrata (1675) and Cantabrigia Illustrata (1690), and for portrait engravings and drawings of the great and the learned. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man, dated 1680, is a drawing in plumbago, or graphite, on vellum, a delicate technique in which the likeness is built from minute pencil lines. Loggan died in London in 1692. His views remain the principal record of the universities in his day.","q20117274":"David Myers was an English miniature painter, recorded as active from about 1659 to 1676; the Metropolitan Museum catalogues him as British. His working life fell in the years after the Restoration of Charles II in 1660, when portrait miniature painting was led by Samuel Cooper. The Museum's only work by him, Portrait of a Man, dated 1664, is painted on vellum laid on prepared gessoed card, the usual support of miniatures before ivory became common. The sitter is not named. The portrait came to the Museum with the bequest of Mary Clark Thompson in 1923. Nothing else is recorded here about Myers's training, patrons or reputation.","q323932":"David Ryckaert III was born in Antwerp in 1612 into a family of painters; his father and grandfather, both named David Ryckaert, were also artists. He became a master in the Antwerp guild in the 1630s and painted peasant scenes, tavern and farm interiors, and scenes of temptation, in a dark, brownish palette close to that of Adriaen Brouwer and David Teniers the Younger. The Metropolitan Museum has two, Rustic Interior and The Yard of the Inn at Emmaus, both bought in 1871. He died in Antwerp in 1661. Less famous than Teniers, he painted carefully, and his work shows how widespread the taste for rustic subjects was among the Flemish public of his day. Little else is recorded here about his patrons.","david_teniers":"David Teniers the Younger was born in Antwerp in 1610 and trained with his father, the painter David Teniers the Elder; in 1637 he married Anna Brueghel, daughter of Jan Brueghel the Elder. He painted small, lively scenes of peasants dancing, drinking and smoking, which the Cleveland Museum's catalogue describes as made for an elite urban audience nostalgic for a simpler life, and surviving drawings show that he sketched peasants from life. Village Festival and Landscape with Peasants Dancing show the festivals; Peasants Smoking in an Inn a young man overcome by tobacco; Game of Backgammon a popular wagering game. He was among the most popular painters of his time and was much imitated, and one picture in this group, Old Man and Woman, is catalogued as probably painted in the eighteenth century. He died in Brussels in 1690.","q335022":"David Teniers the Younger, born in Antwerp in 1610, was the most successful Flemish genre painter of his century. He entered the Antwerp guild in 1632 and made his name with peasant festivals and guardroom scenes, such as Guardroom with the Deliverance of Saint Peter, in the Metropolitan Museum. In 1651 he moved to Brussels as court painter to Archduke Leopold Wilhelm, governor of the Spanish Netherlands, and keeper of his great picture collection; he painted views of the gallery and published an engraved catalogue of its Italian pictures, the Theatrum Pictorium (1660). He also painted religious subjects and landscapes, such as The Good Samaritan, and helped found the Antwerp Academy in 1665. He died in Brussels in 1690, rich and honoured.","q1145811":"Davide Ghirlandaio, born David Bigordi in Florence in 1452, was the younger brother of the painter Domenico Ghirlandaio and worked in his brother's busy workshop, helping with the frescoes and altarpieces that made it the most successful in Florence. He is credited especially with work in mosaic, including the Annunciation mosaic over the Porta della Mandorla of Florence Cathedral, made with his brother. After Domenico's death in 1494 he ran the workshop with his brother Benedetto and his brother-in-law Sebastiano Mainardi. The Metropolitan Museum attributes to him three panels of about 1479 showing the Burial of Saint Zenobius, the Marriage of the Virgin and Tobias and the Angel, and the portrait of Selvaggia Sassetti, painted about 1487 to 1488. He died in Florence in 1525.","q21498214":"T. Davies is known only from one picture. Ship in Full Sail, dated 1827, is an oil on canvas in the National Gallery of Art, catalogued as American and naive in style, with no first name, birthplace or life dates beyond a wide bracket of 1747 to 1887 that clearly does not mark a lifetime. Portraits of ships, painted for captains, owners and shipbuilders, were a favourite type of American art in the first half of the nineteenth century, and many were made by self-taught painters who worked in ports. Whether Davies was one of them is not recorded. The picture was given by Edgar William and Bernice Chrysler Garbisch, who collected American folk painting. Nothing is recorded of Davies's training or patrons.","q5346352":"Edwin Deakin was born at Sheffield in England in 1838 and emigrated to the United States in 1856, working first in Chicago, where he began to paint. He moved to California in the 1870s, living in San Francisco and later Berkeley, and is best remembered for two kinds of picture: still lifes of grapes and other fruit, painted with close attention to texture, light and shadow, often against white walls, and views of the old Spanish missions of California, to which he devoted a series of paintings. Grapes Against White Wall (1883) is an example of his still lifes. He died in Berkeley in 1923. His fruit pictures were popular with Californian buyers and are now collected as examples of American still life.","q20859324":"Joseph Decker was born in 1853 in Württemberg, Germany, was brought to the United States as a boy, and settled in Brooklyn. He studied at the National Academy of Design and took up still life, and especially fruit: plums, grapes, pears and apples shown close up, often in bunches on a plain ground, painted with attention to bloom and colour that lets them seem almost three-dimensional. Green Plums, Grapes, Ripening Pears and Still Life with Crab Apples and Grapes are among his pictures in the National Gallery. He remained little known in his lifetime and died in Brooklyn in 1924. He was largely forgotten until the revival of interest in American still life in the later twentieth century, which brought his work into museums.","q43174":"Defendente Ferrari was a Piedmontese painter active between 1510 and 1531, working at Chivasso and in the neighbouring towns of Turin, Vercelli and the Susa valley. He painted altarpieces with figures of saints set against gilded and ornamented backgrounds, in a style that combined the Lombard tradition, the influence of Leonardo's followers and northern European prints, especially the engravings of Dürer. The Metropolitan Museum's Saints John the Evangelist and Lawrence, painted in oil on wood, shows the two saints with a book and a chalice, and was probably part of a larger altarpiece. Ferrari's altarpieces remained in the churches for which they were painted, and his reputation is mostly regional. Little else is documented about his training and his birth and death dates.","q46373":"Edgar Degas was born in Paris in 1834, the son of a banker whose family came from Naples; his mother was from a Creole family of New Orleans. After studying law briefly he entered the École des Beaux-Arts and spent three years in Italy, where he painted family portraits and copied the old masters; The Old Italian Woman (1857) belongs to this period. He admired Ingres, and his early portraits and history pictures are exact in drawing. In 1872 and 1873 he visited his mother's relatives in New Orleans. Back in Paris, he painted the Opéra and its dancers, as in The Ballet from Robert le Diable (1871), the racecourse and the circus. He joined seven of the Impressionist exhibitions, worked more and more in pastel and sculpture as his sight failed, and died in Paris in 1917.","q33477":"Eugène Delacroix was born in 1798 at Charenton-Saint-Maurice, near Paris, and is the great painter of French Romanticism, rivalling Ingres. The Metropolitan Museum's pictures show his range beyond the big public canvases. The Natchez, begun in 1823 to 1824 and reworked in 1835, takes its subject from a novel by Chateaubriand about a Native American family in Louisiana; Hamlet and His Mother (1849) and Christ Asleep during the Tempest (about 1853) show his love of Shakespeare and of dramatic scenes; the Madame Henri François Riesener (1835) is a portrait from his family circle; George Sand's Garden at Nohant (about 1842 to 1843) and Basket of Flowers (1848 to 1849) show his quieter side. He kept a Journal from 1822 on, which is a classic. He died in Paris in 1863.","q3484255":"Simon Denis was born in Antwerp in 1755 and trained there as a landscape painter before going to Paris, and in the 1780s he settled in Italy, where he spent the rest of his life, in Rome and from 1806 in Naples. There he painted views of the Roman Campagna and the Bay of Naples with Vesuvius, in the clear, ordered manner of classical landscape, and he worked from nature in oil on paper, as in View near Naples, painted about 1806. Small outdoor sketches like this were studies for larger pictures and were also admired in their own right. He died in Naples in 1812. Although Belgian by birth, he is often counted among the French landscape painters in Italy, whose habit of sketching in oil from nature he shared.","q21498324":"The Denison Limner is the name given to an unknown painter who made portraits of the Denison family of Stonington, Connecticut, about 1790. The National Gallery of Art owns four: Captain Elisha Denison, Mrs. Elizabeth Noyes Denison, Elizabeth Denison and a portrait of a young woman, Miss Denison of Stonington, possibly Matilda Denison. A limner is an old word for a portrait painter, and in American art such names are given to anonymous artists known only from their works. The catalogue's dates of 1710 to 1850 are not those of a life but a wide guess. The portraits are naive in style, with careful attention to faces and costume, and come from the Garbisch collection. The painter's real name, training and patrons are not recorded.","q3286189":"Arthur Devis was born in Preston, Lancashire, in 1712 and trained in London with the Flemish-born painter Pieter Tillemans. He specialised in conversation pieces, small full-length portraits of families and friends in carefully furnished rooms or parks, painted in clear colours, with slender, doll-like figures. Members of the Maynard Family in the Park at Waltons shows a gentry family on their estate; Portrait of a Gentleman Netting Partridges shows a sporting scene; and Arthur Holdsworth Conversing with Thomas Taylor and Captain Stancombe by the River Dart shows three friends in the open air. His patrons were the country gentry and rising merchants who wanted their possessions displayed. Out of fashion by the 1760s, he is now studied for his record of English life. He died in 1787.","q1378807":"Thomas Wilmer Dewing was born at Newburyport, Massachusetts, in 1851 and trained in Boston and, from 1876 to 1879, at the Académie Julian in Paris. He settled in New York and spent summers in the artists' colony at Cornish, New Hampshire. He became the leading painter of the Tonalist taste for hazy, dreamy pictures: slim, elegant women in dim interiors or misty meadows, painted in muted greens, golds and greys. Lady with a Lute (1886) is an early example, with a woman playing a lute. He was a member of the Ten American Painters, and his chief patron was the Detroit industrialist Charles Lang Freer, who bought many of his works. His wife Maria Oakey Dewing was a flower painter. He died in 1938.","q3638104":"Benedetto Diana, born Benedetto Rusconi, was a Venetian painter who lived from about 1460 to 1525. He trained in the circle of Giovanni Bellini and was influenced by Cima da Conegliano and Vittore Carpaccio, painting altarpieces and devotional pictures for churches and confraternities in Venice and the Veneto, with clear colour and a taste for architecture and landscape. The Presentation and Marriage of the Virgin, and the Annunciation, of 1520 to 1525, in the National Gallery of Art, is a small panel narrating episodes from the life of the Virgin. He worked at a time when Titian was transforming Venetian painting, and his reputation is that of a respectable follower in the Bellini tradition. Little else is documented of his teachers and patrons.","q724140":"Narcisse Diaz de la Peña was born in Bordeaux in 1807 to Spanish refugee parents and was orphaned young. He learned his craft as a porcelain painter at Sèvres, where he met Jules Dupré and Constant Troyon, and in the 1830s began painting the forest of Fontainebleau, where, according to the usual account, a snakebite cost him a leg. Associated with the Barbizon painters, and a friend of Théodore Rousseau, he painted dense woodland, pools and the dappled light of sunlit glades, as in The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard and The Frog Pond, and also small romantic figure pictures of nymphs and gypsies, in glowing, jewel-like colour, which sold well. He received the Legion of Honour in 1851 and died at Menton in 1876. American collectors were fond of his work.","diego_vel_zquez":"Diego Velázquez was born at Seville in 1599 and apprenticed to the painter Francisco Pacheco, whose daughter he married in 1618. He painted earthy kitchen and tavern scenes before moving to Madrid in 1623 to become painter to King Philip IV, whom he served for the rest of his life. At court he painted royal portraits, hunting scenes, mythology and the Surrender of Breda, and, in 1656, Las Meninas. He visited Italy in 1629 to 1631 and again in 1649 to 1651, when he painted Pope Innocent X. The Portrait of the Jester Calabazas, about 1631 to 1632, in Cleveland, shows a court jester with a pinwheel and a small portrait in his hands; Velázquez shows his disabilities honestly and with dignity. He was made a knight of Santiago in 1659 and died in Madrid in 1660.","dietrich_adelheid":"Adelheid Dietrich (1827 to 1891) was a German painter of flowers, and the National Gallery of Art's Still Life of Flowers, of 1868, painted in oil on wood, is catalogued as her work. Flower painting was one of the few branches of art open to women painters in nineteenth-century Germany, since the academies were closed to them and flowers could be painted at home from a bouquet. Her bouquet belongs to a long tradition of Dutch and German flower painting, which she inherited. The panel comes from the John Wilmerding collection. Little is recorded here about her birthplace, teachers or patrons, and the Gallery's dates give the only fixed points in her life.","q3028747":"Dionisio Baixeras y Verdaguer was born in Barcelona in 1862 and died there in 1943. He trained in his native city and became known as a painter of the sea and the coast of Catalonia. Boatmen of Barcelona, dated 1886, shows the working men of the harbour, and was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1886 by George I. Seney, the New York banker and collector who also gave the museum many American paintings. It was painted when the artist was twenty-four, and its early arrival in an American museum suggests the interest that Spanish genre painting aroused in the United States in the 1880s. Little else is recorded here of his teachers, patrons or later career.","dirck_dircksz_santvoort":"Dirck Dircksz. Santvoort was a Dutch painter born in 1610 or 1611 who died in 1680, and he worked in Amsterdam. He painted portraits of Amsterdam burghers and their families, and the Cleveland Museum of Art's Portrait of Elizabeth Spiegel, dated 1639, belongs to a series of five portraits representing the five senses, each showing a daughter of the same wealthy family. Eleven-year-old Elizabeth holds a recorder and stands for hearing; her satin gown, her pearls and the ostrich feathers imported from Africa proclaim her family's wealth and show Amsterdam's role as a trading centre. The hands, delicate and natural around the instrument, are a notable detail. Little else is recorded here about his training and patrons.","q759486":"Dirck Hals was born at Haarlem in 1591 and died there in 1656, the younger brother of the great portraitist Frans Hals. He painted merry companies and banquets, groups of elegantly dressed young people chatting, flirting, drinking and making music in rooms or gardens, in small, lively pictures that helped establish this type of Dutch genre painting in the 1620s. A Banquet, of 1628, is an example, with figures around a table in an interior. His figures are small, graceful and quickly painted, with a spontaneity that recalls his brother's, and his pictures influenced later painters of genre scenes. Less admired than Frans, he is now valued for his lively social scenes.","dirck_van_baburen":"Dirck van Baburen was born at Utrecht about 1595 and trained there with Paulus Moreelse. Between about 1612 and 1620 he lived in Rome, where he painted the Pietà in a chapel of San Pietro in Montorio (1617) and learned the new realist style of Caravaggio, with strong light and shadow and half-length figures close to the viewer. Back in Utrecht in 1620, he became, with Hendrick ter Brugghen and Gerard van Honthorst, a leading Utrecht Caravaggist. The Violin Player with a Wine Glass of 1623, in Cleveland, shows a half-length musician with a bold gaze, unshaven face and broken tooth; the same model appears in other Utrecht pictures. Baburen died young, in Utrecht, in 1624.","dirck_van_der_lisse":"Dirck van der Lisse was a Dutch painter of small mythological landscapes in the middle of the seventeenth century, and the Cleveland Museum of Art gives only the date 1669 for him, the year in which he is recorded as dead. He belonged to the circle of painters who followed Cornelis van Poelenburch, who painted Italianate landscapes with small nude figures. Mercury and Argus in a Landscape, painted about 1635 on copper, illustrates the myth of Juno's jealousy: she changed Jupiter's beloved Io into a cow and set the many-eyed giant Argus to watch her, and Mercury lulled Argus to sleep with his flute and killed him. Copper gave a smooth, enamel-like surface for fine detail. The picture was bequeathed to Cleveland by Mrs. Severance A. Millikin in 1989.","q320118":"Domenico Zampieri, called Domenichino, was born at Bologna in 1581 and trained with Denys Calvaert and then in the Carracci academy, before following Annibale Carracci to Rome. There he became the leading classical painter of his generation, with frescoes such as the scenes of Saint Cecilia in San Luigi dei Francesi (1612 to 1615) and the Last Communion of Saint Jerome, which Poussin admired. He also painted landscapes in the Carracci tradition, such as Landscape with Moses and the Burning Bush, on copper, and religious pictures such as The Rebuke of Adam and Eve (1626). In 1631 he went to Naples to paint the chapel of Saint Januarius in the cathedral, where rivals harassed him, and he died there in 1641. His reputation was very high for two centuries and fell in the nineteenth.","q1905185":"Domenico Bossi was a Venetian painter of portrait miniatures, born in 1765 and dead in 1853. He worked for aristocratic patrons in Italy and abroad, and his miniatures on ivory, delicate in colour and finish, belong to the last great age of the portrait miniature, before photography replaced it. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of Count Alexander Ivanovich Sollogoub (1788 to 1844), dated 1810, shows a young Russian nobleman of twenty-two, whose features Bossi has rendered in minute strokes. The miniature was given to the Museum in 1972 by Humanities Fund Inc. Beyond these facts, little is recorded here about Bossi's training, his places of work and his career.","q737160":"Domenico di Bartolo was a Sienese painter who lived from about 1400 to 1447. He was an early Renaissance innovator: he absorbed the ideas of Florentine painters such as Masaccio and Donatello and brought to Siena a new interest in solid, volumetric figures and perspective. He is best known for the frescoes of the Pellegrinaio, the pilgrims' ward of the hospital of Santa Maria della Scala in Siena (1440 to 1444), which show the daily life of the hospital: the care of the sick, the distribution of alms and the marriage of orphan girls, with vivid contemporary detail. The National Gallery's Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saint Peter and Saint Paul, about 1430, is earlier. He died in 1447, relatively young, and his reputation rests on the Siena frescoes.","q551695":"Domenico Fetti was born in Rome about 1589 and trained under the Florentine painter Ludovico Cigoli. He was also influenced by the followers of Caravaggio, and by Venetian painting, which he knew well after his patron, Cardinal Ferdinando Gonzaga, became Duke of Mantua in 1612 and took him there as court painter in 1613. In Mantua he painted altarpieces and portraits, and a series of small pictures of Christ's parables, made with loose, quick brushwork and rich colour, which became his best known work. The influence of Rubens, who had worked at the Gonzaga court earlier, can be seen in his handling. In 1622 he left for Venice, where he died in 1623, aged about thirty-four. The National Gallery of Art owns The Veil of Veronica, of about 1618 to 1622.","q191423":"Domenico Bigordi, called Ghirlandaio, was born in Florence in 1448 or 1449, the son of a goldsmith, and ran the most successful painting workshop in the city in the 1480s, together with his brothers David and Benedetto. He is best known for fresco cycles in which sacred stories are set in contemporary Florence, among them the Sassetti Chapel in Santa Trinita and the Tornabuoni Chapel in Santa Maria Novella (1485 to 1490), crowded with portraits of the families who paid for them. He was also called to Rome to paint in the Sistine Chapel in 1481 and 1482, and the young Michelangelo spent time in his workshop. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of Francesco Sassetti and his son Teodoro, about 1488, shows the patron of the Santa Trinita chapel. Ghirlandaio died in Florence in 1494.","q3713080":"Domenico Guidobono was a Genoese painter who lived from 1668 to 1746. He worked in Liguria in the late Baroque taste of the region, painting altarpieces, allegorical subjects and decorative pictures with light, flowing figures. The Metropolitan Museum's An Allegory, of 1710 to 1720, is crowded with symbolic objects: books, skulls, birds, a cat and a dog, and girls and women, the kind of picture that was meant to be read and decoded by an educated viewer, in which each object had a meaning. The Museum bought it with a group of donors' funds. His reputation is mainly regional. Little else is recorded here about his teachers, patrons or the rest of his career.","q368546":"Domenico Veneziano, Domenico of Venice, was born in Venice about 1410 and made his career in Florence. In 1438, writing from Perugia to Piero de' Medici, he boasted that he could paint as well as Fra Angelico or Fra Filippo Lippi, and hoped for a commission, and by the 1440s he was at work in Florence, painting a lost fresco cycle in Sant'Egidio, in which the young Piero della Francesca assisted him. His masterpiece is the Saint Lucy Altarpiece (about 1445 to 1447), now in the Uffizi, painted in clear, luminous colours in a calm, measured space. The National Gallery's Saint Francis Receiving the Stigmata and Saint John in the Desert belonged to its predella; the Madonna and Child is of about the same date. Few of his works survive. He died in Florence in 1461.","q3713796":"Donato de' Bardi was a Lombard painter, said to have been born in Pavia, who worked in Genoa and Liguria and is recorded from 1426 until his death in 1450 or 1451. He is one of the few painters active in northern Italy in the early fifteenth century whose works can be identified, and he joined the Lombard late Gothic tradition of courtly detail to a striking realism of face and gesture. The Metropolitan Museum's Madonna and Child with Saints Philip and Agnes, painted in tempera on a gold ground about 1425 to 1430, belongs to his early career. A Crucifixion at Savona is among his surviving works. Little else is documented, and his influence on younger painters, among them Vincenzo Foppa, is often suggested.","q21408810":"Frans van Doornik is a name attached to two portraits in the National Gallery of Art, Isaac de Peyster (?) and Anna de Peyster (?), both dated 1731, and almost nothing else is recorded of him. The dates 1635 to 1774 in the catalogue are not those of a life but a wide guess, and the question marks in the titles show that the identity of the sitters is uncertain too. If the identification is right, the sitters belonged to the De Peysters, a prominent family of Dutch descent in New York. The catalogue describes the painter as Flemish and the style as Baroque. The pair came from the Chester Dale Collection. Nothing is recorded of van Doornik's training, home or patrons.","q356777":"Dosso Dossi, born Giovanni di Niccolò de Lutero, was born about 1486 at Tramuschio near Mirandola, and from the 1510s worked at Ferrara as the leading painter at the court of the Este dukes, Alfonso I and Ercole II, often with his brother Battista. His pictures are fantasies drawn from romantic poetry: enchantresses, heroes and gods in glowing, shimmering colour, in dreamy landscapes. Circe and Her Lovers in a Landscape (about 1525) shows the sorceress who turned men into beasts, in a setting of rich, odd detail; The Trojans Repairing Their Ships in Sicily and The Trojans Building the Temple to Venus come from a cycle of scenes from Virgil's Aeneid; Saint Lucretia (about 1520) is a single figure. The poet Ariosto praised him in Orlando Furioso (1532). He died in Ferrara in 1541 or 1542.","q335927":"Gerrit Dou was born at Leiden in 1613, the son of a glass engraver, and at fifteen became the first pupil of the young Rembrandt, in the years when Rembrandt lived in Leiden, until Rembrandt left for Amsterdam in 1631. He then developed his own manner, of minute precision and glossy, enamel-like finish, which he practised with the finest brushes, and he founded the Leiden school of fine painters, known as the fijnschilders. He painted small, exact pictures of ladies, scholars, old women reading and night scenes by candle, often in a stone niche or window frame, and sold them at very high prices to patrons such as the Swedish diplomat Pieter Spiering. His pupils included Frans van Mieris. He died at Leiden in 1675. The Metropolitan Museum owns An Evening School and a Self-Portrait; the Cleveland Museum of Art owns A Young Woman.","q284954":"Thomas Doughty was born in Philadelphia in 1791 and worked as a leather currier before giving it up about 1820 to paint. Self-taught, he became one of the first American painters to make a living by landscape alone, with quiet views of rivers, hills and woods in soft, silvery light, often with a small angler or traveller in the foreground. He worked in Philadelphia, Boston and New York and travelled to Europe in the late 1830s and again in the 1840s. He also drew birds and sporting scenes for a magazine, The Cabinet of Natural History and American Rural Sports. View on the Hudson in Autumn (1850) is a late work; Fanciful Landscape (1834) is an imagined scene. He died in New York in 1856. His calm landscapes were an important forerunner of the Hudson River School.","q946487":"François-Hubert Drouais was born in Paris in 1727, the son of the portrait painter Hubert Drouais, and studied with Carle Van Loo, Nattier and Boucher. He was received into the Academy in 1758 and became one of the most sought-after portraitists of the court of Louis XV, painting Madame de Pompadour, Madame du Barry, the king's daughters and fashionable ladies in the guise of shepherdesses or vestals, and many children, in silky colour and smooth, polished surface. Madame Sophie de France, of 1762, and Boy with a Black Spaniel, of about 1767, are both in the Metropolitan Museum. His son Jean-Germain Drouais became a favourite pupil of David. François-Hubert died in Paris in 1775, before the Revolution swept away the society he painted.","q20109571":"Victor Dubreuil (1842 to 1910) was an American painter who specialised in trompe l'oeil pictures of paper money, in which banknotes seem to be real objects that the viewer could pick up. Pictures of this kind became popular in the 1880s and 1890s, a time of financial panics and distrust of banks, and Dubreuil painted stacks, bundles and barrels of notes. Safe Money, dated 1898 or later, is one of them. It came to the National Gallery with the Corcoran Collection, through a gift from the heirs of George E. Lemon. Trompe l'oeil, French for deceiving the eye, was a tradition going back to ancient Greece, and Dubreuil gave it a distinctly American subject. Little is recorded here of his life, training or patrons beyond his dates.","q15792":"Duccio di Buoninsegna was born in Siena about 1255 and is first recorded there in 1278, when he was paid for painting wooden covers for the city's account books. He is regarded as the founder of the Sienese school, joining Byzantine elegance to a new softness. In 1285 he painted a great altarpiece, the Rucellai Madonna, for a Florentine confraternity, and in 1308 he contracted to paint the Maestà, the double-sided altarpiece for Siena Cathedral, which was carried in procession through the city on 9 June 1311. The National Gallery owns two of its panels, The Nativity with the Prophets Isaiah and Ezekiel and The Calling of the Apostles Peter and Andrew. The Metropolitan Museum bought its small Madonna and Child, about 1290 to 1300, in 2004 for a reported $45 million. He died in 1318 or 1319.","q22575809":"Dulieu du Chenevoux is known from a single enamel miniature, a portrait of Benjamin Franklin dated 1785 and painted on copper, which the Metropolitan Museum holds in its American Wing though its maker is classed as French. Franklin lived in France from 1776 to 1785, first as one of the American commissioners and then as minister to the court of Louis XVI, and became an object of fashion there; portraits of him were made in painting, sculpture, medals and miniatures. The year 1785 was that of his departure for America. Enamel is a hard, bright medium whose colours do not fade, and it was well suited to souvenir portraits. The miniature was given to the Museum in 2006 by Gloria Manney. Nothing is recorded here of the painter's given names, dates or career.","q2699889":"Robert Seldon Duncanson was born in 1821 in Fayette, New York, into a family of house painters and carpenters, and grew up in Monroe, Michigan, learning the house painter's trade. He moved to Cincinnati in the 1840s, where he taught himself to paint, first portraits and still lifes, such as Still Life with Fruit and Nuts (1848) and Fruit Still Life (about 1849), then landscapes. Supported by Cincinnati patrons, among them the collector Nicholas Longworth and abolitionist friends, he made two trips to Europe, in 1853 and 1863 to 1866, and is regarded as the first African American artist to win an international reputation. His landscapes, such as Land of the Lotus Eaters (1861), are calm and luminous, in the Hudson River School manner. He suffered from mental illness in his last years and died in Detroit in 1872.","dunning_robert_spear":"Robert Spear Dunning was born in 1829 at Brunswick, Maine, and settled in Fall River, Massachusetts, where he became the leader of what has been called the Fall River school of still-life painters. He specialised in fruit pieces of cherries, strawberries, peaches and grapes, set on tables or ledges, with a crisp, bright clarity and a soft, even light, and he exhibited his work in Boston and New York as well as locally. Red Cherries (1866) is typical of his small, precise fruit still lifes, painted in the decade when still life enjoyed a wide popularity among American buyers. He taught and encouraged other painters in the area. He died in Fall River in 1905. The picture in the National Gallery was a gift of William and Abigail Gerdts, scholars of American still life.","q5517292":"Gainsborough Dupont was born in 1754, the nephew of Thomas Gainsborough, whose name he was given, and was his pupil and assistant, working in the studio of his famous uncle and copying and finishing portraits. After Gainsborough's death in 1788 he carried on as a portraitist in London in the manner of his master, with the same long, elegant figures and feathery handling, though with less genius. He painted society portraits and a series of the Elder Brethren of Trinity House, and he died in 1797, aged forty-three. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his portrait of Mary Anne Jolliffe, painted after 1788, and the Metropolitan Museum his portrait of Anne Elizabeth Cholmley, later Lady Mulgrave.","q959212":"Jules Dupré was born in Nantes in 1811, the son of a porcelain manufacturer, and began as a painter on porcelain before turning to landscape. Visits to England and the example of John Constable, whose pictures had been shown in Paris in 1824, encouraged his taste for free handling and dramatic skies. He settled at L'Isle-Adam, north of Paris, and with his friend Théodore Rousseau became a leader of the Barbizon generation of landscape painters, though he never lived at Barbizon itself. He painted stormy skies, great oaks and cattle at water in thick, dark, richly worked paint. Cows Crossing a Ford, dated 1836, comes from his early years. Collectors in France and America admired him, and he died at L'Isle-Adam in 1889.","dupuis_pierre":"Pierre Dupuis was a French painter of still life, born in 1610 and dead in 1682. His specialty was fruit, flowers and game, laid out on tables or ledges in the French manner of the mid-seventeenth century, which preferred orderly, restrained arrangements to the lavishness of Flemish pictures. Still Life with Trophies of the Hunt, in the National Gallery, shows a rabbit and birds, the quarry of a day's sport, with other objects, as a record of the pleasures of the hunt. The picture came as a gift from Jeffrey E. Horvitz. Still life ranked low in the academic hierarchy of subjects, so his career is poorly documented, and little is recorded here about his teachers, patrons or reputation.","q22094662":"Dupuy is the name of an enamel painter, thought to be German, who was active about 1801 to 1817. The Metropolitan Museum has a single work, a portrait of Margaret Rieche Richard, painted in enamel, the hard, glassy medium in which colours are fused to a metal plate by firing, and which keeps its brilliance for centuries. Miniature portraits in enamel were still made in the early nineteenth century, long after ivory had become the favoured medium, and were often wanted for brooches and lockets. The portrait was left to the Museum in 1918 by Georgiana Emily Reynolds. The painter's first name, birthplace and training are not recorded, and the question mark after his nationality shows that even that is uncertain.","q5540446":"George Henry Durrie was born at Hartford, Connecticut, in 1820 and grew up in New Haven, where he trained with the portrait painter Nathaniel Jocelyn. He travelled as a portrait painter, then in the 1850s turned to the landscape he knew, painting New England farms and villages in the snow, with sleighs, skaters, hay wagons and children in the lanes. Currier and Ives made lithographs of several of his pictures, among them Home to Thanksgiving, which reached a very wide public and made his idea of rural America a familiar one. He died young, in 1863, aged forty-three. The Metropolitan Museum owns Red School House (Country Scene), of 1858, a winter scene of a schoolhouse among snowy fields, with wagons.","dutch_17th_century":"Dutch 17th Century is the label used by the National Gallery of Art for a group of nine pictures whose painters cannot be named, seven of them catalogued as Rembrandt Workshop, meaning that they were made by pupils and assistants working in Rembrandt's studio or in his manner. Most of them hung for a long time as works by Rembrandt himself. Some carry cautious suggestions of a hand: A Girl with a Broom is thought possibly to be by Carel Fabritius, The Descent from the Cross probably by Constantijn van Renesse, and The Philosopher possibly by Willem Drost. The group also includes a Portrait of Rembrandt (1650), Joseph Accused by Potiphar's Wife (1655) and two character heads. Two further pictures, a portrait of a man and a trompe l'oeil of an etching by Ferdinand Bol, are likewise unattributed.","dutch_painter":"Dutch Painter is the Metropolitan Museum's label for an unnamed artist whose only recorded date is 1636, the date found on the picture A Young Woman in a Landscape. It is an oil on wood and shows a young woman in a landscape, perhaps a portrait in the pastoral guise that was fashionable in the Dutch Republic in the 1630s, when sitters were sometimes painted as shepherdesses or in the open air as allusions to love and courtship. The painting came to the Museum in 1931 with the bequest of Michael Friedsam. Nothing is recorded of the painter's name, training or home city, and the sitter is not identified, so the question of whether this is a portrait or a character study cannot be settled from the information given here.","q660767":"Frank Duveneck was born at Covington, Kentucky, in 1848 and began as a decorative painter of church altars. In 1870 he went to Munich, where he studied under Wilhelm von Diez and learned the dark, bold, richly loaded brushwork of the Munich school, inspired by Frans Hals and Velázquez. His Whistling Boy of 1872 made his name. In 1875 he returned to Munich to teach a circle of American students, called the Duveneck Boys, and in 1879 he took them to Venice, where they etched and painted the city. His wife, Elizabeth Boott, died in 1888, and he returned to Cincinnati, where he taught for years at the art academy and became a revered figure. He died in 1919. The Metropolitan Museum owns Lady with Fan (1873); the Cleveland Museum of Art owns The Venetian Girl, of about 1880.","q1268511":"Dwight William Tryon was born in Hartford, Connecticut, in 1849, worked in a bookstore, and taught himself painting before going to Paris in 1876, where he studied with Jacquesson de la Chevreuse and Harpignies and admired Daubigny. Returning to the United States in 1881, he settled in New York, spent summers at South Dartmouth, Massachusetts, and from 1886 taught at Smith College in Northampton. He became a leading painter of the Tonalist taste for quiet, hazy landscapes at dawn, evening and moonlight, in subtle harmonies of grey, green and gold, as in Dawn, Early Spring, Evening, New Bedford Harbor and Moonlight. The collector Charles Lang Freer was his chief patron and bought many pictures. A gallery at Smith College bears his name. He died in 1925.","q214905":"Thomas Eakins was born in Philadelphia in 1844, studied at the Pennsylvania Academy and anatomy at Jefferson Medical College, and from 1866 to 1870 trained in Paris with Jean-Léon Gérôme and Léon Bonnat. In Spain, he admired Ribera and Velázquez. He returned to Philadelphia and painted what he saw, with a surgeon's honesty: rowers on the Schuylkill, as in The Biglin Brothers Racing (1872), surgeons at work in The Gross Clinic (1875), and portraits that refuse flattery, such as Louis Husson (1899). He taught at the Pennsylvania Academy, where his insistence on drawing from the nude led to his dismissal in 1886. Few of his pictures sold, and he died in 1916 in relative obscurity. He is now regarded as one of the greatest American painters.","q931422":"Seth Eastman was born in 1808 at Brunswick, Maine, graduated from West Point in 1829 and served as a United States Army officer all his working life. He taught drawing at West Point from 1833 to 1840 and was posted to Fort Snelling, in Minnesota, from 1841 to 1848 and again later, where he lived among the Dakota, or Sioux, and painted their daily life, ceremonies and games with careful detail. He illustrated Henry Rowe Schoolcraft's six-volume Indian Tribes of the United States (1851 to 1857) with hundreds of plates, and painted a series of views of forts for the Capitol. Ball Playing among the Sioux Indians (1851) shows a stick-and-ball game much like lacrosse. He retired as a brevet brigadier general and died in 1875. His pictures are valued both as art and as ethnography.","q1278282":"Eastman Johnson was born in 1824 at Lovell, Maine, and began as a crayon portraitist in Boston. He studied in Düsseldorf from 1849, then in The Hague, where he admired the Dutch masters and was nicknamed the American Rembrandt, and in Paris, returning to the United States in 1855. He painted portraits of leading men, such as Genio C. Scott (1859), and scenes of everyday life that made him a favourite of the 1860s and 1870s: Black families in the South, as in Old Kentucky Home (1859), children, farm work and the cranberry harvest on Nantucket. Winding Yarn (1872) shows a courtship scene in a Nantucket kitchen. He was a founder of the Metropolitan Museum of Art in 1870. He died in New York in 1906, one of the leading American genre painters.","q1066388":"Charles Warren Eaton was born in 1857 and became one of the American painters of the Tonalist movement. He painted quiet, almost monochrome landscapes of pine woods, marshes and winter fields at dusk and evening, in soft browns, greys and blues, and gave them an air of poetic stillness. Tonalist painters admired the French Barbizon artists and the Dutch landscapists of the seventeenth century. Woods in Winter (1886) is an early example, painted in the first decade of his career, with a snowbound grove. It was given to the National Gallery by Stephen and Andrew Trachtenberg in memory of relatives. Little is recorded here of his birthplace, teachers and patrons, and he died in 1937.","q22691329":"Ebenezer F. Brundage is recorded as active between 1843 and 1867, and the Metropolitan Museum catalogues him as an American miniature painter. The one work by him in the museum is a Portrait of a Gentleman, painted about 1845 in watercolour on ivory, a small portrait of the sort that Americans commissioned in the 1840s for a locket, a case or a family album, at a time when the daguerreotype was beginning to replace the miniature. The sitter is not named. The miniature was acquired in 2006 with the Fletcher Fund. The span of years in which he is recorded suggests a long career, but his birthplace, training, teachers and patrons are not recorded here.","q20204965":"Ebenezer Mack is recorded as active between 1785 and 1808, and the Metropolitan Museum catalogues him as an American portrait painter. The museum's one example is a watercolour on ivory of Jasper Ely Cropsey, painted about 1794, a miniature of the kind made for sitters in the first years of the republic, when ivory miniatures were fashionable in the northeastern cities and were kept in cases or set in jewellery. Miniaturists of the time worked in small, closely stippled strokes, often copying a sitter's features in a single sitting. The miniature was acquired by the Museum in 2000. Nothing is recorded here about Mack's birth, training or home, and he is known from a handful of dated works.","q4476179":"Edgar Melville Ward (1839 to 1915) was an American painter and teacher, the brother of the sculptor John Quincy Adams Ward. He studied in New York and in Paris, and became known for paintings of craftsmen at work. The Coppersmith, of about 1898, shows a workman in his shop, an interior with tools and copper vessels, in a realist manner that treats the dignity of manual labour. Ward taught at the National Academy of Design in New York, and the picture was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1900 by a group of gentlemen. Little else is recorded here about his early life, and his reputation rests on a few pictures of working men.","q21857368":"Edmé Charles de Lioux de Savignac is recorded as a French painter active about 1766 to 1772, and he appears here with two small works of those years in the Metropolitan Museum, A River Landscape and A Picnic, which are catalogued as miniatures and painted on paper, not on the ivory, vellum or enamel of more usual miniatures. Both show men and women in a landscape, one beside a river, the other at a picnic, small outdoor scenes in the pastoral taste of the French Rococo, with figures enjoying the countryside. They came to the Museum with the bequest of Mary Clark Thompson in 1923. Nothing else is recorded here of his training, patrons, or other works.","q1285488":"Edme Quenedey was born at Riceys-le-Haut in Champagne in 1756 and died in Paris in 1830. He is known above all for the physionotrace, a mechanical device that traced a sitter's profile; he devised his own version in the 1780s, at the same time as Gilles-Louis Chrétien, who is more often credited with the invention. With it portraits could be made quickly and cheaply, then reduced and engraved in numbers. He worked in Paris and produced thousands of small profile portraits, many of men and women of the Revolutionary years. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man, of about 1780, is a profile on ivory in the manner he made popular. Quenedey also worked as a miniaturist and engraver.","q576570":"Edmond-François Aman-Jean was born at Chevry-Cossigny in 1858 and trained in Paris at the École des Beaux-Arts under Henri Lehmann, a pupil of Ingres. There he became a close friend of Georges Seurat, with whom he shared a studio, and he admired Puvis de Chavannes. He became a Symbolist painter, known for soft, dreamy, almost pastel-coloured portraits and allegories of women, painted in muted tones with thin outlines. The Outskirts of a Village, of about 1880, is an early landscape, painted before his mature style, and was acquired by the Metropolitan Museum in 2002. He was a popular portraitist of Parisian society, and he died in Paris in 1936.","q3680885":"Francis William Edmonds was born at Hudson, New York, in 1806 and worked as a bank clerk and later a bank officer in New York while painting in his spare time, studying at the National Academy of Design, of which he became a member. After a visit to Europe he took up the scenes of everyday American life: farmhouse kitchens, children, courting couples and the census taker, painted with humour and clear, careful detail. His pictures were bought by the American Art-Union, which distributed them to its subscribers as engravings and prizes, and made him widely known. Taking the Census, of 1854, The New Bonnet, of 1858, and Preparing for Christmas, of 1851, are in the Metropolitan Museum. He died in 1863.","q21994056":"Edmund C. Coates is recorded as a painter active in New York between 1837 and 1872. The Metropolitan Museum's Bay and Harbor of New York from Bedlow's Island, dated 1864 or 1866, shows ships in the harbour as seen from a small island in the Upper Bay, where Fort Wood stood; the island was later the site of the Statue of Liberty, placed there in 1886. Harbour views of this kind were bought by shipowners, merchants and visitors, who liked pictures of the port's busy shipping. The picture came to the Museum with the Edward W. C. Arnold Collection of New York prints, maps and pictures. Nothing is recorded here of Coates's birth, training or patrons.","q724148":"Édouard Detaille was born in Paris in 1848 and became a pupil of Ernest Meissonier, the leading painter of military history, at seventeen. He fought in the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 to 1871 and spent the rest of his career painting the French army, from the soldiers of that war to the Napoleonic Imperial Guard. With his friend Alphonse de Neuville he made huge panoramas of battles, and his small, accurate pictures, such as A Dragoon on Horseback (1876), were bought by collectors. The Defense of Champigny (1879) recalls the battle of December 1870 near Paris, and Gendarmes d'Ordonnance (1894) shows mounted soldiers of Napoleon's guard. Elected to the Institut in 1892, he died in Paris in 1912. His patriotic style was very popular, especially in America.","q40599":"Édouard Manet was born in Paris in 1832 into a prosperous family and, after failing the naval examination, studied from 1850 to 1856 with Thomas Couture while copying old masters in the Louvre. His frank, flat, boldly lit pictures of modern life, such as Luncheon on the Grass, shown at the Salon des Refusés in 1863, and Olympia, which caused a scandal at the Salon of 1865, made him the leader of the avant-garde, though he always wanted success at the official Salon. Spanish painting, above all Velázquez, and Japanese prints shaped his black outlines and broad, quick brushwork. His friends included Baudelaire, Zola, Mallarmé and Berthe Morisot, who married his brother. The Railway, of 1873, and Oysters are in Washington. He died in 1883, after the amputation of a leg.","q880509":"Eduard Gaertner was born in Berlin in 1801 and trained at the royal porcelain factory there as a painter of porcelain, then went to Paris in 1821, where he studied with the architectural painter Ouvrié and stayed until 1825. Returning to Berlin, he became the leading painter of the city, producing precise, clear, sunlit views of its streets, squares and palaces, which were admired by the court and the king of Prussia. He later travelled to Russia. His City Hall at Thorn, of 1848, in the National Gallery of Art, shows the Gothic town hall of Thorn, now Toruń in Poland, with its tower and market square. He died in Berlin in 1877. His pictures are valued today as faithful records of the Berlin of the Biedermeier years, before the city was transformed by industry and by the later imperial capital.","q781137":"Edwaert Collier, also known as Evert Collier, was a Dutch painter of still lifes, born at Breda about 1640 and recorded until after 1707. He worked in Leiden for many years, where he joined the guild and painted vanitas pictures, symbolic still lifes of skulls, books, musical instruments, jewellery and globes, which remind the viewer that life is brief and wealth, learning and pleasure are vain. He also painted trompe l'oeil letter racks. About 1693 he moved to England, and he may have died there or back in Leiden. The Metropolitan Museum's Vanitas Still Life, of 1662, includes violins, books, jewels, a skull and a globe, a thorough set of such emblems. It was bought for the Museum in 1871.","q22691438":"Edward Crowell Potter is recorded as an American portrait painter active about 1825 to 1830. The Metropolitan Museum's only example, Portrait of a Gentleman, is a miniature in watercolour on ivory, the favourite small portrait type of early nineteenth-century America. Ivory miniatures were often set in cases or lockets and served as keepsakes or as gifts between family members. The sitter is not named. The portrait was a gift to the Museum in 2006 from Gloria Manney, whose gifts include many miniatures. Beyond the few years in which he is recorded, little is known of Potter's birth, training, patrons and place of work, and no other documented works are mentioned here.","q20857484":"Edward Dalton Marchant (1806 to 1887) was an American portrait painter, and the Metropolitan Museum owns one of his miniatures, Portrait of a Lady, dated 1842, a watercolour on ivory acquired in 2006 with the Fletcher Fund. In 1842 the miniature was still a standard small portrait in America, kept in a case, but the daguerreotype, introduced in 1839, was already taking away the trade, and many miniaturists of Marchant's generation turned to larger oil portraits. Marchant is known to have worked in oils as well. The details of his training, place of work and patrons are not given in the Museum's record, and the sitter is not named.","q5343075":"Edward Gay was born in Dublin in 1837 and emigrated with his family to the United States in 1848, settling in Albany, New York, where he trained with the landscape painter James M. Hart. He studied in Germany and then lived in New York, becoming a landscape painter of the farms, marshes and tidal flats of the northeastern states. Broad Acres (1887) is a characteristic view of open fields under a wide sky. He painted in subdued tones with a feeling for light and weather. The picture was given to the Metropolitan Museum in 1887 by several gentlemen, soon after it was painted. He died in 1928, at the age of ninety, and is now known chiefly for his quiet views of the American countryside.","q1292928":"Edward Greene Malbone was born at Newport, Rhode Island, in 1777 and, though largely self-taught, became by his teens the leading miniature painter in America. He worked in Providence, Boston, New York, Philadelphia and Charleston, and in 1801 visited London with his friend Washington Allston, where he met Benjamin West and was praised by English painters. His miniatures on ivory are delicate and clear, with softly modelled faces, luminous skin tones and an elegant simplicity; examples include Mrs. James Lowndes (1801), Joel Roberts Poinsett (1801) and Cornelius George Bowler. He painted an allegory, The Hours, about 1801, his most ambitious work. He died of tuberculosis at Savannah in 1807, aged twenty-nine, and is regarded as the finest American miniaturist.","q2723190":"Edward Hicks was born in Pennsylvania in 1780 and apprenticed to a coach maker, learning to paint coach panels and signs. In his twenties he became a Quaker and, from about 1812, a travelling minister, and he painted on the side to support his family, in Newtown, Bucks County. He is famous for his Peaceable Kingdoms, of which he made some sixty versions, showing the prophet Isaiah's vision of the lion lying down with the lamb, with William Penn's treaty with the Indians in the background, a sign of the harmony Quakers hoped to see on earth. He also painted farms, such as the Cornell Farm of 1848, with its prize cattle. He died in Newtown in 1849. Admired today as one of America's greatest folk painters, he was self-taught and uneasy about painting as a worldly occupation.","q2605527":"Edward Lamson Henry was born in Charleston, South Carolina, in 1841, grew up in New York, and studied in Philadelphia and in Paris. He served with the Union army during the Civil War and made sketches of army life. After the war he became a painter of nostalgic scenes of old America: horse-drawn coaches, country stations, colonial houses and the people who lived in them, painted with the exactness of an antiquarian, since he collected old carriages, costumes and furniture himself. The Washington Old Westover House, painted in 1869, shows the great eighteenth-century mansion on the James River in Virginia not long after the war. From 1884 he lived at Cragsmoor, an artists' colony in the Shawangunk mountains of New York, and he died in 1919.","q18730215":"Edward Miles was born at Yarmouth, England, in 1752 and trained as a painter at the Royal Academy Schools in London. He became a successful miniaturist, painting aristocratic sitters, and his portraits on ivory, delicate in colour, were widely admired. In 1797 he went to Russia, where he worked as a miniature painter at the court of the Tsars Paul I and Alexander I. In 1807 he emigrated to the United States, and settled in Philadelphia, where he painted miniatures of Americans until his death in 1828. The Metropolitan Museum holds two of his watercolours on ivory in the American Wing, L. B. Walker and a Portrait of a Gentleman, both given by Gloria Manney in 2006. His career thus spans three countries and the years in which the miniature was at its height.","q966864":"Edward Moran was born at Bolton, Lancashire, in 1829, the son of a hand-loom weaver, and emigrated with his family to the United States in 1844, settling in Philadelphia. He worked as a weaver, then studied painting with the marine painter James Hamilton, and became a marine and landscape painter, living in New York from 1872. His brothers Thomas and Peter were painters too, and so were his sons Percy and Leon. He painted ships, harbours and storms in a broad, luminous manner influenced by Turner and by Hamilton, and is best known for a series of paintings of American naval history and for The Unveiling of the Statue of Liberty (1886). Marine, of about 1871, is a characteristic sea picture. He died in New York in 1901.","q22667542":"Edward S. Dodge (1816 to 1857) was an American miniature painter whose work is represented in the Metropolitan Museum by four watercolours on ivory, painted between about 1836 and 1845. One portrait, of about 1836 to 1837, shows John Wood Dodge, probably his brother, who was also a miniaturist; another shows William Henry Tallmadge (1841). The other two are Portraits of a Gentleman, unidentified. His career fell in the last great years of American miniature painting, before the daguerreotype took over the trade of cheap portraits in the 1840s and 1850s, and he died young, aged forty-one. The miniatures came to the Museum in 2006, partly as a gift of Gloria Manney. Little else is recorded here about his training, home or patrons.","q3735411":"Edwin Lord Weeks was born in Boston in 1849 and went to Paris in the 1870s, where he studied under Léon Bonnat and Jean-Léon Gérôme and settled for most of his life. He travelled widely in the East, in Morocco, Egypt, Persia and, in 1882 to 1883 and again in 1892 to 1893, in India, where he was received at the courts of several Indian princes. His large, sharply detailed Orientalist pictures of bazaars, mosques and royal processions, painted in vivid colour, were shown at the Paris Salon and bought by wealthy American collectors. The Rajah Starting on a Hunt, of about 1895, shows a prince setting out on horseback with his retinue. He wrote and illustrated a travel book, From the Black Sea through Persia and India (1896). He died in Paris in 1903.","q550146":"Gerbrand van den Eeckhout was born at Amsterdam in 1621, the son of a goldsmith, and was a pupil and lifelong friend of Rembrandt. He learned from him in the later 1630s, and painted biblical and historical scenes, portraits and genre pictures in a rich, warm manner close to Rembrandt's, such as Isaac Blessing Jacob, of 1642, while later his work grew lighter and more elegant, with musical parties and elegant figures in silvery tones of the kind the Amsterdam taste of the 1650s preferred. He was also a poet and a collector, and lived in Amsterdam until he died in 1674. The Metropolitan Museum owns both Isaac Blessing Jacob and A Musical Party, of the early 1650s.","q874770":"Eglon van der Neer was born in Amsterdam in 1635 or 1636, the son of the landscape painter Aert van der Neer, and trained with Jacob van Loo. He worked in Rotterdam and in the Spanish Netherlands, serving the governor in Brussels, and later became court painter to the Elector Palatine, Johann Wilhelm, at Düsseldorf, where he died in 1703. He painted elegant genre scenes of young women reading, writing or making music in well-furnished rooms, painted smoothly and with careful attention to satin and carpets, in the manner of Gabriel Metsu and Gerard ter Borch, as well as portraits and mythological subjects. The Reader, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a typical example. His reputation has been modest, but his refined interiors are well liked.","q6118567":"Jacob Eichholtz was born in Lancaster, Pennsylvania, in 1776 and trained as a coppersmith. He turned to portrait painting around 1805, largely teaching himself, and sought advice from Thomas Sully on visits to Philadelphia. For the rest of his life he worked from Lancaster, travelling to other cities for commissions, and painted more than two hundred portraits of merchants, officials, clergymen and their families. His likenesses are direct, firmly drawn and honest, with dark, rich backgrounds that recall Gilbert Stuart. He was a respected regional portraitist rather than a national celebrity, and the Met's portrait of Samuel Humes, about 1825, shows him in full maturity. He died in Lancaster in 1842, and his portraits remain a useful record of Pennsylvania society in the early republic.","q301":"El Greco, Domenikos Theotokopoulos, was born at Candia on Crete in 1541 and died at Toledo in 1614, after a career that took him from icon painting to Venice and Rome and to Spain. In his last years he ran a large workshop in Toledo with his son Jorge Manuel, and repeated his successful compositions in versions of differing quality. The Adoration of the Shepherds, of about 1612 to 1614, in the Metropolitan Museum, is catalogued as by El Greco and workshop; it is a version of the painting he made for the chapel of his own burial in the convent of Santo Domingo el Antiguo, which is now in the Prado. Workshop participation was usual for a master with many commissions, and shows how his pictures were in demand in Toledo.","q389726":"Elihu Vedder was born in New York in 1836 and trained in Paris and Florence, where from 1857 to 1860 he knew the Italian realist painters known as the Macchiaioli. He settled in Rome in 1866 and lived in Italy for most of the rest of his life, with a summer home on Capri. His early landscapes, like Three Trees: Italy (1871), are small and fresh, but he became known for dreamlike, symbolic pictures, among them The African Sentinel (1865), Lair of the Sea Serpent and The Cup of Love, with mysterious figures and strange light. His most famous work is a set of illustrations for the Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám (1884), and he also painted a mural for the Library of Congress. His memoir, The Digressions of V., appeared in 1910. He died in Rome in 1923.","q213163":"Elisabeth Louise Vigée-LeBrun painted the Portrait of Jean-Baptiste Lemoyne the Younger in 1774, when she was only nineteen. The sitter, a leading French sculptor then seventy, is shown in working clothes, with close-cropped grey hair, a frank, observant stare and a bemused smile, and the portrait reinterprets a well-known pastel of him by Maurice-Quentin de La Tour. It shows her early talent and the friendly ties she formed with older artists. In that year she was admitted to the Academy of Saint Luke in Paris; two years later she married the dealer Jean-Baptiste-Pierre Le Brun, and in the late 1770s she became Marie Antoinette's portraitist. She lived until 1842. The Cleveland Museum of Art received this picture as a bequest of Dr. Paul J. Vignos Jr. in 2011.","q5362847":"Eliza Goodridge (1798 to 1882) was an American miniature painter, born in Massachusetts, who worked in Boston, where her elder sister Sarah Goodridge was the city's leading miniaturist and had learned something from Gilbert Stuart. The Metropolitan Museum has five of her portraits in watercolour on ivory, painted between about 1825 and 1835, among them a Portrait of a Lady (1829), a Portrait of a Boy and a portrait of Alice Goudry. They show the clean, delicate style of the Boston school, in which faces are modelled in tiny strokes of colour and set in simple, uncluttered ovals. The miniatures came to the Museum in 1929 and 2006. Her sister's fame has overshadowed her, and little else is recorded here about her training, patrons or reputation.","q5364190":"Elkanah Tisdale was born at Lebanon, Connecticut, in 1771, and died after 1834. He was a miniature painter and engraver who worked in Hartford and New York, and he is credited with the famous cartoon The Gerry-mander of 1812, a map of a Massachusetts senate district that looked like a salamander and gave a name to the practice of drawing electoral boundaries for political advantage. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of James Fowle Baldwin, painted in watercolour on ivory in 1817, shows his skill as a miniaturist. It was given to the Museum in 2006 by Gloria Manney. Little is recorded about his training, though he is said to have studied with the painter John Trumbull.","q711203":"Emanuel de Witte was born at Alkmaar about 1616 or 1617 and trained there and in Delft, where he was a member of the painters' guild from 1642. He began with portraits and history pictures, but about 1650 turned to the subject that made his name: the interiors of Dutch churches, whitewashed after the Reformation, with their tall columns, light falling through high windows, and small groups of visitors, worshippers and dogs. His church interiors are often partly imaginary; Interior of a Church, of about 1680, combines features of Amsterdam's Oude Kerk with invented ones, and Interior of the Oude Kerk, Delft (probably 1650), shows a real church. He moved to Amsterdam in 1652 and lived there, in debt, until his death in 1692. His light-filled interiors are among the finest of their kind.","q452321":"Emanuel Leutze was born in 1816 at Schwäbisch Gmünd in Württemberg and was taken as a child to the United States, where he grew up in Philadelphia and began painting portraits. In 1841 he went to study in Düsseldorf, and he stayed in Germany until 1859, becoming the leader of the American artists there. His Washington Crossing the Delaware (1851), the version in the Metropolitan Museum, was painted in Düsseldorf, with the help of American visitors who posed for the figures; the painter Worthington Whittredge stood in as Washington, and Leutze later painted Whittredge's portrait (1856). The picture was enormously popular, a patriotic image painted in the years of the revolutions of 1848 in Europe. He returned to America and painted a mural in the Capitol. He died in Washington in 1868.","q765989":"Emanuel Murant was born in Amsterdam in 1622 and died at Leeuwarden in Friesland in 1700. He is said to have been a pupil of Philips Wouwerman in Haarlem, and he specialised in small paintings of weathered farmhouses, ruined castles and village courtyards, in a quiet, silvery brown tonality and with careful detail of brick, thatch and timber. The Metropolitan Museum's The Old Castle shows a castle among trees and clouds, with men, women and dogs in the foreground. Pictures of this kind appealed to Dutch collectors of the later seventeenth century, who liked rustic, picturesque views at moderate prices. He spent his last decades in Friesland. Little else is recorded here about his patrons and his reputation, which has remained modest.","q1530172":"Émile Loubon was born at Aix-en-Provence in 1809 and trained under his fellow Aixois François-Marius Granet, whom he accompanied to Rome in 1829. There he made open-air studies of clouds and sky, such as the Metropolitan Museum's Landscape Study with Clouds, painted about 1829 to 1831 in oil on cardboard. Back in Provence he became the leader of a regional school of landscape and animal painting, and from 1845 he directed the fine arts school in Marseille, where he guided a generation of Provençal painters, among them Paul Guigou. He painted the farms, flocks and sunlit hills of Provence. He died at Marseille in 1863. The cloud study came to the Museum in 2011, a reminder of how much French landscape painters learned from sky studies made out of doors.","q742379":"Émile van Marcke de Lummen was born at Sèvres in 1827 and trained as a porcelain painter at the Sèvres manufactory, where his friend and mentor Constant Troyon turned him to animal painting. He settled at Bonnières-sur-Seine and then in Normandy, and painted cattle at pasture, by streams and by farm buildings in clear, sunlit landscapes, with a sharp, accurate rendering of the animals and a fresh handling of grass and sky. The Flood Gate, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a typical pastoral scene with cows, and came to the Museum with the bequest of Susan P. Colgate in 1936. He exhibited at the Paris Salon from 1857 and won medals, and was popular with English and American buyers. He died at Hyères in 1890.","emilian_16th_century":"Emilian 16th Century is the label used by the National Gallery of Art for an unnamed painter of Emilia, the region of northern Italy around Bologna, Modena, Parma and Ferrara, who painted in the later sixteenth century. The catalogue's dates of 1500 to 1599 mark the whole century, not a life. The one picture, Fantastic Landscape with Figures, is an oil on canvas given by Duncan Phillips, the founder of the Phillips Collection in Washington, and is classed with literary and theatrical themes, which suggests a scene from a poem or play set in an imagined countryside. Emilia produced Correggio, Parmigianino and the Carracci, and its painters liked romantic landscapes and poetry. Nothing is recorded of the painter's name, training or patrons.","q1338447":"Emmanuel Tzanes was a Greek painter of the Cretan school, recorded from 1636 until his death in 1690, who worked as an icon painter in Venice and elsewhere in the Greek world, where Greek communities had lasting ties with the Republic of Venice. He painted in the traditional Byzantine manner, with gold backgrounds and solemn, frontal faces, but his figures show the influence of Italian painting in their softer modelling and more natural faces. The Metropolitan Museum has three heads in tempera on a gold ground, the Head of Christ, Head of the Virgin and Head of Saint John the Baptist, which together form the traditional Deësis, the group in which the Virgin and the Baptist intercede with Christ. The heads were given to the Museum in 1933 by Mrs. Henry Morgenthau.","q1343521":"Enoch Seeman the Younger was born at Danzig about 1690 and died in London in 1744. The son of a portrait painter of the same name, he trained with his father and became a fashionable portraitist in London, painting gentlemen, officials and their families. His portraits are smooth, polished and rather formal, in the manner of the late style of Godfrey Kneller. The Metropolitan Museum's Sir James Dashwood (1715 to 1779), dated 1737, shows a young baronet of twenty-two in elegant dress. Overshadowed by the leading portraitists of his generation, he is little known today. The portrait was bought for the Museum in 1956 by the Victor Wilbour Memorial Fund. Little else is recorded here of his patrons or reputation.","q5379343":"Enoch Wood Perry (1831 to 1915) was an American painter, born in Boston, who studied in Europe and later painted genre scenes, portraits and landscapes. He travelled widely, and painted scenes from the places he visited. The Metropolitan Museum owns two of his pictures: Talking It Over (1872), in which men and horses are in a rural setting, and The True American (about 1874), a man reading a newspaper, a subject that reflects the new importance of the daily press in American life after the Civil War. Pictures of ordinary Americans at their daily business, painted with a light touch and a little humour, were popular with buyers of the 1870s. Perry's teachers, patrons and career are not further described here.","q597126":"Erastus Salisbury Field was born in 1805 at Leverett, Massachusetts. He went to New York in 1824 to study with Samuel F. B. Morse, but stayed only a few months, and spent the next two decades as a travelling portrait painter in the Connecticut River valley, painting farmers, shopkeepers and their wives, as in Mr. Pease and Mrs. Harlow A. Pease (about 1837). As photography took over the trade of portraits, he settled in Sunderland, Massachusetts, and painted biblical and historical scenes from imagination, such as Pharaoh's Army Marching and Ark of the Covenant, ending with the vast Historical Monument of the American Republic, made about the time of the centennial. He died in 1900, and is now ranked among the best American folk painters. The National Gallery of Art owns a dozen of his pictures.","q1348628":"Ercole Banci was a Bolognese painter, recorded as active in the early sixteenth century; the Metropolitan Museum gives no dates of birth or death. Bologna at that time was dominated by Francesco Francia and Lorenzo Costa, whose gentle, clear Madonnas and altarpieces set the standard of the local school, and Banci's work belongs to the same tradition. The Museum owns one example, a Madonna and Child in tempera on wood, part of the Friedsam Collection bequeathed in 1931. Tempera, a paint made with egg yolk, dried fast and gave the clean, bright colour and fine detail of Early Renaissance panels, though by this time oil was becoming usual. Nothing further is recorded here about Banci's training, patrons or other works.","q354786":"Ernest Meissonier was born at Lyons in 1815, grew up in Paris and, after a short time in Léon Cogniet's studio, taught himself mainly by studying the Dutch masters. He made his name with tiny, minutely finished pictures of cavaliers, card players, musicians and soldiers in seventeenth-century dress, such as The Card Players (1863) and Soldier Playing the Theorbo (1865), which brought some of the highest prices ever paid for the work of a living artist. Later he painted Napoleonic battle scenes, including 1807, Friedland, which belonged to the New York merchant A. T. Stewart and came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1887. In 1881 the photographer Eadweard Muybridge showed him how horses really gallop, and Meissonier was struck by it. He died in Paris in 1891.","q2826950":"Agnolo degli Erri was an Emilian painter, born in 1417 and dead in 1497, who worked at Modena with his brother Bartolomeo, running a workshop that produced altarpieces and panels for churches in Modena and nearby towns. His style belongs to the Ferrarese-Emilian tradition of the fifteenth century, with sharp, crisp outlines, clear colour and some influence from the work of Piero della Francesca and Mantegna. A Dominican Preaching, of about 1470, is a small panel in tempera on poplar, probably part of an altarpiece predella, which shows a friar addressing a crowd; such preaching scenes illustrated the lives of Dominican saints. The panel was given to the National Gallery by Frieda Schiff Warburg in memory of her husband Felix. Little else is documented about Agnolo.","esaias_van_de_velde":"Esaias van de Velde was born in Amsterdam in 1587, moved to Haarlem in 1610, joined the guild there, and in 1618 settled in The Hague, where he painted for Prince Maurits and Frederik Hendrik. He was a founder of Dutch realist landscape: the Cleveland Museum says that he and other Haarlem painters began a revolution in the 1610s by painting the humble, everyday land and people of the Netherlands in convincing detail, instead of using landscape as an idealised backdrop. His winter scenes, dunes and country roads, with small figures, were models for Jan van Goyen, who studied with him in Haarlem. Travelers by a Lake, painted in 1625, is a small round panel, only about seven inches across. He died in The Hague in 1630.","q21995361":"Étienne Bouchardy was born in Paris in 1797 and died there in 1849. He was a miniature painter who worked in Paris in the reigns of Charles X and Louis-Philippe, when portraits on ivory were still in demand from the Parisian middle class despite the new competition of the daguerreotype from 1839. The Metropolitan Museum has two of his miniatures, both called Portrait of a Woman, dated 1832 and 1838, painted on ivory, one laid on card for support. The sitters are not named. The pictures came to the Museum in 1925 and 1933 as a gift from Mrs. Louis V. Bell and the bequest of Margaret Crane Hurlbut. Little else is recorded here about his training, teachers, patrons or reputation.","q21997260":"Étienne-Charles Le Guay was a French miniature painter born in 1762 and dead in 1846, who lived through the Revolution, the Empire and the Restoration. The Metropolitan Museum owns one example of his work, a Portrait of a Woman, painted about 1800 on ivory, from the bequest of the railroad magnate Collis P. Huntington in 1900. It belongs to the years of the Directory and Consulate, when fashionable Parisian women wore simple, high-waisted dresses and cropped, loosely dressed hair in the manner of the ancients, and when the small portrait on ivory was at the height of its popularity. The sitter is not identified. Nothing certain is recorded here of Le Guay's training, patrons or career.","q676001":"Eugène Devéria was born in Paris in 1805, the brother of the lithographer and painter Achille Devéria, and studied with Girodet and Lethière. He caused a sensation at the Salon of 1827 with The Birth of Henri IV, a huge picture in the warm, rich colour of Rubens and Veronese, that made the twenty-two-year-old the hope of the Romantic generation, alongside Delacroix. He was a friend of Victor Hugo and the writers of the circle around him. His later career did not fulfil this promise: he painted religious pictures and decorations, and in 1848 moved to the south of France, living at Pau, where he died in 1865. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of Louis-Félix Amiel (1837) belongs to his middle years.","q353746":"Eugène Fromentin was born at La Rochelle in 1820, the son of a physician, and went to Paris to study law, but turned to painting, working with the landscape painters Rémond and Cabat. In 1846 he travelled to Algeria and returned in 1847 to 1848 and in 1852 to 1853, and these journeys gave him the subjects of his life: Arab horsemen, falconers, caravans and camps in blond, silvery light, in a restrained and truthful manner. He also wrote, and his books, A Summer in the Sahara (1857), A Year in the Sahel (1859), the novel Dominique (1862) and The Masters of Past Time (1876), a study of Flemish and Dutch painting, ranked him among the best writers on art. He died near La Rochelle in 1876. The National Gallery of Art owns Arab Encampment, of 1873.","q714341":"Eugène Isabey was born in Paris in 1803, the son of Jean-Baptiste Isabey, Napoleon's favourite miniature painter, and became one of the leading marine painters of the French Romantic school. In 1830 he sailed with the French fleet to Algiers as the Navy's official painter, and he spent much of his life painting the coasts of Normandy and Brittany, with storm-lit seas, wrecks, fishing boats and old harbours. He also painted festive scenes in costume, fairs, weddings and tavern feasts, in rich colour and with a lively touch that owes something to Rubens and the Dutch. The English painter Bonington and Delacroix were friends and influences, and Johan Jongkind was among his pupils. He died in Montévrain in 1886. The Washington Marine, of about 1830, belongs to his early years.","q3059972":"Eugène Lavieille was born in Paris in 1820 and died there in 1889. A landscape painter of the generation that followed the Barbizon artists, he painted the villages and riversides of the Île-de-France, the forest of Fontainebleau and the valley of the Loing. The Metropolitan Museum's The Village of La Celle-sous-Moret shows a village near Moret-sur-Loing, close to Fontainebleau, an area where many landscape painters worked, and where Sisley later settled. It was given to the Museum in 1960 by Arthur Wiesenberger. He exhibited at the Salon from the 1840s. Little else is recorded here about his teachers, patrons or reputation, which was strongest among Parisian collectors of landscape.","european_18th_century":"European 18th Century is the label the National Gallery of Art uses for a painter, or several painters, who cannot be named or even placed in a country: the dates 1700 to 1799 simply cover the whole century. The one picture, Portrait of a Man, of about 1770, in oil on canvas, is catalogued as Rococo in style, which suggests a date and a taste of the middle of the century, the elegant, light, graceful manner of French and Italian courts. It is part of the Andrew W. Mellon Collection, the founding gift of the Gallery. The sitter is not identified. Portraits of anonymous men by anonymous painters were produced in great numbers in eighteenth-century Europe, and the label records how little can now be said.","european_painter":"European Painter is the Metropolitan Museum's label for an unnamed artist known only from a single work dated before 1500: a Head of Christ, painted in tempera on wood against a gold ground. Heads of Christ were common devotional images in the Middle Ages and early Renaissance, often derived from the old tradition of the image of the face of Christ on the cloth of Saint Veronica, and were painted as separate panels for private devotion or as parts of larger altarpieces. Tempera, the paint made with egg yolk, and gold leaf are the usual materials of such panels. The picture came to the Museum with the bequest of Harry G. Sperling in 1971. Nothing is recorded of the painter's name, place or training.","q1378723":"Eustache Le Sueur was born in Paris in 1616 and trained under Simon Vouet, whose lessons he absorbed, but he developed a calmer, more classical style, influenced by Raphael through engravings and by Poussin; he never went to Italy. His masterpiece is the series of twenty-two paintings of the life of Saint Bruno (1645 to 1648) for the Carthusian monastery in Paris, now in the Louvre. He also painted the Cabinet of Love and the Cabinet of the Muses at the Hôtel Lambert (1646 to 1649), and in 1648 was one of the founders of the Royal Academy. The Rape of Tamar, probably of about 1640, is an early work. He died in Paris in 1655, aged thirty-eight, and was called the French Raphael.","q539036":"Allart van Everdingen was born at Alkmaar in 1621 and studied in Utrecht with Roelant Savery and in Haarlem with Pieter de Molijn. About 1644, on a voyage, his ship was driven by a storm onto the coast of Norway or Sweden, and he spent some time there, sketching mountains, waterfalls, pine forests and wooden cabins. Back in the Netherlands he settled in Haarlem and, from 1657, in Amsterdam, and made paintings from those sketches for years, with rushing waterfalls and rough rock, which were novel in Holland and influenced Jacob van Ruisdael, who took up the waterfall as a subject. Waterfall with Log Cabins (about 1665 to 1670) is a late example. He also etched, notably a series illustrating the fable of Reynard the Fox. He died at Amsterdam in 1675.","q1840555":"Adrianus Eversen was a Dutch painter of town views, born in 1818 and dead in 1897, who worked in the Biedermeier style of the mid-nineteenth century. He painted the streets, canals and squares of Dutch towns, among them Amsterdam, in precise, bright pictures, often with small figures, shops and carts, in a revival of the town-view tradition of the seventeenth century. Amsterdam Street Scene, a small oil on wood, belongs to the National Gallery's Ailsa Mellon Bruce Collection, given by Paul Mellon's sister, which includes a number of small French and Dutch pictures. Pictures of this kind were bought by Dutch and foreign collectors who liked cities seen in a clear, friendly light. Little is recorded here about his training and patrons.","q102272":"Jan van Eyck was born at Maaseik, in the present Belgian Limburg, about 1390 and was court painter to John of Bavaria, Count of Holland, at The Hague, from 1422 to 1424, and then to Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, from 1425. He lived at Lille and from 1430 at Bruges, where he died in 1441. Philip trusted him with diplomatic missions, among them a voyage to Portugal in 1428 to 1429 to paint the portrait of a prospective bride. His Ghent Altarpiece (1432), begun by his brother Hubert, and the Arnolfini Portrait (1434) are great works of early Netherlandish painting, in which he used oil paint to achieve unequalled detail, depth of colour and reflected light. The National Gallery's Annunciation, of about 1434 to 1436, was probably one wing of a diptych.","q22691293":"Ezra Atherton is recorded as active between 1838 and 1842, and the Metropolitan Museum catalogues him as an American miniature painter. The one picture by him in the museum is a Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1840, painted in watercolour on ivory. He worked in the last decade in which the miniature was a standard form of portrait in America, before the daguerreotype, introduced in 1839, took over the cheap end of the trade. The miniature was acquired for the Museum in 2006 with the Fletcher Fund. The sitter is not named, and nothing is recorded here about Atherton's birthplace, training, teachers or patrons, so the few years in which he is recorded are all that can be said of his career.","q22687958":"Nothing is recorded of F. A. Mead beyond a single dated picture, Old Brewery, Five Points Mission, New York (1870), an oil on canvas in the Metropolitan Museum. Its subject is a famous New York site: the Old Brewery, a converted brewery in the Five Points district of lower Manhattan, became in the first half of the nineteenth century a notorious tenement housing hundreds of the poor, and it was pulled down in 1852, when a Methodist mission was built on the spot. The painting shows the street and the mission buildings, with figures and roads in view. It came to the Museum with the Edward W. C. Arnold Collection of New York prints, maps and pictures. Mead's first name, dates and training are not recorded.","q22094639":"F. Carbonara is an Italian painter of whom the Metropolitan Museum records only an initial and a surname. No dates are given, and nothing certain is known of where he or she trained, who taught them or who bought the work. The one picture in the collection, Portrait of a Woman, is a miniature painted on ivory, a round disc about two and five eighths inches, or 66 millimetres, across. Portrait miniatures of this kind were made throughout Europe from the sixteenth century into the nineteenth, to be worn as jewellery, kept in a case or given as a keepsake, and many of their makers are known today only by a signature or a name in an old inventory. Little more can be said with confidence.","q310715":"Henri Fantin-Latour was born at Grenoble in 1836 and trained in Paris, learning chiefly by copying old masters in the Louvre. He knew the painters of the new movements, Courbet, Manet and Whistler, and exhibited at the Salon des Refusés in 1863, but he remained a quiet, independent man who never joined the Impressionists. His group portraits of artists and writers, among them Homage to Delacroix (1864) and A Studio at Batignolles (1870), record the Paris avant-garde. His still lifes of roses, peaches and pansies, painted with a calm, tender realism, were bought above all in England, where his friend Edwin Edwards found him buyers. He also made lithographs inspired by Wagner, Berlioz and Schumann. He died in 1904 in the Orne. The nine New York pictures are mainly flower and fruit pieces, with one portrait.","q21403125":"Joseph Anderson Faris is listed as an American painter who lived from 1833 to 1909, and the National Gallery of Art holds one small picture by him, The Neigh of an Iron Horse, an oil on canvas about 35 by 45 centimetres, dated to the 1860s with the last figure uncertain. The title plays on iron horse, the nickname in common use for the steam locomotive in nineteenth-century America. The railway, which opened the West and carried armies in the Civil War, was a frequent subject of American painting and prints in those years. Nothing certain is recorded here about where Faris trained, whom he worked for or how his reputation stood, and nothing more can honestly be added.","q7788344":"Thomas Charles Farrer was born in London in 1839 and went to the United States in 1860, where he joined the small circle of American Pre-Raphaelites. These painters followed the English critic John Ruskin in urging artists to paint nature faithfully, leaf by leaf and rock by rock, out of doors and in clear colour. Farrer's Mount Tom, of 1865, shows a hill in the Connecticut River valley of Massachusetts in just that precise manner. His brother Henry was also a painter and etcher. Thomas later returned to England and died in London in 1891. The Pre-Raphaelite movement in America was short-lived, and its pictures drew little notice until the twentieth century revived interest in them.","q1361116":"Paolo di Giovanni Fei was a painter of Siena, active in the later fourteenth century and until his death in 1411. He is generally thought to have learned from the older Sienese master Bartolo di Fredi, and his work shows the influence of Taddeo di Bartolo, painters who kept alive the gold grounds, graceful figures and sweet colour of the Sienese tradition while Florence was moving toward new ways of painting. His panels were made for churches and chapels in and around Siena. The National Gallery of Art holds two: The Presentation of the Virgin in the Temple, dated 1398 to 1399, in tempera on wood, and a small Assumption of the Virgin with busts of the Archangel Gabriel and the Virgin of the Annunciation, of about 1400 to 1405. He ranks among the lesser Sienese masters of his time.","q2157088":"Robert Feke was born about 1707 at Oyster Bay, on Long Island, and became one of the most gifted portrait painters of colonial America. Little is recorded of his early life, and the date and place of his death, about 1751, are also uncertain. In the 1740s he worked in Boston, Newport and Philadelphia, painting merchants, officers and their wives in a crisp, rather formal manner, with fine silks and carefully drawn faces. His group portrait of Isaac Royall and his family, of 1741, is his best known work. Captain Alexander Graydon, of about 1746, and Simon Pease, of about 1749, are in the National Gallery of Art. Later generations rediscovered him, and he is often named as the first great painter born in the American colonies.","q214564":"Ferdinand Hodler was born in Bern in 1853, the son of a poor carpenter, and lost his parents and several of his brothers and sisters to tuberculosis while still young. He learned painting from a decorative painter, then from a view-painter at Thun who sold landscapes to tourists, and in 1871 moved to Geneva, where he studied under Barthélemy Menn. His mature style, which he called parallelism, set repeated figures and gestures in rhythmic patterns against broad, simple landscapes. Swiss in subject and symbolic in meaning, it won him praise in Paris, a room of his own at the Vienna Secession of 1904, and the commission for a great mural of Swiss soldiers for the national museum in Zurich. He died in Geneva in 1918. The Dream of the Shepherd, of 1896, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q21994070":"Ferdinand Machéra was a French painter of portrait miniatures, born in 1776 and dead in 1843. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Man, dated 1827, is a small oval on ivory, about 57 by 43 millimetres, the size of a locket or the lid of a snuffbox. Such miniatures were the usual way to commission a likeness for a family or a friend before the daguerreotype arrived in 1839, and the art flourished in France from the Revolution through the reign of Charles X, when miniatures were a regular part of the Paris Salon. The sitter is not named in the museum's record, and little is recorded of Machéra's teachers, patrons or later reputation.","q3068652":"Ferdinand Richardt was a Danish landscape painter, born in 1819 and dead in 1895. He trained at the Royal Academy in Copenhagen and in the 1850s crossed the Atlantic, settling in the United States, where he spent much of his later career. He became known for views of Niagara Falls, a subject that drew visitors and painters in great numbers in the middle of the century, and he painted the cataract many times. Underneath Niagara Falls, dated 1862, takes the viewer beneath the falling water, as the title says, and is now in the Metropolitan Museum. Richardt's later life and the extent of his reputation are not well documented, and his name is far less familiar than those of the American painters who worked at Niagara.","q559559":"Fernand Khnopff was born in 1858 at Grembergen, in Belgium, and grew up in Bruges and Brussels. He studied law before turning to art, took lessons from Xavier Mellery at the Brussels Academy, and travelled to Paris, where he admired Gustave Moreau and the English Pre-Raphaelites, especially Burne-Jones. He was a founder member in 1883 of Les XX, the Brussels group of progressive artists, and became the leading Belgian Symbolist, painting cool, silent, enigmatic women, many modelled on his sister Marguerite, in pictures such as Memories (1889) and The Caresses (1896). He also designed his own house in Brussels, built as a kind of private temple. He died in Brussels in 1921. Hortensia, an early work of 1884, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q776171":"Fernando Yáñez de la Almedina was a Spanish painter documented from about 1505 into the 1530s, and the Metropolitan Museum dates its Head of Christ to about 1506. His name points to Almedina, in La Mancha. He worked in Florence, where he is thought to be the Ferrando Spagnuolo who assisted Leonardo da Vinci on the lost Battle of Anghiari, a link that may explain the soft shading and calm modelling of his work. Back in Spain, he and a fellow painter, Fernando de los Llanos, painted the doors of the high altar of Valencia Cathedral, a work finished about 1510. He later worked in Cuenca, where he painted for the cathedral. His dates of birth and death are unknown.","q3181492":"John Ferneley was born in 1782 at Thrussington, in Leicestershire, the son of a wheelwright. His talent for drawing horses brought him to the notice of the Duke of Rutland, who sent him to London to train under the sporting painter Benjamin Marshall. In 1814 Ferneley settled at Melton Mowbray, the centre of English fox-hunting, and made a long and successful career painting the hunters, hounds and patrons of the Leicestershire hunts, as well as racehorses and country-house groups. His pictures are clear, bright and exact about the animals, in the English sporting tradition. Heaton Park Races (1829), in the National Gallery of Art, shows a meeting held in the park of Heaton Hall, near Manchester, seat of the Earl of Wilton. He died at Melton Mowbray in 1860.","ferrarese_15th_century":"Ferrarese 15th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for an unnamed painter of Ferrara, working somewhere between about 1455 and 1470, the dates given for the one picture, a small Madonna and Child with Angels in tempera on a poplar panel. The catalogue's dates of 1400 to 1499 mark the whole century, not a life. Under the Este family, Ferrara was a brilliant court town, which drew painters such as Pisanello and Rogier van der Weyden and bred its own masters, Cosimo Tura, Francesco del Cossa and Ercole de' Roberti, known for hard, brilliant colour and sharply drawn forms. A panel of this size was probably made for private prayer. Nothing is recorded of the painter's name, training or patrons.","q21498094":"Martin Edgar Ferrill is listed as an American painter, born in 1836 and dead in 1897, and the National Gallery of Art holds one work by him, Country Dance, an oil on canvas of 1883, about 62 by 72 centimetres. As the title says, it is a scene of rural amusement, a subject that nineteenth-century American painters from William Sidney Mount to Eastman Johnson treated for city buyers who liked to picture the countryside as friendly and musical. The date puts it in a decade when pictures of old-fashioned country life were popular with such buyers. Little is recorded here about where Ferrill lived, how he trained or who bought his pictures, and nothing beyond the dates and the title can be stated with confidence.","q296265":"Filippino Lippi was born at Prato in 1457, the son of the painter Fra Filippo Lippi and a nun, Lucrezia Buti. His father died when he was about twelve, and he was trained by his father's assistant Fra Diamante and then by Botticelli, from whom he took his flowing line. About 1481 he was chosen to complete the Brancacci Chapel frescoes in Florence, left unfinished by Masaccio half a century earlier. He later painted the Strozzi Chapel in Santa Maria Novella and, in Rome, the Carafa Chapel in Santa Maria sopra Minerva for Cardinal Oliviero Carafa. His late style grows fantastic and crowded with antique ornament. He died in Florence in 1504. The Metropolitan Museum holds his Madonna and Child of about 1483 to 1484.","q3745336":"Filippo Tarchiani was a painter of Florence, born in 1576 and dead in 1645, who belonged to the generation that followed the great Florentine Mannerists. Painters of his day, among them Cigoli and Passignano, favoured clearer light, plainer stories and warmer feeling than their elders, in step with the reforms of the Counter-Reformation church. Tarchiani painted altarpieces and devotional pictures for churches and convents. The Metropolitan Museum owns Saint Dominic in Penitence, of about 1607, an oil on canvas some 132 by 109 centimetres, which as the title says shows the founder of the Dominican order at penance, a subject much favoured in those years. Few details of his training, patrons or later reputation can be given with certainty.","q3063754":"Alvan Fisher was born at Needham, Massachusetts, in 1792 and became one of the first American painters to make landscape and country scenes his main business. He had little formal training, working at first for a Boston ornamental painter and teaching himself by copying and by painting out of doors. In 1825 he went to Europe, seeing the galleries of England, France and Italy. He painted farms, cattle, hunters and harbours, and portraits to earn his keep, and he exhibited at the Boston Athenaeum. Mishap at the Ford, of 1818, in the National Gallery of Art, is an early genre picture whose title tells of an accident at a river crossing. He died at Dedham, Massachusetts, in 1863. Today he is valued as an early interpreter of American rural life.","flemish_17th_century":"Flemish 17th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for an unnamed painter of the southern Netherlands, represented by one picture, Portrait of a Man in a Wide-Brimmed Hat, an oil on panel of the early 1630s about 105 by 73 centimetres. The catalogue dates of 1600 to 1699 mark the whole century, not a life. The early 1630s were the years in which Rubens, Van Dyck, who left Antwerp for London in 1632, and a host of lesser painters made Flanders the centre of fashionable portraiture, with loose brushwork, easy poses and rich blacks for the sitters' clothes. Wide-brimmed hats of this kind were the fashion among men of rank and wealth. The sitter is not named, and nothing is recorded of the painter's training.","flemish_painter":"Flemish Painter is the Metropolitan Museum's name for an unknown artist, or for several, recorded as active about 1625. Two pictures are filed under it: a Portrait of a Woman, painted in oil on a small oval of copper only about 86 by 67 millimetres, and a Harbor Scene on vellum, about 104 by 145 millimetres. Both are tiny, cabinet-sized works of the kind collectors in Antwerp and Brussels prized in the age of Rubens, when painters such as Jan Brueghel the Elder worked on copper with jewel-like finish. The museum's record gives no names, dates of birth or death, teachers or patrons, and the two works may well be by different hands.","q3083823":"Léon-François-Antoine Fleury was a French landscape painter, born in 1804 and dead in 1858, of the generation that followed Corot. Like them he went to Italy, the school for French landscape painters in the 1820s and 1830s, and painted small oil studies out of doors on paper and canvas, catching the light of the Roman Campagna and the Bay of Naples. The two pictures in the National Gallery of Art, On the Bay of Naples and The Tomb of Caecilia Metella, both of about 1830, are of this kind: quick, fresh and small, each about 25 to 30 centimetres across. The tomb stands on the Appian Way outside Rome and was one of the great sights for travellers. Little is recorded of his teachers or patrons.","florentine_15th_century":"Florentine 15th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of Florence in the 1400s. Two portraits hang under it: a Profile Portrait of a Young Man, dated 1430 to 1450, and a portrait identified, with a query, as Matteo Olivieri, of the 1430s. Profile portraits, with the sitter shown in strict side view like a head on a Roman coin, were the fashion in Florence at that period, a taste fed by the humanists' interest in antiquity and by medals. Painters as various as Domenico Veneziano, Paolo Uccello, Piero della Francesca and, later, Antonio Pollaiuolo made them. Nothing is recorded of the artists' names or training here, and the question mark in the title of the second portrait shows that even the sitter is uncertain.","florentine_16th_century":"Florentine 16th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of Florence in the 1500s. Three portraits hang under it: Ugolino Martelli, of the middle of the century; a Portrait of a Young Woman of 1530 to 1540; and an Allegorical Portrait of Dante, of the later century. Florence in these years was ruled by the Medici, who became hereditary dukes in 1532 and, under Cosimo I from 1537, built a brilliant court, and the city's portraitists, led by Pontormo and Bronzino, painted cool, polished, aristocratic likenesses. Ugolino Martelli, a Florentine humanist, was the sitter of Bronzino's famous portrait now in Berlin. The painters of the Gallery's pictures are not named, and nothing is recorded of their training or patrons.","follower_of_rogier_van_der_weyden":"This entry reads Follower of Rogier van der Weyden, with the further note Master of the Saint Ursula Legend Group, Netherlandish, active in the late fifteenth century. Rogier van der Weyden, city painter of Brussels, died in 1464 and was one of the most widely imitated painters in northern Europe; his graceful, tender, emotional figures were copied for decades by workshops that never knew him. The Master of the Saint Ursula Legend is a modern name for a painter, or group of painters, usually placed in Bruges and called after a series of panels telling the story of Saint Ursula. The Metropolitan Museum's Virgin and Child, an arched panel of about 1480 to 1490 in oil on wood, shows the Virgin nursing her child. No personal name, date of birth or patron is known.","q236050":"Lavinia Fontana was born in Bologna in 1552, the daughter of the painter Prospero Fontana, from whom she learned her art. She married a fellow painter, Gian Paolo Zappi, who helped in her workshop while she supported a large family of eleven children, and she became one of the first women to make a career as a professional painter, working on altarpieces, mythologies and above all portraits of the ladies and officials of Bologna. The Portrait of a Prelate in the Metropolitan, about 1580, is an early example. About 1603 she was called to Rome by Pope Clement VIII, where she painted for papal patrons and was admitted to the Academy of St Luke. Her Minerva Dressing of 1613 is a rare female nude by a woman. She died in Rome in 1614.","q222923":"Vincenzo Foppa was born at Brescia, in Lombardy, about 1430 and became the leading painter of Milan and Pavia in the generation before Leonardo. His early style shows the influence of Padua and of Mantegna, and his earliest dated work is a Crucifixion of 1456, now in Bergamo. He worked for the Sforza dukes at Milan and Pavia, painting frescoes, among them the Portinari Chapel in Sant'Eustorgio, Milan, in the 1460s, and he returned to Brescia in 1489, working there until his death in 1515 or 1516. His figures are sturdy, simple and softly lit, with the silvery colour that marks the Lombard school. The National Gallery of Art owns two tall panels, Saint Anthony of Padua and Saint Bernardino of Siena, of about 1495 to 1500.","q719614":"Jean-Louis Forain was born at Reims in 1852 and studied in Paris, where he was taught by the sculptor Carpeaux and the painter Jacquesson de la Chevreuse. A close friend of Degas, he exhibited with the Impressionists from 1879 to 1886 and drew on Degas for his subjects: the stage, the wings, the dancers, the races at Longchamp and the law courts. He was also one of the most celebrated newspaper caricaturists of his time, drawing biting cartoons for the Parisian press, and in the Dreyfus affair he took the anti-Dreyfus side. In 1891 he married the painter Jeanne Bosc, who is the subject of The Artist's Wife Fishing (1896). He died in Paris in 1931. The National Gallery of Art has five of his works.","forster_george":"George Forster is listed in the National Gallery of Art as an American painter who lived from 1817 to 1896. The Gallery owns one picture by him, Still Life with Fruit, also called Gooseberries in a Bowl, of 1870, a very small oil on canvas only about 20 by 25 centimetres. Little pictures of fruit were popular in the United States in the decades after the Civil War, when the still-life tradition of Philadelphia and the Peale family, and the cheap coloured prints of fruit that decorated many homes, made them a staple of the American parlour. Forster's training, home, patrons and later reputation are not recorded in what is known here, and little more can honestly be said.","q380052":"Mariano Fortuny y Marsal was born in Reus, in Catalonia, in 1838, was orphaned young and raised by his grandfather, a cabinetmaker, and trained in Barcelona before winning a scholarship to Rome in 1858. In 1860 he was sent by the Barcelona authorities to Morocco to record the Spanish army's campaign, and the colourful North African subjects he found there made his name. His polished, jewel-like technique, seen in The Spanish Wedding of 1870, set the fashion for a generation of costume pictures and brought him fame and high prices through Paris dealers such as Goupil. He settled in Rome and spent time in Granada, and died of malaria in Rome in 1874, aged thirty-six. Mirope Savati Gaye, of 1865, is an early work in the Metropolitan.","q21288920":"Frédéric Dubois was a French painter of portrait miniatures, born in 1780 and dead in 1819, so that his working life belonged to the years of Napoleon's Empire and the early Restoration. The Metropolitan Museum holds one example, Portrait of a Woman, a round miniature on ivory only about 66 millimetres across. In Paris at that time the miniature painter Jean-Baptiste Isabey set the tone, with transparent colour and delicate, lightly sketched backgrounds, and the sitters were officials, officers and their families, who wanted small likenesses to give or to keep. The sitter here is not named. The record of Dubois's training and patrons is thin, and he died at the age of thirty-nine or thereabouts.","fra_angelico":"Fra Angelico was born Guido di Pietro, near Vicchio in the Mugello, about 1395, and entered the Dominican convent at Fiesole about 1420, taking the name Fra Giovanni. He had probably already trained as an illuminator and panel painter. His clear, luminous colour, gold and pure blues, and his gentle, devout figures made his name, and the fame of his goodness led later generations to call him Angelico, the angelic, and Pope John Paul II to beatify him in 1982. His greatest work is the series of frescoes in the convent of San Marco in Florence, painted about 1438 to 1445 for Cosimo de' Medici. In 1445 he went to Rome and decorated a chapel in the Vatican for Pope Nicholas V. He died in Rome in 1455. The Metropolitan Museum holds a Saint Alexander and two Crucifixions.","q7780":"Fra Bartolomeo was born Bartolomeo di Paolo del Fattorino in Florence in 1473 and trained in the workshop of Cosimo Rosselli, where he met Mariotto Albertinelli, with whom he later shared a studio. A follower of the preacher Girolamo Savonarola, he gave up painting after the friar's execution in 1498 and entered the Dominican convent of San Marco in 1500, resuming painting about 1504 as head of its workshop. His altarpieces, grand, calm and monumental, with strong colour and drapery studied on a lay figure, helped create the High Renaissance style in Florence. He visited Venice in 1508 and Rome in 1514, where he knew Raphael. He died in Florence in 1517. The Metropolitan Museum holds his Portrait of a Man and a Madonna and Child with the Young Saint John the Baptist, both from about 1497.","q205148":"Fra Filippo Lippi was born in Florence about 1406, was orphaned young and raised by the Carmelite friars of Santa Maria del Carmine, where he saw Masaccio painting the Brancacci Chapel frescoes. He took vows but lived as a working painter, favoured by Cosimo de' Medici, painting Madonnas with a warm, tender humanity and a fine line, and with solid figures learned from Masaccio and Donatello. His frescoes in the cathedral of Prato, painted from 1452 to 1465, include the Feast of Herod. In Prato he fell in love with a nun, Lucrezia Buti, and their son was the painter Filippino Lippi. He died at Spoleto in 1469, where he was painting the cathedral apse. The Metropolitan Museum holds four of his panels, among them the Portrait of a Woman with a Man at a Casement.","q127171":"Jean-Honoré Fragonard was born in Grasse in 1732 and grew up in Paris, where he briefly trained with Chardin and then with François Boucher. He won the Prix de Rome in 1752 and lived in Italy from 1756 to 1761, painting with Hubert Robert at the Villa d'Este in the company of the Abbé de Saint-Non. Back in Paris he made his name with playful, sensuous scenes of love and gardens, above all The Swing of 1767 to 1768, painted for the Baron de Saint-Julien. When Madame du Barry rejected his Progress of Love panels in the 1770s he lost an important patron, and the Revolution ended his career. He died in 1806, and his art was rediscovered in the later nineteenth century.","q1383101":"François-Auguste Biard was born at Lyon in 1799 and trained there before settling in Paris, where he became a popular Salon painter of anecdotal and dramatic subjects, from comic scenes to pictures of the slave trade. In 1839 he joined a scientific expedition to Lapland and Spitsbergen, with his wife, the writer Léonie d'Aunet, who wrote an account of it; his Arctic landscapes were novelties in Paris. Their marriage ended in scandal in 1845, when Biard surprised her with the poet Victor Hugo. In 1858 and 1859 he travelled to Brazil and wrote a book about it. He died at Samois-sur-Seine in 1882. The Metropolitan Museum holds a Bust-Length Study of a Man, an oil sketch on paper of 1848.","q3083465":"François-Joseph Heim was born at Belfort in 1787, trained in Paris and won the Prix de Rome in 1807. He spent several years in Italy and returned to become a successful history painter of the Restoration, taking medals at the Salon and orders for large religious and historical pictures for churches and palaces. His best known work is probably Charles X Distributing Awards to Artists at the Salon of 1824, a crowded group portrait of the artists of his day, now in the Louvre. He was elected to the Institut in 1829 and died in Paris in 1865. The Sack of Jerusalem by the Romans, dated 1824 and small in size, about 36 by 38 centimetres, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q647641":"François-Joseph Navez was born at Charleroi, in what is now Belgium, in 1787 and studied at the Brussels Academy, then in Paris. When Jacques-Louis David went into exile in Brussels after 1815, Navez became his pupil and friend, and he followed David's austere Neoclassical manner. He also travelled to Italy, and became the leading Belgian painter of his generation, producing portraits, notably of Brussels families, as well as religious and history pictures. From 1835 until 1862 he directed the Brussels Academy. He died in Brussels in 1869. The Massacre of the Innocents, of 1824, in the Metropolitan Museum, shows the biblical slaughter of the children of Bethlehem in the manner of David's school, with firm outlines and dramatic gestures.","q943826":"François-Louis Français was born at Plombières, in the Vosges, in 1814 and came to Paris as a young man, where he worked for a bookseller and as an illustrator while learning to paint. He first exhibited at the Salon in 1837 and was associated with the painters of Barbizon, whose study of the forest at Fontainebleau he shared, but he developed a more ordered, poetical style with a warm, atmospheric light, painting woods, rivers and Italian views. He travelled to Italy several times, and made the olive groves and waterfalls of Tivoli the subject of Gathering Olives at Tivoli (1868), a tall decorative picture in the Metropolitan Museum. He was a highly honoured landscape painter, elected to the Institut in 1890, and died in Paris in 1897.","q964812":"François Marius Granet was born at Aix-en-Provence in 1775 and trained at the drawing school of his native town under the landscape painter Constantin. In Paris he met Ingres, and in 1802 he went to Rome, where he stayed for over twenty years. He is known for small, luminous pictures of church interiors, cloisters and crypts, with strong beams of light falling on monks and arches, such as The Choir of the Capuchin Church in Rome (1814 to 1815), of which he made many versions. After his return to France he held posts in the royal museums at the Louvre and Versailles, and he left his collection to Aix, where the museum now bears his name. He died near Aix in 1849.","q3085351":"François Meuret was a French painter, born in 1800 and dead in 1887, known to the Metropolitan Museum as a maker of portrait miniatures. His Portrait of a Woman, Said to Be Marie de Mautesson, of about 1830, is a small oval on ivory about 58 by 47 millimetres. The date falls in the high Romantic years of Paris, when fashionable women had themselves painted with ringlets, wide sleeves and lace collars, and when miniatures were still a favoured keepsake. The sitter's name is recorded only as a tradition, and the museum's record gives little about Meuret's training, patrons or place in the market. He lived to the age of eighty-seven, long enough to see the photograph replace the miniature.","q18810720":"François-Théodore Rochard was a French miniature painter, born in 1798 and dead in 1858, whose elder brother, Simon Jacques Rochard, was also a well known miniaturist. The brothers are recorded in London as well as Paris, where the fashion for portraits on ivory was strong. His Portrait of a Woman, dated 1829, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a miniature on ivory about 98 by 83 millimetres, larger than most, in the soft, transparent colour then admired. Miniature painters of the 1820s competed for orders from private sitters, and many showed their work at the Salon in Paris or at the Royal Academy in London. Rochard died in 1858, as the photograph was replacing the miniature.","q983275":"Francesco Botticini was born in Florence in 1446 and was trained in the workshop of Neri di Bicci, a busy maker of altarpieces, and later absorbed the lessons of Verrocchio, Botticelli and the Pollaiuolo. He ran a large workshop that produced altarpieces and Madonnas for Florentine churches, and his figures are graceful, lively and brightly coloured, if less inventive than his models. His best known work is an Assumption of the Virgin painted for the humanist Matteo Palmieri, now in the National Gallery, London, which shows the Virgin lifted through the circles of heaven and includes portraits of Palmieri and his wife. His son Raffaello was also a painter. He died in 1497. The Metropolitan Museum holds his large Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints and Angels (1493 to 1495) and a Saint Sebastian.","q2253244":"Francesco Cairo was born in Milan in 1607 and trained there in the Lombard school of Morazzone and Cerano, painters of fervent, dramatic religious pictures. He is best known for intense half-length figures of tormented saints and biblical women, such as Judith and Herodias, with parted lips, upturned eyes and gleaming silks, painted with a strong feeling for pathos and a polished finish. Little is recorded here of his patrons, but he is counted among the most original Lombard painters of the seventeenth century. He died in Milan in 1665. His Herodias, in the Metropolitan Museum, was painted before 1635.","q983804":"Francesco Casanova was born in London in 1727 to a family of Venetian actors and grew up in Venice, the brother of the famous adventurer Giacomo Casanova. He learned painting in Venice, from Francesco Guardi among others, then went to Paris about 1751 and studied battle painting under Charles Parrocel. He became a specialist in cavalry skirmishes, hunts and landscapes with horsemen, painted in a rich, dark, dramatic manner that recalls the seventeenth-century battle painter Jacques Courtois, and he was received into the Academy in Paris in 1763. In 1783 he moved to Vienna, where he worked for the court and noble patrons, and he died near Vienna in 1803. The Metropolitan Museum holds Cavalier and Shepherd, a landscape with a horseman and sheep.","francesco_conti":"Francesco Conti, born in 1681 and dead in 1760, was a Florentine painter of the late Baroque, active in the last decades of the Medici grand dukes, whose line ended in 1737 with Gian Gastone. He is known to the museum chiefly for religious pictures in a soft, graceful style with warm colour. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns Return to Nazareth, of 1735, a scene from the childhood of Christ after the Holy Family's return from Egypt. The Florence he knew was a quieter place than it had been under the earlier Medici, and its painters were largely content to continue older traditions. Detailed records of his training, teachers and patrons are not given here, and his reputation has always been modest beside that of the great Florentines of earlier centuries.","q434425":"Francesco di Giorgio Martini was born at Siena in 1439 and was painter, sculptor, architect and military engineer in one. He trained in Siena with Vecchietta, painting panels and illuminating books, and about 1477 entered the service of Federico da Montefeltro at Urbino, where he helped to build the ducal palace and designed fortresses for the duke across the Marches. He wrote a treatise on architecture and military engineering, with drawings of machines that Leonardo later studied, and in 1490 he and Leonardo were both consulted on the cathedral of Pavia. He died at Siena in 1501. Among his few paintings is The Nativity, of about 1470, in the National Gallery of Art, while the Metropolitan Museum owns Goddess of Chaste Love, of 1468 to 1475.","q260057":"Francesco Francia, in the National Gallery of Art's listing, was the leading painter of Bologna around 1500. Born Francesco Raibolini, he trained as a goldsmith, worked as a medallist and for years directed the Bologna mint under the ruling Bentivoglio family, turning to painting in his thirties. His altarpieces and Madonnas, gentle, clear in colour and tender in feeling, made him famous across northern Italy, and Raphael wrote to him with praise in 1508. Vasari tells that Francia, on seeing Raphael's Saint Cecilia altarpiece, sent to Bologna about 1514, was so struck by its beauty that he fell ill and died; the story is a legend, since he lived on until 1517. The Gallery's picture is a portrait of Bishop Altobello Averoldo, painted about 1505, and shows the quieter, realist side of his art.","q318769":"Francesco Guardi was born in Venice in 1712 into a family of painters. For many years he worked in the family workshop under his elder brother Gianantonio, painting altarpieces and figures, and his sister Maria Cecilia married Giambattista Tiepolo, who was thus his brother-in-law. After Gianantonio's death in 1760 he turned increasingly to views of Venice and imaginary scenes of ruins and lagoons, painted in a quick, flickering, nervous touch that is the opposite of Canaletto's calm precision. He sold them to visitors and Venetian collectors, never very prosperous, and was admitted to the Venetian Academy only in 1784. He painted the festivities for Pope Pius VI's visit of 1782, two of which are in the Cleveland Museum of Art, and he died in Venice in 1793.","q3289164":"Francesco Maffei was born at Vicenza about 1605 and trained there, then studied the great painters of Venice, Veronese and Tintoretto, and the fashionable Domenico Fetti, and became the leading painter of the Veneto in the middle of the seventeenth century. His late works, such as Hagar and the Angel, of about 1657, show a restless, nervous manner, with long, flickering strokes, streaming draperies and a feverish light that was unlike anything else in Italian painting of his day. He painted altarpieces and decorations for churches and palaces in Vicenza, Padua and Venice, and later writers admired his originality. He died at Padua in 1660. The Metropolitan Museum owns Hagar and the Angel, a biblical scene of the angel appearing to the outcast servant in the wilderness.","q3081023":"Francesco Montemezzano was a Venetian painter of the later sixteenth century, generally described as a pupil and follower of Paolo Veronese. Veronese's workshop supplied the churches and palaces of Venice with large decorative pictures, and its assistants learned to produce his cool, silvery colour, rich costumes and graceful poses. Montemezzano worked in that tradition. The Metropolitan Museum owns a Portrait of a Woman with a dog, an oil on canvas about 119 by 99 centimetres, a stately, formal portrait in the Venetian manner. No dates of birth or death are given in the museum's record, and little is recorded of his life beyond his association with the master, so that he is usually remembered as one of the many painters who carried Veronese's style forward after the master's death in 1588.","francesco_renaldi":"Francesco Renaldi was a British portrait painter with an Italian name, born in 1755 and dead in 1800. He exhibited at the Royal Academy in London in the 1770s and 1780s, painting portraits and miniatures of fashionable sitters in a soft, sentimental manner. In the 1780s he went to India, where artists of the East India Company's settlements found a ready market among British officials and merchants and also among Indian princes and nobles, and he painted both. His Portrait of a Mughal Woman, dated 1787, in the Metropolitan Museum, is an oil on canvas almost a metre high, showing a lady of the Mughal world in Indian dress. Few other details of his training, patrons or reputation are given here, and his name is little known today.","q704594":"Francesco Salviati was born Francesco de' Rossi at Florence in 1510 and took his name from his patron, Cardinal Giovanni Salviati, in Rome. He trained under Andrea del Sarto and others and became one of the most brilliant Mannerist painters of the century, with elegant, twisting figures, rich colour and classical learning, working in Rome, Florence, Venice and, in 1554 to 1556, in France. His frescoes include rooms in the Palazzo Vecchio and the Palazzo Farnese. He was also a fine portraitist: Bindo Altoviti, painted about 1545 on a slab of marble, shows the Florentine banker in Rome whom Raphael had portrayed thirty years before. Salviati died in Rome in 1563. The Metropolitan Museum also holds his portraits of Carlo Rimbotti, dated 1548, and of a man.","q736065":"Francesco Solimena was born near Avellino in 1657 and trained with his father, Angelo, before settling in Naples, where he studied Luca Giordano, Mattia Preti and the Baroque masters and became the dominant painter in the city for half a century. His grand altarpieces and ceilings, in warm colour with strong contrasts of light and shade, filled the churches of Naples, and his large, busy studio trained a generation of painters, among them Corrado Giaquinto and Francesco de Mura. Patrons from across Europe sought him out. He was nicknamed Abate Ciccio, and he died at Barra, near Naples, in 1747. The Metropolitan Museum holds a Birth of the Virgin and a double portrait of Diego Pignatelli d'Aragona with an enslaved servant, painted when Solimena was in his seventies.","q963875":"Francesco Trevisani was born at Capodistria, in Istria, in 1656 and trained in Venice before moving to Rome in the late 1670s, where he spent the rest of his career. He became a leading painter of the Roman late Baroque, favoured by the Venetian cardinal Pietro Ottoboni, a great patron of music and art, and by Pope Clement XI, and painted altarpieces, frescoes and portraits in a graceful, polished manner, with smooth surfaces and clear, cool colour that points toward the Rococo. His workshop was large and busy. He died in Rome in 1746. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his sketch of about 1729 for a Holy Family altarpiece for Santa Maria in Via Lata, and the Metropolitan Museum holds a Dead Christ Supported by Angels.","q3081126":"Francesco Zaganelli, also called Francesco di Bosio, was a painter from Cotignola, in the Romagna, who worked with his brother Bernardino at Cotignola and Ravenna in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries. The brothers painted altarpieces and devotional panels in a gentle manner influenced by Marco Palmezzano and by the Bolognese Francesco Francia, then the leading artist of the region. Francesco is thought to have been born about 1470 and to have died at Ravenna in 1532. The Metropolitan Museum owns a small panel of Saint Lucy, in tempera and gold on wood, about 31 by 20 centimetres. The brothers' reputation today is largely local, and little else about their patrons or training can be stated with confidence.","q919093":"Francesco Zuccarelli was born at Pitigliano, in Tuscany, in 1702 and trained in Florence and Rome before settling in Venice about 1732. There he became the favourite painter of pastoral landscape, with peasants, shepherdesses, cattle and ruins in a sunny, idyllic countryside painted in light, sweet colour that suited the taste of the Rococo. His patrons included the British consul Joseph Smith, and he made two long visits to London, in 1752 to 1762 and 1765 to 1771, where George III bought many of his pictures and he was a founder member of the Royal Academy in 1768. He later returned to Italy and died at Florence in 1788. The Metropolitan Museum owns his Landscape with Peasants at a Fountain.","q3081147":"Francescuccio Ghissi, whose full name was Francesco di Cecco Ghissi, was a painter of Fabriano, in the Marche, active in the later fourteenth century. Fabriano was a prosperous paper-making town that later gave Italy Gentile da Fabriano, and its painters of the period worked in a refined, linear style that mixed the art of Siena, Rimini and Bologna. Ghissi painted altarpieces and panels for churches in the region, in tempera on wood with gold grounds. The Metropolitan Museum owns three small panels of about 1370 showing episodes from the legend of Saint John the Evangelist, among them the saint raising Satheus to life and causing a pagan temple to collapse, probably once parts of a larger altarpiece. Little else is recorded of his life.","q537712":"Franciabigio, born Francesco di Cristofano in Florence in 1484, trained with Mariotto Albertinelli and shared a workshop with Andrea del Sarto about 1506 to 1509. He is known for the fresco of the Marriage of the Virgin in the cloister of the Santissima Annunziata, painted in 1513, which Vasari says he damaged with a hammer when the Servite friars uncovered it before he had finished, and for the Triumph of Cicero at the Medici villa at Poggio a Caiano, left incomplete when he died in 1525. A fine portraitist, he painted clear, cool, finely observed likenesses of Florentine citizens, in a manner more sober than Andrea's. The Metropolitan Museum owns his Head of the Madonna, of about 1509.","q6232278":"John F. Francis was born in Philadelphia in 1808 and was largely self-taught. He began as a portrait painter in small Pennsylvania towns, but from the 1850s he became known for still lifes of tables laid with wine, fruit, cheese, nuts and tableware, painted in an orderly and abundant manner that appealed to middle-class buyers. The Met's Still Life with Fruit, of about 1857, is a good example of this lavish yet tidy style, which looks back to the Philadelphia still-life tradition of the Peale family. He lived much of his later life in Pennsylvania and died in 1886. His reputation, forgotten for many years, was revived in the later twentieth century.","q5480254":"Francis Blackwell Mayer was born in Baltimore in 1827, studied art in Paris in the early 1860s and lived in France for many years, showing at the Paris Salon. He painted genre scenes, portraits and views, with a careful eye for costume and anecdote, often drawing on his native Maryland and on the peasants of Brittany, and he returned to the United States late in life. Old Annapolis, Francis Street, dated 1876, in the Metropolitan Museum, shows a street in the old Maryland capital, a small oil in a fresh, sketch-like manner, painted on a visit home. He died in 1899. His reputation has never matched that of the American painters who made their careers in New York, and little is recorded here of his teachers or patrons.","q5482749":"Francis Wheatley was born in London in 1747 and studied at the Royal Academy Schools, beginning as a painter of small portraits and conversation pieces. In 1779 he left London under a cloud for Dublin, where he painted large patriotic pictures such as the Irish House of Commons, then returned in 1783 and became popular as a painter of rustic scenes and sentimental village life. His best known work is the series of Cries of London, begun in 1792, pictures of street sellers that were widely engraved. The Saithwaite Family, of about 1785, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a conversation piece, a group of a family at home. He suffered from gout in later years and died in 1801.","francisco_de_goya":"Francisco de Goya was born at Fuendetodos, in Aragon, in 1746 and trained in Zaragoza before going to Madrid, where he painted designs for the royal tapestry factory from 1775, helped by his brother-in-law Francisco Bayeu. He became painter to the king in 1786 and first court painter in 1799. A serious illness in 1792 to 1793 left him deaf, and from then his art grew more private and more searching, in the Caprichos etchings, the Disasters of War, the Third of May 1808 and the Black Paintings. Charles IV and his family, and the Duchess of Alba, sat to him. In 1824 he went into exile at Bordeaux, where he died in 1828. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns a Saint Ambrose of about 1796 to 1799.","q209615":"Francisco de Zurbarán was born at Fuente de Cantos, in Extremadura, in 1598 and was apprenticed in Seville, where from 1629 the city council invited him to settle. He painted for monasteries, above all the Carthusians, Hieronymites and Mercedarians, cycles of monks and saints in white or brown habits, with strong light falling on solid, quiet figures against dark backgrounds, and he sent large shipments of paintings to the Spanish colonies in America. In 1634 he was called to Madrid to paint for the king's palace, the Buen Retiro. As Murillo's softer art became fashionable, his own fell out of favour, and he moved to Madrid in 1658 and died there in 1664. The Metropolitan Museum holds Saint Benedict and The Young Virgin; the Cleveland Museum of Art owns Christ and the Virgin in the House at Nazareth.","q332725":"Francisco Domingo y Marqués was a Spanish painter from Valencia, born in 1842 and dead in 1920. He belonged to the generation of Spanish painters who looked to Paris in the 1870s and 1880s, when the success of Mariano Fortuny had made small, brilliant genre pictures and costume scenes highly saleable there. The Metropolitan Museum owns one picture by him, Portrait of an Old Man, painted in 1882 in oil on wood, only about 27 by 22 centimetres, a character study in the Spanish tradition of the expressive head. Details of his training, travels and patrons are not given here, and his reputation outside Spain has remained modest.","q3751077":"Francisque Millet was born at Antwerp in 1642, of French descent, and went to Paris in his youth. There he became a painter of classical landscapes in the manner of Nicolas Poussin and Gaspard Dughet, with mythological or biblical figures set in wide, ideal scenery of trees, hills and ruins, and in 1673 he was received into the French Academy. He died in Paris in 1679, aged only thirty-seven. The Metropolitan Museum owns Mercury and Battus, a large landscape of about 119 by 178 centimetres, whose subject comes from Ovid: Mercury steals Apollo's cattle, is seen by the shepherd Battus, who is bribed to keep silent, and afterwards turns him into stone when he breaks his word.","q1442507":"Frans Francken the Younger was born in Antwerp in 1581 into a large family of painters, and learned from his father, Frans Francken the Elder. He became a master in Antwerp in 1605 and ran a busy workshop producing small, crowded cabinet pictures on panel and copper, with biblical, mythological and allegorical subjects, scenes of witches and views of collectors' galleries, in bright colour and fine detail. He often painted the figures in works whose landscapes, flowers or buildings were done by specialists, among them Jan Brueghel the Elder. In the Gallery's Garland of Flowers with Adoration of the Shepherds, of about 1625 to 1630, a small painting on copper, the wreath of flowers is credited to a flower painter known only as Master HDB. Francken died in Antwerp in 1642.","franco_flemish_15th_century":"Franco-Flemish 15th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for an unnamed painter working in the French or Burgundian lands about 1410, the date of the one picture, Profile Portrait of a Lady, a panel about 52 by 37 centimetres. The catalogue dates of 1400 to 1499 mark the whole century, not a life. The years around 1410 belong to the late Gothic courts of Paris, Burgundy and the Loire, where princes such as the Duke of Berry and the Duke of Burgundy employed painters from the Netherlands, and independent portraits of women in profile, with elaborate headdresses and costly dress, were just beginning to appear. The lady is not named, and nothing is recorded of the painter's name, training or patrons.","q22667520":"Francois M. Guyol de Guiran was a painter with a French name, whose birth year is given as 1773 or 1777 and who died in 1849. The Metropolitan Museum owns his Portrait of a Gentleman and His Daughter, of about 1805, a watercolour on ivory and painted paper only about 9 by 12 centimetres, a miniature that shows a man with his small daughter, a more intimate subject than the usual single bust. The date falls in the years of the Consulate and the Empire, when miniature painters in France and across Europe found plentiful custom among officers, officials and merchants. Little else is recorded of his training, patrons or later reputation, and nothing certain can be added to the dates and the one miniature.","q1345302":"Francis Davis Millet, known as Frank Millet, was born at Mattapoisett, Massachusetts, in 1846 and served in the Union army in the Civil War, as a drummer boy and then a surgeon's assistant, before graduating from Harvard. He studied at the academy in Antwerp, where he met Edwin Austin Abbey, and settled in England, living for years at Broadway, in Worcestershire, among a colony of artists. He painted genre scenes and classical subjects in a light, pretty manner, such as A Cosey Corner (1884), and murals, notably for the 1893 World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago. He was also a writer and a war correspondent in the Balkans and the Philippines. He died in 1912 in the sinking of the Titanic.","q21993970":"Frank Waller was an American painter, born in 1842 and dead in 1923. The Metropolitan Museum holds two of his pictures, both showing the museum's former home: Entrance Hall of the Metropolitan Museum of Art when in Fourteenth Street, of about 1881, a small oil on wood, and Interior View of the Metropolitan Museum of Art when in Fourteenth Street, of 1881. The museum, founded in 1870, occupied a former mansion on West Fourteenth Street in the 1870s and moved to its present building in Central Park in 1880, so the pictures are memories made soon after the move, with women visitors and sculpture in the rooms. Records of Waller's training, patrons and later career are not given here.","frans_francken":"Frans Francken, also called Frans Francken the Younger or the Second, was born at Antwerp in 1581, the son of the painter Frans Francken the Elder, and belonged to the large Francken dynasty of painters in the city. He is known for small cabinet pictures, painted with fine detail and bright colour on panel or copper, of biblical stories, classical myths, allegories and scenes of everyday life, and for crowded views of collectors' cabinets. His shop sold them to local and foreign buyers, and he worked beside other Antwerp specialists. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns The Triumph of Neptune and Amphitrite, of the 1630s, painted on copper mounted on wood, a mythological scene of the sea god and his bride in triumph. He died in Antwerp in 1642.","q167654":"Frans Hals was born at Antwerp about 1582 and was taken as a child to Haarlem, where his family settled and where he worked for the rest of his life. He is thought to have learned from the painter and writer Karel van Mander, joined the painters' guild in 1610, and became the foremost portraitist of the town. His loose, flickering, confident brushwork, in which every stroke can be seen, gave his sitters, civic guardsmen, merchants, brewers and musicians, a laughing immediacy, as in The Laughing Cavalier of 1624. He painted group portraits of the Haarlem militia companies and lively genre pictures such as Merrymakers at Shrovetide. He grew poor in old age and was helped by the city, and he died in 1666. The Metropolitan Museum and the Cleveland Museum of Art hold fine examples.","q577489":"Frans Post was born at Haarlem about 1612, the son of a glass painter and brother of the architect Pieter Post. In 1637 Count Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen, governor of the Dutch colony in northeastern Brazil, took him there as landscape painter, and Post spent some seven years drawing and painting the coast, the sugar mills, rivers and plantations. He is regarded as the first European to paint the landscape of the Americas. Back in Haarlem he went on painting Brazilian views from memory and sketches for the rest of his life, in calm, bright, orderly compositions that collectors in the Netherlands and abroad prized, among them paintings that Johan Maurits sent to Louis XIV. He died in Haarlem in 1680. A Brazilian Landscape, of 1650, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","frans_pourbus":"Frans Pourbus the Younger was born at Antwerp in 1569, the son of the painter Frans Pourbus the Elder and grandson of Pieter Pourbus of Bruges, and trained in the family tradition of exact, polished portraiture. In 1600 he became court painter to the Gonzaga at Mantua, and in 1609 he moved to Paris, where Marie de' Medici and the young Louis XIII employed him as their portraitist, and he painted the royal family and the court until his death in 1622. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his tall, formal portrait of King Louis XIII, dated 1611, painted when the boy king was ten and his mother ruled as regent.","q934540":"Frans Pourbus the Younger, born at Antwerp in 1569 and dead in Paris in 1622, was a portraitist to courts across Europe. He was trained by his father, himself a portraitist, and worked at Antwerp before going in 1600 to Mantua, where he painted the Gonzaga family for nearly a decade, including Duke Vincenzo's daughter Margherita, who was born in 1591 and in 1606 married the Duke of Lorraine. His manner was polished and cool, with exact rendering of costume and jewellery, in the international court style of the day. In 1609 he went to Paris to serve Marie de' Medici. The Metropolitan Museum owns the portrait of Margherita Gonzaga as Princess of Mantua, a young girl in sumptuous dress.","q959236":"Frans van Mieris the Elder was born at Leiden in 1635, the son of a goldsmith, and was trained by glass painters and then by Abraham van den Tempel and Gerrit Dou, the founder of the Leiden school of fine painters, who is said to have called him the prince of his pupils. He painted small cabinet pictures of doctors' visits, tavern scenes, ladies at their toilet and musical parties, with a delicate, enamel-like finish and wonderfully rendered satin, velvet and metal. Archduke Leopold Wilhelm of Austria and later Cosimo III de' Medici, who visited his studio, were among his buyers. His sons and a grandson also painted. He died in Leiden in 1681. The Serenade, of about 1678 to 1680, a tiny panel of 15 by 11 centimetres, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q689388":"Franz Anton Maulbertsch was born at Langenargen, on Lake Constance, in 1724 and went to Vienna as a boy, where he studied at the Academy and became a member in 1759. He became the leading painter of the late Baroque and Rococo in the Habsburg lands, decorating church ceilings and library halls in Austria, Hungary and Bohemia with fresco in silvery, shimmering colour and flickering, nervous brushwork, with swirling clouds and figures lit by a cool light. He also made small oil sketches, the models by which patrons approved his designs, which collectors prize. He died in Vienna in 1796. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his sketch for an altarpiece for Saint Ulrich in Vienna, and the Metropolitan Museum a small oil of 1772 to 1773, The Glorification of the Royal Hungarian Saints.","q879031":"Franz Ludwig Catel was born in Berlin in 1778 and trained there, then went to Paris in 1807 and in 1811 to Rome, where he stayed for the rest of his life. As a landscape and genre painter he specialised in views of Rome and Naples and in Italian country life, with picturesque peasants, festivals and monks, in clear, bright, tidy colour that suited the taste of northern visitors on the Grand Tour. His patrons included Crown Prince Ludwig of Bavaria and the Prussian royal family, and he was a friend of the architect Schinkel. He died in Rome in 1856. First Steps, of about 1820 to 1825, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a gentle domestic scene of a family with a small child.","q1389116":"Franz Napoleon Heigel was a German painter, born in 1813 and dead in 1888, who worked as a portrait miniaturist. His Henriette Sontag, of about 1835, in the Metropolitan Museum, is an oval on ivory of about 73 by 60 millimetres. The sitter was one of the most celebrated sopranos of her age, born at Koblenz in 1806 and famous in Berlin, Vienna, Paris and London. She left the stage when she married Count Rossi in 1828, returned to it in 1849 and died of cholera in Mexico City in 1854 while on tour. Heigel's training, patrons and later career are not well recorded here, and the miniature is the only work of his that can be named with certainty.","q585323":"Franz von Lenbach was born at Schrobenhausen, in Bavaria, in 1836, the son of a master builder, and trained in Munich, where he studied under Karl von Piloty. In the 1860s he travelled in Italy and Spain, and made copies of Titian, Rubens and Velázquez for the Munich collector Count Schack, which taught him the old masters' rich tones. He became the most sought-after portraitist in Germany, painting Bismarck dozens of times, as well as Kaiser Wilhelm I, Pope Leo XIII, Wagner, Liszt and Gladstone, and he built a grand Florentine villa in Munich, now the Lenbachhaus. He died in 1904. His portrait of his daughter Marion, dated 1900, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q3083165":"Franz Wolfgang Rohrich was a German painter, born in 1787 and dead in 1834, recorded by the Metropolitan Museum for a single picture, Sophia of Poland, with the dates 1464 to 1512. The sitter, a daughter of King Casimir IV of Poland, married the Margrave Frederick of Brandenburg-Ansbach and died in 1512, so a portrait of her made in the early nineteenth century must have been painted from an earlier likeness. Such historical portraits were in demand when German princes and the Romantic movement revived interest in medieval and Renaissance history. The picture is an oil on canvas about 77 by 56 centimetres. Little is recorded about Rohrich's training, patrons or reputation.","q168659":"Franz Xaver Winterhalter was born in 1805 at Menzenschwand, in the Black Forest, and trained in Freiburg and Munich as a lithographer and painter. In 1828 he became court painter to the Grand Duke of Baden, and in 1834 he settled in Paris, where he became the most successful portraitist in Europe, painting Louis-Philippe, then Napoleon III and the Empress Eugénie, and from 1842 Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. His courtiers in silk and satin, with smooth, flattering surface and glowing colour, defined royal portraiture in the middle of the century. After 1870 his reputation fell with his sitters' fortunes and the rise of realism, but it has recovered. He died at Frankfurt in 1873. The Metropolitan Museum holds Florinda, a large nude, and portraits of Eugénie and Countess Lamsdorff.","q160252":"Frederic Leighton was born at Scarborough in 1830, the son of a physician, and travelled and studied in Europe from his youth, in Rome, Florence, Frankfurt and Paris. His first great success was Cimabue's Madonna Carried in Procession, shown at the Royal Academy in 1855 and bought by Queen Victoria. He settled in London in 1860 and built a house in Holland Park with a famous Arab Hall. He painted classical and mythological subjects in a cool, elegant, polished style, and made sculpture too. Elected president of the Royal Academy in 1878 and knighted that year, he was raised to the peerage as Baron Leighton in January 1896, the first painter so honoured, and died the next day. The Metropolitan Museum holds Lachrymae, The Return of Persephone and other works.","q560787":"Frederic Remington was born at Canton, New York, in 1861 and studied briefly at the Yale art school before going west in 1881, where he travelled in Montana and the Southwest and tried ranching. He made his living as an illustrator for Harper's Weekly and other magazines, drawing cowboys, soldiers, scouts and Native Americans with unmatched energy, and from the 1890s he was also a sculptor, famous for The Bronco Buster (1895). He reported on the Spanish-American War in Cuba in 1898. Later he turned to atmospheric night scenes in oil. He died at Ridgefield, Connecticut, in 1909, aged forty-eight. The Metropolitan Museum holds his oil Pitched It Sheer into the River . . . Where It Still Is Seen in the Summer, of 1889, with its long title.","q720073":"Frederick Goodall was born in London in 1822, the son of the engraver Edward Goodall, and grew up in a family of engravers and painters. He began with scenes of English and Breton rural life, and in 1858 he travelled in Egypt with the artist Carl Haag, living among the Bedouin and sketching the desert, the Nile and its people, and he made later trips. He turned to Egyptian and biblical subjects, painted with local colour from his sketches and costumes, and was elected to the Royal Academy in 1863. He died in 1904. The Metropolitan Museum owns his portrait of William Edward Dighton in Middle Eastern dress, of about 1852 to 1853, painted several years before Goodall's first journey east.","q3087120":"Frederick Kemmelmeyer is an elusive figure. Records place him at work from about 1788 to 1816, mainly in Maryland, where he is known as a drawing teacher and painter of portraits and patriotic subjects; the life dates 1736 to 1848 sometimes printed for him cannot be right, since they would give him a span of 112 years. His best-known work is a picture of George Washington reviewing the troops at Fort Cumberland, Maryland, in 1794. First Landing of Christopher Columbus, about 1800 to 1805, is typical of his self-taught, nearly folk style, with flat, bright figures and simplified space, and it shows how Americans of the young republic took up Columbus as a national hero. Few details of his birth or training are known.","q20859506":"Frederick R. Spencer was an American portrait painter, born in 1806 and dead in 1875. He painted both in oil and in miniature, as the Metropolitan Museum's two examples show: Mary Ann Garrits, an oil on canvas of 1834 about 84 by 66 centimetres, and a Portrait of a Lady of 1830, a watercolour on ivory only about 6 by 6 centimetres. American portraitists of those years often did both, working from town to town for merchants and their wives, until the photograph took over their trade in the 1840s and 1850s. Spencer's life spans the great age of the American portrait before photography. Little is recorded here of his training, patrons or later reputation.","q5498619":"Frederick Richard Lee was born at Barnstaple, in Devon, in 1798 and served for some years as an army officer before giving up his commission to paint. He exhibited at the Royal Academy from the 1820s and was elected an Academician in the 1830s, specialising in English woodland and river landscapes in a traditional style, with warm tones and careful handling of trees and water. He often worked with the animal painter Thomas Sidney Cooper, who added the cattle to his pictures. In 1865 he painted General Garibaldi's Residence at Caprera, the small island off Sardinia where the Italian patriot lived, a picture now in the Metropolitan Museum. Lee retired to Devon and died at Barnstaple in 1879.","q5498969":"Frederick Waters Watts was an English landscape painter, born in 1800 and dead in 1870, often described as a follower of John Constable. Like Constable he painted the villages and countryside north of London, in Middlesex, with broad skies, soft greens and the daily life of the farm and the lane, and he showed his pictures in London exhibitions. An Old Bridge at Hendon, Middlesex, of about 1828, in the Metropolitan Museum, shows a bridge over a stream with men and dogs, in a village then well outside London. His reputation has always stood behind that of Constable, and little is recorded of his training, patrons or life beyond his exhibitions.","q10285877":"Frei Carlos, whose title means Brother Charles, was a painter active in Portugal in the early sixteenth century. He is generally thought to have been of Flemish origin and to have lived as a friar, perhaps at the monastery of Espinheiro near Évora, and he is documented about 1517 and until about 1540. His paintings combine the precise detail and rich colour of Netherlandish art with Portuguese court taste in the age of King Manuel I, when Portugal's overseas trade made Lisbon and Évora wealthy. The Metropolitan Museum's Saint Vincent, Patron Saint of Lisbon, a tall panel about 163 by 53 centimetres, shows the saint with a ship and a book; the ship recalls the legend that his relics reached Lisbon by sea in 1173. Few details of his life are certain.","french_15th_century":"French 15th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of France in the 1400s. Three pictures hang under it: The Expectant Madonna with Saint Joseph, of about 1425 to 1450, in tempera on walnut wood; A Miracle of Saint Benedict, of about 1480; and a Portrait of an Ecclesiastic, of about 1480. The dates span half a century, and the pictures are probably by different hands. The Expectant Madonna shows the pregnant Virgin, a rare subject of private devotion. The fifteenth century was the age of Jean Fouquet, the Master of Moulins and Enguerrand Quarton, when French painting recovered after the Hundred Years' War. The catalogue gives no names, training or patrons for the unnamed painters.","french_16th_century":"French 16th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of France in the 1500s. Three portraits hang under it: Portrait of a Nobleman, of about 1570, a small panel; Prince Hercule-François, Duc d'Alençon, of 1572, a tall canvas; and Portrait of a Member of the Quaratesi Family, of 1561. Hercule-François was the youngest son of Henri II and Catherine de' Medici, and was about seventeen when painted; he later courted Queen Elizabeth I of England. French court portraiture of this period was dominated by the Clouet family and by Corneille de Lyon, whose small, exact, cool likenesses were the model for lesser painters. The Quaratesi were a Florentine family, and many Florentines lived in France. The painters are not named, and nothing is known of their training.","french_17th_century":"French 17th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of France in the 1600s. Three pictures hang under it: two portraits of men, one of the middle of the century and one of the later years, and Flowers in a Classical Vase, of the late 1600s. The age of Louis XIV, who reigned from 1643 to 1715, was a time of formal portraiture in the manner of Rigaud and Largillière, and of rich flower painting for the decoration of royal and noble houses, at which Jean-Baptiste Monnoyer was the leading master. Portraits of this kind were also made in great numbers by painters who are now forgotten. The catalogue records no names, dates of birth or death, or patrons, and the sitters are not identified.","french_18th_century":"French 18th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of France in the 1700s, and gathers eight pictures of different kinds: The Rape of the Sabine Women, an oil sketch of about 1770; Young Woman with a Muff, of about 1750; Old Woman with a Muff; a Portrait of a Woman of about 1711; a Portrait of a Young Man; a Portrait of a Young Boy of the 1790s; a tiny Young Woman and Man; and Divertissement. They span the Regency, the Rococo and the Revolution, and are clearly the work of many hands. The century produced Watteau, Boucher, Fragonard, Greuze and David, and for each famous name there were dozens of anonymous painters who supplied portraits, decorative scenes and copies. None of these painters can be named.","french_19th_century":"French 19th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for unnamed painters of France in the 1800s. Four pictures hang under it: Portrait of a Lady, of about 1810; Race Course at Longchamps, of about 1870; Roses in a Vase, a tiny panel of 1880 to 1900; and Melon and Lemon, of about 1900. The race course at Longchamp, in the Bois de Boulogne, opened in 1857 under Napoleon III and quickly became a fashionable scene, painted by Manet, Degas and Forain among others. The lady's portrait belongs to the years of the Empire, while the small still lifes belong to the age of the Impressionists and their followers. These painters are not named, and nothing is recorded of their training or patrons.","french_painter":"French Painter is the Metropolitan Museum's name for unnamed artists of France, and the label covers dozens of pictures of very different dates. The titles shown include portraits of men and monks, among them Monsieur de Bellefourière and a Member of the de Thou Family; Henri III, King of France, who reigned from 1574 to 1589; Louis XV; a Holy Family with Saint Anne of the early seventeenth century; and a Still Life with Strawberries. Several are very small panels, painted on wood. The works range from the Renaissance to the Romantic years of the nineteenth century and are by many hands. No names, training or patrons can be given, and nothing links these pictures but the museum's cataloguing.","q447963":"Friedrich August von Kaulbach was born in Munich in 1850 into a family of painters and studied at the Munich academy under Karl von Piloty. He became one of the most fashionable portraitists in Bavaria, painting elegant, soft, lightly handled pictures of women and children in a graceful, slightly sentimental manner that appealed to the nobility and the wealthy middle class. His wife, the Danish violinist Frida Scotta, whom he married in 1891, and their daughters were his favourite models. He belonged to the Munich Secession and was counted, with Lenbach, among the leading painters of the city. He died in 1920. Italian Garden, of 1894, a small landscape with cypresses, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q1415414":"Friedrich Johann Gottlieb Lieder was a German painter and portraitist, born in 1780 and dead in 1859. His likeness of Prince Klemens von Metternich, dated 1822, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a portrait on card in an oval about 22 by 17 centimetres, laid on a later support. Metternich was the Austrian foreign minister and, from 1821, chancellor, the architect of the settlement of the Congress of Vienna and of the conservative order that followed it, and he and the painter, who was seven years his junior, died in the same year, 1859. Little else is recorded here of Lieder's training, patrons or later reputation.","q445891":"Frits Thaulow was born in Christiania, now Oslo, in 1847 and studied in Karlsruhe, where he worked under the Norwegian landscape painter Hans Gude, and later in Paris. From the 1880s he lived chiefly in France, and became the painter of rivers: fast or still water in sun, rain and snow, with reflections, mills and old village houses, painted in a bold, fresh, atmospheric manner that won him medals and many buyers in France, Britain and America. His first wife, Ingeborg Gad, was a sister of Gauguin's wife Mette, which made the two painters brothers-in-law. Picquigny, painted in 1899, shows a village on the Somme, one of the French river towns where he worked. He died at Volendam, in Holland, in 1906.","q3432084":"Fritz Petzholdt was a Danish landscape painter, born in 1805 and dead in 1838, who died at thirty-three. He belonged to the Danish Golden Age, the flowering of Copenhagen painting in the decades after 1800 under C. W. Eckersberg and his pupils, who painted clear, bright, closely observed landscapes and sea views. The Metropolitan Museum's German Landscape with View toward a Broad Valley, of about 1829 to 1830, is an oil sketch on paper only about 13 by 25 centimetres, painted out of doors, with the quick, fresh notation that was a hallmark of Danish studies of the period. His early death cut short a promising career, and little else is recorded of his life.","q210201":"Fritz von Uhde was born in Saxony in 1848 and served as an officer of Saxon cavalry until 1877, when he left the army to study painting, in Munich and then in Paris, where he met the Hungarian painter Munkácsy, and in Holland, where he admired Frans Hals. He painted scenes of peasant and working-class life with an Impressionist handling of light, and he shocked many by placing Christ among poor Germans of his own day, as in Let the Little Children Come unto Me (1884) and The Holy Night (1888 to 1889). He was a founder member of the Munich Secession in 1892 and a professor at the Munich Academy. He died in 1911. Going Home, of about 1889, a wide picture of women and girls with baskets near a farm, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","fulchran_jean_harriet":"Fulchran-Jean Harriet was a French history painter, born in 1778 and dead in 1805, aged twenty-seven. He belonged to the generation that came to maturity in the last years of the French Revolution, when Neoclassical history painting flourished, painting stories from ancient history and myth in firm outline, with strong emotion and a taste for stern, solemn subjects. His Oedipus at Colonus, of 1798, in the Cleveland Museum of Art, takes its story from Sophocles: the blind Oedipus, an outcast, is led by his daughter Antigone to the sacred grove at Colonus near Athens, where he is to find rest at last. It is his best known picture, and was painted when he was about twenty. He died young, in the first years of the Napoleonic period, and little else is recorded.","q5539521":"George Fuller, born at Deerfield, Massachusetts, in 1822, is remembered for the dreamy, shadowy figure pictures of his last years, in which a girl or a young woman emerges from a golden haze, as in Nydia (1882), whose subject is the blind flower seller of Pompeii in Bulwer-Lytton's novel The Last Days of Pompeii. He worked slowly, laying thin glazes over an underpainting and rubbing them back, so that edges dissolve into a warm, smoky atmosphere that Boston critics often compared to Giorgione and to the French painter Millet. His Ideal Head of a Boy, of about 1867 to 1873, a portrait of his son George Spencer Fuller, is in the Metropolitan Museum, together with And She Was a Witch, and Cleveland owns Head of a Boy. Fuller died in Boston in 1884, as his reputation was at its height.","q122382":"Henry Fuseli was born Johann Heinrich Füssli at Zurich in 1741, the son of a painter, and was ordained a Zwinglian minister in 1761. A political scandal forced him to leave Zurich in 1763, and in London, where he settled in 1764, Joshua Reynolds urged him to become a painter. He spent 1770 to 1778 in Italy, where he fell under the spell of Michelangelo, and then made his name in London with The Nightmare, shown in 1782, a disturbing image of a sleeping woman and an incubus. Subjects from Shakespeare, Milton and Dante filled his later work, including the Night-Hag Visiting Lapland Witches, painted in 1796 for his Milton Gallery. He became professor of painting at the Royal Academy in 1799 and keeper in 1804, and he died in 1825. His taste for the terrible and the fantastic made him a forerunner of Romanticism.","q21498126":"O. G. is the National Gallery of Art's listing for an American painter known only by initials, with the dates 1819 to 1910. The Gallery's single picture, Retriever, an oil on canvas about 56 by 81 centimetres, is catalogued as of the second half of the nineteenth century, and shows a hunting dog of the type used to fetch game from water. Portraits of sporting dogs were a favourite subject of American painters of the period, folk painters and professionals alike, made for the owners of prized animals. The painter's full name, training, home and patrons are not recorded in what is known here, and nothing more can honestly be said about the man or woman behind the initials.","q22670109":"G. Baker is the Metropolitan Museum's name for a painter recorded as active about 1870 to 1900, known by one initial and a surname. The museum holds one picture, New York Harbor with Brooklyn Bridge, dated after 1869, an oil on canvas about 66 by 112 centimetres. The date points to the years when the East River bridge, designed by John A. Roebling, was planned and built: Roebling died in 1869 after an accident, construction began in 1870, and the bridge, then the longest suspension bridge in the world, opened in 1883. Harbour views with ships and the new bridge were popular souvenirs of the city. Nothing is recorded of Baker's first name, training, patrons or reputation.","q212499":"Jean-Léon Gérôme was born in Vesoul in 1824 and trained in Paris with Paul Delaroche and Charles Gleyre. He made his name at the Salon of 1847 with Young Greeks Attending a Cock Fight, which launched the Néo-Grec movement. After travelling to Egypt in 1856, and later to Turkey and the Near East, he painted meticulously detailed Orientalist scenes, such as the Met's Prayer in the Mosque and Cafe House, Cairo, as well as scenes of ancient Rome, including Pollice Verso of 1872. In 1863 he married the daughter of the art dealer Adolphe Goupil, whose firm made his pictures widely known through engravings. A professor at the École des Beaux-Arts, he taught many Americans. He was long unfashionable and is now reappraised. He died in 1904.","q271484":"Marguerite Gérard was born at Grasse in 1761, and in 1778, at the age of seventeen, went to live with her sister and brother-in-law, Jean-Honoré Fragonard, in Paris, who taught her to paint and engrave. She worked beside him at first, and then became an independent painter of small domestic scenes, ladies reading letters, musical evenings and mothers with children, in a polished manner inspired by Dutch masters such as Gerard ter Borch and Gabriel Metsu. She never married, and sold her pictures to wealthy collectors, among them members of Napoleon's family, exhibiting at the Salon from 1799. She died in Paris in 1837. The National Gallery of Art holds a pair of small canvases of 1788 to 1789, Deserved Sorrows and its companion piece, early works from the years she lived with the Fragonards.","q355213":"Gabriël Metsu was born at Leiden in 1629, the son of a Flemish painter, and was a founder member of the Leiden painters' guild in 1648, when only nineteen. He is thought to have trained there with Gerrit Dou, and about 1657 he moved to Amsterdam, where he painted the most varied pictures of any Dutch genre painter of his day: market scenes, doctors' visits, musical parties, ladies at letters and portraits of the rich, in warm colour with a lighter, freer touch than the Leiden fine painters. He died young, in Amsterdam, in 1667, aged thirty-eight. The Metropolitan Museum owns A Musical Party (1659), A Woman Seated at a Window (early 1660s) and The Visit to the Nursery (1661), in which a well-to-do family gathers around a baby's cradle.","q1491011":"Gaetano Gandolfi was born at San Matteo della Decima, near Bologna, in 1734, and with his elder brother Ubaldo studied at the Bolognese Accademia Clementina. A visit to Venice in 1760 opened his eyes to Tiepolo, Titian and Veronese, whose rich colour, light touch and grand compositions entered his work, and he became the leading Bolognese painter of his generation, painting altarpieces, frescoes in the city's palaces, and mythological and religious pictures, often small and lively sketches. His son Mauro was also a painter and etcher. He died at Bologna in 1802. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns The Holy Family, of 1776, and the Metropolitan Museum a Head of a Bishop and The Sacrifice of Iphigenia, of 1789.","q192720":"Thomas Gainsborough was born at Sudbury, in Suffolk, in 1727 and went to London as a boy, where he studied with the engraver Hubert Gravelot and at the Saint Martin's Lane academy. He worked at Sudbury, Ipswich and, from 1759, in fashionable Bath, becoming the equal of Reynolds as a portrait painter, with a light, feathery, flickering touch and a tender grace in his portraits of ladies and children, such as The Blue Boy. He loved landscape and painted it for his own pleasure. A founder member of the Royal Academy in 1768, he moved to London in 1774, quarrelled with the Academy and stopped exhibiting there in 1784. He died in 1788 and, on his deathbed, sent a friendly message to Reynolds. The National Gallery of Art holds many of his portraits and landscapes, among them Georgiana, Duchess of Devonshire (1783).","q2428749":"Fede Galizia was born at Milan in 1578, the daughter of Nunzio Galizia, a miniature painter from Trent who worked in Milan and who taught her. She was already known as a gifted child, praised in a book by the Milanese painter and writer Giovanni Paolo Lomazzo in 1590 when she was about twelve. She painted portraits and religious pictures, including a signed Judith with the Head of Holofernes of 1596, but she is best remembered as one of the first Italian painters of pure still life, making exact, calm arrangements of fruit in dishes of metal and porcelain on a plain ledge, with a stillness and clarity that are rare in her day. She died in Milan in 1630. The National Gallery of Art owns her Still Life with Apples, Pears, Cucumbers, Figs, Plums, and a Melon, of about 1625 to 1630.","q21498349":"The Gansevoort Limner is the name given to an unknown painter, since identified by some scholars with Pieter Vanderlyn, who painted the portraits of Dutch families of Albany and the Hudson Valley in the 1730s. The name comes from portraits of the Gansevoort family of Albany; a limner was the old word for a portrait painter. His pictures, stiff, flat and lightly coloured, with clear outlines, bright costumes and a decorative sense of pattern, were made for merchants and their families who wanted a likeness at modest cost, and were often based on English prints. The National Gallery of Art owns Miss Van Alen, of about 1735, and Young Lady with a Fan, of 1737. The catalogue dates of 1665 to 1790 are only a wide span.","q4906954":"Daniel Gardner was born at Kendal, in Westmorland, in 1750 and went to London as a boy of seventeen to train with his fellow northerner George Romney. He set up on his own and became a successful portraitist, working in oil, pastel and gouache, in a fresh, delicate, rather decorative style, often of women and children in the light, sentimental taste of the 1780s. He exhibited at the Royal Academy and at the Society of Artists. He died in 1805. The National Gallery of Art owns his portrait of the Hon. Mrs. Gray, of about 1785 to 1790. Further details of his patrons and later reputation are not given here, and he has always stood in the second rank of English portrait painters of his period.","q728512":"Garofalo, whose real name was Benvenuto Tisi, was born at Ferrara in 1481 and took his nickname from the carnation, garofano, which he used as his mark on his pictures. He studied in Cremona, in Ferrara and then in Rome about 1509 to 1512, where he saw Raphael's work and learned a clearer, more classical style, and for the rest of his life he worked in Ferrara for the Este dukes and for churches, making altarpieces and cabinet pictures in bright, enamel-like colour with elegant figures and landscape backgrounds. He worked beside Dosso Dossi, the greater court painter of Ferrara. He lost his sight in his last years and died in 1559. The Metropolitan Museum holds two panels of about 1530 showing Saint Nicholas of Tolentino at work miracles; the National Gallery of Art holds Christ Washing the Disciples' Feet.","q570172":"Gaspar de Crayer was born at Antwerp in 1584 and settled in Brussels, where he trained with Raphael Coxcie and became the leading painter of the city, court painter to the Spanish governors of the southern Netherlands. He painted altarpieces for churches across Flanders and Brabant and, in the 1620s and 1630s, portraits of Spanish rulers, including Philip IV. His style is graceful, warm and pious, close to Rubens and Van Dyck, though gentler, and he was a respected figure beside them. In 1664 he moved to Ghent, where he died in 1669. The Metropolitan Museum holds his Philip IV in Parade Armor, of about 1628, and The Meeting of Alexander the Great and Diogenes, a large canvas about 225 by 324 centimetres.","q741375":"Gaspard Dughet was born in Rome in 1615 to a French family and became the pupil and brother-in-law of Nicolas Poussin, who had married his sister in 1630. Known in England as Gaspard Poussin, he made his name as a landscape painter in the Roman Campagna, painting wooded hills, rocky glens, waterfalls, classical ruins and stormy skies, with a heroic breadth and a dramatic, serious mood. He worked in fresco for Roman churches and palaces, among them San Martino ai Monti, and in oil for Roman patrons. His landscapes were especially admired by English collectors on the Grand Tour and shaped the taste of English landscape gardeners and painters. He died in Rome in 1675. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns Rocky Landscape with Hunters, of about 1635, and the Metropolitan Museum an Imaginary Landscape.","q744250":"Gaspare Diziani was born at Belluno in 1689 and trained in Venice, where his teachers included Gregorio Lazzarini and Sebastiano Ricci, and he became one of the busiest painters of the Venetian Rococo, painting altarpieces, ceilings, mythological scenes and theatre sets in light, airy, warm colour with many small figures and clouds. He is recorded as working for the court at Dresden as well as in Venice. He died in Venice in 1767. The Metropolitan Museum owns Dawn, a large canvas of about 198 by 240 centimetres, which shows the break of day with putti, in the manner of Venetian decorative painting. Much of his output was for churches and palaces, and he has never been counted among the great names of the Venetian eighteenth century.","q1388163":"Gaspare Traversi was born at Naples about 1722 and trained there, probably under Francesco Solimena, before moving to Rome about 1752, where he worked until his death in 1770. He is remembered for his genre scenes of everyday life, painted with sharp, humorous observation: music lessons, quarrels, tricks played on sleepers, fidgeting children and gossips, in a clear, cool colour that was quite unlike the grand religious pictures that earned him his living. He also painted altarpieces. He was rediscovered in the twentieth century, when critics hailed him as one of the most original Italian painters of his century. The Metropolitan Museum owns Teasing a Sleeping Girl, of about 1760, and Saint Margaret of Cortona, of about 1758.","q37693":"Paul Gauguin was born in Paris in 1848 and spent part of his childhood in Peru. He worked as a sailor and then as a stockbroker, collecting Impressionist paintings and learning to paint from Camille Pissarro, and he gave up his career about 1883 to be a painter. He worked in Brittany at Pont-Aven, where with Émile Bernard he developed flat, bright, symbolic colour and bold outline, spent nine tense weeks in Arles in 1888 with Van Gogh, and in 1891 sailed to Tahiti, returning there in 1895 and moving in 1901 to the Marquesas, where he died in 1903. His Tahitian pictures made him a hero to later artists, though his colonial attitudes and relations with young Polynesian women are now criticised. The National Gallery of Art holds many of his works.","q5560919":"William Gilbert Gaul was born at Jersey City, New Jersey, in 1855 and studied art in New York. He became a well-known painter and illustrator of American history, especially military scenes: he drew for the Century magazine's Civil War series, painted subjects from the Spanish-American War of 1898, and travelled to the West in the 1890s to paint the Sioux and other Native peoples. He also painted the life of the postwar South, among them pictures of cotton pickers and farm workers. Picking Cotton, of about 1890, a small oil on academy board, is in the National Gallery of Art. He died in New York in 1919. His work is now valued as a record of the way Americans pictured their own recent past.","q22575805":"Gennarino Persico was an Italian painter of portrait miniatures, born about 1800 and dead about 1859, who apparently worked in the United States. The Metropolitan Museum owns two watercolours on ivory, each about 7 by 5 centimetres, portraits of Dr. Richard Wilmot Hall and Mrs. Richard Wilmot Hall, who was born Eliza Taylor, evidently painted as a pair. Italian miniaturists were prominent among the portraitists who crossed the Atlantic in the first half of the nineteenth century, finding patrons among the merchants and professional families of the American cities. Little else is recorded of Persico's training, travels or reputation, and nothing certain can be added to the dates and the two portraits.","q312393":"Gentile da Fabriano was born about 1370 at Fabriano, in the Marche, and became the most famous painter of the International Gothic style in Italy, with courtly elegance, rich gold, bright colour and delicate detail. He worked at Venice from 1408, at Brescia for the Malatesta lord Pandolfo, and in Florence, where in 1423 he painted the Adoration of the Magi for Palla Strozzi, now in the Uffizi, and in 1425 the Quaratesi polyptych, from which the National Gallery of Art's panel showing the Crippled and Sick Cured at the Tomb of Saint Nicholas comes. He died in 1427 in Rome while painting frescoes at San Giovanni in Laterano, which Pisanello completed. His art influenced the Florentine and Venetian painters of the next generation. The Gallery also owns a Madonna and Child Enthroned, of about 1420.","q472394":"Georg Flegel was born at Olmütz, in Moravia, in 1566 and settled in Frankfurt am Main, where he became a citizen in 1597 and spent the rest of his life. He is thought to have trained with the Flemish painter Lucas van Valckenborch and became one of the first German painters to specialise in still life. His pictures, small and exact, show breakfast tables with bread, cheese, wine, nuts and sweets, silver cups, flowers and insects, arranged on a ledge in a clear light, and are among the earliest German examples of this kind. His patrons were wealthy Frankfurt merchants and collectors. He died in Frankfurt in 1638. The Metropolitan Museum owns a Still Life of about 1625 to 1630 with bread, lemons, a pitcher and a bird.","q20007054":"George Augustus Baker Jr. was born in 1821, the son of the miniature painter George Augustus Baker, and learned from him, becoming a portraitist in oil and in miniature and exhibiting at the National Academy of Design in New York. He was known for soft, delicate portraits of women. The Metropolitan Museum owns a miniature of Mary Crosby, a watercolour on ivory of 1840, an oil portrait of Mrs. William Loring Andrews of 1861 to 1863 and a portrait of the landscape painter John F. Kensett, dated 1875, which was painted three years after the sitter's death in 1872. Baker died in 1880. Few other details of his training or patrons are given here, and he is little known today.","q22575801":"George Augustus Baker Sr. was born at Strasbourg in 1760 and died in New York in 1847. He was a painter of miniatures who made his career in the United States, painting portraits on ivory for New Yorkers in the early republic, in the years when the miniature was the usual way of making a small likeness. His son, George Augustus Baker Jr., also became a portrait painter. The Metropolitan Museum owns one example, Stephen Thorn, a watercolour on ivory of 1818 about 7 by 5 centimetres. Miniatures of that date were made for lockets, cases and family keepsakes, and the sitters were the merchants and professional men of the city. Little is recorded of Baker's training in Europe, or of the date and circumstances of his coming to America.","george_b_butler":"George B. Butler, born in 1838 and dead in 1907, was an American painter whose Capri Lace Maker, of 1884, is the Cleveland Museum of Art's picture by him. It is a tall canvas about 160 by 85 centimetres showing a woman at her lace work on the island of Capri, in the Bay of Naples, which attracted painters in the later nineteenth century with its light, its blue grotto and its picturesque inhabitants, and pictures of Italian peasants and craftswomen found a ready market at home. Butler's training, travels and patrons are not recorded in what is known here, and he is now remembered mainly through this one canvas.","q3089402":"George Chinnery was born in London in 1774, a grandson of the painter William Pether, and exhibited miniatures and portraits at the Royal Academy. He went to India in 1802, living at Madras and Calcutta, and in 1825 left Calcutta for Macao, on the China coast, partly to escape his creditors, and remained there until his death in 1852, never returning to England. In Macao and Canton he painted the European and Chinese merchants, harbour scenes, street life and landscape in oils and, above all, in swift pencil and ink sketches that are admired for their liveliness. He taught Chinese pupils, among them, it is said, the portraitist Lam Qua. His self-portrait of 1825 to 1828, a small oil on canvas about 22 by 18 centimetres, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q5538011":"George Cochran Lambdin was born in Pittsburgh in 1830, the son of the portrait painter James Reid Lambdin, and grew up in Philadelphia. After painting figures and sentimental scenes in the 1850s and 1860s, he turned in the 1870s to flower painting and became famous for his roses, shown in lush, loosely arranged bouquets in vases or on a table. He is said to have grown the blooms he painted in his own garden at Germantown, Pennsylvania. A Vase of Flowers, dated 1875, comes from the start of this phase, and his rose pictures made his name with the public. His earlier work as a painter of figures and landscape is far less known. He died in 1896.","q358195":"George Engleheart was born at Kew in 1750 and became one of the leading English miniature painters of his day. He was trained in the circle of Sir Joshua Reynolds, set up as a miniaturist in London about 1775, painting delicate portraits on ivory in clear, fresh colour, and about 1790 was appointed miniature painter to George III. He worked quickly and kept a fee book that records close to five thousand miniatures painted between 1775 and 1813, an exceptional record of the trade. He died in 1829. The Metropolitan Museum owns four of his ivory miniatures, among them Mrs. Peter De Lancey, of 1783, an oval only about 34 by 28 millimetres, and Colonel Woodford, probably of 1788, a little larger.","q183245":"George Frederic Watts was born in London in 1817, the son of a piano maker, and studied sculpture and then painting at the Royal Academy Schools. He won a prize in the Westminster Hall competition of 1843 and spent four years in Italy, in Florence, where he studied Titian and Michelangelo. Back in London he painted portraits of eminent Victorians, which he gave to the nation as the Hall of Fame, and large symbolic pictures of Hope, Love and Death, in glowing, smoky colour, which made him the most admired idealist painter in Britain. He refused a baronetcy and accepted the Order of Merit in 1902. He married the actress Ellen Terry in 1864, and later Mary Fraser Tytler. He died in 1904. His Ariadne, of 1894, is in the Metropolitan Museum.","q20203237":"George Freeman was a painter of portrait miniatures, born in 1789 and dead in 1868, whose nationality is not given in the museum's record. The Metropolitan Museum owns his Portrait of a Lady, of about 1825, a watercolour on ivory about 10 by 7 centimetres, large for a miniature. The date falls in the great age of the ivory miniature, between the Napoleonic wars and the arrival of photography, when a portrait of this sort cost far less than a painting in oil and was prized as a family keepsake. The sitter is not named. Little is recorded here of Freeman's training, teachers, patrons or reputation, and his name is one of many that survive only on a few small ivories.","q21481733":"George Harrison Hite was an American miniature painter, born in 1807 and dead in 1880, who worked in New York. The Metropolitan Museum owns two of his watercolours on ivory: Henry L. Taylor, of 1833, about 7 by 5 centimetres, and the larger Daniel Webster, of 1844, about 11 by 9 centimetres. Webster, the Massachusetts statesman and orator, had left the post of secretary of state in 1843 and returned to the Senate in 1845, and was among the most painted Americans of his day, so that a likeness of him was a sound commission for any portraitist. Hite worked at a time when the miniature was the standard small portrait for the American middle class, until photography replaced it. Little else is recorded of his training or career.","q21152710":"George Harvey was an English-born American painter, born about 1800 and dead in 1878, who began as a miniature painter and became known for landscape. He emigrated to the United States in the 1820s and painted miniatures, as shown by the four watercolours on ivory in the Metropolitan Museum, among them Joseph Stevens Buckminster Thacher, of about 1829, and a Self-Portrait of 1830. About 1835 he turned to a new kind of landscape. His series called Atmospheric Landscapes, small pictures of American scenery in changing weather, seasons and light, were reproduced as colour prints and made him known. He lived for years at Hastings-on-Hudson and died in 1878. His interest in mood and atmosphere anticipated a later American taste.","q2733223":"George Henry Harlow was born in London in 1787 and studied with Henry de Cort, then Samuel Drummond, and briefly with Thomas Lawrence, whose manner he followed, before quarrelling with him. He painted lively portraits and theatrical subjects, among them a large picture of the Trial of Queen Katharine, from Shakespeare's Henry VIII, in which the Kemble family and other actors appear in character. In 1818 he went to Italy, where he was well received in Rome. He died in London in 1819, aged thirty-one, soon after his return. The Metropolitan Museum owns his Self-Portrait, an oil on canvas about 76 by 64 centimetres. His early death ended a promising career, and he is remembered today mainly for the Kemble picture.","q5540480":"George Henry Smillie was born in New York in 1840, the son of the engraver James Smillie, and brother of the painter James David Smillie. He studied with the landscape painter James M. Hart, and went on to become a member of the National Academy of Design and the American Watercolor Society. He painted in the tradition of the Hudson River School, with clear light and detailed foreground, and travelled widely in the United States, while his later work became softer and more atmospheric. East Hampton Meadows, of 1883, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a small oil of the marshes and fields of eastern Long Island, where in the 1880s artists were beginning to gather. He died in 1920.","q19975476":"George Henry Yewell was born at Havre de Grace, Maryland, in 1830 and became a painter who spent many years in Europe, studying in Paris and Rome and living in Italy, especially Venice. He painted genre scenes and, for American tourists, views of Italian interiors, from the Doge's Palace to the churches, in careful detail with strong light, and also portraits. He later settled in New York and died in 1923 at a great age. The Metropolitan Museum owns Pulpit in Saint Mark's, Venice, of about 1870, a tall small canvas of the basilica's interior, a favourite subject of travellers. Details of his teachers and patrons are not given here, and he is little known today.","q5540573":"George Hewitt Cushman was an American painter of portrait miniatures, born in 1814 and dead in 1876, who painted on ivory in watercolour. The Metropolitan Museum owns four of his miniatures: a Self Portrait of 1839 only about three centimetres high; Alice Cushman, of 1856; Ida Cushman, of about 1856; and Rebecca Wetherill, of about 1849, a large piece on ivory about 13 centimetres square. The two Cushman girls share his surname, which suggests that they were members of his family, perhaps his daughters. His career spanned the last great years of the American miniature, before photography took over the market in the 1850s and 1860s. His training, home and patrons are not recorded here.","q3720237":"George Hitchcock was born at Providence, Rhode Island, in 1850 and studied law at Harvard before turning to painting. He worked in Paris and then settled in Holland, at Egmond aan Zee, in the 1880s, where with the American painter Gari Melchers he formed a colony of painters of the Dutch coast. Hitchcock painted devout, soft scenes of Dutch women and girls in their costume, with strong, high-keyed colour, and the bright tulip fields in spring, in works that won him medals at the Paris Salon. He died at Marken, in Holland, in 1913. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns The Blessed Mother, of 1892, and the Metropolitan Museum Vespers, of about 1895 to 1900, a picture of women near a windmill.","q704868":"George Inness was born at Newburgh, New York, in 1825 and grew up in Newark, New Jersey, learning his art as an engraver's apprentice and from a few lessons. His early landscapes of the 1850s, like The Lackawanna Valley, painted about 1856 for a railroad company, show the sharp detail of the Hudson River School, and the new railway as part of the American scene. Visits to Italy and France in the 1850s and 1870s taught him the more suggestive manner of the Barbizon painters, and his deepening religious interests, influenced by the mystical writings of Swedenborg, led him to paint softer, glowing, near-abstract landscapes in which light and mood count more than detail, as in Sunset in the Woods and Harvest Moon, both of 1891. He lived in Montclair, New Jersey, from 1885 and died in Scotland in 1894, while on a trip.","q16065986":"George Lethbridge Saunders was a British miniature painter, born in 1807 and dead in 1863, who worked for some years in the United States. The Metropolitan Museum owns four of his watercolours on ivory, among them Mrs. George M. Gill, of 1841, Mrs. Israel Thorndike, of 1843, and Jubal Anderson Early, of 1847 to 1848, a Virginia lawyer and Mexican War officer who became a Confederate general in the Civil War. Painted in a smooth, rather formal manner, the miniatures show a trade at its last stage, as the photograph, introduced in 1839 and cheaper than the miniature, gradually took over its market in the 1840s and 1850s. Details of his training, patrons and later reputation are not given here.","q18511860":"George Linen, born in 1802 and dead in 1888, was a painter whose William Popham, of about 1845, a small oil on canvas about 30 by 25 centimetres, is in the Metropolitan Museum. The museum's record gives no nationality. Such small, bust-length oil portraits were made in quantity in the middle of the nineteenth century for merchants and professional men, in the years when they could still afford the cost of a hand-painted likeness, before the daguerreotype and the cheap photograph undercut the trade. Nothing certain can be said here about Linen's training, patrons or reputation beyond his dates and this one portrait.","q3544381":"George Loring Brown was born at Boston in 1814 and was apprenticed as a wood engraver, then taught himself to paint, and in 1832 went to Europe, where he copied Claude Lorrain in the Louvre so closely, and with such success, that he was nicknamed Claude Brown. He lived in Florence and Rome from 1840 to 1859, painting Italian views and Claude-like landscapes with golden light, which sold to American and English tourists. Back in the United States he settled in Boston, and painted the White Mountains and New England as well as Italy. He died at Malden, Massachusetts, in 1889. The Metropolitan Museum owns View at Amalfi, Bay of Salerno, of 1857, a view of the coast south of Naples with boats in the foreground.","q2405427":"George Morland was born in London in 1763, the son of the painter Henry Robert Morland, who trained him hard and exhibited his drawings at the Royal Academy when he was ten. He became the most popular painter of English rustic life in the 1780s and 1790s: farm horses, pigs and cattle, stable yards, smugglers, village inns and cottage children, in warm brown tones and loose brushwork that was widely engraved, so that many households owned a Morland print. He painted fast, and was a drinker and spendthrift who dodged his creditors for years. He died in London in 1804 at forty-one. The Metropolitan Museum owns The Bell Inn, of the late 1780s, an inn yard with men, women, dogs and pigs.","q64633":"George Peter Alexander Healy was born in Boston in 1813 and was self-taught until he went to Paris in 1834, where he studied with Baron Gros and soon began painting for the French court; in 1838 he was commissioned to paint King Louis-Philippe. For the rest of his long life he moved between Paris, Rome, Chicago and Washington, painting portraits of American presidents, European statesmen and many famous people of the day. He painted Abraham Lincoln from life in 1860, and in 1868 produced The Peacemakers, showing Lincoln with Grant and Sherman. His portraits are straightforward, solid and informative rather than flattering, and made him the most widely travelled portraitist of his generation. He died in Chicago in 1894. The Washington pictures include the Lincoln of 1860, a Jefferson, and a portrait of Roxana Atwater Wentworth.","george_peter_alexander_healy":"George Peter Alexander Healy was an American portrait painter, born at Boston in 1813, who became one of the great portraitists of the Victorian age in America and Europe. He painted American presidents and King Louis-Philippe, and made the huge Webster Replying to Hayne, of 1851, a group of about 130 portraits in Faneuil Hall, Boston. His best known picture is The Peacemakers, of 1868, showing Lincoln, Grant, Sherman and Admiral Porter in conference aboard the River Queen in March 1865, now in the White House. He had a studio in Chicago from 1855 and also lived in Paris and Rome, and died in Chicago in 1894. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his Self-Portrait of 1852.","q371280":"George Romney was born at Dalton-in-Furness, in Lancashire, in 1734 and was apprenticed to a travelling portrait painter, Christopher Steele, at Kendal, then settled there. In 1762 he left his wife and children and went to London, and in 1773 to 1775 he studied in Italy. He became one of the three great portraitists of his day, with Reynolds and Gainsborough, with a style of broad, simple, graceful form and soft colour, and he refused to exhibit at the Royal Academy. His muse was Emma Hart, later Lady Hamilton, whom he painted many times from 1782 as a Bacchante, a Circe and a saint. Failing health took him back to Kendal in 1799, where he died in 1802. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his Portrait of Jane Hoskyns, and the Metropolitan Museum a portrait of Lady Elizabeth Stanley, Countess of Derby.","q381728":"George Stubbs was born at Liverpool in 1724, the son of a currier, and taught himself to paint. He studied anatomy at York, where he drew dissected bodies for a surgeon, and in 1756 and 1757 he spent eighteen months at a farmhouse at Horkstow, in Lincolnshire, dissecting horses, the work he published as The Anatomy of the Horse in 1766. His knowledge made him the greatest painter of horses in Britain: racehorses, hunters, and mares and foals, with grooms, jockeys and landscapes, for patrons such as the Duke of Richmond and the Marquess of Rockingham, for whom he painted Whistlejacket. He also painted wild animals, and experimented with enamel. He died in London in 1806. The Metropolitan Museum holds The Third Duke of Dorset's Hunter with a Groom and a Dog, of 1768.","q22688493":"George W. Stevens, born about 1820 and dead in 1891, was an American portrait painter, of whom the Metropolitan Museum owns one example, Portrait of a Boy, dated 1845, a watercolour on ivory about 6 by 5 centimetres. In the 1840s portrait miniatures were still a common way for middle-class families to record their children, though the daguerreotype had arrived in 1839 and was soon to replace the miniature as the cheaper choice. The boy's name is not known. Little else is recorded of Stevens's training, travels, patrons or later reputation, and the dates given for him are approximate, so that nothing more can be said with confidence about the course of his career.","q22341565":"George W. Twibill Jr. was an American portrait painter, born in 1806 and dead in 1836, who died at about thirty. The Metropolitan Museum owns his Portrait of a Lady and Child, of 1833, an oil on canvas about 90 by 72 centimetres, a mother and a young child under trees. The picture shows the formal, polished, softly lit style of the leading American portraitists of the 1830s, such as Thomas Sully, whose manner many younger painters followed. Twibill's career lasted barely a decade, and the museum's record gives few details of his training, patrons or later reputation, so that his early death left only a small body of work.","q1398223":"Georges Clairin was born in Paris in 1843 and studied at the École des Beaux-Arts under Isidore Pils. A friend and travelling companion of the painter Henri Regnault, he went with him to Spain and Morocco in 1869 and 1870, and the glittering light and colour of the Maghreb marked his work for life. He painted Orientalist scenes, society portraits and decorations for theatres, and became the close friend and portraitist of the actress Sarah Bernhardt, whose portrait of 1876 is his best known picture. He died in Paris in 1919. The Opium Smokers, of 1872, a canvas about 108 by 100 centimetres in the Metropolitan Museum, is an interior scene of men smoking opium, in the exotic taste of the day.","q203371":"Georges de La Tour was born in 1593 at Vic-sur-Seille in Lorraine and spent most of his life in nearby Lunéville, where he married a woman of the local nobility and became a prosperous painter. He is now famous for night scenes lit by a single candle, in which simplified, almost sculptural figures glow with a warm red light, but he also painted daylight pictures of musicians, card sharps and fortune tellers. He worked for the Duke of Lorraine and, in 1639, was named painter to Louis XIII. The Repentant Magdalen, about 1635 to 1640, is one of several versions of a subject he returned to. He died in Lunéville in 1652. Largely forgotten for nearly three centuries, he was rediscovered in the early twentieth century and is now ranked among the great French painters.","q2028017":"Georges Michel was born in Paris in 1763 and died there in 1843. He worked as a restorer and a dealer, repairing and copying old pictures for the Louvre and for collectors, and studied the Dutch landscape masters, Rembrandt, Ruisdael and Hobbema, whose broad skies and stormy light he took as models. Drawing and painting in the Paris suburbs, on the plain of Saint-Denis and the hill of Montmartre, with its windmills, he made dark, dramatic landscapes of lonely roads, mills and storm clouds, almost without figures. He was little known in his lifetime and sold his pictures cheaply, but he was rediscovered after his death and admired by later landscape painters as a forerunner of the Barbizon school. The Metropolitan Museum owns The Mill of Montmartre, of about 1820.","q34013":"Georges Seurat was born in Paris in 1859 and studied at the École des Beaux-Arts under a pupil of Ingres, Henri Lehmann, before leaving in 1879 to teach himself, reading the colour theories of Chevreul and others. He developed a system of painting in tiny dots of pure colour that the eye blends at a distance, which his friends called Neo-Impressionism, the greatest result being A Sunday on La Grande Jatte, shown in 1886 and now in Chicago. He also made masterly black chalk drawings and small oil sketches, many in the Metropolitan Museum, such as the Study for La Grande Jatte (1884) and Circus Sideshow (1887 to 1888). He died suddenly in Paris in 1891, aged thirty-one, probably of an infection. His method was taken up by Signac, Pissarro and others, and he is now a central figure of modern art.","q333380":"Gerard David was born at Oudewater, in Holland, about 1460 and settled in Bruges, where he joined the painters' guild in 1484 and, after Hans Memling's death in 1494, became the leading painter of the city. He was dean of the guild in 1501, and in 1498 he painted for the city hall two panels on the story of the unjust judge Cambyses, a warning to magistrates. He and his wife, Cornelia Cnoop, herself a miniaturist, joined the guild in Antwerp in 1515. His altarpieces and Madonnas, serene and gentle, with soft, rich colour, careful landscapes and a grave, still mood, were widely copied, and workshops in Bruges and Antwerp went on making versions for decades. He died at Bruges in 1523. The Metropolitan Museum has a large group of his pictures, among them The Annunciation, of 1506.","q533958":"Gerard de Lairesse was born at Liège in 1641, the son of a painter, and studied there before settling in Amsterdam in 1665, where he became the leading painter of classical subjects, with smooth, elegant figures in the style of Poussin, for patrons who decorated their houses with mythological ceilings and wall pictures. Rembrandt painted his portrait about 1665, showing a young man with a face marked by illness, which is now in the Metropolitan Museum. He drew the plates for Govert Bidloo's anatomy book of 1685. About 1690 he went blind and spent his last years lecturing and writing: his Principles of Drawing (1701) and Great Painting Book (1707) were used for generations. He died in Amsterdam in 1711. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns Hermes Ordering Calypso to Release Odysseus, of about 1670; the Metropolitan Museum owns Apollo and Aurora (1671).","gerard_ter_borch":"Gerard ter Borch was born at Zwolle in 1617 and learned drawing and painting from his father, Gerard ter Borch the Elder, a painter and customs official who had lived in Italy. He travelled in his youth, in England and probably Italy and Spain, and in 1646 to 1648 was in Münster, where he painted the delegates at the peace negotiations that ended the Thirty Years' War, and made the small Swearing of the Oath of Ratification of the Treaty of Münster (1648). By 1654 he had settled at Deventer, where he married, became a town councillor and painted until his death in 1681. He painted portraits and quiet scenes of polite life, with astonishing renderings of satin gowns. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns his Portrait of a Woman, of about 1665.","q184212":"Théodore Géricault was born at Rouen in 1791 and studied in Paris with the horse painter Carle Vernet and the classical painter Guérin, and copied old masters in the Louvre. He made his name at the Salon of 1812 with a young cavalry officer charging, and in 1814 exhibited the Wounded Cuirassier. In Italy in 1816 to 1817 he studied Michelangelo, and on his return he painted The Raft of the Medusa (1818 to 1819), a monumental picture of the survivors of a wrecked French frigate, shown at the Salon of 1819, which became a rallying point of Romantic painting. He also painted horses, and in his last years made portraits of the insane. A fall from a horse in 1823 led to his early death in 1824, aged thirty-two. The National Gallery of Art owns Mounted Trumpeters of Napoleon's Imperial Guard and Nude Warrior with a Spear.","german_16th_century":"German 16th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for an unnamed painter of Germany in the 1500s, represented by one picture, Portrait of a Lady, dated 1532, a tempera on panel about 44 by 32 centimetres. The date is precise even though the painter is not. German portraiture of those years was at a high point, with Holbein, Cranach, Dürer's followers and Barthel Beham painting bust-length portraits of merchants, scholars and their wives, strongly lit and boldly outlined, often against a plain coloured background and sometimes with an inscription giving the sitter's age and the year. The lady is not identified, and nothing is recorded of the painter's name, training or patrons.","german_19th_century":"German 19th Century is the National Gallery of Art's label for an unnamed painter of Germany in the 1800s, represented by one picture, An Artist and His Family, of about 1830, an oil on canvas about 75 by 64 centimetres. The date falls in the Biedermeier years, when German painters turned from the grand subjects of the Neoclassical and Romantic generations to the quiet life of the middle-class home, painted with sharp detail and calm affection. Group portraits of painters with their wives and children, in their studios, were a type that Biedermeier painters liked, setting the artist's work within the family. Neither the painter nor the sitters are named in the catalogue, and nothing is recorded of the painter's training or patrons.","german_painter":"German Painter is the Metropolitan Museum's name for unnamed painters of Germany, and the label covers sixteen pictures from several centuries, among them a Portrait of an Italian Woman painted on a linden panel, several tiny octagonal portraits on ivory of about 29 by 19 millimetres, Caroline Maria Therese of Parma (1770 to 1804), and a portrait said to be Alexander von Humboldt (1769 to 1859). The panel and the ivories plainly belong to different periods and hands: the first suggests the German portrait tradition of the early sixteenth century, the others the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, when ivory miniatures were common. No names, dates, training or patrons can be given.","gerrit_van_honthorst":"Gerrit van Honthorst was born at Utrecht about 1590 and trained with Abraham Bloemaert before going to Rome about 1610 to 1612, where he lived for some ten years, protected by the marquis Vincenzo Giustiniani and Cardinal Scipione Borghese. There he took up Caravaggio's manner, with torchlight and candlelight and strong shadows, and became so well known for night scenes that Italians called him Gherardo delle Notti, Gerard of the Nights. Back in Utrecht in 1620 he ran a large workshop. In 1628 he went to London to paint for Charles I, and he later painted portraits for the court at The Hague, where the children of Elizabeth of Bohemia were his pupils, and for the king of Denmark. He died at Utrecht in 1656. The Cleveland Museum of Art owns Samson and Delilah, of about 1616.","q956102":"Henri Gervex was born in Paris in 1852 and studied with Cabanel, Brisset and Fromentin. He first drew attention in 1876 with Autopsy at the Hôtel-Dieu, a realist picture of a dissection that he painted at twenty-four, and in 1878 his Rolla, a nude from a poem by Musset, was excluded from the Salon as indecent, and made him famous after being shown instead in a dealer's gallery. He painted portraits, modern-life subjects, scenes in hospitals and the theatre, and large decorations for public buildings, a friend of the Impressionists but no radical. He died in Paris in 1929. The National Gallery of Art owns his oil Study for Autopsy at the Hôtel-Dieu, of 1876.","q1440305":"Giuseppe Ghislandi was born at Bergamo in 1655 and trained in Venice with Sebastiano Bombelli, a leading portraitist, returning to Bergamo in 1701. He lived and worked in the Paolotti convent of Galgario, which is why he is called Fra Galgario, though he was a lay friar who painted for a living. He was the leading portrait painter of north Italy in the early eighteenth century, with vivid, realistic likenesses of noblemen, soldiers, artists and country gentlemen, in rich, bold colour and rapid, free brushwork, with a frankness that avoided the flattery of the court portraits of the day. He died at Bergamo in 1743. The National Gallery of Art owns his Portrait of a Young Man, painted after 1720.","q22094536":"Giacomo Andreoli was an Italian painter of portrait miniatures, of whom the Metropolitan Museum records no dates. His one picture there is a miniature on ivory, about 60 by 48 millimetres, of Jérôme Bonaparte, the youngest brother of Napoleon, who was made King of Westphalia in 1807 and reigned at Kassel until 1813. Jérôme's life was eventful: in 1803 he married Elizabeth Patterson of Baltimore, and Napoleon had the marriage annulled; he took a German princess as his second wife; after 1815 he lived in exile in Italy, and he died in 1860, having become president of the French Senate under Napoleon III. The sitter's career suggests the miniature was painted in the years of his kingship or his Italian exile. Nothing is recorded of Andreoli's life.","q524248":"Giacomo Ceruti was born at Milan in 1698 and worked chiefly at Brescia, Venice, Padua and Milan. He is known for his large, honest paintings of the poor, beggars, porters, spinners and travelling peddlers, painted without mockery or sentiment and with dignity, which won him the nickname Pitocchetto, the little beggar painter. He also painted portraits of the Brescian nobility and of other sitters, in a clear, direct style. He died at Milan in 1767. He was forgotten after his death, and rediscovered in the twentieth century by the critic Roberto Longhi, who put him among the great painters of reality in Lombardy. The Metropolitan Museum owns A Woman with a Dog and An Old Man with a Dog, both of the 1740s.","q1361095":"Michele Giambono was a Venetian painter and mosaicist, documented from the 1420s until about 1462, and one of the leading painters of Venice in the middle of the fifteenth century. His art grew from the International Gothic style of Gentile da Fabriano, who had worked in Venice, and he painted altarpieces and panels of saints with elegant, flowing, decorative lines and bright, rich colour, as well as mosaics in Saint Mark's, among them in the Mascoli Chapel. The National Gallery of Art owns his tall panel Saint Peter, of about 1445 to 1450, a standing figure, probably once part of a polyptych. The catalogue dates of 1382 to 1480 are only broad limits for a life of which few facts are certain.","q940454":"Giampietrino, whose full name is given as Giovanni Pietro Rizzoli, was a painter active in Milan from the 1490s to about the middle of the sixteenth century, and one of the most faithful followers of Leonardo da Vinci in the city. He painted Madonnas, penitent Magdalens and mythological and classical nudes in Leonardo's soft, smoky shading and rounded, sweet-faced style, and made copies after Leonardo's works, among them a Last Supper now in the Royal Academy in London. The Metropolitan Museum owns Diana the Huntress, a tall panel of about 114 by 59 centimetres in oil on wood, showing the goddess as a standing nude with bow and deer. No firm dates of birth and death are recorded, and he is usually named beside Leonardo's other Milanese followers.","q3105284":"Giannicola di Paolo, born about 1460 and dead in 1544, was a painter of Perugia and a follower of Perugino, whose sweet figures, soft colour and clear, orderly compositions he continued, with some influence from Raphael, who worked in Perugia in his youth. His best known work is the fresco decoration of the chapel of Saint John the Baptist in the Collegio del Cambio at Perugia, made in the 1510s and 1520s, and he painted altarpieces for churches in the city and its region. The National Gallery of Art owns his small Annunciation, of 1510 to 1515, an oil on panel about 40 by 36 centimetres. Perugino's followers were many, and Giannicola ranks among the best of them, though he is far less famous than his master.","q615161":"Sanford Robinson Gifford was born at Greenfield, New York, in 1823 and grew up at Hudson, New York. He studied painting in New York, became a member of the National Academy of Design, and was a leading painter of the second generation of the Hudson River School, known for hazy, golden, luminous skies and soft light over lakes and mountains. He travelled to Europe in 1855 to 1857 and again in 1868 to 1869, to Italy, Egypt and the Near East, served with the Seventh Regiment during the Civil War, and in 1870 joined a scientific expedition to the Rocky Mountains. He died in New York in 1880, and the Metropolitan Museum held a memorial exhibition of his work soon afterwards. The National Gallery of Art owns Siout, Egypt (1874) and Ruins of the Parthenon (1880).","gijsbrechts_cornelis_norbertus":"Cornelis Norbertus Gijsbrechts, a Flemish painter whose life is only partly known, was born at Antwerp about 1630, and was recorded as a master in the Antwerp guild in 1659. He became a specialist in trompe l'oeil, pictures that deceive the eye, painting letter racks, cupboard doors, hanging pouches and even the back of a canvas in minute detail, so that they seemed real objects. He worked in German cities and, from 1668, at the court of King Frederick III of Denmark in Copenhagen, where he painted for the royal collection. He is not recorded after the 1670s. The National Gallery of Art owns A Hanging Wall Pouch, of about 1677, an oil on panel with mirror glass, painted to look like a leather pouch hanging on the wall.","q41402":"Gilbert Stuart was born at North Kingstown, Rhode Island, in 1755 and learned painting from the Scottish painter Cosmo Alexander, who took him to Edinburgh. After Alexander's death he returned penniless to America and in 1775 went to London, where he became Benjamin West's assistant and then a successful portrait painter, rivalling Reynolds and Gainsborough. Debts drove him to Dublin in 1787, where he painted Baron FitzGibbon, and in 1793 back to America, where he painted George Washington from life three times in 1795 and 1796: the Vaughan, Lansdowne and Athenaeum portraits. The unfinished Athenaeum portrait became the face on the dollar bill. He made dozens of replicas and painted the leading Americans of his day. He lived in Boston from 1805 and died there in 1828. The Cleveland Museum of Art and the Metropolitan Museum hold many of his portraits.","gillis_van_coninxloo":"Gillis van Coninxloo was born in Antwerp in 1544 and entered the city's guild of Saint Luke as a master in 1570. He came from a family of painters and is remembered above all for landscape. Like many painters of the southern Netherlands he left Antwerp after its capture by Spanish forces in 1585, working for some years at Frankenthal in the Palatinate before settling in Amsterdam in the mid-1590s, where he died in 1607. His deep, shadowy forests, with a clear passage from a dark foreground to a bright distance and small figures from myth or the Bible set among the trees, replaced the older high-horizon panoramas. Painters such as David Vinckboons and Roelant Savery followed him, and he is counted among the founders of the Dutch forest landscape.","q187132":"Gillis van Tilborgh was a Flemish painter who worked in Brussels in the middle of the seventeenth century; his life is usually given as about 1625 to 1678, though the museum records no dates. He is best known for small genre scenes of peasant festivities and for conversation pieces, groups of well-to-do sitters shown at table, in a garden or at a celebration, a type made fashionable in the Spanish Netherlands by Gonzales Coques. The wedding celebration in New York belongs to that family of pictures: many figures, small in scale, painted with clear colour and close attention to costume. Little is recorded of his training or patrons, and he remains a minor master, valued mainly as a lively recorder of the manners of Brussels society.","q332494":"Luca Giordano was born in Naples in 1634 and trained there, reportedly under Jusepe de Ribera, and then in Rome, Venice and Florence, absorbing Pietro da Cortona's Baroque and the colour of Veronese. His great speed earned him the nickname Fa Presto, meaning Work Fast, and he produced thousands of pictures and enormous ceiling frescoes, among them the ceiling of the Palazzo Medici Riccardi in Florence in 1682 to 1683. From 1692 to 1702 he worked in Spain as court painter to Charles II, decorating the Escorial and the Buen Retiro in Madrid. He returned to Naples and died there in 1705. The Annunciation of 1672 and The Flight into Egypt of 1701 show how his style stayed supple and warm over thirty years.","q8459":"Giorgione, born at Castelfranco Veneto about 1477, died in Venice in 1510, apparently of plague, so that his whole career lasted perhaps fifteen years. Vasari says he learned from Giovanni Bellini, and his own pictures show it, but he turned Bellini's lucid manner toward soft, atmospheric colour and a poetic, often puzzling subject matter, painted for private collectors rather than churches. The Tempest in Venice is the best known; only a handful of works, among them the portrait called Laura of 1506, can be tied to him with confidence, and attributions are still argued. In 1508 he painted frescoes, now almost lost, on the Fondaco dei Tedeschi beside the Grand Canal. Titian and Sebastiano del Piombo were among the younger painters who took up his ideas. The two small Washington pictures belong to the group of works linked to his name.","q7814":"Giotto di Bondone was the most celebrated Florentine painter of his century. Boccaccio praised him in the Decameron as a genius who had brought back to light an art long buried under the errors of those who painted to please the eye rather than the mind. He died in 1337 and was buried in Florence. The small New York panel of the Adoration of the Magi, dated possibly to about 1320, is a devotional work from his late years; as with many pictures that carry his name, scholars have debated how much of it came from his own hand and how much from assistants in a busy workshop. Workshops of that kind took up his designs and repeated his figure types, and spread his manner through Italy within a generation. His reputation, once fixed by Dante, Boccaccio and Vasari, has hardly wavered since.","q1373474":"Matteo di Giovanni, listed here under his patronymic, was born at Borgo San Sepolcro about 1430 and spent most of his working life in Siena, where he died in the 1490s. Little is recorded of his training; his gentle, linear drawing and gold-ground Madonnas keep to the Sienese tradition, though a touch of Piero della Francesca's clear light, from their shared native town, is often noted. He ran a busy workshop, and the two Washington Madonnas, painted for private devotion in the 1460s, are typical: a slender Virgin, a lively Child, gilded haloes and a crowd of angels or saints. His best-known works are two large versions of the Massacre of the Innocents, painted for Sienese churches in 1482 and 1491, whose crowded, sculptural figures were admired by later generations.","q1358364":"Giovanni Antonio Pellegrini was born in Venice in 1675 and trained with the decorative painter Paolo Pagani. He married Angela, sister of the pastellist Rosalba Carriera, and spent much of his life travelling for commissions, which was unusual for a Venetian of his day. Between 1708 and 1713 he worked in England, where he painted the great hall and staircase at Kimbolton Castle and a dome at Castle Howard, bringing light, airy Venetian decoration to English country houses. Later journeys took him to the Low Countries, Düsseldorf, Vienna, Paris and Dresden; in Paris, in 1720, he decorated the Banque Royale for John Law, a ceiling now lost. His mythologies, among them the Bacchus and Ariadne in New York, are painted in pale, silvery tones with quick brushwork that anticipates the Rococo. He died in Venice in 1741.","q2519261":"Giovanni Battista Caracciolo, called Battistello, was born in Naples in 1578 and died there in 1635. He is usually named as the first Neapolitan painter to take up the lessons of Caravaggio, who was in Naples in 1606 to 1607 and again in 1609 to 1610. Battistello's Liberation of Saint Peter of 1615, painted for the Pio Monte della Misericordia, is his best-known early work, a deep prison scene lit by a single torch-like source. He travelled to Rome, Florence and Genoa and also painted frescoes in Naples, including some at the Certosa di San Martino. After the 1610s his shadows lightened and his figures grew calmer and more monumental, as the Calling of Saint Matthew at the Metropolitan, from the later 1620s, shows. He is now regarded as a leading figure of the Neapolitan Caravaggesque school.","giovanni_battista_cima_da_conegliano":"Giovanni Battista Cima, called Cima da Conegliano after his native town in the hills north of Venice, was born about 1459 and is recorded in Venice from the early 1490s. He worked in the manner of Giovanni Bellini, whose clear light and calm Madonnas he followed, but gave his altarpieces a particular freshness by setting them against landscapes of the Venetian foothills, with a hilltop castle often taken to be his home town. His Baptism of Christ in San Giovanni in Bragora in Venice dates from about 1494 and is among his best-known works. He also painted for patrons in Parma and the towns of the Veneto, and he died at Conegliano around 1517 or 1518. The Cleveland Virgin and Child with Saints and Donors comes from the last years of his career.","q18508242":"Giovanni Battista Gigola was born at Brescia in 1767 and died at Milan in 1841. He is remembered chiefly as a miniaturist and portrait painter in the years when northern Italy was ruled by Napoleon, first as the Cisalpine Republic and then as the Kingdom of Italy, and his sitters came from the noble and official families that rose under the new regime. Little is recorded in detail of his training. The New York portrait of Francesca Ghirardi Lechi, who died young in 1806, belongs to this Brescian circle of patrons; the Lechi were a family of note in Brescia during the Napoleonic years, with several sons serving as officers in the French-allied armies. Gigola is a minor name today, but a useful witness to the portraiture of that brief and busy period.","giovanni_battista_langetti":"Giovanni Battista Langetti was born in Genoa in the first half of the seventeenth century and made his career in Venice, where he died in 1676. He is said to have trained in Rome with Pietro da Cortona before the move. In Venice he joined the painters known as the tenebrosi, the dark-manner men, whose canvases of saints, hermits and ancient philosophers are built from deep shadow, raking light and heavily modelled flesh, with an earthy realism owed to Ribera and the Neapolitans. The Vision of Saint Jerome in Cleveland, about 1660, is a good example, the old penitent drawn almost as a sculptor would carve him. Langetti was admired in his day for the force of his figures, but his dark style faded with the lighter taste of the next century, and it is only in recent decades that he has been studied seriously again.","q504949":"Giovanni Battista Moroni was born about 1520 to 1525 at Albino, near Bergamo, and died in 1578. He trained in Brescia with Alessandro Bonvicino, called Moretto, and spent most of his life in Bergamo. He painted altarpieces for local churches, but he is remembered for portraits: honest, standing figures of provincial nobles, lawyers and doctors, in plain dress against bare backgrounds, with unflinching attention to the face and hands. His Tailor, now in the National Gallery in London, shows a craftsman at his bench with a dignity then rare in Italian portraiture. Moroni was little known outside Bergamo until the nineteenth century; since then he has been ranked among the great portraitists of the Italian sixteenth century. The Cleveland and New York pictures, quiet portraits of Bergamasque sitters, are typical of his sober and truthful manner.","q282670":"Giovanni Battista Piazzetta was born in Venice in 1682, trained first with Antonio Molinari, and then went to Bologna about 1703 to study with Giuseppe Maria Crespi, whose warm, brown, closely observed manner shaped everything he did afterwards. Back in Venice he became known for deep chiaroscuro and for half-length figures of saints, fortune-tellers and shepherds, painted in russets, greys and whites, and also for drawings, which collectors prized. He was a slow, exacting worker and left relatively few paintings; the Supper at Emmaus in Cleveland and the Saint Christopher in New York are among them. He drew the illustrations for the 1745 Venice edition of Tasso's Gerusalemme Liberata, and in 1750 became head of the new painting school at Venice. He died in 1754, and his dark manner left its mark on the young Tiepolo among others.","giovanni_battista_pittoni":"Giovanni Battista Pittoni was born in Venice in 1687, the nephew of the painter and engraver Francesco Pittoni, with whom he is said to have trained. He became one of the busy painters of the Venetian eighteenth century, producing altarpieces for churches in Venice and on the mainland, mythological and historical canvases, and small, sweet-toned devotional pictures for collectors at home and abroad. His manner moved from the strong shadows of his early years toward the pale, graceful colour of the Rococo, with clear, rounded figure types and a neat finish that kept him in demand. He later served as head of the Venetian painters' academy and died in Venice in 1767. The Cleveland painting of saints presenting a devout woman to the Virgin and Child belongs to the 1720s, the early part of his career. Little personal detail survives.","q186202":"Giovanni Battista Tiepolo, born in Venice in 1696, trained with Gregorio Lazzarini and absorbed the colour of Veronese, the great Venetian decorator of the sixteenth century. In 1719 he married Cecilia Guardi, sister of the painters Francesco and Gianantonio Guardi. His fame rests on ceilings: the Palazzo Labia in Venice, a staircase and imperial hall for the Prince-Bishop of Würzburg between 1750 and 1753, and villas in the Veneto, where his son Giandomenico worked at his side. The New York canvases include early Roman history paintings made for a Venetian palace and sketches for the Würzburg staircase and for the throne-room ceiling in Madrid. Charles III invited him to Spain in 1762, and he died in Madrid in 1770. Neoclassical taste then neglected him; he is now ranked the last great master of Venetian decoration.","q17169":"Giovanni Bellini, born in Venice, probably in the early 1430s, was the son of the painter Jacopo Bellini, brother of Gentile Bellini and brother-in-law of Andrea Mantegna, whose hard, sculptural style left its mark on his early work. Over a long career he softened that manner into the warm, luminous oil painting that came to define Venetian art, with Madonnas set before landscapes at dawn or evening and altarpieces whose light seems to fill the church itself. From 1479 he was official painter to the Venetian Republic in the Doge's Palace, working on history canvases lost in the fire of 1577. When Albrecht Dürer visited Venice in 1506 he wrote that Bellini, though very old, was still the best in painting. Giorgione and Titian are said to have learned from him. He died in Venice in 1516.","q362003":"Giovanni Boldini was born at Ferrara in 1842, the son of a painter, and studied in Florence, where about 1862 he joined the Macchiaioli, the painters who gathered at the Caffè Michelangiolo to paint in bold patches of light. He moved to London in 1870 and to Paris in 1871, and there he became the most sought-after society portraitist of the Belle Époque, painting aristocrats, actresses and heiresses in dashing, spiralling brushwork that earned him the name of master of the swish. His sitters included Giuseppe Verdi and the dandy Robert de Montesquiou, and he was a friend of Degas. Gossip, painted in 1873, belongs to his earlier genre manner, a small Parisian scene in the polished style then fashionable, owing something to Meissonier. He died in Paris in 1931.","q684959":"Giovanni d'Alemagna, Giovanni of Germany, was a German-born painter who settled in Venice and worked there from the early 1440s until his death about 1450. His name and his surviving works are bound up with Antonio Vivarini of Murano, with whom he formed a partnership; together they signed the Coronation of the Virgin in the church of San Pantalon, Venice, in 1444. In 1448 the partners painted the vault of the Ovetari Chapel in Padua, whose walls the young Mantegna was soon to cover. Their style joins a Gothic taste for gold, rich ornament and flowing drapery to the first signs of Renaissance space, with an unmistakably northern love of detail. The Washington panel of Saint Apollonia smashing a pagan idol belongs to the same period. Little else is recorded of his life, and his birthplace in Germany is not securely known.","q2628927":"Giovanni da Milano, Giovanni of Milan, was a Lombard painter who worked in Florence from the mid-1340s and is recorded there into the 1360s. His most secure works include a polyptych at Prato, signed and dated 1354, and the frescoes of the lives of the Virgin and of Mary Magdalen in the Rinuccini Chapel of Santa Croce, Florence, painted about 1365. These show an artist who joined Lombard tenderness and sharp observation of faces to the Florentine tradition of Giotto and his heirs, so that his figures have both weight and sweetness. The New York Madonna and Child with Donors, dated about 1365, is a small devotional panel from the same years. Little is recorded of his life outside these works, and he is now counted among the more individual Florentine painters of the generation after Giotto's.","q3768475":"Giovanni di Bartolommeo Cristiani was a painter of Pistoia, the small Tuscan city between Florence and Lucca, recorded in the later fourteenth century. Little is known of his career beyond a few polyptychs and panels that survive; he worked in the Florentine tradition of the period, with gold grounds, clear contours and a sweet, rather formal grace. The New York panel gathers four scenes from the legend of Saint Lucy: she and her mother praying at the shrine of Saint Agatha, her giving of alms, her appearance before the governor Paschasius, and the moment when every effort to drag her from where she stood failed. Panels of this sort usually belonged to a larger altarpiece or predella, and the museum records no secure date for this one. He is a minor master known mainly to specialists.","q1918707":"Giovanni di Francesco del Cervelliera, whose dates the museum gives as 1412 to 1459, was a Florentine painter of the generation of Fra Filippo Lippi and Domenico Veneziano. Little is recorded of his life, and his name is attached to altarpieces, devotional panels and furniture decoration made in Florence in the middle of the fifteenth century. The New York Portrait of a Woman, from about 1445, belongs to a typical Florentine genre: a profile bust against a plain ground, with the sitter's costume, jewels and hairstyle described in fine detail, often made to mark a betrothal or marriage and kept in a family house. Such pictures tell more about fashion and about the way Florentine families presented their daughters than about the personalities of the sitters, and the maker of this one is known only through modern attribution.","q946420":"Giovanni di Paolo was born in Siena around 1400 and died there in 1482, and his whole career belongs to that city. Little is recorded of his training, but he worked as a miniaturist as well as a panel painter. His altarpieces and small narrative panels, with their elongated, restless figures, brilliant colour and bare, sometimes fantastical landscapes, carry the Gothic tradition of Siena into the age of the Renaissance. He illuminated a manuscript of Dante's Divine Comedy in the 1440s, now in the British Library, whose Paradise leaves show his gift for visionary space; the Paradise panel in New York shares that spirit. The Washington and New York pictures also include several Adorations of the Magi and many saints. Neglected for long periods, he is now ranked among the most original Sienese painters of the fifteenth century.","q2738832":"Giovanni di ser Giovanni Guidi, called Lo Scheggia, the Splinter, was born at San Giovanni Valdarno in 1406 and died in Florence in 1486. He was the younger brother of Masaccio, who died in Rome in 1428, and he spent his career in Florence as a painter of furniture and festive objects rather than great altarpieces: decorated chests, wedding panels and birth trays. The Adimari cassone panel, showing a Florentine wedding procession before the Baptistery, is often attributed to him. The New York tray is a desco da parto, a painted tray presented to a mother after a birth; its Triumph of Fame on one side and the arms of the Medici and Tornabuoni families on the other suggest it was made about 1449 for the birth of Lorenzo de' Medici, later called the Magnificent. Scheggia is known mainly for such cheerful, practical pictures.","q21850531":"Giovanni di Tano Fei was a Florentine painter of the late fourteenth century; the dates 1384 and 1405 given for him seem to mark the years in which he is on record rather than his birth and death. He worked in the generation after Giotto, when Florentine painting held to gold grounds, clear drawing and bright, firmly outlined colour, and he is known mainly from altarpieces and panels of devotional subjects. The New York Coronation of the Virgin, and Saints, dated 1394, is among the works tied to his name. Its subject is the usual one: Christ crowns his mother in heaven, among angels and saints who stand in rows on either side, a favourite theme for Florentine altarpieces of the time. Little more is recorded about him, and nothing reliable about his teachers or patrons.","q435316":"Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo was born in Venice in 1727, the eldest surviving son of Giovanni Battista Tiepolo, and spent the first half of his career as his father's close assistant: at Würzburg in 1750 to 1753, at the Villa Valmarana in 1757, and in Madrid from 1762, where the father died in 1770. In his early twenties he painted the Stations of the Cross for the Oratorio del Crocifisso at San Polo in Venice, his best-known religious cycle. After returning to Venice he turned to quieter, more personal work: genre scenes, drawings of Punchinello and, for his own villa at Zianigo, a series of wall paintings now in the Ca' Rezzonico. He also made etchings. Long seen as a follower, he is now valued for the humour and humanity of his drawings and genre pictures. He died in Venice in 1804.","q2983846":"Giovanni Francesco Romanelli was born at Viterbo about 1610 and trained in Rome, first with Domenichino and then, more lastingly, with Pietro da Cortona, whose sweeping Baroque decoration he learned to temper with clearer lines and more graceful colour. He worked for the Barberini family, nephews of Pope Urban VIII, and helped decorate rooms of their palace in Rome. In the 1640s and 1650s he was called to Paris by Cardinal Mazarin, for whom he painted the ceiling of the gallery of Mazarin's palace in 1646 to 1647, and later he worked in the Louvre for Anne of Austria. In France his smooth, courtly manner influenced local painters. He died at Viterbo in 1662. The New York Sacrifice of Polyxena, a refined classical subject rendered in soft colour, shows the polished, elegant side of his art.","q938442":"Giovanni Gerolamo Savoldo, a native of Brescia who worked in Florence and Venice, was among the most original painters of north Italy in the early sixteenth century. His date of birth, about 1480, and of death, after 1548, are not exactly known. His particular gift was for light: night scenes lit by lamps and fires, dawn skies, and the glint of armour and mirrors that doubled a figure, as in the Louvre portrait called Gaston de Foix. He painted few pictures, mostly devotional, for private patrons and churches in Venice and Brescia, and favoured quiet, poised figures over bustle. The New York Saint Matthew and the Angel is among his best-known paintings, the saint turning in lamplight to listen to the angel's dictation. His nocturnal effects are sometimes suggested as an influence on Caravaggio.","giovanni_girolamo_savoldo":"Giovanni Girolamo Savoldo, the same painter as the Savoldo above under another spelling, was born at Brescia about 1480 and is recorded in Florence in the first decade of the sixteenth century and in Venice from the 1520s. He joined the Venetian tradition of Giorgione and Titian but made his own the cooler habits of his native Lombardy: close observation, quiet gestures and a taste for reflected and artificial light. His Penitent Magdalene in London, glowing in dawn light, is the best known of his pictures. The Cleveland Christ with Joseph of Arimathea, about 1525, is a half-length, intimate treatment of the Passion of the sort that Venetian patrons liked for private devotion. Savoldo worked for few patrons and left few paintings, and details of his later life are scanty.","q21850154":"Giovanni Maldura is an obscure painter: the museum gives no dates, and little is recorded of his life beyond his name and the fact that he was Italian. The New York picture, David at the Cave of Adullam, shows an episode from the Old Testament in which David, hiding from King Saul, longs for a drink from the well at Bethlehem, then held by the Philistines; three of his captains break through the enemy lines to fetch it, and David, humbled, pours it out as an offering to God. It is an uncommon subject in painting, and the picture is the only work by this hand known to this book. Beyond that, nothing certain can be said of his training, his dates or the place where he worked.","q286670":"Giovanni Paolo Panini was born at Piacenza in 1691 and trained there in the tradition of architectural perspective and stage design. He settled in Rome about 1711 and became the city's most famous view painter, producing capricci of ancient ruins and grand views of the modern city for visitors on the Grand Tour. His Interior of the Pantheon and Interior of Saint Peter's are among the best-known images of those buildings. He also painted festival scenes and gallery pictures, rooms crowded with paintings of Roman sights; the New York pair of 1757, Ancient Rome and Modern Rome, was made for the French ambassador, the Comte de Stainville, later Duc de Choiseul. Panini taught perspective at the French Academy in Rome, and Hubert Robert studied with him. He died in Rome in 1765, and his views remain a vivid record of eighteenth-century Rome.","q2701846":"Girolamo da Carpi was born at Ferrara in 1501 into a family from Carpi, and trained with the Ferrarese painter Garofalo. He was a restless student of the greater painters: he is known to have studied Correggio and Parmigianino, and the Apparition of the Virgin in Washington shows the soft, melting faces and twisting forms of those masters. He worked in Ferrara, Bologna and Rome and is recorded as an architect in Rome around 1550, in the service of Pope Julius III and of Cardinal Ippolito d'Este. Vasari wrote his Life. Girolamo died in Ferrara in 1556. His religious pictures and portraits, among them the Portrait of a Prelate in Cleveland and the Adoration of the Shepherds in New York, show a talent that was versatile and quick to absorb new ideas, but that rarely settled into a style of its own.","q2485838":"Girolamo da Cremona was a Lombard painter and illuminator of the fifteenth century, recorded from the 1450s into the 1480s. He is best known as a book decorator: he worked in Siena, painting initials and miniature scenes for choir books of the cathedral, and in Venice, where he illuminated printed books and manuscripts for scholars and princes. Admired by contemporaries for rich colour, fine detail and a feeling for antique ornament, he also painted panels, and the Metropolitan Museum's Descent from the Cross is attributed to him. His style shows the influence of Mantegna and the painters of Padua. Little is recorded of his early training, and his dates and the order of his works are still debated by historians of manuscript painting, so the outline of his life remains less clear than the beauty of his surviving pages.","q1440424":"Girolamo dai Libri, born in Verona in 1474, took his nickname, meaning of the books, from his family's trade: his father Francesco was a famous illuminator of choir books, and Girolamo began as a miniaturist before turning to altarpieces in oil. He became one of the most admired painters of Verona in the early sixteenth century, producing Madonnas with saints for the city's churches, with fresh, jewel-like colours, delicately drawn faces and landscapes of rocks and trees that recall Mantegna and the Venetians. The Madonna of the Orange Tree, now in the Castelvecchio in Verona, is the best known; the Madonna and Child with Saints in New York has the same gentle tone. Vasari wrote of him in his Lives, and he died in Verona in 1555.","q1334726":"Girolamo di Benvenuto was born in Siena in 1470 and died there in 1524. He was the son and pupil of the painter Benvenuto di Giovanni, with whom he worked on altarpieces in the 1490s, among them an Assumption of the Virgin of 1498 now in Washington, and he spent his working life in his native city, painting altarpieces, small devotional panels and a few portraits. His manner began as the gentle, linear one inherited from his father and from the older Sienese tradition, and over time took in the broader forms and softer modelling of the High Renaissance as these reached Siena. The Washington Portrait of a Young Woman, about 1508, is one of the few portraits associated with the Sienese school of those years. He is a modest but attractive figure among the painters of Siena.","q732766":"Girolamo Romanino was born in Brescia about 1484 and died about 1560. He is the great rival of his fellow Brescian Moretto: where Moretto is cool, silvery and composed, Romanino is warm, rough and exuberant, with broad figures, strong gestures and a vivid feeling for country life. He learned from the Venetians, above all Titian and Giorgione, and later took lessons from Pordenone. He worked in Padua and in Cremona, where he painted scenes of the Passion in the cathedral about 1519 to 1520, and in Trento, where Cardinal Bernardo Cles employed him at the Castello del Buonconsiglio in the early 1530s. About 1534 he painted the frescoes of the church at Pisogne on Lake Iseo, a rustic masterpiece. The New York Flagellation, with the Madonna of Mercy on the reverse, is a two-sided panel of the kind confraternities often carried in procession.","q2847853":"André Giroux was born in Paris in 1801, the son of Alphonse Giroux, a cabinetmaker, stationer and art dealer whose shop later made the first Daguerreotype cameras sold to the public. André trained as a landscape painter and in 1825 won the Prix de Rome for historical landscape, which sent him to Italy. There, like other young French landscapists of the time, he painted small oil sketches out of doors, in the woods of Civita Castellana and Papigno and over the roofs of Rome, catching the light and shade of the Italian countryside directly from the motif. The three small Washington oils date from this stay, about 1825 to 1830. On his return to Paris he went on to exhibit at the Salon. He died in 1879, and such fresh open-air studies are now much admired.","giulio_campi":"Giulio Campi, born at Cremona about 1500 and dead there in 1572, was the eldest of the brothers Campi, with Antonio and Vincenzo, painters who dominated the artistic life of Cremona in the sixteenth century. He trained with his father, Galeazzo Campi, and is said to have learned from Giulio Romano at Mantua. His own style is eclectic: northern Italian love of light and rich colour joined with Mannerist elegance and with the monumental figures of Pordenone, whose frescoes in Cremona Cathedral he knew. Giulio painted altarpieces and frescoes in the churches of Cremona, among them San Sigismondo, and portraits such as the Portrait of an Old Man in Cleveland. He is regarded as the leader of a lively and productive family workshop, and as the most capable painter of the Cremonese school of his generation.","q1119589":"Giulio Cesare Procaccini was born at Bologna in 1574 and moved with his family to Milan about 1587, where he spent his working life. Trained first as a sculptor, he turned to painting within the family workshop, in the company of his brother Camillo, and developed a warm, soft, sensuous style that drew on Correggio and on the Bolognese Carracci. He worked for the great churches of Milan and for Genoese patrons, among them the Doria and Spinola families. A famous episode is the Picture of Three Hands, the Martyrdom of Saints Rufina and Seconda, in which Procaccini, Cerano and Morazzone, the leading painters of Lombardy, each painted a share. He died in Milan in 1625. The New York Madonna and Child with Saints Francis and Dominic and Angels, about 1612, shows the sweetness of his manner.","giuseppe_abbati":"Giuseppe Abbati was born in Naples in 1836 and died in Florence in 1868, in his early thirties. After studies in Naples he moved to Florence, where he joined the Macchiaioli, the circle of painters who met at the Caffè Michelangiolo and sought a truthful, modern art built from patches, macchie, of light and shade. He was a patriot as well: he fought with Garibaldi in 1860 and was wounded at the Volturno, losing the sight of an eye, an injury that did not stop him painting. His surviving pictures, with their broad planes of colour and strongly simplified forms, anticipate twentieth-century painting. The New York canvas of a man seated and asleep, about 1865, is a characteristic image of daily life, painted without a story or a moral. His early death cut short a promising career.","giuseppe_baldrighi":"Giuseppe Baldrighi was born at Stradella, near Pavia, in 1723 and died at Parma in 1803. He made his career at Parma, whose duke, Philip, belonged to the Spanish Bourbon line, and he is recorded as a portraitist in the service of the court, painting the ducal family and courtiers in the elegant, light, French-influenced style that Duke Philip and his wife Louise-Élisabeth favoured. He also taught at the Parma academy of fine arts. The Cleveland Lion, of the 1750s, is a reminder that his range extended beyond portraits. Little else is firmly recorded of his training or private life, and he remains a minor but characteristic figure of Italian court painting in the middle of the eighteenth century.","q3107986":"Giuseppe Bartolomeo Chiari was born at Lucca in 1654 and trained in Rome as a pupil of Carlo Maratta, the leading painter of the city in the later seventeenth century, whose clear, classical compositions and gentle colour he followed all his life. He settled in Rome, became a member of the Accademia di San Luca, and worked for churches and noble families on altarpieces, mythologies and biblical scenes, with a particular sweetness of type and clarity of pose. His elegance suited the taste of the Rome of Pope Clement XI, for whom he is recorded as working. He died in Rome in 1727. The New York Bathsheba at Her Bath, about 1700, shows his manner: a biblical story treated as an excuse for a graceful nude in a refined, courtly setting.","q3107999":"Giuseppe Bottani was born at Cremona in 1717 and died at Mantua in 1784. He trained in Cremona and then in Rome, where he came under the influence of the classicizing painters then in favour, and he spent the later part of his career at Mantua, where he was a leading figure of the academy there. He was a history painter, producing biblical and mythological pictures in a tidy, clear style close to Roman academic painting, and also painted landscapes. His career is a useful reminder of how widely the Roman academic taste spread across northern Italy in the middle of the eighteenth century. The New York Departure of Saints Paula and Eustochium for the Holy Land shows the early Christian pilgrims, a mother and daughter, setting out on their voyage in a quiet, orderly composition in which the figures dominate.","q1528623":"Giuseppe Recco was born in Naples in 1634 into a family of still-life painters and learned his art from his father, Giovan Battista Recco. He specialised in the kitchen and market scene, with fish, shellfish and game heaped on tables and floors in lively, almost theatrical light, and in flower pieces, building on the Neapolitan tradition of Luca Forte and Paolo Porpora. He became one of the best-known still-life painters of Naples in the later seventeenth century, with a market among the city's collectors and churchmen. In 1695 he set out for Spain at the invitation of Charles II but died at Alicante on the way. The New York Cat Stealing Fish, painted in the late 1660s, is a lively, humorous example of the kitchen pictures for which he is best remembered.","q18573601":"Giuseppe Ruoppolo was born in Naples in 1639 and died there in 1710. He belonged to the Ruoppolo family of still-life painters, in which the better-known name is Giovan Battista Ruoppolo, and he worked in the Neapolitan tradition that also produced Luca Forte, Paolo Porpora and the Recco family. Such painters filled their canvases with heaped fruit, vegetables, fish and shellfish, arranged on stone ledges or in baskets against dark backgrounds, with strong, warm light picking out the textures of skins, shells and leaves. The New York Still Life of Fruit and Nuts, from the 1660s, belongs to this tradition. Naples was a large market for such pictures, which were bought by aristocrats, churchmen and merchants for the decoration of palaces and country houses. Little is recorded of his life beyond his dates and the pictures themselves.","q874868":"Godfried Schalcken was born in 1643 at Made, near Dordrecht, and grew up in Dordrecht, where he first studied with Samuel van Hoogstraten before moving to Leiden to learn from Gerrit Dou, the founder of the Leiden school of fine painting. From Dou he took the smooth, enamelled finish and the favourite device of figures lit by a candle or lamp, and he became the best-known candlelight specialist of his day. He worked in Dordrecht and The Hague and spent several years in London in the 1690s, painting portraits for English patrons, before going to Düsseldorf in 1703 to work for the Elector Palatine. He died in The Hague in 1706. Cephalus and Procris, a mythological scene probably from the 1680s, shows the polished, precise handling that fetched high prices from collectors.","q3109847":"Goffredo Wals was a German landscape painter, born at Cologne towards the end of the sixteenth century and active in Italy, first in Rome and then in Naples, where he appears to have died, probably in the later 1630s. The museum gives no dates. His chief claim to remembrance is that the young Claude Gellée, later called Claude Lorrain, is recorded by the German painter and biographer Joachim von Sandrart as having studied with him. His Italian landscapes, with their classical ruins, groves and distant hills in warm light, are counted among the early links in the Roman tradition that led to Claude, and he is thought to have learned from the earlier northern landscapists in Rome. The New York Roman Landscape with Figures, probably of the 1630s, is typical. Little else about his life is securely recorded.","q5582":"Vincent van Gogh was born at Zundert, in the Netherlands, in 1853, the son of a pastor. He worked as an art dealer, a teacher and a lay preacher before he began to paint seriously about 1880, supported by his brother Theo, a Paris dealer, to whom he wrote the hundreds of letters that survive. In Paris from 1886 he met the Impressionists and brightened his dark Dutch palette; at Arles in 1888 he painted Roulin's Baby and La Mousmé, and shared the Yellow House with Gauguin for a few troubled weeks. After a breakdown he entered the asylum at Saint-Rémy, where he painted olive orchards in 1889, and from May 1890 he lived at Auvers-sur-Oise, painting wheat fields and flowers. He died there in July 1890 of a gunshot wound. He sold almost nothing in his lifetime, and his fame grew swiftly after his death.","q165367":"Hendrick Goltzius was born in 1558 at Mühlbracht, near Venlo, trained as an engraver with Dirck Volckertsz Coornhert, and settled in Haarlem in 1577. He became the most celebrated printmaker in Europe, famous for the swelling, flowing lines of his engravings and for feats of virtuosity such as the Master Engravings of 1593 to 1594, in which he imitated the styles of Dürer, Lucas van Leyden and other earlier masters. With Karel van Mander and Cornelis van Haarlem he formed an informal academy in Haarlem. In 1590 to 1591 he travelled to Italy, apparently under the assumed name Hendrick van Bracht, to avoid attention. About 1600, in poor health, he gave up engraving for painting. The Fall of Man, dated 1616, belongs to his last years, painted in a clear, polished manner. He died in Haarlem in 1617.","q464300":"Eva Gonzalès was born in Paris, the daughter of the novelist Emmanuel Gonzalès, and studied first with the fashionable painter Charles Chaplin. In 1869 she became the only formal pupil of Édouard Manet, who painted her portrait in 1870, and her work shows his influence in its clear light, strong tonal contrasts and unfussy handling, though she kept a freshness of her own, working in oil and in pastel. She exhibited at the Salon from 1870 and showed A Box at the Théâtre des Italiens there in 1874, but was not part of the Impressionist exhibitions. In 1879 she married the engraver Henri Guérard. She died in 1883, only days after the birth of her son and a week after the death of Manet. Nanny and Child, of 1877 to 1878, a domestic scene, belongs to her mature work.","q21498086":"Nothing reliable is recorded here about M. A. Goode beyond the initials, the dates 1819 to 1910 and the label American; even the painter's first names are not given. The National Gallery's Still Life, dated only to the second half of the nineteenth century, belongs to a period when still-life painting was popular with professional and amateur painters across the United States, and when many pictures were made by painters whose names and careers have gone unrecorded. Whether Goode trained in a studio, learned from prints and instruction books, or worked mainly for friends and neighbours is not known, and no patrons, exhibitions or teachers can be named. The picture is kept for its own qualities as an example of American still-life painting of the period, and a longer life of its painter cannot honestly be written.","q18562176":"The Goodhart Ducciesque Master is not a known person but a convenience name given by modern scholars to the painter of a small group of pictures in the manner of Duccio di Buoninsegna, the great Sienese master who painted the Maestà for Siena Cathedral between 1308 and 1311. Ducciesque means in the style of Duccio. The painter, evidently Sienese and probably trained in or near Duccio's workshop, worked about 1310 to 1315, and the New York panel of the Madonna and Child with the Annunciation and the Nativity is one of the pictures grouped under his name. Its gold ground, graceful figures and gentle faces, with small narrative scenes set around the central group, follow the manner that Duccio made famous. Nothing is known of his real name, life or patrons.","q346952":"Jan Gossart, called Mabuse, was born in Maubeuge about 1478 and became one of the most inventive painters of the early Netherlandish Renaissance. After joining the Antwerp guild in 1503 he travelled to Rome with Philip of Burgundy in 1508, and his drawings after antique statues and Roman buildings gave him a vocabulary of classical forms that he was among the first to bring to the north. His altarpieces and nudes, such as Neptune and Amphitrite of 1516 and Danaë of 1527, show this learning, and he also painted vivid portraits like the Met's Portrait of a Man of about 1520 to 1525. He served the Burgundian court at Middelburg and Wijk bij Duurstede and was dead by 1532.","govaert_flinck":"Govaert Flinck was born at Cleves in 1615 and trained first in Leeuwarden, in Friesland, with Lambert Jacobsz before moving to Amsterdam about 1633 to work in Rembrandt's studio. For some years he painted so much in Rembrandt's manner that his portraits and biblical scenes were sometimes mistaken for his master's. Later he turned to a smoother, more elegant style, following Van Dyck and the fashionable Amsterdam portraitists, and became one of the most successful portrait painters in the city. He painted regents, guild members and their wives, as well as biblical and mythological scenes. The Cleveland Portrait of a Woman, dated 1646, belongs to the middle of his career, when his portraits were most in demand. He died in Amsterdam in 1660, at the height of his reputation.","q550401":"Govert Flinck, whose name is also spelled Govaert, was a Dutch painter born at Cleves in 1615, a pupil of Lambert Jacobsz at Leeuwarden and then of Rembrandt, in whose workshop he developed his command of light and character. In his maturity he became one of the most successful painters in Amsterdam and received the biggest commission of his life in 1659, for twelve large paintings of the revolt of the Batavians against Rome to hang in the new Town Hall. He died suddenly in 1660, leaving only sketches, and the series was divided among other painters; Rembrandt's Conspiracy of Claudius Civilis, now in Stockholm, was one of them. The New York Bearded Man with a Velvet Cap, of 1645, and the Young Woman as a Shepherdess are examples of his portraits and fanciful pictures, graceful and smoothly finished.","q5432":"Francisco Goya was born in 1746 at Fuendetodos in Aragon and trained in Zaragoza before going to Madrid and Italy. From the 1770s he designed cartoons for the royal tapestry factory, and after 1786 he served the Spanish crown, becoming First Court Painter in 1799. His portraits, from the Marquesa de Pontejos of about 1786 to the officers and merchants of the 1800s, are among the most searching of any age; he painted the Family of Charles IV about 1800 without flattery. A severe illness in 1792 to 1793 left him deaf, and his art turned toward satire and nightmare in the prints of the Caprichos and the Disasters of War, and in the Black Paintings he made for his own house. In 1824 he left Spain for Bordeaux, where he died in 1828. The Sebastián Martínez portrait dates from the year the illness began.","q315996":"Jan van Goyen was born in Leiden in 1596 and trained with a succession of local painters before spending a year in Haarlem with Esaias van de Velde, who gave him his taste for real Dutch scenery. From 1634 he lived in The Hague, painting river views, dunes, villages and beaches in a nearly monochrome palette of greys, greens and browns under wide, moving skies. A prolific painter, he also dealt in paintings, houses and land and speculated in tulips, and he died in debt in 1656. His daughter Margriet married Jan Steen. The Met's View of Haarlem and the Haarlemmer Meer, of 1646, shows the flat, watery scenery he loved.","q154338":"Matthias Grünewald is the name by which this painter has been known since the seventeenth century, when the biographer Joachim von Sandrart gave it to him in error; documents call him Mathis Gothart Nithart. He was probably born at Würzburg about 1475 and worked for Uriel von Gemmingen and then for Albrecht of Brandenburg, successive Archbishops of Mainz, as painter and as an engineer. His masterpiece, the Isenheim Altarpiece, painted about 1512 to 1516 for the Antonite hospital at Isenheim in Alsace, includes a Crucifixion in which the dead Christ's body is covered with sores, a sight meant to comfort the patients nursed there. Only a small number of his paintings survive, among them the little Washington Crucifixion. Largely forgotten after his death in 1528, he was rediscovered about 1900 and became a hero of German Expressionism.","q5078935":"Charles Henry Granger, an American painter given the dates 1812 to 1893, is known here only from a single picture, Muster Day, which the museum dates to 1843 or after. Muster day was the day on which the local militia assembled in an American town for drill and inspection, and it was often as much a holiday as a military exercise, bringing out the whole neighbourhood. Pictures of such local events were popular in the nineteenth century with painters who had little or no academic training, and they are valued today as records of everyday American life. Nothing certain is said here of where Granger trained, where he lived or who bought his work, and it is better to say so than to guess.","greek_painter":"The painter of this icon is not recorded: the museum labels him only Greek and possibly of the eighteenth century. In the Greek Orthodox world icons were made by painters trained in the workshops of the great centres, among them Crete, the Ionian Islands, Mount Athos and Constantinople, who worked within long traditions and seldom signed their work. All Saints, the subject here, is a feast celebrated in the Orthodox Church on the Sunday after Pentecost, and icons of it typically show a gathering of saints of every rank, apostles, martyrs, bishops and holy men and women, in ordered rows before Christ. The Metropolitan Museum picture is thus a devotional image rather than a portrait of one holy figure, and its maker, whatever his name, belonged to a craft tradition rather than to a school of individual masters.","q21408244":"Benjamin Greenleaf was an American portrait painter of the early nineteenth century; the dates the museum gives are 1769 to 1821, though little about his life is securely recorded here. He painted portraits of private sitters, and his name is attached to a small group of simple, flat, linear pictures of the kind made by village and travelling painters who often had little formal training. The two Washington pictures, the Lady in a White Mob Cap, about 1805, and the Portrait of J. L., about 1810 to 1818, show that tradition's strengths: clear outlines, honest likeness, attention to details of dress such as a lady's white cap, and no flattery. Such portraits were affordable and were treasured in homes long after their makers were forgotten, and they are now valued by collectors of early American art.","q6236227":"John Greenwood was born in Boston in 1727 and was apprenticed to the engraver Thomas Johnston. By his early twenties he was a capable portrait painter, and the Washington portrait of Elizabeth Fulford Welshman, dated 1749, is among his earliest known works. In 1752 he sailed to Surinam, where he painted the lively Sea Captains Carousing in Surinam, now in the Saint Louis Art Museum, a drunken tavern scene that is one of the most famous genre pictures made by an American of the colonial period. He moved to Amsterdam about 1758 and to London in 1763, and there he turned from painting to a successful career as an art auctioneer, dealing in prints and pictures. He died at Margate in 1792.","q347139":"Jean-Baptiste Greuze was born in 1725 in Tournus, Burgundy, trained in Lyon and settled in Paris about 1750. He made his reputation at the Salon of 1755 with sentimental scenes of virtuous family life, such as A Father Reading the Bible to His Family, and won the enthusiastic backing of the critic Denis Diderot. His moralising pictures, tearful girls and tender portraits were widely collected and engraved, but his career suffered a blow in 1769 when the Royal Academy admitted him only as a genre painter, not a history painter, after he submitted an ambitious history picture, Septimius Severus Reproaching Caracalla. The Revolution left him poor, and he died in 1805. The Met's Broken Eggs of 1756 shows his early success.","q16135327":"Grifo di Tancredi was a Florentine painter of the early fourteenth century; the museum gives 1271 and 1328, which seem to be the years in which he is on record, and little else is known of his life. His name is attached to a group of panels in the style of Florentine painting about 1300, to which the three Washington panels, Saint James Major, Christ Blessing and Saint Peter, painted about 1310, belong. Such half-length figures, set against shimmering gold, were typically parts of a polyptych, a many-panelled altarpiece, with Christ in the centre and saints on either side. The panels show the solemn, rather stiff, gilded manner of Florentine painters in the years when Giotto's innovations were spreading, with strong outlines, calm faces and patient attention to ornament.","q1391765":"Guariento di Arpo was a Paduan painter, recorded from 1338 into the late 1360s, and the leading painter of Padua in the middle of the fourteenth century. He worked for the Carrara family, the lords of Padua, for whom he painted in their palace chapel and in the church of the Eremitani, where his frescoed angels in the apse survive. In 1365 the Republic of Venice called him to paint the Coronation of the Virgin on the wall of the Great Council Hall in the Doge's Palace; the fresco was damaged in the fire of 1577, and its remains were rediscovered in the twentieth century. He combined the solemn weight he learned from Giotto's work in Padua with the Byzantine and Venetian richness of gold and colour. The New York Madonna and Child is a small devotional panel from his workshop tradition.","q334262":"Guercino, the Squinter, was the nickname of Giovanni Francesco Barbieri, born in 1591 at Cento, near Bologna, where he painted his first altarpieces in a dramatic, shadowy style much influenced by Ludovico Carracci. In 1621 Pope Gregory XV, a Bolognese, summoned him to Rome, where he painted the ceiling of the Casino Ludovisi with the Aurora, a sweeping vision of dawn, and a huge altarpiece for Saint Peter's. After the pope's death in 1623 he went home to Cento, and after the death of Guido Reni in 1642 he moved to Bologna, where he became the leading painter. His manner grew calmer, clearer and lighter. He kept an account book listing his paintings and their prices from 1629 to the end of his life, and he reportedly declined invitations to the courts of France and England. He died in Bologna in 1666.","q2449649":"Guido Cagnacci was born at Santarcangelo di Romagna in 1601 and trained in Bologna and Rome, absorbing the soft, idealised manner of Guido Reni and the dramatic light of Guercino. He worked in Rimini, Forlì, Bologna and Venice, and from about 1658 until his death in 1663 he lived in Vienna as painter to the Emperor Leopold I. His religious and mythological subjects are painted in pale, glowing flesh against dark grounds, with a frank sensuality that was sometimes thought improper, and he is best known for half-length heroines such as Cleopatra and the Magdalene. The New York Death of Cleopatra dates from about 1645 to 1655. Little known for centuries, he was rediscovered in the twentieth century and is now seen as one of the most original painters of seventeenth-century Italy.","guido_reni":"Guido Reni was born in Bologna in 1575 and trained first with the Flemish painter Denys Calvaert and then in the Carracci academy. He worked in Rome in the early seventeenth century, where, for Cardinal Scipione Borghese and Pope Paul V, he painted frescoes including the Aurora in the Casino Rospigliosi, before settling in Bologna to run a large and busy workshop. His Saint Michael in Rome and his Massacre of the Innocents show a classical grace that sought to improve on nature, and his smooth, pearly figures in clear light made him, in the eyes of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, the divine Guido. A notorious gambler, he sometimes painted to pay his debts. His fame suffered in the nineteenth century, when critics such as Ruskin attacked him, but he is again admired. He died in Bologna in 1642, the year of the Cleveland Adoration of the Magi.","q2348377":"Paul Guigou was born in 1834 at Villars in the Vaucluse, in the south of France, and worked as a notary's clerk in Marseille before turning to painting, largely self-taught. He moved to Paris in 1861 and spent his winters there, going back each summer to Provence to paint its dry hills, white roads and the river Durance in a clear, direct light. His landscapes, with their firm construction and simplified planes of colour, owe something to Corot and Courbet and look forward to Cézanne's. He exhibited at the Salon and had friends among the painters of Marseille, among them Adolphe Monticelli. He died in Paris in 1871, aged thirty-six, and was little known outside southern France for decades. The Washington Washerwomen on the Banks of the Durance, from 1866, is one of his most characteristic pictures.","q436844":"Armand Guillaumin was born in Paris in 1841 and, because he needed to earn a living, worked by day for the Paris–Orléans railway and then for the city's department of bridges and roads, painting in the evenings and at weekends. He met Pissarro and Cézanne at the Académie Suisse in the 1860s, and showed with the Impressionists from their first exhibition in 1874. His landscapes of the Seine quays, of Paris under the smoke of factories and of the valleys of the Creuse are painted in bold, often hot colour, which later critics saw as anticipating the Fauves. In 1891 he won 100,000 francs in a lottery and could at last paint full-time, travelling in Holland and the south. Never a fashionable name, he had loyal friends among his fellow painters and died in 1927. The Washington Bridge of Louis Philippe dates from 1875.","q34661":"Gustav Klimt was born near Vienna in 1862 and studied at the School of Arts and Crafts there. With his brother Ernst and Franz Matsch he formed a firm of decorative painters that won commissions for theatres and for the staircase of the Kunsthistorisches Museum. In 1897 he led a group of younger artists out of the established Viennese artists' association to found the Vienna Secession, of which he was the first president. His great allegorical paintings for the university were attacked as indecent, but his portraits of Viennese society ladies, such as Serena Pulitzer Lederer in 1899, made him fashionable, and the Lederer family became among his most important patrons. In his golden period he covered his pictures with gold leaf, as in The Kiss. He died in Vienna in 1918, after a stroke.","q6682":"Gustave Doré was born at Strasbourg in 1832 and was a prodigy: he published his first lithographed story at the age of fifteen and moved to Paris, where he became the most famous illustrator in Europe. His wood engravings for Rabelais, Dante's Inferno, Cervantes' Don Quixote of 1863, the Bible and Milton's Paradise Lost were printed in vast editions, and in London: A Pilgrimage he recorded the crowded streets and slums of the English capital. He thought himself a painter first and made huge religious and fantastic canvases, which were shown in a gallery of his own in London from 1869 but were less praised by critics. The Metropolitan's Don Quixote scene, Basil and Quiteria, relates to the Cervantes project. He died in Paris in 1883, remembered more for his illustrations than for his paintings.","q203828":"Gustave Moreau was born in Paris in 1826, the son of an architect, and studied with François-Édouard Picot and became a friend of the painter Théodore Chassériau, whose rich colour shaped his own. After a stay in Italy in 1857 to 1859 he scored his first big success at the Salon of 1864 with Oedipus and the Sphinx, now in New York. His art turned from the Greek myths to biblical and symbolic subjects: Salome and Jupiter and Semele, painted with jewelled colour and intricate ornament, found a devoted reader in J.-K. Huysmans, who described a Salome in his novel À rebours. From 1892 Moreau taught at the École des Beaux-Arts, where Matisse and Rouault were among his students. He left his house and thousands of works to the French state, and it opened as the Musée Gustave Moreau in 1903, five years after his death.","q21498607":"The painter of Abraham Clark and His Children, dated 1822, is known only by the initials J. H. The dates 1742 to 1882 attached to the name in the record are clearly not a lifespan, and nothing certain is known about the painter: not the first name, the place of work nor the training. The picture belongs to the large body of portraits made in the early years of the American republic by itinerant and village painters, who travelled from town to town painting families at modest prices, often in a flat, linear manner with stiff, solemn poses and careful attention to clothes. A group portrait of a father with his children, as the title says, was a typical subject for such painters. Nothing more can honestly be added.","q17619497":"Hugh Bolton Jones, who signed himself H. Bolton Jones, was born in Baltimore in 1848 and became a New York landscape painter of the generation after the Hudson River School. He studied in New York and spent several years in France, and his quiet pastoral scenes of fields, meadows and wooded streams in a soft light show the influence of the Barbizon painters. He was elected to the National Academy of Design in the early 1880s and exhibited there for decades. His brother Francis Coates Jones was also a painter. Spring, of 1885 to 1886, in New York, is typical of his gentle, unforced landscape art, which found its buyers among American collectors who preferred a poetic and tranquil view of nature. He died in 1927.","q1700340":"John Haberle was born in New Haven, Connecticut, in 1856 and spent most of his life there. He began as a lithographer and draughtsman and became one of the leading American masters of trompe-l'oeil, the picture meant to fool the eye, with William Harnett and John Peto: his pictures of bills, coins, stamps, clippings and photographs seem to lie or hang on the surface, so exact in every crease that visitors reached out to touch them. Imitation, of 1887, is among the best known. Some of his illusions are also jokes aimed at the viewer. His output shrank in his later years, and he died in New Haven in 1933. Like Harnett and Peto, he was largely forgotten until the middle of the twentieth century, when a revival of interest in American trompe-l'oeil restored his name.","q21497634":"The artist A. Haddock is known only by that signature, and the dates 1748 to 1888 given in the record are clearly not a lifespan; nothing reliable can be said of the painter's training, place of work or patrons. The picture's title, Red Jacket, points to Sagoyewatha, called Red Jacket, the Seneca orator and chief who died in 1830 and who was a famous figure in New York State and beyond. The date after 1828 suggests that the painter may have copied the portrait Robert W. Weir painted that year, the best-known likeness of him, and the picture should be read as a popular image of a celebrated man. It belongs to the tradition of nineteenth-century American images of Native leaders, made for a public curious about them.","hall_george_henry":"George Henry Hall was born at Manchester, New Hampshire, in 1825, grew up in Boston and studied in Düsseldorf in 1849 and later in Paris and Rome. He settled in New York in the 1850s and became known for still lifes of fruit and flowers painted with the precision and fine finish of the Düsseldorf school, and also for genre scenes from Spain and Italy. He travelled often, and several Washington titles carry place names: A Peach, Seville, of 1866, and A Pomegranate, Siena, of 1885, show a single piece of fruit, studied with affection. He was elected an associate of the National Academy of Design in 1857. Hall exhibited widely and was admired for the brilliant colour of his fruit. He died in New York in 1913.","q20853498":"Sturtevant J. Hamblin was an itinerant American portrait painter, active from the late 1830s into the 1850s; the dates 1776 to 1897 in the record are not his life. He painted children and families in a flat, bright, linear style, with solemn faces, firm outlines, clear colours and the particular details, a rabbit, an apple, a lace collar, that told the viewer something about the sitter. The five Washington pictures, Little Girl with Pet Rabbit, Little Girl Holding Apple, Sisters in Blue, Sisters in Red and The Younger Generation, are typical of this work. Itinerant painters of this kind often worked quickly and cheaply, moving from town to town as commissions allowed. Little else of his life can be said with confidence, though his pictures are much sought after today for their charm and directness.","q1710710":"Juan van der Hamen y León was born in Madrid in 1596, the son of a Flemish nobleman who served at the Spanish court and a Spanish mother, and he made his career at the court of Philip IV. He painted portraits and religious pictures, but is best remembered for still lifes, bodegones, in which fruit, sweets, glassware and pottery stand in clear rows on dark ledges. These calm and brilliantly lit arrangements, with a strong sense of space and texture, helped to found the Spanish still-life tradition in Madrid. He died young, in 1631. The Washington Still Life with Sweets and Pottery, dated 1627, is a fine example of his ordered style, in which, as the title says, sweets and pottery are set out with great clarity as a picture of the pleasures of the table.","q48319":"Hans Holbein the Younger, born in Augsburg in 1497, became the greatest portraitist of the northern Renaissance. After working in Basel, he went to London, and from 1536 he was King's Painter to Henry VIII, recording the Tudor court in drawings and paintings of extraordinary precision, among them the double portrait The Ambassadors of 1533. The Washington portrait of Sir Brian Tuke, Treasurer of the King's Chamber and Master of the Posts, is among his portraits of royal servants. His Edward VI as a Child, probably of 1538, is usually identified with the portrait that Holbein gave to Henry VIII as a New Year's gift in 1539, a likeness of the infant prince who would become king; the king rewarded the painter with a gilt cup. Holbein died in London in 1543.","q511444":"Hans Makart was born at Salzburg in 1840 and studied in Vienna and then in Munich under the history painter Karl von Piloty. In 1869 he settled in Vienna, where he became the leading painter of the Ringstrasse era, a master of vast, glowing allegories and historical pageants in sumptuous colour and sensual display, which pleased the new rich of the capital. His huge studio was a social centre, and his taste for rich fabrics, peacock feathers and dried flower bouquets became a style in its own right, the Makart style. In 1879 he directed the grand procession through Vienna in honour of the silver wedding of Emperor Franz Joseph and Empress Elisabeth. He died in Vienna in 1884, still in his forties. Forgotten for decades afterward, he is now seen as a forerunner of Klimt and the Viennese Secession.","q566772":"Hans Maler worked at Schwaz in the Tyrol, the silver-mining town that made the fortunes of mining entrepreneurs and the bankers who financed them, and is documented there in the early sixteenth century. He is known above all as a portrait painter: dated portraits survive from the 1510s and 1520s, many of them of bankers, mine owners and noblemen from southern Germany and the Habsburg circle. His portraits are bust-length, placed against a plain ground, with careful attention to the face and costume. The two New York portraits, Sebastian Andorfer, dated 1517, and Ulrich Fugger the Younger, dated 1525, belong to the best of this wealthy clientele. His name simply means Hans the painter, which suggests a craftsman of local standing rather than a celebrated individual, and his dates of birth and death are not securely known.","q106851":"Hans Memling was born at Seligenstadt, near Frankfurt, about 1435 and was probably trained in Brussels in the workshop of Rogier van der Weyden. By 1465 he was in Bruges, where he remained, the most sought-after painter of the city. His altarpieces, devotional triptychs and portraits, painted with serene faces, luminous colour and delicate landscapes, were bought by Bruges institutions such as the Hospital of Saint John, and by foreign merchants resident in the city, among them Tommaso Portinari, the Medici agent whose portrait with his wife is in New York. A Last Judgment triptych that Memling painted for another Italian, Angelo Tani, was captured at sea in 1473 by privateers from Danzig on its way to Florence, and it remains in Gdańsk to this day. Memling died in Bruges in 1494. His quiet manner was admired for centuries.","hans_mielich":"Hans Mielich was born in Munich in 1516 and spent his career there as court painter to the Dukes of Bavaria. He is said to have trained in Regensburg with Albrecht Altdorfer before returning to Munich in the 1540s. He was both a portraitist and a miniaturist: his portraits, among them the Cleveland Maria Kitscher, Frau von Freyberg, of 1545, are precise, richly dressed and strongly characterised. For Duke Albrecht V he illuminated the Penitential Psalms set to music by Orlande de Lassus, a famous manuscript now in Munich, and he made the Kleinodienbuch, a book of the jewels of Duchess Anna. He died in Munich in 1573. His miniatures of the Bavarian court are a valuable record of Renaissance Munich, and show the care for fine detail that also marks his portraits.","q48797":"Hans Süss von Kulmbach was born in Kulmbach, in Franconia, about 1480 and trained in Nuremberg, first with the Venetian Jacopo de' Barbari and then in Albrecht Dürer's workshop, where he became one of the closest assistants. His own manner is lighter and more decorative than Dürer's, with elegant, often elongated figures, gracious colour and a feeling for Italian forms that he may have owed to Barbari. He painted altarpieces for churches in Nuremberg, portraits, and designs for stained glass. About 1514 he went to Kraków, where he worked for churches and patrons of the Polish capital, returning to Nuremberg a few years later. He died there in 1522, only about forty years old. The New York Ascension of Christ, dated 1513, comes from the Nuremberg years, and the Portrait of a Young Man with the Girl Making a Garland on its reverse is an engaging double-sided work.","q675272":"Hans Schäufelein was born at Nuremberg or its neighbourhood about 1480 and trained in Albrecht Dürer's workshop about 1503 to 1507, working on altarpieces and designing woodcuts. After a period in Augsburg he settled at Nördlingen in Swabia in 1515, became a citizen, and painted for the town and its church of Saint George; he stayed there until his death about 1538 to 1540. He is most famous for his woodcuts, including a large share of the illustrations for the epic poem Theuerdank (1517), written to celebrate the Emperor Maximilian I. His paintings show Dürer's influence in their strong outlines and crowded, expressive scenes. The New York Dormition of the Virgin, on a panel with Christ Carrying the Cross on the back, dates from about 1510, soon after he left Dürer's workshop.","hardenstein_dirk":"Dirk Hardenstein was a Dutch painter of the seventeenth century whose life is poorly recorded; the dates given for him, 1620 to 1681, are the museum's. The one picture of his in this collection, Hauling the Net, dated 1663, belongs to the Dutch tradition of paintings of fishermen at work by the shore or on a river, a subject made popular by earlier landscape painters such as Esaias van de Velde and Jan van Goyen, and a natural one in a country whose wealth rested in part on the sea and its fisheries. Beyond the date, which shows that he was working in the 1660s, little can safely be said of his training, his home town or the people who bought his pictures, and it is better to say so than to guess.","q5093586":"Chester Harding was born at Conway, Massachusetts, in 1792, and came to painting late and almost without training. He worked as a drummer, a chair-maker, a tavern keeper and a house and sign painter before taking up portraits in his late twenties. Shortly before the old man's death in 1820 he painted the frontiersman Daniel Boone from life. After success in Pittsburgh and Kentucky he went to England in 1823, where his frank, vigorous likenesses made him the fashion in London society for three years. Back in Boston he painted leading American figures, among them John Randolph of Roanoke and Charles Carroll of Carrollton, the last surviving signer of the Declaration of Independence, whose portraits hang in Washington. He died in Boston in 1866. The Washington Self-Portrait, about 1825, shows a confident man who had risen by his own efforts.","hardy_jeremiah_pearson":"Jeremiah Pearson Hardy, an American painter of portraits and still lifes, is given the dates 1800 to 1888, but few details of his career are firmly recorded. The Washington picture, Two McLaughlin Pears, which has no date, belongs to the large tradition of fruit still life in nineteenth-century America, a subject much loved by painters and buyers alike because it required no grand patron and could be hung in any parlour or dining room. The McLaughlin pear was a named American variety, and the picture reads as a careful portrait of two particular fruit, painted with attention to bloom and blemish. Nothing else about the painter's training, home or patrons can be said with confidence, and the picture is best enjoyed for what it shows.","q935186":"William Michael Harnett was born in Clonakilty, Ireland, in 1848, and was brought as a baby to Philadelphia, where he learned silver engraving and studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts and in New York. From 1874 he devoted himself to still lifes of dazzling illusionism: pipes, mugs, violins, books, bank notes and game hanging on a door, painted so exactly that they seem real objects. In 1880 he went to Europe, working in London, Frankfurt, Munich and Paris, where he painted After the Hunt in the mid-1880s. Back in New York he painted The Old Violin in 1886 and My Gems in 1888. He died in 1892, aged forty-four. Largely forgotten after his death, he was rediscovered in the middle of the twentieth century and is now seen as the master of American trompe-l'oeil.","q187496":"Henri-Joseph Harpignies, born at Valenciennes in 1819, was one of the longest-lived painters of the nineteenth century, still working in his nineties and dying in 1916 at the age of ninety-seven. His career stretched from the Barbizon generation, with Corot as his model and friend, to the eve of the First World War, and in his later decades he was honoured as a venerable master of the French landscape tradition. The New York Moonrise, of 1885, is an evening scene, a type that suited his taste for broad, quiet effects of light on trees and water. He was also a distinguished watercolourist and made many drawings of trees, and he remained faithful to the manner he had formed in mid-century, long after fashion had moved on to Impressionism and beyond.","q22343684":"Harvey Otis Young was an American landscape painter, born in Vermont in 1840, who is said to have gone west in the Colorado gold rush of 1859 and became one of the early painters of the Rocky Mountains. He studied in Europe, and he is said to have brought home from France a taste for the soft, atmospheric effects of the Barbizon painters. He later settled in Colorado, where he taught art, and he died in 1901. The New York Sunset, dated about 1870 to 1880, with its glowing sky, belongs to the long American tradition of sunset pictures, a theme that painters of the Hudson River School and their followers used to express wonder, calm and the passing of time. Further details of his career are not given here.","q2762575":"William Stanley Haseltine was born in Philadelphia in 1835 and studied at Harvard before turning to art. He trained in Düsseldorf and travelled in Switzerland and Italy, and was in Rome in the 1850s; after some years in New York he settled again in Rome in 1869 and made it his home for the rest of his life. He is known for rocky coasts painted with the exactness of a geologist and the light of a poet: the sea breaking on cliffs at Capri, Narragansett Bay, the coast of New England. His friend Sanford Gifford and others of the Hudson River School shared his love of clear air. Haseltine's studio in Rome was a gathering place for American visitors. His son Herbert became an animal sculptor. He died in Rome in 1900. The Washington Natural Arch at Capri, dated 1871, is among his best-known pictures.","q21497639":"A. Hashagen is known here only through one picture, Ship Arkansas Leaving Havana, painted in 1847, and the dates 1767 to 1907 given in the record are plainly not a lifespan. The painting belongs to the tradition of the ship portrait, in which an owner or captain commissioned an exact picture of a vessel under sail, with every sail and flag in order, often shown leaving or entering a well-known harbour. Havana was a busy port for American merchant ships in the nineteenth century, and a picture of a ship departing from it was a natural souvenir for the people involved in the voyage. Nothing certain is known of Hashagen's training, nationality or place of work, and it is better to say so than to guess.","q737635":"Childe Hassam was born in Dorchester, Massachusetts, in 1859, and began as a wood-engraver and illustrator in Boston. In 1886 he went to Paris, studied at the Académie Julian and absorbed the light and colour of the Impressionists, and on his return to America in 1889 he became the leading painter of American Impressionism. He helped found the group called Ten American Painters in 1898, and painted streets in New York, Boston and Paris, the New England coast and gardens. For many summers he stayed with the poet Celia Thaxter on the Isles of Shoals, off New Hampshire and Maine, and Poppies, Isles of Shoals, of 1891, in Washington, shows her flower garden there. In 1916 to 1919 he painted a series of flag-filled avenues in New York. He died in 1935, leaving a great number of works to the American Academy of Arts and Letters.","q21403127":"George A. Hayes is known only as the painter of Bare Knuckles, dated about 1870 to 1885, and the dates 1805 to 1930 given in the record are clearly not a lifespan. The picture shows a prizefight fought without gloves, a sport that was illegal in many American states and usually held out of town before a crowd of men, and that gave way in the late nineteenth century to boxing under the Queensberry rules; the last heavyweight championship fought bare-knuckle, between John L. Sullivan and Jake Kilrain, took place in Mississippi in 1889. Hayes may have been a self-taught painter of everyday American scenes, but nothing is securely recorded of his training, home or patrons, and the picture is best read as a rare record of a rough pastime.","q3123472":"Martin Johnson Heade was born in Lumberville, Pennsylvania, in 1819 and studied with a local painter before travelling to Europe in the 1840s and settling in the American cities, including New York and Boston. He painted portraits, then marsh landscapes with tall haystacks under still or stormy skies, such as Approaching Thunder Storm, of 1859, and Newburyport Meadows, in an almost glassy light that later critics called luminist. In 1863 to 1864 he travelled to Brazil to paint hummingbirds, and he later made lush pictures of orchids, passionflowers and apple blossoms with the little birds, together with magnolias on velvet. In 1883 he settled in St Augustine, Florida, where he died in 1904. His reputation, modest in his lifetime, was revived in the twentieth century.","q454656":"Jan Davidsz de Heem was born in Utrecht in 1606 and trained there before working in Leiden in the 1620s and settling about 1636 in Antwerp, where he joined the Guild of St Luke and lived for most of his remaining life. He became the most admired still-life painter of his day, combining Dutch precision with Flemish abundance in sumptuous banquet pieces of silver cups, glasses, oysters, lemons and fruit, and in great garlands of flowers. Collectors and princes paid high prices for his pictures, and his son Cornelis and his pupil Abraham Mignon carried on his manner. The Met's two works of about 1640 are a still life with a glass and oysters and a banqueting scene. He died around 1683.","q538350":"Maarten van Heemskerck was born in 1498 in the village of Heemskerck, near Haarlem, the son of a farmer, Jacob Willemsz van Veen. He trained in Haarlem and Delft and then with Jan van Scorel, whose Italian-influenced art opened his eyes to the Renaissance. His portrait of his father, dated 1532, painted shortly before he left for Italy, shows an old farmer with a startling frankness, down to every wrinkle. After four years in Rome he returned to Haarlem in 1537 and became the leading painter there, a prolific designer of Biblical and moralising prints and altarpieces that carried Roman Mannerist forms into Dutch art. He died in 1574, leaving a legacy to the city and the church of Saint Bavo.","q21856816":"Heinrich Franz Schalck is a little-recorded German painter of the early nineteenth century; the museum gives his dates as 1791 to 1832, which would make him a young man when he painted the New York double portrait of Joseph and Karl August von Klein, dated about 1810 to 1815. The picture belongs to the years of the Napoleonic wars, when German portraitists, often trained in the academies of Munich, Vienna or Dresden, painted the educated middle class and minor nobility with clear outlines, restrained colour and fine attention to dress and furnishings. Nothing certain about Schalck's training, home town or other works is given here, and it would be wrong to guess; he is known to this book only through this picture and the dates attached to his name.","q213739":"Heinrich Friedrich Füger was born at Heilbronn in 1751 and trained in Stuttgart, Leipzig and Dresden before going to Vienna in 1774. After years in Rome and Naples in the late 1770s and early 1780s he returned to Vienna as a leading Neoclassical painter and a very gifted miniaturist, and in 1795 became director of the Academy of Fine Arts, and later keeper of the imperial picture gallery. He painted portraits of the Habsburg family and of the Viennese aristocracy, as well as large history pictures in the clear, cool style of the antique-inspired school. The New York portrait of Maria Louisa, wife of the Emperor Leopold II, from about 1790, is a good example of his courtly manner. He died in Vienna in 1818.","q1237823":"Hendrick Sorgh, whose full name was Hendrick Martensz. Sorgh, was a painter of Rotterdam who lived from about 1610 to 1670, though the museum records no dates. He is said to have been a pupil of Willem Buytewech in Rotterdam and of David Teniers the Younger in Antwerp, and he painted kitchens, market scenes, peasant interiors and, later, harbour and river views and portraits, becoming a respected figure in his city. His kitchens, with their still-life details of pots, vegetables and fish, are among his best-known works, and A Kitchen in New York, about 1643, belongs to the group. Sorgh's pictures were popular in his lifetime and have remained valued by collectors of Dutch genre painting.","q709393":"Hendrick van Steenwijck the Younger was born about 1580 and followed his father, Hendrick van Steenwijck the Elder, as a specialist in architectural painting, picturing the interiors of churches and the courtyards and halls of imagined Renaissance palaces in careful perspective. Like many Netherlandish painters of his day, he moved between cities, and he is said to have worked in Germany, the Netherlands and England. Such pictures were often painted in collaboration, with the architecture by Steenwijck and the small figures by a figure painter, a practice common in Antwerp. The New York Renaissance Portico with Elegant Figures, about 1615, with its colonnade and courtly figures, shows his fascination with classical architecture, a taste for the new Italian style then reaching the north. The dates 1580 to 1649 are those the museum records.","q864173":"Hendrick van Vliet was born at Delft about 1611 and made his career there, joining the Delft guild of Saint Luke in the 1630s and dying in 1675. He was one of the Delft painters of church interiors, the group that included Gerard Houckgeest and Emanuel de Witte, who painted the whitewashed, sunlit interiors of the Oude Kerk and Nieuwe Kerk with small figures and with the tomb of William the Silent as their most celebrated landmark. He began with portraits and moved to church views in the 1650s, painting them in strong perspective and clear, cool light. The New York Interior of the Oude Kerk, Delft, dated 1660, belongs to his best period. These pictures were bought by citizens proud of their city and its monuments, and give a vivid record of the Delft churches.","hendrik_dirk_kruseman_van_elten":"Hendrik Dirk Kruseman van Elten was born at Alkmaar, in the Netherlands, in 1829, and emigrated to the United States in the 1860s, where he became an American landscape painter, working in the manner of the Hudson River School. He painted woodland interiors, streams, meadows and farm country in the Hudson valley, New Jersey and Connecticut, in soft green and brown tones that suited American collectors who wished for a gentle, homely view of nature. He died in 1904. The Cleveland Landscape near Granby, Connecticut, of the 1870s, shows the kind of ordinary New England farmland he painted: a quiet scene, with a feeling for light and air. Little is recorded in detail of his training in the Netherlands or of his patrons in America.","hendrik_gerritsz_pot":"Hendrick Gerritsz. Pot was born in Amsterdam about 1585, and he spent most of his career in Haarlem, where he joined the painters' guild in the early 1610s. He painted portraits, group portraits of civic guard companies and lively genre scenes of merry companies, and also allegories, in a style close to Frans Hals's in the 1620s. In the early 1630s he travelled to London, where he painted Charles I and Queen Henrietta Maria, and a portrait of the king by him is now in the Louvre. About 1650 he moved to Amsterdam, where he died in 1657. The Cleveland Portrait of a Woman with a Dog, about 1635, is typical of his mature portraits: a well-dressed, dignified sitter, with her pet, in a plain interior. Though now a minor name, he was well regarded in his lifetime.","hendrik_van_balen":"Hendrik van Balen was born in Antwerp in 1575, studied with Adam van Noort, the teacher of Rubens and Jordaens, and became a master in the Antwerp guild in 1593, rising to its dean in 1609. He ran a large workshop and is best known as the first master of the young Anthony van Dyck, who worked with him about 1609 to 1615. He painted altarpieces, mythologies and, above all, small cabinet pictures, with graceful figures in bright colours and luminous skin. He often painted the figures in landscapes and flower garlands by other specialists, among them Jan Brueghel the Elder and Joos de Momper. He died in Antwerp in 1632. The Cleveland Trinity, about 1620, is a devotional work in the fluent, courtly Antwerp manner of the years around 1620.","q665818":"Jean-Jacques Henner was born in 1829 at Bernwiller in Alsace and trained in Strasbourg and then in Paris, under Michel Martin Drolling and François-Édouard Picot, before winning the Prix de Rome in 1858. He spent five years in Italy, where the soft modelling of Correggio and Leonardo left a lasting mark. He became known for pale, dreamy nudes, often with red hair, emerging from dark, misty backgrounds, such as the Met's Bather (Echo) of 1881, and for religious and mythological subjects, and he was a sought-after portraitist. After Alsace was lost to Germany in 1871 he painted a patriotic Alsace, She Waits. A Paris museum now bears his name. He died in 1905.","q82445":"Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec was born at Albi in 1864 into an old aristocratic family. He broke both thighs in adolescence and his legs stopped growing, leaving him short in stature but not in energy. Moving to Paris in 1882, he studied with Léon Bonnat and Fernand Cormon and settled in Montmartre, the district of cabarets, dance halls and brothels that became his subject. He drew and painted entertainers and prostitutes with sharp, unsentimental economy of line and sudden angles learned from Degas and from Japanese prints. His poster for the Moulin Rouge in 1891 made him famous almost overnight, and his lithographs of Jane Avril, Yvette Guilbert and Aristide Bruant defined the image of Parisian night life. Drink undermined his health, and he died at his mother's château of Malromé in 1901, aged thirty-six. The Metropolitan's Englishman at the Moulin Rouge dates from 1892.","q16038756":"Henri Joseph van Blarenberghe was born in 1750 into a family of painters from Lille, and belonged to a line of artists who specialised in small, precise views, often in gouache, of military campaigns, ports and ceremonies, some of them made for snuffboxes and cabinets. Painters of this kind were valued for their minute accuracy, which made their work a kind of visual reportage for the court and the nobility. The New York Outer Harbor of Brest, dated 1773, is a view of the great naval port in Brittany, with ships, quays and busy figures drawn with patient attention to detail. Henri Joseph lived until 1826, long enough to see the Revolution and the Empire. Little more is recorded of his career in the information at hand.","q240483":"Henri Lehmann was born at Kiel in 1814 and went to Paris as a young man to study with Ingres, whose clear drawing and polished surface he made his own. He became a French citizen in 1847 and made his career in France, painting portraits, religious pictures and large decorative schemes. His portraits of the pianist Franz Liszt, painted in 1839, and of Liszt's companion, the writer Marie d'Agoult, are among the best-known likenesses of that circle, and the New York portrait of Faustine Léo, of 1842, a young woman, comes from the same years. He also taught foreign students in Paris. He died there in 1882. His work is polished, careful and a little cool, in the manner of his master, and the New York Study of a Female Nude, of 1840, shows his schooling in Ingres's careful drawing.","q3131613":"Henri Mauperché was a French landscape painter, born, according to the museum, in 1602 and dead in 1686, who worked in Paris in the age of Louis XIII and Louis XIV. Little is recorded in detail of his life; he is associated with the French taste for classical landscape, in which tall trees, ruins and winding paths frame small figures from the Bible or mythology, a taste fed by the Italian landscapes of Paul Bril and by the work of Poussin and Claude Lorrain in Rome. The New York Classical Landscape with Figures shows that taste: a calm, orderly scene, with a few small figures set among buildings and trees. Beyond these outlines, nothing certain can be added without guessing, and the picture is best valued as an example of the classical landscape taste of seventeenth-century France.","henri_pierre_danloux":"Henri-Pierre Danloux was born in Paris in 1753 and studied with the history painter Joseph-Marie Vien, going on to Rome in the 1770s. He became a fashionable portraitist in Paris in the 1780s, with a soft and elegant touch. When the Revolution began he left France as a royalist, and from 1792 until 1801 he lived in London, painting portraits of French émigrés and English sitters, and he kept a journal of the exile that is a valuable record. The Cleveland Portrait of a Mother and Daughter, about 1794 to 1795, belongs to these London years. He returned to France in 1801 and died in Paris in 1809. His portraits, with their sensitivity and relaxed poses, show the transition from the Rococo grace of the old regime to a simpler, more natural manner.","q553571":"Henri Regnault was born in Paris in 1843, the son of a famous chemist, and trained with Cabanel and Gérôme, winning the Prix de Rome in 1866. In Rome he painted Automedon with the Horses of Achilles, and in 1868 he travelled to Spain, where he fell under the spell of Goya and Velázquez and the strong colours of Granada and the Alhambra. He then went to North Africa, and the Orientalist pictures of these years show a brilliant, daring technique and a taste for violent, exotic subjects. Regnault was killed on 19 January 1871 at the battle of Buzenval, defending Paris against the Prussians, at the age of twenty-seven, and he was remembered as a patriotic martyr. The New York Salome, shown at the Salon of 1870, is among his best-known works.","q15453845":"Henry Alexander is known here only through the dates 1860 to 1894, which show that he died young, at about thirty-four, and through one picture, In the Laboratory, dated about 1885 to 1887, in the Metropolitan Museum. The title points to a scene of scientific work, a subject that attracted a few American painters of the 1880s, an age that admired the achievements of science and medicine, and the picture belongs to the wider fashion for genre painting with figures at work, painted with careful attention to instruments and light. Nothing certain about his training, home town or other works is recorded here, and the museum's date range is all that can be safely stated.","q21994084":"Henry Augustus Loop was an American painter, 1831 to 1895, who is associated with New York and is remembered for portraits and figure subjects in the years after the Civil War. The Metropolitan's picture shows John Quincy Adams Ward, painted in 1863, a sculptor who became one of the leading American sculptors of the century and who made, among other works, the statue of George Washington at Federal Hall in New York. A portrait of a fellow artist suggests that Loop moved in the city's artistic circles. Little else is recorded of Loop's career in the information here, and it would be wrong to guess at his training, his patrons or the extent of his work.","q5718019":"Henry Benbridge was born in Philadelphia in 1743 and went to Italy in 1765, where he studied with Pompeo Batoni in Rome and painted the Corsican patriot Pasquale Paoli. He returned to America in 1770 and settled in Charleston, South Carolina, where he became the leading portrait painter of the city, painting planters, merchants and their wives with the polish of the Italian and English schools. He remained in Charleston through the Revolution and died in 1812. The six Metropolitan portraits, including Mrs. Benjamin Simons, show his composed, graceful style. Benbridge's career shows how European training, gained in Rome under a famous master, reached the American colonies, and how a painter who had studied abroad could build a practice among the planters and merchants of a prosperous port.","q5196210":"Henry Bone was born at Truro, in Cornwall, in 1755 and trained as a painter of porcelain at Plymouth and Bristol before moving to London in 1779. There he became the foremost enamel painter of his age, working in the old craft of painting on small plates of metal coated with glass and fired in a kiln, which gave rich colour that does not fade. He was appointed enamel painter to George III, then to the Prince of Wales, who as George IV continued to employ him, and he was elected to the Royal Academy in 1811. His celebrated large enamel after Titian's Bacchus and Ariadne took several years to finish. The six New York enamels, portraits after Van Dyck, Rubens, Hoppner, Lawrence and Gérard, show his mastery of small-scale copying. He died in 1834.","henry_church":"Henry Church was an American painter, given the dates 1836 to 1908, of whom little is recorded here. His Self-Portrait with Five Muses, of about 1880, in Cleveland, shows the artist in the company of five of the nine Muses, the classical goddesses of the arts, an ambitious and unusual allegory in which a painter presents himself as favoured by the divine patrons of his craft. Self-portraits of this kind belong to a long tradition, going back to the Renaissance, in which artists claimed a place among scholars and poets. Nothing certain is recorded about Church's training, patrons or other works, and a longer life of him cannot honestly be written from the information at hand.","q16063027":"Henry Colton Shumway was born in 1807 and died in 1884, and was an American painter of miniatures and portraits. He appears to have worked chiefly in New York, where he painted the city's merchants, artists and their families in small, finely finished portraits, in the years when the miniature was still a favourite keepsake and before the photograph replaced it. The six Metropolitan portraits include the painter Henry Peters Gray, painted in 1842, and several other sitters of the 1830s and 1840s, among them William Boerum and Catherine Augusta Wilmerding, and show a smooth, careful and flattering style. Little more is recorded of him here than the picture of a successful craftsman in a busy trade, which is itself a useful reminder of how much early American portraiture was made by minor but competent hands.","q22688157":"Henry I. Brown is recorded only as active from 1837 to 1851, and nothing certain is known of his birth, training or home. The Metropolitan's portrait shows Benjamin Daniel Greene and is dated 1848. It belongs to the large body of American portraiture made before photography took over the market for likenesses in the 1850s, when painters travelled or settled in towns and cities to supply families, officials and professional men with a record of their appearance at modest cost. No patrons, exhibitions or other works can be named here with confidence, and the dates of activity are the only firm facts about the painter in the information available, so a fuller life cannot honestly be written.","q3132855":"Henry Inman was born at Utica, New York, in 1801 and apprenticed at fourteen to the portrait painter John Wesley Jarvis in New York. He became one of the leading portraitists in the city, a founder in 1826 of the National Academy of Design and its vice-president, and he painted statesmen, writers and actors, including President Martin Van Buren and the English tragedian William Charles Macready as William Tell. He also painted genre scenes, landscapes and miniatures, among them The Young Fisherman of about 1829. In 1844 he went to England to paint Wordsworth, Macaulay and other notables. His health was poor, and he died in New York in 1846. His portraits, including the Betts family pictures in Cleveland, are fluent, graceful and dignified, and the twelve Metropolitan and Cleveland works show the full range of his career.","q2625979":"Henry Lerolle was born in Paris in 1848, trained with Louis Lamothe, a pupil of Ingres, and exhibited at the Salon from the 1870s. He painted religious and musical subjects in a quiet, harmonious manner, as well as portraits and decorations, and he is perhaps better remembered as a collector and friend of artists. His house in Paris was a meeting place for Degas, Renoir, the composer Ernest Chausson, who married his wife's sister, and Claude Debussy, and he owned works by Degas, Renoir and Cézanne; Renoir painted his daughters, Yvonne and Christine, at the piano in 1897. The New York Organ Rehearsal, of 1885, with its musical subject, links his two loves, painting and music. He died in 1929.","q5726826":"Henry Pelham was born in Boston in 1749, the half-brother of the painter John Singleton Copley, whose mother had married the engraver Peter Pelham. He grew up in Copley's household and learned from him, painting portraits and a Finding of Moses, and he also engraved. His print of the Boston Massacre of 1770 was copied by Paul Revere, to Pelham's furious protest. A loyalist in the troubled years before the Revolution, he made an engraved plan of Boston in 1777 and left for England, and later moved to Ireland, where he worked as an agent for the estates of the Lansdowne family in County Kerry and died in 1806. The Metropolitan's portraits, Stephen Hooper, of 1773, and a Portrait of a Gentleman, of 1779, reflect his training in Copley's household.","q9002959":"Henry Peters Gray was born in New York in 1819 and trained there with Daniel Huntington, then spent several years in Italy from 1838 to 1841, copying and admiring Titian and the Venetians. He returned to a career as the painter of poetic, literary and allegorical subjects, in rich colour and smooth finish, which were very popular with American collectors of his day and were widely reproduced in engravings: The Greek Lovers, of 1846, and The Wages of War, of 1848, are in New York, as is The Pride of the Village, of 1858 to 1859. He was elected to the National Academy of Design in 1842 and served as its president from 1869 to 1871. He died in New York in 1877. His idealised, sweet manner fell out of fashion after his death.","q5729856":"Henry Ward Ranger was born at Syracuse, New York, in 1858 and studied in Europe, where he absorbed the Barbizon painters of France and the Dutch landscapists of the seventeenth century. He painted woods, marshes and shores in soft, dark, brownish tones and thick, heavily worked paint, a tonalist approach that stressed mood over detail. In 1899 he began visiting the art colony at Old Lyme, Connecticut, and became one of its leading figures. He died in 1916 and left money for the Ranger Fund, through which the National Academy of Design has bought American paintings for public collections. The Cleveland Lone Sentinel and Seascape, and the New York Spring Woods, show his range from coast to forest.","q20127277":"Henry Williams was born in 1787 and died in 1830, and is associated with Boston. He worked as a painter of portraits and miniatures, and the six Metropolitan pictures show him as a painter of apparently New England sitters of the 1810s and 1820s: couples such as John Cox and Mrs. John Cox, and men such as Edward Coverly and Gorham L. Freeman, shown in plain dress, with a direct likeness and careful treatment of face and costume. He belongs to the large generation of American portraitists, many of them trained in a workshop rather than an academy, who supplied a growing middle class with likenesses before photography. Little else is recorded of his training or career here, and he is a modest and honest practitioner of a modest and honest trade.","q21498157":"Salome Hensel is known here only by her name and the work To the Memory of the Benevolent Howard, dated 1823, and the dates 1743 to 1883 in the record are plainly not a lifespan. The subject is John Howard, the English philanthropist and prison reformer, who died in 1790 at Kherson, in southern Russia, while investigating epidemic disease, and who was honoured in America as a model of Christian benevolence. Memorial pictures of this kind, often made by girls and women at school or at home in watercolour or needlework, were a popular form of commemoration in the early American republic. Nothing certain is known of Hensel's age, home or training, and the work is best valued as a tribute from a private person to a public hero.","herman_van_swanevelt":"Herman van Swanevelt was born at Woerden, in the Netherlands, in 1600 and became a landscape painter of the Italianate type, working in Rome from about 1629 to 1641, where he was known as Herman d'Italie and belonged to the club of Dutch and Flemish artists called the Bentvueghels. He moved in the circle of Claude Lorrain, whose golden light and classical ruins he followed, and he also etched landscapes of great charm. In 1641 he settled in Paris, where he worked for patrons at court and became a member of the Academy in 1651. He died in Paris in 1655. The Cleveland Landscape with Travelers, of the 1630s, belongs to the Roman years: a gentle landscape with small figures in a warm, hazy light.","herri_met_de_bles":"Herri met de Bles was a Flemish landscape painter, born about 1510 at Bouvignes or Dinant, in the Meuse valley, and recorded in the Antwerp guild in 1535. He is called a follower of Joachim Patinir, whose high-viewpoint world landscapes he took up and made his own: rocky peaks, rivers and fields stretching to a misty blue horizon, with tiny biblical figures such as Saint John the Baptist placed among them. His name means Herri with the tuft, said to refer to a white lock of hair; he hid a small owl in many of his pictures as a kind of signature, so that Italian writers called him Civetta, the owl. He is last recorded in the 1550s, and the date of his death is unknown, though the museum gives 1572. His work was admired and collected in Italy as well as in the Netherlands.","q370567":"Jan van der Heyden was born in Gorinchem in 1637 and settled in Amsterdam, where he became one of the most precise painters of townscapes and buildings, rendering every brick, window and leaf with astonishing clarity, though he often combined real buildings into imaginary views. He was also an inventor: in 1669 he was put in charge of Amsterdam's street lighting and designed improved lamps, and with his brother he developed a flexible fire hose and a more efficient fire engine, publishing a book on fire-fighting equipment in 1690. The Met's two views of the Huis ten Bosch at The Hague and its formal garden, about 1668 to 1670, show his gift for architecture and clean light. He died in Amsterdam in 1712.","q1707243":"Joseph Highmore was born in London in 1692, nephew of the court painter Thomas Highmore, and started in the law before turning to art, studying at Sir Godfrey Kneller's academy. He became one of the best portrait painters in England between Kneller and Reynolds, painting the gentry, clergymen and actors in a clear, graceful, rather French manner, and also conversation pieces. His most famous work is a series of twelve scenes from Samuel Richardson's novel Pamela, painted in 1743 to 1744, in which he turned a bestselling book into pictures at a time when the English public was mad for the story. He retired to Canterbury and died in 1780, a long-lived survivor of the generation of Hogarth. The Washington Portrait of a Lady, about 1730 to 1735, shows his polished early manner.","q21498006":"John Hilling is known here by the dates 1822 to 1894 and by one picture, the Burning of Old South Church, Bath, Maine, painted about 1854; the museum calls him British, and nothing else of his life, training or career is firmly recorded. The picture belongs to a lively tradition of American paintings and prints of fires, a spectacle that fascinated nineteenth-century townspeople, and in which flames at night give the painter a chance to use strong contrasts of dark and light, with crowds of onlookers and firemen in the foreground. Pictures of local disasters of this kind were often painted close to the event as a record for those who had lived through it. Beyond these outlines, nothing certain can be said of Hilling.","q336908":"Meindert Hobbema was born in Amsterdam in 1638 and is recorded in 1660 as having been with Jacob van Ruisdael for some years, whose woodland scenes he followed. His own pictures show winding roads and village paths among tall trees, with ponds, cottages and sparkling watermills lit by breaks of sunlight, painted with a love of detail and a fresh, clear light. In 1668 he married and took up a post as a wine gauger for the Amsterdam city customs, and he painted much less afterward, though he produced one late masterpiece, The Avenue at Middelharnis, in 1689. He died in Amsterdam in 1709. Neglected in the Netherlands, he was loved in eighteenth-century England, where his tree-lined landscapes influenced painters such as Gainsborough and Constable.","hoecke_jan_van_den":"Jan van den Hoecke was born in Antwerp in 1611, the brother of the battle painter Robert van den Hoecke, and became a pupil and assistant of Rubens, whose broad, fleshy style marks his work. After a stay in Italy he worked for the Habsburg court, notably for Archduke Leopold Wilhelm, governor of the Spanish Netherlands, for whom he painted allegories and portraits. He died in 1651, in his early forties. The Washington Judgment of Midas, about 1640, takes its subject from Ovid: King Midas, judging a musical contest between Apollo and Pan, preferred Pan, and Apollo gave him a donkey's ears. His pictures are lively and handsome, in the heroic manner of Rubens's Antwerp, and he remains a lesser-known member of the master's circle.","q171344":"William Hogarth was born in London in 1697 and was apprenticed to a silver-plate engraver before setting up as an engraver and then a painter. He studied in the academy of Sir James Thornhill, whose daughter Jane he secretly married in 1729. His fame rests on his modern moral subjects, series of paintings turned into engravings that told stories of vice and folly in contemporary London: A Harlot's Progress, A Rake's Progress and Marriage A-la-Mode. He also painted portraits, conversation pieces and scenes from the theatre, among them several versions of a scene from John Gay's Beggar's Opera, the stage hit of 1728. He fought for artists' rights, and the Engravers' Copyright Act of 1735 is sometimes called Hogarth's Act. He died in London in 1764. He was the first great English-born painter of his century.","q344838":"Winslow Homer was born in Boston in 1836, apprenticed to a lithographer, and made his name as an illustrator for Harper's Weekly, which sent him to the front in 1861 to sketch the Union army in the Civil War. He took up oil painting in earnest in the 1860s, and after a stay in Paris in 1866 to 1867 he turned to American subjects: children at school and play, farm life, the seaside and the Adirondack woods. In the 1870s he mastered watercolour. After an English year at Cullercoats, on the North Sea, in 1881 to 1882, which turned his attention to men and women at work against the sea, he settled in 1883 at Prouts Neck, Maine, where he lived until his death in 1910. Breezing Up, of 1873 to 1876, in Washington, is among the best-loved of American paintings.","q3139663":"Homer Dodge Martin was born at Albany, New York, in 1836 and began as a painter in the manner of the Hudson River School, studying briefly with James M. Hart and painting the Adirondacks and Lake Champlain. After years in New York he travelled to England and then to Normandy, where he lived from 1881 until 1886 and where his style became softer, more tonal and more poetic under the influence of Barbizon and of Corot. His late landscapes of the Seine, painted from memory in the 1890s as his sight was failing, are his best known, among them View on the Seine: Harp of the Winds, in New York. He died in St. Paul, Minnesota, in 1897. Though he never became rich or famous in his lifetime, later painters admired his mood and his quiet, tonal harmonies.","q314548":"Gerrit van Honthorst was born in Utrecht in 1592 and trained with Abraham Bloemaert before going to Rome about 1610, where he worked for the Marchese Vincenzo Giustiniani and Cardinal Scipione Borghese and became known for night scenes lit by a single candle, which earned him the nickname Gherardo delle Notti, Gerard of the Nights. He came back to Utrecht in 1620 and was one of the leading Caravaggisti there, painting half-length musicians, merry companies and religious subjects. In 1628 he was invited to England by Charles I and painted the king and queen as Apollo and Diana. He later served as a court painter at The Hague, where he taught drawing to the children of Elizabeth of Bohemia, and he also worked for the Danish king. He died in Utrecht in 1656. The Washington Concert, of 1623, is a good example of his Utrecht style.","q314889":"Pieter de Hooch was born in Rotterdam in 1629 and trained there, probably with the landscape painter Nicolaes Berchem. In 1652 he was a painter and servant in the household of a cloth merchant, Justus de la Grange, and he followed him to Delft, where he joined the painters' guild in 1655. His best pictures date from the Delft years, until about 1661: sunlit courtyards and quiet rooms, with women and children at their daily tasks, painted with a clear sense of perspective and a tender feeling for light falling through a doorway. Delft was also the city of Vermeer, with whom he has often been compared. He moved to Amsterdam about 1661, where his subjects became grander and darker, and he died there in 1684, in an asylum. The Washington pictures include A Dutch Courtyard and The Bedroom.","q21498111":"Milton W. Hopkins was an American portrait painter, given the dates 1789 to 1844, who worked in the plain, linear manner of the early nineteenth-century folk portraitists. His Washington picture, Aphia Salisbury Rich and Baby Edward, about 1833, is a portrait of a mother and her infant son. Folk portraits of the period typically favour firm outlines, flat colour and close attention to costume, and were often painted quickly, in or near the sitter's home, so that families without great wealth could have a likeness. They were treasured as family records long after their painters were forgotten, and are now prized by collectors of early American art. Little else about Hopkins's life, training or home is recorded here, and it would be wrong to say more than the museum does.","q326066":"John Hoppner was born in London in 1758, of German parents, and as a boy sang in the Chapel Royal; George III took an interest in him, and a persistent rumour held that Hoppner was the king's natural son. He studied at the Royal Academy Schools, winning a gold medal in 1782, and after the Prince of Wales gave him his patronage he became, with Thomas Lawrence as his rival, the most fashionable portrait painter in England after Reynolds's death in 1792. He painted ladies and children with a warm, slightly romantic touch and was elected a Royal Academician in 1795. The Met's Sackville Children, of 1796, and Mrs. John Garden and Her Children show his charm. He died in 1810.","q18671476":"Horace Hone was a British miniature painter, born in the 1750s, the son of the painter Nathaniel Hone, and he followed his father in the profession. He is recorded in London and Dublin and exhibited at the Royal Academy, and he became a successful painter of portrait miniatures and enamels for the aristocracy and the royal family; he is recorded as miniature painter to the Prince of Wales. The two Metropolitan pictures, a Portrait of a Woman said to be Lady Agnes Anne Wrothesley, of 1791, and a Portrait of a Gentleman, of 1807, show his careful, elegant style on a small scale. He died in 1825. The museum gives no birth date, and the details of his training and career are only partly recorded.","q161145":"Horace Vernet was born in Paris in 1789, the third in a dynasty of painters, grandson of the marine painter Joseph Vernet and son of the horse and battle painter Carle Vernet. He became a close friend of Géricault, a lover of horses and a champion of Napoleon, and he painted soldiers and battles with such detail and verve that he became a favourite painter of the Orléans family; Louis-Philippe commissioned from him huge pictures of French victories for the Galerie des Batailles at Versailles. He directed the French Academy in Rome from 1829 to 1835, and made journeys to Algeria and Russia, drawing and painting Arab warriors, Mamelukes and Cossacks. He died in Paris in 1863. The Metropolitan's Start of the Race of the Riderless Horses, of 1820, shows the Roman Carnival horse race.","q548205":"Hubert Robert was born in Paris in 1733 and spent eleven years, from 1754 to 1765, in Rome, where he learned from Panini and became a friend of Fragonard, with whom he drew in the Roman countryside and at Tivoli. His specialty was views of ancient ruins, mixed with imagined buildings, caves, grottoes and fountains, with small laundresses, shepherds and strollers, a taste that earned him the nickname Robert des Ruines. Back in Paris he became a favourite of collectors and a designer of gardens, notably at Méréville. He was named keeper of the king's pictures in 1778 and was involved in plans for the Louvre. During the Terror of 1793 to 1794 he was imprisoned for about ten months and went on painting in his cell. He died in Paris in 1808. The Cleveland and New York pictures show his ruins, colonnades and cascades.","q21403134":"Jurgan Frederick Huge, an American painter given the dates 1809 to 1878, is known here by one picture, the Composite Harbor Scene with Castle, of about 1875. As its title suggests, the view is a composite, an imaginary harbour put together from elements of several places and from the painter's imagination, with a castle on the shore. Such compositions were popular with self-taught painters of the nineteenth century, whose pictures were often made from prints, memories and fancy rather than from observation, and are valued today for their charm and directness. Little else is recorded of Huge's life, training or patrons in the information here, and a fuller account cannot honestly be written.","q20887106":"Hugh Bridport was born in England and worked as a portrait painter and miniaturist in the United States, where he appears to have been active chiefly in Philadelphia in the first half of the nineteenth century. The museum gives no dates. His sitters included Joel Roberts Poinsett, the diplomat and botanist from South Carolina after whom the poinsettia is named, painted in 1843. The three Metropolitan portraits, Carolyn Mishner, about 1825, Poinsett, and a Portrait of a Lady, about 1835, show the careful, smooth style of a competent likeness-maker in the years before photography. He is a reminder of the many English-born painters and engravers who made their careers in America in the early republic.","q215251":"Hugo van der Goes was born about 1440 and became a master in the painters' guild of Ghent in 1467; in 1474 he was its dean. He was the most powerful painter in the Netherlands in the 1470s, with a monumental, emotionally intense style, strong in character and colour, and his masterpiece, the Portinari Altarpiece, painted about 1475 for Tommaso Portinari, the Medici agent in Bruges, was sent to Florence, where its realism and size deeply impressed Italian painters. In the late 1470s he entered the Red Cloister, a monastery near Brussels, as a lay brother, but he continued to paint. A deep depression, described by a monk of the house, Gaspar Ofhuys, overtook him on a journey in 1481, and he died in 1482. The two New York portraits of men, about 1470 to 1475, are attributed to him.","q5934744":"Hugues Merle was born at La Sône, in the Dauphiné, in 1823, and trained in Paris with the painter Léon Cogniet. He began exhibiting at the Salon in the 1840s and made his career with polished, sentimental pictures of mothers and children, young peasant girls and idealised women, in the smooth academic manner of the Second Empire. His works were widely reproduced and admired, and were bought by collectors in France and abroad who were fond of such tender subjects. He died in 1881. The New York Falling Leaves, Allegory of Autumn, of 1872, with a title that names the season, is typical of his taste for allegory made charming. In the twentieth century his kind of academic painting was out of favour, and his name faded, though the finish of his best pictures is still impressive.","q1677696":"Jacob van Hulsdonck was born in Antwerp in 1582 and entered the city's Guild of St Luke in 1607. Little is recorded of his training, but his pictures belong to the tradition of Osias Beert, who made Antwerp the home of the tidy, low-viewpoint still life. Hulsdonck painted fruit, oysters, bread and tableware on a table or in a basket against a plain dark ground, with great care for the sheen of grapes and the weave of linen. His range runs from the laden kitchen table of meat, fish, vegetables and fruit in Cleveland to the simpler basket of grapes in the Met. He worked in Antwerp for the rest of his life and died there in 1647.","q21408649":"Charles S. Humphreys was an American painter of horses, 1818 to 1880, active in the age when harness racing was one of the nation's favourite sports. His Washington pictures, The Trotter, about 1860, and Budd Doble Driving Goldsmith Maid at Belmont Driving Park, 1876, belong to that world. Goldsmith Maid was the most famous trotting mare of the 1870s, a champion who set trotting records and drew crowds, and her driver, Budd Doble, was a celebrity. Pictures of racehorses were commissioned by owners and breeders and often reproduced as lithographs for a large public. Humphreys's life, training and patrons are not recorded in the information here; he is a specialist in a lively corner of American sporting art.","q527869":"Jan van Huysum was born in Amsterdam in 1682, the son of the painter Justus van Huysum, and became the most famous flower painter of the eighteenth century. He painted large bouquets in urns and vases, set against light, golden backgrounds, with fruit, birds' nests and insects, rendering every petal, dewdrop and butterfly wing with enamel-like finish and a brighter, fresher colour than the Dutch flower painters before him. Since flowers of different seasons do not bloom together, his bouquets were assembled from studies made over many months. He was secretive about his methods and kept his studio closed to most visitors. His pictures were bought by princes and wealthy collectors across Europe and fetched very high prices. He died in Amsterdam in 1749. The Washington Flowers in an Urn, about 1720 to 1722, shows his early maturity.","q49898":"Hyacinthe Rigaud was born at Perpignan in 1659, trained in Montpellier and Lyon, and came to Paris in 1681. Having won the Academy's prize for a stay in Rome in 1682, he gave it up on the advice of the Academy's leading painter, Charles Le Brun, and chose instead to remain in Paris, where his portraits made him famous. His Louis XIV in Coronation Robes of 1701, in the Louvre, which was intended as a gift for the king's grandson Philip V of Spain, so pleased the king that he kept it. For over fifty years Rigaud painted the court, the clergy and the financiers, in splendid robes, and his large workshop employed specialists for costumes and draperies. Cardinal Dubois, painted in 1723, is among his later portraits. He died in Paris in 1743.","q18600114":"Ignacio de León y Escosura was a Spanish painter, born in 1834 and dead in 1901, who made his career as a painter of anecdotal scenes. He is associated with Paris and with the type of small, elaborately finished costume pieces and genre scenes then in fashion there, in the line of Meissonier and Fortuny, which sold well to collectors on both sides of the Atlantic. The Metropolitan's Auction Sale in Clinton Hall, New York, 1876, is, as the title says, a record of an auction sale held in New York in the centennial year of the Republic. Beyond these outlines, little is firmly recorded of his training or patrons in the information here, and a fuller life cannot honestly be written.","q2842736":"Ignacio de Ries was a Spanish painter of the seventeenth century, associated with Seville in the 1640s and later, and probably of Flemish family, since his surname suggests it; the museum gives no dates. Little is recorded of his life, and his name belongs to the lesser-known masters of the Seville school, the school of Zurbarán and Murillo, whose pictures often show strong light and shadow and a firm naturalism. The Metropolitan's Saint Michael the Archangel, of the 1640s, shows the warrior angel who defeated Satan, a popular figure in devotional painting, usually shown in armour with a sword or lance and the devil underfoot. Few paintings are securely attributed to him, and further details of his career are not given here.","ingannati_pietro_degli":"Pietro degli Ingannati was a Venetian painter of the earlier sixteenth century; the dates 1468 to 1589 in the record span more than a century and are clearly not those of one lifetime. He belongs to the generation that followed Giovanni Bellini and Giorgione, whose soft colour and poetic taste the Venetian painters of his day inherited. As its title says, the Washington picture, painted about 1515 to 1525, is an allegory, a work whose exact meaning is hard to recover, a common problem with Venetian pictures of the time made for private collectors. Little is recorded about his training, patrons or other works in the information here, and the paintings given to him are few. He is a minor name, but a revealing one, of Venetian painting in the years of Giorgione's influence.","q23380":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres was born in Montauban in 1780, the son of a painter and decorator, and in 1797 entered the Paris studio of Jacques-Louis David. He won the Prix de Rome in 1801 but, for lack of funds, did not reach Italy until 1806, and he stayed in Rome and Florence for eighteen years. He made his name with the Grande Odalisque of 1814 and the Vow of Louis XIII of 1824, which established him as the leader of the classical school against Delacroix and the Romantics. Famed for his exquisitely drawn portraits, he also directed the French Academy in Rome from 1835 to 1841. A keen violinist, he gave French the phrase violon d'Ingres for a favourite hobby. He died in Paris in 1867.","q22094467":"Ioannes Mokos was a Greek painter whose dates the museum gives as 1680 to 1724, and of whom little is recorded here beyond his name. He worked in the tradition of Greek icon painting, in which painters followed long-established models handed down in workshops and pattern books, with the figures set against gold grounds and arranged according to the rules of the Orthodox Church. The Metropolitan's Dormition of the Virgin shows one of the great feasts of that church: the Virgin lies on her deathbed, surrounded by the apostles and mourners, while Christ stands behind her and holds her soul, shown as a swaddled infant. This standard composition was repeated for centuries with only small variations, which is why an individual painter's name, rare in so traditional a craft, is valuable.","q19408393":"Isabella Beetham was a British miniature painter of the later eighteenth century, one of the women who practised the art of the portrait miniature, a form well suited to professional women, who could work at home and in small format and sell likenesses to a wide public of friends and acquaintances. Her Metropolitan work, Miss Chambers, dated after 1782, is a likeness of an unmarried sitter, presumably painted with the fine, patient touches of the brush that a miniature required. The museum gives no dates for her, and the details of her training, home and clients are not recorded in the information here. Portrait miniatures of this kind were often exchanged as keepsakes between family members and friends, and were set in lockets, bracelets and boxes.","q708968":"Isidore Pils was born in Paris in 1813 and died at Douarnenez, in Brittany, in 1875. He studied with the painter François-Édouard Picot and won the Prix de Rome in 1838, becoming a successful painter of military and religious subjects and of scenes from the army, the Crimean War and the Second Empire, among them the Battle of the Alma. His Rouget de Lisle Singing the Marseillaise, shown in 1849, was a public success. He taught at the École des Beaux-Arts and received government commissions, including one for the visit of Napoleon III and the Empress Eugénie to Algeria in 1860, for which the Metropolitan has a sketch of about 1862 to 1867, showing the Kabyle chiefs paying homage at Algiers. His work is typical of the official French art of the Second Empire.","italian_17th_century":"The painter of this still life is not known; the museum labels the picture only Italian, of the seventeenth century. Still-life painting flourished in Italy in that century, in Naples, Rome, Milan and Bologna, where painters set out fruit, vegetables, game, flowers and kitchen objects on tables and ledges in strong light, either as celebrations of nature's abundance or as quiet reminders that all things pass. Artichokes, a favourite Italian vegetable, and a parrot, a prized exotic bird brought to Europe from distant lands and kept as a pet in wealthy households, appear in the Washington picture, a pairing that suggests a taste for both the humble and the rare. No name, school or date can be given with more precision than the museum's label.","italian_painter":"Italian Painter is not the name of a person but a label that the museum uses for works by painters it cannot identify, here with a general date of about 1525. The twelve Metropolitan pictures listed under it differ in subject and date: Madonnas and Child enthroned or with angels and saints, an Annunciation, portraits of men and a woman, a sheet of Twelve Heads, and even a book cover. They come from different hands and different places in Italy, and they show how much devotional painting and portraiture was made in the Renaissance by artists whose names have been lost. Nothing can be said about a single painter, training, patron or reputation, and the label should be read as a heading for a group, not as a biography.","q181568":"Ivan Konstantinovich Aivazovsky, born Hovhannes Aivazian, was born at Feodosia, in the Crimea, in 1817 into an Armenian family. Local patrons noticed his talent for drawing, and he was sent to the Imperial Academy of Arts in Saint Petersburg, where he studied landscape. From 1840 to 1844 he travelled in Italy, Holland and France, and in 1844 he was named painter of the Russian Navy's Main Staff. He painted over six thousand pictures, mainly of the sea: storms, calms, moonlit bays and naval battles, among them The Ninth Wave (1850). He founded an art school and gallery in Feodosia, and died there in 1900. The two Metropolitan pictures, A Ship in a Stormy Sea of 1892 and Ship by Moonlight, show his taste for light and drama on the water.","j_c_arter":"J. C. Arter was an American painter, given the dates 1859 to 1923. Two of his pictures hang in Cleveland, The Last Price and Japanese Boy Building a Man-of-War, both dated 1895, and they are the only works by him in this collection. The second belongs to the fashion for Japanese subjects in American art of the 1890s, an age of Japanese prints, tea houses, porcelain and the Japonisme that swept through the arts in Europe and America. Little else is recorded about Arter's training, home or patrons in the information here, and the pictures themselves are the best guide to a painter who is otherwise little known; a longer life of him cannot honestly be written from what is available.","q22094540":"J. Lecourt is known only by that name and by one picture, the Portrait of a Man in the Metropolitan Museum, dated about 1810; the museum calls him French and gives no dates or first name. Portraits of that date, made in the years of Napoleon's Empire, were often painted by pupils of the great studios of Jacques-Louis David and his school, with clear drawing, smooth modelling and plain backgrounds, or by small provincial painters working in the same style for local families, officials and officers. Which kind of painter Lecourt was cannot be said from the information here. Nothing certain is known of his training, home, patrons or other works, and it is better to say so than to guess.","q370576":"Jürgen Ovens was born at Tönning, in Schleswig-Holstein, in 1623, and trained in Amsterdam, where he is said to have studied with Rembrandt and Jacob Backer, and absorbed the manner of the Amsterdam portrait painters. He worked in Amsterdam in the 1640s and 1650s, painting portraits and large allegorical and history pictures for the city and its burghers, and in 1651 returned to Schleswig-Holstein to be court painter to Duke Frederick III of Holstein-Gottorp, at Gottorf castle. He also painted for the Danish court. He died in 1678. The Metropolitan's Portrait of a Woman, dated 1650, comes from his last year in Amsterdam, and is a good example of his polished, courtly manner, which owed more to the fashionable portraitists of the city than to Rembrandt.","q22688210":"Jacob D. Blondel is given the dates 1817 to 1877, and little else is recorded about him here. The Metropolitan's portrait shows Luigi Palma di Cesnola, painted in 1865, the year the sitter, an Italian-born officer of the Union army, was appointed American consul in Cyprus. Cesnola's excavations there produced a huge collection of antiquities, which the Metropolitan bought in 1872, and in 1879 he became the museum's first director. The portrait is a document of that career, painted at its beginning. Of Blondel's training, home and other works nothing certain is given here, and the sitter is better remembered than the painter.","q3117749":"Jacob de Wit was born in Amsterdam in 1695 and learned his craft in Antwerp, where he copied the ceiling paintings of Rubens in the Jesuit church. Back home he became the city's leading decorative painter, supplying allegorical ceilings, wall hangings and overdoors for the canal houses of merchants and regents. He was especially admired for grisaille panels that imitate carved marble and stucco so convincingly that they were nicknamed witjes, a pun on his name (wit means white). His light, pastel-toned manner kept the spirit of Rubens alive deep into the eighteenth century. The Metropolitan Museum's Allegory of the Arts and Flora and Zephyr show the graceful, airy style that made him fashionable. He died in Amsterdam in 1754.","q1284751":"Jacob Duck was born in Utrecht around 1600 and is recorded in the city's painters' guild in 1621. Little is known of his training. He painted small genre scenes, above all guardroom pictures of soldiers dicing, drinking and courting, and elegant couples in tidy interiors, like the Met's fortune-teller scene of the early 1630s. His slender figures are brightly dressed and set in cool, pale light, and the pictures often carry a gentle moral about idleness and vanity. He also tried biblical subjects, such as the Salome in Cleveland. He is recorded in several Dutch cities and died about 1667. He was a modest figure beside the Utrecht followers of Caravaggio, but his pictures are valued today as lively records of seventeenth-century soldier life.","q21009925":"Jacob Hart Lazarus was born in 1822 and worked in New York as a portrait painter. He studied with Henry Inman, one of the city's leading portraitists, and the Metropolitan Museum owns the young Lazarus's portrait of his teacher, painted about 1837 to 1840. In later years he painted men of business and public life, among them the banker Joseph W. Drexel, shown in a portrait dated 1877, and Charles A. Macy, as well as a self-portrait made in his fifties. Beyond these works little is recorded of his career, and he is known today mainly through the pictures that reached public collections. He remained a competent, sober portraitist in the tradition he had learned from Inman, and he died in 1891.","q270658":"Jacob Jordaens was born in Antwerp in 1593, the eldest child of a linen merchant, and trained there with Adam van Noort, whose daughter Catharina he married in 1616. He joined the Guild of St Luke in 1615 and worked beside Rubens on large projects, including the street decorations for the entry of Cardinal-Infante Ferdinand into Antwerp in 1635. After Rubens died in 1640 and Van Dyck in 1641, Jordaens was the city's leading painter, though he never travelled to Italy. His warm, fleshy style suits his feasts, satyrs, proverbs and altarpieces. His biggest commission was the huge Triumph of Frederik Hendrik for the Oranjezaal at Huis ten Bosch in The Hague, painted in the early 1650s. He later turned to Calvinism, and died in 1678.","q978158":"Jacob Maris was born in The Hague in 1837, the eldest of three painter brothers, with Matthijs and Willem. He studied at the academy in The Hague and in Antwerp, spent the later 1860s in Paris, where the Barbizon painters influenced him, and returned to The Hague in 1871. With Anton Mauve, Hendrik Willem Mesdag and Jozef Israëls he became a leader of the Hague School, painting canals, mills, polders and city views under broad, silvery skies in loose, confident brushwork. View of the Mill and Bridge on the Noordwest Buitensingel in The Hague, of 1873, is a fine example. His work sold well to collectors in the Netherlands, Britain and America. He died at Karlsbad in 1899.","q724646":"Jacob Ochtervelt was born in Rotterdam in 1634 and, according to the early biographer Arnold Houbraken, studied with Nicolaes Berchem. He worked in Rotterdam until the early 1670s, then moved to Amsterdam, where he died in 1682. He painted well-dressed company in bright interiors, such as musical parties and ladies receiving letters, and also doorway scenes with peddlers and fishwives. Influenced by Gerard ter Borch, Gabriel Metsu and Frans van Mieris, he excelled at satin gowns and the fall of light. In his lifetime he never reached the fame of those masters and was largely forgotten until the nineteenth century. Today he is admired as one of the finest genre painters of his generation, and A Musical Company and The Love Letter show his refined, silvery touch.","q221131":"Jacob Pynas was a Dutch painter and etcher of the early seventeenth century, probably born in Haarlem about 1592. With his elder brother Jan he belonged to the circle of Pieter Lastman in Amsterdam, the history painters now called the Pre-Rembrandtists, who studied Italian art and the work of Adam Elsheimer. Like them he painted biblical and mythological stories in Italianate landscapes, with bright, jewel-like colour and carefully observed figures. He is recorded in Amsterdam and later in Delft, and documents about him continue to about 1650. The Met's Paul and Barnabas at Lystra, in which the apostles are mistaken for pagan gods, belongs to the kind of story-telling picture that the Lastman circle favoured. Little else about his life is securely known.","q21857376":"Jacob Ritter von Hartmann was a German painter, born in 1795 and dead in 1873, and little more of his life is documented. He is represented here by a portrait of Ali Pasha, dated 1822, the year the sitter died. Ali Pasha of Ioannina, born about 1741, was an Albanian-born governor who ruled much of northwestern Greece and Albania for the Ottoman sultan almost as an independent prince. Lord Byron visited his court in 1809 and described its splendour in Childe Harold. After a long siege the sultan's forces killed him early in 1822. Whether Hartmann painted the likeness from life or from earlier images is not recorded, and the rest of his career as a painter remains obscure.","q213612":"Jacob van Ruisdael was born in Haarlem in 1628 or 1629 and became the greatest Dutch landscape painter of the seventeenth century. His father Isaac was a frame maker and painter, and his uncle Salomon van Ruysdael, an established landscapist, probably taught him. He entered the Haarlem guild in 1648, travelled about 1650 to Bentheim in Germany, probably with Nicolaes Berchem, and moved to Amsterdam around 1656. He painted dunes, windmills, wheat fields, forests, waterfalls and brooding skies, giving nature a grandeur rarely seen before. He died in 1682, and his reputation grew steadily afterwards: Goethe, Constable and the Barbizon painters admired him, and the Hudson River painters followed. The Cleveland Landscape with a Windmill is dated 1646, when he was about seventeen.","q597322":"Jacob van Strij was born in Dordrecht in 1756 and spent his life in that river city. With his elder brother Abraham, who was also a painter, he worked in the long shadow of Dordrecht's most famous artist, Aelbert Cuyp, whose golden-lit pastures, river views and cattle he took as his model. His pictures, such as the Met's Landscape with Cattle of about 1800, show cows and herdsmen resting beside quiet water under warm, hazy skies, painted in the manner of the Dutch Golden Age more than a century after it ended. He is a good example of how seventeenth-century landscape stayed alive as a model for later Dutch painters. He died in Dordrecht in 1815.","q1478130":"Jacob Vosmaer was born in Delft in 1584 and died there in 1641. He is recorded as an early Delft specialist in flower painting, working in the generation that followed Jan Brueghel the Elder and Ambrosius Bosschaert, whose tightly arranged bouquets set the pattern. Little is documented of his training or his patrons, and his surviving pictures are few. They show bouquets of garden flowers in a vase, painted petal by petal, a type of picture that doubled as a quiet reminder of the shortness of life. The Met's A Vase with Flowers, probably dated 1613, comes from the early years of this kind of painting in the Netherlands. His reputation rests on that early date and on the careful delicacy of such pictures.","q6223610":"Jacometto, called Jacometto Veneziano, was a Venetian painter of the later fifteenth century, documented by about 1472 and dead by 1498. He is known for portraits of extraordinary smallness and finish, painted in oil with a delicacy that suggests close knowledge of Netherlandish pictures. Almost nothing is recorded of his life. His name is kept alive by Marcantonio Michiel, a Venetian connoisseur who described his small portraits in collectors' houses in the early sixteenth century, and modern scholars have rebuilt his surviving work from those descriptions and from comparison. The Met's Portrait of a Young Man, painted in the 1480s, shows the careful modelling and quiet, observant likeness for which he is now admired.","jacometto_veneziano":"Jacometto Veneziano worked in Venice in the later fifteenth century, in the generation of Giovanni Bellini, and made small portraits in oil that rank among the most delicate of their time. He is recorded from about 1472 and is thought to have died by 1498. His life is poorly documented, and much of what is known comes from the notes of the Venetian collector Marcantonio Michiel, who admired his work in the houses of his friends. His miniature-like finish and exact observation of faces and costume owe something to Netherlandish painting, which Venetian patrons collected avidly. The Cleveland portrait of a woman, perhaps a novice of the convent of San Secondo, dates from about 1490 and shows the cool, exact manner on which his reputation rests.","q2538308":"Jacopino del Conte was born in Florence in 1510 and, according to Vasari, trained with Andrea del Sarto before settling in Rome in the 1530s. There he moved in the circle of Michelangelo and painted frescoes for the oratory of the confraternity of San Giovanni Decollato, including the Preaching of the Baptist of 1538. He was more successful as a portraitist, and his sitters included Pope Paul III and, around 1535, Michelangelo himself. The Madonna and Child with Saint Elizabeth and the infant Baptist in Washington, about 1535, shows the elegant, rather stiff Mannerism of the Roman school of those years. He lived to the great age of eighty-eight and died in Rome in 1598.","q380897":"Jacopo Amigoni was a Venetian painter, born in 1682, who made his career abroad, working in Bavaria, England, Paris and Spain. After early years at the Bavarian court he lived in London from 1729 to 1739, where he painted mythological ceilings and wall paintings for country houses, including Moor Park in Hertfordshire, as well as many portraits. His light, pastel-toned Rococo manner of cupids, nymphs and gods is seen in the Met's Flora and Zephyr. His close friend was the castrato singer Farinelli, whom he portrayed more than once, and in 1747 he followed him to Madrid, where he became court painter to Ferdinand VI. He died in Madrid in 1752.","q313310":"Jacopo Bellini was born in Venice around 1400 and was the father of the painters Gentile and Giovanni Bellini; his daughter Nicolosia married Andrea Mantegna. He is thought to have trained with Gentile da Fabriano, whom he followed to Florence in 1423, and he later worked in Padua, Verona and Ferrara as well as Venice. In 1441 he won a portrait competition against Pisanello at the Este court of Ferrara, painting Lionello d'Este. Few of his paintings survive, among them a Madonna of the 1440s now at the Met, but his two great albums of drawings, in the British Museum and the Louvre, show an early Renaissance passion for perspective and architecture. He died in Venice about 1470.","q2525549":"Jacopo di Arcangelo, called Jacopo del Sellaio after his father's trade of saddle-making, was a Florentine painter born in 1441 or 1442 and dead in 1493. He is described as a follower of Filippo Lippi and was later much influenced by Botticelli, in whose circle of Florentine patrons he worked. His workshop produced the painted chests that families commissioned for weddings, with stories from classical literature such as the Argonauts panels at the Met, as well as small devotional pictures of the Virgin adoring the Child, like the late work shown here. His pleasantly decorative style made him a popular provider of the many smaller commissions of fifteenth-century Florence, and he is valued today for his lively narrative detail.","q2258950":"Jacopo di Cione was a Florentine painter documented from the 1360s to the 1390s, and a brother of Andrea Orcagna and Nardo di Cione. After Orcagna's death in 1368 Jacopo took charge of the family workshop, which supplied altarpieces and panels to Florentine churches and guilds in the second half of the fourteenth century. His best-known commission, from 1370, was the high altarpiece of San Pier Maggiore in Florence, a Coronation of the Virgin now in the National Gallery, London, painted with assistants. Like much art made in the decades after the Black Death, his work keeps to the formal, gilded tradition of Giotto's heirs, as in the Washington Madonna and Child with God the Father and angels of about 1370 to 1375.","q1337128":"Jacopo Ligozzi was born in Verona in 1547, into a family of painters, and trained as a miniaturist and draughtsman before moving to Florence in 1577 to enter the service of the Medici, first of Grand Duke Francesco I and then of Ferdinando I. He remained there for the rest of his life, supplying the court with paintings of saints and allegories, designs for pietra dura and festivals, and remarkable watercolours of plants, birds and animals, many made for the naturalist Ulisse Aldrovandi of Bologna as well as for the Medici. His crisp, brightly coloured style has the precision of a scientific illustrator, and the Met's Allegory of Avarice shows his taste for moralising subjects. He died in Florence in 1627.","q651795":"Jacopo Palma the Younger, known as Palma il Giovane, was born in Venice in 1548, the son of a painter and great-nephew of Palma Vecchio. With the support of the Duke of Urbino he studied in Urbino and then in Rome, where he copied Michelangelo and Raphael before returning home in the early 1570s. After the deaths of Titian, Veronese and Tintoretto he was the leading painter in Venice, supplying altarpieces, ceilings for the Doge's Palace and church decoration with tireless speed. His best-known act of homage was to finish Titian's last unfinished work, the Pietà, after the master died in 1576. His style hardens into a stiff, hurried Mannerism in his late years. He died in Venice in 1628.","jacques_andr_joseph_aved":"Jacques André Joseph Aved was born in Douai in 1702 and, as a young man, went to Amsterdam, where he trained with the engraver Bernard Picart. He settled in Paris around 1721, was received into the Royal Academy in 1734, and became one of the city's most sought-after portraitists, painting financiers, diplomats and, in 1742, the Ottoman ambassador Mehmed Said Efendi. His pictures are frank, carefully lit likenesses, rich in the detail of fabrics and furnishings. The Cleveland portrait of Jean-Gabriel du Theil at the Signing of the Treaty of Vienna, painted around 1738 to 1740, shows his ability to turn a diplomat's moment of duty into a dignified, finely observed portrait. He died in Paris in 1766.","q385845":"Jacques Blanchard was born in Paris in 1600 and trained with his uncle, the painter Nicolas Bollery, before working in Lyon and then in Rome and Venice, returning to France around 1628. In Venice he studied the colour of Titian and Veronese, and at home he was nicknamed the French Titian for his warm, glowing flesh and soft handling. Back in Paris he painted altarpieces, mythological and romantic subjects, and portraits, and he was among the painters who set the tone of French art in the 1630s. His Angelica and Medoro, in the Met, shows his taste for tender lovers drawn from Ariosto's Orlando Furioso. He died young, in 1638, aged thirty-eight, before he could fulfil the promise his admirers saw in him.","q1623984":"Jacques d'Arthois was a Flemish landscape painter, baptised in Brussels in 1613 and dead there about 1686. He specialised in views of the great beech woods near Brussels, the forest of Soignes, painting sandy forest roads, tall trees and sunlit glades with a feathery touch and warm greens and browns. Like many Flemish landscapists he often relied on other painters to add the small figures and animals, and his woodland scenes were prized by Brussels patrons and by collectors elsewhere in Europe. He also supplied designs for the tapestry weavers of Brussels. The Met's Family Group in a Landscape, about 1645, combines his wooded setting with a company of elegantly dressed figures.","q1347434":"Jacques de Gheyn II was born in Antwerp in 1565, the son of a glass painter, and grew up in the northern Netherlands, training as an engraver with Hendrick Goltzius in Haarlem. He later worked in Amsterdam, Leiden and, from about 1600, in The Hague, where he served the court of Prince Maurits. A restless draughtsman, he made precise studies of plants, insects and animals and a series of strange drawings of witches. In 1607 he published the Exercise of Arms, the first illustrated drill manual for musket, pike and arquebus, made for the army of Prince Maurits. His Vanitas Still Life of 1603, at the Met, is among the earliest Dutch pictures of its kind. He died in The Hague in 1629.","q348421":"Jacques de La Joue the Younger was born in Paris in 1686 and worked there until his death in 1761. The son of a painter of the same name, he became a member of the Royal Academy and made his living as a decorative painter in the Rococo taste, supplying light, ornamental panels and overdoors for Parisian town houses. Allegories of the seasons were a popular theme for such rooms, and the Met's Allegory of Winter is an example. His pictures were made to form part of a decorative scheme rather than to hang alone. He is a minor figure of the French Rococo, and few details of his life and training survive.","q3158673":"Jacques Delaplace was a French portrait painter whose name is recorded here only through a Portrait of a Woman dated about 1805 to 1810. Those were the years of Napoleon's empire, when French portraiture was dominated by Jacques-Louis David and his pupils and favoured clear outlines, plain backgrounds and simple, high-waisted dress. Nothing certain about Delaplace's birth, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His picture is valuable mostly as a record of one unnamed sitter and of the fashions of the Consulate and Empire, and it shows how widely the portrait style of David's school was copied by lesser painters.","q21997158":"Jacques Joseph de Gault was a French painter known here from a single portrait of 1795, and little is recorded of his life. The sitter is Marie-Thérèse-Charlotte, the daughter of Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette. She was the only member of the immediate royal family to survive the Revolution, and in December of that year, after three years in the Temple prison in Paris, she was exchanged for French prisoners and sent to Austria. The portrait belongs to the moment when her fate drew attention across Europe. She later married her cousin the Duke of Angoulême and returned to France after 1814. Nothing certain about the painter's training or career is recorded in the sources available here.","q83155":"Jacques-Louis David was born in Paris in 1748 and trained with Joseph-Marie Vien, winning the Prix de Rome on his fourth attempt in 1774. In Rome he absorbed antiquity, and on his return he created the Oath of the Horatii (1784), the manifesto of Neoclassicism, followed by the Death of Socrates in 1787, now at the Met. A fervent Revolutionary, he voted for the death of Louis XVI and painted the Death of Marat in 1793; after Robespierre's fall he was briefly imprisoned. Under Napoleon he became First Painter and made the Coronation. As a regicide he was exiled after 1815 and died in Brussels in 1825. His pupils, among them Gros, Girodet, Gérard and Ingres, shaped French painting for a generation.","q21857072":"Jacques Saillant was a French portrait painter of the reign of Louis XIII, and almost everything known of him comes from his pictures. The Met owns two portraits by him of about 1628: one is identified as Gaspard de Daillon du Lude, born about 1602, and the other is a Portrait of a Man. Nothing certain about the painter's birth, training or later career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. As portraits of the late 1620s, the two works record the faces and costume of the French elite in the early years of the career of Cardinal Richelieu, who became Louis XIII's chief minister in 1624.","james_bard":"James Bard was born in New York in 1815 and, with his twin brother John, began painting portraits of steamboats while still in his teens. James carried on for decades and became the best-known American painter of Hudson River and coastal steamers. He painted each vessel in exact profile, and his pictures often record the ship's measurements and machinery, so that owners and shipbuilders could treat them as accurate records as well as advertisements. He had no formal training and kept the same neat, clear style from the 1830s into the 1890s. He died in 1897. The Met's The Thomas Hunt and the America, of 1852, shows two steamers under way.","q6134276":"James Frothingham was born in Charlestown, Massachusetts, in 1786 and learned the trade of coach and sign painter before teaching himself portraiture by studying the work of Gilbert Stuart, whose manner he followed closely. He worked in Boston and then, from about 1826, mainly in New York, painting merchants, officials and their families. His portraits are firmly modelled and economical, with the soft flesh and dark, plain backgrounds of Stuart's school, and they were valued by sitters who wanted a dignified likeness at a moderate price. The Cleveland portrait of Samuel Barber Clark, about 1810, and the Met's portrait of Christopher Colles, about 1812 to 1816, show his steady, honest manner. He died in 1864.","q6134904":"James Green was an English portrait painter, born in 1771 and dead in 1834, known here for his portrait of Benjamin West, painted in 1818. West was by then in his eightieth year: born in Pennsylvania in 1738, he had gone to Italy and then to London, become history painter to George III, and, except for a short break, had been President of the Royal Academy since 1792, succeeding Sir Joshua Reynolds. Green's likeness shows the veteran painter near the end of his long career; West died in 1820. Little more is securely recorded of Green's life and training in the sources available here, and his reputation rests largely on this one portrait of a famous sitter.","q21994088":"James Guy Evans was an American painter recorded as active from about 1835 to 1860, and little else is securely known of his life. He is represented here by The Tow Boat Conqueror, dated 1852, a ship portrait of a steam towboat, the kind of powerful working vessel that hauled barges and canal boats along American waters in the middle of the nineteenth century. Such pictures were commissioned by owners and builders who wanted a clear, accurate record of a new vessel, and they are prized today as documents of American transport and trade as well as for their lively, simplified style. Evans appears to have been one of many specialists in this popular kind of painting.","q21994089":"James H. Cafferty was born in 1819 and worked in New York as a painter. He is best remembered for one large, much-reproduced picture, Wall Street, Half Past Two, October 13, 1857, usually credited to Cafferty together with Charles G. Rosenberg. It shows a crowd of anxious men packed into the street on a day of financial panic, when the failure of banks and railroad companies brought business in New York to a standstill and set off a depression across the country. The picture is valued as a vivid record of a real event and of the fashions and faces of the city's business world. Cafferty died in 1869.","q21994090":"James H. Kimberly is recorded as an American portrait painter active between 1834 and 1846, and little more of his life is known. The Met has one picture by him, a Portrait of a Gentleman dated 1840, in which the sitter's name has not been recorded. Portraits like this were made in the years just before photography took over: the daguerreotype was announced in 1839 and reached American towns within a few years, and by the 1850s it had put many itinerant and local portrait painters out of work. Kimberly's name survives through this single dated picture and a handful of others, and further details of his training and travels remain to be found.","q20127413":"James Hamilton was born in Ireland in 1819 and arrived in Philadelphia as a teenager. He taught himself painting and became known as a marine and landscape painter, often called the American Turner for his stormy seas, shipwrecks and glowing sunsets in the manner of J. M. W. Turner, whose pictures he studied during a stay in England in the 1850s. He also made drawings for Elisha Kent Kane's Arctic Explorations, published in 1856, working from Kane's sketches of the ice. The Met's On Hampstead Heath, dated 1856, comes from the English stay. His moody, atmospheric pictures were admired in Philadelphia, where he worked for much of his life, and he died in 1878.","james_harvey_young":"James Harvey Young was an American portrait painter, born in 1830 and dead in 1918, whose long life spanned the years from the Hudson River painters to the Gilded Age. The Cleveland Museum of Art has his portrait of Frederic W. Lincoln, Jr., painted in 1889, a formal likeness of the kind that businessmen and civic leaders still commissioned in oil even though photography had long since taken over the market for cheap portraits. Beyond these dates and this picture little is recorded in the sources available here about his training, his sitters or his places of work, and it would be unwise to guess.","q731056":"James Jebusa Shannon was born in Auburn, New York, in 1862 and moved to London at sixteen, studying at the South Kensington schools. His early success came in the 1880s, and by the 1890s he was one of the fashionable portrait painters of London society, painting elegant women and children in soft, silvery harmonies beside his friends Sargent and Abbey. He was elected a Royal Academician, served as president of the Royal Society of Portrait Painters from 1910, and was knighted in 1922, a year before his death in 1923. The Met's Jungle Tales (Contes de la Jungle), of 1895, shows the easy, graceful manner that brought him commissions on both sides of the Atlantic.","q20011003":"James Jennings was a British portrait painter of the eighteenth century whose life is poorly recorded. The Met owns a Portrait of a Woman by him, dated probably to the early 1770s, a few years after the Royal Academy was founded in 1768 and in the age of Reynolds, Gainsborough and Romney, when portraiture was the busiest branch of the profession in Britain. Nothing certain about Jennings's birth, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His picture is useful mainly as a record of one unnamed sitter and of the taste of the time, and it shows that competent painters of modest fame worked in every English town.","q724069":"James M. Hart was born in Kilmarnock, Scotland, in 1828 and was brought as a child to Albany, New York. He worked first as a decorative and sign painter and then went to Düsseldorf to study landscape painting. Back in America he settled in New York, became one of the leading Hudson River painters of the second generation, and was elected to the National Academy. His broad landscapes with cattle, such as the Met's Pasture Scene of 1876, show a soft, sunny style that grew gradually freer. He painted in the Adirondacks as well as New England, and several members of his family, including his brother William, were painters. His reputation was high in the 1870s and faded after his death in 1901.","james_mcdougal_hart":"James McDougal Hart was born in Kilmarnock, Scotland, in 1828 and grew up in Albany, New York, where he began as a decorative and sign painter. His brother William, born in 1823, also became a landscape painter, and the two worked in the same Hudson River tradition. After study in Düsseldorf, James settled in New York and became known for pastoral landscapes with cattle, painted in a soft, harmonious palette with a particular feeling for light on meadows and water. His wife, Marie Theresa Gorsuch Hart, and their daughters were also painters. He spent his summers in the Adirondacks as well as in New England, and he died in 1901. Cleveland's Morning in New England, of 1873, is a typical pastoral scene.","q203643":"James McNeill Whistler was born in Lowell, Massachusetts, in 1834 and spent part of his childhood in Russia, where his father built a railway. He briefly attended West Point, worked for the Coast Survey, and in 1855 went to Paris, where he studied with Charles Gleyre and met Courbet and Fantin-Latour. From 1859 he lived mainly in London, painting portraits and river scenes that he gave musical titles, calling them arrangements, harmonies and nocturnes. In 1877 John Ruskin accused him of flinging a pot of paint in the public's face; Whistler sued and won a farthing in damages in 1878, but the costs bankrupted him. His etchings, the Peacock Room and his belief in art for art's sake shaped later taste. He died in London in 1903.","q16858107":"James Nixon was an English miniature painter, born in 1741, who exhibited at the Royal Academy and was elected an Associate of that body in 1778. He painted portraits on ivory and small subject pieces in the delicate, sparkling manner popular in late Georgian England. Miniatures were intimate keepsakes, given to lovers and relatives and often worn in lockets, and the Met's two portraits of women, from about 1780 to 1785 and about 1790, belong to that tradition. Little else is securely recorded of his training and private life in the sources available here. His reputation today is that of a capable, fashionable miniaturist of the generation after Reynolds, and he died in 1812.","q22575799":"James Passmore Smith was born in 1803 and died in 1888, and he is represented here by two portraits at the Met: a Portrait of a Gentleman dated 1829 and a Portrait of a Lady dated 1834. Nothing certain about his birthplace, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. Pairs of portraits, one of a gentleman and one of a lady, were a usual way for families of the time to commission likenesses, and the fact that both sitters are unnamed shows that the pictures passed out of the family's memory. Their value now is as records of dress and manner in the years around 1830.","q3161332":"James Peale was born in Chestertown, Maryland, in 1749 and trained as a cabinetmaker before learning to paint from his elder brother Charles Willson Peale. He served as an officer in the Continental Army during the Revolution, and after the war settled in Philadelphia, where he became a miniature painter, making delicate portraits on ivory, including likenesses of Washington. In later years he turned increasingly to still life and landscape, painting quiet, precise arrangements of fruit and vegetables such as the Met's Balsam Apple and Vegetables. He was the head of a remarkable family of painters, among them his daughters Anna Claypoole, Margaretta and Sarah Miriam Peale, and his portrait of Anna is in the Met. He died in Philadelphia in 1831.","q13629171":"James Reid Lambdin was born in Pittsburgh in 1807 and studied in Philadelphia, where he took advice from Thomas Sully. He worked as a portrait painter in Pittsburgh and Louisville before settling in Philadelphia, and over a long career he painted many prominent Americans, including several presidents. Later in life he was professor of fine arts at the University of Pennsylvania. His son, George Cochran Lambdin, became a well-known painter of flowers and sentimental subjects. The Met's two portraits of gentlemen, dated 1831 and about 1840 to 1850, show his plain, sober, well-finished manner. His portraits had the quiet dignity that civic and professional men wanted in the decades before photography replaced them. He died in 1889.","q2674488":"James Seymour was born in London in 1702, the son of a well-to-do banker and collector, and became one of the first English painters to specialise in horses. He painted racehorses with their grooms and owners at Newmarket, hunting parties and portraits of favourite mounts, often for the gentlemen who bred them, and he also made drawings for engravings. His horses are lively and carefully observed, though his work was later overshadowed by the anatomical authority of George Stubbs. He reportedly spent his inheritance freely and had to live by his brush, and he died in 1752. The Met's Portrait of a Horseman shows the sporting world that supplied most of his patrons.","q381248":"James Tissot was born Jacques-Joseph Tissot in Nantes in 1836, the son of a draper, and later adopted the English form of his first name. He studied in Paris with Louis Lamothe and Hippolyte Flandrin, both pupils of Ingres, and became friends with Degas and Whistler. After the Paris Commune of 1871 he moved to London, where his glossy, minutely detailed scenes of fashionable life, such as Tea of 1872, made him rich. Following the death of his companion Kathleen Newton in 1882 he returned to Paris, then travelled to Palestine and produced hundreds of watercolours of the life of Christ, which toured Paris, London and New York in the 1890s and are now in the Brooklyn Museum. He died in 1902.","q22688294":"James Van Dyck is recorded as a painter active between 1825 and 1844. His name recalls the great Flemish portraitist Anthony van Dyck, but he is a different and far more obscure figure, and little about him is documented. The Met owns his Self Portrait, painted between 1834 and 1836, which gives a rare glimpse of how a painter of the period chose to present himself to the world. Nothing certain about his birthplace, teachers or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His reputation, such as it is, rests on this single self-portrait and on the dates of his activity.","q22691252":"James Whitehorne was born in 1803 and died in 1888, and he is known here from a pair of portraits at the Met, Mary Kellogg and Nancy Kellogg, painted about 1838. Matching portraits of related sitters were a common commission in the American portrait trade of the 1830s, when local and travelling painters offered quick and inexpensive likenesses to families who could not afford a leading city artist. Nothing certain about Whitehorne's training, travels or other works is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His portraits are valued today for their directness and as faces from a particular time and place.","jan_anthonisz_van_ravesteyn":"Jan Anthonisz. van Ravesteyn was born in The Hague around 1570 and spent his whole life there, working as one of the city's leading portrait painters in the early decades of the Dutch Republic. He became a master in the painters' guild in the late 1590s and painted group portraits of the civic guard companies, as well as likenesses of wealthy burghers, officials and officers, in a clear, dignified manner close to that of Michiel van Mierevelt, the leading portraitist of neighbouring Delft. His careful treatment of lace, jewellery and black silk made him a favourite of patrician families. The Cleveland Portrait of a Woman, from the 1620s, shows this style. He died in The Hague in 1657, aged about eighty-seven.","jan_baptist_weenix":"Jan Baptist Weenix was born in Amsterdam in 1621 and trained there and in Utrecht, where he is said to have studied with Abraham Bloemaert and Claes Moeyaert. In the mid-1640s he lived in Italy, working in Rome and its surroundings, where he painted harbours, classical ruins and landscapes with peasants and animals in warm southern light. He returned to the Netherlands in 1647, settled near Utrecht, and became one of the leading Italianate painters there, also painting hunting scenes and still lifes of game. His son Jan Weenix followed his career. The Cleveland Hunters Near Ruins, of 1648, shows how Italy remained in his imagination after he came home. He died near Utrecht in 1660.","q285933":"Jan Brueghel the Younger was born in Antwerp in 1601, the son of Jan Brueghel the Elder and grandson of Pieter Bruegel the Elder. He trained in his father's workshop and went to Italy in the early 1620s, working in Milan for Cardinal Federico Borromeo, his father's patron. When his father died of cholera in 1625 he hurried home and took over the studio, which he ran for more than fifty years, painting flower garlands, small landscapes, mythological scenes and visions of paradise and hell in his father's manner. The Met's Aeneas and the Sibyl in the Underworld and Basket of Flowers show both sides of this family business. He died in Antwerp in 1678.","q2450553":"Jan Ekels the Younger was born in Amsterdam in 1759 and died there in 1793, at the age of thirty-four. He came from a family of painters, and his father, Jan Ekels the Elder, was also an Amsterdam painter. The Met's Conversation Piece, subtitled The Sense of Smell and probably painted in 1791, points to the traditional theme of the Five Senses, a favourite subject since the Golden Age, and shows how Dutch painters of the late eighteenth century still looked to seventeenth-century models. Because he died so young, his surviving work is small. Few other facts about his training and his patrons are recorded in the sources available here.","q624802":"Jan Fyt was born in Antwerp in 1611 and trained with the painter Hans van den Berch before becoming a master of the Guild of St Luke in 1629. He travelled in France and Italy in the 1630s, and after returning to Antwerp became, with Frans Snyders, the leading painter of animals and still life in the city. He specialised in dead game, hounds, live birds and baskets of fruit, painted with fluent brushwork, rich yet silvery colour and a keen eye for fur and feathers. Collectors across Europe sought his pictures. The Met's four gamepieces, with hare, partridges, small birds and a basket, show his usual subjects. He died in Antwerp in 1661.","jan_gossaert":"Jan Gossaert, called Mabuse after his birthplace Maubeuge, was born about 1478 and died in 1532. He joined the Antwerp Guild of St Luke in 1503, and in 1508 travelled to Rome in the retinue of Philip of Burgundy, studying antique sculpture and the buildings of the city. On his return he introduced Italian ideas into northern painting, setting the Virgin and saints within classical architecture and painting daring mythological nudes. He worked for Philip of Burgundy, Admiral of Zeeland, and later bishop of Utrecht, living at Middelburg and then near Utrecht. The Cleveland Virgin and Child in a Landscape, dated 1531, was painted a year before his death.","jan_griffier":"Jan Griffier was born in Amsterdam about 1645 or 1648 and trained there before going to England around 1667, soon after the Great Fire of London, where he spent most of his working life. He painted panoramic views of the Thames and of English country houses, as well as imaginary Italianate landscapes in the manner of Jan Both and Herman Saftleven, and winter scenes with skaters in the Dutch tradition. He found a ready market among English collectors who liked topographical views and the Dutch manner. His son Robert Griffier was also a painter. The Cleveland Winter Landscape, of about 1680 to 1718, belongs to this Dutch-in-England tradition. He died in London in 1718.","q431137":"Jan Massys was born in Antwerp in 1509, the son of the painter Quinten Massys, and became a master in the Guild of St Luke in 1531. He began in his father's manner, but his pictures grew more Italian under the influence of Fontainebleau and Rome, as in his sensuous nude figures of Flora and Judith, and he also painted moralising scenes of tax collectors and merry companies. He was banished from Antwerp in 1544 and returned to the city only after some years. The Met's Rest on the Flight into Egypt, of 1537 to 1540, shows the careful modelling and soft light of his early manner. He died in Antwerp in 1573.","q979945":"Jan Miel was born in Beveren-Waas, near Antwerp, in 1599 and trained in Antwerp with Gerard Seghers before going to Rome around 1636, where he spent about two decades. He joined the Dutch and Flemish painters known as the Bentvueghels and became one of the leading Bamboccianti, artists who painted the lively street life of Rome: carnival, peasants, pilgrims, beggars and soldiers, in small canvases with warm colour and quick brushwork. In 1658 he left Rome for Turin, where he became court painter to Duke Charles Emmanuel II of Savoy, and he died there in 1664. The Met's Landscape with a Battle between Two Rams, about 1640, shows his taste for animal and rustic subjects.","q577279":"Jan Mostaert was born in Haarlem about 1475 and died there in 1555 or 1556, and he was Haarlem's leading painter of his generation. He painted altarpieces, devotional pictures and portraits in a refined, rather archaic style, and for some years served at the court of Margaret of Austria, regent of the Netherlands. His most unusual picture, the Landscape with an Episode from the Conquest of America, is one of the first Western paintings of the New World, painted from reports rather than from sight. The Met's Christ Shown to the People, of 1510 to 1515, shows his crowded, expressive storytelling. He is now seen as a key figure of Haarlem painting before Maarten van Heemskerck.","jan_siberechts":"Jan Siberechts was born in Antwerp in 1627 and died in England about 1703. In Antwerp he painted rustic landscapes with peasants and cattle fording streams, in a clear, bright, naturalistic style admired for its freshness. About 1672 the Duke of Buckingham brought him to England to paint views of his estate at Cliveden, and he stayed for the rest of his life, becoming the leading painter of English country houses in the reigns of Charles II and his successors, with high, clear views of houses such as Longleat and Wollaton Hall. The Cleveland Peasants Crossing a Stream, about 1670, belongs to his Flemish period.","q205863":"Jan Steen was born in Leiden in 1626, the son of a brewer, and is thought to have studied with several masters, including Nicolaes Knupfer in Utrecht, Adriaen van Ostade in Haarlem and Jan van Goyen in The Hague, whose daughter Margriet he married in 1649. He lived and worked in Leiden, The Hague, Delft, Warmond and Haarlem, and in his last years again in Leiden, where he also kept a tavern. His comic, crowded scenes of family life, feasts, doctors' visits and quarrels, painted with a masterly sense of character and a quick, sparkling touch, gave rise to the Dutch saying a Jan Steen household, meaning a disorderly home. The Met's Dissolute Household shows the idea. He died in 1679.","q759804":"Jan van de Cappelle was born in Amsterdam in 1626 and was never a professional painter in the usual sense: he inherited his father's prosperous dye works and painted as a gifted amateur, apparently teaching himself by studying Simon de Vlieger. He painted calm seas, state yachts and ships at anchor, in silvery light, with still, reflective water and tall, cloud-filled skies. His estate was found to include some two hundred paintings and hundreds of drawings by other masters, including Rembrandt, which made it one of the notable collections of the time. He died in 1679. The Met's A State Yacht and Other Craft in Calm Water, about 1660, shows the quiet balance of ships and sky for which he is remembered.","jan_van_eyck":"Jan van Eyck was born about 1390 in the Maas valley, probably near Maaseik, and died in Bruges in 1441. By 1425 he served Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, as court painter and valet de chambre, with a handsome salary and secret diplomatic journeys, including one to Portugal in 1428 to paint the duke's future bride, Isabella. From about 1430 he lived in Bruges. With his brother Hubert he is credited with the Ghent Altarpiece, finished in 1432, and he painted the Arnolfini Portrait of 1434 and the Madonna of Chancellor Rolin, as well as the Met's small diptych of the Crucifixion and the Last Judgment. His oil technique, with luminous glazes and minute detail, set the standard for northern painting.","q864059":"Jan van Ravesteyn, born in The Hague about 1572, was one of the principal portrait painters of the city in the first half of the seventeenth century. Little is recorded of his training, but his sober, clear portraits follow those of Michiel van Mierevelt. Among his sitters were officers of the civic guards, for whom he painted large group portraits, and the wives and husbands of well-to-do burghers and officials. He remained active into his eighties and died in The Hague in 1657. The Met's Portrait of a Woman, dated 1635, shows his careful records of lace, collar and black silk. His portraits were admired in his day for faithful likenesses, but in later centuries his reputation was overshadowed by that of Hals and Rembrandt.","q1357477":"Jan Victors was born in Amsterdam in 1619 and is thought to have been a pupil of Rembrandt in the later 1630s. He painted biblical scenes in warm, golden tones close to Rembrandt's own, as well as peasant interiors, farmyards and some portraits, selling them in Amsterdam, where he was well regarded in the 1640s and 1650s. In 1676 he sailed for the Dutch East Indies, and nothing more is heard of him, so he is presumed to have died there. The Met's Abraham's Parting from the Family of Lot, about 1655 to 1665, is typical of his Old Testament scenes, with their rich colour, crowded figures and homely detail.","q1346044":"Jan Weenix was born in Amsterdam in 1642, the son and pupil of Jan Baptist Weenix, and grew up near Utrecht. He became the leading Dutch painter of hunting still lifes in the later seventeenth century, with large pictures of dead game, hounds and hunting gear set in park landscapes beside classical statues and urns. In 1702 he was invited to Germany by the Elector Palatine Johann Wilhelm, for whose hunting lodge at Bensberg he painted a famous series of large decorative pictures. He returned to Amsterdam about 1712 and died there in 1719. The Met's Gamepiece with a Dead Heron, of 1695, shows his polished, decorative style and his gift for the soft texture of feathers.","jan_wijnants":"Jan Wijnants was born in Haarlem about 1635 and worked there before moving to Amsterdam around 1660, where he died in 1684. He painted dunes, sandy paths and wooded hillsides with birches, tree stumps and wide skies, in cool, silvery light and a smooth, delicate technique that influenced later landscape painters. Wijnants left the small figures and animals in his landscapes to other artists, notably Adriaen van de Velde, Johannes Lingelbach and Philips Wouwerman. The Cleveland Herengracht, Amsterdam, of about 1661, is a city view, unusual among his landscapes, and was painted soon after his move to the city; a second Cleveland picture shows hunters in a landscape.","q3182713":"John Wesley Jarvis was born in South Shields, England, in 1780 and came to Philadelphia as a child; his name came from his reputed relative the Methodist founder John Wesley. After an apprenticeship with the engraver Edward Savage he set up as a portrait painter in New York, where for some years he was the city's busiest and most celebrated. He was a famous wit and drinker, a friend of actors and writers, and a fast painter who could finish a portrait in a few sittings. Among his sitters were the city's naval heroes, and in 1819 he painted General Andrew Jackson on his visit to New York. He trained apprentices who became painters in their own right, notably Henry Inman, and made winter trips to southern cities such as Charleston and New Orleans. He died in 1840.","jasper_f_cropsey":"Jasper F. Cropsey was born on Staten Island in 1823, trained as an architect, and took up landscape painting in his twenties, drawing inspiration from Thomas Cole and the Hudson River School. He went to Europe in 1847, spending about two years in England and Italy, and again from 1856 to 1863 he lived in London, where his brilliant autumn landscapes astonished English audiences. Back in America he built a Gothic-style house of his own design, Aladdin, in Warwick, New York. His taste for rich colour and drama, seen in the Cleveland Mount Washington, New Hampshire, of 1870 and The Clove, a storm scene of 1851, went out of fashion late in life. He died in 1900.","q156426":"Alexej von Jawlensky was born in 1864 in Torzhok, Russia, and served as an officer in the Imperial Guard before turning to painting. In St Petersburg he met the painter Marianne von Werefkin, who became his companion and supporter, and in 1896 they moved to Munich, where he studied with Anton Ažbe and befriended Kandinsky. Visits to Paris around 1905 to 1907 brought him close to the colour of Matisse and the Fauves. He helped found the Neue Künstlervereinigung München in 1909, lived in Switzerland during the First World War, and later painted series of simplified human heads in glowing colour. He settled in Wiesbaden, was banned by the Nazis, and died there in 1941. The Washington Still Life of 1900 is an early work.","q1232255":"Jean Alaux was born in Bordeaux in 1786 and trained there and in Paris, where he studied with Pierre-Narcisse Guérin and won the Prix de Rome in 1815. He lived at the Villa Medici in Rome as a pensioner of the French Academy and returned there as its director from 1847 to 1852. He painted for the French state, notably large historical scenes for the museum of French history that King Louis-Philippe created at Versailles. The Met's portrait of Léon Pallière in his room at the Villa Medici, painted in 1817, is a famous record of the life of young artists in Rome, showing the cluttered room of a fellow pensioner. Alaux died in Paris in 1864.","q551132":"Jean-Baptiste Isabey was born in Nancy in 1767 and went to Paris as a young man, where he studied with the miniaturist Dumont and with Jacques-Louis David and became the most fashionable French miniature painter of his day. He survived the Revolution and became the court painter and designer of Napoleon, drawing and painting the emperor and his family, designing costumes and ceremonial for the Coronation of 1804, and teaching the empress Marie-Louise. He kept his position under Louis XVIII and Charles X, and after the fall of Napoleon he drew the delegates to the Congress of Vienna in 1815. The Met's portraits of Napoleon, dated 1810 and 1812, come from the height of his fame. He died in Paris in 1855.","q731533":"Jean-Baptiste Joseph Pater was born in Valenciennes in 1695, the son of a sculptor, and trained locally before going to Paris. He became the only pupil of Antoine Watteau, a fellow native of Valenciennes, though the two quarrelled; Watteau, dying in 1721, is said to have recalled him and taught him in his last months. Pater then took up Watteau's themes of elegant outdoor parties and made them his own, painting fêtes galantes, bathers, concerts and also soldiers on the march in a lighter, more decorative manner, as in the Met's Fair at Bezons and Concert Champêtre. He was received into the Royal Academy in 1728, but he suffered from poor health and died at forty in 1736.","q2327270":"Jean-Baptiste Marie Pierre was born in Paris in 1714 and studied with Charles-Joseph Natoire before winning the Prix de Rome in 1735 and spending five years in Italy. Back in Paris he was received into the Royal Academy in 1741 and became a successful history painter, producing religious and mythological pictures for churches and for the Duke of Orléans, including decorations at the Palais-Royal. In 1770, after the death of François Boucher, he was named First Painter to the King and director of the Academy, a post he held until his death in 1789. His reputation today is that of a capable academic painter. The Met's Death of Harmonia, about 1740 to 1741, dates from the years around his return from Italy.","q737137":"Jean-Baptiste Oudry was born in Paris in 1686, the son of a painter and picture dealer, and studied with Michel Serre and then the portraitist Nicolas de Largillière, from whom he learned his rich handling of colour. He was received into the Royal Academy in 1719 and became the leading French painter of animals and hunting scenes, painting dogs, game and the royal hunts of Louis XV. From 1726 he designed tapestries for the Beauvais manufactory, of which he became director in 1734, and later for the Gobelins, and he made a famous series of illustrations for La Fontaine's Fables. In 1749 he painted a portrait of Clara, the rhinoceros then touring Europe. His trompe l'oeil still lifes such as A Hare and a Leg of Lamb are admired. He died in 1755.","jean_baptiste_pater":"Jean-Baptiste Pater was born in Valenciennes in 1695 and became the pupil and chief follower of Antoine Watteau. In 1721 Watteau, then dying, took him back into his studio after an earlier quarrel, and Pater took the older master's art as his model. His own career in Paris was spent painting small, bright scenes of fashionable people dancing, bathing, playing music and picnicking in gardens, typical of the French Rococo. The Cleveland Spring and Summer, from a set of seasons, and Dancers in a Pavilion show this decorative gift. He was admitted to the Royal Academy in 1728, his work was collected by Frederick the Great, who bought many pictures for his palaces, and Pater died young, in 1736, aged forty.","q2518370":"Jean-Baptiste Perronneau was born in Paris in 1715 and trained as an engraver with Laurent Cars and as a painter with Charles-Joseph Natoire. He became one of the great French portraitists in pastel and oil, exhibiting at the Salon from 1746 and becoming a member of the Academy in 1753. He was the rival of Maurice Quentin de La Tour, whom he matched in insight into character, though he worked with a more tender, subtle touch. Often away from Paris, he worked for merchants and officials in Orléans, Bordeaux, Lyon and the Netherlands, and he died in Amsterdam in 1783. The Met's portrait of Olivier Journu, dated 1756, shows his gift for the quiet, convincing likeness.","q21997244":"Jean-Baptiste Ponce Lambert is recorded here as a Swiss painter, with a question mark, and little else is known of him. The Met owns his Portrait of a Man, dated 1801, painted in the first year of the new century, when Napoleon was First Consul of France and many portraitists in French-speaking lands worked in a simple, sober Neoclassical style. Nothing certain about his birth, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The picture is valued now as a record of one unnamed sitter and of the fashions of the years around 1800.","q932136":"Jean Bellegambe was born in Douai about 1470 and lived there until his death in 1535, painting altarpieces for the great abbeys of what was then the Habsburg Netherlands. Known as the master of colours for his glowing reds, blues and greens, he worked in a late Gothic style touched by Italian ideas, filling his panels with crowds of saints, angels and kneeling donors. His chief work, the Anchin Polyptych, commissioned by Abbot Charles Coguin for the abbey of Anchin, is now in Douai. The Met's Cellier Altarpiece, of 1511 to 1512, and the portrait of Coguin, from about 1511 to 1520, belong to the same monastic world. He is regarded as the leading painter of Douai in his day.","jean_bernard_restout":"Jean Bernard Restout was born in Paris in 1732 into a family of painters: his father, Jean Restout, was a leading history painter, and his great-uncle was Jean Jouvenet. He trained with his father and became a member of the Royal Academy, painting religious and mythological pictures in a cooler, more classical manner than his father's. His Sleep, from about 1771, now in Cleveland, shows an early Neoclassical taste for clear outlines, simplified forms and calm. He lived through the Revolution, which he supported, and died in 1797. His fame has always been overshadowed by that of his father and of Jacques-Louis David, but his work is a useful marker of the change in French taste in the years before 1789.","q22667535":"Jean Christophe Alexandre is recorded as a painter active between 1834 and 1839, and he is known here from a pair of portraits dated 1834, Thomas Dillard and Mrs. Thomas Dillard (Matilda Kuhl). Matching husband-and-wife portraits were a staple of the portrait trade, painted for couples who wanted a record of their dress and standing at modest cost. Nothing certain about Alexandre's birth, training, origins or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and the Met's catalogue entries give only the dates of his activity. His portraits of the Dillards are valuable chiefly as records of a particular couple of the 1830s.","q2305074":"Jean Claude Richard, known as the Abbé de Saint-Non, was born in Paris in 1727 into a wealthy family and held a church title without ever following a priest's career. He devoted his life to art as an amateur painter, etcher and patron, and became a friend and supporter of the young Fragonard and Hubert Robert, with whom he travelled in Italy in 1760 and 1761 and lived at the Villa d'Este in Tivoli. He later published the great Voyage pittoresque of Naples and Sicily (1781 to 1786), a lavishly illustrated book with plates after leading artists, some of them aquatints that he made himself. The Met's Two Sisters of 1770, a subject also painted by Fragonard, shows how close the two men worked. He died in 1791.","q378800":"Jean Clouet, also called Janet, was a painter at the court of Francis I of France, recorded in the king's service from the 1510s and dead by 1541. He is thought to have been born in the Low Countries about 1480 and to have come to France as a young man, and his son François Clouet succeeded him as court painter. He is celebrated for his portraits of the king and his courtiers, in oil and in chalk drawings, which record faces with sober, almost unflinching accuracy. The Met's portraits of Guillaume Budé, the scholar whose advice led Francis I to found the Collège de France, and of Charles de Cossé, Count of Brissac, date from the 1530s. Many works once given to him are now assigned to others.","q707729":"Jean François de Troy was born in Paris in 1679, the son and pupil of the portraitist François de Troy, and trained in Rome from 1699 to 1706. Returning to France, he joined the Royal Academy in 1708 and became a leading painter of religious and mythological subjects and of lively scenes of fashionable life, such as the Met's Declaration of Love and The Garter, both of the 1720s. He designed tapestry cartoons for the Gobelins, notably the Story of Esther, for which the Met's Triumph of Mordecai is a preparatory picture, and in 1738 he became director of the French Academy in Rome, a post he held until 1751. He died in Rome in 1752.","q148458":"Jean-François Millet was born in 1814 into a peasant family at Gruchy, on the Normandy coast, and trained at Cherbourg and then in Paris under Paul Delaroche. He began by painting portraits and charming pastoral nudes, but after the Revolution of 1848 and a cholera outbreak he moved in 1849 to the village of Barbizon, on the edge of the forest of Fontainebleau, where he lived and worked for most of his remaining life. His subject was the dignity of peasant labour, in The Sower of 1850, The Gleaners of 1857 and The Angelus. Conservative critics found his labouring figures threatening, and some called him a socialist. He died in 1875, and Van Gogh and the Impressionists revered him.","q19629527":"Jean-François Montessuy was born in 1804 and died in 1876, and he is known here from a single picture, Pope Gregory XVI Visiting the Church of San Benedetto at Subiaco, dated 1843. The pope, who reigned from 1831 to 1846, is shown at the monastery near Rome where Saint Benedict lived as a hermit around the year 500 before founding the Benedictine order. The picture records a papal visit as a topical event, in the manner of a history painting. Nothing more about Montessuy's training, travels or other works is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess.","q21598778":"Jean François de la Vallée is recorded as a painter active between 1785 and 1815, and he is known here from a single portrait, Mrs. Nathaniel Bowen (Margaret Blake), painted about 1805. Nothing certain about his birth, training or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The sitter is named in the museum's title by both her married and her maiden name, which makes the picture a documented likeness rather than an anonymous one. It is valued today as a record of a woman of about 1805, painted in the years when the simple, high-waisted dress of the Empire style was fashionable on both sides of the Atlantic.","q1280412":"Jean-Georges Vibert was born in Paris in 1840, the grandson of the engraver Jean-Pierre-Marie Jazet, and studied under Félix Barrias and at the École des Beaux-Arts. He became one of the most successful painters of the Third Republic, specialising in small, polished, comic scenes of cardinals, monks and priests in rich robes, gossiping, bargaining and dining, which poked gentle fun at clerical worldliness. His pictures were prized by American collectors, and the Met's The Missionary's Adventures, in which a missionary tells his story to a group of prelates, shows his sense of comic character. He also wrote plays and a treatise, La science de la peinture, published in 1891. He died in 1902.","q1523362":"Jean-Laurent Mosnier was a French portrait painter, born about 1743 and dead in 1808. He worked in oil, pastel and miniature and exhibited at the Salon from the 1780s, painting fashionable ladies and members of the court. The Revolution disrupted his career: he left France in the 1790s and worked abroad, including in England and Russia, and he returned to Paris in 1801. The Met's portrait of Louis XVI, dated 1790, was painted when the king was still on his throne but the monarchy's authority was fast failing; Louis was executed three years later. The picture is valued as a record of the king in the first year after the fall of the Bastille.","q277738":"Jean-Marc Nattier was born in Paris in 1685, the son of the portraitist Marc Nattier and a miniaturist mother, and trained with his father and by drawing the Rubens cycle in the Luxembourg Palace, which he engraved. In 1717 Peter the Great, visiting Paris, invited him to Russia, but he declined. He was received into the Academy in 1718, then lost his savings in the collapse of John Law's financial system in 1720 and turned to portraiture. As portraitist to Louis XV's court he became famous for his flattering likenesses of the king's daughters and the queen, and for portraits historiés showing ladies as goddesses, such as the Met's Madame Bergeret de Frouville as Diana. His style fell from favour in his last years, and he died in 1766.","q6171195":"Jean Michelin was a French painter, born about 1616 and dead in 1670, active in the 1650s in Paris. He painted scenes of peasants, tradesmen and street life in a sober, realist manner close to that of the Le Nain brothers, with muted colours and a quiet dignity, and some of his works have been mistaken for theirs. The Met's Baker's Cart, signed and dated 1656, is among his best-known pictures and is valued as a rare record of everyday trade in seventeenth-century France. Little else is documented of his life and training in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His reputation rests on a small number of signed pictures.","q374713":"Jean Pillement was born in Lyon in 1728, the French silk capital, and began as a designer of ornament and textiles. He travelled widely, working in London, Lisbon, Madrid, Vienna, Warsaw, Italy and Paris, often for royal and noble patrons, and became one of the most famous designers of chinoiserie: light Rococo fantasies of exotic birds, flowers and pagodas, published as engraved pattern books and used for textiles, porcelain and wallpaper across Europe. He also painted landscapes, seascapes and pastoral scenes in a soft, pastel palette. His marine pictures, such as the Met's Shipwreck in a Storm of 1782, show a taste for drama. He returned to Lyon and died there in 1808.","q3170062":"Jean Urbain Guérin was born in Strasbourg in 1761 and settled in Paris, where he became one of the leading French miniature painters of the Revolution and the Empire. He exhibited at the Salon from 1793 and painted many of the political and military leaders of the day, including the generals Kléber and Hoche and Bonaparte, as well as deputies of the Constituent Assembly. The Met's portrait of Alexandre de Lameth, a leading member of that assembly, painted about 1789 to 1790, comes from the first months of the Revolution, and the Portrait of a Woman of about 1815 comes from the end of the Napoleonic age. His small, clear, finely drawn likenesses were prized by sitters who wanted a precise record of a face. He died in 1836.","q18560378":"Jeanne Philiberte Ledoux was born in Paris in 1767 and became a painter at a time when women were barred from the Royal Academy's schools and had to find teachers privately. She is usually described as a pupil of Greuze, and, like him, she painted tender portraits and genre scenes of young women and children. She exhibited at the Salon from 1793, when the exhibition was open to all artists, and went on showing there for many years. The Met's Portrait of a Man, about 1790, comes from the very start of her career and shows the clear, unaffected manner she learned from her teacher. She died in 1840.","q8013556":"William Jennys was an itinerant American portrait painter who worked in New England and New York in the years around 1800 and is recorded as active between about 1795 and 1807. The dates 1727 to 1853 given with his name in the museum listing cannot be right, and his real birth and death dates are not known. His portraits are plain, clear and strongly drawn, with careful attention to the sitter's face and clothing. The Washington portraits of Asa Benjamin, Mrs. Asa Benjamin and Everard Benjamin, all of 1795, are among his earliest dated works. Little else is known of his life and training, and some of his pictures were long attributed to other hands.","jeptha_homer_wade":"Jeptha Homer Wade was born in Seneca County, New York, in 1811 and began as a carpenter and cabinetmaker before turning to portrait painting in the 1830s. His Cleveland pair of Nathaniel Olds and Sally Avery Olds, dated 1837, comes from this period. In the 1840s he took up photography and then the new electric telegraph, and he became a founder of the Western Union Telegraph Company and one of the richest men in Ohio. He gave Wade Park to the city of Cleveland, and his grandson, Jeptha H. Wade II, was a principal founder of the Cleveland Museum of Art, which stands in that park. Wade is thus better remembered as a businessman and benefactor than as a painter. He died in 1890.","q6180909":"Jeremiah Meyer was born in Tübingen in Württemberg in 1735 and came to London as a boy, where he trained under the miniaturist Christian Friedrich Zincke and became one of the finest English miniature painters of the age. In 1764 he was appointed miniature painter to Queen Charlotte, and later he also worked for the king, and in 1768 he was one of the founding members of the Royal Academy. His portraits on ivory are known for their warm, soft flesh tints and delicate stippling, and they were popular with the court and the fashionable public. The Met's Hannah Mahady, about 1760, is an early work. He died in 1789.","q22577466":"Jeremiah Paul is recorded here with the dates 1775 to 1820 and is known from a single Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1800, in the Met. Nothing certain about his birth, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The sitter's name has not been recorded either, so the picture survives as a likeness of an unknown man by a little-known painter, made at the turn of the nineteenth century, when portrait painters in both Britain and America were still working in the manner of Reynolds and Gilbert Stuart. Its value now lies in its date and in the plain, direct record it gives of a face of that time.","q3177291":"Jeremiah Theus was born in Switzerland about 1716, probably in Chur, and emigrated with his family to Charleston, South Carolina, in 1735. For more than thirty years he was the leading portrait painter in the colony, advertising in the South Carolina Gazette from 1740 and painting the families of the planters, merchants and officials who made Charleston rich on rice and indigo. His portraits are formal and carefully drawn, with attention to lace, silk and jewels. The Met's pairs of Gabriel Manigault and his wife, painted in 1757, and John Dart and his wife, about 1772 to 1774, show his range and the long span of his career. He died in Charleston in 1774.","q52154962":"Elizabeth Gilbert Jerome was born in 1824 and died in 1910, and she is known here from a single picture, American Landscape, dated 1872, in the National Gallery of Art. She is recorded as a woman painter at a time when landscape painting in America was still dominated by men of the Hudson River School. Little else is documented about her training, travels or other works in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The picture's title and date place it in the years after the Civil War, when images of the American countryside were cherished as symbols of national recovery, and it survives as a rare record of her work.","q11724276":"Jerome B. Thompson was born in Massachusetts in 1814 and began as a portrait painter in New England before turning to genre scenes, landscapes and still lifes. He is best remembered for sentimental pictures of rural life and for views in the New England mountains, such as The Belated Party on Mansfield Mountain, of 1858, which shows a party of travellers overtaken by evening on the highest peak in Vermont, and for flower pieces like Summer Flowers of 1859, both now in the Met. His pictures were popular with the growing middle-class public that bought engravings and paintings of homely American subjects in the 1850s and 1860s. The Met also has his Song of the Waters, about 1870. He died in 1886.","q2580100":"Jervis McEntee was born in Rondout, New York, on the Hudson, in 1828 and studied in 1850 with Frederic Church, who encouraged him to become a landscape painter. Settling in New York, he took a studio in the Tenth Street Studio Building and became a respected member of the Hudson River School, elected to the National Academy in 1861. His pictures are quiet, melancholy views of late autumn and early winter, with bare trees and grey skies, such as Autumn in the Catskills and Saturday Afternoon. He was a close friend of Church, Sanford Gifford and Worthington Whittredge, and his diaries, kept for many years, are a valuable record of the New York art world. He died in 1891.","joachim_anthoniz_wtewael":"Joachim Wtewael was born in Utrecht in 1566, the son of a glass painter, and trained with his father and with local painters before travelling to France and, probably, Italy in the late 1580s. He returned to Utrecht about 1592 and became a founder of its painters' guild and the leader of Dutch Mannerism, painting small, brilliantly coloured mythological scenes with twisting, elongated nudes, such as The Judgment of Paris of 1602, now in Cleveland. He was also a successful flax merchant and served as a city councillor and officer of the civic guard, so he was never dependent on painting. His style grew old-fashioned after 1620, as followers of Caravaggio took over Utrecht, but he kept on painting until his death in 1638.","q533314":"Joachim Beuckelaer was born in Antwerp in 1533 and trained with his uncle Pieter Aertsen, with whom he developed a new kind of painting: large market and kitchen scenes crowded with meat, fish, vegetables and fruit, with a small biblical episode, such as Christ in the House of Martha and Mary, glimpsed in the background. He became a master in the Guild of St Luke in 1560 and worked in Antwerp until his death about 1574. His careful, glowing pictures of food and country people won the favour of collectors in the Netherlands and abroad, and later Flemish still-life painters learned from them. The Met's Fish Market, of 1568, is a fine example of this unusual type of picture.","joachim_patinir":"Joachim Patinir was born about 1480 in or near Dinant, in what is now Belgium, and worked in Antwerp, where he joined the Guild of St Luke in 1515 and died in 1524. He is regarded as the first Western painter to specialise in landscape. In his panels the sacred story, a Saint Jerome in penitence or the Flight into Egypt, is a small episode in a vast panorama of blue-green rocks, rivers, towns and distant seas viewed from a high point, a type of picture later called a world landscape. Albrecht Dürer attended his wedding in 1521 and called him the good landscape painter. Patinir often worked with Quinten Massys, who painted the figures. The Met's Penitence of Saint Jerome, about 1515, is a typical work.","q541644":"Joachim Wtewael, born in Utrecht in 1566, was the leading Dutch Mannerist painter and, at the same time, a prosperous flax merchant and city official. He trained with his father, a glass painter, and with local masters, and spent time in France in the late 1580s before settling in Utrecht about 1592. His small, exquisitely finished mythological scenes, with their twisting, elongated bodies, bright colour and playful eroticism, were collected by learned and wealthy patrons. The Met's Golden Age of 1605 shows the classical myth of a lost age of innocence, with a crowd of figures at ease in a landscape. He died in 1638, and his style, so sophisticated and artificial, was long neglected before being admired again in the twentieth century.","q576120":"Job Berckheyde was born in Haarlem in 1630 and, like his younger brother Gerrit, is said to have studied with Frans Hals. He joined the Haarlem guild in 1654 and made a long journey through Germany with Gerrit, working at the court of the Elector Palatine in Heidelberg. Back in Haarlem he painted market scenes and bakers' and butchers' shops, church interiors and city views, in a smooth, clear style. He died in Haarlem in 1693. The Met's Interior of the Sint-Bavokerk, Haarlem, about 1676, shows the great late Gothic church in the heart of the city and records the plain, whitewashed look of a Protestant church of that time.","q704585":"Johan Barthold Jongkind was a Dutch painter, born in 1819 at Lattrop and dead in 1891, who spent most of his working life in France. A pupil of Andreas Schelfhout in The Hague, he moved to Paris in 1846 and painted views of the Seine and of the Normandy coast in quick, luminous watercolours and oils, working beside Eugène Boudin; in 1862 the young Claude Monet met him at Le Havre. He was unstable and drank heavily, and from the 1860s he was cared for by his companion Joséphine Fesser, a Dutch art teacher, with whom he settled in the Isère. The Washington Towpath, of 1864, shows his fresh handling of water and sky, which made him a forerunner of Impressionism.","q702272":"Johan Joseph Zoffany was born near Frankfurt in 1733 and trained in Regensburg and Rome before coming to England about 1760, where he worked first as an assistant to the portrait painter Benjamin Wilson. The actor David Garrick commissioned him to paint scenes from the stage, which made his name; George III and Queen Charlotte became patrons, and he was a founding member of the Royal Academy in 1768. He specialised in conversation pieces, small group portraits of families and friends in their homes, and also painted single portraits such as the Met's Reverend Philip Cocks, of the late 1760s. He travelled to India in 1783 and returned in 1789 with a store of pictures and stories. He died in London in 1810.","q18600112":"Johan Mengels Culverhouse was born in the Netherlands in 1820 and emigrated to the United States, where he worked in New York and later in Philadelphia, and he is recorded as dying about 1891. He painted harbour scenes, landscapes and genre pictures, including winter scenes with moonlight, snow and skaters on ice. The Met's Skating on the Wissahickon, of 1875, shows skaters on the creek that runs through a wooded valley in Philadelphia's Fairmount Park, a favourite place for winter pleasure. His reputation, though modest, rests on lively, small-scale views of American leisure of this kind, which were popular with middle-class buyers in the years after the Civil War and the Centennial.","q921230":"Johan Thomas Lundbye was born in Kalundborg, Denmark, in 1818 and studied at the Academy in Copenhagen, where he belonged to the generation of painters of the Danish Golden Age. He travelled in Germany and Italy in 1845 and 1846, but his best-loved pictures show the Danish countryside, with cows, burial mounds, fields and lakes, painted with a fresh feeling for light and national character. When the First Schleswig War broke out in 1848 he volunteered for the army and was killed within weeks, aged twenty-nine, by an accidental gunshot. The Met's An Evening beside Lake Arresø, of about 1837, dates from his teens and already shows his sensitive eye for the Zealand landscape.","q708274":"Johan Vilhelm Gertner was born in Copenhagen in 1818 and trained at the Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts. He became a favoured portrait painter of the Danish royal family and court, and painted portraits of King Frederick VII, who came to the throne in 1848. The Met's A Bedroom in Bernstorff Palace near Copenhagen, of about 1845, is a small interior in the tradition of Danish Golden Age room paintings, in which painters recorded rooms with loving attention to furniture, light and the traces of everyday life. It is valued today as a vivid document of a royal summer residence of the 1840s. Gertner died in 1871.","johann_carl_koch":"Johann Carl Koch is recorded with the dates 1806 to 1900 and is known here from one picture, a Portrait of a Gentleman dated 1827, in the Met. If the dates are right, he painted it when he was about twenty-one, which would make it a youthful work. Nothing certain about his birthplace, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The sitter's name has not been recorded either, so the picture survives as a likeness of an unknown man by a little-known painter, and it is valued for its date and for the record it gives of a face of the late 1820s.","q1395300":"Johann Esaias Nilson was born in Augsburg in 1721 and spent his life there. He trained as a painter and engraver and became one of the best-known Rococo artists of Augsburg, then a leading centre for the printing of engravings. He is famous for lively ornamental prints in the rocaille style, for satirical scenes of fashionable life, and for miniature and pastel portraits. His prints were widely used by craftsmen as patterns and helped spread the taste of Augsburg Rococo across Germany. The Met's Portrait of a Man shows his skill as a portraitist. He died in Augsburg in 1788, and his reputation today rests chiefly on his engravings.","q169311":"Johann Friedrich Overbeck was born in Lübeck in 1789 and studied at the Vienna Academy, where he and a group of friends, dissatisfied with its routine classicism, founded the Brotherhood of St Luke in 1809. In 1810 he moved to Rome, where the group lived in the abandoned monastery of Sant'Isidoro and were nicknamed the Nazarenes for their long hair and biblical dress. Overbeck, who converted to Catholicism in 1813, sought to revive the clear outlines, pure colour and devout spirit of early Italian and German painting, and he painted frescoes in Rome, among them those of the Casa Bartholdy. He stayed in Rome for life and died there in 1869. The Met's Banishment of Hagar, of 1839 to 1841, shows his smooth, linear religious style.","johann_georg_platzer":"Johann Georg Platzer was born in 1704 at St. Michael in Eppan, in South Tyrol, and trained in Passau and then in Vienna, where he spent most of his career. He became popular for small, brilliantly coloured cabinet pictures, often on copper, of elegant company at feasts, concerts and masquerades, set in lofty halls with marble columns and attendants, as well as biblical and mythological subjects. His crowded interiors, glittering costumes and smooth surface made him a favourite of collectors, and the Met's Amusement at the Court, of about 1738, is typical. The Cleveland Artist's Studio belongs to the 1740s or 1750s. He died in Vienna in 1761.","johann_heinrich_sch_nfeld":"Johann Heinrich Schönfeld was born in Biberach, Swabia, in 1609 and trained in Ulm before going to Italy in the early 1630s, where he spent almost twenty years working in Rome and Naples. He came under the influence of Poussin and of the Dutch and Flemish painters of Rome, and developed a lively, brightly coloured style of small figures in landscapes and ruins, painting battles, biblical and classical scenes and subjects from ancient history. He returned to Augsburg about 1651, where he painted altarpieces and designs for engravers until his death in 1684. His Abduction of the Sabine Women, about 1640, in Cleveland, is a good example of his energetic Italian-period work.","q213973":"Johann Heinrich Wilhelm Tischbein was born in Haina, Hesse, in 1751, a member of the large Tischbein family of painters, and studied with his uncles in Kassel and Hamburg before travelling in 1779 to Italy, where he lived for many years in Rome and Naples. He became a friend of Goethe, whom he painted in the famous Goethe in the Roman Campagna, of 1787, and in 1789 he became director of the Naples Academy. He also drew the ancient Greek vases in Sir William Hamilton's collection, which were published in a splendid book that helped spread the Neoclassical taste for Greek decoration. Forced to leave Naples by the French, he later worked in northern Germany, and from 1808 as court painter to the Duke of Oldenburg. The Met's portrait of the Heckscher children dates from 1803. He died in 1829.","johann_k_nig":"Johann König was born in Nuremberg in 1586 and died in Augsburg in 1642, and he spent his working life as a painter of small, finely finished pictures for private collectors. His biblical and mythological scenes belong to the tradition of cabinet painting, often on copper, that was popular in Augsburg and Nuremberg in the early seventeenth century and owed much to the example of Adam Elsheimer. The Cleveland Resurrection of Christ, dated 1622, is an example of the type. Little else is securely recorded of his training, patrons or travels in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His name is better known to specialists in German painting than to the general public.","q700279":"Johann Liss was born in Holstein, in northern Germany, about 1597 and trained in the Netherlands, where he worked in Haarlem and Amsterdam and learned from the Dutch genre painters. He travelled on to Paris and then to Italy, where by the 1620s he was working in Venice and Rome, and became one of the most original painters in the Venetian circle, combining Dutch and Flemish naturalism, Caravaggio's light and Venetian colour in pictures of quick, flickering paint. His subjects included peasants, soldiers, mythological and religious scenes, such as The Satyr and the Peasant, Aesop's fable of the man who blew hot and cold. He died of plague in Verona in 1631, aged about thirty-four, leaving few paintings but a strong influence on later Venetian painters.","q21117075":"Johann Nikolaus Grooth was a German portrait painter of the eighteenth century, and little is documented of his life. The Met owns a Portrait of a Woman by him, without a firm date. Nothing certain about his birth, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. Portrait painters were among the busiest artists in eighteenth-century Germany, working for courts, merchants and officials, and many of them are known today by only a few signed pictures. His portrait of an unnamed woman is valued as a record of a face and costume of the period, and it shows the competent, workmanlike skill of a provincial portraitist.","johann_zoffany":"Johann Zoffany, born near Frankfurt in 1733, trained in Regensburg and Rome before settling in England in the 1760s, where he became the leading painter of conversation pieces: informal group portraits of families and friends in their homes. The Cleveland Dutton Family in the Drawing Room of Sherborne Park, Gloucestershire, painted about 1772, is a fine example, showing the family at ease among their furniture and books. He was a founding member of the Royal Academy in 1768, enjoyed the patronage of George III and Queen Charlotte, and lived in Italy in the 1770s and in India from 1783 to 1789. He died in London in 1810.","q2604246":"Johannes Beerstraten was born in Amsterdam in 1622 and died there in 1666. He painted views of Amsterdam in snow and in summer, harbours and seascapes, as well as winter scenes with skaters and sledges, in a clear, bright and rather decorative style. His best-known pictures record the old Town Hall of Amsterdam after it burned down in 1652, and the city's snow-covered streets and canals, which are valued as topographical records. The Met's Skating at Sloten, near Amsterdam, shows his fondness for winter leisure in the flat, frozen country around the city. He died young, at forty-four, and his reputation rests on a relatively small number of clear and well-composed pictures.","q629856":"Johannes Lingelbach was born in Frankfurt in 1622 and moved as a boy to Amsterdam, where he trained, and then spent time in Paris and in Rome, where he lived from 1647 to 1650. In Rome he painted lively scenes of street life in the manner of the Bamboccianti, with peasants, quack doctors, beggars and travellers. Returning to Amsterdam in 1650, he became a popular painter of harbours, markets and Italian scenes, and he was often called on to add the figures in other artists' landscapes and cityscapes. His pictures are small, bright and well organised. He died in Amsterdam in 1674. The Met's Peasants Dancing, of 1651, belongs to his first years back in Holland, and its Battle Scene of 1671 shows his late work.","q41264":"Johannes Vermeer was baptised in Delft in 1632, the son of a silk weaver who also kept an inn and dealt in paintings. He became a master of the Delft Guild of St Luke in 1653, the year he married Catharina Bolnes, a Catholic, and he later served as the guild's head. How he trained is not recorded. He painted slowly, and fewer than forty paintings survive, mostly quiet interiors with a few figures lit from a window, in luminous blues, yellows and greys. He sold chiefly to a small circle of Delft collectors, above all Pieter van Ruijven. Money troubles after the French invasion of 1672 overwhelmed him, and he died in 1675, leaving his widow with a large family and heavy debts. His fame, lost for two centuries, was revived by the critic Théophile Thoré-Bürger in the 1860s.","q577974":"Johannes Verspronck was born in Haarlem about 1600 or 1603 and spent his whole life there. He was the son of the painter Cornelis Engelsz and, according to tradition, studied with Frans Hals, whose rival he became as the city's second great portraitist. Verspronck's portraits are smoother, cooler and more finished than Hals's, and he was prized for his delicate rendering of lace, collars and silk, and for his dignified, calm sitters. His most famous work is the Girl in a Blue Dress of 1641, now in Amsterdam. The Met's Portrait of a Man, dated 1645, belongs to his mature period. He died in Haarlem in 1662.","q22667538":"John Archibald Woodside was born in Philadelphia in 1781 and made his living as a sign and ornamental painter before turning to still life, landscape and genre scenes. He became one of the earliest still-life painters in Philadelphia outside the Peale family, and the Met's two pictures of peaches, grapes, an apple and a pear, from about 1825, show his plain, clear handling of fruit on a table. These simple arrangements of fruit were a staple of American painting in the early republic, when abundance was a favourite symbol of national prosperity. Little else is securely recorded of his training in the sources available here. He died in 1852, and his work is valued today mainly for its freshness and honest observation.","q20203252":"John Alexander McDougall was born in 1810 or 1811 and died in 1894, and he is known here from two pictures at the Met: a portrait of the statesman Henry Clay, about 1840, and a Portrait of a Boy. Henry Clay of Kentucky, the Whig leader known as the Great Compromiser, was at that time one of the most famous men in America, having already run twice for President, and in 1840 he lost the Whig nomination to William Henry Harrison; portraits of him were in demand. Nothing certain about McDougall's birthplace, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess.","q20116177":"John Barry is listed here as a British painter with the dates 1784 to 1827, but the Met dates his three portraits to about 1790, when a painter born in 1784 would have been a small child, so the dates probably belong to a different man or are mistaken. The portraits themselves are a Portrait of a Woman, a Portrait of a Man, and a Portrait of a Man Said to Be John Durham. Nothing certain about the painter's life and training is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The three pictures are valued as modest likenesses of the late eighteenth century, when portrait painting flourished in Britain.","q6222351":"John Bogle was a British miniature painter, born in 1746 and dead in 1804. The Met owns two of his miniatures, A Woman with the Initials MCC, dated 1773, and A Man with the Initials AC, dated 1774, whose sitters are known only by their initials. Miniatures of this kind, small portraits painted in watercolour on ivory, were treasured keepsakes, given to relatives and lovers and often set in lockets, bracelets or cases. Nothing certain about Bogle's training, patrons or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His two small portraits survive as quiet records of ordinary sitters of the 1770s.","q528274":"John Brett was born in Reigate, Surrey, in 1831 and entered the Royal Academy Schools in 1853. Inspired by the Pre-Raphaelites and the critic John Ruskin, he painted landscapes in minute detail, notably The Stonebreaker of 1857 to 1858 and the glittering Val d'Aosta of 1858, which Ruskin admired. From the 1870s he turned to coastal pictures, painted from a yacht in which he cruised the coasts of Britain; Queen Victoria bought his British Channel Seen from the Dorsetshire Cliffs in 1871. He was also a keen amateur astronomer. The Met's Kynance, of 1888, shows the Cornish cove in the bright, precise manner of his later years. He died in 1902.","q1699546":"John Carlin was born in Philadelphia in 1813 and became deaf in early childhood. He was educated at the Pennsylvania Institution for the Deaf and Dumb and became a skilled miniature painter, travelling to Europe in the late 1830s to study in London and Paris. Settling in New York, he painted portraits in oil and miniature, landscapes and genre scenes, such as the Met's After a Long Cruise of 1857, as well as charming portraits of children, like The Allen Children and The Thompson Children of the 1840s. He also wrote poetry and was one of the best-known deaf Americans of his time, a respected figure in the deaf community. He died in 1891.","q19894842":"John Durand was a portrait painter active in the American colonies between about 1765 and 1782, and little is securely known of his life. He is known for portraits of colonial families, painted in bright colour with careful attention to costume and to the ribbons, lace and silks that showed a family's standing. The Met's two portraits of children, Boy of the Crossfield Family and Susannah or Mary Bontecou, date from the later 1760s, and the uncertainty in their titles shows how much about the sitters has been lost. Nothing certain about Durand's birthplace, training or later travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess.","q978526":"John Frederick Peto was born in Philadelphia in 1854 and studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts. He became a friend of the trompe l'oeil painter William Harnett and painted the same kind of picture: realistic still lifes of everyday objects, such as books, letters, violins, mugs and pipes, arranged on shelves and doors. In 1889 he moved to Island Heights, New Jersey, a Methodist seaside community, where he played the cornet at religious revivals and lived quietly, selling little. After his death in 1907 many of his pictures were sold as Harnett's, whose signature was added to them; the art historian Alfred Frankenstein sorted them out in the mid-twentieth century, restoring Peto's reputation for softer, more melancholy still lifes such as The Old Cremona.","q982284":"John Frederick Kensett was born in 1816 at Cheshire, Connecticut, the son of an English-born engraver, and trained as an engraver himself before turning to painting. In 1840 he sailed for Europe with fellow painters, staying seven years in England, France and Italy, and on his return in 1847 he became one of the leading landscapists of the Hudson River School. In his later work he painted calm, light-filled views of the coasts, lakes and rocks of New England and New York, with level horizons and softly glowing skies, qualities often called luminist, as in Beacon Rock, Newport Harbor of 1857. He was a founder and trustee of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and he died in New York in 1872.","q2659120":"John Gadsby Chapman was born in Alexandria, Virginia, in 1808 and studied in Philadelphia and in Italy before settling in New York. He painted the Baptism of Pocahontas for the Rotunda of the US Capitol, completed in 1840, a huge picture that made his name, and he illustrated books; his American Drawing-Book, published in 1847, taught generations of Americans to draw. In 1850 he moved with his family to Rome, where he painted views of the Roman Campagna, the countryside around the city, which he sold to American visitors. The Cleveland Harvest on the Roman Campagna, of 1868, and the Met's Roman Campagna, of 1864, show this late specialisation. He died in 1889.","q2919973":"John George Brown was born in Durham, England, in 1831 and trained as a glass cutter in Newcastle upon Tyne, studying art in the evenings and then at the Trustees' Academy in Edinburgh. In 1853 he emigrated to the United States, working at a Brooklyn glassworks while he studied at the National Academy of Design, and from about 1860 he made his living entirely as a painter. He became enormously popular for sentimental pictures of ragged but cheerful street children, newsboys and bootblacks, which he painted in great numbers and sold at high prices, and he kept a studio in the Tenth Street Studio Building. The Met's The Music Lesson of 1870 and To Decide the Question of 1897 show his well-liked subjects. He died in 1913.","john_harrison_witt":"John Harrison Witt was an American portrait painter, born in 1840 and dead in 1901. The Cleveland Museum of Art has his portrait of Hinman B. Hurlbut, painted in the 1870s, a formal likeness of the kind that businessmen and civic leaders commissioned in the decades after the Civil War, when oil portraits were still a mark of standing even though photography was everywhere. Nothing certain about Witt's birthplace, training, teachers or other works is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His reputation rests chiefly on this portrait and on the dates of his long life, which spanned the whole of the Gilded Age.","q6238441":"John Hazlitt was born in Marshfield, Gloucestershire, in 1767, the eldest son of a Unitarian minister and the elder brother of the essayist William Hazlitt, whom he introduced to painting. He trained in London, reportedly under Joshua Reynolds, and exhibited at the Royal Academy from 1788, working as a portrait and miniature painter in London and in provincial towns. He painted sitters from the circle of Unitarians and dissenters to which his family belonged, and he is today best known as the brother of a more famous writer. The Met's Samuel Goodwin, Sr., is one of his portraits. Little else is securely recorded of his career in the sources available here. He died in 1837.","q20127273":"John Henry Brown was born in Lancaster, Pennsylvania, in 1818 and became one of the leading American miniature painters of his generation, working in Philadelphia. His delicate watercolours on ivory are noted for their clear outlines and bright, accurate colour, and he painted many prominent sitters. In 1860 he travelled to Springfield, Illinois, to paint a miniature of Abraham Lincoln from life. The Met's group of his small portraits includes a self-portrait of about 1846 and portraits of Adaline Peters Brown and Ada Brown, probably members of his family, made in the 1840s. His career ran through the decades when the photograph slowly took over the market for small likenesses, and he died in 1891.","q20127348":"John Henry Hill was born in West Nyack, New York, in 1839, the son of the English-born aquatint engraver and painter John William Hill, who taught him. He joined the American Pre-Raphaelites, the group that followed Ruskin's call to paint nature with scrupulous truth, and his Study of Trap Rock (Buttermilk Falls), of 1863, painted on the Hudson, is among the finest examples: a close view of rocks, moss and ferns painted leaf by leaf. He later worked mostly as an etcher and illustrator, making etchings and views of the Hudson Valley, and he lived in New York State until his death in 1922.","q1342683":"John Henry Twachtman was born in Cincinnati in 1853 and trained there at the School of Design and with Frank Duveneck, who took him to Munich in 1875 and Venice in 1877. After study at the Académie Julian in Paris in 1883 to 1885 he returned to America, and by 1890 he had settled on a farm at Greenwich, Connecticut, where he painted the brook, waterfall and winter woods around his home in soft, silvery harmonies of grey, green and white. With Childe Hassam, J. Alden Weir and others he founded the Ten American Painters in 1897. He taught at the Art Students League and died in Gloucester, Massachusetts, in 1902. The Met's Horseneck Falls and Waterfall show his late style.","q5804007":"John Hesselius was born in Philadelphia in 1728, the son of the Swedish-born painter Gustavus Hesselius, who taught him. He worked as a portrait painter in Maryland and Virginia, painting the planters and merchants of the Chesapeake in a bright, firm manner, as in the Met's portraits of Archibald McCall and Mrs. Richard Galloway. He is remembered for a famous story: in 1763 the young Charles Willson Peale, then a saddler, visited him and, in exchange for a saddle, was taught the rudiments of painting, an encounter that began Peale's career. Hesselius married a wealthy widow in 1763 and settled at Primrose Hill, near Annapolis, where he died in 1778.","q1934948":"John Hoskins was an English miniature painter, born about 1590 and dead in 1665, and the leading miniaturist at the court of Charles I. He began as a painter in oil but turned to miniatures and carried the tradition of Nicholas Hilliard into a more naturalistic, robust style. He painted the king, Queen Henrietta Maria and members of the court, and he trained his nephew Samuel Cooper, who became the greatest English miniaturist of the century. The Met's portraits of Endymion Porter, about 1630, of Philip Wharton, Fourth Baron Wharton, in 1648, and of Dr. Brian Walton, the editor of the London Polyglot Bible, in 1657, show that he kept working for patrons through the Civil War and the Commonwealth.","john_james_audubon":"John James Audubon was born in 1785 at Les Cayes, Saint-Domingue, now Haiti, the illegitimate son of a French naval officer, and was raised in Nantes, France. In 1803 he was sent to a family property at Mill Grove, near Philadelphia, where he began to study birds; he is credited with the first bird-banding experiment in America, tying silver thread to the legs of young phoebes. After business failures in Kentucky he devoted himself to painting, and he travelled the country to draw birds life-size from freshly shot specimens wired into lifelike poses. Unable to find a publisher in America, he went to Britain, where The Birds of America (1827 to 1838) was engraved by Robert Havell as 435 hand-coloured plates. He died in 1851.","q3120087":"John La Farge was born in New York in 1835 to French émigré parents and came to painting by an unusual road, after studying law and travelling in Europe, where he spent a short time in Paris with Thomas Couture. In Newport he worked with William Morris Hunt and painted fine landscapes and flower pieces, such as the early Flowers on a Window Ledge. He became America's leading muralist, decorating Trinity Church in Boston with H. H. Richardson in the 1870s, and in 1880 he patented opalescent glass, which he used in stained-glass windows that rivalled Tiffany's. With the historian Henry Adams he travelled to Japan in 1886 and to the South Seas in 1890 to 1891, and The Entrance to the Tautira River, Tahiti, belongs to this later period. He died in 1910.","q16857946":"John Mare was born in 1739 and died in 1802 or 1803, and he is known as a portrait painter active in colonial New York in the 1760s, the decade of his Jeremiah Platt, dated 1767, now in the Met. Colonial portraitists of that generation often borrowed poses and costumes from English mezzotint portraits, and Mare's picture of a New York sitter belongs to that tradition of provincial, carefully detailed likenesses. Little is recorded of his training, patrons or later career in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His portrait of Platt is valued today as a rare record of a New York sitter of the colonial period.","q2676888":"John Mix Stanley was born in Canandaigua, New York, in 1814 and learned to paint as a sign and coach painter, then as a portrait painter in Detroit and elsewhere. He became a painter of the American West and its people, travelling with the army expedition of General Stephen Kearny to California in 1846 and with the Pacific Railroad Survey under Isaac Stevens in 1853, and painting portraits of Native Americans of many nations, as well as landscapes and scenes of Western life. His Indian gallery, which he exhibited in Washington, was largely destroyed in the fire of 1865 at the Smithsonian Institution, where most of it was kept. He died in Detroit in 1872. The Met's Williamson Family, about 1841 to 1842, shows his early portraiture.","q1701195":"John Neagle was born in Boston in 1796 and grew up in Philadelphia, where he trained with the drawing master Pietro Ancora and took lessons from Thomas Sully, whose stepdaughter he later married. He became one of the leading portrait painters of Philadelphia, painting prominent citizens, clergy and actors in a vigorous, direct manner. His most famous picture, Pat Lyon at the Forge, of 1826 to 1827, shows a blacksmith in his leather apron, a prosperous lock-maker who insisted on being shown at his trade rather than in a gentleman's finery. Neagle also painted Henry Clay and served as president of the Artists' Fund Society. The Cleveland and Met portraits of the 1820s to 1840s show his sturdy, straightforward manner. He died in 1865.","john_opie":"John Opie was born in 1761 near St Agnes in Cornwall, the son of a carpenter, and was discovered as a boy by the satirist and physician John Wolcot, who took him to London in 1781 and presented him as the Cornish Wonder. His strong, dark portraits and striking pictures of beggars, old men and children won him quick fame, and he turned to history subjects; he became a Royal Academician in 1787 and professor of painting at the Academy in 1805. In 1798 he married the novelist Amelia Alderson, as his second wife. His lectures on painting were published after his death. He died in 1807 and was buried in St Paul's Cathedral. The Cleveland Street Singer and Child shows his taste for humble subjects.","q20127418":"John Paradise was born in 1783 and died in 1833, and he is known here from a single portrait at the Met, Mrs. Warren Rogers, painted about 1821. Nothing certain about his birthplace, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The sitter is named only through her husband, as was usual in titles of the period, and the picture is valued as a record of a woman of the early 1820s, painted in the plain, direct manner that was typical of portraits of that time. It is a reminder of how many competent portraitists of the early nineteenth century are now known only by a name and a few dates.","q3182324":"John Quidor was born in Tappan, New York, in 1801 and grew up in New York City, where he trained in the studio of the portrait painter John Wesley Jarvis, as did Henry Inman. He worked as a sign and banner painter and tried with little success to make a living with his pictures, which show comic and fantastic scenes from the tales of Washington Irving, such as Ichabod Crane, Rip Van Winkle and The Devil and Tom Walker of 1856, and from James Fenimore Cooper, such as Leatherstocking's Rescue of 1832. His crowded, shadowy, expressively distorted figures were little understood in his own time, and he died in obscurity in Jersey City in 1881. They were rediscovered in the twentieth century.","q6254141":"John Ramage was born in Ireland about 1748, settled in North America in the 1770s, and was working in New York by 1777. After the Revolution he became the leading miniature painter in the city, making delicate portraits on ivory in pale colours with neat, crisp outlines. His sitters included George Washington, painted about 1789, Governor George Clinton, Gulian Ludlow and other New York leaders, and the Met holds many of his works. He later moved to Montreal, where he died in 1802. His miniatures, small enough to be worn or carried, were prized as keepsakes in the years when New York was the national capital, and they are now valued as a record of the faces of the early republic.","q21995169":"John Rasmussen was born in 1828 and died in 1895, and he is known here from a single picture, Berks County Almshouse, 1880, in the Met. The picture is a topographical view of a county institution in Pennsylvania, painted in a plain, careful style, with the buildings drawn precisely and with its date and subject placed in the title. Nothing certain about Rasmussen's origins, training or other works is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. The picture is valued as a document of a nineteenth-century county almshouse, an institution that sheltered the poor, the sick and the old.","john_robinson":"John Robinson was born in 1774 and died about 1829, and he is known here from a single picture, a Portrait of a Lady dated 1822, in the Met. Nothing certain about his birthplace, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess, especially since Robinson is a very common name. The sitter's name has not been recorded, so the picture survives as a likeness of an unknown woman by a little-known painter, and it is valued for its date and for the record it gives of dress and manner in the early 1820s, when the high-waisted Empire fashion was still current.","q6255995":"John Russell was born in Guildford in 1745 and was apprenticed to the pastel painter Francis Cotes in London, becoming the leading English pastel portraitist of his generation. A convert to Methodism in his youth, he led a strict religious life and kept diaries. He wrote a manual, Elements of Painting with Crayons, in 1772, became a Royal Academician in 1788, and was appointed crayon painter to George III and the Prince of Wales. His soft, pale portraits of families and children were fashionable, and he also painted in oil, as in the Met's pictures of the Shurlock and Godschall families. He also made a remarkably detailed lunar globe and maps of the moon in pastel, the fruit of years of telescope observation. He died in Hull in 1806.","q22691515":"John S. Porter is recorded as an American portrait painter active between 1822 and 1834, and he is known here from a single picture, Stephen Danforth Hassam, painted about 1820 to 1825. Nothing certain about Porter's birthplace, training or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. Portraits of this kind, made by local and travelling painters in the years after the War of 1812, were usually commissioned by families who wanted a lasting, affordable likeness. The picture is valued today as a documented portrait of a named sitter, and as a modest example of the plain, direct portrait style of the early republic.","john_semon":"John Semon was an American landscape painter, born in 1852 and dead in 1917, and he is known here from Edge of the Woods, painted in the 1890s and now in Cleveland. The picture, with its quiet subject of a wood's border, belongs to the late nineteenth-century American taste for quiet, close-up views of the countryside, a taste that replaced the grand panoramas of the Hudson River School. Nothing certain about Semon's birthplace, training or career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His reputation rests on this single picture and on the dates of a life that ran from the Civil War era to the First World War.","q155626":"John Singer Sargent was born in Florence in 1856 to American parents who lived in Europe, and grew up travelling through Italy, France, Germany and Switzerland. He studied in Paris with Carolus-Duran from 1874 and became a brilliant portrait painter, making his name with Madame X (Virginie Amélie Avegno Gautreau), shown at the Salon of 1884, where the dress and the sitter's bold pallor caused a scandal that led him to settle in London. He painted the rich and fashionable on both sides of the Atlantic, and also landscapes, watercolours and scenes of travel in Spain, Morocco, Egypt, Venice and the Alps. After 1907 he refused most portrait commissions. The Met bought Madame X from him in 1916. He died in 1925.","q6258207":"John Smart was born in Norfolk in 1741 and studied in London at William Shipley's drawing school, winning prizes from the Society of Arts. He became the leading English miniature painter of his generation, exhibiting at the Society of Artists and at the Royal Academy, and his miniatures are admired for their precise, delicate stippling and clear, cool tones. He signed them with his initials and dated them, an unusual habit at the time. In 1785 he went to India, where he worked in Madras for ten years painting officials of the East India Company and Indian rulers, and returned to London in 1795. The Met's miniatures include portraits of the writer Charlotte Lennox, in 1777, and of Sir William Hood. He died in 1811.","q3182482":"John Smibert was born in Edinburgh in 1688, trained as a house painter, and went to London in 1709, where he studied and copied pictures; he then spent three years in Italy, from 1717 to 1720, copying old masters in Florence and Rome. In 1728 he joined Bishop George Berkeley's project for a college in Bermuda and sailed with him, landing in Newport, Rhode Island, in 1729. The college never materialised, and Smibert settled in Boston, where he became the foremost portrait painter of the colonial town. His most famous work is the group portrait of Berkeley and his companions, painted in 1729. His studio, filled with copies of old masters, later let young Americans such as Copley see European painting, and he is said to have designed Faneuil Hall. He died in 1751.","q369263":"John Trumbull was born in 1756 in Lebanon, Connecticut, the son of the colony's governor, Jonathan Trumbull. He served as an officer in the Revolutionary army, briefly as an aide to George Washington, and in 1780 travelled to London to study with Benjamin West. Arrested there as a reprisal for the execution of the British spy John André, he was held for months and then expelled. He later painted dramatic history pictures such as the Met's Sortie Made by the Garrison of Gibraltar of 1789, and in Paris in 1786 and 1787 he met Thomas Jefferson, whose portrait of 1788 is also at the Met. His life's work was a series of scenes of the Revolution, four of which hang in the Capitol Rotunda. He gave his paintings to Yale, and its Trumbull Gallery became the first university art museum in America. He died in 1843.","q1661665":"John Vanderlyn was born in Kingston, New York, in 1775 and studied with Gilbert Stuart before Aaron Burr paid for his study in Paris, where he trained from 1796 under François-André Vincent, one of the first Americans to study there. His Ariadne Asleep on the Island of Naxos, painted between 1809 and 1814, was admired in Paris and criticised in Philadelphia for its nude. He painted portraits of Burr and of other leading Americans, such as Egbert Benson, and cherished the idea of panoramas: his view of Versailles, painted in 1818 to 1819, was shown in his own Rotunda in New York's City Hall Park, which lost money. He received a commission for the Landing of Columbus in the Capitol Rotunda, completed in 1847. He died in Kingston in 1852.","q1702146":"John White Alexander was born in Allegheny, Pennsylvania, in 1856, was orphaned young, and began as an office boy at Harper and Brothers in New York, where his drawing talent was noticed; he went on to study in Munich, Venice and Paris. He became a leading American portraitist of the Gilded Age, painting the poet Walt Whitman in 1889 and many men and women of literary society, and his Art Nouveau-influenced decorative paintings of graceful women in flowing dress, such as Repose of 1895, made him famous in Paris and New York. He also painted murals, including the Evolution of the Book for the Library of Congress, and served as president of the National Academy of Design from 1909 until his death in 1915.","q3182782":"John Wollaston was an English-born portrait painter who worked in the American colonies from about 1749, arriving in New York and then travelling to Philadelphia, Maryland, Virginia and Charleston. He painted hundreds of portraits of colonial merchants, planters and their wives in a graceful, polished style, with silky draperies and sitters with distinctive, almond-shaped eyes, a trait that makes his portraits easy to recognise. Many of his sitters were wealthy families who wanted to be shown in the latest English fashions. He worked in the colonies for about a decade and then left for good. The Met's portraits of Cadwallader Colden, Joseph Reade, William Axtell and their wives, painted in New York about 1749 to 1752, are typical of his early American work.","q16062203":"John Wood Dodge was born in New York in 1807 and became a miniature painter, making delicate portraits on ivory in the 1830s and 1840s. His sitters included the painter and chronicler of Native American life George Catlin, whom he portrayed in 1835, and the Met also holds his portraits of members of his own family, among them Edward S. Dodge and Kate Roselie Dodge. His small, clear likenesses were prized as keepsakes in the years before photography. Nothing more about his training and his later career is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. He lived to the age of eighty-six and died in 1893.","q18508415":"John Woodhouse Audubon was born in Henderson, Kentucky, in 1812, the younger son of John James Audubon, and grew up amid the family's travels and money troubles. He helped his father paint backgrounds and quadrupeds for The Birds of America and, as his father's sight failed, painted most of the plates for The Viviparous Quadrupeds of North America, on which he worked with the Reverend John Bachman, whose daughter Maria he married. He travelled to Texas in 1845 and 1846 to collect specimens, and in 1849 led a gold-rush expedition to California in which many men died of cholera. The Met's Hudson's Bay Lemming, of 1846, belongs to the quadrupeds project. He died in New York in 1862.","q20204799":"John Johnston was born in Boston in 1753 and belonged to the Johnston family of Boston craftsmen and artists, who made engravings, decorative painting and portraits in the colonial town. He worked as a portrait painter in Boston and died in 1818. The Washington portrait of John Peck, painted about 1795, is a plain, direct likeness of the kind that was typical of American portraits in the years after the Revolution. Little else is securely recorded of his training, travels and career in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His portrait of Peck is the work by which the National Gallery represents him, and it is valued as a documented likeness of a named sitter.","q22673690":"Jonathan K. Trego was born in 1817 and died about 1868, and he is known here from a single picture, Trappers, painted about 1855, in the Met. Trappers and hunters were favourite subjects of American genre painting in the 1840s and 1850s, when Eastern buyers who had never seen the frontier delighted in pictures of fur trappers, canoes and wilderness camps. Trego's picture belongs to that popular type. Nothing certain about his birthplace, training or travels is recorded in the sources available here, and it would be wrong to guess. His reputation rests on this one canvas and on the dates of a life that ended in the years just after the Civil War.","q515796":"Joos van Wassenhove, known in Italy as Justus of Ghent, was a Flemish painter whose life survives only in outline. He is recorded as a master in Antwerp in 1460 and in Ghent in 1464, and by 1473 he was in Urbino, working for Federico da Montefeltro. There he painted the Communion of the Apostles for the Confraternity of Corpus Domini, finished in 1474, and he is credited with some of the portraits of famous men made for the ruler's study. Nothing certain is recorded of his teacher or of his later years. The Metropolitan's Adoration of the Magi, dated 1472 to 1474, belongs to the period of his move south. He is valued as a rare Netherlandish painter who worked at an Italian court.","q21498175":"Little is recorded of Samuel Jordan beyond his dates, 1803 to 1836, and the single picture that hangs here, the Eaton Family Memorial of 1831. Memorial pictures of this kind were popular in the early years of the American republic. They set a family's names and dates of death among mourning emblems such as urns, tombs and weeping willows, and they were often made by amateur, itinerant or self-taught hands rather than by trained academicians. Families kept them as keepsakes, and later collectors prize them as American folk art. No firm details of Jordan's training, home or patrons are given here, and nothing about his life should be inferred from the picture alone. He died in his early thirties.","jordanus_hoorn":"Jordanus Hoorn was a Dutch painter who lived from 1753 to 1833, a span that took in the fall of the old Dutch Republic and the Napoleonic years. Few details of his training or patrons are given here. The picture in Cleveland, a View of the Village of Eemnes dated 1778, shows a small community near the River Eem, in the province of Utrecht, painted when he was about twenty-five. Dutch topographical painters of the later eighteenth century kept alive the careful village and townscape tradition of the Golden Age, usually for local buyers who wanted a faithful record of a place they knew. Hoorn is a minor name today, known chiefly to specialists in Dutch landscape and topography.","q517574":"José Villegas y Cordero was a Spanish painter born in Seville, who trained there and in Madrid before moving to Rome as a young man. In Rome he came under the influence of his compatriot Mariano Fortuny, whose glittering small scenes of connoisseurs, soldiers and Moroccan life set the fashion for finely finished costume pictures. Examining Antique Arms, dated 1870, comes from this early period. Villegas went on to paint bullfights, Venetian scenes and large figure compositions, and he spent much of his career in Rome. In 1901 he returned to Madrid as director of the Prado, a post he held until 1918. He died in Madrid in 1921. His polished manner was admired in his lifetime, though Fortuny's name has outlasted his.","q1581203":"Joseph Alexander Ames was born in Roxbury, New Hampshire, in 1816 and died in New York in 1872. He worked as a portrait painter, first in Boston and later in New York, and earned his living, like most painters of his day, from professional men, merchants and public figures. The Metropolitan's portrait of James Topham Brady, dated 1869, shows a leading New York lawyer who had helped defend Congressman Daniel Sickles at his sensational murder trial of 1859. It is a good example of the commissioned likeness that supported a mid-century American portraitist. Little more can be said here with confidence about his training or patrons, and he is known today mainly to specialists in nineteenth-century American portraits.","q263569":"Joseph Anton Koch was born in 1768 in a Tyrolean mountain village and died in Rome in 1839. He trained at the Karlsschule in Stuttgart, travelled in Switzerland, and in 1795 settled in Rome, apart from a stay in Vienna from 1812 to 1815. There he was influenced by the painter Asmus Jacob Carstens and helped shape the German Romantic landscape, a monumental and carefully ordered view in which small figures stand in a vast, Poussin-like scene of crags, rivers and stormy light. Heroic Landscape with Rainbow, dated 1824, is a fine example. He also painted Dante frescoes in the Casino Massimo and moved in the circle of the German Nazarenes. Often poor and quarrelsome, he published a satire on Roman art life in 1834. His reputation has risen steadily since.","q20646101":"Joseph Bartholomew Kidd was born in Scotland in 1808 and died in 1889. The four small bird pictures that hang in Washington belong to the oil copies he made after John James Audubon's Birds of America, a commission from Audubon himself in about 1830 to 1833. Audubon, then publishing his great book as engraved plates, wanted painted versions of his compositions to raise money, and Kidd's oils, among them the Orchard Oriole, Yellow Warbler, Sharp-Tailed Finch and Black-Backed Three-Toed Woodpecker, follow the plates with scrupulous care while adding the full colour of oil paint. They are now valued for their own sake and as an episode in the history of American natural-history illustration. Little else of Kidd's career is documented here.","q21995140":"Little is recorded of Joseph Bordes, a French portrait painter whose three pictures in the Metropolitan Museum, dated 1808, about 1810 and 1812, are the main evidence of his career. They fall within the years of the First Empire, when Paris and the provinces supported a crowded trade in portraits of officials, officers, merchants and their wives. Portraitists of the period commonly worked in the clear, smooth, closely drawn style taught in the school of Jacques-Louis David, with plain backgrounds and careful attention to costume. His birth and death dates, training and patrons are not documented here, and no more should be claimed. He is a reminder of how much of the period's portraiture was made by competent painters who left few traces in the record.","q47455747":"Joseph Dunkerley is known chiefly by the period in which he worked, recorded as active from 1776 to 1806, and by the portrait of William Gale, dated 1776, that hangs here. His birthplace, training and patrons are not given in the sources used for this book, and nothing should be guessed from the name alone. Portraitists active in these decades earned their living by supplying likenesses to merchants, officers, clergymen and their families, in the years before the camera, and their pictures are now valued as records of the faces and clothes of the time. Dunkerley belongs to the large company of such painters whose names survive while the details of their lives have been lost.","q18508246":"Joseph Fagnani, born Giuseppe Fagnani in Naples in 1819, was an Italian-born portrait painter who worked in New York and died in 1873. He painted fashionable sitters, and little else of his career is documented here. The Metropolitan Museum holds his set of the nine Muses, dated 1869: Calliope, Clio, Erato, Euterpe, Melpomene, Polyhymnia, Thalia, Terpsichore and Urania. Painted together in a single year, the pictures were evidently planned as one scheme, probably as decoration for a room, with each Muse given her own figure and attribute from the classical tradition. His training, patrons and later reputation are not recorded in the sources used here, and he is not a household name today.","q21995172":"Joseph H. Hidley lived from 1830 to 1872 and spent his working life in and around Poestenkill, a small farming and mill village in Rensselaer County, New York, east of Albany. He was a self-taught painter of the kind later called a folk or naive artist, and his chief subjects were the village, houses and fields he knew. View of Poestenkill, New York, about 1870, is typical of his work. Pictures like this were made for neighbours and local patrons rather than for city collectors, and his name is known today mainly through collectors and museums of American folk art. His pictures are valued both as paintings and as a record of a rural New York community in the years after the Civil War.","q20127345":"Joseph Kyle, who lived from 1815 to 1863, was an American portrait painter of the mid-nineteenth century, a time when a town portraitist could still make a living painting merchants, doctors and their families, before photography took over much of the trade. The two pictures by him at the Metropolitan, a Portrait of a Lady dated 1861 and a Self-portrait of about 1859, belong to his last years. Details of his birthplace, training and patrons are not given here, and none should be assumed. The self-portrait is a useful reminder that even modest portraitists often turned their skills on themselves, leaving a record of their own faces when the record of their careers is thin.","q159758":"J. M. W. Turner was born in Covent Garden, London, in 1775, the son of a barber, and entered the Royal Academy Schools at fourteen. He first made his name with watercolours of cathedrals and ruins, became a full Academician in 1802, and travelled constantly, to Switzerland, France and Italy, visiting Venice in 1819, 1833 and 1840. Patrons such as Walter Fawkes of Farnley Hall supported him, and John Ruskin later became his great champion. His oils moved from the clear topography of Saltash with the Water Ferry, painted in 1811, toward luminous, nearly abstract light and weather in works like Whalers and the Venice views. He died in 1851 and was buried in St Paul's Cathedral, leaving the contents of his studio to the nation; the Turner Bequest is now largely at Tate Britain.","q22001988":"Little is recorded of Joseph Marie Bouton, whom the Metropolitan Museum lists as probably French, with dates of 1768 to 1823. The one picture that hangs here, A Woman Playing a Harp, has no known date, and the museum marks his nationality with a question mark. The subject belongs to the years around 1800, when the harp was the fashionable instrument for a lady in Paris, played by Marie Antoinette and later by the Empress Josephine, and when portraits and genre scenes of women at music were popular with the new buyers of the age. No training, patrons or other works are documented here for Bouton, and none should be supposed.","q647934":"Joseph-Marie Vien was born in Montpellier in 1716 and won the Prix de Rome in 1743, spending the years from 1744 to 1750 in Italy. Back in Paris he turned from the lighthearted rococo toward a cooler, simpler style inspired by antiquity and by the newly excavated paintings of Herculaneum, as in The Seller of Cupids of 1763, often cited as an early neoclassical picture. He directed the French Academy in Rome from 1775 to 1781 and became First Painter to Louis XVI in 1789. His great pupil was Jacques-Louis David. Napoleon later made him a senator and a count. He died in Paris in 1809, honoured as a founder of French neoclassicism, though his own pictures are now less admired than those of his pupils.","q3185485":"Joseph-Pierre Picot de Limoëlan de Clorivière was born at Broons, in Brittany, in 1768 and is better known for his politics than for his painting. A royalist conspirator, he took part in the plot of December 1800 in which a bomb exploded in the rue Saint-Nicaise in Paris as Napoleon passed, narrowly missing him. He escaped to the United States, where he painted portraits and miniatures for a time. His portrait of Mrs. Phineas Miller, dated 1806, shows Catherine Littlefield, the widow of the Revolutionary general Nathanael Greene. He later entered the Catholic priesthood, was ordained in Baltimore in 1812, and served as a priest in America until his death in 1826. His few surviving pictures are curiosities of an extraordinary life.","q2286906":"Joseph Siffred Duplessis was born in Carpentras in 1725 and learned to paint from a Carthusian painter-monk, Frère Imbert, before studying in Rome. He settled in Paris and by the 1770s had joined the Royal Academy, becoming one of the most sought-after portraitists of Louis XVI's reign. He avoided grand allegory in favour of honest, lively likenesses of scientists, musicians and courtiers, among them the composer Gluck, the king himself, and Madame de Saint-Morys, wife of a noted collector. In 1778 Benjamin Franklin, then American envoy in Paris, sat to him, and the portrait was copied and engraved so often that it fixed Franklin's image in France. Duplessis later became a curator at Versailles and died there in 1802.","q6287192":"Joseph Steward was born in 1753 and was a minister before he was a painter, trained for the ministry and served as a clergyman in Connecticut. He took up portraiture in the Hartford area, where he is said to have studied with Ralph Earl, and his early portraits, like the Metropolitan's Jonathan Dwight of about 1790, share Earl's plain, direct manner. In 1797 he opened Steward's Museum in Hartford, which combined natural-history specimens, curiosities and pictures, and which later moved into the city's State House. He is remembered as a New England painter-minister and museum founder rather than as a major artist, and his portraits are valued as honest records of the faces of the early republic. He died in 1822.","q15428881":"Joseph Whiting Stock was born in 1815 in Springfield, Massachusetts, and died there in 1855. An accident at about the age of eleven left him largely confined to a wheelchair for the rest of his life, and he made his career as a painter without leaving his home town. He became the busiest portraitist in Springfield, and his account book records hundreds of sitters, from local merchants to children, whom he painted in a plain, direct manner with clear outline and little shadow. He also painted landscapes and signs and taught drawing. John and Louisa Stock, dated 1845, shows two sitters who share his surname. His work is prized today as American folk portraiture and as a record of the New England middle class of his day.","q4128223":"Joseph Wood was born in 1778 in Clarkstown, New York, and died in 1830. He began as a miniature painter in New York, where he worked in partnership with the portraitist John Wesley Jarvis in the early 1800s, and later painted in Philadelphia and Washington, producing both miniatures and small oil portraits. Naval officers, statesmen and gentlemen sat to him. The Metropolitan's Thomas Macdonough, about 1818, shows the American commander who won the Battle of Lake Champlain in 1814, and the portraits of Joseph Dugan, George William Fairfax and the artist himself show the same clear drawing and fine finish. He is counted among the leading American miniaturists of his generation.","q214062":"Joseph Wright was born in Derby in 1734 and is known as Wright of Derby. After two periods as the pupil of the London portraitist Thomas Hudson he returned to Derby, where he painted gentry and manufacturers and, from the 1760s, his famous night scenes lit by lamp, candle or moon. He was the painter of the Midlands Enlightenment, a friend of Josiah Wedgwood and Erasmus Darwin, and among the first to put scientific experiments into pictures. From 1773 to 1775 he travelled in Italy, where he sketched Vesuvius in eruption and the moonlit coast near Naples. Virgil's Tomb by Moonlight, with Silius Italicus Declaiming, painted in 1779, grows from that journey. He died in Derby in 1797.","joseph_wright_of_derby":"Joseph Wright of Derby spent almost all of his career in his home town, apart from study in London with Thomas Hudson in the 1750s, a stay in Italy from 1773 to 1775, and two years in Bath. He went to Bath in 1775 hoping to take over the portrait trade left by Gainsborough, but returned to Derby in 1777. Portraits of Derbyshire landowners, industrialists and their families gave him a steady income; the Portrait of Colonel Charles Heathcote, about 1771 to 1772, is one of these. His most original pictures were scenes of science and labour by candlelight, such as A Philosopher Lecturing on the Orrery, and his sitters included Erasmus Darwin and, later, the cotton spinner Richard Arkwright. He died in 1797.","q958068":"Joshua Johnson, sometimes spelled Johnston, is the first African American painter known to have worked as a professional portraitist. He is thought to have been born into slavery in Maryland in the 1760s and to have been freed in 1782. By the 1790s he was working in Baltimore, where in a newspaper notice of 1798 he called himself a self-taught genius who had met many obstacles in his studies. Over the next quarter-century he painted dozens of portraits of Baltimore merchants, ship captains and their children, in a plain, linear style with upright poses, careful costume and small touches such as books, fruit and toys. Edward and Sarah Rutter, about 1805, is a double portrait of this kind. His life is thinly documented, and his work was rediscovered only in the twentieth century.","joshua_reynolds":"Joshua Reynolds was born in Plympton, Devon, in 1723 and was apprenticed in London in 1740 to the portraitist Thomas Hudson. A visit to Italy from 1749 to 1752 taught him to look hard at Raphael, Michelangelo and the Venetians, and after settling in London in 1753 he soon became the leading portrait painter in Britain. His Grand Manner raised portraiture by giving sitters the poses of classical statues and old masters, and by using rich colour and bold light. In 1768 he became the first President of the Royal Academy, was knighted the next year, and delivered fifteen Discourses on art to its students. Samuel Johnson and Edmund Burke were among his friends. He lost the sight of one eye in 1789 and died in 1792.","q12068855":"Joshua Shaw was born in Billingborough, Lincolnshire, in 1776, began as a sign painter, and became a self-taught landscapist who exhibited at the Royal Academy and the British Institution. He sailed to Philadelphia in 1817 and made himself one of the early painters of American scenery, issuing a set of aquatint views, Picturesque Views of American Scenery, between 1819 and 1821. He was also an inventor, and claimed an early percussion cap for firearms, though others disputed the credit. The Deluge towards Its Close, about 1813, dates from his English years and belongs to the taste for dramatic biblical catastrophes in the age of Turner and John Martin. He died in the United States, around 1860.","q16211318":"Almost nothing is recorded of Joshua Wilson Faulkner, a painter the Metropolitan Museum lists as British, without dates. The one picture that hangs here, a Portrait of a Woman, is likewise undated, and the museum gives no sitter. With no secure information about his birth, training, teachers or patrons, it would be a mistake to guess at them. He is known here from a single portrait rather than from documents, and he belongs to the large company of provincial portrait painters in Britain who supplied likenesses to local families and whose lives have left little trace. Research in exhibition records and local archives would be needed to say more about him.","q6790646":"Matthew Harris Jouett was born in Mercer County, Kentucky, in 1788 and trained for the law before serving in the War of 1812. He turned to painting about 1815, and in 1816 he spent some months in Boston as a pupil of Gilbert Stuart, whose remarks on colour and flesh tones he wrote down in notes that became a valued source on Stuart's methods. Returning to Kentucky, he became the leading portraitist of the Ohio Valley, working in Lexington and travelling to New Orleans and other cities for commissions. His sitters included Henry Clay and, in 1825, the Marquis de Lafayette on his American tour. He is said to have painted some three hundred portraits before dying in Lexington in 1827, at the age of thirty-nine.","q16725958":"Jules-Romain Joyant was born in Paris in 1803 and died there in 1854. He specialised in architectural views and is known above all for his pictures of Venice, a city that drew many foreign painters in the 1820s and 1830s. The two canvases in Washington, The Church of San Trovaso and The Scuola di San Marco, both about 1830, show him painting Venetian buildings with close attention to masonry, water and reflection, in the topographical tradition of the city view. He exhibited in Paris and produced views of Italy and France for buyers who wanted travel souvenirs on a larger scale than a print. Few details of his teachers or patrons are given here, and he is a minor but skilful painter of the Romantic age's enthusiasm for travel.","q771658":"Juan Bautista Martínez del Mazo was born around 1612 in the Cuenca region of Castile and died in Madrid in 1667. He became the pupil and assistant of Diego Velázquez and, in 1633, his son-in-law, absorbing the master's manner so thoroughly that some of his pictures were once taken for Velázquez's own. After Velázquez died in 1660 he succeeded him as painter to Philip IV, in 1661. He painted portraits of the royal children, among them the Infanta María Teresa, who became queen of France as the wife of Louis XIV, and a large View of Zaragoza of 1647. Mazo is usually seen as a talented follower, but his landscapes and portraits have a quiet, silvery charm of their own.","q512863":"Juan de Flandes, whose Spanish name means John of Flanders, was a Netherlandish painter trained in the Ghent and Bruges tradition, though his birthplace and teacher are not recorded. He is documented in Spain from 1496, when he entered the service of Queen Isabella of Castile, and painted for her a collection of small devotional panels on the life of Christ, from which the Metropolitan's Marriage Feast at Cana and Christ Appearing to His Mother come. After her death in 1504 he settled in Salamanca and later in Palencia, painting large altarpieces for churches there; the Washington panels of the Annunciation, Nativity, Adoration and Baptism belong to the period. He died in Palencia in 1519. He is now valued for his clear colour and small-scale precision.","q518898":"Juan de Valdés Leal was born in Seville in 1622 and trained in Córdoba, where he worked with Antonio del Castillo. After settling in Seville in 1656 he became, with Murillo, one of the founders of the city's Academy of Painting in 1660, and he later served as its president. His style was fiery, with quick brush strokes, bold light and dramatic colour, in marked contrast to Murillo's gentler manner, and the two were rivals for the city's commissions. He is best known for the Hieroglyphs of Our Last Ends, painted in 1672 for the Hospital de la Caridad at the request of its founder Miguel de Mañara, grim images of death and the end of worldly glory. He died in Seville in 1690.","juan_van_der_hamen_y_l_on":"Juan van der Hamen y León was born in Madrid in 1596 to a family of Flemish origin who served the Spanish court, and he spent his short life there, dying in 1631. He painted portraits and religious subjects for the court of Philip IV, but he is remembered above all for his still lifes, which helped establish the Spanish tradition of the bodegón, the plain, sharply lit arrangement of food and vessels on a table. In works such as the Still Life with Sweets of 1622, dishes of preserved fruit and confectionery are set in firm, ordered rows with great precision of touch. His reputation faded after his early death, but modern scholars count him among the leading Spanish still-life painters.","q433973":"Jules-Joseph Lefebvre was born in 1836 at Tournan-en-Brie, near Paris, and trained in Paris with Léon Cogniet, winning the Prix de Rome in 1861. He became one of the best-known academic painters of the Second Empire and Third Republic, famous for polished female nudes and graceful single figures, among them La Vérité of 1870, now in the Musée d'Orsay. Graziella, painted in 1878, shows the Neapolitan fisher girl of Lamartine's popular romance. Lefebvre was also a celebrated teacher, giving instruction for many years at the Académie Julian in Paris, where large numbers of foreign students, including many Americans and women, learned to draw the figure. He was elected to the Institut in 1891 and died in Paris in 1912. His smooth manner fell from favour with the modernists.","q1284220":"Julian Alden Weir was born in 1852 at West Point, New York, where his father, Robert W. Weir, taught drawing at the military academy. After study at the National Academy of Design he went to Paris in 1873 and worked under Jean-Léon Gérôme, then came under the spell of Bastien-Lepage's open-air naturalism. In 1877 he bought two paintings by Manet in Paris on behalf of a New York collector, Erwin Davis. Back in America he became, with Childe Hassam and John Twachtman, one of the leading American Impressionists, and helped to form The Ten American Painters in 1897. He painted at his Connecticut farm and in the mill town of Willimantic, as in The Factory Village. He died in 1919.","julie_h_beers":"Julie Hart Beers, born in 1835, was the sister of the landscape painters William and James M. Hart and so grew up inside the world of the Hudson River School. She was one of the few women of her day to build a career as a landscape painter, exhibiting at the National Academy of Design in New York in the 1860s and 1870s and painting the woods, streams and mountains of the Catskills, the Adirondacks and neighbouring regions. A Mountain Stream, dated 1870, is typical of the careful, quiet woodland scenes for which she is now valued. She married more than once and was known by several surnames, which has made her work harder to trace. She died in 1913, and her reputation has risen in recent years.","julius_caesar_ibbetson":"Julius Caesar Ibbetson was born in 1759 near Leeds, was apprenticed to a ship painter in Hull, and moved to London in 1777, where he found work with a picture dealer. In 1787 he sailed as draughtsman with Lord Cathcart's embassy to China, a mission that ended when Cathcart died on the voyage out. Back in England he painted rustic scenes and landscapes with small lively figures, often in the manner of Philip James de Loutherbourg, and in 1799 he moved to the Lake District, later settling at Masham in Yorkshire, where he died in 1817. A Storm Behind the Isle of Wight shows his taste for rough weather. He also published a short treatise on mixing colours and varnishes. His reputation, modest in his lifetime, has grown with interest in British landscape.","q70758":"Julius Schrader was a German portrait and history painter, born in 1815 and died in 1900, who worked chiefly in Berlin. He is best known today for the portrait of Baron Alexander von Humboldt, dated 1859, the year of the great naturalist's death at the age of eighty-nine. Humboldt had been famous across Europe and America since his journey through Spanish America with Aimé Bonpland from 1799 to 1804, and his Kosmos, a survey of the natural world, had been a bestseller in the 1840s and 1850s. By his last years he was an international celebrity, and images of him were widely circulated. Few other details of Schrader's training or career are given here.","q1713114":"Julius von Leypold was a German landscape painter, born in 1806 and died in 1874, and few details of his training or patrons are given here. The Metropolitan's Wanderer in the Storm, dated 1835, belongs to the Romantic taste for a lone figure dwarfed by threatening nature, a theme Caspar David Friedrich had made central to German painting a generation earlier. Such pictures answered the nineteenth century's appetite for emotion in landscape and for scenes of travel, weather and danger. The small traveller stands in for the viewer, who feels the force of the storm through him. Leypold is today a minor name, known chiefly through this picture.","q28144":"Willem Kalf was born in Rotterdam in 1619 and is said to have trained there with Hendrick Pot. In the 1640s he lived in Paris, where he painted rustic kitchen interiors with peasants and humble utensils, then returned to the Netherlands and from 1653 lived in Amsterdam, where he died in 1693. In Amsterdam he perfected the pronk still life, a display of costly objects: Venetian glasses, Chinese porcelain, silver ewers, nautilus cups and Turkish carpets, with a half-peeled lemon spiralling out of a dark background into glowing light. He painted for wealthy merchants and collectors, and his meticulous handling of texture and reflection made him the leading Dutch painter of luxury still life.","q505758":"Karl Theodor von Piloty was born in Munich in 1826 and studied at the Munich Academy under Julius Schnorr von Carolsfeld, later visiting Belgium and France, where the large history paintings of Gallait and De Keyser impressed him. He scored his first great success with Seni before the Corpse of Wallenstein in 1855, a stage-like scene of dramatic gesture and meticulous detail, and became a professor at the Munich Academy the next year and its director in 1874. As a teacher he shaped a generation, including Hans Makart, Franz von Lenbach and Gabriel von Max. Thusnelda at the Triumphal Entry of Germanicus into Rome, a theme from ancient German history, exists in a large version in Munich and a smaller one in New York. Piloty died in 1886.","q3195528":"Kerstiaen de Keuninck was a Flemish landscape painter who lived from 1560 to 1632, and few details of his life are given here. His mountain scenes with waterfalls, dark wooded foregrounds and tiny travellers belong to the late Mannerist tradition of Flemish landscape, shaped by Gillis van Coninxloo, who developed the dense, wooded landscape that later painters in Flanders and the Dutch Republic adopted. The Metropolitan's A Mountainous Landscape with a Waterfall, about 1600, is of this type: pictures of this kind typically use a high viewpoint, contrasts of light and shadow, and a deep recession of rocks and trees toward a bluish distance. Keuninck is a minor but characteristic master of the type.","q515553":"Jan van Kessel the Elder was born in Antwerp in 1626, a grandson of Jan Brueghel the Elder through his mother, and trained with Simon de Vos and with his uncle Jan Brueghel the Younger. He became a master in the Antwerp guild in 1645 and spent his career there, dying in 1679. He specialised in small, exact studies of insects, flowers, shells and little animals, painted on copper or panel and set out like specimens on a pale ground, as in Insects and a Sprig of Rosemary of 1653. These pictures suited collectors with a taste for natural history, and he also painted larger still lifes, landscapes and the Four Continents series. His Vanitas Still Life reminds the viewer of the brevity of life. His sons Ferdinand and Jan the Younger also became painters.","q932263":"Thomas de Keyser was born in Amsterdam about 1596, the son of the sculptor and architect Hendrick de Keyser, and became the city's leading portrait painter in the 1620s, before Rembrandt arrived about 1631. His speciality was the small full-length portrait of a merchant, magistrate or scholar in elegant dress, set in a tidy interior with clear light, cool colour and close attention to costume and accessories. His Constantijn Huygens and His Clerk of 1627 is among his best-known works. He also painted large group portraits of civic guards and surgeons. The Portrait of a Gentleman Wearing a Fancy Ruff, dated 1627, belongs to this high point. After Rembrandt's rise Keyser's smoother style fell out of fashion, and he died in Amsterdam in 1667.","q2958563":"Charles Bird King was born in Newport, Rhode Island, in 1785 and studied in New York with Edward Savage, then in London from 1805 to 1812 under Benjamin West, alongside other young Americans in West's circle. He settled in Washington, DC, in 1816 and spent the rest of his life there, painting portraits of politicians, among them Henry Clay. His best-known work was a series of portraits of Native American delegates who visited the capital, painted for Thomas McKenney's Indian Gallery and reproduced in lithographs in a famous three-volume history. Most of the originals burned in the Smithsonian fire of 1865. The Poor Artist's Cupboard, about 1815, a witty still life of an impoverished painter's possessions, is also much loved. He died in 1862.","q940505":"Johann Koerbecke was a painter of Münster in Westphalia, recorded there from the 1440s and dying in 1491. He is known above all for the high altarpiece of the Cistercian abbey of Marienfeld, painted in 1456 to 1457 and now divided among several museums; The Ascension, in Washington, is one of its scenes. The panels show tall figures in sombre colour, with strong, plain contours and deep-set faces, and an awareness of Netherlandish painting that reached Westphalia by trade and travel. He ran the leading workshop of his city, and his work is a main source for the study of mid-fifteenth-century German painting.","q2292936":"Little is recorded of Philip van Kouwenbergh, a Dutch painter of flowers who lived from 1671 to 1729. The Flowers in a Vase in Washington, about 1700, belongs to the great age of Dutch flower painting at the turn of the eighteenth century, when artists such as Rachel Ruysch and Jan van Huysum filled their pictures with showy blossoms, insects and dewdrops, and wealthy collectors paid high prices for them. Such pictures were usually built from carefully studied flowers of different seasons, so they were imaginary arrangements rather than records of a real bouquet. Details of Kouwenbergh's training, teachers and patrons are not given here, and his name is known today mostly to specialists in Dutch still life.","q21403104":"Little is recorded here about Nicolaus Kremer, a German painter who lived from about 1500 to 1553. The Portrait of a Nobleman in Washington, dated 1529, comes from the years when portraiture flourished in Germany under the influence of Dürer, Holbein and Cranach, with half-length sitters shown against plain or patterned backgrounds, in rich furs and caps, often with inscriptions giving date and age. German patrons, both court and civic, wanted honest likenesses, and the best portraits of the age have a sharp, even severe accuracy. Kremer's own training, home town and patrons are not documented in the sources used here, and no further claims should be made about him.","q3270988":"Léon-Germain Pelouse was born in 1838 and became one of the leading landscape painters of the generation after the Barbizon masters, working above all at Cernay-la-Ville, in the wooded valley of Chevreuse near Rambouillet. He is described as largely self-taught, and he painted outdoors through the changing seasons, in a restrained manner with soft grey and brown tones. January: Cernay, near Rambouillet, in the Metropolitan, shows his taste for bare winter trees and subdued light. He exhibited at the Paris Salon, and he died in 1891. Later critics have valued him as a quiet, honest observer of the countryside south-west of Paris, and his pictures of the Cernay district are among the best records of its woods and ponds.","q3271490":"Léonard Defrance was born in Liège in 1735 and died there in 1805. After studying in Rome and Paris he returned to Liège, where he became the city's leading painter of small genre scenes. Following the example of seventeenth-century Flemish and Dutch painters such as Teniers and Ostade, he painted forges, workshops, cabinets of curiosities and popular entertainments, often lit by a glowing fire or lamplight, as in The Forge. He was a man of the Enlightenment who sided with reform, and he criticised the church and the old order in some of his pictures. The Rope Dance, in the Metropolitan, belongs to his scenes of popular life. His reputation has grown in Belgium, where he is regarded as an original eighteenth-century painter of working life.","q3356826":"Giovanni Battista Benvenuti, nicknamed L'Ortolano, meaning the gardener, was a painter of Ferrara who lived in the early sixteenth century, in a generation that included Dosso Dossi and Garofalo. Altarpieces and devotional pictures for Ferrara and nearby towns are his main work, and scholars have seen in them a blend of Ferrarese sharpness, Venetian colour in the manner of Giorgione and Titian, and the influence of Raphael. The Adoration of the Shepherds, dated by the museum to the late 1520s, is a humble, tender treatment of the Nativity. The details of his life are poorly recorded, and the museum gives no dates for him, so further claims about his training and patrons are best left aside.","q1373489":"Stanislas Lépine was born in Caen in 1835 and moved to Paris, where he became a follower of Corot, whose soft grey light and tender mood shaped his own style. He painted the quays and bridges of Paris, the Normandy countryside and the harbours of the north coast, in small, delicately toned pictures such as Pont de la Tournelle, Paris, of 1862, and View of the Louvre. He exhibited at the Salon and in 1874 showed work at the first Impressionist exhibition, though his refined, subdued manner always remained closer to Corot than to Monet. He was never as famous as the Impressionists with whom he exhibited, and he died in Paris in 1892. Collectors have since come to value the quiet poetry of his city views.","q21497737":"Émile Lévy was a French painter, born in Paris in 1826 and died in 1890, who is remembered chiefly as an academic painter of history and mythological subjects and as a teacher; he is recorded as a winner of the Prix de Rome in the mid-1850s. The small Rowing Scene: Crowds Watching from the River Banks in Washington, dated only to the late nineteenth century, is a lively view of a regatta with spectators lining the banks, and it is far from the large academic subjects for which he is usually known. Few details of his training and patrons are given here, and his name today is known mostly to students of the French academic tradition of the Second Empire.","lacroix_paul":"Little is recorded of Paul Lacroix, an American painter of still life who lived from 1827 to 1869. His Asparagus, Tomatoes, and a Squash, dated 1865, is a plain tabletop arrangement of vegetables painted at the end of the Civil War, in the careful, close-up manner of American still life of the 1860s, when there was a steady market for small, modest pictures of fruit and kitchen produce. Specific details of his training, the places he worked and his patrons are not given here. Still lifes of food, once thought minor, are now valued for the way they show the abundance of American farms and markets and the close, patient looking of the painters who made them.","q21497641":"Almost nothing is recorded of A. A. Lamb, and the life dates 1784 to 1924 sometimes printed for him cannot be right, since they would give him a span of 140 years. The one picture that hangs here, Emancipation Proclamation, dated 1864 or after, was made soon after Lincoln's proclamation took effect on 1 January 1863, and it belongs to the many commemorations, prints and paintings of emancipation produced during and after the Civil War. Pictures of this kind were made for homes, meeting halls and churches, and they are now valued as expressions of how people of the time marked the end of slavery. Nothing certain is known here of Lamb's training, home or patrons.","q1357759":"Lambert Sustris was a painter from the Netherlands who spent much of his career in Italy; his dates are not firmly known, but he was probably born in Amsterdam about 1515 to 1520. He is recorded in Venice in the 1530s, where he may have worked in Titian's circle, and later in Padua. He painted portraits, religious subjects and mythologies with elaborate landscape backgrounds of ruins, trees and hills, joining a Netherlandish love of landscape to Venetian colour. He later worked in Augsburg, in southern Germany, and his son Friedrich became a court painter in Munich. The Metropolitan's Portrait of a Man shows his skill as a portraitist. Beyond these facts his career is poorly documented, and the museum gives no dates for him.","q188172":"Nicolas Lancret was born in Paris in 1690 and trained first with the painter Pierre Dulin, then with Claude Gillot, who had also taught Watteau. He joined the Royal Academy in 1719 as a painter of fêtes galantes, the elegant outdoor entertainments that Watteau had invented, and after Watteau's death in 1721 he became the leading master of the type in France, working for the court and wealthy collectors with lively, light-toned scenes of dances, music, theatre and picnics. La Camargo Dancing, about 1730, shows the celebrated dancer Marie Camargo. Picnic after the Hunt belongs to the later part of his career. Frederick the Great of Prussia was among his collectors. He died in Paris in 1743, and he is now valued for his own freshness and wit.","q328369":"Edwin Landseer was born in London in 1802, the son of an engraver, and was a prodigy who exhibited at the Royal Academy at thirteen. He studied animals closely, at first under Benjamin Robert Haydon, and became the most famous animal painter in Britain, elected an Academician in 1831 and knighted in 1850. Queen Victoria and Prince Albert were among his patrons, and he painted the Scottish Highlands and its deer, as well as dogs and lions with almost human feelings. Alpine Mastiffs Reanimating a Distressed Traveller, painted in 1820, helped fix the popular image of the St Bernard dog with a small barrel at its collar. He also modelled the four bronze lions at the foot of Nelson's Column. He suffered mental illness in later life and died in 1873.","q462404":"Fitz Henry Lane was born in Gloucester, Massachusetts, in 1804 and was partly crippled by an illness in childhood. He trained as a lithographer in Boston, where he made popular views of ships and harbours, and from the 1840s he turned to oil painting, returning to Gloucester in 1848 and building a granite studio house there. His pictures of Boston, Gloucester, New York and the Maine coast show ships under calm, brilliant skies above clear, glassy water; the quality of light has led later critics to call him a luminist. Lumber Schooners at Evening on Penobscot Bay, of 1863, is a late example, and he also painted naval battles, such as the engagement of the frigate President in 1815. He died in Gloucester in 1865.","q3092335":"Félix-Hippolyte Lanoüe was born in Versailles in 1812 and trained as a landscape painter in Paris, where he won the Prix de Rome for historical landscape and so went to Italy for several years. His taste for Italian views remained with him throughout his career, as in the View of La Cava, a mountain town near Salerno, dated about 1840. Back in France he exhibited at the Salon for decades and painted grand landscapes in the classical tradition of Claude and Poussin, with calm light, framing trees and gentle distance. He died in Paris in 1872. His careful, idealised landscapes were admired in his day, but they fell out of fashion with the rise of Impressionism, and he is now a minor figure.","q1918394":"Giovanni Larciani, who lived from 1484 to 1527, is known to scholars as the Master of the Kress Landscapes, a name given to a Florentine painter whose work was first grouped around three panels of scenes from a legend, now in Washington, that came from the Kress Collection. Painted about 1515 to 1520, the panels tell their story in small figures moving through broad, fanciful landscapes of hills, rivers and buildings, a taste that belongs to the Florentine tradition of Piero di Cosimo. Panels of this kind were usually made to decorate a room in a patrician house. The anonymous master has been identified by scholars with Larciani, and little else of his life is recorded here.","q550302":"Nicolas de Largillierre was born in Paris in 1656, spent his childhood in Antwerp and trained there before going to London about 1674, where he worked as an assistant to Peter Lely. Returning to Paris in 1682, he soon joined the Royal Academy, with a portrait of Charles Le Brun, and became one of the most successful portraitists in France, painting courtiers, bankers, magistrates and artists with solid modelling, warm colour and a sense of social ease. His early Portrait of a Young Man and His Tutor, dated 1685, and the Self-Portrait of 1707 show the range of his skill, while Elizabeth Throckmorton records one of the English Catholic exiles of Paris. He later directed the Academy and died in 1746, aged nearly ninety.","q462529":"Karin Bergöö Larsson was born in 1859 in Sweden and studied at the Royal Academy of Fine Arts in Stockholm before going to Paris in the early 1880s, where she met the painter Carl Larsson among the Scandinavian artists at Grez-sur-Loing. They married in 1883. She gave up a promising career as a painter to raise their eight children, but she remained creative, designing textiles, clothing and furniture and shaping the colourful interior of their home at Sundborn in Dalarna, which Carl's watercolours made famous across Europe. Her early portrait Pierre Louis Alexandre, dated 1879 to 1880, shows her skill before marriage. She died in 1928, and her reputation as both a designer and a painter has risen in recent decades.","laurence_augustine_jub_h_quet":"Little is recorded of Laurence-Augustine Jubé Héquet, known as Madame Héquet, a French painter whom the Metropolitan Museum lists without dates. The one picture that hangs here, dated about 1850 to 1860, shows Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres as a young man; Ingres was the leading French painter of his time and had painted a famous self-portrait of his youthful self in 1804. Portraits of great artists, made by admirers and pupils, were a favourite form of homage in nineteenth-century France, and women of the period often studied privately, because the École des Beaux-Arts did not admit them until 1897. No details of her training, family or patrons are given here, and no further claim is made.","q59894":"Laurent de La Hyre was born in Paris in 1606, the son of a painter, and learned his art from his father and from the works of the school of Fontainebleau. He rose quickly as a painter of altarpieces for Paris churches, mythological scenes and, unusually for his time, landscapes. In 1648 he was one of the founder-members of the Royal Academy of Painting and Sculpture. His style grew from the lively manner of his early years into a calm, clear classicism with cool colour and balanced compositions, as in the Allegory of Music of 1649 and The Kiss of Peace and Justice of 1654, and he is sometimes ranked beside Poussin and Le Sueur among the leaders of French classical painting in his generation. He died in Paris in 1656.","q3219465":"Laurent Pécheux was born in Lyon in 1729 and trained there before going to Rome in the early 1750s, where he stayed for about a quarter of a century. He worked in the neoclassical circle of Rome, painting history subjects and portraits for Italian and foreign patrons, and was made a member of the Academy of St Luke. His portrait of Maria Luisa of Parma, painted in 1765, shows the young princess at about the time of her marriage to the heir to the Spanish throne; she later became Queen of Spain. In 1777 he moved to Turin as first painter to the House of Savoy and a teacher at the Academy, and he died there in 1821, aged ninety-two.","q312096":"Thomas Lawrence was born in Bristol in 1769, the son of an innkeeper, and as a child was already drawing portraits of the guests, then of fashionable visitors at Bath, where he worked in pastel. He moved to London in 1787, was elected a Royal Academician in 1794, and in 1792 succeeded Reynolds as painter in ordinary to George III. Dashing, fluent portraits of women and statesmen made him the most celebrated portraitist in Europe. After 1814 the Prince Regent sent him to paint the allied monarchs and generals of the Napoleonic wars for the Waterloo Chamber at Windsor. He was knighted in 1815 and became President of the Royal Academy in 1820. Lady Mary Templetown and Her Eldest Son, 1802, is a tender portrait of mother and child. He died in 1830 and was buried in St Paul's Cathedral.","q19874871":"Lawrence Kilburn, also written Kilbrunn, was an English-born portrait painter who worked in colonial New York, and the Metropolitan Museum gives his dates as 1720 to 1775. He is recorded in the city from the mid-1750s, painting the portraits of its merchant families in the plain, flat style of the colonial portraitists, with attention to lace, silk and jewellery rather than to modelling. His Portrait of a Lady, dated 1764, is a typical example. Little is known of his training or earlier career in England, and the picture is more valuable as a record of colonial New York dress and manners than as a work of high art, though it has the straightforward charm of its type.","q20981230":"Lawrence Sully was born in Kilkenny, Ireland, in 1769, the eldest of a family of theatrical and artistic people who emigrated to America in 1792, settling first in Charleston, South Carolina. A miniature painter, he made his career in Richmond, Virginia, painting local gentry and visitors in small portraits on ivory. His younger brother Thomas Sully, who became one of the most successful American portraitists, learned the basics of painting from him, and took over his clients in Richmond after Lawrence's death in 1804. The Metropolitan's group of portraits, dated from about 1795 to 1800, shows Lawrence's delicate miniature manner. His name has been overshadowed by that of his brother.","q7787260":"Thomas Bayley Lawson was an American portrait painter who lived from 1807 to 1888, and few details of his training or patrons are given here. The picture in Washington is his portrait of William Morris Hunt, painted about 1879, the year Hunt died. Hunt, born in 1824, was Boston's leading painter and teacher of his day, a former student of Millet in France who persuaded Boston collectors to buy the work of Millet and the Barbizon school, and who taught many of the city's young painters. A portrait of such a figure by a fellow artist is a record of respect as well as of likeness, and it is the picture by which Lawson is best represented here.","q3260646":"Louis-Joseph Le Lorrain was born in Paris in 1715 and studied in Rome in the 1740s as a prizewinner of the Royal Academy. Back in Paris he designed furniture and decorations in the new Greek taste for the collector La Live de Jully, in a stately style that anticipated Louis XVI neoclassicism. In 1758 he accepted an invitation to Russia, where he took up a post at the new Imperial Academy of Arts in St Petersburg, and he died there in 1759. The Three Figures Dressed for a Masquerade, painted in the 1740s, dates from his Roman years and shows a lively interest in costume and carnival. He was a talented designer and painter whose short career left few pictures, and his influence on the style of his time is out of proportion to his output.","q19595026":"Little is recorded of Olivier Le May, a French painter who lived from 1734 to 1797. His View of Cap-Français, Saint-Domingue, dated 1787, shows the harbour and town of the richest of France's Caribbean colonies, now Cap-Haïtien in Haiti, at the height of a prosperity built on sugar and coffee grown by enslaved labour. Four years later, in 1791, a great uprising of the enslaved began the Haitian Revolution, and in 1793 much of the town was burned, so views of this kind are valuable as records of a vanished place. Le May's training, travels and patrons are not documented here, and no further claim should be made about his life.","le_nain_family":"The Le Nain family means three brothers from Laon, Antoine, Louis and Mathieu, who settled in Paris in the 1620s and shared a workshop for the rest of their careers. Their pictures are mostly signed only with the family name, so scholars have long argued over which brother painted which: Antoine is linked to small portraits and group scenes, Louis to the peasant scenes, and Mathieu, the youngest, to larger official portraits and later works. Antoine and Louis became founder-members of the Royal Academy in 1648 and died within days of each other that year, while Mathieu lived until 1677 and was painter to the city of Paris. A Family Meal, in Cleveland, is a quiet meal scene of the kind associated with the workshop.","q1383731":"Henri Le Secq was born in Paris in 1818 and trained as a painter under Paul Delaroche, in a studio that also produced Gustave Le Gray, Charles Nègre and Roger Fenton, all early photographers. He turned to photography about 1850, and in 1851 he was one of five photographers commissioned by the French historic monuments commission to record medieval buildings across France, the Mission Héliographique. His photographs of the cathedrals of Reims, Strasbourg, Amiens and Chartres and of Parisian street life are among the most admired of the 1850s, and the Head of a Woman, about 1852, dates from this period. He gave up photography about 1856 and devoted himself to collecting wrought ironwork, whose collection is now the Musée Le Secq des Tournelles in Rouen. He died in 1882.","leandro_bassano":"Leandro Bassano was born in 1557, the third son of the painter Jacopo Bassano, and worked with his father and brothers in the family workshop at Bassano del Grappa, which produced rural scenes, religious pictures and night effects that were popular with Venetian buyers. After Jacopo's death in 1592 he moved to Venice and became one of the city's leading portraitists, painting doges, senators, officials and professional men in sober, dignified likenesses; Doge Marino Grimani made him a knight in 1595. He also painted altarpieces and religious subjects, among them The Dead Christ with Angels, about 1580, from his years in his father's studio. He died in Venice in 1622 or 1623.","q957538":"Alphonse Legros was born in Dijon in 1837 and trained in Paris under Horace Lecoq de Boisbaudran, whose method of drawing from memory shaped a generation, before making his name with the large Realist painting Ex-Voto of 1860. With Fantin-Latour and Whistler he formed the Société des Trois, and in 1863 he moved to London on Whistler's advice. He became a prolific etcher and a painter of religious and peasant subjects, a naturalised British subject in 1881, and the Slade Professor of Fine Art at University College London from 1876 to 1892, where his etching classes helped revive the art in Britain. His Portrait of a Woman, dated 1875, comes from this period. He died in 1911.","lehmann_henri":"Henri Lehmann was born in Kiel in 1814 and trained in Paris, where he became one of Ingres's most devoted pupils. He followed Ingres to Rome, where in 1839 he painted a portrait of Franz Liszt, later followed by one of Liszt's companion, the writer Marie d'Agoult; both became famous. He became a French citizen in 1847 and made his career in Paris as a painter of portraits, history and religious subjects, including decorations for the Hôtel de Ville, and as a teacher whose pupils included Georges Seurat at the École des Beaux-Arts. Woman of the Orient, dated 1837, belongs to his years of training under the Ingres tradition, with its smooth contours and firm drawing. He died in 1882.","q979381":"Leonaert Bramer was born in Delft in 1596 and spent more than a decade in France and Italy, from 1614, working in Rome among the circle of Northern painters, before returning to Delft in 1628. He painted small, dramatic scenes of biblical, mythological and literary subjects, often lit by torches or moonlight and set among ruins, in a style that owes something to Adam Elsheimer and the Roman followers of Caravaggio. He was unusual in the Netherlands for also painting frescoes, in Delft and elsewhere. He was a friend of the younger Delft painter Johannes Vermeer, for whom he acted as a witness in 1653. The Judgment of Solomon dates from the 1640s. He died in Delft in 1674.","q762":"Leonardo da Vinci was born in 1452 at Vinci, near Florence, and trained in the workshop of Andrea del Verrocchio. From 1482 to 1499 he served Ludovico Sforza in Milan, where he painted The Last Supper, and he later worked in Florence, Milan again, Rome and finally France, where he died at Amboise in 1519 as the guest of Francis I. Painter, engineer, anatomist and student of water, flight and light, he left fewer than twenty surviving paintings and thousands of notebook pages, the paintings built up from delicate layers of glaze and soft shadow. Ginevra de' Benci, about 1474 to 1478, is the only painting by him in the Americas; its reverse bears a wreath of laurel, palm and juniper around a motto meaning that beauty adorns virtue. His fame has never dimmed.","q22667524":"Little is recorded of Leopold Paul Unger, a portrait painter whose dates are given by the Metropolitan Museum as 1812 to 1859, without a statement of nationality. His Charlotte Allard, dated 1840, is the one picture that hangs here. The 1840s were years in which portraiture was increasingly challenged by the daguerreotype, introduced in 1839, but painted portraits remained the choice of families who wanted dignity and could afford it, and many competent painters worked steadily for local clients. Unger's training, home city and patrons are not documented here, and nothing should be assumed beyond the dates and the portrait.","q232423":"Judith Leyster was born in Haarlem in 1609, the daughter of a brewer, and became a master painter in the city's guild of St Luke in 1633, a rare distinction for a woman. She ran her own workshop and took pupils, and the guild records a dispute in 1635 about a pupil who had left Frans Hals's studio for hers. Her lively half-length figures of musicians, drinkers and children are painted in quick, bright strokes in a manner close to Hals's, and she also painted still life and tender candlelit scenes. In 1636 she married the painter Jan Miense Molenaer, and few works are known after her marriage. She died in 1660. Her paintings were long attributed to Hals until 1893, when her monogram was found on one in the Louvre. The Self-Portrait of about 1630 is her best-known picture.","li_louis_p_rin":"Lié Louis Périn, who signed himself Périn-Salbreux, was a French painter who lived from 1753 to 1817, and little else is recorded of him here beyond the dates. The Self-Portrait in Cleveland, dated to the 1790s, belongs to the decade of the French Revolution, when the Salon was opened to all artists in 1791 and many painters, long shut out by the Royal Academy, exhibited for the first time. A self-portrait of the period is a useful document of how a painter wished to look and be seen in an age of upheaval, and it could serve as a calling card for portrait commissions. Further details of his training, teachers and patrons are not given here, and none should be assumed.","q1374872":"Liberale da Verona was born in Verona about 1445 and began as a manuscript illuminator, a training that left his painting with jewel-like colour, crisp outline and love of detail. From about 1467 to 1476 he worked in Siena, illuminating choir books for the cathedral and for the monastery of Monte Oliveto Maggiore, and painting panels for Sienese patrons. Returning to Verona, he painted altarpieces and frescoes for churches, in a style influenced by Mantegna and by the Venetian painters. The Chess Players, about 1475, and Scene from a Novella are small narrative panels of the kind often made to decorate furniture, and they show his lively storytelling. Vasari praised him in the Lives. He died in Verona around 1526, aged about eighty.","q5535682":"Georg Emil Libert was a Danish landscape painter, born in 1820 and died in 1908, who belonged to the generation shaped by the Golden Age of Danish painting, the school of clear light, honest observation and precise drawing led at the Copenhagen Academy by C. W. Eckersberg. His View of Sommerspiret, the Cliffs of Møn, dated 1846, shows a famous chalk pinnacle on the island of Møn, one of the most admired natural sights of Denmark, which Danish painters visited to study the white cliffs against the sea. Few other details of his training and patrons are given here. His long life spanned the later Golden Age, the war of 1864 and the rise of Danish Impressionism.","q430783":"Jan Lievens was born in Leiden in 1607 and was a prodigy who studied in Amsterdam with Pieter Lastman while still a boy. Back in Leiden he worked beside the young Rembrandt from about 1625 to 1631, the two painting similar subjects and perhaps sharing a studio, and in 1629 the courtier and poet Constantijn Huygens praised both in his memoirs, finding Lievens the bolder in invention and Rembrandt the surer in emotion. Lievens went to London in 1632, then to Antwerp, where the example of Rubens and Van Dyck made his manner grander, and returned to the Dutch Republic in 1644, painting portraits and allegories for the court at The Hague and for Amsterdam. Bearded Man with a Beret, about 1630, comes from his Leiden years. He died in Amsterdam in 1674.","q2986384":"Lilly Martin Spencer was born in Exeter, England, in 1822 to French parents who emigrated to Ohio in 1830. Showing early talent, she studied in Cincinnati and, with the backing of the patron Nicholas Longworth, moved to New York in 1848. There she became one of the best-known American genre painters, specialising in lively domestic scenes of mothers, children and husbands that sold widely as prints. Young Husband: First Marketing, of 1854, is a comic picture of a young man struggling home with the shopping. She was also the main earner for her large family, while her husband, Benjamin Spencer, managed the house and helped in her work; they had thirteen children. She kept painting into her eighties and died in 1902.","q3242255":"Lippo di Benivieni was a Florentine painter recorded in documents between 1296 and 1327, in the generation that followed Cimabue and saw the rise of Giotto. Few of his pictures are certain, and his career is imperfectly documented, so that scholars still discuss how many works should be given to him. The Metropolitan's Madonna and Child, undated, follows the pattern of the devotional panel of its time, with a gold ground, a calm enthroned Virgin and a Child held on her arm, in a style that is still partly Byzantine yet turning toward a more natural modelling. Details of his training and patrons are not recorded here, and nothing further should be assumed.","q504963":"Lippo Memmi was a painter of Siena, born about 1290 and active until his death around 1356; the dates 1267 to 1377 sometimes printed for him are impossible, since they would give him a span of 110 years. He was the son of the painter Memmo di Filippuccio and the brother-in-law and close collaborator of Simone Martini, with whom he signed the Annunciation of 1333 now in the Uffizi, painting the two flanking saints. He also painted a Maestà in the town hall of San Gimignano and many altarpieces and half-length Madonnas, with soft faces, delicate gold patterning and a refined, graceful line learned from Simone. The Madonna and Child with Donor, and the saints in Washington and New York, show his gentle manner.","q966135":"Lippo Vanni was a painter and manuscript illuminator of Siena, documented from 1341 until about 1375, in the generation after the Lorenzetti brothers who died in the plague of 1348. He painted altarpieces and frescoes, among them the scenes of the Sienese victory in the Val di Chiana, painted in the town hall about 1363, and he is also known as an illuminator of choir books. His Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints Peter and Paul and Angels, in the Metropolitan, is typical of Sienese devotional painting of the later fourteenth century, with rich gold, elegant line and a calm, symmetrical arrangement. Few other details of his life are recorded here, and the museum gives no dates for him.","q22672217":"Almost nothing is recorded of Lodewijk Tieling beyond the period of his activity, given by the Metropolitan Museum as about 1695 to 1700, and the painting of The Ark, about 1700, that hangs here; the museum gives no nationality, though his name suggests a Dutch or Flemish origin. Noah's Ark was a favourite subject in Netherlandish painting from the sixteenth century, when artists of the Bassano and Brueghel tradition used the story as a pretext for painting many animals in orderly pairs, with a landscape and a great wooden vessel. Tieling's training, birthplace and patrons are not documented in the sources used here, and no further claim should be made about his life.","q447058":"Pietro Longhi was born in Venice in 1702, the son of a goldsmith, and trained under Antonio Balestra and then in Bologna with Giuseppe Maria Crespi, whose homely genre scenes shaped his own. From the 1740s he made his name with small conversation pieces of Venetian life: masked nobles at carnival, a lady fainting, a dancing lesson, a game of the cooking pot, and a rhinoceros on show. In these scenes of the patrician class, painted in soft, pale colour with gentle humour, he recorded the habits of a city in decline. The Faint and The Game of the Cooking Pot date from about 1744. The playwright Carlo Goldoni praised him for showing on canvas the life he showed on the stage. Longhi was a founding member of the Venetian Academy in 1756 and died in Venice in 1785.","q309635":"Pietro Lorenzetti was a painter of Siena, documented between 1306 and 1345, who probably died in the plague of 1348, like his younger brother Ambrogio. He learned from Duccio's Sienese tradition but also from the new, solid, human forms of Giotto, and he worked at Arezzo, where he painted a great polyptych for the Pieve in 1320, and at Assisi, where his frescoes of the Passion in the Lower Church of San Francesco show powerful grief and movement. His Birth of the Virgin, painted for Siena Cathedral in 1342, is a landmark in the painting of interiors. The triptych in Washington, with the Madonna and Child between Saints Mary Magdalene and Catherine, dates from about 1340. His work is among the most dramatic of the Sienese fourteenth century.","q8511":"Lorenzo di Credi was born in Florence in 1457 and trained in the workshop of Andrea del Verrocchio, where he worked alongside Leonardo da Vinci. When Verrocchio died in 1488 he took over the workshop and finished the altarpiece for Pistoia that his master had left incomplete. He painted devotional pictures, portraits and altarpieces in a smooth, cool, highly polished manner, with careful draughtsmanship and clear colour, and his Madonna and Child with a Pomegranate, of 1475 to 1480, shows the refinement of the Verrocchio shop. In 1504 he was among the artists consulted about where to place Michelangelo's David. His later works looked old-fashioned beside those of Raphael and Michelangelo, though he remained respected, and he died in Florence in 1536.","q310973":"Lorenzo Lotto, born in Venice about 1480 and trained there, began his career in Treviso, where in 1505 he painted portraits and allegories for the bishop and other patrons, among them the small Allegory of Virtue and Vice now in Washington, which is thought to have served as the cover of a portrait. After years in the Marche and Rome he settled in Bergamo, where from 1513 to 1525 he painted altarpieces and portraits for the town's wealthy families and churches, among them the Saint Catherine of 1522 and the night-lit Nativity of 1523. A restless, individual painter, he combined Venetian colour with unusual, sometimes puzzling symbolism and deep psychological insight. He ended his life as a lay brother at the shrine of Loreto, where he died about 1556 or 1557.","q155376":"Lorenzo Monaco, whose name means Lawrence the Monk, was born in Siena about 1370 and entered the Camaldolese monastery of Santa Maria degli Angeli in Florence in 1391, where he trained as a painter and illuminator and spent his career, working for churches across the city. He was the last great master of the elegant International Gothic style in Florence, painting long, flowing, rhythmic figures in brilliant, jewel-like colour against gold backgrounds. The Metropolitan's four prophets, Abraham, David, Moses and Noah, about 1408 to 1410, come from the upper part of an altarpiece, and the Washington Madonna and Child is dated 1413. He died about 1425, shortly before Masaccio and Fra Angelico brought a new naturalism to the city.","q214074":"Claude Lorrain was born about 1604 in the duchy of Lorraine, was orphaned young, and went to Rome in his teens, where he worked for the landscape painter Agostino Tassi and learned perspective and the painting of light. Apart from about two years in Nancy in the mid-1620s, he lived in Rome for the rest of his life. His patrons included Pope Urban VIII and Cardinal Bentivoglio. He perfected the ideal landscape, a calm, ordered scene of harbours, groves and ruins bathed in the golden light of dawn or evening, with small figures from mythology or the Bible. To protect his works from forgers he kept the Liber Veritatis, a book of drawings after each finished picture. The Judgment of Paris, of 1645 to 1646, is typical of his later style. He died in Rome in 1682.","q21995141":"Little is recorded of Louis Antoine Collas, a French portrait painter whom the Metropolitan Museum lists without dates. Two portraits by him hang here, a Portrait of a Lady of 1816 and Eugénie Simon of 1827, which show that he worked in the years of the Restoration, after the fall of Napoleon, when the restored Bourbon monarchy and a rising middle class kept portrait painters busy. French portraits of the period often show sitters in plain, dark or pale clothing against simple backgrounds, with smooth finish and firm drawing in the tradition of David and Ingres. Collas's training, home town and patrons are not documented here, and nothing further should be assumed about him.","q22001675":"Little is recorded here of Louis Francis DePaul Binsse, whose dates are given by the Metropolitan Museum as 1774 to 1844, and whose Portrait of a Gentleman, about 1810, is the one picture that hangs here. The decade around 1810 was a period of plain, direct portraiture, when a gentleman was usually shown half-length in a dark coat and white cravat against a simple background, with the face as the focus. Painters of this kind were often amateurs or part-time artists as well as professionals, and many are known only from a few signed works. Nothing certain is given here about Binsse's training, nationality or patrons, and nothing should be assumed beyond the dates and this portrait.","q6687620":"Louis Lang was born in Württemberg, in southern Germany, in 1814 and came to the United States as a young man, working first in Philadelphia and later in New York, where he became a painter of portraits, genre scenes and sentimental or historical subjects for a popular audience. The Basket Maker, of 1853, with its homely, kindly subject and fine finish, and Art Students, about 1871, show his taste for scenes of everyday life, painted in the careful, polished manner he had learned in Europe. He died in 1893. His pictures are valued today as records of mid-nineteenth-century American taste, which liked its genre pictures anecdotal, finely finished and morally pleasing.","q18508479":"Little is recorded of Louis Marie Sicardi, a French portrait painter who lived from 1743 to 1825, a span that took in the last decades of the old monarchy, the Revolution and the Empire. His Portrait of a Man, about 1780, is the picture that hangs here. In the 1780s Parisian portraiture favoured natural, informal likenesses, in simpler dress than the courtly manner of earlier decades, with the sitter shown as a thoughtful or genial individual rather than as a grandee. Further details of Sicardi's training, home town and patrons are not given here, and no further claim should be made about his life or his reputation.","q1375132":"Louis Tocqué was born in Paris in 1696 and trained with Nicolas Bertin and then with Jean-Marc Nattier, whose daughter he married in 1747. He became a member of the Royal Academy in 1734 and one of the most admired portraitists of the reign of Louis XV, painting Queen Marie Leszczynska, the court and Parisian financiers with a quiet naturalism and a skilful touch for satin and lace. His portrait of Nattier, about 1740, is a fine example. In 1756 he was invited to Russia, where he painted the court of Empress Elizabeth, and he went on to Denmark, returning to Paris about 1759. He died in 1772.","q20925014":"Little is recorded of Louisa Catherine Strobel, who lived from 1803 to 1883, beyond a group of five portraits at the Metropolitan Museum dated about 1830. They include a self-portrait and likenesses of Daniel Strobel Jr. and his wife, Anna Church, and of Jacob Gerard Koch and his wife, Jane Griffith, which suggests that she painted within a circle of family and neighbours. Portraits painted within a family circle were common in the early American republic, whether by amateurs or by modest professionals, and they are now valued as records of a family's faces and clothes. Further details of her training and later career are not given here.","q21851359":"Luca di Tommè di Nuto was a painter of Siena who is documented between 1356 and 1389, though the museum gives no dates. He worked in the Sienese tradition of the generation that followed the Lorenzetti brothers and the Black Death of 1348, painting altarpieces and devotional panels in tempera on gold ground, with firm drawing, bright colour and an elegant, sometimes severe gravity. The Metropolitan's Madonna and Child, about 1360 to 1365, is typical of the devotional Madonnas of the period, with the Child held close on the Virgin's arm. He is regarded as a skilful follower of the Lorenzettis' manner, which gave later fourteenth-century Siena its sweet and humane character. Few further details of his life are recorded here.","q7031":"Luca Signorelli was born in Cortona about 1445 and, according to Vasari, was a pupil of Piero della Francesca, whose monumental calm shaped his figures. He also absorbed the muscular nudes of Antonio Pollaiuolo. He worked in the Sistine Chapel in 1481 to 1482 and in the monastery of Monte Oliveto Maggiore, but his most important work is the cycle of the End of the World and the Last Judgement in the San Brizio Chapel of Orvieto Cathedral, painted from 1499 to 1504, whose powerful nudes in violent action impressed Michelangelo. The Assumption of the Virgin with Saints Michael and Benedict and the Madonna and Child, both in the Metropolitan, belong to his mature career. He died in Cortona in 1523.","lucas_cranach":"Lucas Cranach the Elder was born in Kronach, in Franconia, in 1472, worked in Vienna about 1501 to 1504, and in 1505 became court painter to Frederick the Wise, Elector of Saxony, at Wittenberg, where he spent most of his life. He ran a large and efficient workshop producing altarpieces, portraits, mythological nudes and prints, and he became a friend of Martin Luther and a leading painter of the Reformation, making portraits of Luther and his circle. Hunting near Hartenfels Castle, dated 1540, shows an electoral hunt near the Saxon residence at Torgau, and The Baptism of Christ is of the 1530s. He also owned a pharmacy and a printing house and served as mayor of Wittenberg. He joined the captive Elector John Frederick in Augsburg and died at Weimar in 1553.","q170339":"Lucas Cranach the Younger was born in Wittenberg in 1515, the second son of Lucas Cranach the Elder, and took over the family workshop after his elder brother Hans died in 1537 and then after his father's death in 1553. He continued the family style and trade in portraits of Protestant leaders and princes, in Lutheran altarpieces, and in religious pictures such as Christ and the Adulteress and Christ Blessing the Children, both about 1545 to 1550, which presented the Reformation themes of forgiveness and of the welcome given to children. He served as mayor of Wittenberg, and his workshop's pictures are often hard to tell from his father's. He died at Wittenberg in 1586.","q72164":"Eugenio Lucas Villamil was born in Madrid in 1858, the son of the painter Eugenio Lucas Velázquez, whose lively, free manner was built on the example of Goya. He followed his father's style closely, painting bullfights, village festivals, fantastic and gypsy scenes, and landscapes in quick, thick brushwork with dramatic skies, and he sometimes worked in a Goya-like manner that makes his pictures, and his father's, hard to tell apart. The Bullfight, about 1890 to 1900, in Washington is typical of his subjects. He died in 1918. His work has been remembered for its energy and for the lively market in Spanish pictures in Paris and America in the later nineteenth century, though it is less original than his father's.","q1990838":"Ludolf de Jongh was born near Rotterdam in 1616 and trained in the south of Holland, and he spent most of his working life in Rotterdam and its neighbourhood, dying at Hillegersberg in 1679. He painted portraits, hunting parties, cavalry scenes and, in the 1650s and 1660s, quiet genre scenes of households and courtyards in the manner of Pieter de Hooch, with clear light, tidy brickwork and women at their chores. The Metropolitan's Scene in a Courtyard, from the early 1660s, is of this type. His reputation was never as great as that of De Hooch or Vermeer, but his pictures give a pleasing view of ordinary Dutch life in the seventeenth century, and he is valued for their gentle, orderly charm.","q380553":"Ludovico Carracci was born in Bologna in 1555 and with his younger cousins Agostino and Annibale founded about 1582 a studio-academy, later called the Incamminati, that reformed Italian painting by returning to the study of nature, drawing and the great painters of the High Renaissance. He was its senior teacher, and his pupils included Guido Reni and Domenichino, with the young Guercino as a follower. His own work is emotional and tender, with soft light and warm shadows learned from Correggio and the Venetians; he painted altarpieces and frescoes for Bologna's churches, among them the cycle in San Michele in Bosco. The Lamentation, about 1582, and the Madonna and Child with Saints of 1607 belong to different phases of his career. He died in Bologna in 1619.","q1351631":"Ludovico Mazzolino was a painter of Ferrara, born about 1480 and dying about 1528, who worked for churches and for the Este court in the early sixteenth century. His small pictures, painted with enamel-like colour and sharp, crowded detail, tell Gospel stories in a lively setting of ruins, costumes and unusual faces, often with an air of the exotic. The Lamentation, about 1514 to 1516, in the Metropolitan, shows his taste for emotion and precise finish. He seems to have trained in the Ferrarese school of the generation of Ercole de' Roberti and Lorenzo Costa, and his sources included northern prints. Few other details of his life are recorded, and the museum gives no dates for him. His Christ among the Doctors of 1524 is in London.","q1874985":"Ludwig Schongauer was a German painter from the family of goldsmiths and artists of Colmar in Alsace, best known as a brother of the engraver Martin Schongauer, the most celebrated German printmaker before Dürer. Little is recorded of his own career, and the museum gives no dates, but he belongs to the generation working on the Upper Rhine in the later fifteenth century, when painters looked to Netherlandish models such as Rogier van der Weyden for their drama and detail. The Metropolitan's panel pairs two scenes of the Passion story, Christ before Pilate and the Resurrection. Further claims about his training and patrons are best left aside, because the record is thin.","q22094609":"Little is recorded of Luigi Marta, an Italian painter who lived from 1790 to 1858. His Napoléon I on Horseback, dated 1830, belongs to the long afterlife of the Napoleonic legend: after the emperor's fall in 1815 and his death on St Helena in 1821, images of Napoleon, many based on Jacques-Louis David's Napoleon Crossing the Alps, were made across Europe, and enthusiasm for them grew in the years around the revolution of 1830. A rider on a rearing horse was the most famous form of Napoleon's image. Marta's training, home town and patrons are not documented here, and nothing further should be assumed.","q8537":"Bernardino Luini was born near Lake Maggiore about 1480 and worked mainly in Milan, where he absorbed the manner of Leonardo da Vinci, adopting his soft shadow and sweet, gentle faces and spreading it widely in altarpieces and frescoes across Lombardy. His great works include the frescoes of San Maurizio in Milan, the Passion cycle at Lugano of 1529 and the Sanctuary at Saronno. The series of scenes from the story of Cephalus and Procris, about 1520 to 1522, in Washington, are frescoes from a villa near Milan, painted from Ovid's tale of the jealous wife and the unlucky javelin, and the Madonna of the Carnation reflects Leonardo's compositions. He died about 1532, and for centuries his work was sometimes mistaken for his master's.","q1876555":"Luis de la Cruz y Ríos was a Spanish painter of portraits and miniatures who worked in Madrid in the reign of Ferdinand VII; the museum gives no dates, though Spanish sources place him roughly between 1776 and 1853. His Princess María Francisca de Asís de Borbón and Her Son Infante Carlos Luis, dated 1818, shows the wife of Ferdinand's brother Don Carlos and their infant son, who later became a claimant to the Spanish throne. Portraits of this kind, painted for the court, followed a plain, neat style of firm drawing and clear detail. His training and later career are not described here, and further claims should be treated with care.","q703250":"Luis de Morales was born about 1510 in or near Badajoz, in Extremadura, and spent nearly all his life in that region, dying in 1586. He was called El Divino, the Divine, for the intense devotion of his pictures: the Virgin and Child, the Man of Sorrows and the Pietà, painted in cool, polished colour on panel, with soft shadow in the manner of Leonardo's followers, and with thin, fine detail in the northern tradition. The Lamentation, about 1560, in the Metropolitan, is typical. His small, moving panels were made for private prayer and for churches in a region far from the main artistic centres. In 1581, when Philip II passed through Badajoz, the king granted the aged painter a pension. Few details of his early training are certain.","q707478":"Luis Meléndez was born in Naples in 1716 and raised in Madrid, the son of a court miniaturist, and became the leading Spanish still-life painter of the eighteenth century. He was trained at the Madrid academy, but a quarrel with its director led to his expulsion, and he never won a court post. His great achievement was a large series of still lifes, painted about 1760 to 1772 for the Prince of Asturias, the future Charles IV, in which humble things such as bread, fish, a kettle, melons and fruit are rendered with sober light, firm form and a close sense of texture. The Afternoon Meal and Still Life with Fish, Bread, and Kettle date from about 1772. He died poor in Madrid in 1780, and his reputation has soared since.","q20888900":"Little is recorded of Fritz Müller beyond the dates 1814 to 1894 and the picture that hangs here, Capture of the Savannah by the U.S.S. Perry, dated 1861. It shows an event of the opening weeks of the American Civil War: on 3 June 1861 the Union brig Perry captured the Savannah, a Confederate privateer, off the Carolina coast, an early prize that was widely celebrated in the North. The Savannah's crew were later tried for piracy in New York, though the jury failed to agree and the men were afterwards treated as prisoners of war. Pictures of naval actions such as this, painted soon after the event, served both as news and as patriotic keepsakes. Müller's training, home and patrons are not documented here.","q20858917":"Nothing certain is known here of the American painter recorded only as MacKay, and the life dates 1711 to 1851 sometimes printed for him would give a span of 140 years and cannot be right. The one picture that hangs here is a portrait of Catherine Brower, dated 1791. In the years just after the founding of the American republic, portraits were painted largely by itinerant and local artists of modest training, who travelled to the homes of sitters and charged by the likeness. Such pictures, in a plain style with flat colour and careful lace and costume, are now prized as records of the people and dress of the period. The painter's full name, training and patrons remain unrecorded in the sources used here.","macleod_william":"William MacLeod was born in 1811 and died in 1892, and spent his later career in Washington, where he painted landscapes and became the first curator of the Corcoran Gallery of Art in 1872, holding the post until his death. His Maryland Heights: Siege of Harpers Ferry, dated 1863, shows the wooded ridge above the Potomac and Shenandoah rivers that was fought over in the Civil War, notably in September 1862, when Confederate forces seized the Union garrison at Harpers Ferry. As a landscape painter he worked in the American tradition of the broad, detailed view with a strong sense of place, and applied it to the scenes and sites of the war. His reputation is local rather than national, though his curatorial work helped shape a major collection.","q21498058":"Almost nothing is recorded of Louis Mader beyond the dates 1842 to 1899 and the picture that hangs here, Berks County Almshouse, 1895. It shows a county poorhouse in Berks County, Pennsylvania, in the Pennsylvania German farming country around Reading, in a plain, precise manner in the tradition of American folk art. Almshouses were public institutions that housed the poor, the sick and the elderly, and pictures of them are rare, since most painters preferred more cheerful subjects. A careful view of such a place, dated to the year it was made, is now valued as a record of a social institution of the late nineteenth century. Mader's training, circumstances and patrons are not documented here.","maerten_van_heemskerck":"Maerten van Heemskerck was born in 1498 near Haarlem and in 1532 left for Rome, where he stayed until 1536, filling sketchbooks with drawings of antique sculpture and ruins that are now a famous record of the city, and of Michelangelo's Sistine ceiling. Back in Haarlem he became the leading painter of the town, a church warden of Saint Bavo, and a prolific designer of prints, many engraved by Dirck Volckertsz Coornhert, with Biblical and moral subjects in a muscular Roman Mannerist style. He also painted sober portraits, among them Machtelt Suijs, about 1540 to 1545, in the Cleveland Museum. He died in 1574 and left money to support the wedding of a poor couple each year. His prints carried Roman forms across northern Europe, and they were widely used by other artists.","q505150":"Nicolaes Maes was born in Dordrecht in 1634 and trained in Amsterdam under Rembrandt, probably from about 1648 to 1653, before returning to his home town. In the 1650s he painted intimate scenes of women at home, such as old women at prayer and servants listening at doors, in warm, deep reds and browns and a Rembrandtesque play of light, among them An Old Woman Dozing over a Book, about 1655. In the 1660s he turned to portraiture, adopting a lighter, more elegant manner under the influence of Flemish and French fashions, and in 1673 he moved to Amsterdam, where he became the most popular portraitist in the city. The Portrait of a Lady of 1676 comes from this later period. He died in Amsterdam in 1693.","q2543592":"Marcantonio Franceschini was born in Bologna in 1648 and trained with Carlo Cignani, the leading Bolognese painter of his generation, whose graceful, classical manner he adopted and later spread widely. He became a leading painter of altarpieces, mythological scenes and ceiling decorations, and in Genoa he decorated the Great Council Hall of the Doge's Palace. He also worked for Prince Johann Adam of Liechtenstein, and for the Elector Palatine Johann Wilhelm, for whom he painted many pictures. The Last Communion of Saint Mary of Egypt, of 1680, is an early work. He died in Bologna in 1729 and is regarded as the last great painter of Bolognese classicism, a calm, polished style of clear drawing and pale, sweet colour.","q935830":"Marcellus Coffermans was a Netherlandish painter who worked in Antwerp, where he is recorded as a master in the painters' guild of St Luke in 1549 and was still active in the 1570s; the museum gives no birth or death dates. He is known for religious panels, many of them variations on popular older compositions, made for a lively Antwerp market in devotional pictures that sold at home and abroad. The Adoration of the Shepherds, about 1550, in the Metropolitan, belongs to this production. Antwerp in his time was the commercial capital of northern Europe, and its painters organised workshops and dealers to supply pictures in large numbers. Further details of his training and patrons are not recorded here.","marco_d_oggiono":"Marco d'Oggiono was born about 1470 at Oggiono, near Lake Como, and became one of the most faithful followers of Leonardo da Vinci in Milan, where he worked in Leonardo's circle. He painted altarpieces, Madonnas and religious pictures with Leonardo's soft shadow and gentle, smiling faces, and made a well-known full-size copy of the Last Supper, now at Écouen near Paris. His Portrait of a Youth as Saint Sebastian, in Cleveland, from the late 1480s, belongs to the early period of his career. He also worked on frescoes and other decorations for churches in Milan. He died in 1524. His pictures remain a valuable guide to how Leonardo's Milanese followers imitated and spread his style.","q17510972":"Marco del Buono Giamberti was a Florentine painter who lived from 1402 to 1489 and is known as the partner of Apollonio di Giovanni in one of the busiest workshops of fifteenth-century Florence, which from the 1440s specialised in painted wedding chests, or cassoni, and other furniture panels, as well as book illustrations and small religious pictures. These chests, made for the weddings of Florentine families, were painted with stories from Virgil, Ovid, Boccaccio and the Bible, in bright colour with gold detail, and are important sources for how Florentines pictured the ancient world. The Story of Esther, in the Metropolitan, belongs to this tradition. The workshop's output was extensive, though attribution between the two partners is not always certain.","q98687170":"Little is recorded of Marco di Paolo Veneziano, a Venetian painter whom the Metropolitan Museum lists without dates. His name means Marco, son of Paolo, and he is thought to belong to the circle or family of Paolo Veneziano, the leading Venetian painter of the fourteenth century, whose gold grounds, rich patterns and Byzantine-influenced figures set the manner of Venice for generations. The Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints James Minor and Lucy, in the Metropolitan, follows this tradition of the solemn, symmetrical altarpiece, with the Virgin on a throne between standing saints. Details of his training, patrons and exact dates are not given here, and nothing further should be assumed.","q13583490":"Marcus Gheeraerts the Younger was born in Bruges about 1561 and was brought to England as a small child in 1568, when his father, the painter and etcher Marcus Gheeraerts the Elder, fled religious persecution in the Netherlands. He became the leading portraitist of the late Elizabethan and Jacobean courts, painting nobles who wished to appear jewelled, graceful and learned. The famous Ditchley portrait of Elizabeth I, painted about 1592 and showing the queen standing on a map of England, is attributed to him, and he later painted Queen Anne of Denmark. His portraits are marked by exact, flat pattern, rich embroidery and an air of cool poise, and Ellen Maurice, dated 1597, is a fine example. He married Magdalena de Critz and died in London in 1636.","q21647194":"Little is recorded here of Margaret Bogardus, an American portrait painter who lived from 1804 to 1878. The two portraits that hang here, Mr. Boardman, dated 1837, and Paul Joseph Revere, about 1840, come from the years when American portraiture was still a thriving trade, shortly before the daguerreotype, introduced in 1839, began to replace it for ordinary buyers. Women painters of this generation often worked within a network of family, friends and local patrons, and few of them left detailed records, so that their names survive mainly on the portraits themselves. Details of Bogardus's training, home and patrons are not given here, and no further claim should be made.","q3290536":"Margareta Haverman was a Dutch flower painter, born about 1693 and probably dead not long after 1722, and almost nothing certain is known of her life. She is said to have been a pupil of Jan van Huysum, the leading flower painter of the age, who is reported to have sent her away from his studio out of jealousy. In 1722 she was received into the Royal Academy in Paris, one of the few women admitted in the eighteenth century, and A Vase of Flowers, dated 1716, in the Metropolitan, is the picture she presented for admission. It is painted in a bright, precise manner, with a rich variety of blooms and a glassy shine. After 1722 she disappears from the record.","q2518566":"Margaritone d'Arezzo was a painter of Arezzo in Tuscany, active in the later thirteenth century, whose signed works include panels of the Virgin and of saints and painted crucifixes. He painted in the severe, Byzantine-inspired style called the maniera greca, with gold grounds, flat patterned drapery and stern, formal faces. Vasari wrote a Life of him, treating him as the example of the old Greek manner that Cimabue and Giotto were soon to replace, and gave him a very long life. The Madonna and Child Enthroned with Four Saints, in Washington, is dated by the museum to about 1240 to 1245. He is valued today as a witness to the style that the generation of Giotto overturned, and for the strong, grave beauty of his best panels.","q8309899":"Marie Anne Gérard was born in Grasse in 1745 and in 1769 married the painter Jean-Honoré Fragonard, which is why she is known as Madame Fragonard. She was a miniature painter and is recorded as a talented portraitist, though few works are firmly attributed to her. In 1775 her much younger sister, Marguerite Gérard, came to live with the couple in Paris and became Fragonard's pupil, so that the two women worked in the same household. The Portrait of a Boy, about 1775, is attributed to her and shows a child with the fresh, intimate touch that the Fragonard circle valued. She died in 1823. The fame of her husband and, later, of her sister has long overshadowed her own modest career.","q452123":"Marie-Denise Villers was born in Paris in 1774 and studied with Anne-Louis Girodet. She exhibited at the Paris Salon around 1800, but few records of her career survive, and her works were largely forgotten and misattributed. The Metropolitan's Marie Joséphine Charlotte du Val d'Ognes, of 1801, a girl in white shown drawing beside a window with a cracked pane, was for years attributed first to Jacques-Louis David and then to Constance Charpentier, and was identified as Villers's work only in the 1990s. Some viewers have read the broken pane as a symbol of the barriers that faced women artists, though its meaning is debated. Her calm, direct portraits are now prized. She died in 1821.","q118810":"Marie-Guillemine Benoist was born in Paris in 1768 and studied first with Élisabeth Vigée Le Brun and then, from about 1786, with Jacques-Louis David, becoming one of the most gifted of his women pupils. She exhibited at the Salon from 1791 and married Pierre-Vincent Benoist in 1793. Her most famous picture is the Portrait of a Black Woman, shown in 1800, between the Revolution's abolition of slavery in 1794 and its restoration in 1802, a dignified portrait of a woman named Madeleine, now in the Louvre. The Metropolitan's Madame Philippe Panon Desbassayns de Richemont and Her Son, Eugène, of 1802, is a grand society portrait. She stopped exhibiting after 1818, when her husband became a councillor of state, and died in Paris in 1826.","q529270":"Marie Victoire Lemoine was born in Paris in 1754 and became one of the most successful women painters of the later eighteenth century, exhibiting in Paris and earning an independent living from portraits, miniatures and genre subjects. She worked in the circle of Élisabeth Vigée Le Brun and lived through the Revolution. The Interior of a Woman Painter's Studio, of 1789, is her best-known work and a rare picture of a woman's working life as an artist; it is often read as an assertion of women's place in the art world, painted in the year the Revolution began. She died in Paris in 1820, and her work was little known until recent decades, when scholars of women artists rediscovered it.","q21463891":"Little is recorded here of George Washington Mark, an American painter who lived from 1795 to 1879. The picture that hangs here, Marion Feasting the British Officer on Sweet Potatoes, of 1848, shows a popular patriotic tale of the Revolutionary War. According to the story, popularised by Parson Weems's life of Francis Marion, the guerrilla leader known as the Swamp Fox entertained a British officer, who had come to arrange an exchange of prisoners, with a meal of roasted sweet potatoes in the Carolina woods, and the officer was so struck by the frugality of Marion's men that he left the British service. Such scenes were favourites of nineteenth-century American history painting. Mark's training and patrons are not documented here.","q15123417":"Benjamin Marshall was born in 1768 at Seagrave in Leicestershire and trained in London with the portraitist Lemuel Francis Abbott before turning to the subjects that made his name. He became one of the leading British sporting painters of his time, specialising in portraits of racehorses, jockeys, hunters and sportsmen with their dogs, painted with solid drawing, clear light and close observation. The picture that hangs here, J. G. Shaddick, the Celebrated Sportsman, dated 1806, shows how he treated an owner of dogs and guns with the same care as a champion horse. In 1812 he moved to Newmarket, the centre of racing, where he worked for the sporting nobility. He died in 1835.","q427999":"Martín Rico y Ortega was born in Madrid in 1833 and trained there before going to Paris, where he came under the influence of Daubigny and the Barbizon painters and learned to paint in clear, open light. He exhibited at the Salon and was one of the most successful Spanish landscape painters of the later nineteenth century, with a special success in Venice, where he spent many summers painting sparkling canals and palaces, as in A Canal in Venice, of 1879. He also painted views of the Seine, as in On the Seine, of 1869, and Spanish gardens and towns, such as A Spanish Garden, of 1871. His pictures were sought by American collectors. He died in Venice in 1908.","q21995239":"Little is recorded here of Martha Susan Baker, an American painter who lived from 1871 to 1911 and died in her early forties. The one picture that hangs here, Elizabeth Humphrey, of 1899, was painted when she was about twenty-eight, in the years when many young American women studied art seriously in Chicago, New York and Paris and some earned a living from portraits. Portraiture was one of the few branches of painting open to women of the period, since it could be practised at home and for a circle of friends and acquaintances. Details of Baker's training, teachers and patrons are not given here, and no further claim should be made about her career.","q937096":"John Martin was born in 1789 at Haydon Bridge, in Northumberland, and went to London in 1806, where he worked at first as a painter of china and glass. He became famous for huge, theatrical paintings of biblical and historical catastrophes, with tiny figures dwarfed by gigantic architecture and cosmic events, among them Joshua Commanding the Sun to Stand Still upon Gibeon, of 1816, which first brought him fame, and Belshazzar's Feast, of 1820, which drew crowds. He also made dramatic mezzotints, notably for Milton's Paradise Lost, and drew up plans for the water supply and sewers of London. His brother Jonathan set fire to York Minster in 1829. Martin died in the Isle of Man in 1854, and his work has been revived by modern interest in the sublime.","q1082324":"Martin van Meytens the Younger was born in Stockholm in 1695, the son of a painter of the same name, and trained with his father before travelling to the Netherlands, England, France and Italy. From the 1720s he worked in Vienna, where he became the leading court painter to Maria Theresa and her husband, Emperor Francis I, painting the family, the coronations and weddings of the imperial house in a polished, courtly manner with brilliant silks and jewels, and in 1759 he became director of the Vienna Academy. The double portrait of Count Giacomo Durazzo, the director of the court theatres and patron of Gluck, and his wife, Ernestine Aloisia Ungnad von Weissenwolff, dates from about the early 1760s. He died in Vienna in 1770.","q1155177":"Martino di Bartolommeo di Biagio is the full name of the Sienese painter also known as Martino di Bartolomeo, who worked in the late Gothic style of Siena in the early fifteenth century; the museum gives no dates for him. The Metropolitan's four narrow panels, showing Saint Anthony Abbot, Saint James the Greater, Saint Julian the Hospitaler and Saint Stephen, each paired with a second figure such as Saint John the Baptist, the Virgin of the Annunciation or the Angel, are probably pilaster panels from a larger altarpiece, in which saints stood in tall narrow frames beside the main scene. They show his clear outline, gold ground and bright, decorative colour. Little else of his career is recorded here.","q979541":"Martinus Rørbye was born in Drammen, Norway, in 1803 and grew up in Denmark, where he trained at the Copenhagen Academy under C. W. Eckersberg, the master of the Danish Golden Age. He travelled to Italy in the 1820s and in 1835 to Greece and Turkey with the architect Gottlieb Bindesbøll, which gave him many Eastern subjects, but he is best known for his clear, quiet views of Copenhagen and everyday Danish life. View from the Citadel Ramparts in Copenhagen by Moonlight, of 1839, is a fine example, with its hushed light and still air. He died in 1848, aged forty-four, in the year of the first war over Schleswig.","q6780619":"Mary Roberts, who died in Charleston, South Carolina, in 1761, is one of the earliest women recorded as a portrait painter in colonial America, though little else is known of her life, training or origins. The Metropolitan holds five portraits of members of the Middleton family, painted about 1752 to 1758, in the plain, clear manner of Charleston portraiture of the time. Charleston in these years was among the richest cities in the colonies, and its planters and merchants commissioned family portraits as marks of status and refinement. Roberts's pictures are valued today as a rare record of a woman's work in colonial art and as likenesses of one of the leading families of the Carolina low country.","q21995241":"Mary Way was born in 1769 and spent her working life in New London, Connecticut, where she made her living as a portrait miniaturist and teacher. Her advertisements and surviving portraits show that she painted the families of the port, among them sea captains, merchants and their wives, in small, clear likenesses with careful detail, and she is among the best-documented of the early American women professionals. Mary House, about 1795, is typical of her early work. Miniatures of this kind were treasured keepsakes, given to family and friends, worn as jewellery or kept in a case. She died in 1833, and her work is now valued as a record of New England provincial society of the early republic.","q5811":"Masaccio, whose real name was Tommaso di Ser Giovanni di Simone, was born in 1401 at San Giovanni Valdarno and went to Florence, where he joined the painters' guild in 1422. His nickname, meaning Clumsy Tom, set him apart from his older collaborator Masolino, Little Tom, with whom he worked on the Sant'Anna Altarpiece and the Brancacci Chapel in Santa Maria del Carmine. His frescoes there, among them the Tribute Money and the Expulsion from Eden, and the Holy Trinity in Santa Maria Novella, introduced solid, weighty figures, natural light and the new Florentine system of linear perspective. The Madonna of Humility, about 1423 to 1424, in Washington, is an early work. He died in Rome in 1428 at about twenty-six.","q514664":"Maso di Banco was a Florentine painter of the generation after Giotto and is regarded as one of Giotto's most gifted followers; he is recorded in the Florentine painters' guild in 1341, though little else of his life is known and the museum gives no dates. His best-known work is the fresco cycle of the Legend of Saint Sylvester and the emperor Constantine, painted about 1335 to 1340 in the Bardi di Vernio Chapel of Santa Croce in Florence, whose monumental figures and carefully built architectural spaces extend Giotto's ideas. Saint Anthony of Padua, about 1340, a panel in the Metropolitan, is attributed to him. He is a major figure of early Florentine painting, though his work is better known to specialists than to the public.","q318117":"Masolino da Panicale was born in 1383 at Panicale in Tuscany, trained as a goldsmith and as an assistant to Lorenzo Ghiberti, and entered the Florentine painters' guild in 1423. He worked with the younger Masaccio on the Sant'Anna altarpiece and the Brancacci Chapel frescoes in Santa Maria del Carmine, painting there the graceful Adam and Eve of the Temptation, with soft modelling and gentle faces that contrast with Masaccio's rugged strength. From 1425 to 1427 he worked in Hungary for the condottiere Pippo Spano, then in Rome and in Castiglione Olona in Lombardy, where he painted the Baptistery frescoes in 1435. His Annunciation, about 1423 to 1424, is in Washington. He is last documented in 1435.","q982384":"Massimo Stanzione was born near Naples in 1585 and became one of the leading painters of the city in the 1630s and 1640s. He is said to have spent time in Rome about 1617 to 1618, studying Raphael, the Carracci and the Bolognese Guido Reni, whose graceful idealism he blended with the dramatic light of Caravaggio's followers. He painted large altarpieces and frescoes for the churches of Naples, including the Gesù Nuovo and the Certosa di San Martino, working alongside Jusepe de Ribera, whose rougher figures offered a contrast to his own. His women, saints and heroines, such as Judith with the Head of Holofernes, about 1640, have a stately elegance and smooth finish. He died in Naples in 1656.","q314275":"Quinten Massys was born in Leuven about 1466 and entered the Antwerp painters' guild in 1491, soon becoming the leading painter of that fast-growing city. He combined the Netherlandish tradition of Van Eyck and Memling with Italian ideas, perhaps absorbed through Leonardo's drawings, making altarpieces, devotional pictures, portraits and a new kind of satirical genre. His friends included Erasmus and Pieter Gillis, whom he painted in 1517 as a pair of portraits for Thomas More. Ill-Matched Lovers, about 1520 to 1525, is typical of his pictures of grotesque old men and foolish couples, which mock greed and lust with sharp wit. He died in Antwerp in 1530. A legend that he began as a blacksmith is a later romantic story with little support.","q18577975":"The Master called Pseudo-Pier Francesco Fiorentino is the name given by scholars to an anonymous painter, or workshop, active in Florence between about 1460 and 1500. It comes from a large group of devotional pictures once ascribed to a documented painter, Pier Francesco Fiorentino, but now thought to be by another hand. The group consists chiefly of small Madonnas, often with the infant Saint John the Baptist and angels, painted in a sweet, graceful style that owes much to Fra Filippo Lippi and his followers, and made in numbers for the homes of Florentine families. The Metropolitan's Madonna and Child with the Infant Saint John the Baptist and Angels, undated, belongs to this group. The master's real name is not known.","q21850319":"Master G.Z. is known by the initials G.Z., and the Metropolitan Museum suggests, as a possibility only, that he may be Michele dai Carri, a painter of Ferrara documented from 1405 who died there in 1441. The museum's picture, painted about 1420 to 1430, shows the Madonna and Child with the donor, Pietro de' Lardi, presented by Saint Nicholas, in the elegant, courtly manner of the International Gothic style of northern Italy, with rich colour and gold. Further details of this painter's training and patrons are unknown, and the identification with Michele dai Carri remains tentative, so no more should be claimed about his life.","q10329047":"The Master of 1416 is a name given by scholars to an anonymous Italian painter, probably Florentine; the name refers to a dated work of 1416, and little else is known of him. He is known especially through two small panels in the Metropolitan, Ameto's Discovery of the Nymphs and A Contest between the Shepherds Alcesto and Acaten, of about 1410, which illustrate Boccaccio's pastoral romance Ameto, also called the Comedia delle ninfe fiorentine. Narrative panels of this kind, with small figures in bright colours, were typically made to decorate the fronts of wedding chests or the walls of chambers in wealthy houses, and they show the taste of early fifteenth-century Florence for secular stories alongside religious painting. The master's real name and training remain unknown.","q21850415":"The Master of Charles of Durazzo is the name given to an anonymous Italian painter after the Metropolitan's panel, The Conquest of Naples by Charles of Durazzo, dated 1381 to 1382. It is a rare painting of an event only a year or so old: in 1381 Charles of Durazzo took Naples and captured Queen Joanna I, whom he had put to death the following year, becoming King Charles III. Narrative panels of this kind, painted with lively small figures, banners and coats of arms, may have been made for a wedding chest or for a patron's room. The museum also holds a panel of Scenes from a Legend, with Coats of Arms, by the same hand. The painter's real name and training are not recorded.","q4284300":"The Master of Città di Castello is the name given to an anonymous painter active in Umbria between about 1290 and 1320, named for that Umbrian town. He belongs to the generation of Cimabue, Duccio and the young Giotto, when painters in central Italy were slowly moving from the flat Byzantine manner toward more solid, human forms. The Maestà in Washington, of about 1290, shows the Virgin enthroned with her Child among four angels, in the formal, symmetrical arrangement used for altarpieces in the late thirteenth century, with a gold ground and a richly patterned throne. The painter's real name, training and patrons are unknown, and he is known only through a small group of works.","q21685956":"The Master of Eggenburg is the name given to an anonymous Austrian painter active about 1490 to 1500, apparently taken from the town of Eggenburg in Lower Austria. His two panels in the Metropolitan, Saint Adalbert and Saint Procopius, and The Burial of Saint Wenceslas, both dated about 1490 to 1500, show saints specially honoured in the Bohemian lands, which lay close to Lower Austria, and so they may have been made for a church in the region. The style is that of late Gothic painting in Central Europe, with sharp, angular drapery, clear colour and a taste for narrative detail. Nothing is known of the master's real name, training or patrons.","q1918369":"The Master of Flora is the conventional name for an anonymous painter whom the Metropolitan Museum lists as Italian, without dates; the name comes from a painting of Flora, the goddess of spring and flowers. The one picture that hangs here, The Birth of Cupid, is undated and shows a mythological subject: Cupid, the child of Venus and god of love, was a favourite theme in Italian art of the Renaissance, in furniture panels, small cabinet pictures and palace decorations, where such stories flattered the learned tastes of patrons. The painter's real name, training and patrons are unknown, and nothing further should be assumed about him from the picture alone.","q204874":"The Master of Frankfurt is the name given to an anonymous Netherlandish painter who worked in Antwerp from about 1490 into the 1510s, and takes his name from an altarpiece in Frankfurt. He painted devotional pictures, altarpieces and portraits, and is thought to be the author of a double portrait dated 1496 that may show the painter and his wife, now in Antwerp. His style combines careful Netherlandish detail with a love of bright colour and busy, crowded settings. Saint Anne with the Virgin and the Christ Child, about 1511 to 1515, in Washington, is typical of his Saint Anne subjects, which were very popular in Antwerp at the time. It has been proposed that he is the painter Hendrik van Wueluwe, though this is uncertain.","q1918689":"The Master of Heiligenkreuz is the name given to an anonymous Austrian painter active about 1400, named after altarpiece panels connected with Heiligenkreuz, the Cistercian abbey in the Vienna Woods. He worked in the International Gothic style then fashionable across Europe, with soft, flowing drapery, gentle faces and rich, clear colour, and he is regarded as a leading Austrian painter of his generation. The Death of Saint Clare, about 1400 to 1410, in Washington, shows the saint on her deathbed among mourners, a moving scene of a Franciscan nun's last hours. The master's real name, training and patrons are unknown, and the dates given for him, 1375 to 1430, are approximate.","q21851408":"The Master of Lecceto is the conventional name for an anonymous Italian painter, apparently named for Lecceto, an Augustinian hermitage in the hills near Siena, and the museum gives no dates for him. The one picture that hangs here is King Solomon and the Queen of Sheba, undated. The visit of the Queen of Sheba to the wise King Solomon was a popular subject for Italian wedding chests and furniture panels in the fifteenth century, where it carried associations of wisdom, wealth and a splendid, honourable meeting. The painter's real name, training and patrons are not recorded, and no further claims about his career should be made.","q18508515":"The Master of Monte Oliveto is the name given to an anonymous painter of Siena, active in the 1310s and 1320s and named for a Madonna associated with the abbey of Monte Oliveto Maggiore, near Siena. He belonged to the circle of Duccio, the founder of the Sienese school, and painted small devotional pictures with gold backgrounds, soft modelling and a delicate, lyrical line. The Metropolitan's Madonna and Child Enthroned, about 1315 to 1320, and Saints and Scenes from the Life of the Virgin, about 1320, are typical of the portable panels of the period, made for private prayer or small chapels. The painter's real name, training and patrons are not recorded, and the museum gives no dates for him.","q377375":"The Master of Saint Francis is the name given to an anonymous painter active in Umbria in the third quarter of the thirteenth century, taken from a panel of Saint Francis in Assisi. He was one of the first to paint the saint, who had died in 1226 and been canonised in 1228, in the plain, lifelike manner that became the standard image of the friar. The two small saints in Washington, Saint James Minor and Saint John the Evangelist, about 1272, probably belonged to a larger altarpiece. His work, with its heavy outlines, flat gold and Byzantine-inspired forms, comes from the generation before Cimabue and Giotto. His real name is unknown.","q1854159":"The Master of Saint Giles is the name given to an anonymous painter, called Franco-Flemish, who worked about 1500 and is named after two panels of the legend of Saint Giles, now in London. His pictures combine the careful detail of Flemish painting with a French court taste for elegance, and they are noted for lively narrative and for views of contemporary buildings and costume. The Baptism of Clovis, about 1500, in Washington, tells the story of the conversion of the first Christian king of the Franks, and Episodes from the Life of a Bishop Saint, also about 1500, may come from the same altarpiece. The painter's real name is unknown, and he is probably to be placed in or near Paris.","q506381":"The Master of Saint Veronica is the name given to the leading painter of Cologne in the late fourteenth and early fifteenth centuries, taken from a panel showing Saint Veronica holding the cloth that bears the face of Christ. He is the chief representative in Cologne of the courtly International Gothic style, with sweet, delicate faces, flowing draperies and soft, light colour, and his example led the way to Stefan Lochner, the next great Cologne painter. The Crucifixion, about 1400 to 1410, in Washington, comes from the middle of his career. His real name is unknown, and the dates 1340 to 1455 sometimes given for him are not reliable.","q1277376":"The Master of the Acts of Mercy is the name given to an anonymous Austrian painter, whom the Metropolitan Museum lists without dates, after a subject that he painted: the Acts of Mercy, the charitable works that the Gospel of Matthew says will be rewarded at the Last Judgement, such as feeding the hungry, giving drink to the thirsty and visiting the sick and the imprisoned. The museum's panel has The Martyrdom of Saint Lawrence on one side and Giving Drink to the Thirsty on the other. Such cycles were popular for hospitals and charitable confraternities, which wished their patrons to be reminded of their duties. The painter's real name, training and patrons are not recorded.","master_of_the_annunciation_to_the_shepherds":"The Master of the Annunciation to the Shepherds is the conventional name for an anonymous Italian painter, named after a picture of that Gospel scene, and the museum gives no dates for him. The Metropolitan's The Sense of Sight belongs to a type that was popular in the seventeenth century, the allegory of the five senses, in which each sense is shown through a figure, often humble or everyday, in the dramatic, realistic manner introduced by Caravaggio. Some scholars place the master among the Caravaggesque painters of Naples, though his real name and career remain uncertain. Further claims about his training and patrons are best left aside.","bronzino":"Agnolo di Cosimo, called Bronzino, was born near Florence in 1503 and died there in 1572. He trained under Raffaellino del Garbo and then under Jacopo Pontormo, whom he assisted on frescoes and whose strained, elongated manner he refined into something cooler and more polished. From about 1540 he was court painter to Duke Cosimo I de' Medici, for whom he painted the ducal family and decorated the chapel of the duchess Eleonora of Toledo in the Palazzo Vecchio. His portraits of Florentine courtiers, with their enamelled surfaces, costly fabrics and guarded, unreadable faces, set a pattern for formal portraiture across Europe. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Young Man, from the 1530s, shows a composed youth whose identity is unknown. Bronzino also wrote witty verse. Later critics found him chilly; modern ones admire his control.","chester_harding":"Chester Harding was born in Conway, Massachusetts, in 1792 and came to painting late, after working as a chairmaker and sign painter. He had almost no formal training. After taking likenesses in Pittsburgh and Kentucky, he painted the aged frontiersman Daniel Boone from life in Missouri in 1820, a portrait that helped to make his name. In 1823 he sailed to England, where his direct, forceful American portraits found favour among the aristocracy and he exhibited at the Royal Academy. Back in Boston by 1826, he became one of the busiest portraitists in New England, painting senators, judges and merchants, among them Daniel Webster and Chief Justice John Marshall. His portrait of John Childe dates from about 1835. His lively memoir, A Sketch of Chester Harding, Artist, appeared in 1866, the year he died.","claude_lorrain":"Claude Gellée, called Claude Lorrain after his native duchy of Lorraine, was born in 1604 and became the most admired landscape painter of the seventeenth century. Early biographers say he went to Rome as a young man and entered the service of the landscape painter Agostino Tassi. He was settled in the city by about 1627 and rarely left it again. Popes, cardinals and foreign collectors bought his work, among them Pope Urban VIII. His subject was an ideal Italian countryside of harbours, ruins and shepherds, bathed in the low gold light of dawn or evening, with a biblical or mythological story kept small in the foreground. To guard against forgers he kept the Liber Veritatis, a book of drawings recording his finished paintings. He died in Rome in 1682. Turner and Constable studied him, and English garden designers laid out parks in his manner.","dosso_dossi":"Dosso Dossi, born Giovanni Luteri, was the leading painter at the court of Ferrara. He was probably born about 1486 and died in Ferrara in 1542. He served the Este dukes Alfonso I and Ercole II, painting altarpieces, mythologies, banners and festival decorations, often alongside his brother Battista, and he supplied pictures for Alfonso's famous camerino, where Bellini and Titian also worked. His Venetian colour, glowing woods and strange, half-comic fantasy show the example of Giorgione and Titian. Among his best-known pictures are the sorceress Circe and Jupiter painting butterflies. The poet Ariosto praised both brothers in Orlando Furioso. Fashion later passed him by, but modern scholars have restored him as one of the most inventive painters of the Italian Renaissance. The Cleveland portrait of a young man dates from about 1530.","fitz_henry_lane":"Fitz Henry Lane was born in Gloucester, Massachusetts, in 1804 as Nathaniel Rogers Lane and took his new name as a young man; many books print it Fitz Hugh. A childhood illness left him unable to walk without crutches. He learned lithography at a Boston print shop in the 1830s, turned to oil painting in the 1840s, and was influenced by the marine painter Robert Salmon. From 1848 he lived in Gloucester, in a granite house built to his design above the harbour, painting ships, coves and harbour towns from New York to Maine. His calm water, clear light and exact drawing of vessels were later grouped under the name luminism. The Cleveland view of Boston harbour dates from about 1846 to 1847. He died in Gloucester in 1865 and was nearly forgotten until the mid twentieth century.","francesco_granacci":"Francesco Granacci was born near Florence in 1469 and trained in the workshop of Domenico Ghirlandaio, where he met the boy Michelangelo, who became his lifelong friend. Vasari records that Granacci lent Michelangelo drawings and encouraged him toward painting. In 1508 Michelangelo summoned him to Rome as one of the assistants for the Sistine Chapel ceiling, though he soon returned to Florence. There he ran a busy workshop, painting altarpieces, Madonnas and small narrative panels such as the Cleveland scene of John the Baptist being carried to his father Zacharias, and he arranged festival decorations for the Medici, including the 1515 entry of Pope Leo X. His clear, pleasant, rather conservative style never matched his friend's. He died in Florence in 1543.","gentileschi_artemisia":"Artemisia Gentileschi was born in Rome in 1593 and learned to paint in the workshop of her father, Orazio Gentileschi, a follower of Caravaggio. In 1612 she married a Florentine and moved to Florence, where she painted for the Medici court, took part in decorating the Casa Buonarroti for Michelangelo's great-nephew, and in 1616 became the first woman admitted to the Accademia del Disegno. She corresponded with Galileo. Later she worked in Rome, Venice and, from about 1630, mostly in Naples, and around 1638 joined her father at the court of Charles I in London. Her heroines, among them Judith, Susanna, Cleopatra and Mary Magdalene, are painted with strong light, firm drawing and rich colour. Long overshadowed by her father, she has been studied and exhibited widely since the 1970s. She died in Naples around 1655.","giovanni_battista_gaulli":"Giovanni Battista Gaulli, nicknamed Baciccio, was born in Genoa in 1639 and moved to Rome as a young man. The sculptor and architect Gian Lorenzo Bernini befriended him and helped him win the commission that made his name, the decoration of the nave vault of the Jesuit mother church, Il Gesù. There, in the 1670s, he painted the Triumph of the Name of Jesus, in which figures seem to tumble out of the painted heaven and over the stucco frame made by Antonio Raggi. He was also a sought-after portraitist, painting several popes and Bernini himself. His work joined Roman grandeur to a Genoese warmth of colour that recalls Rubens and Van Dyck, whose pictures were known in Genoa. The Cleveland oil sketch for the Four Prophets of Israel shows his rapid brushwork. He died in Rome in 1709.","giovanni_benedetto_castiglione":"Giovanni Benedetto Castiglione, called Il Grechetto, was born in Genoa in 1609 and died in Mantua in 1664. He trained in Genoa, where Van Dyck had worked and Rubens's pictures were known, and later worked in Rome, Naples, Florence and Venice. He painted herds, shepherds and biblical patriarchs, such as Abraham's servant swearing his oath, in warm, russet landscapes crowded with beasts, baskets and bundles. He was also a brilliant draftsman and etcher, in touch with Rembrandt's prints and the classical landscapes of Poussin, and he is generally credited with inventing the monotype, a single impression pulled from a painted plate. In his last years he served the Gonzaga court at Mantua. His brush sketches on paper were widely admired, and his drawings and prints are prized by collectors today.","hals_dirck":"Dirck Hals was born in Haarlem in 1591, the younger brother of the portraitist Frans Hals, and joined the Haarlem painters' guild in 1627. Where Frans painted portraits with dashing brushwork, Dirck specialised in small, bright pictures of merry companies: fashionable young men and women drinking, flirting and making music indoors or on a terrace, in silvery silks and light colours. His manner grew out of Haarlem painters such as Willem Buytewech and Esaias van de Velde, who had made this kind of picture popular, and the Washington terrace scene of 1625 comes from his early years. Dutch townspeople bought such small panels to hang in their homes. His work was long overshadowed by his famous brother, but collectors now value it for its lively figures and fine detail. He died in Haarlem in 1656.","inman_john_o_brien":"John O'Brien Inman was an American painter, born in 1828, who died in 1896. He was the son of Henry Inman, the New York portrait painter and a founder of the National Academy of Design, who died in 1846. Little is recorded of the younger Inman's own career beyond a small number of pictures. The two in the National Gallery of Art are still lifes, dated 1868 and 1871, and they belong to the American taste of the years after the Civil War for carefully drawn arrangements of food, drink and household objects on a table. Pictures of this kind were small enough for a dining room or a gentleman's study and suited the homes of the growing middle class. His reputation today rests on these few works, and his name is far less known than his father's.","jacopo_bassano":"Jacopo Bassano, born Jacopo da Ponte about 1510, took his name from the small town of Bassano, below the Venetian Alps, where he lived and worked almost all his life and died in 1592. He learned from his father, Francesco da Ponte the Elder, and spent a short time in Venice in the studio of Bonifazio de' Pitati, where Titian, Tintoretto and the prints of Parmigianino shaped his taste. His speciality was the Bible told as a country story: shepherds, labourers, dogs, cattle, baskets and kettles, painted in warm, glowing colour, often by lamplight or at dusk. Lazarus and the Rich Man, in Cleveland, tells the parable with the poor man and the dogs at the rich man's door. Venetian patrons and collectors across Europe bought his work, and his four sons, who ran the busy workshop, made many versions of his pastoral scenes.","jacopo_tintoretto":"Jacopo Robusti, called Tintoretto, the little dyer, after his father's trade, was born in Venice in 1518 and lived there all his life. He is said to have spent only a short time in Titian's workshop and to have taught himself largely by drawing from casts and from works after Michelangelo, though the details of his training are poorly documented. His reputation was made by the Miracle of the Slave in 1548, with its daring, energetic figures. From 1564 until 1587 he painted the walls and ceilings of the Scuola Grande di San Rocco in Venice, a vast cycle of biblical scenes; he won the first commission there by delivering a finished ceiling painting when his rivals submitted only sketches. Late in life he painted Paradise in the Doge's Palace. He died in Venice in 1594. Quick brushwork, sharp perspective and flickering light mark his style.","jusepe_de_ribera":"Jusepe de Ribera was born at Xàtiva, near Valencia, in 1591 and left Spain for Italy while still young. He is recorded in Rome around 1613 and in Naples from 1616, where he married the daughter of the Sicilian-born painter Giovanni Bernardino Azzolino, worked for the Spanish viceroys and stayed for the rest of his life. Italians called him Lo Spagnoletto, the Little Spaniard, and he proudly signed pictures as a Spaniard. His early work follows Caravaggio, with hard raking light, weathered models and unflinching detail; after about 1630 his colour warmed under the influence of Venetian painting. He painted saints, philosophers and martyrs, and his etchings carried his compositions across Europe. His Saint Jerome in Cleveland, from about 1638 to 1640, is a late example of a subject he returned to often. He died in Naples in 1652.","le_nain":"Le Nain is the name of three brothers from Laon in northern France, Antoine, Louis and Mathieu, who were established in Paris by about 1629 and worked from a shared workshop. They signed pictures only with the family name and a date, never a first name, so scholars still argue over who painted what. Antoine, the eldest, is credited with small group portraits; Louis with grave scenes of peasant families in muted greys and browns; Mathieu, the youngest and the only one to live long, with portraits and guardroom scenes, and he served as painter to the city of Paris. Antoine and Louis died within days of each other in May 1648. Their peasants, shown with dignity rather than humour, drew little notice in their own century and were championed in the nineteenth by the critic Champfleury and the realists.","master_of_the_brussels_calling_of_saint_matthew":"The Master of the Brussels Calling of Saint Matthew is an anonymous Netherlandish painter. Art historians coin such names for an artist known only through pictures that seem to share one hand, and this one takes his from a painting of the Calling of Saint Matthew in Brussels. No record of his real name, birth, death, teacher or patrons has been found. The Metropolitan Museum dates its picture, The Arrival in Bethlehem, to about 1540 to 1550, which places him in the middle of the sixteenth century, when painters in the Low Countries were learning from Italian art while keeping the northern love of crowded detail. Beyond that, little can be said with confidence, and the attribution of other pictures to him remains a matter of scholarly judgement.","master_of_the_canesso_peddler":"The Master of the Canesso Peddler is an anonymous painter active in northern Italy in the later seventeenth century. Art historians give such temporary names to artists known only from pictures that seem to share one hand, and no real name, dates, teacher or patron has been established for this one. The Metropolitan Museum's A Book Peddler, dated about 1670 to 1690, shows a travelling seller of books, a figure from everyday street life. Pictures of peddlers, beggars and tradesmen found buyers in Italy in the seventeenth century, alongside the more usual religious and mythological subjects. Beyond this, little is known, and any further attributions rest on comparison of style rather than on documents.","master_of_the_holden_tondo":"The Master of the Holden Tondo is an anonymous Italian painter of the early sixteenth century. Art historians give such names to artists known only through pictures that seem to share one hand, and no real name, dates, teacher or patron is recorded for this one. His name comes from a tondo, a round devotional picture, and the Cleveland Museum's Virgin and Child with Angels, dated to the early 1500s, is the picture shown here. Round pictures of the Virgin were a favourite in Italian homes, hung in a bedroom or a private chapel, and Florentine painters made many of them in the decades around 1500. Beyond this, little can be said with certainty, and scholars continue to debate which other works belong to the same hand.","mattia_preti":"Mattia Preti was born in 1613 at Taverna in Calabria and became known as Il Cavalier Calabrese, the Calabrian Knight. He trained in Rome, where Caravaggio's followers and the Bolognese painter Guercino left their mark on him, and he worked in Modena, Venice and Naples. In Naples, during the plague of 1656, he painted votive frescoes on the city gates. From 1661 he lived on Malta, where the Knights of St John admitted him to their order and where he painted the vault of St John's Co-Cathedral in Valletta with scenes from the life of John the Baptist. His dramatic pictures combine Caravaggio's dark shadows with Venetian colour and bold foreshortening. He remained on Malta until his death in 1699, and his Saint Paul the Hermit in Cleveland belongs to those years.","max_bohm":"Max Bohm was born in Cleveland, Ohio, in 1868 and spent much of his career in France. He studied in Paris in the early 1890s, as many young Americans did, and painted at Étaples on the Channel coast, among the fishing families and the colony of foreign artists who gathered there. His subjects were sailors, fisherfolk and their wives and children, treated in a broad, solid manner with deep, dark colour; he also painted portraits, such as the Cleveland picture of Mr. C., from his student years. He exhibited in both France and America. Bohm died in 1923. His work is less well known today than that of other Americans of his generation, but museums, including the one in his home city, still hold it.","meindert_hobbema":"Meindert Hobbema was baptised in Amsterdam in 1638 and died there in 1709. He is recorded as a pupil of Jacob van Ruisdael, and his woodland pictures, with cottages, watermills and sunlit paths among tall trees, show how closely he learned from that master. In 1668 he married and took a post as a wine gauger for the city of Amsterdam, and afterwards he painted far less, though his masterpiece, The Avenue at Middelharnis, dates from 1689. His reputation grew mainly after his death: in the eighteenth century English collectors prized his landscapes, and British painters, Gainsborough and Constable among them, learned from them. The Cleveland cottage in the woods dates from about 1662, in his early years, when his debt to Ruisdael was at its clearest.","melchior_de_hondecoeter":"Melchior de Hondecoeter was born in Utrecht in 1636, the son and pupil of the bird painter Gijsbert de Hondecoeter, and later learned from his uncle Jan Baptist Weenix. He worked in Utrecht, then in The Hague, where he joined the painters' confraternity, and from about 1663 in Amsterdam, where he spent the rest of his life and died in 1695. He specialised in birds: farmyard cockerels, hens and ducks, peacocks, and exotic species from the aviaries of wealthy collectors, usually in a garden or park, with feathers painted in rich colour and fine detail. His pictures of birds squabbling, like the Cleveland rooster and turkey, were popular, and he painted large decorative canvases for the stadholder William III. His best-known picture, The Floating Feather, is in the Rijksmuseum.","miller_william_rickarby":"William Rickarby Miller was born in England in 1818, worked in the United States, chiefly in New York, and died in 1893. He is remembered mainly as a watercolourist of New York streets, buildings and country views, which later historians have used as a record of a fast-changing city. Little else about his training or patrons is firmly recorded. The National Gallery of Art's Study of Apples from Nature, dated 1863, is a close, careful study of fruit, painted in the decade when American admirers of John Ruskin urged artists to paint what they saw with exact attention to detail. The title says plainly what he set out to do.","moillon_louise":"Louise Moillon was born in Paris in 1609 or 1610 into a Protestant family of painters and art dealers; her father Nicolas Moillon died when she was a child, and her stepfather, François Garnier, was also a painter and dealer. She was painting still lifes by her late teens and signed and dated pictures from 1629. Her subjects were fruit and vegetables, often in a basket or on a stone ledge, set in clear, quiet light and painted with great precision and a rich, glowing surface. The peaches and grapes of 1636 in Washington belong to her best years. Parisian collectors bought her work. After her marriage in 1640 she painted much less, and she died in Paris in 1696. Overlooked for a long time, she is now ranked among the finest French painters of still life.","netherlandish_16th_century":"Netherlandish 16th Century is not a single painter but the label the National Gallery of Art gives to pictures made in the Low Countries in the 1500s whose authors cannot be named. Its Healing of the Paralytic, dated between about 1560 and 1590, shows one of the miracles of healing from the Gospels. Nothing is recorded of who painted it, where he trained or who bought it. The later sixteenth century was a time of upheaval in the Netherlands, with the iconoclastic riots of 1566 and the revolt against Spain, yet Antwerp and other cities still supported many workshops that produced religious scenes and portraits for churches and private patrons. Without a signature or a document, a picture like this can only be placed by its style and its materials.","netherlandish_painter":"Netherlandish (Antwerp) Painter, about 1520, is a label rather than a person. The Metropolitan Museum uses it for pictures made in the southern Netherlands by painters whose names have not been recovered, and the twelve works shown here may be by several different hands. They include portraits of men and women, among them a likeness of the Emperor Charles V and one of a noblewoman thought to be Isabella of Portugal, and devotional pictures such as an Adoration of the Magi and The Fifteen Mysteries and the Virgin of the Rosary, painted about 1515 to 1520. Around 1520 Antwerp was the leading trading port of northern Europe, with a guild of painters, large workshops and a ready market for altarpieces, portraits and small devotional panels. No names, teachers or firm dates can be given.","netherlandish_painters":"Netherlandish (Antwerp) Painters is a label, not a single artist, and the plural suggests a workshop or a group of hands that cannot be told apart. The Metropolitan Museum's two pictures here are a Rest on the Flight into Egypt and a Last Supper dated 1515 to 1520, and the date given for the group, the middle of the sixteenth century, is only approximate. Antwerp in the first half of the century was the commercial capital of northern Europe, and its guild of Saint Luke supported busy workshops that produced altarpieces and devotional panels for churches, merchants and export. Such pictures were made as a shared craft, often without signatures, and no individual names, dates or patrons can be given.","nicolas_bernard_l_pici":"Nicolas-Bernard Lépicié was born in Paris in 1735 and died there in 1784. His father, François-Bernard Lépicié, was an engraver and secretary of the Académie royale de peinture et de sculpture, and the son studied with the history painter Carle Van Loo. He became a member of the Academy in 1769 with a history subject, the Courage of Portia, but he is remembered for something quieter: small scenes of modest domestic life, with mothers, children and servants, in the tradition of Chardin and Greuze, which found a ready market among Parisian collectors. His Woman Reading, dated 1769 and now in Cleveland, comes from the same year as his election. He exhibited at the Salon regularly until his early death. Often compared with Chardin, he remains a lesser-known figure.","nicolas_de_largilli_re":"Nicolas de Largillière was born in Paris in 1656 and brought up in Antwerp, where he trained. In 1674 he went to London and worked with Peter Lely, the leading portraitist there, before returning to Paris in the early 1680s. Elected to the Académie royale in 1686 with a portrait of the court painter Charles Le Brun, he became, with Hyacinthe Rigaud, one of the two most sought-after portrait painters in France. He painted nobles, magistrates, financiers, artists and city officials in rich fabrics and warm, silvery colour, and also still lifes and some history subjects. Jean-Baptiste Oudry was his pupil. The Cleveland portrait of the Comte de Richbourg dates from 1734, when Largillière was almost eighty. He died in Paris in 1746, aged ninety.","no_l_nicolas_coypel":"Noël-Nicolas Coypel was born in Paris in 1690 and died there in 1734. He belonged to the Coypel family, which supplied painters to the French court for three generations: his father Noël Coypel had directed the French Academy in Rome and worked for Louis XIV, and his half-brother Antoine Coypel became first painter to the king in 1715. He trained within the family and entered the Académie royale, and he painted mythological scenes and religious pictures in the light, graceful manner that followed the grand style of Louis XIV's reign. The Cleveland picture of the Miracles of Saint James the Greater is dated 1726. He died at forty-four, before he could rival his half-brother's career, and he is now less well known than his father and his half-brother.","north_italian_15th_century":"North Italian 15th Century is a label, not a painter. The National Gallery of Art uses it for portraits made in northern Italy in the 1400s by artists whose names have not survived. The three pictures shown here are a Profile Portrait of a Boy, from about 1460 to 1470, a Portrait of a Man, and a portrait of Francesco Sforza, who took power in Milan in 1450 and ruled as duke until his death in 1466. That picture is dated about 1480 to 1500, so it was painted well after his death, perhaps from an earlier likeness. Profile portraits, modelled on ancient coins and on the medals of Pisanello, were fashionable at the courts of Milan, Ferrara and Mantua. No painter's name, dates or patron can be given for any of the three.","north_italian_16th_century":"North Italian 16th Century is a label, not a painter: the National Gallery of Art uses it for a picture made in northern Italy in the 1500s by an artist whose name has not survived. Its Alessandro Alberti with a Page, possibly of 1544 or 1545, shows a gentleman with a young attendant, a type of portrait popular across Lombardy and the Veneto, where painters such as Titian, Lotto and Moretto set the standard for sober dress, direct gaze and quiet settings. The title names the sitter but not the painter, and scholars can place the picture only by its style. Nothing can be said of the painter's training, patrons or dates.","north_italian_painter":"North Italian Painter (Verona?) is a label, not a named artist, and the question mark shows that even the place is uncertain. The Metropolitan Museum dates its Two Angels to about 1330, the age of Giotto's followers, when painters in northern Italy were beginning to absorb the Florentine and Paduan reforms of figure and space but still painted on gold grounds for churches and for the local lords. Small angels of this kind are often fragments of larger altarpieces that were later taken apart. A Portrait of a Man carries the same label in the museum's catalogue. Nothing is recorded of the painter's name, training, patrons or dates, and the attribution of other pictures to the same hand rests on comparison of style alone.","north_netherlandish_15th_century":"North Netherlandish 15th Century is a label, not a painter. The National Gallery of Art uses it for pictures made in the northern Netherlands, the region of Holland and Utrecht, in the 1400s by unnamed artists. Its Adoration of the Magi is dated to the last quarter of the century. At that time the northern provinces had their own painters, among them Geertgen tot Sint Jans in Haarlem, who worked in a quieter, more rounded manner than the Flemish masters of Bruges and Ghent. Many artists of the region are known only by invented names that scholars give them. For this picture no author, dates, teacher or patron can be given, and it can be placed only by its style.","north_netherlandish_painter":"North Netherlandish Painter, about 1516 to 1520, is a label, not a named artist. The Metropolitan Museum gives it to two pictures from the northern Netherlands, painted by artists whose names have not been recovered. One consists of portraits of Jan, first Count of Egmond, who lived from 1438 or 1441 to 1516, and of his wife Magdalena, who lived from 1464 to 1538. The Egmonds were one of the great noble families of Holland. The other picture is a Christ Bearing the Cross. The date given for the portraits falls close to the count's death in 1516. Beyond that, nothing is recorded about the painter's name, training or workshop, and the attribution can rest only on comparison with other pictures.","northern_european_painter":"Northern European Painter, about 1770, is a label, not a named artist. The Metropolitan Museum uses it for a portrait that it cannot place more closely than northern Europe, which might mean the Netherlands, Germany or Scandinavia. The picture is catalogued as A Man with the Initials CJD, taking its title from the letters rather than from a sitter's name. Portraits of this kind, made in the later eighteenth century for merchants, officials and professional men, were often painted by local artists who rarely signed their work and left few records. Nothing is known of this painter's name, training or dates, and the sitter, too, remains unidentified beyond his initials.","northern_french_painter":"Northern French Painter, about 1450, is a label, not a named artist, and the five entries shown here may not all come from one hand. The Metropolitan Museum gives the label to several small panels of the mid-fifteenth century, among them saints' legends and an Annunciation with their donors, two double-sided panels of the Crucifixion and the Resurrection, a Christ Bearing the Cross, the martyrdom of Saint Adrian, and a Portrait of a Man of the Moncheaux Family. Northern France then lay close to the Flemish and Burgundian centres of painting, and many painters of the region remain anonymous. No names, dates or teachers can be given, and the pictures can be grouped only by their style.","orazio_gentileschi":"Orazio Gentileschi was born in Pisa in 1563 and went to Rome as a youth. He became one of the first and most gifted followers of Caravaggio, whom he knew; at a libel trial in 1603 he testified that Caravaggio had borrowed from him a pair of wings to use as a prop. His clear, cool Caravaggesque pictures grew lighter in colour over the years. He worked in Rome and the Marche, in Genoa and then at the French court, and in 1626 he settled in London as painter to Charles I, for whom he decorated a ceiling in the Queen's House at Greenwich. His daughter Artemisia trained in his workshop and later joined him in England. He died in London in 1639. The Cleveland Danaë, dated about 1623, comes from the years when he was working in Genoa.","otto_h_bacher":"Otto Bacher was born in Cleveland, Ohio, in 1856 and studied in Cincinnati and then in Munich under the American painter Frank Duveneck, becoming one of the young Americans known as the Duveneck Boys. In 1879 and 1880 he was in Venice with Duveneck's circle, where he took up etching and came to know James McNeill Whistler, who was then making his Venice etchings and pastels. Bacher recalled that time in his book With Whistler in Venice, published in 1908. Back in the United States he worked as an etcher, illustrator and painter in oil and watercolour. His Cleveland pictures include a portrait of his wife, Mary Holland Bacher, of 1891, and a late study of a diving cormorant. He died in 1909.","paolo_veronese":"Paolo Caliari, called Veronese after his birthplace, was born in Verona in 1528 and trained there with the painter Antonio Badile. By the early 1550s he had settled in Venice, where he worked alongside Titian and Tintoretto. He painted festive biblical banquets filled with silks, architecture and onlookers, among them the vast Wedding Feast at Cana of 1563, now in the Louvre. In 1573 the Inquisition summoned him over a Last Supper that included buffoons and soldiers, and he defended an artist's right to take liberties; the painting was retitled Feast in the House of Levi. His clear, silvery colour, his ceilings in the Doge's Palace and his frescoes at the Villa Barbaro in Maser were admired across Europe. His portrait of Agostino Barbarigo recalls the admiral killed at Lepanto in 1571. Veronese died in Venice in 1588.","pedro_berruguete":"Pedro Berruguete was born about 1450 at Paredes de Nava in Castile and is generally counted the first great Renaissance painter of Spain. He worked in Urbino in the later 1470s for Duke Federico da Montefeltro, where he is credited, with the Flemish painter Justus of Ghent, with portraits of famous men for the duke's study, and where he absorbed Italian perspective and the Flemish oil technique. By the early 1480s he was back in Castile, working at Toledo, at Ávila and in his native town. His altarpiece for the Dominican convent of Santo Tomás at Ávila, painted in the 1490s, and a scene of Saint Dominic presiding at an auto-da-fé, now in the Prado, are among his best-known works. His son Alonso became a celebrated sculptor. Berruguete died in 1503.","peter_lely":"Peter Lely was born Pieter van der Faes in 1618 at Soest in Westphalia, to Dutch parents; the name Lely came from a lily on his father's house in The Hague. He trained in Haarlem under Frans Pietersz de Grebber and went to England in the early 1640s, where he made his career as a portrait painter. He painted Charles I and his children in captivity, and later the leaders of the Commonwealth. After the Restoration in 1660 he became Principal Painter to Charles II and painted the court beauties and the admirals of the Dutch wars in a languid, silky manner. His workshop produced many replicas, and he built up a fine collection of old master drawings. He was knighted in 1679 and died in 1680. The Cleveland portrait of Mrs. Leneve, about 1657, belongs to the Commonwealth years.","pier_francesco_foschi":"Pier Francesco Foschi was born in Florence in 1502 and died there in 1567. Vasari and later writers link him with the circle of Andrea del Sarto, whose soft colour and gentle, rounded figures he followed, and he later moved toward the more polished manner of Pontormo and Bronzino that dominated Florence under Duke Cosimo I. He painted altarpieces, devotional pictures and portraits for churches and private patrons in the city and its countryside. The Cleveland portrait, about 1560, shows a woman in the guise of Saint Catherine, identified by her attributes, an example of the fashion for portraits in sacred disguise. Few of his works are signed or documented, and he is less well known than the great names of his day.","pierre_rousseau":"Pierre Rousseau, who lived from 1751 to 1829 according to the museum, is represented here not by easel paintings but by interior decoration of the 1790s: pairs of double-leaf doors, decorative panels and overdoor paintings now in Cleveland. Rooms of this kind were fitted out in Paris in the years of the Revolution and the Directory in the new antique taste, with arabesques, urns, garlands and figures painted in light, clear colours after Roman wall paintings and Raphael's loggia in the Vatican. Almost nothing about Rousseau's life, training or patrons is recorded in the sources available here, and, as with most such schemes, several craftsmen probably worked on the finished rooms. His name survives chiefly through these painted doors and panels.","pieter_bruegel_the_elder":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder was probably born between about 1525 and 1530; his birthplace is uncertain. He trained under the Antwerp painter Pieter Coecke van Aelst, entered the Antwerp guild of Saint Luke in 1551, and travelled to Italy in 1552 to 1554, reaching the far south and drawing the Alps on his way home. In Antwerp he designed prints for the publisher Hieronymus Cock; from 1563 he lived in Brussels, where he married his master's daughter. His paintings of peasants at work and festival, proverbs, biblical scenes set in Flemish villages and wide landscapes were bought by merchants and officials. In 1565 he painted a series of the months for the Antwerp collector Nicolaes Jonghelinck, which includes The Harvesters. He died in Brussels in 1569. His sons Pieter and Jan Brueghel became painters.","pieter_cornelisz_van_slingelandt":"Pieter Cornelisz van Slingelandt was born in Leiden in 1640 and died there in 1691. He trained with the Leiden painter Gerrit Dou, the master of the fijnschilders, or fine painters, who built up their small pictures from tiny, almost invisible touches. Slingelandt painted domestic scenes, kitchens, tradespeople and portraits, polished to an enamel finish and made slowly, so that his output was small. He belonged to the Leiden painters' guild, and his chief patron and collector is said to have been the cloth manufacturer Pieter de la Court van der Voort, who bought many of his pictures. Collectors in the century after his death valued his work for its finish. The Cleveland Portrait of a Man Holding a Watch dates from about 1665 to 1670.","pietro_bardellino":"Pietro Bardellino was born in Naples in 1728 and died there in 1810. He studied with Francesco de Mura, the leading painter of the Neapolitan late Baroque, and worked chiefly in Naples as a painter of altarpieces and frescoes for churches and for the Bourbon court of Ferdinand IV. His largest commission was the ceiling of the great hall of the Palazzo dei Regi Studi, the university building that is now the National Archaeological Museum, where in the early 1780s he painted the Apotheosis of Ferdinand IV and Maria Carolina, King and Queen of Naples. The Cleveland sketch, dated about 1781, is a study for that scheme, an allegory glorifying the sovereigns, and shows how a Neapolitan painter planned a ceiling in oil before working it in fresco.","pietro_del_donzello":"Pietro del Donzello, sometimes called Pietro Donzelli, lived from 1452 to 1509 according to the museum. He is usually described as a Florentine painter who also worked in Naples, and his brother Polito, or Ippolito, was a painter too, but the surviving documents are thin and the works firmly given to him are few. The Cleveland Virgin and Child with John the Baptist and Angels, dated about 1500, is a devotional picture of a kind produced in quantity for Florentine homes and chapels in the late fifteenth century, showing the Madonna with the infant Baptist and attendant angels. Little else can be said with confidence about his training, patrons or career, and he is a minor figure beside the great Florentine workshops of his day.","portuguese_15th_century":"Portuguese 15th Century is a label, not a painter. The National Gallery of Art uses it for four panels of about 1475 to 1500 showing Saint Dionysius, Saint Sebastian, Saint Barbara and Saint Lawrence, whose painter has not been named and which may have belonged to a larger altarpiece. Painting in Portugal in the later fifteenth century drew on the art of Flanders, with which Lisbon traded closely, and its best-known master, Nuno Gonçalves, court painter to Afonso V, painted the Saint Vincent panels about 1470. Portuguese altarpieces of this period often show rows of saints in rich robes against plain or gilded grounds. No name, dates, teacher or patron can be given for these four panels, and they can be placed only by their style.","prior_hamblin_school":"Prior-Hamblin School is a label for a kind of American folk portraiture, named after William Matthew Prior, who was born in Bath, Maine, in 1806 and died in 1873, and his brother-in-law Sturtevant J. Hamblen. Prior worked in Maine and later in Boston and was known for quick, inexpensive likenesses; he advertised flat portraits, painted without shadows, at a lower price than shaded ones. Hamblen worked in his circle and in New England towns, and their pictures of stiff, bright-faced sitters in plain clothes, with flat colour and sharp outlines, are hard to tell apart. The three pictures of about 1845 shown here, a Husband, a Daughter and a Little Girl with Slate, were affordable to farming and trading families who could not pay a city portraitist.","q20127403":"William Lewis was born in 1788 and is recorded as living after 1838, and beyond those dates little is known of him. His work in this collection is three portraits at the Metropolitan Museum of Art: Louisa W. Dixon and Lazarus M. Goldsmith, both dated 1833, and a Portrait of a Gentleman from about 1835. Two named sitters show that these were commissioned likenesses, the everyday trade of a portrait painter in the years before photography, when a family of some means would pay for a painted portrait to record its members. Where Lewis trained, whom he worked beside, what he charged and where he lived are not recorded here, and nothing further is claimed about him.","q20174687":"Richard William Hubbard was an American landscape painter, born in 1816 and dead in 1888. He worked in the middle of the nineteenth century, when views of mountains, lakes and river valleys were among the most popular pictures in the United States, bought by merchants and shown each year at exhibitions in New York. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds Morning on the Mountain, dated 1856, a title that promises the early light and open distance on which such painters built their effects. Little is recorded in the sources at hand about his schooling, his teachers or his patrons, and his name is now known chiefly from a few works in public collections. No fuller life is offered here than the record supports.","q20496807":"Nicholas Biddle Kittell, born in 1822 and dead in 1894, was an American portrait painter. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns his portrait of Charles Henry Augustus Carter, painted about 1845, when the artist was in his early twenties. Painters of his generation made their living from commissioned likenesses of local families, and by the 1840s they were beginning to compete with the daguerreotype, which could produce a portrait in minutes for a small sum. The sources available here do not say where Kittell trained, whom he studied under or how large his practice grew, so this account stops at his dates, his nationality and the single picture, and does not guess at the rest.","q206697":"Santi di Tito was born in 1536 at Sansepolcro and trained in Florence, where he is recorded as a pupil of Agnolo Bronzino and Baccio Bandinelli. He spent some years in Rome before settling again in Florence, and by the 1570s he was one of its busiest painters, working for churches, convents and the Medici court, including the Palazzo Vecchio. Against the crowded, artificial late Mannerist manner of the day he offered clear compositions, solid figures, natural light and quiet feeling, a style that suited the reforming temper of the Counter-Reformation. Ludovico Cigoli was among his pupils, and later writers placed him at the start of the Florentine Baroque. The Madonna and Child with the Infant Saint John at the Metropolitan Museum dates from the early 1570s. He died in Florence in 1603.","q2076864":"Henri Moret was born in Cherbourg in 1856 and died in Paris in 1913. He trained in Paris, but his career belongs to Brittany, where he went in the late 1880s and worked for years on the rocky, windy coast of Finistère and Morbihan, at Pont-Aven, Doëlan, Raguenez and elsewhere. There he knew Paul Gauguin and the painters of the Pont-Aven group, and his pictures join Gauguin's firm outlines and flattened shapes to the lighter, broken colour of the Impressionists. His subjects were sea, cliff, island and fishing village, painted in clear blues, greens and golds. The Island of Raguenez, Brittany, of about 1890 to 1895, at the National Gallery of Art, is typical of this work. He showed in Paris at the Salon and with dealers, and is now counted among the principal painters of the Breton coast.","q207929":"Pontormo, whose real name was Jacopo Carucci, was born in 1494 at Pontorme near Empoli and died in Florence in 1557. Vasari says he studied with several leading Florentines, among them Leonardo, Piero di Cosimo and Andrea del Sarto, and by the 1510s he was a master in his own right. His most famous work is the Deposition in the Capponi Chapel of Santa Felicita, painted in the later 1520s, with pale pink and blue figures floating in an airless space. He worked for the Medici at their villa at Poggio a Caiano and later at San Lorenzo, where his frescoes were lost. His pupil Bronzino carried on his manner. The portrait of Monsignor della Casa at the National Gallery of Art dates from about 1541 to 1544. His diary of 1554 to 1556 survives, and he was little regarded until the twentieth century.","q20822488":"William P. Chappel, who lived from 1801 to 1878, was an American painter best known for a series of small pictures made late in life, in the 1870s, which recall the New York of earlier decades. Many of them are at the Metropolitan Museum of Art and show the street trades and public life of the early nineteenth-century city: a boot black, a buttermilk pedlar, chimney sweeps, a city watchman and the dog killer, as well as a baptism, a funeral procession, a bathing party, a tavern, a ship yard and the fighting of a fire. They are remembered scenes rather than studies from life, and their interest lies in details of dress, work and custom that written histories seldom recorded. Little is recorded here of his early training, and his reputation rests almost wholly on this late series of reminiscences.","q20823779":"Nathaniel Rogers, born in 1788 and dead in 1844, was an American portrait painter, best known for miniatures, who worked in New York in the early nineteenth century. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds a dozen of his portraits, dated from 1813 to the mid-1830s, many of them of sitters whose names were later lost and are now called simply Portrait of a Gentleman or Portrait of a Lady. The named ones show the level of society that employed him: Mrs. Stephen Van Rensselaer III, who was Cornelia Paterson and the wife of the great Albany landowner known as the patroon, sat to him about 1825. Miniatures like these were kept in cases, worn as jewellery or sent to distant relatives, and the demand for them faded after the daguerreotype arrived around 1840. Little else of his life is recorded here.","q20830848":"Robert Charles Dudley, born in 1826 and dead in 1909, was an English painter remembered for one episode, the laying of the first lasting telegraph cable across the Atlantic. He sailed in the Great Eastern, the huge steamship chosen to carry the cable, and made drawings and paintings of the expeditions of 1865 and 1866. The six pictures at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, all about 1866, show the shore end landed, the splice made, the cable brought ashore at Newfoundland, the grappling for the cable lost in 1865, and the Great Eastern homeward bound. They were meant to be reproduced, and colour lithographs after his work were published in London soon afterwards to satisfy the public's interest in the enterprise. His reputation rests on this record of a famous feat.","q20853358":"Thomas Hewes Hinckley was an American painter of animals and sporting scenes, born in 1813 and dead in 1896. He worked in the middle of the nineteenth century, when English animal painting by Edwin Landseer and others was widely admired and prosperous buyers on both sides of the Atlantic liked pictures of dogs, cattle and game. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two of his works, The Rabbit Hunters of 1850 and Rats amongst the Barley Sheaves of 1851, whose titles point to country sport and the farmer's war on pests. Little is recorded here about his teachers or his patrons. He is remembered today mainly among students of American animal painting, and nothing more is claimed about his life than the record supports.","q20855654":"Walter Robertson was born in Ireland about 1750 and died in 1802. He was a miniature painter, and in 1793 he crossed to America in the company of the portraitist Gilbert Stuart, who like him had worked in Dublin. In the 1790s he painted small portraits on ivory for sitters in Philadelphia and other cities of the eastern seaboard, and Washington is said to have sat to him in 1794. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds four of his miniatures, among them Mrs. Gabriel Manigault, who was Margaret Izard, a member of a leading South Carolina family, and the young Augustus Vallette Van Horne, Jr. Miniatures were exchanged between families and sweethearts and were popular because they were portable and inexpensive. His American career was short, and he died in 1802.","q20859501":"Robert Peckham, born in 1785 and dead in 1877, was an American portrait painter of rural New England who lived to about ninety. He painted in the manner of the country limners, with firm outlines, flat bright colour and plain backgrounds, and his sitters were the families of the towns where he worked. The National Gallery of Art owns The Hobby Horse, about 1840, and the Metropolitan Museum of Art has The Raymond Children, about 1838, both pictures of children, who were favourite subjects for painters of his kind. Likenesses of this sort, made for families who wanted a lasting record and could not afford a city painter, were long overlooked and were then admired by twentieth-century collectors for their directness. Of his training and prices little is recorded here.","q20879083":"Robert Street was an American portrait painter, born in 1796 and dead in 1865. The National Gallery of Art holds two of his portraits, both dated 1834: George Washington Deal and Elizabeth Price Thomas. The first sitter's name is a reminder of how often American parents of the early republic named their sons for the first president. Portraits like these were commissioned by families who wanted a likeness for the parlour, and a painter who could catch a good one could find work in towns that had no resident academy. The sources at hand do not say where Street was born, whom he studied with or what he charged, and no details are supplied here that the record does not support.","q2090482":"Alfred Jacob Miller was born in Baltimore in 1810 and died there in 1874. He studied with Thomas Sully in Philadelphia, worked in Paris and Rome in the early 1830s, and was in New Orleans in 1837 when the Scottish adventurer Sir William Drummond Stewart hired him to accompany an expedition to the Rocky Mountains. Miller was one of the first trained artists to see the fur-trade West, and he made quick watercolour sketches of Native Americans, trappers and the annual trading rendezvous on the Green River. From them he later painted large oils for Stewart's Scottish castle, Murthly, and hundreds of watercolours for the Baltimore collector William T. Walters. The National Gallery of Art's Election Scene, Catonsville, Baltimore County, of about 1860, shows another side of him, the local life near his home.","q2093300":"Pierre Charles Trémolières was born at Cholet, in Anjou, in 1703 and died in Paris in 1739, aged only thirty-six. He painted religious subjects, mythologies and allegories in the Rococo manner, with graceful figures, warm colour and an easy, decorative touch. His early death cut short a promising career and left a small body of work. Comedy, about 1736, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, is an allegory of the dramatic art, the kind of figure subject that French painters of his generation produced for decorative schemes and for collectors in Paris. The sources at hand give few details of his teachers or patrons, and no more is claimed here than his dates, his birthplace and the character of his work.","q20936665":"Asahel Powers was born in 1813 and died in 1843, so his working life lasted little more than a dozen years. He was an itinerant portrait painter, active in Vermont and neighbouring states, who travelled from town to town painting local families in a plain, bold style: strong outline, firm flat colour and an unfussy record of dress and features. The National Gallery of Art holds three of his portraits, two of about 1831, when he was only eighteen, and Hannah Fisher Stedman of 1833. Two of the sitters are catalogued as possibly William Sheldon and possibly Mrs. William Sheldon, which shows how names can slip away from even a signed family portrait. Powers is now prized by collectors of American folk portraits, and his life is known mainly through the pictures themselves.","q20937561":"Dana Smith is recorded with life dates of 1805 to 1901, which would give him ninety-six years; the dates are given as the museum has them and cannot be checked here. His one picture in this collection, Southern Resort Town, was painted about 1880 and is now at the National Gallery of Art, so the painter would have been in his seventies. The title points to a holiday town in the American South, and the date places it in the years after the Civil War, when railways and steamers had made winter and spring resorts in the South popular with Northern visitors, and pictures of them found buyers. Nothing reliable is recorded here about where he trained, where he lived or whom he painted for, and no more is claimed.","q20981242":"Moses B. Russell was born about 1810 and died in 1884. He worked as a portrait painter in the 1830s, and the Metropolitan Museum of Art holds nine portraits under his name: three dated 1834 or about that year, and six undated, most of them paired gentlemen and ladies whose names have been lost. The museum also owns The Starbird Children of 1841 by Mrs. Moses B. Russell, who was Clarissa Peters and his wife, a painter in her own right, so the two make a household of artists, a thing less common then than now. The sources available here do not say where Russell trained or what he charged, and no further biography is offered than the dates and the pictures support.","q21004139":"Philippe Pavy, born in 1860 and dead in 1891, was a French painter who died young, at about thirty, and left too little to show how his work might have developed. He is classed among the Orientalists, the painters who took North Africa and the Near East as their subject, and the single picture at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, dated 1886, shows a girl in a courtyard. It was long catalogued as a courtyard in Tangier and has since been identified as a scene in Algiers, which shows how hard it can be to fix such places from the picture alone. Orientalist painting enjoyed a large market in France in the 1880s, and the Salon gave artists of this kind a public. Of his training little is recorded here.","q21010227":"Joseph Goodhue Chandler was an American portrait painter, born in 1813 and dead in 1884. He began as a travelling painter in New England, where such artists went from town to town painting local families, and his best-known picture, Girl with Kitten, of about 1836 to 1838, is now at the National Gallery of Art and is much reproduced as an example of the plain, bright, finely observed style of American folk portraiture. The National Gallery also holds his Charles H. Sisson of 1850, made when the daguerreotype was already taking away much of the trade in cheap likenesses. Chandler's reputation has grown since the twentieth century, when collectors began to value such work. The sources at hand give little detail of his training or of the later part of his career.","q21071029":"Philippe Abraham Peticolas, born in 1760 and dead in 1841, was a French painter who made his career in the United States and is associated with Richmond, Virginia. He is recorded chiefly as a miniature painter, the maker of small portraits meant to be held in the hand, kept in a case or worn as a keepsake. The two pictures at the Metropolitan Museum of Art are Portrait of a Girl, dated 1797, and Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1798, both made when he was in his late thirties. The sitters have not been identified, which is common with such small works once they leave the families that owned them. Little else about his training or his patrons is recorded here, and no more is claimed.","q21083963":"Mikhail Ivanovich Terebenev was a Russian artist, born in 1795 and dead in 1866. His only picture in this collection is the Portrait of a Woman, painted about 1830 and now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and the sitter is not named. In the Russia of those years portraiture was dominated by Orest Kiprensky, who died in 1836, and by Karl Bryullov, whose lively, romantic likenesses of St Petersburg society set the standard that other portrait painters were measured against. Nothing in the sources at hand describes Terebenev's training, his teachers or his patrons, and this account does not guess at them. It records only his dates, his nationality and the single dated picture, and it leaves the rest to specialists in Russian art.","q2123501":"Quirijn van Brekelenkam was born in the early 1620s near Zwammerdam in Holland and worked chiefly in Leiden, where he joined the painters' guild in 1648; he died about 1669. He painted small, quiet scenes of domestic and working life, among them tailors' workshops, kitchens, women spinning and old people at their meals, in the smooth, careful manner of the Leiden school that Gerrit Dou had founded. His colours are soft and his rooms plain, and the effect is of ordinary life observed with patience. The Spinner of 1653 and Sentimental Conversation of the early 1660s are both at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The buyers of such pictures were prosperous townsmen. His birth and death dates are uncertain, and little is recorded of his training, so this account keeps to what his pictures and the guild records show.","q2126155":"Willem Wissing was born in Amsterdam in 1656 and died in 1687, at only thirty. He studied at The Hague with Willem Doudijns and in 1676 went to England, where he worked in the studio of Sir Peter Lely until Lely's death in 1680, then became a fashionable portrait painter at the court of Charles II and James II. In 1685 James II sent him to The Hague to paint his daughter Mary, Princess of Orange. Wissing's smooth, elegant, rather pale style followed Lely's. He died at Burghley House, near Stamford, where he was working for the Earl of Exeter, and it was said that he was poisoned out of jealousy, though the story was never proved. The Portrait of a Woman, about 1687, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art belongs to the last year of his life.","q21289491":"The Master of Varlungo is the name given to an unknown Florentine painter of the later thirteenth or earlier fourteenth century, who takes his name from Varlungo, a place on the Arno just east of Florence. He is known only through works grouped by style, not through any document, and no personal name, birthplace or dates have come down. He painted devotional panels of the Madonna and Child on gold grounds in the formal, hieratic manner of Florence in the generation of Cimabue and Giotto, for churches and religious houses. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns a Madonna and Child Enthroned with Angels attributed to him. Because the name is a convenience of scholars, nothing more than this can safely be said, and no biography is offered.","q21295011":"Charles S. Raleigh was born in 1831 and died in 1925, and is recorded as an American painter. The only picture of his in this collection is Law of the Wild, dated 1881, now at the National Gallery of Art. A title of that kind belongs to the animal and adventure subjects that found buyers in the 1880s, when large paintings of hunters, wildlife and struggles in nature hung in homes, hotels and gentlemen's clubs, and when a painter could make a living by supplying such pictures. The sources at hand do not describe the painting in any detail, and they give nothing reliable about where Raleigh trained, whom he studied with or where he lived, so this account records his dates, his nationality and the dated work, and goes no further.","q21403085":"The Master of the Franciscan Crucifixes is a convenient name for an anonymous Italian painter who worked in the 1260s and 1270s and takes his name from a group of painted crosses made for Franciscan churches. Such crosses, hung high in a church above the altar or the screen, showed the dying Christ in the formal, almost decorative style then used in central Italy, with the Virgin and Saint John at the ends of the cross arms in attitudes of grief. The two panels at the National Gallery of Art, The Mourning Madonna and The Mourning Saint John the Evangelist, of about 1270 to 1275, come from a cross of this kind. Nothing is known of the painter's real name, birthplace or workshop, and the name is a scholar's label.","q21403093":"This painter is known only by an invented name, the Master of the Death of Saint Nicholas of Münster, and is described as German, active in the later fifteenth century and linked by his name to the Westphalian city of Münster. Scholars give such names to painters whose hand can be recognised across several panels but whose own names were never recorded; here the name comes from a painting of the death of the saint. His Calvary at the National Gallery of Art, of about 1470 to 1480, shows the Crucifixion, the commonest subject of devotional painting in northern Europe at that date, made for churches, chapels and private prayer. The broad range of 1410 to 1520 in some records is an estimate, not a documented career, and nothing certain is known of his life, training or patrons.","q21403095":"The Master of the Starck Triptych is an anonymous German painter of the later fifteenth century, known by a scholar's name taken from an altarpiece of the kind now at the National Gallery of Art. The three panels there show the Raising of the Cross at the centre, the moment when the cross bearing Christ is hoisted upright, and on the outsides of the wings, visible when the triptych was closed, the standing Saints Barbara and Catherine, dated about 1480 to 1490. A triptych of this kind could be shut for ordinary days and opened on feast days, so that the saints were seen first and the Passion revealed afterwards. Nothing is recorded of the painter's real name, birthplace, workshop or patrons, and the broad dates 1415 to 1540 in some records are only a guess.","q21403118":"Abram Ross Stanley was an American portrait painter, born in 1816 and dead in 1875. The National Gallery of Art holds two of his portraits, Eliza Wells of 1840 and Joshua Lamb of 1842, the likenesses of named sitters made in the first years after Daguerre's process was announced in 1839, when the daguerreotype was spreading quickly through American towns and beginning to take the trade in cheap portraits from painters. A painted likeness remained a larger and more costly object, with colour and a place in the parlour, and painters of Stanley's kind found customers in families who preferred it. The sources at hand give little detail of his teachers or his career, and this account does not guess at them.","q21403122":"John Wesley Paradise was an American portrait painter, born in 1809 and dead in 1862. His portrait of Elizabeth Oakes Prince Smith, painted about 1845 and now at the National Gallery of Art, shows a sitter better known than her painter. She was married to Seba Smith, the humorist who created the Major Jack Downing letters, and she was herself a poet, novelist and lecturer who in the 1850s spoke for women's rights; in the 1840s the couple lived in New York, where she was part of the literary world. Portrait painters of Paradise's generation drew much of their trade from authors, ministers, merchants and their families. The sources at hand give little detail of his training or his career, and this account does not guess at them.","q21403229":"Charles David was a French painter, born in 1797 and dead in 1869, and not to be confused with the better known Jacques-Louis David. The only picture of his in this collection is Portrait of a Young Horsewoman, dated 1839 and now at the National Gallery of Art. Pictures of women on horseback were a fashionable subject in France in the 1830s, when riding in the Bois de Boulogne and in the countryside was a part of polite life and the riding habit, with its long skirt and tall hat, was a mark of elegance. The sitter's name has not come down. The sources at hand do not record his training, his teachers or his patrons, so nothing beyond his dates and this dated picture is claimed.","q21408926":"Frederick W. Mayhew, born in 1785 and dead in 1854, was an American portrait painter. The National Gallery of Art holds two of his portraits, John Harrisson and Mrs. John Harrisson and Daughter, both about 1823. The pair were evidently painted as companions, the husband alone and the wife with a child, a common arrangement in family portraiture of the early republic, when a merchant or tradesman who had prospered wanted a record of his household. Likenesses of this sort were usually painted from life in a few sittings and were often made by travelling painters who moved from town to town. The sources at hand give little about where Mayhew trained or lived, and no more is claimed here than his dates and the two portraits.","q21409329":"Isaac Sheffield was an American portrait painter, born in 1807 and dead in 1845, so that his career lasted only some fifteen years. The National Gallery of Art holds two portraits of 1833, Connecticut Sea Captain and Connecticut Sea Captain's Wife, which are plainly companion pictures, and their titles place his sitters among the shipmasters of that state, where the coastal and overseas trade made men of the sea prosperous and keen to have their families painted. The names of the sitters have not been preserved with the pictures. Portraits of this kind, direct and simply modelled, are now prized as American folk art. The sources at hand record little of his teachers or his working life, and nothing further is claimed here.","q21409330":"J. C. Robinson is known here only by his initials and two pictures, Portrait of an Old Man and Portrait of an Old Lady, both dated 1848 and both at the National Gallery of Art. The titles show that the sitters' names were lost, and the pictures appear to be a pair. Some records give his dates as 1768 to 1908, a span of 140 years that cannot belong to one man and seems only to mark the wide uncertainty of the record. Painted records of elderly husbands and wives were a staple of American portraiture in the middle of the nineteenth century, when the daguerreotype was spreading but many families still preferred a painted likeness. Nothing certain is recorded of Robinson's life, training or patrons, and no further biography is offered.","q21409331":"Linton Park was an American painter, born in 1826 and dead in 1906, who is remembered above all for Flax Scutching Bee, dated 1885 and now at the National Gallery of Art. Scutching is the beating of dried flax stalks to free the fibre from the woody core, and a bee is a neighbourly gathering at which people shared a hard task and then enjoyed company; the picture looks back to an older farm custom. The National Gallery also holds his The Exhumation, of about 1890. Painters of Park's kind often worked from memory, and their pictures are valued as records of daily life as much as for their style. The sources at hand give little about his training, and no more is claimed here.","q21409336":"Royall Brewster Smith was an American portrait painter, born in 1801 and dead in 1855. The National Gallery of Art holds two of his portraits, John G. Read and Eliza R. Read, both dated 1833. The matching surname and date suggest a married couple painted as a pair, in the manner of the time, when a husband and wife were often shown in separate panels of the same size to hang side by side. Portraits of this kind, painted for families in towns that had no resident painters, are now valued as American folk art and as records of the clothes, jewellery and manners of the day. The sources at hand do not say where Smith trained or what he charged, and no further details are offered.","q2144813":"René Princeteau was born at Libourne, near Bordeaux, in 1843 and died in 1914. He was deaf from childhood and painted horses, hunts, racecourses and riders, subjects from the sporting world that his friends, Lautrec's father among them, knew well. He is best remembered today as the first teacher of Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec: the boy's father, Count Alphonse de Toulouse-Lautrec, was an old friend, and Princeteau taught the teenager in his Paris studio in 1881 and 1882 before Lautrec moved to the studio of Léon Bonnat. Lautrec's early pictures of horses owe much to him. Riders on the Beach at Dieppe, of 1892, at the National Gallery of Art, shows riders on the sands of the fashionable Channel resort. He is now remembered chiefly through his famous pupil.","q21486266":"L. de Vuillemin is known here by a single picture, Portrait of a Young Woman, dated 1849 and now at the National Gallery of Art. The museum gives his nationality as French with a question mark, and some records give dates of 1780 to 1899, a span that cannot belong to one life and only marks how little is established. The year 1849 followed the revolutions of 1848 in France, and the sitter, who is not named, would have been painted in an age when a painted portrait was still the usual way for a family to record a daughter or wife. Nothing certain is recorded of the painter's first name, birthplace, training or patrons, and no further biography is offered.","q21497624":"The Sherman Limner is the name given to an unknown American portrait painter of the later eighteenth century. A limner was the old word for a painter of likenesses, and the term is used for the often itinerant artists who worked in the colonies and the early republic without leaving their names. The National Gallery of Art holds two of his pictures, Portrait of a Man in Red and Portrait of a Lady in Red, both about 1785 to 1790, which appear to be companion portraits, perhaps of a married couple. Some records give his dates as 1710 to 1845, a range that cannot belong to one life and only reflects how little is known. The sitters are not named, and nothing certain is recorded of the painter's own name, birthplace or training.","q21497646":"A. M. Randall is known here by a single painting, Basket of Fruit with Parrot, dated 1777 and now at the National Gallery of Art. If the date is read correctly it is early for an American still life, a kind of picture that became widespread only after 1800, when painters such as the Peale family made heaped fruit and tabletop arrangements popular. A parrot among the fruit adds a touch of the exotic, as it did in many European still lifes, where birds and fruit were painted to show abundance and the wealth of trade. Some records give the painter's dates as 1697 to 1837, a range too wide to belong to one life. Nothing certain is recorded of Randall's first name, training or patrons, and nothing more is claimed.","q21497656":"Amzi Emmons Zeliff was an American painter, born in 1831 and dead in 1915, whose long life spanned the whole second half of the nineteenth century. His one picture in this collection is The Barnyard, dated only to the late nineteenth century and now at the National Gallery of Art. Farmyard scenes, with fowl, cattle and the buildings of a farm, were a favourite subject then, spread by cheap colour prints as well as by painters, and country people who wished to see their own way of life on a wall were their natural buyers. Such pictures are now collected as American folk art. The sources at hand record little of Zeliff's training, career or patrons, and no more is claimed here than his dates and the one painting.","q21497699":"C. F. Senior is known here only by his initials, his surname and one painting, The Sportsman's Dream, dated 1881 or after and now at the National Gallery of Art. The wording of the date means that 1881 is the earliest year in which the picture can have been painted. Hunting and shooting subjects were among the most popular in American popular painting and colour printing in the later nineteenth century, when game birds, dogs and guns hung in country houses, taverns and clubs, and a title like this one points to the reverie of a hunter. Some records give dates of 1801 to 1941, a range far too wide for one life. Nothing certain is recorded of Senior's first name, training or patrons, and no more is claimed.","q21497728":"D. G. Stouter is known here by his initials and surname and a single painting, On Point, dated 1854 or after and now at the National Gallery of Art. The wording of the date means that 1854 is the earliest year in which the picture can have been painted. In the language of field sport, a bird dog is on point when it stands rigid, nose and tail in a line, to show its master where hidden game lies, and pictures of pointers and setters at work were much liked by American sportsmen in the middle of the nineteenth century. Some records give dates of 1774 to 1914, a range far too wide for one life. Nothing certain is recorded about Stouter's training, birthplace or patrons, and no more is claimed.","q21497852":"George M. Miller is given by the National Gallery of Art as a Scottish painter, with dates of 1739 to 1819. His one picture in this collection is a portrait of William Henry Vining, painted about 1810, when the painter would have been about seventy if the dates are right. The sitter's name has been kept with the picture, which is not always so with portraits of the period. The sources at hand do not say where Miller was born, where he trained, whether he came to America or whom he painted for. No more is claimed here than his dates, the nationality given and the one portrait.","q21497855":"George Ropes was an American painter, born in 1788 and dead in 1819, who lived and worked in Salem, Massachusetts, a seaport then rich from its overseas trade. He was deaf, and he died young, at about thirty. The National Gallery of Art holds his Mount Vernon, painted in 1806 when he was eighteen. Washington had died in 1799, and the house on the Potomac had already become a place of pilgrimage, with views of it in demand as prints and paintings. Ropes painted landscapes and views, and his short career left a small body of work. The sources at hand give little detail of his teachers, and no more is claimed here than his dates and his place of work.","q21498010":"John Toole was an American painter, born in 1815 and dead in 1860. His one picture in this collection is Skating Scene, painted about 1835, when he was about twenty, and now at the National Gallery of Art. Winter scenes with skaters were a lively subject, popular in Dutch painting two centuries earlier and taken up again by American and English artists of the nineteenth century, who saw in a frozen pond a chance to paint a crowd at play, in motion, in their best winter clothes. The sources at hand do not say where Toole was born, whom he studied with or whom he painted for, and no more is claimed here than his dates, the nationality given and the one dated painting.","q21498140":"Redpath is known here by a single name and a single painting, Mounting of the Guard, dated to the middle of the nineteenth century and now at the National Gallery of Art. Mounting the guard is the formal ceremony in which a fresh detachment of soldiers takes over a post from the old one, a parade that drew crowds in garrison towns and was a favourite subject of military prints and popular pictures. The painter is given as American, and some records offer dates of 1770 to 1910, a range far too wide to belong to one life. No first name, birthplace, training or patron is recorded, and the surname may be a signature or a later attribution, so nothing more is claimed here.","q21498275":"Wagguno is the single name under which the National Gallery of Art records the painter of Fruit and Baltimore Oriole, dated 1858. The name looks like a signature or a nickname, and nothing reliable is recorded of the person behind it; some records give dates of 1778 to 1918, a range too wide to belong to one life. The bird is named for the orange and black colours of the coat of arms of Lord Baltimore, the proprietor of colonial Maryland, and it was a favourite among American painters from Audubon's time on. Pictures of fruit and a bird belonged to a decorative tradition of still life. Nothing more is claimed here about the painter's life, training or patrons.","q21498280":"William Stearns is known here by a single painting, Bowl of Fruit, dated about 1830 to 1840 and now at the National Gallery of Art. A bowl of fruit was one of the favourite subjects of the time, painted by trained still-life artists such as the Peale family of Philadelphia and by many amateur and folk painters for whom the arrangement of apples, pears and grapes was a pleasant exercise in colour and design. The painter is given as American, but some records show dates of 1760 to 1900, a range far too wide to belong to one life, and nothing certain is known of his training, his place of work or his patrons. No more is claimed here than the single dated picture.","q21498504":"Thomas Wilcocks Sully was born in 1811 and died in 1847, the son of Thomas Sully, the most fashionable portrait painter of Philadelphia, from whom he learned the trade in the family studio. The Sully household was a nursery of painters, for several of his sisters also took up the brush. His portrait of Major Thomas Biddle at the National Gallery of Art is dated 1832, when he was about twenty-one, and the Metropolitan Museum of Art has a George Washington of 1840, the kind of picture that Americans of the period commissioned or bought in large numbers, usually based on Gilbert Stuart's well-known likeness. He lived only thirty-six years, and his work has always been overshadowed by his father's.","q21498577":"Louis Vallée is given as a Dutch painter with life dates of 1573 to 1653, and that is nearly all that can be said of him with confidence. His one picture in this collection, at the National Gallery of Art, is Silvio with the Wounded Dorinda, dated about 1651 with a question mark, so that the painter would have been in his late seventies. It illustrates Guarini's pastoral play Il Pastor Fido, first printed in 1590 and one of the most widely read works of its day, in which the hunter Silvio, who scorns love, wounds the nymph Dorinda by mistake and is moved to pity. Painters across Europe took scenes from the play as an excuse for landscapes with graceful figures. Where Vallée trained and whom he served is not recorded here.","q21501040":"The Osma Master is the name given to an anonymous Spanish painter, and the name points to Osma, the old Castilian cathedral town of El Burgo de Osma, in the province of Soria, with which his work is connected. His own name, birthplace and dates have not come down. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds a panel of Saint Anne Enthroned with the Virgin and Child, a subject that shows three generations of the Holy Family together, since Saint Anne was the mother of the Virgin, and one that was much loved in Spain and northern Europe at the end of the Middle Ages. The museum gives no date for the panel, and nothing is known of the painter's workshop or patrons, so no further biography is offered.","q2155480":"Pietro Testa was born at Lucca in 1612 and went to Rome as a young man, where he studied with Domenichino and for a time worked in the circle of Pietro da Cortona. He earned his living at first by drawing antiquities for the antiquarian Cassiano dal Pozzo, whose paper museum recorded hundreds of Roman statues and reliefs, and the habit of study left him a learned, classical painter and a superb draughtsman. His etchings, crowded with allegorical figures, are his best-known work, and they were more admired than his few paintings. A melancholy man, he found few patrons. He drowned in the Tiber in 1650, by accident or by his own hand, and his reputation grew only in the twentieth century. The Metropolitan's Alexander the Great Rescued from the River Cydnus dates from about the end of his life.","q2157495":"Robert Havell Jr. was born in England in 1793 and trained as an engraver and printseller in his father's London workshop. He is famous for one great job: from 1827 to 1838 he engraved and printed most of the 435 hand-coloured plates of John James Audubon's Birds of America, working in aquatint over etching, a landmark of bird illustration. In 1839 he emigrated to the United States and settled on the Hudson, and there he painted landscapes of the river valley and of New York, as in his View of the Bay and City of New York from Weehawken, painted in 1840 and now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He died at Tarrytown, New York, in 1878, and his name is still joined to Audubon's wherever the great bird book is discussed.","q216406":"Edward Burne-Jones was born in Birmingham in 1833 and went up to Oxford to study for the Church, but friendship with William Morris and a meeting with Dante Gabriel Rossetti turned him to painting, and he left without a degree in 1856. He learned chiefly from Rossetti, whom he helped with the Oxford Union murals, and he was encouraged by Ruskin. With Morris he designed stained glass, tapestries and book illustrations for Morris and Company. His paintings of knights, ladies and legends, soft in colour and dreamlike in feeling, had great success at the Grosvenor Gallery from 1877, and patrons such as the shipowner Frederick Leyland bought them. The Love Song, begun in 1868 and finished in 1877, is at the Metropolitan Museum. He was made a baronet in 1894 and died in 1898.","q21647558":"Pierre Henri was born in Paris about 1760 and died in New York in 1822. He was in the United States by 1798, the year of his portrait of Gouverneur Morris, and the Metropolitan Museum of Art holds this and a second picture, The Artist's Family, of about 1800. Morris was one of the most distinguished Americans of his time, a New Yorker who helped to draft the Constitution, wrote much of its final wording and served as minister to France during the Revolution, so Henri had a good client. The family group is a more personal piece, a painter's record of his own household. Little else is recorded here about his training in France, his journeys or his later years, and nothing more is claimed.","q21647741":"Thomas E. Barratt was born about 1814, and the date of his death is not recorded. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds one picture by him, a Portrait of a Gentleman, of about 1840, painted when he was in his mid twenties. The sitter's name has not been preserved with the portrait. Unnamed gentlemen are common in the museum's American portraits of that decade, when families commissioned likenesses in the years just before the daguerreotype began to take the trade away from painters, and many sitters lost their names when pictures passed out of family hands. The sources at hand give nothing on where Barratt trained, whom he painted for or where he worked, and nothing further is claimed here.","q21647793":"Washington Blanchard was born in 1808 and is recorded as living after 1849; the date of his death is not known. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds one portrait by him, Henry Larcom Abbot, dated 1835, when the painter was in his late twenties. The sitter is named, which is not true of many portraits of the period, but the sources at hand say nothing about the sitter's family or about how the commission came about. In the 1830s many American portrait painters moved from town to town, painting sitters in their own homes, though nothing here says that Blanchard did so. His training, his teachers, his patrons and his later career are not recorded in the material consulted, and no more is claimed.","q216873":"Pierre Puvis de Chavannes was born in Lyon in 1824 and trained briefly with Henri Scheffer and Thomas Couture before finding his own way, helped by the example of Chassériau. He turned to the large mural, painting calm, pale, simplified figures in a matt, fresco-like colour that seemed to belong to the wall. His scheme for the museum at Amiens, of which Rest and Work are smaller versions of two canvases, led to the cycle of Saint Geneviève in the Panthéon, the amphitheatre of the Sorbonne, the museum at Lyon and the Boston Public Library. The Allegory of the Sorbonne relates to the Sorbonne scheme of 1887 to 1889. Admired by Seurat, Gauguin and Denis, he was revered by the Symbolists and by the young. He died in Paris in 1898.","q21693931":"Savinien Edme Dubourjal was born in 1795 and died in 1865, and is recorded as a French painter of portrait miniatures. His one picture in this collection, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, is a portrait of Henry Clay, painted about 1845. Clay, the Kentucky statesman called the Great Compromiser, had been Speaker of the House and Secretary of State, and he was beaten for the presidency in 1844, so that he was among the most portrayed Americans of his day and a likeness of him could be sure of a market. The sources at hand do not say where Dubourjal studied, whether he met Clay or worked from another likeness, or what his reputation was in France, and nothing further is claimed.","q21693937":"Theodore E. Pine, born in 1827 and dead in 1905, was an American portrait painter who worked in the 1850s, the decade of all three of his pictures at the Metropolitan Museum of Art: Kate Lyon Cornell of 1856, The Daughters of Daniel T. MacFarlan of 1857, and Mr. and Mrs. Daniel T. MacFarlan of 1858. He was then in his late twenties and early thirties. Two pictures of one family painted in successive years show that he found repeat custom, and the portrait of the daughters together is a group commission, more demanding than a single likeness. The sources at hand give little about his training or his later career, and no more is claimed here than the dates and the pictures support.","q217648":"Isack van Ostade was baptised at Haarlem in 1621 and died there in 1649, aged only twenty-eight. He was the younger brother and pupil of Adriaen van Ostade, joined the Haarlem painters' guild in 1643, and began as a painter of peasant interiors in his brother's manner. His best work is of another kind: village scenes out of doors, in which travellers rest their horses at an inn, labourers pause by a cottage or skaters cross the ice, set under a wide, hazy sky and painted in silvery, brownish tones that owe something to the Haarlem landscape painters. The Halt at the Inn and Workmen before an Inn, both of 1645, at the National Gallery of Art, are good examples. He died at the height of his powers, and collectors have long prized his pictures for their freshness.","q21809664":"Shepard Alonzo Mount was born in 1804 and died in 1868. He belonged to a family of Long Island painters: his younger brother William Sidney Mount became the best-known American genre painter of the time, and a third brother, Henry Smith Mount, painted too. Shepard worked as a portrait painter in New York and on Long Island, in a quiet, careful manner. His portrait of Catherine Brooks Hall, about 1830, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. His reputation has always been smaller than his brother William's, and he is now remembered chiefly as a member of this family of artists. The sources at hand record little about his training or his prices, and no more is claimed here.","q21850207":"The Master of the Dinteville Allegory is the name given to an unknown painter, Netherlandish or French, from the one picture that defines him: Moses and Aaron before Pharaoh: An Allegory of the Dinteville Family, dated 1537 and now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The Dintevilles were a noble family of Champagne, and among them was Jean de Dinteville, French ambassador to England, whom Holbein painted with his friend Georges de Selve in The Ambassadors of 1533. The picture of 1537, as its title says, reads the family's story as an allegory in the Old Testament scene of Moses and Aaron before Pharaoh. The painter's own name, training and workshop are not recorded, and the title is a scholar's label.","q21851468":"Tommaso di Stefano Lunetti, called Tommaso Fiorentino, the Florentine, was born in 1495 and died in 1564, and is recorded as an Italian painter. He belonged to the Florentine school in the years when Andrea del Sarto, Pontormo and Rosso Fiorentino were changing painting there, and the Portrait of a Man at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, dated 1521, was painted when he was about twenty-six. The sitter is not named. The painter is far less well known than those famous contemporaries, and the portrait is the one work of his in this collection. Little is recorded here about his training, his teachers or his patrons, and no more is claimed than his dates and the dated portrait.","q21852458":"The Master of the Saint Godelieve Legend is the name given to an unknown Netherlandish painter, taken from a picture of the life and miracles of Saint Godelieve, now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art and not dated by the museum. Godelieve was a Flemish saint of the eleventh century who, according to her legend, was ill-treated by her husband, Bertulf of Gistel, and murdered by his order about 1070; her tomb at Gistel, near Bruges, became a place of pilgrimage, and her story was retold in cycles of painted scenes. The painter's own name, birthplace, dates, teachers and patrons are not recorded, and the title is a scholar's label, so no biography can honestly be given.","q21852728":"The Master of the Lanckoronski Annunciation is a name of convenience for an anonymous Italian painter, taken from an Annunciation once in the collection of the Lanckoronski family, Polish-Austrian collectors who formed a famous gathering of early Italian pictures in Vienna. Scholars group works by hand and style and then give the group a name, since the painter's real name was not recorded. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns a Madonna and Child attributed to him, whose date is not given. Beyond the name and the nationality, nothing is known of the painter's birthplace, dates, teachers or patrons, and no biography is offered rather than an invented one.","q21852999":"Monogrammist FA is the name given to a painter known only by the initials F and A, which appear on his work. A single picture is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art: An Artist Painting a Heraldic Shield in a Cabinet of Curiosities, dated 1664 and described as the work of a Northern European. Rooms for collecting curiosities, with shells, coins, scientific instruments, weapons and works of art, were fashionable among scholars and rich merchants in northern Europe in the seventeenth century, and the picture joins two trades, the collector's and the painter's, since heraldic shields were painted by decorative painters for families and for funerals. Who the artist was, where he worked and whom he served is not recorded in the sources at hand, and no more is claimed here.","q21853443":"The Morata Master is the name given to an anonymous Spanish painter, whose name refers to a place called Morata and who is known only through works grouped by their style. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns a panel of the Virgin and Child Enthroned with Scenes from the Life of the Virgin, the kind of altarpiece, with an enthroned Madonna surrounded by small narrative scenes, that was a staple of church painting in medieval Spain. The panel is undated in the record, and no personal name, birthplace, dates or patrons are known for the painter. Because the name is a scholar's convenience and the sources at hand add nothing, nothing more can safely be said, and no biography is offered.","q21853726":"Pieter van Overschee is recorded as a Flemish still-life painter, and the one picture of his in this collection, Still Life of Fruit and Game, is dated 1645 and hangs at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Pictures of heaped fruit and dead game were a speciality of Antwerp, where Frans Snyders and Jan Fyt made large canvases of the hunt, the market and the larder for rich merchants, churches and nobles, and smaller painters supplied a wide market with lesser versions. The sources at hand give no dates of birth or death for Overschee and say nothing of his training, his teachers or his patrons, so the date on the picture is the only firm evidence of when he worked, and no more is claimed here.","q21985529":"Harriet Cany Peale was born in 1800 and died in 1869. She became the second wife of the portrait painter Rembrandt Peale, who married her in 1840, and so belonged to the most celebrated family of painters in the early United States, begun by Charles Willson Peale, whose sons and daughters made portraits and still lifes. She painted too, and the National Gallery of Art holds her Fruit, of about 1860, painted when she was about sixty, in the tradition of the family's fruit still lifes. Fruit pieces of this kind were in demand for parlours and dining rooms, and were a respectable branch of painting for women of the period. The sources at hand give little detail of her training or her sales, and no more is claimed here.","q21993961":"Theodora W. Thayer was born in 1868 and died in 1905, at only thirty-seven. She was an American portrait painter, and the picture of hers at the Metropolitan Museum of Art is a portrait of Parke Godwin, dated 1900. Godwin, who lived from 1816 to 1904 and was therefore eighty-four when he sat to her, was a New York journalist, associate editor of the Evening Post under William Cullen Bryant, whose daughter he married, an early American follower of the French socialist Fourier, and the author of a life of Bryant. Little else is recorded here about her training, her teachers or her career, which was cut short.","q21993988":"William P. Sheys is recorded as active between 1813 and 1821, and no dates of birth or death are known. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two portraits by him, Portrait of a Lady, dated 1813, and Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1821. They are eight years apart and do not form a pair, and the sitters have lost their names, as many portraits of the early republic did when they left the families that commissioned them. The range of years in which he is recorded comes from works like these, and from little else. Where Sheys was born, whom he studied with, which towns he worked in and what he charged are not recorded in the sources at hand, and nothing further is claimed.","q21995153":"Nathaniel Hancock is recorded as a portrait painter active between 1785 and 1809, and no dates of birth or death are known. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two of his portraits, a Portrait of a Gentleman of about 1785 and Mrs. Abijah Cheever, who was Elizabeth Scott, of about 1795, so that they span the first decade of his recorded work. These were the years in which a newly independent America was building a class of merchants and professional men who wanted likenesses of themselves and their wives, and painters who could supply them found plenty of work. Where Hancock was born, whom he studied with and which towns he worked in are not recorded in the sources at hand, and nothing further is claimed here.","q21995260":"Nicolas François Dun was born in 1764 and died in 1832, and is recorded as a French portrait painter. His picture at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, painted about 1812 to 1814, is said to show Madame Récamier, who lived from 1777 to 1849. Juliette Récamier was the most admired beauty and salon hostess of Paris under Napoleon and the Restoration, and the most famous likenesses of her are by Jacques-Louis David, left unfinished in 1800, and by François Gérard, painted in 1802. The words said to be show that the identification is not certain. The sources at hand give little about Dun's training, teachers or patrons, and nothing more is claimed.","q21995263":"Oliver Frazer was born in Lexington, Kentucky, in 1808 and died in 1864. He is recorded as having studied with Matthew Harris Jouett, the leading portrait painter of the Kentucky frontier, and then with Thomas Sully in Philadelphia, and he returned to practise in his home town. His portrait of Henry Clay, of about 1840, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Clay, the statesman who was Speaker of the House and Secretary of State and who ran for the presidency three times, lived at Ashland, his estate outside Lexington. Frazer's reputation rests chiefly on portraits made in his native state, and little more is recorded here about his career.","q21995264":"Oliver Tarbell Eddy was an American portrait painter, born in 1799 and dead in 1868. The one picture of his in this collection, The Alling Children, painted about 1839 and now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, is a group portrait of siblings. Pictures of this kind, in which a prosperous family had its children painted together, often with toys, fruit or a pet to occupy them, were a staple of American portraiture in the 1830s and 1840s, and are now much admired for their directness and for the details of clothing and furniture they preserve. The sources at hand say little about where Eddy trained, which towns he worked in or whom he painted for, and no more is claimed here.","q21995339":"Thomas Gimbrede was born in France in 1781 and died in the United States in 1832, an engraver and portrait painter. He emigrated to America as a young man and worked in New York, making portrait miniatures and engraving portraits and views for books and periodicals, and in his last years he taught drawing to the cadets at the United States Military Academy at West Point, where drawing was part of the training of army engineers. His Self-portrait, of about 1805, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, was made when he was in his twenties. Little else is recorded here about his training in France or about his reputation in his own day, and no more is claimed.","q21995342":"Thomas Peat is given as a British painter who lived from 1791 to 1831. His one picture in this collection, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, is a portrait of Sir Joshua Reynolds, who lived from 1723 to 1792, with the date 1792 attached to it. Reynolds, the first president of the Royal Academy and the leading English portrait painter of his day, died in that year, and a painter born in 1791 cannot have worked from life in 1792, so the picture must be a later work, perhaps copied from an existing likeness. The sources at hand give little about Peat's life, training or patrons, and the date of the portrait may be the year of the sitter's death, not of the painting, so no more is claimed here.","q21995348":"Vincent is known here by a single name, with no first name or dates, as a French painter whose one picture in this collection is a portrait of Madame Ingouf, of about 1790, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The surname is shared by François-André Vincent, a celebrated French history painter and portraitist who lived from 1746 to 1816, and it would be natural to wonder whether he is meant, but the museum's record gives only the surname, and this account makes no identification. A portrait of about 1790 was painted in the first year or so of the Revolution, when many French sitters still posed in the fashions of the old regime. The sitter is named by her married name only, and nothing more is recorded.","q21995351":"Vincent Bertrand is recorded as a French painter, with no dates of birth or death, and the Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two pictures by him, both called Portrait of a Woman and both dated about 1810. The sitters are not named, and nothing in the record says whether the two pictures show the same woman. About 1810, under the Empire, French women's fashion favoured high waists, short sleeves and simple, classical lines, and painted portraits of ladies in such dress were produced in large numbers for the new society of the period. The sources at hand say nothing of where Bertrand was born or trained, whom he studied with or whom he painted for, and no more is claimed here.","q21995355":"William Magrath was born in 1838 and died in 1918, and is recorded as an Irish-born painter who worked in the United States. His On the Old Sod, of 1879, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, takes its title from the affectionate name that Irish emigrants gave to Ireland, and so points to a scene of Irish country life, painted for a public in America that included a large community of Irish immigrants and their descendants. In the 1870s Irish rural subjects, tender or humorous, found a ready public there, among emigrants and others. The sources at hand give little about Magrath's training or career, and no more is claimed here.","q21997236":"Rudolphe Bel is recorded as a Swiss painter, and the one picture of his in this collection, a Portrait of a Woman dated 1822, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The sitter is not named. Switzerland had a lively tradition of portrait painting in small formats, above all miniatures and enamels at Geneva, and many Swiss artists worked abroad for the courts and cities of Europe, but whether Bel belonged to that world is not recorded here. No dates of birth or death, no teachers and no patrons are known from the sources at hand, and the spelling of the first name may itself be a variant, so no biography is offered beyond the nationality and the dated portrait.","q22001875":"Pierre Laurent Canon was born in 1787 and died in 1852, and is recorded as a French painter. His picture at the Metropolitan Museum of Art is a portrait of Baron Joseph Dominique Louis, dated 1844. The sitter, who lived from 1755 to 1837, was a former priest turned financier and statesman who served several times as finance minister under the Restoration and again after the revolution of 1830. As he had been dead for seven years in 1844, the portrait cannot have been taken from life, and must have been made from an earlier likeness, a death mask or memory. The sources at hand give little about Canon's training, teachers or patrons, and no more is claimed here.","q22086808":"Thomas Story Officer was born in 1810 and died in 1859. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds one picture by him, Holy Eyes, dated 1848, when he was about thirty-eight. The title does not say whether the picture is a portrait, a religious scene or a genre subject, and the sources at hand do not describe it further. The title, with its devotional tone, could belong to any of several kinds of picture popular in the 1840s, from sentimental portraits of children to religious subjects, and the date is the only firm fact about the work. Where Officer was born, whom he studied with, where he worked and whom he painted for are not recorded in the material consulted, and no more is claimed here than his dates and the one dated work.","q22094516":"Villers is known here by a surname alone, as a French painter, with no first name or dates. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two portraits by him: Portrait of a Woman, of about 1790, and A Man with the Initials JD, dated 1790. The second title shows that the sitter is identified only by the letters JD, which probably appear on the picture, so that his name has been lost. In 1790 France was in the second year of the Revolution, and portraits of that date still show the dress and poses of the last years of the old regime. The sources at hand say nothing of where Villers was born or trained or whom he painted for, and no more is claimed.","q22094527":"Mortier is known here by a surname alone, as a French painter, and the dates of 1790 to 1800 given in some records seem to mark when he worked, not his life. His one picture in this collection, A Hunter with a Dog, dated 1794 to 1795, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Hunting had been a mark of noble privilege in France until the Revolution abolished seigneurial hunting rights in 1789, after which any landowner might shoot, so that a portrait of a hunter and his dog in the mid-1790s could show a citizen rather than a noble, though nothing in the sources at hand says what the sitter or the painter intended. No first name, birthplace, training or patron is recorded, and no more is claimed.","q22094606":"Monogrammist JG is the name given to a painter known only by the initials J and G, which appear on his work, and he is described as Northern European. The one picture of his in this collection, Traveling Players, of about 1630, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Strolling actors, in the commedia dell'arte tradition of masks and stock characters, were a popular subject in the early seventeenth century, when Jacques Callot's etchings of comic players spread the type across Europe, and painters in the Netherlands and elsewhere made pictures of troupes performing on temporary stages in the streets. Who the painter was, where he worked and whom he served is not recorded in the sources at hand, and no more is claimed here.","q221043":"Simone Martini was born in Siena about 1284 and is thought to have trained in the circle of Duccio. His first great work, the Maestà in the Palazzo Pubblico in Siena, was painted in 1315 and reworked in 1321, and its linear grace, rich colour and gold set a standard for Sienese painting. He worked for Robert of Anjou at Naples, painted the frescoes of Saint Martin at Assisi, and in 1333 signed with his brother-in-law Lippo Memmi an Annunciation for Siena cathedral, now in the Uffizi. About 1336 he moved to Avignon, where the papal court had settled, and there he became a friend of Petrarch and painted a portrait of Laura, now lost, that the poet praised in two sonnets. The panels at the National Gallery of Art and the Metropolitan Museum come from altarpieces later taken apart. He died at Avignon in 1344.","q22276295":"William Parsons Winchester Dana was born in 1833 and died in 1927, and is generally described as an American painter who spent much of his career in Europe, chiefly in Paris, and who became known above all for pictures of the sea. His Heart's Ease, dated 1863, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, was painted when he was about thirty. Heart's-ease is an old English name for the wild pansy, a flower long associated with peace of mind and with love, and the name was often borrowed for sentimental pictures in the nineteenth century. The sources at hand give little about his training, his teachers or his patrons, and the museum's picture is a single early work, so no more is claimed here than his dates and the dated picture.","q22276313":"William Franks is recorded as active between 1795 and 1798, and no dates of birth or death are known. The one picture of his in this collection is a Portrait of a Gentleman, of about 1795, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The sitter's name has been lost, as with many portraits of the period, and the three years of his recorded activity may be drawn from works like this one. In the 1790s, when portraits of gentlemen were commissioned by merchants, officers and professional men, a painter's name could be remembered only through a signature or a family tradition, and a short career easily left little trace. Where Franks was born, trained or worked is not recorded in the sources at hand, and nothing further is claimed.","q22276320":"Vincent G. Stiepevich was born in 1841 and is recorded as living after 1910; the date of his death is not known. His one picture in this collection is The Parthenon, dated 1880 and now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. By 1880 the Parthenon had long been the best-known monument of classical Athens: after Greek independence in the 1830s the Acropolis had been cleared of its later buildings, and travellers, artists and archaeologists came in numbers to see it, so views of it found a ready market as souvenirs and as pictures for the walls of educated homes. The sources at hand give little about where Stiepevich was born, where he trained or whom he served, and the spelling of his name varies, so no more is claimed here.","q22341569":"Samuel Broadbent Jr. was an American portrait painter, born in 1810 and dead in 1880. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two portraits by him, a Portrait of a Lady dated 1837 and a Portrait of a Gentleman dated 1838, painted when he was in his late twenties. They are a year apart, and the record does not say whether the sitters were related or whether the two works were meant to hang together. Their dates fall in the years when portraits were still the usual way for a family to record its members, before the daguerreotype of 1839 began to change the trade. The sitters' names are not preserved, and the sources at hand give little about where Broadbent trained, which towns he worked in or whom he painted for.","q22343687":"Prince Demah Barnes was born about 1745 and died in 1778. He was an enslaved man in Massachusetts and is among the earliest known portrait painters of African descent in colonial America, a fact that gives his surviving work unusual historical importance. He is recorded in the Boston area as a painter who made likenesses for paying sitters. His Portrait of William Duguid, dated 1773, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He died at about thirty-three, in the years of the American Revolution. The sources at hand give little detail of his training, his patrons or the circumstances of his life and death, and no more is claimed here than is firmly recorded.","q2253952":"Pier Francesco Mazzucchelli, called Morazzone after his birthplace near Varese, was born in 1573 and died at Piacenza in 1626. He went to Rome as a young man and worked there for some years in the 1590s, where he absorbed the late Mannerism of the Cavaliere d'Arpino, and he returned to Lombardy to become, with Cerano and Giulio Cesare Procaccini, one of the three leading painters of Milan. His manner is dramatic and emotional, with strong light and sharply expressive figures, and he painted frescoes in the pilgrimage chapels at Varallo and Varese. The three masters painted together the large altarpiece of the martyrdom of Saints Rufina and Secunda for Milan cathedral, about 1625. Morazzone died while painting the dome of Piacenza cathedral, which Guercino finished. The Adoration of the Magi at the National Gallery of Art dates from about 1600.","q22575818":"Theodore Sidney Moise was an American portrait painter, born in 1808 and dead in 1885. His portrait of Henry Clay, painted in 1843, is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Clay, the Kentucky statesman who had run for the presidency in 1824 and 1832, was about to run again in 1844, and he was one of the most portrayed Americans of the time. Moise's painting records the sitter in the year before that campaign, which he lost to James K. Polk. The sources at hand give little about where Moise was born, trained or worked, or about whether Clay sat to him in person, and no more is claimed here than his dates and the dated portrait.","q22576542":"Theodore Lund was born in 1810 and died in 1895. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds three of his portraits: Samuel Baldwin Ludlow, dated 1838, and a pair of 1839, Warren Rogers and Mrs. Warren Rogers, who was Julie Françoise Gabrielle d'Anterroches. The sitter's French name, that of an old noble family, suggests a connection with France, though the museum's record does not say where the pictures were painted. Pairs of portraits of husbands and wives were a staple of a portrait painter's trade in the late 1830s. The sources at hand give little about Lund's training or career, and nothing further is claimed here.","q2265356":"Orazio Borgianni was born in Rome in 1578 and trained there, then spent several years in Spain from about 1598, in Madrid and Toledo, before returning to Rome in the early 1600s. He came under the influence of Caravaggio, though he was not his pupil, and painted religious subjects with strong light and shadow, dark, sober colour and a rough, melancholy intensity quite unlike the graceful manner then fashionable. Among his best-known works is a Trinity painted for a Trinitarian church in Rome. His Head of an Old Woman at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, after 1610, is a study of an aged face without idealisation, the kind of head favoured by painters of the Caravaggesque circle. He died young, in Rome, in 1616, and his reputation has grown since the twentieth century.","q22667429":"Parmenas Howell was born in 1784 and died in 1808, at only twenty-four, so that his whole career lasted a few years. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds one portrait by him, Rudolphus Bogert, painted about 1806, when he was about twenty-two. The sitter's surname, from the Dutch Bogaert, was common in the Dutch-settled districts of New York and New Jersey, but the museum's record does not say where the picture was painted, and nothing links the painter to any particular town. A career of only a few years leaves little trace. The sources at hand record nothing certain about his training, teachers or patrons, and no more is claimed here.","q296":"Claude Monet was born in Paris in 1840 and grew up in Le Havre, where the landscape painter Eugène Boudin taught him to work out of doors. In Paris he met Renoir, Sisley and Bazille, and his harbour view Impression, Sunrise gave Impressionism its name when it hung in the group's first exhibition in 1874. He lived at Argenteuil, at Vétheuil and, from 1883, at Giverny, painting the Seine, his gardens and the changing light on a single subject. In the 1890s he painted some thirty views of Rouen Cathedral from rented rooms opposite and showed twenty of them together in 1895. Poor for many years, he found steady support in the dealer Paul Durand-Ruel and was famous and prosperous long before his death at Giverny in 1926.","q2966287":"Christophe Huet was born in Paris in 1700 and died there in 1759. He was a decorative painter, and his name is tied to the singerie, the French fashion for scenes in which monkeys, dressed and posed like courtiers, take part in concerts, hunts, picnics and painting lessons. Watteau's teacher Claude Gillot had helped to make the taste popular; Huet carried it into whole painted rooms. His best-known schemes are the Grande Singerie at Chantilly, made in the 1730s for the Prince de Condé, and a monkey room in the Hôtel de Rohan in Paris. The six panels at the National Gallery of Art, from about 1739, show monkeys as fishermen, musicians, dancers, a sculptor and a painter, a gentle joke at the expense of the arts and of the courtiers who enjoyed them.","q297":"Diego Velázquez was born in Seville in 1599 and trained under Francisco Pacheco, whose daughter he married in 1618. His early pictures, among them The Supper at Emmaus, are sober kitchen and tavern scenes lit with hard, direct realism. In 1623 he went to Madrid, painted the young Philip IV and was made court painter, a post he kept for life. Two journeys to Italy, in 1629–31 and 1649–51, widened his range. In Rome he painted his assistant Juan de Pareja, whom he freed in 1650, and the portrait drew wide praise when shown in public. At court he painted the royal family, jesters and dwarfs, and Las Meninas in 1656. Made a knight of Santiago in 1659, he died in Madrid in 1660; Manet later called him the painter of painters.","q297838":"Jusepe de Ribera was born in 1591 at Xàtiva, near Valencia, and left Spain for Italy as a young man. He was in Rome by 1613 and settled in Naples by 1616, then ruled by Spanish viceroys, and he stayed there for life. Italians called him Lo Spagnoletto, the Little Spaniard, and he often signed his canvases with the word español. Caravaggio's strong contrasts of light and shadow shaped his early pictures, with their weathered saints and philosophers; after about 1630 his colour grew warmer under the influence of Venetian painting. Viceroys, churches and collectors in Spain and Italy bought his work, and his etchings carried it abroad. The Martyrdom of Saint Bartholomew of 1634 shows his gift for stark, physical drama. He died in Naples in 1652.","q2997495":"Cornelis Verbeeck, born about 1590 and dead in 1637, was a Dutch painter of ships and naval actions, generally associated with Haarlem, the city where Dutch marine painting took shape under Hendrick Vroom. His pictures belong to that earlier manner, which favoured crowded, carefully drawn ships, clear colour and a high viewpoint from which the whole action could be followed. A Naval Encounter between Dutch and Spanish Warships, from about 1618 to 1620, dates from the years of the long war against Spain, when scenes of sea fights found ready buyers among patriotic townsmen. Little is recorded of his life, his training or his patrons beyond his dates, and it would be unsafe to say more than that.","q304411":"Melchior d'Hondecoeter was born in Utrecht in 1636 into a family of bird painters: his father Gijsbert and his grandfather Gillis both painted birds, and after his father's death in 1653 he learned from his uncle, Jan Baptist Weenix. He worked in The Hague before settling in Amsterdam in 1663, and he died there in 1695. He specialised in large canvases of farmyard and ornamental fowl, such as cockerels, hens, ducks, turkeys and peacocks, shown in park settings and often squabbling over a scrap or defending their young. Such pictures hung in the town houses of Amsterdam merchants, and he also worked for the stadholder William III. His Peacocks of 1683 shows his command of glossy, richly coloured plumage. His fame lasted well into the eighteenth century.","q3048892":"Ulrich Apt the Elder, born about 1460 and dead in 1532, belonged to a family of painters in Augsburg, then one of the richest trading cities of the Holy Roman Empire and a busy market for altarpieces and portraits. He is known from altarpiece panels and a small number of portraits. The double portrait of 1512 at the Metropolitan Museum of Art shows a husband and wife, tentatively named as Lorenz Kraffter and Honesta Merz; the question mark in the museum's title shows that the identification is not certain. Paired portraits like this one, made to record a marriage or a family, reflect the confidence of the merchant society that Augsburg's banking houses supported. Little else is securely recorded here about his training, his teachers or his patrons.","q310025":"Théodore Rousseau was born in Paris in 1812 and became the leading figure of the Barbizon school of landscape painters. He trained briefly in studios but learned chiefly from nature, travelling through the Auvergne, the Jura and the Alps and, from the late 1840s, living at Barbizon beside the Forest of Fontainebleau, where his close friend was Jean-François Millet. The Salon jury refused his work through the late 1830s and 1840s, and he was nicknamed the great refused; recognition came after 1848, and he was honoured at the Paris World's Fair of 1855. He campaigned to save the old trees of the forest from felling, and an artists' reserve was decreed there in 1861. His landscapes are careful, slowly built studies of oaks, still water and passing weather. He died at Barbizon in 1867.","q3119489":"Pseudo-Palmeruccio is not a name but a label. Scholars use it for an Italian painter of the early fourteenth century whose real name is unknown, but whose pictures show the manner of Guiduccio Palmerucci, a painter documented at Gubbio, a hill town in Umbria. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Saint Romuald, dated only as possibly about 1320 to 1330, shows the founder of the Camaldolese order, the white-robed hermit-monks who lived in the mountains of central Italy. Panels of single saints like this were often parts of larger altarpieces made for a church or monastery, painted in tempera on a gold ground. Nothing certain is recorded about this painter's life, training or patrons, and nothing more can be said without inventing it.","q312617":"Rosso Fiorentino, born Giovanni Battista di Jacopo in Florence in 1494, was trained in the circle of Andrea del Sarto and took his nickname, the Red Florentine, from his red hair. His early pictures, such as the Madonna and Child with Saint John the Evangelist of 1512 to 1513, already show a taste for sharp edges and unusual colour, and in his mature work the angular figures, tense gestures and acid hues of Mannerism are fully formed. After work in Florence, Volterra and Rome he fled the sack of Rome in 1527, spent time in Sansepolcro and Venice, and in 1530 went to France. Francis I made him a canon of the Sainte-Chapelle and employed him at Fontainebleau, where the Gallery of Francis I, with its painted panels and stucco figures, became a model for court decoration. He died in Paris in 1540.","q314655":"Maurice Quentin de La Tour was born in Saint-Quentin in 1704 and spent his working life in Paris, where he became the most admired French portraitist in pastel. He was approved by the Académie royale in 1737 and received as a full member in 1746, and he showed at the Salon for decades. His sitters ranged from Louis XV and Madame de Pompadour to Voltaire, Rousseau and d'Alembert; the great pastel of Pompadour, shown in 1755, is now in the Louvre. He caught faces at a moment of animation, often with a half smile, and he made many preparatory studies, which survive in Saint-Quentin. Late in life his mind failed, and he returned to his native town, where he died in 1788. He founded a free drawing school there.","q314814":"Philippe de Champaigne was born in Brussels in 1602 and trained there as a landscape painter before going to Paris in 1621. He worked on the decoration of the Luxembourg Palace for Marie de' Medici and became her painter, then served Louis XIII and Cardinal Richelieu, whom he painted several times, most famously in a triple portrait now in London. He was a founding member of the Académie royale in 1648. In his later years he grew close to the Jansenists of Port-Royal, where his daughter Catherine was a nun; his Ex-voto of 1662, painted after her recovery from illness, hangs in the Louvre. His portraits, among them the magistrate Omer Talon and the minister Colbert, are marked by sober colour, clear drawing and quiet gravity. He died in Paris in 1674.","q315237":"Vasily Perov was born in Tobolsk, Siberia, in 1834, the illegitimate son of a baron who held a post in the town, and trained at the Moscow School of Painting, Sculpture and Architecture. He painted Russian life with a critic's eye: village processions, poor children, drunken clergy and wandering peasants, pictures that were sometimes thought too pointed for the censor. A gold medal from the Academy in 1861 paid for study abroad, but he came home from Paris early. He taught at the Moscow school and in 1870 was among the founders of the Wanderers, the group that took exhibitions out of the capital to the provinces. His portraits of writers, including Dostoevsky in 1872, are among the best of their time. He died in 1882. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Head of a Man is a small study.","q315803":"Peter Candid was born in Bruges in 1548 as Pieter de Witte; Candid, or Pietro Candido in Italian, renders his Flemish surname, which means the white, and reflects the years he spent training and working in Italy. He studied in Florence under Giorgio Vasari and worked with him on the decoration of the Palazzo Vecchio, before entering the service of the Medici grand dukes. In 1586 he accepted the call of Duke Wilhelm V of Bavaria to Munich, and he remained at the Bavarian court, under Maximilian I as well, until his death in 1628. He painted altarpieces and portraits, and designed tapestries and decorations for the Munich Residenz, bringing a Tuscan late-Renaissance manner north of the Alps. The Annunciation at the Metropolitan Museum of Art dates from about 1585, just before his move to Bavaria.","q315819":"Claude-Joseph Vernet was born in Avignon in 1714, the son of a decorative painter, and went to Rome in 1734, where he stayed some twenty years. He painted Italian harbours, coastal views, storms and moonlit seas, and found buyers among the French and English visitors on the Grand Tour. Back in France he accepted in 1753 a royal commission from Louis XV for a series of views of the ports of France, which occupied him for years and took him along the coast to draw on the spot. Diderot wrote at length about his pictures in the Salon reviews, admiring their light and weather. The Shipwreck and Moonlight, both of 1772, make a pair of storm and calm. He died in Paris in 1789; his son Carle and grandson Horace were painters too.","q315835":"Taddeo Gaddi, the son of the painter Gaddo Gaddi, was born in Florence around 1300 and died there in 1366. Cennino Cennini, who learned from Taddeo's son Agnolo, recorded that Taddeo worked with Giotto for twenty-four years, and he became the most successful follower of the master in Florence. His best-known works are the frescoes of the Life of the Virgin in the Baroncelli Chapel in Santa Croce, painted in the 1330s, which include one of the first night scenes in Italian painting, the angel appearing to the shepherds in a blaze of light. He also painted altarpieces and small devotional panels. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints, from about 1340, was repainted around 1480, a sign of how long it stayed in use.","q3164509":"Jean Baptiste de Champaigne was born in Brussels in 1631, the nephew of the painter Philippe de Champaigne. He came to Paris as a boy and was trained in his uncle's workshop, where he learned the grave, clear manner for which the family became known, and he assisted on his uncle's commissions. He was received into the Académie royale in the 1660s. He painted religious subjects and portraits, and The Martyrdom of Saint Lawrence at the National Gallery of Art, from about 1660, shows his taste for a solemn, carefully arranged composition. His reputation has always stood in the shadow of his uncle's. He died in Paris in 1681. Little else of his career can be stated here with confidence.","q317920":"Simon Vouet was born in Paris in 1590 and learned to paint from his father, who was also a painter. He spent about fifteen years in Italy, mainly in Rome, where he took up Caravaggio's dramatic realism, worked for the Barberini circle and in 1624 was elected head of the Academy of Saint Luke. Louis XIII called him home in 1627 and made him first painter to the king. In Paris he ran a very large workshop, and his decorations for royal and private houses, together with altarpieces for churches, brought a lighter, brighter Italian Baroque to France. Charles Le Brun and Eustache Le Sueur were among his pupils. Saint Jerome and the Angel and The Muses Urania and Calliope show the two sides of his art, grave and graceful. He died in Paris in 1649.","q318114":"Nicholas Hilliard was born in Exeter in 1547, the son of a goldsmith, and was apprenticed to Robert Brandon, goldsmith to Queen Elizabeth I. He became the queen's limner, painting in watercolour on vellum the tiny portraits known as miniatures, which were worn as jewels or given as keepsakes at court. His portraits of Elizabeth and of courtiers such as Sir Walter Ralegh are bright, precise and often lit with gold. In his Treatise Concerning the Arte of Limning, written around 1600, he recalled that the queen chose to sit in an open garden, so that no shadow fell on her face. James I continued to employ him, and Isaac Oliver, his pupil, became his rival. Hilliard died in London in 1619.","q320899":"Théodore Caruelle d'Aligny was born in 1798 at Chaumes, in the Nièvre, and trained in Paris in the classical tradition of landscape painting. In 1822 he went to Italy, where he befriended Corot and remained for several years. On his return he was among the first painters to work in the Forest of Fontainebleau, which is why he is counted among the forerunners of the Barbizon school, though he held to the noble, carefully composed landscape of the Poussin tradition and drew trees and rocks with a firm, clear outline. From 1857 he directed the École des Beaux-Arts in Lyon, and he died there in 1871. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns a view of the edge of a wood from about 1850 and a landscape with a cave.","q321484":"Philippe Rousseau was born in Paris in 1816 and studied with the painters Antoine-Jean Gros and Jean-Victor Bertin. He began as a landscape and animal painter and became best known for still lifes of food and tableware: hams, game, fruit, glass, pottery and silver, set out on plain tables with a quiet, tactile sense of texture. Critics often compared him with Chardin, the eighteenth-century master of the kitchen picture, and his work was admired and bought by collectors in Paris under the Second Empire. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Still Life with Ham dates from the 1870s. He died in 1887. Few personal details of his life are well documented, and none is added here beyond what his dates and his pictures show.","q3216954":"Lancelot-Théodore Turpin de Crissé was born in Paris in 1782 into an old noble family and is usually known as the Comte Turpin de Crissé. He painted landscapes and views, travelled in Italy, exhibited at the Salon and sat in the Académie des Beaux-Arts. His View of a Villa, Pizzofalcone, Naples, of about 1819, shows the hill of Pizzofalcone, with its terraces and its outlook over the bay, which was a favourite subject for visiting artists. He also collected antiquities and works of art, and bequeathed them to the city of Angers, where the Musée Turpin de Crissé now bears his name. He died in Paris in 1859. Beyond these facts, little of his personal life can be stated with confidence here.","q3263639":"Louise-Joséphine Sarazin de Belmont was born in 1790 and died in 1870. She was one of the few women of her generation to make a career as a landscape painter, which meant working outdoors and travelling, and she trained in the Paris tradition of classical landscape. She spent time in Italy, and in the 1820s she travelled in Sicily, where she made views of ruined theatres and coastal castles. The two pictures at the National Gallery of Art, the View of the Castello di San Giuliano near Trapani and the Roman Theater at Taormina of 1828, come from that journey. They show clear light, careful drawing and an interest in the remains of antiquity. Few other details of her life can be given with confidence, so none are added here.","q326434":"Thomas Couture was born in Senlis in 1815 and trained in Paris under Antoine-Jean Gros and Paul Delaroche. He never won the Prix de Rome, though he came second in 1837, and he made his name at the Salon of 1847 with Romans of the Decadence, an enormous banquet scene that critics read as a rebuke to the morals of the age. A commission to paint the christening of the Prince Imperial was left unfinished after a quarrel, and he withdrew from Paris. He is remembered above all as a teacher: Édouard Manet worked in his studio from 1850 to 1856, and Americans such as William Morris Hunt and John La Farge studied with him. Soap Bubbles, of about 1859, recasts an old emblem of the brevity of life. He died in 1879.","q326606":"Paul Sérusier was born in Paris in 1863 and studied at the Académie Julian. In the summer of 1888 he went to Pont-Aven in Brittany, where Paul Gauguin urged him to paint with pure, bold colour and flat, simplified forms. The small landscape he made on a cigar-box lid under that guidance, called The Talisman, was carried back to Paris and became the founding image of the Nabis, the group that included Bonnard, Denis and Vuillard. Sérusier returned to Brittany often, and in 1890 he painted at Le Pouldu, where Gauguin's circle gathered. In later life he lived at Châteauneuf-du-Faou and wrote a book on the theory of colour and composition, ABC de la peinture, published in 1921. He died in 1927.","q330400":"Abraham Mignon was born in Frankfurt in 1640 and learned to paint flowers and fruit from Jacob Marrel, a Frankfurt still-life painter who had worked in Utrecht. He then went to the Netherlands himself and became a pupil of Jan Davidsz. de Heem in Utrecht, where he settled. His still lifes bring flowers, fruit, insects, small animals and sometimes fish together in rich colour against a dark ground, with close attention to petals, dewdrops and the sheen of fruit, and often a quiet reminder that beauty does not last. He later returned to Germany and died at Wetzlar in 1679, in his late thirties. The National Gallery of Art's hanging bouquet and its still life with fruit, fish and a nest show the range of his art.","q3304139":"Pieter Neeffs the Younger was a Flemish painter of church interiors, the son and pupil of Peeter Neeffs the Elder. He worked in Antwerp in the middle of the seventeenth century and kept so closely to his father's manner that the two are not always easy to tell apart. Their pictures show the high, narrow nave of a Gothic church in careful perspective, with small figures at prayer or in talk, and both men painted such interiors by day and by night, with candle and lamplight glowing in the dark vaults. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Interior of a Gothic Church at Night dates from about 1660. The small figures in pictures of this kind were often added by specialist figure painters. His dates are not firmly established, and little of his career is documented.","q3304596":"Meiffren Conte was born in Avignon in 1630 and died there in 1705. He is generally counted among the leading still-life painters of the south of France in the later seventeenth century. Avignon then belonged to the Pope, not to the French crown, and was a prosperous city of clergy, merchants and travellers where Italian, Flemish and French taste met. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's picture, in which silver and gold plate, shells and a sword are gathered together, belongs to the family of still lifes that display costly things for admiration and may also hint at vanity, the passing of worldly wealth. Little is securely recorded about his teachers or patrons, and nothing is added here beyond what his dates and his pictures show.","q330784":"Abraham de Verwer was born about 1585 and died in 1650. He is recorded as a Dutch painter of ships, harbours and town views. His View of Hoorn, from about 1650, shows the old Zuiderzee port from the water, with its towers and gates along the shore and sailing boats in the foreground. Hoorn was one of the six seats of the Dutch East India Company and a city proud of its shipping, so a view of it found a ready audience at home. The picture at the National Gallery of Art is the work by which he is known in this collection. Little is recorded of his life, his training or his patrons, and nothing more is claimed here.","q3311323":"Michele da Verona, also called Michele di Zenone, was born about 1470 and died about 1536, and worked in Verona, the city on the Adige that had belonged to Venice since 1405. Veronese painters of his generation grew up in the shadow of Andrea Mantegna's San Zeno altarpiece, with its firm, sculptural drawing, and he is usually placed within the tradition that followed from it. He painted devotional pictures for churches and private houses. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Madonna and Child with the Infant Saint John the Baptist, probably from the late 1490s, is a small picture of the sort that families kept for prayer at home. His life is thinly documented, and little more can be said about him with confidence.","q3339920":"Nicola di Maestro Antonio d'Ancona, a name that means Nicola, son of Master Antonio, of Ancona, was an Italian painter from the Marche, the region on the Adriatic coast, who apparently worked in the later fifteenth century. Ancona was a busy seaport trading across the Adriatic, and its painters served churches and confraternities in the surrounding towns. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Scenes from the Life of King Nebuchadnezzar illustrates episodes from the story of the Babylonian king told in the Book of Daniel, in a picture-book manner. Dates of birth and death are not given in the sources used here, and nothing further about his training, family or patrons is claimed.","q3339947":"Nicolas-Antoine Taunay was born in Paris in 1755 and trained under the history painter Nicolas-Bernard Lépicié and the battle painter François Casanova. He painted small landscapes with figures, lively genre scenes and the battles of Napoleon's campaigns, and he spent some years in Italy in the 1780s. The Billiard Room, painted after 1810, is a good example of the cabinet picture he made for private buyers. In 1816 he sailed to Brazil with the group of French artists known as the French Artistic Mission, invited by the Portuguese court then settled in Rio de Janeiro. There he painted views of the city and its surroundings and helped to found an art academy. He returned to France in 1821 and died in Paris in 1830; his son Félix-Émile stayed on in Brazil.","q334946":"Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes was born in Toulouse in 1750 and studied in Paris under the history painter Gabriel-François Doyen. He made two long stays in Italy, in the 1770s and again in 1782 to 1785, and filled them with small oil sketches painted outdoors: studies of skies, rooftops, ruins and the light of the Roman Campagna, like the cloud study here, made quickly and never meant for exhibition. Back in Paris he turned such material into polished classical landscapes, and he wrote a treatise, published in 1799 and 1800, urging young painters to study nature directly. His campaigning helped to bring about the Prix de Rome for historical landscape in 1817. His pupils included Jean-Victor Bertin and Achille-Etna Michallon, and through them Corot. He died in Paris in 1819.","q3356695":"Orsola Maddalena Caccia was born in 1596 at Moncalvo in Piedmont, the daughter of the painter Guglielmo Caccia, called Il Moncalvo, and learned to paint in his workshop. She became a nun in the Ursuline convent at Moncalvo, which her father had helped to found, and there she kept a studio that supplied altarpieces and devotional pictures and helped to support the community. Her still lifes of flowers and fruit in vases and baskets are among the earliest independent ones painted by a woman in Italy. The Metropolitan Museum of Art has her Madonna and Child with the Infant Saint John the Baptist and two still lifes, from about 1630 to 1645. Her father died in 1625, and she lived on at the convent until her death in 1676.","q3382227":"Piat Joseph Sauvage was born in Tournai in 1744, in what was then the Austrian Netherlands, and died there in 1818. He is known as a master of grisaille, painting in tones of grey and cream so cleverly that his panels pass for carved stone or plaster relief. After training in his home region he made his career in Paris, where in the 1770s and 1780s he supplied decorative panels and overdoors for the fashionable houses of the Louis XVI period, with putti, nymphs and Bacchic processions in the antique manner. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's panels, among them a Triumph of Bacchus and an Infant Bacchanal, belong to that taste. In later life he returned to Tournai, where he remained until his death.","q3382678":"Pier Francesco Fiorentino, which means Peter Francis the Florentine, was born in 1444 and died in 1497. He worked in the generation after Fra Filippo Lippi and Benozzo Gozzoli, whose gentle manner he followed, and he ran a busy workshop that supplied devotional pictures to churches and families in Florence and in small Tuscan towns such as San Gimignano. Most of his surviving work shows the Madonna and Child, painted in soft colour with sweet, rounded faces and often to a pattern that sold well. The National Gallery of Art's Madonna and Child, of about 1475, is a typical example. He is not counted among the greatest Florentine masters, but his panels show the steady, workaday trade in religious pictures that kept the city's studios busy.","q3383448":"Pierre-Paul-Léon Glaize, usually called Léon Glaize, was born in Paris in 1842, the son of the painter Auguste Glaize, and lived a very long life, dying in 1932. He trained first under his father and made his career at the Salon, sending large mythological, historical and religious pictures in the polished academic manner, together with figures of young women such as Before the Mirror of 1873. This was the kind of picture that earned official honours and public attention in the Paris of the Third Republic. By the time he died, the taste for it had long given way to Impressionism and modern art, and academic painters of his kind were largely forgotten; in recent decades they have been studied and exhibited again. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds the 1873 picture.","q3386523":"Pierre Pasquier was born in 1731 and died in 1806. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds two of his portraits: the Abbé Charles Bossut, dated 1772, and Gérard de Vesme, dated 1773 though the last digit is uncertain. Bossut (1730 to 1814) was a priest and mathematician who belonged to the Académie des sciences, and the portrait shows the kind of sitter, a respected man of learning, who was painted in Paris in the years before the Revolution. In the 1770s a portrait was the usual way for a man of standing to record himself for family and friends. Beyond his dates and these two likenesses, little is recorded of Pasquier's training, teachers or career, and none of it is guessed at here.","q3388216":"Pietro di Domenico da Montepulciano, which means Peter, son of Domenico, of Montepulciano, was an Italian painter who worked in the early fifteenth century. Montepulciano is a hill town in southern Tuscany, between the territories of Siena and Florence, and painting there drew on the traditions of both cities. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Madonna and Child with Angels carries the date 1420, which makes it a fixed point in a career about which little else is firmly known. It is a devotional panel in the late Gothic taste of the time, with the Virgin holding her child among adoring angels. Dates of birth and death are not given in the sources used here, and no teacher or patron is named without evidence.","q339270":"Otto Marseus van Schrieck was born in Nijmegen about 1619 and died in Amsterdam in 1678. He travelled in France and Italy, spent time in Florence, and in Rome joined the Dutch artists' fraternity known as the Bentvueghels, who gave him the nickname Snuffelaer, the sniffer, a reference to his habit of hunting in the undergrowth for insects and reptiles. By 1657 he had settled in Amsterdam. He painted forest-floor still lifes, called sottobosco: thistles, poppies and toadstools in dark undergrowth, with snakes, lizards, toads, butterflies and snails drawn with minute care. His early biographer Arnold Houbraken reported that he kept a garden of such creatures to paint from. His pictures were popular, and followers took up the type.","q3410504":"Puccio di Simone was a Florentine painter recorded as active from about 1330 to 1360, in the generation after Giotto. His work is close to that of Bernardo Daddi and Taddeo Gaddi, with smooth rounded faces, soft folds and rich gold grounds. His best-known picture is the triptych now at the National Gallery of Art, dated 1354, which shows the Madonna and Child enthroned among eighteen angels, flanked by Saint Anthony Abbot and Saint Venantius. The gallery shows the centre and wings as separate panels as well as the whole. Pictures of this size were made as portable altarpieces for a chapel, a confraternity or a private patron. Little else is recorded of his life, and nothing further is claimed here.","q342467":"Théodore Chassériau was born in 1819 at Samaná, in what is now the Dominican Republic, and was brought to France as a small child. At eleven he entered the studio of Ingres, and he learned that master's pure line, then added the warm colour and romantic subjects of Delacroix, so that his art stands between the two. He visited Italy in 1840 and Algeria in 1846; the Scene in the Jewish Quarter of Constantine of 1851 draws on the Algerian journey, and Desdemona of 1849 on Shakespeare's Othello. His largest work was the decoration of the grand staircase of the Cour des Comptes in Paris, made between 1844 and 1848; the murals were wrecked in the fire of 1871, though fragments survive in the Louvre. He died young, in Paris in 1856, at thirty-seven.","q3442328":"Rose-Adélaïde Ducreux was born in Paris in 1761, the daughter of the portraitist Joseph Ducreux, who became first painter to Queen Marie-Antoinette, and she learned to paint from him. Her Self-Portrait with a Harp, dated 1791, is the picture by which she is best known; it presents her as both painter and accomplished musician. The year matters, for in 1791 the Paris Salon was opened to all artists, not only members of the Academy, and women exhibited there in larger numbers than before. She died in 1802, aged about forty-one, the same year as her father. Her life is little documented, and little more can be said about her with certainty.","q3452796":"Rufus Hathaway was born in 1770 and died in 1822. He lived in southeastern Massachusetts, around Duxbury, and painted portraits of his neighbours in about 1790 in a flat, decorative, self-taught manner that is now prized as American folk art. He is also recorded as a physician, and in time he turned from painting to the practice of medicine. His best-known picture is Molly Wales Fobes, painted in 1790 and also called Lady with Her Pets, which shows a young woman seated in a room with her animals, drawn with a lively eye for pattern and detail. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns it. He was about twenty when he painted it. Beyond these facts, little is firmly recorded of his life here, and no more is claimed.","q3459812":"Pacino di Bonaguida was a Florentine painter and manuscript illuminator, recorded in the first half of the fourteenth century, and one of the leading figures in Florence in the generation after Giotto. He ran a busy workshop producing panel paintings, frescoes and illuminated books, and he is known for a lively, narrative style with small, vivid figures, bright colour and gold. His Tree of Life, painted about 1310 to 1315 for the convent of Monticelli and now in the Galleria dell'Accademia in Florence, shows scenes from the life of Christ on the branches of a tree-shaped cross. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's small panel, with Saint John on Patmos, the Madonna enthroned and the Death of the Virgin on one side and the Crucifixion on the other, is a work for private devotion.","q3510246":"Sébastien-Louis-Guillaume Norblin de la Gourdaine was born in 1796 and died in 1884, a long life that spanned the changing French regimes of the nineteenth century. He came of an artistic family: his father, Jean-Pierre Norblin de la Gourdaine, was a French painter who spent much of his career in Poland. The younger Norblin is known to have travelled in Italy in the 1820s, where he made small studies from nature; the National Gallery of Art's Village on the Island of Ischia, of about 1826, is a quick, light view of the kind. Few other facts about his training and career can be given with confidence here, and none is guessed.","q352438":"Adriaen van Ostade was born in Haarlem in 1610 and spent nearly all his life there. He is said to have studied with Frans Hals, and he learned a great deal from the rough tavern scenes of Adriaen Brouwer. He joined the Haarlem painters' guild in 1634 and later served as one of its officers. He painted peasants drinking, quarrelling, smoking, dancing and making music in barns, taverns and cottage yards, at first with a coarse, comic edge and later with a warmer, gentler light and a more orderly mood, as in The Cottage Dooryard of 1673. Hundreds of paintings and about fifty etchings survive. His younger brother Isack was his pupil. Dutch buyers prized his work, and collectors in the eighteenth century paid high prices for it. He died in Haarlem in 1685.","q3525211":"Thomas Hovenden was born in 1840 at Dunmanway, County Cork, Ireland, and was apprenticed as a carver and gilder before studying at the Cork School of Design. He emigrated to the United States in 1863, studied at the National Academy of Design in New York, then spent several years in Paris and in Brittany in the 1870s. After returning in 1880 he settled near Philadelphia, taught at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, and married the painter Helen Corson. His pictures of homely American life and of historical moments were widely loved. The Last Moments of John Brown, painted in 1882 to 1884, shows the abolitionist pausing on his way to the gallows to kiss a Black child, and was among the most discussed of them. He was killed at a railway crossing in 1895.","q355406":"Paulus Potter was born at Enkhuizen in 1625, the son of the painter Pieter Potter, and worked in Delft, The Hague and finally Amsterdam. He painted cattle, horses, dogs, hunts and farmyards in the open Dutch landscape, rendering animals with an exactness that was exceptional for its day, as in The Young Bull of 1647, now in the Mauritshuis, a bull shown nearly life size. He joined the Delft guild in 1646, and A Farrier's Shop of 1648 dates from those years. He married in 1650 and moved to Amsterdam in 1652. He died of tuberculosis in 1654, aged twenty-eight, leaving about a hundred paintings and a few etchings. His reputation rose steadily after his death, and he was much admired and collected in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.","q3568746":"William John Thomson, who lived from 1771 to 1845, painted portraits in the early nineteenth century, and the three in this collection are dated 1817, 1822 and about 1825. Their titles, two called Portrait of a Lady and one Portrait of a Gentleman, show that the sitters' names have not been recorded, which often happens when portraits leave the families that commissioned them. In those years a painted likeness was the usual way for a family of some standing to keep a record of its members, and a painter who could take a good likeness found steady work. The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds all three. Little is firmly recorded here about where he was born, where he trained or where he worked, and no more is claimed.","q3569029":"William Williams, who lived from 1727 to 1791, was a portrait painter in the American colonies in the middle of the eighteenth century. Like many painters of the day he moved from town to town, working in Philadelphia and New York among other places, taking likenesses of merchants' families and painting other pictures to order. He is remembered also as a writer, for an adventure story, The Journal of Llewellin Penrose, a Seaman, which was published after his death. The young Benjamin West later recalled that Williams lent him books on painting when West was a boy in Philadelphia. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Portrait of a Boy, probably of the Crossfield family, was painted around 1770 to 1775. Other details of his career are uncertain.","q3592147":"Robert Frederick Blum was born in Cincinnati in 1857 and began as a commercial lithographer before turning to painting. He worked in New York and visited Venice in 1880, where the example of James McNeill Whistler, then making his Venetian etchings, helped to shape his taste for delicate, subtle effects. His most important journey was to Japan in 1890 to 1892, as an illustrator for a magazine, and the pictures he made after it, among them The Ameya, a candy seller at work among children, brought Japanese street life to American audiences. He painted large murals for Mendelssohn Hall in New York in the 1890s, and later turned to views of the city, such as the one from his studio window on Grove Street. He died at forty-five, in 1903.","q359421":"Salvator Rosa was born at Arenella, near Naples, in 1615 and trained there among painters close to Jusepe de Ribera. He went to Rome in 1635 and from 1640 to 1649 worked in Florence for the Medici, where he founded an academy of wits, wrote satires and became known for wild, dramatic landscapes of rocky coasts, bandits, soldiers and witches, such as the Scenes of Witchcraft at Cleveland. Back in Rome he turned to large history paintings, which he valued above landscapes, and to portraits and satirical prints; his Self-Portrait of about 1647 is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He prized his independence and resisted being told what to paint. He died in Rome in 1673. In the eighteenth century his savage scenery, praised by English connoisseurs, helped to set the taste for the picturesque.","q362016":"Sassetta, whose real name was Stefano di Giovanni, was born around 1390 and died in 1450. He worked in Siena and the surrounding towns and is the most poetic Sienese painter of the fifteenth century, an artist who kept the gold grounds, graceful line and glowing colour of Gothic art while learning from the new Florentine interest in space and perspective. His pictures are mostly altarpieces and small devotional panels, with gentle figures and storytelling in clear, jewel-like landscapes, as in The Journey of the Magi at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. His largest commission, an altarpiece for the Franciscans of Borgo San Sepolcro, painted between 1437 and 1444, was later broken up, and its panels are now in museums in several countries. Long neglected, he was rediscovered by connoisseurs in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries.","q367360":"Orazio Gentileschi was born in Pisa in 1563 and went to Rome as a young man, where he became one of the first and best of Caravaggio's followers; he gave evidence in the libel case of 1603 that involved Caravaggio and the painter Giovanni Baglione. He took from Caravaggio the use of live models and strong light, but his colour grew brighter and cooler, with silky drapery and a lyrical feeling, as in The Lute Player. He worked in Rome, the Marche, Genoa and Turin, and in Paris for Marie de' Medici. In 1626 he went to England, where he became court painter to Charles I and painted a ceiling for the Queen's House at Greenwich. He died in London in 1639. His daughter Artemisia, his best-known pupil, became a painter of great renown.","q3688068":"William Keith was born in 1839 at Oldmeldrum, in Aberdeenshire, Scotland, and was taken to New York as a boy. He trained as a wood engraver and in 1859 moved to San Francisco, where he drew for magazines before turning to painting in the 1860s. After a period of study in Germany around 1869 he became the leading landscape painter of California, often called its dean of painters. He made trips to the Sierra Nevada with the naturalist John Muir, a close friend, and painted Yosemite, mountains and meadows, first in a bright, detailed manner and later in a darker, more atmospheric style. In 1906 the earthquake and fire in San Francisco destroyed many hundreds of his paintings. He died in 1911. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns his Yosemite Falls of 1879 and an Approaching Storm of 1880.","q3695965":"Robert Walter Weir was born in New Rochelle, New York, in 1803, and studied in Italy in the mid-1820s after early training in New York. He is best known as instructor of drawing at the United States Military Academy at West Point, a post he held from 1834 to 1876; among his pupils were Ulysses S. Grant and James McNeill Whistler. He painted history, landscape and portraits, and his Embarkation of the Pilgrims, commissioned for the Rotunda of the United States Capitol and put in place in 1844, is his most public work. His sons John Ferguson Weir and Julian Alden Weir both became painters. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's portrait of General Winfield Scott, the army's commander, dates from about 1855. He died in 1889.","q372203":"Michel Sittow was born in Reval, now Tallinn in Estonia, in 1469 and is believed to have trained in Bruges in the workshop of Hans Memling. From 1492 he served Queen Isabella of Castile as court painter, painting small devotional panels and portraits; the Assumption of the Virgin of about 1500 at the National Gallery of Art is probably from a series of small panels made for the queen. After her death in 1504 he travelled to the Netherlands, England and Denmark, and by 1506 he had returned to Reval, where he settled for the rest of his life. His Portrait of Diego de Guevara, of about 1515 to 1518, shows a courtier of the Habsburg court in the Netherlands. He died in Reval in 1525. Only a small number of works can be attributed to him.","q375926":"Rembrandt Peale was born in 1778 in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, the son of the painter and museum founder Charles Willson Peale, who named several of his children after great artists. At seventeen he painted George Washington from life in Philadelphia, and he spent much of the rest of his career reworking that likeness, finally in the porthole portrait of 1823, which Congress bought in 1832. He studied in London with Benjamin West in 1801 to 1803 and later visited Paris. In 1814 he opened the Peale Museum in Baltimore, in a building made for the purpose and soon lit by gas. He painted Thomas Jefferson, Lafayette and many others, as well as large moral allegories. He died in Philadelphia in 1860.","q379521":"Ercole de' Roberti was born in Ferrara about 1455 and trained in the lively school of the Este court, learning from Cosmè Tura and Francesco del Cossa, with whom he worked on the frescoes of the Palazzo Schifanoia. From the 1470s he also worked at Bologna, where he painted the portraits of Giovanni II Bentivoglio, the city's ruler, and his wife Ginevra, and a great altarpiece for the Garganelli Chapel. In 1486 he became court painter to Ercole I d'Este at Ferrara, where he designed festivals and decorations as well as pictures. His style is sharp and intense, with brittle, sculptural forms and tense emotion. He died in Ferrara in 1496. The Wife of Hasdrubal and Her Children, at the National Gallery of Art, is a late work.","q3806921":"Jan Philips van Thielen was born at Mechelen in 1618 and died in 1667. He is said to have learned from Daniel Seghers, the Jesuit lay brother of Antwerp who was famous for garlands of flowers painted around devotional images, and he followed Seghers in painting flowers in glass vases and in wreaths that frame a religious subject. Flower painting was prized in the Spanish Netherlands, where gardens, botany and the trade in bulbs and rare plants were fashionable, and roses and tulips were favourite subjects. His Roses and a Tulip in a Glass Vase, of about 1650 to 1660, is at the National Gallery of Art. Beyond these outlines, little of his life is securely recorded here, and no more is claimed.","q3806935":"Giovanni Sons, who lived from 1553 to 1614, is described as a Flemish painter; the Italian form of his first name suggests a northerner who spent his working life in Italy, as many Flemish artists did in this period. The National Gallery of Art owns his Judgment of Paris, of the late sixteenth century, which shows the story of the Trojan prince who gave the golden apple to Venus, the goddess of love, rather than to Juno or Minerva, and so set off the Trojan War. Mythologies of this kind, with nude goddesses in landscapes, were in demand among cultivated patrons. Little is recorded of his life, his training or his career in the sources available here, and nothing further is claimed.","q3809574":"John Riley was born in London in 1646 and died there in 1691. He trained with the portrait painters Gerard Soest and Isaac Fuller and, after the death of the court painter Sir Peter Lely in 1680, became one of the leading portraitists in England. After the revolution of 1688 he and Godfrey Kneller were appointed joint principal painters to William and Mary. Riley's likenesses are quieter and more individual than Kneller's grander style, with a subdued, rather serious palette. His portrait of Bridget Holmes, painted in 1686, shows a royal servant who was a hundred years old and is one of the most vivid pictures of its time. The National Gallery of Art's portrait of John Eldred dates from the 1670s or 1680s.","q3842963":"The Master of the Berswordt Altar is the name given to an anonymous German painter of about 1400, taken from an altarpiece connected with the Berswordt family, patricians of the Westphalian city of Dortmund. He belongs to the generation of painters active in Westphalia in the age of the International Gothic style, with gold grounds, elegant, flowing figures and a taste for tender, emotional detail. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's two panels, The Crucifixion and The Flagellation, both from about 1400, are scenes from the Passion, the story of Christ's suffering that was a favourite subject for church altarpieces. His real name, his training and his dates are not known, and nothing more is claimed here beyond what the surviving pictures show.","q3842992":"The Master of the Castello Nativity is the name given by scholars to an anonymous Florentine painter of the mid-fifteenth century, taken from a Nativity painted for the Medici villa at Castello, near Florence. He is placed in the circle of Fra Filippo Lippi, whose soft, graceful figures and warm colour he followed. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Portrait of a Woman, probably from the 1450s, belongs to a Florentine fashion of those years for portraits of women of rank, made for a wedding or a family, with careful attention to hair, dress and jewellery. Who the painter actually was has been much discussed, and nothing certain is recorded of his life, so no name, dates or patrons are given here.","q3850027":"Martin Archer Shee was born in Dublin in 1769 and studied at the Dublin Society's drawing school before going to London in 1788, where Edmund Burke helped him make his way. He entered the Royal Academy Schools, became an academician in 1800 and was for years a leading portraitist, painting politicians, soldiers and fashionable families in a solid, straightforward manner. After the death of Sir Thomas Lawrence in 1830 the academicians elected him President and he was knighted. He held the office until his death in 1850, and in 1837 the Academy moved to new rooms in the National Gallery building during his presidency. He also wrote poetry, a novel and a play. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's portrait of his son William Archer Shee dates from about 1820.","q3875919":"Niccolò di Buonaccorso was a Sienese painter of the later fourteenth century, recorded in Siena from the 1370s until his death in 1388. His work follows the graceful, linear tradition of the city's painting, with gold grounds, delicate colour and slender figures. His best-known picture, The Marriage of the Virgin, is in the National Gallery in London. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Saint Paul, whose date is not known, is a single saint's panel of the kind that formed part of a larger altarpiece; saints were shown holding their attributes, and Paul is traditionally shown with a book and a sword. Little else is recorded of his training or patrons, and nothing more is claimed here.","q389768":"Ottavio Leoni was born in Rome in 1578, the son of the Paduan painter Ludovico Leoni, which explains his nickname, Il Padovano. He painted altarpieces and made engravings, but he is best known for portrait drawings in black and red chalk, often on blue paper, which he made of the people of Rome: cardinals, nobles, poets, musicians, scholars and fellow artists. Hundreds survive. His likeness of Caravaggio, of about 1621, is generally thought to be the only portrait of the painter drawn from life. The drawings were collected and prized for their quick, lively resemblance. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's A Cardinal's Procession dates from 1621. He died in Rome in 1630.","q390073":"Samuel Lovett Waldo was born in Windham, Connecticut, in 1783 and began as a portrait painter in his native state. From 1806 to 1809 he studied in London, where he worked under Benjamin West, and on his return he settled in New York. From about 1820 until 1854 he worked in partnership with William Jewett, producing a very large number of portraits, in which Waldo is said to have painted the heads and Jewett the rest. His sitters included General Andrew Jackson, painted in 1819, and many New York merchants and their families; he also painted a famous picture of a local character, Old Pat, the Independent Beggar. He died in New York in 1861, one of the busiest portraitists of his time.","q3907293":"Polidoro Lanzani, who lived from 1515 to 1565 and is also called Polidoro Veneziano, was a Venetian painter of the generation after Titian. He followed Titian and Palma Vecchio in his warm colour, soft modelling and landscape backgrounds, and he specialised in half-length Madonnas and Holy Families, which were popular in Venice and on the mainland for private devotion. Some of his pictures have been attributed in the past to greater names, which shows how closely he imitated their manner; his reputation is that of a capable follower rather than an innovator. The National Gallery of Art's Madonna and Child and the Infant Saint John in a Landscape was painted between 1540 and 1550. Little is securely known of his life, and no more is claimed here.","q3960740":"The Master of the Orcagnesque Misericordia is the name given by scholars to an anonymous Florentine painter of the later fourteenth century, taken from a painting of the Madonna of Mercy, the Misericordia, in the manner of Andrea Orcagna, the leading Florentine painter and sculptor of the middle of the century. He worked in Orcagna's tradition, with solemn, monumental figures and rich gold. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Crucifix, dated 1370 to 1375, is a painted cross of the type that once hung over a church altar or chancel for all to see, and its Head of Christ is another work attached to his name. Nothing certain is known of his real name, life or patrons, and no more is claimed here.","q40150":"Willem Claesz Heda was born in Haarlem in 1594 and spent his working life there; he is recorded in the painters' guild by the early 1630s. He painted a particular kind of still life, the banquet piece or breakfast piece, in which a table is shown part cleared after a meal: a pewter plate, a silver tazza, a fine glass, oysters, a half-peeled lemon, a pie, a knife left at an angle. His colour is nearly monochrome, silver, grey, olive and gold, tuned with great delicacy, and his pictures give an impression of order even while they suggest a meal interrupted. He and Pieter Claesz were the masters of the type in Haarlem. His son Gerret also painted still lifes. He died in 1680.","q40823":"Giovanni Benedetto Castiglione was born in Genoa in 1609 and worked there, in Rome, Naples and Florence, and finally at Mantua, where he was court painter to the Gonzaga dukes and died in 1664. He was nicknamed Il Grechetto. He painted animals, pastoral scenes with shepherds and flocks, biblical stories such as Noah's Ark and the journeys of the patriarchs, and religious pictures, and he was also a gifted draughtsman and etcher whose manner shows the influence of Van Dyck, Poussin and Rembrandt's prints. He is credited with the invention of the monotype, in which a design painted or drawn on a plate is printed in a single impression. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns his Saint Francis in Ecstasy and a Roman landscape with a shepherd.","q41554":"Nicolas Poussin was born near Les Andelys, in Normandy, in 1594 and went to Paris as a young man. He reached Rome in 1624 and, apart from two unhappy years in Paris from 1640 to 1642, lived there for the rest of his life. His patrons in Rome were collectors and scholars, such as Cassiano dal Pozzo and Cardinal Francesco Barberini, and he painted cabinet pictures of mythology, ancient history and the Bible, composed with great thought, clear drawing and calm, antique-looking figures. Louis XIII and Richelieu summoned him back to Paris to decorate the Louvre's Grande Galerie, but he found the intrigues of the court uncongenial and returned to Rome. His two sets of the Seven Sacraments are among his masterpieces. Later French painters took him as a model of reason in art. He died in Rome in 1665.","q420237":"Pieter van Slingelandt was born in Leiden in 1640 and was a pupil of Gerrit Dou, the founder of the Leiden school of fine painters, whose small pictures are worked up with microscopic care. He followed his master's example, painting genre scenes, portraits and still-life details on a small scale, smooth as enamel and finished in every detail, so slowly that his output was small. His pictures were costly in their day and later fetched high prices in eighteenth-century collections. He spent his life in Leiden and died there in 1691. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's portrait of Johan Hulshout, who lived from 1623 to 1687, was painted about 1670 or slightly later.","q4231486":"Placido Costanzi was born in Rome in 1702 and spent his career there as a painter of altarpieces and religious scenes in the late Baroque style, with luminous colour and graceful, flowing figures. He trained in the Roman school of the early eighteenth century, which looked back to Raphael and the Carracci for models, and he worked for churches in the city. A Miracle of Saint Joseph of Cupertino, of 1750, shows the Franciscan friar who lived from 1603 to 1663 and was famed for his levitations; he was beatified in 1753 and canonised in 1767. Costanzi died in Rome in 1759. Little else is documented of his career in the sources used here, and nothing more is claimed.","q4320785":"Niccolò di Pietro, recorded between 1394 and 1427, was a Venetian painter of the late Gothic period, working at a time when Venetian painting still combined gold grounds, a strong inheritance from Byzantine art, with the elegant line, rich colour and decorative detail of the International Gothic style. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Saint Ursula and Her Maidens, from about 1410, shows the legendary princess who, according to the Golden Legend, was martyred at Cologne together with a great company of virgins; she was one of the most popular saints of the Middle Ages, and the panel shows her with her maidens. Little else is firmly recorded of his life, his training or his patrons, and nothing more is claimed here.","q432266":"Willem van de Velde the Younger was born in Leiden in 1633, the son of the marine draughtsman Willem van de Velde the Elder, and trained with his father and in Weesp with the marine painter Simon de Vlieger. Based in Amsterdam, he painted calm seas, gales and sea battles with great exactness and fine, silvery skies. In about 1672, the year of disasters for the Dutch Republic, father and son moved to England and worked for Charles II and the Duke of York, who paid them annual salaries and gave them a studio in the Queen's House at Greenwich; the father drew the battles and the son painted them. He lived in England for the rest of his life and died in 1707. Often considered the greatest marine painter of the Dutch school, he influenced British marine painting down to Turner.","q433804":"Pieter Jansz. Saenredam was born at Assendelft in 1597 and lived in Haarlem, where he trained with the painter Frans de Grebber and became a pioneer of the church interior as a subject in its own right. He measured and drew buildings on the spot, in pencil and chalk, and returned to the studio to work the drawings into paintings in a cool, light, whitewashed palette, adjusting proportions for effect; the results are among the calmest pictures of the Golden Age. He is not known to have left the Netherlands, and he painted the church of Santa Maria della Febbre in Rome from other artists' prints and drawings. Only a few dozen of his paintings survive. He died in Haarlem in 1665.","q436797":"Samuel van Hoogstraten was born in Dordrecht in 1627 and trained first with his father, then in Amsterdam in the workshop of Rembrandt. He was a restless, many-sided man: he travelled to Vienna, where Emperor Ferdinand III honoured him with a gold chain, and to Rome, and worked in London from 1662 to 1666. He wrote a treatise on painting, Inleyding tot de hooge schoole der schilderkonst, published in 1678, made trompe l'oeil pictures, and built a perspective box, now in the National Gallery in London, which shows the inside of a Dutch house through peepholes. He died in Dordrecht in 1678. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's The Annunciation of the Death of the Virgin dates from about 1670.","q4518312":"Gerret Willemsz Heda was born in Haarlem in 1620, the son of the still-life painter Willem Claesz Heda, and followed his father's example. He painted the same sort of picture: banquet and breakfast pieces with a laid table, silver, pewter, glass and food, a type that Haarlem painters made popular and that gave them a chance to show their skill in rendering reflections, textures and surfaces. The National Gallery of Art's Still Life with Ham, of 1650, gives a ham pride of place on such a table. Gerret died in 1661, aged about forty-one, so his output is smaller than his father's, and his style is closely linked to it. Little else of his life is recorded here, and no more is claimed.","q45431":"William Sidney Mount was born in 1807 at Setauket, on Long Island, and was apprenticed to his elder brother, a sign painter, before studying at the new National Academy of Design in New York in 1826. He returned to Long Island and made his career as a painter of everyday country life: farmers, boys, dances, barn scenes, bargaining and storytelling, treated with humour and without condescension, as in The Raffle of 1837. African Americans appear in some of his best-known pictures, such as The Power of Music, in which a Black man listens at a barn door to the fiddlers inside. Engravings after his work, circulated by the American Art-Union, made his scenes known across the country. He was also a keen violinist and patented a new violin, the Cradle of Harmony, in 1852. He died in 1868.","q454671":"Philips Wouwerman was born in Haarlem in 1619, the son of the painter Paulus Wouwerman, and is said to have learned also from Frans Hals. He joined the Haarlem guild in 1640 and spent his career there, producing hundreds of pictures of horses and the people who rode them: cavalry skirmishes, hunting parties, riders at rest outside inns, horse fairs and peasants with carts, in broad, sunlit landscapes with a luminous, silvery tone. A pale or dappled grey horse often appears in his pictures. His work was highly priced and widely collected in his lifetime and even more so in the eighteenth century, when princes and noble collectors across Europe bought it. He died in Haarlem in 1668. His brothers Pieter and Jan were also painters.","q464898":"Pierre Subleyras was born in 1699 at Saint-Gilles, near Nîmes, and trained at Toulouse and in Paris, where he won the Prix de Rome in 1727. He went to Rome in 1728 and stayed there for the rest of his life, becoming one of the most successful foreign painters in the city. He painted altarpieces, portraits and witty genre scenes, and his religious pictures have a quiet dignity, shaped by classical models and by the Roman Baroque. His portrait of Pope Benedict XIV, painted in 1746, is among his best likenesses, and The Mass of Saint Basil, also of 1746, shows his skill with large religious compositions. He married the miniaturist Maria Felice Tibaldi. He died in Rome in 1749.","q468632":"Mattia Preti, called Il Cavaliere Calabrese, the Calabrian Knight, was born in 1613 at Taverna in Calabria and trained in Rome, where he took in the lessons of Caravaggio's followers and of the Bolognese school, notably Guercino. He worked in Rome, Modena and Naples, where in 1656 he painted frescoes of saints on the city gates as a vow during the plague. In 1661 he went to Malta, was made a Knight of the Order of Saint John and spent the rest of his life there, painting the vault and chapels of Saint John's Co-Cathedral in Valletta, among much else. He died in Valletta in 1699. His painting is dark, strong and dramatic, with powerful figures and bold light. The Martyrdom of Saint Gennaro, of about 1685, shows the patron saint of Naples.","q469056":"Pierre-Paul Prud'hon was born at Cluny in 1758, the youngest of many children of a stonecutter, and was trained in Dijon and then in Paris. The Burgundy estates paid for his study in Rome from 1784 to 1788, where he admired Leonardo and Correggio and met Canova. He developed a soft manner of painting, with melting shadows and a silvery, dreamlike light, and in Revolutionary and Napoleonic Paris he painted allegories, portraits and decorations, and designed for Empress Josephine and later Empress Marie-Louise. His cradle for the King of Rome, the infant son of Napoleon, was made in 1811. He was nicknamed the Correggio of France. His portrait of Talleyrand was painted in 1817. He died in Paris in 1823.","q471387":"Richard Wilson was born at Penegoes, in Montgomeryshire, Wales, in 1713, the son of a clergyman, and went to London as a boy to train under the portrait painter Thomas Wright. He began as a portraitist, but in Italy, where he stayed from about 1750 to 1757, he turned to landscape, encouraged, it was said, by the painters Zuccarelli and Vernet. His views of the Roman Campagna and the lakes of Albano and Nemi, built on Claude and Poussin, made him the first great British landscape painter. He was a founding member of the Royal Academy in 1768, but his patrons were few, and he fell into poverty and was given the post of librarian to the Academy in 1776. Later he returned to Wales and painted views such as Cader Idris. He died in 1782.","q474238":"Hendrik Willem Mesdag was born in Groningen in 1831 into a banking family and worked as a banker until 1866, when he decided to paint full time, learning from Willem Roelofs. He became a leading member of the Hague School, a group of Dutch painters who worked from nature in muted, silvery tones. He specialised in seascapes, above all the North Sea at Scheveningen, with fishing boats, beach and heavy skies, as in his Sunset at Scheveningen of 1894. In 1881 he made, with his wife Sientje Mesdag-van Houten and other painters, the Panorama Mesdag, a vast circular painting of the Scheveningen shore, which can still be seen in The Hague. He also collected French Barbizon and Hague School pictures; his collection is the Museum Mesdag. He died in The Hague in 1915.","q47551":"Titian, Tiziano Vecellio, was born at Pieve di Cadore, in the mountains north of Venice, in the late 1480s or about 1490, and trained in Venice with Giovanni Bellini and worked beside Giorgione. After Bellini's death in 1516 he was the leading painter of the Venetian Republic, and his Assunta in the Frari was the proof. He painted altarpieces, mythologies and portraits with warm, glowing colour and increasingly free, broken brushwork. His patrons were the greatest of Europe: Alfonso d'Este of Ferrara, Federico Gonzaga of Mantua, Pope Paul III and, above all, the Emperor Charles V, who made him a count and a knight in 1533, and Charles's son Philip II of Spain, for whom he painted a series of mythologies he called poesie. He died in Venice in 1576, during an outbreak of plague. Rubens, Velázquez and Rembrandt all learned from him.","q4802575":"Ary de Vois was born in Leiden about 1632 and died there in 1680. He is counted among the Leiden fijnschilders, the fine painters of the circle of Gerrit Dou, who made small pictures with enamel-like smoothness and minute detail for collectors who paid high prices. De Vois painted portraits, genre scenes, pastoral landscapes and mythological subjects, in a polite, elegant manner that suited the taste of the 1660s and 1670s. The National Gallery of Art's The Artist as Lover is dated to about the 1660s. The sources available here do not record his teachers, his patrons or the details of his career, and nothing more is claimed than his dates, his city and his place among the Leiden painters.","q483992":"William Bouguereau was born in La Rochelle in 1825, studied at Bordeaux and then at the École des Beaux-Arts in Paris under François-Édouard Picot, and won the Prix de Rome in 1850. After three years at the Villa Medici he became the most successful academic painter in France, with mythological nudes, religious scenes and idealised peasants, mothers and children, painted with a smooth finish and soft, clear colour, as in Young Mother Gazing at Her Child. He was a member of the Institut and taught for decades at the Académie Julian, where many foreign and women students trained. His work sold well in America through dealers. In 1896 he married his former student, the American painter Elizabeth Jane Gardner. After his death in 1905 his reputation fell with the rise of modern art, and it has since partly revived.","q4888663":"Benjamin Franklin Reinhart was an American painter, born in 1829 and dead in 1885, who painted portraits, history subjects and scenes from everyday life. His An Evening Halt, Emigrants Moving to the West in 1840 was made in 1867, a generation after the westward migrations it recalls and soon after the Civil War, when American painters often looked back to scenes from the pioneer past; it shows a party of settlers who have stopped for the night. Overland emigration to Oregon, California and the western territories was a great national story in the nineteenth century, and pictures of it found ready buyers. Little else of his life can be given with confidence here, and no more is claimed than his dates and this picture show.","q495226":"Andrea di Vanni was a Sienese painter, born about 1330 and dead in 1413, who was also an active public man: he held offices in the Sienese government. He is known as a friend and correspondent of Saint Catherine of Siena, who wrote to him, and a portrait of her in the Dominican church in Siena, ascribed to him, is thought to be the earliest likeness of the saint, painted in her lifetime. His paintings follow the graceful, gold-ground tradition of Sienese art, with delicate colour. The National Gallery of Art's triptych of scenes from Christ's Passion, painted in the 1380s, shows the Agony in the Garden, the Crucifixion and the Descent into Limbo. His career was long, and he was active until the early fifteenth century.","q506483":"Sebastiano Ricci was born at Belluno in 1659 and trained in Venice, then worked in Bologna, Parma, Rome, Milan and Vienna before returning to his base in Venice. He spent the years from 1712 to 1716 in England with his nephew Marco, painting decorations for noble houses and the Resurrection in the apse of the chapel of the Royal Hospital at Chelsea. Back in Venice, he developed a light, warm, glittering manner, based on Veronese, that suited the Rococo and helped to pave the way for the younger Tiepolo. The Memorial to Admiral Sir Clowdisley Shovell was painted in 1725, and the large pictures of 1733 are late works. He died in Venice in 1734. The Last Supper at the National Gallery of Art dates from 1713 to 1714, in his English years.","q5077434":"Charles Ethan Porter was born in Connecticut in 1847 and became one of the few African American painters of his time to make a career from still life. He studied in New York and worked mainly in Connecticut, showing his pictures at the National Academy of Design and in Hartford. He painted fruit and flowers, such as the apples and peonies at the National Gallery of Art, in warm, rich colour and with full, rounded forms; the subjects were the kinds of things a prosperous New England household enjoyed, including fruit from the orchard and flowers from the garden. His work was little noticed for a long period after his death in 1923 and has been rediscovered in recent decades.","q513815":"Wallerant Vaillant was born at Lille in 1623 and died in Amsterdam in 1677. He was a portrait painter and draughtsman, and he is also remembered as one of the earliest practitioners of mezzotint, the tonal printmaking method newly invented in the 1640s, which he used from about 1658 for portraits. He worked in Middelburg and Frankfurt before settling in Amsterdam, and painted trompe l'oeil letter racks as well as portraits. Several of his brothers, among them Bernard, Jacques and Jean, were also artists. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Portrait of a Boy with a Falcon is undated. Beyond these outlines, little of his career can be given with confidence here, and no more is claimed.","q520573":"Giovanni Battista Gaulli, called Il Baciccio, was born in Genoa in 1639 and went to Rome around 1657, where Gian Lorenzo Bernini became his friend and patron. His greatest work is the ceiling of the Jesuit mother church in Rome, Il Gesù, the Triumph of the Name of Jesus, painted between 1672 and 1679, in which painted figures seem to tumble out of the sky over stucco that projects into the nave, a masterpiece of Baroque illusion. He was also an excellent portraitist; his sitters included Bernini and several popes, among them Clement X, whose portrait of about 1670 to 1671 is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He worked mainly in Rome until his death in 1709.","q527599":"William Morris Hunt was born in Brattleboro, Vermont, in 1824 and studied in Düsseldorf and Paris, where he worked with Thomas Couture, before settling at Barbizon in 1853 beside Jean-François Millet, who became his friend and teacher. Back in America in 1855, he brought home both the French painters' soft, tonal manner and their taste for peasant subjects, and he persuaded Boston collectors, such as Quincy Adams Shaw, to buy Millet, which is why Boston holds so many of his pictures. A popular teacher, he ran classes for women artists in Boston, and his Talks on Art appeared in 1875. In 1878 he painted two large murals for the State Capitol at Albany; the Metropolitan Museum of Art owns studies for them. He died in 1879.","q539074":"Herman Saftleven was born in Rotterdam in 1609 into a family of painters and settled in Utrecht in 1632, where he spent the rest of his life. He painted landscapes, especially views of the Rhine valley with castles, villages and winding rivers seen from a height, many of them partly imagined, and wide, hazy scenes in a soft, golden light, as in the Imaginary River Landscape of 1670. He was also a fine draughtsman, and his drawings were much collected. His brother Cornelis was a painter of peasants and fantastic scenes. He died in Utrecht in 1685. Utrecht was a university city with an active painters' guild and a good number of collectors.","q539078":"Gerard Soest was a painter of Netherlandish origin, born about 1600 and dead in 1681, who settled in England by about 1650 and became one of the notable portraitists in London in the years before and during the career of Sir Peter Lely. His portraits are serious and individual, with plain backgrounds, a sober palette and a strong feeling for character rather than fashionable gloss. Among his pupils was John Riley. The National Gallery of Art's Lady Borlase, from about 1672 to 1675, belongs to his later period. He died in London in 1681. Details of his early training and birthplace are not securely recorded, and nothing more is claimed here.","q539786":"Tiberio Tinelli was born in Venice in 1586 and trained under Matteo Ponzone, then spent some years in Rome, where he took in the dramatic light and shadow of Caravaggio's followers. Back in Venice he became the leading portrait painter of the city's ruling families in the 1620s and 1630s, painting procurators, senators, doges and rich merchants in a solid, direct manner that carried on the tradition of Titian and Tintoretto. He is said to have visited the French court in the early 1630s and to have painted Louis XIII and Cardinal Richelieu. He died in Venice in 1638. The National Gallery of Art's portrait of Lodovico Widmann, probably painted in 1637, comes from the last year or so of his life.","q551652":"Mihály Munkácsy was born in 1844 at Munkács, in what was then Hungary, as Michael Lieb, and took the name of his birthplace as an artist. Orphaned young, he was apprenticed to a carpenter before studying painting in Budapest, Vienna, Munich and Düsseldorf, and in 1871 he settled in Paris, where he lived for most of his life. His dark, realistic pictures of poor people, such as The Last Day of a Condemned Man, won him early success, and he later became a society painter, famous for large religious pictures: Christ before Pilate, of 1881, drew huge crowds in Europe and America. His Paris house was a centre of fashionable society. The Music Room, painted in 1878, is a salon interior of this period. His mind failed in his last years, and he died in 1900 near Bonn.","q553186":"Hans Mielich was born in Munich in 1516 and died there in 1573. He is said to have worked in the workshop of Albrecht Altdorfer in Regensburg, and he became the leading painter at the Bavarian court, serving Dukes Wilhelm IV and Albrecht V. He painted portraits, altarpieces and, above all, miniatures. Two works are especially famous: the Jewel Book of Duchess Anna of Bavaria, made in the 1550s, a manuscript with painted pictures of her jewels, and the illuminated copies of the Penitential Psalms of Orlando di Lasso, the court composer, which he made in the 1560s. The portrait at the National Gallery of Art, of a member of the Fröschl family, dates from about 1539 to 1540, when he was in his early twenties.","q555022":"Maarten de Vos was born in Antwerp in 1532 and trained with his father, Pieter de Vos, and then with Frans Floris, the leading Antwerp painter of the day. He went to Italy about 1552 and stayed several years, working in Venice, where he is said to have assisted Tintoretto with landscape backgrounds. Back in Antwerp in 1558, he joined the guild in 1559 and served as its dean in 1572. He painted altarpieces and portraits, but his greatest influence came from his drawings for engravers: hundreds of his designs, of biblical scenes, saints and allegories, were printed by Antwerp publishers and spread his lively compositions across Europe. The Portrait of a Woman Aged Sixty-Eight at the National Gallery of Art dates from 1569. He died in Antwerp in 1603.","q5571":"Andrea del Sarto, whose real name was Andrea d'Agnolo, took his nickname from his father's trade as a tailor, sarto. He was born in Florence in 1486 and trained there, partly in the workshop of Piero di Cosimo. In the 1510s and 1520s he was one of the leading painters of Florence, admired for soft modelling, harmonious, glowing colour and gentle, natural gesture; Vasari called him the faultless painter. His chief works include the grisaille frescoes of the Life of Saint John the Baptist in the Chiostro dello Scalzo and the Madonna of the Harpies. In 1518 he went to France to work for Francis I, and Vasari reports that he lost the king's favour by not returning as promised. Pontormo and Rosso Fiorentino were among his pupils. He died in Florence in 1530.","q5597":"Raphael, Raffaello Sanzio, was born in Urbino in 1483, the son of the court painter Giovanni Santi, and trained with Perugino in Umbria. In 1504 he went to Florence, where he learned from Leonardo and Michelangelo and painted the Madonnas for which he is best loved: gentle, balanced and warm, as in the Alba Madonna and the Cowper Madonnas. In 1508 Pope Julius II called him to Rome, and there he painted the Vatican Stanze, including the School of Athens, designed the tapestry cartoons for the Sistine Chapel for Pope Leo X, and from 1514 was architect of Saint Peter's. His workshop in Rome was large and busy. He also painted fine portraits, such as Bindo Altoviti. He died in Rome in 1520, on his thirty-seventh birthday according to tradition, and was buried in the Pantheon.","q5598":"Rembrandt van Rijn was born in Leiden in 1606, the son of a miller, and studied briefly at the university before training with Jacob van Swanenburgh and then with Pieter Lastman in Amsterdam. He settled in Amsterdam about 1631, and his group portrait The Anatomy Lesson of Dr Nicolaes Tulp, of 1632, made him the city's leading portraitist. He painted portraits, Bible stories, mythology and landscapes, with dramatic light and a deep feeling for character, and his etchings were famous in his lifetime. His pupils included Gerrit Dou, Ferdinand Bol and Govert Flinck. His prosperity ended with the death of his wife Saskia in 1642 and, in 1656, his bankruptcy; he went on painting, in a freer, richer manner, and made some of the great self-portraits. He died in Amsterdam in 1669.","q5599":"Peter Paul Rubens was born in 1577 at Siegen, in Germany, where his Antwerp family was living in exile, and grew up in Antwerp, where he trained with Tobias Verhaecht, Adam van Noort and Otto van Veen. From 1600 to 1608 he worked in Italy, mostly for the Duke of Mantua, and in 1603 went on a diplomatic mission to Spain. Back in Antwerp he became court painter to the Archdukes Albert and Isabella and ran a large workshop that turned out altarpieces, portraits, mythologies and hunting scenes with great energy and sensual colour. His cycle for Marie de' Medici is in the Louvre. He was also a diplomat, knighted by both Philip IV of Spain and Charles I of England, for whom he painted the ceiling of the Banqueting House in London. He died in Antwerp in 1640.","q561916":"Henry Raeburn was born at Stockbridge, near Edinburgh, in 1756 and was apprenticed to a goldsmith, learning to paint largely by himself, beginning with miniatures. After a marriage that brought him some wealth, he spent about two years in Italy in the mid-1780s, then returned to Edinburgh and became Scotland's leading portrait painter, painting lawyers, professors, soldiers, landowners and Highland chiefs in a bold, broad manner, with strong light on the faces and direct, unflattering likenesses. He also painted children, such as the Drummond Children at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. In 1822 he was knighted by George IV during the king's visit to Edinburgh, and in 1823 he was named the King's Limner for Scotland. He died in Edinburgh the same year.","q572743":"Michael Dahl was born in Stockholm in 1659 and trained there under the court painter David Klöcker Ehrenstrahl before going to London in 1682. He then travelled in France and Italy, and was back in London by 1689, where he remained one of the leading portrait painters for more than three decades. He was the chief rival of Godfrey Kneller, and he found favour at the court of Queen Anne, painting her and her husband, Prince George of Denmark. His likenesses are gentler and more modest than Kneller's grander style. He died in London in 1743. The Metropolitan Museum of Art owns his Portrait of a Woman, whose sitter is not named, a reminder that many portraits of the time lost their identities as they passed out of the families that commissioned them.","q576860":"The Master of the Story of Joseph is the name given by scholars to an anonymous painter of the late fifteenth or early sixteenth century in the southern Netherlands, taken from pictures that tell the story of Joseph from the Book of Genesis. The tale of Joseph, sold by his brothers, imprisoned in Egypt and raised to high office through his skill in interpreting dreams, was popular in the late Middle Ages and was often shown in a series, with the costume and settings of the painter's own day. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's panel shows Joseph interpreting the dreams of his fellow prisoners. The painter's real name, his training and his dates are not known, and nothing more is claimed here.","q577842":"Pieter Claesz. Soutman was born in Haarlem and died there in 1657. He is said to have worked in Rubens's circle in Antwerp, where he made engravings after the master's pictures, helping to spread Rubens's compositions. He is also recorded as working for the king of Poland in the 1620s before returning to Haarlem, where he painted portraits and went on making prints. His portraits are dignified and plainly painted, using Rubens's example of pose and drapery in a form restrained for Dutch taste. The National Gallery of Art's A Young Man Holding a Staff is dated 1640. Details of his life beyond these outlines are not well documented in the sources used here, and no more is claimed.","q5827":"Perugino, Pietro Vannucci, was born at Città della Pieve in Umbria about 1450 and takes his name from Perugia, where he worked. He trained in Florence, in the workshop of Verrocchio, where Lorenzo di Credi was a fellow pupil; Perugino painted his portrait in 1488. In 1481 and 1482 he painted frescoes in the Sistine Chapel in Rome, among them Christ Giving the Keys to Saint Peter. His style is serene and lucid, with wide, clear space, balanced figures with sweetly tilted heads and soft colour; it was widely imitated, and Raphael began in his circle. His reputation was very high around 1500 and then declined, as his repeated formulas fell out of fashion. He died of plague at Fontignano in 1523.","q585067":"Paul Ranson was born in Limoges in 1864 and studied in Paris, at the Académie Julian, where he became a close friend of Paul Sérusier and Maurice Denis and joined the Nabis, the group of young painters who followed Gauguin's call for flat colour and decorative design. He painted and designed in a decorative, linear, Symbolist style, inspired by Japanese prints and Art Nouveau, producing pictures, tapestry designs, screens and posters. His Paris studio, called the Temple, was the Nabis' Saturday meeting place. In 1908 he and his wife France founded the Académie Ranson, where Sérusier and Denis taught; he died in 1909, and she kept it going. His Woman Standing Beside a Balustrade with a Poodle, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, dates from about 1895.","q590969":"Gian Antonio Guardi, who lived from 1699 to 1761, was the elder brother of Francesco Guardi and for many years head of the family workshop in Venice, which he took over after their father's death in 1716. He painted altarpieces, figure compositions and religious scenes in a light, sketchy Rococo manner with flickering brushwork; Francesco worked in the workshop and later became the famous painter of Venetian views. Their sister Cecilia married Giambattista Tiepolo in 1719. The picture at the National Gallery of Art, of about 1750 to 1755, shows a scene from Tasso's Jerusalem Delivered, in which the knights Carlo and Ubaldo resist the enchantments of Armida's nymphs. Attributions within the Guardi workshop are still debated, and works by the two brothers are not always easy to tell apart.","q591684":"Claude Vignon was born in Tours in 1593 and went to Italy as a young man, spending several years in Rome, where he studied the work of Caravaggio and his followers and the small, glowing pictures of Adam Elsheimer. He returned to Paris about 1624 and became one of the busiest and most inventive painters in France, painting religious subjects, historical scenes and portraits, and making many etchings. His style is quick and lively, with rich, strong colour, free brushwork and costumes with a touch of the exotic. Mary Magdalene Comforted by an Angel, of 1626, belongs to the first years after his return. He died in Paris in 1670, and his reputation has been rising since the later twentieth century after a long period in which he was little known.","q595150":"Peter Wtewael was born in Utrecht in 1596, the son of the painter Joachim Wtewael, the city's leading Mannerist, and learned to paint in his father's workshop. Like his father, he painted both religious subjects and kitchen scenes, a type of picture that goes back to the Flemish painters Pieter Aertsen and Joachim Beuckelaer, in which food, fowl and utensils take the front of the picture. The Denial of Peter, at the Cleveland Museum of Art, treats a subject that the Utrecht followers of Caravaggio often painted, and the Kitchen Scene at the Metropolitan Museum of Art dates from the 1620s. He died in Utrecht in 1660. Little more is securely recorded of his career, and nothing further is claimed here.","q596352":"Peeter Neeffs the Elder was born in Antwerp about 1578 and worked there all his life, becoming one of the first and most successful painters of church interiors in the southern Netherlands. His subject was the Gothic cathedral of Antwerp and other Flemish churches, drawn in clear perspective with great care for the architecture, in small, jewel-like pictures on panel or copper, often showing the same view by day and by night, as in the pair at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The small figures in such pictures were commonly added by specialist figure painters. His son Pieter the Younger continued the business. He was still living in the 1650s and may have died as late as 1661. The National Gallery of Art owns his Antwerp Cathedral.","q60099":"Willem Drost was born in Amsterdam in 1633 and studied in the workshop of Rembrandt, probably in the early 1650s, working so closely in his style that some of his pictures have been taken for Rembrandt's own. He painted half-length figures, portraits and biblical and historical subjects with rich colour and broad, flowing brushwork. About 1654 he left the Netherlands for Italy and settled in Venice, where he was in touch with the German painter Johann Carl Loth, and his work moved toward the warm, dark colour of Venetian painting, as in The Sibyl. He died in Venice in 1659, aged only twenty-six, which explains why his work is rare. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Portrait of a Man, dated 1653 or 1655, may be a self-portrait.","q604107":"Wilhelm von Kaulbach was born at Arolsen in 1804 and studied at Düsseldorf under Peter Cornelius, whom he followed to Munich in 1826. He became one of the leading German history painters of the century, painting vast, learned compositions on historical and philosophical themes, such as the Destruction of Jerusalem and the cycle of murals on the stairway of the Neues Museum in Berlin, which presents the great epochs in the history of civilisation. He was also a well-known illustrator, especially of Goethe's Reineke Fuchs, and in 1849 succeeded Cornelius as director of the Munich Academy. His Crusaders before Jerusalem is at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He died in Munich in 1874. His reputation, high in his lifetime, fell in the twentieth century.","q6119594":"Jacob van Walscapelle was born in 1644 and died in 1727, and worked in Amsterdam as a painter of fruit and flowers in the tradition of the great still-life painter Jan Davidsz. de Heem. Such pictures were prized as displays of fine technique and as reminders of nature's abundance, and sometimes of its transience; they were made for the well-off townspeople and merchants who could afford to hang them in their houses. His Still Life with Fruit, dated 1675, is at the National Gallery of Art. He lived to be more than eighty, a long career that carried his late seventeenth-century style into the eighteenth century. Few details of his training or patrons can be given with confidence here, and nothing more is claimed.","q6139752":"James Millar, who lived from 1740 to 1805, was a British portrait painter, and the National Gallery of Art owns a pair of his portraits, Lord Algernon Percy and Lady Algernon Percy, painted about 1777 to 1780. The courtesy title Lord Algernon suggests a younger son of a duke, and the Percys were the family of the Dukes of Northumberland. Pairs of portraits of husband and wife, painted to hang together, were a standard commission in the period and gave painters steady work. Beyond his dates, little of Millar's training, career or patrons can be given with confidence here, and nothing more is claimed.","q6148705":"Tomás de Merlo was born in 1694 and died in 1739, and worked in Guatemala, in what is now Antigua Guatemala, then the colonial capital and one of the richest artistic centres of Central America, with many churches and convents that needed altarpieces and paintings. He is counted among the leading Guatemalan painters of the early eighteenth century. Colonial painters often worked from European prints, in a Baroque style with strong colour and emotion, and the Lamentation over the Dead Christ at the National Gallery of Art, painted in the 1730s, is an example of the devotional art of the region. Little else of his life can be given with confidence here, and no more is claimed.","q6262998":"John Ward was born in Hull in 1798 and is known as John Ward of Hull to tell him from other painters of the same name. Largely self-taught, he worked in his home port as a marine painter, making ship portraits for owners and captains, views of the busy Humber docks, and scenes of the Arctic whale fishery, then an important trade of Hull. The Northern Whale Fishery: The Swan and Isabella, of about 1840, shows two whaling ships at work in northern waters. He painted the ships and the whaling with a seaman's care for rigging and detail, and his work is well represented in Hull's museums. He died in Hull in 1849.","q6288081":"Joseph Wright was born in 1756 at Bordentown, New Jersey, the son of Patience Wright, a celebrated modeller of wax portraits who took her family to London in the 1770s. There he studied painting, and he also visited Paris. He returned to America in 1782 and painted George Washington from life in 1783, a portrait that was widely copied and engraved. Wright took up engraving and in 1793 worked on dies for the new United States Mint, but he died that autumn in Philadelphia of yellow fever, aged thirty-seven. His career was cut short. The National Gallery of Art's portrait of Elizabeth Stevens Carle dates from about 1783 to 1784.","q632385":"The Master of the Saint Bartholomew Altar is the name given to a German painter active in Cologne from about 1480 to 1510, taken from his altarpiece of Saint Bartholomew, painted for a church in the city around 1500 to 1505 and now in the Alte Pinakothek in Munich. He is regarded as the most important Cologne painter of his time. His work shows strong Netherlandish influence, in its sharp detail, rich, deep colour and closely observed faces, and it is thought that he trained in the Low Countries. He painted altarpieces and portraits for the churches and patrician families of Cologne, then one of the largest cities in the Empire. The National Gallery of Art's Baptism of Christ is dated between about 1485 and 1500. His real name is not securely known.","q632846":"Nicolaes Eliasz. Pickenoy was born in Amsterdam in 1588, studied with Cornelis van der Voort and became one of the city's leading portraitists in the 1620s and 1630s, the years before Rembrandt arrived. He painted regents, merchants and their wives in a cool, precise, rather formal style, and he painted civic guard group portraits. His clients were the Amsterdam elite. After 1631 Rembrandt and his imitators drew some of the fashionable custom away, and Pickenoy's reputation faded; he is still valued for his sober, accurate likenesses. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Man with a Celestial Globe, of 1624, shows a sitter whose globe suggests an interest in the stars or in navigation. Pickenoy died about 1650.","q633742":"Michele Desubleo, born Michel Desoubleay at Maubeuge in 1602, was a Flemish painter who made his career in Italy. He worked in Rome in the 1620s, then in Bologna, where he became the pupil and assistant of Guido Reni and learned his graceful figures and clear, bright colour, adding to it a touch of Caravaggio's naturalism. From the 1650s he worked in Parma, for the court of the Farnese, and he died there in 1676. His paintings of biblical and classical heroines, saints and allegories, in a polished, elegant style, were popular with Italian collectors. Allegory of Sacred and Profane Love, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, is a late work, painted about 1665 to 1675.","q63486571":"David Norslup is a painter about whom little is known: the National Gallery of Art credits him with Negro Boys on the Quayside, of about 1865, and gives his nationality as American and his dates as 1850 to 1880. If those are his years of birth and death, he painted the picture in his teens, and the record leaves much unclear. The title preserves the language of its time; today the picture would be described as a scene of Black boys on a quay. Harbours and quaysides, with working people and children, were popular subjects for nineteenth-century genre painters. Nothing certain about his training, travels or patrons is recorded here, and nothing more is claimed.","q64881":"Ridolfo Ghirlandaio was born in Florence in 1483, the son of the famous painter Domenico Ghirlandaio, who died when the boy was a child. He grew up in the family workshop and, according to Vasari, learned from Fra Bartolomeo and was a friend of Raphael; in time he took over the workshop and ran it for decades. He painted altarpieces, Madonnas and portraits, in a clear, balanced style that mixes his father's tradition with the manner of the High Renaissance. His portrait of Lucrezia Sommaria, of about 1510, is at the National Gallery of Art. He died in Florence in 1561. He is often overshadowed by his father, but he is seen as a steady, capable master of the Florentine tradition.","q650080":"William Hart was born in Paisley, Scotland, in 1823 and was brought to the United States as a child, settling in Albany, New York, where he trained as a coach painter before taking up landscape. He became a painter of the later Hudson River school, known for tranquil views of woods, meadows and streams, often with cattle, in the Catskills, the Adirondacks and the Berkshires. His brother James McDougal Hart was also a well-known landscape painter. He moved to New York City in the 1850s, where he became a member of the National Academy of Design. He died in Mount Vernon, New York, in 1894. His Autumn Scene in the Adirondacks and Seashore, Morning are among the works of his held by American museums.","q65317":"Godfrey Kneller was born in Lübeck in 1646 as Gottfried Kniller and trained in the Netherlands, in Leiden and Amsterdam, before going to Italy in 1672. He came to England in 1676 and, after the death of Sir Peter Lely in 1680, became the leading court portraitist, serving Charles II, James II, William and Mary, Anne and George I; he was knighted in 1692 and made a baronet in 1715. He ran a very large studio, with assistants painting drapery and backgrounds, and produced hundreds of portraits in a stately, flattering manner. His best-known series is the portraits of the members of the Kit-Cat Club, painted between about 1700 and 1717, whose canvas size gave us the term kit-cat. He died in London in 1723.","q671883":"Théo van Rysselberghe was born in Ghent in 1862 and studied at the Brussels academy. He travelled in Spain and Morocco, and became a member of Les XX, the Brussels group that invited the leading avant-garde artists of Europe to exhibit. When Georges Seurat's A Sunday on La Grande Jatte was shown with Les XX in 1887, Rysselberghe was among those who took up the new method of painting in separate dots of pure colour, and from about 1889 he was the leading Neo-Impressionist in Belgium. He lived mostly in Paris and, after 1911, in the south of France. He was also a fine portraitist; his Denise Maréchal, of about 1894, is at the National Gallery of Art. He died in 1926.","q673615":"Pieter Snayers, born at Antwerp about 1592 and dead at Brussels in 1667, was a Flemish painter of battles, sieges and cavalry skirmishes, and one of the leading battle painters of the southern Netherlands. He trained with Sebastiaen Vrancx, himself a painter of soldiers and campaigns, and entered the service of the governors of the Spanish Netherlands, the Archduchess Isabella and later Archduke Leopold Wilhelm, for whom he painted panoramic views of recent battles and sieges in the long wars of the age, such as the Battle of the White Mountain. His pictures combine accurate topography with careful, convincing detail of troops and camps, and they were valued as records as well as works of art. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Soldiers Bivouacking is a camp scene.","q677922":"Nicolaes van Veerendael was born in Antwerp in 1640 and died there in 1691. He was a painter of flower still lifes. His pictures show bouquets in glass vases, and sometimes wreaths of flowers around a framed image, painted in a clear, bright palette with fine detail, with here and there an insect or a dewdrop. He belongs to the late generation of Antwerp flower painters who worked in the city's established tradition of such pictures, which found buyers among well-off townspeople and churchmen. A Bouquet of Flowers in a Crystal Vase, of 1662, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, was painted when he was in his early twenties. Not much else about his life, his teachers or his patrons is recorded here, and no more is claimed.","q6786928":"Mather Brown was born in Boston in 1761 and went to London in 1781, where he became a pupil of Benjamin West and exhibited at the Royal Academy, one of the first Americans to build a career in the English capital. He painted history pictures, which were his ambition, and many portraits: Americans passing through London, such as John Adams and Thomas Jefferson, and British sitters. His portrait of General George Eliott, Lord Heathfield, the defender of Gibraltar, dates from 1790. He had a successful career in the 1780s and 1790s, but his fortunes declined in the new century, and he died in London in 1831. The National Gallery of Art owns his portraits of William Vans Murray, an American diplomat, and of Viscount Cremorne.","q6791089":"Matthew Pratt was born in Philadelphia in 1734 and trained in the workshop of his uncle, a painter and glazier. In 1764 he sailed to London to study with a fellow Philadelphian, Benjamin West, with whom he lived, and in 1765 he painted The American School, a picture of West teaching a group of young Americans in his studio, now at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. He returned to Philadelphia in 1768 and painted portraits and signs, and later worked in New York. His portraits of colonial sitters, such as Cadwallader Colden and Mrs. Peter De Lancey, are straightforward likenesses in the colonial manner of the years before the Revolution. He died in 1805.","q68631":"Rogier van der Weyden was born at Tournai in 1399, trained in the workshop of Robert Campin there and became a master in 1432. About 1435 he moved to Brussels, where he was appointed city painter and became the most famous painter in the Netherlands. His Descent from the Cross, now in the Prado, set a standard for emotional intensity, with figures in tightly composed, sculptural poses and faces wet with tears. He painted altarpieces, devotional panels and portraits for the Burgundian court and for rich merchants, such as the portrait of Francesco d'Este, an Italian prince who lived at the Burgundian court, in about 1460. He visited Italy in the Jubilee year of 1450, and his reputation spread through Europe. He died in Brussels in 1464.","q704661":"Wilhelm Leibl was born in Cologne in 1844 and studied at the Munich Academy. In 1869 the French painter Gustave Courbet visited Munich for an international exhibition and admired Leibl's portrait of Frau Gedon; Leibl went to Paris and stayed until the Franco-Prussian War forced him home in 1870, and he became the leading German Realist. From 1873 he lived in remote Bavarian villages, painting peasants with intense, patient honesty. His largest work, Three Women in a Village Church, painted between 1878 and 1882 and now in Hamburg, took years of labour. He was a solitary man who avoided the art world. Peasant Girl with a White Headcloth, at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, is a work of 1885. He died in Würzburg in 1900.","q706041":"Théophile-Alexandre Steinlen was born in Lausanne in 1859 and settled in Paris in 1881, living in Montmartre, the home of artists, cabarets and anarchist politics. He became one of the great graphic artists of his time, drawing for magazines such as Gil Blas and L'Assiette au Beurre and making posters, the best known of which, advertising the tour of the cabaret Le Chat Noir in 1896, shows a black cat. His subjects were the Parisian working poor: laundresses, street children and workmen, and he drew cats all his life. His socialist sympathies show in his sympathetic, unsentimental treatment of such figures. The Laundresses, of 1899, is at the National Gallery of Art. He died in Paris in 1923.","q706519":"Domenico Tintoretto was born in Venice about 1560, the son of the great Jacopo Tintoretto, and trained in his father's workshop, working beside his father and his brother Marco. After his father's death in 1594 he led the workshop, completing unfinished commissions and repeating the well-known compositions. He painted large religious and historical pictures for churches and public buildings in Venice, but he was better known as a portraitist, painting Venetian senators, procurators and doges in sober, dignified likenesses. His Susanna, of the 1580s, at the National Gallery of Art, shows the biblical heroine of the story of Susanna and the Elders. He died in Venice in 1635. His name is often overshadowed by his father's.","q706913":"Sebastian Stoskopff was born in Strasbourg in 1597 and trained in Hanau, near Frankfurt. He spent several years in Paris and then returned to Strasbourg, and later served the Count of Hanau-Lichtenberg; he died in 1657. He painted still lifes of glasses, shells, pastries, books and small objects, with a restrained, cool palette and an unusual, almost dreamlike clarity; his pictures of glassware, shells and baskets are among the most original of their time. Little known in later centuries, he was rediscovered in the twentieth century, and exhibitions have since made his name. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Still Life with Shells and a Chip-Wood Box dates from the late 1620s.","q707046":"Piero del Pollaiuolo, whose full name was Piero di Jacopo Benci, was born in Florence about 1441 and worked in the workshop he shared with his older brother Antonio, the more famous of the two. The brothers made altarpieces, paintings, embroideries and metalwork; the best known of Piero's commissions is the set of Virtues painted in 1469 and 1470 for the tribunal of the Mercanzia in Florence, to which Botticelli added Fortitude. Piero and Antonio painted the altarpiece in the Cardinal of Portugal's chapel in San Miniato al Monte, between 1466 and 1468, and Piero signed alone the Coronation of the Virgin at San Gimignano in 1483. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Portrait of a Woman is dated about 1480. He died in 1496.","q708036":"Matthijs Naiveu was born in Leiden in 1647 and belongs to the Leiden school of fine painters in the tradition of Gerrit Dou, who painted small pictures with great care and finish. According to early biographers, he learned from several Leiden masters, among them Abraham van den Tempel, Gabriel Metsu and Dou. He painted small genre scenes, portraits and some history pictures, with a taste for sentiment, comedy and anecdote. He lived to be nearly eighty and died in Amsterdam in 1726. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's The Newborn Baby, of 1675, is a domestic subject of the kind that Leiden painters made popular. Few other details of his career are given in the sources used here, and no more is claimed.","q711560":"Priamo della Quercia, also called Priamo del Pietro, was a Sienese painter and illuminator, a younger brother of the sculptor Jacopo della Quercia, and worked in Siena in the first half of the fifteenth century. He is best known as an illuminator: he is credited with some of the illustrations, shared with Giovanni di Paolo, of a famous manuscript of Dante's Divine Comedy made in Siena in the 1440s. He also painted altarpieces; the Metropolitan Museum of Art's Madonna and Child with Saints is dated about 1442. Sienese painters of this time kept the gold grounds and elegant line of Gothic painting alongside new ideas from Florence. His dates of birth and death are not given in the sources used here.","q7147317":"Patrick Nasmyth was born in Edinburgh in 1787, the son of Alexander Nasmyth, the founder of Scottish landscape painting, and moved to London in 1807, where he became known for woodland and farmland scenes in the manner of the Dutch landscape painters, particularly Meindert Hobbema, so that he was nicknamed the English Hobbema. His views of rural England, with winding lanes, cottages, farm carts, pools and big trees, were popular. His brother James became famous as an engineer and the inventor of the steam hammer. Patrick died in London in 1831. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Near Penshurst, Kent, dated 1828, is a late work.","q717097":"Francesco Granacci was born in Florence in 1469 and trained in the workshop of Domenico Ghirlandaio, where he was a fellow apprentice of the young Michelangelo; Vasari tells that Granacci befriended the boy and helped him. He painted altarpieces, Madonnas and scenes from the Bible and saints' lives, in a pleasant, clear style that follows Ghirlandaio and shows traces of Michelangelo and Fra Bartolomeo. He also designed festival decorations for the Medici, and painted panels with the story of Joseph for the Borgherini family. He lived in Florence all his life and died there in 1543. His Scenes from the Life of Saint John the Baptist at the Metropolitan Museum of Art date from about 1506 to 1507.","q722912":"Willem van de Velde the Elder was born in Leiden in 1611 and worked in Amsterdam as a painter of ships, the first of a family of marine artists. He is best known for his pen paintings, large, detailed drawings in black ink on panel or canvas, showing fleets and sea battles with great exactness, and for the drawings he made at sea; he is said to have followed the Dutch fleet in a small boat during the wars with England, to sketch the actions as they happened. In 1672 he moved to England with his son Willem the Younger and worked for Charles II and the Duke of York; he died in London in 1693. The National Gallery of Art's Dutch Ships near the Coast dates from the early 1650s.","q724508":"Stefano da Verona, whose full name was Stefano di Giovanni d'Arbosio di Francia, which suggests that his father came from France, was born in the 1370s and was recorded until the late 1430s. He was one of the leading Italian painters of the International Gothic style, with its elegant, curving line, rich, flowery colour and love of detail, and he worked in Verona and, it is believed, in other cities of northern Italy. His best-known picture, the Madonna of the Rose Garden, shows the Virgin and Child in an enclosed garden of roses and birds, and is in Verona. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Crucifixion is dated to about 1400. He is also called Stefano da Zevio.","q7287448":"Ralph Eleazer Whiteside Earl was born in 1788, the son of the portrait painter Ralph Earl, and is said to have studied in England with Benjamin West. He is known above all as the portraitist of Andrew Jackson: he became a member of Jackson's household in Tennessee, married a niece of Jackson's wife Rachel, and after Jackson was elected President lived at the White House as his friend and painter, producing many portraits of him. He died in Nashville in 1838. The Metropolitan Museum of Art's Mrs. Williams, of 1837, is a late work, and the Family Portrait at the National Gallery of Art is dated 1804. Beyond these outlines, little of his career can be given with confidence here.","q7324981":"Richard Crosse was born in 1742 at Knowle, near Cullompton in Devon, and was deaf from birth. He went to London as a young man to study and made his career as a miniature painter, working in watercolour on ivory. His small, delicately stippled likenesses of fashionable sitters were shown at the Society of Artists and the Royal Academy, and he was appointed miniature painter to the King. Crosse stood just below the leaders of the English miniature, such as Richard Cosway and Jeremiah Meyer, and his name is now known chiefly to collectors of miniatures and to historians of deaf artists. The Met's Portrait of a Woman, possibly of about 1780, comes from the middle of his career. He died in 1810.","q7327902":"Richard Morrell Staigg was born in Leeds, England, in 1817 and was brought as a boy to Newport, Rhode Island, where he grew up and where he died in 1881. He began as a painter of miniatures in watercolour on ivory and found his sitters among Newport and Boston families; one of them was the painter Washington Allston, whom he portrayed in 1841. His likenesses are small, fresh in colour and carefully drawn, and he was counted among the best American miniaturists of his day. As photography took over the trade in small portraits he turned increasingly to oils, painting portraits and figure subjects. He exhibited at the National Academy of Design, and he is now valued chiefly for his miniatures.","q7344323":"Robert Field was born in England in 1769, probably at Gloucester, and trained as a miniature painter, though little is recorded of his early teachers. He crossed to the United States in the 1790s and worked in Baltimore, Philadelphia and New York, painting watercolour miniatures on ivory and a smaller number of oil portraits for merchants and officials. The Met holds several of these, among them the Baltimore merchant Robert Gilmor Sr. (1804) and a portrait of George Washington (about 1800). In 1808 he moved to Halifax, Nova Scotia, where he painted leading citizens of the colony, and in 1816 he went on to Jamaica, where he died in 1819. His likenesses are crisp and carefully drawn, if somewhat dry, and he is remembered as a capable travelling professional and a record of the early Republic's sitters.","q7346973":"Robert Loftin Newman was born in Richmond, Virginia, in 1827 and grew up in Tennessee. He went to Paris about 1850 and studied with Thomas Couture, but the artists who shaped him were colourists: he looked to Titian and Giorgione and to the glowing colour of Delacroix. Back in America he settled in New York and painted small, low-toned pictures of biblical, mythological and literary subjects, such as Hagar and Ishmael, the Madonna and Child and the Fortune Teller, in rich, dark, jewel-like colour. He sold few pictures and lived modestly, supported by a small circle of admirers. His reputation grew after his death, when his work was grouped with that of Albert Pinkham Ryder and Ralph Blakelock as American Romantic painting. He died in New York in 1912.","q736207":"Louis Le Nain was born at Laon in Picardy around 1600, the exact year being uncertain, and worked in Paris with his brothers Antoine and Mathieu, who also painted. The three kept a joint workshop, signed only Le Nain, and are hard to tell apart; Louis is usually credited with the finest peasant scenes. These are quiet, grey-brown pictures of farm families at table, in barns or in the fields, painted with a gravity unusual in French art of the time. Antoine and Louis were among the founder members of the Académie royale in 1648, and both died within days of each other that May. Largely forgotten for two centuries, the brothers were revived in 1850 by the critic Champfleury and admired by the Realists, and Louis is now counted among the great French painters of the reign of Louis XIII.","q736843":"Willard Leroy Metcalf was born in Lowell, Massachusetts, in 1858 and trained in Boston, first with the landscape painter George Loring Brown and then at the Museum of Fine Arts school. In 1883 he went to Paris and studied at the Académie Julian, and in the late 1880s he worked at Giverny among the American painters near Claude Monet. Back in America he taught and exhibited, and in 1897 he was a founder member of the Ten American Painters, the group that showed Impressionist work apart from the larger academies. His best pictures, of New England hills, streams and snow, painted in clear, high-keyed colour and often at Old Lyme, Connecticut, brought him prizes and steady sales, and he now ranks among the leading American Impressionists. He died in New York in 1925.","q737726":"Robert Peake the Elder was born about 1551 and worked in London, where little is recorded of his training. He was one of the leading portrait painters of the reign of James I, working between the world of Nicholas Hilliard and the arrival of Van Dyck. By the early 1600s he was painting for the young Henry, Prince of Wales, whose likeness he painted again and again. The Met's picture of 1603 shows the nine-year-old prince in the hunting field with his companion Sir John Harington, and the picture of about 1606 shows the prince's sister Elizabeth, later Queen of Bohemia. Peake's manner is flat, linear and precise, rich in jewels, lace and embroidery, and descended from Elizabethan court portraiture. He died in 1619, and is valued today as a record of Jacobean costume and of the royal children.","q740231":"Taddeo di Bartolo was born in Siena about 1362, the son of a barber, and worked in Siena and in many other towns, among them San Gimignano, Pisa, Genoa, Perugia and Montepulciano. His training is not securely recorded, though his early work shows the influence of Bartolo di Fredi, and he stands between the heirs of Simone Martini and the Lorenzetti and the early Renaissance. He painted large altarpieces and fresco cycles, including, in 1406-08, the frescoes of the chapel of Siena's town hall, the Palazzo Pubblico, and a few years later its heroes of antiquity. His colour is bright and his figures lively, with a taste for narrative detail. Busy and well regarded in his lifetime, he is now counted among the leading Sienese painters of his generation. He died in 1422; the Met's Christ and the Twelve Apostles dates from about 1400.","q740592":"Vladimir Egorovich Makovsky was born in Moscow in 1846 into a family of artists: his father Egor helped to found the Moscow art school, and his elder brother Konstantin became a fashionable society and history painter. He studied at the Moscow School of Painting, Sculpture and Architecture, where the genre painter Vasily Perov was a strong influence, and in 1872 he joined the Peredvizhniki, the Wanderers, who sent travelling exhibitions to Russian provincial towns. His pictures are anecdotal scenes of ordinary city life, with clerks, pedlars, street musicians, small merchants and the poor, told with humour and sympathy; Bank Failure and The Party are among the best known. He later taught at the Imperial Academy of Arts in St Petersburg and died in 1920. Admired in his day for their humane wit, his pictures remain popular in Russia, though less known in the West.","q7410252":"Sampson Towgood Roch was an Irish miniature painter, born in 1759 and dead in 1847. Little is recorded of his early life or training, and what is secure rests mainly on his surviving work, which was painted in watercolour on ivory in the years around 1790. The Met's Portrait of a Girl is one of these: a likeness only a few inches high, of the kind made to be worn in a locket, kept in a case or exchanged between families and friends before photography replaced the trade. Miniaturists of his generation worked for a middle-class market that wanted a faithful and pleasing face at modest cost. Roch's reputation is that of a competent specialist, known mostly to collectors of miniatures.","q741062":"Paolo Veneziano is the first Venetian painter of whom a real body of signed and documented work survives, with records running from 1333 to 1358. Almost nothing is known of his birth or teachers. His art joins the gold-ground, solemn tradition of Byzantine icons, which Venice knew well through its eastern trade, with the softer drawing, rich fabrics and courtly grace of Gothic art. His most celebrated commission was the Pala Feriale of 1345 for St Mark's Basilica, a set of painted panels made for Doge Andrea Dandolo to cover the Pala d'Oro on ordinary days, which he signed together with his sons Luca and Giovanni. He also painted altarpieces and small devotional panels such as the Crucifixion in the National Gallery of Art. His sons carried on the workshop, and his manner set the pattern for Venetian painting for a generation.","q7411149":"Samuel Collins is recorded only as a British painter of the eighteenth century, and the Met gives no dates for him. A miniature painter named Samuel Collins is recorded in Bath and then Dublin in the 1760s, and he may be the man intended, but the identification is unproven. The name is a common one among minor painters, and no pupil, patron or famous commission can be tied to this Portrait of a Man with confidence. Pictures of this kind were made for private families rather than public display: close, small likenesses of husbands, brothers and friends, kept as keepsakes. Museums now prize them mostly as evidence of dress, manners and the portrait trade in Britain, rather than as the work of a famous hand.","q7411168":"Samuel Cotes was born in London in 1734, the younger brother of Francis Cotes, the leading pastel portraitist of the day, who taught him. He painted portrait miniatures in watercolour on ivory, in a delicate, pearly manner, and exhibited them at the Society of Artists and the Royal Academy. His sitters were fashionable and professional people of London and the country, and the Met's Portrait of a Woman, dated 1767, comes from the busiest part of his career. He worked in the same years as Richard Cosway and Ozias Humphry, and although he never matched Cosway's fame he was a well-respected London miniaturist. He lived to a great age and died in 1818; today his work is chiefly found in museum collections of miniatures.","q7412607":"Samuel Shelley was born in London about 1756 and worked there as a miniature painter and watercolourist. He began to exhibit at the Royal Academy in the 1770s and made his living in the fashionable portrait trade, but he also painted small classical and literary subjects, among them the allegory The Hours of 1801, now in the Met. In 1804 he was a founder member of the Society of Painters in Water Colours, the first body in Britain devoted to watercolour, a sign of how far the medium had risen in esteem during his lifetime. His reputation was that of a graceful, accomplished minor master. He died in 1808, and his miniatures are now seen mostly in museums and private collections.","q7422343":"Sarah Goodridge was born in 1788 in Templeton, Massachusetts, and was largely self-taught. She moved to Boston about 1820 and made her living painting miniatures on ivory, and Gilbert Stuart took an interest in her work and sat for her in 1825; her portrait of him is among the most reproduced likenesses of the painter. Her sitters were Boston professional and merchant families, and her miniatures are known for firm drawing and fresh colour. In 1828 she painted Beauty Revealed, a small self-portrait showing her bare breasts, and sent it to Daniel Webster, recently widowed; he kept it until his death. She never married, worked into the 1850s and died in Boston in 1853, and she is now regarded as one of the finest American miniaturists of her day.","q748491":"Nardo di Cione was a Florentine painter, recorded from the 1340s until his death about 1365 or 1366, and the brother of the better-known Andrea di Cione, called Orcagna, and of Jacopo, also a painter. He is generally credited with the frescoes of the Last Judgment, Paradise and Hell, drawn from Dante, in the Strozzi Chapel of Santa Maria Novella in Florence, painted in the 1350s while Andrea made the altarpiece for the same chapel. His figures are solemn and monumental, with heavy robes and calm faces, and his colour is gentler than his brother's. He painted altarpieces for churches and religious houses, among them the triptych now in the National Gallery of Art. Long overshadowed by Orcagna, he is now seen as one of the most accomplished Florentine painters of the generation after Giotto.","q75560":"Pierre Louis Dumesnil the Younger, born in 1698 and dead in 1781, belonged to a family of Paris painters and worked in the French capital. Little is recorded of his training. He painted small scenes of polite life, such as card parties, conversations and games in drawing rooms, in the lively, small-scale manner of the Rococo, for Parisian collectors who liked such intimate subjects. The Met's Card Players in a Drawing Room, painted about 1756 to 1759, shows the fashionable passion for cards that ran through eighteenth-century French society, and it records furniture, dress and manners in some detail. Dumesnil is a minor master, now known mostly to specialists in French genre painting and to visitors who meet his pictures as a record of the Parisian interior.","q75698":"Samuel F. B. Morse was born in Charlestown, Massachusetts, in 1791 and graduated from Yale in 1810. In 1811 he sailed to London to study under Washington Allston and entered the Royal Academy schools; on his return in 1815 he made a living as a portraitist in Charleston, New Haven and New York. His commissions included a full-length of the Marquis de Lafayette for New York City (1825-26) and the portrait of Governor De Witt Clinton now in the Met. The House of Representatives (1822-23), a large picture of the chamber lit at night, failed to bring the profit he hoped for. In 1826 he was a founder and first president of the National Academy of Design. After 1832 he turned to the electric telegraph, and his fame as an inventor has overshadowed his art, though his portraits are now admired.","q759588":"Jan Miense Molenaer was born in Haarlem about 1610 and is thought to have trained there with Frans Hals, whose lively brushwork and merry tavern figures left a strong mark on his early pictures. In 1636 he married the painter Judith Leyster; the couple lived in Amsterdam for some years and settled at Heemstede, near Haarlem, in 1648. He died in Haarlem in 1668. Molenaer painted peasant and tavern scenes, music-making, children and proverbs, always with a humorous eye, as well as portraits and some biblical subjects, and he sold them on the busy open market for pictures. He is often remembered today as the husband of Leyster, though his own wit and skill are increasingly admired. The National Gallery's Self-Portrait as a Lute Player, about 1637 to 1638, shows his early debt to Hals.","q762544":"Pierre Gobert was born at Fontainebleau in 1662 and spent his working life in Paris, where he painted portraits through the late reign of Louis XIV, the Regency and the early years of Louis XV. He became known for likenesses of princesses and ladies of the court, many of them shown as goddesses or nymphs, a fashion of the age that let sitters be flattered with classical attributes. Little is recorded of his training. The Met's portrait of Marie Adélaïde of Savoy, Duchess of Burgundy, dates from 1710, the year her youngest son, the future Louis XV, was born; she died in 1712, aged twenty-six. Admired by court circles in his day and later somewhat neglected, Gobert is now studied as a smooth, decorative record of court taste. He died in 1744.","q7648509":"Little is recorded here of Susan C. Waters beyond her dates, 1823 to 1900, and her place among the many American portrait painters who worked for local families in the middle of the nineteenth century, most of them self-taught. Her two pictures in the National Gallery of Art, Henry L. Wells of 1845 and a double portrait called Brothers of about 1845, are early works in the direct, flat-toned style of the folk portrait: carefully drawn faces and clothes, clear local colour and plain settings. Pictures of this kind were bought by farmers, tradesmen and professional families who wanted a record of their children and elders at modest cost, before photography took over that market. Long thought merely quaint, they are now collected and shown for their directness and freshness.","q766362":"William Etty was born in York in 1787, the son of a miller and baker, and was apprenticed at twelve to a printer in Hull. He went to London to study under Sir Thomas Lawrence and entered the Royal Academy Schools in 1807, where he drew from the life class for many years. Journeys to Italy, and above all a stay in Venice, taught him to admire Titian, Veronese and Tintoretto, and the glowing, pearly flesh of his nudes owes much to them. His Cleopatra's Arrival in Cilicia (1821) made his name, and he became an Academician in 1828. Critics attacked his nudes as indecent, but patrons bought them. In later life he returned to York and campaigned successfully to save its medieval walls and gates; he died there in 1849. Admired by the Victorians, then neglected, he is again respected.","q768127":"Augustin Théodule Ribot was born in 1823 at Saint-Nicolas-d'Attez in Normandy and made his way slowly. He began as a decorative painter and copyist and was largely self-taught, learning from the Spanish masters he admired, Ribera and Zurbarán, and from the still lifes of Chardin. From them he took dark grounds and strongly lit highlights, laid on in heavy, rich paint. He painted cooks, kitchen scenes, still lifes and religious subjects, and in his later years Breton fishing families and seated women, as in the Met and National Gallery pictures. He won attention at the Salon in the 1860s and was admired by younger realists and by American collectors and painters. He also made etchings, and died in 1891.","q77444299":"Almost nothing certain is known of Peter McCallion. The dates 1890 to 1900 attached to his name in the record belong to his one picture in the National Gallery of Art, Slate, Pipe, Tobacco, and Box of Matches, painted about 1890 to 1900, and not to his life, which cannot be given here. The subject, a still life of a pipe, tobacco and matches with a slate, points to the American trompe-l'oeil tradition of the later nineteenth century, associated with William Harnett and John Frederick Peto, in which everyday objects were painted so exactly that they seemed real enough to touch. Pictures of this kind were bought by businessmen and hung in offices, clubs and saloons. McCallion remains a little-known name, and the picture is valued as a small, sharp example of the genre.","q7781868":"Theodor Kaufmann was born in 1814 at Uelzen, in the kingdom of Hanover, and studied in Munich. A liberal, he took part in the revolutionary movement of 1848 and, when it failed, left Germany; he settled in New York about 1850. There he painted portraits, genre scenes and, during the Civil War, pictures in support of the Union and the abolition of slavery. His best-known work is On to Liberty (1867), now in the Met, which shows a group of fugitives from slavery, women and children among them, making their way toward the Union lines; it is a rare large picture of its time that treats enslaved people as its central figures, and it has been much studied in recent years. Kaufmann died in 1896.","q7790538":"Thomas Hazlehurst is catalogued as a British painter without dates, and little can be said of his life with confidence. A miniature painter of the name is recorded at Liverpool around 1800, and he may be the man meant, but the museum does not say so. The Met holds one picture by him, a portrait of Agnes Sewell, possibly about 1800. If, as seems likely, it is a miniature, it belongs to the years when small portraits painted in watercolour on ivory were exchanged as keepsakes and worn as jewels. Where he trained and who his patrons were are questions that cannot be answered here, and it is better to leave them open than to guess. His name reaches us chiefly through this portrait, which preserves the likeness and the name of a woman of the period.","q7791757":"Thomas Le Clear was born in 1818 at Owego, in upstate New York, and was largely self-taught. He began as a travelling portrait painter and settled in Buffalo, where he painted local worthies and made a name with genre scenes of children and street life; about 1860 he moved to New York City, and he was elected to the National Academy of Design in 1863. His portraits are plainly drawn and carefully finished. The Met has his likeness of the poet and editor William Cullen Bryant, dated 1876, and one of Amory Sibley Carhart. He painted American subjects in a sober, direct manner close to the realism of his day, and was well regarded in his lifetime. He died in 1882, and he is now remembered mainly for his portraits and genre pictures.","q7793282":"Thomas Pritchard Rossiter was born in New Haven, Connecticut, in 1818 and studied there with the portrait painter Nathaniel Jocelyn before going to Europe, where he worked in Paris and Rome and absorbed the religious and historical painting then in favour. Back in the United States he painted portraits, biblical and allegorical pictures and scenes from American history, and exhibited at the National Academy of Design. His best-known work, in the Met, is Washington and Lafayette at Mount Vernon, 1784 (1859), painted in collaboration with the landscape painter Louis Rémy Mignot, who provided the setting. Rossiter died at Cold Spring, New York, in 1871. His reputation, high among mid-century patrons of historical painting, faded with their taste, though the Mount Vernon scene is still valued as a view of the early republic.","q7793863":"Thomas Seir Cummings was born in Bath, England, in 1804 and was brought as a child to New York, where he became a pupil of Henry Inman and one of the leading American miniature painters. He painted portraits in watercolour on ivory for New York families such as the Clarksons and the Cammanns, and also made sentimental pictures such as The Bracelet and A Mother's Pearls. In 1825-26 he was one of the founders of the National Academy of Design and served it for many years as treasurer; his Historic Annals of the National Academy of Design (1865) is still a main source for its early years. He died in 1894 at the age of ninety. As a miniaturist he is respected; as a witness to the Academy's beginnings he is indispensable.","q7794913":"Thomas Waterman Wood was born in Montpelier, Vermont, in 1823 and began as a portrait painter in his native state and in Boston, then settled in New York after study in Europe in the 1860s. He is best known for his sympathetic genre pictures of country people and, above all, for A Bit of War History (1865-66), three paintings in the Met, The Contraband, The Recruit and The Veteran, which follow a single African American man from fugitive to Union soldier. He was a leading figure in American art institutions and served as president of the National Academy of Design from 1891 to 1899. He gave his native Montpelier a gallery of pictures, and he died in 1903. His careful realism was widely admired in the later nineteenth century, and the war triptych is now his most discussed work.","q7803":"Agnolo di Cosimo, called Bronzino, was born near Florence in 1503, trained under Raffaellino del Garbo and then under Jacopo Pontormo, with whom he worked closely for years. From 1539 he was court painter to Cosimo I de' Medici, and he painted formal portraits of the duke, of the duchess Eleonora of Toledo and of their children, in cool, polished, enamel-like colour that shows rank rather than feeling. He also frescoed the duchess's chapel in the Palazzo Vecchio, designed tapestries, and painted the Allegory with Venus and Cupid, now in London, which Cosimo is said to have sent to Francis I of France. A founder member of the Florentine Accademia del Disegno in 1563, he also wrote poetry. He died in Florence in 1572. His court portrait set a pattern for Europe, and he is the standard example of Florentine Mannerism.","q786545":"Thomas Sully was born in 1783 at Horncastle, Lincolnshire, and was taken to Charleston, South Carolina, as a boy. He learned first as a miniature painter, then from Gilbert Stuart in Boston, and in 1809 went to London, where he studied under Benjamin West and took on the soft, flowing, romantic manner of Sir Thomas Lawrence. Settling in Philadelphia in 1810, he became the leading American portrait painter after Stuart's death, painting statesmen, officers, actors and handsome sitters such as Eliza Ridgely, shown with a harp in 1818. In 1837-38 he returned to London to paint the young Queen Victoria for the St George's Society of Philadelphia. He died in Philadelphia in 1872. His portraits are valued for their elegance and fluency, rather than for any deep probing of character.","q7925882":"Victor Eeckhout was a Belgian painter, born in 1821 and dead in 1879, who worked in the generation after Gustaf Wappers and Henri Leys, when Belgian art was celebrated across Europe for its historical and genre subjects. Little is recorded of his training or career. The Met's Wedding Procession is of a type very popular in the middle of the nineteenth century: a finished figure picture of a festive or ceremonial occasion, painted in a smooth, detailed manner owing much to Dutch and Flemish genre painting of the seventeenth century, and sold through dealers to buyers in Belgium, France, Britain and America. Eeckhout is a minor figure, known today mostly to specialists in Belgian nineteenth-century art and to visitors who meet this canvas.","q7966127":"Walter Shirlaw was born in Paisley, Scotland, in 1838 and was taken to the United States as a small child. He worked as a banknote engraver in New York before going to Munich in 1870 to study painting at the Royal Academy, where he formed friendships with Frank Duveneck and William Merritt Chase. Returning to New York in 1877, he became a leading organiser of American art life: he helped found the Society of American Artists, which challenged the conservatism of the National Academy, and he taught at the Art Students League. He painted Bavarian peasant scenes, portraits and later murals, including a set for the Library of Congress. The Met's In Mischief shows his early taste for lively genre. He died in Madrid in 1909, and he is remembered as an influential teacher and organiser as well as a painter.","q8005829":"William Bradford was born in Fairhaven, Massachusetts, in 1823 and grew up as a Quaker in the shipping and whaling world of the New Bedford coast. He worked first in the family cloth business and was largely self-taught as a painter, though he learned from the Dutch marine painter Albert van Beest, who worked nearby. He painted ships, storms and coastal scenes, and from the early 1860s he made voyages to Labrador and Greenland. In 1869 he chartered the steamer Panther to carry painters, photographers and guests into Arctic waters, and the results were huge canvases of icebergs and ice floes, such as An Arctic Summer in the Met, and a photographic book, The Arctic Regions, published in London in 1873. He showed his pictures with lectures and lantern slides, and sold them to collectors in America and Britain. He died in 1892.","q8013463":"William James Hubard was born in 1807 at Whitchurch, Shropshire, and first became known as a child prodigy, Master Hubard, who cut finely detailed silhouettes in paper and toured Britain and then the United States with them. In America he turned to portrait painting in oils, working in Baltimore and then Virginia, and about 1830 he painted the aged Charles Carroll of Carrollton, the last surviving signer of the Declaration of Independence, a portrait now in the Met. He later studied in Europe, settled in Richmond, Virginia, and turned to sculpture, casting bronze replicas of Houdon's statue of George Washington in the state capitol. He died in 1862 in an accident at his Richmond foundry, which was then making munitions for the Confederacy. He is remembered for his prodigious youth, his portraits and his foundry.","q8014260":"William Lamb Picknell was born in Hinesburgh, Vermont, in 1853 and trained as a landscape painter. He studied with George Inness in Rome and Paris, and then under Jean-Léon Gérôme in Paris, before settling in Brittany, where he painted at Pont-Aven and Concarneau among the American colony there. The Road to Concarneau (1880) earned him an honourable mention at the Paris Salon. He worked in a plain, sunlit manner, with broad areas of clear colour and sandy roads under big skies, closer to Barbizon and early Impressionism than to the Hudson River tradition. The Met's Banks of the Loing, of the mid-1890s, is a late work from the region near Fontainebleau. He died at Marblehead, Massachusetts, in 1897, aged only forty-three.","q8014712":"William Louis Sonntag was born in 1822 near Pittsburgh, grew up in Cincinnati, and was largely self-taught. He became a landscape painter in the tradition of the Hudson River School, painting mountains, lakes and waterfalls in a romantic, atmospheric manner, and after a stay in Italy in the 1850s he found patrons among the collectors of Cincinnati, then a thriving centre of the arts in the West. He exhibited at the National Academy of Design and moved to New York in the early 1870s, where he spent the rest of his career. The Met's Landscape with Waterfall and Figures, about 1865, shows the taste for grand, picturesque scenery that made him popular in his day. He died in 1900. His reputation faded as taste moved on from the Hudson River School, but he is again appreciated by students of American landscape painting.","q8015236":"William Matthew Prior was born in Bath, Maine, in 1806 and worked as a travelling portrait painter, largely self-taught, in Maine and then in Boston, where he settled about 1840. He worked beside a circle of relatives by marriage, the Hamblens, in the same plain manner. He painted in two styles and priced them accordingly: a flat, linear likeness for a few dollars, and a more modelled one at a higher fee. Many of his sitters were children, among them those in the National Gallery's Baby in Blue and Master Cleeves. He died in Boston in 1873. Prior's bright, simple portraits were bought by ordinary families, who wanted a likeness at a price they could afford, and they are now prized as outstanding examples of American folk painting.","q8016347":"William Oliver Stone, who lived from 1830 to 1875, was an American portrait painter, and little is recorded of his training here. The Met's Mary Cadwalader Rawle, dated 1868, shows a sitter from the Rawle and Cadwalader families, which were prominent in Philadelphia, and suggests that he painted for well-to-do clients in the years after the Civil War. Portraits of this kind were commissioned to record family likenesses, and they often passed down the generations as heirlooms before reaching a museum. Stone died in his mid-forties. His work is known today chiefly through a few museum portraits, and his reputation rests on that small group rather than on any famous commission.","q8016422":"William Owen was born at Ludlow, Shropshire, in 1769 and went to London in 1786, where he studied at the Royal Academy Schools and began exhibiting at the Academy in 1792. He rose steadily as a portrait painter: he was elected an Academician in 1806, and he was appointed portrait painter to the Prince Regent, painting statesmen, military men and the families of the great. His style followed that of John Hoppner and Sir Thomas Lawrence, with smooth finish and fashionable elegance, as in the Met's group portrait of the grandchildren of Sir William Heathcote. He died in 1825. Though overshadowed by Lawrence, he was in his day one of the busiest portrait painters in London, and his best likenesses are still admired for their polish.","q8016524":"William Page was born in Albany, New York, in 1811 and grew up in New York City. Samuel F. B. Morse encouraged him to paint, and he studied at the National Academy of Design, working first as a portraitist and then on religious and mythological pictures. He lived in Boston in the 1840s and in Italy in the 1850s, where he knew the Brownings and painted Robert Browning's portrait. Page was obsessed with the methods of Titian, and developed a theory of flesh colour and proportion; his experimental techniques, however, caused many of his pictures to darken, which damaged his reputation. He served as president of the National Academy of Design from 1871 to 1873 and died in 1885. The Met's portraits of his daughters, painted about 1837 to 1840, are among his freshest early work.","q8017370":"William Tylee Ranney was born in Middletown, Connecticut, in 1813 and was apprenticed to a tinsmith in North Carolina. In 1836 he went to Texas and served as a soldier in the war against Mexico, an experience that gave him a lasting taste for frontier life. Returning east, he settled in the New York area, studied painting and became a professional artist, working at Brooklyn and later at Weehawken, New Jersey. He is best known for pictures of trappers, hunters and frontiersmen and for scenes of the American Revolution, in a clear, narrative style; The Retrieve (1850) in the National Gallery is a hunting scene. The American Art-Union bought his work, and prints made it widely known. He died of tuberculosis in 1857, aged forty-four, and he is now regarded as one of the leading painters of the American frontier.","q8017857":"William Russell Birch was born at Warwick, England, in 1755 and trained in London as an enamel painter, making small portraits and copies of paintings in vitreous enamel. He sailed for the United States in 1794 and settled in Philadelphia, where he made enamel portraits of wealthy citizens, among them Mrs. William Bingham, and copies of Gilbert Stuart's portraits of George Washington. His best-known work, though, is a book: The City of Philadelphia, as it Appeared in the Year 1800, a set of twenty-eight engraved views of the city, followed by Country Seats of the United States in 1808. His son Thomas became the first major American marine painter. William Birch died in 1834. His enamels are small and exact, and his views remain a major visual record of the early republic's capital.","q8019715":"William Verstille was born in 1757 and worked as a miniature painter in New England and New York in the later 1780s and 1790s, dying in 1803. Little is recorded of his training. The Met holds ten of his small watercolours on ivory, among them portraits of Lieutenant Colonel Elias Parker, members of the Storrs and Stone families, and several unnamed gentlemen. His likenesses are straightforward and firmly drawn, in the bold, slightly flattened manner of provincial American portrait miniatures, with careful attention to coat, wig and lace. Before photography such small portraits were the usual way for a middle-class family to keep a likeness, and Verstille's are now valued as records of the post-Revolutionary generation, although his name is known mainly to students of American miniatures.","q8026559":"Winthrop Chandler was born in 1747 at Woodstock, Connecticut, and little is recorded of his training. He earned his living as a house, sign and ornamental painter as well as a portraitist, working in the towns of eastern Connecticut and in central Massachusetts, notably Worcester. In a bold, linear manner he painted likenesses of ministers, merchants and farmers, and landscape overmantels for their houses. The National Gallery's paired portraits of Captain Samuel Chandler and his wife date from about 1780. His hard, patterned style, with firm outlines and flat patches of colour, was long thought crude, but it has been admired by collectors of American folk art since the twentieth century, and he is now ranked among the most vigorous portraitists of Revolutionary-era New England. He died in 1790.","q8506":"Giovanni Antonio Bazzi, called Il Sodoma, was born at Vercelli in Piedmont in 1477 and trained in Milan, where the art of Leonardo da Vinci left a lasting mark on his soft modelling and dreamy faces. He settled in Siena about 1501 and became the city's leading painter of the early sixteenth century, completing the frescoes of the life of Saint Benedict in the cloister of Monte Oliveto Maggiore (1505 to 1508). In Rome he worked for Pope Julius II in the Vatican and for the banker Agostino Chigi, for whom he painted the Marriage of Alexander and Roxana in the Villa Farnesina. Pope Leo X made him a knight about 1518, the year of the Saint George and the Dragon in the National Gallery of Art. Vasari tells stories of his eccentric household of pet animals. He died in Siena in 1549.","q864092":"Michiel van Miereveld was born in Delft in 1567 and became the leading portrait painter of the Dutch Republic. He trained under painters in Delft and Utrecht, and from the early 1600s he painted for the House of Orange, the stadtholders Maurits and Frederick Henry, as well as the regents, merchants and clergy of Delft and The Hague. He ran a large and efficient workshop in Delft, helped by his sons and pupils, and is said to have turned out thousands of portraits, many of them copied and engraved. His likenesses are smooth, precise and sober, with lace collars and black dress carefully rendered, as in the Met's portrait of Jacob van Dalen. Praised by contemporaries, he was later thought dull beside Hals and Rembrandt, but his best portraits are respected for their accuracy. He died in Delft in 1641.","q874537":"Pieter Jansz. Quast was a Dutch painter and etcher, born in Amsterdam about 1605 and dead in 1647. Little is recorded of his teachers. He painted humble people: beggars, peasants, soldiers and merry companies, often with a gently comic eye, in small pictures with quick, thin brushwork and a limited palette, in the manner of the low-life painters of his day. The Met's A Party of Merrymakers, about 1635 to 1638, is a typical example, showing a group of revellers. His market was the Dutch townsman who liked scenes of low life, a taste that kept many painters busy in the years of the Republic's prosperity. He died young, in his early forties, and is known today chiefly to specialists in Dutch genre painting.","q878197":"Wilhelm von Kobell was born in Mannheim in 1766, the son of the landscape painter and etcher Ferdinand Kobell, from whom he learned his trade. He moved to Munich in 1793, where he worked for the Bavarian court and later taught landscape painting at the Academy. His small, clear, sunlit landscapes, with horsemen, huntsmen, peasants and cattle, follow the example of the Dutch painters of the seventeenth century, such as Philips Wouwerman and Paulus Potter, while keeping a fresh view of the Bavarian countryside; the Met's A Huntsman and a Peasant Woman by the Isar River with a View of Munich (1823) is a late example. He also painted battle pictures for the Bavarian court and made fine etchings. He died in Munich in 1853, and he is now valued as one of the best German landscape painters of his generation.","q1056484":"Antonio d'Enrico, called Tanzio da Varallo, was born in the Valsesia, in Piedmont, about 1575 and died in 1633. He probably learned his craft from his elder brother Giovanni, a painter and sculptor who worked at the Sacro Monte of Varallo, the pilgrimage complex of painted and sculpted chapels. A stay in Rome in his youth brought him into contact with Caravaggio's followers, and he returned north to paint altarpieces and frescoes for churches in Piedmont and Lombardy. His figures are muscular, angular and fiercely lit, with strong reds and cold greys, and his saints often seem tense rather than serene. Long neglected, he has been much studied since the early twentieth century and is now admired as one of the most original painters of the northern Italian Baroque. The Saint Sebastian in Washington and the Cleveland Portrait of a Man date from about 1620.","q1093129":"Simon de Vlieger was born in Rotterdam about 1600 and died at Weesp, near Amsterdam, in 1653. He worked in Rotterdam, Delft, Amsterdam and finally Weesp, and he is counted among the painters who made the Dutch seascape calm and atmospheric, replacing busy naval incident with wide skies, low horizons and a narrow range of silvery greys and browns. He also painted beach scenes, wooded landscapes and religious subjects, and made designs for stained glass and tapestry. Willem van de Velde the Younger studied with him at Weesp, and his quiet estuaries and harbours influenced Jan van de Cappelle. The Cleveland picture of sleeping peasants, dated 1650 to 1653, shows how far he could stray from the sea. Today he is ranked among the finest Dutch marine painters, though his pupil became more famous.","q1117749":"Master of the Magdalen is the name modern scholars have given to an unidentified Florentine painter of the later thirteenth century. It comes from a panel now in the Accademia in Florence showing Saint Mary Magdalen surrounded by scenes from her legend, the work around which his other pictures were grouped. No document records his real name, his dates or his teacher. He worked in the so-called Greek manner, the Byzantine-derived style that still governed Tuscan painting in the generation before Cimabue and Giotto, with gold backgrounds, a stately, frontal Virgin and linear, patterned drapery. The Metropolitan Museum holds three panels of the Madonna and Child by him, one dated about 1280. Pictures of this kind were made for churches, convents and private chapels, and they show Florentine painting just before the great changes of the early fourteenth century.","q11815548":"Pensionante del Saraceni, the lodger of Saraceni, is a name coined in the twentieth century, apparently by the Italian art historian Roberto Longhi, for an unidentified painter active in Rome in the 1610s. The nickname points to a connection with Carlo Saraceni, the Venetian-born follower of Caravaggio then at work in the city, with whom the unknown painter is supposed to have lodged or worked. He may have come from France or the Low Countries, and the museum's label, French with a question mark, and its very wide date range show how little is settled. His rare pictures include half-length figures and still lifes of fruit and glass, painted with clear, hard light against dark grounds in the Caravaggesque way. No identification has won general agreement, and no documents record his life.","q1192715":"Pier Francesco Mola was born at Coldrerio, in what is now Swiss Ticino, in 1612 and died in Rome in 1666. His father, an architect and painter, took the family to Rome, where the boy studied with the Cavaliere d'Arpino; he later worked in Bologna with Francesco Albani and studied Venetian painting, above all Titian. Settled in Rome by the 1640s, he painted small landscapes with hermits, saints and biblical figures in warm, glowing light, as well as portraits and large fresco decorations, among them a commission for Pope Alexander VII in the Quirinal Palace. In 1662 he was elected head of the Accademia di San Luca. His reputation stood high at his death, faded in the nineteenth century, and has since recovered. The Metropolitan Museum's Rest on the Flight into Egypt shows his landscape manner.","q12056590":"William M. S. Doyle was born in Boston in 1769 and died there in 1828. He worked as a portrait painter in Boston in the years around 1800, when the young republic's merchants and professional men wanted likenesses of themselves and their families. Beyond this, little is recorded of his life. The Metropolitan Museum holds six of his pictures, among them a Self Portrait dated 1801 and a run of small portraits of gentlemen dated between 1804 and 1814, all of them with the sitters unnamed. That anonymity shows how easily a provincial portraitist's patrons are forgotten, even when the pictures themselves are kept, and it leaves Doyle known chiefly by his own face and by a handful of likenesses of unknown men.","q12072854":"Robert White was born in London in 1645 and died there in 1703. He was apprenticed to the engraver and draughtsman David Loggan and became the leading English portrait engraver of his generation. White drew many of his sitters from life, in fine black lead on vellum, and then engraved the likeness himself with a clean, delicate line, so that his prints became the standard images of preachers, statesmen, scholars and nobility of the later Stuart age. His life drawing of John Bunyan, dated 1688, the year Bunyan died, is the best known of them. He also engraved after painters such as Godfrey Kneller. The portrait of a man dated 1690 in the Metropolitan Museum belongs to this world of small, exact likenesses. His son George White continued the family trade as an engraver.","q12312712":"Frederik Sødring was a Danish landscape painter, born in 1809 and dead in 1862. He belongs to the Danish Golden Age, the generation of painters who worked around the Royal Academy in Copenhagen in the decades after 1815 and made a fresh, attentive art out of the plain Danish countryside: its beech woods, coasts and country estates, seen in clear daylight. Beyond these outlines, little is firmly recorded, and no teachers, patrons or commissions are named. The National Gallery of Art's View of Bregentved Forest, Zealand, painted in the mid-1830s, shows woods on the great Bregentved estate in central Zealand, long the property of the Moltke family, and gives a good idea of the close, observant treatment of trees that Danish painters of his day prized.","q123740":"Félix Vallotton was born in Lausanne in 1865 and went to Paris at seventeen, where he studied at the Académie Julian and supported himself as a portraitist and printmaker. He joined the circle of the Nabis in the early 1890s and became famous for stark black-and-white woodcuts, which gave a hard, ironic view of Parisian life and appeared in the Revue blanche and the anarchist press. After his marriage in 1899 into the family of the dealers Bernheim-Jeune he turned increasingly to painting, with smooth, cool, flattened interiors, nudes and landscapes. He became a French citizen in 1900. In 1917 he visited the front in Champagne and painted ruined villages such as Souain. He died in Paris in 1925, and his reputation, always high among artists, grew steadily in the later twentieth century.","q1256140":"Lambert Sachs was an American portrait painter who lived from 1818 to 1903, a long life that carried him from the age of painted likenesses into the age of the snapshot. Little is recorded of his training or career, and he is known to this collection by a single picture, The Herbert Children, dated 1857. It is a group portrait of the children of one family, the kind of picture that American households of comfortable means often ordered in the 1850s, when a painter still competed with the new and cheaper daguerreotype and photograph by offering colour, scale and a more flattering finish. It belongs to the large body of mid-century American family portraiture.","q1258585":"Alvise Vivarini was born in Venice about 1442 and died there about 1505. He came from the Vivarini family of painters from Murano, whose workshop under his father Antonio and his uncle Bartolomeo had long supplied altarpieces to Venice and the mainland. Alvise took up the oil technique, solid modelling and clear, glowing light of Antonello da Messina, who was in Venice in 1475 and 1476, and he became a rival of Giovanni Bellini. He painted altarpieces, half-length Madonnas and portraits, and was among the painters engaged to decorate the Great Council Hall of the Doge's Palace, whose pictures were destroyed in the fire of 1577. The National Gallery of Art's Saint Jerome Reading dates from about 1476 and its Portrait of a Man from about 1495.","q126949261":"Almost nothing can be said with certainty about María Josefa Sánchez. The museum record gives her name, her Spanish nationality and a single work, a Cell Cross dated about 1639 to 1655, and no dates of birth or death. A cell cross, as the title suggests, was a devotional cross made for the private cell of a monk or nun, where it served as a focus for prayer and meditation. Women painters of seventeenth-century Spain are poorly documented, and pictures by many of them have been identified only in recent decades. Nothing is recorded of her teachers, her patrons or any other work, so this entry can say only that the Cell Cross is the one picture by which she is represented.","q128027":"Giorgio Vasari was born at Arezzo in 1511 and died in Florence in 1574. He trained first with the French stained-glass painter Guillaume de Marcillat in Arezzo and then in Florence, where the example of Andrea del Sarto and Michelangelo shaped him. He painted and designed for Cardinal Ippolito de' Medici, for the Farnese in Rome and above all for Duke Cosimo I in Florence, for whom he built the Uffizi, laid out the corridor that bears his name and frescoed the Salone dei Cinquecento in the Palazzo Vecchio. He helped to found the Accademia del Disegno in 1563 and began the Last Judgment in the cathedral dome, finished by Federico Zuccaro after his death. His Lives of the Most Excellent Painters, Sculptors and Architects, published in 1550 and enlarged in 1568, founded art history. Today he is better known as a writer than as a painter.","q1280976":"Paul Weber was born in Darmstadt, Germany, in 1823 and died in 1916, aged about ninety-three. He left Germany in 1848, a year of revolutions that sent many educated Germans abroad, and settled in Philadelphia, where he worked for the rest of his long career as a landscape painter. His views of Pennsylvania, the Hudson valley and the Catskills show the influence of the Hudson River School together with the careful finish of the German landscape tradition he had known at home, and a strong feeling for woodland light. The National Gallery of Art's Scene in the Catskills, dated 1858, comes from his first decade in America. Beyond this, little is recorded about his training, patrons or later reputation, and his name is now known mainly to specialists in American landscape painting.","q1284002":"Zanobi Strozzi was born in Florence in 1412 and died there in 1468. He is recorded as a pupil and follower of Fra Angelico, and he made his name chiefly as an illuminator of choir books and manuscripts for Florentine churches and convents, among them San Marco, the Dominican house that Cosimo de' Medici had rebuilt. He also painted panels, mainly Annunciations, Nativities and Madonnas, in a gentle, Angelico-derived style with clear colour, a fondness for architectural settings and soft gilded detail. The Metropolitan Museum's Nativity is one such work. His fame was long eclipsed by his teacher's, but modern scholars have reconstructed his career from documents and style, and he is now recognised as a distinct and attractive figure of mid-fifteenth-century Florence.","q128954":"Charles Amédée Philippe Van Loo was born in Turin in 1719 into the Van Loo dynasty, a family of painters that spread from the Netherlands to France and Italy. He was the son of Jean-Baptiste Van Loo and a nephew of Carle Van Loo, and he learned the trade from them. In 1748 he went to Berlin as a court painter to Frederick the Great, painting portraits and decorative pictures, and he spent most of his later life in Prussia, dying in Berlin in 1795. The two pictures in the National Gallery of Art, The Camera Obscura and Soap Bubbles, both dated 1764, are light, elegant scenes of fashionable leisure, typical of the late Rococo. His name is overshadowed by his uncle's, but he held a respected post at one of Europe's most cultivated courts.","q1293506":"Edward Savage was born at Princeton, Massachusetts, in 1761 and died there in 1817. He began as a goldsmith, turned to painting and engraving, and in 1791 went to London, where he studied with the American expatriate Benjamin West. He is chiefly remembered for his portraits of George Washington, whom he painted from life in 1789, and for The Washington Family, begun that year and finished in 1796, which shows Washington with his wife, her grandchildren and a servant beside a plan of the new federal city. Savage engraved the composition himself, and the prints, issued in 1798, carried it into countless homes. He also ran an exhibition room of paintings and curiosities, the Columbian Gallery, in Philadelphia and later in New York. Though not a first-rank painter, he left the best-known image of the first presidential family.","q1319924":"Master of the Griselda Legend is the name given to an anonymous painter of the late fifteenth century, usually placed in Siena or Umbria and in the orbit of Pinturicchio. It comes from three panels of the story of patient Griselda, from Boccaccio's Decameron, now in the National Gallery in London. No document gives his real name or dates, and the range of 1415 to 1550 attached to him here is only a broad estimate. His narrative panels are lively and decorative, with slender figures in rich costume set against bright architecture and landscape. The National Gallery of Art's Joseph of Egypt dates from about 1490 to 1495. Pictures of this kind, illustrating stories from literature and the Bible, were commonly made for the furnishings of Italian houses, notably the painted marriage chests called cassoni.","q1337275":"Valentin de Boulogne, born at Coulommiers in Brie in 1591, was in Rome by about 1612 and stayed there until his death in 1632. He became the leading French follower of Caravaggio, painting taverns, card-players, fortune-tellers, musicians and half-length saints in dark rooms lit by a hard, low light, with a quiet, serious feeling. He worked beside French and Netherlandish painters in the city, and his patrons included Cardinal Francesco Barberini and, through him, the Barberini pope Urban VIII. In 1629 and 1630 he painted the altarpiece of the Martyrdom of Saints Processus and Martinian for Saint Peter's, an honour he shared with Nicolas Poussin. He died at forty-one, and his reputation, long dimmed, was rebuilt in the twentieth century.","q1342328":"Michael Sweerts was born in Brussels in 1618 and is one of the most individual, and most mysterious, Flemish painters of the seventeenth century. He was in Rome by about 1646, where he enjoyed the patronage of the Marchese Camillo Massimo and painted street scenes, artists drawing from casts, and quiet, sympathetic portraits and half-length figures in clear, even light. Back in Brussels from about 1655 he opened a drawing academy, and then worked in Amsterdam. In 1661 he joined a French Catholic missionary expedition bound for the East under Bishop François Pallu; he was dismissed from it in Persia, and went on to Goa in India, where he died in 1664. His pictures were neglected for centuries and are now highly prized.","q13426391":"Richard Collins was born in Gosport, Hampshire, in 1755 and died in 1831. A miniature painter, he was a pupil of Jeremiah Meyer, the leading miniaturist of the 1770s, and he exhibited at the Royal Academy for many years. He became Painter in Miniature to King George III and portrayed members of the royal family, as well as nobility and gentry, on ivory in delicate, smooth, finely stippled colour. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of George Howard, Lord Morpeth, who later became sixth Earl of Carlisle, shows a member of one of England's great families in the polished manner of the day. Miniatures of this kind were given as keepsakes, worn as jewellery and exchanged between families, and the best of them, like his, combine a close likeness with refined technique.","q1349738":"Vittore Crivelli was born in Venice, probably in the early 1440s, and was the younger brother of the better-known Carlo Crivelli. He settled in the Marche, around Fermo, by the 1480s and worked there and in the neighbouring hill towns for the rest of his life, painting polyptychs and altarpieces for Franciscan and parish churches in a style close to his brother's, with gilded ornament, raised gesso and crisp, hard-edged forms. He often painted the Madonna with angels, as in the two panels in the Metropolitan Museum dated about 1481 to 1482. His work is more provincial than Carlo's, but pleasing and richly decorative, and it shows how the Venetian taste for gold and colour travelled to the Adriatic coast. He died, probably in 1501 or 1502, at Fermo.","q1350959":"Ralph Earl was born in Leicester, Massachusetts, in 1751 and died in Bolton, Connecticut, in 1801. He had little formal training when, in 1775, he made drawings of the battles of Lexington and Concord that were engraved by Amos Doolittle. A loyalist sympathiser, he left for England in 1778, studied with Benjamin West and exhibited at the Royal Academy. Back in America in 1785, he was briefly jailed for debt in New York, then settled in Connecticut, where he painted the lawyers, merchants and landowners of the Connecticut Valley, often in their own rooms or before a view of their land. His portrait of the New Milford merchant Elijah Boardman, 1789, shows the sitter with bolts of cloth from his shop. Earl worked fast and was uneven, but his best portraits are among the most vivid of the early republic.","q1352158":"Paul Sébillot was born at Matignon in Brittany in 1843 and died in Paris in 1918. He began as a painter, showing Breton landscapes at the Paris Salon, but he is remembered above all as a folklorist and writer. From his early years in Upper Brittany he collected folk tales, songs, legends and sayings from farmers and sailors, and published them in many volumes, among them Contes populaires de la Haute-Bretagne and Le Folk-lore de France, which appeared in four volumes between 1904 and 1907. In 1886 he helped to found the Société des traditions populaires and its journal, which gave French folklore a scholarly footing. The Metropolitan Museum's Spring in Brittany, dated 1874, belongs to the earlier part of his life, when he still thought of himself as a painter.","q1354027":"Paul Huet was born in Paris in 1803 and died there in 1869. He studied under Pierre-Narcisse Guérin and Antoine-Jean Gros, the leading teachers of the day, and became a friend of Eugène Delacroix and of the young English painter Richard Parkes Bonington, whose watercolours and open-air sketches encouraged him to work out of doors. Huet was among the first French Romantics to take landscape as seriously as history painting, and in the 1820s and 1830s he painted woods, rivers, floods and the Normandy coast in loose, dramatic brushwork, with stormy skies and deep colour. Often passed over in favour of the Barbizon painters who followed him, such as Théodore Rousseau, he is now seen as a pioneer of Romantic landscape. The National Gallery of Art's Woodland Stream dates from about 1840.","q13560751":"Virginia Richmond Reynolds lived from 1866 to 1903 and died in her thirties. Little is recorded of her training or career, and the museum record gives neither her nationality nor any other detail of her life. She is represented in this collection by a single picture, a portrait titled Bessie Moore and dated 1899, now in the Metropolitan Museum. Portraiture was one of the few branches of painting in which women could build a professional career in the 1890s, when art schools in Europe and America were opening their doors to them in growing numbers, and many of these painters, working for families and friends, have since faded from the record. This entry can say no more with confidence.","q1361950":"Pietro Rotari was born in Verona in 1707 and died in St Petersburg in 1762. He trained under Antonio Balestra in Verona and then in Venice, Rome and Naples, and became a travelling court painter, working in Vienna and Dresden before going to Russia in 1756 to serve Empress Elizabeth. For her he painted several hundred half-length pictures of young women in varied costumes, with lowered or sidelong glances, which were set into the walls of a hall at Peterhof, where many of them remain. Their sweetness, delicate colour and light touch made them popular, and Rotari's name became a byword for Rococo charm. The two girls in the National Gallery of Art, one asleep and one with a flower in her hair, date from 1760 to 1762 and are late works of this kind.","q1367776":"Jacobus Vrel was a Dutch painter of quiet interiors and street scenes, active in the 1650s and early 1660s, and one of the most mysterious artists of the Dutch Golden Age. Nothing is recorded of his birth, death, training or place of work, and his name survives only on a small number of signed pictures. He painted plain rooms with a single figure, usually a woman sewing, sitting by a window or tending a fire, and views of narrow streets with women at their doors, in subdued browns and greys with a strong sense of stillness. His pictures have at times been attributed to better-known painters, and they have been admired in modern times for their austerity. The National Gallery of Art's Young Woman in an Interior dates from about 1660.","q1369392":"Marco Zoppo was born at Cento, near Bologna, in 1433 and died in Venice in 1478. As a youth he joined the Paduan workshop of Francesco Squarcione, the teacher and entrepreneur who also trained Andrea Mantegna, and he absorbed Padua's hard, sculptural, antique-inspired manner, with its sharp outlines and swags of fruit. He later worked in Bologna, where in 1461 he painted a polyptych for the Spanish College, and in Venice, where he spent his last years painting Madonnas, crucifixions and altarpieces. He also made vigorous pen drawings, collected in a sketchbook now in the British Museum. The two panels in the National Gallery of Art, a Saint Peter and a Madonna and Child, date from about 1467 to 1468.","q1370512":"Worthington Whittredge was born near Springfield, Ohio, in 1820 and began as a house and portrait painter in Cincinnati before turning to landscape. In 1849 he went to Düsseldorf, where he became friendly with Emanuel Leutze and, as he recalled in his memoirs, posed for the figure of Washington in Leutze's Washington Crossing the Delaware. He then spent several years in Rome and returned to New York in 1859, becoming a leading figure of the second generation of the Hudson River School. In 1866 he rode across the plains with General John Pope, and the open West changed his sense of space and light. His later pictures are quieter, more intimate woodland and shore scenes, such as Second Beach, Newport. He served as president of the National Academy of Design and died in 1910 in Summit, New Jersey.","q1372122":"Peter Jacob Horemans was born in Antwerp in 1700 and died in Munich in 1776. Trained in Flanders, he settled in Bavaria while still young and spent the rest of his life at the Munich court, serving the Wittelsbach electors, Karl Albrecht, who became the emperor Charles VII, and later Max III Joseph. His speciality was the small conversation piece and genre scene: courtiers at dinner, in concert or at play, painted with a careful, detailed brush and bright colour. The Metropolitan Museum's Musical Gathering at the Court of the Elector Karl Albrecht of Bavaria is dated 1730. He also painted domestic and tavern scenes in the Flemish tradition. His pictures are valued today as lively records of daily court life in eighteenth-century Bavaria.","q1374536":"Raphaelle Peale was born in Annapolis, Maryland, in 1774, the eldest son of the Philadelphia painter and museum-keeper Charles Willson Peale, who named his children after great artists and trained several of them as painters. Raphaelle worked in his father's studio and in the Peale Museum in Philadelphia, painting miniatures and portraits. He is remembered chiefly for his still lifes of fruit, cake, wine and tea, which are plain, exact and restrained, with objects set on a ledge in clear light, and he is often called the first important American painter of still life. Few buyers wanted them, and he lived poorly, troubled by ill health, until his death in Philadelphia in 1825. Those quiet pictures, such as Strawberries and Cream of 1816, are now counted among the finest early American paintings.","q1375830":"Matthijs Maris was born in The Hague in 1839 and died in London in 1917. The middle one of three painter brothers, with Jacob and Willem, he trained in The Hague and at the Antwerp Academy, then worked in Paris and from 1877 in London, where he stayed for the rest of his life. Although often grouped with the Hague School, he was a solitary dreamer: his paintings, especially those made from memory of the Dutch towns and children of his youth, are soft, misty and mysterious, with figures half dissolved in warm greys and golds. The dealer and designer Daniel Cottier took him to London and supported him, and few of his works were sold in his lifetime. His reverie-like style later led critics to see him as a forerunner of Symbolism. The Metropolitan Museum's Reverie dates from 1875.","q1379714":"Sano di Pietro was born in Siena in 1405 and died there in 1481. He was probably trained with or by Sassetta, and became the most productive Sienese painter of the mid-fifteenth century, running a large workshop that supplied altarpieces, small devotional panels and manuscript illuminations to churches and confraternities in and around the city. He stayed loyal to the gold grounds, graceful figures and sweet, pale colours of the Sienese tradition, even as Florence moved toward perspective and sculptural volume. He painted scenes from the life of Saint Bernardino of Siena, the Franciscan preacher canonised in 1450, including the saint preaching in the city's squares. His work was long thought charming but old-fashioned; modern scholars value its narrative freshness and fine detail.","q1382316":"Samuel Cooper was born in London about 1609 and died there in 1672. Brought up by his uncle, the miniaturist John Hoskins, he became the greatest English portrait miniaturist of the seventeenth century, famed for faces of solid, modelled, almost sculptural realism rather than the delicate decorative effect of his predecessors. His sitters included Oliver Cromwell, whose unfinished miniature became the pattern for many copies, and Charles II, who appointed him limner after the Restoration. He worked for Royalists and Parliamentarians alike. In 1668 the diarist Samuel Pepys watched him paint a miniature of Mrs Pepys and praised his skill. The two pictures in the Metropolitan Museum, a portrait of Henry Carey, second Earl of Monmouth, dated 1649, and a portrait of a woman dated 1653, come from the middle of his career.","q1384543":"Theodore Robinson was born in Irasburg, Vermont, in 1852 and grew up in Evansville, Wisconsin. He studied in New York and, from 1876, in Paris under Carolus-Duran and Jean-Léon Gérôme, then worked in New York as an assistant to John La Farge on mural and decorative commissions before returning to France in 1887. At Giverny from 1888 he became the friend and close follower of Claude Monet, and was the American painter who knew Monet best; his diaries record their conversations. His small, soft, luminous landscapes and figures, such as The Layette and The Valley of the Seine, translate Impressionism into a quiet American idiom. Teaching and exhibiting helped to spread the style at home. He died in New York of asthma in 1896, aged forty-three.","q1387035":"Gari Melchers was born in Detroit in 1860, the son of a German-born sculptor. He studied at the Düsseldorf Academy and then at the Académie Julian in Paris, and in 1884 he settled at Egmond in Holland, where he lived for some thirty years, painting peasants, religious scenes and women at home in a dark, naturalistic manner that later brightened toward Impressionism. He won medals at the Paris Exposition of 1889 and at the Chicago World's Fair of 1893, and in 1896 painted the murals War and Peace for the Library of Congress. He taught in Weimar for some years before the First World War, returned to America in 1915 and settled at Belmont, near Fredericksburg, Virginia, where he died in 1932. The Sisters dates from about 1895.","q1390942":"Richard Caton Woodville was born in Baltimore in 1825 and died in London in 1855, aged thirty. The son of a well-off Baltimore merchant, he began medical studies but gave them up to paint, and in 1845 went to the academy at Düsseldorf, where he learned the polished, anecdotal genre painting then admired across Europe. He later lived in Paris and London. From Europe he sent small, lively scenes of American life to the American Art-Union in New York, which bought and engraved them for its many members and made him widely known at home; War News from Mexico, of 1848, is the most famous. Waiting for the Stage dates from 1851. His death was reported as the result of an overdose of morphine. His son, also named Richard Caton Woodville, became a well-known British battle painter.","q1398270":"Neroccio de' Landi was born in Siena in 1447 and died there in 1500. He was trained by Vecchietta, the leading Sienese painter and sculptor of the previous generation, and for several years from 1468 shared a workshop with Francesco di Giorgio, the painter, sculptor and architect. Neroccio worked mostly in Siena, painting altarpieces, Madonnas, portraits and cassone fronts, and he also worked as a sculptor. His figures are slender and delicate, with long throats, pale faces, golden hair and soft colour, a refined late form of the Sienese tradition. The National Gallery of Art's Claudia Quinta, which shows the Roman woman who proved her virtue by drawing a stranded ship up the Tiber, is one of the most appealing secular pictures of the Sienese Quattrocento.","q14089634":"Miguel Alcañiz, in Valencian Miquel Alcanyís, was a painter active in Valencia in the first half of the fifteenth century, in the International Gothic style that flourished in that wealthy Mediterranean port after the Florentine painter Gherardo Starnina's stay there in the 1390s. He is documented between about 1408 and 1447, painting altarpieces for the churches of Valencia and its region in a rich, courtly style with gold grounds, patterned fabrics and clear narrative. The Metropolitan Museum's panel of about 1408, showing Saint Giles with Christ Triumphant over Satan and the Mission of the Apostles, almost certainly formed part of a larger altarpiece. Little else is recorded of his life, and many such altarpieces survive only in fragments dispersed among museums.","q142710":"Roelant Savery was born at Kortrijk in Flanders in 1576, and his family, Protestant refugees, settled in the northern Netherlands, where he learned to paint with his older brother Jacob and was influenced by the forest landscapes of Gillis van Coninxloo. About 1603 he entered the service of the Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf II at Prague, who sent him to the Tyrol to draw Alpine scenery, which gave his later landscapes their craggy rocks and deep gorges. He is credited with the best-known early images of the dodo, probably drawn from a living bird in the imperial collection. After Rudolf's death in 1612 he returned to the Netherlands and by 1619 had settled in Utrecht, where he died in 1639. He painted landscapes with animals, flower pieces and biblical subjects, such as the Flight into Egypt of 1624.","q1441279":"Francesco de Mura was born in Naples in 1696 and died there in 1782. He was the pupil and assistant of Francesco Solimena, the dominant painter of Naples, and after Solimena's death in 1747 he was regarded as the city's leading painter. He painted frescoes and altarpieces for Neapolitan churches and palaces, worked for Charles of Bourbon, the king of Naples, and also for the court of Savoy in Turin. Where Solimena was dark and dramatic, de Mura moved toward lighter, sweeter colour, with silvery blues and rosy pinks that look forward to the Rococo. The National Gallery of Art's Alexander Condemning False Praise dates from the 1760s. His reputation faded after the neoclassical reaction, but his graceful, fluent style has been appreciated again in recent decades.","q1517240":"Robert Swain Gifford was born in 1840 on Naushon Island, off the Massachusetts coast, and grew up near New Bedford, where he studied painting with the Dutch-born marine painter Albert Van Beest. He moved to New York in 1864 and became known for quiet landscapes of the New England shore, with salt marshes and dunes painted in soft, low tones. He travelled widely, to the Pacific coast, to Europe, and to Egypt and North Africa in 1870 with Louis Comfort Tiffany and Samuel Colman, and he taught design at the Cooper Union for many years. In 1899 he went to Alaska as one of the artists on the Harriman Expedition. His Near the Coast, about 1885, is a typical shore view. He died in New York in 1905, admired in his lifetime as a painter and etcher.","q1529806":"Pietro della Vecchia, born Pietro Muttoni, was a Venetian painter of the seventeenth century, born about 1602 and dead in the 1670s. Said to have trained with Alessandro Varotari, called Padovanino, he spent his career in Venice as a painter and restorer, and was famed for imitating the colours, subjects and poses of Giorgione and Titian so skilfully that his pictures were sometimes taken for theirs. He also painted odd, theatrical subjects: swaggering bravos, soldiers, fortune-tellers, philosophers in exotic dress, and gloomy half-length figures with deep shadows, in a manner that drew on Caravaggio's followers as well as on the Venetian Renaissance. The National Gallery of Art's Imaginary Self-Portrait of Titian, probably painted in the 1650s, is typical of his games with the great Venetian past.","q154349":"Odilon Redon was born in Bordeaux in 1840 and died in Paris in 1916. He studied briefly in Paris with Jean-Léon Gérôme, but his real teachers were the Bordeaux printmaker Rodolphe Bresdin, who taught him etching and lithography, and the botanist Armand Clavaud, who introduced him to the microscopic world and to Darwin. For some thirty years he worked almost entirely in black, in charcoal and lithographs, creating dreamlike images of floating eyes, strange plants and spectral heads, which writers such as Joris-Karl Huysmans praised as the art of the imagination. From about 1890 he turned to colour, in pastel and oil, painting flowers, mythological figures and visionary scenes in glowing hues. The two Breton villages in the National Gallery of Art date from about 1880 and about 1890.","q15637671":"Constantijn van Renesse was born at Maarssen, near Utrecht, in 1626 and died at Eindhoven in 1680. He is recorded as a pupil of Rembrandt in Amsterdam about 1650, and he spent his later life in Eindhoven, in the south of the Netherlands, far from the main centres of Dutch painting. His work includes biblical scenes and pen drawings in the manner of his master, as well as small genre pictures of peasants and townsfolk. The National Gallery of Art's Conviviality Near the Inn, from the early 1650s, belongs to the latter group and shows people eating, drinking and talking outside a village inn. He is a minor figure, and little is recorded of his patrons or his pictures, but he is among the many painters who passed through Rembrandt's workshop.","q156386":"Henri Rousseau was born at Laval in 1844 and died in Paris in 1910. He worked for years as a toll collector at the gates of Paris, which gave him the nickname Le Douanier, and he taught himself to paint, retiring in 1893 to devote his time to art. From 1886 he showed at the Salon des Indépendants, where the unjuried hanging let an untrained painter hang beside the professionals. His jungle scenes, of tigers, lions and sleeping figures among huge leaves and flowers, were composed from visits to the Paris zoo and the hothouses of the Jardin des Plantes, not from travel, though he liked to hint at service in Mexico. Mocked by many critics, he was championed by Alfred Jarry, by Guillaume Apollinaire and by Picasso, who gave a banquet in his honour in 1908.","q1567177":"Michele Gordigiani was born in Florence in 1835 and died there in 1909. The son of the composer Luigi Gordigiani, he became the fashionable portrait painter of Florence and its visitors in the second half of the nineteenth century, painting aristocrats, foreign residents and artists with polished finish and flattering elegance. Among his sitters were Robert and Elizabeth Barrett Browning, who lived in the city. His career coincided with Florence's years as capital of the new kingdom of Italy, from 1865 to 1871, when the city was crowded with courtiers and diplomats. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of 1864 shows a woman identified only as one of two Tuscan noblewomen, Marianna Panciatichi or her sister-in-law, a reminder that even recent portraits can lose their sitters.","q159606":"Sir John Everett Millais was born in Southampton in 1829 into a family from Jersey and showed such early skill that he entered the Royal Academy Schools at eleven. In 1848 he joined Dante Gabriel Rossetti and William Holman Hunt in founding the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood, and in pictures such as Christ in the House of His Parents and Ophelia he painted from nature with fierce precision and brilliant colour; the model for Ophelia, Elizabeth Siddal, lay in a bath of water to pose. In 1855 he married Effie Gray, whose marriage to John Ruskin had been annulled. His later work turned to fluent portraits and popular pictures such as Bubbles. He was made a baronet in 1885, the first painter to receive a hereditary title, and became president of the Royal Academy in 1896, a few months before his death. Portia dates from 1886.","q16038558":"Master of the Prado Adoration of the Magi is a conventional name given by scholars to an anonymous Netherlandish painter of the later fifteenth century, after an Adoration of the Magi in the Prado in Madrid. His real name, birthplace and dates are not known, and the range of 1395 to 1535 given here is only a broad estimate. The National Gallery of Art's Presentation in the Temple, dated about 1470 to 1480, is among the paintings grouped under his name by their similarities of style. In the later fifteenth century the workshops of Bruges, Brussels, Ghent and other Flemish towns produced altarpieces and devotional panels in great numbers, and many of the painters who made them are now known only by such invented names. Nothing further is recorded of his life.","q16065989":"Samuel John Stump lived from 1778 to 1863. The museum record lists him as American, although a miniature painter of the same name and dates is known in London, where he exhibited at the Royal Academy for many years, and it is not settled where he worked or whether the two are one man. He is represented in this collection by a single Portrait of a Man, painted about 1825. By then the portrait trade on both sides of the Atlantic was beginning to change, for the new art of photography, which arrived in the late 1830s, took over much of the demand for small, inexpensive likenesses within a generation. Nothing further about his training, sitters or patrons is recorded.","q161336":"Sir Peter Lely was born Pieter van der Faes at Soest in Westphalia in 1618, to Dutch parents, and took the name Lely from a lily on the family's house at The Hague. He trained at Haarlem under Pieter de Grebber and came to England about 1641, where he succeeded Van Dyck as the leading portraitist, painting Charles I, Cromwell and, after the Restoration, Charles II, whose Principal Painter he became in 1661. He was knighted in 1679. His languid, silken court beauties, now at Hampton Court, and his portraits of the admirals of the Dutch wars made him famous, and his large studio, where assistants painted drapery and backgrounds, turned out likenesses in great numbers. He died suddenly in London in 1680. The Metropolitan Museum holds his double portrait of the Capel sisters.","q1649002":"Ubaldo Gandolfi was born at San Matteo della Decima, near Bologna, in 1728 and died at Ravenna in 1781. He and his younger brother Gaetano both trained at the Accademia Clementina in Bologna and became leading Bolognese painters of the later eighteenth century, working in a manner that looked back to the Carracci and Guercino rather than forward to the new neoclassicism, while also taking something of the lightness of Venetian painters such as Giambattista Tiepolo. Ubaldo painted altarpieces, frescoes and religious and mythological pictures, and was especially praised for the fluent brushwork and strong anatomical drawing of his oil sketches, which collectors prized. The Metropolitan Museum's Execution of Saint John the Baptist dates from about 1770. The family workshop continued with Gaetano's son Mauro.","q16626156":"Romà Ribera Cirera, a Catalan painter, was born in Barcelona in 1849 and died in 1935. He belongs to the generation of Spanish artists who looked to Rome and Paris in the 1870s, drawn by the success of Mariano Fortuny, whose brilliant, finely detailed genre scenes were eagerly bought by international dealers and collectors. Ribera is associated with that polished, colourful manner of figure painting, and he lived to a great age, outliving by many years the fashion in which he first made his name. The National Gallery of Art's Café Singer, dated 1876, comes from the early part of his career, when scenes of fashionable entertainment found a ready market. Little else is recorded of his teachers, patrons or later life.","q166379":"Philips Koninck was born in Amsterdam in 1619 and died there in 1688. He is said to have been a pupil of Rembrandt, and his brother Salomon was also a painter, but his own fame rests on panoramic landscapes, which show the flat Dutch countryside from a raised viewpoint, with wide rivers, distant towns, low horizons and huge skies full of cloud, painted in luminous colour. He lived in Rotterdam in the 1640s, where he is recorded as running a ferry service between Rotterdam and Amsterdam, and was back in Amsterdam by about 1651, painting for a circle of well-off collectors; he also painted portraits and figure subjects in the manner of Rembrandt. He is now ranked among the greatest Dutch landscape painters. The Metropolitan Museum has three of his landscapes, dating from about 1648 to the 1670s.","q16648954":"Seymour Joseph Guy was born at Greenwich, England, in 1824 and moved to New York in 1854, where he became one of the best-known genre painters of the American Gilded Age. He specialised in sentimental domestic scenes of children, among them Story of Golden Locks and The Crossing Sweeper, painted in a tight, polished manner with close attention to costume, furniture and light, and bought by the prosperous middle class of the Northeast. He also painted portraits, including one of the painter Charles Loring Elliott, dated 1868, and a large group portrait, The Contest for the Bouquet, of the family of Robert Gordon in their New York dining room, 1866, which records a wealthy household's furnishings in loving detail. He died in 1910, long after the taste for such pictures had faded.","q16665106":"Monogrammist IS is the name given to an unidentified Flemish portrait painter who marked his work with the initials IS and is otherwise unknown. The museum record gives neither dates nor a birthplace, and no name, teacher or patron is recorded. He is represented by a single work, a Portrait of a Man dated 1621. At that date the Southern Netherlands, and Antwerp above all, was dominated by Rubens, whose workshop and followers also set the standard for portraiture, and many lesser painters supplied serviceable likenesses of merchants, officials and clergy to the local market. Where a painter's name has not survived, a monogram like this one is often the only means by which scholars can group his pictures.","q16738131":"Willem Reuter was a Flemish painter, born in 1642 and dead in 1681, who died before reaching forty. Little is recorded of his training or career, and he appears in this collection through a single picture, Saint John the Baptist Preaching, painted about 1665, when he was in his early twenties. The subject, in which the prophet addresses a crowd in the wilderness, was a favourite with painters of the Low Countries, because it allowed them to show many figures of different ages, costumes and expressions grouped in a landscape; Pieter Bruegel the Elder had painted a famous version in 1566. Reuter worked in the generation after Rubens and Jordaens, when Flemish painting still drew its strength from their example.","q16955578":"Nelson Cook lived from 1808 to 1892 and was an American portrait painter. Little is recorded of his training or career. His two pictures in the Metropolitan Museum are a matched pair of portraits dated 1840, showing James Merrill Cook and Mrs. James Merrill Cook, who share his surname and may have been relatives. Pairs of this kind, made to hang together in a family parlour, were the staple of American portrait painting in the 1830s and 1840s, before the daguerreotype began to take over the trade, and the painters who made them usually offered a plain, honest likeness with careful attention to face and dress. Beyond these facts nothing certain is recorded.","q17006553":"Master of the Saint Catherine Legend is the conventional name for an anonymous Netherlandish painter of the later fifteenth century, active, according to the museum record, between about 1470 and 1500. The name refers to a picture or series showing the legend of Saint Catherine of Alexandria, the learned virgin martyr who, in the story, confounded fifty pagan philosophers and was condemned to death on a spiked wheel. His real name and birthplace are not known, and nothing is recorded of his teachers or patrons. The Metropolitan Museum holds a work by him in three parts, showing Saint Michael, the Mass of Saint Gregory and Saint Jerome. Painters of this kind served the local churches, confraternities and merchants of the Low Countries, and many are now remembered only by invented names.","q1716463":"Nicolas Henri Jacob was born in Paris in 1782 and died there in 1871. He studied under Jacques-Louis David, whose clear outlines and firm modelling shaped his early portraits and history pictures, and he exhibited at the Paris Salon. He is now remembered chiefly, however, as a draughtsman of anatomy. For more than twenty years he drew the large, finely coloured plates for Jean-Baptiste Marc Bourgery's Traité complet de l'anatomie de l'homme, issued in parts between 1831 and 1854, a monumental atlas still admired as a high point of medical illustration. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Woman, dated 1817, comes from the period before he turned to this work, and shows his training in David's school in its clarity and restraint.","q1722657":"Robert Salmon was born at Whitehaven, in the north-west of England, in 1775, and became one of the most accomplished ship and harbour painters of his day. He worked in Liverpool and then at Greenock on the Clyde, painting ships and waterfronts for owners and captains, and about 1828 crossed the Atlantic to Boston, where he painted harbour views and ship portraits for merchants. His pictures are known for their crisp detail, clear, luminous skies and sparkling water, and for a calm, almost miniature precision. He is thought to have left America in the 1840s, and his last years are poorly documented. The National Gallery of Art's Ship Favorite off Greenock, 1819, and Boston Harbor, 1843, belong to the two halves of his career, and the Metropolitan Museum has a view of the Custom House at Greenock.","q1750916":"Pieter Vanderlyn was a Dutch-born portrait painter who lived from about 1687 to 1778 and worked in the Hudson River valley of colonial New York, in Kingston and Albany, painting the families of the old Dutch settlers. He lived to be about ninety. His portraits, such as that of the young girl Susanna Truax, dated 1730, show the plain, flat, linear style of provincial portraiture, with a clear outline, small areas of strong colour and careful detail in lace, jewellery and fabric, inherited from Dutch painting of the previous century. He is often identified with the painter long known as the Gansevoort Limner. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Lady, titled as possibly Hannah Stillman, dates from the 1720s or 1730s, and the identification of its sitter remains uncertain.","q1751436":"Samuel Colman was born in Portland, Maine, in 1832 and died in 1920. He grew up in New York and became a landscape painter of the later Hudson River School, but he also travelled widely, to Spain in the 1860s, to Egypt and North Africa in 1870 with Louis Comfort Tiffany and R. Swain Gifford, and to the American West in the 1880s, painting scenes such as Spanish Peaks, Southern Colorado, Late Afternoon of 1887. A founder and first president of the American Society of Painters in Water Colors, he was also a keen collector of Asian art and, in 1879, a partner with Tiffany, Candace Wheeler and Lockwood de Forest in the decorating firm Associated Artists. His picture of the finish of the first America's Cup challenge race, in 1870, records a famous sporting event.","q18155785":"Reuben Moulthrop was born in 1763 and died in 1814, and worked as a portrait painter in Connecticut, in and around New Haven. He painted the merchants, ministers, sea captains and their families of his region in a plain, direct manner, with strong outline, bright local colour and a flat, close-up effect that places him with the folk portraitists of the early republic. The Metropolitan Museum has a pair of his portraits dated 1790, of Job Perit and his wife Sally Sanford Perit, painted when the artist was about twenty-seven. Beyond this, little is recorded of his training, patrons or later reputation, and his work is known now chiefly to specialists in early American portraiture, who value such pictures as records of ordinary New England households.","q18157356":"Samuel King was born in Newport, Rhode Island, in 1748 and died there in 1819. He was a maker of mathematical and navigational instruments as well as a painter, a combination that was not unusual in colonial towns, and he is best known as a portraitist of the leading citizens of Newport, then one of the busiest ports and wealthiest towns in the colonies. His portrait of the minister Ezra Stiles, dated 1770, shows the learned pastor of the Second Congregational Church, who later became president of Yale College. King was also a teacher: among his pupils in Newport were the young Washington Allston and the miniature painter Edward Greene Malbone, both of whom went on to much greater fame.","q18210625":"Nicolaos Tzafouris was a Greek painter of the Cretan school, active in the late fifteenth century at Candia, the modern Heraklion, then the chief city of Venetian Crete. He is documented in the years around 1487 to 1501, and only a small number of paintings are attributed to him. Cretan painters of his day worked at the meeting point of two traditions, the Byzantine icon and the Italian devotional picture, and Tzafouris is notable for an unusually Western manner, with fuller modelling and Italian-style compositions. The Metropolitan Museum's Christ Bearing the Cross shows this blending. Cretan icons were exported widely, to Venice and the Aegean islands, and made the island one of the busiest centres of panel painting in the eastern Mediterranean until Candia fell to the Turks in 1669.","q18217672":"Agnolo di Domenico del Mazziere was a Florentine painter, born in 1466 and dead in 1513, who worked in the generation of Leonardo, Michelangelo and Raphael. His added name, del Mazziere, means of the mace-bearer, presumably from the occupation of a forebear. Little is recorded of his training, patrons or commissions, and he is known to this collection by a single work, the National Gallery of Art's Portrait of a Youth, dated about 1495 to 1500. Florentine portraits of this period often show a young man in half-length against a plain or landscape ground, in a clear, well-drawn style that owed much to the great workshops of the city, and they were commissioned by families as records of sons and as gifts on betrothal. Little else of his career is recorded.","q182882":"John James Audubon was born in 1785 at Les Cayes, in what is now Haiti, the illegitimate son of a French naval officer and plantation owner, and was raised in France at Nantes. Sent to America in 1803 to manage a family property near Philadelphia, he failed at business in Kentucky but taught himself to draw and paint birds from life, wiring freshly killed specimens into natural poses. After years of travel and rejection by American publishers he sailed to Britain in 1826, where the engraver Robert Havell Jr. produced The Birds of America, 435 hand-coloured plates on double elephant folio sheets, issued between 1827 and 1838 and sold to subscribers. He later worked on a book of American mammals, and died in New York in 1851. His watercolours include Farmyard Fowls and Osprey and Weakfish.","q18508546":"Monogrammist LAM is the name used for an unidentified painter who marked his work with the initials LAM, and whom the museum lists as French. Nothing is recorded of his birth, death or training, and no further name is recorded. His one picture in this collection is a Portrait of a Man in White, dated 1574, now in the Metropolitan Museum. In France in the 1570s, in the midst of the Wars of Religion, portraits were still made in the manner of the court painter François Clouet and his circle, with small, precise, restrained likenesses of nobles, officials and clerics against plain backgrounds, and this picture should be understood in that setting. Beyond the monogram and the date, nothing is known.","q18508617":"Platt Powell Ryder was an American painter, born in 1821 and dead in 1896, who worked mainly as a portraitist in New York. Little is recorded of his training. His two portraits in the Metropolitan Museum show the sort of sitter he attracted: the publisher George P. Putnam, founder of the firm that still bears his name, painted in 1872, and the New York book collector Stephen Whitney Phoenix, painted in 1892. Portraits of this kind, ordered by families, clubs and institutions to hang in boardrooms and libraries, formed a steady market for American painters in the later nineteenth century, and Ryder is typical of the artists who supplied it, producing sober, dignified likenesses of professional men.","q18526619":"Beatrix Whistler was born Beatrix Birnie Philip in 1857, the daughter of the sculptor John Birnie Philip, and died in 1896. She first married the architect and designer Edward William Godwin, in 1876, and after his death in 1886 married the American painter James McNeill Whistler, in 1888. She was herself an artist and designer, and she became Whistler's model and assistant, a calm and capable companion to a difficult man; she helped to manage his affairs and posed for several of his late pictures. She died of cancer in 1896, at about thirty-nine, and Whistler never recovered from the loss. The National Gallery of Art's Peach Blossom dates from the early 1890s, the years the couple spent partly in Paris.","q1856567":"Robert William Vonnoh was born in Hartford, Connecticut, in 1858 and trained in Boston at the Massachusetts Normal Art School, where he later taught, and then in Paris at the Académie Julian. Living partly in France in the later 1880s, he spent time at the artists' colony at Grez-sur-Loing and absorbed Impressionist methods; his Poppies of 1890, painted there, is often cited as an early American Impressionist picture. He taught at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts from 1896 to 1909, and was admired as a portraitist of the American upper class. His second wife, Bessie Potter Vonnoh, was a sculptor. He died at Nice in 1933. Notre Dame de Paris, about 1890, belongs to his years in France.","q18575690":"William Hudson Jr. was born about 1787 and died about 1861, and no more precise dates are known. He is recorded as a portrait painter of the early nineteenth century, and he is represented here by a single picture in the Metropolitan Museum, a Portrait of a Gentleman, painted in 1821, whose sitter has not been identified. Many American portrait painters of his generation worked without academy training, learning their craft in the studios of older men or by copying prints, and travelled from town to town to find sitters among merchants, farmers and professional men. Of his training, his patrons and his career, nothing certain is recorded, and the entry can add little more.","q18577807":"William Mason Brown was born in Troy, New York, in 1828 and died in 1898. He worked in the New York area and is remembered chiefly as a painter of still lifes of fruit, among them cherries, peaches, grapes and melons, laid out on a ledge or table in sharp, clear light, though he also painted landscapes. His fruit pieces, with their rich surfaces and abundance of detail, found a ready audience among the middle-class buyers of the years after the Civil War, who liked a picture of plenty for the dining room. The National Gallery of Art's Mound of Cherries is undated. Little is recorded of his training, and he remains a minor figure, known to students of American still-life painting rather than to the wider public.","q18593505":"Thomas Forster was an English portraitist, active in London about 1690 to 1713, who is known for small, finely finished portraits, a number of them in black lead, or plumbago, on vellum, a precise and delicate technique perfected a generation earlier by Robert White. Few details of his life are recorded. The Metropolitan Museum holds four of his portraits of men and women, dated between 1700 and 1705, of the kind made as keepsakes or as records of family members. In the reign of William III and Queen Anne, many sitters wanted a likeness that was smaller and cheaper than an oil painting, and artists like Forster supplied them, often signing and dating the work with a small flourish.","q186816":"Ferdinand Georg Waldmüller was born in Vienna in 1793 and died near the city in 1865. He studied at the Vienna Academy and became one of the leading painters of the Biedermeier period, known for sharp, luminous portraits, sunlit scenes of peasants and children, and landscapes of the Vienna Woods and the Alps, painted with an almost photographic clarity and a bright, cool palette. He taught at the Academy from 1829, but his insistence that students should paint directly from nature, rather than from antique casts and idealised models, brought him into conflict with its directors; he published his criticisms and was forced into early retirement in 1857. His double portrait of the cartographer Josef Jüttner and his wife, dated 1824, comes from the years when he was the portraitist of choice for Vienna's educated middle class.","q18811106":"Nicholas Dixon was an English miniature painter active in London from the 1660s into the early eighteenth century, in the age of Samuel Cooper and Peter Lely. He is recorded as a limner to King Charles II, and he was much employed in making small copies of Lely's paintings, so that sitters and their families could have portable versions of fashionable pictures. Little else of his life is recorded. The Metropolitan Museum's portrait of Sir Henry Blount, from the 1660s, shows a sitter of unusual interest: Blount, who lived from 1602 to 1682, was a courtier and traveller who journeyed to Constantinople and published in 1636 A Voyage into the Levant, one of the most widely read travel books of the seventeenth century.","q1918313":"Master of the Saint Ursula Legend is the conventional name for an anonymous Netherlandish painter of the late fifteenth century, usually placed in Bruges, who takes his name from a series of scenes from the legend of Saint Ursula, the British princess said to have been martyred at Cologne with eleven thousand companions. His real name and dates are not known. He worked in the tradition of the great Bruges painters of the age, notably Hans Memling, and painted altarpieces and devotional panels with clear colour, delicate faces and careful landscape. The Metropolitan Museum's work, about 1485, combines Saint Paul with a donor and Christ appearing to his mother, a pairing typical of a private devotional altarpiece in which the patron prays beside the saints.","q1918409":"Master of the Mansi Magdalen is the conventional name for an anonymous painter of the early sixteenth century, usually placed in Antwerp, who takes his name from a Mary Magdalen in the Palazzo Mansi in Lucca. His real name and dates are not known. He is associated with the circle of Quentin Massys, the leading Antwerp painter of the time, and he and his workshop painted half-length Madonnas, Magdalens and other devotional figures in a smooth, graceful, slightly sweet manner, with rich costume, clear colour and a landscape glimpsed at the side, many of them made in numbers for the busy Antwerp art market, which exported pictures widely. The Metropolitan Museum's Virgin and Child, which the museum does not date more closely, is typical of that large and anonymous production.","q1918459":"Master of the Washington Coronation is the conventional name for an anonymous Italian painter active in the early fourteenth century, named after the Coronation of the Virgin in the National Gallery of Art in Washington, dated 1324. His real name is unknown, and the range of 1300 to 1333 given for him is only a broad estimate of his career. The picture is unusual because its exact date is known, a rare luxury in a century of mostly undated panel paintings, and so it can help scholars to place related works. The Coronation of the Virgin, in which Christ crowns his mother in heaven among angels, was one of the most popular subjects of the time, painted for churches and convents throughout Italy.","q1918545":"Master of the Holy Blood is the conventional name for an anonymous Netherlandish painter; his real name and dates are not recorded, and it is not clear which picture gave him his name. The name suggests a link with the devotion to the Holy Blood, the relic of Christ's blood that was venerated in Bruges and for which pilgrims travelled to that city, but nothing more can be said of his workshop or patrons. The Metropolitan Museum holds a single picture by him, a Descent from the Cross, the scene in which the dead body of Christ is lowered from the cross by Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus while the Virgin and the holy women look on. The subject was a favourite of Netherlandish painters ever since Rogier van der Weyden's great version of the 1430s.","q1918711":"Master of the View of Saint Gudula is the conventional name for an anonymous Netherlandish painter of the late fifteenth century, named after a picture that shows the church of Saint Gudula, now the Cathedral of Saints Michael and Gudula, in Brussels, so that he is usually placed in that city. His real name and dates are not known. The Metropolitan Museum's Young Man Holding a Book, about 1480, is a portrait of the kind that was popular in the Netherlands in the years after Rogier van der Weyden and Hans Memling: a half-length figure turned slightly toward the viewer, shown with a book or other attribute that suggests his learning or piety. Nothing is recorded of his teachers or patrons, and he is known only through a small group of works.","q192488":"Paolo di Dono, called Uccello, meaning bird, was born in Florence in 1397 and died there in 1475. As a boy he was apprenticed to the sculptor Lorenzo Ghiberti, and he worked on Florence Cathedral, where he painted a clock face, designed stained-glass windows for the dome and frescoed the equestrian monument of the mercenary captain Sir John Hawkwood. He spent some years in Venice making mosaics for San Marco, and is best known for the three panels of the Battle of San Romano, painted for the Medici and now divided between London, Paris and Florence, in which broken lances and foreshortened horses show off the new science of linear perspective. In a tax return of 1469 he described himself as old and poor, his wife ill. The Metropolitan Museum's Crucifixion is dated probably to the mid-1450s.","q192496":"Robert Fulton was born in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, in 1765 and died in New York in 1815. He is famous as an engineer, but he began as a painter, making miniature portraits and landscapes in Philadelphia, and in 1786 he sailed to London to study with Benjamin West, a fellow American, remaining in England for about ten years, exhibiting at the Royal Academy and turning gradually to engineering. He moved to Paris in 1797, where he exhibited panoramas, tested a submarine, the Nautilus, in 1800 and began work on steam navigation. His Clermont, financed by Robert R. Livingston, steamed up the Hudson from New York to Albany in 1807 and began the first commercially successful steamboat service. His portraits in the Metropolitan Museum range from the 1780s to about 1813.","q1936604":"Reinier Nooms, called Zeeman, was born in Amsterdam about 1623 and died there about 1664. The nickname means seaman, and he is said to have gone to sea in his youth before turning to art. He is best known as a painter and above all as an etcher of ships, harbours and the waterfronts of Amsterdam, Paris and other cities, in clear, crisp drawing that gave later marine painters a store of accurate models for hulls and rigging. His Amsterdam Harbor Scene, dated about 1654 to 1655, comes from the years when the Dutch republic was the greatest maritime power in Europe and its harbours the busiest in the world. His etched series of ships and cityscapes were widely collected, and are still prized.","q19423968":"Walter Gould lived from 1829 to 1893 and was an American painter. Few details of his life are known. The one picture of his in this collection, in the Metropolitan Museum, is a portrait of Edward Ranstead Jones, dated 1847, when Gould was about eighteen, and it shows him at the very beginning of his career as a portraitist. Young American painters of the 1840s often learned their craft by working in the studios of established men, by copying prints and pictures, and by taking commissions from local families as soon as they had the skill. Nothing further of his training or later career is recorded.","q194402":"Sir Joshua Reynolds was born at Plympton, Devon, in 1723 and trained in London under the portrait painter Thomas Hudson before spending three years in Italy from 1749, where he studied the old masters, above all Raphael, and caught a cold in the Vatican that left him partly deaf. Settled in London from 1753, he became the leading portrait painter in Britain, raising the genre by borrowing poses from antique sculpture and Renaissance painting and by giving his sitters, among them peers, actresses and children, a dignity he called the grand style. In 1768 he became the first president of the Royal Academy, and his fifteen Discourses on art, delivered to its students, shaped British taste. He was knighted in 1769 and died in London in 1792. His portraits in the National Gallery of Art include Lady Elizabeth Delmé and her children.","q19543172":"Master of the Béguins is the conventional name for an unidentified painter of the mid-seventeenth century, French or Flemish, active about 1650 to 1660, whose pictures of poor families and beggars are close in feeling to those of the Le Nain brothers, the French painters of peasant life; the name apparently refers to the béguins, the small caps worn by the children in his pictures. Some scholars have suggested that he may be the Flemish painter Abraham Willemsens, who was active by 1627 and died in 1672, but the identification is not settled. The Metropolitan Museum's Beggars at a Doorway shows the quiet, unsentimental, dignified treatment of poverty that makes such pictures appealing to modern viewers. Nothing certain is known of his training or patrons.","q19569582":"Master of the Catholic Kings is the conventional name for an anonymous painter active in Spain in the later fifteenth century, in the age of Ferdinand and Isabella, the Catholic Monarchs, who united Castile and Aragon and completed the conquest of Granada in 1492. The name is usually linked to a Madonna altarpiece in which the royal couple kneel with their children; it is not a personal name, and his real identity, dates and training are unknown. The range 1420 to 1545 given here is only a broad estimate. The National Gallery of Art holds two Gospel scenes of about 1495 to 1497 by him, the Marriage at Cana and Christ among the Doctors. Queen Isabella also employed Netherlandish painters such as Juan de Flandes and Michel Sittow, and Flemish models were influential in Spain.","q19670719":"Wilhelm Koller, who also appears under the French form Guillaume Koller, was an Austrian painter, born in 1829 and dead in 1884. Little is recorded of his training and career, and the museum's use of the French form of his first name suggests that he worked for a time in France. His one picture in the Metropolitan Museum, Hugo van der Goes Making a Portrait of Mary of Burgundy, painted about 1872, belongs to a nineteenth-century fashion for small historical anecdotes about the lives of the old masters, in which Renaissance painters are shown at work in their studios and among patrons. Hugo van der Goes was a great Flemish painter of the fifteenth century, and Mary of Burgundy the young duchess who ruled the Burgundian Netherlands from 1477 until her death in 1482.","q1969461":"Thomas Fearnley was born at Fredrikshald, now Halden, in Norway in 1802 and died in Munich in 1842, aged only thirty-nine. After early studies in Stockholm and Copenhagen he is said to have worked with the Norwegian painter J. C. Dahl in Dresden, and he travelled in Germany, Italy, France and Britain, where in London in 1836 he saw and was influenced by the paintings of Constable and Turner. Fearnley became, with Dahl, one of the founders of the Norwegian landscape tradition, painting waterfalls, fjords and mountain scenes in a blend of close observation and Romantic grandeur; his Labrofossen of 1837 is among the best known. The Metropolitan Museum's Gudvangen, 1839, shows the head of one of Norway's narrow fjords. His early death cut short a career of great promise.","q1982374":"Pietro da Rimini was a painter of the Riminese school, which flourished in the Adriatic city of Rimini in the first half of the fourteenth century, after Giotto's visit there, about 1300 to 1310, had left a lasting mark on local artists. He was active in the earlier fourteenth century, but the museum record gives no dates and little is firmly recorded of his life. Works attributed to him include altarpieces and frescoes in the Romagna and the Marche, in a lively, emotional narrative style with bright, enamel-like colour and expressive faces, which is more restless than Giotto's calm monumentality. The Metropolitan Museum's Crucified Christ, which the museum does not date precisely, is an example of the devotional Crucifixions made in large numbers in the school.","q1985982":"William Trost Richards was born in Philadelphia in 1833 and studied there with the German-born landscape painter Paul Weber. Influenced by the writings of John Ruskin, he painted in the 1850s and 1860s with the minute fidelity to nature of the American Pre-Raphaelites, producing close studies of leaves, rocks and woodland in the Adirondacks, in Pennsylvania and in New Jersey, such as October of 1863. After visits to Europe he turned from the 1870s to the sea, painting waves breaking on the coasts of New Jersey, Rhode Island and Cornwall in an increasingly fluid, atmospheric manner, and became the best-known American painter of the sea in his day. He lived at Newport, Rhode Island, and died there in 1905. His daughter Anna Richards Brewster also became a painter.","q19887584":"William Johnston was born in 1732 and died in 1772, and was a portrait painter in colonial Boston. Little is recorded of his training or career, and he worked in the same town as John Singleton Copley, whose polished, richly coloured portraits of the 1760s set a standard that artists such as Johnston could not match. The Metropolitan Museum holds four of his portraits: Jacob Hurd and Mrs. Jacob Hurd with her child, painted about 1762, and Samuel Gardiner and Mrs. Samuel Gardiner, painted in 1763. Portraits of this kind were commissioned by the families of merchants, craftsmen and professional men in Boston, then one of the largest towns in British North America. He died at about forty.","q19890737":"The Schuyler Limner is the name given to an anonymous colonial American portrait painter, a limner being an old term for a painter of portraits, usually without academic training. He is apparently named after the Schuyler family, one of the leading Dutch families of Albany and the Hudson valley, and his real name is not known; the dates 1697 to 1785 attached to him here are only a broad range. The National Gallery of Art has two of his portraits, Mr. Van Vechten, dated 1719, and Mr. Willson, dated 1720, painted when Dutch and English settlers in New York and the Hudson valley commissioned likenesses in the flat, linear, brightly coloured style inherited from the portraits of the Netherlands in the previous century.","q19975177":"William Harrison Scarborough lived from 1812 to 1871 and was an American portrait painter. Few details of his training or career are known, and he is represented in the Metropolitan Museum by two portraits of ladies, both dated about 1840. Portraits of this kind, made in the years before the daguerreotype began to take over the trade, were commissioned by families who wanted a record of wives and daughters in their best dress, and the painters who supplied them, working in cities and small towns across the young republic, usually combined careful drawing of the face with detailed, decorative treatment of lace, jewellery and hair. Scarborough's pair offers a glimpse of that world, though little more can be said of him here.","q20116880":"William Wood was born in 1769 and died in 1810, and was a British portrait painter. Little is recorded of his life, though the Metropolitan Museum's three works show something of his range: a portrait of Joanna de Silva, dated 1792, a man possibly named William Abington, about 1798, and a picture of 1806 titled An Interesting Story (Miss Ray). In the years around 1800 small portraits were fashionable keepsakes in Britain, exchanged as gifts, set into lockets and rings and kept as memorials, and the best portraitists in the genre were kept busy by a large public. Wood died at forty-one, and the little that is known of him has to be pieced together from his pictures.","q2012122":"Peter Monamy was born in London in 1681, of a family from Jersey, and died there in 1749. He began as a painter's apprentice on London Bridge, learning the trade of a house and sign painter, and is said to have taught himself marine painting by studying the sea pictures of the Van de Veldes, the Dutch father and son who had worked in England for Charles II. He became the leading English marine painter of his time, producing calm harbour scenes, royal yachts, naval actions and storms in a style close to theirs, sold to naval officers, merchants and collectors. His work influenced Samuel Scott and other later marine painters. The Metropolitan Museum's Harbor Scene, in which an English ship fires a gun with its sails loosened, is typical of his subjects.","q20127275":"Thomas Thompson was an English-born painter who worked in the United States; the museum record gives no dates, and little of his life is known. He is represented by one picture in the Metropolitan Museum, The U. S. Ship Franklin, with a View of the Bay of New York, painted in the 1820s or 1830s. The Franklin was a 74-gun ship of the line, launched at Philadelphia in 1815, one of the largest warships in the American navy, and ship portraits of this kind, made for naval officers, owners and the public, were a staple of the marine painter's trade in the great ports. Thompson's picture, with its view of New York's busy bay behind the great ship, is a record of that trade.","q22667536":"Miss Leland is known only by a title and a surname; the museum record gives neither a first name nor any dates, and no biography is known. She is represented by one picture, a Portrait of a Boy painted about 1840, now in the Metropolitan Museum. Women painters in the early United States are often recorded in this fragmentary way. Many taught drawing and painting at girls' academies, took portrait commissions from neighbours, or travelled from town to town, and the pictures they left, usually unsigned or signed with a simple name, were later filed as folk portraits. Where a name has survived at all, as it has here, it gives scholars a thread to follow in town records, newspapers and family papers.","q22669543":"Samuel V. Homan is known only from a brief span of activity, the years from 1841 to 1845, and neither his birth and death dates nor his birthplace are known. He is represented by one picture in the Metropolitan Museum, a Portrait of a Boy painted about 1843. The date places the picture in the first years of the daguerreotype, which reached America in 1839 and rapidly became a cheaper rival to the painted likeness. Many portrait painters of these years either took up photography or left the trade, and a career that can be traced for only five years may reflect that change as much as any personal history. Nothing certain is recorded of his training or his sitters.","q22669548":"Clarissa Peters was born in 1809 and died in 1854, and is recorded as Mrs. Moses B. Russell after her marriage to the painter Moses B. Russell. She painted portraits in her own right, and the museum attributes to her The Starbird Children, dated 1841, a group portrait of brothers and sisters. Her husband was also a portrait painter, and in the early nineteenth century painting was one of the few occupations open to women who wished to earn a living from their own skill, often by working alongside a husband or father in a family studio. Little else is recorded of her training, her patrons or the rest of her career, and she died at about forty-five.","q22669550":"Thomas Dawson was an American portrait painter, born about 1800 and last recorded after 1850, and no more exact dates are known. He is represented by a single picture in the Metropolitan Museum, a Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1844, whose sitter is not named. Unnamed sitters are common among the American portraits of the period, because pictures were handed down in families, lost their labels, and were sold and resold by dealers long after the people who commissioned them had died. Nothing certain is known of Dawson's training, his place of work or his patrons, and the entry rests on the one dated portrait and the approximate years of his life.","q22669631":"Samuel Folwell was born in 1764 and died in 1813, and is recorded as working in Philadelphia as an engraver and drawing teacher as well as a painter. His one picture in the Metropolitan Museum, Memorial to Peter Philip Walter, dated between 1800 and 1805, belongs to the American mourning picture, a form that became popular after the death of George Washington in 1799. Such pictures commonly show a tomb or urn, a weeping willow and grieving figures, with the name and dates of the dead person inscribed below, and were made in watercolour, ink or silk embroidery, often by schoolgirls at female academies and sometimes from designs supplied by drawing masters. Little else is recorded of Folwell's training or career.","q22669652":"William Lydston Jr. was an American painter, born about 1813 and dead in 1881, and no more precise dates are known. He is represented by two pictures in the Metropolitan Museum, both titled Portrait of a Child, one dated 1842 and the other undated. Child portraits of this kind were among the commonest commissions of the 1840s, when a family would have a son or daughter painted as a keepsake, in a plain, clearly lit style, often with a toy or a flower in the hand. Nothing is recorded of Lydston's training, place of work or patrons, and his two pictures are the only evidence for his career.","q22670107":"William Warner Jr. was an American portrait painter, born about 1813 and dead in 1848, so that he died at about thirty-five. Few other details are known. He is represented in the Metropolitan Museum by a single Portrait of a Gentleman, dated 1836, when he was in his early twenties, and the sitter has not been identified. Painters of his generation often began working professionally in their teens, learning from a master or from pattern books, and moved between towns in search of commissions; a career that ended in the late 1840s lies close to the arrival of the daguerreotype. Nothing certain is recorded of his training or patrons.","q22670111":"William H. Schenck is known as a painter active in New York from about 1854 to 1864, and no birth or death dates are known. His one picture in the Metropolitan Museum, The Third Avenue Railroad Depot, painted about 1859 to 1860, is an unusual and valuable record of the city's transport. The Third Avenue Railroad ran horse-drawn cars up Manhattan's Third Avenue, and its depot was a busy place, where cars and horses were kept. Pictures of working buildings like this are much rarer than portraits or landscapes, and they give historians exact information on vehicles, dress and street life that few other sources preserve. Nothing is recorded of Schenck's training.","q22673609":"Otto Boetticher was a German-born painter, born about 1816, who worked in New York in the 1850s and is not traced after 1864. He is best known for pictures of military subjects. His Seventh Regiment on Review, Washington Square, New York, dated 1851, in the Metropolitan Museum, shows the city's best-known militia unit, nicknamed the Silk Stocking Regiment because its ranks were filled with the sons of the wealthy, on parade in what was then a chief parade ground. The regiment later became famous as one of the first to march to the defence of Washington in April 1861. Little else is recorded of Boetticher's training, patrons or later life, and his date of death is unknown.","q22673641":"Samuel H. Sexton was born in 1813 and died in 1890, and was an American painter whose career is known chiefly through a single portrait. The Metropolitan Museum's Levi Hale Willard, dated 1857, shows a named sitter, which is not always the case with portraits of the period, so that the picture can be linked to a family and a place by genealogical research. Nothing certain is recorded of Sexton's training, place of work or other pictures. His long life, from 1813 to 1890, spanned the whole age of American portrait painting, from the itinerant limners of the early republic to the rise of the commercial photograph studio.","q22691230":"William A. Watkins is recorded as active from about 1825 to 1867, a working life of some forty years, and no birth or death dates are known. He is represented by a single portrait in the Metropolitan Museum, L. P. Church, painted in 1834, when he was already some years into his career. A span of activity as long as this suggests a painter who adapted to changing tastes and may have worked in several towns, as many portraitists of the early republic did, but the details of his training, his travels and his sitters are not recorded. The sitter is identified only by initials and a surname, L. P. Church.","q22691343":"Mrs. Dove is known only by a title and a surname; the museum record gives no first name or dates, and no biography is known. She is represented by a single picture in the Metropolitan Museum, a portrait of Mrs. Edward Loyd, whose maiden name was C. Louisa Foster, painted about 1835. Among portraitists of the early nineteenth century there were women who worked as teachers, as the wives or daughters of artists, or as amateurs who found that their skill could earn money, and their names were seldom recorded with care. That the sitter is identified by both her married and her maiden name helps to place the picture within a family, even though the painter herself remains hard to trace.","q22704559":"W. H. Bean is recorded with the approximate dates of about 1830 to about 1900, and no birthplace or biography is known. His one work in the Metropolitan Museum, American Eagle on Red Scroll, is dated simply between 1860 and 1900. The American eagle was the favourite emblem of the republic from its earliest years, and in the decades after the Civil War it appeared on flags, sign boards, furniture, carved and painted wooden panels and the work of folk painters who made patriotic pictures for homes, lodges and shops. Such pictures were usually made by craftsmen rather than academy-trained artists, and the makers' names seldom survive, so that the survival of Bean's initials and surname is itself unusual.","q2272816":"Peder Balke was born in Hedemarken, Norway, in 1804, of modest family, and died in Christiania, now Oslo, in 1887. He began as a decorative painter, studied in Stockholm and then, about 1829, briefly in Dresden with J. C. Dahl, the leading Norwegian landscape painter. In 1832 he travelled to the far north of Norway as far as the North Cape, and the experience shaped the rest of his career: for decades he painted dramatic, visionary views of Arctic cliffs, stormy seas and moonlit waves, in a bold, simplified manner far ahead of his day. In the 1840s his northern pictures found favour in Paris with King Louis-Philippe, whose fall in 1848 ended the prospect of royal patronage. Balke died in poverty, and his reputation was recovered only in the twentieth century. The Metropolitan Museum's North Cape by Moonlight is dated 1848.","q2295535":"Ippolito Scarsella, called Scarsellino, was born in Ferrara about 1550 and died there in 1620. The son and pupil of the painter Sigismondo Scarsella, he is said to have studied in Venice with Paolo Veronese and to have admired Correggio, and he became the leading painter of Ferrara in the last decades of the Este dukes, whose court there ended in 1598, when the city passed to the Pope. He painted altarpieces for the churches of Ferrara and its region, and small, charming cabinet pictures of religious and mythological subjects, with soft Venetian colour, glowing light and landscape backgrounds that he treated with unusual care. He is regarded as one of the last important painters of the Ferrarese school. The Metropolitan Museum's Virgin Adored by Saints dates from about 1609.","q2309952":"Peter Edward Stroely was a German painter whose dates are not given in the museum record, and little of his life is known. He is represented by one picture, a Portrait of a Woman painted about 1800, now in the Metropolitan Museum. German-trained portrait painters of this period often travelled widely in search of patrons, to London, Copenhagen, St Petersburg and even America, and the museum record does not say where he worked. Many such painters are now remembered only by a few signed pictures, and variant spellings of their names add to the difficulty of tracing their careers. Of his training, his patrons and his later years nothing certain is recorded, and the picture itself is the main evidence for his skill.","q234370":"Paula Becker was born in Dresden in 1876 and grew up in Bremen. She trained in Berlin, and in 1898 joined the artists' colony at Worpswede, near Bremen, where she married the landscape painter Otto Modersohn in 1901 and became friends with the poet Rainer Maria Rilke. Restless in the colony, she made four extended visits to Paris between 1900 and 1907, studied there, and learned from the work of Cézanne, Gauguin and Van Gogh, which she saw in the galleries. Her paintings of peasants, children, mothers and her own self-portraits, including nudes, are simplified, monumental and direct in a way that anticipates Expressionism. She sold few works, and died at Worpswede in 1907, aged thirty-one, soon after the birth of her daughter. Old Factory dates from 1900. The Nazis later condemned her art as degenerate.","q237726":"Rosalba Carriera was born in Venice in 1673 and died there in 1757. She began by painting miniatures on ivory for snuffbox lids, then turned to pastel, a technique she raised to a high art in portraits of delicate colour and soft, powdery finish. Her fame spread across Europe through the visitors who came to Venice, among them English and Irish gentlemen on the Grand Tour, such as Gustavus Hamilton, Viscount Boyne, whom she portrayed in masquerade costume in 1730 and 1731. In 1720 and 1721 she visited Paris, was admitted to the Royal Academy and was lionised by the court and collectors, and in 1730 she went to Vienna. Augustus III of Saxony assembled a large group of her pastels, now in Dresden. She became blind in her last years. Her light, flattering manner became a model for the Rococo portrait.","q239394":"Édouard Vuillard was born in 1868 at Cuiseaux, in eastern France, and was brought up in Paris by his widowed mother, a dressmaker and corset-maker who ran her workshop from their apartment, where he lived until her death in 1928 and which became the subject of many of his early pictures. At school he befriended Ker-Xavier Roussel and Maurice Denis, and he joined the Nabis, the group of young painters who admired Gauguin and flattened their shapes and patterned their surfaces. His small, soft, richly patterned interiors of the 1890s, such as those in the National Gallery of Art, show women sewing, reading and talking in crowded rooms. He also designed theatre sets and decorative panels for patrons such as the Natanson family, and later became a portraitist of Parisian society. He died at La Baule in 1940.","q241732":"Rosa Bonheur was born in Bordeaux in 1822 and grew up in Paris, where her father, the painter Raymond Bonheur, taught her and her siblings, and where she studied animals directly, drawing in the stables and slaughterhouses and visiting horse markets. She became the most celebrated animal painter of her age. Her huge Horse Fair, shown at the Paris Salon in 1853 and finished in 1855, brought her international fame, toured Britain and America, and was given to the Metropolitan Museum by Cornelius Vanderbilt in 1887. She obtained an official permit to wear trousers in order to work in the markets, and in 1865 Empress Eugénie, visiting her studio at By, near Fontainebleau, awarded her the Legion of Honour, the first woman artist so honoured. She died in 1899.","q24238356":"Unknown Artist is not one painter but a label that gathers together the pictures of many whose names have not survived, so the five works listed here have no common author. They include a portrait of Catherina Elmendorf, dated 1752, whose surname belongs to a Dutch family of colonial New York; a portrait of M. T. Webb, about 1830; two scenes of New York street life about 1827, Moving Day and The Five Points, the latter showing the notorious slum district of lower Manhattan, whose crowded streets later horrified visitors including Charles Dickens in 1842; and a Chinese view of the Whampoa Anchorage of 1840, made for Western merchants at the river port below Canton, where foreign ships had to anchor. Each is valued for the world it records rather than for a named hand.","q2425777":"Thomas Lefebure is recorded as a Flemish painter, born in 1636 and dead in 1720, who lived to be about eighty-four, and little more is known of his life. He is represented by a single picture in the Metropolitan Museum, Vertumnus and Pomona, dated 1676. The subject comes from the Metamorphoses of the Roman poet Ovid, in which Vertumnus, god of the changing seasons, takes the form of an old woman in order to win the love of Pomona, goddess of fruit trees, and finally reveals himself as a handsome youth. Painters of the Southern Netherlands often chose such stories because they allowed a graceful mixture of figures, fruit and garden scenery. Nothing certain is recorded of his teachers or patrons.","q247005":"Salomon van Ruysdael was born at Naarden about 1602 and died in Haarlem in 1670. He settled in Haarlem, joined the painters' guild in 1623, and became one of the leading Dutch landscape painters of the mid-seventeenth century, with Jan van Goyen and Pieter Molijn, in the tonal manner, using soft greens, browns and silvery greys. He is best known for calm river views with ferries, sailing boats, trees and village churches under broad skies, as in the National Gallery of Art's River Landscape with Ferry of 1649; his later pictures are brighter in colour and higher in their skies. He was the uncle of Jacob van Ruisdael, the greatest Dutch landscape painter, who may have learned from him. The family name, spelled Ruysdael or Ruisdael, is said to come from a castle near Blaricum.","q247183":"Cornelis van Poelenburch was born in Utrecht about 1594 and died there in 1667. He was a pupil of Abraham Bloemaert, then went to Rome about 1617, where he settled among the Dutch and Flemish painters and came under the influence of Adam Elsheimer's small, jewel-like paintings. He returned to Utrecht in 1625 and became an influential Italianate painter, making small, smooth panels and copper plates of Roman ruins and sunlit hills, with bathing nymphs and shepherds, as well as small religious and mythological scenes. In 1637 he visited England and worked for Charles I. His clear, graceful pictures were sought by Dutch and foreign collectors. The National Gallery of Art's two small pictures, Christ Carrying the Cross and Elijah and the Widow of Zarephath, show his figure style.","q248707":"Maurice Prendergast was born in St John's, Newfoundland, in 1858 and grew up in Boston, where he worked as a show-card painter and commercial artist before saving enough to study in Paris, from 1891 to 1894, at the Académie Julian and Colarossi's. There he met the Canadian painter James Wilson Morrice and absorbed the colour of Whistler, the Nabis and the Post-Impressionists, and he returned to paint small watercolours and then oil paintings of parks, beaches and festivals in a bright, flat, mosaic-like manner, with crowds of fashionably dressed figures, as in In the Park, Paris of 1891. He visited Venice in 1898 and 1899 and again in 1911 and 1912. He exhibited with the group called The Eight in 1908, and lived and worked closely with his brother Charles, a frame-maker. He died in New York in 1924.","q2501622":"Adriana Verelst, who lived from 1682 to 1769, bears the name of the Verelst family of painters, which came from the Netherlands to England in the seventeenth century and produced several portraitists and flower painters in London. The museum record calls her English. She appears here through a single picture, a Portrait of a Lady in the National Gallery of Art, dated about 1715 to 1730 and perhaps close to 1725, painted in the manner of the fashionable English portraits of the reigns of Queen Anne and George I. Women who painted professionally in this period were uncommon, and little is recorded of her training, her sitters or the rest of her career.","q2506493":"Raimundo de Madrazo y Garreta was born in Rome in 1841 into the leading Spanish family of painters: his father Federico de Madrazo was director of the Prado, and his grandfather José de Madrazo had been a court painter. He studied with his father in Madrid, then in Paris with Léon Cogniet, and settled in Paris, where he became one of the most successful society painters of his generation, with polished portraits and elegant genre pictures of Spanish and Parisian women painted with sparkle and dash. His brother-in-law was the famous Mariano Fortuny. Wealthy American collectors were among his best clients, and he painted the New York art dealer Samuel P. Avery in 1876. He died in Versailles in 1920. Girls at a Window dates from about 1875.","q2517498":"Michelino da Besozzo, whose family name was Molinari, was born at Besozzo, on Lake Maggiore, about 1388, and worked as a painter and manuscript illuminator for the Visconti court in Lombardy and for patrons in other Italian cities. A signed panel of the Mystic Marriage of Saint Catherine, now in Siena, is the main secure work, and the humanist Pier Candido Decembrio later praised him as the greatest painter of his time. His elegant, courtly style, with flowing outlines, delicate faces, rich costume and a sweet, light palette, represents the International Gothic in Lombardy, alongside Gentile da Fabriano and Pisanello. The Metropolitan Museum's Marriage of the Virgin dates from about 1430. He died about 1450.","q2524442":"Titian Ramsay Peale was born in Philadelphia in 1799, the son of the painter and museum-keeper Charles Willson Peale, who named his sons after great artists, and he is remembered chiefly as a naturalist and illustrator. As a young man of nineteen he joined Major Stephen Long's expedition to the Rocky Mountains in 1819 and 1820, drawing animals, plants and scenery, and later served as a naturalist on the United States Exploring Expedition under Charles Wilkes, which sailed round the world from 1838 to 1842. He also made fine drawings of butterflies and moths, took up photography, and worked for many years in the United States Patent Office. He died in Philadelphia in 1885. The National Gallery of Art's Spray of Flowers and Ferns is undated.","q2534502":"Thomas Hill was born in Birmingham, England, in 1829 and emigrated with his family to Massachusetts in 1844. He trained as a decorative painter, studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, and settled in San Francisco in 1861 for his health, becoming the leading California landscape painter of his day. After study in Paris in 1866 and 1867 he returned to California and painted vast panoramas of Yosemite and the Sierra Nevada, in direct competition with Albert Bierstadt, and kept a studio for years in Yosemite. His largest picture, Driving of the Last Spike, painted in 1881, commemorates the completion of the first transcontinental railroad in 1869. He died in Raymond, California, in 1908. The Metropolitan Museum's View of Yosemite Valley is dated 1885.","q2539929":"Richard Cosway was born at Tiverton, Devon, in 1742 and came to London as a boy, where he studied at William Shipley's drawing school and at the Royal Academy, of which he became a member in 1771. He became the most fashionable English miniature painter of his day, painting aristocrats and royalty in an elegant, rapid, lightly tinted style in which faces are flattered and costumes sparkle. In 1785 he was named Principal Painter to the Prince of Wales, the future George IV. In 1781 he married Maria Hadfield, a painter born in Florence, and the couple entertained London society at Schomberg House, Pall Mall; Maria's friendship with Thomas Jefferson in Paris in 1786 produced Jefferson's famous letter, A Dialogue between My Head and My Heart. He died in 1821. The Metropolitan Museum has miniatures by him and a self-portrait of about 1770 to 1775.","q2542057":"Samuel Scott was born in London about 1702 and became the leading English marine painter of the generation after Peter Monamy, painting sea battles and ships in a clear, firm, well-drawn style. About 1730 he began to paint views of the Thames and London, in the manner of the Venetian view painters, and in the 1740s, when Canaletto was working in England, he was sometimes called the English Canaletto. In 1732 he went with William Hogarth and three friends on the high-spirited five-day expedition around the Thames estuary and Kent known as the Peregrination, an account of which was written by one of the party and illustrated by Hogarth and Scott. He painted the building of Westminster Bridge, a great engineering project of 1738 to 1750. He moved to Bath in 1765 and died there in 1772.","q2557532":"Roelof van Vries was a Dutch landscape painter of the seventeenth century, usually placed in Haarlem and Amsterdam, and neither birth nor death dates are known. He is thought to have been a follower of Jacob van Ruisdael, whose wooded landscapes, dune scenes and cottages he echoes in his own smaller, simpler pictures. The Metropolitan Museum's The Pigeon House shows a dovecote, a subject that appealed to Dutch landscape painters because such buildings were a familiar feature of farms and country estates, and because their weathered timber and brick, with birds wheeling round them, gave a picturesque and quietly humorous centre to a rustic scene. Little else is recorded of his training or patrons.","q2578528":"William Dunlap was born at Perth Amboy, New Jersey, in 1766 and lost the sight of one eye in a childhood accident. He went to London in 1784 to study with Benjamin West but, by his own account, spent more time at the theatre than at the easel, and on his return to America he divided his career between painting portraits and writing for the stage; his play The Father, produced in 1789, was among the first by an American to be professionally staged, and he managed New York's Park Theatre. A founder of the National Academy of Design in 1826, he is best remembered for his History of the Rise and Progress of the Arts of Design in the United States, published in 1834, the first full account of American art and still an essential source. He died in New York in 1839.","q260114":"Master of the Osservanza is the conventional name for an anonymous Sienese painter of the second quarter of the fifteenth century, named after a triptych in the church of the Osservanza outside Siena. His real name is not known, and the range of 1365 to 1485 given here is only a broad one. His style is closely related to that of Sassetta, the leading Sienese painter of the day, and some scholars have tentatively identified him with a documented Sienese painter, though no identification has won general agreement. The National Gallery of Art's four panels, dated about 1430 to 1435 and showing scenes from the life of Saint Anthony Abbot, probably formed the predella, or lower tier, of an altarpiece. They are lively narratives in which the saint meets Saint Paul the Hermit and gives away his wealth.","q2605835":"Pedro Orrente was born at Murcia in 1580 and died at Valencia in 1645. He is said to have studied in Toledo, where he may have met El Greco, and to have visited Venice, where he learned from the Bassano family of painters, whose pastoral scenes of animals, shepherds and peasants, often by firelight or at night, he adapted to biblical subjects; as a result he was called the Spanish Bassano. He worked in Toledo, Murcia and Valencia, and settled in Valencia, where he led a busy workshop and taught younger painters. His Old Testament and gospel pastorals, with hay, cattle and sheep, were popular with Spanish patrons. The Metropolitan Museum's Crucifixion dates from about 1625 to 1630.","q2625028":"Paulus Bor was born at Amersfoort, in the province of Utrecht, about 1601, and died there in 1669. He trained in Utrecht, where Abraham Bloemaert was the most influential teacher, and went to Rome in the 1620s, where he lived among the Dutch and Flemish painters who admired Caravaggio. Back in the Netherlands he divided his time between Amersfoort and Utrecht, painting history pictures, portraits and allegories in a refined Italianate classicism with a subtle sense of drama. His Disillusioned Medea, about 1640, in the Metropolitan Museum, shows the sorceress of Greek legend, a subject that allowed a painter to combine mythology, strong feeling and an exotic costume, and it belongs to the small group of pictures on which his modern reputation rests.","q2632216":"Scipione Pulzone, called Il Gaetano after his birthplace, Gaeta, was born in the 1540s and died in Rome in 1598. He trained in Rome, where he spent his working life, and became the leading portraitist of the Counter-Reformation city, painting cardinals, noblemen and ladies, as well as devotional altarpieces, in a precise, clear, polished style that prized likeness, clarity and moral seriousness over display. The sober, easily read manner of his religious pictures suited the reformed taste of the Church after the Council of Trent. His Lamentation, dated 1593, in the Metropolitan Museum, shows the mourning over the dead Christ in this manner. A generation later Caravaggio's more dramatic art overshadowed him, and his reputation faded; modern scholars have rebuilt it.","q265665":"Pierre-Auguste Cot was born at Bédarieux, in the south of France, in 1837 and died in Paris in 1883. He studied in Paris with Léon Cogniet, Alexandre Cabanel and William Bouguereau, and made his name at the Salon with graceful, romantic pictures of young lovers and mythological figures, painted in the polished academic manner of the Second Empire and the early Third Republic. His most famous works, Springtime, of 1873, and The Storm, shown at the Salon of 1880, came to the Metropolitan Museum in 1887 through the bequest of the New York collector Catharine Lorillard Wolfe, and were long popular with visitors. The Storm shows two young lovers hurrying through a rainstorm. After his early death modern taste turned against such pictures, but they have been reappraised in recent decades.","q2693931":"Christoffel Pierson was born in Rotterdam in 1631 and died at Gouda in 1714, and lived most of his life at Gouda. He is remembered for a small group of trompe l'oeil still lifes, in which weapons, hunting bags, falconry gear, powder horns and other sporting equipment hang or lie in a shallow niche or on a wall, painted with such exactness that they appear real. The National Gallery of Art's Niche with Falconry Gear, probably of the 1660s, is a fine example. Such pictures were made for hunting gentlemen and collectors who enjoyed the ingenuity of illusion, and the sober browns, greys and muted colours of the objects give them a quiet, almost austere dignity. He is known today almost wholly for these pictures.","q2747886":"Peter Adolf Hall was born in Sweden in 1739 and made his career in Paris, where he settled in the 1760s and became the most celebrated miniature painter of the reign of Louis XVI. His miniatures, painted on ivory in bright, loose, sparkling strokes rather than the smooth stippling of earlier artists, show fashionable men and women in silks, lace and powdered hair, and he exhibited regularly at the Paris Salon from the late 1760s. He died at Liège in 1793. The Metropolitan Museum has his portrait of the painter Louis Joseph Maurice, dated 1772, and a portrait of a young woman of about 1790. His free, spirited handling gave the small portrait something of the vivacity of a larger painting.","q2757568":"Thomas Anshutz was born at Newport, Kentucky, in 1851 and grew up in Wheeling, West Virginia. He studied at the National Academy of Design in New York and then, in the 1870s, at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts in Philadelphia under Thomas Eakins, whose emphasis on drawing from the living model and on anatomy shaped his whole approach. He painted candid scenes of working life, such as his Ironworkers' Noontime of 1880, and The Way They Live of 1879, which is in the Metropolitan Museum. In 1881 he joined the Academy's faculty, and when Eakins was forced out in 1886 he stayed to lead the school's teaching. Among his students were Robert Henri and Charles Demuth. He died in Fort Washington, Pennsylvania, in 1912.","q2758548":"Panfilo Nuvolone was born in Cremona in 1581 and died in Milan in 1651. He began in Cremona, then worked in Milan, where he painted altarpieces and religious pictures for churches in the city and its region in a clear, devout, rather conservative Lombard style, in the age of Cardinal Federico Borromeo, the archbishop who tried to reform both religion and religious art. He is also counted among the earliest painters of still life in Lombardy: his Still Life of Grapes and Peaches, about 1617, is an early example, painted at a time when Borromeo himself owned Caravaggio's Basket of Fruit. His sons Carlo Francesco and Giuseppe Nuvolone also became painters and continued the workshop.","q2801572":"Peter Faes was a Flemish painter, born in 1750 and dead in 1814, who specialised in flower pieces. The Metropolitan Museum has a pair of his pictures, Flowers by a Stone Vase and Flowers in a Stone Vase, both dated 1786, painted in the tradition of Flemish flower painting that had flourished since Jan Brueghel the Elder and Daniel Seghers in the early seventeenth century and was still alive in the later eighteenth century, with rich, crowded bouquets set against a dark ground. A pair of this kind was probably made to hang as pendants in a salon, matching one another in design and balance. Little else is recorded of his training or patrons.","q280851":"Piero di Cosimo was born in Florence in 1462 and died there in 1522. His name comes from his teacher, the painter Cosimo Rosselli, whom he assisted on frescoes in the Sistine Chapel in Rome in 1481 and 1482. He painted altarpieces and portraits, but is best known for strange, inventive mythological scenes and hunts, set in woods full of animals, satyrs and primitive peoples, for Florentine patrons who commissioned pictures for the walls and furniture of their houses. According to Vasari, he was a shy eccentric who lived alone, hated noise, and ate hard-boiled eggs which he cooked fifty at a time while preparing his glue. His Return from the Hunt and Hunting Scene, in the Metropolitan Museum, are two panels from a series. His reputation, once dim, has risen steadily in the last century.","q285423":"Sebastiano Luciani was born in Venice about 1485 and trained there under Giovanni Bellini and then Giorgione, absorbing Venetian colour and soft shadow. In 1511 he went to Rome for the banker Agostino Chigi, for whom he painted frescoes in the Villa Farnesina beside Raphael, and he became the ally of Michelangelo, who supplied drawings for some of his pictures in the rivalry with Raphael's circle. His Raising of Lazarus, now in London, was painted in competition with Raphael's Transfiguration. A fine portraitist, he painted Cardinal Bandinello Sauli with his secretary and two geographers in 1516, and a portrait of 1519 long said to show Christopher Columbus, who died in 1506, though the identification is doubted. In 1531 he received the office of keeper of the papal lead seals, the piombo, and was called del Piombo ever after. He died in Rome in 1547.","q2857352":"Anton Maria Vassallo was a painter usually placed in Genoa, born in 1620 and dead in 1672, who is known mainly for still lifes and pictures of animals. Seventeenth-century Genoa was a rich port that attracted Flemish painters, among them Jan Roos and Cornelis de Wael, who brought a taste for lavish still lifes and animal pictures, and local painters such as Vassallo took up the genre. The National Gallery of Art's The Larder, probably of about 1650 to 1660, belongs to this tradition, in which meat, fowl, fish and vegetables are piled for the table in a way that celebrates abundance and shows off the painter's skill in textures. Little else is recorded of his life, training or patrons.","q28771":"Pieter Molijn was born in London in 1595 to Flemish parents, moved to Haarlem, where he joined the painters' guild in 1616, and died there in 1661. With Esaias van de Velde and Jan van Goyen he helped to found the tonal landscape painting of Haarlem, which replaced the crowded, brightly coloured fantasy of the earlier Flemish tradition with simple, realistic views of the Dutch countryside: sandy dune roads, gnarled trees, cottages and farm gates under broad, cloudy skies, painted in a narrow range of browns, greens and greys. He also made etchings of landscape. The National Gallery of Art's Landscape with Open Gate, about 1630 to 1635, and the Metropolitan Museum's Landscape with a Cottage of 1629 are typical.","q287934":"Spinello Aretino, whose full name was Spinello di Luca Spinelli, was born in Arezzo about 1350 and died there in 1410. He became one of the busiest Italian painters of the late fourteenth century, working in Florence, Pisa, Lucca, Siena and his native city. In Florence he painted the frescoes of the life of Saint Benedict in the sacristy of San Miniato al Monte, about 1387, in Pisa he worked for the Campo Santo, and in Siena in 1407 and 1408 he frescoed the Palazzo Pubblico with scenes from the life of Pope Alexander III. His narrative style is lively and crowded, with bright colour and expressive faces, and looks back to Giotto's tradition while hinting at the International Gothic. His son Parri Spinelli also became a painter. The Metropolitan Museum's double-sided panel shows Saint Mary Magdalen and, on the reverse, the Flagellation.","q29231":"Frans Snyders was born in Antwerp in 1579 and died there in 1657. He trained under Pieter Brueghel the Younger and the figure painter Hendrick van Balen, visited Italy in 1608 and 1609, and became the greatest Flemish painter of still life and animals, with huge market and larder scenes piled with game, fish, fruit and vegetables, and fierce hunts and animal fights. He often worked with Rubens, who painted the figures while Snyders supplied the animals and food, and with Jacob Jordaens. His patrons included the Archduke Albert and Archduchess Isabella in Brussels, the Spanish king Philip IV and Antwerp merchants. He was the brother-in-law of the painter Cornelis de Vos. The National Gallery of Art's Still Life with Grapes and Game and Still Life with Flowers, Grapes, and Small Game Birds show his mature and early styles.","q88498":"Severin Roesen was born in Germany, probably at Cologne, about 1815 or 1816, and trained as a porcelain painter, a craft in which he learned to paint flowers and fruit with great exactness. He left Germany after the revolutions of 1848 and arrived in New York, where he painted large, sumptuous still lifes in the tradition of Dutch and German flower painting, with piles of fruit, flowers and glasses arranged on marble ledges. In 1857 he moved to the Pennsylvania lumber town of Williamsport, where he found a market among wealthy local families, and he is last recorded about 1872, after which he disappears from the record. His abundant, brightly coloured pictures were among the most popular American still lifes of the 1850s and 1860s, and are now prized.","q902759":"Sir David Wilkie was born at Cults in Fife in 1785, the son of a minister, trained at the Trustees' Academy in Edinburgh, and moved to London in 1805 to study at the Royal Academy. His first great success was The Village Politicians of 1806, followed by The Blind Fiddler, small, closely observed scenes of Scottish village life in the tradition of the Dutch genre painters, which were bought by aristocratic patrons. His Chelsea Pensioners Reading the Waterloo Despatch, painted for the Duke of Wellington in 1822, made him famous. He became Painter in Ordinary to George IV and was knighted in 1836. Later he travelled to Italy, Spain and the Near East, and died at sea off Gibraltar in 1841, on the voyage home, an event commemorated in Turner's Peace, Burial at Sea. The Highland Family dates from 1824.","q918778":"Perino del Vaga was born Pietro Buonaccorsi in Florence in 1501 and trained there before going to Rome about 1516, where he became one of the most gifted assistants in Raphael's workshop, working on the decorations of the Vatican Loggia. After Raphael's death in 1520 he stayed in Rome until the Sack of 1527, when he lost much and fled to Genoa. There, from 1528, he decorated the palace of the admiral Andrea Doria with frescoes of gods and heroes, introducing the Roman High Renaissance and Mannerist style to northern Italy. Returning to Rome in 1537, he directed decoration at the Castel Sant'Angelo for Pope Paul III and worked on the Sala Paolina until his death in 1547. The Metropolitan Museum's Holy Family dates from about 1524 to 1526 and the National Gallery of Art's Nativity from 1534.","q929809":"Segna di Buonaventura was a Sienese painter, recorded in documents from about 1298 to 1331, who was a follower of Duccio, the greatest Sienese master of his time. He is best known for gold-ground panels in the Duccio manner, among them Crucifixes and Madonnas, painted with a delicate line and an elegant, slightly archaic grace. The Metropolitan Museum holds five of his panels, dated from about 1311 to the 1320s, showing Christ Blessing, the Madonna and Child, Saint John the Evangelist, Saint Benedict and Saint Silvester Gozzolini, the founder of the Silvestrine order, the last two pointing to a Benedictine commission. His son Niccolò di Segna also became a painter, and the workshop continued in Duccio's tradition into the later fourteenth century.","q930831":"Francesco di Stefano, called Pesellino, was born in Florence in 1422 and died there in 1457, at only thirty-five. He was brought up by his grandfather, the painter Giuliano Pesello, from whom he took his nickname, and he came under the influence of Fra Filippo Lippi, painting devotional pictures and, notably, decorated chests, or cassoni, for Florentine families, with lively scenes from the Bible, ancient history and romance. His largest altarpiece, a Trinity for Pistoia, was left unfinished at his death and completed by Fra Filippo Lippi's workshop. The National Gallery of Art's Crucifixion with Saint Jerome and Saint Francis, about 1445 to 1450, and the Metropolitan Museum's Madonna and Child with Six Saints, of the late 1440s, show his gentle, graceful, colourful style.","q9319":"Jacopo Robusti, called Tintoretto, the little dyer, after his father's trade, was born in Venice in 1518 and died there in 1594. He is said to have spent a short time in Titian's workshop and otherwise to have taught himself, and he aimed, as a famous motto has it, to unite Michelangelo's drawing with Titian's colour. His dramatic, fast, strongly lit pictures, with bold perspective and energetic figures, made his name from about 1548, when he painted the Miracle of the Slave for the Scuola Grande di San Marco. In 1564 he began his greatest work, the huge cycle of paintings for the Scuola Grande di San Rocco, which occupied him for some twenty-three years. A busy portraitist of Venetian senators and doges, among them Alvise Mocenigo, he ran a large workshop with his son Domenico and daughter Marietta. His reputation rose again in the nineteenth century.","q9440":"Paolo Caliari, called Veronese after his birthplace, Verona, was born in 1528 and died in Venice in 1588. He trained in Verona under the painter Antonio Badile, moved to Venice about 1553, and soon became, with Titian and Tintoretto, one of the city's three great painters, loved for sumptuous colour, silvery light, rich costume and grand architectural settings. He decorated the Doge's Palace, the church of San Sebastiano and, with the architect Palladio, the Villa Barbaro at Maser. His huge banquet scenes brought him before the Inquisition in 1573, after the Last Supper he painted for the refectory of Santi Giovanni e Paolo was criticised for including buffoons, drunkards and Germans; he did not change the picture but simply retitled it Feast in the House of Levi. The National Gallery of Art has several of his late religious pictures.","q947105":"Ambrogio de Predis was born in Milan about 1455 and died after 1508. He and his half-brother Evangelista, a painter and miniaturist, worked at the court of the Sforza dukes, and in 1483 the brothers and Leonardo da Vinci signed a contract to paint an altarpiece for the Confraternity of the Immaculate Conception, which became the Virgin of the Rocks; Ambrogio is generally credited with the angels on the wings of the London version. He painted carefully finished portraits of the Sforza court and, after the fall of the Sforzas, of the Emperor Maximilian I. The National Gallery of Art's portrait of Bianca Maria Sforza, probably of 1493, shows the niece of Duke Ludovico, who in that year married Maximilian as his second wife; its jewels and brocade reflect the courtly taste of Milan.","q949627":"Matthias Stom, also called Stomer, is thought to have been born in the Netherlands about 1600, probably at Amersfoort, and to have trained in Utrecht in the circle of Gerrit van Honthorst and the other Utrecht painters who followed Caravaggio. He is documented in Rome by 1630, and afterwards worked in Naples and, from the 1640s, in Sicily, where he painted altarpieces for churches in Palermo and Messina and was last recorded in the early 1650s. He is known for nocturnal scenes lit by a single candle or lamp, with strongly contrasted light and dark, and for the intense, humble realism of his religious figures, which are painted with a thick, fluid touch. The Metropolitan Museum's Old Woman Praying dates from the late 1630s or early 1640s.","q950362":"Robert Edge Pine was born in London in 1730, the son of the engraver John Pine, and trained as a painter, winning a prize from the Society of Arts in 1760. He painted portraits of actors and gentry, historical subjects and Shakespearean scenes, and exhibited in London for many years. In 1784 he moved to Philadelphia, hoping to paint a series of pictures from the American Revolution, and in 1785 he painted George Washington from life at Mount Vernon. He portrayed leading figures of the new republic, among them General William Smallwood of Maryland, whose portrait is dated 1785 to 1788. Pine died in Philadelphia in 1788, only a few years after his arrival, and he is now remembered chiefly as an English painter who brought a London manner to the portraits of the first American leaders.","q954072":"Raffaellino del Garbo was born in Florence about 1466 and died there about 1524. He is also recorded as Raffaele de' Capponi and Raffaele de' Carli, names which show how uncertain the details of his family are. He was the pupil and follower of Filippino Lippi, and his early pictures, with graceful figures, soft colour and delicate sentiment, show the influence of Filippino and of Botticelli. He painted altarpieces and Madonnas for Florentine churches and private patrons, and he was the teacher of the young Bronzino. His later style grew more conventional as the new manner of Leonardo, Michelangelo and Raphael overtook him. The Metropolitan Museum's Holy Family with an Angel dates from about 1490.","q954504":"Alessandro Bonvicino, called Moretto da Brescia, was born in Brescia about 1498 and died there in 1554. He trained in Brescia under Floriano Ferramola, and studied the work of Titian in Venice and of Raphael, producing altarpieces for the churches of his native city in a distinctive style of cool, silvery colour, clear light and calm, monumental composition. He was also an outstanding portraitist, whose portraits of Brescian noblemen, set in rooms and landscapes, were influential, and his Portrait of a Lady in White, about 1540, is among his best known. His pupil Giovanni Battista Moroni, of Bergamo, carried his portrait manner further. His works in the Metropolitan Museum include an Entombment dated 1554, the year of his death.","q955750":"William Powell Frith was born in Yorkshire in 1819 and trained in London, where he became the leading painter of Victorian crowd scenes. His Ramsgate Sands of 1854 was bought by Queen Victoria, and Derby Day, shown at the Royal Academy in 1858, with hundreds of figures on Epsom Downs, was so popular that a rail had to be put up to protect it from the crowd. It was followed by The Railway Station of 1862, which a dealer bought before the painting was finished for the then enormous sum of 4,500 guineas. Frith wrote a lively autobiography. The Metropolitan Museum's picture of 1860 is a separate painting of the two central figures of Derby Day. His work was scorned by modernist critics but is now valued as a record of Victorian life.","q963476":"Master of the Saint Lucy Legend is the conventional name for an anonymous Netherlandish painter active in Bruges in the later fifteenth century, named after an altarpiece of 1480 showing the legend of Saint Lucy, the Sicilian virgin martyr, in the church of Saint James in Bruges. His real name is not known. He worked in the tradition of Hans Memling and Rogier van der Weyden, painting altarpieces, portraits and devotional panels with delicate, sweet-faced figures, rich costume and clear landscape. The National Gallery of Art's Mary, Queen of Heaven, of about 1485 to 1500, shows the Virgin crowned as Queen of Heaven, a theme of the Marian devotion that flourished in the Low Countries. Nothing is recorded of his training or patrons.","q974618":"Thomas Hicks was born at Newtown, Pennsylvania, in 1823 and studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts and then at the National Academy of Design in New York. From 1845 to 1849 he worked in Europe, in Paris with Thomas Couture and in London and Italy, and on his return to New York he became a successful portrait and figure painter, with a clientele of authors, actors and statesmen. He painted Abraham Lincoln from life at Springfield, Illinois, in 1860, during the presidential campaign, a portrait much reproduced in prints. He was a cousin of the Quaker painter Edward Hicks. He died in 1890. The Metropolitan Museum has his portrait of his wife, about 1884, and a portrait of a man of about 1850.","q979137":"Adam Pynacker was born at Schiedam in 1620 or 1621 and died in Amsterdam in 1673. He spent about three years in Italy from the mid-1640s, and afterwards lived in Delft and Amsterdam. His Italianate landscapes are marked by a golden, southern light, sharply drawn foliage, tall reeds and rushes, cascades and ruined buildings, and shepherds and travellers, painted with a fresh, decorative brilliance that sets them apart from the tonal landscapes of the earlier generation. He also painted large decorative landscapes for the walls of Dutch houses, as well as a few religious and mythological subjects. The National Gallery of Art's Wooded Landscape with Travelers dates from the late 1640s and may reflect his Italian journey.","q981558":"Pieter Pourbus was born at Gouda in 1523 or 1524 and settled in Bruges, where he became the leading painter of the mid-sixteenth century, and died in 1584. His teacher and father-in-law was Lancelot Blondeel, the Bruges painter, architect and engineer. He painted altarpieces, among them a Last Judgment, and sober, carefully observed portraits of Bruges burghers and their wives; he was also a surveyor and mapmaker, and in 1571 made a great map of the Liberty of Bruges, the territory around the city. His son Frans Pourbus the Elder and grandson Frans Pourbus the Younger became leading painters, the younger as a court portraitist in Mantua and Paris. The Metropolitan Museum's Portrait of a Young Woman, which the museum does not date precisely, belongs to this tradition.","q983441":"Thomas Moran was born at Bolton, in Lancashire, in 1837 and was taken to the United States as a child, growing up in Philadelphia, where he began as an apprentice to a wood engraver. He admired J. M. W. Turner and made a study trip to England in 1862 to see his work. In 1871 he joined the Hayden geological survey of Yellowstone as artist, and his sketches and watercolours of the geysers and canyons, shown to Congress together with William Henry Jackson's photographs, helped to persuade the legislators to create Yellowstone as the first national park in 1872. Congress bought his large Grand Canyon of the Yellowstone, and later his Chasm of the Colorado. He lived from 1884 at East Hampton, on Long Island, painting the sea, as in The Much Resounding Sea, and died in 1926.","r_way_smith":"R. Way Smith was an American painter, born in 1840 and dead in 1900, and the museum record gives only his surname, his initials and his dates. No biography is recorded, and nothing certain is known of his training, his place of work or his patrons. He is represented by one picture, A Summer Afternoon, dated 1882, in the Cleveland Museum of Art. In the early 1880s American painters were drawn to quiet pictures of rural leisure, to landscape and figure scenes of ordinary summer days, a taste fostered by growing prosperity, summer travel and a feeling that country life was threatened by the cities; the title suggests that Smith's picture belongs to that taste, though little more can be said of its maker.","rachel_ruysch":"Rachel Ruysch was born in Amsterdam in 1664, the daughter of the celebrated anatomist and botanist Frederik Ruysch, and became one of the greatest flower painters of the Dutch Golden Age. She trained with the flower painter Willem van Aelst, married the portrait painter Juriaen Pool in 1693, and had ten children, yet she painted for some sixty years, producing rich, elegantly composed bouquets with insects, dewdrops and rare blooms in a light, crisp, glowing manner. From 1708 to 1716 she was court painter to Johann Wilhelm, Elector Palatine, at Düsseldorf. She died in Amsterdam in 1750, aged eighty-six. The Metropolitan Museum's picture of 1692 is titled with her own name and dates, which suggests that it is a portrait of her rather than a flower piece; who painted it is not stated here.","rafel_govertsz_camphuysen":"Rafel Govertsz. Camphuysen was a Dutch painter who bore the name of the Camphuysen family, which produced several painters and a well-known poet and was active in the first half of the seventeenth century. No dates are known for him, and little is recorded of his training or patrons. His one picture in the Cleveland Museum of Art, An Estuary Scene with Fisherman, is dated only to the years between 1625 and 1650. Broad river mouths with fishermen, sailing boats and low horizons under wide skies were among the favourite subjects of Dutch painters of the 1630s and 1640s, who worked in the tonal manner of Jan van Goyen and Salomon van Ruysdael, with soft greys, greens and browns; the title suggests a picture of that kind.","ralph_a_blakelock":"Ralph Albert Blakelock was born in New York in 1847, was meant for a career in medicine, and taught himself to paint. Between 1869 and 1872 he travelled across the American West, seeing the plains, the Rocky Mountains and Native American encampments, and these experiences fed a lifetime of dark, moody landscapes, usually of moonlit woods and encampments, in which thick glazes and scraped, rubbed surfaces give a glowing, mysterious effect. He sold his paintings for small sums and struggled to support a large family, and in 1899 he was committed to a mental hospital, where he spent much of the rest of his life. During his confinement his pictures became fashionable and fetched high prices, from which he did not benefit. He was released in 1916 and died in 1919. Indian Camp dates from about 1890.","ramsey_milne":"Milne Ramsey was born in Philadelphia in 1846 and studied at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, then went to Paris, where he studied under Adolphe-William Bouguereau and lived for some years in the 1870s. He became known as a painter of still lifes, in the meticulous, almost illusionistic manner of the Philadelphia tradition that went back to Raphaelle Peale and that his contemporaries William Harnett and John Peto carried further. His Marble Tabletop with Fruit and Wineglass of 1869 is an early example of his work. He exhibited at the Paris Salon and the Pennsylvania Academy, and died in Philadelphia in 1915.","regnault_henri":"Henri Regnault was born in Paris in 1843, the son of a famous chemist, studied under Louis Lamothe and Alexandre Cabanel, and won the Prix de Rome in 1866. Bored by the discipline of the Villa Medici, he left Rome for Spain and then Morocco in 1869 and 1870, where he painted brilliant Orientalist pictures of violence and exotic splendour, among them Salome and Execution Without Judgement under the Moorish Kings of Granada. His A Chief of Abyssinia dates from about 1870. When the Franco-Prussian War broke out he enlisted, and he was killed at Buzenval, near Paris, in January 1871, at the age of twenty-seven, only days before the armistice. His early death made him a national hero, and a memorial to him was set up at the École des Beaux-Arts.","roelant_savery":"Roelant Savery, born at Kortrijk in 1576, belonged to a family of painters who left Flanders for the northern Netherlands as religious refugees, and he worked in Amsterdam before being called to Prague, about 1603, to serve the Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf II. There he painted for the emperor's collection and drew from his menagerie and from his own sketches of the Tyrol, which gave his later landscapes their jagged rocks and deep valleys. After Rudolf's death in 1612 he returned to the Netherlands, and by 1619 he had settled in Utrecht, where he died in 1639. He specialised in crowded landscapes of wild and tame animals, among them lions, deer and birds, in an imaginary paradise. The Cleveland picture, Lions and Fox in a Rugged Landscape, is not securely dated.","rol_n_de_moys":"Rolán de Moys is the Spanish form of the name of Roland de Moys, a Netherlandish painter who lived from about 1520 to 1592 and, as the Spanish spelling suggests, perhaps spent part of his career in Spain, where Netherlandish painters were prized at the court of Philip II. Little is recorded of his training, patrons or other works. The Metropolitan Museum holds a single Portrait of a Woman by him, which the museum does not date more precisely. Portraiture at the Spanish court in the second half of the sixteenth century was dominated by the Dutch painter Antonis Mor and his Spanish follower Alonso Sánchez Coello, who set a formal, dark-toned, richly dressed pattern that painters of Moys's generation would have known well.","russian_19th_century":"Russian 19th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an anonymous Russian artist or workshop of the 1800s whom the museum cannot identify, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's single picture, a Crucifixion of the nineteenth century, probably belongs to the long tradition of Orthodox devotional painting, in which a few fixed images, among them the Crucifixion with the Virgin and Saint John, were repeated for homes, churches and chapels. Icon painting continued on a large scale throughout the nineteenth century in villages such as Palekh, Mstera and Kholui, whose workshops supplied icons to the whole country, and some painters, especially in Old Believer communities, deliberately preserved the older styles.","russian_painter":"Russian Painter is a label, not the name of one artist: it gathers twelve pictures in the Metropolitan Museum by unidentified Russian icon painters, which the museum record places in the sixteenth century, though no individual dates are given here. Icon painters were usually monks or craftsmen who worked from approved models, and after the Church council of 1551, the Stoglav, required them to follow the old patterns, such as those of Andrei Rublev, they seldom signed. The group includes feast scenes such as the Annunciation, Christ's Entry into Jerusalem and the Harrowing of Hell, the Protection of the Mother of God, which honours a vision of the Virgin's veil over Constantinople, the first Russian saints Boris and Gleb, murdered in 1015, Saint George, the fiery ascension of the prophet Elijah, and calendar panels listing the saints and festivals of the church year.","samuel_isham":"Samuel Isham was born in New York in 1855 and studied in Paris at the Académie Julian, then returned to New York, where he painted landscapes, portraits and figure pieces in a quiet, muted, tonal manner and exhibited with the younger American painters of his day. He is now best known, however, as a writer: his History of American Painting, published in 1905, was the first full-length survey of its subject, and it was brought up to date by the critic Royal Cortissoz in a new edition of 1927. His Portrait of an Old Sea Captain, in the Cleveland Museum of Art, is a nineteenth-century portrait of the kind that celebrates the weathered character of its sitter. He died in 1914.","sandro_botticelli":"Sandro Botticelli, born Alessandro di Mariano Filipepi, lived from 1445 to 1510 and was the leading painter of Florence under the Medici in the later fifteenth century. He was apprenticed to Fra Filippo Lippi, and painted altarpieces, portraits and devotional pictures as well as the great mythologies, the Primavera and the Birth of Venus, for a younger branch of the Medici family. In 1481 and 1482 he went to Rome to paint frescoes in the Sistine Chapel for Pope Sixtus IV. His late work, after the preaching of Savonarola, became more severe and mystical. He died almost forgotten, and was rediscovered in the nineteenth century by Ruskin and the Pre-Raphaelites. The Cleveland Museum of Art's Virgin and Child with the Young Saint John the Baptist, about 1490, dates from the years in which he and a large workshop produced many such devotional pictures.","sarah_freeman_clarke":"Sarah Freeman Clarke was born in 1808 and died in 1896, the sister of the Unitarian minister and author James Freeman Clarke, and was an American painter who studied in Boston with Washington Allston. She belonged to the circle of New England writers and thinkers around Emerson and Margaret Fuller, travelled in Europe, and lived for some years in Rome, where she was part of the American artistic colony. Her Egyptian Revival Triptych, dated 1874 or after, in the Metropolitan Museum, reflects a fashion for Egyptian motifs that spread in the 1870s after the opening of the Suez Canal in 1869 and the first performance of Verdi's Aida in Cairo in 1871. Little else is recorded of her work.","sienese_16th_century":"Sienese 16th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an anonymous artist working in Siena in the sixteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's single picture, Apollo and Marsyas, of about 1540, shows a story from Ovid's Metamorphoses in which the satyr Marsyas challenges the god Apollo to a musical contest, loses, and is flayed alive for his presumption, a tale much liked by sixteenth-century patrons as a warning against pride and as an image of the triumph of reason and harmony over passion. Siena in this period was the home of the elegant, restless Mannerism of Domenico Beccafumi, whose twisted figures and brilliant colours influenced many lesser painters, and a picture of this kind may reflect that tradition.","simon_verelst":"Simon Verelst was born at The Hague in 1644, the son of the painter Pieter Verelst, and trained there before moving to London about 1669. He quickly won fashionable patrons, among them the Duke of Buckingham, and became one of the most sought-after painters of flowers and of portraits at the court of Charles II, painting rich, glowing flower pieces with rare blooms, dewdrops and insects, and rather mannered portraits of court beauties. According to an eighteenth-century anecdote he was so vain that he called himself the god of flowers. He died in London, probably in 1721, and other members of his family also painted in England. The Cleveland Museum's Flowers in a Vase dates from about 1669, the year he arrived.","skynner_thomas":"Thomas Skynner appears in the museum record as an American painter whose dates are given only as a very wide range, 1772 to 1900, which means that his life dates are unknown. He is represented in the National Gallery of Art by four portraits, all dated about 1845: a pair showing John Stone and Eliza Welch Stone, and portraits of an unnamed woman and an unnamed man. A group of portraits painted together suggests a commission from a single family or neighbourhood, of the kind that travelling portraitists took in the 1840s, when a painter could arrive in a town, advertise, paint a series of local sitters in a few weeks and move on. Nothing certain is recorded of his training, travels or later career.","sorolla_y_bastida_joaqu_n":"Joaquín Sorolla y Bastida was born in Valencia in 1863 and was orphaned at two, when his parents died in a cholera epidemic, and raised by relatives. He trained at the Valencia academy, studied in Rome and Paris in the 1880s, and married Clotilde García del Castillo in 1888. He became the leading Spanish painter of his generation, known for brilliant, sunlit scenes of beaches, fishermen and children painted in a fluent, impressionistic manner, with vivid white sails and clothes. A Grand Prix at the Paris Exposition of 1900 brought him international fame, and his 1909 exhibition at the Hispanic Society of America in New York was a huge success. For the Society's founder Archer Huntington he painted the Vision of Spain, a set of vast murals of the Spanish regions. He suffered a stroke in 1920 and died in 1923. Isabelita and Thor dates from 1893.","south_german_painter":"South German (Bavarian?) Painter is a label, not the name of an artist: it stands for an unidentified painter of southern Germany, perhaps Bavaria, working about 1440 to 1450, and the question mark in the museum's wording shows that even the region is uncertain. The Metropolitan Museum's single picture is an Annunciation of those years. In the 1430s and 1440s the new, realistic art of the Netherlands, with its interest in solid forms, light and everyday objects, began to reach German painters, who adapted it to local taste; Lukas Moser's Magdalen altarpiece at Tiefenbronn of 1431 and the work of Konrad Witz at Basel are well-known examples. Nothing further can be said of this painter, whose name and workshop are unrecorded.","south_netherlandish_painter":"South Netherlandish Painter is a label, not the name of an artist: it stands for an unidentified painter from the southern Netherlands, the region of modern Belgium, working about 1460, and the museum record gives neither name nor dates. The Metropolitan Museum holds two panels, a Nativity and an Annunciation, which are probably related scenes from the life of the Virgin and Christ, such as often formed a series on the wings of an altarpiece or in a devotional cycle. About 1460 the leading masters of the region were Rogier van der Weyden at Brussels, Petrus Christus at Bruges and Dirk Bouts at Louvain, and many anonymous painters worked in their shadow, following their types of composition. Nothing certain is recorded of this painter.","spanish_painter":"Spanish (Aragonese) Painter is a label, not the name of one artist: it gathers twelve unidentified pictures in the Metropolitan Museum, none dated precisely, which the museum links with Aragon and with the middle of the fifteenth century. Most are panels of the kind that filled the great altarpieces of late Gothic Spain, with scenes such as the Resurrection, the Mass of Saint Gregory, Christ among the Doctors and the Beheading of Saint John the Baptist, painted on gilded and patterned grounds by workshops that combined local traditions with Flemish and Italian influence. The group is mixed, and includes a portrait of Doña Marianna Stampa Parravicina, born in 1612, apparently a noblewoman of Milan, which Spain then ruled. No single biography can be given.","stanzione_massimo":"Massimo Stanzione was born near Naples in 1585 or 1586 and died in Naples in 1656. He began under the influence of Caravaggio's Neapolitan followers, and after a stay in Rome in 1617 to 1618, where he studied Guido Reni, Domenichino and the Carracci, he developed a more graceful, classical, clearly lit style that made him the leading rival of Jusepe de Ribera among Neapolitan painters. He painted altarpieces and frescoes for many Naples churches, among them the Certosa di San Martino and the Gesù Nuovo, and, with Artemisia Gentileschi and other Italians, contributed paintings to the Buen Retiro Palace for Philip IV of Spain. The National Gallery of Art's Madonna and Child dates from 1635 to 1640.","swiss_painter":"Swiss Painter is a label, not the name of an artist: it stands for an unidentified painter or painters of Switzerland, working in the last quarter of the fifteenth century and about 1500 to 1505, so no biography can be given. The Metropolitan Museum's three entries include a Portrait of a Woman and two double-sided altarpiece wings, painted on both faces so that they showed one cycle when closed and another when opened, a usual arrangement in late Gothic altarpieces. One shows Saint Agapitus of Praeneste, a young martyr thrown to wild beasts in the arena and beheaded, and the other scenes from the legend of Saint Remigius, the bishop of Reims who baptised Clovis, in which he refills a barrel of wine and deals with burning wheat.","thomas_badger":"Thomas Badger is recorded in the museum's data with the dates 1764 to 1826, but those dates cannot be right for his only picture in this collection, the Portrait of Three Boys, which the museum dates to the 1830s, so the information given here is clearly unreliable. No biography is known, and the discrepancy shows how uncertain the identification of early nineteenth-century American portraitists can be, since names, dates and attributions were often assembled long afterwards from signatures, family tradition and town records. The portrait, showing three brothers together, is the kind of picture that a prosperous family ordered to record its children, and group portraits of siblings were popular in the period.","thomas_lawrence":"Sir Thomas Lawrence was born in Bristol in 1769, the son of an innkeeper, and was a child prodigy who drew and sold portraits of the guests at his father's inns at Devizes and Bath. He came to London in 1787 and in 1792, at twenty-two, succeeded Reynolds as Painter in Ordinary to George III; he became a Royal Academician in 1794 and was knighted in 1815. As the Prince Regent's favourite he was sent in 1818 to paint the sovereigns and statesmen who had defeated Napoleon, at Aix-la-Chapelle, Vienna and Rome, portraits that now hang in the Waterloo Chamber at Windsor Castle. In 1820 he became president of the Royal Academy. He never married, was always in debt, and died in 1830. The Cleveland Museum of Art's portrait of Catherine Grey, Lady Manners, is dated 1794.","thomas_moran":"Thomas Moran, born in Lancashire in 1837 and brought up in Philadelphia, was among the most widely known American landscape painters of the later nineteenth century, a painter and, equally, an illustrator and printmaker. His wife Mary Nimmo Moran was a distinguished etcher, and his brothers Edward and Peter were painters too. He worked on illustrations for the great illustrated book Picturesque America in the early 1870s, a task that sent him to many regions, and he painted not only the great sights of the West but also quieter coastal and river scenes in the East and the South. Fort George Island, Florida, painted in 1878 and now in the Cleveland Museum of Art, belongs to that second group, made when Florida was becoming a winter resort for northern visitors.","tommaso_d_antonio_manzuoli_called_maso_da_san_fr":"Tommaso di Antonio Manzuoli, called Maso da San Friano, was born in Florence in 1531 and died there in 1571, aged only about forty. He is said to have trained under Pier Francesco di Jacopo Foschi, and he became one of the graceful, elaborate Florentine Mannerists of the generation after Pontormo and Bronzino, painting altarpieces, devotional pictures and portraits, with elegant, elongated figures and cool, bright colour. The Cleveland Museum of Art's Holy Family with the Mystic Marriage of Saint Catherine dates from about 1560. Its subject, in which the infant Christ places a ring on the finger of the kneeling Saint Catherine of Alexandria, was one of the most popular devotional themes of the sixteenth century, and was painted for churches, convents and private chapels.","tuscan_13th_century":"Tuscan 13th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an anonymous Tuscan artist of the thirteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's single picture, a Madonna and Child with Saint John the Baptist, Saint Peter and two angels, of about 1290, comes from the generation before Giotto, when Tuscan painters, among them Cimabue in Florence and Duccio in Siena, were gradually turning the formal, gold-ground Byzantine manner, known as the maniera greca, into a more natural art with softer modelling and more feeling. Panels like this, painted in egg tempera on wood with gold leaf, were made for churches, convents and private devotion, and most of their makers are now unknown.","tyrolean_15th_century":"Tyrolean 15th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an unidentified painter of the Tyrol, in the Alps of what is now Austria and Italy, working in the fifteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's Portrait of a Man, of about 1490 to 1500, comes from the last decades of the century, when the Tyrol was rich from its silver mines at Schwaz and, from 1490, ruled by Maximilian I, who made Innsbruck an imperial court. The leading Tyrolean artist of the age was the painter and sculptor Michael Pacher of Bruneck, whose altarpieces show Italian influence. Portraits like this, showing an individual with calm, searching realism, reflect the new Renaissance interest in the person, which crossed the Alps with traders and artists.","tyrolean_16th_century":"Tyrolean 16th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an anonymous painter or workshop of the Tyrol in the sixteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's four pictures, dated about 1500 to 1525, show Saint Alban of Mainz, Saint Wolfgang, Saint Valentine and Saint Alcuin, and probably come from a single altarpiece. The saints chosen are those favoured in the German-speaking lands: Alban was a martyr venerated at Mainz, Wolfgang was the bishop of Regensburg, especially honoured in Austria and Bavaria, Valentine was a bishop and martyr, and Alcuin was the English scholar who served Charlemagne and ended his life as abbot of Saint-Martin at Tours. Little else can be said of the painter.","umbrian_16th_century":"Umbrian 16th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an anonymous painter of Umbria, the hill country of central Italy, in the sixteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's Head of a Woman, of about 1510 to 1520, may be a fragment or study connected with a larger religious composition, and it belongs to the soft, sweet, devotional style that Perugino had made famous, and in which the young Raphael trained. Umbrian painters of this period typically gave their figures gentle, downcast eyes, smooth faces and calm, symmetrical poses against clear landscape backgrounds. Many workshops in Perugia and the smaller hill towns continued this manner for decades, and their works are often distinguished from one another only with difficulty.","velde_iii_jan_jansz_van_de":"Jan Jansz. van de Velde III was born in Haarlem in 1620 and died at Enkhuizen in 1662. He came from a Haarlem family of artists, the son of the engraver and painter Jan van de Velde II, and he became a painter of still lifes. His specialities were quiet, muted breakfast and tobacco pieces, in which a pipe, a stoneware jug, a glass, bread and similar humble objects are set on a table with exact attention to texture and reflected light, and which are among the austere masterpieces of the genre. The National Gallery of Art's Still Life with Stoneware Jug and Pipe dates from the mid-1650s. Such pictures were made for the prosperous burghers of the Dutch towns, who enjoyed seeing familiar household things painted with such care.","venetian_15th_century":"Venetian 15th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for an anonymous Venetian painter of the later fifteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's Portrait of a Man, of the late fifteenth century, belongs to a great age of Venetian portraiture, when Giovanni Bellini and, after Antonello da Messina's visit in 1475 and 1476, many other painters adopted the oil technique and the half-length, three-quarter view set before a dark or landscape background. Such portraits were commissioned by merchants, senators, physicians and humanists, who wanted a record of themselves in the prosperous city, and some are now identified only by style, because the sitter, painter and patron left no record in the picture itself.","venetian_16th_century":"Venetian 16th Century is a label, not the name of a painter: it stands for anonymous Venetian painters of the sixteenth century, so no biography can be given. The National Gallery of Art's two pictures, a Portrait of a Young Man of about 1505 and an Orpheus of about 1515, come from the years when Venetian painting was changing fast, around the death of Giorgione in 1510 and the rise of the young Titian, and they reflect the new interest in soft light, warm colour and poetic, half-mysterious subjects. Orpheus, the legendary Thracian singer whose music charmed wild beasts, appealed to learned patrons who prized classical stories. Portraits of young men of this date commonly show them half-length against a dark ground. The painters themselves are unrecorded.","victors_jan":"Jan Victors was born in Amsterdam in 1619 and is recorded as a pupil of Rembrandt in the later 1630s, and his early work, with its warm brown tones, strong chiaroscuro and emotional gestures, is close to his master's. He is best known for large biblical scenes, mainly from the Old Testament, and for scenes of village and farm life, painted with a rough humour. The Slaughtered Hog, of 1653, in the National Gallery of Art, belongs to this second group and recalls Rembrandt's own pictures of slaughtered oxen. Victors painted for the burghers of Amsterdam, but after the disasters of 1672, when the Dutch Republic was invaded and the art market collapsed, he is said to have gone to the East Indies, where he was last recorded in 1676.","vroom_hendrick_cornelisz":"Hendrick Cornelisz. Vroom was born in Haarlem in 1566 and died there in 1640. As a young man he travelled widely, working in Spain and Italy and, it is said, surviving a shipwreck, before returning to Haarlem, where he became the first great painter of the Dutch sea. His ship portraits and sea battles are painted with precise knowledge of rigging and hulls, in clear, bright colour, from the high viewpoint of the older Netherlandish landscape tradition. He designed the tapestries of the defeat of the Spanish Armada of 1588 for the English Lord High Admiral, Lord Howard of Effingham; they hung in the House of Lords until destroyed in the fire of 1834. His son Cornelis Vroom became a landscape painter. The National Gallery of Art's Fleet at Sea dates from 1614.","walter_gay":"Walter Gay was born at Hingham, Massachusetts, in 1856 and studied in Boston with William Morris Hunt before going to Paris in 1876, where he trained under Léon Bonnat and settled for the rest of his life. His early pictures, such as A Philosopher of 1882, are figure subjects painted with careful finish in the polished manner of the Paris Salon. From the 1890s he turned to what became his speciality, quiet, light-filled pictures of empty rooms in French houses and châteaux, painted as though their owners had just left, and these made him the favourite of the wealthy American society in Paris. He lived with his wife Matilda in Paris and at the Château du Bréau, near Fontainebleau, and died there in 1937.","walter_macewen":"Walter MacEwen was born in Chicago in 1860 and spent most of his working life in Europe, chiefly in Paris, where he exhibited at the Salon. He was one of the many young Americans who trained abroad and then stayed on, sending pictures home to exhibitions and collectors. His reputation rests on genre scenes of the kind then popular at the Salons, carefully finished and often taking their subjects from everyday life or from the past. The Ghost Story, painted in 1887, is in the Cleveland Museum of Art, and the idea of an evening's tale told in a quiet room was one that painters of the day liked to treat. Little else is recorded of his teachers or patrons. He died in 1943, aged about eighty-three.","way_andrew_john_henry":"Andrew John Henry Way was born in 1826 and died in 1888, and was an American still-life painter associated with Baltimore. He is known above all for pictures of grapes, apples, peaches and other fruit, painted with fine detail and close attention to the soft bloom on the skin. The National Gallery of Art's Two Bunches of Grapes is undated. Grapes, with their translucency, their many small forms and their rich colour, were a favourite test of a still-life painter's skill in nineteenth-century America, and such pictures found a ready market among householders who liked to hang a picture of abundance in the dining room. Little else is recorded of his training or patrons, and his name is now known chiefly to students of American still life.","wenzler_sarah_wilhelmina":"Sarah Wilhelmina Wenzler was an American painter of still life, and the museum record gives the dates 1861 to 1872, which may mark the years in which she is recorded as working rather than her life dates. Little is known of her training or career. She is represented by one picture in the National Gallery of Art, Hanging Bunch of Grapes, dated 1867. Women painters of the mid-nineteenth century found still life one of the most accessible branches of art, because it could be practised at home without models and was considered suitable for them, and many showed fruit and flower pieces in the exhibitions of their day. Wenzler's grapes belong to that large and mostly anonymous body of work.","william_l_picknell":"William Lamb Picknell was born in Vermont in 1853 or 1854 and went to Paris in the 1870s, where he studied with Jean-Léon Gérôme before joining the American painter Robert Wylie at Pont-Aven, in Brittany, an artists' colony that drew many Americans. He painted the open, windswept landscape of Brittany in a fresh, direct manner, working out of doors, and his Road to Concarneau of 1880 was shown at the Paris Salon and widely admired, making him one of the best-known American landscape painters of his day. The Cleveland Museum of Art's Pointe de Cabellou, Brittany, painted in 1881, shows a headland at Concarneau. He later travelled in France and Italy and returned to America, settling in Marblehead, Massachusetts, where he died in 1897.","william_mason_brown":"William Mason Brown was a painter from Troy, New York, who lived from 1828 to 1898. Like many American painters of his generation he began with landscape, and he later specialised in the fruit still lifes that sold best in the prosperous years after the Civil War, when buyers of modest means wanted cheerful, decorative pictures for their parlours and dining rooms, and when painted fruit pieces and the colour prints made after them were everywhere. The Cleveland Museum of Art's Still Life with Cantaloupe, about 1880, comes from that later period and takes a common summer fruit as its subject. Pictures of this kind spoke of abundance and the comforts of a settled household. Little else is recorded of his life or patrons, and his name is now known chiefly to students of American still life.","wores_theodore":"Theodore Wores was born in San Francisco in 1859 and studied in Munich, where he was a friend and follower of Frank Duveneck. Returning to San Francisco in 1881, he became known for pictures of the city's Chinatown, among the first by a serious painter. In 1892 he travelled to Japan, where he lived for two years, studying Japanese life and painting villages, tea houses, flower festivals and temples in bright, delicate colour, work that sold well in America and Europe; Buddha's Flowers: Lotus, Tokyo, of 1894, belongs to this series. He later painted the California landscape in the manner of Impressionism, and travelled to Hawaii and Samoa. He died in San Francisco in 1939."};
